window.CABRA_QUESTIONS=[{"id":"Q00001","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Universal Immunization Programme (UIP) Cold Chain","q":"At a sub-centre immunization session, an ANM inspects a Pentavalent vaccine vial and sees that the inner square of the Vaccine Vial Monitor (VVM) is the EXACT SAME colour as the outer circle (Stage 3). Another BCG vial was reconstituted 5 hours ago. What action should the ANM take?","o":["Discard BOTH vials immediately — VVM Stage 3 (inner square matches or is darker than outer ring) indicates heat damage, and reconstituted BCG must be discarded after 4 hours","Use the Pentavalent vial immediately if stored in ice-lined refrigerator, and keep reconstituted BCG for 28 days","Use both vials only for catch-up doses above 1 year of age","Freeze the Pentavalent vial overnight to reset the VVM colour before use"],"a":0,"e":"Under India UIP guidelines: (1) VVM Stage 1 (inner square white) and Stage 2 (inner square lighter than outer circle) are usable; Stage 3 (inner square matches outer circle) and Stage 4 (darker) MUST be discarded. (2) Reconstituted BCG, Measles/MR, and JE vaccines do NOT qualify for the 28-day Open Vial Policy and MUST be discarded 4 hours after reconstitution (or at end of session, whichever is earlier). (3) Never freeze Pentavalent (DPT-HepB-Hib), which is freeze-sensitive.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00002","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Diarrhoea Dehydration \u0026 Zinc + ORS Therapy","q":"Under India IMNCI and NHM guidelines for a 10-month-old infant with acute watery diarrhoea and some dehydration (restless, sunken eyes, drinks eagerly, skin pinch goes back slowly), what is the correct Zinc supplementation regimen alongside WHO low-osmolarity ORS?","o":["20 mg elemental Zinc orally once daily for 14 days (10 mg/day only for infants under 6 months)","5 mg Zinc single dose only if diarrhoea lasts longer than 10 days","Ciprofloxacin + loperamide syrup for 5 days with no zinc","100 mg Zinc IV bolus once"],"a":0,"e":"Per WHO/IMNCI and MoHFW guidelines, oral Zinc reduces diarrhoea duration, severity, and recurrence: infants 2 to \u003c6 months receive 10 mg/day for 14 days, and children \u003e=6 months to 5 years receive 20 mg/day for 14 days, dissolved in breastmilk or clean water alongside low-osmolarity ORS (Plan B: 75 mL/kg over 4 hours for some dehydration).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00003","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Sub-Centre / Ayushman Arogya Mandir Norms","q":"Under Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS) / National Health Mission norms, what is the standard population coverage of one Sub-Centre (Ayushman Arogya Mandir - Sub-Health Centre) in plain areas versus hilly, tribal, or difficult areas?","o":["1,000 in plain areas and 500 in hilly/tribal areas","5,000 in plain areas and 3,000 in hilly/tribal/difficult areas","30,000 in plain areas and 20,000 in hilly/tribal areas","10,000 in plain areas and 5,000 in hilly/tribal areas"],"a":1,"e":"In India\u0027s three-tier rural healthcare system, a Sub-Centre (upgraded as Ayushman Arogya Mandir / Health and Wellness Centre) serves 5,000 population in plain areas and 3,000 population in hilly, tribal, or difficult terrain. By comparison, a Primary Health Centre (PHC) serves 30,000 (plain) / 20,000 (hilly/tribal), and an ASHA covers approximately 1,000 population.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00004","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Navjat Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (NSSK)","q":"Immediately after birth at a Sub-Centre delivery room, a term newborn is dried, stimulated, and has the mouth/nose cleared, yet remains limp and is not breathing or crying at 30 seconds. Per NSSK guidelines, what must the ANM initiate within the first 60 seconds (\u0027Golden Minute\u0027)?","o":["Administer intravenous sodium bicarbonate before establishing ventilation","Give 100% oxygen by nasal prongs while slapping the infant\u0027s back vigorously","Begin positive pressure ventilation (PPV) with a self-inflating bag and mask using room air (21% oxygen) at 40–60 breaths per minute","Wait 5 minutes to observe for spontaneous breathing before touching the baby"],"a":2,"e":"Under MoHFW NSSK (Navjat Shishu Suraksha Karyakram) guidelines, if a newborn fails to cry/breathe or is gasping after initial steps (warmth, position, clear airway if needed, dry, stimulate), effective bag-and-mask ventilation with room air (21% O2) at 40–60 breaths/min must begin within the Golden Minute (first 60 seconds) to establish lung inflation and prevent hypoxic brain injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00005","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"High-Risk Pregnancy Screening","q":"During a Village Health Sanitation and Nutrition Day (VHSND) antenatal check-up, a 25-year-old woman (G2P1) at 30 weeks gestation has marked palmar pallor, breathlessness on mild exertion, and haemoglobin measured at 6.4 g/dL. What is the ANM\u0027s priority management decision?","o":["Perform immediate artificial rupture of membranes at the Sub-Centre","Advise only green leafy vegetables and ask her to return after 2 months","Give 1 tablet of prophylactic IFA weekly and conduct delivery at home","Classify as severe anaemia in pregnancy (Hb \u003c 7 g/dL) and arrange immediate referral to a First Referral Unit (FRU) / CHC / District Hospital for specialist management"],"a":3,"e":"Under MoHFW Anaemia Mukt Bharat and Antenatal Care guidelines, haemoglobin \u003c 7.0 g/dL in pregnancy defines severe anaemia, a high-risk obstetric condition carrying major risk of heart failure, postpartum haemorrhage, and maternal mortality. Such mothers require immediate referral to an FRU/CHC/District Hospital for parenteral iron (IV iron sucrose/ferric carboxymaltose) or packed RBC transfusion evaluation and institutional delivery planning.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00006","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Anaemia Mukt Bharat (AMB)","q":"Under the Anaemia Mukt Bharat (AMB) programme in India, what is the standard prophylactic Iron and Folic Acid (IFA) tablet composition and duration advised by the ANM for non-anaemic pregnant women starting from the second trimester?","o":["1 red tablet daily (60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid) for at least 180 days in pregnancy and 180 days postpartum","Folic acid alone without iron throughout pregnancy","1 tablet monthly (10 mg elemental iron) taken strictly with hot tea or milk","3 tablets daily taken concurrently with calcium tablets"],"a":0,"e":"Under Anaemia Mukt Bharat, pregnant women receive 1 red IFA tablet daily (60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid) starting from the second trimester (14 weeks) for a minimum of 180 days during pregnancy, continued for 180 days postpartum. Mothers are counselled to take IFA after meals or with vitamin C-rich foods (lemon/amla) and avoid taking it simultaneously with calcium tablets, tea, coffee, or milk, which impair iron absorption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00007","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Ice-Lined Refrigerator (ILR) Management","q":"During a cold-chain OSPE inspection at a Primary Health Centre, the ANM/cold-chain handler must verify the Ice-Lined Refrigerator (ILR) temperature and placement of Pentavalent and Hepatitis B vaccines. Which practice is correct?","o":["Keep Pentavalent in the deep freezer because all vaccines require sub-zero storage","Maintain ILR temperature between +2°C and +8°C, and place freeze-sensitive vaccines (HepB, Pentavalent, DPT, Td, IPV) in the upper basket away from the bottom","Maintain ILR temperature at -15°C to -25°C and place Pentavalent and HepB directly on the bottom lining","Store drinking water bottles and lunch boxes alongside vaccines inside the ILR"],"a":1,"e":"Under India\u0027s Universal Immunization Programme (UIP), the ILR cabinet must be maintained strictly between +2°C and +8°C (checked twice daily). Because the coldest zone in an ILR is at the bottom, freeze-sensitive adsorbed vaccines (HepB, Pentavalent, DPT, Td, IPV, PCV, Rotavirus) must be placed in the upper basket to prevent freezing damage, while OPV, BCG, and Measles/MR can sit lower. Only vaccines and diluents belong inside the ILR.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00008","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Vaccine Vial Monitor and Open Vial Policy","q":"Before conducting an outreach immunization session, the ANM inspects a Pentavalent vaccine vial and notices that the inner square of the Vaccine Vial Monitor (VVM) is darker than the outer circle (Stage 4), while another reconstituted BCG vial has been open for 5 hours. How should the ANM manage both vials?","o":["Use both vials immediately if the expiry date printed on the label has not passed","Discard both vials immediately—the Pentavalent vial due to Stage 4 VVM heat exposure and the BCG vial because reconstituted BCG must be discarded after 4 hours","Use the Pentavalent vial first and store the open BCG vial in the ILR for 28 days","Shake the Pentavalent vial vigorously and administer only half a dose"],"a":1,"e":"On a VVM, when the inner square matches (Stage 3) or is darker than (Stage 4) the outer reference circle, the vaccine has exceeded safe cumulative heat exposure and must be discarded regardless of expiry date. Furthermore, under the UIP Open Vial Policy, 28-day reuse applies only to eligible multi-dose liquid vials (DPT, Td, HepB, Pentavalent, OPV, IPV, PCV) with intact VVM/cold chain—never to reconstituted freeze-dried vaccines (BCG, Measles/MR, JE), which must be discarded within 4 hours of reconstitution or at the end of the session, whichever is earlier.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00009","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing (ANM)","topic":"Postnatal Home Visit Schedule","q":"According to MoHFW / NHSRC Home Based Newborn Care (HBNC) guidelines, how many postnatal home visits and on which days are scheduled by the ASHA (and supervised by the ANM) following an institutional delivery of a term newborn?","o":["1 visit only on Day 42","3 visits on Days 1, 14, and 42","6 visits on Days 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, and 42","7 visits starting on Day 1 through Day 7 consecutively"],"a":2,"e":"Under NHSRC/MoHFW HBNC guidelines, a newborn delivered at a health facility (where the first 48 hours are monitored in hospital) receives 6 home visits on Days 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, and 42. For home deliveries, an additional Day 1 visit is conducted, making 7 visits (Days 1, 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, and 42). High-risk/LBW babies require additional follow-up.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00010","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing (ANM)","topic":"Neonatal Sepsis and Danger Signs","q":"During a Day 4 postnatal home visit, the ANM examines a newborn who is feeding poorly and lethargic, with axillary temperature 35.1°C, respiratory rate 68 breaths/min, and lower chest indrawing. What should the ANM do immediately?","o":["Bathe the newborn in warm water to raise body temperature","Wait until Day 7 HBNC visit to weigh the baby again","Advise the mother to give honey water and recheck after 3 days","Recognize neonatal sepsis/PSBI danger signs, wrap the baby warm (skin-to-skin/KMC during transport), give pre-referral antibiotic if authorized under PSBI protocol, and refer urgently to an SNCU/FRU"],"a":3,"e":"Hypothermia (\u003c 35.5°C), fast breathing (\u003e= 60 breaths/min), severe chest indrawing, lethargy, and poor feeding are hallmark danger signs of Possible Serious Bacterial Infection (PSBI) / neonatal sepsis. Prompt thermal protection (preventing further hypothermia via skin-to-skin contact/Kangaroo Mother Care during transport), pre-referral stabilization per MoHFW PSBI/IMNCI guidelines, and immediate referral to a Special Newborn Care Unit (SNCU) or FRU are life-saving.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00011","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM)","q":"At an Anganwadi nutrition screening camp, an ANM measures the Mid-Upper Arm Circumference (MUAC) of a 24-month-old child and records 11.1 cm (Red zone) without oedema. How should the child be classified and managed?","o":["Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM); perform appetite test/medical check and refer to Nutrition Rehabilitation Centre (NRC) or CMAM services","Normal nutritional status; reassure the mother","Moderate Acute Malnutrition (MAM); give single deworming tablet only","Overweight; restrict dietary fat"],"a":0,"e":"For children aged 6–59 months, a MUAC \u003c 11.5 cm (Red zone on Shakir/MUAC tape), Weight-for-Height \u003c -3 SD, or bilateral pitting oedema classifies Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM). Yellow (11.5–12.4 cm) indicates Moderate Acute Malnutrition (MAM), and Green (\u003e= 12.5 cm) indicates normal status. Children with SAM require medical evaluation for complications, appetite testing, and referral to an NRC (if complicated/failed appetite) or structured community-based therapeutic feeding.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00012","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (IDSP)","q":"Under India\u0027s Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (IDSP), which reporting form does the ANM / Health Worker (Female) compile and submit weekly from the Sub-Centre level based on symptom complexes before laboratory confirmation?","o":["Form L (Laboratory-confirmed cases)","Form S (Syndromic reporting by health workers/MPWs)","Form P (Presumptive diagnosis by Medical Officer)","Form M (Maternal death audit only)"],"a":1,"e":"IDSP operates three weekly surveillance forms: Form S (Syndromic surveillance reported by paramedical health workers/ANMs/MPWs at Sub-Centre level for syndromes like fever, cough, acute watery diarrhoea, jaundice, and acute flaccid paralysis), Form P (Presumptive clinical diagnosis reported by Medical Officers at PHCs/CHCs/hospitals), and Form L (Laboratory-confirmed cases reported by diagnostic laboratories). Unusual clustering triggers immediate outbreak alert.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00013","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Puerperal Sepsis","q":"During a postnatal home visit on Day 5 after delivery, an ANM finds the mother has fever of 38.9°C, chills, foul-smelling purulent lochia, lower abdominal pain, and a tender uterus still palpable near the umbilicus (subinvolution). What is the ANM\u0027s priority action?","o":["Prescribe only paracetamol and tell her to stop breastfeeding","Advise hot sitz baths alone for 1 week","Recognize puerperal sepsis, check vital signs/hydration, counsel on danger signs, and arrange immediate referral to a CHC/FRU for parenteral antibiotics","Reassure the mother that foul-smelling lochia is normal after delivery"],"a":2,"e":"Pyrexia \u003e= 38°C after the first 24 hours postpartum accompanied by foul-smelling lochia, uterine tenderness, and delayed involution indicates puerperal sepsis—a leading preventable cause of maternal mortality in India. Prompt referral to an FRU/CHC for parenteral broad-spectrum antibiotics and evaluation for retained products of conception is life-saving; breastfeeding can generally continue unless the mother is critically unstable.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00014","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing (ANM)","topic":"Kangaroo Mother Care (KMC)","q":"A clinically stable newborn weighing 1,750 g is discharged from an SNCU to home care. How should the ANM instruct the mother to practice Kangaroo Mother Care (KMC) properly?","o":["Lay the baby prone on a mattress beside the mother for 10 minutes daily","Feed formula milk before KMC instead of breastmilk","Wrap the baby tightly in four layers of heavy blankets before placing on the mother\u0027s lap","Place the baby (wearing only a cap, diaper, and socks) upright in frog-leg position directly skin-to-skin against the mother\u0027s bare chest between the breasts, head turned to one side, secured with a binder/dupatta"],"a":3,"e":"Per MoHFW KMC guidelines for stable low-birth-weight (\u003c 2,000 g) infants, direct skin-to-skin contact (baby naked except cap, socks, and diaper) in an upright frog-leg position between the mother\u0027s breasts transfers maternal body heat smoothly, prevents hypothermia and apnoea, promotes exclusive breastfeeding, lowers infection risk, and accelerates weight gain.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00015","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Freeze Sensitivity Verification","q":"After a power fluctuation, an ANM suspects that a batch of Pentavalent (adsorbed) vaccine vials may have frozen. She conducts the WHO/UIP Shake Test by comparing a suspect test vial against a control vial of the same batch that was intentionally frozen overnight and thawed. Both vials are shaken vigorously and placed on a flat surface. Which result proves the suspect vial is freeze-damaged and must be discarded?","o":["The suspect vial stays cloudy and settles much slower than the frozen control vial","The suspect vial settles rapidly at the same fast rate as the frozen control vial, leaving clear liquid above thick sediment within minutes","Both vials turn bright blue immediately","A frozen control vial cannot be used for Pentavalent vaccine"],"a":1,"e":"Freezing destroys the aluminium adjuvant lattice in freeze-sensitive adsorbed vaccines (Pentavalent, DPT, Td, HepB), causing granules to clump and sink rapidly. In the Shake Test, if the suspect test vial sediments at the SAME rapid rate as the intentionally frozen control vial, it has been frozen and must be discarded. If the test vial stays uniformly cloudy and settles much slower than the frozen control, it passes the Shake Test and remains usable (provided VVM and expiry are valid).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00016","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"9-Month Immunization and Vitamin A","q":"At a routine UIP immunization session, a healthy 9-month-old infant is brought for scheduled vaccination. Which Measles-Rubella (MR-1) dose/route and oral Vitamin A first dose should the ANM administer?","o":["MR-1: 0.5 mL subcutaneous in right upper arm; Vitamin A: 1,00,000 IU (1 mL) orally","MR-1: 0.1 mL intradermal in left forearm; Vitamin A: 50,000 IU intramuscularly","MR-1: 0.5 mL intramuscular in left thigh; Vitamin A: 2,00,000 IU orally","MR-1: 2 drops orally; Vitamin A: deferred until 5 years of age"],"a":0,"e":"Under India\u0027s Universal Immunization Programme (UIP), at 9 completed months (9–12 months), the infant receives MR 1st dose (0.5 mL subcutaneously in the right upper arm) along with the 1st dose of Vitamin A (1,00,000 IU = 1 mL orally, using the 2 mL spoon filled halfway), followed by 2,00,000 IU every 6 months from 16–18 months up to 5 years of age (total 9 doses).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00017","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing (ANM)","topic":"Neonatal Jaundice Assessment","q":"During an assessment of a 3-day-old newborn under IMNCI (young infant \u003c 2 months), the ANM observes yellow discolouration extending down onto the infant\u0027s palms and soles. How does IMNCI classify this finding and what action is required?","o":["Physiological jaundice; reassure parents and advise morning sunlight only","Severe Jaundice (Pink classification); keep warm, continue breastfeeding, and refer urgently to hospital/SNCU for serum bilirubin and phototherapy/exchange transfusion","Jaundice only requires referral after 30 days of life","Mild jaundice; give oral glucose water"],"a":1,"e":"Under MoHFW/WHO IMNCI for young infants (0–2 months), ANY yellow palms and soles at any age—or jaundice appearing within \u003c 24 hours of birth, or persisting in an infant \u003e= 14 days old—classifies as \u0027Severe Jaundice\u0027 (Pink row) due to high risk of bilirubin encephalopathy (kernicterus). Urgent referral to an SNCU/hospital while maintaining warmth and breastfeeding is mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00018","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Postpartum Haemorrhage Pre-Referral Stabilization","q":"After a normal delivery at a Sub-Centre, a mother continues to bleed heavily despite 10 IU IM oxytocin and external uterine massage, with BP falling to 86/54 mmHg and pulse 124 bpm. While organizing immediate ambulance transfer to the FRU, which stabilization measures should the ANM perform?","o":["Withhold IV fluids until reaching the District Hospital","Sit the mother upright and remove the IV cannula before moving","Insert two large-bore IV lines with Normal Saline/Ringer\u0027s Lactate, keep the mother flat and warm, perform internal bimanual uterine compression (or external aortic compression) if trained, empty bladder, and give repeat uterotonic/misoprostol per SBA protocol","Pack the uterus tightly with unsterile cotton rags"],"a":2,"e":"In refractory atonic PPH at a peripheral centre, preventing haemorrhagic shock during referral is critical. Establishing two large-bore IV lines for rapid crystalloid resuscitation, keeping the mother flat and warm, emptying the bladder, administering protocol uterotonics (e.g., oxytocin infusion/repeat dose or misoprostol 800 mcg), and applying bimanual uterine compression or abdominal aortic compression physically slows blood loss until definitive FRU care.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00019","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Complementary Feeding Initiation","q":"A mother brings her 6-month-old infant to the Sub-Centre and asks what foods to start alongside breastmilk. Which IYCF counselling guidance by the ANM is accurate?","o":["Feed exclusively commercial baby biscuits dipped in tea","Delay all solid or semi-solid foods until the child reaches 12 months","Stop breastfeeding immediately at 6 months and give thin watery dal soup only","Continue frequent breastfeeding while introducing thick, mashed energy-dense family foods (e.g., khichdi/porridge enriched with oil/ghee, mashed banana/egg/dal) 2–3 times daily, ensuring handwashing before preparation"],"a":3,"e":"Per MoHFW MAA/IYCF guidelines, exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months must be followed at completed 6 months by timely, adequate, safe, and appropriately thick complementary foods while continuing breastfeeding up to 2 years or beyond. Thick, mashed foods that stay on a spoon (unlike watery dal/rice water) provide adequate calories and micronutrients in a small infant stomach.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00020","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Acute Flaccid Paralysis (AFP) Surveillance","q":"An ANM visits a household where a 4-year-old child developed sudden-onset weakness and floppiness in one leg 3 days ago. Under India\u0027s Acute Flaccid Paralysis (AFP) surveillance protocol for polio eradication, what notification and stool collection action applies?","o":["Notify the Medical Officer/DIO immediately, and ensure two adequate stool specimens are collected 24–48 hours apart within 14 days of paralysis onset and sent in reverse cold chain (+2°C to +8°C)","Wait 1 month to see if the leg recovers spontaneously before reporting","Administer an intramuscular injection into the weak limb immediately","Collect a single throat swab after 30 days at room temperature"],"a":0,"e":"Every case of Acute Flaccid Paralysis (AFP) in a child \u003c 15 years (or suspected poliomyelitis at any age) must be reported immediately and investigated within 48 hours. Two stool samples (each ~8–10 g) collected 24–48 hours apart within 14 days of onset and transported in reverse cold chain (+2°C to +8°C) to a WHO-accredited lab maximize poliovirus isolation sensitivity, followed by a 60-day residual paralysis check.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00021","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Malaria Treatment Precautions","q":"A 24-year-old woman in her second trimester of pregnancy tests positive for Plasmodium vivax on a bivalent malaria Rapid Diagnostic Test (RDT) at a Sub-Centre. Under NVBDCP guidelines, which antimalarial regimen is safe and which drug MUST be withheld during pregnancy?","o":["Withhold all antimalarials until after delivery","Administer full 3-day chloroquine course (25 mg/kg over 3 days) and strictly WITHHOLD primaquine during pregnancy (and in infants \u003c 1 year and G6PD deficiency)","Give intravenous quinine plus primaquine orally for 14 days","Give full 3-day chloroquine plus 14-day primaquine immediately"],"a":1,"e":"Under India\u0027s NVBDCP drug policy, uncomplicated Plasmodium vivax malaria is treated with chloroquine for 3 days (safe in all trimesters of pregnancy) followed by primaquine for 14 days to eradicate hepatic hypnozoites. However, primaquine causes severe oxidative haemolysis in G6PD-deficient individuals (including a foetus whose G6PD status is unknown) and is strictly contraindicated in pregnancy, infants \u003c 1 year, and known G6PD deficiency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00022","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Hand Hygiene Protocol","q":"At an infection-control OSPE station, an ANM has just completed assisting a normal vaginal delivery and has visible blood and amniotic fluid stains on her gloved hands after removing gloves. Which hand hygiene method is mandatory?","o":["Wipe hands on a dry cloth apron and put on new gloves","Apply 2 mL of alcohol-based hand rub for 10 seconds only","Wash hands thoroughly with soap and running water for 40–60 seconds following WHO steps","Dip fingers briefly in a shared bowl of plain water"],"a":2,"e":"Per WHO Hand Hygiene Guidelines, alcohol-based hand rub (20–30 s) is preferred for routine decontamination when hands are NOT visibly soiled. However, whenever hands are visibly dirty or contaminated with blood, body fluids, or proteinaceous material—or after exposure to spore-forming pathogens like C. difficile—soap and running water handwashing (40–60 seconds) is mandatory because organic matter impairs alcohol efficacy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00023","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK)","q":"Under the Government of India\u0027s Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK), which comprehensive cashless entitlements apply to pregnant women delivering in public health facilities and to sick infants?","o":["Only free normal delivery for the mother; Caesarean section and blood are charged","Benefits restricted exclusively to private corporate hospitals","Only free ambulance transport one way from home to hospital","Zero-expense care including normal/Caesarean delivery, essential drugs, diagnostics, blood transfusion, diet during stay, and free transport (home-to-facility, inter-facility referral, and drop-back) for pregnant women and sick infants up to 1 year of age"],"a":3,"e":"JSSK eliminates out-of-pocket expenses in public health institutions by guaranteeing completely free vaginal and Caesarean delivery, free medications/consumables, diagnostics, blood, inpatient diet (3 days normal / 7 days C-section), and all-way free transport for pregnant women, postpartum mothers (up to 42 days), and sick newborns/infants up to 1 year of age.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00024","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Umbilical Cord Care and Clamping","q":"At a normal full-term Sub-Centre birth where the newborn cries vigorously immediately, when should the ANM clamp the umbilical cord, and how should the family care for the cord stump at home?","o":["Clamp and cut immediately at 5 seconds and apply warm mustard oil and turmeric","Wait 1–3 minutes (delayed cord clamping until pulsations slow/stop) before clamping and cutting with a sterile instrument, and keep the stump clean, dry, and exposed above the diaper without applying oil, ash, or cow dung","Tie with unsterile sewing thread and cover tightly with plastic wrap","Apply antibiotic powder and wet bandages every hour"],"a":1,"e":"Delayed cord clamping (1–3 minutes after birth in vigorous term and preterm infants not needing immediate resuscitation) facilitates placental transfusion, boosting neonatal iron stores and preventing infant anaemia. Keeping the cord stump clean and dry (avoiding traditional applications like cow dung, ash, sindoor, or oil—which cause neonatal tetanus and omphalitis) allows safe mummification and separation within 5–10 days.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00025","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Preeclampsia Detection","q":"During a routine antenatal clinic at 27 weeks gestation, a previously normotensive primigravida has BP 150/96 mmHg confirmed on two readings 4 hours apart, along with 2+ proteinuria on urine dipstick. How should the ANM classify this finding and act?","o":["Suspected preeclampsia (new-onset hypertension \u003e= 140/90 mmHg after 20 weeks with proteinuria); record carefully, counsel on danger signs (headache, blurring, epigastric pain), and refer promptly to PHC/CHC/FRU for obstetric care","Normal pregnancy adaptation; schedule next visit in 3 months","Mild urinary tract infection; prescribe oral antibiotics only","Gestational diabetes; restrict all dietary salt to zero"],"a":0,"e":"New-onset BP \u003e= 140/90 mmHg after 20 weeks of gestation accompanied by \u003e= 1+ (here 2+) dipstick proteinuria defines preeclampsia, which can rapidly escalate to severe preeclampsia, eclampsia, abruption, or fetal growth restriction. Early ANM detection, danger-sign counselling, and prompt medical officer/FRU referral enable safe antihypertensive therapy, fetal surveillance, and planned institutional delivery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00026","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Ice Pack Conditioning","q":"Before placing frozen ice packs from a deep freezer (-20°C) into a 4-ice-pack vaccine carrier for an outreach immunization session, why must the ANM \u0027condition\u0027 the ice packs on a table until frost melts and liquid water is heard sloshing inside?","o":["To sterilize the plastic casing of the ice pack","To bring the ice packs\u0027 surface temperature to ~0°C so freeze-sensitive vaccines (Pentavalent, HepB, DPT, Td, IPV) inside the carrier do not freeze","To make the ice packs heavier for transport","To warm the ice packs to body temperature (37°C) so vaccines melt faster"],"a":1,"e":"Ice packs removed directly from a -15°C to -25°C deep freezer can freeze nearby vials in a closed vaccine carrier, permanently destroying adsorbed freeze-sensitive vaccines (Pentavalent, HepB, DPT, Td, IPV, PCV, Rotavirus). Conditioning means leaving ice packs at room temperature until beads of condensation appear and ice rattles/sloshes in water inside (~0°C), safely keeping the carrier interior at +2°C to +8°C.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00027","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Adverse Events Following Immunization (AEFI)","q":"Ten minutes after receiving a routine vaccine at an outreach session, an infant develops sudden generalized urticaria, wheezing/stridor, and limpness (anaphylaxis). Using the mandatory AEFI anaphylaxis kit, what is the ANM\u0027s immediate life-saving pharmacological intervention?","o":["Administer oral cetirizine drops and ask the parents to wait 2 hours","Administer intramuscular dexamethasone alone as first-line rescue","Administer age/weight-appropriate Adrenaline (1:1,000) 0.01 mL/kg intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh immediately, lay the infant flat/elevate legs (if breathing), call for emergency transport, and report AEFI","Inject adrenaline intravenously as an undiluted bolus into a scalp vein"],"a":2,"e":"Anaphylaxis after immunization is a rare but rapidly fatal emergency if delayed. Every outreach session site must have an AEFI anaphylaxis kit containing 1:1,000 Adrenaline ampoules, 1 mL syringes, and a dosing chart. Immediate intramuscular Adrenaline (0.01 mL/kg of 1:1,000, max 0.5 mL) into the anterolateral thigh reverses bronchospasm, laryngeal oedema, and shock rapidly; antihistamines and steroids are secondary adjuncts only.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00028","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing (ANM)","topic":"Thermal Care and Warm Chain","q":"During a Day 1 postnatal visit after a home delivery of a 2.3 kg newborn, the grandmother is preparing cold well water and soap to scrub off the white waxy coating (vernix caseosa) from the baby\u0027s skin. What evidence-based counselling should the ANM give immediately?","o":["Scrub the vernix off briskly with dry salt and mustard oil","Expose the wet newborn to direct sunlight for 2 hours","Agree with bathing immediately using cold water to toughen the infant","Advise delaying the first bath for at least 24 hours (or longer for low-birth-weight babies), gently wiping only blood/meconium while leaving protective vernix intact, and keeping the baby warm skin-to-skin with a cap"],"a":3,"e":"Newborns—especially low-birth-weight (\u003c 2.5 kg) infants—lose heat rapidly via evaporation, conduction, convection, and radiation, risking neonatal hypothermia, hypoglycaemia, and sepsis. Under HBNC/ENBC warm-chain guidelines, bathing should be delayed for \u003e= 24 hours (or until temperature is stable and weight improves in LBW babies), and vernix caseosa should be preserved because it insulates thermally and provides antimicrobial barrier protection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00029","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Weekly Iron and Folic Acid Supplementation (WIFS)","q":"During an Anganwadi adolescent health session under India\u0027s Weekly Iron and Folic Acid Supplementation (WIFS) / Anaemia Mukt Bharat programme, what regimen is provided to adolescent girls and boys (10–19 years) for anaemia prevention?","o":["1 blue IFA tablet (60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid) once every week throughout the year, plus biannual albendazole 400 mg deworming every 6 months","Vitamin C tablets only without iron","1 red IFA tablet twice daily forever without deworming","Albendazole 400 mg taken every day for 30 days"],"a":0,"e":"Under WIFS / Anaemia Mukt Bharat, adolescents aged 10–19 years (both school-going and out-of-school girls/boys) receive 1 blue enteric-coated IFA tablet (60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid) on a fixed weekly day (52 doses/year) plus biannual deworming with single-dose albendazole 400 mg (February and August) to eliminate intestinal helminths contributing to iron loss.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00030","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Syndromic Case Management (NACP)","q":"In India\u0027s National AIDS Control Programme (NACP) syndromic management of Sexually Transmitted and Reproductive Tract Infections (STI/RTI), what do the essential \u00274 Cs\u0027 of counselling and follow-up stand for?","o":["Cost, Cure, Isolation, Quarantine","Compliance with full drug course, Condom promotion/provision, Contact (partner) notification and treatment, and Counselling for risk reduction","Calcium, Carbohydrates, Calories, Caffeine","Cultures, CT scan, Colposcopy, Curettage"],"a":1,"e":"Syndromic STI/RTI management uses colour-coded pre-packaged drug kits (e.g., Grey Kit 1 for discharge, Green Kit 2 for vaginitis, White Kit 3 for non-herpetic genital ulcer, Yellow Kit 6 for lower abdominal pain/PID) paired with the \u00274 Cs\u0027: ensuring full Compliance (completing medications even if symptoms fade), Condom education/supply, Contact/partner tracing and simultaneous treatment (to prevent \u0027ping-pong\u0027 reinfection), and Counselling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00031","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Auto-Disable (AD) Syringe Disposal","q":"At a Sub-Centre immunization OSPE station, the ANM has just administered an intramuscular Pentavalent dose using a 0.5 mL Auto-Disable (AD) syringe. What is the safest immediate disposal step under UIP and Biomedical Waste Rules (2016)?","o":["Recap the needle carefully with both hands and throw the intact syringe in general municipal trash","Do NOT recap the needle; immediately cut the needle/hub using a needle-hub cutter (dropping metal sharps into the white puncture-proof container) and place the plastic syringe barrel into the Red bag","Bend the needle manually with gloved fingers before placing it in a Yellow bag","Wash the AD syringe in boiling water for reuse at the next session"],"a":1,"e":"Never recapping needles prevents needlestick injuries and bloodborne pathogen transmission (HBV, HCV, HIV). Under UIP injection-safety and BMW 2016 guidelines, immediately severing the needle hub with a hub cutter directs metallic sharps into the White translucent puncture-proof container while the plastic barrel goes into the Red bag for autoclaving/recycling. AD syringes lock automatically after single use and must never be reused.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00032","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Family Planning","topic":"Intrauterine Contraceptive Device (Cu-T 380A)","q":"A woman has a Copper-T 380A (IUCD) inserted at a PHC and is counselled on the \u0027PAINS\u0027 warning mnemonic before discharge. Which symptom belongs to the \u0027PAINS\u0027 warning signs requiring prompt clinic return?","o":["Desire to exercise moderately after 1 week","Mild transient cramping only in the first 24 hours","Period late (suspected pregnancy), Abdominal pain (severe), Infection signs (fever/chills/foul vaginal discharge), Not feeling strings (missing/longer strings), or Severe bleeding/spotting","Resuming breastfeeding"],"a":2,"e":"Cu-T 380A provides highly effective reversible contraception for up to 10 years. Clients are taught how to check threads gently and to return immediately for any \u0027PAINS\u0027 sign: P (Period late / pregnancy signs—rule out ectopic pregnancy), A (Abdominal pain, severe), I (Infection—fever, chills, foul discharge), N (Not feeling strings / strings suddenly longer or shorter suggesting expulsion/perforation), and S (Severe spotting/heavy bleeding).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00033","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY)","q":"What is the primary objective of the Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY) implemented under India\u0027s National Health Mission?","o":["Offering cash incentives solely for home deliveries without skilled attendants","Providing free cosmetic dental surgeries to adults","Replacing all routine childhood vaccinations with oral herbal drops","Reducing maternal and neonatal mortality by promoting institutional deliveries among pregnant women—especially poor/BPL, SC, and ST women—through conditional cash assistance and ASHA facilitation"],"a":3,"e":"Launched in 2005 under the National Rural Health Mission (now NHM), Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY) is a safe motherhood intervention integrating conditional cash assistance with delivery and post-delivery care to shift births from unsafe home settings to accredited public/private health facilities, particularly in Low Performing States (LPS), with ASHAs acting as community link workers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00034","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Child Health Nursing","topic":"Breastfeeding Attachment Assessment","q":"During a Day 2 postnatal visit, a mother complains of sore cracked nipples and the baby cries frequently after short feeds. Which four clinical signs confirm GOOD breastfeeding attachment that the ANM should help the mother achieve?","o":["Baby\u0027s lips pursed forward sucking only the nipple tip, chin away from breast, cheeks dimpling inward","Baby\u0027s chin touching the breast, mouth wide open, lower lip turned outward, and more areola visible above the mouth than below","Baby lying flat on back with head twisted 90 degrees","Feeding strictly for 2 minutes per breast every 8 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Under MoHFW MAA (Mothers\u0027 Absolute Affection) and HBNC guidelines, effective latching prevents sore nipples and ensures adequate hindmilk transfer. The four signs of good attachment are: (1) Chin touching the breast, (2) Mouth wide open, (3) Lower lip turned outward, and (4) More dark areola visible above the baby\u0027s upper lip than below the lower lip—alongside slow deep sucks and audible swallowing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00035","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) — DIPSI Test","q":"At a Sub-Centre / PHC antenatal clinic, a pregnant woman at 24 weeks gestation undergoes the MoHFW-recommended single-step DIPSI screening test (75 g oral glucose dissolved in 300 mL water regardless of prior meal timing), and her 2-hour blood glucose is 158 mg/dL. How should the ANM interpret this result and act?","o":["Positive for Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (2-hour plasma/blood glucose \u003e= 140 mg/dL); document as high-risk pregnancy, initiate dietary/physical activity counselling, and refer to PHC Medical Officer/FRU for MNT and glycaemic management","Advise fasting completely for 48 hours","Repeat the 75 g glucose load daily for 1 week","Normal result because only values \u003e 250 mg/dL indicate diabetes in pregnancy"],"a":0,"e":"Under MoHFW National Guidelines for Diagnosis \u0026 Management of Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (based on DIPSI), a single-step 75 g oral glucose load is given irrespective of fasting state, and a 2-hour venous/plasma-calibrated glucometer value \u003e= 140 mg/dL diagnoses GDM. Early referral to the Medical Officer enables medical nutrition therapy (MNT), blood sugar monitoring, and metformin/insulin if needed to prevent macrosomia, preeclampsia, and stillbirth.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00036","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"BCG Vaccine Reconstitution and Administration","q":"Under India\u0027s Universal Immunization Programme (UIP), which diluent, dose, route, and site are correct when administering BCG vaccine to a 6-week-old infant who missed birth vaccination?","o":["Reconstitute with sterile distilled water; give 0.5 mL IM in right thigh","Reconstitute ONLY with manufacturer-supplied Normal Saline (0.9% NaCl); administer 0.1 mL intradermally in the left upper arm (just above deltoid insertion)","Reconstitute with OPV drops; give 0.5 mL subcutaneously in abdomen","Administer 2 drops orally without reconstitution"],"a":1,"e":"Freeze-dried BCG must be reconstituted strictly using the matching cold (+2°C to +8°C) Normal Saline diluent supplied with the vial (distilled water causes severe irritation and sterile abscesses) and used within 4 hours. Standard UIP dose is 0.05 mL (\u003c 1 month of age) and 0.1 mL (\u003e= 1 month up to 1 year), injected intradermally using an AD syringe into the left upper arm to form a raised ~5–6 mm wheal.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00037","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Fractional IPV and Pentavalent Schedule","q":"A healthy 14-week-old infant who received Birth, 6-week, and 10-week UIP vaccines arrives at the Sub-Centre session. Which combination of Pentavalent and fractional Inactivated Polio Vaccine (fIPV) is due at this 14-week visit?","o":["BCG only at 14 weeks","Pentavalent-3 (0.5 mL IM, anterolateral left mid-thigh) and fIPV-2 (0.1 mL intradermal, right upper arm) along with OPV-3 and Rotavirus-3","Hepatitis B birth dose only","MR-2 and DPT booster-1 at 14 weeks"],"a":1,"e":"Under India\u0027s UIP schedule, Pentavalent (DPT+HepB+Hib, 0.5 mL IM in anterolateral aspect of left mid-thigh), OPV, and Rotavirus are administered at 6, 10, and 14 weeks. Fractional IPV (0.1 mL intradermal in the right upper arm) is given at 6 weeks (fIPV-1), 14 weeks (fIPV-2), and 9 months (fIPV-3, introduced under updated UIP guidelines).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00038","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing (ANM)","topic":"Neonatal Ophthalmia (Conjunctivitis)","q":"During a Day 3 HBNC home visit, an ANM inspects a newborn whose eyelids are markedly swollen and stuck together with thick yellow purulent discharge oozing from both eyes. What is the ANM\u0027s priority management?","o":["Wash eyes once with cow\u0027s urine and wait 10 days","Reassure that thick pus from swollen eyes is normal birth tearing","Recognize severe neonatal conjunctivitis (risk of gonococcal/chlamydial corneal ulceration and blindness), gently clean outer lids with sterile normal saline/clean water, counsel against kajal/milk drops, and refer urgently to PHC/SNCU for systemic and topical antibiotics","Advise the mother to instil expressed breastmilk and apply black kajal inside the lower eyelids"],"a":2,"e":"Purulent eye discharge with eyelid swelling in the first days of life (ophthalmia neonatorum) may stem from Neisseria gonorrhoeae or Chlamydia trachomatis acquired during birth, which can rapidly ulcerate the cornea and cause permanent blindness. Under HBNC/IMNCI, draining pus from the eyes is a local/serious infection requiring urgent PHC/SNCU referral for targeted antibiotics, while firmly discouraging unsafe traditional applications like kajal or breastmilk in the eyes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00039","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP)","q":"During a household nutrition survey using a field starch-iodine Salt Testing Kit (MBI kit), what minimum iodine concentration in household salt indicates adequate iodization under India\u0027s National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP)?","o":["100 ppm at household level","0 ppm if rock salt is used","1 ppm at household level","15 ppm at consumer/household level (\u003e= 30 ppm at production level)"],"a":3,"e":"Under India\u0027s NIDDCP, edible salt must contain \u003e= 30 parts per million (ppm) of iodine at the manufacturing/production point and at least 15 ppm at the consumer/household level (indicated by a deep blue/violet colour change on the field starch-iodine test kit). ANMs educate families to store iodized salt in airtight, dark containers away from stove heat/moisture and add it near the end of cooking to prevent iodine volatilization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00040","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Suspected Measles Outbreak Response","q":"An ANM identifies three unvaccinated children (ages 18–30 months) in the same hamlet presenting with fever, generalized maculopapular rash, cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis. Alongside immediate IDSP/Medical Officer notification and isolation advice, which specific micronutrient protocol reduces measles blindness and mortality?","o":["Two age-appropriate doses of oral Vitamin A (2,00,000 IU on Day 1 and 2,00,000 IU on Day 2 for children \u003e= 12 months) to prevent corneal ulceration/keratomalacia and severe complications","Withholding all fluids and food for 3 days","High-dose oral iron syrup during acute high fever","Single dose of Vitamin D drops once"],"a":0,"e":"Acute measles rapidly depletes hepatic Vitamin A stores and damages epithelial integrity in the cornea, respiratory tract, and gut. Per WHO/MoHFW measles case-management guidelines, every child with suspected measles should receive two daily doses of Vitamin A (50,000 IU \u003c 6 months; 1,00,000 IU 6–11 months; 2,00,000 IU \u003e= 12 months on Day 1 and Day 2, plus a 3rd dose 2–4 weeks later if clinical xerophthalmia signs exist) alongside prompt outbreak notification.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00041","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Primary Health Care","topic":"Anaemia Classification in Pregnancy","q":"At a Sub-Centre ANC OSPE station, the ANM measures a 20-week pregnant woman\u0027s capillary blood on a calibrated digital haemoglobinometer and obtains Hb = 9.6 g/dL. According to MoHFW Anaemia Mukt Bharat pregnant-woman cut-offs, how is this classified and initially managed?","o":["Normal haemoglobin; give no iron tablets","Moderate anaemia (7.0–9.9 g/dL); initiate therapeutic oral IFA (2 red tablets daily = 120 mg elemental iron + 1,000 mcg folic acid, or parenteral iron as per PHC MO evaluation) with 1-month Hb recheck","Mild anaemia (10.0–10.9 g/dL); give 1 tablet daily","Severe anaemia (\u003c 7.0 g/dL); immediate ICU ventilation"],"a":1,"e":"Under MoHFW/WHO anaemia thresholds for pregnant women: \u003e= 11.0 g/dL is normal, 10.0–10.9 g/dL is mild anaemia, 7.0–9.9 g/dL is moderate anaemia, and \u003c 7.0 g/dL is severe anaemia. A reading of 9.6 g/dL falls in the moderate anaemia category, requiring therapeutic iron escalation (2 IFA tablets daily or Medical Officer evaluation for IV iron sucrose if intolerant/late gestation) and follow-up Hb reassessment after 1 month.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00042","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion \u0026 Reproductive Health","topic":"Menstrual Hygiene Management","q":"During an adolescent girls\u0027 health education session under Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram (RKSK), which menstrual hygiene practice should the ANM emphasize to prevent reproductive tract infections (RTIs) and toxic shock?","o":["Wash vaginal canal internally with harsh undiluted antiseptic bleach daily","Wear a single sanitary pad for 18–24 hours to economize supplies","Change clean sanitary pads every 4–6 hours (or more often during heavy flow), wash the perineal area gently with clean water wiping from front to back, and dispose of used pads safely wrapped in paper/incineration—never flushing into drains","Dry reused cloth pads hidden inside dark damp cupboards"],"a":2,"e":"Prolonged wearing of soaked sanitary pads (\u003e 6–8 hours) promotes bacterial proliferation (e.g., Staphylococcus aureus, E. coli) and vulvovaginal irritation/RTIs. Changing pads every 4–6 hours, cleansing externally from front (urethra) to back (anus) to avoid faecal contamination of the urethra/vagina, handwashing before/after, and safe wrapped solid-waste disposal/incineration uphold RKSK menstrual hygiene standards.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00043","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"ASHA Worker Norms and Responsibilities","q":"Under India\u0027s National Health Mission (NHM), what is the standard rural population norm for one Accredited Social Health Activist (ASHA), and what is her primary role alongside the ANM?","o":["1 ASHA per 100,000 population working exclusively inside ICU wards","1 ASHA per 30,000 population acting as a specialist surgeon","1 ASHA per 50,000 population managing hospital payroll","1 ASHA per approximately 1,000 population serving as a resident community health mobilizer and link worker for ANC, institutional delivery (JSY), HBNC visits, UIP mobilization, and basic community health education"],"a":3,"e":"An ASHA is a trained local woman resident selected by the community/Gram Panchayat, covering roughly 1,000 population in rural villages (relaxed in sparse hilly/tribal/desert hamlets). She bridges households and the Sub-Centre/ANM—mobilizing pregnant women for ANC and institutional birth, conducting HBNC postnatal visits, assisting VHSND immunization, and delivering basic first-contact care (ORS, IFA, malaria RDT/DOTS support).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00044","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Village Health, Sanitation and Nutrition Day (VHSND)","q":"Which triad of frontline workers jointly conducts the monthly Village Health, Sanitation and Nutrition Day (VHSND) at the village Anganwadi Centre, and which core services are delivered there?","o":["ANM, ASHA, and Anganwadi Worker (AWW) jointly delivering antenatal check-ups, Td/childhood immunization, child growth monitoring, IFA/ORS/zinc/contraceptive distribution, and nutrition counselling","Only hospital accountants conducting tax audits","Only private pharmacists selling cosmetic supplements","District magistrates performing major surgeries in the school"],"a":0,"e":"VHSND (often held on fixed days like Wednesdays/Thursdays at the Anganwadi Centre) exemplifies grassroots convergence between the Health Department (ANM + ASHA) and ICDS/Women \u0026 Child Development (Anganwadi Worker). Families receive integrated preventive services—ANC registration/BP/Hb/urine check, UIP vaccines, weight plotting on MCP cards, take-home rations, IFA/ORS/zinc/contraceptives, and early high-risk referrals—at one accessible neighbourhood site.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00045","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Imminent Eclampsia Danger Signs","q":"At a Sub-Centre ANC visit at 33 weeks gestation, a woman with BP 154/102 mmHg complains of severe persistent frontal headache, sudden blurring of vision with seeing flashes of light, and sharp pain in the upper middle abdomen (epigastric pain). What must the ANM recognize and do immediately?","o":["Reassure her that headache and blurred vision are ordinary indigestion and send her home walking","Recognize red-flag signs of severe preeclampsia / imminent eclampsia, keep her resting quietly on her left side, notify the PHC Medical Officer, and arrange immediate 108/102 ambulance referral to an FRU/CEmOC facility","Give hot spicy tea and advise returning next month","Ask her to climb stairs briskly to lower blood pressure"],"a":1,"e":"Severe headache, visual disturbances (blurring/scotomata from retinal/cerebral vasospasm), and epigastric/RUQ pain (from hepatic capsule stretching/HELLP) accompanying gestational hypertension signal imminent eclamptic convulsions and maternal/foetal catastrophe. Immediate left-lateral rest, zero delay, and emergency ambulance transport to a CEmOC/FRU for MgSO4, antihypertensives, and delivery planning are mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00046","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Primary Health Care","topic":"Antenatal Td (Tetanus-Diphtheria) Schedule","q":"A 23-year-old primigravida registers at 12 weeks gestation, and a 26-year-old multigravida (who received two documented Td doses during her previous pregnancy 20 months ago) registers at 16 weeks gestation. According to India\u0027s UIP antenatal schedule, what Td doses should the ANM administer to each woman?","o":["Give 5 doses to both women regardless of history","Primigravida: Td-1 early in pregnancy and Td-2 at least 4 weeks later (0.5 mL IM upper arm); Multigravida immunized within the past 3 years: 1 Td booster dose only","Give zero doses to the primigravida and 3 doses to the multigravida","Give Td orally with ORS to both"],"a":1,"e":"Under India\u0027s Universal Immunization Programme (which replaced TT with Td to confer dual tetanus-diphtheria protection), a previously unimmunized pregnant woman receives Td-1 as early as possible in pregnancy and Td-2 \u003e= 4 weeks later (ideally \u003e= 4 weeks before expected delivery). If a woman received 2 documented doses in a pregnancy within the last 3 years, only a single Td booster (0.5 mL IM deltoid) is needed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00047","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Pentavalent Vaccine","q":"When counselling parents at a Sub-Centre immunization clinic before giving the 6-week Pentavalent injection (0.5 mL IM in left anterolateral mid-thigh), which five diseases does the Pentavalent vaccine protect the infant against?","o":["Polio, Measles, Rubella, Tuberculosis, and Rotavirus","Malaria, Dengue, Typhoid, Cholera, and Rabies","Diphtheria, Pertussis, Tetanus, Hepatitis B, and Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib pneumonia/meningitis)","Chickenpox, Mumps, Influenza, Hepatitis A, and HPV"],"a":2,"e":"Under UIP, the Pentavalent liquid combination vaccine combines five antigens in one 0.5 mL IM injection given at 6, 10, and 14 weeks—protecting infants against Diphtheria, Pertussis (whooping cough), Tetanus, Hepatitis B, and Haemophilus influenzae type b (a leading cause of bacterial meningitis and pneumonia in young children), reducing needle pricks and improving coverage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00048","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Deep Freezer vs ILR Temperature Logging","q":"At a PHC cold-chain OSPE station, how frequently must the Ice-Lined Refrigerator (ILR) and Deep Freezer temperatures be read and recorded, and what belongs inside a PHC-level Deep Freezer (-15°C to -25°C)?","o":["Check temperature once a month; store Pentavalent and HepB inside the deep freezer","Never open the logbook unless a vial breaks","Check temperature only during power outages; store milk and ice cream inside","Check and chart temperatures TWICE daily (morning and evening, including Sundays/holidays) on the temperature logbook; use the PHC Deep Freezer ONLY for freezing water-filled ice packs (never store routine UIP liquid/freeze-dried vaccines inside a PHC deep freezer)"],"a":3,"e":"Cold chain integrity requires monitoring and plotting dial/stem thermometer readings inside the ILR (+2°C to +8°C) and Deep Freezer (-15°C to -25°C) twice daily every day of the week to detect thermostat failure or power excursions immediately. At PHC level, the Deep Freezer is dedicated solely to freezing ice packs (arranged with air gaps for even freezing) and never holds Pentavalent, HepB, DPT, Td, or IPV.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00049","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Oral Polio Vaccine (OPV) Technique","q":"An ANM administers 2 drops of bivalent Oral Polio Vaccine (bOPV) onto the tongue of a 6-week-old infant without letting the dropper touch the mouth, and the infant immediately spits/vomits all the drops back out within 15 seconds. What should the ANM do?","o":["Re-administer 2 fresh drops of bOPV right away and document a single valid dose","Administer 10 drops simultaneously to compensate","Inject the OPV drops intramuscularly into the thigh","Record the dose as complete and ask the mother to return next month"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike injectable vaccines, if an infant immediately spits out or vomits an oral OPV dose before swallowing, mucosal uptake fails; standard UIP guidelines direct giving a repeat 2-drop oral dose immediately while ensuring the dropper tip never contacts the infant\u0027s lips or tongue (preventing vial contamination).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00050","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing (ANM)","topic":"Very Low Birth Weight (\u003c 1,800 g) Referral","q":"During a Day 1 HBNC visit following a home birth, the ANM weighs the newborn using a calibrated scale and records a birth weight of 1,650 g, though the baby is currently breathing steadily. Under MoHFW HBNC guidelines, what is the required decision for a newborn weighing \u003c 1,800 g?","o":["Manage solely at home with ordinary cotton wraps and weekly weighing","Classify as high-risk very low birth weight (\u003c 1,800 g) requiring urgent referral to a Special Newborn Care Unit (SNCU) / NBSU while maintaining thermal protection (skin-to-skin/KMC) during transport","Give diluted cow\u0027s milk by bottle at home","Bathe the baby immediately in warm water before weighing"],"a":1,"e":"Under MoHFW HBNC / FBNC triage criteria, newborns weighing between 1,800 g and 2,499 g who are clinically stable and feeding well can receive intensified home-based care with KMC and extra visits, whereas birth weight \u003c 1,800 g carries high risk of severe hypothermia, hypoglycaemia, apnoea, feeding failure, and sepsis—mandating urgent referral to an SNCU/NBSU with KMC warmth during transit.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00051","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Family Planning","topic":"Lactational Amenorrhoea Method (LAM)","q":"A postpartum mother 4 months after delivery asks whether breastfeeding alone still protects her from pregnancy. Which THREE criteria must ALL be met simultaneously for the Lactational Amenorrhoea Method (LAM) to remain effective (~98% protection)?","o":["Baby sleeps 10 hours nightly without feeds, mother takes herbal tea, and baby is 11 months old","Infant receives cow\u0027s milk bottles every afternoon while mother works","Mother is amenorrhoeic (no menstrual bleeding since delivery), infant is \u003c 6 months old, AND infant is exclusively (or nearly exclusively) breastfed day and night","Mother has resumed regular monthly periods but feeds twice a day"],"a":2,"e":"LAM relies on frequent suckling-induced suppression of GnRH/LH ovulation. Protection exceeds 98% ONLY when all three criteria hold simultaneously: (1) postpartum amenorrhoea (no menses after 56 days postpartum), (2) exclusive/fully breastfeeding on demand day and night without long intervals or supplements, and (3) infant age \u003c 6 months. Once any single criterion ends (e.g., menses returns, mixed feeds start, or baby turns 6 months), another contraceptive method (PPIUCD/IUCD, progestin-only pill/injection, or condoms) must start immediately.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00052","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"WHO / ICDS MCP Card Growth Chart","q":"While reviewing a 14-month-old child\u0027s monthly weight-for-age dots plotted on the Mother and Child Protection (MCP) card, the ANM sees that the line connecting the last 3 monthly weights has become completely flat (horizontal). What does a flat growth line signify?","o":["Equipment error that can be ignored for 6 months","Overweight obesity requiring calorie restriction","Ideal healthy rapid growth requiring no advice","Growth stagnation (early growth faltering—child is failing to gain weight); investigate feeding practices/recent infections and provide intensive nutrition counselling and follow-up"],"a":3,"e":"On the ICDS/WHO MCP growth chart, the direction of the child\u0027s growth curve is more sensitive than a single static point: an upward curve parallel to reference lines indicates healthy growth; a flat horizontal line indicates growth faltering/stagnation requiring early dietary/illness intervention; and a downward-sloping curve signals acute weight loss needing prompt medical evaluation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00053","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Nutrition","topic":"Maternal Calcium and Albendazole Guidelines","q":"Under MoHFW maternal nutrition guidelines, pregnant women receive Calcium + Vitamin D3 tablets (500 mg elemental calcium + 250 IU Vitamin D3 twice daily from 14 weeks gestation until 6 months postpartum) in addition to daily IFA. Why must the ANM instruct the mother NEVER to swallow Calcium and IFA tablets at the same time?","o":["Taking them together turns teeth bright yellow","Calcium competes with and significantly inhibits intestinal absorption of non-haeme iron from IFA; therefore advise taking IFA and Calcium at different meals (e.g., Calcium morning/night, IFA afternoon)","Calcium converts folic acid into toxic cyanide","Both tablets cancel out maternal blood pressure medication"],"a":1,"e":"MoHFW guidelines recommend 1,000 mg elemental calcium daily (two 500 mg tablets + Vitamin D3, reducing preeclampsia and supporting foetal bone growth) from 14 weeks gestation through 6 months lactation, plus single-dose albendazole 400 mg in the 2nd trimester. Because divalent calcium inhibits duodenal iron transport, separating IFA and calcium administration by several hours preserves bioavailability of both supplements.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00054","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Suspected Cholera / Acute Diarrhoea Cluster","q":"Six adults in a peri-urban slum cluster develop sudden profuse painless \u0027rice-water\u0027 watery diarrhoea and sunken eyes within 18 hours (suspected cholera outbreak). What is the ANM\u0027s immediate dual clinical and surveillance action?","o":["Initiate immediate ORS/rapid IV referral for severe cases, notify the PHC Medical Officer and IDSP immediately as an outbreak alert, assist stool/rectal swab transport (Cary-Blair medium) if directed, and launch household water chlorination and ORS distribution","Administer loperamide tablets to stop diarrhoea immediately in all cases","Seal off the village road without providing ORS or water treatment","Wait 14 days for the next routine monthly report before mentioning the cases"],"a":0,"e":"Profuse painless \u0027rice-water\u0027 stools causing rapid hypovolaemic shock signify suspected cholera (Vibrio cholerae)—an IDSP trigger event requiring immediate notification (within 24 hours, without waiting for weekly Form S compilation). Rapid rehydration (ORS for mild/some dehydration; immediate IV Ringer\u0027s Lactate referral for severe shock), safe water chlorination (free residual chlorine \u003e= 0.5–1 mg/L), and community hygiene halt mortality and secondary spread.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00055","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Kala-Azar (Visceral Leishmaniasis)","q":"In an endemic eastern Indian block, an adult presents with persistent irregular fever \u003e 2 weeks unresponsive to antimalarials, progressive weight loss, darkening of skin, and marked splenomegaly. Which vector transmits Kala-Azar (Visceral Leishmaniasis), and what is the primary confirmatory/treatment pathway under NVBDCP?","o":["Housefly; topical permethrin cream","Female Phlebotomus argentipes sandfly (breeding in damp mud cracks/cattle sheds); refer to PHC/CHC for rK39 rapid diagnostic test and single-dose Liposomal Amphotericin B (LAmB)","Culex mosquito; oral albendazole","Female Aedes mosquito; oral chloroquine"],"a":1,"e":"Kala-Azar (caused by Leishmania donovani) is transmitted by the female Phlebotomus argentipes sandfly, which hides in damp organic crevices of mud walls and cattle sheds and hops short distances low to the ground. Under India\u0027s Kala-Azar Elimination Programme (NCVBDC/NVBDCP), fever \u003e 2 weeks with splenomegaly in endemic blocks is screened via rK39 dipstick test and treated curatively with single-dose IV Liposomal Amphotericin B (LAmB), alongside indoor residual spraying (synthetic pyrethroids) and plastering mud wall cracks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00056","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Family Planning","topic":"Combined Oral Contraceptive Pills (Mala-N)","q":"A woman taking combined oral contraceptive pills (Mala-N) calls the Sub-Centre worried because she forgot to take her active hormonal pills for TWO consecutive days (Days 9 and 10 of the cycle). What instruction should the ANM give?","o":["Discard the entire remaining packet and stop contraception until next year","Continue without any backup protection because ovulation is impossible","Take one missed active pill as soon as remembered, continue taking one active pill daily as scheduled, AND use a backup barrier method (condoms) or abstain for the next 7 consecutive days","Swallow 5 pills at once to catch up immediately"],"a":2,"e":"Missing 2 or more active hormonal combined pills (especially in week 1 or 2) lowers circulating ethinylestradiol/progestin enough to permit follicular maturation and breakthrough ovulation. Per MoHFW/WHO Selected Practice Recommendations, the woman should take the most recently missed pill immediately, resume daily pills on schedule, and use condoms/abstinence for 7 consecutive days (plus emergency contraception if unprotected intercourse occurred in the past 5 days and pills were missed in week 1).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00057","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Population-Based Screening \u0026 CBAC Form","q":"Under India\u0027s NP-NCD population-based screening at Ayushman Arogya Mandir (Health \u0026 Wellness Centres), from what minimum age are all adults screened using the Community Based Assessment Checklist (CBAC) for Hypertension, Diabetes, and Oral, Breast, and Cervical cancers?","o":["15 years and above","18 years and above","30 years and above","65 years and above only"],"a":2,"e":"Under MoHFW Operational Guidelines for Prevention, Screening and Control of Common Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD), ASHAs complete the CBAC risk-assessment form during home visits for all individuals aged \u003e= 30 years (with a CBAC score \u003e 4 prioritizing urgent screening), and ANMs/CHOs screen for hypertension, diabetes, and oral cancer (plus breast and cervical cancer for women aged 30–65 years).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00058","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Community Bag Technique","q":"During a postnatal home visit, an ANM prepares to use her community health bag. Which sequence of actions demonstrates correct bag technique to prevent cross-contamination?","o":["Open the bag immediately, take out the soap dish, wash hands, and place the bag directly on the mud floor","Take out the thermometer first, check temperature, and then wash hands before closing the bag","Keep soiled dressings in the clean compartment and discard them at the next house","Spread a clean newspaper/plastic sheet on a flat elevated surface, place the bag on it, wash hands with soap and water from the outside pocket before opening the inner clean compartment"],"a":3,"e":"Proper bag technique protects the clean contents of the ANM bag from household and inter-home contamination. The bag is placed on a protective paper/mat away from pets and children; handwashing supplies are kept in an outer/side pocket so hands are washed BEFORE touching the sterile/clean articles inside.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00059","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Village Level Health Workers","q":"Under the National Health Mission (NHM), what is the standard population norm for deploying one Accredited Social Health Activist (ASHA) in rural areas, and what is her primary coordination platform with the ANM?","o":["One ASHA per 1,000 population; Village Health, Sanitation and Nutrition Day (VHSND) at the Anganwadi Centre","One ASHA per 5,000 population; District Hospital outpatient department","One ASHA per 30,000 population; Community Health Centre surgical ward","One ASHA per 100 population; Block Primary Health Centre laboratory"],"a":0,"e":"Under NHM, the general norm is one ASHA (a local female resident preferably aged 25–45 years with minimum 8th/10th class education) per 1,000 population (relaxed in tribal/hilly/desert areas to one per habitation). She mobilizes pregnant women and children to the monthly VHSND held at the Anganwadi Centre where the ANM conducts ANC checkups and immunization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00060","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Anaemia Mukt Bharat (AMB)","q":"According to the Anaemia Mukt Bharat (AMB) strategy, what is the standard prophylactic Iron and Folic Acid (IFA) tablet formulation and duration provided to non-anaemic pregnant women starting from the second trimester?","o":["120 mg elemental iron + 400 mcg folic acid twice daily in the 1st trimester only","60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid (red tablet) once daily for 180 days starting from the 2nd trimester, continued 180 days postpartum","20 mg elemental iron + 100 mcg folic acid weekly throughout pregnancy","100 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid daily for 60 days"],"a":1,"e":"Under Anaemia Mukt Bharat (AMB), pregnant women receive one red sugar-coated IFA tablet containing 60 mg elemental iron and 500 mcg folic acid daily starting from the 4th month (2nd trimester) for 180 days during pregnancy and 180 days postpartum. In the 1st trimester, 400–500 mcg folic acid alone is given to prevent neural tube defects.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00061","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Environmental Sanitation)","topic":"Disinfection of Water","q":"An ANM conducts a Horrock’s test on a village open well to determine the dose of fresh bleaching powder (33% available chlorine) needed for disinfection. In the six white cups, the first cup showing a distinct blue colour after adding starch-iodide indicator is Cup No. 3. How many grams of bleaching powder are required to disinfect 455 litres of this well water?","o":["9 grams","12 grams","6 grams","2 grams"],"a":2,"e":"In Horrock’s apparatus, one level spoon (2 grams) of bleaching powder is dissolved in the black cup (150 mL water), and 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6 drops of this stock solution are added to the six white cups (each holding 200 mL test water). The cup number (n) that first shows a distinct blue colour with starch-iodide indicator equals n × 2 grams of bleaching powder needed per 455 litres (100 gallons) of water. For Cup No. 3: 3 × 2 = 6 grams.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00062","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Cold Chain Maintenance","q":"During a cold chain inspection at a PHC/Sub-centre hub, the ANM finds vials of Hepatitis B and Pentavalent vaccines placed at the very bottom of the Ice-Lined Refrigerator (ILR) basket, while OPV and Measles-Rubella (MR) are in the top basket. A shake test on the Pentavalent vials shows rapid sedimentation with clear supernatant. What has occurred and what must be done?","o":["Place Pentavalent inside the deep freezer compartment for 2 hours to restore potency","Pentavalent is safe because it requires freezing; leave all vials unchanged","OPV and MR failed the shake test and must be discarded","Freeze-sensitive Pentavalent and Hep B were exposed to sub-zero temperatures at the ILR bottom, failed the shake test, and must be discarded; move freeze-sensitive vaccines to the upper part of the basket"],"a":3,"e":"In an ILR (+2°C to +8°C), the coldest zone is at the bottom. Heat-sensitive vaccines (OPV, MR, BCG, JE) belong in the lower basket, whereas freeze-sensitive T-series/adjuvanted vaccines (Pentavalent, DPT, Td, Hep B, IPV, PCV) must be stored in the upper part of the basket away from the bottom/walls. A positive (failed) shake test—rapid settling of granules compared to a deliberately frozen control—proves freeze damage and requires discarding the damaged lot.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00063","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Vaccine Potency Monitoring","q":"Before loading OPV vials into her vaccine carrier for an outreach session, an ANM inspects the Vaccine Vial Monitor (VVM) printed on the cap/label. On one vial, the inner square is darker than the surrounding outer circle (Stage 4), while on another vial, the inner square is clearly lighter than the outer circle though slightly shaded (Stage 2). Which action is correct?","o":["Use both vials if the expiry date printed on the label has not yet passed","Use the Stage 2 vial first before expiry, and immediately discard the Stage 4 vial into the quarantine/discard bin","Place the Stage 4 vial in the deep freezer overnight to reverse the colour back to white","Use the Stage 4 vial only for booster doses"],"a":1,"e":"A VVM measures cumulative heat exposure. In Stage 1 (inner square white) and Stage 2 (inner square lighter than outer circle), the vaccine is usable provided it is not expired (Stage 2 vials should be used first). Once the inner square matches (Stage 3, discard point) or is darker than the outer circle (Stage 4, beyond discard point), the vaccine has lost potency and MUST be discarded.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00064","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Outreach Cold Chain Logistics","q":"Before packing 4 ice packs taken from a deep freezer (-15°C to -25°C) into a vaccine carrier for a VHSND session, why must the ANM \"condition\" the ice packs on a table until beads of water appear on the surface and ice rattles slightly inside?","o":["To raise the ice pack temperature to 0°C so that freeze-sensitive vaccines inside the carrier do not freeze","To prevent condensation inside the OPV dropper","To melt the ice completely into warm water so the carrier becomes lighter","To sterilize the plastic exterior of the ice packs"],"a":0,"e":"Ice packs removed straight from a deep freezer are at -15°C to -25°C. Packing them directly next to freeze-sensitive vaccines (Pentavalent, Td, Hep B, IPV, PCV) will freeze and destroy those vaccines. Conditioning allows the ice packs to warm to 0°C (evidenced by surface sweating and liquid water sloshing around the ice core when shaken), keeping the carrier safely between +2°C and +8°C.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00065","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Vector-Borne Disease Control (NVBDCP)","q":"A 26-year-old woman in her second trimester of pregnancy presents to a Sub-centre with fever and chills. Bivalent Rapid Diagnostic Test (RDT) is positive for Plasmodium vivax. Under National Center for Vector Borne Diseases Control (NCVBDC / NVBDCP) guidelines, what is the correct treatment decision regarding Chloroquine and Primaquine?","o":["Give full-dose Chloroquine for 3 days plus Primaquine 0.25 mg/kg daily for 14 days immediately","Give full-dose Chloroquine (25 mg/kg over 3 days) and WITHHOLD Primaquine because it is contraindicated in pregnancy","Withhold all antimalarials until delivery","Give Artesunate-Sulfadoxine-Pyrimethamine (ACT-SP) plus single-dose Primaquine"],"a":1,"e":"Under India’s National Drug Policy on Malaria, uncomplicated P. vivax is treated with Chloroquine (25 mg base/kg divided over 3 days: 10, 10, 5 mg/kg) followed by Primaquine (0.25 mg/kg/day for 14 days) for radical cure of hypnozoites. However, Primaquine is strictly CONTRAINDICATED in pregnant women, infants (\u003c 1 year), and individuals with G6PD deficiency due to risk of severe fetal/maternal hemolysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00066","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Adverse Events Following Immunization (AEFI)","q":"Ten minutes after receiving a vaccine at a Sub-centre outreach session, a 15-month-old child (weight 10 kg) develops sudden hoarse cry, stridor, generalized urticaria, and feeble rapid pulse. What is the single most important emergency drug, dose, and route the ANM must administer immediately from the AEFI kit?","o":["Subcutaneous Chlorpheniramine maleate before checking airway","Intramuscular Hydrocortisone 100 mg as the sole first-line drug","Intramuscular Adrenaline (1:1000) 0.1 mL (0.01 mL/kg) into the anterolateral aspect of the mid-thigh","Oral Cetirizine syrup and observe for 2 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Anaphylaxis is a life-threatening AEFI requiring immediate intramuscular Adrenaline (1:1000 concentration) at 0.01 mL/kg (max 0.5 mL per dose; for a 10 kg child = 0.1 mL) injected into the anterolateral mid-thigh (vastus lateralis). It rapidly reverses bronchospasm, laryngeal edema, and hypotension. Afterwards, position supine with legs elevated, ensure airway/oxygen, and arrange urgent referral.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00067","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Assessment of Cough or Difficult Breathing","q":"A 14-month-old child is brought to the Sub-centre with cough and fever for 3 days. There are no general danger signs, no chest indrawing, and no stridor when calm. His respiratory rate counted over one full minute is 44 breaths/minute. How does the ANM classify and treat this child under IMNCI?","o":["No Pneumonia (Cough or Cold); give home remedies only","Severe Pneumonia or Very Severe Disease; give first dose parenteral antibiotic and refer urgently","Bronchial Asthma; give oral aspirin and refer","Pneumonia; give oral Amoxicillin for 5 days, soothe throat/relieve cough with safe remedy, and advise mother when to return immediately"],"a":3,"e":"Under IMNCI, fast breathing cut-offs are: \u003e= 60 breaths/min (\u003c 2 months), \u003e= 50 breaths/min (2 to \u003c 12 months), and \u003e= 40 breaths/min (12 months to 5 years). At 14 months with 44 breaths/min and no chest indrawing or general danger signs, the classification is Pneumonia (Yellow row), treated with oral Amoxicillin (twice daily for 5 days per revised WHO/MoHFW IMNCI) and follow-up in 2 days.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00068","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Assessment of Diarrhoea and Dehydration","q":"An 18-month-old child presents with watery diarrhoea for 2 days. On IMNCI assessment, the child is lethargic, has deeply sunken eyes, is unable to drink, and an abdominal skin pinch goes back very slowly (more than 2 seconds). What is the correct IMNCI classification and immediate action at a Sub-centre without IV therapy facilities?","o":["Severe Dehydration; classify under Plan C, refer URGENTLY to hospital with mother giving frequent sips of ORS on the way if child can swallow","No Dehydration; advise Plan A extra fluids at home","Persistent Diarrhoea; give oral metronidazole and send home","Some Dehydration; give Plan B ORS at the Sub-centre for 4 hours"],"a":0,"e":"Two or more of the following classify Severe Dehydration (Pink row): lethargic or unconscious, sunken eyes, not able to drink or drinking poorly, and skin pinch goes back very slowly (\u003e 2 seconds). Under Plan C, if the facility cannot administer IV Ringer’s Lactate (100 mL/kg) or NG tube rehydration, the ANM must refer the child URGENTLY to the nearest PHC/CHC/FRU.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00069","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Home-Based Newborn Care (HBNC)","q":"Under the Government of India Home-Based Newborn Care (HBNC) guidelines, how many scheduled home visits are conducted for a baby born in an institution (discharged after 48 hours) versus a baby born at home?","o":["Institutional delivery: 2 visits (Days 7, 28); Home delivery: 3 visits (Days 1, 7, 28)","Institutional delivery: 6 visits (Days 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42); Home delivery: 7 visits (Days 1, 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42)","Institutional delivery: 10 visits; Home delivery: 12 visits","Institutional delivery: 1 visit (Day 42); Home delivery: 4 visits"],"a":1,"e":"Under MoHFW HBNC operational guidelines, ASHA conducts 6 visits for institutional deliveries (Days 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, and 42) since the first 48 hours are monitored in the health facility, and 7 visits for home deliveries (Days 1, 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, and 42) to detect neonatal sepsis, hypothermia, and feeding difficulties early.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00070","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (PMSMA)","q":"During an ANC clinic at the Sub-centre, an ANM examines a 29-year-old G2P1 woman at 32 weeks gestation. Her BP is 148/96 mmHg on two readings 4 hours apart, urine dipstick shows 2+ proteinuria, and her haemoglobin is 6.5 g/dL. How should the ANM classify this pregnancy and manage it?","o":["Normal physiological pregnancy changes; reassure and recheck at 38 weeks","Low-risk pregnancy; advise routine monthly follow-up at the Sub-centre with oral iron tablets only","High-risk pregnancy (pre-eclampsia with severe anaemia); mark with Red sticker on MCP card, initiate pre-referral stabilization/counselling, and refer urgently to Medical Officer/FRU for specialist care and institutional delivery","Conduct home delivery with Dai supervision"],"a":2,"e":"BP \u003e= 140/90 mmHg with \u003e= 1+ proteinuria after 20 weeks indicates pre-eclampsia, and Hb \u003c 7.0 g/dL in pregnancy is Severe Anaemia (requiring parenteral iron sucrose or packed RBC transfusion per MoHFW guidelines). Under PMSMA, high-risk pregnancies are flagged with a Red sticker on the Mother and Child Protection (MCP) card and referred immediately to a Medical Officer/FRU.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00071","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Maternal Immunization","q":"A pregnant woman registers at 16 weeks gestation for her second pregnancy. Her MCP card confirms that she received two full doses of Td vaccine during her first pregnancy 18 months ago. According to UIP India guidelines, what Td immunization does she require during this current pregnancy?","o":["Two doses of Td (Td-1 now and Td-2 after 4 weeks)","Three doses of Td spaced 1 month apart","No Td dose is needed for 10 years","A single dose of Td-Booster (0.5 mL IM in upper arm) as early as possible"],"a":3,"e":"Under UIP (which replaced TT with Td), a primigravida or unimmunized pregnant woman receives Td-1 early in pregnancy and Td-2 at least 4 weeks later. If a woman becomes pregnant again within 3 years of a previous pregnancy in which she received two documented doses, only a single Td-Booster dose (0.5 mL IM) is required.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00072","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Labour Monitoring","q":"An ANM is monitoring a primigravida in active labour at a Sub-centre/PHC delivery point using the simplified WHO partograph (starting at 4 cm cervical dilatation). Four hours later, cervical dilatation is plotted at 5 cm—crossing to the right of the Alert Line. What is the mandatory action?","o":["Arrange immediate referral and transfer to a First Referral Unit (FRU) / CHC with obstetric and emergency surgical capability","Wait another 4 hours until the plot reaches the Action Line before taking any step","Encourage the mother to push vigorously with each contraction","Rupture the membranes artificially and administer intramuscular oxytocin 10 IU"],"a":0,"e":"On the simplified WHO partograph, the Alert Line starts at 4 cm and rises at 1 cm/hour; the Action Line is parallel and 4 hours to the right. At a peripheral facility (Sub-centre/PHC without caesarean section capability), crossing the Alert Line indicates slower-than-normal labour (\u003c 1 cm/hr) and mandates immediate referral to an FRU so the woman arrives before reaching the Action Line.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00073","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Postpartum and Neonatal Danger Signs","q":"Match each postnatal finding observed during a Day-3 home visit with its most likely clinical condition: 1. Mother with temperature 38.8°C, lower abdominal tenderness, and foul-smelling lochia. 2. Mother soaking more than one pad per hour with boggy, soft uterus above the umbilicus. 3. Newborn with axillary temperature 35.8°C and cold feet. 4. Newborn with yellow staining of palms and soles on Day 3. Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Urinary retention - Cervical tear - Cold stress normal variant - Benign erythema toxicum","Puerperal sepsis - Secondary/atonic PPH - Moderate neonatal hypothermia - Severe neonatal hyperbilirubinemia","Mastitis - Placenta previa - Neonatal hyperthermia - Kernicterus prophylaxis","Normal involution - Lochia serosa - Physiological shivering - Breastmilk jaundice"],"a":1,"e":"(1) Maternal fever \u003e= 38°C with uterine tenderness and foul lochia indicates puerperal sepsis; (2) Heavy bleeding with a boggy uterus indicates uterine atony/PPH; (3) Newborn axillary temperature 32.0–35.9°C is moderate hypothermia requiring immediate skin-to-skin Kangaroo Mother Care and warming; (4) Jaundice extending to palms and soles indicates severe hyperbilirubinemia needing urgent phototherapy/referral.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00074","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Postpartum Intrauterine Contraceptive Device (PPIUCD)","q":"Within what timeframe can a Postpartum Intrauterine Contraceptive Device (PPIUCD - Cu 380A) be safely inserted after a normal vaginal delivery, and in which clinical condition is its insertion strictly contraindicated?","o":["Within 10 minutes only; contraindicated in multiparous women","Only between Day 5 and Day 14 postpartum; contraindicated in breastfeeding mothers","Within 10 minutes (post-placental) or within 48 hours of delivery; contraindicated in chorioamnionitis, puerperal sepsis, or unresolved PPH","Only after resumption of menses at 6 months; contraindicated in anaemia"],"a":2,"e":"Under the MoHFW Family Planning Manual, PPIUCD (Cu 380A, effective for 10 years) can be inserted post-placentally (within 10 minutes), intra-caesarean, or postpartum up to 48 hours after delivery. Between 48 hours and 4–6 weeks postpartum, insertion is avoided due to high perforation/expulsion risk (interval IUCD is done after 4–6 weeks). Active uterine infection (chorioamnionitis, puerperal sepsis), ruptured membranes \u003e18 hours, and unresolved PPH are absolute contraindications.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00075","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS)","q":"According to the Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS), what is the standard population norm covered by one rural Sub-centre (Ayushman Arogya Mandir - Sub Health Centre) in plain areas versus hilly, tribal, or difficult areas?","o":["1,000 in plain areas; 500 in hilly/tribal areas","1,20,000 in plain areas; 80,000 in hilly/tribal areas","30,000 in plain areas; 20,000 in hilly/tribal areas","5,000 in plain areas; 3,000 in hilly/tribal/difficult areas"],"a":3,"e":"IPHS norms establish one Sub-centre for every 5,000 population in plain areas and 3,000 population in hilly, tribal, and desert/difficult-to-reach areas. Under Ayushman Bharat, Sub-centres are upgraded to Health and Wellness Centres (Ayushman Arogya Mandir) led by a Community Health Officer (CHO) working with MPW(F)/ANM and MPW(M).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00076","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Vaccine Requirement Calculation","q":"A Sub-centre has a monthly target of 25 newborns requiring BCG vaccination. Given that the maximum permissible wastage rate for reconstituted 10-dose BCG vials under UIP is 50% (Wastage Multiplication Factor = 2.0), how many 10-dose vials of BCG should the ANM indent for one month?","o":["5 vials","8 vials","10 vials","3 vials"],"a":0,"e":"Required doses = Target beneficiaries × Wastage Multiplication Factor (WMF). With 50% permissible wastage, WMF = 100 / (100 - 50) = 2.0. Thus, required doses = 25 × 2.0 = 50 doses. Since each BCG vial contains 10 doses, number of vials = 50 / 10 = 5 vials.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00077","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Sub-centre Documentation and HMIS","q":"Match each Sub-centre record or portal with its primary purpose: 1. Eligible Couple Register (ECR) 2. Mother and Child Protection (MCP) Card 3. RCH Portal (Reproductive and Child Health Portal) 4. HMIS (Health Management Information System) Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Record daily OPD drug stock - Hospital inpatient discharge summary - Cold chain temperature log - Biometric staff attendance","Track married women aged 15–49 for spacing/limiting methods - Joint MoHFW/MWCD home-retained record for ANC, immunization, and growth monitoring - Name-based digital tracking of pregnant women and children - Monthly aggregated facility service delivery reporting","Monthly aggregated facility service delivery reporting - Cold chain temperature log - Eligible couple survey - Birth certificate issuance","Name-based digital tracking - Monthly aggregated facility reporting - Home-retained growth chart - Stock ledger"],"a":1,"e":"At the Sub-centre, the Eligible Couple Register tracks couples (wife 15–49 yrs) for family planning needs; the MCP Card is retained by the family to document ANC, delivery, PNC, immunization, and WHO growth charts; the RCH Portal enables individual name-based tracking of mother-child dyads; and HMIS compiles monthly aggregated service statistics up to the national level.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00078","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Primary Health Centre (PHC) Norms","q":"Under Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS), what is the standard population norm covered by one Primary Health Centre (PHC) in plain areas versus hilly, tribal, or difficult areas?","o":["1,000 in plain areas and 500 in hilly/tribal areas","5,000 in plain areas and 3,000 in hilly/tribal areas","30,000 in plain areas and 20,000 in hilly/tribal/difficult areas (acting as referral centre for ~6 Sub-Centres)","1,20,000 in plain areas and 80,000 in hilly/tribal areas"],"a":2,"e":"In India\u0027s rural three-tier health infrastructure: Sub-Centre covers 5,000 (plain) / 3,000 (hilly/tribal); Primary Health Centre (PHC) covers 30,000 (plain) / 20,000 (hilly/tribal) with a Medical Officer, nurses, 4–6 beds, and basic lab serving ~6 Sub-Centres; and Community Health Centre (CHC) covers 1,20,000 (plain) / 80,000 (hilly/tribal).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00079","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Ayushman Arogya Mandir (HWC) vs AB-PMJAY","q":"A rural family asks the ANM at an upgraded Sub-Health Centre (Ayushman Arogya Mandir / Health \u0026 Wellness Centre) how it differs from the Ayushman Bharat Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (AB-PMJAY) card. Which explanation is accurate?","o":["Ayushman Arogya Mandir provides only emergency open-heart surgery; AB-PMJAY gives free ORS only","Ayushman Arogya Mandir (HWC) delivers free Comprehensive Primary Health Care across 12 service packages (MCH, immunization, NCD screening/drugs, mental health, elderly/palliative care, basic lab/teleconsultation), whereas AB-PMJAY provides up to Rs. 5 lakh per family per year for secondary and tertiary inpatient hospitalization at empanelled hospitals","Both schemes serve only urban government officers","AB-PMJAY covers only outpatient vitamin tablets"],"a":1,"e":"Ayushman Bharat rests on two complementary pillars: (1) Health \u0026 Wellness Centres (now Ayushman Arogya Mandirs) delivering universal free primary care closer to home across 12 expanded packages (led by CHO, ANM, MPW-M, ASHA) with free essential drugs/diagnostics and eSanjeevani teleconsultation; and (2) AB-PMJAY providing cashless secondary and tertiary hospitalization coverage up to Rs. 5 lakh per eligible family annually.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00080","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Secondary Postpartum Haemorrhage","q":"On Day 10 after an uncomplicated institutional delivery, a mother calls the ANM reporting sudden heavy bright-red vaginal bleeding soaking three thick pads in 90 minutes, passing large clots, dizziness, and mild lower abdominal cramps. What is the ANM\u0027s priority action?","o":["Insert unsterile cloth packing tightly into the vagina","Reassure her that sudden heavy bleeding soaking pads at Day 10 is normal lochia alba","Advise drinking warm herbal tea and waiting 5 days","Recognize Secondary Postpartum Haemorrhage (commonly due to retained placental fragments or endometritis), arrange immediate 108/102 ambulance referral to an FRU/CHC, keep her lying flat/warm, and monitor vitals/start IV fluids if trained/equipped"],"a":3,"e":"Secondary Postpartum Haemorrhage is excessive vaginal bleeding occurring between 24 hours and 6 weeks postpartum—most commonly caused by retained placental/membrane fragments or puerperal endometritis causing uterine subinvolution. Soaking \u003e 1–2 pads per hour with clots and dizziness signals acute blood loss requiring urgent FRU referral for antibiotics, uterotonics, and ultrasound/evacuation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00081","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Minimum 4 Antenatal Check-Ups (ANC) Schedule","q":"Under MoHFW Maternal Health guidelines in India, what are the four minimum recommended antenatal check-up (ANC) timing windows from early registration through term?","o":["1st visit within 12 weeks (early registration), 2nd visit between 14–26 weeks, 3rd visit between 28–34 weeks, and 4th visit from 36 weeks to term","Monthly check-ups starting only after delivery","Four visits all completed during the 1st month only","Only one check-up at 39 weeks gestation"],"a":0,"e":"MoHFW mandates early registration (\u003c 12 weeks) and at least 4 quality ANC contacts across pregnancy (plus additional monthly/biweekly contacts in PMSMA/high-risk cases): Visit 1 (\u003c 12 weeks), Visit 2 (14–26 weeks), Visit 3 (28–34 weeks), and Visit 4 (36 weeks–term) to monitor maternal weight, BP, Hb, urine protein/glucose, GDM, foetal growth/lie, Td, IFA, calcium, and birth preparedness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00082","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Birth-Dose Vaccines","q":"For an institutional birth under India\u0027s Universal Immunization Programme (UIP), which three vaccines are scheduled at birth, and what is the strict maximum time window for giving the Hepatitis B birth dose?","o":["Pentavalent, MR, and DPT; Hepatitis B given at 6 months","BCG (0.05 mL ID left upper arm), OPV-0 (2 drops oral), and Hepatitis B birth dose (0.5 mL IM anterolateral thigh—strictly within 24 hours of birth)","Td, Vitamin A, and PCV; BCG given orally","IPV, Rotavirus, and JE; Hepatitis B given within 30 days"],"a":1,"e":"At institutional birth, neonates receive BCG (0.05 mL intradermal if \u003c 1 month), OPV-0 (within 15 days), and Hepatitis B birth dose (0.5 mL IM within 24 hours of birth). Giving HepB strictly within 24 hours blocks perinatal mother-to-child HBV transmission during delivery—if missed beyond 24 hours, the child receives HepB via Pentavalent starting at 6 weeks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00083","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Vaccine Carrier Foam Pad Use","q":"At an outreach immunization OSPE station, once the ANM opens a vaccine carrier containing 4 conditioned ice packs, where should opened/reconstituted vaccine vials (such as BCG, MR, or Pentavalent) be placed between consecutive infants during the session?","o":["Drop opened reconstituted BCG vials directly into pooled ice-melt water at the bottom of the carrier","Leave opened vials standing out on a sunny wooden table in room heat","Keep opened/in-use vials securely inserted into the slits of the foam pad fitted at the top of the vaccine carrier (keeping the carrier lid closed between children), while unopened vials stay inside the cold well","Hold the vials continuously inside a warm shirt pocket"],"a":2,"e":"The top sponge/foam pad inside a UIP vaccine carrier holds opened in-use vials upright in cold air while shielding their rubber stoppers from contamination, keeping them cool without exposing vials to direct sunlight or submerging stoppers in melted ice water (which causes bacterial contamination/sepsis). Unopened vials remain inside the carrier well until needed, with the lid closed after every retrieval.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00084","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Left-Out vs Drop-Out Children","q":"While reviewing the Sub-Centre immunization due-list (U-WIN / MCH register), an ANM identifies two 11-month-old children: Child A received BCG and Pentavalent-1 at 6 weeks but never returned for later doses; Child B has never received a single vaccine since birth. How are Child A and Child B classified?","o":["Child A is a Left-out; Child B is a Drop-out","Child A is a Drop-out (started UIP series but failed to complete subsequent due doses); Child B is a Left-out (never reached/vaccinated at all)","Both are classified as fully immunized","Neither requires catch-up vaccination"],"a":1,"e":"UIP microplanning and Intensified Mission Indradhanush distinguish \u0027Drop-outs\u0027 (children who initiated vaccination such as BCG/Penta-1 but failed to return for Penta-3/MR-1 due to side-effect fears, migration, or missed reminders) from \u0027Left-outs\u0027 (unreached children who received zero vaccine doses—often in hard-to-reach hamlets, slums, or migrant brick-kilns). Both require ASHA home mobilization and catch-up sessions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00085","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Child Health Nursing","topic":"Breast Engorgement Management","q":"During a Day 4 HBNC visit, a primiparous mother has bilaterally hard, swollen, warm, tender breasts with tight shiny areolae and a mild temperature of 37.6°C; the newborn cannot latch onto the flattened nipple. What advice should the ANM give?","o":["Discard colostrum/milk from engorged breasts","Stop breastfeeding completely and bind breasts tightly with a dry cloth","Give formula milk by bottle exclusively for 7 days","Apply warm moist compresses briefly or gentle hand-expression just before feeds to soften the areola so the baby latches deeply, feed frequently on demand (every 2–3 hours), and apply cool compresses between feeds for swelling/comfort"],"a":3,"e":"Physiological breast engorgement (Days 3–5 postpartum) results from increased blood/lymphatic flow and milk accumulation. Brief warm compresses and gentle manual expression soften the rigid areola so the infant can achieve a deep latch and drain the breast effectively; cool packs afterward reduce oedema and discomfort. Tight binding or stopping feeds worsens stasis and precipitates mastitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00086","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing (ANM)","topic":"Home-Based Care for Young Child (HBYC)","q":"Extending newborn follow-up beyond the 42-day HBNC period, at which five specific ages does the ASHA (supervised by the ANM) conduct structured Home-Based Care for Young Child (HBYC) visits under NHM / POSHAN Abhiyaan?","o":["Months 3, 6, 9, 12, and 15","Years 3, 6, 9, 12, and 15","Months 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 only","Only at 24 months once"],"a":0,"e":"HBYC bridges the high-vulnerability gap from 3 to 15 months through 5 structured quarterly home visits at 3, 6, 9, 12, and 15 months. Key interventions include promoting exclusive breastfeeding (3m), initiating timely complementary feeding + IFA syrup + ORS/zinc readiness (6m), continued IYCF + Vitamin A + MR-1 check (9m), growth/handwashing/diet diversity (12m), and full immunization + developmental milestone screening (15m).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00087","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Vitamin A Deficiency (Xerophthalmia)","q":"During an Anganwadi eye check-up of a 3-year-old child with poor dietary intake and night blindness, the ANM observes dry, foamy, triangular silvery-white plaques on the outer white part of the conjunctiva (\u0027Bitot\u0027s spots\u0027). Which micronutrient deficiency do Bitot\u0027s spots signify?","o":["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Vitamin A deficiency (WHO Xerophthalmia stage X1B)","Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy)","Vitamin D deficiency (rickets)"],"a":1,"e":"Bitot\u0027s spots (keratinized epithelial debris mixed with Corynebacterium xerosis forming foamy silvery-white triangular patches on the temporal bulbar conjunctiva) alongside night blindness (XN) and conjunctival xerosis (X1A) are classic clinical indicators of Vitamin A deficiency. Under MoHFW/WHO xerophthalmia treatment, therapeutic oral Vitamin A is given on Day 1, Day 2, and 2–4 weeks later (2,00,000 IU per dose for \u003e= 12 months) to prevent corneal ulceration and permanent blindness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00088","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Anaemia Mukt Bharat Paediatric IFA Syrup","q":"Under India\u0027s Anaemia Mukt Bharat (AMB) programme, what is the standard prophylactic oral Iron and Folic Acid (IFA) syrup regimen advised for healthy children aged 6–59 months?","o":["10 mL daily mixed into hot tea","No iron prophylaxis until age 18 years","1 mL of paediatric IFA syrup (containing 20 mg elemental iron + 100 mcg folic acid) TWICE a week (biweekly) throughout 6–59 months, paired with biannual albendazole deworming from 12 months onward","One adult red tablet daily swallowed whole by a 9-month-old infant"],"a":2,"e":"Under Anaemia Mukt Bharat, children aged 6–59 months receive 1 mL of IFA syrup (20 mg elemental iron + 100 mcg folic acid per mL) biweekly (e.g., Wednesdays and Saturdays, 100 doses/year) administered using an auto-dispenser bottle after meals, complemented by biannual albendazole (200 mg for 12–23 months; 400 mg for 24–59 months) on National Deworming Day.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00089","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Scabies Community Control","q":"Four children from one extended joint family present with intense nocturnal itching and tiny vesicular burrows in the finger webs, wrists, and axillae (scabies). Why does treatment repeatedly fail if only the four symptomatic children apply Permethrin 5% cream while their asymptomatic parents and grandparents do not?","o":["Permethrin kills mites in 10 years only","Permethrin must be swallowed orally like ORS","Scabies is transmitted exclusively by mosquito bites","Asymptomatic household contacts can harbour Sarcoptes scabiei mites during the 2–6 week incubation period and immediately reinfect treated children; ALL close household contacts must apply Permethrin 5% neck-to-toe on the SAME night and wash/sun-dry linens"],"a":3,"e":"Primary scabies sensitization takes 2–6 weeks before pruritus appears, so infected family members can transmit mites via skin-to-skin contact or shared bedding while asymptomatic. Simultaneous overnight neck-to-toe application of Permethrin 5% cream (washed off after 8–12 hours) for ALL household contacts plus hot-water laundering/sun-drying of clothes and bedding breaks the \u0027ping-pong\u0027 reinfestation cycle.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00090","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"Childhood \u0026 Adolescent Booster Schedule","q":"Under India\u0027s Universal Immunization Programme (UIP) schedule for children and adolescents, which booster doses are administered at 16–24 months, 5–6 years, 10 years, and 16 years respectively?","o":["DPT Booster-1 (+ MR-2 \u0026 OPV Booster) at 16–24 months; DPT Booster-2 at 5–6 years; Td at 10 years; and Td at 16 years","BCG at 5 years and Rotavirus at 16 years","Pentavalent-1 at 10 years and fIPV at 16 years","OPV-0 at 5–6 years only"],"a":0,"e":"Following infant primary doses, UIP sustains immunity via: 16–24 months (DPT Booster-1, MR-2, OPV Booster, and JE-2 in endemic districts), 5–6 years (DPT Booster-2), 10 years (Td), and 16 years (Td)—protecting school-age children and adolescents against diphtheria, pertussis, tetanus, measles, rubella, and polio.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00091","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Family Planning","topic":"Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg Emergency Contraceptive Pill (ECP)","q":"A married woman visits the Sub-Centre worried about a broken condom during intercourse 18 hours ago and requests Emergency Contraceptive Pills (ECP — Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg). Which counselling fact is accurate?","o":["ECP induces abortion even if she is already 12 weeks pregnant","Take Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg as soon as possible within 72 hours of unprotected intercourse (up to 120 hours with lower efficacy); it works primarily by delaying or inhibiting ovulation and will NOT harm or abort an already established pregnancy","Wait exactly 10 days before swallowing the ECP tablet","Use ECP daily every morning as a regular routine pill"],"a":1,"e":"Levonorgestrel ECP (1.5 mg single oral dose) prevents unintended pregnancy after unprotected sex, condom breakage, or missed pills primarily by suppressing the LH surge and delaying/preventing ovulation. Efficacy is highest when taken promptly within 72 hours. It is NOT an abortifacient and should not be substituted for ongoing regular contraception (counsel on condoms, OCPs, IUCD, or Antara).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00092","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing (ANM)","topic":"Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK)","q":"Under India\u0027s Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK), Mobile Health Teams screen children from birth to 18 years across Anganwadi Centres and government schools for which \u00274 Ds\u0027?","o":["Diabetes, Dengue, Diarrhoea, and Dementia only","Disasters, Drowning, Burns, and Accidents","Defects at birth, Deficiencies, Diseases of childhood, and Developmental delays including disability","Drugs, Diet, Dentistry, and Dermatology only"],"a":2,"e":"RBSK conducts systematic early identification and free management (at District Early Intervention Centres—DEICs and tertiary hospitals) for 30+ selected health conditions grouped into the \u00274 Ds\u0027: Defects at birth (e.g., neural tube defects, cleft lip, CHD, clubfoot), Deficiencies (SAM, anaemia, Vitamin A/D), Diseases (scabies, otitis media, rheumatic heart disease, dental caries), and Developmental delays/disabilities (vision, hearing, motor, speech, autism).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00093","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Levels of Prevention","q":"Match each public health intervention performed at the community level with its corresponding Level of Prevention: 1. Discouraging school children from adopting tobacco smoking or high-salt fast food habits before risk factors emerge 2. Administering Measles-Rubella (MR) vaccine to a 9-month-old infant 3. Performing VIA cervical cancer screening and blood pressure checks on asymptomatic adults aged \u003e= 30 years 4. Providing physiotherapy and crutches to a child with residual paralytic contracture Select the correct sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Secondary prevention - Primary prevention - Primordial prevention - Tertiary prevention","Primordial prevention - Secondary prevention - Primary prevention - Tertiary prevention","Primary prevention - Primordial prevention - Tertiary prevention - Secondary prevention","Primordial prevention - Primary prevention - Secondary prevention - Tertiary prevention"],"a":3,"e":"(1) Primordial prevention prevents the emergence/development of lifestyle risk factors in the population; (2) Primary prevention (specific protection such as immunization, chlorination, IFA prophylaxis) prevents disease onset despite exposure; (3) Secondary prevention (screening, early diagnosis, prompt treatment) halts disease progression at an early stage; (4) Tertiary prevention (disability limitation and rehabilitation) restores function after established disease.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00094","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Maternal and Child Health Indicators","q":"Which denominators are used internationally and by the Sample Registration System (SRS, India) to express Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) and Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) respectively?","o":["MMR per 100,000 live births; IMR per 1,000 live births","MMR per 1,000 total population; IMR per 100,000 live births","MMR per 10,000 deliveries; IMR per 1,000 children under 5 years","MMR per 1,000 pregnant women; IMR per 1,000 total births (including stillbirths)"],"a":0,"e":"Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) is defined as the number of maternal deaths (during pregnancy or within 42 days of termination of pregnancy from pregnancy-related causes) per 100,000 live births in a given year. Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) is the number of deaths of infants under 1 year of age per 1,000 live births in the same year.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00095","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Vitamin A Prophylaxis Programme","q":"Under India’s Universal Immunization Programme (UIP), what is the correct first dose of oral Vitamin A solution given at 9 months of age (with MR-1), and what is the subsequent dose interval up to 5 years of age?","o":["1,00,000 IU at 6 weeks, followed by 1,00,000 IU every 3 months until 2 years","1,00,000 IU (1 mL) at 9 months, followed by 2,00,000 IU (2 mL) every 6 months from 16–18 months up to 5 years (total 9 doses)","50,000 IU at 9 months, followed by 1,00,000 IU annually until 5 years","2,00,000 IU at birth, followed by 2,00,000 IU monthly for 6 months"],"a":1,"e":"To prevent nutritional blindness (xerophthalmia) and reduce child mortality, children receive 1 lakh IU (1 mL using the provided calibrated spoon) at 9 completed months alongside MR-1, then 2 lakh IU (2 mL) at 16–18 months (with DPT/OPV booster and MR-2) and every 6 months thereafter up to 5 years (9 doses totalling 17 lakh IU).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00096","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Weekly Iron and Folic Acid Supplementation (WIFS)","q":"Under the MoHFW Weekly Iron and Folic Acid Supplementation (WIFS) programme for school-going adolescent girls and boys (Classes 6–12) and out-of-school adolescent girls, which tablet colour/composition and deworming schedule are provided?","o":["White Calcium tablet weekly + Albendazole 400 mg once at age 18","Pink IFA tablet (20 mg iron + 100 mcg folic acid) twice weekly + Mebendazole 100 mg yearly","Blue IFA tablet (60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid) once weekly + Albendazole 400 mg biannually (every 6 months)","Red IFA tablet (100 mg iron) daily + Albendazole 200 mg monthly"],"a":2,"e":"Under WIFS (integrated into Anaemia Mukt Bharat), adolescents aged 10–19 years receive one Blue sugar-coated IFA tablet (60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid) on a fixed weekly day (e.g., Monday/Wednesday) supervised at schools or Anganwadi centres, plus biannual deworming with single-dose Albendazole 400 mg (National Deworming Day).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00097","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP)","q":"Under India’s National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP), what is the minimum mandatory iodine concentration in iodized salt at the production/manufacturing level versus the household/consumer level?","o":["Not less than 10 ppm at production; not less than 5 ppm at consumer level","Not less than 5 ppm at production; not less than 2 ppm at consumer level","Not less than 100 ppm at production; not less than 50 ppm at consumer level","Not less than 30 ppm at production; not less than 15 ppm at consumer/household level"],"a":3,"e":"To compensate for iodine losses during transport, humidity, and storage, FSSAI and NIDDCP standards stipulate \u003e= 30 parts per million (ppm) of iodine at the manufacturing/production point and \u003e= 15 ppm at the distribution/retail/consumer household level. ANMs/ASHAs verify \u003e= 15 ppm in the field using starch-based Salt Testing Kits (turning dark blue/violet).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00098","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Mental Health and Counselling)","topic":"Interpersonal Counselling Framework","q":"When an ANM uses the standard \"GATHER\" counselling approach to help a postnatal couple choose a suitable spacing contraceptive method, what do the six letters of GATHER stand for?","o":["Greet, Ask, Tell, Help, Explain, Return for follow-up","Greet, Advise strictly, Transfer, Hold supplies, Examine, Report","Guide, Assess, Treat, Hospitalize, Evaluate, Refer","Gather history, Administer drug, Test urine, Handle waste, Educate, Record"],"a":0,"e":"The WHO/MoHFW GATHER framework ensures client-centred, non-coercive counselling: G – Greet warmly and ensure privacy; A – Ask about needs, reproductive goals, and medical history; T – Tell about all available choices clearly; H – Help the client select the method best suited to them; E – Explain how to use the chosen method, side effects, and warning signs; R – Return for follow-up or resupply.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00099","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"National Immunization Schedule (UIP)","q":"Match each UIP infant vaccine with its correct dose, route, and anatomical site: 1. BCG at birth 2. Pentavalent at 6, 10, 14 weeks 3. Measles-Rubella (MR-1) at 9 completed months 4. Rotavirus vaccine at 6, 10, 14 weeks Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["0.05 mL Intramuscular right arm - 0.1 mL Intradermal thigh - 0.5 mL Intramuscular deltoid - 5 drops Intravenous","0.05 mL (up to 1 month) Intradermal left upper arm - 0.5 mL Intramuscular anterolateral mid-thigh - 0.5 mL Subcutaneous right upper arm - 5 drops Oral","0.1 mL Subcutaneous right thigh - 0.5 mL Intradermal left upper arm - 0.5 mL Intramuscular buttock - 1 mL Oral","0.5 mL Intramuscular gluteal region - 0.5 mL Subcutaneous upper arm - 0.1 mL Intradermal left forearm - 2 drops Oral"],"a":1,"e":"Under UIP India: BCG is 0.05 mL (0.1 mL after 1 month of age up to 1 yr) intradermally at the insertion of the left deltoid; Pentavalent is 0.5 mL IM into the anterolateral aspect of the mid-thigh; MR is 0.5 mL subcutaneously in the right upper arm; liquid Rotavirus vaccine is 5 drops orally.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00100","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Polio Eradication Endgame Strategy","q":"Under India’s updated Universal Immunization Programme (UIP) schedule (including the 3rd dose of fIPV introduced in 2023), what is the exact dose, route, anatomical site, and timing of fractional Inactivated Poliovirus Vaccine (fIPV)?","o":["2 drops Oral at 6, 10, and 14 weeks","0.5 mL Subcutaneous in the abdomen at 16–24 months","0.1 mL Intradermal in the right upper arm at 6 weeks, 14 weeks, and 9–12 months (alongside MR-1)","0.5 mL Intramuscular in thigh at birth and 6 weeks only"],"a":2,"e":"India uses fractional-dose IPV (0.1 mL intradermal using a 0.1 mL AD syringe in the right upper arm—opposite BCG on the left arm) at 6 weeks and 14 weeks, plus a 3rd dose at 9–12 months alongside MR-1 to boost humoral immunity against poliovirus types 1, 2, and 3.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00101","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"National Leprosy Eradication Programme (NLEP)","q":"During active case detection in a village, an ANM examines a 32-year-old man who has 7 hypopigmented, well-defined skin patches with loss of temperature and pain sensation on the back and arms, plus palpable thickened ulnar and lateral popliteal nerves. Under NLEP classification, how is this case categorized and what is the standard adult Multi-Drug Therapy (MDT) regimen?","o":["Tinea versicolor; topical clotrimazole for 2 weeks","Paucibacillary (PB) leprosy; Rifampicin + Dapsone for 6 months","Paucibacillary (PB) leprosy; Dapsone monotherapy for 24 months","Multibacillary (MB) leprosy; 3-drug MDT (Rifampicin, Clofazimine, and Dapsone) for 12 months"],"a":3,"e":"Under NLEP clinical criteria, Multibacillary (MB) leprosy is diagnosed when there are \u003e= 6 skin lesions, OR \u003e 1 peripheral nerve trunk involved, OR a positive slit-skin smear (1–5 patches with \u003c=1 nerve and negative smear = Paucibacillary). Adult MB-MDT consists of monthly supervised Rifampicin 600 mg + Clofazimine 300 mg, plus daily self-administered Clofazimine 50 mg + Dapsone 100 mg for 12 months (12 blister packs within 18 months).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00102","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"National Rabies Control Programme (NRCP)","q":"A 9-year-old boy runs into a Sub-centre 15 minutes after being bitten on the calf by a stray dog, resulting in two deep transdermal puncture wounds that are actively bleeding. Which immediate wound management is critical, how is this exposure categorized, and what Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) is required?","o":["Apply chilli powder and suture the wound tightly immediately; Category I; no vaccine needed","Wash the wounds immediately under running water with soap for at least 15 minutes followed by povidone-iodine, avoid suturing; Category III exposure requiring BOTH Anti-Rabies Vaccine (ARV) AND Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG) infiltrated into/around the wound","Wash for 1 minute with alcohol and cover with tight bandage; Category II exposure requiring ARV alone","Cauterize the wound with carbolic acid; wait 10 days to observe the stray dog before starting vaccine"],"a":1,"e":"Transdermal bites/scratches with bleeding constitute WHO/NRCP Category III exposure. Immediate thorough flushing with soap and running water for at least 15 minutes removes \u003e90% of viral inoculum mechanically/chemically, followed by virucidal antiseptic (povidone-iodine). Primary suturing is avoided (or delayed after RIG infiltration if unavoidable). Category III requires BOTH full ARV course (e.g., 2-site intradermal on Days 0, 3, 7, 28) AND passive immunization with RIG (human or equine/monoclonal) infiltrated directly into all wounds.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00103","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Lymphatic Filariasis Elimination","q":"During an annual Mass Drug Administration (MDA) round for Lymphatic Filariasis elimination using Diethylcarbamazine (DEC) + Albendazole (or IDA: Ivermectin + DEC + Albendazole), which household members MUST be excluded from receiving the MDA tablets?","o":["Pregnant women, children under 2 years of age (under 5 yrs / \u003c15 kg for Ivermectin in IDA), and severely ill/bedridden persons","Healthy school-going children aged 8–12 years","Adult male farmers aged 30–50 years","Elderly ambulant men aged 65 years with no acute illness"],"a":0,"e":"Under NCVBDC Lymphatic Filariasis MDA guidelines, annual directly observed single-dose DEC + Albendazole is given to the entire eligible endemic population EXCEPT pregnant women, children \u003c 2 years old (and \u003c 5 years / \u003c 90 cm / \u003c 15 kg for Ivermectin in triple-drug IDA districts), and seriously ill patients. Women in the 1st week of lactation are also typically deferred per state operational guidance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00104","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Population-Based Screening at HWC/Sub-centre","q":"Under India’s National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD) at Health \u0026 Wellness Centres, from what age does population-based screening for hypertension, diabetes, and oral/breast/cervical cancers begin, and what CBAC score indicates high risk requiring prioritized referral for screening?","o":["Age \u003e= 18 years; CBAC score \u003e 1","Age \u003e= 30 years; CBAC score \u003e 4 (i.e., 5 or higher)","Age \u003e= 50 years; CBAC score \u003e 10","Age \u003e= 60 years; CBAC score \u003e 2"],"a":1,"e":"Under MoHFW NP-NCD (formerly NPCDCS) operational guidelines for Comprehensive Primary Health Care, ASHA administers the Community Based Assessment Checklist (CBAC) for all individuals aged 30 years and above. A CBAC score \u003e 4 flags high NCD risk (smoking, alcohol, waist circumference, physical inactivity, family history, age), prioritizing referral to the ANM/CHO at the Sub-centre HWC for BP, blood glucose, oral visual examination, CBE, and VIA screening.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00105","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Sick Young Infant (0 to 2 Months)","q":"A 10-day-old newborn is brought to the Sub-centre because the mother noticed poor feeding since morning. On IMNCI examination, the baby’s respiratory rate is 66 breaths/minute (confirmed on repeat count), axillary temperature is 35.3°C, and the baby moves only when stimulated. What is the IMNCI classification and urgent action?","o":["Local Bacterial Infection; prescribe oral cotrimoxazole and send home","Normal newborn physiological adaptation; advise routine home follow-up in 7 days","Possible Serious Bacterial Infection (PSBI) or Very Severe Disease; keep baby warm (skin-to-skin), give first dose pre-referral antibiotic if authorized/available, prevent low blood sugar, and refer URGENTLY to hospital","Mild hypothermia only; bathe baby in warm water"],"a":2,"e":"In a young infant (0–2 months), ANY of the following signs classifies PSBI (Pink row): not able to feed/stopped feeding well, convulsions, fast breathing (\u003e= 60 breaths/min), severe chest indrawing, fever (\u003e= 37.5°C) or low body temperature (\u003c 35.5°C), movement only when stimulated or no movement, or bulging fontanelle/umbilical redness extending to skin. This baby has multiple PSBI signs (poor feeding, RR 66, temp 35.3°C, lethargic movement) requiring urgent referral while maintaining warmth (KMC) and preventing hypoglycemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00106","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"WHO Growth Standards on MCP Card","q":"At the Anganwadi Centre during VHSND, a 15-month-old girl’s weight is plotted on her MCP Card WHO Weight-for-Age chart. Her plotted point falls inside the lowest Red/Orange shaded band below the -3 SD curve, and her growth line has remained completely flat for the last 3 months. How should the ANM interpret this finding and counsel/manage?","o":["Overweight; restrict dietary fats immediately","Normal growth for a female child; no intervention needed","Moderately underweight (-2 SD to -3 SD); advise discontinuing solid foods and giving only milk","Severe Underweight (\u003c -3 SD) with growth faltering; assess for acute illness/feeding practices, check MUAC/edema for SAM, refer to Medical Officer/NRC if SAM with complications, and counsel on energy-dense complementary feeding"],"a":3,"e":"On the MCP Card WHO Weight-for-Age chart, the Green band represents normal weight (above -2 SD), the Yellow band represents Moderately Underweight (-2 SD to -3 SD), and the Red/bottom band represents Severely Underweight (\u003c -3 SD). A flat growth line over consecutive months signals growth faltering requiring immediate medical evaluation for underlying infections (TB, chronic diarrhoea), SAM screening (MUAC/weight-for-height), Take-Home Ration/SNP linkage, and intensive IYCF counselling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00107","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Estimated Date of Delivery (EDD)","q":"A pregnant woman with regular 28-day menstrual cycles reports that the first day of her Last Menstrual Period (LMP) was 14 March 2025. Using Naegele’s rule, what is her Estimated Date of Delivery (EDD)?","o":["21 December 2025","7 December 2025","14 December 2025","21 January 2026"],"a":0,"e":"Naegele’s rule calculates EDD for a standard 28-day cycle as: LMP + 9 calendar months + 7 days (equivalently, LMP + 7 days - 3 months + 1 year). Starting from 14 March 2025: 14 + 7 = 21 March 2025; adding 9 months yields 21 December 2025.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00108","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM)","q":"According to MoHFW National Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (single-step DIPSI method), how is GDM screening performed at antenatal clinics regardless of the woman’s fasting state, and what 2-hour plasma glucose value confirms GDM?","o":["Give 50 g oral glucose only after 12-hour fasting; 1-hour plasma glucose \u003e= 130 mg/dL","Give 75 g oral anhydrous glucose dissolved in 300 mL water regardless of last meal timing; 2-hour plasma glucose \u003e= 140 mg/dL","Give 100 g oral glucose fasting; 3-hour plasma glucose \u003e= 180 mg/dL","Check random capillary glucose without glucose load; \u003e= 110 mg/dL"],"a":1,"e":"MoHFW recommends universal single-step GDM testing (first at initial ANC registration and repeated at 24–28 weeks if initially negative): the pregnant woman drinks 75 g oral glucose in ~300 mL water over 5–10 minutes irrespective of when she last ate, and venous plasma glucose is measured at 2 hours. A 2-hour value \u003e= 140 mg/dL diagnoses GDM.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00109","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Essential Newborn Care at Birth","q":"For a vigorous term newborn crying at birth, what is the MoHFW/WHO recommended timing for umbilical cord clamping, and what is its primary physiological benefit?","o":["Immediate clamping within 10 seconds to prevent neonatal polycythemia","Clamping only after the placenta is delivered at 30 minutes","Delayed clamping at 1 to 3 minutes after birth (or until cord pulsations stop) to boost neonatal iron stores and reduce infant anaemia","Milking the cord vigorously toward the placenta before clamping"],"a":2,"e":"Delayed cord clamping (1–3 minutes after delivery while keeping the baby on the mother’s abdomen for skin-to-skin contact) allows ~80–100 mL of placental blood transfusion into the newborn. This improves haematocrit, provides an extra 40–50 mg/kg of iron stores protecting against iron deficiency anaemia through infancy, and supports smoother cardiopulmonary transition.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00110","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Care of Low Birth Weight (LBW) Babies","q":"Assertion (A): Kangaroo Mother Care (KMC) is recommended for all clinically stable low-birth-weight (\u003c 2,500 g) and preterm newborns to prevent hypothermia, promote exclusive breastfeeding, and reduce neonatal infections. Reason (R): In KMC, the newborn is placed naked (wearing only a cap, socks, and nappy) in an upright frog-like skin-to-skin position between the mother’s breasts under her clothing. Choose the correct option:","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"KMC consists of prolonged skin-to-skin contact (upright frog-like position between the mother’s breasts with cap, socks, and diaper so maternal body warmth acts as a natural thermal incubator), exclusive breastfeeding, and early supported discharge/follow-up. This direct thermal conduction, maternal skin colonization with benign flora, and frequent feeding directly achieve the clinical benefits stated in A.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00111","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Spacing Contraceptive Methods","q":"Under India’s National Family Planning Programme (Mission Parivar Vikas), what are the active drugs and dosing schedules of \"Antara\" and \"Chhaya\" respectively?","o":["Antara: Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg single emergency pill; Chhaya: Combined estrogen-progestin daily pill","Antara: Subdermal etonogestrel implant lasting 3 years; Chhaya: Copper T 380A IUCD","Antara: Norethisterone enanthate monthly injection; Chhaya: Mifepristone weekly pill","Antara: Depot Medroxyprogesterone Acetate (DMPA) 150 mg IM injection every 3 months; Chhaya: Centchroman (Ormeloxifene) 30 mg non-hormonal/non-steroidal oral pill (twice weekly for first 3 months, then once weekly)"],"a":3,"e":"Antara is injectable DMPA (150 mg deep IM every 3 months/13 weeks), a reversible progestin-only spacing method safe during breastfeeding (from 6 weeks postpartum). Chhaya is Centchroman (Ormeloxifene 30 mg), an indigenous non-steroidal selective estrogen receptor modulator taken twice weekly for the first 3 months and once weekly thereafter, free of steroidal side effects.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00112","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Sub-centre Annual Action Plan","q":"An ANM is preparing the annual Community Needs Assessment (CNA) microplan for a Sub-centre covering a population of 5,000 in an area with a Crude Birth Rate (CBR) of 20 per 1,000 population. Applying the standard MoHFW 10% pregnancy-wastage correction factor, what are the expected annual numbers of live births and pregnant women in her Sub-centre area?","o":["100 live births and 110 pregnant women","200 live births and 220 pregnant women","100 live births and 100 pregnant women","50 live births and 55 pregnant women"],"a":0,"e":"Expected annual live births = (Population × CBR) / 1,000 = (5,000 × 20) / 1,000 = 100 live births. Under MoHFW RCH/CNA planning norms, 10% is added to expected live births to account for pregnancy wastage (spontaneous/induced abortions and stillbirths): Expected pregnant women = 100 + (10% of 100) = 110 pregnant women.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00113","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Kayakalp and NQAS at Sub-centre / HWC","q":"During a Kayakalp / NQAS (National Quality Assurance Standards) internal assessment of a Sub-centre Health \u0026 Wellness Centre, which practice best satisfies the \"Infection Control and Bio-Medical Waste Management\" thematic area?","o":["Mopping the clinic floor once weekly with plain water only","Preparing fresh 0.5% chlorine/hypochlorite solution daily for blood spill decontamination and visibly displaying BMW colour-coded posters next to covered foot-operated bins","Storing vaccines and packed lunch boxes together inside the ILR","Keeping open lidless dustbins in the labour room and burning plastic syringes behind the Sub-centre building"],"a":1,"e":"Kayakalp and NQAS Sub-centre checklists mandate daily preparation of fresh hypochlorite solution (0.5%–1% for blood spills/high-touch surfaces), foot-operated covered colour-coded BMW bins with bilingual segregation charts, handwashing stations with soap and elbow-operated taps/running water, and authorized CBWTF pickup (or deep burial/sharp pit where CBWTF is unavailable in remote areas—never open burning of plastics).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00114","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Epidemiological Triad and Herd Immunity","q":"In community epidemiology, how does achieving high immunization coverage (herd immunity) protect even the few unvaccinated infants or immunocompromised individuals in a village?","o":["Vaccines transfer live antibodies directly through village drinking water","Herd immunity permanently eliminates the need for cold chain maintenance","A high proportion of immune hosts breaks the chain of person-to-person transmission, markedly reducing the probability of an infectious agent contacting a susceptible host","It transforms viral agents into harmless commensal bacteria"],"a":2,"e":"Herd immunity occurs when a sufficiently large percentage of a population is immune (via vaccination or prior infection), interrupting transmission chains so the basic reproduction number falls below 1 and indirectly shielding susceptible individuals (Note: tetanus is an exception because Clostridium tetani comes from soil, not person-to-person spread).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00115","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Occupational Pneumoconioses in India","q":"Match each occupational lung disease with its characteristic causative dust/exposure in India: 1. Silicosis 2. Byssinosis (\"Monday fever\") 3. Bagassosis 4. Farmer’s lung Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Asbestos fibres - Coal mine dust - Iron ore dust - Lead fumes","Cotton dust - Silica dust - Sugarcane juice - Bird droppings","Coal dust - Mouldy hay - Cotton fibres - Asbestos","Free crystalline silica dust (quarrying/stone cutting/slate pencil) - Cotton textile mill dust - Mouldy sugarcane fibre (Thermoactinomyces sacchari) - Mouldy hay/grain dust (Micropolyspora faeni)"],"a":3,"e":"(1) Silicosis results from inhaling free silica in mining, stone crushing, and slate-pencil industries (strongly predisposing to silico-tuberculosis); (2) Byssinosis affects cotton gin/textile workers with chest tightness worst on Monday mornings; (3) Bagassosis is hypersensitivity pneumonitis from mouldy bagasse (crushed sugarcane); (4) Farmer’s lung arises from thermophilic actinomycetes in damp stored hay/fodder.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00116","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS)","q":"Under the Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS / Saksham Anganwadi) Supplementary Nutrition Programme, what are the daily calorie and protein allowances provided to a moderately malnourished/normal child aged 6–72 months versus a severely underweight child?","o":["Normal/general child (6–72 months): 500 kcal \u0026 12–15 g protein; Severely underweight child: 800 kcal \u0026 20–25 g protein","Only pregnant women receive supplementary calories under ICDS","Normal/general child: 100 kcal \u0026 2 g protein; Severely underweight child: 250 kcal \u0026 5 g protein","Normal/general child: 1,200 kcal \u0026 40 g protein; Severely underweight child: 500 kcal \u0026 10 g protein"],"a":0,"e":"ICDS revised supplementary nutrition norms provide 500 kcal and 12–15 g protein/day for children 6–72 months (via Take-Home Ration for 6–36 months and hot cooked meal/morning snack for 3–6 years), 800 kcal and 20–25 g protein/day for severely underweight children, and 600 kcal and 18–20 g protein/day for pregnant and lactating mothers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00117","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Kwashiorkor vs Marasmus","q":"During a community nutrition survey, an ANM examines a 2-year-old child who was weaned early onto dilute starchy gruel. The child is apathetic and irritable, with bilateral pitting pedal edema, puffy moon face, sparse hypopigmented hair (\"flag sign\"), and peeling \"flaky-paint\" skin lesions over the thighs. Which form of Severe Acute Malnutrition is present?","o":["Marasmus","Kwashiorkor","Scurvy","Rickets"],"a":1,"e":"Kwashiorkor (oedematous malnutrition) arises from severe protein/micronutrient deficiency with relatively preserved carbohydrate calories, presenting with bilateral pitting edema, moon face, apathy/anorexia, fatty hepatomegaly, hair dyspigmentation (\"flag sign\"), and \"flaky-paint\" dermatosis. In contrast, Marasmus shows extreme muscle and subcutaneous fat wasting (\"skin and bones\", baggy pants, monkey-like facies) WITHOUT edema and usually with a preserved appetite.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00118","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Environmental Sanitation)","topic":"Water Quality Surveillance","q":"One hour after chlorinating a village drinking water source, an ANM adds 0.1 mL of Orthotolidine (OT) reagent to 1 mL of the water sample in a comparator tube and reads the yellow colour immediately within 10 seconds. What minimum free residual chlorine level should be present for safe potable drinking water?","o":["5.0 mg/L (ppm)","10.0 mg/L (ppm)","At least 0.5 mg/L (ppm) after 1 hour contact period","0.0 mg/L (completely absent)"],"a":2,"e":"In the Orthotolidine (OT) test, yellow colour appearing within 10 seconds measures FREE residual chlorine (reading after 15–20 minutes measures combined + free chlorine; OTA differentiates interfering nitrites/iron). For safe drinking water, minimum free residual chlorine must be \u003e= 0.5 mg/L after a minimum contact time of 1 hour (and \u003e= 2 mg/L at swimming pools or during cholera outbreaks as specified).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00119","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Environmental Sanitation)","topic":"Medically Important Mosquito Vectors in India","q":"Match each mosquito genus with its primary breeding habitat, biting time, and transmitted disease in India: 1. Anopheles (e.g., A. culicifacies / A. stephensi) 2. Aedes aegypti 3. Culex quinquefasciatus / C. tritaeniorhynchus Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3):","o":["Dirty sewage drains; daytime biter; Dengue - Clean streams; night biter; Malaria - Artificial coolers; night biter; Kala-azar","Fast flowing rivers; night biter; Onchocerciasis - Brackish coastal water; noon biter; Plague - Tree holes; night biter; Typhus","Paddy fields; daytime biter; Chikungunya - Dirty drains; night biter; Malaria - Clean tanks; daytime biter; Filariasis","Clean sunlit water/overhead tanks; night biter; Malaria - Small artificial clean-water containers (coolers, tyres, flower pots); daytime biter; Dengue \u0026 Chikungunya - Dirty stagnant drains/cesspools or paddy fields; night biter; Bancroftian Filariasis \u0026 Japanese Encephalitis"],"a":3,"e":"Vector-specific source reduction relies on ecology: Anopheles breeds in clean water collections/wells/tanks and bites at night (Malaria); Aedes aegypti (\"tiger mosquito\") breeds in small domestic artificial clean-water containers and bites predominantly during the daytime (Dengue, Chikungunya, Zika); Culex breeds in polluted drains (C. quinquefasciatus -\u003e Lymphatic Filariasis) and irrigated rice paddies (C. tritaeniorhynchus -\u003e JE).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00120","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Delayed / Catch-up Vaccination under UIP","q":"A previously unimmunized 18-month-old child is brought to the Sub-centre immunization session for the very first time. According to Government of India UIP catch-up guidelines, which vaccines CAN still be administered at 18 months of age, and which are omitted due to exceeding the upper age limit?","o":["Give MR-1, DPT-1, OPV-1, and Vitamin A (2 lakh IU); OMIT BCG, Pentavalent, and Rotavirus because their UIP upper age limit is 1 year (12 months)","Give BCG, Pentavalent-1, and Rotavirus-1 immediately; omit MR and DPT","Give only adult Td vaccine and send home","Refuse all vaccines because the child missed the 6-week schedule"],"a":0,"e":"Under UIP India upper age limits: BCG, Pentavalent, Rotavirus, fIPV, and PCV are generally given up to 1 year of age (if first dose of Penta/PCV/Rota was started \u003c1 yr, remaining doses can be completed up to 1 yr or per state catch-up rules for Penta/PCV, whereas an unimmunized child presenting at 18 months does NOT start BCG, Pentavalent, or Rotavirus). Instead, give DPT-1, OPV-1, MR-1, JE-1 (in endemic districts), and Vitamin A (2 mL), followed by DPT-2/OPV-2 after 4 weeks and MR-2 after 4 weeks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00121","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Intensified Mission Indradhanush (IMI) \u0026 U-WIN","q":"In UIP programme monitoring and Intensified Mission Indradhanush (IMI) microplanning, what is the operational distinction between a \"left-out\" child and a \"drop-out\" child?","o":["A \"left-out\" child never received even a single vaccine dose (zero-dose), whereas a \"drop-out\" child started vaccination (e.g., BCG/Penta-1) but failed to complete subsequent due doses","A \"left-out\" child lives in urban slums, whereas a \"drop-out\" child refuses oral polio drops only","Both terms mean children above 10 years of age","A \"drop-out\" child received all vaccines ahead of schedule"],"a":0,"e":"Tracking left-outs (unreached/zero-dose children who never entered the immunization system, often in migratory/brick-kiln/hard-to-reach settlements) and drop-outs (children who initiated vaccination but missed later visits due to migration, AEFI fear, or session cancellations) using ASHA due-lists and the U-WIN digital portal is central to achieving \u003e90% full immunization coverage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00122","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Acute Diarrhoeal Outbreak (Cholera)","q":"Four adults from the same hamlet present to a Sub-centre with sudden profuse, painless watery \"rice-water\" stools and severe dehydration. While initiating urgent ORS/referral for IV Ringer’s Lactate and alerting the PHC Medical Officer under IDSP, which transport medium is used to send a rectal swab/stool specimen to the district lab for Vibrio cholerae confirmation?","o":["10% Neutral Buffered Formalin","Cary-Blair transport medium or Venkatraman-Ramakrishnan (VR) medium / Alkaline Peptone Water","Lowenstein-Jensen (LJ) slant","Sabouraud dextrose broth"],"a":1,"e":"Suspected cholera outbreaks require immediate notification under the Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (IDSP), aggressive rehydration (Ringer’s Lactate IV / ORS), antimicrobial therapy for severe cases (doxycycline/azithromycin per MO), superchlorination of village water, and stool/rectal swab transport in alkaline/holding media such as Venkatraman-Ramakrishnan (VR), Cary-Blair, or Alkaline Peptone Water (APW).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00123","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Faecal-Oral Disease Prevention","q":"During community health education after two cases of Hepatitis A and Typhoid fever in a village, the ANM illustrates the \"5 Fs\" of faecal-oral disease transmission. Which list correctly names the five pathways?","o":["Fever, Fatigue, Fasting, Fungi, and Fomites","Follicles, Fluids, Fractures, Fats, and Fibres","Fluids (water), Fingers, Flies, Fields (soil/open defecation), and Food","Fishes, Forests, Fog, Fodder, and Fire"],"a":2,"e":"The classic sanitation \"5 Fs\" diagram maps how pathogens from human faeces reach a new host via Fluids (contaminated water), Fingers (unwashed hands), Flies (vectors landing on faeces then food), Fields (open defecation contaminating soil/crops), and Food. Primary barriers (sanitary latrines, handwashing with soap) and secondary barriers (safe water chlorination, covered food) break every pathway.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00124","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"NACO STI/RTI Syndromic Case Management","q":"Match each STI/RTI syndrome under NACO Syndromic Management with its designated colour-coded pre-packaged drug kit: 1. Urethral / Cervical / Anorectal Discharge (Azithromycin 1 g + Cefixime 400 mg) 2. Vaginal Discharge / Vaginitis (Secnidazole 2 g + Fluconazole 150 mg) 3. Non-herpetic Genital Ulcer Disease (Benzathine Penicillin + Azithromycin) 4. Lower Abdominal Pain / PID in females (Cefixime + Metronidazole + Doxycycline) Select the correct kit colour sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Kit 2 (Green) - Kit 3 (White) - Kit 7 (Black) - Kit 1 (Grey)","Kit 6 (Yellow) - Kit 1 (Grey) - Kit 5 (Red) - Kit 2 (Green)","Kit 4 (Blue) - Kit 5 (Red) - Kit 1 (Grey) - Kit 3 (White)","Kit 1 (Grey) - Kit 2 (Green) - Kit 3 (White) - Kit 6 (Yellow)"],"a":3,"e":"NACO colour-coded STI/RTI kits simplify syndromic therapy: Kit 1 (Grey) = Urethral/Cervical/Anorectal discharge; Kit 2 (Green) = Vaginitis/Vaginal discharge; Kit 3 (White) = Non-herpetic Genital Ulcer (penicillin-tolerant); Kit 4 (Blue) = Non-herpetic Genital Ulcer (penicillin-allergic: Doxycycline + Azithromycin); Kit 5 (Red) = Herpetic Genital Ulcer (Acyclovir); Kit 6 (Yellow) = Lower Abdominal Pain (PID); Kit 7 (Black) = Inguinal Bubo.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00125","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Kala-azar (Visceral Leishmaniasis) Elimination","q":"In Kala-azar (Visceral Leishmaniasis) endemic districts of Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal, and Uttar Pradesh, which rapid diagnostic test is used in the field for fever \u003e 2 weeks with splenomegaly, and what is the NCVBDC first-line treatment?","o":["rK39 immunochromatographic rapid dipstick test; single-dose intravenous Liposomal Amphotericin B (AmBisome, 10 mg/kg)","Mantoux skin test; 6 months of HRZE","NS1 antigen ELISA; oral paracetamol only","Widal slide test; oral chloramphenicol for 14 days"],"a":0,"e":"Under India’s Kala-azar Elimination Programme (transmitted by sandfly Phlebotomus argentipes), patients with fever \u003e 14 days and splenomegaly (not responding to antimalarials/antibiotics) are screened using the rK39 rapid dipstick test. Confirmed primary Kala-azar is treated at PHC/CHC/DH with a single IV infusion of Liposomal Amphotericin B (10 mg/kg), alongside indoor residual spraying (synthetic pyrethroids) for sandfly control.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00126","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Heat-Related Illnesses","q":"During an intense May heatwave, a 55-year-old construction labourer is brought to the Sub-centre unconscious with hot, dry, flushed skin, rectal/core temperature 41.2°C, pulse 134/min, and BP 88/58 mmHg. What is the diagnosis and the immediate pre-referral emergency intervention?","o":["Hypoglycaemia; give oral glucose powder without cooling","Classic/Exertional Heat Stroke; move to cool shaded area, remove excess outer clothing, initiate rapid evaporative/conductive cooling (mist/sponge with cool water + fanning, ice packs to neck, axillae, and groin) and arrange urgent referral","Heat cramps; give oral salt tablets to the unconscious patient","Mild heat exhaustion; wrap in heavy blankets to induce sweating"],"a":1,"e":"Heat stroke is a medical emergency defined by core hyperthermia (\u003e 40°C / 104°F) accompanied by central nervous system dysfunction (delirium, seizures, or coma) due to thermoregulatory failure. Immediate rapid cooling (\"cool first, transport second\" or cool continuously during transport) lowers mortality by preventing multi-organ failure. Never force oral fluids in an unconscious patient.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00127","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Assessment of Ear Problems","q":"A 3-year-old child is brought with right ear pain and fever for 4 days. On IMNCI examination, there is a firm, tender swelling directly behind the right ear (over the mastoid bone) pushing the pinna forward. How is this child classified and managed under IMNCI?","o":["Chronic Ear Infection; teach mother dry wicking of ear with clean cotton cloth","Acute Ear Infection; send home with ear drops only","Mastoiditis (Pink row); give first dose of appropriate antibiotic, give paracetamol for pain, and refer URGENTLY to hospital","No Ear Infection; reassure mother"],"a":2,"e":"In the IMNCI Ear Problem chart, ANY tender swelling behind the ear classifies Mastoiditis (Pink urgent referral row) because bacterial infection has spread into the mastoid air cells, risking meningitis or brain abscess. Give first-dose antibiotic and paracetamol, then refer urgently for IV antibiotics/surgical drainage. Dry wicking + oral amoxicillin is for acute (\u003c14 days) or chronic (\u003e=14 days) ear discharge without mastoid swelling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00128","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK)","q":"India’s Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK) screens all children from birth to 18 years (at delivery points, Anganwadi centres, and government/aided schools) for \"4 Ds\". Which option correctly lists the 4 Ds of RBSK?","o":["Drug allergy, Dysplasia, Dwarfism, and Deafness only","Dehydration, Dengue, Diabetes, and Diphtheria","Diarrhoea, Dermatitis, Dental caries, and Dysentery","Defects at birth, Deficiencies, Diseases, and Development delays including disability"],"a":3,"e":"RBSK targets early identification and free management (via District Early Intervention Centres - DEIC) of 30+ selected health conditions grouped under the 4 Ds: (1) Defects at birth (e.g., neural tube defects, cleft lip/palate, congenital heart disease, clubfoot); (2) Deficiencies (SAM, severe anaemia, Vitamin A/D deficiencies); (3) Childhood Diseases (skin, otitis media, rheumatic heart disease, reactive airway); and (4) Development delays including disability (vision, hearing, neuromotor, speech/autism).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00129","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Antenatal Abdominal Examination","q":"During an antenatal check-up of a woman with a singleton pregnancy and accurate dates, at which weeks of gestation does the uterine fundus normally reach (a) the level of the pubic symphysis, (b) the level of the umbilicus, and (c) the xiphisternum (xiphoid process)?","o":["6 weeks (pubic symphysis), 16 weeks (umbilicus), 30 weeks (xiphisternum)","12 weeks (just palpable above pubic symphysis), 24 weeks (at umbilicus), 36 weeks (at xiphisternum)","20 weeks (pubic symphysis), 28 weeks (umbilicus), 40 weeks (xiphisternum)","12 weeks (umbilicus), 24 weeks (xiphisternum), 36 weeks (pelvic cavity)"],"a":1,"e":"Standard obstetric anatomical landmarks for uterine fundal height in a normal singleton pregnancy: 12 weeks = just palpable abdominally above the symphysis pubis; 16 weeks = midway between symphysis and umbilicus; 20–24 weeks = at the lower/upper border of the umbilicus (classic teaching: 24 weeks at umbilicus, 20 weeks slightly below); 36 weeks = reaches the xiphisternum (and may descend slightly by 38–40 weeks due to lightening).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00130","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Anaemia Mukt Bharat Therapeutic Regimen","q":"At her 22-week ANC visit, a pregnant woman’s haemoglobin tested by digital haemoglobinometer is 9.2 g/dL (moderate anaemia: Hb 7.0–9.9 g/dL). Under Anaemia Mukt Bharat (AMB) guidelines, what is the recommended therapeutic oral IFA dosage, and when should her haemoglobin be re-evaluated?","o":["Two red IFA tablets daily (total 120 mg elemental iron + 1,000 mcg folic acid/day) taken with citrus/lemon water away from tea/milk, and recheck Hb after 1 month (if \u003c 1 g/dL rise or intolerance, refer for IV Iron Sucrose)","One IFA tablet (60 mg elemental iron) once weekly; recheck at delivery","Folic acid alone without iron","Immediate packed red blood cell transfusion at the Sub-centre"],"a":0,"e":"Per AMB/MoHFW guidelines for pregnant women with mild (Hb 10.0–10.9 g/dL) or moderate anaemia (Hb 7.0–9.9 g/dL), therapeutic dosing is 2 IFA tablets daily (120 mg elemental iron total, e.g., 1 after morning and 1 after evening meal or away from calcium/tea/coffee phytates), with monthly Hb monitoring. Expected response is \u003e= 1 g/dL rise in 1 month; poor compliance/response or moderate anaemia presenting \u003e34 weeks warrants referral to PHC/FRU for parenteral IV Iron Sucrose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00131","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Pre-Referral Stabilization of Postpartum Haemorrhage (PPH)","q":"Twenty minutes after placental delivery at a PHC/Sub-centre delivery point, a mother begins bleeding heavily (\u003e 500 mL), with a soft boggy uterus above the umbilicus, pulse 118/min, and BP 92/60 mmHg. While alerting the Medical Officer and arranging emergency FRU transfer, which combination of pre-referral actions must the ANM/SBA perform immediately?","o":["Apply an ice pack to the feet and restrict IV fluids","Perform vigorous uterine massage, empty the urinary bladder (catheterize if full), administer Oxytocin 10 IU IM (plus IV Oxytocin infusion / Misoprostol 800 mcg sublingually per protocol), start rapid IV Ringer’s Lactate/NS infusion, and apply bimanual uterine compression or NASG if available","Give oral ergometrine only and wait 2 hours before starting an IV line","Pack the vagina tightly with dry cotton rags and keep the mother standing"],"a":1,"e":"Uterine atony accounts for ~80% of primary PPH. Immediate SBA/ANM emergency stabilization includes: shouting for help, continuous uterine massage to stimulate contraction, emptying a full bladder (which displaces the uterus and prevents contraction), uterotonics (Oxytocin 10 IU IM / 20 IU in 500 mL RL IV infusion; or Misoprostol 800 mcg sublingually when IV oxytocin is unavailable), two wide-bore IV lines with crystalloid resuscitation, bimanual uterine compression/Non-Pneumatic Anti-Shock Garment (NASG) during transport, and checking for retained placenta/cervical tears.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00132","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Postnatal Breast Complications","q":"On postnatal Day 10, a breastfeeding mother presents with a painful, hot, swollen, wedge-shaped erythematous area on the outer quadrant of her left breast, fever of 38.6°C, and chills. There is no fluctuant lump. Along with referring to the Medical Officer for oral antibiotics (cloxacillin/cephalexin) and analgesics, what crucial breastfeeding advice should the ANM give?","o":["Stop breastfeeding from both breasts permanently and bind the breasts tightly","Continue frequent breastfeeding (starting on the affected breast or expressing milk gently if too painful) to ensure complete breast emptying, plus warm compresses before feeds","Apply cold cabbage leaves and discard all milk for 14 days","Incise the breast at home with a sterile blade"],"a":1,"e":"Unilateral localized redness, warmth, tenderness, and systemic fever indicate acute lactational mastitis (usually Staphylococcus aureus from cracked nipples and milk stasis). Effective milk removal via continued frequent breastfeeding (or gentle expression) is essential alongside antibiotics to prevent progression to a breast abscess; milk from a mastitic breast without an open draining abscess is safe for the term infant.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00133","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Natural and Emergency Contraception","q":"Assertion (A): The Lactational Amenorrhoea Method (LAM) provides \u003e 98% contraceptive protection ONLY when all three conditions are simultaneously met: the mother’s menses have not returned, the baby is exclusively/fully breastfed day and night, and the infant is less than 6 months old. Reason (R): Once the baby reaches 6 months of age, begins complementary foods, or maternal menstruation resumes, ovulation may precede bleeding and the mother must immediately transition to another modern contraceptive method. Choose the correct option:","o":["Both A and R are true, and R reinforces the clinical follow-up principle of A","Both A and R are true, but R contradicts A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Frequent suckling (every 4 hours by day and 6 hours at night) suppresses pulsatile GnRH and LH surge via high prolactin, inhibiting ovulation—making LAM \u003e98% effective if all 3 criteria hold: (1) Amenorrhoea; (2) Exclusive breastfeeding; (3) Infant \u003c 6 months. Breaking any single criterion requires immediate transition to an alternative method (e.g., PPIUCD/interval IUCD, Antara DMPA, Chhaya, POPs, or condoms).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00134","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Maternal Death Surveillance and Response (MDSR)","q":"Under the Government of India’s Maternal Death Surveillance and Response (MDSR) guidelines, within what timeframe must an ANM/ASHA notify a community maternal death (of a woman aged 15–49 years during pregnancy, delivery, or within 42 days postpartum) to the Primary Informant / Block Medical Officer?","o":["Only at the end of the financial year during annual audit","Only if the death occurred inside a tertiary medical college hospital","Within 24 hours of learning of the death (followed by line-listing within 7 days and verbal autopsy within 3 weeks)","After 6 months once the birth register is closed"],"a":2,"e":"MDSR aims to count every maternal death (both community and facility), identify medical and social delays (Delay 1: decision to seek care; Delay 2: reaching facility; Delay 3: receiving adequate care), and institute corrective action without punitive blame. Community maternal deaths must be notified within 24 hours to the MO/District Nodal Officer so Community-Based MDR (verbal autopsy) can be completed promptly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00135","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Immunization Dropout Rate Calculation","q":"In a Sub-centre’s annual HMIS immunization review, 120 infants received Pentavalent-1st dose during the year, and 102 infants completed Pentavalent-3rd dose. Calculate the Penta-1 to Penta-3 dropout rate and state whether it falls within the acceptable UIP quality benchmark (\u003c 10%).","o":["85.0%; acceptable","10.0%; acceptable","17.6%; acceptable","15.0%; unacceptable (exceeds the \u003c 10% benchmark, indicating gaps in tracking/follow-up)"],"a":3,"e":"Immunization Dropout Rate (%) = [(Doses of first vaccine given - Doses of subsequent vaccine given) / Doses of first vaccine given] × 100. Here, [(120 - 102) / 120] × 100 = (18 / 120) × 100 = 15.0%. Under UIP monitoring standards, a dropout rate \u003e= 10% signals poor retention/follow-up or service quality issues requiring ASHA due-list tracking.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00136","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Community Health Centre (CHC) Norms","q":"Under Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS), what is the standard population coverage of one Community Health Centre (CHC — 30-bedded rural referral hospital) in plain areas versus hilly/tribal areas?","o":["1,20,000 in plain areas and 80,000 in hilly/tribal/difficult areas (serving as referral centre for ~4 PHCs)","5,000 in plain and 3,000 in hilly/tribal areas","10,00,000 in plain and 5,00,000 in hilly/tribal areas","30,000 in plain and 20,000 in hilly/tribal areas"],"a":0,"e":"A Community Health Centre (CHC) forms the secondary rural block-level referral tier serving 1,20,000 population in plain areas and 80,000 in hilly/tribal/difficult areas (~4 PHCs). Equipped with 30 beds, operating theatre, labour room, X-ray, lab, and blood storage, an FRU-designated CHC provides Comprehensive Emergency Obstetric and Newborn Care (CEmOC) via core specialists (Obstetrician-Gynaecologist, Paediatrician, General Surgeon, Physician/Anaesthetist).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00137","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Community Health Nursing","topic":"LaQshya and SUMAN Initiatives","q":"Which practices uphold MoHFW\u0027s LaQshya (Labour Room Quality Improvement Initiative) and SUMAN (Surakshit Matritva Aashwasan) standards for every woman delivering in a public health facility?","o":["Charging informal fees for clean delivery sheets","Ensuring dignified, respectful maternity care with zero tolerance for abuse or service denial, allowing a chosen female birth companion during labour/delivery, maintaining privacy, and providing zero-expense cashless care","Refusing admission at night if the mother arrives late in labour","Shouting at labouring mothers, banning all female birth companions, and performing routine episiotomy on every multipara"],"a":1,"e":"MoHFW\u0027s LaQshya and SUMAN programmes institutionalize Respectful Maternity Care (RMC): zero denial of care, zero out-of-pocket expenses, strict prohibition of verbal/physical mistreatment, visual/auditory privacy, freedom of labour positioning, and presence of a supportive birth companion (who significantly reduces maternal anxiety and C-section rates) alongside evidence-based clinical safety.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00138","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Retained Placenta (\u003e 30 Minutes)","q":"At a Sub-Centre delivery, a healthy newborn was born 35 minutes ago and 10 IU IM oxytocin was given within 1 minute, yet the placenta has NOT delivered despite gentle controlled cord traction and bladder emptying. What must the ANM do now?","o":["Pull forcefully on the umbilical cord with both hands until it snaps or pulls out","Recognize Retained Placenta (\u003e 30 minutes), do NOT pull forcefully on the cord or push on an uncontracted uterus, start an IV Ringer\u0027s Lactate line, monitor vitals/bleeding, and arrange immediate ambulance referral to an FRU for manual removal of placenta under anaesthesia","Wait 12 hours at the Sub-Centre without referral","Insert unsterile bare hands to scrape the uterine wall without anaesthesia"],"a":1,"e":"Failure of placental expulsion within 30 minutes after delivery despite AMTSL defines Retained Placenta, carrying high risk of torrential postpartum haemorrhage and puerperal sepsis. Forceful traction on an unseparated placenta risks cord avulsion or lethal uterine inversion, and unsterile manual exploration without anaesthesia causes neurogenic shock/perforation. Start IV fluids and refer immediately to an FRU/CEmOC.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00139","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Community Health","topic":"Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (PMSMA)","q":"Under India\u0027s Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (PMSMA), on which fixed monthly date do Medical Officers/OBG specialists provide assured comprehensive antenatal screening, and how are identified high-risk pregnancies flagged on the MCP card?","o":["Only once a year on 15 August; no colour sticker used","On Sundays only for postpartum mothers","On the 9th of every month for 2nd/3rd trimester pregnant women; identified high-risk pregnancies are flagged with a RED sticker on the Mother and Child Protection (MCP) card for prioritized follow-up and FRU birth planning","On the 1st of every month; flagged with a Green sticker"],"a":2,"e":"PMSMA guarantees free specialist/Medical Officer quality ANC check-ups, lab/ultrasound investigations, and high-risk pregnancy identification on the 9th of every month (next working day if holiday) across public facilities for all 2nd/3rd trimester pregnant women. High-risk cases (severe anaemia, PIH, GDM, previous C-section, malpresentation, twin gestation) receive a Red sticker on their MCP card (Green sticker = normal) ensuring close ANM/ASHA tracking and planned FRU delivery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00140","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"PCV and Rotavirus Infant Schedule","q":"Under India\u0027s Universal Immunization Programme (UIP) schedule, at which ages are the three doses of Pneumococcal Conjugate Vaccine (PCV — 0.5 mL IM right anterolateral mid-thigh) and the three doses of oral Rotavirus vaccine administered?","o":["PCV at birth, 6 weeks, and 10 weeks; Rotavirus at 5 years","PCV at 6 weeks, 14 weeks, and 9 months (booster); Rotavirus orally at 6 weeks, 10 weeks, and 14 weeks","Both given only as a single dose at 16–24 months","PCV given orally; Rotavirus injected intramuscularly"],"a":1,"e":"Under India\u0027s UIP nationwide schedule, PCV protects against pneumococcal pneumonia and meningitis in a 2+1 schedule (two primary doses at 6 and 14 weeks plus booster at 9 completed months—0.5 mL IM in the right anterolateral mid-thigh, opposite side to Pentavalent in the left thigh). Oral Rotavirus vaccine is administered in three doses at 6, 10, and 14 weeks to prevent severe dehydrating infant rotavirus diarrhoea.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00141","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"PHC Power Failure Protocol","q":"At a cold-chain OSPE station, grid electricity fails at a Primary Health Centre at 10:00 AM and the backup generator malfunctions. What is the ANM/cold-chain handler\u0027s immediate best practice for the Ice-Lined Refrigerator (ILR) containing UIP vaccines?","o":["Prop the ILR lid wide open immediately to let warm air circulate around vials","Move all Pentavalent vials into the deep freezer right away","Pour tap water directly inside the ILR cabinet","Keep the ILR lid tightly closed (an unopened ILR maintains +2°C to +8°C holdover for ~24–48+ hours depending on ambient heat), monitor external temperature closely, and if outage approaches holdover limits or temp nears +8°C, transfer vaccines into conditioned cold boxes with ice packs and alert the DIO"],"a":3,"e":"An Ice-Lined Refrigerator (ILR) has water/ice-lined internal walls that act as a thermal battery during power cuts—maintaining +2°C to +8°C for 24–72 hours if the lid stays tightly shut! Opening the lid repeatedly drains cold air rapidly. Only if outage persists and internal temperature threatens to rise above +8°C should vaccines be shifted to conditioned cold boxes and reported to the District Immunization Officer.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00142","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care Nursing","topic":"True vs False Contraindications to Vaccination","q":"A mother brings her 10-week-old infant to an outreach session for Pentavalent-2 and OPV-2, mentioning the baby had mild watery nose (common cold), low-grade fever (37.6°C), and took oral amoxicillin yesterday for a mild ear infection, though the baby is alert and feeding well today. What should the ANM do?","o":["Vaccinate the infant as scheduled today—mild upper respiratory illness, low-grade fever, mild diarrhoea, and current oral antibiotic use are FALSE contraindications (reserve deferral only for severe acute illness needing hospitalization or prior anaphylaxis)","Give only half the Pentavalent dose","Defer all vaccines for 3 months because mild cold and antibiotic use are absolute contraindications","Advise stopping breastfeeding before giving OPV"],"a":0,"e":"Avoiding false contraindications eliminates missed vaccination opportunities. Under WHO and India UIP guidelines, mild afebrile/low-grade-fever illnesses (mild URI, mild diarrhoea), current antibiotic therapy, malnutrition, or prematurity (once medically stable) do NOT impair vaccine safety or seroconversion. Only severe life-threatening anaphylaxis to a previous dose/component (or acute severe febrile hospitalization) warrants deferral.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00143","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing (ANM)","topic":"Neonatal Omphalitis (Umbilical Sepsis)","q":"During a Day 6 HBNC visit, the ANM inspects a newborn\u0027s umbilical cord stump and notes bright red swelling extending \u003e 1 cm onto the surrounding abdominal skin, thick foul-smelling pus at the base, and mild lethargy. What is the ANM\u0027s priority action?","o":["Reassure the family that pus and widening skin redness around the cord are normal separation signs","Classify as umbilical omphalitis / serious neonatal infection, gently wipe excess outer pus with clean sterile gauze without applying traditional ash/oil, maintain warmth, and refer urgently to PHC/SNCU for systemic IV antibiotics","Apply hot cow-ghee and turmeric powder over the red skin","Wait until Day 14 to see if the redness spreads further"],"a":1,"e":"While slight moisture at the stump base before separation can occur, pus accompanied by peri-umbilical skin erythema/induration extending \u003e 1 cm beyond the cord base indicates omphalitis—which can spread rapidly along the umbilical vein to cause portal vein thrombophlebitis, necrotizing fasciitis, and fatal neonatal sepsis. Immediate SNCU/FRU referral for parenteral antibiotics is mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00144","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Family Planning","topic":"Postpartum IUCD (PPIUCD) Insertion Windows","q":"A healthy woman who delivered vaginally 4 days ago (96 hours postpartum) requests immediate insertion of a Cu-T 380A (PPIUCD) during her postnatal visit. According to MoHFW Postpartum Family Planning (PPFP) guidelines, what should the ANM advise?","o":["Insert the PPIUCD immediately at Day 4 anyway","Tell her she can never use an IUCD for the rest of her life","Do NOT insert an IUCD between 48 hours and 6 weeks postpartum due to elevated risks of uterine perforation, infection, and expulsion; provide interim condoms/LAM/progestin-only contraception and schedule safe interval IUCD insertion at 6 weeks postpartum","Advise combined high-dose oestrogen pills immediately on Day 4 while exclusively breastfeeding"],"a":2,"e":"Under MoHFW PPFP guidelines, Postpartum IUCD insertion is safe ONLY within three specific early windows: postplacental (within 10 minutes), intracaesarean, or immediate postpartum within 48 hours of birth. Between 48 hours and 6 weeks (4–6 weeks involution period), soft involuting myometrium increases perforation, infection, and expulsion rates—so insertion is deferred until 6 weeks postpartum while offering safe interim spacing (LAM/condoms/POP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00145","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Exclusive Breastfeeding for 6 Months","q":"During peak summer heat (44°C), a grandmother insists on giving spoons of plain boiled water and honey (\u0027janam ghutti\u0027) to a healthy 2-month-old exclusively breastfed infant. How should the ANM counsel the family?","o":["Agree that extra plain water is mandatory every hour in summer","Give honey water only after every breastfeed","Give 200 mL diluted cow\u0027s milk twice daily","Explain that breastmilk is ~88% water and completely meets ALL hydration and nutritional needs even in extreme summer heat; giving water, honey, or ghutti before 6 months displaces breastmilk, risks fatal diarrhoeal infection, and honey risks infant botulism"],"a":3,"e":"Exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 completed months (180 days) means zero other liquids or solids—not even plain water—except prescribed medicines/ORS/vitamins. Foremilk is naturally water-rich (~88% water) and quenches thirst completely during hot summers when breastfed frequently on demand. Introducing water or prelacteal honey/ghutti introduces gastrointestinal pathogens, suppresses maternal milk supply, and honey can harbour Clostridium botulinum spores causing infant botulism.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00146","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS)","q":"Which primary beneficiary groups receive Supplementary Nutrition Programme (SNP) rations/meals, growth monitoring, and health referrals through the Anganwadi Centre under India\u0027s Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS / Saksham Anganwadi)?","o":["Children aged 0–6 years (with extra calorie-protein norms for severely underweight/SAM children), pregnant women, and lactating mothers (plus adolescent girls under targeted schemes)","Hospitalized surgical patients exclusively","Adult male factory workers aged 40–60 years only","Retired pensioners over 70 years"],"a":0,"e":"ICDS (Saksham Anganwadi \u0026 POSHAN 2.0) targets the critical early-childhood and maternal window—providing supplementary nutrition (~500 kcal/12–15 g protein for children 6–72 months; ~800 kcal/20–25 g protein for severely underweight children; ~600 kcal/18–20 g protein for pregnant and lactating women), preschool non-formal education, nutrition counselling, and convergence with ANM/ASHA at monthly VHSNDs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00147","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Chikungunya Outbreak Management","q":"Eight residents in an urban ward develop abrupt high fever, maculopapular rash, and severe crippling pain and swelling in the small joints of wrists, fingers, and ankles (making walking difficult), alongside Aedes mosquito breeding in uncovered rooftop tanks. Until co-circulating dengue fever is excluded by lab testing, which analgesic rule is safest?","o":["Prescribe 14 days of primaquine to every case","Suspect Chikungunya fever (Aedes-borne alphavirus); use paracetamol and hydration initially while avoiding aspirin/NSAIDs until dengue is ruled out, notify IDSP/MO, and implement weekly Aedes dry-day larval elimination","Start high-dose aspirin 1,000 mg immediately in every febrile case","Isolate patients inside dark rooms without fluids"],"a":1,"e":"Both Dengue and Chikungunya are transmitted by day-biting Aedes mosquitoes and frequently co-circulate during Indian monsoons; Chikungunya characteristically causes prominent symmetrical small-joint polyarthralgia/arthritis that can persist weeks to months. Because acute dengue cannot be excluded clinically on Day 1–2, avoiding aspirin/ibuprofen/diclofenac initially and using paracetamol + fluids prevents catastrophic GI bleeding if the patient actually has dengue thrombocytopenia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00148","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Sanitation Barrier \u0026 Safe Latrine Siting","q":"Under rural environmental sanitation standards (Park\u0027s PSM / Swachh Bharat Mission), what is the minimum safe horizontal distance required between a sanitary pit latrine and a drinking-water well or handpump (placed downhill from the water source) to maintain an effective \u0027Sanitation Barrier\u0027?","o":["1 metre (3 feet)","Right next to the well wall for convenience","At least 15 metres (50 feet), sited at a lower elevation than the drinking-water source","5 metres (15 feet)"],"a":2,"e":"The \u0027Sanitation Barrier\u0027 segregates human faeces from soil, water, flies, and fingers to halt cholera, typhoid, hepatitis A/E, polio, and helminth transmission. A properly constructed sanitary latrine (such as the rural RCA water-seal twin-pit pour-flush latrine) should be located at least 15 metres (50 feet) away from—and never uphill from—any shallow drinking-water well or handpump so faecal coliforms cannot percolate into groundwater.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00149","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Family Planning","topic":"Chhaya (Centchroman / Ormeloxifene)","q":"A 26-year-old exclusively breastfeeding mother (3 months postpartum) wants an oral contraceptive pill that does NOT contain oestrogen or reduce her breastmilk supply. Under India\u0027s MoHFW Basket of Choice, how is \u0027Chhaya\u0027 (Centchroman 30 mg) taken?","o":["Once daily at bedtime like Mala-N","Taken only immediately after intercourse as emergency pill","Once every 5 years","Twice a week for the first 3 months, followed by ONCE a week thereafter (non-hormonal, non-steroidal selective oestrogen receptor modulator safe during breastfeeding)"],"a":3,"e":"Chhaya (Centchroman / Ormeloxifene 30 mg), developed by CDRI Lucknow and supplied free in India\u0027s public family planning programme, is a unique non-steroidal, non-hormonal oral contraceptive (selective oestrogen receptor modulator). Taken twice weekly (e.g., Sunday \u0026 Wednesday) for the first 3 months and once weekly thereafter, it preserves lactation volume and avoids oestrogenic thromboembolic side effects (occasional delayed menses may occur benignly).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00150","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Oral Cancer Screening (NP-NCD)","q":"During an NP-NCD oral visual examination (OVE) of a 46-year-old gutkha and beedi user, which oral cavity finding requires immediate referral to the PHC/CHC dental or surgical specialist to rule out oral malignancy?","o":["Non-healing indurated ulcer lasting \u003e 2 weeks, persistent white patch that cannot be scraped off (leukoplakia), velvety red patch (erythroplakia), or restricted mouth opening from fibrotic bands (submucous fibrosis)","Normal anatomical linea alba along teeth occlusion","Normal pink moist buccal mucosa","Temporary mild aphthous sore lasting 2 days after biting the cheek"],"a":0,"e":"Oral cancer is among the top three cancers in India, strongly linked to smokeless tobacco (gutkha/khaini), areca nut, smoking, and alcohol. Early detection via systematic bright-light Oral Visual Examination identifies premalignant lesions—non-scrapable leukoplakia, high-risk erythroplakia, oral submucous fibrosis (trismus/blanched stiff mucosa), and any painless/indurated ulcer \u003e 14 days—for prompt biopsy and tobacco cessation counselling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00151","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Patterns of Disease Occurrence","q":"A village normally reports 2–3 cases of acute gastroenteritis each July, but this July 45 residents develop severe watery diarrhoea within 48 hours after attending the same community feast. Which epidemiological term describes this sudden excess above expected baseline frequency?","o":["Endemic","Epidemic (outbreak)","Holoendemic","Sporadic"],"a":1,"e":"An epidemic (or localized outbreak) is the occurrence of cases of an illness in a community or region clearly in excess of normal expectancy (here a point-source common-vehicle foodborne outbreak). Endemic refers to constant habitual presence within a given geographic area; sporadic means scattered irregular cases; pandemic is an epidemic crossing international borders/continents.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00152","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Methods of Health Communication","q":"During an adolescent girls’ nutrition session at an Anganwadi Centre, an ANM gives a brief live 15-minute step-by-step hands-on display showing mothers how to prepare standard 1-litre packet ORS using clean boiled cooled water and wash hands with soap. Which health education method is she using?","o":["Didactic lecture","Mass radio broadcast","Demonstration","Symposium"],"a":2,"e":"A Demonstration combines visual and auditory learning (\"seeing is believing\" and \"learning by doing\") by showing exact practical steps in real life—ideal for teaching ORS preparation, handwashing, breastfeeding attachment, or chlorine mixing to mothers and families.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00153","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Vitamin Deficiency Syndromes","q":"Match each classic nutritional deficiency syndrome with its deficient vitamin: 1. Pellagra (\"3 Ds\": Dermatitis in sun-exposed Casal’s necklace, Diarrhoea, Dementia) 2. Scurvy (spongy bleeding gums, petechiae, delayed wound healing) 3. Rickets in children (rachitic rosary, bowed legs, craniotabes) 4. Wet/Dry Beriberi (high-output heart failure or peripheral neuropathy) Select the correct vitamin sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Thiamine (B1) - Vitamin A - Vitamin K - Riboflavin (B2)","Vitamin B12 - Niacin (B3) - Vitamin C - Vitamin D","Riboflavin (B2) - Vitamin E - Folic acid - Pyridoxine (B6)","Niacin (Vitamin B3) - Ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) - Vitamin D (Cholecalciferol) - Thiamine (Vitamin B1)"],"a":3,"e":"(1) Niacin (B3) deficiency—seen in maize/jowar-staple diets low in tryptophan—causes Pellagra (Dermatitis, Diarrhoea, Dementia); (2) Vitamin C deficiency impairs collagen hydroxylation causing Scurvy; (3) Vitamin D deficiency causes defective bone mineralization (Rickets in children, Osteomalacia in adults); (4) Thiamine (B1) deficiency—linked to polished white rice diets—causes Beriberi.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00154","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Nutrition)","topic":"Foodborne Toxicities in India","q":"Adulteration of edible mustard oil with Argemone mexicana seed oil (containing the alkaloid sanguinarine) causes which acute multisystem food toxicity characterized by bilateral pitting leg edema, erythema, glaucoma, and cardiac failure?","o":["Epidemic dropsy","Endemic fluorosis","Neurolathyrism","Ergotism"],"a":0,"e":"Epidemic dropsy results from unintentional or deliberate contamination of mustard oil with Argemone mexicana (prickly poppy) oil. Sanguinarine increases capillary permeability, producing sudden non-inflammatory bilateral lower-limb edema (\"dropsy\"), skin flushing, diarrhoea, glaucoma, and high-output cardiac failure (detected by the Nitric Acid test).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00155","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Environmental Sanitation)","topic":"Sanitary Latrines for Rural India","q":"In a rural household sanitary water-seal latrine (RCA type), what is the primary public-health purpose of the 2 cm deep water seal created by the S-shaped/P-shaped trap beneath the squatting pan?","o":["To heat the septic tank biologically","To create a physical water barrier that prevents foul odour from escaping and blocks flies from accessing faeces and carrying pathogens to food","To dilute the excreta with 50 litres of water per flush","To serve as a drinking water reservoir for livestock"],"a":1,"e":"The Research-Cum-Action (RCA) sanitary latrine uses a smooth steeply sloped pan requiring only 1.5–2 litres of water per flush while maintaining a 2 cm water seal in the trap. This water seal blocks odour and eliminates fly breeding/contact, breaking faecal-oral disease transmission.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00156","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Promotion (Environmental Sanitation)","topic":"Village Sullage Disposal","q":"To prevent Culex mosquito breeding from domestic kitchen and bathing wastewater (\"sullage\") pooling in village lanes, the ANM and VHSNC recommend constructing a household Soakage Pit. What materials fill a standard soakage pit, and how far should it be located from a drinking water well?","o":["Filled with chlorinated bleaching powder daily inside the living room","Filled with plastic bags and cow dung; placed 1 metre from the well","Graded layers of coarse stones/brickbats at the bottom, gravel/pebbles in the middle, and fine sand/soil cover on top (with grease trap); located at least 15 metres away from any well/handpump","Left completely open and lined with waterproof cement at the bottom so water never drains"],"a":2,"e":"A soakage pit (typically ~1 m³ filled bottom-to-top with large stones/brickbats, smaller gravel, and covered to prevent open water exposure) filters sullage cleanly into the subsoil without creating stagnant puddles for Culex mosquitoes. Like latrines, it must sit \u003e= 15 m away (and downhill) from drinking water sources.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00157","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"UIP Infant Vaccines","q":"Which FIVE diseases are prevented by a single 0.5 mL intramuscular injection of the Pentavalent vaccine administered at 6, 10, and 14 weeks under India’s Universal Immunization Programme (UIP)?","o":["Polio, Measles, Rubella, Tuberculosis, and Hepatitis A","Tetanus, Japanese Encephalitis, Mumps, Varicella, and Hepatitis B","Diphtheria, Polio, Typhoid, Rotavirus, and Pneumococcal pneumonia","Diphtheria, Pertussis, Tetanus, Hepatitis B, and Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib)"],"a":3,"e":"The Pentavalent liquid vaccine combines DPT (Diphtheria, Pertussis, Tetanus), Hepatitis B, and Hib (Haemophilus influenzae type b, preventing childhood meningitis and pneumonia) into a single 0.5 mL IM injection given in the anterolateral mid-thigh at 6, 10, and 14 weeks (alongside 3 doses of PCV at 6 weeks, 14 weeks, and 9 months booster).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00158","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"AEFI Causality Classification","q":"Match each Adverse Event Following Immunization (AEFI) scenario with its WHO/MoHFW cause-specific category: 1. Toxic Shock Syndrome in 3 infants after using a reconstituted MR vial kept overnight for 24 hours 2. Vasovagal syncope (fainting) in an adolescent girl immediately before/upon seeing the Td syringe 3. Sterile injection-site swelling/mild fever after Pentavalent vaccine (known inherent vaccine property) 4. Infant develops rotavirus-negative intussusception or febrile seizure strictly due to unrelated concurrent viral malaria/URI Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Immunization error-related reaction - Immunization anxiety-related reaction - Vaccine product-related reaction - Coincidental event","Vaccine product-related - Coincidental event - Immunization anxiety-related - Immunization error-related","Coincidental event - Immunization error-related - Immunization anxiety-related - Vaccine quality defect-related","Vaccine quality defect-related - Vaccine product-related - Coincidental event - Immunization error-related"],"a":0,"e":"(1) Reusing reconstituted MR/BCG beyond 4 hours breaches protocol -\u003e Immunization error-related (programmatic error); (2) Fear-triggered fainting/hyperventilation -\u003e Immunization anxiety-related; (3) Expected mild local pain/fever from intrinsic antigen/adjuvant -\u003e Vaccine product-related; (4) Unrelated illness happening by chance around vaccination -\u003e Coincidental event.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00159","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Measles Case Management","q":"A 2-year-old unimmunized child presents with 4 days of high fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, tiny bluish-white spots on the buccal mucosa opposite the lower molars (Koplik’s spots), and a maculopapular rash starting behind the ears and spreading downward. Under MoHFW/WHO guidelines, what Vitamin A regimen must be given immediately alongside supportive care and IDSP reporting?","o":["Topical Vitamin A ointment without oral doses","Two age-specific therapeutic doses of Vitamin A (2,00,000 IU orally on Day 1 and repeated on Day 2, plus a 3rd dose 2–4 weeks later if eye signs of deficiency exist)","Single dose of 50,000 IU IM only","No Vitamin A is given during acute measles"],"a":1,"e":"Measles acutely depletes hepatic Vitamin A stores, precipitating corneal ulceration (keratomalacia), severe pneumonia, and diarrhoea. Every child diagnosed with measles MUST receive two consecutive daily oral doses of Vitamin A: 2,00,000 IU (\u003e=12 months), 1,00,000 IU (6–11 months), or 50,000 IU (\u003c6 months), with a 3rd dose after 2–4 weeks if xerophthalmia signs are present.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00160","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Vaccine-Preventable Respiratory Diseases","q":"An 8-year-old boy with incomplete DPT immunization presents with sore throat, low-grade fever, marked cervical lymphadenopathy (\"bull neck\"), and a tough, greyish-white adherent pseudomembrane over the tonsils that bleeds when gently touched. What is the suspected diagnosis and immediate public-health action?","o":["Oral thrush; apply clotrimazole mouth paint","Simple viral pharyngitis; send home with warm saline gargles","Suspected Diphtheria; do NOT forcefully peel the membrane, refer URGENTLY to hospital for Diphtheria Antitoxin (DAT) and parenteral penicillin/erythromycin, notify under IDSP, and trace/prophylax close household contacts","Pertussis; give salbutamol syrup only"],"a":2,"e":"A dense, leathery greyish pseudomembrane tightly adherent to the tonsils/pharynx that bleeds on removal plus cervical \"bull neck\" edema is pathognomonic for Diphtheria (Corynebacterium diphtheriae). Immediate hospital transfer for Diphtheria Antitoxin (to neutralize unbound exotoxin causing myocarditis/neuropathy), airway monitoring, antibiotics, IDSP alert, and erythromycin/azithromycin chemoprophylaxis plus Td/DPT booster for contacts are mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00161","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Prevention of Parent-to-Child Transmission (PPTCT) of HIV","q":"Under the NACO (National AIDS Control Programme) PPTCT guidelines in India, what antiretroviral regimen is recommended for a pregnant woman newly diagnosed as HIV-positive at an ANC clinic, and what prophylaxis is given to her HIV-exposed newborn?","o":["Withhold all ART during pregnancy until CD4 falls below 200","Give single-dose Nevirapine only during labour and stop after delivery","Advise termination of pregnancy regardless of gestational age","Start lifelong triple-drug Antiretroviral Therapy (TLD: Tenofovir + Lamivudine + Dolutegravir) immediately regardless of CD4 count/gestational age, and give daily Nevirapine syrup prophylaxis to the newborn for 6 weeks (12 weeks if high risk)"],"a":3,"e":"NACO’s \"Test and Treat\" / Option B+ PPTCT protocol initiates lifelong daily fixed-dose triple ART (Tenofovir 300 mg + Lamivudine 300 mg + Dolutegravir 50 mg) immediately upon HIV diagnosis in pregnancy to suppress maternal viral load to undetectable levels, paired with institutional delivery, exclusive breastfeeding (with maternal ART cover), infant Nevirapine prophylaxis from birth for 6–12 weeks, and Early Infant Diagnosis (EID) DNA-PCR at 6 weeks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00162","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Scabies Control in Rural Households","q":"A schoolchild presents to the Sub-centre with intense nocturnal itching and burrows/papules in the finger webs, wrists, and axillae (Scabies), and two younger siblings also scratch at night. Which treatment advice ensures complete household eradication?","o":["Treat ALL household close contacts simultaneously: apply 5% Permethrin cream from neck down to soles overnight (8–12 hours) after a bath, wash off in the morning, and wash all bedding/clothes in hot water and sun-dry","Apply kerosene oil over the child’s skin","Give oral albendazole once and no topical cream","Apply 5% Permethrin cream only to the itchy spots of the index child for 10 minutes"],"a":0,"e":"Sarcoptes scabiei spreads by prolonged skin-to-skin contact and fomites. Treating only symptomatic spots or only one child guarantees rapid re-infestation from asymptomatic family carriers. Whole-body (neck-to-toes, including under nails and groin; scalp/face also in infants) overnight 5% Permethrin for all close contacts simultaneously plus hot laundering/sun-drying breaks transmission (repeat in 7–14 days if needed).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00163","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Primary Health Care","topic":"Agricultural Pesticide Poisoning","q":"A farmer who was spraying cotton crops without gloves or mask is brought to the Sub-centre drowsy, smelling strongly of garlic-like insecticide, with profuse salivation, sweating, vomiting, pinpoint pupils (miosis), wheezing, and heart rate 48/min. Which emergency actions must be taken while arranging immediate transport to the PHC/CHC for Atropine and Pralidoxime?","o":["Induce vomiting immediately and wash the farmer without wearing gloves","Wear protective gloves, remove all contaminated clothing, wash skin/hair thoroughly with soap and water, clear airway secretions in lateral recovery position, and refer urgently (with pre-referral Atropine per MO protocol)","Administer oral paracetamol and observe for 6 hours","Give oral milk and put the patient supine"],"a":1,"e":"Organophosphate insecticides irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase, producing cholinergic excess (\"DUMBELS\"/SLUDGE: Diarrhoea, Urination, Miosis, Bronchorrhea/Bronchospasm, Emesis, Lacrimation, Salivation). Health workers must wear gloves to prevent secondary dermal absorption while stripping contaminated clothes and washing skin, protect the airway from drowning in bronchorrhea secretions, and expedite IV Atropine (until lungs clear/atropinization) + Pralidoxime (2-PAM).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00164","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Complementary Feeding Principles","q":"A mother brings her 8-month-old infant for growth monitoring and says she feeds him only breastmilk and very thin watery dal soup twice a day. According to WHO/MoHFW IYCF/IMNCI guidelines, what feeding correction must the ANM counsel?","o":["Give only cow’s milk diluted 1:1 with water in a feeding bottle","Wait until 12 months of age before introducing thick semi-solid foods","Continue breastfeeding on demand and give thick, mashed, energy-dense family foods (e.g., khichdi/suji kheer/mashed roti-dal with added oil/ghee, vegetables, egg/fruit) 3 times a day (plus 1–2 nutritious snacks) rather than thin watery soups","Stop breastfeeding completely at 8 months so the child eats more dal water"],"a":2,"e":"Starting at 6 completed months, breastmilk alone cannot meet infant energy/iron/zinc needs. Watery dal/rice water fills the small infant stomach with very low calories. Under IYCF guidelines, food should be thick enough to stay on a spoon without dripping, enriched with oil/ghee/jaggery/diverse food groups, given 2–3 meals/day at 6–8 months and 3–4 meals/day at 9–23 months alongside continued breastfeeding up to 2 years or beyond.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00165","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Neonatal Eye Infections","q":"During a Day-3 HBNC visit, an ANM notices both eyelids of a home-delivered newborn are markedly swollen with thick, profuse yellow-green pus discharging from the eyes (Ophthalmia Neonatorum). What is the ANM’s priority action?","o":["Advise the mother to instil breastmilk or kajal into the eyes","Clean the eyelids gently outward using sterile saline/boiled cooled water swabs (one swab per wipe) and refer URGENTLY to a Medical Officer/hospital for systemic antibiotics to rule out gonococcal/chlamydial infection","Reassure the mother that yellow pus is normal tearing in newborns","Patch both eyes tightly with dry cotton for 3 days"],"a":1,"e":"Purulent conjunctivitis within the first 28 days of life—especially profuse hyperacute pus in Days 2–5—raises urgent suspicion for Neisseria gonorrhoeae (which can perforate the cornea and cause permanent blindness within 24–48 hours) or Chlamydia trachomatis. Gentle eye cleansing with clean saline swabs and immediate referral for systemic ceftriaxone/azithromycin are essential.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00166","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Calcium Supplementation in Pregnancy","q":"Under MoHFW guidelines for preventing pre-eclampsia, a pregnant woman at 16 weeks gestation is prescribed Calcium tablets (500 mg elemental calcium + 250 IU Vitamin D3 twice daily = 1,000 mg/day) alongside her daily Iron and Folic Acid (IFA) tablet. How should the ANM instruct her to schedule these tablets?","o":["Swallow the Calcium tablet and IFA tablet together at the exact same moment with a cup of hot tea","Take Calcium tablets after morning and evening meals, and take the IFA tablet at a SEPARATE time (e.g., after lunch or bedtime, at least 2 hours apart from calcium and milk/tea) because calcium competitively inhibits intestinal iron absorption","Stop taking IFA completely once calcium starts at 14 weeks","Take both calcium tablets only during the final week before delivery"],"a":1,"e":"MoHFW recommends 1,000 mg elemental calcium/day (500 mg BID with Vitamin D3) from 14 weeks gestation through 6 months postpartum to reduce gestational hypertension/pre-eclampsia. Because divalent calcium ions (and dairy/tea tannins) inhibit non-heme iron absorption in the duodenum, Calcium and IFA must ALWAYS be separated by at least 2 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00167","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Normal Puerperium Physiology","q":"During the normal puerperium, what is the expected physiological sequence and approximate duration of postpartum uterine discharge (lochia)?","o":["Bright red heavy clots continuously for 6 weeks is normal","Lochia alba (Days 1–4) -\u003e Lochia rubra (Days 5–9) -\u003e Lochia serosa (Days 10–15)","Lochia serosa (Days 1–4) -\u003e Lochia alba (Days 5–9) -\u003e Lochia rubra (Days 10–15)","Lochia rubra (bright red, Days 1–4) -\u003e Lochia serosa (pinkish/brownish, Days 5–9) -\u003e Lochia alba (yellowish-white, Days 10–15+)"],"a":3,"e":"Postpartum endometrial shedding progresses sequentially: Lochia rubra (Days 1–4, red blood/decidua/trophoblast), Lochia serosa (Days 5–9, paler pink-brown serous exudate and leukocytes), and Lochia alba (Days 10–15+, creamy white/yellow leukocytes and mucus). Return of bright red bleeding after Day 7–10 suggests secondary PPH (retained placental fragments/subinvolution), and foul odour signals puerperal infection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00168","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery","topic":"Menstrual Hygiene Scheme (MHS)","q":"Under India’s Menstrual Hygiene Scheme (MHS / RKSK), what safe menstrual hygiene guidance should the ANM and ASHA emphasize to adolescent girls to prevent Reproductive Tract Infections (RTIs) and Toxic Shock?","o":["Change sanitary napkins/clean pads every 4–6 hours regardless of flow volume, wash external genitalia with clean water, wash hands with soap before/after changing, and wrap used pads for safe disposal (never flush into drains)","Perform vigorous internal vaginal douching with antiseptic Dettol twice daily during menses","Dry reused menstrual cloths inside dark damp rooms without soap","Wear one high-absorbency pad continuously for 24 hours on light days to save pads"],"a":0,"e":"Prolonged wear of damp pads/cloths promotes bacterial/fungal overgrowth and vulvovaginal RTIs. Changing every 4–6 hours, handwashing, gentle external water cleansing, sun-drying if clean reusable cloth is used, and wrapped solid-waste/incinerator disposal prevent infection and environmental blockage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00169","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"PCPNDT Act (1994, Amended 2003)","q":"Assertion (A): Under India’s Pre-Conception and Pre-Natal Diagnostic Techniques (PCPNDT) Act, communicating or revealing the sex of the fetus by words, signs, or ultrasound images to pregnant women or their relatives is a punishable criminal offence. Reason (R): Prenatal diagnostic techniques such as ultrasonography and amniocentesis are legally permitted ONLY to detect specified congenital anomalies or genetic/chromosomal abnormalities, never for fetal sex selection. Choose the correct option:","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the legal rationale behind A","Both A and R are true, but R contradicts A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"To combat female foeticide and skewed Child Sex Ratio, the PCPNDT Act strictly prohibits pre-conception sex selection and disclosure of fetal sex during/after prenatal diagnostics (carrying imprisonment and fines for practitioners, clinic owners, and those seeking sex determination). Diagnostics are restricted to legitimate clinical/genetic indications with mandatory Form F documentation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00170","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"Antenatal Supplement Forecasting","q":"A Sub-centre has 40 non-anaemic pregnant women entering their second trimester who each require the standard 180-day antenatal prophylactic course of red IFA tablets (1 tablet daily for 180 days during pregnancy). Excluding postpartum tablets and buffer stock, how many red IFA tablets are required for their complete antenatal prophylaxis?","o":["3,600 tablets","7,200 tablets","4,000 tablets","14,400 tablets"],"a":1,"e":"Total antenatal prophylactic IFA tablets = Number of pregnant women × 1 tablet/day × 180 days = 40 × 180 = 7,200 tablets (another 7,200 tablets would be needed separately for their 180-day postpartum course).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00171","course":"ANM Nursing","subject":"Health Centre Management","topic":"eSanjeevani HWC Telemedicine","q":"At an upgraded Ayushman Arogya Mandir (Sub-centre HWC), a diabetic/hypertensive villager needs physician review for medication titration without travelling 25 km to the District Hospital. Which Government of India digital platform enables the CHO/ANM (\"Spoke\") to conduct a real-time video teleconsultation with a Medical Officer or specialist (\"Hub\") and generate a valid e-prescription?","o":["Nikshay Poshan Portal","e-RaktKosh","eSanjeevani HWC (Assisted Telemedicine platform)","e-VIN (Electronic Vaccine Intelligence Network)"],"a":2,"e":"eSanjeevani HWC operates on a hub-and-spoke model connecting rural Sub-centres/PHCs (Spokes, operated by CHO/ANM) with MBBS Medical Officers and specialists at District Hospitals/Medical Colleges (Hubs) to deliver real-time teleconsultations and digital prescriptions for NCDs, ANC, and acute illnesses.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00172","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Samhita Adhyayana \u0026 Kriya Sharir","topic":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha Guna \u0026 Karma","q":"According to classical Ayurvedic Siddhanta (Ashtanga Hridaya / Charaka Samhita), match the Dosha to its predominant Panchamahabhuta composition: 1) Vata, 2) Pitta, 3) Kapha.","o":["1-Akasha + Prithvi; 2-Vayu + Jala; 3-Agni + Vayu","1-Prithvi + Jala; 2-Vayu + Akasha; 3-Agni + Akasha","1-Agni + Prithvi; 2-Jala + Prithvi; 3-Vayu + Agni","1-Vayu + Akasha; 2-Agni (Tejas) + Jala; 3-Prithvi + Jala"],"a":3,"e":"In Ayurveda Kriya Sharir: Vata is predominantly composed of Vayu and Akasha mahabhutas (Ruksha, Laghu, Shita, Khara, Sukshma, Chala gunas); Pitta is predominantly Agni/Tejas with slight Jala (Sasneha, Tikshna, Ushna, Laghu, Visra, Sara, Drava); Kapha is predominantly Prithvi and Jala (Snigdha, Shita, Guru, Manda, Shlakshna, Mritsna, Sthira).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00173","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Roga Nidana evam Vikriti Vigyana","topic":"Shatkriyakala (Six Stages of Pathogenesis)","q":"In Sushruta Samhita\u0027s concept of Shatkriyakala (six stages of pathogenesis), during which specific Kriyakala does Dosha-Dushya Sammurchhana (interaction of vitiated Dosha with vulnerable Dhatu/Srotas) occur, giving rise to Purvarupa (prodromal symptoms)?","o":["Fourth stage — Sthanasamshraya","Fifth stage — Vyakti","Second stage — Prakopa","Third stage — Prasara"],"a":0,"e":"In Sushruta Samhita (Sutrasthana Ch. 21), Shatkriyakala comprises: (1) Sanchaya (accumulation at own seat), (2) Prakopa (aggravation), (3) Prasara (dissemination/overflow across the body), (4) Sthanasamshraya (localisation in a Kha-vaigunya/defective Srotas producing Dosha-Dushya Sammurchhana and Purvarupa), (5) Vyakti (full clinical manifestation/Rupa), and (6) Bheda (differentiation/chronicity/complications).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00174","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Panchakarma","topic":"Antiki Shuddhi Lakshana","q":"According to Charaka Samhita (Siddhisthana), what is the classical \u0027Antiki Shuddhi\u0027 (terminal endpoint) sign indicating completion of Samyak Vamana Karma versus Samyak Virechana Karma?","o":["Pittanta (appearance of bile/Pitta) at the end of Vamana; Kaphanta (appearance of mucus/Kapha) at the end of Virechana","Kaphanta at the end of Vamana; Raktanta at the end of Virechana","Vatanta at the end of Vamana; Pittanta at the end of Virechana","Aushadhanta (pure medicine expulsion) at the end of both Vamana and Virechana"],"a":0,"e":"In Charaka Samhita Siddhisthana Ch. 1 (Kalpanasiddhi), Antiki Shuddhi denotes the final material expelled confirming complete evacuation of the targeted Dosha: Vamana targets Amashaya-stha Kapha and stops safely upon appearance of Pitta (Pittanta); Virechana expels Pitta and Mala from the lower GI tract and concludes satisfactorily upon appearance of Kapha/mucus (Kaphanta).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00175","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Dravyaguna Vigyana","topic":"Rasapanchaka Siddhanta","q":"In Ayurvedic Dravyaguna Vigyana, Danti (Baliospermum montanum) and Chitraka (Plumbago zeylanica) share identical Rasa (Katu), Vipaka (Katu), and Virya (Ushna), yet Danti produces strong Virechana (purgation) whereas Chitraka acts primarily as Deepana-Pachana without purgation. Which Rasapanchaka principle explains this specific, non-predictable pharmacological action?","o":["Samanya Virya (general thermal potency)","Prabhava (Achintya Shakti — specific inexplicable potency of the drug)","Anurasa (secondary subtle taste)","Vipaka (post-digestive transformation taste)"],"a":1,"e":"Charaka Samhita (Sutrasthana Ch. 26) defines Prabhava as the specific, inconceivable (Achintya) therapeutic potency of a Dravya when two substances share identical Rasa, Guna, Virya, and Vipaka yet exhibit distinct actions (e.g., Danti\u0027s Virechana karma versus Chitraka, or Vishaghna action of Shirisha, or Vidradhihara/Manidharan effects). Prabhava supersedes Rasa, Virya, and Vipaka in strength.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00176","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Rasashastra evam Bhaishajya Kalpana","topic":"Asava-Arishta \u0026 Avaleha Preparation","q":"In Bhaishajya Kalpana (Ayurvedic Pharmaceutics), match each formulation/quality parameter accurately: 1) Asava, 2) Arishta, 3) Dhataki Pushpa (Woodfordia fruticosa), 4) Tantumatva \u0026 Apsu Nimajjanam.","o":["1-Fermentation using boiled Kwatha; 2-Fermentation using unboiled Swarasa; 3-Kwatha thickening agent; 4-Asava spoilage signs","1-Cold aqueous infusion; 2-Semi-solid confection; 3-Clarifying agent for Ghrita; 4-Churna particle fineness test","1-Self-generated alcoholic fermentation using unboiled Swarasa/Hima/Phanta; 2-Self-generated alcoholic fermentation using boiled Kwatha (decoction); 3-Sandhana Dravya (fermentation initiator); 4-Paka Siddhi Lakshana (completion tests) of Avaleha","1-Topical oil preparation (Sneha Kalpana); 2-Mineral incineration (Bhasma); 3-Antioxidant preservative; 4-Arishta filtration test"],"a":2,"e":"Per Sharangadhara Samhita (Madhyama Khanda): Asava is prepared by fermenting unboiled raw herbal juices/cold infusions with sweetening agents (Guda/Madhu), whereas Arishta is prepared by fermenting a boiled herbal decoction (Kwatha). Dhataki pushpa supplies wild yeast inoculum acting as Sandhana Dravya. Avaleha (semi-solid confection) completion is verified by Tantumatva (1-2 thread consistency between fingers) and Apsu Nimajjanam (sinking intact in water without dispersing).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00177","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Shalya Tantra","topic":"Apamarga Ksharasutra Therapy","q":"In the standardised CCRAS/NCISM preparation of Apamarga Ksharasutra used for treating Bhagandara (fistula-in-ano), 21 sequential coatings are applied onto surgical Barbour linen thread No. 20. Which exact coating sequence is followed?","o":["7 coatings of Snuhi Ksheera alone and 14 coatings of Tankana Bhasma","11 coatings of Haridra Churna, 7 coatings of Honey, and 3 coatings of Apamarga Kshara","21 coatings of Apamarga Kshara mixed with sesame oil without Snuhi latex","11 coatings of Snuhi Ksheera alone, followed by 7 coatings of Snuhi Ksheera + Apamarga Kshara, followed by 3 coatings of Snuhi Ksheera + Haridra Churna"],"a":3,"e":"Standard Apamarga Ksharasutra preparation (21 coatings on Barbour linen thread No. 20 dried in a Ksharasutra cabinet) comprises: 11 initial coatings of fresh Snuhi Ksheera (Euphorbia neriifolia latex, acting as binder and mild proteolytic/debriding agent), 7 coatings of Snuhi Ksheera + Apamarga Kshara (Achyranthes aspera alkali, providing controlled chemical cauterisation and curettage), and 3 final coatings of Snuhi Ksheera + Haridra (Curcuma longa powder, providing antiseptic, anti-inflammatory, and healing action).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00178","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Kriya Sharir","topic":"Sthana and Karma of Dosha Subtypes","q":"According to Ashtanga Hridaya (Sutrasthana Ch. 12 — Doshabhediya Adhyaya), match each Dosha subtype to its primary physiological seat (Sthana) and function (Karma): 1) Prana Vata, 2) Sadhaka Pitta, 3) Avalambaka Kapha, 4) Pachaka Pitta.","o":["1-Murdha \u0026 Uras (Buddhi, Hridaya, Indriya, Chitta dharana, Anna praveshana); 2-Hridaya (Buddhi, Medha, Abhimana); 3-Uras (Trika \u0026 Hridaya avalambana); 4-Pakwashaya-Amashaya madhya (Annapachana \u0026 Sara-Kitta vivechana)","1-Nabhi (Samanagnibala); 2-Yakrit-Pliha (Rasa ranjana); 3-Shira (Indriya tarpana); 4-Jihwa (Rasa bodhana)","1-Pakwashaya (Mala-mutra nishkramana); 2-Twak (Bhrajana); 3-Sandhi (Shleshana); 4-Netra (Alochana)","1-Hridaya (Buddhi/Abhimana); 2-Uras (Trika dharana); 3-Murdha (Anna praveshana); 4-Amashaya (Anna kledana)"],"a":0,"e":"In Ashtanga Hridaya Sutrasthana Ch. 12: Prana Vata resides in Murdha and moves in Uras/Kantha sustaining Buddhi, Hridaya, Indriya, Chitta, respiration, and deglutition; Sadhaka Pitta resides in Hridaya governing Buddhi, Medha, Abhimana, and goal attainment; Avalambaka Kapha resides in Uras supporting the Trika (shoulder-scapular-thoracic complex), Hridaya, and all other Kapha sites via Anna-virya; Pachaka Pitta sits between Pakwashaya and Amashaya digesting food and separating Sara and Kitta.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00179","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Panchakarma","topic":"Kala Basti \u0026 Yoga Basti Schedules","q":"In classical Panchakarma Basti regimens (Charaka Samhita Siddhisthana / Chakrapani), how many Niruha (Asthapana) and Anuvasana Bastis constitute a \u0027Yoga Basti\u0027 (8-day schedule) versus a \u0027Kala Basti\u0027 (16-day schedule), and why must both schedules begin and end with Anuvasana Basti?","o":["Yoga Basti = 1 Niruha + 7 Anuvasana; Kala Basti = 15 Niruha + 1 Anuvasana","Yoga Basti = 3 Niruha + 5 Anuvasana (total 8); Kala Basti = 6 Niruha + 10 Anuvasana (total 16); beginning and ending with Anuvasana provides Sneha protection preventing Vata aggravation and Rukshata from Niruha","Yoga Basti = 5 Niruha + 3 Anuvasana; Kala Basti = 10 Niruha + 6 Anuvasana; Niruha starts and ends the course to maximise colon dryness","Yoga Basti = 4 Niruha + 4 Anuvasana; Kala Basti = 8 Niruha + 8 Anuvasana; alternating purely in equal numbers"],"a":1,"e":"Classical Basti schedules follow specific ratios: Yoga Basti (total 8) comprises 1 initial Anuvasana, 3 alternating pairs of Niruha + Anuvasana, and 1 final Anuvasana (3 Niruha + 5 Anuvasana). Kala Basti (total 16) comprises 1 initial Anuvasana, 6 alternating pairs of Niruha + Anuvasana, and 3 final Anuvasanas (6 Niruha + 10 Anuvasana). Karma Basti (total 30) comprises 12 Niruha + 18 Anuvasana. Starting and concluding with Snigdha Anuvasana lubricates the colon and prevents Ruksha Vata-prakopa induced by consecutive/terminal Niruha.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00180","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Dravyaguna Vigyana","topic":"Dashamoola Composition","q":"Dashamoola is one of the foremost Tridoshaghna/Vatahara formulations in Ayurveda, comprising Brihat Panchamoola (5 tree roots) and Laghu Panchamoola (5 shrub/herb roots). Which set accurately lists the FIVE constituents of Brihat Panchamoola?","o":["Haritaki, Bibhitaki, Amalaki, Shunthi, and Pippali","Guduchi, Ashwagandha, Shatavari, Bala, and Punarnava","Bilva (Aegle marmelos), Agnimantha (Premna integrifolia), Shyonaka (Oroxylum indicum), Patala (Stereospermum suaveolens), and Gambhari (Gmelina arborea)","Shalaparni, Prishniparni, Brihati, Kantakari, and Gokshura"],"a":2,"e":"Dashamoola combines Brihat Panchamoola (5 large trees: Bilva, Agnimantha, Shyonaka, Patala, Gambhari — predominantly Kashaya-Tikta rasa, Ushna virya, Kapha-Vata shamaka) and Laghu Panchamoola (5 smaller herbs/undershrubs: Shalaparni, Prishniparni, Brihati, Kantakari, Gokshura — predominantly Madhura-Tikta, Vata-Pitta shamaka). Together they achieve balanced Tridosha-shothahara and Shoola-prashamana actions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00181","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Rasashastra evam Bhaishajya Kalpana","topic":"Classical Bhasma Pariksha (Quality Assessment Tests)","q":"During laboratory quality evaluation of Abhraka Bhasma or Lauha Bhasma before internal administration, the prepared Bhasma exhibits NO glittering metallic particles under bright sunlight, fills cleanly into the micro-grooves of the thumb and index finger, floats on stagnant water in a beaker, and does not revert to free elemental metal when heated strongly with Mitra Panchaka. Which quartet of classical Bhasma Pariksha criteria is satisfied?","o":["Gandha-pariksha, Shita-virya, Kashaya-rasa, and Varti-paka","Lepa-pariksha, Sneha-siddhi, Tantumatva, and Patana-pariksha","Avami, Amlapariksha, Guru-pariksha, and Dhumapariksha","Nischandratva, Rekhapurnatva, Varitaratva, and Apunarbhava"],"a":3,"e":"Rasaratna Samucchaya specifies physical and chemical Bhasma Pariksha tests to confirm complete Marana (incineration) and non-toxicity: (1) Nischandratva — total absence of shiny/glittering metallic particles; (2) Rekhapurnatva — extreme micro-fineness filling fingerprint furrows; (3) Varitaratva — lightness allowing sprinkled Bhasma to float on still water surface tension; (4) Apunarbhava — irreversible conversion into an organometallic/oxide complex that fails to regenerate free metal when fused with Mitra Panchaka (Guda, Gunja, Tankana, Madhu, Ghrita).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00182","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Shalakya Tantra","topic":"Akshitarpana Procedure \u0026 Contraindications","q":"A 48-year-old patient presents with chronic dry eyes, gritty sensation, photophobia, and Vata-Pitta Netra Roga (Shushkakshipaka) without purulent discharge or acute conjunctival congestion. Akshitarpana with Jeevantyadi Ghrita is planned. In which ocular clinical condition would Akshitarpana be STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED?","o":["Acute infective/Ama-stage Abhishyanda (acute mucopurulent conjunctivitis with thick discharge, severe redness, and oedema)","Chronic Vata-dominant Shushkakshipaka (dry eye syndrome)","Adhimantha after complete Ama-pachana and clearance of acute inflammation","Timira (early non-inflammatory refractive/degenerative visual weakness) during clear weather"],"a":0,"e":"In Sushruta Samhita (Uttaratantra Ch. 18) and Ashtanga Hridaya (Sutrasthana Ch. 24), Akshitarpana—retaining lukewarm medicated Ghrita inside a circular Masha pishti (black gram flour) rim around the orbit—nourishes and pacifies Vata-Pitta ocular tissues in Shushkakshipaka, Timira, and Krichhronmilana. However, applying unctuous Ghrita during Ama/acute congestive infective states (Nava Abhishyanda, acute discharge, cloudy/rainy extreme weather, or severe fever) aggravates Kapha-Ama and worsens infection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00183","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Rachana Sharir","topic":"Classification of 107 Marmas by Prognosis (Parinama)","q":"According to Sushruta Samhita (Sharirasthana Ch. 6 — Pratyekamarmanirdesha), match each prognostic Marma category (out of total 107 Marmas) to its exact count and Mahabhuta/clinical outcome: 1) Sadyah Pranahara, 2) Kalantara Pranahara, 3) Vishalyaghna, 4) Vaikalyakara, 5) Rujakara.","o":["1-25; 2-25; 3-25; 4-25; 5-7","1-19 (Agneya, fatal within 7 days); 2-33 (Saumya-Agneya, fatal over ~2-4 weeks); 3-3 (Vayavya, patient survives while foreign body plugs wound but dies on extraction); 4-44 (Saumya, causes permanent deformity); 5-8 (Agni-Vayu, causes severe pain)","1-33 (Vayavya); 2-19 (Saumya); 3-8 (Agneya); 4-3 (Agni-Vayu); 5-44 (Saumya-Agneya)","1-44 (causes deformity); 2-8 (severe pain); 3-19 (instant death); 4-3 (foreign body); 5-33 (delayed death)"],"a":1,"e":"Sushruta enumerates 107 Marmas summing across 5 prognostic classes: Sadyah Pranahara = 19 (predominantly Agneya; rapid death within 7 days), Kalantara Pranahara = 33 (Saumya-Agneya; delayed fatality within ~15-30 days), Vishalyaghna = 3 (Utkshepa-2, Sthapani-1; Vayavya — fatal once Shalya is prematurely extracted allowing Vayu/bleeding escape), Vaikalyakara = 44 (Saumya; functional/structural deformity), and Rujakara = 8 (Agni-Vayu; severe pain). Sum: 19 + 33 + 3 + 44 + 8 = 107.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00184","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Kayachikitsa","topic":"Sthula vs Krisha Pramehi Management Principles","q":"In Charaka Samhita (Chikitsasthana Ch. 6 — Prameha Chikitsa), two diabetic patients present: Patient A is obese (Sthula) and physically strong (Balavan) with Kapha-predominant Prameha, while Patient B is emaciated (Krisha) and debilitated (Durbala) with Vataja Madhumeha. How must their core Ayurvedic Chikitsa Siddhanta differ?","o":["Patient A (Sthula-Balavan) receives Samshodhana (Vamana/Virechana) and Apatarpana/Vyayama; Patient B (Krisha-Durbala) MUST NOT undergo strong Shodhana and instead receives Brimhana, Shamana, and Rasayana (e.g., Shilajatu, Amalaki)","Patient A receives heavy Brimhana with Mamsarasa and day-sleep; Patient B receives aggressive daily Vamana and Langhana","Both patients undergo identical repeated Raktamokshana and fasting regardless of Bala","Patient B receives Ruksha Tikshna Virechana every week to eliminate remaining Dhatus"],"a":0,"e":"Charaka explicitly divides Prameha patients into two management phenotypes: (1) Sthula \u0026 Balavan (obese, strong, Santarpanottha Kapha-Pitta dominant) — treated with Samshodhana (bio-purification via Vamana/Virechana), Aptarpana, Vyayama, and old Yava/Mudga diet; (2) Krisha \u0026 Durbala (lean, weak, Dhatukshayaja Vataja Madhumeha) — Shodhana is contraindicated as it worsens Vata and Dhatukshaya; instead, gentle Brimhana, Santarpana-Shamana, and Rasayana therapies (e.g., Shilajatu, Nishamalaki) are administered.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00185","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Swasthavritta and Yoga","topic":"Seasonal Chaya, Prakopa and Prashama of Tridosha","q":"According to classical Ayurvedic Ritucharya (Ashtanga Hridaya / Sushruta Sutrasthana Ch. 6), in which exact seasons do Vata, Pitta, and Kapha undergo natural Prakopa (aggravation), requiring preventive seasonal Shodhana (Basti, Virechana, Vamana respectively)?","o":["Vata Prakopa in Grishma; Pitta Prakopa in Varsha; Kapha Prakopa in Shishira","Vata Prakopa in Vasanta; Pitta Prakopa in Hemanta; Kapha Prakopa in Varsha","Vata Prakopa in Varsha (monsoon); Pitta Prakopa in Sharad (autumn); Kapha Prakopa in Vasanta (spring)","All three Doshas undergo Prakopa simultaneously in Sharad"],"a":2,"e":"Natural seasonal Dosha rhythm follows a predictable triad: (1) Kapha accumulates (Chaya) in Shishira, aggravates (Prakopa) in Vasanta (spring warmth liquefies stored Kapha -\u003e preventive Vamana), and pacifies in Grishma; (2) Vata accumulates in Grishma (dry heat), aggravates in Varsha (rainy cold/cloudy -\u003e preventive Basti), and pacifies in Sharad; (3) Pitta accumulates in Varsha, aggravates in Sharad (sharp autumn sun -\u003e preventive Virechana/Raktamokshana), and pacifies in Hemanta.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00186","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Prasuti Tantra evam Stri Roga","topic":"Monthly Antenatal Regimen (Masanumasika Garbhini Paricharya)","q":"In Charaka Samhita (Sharirasthana Ch. 8) Masanumasika Garbhini Paricharya, during the 8th and 9th months of uncomplicated pregnancy, which specific medicated procedures are recommended to achieve Vata Anulomana, soften the birth canal (Garbhamarga), and facilitate Sukhaprasava (uncomplicated vaginal delivery)?","o":["Complete fasting (Nirjala Langhana) during the entire ninth month","Kshara-karma over the cervix and heavy Vyayama","Tikshna Vamana and Lekhana Niruha Basti daily","Anuvasana Basti (with Madhura-aushadha siddha Taila) and Yoni Pichu (oil-soaked vaginal tampon) alongside Ksheera-Yavagu with Ghrita"],"a":3,"e":"In the 9th (and late 8th) month of pregnancy, Charaka prescribes Anuvasana Basti prepared with Madhura-skandha herbs and oil, plus Yoni Pichu (vaginal retention of sterile medicated oil tampon), in addition to nourishing Ksheera-Yavagu with Ghrita. These lubricate and soften pelvic/cervical-vaginal tissues, regulate Apana Vata Anulomana, clear Mala/pureesha obstruction, and promote smooth, uncomplicated labour (Sukhaprasava).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00187","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Agada Tantra evam Vidhi Vaidyaka","topic":"Dushi Visha (Latent Cumulative Poison)","q":"A 33-year-old patient with a remote history of low-grade pesticide/toxin exposure experiences recurrent episodes of pruritic wheals (Kotha/Mandala), indigestion, lassitude, and skin discolouration specifically during cloudy, windy, or cold rainy weather (Durdina) and after daytime sleeping or incompatible diet. Which classical Ayurvedic toxicological entity and primary formulation match this presentation?","o":["Dushi Visha (weakened, Kapha-enveloped latent cumulative toxin); managed by Swedana, Vamana/Virechana followed by Dushivishari Agada","Sadyah Pranahara Halahala Visha; fatal within seconds","Garavisha caused exclusively by acute cobra envenomation","Kaphaja Arsha; managed solely by surgical excision"],"a":0,"e":"Sushruta (Kalpasthana Ch. 2) defines Dushi Visha as any Sthavara, Jangama, or Kritrima poison that is attenuated (by time, Agni, sun, wind, or incomplete antivenom), remains dormant/encapsulated by Kapha in Dhatus for years without killing immediately, and flares up upon exposure to Durdina (cloudy/windy/cold days), Ajirna, or Divaswapna—manifesting as Kitibha, Kotha, Mandala, Bhinnapurisha, and Dhatukshaya. Treatment involves Swedana, Vamana/Virechana, and daily Dushivishari Agada.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00188","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Samhita Adhyayana \u0026 Padartha Vigyana","topic":"Chaturvidha Pariksha — Yukti Pramana","q":"In Charaka Samhita (Sutrasthana Ch. 11 — Tistraishaniya Adhyaya), four Pramanas (Aptopadesha, Pratyaksha, Anumana, Yukti) are described for valid clinical knowledge. Which specific Pramana is defined by Charaka as \u0027Buddhi pashyati ya bhavan bahu-karana-yogajan\u0027—the physician\u0027s analytical intellect synthesising multiple interdependent factors (Dosha, Dushya, Agni, Desha, Kala, Bala) to formulate an individualised diagnosis and treatment plan?","o":["Aptopadesha Pramana (authoritative scriptural/expert testimony)","Yukti Pramana (rational scientific reasoning/application)","Upamana Pramana (analogy/comparison)","Pratyaksha Pramana (direct sensory perception)"],"a":1,"e":"Charaka uniquely elevates Yukti (\u0027Bahu-karana-yogajan\u0027 — phenomena produced by the conjunction of multiple causes) as an independent fourth Pramana (Sutrasthana 11/23-25): just as crop yield requires soil, seed, water, season, and effort, clinical mastery requires Yukti to integrate Dosha, Dushya, Prakruti, Desha, Kala, Satmya, and Aushadha Yukti across past, present, and future.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00189","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Kayachikitsa","topic":"Parinama Shoola vs Annadrava Shoola","q":"A 36-year-old man presents with burning epigastric pain that characteristically begins 2-3 hours after eating (during the Pachyamana / digestive phase of food), eases temporarily upon taking small amounts of light food or after bilious/sour vomiting, and radiates to the flanks or back. According to Madhava Nidana (Ch. 26), which clinical diagnosis fits this exact post-prandial pain pattern?","o":["Jalodara (ascites with fluid thrill)","Annadrava Shoola (pain occurring continuously regardless of food intake or fasting, unrelieved by eating)","Parinama Shoola (Tridoshic Shoola triggered during food digestion — analogous to duodenal ulcer syndrome)","Udavarta (upward Vata movement with severe constipation and tympanites)"],"a":2,"e":"Madhava Nidana (Ch. 26) distinguishes two classical acid-peptic Shoola entities: (1) Parinama Shoola — Vata enveloped by Pitta-Kapha produces severe pain specifically during \u0027Bhukte jiryati\u0027 (the digestive phase 2-3 h post-meal) and subsides upon vomiting, digestion completion, or light food; (2) Annadrava Shoola — pain persists at all times (before, during, and after meals, unaffected by wholesome/unwholesome food), relieved chiefly upon vomiting.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00190","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Kaumarabhritya","topic":"Suvarna Prashana (Lehana Karma)","q":"According to Kashyapa Samhita (Sutrasthana — Lehadhyaya), what ingredients constitute classical Suvarna Prashana (Swarna Bindu Prashana) administered to infants and children, and what primary therapeutic benefits are attributed to it?","o":["Crude metallic mercury mixed with castor oil and rock salt; induces acute purgation (Virechana) in neonates","Raw Haritaki decoction mixed with sesame oil; used solely to treat neonatal jaundice","Mineral arsenic (Haratala) mixed with lemon juice; used as a topical scalp paste only","Purified/incinerated gold (Suvarna) triturated with Madhu (honey) and Ghrita (ghee, often medicated with Medhya herbs); enhances Medha (intellect), Agni (digestion), Bala (strength), Ayu (longevity), Varna, and Vyadhikshamatva (immunity against infections/grahas)"],"a":3,"e":"Kashyapa Samhita (Sutrasthana Lehadhyaya) describes rubbing pure gold on a clean stone with water/medicated drops and administering it with Madhu and Ghrita (traditionally on Pushya Nakshatra or daily for 1-6 months) to promote Medha, Agni, Bala, Ayu, Mangala, Punya, Vrishya, Varna, and protective immunity (Grahapaha / Vyadhikshamatva). Note: in infants, Madhu-Ghrita must be given in unequal proportions (Vishama matra) and pure micro-fine Suvarna Bhasma/processed gold used per NCISM/AYUSH safety standards.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00191","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Kriya Sharir","topic":"13 Types of Agni \u0026 Four Functional States of Jatharagni","q":"In Ayurvedic Kriya Sharir (Ashtanga Hridaya / Charaka Samhita), match each functional state of Jatharagni to its governing Dosha and clinical digestive pattern: 1) Vishamagni, 2) Tikshnagni, 3) Mandagni, 4) Samagni.","o":["1-Vata predominant (erratic digestion alternating between proper digestion and bloating/constipation); 2-Pitta predominant (hyperactive rapid digestion tolerating heavy meals, causing Amlapitta/Bhasmaka if excessive); 3-Kapha predominant (sluggish digestion generating Ama); 4-Tridosha equilibrium (timely complete digestion without distress)","1-Pitta predominant; 2-Kapha predominant; 3-Tridosha equilibrium; 4-Vata predominant","1-Kapha predominant; 2-Vata predominant; 3-Pitta predominant; 4-Rakta predominant","1-Bhutagni; 2-Dhatwagni; 3-Sadhaka Pitta; 4-Prana Vata"],"a":0,"e":"Ayurveda enumerates 13 Agnis (1 Jatharagni, 5 Bhutagnis, 7 Dhatwagnis). Jatharagni exhibits four states governed by Dosha dominance: Vishamagni (Vata: irregular/unpredictable digestion), Tikshnagni (Pitta: intense/rapid digestion), Mandagni (Kapha: weak/slow digestion that fails to digest even light food, producing toxic Ama—root of all diseases per \u0027Rogah sarvepi mandagnau\u0027), and Samagni (balanced Tridosha: optimal physiological digestion).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00192","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Kayachikitsa","topic":"Amavata (Rheumatoid Arthritis) Chikitsa Siddhanta","q":"A 38-year-old woman presents with symmetrical painful swelling of the metacarpophalangeal and wrist joints (Vrishchikadamsavat Vedana), morning stiffness (Jadya), anorexia, heaviness, and low-grade fever (Jwara), diagnosed as acute Sama Amavata (Madhava Nidana Ch. 25). Why MUST unctuous Abhyanga (oil massage) be AVOIDED in the acute Sama stage, and what initial Chakradatta line of therapy is prescribed?","o":["Perform immediate bloodletting without Ama-pachana","Early Snigdha Abhyanga aggravates Kapha-Ama and drives Ama deeper into Sandhis; initiate Langhana, Ruksha Swedana (dry Valuka/sand-bag fomentation), Tikta-Katu Deepana-Pachana, Virechana, and Saindhavadi/Kshara Basti","Perform daily heavy Ghritapana and Snigdha taila Abhyanga for 30 days immediately","Administer cold ice-water immersion (Sheeta Parisheka) and heavy curd diet"],"a":1,"e":"Per Chakradatta (Amavata Chikitsa 25/1): \u0027Langhanam swedanam tiktam dipanani katuni cha; Virechanam snehapanam bastayascha amamarute\u0027. In acute Sama Amavata, undigested sticky Ama blocks Srotas alongside vitiated Vata. Applying external/internal Sneha (oil massage or plain Ghritapana) prior to Ama-pachana increases Kapha/Ama, worsening joint stiffness and swelling. Treatment starts with Langhana, dry heat (Ruksha Valuka Sweda), bitter-pungent Ama-pachana (Shunthi, Guduchi, Rasna), Virechana (Eranda taila), and Vaitarana/Kshara Basti; Sneha is introduced only in the Nirama stage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00193","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Dravyaguna Vigyana","topic":"Commiphora mukul (Guggulu) Pharmacodynamics","q":"According to Bhavaprakasha Nighantu and Sushruta Samhita, how do the pharmacological actions of Nava (fresh) Guggulu versus Purana (aged \u003e1 year) Guggulu (Commiphora mukul oleo-gum-resin) differ clinically, and what standard liquid media are used for Guggulu Shodhana before preparing formulations like Triphala Guggulu?","o":["Nava Guggulu is Brimhana and Vrishya (anabolic/nourishing), whereas Purana Guggulu is Ati-Lekhana (scraping/hypolipidaemic, reducing Medoroga and Sthoulya); purified via Swedana in Dolayantra using Triphala Kwatha, Dashamoola Kwatha, or Godugdha","Nava Guggulu causes extreme emaciation; Purana Guggulu is strictly poisonous and discarded","Both Nava and Purana Guggulu are purified by frying in castor oil at 300 C","Guggulu is an inorganic mineral salt purified solely in lemon juice"],"a":0,"e":"Bhavaprakasha Nighantu (Karpuradi Varga) states \u0027Navam brimhanam vrishyam puranam tvati lekhanam\u0027—fresh Guggulu nourishes tissues (Brimhana, Vrishya), whereas aged (\u003e1 year) Purana Guggulu develops potent Lekhana (fat-scraping/anti-atherosclerotic) properties for Medoroga, Sthoulya, and Amavata. Raw Guggulu contains physical impurities and gastric-irritating volatile resins; Dolayantra Swedana in Triphala Kwatha, Guduchi Kwatha, or Godugdha followed by filtration/drying (Shodhana) ensures safety and efficacy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00194","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Panchakarma","topic":"Marsha vs Pratimarsha vs Pradhamana (Dhmapana) Nasya","q":"In Ashtanga Hridaya (Sutrasthana Ch. 20 — Nasyavidhi), how does \u0027Pratimarsha Nasya\u0027 differ from \u0027Marsha Nasya\u0027 and \u0027Pradhamana (Dhmapana) Nasya\u0027 regarding dosage form, drop count, and patient eligibility?","o":["Marsha Nasya is indicated only in neonates while Pratimarsha is contraindicated in adults","Pradhamana Nasya uses exclusively aqueous rosewater eye drops","Pratimarsha Nasya uses only 2 drops (Bindu) of Sneha per nostril daily and is safe across all ages from birth to old age across all seasons without strict Parihara; Marsha Nasya uses larger therapeutic oil doses (6-10 drops) with strict regimen; Pradhamana Nasya blows dry fine Churna into the nostrils via a 6-angula Nadi for severe Kapha-obstruction/Murchha","Pratimarsha Nasya uses 32 drops of Tikshna Kshara juice; Pradhamana uses lukewarm ghee enema"],"a":2,"e":"Vagbhata (Ashtanga Hridaya Sutrasthana 20) classifies Nasya by dose and formulation: Marsha Nasya uses 6 (Hrasva), 8 (Madhyama), or 10 (Uttama) Bindu of medicated Sneha per nostril requiring strict age/weather/dietary Parihara; Pratimarsha uses 2 Bindu per nostril daily as preventive Dinacharya safe in infants, elderly, and all seasons without complications; Pradhamana (Dhmapana) Nasya blows dry medicinal Churna (using a 6-angula double-ended tube) for acute coma (Murchha), Apasmara, and heavy Kaphaja Shiroroga.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00195","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Shalakya Tantra","topic":"Pratishyaya \u0026 Dushta Pratishyaya","q":"A 30-year-old patient with untreated chronic rhinitis (neglected Pratishyaya) progresses to persistent foul-smelling nasal blockage alternating with thin/purulent discharge, anosmia (Aghranatva), progressive hearing impairment (Badhirya), and ocular dimness (Andhya). According to Sushruta Samhita (Uttaratantra Ch. 24), what is this complicated stage called, and why is heavy Ghritapana/Virechana avoided in the early Nava (acute Ama) stage of Pratishyaya?","o":["Sadhya Suryavarta; managed exclusively with cold milk irrigation on Day 1","Ardhavabhedaka; cured solely by bloodletting from the toe","Nasa Arsha; requires immediate caustic Kshara instillation into the eye","Dushta Pratishyaya; early Nava Pratishyaya has Sama Dosha so heavy Ghrita/cold regimens are avoided initially in favour of Swedana, Langhana, Ushna-Amla-Katu Pachana until Pakwa (ripe) stage"],"a":3,"e":"Sushruta (Uttaratantra Ch. 24 — Pratishyaya Pratishedha) warns that neglected or improperly treated Pratishyaya advances to \u0027Dushta Pratishyaya\u0027, producing severe complications across Urdhwajatru organs: Badhirya (deafness), Andhya (blindness), Aghranatva (anosmia), Shotha, and Kasa/Kshaya. In early Nava (acute/unripe Ama) Pratishyaya, Shodhana and Ghritapana (except in Vataja specific indications) are avoided; instead, Swedana, Ushna laghu ahara, and Ama-pachana mature the Doshas to the Pakwa stage before Nasya/Dhumapana.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00196","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Rachana Sharir \u0026 Rogavijnana","topic":"Moolasthana of Abhyantara Srotas","q":"According to Charaka Samhita (Vimanasthana Ch. 5) and Sushruta Samhita (Sharirasthana Ch. 9), match each Srotas to its classical Moolasthana (root/origin): 1) Pranavaha Srotas, 2) Raktavaha Srotas, 3) Medovaha Srotas, 4) Artavavaha Srotas (Sushruta).","o":["1-Hridaya \u0026 Mahasrotas (Rasavahi Dhamani); 2-Yakrit \u0026 Pliha; 3-Vrikka \u0026 Vapavahana; 4-Garbhashaya \u0026 Artavavahi Dhamani","1-Basti \u0026 Vankshana; 2-Pakwashaya \u0026 Sthulaguda; 3-Asthi \u0026 Jaghana; 4-Talu \u0026 Kloma","1-Shiras \u0026 Twak; 2-Rakta-dhamani only; 3-Mamsa-snayu; 4-Majja-sandhi","1-Yakrit \u0026 Pliha; 2-Vrikka \u0026 Vapavahana; 3-Hridaya \u0026 Mahasrotas; 4-Amashaya \u0026 Kloma"],"a":0,"e":"Charaka Vimanasthana Ch. 5 defines Srotomoola (primary anatomical/functional seats whose injury deranges the entire channel): Pranavaha = Hridaya \u0026 Mahasrotas (Sushruta adds Rasavahi Dhamani); Raktavaha = Yakrit \u0026 Pliha (liver \u0026 spleen); Medovaha = Vrikka \u0026 Vapavahana (kidneys \u0026 omentum/mesentery; Sushruta: Kati \u0026 Vrikka). Sushruta Sharirasthana Ch. 9 adds two extra Bahirmukha/female channels including Artavavaha Srotas = Garbhashaya \u0026 Artavavahi Dhamani.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00197","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Kayachikitsa","topic":"Koshthashrita (Bahupitta) vs Shakhashrita (Ruddhapatha) Kamala","q":"A patient with deep yellow sclera and skin (Haridra-netra-twak) passes distinctly clay-white/sesame-paste-coloured stools (Tilapishtanibha Varchas) due to Kapha-obstructed biliary channels preventing Pitta entry into the GI tract—diagnosed as Shakhashrita (Ruddhapatha) Kamala (Charaka Chikitsasthana Ch. 16). How MUST its initial Chikitsa differ from Koshthashrita (Bahupitta) Kamala?","o":["Give strong drastic purgation with Trivrit and cold milk immediately in Tilapishtanibha stool","In Shakhashrita/Ruddhapatha Kamala, avoid immediate strong Virechana/Pitta-shamana initially and administer Kapha-Vata-hara Ushna-Tikshna-Ruksha drugs (Pippali, Shunthi, Saindhava, Matulunga, Mamsarasa) until stools regain yellow colour (Pitta enters Koshtha), whereas Bahupitta Kamala receives Mridu Tikta Virechana first","Perform continuous Langhana and Raktamokshana without Kapha-pachana","Administer heavy cold Ghritapana continuously during biliary Kapha obstruction"],"a":1,"e":"Charaka (Chikitsasthana 16/124-131) sharply differentiates two Kamala phenotypes: (1) Koshthashrita/Bahupitta Kamala (excess Pitta overflowing into circulation with dark yellow stools/urine) is treated straightaway with Snigdha-Mridu Tikta Virechana (Trivrit, Katuki, Triphala); (2) Shakhashrita/Ruddhapatha Kamala occurs when aggravated Kapha + Vata obstruct Pitta pathways, forcing Pitta into Shakhas while leaving stools white (Tilapishtanibha). Here, cooling/Pitta-shamana or harsh Virechana fails until Kapha-clearing Ushna-Tikshna/Ruksha formulations reopen channels and restore normal yellow stool pigmentation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00198","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Rasashastra evam Bhaishajya Kalpana","topic":"Four Classical Murcchana Forms (Khalviya, Parpati, Pottali, Kupipakwa)","q":"Among the four classical Sagandha/Nirgandha mercurial Murcchana preparations in Ayurvedic Rasashastra, which pair accurately describes Parpati Rasayana (e.g., Panchamrita Parpati for Grahani) versus Kupipakwa Rasayana (e.g., Makaradhwaja / Rasasindura)?","o":["Parpati is prepared by gently melting Kajjali in an iron ladle with Ghrita and pressing it between banana leaves over fresh cow-dung bed into thin crisp flakes; Kupipakwa is prepared by prolonged Kramagni heating of Kajjali inside a Kapadamitti-wrapped glass Kachakupi within a Valukayantra (sand bath) yielding a sublimed sublimate at the bottle neck","Parpati is sublimed at 900 C in a glass bottle for 72 hours; Kupipakwa is pressed cold between banana leaves","Both are prepared simply by mixing raw mercury with honey in a plastic beaker","Kupipakwa involves boiling herbs in 16 parts water without Parada or Gandhaka"],"a":0,"e":"Rasashastra classifies Parada Murcchana (eliminating toxicity while imparting therapeutic potency) into four groups: (1) Khalviya (mortar trituration, e.g., Arogyavardhini, Tribhuvanakirti), (2) Parpati (melting Kajjali into molten pool, pouring onto cow-dung-supported Kadali-patra and pressing into thin papad-like flakes, prized in Grahani/Atisara), (3) Pottali (compacted in molten Gandhaka or silk pouch), and (4) Kupipakwa (graduated Mridu-Madhyama-Tikshna Kramagni heating inside a 7-layer mud-smeared glass bottle in Valukayantra).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00199","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Prasuti Tantra evam Stri Roga","topic":"Asrigdara (Abnormal Uterine Bleeding) vs Udavarta Yonivyapada","q":"A 34-year-old woman presents with heavy, prolonged menstrual bleeding (Ati-pravritti of Artava) accompanied by pelvic burning, weakness, and giddiness—diagnosed in Charaka Samhita (Chikitsasthana Ch. 30) as Pittaja Asrigdara (Pradara). What is the classical Samprapti of Asrigdara and its first-line Ayurvedic therapeutic approach?","o":["Apana Vata moves upward causing dysmenorrhoea relieved immediately after passing a single clot (Udavarta)","Vitiated Kapha completely blocks menstrual blood causing amenorrhoea; treat with drastic Tikshna Vamana","Prakopita Vata (and Pitta) increases Rakta volume and drives excessive Artava outflow through Garbhashaya vessels; treat on Raktapitta/Raktatisara lines using Kashaya-Tikta-Shita Stambhana herbs (Ashoka, Lodhra, Durva, Nagakeshara, Pushyanuga Churna with Tandulodaka)","Purely uterine prolapse (Phalini); treat with Kshara cauterisation"],"a":2,"e":"Charaka (Chikitsasthana 30/204-228) explains Asrigdara (\u0027Asrik-darana\u0027, excessive/prolonged menstrual or intermenstrual bleeding): vitiated Vata augments circulating Rakta, reaching Rajovaha Siras of Garbhashaya to produce heavy bleeding (Pittaja subtype shows bright red/yellowish hot burning flow). Management mirrors Raktapitta and Raktatisara—employing Kashaya-Tikta-Shita Raktastambhana formulations like Pushyanuga Churna (with Tandulodaka/rice-wash water), Ashokarishta, Pradarantaka Lauha, and Bolabaddha Rasa.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00200","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Swasthavritta and Yoga","topic":"Patanjali Ashtanga Yoga \u0026 Hatha Yoga Shatkarmas","q":"In Hatha Yoga Pradipika and Gheranda Samhita (NCISM Swasthavritta \u0026 Yoga curriculum), which six yogic purification practices constitute the \u0027Shatkarmas\u0027 (performed prior to Pranayama to balance Kapha, Pitta, and Vata), and what is the correct sequence of the eight limbs of Patanjali\u0027s Ashtanga Yoga?","o":["Shatkarmas: Vamana, Virechana, Nasya, Raktamokshana, Shirodhara, Kati Basti; Ashtanga Yoga starts with Samadhi","Ashtanga Yoga: Niyama, Yama, Dhyana, Asana, Dharana, Samadhi, Pranayama, Pratyahara","Shatkarmas: Surya Namaskar, Savasana, Bhramari, Ujjayi, Bhastrika, Sheetali","Shatkarmas: Dhauti, Basti, Neti, Trataka, Nauli, Kapalabhati; Ashtanga Yoga: Yama, Niyama, Asana, Pranayama, Pratyahara, Dharana, Dhyana, Samadhi"],"a":3,"e":"Hatha Yoga Pradipika (Ch. 2) prescribes six Shatkarmas (\u0027Dhautir bastistatha netis tratakam naulikam tatha; Kapalabhatish chaitani shatkarmani prachakshate\u0027): Dhauti (upper GI/gastric wash), Basti (yogic colon wash), Neti (nasal cleansing), Trataka (steady gazing for ocular/mental focus), Nauli (abdominal recti churning), and Kapalabhati (rapid frontal-sinus/respiratory exhalations). Patanjali\u0027s eight limbs progress from external/ethical discipline to absorption: Yama, Niyama, Asana, Pranayama, Pratyahara, Dharana, Dhyana, Samadhi.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00201","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Kriya Sharir \u0026 Samhita Adhyayana","topic":"Seven Deha Prakritis — Formation \u0026 Prognostic Grading","q":"According to Charaka (Vimanasthana Ch. 8) and Sushruta (Sharirasthana Ch. 4), when is an individual\u0027s congenital Deha Prakriti (physical constitution) determined, and what is the classical hierarchy among the seven Prakritis from least resilient (Heena) to most resilient/optimal (Uttama)?","o":["Determined irreversibly at Shukra-Shonita Samyoga (conception/fertilisation); Vatala is Heena (lowest resilience), Pittala is Madhya, Kaphaja/Shleshmala is Uttama among Ekadoshaja, and Samadhatu/Sama-Prakriti (balanced Tridosha) is Shreshtha (best overall)","Formed exclusively by maternal postnatal sleep habits; Kaphaja is Heena","Changes every month after birth according to diet; Vatala is best overall","Determined only at puberty; Dwidoshaja is strictly superior to Samadhatu Prakriti"],"a":0,"e":"Ayurveda describes 7 Deha Prakritis (3 Ekadoshaja: Vata, Pitta, Kapha; 3 Dwidoshaja; 1 Sama/Sannipataja), genetically/epigenetically fixed at conception by the predominant Dosha at Shukra-Shonita Samyoga plus Matrija Ahara-Vihara, Kala, and Garbhashaya factors (\u0027Prakritir janma-marana-antam na parivartate\u0027). Charaka (Vimanasthana 8/95-99) ranks Sama-Prakriti as Shreshtha (highest health/immunity), followed by Kaphaja (Uttama), Pittaja (Madhya), Vataja (Heena), and Dwidoshaja as Nindya (mixed susceptibility).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00202","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Kayachikitsa","topic":"Uttana vs Gambhira Vatarakta Diagnosis \u0026 Chikitsa","q":"A 49-year-old man presents with acute severe burning pain, tense swelling, stiffness, and throbbing tenderness deep inside the first metatarsophalangeal and ankle joints (feeling as if rats are gnawing inside the bones — \u0027Mushakavaddamsha\u0027, progressing toward deformity), diagnosed as Gambhira Vatarakta (Charaka Chikitsasthana Ch. 29). How does Gambhira Vatarakta differ clinically and therapeutically from Uttana Vatarakta?","o":["Uttana affects superficial Twak-Mamsa (itching, burning, copper-red skin) treated primarily with Alepa, Abhyanga, Parisheka, and Upanaha; Gambhira affects deep Sandhi-Asthi-Majja causing bone pain/swelling/deformity treated with Mridu Virechana, Basti (Ksheera-Basti/Asthapana), Guduchi, and Kaishora Guggulu","Uttana requires immediate surgical amputation; Gambhira requires cold ice immersion exclusively","Both Uttana and Gambhira Vatarakta are pure Kapha diseases treated with heavy sweet curd and day-sleep","Gambhira Vatarakta affects only hair follicles without joint involvement"],"a":0,"e":"In Charaka Chikitsasthana Ch. 29 (Vatarakta Chikitsa), vitiated Vata obstructed by vitiated Rakta manifests in two depths: (1) Uttana Vatarakta stays in Twak and Mamsa (Kandu, Daha, Toda, Ayama, Shyava-Tamra skin) managed predominantly externally (Alepa, Abhyanga with Madhuyashtyadi/Pinda Taila, Parisheka, Upanaha); (2) Gambhira Vatarakta penetrates Sandhi-Asthi-Majja (Shwayathu, Stabdha, Kathina, Vrishchika/Mushaka-like deep gnawing pain, Khanja/Pangu deformity) requiring systemic Shodhana (Raktamokshana, Mridu Virechana, Basti — especially Ksheera-Basti) and Agrya Rasayanas like Guduchi, Amritadi/Kaishora Guggulu, and Kokilaksha.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00203","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Bhaishajya Kalpana","topic":"Standard Ratio (1:4:16) \u0026 Sneha Paka Grades (Mridu, Madhyama, Khara)","q":"In classical Sneha Kalpana (Sharangadhara Samhita Madhyama Khanda Ch. 9) using the standard Kalka : Sneha : Drava ratio of 1 : 4 : 16, match each Sneha Paka completion grade to its physical test and specific therapeutic route: 1) Mridu Paka, 2) Madhyama Paka, 3) Khara Paka.","o":["1-Used for external Abhyanga; 2-Used for eye drops only; 3-Used for oral Pana","1-Kalka retains slight moisture/softness (used for Nasya); 2-Kalka rolls smoothly into non-sticky Varti without crackling sound on fire (used for oral Pana \u0026 Basti); 3-Kalka becomes slightly crisp/hard on rubbing (used for external Abhyanga)","1-Ratio 16:4:1; 2-Dagdha Paka; 3-Ama Paka","1-Burnt black carbonised Kalka (used for oral Pana); 2-Watery emulsion (used for Nasya); 3-Raw unboiled Kalka (used for Basti)"],"a":1,"e":"Sharangadhara Samhita prescribes general Sneha Kalpana proportions of 1 part Kalka : 4 parts Sneha (Taila/Ghrita) : 16 parts Drava (Kwatha/Swarasa), boiled mildly until Sneha Siddhi Lakshanas appear (Varti-pariksha without moisture/Nihshabda on fire, Phenodgama in Taila / Phenashanti in Ghrita). The three therapeutic Paka endpoints are: Mridu Paka (for Nasya), Madhyama Paka (for all internal Pana and Basti uses), and Khara Paka (for external Abhyanga); Ama Paka and Dagdha (burnt) Paka are therapeutically unusable.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00204","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Panchakarma","topic":"Brimhana vs Ruksha Pinda Sweda Selection","q":"In clinical Panchakarma practice, a child with muscular dystrophy / post-stroke Dhatukshaya Pakshaghata (emaciated Vata-dominant neuromuscular wasting) requires a nourishing Brimhana sudation therapy, whereas an obese patient with acute Sama Amavata requires dry Ama-pachana sudation. Which pair of Swedana therapies should be selected respectively?","o":["Upanaha with heavy oil for acute Sama Amavata; starvation Niragni Sweda for Dhatukshaya","Valuka Sweda for emaciated Dhatukshaya; cold ice bath for Sama Amavata","Shashtika Shali Pinda Sweda (rice cooked in Bala Moola Kwatha + Ksheera) for Dhatukshaya/Pakshaghata; Valuka Sweda (dry heated sand bolus) or Ruksha Churna Pinda Sweda for Sama Amavata","Shashtika Shali Pinda Sweda for acute Sama Kapha-Amavata; dry mud pack for muscular dystrophy"],"a":2,"e":"Shashtika Shali Pinda Sweda (popularly Navarakizhi in Kerala Panchakarma) uses boluses of red Shashtika rice boiled in Bala Moola decoction and milk—delivering Snigdha-Brimhana-Balya nourishment ideal for Vata vyadhi, Pakshaghata, Balashosha, and neuromuscular wasting (contrary to routine Swedana\u0027s Dhatu-reducing effect). Conversely, Sama Amavata/Kapha-Meda disorders require Ruksha (dry, non-unctuous) Valuka Sweda (heated river sand) or dry Churna Pinda Sweda (Kottamchukkadi/Kolakulathadi dry bolus) to digest Ama without aggravating Kapha.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00205","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Shalya Tantra","topic":"Sushruta\u0027s Chaturvidha Chikitsa of Arsha","q":"In Sushruta Samhita (Chikitsasthana Ch. 6 — Arsha Chikitsa), four therapeutic modalities (Bheshaja, Kshara, Agni, Shastra) are matched precisely to pile morphology. Which pile characteristics specifically indicate Kshara Karma (caustic alkaline application) versus Agnikarma (thermal cautery)?","o":["Shastra excision for every Grade I asymptomatic pile; Bheshaja only after failed surgery","Kshara Karma exclusively for hard stone-like external tags; Agnikarma exclusively for early painless internal bleeding","Agnikarma inside the brain ventricles; Kshara only as an oral tonic","Kshara Karma for Mridu, Prasrita, Avagadha, and Ucchrita (soft, broad-based, deeply situated, elevated) piles; Agnikarma for Karkasha, Sthira, Prithu, and Kathina (rough, fixed, broad, hard external/Vata-Kapha) piles"],"a":3,"e":"Sushruta (Chikitsasthana 6/3) stratifies Arsha management cleanly: (1) Bheshaja (oral/local medicines) for recent, minimal-Dosha, non-prolapsing piles; (2) Pratisaraniya Kshara Karma (chemical cauterisation) for soft (Mridu), broad/spreading (Prasrita), deep-seated (Avagadha), and protruding/elevated (Ucchrita) internal piles (often Pitta-Rakta dominant); (3) Agnikarma for rough (Karkasha), fixed (Sthira), thick (Prithu), and hard (Kathina) external Vata-Kapha piles; (4) Shastra (surgical excision/Ksharasutra ligation) for narrow-pedicled, profusely bleeding, or refractory piles.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00206","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Padartha Vigyana evam Ayurveda Itihasa","topic":"Application of Dravya, Guna \u0026 Karma Samanya-Vishesha","q":"Charaka Samhita (Sutrasthana 1/44) states the foundational therapeutic axiom: \u0027Sarvada sarva bhavanam samanyam vriddhikaranam; hrasa hetur visheshascha\u0027. Matching clinical practice to Dravya, Guna, and Karma Samanya-Vishesha, prescribing Ksheera (milk) and Ghrita to increase Shukra Dhatu, versus advising brisk Vyayama (physical exercise) to reduce Medoroga (obesity), illustrate which two specific principles respectively?","o":["Guna Samanya (using Snigdha-Madhura-Shita properties similar to Shukra) and Karma Vishesha (using opposite active movement/exercise to reduce Guru-Sthira Medas)","Karma Samanya and Samavaya","Pratyahara and Srotas-dushti","Dravya Vishesha and Abhava Padartha"],"a":0,"e":"Chakrapani explains Charaka\u0027s Samanya-Vishesha triad in clinical therapeutics: (1) Dravya Samanya uses identical structural tissue (e.g., Mamsa for Mamsakshaya, Rakta for Raktakshaya); (2) Guna Samanya increases a Dhatu via homologous qualities (Ksheera/Ghrita are non-identical to Shukra yet share Snigdha, Madhura, Shita, Guru gunas increasing Shukra); (3) Karma Vishesha decreases excess Kapha/Medas via opposing action (Laghu-Chala Vyayama opposing Guru-Manda-Sthira Medas).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00207","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Kayachikitsa","topic":"Navajwara (Acute Ama Stage) vs Jeernajwara Protocol","q":"A 26-year-old man presents on Day 2 of acute high fever with anorexia, heaviness, body ache, and coated tongue (Taruna / Navajwara in Ama stage, Charaka Chikitsasthana Ch. 3). Why are strongly astringent (Kashaya) decoctions, daytime sleep, cold bath, and early Ghritapana STRICTLY AVOIDED during the first 6 days of Navajwara, and what classical regimen is prescribed instead?","o":["Give heavy cold buffalo milk and thick Kashaya astringents on Day 1 to freeze Ama inside Amashaya","Early Kashaya/Ghrita obstructs Dosha-pachana (Stambhana of Ama Dosha) converting acute fever into chronic Vishamajwara; treat Navajwara with Langhana, Swedana, Kala (waiting up to 7 days), Yavagu, and Shadanga Paniya / Tikta-Pachana (while reserving Ghritapana/Ksheera for Jeernajwara \u003e21 days)","Perform immediate heavy Vamana and Virechana on Day 1 regardless of Ama and patient strength","Administer iced water baths and heavy non-vegetarian Mamsarasa continuously on Day 2"],"a":1,"e":"Charaka (Chikitsasthana 3/139-165) prescribes \u0027Langhanam swedanam kalo yavagvastikta ko rasah; Pachananyavipakwanam doshanam tarune jware\u0027. In acute Navajwara (first 6-7 days), immature Ama blocks Srotas; astringent Kashaya-rasa (binding/Stambhana) and heavy Snigdha Ghrita seal unripe Doshas inside Dhatus, precipitating irregular/chronic Vishamajwara. Instead, Langhana, mild Swedana, lukewarm Shadanga Paniya (Musta, Parpata, Ushira, Chandana, Udichya, Nagara), and Tikta Yavagu digest Ama until Nirama Pakwa Jwara (~Day 7-12), while nourishing Ghrita/Ksheera enter only in Jeernajwara (\u003e21 days).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00208","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Rasashastra evam Agada Tantra","topic":"Classical Shodhana Media for Vatsanabha, Kupilu \u0026 Bhallataka","q":"In Ayurvedic Rasashastra \u0026 Dravyaguna (AFI / Rasatarangini), match each potent Visha/Upavisha drug to its classical Shodhana (detoxification) medium and method: 1) Vatsanabha (Aconitum ferox root), 2) Kupilu (Strychnos nux-vomica seeds), 3) Bhallataka (Semecarpus anacardium nuts).","o":["1-Washing with soap only; 2-Grinding raw testa and embryo; 3-Steaming in mercury","1-Rubbing in brick powder; 2-Boiling in cold water for 10 min; 3-Soaking in raw alcohol without heat","1-Soaking thin pieces in Gomutra (cow\u0027s urine) for 3 days under sunlight or Swedana in Godugdha via Dolayantra; 2-Soaking in Gomutra for 7 days followed by peeling outer testa/embryo and frying in Goghrita; 3-Rubbing punctured nuts in brick powder (Ishtika churna) inside a cloth bag after soaking in Gomutra/Narikelodaka to absorb corrosive oil","1-Frying in mustard oil; 2-Mixing directly raw into Triphala; 3-Subliming in Valukayantra at 800 C"],"a":2,"e":"Classical Shodhana measurably attenuates toxic alkaloids and blister-causing phenolic oils while retaining therapeutic efficacy: Vatsanabha (aconitine/pseudoaconitine) undergoes 3-day Gomutra Nimajjana or Godugdha Dolayantra Swedana (partially hydrolysing toxic diester diterpene alkaloids to less toxic benzoylaconine); Kupilu (strychnine/brucine) undergoes 7-day Gomutra soaking, removal of toxic seed coat/cotyledonous embryo (Kankustha/embryo leaf), and Goghrita Bharjana; Bhallataka nuts are soaked and vigorously rubbed with absorbent brick powder (Ishtika Churna) to strip blistering bhilawanol/cardol oil.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00209","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Panchakarma","topic":"Shirodhara (Tailadhara / Takradhara / Ksheeradhara)","q":"Among Vagbhata\u0027s four Murdha Taila procedures (Shiroabhyanga, Shiroseka/Shirodhara, Shiropichu, Shirobasti), Shirodhara delivers a continuous rhythmic liquid stream onto the forehead. What is the standard pouring height above the forehead, and which specific liquid medium (Dhara dravya) is classically preferred in Pitta-Kaphaja Kushtha (e.g., scalp psoriasis) and Prameha-associated stress disorders?","o":["Pour from 0.1 cm touching the nose tip; use concentrated Gomutra only for insomnia","Shirodhara involves dry sand friction over the knee joint","Pour boiling oil at 95 C from 100 cm height directly into open eyes; use pure raw mustard oil in acute psoriasis","Pour from ~4 angula (~7-8 cm) height smoothly across the forehead (Ajna/Sthapani region) for 30-45 minutes; Takradhara (medicated buttermilk processed with Musta \u0026 Amalaki) is specifically preferred for Pitta-Kaphaja Kushtha/psoriasis and Prameha"],"a":3,"e":"In Shirodhara (Shiroseka, one of Ashtanga Hridaya\u0027s four Murdha Tailas—highest potency belongs to Shirobasti, followed by Shirodhara/Pichu/Abhyanga), lukewarm medicated liquid flows rhythmically from a Dhara-patra suspended 4 angula (~7.5 cm) above the mid-forehead/eyebrows for 30-45 min: Tailadhara (Ksheerabala/Brahmi/Dhanwantaram) pacifies Vata (insomnia, anxiety, Shirashoola), Ksheeradhara cools severe Pitta, and Takradhara (Amalaki-Musta processed buttermilk) excels in Ekakushtha/psoriasis, greying/alopecia, and stress-linked metabolic disorders.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00210","course":"BAMS - Ayurveda","subject":"Kaumarabhritya","topic":"Pranapratyagamana (Ayurvedic Neonatal Resuscitation)","q":"In Charaka Samhita (Sharirasthana Ch. 8) and Ashtanga Hridaya (Uttaratantra Ch. 1), which sequential set of neonatal resuscitation and immediate newborn care steps constitutes \u0027Pranapratyagamana\u0027 and Navajata Shishu Paricharya?","o":["Striking two stones near the infant\u0027s ears and fanning with a winnowing basket (Shurpa) if apnoeic/unresponsive, clearing mouth/throat mucus (Mukha-vishodhana), removing vernix (Ulba) gently with Saindhava-Ghrita/Bala Taila, placing a Bala Taila-soaked cotton Pichu over the anterior fontanelle (Talu), and gentle Garbhodaka Vamana with Saindhava-Ghrita","Withholding all tactile stimulation and feeding 200 mL honey-ghee bolus forcefully in the first 30 seconds","Applying caustic Apamarga Kshara over the umbilical stump and anterior fontanelle","Submerging the newborn immediately in ice-cold river water for 10 minutes before cord clamping"],"a":0,"e":"Charaka and Vagbhata describe structured immediate neonatal resuscitation (Pranapratyagamana): auditory/tactile stimulation (striking stones near the ears, cold/warm water sprinkling in faint neonates, gentle fanning via Krishna-kapalika Shurpa), airway clearing of pharyngeal mucus (Mukha-vishodhana), gentle vernix (Ulba) cleansing with Bala Taila/Saindhava-Sarpi, protecting the open fontanelle (Talu/bregma) with a Sneha-soaked Pichu, umbilical cord clamping/cutting (Nabhinala Chedana at 4-8 angula), and clearing swallowed amniotic fluid (Garbhodaka Vamana).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00211","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Diagnostic Audiology","topic":"Tympanometry Classification (Jerger)","q":"On 226-Hz tympanometry, a patient with conductive hearing loss demonstrates a flat tympanogram with NO measurable peak compliance and normal ear canal volume (0.9 mL). What is the Jerger classification and most consistent clinical correlate?","o":["Type As tympanogram — consistent with ossicular chain discontinuity","Type B tympanogram with normal ear canal volume — consistent with middle ear effusion (otitis media with effusion)","Type B tympanogram with large ear canal volume (\u003e2.5 mL) — consistent with tympanic membrane perforation","Type C tympanogram — consistent with normal Eustachian tube function"],"a":1,"e":"In Jerger tympanogram classification: Type A = normal peak (-100 to +50 daPa, normal compliance); Type As = shallow peak (stiff system, e.g., otosclerosis); Type Ad = hypercompliant deep peak (flaccid TM or ossicular discontinuity); Type C = negative peak pressure \u003c-100 daPa (Eustachian tube dysfunction); Type B = flat trace with normal ear canal volume indicates middle ear fluid/effusion (whereas Type B with high ECV indicates TM perforation or patent grommet).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00212","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Diagnostic Audiology","topic":"Tympanometry \u0026 Acoustic Reflexes","q":"A 34-year-old woman with progressive bilateral conductive hearing loss and normal otoscopic examination undergoes 226 Hz tympanometry. Results show ear canal volume 1.1 mL, tympanometric peak pressure 0 daPa, static compliance 0.18 mL (markedly reduced), and absent ipsilateral and contralateral acoustic reflexes. Which Jerger tympanogram type and diagnosis fit best?","o":["Type B with normal ear canal volume; tympanic membrane perforation","Type C; acute Eustachian tube dysfunction","Type As; stapedial fixation (otosclerosis)","Type Ad; ossicular chain discontinuity"],"a":2,"e":"A Type As (\u0027shallow\u0027) tympanogram exhibits normal peak pressure near 0 daPa alongside reduced static admittance/compliance (\u003c 0.3 mL in adults), reflecting increased stiffness of the middle ear system—classically seen in otosclerosis (stapes footplate fixation) or tympanosclerosis—accompanied by absent acoustic reflexes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00213","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Audiometry \u0026 Clinical Masking","topic":"Cross-Hearing \u0026 Interaural Attenuation","q":"During pure-tone audiometry using TDH-39 supra-aural headphones (interaural attenuation = 40 dB), a patient\u0027s unmasked air-conduction threshold in the right ear at 1000 Hz is 65 dB HL, while the left ear bone-conduction threshold is 10 dB HL. Why is masking of the left ear required, and what bone-conduction notch at 2000 Hz supports otosclerosis?","o":["Masking is applied to the right (test) ear using wideband noise; Tullio notch at 1000 Hz","Bone conduction has 40 dB interaural attenuation so no masking is ever required; Schwartz sign at 500 Hz","Unmasked AC(right) - AC(left) must exceed 80 dB before masking; Citelli\u0027s notch at 4000 Hz","Unmasked AC(right) - BC(left) = 55 dB (\u003e= 40 dB IA), risking cross-hearing; Carhart\u0027s notch at 2000 Hz"],"a":3,"e":"When using supra-aural earphones (minimum interaural attenuation ~40 dB), masking of the non-test (left) ear is mandatory whenever unmasked AC of the test ear minus best BC of the non-test ear \u003e= 40 dB (here 65 - 10 = 55 dB), because signal crosses via skull vibration to stimulate the better cochlea. Carhart\u0027s notch is an artificial 15-20 dB dip in bone-conduction thresholds peaking at 2000 Hz caused by loss of ossicular inertial component in otosclerosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00214","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Adult Neurogenic Communication Disorders","topic":"Differential Diagnosis of Aphasias","q":"A 61-year-old man recovering from a left parietal ischemic stroke produces fluent, grammatically well-structured spontaneous speech interrupted by frequent phonemic paraphasias (e.g., \u0027tevelision\u0027 for \u0027television\u0027) that he immediately attempts to self-correct. His auditory comprehension is intact for multi-step commands, but he fails completely on verbatim sentence repetition. Which aphasia syndrome does this profile represent?","o":["Conduction aphasia","Transcortical sensory aphasia","Wernicke\u0027s aphasia","Broca\u0027s aphasia"],"a":0,"e":"Conduction aphasia results from damage to the arcuate fasciculus / left supramarginal gyrus disconnecting Wernicke\u0027s and Broca\u0027s areas. Its hallmark triad is fluent spontaneous speech with phonemic paraphasias (and conduit d\u0027approche self-corrections), preserved auditory comprehension, and disproportionately impaired repetition.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00215","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Pediatric \u0026 Electrophysiological Audiology","topic":"Auditory Neuropathy Spectrum Disorder (ANSD)","q":"A 4-month-old infant with a history of NICU stay for severe hyperbilirubinemia passes bilateral Transient Evoked Otoacoustic Emissions (TEOAEs), but click-evoked Auditory Brainstem Response (ABR) shows absent Waves I-V at 90 dB nHL alongside a prominent early ringing waveform that inverts when stimulus polarity flips from rarefaction to condensation. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Bilateral otitis media with effusion","Auditory Neuropathy Spectrum Disorder (ANSD)","Normal neonatal auditory maturation","Pure outer hair cell cochlear sensory loss"],"a":1,"e":"ANSD involves normal outer hair cell electromotility—evidenced by present OAEs and a large cochlear microphonic (CM) that reverses phase when rarefaction/condensation clicks are alternated—paired with disrupted inner hair cell/ribbon synapse/CN VIII neural synchrony, resulting in absent or grossly abnormal ABR waves. Severe neonatal hyperbilirubinemia is a classic risk factor.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00216","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Voice \u0026 Laryngeal Disorders","topic":"Vocal Fold Nodules","q":"A primary school teacher presents with chronic hoarseness and vocal fatigue worsening at the end of the workday. Videolaryngostroboscopy shows bilateral, symmetric pearly-white mucosal swellings creating an \u0027hourglass\u0027 glottic closure pattern. Where are vocal fold nodules anatomically located, and what is the primary treatment?","o":["False vocal folds (ventricular bands); systemic corticosteroids","Posterior glottis over the vocal processes of arytenoids; immediate CO2 laser excision","Junction of the anterior one-third and middle one-third of the membranous true vocal folds; behavioral voice therapy and vocal hygiene","Subglottic cricoid ring; botulinum toxin injection"],"a":2,"e":"The midpoint of the membranous vocal fold—corresponding to the junction of the anterior one-third and middle one-third of the entire vocal fold length—experiences maximal mechanical shearing stress during phonotrauma, producing bilateral symmetric vocal fold nodules and an hourglass chink on stroboscopy. Behavioral voice therapy (resonant voice, vocal hygiene) is first-line treatment and resolves most nodules without surgery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00217","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Dysphagia Assessment \u0026 Rehabilitation","topic":"Upper Esophageal Sphincter (UES) Dysfunction","q":"On a Videofluoroscopic Swallow Study (VFSS), a patient with brainstem stroke demonstrates reduced superior-anterior hyolaryngeal excursion and incomplete cricopharyngeal / Upper Esophageal Sphincter (UES) opening, resulting in bilateral pyriform sinus residue and post-swallow aspiration. Which swallowing maneuver specifically prolongs laryngeal elevation to maximize the width and duration of UES opening?","o":["Head rotation toward the stronger (unaffected) pharyngeal side","Head extension (chin-up) posture during thin-liquid bolus intake","Thermal-tactile application to the anterior faucial pillars only","Mendelsohn maneuver (voluntarily holding the larynx elevated at its peak height for 2-3 seconds during the swallow)"],"a":3,"e":"The Mendelsohn maneuver instructs the patient to feel the larynx rise during a swallow and voluntarily hold it at its highest/most anterior point for 2-3 seconds before relaxing. Because anterior-superior traction of the hyolaryngeal complex mechanically pulls open the cricopharyngeus (UES), this maneuver directly increases UES opening width and duration, clearing pyriform sinus residue.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00218","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Aural Rehabilitation \u0026 Amplification","topic":"Real-Ear-to-Coupler Difference (RECD)","q":"When fitting behind-the-ear (BTE) hearing aids on a 9-month-old infant with bilateral moderate sensorineural hearing loss using DSL v5 targets, why MUST the audiologist measure the infant\u0027s individual Real-Ear-to-Coupler Difference (RECD) rather than using adult 2-cc coupler averages?","o":["An infant\u0027s much smaller residual ear canal volume generates higher sound pressure levels (often +10 to +20 dB higher) at the tympanic membrane than in a standard 2-cc coupler, risking over-amplification and acoustic trauma if uncorrected","RECD measures middle ear stapedial reflex fatigue","RECD is used solely to program bone-anchored hearing aids (BAHA)","Infants have vastly larger ear canals than adults, causing under-amplification by 15 dB"],"a":0,"e":"Acoustic pressure generated by a hearing aid receiver is inversely proportional to enclosed cavity volume. Because an infant\u0027s ear canal between the earmold tip and eardrum is far smaller than an adult\u0027s (or standard 2-cc HA-2 coupler), real-ear SPL can exceed coupler values by 10-20 dB—making individual RECD probe-microphone measurement essential to accurately convert 2-cc coupler output to real-ear SPL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00219","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Implantable Hearing Devices","topic":"Tonotopic Electrode Array \u0026 Intraoperative ECAP/NRT","q":"In a multichannel cochlear implant system inserted into the scala tympani, which neural elements are directly depolarized, how are frequencies mapped along the electrode array, and what does intraoperative Neural Response Telemetry (NRT / ECAP) confirm?","o":["Stapes footplate vibration; all electrodes fire identically without frequency mapping","Spiral ganglion neurons (auditory nerve fibers) are directly stimulated electrically; basal electrodes encode high frequencies and apical electrodes encode low frequencies; NRT/ECAP confirms synchronous auditory nerve firing and electrode integrity","Outer hair cells are amplified mechanically; apical electrodes encode high frequencies; NRT tests tympanic membrane compliance","Inferior colliculus neurons; basal electrodes encode low frequencies; NRT measures middle ear air pressure"],"a":1,"e":"Cochlear implants bypass absent/damaged cochlear hair cells to directly stimulate surviving spiral ganglion neurons inside the modiolus. Preserving natural cochlear tonotopy, basal contacts (near the round window) deliver high-frequency channels while deep apical contacts deliver low-frequency channels. Intraoperative ECAP (e.g., NRT/NRI) records whole-nerve compound action potentials from adjacent electrodes to verify neural responsiveness and guide initial programming.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00220","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Motor Speech Disorders","topic":"Acquired Apraxia of Speech (AOS)","q":"Following a left anterior insular/inferior frontal stroke, a 58-year-old patient demonstrates normal oral-motor strength and intact swallowing, yet struggles visibly with articulatory groping, produces inconsistent phoneme substitutions on repeated attempts at the same multisyllabic word (\u0027catastrophe\u0027), shows prosodic slowness, and yet speaks automatic phrases (\u0027thank you\u0027, counting 1-10) smoothly without errors. Which motor speech disorder is present?","o":["Flaccid dysarthria (lower motor neuron bulbar palsy)","Ataxic dysarthria (cerebellar incoordination)","Acquired Apraxia of Speech (AOS, a disorder of speech motor planning/programming)","Hypokinetic dysarthria (Parkinsonian rigidity)"],"a":2,"e":"Acquired Apraxia of Speech (AOS) is a sensorimotor planning/programming disorder (most often linked to left anterior insula / Broca\u0027s area lesions) in the absence of muscle weakness or paralysis. Diagnostic hallmarks include visible/audible articulatory groping, inconsistent errors across repetitions, word-length complexity effect, prosodic disruption, and \u0027islands of error-free automatic speech.\u0027","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00221","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Vestibular Assessment \u0026 Rehabilitation","topic":"Bithermal Caloric Testing","q":"During bithermal water caloric testing (30 C cool, 44 C warm) in Videonystagmography (VNG), which direction does the fast phase of induced nystagmus normally beat, and what does a Jongkees Unilateral Weakness (canal paresis) calculation of 42% (\u003e 25% cutoff) on the left side indicate?","o":["Cool beats toward the irrigated ear, Warm beats away; indicates a central cerebellar tumor only","COWS (Cool Opposite, Warm Same) fast-phase direction; \u003e= 25% Unilateral Weakness on the left localizes a peripheral vestibular hypofunction involving the left horizontal semicircular canal / superior vestibular nerve","Both cool and warm beat downward; indicates normal bilateral symmetry","COWS applies only to vertical semicircular canals during rotational chair testing"],"a":1,"e":"With the patient supine and head elevated 30 degrees (placing the horizontal semicircular canal vertical), warm 44 C irrigation creates ampullopetal endolymph convection (excitatory -\u003e fast-phase nystagmus toward the SAME ear), whereas cool 30 C irrigation causes ampullofugal flow (inhibitory -\u003e fast-phase toward the OPPOSITE ear: COWS). A \u003e= 20-25% asymmetry on Jongkees formula indicates unilateral peripheral hypofunction on the weaker side.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00222","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Fluency Disorders","topic":"Core Disfluencies \u0026 Early Intervention","q":"Which speech disfluency profile distinguishes early childhood Developmental Stuttering from typical normal childhood disfluencies, and what characterizes the evidence-based Lidcombe Program for preschoolers (\u003c 6 years)?","o":["Whole-phrase repetitions without tension; Lidcombe uses delayed auditory feedback devices exclusively","Complete mutism in school only; Lidcombe teaches sign language exclusively","Rapid irregular speech rate with omitted syllables and unawareness (cluttering); Lidcombe prescribes daily haloperidol","Stuttering-like disfluencies (part-word/sound-syllable repetitions, audible/silent blocks, sound prolongations, often with secondary physical tension); Lidcombe is a parent-delivered behavioral operant program using positive verbal contingencies for stutter-free speech"],"a":3,"e":"While normal disfluencies feature interjections (\u0027um\u0027), revisions, and multi-word phrase repetitions, Stuttering-Like Disfluencies (SLDs) consist of part-word/single-syllable repetitions (\u0027b-b-ball\u0027), prolongations (\u0027ssssun\u0027), and tense blocks. The Lidcombe Program is a randomized-trial-validated operant treatment where trained parents deliver frequent positive feedback for stutter-free speech and gentle acknowledgments/corrections during daily conversations.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00223","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Craniofacial \u0026 Resonance Disorders","topic":"Velopharyngeal Insufficiency (VPI)","q":"A 6-year-old boy who underwent primary cleft palate repair at 18 months exhibits marked hypernasality on vowels, audible nasal air emission on high-pressure oral consonants (/p, b, t, d, k, g, s/), weak intraoral consonant pressure, and substitution of glottal stops for plosives. Instrumental nasendoscopy shows a persistent central velopharyngeal gap during connected speech. Why is speech therapy ALONE insufficient here?","o":["Structural Velopharyngeal Insufficiency (VPI) prevents physical velopharyngeal port closure; surgical revision (e.g., Furlow palatoplasty/pharyngeal flap) or a palatal lift/speech bulb prosthesis is required to restore anatomical closure, followed by speech therapy to eliminate learned compensatory glottal stops","The child simply has isolated hyponasality from enlarged adenoids","Nasal air emission improves spontaneously with tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy","Glottal stops are normal developmental speech milestones at age 6"],"a":0,"e":"When a child cannot physically seal the velopharyngeal port due to short/scarred velum (VPI), air escapes into the nose during high-pressure consonants (plosives, fricatives), prompting compensatory articulation below the leak (glottal stops/pharyngeal fricatives). Behavioral speech therapy cannot overcome a physical anatomical gap—physical management (secondary surgery or prosthesis) is required alongside articulation therapy to unlearn compensatory habits.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00224","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Diagnostic Audiology \u0026 Psychoacoustics","topic":"Metz Loudness Recruitment \u0026 Acoustic Reflex Decay","q":"How do the Metz test (Acoustic Reflex Threshold sensation level) and the Acoustic Reflex Decay test (10-second sustained stimulation at 500/1000 Hz) differentiate COCHLEAR pathology from RETROCOCHLEAR (CN VIII) pathology?","o":["Cochlear lesions show absent reflexes at all intensities; retrocochlear lesions show enhanced reflexes with zero decay","Cochlear lesions show loudness recruitment—acoustic reflexes appear at REDUCED sensation levels (\u003c 60 dB SL, positive Metz test) WITHOUT decay; retrocochlear lesions show elevated/absent reflexes or \u003e 50% amplitude decay within 10 seconds (positive reflex decay)","Both cochlear and retrocochlear lesions produce identical Type B flat tympanograms","Metz test evaluates otoacoustic emissions exclusively"],"a":1,"e":"Normally, the stapedial acoustic reflex triggers 70-90 dB above pure-tone threshold (SL). In cochlear outer hair cell damage (Meniere\u0027s, NIHL), abnormal rapid loudness growth (\u0027recruitment\u0027) triggers the reflex at \u003c 60 dB SL (positive Metz test) while maintaining full contraction over 10 s. Conversely, CN VIII compression (vestibular schwannoma) causes neural fatigue/adaptation—seen as \u003e= 50% reflex amplitude drop within 10 s at 500/1000 Hz (positive reflex decay).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00225","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Audiometric Pattern Interpretation","topic":"Noise-Induced Hearing Loss vs Meniere\u0027s Disease","q":"Audiologist A tests a 42-year-old textile loom operator with chronic bilateral tinnitus, while Audiologist B tests a 38-year-old woman with episodic 3-hour vertigo spells, aural fullness, and fluctuating roaring tinnitus in the left ear. Which classic pure-tone audiometric configurations match Patient A and Patient B respectively?","o":["Patient A: Low-frequency conductive loss; Patient B: 4000 Hz bilateral bone-conduction notch","Patient A: Bilateral symmetric sensorineural \u0027notch\u0027 deepest at 4000 Hz (3000-6000 Hz) with recovery at 8000 Hz; Patient B: Unilateral low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss (rising/upward-sloping audiogram) in early Meniere\u0027s disease","Patient A: Flat 90 dB mixed loss; Patient B: Isolated 2000 Hz Carhart notch","Patient A: High-frequency ski-slope down to 8000 Hz without recovery; Patient B: Bilateral Type As tympanogram"],"a":1,"e":"External ear canal resonance (~2700-3000 Hz) and basal cochlear vulnerability concentrate acoustic trauma energy half an octave above resonance, producing the hallmark 4000 Hz (3000-6000 Hz) sensorineural notch with improved thresholds at 8000 Hz (distinguishing NIHL from presbycusis, which slopes steadily down through 8000 Hz). Endolymphatic hydrops (Meniere\u0027s disease) characteristically affects the cochlear apex first, producing fluctuating low-frequency SNHL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00226","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC)","topic":"Bulbar ALS Communication Support","q":"A 54-year-old man newly diagnosed with bulbar-onset Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) currently has mild dysarthria (speaking rate dropping toward 100 words/min) and progressive upper-limb weakness, with intact cognition and extraocular eye movements. Which proactive Speech-Language Pathology AAC sequence preserves personalized communication into late-stage disease?","o":["Fit a direct-touch touchscreen tablet only, ignoring progressive hand paralysis","Wait until the patient becomes completely anarthric and quadriplegic before discussing AAC","Perform early VOICE BANKING (recording speech samples while intelligible to build a personalized synthetic voice) and introduce multimodal AAC transitioning to an Eye-Gaze-controlled Speech-Generating Device (SGD) as upper-limb access declines","Teach forceful shouting drills to strengthen bulbar motor neurons"],"a":2,"e":"In ALS, declining speaking rate (\u003c 100-125 words/min) predicts imminent loss of intelligible speech. Best-practice SLP care initiates voice/message banking EARLY while speech remains clear, then introduces non-fatiguing AAC strategies—ultimately transitioning to eye-gaze tracking Speech-Generating Devices because extraocular motor nuclei remain spared even in advanced ALS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00227","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Speech Acoustics \u0026 Phonetics","topic":"Vowel Formants (F1, F2) \u0026 Voice Onset Time (VOT)","q":"In the acoustic Source-Filter Theory of vowel production, how do the first two vowel formants (F1 and F2) correspond to tongue articulation, and which acoustic cue distinguishes voiced (/b, d, g/) from voiceless (/p, t, k/) stop consonants?","o":["Voiceless stops have negative pre-voiced VOT (\u003c -50 ms) while voiced stops have VOT \u003e +80 ms","F0 measures pharyngeal cavity length; F1 measures nasalance exclusively","F1 correlates directly with tongue height (high vowel = high F1); F2 tracks lip rounding only","F1 is INVERSELY related to tongue height (high vowel /i/ has low F1; low vowel /a/ has high F1), while F2 correlates DIRECTLY with tongue advancement (front vowel /i/ has high F2; back vowel /u/ has low F2); Voice Onset Time (VOT) distinguishes voiced vs voiceless stops"],"a":3,"e":"In spectrographic acoustic analysis, vocal fold vibration rate sets Fundamental Frequency (F0), while the supraglottic vocal tract filters formants: F1 inversely tracks tongue height (low F1 for /i, u/, high F1 for /a/), and F2 tracks tongue front-back position (high F2 for front /i/, low F2 for back /u/). Voice Onset Time (interval from stop-burst release to laryngeal voicing onset) is short/negative for voiced stops and long (+30 to +80 ms) for voiceless aspirated stops.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00228","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Child Language Disorders","topic":"Joint Attention \u0026 Social-Pragmatic Deficits","q":"A 30-month-old boy is brought for delayed speech. He pulls his mother\u0027s hand toward a shelf (\u0027hand-leading\u0027 proto-imperative) without making eye contact, fails to follow her gaze or point to share interest in a passing train (absent proto-declarative joint attention), repeats cartoon phrases verbatim (delayed echolalia), and has normal pure-tone/ABR hearing. Which core communication domain is impaired, and what early SLP intervention is prioritized?","o":["Social-pragmatic communication and Joint Attention characteristic of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD); prioritize Naturalistic Developmental Behavioral Interventions (NDBI) targeting shared joint attention, functional communication (e.g., PECS/AAC if needed), and caregiver-mediated responsiveness","Isolated Childhood Apraxia of Speech with normal social reciprocity; oral non-speech blowing exercises","Isolated peripheral conductive hearing loss; tympanostomy tubes","Isolated phonological fronting process; minimal pair drills only"],"a":0,"e":"Absent proto-declarative pointing/gaze-following (joint attention), instrumental hand-leading without social referencing, and non-communicative delayed echolalia in the presence of intact hearing point strongly to Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD). Evidence-based SLP intervention uses Naturalistic Developmental Behavioral Interventions (NDBI, e.g., ESDM, JASPER, Hanen More Than Words) and early multimodal AAC/PECS to build joint attention and functional symbolic communication.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00229","course":"BASLP - Audiology \u0026 Speech-Language Pathology","subject":"Industrial \u0026 Environmental Audiology","topic":"Permissible Noise Exposure \u0026 Standard Threshold Shift (STS)","q":"In an occupational hearing conservation program following NIOSH recommended standards (85 dBA 8-hour Time-Weighted Average with a 3-dB exchange rate), what is the maximum safe daily unprotected exposure duration at 91 dBA, and how is a reportable Standard Threshold Shift (STS) defined on annual monitoring audiometry?","o":["4 hours; \u003e= 5 dB shift at 500 Hz only","2 hours (every +3 dB halves allowable duration: 85-\u003e8 h, 88-\u003e4 h, 91-\u003e2 h); STS is a change in hearing threshold relative to baseline of an AVERAGE of \u003e= 10 dB at 2000, 3000, and 4000 Hz in either ear","1 hour; \u003e= 15 dB shift at 250 and 500 Hz","8 hours; \u003e= 25 dB shift across all frequencies"],"a":1,"e":"Under the equal-energy 3-dB exchange rate (NIOSH/ISO 1999 and India\u0027s Directorate General Factory Advice Service/DGMS norms), each 3 dB increase doubles acoustic energy and halves permissible exposure time: 85 dBA = 8 h, 88 dBA = 4 h, 91 dBA = 2 h, 94 dBA = 1 h. A Standard Threshold Shift (STS) is defined as an average worsening of \u003e= 10 dB across 2000, 3000, and 4000 Hz in either ear compared with the baseline audiogram, triggering retesting, refitting hearing protection, and counseling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00230","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Conservative Dentistry \u0026 Endodontics","topic":"Root Canal Instrumentation \u0026 Working Length","q":"During root canal therapy of a mandibular first molar, an electronic apex locator indicates \"0.0 / APEX\" while the radiographic working length file tip lies 0.8 mm short of the radiographic apex. Where should the working length termination point ideally be established?","o":["4.0 mm short of the radiographic apex regardless of apex locator reading","2.0 mm beyond the radiographic apex to ensure complete periapical debridement","At the minor apical diameter (apical constriction / cementodentinal junction), typically 0.5-1.0 mm short of the radiographic apex","At the pulp chamber floor orifice"],"a":2,"e":"The apical constriction (minor apical diameter, closely approximating the cementodentinal junction) is typically 0.5-1.0 mm short of the anatomical/radiographic apex because the apical foramen frequently exits laterally. Electronic apex locators detect the periodontal ligament impedance transition accurately at the apical constriction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00231","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Dental Anatomy, Embryology and Oral Histology","topic":"Mandibular First Molar Anatomy","q":"Which anatomical and notation description accurately identifies the permanent mandibular left first molar according to the FDI two-digit system?","o":["Tooth 26; typically has 5 cusps including the cusp of Carabelli and 3 roots","Tooth 46; typically has 4 cusps with an oblique ridge and 3 roots","Tooth 37; typically has 4 symmetrical cusps forming a \u0027+\u0027 occlusal groove pattern and 1 fused root","Tooth 36; typically has 5 cusps (mesiobuccal, distobuccal, distal, mesiolingual, distolingual) and 2 roots (mesial usually with 2 canals, distal with 1 or 2 canals)"],"a":3,"e":"In the FDI two-digit system, quadrant 3 is the permanent mandibular left quadrant and tooth 6 is the first molar (36). The mandibular first molar is the largest mandibular tooth, characteristically bearing 5 functional/non-functional cusps (3 buccal: MB, DB, distal; 2 lingual: ML, DL) with a Y-shaped dryopithecine central groove pattern, and 2 roots (wider mesial root containing mesiobuccal and mesiolingual canals; distal root with 1 or 2 canals).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00232","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral Pathology and Oral Microbiology","topic":"Ameloblastoma Histopathology \u0026 Radiology","q":"A 36-year-old man presents with a painless, slow-growing expansile swelling of the posterior right mandible causing buccolingual cortical expansion. Orthopantomogram reveals a multilocular \u0027soap-bubble\u0027 radiolucency with root resorption of adjacent molars. Incisional biopsy shows islands of odontogenic epithelium lined peripherally by tall columnar cells exhibiting reverse nuclear polarisation away from the basement membrane, subnuclear vacuolisation, and central stellate reticulum-like cells. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Conventional (solid/multicystic) ameloblastoma","Calcifying epithelial odontogenic tumour (Pindborg tumour)","Dentigerous (follicular) cyst","Adenomatoid odontogenic tumour (AOT)"],"a":0,"e":"Conventional ameloblastoma most commonly involves the posterior mandible (~80%), presenting as a painless expansile multilocular (\u0027soap-bubble\u0027 or \u0027honeycomb\u0027) radiolucency causing root resorption. Histopathologically, it fulfils the Vickers-Gorlin criteria: peripheral tall columnar ameloblast-like cells showing palisading, hyperchromatism, reverse nuclear polarisation (nuclei displaced away from the basement membrane), and cytoplasmic subnuclear vacuolisation surrounding loose stellate reticulum-like central cells.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00233","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"NaOCl, EDTA \u0026 Chlorhexidine Interactions","q":"During chemomechanical root canal preparation, a clinician irrigates with 2.5% sodium hypochlorite (NaOCl) and plans to follow with 2% chlorhexidine (CHX) as a final rinse. Why must 17% EDTA be used sequentially with NaOCl for smear layer removal, and why must direct contact between residual NaOCl and CHX inside the canal be avoided?","o":["17% EDTA polymerises gutta-percha cones; mixing NaOCl with CHX causes immediate root vertical fracture","17% EDTA chelates Ca2+ to remove the inorganic smear layer while NaOCl dissolves organic debris; mixing NaOCl directly with CHX forms an insoluble orange-brown precipitate containing parachloroaniline (PCA)","17% EDTA neutralises Enterococcus faecalis biofilms instantaneously; mixing NaOCl with CHX produces benign sodium chloride crystals","17% EDTA dissolves necrotic organic pulp tissue; mixing NaOCl with CHX yields effervescent oxygen bubbles that strengthen dentine bonding"],"a":1,"e":"Complete smear layer removal requires both an organic tissue solvent (NaOCl) and a calcium chelator (17% EDTA) to open dentinal tubules. However, direct interaction between residual NaOCl and CHX produces a dense orange-brown flocculate containing parachloroaniline (PCA), which occludes dentinal tubules, discolours teeth, and is potentially cytotoxic/carcinogenic—requiring an intermediate flush (e.g., saline or distilled water) before CHX.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00234","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Inferior Alveolar Nerve Block (IANB)","q":"Minutes after receiving a right Inferior Alveolar Nerve Block (IANB) for mandibular molar extraction, a 42-year-old patient is unable to wrinkle the right forehead, raise the right eyebrow, or close the right eyelid completely, with drooping of the right corner of the mouth. What anatomical error caused this complication, and what is the appropriate management?","o":["Anaesthetic entered the pterygoid venous plexus causing permanent trigeminal motor paralysis; refer for surgical decompression","Needle entered the infraorbital foramen damaging the maxillary nerve; administer systemic corticosteroids immediately","Needle was inserted too far posteriorly without contacting the mandibular ramus, depositing anaesthetic into the parotid capsule around branches of the facial nerve (CN VII); reassure the patient and protect the eye until anaesthesia wears off","Anaesthetic diffused into the sublingual gland blocking the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII); prescribe oral acyclovir"],"a":2,"e":"In a standard IANB, bony contact on the medial aspect of the mandibular ramus (typically 20-25 mm depth) confirms correct placement in the pterygomandibular space. If the clinician fails to contact bone and advances too far posteriorly past the posterior border of the ramus, solution is deposited within the parotid gland capsule, transiently paralysing the facial nerve (CN VII) motor branches (Bell\u0027s palsy-like picture). It resolves spontaneously within 2-4 hours as the anaesthetic wears off; temporary corneal protection (eye patch/lubricant) is advised.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00235","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Periodontology","topic":"Glickman Classification of Furcation Defects","q":"On probing a mandibular first molar with a curved Nabers furcation probe, the probe passes completely through the furcation from the buccal to the lingual aspect, and the furcation entrance is clinically visible without retracting the gingiva due to apical gingival recession. Which Glickman grade of furcation involvement is present?","o":["Grade II furcation involvement","Grade III furcation involvement","Grade I furcation involvement","Grade IV furcation involvement"],"a":3,"e":"In Glickman\u0027s classification: Grade I is incipient suprabony involvement without radiographic bone loss; Grade II is a cul-de-sac defect with partial horizontal bone loss under the roof where the Nabers probe enters but does not pass through; Grade III is through-and-through bone destruction still covered and concealed by intact gingival soft tissue; Grade IV is through-and-through bone loss accompanied by gingival recession rendering the furcation tunnel clinically visible.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00236","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral Pathology and Oral Microbiology","topic":"Oral Submucous Fibrosis (OSMF)","q":"A 30-year-old man from Central India with a 10-year history of chewing gutkha and pan masala presents with burning sensation on eating spicy food, blanched marble-like buccal mucosa with palpable vertical fibrous bands, shrunken uvula (\u0027hockey-stick\u0027 appearance), and progressive limitation of mouth opening (interincisal distance 19 mm). What is the principal alkaloid mediator and hallmark histopathological feature of this potentially malignant disorder?","o":["Arecoline (from Areca catechu) stimulating fibroblast collagen synthesis and inhibiting collagenase via lysyl oxidase/TGF-beta; juxta-epithelial hyalinisation with atrophic stratified squamous epithelium","Human papillomavirus E6/E7 integration; koilocytic papillomatosis without subepithelial fibrosis","Candida albicans hyphae invading stratum corneum; Munro microabscesses","Nicotine inducing hyperorthokeratinised chevron peaks; acantholytic Tzanck cells in intraepithelial vesicles"],"a":0,"e":"Oral Submucous Fibrosis (OSMF) is strongly associated with areca nut (Areca catechu) chewing. Arecoline and tannins upregulate TGF-beta and copper-dependent lysyl oxidase, promoting cross-linked collagen deposition while reducing collagen degradation. Clinically it causes mucosal blanching, palpable vertical fibrous bands, depapillated tongue, bud-like/hockey-stick uvula, and progressive trismus. Histologically, dense juxta-epithelial collagen hyalinisation, obliterated blood vessels, and overlying atrophic epithelium predispose to oral squamous cell carcinoma.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00237","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"Enamel and Dentine Acid Etching (Total-Etch)","q":"In total-etch (etch-and-rinse) composite resin bonding using 37% orthophosphoric acid, what ultrastructural effect does acid etching produce on dentine versus enamel to achieve micromechanical retention?","o":["Seals dentinal tubules permanently by precipitating calcium phosphate crystals without exposing collagen","Removes the smear layer and demineralises superficial intertubular/peritubular dentine to expose a collagen fibril scaffold for resin infiltration (forming the hybrid layer), while creating microporosities in enamel rods for resin tags","Dissolves organic collagen completely from dentine while leaving hydroxyapatite enamel intact","Polymerises the methacrylate composite monomers directly without requiring a bonding primer"],"a":1,"e":"Applying 37% phosphoric acid dissolves smear layer and smear plugs, opening dentinal tubules and demineralising the top 3-5 microns of intertubular dentine to expose a moist collagen network. Subsequent hydrophilic primer and adhesive resin infiltrate this collagen scaffold and polymerise in situ to create the resin-dentine interdiffusion zone (\u0027hybrid layer\u0027 described by Nakabayashi), alongside microporous enamel resin tags (~20-50 microns). Over-drying etched dentine collapses the collagen network and compromises bond strength.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00238","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Biomechanics of Mandibular Angle Fractures","q":"On a lateral radiograph/OPG, a mandibular angle fracture line runs obliquely downward and anteriorly (from posterior-superior at the retromolar trigone to anterior-inferior near the inferior border). How is this fracture classified biomechanically in the horizontal plane, and why?","o":["Vertically favourable fracture — the suprahyoid muscles elevate the anterior symphysis upward","Horizontally favourable fracture — the elevator muscles compress the proximal fragment firmly against the distal body fragment","Horizontally unfavourable fracture — the elevator muscles (masseter, medial pterygoid, temporalis) pull the proximal ramus fragment upward and anteriorly, displacing it away from the distal body fragment","Greenstick subcondylar fracture — the lateral pterygoid muscle pulls the condyle posteriorly"],"a":2,"e":"In mandibular angle fractures viewed laterally (horizontal plane): if the fracture line slopes downward and posteriorly, upward pull of the pterygomasseteric sling and temporalis seats the proximal fragment tightly against the distal fragment (horizontally favourable). Conversely, if the fracture line slopes downward and anteriorly, there is no bony stop against superior-anterior pull of the elevator muscles, causing upward displacement of the proximal fragment (horizontally unfavourable) while suprahyoid/mylohyoid muscles depress the anterior segment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00239","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Periodontology","topic":"Infrabony Defect Classification (Goldman \u0026 Cohen)","q":"Among infrabony (intrabony) periodontal osseous defects classified by the number of remaining bony walls, which defect configuration offers the HIGHEST predictability for successful bone regeneration using Guided Tissue Regeneration (GTR) and bone grafting?","o":["Combined circumferential furcation-involved trough without walls","Zero-wall horizontal suprabony bone loss","One-wall hemiseptal defect","Three-wall intrabony defect (bounded by three bony walls and one tooth surface)"],"a":3,"e":"Periodontal regeneration requires vascular supply, osteoprogenitor/periodontal ligament cells, clot stabilisation, and space maintenance. A narrow, deep three-wall intrabony defect provides maximum surrounding bony surface area for cellular/vascular ingrowth and naturally shields the blood clot and graft/membrane from flap collapse—giving the highest regenerative predictability compared with two-wall, one-wall, or horizontal defects.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00240","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Dental Materials \u0026 Prosthodontics","topic":"Zinc Oxide Eugenol (ZOE) Polymerisation Inhibition","q":"Why must a clinician AVOID placing a Zinc Oxide Eugenol (ZOE) base/liner or using a eugenol-containing temporary cement immediately prior to bonding a methacrylate-based composite resin restoration?","o":["Residual phenolic eugenol acts as a free-radical scavenger that inhibits addition polymerisation of composite resin, softening the interface and compromising bond strength","Eugenol accelerates composite shrinkage tenfold causing immediate pulp necrosis","Zinc oxide reacts violently with 37% phosphoric acid liberating toxic chlorine gas","Eugenol raises local enamel pH to 12, dissolving the composite filler quartz particles"],"a":0,"e":"Eugenol (4-allyl-2-methoxyphenol) contains a phenolic hydroxyl group that donates hydrogen atoms to quench free radicals generated by camphorquinone/benzoyl peroxide initiators. Consequently, eugenol diffusing into dentinal tubules inhibits free-radical polymerisation of methacrylate adhesives and resin composites, resulting in incomplete curing, marginal microleakage, and reduced bond strength; non-eugenol temporary cements or GIC bases should be used instead.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00241","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Dental Anatomy, Embryology and Oral Histology","topic":"Hunter-Schreger Bands vs Striae of Retzius","q":"When examining a longitudinal ground section of human dental enamel under reflected oblique light, alternating dark and light bands (diazones and parazones) are seen extending through the inner two-thirds of enamel thickness. What are these bands, and what structural feature causes them?","o":["Tomes\u0027 granular layer — caused by unmineralised interglobular spaces beneath root cementum","Hunter-Schreger bands — an optical phenomenon caused by rhythmic periodic changes in the direction/orientation of adjacent groups of enamel rods (prisms)","Incremental lines of Retzius — caused purely by daily circadian arrest of Tomes\u0027 process at the outer enamel surface","Neonatal line — an accentuated hypocalcified band present exclusively in permanent third molars"],"a":1,"e":"Hunter-Schreger bands are optical bands visible in reflected light on longitudinal ground sections of enamel, resulting from undulating changes in enamel rod orientation (parazones where rods are cut more longitudinally vs diazones where rods are cut more transversely) that reinforce enamel against cleavage/fracture. By contrast, Striae of Retzius reflect weekly (~7-10 day) incremental mineralisation apposition, and Tomes\u0027 granular layer is located in peripheral root dentine beneath cementum.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00242","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral Medicine and Radiology","topic":"ALARA Principle \u0026 Stochastic vs Deterministic Effects","q":"In dental radiology, radiation-induced carcinogenesis is classified as a stochastic effect (probability increases with dose, with no safe threshold), whereas skin erythema and cataracts are deterministic effects. Which combination of operator and equipment practices BEST enforces the ALARA principle during intraoral periapical radiography?","o":["Stand directly behind the patient\u0027s head in the path of the exit beam at 30 cm","Use pointed plastic cones without collimation, slow D-speed film, and hold the sensor inside the patient\u0027s mouth with bare fingers","Use rectangular collimation, high-speed F-speed film or digital sensors, lead apron with thyroid collar, \u003e=1.5-2.0 mm Al filtration, and stand \u003e=2 m (6 feet) away at 90-135 degrees to the primary beam","Increase exposure kVp and mAs maximally on every patient regardless of anatomical thickness"],"a":2,"e":"ALARA (As Low As Reasonably Achievable) minimises patient and operator exposure by: (1) rectangular collimation (cutting beam area by ~60% vs round cones), (2) digital receptors/F-speed film reducing exposure \u003e50%, (3) adequate aluminium filtration removing soft non-diagnostic X-rays, (4) thyroid collar protection (especially in children/adolescents), and (5) position-and-distance rule for the operator (\u003e=6 feet / 2 m from tube head at 90-135 degrees to the primary beam, or behind a lead barrier).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00243","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Alveolar Osteitis (Dry Socket)","q":"Three days after surgical extraction of an impacted mandibular third molar, a 29-year-old smoker returns with severe throbbing pain radiating to the ipsilateral ear, foul taste/odour, and an empty extraction socket revealing greyish exposed alveolar bone without suppuration or fever. What is the underlying pathophysiology and evidence-based primary management?","o":["Vigorous sharp curettage of bare bone under local anaesthesia without dressing to scrape off all periosteum","Systemic oral metronidazole plus amoxicillin alone without any local socket irrigation or dressing","Acute osteomyelitis requiring immediate radical sequestrectomy and 6 weeks of IV vancomycin","Premature fibrinolytic dissolution of the blood clot exposing bare bone nerve endings; gently irrigate with warm sterile saline and place an intra-alveolar obtundent medicated dressing (e.g., zinc oxide-eugenol or iodoform-eugenol/Alvogyl) with analgesics"],"a":3,"e":"Alveolar osteitis (\u0027dry socket\u0027) arises from premature fibrinolysis (plasminogen-to-plasmin activation triggered by surgical trauma, smoking, oral contraceptives, or bacterial fibrinolysins) disintegrating the clot 2-4 days post-extraction. Treatment is palliative and local: gentle warm saline irrigation to clear food debris, placement of a soothing obtundent dressing (ZOE/eugenol-based paste on gauze or resorbable dressing) changed every 24-48 h as needed, and analgesics. Aggressive curettage is avoided as it worsens pain and delays healing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00244","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Public Health Dentistry","topic":"Community Water Fluoridation \u0026 Dental Fluorosis","q":"In community water fluoridation programmes, what optimal fluoride concentration range balances maximum dental caries reduction (via fluorapatite formation and remineralisation) against the risk of endemic dental fluorosis in tropical regions like India, and what happens to enamel if drinking water chronically exceeds 1.5-2.0 ppm?","o":["Optimal ~0.7-1.0 ppm (often 0.5-0.8 ppm in hot Indian climates where water intake is higher); \u003e1.5-2.0 ppm during amelogenesis causes hypomineralised mottled enamel (dental fluorosis)","Fluoride acts strictly by killing oral Candida albicans and has no effect on hydroxyapatite crystals","Optimal 0.01 ppm; \u003e0.1 ppm causes total anodontia","Optimal 5.0-10.0 ppm; \u003e15 ppm causes acute pulp calcification exclusively"],"a":0,"e":"Topical/systemic fluoride substitutes hydroxyl ions in hydroxyapatite [Ca10(PO4)6(OH)2] to form more acid-resistant fluorapatite [Ca10(PO4)6F2], lowers critical enamel dissolution pH from ~5.5 to ~4.5, enhances remineralisation, and inhibits bacterial enolase. Optimal water fluoride is 0.7-1.2 ppm (adjusted lower, ~0.5-0.8 ppm, in tropical Indian belts where people drink more water). Chronic excess intake (\u003e1.5 ppm per WHO/BIS) during tooth development impairs ameloblast matrix protein removal, causing opaque white-to-brown pitted enamel (dental fluorosis, scored by Dean\u0027s Index).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00245","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Prosthodontics and Crown \u0026 Bridge","topic":"Kennedy\u0027s Classification \u0026 Applegate\u0027s Rules","q":"A patient has lost all four maxillary incisors (anterior edentulous span crossing the midline from canine to canine) AND is also missing the maxillary right first molar (bounded posteriorly by the second molar), with all other teeth present. Applying Kennedy\u0027s Classification with Applegate\u0027s Rules, how is this arch classified?","o":["Kennedy Class II unmodified","Kennedy Class III modification 1 (because the posterior bounded edentulous molar area dictates classification over the anterior span crossing the midline)","Kennedy Class I modification 1","Kennedy Class IV modification 1 (because anterior edentulous areas always override posterior areas)"],"a":1,"e":"By Applegate\u0027s Rule 5, the most posterior edentulous area(s) always determine the Kennedy Classification (excluding third molars unless used as abutments, Rule 2/3). Because the missing right first molar is a unilateral bounded posterior edentulous space (Class III) located posterior to the anterior span, the arch is classified as Kennedy Class III, with the single additional anterior edentulous space counted as \u0027modification 1\u0027 (note Applegate\u0027s Rule 8: Class IV cannot have modification spaces).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00246","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral Pathology and Oral Microbiology","topic":"Oral Lichen Planus (OLP)","q":"A 44-year-old woman presents with asymptomatic, bilateral, symmetrical interlacing white lacy lines (Wickham\u0027s striae) on the posterior buccal mucosa that cannot be scraped off. Biopsy shows hyperorthokeratosis, \u0027saw-tooth\u0027 shaped epithelial rete pegs, liquefactive degeneration of the basal cell layer with eosinophilic apoptotic Civatte bodies, and a dense band-like lymphocytic infiltrate confined to the superficial lamina propria. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Pseudomembranous oral candidiasis (thrush)","Pemphigus vulgaris","Reticular oral lichen planus","Verrucous carcinoma"],"a":2,"e":"Reticular oral lichen planus is a chronic T-cell-mediated mucocutaneous disorder classically presenting bilaterally on posterior buccal mucosa as interlacing white keratotic Wickham\u0027s striae. Characteristic histopathology includes hyperkeratosis, saw-tooth rete ridges, hydropic basal layer degeneration, apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte / colloid / hyaline bodies), and a dense juxta-epithelial band-like CD8+ T-cell infiltrate.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00247","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"Calcium Hydroxide [Ca(OH)2] Mechanism of Action","q":"Why is calcium hydroxide [Ca(OH)2] paste widely used as an interappointment intracanal medicament in necrotic teeth with periapical lesions?","o":["It lowers intracanal pH to 2.0 to dissolve apical gutta-percha cones","It releases eugenol vapours that polymerise resin sealers instantly","It acts as a permanent root-end amalgam retrograde filling","Sustained dissociation of hydroxyl ions (OH-) raises intracanal pH to ~12.5, denaturing bacterial cytoplasmic membranes/DNA, hydrolysing Gram-negative lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin), and activating alkaline phosphatase for hard-tissue repair"],"a":3,"e":"Ca(OH)2 slowly dissociates into Ca2+ and OH- ions creating a highly alkaline microenvironment (pH ~12.5). Hydroxyl ions cause lipid peroxidation of bacterial membranes, protein denaturation, DNA strand damage, and ester-bond hydrolysis of toxic Gram-negative bacterial lipopolysaccharide (lipid A endotoxin), while Ca2+/high pH stimulates alkaline phosphatase and osteogenic/cementogenic hard-tissue deposition (note: Enterococcus faecalis proton-pump resistance is why short-term placement or CHX adjuncts may be needed in retreatment).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00248","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopaedics","topic":"Leeway Space of Nance","q":"What is the average magnitude of the Leeway Space of Nance (combined mesiodistal width excess of primary canine + first molar + second molar over permanent canine + first premolar + second premolar) per quadrant in the maxilla versus the mandible, and what physiological occlusal transition does it enable?","o":["~0.9 mm per quadrant in the maxilla (1.8 mm arch total) and ~1.7 mm per quadrant in the mandible (3.4 mm arch total); facilitates late mesial shift of the mandibular permanent first molar into a Class I molar relationship","~3.5 mm per quadrant in the maxilla and ~0.5 mm per quadrant in the mandible; creates permanent open bite","Zero millimetres in both arches; forces extraction of all four second premolars","~5.0 mm per quadrant in the mandible only; shifts permanent incisors distally out of occlusion"],"a":0,"e":"Because primary molars (especially the mandibular second primary molar) are wider mesiodistally than their permanent premolar successors, exfoliation liberates the Leeway Space of Nance: ~0.9 mm per side in the maxilla (1.8 mm total) and ~1.7 mm per side in the mandible (3.4 mm total). The greater mandibular leeway space permits the mandibular permanent first molar to undergo a \u0027late mesial shift\u0027 from a flush terminal plane into an Angle Class I molar relationship.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00249","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Acute Oroantral Communication (OAC)","q":"Immediately after extraction of a maxillary first molar with divergent roots approximating the maxillary antrum, gentle nose-blowing produces bubbling of blood through the socket, and probing confirms a 7-mm bony defect into the maxillary sinus without pre-existing sinusitis. What is the appropriate surgical management for an acute oroantral communication \u003e5-6 mm?","o":["Immediate tension-free surgical primary closure using a buccal advancement flap (Rehrmann flap) or pedicled buccal fat pad flap, plus antibiotics, nasal decongestants, and sinus precautions","Irrigate aggressively under high pressure with 5.25% sodium hypochlorite into the antrum","Re-implant the extracted molar roots deeply into the sinus floor","Leave the 7-mm defect completely open with tight gauze packing pushed up into the maxillary sinus"],"a":0,"e":"Acute OAC management depends on defect size and sinus health: \u003c2 mm defects generally heal spontaneously via stable blood clot and sinus precautions; 2-6 mm defects are managed with resorbable collagen/Gelfoam plug and figure-of-eight sutures; large defects (\u003e5-6 mm) carry high risk of chronic oroantral fistula and require immediate tension-free mucoperiosteal flap closure (Rehrmann buccal advancement flap, palatal rotational flap, or buccal fat pad flap) with systemic antibiotics, nasal decongestants, and strict avoidance of nose-blowing/straws/smoking.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00250","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Periodontology","topic":"Gingivitis vs Periodontitis Differentiation","q":"A patient presents with oedematous, bleeding gingivae and a 5 mm probing pocket depth on the maxillary premolars, where the gingival margin sits 2 mm coronal to the cementoenamel junction (CEJ). What is the Clinical Attachment Loss (CAL), and why does this finding distinguish plaque-induced gingivitis from periodontitis?","o":["CAL = 7 mm (5 mm + 2 mm), proving advanced Stage IV periodontitis","CAL = 3 mm (5 mm probing depth minus 2 mm coronal gingival hyperplasia/oedema) if attachment migrated apically, OR 0 mm if junctional epithelium remains at the CEJ (pseudopocket = 5 mm probing depth with 0 mm CAL indicating gingivitis without attachment loss)","Probing depth alone \u003e= 4 mm always defines periodontitis regardless of CEJ position","CAL is measured strictly from the incisal edge to the alveolar crest on a periapical radiograph"],"a":1,"e":"Probing pocket depth (PPD, gingival margin to pocket base) can overestimate disease in gingival enlargement/oedema (pseudopockets). True Clinical Attachment Loss (CAL) is always measured from a fixed anatomical landmark—the Cementoenamel Junction (CEJ)—to the base of the periodontal pocket: when the gingival margin is coronal to the CEJ, CAL = PPD - (distance from GM to CEJ). Thus, a 5 mm pocket with 5 mm coronal gingival overgrowth yields 0 mm CAL (gingivitis), whereas apical migration of junctional epithelium below the CEJ (\u003e0 mm interdental CAL + radiographic bone loss) defines periodontitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00251","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral Medicine and Radiology","topic":"Pemphigus Vulgaris vs Mucous Membrane Pemphigoid","q":"A 48-year-old woman presents with persistent, painful oral erosions, sloughing buccal mucosa on gentle lateral finger pressure (positive Nikolsky sign), and intact skin bullae. Perilesional mucosal biopsy shows suprabasal intraepithelial clefting with acantholytic Tzanck cells and a single basal layer attached to the basement membrane (\u0027row of tombstones\u0027), while direct immunofluorescence (DIF) demonstrates a \u0027fish-net\u0027 intercellular IgG pattern. What is the diagnosis and primary autoantigen target?","o":["Recurrent aphthous stomatitis major — neutrophil microabscess without IgG autoantibodies","Erythema multiforme — target iris skin lesions with subepithelial split","Pemphigus vulgaris — IgG autoantibodies directed against desmosomal cadherins Desmoglein-3 (and Desmoglein-1)","Mucous membrane (cicatricial) pemphigoid — linear IgG/C3 along the basement membrane zone"],"a":2,"e":"Pemphigus vulgaris is a potentially fatal autoimmune mucocutaneous blistering disease (oral lesions precede skin lesions in \u003e50% of cases) caused by IgG autoantibodies targeting desmoglein-3 (mucosal-dominant) and desmoglein-1 (mucocutaneous), disrupting desmosomal cell-cell adhesion. Histopathology reveals suprabasal acantholysis leaving basal cells attached via intact hemidesmosomes (\u0027row of tombstones\u0027), and DIF shows diagnostic intercellular \u0027chicken-wire/fish-net\u0027 IgG deposition.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00252","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Maximum Recommended Dose (MRD) of Lidocaine","q":"Using Malamed\u0027s standard maximum recommended dose (MRD) of 7.0 mg/kg (capped at 500 mg) for 2% lidocaine with 1:100,000 epinephrine in a healthy 60-kg adult undergoing multiple quadrant extractions, what is the maximum safe total dose (mg) and equivalent solution volume (mL)?","o":["120 mg (equivalent to 6.0 mL of 2% lidocaine solution)","600 mg (equivalent to 30.0 mL of 2% lidocaine solution)","264 mg (equivalent to 13.2 mL of 2% lidocaine solution)","420 mg (equivalent to 21.0 mL of 2% lidocaine solution)"],"a":3,"e":"2% w/v lidocaine contains 20 mg/mL (36 mg per 1.8 mL dental cartridge). For a healthy 60-kg adult using 2% lidocaine with epinephrine (MRD = 7.0 mg/kg, max 500 mg): Maximum safe dose = 60 kg x 7.0 mg/kg = 420 mg (which is below the 500 mg ceiling). Equivalent volume = 420 mg / (20 mg/mL) = 21.0 mL (~11.6 standard 1.8 mL cartridges).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00253","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"Class I/II Silver Amalgam Cavity Preparation","q":"When preparing a Class I or Class II cavity for a dental amalgam restoration, why MUST the enamel cavosurface margin form a 90-degree butt joint (with NO occlusal bevel) and maintain at least 1.5-2.0 mm pulpal depth?","o":["Amalgam is a brittle material with low edge/tensile strength—thin feathered bevelled margins fracture under occlusal load (producing marginal ditching), while 1.5-2.0 mm bulk thickness provides adequate resistance against compressive/flexural fracture","Amalgam requires a 45-degree cavosurface bevel to chemically bond to acid-etched enamel prisms","90-degree margins allow eugenol liners to flow out onto the occlusal table","Amalgam expands 50% upon setting unless thinned below 0.5 mm at the margin"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike resin composite (which uses enamel bevels to expose prism ends for micromechanical bonding) or cast gold inlays (bevelled for burnishing), silver amalgam is brittle with poor tensile and edge strength. A 90-degree cavosurface butt-joint angle ensures bulk amalgam thickness (\u003e=1.5-2.0 mm vertically) and prevents thin (\u003c70-degree) feathered amalgam edges from chipping under mastication, while convergent buccal/lingual walls provide mechanical retention.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00254","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Prosthodontics and Crown \u0026 Bridge","topic":"Posterior Palatal Seal (PPS / Post-Dam) Area","q":"In maxillary complete denture fabrication, what are the anatomical boundaries of the Posterior Palatal Seal (PPS) area, and what primary biomechanical function does the post-dam fulfil?","o":["Bounded anteriorly by the anterior vibrating line (Cupid\u0027s bow contour near foveae palatinae/hard-soft palate junction) and posteriorly by the posterior vibrating line (junction of aponeurosis/movable soft palate passing through both hamular notches); compensates for acrylic resin polymerisation shrinkage and maintains posterior peripheral border seal","Located anterior to the incisive papilla only; provides anterior canine guidance","Extends 15 mm posterior to the uvula onto the posterior pharyngeal wall; prevents gagging exclusively","Positioned solely over the mid-palatine torus; increases vertical dimension of occlusion"],"a":0,"e":"The Posterior Palatal Seal lies in the displaceable glandular tissue between the anterior vibrating line (visualised during Valsalva/gentle \u0027ah\u0027 at the junction of hard palate and immobile soft palate) and posterior vibrating line (demarcating actively movable soft palate, passing bilaterally through the pterygomaxillary/hamular notches). Carving the post-dam slightly into the master cast compensates for PMMA heat-curing shrinkage along the posterior border, improves denture retention, and reduces food accumulation under the denture.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00255","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Paediatric and Preventive Dentistry","topic":"Primary Eruption Sequence \u0026 Natal Teeth Management","q":"A healthy newborn presents at birth with a prematurely erupted lower central incisor (\u0027natal tooth\u0027) that is firm in the ridge without maternal nursing trauma or tongue ulceration. Statistically, over 85% of natal/neonatal teeth represent which tooth, and what is the appropriate clinical management if the tooth is stable?","o":["Supernumerary mesiodens; extract every natal tooth immediately under general anaesthesia on Day 1","Normal primary mandibular central incisor (of the normal complement of 20 primary teeth); retain and monitor conservatively rather than extracting unnecessarily (extract ONLY if severely hypermobile with aspiration risk or refractory Riga-Fede sublingual ulceration)","Odontogenic keratocyst; perform enucleation and curettage","Permanent mandibular first molar; apply orthodontic brackets immediately"],"a":1,"e":"Natal teeth (present at birth) and neonatal teeth (erupting within the first 30 days) are normal early-erupting primary mandibular central incisors in \u003e85-90% of cases (supernumeraries account for \u003c10%). Because extracting a normal primary tooth causes premature edentulism and potential space loss, stable natal teeth are retained (smoothing sharp incisal edges if causing mild ventral tongue irritation/Riga-Fede disease); extraction (after confirming Vitamin K status in the first 10 days of life) is reserved for extreme mobility posing aspiration risk or persistent feeding/ulceration problems.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00256","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Dental Anatomy, Embryology and Oral Histology","topic":"Reactionary vs Reparative Tertiary Dentine","q":"In response to dental caries or operative trauma, the pulp-dentine complex lays down tertiary dentine beneath the injury site. What key cellular distinction separates \u0027reactionary dentine\u0027 from \u0027reparative dentine\u0027?","o":["Reactionary dentine is secreted by surviving original post-mitotic odontoblasts following mild stimuli; reparative dentine is secreted by newly differentiated odontoblast-like cells recruited from pulpal mesenchymal stem cells after severe injury destroys original odontoblasts","Reactionary dentine forms exclusively prior to tooth eruption; reparative dentine forms only in enamel","Reactionary dentine contains ameloblasts; reparative dentine contains cementocytes only","Both terms refer strictly to normal age-related secondary dentine covering the entire pulp chamber roof"],"a":0,"e":"Tertiary dentine is focal protective dentine formed at sites of irritation (caries, attrition, cavity prep). Mild stimuli (shallow caries/slow wear) stimulate existing primary odontoblasts to secrete tubular \u0027reactionary dentine\u0027 continuous with secondary dentine. Intense stimuli (deep caries/pulp exposure) kill original odontoblasts, triggering migration and differentiation of pulpal progenitor/stem cells into replacement odontoblast-like cells that lay down less-tubular or atubular \u0027reparative dentine\u0027 (osteodentine).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00257","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral Pathology and Oral Microbiology","topic":"Odontogenic Keratocyst (OKC) \u0026 Gorlin-Goltz Syndrome","q":"A 21-year-old man presents with multiple bilateral jaw radiolucencies expanding anteroposteriorly through the mandibular medullary space with minimal cortical bulging, palmar/plantar pits, bifid ribs, and lamellar calcification of the falx cerebri. Biopsy of a mandibular lesion reveals a uniform 6-8 cell thick parakeratinised stratified squamous epithelial lining with a corrugated surface, flat basement membrane, and hyperchromatic palisaded (\u0027picket-fence\u0027) basal cells. What is the lesion and underlying syndrome?","o":["Nasopalatine duct cyst associated with Albright syndrome","Radicular periapical cyst associated with Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","Odontogenic keratocyst (OKC) associated with Gorlin-Goltz (nevoid basal cell carcinoma) syndrome caused by PTCH1 tumour-suppressor gene mutation","Dentigerous cyst associated with Gardner syndrome (APC mutation)"],"a":2,"e":"Odontogenic keratocyst (OKC) grows preferentially anteroposteriorly within cancellous bone with high recurrence (due to thin friable lining and daughter/satellite cysts) and pathognomonic histology: uniform 6-8 cell parakeratinised lining, corrugated luminal surface, flat rete-peg-free interface, and tall/cuboidal palisaded hyperchromatic basal nuclei. Multiple OKCs + multiple basal cell carcinomas + palmar/plantar pits + calcified falx cerebri + bifid ribs define Gorlin-Goltz syndrome (autosomal dominant PTCH1 mutation on chromosome 9q22.3).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00258","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"Pulp Sensibility (Cold/EPT) vs Vitality (Vascular) Tests","q":"Two days after a luxation concussion injury to tooth 21, cold testing (Endo-Ice) and Electric Pulp Testing (EPT) yield no response, even though the tooth shows normal colour and no periapical radiolucency. Why should immediate root canal therapy NOT be started solely on a negative Day-2 EPT/cold test, and what does EPT actually measure versus Laser Doppler Flowmetry / Pulse Oximetry?","o":["Laser Doppler Flowmetry measures solely enamel fluoride thickness","Negative EPT 48 hours after concussion proves irreversible gangrenous necrosis 100% of the time","EPT measures arterial systolic blood flow inside odontoblasts directly","Cold and EPT measure neural sensibility (A-delta nerve fibre conduction), which frequently suffers temporary post-traumatic paresthesia for weeks despite intact blood supply; true pulp vitality depends on vascular circulation measured by Laser Doppler Flowmetry or Pulse Oximetry"],"a":3,"e":"Thermal tests and EPT are \u0027sensibility\u0027 tests assessing sensory A-delta nerve responsiveness—not blood flow. Acute luxation trauma frequently stretches/stuns apical nerve fibres causing transient sensory paresthesia (false-negative response lasting weeks to months) while pulpal vasculature remains alive and capable of healing. True \u0027vitality\u0027 tests (Pulse Oximetry, Laser Doppler Flowmetry) detect haemoglobin oxygenation or erythrocyte blood flow directly; traumatised teeth should be monitored clinically/radiographically rather than immediately extirpated on early negative sensibility alone.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00259","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Infective Endocarditis (IE) Antibiotic Prophylaxis (AHA)","q":"A 52-year-old man with a mechanical prosthetic aortic valve (no penicillin allergy) requires extraction of a periodontally compromised mandibular molar involving gingival and periapical manipulation. According to AHA Infective Endocarditis Prophylaxis guidelines, what antibiotic prophylaxis regimen is indicated?","o":["Single oral dose of Amoxicillin 2 g (50 mg/kg in children) administered 30-60 minutes BEFORE the procedure","No antibiotic prophylaxis needed for prosthetic heart valves during dental extraction","Oral Amoxicillin 500 mg TID for 14 days AFTER the procedure with no pre-operative dose","Intramuscular Benzathine Penicillin G given 2 weeks after healing"],"a":0,"e":"Per AHA guidelines, IE antibiotic prophylaxis is reserved for highest-risk cardiac conditions (prosthetic heart valves/material, previous IE, unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease, or cardiac transplant valvulopathy) undergoing dental procedures that manipulate gingival tissue, periapical region, or perforate oral mucosa. The standard non-allergic adult regimen is a single oral dose of Amoxicillin 2.0 g given 30-60 minutes pre-procedure (penicillin-allergic alternatives per 2021 AHA update: Cephalexin 2 g, Azithromycin/Clarithromycin 500 mg, or Doxycycline 100 mg—note clindamycin is no longer recommended).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00260","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Public Health Dentistry","topic":"Simplified Oral Hygiene Index (OHI-S — Greene \u0026 Vermillion)","q":"When recording Greene and Vermillion\u0027s Simplified Oral Hygiene Index (OHI-S) during a community dental survey, which exact six tooth surfaces are examined for Debris Index-Simplified (DI-S) and Calculus Index-Simplified (CI-S)?","o":["Lingual surfaces of 16, 26 and buccal surfaces of 36, 46","Buccal surfaces of maxillary first molars (16, 26) and facial surfaces of maxillary right \u0026 mandibular left central incisors (11, 31), PLUS lingual surfaces of mandibular first molars (36, 46)","All 28 permanent teeth on four surfaces each (112 surfaces)","Only mandibular second premolars and third molars"],"a":1,"e":"OHI-S examines 6 representative surfaces across 6 sextants: Buccal of 16 and 26 (adjacent to Stensen\u0027s parotid duct orifice), Labial/facial of 11 and 31, and Lingual of 36 and 46 (adjacent to Wharton\u0027s submandibular duct). Each surface scores 0-3 for debris (DI-S) and 0-3 for calculus (CI-S); summing and dividing by 6 gives DI-S (0-3) and CI-S (0-3), whose sum equals OHI-S (0-6: 0-1.2 Good, 1.3-3.0 Fair, 3.1-6.0 Poor).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00261","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Dental Anatomy, Embryology and Oral Histology","topic":"Maxillary First Premolar (14/24) Diagnostic Features","q":"Which anatomical feature uniquely distinguishes the permanent maxillary first premolar (FDI 14/24) from the maxillary second premolar and complicates Class II matrix-band adaptation and periodontal scaling?","o":["A single conical root with a cusp of Carabelli on the mesiopalatal aspect","A longer palatal cusp than buccal cusp and five occlusal cusps","A pronounced mesial crown-cervical concavity (mesial developmental depression) continuous with a deep longitudinal root depression, mesial marginal groove crossing the mesial marginal ridge, and typically two roots (buccal and palatal)","Complete absence of any developmental grooves on the occlusal table"],"a":2,"e":"The maxillary first premolar (14/24) is the only premolar consistently exhibiting two roots (buccal and palatal, ~60-80%), a sharp buccal cusp ~1 mm longer than the palatal cusp, a mesial developmental marginal groove interrupting the mesial marginal ridge, and a deep mesial developmental concavity (\u0027canine fossa\u0027) running across the cervical crown onto the mesial root trunk—creating matrix-band overhangs and plaque traps.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00262","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral Pathology and Oral Microbiology","topic":"Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma (OSCC)","q":"A 54-year-old man who keeps khaini (smokeless tobacco + slaked lime) tucked in his lower left buccal vestibule for 20 years presents with a painless, indurated, non-healing ulcer with everted rolled margins and firm, fixed, painless ipsilateral submandibular lymphadenopathy. Incisional biopsy shows malignant squamous epithelial islands breaching the basement membrane into underlying connective tissue with concentric laminated keratin pearls and nuclear pleomorphism. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Melanoacanthoma","Benign traumatic eosinophilic ulcer","Necrotising sialometaplasia","Well-differentiated oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC)"],"a":3,"e":"Oral squamous cell carcinoma accounts for \u003e90% of oral malignancies; in India, habitual quid/khaini placement makes the gingivobuccal sulcus and buccal mucosa predominant sites (alongside lateral tongue/floor of mouth globally). Induration, rolled/everted margins, and firm fixed regional nodes signify malignancy. Histopathologically, invasion across the epithelial basement membrane by malignant dysplastic sheets/cords forming concentric keratin pearls confirms well-differentiated OSCC.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00263","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"Glass Ionomer Cement (GIC) Chemistry \u0026 Advantages","q":"Conventional Glass Ionomer Cement (GIC) sets via an acid-base reaction between calcium-fluoroaluminosilicate glass powder and aqueous polyalkenoic (polyacrylic/itaconic) acid. Which pair of unique biological and adhesive properties makes GIC ideal for Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART) and root-surface caries in high-caries-risk patients?","o":["Direct chemical ionic bonding (chelation of carboxyl groups to tooth Ca2+ hydroxyapatite) WITHOUT requiring phosphoric acid etching, PLUS sustained anticariogenic fluoride release and rechargeability","Strong mercury-silver intermetallic phase requiring 90-degree retentive dovetails","Complete solubility in saliva within 48 hours liberating eugenol","Free-radical light polymerisation shrinkage of 12% and zero fluoride release"],"a":0,"e":"Conventional GIC sets by an acid-base reaction forming a cross-linked calcium (initial) and aluminium (mature) polycarboxylate hydrogel matrix around unreacted glass cores. Free carboxylate (-COO-) groups chelate directly with calcium ions in enamel and dentine hydroxyapatite—providing true chemical adhesion (enhanced by mild polyacrylic acid conditioning) and matching dentine\u0027s thermal expansion coefficient. Additionally, leached fluoride ions inhibit demineralisation and act as a rechargeable fluoride reservoir.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00264","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Trigeminal Neuralgia (Tic Douloureux)","q":"A 61-year-old woman complains of brief (5-20 second), excruciating, electric-shock-like stabbing pains along the right mandibular gingiva and chin (V3 distribution) provoked instantly by brushing teeth, cold wind, or lightly touching her lower lip (\u0027trigger zone\u0027), with normal neurological examination between attacks. What is the diagnosis and FIRST-LINE medical drug of choice?","o":["Myofascial temporomandibular disorder — first-line is immediate extraction of all mandibular right molars","Classical trigeminal neuralgia (tic douloureux) — first-line drug of choice is oral carbamazepine (or oxcarbazepine) to block high-frequency voltage-gated Na+ channel firing","Acute irreversible pulpitis — perform blind root canal therapy on sound teeth","Glossopharyngeal neuralgia — first-line is high-dose oral ibuprofen alone"],"a":1,"e":"Trigeminal neuralgia presents with paroxysmal, unilateral, lancinating electric-shock pain lasting seconds to \u003c2 minutes strictly confined to trigeminal branches (most often V2/V3), triggered by innocuous cutaneous/mucosal touch at trigger zones and completely pain-free interictally (frequently misdiagnosed as toothache leading to needless extractions!). Oral carbamazepine (or oxcarbazepine) is the evidence-based first-line medical therapy (with MRI brain to exclude cerebellopontine angle tumours/MS in younger/atypical cases).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00265","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Periodontology","topic":"Area-Specific Gracey Curettes","q":"During subgingival scaling and root planing of a mandibular first molar, how do area-specific Gracey curettes differ structurally from Universal curettes, and which Gracey numbers specifically instrument MESIAL versus DISTAL surfaces of posterior teeth?","o":["Gracey curettes have a blade offset at 70 degrees to the terminal shank with only ONE lower cutting edge; Gracey #11/12 adapts to posterior MESIAL surfaces and Gracey #13/14 adapts to posterior DISTAL surfaces","Gracey curettes have a 90-degree blade with two cutting edges; #1/2 is for posterior distal and #13/14 is for anterior labial","Gracey #13/14 adapts to mesial surfaces while #11/12 adapts to distal surfaces","Both Gracey #11/12 and #13/14 are non-cutting exploratory probes"],"a":0,"e":"Area-specific Gracey curettes feature a blade angled at 60-70 degrees (offset) to the lower terminal shank, meaning the outer/lower cutting edge automatically sits at the ideal 60-80 degree working angulation against the root surface when the terminal shank is kept parallel to the tooth axis (whereas Universal curettes have a 90-degree blade with dual cutting edges). Standard Gracey sequence: #1/2, #3/4, #5/6 (anteriors); #7/8, #9/10 (posterior facial/lingual); #11/12 (posterior MESIAL); #13/14 (posterior DISTAL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00266","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Dental Anatomy, Embryology and Oral Histology","topic":"Parotid, Submandibular \u0026 Sublingual Glands","q":"Match each major human salivary gland to its secretory acinar type, excretory duct, and anatomical intraoral orifice: 1) Parotid gland, 2) Submandibular gland, 3) Sublingual gland.","o":["1-Wharton\u0027s duct at sublingual caruncle; 2-Stensen\u0027s duct opposite maxillary second molar; 3-Nasolacrimal duct","1-Produces \u003c5% of total saliva; 2-Produces 0% of saliva; 3-Produces 95% of stimulated saliva","1-Purely serous acini (Stensen\u0027s duct opening opposite maxillary second molar); 2-Mixed predominantly serous acini with serous demilunes (Wharton\u0027s duct opening at sublingual caruncle, highest sialolithiasis rate); 3-Mixed predominantly mucous acini (Bartholin\u0027s/Rivinus ducts along sublingual fold)","1-Purely mucous acini; 2-Purely serous acini; 3-Purely sebaceous glands"],"a":2,"e":"Histology and ductal anatomy of major salivary glands: Parotid (largest gland, purely serous acini secreting watery amylase-rich fluid via Stensen\u0027s duct piercing buccinator opposite crown of maxillary second molar); Submandibular (mixed gland predominantly serous ~9:1 with von Ebner/Gianuzzi serous demilunes, draining uphill against gravity via long tortuous alkaline/mucin-rich Wharton\u0027s duct to sublingual caruncle—accounting for ~80-90% of salivary stones/sialolithiasis); Sublingual (smallest, predominantly mucous acini via Bartholin/Rivinus ducts).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00267","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Oral Pathology and Oral Microbiology","topic":"Pleomorphic Adenoma vs Warthin\u0027s Tumour","q":"A 62-year-old heavy male smoker presents with a painless, slow-growing, fluctuant-to-firm mass in the inferior pole (tail) of the parotid gland, and ultrasound reveals a second smaller nodule in the contralateral parotid tail. Excisional biopsy demonstrates papillary cystic spaces lined by a double layer of eosinophilic columnar/basal oncocytic epithelium resting over dense lymphoid tissue with germinal centres. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Pleomorphic adenoma (benign mixed tumour)","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma","Adenoid cystic carcinoma (\u0027Swiss-cheese\u0027 cribriform pattern with perineural invasion)","Warthin\u0027s tumour (papillary cystadenoma lymphomatosum)"],"a":3,"e":"Warthin\u0027s tumour is the second most common benign parotid neoplasm, occurring almost exclusively in the parotid tail (rarely intraorally), showing strong epidemiological linkage to cigarette smoking, older male predominance, and ~10% bilateral/multifocal occurrence. Histopathology is pathognomonic: cystic papillary projections lined by bilayered tall columnar + cuboidal eosinophilic oncocytes (rich in mitochondria) supported by reactive lymphoid tissue with germinal centres. By contrast, pleomorphic adenoma features ductal/myoepithelial cells in chondromyxoid stroma.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00268","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"Direct Pulp Capping Indications \u0026 Bioceramic Superiority","q":"During deep caries excavation under rubber dam isolation on a vital, asymptomatic mandibular first molar in a 16-year-old patient, a tiny (\u003c0.5 mm) mechanical pulp exposure occurs surrounded by sound dentine, with bleeding stopping within 2 minutes using gentle sterile saline/NaOCl cotton pressure. Why are MTA or Biodentine preferred over traditional hard-setting Calcium Hydroxide for Direct Pulp Capping here?","o":["MTA/Biodentine provide a superior hermetic bacterial seal, lower solubility, less pulpal necrosis, and stimulate a thicker, continuous reparative dentine bridge free of the porous \u0027tunnel defects\u0027 frequently seen beneath Ca(OH)2","Direct pulp capping is strictly contraindicated under rubber dam isolation","MTA dissolves completely in 24 hours liberating phosphoric acid","Ca(OH)2 kills the entire pulp instantly at pH 2"],"a":0,"e":"Direct pulp capping succeeds best in young vital asymptomatic teeth with small (\u003c1 mm) aseptic/mechanical exposures under rubber dam where haemostasis occurs promptly (\u003c5 min). While traditional Ca(OH)2 induces dentine bridging, its high solubility leaves gaps and porous \u0027tunnel defects\u0027 allowing bacterial microleakage over time. Tricalcium-silicate bioceramics (MTA, Biodentine) set in moisture with superior sealing ability, minimal inflammation, and uniform, denser reparative dentine bridges.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00269","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Prosthodontics and Crown \u0026 Bridge","topic":"Boucher\u0027s Selective Pressure Impression \u0026 Stress-Bearing Areas","q":"In complete denture prosthodontics using Boucher\u0027s Selective Pressure Impression philosophy, which anatomical landmark serves as the PRIMARY stress-bearing area in the edentulous mandible (and why), versus a mandatory relief area?","o":["Anterior labial vestibule is the primary stress-bearing area; hard palate slopes are relieved","Mandibular buccal shelf area (bounded by external oblique ridge laterally, crest of ridge medially, masseteric/retromolar pad posteriorly) — because dense cortical bone lies nearly perpendicular to vertical occlusal forces; incisive papilla/mid-palatine raphe and sharp mylohyoid ridge require relief","Incisive papilla is the primary stress-bearing area; mandibular buccal shelf must always be heavily relieved","Mental foramen serves as the primary stress-bearing bone pad"],"a":1,"e":"Boucher\u0027s Selective Pressure Impression distributes vertical masticatory load preferentially onto dense cortical bone oriented perpendicular to biting forces while relieving fragile neurovascular bundles and thin non-resilient mucosa. In the mandible, the wide flat cortical buccal shelf resists resorption best and serves as the primary stress-bearing zone, whereas the mental foramen (nerve compression), sharp mylohyoid ridge, and mandibular tori require relief (in the maxilla, posterior palatal horizontal slopes/residual ridge bear load while incisive papilla and mid-palatine raphe are relieved).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00270","course":"BDS - Dental Surgery","subject":"Public Health Dentistry","topic":"Leavell \u0026 Clark Levels of Prevention in Dentistry","q":"Applying Leavell and Clark\u0027s epidemiological Levels of Prevention to clinical and community dentistry, match each intervention to its correct prevention level: 1) Pit-and-fissure sealants, topical fluoride \u0026 community water fluoridation, 2) Early caries detection with Class I composite/amalgam restoration \u0026 scaling of gingivitis, 3) Root canal therapy / crown / removable partial denture rehabilitation after tooth loss.","o":["All three scenarios represent solely Tertiary rehabilitation","1-Secondary prevention; 2-Tertiary prevention; 3-Primordial prevention only","1-Primary prevention (pre-pathogenesis health promotion \u0026 specific protection); 2-Secondary prevention (early diagnosis \u0026 prompt treatment arresting early disease); 3-Tertiary prevention (disability limitation \u0026 prosthetic rehabilitation)","1-Tertiary prevention; 2-Primary prevention; 3-Secondary prevention"],"a":2,"e":"Leavell and Clark\u0027s levels map cleanly across dental care: Primary prevention acts before disease begins (fluoridation, sealants, plaque control education, tobacco cessation); Secondary prevention intercepts incipient disease via screening, radiographs, scaling, and simple fillings to halt progression; Tertiary prevention limits complex disability (pulp capping/RCT) and restores lost mastication/speech/aesthetics via crowns, FPDs, RPDs, implants, or maxillofacial obturators.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00271","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine \u0026 Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"50 Millesimal (LM) Potency Scale","q":"In the 6th Edition of Samuel Hahnemann’s Organon of Medicine (Aphorism 270), what dilution ratio is used at each step of preparation for the 50 Millesimal (LM / Q) potency scale?","o":["1 : 1,000","1 : 10","1 : 100","1 : 50,000"],"a":3,"e":"In Organon 6th Edition (Aphorism 270), Hahnemann introduced the renewed dynamisation (50 Millesimal / LM / Q scale), achieving an approximate dilution factor of 1:50,000 at every successive potency degree (via 100 drops of alcohol dissolving 1 medicated globule into 500 unmedicated globules of #10 size), aiming for gentler, rapid repetition without severe medicinal aggravation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00272","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Primary Action vs Secondary Curative Counter-Action","q":"In Samuel Hahnemann\u0027s Organon of Medicine (Aphorisms 63-65), how do Primary Action and Secondary Action interact to explain why palliative antipathic dosing fails in chronic diseases whereas homoeopathic similimum cures gently?","o":["Primary Action is the direct impression of the medicine on the Vital Force; Secondary Action is the Vital Force\u0027s automatic opposing counter-reaction, so antipathic opposites provoke a stronger rebound of the original symptom while a similimum stimulates a curative counter-response extinguishing the natural disease","Primary Action occurs only in 50 Millesimal potencies; Secondary Action occurs only in mother tinctures","Primary Action is permanent structural healing; Secondary Action is an undesirable allergic aggravation","Primary Action is produced solely by the patient\u0027s immune antibodies; Secondary Action occurs only after toxicological overdose"],"a":0,"e":"In Aphorisms 63-65, Hahnemann explains that every agent acting on life alters the Vital Force\u0027s state (Primary Action), to which the living organism responds with an exact opposing state (Secondary/Counter Action) to restore equilibrium. Thus, giving a palliative opposite (e.g., coffee for drowsiness or opium for pain) temporarily masks the symptom during Primary Action but triggers a worse Secondary rebound, whereas a minute dose of a similar remedy (similimum) elicits a Vital Force counter-action opposing the similar artificial disease and simultaneously extinguishing the weaker natural disease.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00273","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Materia Medica","topic":"Arsenicum album vs Pulsatilla vs Nux vomica","q":"A 40-year-old woman with acute gastroenteritis complains of burning epigastric pain that paradoxically improves with warm drinks and hot applications, intense restlessness with fear of death, marked worsening between midnight and 2 AM, and unquenchable thirst for small sips of water taken frequently. Which polychrest remedy matches this totality of keynotes?","o":["Pulsatilla nigricans","Arsenicum album","Nux vomica","Bryonia alba"],"a":1,"e":"Arsenicum album is uniquely characterised by burning pains ameliorated by heat/warm applications, profound physical exhaustion paired with anxious mental restlessness and fear of death, strict periodicity/aggravation around midnight to 2 AM, and thirst for small quantities of water sipped frequently (often vomited soon after).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00274","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Repertory and Case Taking","topic":"Kent\u0027s Repertory vs Boenninghausen\u0027s Therapeutic Pocket Book","q":"When repertorising a chronic case that has well-defined Mental Generals, Thermal Reaction, and Physical Generals, why is Kent\u0027s Repertory chosen over Boenninghausen\u0027s Therapeutic Pocket Book (BTPB), and what unique principle defines Boenninghausen\u0027s method?","o":["Kent\u0027s Repertory follows a deductive General-to-Particular hierarchy prioritising Mental and Physical Generals, whereas Boenninghausen\u0027s BTPB uses Grand Generalisation (extending a modality/sensation observed in one anatomical part to the whole patient via Location, Sensation, Modality, and Concomitant)","Kent\u0027s Repertory omits mental symptoms entirely, whereas Boenninghausen\u0027s BTPB lists 500 mental rubrics arranged alphabetically","Kent\u0027s Repertory relies strictly on pathological tissue names, whereas Boenninghausen\u0027s BTPB uses clinical nosology","Both repertories reject modalities and repertorise exclusively using laboratory blood counts"],"a":0,"e":"James Tyler Kent constructed his Repertory of the Homoeopathic Materia Medica on a deductive philosophical hierarchy: Mental Generals \u003e Physical Generals (thermals, desires/aversions, sleep) \u003e Particular symptoms. Conversely, Boenninghausen\u0027s Therapeutic Pocket Book (inductive/Grand Generalisation) completes partial symptoms by assuming that a strong modality, sensation, or concomitant belongs to the patient as a whole rather than only to one local organ.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00275","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Pharmacy","topic":"Preparation of 50 Millesimal (LM / Q) Potencies","q":"Per Organon of Medicine (6th Edition, Aphorism 270) and the Homoeopathic Pharmacopoeia of India (HPI), before preparing the liquid LM 0/1 mother solution for 50 Millesimal potencies, to what initial centesimal degree MUST every medicinal substance (including soluble plants) be triturated in sugar of milk, and how many downward succussions are given to convert the mother solution drop into potency 0/1?","o":["Triturated up to the 12th centesimal (12C) degree, followed by 1,000 mechanical rotations","Triturated up to the 6th decimal (6X) degree, followed by 10 upward succussions","Triturated up to the 3rd centesimal (3C / 1:1,000,000) degree, followed by 100 strong downward succussions","Prepared directly as a 1X aqueous maceration without trituration, followed by 50 succussions"],"a":2,"e":"In Aphorism 270 of the 6th Edition of the Organon (adopted in HPI), Hahnemann mandated that every medicinal substance—mineral, animal, or vegetable—be first triturated with sugar of milk up to the 3rd centesimal (3C) potency. One grain of 3C trituration is dissolved in 500 drops of 20% dilute alcohol (400 drops distilled water + 100 drops strong alcohol); 1 drop of this solution + 100 drops strong alcohol receives 100 powerful downward strokes (succussions) to produce LM 0/1, used to medicate 500 #10 globules.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00276","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Hering\u0027s Law of Direction of Cure","q":"A 35-year-old woman with chronic bronchial asthma (whose childhood eczema had disappeared years ago after strong topical suppressive ointments) receives a constitutional homoeopathic similimum. Over 4 weeks, her wheezing and dyspnoea resolve completely while a mild transient dry eczematous rash reappears briefly on her forearms. How does Hering\u0027s Law of Cure interpret this clinical evolution?","o":["Unfavourable disease metastasis — the remedy has failed and driven asthma deeper into the kidneys","Pure medicinal proving aggravation requiring immediate antidoting with camphor and topical steroids","Allergic anaphylaxis unrelated to constitutional response","Favourable curative direction — healing is progressing from a more vital internal organ (lungs) outward to a less vital organ (skin) and in reverse order of symptom appearance"],"a":3,"e":"Constantine Hering\u0027s Law of Direction of Cure states that true homoeopathic cure proceeds: (1) from within outward, (2) from above downward, (3) from more vital organs (heart, brain, lungs) to less vital organs (skin, extremities), and (4) in the reverse order of appearance of symptoms. Reappearance of previously suppressed eczema alongside complete resolution of asthma confirms centrifugal curative elimination and should NOT be suppressed topically.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00277","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Kent\u0027s Twelve Observations","q":"Four weeks after a single dose of a high-potency constitutional remedy, a patient with chronic migraine experiences a marked reduction in headache intensity alongside improved sleep and energy, and spontaneously recalls that an old joint stiffness from 5 years ago has briefly returned for 3 days before fading. Which of Kent\u0027s Twelve Observations describes this response, and what should the physician\u0027s second prescription action be?","o":["Kent\u0027s 11th Observation (Return of old symptoms) — signifies deep curative reaction; \u0027Wait and watch\u0027 (placebo/unmedicated Sac lac) without repeating or changing the remedy","Kent\u0027s 12th Observation (Symptoms take the wrong direction) — immediately antidote the remedy","Kent\u0027s 5th Observation (Amelioration comes first, aggravation afterwards) — indicates superficial palliation; switch immediately to mother tincture","Kent\u0027s 1st Observation (Prolonged aggravation and final decline) — prognosis is incurable; immediately repeat the same potency daily"],"a":0,"e":"In Kent\u0027s Lectures on Homoeopathic Philosophy (Lecture XXXV, Prognosis after observing the action of the remedy), the 11th Observation is the brief return of old, long-forgotten symptoms in reverse chronological order while the patient improves mentally and physically. This confirms the remedy matches the dynamic constitutional state and is actively unwinding past pathology; the mandatory rule is DO NOT interfere—prescribe placebo (Sac lac) and wait until improvement ceases.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00278","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Materia Medica","topic":"Lycopodium clavatum Keynote Totality","q":"A 46-year-old sedentary corporate executive presents with chronic flatulent dyspepsia and recurrent right-sided renal colic with red sandy sediment in urine. He feels ravenously hungry yet develops immediate fullness and abdominal distension after eating just a few mouthfuls, craves warm drinks and sweets, fears public speaking despite high intellectual competence, and feels distinctly worse daily between 4 PM and 8 PM. Which remedy is indicated?","o":["Ignatia amara","Lycopodium clavatum","Lachesis mutus","Calcarea carbonica"],"a":1,"e":"Lycopodium clavatum classically exhibits right-sided predominance (and right-to-left progression, e.g., throat, renal stones with red lithic acid sand), unmistakable 4 PM to 8 PM aggravation, early satiety after a few mouthfuls with trapped lower abdominal flatulence, desire for sweets and warm food/drinks, and anticipatory anxiety (\u0027stage fright\u0027) masked by intellectual/authoritative demeanour.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00279","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Repertory and Case Taking","topic":"Boger-Boenninghausen\u0027s Characteristics and Repertory (BBCR)","q":"How did C.M. Boger\u0027s \u0027Boger-Boenninghausen\u0027s Characteristics and Repertory\u0027 (BBCR) significantly expand and modify Boenninghausen\u0027s original Therapeutic Pocket Book (BTPB)?","o":["Eliminated all modalities and concomitants to focus exclusively on dream rubrics","Converted all rubrics into a purely clinical disease-name index without grading remedies","Added a dedicated \u0027Time\u0027 aggravation chapter, expanded \u0027Pathological Generals\u0027 (tissue affinities) and \u0027Fever\u0027 (chill-heat-sweat-concomitants), and integrated Boenninghausen\u0027s Aphorisms of Hippocrates concepts","Restricted the repertory solely to 50 Millesimal LM nosodes"],"a":2,"e":"C.M. Boger compiled BBCR (1905) combining Boenninghausen\u0027s original BTPB (1846) with the Aphorisms of Hippocrates, Repertory of Antipsoric Medicines, and Clinical Cases. Key enhancements in BBCR include a prominent \u0027Time\u0027 section (morning, forenoon, afternoon, evening, night, periodicity), detailed \u0027Fever\u0027 (chill, heat, sweat, concomitants), \u0027Pathological Generals\u0027 (constitutional tissue/miasmatic tendencies), and \u0027Mind/Sensorium\u0027 expansions while preserving Boenninghausen\u0027s 7-component symptom evaluation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00280","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Pharmacy","topic":"Hahnemann\u0027s Old Method (Classes I-IV) vs HPI New Method","q":"Under Hahnemann\u0027s Old Method of preparing homoeopathic Mother Tinctures, what is the Drug Power (drug strength) of a Class I tincture (most juicy fresh plants like Belladonna or Bryonia mixed 1:1 by weight of expressed juice and strong alcohol) compared with a Class IV tincture (dried vegetable/animal substances macerated 1 part to 5 parts alcohol) and the uniform New Method (HPI)?","o":["Class I = 1/2 drug power; Class IV = 1/10 drug power; HPI New Method = uniform 1/10 (10%) drug power across all classes","Class I = 1/10 drug power; Class IV = 1/2 drug power; HPI New Method = 1/100 drug power","Class I = 1/6 drug power; Class IV = 1/20 drug power; HPI New Method = 1/50,000 drug power","Both Class I and Class IV have 1/1 (100%) pure drug power"],"a":0,"e":"In Hahnemann\u0027s Old Method (9-class posology/pharmacy scheme): Class I uses equal weights of fresh expressed plant juice and strong alcohol yielding Drug Power 1/2 (so 2 drops of Class I Q + 98 drops dilute alcohol gives 1C); Class II uses 3 parts plant + 2 parts alcohol (Drug Power 1/2); Class III uses 1 part less-juicy plant + 2 parts alcohol (Drug Power 1/6); Class IV uses 1 part dried crude drug + 5 parts strong alcohol yielding Drug Power 1/10 (matching 1X decimal strength). The modern Homoeopathic Pharmacopoeia of India (HPI) New Method standardises mother tincture preparation (maceration/percolation) to a uniform 1/10 (10%) drug strength.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00281","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Indisposition vs True Natural Diseases","q":"In Organon of Medicine (Aphorism 150), how does Samuel Hahnemann define \u0027Indisposition\u0027 in contrast to true acute or chronic natural disease, and what is the prescribed clinical management?","o":["An incurable structural pathology requiring lifelong surgical palliation","A deep-seated sycotic miasm requiring immediate CM potency daily","An acute epidemic cholera infection requiring rapid 50 Millesimal repetition every 10 minutes","A trivial, transient disturbance caused by an obvious external dietary or lifestyle excess (e.g., overeating, tight clothing, mild chill) in an otherwise healthy person; managed by removing the exciting cause (hygienic/dietary correction) WITHOUT homoeopathic medication"],"a":3,"e":"In Aphorism 150 (and footnote), Hahnemann warns physicians not to mistake a slight \u0027indisposition\u0027—arising from a recent minor, obvious error in diet or regimen (causa occasionalis)—for a serious dynamic disease requiring medicinal prescribing. Removing the maintaining/exciting cause and resting allows the uncorrupted Vital Force to restore health spontaneously without drugs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00282","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Materia Medica","topic":"Sepia officinalis Keynote Differentiation","q":"A 37-year-old multiparous woman complains of pelvic dragging and a \u0027bearing-down\u0027 sensation as if the uterus might prolapse (forcing her to cross her legs when sitting), a brownish-yellow \u0027saddle\u0027 across her nose and cheeks, chilly constitution, and emotional indifference toward her spouse and children. Remarkably, her fatigue and pelvic heaviness feel MUCH better after vigorous brisk dancing or strenuous aerobic exercise. Which remedy is indicated?","o":["Sepia officinalis","Natrum muriaticum","Pulsatilla nigricans","Lilium tigrinum"],"a":0,"e":"Sepia officinalis is characterised by pelvic venous stasis and uterine relaxation producing the classic bearing-down sensation relieved by crossing the legs, a brownish chloasma saddle across nose/cheeks, hormonal apathy/indifference toward loved ones, chilliness, and the paradoxical keynote amelioration from vigorous physical exertion (dancing, running, brisk exercise) which relieves venous congestion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00283","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Repertory and Case Taking","topic":"Aphorism 153 — PQRS (Characteristic) Symptoms","q":"When constructing a totality of symptoms for repertorisation per Organon Aphorism 153, a patient with lobar pneumonia exhibits fever, cough, dyspnoea, AND a striking keynote of \u0027stitching chest pain relieved ONLY by lying on the painful right side and pressing firmly against the chest, plus thirst for large volumes of cold water at long intervals\u0027. Which symptoms take HIGHEST priority for remedy selection?","o":["Only past childhood vaccination history","The Peculiar, Queer, Rare, and Strange (PQRS) individualising modalities (amelioration lying on painful side/firm pressure, large infrequent cold drinks) rather than common diagnostic symptoms (fever, cough, dyspnoea)","Solely the radiographic lobar opacity size","Common diagnostic symptoms (fever, cough, dyspnoea) alone because PQRS symptoms are clinically irrelevant"],"a":1,"e":"In Aphorism 153, Hahnemann emphasises that in searching for the homoeopathic specific remedy, the more striking, singular, uncommon, and peculiar (characteristic / PQRS) signs and symptoms of the case must be chiefly and almost solely kept in view—because common pathognomonic signs (fever, cough, dyspnoea) appear in hundreds of remedies and merely name the disease, whereas PQRS modalities (e.g., Bryonia\u0027s relief from pressure and lying on the painful side) individualise the patient and pinpoint the similimum.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00284","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Pharmacy","topic":"Sugar of Milk (Saccharum lactis) \u0026 Globule Grading","q":"Why is purified Sugar of Milk (Saccharum lactis / alpha-lactose monohydrate) used as the standard solid vehicle for homoeopathic trituration of insoluble drugs, and what does the numerical size designation (e.g., #10, #20, #30, #40) of cane-sugar globules signify under the Homoeopathic Pharmacopoeia of India (HPI)?","o":["Sugar of milk acts as a strong oxidising catalyst; globule number indicates weight in grams per single globule","Sugar of milk is fermented to yield 95% ethanol; globule number indicates sucrose percentage","Hard sharp lactose crystals mechanically grind insoluble drugs into submicron particles without chemical reactivity or odour; globule number indicates the diameter (in mm) of 10 globules placed side-by-side in a straight row","Sugar of milk chemically dissolves metallic gold and mercury instantly; globule number indicates potency strength in Centesimal scale"],"a":2,"e":"Hahnemann chose alpha-lactose monohydrate (Sugar of Milk, prepared from skimmed milk whey and purified with alcohol) for trituration because its hard, gritty crystals exert vigorous mechanical friction in mortar-and-pestle grinding to comminute insoluble minerals/metals while remaining chemically inert, non-hygroscopic, and nearly tasteless. Per HPI, globule sizes (#10, #20, #30, #40, etc.) correspond to the total length in millimetres occupied by 10 uniform globules arranged touching in a single line (e.g., 10 #30 globules measure 30 mm across, so each is ~3 mm diameter).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00285","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"One-Sided Diseases (Aphorisms 172-184)","q":"In Organon of Medicine (Aphorisms 172-184), when a chronic patient presents with a \u0027one-sided disease\u0027 exhibiting only one or two prominent physical complaints and too few characteristic symptoms to find a complete similimum immediately, what stepwise therapeutic strategy does Hahnemann prescribe?","o":["Switch immediately to allopathic surgical excision because homoeopathy cannot treat one-sided diseases","Repeat the first partial similimum in CM potency hourly for 6 months regardless of new symptoms","Give ten different remedies simultaneously in a single mixture to cover all organs","Select the closest available partial similimum based on the few observable symptoms; observe the new \u0027accessory symptoms\u0027 brought out by that first remedy (which unmask latent hidden symptoms of the natural disease), construct the now-completed totality, and prescribe the second, more accurate similimum"],"a":3,"e":"Hahnemann addresses symptom-poor \u0027one-sided diseases\u0027 in Aphorisms 172-184: after careful inquiry still leaves an incomplete picture, the physician prescribes the most similar imperfect/partial remedy available (Aphorism 176). This first medicine partially clears the original complaint and elicits \u0027accessory symptoms\u0027—which represent latent hidden facets of the patient\u0027s own chronic disease awakened by the remedy—enabling the physician (Aphorism 180-183) to form a complete picture and select a truly curative second similimum.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00286","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Aphorisms 105-145 — Pure Drug Proving Protocol","q":"According to Hahnemann\u0027s Organon of Medicine (Aphorisms 105-145) and CCRH/NCH drug proving guidelines, which strict condition MUST be observed when conducting a homoeopathic pathogenetic trial (drug proving) to establish reliable Materia Medica Pura?","o":["Prove one single, pure, unmixed medicinal substance at a time on healthy, sensitive, truthful human provers following a bland non-medicinal diet, recording subjective/objective symptoms with exact time, location, and modalities","Allow provers to drink strong black coffee, alcohol, and spicy medicinal herbs throughout the proving","Prove mixtures of 4-5 plants simultaneously on critically ill ICU patients","Record symptoms solely from animal toxicology LD50 experiments without human volunteers"],"a":0,"e":"Hahnemann\u0027s Aphorisms 108-145 require proving a single simple medicine on healthy human volunteers of varying constitutions/sexes (not sick patients, where natural disease symptoms confound drug effects), abstaining from coffee, green tea, spices, alcohol, and extraneous medicines, and recording every symptom in the prover\u0027s own words together with exact onset time, anatomical site, sensations, and modalities (including testing conditions that ameliorate/aggravate).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00287","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Materia Medica","topic":"Arnica montana Keynote Totality","q":"A 31-year-old labourer presents 24 hours after a fall from scaffolding with extensive symmetrical ecchymosis and blunt soft-tissue trauma. He complains of a sore, lame, bruised sensation throughout his entire body, insists the hospital mattress feels hard as a board (\u0027bed feels too hard\u0027), angrily waves away the examining doctor fearing anyone will touch or approach him, and exhibits a hot head with cold nose/extremities. Which remedy is classically indicated?","o":["Rhus toxicodendron","Arnica montana","Hypericum perforatum","Ruta graveolens"],"a":1,"e":"Arnica montana is the supreme homoeopathic remedy for blunt trauma, contusions, ecchymosis, and post-traumatic/post-partum/post-surgical soreness. Hallmark keynotes include: bruised sore lame feeling all over as if beaten; bed feels too hard causing constant shifting to find a soft spot; morbid dread/fear of being touched or approached; and characteristic thermal disharmony (hot head/face with cold body/nose).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00288","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Repertory and Case Taking","topic":"Clinical \u0026 Card Repertories vs General Logico-Utilitarian Repertories","q":"In homoeopathic repertorisation practice, when a clinician encounters an acute or organ-focused case lacking rich mental or physical general symptoms (e.g., acute coryza, otitis, or diagnostic clinical pathology with clear regional modalities), which type of repertory is specifically designed for rapid bedside consultation, and how do punched Card Repertories (e.g., Kishore / Boger) accelerate remedy filtering?","o":["Kent\u0027s Repertory excludes all regional organs; Card Repertories require manual alphabetical tallying of 2,000 rubrics in ink","Synthesis Repertory contains zero mental symptoms; Clinical Repertories ban all modalities","Clinical/Regional Repertories (e.g., Boericke\u0027s Repertory, Clarke\u0027s Clinical Repertory) index clinical conditions, regional pathology, and organ affinities; Card Repertories superimpose punched symptom cards to identify remedies whose holes align across all selected cards instantaneously","BTPB contains only laboratory PCR rubrics; Card Repertories calculate 50 Millesimal alcohol dilutions"],"a":2,"e":"Non-Kentian Clinical/Regional repertories (such as William Boericke\u0027s Pocket Manual Repertory, J.H. Clarke\u0027s Clinical Repertory, and Bell\u0027s Diarrhoea) are arranged around clinical diagnoses, anatomical organs, concomitants, and modalities—ideal when general PQRS mental symptoms are absent in acute/clinical cases. Mechanical Card Repertories (Boger, Jugal Kishore, Shukla) use pre-punched symptom cards where aligning selected cards reveals light/pinholes corresponding exclusively to remedies covering every chosen symptom.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00289","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Pharmacy","topic":"Decimal (X), Centesimal (C) \u0026 Korsakovian (K) Potency Scales","q":"In homoeopathic potentisation (dynamisation), match each potency scale/method to its exact dilution ratio and originator/technique: 1) Decimal scale (X or D), 2) Hahnemannian Centesimal scale (C), 3) Korsakovian scale (K).","o":["1-1:100 dilution (Hahnemann); 2-1:50,000 dilution (Korsakov); 3-1:10 dilution (Hering)","1-1:2 dilution; 2-1:5 dilution; 3-1:1000 dilution without succussion","1-1:50,000 dilution (Hering); 2-1:10 dilution (Boenninghausen); 3-Trituration in mortar only","1-1:10 dilution per step (introduced by Constantine Hering); 2-1:100 dilution per step using a separate fresh phial at each potency (Samuel Hahnemann); 3-Single-vial emptying-and-refilling method retaining ~1% residual film per step (Semyon Korsakov)"],"a":3,"e":"Homoeopathic pharmacy recognises three liquid dilution scales alongside LM (1:50,000): (1) Decimal scale (1:10 at each step, denoted X or D, introduced by Dr. Constantine Hering); (2) Hahnemannian Centesimal scale (1:100 at each step with 10 downward succussions using a new clean phial for every potency step, denoted C or CH); (3) Korsakovian single-vial method (devised by Russian homoeopath Semyon Korsakov, emptying 99% of a vial and refilling the same vial with vehicle before succussion for high potencies like 200K, 1MK, 10MK).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00290","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Ideal Cure \u0026 Three Requisites of a Physician","q":"In Samuel Hahnemann\u0027s Organon of Medicine (Aphorisms 2 and 3), how is the \u0027highest ideal of cure\u0027 defined, and what three essential areas of knowledge must a true physician possess?","o":["Ideal cure (Aphorism 2) is the rapid, gentle, and permanent restoration of health in the shortest, most reliable, and most harmless way; Aphorism 3 requires knowledge of disease (what is curable), knowledge of medicines (pathogenetic powers), and judicious application/adaptation (posology, repetition, and removing obstacles to recovery)","Ideal cure applies only to acute fevers; Aphorism 3 prohibits knowledge of diet and regimen","Ideal cure is permanent surgical excision of affected organs only; Aphorism 3 requires purely anatomical dissection","Ideal cure is temporary suppression of skin eruptions with zinc ointment; Aphorism 3 mandates polypharmacy"],"a":0,"e":"Aphorism 2 defines the highest ideal of cure: \u0027rapid, gentle and permanent restoration of the health, or removal and annihilation of the disease in its whole extent, in the shortest, most reliable, and most harmless way, on easily comprehensible principles\u0027. Aphorism 3 specifies the physician\u0027s three pillars: (1) perceiving what is to be cured in diseases (knowledge of disease), (2) knowing what is curative in each individual medicine (knowledge of medicinal powers), and (3) adapting the curative drug to the case in exact remedy, preparation, dose, repetition, and removal of obstacles to cure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00291","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Aphorisms 210-230 — Management of Mental Diseases","q":"In Organon of Medicine (Aphorisms 215-224), Hahnemann observes that most chronic mental and emotional illnesses are essentially one-sided corporeal diseases in which physical symptoms have declined while the internal psoric disturbance has concentrated upon the mind. How MUST a homoeopathic physician construct the totality to cure such a case?","o":["Incorporate BOTH the earlier bodily/physical symptoms (before they receded) AND the present altered mental/emotional symptoms to select an appropriate deep anti-psoric similimum, supplemented by compassionate humane mental hygiene (never harsh physical coercion)","Ignore all past bodily history completely and prescribe exclusively on the current psychiatric diagnostic label","Use punitive physical restraint, cold douches, and blistering plasters to force physical symptoms back","Prescribe mother tinctures of opiates nightly to sedate the patient permanently"],"a":0,"e":"Hahnemann (Aphorisms 210-230) taught that mental symptoms are always integrated into the whole totality, and that chronic insanity/melancholia frequently stems from suppression or recession of chronic physical psoric complaints. By eliciting the antecedent physical symptom picture from family/attendants and combining it with current mental aberrations (Aphorism 217), the physician finds a true anti-psoric similimum—leading to simultaneous mental recovery and gentle restoration of health, supported by gentle psychical regimen (Aphorism 228).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00292","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Materia Medica","topic":"Bryonia alba vs Rhus toxicodendron","q":"A 41-year-old carpenter with right-sided pleurisy and dry hacking cough complains of sharp stitching chest pain and dry parched lips with unquenchable thirst for large tumblers of cold water taken at long intervals. He lies completely motionless on his painful right side pressing his chest firmly against the pillow, snapping irritably whenever asked to sit up because the slightest movement aggravates his pain. Which remedy fits this classic modality totality?","o":["Rhus toxicodendron","Bryonia alba","Pulsatilla nigricans","Kalium carbonicum"],"a":1,"e":"Bryonia alba is anchored by: (1) aggravation from the SLIGHTEST motion (even deep breathing or eye movement), (2) marked amelioration from absolute rest, firm pressure, and lying on the painful side, (3) extreme dryness of all mucous membranes with thirst for large quantities of cold water at long intervals, and (4) irritable mental desire to be left quiet/alone (\u0027wants to go home\u0027).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00293","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Materia Medica","topic":"Lachesis mutus Keynote Totality","q":"A 50-year-old perimenopausal woman presents with hot flushes, left-sided throat constriction extending toward the right, extreme sensitivity to tight collars or waistbands around her neck and abdomen, rapid loquacious speech jumping incessantly from topic to topic, and severe worsening of her headaches and palpitations immediately upon waking (\u0027sleeps into aggravation\u0027), noticeably relieved once menstrual flow or nasal bleeding begins. Which remedy is indicated?","o":["Graphites","Phosphorus","Lachesis mutus","Lycopodium clavatum"],"a":2,"e":"Lachesis mutus (bushmaster snake venom) exhibits unmistakable keynotes: (1) left-sidedness and left-to-right progression, (2) marked intolerance of touch or tight clothing/collars around the neck and waist, (3) dramatic aggravation after sleep (waking in distress/suffocation), (4) intense loquacity with rapid topic-jumping and suspicious jealousy, and (5) amelioration from the onset of free discharges (menses, epistaxis, diarrhoea).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00294","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Pharmacy","topic":"HPI Ethanol Grades \u0026 Aphorisms 285-290 External Rules","q":"Under the Homoeopathic Pharmacopoeia of India (HPI), which standard ethanol strength (% v/v) corresponds to Strong/Rectified Spirit versus Dispensing Alcohol and Dilute Alcohol, and what rule does Hahnemann state (Organon Aphorisms 187-205 \u0026 285) regarding external medicinal applications in non-traumatic chronic skin diseases?","o":["Dispensing Alcohol is pure distilled water without ethanol; external arnica is banned even in acute bruises","Dilute Alcohol is 99% v/v; external remedies should always precede internal remedies in psoriasis","Strong Alcohol is 20% v/v, Dispensing Alcohol is 100% methanol; Hahnemann mandates steroid/tar ointments on all chronic eczema","Strong/Rectified Spirit ~95% v/v, Dispensing Alcohol ~88-90% v/v, Dilute Alcohol ~60% v/v; Hahnemann prohibits suppressive local external medications in dynamic chronic skin eruptions (permitting pure local remedies only for acute mechanical trauma alongside internal similimum)"],"a":3,"e":"HPI standardises liquid ethyl alcohol vehicles by % v/v strength: Strong Alcohol (94.9-96.0% v/v, ~95% v/v) for resinous/oleaginous mother tinctures; Dispensing Alcohol (~88-90% v/v) for routine liquid potencies and medicating globules; Dilute Alcohol (~60% v/v) for aqueous-soluble preparations. Clinically (Organon Aphorisms 187-205), Hahnemann strictly forbids suppressing chronic local skin manifestations with external drugs (which drives internal miasmatic disease deeper), reserving gentle external use (e.g., Calendula, Arnica on intact skin) solely for acute mechanical injuries.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00295","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Aphorisms 231-244 — Timing of Remedy in Intermittent Fevers","q":"In Organon of Medicine (Aphorisms 235-237), when treating a patient with a classic paroxysmal intermittent fever (distinct chill, heat, and sweat stages separated by an afebrile apyrexia interval), WHEN does Samuel Hahnemann instruct administering the selected homoeopathic similimum?","o":["Immediately after the termination of the febrile paroxysm (during the beginning of the apyrexia/afebrile interval) so the Vital Force can react quietly before the next expected paroxysm","Immediately 5 minutes before the next anticipated chill begins","Right at the peak of the rigor/chill stage to collide head-on with the paroxysm","Only in massive crude Cinchona bark doses throughout the fever spike"],"a":0,"e":"Hahnemann warns in Aphorism 236-237 that giving a homoeopathic medicine shortly before or during an intermittent fever paroxysm causes its primary medicinal action to coincide violently with the natural febrile crisis—distressing and exhausting the patient. Administering the dose immediately after a paroxysm subsides (at the onset of the apyrexia period) gives the Vital Force ample peaceful time to develop the curative counter-action and prevent or attenuate the next attack.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00296","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Aphorisms 245-248 — Aqueous Split-Dose \u0026 Succussion Modification","q":"In the 6th Edition of Organon of Medicine (Aphorisms 246-248), what major posological modification did Hahnemann introduce over the 5th Edition to allow safe, frequent repetition of doses in chronic diseases WITHOUT provoking severe homoeopathic aggravations?","o":["Mix three different LM potencies into a single cup without succussion","Dissolve one medicated globule (especially of 50 Millesimal LM potency) in an aqueous/alcoholic bottle solution and give the bottle 8-12 downward succussions immediately BEFORE each spoonful dose, slightly altering the potency degree every time so it is never identical to the preceding dose","Start every chronic case directly with CM dry powder daily","Repeat the exact same dry globule unsuccussed every 15 minutes for 6 months"],"a":1,"e":"In the 5th Edition, Hahnemann advised giving a single dry dose and waiting weeks until improvement ceased, because repeating an identical dry potency provoked sharp aggravations. In the 6th Edition (Aphorisms 246-248), he introduced the aqueous medicinal solution method: 1 globule of LM (or Centesimal) potency is dissolved in water + preservative alcohol; before each dose (daily or every second day), the bottle is succussed 8-12 times (and diluted further via a second cup if hyper-sensitive), raising the dynamic degree minutely each time so the Vital Force accepts rapid repetition smoothly without aggravation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00297","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Materia Medica","topic":"Calcarea carbonica Keynote Totality","q":"A chubby, pale, fair 14-month-old toddler has delayed closure of the anterior fontanelle, delayed dentition, and slow walking. His mother reports profuse sour-smelling sweat exclusively on his head and occiput during sleep—soaking the pillow wet—along with cold clammy feet, sour vomitus, constipation that strangely leaves him comfortable, and an intense craving for boiled eggs. Which constitutional remedy is clearly indicated?","o":["Silicea","Sulphur","Calcarea carbonica","Baryta carbonica"],"a":2,"e":"Calcarea carbonica (oyster-shell calcium carbonate) is the archetype of defective calcium assimilation and sluggish lymphatic constitution (\u0027fair, fat, flabby\u0027). Unmistakable keynotes include: profuse sour head/occipital perspiration during sleep wetting the pillow around the head; delayed milestones (open fontanelles, slow teething/walking); cold damp feet (\u0027feels as if wearing cold damp stockings\u0027); pronounced craving for boiled eggs (and indigestible lime/chalk); and feeling better when constipated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00298","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Materia Medica","topic":"Ignatia amara vs Natrum muriaticum","q":"A 29-year-old woman seeks help 1 year after a bereavement. Unlike acute hysterical sobbing, she cries only when strictly alone, broods silently over past hurts, becomes markedly irritable and worse whenever friends try to console her (\u0027\u003c consolation\u0027), craves table salt strongly, exhibits a mapped tongue, and suffers blinding hammering headaches from sunrise to sunset. Which chronic grief remedy is indicated over acute Ignatia amara?","o":["Phosphorus","Ignatia amara","Pulsatilla nigricans","Natrum muriaticum"],"a":3,"e":"While Ignatia amara is the primary acute remedy for recent shock/grief (marked by sighing, globus hystericus, mood swings, and paradoxical physical modalities), Natrum muriaticum is its chronic deeper counterpart for long-standing silent grief and suppressed resentment. Natrum mur keynotes include: marked aggravation from consolation (wants to be alone to weep), dwelling on disagreeable past events, intense craving for salt, mapped/geographic tongue, herpes labialis, and sunrise-to-sunset throbbing headaches.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00299","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Repertory and Case Taking","topic":"Complementary vs Inimical (Incompatible) Remedy Pairs","q":"In classical homoeopathic practice (Boenninghausen\u0027s Concordances and Gibson Miller\u0027s Remedy Relationships), certain \u0027inimical\u0027 (incompatible) remedies disturb each other\u0027s curative action and MUST NOT be prescribed immediately before or after one another without an intervening remedy. Which pair is classically listed as INIMICAL (incompatible)?","o":["Apis mellifica and Rhus toxicodendron (and similarly Causticum and Phosphorus, or Mercurius and Silicea)","Belladonna and Calcarea carbonica (classical acute-to-chronic complementary pair)","Nux vomica and Sulphur (classical complementary pair)","Pulsatilla and Silicea (classical complementary pair)"],"a":0,"e":"Gibson Miller and Clarke\u0027s Remedy Relationship tables warn against administering inimical pairs sequentially without an intercurrent remedy: classic incompatible/inimical pairs include Apis mellifica \u003c-\u003e Rhus toxicodendron, Causticum \u003c-\u003e Phosphorus, Mercurius \u003c-\u003e Silicea, and Lachesis \u003c-\u003e Aceticum acidum/Psorinum. Conversely, Belladonna -\u003e Calcarea carb, Pulsatilla -\u003e Silicea, and Nux vomica -\u003e Sulphur are renowned complementary pairs that follow each other harmoniously to complete a cure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00300","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Dynamic Derangement of Vital Force in Disease \u0026 Cure","q":"In Organon of Medicine (Aphorisms 9-16), how does Hahnemann characterise the relationship between the Vital Force (Dynamis), disease, and medicinal cure?","o":["Physical organs operate mechanically completely independent of any animating Vital Force","In health, the spirit-like autocratic Vital Force animates and maintains harmonious sensation/function; disease is exclusively an immaterial dynamic derangement of the Vital Force manifested through perceptible symptoms, curable ONLY by the dynamic spirit-like medicinal power of a similimum","The Vital Force exists only during sleep and ceases during waking hours","Disease is a tangible solid toxin lodged in blood vessels removable only by venesection and violent catharsis"],"a":1,"e":"Aphorisms 9-16 establish Hahnemann\u0027s vitalistic paradigm: without the Vital Force (Vital Principle in 6th Ed.), the material organism is dead and incapable of sensation or self-preservation (Aphorism 10). When noxious morbific influences act dynamically upon it, the untuned Vital Force expresses internal disharmony outwardly as the totality of symptoms (Aphorism 11-12)—which therefore cannot be cured by crude bloodletting or evacuants, but solely by matching dynamic potentised medicinal forces (Aphorism 16).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00301","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Unprejudiced Observation \u0026 Avoiding Leading Questions","q":"In Organon of Medicine (Aphorisms 83-90), why does Hahnemann prohibit the physician from interrupting the patient\u0027s initial narration or asking direct suggestive yes/no questions (e.g., \u0027Is your pain worse at 4 PM?\u0027) during homoeopathic case taking?","o":["Physicians must never write down any patient statements during the consultation","Only laboratory radiographs should be recorded in the case sheet","Interrupting breaks the patient\u0027s spontaneous train of thought, while leading/suggestive questions prompt an indolent or compliant patient to falsely agree (\u0027yes\u0027)—contaminating the authentic symptom totality with fabricated modalities; specific precision is sought ONLY after uninterrupted narration via open-ended questions","Asking leading questions saves time and guarantees 100% accurate rubrics in 2 minutes"],"a":2,"e":"Hahnemann\u0027s case-taking rules (Aphorisms 83-90) require freedom from prejudice, attentive listening while writing down the patient\u0027s and attendants\u0027 exact expressions silently (Aphorism 84), followed by neutral open-ended inquiry (\u0027At what time of day is it worse? What makes it better?\u0027) to clarify location, sensation, modalities, and concomitants—strictly avoiding suggestive yes/no or multiple-choice prompts (Aphorism 87) that distort the genuine image of the disease.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00302","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Materia Medica","topic":"Nux vomica Keynote Totality","q":"A 38-year-old driven, irritable, workaholic banker living on strong black coffee, alcohol, spicy dinners, and laxatives complains of frequent ineffectual urging for stool (\u0027passes only small amounts with feeling as if more remained\u0027), morning nausea relieved after vomiting, extreme chilliness (cannot tolerate slightest uncover/draft), oversensitivity to noise and light, and waking daily at 3:00 AM unable to sleep until dawn. Which remedy is classically indicated?","o":["Aloe socotrina","Veratrum album","Pulsatilla nigricans","Nux vomica"],"a":3,"e":"Nux vomica (Strychnos nux-vomica) is the classic polychrest for sedentary, zealous, irritable personalities overindulging in stimulants, alcohol, spicy food, and purgatives/allopathic drugs. Hallmark keynotes include: frequent ineffectual urging for stool (antiperistaltic spasm), waking reliably at 3-4 AM with racing business thoughts and unrefreshing dawn sleep, extreme chilliness/oversensitivity to sensory stimuli (noise, odours, light, drafts), and gastric distress/nausea relieved by vomiting.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00303","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Pharmacy","topic":"Nosodes, Sarcodes \u0026 Imponderabilia Classification","q":"In Homoeopathic Pharmacy (HPI), match each non-botanical/non-mineral drug source category to its exact definition and classic examples: 1) Nosodes, 2) Sarcodes, 3) Imponderabilia.","o":["1-Potentised preparations from pathological disease products/cultures (e.g., Psorinum, Tuberculinum, Medorrhinum, Carcinosin); 2-Potentised preparations from healthy animal organs, glands, or physiological secretions (e.g., Thyroidinum, Insulinum, Lac caninum); 3-Potentised preparations made from immaterial physical energy/radiation sources (e.g., X-ray, Sol, Magnetis poli ambo)","1-Synthetic sulfa drugs; 2-Mineral quartz; 3-Dried plant roots","1-Healthy animal hormones; 2-Plant alkaloids; 3-Diseased pus discharges","1-Immaterial sunlight/X-ray; 2-Crude arsenic salts; 3-Tubercular sputum"],"a":0,"e":"Homoeopathic Pharmacy classifies six major drug sources (Vegetable, Animal, Mineral, Nosodes, Sarcodes, Imponderabilia): Nosodes (from Greek \u0027nosos\u0027 = disease) are potentised pathological tissues, secretions, or microbial cultures (Psorinum, Syphilinum, Medorrhinum, Tuberculinum, Variolinum); Sarcodes (from \u0027sarx\u0027 = flesh) come from healthy endocrine glands/tissues/secretions (Thyroidinum, Adrenalinum, Lac caninum, Cholesterinum); Imponderabilia are immaterial weighless physical energies (X-ray, Sol, Luna, Magnetis poli ambo, Electricitas).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00304","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Allopathic vs Antipathic (Palliative) vs Homoeopathic Prescribing","q":"In Organon of Medicine (Aphorisms 22-27 \u0026 54-60), Hahnemann compares three possible therapeutic modes of applying medicines to disease: Allopathic (heteropathic), Antipathic (enantiopathic/palliative), and Homoeopathic. Why does Antipathic prescribing (\u0027contraria contrariis\u0027 — e.g., laxatives for chronic constipation or strong sedatives for chronic insomnia) fail to cure chronic disease?","o":["Antipathic prescribing obeys Nature\u0027s Law of Similars in Aphorism 26","An antipathic medicine acts against only a single symptom via an opposing Primary Action; once that wears off, the Vital Force mounts a Secondary counter-action in the exact direction of the original malady, leaving the chronic complaint progressively worse and requiring escalating doses","Antipathic drugs permanently annihilate psoric miasms by producing similar symptoms","Antipathic medicines cure chronic diseases too rapidly in a single dose"],"a":1,"e":"Hahnemann demonstrates in Aphorisms 56-60 that antipathic/palliative remedies target an isolated symptom with its exact opposite (e.g., purgatives for constipation, opium for pain/diarrhoea, cold water for burns). While the Primary Action briefly masks discomfort, the Vital Force automatically responds with an opposing Secondary Action—intensifying the original symptom afterward and driving tolerance/dose escalation (justifiable only in dire acute emergencies like suspended animation/poisoning where life is immediately threatened, Aphorism 67).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00305","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Organon of Medicine and Homoeopathic Philosophy","topic":"Outcome When Two Dissimilar vs Two Similar Diseases Meet","q":"In Organon of Medicine (Aphorisms 35-46), Hahnemann derives the natural law of cure by observing what happens when two natural diseases meet in the same living organism. What are the three possible outcomes when two DISSIMILAR diseases meet, compared with when two SIMILAR diseases meet?","o":["Two similar diseases always combine into a lifelong complex incurable disease","Two dissimilar diseases always permanently cure each other within 24 hours","Two DISSIMILAR diseases cannot cure each other: (1) if the older is stronger, it repels the newer; (2) if the newer is stronger, it merely SUSPENDS the weaker until the new one finishes, after which the old returns; or (3) two chronic dissimilar diseases form a COMPLEX disease; conversely, two SIMILAR diseases permanently ANNIHILATE the weaker one","A weaker dissimilar disease permanently destroys a stronger chronic disease"],"a":2,"e":"Hahnemann cites historical clinical observations (Aphorisms 36-46): (1) A strong pre-existing chronic disease (e.g., severe scurvy/psora) repels mild dissimilar infection; (2) A stronger acute dissimilar disease (e.g., measles/smallpox) temporarily suspends an existing dissimilar chronic complaint (like epilepsy/tinea), which promptly re-emerges once the acute fever clears; (3) Two dissimilar chronic diseases of nearly equal strength (e.g., syphilis + psora) co-exist as a complex disease. ONLY when a stronger SIMILAR disease meets a weaker one (e.g., cowpox vaccinia extinguishing smallpox ophthalmia/eruption) does permanent homoeopathic annihilation occur.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00306","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Materia Medica","topic":"Sulphur vs Psorinum Differentiation","q":"A 48-year-old stooped, untidy philosophy teacher complains of chronic itching eczema and haemorrhoids. He is intensely hot-blooded, kicks the blankets off at night because the soles of his feet burn like fire, has bright red lips and ear margins, hates bathing (\u0027worse from washing\u0027), gets worse standing for even 10 minutes, and develops a sudden empty, weak \u0027all-gone\u0027 hunger pang daily at 11:00 AM. Which deep anti-psoric polychrest is clearly indicated over chilly Psorinum?","o":["Psorinum","Calcarea carbonica","Graphites","Sulphur"],"a":3,"e":"Sulphur (\u0027King of Anti-psorics\u0027) presents unmistakable hot, congestive keynotes: burning soles of feet thrust out of bed covers at night (also burning vertex); vivid redness of all orifices (lips, eyelids, ears, anus); extreme aversion to bathing with relapse after washing; sudden 11 AM empty faintness in the epigastrium requiring food; aggravation from standing; and slovenly \u0027ragged philosopher\u0027 disposition. By contrast, Psorinum is its chilly counterpart (\u0027always wears a warm coat even in summer\u0027, offensive discharges, deep hopelessness, and nocturnal hunger).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00307","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Materia Medica","topic":"Belladonna Keynote Totality","q":"An 8-year-old boy suddenly develops a spiking high fever (40 C) at 3 PM with bright scarlet-red flushed face, visibly throbbing carotid arteries, widely dilated glassy pupils, dry burning radiating heat (so hot the hand feels scorched approaching his forehead while his hands/feet feel cold), right-sided tonsillitis worse swallowing liquids, and screaming sensitivity to bright light, noise, and jarring the bed. Which acute remedy fits this totality?","o":["Belladonna","Bryonia alba","Apis mellifica","Gelsemium sempervirens"],"a":0,"e":"Atropa belladonna corresponds to sudden, violent, hyperaemic acute inflammations with intense vascular engorgement: red face, bounding throbbing carotids, dilated pupils, hot head/skin radiating palpable heat with cold extremities, dry strawberry tongue/right-sided throat spasm (\u003c liquids/touch), and marked sensory hyperaesthesia (\u003c light, noise, jarring/stepping near the bed).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00308","course":"BHMS - Homoeopathy","subject":"Homoeopathic Repertory and Case Taking","topic":"Seven Factors of a Complete Symptom (Latin Hexameter)","q":"In Boenninghausen\u0027s methodology for constructing a Complete Symptom (adapted from the classical Latin hexameter: \u0027Quis, quid, ubi, quibus auxiliis, cur, quomodo, quando\u0027), match each Latin interrogative term to its clinical meaning: 1) Quis, 2) Ubi, 3) Quibus auxiliis, 4) Quomodo, 5) Quando.","o":["1-Causation; 2-Time; 3-Location; 4-Personality; 5-Concomitants","1-Patient\u0027s personality/constitution \u0026 temperament; 2-Anatomical seat/location; 3-Concomitant symptoms; 4-Modalities (aggravation/amelioration); 5-Time of onset, periodicity \u0026 duration","1-Sensation; 2-Potency scale; 3-Causation; 4-Location; 5-Dietary vehicle","1-Concomitants; 2-Modalities; 3-Time; 4-Personality; 5-Anatomical seat"],"a":1,"e":"Boenninghausen applied the classical seven-question framework to make every symptom complete for repertorisation: Quis (Who — patient\u0027s constitution/mentals), Quid (What — nature \u0026 sensation of complaint), Ubi (Where — exact anatomical location \u0026 direction), Quibus auxiliis (With what concomitants — accompanying symptoms), Cur (Why — exciting/maintaining causation), Quomodo (How — modifying modalities \u003c and \u003e), and Quando (When — time aggravation \u0026 periodicity).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00309","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"OT in Physical Dysfunction \u0026 Orthotics","topic":"Radial Nerve Palsy","q":"A 30-year-old carpenter sustains a mid-shaft humeral fracture complicated by radial nerve palsy, resulting in inability to extend the wrist, MCP joints, and thumb. Which orthotic prescription best positions the hand to prevent overstretching of denervated extensors while enabling functional grasp during daily tasks?","o":["Thumb spica splint positioning the thumb in palmar adduction","Ulnar gutter splint immobilizing the ring and little fingers in full extension","Dynamic tenodesis / low-profile outrigger extension splint holding the wrist in 20-30 degrees extension with dynamic MCP extension assist","Volar wrist-cock-up static splint holding the wrist in 20-30 degrees flexion"],"a":2,"e":"In radial nerve palsy (wrist drop), placing the wrist in 20-30 degrees of extension optimizes the length-tension relationship of the intact extrinsic finger flexors for power grip and prevents overstretching of paralyzed wrist extensors. Adding a dynamic MCP/thumb extension outrigger allows active finger flexion for grasp followed by passive elastic extension for release.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00310","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Pediatric Occupational Therapy","topic":"Sensory Modulation Disorders","q":"A 6-year-old child with sensory modulation disorder exhibits tactile defensiveness—withdrawing distressfully from light touch, messy play, and clothing tags, accompanied by sympathetic hyperarousal. Under Ayres Sensory Integration (ASI) principles, which sensory modality provides the most effective calming and organizing modulation?","o":["Brisk light-touch brushing with a soft feather across the forearms","Rapid spinning on a suspended bolster swing in erratic planes","High-frequency strobe lighting paired with loud auditory cues","Deep pressure touch and active proprioceptive heavy-work activities (e.g., pushing weighted carts, climbing, joint traction/compression)"],"a":3,"e":"Deep pressure touch and active proprioceptive resistance (\u0027heavy work\u0027) activate dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathways and mechanoreceptors in muscles and joints, exerting an inhibitory, calming, and organizing effect on reticular arousal and reducing over-responsivity to light touch.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00311","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Ergonomics, ADL \u0026 Work Simplification","topic":"Joint Protection Principles","q":"A 45-year-old homemaker with Rheumatoid Arthritis exhibits bilateral MCP synovitis and early ulnar drift of the digits. When educating her on joint protection during kitchen and household ADLs, which adaptive technique is MOST biomechanically sound?","o":["Using built-up foam handles, an electric jar opener, and pushing heavy doors open with the forearm/palm rather than fingers","Carrying heavy cookware by hooking fingers under thin wire handles","Wringing wet clothes tightly using forceful lateral pinch and ulnar wrist rotation","Resting hands in tight clenched fists to strengthen intrinsic muscles against pain"],"a":0,"e":"Joint protection principles dictate distributing loads over larger, stronger joints (forearms/palms), reducing pinch and grip force via built-up handles and assistive devices, avoiding positions that push MCPs ulnarly (such as twisting jar lids clockwise with the right hand or wringing), and respecting pain.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00312","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Neurological Occupational Therapy","topic":"Constraint-Induced Movement Therapy (CIMT)","q":"An occupational therapist is evaluating four chronic (\u003e6 months) post-stroke patients with \u0027learned non-use\u0027 of the paretic upper extremity for Constraint-Induced Movement Therapy (CIMT). Which patient meets the classic minimal active motor inclusion criteria for CIMT?","o":["Patient with severe flexor spasticity (MAS 4) and fixed wrist flexion contracture","Patient demonstrating \u003e= 20 degrees active wrist extension and \u003e= 10 degrees active MCP/IP extension of the thumb and at least two fingers, with intact balance","Patient with active shoulder shrug only and severe unilateral spatial neglect with falls risk","Patient with complete flaccid paralysis (Brunnstrom Stage I) and 0 degrees active wrist/finger movement"],"a":1,"e":"Per the EXCITE trial and classic Taub/Wolf criteria, CIMT requires sufficient distal voluntary motor return to engage in repetitive functional grasp-and-release shaping: specifically, at least 20 degrees of active wrist extension and at least 10 degrees of active extension at the thumb and two digits (lower-functioning protocols require \u003e= 10 deg wrist and \u003e= 10 deg thumb/2 digits), plus adequate cognitive and balance safety while wearing a mitt.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00313","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy Models \u0026 Outcome Measures","topic":"COPM vs FIM","q":"How does the Canadian Occupational Performance Measure (COPM) differ structurally from the Functional Independence Measure (FIM) in occupational therapy evaluation?","o":["COPM scores 18 fixed ADL items on a 1-7 assistance scale; FIM is an open-ended interview","COPM is a client-centered semi-structured interview where the client prioritizes up to 5 occupational problems across self-care, productivity, and leisure and rates Performance and Satisfaction (1-10); FIM grades 18 motor/cognitive items on a 1-7 independence scale","Both COPM and FIM measure only passive joint range of motion in degrees","COPM is exclusively used in neonatal ICUs; FIM is restricted to psychiatric outpatient clinics"],"a":1,"e":"Anchored in the Canadian Model of Occupational Performance (CMOP-E), the COPM empowers the client to identify priority occupational performance issues in self-care, productivity, and leisure, scoring Importance, Performance (1-10), and Satisfaction (1-10) (with \u003e= 2 points indicating clinically significant change). Conversely, FIM is a standardized 18-item (13 motor, 5 cognitive) burden-of-care scale scored 1 (total assistance) to 7 (complete independence), totaling 18-126.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00314","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"OT in Burns \u0026 Plastic Surgery","topic":"Anti-Deformity Positioning","q":"A patient sustains deep partial-thickness thermal burns over the anterior neck, right axilla, and dorsal aspect of the right hand. Applying the principle that \u0027the position of comfort is the position of contracture,\u0027 which anti-deformity positioning regimen must the occupational therapist initiate immediately?","o":["Allow unrestricted fetal positioning until split-thickness skin grafting heals completely at 6 weeks","Cervical flexion collar, shoulder internal rotation sling, and volar flexion splint","Neutral/slight cervical extension (no pillows under head), right shoulder abducted 90-120 degrees with slight horizontal flexion, and hand in intrinsic-plus position (wrist 20-30 deg extension, MCPs 70-90 deg flexion, IPs 0 deg extension, thumb palmar abduction)","Two soft pillows under the head, right arm adducted at side, and hand resting flat with MCPs hyperextended and IPs flexed"],"a":2,"e":"Burn scars contract toward shortened positions of comfort (flexion/adduction). Dorsal hand burns pull the MCPs into hyperextension and IPs into flexion (\u0027claw deformity\u0027); the intrinsic-plus (Edinburgh/safe) splint places MCP collateral ligaments at maximal length (70-90 deg flexion) and IPs in full extension (0 deg). Neck extension (no pillows) prevents chin-to-chest contracture, and 90-120 deg abduction with 15-20 deg horizontal flexion prevents axillary web contracture without stretching the brachial plexus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00315","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Spinal Cord Injury (SCI) Rehabilitation","topic":"C6 Tetraplegia Tenodesis Grasp","q":"A 24-year-old man with complete C6 ASIA-A tetraplegia retains strong wrist extension (Extensor Carpi Radialis Longus/Brevis, Grade 4/5) but has complete paralysis of finger flexors and intrinsic hand muscles. How must the occupational therapist manage passive finger range of motion to preserve a functional tenodesis grasp?","o":["Aggressively stretch the wrist and fingers into simultaneous maximal hyperextension daily","Immobilize the wrist permanently in 45 degrees flexion","Perform passive finger flexion only while keeping the MCP joints hyperextended","Avoid stretching finger flexors with the wrist simultaneously extended; instead allow mild controlled tightness of extrinsic finger flexors so active wrist extension passively flexes the fingers/thumb into functional grasp (tenodesis)"],"a":3,"e":"In C6 tetraplegia, intact ECRL/ECRB enables active wrist extension. By maintaining gentle adaptive shortening (controlled tightness) of the paralyzed extrinsic finger flexors (FDS/FDP/FPL)—stretching fingers ONLY with the wrist flexed, never extending wrist and fingers together—active wrist extension pulls the fingers and thumb into functional grasp/lateral pinch, while gravity-assisted wrist flexion opens the hand.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00316","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Neurocognitive \u0026 Perceptual OT","topic":"Unilateral Spatial Neglect (USN)","q":"A patient post-stroke repeatedly eats food only from the right half of the dinner tray, shaves only the right side of his face, and marks the midpoint far to the right of center on the Line Bisection Test. Which cerebral hemisphere is most typically lesioned, and which standardized OT behavioral scale assesses neglect during real-life ADLs?","o":["Right parietal lobe (non-dominant hemisphere); Catherine Bergego Scale (alongside Star Cancellation / Behavioural Inattention Test)","Left occipital lobe; Berg Balance Scale","Left prefrontal cortex; Semmes-Weinstein Monofilament Test","Cerebellar vermis; Nine-Hole Peg Test"],"a":0,"e":"Left unilateral spatial neglect (inattention to contralesional left space) classically follows right parietal/temporoparietal lobe infarction because the right hemisphere attends bilaterally while the left attends primarily rightward. Paper-and-pencil tools (Line Bisection, Star Cancellation/BIT) paired with ecological ADL observation via the Catherine Bergego Scale quantify neglect severity and guide visual scanning/anchoring and prism adaptation therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00317","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Mental Health \u0026 Psychosocial Occupational Therapy","topic":"Allen Cognitive Level Screen (ACLS)","q":"During inpatient psychiatric occupational therapy evaluation, a patient with chronic schizophrenia achieves a score of 4.2 on the Allen Cognitive Level Screen (ACLS-5, Goal-Directed Actions). When designing a therapeutic craft or ADL task for this patient, which instructional and environmental grading matches Level 4 functional cognition?","o":["Restrict therapy entirely to passive tactile stimulation without any goal-directed object manipulation","Provide a completed visual sample, clear step-by-step demonstration of visible concrete steps (2-3 steps), and check for hidden errors that the patient will not anticipate independently","Provide only verbal abstract instructions for a multi-week unguided woodworking project","Expect independent medication budgeting, complex safety troubleshooting, and inductive new learning without cues"],"a":1,"e":"Under Allen\u0027s Cognitive Disabilities Model, Level 4 (Goal-Directed Actions) patients attend to visible cues and can copy a demonstrated concrete model to complete familiar 2-3 step tasks, but rely on trial-and-error rather than inductive reasoning—failing to detect hidden hazards or plan novel multi-step sequences (Levels 5-6). OT grading provides exact visual samples, pre-set materials, and safety supervision.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00318","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Hand Therapy \u0026 Splinting","topic":"Swan-Neck vs Boutonniere Deformity","q":"A 48-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis presents with hyperextension of the Proximal Interphalangeal (PIP) joint and compensatory flexion of the Distal Interphalangeal (DIP) joint of the middle finger (\u0027swan-neck\u0027 deformity), causing snapping when initiating grip. Which orthotic design specifically corrects this deformity while allowing functional flexion?","o":["Boutonniere splint holding the PIP at 0 degrees extension and leaving the DIP completely free for active flexion","Mallet splint immobilizing only the DIP joint in hyperextension","Figure-of-8 (Silver Ring / Oval-8) three-point orthosis positioned to block PIP hyperextension (holding PIP in slight 10-15 degrees flexion) while permitting unrestricted active PIP flexion","Immobilize the PIP joint rigidly in 15 degrees of hyperextension"],"a":2,"e":"Swan-neck deformity (PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion) results from volar plate attenuation, intrinsic tightness, or dorsal migration of lateral bands. A Figure-of-8 (Silver Ring/Oval-8) splint applies three-point pressure—dorsal proximal/middle phalanges and volar PIP—blocking PIP hyperextension at ~10-15 deg flexion while allowing full active flexion for grasp. Conversely, Boutonniere deformity (PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension) requires splinting the PIP at 0 deg extension while exercising DIP flexion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00319","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Ergonomics \u0026 Work Rehabilitation","topic":"NIOSH Lifting Equation \u0026 Carpal Tunnel Ergonomics","q":"In an industrial ergonomics workplace evaluation to prevent lumbar disc overload and carpal tunnel syndrome (median nerve compression), which two workstation modifications are biomechanically MOST effective?","o":["Hold heavy boxes far forward with extended arms and keep wrists flexed 45 degrees during typing","Use narrow, slippery screwdriver handles requiring high-force tip pinch","Twist the lumbar spine 90 degrees while lifting from floor level","Keep lifted loads as close to the body\u0027s center of gravity as possible (minimizing horizontal distance H in the NIOSH Lifting Equation) and maintain neutral wrist alignment (0-15 degrees extension) avoiding repetitive forceful pinch and ulnar deviation"],"a":3,"e":"In the NIOSH Revised Lifting Equation, horizontal distance (H) from the midpoint between the ankles to the hands is the single largest multiplier penalty because spinal extensor torque scales directly with moment arm. Similarly, carpal tunnel pressure is lowest with the wrist in neutral (0-15 deg extension) and spikes sharply during wrist flexion/extension, ulnar deviation, and forceful repetitive pinch.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00320","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Pediatric Occupational Therapy","topic":"Grasp Progression \u0026 In-Hand Manipulation","q":"A 5-year-old boy struggles to keep up with kindergarten handwriting, stabilizing his pencil using a whole-hand fisted palmar supinate grasp while rotating his entire forearm and shoulder rather than moving his fingers, and drops coins when trying to move them from his palm to his fingertips. Which developmental prehension milestone and in-hand manipulation skill are delayed?","o":["Dynamic tripod pencil grasp (typically mature by 4.5-6 years) and palm-to-finger translation / complex rotation in-hand manipulation, requiring proximal wrist/arch stability and intrinsic finger dissociation","Automatic palmar grasp reflex; Moro startle integration","Primitive ulnar-palmar grasp; gross rolling","Raking grasp (expected at 6 years); bimanual bilateral clapping"],"a":0,"e":"Grasp matures from ulnar-palmar (4-5 mo) -\u003e palmar/radial-palmar (6-7 mo) -\u003e radial-digital/pincer (9-12 mo) -\u003e palmar supinate crayon grasp (1-1.5 yr) -\u003e digital pronate (2-3 yr) -\u003e static tripod (3.5-4 yr) -\u003e dynamic tripod grasp (4.5-6 yr, moving the pencil with isolated thumb, index, and middle finger flexion/extension while ulnar digits stabilize). Moving coins from palm to fingertips tests palm-to-finger translation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00321","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Hand Rehabilitation \u0026 Orthotics","topic":"Low Ulnar Nerve Palsy (Ulnar Claw Hand)","q":"A cyclist with deep handlebar compression at Guyon\u0027s canal develops wasting of the first dorsal interosseous, hyperextension of the 4th and 5th MCP joints with flexion of the PIP/DIP joints (\u0027ulnar claw hand\u0027), and compensates during lateral key pinch by forcefully flexing the thumb IP joint (positive Froment\u0027s sign). Which orthosis prevents volar plate stretching and restores finger extension?","o":["Dynamic radial outrigger holding MCPs in 15 degrees hyperextension","Ulnar anti-claw (lumbrical bar / dynamic MCP flexion-block) orthosis holding the 4th and 5th MCP joints in 30-45 degrees flexion so extrinsic finger extensors can extend the IP joints","Buddy taping the thumb to the index finger","Thumb spica splint holding the thumb IP in full flexion"],"a":1,"e":"Low ulnar nerve palsy paralyzes the 3rd/4th lumbricals, interossei, and adductor pollicis (yielding positive Froment\u0027s sign as median-innervated FPL substitutes for key pinch). Unopposed extensor digitorum hyperextends the 4th-5th MCPs, preventing extrinsic extensors from straightening the IPs. An anti-claw orthosis (dorsal lumbrical bar) blocks 4th-5th MCP hyperextension at 30-45 deg flexion, redirecting extrinsic extensor tension distally to extend the PIP/DIP joints.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00322","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Geriatric Occupational Therapy \u0026 Environmental Modification","topic":"Bathroom \u0026 Staircase Architectural Adaptations","q":"During a pre-discharge home safety evaluation for a 78-year-old post-hip-fracture patient using a rolling walker, which bathroom and environmental modification recommendation is MOST critical for fall prevention?","o":["Replace single-lever faucets with small round slippery knobs","Polish wooden floors with high-gloss wax and remove stair handrails to widen passage","Remove all loose throw rugs/cords, install raised toilet seat and grab bars bolted directly into structural wall studs (rated \u003e= 110-135 kg), apply non-slip bathroom strips, and ensure bright glare-free lighting with nightlights","Install suction-cup grab bars onto ceramic tiles and place plush loose scatter rugs beside the bed"],"a":2,"e":"Bathroom transfers and nighttime toileting are leading causes of geriatric falls. Evidence-based OT home modification removes tripping hazards (throw rugs, trailing cords), adds high-contrast non-slip strips, raises toilet seat height to reduce hip flexion/quadriceps demand, installs structural stud-mounted grab bars (never suction-cup bars or towel racks, which pull off under body weight!), and ensures adequate illumination.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00323","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Neurorehabilitation \u0026 Cognitive-Perceptual OT","topic":"Ideomotor Apraxia","q":"A patient with a left hemisphere stroke understands verbal instructions and can accurately explain what a toothbrush is for, yet when asked on command to \u0027show me how you brush your teeth,\u0027 he rubs his index finger directly across his gums (\u0027body-part-as-object\u0027 error) and struggles to orient a real toothbrush on request—even though he brushes spontaneously when standing at his bathroom sink upon waking. Which deficit is present and what OT intervention is most effective?","o":["Hemiplegic cerebellar tremor; apply 5 kg wrist weights","Ideational apraxia (total loss of object purpose); treat exclusively with abstract verbal lectures","Severe sensory neuropathy; desensitization massage","Ideomotor apraxia (impaired voluntary motor execution/pantomime despite intact conceptual knowledge and automatic contextual performance); treat using contextual real-object task practice in the natural setting, hand-over-hand tactile guiding, and errorless learning"],"a":3,"e":"Ideomotor apraxia (typically left parietal/premotor lesions) disrupts voluntary purposeful movement execution and pantomime (classically producing body-part-as-object errors) while preserving conceptual knowledge of the tool\u0027s purpose and automatic performance in familiar environmental contexts. OT intervention avoids abstract pantomime drills and instead uses real objects in natural contexts, tactile/kinesthetic cueing, and errorless backward/forward chaining.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00324","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Foundations of Occupational Therapy","topic":"Model of Human Occupation (MOHO)","q":"A 52-year-old bank clerk recovering from an uncomplicated myocardial infarction expresses intense fear of returning to work because he doubts his stamina and no longer values his stressful desk job. According to Kielhofner\u0027s Model of Human Occupation (MOHO), which subsystem is primarily disrupted?","o":["Volition (personal causation, values, and interests)","Habituation (habits and internalized roles)","Physical and social environmental press","Performance capacity (musculoskeletal and cardiopulmonary components)"],"a":0,"e":"In the Model of Human Occupation (MOHO), volition encompasses personal causation (sense of capacity and efficacy), values (what one finds important and meaningful), and interests (what one finds enjoyable). The client\u0027s doubt in his stamina reflects altered personal causation, and his loss of meaning in his job reflects shifted values.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00325","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Neurological Conditions","topic":"Motor Recovery Approaches","q":"A 58-year-old right-handed man 4 months post-left MCA ischemic stroke is evaluated for Constraint-Induced Movement Therapy (CIMT) of his paretic right upper limb. Which minimum active motor criterion must he demonstrate on examination to qualify for standard CIMT?","o":["At least 90 degrees of active shoulder flexion against gravity with normal grip strength","At least 20 degrees of active wrist extension and 10 degrees of active MCP/IP extension of at least two fingers and the thumb","Full active supination and pronation with 4/5 triceps strength on manual muscle testing","Complete absence of spasticity across all joints and independent finger-to-nose coordination"],"a":1,"e":"Classical EXCITE trial and standard CIMT inclusion criteria require minimum active distal motor return of at least 20 degrees of wrist extension and 10 degrees of extension at the metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joints of the thumb and at least two fingers, repeated three times in 1 minute, alongside adequate balance and cognition.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00326","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Orthopedics and Hand Rehabilitation","topic":"Hand Burn and Trauma Splinting","q":"A 26-year-old industrial worker sustains deep partial-thickness thermal burns over the dorsum of the right hand. To prevent metacarpophalangeal (MCP) hyperextension and interphalangeal (IP) flexion contractures during immobilization, in which position should the therapist fabricate the resting hand orthosis?","o":["Wrist in 30 degrees flexion, MCP joints in 0 degrees extension, IP joints in 45 degrees flexion, and thumb adducted","Wrist in 45 degrees extension, MCP joints in 10 degrees flexion, IP joints in 60 degrees flexion, and thumb in extension","Wrist in 20–30 degrees extension, MCP joints in 70–90 degrees flexion, IP joints in 0 degrees extension, and thumb in palmar abduction","Wrist in neutral, MCP joints in 20 degrees hyperextension, IP joints in 90 degrees flexion, and thumb in radial adduction"],"a":2,"e":"The intrinsic-plus (anti-deformity or Edinburgh/safe) position places the wrist in 20–30 degrees extension, MCP joints in 70–90 degrees flexion (maintaining maximal length of the collateral ligaments), IP joints in full extension (preventing volar plate contracture and boutonniere/claw deformity), and the thumb in palmar abduction to preserve the first web space.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00327","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Pediatrics","topic":"Ayres Sensory Integration (ASI)","q":"A 6-year-old boy demonstrates extreme panic whenever his feet leave the ground, refuses playground swings and escalators, and clings tightly when tilted backward, yet tolerates messy finger-painting and loud noises without distress. Which sensory modulation dysfunction best explains his presentation?","o":["Auditory hypersensitivity","Somatodyspraxia","Tactile defensiveness","Gravitational insecurity (vestibular-otolith modulation dysfunction)"],"a":3,"e":"Gravitational insecurity, described by A. Jean Ayres, is an over-responsiveness to linear vestibular (otolith) input and changes in head position relative to gravity, manifesting as intense anxiety when feet are off the ground or when tilted backward.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00328","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Ergonomics and Cardiopulmonary Rehabilitation","topic":"Cardiac and Pulmonary ADL Training","q":"A 64-year-old homemaker with severe COPD (GOLD Grade 3) experiences dyspnea (Borg RPE 6/10) while transferring heavy wet laundry from a low bucket to a high clothesline. Which combined occupational therapy modification best reduces her myocardial and ventilatory workload?","o":["Slide laundry in a wheeled basket at waist height, exhale during exertion, sit while folding, and avoid breath-holding","Hold breath briefly while lifting heavy loads quickly to finish sooner, and hang clothes above shoulder height","Carry double loads in fewer trips while keeping arms elevated overhead to expand the rib cage","Perform all laundry tasks immediately after a large meal in a warm steamy bathroom to loosen secretions"],"a":0,"e":"Energy conservation and work simplification emphasize sliding rather than lifting, organizing workspaces between waist and shoulder height, sitting when possible, pacing activities, and exhaling (pursed-lip breathing) during exertion while strictly avoiding the Valsalva maneuver (breath-holding) and sustained overhead arm elevation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00329","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Neurological Conditions","topic":"Tetraplegia Functional Training","q":"A 22-year-old man with a complete C6 ASIA-A spinal cord injury has 4/5 strength in extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL) but 0/5 strength in triceps and all finger flexors and extensors. How should the occupational therapist train functional grasp during feeding and grooming?","o":["Preserve controlled finger flexor tightness to utilize active wrist extension for a passive tenodesis grasp alongside a universal cuff","Aggressively stretch the finger flexors and thumb into full simultaneous wrist and finger extension","Prescribe a dynamic finger extension outrigger splint to keep the fingers continuously extended","Train independent overhead wheelie transfers without assistive devices using triceps push-off"],"a":0,"e":"At the C6 level, preserved ECRL allows active wrist extension, which passively pulls the shortened extrinsic finger flexors to approximate the thumb and fingers (tenodesis grasp), while wrist flexion opens the hand. Therapists avoid overstretching finger flexors into simultaneous wrist and finger extension and use universal cuffs or built-up handles.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00330","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Mental Health","topic":"Mosey\u0027s Developmental Groups","q":"Match Mosey\u0027s developmental group levels with their primary characteristic: (1) Parallel group, (2) Project group, (3) Egocentric-cooperative group, (4) Cooperative group. Which matching sequence is correct?","o":["1-Clients share short-term tasks; 2-Peers meet emotional needs without leader; 3-Clients work side-by-side; 4-Clients perform long-term task with leader support","1-Clients work side-by-side on individual tasks with minimal interaction; 2-Clients share short-term tasks requiring brief cooperation; 3-Clients select, implement, and evaluate a long-term task together with leader support; 4-Peers meet emotional and task needs with therapist as advisor","1-Clients select a long-term task together; 2-Clients work side-by-side without interaction; 3-Peers meet emotional needs independently; 4-Clients perform brief shared tasks","1-Therapist acts solely as advisor; 2-Clients work side-by-side; 3-Clients perform brief shared tasks; 4-Clients engage in long-term task with leader support"],"a":1,"e":"Anne Cronin Mosey\u0027s five developmental groups progress sequentially: Parallel (working side-by-side comfortably), Project (short-term shared task interaction), Egocentric-cooperative (long-term group task with recognition of others\u0027 rights), Cooperative (group satisfies socio-emotional and task needs with therapist as advisor), and Mature (members assume all leadership roles).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00331","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy Assessment and Outcome Measures","topic":"Barthel Index Scoring","q":"On the standard 100-point Barthel Index, a post-stroke patient scores: Feeding = 10, Bathing = 0, Grooming = 5, Dressing = 5, Bowels = 10, Bladder = 10, Toilet Use = 5, Transfers (bed to chair and back) = 10, Mobility (on level surfaces) = 10, Stairs = 0. What is the patient\u0027s total Barthel Index score and corresponding dependency category?","o":["75/100 (Mild dependency)","80/100 (Minimal dependency)","65/100 (Moderate dependency)","55/100 (Severe dependency)"],"a":2,"e":"Summing the 10 standard Barthel Index items: 10 (Feeding) + 0 (Bathing) + 5 (Grooming) + 5 (Dressing) + 10 (Bowels) + 10 (Bladder) + 5 (Toilet Use) + 10 (Transfers) + 10 (Mobility) + 0 (Stairs) = 65/100. On standard Shah/Mahoney-Barthel interpretation, scores of 61–90 (or 60–79 depending on cutoffs, specifically 61–90) indicate moderate dependency (21–60 severe, 0–20 total).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00332","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Neurological \u0026 Visual-Perceptual OT","topic":"Homonymous Hemianopia vs Unilateral Neglect","q":"A 60-year-old accountant with a right occipital PCA stroke has left homonymous hemianopia, yet demonstrates intact right parietal attention—he is fully aware of his left visual field loss, turns his head leftward spontaneously when prompted, and misses words at the start of reading lines unless cued. Which occupational therapy compensatory strategy best improves reading and mobility?","o":["Restricting the patient exclusively to darkened rooms","Eye-patch occlusion over the right eye to suppress diplopia","High-intensity thermal heat packs over the occipital scalp","Systematic wide leftward head-and-eye saccadic scanning training, placing a bright colored vertical anchor strip along the left margin of reading text, and using a line-guide/ruler"],"a":3,"e":"Unlike unilateral spatial neglect (where right parietal lesions impair awareness/attention to left space), patients with pure homonymous hemianopia recognize their blind hemifield and rapidly learn compensatory behaviors: deliberate oversized saccadic eye/head scanning into the blind left field, anchor lines on the left page margin to mark line beginnings during reading, and organized grid search patterns.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00333","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Pediatric Occupational Therapy","topic":"Beery VMI \u0026 Ergonomic 90-90-90 Seating","q":"When evaluating a 7-year-old child with illegible handwriting using the Beery-Buktenica Developmental Test of Visual-Motor Integration (Beery VMI), what ergonomic seated desk posture (\u002790-90-90 rule\u0027) must the occupational therapist establish FIRST to provide proximal postural stability?","o":["Feet dangling 15 cm off the floor with desk at chin height","Feet flat on the floor (or footrest), ankles/knees/hips positioned at ~90 degrees flexion with back supported, and desk surface ~5 cm (2 inches) above the flexed elbow","Standing continuously on one foot while writing on an overhead chalkboard","Lying prone on the floor with wrists hyperextended 60 degrees"],"a":1,"e":"Distal fine-motor precision requires proximal trunk and pelvic stability. Before remediating graphomotor skills assessed by the Beery VMI (visual-motor, visual perception, and motor coordination subtests), the OT ensures the 90-90-90 seating posture (feet flat, 90 deg ankle/knee/hip angles, upright supported back, desktop 2 inches above flexed elbow) so the child does not prop the trunk with the writing arm.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00334","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Upper Extremity Amputation Rehabilitation","topic":"Desensitization \u0026 Hand Dominance Retraining","q":"A 29-year-old right-hand-dominant graphic artist undergoes a right transradial amputation after an industrial press injury and displays severe residual-limb hypersensitivity to light touch alongside frustration with writing and dressing. During the pre-prosthetic acute phase, what sequence of OT interventions addresses both hypersensitivity and functional independence?","o":["Initiate graded tactile desensitization (progressing from gentle constant pressure and soft textures -\u003e terrycloth -\u003e dry rice/beans -\u003e vibration/tapping), scar mobilization once healed, AND structured dominance retraining of the left hand (writing loops, weighted utensils, one-handed dressing/buttoning aids)","Wait until definitive myoelectric prosthesis delivery at 6 months before starting left-hand ADL training","Avoid touching the residual limb completely for 6 months until phantom pain vanishes","Apply painful high-voltage galvanic shocks directly to the surgical incision on Day 2"],"a":0,"e":"Early pre-prosthetic OT prevents chronic pain/learned helplessness by pairing graded tactile desensitization (progressive texture/pressure/vibration inputs to normalize dorsal-horn sensory gating) and scar massage with immediate one-handed ADL adaptation and non-dominant hand dominance retraining (e.g., pre-writing loops/circles, rocker knife, Dycem non-slip mats, button hooks).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00335","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Orthopedics and Hand Rehabilitation","topic":"Radial Nerve Palsy Orthotics","q":"A 32-year-old man sustains a mid-shaft humeral fracture complicated by high radial nerve palsy, presenting with wrist drop and inability to actively extend the wrist, thumb, or metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints, while finger flexion and median/ulnar intrinsic muscles remain intact. Which orthotic design best restores functional grasp during nerve recovery?","o":["Thumb spica cast immobilizing the IP and MCP joints in full flexion","Dynamic radial nerve palsy orthosis supporting the wrist in 20–30 degrees extension with elastic outrigger assistance to MCP extension (allowing active finger flexion)","Ulnar gutter splint immobilizing only the ring and little fingers in 90 degrees MCP flexion","Static wrist flexion splint holding the wrist in 40 degrees volar flexion"],"a":1,"e":"High radial nerve injury denervates the wrist extensors, extensor digitorum communis, and extensor pollicis longus, causing wrist drop that places the intact finger flexors at mechanical disadvantage (active insufficiency). A dynamic radial nerve palsy orthosis stabilizes the wrist in 20–30° extension and uses low-profile elastic slings/tenodesis linkage to assist MCP extension and thumb radial abduction while allowing active IP/MCP flexion for functional pinch and grasp.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00336","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Neurological Conditions","topic":"Unilateral Spatial Neglect","q":"A 65-year-old woman with a right parietal lobe infarction eats food only from the right half of her plate, shaves/grooms only the right side of her face, and fails to turn her head leftward even when reminded, though visual confrontation testing shows intact peripheral fields when cued. Which combined occupational therapy intervention directly addresses this deficit?","o":["Placing all meal items permanently on the extreme right side so she never needs to cross midline","Complete patching of the right eye permanently to force left-side attention without scanning cues","Lighthouse visual scanning training with bright left-margin anchor cues, contralesional left-arm activation during bilateral tasks, and graded shift of items from midline toward the left","Passive stretching of the right upper extremity exclusively without visual-spatial tasks"],"a":2,"e":"Unilateral spatial neglect (most severe after right parietal lesions) is an attentional-representational deficit rather than primary visual loss (unlike homonymous hemianopsia, where patients spontaneously compensate by turning their heads). Remedial OT combines top-down visual scanning strategies (lighthouse technique, colored left-side anchors) with bottom-up contralesional limb activation and graded environmental placement toward the neglected left hemispace.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00337","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Orthopedics and Hand Rehabilitation","topic":"Rheumatoid Arthritis Joint Protection","q":"A 49-year-old woman with active rheumatoid arthritis exhibits early synovitis at the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints and mild ulnar drift. Which kitchen modification best aligns with biomechanical joint protection principles to prevent progression of ulnar deviation?","o":["Use a jar opener mounted under the cabinet or push open the lid with the flat palm, build up utensil handles, and lift pots with both palms rather than tight lateral pinch","Twist tight jar lids forcefully with the right hand using radial-to-ulnar finger torque and carry heavy kettles by the thin handle","Perform repetitive forceful putty squeezing exercises daily during acute inflammatory flares","Wring wet dishcloths tightly by twisting wrists into maximal ulnar deviation"],"a":0,"e":"During gripping and lateral key pinch, extrinsic flexor tendons pull at an angle across inflamed MCP collateral ligaments, driving volar subluxation and ulnar drift. Joint protection distributes forces across larger proximal joints (carrying with both palms/forearms), avoids tight cylindrical grip (built-up lightweight handles), and eliminates deforming ulnar-directed torques when opening jars or wringing cloths.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00338","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Pediatrics","topic":"Fine Motor Milestones","q":"Which chronological sequence accurately represents normal developmental progression of infant object prehension from 4 months to 12 months of age?","o":["Dynamic tripod grasp -\u003e Radial-palmar grasp -\u003e Ulnar-palmar grasp -\u003e Reflexive squeeze","Radial-digital grasp -\u003e Ulnar-palmar grasp -\u003e Scissor grasp -\u003e Lateral tripod grasp","Superior pincer grasp -\u003e Radial-digital grasp -\u003e Palmar squeeze -\u003e Ulnar-palmar grasp","Ulnar-palmar grasp -\u003e Radial-palmar grasp -\u003e Inferior pincer (radial-digital) grasp -\u003e Superior tip-to-tip pincer grasp"],"a":3,"e":"Prehension develops proximally-to-distally and ulnarly-to-radially: voluntary squeeze/ulnar-palmar grasp (~4–5 months) transitions to radial-palmar grasp (~6–7 months), radial-digital/inferior pincer (~8–9 months), and refined tip-to-tip superior pincer grasp using thumb and index fingertips (~10–12 months). Dynamic tripod pencil grasp matures later around 4.5–6 years.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00339","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Geriatric Occupational Therapy and Community Rehab","topic":"Low-Vision Home Safety","q":"A 76-year-old retired teacher with bilateral central scotomas from dry age-related macular degeneration (AMD) and mild postural hypotension has fallen twice at home near her staircase and bathroom. Which home safety modification plan is most evidence-based?","o":["Apply high-contrast matte tape to stair nosings, install shaded non-glare task lighting, remove loose throw rugs, and mount wall-stud-anchored grab bars beside a raised toilet","Place a freestanding plastic suction-cup grab bar onto dry glazed tiles and add plush loose runners","Wax hardwood floors to a high gloss to reflect ambient light and add decorative dim colored nightlights","Install bright unshielded bare halogen spotlights directly facing her eye level"],"a":0,"e":"Central vision loss in AMD impairs contrast sensitivity and depth perception, making glare-free high-contrast visual cues (matte contrasting strips on step edges, contrasting bathroom fixtures) and shaded task lighting essential. Eliminating trip hazards (throw rugs) and anchoring grab bars securely into structural wall studs (never suction cups) reduces fall incidence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00340","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Neurological Conditions","topic":"Compensatory Swallowing Postures","q":"A 61-year-old man recovering from a left lateral medullary (Wallenberg) stroke demonstrates unilateral left pharyngeal wall paresis with post-swallow residue pooling in the left pyriform sinus on videofluoroscopy. Which compensatory head posture should the occupational/dysphagia therapist teach him to direct the food bolus safely down the intact right side?","o":["Tilt the head laterally toward the affected LEFT side with chin elevated","Rotate (turn) the head toward the affected LEFT side while swallowing","Rotate the head toward the intact RIGHT side while extending the neck","Hyperextend the neck backward to let gravity drain the left pyriform sinus"],"a":1,"e":"Turning (rotating) the head toward the WEAK/affected pharyngeal side mechanically compresses and closes off the paretic pyriform sinus and reduces upper esophageal sphincter resting pressure on the contralateral side, effectively routing the bolus down the stronger intact right pharyngeal channel. Conversely, tilting (ear-to-shoulder) is directed toward the stronger side to use gravity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00341","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Assistive Technology and Seating","topic":"Anthropometric Seating Measurements","q":"During a wheelchair seating evaluation, a paraplegic adult client measured in sitting with typical clothing has a widest hip/trochanter breadth of 15.5 inches (39.4 cm) and a posterior-buttocks-to-popliteal-fossa length of 19.5 inches (49.5 cm). Using standard occupational therapy clinical guidelines (add 1–2 inches for width clearance; subtract 2 inches from buttock-popliteal length for seat depth), what standard manual wheelchair seat width and seat depth should be ordered?","o":["15.5 inches width × 19.5 inches depth","14 inches width × 21 inches depth","17–18 inches width × 17.5 inches (or 17–18 inches) depth","20 inches width × 15 inches depth"],"a":2,"e":"Standard wheelchair sizing adds 1–2 inches total (0.5–1 inch per side) to widest hip/thigh breadth to prevent greater trochanter pressure sores while maintaining optimal push-rim reach: 15.5 + 1.5 to 2 = 17–18 inches width. Seat depth subtracts 2 inches (2–3 fingerbreadths) from posterior buttocks-to-popliteal fossa length to prevent popliteal vessel/nerve compression and posterior pelvic tilt: 19.5 − 2 = 17.5 inches (rounded to standard 17 or 18 in).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00342","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Orthopedics and Hand Rehabilitation","topic":"Zone II Flexor Tendon Repair","q":"A 29-year-old carpenter undergoes primary surgical repair of lacerated flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) and superficialis (FDS) tendons in Zone II (\u0027no man\u0027s land\u0027) of the right index and middle fingers. During the first 3 weeks postoperatively using a modified Duran or Kleinert protocol, how must the dorsal blocking orthosis position the hand?","o":["Unrestricted buddy taping with immediate resistive putty squeezing starting on postoperative day 3","Wrist in neutral, MCP joints in 20 degrees hyperextension, and thumb immobilized in adduction","Wrist in 45 degrees extension, MCP joints in 0 degrees extension, and IP joints in 90 degrees flexion","Wrist in 20–30 degrees flexion, MCP joints in 50–70 degrees flexion, and IP joints in full 0 degrees extension"],"a":3,"e":"Zone II flexor tendon repairs pass through the tight fibro-osseous digital sheath and are vulnerable to both adhesion formation and tendon rupture. A dorsal blocking splint holding the wrist in 20–30° flexion, MCPs in 50–70° flexion, and IPs in 0° extension slackens the repaired flexors while preventing PIP/DIP flexion contractures, allowing controlled passive flexion (Duran/Kleinert) and active extension within the splint.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00343","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Neurological Conditions","topic":"Parkinson\u0027s Disease Rehabilitation","q":"A 68-year-old man with Hoehn and Yahr Stage III Parkinson\u0027s disease experiences severe freezing when stepping across his bathroom doorway and writes in progressively smaller handwriting (micrographia). Which occupational therapy strategy bypasses the dysfunctional basal ganglia internal cueing loop?","o":["Use rhythmic auditory metronome beats or high-contrast horizontal floor tape strips at doorways, and provide lined paper with large-amplitude visual target cues","Rest in bed until rigidity resolves spontaneously without structured cueing","Perform rapid dual-task mental arithmetic while turning sharply in cramped hallways","Use low-contrast beige floor mats and unlined pocket notepads"],"a":0,"e":"In Parkinson\u0027s disease, basal ganglia degeneration impairs automatic internal movement generation and amplitude scaling (causing hypokinesia, freezing, and micrographia). External visual cues (bright parallel tape strips perpendicular to walking path) and rhythmic auditory cues (metronome/marching beats) engage intact premotor-parietal-cerebellar pathways to initiate stepping and maintain stride/letter amplitude.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00344","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Pediatrics","topic":"Postural Management in Spastic Quadriplegia","q":"A 5-year-old child with GMFCS Level IV spastic quadriplegic cerebral palsy exhibits strong whole-body extensor thrust, sacral sitting, and lower-extremity scissoring whenever placed in a standard 90-degree upright chair, preventing upper-limb reach for play. Which adaptive seating configuration best inhibits extensor spasticity and stabilizes the pelvis?","o":["A reclined 135-degree backrest with a horizontal lap belt across the soft abdomen and zero footrest support","A 45-degree-angled pelvic belt across the anterior superior iliac spines (ASIS), slight hip flexion (slightly \u003c90° seat-to-back or wedged cushion) with slight tilt-in-space, a medial knee abductor pommel, and lateral trunk supports","A rigid posterior neck brace without pelvic stabilization","Seat tilted forward 15 degrees downward with ankles plantarflexed off the floor"],"a":1,"e":"Proximal pelvic stabilization is the foundation of pediatric neurodevelopmental seating. Anchoring the pelvis with a padded belt angled at 45° over the ASIS (never high on the abdomen) combined with slight hip flexion (often 85–90° or slight anti-thrust wedge), firm footrests, and an abduction pommel breaks up the total extensor/scissoring pattern, freeing the upper extremities for functional reach.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00345","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Orthopedics and Hand Rehabilitation","topic":"Carpal Tunnel Syndrome","q":"When fabricating a volar wrist immobilization orthosis for a data-entry operator with mild-to-moderate Carpal Tunnel Syndrome, at what angle should the wrist be positioned to minimize hydrostatic pressure inside the carpal tunnel?","o":["30 degrees of ulnar deviation with 20 degrees flexion","35 degrees of wrist flexion","Neutral position (0 to 5 degrees of wrist extension)","45 degrees of wrist extension"],"a":2,"e":"Cadaveric and in-vivo manometry studies demonstrate that intracarpal canal pressure is lowest when the wrist is held in neutral alignment (0–5° extension). Both wrist flexion (which compresses the median nerve against the transverse carpal ligament) and pronounced extension (\u003e15–30°) significantly elevate canal pressure and worsen nocturnal paresthesias.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00346","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Oncology and Palliative Occupational Therapy","topic":"Safe ADL Biomechanics in Bone Metastases","q":"A 60-year-old woman with breast cancer metastatic to the T10–L1 vertebral bodies and severe cancer-related fatigue wants to remain independent in dressing her lower body and bathing at home. Which occupational therapy recommendation minimizes pathological vertebral fracture risk?","o":["Prescribe maximal-resistance progressive spinal flexion exercises","Encourage twisting laterally while lifting heavy bath buckets from the floor","Teach vigorous sit-ups and forward toe-touching stretches before putting on trousers","Provide a long-handled reacher, sock aid, elastic shoelaces, long-handled bath sponge, and shower chair to eliminate trunk flexion and twisting, paired with energy-pacing rests"],"a":3,"e":"Vertebral metastases carry high risk of pathological compression fracture under axial-flexion or torsional loads. Providing long-handled adaptive equipment (reacher, sock aid, bath sponge) and seated showering maintains neutral spinal alignment without bending or twisting, while paced activity preserves energy for valued roles.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00347","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Pediatrics","topic":"Beery VMI Supplemental Subtests","q":"A 7-year-old second-grader referred for illegible handwriting scores well below average on the Beery-Buktenica Developmental Test of Visual-Motor Integration (VMI) and the Motor Coordination supplemental subtest, but scores within the normal average range on the Visual Perception (motor-free) supplemental subtest. What does this profile indicate?","o":["Intact visual-spatial perception with impaired fine-motor control/graphomotor execution affecting visual-motor integration","Generalized intellectual disability invalidating all subtests","Primary visual acuity/perceptual discrimination deficit with superior motor dexterity","Exclusive auditory processing disorder"],"a":0,"e":"The Beery VMI pairs a copying test with two supplemental subtests—Visual Perception (motor-free matching) and Motor Coordination (tracing inside borders)—to isolate the bottleneck. Average Visual Perception paired with low Motor Coordination and low VMI proves the child accurately perceives geometric forms but struggles with fine-motor planning/pencil execution, guiding OT toward distal hand strengthening, grasp ergonomics, and motor-control practice.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00348","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy Assessment and Outcome Measures","topic":"Jamar Grip Dynamometry","q":"Using a calibrated Jamar hydraulic hand dynamometer at standardized Handle Position 2 (elbow flexed 90°, forearm neutral), a right-hand-dominant worker recovering from left distal radius fracture records three trials on his uninjured right hand: 41 kg, 43 kg, 42 kg; and three trials on his injured left hand: 24 kg, 26 kg, 25 kg. What is his mean left grip strength and its percentage deficit compared with his uninjured right hand?","o":["Mean left = 25.0 kg; 59.5% deficit relative to the right hand","Mean left = 25.0 kg; 40.5% deficit relative to the right hand","Mean left = 26.0 kg; 68.0% deficit relative to the right hand","Mean left = 42.0 kg; 17.0% deficit relative to the right hand"],"a":1,"e":"Mean right grip = (41 + 43 + 42) / 3 = 42.0 kg. Mean left grip = (24 + 26 + 25) / 3 = 25.0 kg. Percentage grip deficit = [(42.0 − 25.0) / 42.0] × 100 = (17.0 / 42.0) × 100 = 40.48% (~40.5% deficit; note that even accounting for ~10% expected dominant-hand advantage, a \u003e40% gap represents substantial impairment).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00349","course":"BOT - Occupational Therapy","subject":"Occupational Therapy in Orthopedics and Hand Rehabilitation","topic":"Dellon\u0027s Sensory Re-education Protocol","q":"Match each hand sensory assessment or Dellon sensory re-education phase with its clinical purpose: (1) Semmes-Weinstein Monofilament Test, (2) Static vs Moving Two-Point Discrimination, (3) Early-phase Dellon re-education, (4) Late-phase Dellon re-education. Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Measures cortical innervation density; 2-Quantifies touch-pressure threshold; 3-Stereognosis with eyes closed; 4-Constant vs moving touch","1-Quantifies touch-pressure threshold; 2-Stereognosis with eyes closed; 3-Measures cortical innervation density; 4-Constant vs moving touch","1-Quantifies cutaneous touch-pressure threshold using calibrated nylon filaments; 2-Measures cortical innervation density of slowly vs rapidly adapting receptors; 3-Discriminating constant vs moving touch (eraser) with vision open then occluded; 4-Tactile object/texture recognition (stereognosis) with eyes closed","1-Tactile stereognosis; 2-Touch-pressure threshold; 3-Pinprick temperature only; 4-Vibratory tuning fork"],"a":2,"e":"Following median/ulnar nerve repair, recovery progresses from protective pain/temperature to 30 Hz vibration/moving touch (Meissner corpuscles), constant touch (Merkel discs), and finally 256 Hz vibration/stereognosis. Semmes-Weinstein monofilaments grade threshold sensitivity; Two-Point Discrimination grades functional innervation density; Dellon\u0027s protocol trains moving/constant touch localization (vision-\u003eoccluded-\u003evision) in the early phase before advancing to texture/object stereognosis in the late phase.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00350","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Spinal Orthotics","topic":"TLSO (Boston) vs CTLSO (Milwaukee)","q":"A 12-year-old girl with Adolescent Idiopathic Scoliosis (Risser sign 1) has a progressive right thoracic curve measuring 32 degrees (Cobb angle) with its apex at T9. Which spinal orthosis is indicated, and what mechanical principle corrects the coronal curve?","o":["Jewett hyperextension orthosis; four-point sagittal traction","Soft lumbosacral corset; abdominal compression only","Cervicothoracolumbosacral orthosis (Milwaukee CTLSO) is strictly mandatory because the apex is below T7","Thoracolumbosacral orthosis (TLSO, e.g., Boston brace); three-point pressure system with lateral thoracic pad over the convex apex"],"a":3,"e":"For skeletally immature patients (Risser 0-2) with progressive curves of 25-40 degrees whose apex is at or below T7/T8, an underarm TLSO (such as a Boston brace) is preferred over a Milwaukee CTLSO for equivalent efficacy and higher compliance. It applies a three-point corrective force system (lateral pad over the convex rib/apex countered by opposite superior axillary/trochanteric forces) alongside lumbar lordosis reduction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00351","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Limb Orthotics","topic":"Sagittal Ankle Alignment \u0026 Knee Moment Coupling","q":"A patient post-poliomyelitis with severe quadriceps weakness (grade 2/5) and intact hip extensors experiences sudden knee buckling during early-to-mid stance. How should a rigid Solid AFO or Ground-Reaction AFO (GRAFO) ankle angle be aligned in the sagittal plane to mechanically stabilize the knee?","o":["Align the AFO in slight plantarflexion (approx. 3-5 degrees) with a rigid anterior shell/dorsiflexion stop to shift the ground reaction force anterior to the knee, generating an external knee extension moment","Use a flexible posterior leaf spring (PLS) AFO trimmed posterior to the malleoli","Provide a free-motion ankle joint with no plantarflexion or dorsiflexion stops","Align the AFO in 10 degrees dorsiflexion to create a knee flexion moment"],"a":0,"e":"Setting a rigid Solid AFO or GRAFO in 3-5 degrees of plantarflexion (and blocking tibial advancement over the foot via an anterior tibial shell/dorsiflexion stop) forces the Ground Reaction Force (GRF) vector anterior to the knee joint center during mid-stance, generating a stabilizing external knee extension moment that prevents knee buckling in quadriceps weakness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00352","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Pediatric Orthotics","topic":"Pavlik Harness for Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH)","q":"A 6-week-old infant with Barlow-positive reducible Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) is fitted with a Pavlik harness. What hip joint angles define the safe \u0027human position,\u0027 and what iatrogenic complications occur if the harness forces extreme abduction (\u003e 60-70 degrees) or excessive flexion (\u003e 120 degrees)?","o":["140 degrees flexion and 85 degrees abduction; optimal safe frog-leg posture","90-100 degrees hip flexion and 40-50 degrees abduction; forced extreme abduction risks avascular necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head, while excessive flexion risks femoral nerve palsy","15 deg flexion, 10 deg adduction; risk of sciatic nerve palsy","Full hip extension (0 deg) and internal rotation; risk of tibial torsion"],"a":1,"e":"The Pavlik harness dynamically centers the femoral head in the acetabulum by placing the hips in the \u0027human position\u0027 (90-100 degrees flexion, 40-50 degrees gentle abduction within Ramsey\u0027s safe zone). Forcing wide \u0027frog-leg\u0027 abduction (\u003e 60-70 deg) kinks the medial circumflex femoral vessels causing femoral head AVN, whereas excessive hyperflexion (\u003e 120 deg) compresses the femoral nerve beneath the inguinal ligament (loss of active knee extension).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00353","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Diabetic Foot \u0026 Pedorthics","topic":"Total Contact Cast (TCC) \u0026 Rocker-Bottom Soles","q":"Why is the Total Contact Cast (TCC) considered the gold-standard offloading intervention for an uninfected, non-ischemic neuropathic plantar forefoot ulcer in diabetes mellitus, and where must the apex of a rigid rocker-bottom shoe modification be placed?","o":["TCC concentrates weight exclusively onto the heel; rocker apex placed directly beneath the toes","TCC increases plantar shear over the ulcer to stimulate granulation; rocker apex placed at the calcaneus","TCC distributes plantar weight evenly and transfers ~30% of load onto the conical tibial shaft walls while immobilizing the ankle; rigid rocker-bottom apex is placed just PROXIMAL (posterior) to the metatarsal heads","TCC applies localized circular donut padding around the first metatarsal head without touching the leg"],"a":2,"e":"A Total Contact Cast uses minimal padding molded intimately to every plantar and lower-leg contour, eliminating focal shear/peak pressures over metatarsal heads and transferring ~30% of vertical load onto the conical tibial walls (plus ensuring 100% patient adherence). For healed-ulcer footwear, placing a rigid rocker-sole pivot apex just proximal to the metatarsal heads allows smooth rollover without MTP dorsiflexion or peak forefoot pressure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00354","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Rehabilitation Engineering \u0026 Wheelchair Seating","topic":"Anthropometric Wheelchair Sizing","q":"A rehab engineer measures an adult paraplegic patient in seated posture for a custom manual wheelchair: widest hip/pelvic breadth = 16 inches, posterior buttock-to-popliteal fossa length = 18 inches, and popliteal fossa-to-heel length (with shoes) = 17 inches. Applying standard 2-inch clinical clearance rules, what are the prescribed Seat Width, Seat Depth, and Seat-to-Floor Height (maintaining 2 inches footplate-to-floor clearance)?","o":["Seat Width = 20 in; Seat Depth = 14 in; Seat-to-Floor Height = 21 in","Seat Width = 14 in; Seat Depth = 20 in; Seat-to-Floor Height = 15 in","Seat Width = 16 in; Seat Depth = 18 in; Seat-to-Floor Height = 17 in","Seat Width = 18 in; Seat Depth = 16 in; Seat-to-Floor Height = 19 in"],"a":3,"e":"Standard adult wheelchair sizing rules: (1) Seat Width = widest hip/thigh breadth + 2 inches (~1 inch clearance per side) = 16 + 2 = 18 inches; (2) Seat Depth = posterior buttock-to-popliteal length minus 2 inches (preventing popliteal vessel/nerve compression and posterior pelvic tilt) = 18 - 2 = 16 inches; (3) Seat-to-Floor Height = popliteal-to-heel length (17 in) + 2 inches footplate ground clearance = 19 inches.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00355","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Upper Extremity Orthotics","topic":"Zone II Flexor Tendon Repair Dorsal Blocking Orthosis","q":"Following surgical repair of a Zone II flexor digitorum profundus tendon laceration, the orthotist fabricates a protective Dorsal Blocking Orthosis (modified Kleinert/Duran protocol). Which joint angles must this dorsal hood orthosis maintain to unload tensile stress on the repaired tendon while preventing PIP flexion contractures?","o":["Wrist 20-30 degrees flexion (or neutral in newer protocols), MCPs 50-70 degrees flexion, and IPs allowed full active/passive extension (0 degrees) within the dorsal hood","Wrist 45 degrees extension, MCPs 90 degrees hyperextension, IPs 45 degrees flexion","Complete immobilization of wrist and digits in a volar plaster cast for 12 weeks","Wrist 30 degrees extension, MCPs 0 degrees extension, IPs 90 degrees flexion"],"a":0,"e":"A dorsal blocking orthosis places the wrist in slight flexion (20-30 deg, or neutral-to-10 deg in modern early active motion protocols) and the MCP joints in 50-70 deg flexion to slacken the extrinsic flexor tendons and physically block active or passive hyperextension that could rupture the Zone II repair, while keeping IP joints straight (0 deg) during rest to prevent volar plate/PIP flexion contractures.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00356","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Cervical \u0026 Spinal Orthotics","topic":"Halo-Thoracic Vest Orthosis vs Rigid Collars","q":"A 30-year-old trauma patient sustains a displaced Type II odontoid / unstable Jefferson C1-C2 burst fracture managed non-operatively with skeletal traction reduction. Which spinal orthosis provides the HIGHEST degree of triplanar motion restriction (including axial rotation and flexion-extension) specifically at the atlantoaxial (C1-C2) junction?","o":["Sterno-Occipital-Mandibular Immobilizer (SOMI) brace","Halo-Thoracic Vest Orthosis (CTLO with cranial pins fixed to thoracic vest)","Philadelphia or Miami-J rigid two-piece cervical collar","Soft foam cervical collar"],"a":1,"e":"Because ~50% of cervical axial rotation occurs at C1-C2 and standard collars allow significant upper cervical \u0027snaking\u0027 motion, unstable C1-C2 fractures require skeletal cranial fixation. A Halo-Thoracic Vest Orthosis (fixing 4 cranial pins to the outer skull table anchored by uprights to a molded thoracic vest) restricts ~90-95% of flexion-extension and axial rotation at C1-C2—far superior to SOMI or Miami-J collars.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00357","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Foot Orthotics \u0026 Pedorthic Biomechanics","topic":"Metatarsalgia \u0026 Metatarsal Pad Placement","q":"A 52-year-old woman with plantar callosities and sharp pain directly beneath the 2nd and 3rd metatarsal heads (metatarsalgia) is prescribed a custom foot orthosis with a teardrop-shaped metatarsal pad. Where must the apex/bulk of the metatarsal pad be positioned inside the shoe/orthosis to offload the painful metatarsal heads?","o":["Under the medial calcaneal tubercle","Directly underneath the painful 2nd and 3rd metatarsal heads","Just PROXIMAL (posterior) to the 2nd-4th metatarsal heads (supporting the metatarsal necks/shafts to elevate and unload the metatarsal heads during stance)","Distal to the metatarsal heads directly beneath the proximal phalanges of the toes"],"a":2,"e":"A metatarsal pad works by transferring weight onto the less-sensitive metatarsal shafts/necks and restoring the transverse metatarsal arch. Positioning the high contour just proximal (posterior) to the metatarsal heads elevates and splays the heads upward away from the ground—whereas placing the pad directly beneath the tender metatarsal heads concentrates pressure and worsens metatarsalgia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00358","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Thoracolumbosacral Orthotics (TLSO)","topic":"Jewett / CASH Hyperextension Orthosis","q":"A 66-year-old woman sustains an osteoporotic, stable anterior wedge compression fracture at T12 (\u003c 25% height loss, intact posterior column). Which spinal orthosis uses an anterior sternal pad, anterior suprapubic pad, and posterior thoracolumbar pad to restrict thoracic/lumbar FLEXION, and in which fracture type is it contraindicated?","o":["Milwaukee CTLSO; contraindicated in thoracic scoliosis","Boston TLSO; contraindicated in lumbar strain","Soft elastic abdominal binder; contraindicated in pregnancy","Jewett (or CASH) hyperextension TLSO; contraindicated in unstable 3-column burst fractures or posterior-element disruption (where hyperextension risks retropulsion/cord compression)"],"a":3,"e":"The Jewett and Crucial Anterior Spinal Hyperextension (CASH) orthoses utilize a sagittal three-point force system—anterior sternal + suprapubic pads countered by a single posterior thoracolumbar pad—to prevent spinal flexion and unload the fractured anterior vertebral body in stable T10-L2 wedge compression fractures. Because they permit extension and offer poor rotational/three-column control, they are contraindicated in unstable burst fractures or posterior-element injuries (Chance fractures).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00359","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Neurological Lower Limb Orthotics","topic":"Hemiparetic Gait \u0026 Plantarflexion Stop","q":"A 55-year-old post-stroke community ambulator has swing-phase foot drop and mild plantarflexor spasticity, yet retains full passive ankle dorsiflexion (0 to +12 degrees), adequate quadriceps strength (4/5), and needs to ascend stairs and rise smoothly from low chairs. Why is an Articulated (Hinged) AFO with a 90-degree plantarflexion stop and FREE dorsiflexion superior to a rigid Solid AFO locked at 90 degrees?","o":["The 90-degree plantarflexion stop prevents swing-phase equinus foot drop, while FREE dorsiflexion permits natural forward tibial progression during mid-stance, sit-to-stand transitions, and stair ascent without forcing abnormal knee hyperextension","A rigid Solid AFO permits 30 degrees of active ankle dorsiflexion during stairs","Free dorsiflexion locks the knee in full hyperextension throughout stance","The articulated AFO allows unrestricted plantarflexion during swing so the toes drop"],"a":0,"e":"When a patient possesses \u003e= 5-10 degrees passive ankle dorsiflexion and adequate quadriceps control (no severe knee buckling), an articulated AFO with a 90-degree posterior plantarflexion stop prevents foot drop in swing and foot slap at heel strike while allowing free tibial advancement (dorsiflexion) over the stationary foot during second rocker, stair climbing, and standing up from a chair—avoiding the stiff knee-extension thrust caused by a rigid solid AFO.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00360","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Pediatric Orthotics","topic":"Ponseti Foot Abduction Brace (Steenbeek / Denis Browne)","q":"A 3-month-old infant with right idiopathic clubfoot (CTEV) has completed 5 serial Ponseti casts and percutaneous Achilles tenotomy, achieving full correction. Upon removing the final cast, the orthotist fits a bilateral Foot Abduction Brace (Steenbeek/Mitchell Ponseti brace). What exact abduction (external rotation) and dorsiflexion angles must be set for the corrected right foot, and what is the wearing schedule?","o":["90 degrees inversion and equinus; worn 1 hour daily","60-70 degrees external rotation (abduction) and 10-15 degrees dorsiflexion on the corrected clubfoot (30-40 deg external rotation on the normal foot); worn 23 hours/day for the first 3 months, then nights and naps until age 4-5 years","0 degrees neutral alignment; worn only while walking outdoors","30 degrees internal rotation, 20 degrees plantarflexion; worn 4 hours/day for 2 weeks"],"a":1,"e":"Non-adherence to Foot Abduction Bracing is the single greatest cause of clubfoot relapse (\u003e 80% relapse without bracing vs \u003c 6% with strict bracing). Ponseti protocol mandates setting the shoulder-width bar so the corrected clubfoot sits in 60-70 deg external rotation (abduction) and 10-15 deg dorsiflexion (normal contralateral foot at 30-40 deg external rotation), worn 23 h/day for 3 months and during naps/nighttime (~12-14 h/day) until age 4-5 years.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00361","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Knee Orthotics (KO)","topic":"Valgus Unloader Knee Brace","q":"A 58-year-old patient with painful isolated MEDIAL compartment knee osteoarthritis and mild genu varum (\u0027bow-leg\u0027) is prescribed a custom Valgus Unloader Knee Orthosis. Where are the three corrective pressure points applied by this orthosis to offload the medial tibiofemoral compartment?","o":["Lateral thigh, lateral calf, and a medial knee push forcing the knee into varus","Posterior popliteal pressure only to flex the knee 45 degrees","Lateral force (push/strap) directed medially at the lateral knee joint axis, countered by medial opposing forces on the proximal thigh and distal calf to generate an external VALGUS moment that opens the medial compartment","Pure anterior patellar pressure with zero mediolateral uprights"],"a":2,"e":"In medial compartment knee OA with varus alignment, excessive medial joint loading accelerates cartilage wear. A Valgus Unloader Brace applies a three-point bending system: a medially directed force at the lateral knee (or diagonal strap pull) countered by laterally directed forces at the medial proximal thigh and medial distal calf—creating a corrective valgus torque that shifts compressive load onto the preserved lateral compartment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00362","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Pediatric Orthotics","topic":"Clubfoot (CTEV) Post-Ponseti Bracing","q":"A 4-month-old infant with unilateral right Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (CTEV) has completed Ponseti serial casting and percutaneous Achilles tenotomy. What are the correct settings and initial wear schedule for the Foot Abduction Orthosis (Denis Browne / Steenbeek brace)?","o":["Affected right foot in 60–70 degrees external rotation, unaffected left foot in 30–40 degrees external rotation, 10–15 degrees dorsiflexion, bar width equal to shoulder width, worn 23 hours/day for 3 months then nights/naps until age 4–5 years","Both feet in 30 degrees internal rotation and 20 degrees plantarflexion, worn 8 hours/day for 6 weeks","Affected right foot in 0 degrees neutral rotation, unaffected foot unbraced, worn only during walking","Affected foot in 90 degrees external rotation with 45 degrees plantarflexion, worn 23 hours/day for 1 month only"],"a":0,"e":"Non-adherence to post-tenotomy abduction bracing is the single leading cause of Ponseti clubfoot relapse. Standard Ponseti protocol sets the clubfoot shoe at 60–70° external rotation (and the normal foot at 30–40° external rotation) with 10–15° dorsiflexion on a bar matching the infant\u0027s shoulder width, worn 23 hours/day for 3 months followed by nighttime/nap wear until 4–5 years of age.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00363","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Diabetic Foot Care and Pedorthics","topic":"Total Contact Casting and Therapeutic Footwear","q":"A 58-year-old man with long-standing diabetic peripheral neuropathy and intact pedal pulses presents with a non-infected 2 cm plantar neuropathic ulcer beneath the 2nd metatarsal head and mild midfoot swelling. Which offloading modality represents the gold standard for rapid ulcer healing, and what definitive footwear features are required post-healing?","o":["Unmodified canvas running shoes with a rigid high-heeled arch support","Standard open-toed bathroom slippers initially; barefoot walking indoors post-healing","Elastic compression stocking alone with a forefoot metatarsal cutout pad","Total Contact Cast (TCC) initially; post-healing extra-depth shoes with custom multi-density total-contact insert and rigid rocker-bottom sole"],"a":3,"e":"Total Contact Casting (TCC) is the gold-standard offloading intervention for non-infected, non-ischemic plantar neuropathic ulcers because it distributes plantar pressure uniformly, transfers axial load onto the lower leg walls, reduces shear, and enforces 100% adherence. After healing (or after acute Charcot stabilization in a TCC/CROW), extra-depth footwear with custom soft-top/firm-base total-contact inserts and a rigid rocker sole minimizes forefoot peak pressures.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00364","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Biomechanical Principles of Orthotics","topic":"Spinal Hyperextension Orthoses","q":"A patient with a stable T12 anterior wedge compression fracture is fitted with a Jewett hyperextension TLSO. How do the three pressure pads apply forces to restrict thoracic/lumbar spinal flexion?","o":["Two posteriorly directed forces at the sternum and pubic symphysis opposed by one anteriorly directed force at the mid-thoracolumbar spine","One superior traction force at the occiput balanced by two lateral iliac crest forces","Two anteriorly directed forces at the sternum and pubis opposed by one posteriorly directed force at L5","Three circumferentially equal inward forces centered at the umbilicus"],"a":0,"e":"The Jewett (and CASH) hyperextension orthosis uses a classic sagittal three-point pressure system: an anterior sternal pad and an anterior suprapubic pad exert posteriorly directed forces at the proximal and distal ends, while a posterior thoracolumbar pad exerts an equal and opposite anteriorly directed corrective force near the fracture apex—preventing anterior vertebral body compression during flexion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00365","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Advanced Prosthetic Gait Analysis","topic":"Swing and Stance Phase Compensations","q":"A unilateral transfemoral amputee rises onto the toes of his sound-side foot (vaulting) during mid-swing of the prosthetic limb. Which set of prosthetic causes should the prosthetist inspect first?","o":["Socket has an overly soft anterior wall causing medial whip","Prosthesis is too long, excessive knee friction preventing adequate swing-phase knee flexion, or inadequate suspension causing distal pistoning","Prosthesis has excessive toe-out alignment or excessive ankle dorsiflexion","Prosthesis is 3 cm too short, weak contralateral hip flexors, or medial wall too low"],"a":1,"e":"Vaulting (excessive plantarflexion of the sound stance ankle to elevate the pelvis) is a compensatory maneuver to achieve toe clearance for the swinging prosthetic foot. Common prosthetic causes include excessive overall prosthetic length, high knee friction/stiff extension aid that delays knee flexion, inadequate suspension allowing the prosthesis to drop distally in swing, or a plantarflexed foot.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00366","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Limb Prosthetics - Partial Foot and Disarticulations","topic":"Syme Prosthesis Design","q":"What unique anatomical feature of a classical Syme ankle disarticulation residual limb dictates the need for a removable medial/posterior socket window or expandable inner liner during prosthetic socket fabrication?","o":["The flared distal tibial/malleolar epiphysis is wider than the narrow supramalleolar ankle waist above it","Complete absence of any distal end-bearing tolerance requiring ischial weight bearing","Excessive residual limb length requiring a polycentric mechanical knee unit","Severe proximal tibial bowing preventing total-contact lamination"],"a":0,"e":"Because a Syme disarticulation preserves the broad distal tibial flare/heel pad (allowing true distal end-bearing), the distal circumference exceeds the narrow supramalleolar region. A rigid single-piece socket cannot pass over the bulbous end without a removable medial/posterior door (window), a flexible inner expandable wall, or a split-socket design, which simultaneously provides self-suspension.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00367","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Orthotic Joint Systems and Indications","topic":"Specialized Lower Limb Orthoses","q":"Match each orthotic configuration with its classic clinical indication: (1) Posterior Leaf Spring (PLS) AFO, (2) Double-Action Ankle Joint, (3) Craig-Scott KAFO, (4) Patellar Tendon Bearing (PTB) weight-relieving orthosis. Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Isolated mild-to-moderate dorsiflexor weakness (foot drop); 2-Adjustable independent control of both plantarflexion and dorsiflexion via pins/springs; 3-Paraplegia (T12-L1) standing and swing-through ambulation; 4-Axial unloading of distal tibial fracture non-union or calcaneal fracture","1-T12-L1 paraplegia standing; 2-Isolated dorsiflexor weakness; 3-Axial unloading of distal tibia; 4-Adjustable DF/PF pins/springs","1-Axial unloading of distal tibia; 2-Paraplegia standing; 3-Isolated foot drop; 4-Adjustable DF/PF pins/springs","1-Isolated dorsiflexor weakness; 2-Axial unloading of distal tibia; 3-Adjustable DF/PF pins/springs; 4-T12-L1 paraplegia standing"],"a":0,"e":"A flexible Posterior Leaf Spring AFO assists toe clearance in isolated flaccid foot drop without severe spasticity or mediolateral instability. Double-action ankle joints house anterior and posterior channels for pins or springs to separately limit or assist DF and PF. The Craig-Scott KAFO uses bail/pawl-lock knees and solid ankles fixed in slight dorsiflexion for C-posture balance in T12–L1 paraplegia. A PTB orthosis transfers axial load through a patellar-tendon brim to a rigid stirrup, unloading the distal tibia/calcaneus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00368","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Orthotic Engineering Mechanics","topic":"Three-Point Knee-Extension Strap Forces","q":"A Knee-Ankle-Foot Orthosis (KAFO) uses a three-point pressure system in the sagittal plane to resist knee flexion. The central anterior knee pad applies a posteriorly directed force of 120 N directly at the knee axis. The proximal posterior thigh band sits 25 cm (0.25 m) above the knee pad, and the distal posterior calf band sits 15 cm (0.15 m) below the knee pad. Under static equilibrium, what are the exact anteriorly directed reaction forces at the thigh band (F_thigh) and calf band (F_calf)?","o":["F_thigh = 75 N, F_calf = 45 N","F_thigh = 60 N, F_calf = 60 N","F_thigh = 45 N, F_calf = 75 N","F_thigh = 30 N, F_calf = 90 N"],"a":2,"e":"Static equilibrium requires both force balance (F_thigh + F_calf = 120 N) and moment balance about the central knee pad (F_thigh × 0.25 m = F_calf × 0.15 m). Thus F_thigh = (0.15 / 0.25) × F_calf = 0.6 × F_calf. Substituting into force balance: 0.6 F_calf + F_calf = 120 N -\u003e 1.6 F_calf = 120 N -\u003e F_calf = 75 N, and F_thigh = 120 − 75 = 45 N. Notice the shorter distal lever arm bears the larger reaction force.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00369","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Pediatric \u0026 Neuromuscular Orthotics","topic":"Reciprocating Gait Orthosis (RGO)","q":"A 6-year-old child with L1 myelomeningocele (spina bifida) has Grade 4/5 iliopsoas hip flexion bilaterally, absent quadriceps/hip extensors, and wants to ambulate with a reciprocal step-over-step gait rather than a swing-through crutch gait. How does a Reciprocating Gait Orthosis (RGO) mechanically advance the child forward?","o":["Locks both hips at 90 degrees flexion permanently so the child hops with both feet together","Couples bilateral KAFOs to a pelvic thoracic section via dual Bowden cables or a posterior pivot bar so that active flexion of one hip mechanically forces simultaneous EXTENSION of the contralateral stance hip, preventing jack-knifing and generating reciprocal steps","Replaces the child\u0027s shoes with spring-loaded roller skates","Operates solely using functional electrical stimulation without mechanical hip joints"],"a":1,"e":"In thoracic or high-lumbar (T12-L2) paraplegia/spina bifida, active hip flexors without hip extensors normally cause forward trunk collapse (\u0027jack-knifing\u0027) when attempting step-over-step gait. An RGO links left and right hip joints via dual push-pull cables (or a posterior isocentric pivot rocker bar) so flexing the swing hip transfers equal and opposite extension torque to the stance hip—enabling energy-efficient reciprocal ambulation with a walker/crutches.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00370","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Upper Extremity Orthotics","topic":"Functional Resting Position Splint","q":"When molding a volar Resting Wrist-Hand Orthosis (WHO) in the classic FUNCTIONAL POSITION for hand immobilization (to balance resting tendon tension and preserve palmar arches/web space), which joint angles are prescribed?","o":["Wrist 30 deg flexion, MCPs 0 deg hyperextension, IPs 90 deg flexion, thumb adducted flat","All joints immobilized completely flat at 0 degrees on a wooden tongue depressor","Wrist 60 deg extension, MCPs 90 deg flexion, IPs 0 deg rigid extension","Wrist 20-30 degrees extension, MCPs 45-60 degrees flexion, IPs slight 10-20 degrees flexion, and thumb in palmar abduction/opposition preserving the first web space"],"a":3,"e":"The Functional Resting WHO places the wrist in 20-30 deg extension, MCPs in 45-60 deg flexion, PIP/DIPs in gentle 10-20 deg flexion, and the thumb in relaxed palmar abduction/opposition—balancing extrinsic flexor and extensor tone comfortably while maintaining the transverse/longitudinal palmar arches and thumb web space. (Note: the slightly steeper \u0027intrinsic-plus / safe\u0027 position—wrist 20-30 deg ext, MCP 70-90 deg flex, IP 0 deg—is used specifically when maximal ligament-stretch contracture prophylaxis is needed, e.g., acute trauma/burns.)","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00371","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Pedorthics \u0026 Shoe Modifications","topic":"Internal Heel Lift vs External Shoe Buildup","q":"A patient with post-poliomyelitis residual shortening has a true structural right lower-limb shortening of 3.5 cm (measured from ASIS to medial malleolus) causing pelvic obliquity and low back pain. Applying standard pedorthic guidelines (maximum ~1.0 cm internal heel lift inside a standard shoe to prevent heel slippage), how should the total 3.5 cm shoe elevation be distributed?","o":["Place up to 1.0 cm internally inside the shoe (or none if using high-top) and add the remaining 2.5 cm EXTERNALLY to the shoe outsole (using a full-length sole-and-heel buildup tapered anteriorly toward the toe for smooth rollover)","Add 3.5 cm exclusively under the external heel with zero forefoot sole buildup","Place a 3.5 cm lift on the LONGER left shoe","Place the entire 3.5 cm heel wedge inside the standard shoe"],"a":0,"e":"Standard shoes cannot safely accommodate more than ~1.0 cm (3/8 inch) of internal heel lift without pushing the calcaneus out of the heel counter (causing blister/instability) or jamming the dorsum of the foot against the vamp. Discrepancies \u003e 1.0 cm must place the remainder (here 2.5 cm) externally on the outsole, extending smoothly beneath the forefoot (tapered at the toe rocker) rather than only under the heel—which would force the ankle into fixed equinus!","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00372","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Spinal Orthotics","topic":"Motion Restriction Hierarchy","q":"A 38-year-old trauma patient has an unstable C2 (hangman\u0027s) fracture requiring maximal three-planar immobilization (flexion-extension, lateral bending, and axial rotation) during non-operative bone healing. Which cervical orthosis provides the highest degree of multi-planar immobilization (\u003e90% restriction), and how does a SOMI brace compare?","o":["Philadelphia collar immobilizes C1–C2 rotation completely better than a Halo vest","Halo-vest CTO provides maximal multi-planar control (~90–96% restriction); a SOMI is non-invasive and controls upper-cervical flexion well (and can be applied supine) but provides minimal extension/rotation control","Soft foam cervical collar provides 95% immobilization across C1–C2","Milwaukee CTLSO is the primary emergency orthosis for unstable C2 fractures"],"a":1,"e":"Cervical immobilization follows a clear hierarchy: Soft collar (~5–15% restriction, kinesthetic reminder) \u003c Philadelphia/Miami-J (~45–65% sagittal, less rotation) \u003c SOMI (Sternal Occipital Mandibular Immobilizer — strong C1–C3 flexion block, easy supine application because it lacks posterior uprights) \u003c Halo-vest orthosis (titanium/carbon pins anchored to outer skull table connected to a thoracic vest, providing ~90–96% immobilization across all planes for unstable C1/C2 fractures).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00373","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Pediatric Orthotics","topic":"Pavlik Harness Fitting","q":"A 2-month-old infant diagnosed with reducible Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) is fitted with a Pavlik harness. What are the target hip flexion and abduction angles (\u0027human position\u0027), and what complication occurs if the posterior straps force hip abduction beyond 65–70 degrees?","o":["0 degrees neutral extension and 10 degrees internal rotation; forced extension causes scoliosis","130 degrees hip flexion and 0 degrees adduction; forced flexion causes Achilles tendon rupture","90–100 degrees hip flexion and 40–60 degrees abduction; forced abduction \u003e65–70 degrees risks avascular necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head","20 degrees hip flexion and 90 degrees abduction; forced abduction causes clubfoot"],"a":2,"e":"The Pavlik harness dynamically positions the infant\u0027s hips in ~90–100° flexion and 40–60° abduction (\u0027human position\u0027 within Ramsey\u0027s safe zone) to center the femoral head concentrically inside the acetabulum. Over-tightening posterior abduction straps beyond 65–70° compresses the medial circumflex femoral vessels, causing osteonecrosis (AVN) of the femoral capital epiphysis, while hyperflexion (\u003e110–120°) risks femoral nerve palsy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00374","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Upper Extremity Prosthetics","topic":"Surface EMG Control and Targeted Muscle Reinnervation","q":"A 27-year-old engineer with a mid-forearm transradial amputation chooses a dual-site proportional myoelectric hand. Which residual muscle groups normally drive hand opening and closing, and how does Targeted Muscle Reinnervation (TMR) enable intuitive myoelectric control for higher-level transhumeral or shoulder-disarticulation amputees?","o":["Forearm biceps opens the hand while triceps closes it; TMR injects botulinum toxin into residual nerves","Deltoid alone controls all 5 fingers simultaneously via binary on-off pressure switches","Only passive cosmetic gloves are compatible with TMR surgery","Forearm wrist/finger flexors close the hand while extensors open it (output speed/force proportional to EMG amplitude); TMR transfers severed brachial nerves onto denervated chest/arm muscles to create new, independent surface EMG signals for elbow, wrist, and hand functions"],"a":3,"e":"Standard dual-site transradial myoelectric prostheses place surface electrodes over the residual forearm flexor bellies (hand close) and extensor bellies (hand open), using proportional amplification so stronger EMG bursts produce faster motion and tighter grip. In high-level amputees lacking forearm muscles, Targeted Muscle Reinnervation (TMR) transfers residual median, ulnar, and radial nerves into surgically denervated pectoralis/serratus/biceps segments, turning native biological muscles into biological amplifiers that generate intuitive, simultaneous multi-DOF EMG signals.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00375","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Extremity Prosthetics","topic":"TKA Weight-Bearing Reference Line","q":"During sagittal bench alignment of an endoskeletal transfemoral prosthesis with a single-axis mechanical knee for a patient who demonstrates a 10-degree hip flexion contracture on the Thomas test, how should the socket flexion angle and the mechanical knee center relative to the Trochanter-Knee-Ankle (TKA) line be set?","o":["Set socket initial flexion to 15 degrees (10° contracture + 5° pre-flexion) and position the mechanical knee center 5–15 mm POSTERIOR to the TKA line","Set socket initial flexion to 0 degrees and position the knee center 20 mm ANTERIOR to the TKA line","Set socket in 10 degrees hyperextension and align the knee center directly over the toe break","Set socket flexion to 45 degrees and remove the prosthetic foot keel"],"a":0,"e":"Initial socket flexion in a transfemoral prosthesis equals the patient\u0027s hip flexion contracture (measured by Thomas test, here 10°) PLUS 5° to place the hip extensors on optimal stretch and prevent compensatory lumbar lordosis (= 15°). Placing the mechanical knee axis 5–15 mm POSTERIOR to the TKA reference line ensures the ground reaction force passes anterior to the knee axis during midstance, providing passive knee-extension stability.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00376","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Extremity Orthotics","topic":"Unicompartmental Knee Osteoarthritis","q":"A 56-year-old active shopkeeper has symptomatic isolated medial compartment knee osteoarthritis with mild varus (\u0027bow-legged\u0027) malalignment and refuses surgery. To offload the compressed medial tibiofemoral compartment using a custom valgus-unloader knee orthosis (KO), in which frontal-plane directions must the three-point forces act?","o":["A laterally directed force at the medial knee joint line opposed by medially directed thigh and calf cuffs (varus unloader)","A medially directed force at the lateral aspect of the knee joint line opposed by laterally directed forces at the medial thigh and medial calf cuffs","Circular elastic compression around the patella without mediolateral uprights","Rigid ankle plantarflexion lock with no knee upright"],"a":1,"e":"Varus malalignment overloads the medial knee compartment. A valgus-unloader KO creates an external valgus moment by pushing the lateral knee joint medially (with a lateral hinge pad or diagonal tension strap) while pulling the proximal thigh and distal calf laterally—opening and decompressing the painful medial joint space during stance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00377","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Rehabilitation Engineering and Seating","topic":"Pressure Redistribution vs Postural Stability","q":"For an insensate T6 complete paraplegic wheelchair user at very high risk of ischial tuberosity pressure injury, which cushion technology provides the greatest tissue immersion/envelopment to minimize peak interface pressure, and what key clinical trade-off must be managed?","o":["Water-filled pillow without baffle; provides rigid pelvic anti-thrust support","Standard uncontoured 1-inch open-cell urethane sponge; resists bottoming out indefinitely","Interconnected air-filled cellular cushion (e.g., ROHO); requires daily inflation checks and offers lower lateral pelvic postural stability than contoured foam/gel","Hard flat wooden board; zero maintenance and minimal interface pressure"],"a":2,"e":"Air-filled interconnected cellular cushions maximize surface contact area via deep immersion and envelopment of the ischial tuberosities and sacrum, achieving the lowest peak interface pressures for high-risk SCI clients. However, air cushions require routine hand-check inflation monitoring (to avoid bottoming out or overinflation) and provide less intrinsic lateral/anterior pelvic stability than contoured foam-gel hybrids.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00378","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Prosthetic Gait Analysis and Bioenergetics","topic":"Oxygen Cost per Meter Walked","q":"During gait bioenergetics testing, an able-bodied adult walks at 80 m/min with an oxygen consumption rate (VO2) of 12.0 mL/kg/min. A dysvascular unilateral transfemoral amputee walks with a prosthesis at a self-selected speed of 50 m/min and a VO2 of 15.0 mL/kg/min. Calculate the oxygen cost per meter walked (mL/kg/m) for each individual and the percentage increase in O2 cost per meter for the transfemoral amputee.","o":["Able-bodied = 0.15 mL/kg/m; Transfemoral = 0.19 mL/kg/m -\u003e 25% increase in O2 cost per meter","Able-bodied = 0.30 mL/kg/m; Transfemoral = 0.15 mL/kg/m -\u003e 50% decrease","Able-bodied = 6.67 mL/kg/m; Transfemoral = 3.33 mL/kg/m -\u003e 50% decrease","Able-bodied = 0.15 mL/kg/m; Transfemoral = 0.30 mL/kg/m -\u003e 100% increase in O2 cost per meter"],"a":3,"e":"Oxygen cost per unit distance = VO2 (mL/kg/min) / Walking speed (m/min). Able-bodied: 12.0 / 80 = 0.15 mL/kg/m. Dysvascular transfemoral amputee: 15.0 / 50 = 0.30 mL/kg/m. Percentage increase = [(0.30 − 0.15) / 0.15] × 100 = 100% increase! While rate per minute (VO2) rises modestly (25%), the marked drop in walking speed causes energy cost per meter covered to double, explaining why elderly dysvascular transfemoral patients require pre-prosthetic cardiac screening.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00379","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Advanced Prosthetics and Emerging Technologies","topic":"Bone-Anchored Percutaneous Implants","q":"A bilateral transfemoral amputee who failed traditional sockets due to severe heterotopic ossification and recurrent skin ulcers receives titanium bone-anchored osseointegrated (OI) implants. Beyond eliminating socket-skin friction and sweat, what unique sensory advantage and essential mechanical safety device characterize OI prostheses?","o":["Osseoperception (direct transmission of ground/vibration feedback through the skeletal implant) and a fail-safe torque-limiting axial connector designed to release before a fall fractures the femur","Total elimination of bone remodeling; a rigid welded steel rod that never releases under torsional overload","Complete restoration of cutaneous temperature sensation at the prosthetic toes without surgery","Use of an airtight vacuum suction sleeve around the percutaneous stoma to pull skin downward"],"a":0,"e":"Direct skeletal anchorage (OPRA/ILP) bypasses soft tissue entirely, improving hip range of motion, sitting comfort, and \u0027osseoperception\u0027—mechanical vibration sensitivity transmitted through bone mechanoreceptors that enhances balance on uneven terrain. Because high twisting or bending loads during a fall would otherwise cause a periprosthetic femoral fracture, an external fail-safe connector (e.g., Axor/safety clutch) shears/releases above preset torque/bending thresholds.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00380","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Extremity Orthotics","topic":"Genu Recurvatum in Post-Polio","q":"A 48-year-old post-polio survivor has 1/5 quadriceps strength, intact 4/5 hip extensors and ankle plantarflexors, and painful progressive 20-degree genu recurvatum (hyperextension thrust) during stance without mediolateral knee instability or foot drop. Which orthosis specifically blocks pathological knee hyperextension while preserving free active knee flexion during swing?","o":["Posterior Leaf Spring AFO alone set in 20 degrees plantarflexion","Swedish Knee Cage (three-point posterior-directed popliteal pressure system)","Rigid Knee Immobilizer locked permanently at 0 degrees extension","Pavlik hip harness"],"a":1,"e":"When quadriceps weakness is compensated by posterior capsule stretching into painful genu recurvatum—provided hip extensors/plantarflexors and coronal knee ligaments remain competent—a Swedish Knee Cage uses two anteriorly directed bands (distal anterior thigh and proximal anterior shin) opposed by a posteriorly directed popliteal strap/pad behind the knee to block hyperextension past ~5° flexion while permitting unobstructed swing-phase flexion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00381","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Upper Extremity Prosthetics","topic":"Wrist-Driven Mechanical vs Silicone Digits","q":"A 30-year-old graphic designer loses the index, middle, and ring fingers at the proximal phalanx/MCP level in a press injury, retaining an intact thumb, intact little finger, and full 5/5 wrist flexion/extension. He desires active grip closure of the missing digits for holding tools and objects, rather than solely a passive cosmetic opposition post. Which partial-hand prosthetic mechanism utilizes his intact wrist motion?","o":["Wrist-driven mechanical articulating finger prosthesis (linking wrist flexion/extension via dorsal/palmar tenodesis linkage to actively flex and extend the prosthetic fingers)","Passive silicone cosmetic slip-on glove without mechanical joints","Shoulder-harnessed transhumeral split hook mounted above the elbow","Myoelectric biceps-controlled elbow unit"],"a":0,"e":"When wrist flexion/extension is preserved following multi-digit transmetacarpal/MCP loss, wrist-driven mechanical prostheses (such as articulated mechanical finger linkages) convert active wrist flexion into finger flexion (grasp) and wrist extension into finger opening—allowing body-powered opposition against the intact thumb without batteries or shoulder harnesses. Passive silicone fingers restore cosmesis and serve as a static opposition post.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00382","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Pediatric Prosthetics","topic":"Van Ness Rotationplasty","q":"In a child with severe proximal femoral focal deficiency (PFFD) or distal femoral osteosarcoma who undergoes a Van Ness rotationplasty (180-degree rotation of the tibia/foot), which anatomical joint functions inside the modified prosthesis as an actively controlled biological KNEE joint?","o":["The subtalar joint acting as a hip joint","The prosthetic foot SACH keel acting as a hip joint","The rotated ankle joint (where active plantarflexion extends the prosthetic knee and dorsiflexion flexes it)","The contralateral knee joint coupled by an RGO cable"],"a":2,"e":"Van Ness rotationplasty preserves the neurovascular bundle, resects the diseased knee/distal femur, and rotates the distal limb 180° so the heel points anteriorly and toes point posteriorly at the level of the contralateral knee. Placed in a transtibial-style prosthesis with side-joints and thigh corset, the child\u0027s ankle provides durable, active, sensate knee control—eliminating phantom pain and allowing high-impact sports with energy efficiency superior to transfemoral amputation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00383","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Sports Prosthetics and High-Activity Alignment","topic":"J-Shaped Carbon-Fiber Running Blades","q":"A K4 unilateral transtibial sprinter is fitted with a J-shaped carbon-fiber running-specific prosthesis (RSP) blade that has no heel keel. Why do prosthetists routinely align the unloaded running blade ~1.5 to 2.5 cm LONGER than the contralateral intact limb\u0027s standing heel-to-floor height?","o":["To force permanent contralateral hip hiking during every stride","Because carbon fiber permanently stretches longer after 100 meters of sprinting","Because sprinters never flex their prosthetic-side knee during swing phase","Running occurs on the forefoot without heel strike, and the curved carbon-fiber blade compresses vertically by ~1.5–2.5 cm under peak 2.5–3× body-weight impact before springing back at push-off"],"a":3,"e":"During sprinting, ground contact occurs exclusively on the forefoot/toe pad of the J-shaped blade (eliminating a heel lever). Under peak mid-stance running loads (2.5–3× body weight), the C/J-curve deflects vertically by 1–2.5 cm to store elastic strain energy; setting the unloaded blade ~1.5–2.5 cm longer ensures symmetric hip height at peak mid-stance compression.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00384","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Rehabilitation Engineering and Seating","topic":"Flexible vs Fixed Pelvic Obliquity","q":"During a wheelchair mat evaluation of two adult clients with neuromuscular scoliosis, Client A has a FLEXIBLE pelvic obliquity (left ASIS higher in sitting, passively reducible to level in supine), whereas Client B has a FIXED pelvic obliquity (left ASIS permanently 4 cm higher and non-reducible, overloading the right ischial tuberosity). How must custom seating cushions address Client A versus Client B?","o":["Level Client A\u0027s flexible pelvis back to horizontal using symmetric contoured base and lateral pelvic/thigh supports; ACCOMMODATE Client B\u0027s fixed obliquity (build up under the elevated left buttock / recess under the overloaded right ischial tuberosity) rather than forcing it level","Build up a high wedge solely under Client B\u0027s already overloaded lower right ischial tuberosity","Leave Client A unsupported on a sagging sling canvas seat","Force Client B\u0027s fixed pelvis flat onto a hard level board using a tight ratcheted pelvic strap"],"a":0,"e":"The cardinal rule of wheelchair seating is: CORRECT flexible postural asymmetries, ACCOMMODATE fixed asymmetries. Attempting to force a fixed pelvic obliquity onto a flat horizontal cushion concentrates the entire upper-body weight onto the lower, prominent ischial tuberosity—rapidly causing a Grade 4 pressure injury. Building up support under the unweighted high side (and unloading/contouring under the low ischium) distributes load safely across both thighs and buttocks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00385","course":"BPO - Bachelor of Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Spinal Orthotics","topic":"In-Brace Radiographic Correction","q":"A 13-year-old girl (Risser 1) with Adolescent Idiopathic Scoliosis has a baseline standing out-of-brace right thoracolumbar Cobb angle of 36 degrees (apex T11). Two weeks after fitting a custom Boston TLSO, a standing in-brace AP radiograph demonstrates a Cobb angle of 18 degrees. Calculate the percentage of in-brace curve correction and state whether it meets standard orthotic effectiveness benchmarks.","o":["200.0% in-brace correction; over-corrected into severe opposite scoliosis","50.0% in-brace correction; meets/exceeds the standard \u003e=30–50% correction benchmark predicting successful curve stabilization","36.0% in-brace correction; inadequate because target requires 100% zero-degree straightening","18.0% in-brace correction; fails benchmark and requires immediate surgical fusion"],"a":1,"e":"Percentage in-brace correction = [(Out-of-brace Cobb angle − In-brace Cobb angle) / Out-of-brace Cobb angle] × 100 = [(36° − 18°) / 36°] × 100 = (18 / 36) × 100 = 50.0%. Clinical bracing studies (including BrAIST and SRS guidelines) establish that achieving \u003e=30–50% immediate in-brace Cobb correction strongly predicts halting curve progression when combined with \u003e=18 hours/day wear compliance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00386","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Orthopaedics \u0026 Sports","topic":"Special Orthopaedic Tests","q":"A 21-year-old football player presents 48 hours after a non-contact pivoting knee injury with acute haemarthrosis and painful flexion limited to 40 degrees. Which clinical test is MOST sensitive in this acute setting to detect an Anterior Cruciate Ligament (ACL) tear?","o":["Posterior sag (Godfrey) sign at 90 degrees of hip and knee flexion","McMurray test performed in full passive knee hyperflexion with tibial rotation","Lachman test performed at 20-30 degrees of knee flexion","Ober test in side-lying with hip extension and adduction"],"a":2,"e":"The Lachman test (20-30 degrees knee flexion) is the gold-standard, most sensitive clinical test for ACL integrity, especially in acute painful/swollen knees where 90-degree flexion for the Anterior Drawer test is impossible and hamstring spasm/posterior horn meniscal wedging can cause false negatives.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00387","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Electrotherapy \u0026 Physical Agents","topic":"Shortwave Diathermy (SWD) Safety","q":"Which combination lists ONLY absolute contraindications to thermal Shortwave Diathermy (SWD)?","o":["Chronic frozen shoulder without metal implants or vascular insufficiency","Chronic knee osteoarthritis; Subacute lumbar strain; Healed ankle sprain","Superficial skin abrasions covered by dry gauze in a young healthy athlete without implants","Cardiac pacemaker; Metallic implants in the treatment field; Active haemorrhage; Pregnancy over abdomen/pelvis"],"a":3,"e":"High-frequency electromagnetic energy (27.12 MHz SWD) causes selective heating of metallic implants (risking deep thermal burns), disrupts cardiac pacemaker circuitry, increases bleeding in active haemorrhage, and risks fetal hyperthermia/teratogenicity during pregnancy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00388","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Electrotherapy","topic":"Electrodiagnosis","q":"A 28-year-old man develops wrist drop after sleeping with his arm draped over a chair. At 3 weeks post-injury, an electrodiagnostic Strength-Duration (S-D) curve of the extensor carpi radialis brevis reveals a chronaxie of 8 ms, a steeply rising curve shifted upward and to the right, a kink (discontinuity) in the mid-portion of the curve, and sluggish accommodation to triangular pulses. Which nerve injury status does this S-D curve represent?","o":["Partial denervation (axonotmesis with partial sparing)","Complete denervation (neurotmesis)","Normal innervated muscle","Pure neuropraxia with intact distal axon"],"a":0,"e":"A kink or discontinuity in the Strength-Duration curve represents two intersecting excitability curves—one from surviving innervated motor units (lower threshold, shorter chronaxie) and one from denervated muscle fibers (higher threshold, prolonged chronaxie \u003e 1 ms). This bimodal pattern is diagnostic of partial denervation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00389","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Biomechanics \u0026 Kinesiology","topic":"Glenohumeral Joint","q":"A patient with adhesive capsulitis has severely restricted passive glenohumeral abduction (65 degrees) and external rotation (15 degrees). Applying Kaltenborn\u0027s convex-concave rule to mobilize the convex humeral head on the fixed concave glenoid fossa, which pair of translational glides specifically restores abduction and external rotation respectively?","o":["Inferior glide for abduction; posterior glide for external rotation","Inferior glide for abduction; anterior glide for external rotation","Superior glide for abduction; posterior glide for external rotation","Anterior glide for abduction; inferior glide for external rotation"],"a":1,"e":"Because the convex humeral head moves on the concave glenoid fossa, roll and glide occur in opposite directions. During abduction, the humeral head rolls superiorly and must glide inferiorly; during external rotation, it rolls posteriorly and must glide anteriorly to stretch the anterior capsule.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00390","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Physiotherapy","topic":"Exercise Prescription","q":"A 54-year-old post-MI patient entering Phase II outpatient cardiac rehabilitation has a resting heart rate of 68 bpm and achieved a symptom-limited peak heart rate of 148 bpm on a predischarge treadmill test. Using the Karvonen Heart Rate Reserve (HRR) method, what is the target heart rate at 60% training intensity?","o":["89 bpm","124 bpm","116 bpm","108 bpm"],"a":2,"e":"Heart Rate Reserve (HRR) = Peak HR - Resting HR = 148 - 68 = 80 bpm. Target HR = (HRR x intensity) + Resting HR = (80 x 0.60) + 68 = 48 + 68 = 116 bpm. Using HRR accounts for resting heart rate and chronotropic response more accurately than a simple percentage of peak HR.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00391","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Neurological Physiotherapy","topic":"Upper Extremity Diagonal Patterns","q":"A physiotherapist is training a post-stroke hemiparetic patient in upper extremity Proprioceptive Neuromuscular Facilitation (PNF) to restore the ability to reach across the body to feed himself (hand-to-mouth/opposite shoulder). Which PNF diagonal pattern and component motions correspond to this task?","o":["D1 Extension: shoulder extension, abduction, internal rotation with forearm pronation","D2 Extension: shoulder extension, adduction, internal rotation with forearm pronation","D2 Flexion: shoulder flexion, abduction, external rotation with forearm supination and wrist/finger extension","D1 Flexion: shoulder flexion, adduction, external rotation with forearm supination and wrist/finger flexion"],"a":3,"e":"The PNF D1 Flexion pattern moves the upper limb from a position down and out at the side up and across the midline toward the opposite ear/shoulder (shoulder flexion, adduction, external rotation, supination, radial wrist/finger flexion), replicating functional feeding, face washing, and seatbelt reaching.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00392","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Musculoskeletal \u0026 Orthopaedic Physiotherapy","topic":"McKenzie Mechanical Diagnosis and Therapy (MDT)","q":"A 36-year-old software engineer presents with acute posterolateral lumbar pain radiating into the right calf. During repeated standing/prone lumbar extension testing (McKenzie MDT), the calf pain abolishes completely and symptoms retreat proximally to the central low back. What is this clinical phenomenon, and what does it indicate?","o":["Centralization; favorable prognostic sign indicating a posterior disc derangement with an extension directional preference","Peripheralization; an absolute contraindication to extension-based exercises","Waddell non-organic sign; requires cessation of mechanical physiotherapy","Cauda equina red flag; proceed to immediate lumbar traction"],"a":0,"e":"Centralization is defined in McKenzie MDT as the progressive proximal retreat of distal/radicular symptoms toward the spinal midline in response to repeated therapeutic movements or sustained postures. It demonstrates an intact hydrostatic disc mechanism (reducible derangement) with a clear directional preference for extension and predicts a favorable non-surgical outcome.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00393","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Electrotherapy - High Frequency \u0026 Thermal Modalities","topic":"Pulsed Ultrasound Dosimetry","q":"To promote non-thermal collagen synthesis in an acute superficial lateral epicondylitis (target depth ~1.2 cm), a physiotherapist selects a 3 MHz ultrasound transducer with a 1:4 pulsed ratio (20% duty cycle) and sets the Spatial Averaged Temporal Peak (SATP) intensity to 1.5 W/cm2. What is the resulting Spatial Averaged Temporal Averaged (SATA) intensity, and why was 3 MHz chosen over 1 MHz?","o":["SATA = 0.75 W/cm2; 3 MHz penetrates deeper than 1 MHz (up to 5 cm)","SATA = 0.30 W/cm2; 3 MHz is absorbed rapidly in superficial tissues (1-2 cm depth)","SATA = 1.20 W/cm2; 3 MHz eliminates acoustic streaming","SATA = 0.30 W/cm2; 1 MHz has a higher attenuation coefficient in tendon than 3 MHz"],"a":1,"e":"SATA = SATP x Duty Cycle = 1.5 W/cm2 x 0.20 (1:4 pulsed = 1 part on, 4 parts off = 20% on-time) = 0.30 W/cm2, keeping net tissue heating minimal while harnessing non-thermal cavitation and acoustic streaming. Attenuation increases with frequency, so 3 MHz deposits energy superficially (1-2 cm) whereas 1 MHz penetrates deeper (3-5 cm).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00394","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Musculoskeletal \u0026 Sports Physiotherapy","topic":"Anterior Cruciate Ligament (ACL) Tears","q":"A 23-year-old football player sustains a non-contact twisting knee injury with immediate hemarthrosis and inability to continue play. At 48 hours post-injury, painful effusion limits knee flexion to 70 degrees. Which special test performed at 20-30 degrees of knee flexion is the MOST sensitive clinical test for diagnosing an acute Anterior Cruciate Ligament (ACL) rupture?","o":["Posterior sag (Godfrey) sign","90-degree Anterior Drawer test","Lachman test","McMurray test"],"a":2,"e":"The Lachman test (anterior tibial translation performed at 20-30 degrees of knee flexion) is the most sensitive clinical test for acute ACL rupture because: (1) painful acute hemarthrosis often prevents 90-degree flexion, (2) hamstring guarding pulls posteriorly much less at 20-30 degrees than at 90 degrees, and (3) the posterior horn of the medial meniscus does not act as a doorstop block.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00395","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Kinesiology \u0026 Observational Gait Analysis","topic":"Hip Abductor vs Hip Extensor Weakness","q":"During observational gait analysis, as a patient enters right single-limb mid-stance, the LEFT (swing) side of the pelvis drops noticeably downward, accompanied by a compensatory trunk lean toward the right. Which muscle weakness and peripheral nerve lesion account for this positive Trendelenburg gait?","o":["Left gluteus maximus weakness; inferior gluteal nerve","Left tibialis anterior weakness; left deep peroneal nerve","Right iliopsoas weakness; right femoral nerve","Right gluteus medius weakness; right superior gluteal nerve"],"a":3,"e":"During right single-limb stance, the right hip abductors (gluteus medius and minimus, innervated by the right superior gluteal nerve) normally contract isometrically to hold the pelvis level. Weakness on the stance (right) side allows the unsupported contralateral (left) pelvis to drop downward (positive Trendelenburg sign), often triggering a compensated trunk lean over the right hip.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00396","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Physiotherapy","topic":"Postural Drainage \u0026 Precautions","q":"A physiotherapist is planning airway clearance for a post-craniotomy neurosurgical patient with retained secretions in the posterior basal segments of both lower lobes. Why is the classic postural drainage position (prone with 45 cm / 20-degree head-down Trendelenburg tilt) CONTRAINDICATED in this patient, and which alternative technique should be substituted?","o":["Head-down tilt markedly elevates intracranial pressure (ICP); substitute modified horizontal/elevated positioning combined with Active Cycle of Breathing Technique (ACBT) or manual hyperinflation/suctioning as indicated","Head-down tilt reduces lung compliance permanently; substitute deep percussion over the spinous processes","Posterior basal segments drain best in upright high-Fowler position anyway","Head-down tilt causes acute bronchodilation; substitute incentive spirometry for secretion expectoration"],"a":0,"e":"True postural drainage of lower-lobe basal segments requires a 15-20 degree head-down Trendelenburg tilt, which impairs cerebral venous return and sharply elevates intracranial pressure (contraindicated in recent craniotomy, TBI, acute stroke, severe GERD, or active hemoptysis). Modified side-lying/flat positioning paired with ACBT, PEP therapy, or careful suctioning safely mobilizes secretions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00397","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Electrotherapy","topic":"Transcutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation (TENS)","q":"How do the neurophysiological mechanism and electrical parameters of Conventional (High-Rate) TENS differ from Acupuncture-Like (Low-Rate) TENS in clinical pain management?","o":["Conventional TENS uses 2-4 Hz motor twitches to release endorphins; Acupuncture-like TENS uses 100 Hz sensory tingling via A-beta fibers","Conventional TENS uses high frequency (50-100 Hz) and short pulse duration (50-100 us) at comfortable sensory intensity to stimulate A-beta fibers (Gate Control at spinal dorsal horn); Acupuncture-like TENS uses low frequency (2-4 Hz) at strong motor-twitch intensity to release endogenous opioids","Both modes act exclusively by irreversible thermal ablation of C-fibers","Conventional TENS requires painful tetanic muscle contraction and is reversed by naloxone"],"a":1,"e":"Per Melzack and Wall\u0027s Gate Control Theory, Conventional TENS (50-100 Hz, 50-100 us, comfortable paresthesia without muscle contraction) selectively depolarizes large myelinated A-beta afferents, activating inhibitory interneurons in the substantia gelatinosa to close the dorsal horn gate to A-delta/C pain signals (rapid onset, not blocked by naloxone). Acupuncture-like TENS (2-4 Hz, 150-250 us, visible muscle contraction) stimulates A-delta/group III afferents to release beta-endorphins/enkephalins (slower onset, longer carryover, blocked by naloxone).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00398","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Neurological \u0026 Vestibular Physiotherapy","topic":"Posterior Semicircular Canal BPPV","q":"A 56-year-old woman reports brief 20-second episodes of spinning vertigo whenever she rolls onto her right side in bed or looks upward. Performing a right-sided Dix-Hallpike maneuver provokes vertigo after a 4-second latency, accompanied by transient up-beating, torsional (geotropic) nystagmus lasting 18 seconds that fatigues on repeat testing. What is the diagnosis and definitive physiotherapy intervention?","o":["Left horizontal canal cupulolithiasis; Brandt-Daroff exercises only","Right posterior semicircular canal BPPV (canalithiasis); Right Epley canalith repositioning maneuver","Bilateral vestibular hypofunction; gaze stabilization adaptation only","Acute cerebellar brainstem stroke; immediate cervical traction"],"a":1,"e":"Up-beating torsional (top pole of eye beating toward the dependent right ear) nystagmus with a brief 2-5 s latency, crescendo-decrescendo duration \u003c 60 s, and fatigability on the right Dix-Hallpike test is pathognomonic for right posterior semicircular canal canalithiasis. The Right Epley maneuver sequentially rotates the head through four positions to guide free-floating otoconia back into the utricle.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00399","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Spinal Cord Injury Physiotherapy","topic":"Autonomic Dysreflexia (AD)","q":"During mat mobility training, a 27-year-old man with T4 ASIA-A spinal cord injury suddenly develops a pounding occipital headache, profuse sweating and flushing above the T4 level, nasal congestion, BP 206/114 mmHg (baseline 95/60 mmHg), and bradycardia (HR 46 bpm). What is the immediate physiotherapy emergency response?","o":["Administer rapid oral glucose assuming hypoglycemia","Continue vigorous lower-limb passive stretching to lower sympathetic tone","Immediately sit the patient upright (90 degrees) with legs dependent to lower BP via orthostasis, loosen tight clothing/straps, check and relieve kinked urinary catheter/bladder distension or bowel impaction, and summon urgent medical review","Lay the patient flat supine with legs elevated in Trendelenburg and apply ice packs below T4"],"a":2,"e":"SCI lesions at or above T6 impair descending supraspinal inhibition of splanchnic sympathetic outflow. A noxious stimulus below the lesion (most commonly bladder distension/kinked Foley catheter or fecal impaction) triggers massive vasoconstriction below the lesion (hypertensive crisis) with compensatory baroreceptor-mediated vagal bradycardia and sweating/flushing above the lesion. Immediate upright positioning harnesses orthostatic pooling while rapidly removing the noxious trigger.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00400","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Orthopaedic \u0026 Post-Surgical Physiotherapy","topic":"Total Hip Arthroplasty (THA) Precautions","q":"A 68-year-old woman is Day 2 post-right Total Hip Arthroplasty (THA) performed via a POSTERIOR (posterolateral) surgical approach. Before transferring her from bed to chair and prescribing home ADL adaptations (raised toilet seat, abduction pillow), which triad of hip movements MUST the physiotherapist strictly prohibit for the first 6-12 weeks?","o":["Isometric gluteal setting and supine ankle pumps","Hip extension, external rotation, and abduction","Isolated ankle plantarflexion and knee extension","Hip flexion \u003e 90 degrees, hip adduction past the midline, and internal rotation"],"a":3,"e":"A posterior/posterolateral THA incises the posterior capsule and short external rotators. Consequently, combining hip flexion beyond 90 degrees, adduction across the midline (crossing legs), and internal rotation pushes the prosthetic femoral head posteriorly out of the acetabular cup. Patients use elevated chairs/toilet seats, reachers, and abduction pillows until posterior soft tissues scar and stabilize.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00401","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Neuroanatomy \u0026 Clinical Neurology Assessment","topic":"Key Myotomes \u0026 Dermatomes","q":"During an International Standards for Neurological Classification of Spinal Cord Injury (ISNCSCI / ASIA) motor examination of the lower extremities, which key muscles correspond specifically to the L4, L5, and S1 myotomes respectively?","o":["L4: Ankle dorsiflexors (tibialis anterior); L5: Long toe extensors (extensor hallucis longus); S1: Ankle plantarflexors (gastrocnemius-soleus)","L4: Hip flexors (iliopsoas); L5: Knee extensors (quadriceps); S1: Ankle dorsiflexors","L4: Long toe extensors; L5: Ankle plantarflexors; S1: Ankle dorsiflexors","L4: Ankle plantarflexors; L5: Knee flexors (hamstrings); S1: Hip abductors"],"a":0,"e":"Standard ASIA key lower-limb myotomes follow a predictable craniocaudal sequence: L2 = Hip flexors (iliopsoas), L3 = Knee extensors (quadriceps), L4 = Ankle dorsiflexors (tibialis anterior), L5 = Long toe extensors (extensor hallucis longus), and S1 = Ankle plantarflexors (gastrocnemius/soleus).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00402","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Electrotherapy - High-Frequency \u0026 Medium-Frequency Currents","topic":"SWD Safety \u0026 Contraindications","q":"A patient with chronic knee osteoarthritis is referred for Shortwave Diathermy (SWD, 27.12 MHz) deep heating. Before applying condenser pads, the physiotherapist learns the patient has a permanent cardiac pacemaker, a titanium total knee prosthesis on the contralateral leg, and damp perspiration-soaked synthetic trousers. Why must SWD be withheld, and how do metallic implants/moisture cause burns during SWD?","o":["Contralateral metal implants are safe because 27.12 MHz waves never scatter beyond 2 cm","Pacemakers are an absolute contraindication due to electromagnetic interference/lead heating; high-frequency RF fields concentrate eddy currents in metallic implants and water droplets/sweat, causing severe localized deep or superficial thermal burns","SWD generates zero heat in metals; moisture cools the electrodes excessively","SWD only heats bone cortex without affecting soft tissues or pacemakers"],"a":1,"e":"Continuous Shortwave Diathermy emits high-frequency electromagnetic energy (27.12 MHz) that induces eddy currents and rapid dipole oscillation in high-conductivity metals (pacemaker leads, orthopedic plates/prostheses, copper IUDs) and superficial water droplets/sweat. This causes extreme focal heating, deep tissue burns, or pacemaker malfunction/arrest—making implanted electronic devices, local metal, and wet clothing strict contraindications.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00403","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Musculoskeletal \u0026 Orthopaedic Physiotherapy","topic":"Adhesive Capsulitis (Frozen Shoulder)","q":"A 52-year-old diabetic woman presents with insidious 4-month shoulder stiffness and night pain. On passive range-of-motion testing (with scapula stabilized), which proportional restriction hierarchy defines Cyriax\u0027s glenohumeral CAPSULAR PATTERN diagnostic of adhesive capsulitis?","o":["Adduction most limited \u003e Internal rotation \u003e Abduction","Flexion most limited \u003e Extension \u003e External rotation","External rotation most limited \u003e Abduction \u003e Internal rotation","Internal rotation most limited \u003e Abduction \u003e External rotation"],"a":2,"e":"Per Cyriax, every synovial joint exhibits a characteristic proportional limitation when its entire fibrous capsule tightens (capsular pattern). For the glenohumeral joint in adhesive capsulitis, External Rotation is disproportionately most restricted, followed by Abduction, and then Internal Rotation (e.g., ER limited 75%, Abd 50%, IR 25%), guiding targeted anterior/inferior capsular mobilization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00404","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Exercise Therapy","topic":"DeLorme vs Oxford Regimens","q":"When designing a 3-set Progressive Resistive Exercise (PRE) strengthening program based on a patient\u0027s 10-Repetition Maximum (10-RM), how do the DeLorme protocol and the Oxford protocol differ in their set-by-set loading progression?","o":["DeLorme descends from 100% to 50% of 10-RM; Oxford ascends from 50% to 100%","DeLorme ASCENDS across 3 sets of 10 reps (50% -\u003e 75% -\u003e 100% of 10-RM, building a warm-up); Oxford DESCENDS (100% -\u003e 75% -\u003e 50% of 10-RM, accommodating muscular fatigue)","Both regimens use 1 set of 100 repetitions at 10% of 1-RM","Oxford applies exclusively to isometric holds with zero external resistance"],"a":1,"e":"Both DeLorme and Oxford protocols prescribe 30 total repetitions divided into 3 sets of 10 based on the weekly 10-RM. DeLorme progresses from light to heavy (Set 1 at 50%, Set 2 at 75%, Set 3 at 100% of 10-RM) to warm up the muscle progressively. Oxford reverses the pyramid (100% -\u003e 75% -\u003e 50%) so peak load is lifted fresh before fatigue sets in.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00405","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Pediatric Physiotherapy","topic":"Spastic Diplegia \u0026 Crouch Gait","q":"An 8-year-old boy with GMFCS Level II spastic diplegic cerebral palsy underwent isolated bilateral Achilles tendon lengthening for equinus toe-walking 18 months ago. He now walks in a severe \u0027crouch gait\u0027 (excessive ankle dorsiflexion, \u003e 35 degrees knee flexion, and hip flexion throughout stance). What biomechanical mechanism precipitated this crouch gait?","o":["Isolated tibialis anterior hypertonicity forcing ankle plantarflexion","Excessive quadriceps strength locking the knee in hyperextension","Over-strengthening of the gastrocnemius-soleus plantarflexion-knee extension couple","Over-lengthening/weakening of the soleus (disrupting the plantarflexion-knee extension couple that restrains forward tibial collapse) alongside unaddressed hamstring and iliopsoas spasticity/contracture"],"a":3,"e":"In normal stance, the soleus controls tibial forward progression via the plantarflexion-knee extension (PF-KE) couple, stabilizing the knee in extension. Isolated surgical lengthening of the Achilles tendon in spastic diplegia—without addressing tight hamstrings/hip flexors or preserving soleus push-off—causes calcaneus deformity and unrestrained forward tibial collapse into disabling crouch gait (prevented by gastrocnemius recession rather than tendo-Achilles lengthening and ground-reaction AFOs).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00406","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Orthopaedics \u0026 Sports","topic":"Distal Radius Fracture Rehabilitation","q":"A 58-year-old woman presents to the physiotherapy department immediately after removal of a below-elbow Colles\u0027 cast worn for 6 weeks following a closed extra-articular distal radius fracture. She exhibits marked limitation of wrist flexion and radial deviation, mild dorsal swelling, and preserved finger MCP motion. Which peripheral joint mobilisation glide at the radiocarpal joint is biomechanically indicated to restore wrist palmar flexion?","o":["Dorsal glide of the proximal carpal row on the fixed radius","Volar glide of the proximal carpal row on the fixed radius","Radial glide of the proximal carpal row on the fixed ulna","Ulnar glide of the distal radius on the fixed scaphoid"],"a":0,"e":"At the radiocarpal joint, the proximal carpal row (scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum) forms a convex articular surface moving on the concave distal radial articular surface. By the convex-on-concave arthrokinematic rule, rolling and gliding occur in opposite directions: during wrist palmar flexion, the carpals roll volarly and glide dorsally. Therefore, applying a dorsal glide of the proximal carpal row on a stabilised radius specifically restores wrist flexion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00407","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Orthopaedics \u0026 Sports","topic":"Adhesive Capsulitis (Frozen Shoulder)","q":"A 52-year-old diabetic man develops insidious-onset shoulder pain and progressive global stiffness over 4 months without preceding trauma. On passive range-of-motion assessment, which proportional pattern of restriction defines a true glenohumeral capsular pattern according to Cyriax?","o":["Abduction most limited, followed by internal rotation, then external rotation","External rotation most limited, followed by abduction, then internal rotation","Extension most limited, followed by horizontal adduction, then abduction","Internal rotation most limited, followed by flexion, then external rotation"],"a":1,"e":"According to Cyriax, inflammation and fibrosis of the entire glenohumeral joint capsule (as seen in adhesive capsulitis) produces a characteristic capsular pattern of proportional passive motion loss: lateral (external) rotation is most severely restricted, followed by abduction, and internal (medial) rotation is least restricted among the three.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00408","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Orthopaedics \u0026 Sports","topic":"Total Knee Arthroplasty Rehabilitation","q":"On postoperative day 5 following a cemented primary total knee arthroplasty (TKA), a 66-year-old man demonstrates 85 degrees of active-assisted knee flexion, a 10-degree active extensor lag on straight-leg raise, and 5 degrees of fixed knee flexion contracture. Why is restoring full passive knee extension (0 degrees) prioritised alongside flexion in the early postoperative phase?","o":["Achieving 0 degrees extension prevents deep vein thrombosis by compressing the popliteal vein during bed rest","Full passive knee extension is required to unlock the screw-home mechanism before quadriceps isometric exercises can begin","Persistent flexion contracture increases quadriceps demand during stance phase and causes a flexed-knee gait with anterior knee pain","Delaying extension stretching until week 6 prevents disruption of the posterior cruciate-retaining tibial insert"],"a":2,"e":"Even a small residual knee flexion contracture (5-15 degrees) prevents passive locking at terminal stance, forcing the quadriceps to fire continuously to prevent knee buckling. This markedly increases patellofemoral joint reaction forces, energy expenditure of walking, and risk of chronic limp and anterior knee pain. Early heel-prop stretching and posterior capsular/hamstring lengthening are therefore mandatory alongside flexion recovery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00409","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Orthopaedics \u0026 Sports","topic":"Lumbar Disc Herniation Management","q":"A 36-year-old software engineer presents with acute low back pain radiating into the right posterolateral calf and lateral foot after lifting luggage. Prolonged sitting worsens the leg pain. During McKenzie mechanical evaluation, repeated standing lumbar flexion causes pain to migrate farther into the heel, whereas 10 repetitions of prone press-up (lumbar extension) abolish the calf pain while briefly increasing central low back ache. How should the physiotherapist interpret this response and structure the home programme?","o":["Peripheralisation has occurred during extension, indicating acute cauda equina compression requiring immediate surgical referral","Increase in central back ache signifies facet joint jamming, so lumbar flexion in sitting should be prescribed immediately","Alternating maximal flexion and extension exercises in equal sets should be prescribed to balance intradiscal pressure","Centralisation has occurred, establishing extension as the directional preference for repeated prone press-ups and lumbar lordosis support"],"a":3,"e":"Centralisation is defined as the progressive proximal retreat or abolition of distal radicular symptoms toward the spinal midline in response to repeated directional loading, even if central spinal pain temporarily intensifies. It is a strong positive prognostic sign in posterolateral lumbar disc derangement and dictates prescribing the directional preference (extension exercises and sitting with a lumbar roll) while avoiding sustained flexion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00410","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Orthopaedics \u0026 Sports","topic":"Tendinopathies of Wrist and Hand","q":"A postpartum mother complains of sharp pain over the radial styloid when lifting her infant. During physical examination, the physiotherapist instructs her to tuck her thumb into her palm, wrap her fingers over it, and passively ulnar-deviates the wrist, reproducing sharp radial styloid pain. Which pair of tendons is stretched and implicated in this positive test?","o":["Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis","Flexor pollicis longus and abductor pollicis brevis","Extensor pollicis longus and extensor indicis","Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis"],"a":0,"e":"The clinical manoeuvre described is the Eichhoff/Finkelstein test for De Quervain\u0027s stenosing tenosynovitis of the first dorsal extensor compartment of the wrist. This compartment houses the abductor pollicis longus (APL) and extensor pollicis brevis (EPB) tendons, which become painful under ulnar deviation stretch when inflamed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00411","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Exercise Therapy","topic":"Proprioceptive Neuromuscular Facilitation (PNF)","q":"A physiotherapist is teaching a hemiparetic patient the Proprioceptive Neuromuscular Facilitation (PNF) Upper Extremity D2 Flexion diagonal pattern to facilitate overhead reaching. Which exact combination of joint motions constitutes the terminal position of the UE D2 Flexion pattern?","o":["Shoulder flexion, adduction, and external rotation with forearm supination, wrist radial flexion, and finger flexion","Shoulder flexion, abduction, and external rotation with forearm supination, wrist radial extension, and finger extension","Shoulder flexion, abduction, and internal rotation with forearm pronation, wrist ulnar extension, and finger abduction","Shoulder extension, abduction, and internal rotation with forearm pronation, wrist ulnar flexion, and finger flexion"],"a":1,"e":"In PNF, the Upper Extremity D2 Flexion pattern mimics drawing a sword out of a contralateral sheath or reaching up and outward into a high shelf: the scapula elevates/retracts with upward rotation, the shoulder moves into flexion, abduction, and external rotation, the forearm supinates, and the wrist/fingers move into radial extension and abduction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00412","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Exercise Therapy","topic":"PNF Stretching Techniques","q":"A track athlete recovering from a grade I hamstring strain has painful restriction of straight-leg raise at 55 degrees. The physiotherapist wishes to apply a PNF stretching technique that avoids joint shear or painful isotonic movement of the tight muscle against resistance before stretching. Which technique should be selected?","o":["Contract-relax (isotonic maximal concentric rotation of the hamstring through available range before stretching)","Ballistic high-velocity bouncing stretches into the painful range to stimulate tendon viscoelasticity","Hold-relax (isometric contraction of the tight hamstring at the end range followed by relaxation and passive stretch)","Slow-reversal-hold concentrating on the quadriceps while passively forcing knee flexion"],"a":2,"e":"Hold-relax uses a submaximal-to-maximal isometric contraction of the range-limiting (tight) muscle against unyielding manual resistance at the point of mild tension, followed by complete relaxation and passive lengthening into the newly gained range. Because no joint movement occurs during the contraction, hold-relax is specifically preferred over contract-relax when movement causes pain.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00413","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Neurology, Cardiorespiratory \u0026 Community","topic":"Post-Stroke Gait Rehabilitation","q":"Three months after a left MCA ischaemic stroke, a 61-year-old man walks with a classic right hemiplegic circumduction gait characterised by poor hip/knee flexion in initial swing and plantarflexion-inversion foot drop causing toe drag. Passive ankle dorsiflexion reaches 5 degrees past neutral with the knee extended, and plantarflexor spasticity is Modified Ashworth Scale 2. Which orthotic and gait training intervention best addresses the primary mechanical barrier to foot clearance?","o":["Apply a heavy ankle weight on the right leg to dampen quadriceps extensor synergy during stance","Prescribe a 2-cm shoe lift under the right (hemiplegic) foot to equalize pelvic tilt during swing phase","Immobilise the knee in full extension using a rigid knee-ankle-foot orthosis (KAFO) permanently","Prescribe a custom posterior leaf-spring or hinged ankle-foot orthosis (AFO) with plantarflexion stop combined with task-specific hip/knee flexor facilitation"],"a":3,"e":"In spastic hemiplegia, excessive plantarflexion-inversion and weak dorsiflexors elongate the functional leg length during swing, forcing compensatory hip hiking and circumduction to clear the toes. Because passive dorsiflexion reaches +5 degrees, an AFO with a plantarflexion stop (set near neutral) prevents foot drop, stabilizes midstance, and allows active task-specific swing training without blocking controlled tibial advancement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00414","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Neurology, Cardiorespiratory \u0026 Community","topic":"Parkinson\u0027s Disease Rehabilitation","q":"A 68-year-old man with Hoehn and Yahr Stage III Parkinson\u0027s disease experiences severe freezing of gait (FOG) when passing through narrow doorways and initiating turns, despite optimal levodopa timing. Placing high-contrast transverse tape strips on the floor and using a rhythmic metronome markedly improves his stride length and initiation. What neurophysiological mechanism explains why external cues overcome freezing in Parkinson\u0027s disease?","o":["External visual and auditory cues bypass the defective basal ganglia-supplementary motor area internal cueing loop by recruiting intact cerebellar-parietal-premotor cortical pathways","Rhythmic auditory beats directly stimulate nigrostriatal dopamine synthesis in the substantia nigra pars compacta","Transverse floor lines trigger spinal monosynaptic stretch reflexes in the gastrocnemius to propel the tibia forward","Visual cues suppress vestibulospinal tone uniformly across both lower limbs during turning"],"a":0,"e":"In Parkinson\u0027s disease, nigrostriatal dopamine depletion impairs internal automatic motor sequencing mediated by the basal ganglia-supplementary motor area (SMA) circuit, causing hypokinesia and freezing. Goal-directed external visual or auditory cues engage lateral premotor cortex and cerebello-thalamo-cortical networks that remain relatively preserved, shifting gait from automatic to attention-guided cortical control.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00415","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Neurology, Cardiorespiratory \u0026 Community","topic":"Spinal Cord Injury Assessment","q":"During an International Standards for Neurological Classification of Spinal Cord Injury (ISNCSCI / ASIA) examination of a patient with a cervical spinal cord injury, bilateral key muscle grades are recorded as: C5 (elbow flexors) = 5/5, C6 (wrist extensors) = 4/5, C7 (elbow extensors) = 2/5, C8 (finger flexors) = 0/5, and T1 (finger abductors) = 0/5. Assuming all myotomes rostral to C5 are normal, what is the patient\u0027s motor level on each side?","o":["C6","C8","C7","C5"],"a":0,"e":"By ISNCSCI (ASIA) rules, the motor level is defined as the lowest (most caudal) key muscle function that has a grade of at least 3/5 on supine manual muscle testing, provided all key muscle functions represented by segments above that level are graded 5/5. Here, C6 wrist extensors score 4/5 (\u003e=3/5) and C5 above it scores 5/5, while C7 below it scores 2/5 (\u003c3/5). Therefore, the motor level is C6.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00416","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Neurology, Cardiorespiratory \u0026 Community","topic":"Airway Clearance Techniques","q":"A patient with cystic fibrosis has thick retained secretions confirmed on chest radiograph in the lateral and medial segments of the right middle lobe. Which postural drainage position accurately aligns the right middle lobe bronchus vertically for gravity-assisted drainage?","o":["Side-lying on the right side with the bed completely flat and knees flexed","Supine rotated 45 degrees onto the left side (quarter turn from supine) with the foot of the bed elevated approximately 35 cm (14 inches)","Prone with a pillow under the abdomen and the head of the bed elevated 30 degrees","Sitting upright leaning forward 30 degrees over a pillow on a bedside table"],"a":1,"e":"The right middle lobe bronchus projects anteriorly and slightly inferiorly. To place it vertically for gravity drainage, the patient lies on the left side rolled backward one-quarter turn toward supine (45 degrees) supported by pillows behind the back, with the foot of the bed elevated 12-14 inches (~30-35 cm, ~15 degrees Trendelenburg).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00417","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Neurology, Cardiorespiratory \u0026 Community","topic":"ICU Chest Physiotherapy Safety","q":"An intubated 48-year-old man in the neuro-ICU following subarachnoid haemorrhage has a baseline intracranial pressure (ICP) of 22 mmHg, mean arterial pressure (MAP) of 82 mmHg, and basal atelectasis with mucopurulent secretions. Which modification to chest physiotherapy is safest and clinically appropriate?","o":["Perform sustained abdominal thrust coughing manoeuvres every 10 minutes without pre-oxygenation","Initiate prolonged end-expiratory breath holds using PEEP 25 cmH2O to loosen secretions","Avoid Trendelenburg head-down positioning and vigorous chest percussion; maintain head-of-bed elevation at 30 degrees and use gentle vibrations with manual hyperinflation/suctioning only as hemodynamically tolerated","Place the patient in 20-degree Trendelenburg with bilateral manual percussion for 15 minutes to rapidly recruit basal alveoli"],"a":2,"e":"Trendelenburg (head-down) tilt impairs cerebral venous outflow and precipitates dangerous spikes in ICP (\u003e20 mmHg), reducing cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP - ICP). Vigorous percussion can also raise intrathoracic pressure and ICP. Maintaining a 30-degree head-up neutral neck alignment while using modified lateral positioning, gentle expiratory vibrations, and brief pre-oxygenated suctioning protects cerebral perfusion while clearing secretions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00418","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Neurology, Cardiorespiratory \u0026 Community","topic":"Active Cycle of Breathing Techniques (ACBT)","q":"In the Active Cycle of Breathing Techniques (ACBT) for bronchiectasis, what is the specific physiological purpose of incorporating a 2-3 second end-inspiratory hold during Thoracic Expansion Exercises (TEE) prior to the Forced Expiration Technique (FET)?","o":["To induce maximal bronchoconstriction of proximal bronchi and accelerate mucociliary beat frequency","To deplete alveolar surfactant temporarily so small airways collapse during subsequent inhalation","To stimulate hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction and divert blood away from consolidated lung units","To promote collateral ventilation through pores of Kohn and channels of Lambert so air moves behind distal mucus plugs"],"a":3,"e":"Thoracic Expansion Exercises emphasize slow, deep nasal inspirations followed by a 2-3 second end-inspiratory breath hold. This lowers regional time-constant inequalities and promotes airflow through collateral channels (interalveolar pores of Kohn, bronchoalveolar canals of Lambert, and interbronchiolar channels of Martin) behind obstructing secretions, enabling the subsequent Forced Expiration Technique (huffing) to shear and mobilize mucus proximally.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00419","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Neurology, Cardiorespiratory \u0026 Community","topic":"Cardiac Rehabilitation Exercise Prescription","q":"A 55-year-old man entering Phase II outpatient cardiac rehabilitation after an uncomplicated inferior myocardial infarction completes a submaximal exercise test achieving a peak heart rate of 165 bpm, with a resting heart rate of 70 bpm. Using the Karvonen Heart Rate Reserve (HRR) formula, what is his target training heart rate range for an intensity of 60% to 70% HRR?","o":["127 to 137 bpm","142 to 154 bpm","99 to 116 bpm","115 to 125 bpm"],"a":0,"e":"The Karvonen formula calculates Target Heart Rate (THR) = [(HRmax - HRrest) x % intensity] + HRrest. Here, HRR = 165 - 70 = 95 bpm. At 60% HRR: (95 x 0.60) + 70 = 57 + 70 = 127 bpm. At 70% HRR: (95 x 0.70) + 70 = 66.5 + 70 = 136.5 bpm (rounded to 137 bpm).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00420","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Neurology, Cardiorespiratory \u0026 Community","topic":"COPD Breathing Retraining","q":"A 64-year-old smoker with severe emphysema experiences marked exertional dyspnea during stair climbing. Teaching him pursed-lip breathing (inhaling through the nose and exhaling slowly over 4-6 seconds through pursed lips) rapidly reduces his respiratory rate and air trapping. What is the primary physiological mechanism of pursed-lip breathing?","o":["It creates positive intraluminal back-pressure during expiration that shifts the equal pressure point proximally and prevents premature small airway collapse","It increases respiratory rate and anatomical dead space ventilation to blow off excess alveolar carbon dioxide rapidly","It recruits accessory scalene and sternocleidomastoid muscles to generate forceful active expiration","It increases inspiratory transpulmonary pressure above +40 cmH2O to stretch fibrotic alveolar septa"],"a":0,"e":"In emphysema, loss of alveolar elastic recoil causes premature dynamic airway compression during expiration when surrounding pleural pressure exceeds intraluminal pressure. Exhaling against resistance at pursed lips generates mild positive expiratory pressure (~2-5 cmH2O) inside the airways, stenting floppy peripheral bronchioles open, shifting the equal pressure point toward cartilaginous larger airways, prolonging expiration, and reducing dynamic hyperinflation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00421","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Electrotherapy \u0026 Physical Agents","topic":"Shortwave Diathermy (SWD)","q":"A 62-year-old patient with bilateral knee osteoarthritis is referred for thermal Shortwave Diathermy (27.12 MHz). During screening, the physiotherapist learns the patient has a permanent cardiac pacemaker and a stainless-steel intramedullary nail in the ipsilateral femur. Which clinical decision is mandatory?","o":["Wrap the femur in wet towels to shield the intramedullary nail from eddy-current heating","Strictly contraindicate SWD and select a safe alternative modality because high-frequency electromagnetic fields can cause pacemaker malfunction and severe deep thermal burns around metal implants","Proceed with capacitor-plate SWD over the knee only if the machine is switched to pulsed non-thermal mode at minimum intensity","Use coplanar cable SWD strictly below the knee joint line while keeping a wooden plinth at least 10 cm away"],"a":1,"e":"Shortwave diathermy (27.12 MHz) is absolutely contraindicated in patients with cardiac pacemakers (risk of electromagnetic interference, erratic pacing, or lead-tip heating) and over or near implanted metal (which concentrates electric/eddy currents and causes rapid localized deep tissue burns). Even pulsed SWD emits high-frequency electromagnetic radiation capable of disrupting electronic implants and heating metal.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00422","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Electrotherapy \u0026 Physical Agents","topic":"Therapeutic Ultrasound","q":"A physiotherapist plans therapeutic ultrasound for two different patients: Patient A has acute lateral epicondylitis (superficial common extensor tendon, \u003c1 cm depth) and Patient B has chronic hip capsular tightness (4 cm depth) prior to stretching. Which parameter combination is optimal for Patient A and Patient B respectively?","o":["Patient A: 0.5 MHz at 50% pulsed mode; Patient B: 5 MHz at 100% continuous mode","Patient A: 1 MHz at 100% continuous mode; Patient B: 3 MHz at 20% pulsed duty cycle","Patient A: 3 MHz at 20% pulsed duty cycle; Patient B: 1 MHz at 100% continuous mode","Patient A: 3 MHz at 100% continuous mode (3.0 W/cm2); Patient B: 1 MHz at 10% pulsed mode"],"a":2,"e":"Ultrasound attenuation increases proportionally with frequency. A 3 MHz beam is absorbed rapidly in superficial tissues (effective penetration ~1-2 cm), making it ideal for superficial tendons like the lateral epicondyle, and a 20% pulsed duty cycle delivers non-thermal acoustic streaming/cavitation for acute inflammation. Conversely, 1 MHz penetrates deeply (~3-5 cm) to reach the hip capsule, and 100% continuous mode raises tissue temperature to increase collagen extensibility before stretching.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00423","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Electrotherapy \u0026 Physical Agents","topic":"Transcutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation (TENS)","q":"Assertion (A): Conventional TENS (80-120 Hz, 50-100 microseconds pulse duration, comfortable tingling without muscle contraction) provides rapid analgesia that fades relatively quickly after the stimulator is switched off. Reason (R): Conventional TENS selectively depolarises large-diameter myelinated A-beta afferent fibres, activating inhibitory interneurons in the spinal dorsal horn substantia gelatinosa to close the pain gate without relying primarily on slow-acting beta-endorphin release. Choose the correct option.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Conventional (high-frequency, low-intensity) TENS selectively recruits low-threshold A-beta mechanoreceptive afferents (avoiding A-delta/C nociceptors and motor fibres). These excite inhibitory interneurons in lamina II (substantia gelatinosa) of the spinal cord, presynaptically inhibiting A-delta and C nociceptive transmission (Melzack \u0026 Wall Gate Control Theory). This gives fast onset but minimal post-stimulation carryover compared with low-frequency (2-5 Hz) acupuncture-like TENS, which induces slower, longer-lasting beta-endorphin release.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00424","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Electrotherapy \u0026 Physical Agents","topic":"Interferential Therapy (IFT)","q":"During quadripolar Interferential Therapy (IFT) for lumbar myofascial pain, Circuit 1 delivers a medium-frequency alternating current of 4000 Hz and Circuit 2 delivers 4090 Hz. What is the resulting amplitude-modulated beat frequency produced where the two currents intersect in deep tissue, and why are 4000 Hz carrier frequencies used?","o":["8090 Hz beat frequency; high carrier frequencies induce sustained tetanic contraction of deep multifidus","4045 Hz beat frequency; medium-frequency carrier waves bypass all sensory nerves completely","45 Hz beat frequency; skin resistance increases proportionally with carrier frequency to prevent deep burns","90 Hz beat frequency; medium-frequency carrier waves encounter much lower capacitive skin impedance than low-frequency currents"],"a":3,"e":"In IFT, two medium-frequency AC circuits intersect to create an amplitude-modulated beat frequency equal to the difference between the two carrier frequencies: |4090 Hz - 4000 Hz| = 90 Hz (ideal for pain gating). Because capacitive skin impedance (Z = 1 / [2 * pi * f * C]) is inversely proportional to frequency, ~4000 Hz carrier currents penetrate the skin with minimal sensory discomfort and much lower resistance than 90 Hz delivered directly at the skin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00425","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Electrotherapy \u0026 Physical Agents","topic":"Electrodiagnosis","q":"A physiotherapist plots a Strength-Duration (S-D) curve on the tibialis anterior 4 weeks after a common peroneal nerve traction injury. Compared with the contralateral normal side, the curve is shifted upward and to the right, exhibits a visible kink (discontinuity), and yields a chronaxie of 8.5 ms (normal \u003c 1 ms). What does this S-D curve indicate?","o":["Partial denervation showing mixed responses from surviving innervated motor units and denervated muscle fibres","Normal innervation with simple disuse atrophy","Complete denervation with total absence of nerve excitability","Pure upper motor neuron spasticity with intact alpha motor neurons"],"a":0,"e":"A kinked (stepped) Strength-Duration curve shifted upward and rightward with moderately elevated chronaxie (normal innervated mammalian skeletal muscle chronaxie is typically \u003c=1 ms) is pathognomonic of partial denervation. The shorter-duration segment represents excitable surviving motor axons, whereas the longer-duration stepped segment reflects higher-threshold denervated muscle fibres requiring prolonged pulse durations (\u003e=10-100 ms).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00426","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Biomechanics \u0026 Kinesiology","topic":"Arthrokinematics and Joint Mobilisation","q":"A patient exhibits restricted glenohumeral elevation in the scapular plane (abduction) due to inferior capsular tightness. Applying Kaltenborn\u0027s convex-concave rule to the movement of the convex humeral head on the concave glenoid fossa, which mobilisation glide direction directly facilitates superior osteokinematic abduction?","o":["Anterior glide of the humeral head","Inferior (caudal) glide of the humeral head","Posterior glide of the humeral head","Superior (cranial) glide of the humeral head"],"a":1,"e":"Because the convex humeral head articulates with the shallow concave glenoid fossa, arthrokinematic roll and glide occur in opposite directions during osteokinematic elevation. As the humeral head rolls superiorly during abduction, it must simultaneously glide inferiorly (caudally) to prevent impingement against the acromion and coracoacromial arch. Hence, an inferior glide mobilisation restores abduction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00427","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Exercise Therapy","topic":"Universal Goniometry","q":"During an OSCE station assessing passive knee flexion in supine using a universal goniometer, where must the physiotherapist align the fulcrum, stationary arm, and moving arm, and what is the normal end-feel?","o":["Fulcrum over lateral joint line; stationary arm parallel to table; moving arm toward medial malleolus; capsular firm end-feel","Fulcrum over fibular head; stationary arm toward ASIS; moving arm toward fifth metatarsal base; hard bony end-feel","Fulcrum over lateral epicondyle of femur; stationary arm toward greater trochanter; moving arm toward lateral malleolus; soft tissue approximation end-feel","Fulcrum over patellar tendon; stationary arm along femoral shaft; moving arm along tibial crest; empty end-feel"],"a":2,"e":"According to Norkin \u0026 White\u0027s standardized goniometry protocol for knee flexion (normal 0-135/140 degrees), the patient lies supine with the hip flexed as the knee bends. The goniometer fulcrum is centered over the lateral epicondyle of the femur, the proximal (stationary) arm aligns with the greater trochanter along the lateral midline of the femur, and the distal (moving) arm aligns with the lateral malleolus along the lateral midline of the fibula. Normal knee flexion ends with a soft tissue approximation end-feel between the posterior calf and thigh.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00428","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"Biomechanics \u0026 Kinesiology","topic":"Biomechanical Levers and Static Equilibrium","q":"A subject holds the elbow statically at 90 degrees of flexion while supporting a 40 N dumbbell in the hand. The forearm\u0027s own weight is neglected for simplicity. If the perpendicular internal moment arm of the biceps brachii is 5 cm from the elbow joint axis and the perpendicular external moment arm of the dumbbell is 35 cm from the joint axis, what isometric force must the biceps brachii generate to maintain static equilibrium?","o":["200 N","140 N","5.7 N","280 N"],"a":3,"e":"At static rotational equilibrium, internal muscle torque equals external resistance torque: F_muscle x d_muscle = F_load x d_load. Therefore, F_biceps x 5 cm = 40 N x 35 cm, giving F_biceps = 1400 / 5 = 280 N. Because the effort (biceps insertion) lies between the fulcrum (elbow joint) and resistance (hand), this is a Class III lever with mechanical advantage \u003c 1 (5/35 = 0.143), requiring high muscle force to gain speed and range of motion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00429","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Orthopaedics \u0026 Sports","topic":"Anterior Cruciate Ligament (ACL) Reconstruction Rehab","q":"A 23-year-old footballer is 5 weeks post-ACL reconstruction using a quadruple hamstring autograft. His surgeon clears him for progressive quadriceps strengthening while protecting the healing graft from excessive anterior tibial translation strain. Which exercise prescription generates the LEAST anterior shear strain on the ACL graft while strengthening the quadriceps?","o":["Closed kinetic chain exercises (mini-squats and leg press from 0 to 60 degrees knee flexion) and open kinetic chain knee extension restricted to 90 to 45 degrees of flexion","Isokinetic maximal-effort open kinetic chain extension from 40 to 0 degrees at 60 degrees/second","Deep weighted squats to 120 degrees flexion combined with plyometric drop-jumps","Open kinetic chain seated knee extension with ankle weights from 30 to 0 degrees terminal extension"],"a":0,"e":"During open kinetic chain (OKC) knee extension, the quadriceps pulls the tibia anteriorly relative to the femur, peaking in ACL strain between 30 degrees and 0 degrees (terminal extension). Restricting early OKC extension to 90-45 degrees and emphasizing closed kinetic chain (CKC) activities (0-60 degrees) where axial joint compression and hamstring co-contraction counter anterior tibial translation protects the maturing ACL graft.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00430","course":"BPT - Physiotherapy","subject":"PT in Orthopaedics \u0026 Sports","topic":"Shoulder Impingement in Overhead Athletes","q":"A 19-year-old competitive freestyle swimmer develops anterolateral shoulder pain between 60 and 120 degrees of active abduction, positive Neer and Hawkins-Kennedy tests, and visible medial scapular winging with inadequate posterior tilt during arm lowering. Which scapular muscle force couple should be specifically targeted to restore acromiohumeral clearance during elevation?","o":["Levator scapulae, rhomboid major, and pectoralis minor working together to produce scapular downward rotation and anterior tilt","Serratus anterior, lower trapezius, and upper trapezius working together to produce scapular upward rotation and posterior tilt","Pectoralis major and anterior deltoid working maximally to accelerate scapular protraction","Latissimus dorsi and teres major working to depress and internally rotate the humerus continuously"],"a":1,"e":"Adequate upward rotation and posterior tilting of the scapula during overhead elevation keep the acromion clear of the supraspinatus tendon. This motion is driven by the scapular force couple comprising the serratus anterior (strongest upward rotator and stabilizer of the medial border against the thorax) together with the lower and upper trapezius. Weakness of serratus anterior and lower trapezius with tight pectoralis minor causes anterior scapular tilt and subacromial impingement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00431","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology \u0026 Pathophysiology","topic":"Antihypertensive \u0026 Heart Failure Pharmacology","q":"A 56-year-old hypertensive patient started on enalapril develops a persistent dry, hacking cough. Why does switching to losartan typically resolve the cough while maintaining blood pressure control?","o":["Enalapril causes bronchospasm by blocking beta-2 adrenoceptors, whereas losartan is a beta-2 agonist","Losartan inhibits renin secretion directly without affecting angiotensin II receptor binding","Losartan selectively blocks AT1 receptors without inhibiting ACE (kininase II), avoiding bradykinin and substance P accumulation in the airways","Losartan increases bradykinin degradation via neprilysin induction in pulmonary capillaries"],"a":2,"e":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) is identical to kininase II, which degrades bradykinin and substance P. ACE inhibitors (enalapril, ramipril) raise airway bradykinin levels, triggering dry cough and rare angioedema. Angiotensin II receptor blockers (losartan, telmisartan) block AT1 receptors downstream without inhibiting ACE/kininase II, eliminating bradykinin-mediated cough.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00432","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics I","topic":"Hydrophilic-Lipophilic Balance (HLB) System","q":"A formulation scientist must prepare 10 g of a mixed emulsifier system having a required HLB of 10.72 to stabilise an oil-in-water (O/W) emulsion using Tween 80 (HLB = 15.0) and Span 80 (HLB = 4.3). What quantity of Tween 80 is needed in the 10 g blend?","o":["7.5 g","4.0 g","5.0 g","6.0 g"],"a":3,"e":"Using the weighted HLB equation: f_Tween = (HLB_req - HLB_Span) / (HLB_Tween - HLB_Span) = (10.72 - 4.3) / (15.0 - 4.3) = 6.42 / 10.7 = 0.60 (60%). Therefore, in a 10 g blend, Tween 80 = 0.60 x 10 g = 6.0 g (and Span 80 = 4.0 g). Check: 0.6(15.0) + 0.4(4.3) = 9.0 + 1.72 = 10.72.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00433","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics II","topic":"Sedimentation Theory","q":"According to Stokes\u0027 law for dilute pharmaceutical suspensions, which formulation modification produces the GREATEST proportional reduction in the rate of particle sedimentation?","o":["Reducing the suspended drug particle diameter by half (50%)","Reducing the density difference between drug particle and vehicle by 25%","Doubling the viscosity of the dispersion medium","Increasing the gravitational acceleration constant through centrifugation"],"a":0,"e":"Stokes\u0027 law states v = d^2 (rho_s - rho_o) g / (18 eta), where sedimentation velocity v is proportional to the square of particle diameter (d^2) and inversely proportional to medium viscosity (eta). Halving particle diameter reduces sedimentation rate to (0.5)^2 = 0.25 (a 75% reduction), whereas doubling viscosity reduces rate by 50% and reducing density difference by 25% lowers rate by only 25%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00434","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry III","topic":"Structure-Activity Relationship (SAR) of Penicillins","q":"In the medicinal chemistry of semisynthetic penicillins, which structural modification on the C-6 acylamino side chain confers resistance to gastric acid hydrolysis versus resistance to staphylococcal beta-lactamase (penicillinase)?","o":["Electron-withdrawing groups (e.g., phenoxymethyl in Penicillin V or alpha-NH2 in Ampicillin) confer acid stability; bulky ortho-substituted aromatic rings (e.g., Methicillin/Oxacillin) confer beta-lactamase resistance","Bulky ortho-substituted groups confer gastric acid stability; electron-donating alkyl chains confer beta-lactamase resistance","Replacement of the thiazolidine sulphur atom with oxygen confers both acid stability and beta-lactamase resistance","Methylation of the C-3 carboxylic acid group confers both acid stability and enhanced gram-positive binding"],"a":0,"e":"Acid-catalysed degradation of penicillin occurs via nucleophilic attack of the amide carbonyl oxygen onto the strained beta-lactam carbonyl; electron-withdrawing substituents (phenoxymethyl, alpha-amino) pull electron density away from the amide oxygen, preventing this attack. Conversely, bulky ortho-disubstituted aryl/isoxazolyl groups create steric hindrance around the beta-lactam ring, blocking penicillinase access.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00435","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry II","topic":"Prodrug Design \u0026 Bioactivation","q":"Enalaprilat exhibits poor oral bioavailability (~10%) due to its polar zwitterionic dicarboxylic acid structure. How is enalapril designed to overcome this pharmacokinetic limitation while preserving ACE inhibitory activity in vivo?","o":["Hydrogenated at the benzene ring to eliminate first-pass CYP3A4 oxidation","Formulated as the monoethyl ester prodrug (enalapril), which increases lipophilicity for oral absorption and is cleaved by hepatic esterases to active enalaprilat","Administered as a quaternary ammonium salt to enhance paracellular passive diffusion across enterocytes","Conjugated with glucuronic acid at the proline carboxylate to target active hepatic OATP uptake followed by renal beta-glucuronidase cleavage"],"a":1,"e":"Enalapril is the ethyl ester prodrug of enalaprilat. Masking one ionisable carboxyl group as an ethyl ester raises oral bioavailability to ~60%; after gastrointestinal absorption, hepatic carboxylesterases hydrolyse the ester back to the active diacid enalaprilat, which coordinates the catalytic Zn2+ ion of ACE.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00436","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology I","topic":"Non-Linear (Saturation) Pharmacokinetics","q":"A patient receiving phenytoin 300 mg/day orally has a steady-state plasma concentration of 10 mg/L (therapeutic window 10-20 mg/L). When the dose is increased by just 100 mg/day to 400 mg/day, the steady-state plasma concentration surges to 28 mg/L with ataxia and nystagmus. Which pharmacokinetic principle accounts for this disproportionate jump?","o":["Capacity-limited (Michaelis-Menten) hepatic metabolism saturates near therapeutic concentrations, shifting elimination from first-order toward zero-order kinetics","Enterohepatic recirculation suddenly ceases above 300 mg/day, doubling systemic clearance","Oral bioavailability of phenytoin decreases linearly at higher doses due to P-glycoprotein induction","Renal tubular secretion of unchanged phenytoin switches to active reabsorption above 10 mg/L"],"a":0,"e":"Phenytoin has a low hepatic Michaelis constant (Km ~ 4-6 mg/L), meaning its CYP2C9/2C19 metabolising enzymes approach saturation within the therapeutic range (10-20 mg/L). Once saturated (zero-order kinetics), clearance decreases as concentration rises, so small dose increments produce large, nonlinear spikes in steady-state concentration and toxicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00437","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Vaughan-Williams Class III Antiarrhythmics","q":"A 64-year-old man on long-term oral amiodarone for recurrent ventricular tachycardia develops progressive exertional dyspnoea, non-productive cough, bilateral interstitial infiltrates on chest radiograph, and elevated TSH with low free T4. Which electrophysiological mechanism and toxicity profile characterise amiodarone?","o":["Blocks L-type Ca2+ channels exclusively in the AV node; causes gingival hyperplasia and peripheral ankle oedema","Activates K-ATP channels causing coronary vasodilation; causes severe reflex tachycardia and cyanide toxicity","Blocks outward K+ channels (IKr) prolonging action potential duration and effective refractory period; contains ~37% iodine causing thyroid dysfunction and pulmonary fibrosis","Selectively blocks fast inward Na+ channels in ventricular myocytes with minimal effect on action potential duration; causes lupus-like syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Amiodarone is a Class III antiarrhythmic (also exhibiting Class I, II, and IV properties) that blocks delayed rectifier K+ channels, prolonging APD and QT interval (with characteristically low incidence of torsades de pointes). Its iodinated benzofuran structure (~37% iodine by weight) predisposes patients to hypo- or hyperthyroidism, interstitial pneumonitis/pulmonary fibrosis, corneal microdeposits, hepatotoxicity, and slate-grey skin photosensitivity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00438","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry II","topic":"Digitalis purpurea Identification Tests","q":"In the phytochemical screening of Digitalis purpurea leaf extract, the Keller-Kiliani test produces a reddish-brown ring at the junction of the two liquids while the upper glacial acetic acid layer gradually turns bluish-green. Which moiety of the cardiac glycoside is specifically detected by the Keller-Kiliani test?","o":["Cyanogenic nitrile group liberating hydrocyanic acid","Phenolic hydroxyl group on the A-ring of an anthraquinone","Five-membered unsaturated alpha,beta-lactone ring (butenolide) at C-17","2-deoxy sugar (digitoxose) attached to the C-3 position of the steroid nucleus"],"a":3,"e":"The Keller-Kiliani test (glacial acetic acid + ferric chloride + concentrated H2SO4 underlay) is specific for 2-deoxy sugars such as digitoxose found in cardenolide glycosides (digitoxin, digoxin). In contrast, Legal\u0027s test (sodium nitroprusside + pyridine/alkali), Baljet\u0027s test (picric acid + NaOH), and Kedde\u0027s test detect the 5-membered unsaturated lactone ring of cardenolides.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00439","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Engineering","topic":"Freeze Drying (Lyophilisation)","q":"To lyophilise a thermolabile monoclonal antibody injectable without melting the frozen cake during primary drying, the chamber temperature and absolute pressure must be maintained below the triple point of water. What are the approximate triple-point coordinates of pure water and the physical phase transition involved?","o":["Below 0.01 C and 4.58 mmHg (611 Pa), enabling direct sublimation of solid ice to water vapour","Above 100 C and 760 mmHg, enabling rapid azeotropic evaporation","Between 25 C and 40 C at 15 psi, enabling vacuum spray crystallisation","At -196 C and 10 atm, enabling supercritical CO2 extraction"],"a":0,"e":"Freeze drying (lyophilisation) freezes the aqueous product below its eutectic/collapse temperature, then reduces chamber pressure below water\u0027s triple point (0.01 C / 273.16 K and 4.58 mmHg or ~611 Pa) so that supplying controlled heat causes solid ice to sublime directly into vapour without passing through the liquid phase, preserving protein stability and producing a porous cake.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00440","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Quality Assurance","topic":"HVAC \u0026 Cleanroom Pressure Cascades","q":"In an aseptic parenteral filling suite compliant with Schedule M (India) and WHO GMP, how must HEPA filtration and differential air pressure gradients be configured across Grade A, Grade B, Grade C, and Grade D areas?","o":["Terminal H14 HEPA filters (99.97% efficiency at 0.3 micron) with positive pressure cascade decreasing from Grade A \u003e Grade B \u003e Grade C \u003e Grade D","Pre-filters alone with negative pressure in Grade A relative to Grade B to contain sterile product aerosols","Positive pressure highest in Grade D changing rooms stepping downward toward Grade A filling zones","Uniform neutral pressure across all grades with laminar airflow directed upward from floor grilles"],"a":0,"e":"Sterile aseptic manufacturing requires unidirectional laminar airflow under terminal HEPA filters (\u003e=99.97% retention of 0.3 micron particles) in Grade A critical zones and a positive differential pressure cascade (typically 10-15 Pa step-downs from cleanest Grade A/B outward to Grade C/D and unclassified corridors) so air always flows outward from cleaner to less-clean areas.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00441","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Jurisprudence","topic":"Drugs (Prices Control) Order (DPCO), 2013","q":"Under the Drugs (Prices Control) Order (DPCO), 2013 in India, which authority fixes and enforces the ceiling prices of scheduled formulations listed in the National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM), and on what basis is the ceiling price calculated?","o":["Indian Pharmacopoeia Commission (IPC) based strictly on active pharmaceutical ingredient import duty","National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority (NPPA) using market-based pricing (simple average price of brands with \u003e=1% market share plus 16% retailer margin)","Pharmacy Council of India (PCI) using hospital tender lowest-bidder rates only","Central Drugs Standard Control Organisation (CDSCO) using cost-plus-100% manufacturing markup alone"],"a":1,"e":"DPCO 2013 (issued under the Essential Commodities Act, 1955) shifted from cost-based to market-based pricing. The NPPA (Department of Pharmaceuticals, Ministry of Chemicals \u0026 Fertilizers) calculates ceiling prices for NLEM scheduled formulations by taking the simple average Price to Retailer (PTR) of all brands/generics with \u003e=1% market share and adding a 16% margin to the retailer.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00442","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics I","topic":"Displacement Value Calculation","q":"A pharmacist needs to prepare exactly 10 cocoa butter (Theobroma oil) suppositories using a 2.0 g mould, each containing 400 mg (0.4 g) of a drug whose displacement value relative to cocoa butter is 2.0. Ignoring overage, what total weight of cocoa butter base is required for the 10 suppositories?","o":["20.0 g","12.0 g","18.0 g","16.0 g"],"a":2,"e":"Total mould capacity for 10 suppositories = 10 x 2.0 g = 20.0 g. Total drug weight = 10 x 0.4 g = 4.0 g. Volume of cocoa butter displaced by 4.0 g drug = Drug weight / Displacement value = 4.0 / 2.0 = 2.0 g. Therefore, weight of cocoa butter required = 20.0 g - 2.0 g = 18.0 g.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00443","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics II","topic":"Non-Newtonian Flow \u0026 Thixotropy","q":"In pharmaceutical rheology, why is thixotropic shear-thinning behaviour (characterised by a reproducible down-curve lagging behind the up-curve on a rheogram) highly desirable in intramuscular depot suspensions and topical lotions?","o":["The formulation thickens irreversibly under shear so it solidifies inside the syringe needle during injection","The viscosity remains completely independent of shear rate at all temperatures","Shear-thickening dilatancy prevents particles from ever dispersing upon shaking","The formulation stays viscous as a gel on standing to prevent particle sedimentation, breaks down into a fluid sol on shaking/shearing for easy syringing or spreading, and recovers viscosity at rest"],"a":3,"e":"Thixotropy is an isothermal, time-dependent reversible gel-sol-gel transformation exhibiting a hysteresis loop between the ascending and descending shear curves. High viscosity at low/zero shear prevents caking and sedimentation during shelf storage, low viscosity under shear allows easy pouring, spreading, or injection through a needle, and slow structural recovery retains the drug at the application/injection site.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00444","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics I","topic":"Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation","q":"A weakly acidic non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug has a pKa of 4.4. When dissolved in gastric fluid at pH 1.4, what is the equilibrium ratio of unionised [HA] to ionised [A-] drug according to the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, and what does this imply for passive absorption?","o":["1,000 : 1 (99.9% unionised) — favours rapid passive lipid-membrane diffusion across gastric mucosa","100 : 1 (99% ionised) — requires active renal tubular secretion in stomach","1 : 1 (50% unionised) — causes immediate precipitation as sodium salt","1 : 1,000 (99.9% ionised) — completely prevents gastric passive absorption"],"a":0,"e":"For a weak acid, Henderson-Hasselbalch gives pH = pKa + log([A-]/[HA]), so log([A-]/[HA]) = pH - pKa = 1.4 - 4.4 = -3.0. Therefore [A-]/[HA] = 10^-3 = 1/1,000, meaning [HA]/[A-] = 1,000 : 1 (~99.9% unionised lipophilic form), which readily crosses biological lipid membranes by passive diffusion (note: the small intestine\u0027s vast surface area still absorbs most oral dose overall).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00445","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry I","topic":"SAR of Cholinomimetic Esters","q":"Compared with acetylcholine, bethanechol (carbamyl-beta-methylcholine) is resistant to hydrolysis by acetylcholinesterase and lacks nicotinic receptor activity, making it useful orally/subcutaneously for postoperative urinary retention. Which two structural modifications confer these respective properties?","o":["Replacing the quaternary ammonium with a tertiary amine confers cholinesterase resistance, while adding an alpha-methyl group confers nicotinic selectivity","Replacing the acetyl group with a carbamoyl (-OCONH2) ester confers cholinesterase resistance, while adding a beta-methyl group abolishes nicotinic activity to confer muscarinic selectivity","Extending the ethylene bridge to five carbons confers muscarinic selectivity, while replacing oxygen with sulphur accelerates hydrolysis","Aromatic hydroxylation confers muscarinic selectivity, while esterification with benzoate prevents oral absorption"],"a":1,"e":"In the SAR of cholinergic esters: (1) substituting the terminal methyl of the acyl group with an amino group forms a carbamate ester (like carbachol/bethanechol), where resonance stabilisation of the carbonyl resists nucleophilic attack by acetylcholinesterase and plasma cholinesterase; (2) placing a methyl substituent at the beta-carbon (C-2 from nitrogen, as in methacholine and bethanechol) sterically prevents binding to nicotinic receptors, preserving selective muscarinic stimulation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00446","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry II","topic":"Omeprazole Chemical Bioactivation","q":"Omeprazole is administered as a neutral substituted benzimidazole sulfoxide prodrug that is inactive at physiological blood pH 7.4. By what exact chemical mechanism does it achieve irreversible inhibition of parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase in the acidic secretory canaliculus?","o":["Gastric pepsin cleaves the pyridine ring to release a competitive H2-receptor antagonist","Hepatic CYP3A4 oxidises the sulfoxide to a sulfone that reversibly chelates luminal K+ ions","Protonation traps it in the acidic canaliculus (pH ~1), triggering Smiles rearrangement into a reactive cationic cyclic sulfenamide that forms a covalent disulfide bond with Cys813 of H+/K+-ATPase","Enterocyte esterases hydrolyse a methyl ester to open the benzimidazole ring"],"a":2,"e":"As a weak base (pKa ~4), neutral omeprazole diffuses across parietal cell membranes and becomes protonated (ion-trapped) in the strongly acidic secretory canaliculus (pH ~0.8-1.0). Acid catalyses an intramolecular spiro/Smiles rearrangement converting the sulfoxide into a thiophilic cyclic sulfenamide (and sulfenic acid), which covalently binds cysteine sulfhydryl groups (principally Cys813) on the alpha-subunit of H+/K+-ATPase, irreversibly blocking acid secretion until new pump synthesis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00447","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry III","topic":"Cotrimoxazole Sequential Folate Blockade","q":"Sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim are individually bacteriostatic, yet combined in a 5:1 fixed-dose ratio (cotrimoxazole) they exhibit potent bactericidal synergy. Which sequential enzymatic steps in bacterial folate synthesis do they inhibit respectively?","o":["Sulfamethoxazole competes with PABA to inhibit dihydropteroate synthase; trimethoprim selectively inhibits bacterial dihydrofolate reductase","Sulfamethoxazole inhibits bacterial RNA polymerase; trimethoprim inhibits peptidoglycan transpeptidase","Sulfamethoxazole inhibits dihydrofolate reductase; trimethoprim inhibits DNA gyrase","Both drugs inhibit thymidylate synthase at the same binding pocket"],"a":0,"e":"Bacteria must synthesise folic acid de novo from para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA). Sulfamethoxazole structurally mimics PABA and competitively inhibits dihydropteroate synthase (Step 1: PABA + pteridine -\u003e dihydropteroic acid), while trimethoprim is ~50,000-fold more selective for bacterial than mammalian dihydrofolate reductase (Step 2: DHF -\u003e THF). Sequential blockade depletes tetrahydrofolate cofactors required for thymidine/purine DNA synthesis, yielding synergistic bactericidal activity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00448","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Loop vs Thiazide Diuretics on Calcium Handling","q":"A patient with malignancy-associated hypercalcaemia receives intravenous isotonic saline followed by furosemide to promote calciuresis, whereas another patient with recurrent idiopathic hypercalciuria and calcium oxalate kidney stones is prescribed oral hydrochlorothiazide. Why do these two diuretic classes exert opposite effects on urinary calcium excretion?","o":["Furosemide blocks NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb abolishing the lumen-positive potential that drives paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption (increasing calciuria), whereas hydrochlorothiazide blocks NCC in the distal convoluted tubule enhancing basolateral Na+/Ca2+ exchange and Ca2+ reabsorption (reducing calciuria)","Furosemide stimulates parathyroid hormone receptors in the collecting duct, whereas hydrochlorothiazide directly chelates oxalate in the bladder","Furosemide blocks carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule, whereas hydrochlorothiazide blocks aldosterone-sensitive ENaC channels","Both drugs decrease urinary calcium identically, differing only in oral bioavailability"],"a":0,"e":"In the thick ascending limb of the Loop of Henle, K+ back-diffusion via ROMK creates a lumen-positive electrical potential that drives paracellular reabsorption of Ca2+ and Mg2+; furosemide inhibits NKCC2, collapsing this potential and markedly increasing Ca2+ excretion. Conversely, thiazides inhibit the apical Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC) in the early DCT, lowering intracellular Na+ and promoting basolateral Na+/Ca2+ antiport plus mild volume contraction-enhanced proximal Ca2+ reabsorption, thereby decreasing urinary calcium.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00449","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology III","topic":"Cyclophosphamide Urotoxicity Prevention","q":"A patient receiving high-dose intravenous cyclophosphamide for non-Hodgkin lymphoma is co-administered intravenous Mesna (sodium 2-mercaptoethanesulfonate) alongside aggressive hydration. Which toxic urinary metabolite causes haemorrhagic cystitis, and how does Mesna prevent it without blunting systemic antitumour efficacy?","o":["Hepatic bioactivation generates acrolein excreted in urine; Mesna concentrates in the bladder and provides a free sulfhydryl (-SH) group that covalently neutralises acrolein into a non-toxic thioether","Cyclophosphamide releases free phosphoric acid that erodes renal glomeruli; Mesna alkalinises blood pH above 7.8","Aldophosphamide destroys systemic bone marrow stem cells; Mesna selectively regenerates megakaryocytes in marrow","Phosphoramide mustard damages bladder epithelium; Mesna inhibits hepatic CYP2B6 preventing cyclophosphamide activation"],"a":0,"e":"Cyclophosphamide is bioactivated by hepatic CYP2B6/3A4 to 4-hydroxycyclophosphamide/aldophosphamide, which spontaneously cleaves into phosphoramide mustard (the cytotoxic DNA-alkylating agent) and acrolein (an electrophilic alpha,beta-unsaturated aldehyde excreted renally that directly injures urothelium causing haemorrhagic cystitis). Mesna is rapidly oxidised in plasma to inactive dimesna, filtered by glomeruli, and reduced back to active free-thiol Mesna inside renal tubular cells, binding acrolein selectively in the urinary tract without interfering with systemic phosphoramide mustard cytotoxicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00450","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry I","topic":"Leaf Constants","q":"During quantitative microscopy of a cleared Senna leaflet epidermis within a 1 sq mm field, a student counts 20 stomata and 80 epidermal cells. What is the Stomatal Index (SI) of this specimen?","o":["16.0%","80.0%","25.0%","20.0%"],"a":3,"e":"Stomatal Index (SI) is the percentage proportion of ultimate divisions of the epidermis transformed into stomata, independent of leaf size/environment: SI = [S / (E + S)] x 100, where S = number of stomata per unit area (20) and E = number of epidermal cells in the same area (80). Thus SI = [20 / (80 + 20)] x 100 = (20 / 100) x 100 = 20.0%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00451","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry II","topic":"Vinca Alkaloids vs Taxanes","q":"Both vincristine (from Catharanthus roseus) and paclitaxel (from Taxus brevifolia) arrest dividing cancer cells in mitosis (M phase) by targeting tubulin, yet they act via opposite biochemical mechanisms. Which statement accurately contrasts their mechanisms?","o":["Vincristine binds tubulin dimers to inhibit microtubule polymerisation (assembly), whereas paclitaxel binds polymerised beta-tubulin to hyperstabilise microtubules and prevent depolymerisation","Vincristine stabilises mitotic spindle microtubules against disassembly, whereas paclitaxel inhibits tubulin dimer polymerisation","Both drugs cross-link guanine N7 bases in G1 phase DNA","Vincristine inhibits topoisomerase I, whereas paclitaxel inhibits dihydrofolate reductase"],"a":0,"e":"Vinca alkaloids (vincristine, vinblastine) bind beta-tubulin on free alpha/beta-tubulin dimers, blocking polymerisation into mitotic spindle microtubules and causing metaphase arrest. In contrast, taxanes (paclitaxel, docetaxel) bind a luminal pocket on polymerised beta-tubulin, promoting assembly and preventing GTP-regulated depolymerisation—freezing the spindle and halting chromosome segregation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00452","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Microbiology","topic":"Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) Test for Sterility","q":"When performing the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) Test for Sterility on a batch of parenteral vials, which pair of culture media, incubation temperatures, and minimum incubation period is mandatory to detect anaerobic bacteria, aerobic bacteria, and fungi?","o":["Fluid Thioglycollate Medium (FTM) at 30-35 C (anaerobic/aerobic bacteria) and Soyabean-Casein Digest Medium (SCDM) at 20-25 C (fungi and aerobic bacteria), incubated for not less than 14 days","MacConkey agar at 37 C and Sabouraud broth at 42 C, incubated for 24 hours","Lactose broth at 44.5 C and Mannitol salt agar at 35 C, incubated for 48 hours","Nutrient broth at 25 C and Blood agar at 4 C, incubated for 72 hours"],"a":0,"e":"Per IP/USP harmonised sterility testing standards, Fluid Thioglycollate Medium (FTM, containing sodium thioglycollate and resazurin redox indicator) supports anaerobic and aerobic bacteria at 30-35 C, while Soyabean-Casein Digest Medium (SCDM / Tryptone Soya Broth) supports fungi and aerobic bacteria at 20-25 C. Both media must be incubated for a minimum of 14 days to permit recovery of slow-growing or sublethally injured organisms.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00453","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Biotechnology","topic":"Monoclonal Antibody Production","q":"In Köhler and Milstein\u0027s hybridoma technique for producing monoclonal antibodies, immunised mouse B-lymphocytes (HGPRT+) are fused with mutant myeloma cells (HGPRT-) using polyethylene glycol and cultured in HAT medium (Hypoxanthine, Aminopterin, Thymidine). Why do ONLY true B-cell-myeloma hybridomas survive in HAT medium?","o":["Thymidine induces apoptosis in hybridomas unless rescued by exogenous ampicillin","Aminopterin blocks de novo nucleotide synthesis, forcing cells onto the salvage pathway: unfused HGPRT- myeloma cells die because they cannot use hypoxanthine, unfused B-cells die naturally after a short lifespan, whereas hybridomas inherit HGPRT+ from B-cells and immortality from myeloma cells","Myeloma cells possess excess HGPRT enzyme that digests unfused B-cells in the presence of aminopterin","Aminopterin selectively lyses all B-lymphocytes immediately while hypoxanthine supplies viral reverse transcriptase to myeloma cells"],"a":1,"e":"Aminopterin is a dihydrofolate reductase inhibitor that blocks the de novo synthesis of purines and thymidylate. Cells must therefore rely on the salvage pathway using exogenous hypoxanthine (via HGPRT) and thymidine (via thymidine kinase). Unfused myeloma cells are HGPRT-deficient and die in HAT medium; unfused primary B-cells have HGPRT+ but finite lifespan and die within days; only fused hybridomas combine the B-cell\u0027s functional HGPRT enzyme (surviving HAT) with the myeloma cell\u0027s immortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00454","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Engineering","topic":"Steam Distillation of Immiscible Liquids","q":"Eugenol from clove buds (normal boiling point ~254 C) can be distilled smoothly at ~98-99 C at atmospheric pressure using steam distillation without charring. Which physical chemistry principle makes this possible?","o":["For two completely immiscible liquids, total vapour pressure equals the sum of their individual pure vapour pressures (P_total = P_water + P_oil), reaching atmospheric pressure just below 100 C","Steam chemically converts high-boiling eugenol into low-boiling methanol prior to condensation","Water acts as a Raoult\u0027s-law ideal solvent lowering the mole fraction of eugenol below 0.01","Steam raises the atmospheric pressure inside the condenser above 760 mmHg"],"a":0,"e":"According to Dalton\u0027s law for mutually immiscible liquids, each liquid exerts its own pure vapour pressure independently of the other\u0027s mole fraction: P_total = P_water + P_oil. Boiling commences as soon as P_water + P_oil equals external atmospheric pressure (760 mmHg). Because P_water alone reaches ~760 mmHg at 100 C, adding even a small vapour pressure of volatile oil causes the mixture to boil slightly below 100 C, protecting thermolabile essential oils.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00455","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Instrumental Methods of Analysis","topic":"Van Deemter Equation \u0026 Column Efficiency","q":"Switching an analytical HPLC column from 5-micron porous silica particles to sub-2-micron (1.7-micron) UHPLC particles dramatically increases theoretical plate number (N) and chromatographic resolution. According to the Van Deemter equation (H = A + B/u + C*u), which two band-broadening terms decrease most significantly as particle diameter (d_p) shrinks?","o":["Longitudinal molecular diffusion (B term) increases exponentially while Eddy diffusion becomes infinite","Extra-column tubing volume increases H linearly with mobile phase viscosity","Eddy diffusion (A term, proportional to d_p) and mass-transfer resistance (C term, proportional to d_p^2), markedly reducing Height Equivalent to a Theoretical Plate (H)","Detector cell path length doubles the C term at high flow rates"],"a":2,"e":"In the Van Deemter equation H = A + B/u + C*u, the Eddy diffusion (multipath) term A is directly proportional to particle diameter (d_p) and packing uniformity, and the mass-transfer resistance term C (equilibration across stagnant mobile phase inside pores) scales with d_p^2. Reducing d_p from 5 microns to 1.7 microns sharply lowers both A and C (lowering H and flattening the right side of the Van Deemter curve), yielding higher efficiency (N = L/H) even at higher linear velocities (u).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00456","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Quality Assurance","topic":"IP / USP Dissolution Apparatus Selection","q":"When performing an in vitro dissolution test on a hard gelatin capsule that floats to the meniscus of the dissolution vessel, how can floatation be prevented when using IP/USP Apparatus II (Paddle) versus Apparatus I (Rotating Basket)?","o":["Add 10% polysorbate 80 solely to increase fluid density so the capsule sinks","Test the capsule in Apparatus IV (Flow-Through Cell) with zero fluid flow","Double the paddle rotation speed from 50 rpm to 250 rpm in Apparatus II","Wrap a non-reactive helical wire sinker around the capsule in Apparatus II (Paddle), or test inside the wire-mesh basket of Apparatus I without a sinker"],"a":3,"e":"In IP/USP dissolution testing, floating capsules or buoyant tablets remain on the liquid surface in Apparatus II (Paddle), reducing hydrodynamic exposure; a few turns of inert platinum/stainless-steel helical wire (sinker) keep the dosage form submerged at the bottom of the vessel. Alternatively, Apparatus I (Rotating Basket) physically confines the capsule within a rotating 40-mesh stainless-steel basket immersed in the medium.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00457","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics I","topic":"Cryoscopic (Freezing Point Depression) Isotonicity Method","q":"A 1% w/v solution of a medicinal drug produces a freezing point depression (a) of 0.12 C. Given that a 1% w/v sodium chloride solution produces a freezing point depression (b) of 0.58 C and normal human blood plasma/lacrimal fluid freezes at -0.52 C, what concentration (% w/v) of sodium chloride is needed to render the 1% drug solution isotonic?","o":["0.69% w/v","0.40% w/v","1.10% w/v","0.90% w/v"],"a":0,"e":"Using the cryoscopic isotonicity formula: W (% w/v of adjusting substance) = (0.52 - a) / b, where a = freezing point depression of the unadjusted drug solution (0.12 C) and b = freezing point depression of 1% w/v NaCl (0.58 C). Substituting: W = (0.52 - 0.12) / 0.58 = 0.40 / 0.58 = 0.6897% w/v (~0.69% w/v, or 0.69 g NaCl per 100 mL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00458","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics II","topic":"Carr\u0027s Compressibility Index \u0026 Hausner Ratio","q":"A direct-compression tablet blend has an untapped bulk density (rho_b) of 0.40 g/cm3 and a tapped density (rho_t) of 0.50 g/cm3. Calculate Carr\u0027s Compressibility Index (%) and Hausner Ratio, and state the corresponding USP/IP flowability grade.","o":["Carr\u0027s Index = 25%; Hausner Ratio = 0.80 (Excellent flowability)","Carr\u0027s Index = 20%; Hausner Ratio = 1.25 (Fair flowability)","Carr\u0027s Index = 40%; Hausner Ratio = 1.67 (Very, very poor flowability)","Carr\u0027s Index = 10%; Hausner Ratio = 1.11 (Excellent flowability)"],"a":1,"e":"Carr\u0027s Compressibility Index = [(rho_t - rho_b) / rho_t] x 100 = [(0.50 - 0.40) / 0.50] x 100 = (0.10 / 0.50) x 100 = 20%. Hausner Ratio = rho_t / rho_b = 0.50 / 0.40 = 1.25. On the USP \u003c1174\u003e / IP powder flow scale: Carr\u0027s Index 16-20% and Hausner Ratio 1.19-1.25 correspond to \u0027Fair\u0027 flow (whereas \u003c=10% / 1.00-1.11 is Excellent, and \u003e38% / \u003e1.60 is Very, very poor).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00459","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy I","topic":"Sugar Coating Sequential Operations","q":"In traditional pharmaceutical sugar coating of tablet cores, match each sequential stage to its specific functional purpose: 1) Sealing (Waterproofing), 2) Subcoating, 3) Syruping (Smoothing), 4) Polishing.","o":["1-Apply film-forming HPMC spray; 2-Apply carnauba wax; 3-Apply shellac; 4-Dry tablet cores in fluid bed","1-Spray enteric polymer only; 2-Compress outer shell dry; 3-Laser-drill orifice; 4-Apply enteric seal","1-Apply shellac/cellulose acetate phthalate barrier against moisture ingress; 2-Build bulk and round sharp tablet edges using gelatin/acacia-sucrose/talc; 3-Cover imperfections and impart uniform colour with sucrose syrup; 4-Impart final gloss using carnauba wax or beeswax","1-Impart wax gloss; 2-Protect core from moisture; 3-Round tablet edges; 4-Remove colour spots"],"a":2,"e":"Conventional sugar coating proceeds in four classic stages: (1) Sealing — coating cores with hydrophobic polymers (shellac, zein, CAP) to prevent aqueous syrup from softening moisture-sensitive cores; (2) Subcoating — applying sticky binder syrup + dusting powder (talc, CaCO3) to round off edges and increase weight by 50-100%; (3) Syruping/Smoothing \u0026 Colouring — adding dilute sucrose/pigment syrups for smooth uniform colour; (4) Polishing — tumbling with carnauba/beeswax for high gloss.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00460","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy I","topic":"Bacterial Endotoxins Test (BET / LAL Test)","q":"For routine pyrogen quality control of large-volume parenterals (LVPs) and water for injection (WFI), the pharmacopoeial Bacterial Endotoxins Test (BET) has largely replaced the rabbit pyrogen test. What biological reagent and microbial component interact in the BET gel-clot assay?","o":["Trypsin digests Gram-positive peptidoglycan to release blue chromophores","Rabbit serum complement reacts with fungal ergosterol to precipitate IgM","Bacillus stearothermophilus spores ferment phenol red broth to turn yellow","Limulus Amebocyte Lysate (from horseshoe crab Limulus polyphemus amoebocytes) reacts specifically with Gram-negative bacterial lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) to activate a clotting cascade forming a firm gel"],"a":3,"e":"The Bacterial Endotoxins Test (LAL test) uses an aqueous extract of circulating amoebocytes from the Atlantic horseshoe crab (Limulus polyphemus) or Tachypleus species. Trace Gram-negative bacterial lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin lipid A) activates Factor C in the lysate, triggering a serine protease coagulation cascade that cleaves coagulogen into insoluble coagulin (forming a firm gel upon 180-degree tube inversion in the gel-clot method, or measurable turbidity/chromogenic colour).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00461","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry I","topic":"SAR of Sympathomimetic Phenylethylamines","q":"In the structure-activity relationship (SAR) of phenylethylamine sympathomimetics (compared with norepinephrine), how do removing one or both phenolic catechol hydroxyl groups (3,4-di-OH) and increasing steric bulk on the terminal amino nitrogen affect pharmacokinetics and receptor selectivity?","o":["Removing 3,4-catechol OH groups prevents rapid COMT inactivation enabling oral activity and greater lipophilicity/CNS penetration; increasing N-alkyl bulk (e.g., isopropyl in isoprenaline or tert-butyl in salbutamol) shifts selectivity from alpha toward beta receptors","Adding extra phenolic OH groups increases oral bioavailability and blood-brain barrier entry; N-methylation abolishes all beta-receptor binding","Removing catechol OH groups increases COMT affinity tenfold; N-tert-butylation produces pure alpha-1 vasoconstriction","Replacing the benzene ring with cyclohexane confers irreversible MAO-A inhibition"],"a":0,"e":"Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) methylates the 3-OH group of catecholamines (epinephrine, norepinephrine, isoprenaline), causing poor oral bioavailability and short half-life. Removing 4-OH (phenylephrine) or relocating 4-OH to 3,5-resorcinol (terbutaline) or 3-CH2OH (salbutamol) prevents COMT metabolism, imparting oral activity. Meanwhile, bulkier N-substituents (H -\u003e CH3 -\u003e isopropyl -\u003e tert-butyl) progressively reduce alpha-receptor affinity while enhancing beta-2 selectivity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00462","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry II","topic":"5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) Mechanism \u0026 Leucovorin Potentiation","q":"5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) is a fluorinated pyrimidine prodrug widely used in colorectal and gastric cancers, frequently co-administered with leucovorin (folinic acid). By what exact biochemical mechanism does its active metabolite (FdUMP) arrest DNA synthesis, and why does leucovorin potentiate its cytotoxicity?","o":["5-FU selectively blocks RNA polymerase I; leucovorin inhibits thymidine kinase","FdUMP forms a stable covalent ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and N5,N10-methylenetetrahydrofolate (suicide inhibition), blocking dUMP-to-dTMP conversion; leucovorin expands intracellular reduced folate pools to stabilise this ternary complex","5-FU inhibits ribonucleotide reductase purely via iron chelation; leucovorin reverses bone marrow toxicity completely","FdUMP directly alkylates guanine N7 bases in G0 resting cells; leucovorin inhibits hepatic dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase"],"a":1,"e":"Inside tumour cells, 5-FU is ribosylated/phosphorylated to 5-fluoro-2\u0027-deoxyuridine-5\u0027-monophosphate (FdUMP). Because the C-5 fluorine atom cannot be abstracted as a proton like hydrogen in dUMP, FdUMP locks thymidylate synthase (TS), the active-site cysteine, and the folate cofactor N5,N10-methylenetetrahydrofolate into an irreversible covalent ternary complex—depleting dTMP and causing \u0027thymineless death\u0027. Leucovorin (5-formyl-THF) increases intracellular N5,N10-methylene-THF, tightening FdUMP binding to TS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00463","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry III","topic":"Fluoroquinolone SAR \u0026 Metal Chelation","q":"In the medicinal chemistry of ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin, which pharmacophoric feature explains why co-administration with oral antacids, sucralfate, or iron/calcium supplements drastically reduces gastrointestinal fluoroquinolone absorption?","o":["The N-1 cyclopropyl ring oxidises dietary iron into insoluble ferric oxide","The C-7 piperazinyl nitrogen undergoes protonation by gastric antacids precipitating free base inside the liver","The coplanar C-3 carboxylic acid and C-4 keto oxygen atoms chelate polyvalent metal cations (Al3+, Mg2+, Ca2+, Fe2+, Zn2+) to form poorly soluble, non-absorbable complexes","The C-6 fluorine atom covalently reacts with aluminium hydroxide to precipitate fluoride gas"],"a":2,"e":"The essential 4-oxo-1,4-dihydroquinoline-3-carboxylic acid pharmacophore (specifically the C-3 carboxylate and adjacent C-4 carbonyl oxygens) acts as a bidentate ligand that strongly chelates divalent and trivalent cations (Mg2+, Al3+, Ca2+, Fe2+, Zn2+, Bi3+)—the same chelation property involved in Mg2+-bridged DNA gyrase/topoisomerase IV binding. Administering oral fluoroquinolones within 2 hours before or 4-6 hours after polyvalent cation supplements prevents therapeutic absorption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00464","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology I","topic":"G-Protein Coupled Receptors (GPCRs)","q":"Match each autonomic G-protein coupled receptor to its canonical G-alpha subunit and intracellular second-messenger cascade: 1) Beta-1 / Beta-2 adrenoceptor, 2) Alpha-2 / Muscarinic M2 receptor, 3) Alpha-1 / Muscarinic M3 receptor.","o":["1-Gi (decreases cAMP); 2-Gq (increases IP3/DAG); 3-Gs (increases cAMP)","1-Gq (increases IP3/DAG); 2-Gs (increases cAMP); 3-Gi (decreases cAMP)","1-Ligand-gated Na+ channel; 2-Tyrosine kinase receptor; 3-Nuclear transcription receptor","1-Gs (activates adenylyl cyclase -\u003e increases cAMP); 2-Gi (inhibits adenylyl cyclase -\u003e decreases cAMP); 3-Gq (activates phospholipase C-beta -\u003e increases IP3/DAG and intracellular Ca2+)"],"a":3,"e":"Autonomic GPCRs follow classic heterotrimeric G-protein coupling: Gs-coupled (beta-1, beta-2, beta-3, D1, H2) stimulate adenylyl cyclase elevating cAMP and PKA; Gi-coupled (alpha-2, M2, D2) inhibit adenylyl cyclase lowering cAMP (and open GIRK K+ channels in the heart via G-beta-gamma); Gq-coupled (alpha-1, M1, M3, H1, AT1) activate phospholipase C-beta, cleaving PIP2 into IP3 (releasing ER Ca2+) and DAG (activating PKC).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00465","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology III","topic":"Calcineurin Inhibitors (Cyclosporine \u0026 Tacrolimus)","q":"A 39-year-old renal transplant recipient maintained on cyclosporine develops rising serum creatinine, hypertension, gingival hyperplasia, and hypertrichosis. Which molecular immunophilin complex and downstream transcription step does cyclosporine inhibit in T-lymphocytes?","o":["Binds cyclophilin to inhibit calcineurin phosphatase, preventing dephosphorylation and nuclear translocation of NFAT and suppressing Interleukin-2 (IL-2) transcription","Binds FKBP-12 to inhibit mTOR kinase, arresting T-cell proliferation in G1-to-S phase without altering IL-2 transcription","Binds CD20 on mature B-lymphocytes to trigger complement-mediated lysis","Inhibits inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase (IMPDH) to block de novo guanosine synthesis"],"a":0,"e":"Cyclosporine binds intracellular cyclophilin (whereas tacrolimus binds FKBP-12), and the resulting drug-immunophilin complex inhibits the Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent phosphatase calcineurin. Blocking calcineurin prevents dephosphorylation and nuclear entry of Nuclear Factor of Activated T cells (NFAT), selectively suppressing IL-2 and other cytokine gene transcription without causing myelosuppression. Distinctive cyclosporine toxicities include afferent arteriolar nephrotoxicity, hypertension, hyperkalaemia, hirsutism, and gingival hyperplasia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00466","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry II","topic":"Acetate-Mevalonate vs Shikimic Acid Pathway","q":"In plant secondary metabolite biosynthesis, which metabolic pathway generates isopentenyl pyrophosphate (IPP) and dimethylallyl pyrophosphate (DMAPP) for terpenoids and steroidal sapogenins, versus aromatic amino acids (L-phenylalanine, L-tyrosine, L-tryptophan) for lignans, coumarins, and tannins?","o":["Citric acid cycle directly polymerises cinnamic acid into monoterpenes","Mevalonate (MVA) / MEP pathway generates IPP/DMAPP for terpenoids \u0026 steroids; Shikimic acid pathway generates aromatic amino acids, phenylpropanoids \u0026 hydrolysable tannins","Shikimic acid pathway generates IPP/DMAPP for steroids; Urea cycle generates phenylpropanoids","Glyoxylate cycle generates paclitaxel; Pentose phosphate pathway directly forms squalene"],"a":1,"e":"Two primary biosynthetic backbones dominate phytochemistry: (1) the Acetate-Mevalonate (cytosolic) and Methylerythritol Phosphate (plastidic MEP) pathways produce the C5 isoprene units IPP and DMAPP that condense into monoterpenes (C10), sesquiterpenes (C15), diterpenes (C20, e.g. taxol), triterpenes/steroids (C30 via squalene), and carotenoids (C40); (2) the Shikimic acid pathway (phosphoenolpyruvate + erythrose-4-phosphate -\u003e shikimate -\u003e chorismate) yields Phe, Tyr, Trp, gallic acid, coumarins, lignans, and flavonoids.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00467","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Microbiology","topic":"Rideal-Walker Phenol Coefficient Test","q":"In the classical Rideal-Walker test for evaluating the bactericidal potency of phenolic disinfectants, which standard test organism is exposed to serial dilutions of the test disinfectant and standard phenol at 2.5, 5, 7.5, and 10 minutes, and what does a phenol coefficient \u003e 1 signify?","o":["Candida albicans; a coefficient \u003e 1 indicates the disinfectant failed sterility","Escherichia coli bacteriophage T4; a coefficient \u003e 1 indicates bacteriostatic inhibition only","Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi (Salmonella typhi); a coefficient \u003e 1 indicates the test disinfectant is more potent than pure phenol under test conditions","Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores; a coefficient \u003e 1 indicates the disinfectant is weaker than phenol"],"a":2,"e":"The Rideal-Walker Phenol Coefficient compares the highest dilution of test disinfectant killing Salmonella typhi in 7.5 minutes (but not 5 minutes) against the corresponding dilution of phenol killing it in the same interval: Coefficient = (Dilution of test disinfectant) / (Dilution of phenol). Values \u003e 1 denote greater bactericidal activity than phenol (note: Chick-Martin test modifies this by adding 3% dried yeast/organic matter to simulate realistic soiling).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00468","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Biotechnology","topic":"Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)","q":"In a standard three-step Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) thermal cycle using thermostable Taq DNA polymerase (from Thermus aquaticus), what is the correct sequence of steps and approximate temperature ranges?","o":["Annealing (~37 C) -\u003e Denaturation (~72 C) -\u003e Extension (~98 C)","Extension (~25 C) -\u003e Ligation (~95 C) -\u003e Denaturation (~4 C)","Reverse Transcription (~95 C) -\u003e Translation (~72 C) -\u003e Annealing (~100 C)","Denaturation (~94-95 C) -\u003e Primer Annealing (~50-65 C) -\u003e Strand Extension (~72 C)"],"a":3,"e":"Each PCR cycle amplifies a target DNA segment exponentially across three temperature-controlled phases: (1) Denaturation at 94-95 C breaks hydrogen bonds separating dsDNA into single strands; (2) Annealing at 50-65 C (governed by primer Tm) allows forward/reverse oligonucleotide primers to hybridise specifically; (3) Extension at 72 C matches the optimal activity of thermostable Taq DNA polymerase to synthesise complementary strands from 5\u0027 to 3\u0027.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00469","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Engineering","topic":"Darcy\u0027s / Kozeny-Carman Law \u0026 Filter Aids","q":"During pressure filtration of a viscous botanical extract containing compressible gelatinous mucilage, filtrate flow drops rapidly to near zero because the compressible solids collapse and plug the filter cloth pores (blinding). Adding 1-2% diatomaceous earth (kieselguhr) as a filter aid restores rapid flow. Which parameter in the Kozeny-Carman filtration equation explains this improvement?","o":["Kieselguhr forms a rigid, incompressible porous lattice that increases filter-cake porosity (epsilon) and lowers specific cake resistance (alpha)","Kieselguhr chemically dissolves all mucilage polysaccharides into ethanol","Kieselguhr doubles filtrate viscosity (eta) to raise upstream pressure drop","Kieselguhr shrinks the effective surface area (A) of the filter press tenfold"],"a":0,"e":"By Darcy\u0027s and Kozeny-Carman equations, filtration rate (dV/dt) depends inversely on filtrate viscosity and specific cake resistance (alpha), and strongly on cake porosity (epsilon^3 / (1 - epsilon)^2). Compressible mucilaginous slimes deform under pressure, collapsing porosity and spiking resistance. Inert rigid filter aids (diatomaceous earth/kieselguhr, perlite, cellulose) keep pores open, preserving high porosity and permeable channels.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00470","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Instrumental Methods of Analysis","topic":"Functional Group \u0026 Polymorph Fingerprinting","q":"When confirming the identity and polymorphic form of an active pharmaceutical ingredient by Fourier-Transform Infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy (4000-400 cm^-1), which wavenumber region serves as the unique molecular/polymorph \u0027Fingerprint Region\u0027, and which bands identify a carboxylic acid O-H and C=O stretch respectively?","o":["Fingerprint Region = 2000-2500 cm^-1; O-H stretch at 1400 cm^-1 and C=O stretch at 3600 cm^-1","Fingerprint Region = 600-1500 cm^-1; broad H-bonded O-H stretch at 2500-3300 cm^-1 and sharp C=O stretch near 1700-1725 cm^-1","Fingerprint Region = 1800-2200 cm^-1; O-H stretch at 900 cm^-1 and C=O stretch at 1100 cm^-1","Fingerprint Region = 3500-4000 cm^-1; O-H stretch at 500 cm^-1 and C=O stretch at 2250 cm^-1"],"a":1,"e":"In mid-IR spectroscopy, 4000-1500 cm^-1 is the functional group region: carboxylic acids show a characteristically very broad hydrogen-bonded O-H stretching band spanning ~2500-3300 cm^-1 overlapping C-H stretches, plus an intense carbonyl C=O stretch around 1700-1725 cm^-1. Below 1500 cm^-1 (600-1500 cm^-1, the Fingerprint Region), complex coupled C-C, C-O, C-N, and skeletal bending vibrations generate a pattern uniquely diagnostic of the exact compound and crystal polymorph.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00471","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Absolute Oral Bioavailability (F) Calculation","q":"A healthy volunteer receives a 100 mg intravenous bolus dose of a drug yielding an AUC_0-inf of 100 mg*h/L, and on a separate occasion receives a 200 mg oral tablet of the same drug yielding an AUC_0-inf of 40 mg*h/L. What is the absolute oral bioavailability (F) of the tablet?","o":["0.80 (80%)","0.50 (50%)","0.20 (20%)","0.40 (40%)"],"a":2,"e":"Absolute bioavailability (F) compares systemic exposure (AUC_0-inf) of an extravascular dose against an intravenous reference (100% bioavailable), normalised for dose: F = (AUC_oral / Dose_oral) / (AUC_IV / Dose_IV) = (40 / 200) / (100 / 100) = 0.20 / 1.0 = 0.20 (20%).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00472","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry I","topic":"Diazepam IUPAC Structure \u0026 GABA-A Allosteric Mechanism","q":"Which IUPAC name corresponds to diazepam, and how does its molecular mechanism at the pentameric GABA-A receptor differ from phenobarbital?","o":["10,11-dihydro-5H-dibenzo[b,f]azepine-5-carboxamide; enhances glutamate NMDA currents","5-ethyl-5-phenylpyrimidine-2,4,6-trione; directly opens Cl- channels at high doses without GABA","2-(diethylamino)-N-(2,6-dimethylphenyl)acetamide; blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels","7-chloro-1,3-dihydro-1-methyl-5-phenyl-2H-1,4-benzodiazepin-2-one; increases the FREQUENCY of GABA-gated Cl- channel opening allosterically (requiring endogenous GABA)"],"a":3,"e":"Diazepam is 7-chloro-1,3-dihydro-1-methyl-5-phenyl-2H-1,4-benzodiazepin-2-one (an electron-withdrawing 7-chloro group on ring A and 5-phenyl ring C are vital for activity). Benzodiazepines bind at the alpha/gamma subunit interface of the GABA-A receptor, increasing affinity for GABA and increasing the FREQUENCY of Cl- channel openings (yielding a wider therapeutic safety ceiling than barbiturates, which prolong open-state DURATION and directly mimic GABA at high doses).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00473","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry I","topic":"Ibuprofen Stereochemistry \u0026 Aspirin COX Acetylation","q":"Ibuprofen [(RS)-2-(4-isobutylphenyl)propanoic acid] is marketed primarily as a racemate, whereas aspirin (2-acetoxybenzoic acid) inhibits cyclooxygenase irreversibly. Which stereochemical and mechanistic statement accurately describes these two NSAIDs?","o":["Only the (S)-(+)-enantiomer of ibuprofen potently inhibits COX, while ~50-60% of inactive (R)-(-)-ibuprofen undergoes unidirectional metabolic chiral inversion to (S)-(+) in vivo; aspirin irreversibly acetylates Ser530 in the COX active site","Only the (R)-(-)-enantiomer of ibuprofen is active; aspirin forms a reversible ionic salt with COX Arg120 only","Both enantiomers of ibuprofen are achiral pro-drugs converted to naproxen; aspirin methylates COX Cys813","Aspirin is a selective COX-2 diarylheterocycle with zero effect on platelet COX-1"],"a":0,"e":"In 2-arylpropionic acid (\u0027profen\u0027) NSAIDs, COX inhibitory activity resides in the (S)-(+)-enantiomer (dextro-ibuprofen). Uniquely, the inactive (R)-(-)-enantiomer undergoes stereospecific CoA-thioesterification, epimerisation, and hydrolysis in vivo—unidirectionally converting ~50-60% of (R)- to active (S)-ibuprofen. Aspirin is unique among NSAIDs in covalently transferring its acetyl group onto Ser530 (COX-1) / Ser516 (COX-2), permanently blocking arachidonic acid access.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00474","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry III","topic":"Ethambutol Stereochemistry \u0026 Molecular Target","q":"Ethambutol [(2S,2\u0027S)-2,2\u0027-(ethane-1,2-diyldiimino)di(butan-1-ol)] is a first-line antitubercular agent with strict stereochemical requirements. Which stereoisomer is clinically active, and which mycobacterial cell-wall enzyme does it inhibit?","o":["Dextrorotatory (S,S)-isomer is ~500 times more potent than the (R,R)-isomer; inhibits mycobacterial arabinosyl transferases (EmbA/EmbB/EmbC) blocking arabinogalactan synthesis","(R,R)-isomer is the sole active eutomer; inhibits mycolic acid InhA enoyl-ACP reductase","Meso-isomer is the sole active eutomer; inhibits mycobacterial RNA polymerase beta-subunit","Racemic mixture has zero stereoselectivity; inhibits peptidoglycan D-alanine racemase"],"a":0,"e":"Ethambutol contains two chiral centres: the dextrorotatory (+)-(2S,2\u0027S)-enantiomer possesses virtually all antimycobacterial potency (~12x more active than the meso form and ~200-500x more potent than the (R,R)-isomer, yet all isomers share ocular optic neuritis risk, hence pure (S,S)-ethambutol is used clinically). It inhibits arabinosyl transferases encoded by the embCAB operon, disrupting mycobacterial arabinogalactan and lipoarabinomannan cell-wall assembly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00475","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Elimination Rate Constant (Kel) \u0026 Half-Life (t1/2)","q":"Following a single intravenous bolus injection of a drug obeying one-compartment first-order kinetics, plasma concentration measures 40 mg/L at 2 hours and 10 mg/L at 6 hours post-dose. Calculate the elimination half-life (t1/2) and first-order elimination rate constant (k_el).","o":["t1/2 = 3.0 hours; k_el = 0.231 h^-1","t1/2 = 2.0 hours; k_el = 0.347 h^-1","t1/2 = 1.0 hour; k_el = 0.693 h^-1","t1/2 = 4.0 hours; k_el = 0.173 h^-1"],"a":1,"e":"From t = 2 h to t = 6 h (elapsed time = 4 hours), the plasma concentration drops from 40 mg/L -\u003e 20 mg/L -\u003e 10 mg/L, which is exactly 2 half-lives. Therefore, t1/2 = 4 hours / 2 = 2.0 hours. Using k_el = 0.693 / t1/2 = 0.693 / 2.0 = 0.3465 h^-1 (~0.347 h^-1). Alternatively: k_el = ln(40/10) / (6 - 2) = 1.386 / 4 = 0.3465 h^-1.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00476","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Linear Trapezoidal Rule for AUC","q":"After an intravenous bolus dose, plasma drug concentrations are measured as: C(0 h) = 20 mg/L, C(2 h) = 10 mg/L, and C(4 h) = 5 mg/L. Using the linear trapezoidal rule, what is the area under the plasma concentration-time curve from 0 to 4 hours (AUC_0-4h)?","o":["35 mg*h/L","22.5 mg*h/L","45 mg*h/L","60 mg*h/L"],"a":2,"e":"Linear trapezoidal rule sums each time interval area = 0.5 x (C_1 + C_2) x (t_2 - t_1): For 0 to 2 h: 0.5 x (20 + 10) x 2 = 30 mg*h/L. For 2 to 4 h: 0.5 x (10 + 5) x 2 = 15 mg*h/L. Total AUC_0-4h = 30 + 15 = 45 mg*h/L.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00477","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Steady-State Infusion Rate (R0) \u0026 Time to Steady State","q":"A patient requires a constant-rate intravenous infusion of a drug with total systemic clearance (CL) of 4.0 L/h and elimination half-life (t1/2) of 3.0 hours to maintain a steady-state plasma concentration (C_ss) of 15 mg/L. What zero-order infusion rate (R_0) is required, and approximately how long does it take to reach 90% of C_ss without a loading dose?","o":["R_0 = 30 mg/h; ~6 hours (2 x t1/2)","R_0 = 180 mg/h; ~24 hours","R_0 = 3.75 mg/h; ~3 hours (1 x t1/2)","R_0 = 60 mg/h; ~10 hours (3.32 x t1/2)"],"a":3,"e":"At steady state, rate in equals rate out: R_0 = C_ss x CL = 15 mg/L x 4.0 L/h = 60 mg/h. Because approach to steady state depends solely on t1/2, reaching 50% takes 1 t1/2 (3 h), 75% takes 2 t1/2 (6 h), 90% takes 3.32 x t1/2 = 3.32 x 3.0 = 9.96 hours (~10 hours), and \u003e97% takes ~5 half-lives (15 h).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00478","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy I","topic":"Leaker Test \u0026 Sub-Visible Particulate Matter","q":"In routine parenteral quality control of flame-sealed glass ampoules and clear injectable solutions per Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP), how are hairline seal leaks detected in ampoules, and which two non-biological tests quantify sub-visible particulate matter (\u003e=10 micron and \u003e=25 micron)?","o":["Submerge ampoules in 0.5-1% methylene blue dye bath under vacuum (dye enters cracked ampoules on vacuum release); quantify sub-visible particulates via Light Obscuration Particle Count Test (Method 1) and Microscopic Particle Count Test (Method 2)","Submerge ampoules in concentrated nitric acid; measure particulates via Karl Fischer titration","Centrifuge ampoules at 10,000 rpm; measure particulates via refractive index","Heat ampoules at 250 C in olive oil; quantify particulates solely by naked-eye inspection against a white wall"],"a":0,"e":"Ampoule seal integrity (leaker test) places sealed ampoules in a 0.5-1% methylene blue solution inside a vacuum chamber (~25 in Hg); upon returning to atmospheric pressure, external blue dye is forced into any pinhole/microcrack. Sub-visible particulate contamination (extraneous particles \u003e=10 micrometres and \u003e=25 micrometres) is evaluated per IP/USP \u003c788\u003e by automated Light Obscuration (HIAC) as Method 1, or Microscopic membrane filtration counting as Method 2 (mandatory for emulsions/high-viscosity injections).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00479","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy I","topic":"Pharmacopoeial Glass Container Types","q":"According to Indian Pharmacopoeia / USP glass container standards for pharmaceutical packaging, match each glass type to its composition and permitted parenteral/non-parenteral use: 1) Type I, 2) Type II, 3) Type III, 4) Type NP.","o":["1-General-purpose soda-lime for oral tablets; 2-Borosilicate for all alkaline IVs; 3-Plastic HDPE bottle; 4-Surface-treated glass for buffered injectables","1-Highly resistant borosilicate glass for buffered/unbuffered aqueous parenterals; 2-Surface-treated (de-alkalised SO2) soda-lime glass for acidic/neutral parenterals; 3-Untreated soda-lime glass for dry powders/non-aqueous injectables; 4-General-purpose soda-lime glass for oral/topical non-parenterals only","1-Untreated soda-lime for aqueous IVs; 2-Amber borosilicate for light-sensitive oral syrups only; 3-Type NP; 4-Type I","1-Polyvinyl chloride; 2-Borosilicate; 3-Polypropylene; 4-Treated soda-lime"],"a":1,"e":"Pharmacopoeial glass classification by hydrolytic (alkali-leaching) resistance: Type I (neutral borosilicate glass, lowest thermal expansion and minimal alkali release—suitable for all aqueous/alkaline/buffered parenterals and repeated autoclaving); Type II (soda-lime glass surface-treated with sulphur dioxide/ammonium sulphate to neutralise surface Na2O—suitable for acidic and neutral aqueous parenterals); Type III (regular soda-lime glass—only for anhydrous/oily vehicles or dry sterile powders); Type NP (non-parenteral soda-lime glass for oral/topical dosage forms).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00480","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry II","topic":"TLC Retention Factor (Rf) \u0026 Visualisation Reagents","q":"During thin-layer chromatography (TLC) of a Strychnos nux-vomica seed extract on silica gel G, the solvent front migrates 10.0 cm from the origin and a strychnine spot migrates 6.0 cm from the origin. What is the Rf value of strychnine, and which chromogenic spray reagent detects alkaloids as orange-red zones?","o":["Rf = 1.67; Ninhydrin reagent","Rf = 6.00; Biuret alkaline copper reagent","Rf = 0.60; Dragendorff\u0027s reagent (potassium bismuth iodide)","Rf = 0.40; Molisch\u0027s alpha-naphthol reagent"],"a":2,"e":"Retention factor Rf = (Distance moved by analyte spot from origin) / (Distance moved by solvent front from origin) = 6.0 cm / 10.0 cm = 0.60 (Rf is always between 0 and 1). Dragendorff\u0027s reagent (potassium bismuth iodide) is the universal TLC spray reagent for nitrogenous alkaloids, producing bright orange to reddish-brown spots on yellow background.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00481","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry II","topic":"Artemisinin Phytochemistry \u0026 Pharmacophore","q":"Artemisinin (isolated from Artemisia annua) and its semisynthetic derivatives (artesunate, artemether) rapidly reduce Plasmodium falciparum parasitaemia even in chloroquine-resistant strains. Which unique chemical moiety in artemisinin is essential for its antimalarial free-radical mechanism?","o":["A 4-aminoquinoline ring that blocks haem polymerase solely via basic vacuole trapping","A naphthoquinone ring mimicking ubiquinone at parasite cytochrome bc1","A biguanide moiety inhibiting plasmodial dihydrofolate reductase","An internal 1,2,4-trioxane endoperoxide (-O-O-) bridge within a sesquiterpene lactone skeleton, cleaved by intraparasitic haem Fe2+ to generate lethal carbon-centred radicals"],"a":3,"e":"Artemisinin is a cadinane-type sesquiterpene lactone distinguished by an unusual 1,2,4-trioxane endoperoxide bridge. Inside infected erythrocytes, ferrous iron (Fe2+) in digested haemoglobin haem cleaves this endoperoxide bond, generating reactive oxygen species and carbon-centred free radicals that alkylate vital parasite proteins and membranes. Replacing the peroxide with an ether (deoxyartemisinin) completely abolishes activity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00482","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Engineering","topic":"Shell-and-Tube / Double-Pipe Heat Exchangers","q":"When cooling a hot parenteral bulk solution in a pharmaceutical double-pipe or shell-and-tube heat exchanger, why is counter-current flow almost universally preferred over parallel (co-current) flow?","o":["Counter-current flow maintains a more uniform temperature gradient along the exchanger length, yielding a higher Log Mean Temperature Difference (LMTD) and allowing the cold outlet fluid to exceed the hot outlet temperature","Co-current flow prevents Fourier conduction through stainless-steel tube walls","Co-current flow produces a higher LMTD and requires half the heat-transfer surface area","Counter-current flow eliminates convective boundary layers by stopping fluid turbulence completely"],"a":0,"e":"By Q = U * A * (LMTD), for identical inlet/outlet terminal temperatures, counter-current flow (hot and cold streams entering opposite ends) yields a higher Log Mean Temperature Difference (LMTD) than co-current (parallel) flow—meaning a smaller heat-transfer area (A) is needed for the same heat duty. Moreover, counter-current operation permits closer temperature cross-over (cold fluid exiting warmer than hot fluid exits), which is impossible in parallel flow.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00483","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Engineering","topic":"Reynolds Number (Re) \u0026 Flow Regimes","q":"In pharmaceutical fluid mechanics and Clean-in-Place (CIP) sanitary piping design, the dimensionless Reynolds number [Re = (D * v * rho) / mu] predicts the transition between laminar and turbulent flow in circular pipes. Which Reynolds number thresholds define laminar versus fully turbulent pipe flow?","o":["Re \u003c 100 indicates turbulent flow; Re \u003e 10,000 indicates laminar flow","Re \u003c 2,100 indicates laminar (viscous/streamline) flow; Re \u003e 4,000 indicates fully turbulent flow (essential for effective CIP scrubbing and rapid heat/mass transfer)","Re = 0 indicates sonic supersonic flow; Re = 100 indicates cavitation","Re depends exclusively on pipe colour and surface gloss without viscosity"],"a":1,"e":"Reynolds number represents the ratio of inertial forces (D*v*rho) to viscous forces (mu). In smooth circular pipes, Re \u003c 2,100 produces orderly parabolic laminar flow with a stagnant wall boundary layer; 2,100 \u003c= Re \u003c= 4,000 is unstable transitional flow; Re \u003e 4,000 guarantees turbulent eddy mixing, thinning the boundary layer to maximise convective heat/mass transfer and CIP sanitisation of WFI/product lines.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00484","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Engineering","topic":"Wet-Bulb Depression \u0026 Tray Drying Rate","q":"During the constant-rate period of wet tablet-granule drying in a tray dryer, the psychrometer shows that the inlet air\u0027s wet-bulb temperature equals its dry-bulb temperature (wet-bulb depression = 0 C). What does this indicate about the drying air, and what happens to moisture evaporation from the wet granules?","o":["Air velocity has exceeded Mach 1, causing adiabatic flash sublimation","The granules are below their critical moisture content in the second falling-rate period","The air is 100% saturated with water vapour (100% relative humidity); zero vapour-pressure driving force exists, so net evaporation from the granules drops to zero","The air has 0% relative humidity; evaporation rate accelerates infinitely"],"a":2,"e":"Wet-bulb depression (T_dry - T_wet) is directly proportional to the humidity driving force (H_s - H_air) during constant-rate surface evaporation. When air reaches 100% relative humidity (saturation), no further water can evaporate from the wetted thermometer wick, so T_wet equals T_dry (zero depression) and drying ceases unless the air is heated or dehumidified.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00485","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Low-Dose Aspirin Antiplatelet Kinetics \u0026 Reye Syndrome","q":"Why does a single daily low dose of aspirin (75-150 mg) selectively and persistently inhibit platelet aggregation for up to 7-10 days, while aspirin is strictly contraindicated for fever in children with influenza or varicella?","o":["Platelets have nuclear DNA that amplifies aspirin toxicity; in children, aspirin causes acute renal Fanconi syndrome only","Aspirin has a plasma elimination half-life of 10 days; in children it causes grey-baby syndrome","Aspirin selectively inhibits platelet P2Y12 receptors permanently; in children it causes dental fluorosis","Anucleate platelets cannot synthesise new COX-1 protein once acetylated in portal blood (requiring 7-10 days of bone marrow megakaryocyte turnover), whereas endothelial cells rapidly resynthesise COX for PGI2; in children with viral infections, aspirin triggers Reye syndrome (acute hepatic encephalopathy)"],"a":3,"e":"Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1 on platelets in the presystemic portal circulation before hepatic deacetylation. Because circulating platelets lack nuclei and cannot transcribe new COX-1, thromboxane A2 (TXA2) synthesis remains suppressed for the 7-10 day platelet lifespan, whereas nucleated vascular endothelial cells regenerate COX-1/COX-2 within hours to maintain prostacyclin (PGI2). In children/adolescents with viral illnesses (influenza, varicella), salicylates are strongly associated with Reye syndrome (mitochondrial injury causing fulminant fatty liver and cerebral oedema).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00486","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Biotechnology","topic":"Recombinant Human Insulin Production","q":"In industrial biomanufacturing of recombinant human insulin (Humulin) using Escherichia coli expression vectors, why is the expressed fusion/proinsulin polypeptide initially recovered from insoluble intracellular inclusion bodies and treated sequentially with refolding buffers and trypsin/carboxypeptidase B?","o":["Overexpressed human proinsulin forms misfolded insoluble inclusion bodies in E. coli cytoplasm; solubilisation, oxidative disulfide refolding (correct A6-A11, A7-B7, A20-B19 bridges), and enzymatic cleavage remove the C-peptide/leader sequence to yield bioactive heterodimeric human insulin","Trypsin digests the A and B chains completely so only free C-peptide is formulated","E. coli naturally secretes glycosylated hexameric insulin directly into fermentation broth requiring no folding","Carboxypeptidase B converts human insulin into porcine insulin by adding alanine at B30"],"a":0,"e":"High-level recombinant expression in E. coli reducing cytoplasm prevents disulfide bond formation, aggregating proinsulin fusion protein into dense refractile inclusion bodies. Downstream processing isolates and washes inclusion bodies, solubilises them in chaotropic agents (urea/guanidine), performs controlled oxidative refolding to form the three native disulfide bridges, and uses trypsin + carboxypeptidase B to excise the connecting C-peptide cleanly before chromatographic purification.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00487","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics I","topic":"Critical Micelle Concentration (CMC) \u0026 Gibbs Adsorption","q":"As an ionic surfactant such as sodium lauryl sulphate (SLS) is progressively added to pure water, surface tension drops steeply at low concentrations and then plateaus sharply at a specific threshold concentration (Critical Micelle Concentration, CMC). Beyond the CMC, how do interfacial and bulk solution properties behave?","o":["Monomer concentration at the air-water surface saturates so surface tension stays nearly constant, molar conductivity decreases abruptly, osmotic pressure plateaus, and hydrophobic drug solubilisation rises sharply inside micellar cores","Surface tension drops linearly to zero while micelles precipitate as crystals","Molar conductivity jumps tenfold upward as micelles form","Hydrophobic drug solubility drops to zero above the CMC"],"a":0,"e":"Below the CMC, amphiphilic surfactant molecules adsorb at the air-water interface, lowering surface tension per the Gibbs adsorption equation. Once the interface saturates at the CMC, additional surfactant monomers self-assemble into colloidal micelles: surface tension flattens, equivalent conductivity declines (due to counter-ion binding and bulky slower micelles), colligative osmotic pressure levels off, turbidity increases slightly, and apparent solubility of lipophilic drugs jumps sharply via micellar solubilisation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00488","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics II","topic":"First-Order Shelf-Life (t90) Calculation","q":"An aqueous injectable formulation degrades by first-order kinetics at 25 C with an observed degradation rate constant k = 0.00105 day^-1. Defining shelf-life (t_90) as the time required for potency to fall to 90% of initial concentration, what is the predicted shelf-life of this product at 25 C?","o":["95 days","100 days","500 days","660 days"],"a":1,"e":"For a first-order degradation reaction: ln(C_0 / C) = k * t. At 90% remaining potency (10% degradation), C = 0.90 * C_0, so t_90 = ln(1 / 0.90) / k = 0.10536 / k (commonly approximated as 0.105 / k). Substituting k = 0.00105 day^-1: t_90 = 0.105 / 0.00105 = 100 days.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00489","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy I","topic":"IP Friability Test \u0026 Weight Variation Limits","q":"When performing the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) friability test on uncoated 300-mg tablets using a Roche friabilator, what are the standard rotational speed, total revolutions, and maximum acceptable percentage weight loss—and what is the IP percentage weight variation limit for uncoated tablets weighing \u003e= 250 mg?","o":["100 rpm for 1 minute, maximum weight loss \u003c= 5%; weight variation limit +/- 10%","50 rpm for 10 minutes (500 revolutions), maximum weight loss \u003c= 10%; weight variation limit +/- 15%","25 rpm for 4 minutes (100 revolutions), maximum weight loss \u003c= 1.0% (with zero cracked/broken tablets); IP weight variation limit for \u003e= 250 mg tablets is +/- 5%","25 rpm for 4 minutes, maximum weight loss \u003c= 0.01%; weight variation limit +/- 1%"],"a":2,"e":"Per IP/USP \u003c1216\u003e, the Roche friabilator drops dedusted tablets 6 inches per revolution at 25 +/- 1 rpm for 4 minutes (100 drops total); weight loss must not exceed 1.0% and no capped/split/broken tablets are permitted (if sample \u003e= 650 mg tablet weight, test 10 tablets; otherwise ~6.5 g total weight). Under IP Uniformity of Weight for uncoated/film-coated tablets: \u003c=80 mg allows +/-10%, \u003e80 to \u003c250 mg allows +/-7.5%, and \u003e=250 mg allows +/-5% (no more than 2 of 20 tablets may deviate beyond limit, none by \u003e2x limit).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00490","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy I","topic":"Propellant Blend Vapour Pressure Calculation","q":"A pharmaceutical aerosol propellant blend consists of an equimolar (50:50 mole fraction, X_A = 0.50, X_B = 0.50) mixture of Propellant A (pure vapour pressure P_A^0 = 60 psia at 21 C) and Propellant B (pure vapour pressure P_B^0 = 20 psia at 21 C). Assuming ideal behaviour obeying Raoult\u0027s law, what is the total vapour pressure of the blend at 21 C?","o":["20 psia","80 psia","30 psia","40 psia"],"a":3,"e":"By Raoult\u0027s and Dalton\u0027s laws for an ideal liquefied propellant mixture: P_total = (X_A x P_A^0) + (X_B x P_B^0) = (0.50 x 60 psia) + (0.50 x 20 psia) = 30 + 10 = 40 psia (note: gauge pressure psig = psia - 14.7 psi). Formulators blend higher- and lower-vapour-pressure HFAs (e.g., HFA-134a and HFA-227) to tailor spray droplet size and canister pressure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00491","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry I","topic":"SAR of Phenothiazine Neuroleptics (Chlorpromazine)","q":"In the structure-activity relationship (SAR) of tricyclic phenothiazines, promethazine possesses a branched 2-carbon chain between N-10 and the terminal amine and acts as a sedating H1-antihistamine, whereas chlorpromazine and fluphenazine act as potent D2-blocking antipsychotics. Which two structural features convert a phenothiazine into a potent neuroleptic?","o":["An unbranched 3-carbon (propylene) linker between the ring N-10 and terminal tertiary amine, PLUS an electron-withdrawing substituent at the C-2 position (e.g., -Cl, -CF3)","A 1-carbon methylene bridge at N-10 and an electron-donating methoxy group at C-4","Oxidation of the ring sulphur to a sulfone and quaternisation of the terminal nitrogen","Removal of both aromatic benzene rings leaving a single piperidine core"],"a":0,"e":"Classic phenothiazine antipsychotic SAR demonstrates: (1) an exact 3-carbon chain separating N-10 from the basic amino nitrogen is required to match the dopamine pharmacophore (2-carbon chains give antihistaminic/anticholinergic promethazine/diethazine); (2) an electron-withdrawing group at C-2 (-CF3 \u003e -Cl \u003e -COCH3 \u003e -H) asymmetrically tilts the side chain toward the substituted ring, markedly enhancing D2 receptor affinity (note C-1 or C-4 substitution decreases activity).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00492","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry II","topic":"SAR of Thiazide \u0026 Loop Sulfonamide Diuretics","q":"Comparing chlorothiazide with hydrochlorothiazide and furosemide, which pharmacophoric moiety is mandatory for renal NCC/NKCC2 diuretic activity across benzothiadiazine and sulfamoylbenzoate diuretics, and how does saturating the C-3=C-4 double bond affect thiazide potency?","o":["An unsubstituted primary C-7 sulfamoyl (-SO2NH2) group (with an adjacent C-6 electron-withdrawing -Cl or -CF3) is essential; saturating the C-3=C-4 bond to form hydrochlorothiazide increases natriuretic potency ~10-fold","Disubstitution of the sulfamoyl nitrogen with bulky benzyl groups triples potency; saturating C-3=C-4 abolishes all diuresis","The aromatic benzene ring must be completely hydrogenated to cyclohexane for activity","Replacing the sulfamoyl group with a nitro group at C-2 creates loop diuretic potency"],"a":0,"e":"In thiazide and sulfamoyl loop diuretics, a free unsubstituted -SO2NH2 group at C-7 (ortho to an activating electron-withdrawing -Cl or -CF3 at C-6) is indispensable for diuretic activity. Reduction of the C-3=C-4 double bond of chlorothiazide yields dihydrobenzothiadiazine (hydrochlorothiazide), increasing lipophilicity and potency roughly 10-fold (and lipophilic C-3 substituents like bendroflumethiazide boost potency and duration further).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00493","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry II","topic":"Amino-Ester (Procaine) vs Amino-Amide (Lidocaine) SAR","q":"Why does the amino-amide local anaesthetic lidocaine resist heat sterilisation, exhibit a longer duration of action, and cause far fewer allergic reactions than the amino-ester local anaesthetic procaine?","o":["Lidocaine is permanently uncharged at all pH values; procaine covalently cross-links voltage-gated K+ channels","The amide linker is far more resistant to hydrolysis than an ester, two ortho-methyl (2,6-xylidine) groups sterically shield the amide carbonyl against hepatic amidase attack, and lidocaine is NOT metabolised to allergenic para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA)","Lidocaine contains a PABA ring that blocks IgE receptors; procaine is metabolised purely by CYP2D6 in hair follicles","Procaine contains two ortho-methyl groups while lidocaine is an unstable choline ester"],"a":1,"e":"Amino-ester local anaesthetics (procaine, tetracaine, chloroprocaine) are rapidly hydrolysed by plasma pseudocholinesterase (butyrylcholinesterase) liberating para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA), a frequent sensitiser/allergen, and hydrolyse easily upon autoclaving. Amino-amides (lidocaine, bupivacaine, mepivacaine) replace the ester with an intrinsically stable amide bond further shielded by 2,6-dimethyl substitution on the phenyl ring, requiring slower hepatic CYP/amidase metabolism and generating zero PABA.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00494","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry III","topic":"Acyclovir Selective Bioactivation \u0026 Chain Termination","q":"Acyclovir (9-[(2-hydroxyethoxy)methyl]guanine) displays extraordinary selectivity for Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) and Varicella-Zoster Virus (VZV)-infected cells over uninfected host cells. Which two biochemical features account for its selective activation and viral DNA chain termination?","o":["It inhibits neuraminidase sialic acid cleavage on HSV envelopes","It is activated exclusively by host mitochondrial RNA polymerase and cross-links double-stranded RNA","Initial monophosphorylation depends selectively on viral thymidine kinase (hundreds of times higher affinity than host kinase), and acyclovir triphosphate lacks a 3\u0027-hydroxyl (-OH) sugar group causing obligate viral DNA chain termination","It requires HIV reverse transcriptase cleavage of an ether ring to release free guanine"],"a":2,"e":"Acyclovir is an acyclic guanosine analogue that remains inert in uninfected cells because host cellular kinases phosphorylate it negligibly. In HSV/VZV-infected cells, virus-encoded thymidine kinase converts acyclovir to acyclovir monophosphate, which host GMP kinase and nucleoside diphosphate kinase then convert to active acyclovir triphosphate. Acyclovir triphosphate preferentially inhibits viral DNA polymerase and—lacking a cyclic ribose 3\u0027-OH group—halts further 3\u0027-5\u0027 phosphodiester elongation (chain termination).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00495","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology I","topic":"Phase II Glucuronidation \u0026 Neonatal Grey-Baby Syndrome","q":"Premature neonates administered adult weight-scaled doses of chloramphenicol can develop \u0027Grey-Baby Syndrome\u0027 (abdominal distension, vomiting, ashen-grey cyanosis, hypothermia, and cardiovascular collapse). Immature development of which hepatic Phase II drug-metabolising enzyme system directly causes chloramphenicol accumulation in neonates?","o":["Plasma pseudocholinesterase ester hydrolysis","Erythrocyte catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT)","Hepatic CYP2D6 oxidative N-demethylation","UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT) catalysing glucuronide conjugation (compounded by immature neonatal glomerular filtration)"],"a":3,"e":"Chloramphenicol is normally inactivated primarily via hepatic Phase II glucuronidation by UDP-glucuronosyltransferases (UGT2B7/UGT1A) and excreted renally. Neonates—especially preterm infants—have severely deficient hepatic UGT activity (the same immaturity causing neonatal unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia) and low GFR, causing free chloramphenicol to accumulate, inhibit mitochondrial protein synthesis/electron transport, and trigger fatal Grey-Baby Syndrome.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00496","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Bisphosphonates (Alendronate) in Osteoporosis","q":"A 64-year-old postmenopausal woman with osteoporosis (T-score -2.8) is prescribed oral alendronate 70 mg once weekly. What is alendronate\u0027s molecular mechanism in osteoclasts, and why must she swallow it with a full glass of plain water \u003e=30 minutes before breakfast and remain strictly upright for at least 30 minutes?","o":["Inhibits osteoclast farnesyl pyrophosphate (FPP) synthase in the mevalonate pathway inducing osteoclast apoptosis; \u003c1% oral bioavailability is abolished by food/mineral water, and upright posture prevents corrosive pill-induced oesophagitis","Inhibits parathyroid hormone synthesis in chief cells; lying supine speeds colonic absorption","Chelates serum vitamin D3 in stomach lumen unless taken with milk","Directly activates osteoblast RANKL receptors; food turns alendronate into insoluble tetracycline"],"a":0,"e":"Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, zoledronate) bind hydroxyapatite at active bone resorption sites and are endocytosed by osteoclasts, where they inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate (FPP) synthase—blocking prenylation of small GTPases (Rho/Rac) and triggering osteoclast apoptosis. Because oral bioavailability is \u003c1% and completely abolished by calcium/food/coffee/juice, tablets must be taken on an empty stomach with 200 mL plain water \u003e=30 min before food/other drugs, remaining upright (sitting/standing) \u003e=30 min to prevent oesophageal ulceration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00497","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology III","topic":"Bedaquiline in NTEP All-Oral MDR-TB Regimens","q":"Bedaquiline is a core bactericidal component of India\u0027s NTEP all-oral shorter and longer regimens for multidrug-resistant tuberculosis (MDR-TB). What unique mycobacterial target does bedaquiline inhibit, and which cardiac safety parameter requires regular ECG monitoring during therapy?","o":["Inhibits mycobacterial beta-lactamase; requires monitoring for optic neuritis solely","Inhibits mycobacterial F1F0-ATP synthase (proton pump), depleting bacterial ATP; requires serial ECG monitoring for QTc interval prolongation (especially when combined with clofazimine/delamanid/moxifloxacin)","Blocks mycolic acid FAS-II synthase; causes irreversible red-orange urine only","Inhibits 50S ribosomal translocation; requires monitoring for serum uric acid only"],"a":1,"e":"Bedaquiline (a diarylquinoline) selectively targets the c-subunit of mycobacterial F1F0-ATP synthase, halting ATP generation in both actively replicating and dormant Mycobacterium tuberculosis bacilli, with an extremely long terminal half-life (~5.5 months). Major safety precautions under NTEP include baseline and serial ECG QTcF monitoring (additive risk with fluoroquinolones, clofazimine, delamanid) and hepatic transaminase checks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00498","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry I","topic":"Organogenesis \u0026 Hormone Ratios (MS Medium)","q":"In medicinal plant micropropagation using Murashige and Skoog (MS) basal nutrient medium, how does adjusting the ratio of auxin (e.g., NAA/IAA) to cytokinin (e.g., BAP/kinetin) direct explant morphogenesis?","o":["High auxin-to-cytokinin induces exclusively flowers; high cytokinin induces lignified bark only","Abscisic acid maximises rapid shoot multiplication across all explants","High auxin-to-cytokinin ratio induces root formation (rhizogenesis); high cytokinin-to-auxin ratio induces shoot proliferation (caulogenesis); balanced intermediate levels sustain undifferentiated callus growth","Gibberellic acid alone replaces sucrose as the sole carbon energy source"],"a":2,"e":"Skoog and Miller\u0027s classic hormonal control principle in plant tissue culture states that organogenesis depends on the auxin-to-cytokinin balance: an excess of auxin relative to cytokinin promotes adventitious rooting, an excess of cytokinin relative to auxin promotes shoot bud differentiation, and roughly equal/moderate concentrations maintain unorganised proliferating callus biomass (which can then be transferred to bioreactors for secondary metabolite production).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00499","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry II","topic":"Reserpine Mechanism \u0026 Clinical Adverse Effect","q":"Reserpine (an indole alkaloid isolated from Indian snakeroot, Rauwolfia serpentina) lowers systemic blood pressure via central and peripheral sympathetic depletion. Which vesicular transporter does reserpine irreversibly inhibit, and what major neuropsychiatric adverse effect limits its clinical use?","o":["Stimulates postsynaptic alpha-2 receptors exclusively; causes acute mania and insomnia","Blocks angiotensin II AT1 receptors; causes dry cough and angioedema","Inhibits catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT); causes severe hypertensive crisis","Irreversibly blocks Vesicular Monoamine Transporter-2 (VMAT-2), preventing vesicular storage and depleting norepinephrine, dopamine, and serotonin; can precipitate severe suicidal depression and extrapyramidal symptoms"],"a":3,"e":"Reserpine binds tightly and essentially irreversibly to VMAT-2 on presynaptic storage vesicles, exposing cytoplasmic norepinephrine, dopamine, and serotonin to rapid degradation by mitochondrial MAO. Depletion of peripheral sympathetic norepinephrine reduces vascular tone/BP, while central depletion of serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine induces lethargy, severe depression (with suicide risk), nasal congestion, and parkinsonian features.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00500","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Microbiology","topic":"Decimal Reduction Time (D-Value) \u0026 SAL Calculation","q":"A pre-sterilisation parenteral vial batch has a measured bioburden of 10^4 Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores per vial, with a decimal reduction time at 121 C (D_121) of 1.5 minutes. What minimum holding time at 121 C is required to achieve the pharmacopoeial Sterility Assurance Level (SAL) of 10^-6 (less than one non-sterile unit in a million)?","o":["15.0 minutes","1.5 minutes","6.0 minutes","9.0 minutes"],"a":0,"e":"Achieving an SAL of 10^-6 starting from an initial bioburden of N_0 = 10^4 spores requires log10(N_0 / N_t) = log10(10^4 / 10^-6) = 10 decimal (log10) reductions. Since each 1-log10 (90%) reduction requires one D-value (1.5 min), minimum exposure time = 10 x 1.5 min = 15.0 minutes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00501","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Engineering","topic":"Powder \u0026 Semisolid Mixing Equipment","q":"In solid and semisolid pharmaceutical manufacturing, how is an Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) sieve number defined, and which pair of mixers is selected respectively for gentle blending of fragile lubricated tablet granules versus high-shear kneading of stiff wet granulation masses/ointments?","o":["IP sieve number equals wire thickness in millimetres; colloid mill for dry capsule powders","IP sieve number equals the number of meshes per linear inch (25.4 mm); V-cone / double-cone tumbling blender for fragile tablet granules, and Sigma-blade mixer for stiff wet masses/ointments","IP sieve number equals aperture diameter in centimetres; Sigma-blade mixer for fragile dry granules, and V-cone blender for stiff pastes","IP sieve number increases as mesh aperture size gets larger; hammer mill for ointment blending"],"a":1,"e":"IP sieve numbers designate the number of wire-mesh openings per linear inch (25.4 mm)—thus larger sieve numbers indicate finer apertures (e.g., Sieve No. 10 = coarse ~1.7 mm vs Sieve No. 80 = fine ~180 microns). Free-flowing, friable tablet granules/lubricants are blended gently by convective tumbling in V-cone, double-cone, or octagonal blenders without attrition, whereas cohesive wet granulating masses and stiff pastes/ointments require heavy kneading/shearing in a Sigma-blade (double-arm) mixer.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00502","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Instrumental Methods of Analysis","topic":"Beer-Lambert Law Quantitative Assay","q":"A pharmaceutical analyte with molar absorptivity (epsilon) = 12,000 L/(mol*cm) at lambda_max 272 nm is placed in a 1.0 cm quartz cuvette and yields a UV absorbance (A) of 0.60. Assuming Beer-Lambert linearity, what is the molar concentration of the solution?","o":["7.2 x 10^3 mol/L","2.0 x 10^-4 mol/L (200 micromol/L)","5.0 x 10^-5 mol/L (50 micromol/L)","5.0 x 10^-3 mol/L"],"a":2,"e":"By the Beer-Lambert law: A = epsilon x b x c, where A = 0.60, epsilon = 12,000 L/(mol*cm), and path length b = 1.0 cm. Rearranging: c = A / (epsilon x b) = 0.60 / (12,000 x 1.0) = 0.00005 mol/L = 5.0 x 10^-5 mol/L (50 micromol/L).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00503","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Instrumental Methods of Analysis","topic":"Spin-Spin Coupling (n + 1 Rule)","q":"In the 1H-NMR spectrum of benzocaine (ethyl 4-aminobenzoate) recorded in CDCl3, what characteristic spin-spin splitting multiplicities and relative integration areas identify the ethyl ester (-O-CH2-CH3) group?","o":["A downfield 3H singlet and an upfield 2H doublet","A 2H triplet at 4.3 ppm and a 3H quartet at 1.3 ppm","A single unsplit 5H broad peak at 10.5 ppm","A downfield 2H quartet (~4.3 ppm, coupled to 3 adjacent methyl protons: n+1 = 4) and an upfield 3H triplet (~1.3 ppm, coupled to 2 adjacent methylene protons: n+1 = 3)"],"a":3,"e":"By the Pascal\u0027s triangle (n + 1) multiplicity rule for first-order coupling across three bonds: the two equivalent -OCH2- protons are deshielded downfield (~4.2-4.4 ppm) by the electronegative ester oxygen and split by the 3 neighbouring -CH3 protons into (3 + 1 = 4) a quartet (intensity 1:3:3:1, integration 2H); reciprocally, the three shielded -CH3 protons appear upfield (~1.2-1.4 ppm) split by the 2 -CH2- protons into (2 + 1 = 3) a triplet (1:2:1, integration 3H).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00504","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Interpreting Renal Clearance (CLR) Relative to fu x GFR","q":"A patient with normal glomerular filtration rate (GFR = 120 mL/min) is administered an antibiotic whose unbound fraction in plasma (f_u) is 0.50 and measured total renal clearance (CL_R) is 225 mL/min. Comparing unbound renal clearance (CL_R / f_u) against GFR, what net renal handling mechanism predominates?","o":["Net active renal tubular secretion (because CL_R = 225 mL/min vastly exceeds filtration clearance f_u x GFR = 60 mL/min)","Net passive tubular reabsorption exceeding 95%","Complete biliary excretion with zero urinary recovery","Pure passive glomerular filtration with zero tubular transport"],"a":0,"e":"Passive glomerular filtration clearance of unbound drug alone equals f_u x GFR = 0.50 x 120 mL/min = 60 mL/min. Because measured renal clearance (225 mL/min; unbound intrinsic renal clearance = 225 / 0.50 = 450 mL/min) is nearly 4-fold higher than filtration clearance, the kidneys must be actively secreting the drug into the proximal tubule (e.g., via OAT/OCT transporters). Conversely, CL_R \u003c\u003c f_u x GFR indicates net tubular reabsorption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00505","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Quality Assurance","topic":"DQ, IQ, OQ, and PQ Qualification Lifecycle","q":"In pharmaceutical GMP equipment qualification (Schedule M / EU GMP Annex 15), match each qualification stage to its core verification objective: 1) Design Qualification (DQ), 2) Installation Qualification (IQ), 3) Operational Qualification (OQ), 4) Performance Qualification (PQ).","o":["1-Test commercial batches; 2-Verify URS before purchase; 3-Check utilities wiring; 4-Challenge alarm limits","1-Verify proposed user-requirement design complies with GMP before purchase; 2-Verify equipment is received, installed, and calibrated per engineering specs; 3-Challenge control ranges, alarms, and worst-case operating limits; 4-Demonstrate consistent reproducible performance using actual/simulated product batches","1-Verify alarm limits; 2-Run product batches; 3-Verify URS design; 4-Check installation drawings","1-Check vendor invoices only; 2-Run clinical trials; 3-Write SOPs; 4-Dispose obsolete machinery"],"a":1,"e":"The standard 4-stage V-model qualification sequence proceeds chronologically: DQ (documented verification that User Requirement Specifications and engineering design satisfy GMP before procurement), IQ (verifying hardware installation, utilities, manuals, and calibration certificates), OQ (testing functional parameters, safety interlocks, and upper/lower operating limits without full product load), and PQ (proving consistent quality under routine manufacturing conditions with actual/placebo product).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00506","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Practice","topic":"Clinical Trial Phases I to IV","q":"Match each clinical trial phase (under CDSCO NDCT Rules, 2019 / ICH GCP) to its primary scientific objective and typical cohort: 1) Phase I, 2) Phase II, 3) Phase III, 4) Phase IV.","o":["1-Post-marketing surveillance; 2-Confirmatory RCTs; 3-Healthy volunteer MTD; 4-Preclinical mouse LD50","1-Preclinical Ames test; 2-Phase I PK; 3-Phase II exploratory; 4-Stability testing","1-Human pharmacology, safety, tolerability, MTD \u0026 PK (typically 20-80 healthy volunteers or oncology patients); 2-Therapeutic exploratory proof-of-concept \u0026 dose-ranging in target patients; 3-Large multicentre therapeutic confirmatory RCTs establishing benefit-risk for marketing approval; 4-Post-marketing surveillance detecting rare/long-term ADRs in real-world use","1-Therapeutic confirmatory RCTs; 2-Human safety MTD; 3-Post-marketing pharmacovigilance; 4-Dose-ranging proof-of-concept"],"a":2,"e":"Clinical drug development progresses sequentially: Phase I (first-in-human safety, Maximum Tolerated Dose, single/multiple ascending dose PK); Phase II (exploratory efficacy and dose-response in ~100-300 affected patients); Phase III (large pivotal randomised controlled trials in hundreds-to-thousands of patients confirming efficacy/safety prior to NDA/marketing authorisation); Phase IV (post-marketing observational/PMS trials monitoring rare ADRs, special populations, and comparative effectiveness).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00507","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Alligation Alternate Method","q":"A compounding pharmacist needs to prepare 700 mL of 40% v/v ethanol by mixing 90% v/v ethanol with 20% v/v ethanol. What exact volumes of 90% v/v ethanol and 20% v/v ethanol are required?","o":["500 mL of 90% v/v ethanol and 200 mL of 20% v/v ethanol","250 mL of 90% v/v ethanol and 450 mL of 20% v/v ethanol","300 mL of 90% v/v ethanol and 400 mL of 20% v/v ethanol","200 mL of 90% v/v ethanol and 500 mL of 20% v/v ethanol"],"a":3,"e":"Using alligation alternate with target 40%: Parts of stronger (90%) = |40 - 20| = 20 parts; Parts of weaker (20%) = |90 - 40| = 50 parts. Total parts = 20 + 50 = 70 parts. For 700 mL total volume (assuming negligible volume contraction for exam calculation): Volume of 90% ethanol = (20/70) × 700 = 200 mL; Volume of 20% ethanol = (50/70) × 700 = 500 mL. Check: (200 × 0.90) + (500 × 0.20) = 180 + 100 = 280 mL pure ethanol in 700 mL = 40% v/v.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00508","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Suspensions and Interfacial Stability","q":"Which combination of physical properties accurately characterizes a well-formulated flocculated suspension compared with a deflocculated suspension?","o":["Rapid sedimentation rate, high sedimentation volume (F approaching 1), clear supernatant, and easy redispersibility without hard cake formation","Rapid sedimentation rate, low sedimentation volume, permanently turbid supernatant, and irreversible caking","Zero zeta potential with individual particles settling independently to form a tightly packed sediment","Slow sedimentation rate, high sedimentation volume, cloudy supernatant, and formation of a dense irreversible cake"],"a":0,"e":"In a controlled flocculated suspension (reduced zeta potential within the secondary minimum of the DLVO curve), particles aggregate into loose, porous flocs that entrap liquid. Although flocs settle rapidly leaving a clear supernatant, the loose scaffold prevents close particle packing, yielding a high sedimentation volume (F = Vu/V0 close to 1) that resists hard caking and redisperses effortlessly upon gentle shaking.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00509","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Powder Flow Properties","q":"A granulation blend exhibits a poured bulk density of 0.48 g/mL and a tapped density of 0.60 g/mL. What are its Carr\u0027s Compressibility Index (%) and Hausner Ratio, and how is its flowability classified as per USP/IP?","o":["Carr\u0027s Index = 20%, Hausner Ratio = 0.80 (Excellent flow)","Carr\u0027s Index = 20%, Hausner Ratio = 1.25 (Fair flow)","Carr\u0027s Index = 25%, Hausner Ratio = 1.33 (Passable flow)","Carr\u0027s Index = 12.5%, Hausner Ratio = 1.14 (Good flow)"],"a":1,"e":"Carr\u0027s Compressibility Index (%) = [(Tapped density - Bulk density) / Tapped density] × 100 = [(0.60 - 0.48) / 0.60] × 100 = (0.12 / 0.60) × 100 = 20%. Hausner Ratio = Tapped density / Bulk density = 0.60 / 0.48 = 1.25. According to standard pharmacopoeial flowability scales (USP \u003c1174\u003e/IP), a Carr\u0027s Index of 16–20% and Hausner Ratio of 1.19–1.25 correspond to \u0027Fair\u0027 flow.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00510","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"SAR of 1,4-Benzodiazepines","q":"Among 1,4-benzodiazepines, why are lorazepam and oxazepam preferred over diazepam in elderly patients or patients with hepatic cirrhosis?","o":["Absence of an electron-withdrawing substituent at C-7 prevents hepatic uptake","Presence of a fused triazole ring undergoes spontaneous plasma esterase cleavage","Presence of a C-3 hydroxyl group permits direct phase II glucuronidation without prior CYP450 oxidative metabolism","Presence of a methyl group at N-1 enhances rapid renal tubular secretion of the unchanged parent drug"],"a":2,"e":"Diazepam requires hepatic CYP2C19/CYP3A4 N-demethylation and C-3 hydroxylation (forming active metabolites desmethyldiazepam and temazepam/oxazepam), prolonging its half-life markedly in aging or liver disease. In contrast, lorazepam, oxazepam, and temazepam already possess a hydroxyl group at position C-3, allowing direct phase II glucuronidation—a pathway largely preserved in cirrhosis and the elderly—with no active metabolites.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00511","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"Bioactivation of Prodrugs","q":"Cyclophosphamide is an inactive prodrug that undergoes hepatic CYP450-mediated 4-hydroxylation followed by spontaneous cleavage into the active DNA-crosslinking phosphoramide mustard and which urotoxic byproduct prevented by Mesna?","o":["Chloroacetaldehyde","Formaldehyde","Cyanate ion","Acrolein"],"a":3,"e":"Hepatic CYP2B6/CYP3A4 oxidizes cyclophosphamide at C-4 to 4-hydroxycyclophosphamide, which equilibrates with aldophosphamide. Non-enzymatic beta-elimination of aldophosphamide yields the cytotoxic bifunctional alkylating agent phosphoramide mustard along with acrolein (CH2=CH-CHO). Acrolein accumulates in urine and causes hemorrhagic cystitis; co-administration of Mesna (sodium 2-mercaptoethanesulfonate) provides free sulfhydryl groups in the bladder that conjugate and detoxify acrolein.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00512","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"Structure-Activity Relationship of Sulfonamides","q":"Assertion (A): Replacing the free para-amino (-NH2) group of sulfanilamide with a para-methyl or para-nitro substituent abolishes direct in vitro antibacterial activity (unless metabolically reduced in vivo for -NO2). Reason (R): The para-amino group of sulfonamides mimics the para-amino moiety of para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) for competitive binding to bacterial dihydropteroate synthase.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Sulfonamides competitively inhibit dihydropteroate synthase (DHPS) by acting as structural antimetabolites of PABA during bacterial folic acid synthesis. The unsubstituted para-amino (-NH2) group and the para-oriented sulfonamide (-SO2NHR) moiety are strictly essential for molecular mimicry of PABA\u0027s dimensions and hydrogen-bonding interactions in the DHPS active site. Substitution or replacement of the para-NH2 eliminates activity unless it can be cleaved/reduced in vivo back to a free amine (e.g., prontosil, phthalylsulfathiazole).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00513","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"SAR of Morphine Analogues","q":"Which structural modification on the morphinan skeleton converts an opioid mu-receptor agonist like oxymorphone into a pure competitive opioid antagonist such as naloxone?","o":["Replacing the N-17 methyl group with an N-allyl group","Methylating the C-3 phenolic hydroxyl group to a methoxy group","Acetylating both the C-3 and C-6 hydroxyl groups","Reducing the C-7,8 double bond to a saturated ring"],"a":0,"e":"In 4,5-epoxymorphinan opioids, substituting the N-17 methyl group with a three-carbon unsaturated or small cycloalkyl group—such as an N-allyl (naloxone, nalorphine) or N-cyclopropylmethyl (naltrexone)—shifts receptor pharmacology toward antagonism. Combined with a C-14 beta-hydroxyl group (as in oxymorphone -\u003e naloxone/naltrexone), the molecule acts as a pure competitive opioid receptor antagonist across mu, kappa, and delta receptors.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00514","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacodynamics and Receptor Antagonism","q":"In an isolated guinea-pig ileum bioassay, adding increasing fixed concentrations of Drug X shifts the acetylcholine log dose-response curve progressively to the right in a parallel manner without reducing the maximum attainable contraction (Emax), and yields a Schild plot slope of 1.0. Which conclusion about Drug X is valid?","o":["Drug X is a reversible competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors","Drug X is a chemical antagonist that chelates acetylcholine in the bath","Drug X is an irreversible non-competitive antagonist","Drug X is an allosteric negative modulator that decreases Emax"],"a":0,"e":"A reversible competitive antagonist binds to the same orthosteric site as the agonist, increasing the apparent EC50 of the agonist while leaving Emax unaltered because high agonist concentrations can outcompete the antagonist (surmountable blockade). This produces parallel rightward shifts in the log dose-response curve and a linear Schild regression [log(DR - 1) vs -log(B)] with a slope equal to unity (1.0), whose x-intercept equals pA2.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00515","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Mood Stabilizers","q":"A 38-year-old man stabilized on lithium carbonate for bipolar disorder (serum lithium 0.8 mEq/L) is started on hydrochlorothiazide for newly diagnosed hypertension. Two weeks later, he presents with coarse hand tremor, ataxia, dysarthria, and a serum lithium level of 2.1 mEq/L. What is the renal mechanism of this drug interaction?","o":["Thiazide-induced sodium and volume depletion triggers compensatory proximal tubular reabsorption of both sodium and lithium","Thiazides inhibit hepatic CYP2D6 metabolism of lithium","Thiazides displace lithium from plasma albumin binding sites","Thiazides alkalinize the urine and precipitate lithium carbonate in the collecting duct"],"a":0,"e":"Lithium is a monovalent cation that is neither metabolized nor plasma-protein bound; it is freely filtered at the glomerulus and ~80% reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule alongside sodium. Thiazide diuretics inhibit NaCl reabsorption in the distal tubule, causing mild volume contraction that reflexively increases proximal tubular Na+ and water reabsorption—and consequently increases Li+ reabsorption, reducing lithium clearance by 25-40% and precipitating toxicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00516","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antithyroid Drugs","q":"A 30-year-old woman in the 8th week of pregnancy is diagnosed with Graves\u0027 disease requiring antithyroid pharmacotherapy, and propylthiouracil (PTU) is chosen over methimazole for the first trimester. What additional pharmacological advantage does PTU also possess in acute thyroid storm compared with methimazole?","o":["PTU irreversibly destroys thyroid follicular cells via beta-emission","PTU inhibits peripheral 5\u0027-deiodinase-mediated conversion of T4 to active T3","PTU directly blocks thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) secretion from the anterior pituitary","PTU competitively antagonizes nuclear T3 receptors in cardiac myocytes"],"a":1,"e":"Both methimazole and propylthiouracil inhibit thyroid peroxidase (blocking iodide organification and iodotyrosine coupling). However, PTU additionally inhibits peripheral 5\u0027-deiodinase (type 1), blocking extrathyroidal conversion of thyroxine (T4) to the more biologically potent triiodothyronine (T3)—making PTU valuable in thyroid storm. In the first trimester of pregnancy, PTU is preferred to avoid methimazole-associated embryopathy (aplasia cutis, choanal/esophageal atresia), though switching to methimazole in the second trimester is often considered due to PTU\u0027s hepatotoxicity risk.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00517","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiac Glycosides","q":"A patient with heart failure receiving digoxin and furosemide develops nausea, yellow-green visual halos, and premature ventricular contractions when serum potassium falls to 2.7 mEq/L. How does hypokalemia precipitate digoxin toxicity?","o":["Hypokalemia increases renal tubular secretion of digoxin via P-glycoprotein","Low serum potassium stimulates aldosterone release, which directly blocks cardiac L-type Ca2+ channels","Potassium competes with digoxin for the extracellular binding site on Na+/K+-ATPase, so low K+ enhances digoxin binding","Hypokalemia accelerates hepatic CYP3A4 conversion of digoxin to a cardiotoxic metabolite"],"a":2,"e":"Digoxin produces positive inotropy by reversibly inhibiting the sarcolemmal Na+/K+-ATPase pump, raising intracellular Na+, reducing Ca2+ efflux via the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (NCX), and increasing sarcoplasmic reticulum Ca2+ stores. Because potassium ions (K+) and digoxin compete for the same extracellular binding site on the alpha-subunit of Na+/K+-ATPase, loop diuretic-induced hypokalemia permits excessive digoxin binding, resulting in intracellular Ca2+ overload, delayed afterdepolarizations, and arrhythmias.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00518","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy \u0026 Phytochemistry","topic":"Biosynthesis of Secondary Metabolites","q":"Which biosynthetic pathway exclusively supplies the C6-C3 phenylpropanoid precursor (L-phenylalanine / cinnamic acid) required for plant lignans, coumarins, and the B-ring of flavonoids?","o":["2-C-methyl-D-erythritol 4-phosphate (MEP) pathway","Mevalonic acid (MVA) pathway","Acetate-malonate polyketide pathway","Shikimic acid pathway"],"a":3,"e":"The shikimic acid pathway condenses erythrose-4-phosphate and phosphoenolpyruvate to form shikimic acid and chorismic acid, leading to aromatic amino acids (L-phenylalanine, L-tyrosine, L-tryptophan). Phenylalanine ammonia-lyase (PAL) deaminates L-phenylalanine to trans-cinnamic acid, providing the C6-C3 phenylpropanoid skeleton for coumarins, lignans, and the cinnamoyl-CoA starter unit (B-ring + C3 bridge) of flavonoids.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00519","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy \u0026 Phytochemistry","topic":"Organogenesis and Growth Regulators","q":"When subculturing medicinal plant callus on Murashige and Skoog (MS) medium, how should the plant growth regulator ratio be adjusted to induce adventitious shoot formation (caulogenesis) rather than root formation?","o":["High cytokinin-to-auxin ratio (e.g., higher BAP/kinetin relative to NAA/IAA)","High gibberellic acid combined with ethylene precursor ACC","High auxin-to-cytokinin ratio (e.g., higher IBA/NAA relative to kinetin)","High abscisic acid with zero cytokinin and high 2,4-D"],"a":0,"e":"According to Skoog and Miller\u0027s classical hormonal regulation model of morphogenesis in plant tissue culture, the balance between cytokinins and auxins directs organ differentiation: a high cytokinin-to-auxin ratio promotes adventitious shoot bud initiation (caulogenesis), a high auxin-to-cytokinin ratio stimulates root differentiation (rhizogenesis), and intermediate balanced concentrations favor undifferentiated callus proliferation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00520","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy \u0026 Phytochemistry","topic":"Extraction of Volatile Oils","q":"A pharmacy student quantifies the essential oil content of crushed clove buds (Syzygium aromaticum) by hydrodistillation. Because eugenol-rich clove oil has a specific gravity greater than 1.0 (approx. 1.04–1.07), which feature of the Clevenger apparatus is required for accurate volumetric collection?","o":["Soxhlet extraction with boiling petroleum ether followed by direct evaporation at 100 °C","A Clevenger trap designed for oils heavier than water where the oil collects at the bottom of the graduated tube and water returns from the top","A standard light-oil Clevenger trap where oil floats on top of the water column","Enfleurage using cold animal fat plates at room temperature"],"a":1,"e":"Standard IP/BP hydrodistillation of essential oils uses a Clevenger apparatus. Oils lighter than water (e.g., fennel, peppermint, eucalyptus) float above the aqueous condensate and require a light-oil trap. Conversely, clove oil (predominantly eugenol, density ~1.04-1.07 g/mL) is denser than water and sinks beneath the aqueous layer, necessitating a heavy-oil Clevenger receiver whose return arm feeds water back to the flask from the upper level while retaining oil at the bottom.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00521","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Beer-Lambert Law Calculations","q":"A drug solution analyzed in a 1 cm quartz cuvette at its lambda-max gives an absorbance (A) of 0.450. If the pharmacopoeial specific absorbance A(1%, 1 cm) of the drug at that wavelength is 500, what is the concentration of the analyzed solution in µg/mL?","o":["22.5 µg/mL","4.5 µg/mL","9.0 µg/mL","90.0 µg/mL"],"a":2,"e":"Using the Beer-Lambert relationship with specific absorbance: A = A(1%, 1 cm) × c (% w/v) × l (cm). Rearranging for c: c (% w/v, i.e., g/100 mL) = A / [A(1%, 1 cm) × l] = 0.450 / (500 × 1) = 0.0009 g/100 mL. Converting units: 0.0009 g/100 mL = 0.9 mg/100 mL = 900 µg/100 mL = 9.0 µg/mL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00522","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Validation Parameters (ICH Q2)","q":"During HPLC analytical method validation under ICH Q2(R1)/Q2(R2), the standard deviation of the response (sigma) is 0.15 mAU and the slope of the calibration curve (S) is 5.0 mAU/(µg/mL). Using the calibration-curve formula, what are the Limit of Detection (LOD) and Limit of Quantitation (LOQ), respectively?","o":["LOD = 0.150 µg/mL and LOQ = 0.500 µg/mL","LOD = 0.030 µg/mL and LOQ = 0.150 µg/mL","LOD = 0.300 µg/mL and LOQ = 0.099 µg/mL","LOD = 0.099 µg/mL and LOQ = 0.300 µg/mL"],"a":3,"e":"According to ICH Q2(R1)/Q2(R2) guidelines based on the standard deviation of the response (sigma) and the slope (S) of the calibration curve: LOD = 3.3 × sigma / S = (3.3 × 0.15) / 5.0 = 0.495 / 5.0 = 0.099 µg/mL; LOQ = 10 × sigma / S = (10 × 0.15) / 5.0 = 1.50 / 5.0 = 0.300 µg/mL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00523","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Karl Fischer Aquametry","q":"In Karl Fischer (KF) volumetric titration for determining trace water content in hygroscopic pharmaceutical excipients, what is the stoichiometric molar ratio between water (H2O) and iodine (I2) consumed in the presence of methanol, sulfur dioxide, and imidazole/pyridine?","o":["1 mole of H2O reacts with 1 mole of I2","1 mole of H2O reacts with 3 moles of I2","1 mole of H2O reacts with 2 moles of I2","2 moles of H2O react with 1 mole of I2"],"a":0,"e":"In the modern Scholz mechanism of the Karl Fischer reaction using methanol (CH3OH) and a base (B, such as imidazole or pyridine), SO2 first reacts with methanol and base to form the methyl sulfite intermediate ([BH]+[CH3SO3]-). This intermediate is then oxidized by I2 in the presence of H2O to methyl sulfate ([BH]+[CH3SO4]-) and hydroiodic acid: CH3OH + SO2 + 3B + I2 + H2O -\u003e [BH][CH3SO4] + 2[BH]I, establishing an exact 1:1 molar stoichiometry between H2O and I2.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00524","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Biotechnology","topic":"Monoclonal Antibody Production","q":"During monoclonal antibody production via hybridoma technology, spleen B-lymphocytes from an immunized mouse are fused with HGPRT-deficient myeloma cells and cultured in HAT (hypoxanthine-aminopterin-thymidine) medium. Why do unfused myeloma cells selectively die in HAT medium?","o":["Thymidine kinase is overexpressed in myeloma cells, causing lethal ATP depletion","Aminopterin blocks the de novo nucleotide synthesis pathway, and HGPRT-deficient myeloma cells cannot utilize hypoxanthine via the salvage pathway","Aminopterin activates apoptosis selectively through Fc-gamma receptors on myeloma cells","Hypoxanthine directly lyses the plasma membrane of myeloma cells lacking surface IgM"],"a":1,"e":"Aminopterin is a dihydrofolate reductase inhibitor that blocks the de novo biosynthesis of both purines and pyrimidines. Normal cells survive if supplied with hypoxanthine (purine salvage via HGPRT) and thymidine (pyrimidine salvage via thymidine kinase). Because the myeloma partner lacks functional HGPRT (HGPRT-), unfused myeloma cells cannot use hypoxanthine and die; unfused B-cells have limited lifespan and die naturally; only fused hybridomas inherit HGPRT+ from B-cells and immortality from myeloma cells, surviving in HAT medium.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00525","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Biotechnology","topic":"Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)","q":"In a standard Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) thermal cycle, what is the purpose of the 50–65 °C step immediately following the 94–95 °C denaturation step?","o":["DNA strand separation by breaking hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs","Activation of reverse transcriptase to synthesize cDNA from mRNA","Primer annealing, allowing short single-stranded forward and reverse oligonucleotide primers to hybridize to complementary template DNA strands","Extension of the nascent DNA strand by thermostable Taq DNA polymerase at its temperature optimum"],"a":2,"e":"Each PCR cycle consists of three temperature-controlled phases: (1) Denaturation at 94–95 °C separates double-stranded DNA into single strands; (2) Annealing at 50–65 °C (determined by primer Tm) allows specific forward and reverse oligonucleotide primers to hybridize to their complementary target sequences; and (3) Extension at ~72 °C enables thermostable Taq DNA polymerase (isolated from Thermus aquaticus) to synthesize new DNA strands in the 5\u0027-\u003e3\u0027 direction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00526","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy","topic":"Tablet Manufacturing Defects","q":"During high-speed rotary compression of a poorly compressible API granulation, tablets emerge with the upper crown separating horizontally from the body (capping), especially when compression speed is increased. Which combination of root causes and corrective measures best addresses capping?","o":["Caused by uneven dye migration during drying; corrected by increasing disintegrant concentration","Caused by excess magnesium stearate and low moisture only; corrected by increasing upper punch pressure to maximum","Caused by concave punches adhering to damp granules; corrected by decreasing glidant and increasing ambient humidity","Caused by excessive fines, entrapped air, and high punch velocity; corrected by pre-compression, reducing turret speed, adding binder, or using tapered dies"],"a":3,"e":"Capping (partial or complete separation of the top/bottom crown of a tablet) and lamination (splitting into horizontal layers) occur when elastic recovery during decompression exceeds interparticulate bond strength, commonly due to entrapped air during rapid compression, excessive fines (\u003c100 mesh), overly dry granules, or worn/ringed die walls. Effective remedies include applying pre-compression to expel air, lowering turret speed (increasing dwell time), using tapered dies, optimizing moisture/binder content, and removing excess fines.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00527","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy","topic":"Tablet Coating Technology","q":"Which polymer is specifically employed for enteric tablet coating to resist gastric acid (pH 1.2) and dissolve selectively in the proximal or distal small intestine (pH \u003e= 5.5–7.0) via ionization of free carboxylic acid groups?","o":["Cellulose acetate phthalate (CAP) or Eudragit L100/S100","Ethylcellulose (EC) with dibutyl sebacate","Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP K30)","Hydroxypropyl methylcellulose (HPMC E5) alone"],"a":0,"e":"Enteric polymers are polyacids containing ionizable carboxylic acid groups that remain unionized and insoluble in acidic gastric fluid (pH 1–3) but ionize and dissolve rapidly at higher intestinal pH. Cellulose acetate phthalate (CAP, dissolves \u003e= pH 6.0), hydroxypropyl methylcellulose phthalate (HPMCP, pH 5.0–5.5), and methacrylic acid copolymers such as Eudragit L100 (pH \u003e= 6.0) and Eudragit S100 (pH \u003e= 7.0) provide reliable gastro-resistance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00528","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy","topic":"Aseptic Processing and Cleanrooms","q":"In aseptic parenteral filling rooms, High-Efficiency Particulate Air (HEPA) filters are certified using dioctyl phthalate (DOP) or polyalphaolefin (PAO) aerosol challenge testing to remove at least what percentage of 0.3-micron particles?","o":["99.00%","99.97%","95.00%","90.50%"],"a":1,"e":"By standard definition (Schedule M / WHO GMP / ISO / USP), a HEPA filter must exhibit a minimum particle collection efficiency of 99.97% for thermally generated monodisperse 0.3-micron particles (the most penetrating particle size, MPPS) when challenged with DOP or PAO aerosol. Critical aseptic filling zones operate under Grade A (ISO Class 5) unidirectional laminar airflow supplied through leak-tested HEPA filters.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00529","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"One-Compartment Open Model (IV Bolus \u0026 Infusion)","q":"An intravenous antibiotic follows one-compartment linear pharmacokinetics with a volume of distribution (Vd) of 40 L and total body clearance (CL) of 4.0 L/h. If the target steady-state plasma concentration (Css) is 10 mg/L, what are the required IV loading dose and the drug\u0027s elimination half-life (t1/2)?","o":["Loading dose = 400 mg; t1/2 = 10.0 h","Loading dose = 100 mg; t1/2 = 0.10 h","Loading dose = 400 mg; t1/2 = 6.93 h","Loading dose = 40 mg; t1/2 = 6.93 h"],"a":2,"e":"For an IV administration (F = 1), Loading Dose (DL) = Css × Vd = 10 mg/L × 40 L = 400 mg. The first-order elimination rate constant k = CL / Vd = 4.0 L/h / 40 L = 0.10 h^-1. Therefore, elimination half-life t1/2 = 0.693 / k = 0.693 / 0.10 h^-1 = 6.93 hours (Note: maintenance infusion rate R0 = Css × CL = 10 × 4.0 = 40 mg/h).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00530","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Biopharmaceutics Classification System (BCS)","q":"A newly synthesized oral drug exhibits high intestinal membrane permeability but very low aqueous solubility across pH 1.2–6.8 (Dose number Do \u003e\u003e 1). Under Amidon\u0027s Biopharmaceutics Classification System (BCS), how is this drug classified and what is the rate-limiting step for its oral absorption?","o":["BCS Class II; in vivo dissolution is rate-limiting, and solid dispersions or micronization can enhance bioavailability","BCS Class I; gastric emptying is rate-limiting, and sustained-release matrix formulation is required to prevent dose dumping","BCS Class III; intestinal permeability is rate-limiting, and particle size reduction alone maximizes absorption","BCS Class IV; neither solubility nor permeability affects absorption, so simple immediate-release tablets yield 100% F"],"a":0,"e":"Under Amidon\u0027s BCS, Class II drugs possess low aqueous solubility and high membrane permeability (examples: carbamazepine, ibuprofen, fenofibrate). Because they permeate the GI epithelium readily once dissolved, dissolution rate in GI fluids is the rate-limiting step to absorption, often allowing strong in vitro-in vivo correlation (IVIVC). Strategies such as particle size reduction (micronization/nanosuspensions), amorphous solid dispersions, cyclodextrin complexation, or lipid-based self-emulsifying systems improve dissolution and oral bioavailability.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00531","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Absolute Bioavailability Calculation","q":"In a crossover pharmacokinetic study, a single 200 mg oral tablet of Drug Z produced a mean AUC(0-inf) of 48 mg·h/L, whereas a 50 mg intravenous bolus dose in the same subjects produced a mean AUC(0-inf) of 20 mg·h/L. What is the absolute oral bioavailability (F) of Drug Z?","o":["40% (0.40)","24% (0.24)","80% (0.80)","60% (0.60)"],"a":3,"e":"Absolute bioavailability (F) compares the dose-normalized systemic exposure after extravascular administration to an intravenous reference: F = (AUC_oral / Dose_oral) / (AUC_IV / Dose_IV) = (AUC_oral × Dose_IV) / (AUC_IV × Dose_oral). Substituting the values: F = (48 × 50) / (20 × 200) = 2,400 / 4,000 = 0.60, or 60%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00532","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics I","topic":"Noyes-Whitney Dissolution Rate Equation","q":"According to the modified Noyes-Whitney equation dM/dt = [D * A * (C_s - C_b)] / h for solid drug dissolution in GI fluid, which formulation strategy increases dissolution rate primarily by boosting saturation solubility (C_s) inside the stagnant diffusion layer rather than merely increasing surface area (A)?","o":["Converting a weakly acidic/basic drug into a soluble salt (e.g., phenytoin sodium), forming a water-soluble cyclodextrin inclusion complex, or using a high-energy amorphous solid dispersion","Compressing the tablet at double the punch force with 3% magnesium stearate","Reducing paddle stirring speed in the dissolution bath to zero rpm","Passing the unchanged crystalline powder through a #40 sieve without particle-size change"],"a":0,"e":"In Noyes-Whitney dM/dt = D*A*(C_s - C_b)/h: micronisation/nanosuspension boosts effective surface area (A); vigorous GI agitation/stirring reduces stagnant diffusion layer thickness (h); whereas salt formation (which buffers microenvironmental pH in the diffusion layer), amorphous solid dispersions, cosolvents, and cyclodextrin complexation increase the intrinsic saturation solubility (C_s) at the solid-liquid interface, creating a steeper concentration gradient.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00533","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics II","topic":"DLVO Theory — Flocculated vs Deflocculated Suspensions","q":"Using DLVO potential-energy curves (attractive van der Waals vs repulsive electrical double-layer forces), why do formulators intentionally create \u0027controlled flocculation\u0027 in oral/parenteral suspensions rather than keeping particles completely deflocculated?","o":["Controlled flocculation forces particles over the energy barrier into irreversible primary-minimum fusion (hard caking)","Controlled flocculation places particles in the shallow \u0027secondary energy minimum\u0027 forming loose, porous flocs that settle rapidly to a high sediment volume (F ~ 1) and redisperse effortlessly on mild shaking, preventing irreversible hard-cake formation seen in deflocculated suspensions","Deflocculated suspensions always maintain zero sedimentation for 10 years regardless of density difference","Controlled flocculation eliminates the need for any shaking before pouring a dose"],"a":1,"e":"In a deflocculated suspension (high zeta potential), individual particles repel each other and settle very slowly into a tightly packed, non-porous sediment where liquid is squeezed out and particles drop into deep primary-minimum attraction—forming a hard, non-redispersible \u0027clay\u0027 cake. Controlled flocculation (adding counter-ions per Schulze-Hardy rule or bridging polymers) lowers zeta potential just enough so particles aggregate loosely in the shallow secondary minimum, yielding a bulky, clear-supernatant, easily redispersible sediment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00534","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy I","topic":"Complex Coacervation Phase Separation vs Wurster Fluid-Bed Coating","q":"In microencapsulation of volatile oils or hydrophobic drugs by complex coacervation (aqueous phase separation), how does mixing warm aqueous solutions of gelatin and gum acacia at pH ~4.0-4.5 form a continuous polymer coat around dispersed core oil droplets?","o":["Below gelatin\u0027s isoelectric point (pH ~4.8), positively charged gelatin interacts electrostatically with negatively charged anionic acacia, separating as a polymer-rich liquid coacervate phase that wets and engulfs the core droplets before cross-linking/hardening","Both polymers carry identical strong negative charges at pH 4.0 causing violent explosion","Gelatin polymerises covalently with mineral oil above 250 C in a dry pan","Wurster air-suspension nozzles spray dry talc powder without any liquid binder"],"a":0,"e":"Complex coacervation relies on electrostatic attraction between two oppositely charged hydrocolloids in water: adjusting pH to ~4.0-4.5 places Type A/B gelatin below its isoelectric point (giving net positive NH3+ charge) while acacia remains negatively charged (COO- groups). Charge neutralisation induces liquid-liquid phase separation into a dense coacervate phase that wraps uniformly around dispersed oil droplets, followed by cooling and formaldehyde/glutaraldehyde or transglutaminase hardening.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00535","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy II","topic":"Scale-Up of High-Shear Wet Granulation (SUPAC-IR)","q":"When scaling up a wet granulation process from a 10-L laboratory high-shear mixer-granulator to a 300-L commercial production bowl, why should the process engineer maintain constant impeller tip speed (or Froude number) and use impeller power/torque consumption curves rather than simply copying the lab mixing RPM and fixed kneading time?","o":["Because keeping identical RPM slows peripheral tip speed tenfold, leaving dry unmixed powder","Because commercial 300-L bowls require zero binder solution once bowl volume exceeds 100 L","Because keeping identical RPM in a much larger diameter bowl drastically multiplies peripheral tip velocity (v = pi * D * N), over-granulating/densifying the mass and slowing tablet dissolution—whereas scaling by tip speed/Froude number and monitoring power-consumption end-points preserves comparable shear work","Because power consumption curves measure tablet weight variation directly inside the granulator"],"a":2,"e":"In high-shear wet granulation scale-up, impeller blade diameter (D) increases markedly. Since peripheral tip speed = pi * D * N, running the large commercial impeller at the same RPM (N) as the lab unit generates excessive shear impact and frictional heat, causing over-wetting, dense hard granules, and failed dissolution. Scaling via constant tip speed (N_2 = N_1 * D_1/D_2) or constant Froude number (N^2 * D / g) coupled with real-time motor amperage/torque end-point monitoring maintains consistent granule porosity and compressibility.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00536","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry I","topic":"SAR of Tricyclic Antidepressants (Imipramine) vs SSRIs (Fluoxetine)","q":"Replacing the rigid sulphur atom of tricyclic phenothiazines (chlorpromazine) with a flexible two-carbon ethylene bridge (-CH2-CH2-) produces the dibenzazepine core of imipramine. How does this central-ring change alter three-dimensional geometry and pharmacological profile?","o":["Forces all three rings into strict aromatic coplanarity to produce selective GABA-A agonism","Eliminates all monoamine transporter binding, converting imipramine into a pure diuretic","Converts the molecule into an irreversible MAO-B propargylamine inhibitor","Twists the two aromatic rings out of coplanarity (creating a non-planar butterfly geometry), abolishing D2 antipsychotic blockade while conferring potent norepinephrine/serotonin (NET/SERT) reuptake inhibition (antidepressant activity)"],"a":3,"e":"Bioisosteric replacement of the phenothiazine -S- atom with an ethylene (-CH2-CH2-) bridge expands the central 6-membered ring into a 7-membered azepine/cycloheptadiene ring (imipramine, amitriptyline), skewing the two flanking phenyl planes at a ~55-65 degree dihedral angle (\u0027butterfly\u0027 twist). This non-coplanar geometry abolishes dopamine D2 receptor antagonism and instead fits NET and SERT monoamine reuptake transporters (with tertiary amines like imipramine/amitriptyline inhibiting SERT+NET, and secondary amine desipramine/nortriptyline favouring NET).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00537","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry II","topic":"SAR Evolution from Cimetidine to Ranitidine \u0026 Famotidine","q":"First-generation H2-receptor antagonist cimetidine frequently causes CYP450-mediated drug interactions (raising warfarin/phenytoin/theophylline levels) and antiandrogenic gynaecomastia. Which heterocyclic ring modification in ranitidine and famotidine successfully eliminated CYP450 inhibition while increasing H2-antagonist potency?","o":["Replacing cimetidine\u0027s basic imidazole ring (which coordinates the haem Fe2+/Fe3+ of CYP450) with a substituted furan ring (ranitidine) or guanidinothiazole ring (famotidine)","Replacing the entire polar tail with a lipophilic quaternary ammonium salt","Removing the flexible 4-atom thioether chain completely","Adding a second imidazole ring at the cyanoguanidine terminus"],"a":0,"e":"Cimetidine\u0027s unsubstituted/methyl-imidazole nitrogen lone pair forms a tight coordinate bond with the haem iron of hepatic CYP450 isoenzymes (CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2D6, CYP1A2), potently inhibiting drug metabolism, and also binds androgen receptors causing gynaecomastia. Replacing imidazole with a dimethylaminomethyl-furan ring (ranitidine, plus nitroethenediamine tail) or guanidinothiazole ring (famotidine, plus sulfamoylamidine tail) abolished CYP450 haem ligation and antiandrogenic toxicity while raising H2 potency 5- to 30-fold.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00538","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry III","topic":"Degradation Chemistry of Tetracyclines","q":"When older tetracycline capsules were stored under warm, humid, acidic conditions (especially with citric acid excipients), patients ingesting degraded capsules developed renal Fanconi syndrome (proximal tubular acidosis, glycosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia). Which chemical degradation product of tetracycline causes this nephrotoxicity?","o":["4-Aminoquinoline-N-oxide","4-Epianhydrotetracycline (formed via C-4 dimethylamino epimerisation and C-5a/C-6 dehydration across the C-ring)","Beta-lactone penicilloic acid","6-Demethylchlortetracycline glucuronide conjugate"],"a":1,"e":"Under acidic moisture and heat, tetracycline undergoes two harmful transformations: (1) reversible C-4 epimerisation of the natural alpha-dimethylamino group into inactive 4-epitetracycline, and (2) acid-catalysed dehydration of the C-6 tertiary hydroxyl and C-5a hydrogen to aromatise the C-ring into anhydrotetracycline, yielding 4-epianhydrotetracycline—the potent proximal renal tubular toxin causing reversible Fanconi syndrome. Doxycycline and minocycline lack the C-6 OH group and therefore CANNOT form anhydrotetracyclines!","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00539","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry III","topic":"Hansch Quantitative Structure-Activity Relationship (QSAR)","q":"In classical Hansch QSAR linear free-energy modelling [log(1/C) = -k1*(log P)^2 + k2*log P + rho*sigma + k3*E_s + constant], what do the parameters pi (or log P), Hammett sigma, and Taft E_s quantify respectively, and why does biological potency often show a parabolic (inverted-U) relationship with log P?","o":["pi/log P quantifies hydrophobicity (lipophilicity), Hammett sigma quantifies electronic substituent effects, and Taft E_s quantifies steric bulk; potency peaks at an optimal log P_0 because overly hydrophilic drugs fail to cross lipid membranes while overly lipophilic drugs remain trapped in lipid bilayers/plasma proteins","pi quantifies molecular weight, sigma quantifies optical rotation, and E_s quantifies boiling point","Linear QSAR proves that increasing log P infinitely above +10 always increases CNS penetration","Hammett sigma exclusively measures hydrogen-bond donor count"],"a":0,"e":"Corwin Hansch\u0027s multiparameter QSAR correlates biological response log(1/C) with three orthogonal substituent descriptors: hydrophobic constant pi (log P_X - log P_H, governing membrane permeation/hydrophobic binding), Hammett electronic constant sigma (electron-donating vs electron-withdrawing inductive/resonance effects on pKa/reactivity), and Taft steric parameter E_s (size/steric hindrance). The parabolic -(log P)^2 term reflects the random-walk crossing of alternating aqueous and lipid barriers peaking at optimal log P_0 (~2 for CNS agents).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00540","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology I","topic":"Therapeutic Index (TI) vs Certain Safety Factor (CSF)","q":"In preclinical quantal dose-response testing of an investigational sedative, ED_50 = 10 mg/kg, ED_99 = 40 mg/kg, LD_50 = 200 mg/kg, and LD_1 = 20 mg/kg. Calculate both the traditional Therapeutic Index (LD_50 / ED_50) and the Certain Safety Factor (LD_1 / ED_99), and interpret the clinical implication.","o":["Therapeutic Index = 200; Certain Safety Factor = 20","Therapeutic Index = 5.0; Certain Safety Factor = 10.0","Therapeutic Index = 20; Certain Safety Factor = 0.50 (indicating dangerous overlap between the dose achieving 99% efficacy and the lowest 1% lethal dose despite an apparently wide TI of 20)","Therapeutic Index = 0.05; Certain Safety Factor = 2.0 (completely safe across all doses)"],"a":2,"e":"Traditional Therapeutic Index TI = LD_50 / ED_50 = 200 / 10 = 20, which only compares medians and can mislead if quantal log-dose curves have shallow/different slopes. The Certain Safety Factor (CSF) = LD_1 / ED_99 = 20 / 40 = 0.50 (\u003c1.0), exposing that the dose lethal to 1% of subjects (20 mg/kg) is actually LOWER than the dose needed for 99% efficacy (40 mg/kg)—indicating severe safety overlap!","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00541","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Oral Contraceptive Failure via Enzyme Induction","q":"A 27-year-old woman taking a combined oral contraceptive pill (ethinylestradiol + levonorgestrel) is diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis and started on daily rifampin-based NTEP therapy. Within 3 weeks she experiences intermenstrual breakthrough bleeding and risks contraceptive failure. What pharmacokinetic mechanism explains this failure, and what counselling is mandatory?","o":["Rifampin chelates levonorgestrel inside the stomach via iron ions","Rifampin inhibits CYP3A4 causing ethinylestradiol overdose and thromboembolism","Combined oral contraceptives completely inactivate rifampin bactericidal action","Rifampin potently induces hepatic CYP3A4 (and intestinal P-gp/UGTs), accelerating ethinylestradiol and progestin clearance and causing subtherapeutic contraceptive levels; advise reliable non-hormonal barrier contraception (e.g., condoms/copper IUD)"],"a":3,"e":"Rifampin (and rifabutin to a lesser degree, plus carbamazepine/phenytoin/phenobarbital/efavirenz) strongly activates pregnane X receptor (PXR), inducing hepatic and gut CYP3A4 and glucuronosyltransferases. This cuts plasma ethinylestradiol and progestin AUC by 40-70%, precipitating breakthrough bleeding and ovulation/unintended pregnancy. Patients must switch to or add reliable barrier contraception or non-CYP-dependent methods (e.g., copper IUD or depot medroxyprogesterone acetate) during and for 28 days after stopping rifampin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00542","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology III","topic":"Clindamycin \u0026 Macrolides (50S Target \u0026 C. difficile Colitis)","q":"A 56-year-old inpatient develops profuse watery diarrhoea, crampy abdominal pain, fever, and leukocytosis (WBC 21,000/mm3) 8 days after finishing oral clindamycin for a dental abscess; stool assay confirms Clostridioides difficile toxins A/B. At which ribosomal site does clindamycin act, and what is the first-line oral antimicrobial therapy for acute C. difficile infection (CDI)?","o":["Binds 23S rRNA of the 50S bacterial ribosomal subunit (overlapping macrolide/streptogramin B binding site); treat CDI with oral vancomycin 125 mg QID (or oral fidaxomicin) for 10 days and stop clindamycin","Inhibits fungal squalene epoxidase; treat CDI with oral fluconazole","Binds 16S rRNA of the 30S subunit; treat CDI with intravenous gentamicin","Inhibits bacterial cell wall mycolic acid synthesis; treat CDI with oral loperamide alone"],"a":0,"e":"Clindamycin (a lincosamide) binds the 23S rRNA peptidyl transferase tunnel of the 50S ribosomal subunit (overlapping macrolides and streptogramin B — why erm gene methylation confers cross-resistance, MLSB phenotype). By wiping out anaerobic Bacteroides colonic flora, clindamycin strongly predisposes to Clostridioides difficile overgrowth and toxin-mediated pseudomembranous colitis. Per IDSA/SHEA/ICMR guidelines, oral vancomycin (non-absorbed lumenally active) or oral fidaxomicin for 10 days is first-line (avoiding antimotility agents like loperamide, which risk toxic megacolon).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00543","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry I","topic":"Swelling Index Determination (Isabgol / Plantago ovata)","q":"In the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) pharmacognostic evaluation of Ispaghula (Isabgol, Plantago ovata) seeds and husk, how is the \u0027Swelling Index\u0027 measured, and why does Isabgol husk yield a much higher swelling index (\u003e= 40) than whole seeds (\u003e= 10)?","o":["Optical rotation in chloroform at 25 C; seeds contain volatile menthol","Volume in mL occupied by 1 g of drug (and adhering mucilage) after shaking intermittently for 1 h and standing 3 h in 25 mL water in a graduated stoppered cylinder; mucilage is concentrated exclusively in the epidermal seed-coat husk rather than the inner embryo/endosperm","Height of foam produced after shaking 10 g root in 0.1 N HCl; husk contains cardiac glycosides","Weight in grams of dried residue after boiling in 50% KOH for 12 hours; husk contains lignin only"],"a":1,"e":"Swelling Index (IP/WHO) quantifies hydrophilic mucilage/gum content: 1.0 g of whole/coarsely powdered drug is placed in a 25 mL glass-stoppered measuring cylinder with 25 mL water, shaken every 10 min for 1 h, and allowed to stand undisturbed for 3 hours (4 h total) before reading final swollen volume in mL. Because Plantago ovata arabinoxylan mucilage resides strictly in the outer seed epidermis (husk/bhusi), isolated Isabgol husk swells \u003e=40 mL/g vs \u003e=10 mL/g for intact whole seeds.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00544","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry II","topic":"Glycyrrhizin-Induced Pseudoaldosteronism","q":"A 53-year-old man consuming large daily amounts of herbal liquorice (Glycyrrhiza glabra / Yashtimadhu) lozenges develops severe hypertension (178/104 mmHg), muscle weakness, metabolic alkalosis, and serum potassium 2.6 mEq/L, yet plasma renin activity and serum aldosterone levels are suppressed (\u003c1 ng/dL). By what molecular mechanism does glycyrrhetinic acid produce this \u0027pseudoaldosteronism\u0027?","o":["Inhibits angiotensinogen synthesis while inducing severe hyperkalaemia","Blocks epithelial sodium channels (ENaC) in the collecting duct like amiloride","Inhibits renal 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 (11beta-HSD2), preventing local conversion of active cortisol to inactive cortisone so high circulating cortisol illicitly activates mineralocorticoid receptors","Directly secretes excess adrenal aldosterone via ACTH tumour stimulation"],"a":2,"e":"Cortisol binds the mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) with equal affinity to aldosterone and circulates at 100-1,000x higher concentration; renal collecting duct cells rely on 11beta-HSD2 to inactivate cortisol into cortisone so only aldosterone regulates MR. Glycyrrhizin (hydrolysed by gut bacteria to glycyrrhetinic acid) potently inhibits 11beta-HSD2, allowing cortisol to flood MR—driving Na+ retention, hypertension, K+ wasting, and suppressed endogenous renin and aldosterone (\u0027apparent mineralocorticoid excess\u0027).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00545","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Microbiology","topic":"IP Microbiological Assay of Antibiotics","q":"In the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) Microbiological Assay of Antibiotics (such as streptomycin, gentamicin, or penicillin), what parameter is plotted against log10 antibiotic concentration in Method A (Cylinder-Plate / Cup-Plate agar diffusion) versus Method B (Turbidimetric broth assay)?","o":["Method A plots mean diameter of circular zone of growth inhibition (mm) vs log concentration; Method B plots optical absorbance/transmittance (turbidity inhibition) of inoculated broth vs log concentration","Method A measures haemolysis of sheep RBCs; Method B measures pH drop via litmus","Both methods count colonies on dry filter paper without comparing against a Reference Standard","Method A uses HPLC UV peak height; Method B uses flame photometry"],"a":0,"e":"IP Chapter 2.2.10 specifies two parallel biological potency assays comparing test antibiotic dilutions against an official Reference Standard (typically 3+3 dose design): Method A (Cylinder-Plate / Cup-Plate) measures the circular inhibition zone diameter (mm) produced by radial agar diffusion during 18-24 h incubation; Method B (Turbidimetric) measures broth optical density (530-580 nm) after 3-4 h incubation (suitable for water-soluble or poorly diffusing antibiotics like gramicidin/tyrothricin).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00546","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Engineering","topic":"Perforated Basket vs Tubular Bowl Centrifuge","q":"In pharmaceutical downstream processing, which centrifuge type operates by centrifugal filtration through a perforated drum lined with filter cloth (ideal for harvesting and washing crystalline API slurries), versus centrifugal sedimentation at ~15,000-20,000 G in a narrow vertical cylinder (ideal for separating immiscible oil-water emulsions or harvesting bacterial cells)?","o":["Ball mill for emulsion breaking; cyclone separator for liquid-liquid extraction","Tubular bowl centrifuge for coarse crystal cake washing; plate-and-frame press for ultrasonic lysis","Fluidised-bed dryer for continuous centrifugal filtration","Perforated basket centrifuge for crystalline slurry filtration/cake washing; tubular bowl (super) centrifuge for high-G liquid-liquid separation and fine submicron cell/precipitate sedimentation"],"a":3,"e":"A perforated basket centrifuge rotates a porous metal basket lined with filter medium at 1,000-2,000 rpm, forcing mother liquor outward through the retained crystal cake (allowing efficient cake washing and spin-drying). Conversely, a non-perforated tubular bowl centrifuge (e.g., Sharples super-centrifuge) spins a long, narrow cylinder at 15,000+ rpm generating \u003e13,000-20,000 G—ideal for breaking stable emulsions, separating plasma/blood fractions, or clarifying fine microbial/protein precipitates.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00547","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Instrumental Methods of Analysis","topic":"Chemical Derivatisation \u0026 GC Detectors (FID vs ECD)","q":"When analysing polar, thermally labile hydroxyl/carboxylic acid drug metabolites or trace organochlorine pesticide residues by Gas Chromatography (GC), why is silylation (e.g., with BSTFA to form trimethylsilyl derivatives) performed prior to injection, and which GC detector provides femtogram-level selectivity specifically for halogenated/electronegative analytes?","o":["Silylation replaces active polar -OH/-COOH hydrogens with non-polar trimethylsilyl groups to eliminate hydrogen bonding, increase volatility, and improve thermal stability; Electron Capture Detector (ECD) offers ultra-high sensitivity for halogenated/electronegative compounds","Derivatisation reduces molecular volatility so analytes stay permanently at the injector port","Silylation adds heavy lead atoms to slow down elution; Thermal Conductivity Detector (TCD) destroys samples selectively","Silylation converts volatile hydrocarbons into non-volatile ionic salts; Flame Ionisation Detector (FID) detects only noble gases like helium"],"a":0,"e":"Polar -OH, -NH, and -COOH functional groups form strong intermolecular hydrogen bonds causing high boiling points, tailing on GC columns, and thermal decomposition. Silylation replaces active protons with -Si(CH3)3 groups, boosting volatility and sharp symmetrical peaks. Among GC detectors, FID responds nearly universally to C-H organics via ionisation in a H2-air flame, whereas ECD (beta-emitting 63Ni source generating standing electron current) selectively captures electrons on electronegative halogens/nitro groups with femtogram-picogram sensitivity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00548","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Instrumental Methods of Analysis","topic":"Atomic Absorption Spectroscopy (AAS)","q":"Why does Atomic Absorption Spectroscopy (AAS) employ an element-specific Hollow Cathode Lamp (HCL) rather than a broad-spectrum continuum deuterium/tungsten lamp as its primary radiation source?","o":["The Hollow Cathode Lamp heats the liquid sample to 10,000 C without needing a flame or furnace","Ground-state gaseous metal atoms in the flame/graphite furnace absorb extremely narrow discrete atomic resonance lines (~0.002-0.005 nm wide); a Hollow Cathode Lamp lined with the exact analyte element emits matching sharp atomic emission lines required for high selectivity and Beer-Lambert sensitivity","Broad continuum lamps emit purely radiofrequency waves for NMR splitting","A tungsten lamp emits X-rays that shatter metal nuclei inside the nebuliser"],"a":1,"e":"Free unexcited ground-state atoms generated in an AAS flame or graphite furnace have ultra-narrow absorption line widths (~0.002 nm) that conventional monochromators (bandpass ~0.2-1.0 nm) cannot isolate from a broadband continuum lamp without severe loss of sensitivity and linearity. Alan Walsh solved this using a Hollow Cathode Lamp whose cathode contains the target element (e.g., Pb, Zn, Ca, Fe), emitting sharp resonance lines matching the analyte\u0027s exact electronic transitions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00549","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Drug Accumulation Index (Rac) at Steady State","q":"An oral antibiotic obeying linear one-compartment kinetics has an elimination half-life (t_1/2) of 6 hours and is administered repeatedly every 6 hours (dosing interval tau = 6 hours). What is the drug\u0027s steady-state Accumulation Index (R_ac)?","o":["4.0","1.5","2.0 (steady-state plasma concentrations are twice those after the first dose)","1.0 (zero accumulation between doses)"],"a":2,"e":"The multiple-dose Accumulation Index is R_ac = 1 / [1 - e^(-k_e * tau)], where e^(-k_e * tau) is the fraction of dose remaining at the end of one dosing interval. Since tau = 6 h equals one half-life (t_1/2 = 6 h), k_e * tau = 0.693 and e^(-0.693) = 0.50. Therefore R_ac = 1 / (1 - 0.50) = 1 / 0.50 = 2.0.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00550","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Quality Assurance","topic":"Out-of-Specification (OOS) Investigation Protocol","q":"An analyst obtains a failing Out-of-Specification (OOS) HPLC assay result of 87.2% (specification: 90.0-110.0%) for a finished tablet batch. According to USFDA/WHO GMP OOS Investigation Guidelines, what MUST be done FIRST before discarding original volumetric flasks or re-testing the batch?","o":["Release the batch immediately to distribution assuming the HPLC column was simply tired","Delete the failing chromatographic file from the Empower/Chromeleon database without logging","Immediately discard the original sample flasks and repeat the assay 5 times until a passing 95% value appears (\u0027testing into compliance\u0027)","Retain all original test preparations/solutions intact and conduct a Phase I Laboratory Investigation (verifying calculations, pipette/balance logs, HPLC chromatogram integration, and instrument calibration) to identify any assignable laboratory error before progressing to Phase II manufacturing investigation"],"a":3,"e":"Under GMP/USFDA Guidance for Industry (Investigating OOS Test Results), the analyst must notify the QC supervisor immediately and preserve all original dilutions/vials while executing a structured Phase I Laboratory Assessment. Original OOS data may ONLY be invalidated if a clear, documented assignable lab error is proven; arbitrary re-sampling or averaging passing retests over unexplained failing OOS results (\u0027testing into compliance\u0027) is a critical GMP/data-integrity violation. Without proven lab error, a full Phase II manufacturing/CAPA investigation is mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00551","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacovigilance","topic":"Dechallenge, Rechallenge \u0026 Disproportionality Signal Metrics","q":"In post-marketing pharmacovigilance signal detection across large spontaneous ADR databases (such as VigiBase or FAERS), what do \u0027positive dechallenge\u0027 and \u0027Proportional Reporting Ratio (PRR)\u0027 signify respectively?","o":["Positive dechallenge means the adverse event subsides/resolves after withdrawing the suspected drug; PRR compares the proportion of reports for a specific ADR with a target drug against the proportion of that same ADR across all other drugs in the database","Positive dechallenge means the drug was never dispensed; PRR measures serum trough concentration","Positive dechallenge means the patient dies immediately after stopping the drug; PRR measures tablet dissolution rate","Positive dechallenge means doubling the dose cures the rash; PRR measures clinical trial recruitment speed"],"a":0,"e":"In individual case causality assessment (WHO-UMC / Naranjo), positive dechallenge (clinical improvement upon stopping the suspect drug) and positive rechallenge (recurrence on re-exposure, when ethical/inadvertent) strongly support drug causality. At the population database level, disproportionality statistics such as Proportional Reporting Ratio (PRR = [a/(a+b)] / [c/(c+d)], often flagged when PRR \u003e= 2, chi-square \u003e= 4, and N \u003e= 3) identify potential safety signals warranting formal epidemiological evaluation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00552","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Tonicity Calculations","q":"A pharmacist must prepare 100 mL of an isotonic 1% w/v pilocarpine hydrochloride ophthalmic solution. Given that the sodium chloride equivalent (E-value) of pilocarpine hydrochloride is 0.24, how many grams of sodium chloride must be added to render the final 100 mL solution isotonic with lacrimal fluid?","o":["1.14 g","0.66 g","0.90 g","0.24 g"],"a":1,"e":"100 mL of 1% w/v pilocarpine HCl contains 1.0 g of pilocarpine HCl. Multiplying by its E-value (0.24) gives the NaCl tonicity equivalent of the drug: 1.0 g × 0.24 = 0.24 g NaCl. Because 100 mL of an isotonic NaCl solution requires 0.90 g of NaCl (0.9% w/v), the additional NaCl needed = 0.90 g - 0.24 g = 0.66 g.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00553","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Chemical Kinetics and Shelf Life","q":"An aqueous antibiotic syrup undergoes first-order hydrolytic degradation at 25 °C with a rate constant (k) of 0.0005 day^-1. Defining shelf-life (t90) as the time required for potency to decline to 90% of initial concentration, what is the predicted t90 of this syrup at 25 °C?","o":["105 days","200 days","210 days (approx. 211 days)","1,386 days"],"a":2,"e":"For a first-order reaction, C = C0 × e^(-kt), so at 90% potency remaining (C/C0 = 0.90): t90 = [-ln(0.90)] / k = 0.10536 / k (commonly approximated as 0.105 / k). Substituting k = 0.0005 day^-1: t90 = 0.105 / 0.0005 = 210 days (exact 0.10536 / 0.0005 = 210.7 days).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00554","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Non-Newtonian Flow and Thixotropy","q":"A pharmaceutical gel exhibits a progressive decrease in apparent viscosity as shear rate increases, and on a rheogram displays a hysteresis loop where the down-curve lies to the left of (lower shear stress than) the up-curve before slowly recovering its gel structure at rest. How is this rheological behavior classified?","o":["Ideal Newtonian flow","Negative thixotropy (antithixotropy)","Dilatant (shear-thickening) flow with rheopexy","Pseudoplastic (shear-thinning) flow with thixotropy"],"a":3,"e":"Pseudoplastic systems show decreasing apparent viscosity with increasing shear rate (shear-thinning), passing through the origin with concave curvature toward the shear-rate axis. When this shear-induced breakdown of internal three-dimensional structure recovers time-dependently upon removing shear, the up-curve and down-curve fail to coincide, enclosing a hysteresis loop characteristic of thixotropy—a highly desirable property allowing easy pouring/spreading/injection upon shaking while preventing sedimentation at rest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00555","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"SAR of Aryloxypropanolamine Beta-Blockers","q":"Which combination of structural features characterizes cardioselective (beta-1 selective) aryloxypropanolamine beta-blockers such as atenolol and metoprolol compared with non-selective propranolol?","o":["A para-substituted phenyl ring with an oxymethylene (-OCH2-) spacer and an S-configured secondary hydroxyl carbon in the propanolamine side chain","A fused naphthalene ring with an unsubstituted ortho-position and a primary amine lacking N-alkyl branching","A meta-disubstituted catechol ring lacking any oxymethylene spacer and an R-configured hydroxyl carbon","A quaternary ammonium nitrogen replacing the secondary isopropyl/tert-butyl amine"],"a":0,"e":"Aryloxypropanolamine beta-blockers possess an oxymethylene (-OCH2-) bridge inserting between the aromatic ring and the ethanolamine backbone, making the S-enantiomer (equivalent spatially to R-epinephrine/isoprenaline) 50–100 times more potent than the R-enantiomer. Placing polar or linear hydrogen-bonding substituents exclusively at the para-position of the phenyl ring (e.g., -CH2CONH2 in atenolol, -CH2CH2OCH3 in metoprolol) confers preferential beta-1 over beta-2 receptor selectivity, whereas bicyclic naphthyl rings like propranolol are non-selective.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00556","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"Second-Generation H1 Antagonists","q":"Why do second-generation H1 antihistamines such as fexofenadine and cetirizine produce markedly less drowsiness than first-generation agents like diphenhydramine or hydroxyzine?","o":["Second-generation agents lack the basic tertiary amine required for H1 receptor binding","Introduction of polar ionizable groups (such as a carboxylic acid yielding zwitterions) reduces passive blood-brain barrier penetration and increases efflux via P-glycoprotein","Second-generation agents are rapidly hydrolyzed in cerebrospinal fluid before binding H1 receptors","Second-generation agents selectively block central H3 autoreceptors to stimulate alertness"],"a":1,"e":"First-generation H1 antagonists (e.g., diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine) are lipophilic weak bases that readily cross the blood-brain barrier (BBB) and block central H1 (and muscarinic) receptors, causing sedation. Second-generation H1 antagonists (such as cetirizine, the active carboxylic acid metabolite of hydroxyzine, and fexofenadine, the carboxylate metabolite of terfenadine) incorporate polar carboxylate/zwitterionic functionality at physiological pH and serve as P-glycoprotein substrates, restricting brain entry while retaining peripheral H1 potency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00557","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"Penicillinase-Resistant Penicillins","q":"Assertion (A): Cloxacillin and methicillin resist hydrolysis by Staphylococcus aureus beta-lactamase (penicillinase) far better than benzylpenicillin (penicillin G). Reason (R): Bulky ortho-substituted aromatic or isoxazolyl acyl side chains sterically hinder the approach of the penicillinase active-site serine to the beta-lactam carbonyl carbon.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Penicillin G has a flexible, sterically unhindered benzyl side chain, allowing staphylococcal beta-lactamase to attack and open the strained 4-membered beta-lactam ring. Methicillin (2,6-dimethoxyphenyl) and isoxazolyl penicillins like cloxacillin/dicloxacillin introduce bulky ortho substituents adjacent to the amide carbonyl that act as a steric shield, preventing productive binding to penicillinase while preserving transpeptidase (PBP) acylation in methicillin-sensitive S. aureus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00558","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"HMG-CoA Reductase Inhibitors (Statins)","q":"Which pair of statins is administered orally as inactive closed-ring lactone prodrugs requiring in vivo hepatic/plasma esterase hydrolysis to the active 3,5-dihydroxyheptanoic acid pharmacophore mimicking HMG-CoA?","o":["Pravastatin and fluvastatin","Pitavastatin and atorvastatin","Simvastatin and lovastatin","Atorvastatin and rosuvastatin"],"a":2,"e":"Natural/semi-synthetic statins lovastatin and simvastatin are formulated as lipophilic, inactive delta-lactone prodrugs that undergo enzymatic hydrolysis in vivo to expose the active beta,delta-dihydroxy acid moiety structurally mimicking the tetrahedral intermediate of HMG-CoA. In contrast, pravastatin and fully synthetic statins (atorvastatin, rosuvastatin, fluvastatin, pitavastatin) are already administered in their open, intrinsically active dihydroxy acid (salt) forms.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00559","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"5-HT Agonists in Acute Migraine","q":"A 52-year-old man with stable angina pectoris and hypertension requests oral sumatriptan for severe throbbing unilateral headaches. Why are triptans contraindicated in patients with coronary artery disease, and through which receptors do they relieve acute migraine?","o":["Triptans are 5-HT3 antagonists that dilate coronary arteries and worsen hypotension","Triptans are non-selective alpha-1 blockers causing reflex tachycardia","Triptans inhibit peripheral cyclooxygenase-1, precipitating aspirin-exacerbated myocardial ischemia","Triptans are selective 5-HT1B/1D agonists that constrict dilated meningeal vessels and inhibit trigeminal CGRP release, but can also cause coronary vasospasm via vascular 5-HT1B receptors"],"a":3,"e":"Sumatriptan and other triptans act as selective agonists at 5-HT1B receptors on intracranial meningeal blood vessels (producing vasoconstriction) and presynaptic 5-HT1D receptors on trigeminal sensory nerve terminals (blocking release of vasodilatory neuropeptides like CGRP and substance P). Because human coronary arteries also express 5-HT1B receptors, triptans can induce coronary vasospasm and are contraindicated in ischemic heart disease, Prinzmetal angina, stroke/TIA, and uncontrolled hypertension.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00560","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antiplatelet Therapy","q":"A 60-year-old man undergoes drug-eluting coronary stent placement and is discharged on aspirin plus clopidogrel. To provide gastroprotection while minimizing pharmacokinetic interference with clopidogrel activation, which proton pump inhibitor is preferred over omeprazole?","o":["Pantoprazole (minimal CYP2C19 inhibition), preserving hepatic CYP2C19 bioactivation of clopidogrel to its active P2Y12-blocking thiol metabolite","Esomeprazole, because it directly activates platelet P2Y12 receptors","Omeprazole, because CYP2C19 inhibition enhances clopidogrel bioavailability","Lansoprazole high-dose IV bolus every 6 hours exclusively"],"a":0,"e":"Clopidogrel is an inactive thienopyridine prodrug requiring two-step hepatic CYP450 oxidation—heavily dependent on CYP2C19—to generate its active thiol metabolite that irreversibly blocks platelet ADP P2Y12 receptors. Strong CYP2C19 inhibitors such as omeprazole and esomeprazole attenuate generation of the active metabolite and reduce antiplatelet efficacy; pantoprazole (or rabeprazole) exerts minimal CYP2C19 inhibition and is preferred for GI prophylaxis alongside clopidogrel.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00561","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimetabolites (Methotrexate)","q":"During high-dose methotrexate chemotherapy for osteosarcoma, leucovorin (folinic acid) rescue is initiated 24 hours post-infusion alongside aggressive IV hydration and sodium bicarbonate. Why can leucovorin selectively rescue normal host cells without reversing methotrexate\u0027s blockade of dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR)?","o":["Leucovorin chemically cleaves methotrexate in the bloodstream before it enters tumor cells","Leucovorin (5-formyltetrahydrofolate) is already a reduced folate that bypasses the blocked DHFR enzyme to supply tetrahydrofolate cofactors for DNA synthesis","Leucovorin inhibits renal tubular secretion of methotrexate via OAT1/OAT3 transporters","Leucovorin blocks polyglutamation selectively inside healthy bone marrow stem cells only"],"a":1,"e":"Methotrexate potently inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), depleting intracellular reduced tetrahydrofolate (THF) pools required for thymidylate and purine synthesis. Leucovorin (folinic acid / 5-formyl-THF) enters cells via the reduced folate carrier and converts directly to active N5,N10-methylene-THF without needing DHFR, rescuing rapidly dividing normal host tissues (bone marrow and GI mucosa) while urinary alkalinization (pH \u003e= 7.0) keeps methotrexate and 7-hydroxymethotrexate soluble to prevent intratubular precipitation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00562","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Ocular Pharmacology (Glaucoma)","q":"A 63-year-old woman with primary open-angle glaucoma and mild persistent bronchial asthma is prescribed latanoprost 0.005% ophthalmic drops once nightly rather than timolol 0.5% drops. What is the primary mechanism of latanoprost in lowering intraocular pressure, and what local adverse effect should she be counselled about?","o":["Inhibits carbonic anhydrase II in the ciliary epithelium; metabolic acidosis","Constricts the ciliary muscle via M3 activation; acute retinal detachment","Increases uveoscleral outflow of aqueous humor; permanent darkening of iris pigmentation and eyelash lengthening/thickening","Decreases aqueous humor synthesis by blocking ciliary beta-2 receptors; severe bronchospasm"],"a":2,"e":"Latanoprost is a prostaglandin F2-alpha (FP receptor) analogue prodrug that lowers intraocular pressure primarily by enhancing uveoscleral outflow via matrix metalloproteinase-mediated remodeling of the ciliary muscle extracellular matrix. Unlike topical timolol (which can absorb via the nasolacrimal duct to trigger asthma exacerbation), latanoprost is safe in asthma, though patients should be informed about gradual iris hyperpigmentation (melanin synthesis in melanocytes), periocular skin darkening, and hypertrichosis of eyelashes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00563","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy \u0026 Phytochemistry","topic":"Amino Acid Precursors of Alkaloids","q":"Which pair of primary precursors combines biosynthetically in Solanaceae plants (such as Atropa belladonna and Datura stramonium) to form the tropane alkaloid hyoscyamine/atropine?","o":["L-Tyrosine and mevalonic acid","L-Lysine and nicotinic acid","L-Tryptophan and secologanin","L-Ornithine (forming the tropine bicyclic ring) and L-phenylalanine (forming tropic acid)"],"a":3,"e":"Tropane alkaloids (hyoscyamine, atropine, scopolamine) are esters of tropine (or scopine) and tropic acid. Biosynthetically, the pyrrolidine/tropane bicyclic nucleus arises from L-ornithine (via putrescine and N-methylpyrrolinium cation), while the aromatic tropic acid moiety derives from L-phenylalanine via phenylpyruvate rearrangement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00564","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy \u0026 Phytochemistry","topic":"Flavonoid and Steroid Chemical Tests","q":"During phytochemical screening of a methanolic extract of Ginkgo biloba leaves, adding magnesium turnings followed dropwise by concentrated hydrochloric acid produces an intense magenta-pink color. Which phytochemical class and diagnostic test does this confirm?","o":["Flavonoids confirmed by the Shinoda test","Indole alkaloids confirmed by Van Urk\u0027s reagent","Anthraquinones confirmed by Borntrager\u0027s test","Steroidal sapogenins confirmed by the Liebermann-Burchard test"],"a":0,"e":"The Shinoda test (magnesium-hydrochloric acid reduction) identifies flavonoids (flavones, flavonols, flavanones, dihydroflavonols). Nascent hydrogen generated from Mg + HCl reduces the gamma-pyrone ring to intensely colored anthocyanidin/benzopyrylium derivatives, yielding pink, crimson, or magenta coloration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00565","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy \u0026 Phytochemistry","topic":"Taxanes vs Vinca Alkaloids","q":"Paclitaxel (originally isolated from Pacific yew bark, Taxus brevifolia, and semi-synthesized from 10-deacetylbaccatin III of Taxus baccata) differs mechanistically from vincristine in disrupting mitosis by which action?","o":["Paclitaxel crosslinks guanine N7 atoms across complementary DNA strands","Paclitaxel is a diterpenoid taxane that binds beta-tubulin to hyperstabilize polymerized microtubules and block depolymerization (M-phase arrest)","Paclitaxel intercalates DNA base pairs and inhibits topoisomerase II exclusively in S-phase","Paclitaxel is a bis-indole alkaloid that prevents tubulin dimer polymerization"],"a":1,"e":"Paclitaxel is a complex diterpene pseudoalkaloid (taxane) obtained commercially by semi-synthesis from 10-deacetylbaccatin III extracted renewably from European/Himalayan yew needles. Unlike vinca alkaloids (vincristine, vinblastine), which bind beta-tubulin to inhibit microtubule assembly, paclitaxel binds the luminal site on beta-tubulin of intact polymers, promoting assembly and preventing normal dynamic depolymerization—freezing the mitotic spindle in G2/M phase.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00566","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"HPLC Column Efficiency","q":"In the Van Deemter equation describing chromatographic band broadening (H = A + B/u + C·u), what happens to theoretical plate height (H) when replacing a 5 µm silica particle HPLC column with a sub-2 µm UHPLC packed column at high mobile-phase linear velocity (u)?","o":["Both eddy diffusion (A) and resistance to mass transfer (C) decrease sharply with smaller particle diameter (dp), keeping H low even at high flow rates","Longitudinal diffusion (B/u) increases exponentially at high velocity, destroying efficiency","Theoretical plate height H doubles while plate number N decreases proportionally","Van Deemter resistance to mass transfer (C) becomes inversely proportional to flow velocity"],"a":0,"e":"In the Van Deemter equation, H is height equivalent to a theoretical plate (lower H = higher column efficiency N = L/H): A represents eddy diffusion (proportional to particle diameter dp), B/u is longitudinal molecular diffusion (significant only at very low flow velocity u), and C·u is resistance to mass transfer between phases (proportional to dp²). Shrinking dp from 5 µm to \u003c2 µm dramatically reduces A and especially C (by \u003e6-fold), flattening the right side of the Van Deemter curve so high linear velocities yield rapid separations without sacrificing resolution.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00567","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Structure Elucidation by IR and 1H-NMR","q":"An organic intermediate exhibits a strong, sharp IR absorption band at 1738 cm^-1 (with no broad O-H band from 2500–3300 cm^-1) and in its 1H-NMR spectrum displays a 2H quartet at delta 4.12 ppm coupled (J = 7 Hz) to a 3H triplet at delta 1.25 ppm. Which functional moiety is present?","o":["An ethyl amide (-CO-NH-CH2CH3)","A carboxylic acid (-COOH) with a methyl substituent","An ethyl ester (-CO-O-CH2CH3)","An aliphatic ketone (-CO-CH2CH3)"],"a":2,"e":"A strong IR carbonyl C=O stretch near 1735–1740 cm^-1 without broad carboxylic O-H absorption is diagnostic of a saturated aliphatic ester. In the 1H-NMR spectrum, the n+1 splitting rule produces a 2H quartet and 3H triplet for an ethyl group (-CH2CH3), and the downfield shift of the methylene quartet to delta ~4.12 ppm confirms direct attachment to the electronegative ester oxygen (-O-CH2-CH3).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00568","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Stability Testing for Climatic Zone IVb","q":"Because India falls under Climatic Zone IVb (Hot and Very Humid), what are the mandatory long-term and accelerated stability testing storage conditions (temperature / relative humidity) for general solid oral drug products in impermeable/semi-permeable primary packaging?","o":["Long-term: 30 °C ± 2 °C / 35% RH ± 5% RH; Accelerated: 50 °C ± 2 °C / 90% RH ± 5% RH","Long-term: 25 °C ± 2 °C / 60% RH ± 5% RH; Accelerated: 30 °C ± 2 °C / 65% RH ± 5% RH","Long-term: 5 °C ± 3 °C; Accelerated: 25 °C ± 2 °C / 60% RH ± 5% RH","Long-term: 30 °C ± 2 °C / 75% RH ± 5% RH; Accelerated: 40 °C ± 2 °C / 75% RH ± 5% RH"],"a":3,"e":"Following WHO Technical Report Series (TRS 953 / 1010) and CDSCO stability guidelines for Climatic Zone IVb (Hot/Higher Humidity, covering India), general drug products stored at room temperature require long-term stability testing at 30 °C ± 2 °C / 75% RH ± 5% RH (minimum 12 months at submission) and accelerated testing at 40 °C ± 2 °C / 75% RH ± 5% RH (6 months).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00569","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Biotechnology","topic":"Host Expression Systems","q":"Why are full-length therapeutic monoclonal antibodies (e.g., trastuzumab) and erythropoietin produced commercially in Chinese Hamster Ovary (CHO) mammalian cells rather than Escherichia coli, even though recombinant human insulin is successfully produced in E. coli?","o":["CHO cells perform human-compatible N- and O-linked glycosylation and disulfide folding essential for antibody Fc effector function and serum half-life, whereas E. coli lacks eukaryotic glycosylation machinery","E. coli cannot transcribe mammalian cDNA sequences lacking introns","CHO cells replicate faster than E. coli and require zero sterile culture media","E. coli secretes therapeutic proteins exclusively with toxic lipopolysaccharide covalently linked to the Fab region"],"a":0,"e":"Human insulin is a small 51-amino-acid non-glycosylated two-chain peptide whose A and B chains (or proinsulin) can be expressed in E. coli as inclusion bodies and refolded chemically. By contrast, IgG monoclonal antibodies (~150 kDa) and erythropoietin require complex eukaryotic N-linked glycosylation (e.g., conserved Asn297 glycan in the Fc CH2 domain for ADCC/CDC effector function and proper pharmacokinetics) and endoplasmic reticulum chaperones for correct assembly—capabilities absent in prokaryotes like E. coli but faithfully performed by mammalian CHO cells.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00570","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Biotechnology","topic":"Immobilization Techniques","q":"Which enzyme immobilization method avoids chemical modification of amino acid residues at the enzyme\u0027s active site (thereby minimizing conformational inactivation) by physically confining the enzyme within a cross-linked calcium alginate or polyacrylamide gel lattice?","o":["Cross-linked enzyme aggregates (CLEA)","Entrapment (matrix gel entrapment)","Ionic/physical adsorption onto uncharged charcoal","Covalent binding using glutaraldehyde"],"a":1,"e":"In matrix entrapment, enzymes are physically occluded inside a porous polymeric network (such as calcium alginate formed by dripping sodium alginate + enzyme into CaCl2, or polyacrylamide) without forming covalent bonds to the enzyme molecule. This mild technique preserves native active-site conformation, though it suits small diffusible substrates/products best due to mass-transfer diffusion limitations for macromolecular substrates.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00571","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy","topic":"Freeze-Drying Process Stages","q":"During the primary drying stage of lyophilizing an amorphous monoclonal antibody formulation, why must the product temperature be maintained below its glass transition temperature of the maximally freeze-concentrated solute (Tg\u0027) while chamber pressure is held well below the triple point of water (611 Pa / 4.58 Torr)?","o":["To melt the frozen matrix rapidly and evaporate liquid water by boiling","To denature residual host-cell proteins irreversibly","To ensure ice sublimes directly from solid to vapor without melting or viscous cake collapse","To promote crystallization of bound hydration water during secondary drying"],"a":2,"e":"Primary drying removes bulk frozen ice crystals by direct solid-to-vapor sublimation, requiring chamber vacuum pressure below water\u0027s triple point (0.0098 °C, 611 Pa or ~4.58 Torr). Keeping product temperature safely below the formulation\u0027s critical collapse temperature (typically Tg\u0027 for amorphous lyophiles or eutectic temperature Teu for crystalline matrices) preserves the rigid porous structure of the dried cake; exceeding Tg\u0027 causes viscous flow, cake collapse, high residual moisture, and poor reconstitution.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00572","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy","topic":"Aerosol Propellants","q":"Following the Montreal Protocol phase-out of ozone-depleting chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), which hydrofluoroalkane propellants replaced CFC-11/CFC-12 in pressurized Metered-Dose Inhalers (pMDIs) because they contain zero chlorine atoms?","o":["Trichlorofluoromethane (Propellant 11) and dichlorodifluoromethane (Propellant 12)","Nitrogen and compressed carbon dioxide as liquefied propellants","Propane and isobutane exclusively for all oral inhalers","HFA-134a (tetrafluoroethane) and HFA-227ea (heptafluoropropane)"],"a":3,"e":"CFC propellants (such as CFC-11 and CFC-12) release chlorine radicals in the stratosphere that catalyze ozone depletion and were phased out under the Montreal Protocol. Non-chlorinated hydrofluoroalkanes—specifically HFA-134a (1,1,1,2-tetrafluoroethane) and HFA-227ea (1,1,1,2,3,3,3-heptafluoropropane)—have zero ozone depletion potential and serve as standard liquefied pMDI propellants (often requiring ethanol co-solvent and modified valve elastomers due to higher polarity than CFCs).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00573","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy","topic":"Quality Control of Parenterals","q":"In routine quality control of large-volume parenterals, the Bacterial Endotoxins Test (BET / LAL test) detects pyrogenic lipopolysaccharide (LPS) from Gram-negative bacteria by observing gel-clot formation or chromogenic cleavage. From which organism is the LAL reagent derived, and which positive-control standard quantifies sensitivity?","o":["Circulating amebocytes of the Atlantic horseshoe crab (Limulus polyphemus); calibrated against Reference Standard Endotoxin (E. coli LPS in EU/mL)","Hepatic macrophages of New Zealand White rabbits; calibrated against pyrogenic exotoxin A","Bovine plasma fibrinogen; calibrated against fungal beta-glucan","Hemolymph of marine sponges; calibrated against peptidoglycan units"],"a":0,"e":"The LAL (Limulus Amebocyte Lysate) assay uses an aqueous extract of blood cells (amebocytes) from the horseshoe crab Limulus polyphemus (or Tachypleus tridentatus for TAL). Trace Gram-negative bacterial endotoxin (lipopolysaccharide, LPS) activates a serine protease coagulation cascade (Factor C -\u003e Factor B -\u003e proclotting enzyme -\u003e coagulin gel or chromogenic substrate cleavage), standardized in Endotoxin Units (EU/mL) using USP/IP Reference Standard Endotoxin from E. coli.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00574","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Drug Accumulation and Steady State","q":"A drug exhibiting linear one-compartment pharmacokinetics with an elimination half-life (t1/2) of 8 hours is administered intravenously every 8 hours (dosing interval tau = t1/2). What is the accumulation index (R) at steady state, and approximately what percentage of steady state is reached after 5 half-lives (40 hours)?","o":["R = 1.0; approximately 50.0% of steady state","R = 2.0; approximately 96.9% of steady state","R = 4.0; approximately 99.9% of steady state","R = 1.44; approximately 75.0% of steady state"],"a":1,"e":"The steady-state accumulation ratio (R) for repetitive fixed-dose administration is R = 1 / [1 - e^(-k·tau)]. When the dosing interval tau equals one elimination half-life (tau = t1/2), k·tau = 0.693, so e^(-0.693) = 0.5, yielding R = 1 / (1 - 0.5) = 2.0 (steady-state peak and trough concentrations are twice those after the first dose). The fraction of steady state reached after n half-lives is 1 - (0.5)^n; for n = 5, 1 - (0.5)^5 = 1 - 1/32 = 31/32 = 96.875% (~96.9%).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00575","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Hepatic Extraction Ratio (ER)","q":"Propranolol has a high hepatic extraction ratio (ER ≈ 0.8), whereas warfarin has a very low hepatic extraction ratio (ER \u003c 0.05). According to the well-stirred model of hepatic clearance, how does a 30% reduction in hepatic blood flow (QH, as in decompensated heart failure) affect the systemic intravenous clearance of each drug?","o":["Propranolol clearance falls almost proportionally (~30%) because it is blood-flow-limited (CL_H ≈ Q_H), whereas warfarin clearance remains largely unaffected because it is capacity-limited (CL_H ≈ fu × CL_int)","Warfarin clearance drops by 30% while propranolol clearance doubles","Both propranolol and warfarin clearances increase due to prolonged sinusoidal transit time","Propranolol clearance is governed exclusively by unbound fraction (fu) and is insensitive to QH"],"a":0,"e":"Under the well-stirred hepatic clearance model, CL_H = Q_H × (fu × CL_int) / (Q_H + fu × CL_int). For high-extraction drugs (ER \u003e 0.7, e.g., propranolol, lidocaine, morphine) where fu × CL_int \u003e\u003e Q_H, the equation simplifies to CL_H ≈ Q_H (perfusion/blood-flow-limited); thus, reduced cardiac output and hepatic blood flow directly lowers clearance. For low-extraction drugs (ER \u003c 0.3, e.g., warfarin, phenytoin) where Q_H \u003e\u003e fu × CL_int, CL_H ≈ fu × CL_int (capacity/enzyme-limited), remaining insensitive to changes in Q_H.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00576","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Dissolution Profile Comparison","q":"When comparing multipoint dissolution profiles between a reformulated test tablet and the reference listed drug using the Moore and Flanner similarity factor (f2), what numerical range of f2 confirms profile similarity (\u003c= 10% average difference), and how many dosage units per product are minimally tested?","o":["f2 between 15 and 49; minimum 6 units per product","f2 \u003e 100; minimum 24 units per product","f2 between 50 and 100; minimum 12 units of test and 12 units of reference product","f2 between 0 and 15; minimum 3 units per product"],"a":2,"e":"Regulatory guidances (CDSCO, USFDA SUPAC, EMA) use the Moore and Flanner similarity factor f2 = 50 × log{[1 + (1/n) Σ(Rt - Tt)²]^(-0.5) × 100} to compare dissolution curves across \u003e= 3 time points (with at most one point exceeding 85% dissolved), testing at least 12 dosage units of each formulation. An f2 value between 50 and 100 ensures \u003c= 10% average difference across sampling times, demonstrating dissolution similarity (f2 = 100 indicates identical profiles).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00577","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics I","topic":"Gibbs Condensed Phase Rule \u0026 Binary Eutectic System","q":"Lidocaine and prilocaine form a 1:1 binary eutectic mixture (used in EMLA topical cream) that melts below room temperature. Applying the condensed Gibbs phase rule (F\u0027 = C - P + 1, at constant atmospheric pressure) at the exact eutectic point where solid lidocaine, solid prilocaine, and the homogeneous liquid melt coexist in equilibrium, how many degrees of freedom (F\u0027) remain?","o":["Zero degrees of freedom (F\u0027 = 0; invariant temperature and composition)","One degree of freedom (F\u0027 = 1; temperature can vary freely)","Two degrees of freedom (F\u0027 = 2; both temperature and composition vary simultaneously)","Three degrees of freedom (F\u0027 = 3)"],"a":0,"e":"At constant atmospheric pressure, the condensed phase rule is F\u0027 = C - P + 1. For a binary (2-component, C = 2) eutectic system at the eutectic point, three phases coexist in equilibrium: pure solid A, pure solid B, and liquid eutectic melt (P = 3). Thus F\u0027 = 2 - 3 + 1 = 0, making the eutectic temperature and eutectic composition strictly invariant.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00578","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics II","topic":"Ostwald Capillary Viscometer vs Rotational Viscometers","q":"During quality testing of a pseudoplastic Carbopol topical gel versus aqueous glycerin solution, why can an Ostwald U-tube capillary viscometer accurately characterise glycerin ONLY, whereas a multipoint rotational Brookfield or cone-and-plate viscometer is mandatory for the Carbopol gel?","o":["Carbopol gel dissolves glass capillary walls within 2 seconds","Ostwald viscometers measure only yield value while Brookfield measures density","Brookfield viscometers apply zero shear stress to measure Newtonian boiling points","Ostwald is a single-point viscometer operating at one fixed gravitational shear stress valid solely for shear-independent Newtonian fluids (glycerin), whereas multipoint rotational viscometers vary shear rate/RPM to construct the complete rheogram and hysteresis loop of shear-thinning non-Newtonian gels"],"a":3,"e":"In Newtonian liquids (water, simple syrup, glycerin), viscosity is constant regardless of shear rate, so a single-point efflux time in an Ostwald capillary viscometer suffices. Non-Newtonian fluids (pseudoplastic polymers, Bingham creams, thixotropic suspensions) exhibit shear-rate-dependent apparent viscosity; characterising their flow curve, yield value, and thixotropic hysteresis requires varying rotational speed across multiple shear rates using concentric-cylinder (Cup-and-Bob/Brookfield) or cone-and-plate viscometers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00579","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy I","topic":"Preservatives, Toxicity \u0026 Intraocular Contraindications","q":"Benzalkonium chloride (BAK, 0.01% w/v) is a widely used quaternary ammonium antimicrobial preservative in multi-dose eye drops, yet it is strictly EXCLUDED from single-dose intraocular irrigating solutions/injections and drops used over soft hydrogel contact lenses. Why?","o":["BAK is toxic to corneal endothelium during intraocular surgery (causing corneal oedema) and concentrates inside porous soft contact lenses causing prolonged epithelial irritation; furthermore, cationic BAK precipitates with anionic drugs like salicylates or sulfacetamides","BAK turns eye drops deep blue upon exposure to sunlight","BAK permanently dissolves rigid polymethylmethacrylate (PMMA) IOL implants","BAK raises intraocular pressure purely via pupillary miosis"],"a":0,"e":"Per IP/USP ophthalmic standards, any solution entering the anterior/posterior chamber during intraocular surgery (e.g., BSS, intracameral antibiotics) MUST be preservative-free and single-use because preservatives like BAK damage non-regenerating corneal endothelial cells. Moreover, cationic BAK binds/accumulates in soft contact lenses causing superficial punctate keratitis and is chemically incompatible with anionic drugs (nitrates, salicylates, fluorescein sodium).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00580","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy II","topic":"Regulatory Tiers of Post-Approval Formulation/Process Changes","q":"Under SUPAC-IR (Scale-Up and Post-Approval Changes for Immediate-Release Solid Oral Dosage Forms), when a manufacturer changes an excipient grade or component percentage substantially beyond Level 2 limits (e.g., altering disintegrant \u003e +/-6% w/w or changing total tablet weight \u003e10%) in a BCS Class II poorly soluble drug without an established Level A IVIVC, what Level classification and testing are required?","o":["Level 0 change — exempt from GMP stability testing permanently","Level 3 change — requires a Prior Approval Supplement (PAS), multipoint comparative dissolution across media, AND an in vivo bioequivalence study comparing post-change vs pre-change batches","Level 1 change — requires only an internal diary note with zero dissolution or filing","Annual Report notification alone without dissolution testing"],"a":1,"e":"SUPAC-IR stratifies post-approval changes by likelihood of impacting bioavailability: Level 1 (minor changes unlikely to impact quality/PK -\u003e Annual Report + compendial dissolution); Level 2 (moderate changes that could impact quality/PK -\u003e Changes Being Effected/PAS + multipoint f2 dissolution in multiple pH media); Level 3 (major changes likely to significantly impact formulation quality/performance -\u003e Prior Approval Supplement + full multipoint dissolution + in vivo human bioequivalence study unless a validated Level A IVIVC or BCS biowaiver applies).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00581","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry I","topic":"SAR of First- vs Second-Generation H1 Antagonists","q":"First-generation H1 antihistamines (diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine) readily cross the blood-brain barrier causing marked sedation and anticholinergic dry mouth, whereas second-generation agents (cetirizine, fexofenadine) are largely non-sedating. Which medicinal chemistry modification explains this loss of CNS penetration?","o":["Introduction of a polar ionisable carboxylic acid (-COOH) forming a hydrophilic zwitterion at physiological pH 7.4 (plus active BBB P-glycoprotein efflux, notably for fexofenadine) while retaining peripheral H1 receptor affinity","Addition of three lipophilic naphthyl rings increasing log P above +7","Removal of the ethylamine spacer chain completely","Replacing the tertiary amine with an uncharged nitroalkane"],"a":0,"e":"Converting hydroxyzine into its active carboxylate metabolite cetirizine—or terfenadine into fexofenadine—introduces an ionised carboxylate (-COO-) alongside the protonated amine (-NH+). Being zwitterionic and far more polar (lower log D_7.4) plus acting as a substrate for brain capillary endothelial P-glycoprotein efflux pumps (strongly for fexofenadine, moderately for cetirizine/loratadine) blocks passive CNS entry while preserving potent peripheral H1 inverse agonism.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00582","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry II","topic":"SAR of Sulfonylureas (Tolbutamide vs Chlorpropamide vs Glipizide)","q":"In the medicinal chemistry of first- and second-generation sulfonylurea insulin secretagogues (Ar-SO2-NH-CO-NH-R), why does chlorpropamide exhibit a much longer half-life (~36 hours) and higher risk of prolonged hypoglycaemia/SIADH than tolbutamide (~4-6 hours), and what modification yielded 100-fold more potent second-generation agents (glipizide, glibenclamide)?","o":["Chlorpropamide has a 5-minute half-life due to rapid plasma esterase cleavage","Tolbutamide contains a fluorine atom that explodes in stomach acid; chlorpropamide is excreted unchanged in faeces within 1 hour","Tolbutamide\u0027s para-methyl (-CH3) group is rapidly oxidised hepatically to an inactive carboxylic acid, whereas chlorpropamide\u0027s para-chloro (-Cl) blocks CYP oxidative inactivation; second-generation agents replace simple para-groups with a bulky polar carboxamidoethyl-aryl moiety matching secondary SUR1 binding pockets","Replacing the sulfonylurea bridge with an ester group eliminates SUR1 K-ATP binding"],"a":2,"e":"First-generation tolbutamide carries a metabolically labile para-CH3 group rapidly oxidised by hepatic CYP2C9 to hydroxymethyl and inactive carboxy-tolbutamide (short t1/2 ~4-6 h). Substituting para-Cl (chlorpropamide) resists metabolic oxidation and slows renal clearance (t1/2 ~33-36 h), causing severe prolonged hypoglycaemia and ADH potentiation (SIADH). Second-generation sulfonylureas (glibenclamide, glipizide, glimepiride) append a large lipophilic/polar aryl-carboxamidoethyl substituent onto the para-position, engaging an auxiliary SUR1 pocket for ~100x greater potency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00583","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry II","topic":"SAR of 1,4-Dihydropyridines (Nifedipine / Amlodipine)","q":"In the structure-activity relationship (SAR) of 1,4-dihydropyridine (1,4-DHP) L-type calcium channel blockers (nifedipine, amlodipine, felodipine), which combination of structural features is ESSENTIAL for potent arterial vasodilatory activity?","o":["A 1,4-dihydropyridine ring with an unsubstituted N-1 (-NH-), a C-4 phenyl ring bearing ortho- or meta-electron-withdrawing substituents (-NO2, -Cl) forcing a perpendicular orientation relative to the DHP boat ring, and C-3/C-5 ester groups","Oxidation of the 1,4-DHP ring to an aromatic pyridine ring and bulky para-tert-butyl substitution on the C-4 phenyl","N-1 methylation and complete removal of the C-4 aromatic ring","Converting both C-3 and C-5 esters into free carboxylic acids (-COOH)"],"a":0,"e":"1,4-DHP SAR requires: (1) an intact flattened-boat 1,4-dihydropyridine ring with unsubstituted N-1 NH acting as an H-bond donor (oxidising to planar pyridine—e.g. nifedipine photodegradation—abolishes activity!); (2) a C-4 phenyl ring orthogonal (pseudo-axial) to the DHP plane, stabilised by ortho- or meta-substituents (para-substitution sterically clashes and destroys activity); (3) C-3 and C-5 ester groups (unsymmetrical esters like amlodipine/felodipine create a chiral C-4 centre with eutomer selectivity; free diacids lose activity).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00584","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry III","topic":"Prodrug vs Soft-Drug Design (Esmolol)","q":"How does the medicinal chemistry \u0027Soft Drug\u0027 design of the intravenous beta-1 blocker esmolol (t_1/2 ~ 9 minutes) fundamentally differ from a classic \u0027Prodrug\u0027 such as enalapril?","o":["A prodrug (enalapril) is administered INACTIVE and requires enzymatic bioactivation in vivo, whereas a soft drug (esmolol) is administered ALREADY ACTIVE and intentionally incorporates a labile methyl ester cleaved rapidly by erythrocyte cytosolic esterases to an inactive polar carboxylic acid","Both prodrugs and soft drugs are administered completely inactive and activated only by renal CYP450","A soft drug resists all metabolism indefinitely giving a 6-month half-life","Esmolol is converted by plasma esterases into propranolol to extend duration to 48 hours"],"a":0,"e":"Prodrugs mask polar groups to improve delivery and become pharmacologically active only after metabolic cleavage. By contrast, a Soft Drug (Bodor\u0027s retrometabolic concept, exemplified by esmolol, remifentanil, and clevidipine) is intrinsically active as administered and is deliberately engineered with a metabolically fragile ester linkage so ubiquitous tissue/blood esterases (for esmolol, red blood cell cytosolic esterases) rapidly hydrolyse it into a single predictable, non-toxic, inactive metabolite (t1/2 ~ 9 min)—ideal for rapid titratable ICU infusions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00585","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology I","topic":"Competitive vs Non-Competitive Antagonism \u0026 Schild pA2","q":"In an isolated guinea-pig ileum bioassay, adding increasing concentrations of Antagonist X shifts the acetylcholine log-concentration-response curve progressively to the right in parallel fashion WITHOUT reducing maximal contraction (E_max = 100%), whereas adding Antagonist Y depresses E_max down to 45% regardless of how high acetylcholine is pushed. How are Antagonists X and Y classified, and what does the Schild plot parameter pA2 measure for Antagonist X?","o":["Antagonist X is a reversible competitive antagonist (surmountable by higher agonist; pA2 = negative log of antagonist molar concentration requiring 2-fold agonist concentration to restore response); Antagonist Y is an irreversible or non-competitive antagonist (insurmountable)","Antagonist X is a non-competitive allosteric inhibitor; Antagonist Y is a pure partial agonist","Both X and Y are inverse agonists acting on nuclear steroid receptors","pA2 measures oral hepatic first-pass extraction ratio of Antagonist Y"],"a":0,"e":"A reversible competitive antagonist competes dynamically for the exact orthosteric agonist binding site: high agonist concentrations outcompete it, preserving E_max while shifting EC_50 rightward in parallel. Plotting log(Dose Ratio - 1) vs -log[Antagonist] (Schild plot) yields slope ~1 and x-intercept pA2 = -log(K_B), quantifying antagonist affinity. An irreversible/non-competitive antagonist covalently disables the receptor or binds an insurmountable allosteric site, depressing E_max (unless large spare-receptor reserve initially buffers low antagonist doses).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00586","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Long-Acting Muscarinic Antagonist (Tiotropium)","q":"A 67-year-old ex-smoker with moderate-to-severe COPD is initiated on once-daily inhaled tiotropium bromide (LAMA). Which pharmacological property explains tiotropium\u0027s 24-hour bronchodilator duration without paradoxical acetylcholine surge, and what comorbidity requires careful inhalation technique/caution?","o":["Tiotropium is a PDE-4 inhibitor crossing the BBB rapidly; causes severe insomnia and suicidal ideation as its primary M3 mechanism","Tiotropium breaks down airway mucin disulfide bonds directly like acetylcysteine","Tiotropium selectively stimulates bronchial beta-2 receptors; caution in hypothyroidism exclusively","Tiotropium dissociates much more slowly from airway smooth muscle M3 receptors (kinetic selectivity) than from presynaptic inhibitory M2 autoreceptors; caution in benign prostatic hyperplasia (urinary retention) and narrow-angle glaucoma (avoid eye spray contact)"],"a":3,"e":"Although tiotropium binds M1, M2, and M3 muscarinic receptors with similar affinity, it exhibits kinetic selectivity—dissociating rapidly from presynaptic M2 autoreceptors (preserving physiological negative feedback on acetylcholine release) while remaining bound to bronchoconstrictor M3 (and M1) receptors for \u003e24 hours. Being a quaternary ammonium salt, systemic absorption is minimal, yet patients must protect eyes from mist/powder (angle-closure glaucoma precipitation) and be monitored for urinary retention in BPH.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00587","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology III","topic":"Imatinib (BCR-ABL TKI) \u0026 Trastuzumab (Anti-HER2 mAb)","q":"Which pair accurately matches the molecular oncogenic driver targeted by oral imatinib in chronic myeloid leukaemia (CML) versus the dose-limiting organ toxicity requiring serial echocardiographic (LVEF) monitoring during intravenous trastuzumab therapy in HER2-positive breast cancer?","o":["Imatinib competitively blocks the ATP-binding pocket of constitutively active BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase [t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome]; Trastuzumab (anti-HER2/ErbB2 mAb) requires serial LVEF monitoring for reversible cardiomyopathy (heightened when given with/after anthracyclines)","Imatinib targets EGFR in melanoma; Trastuzumab causes haemorrhagic cystitis reversed by Mesna","Imatinib inhibits topoisomerase I; Trastuzumab causes irreversible pulmonary fibrosis via iodine content","Both imatinib and trastuzumab alkylate guanine N7 non-selectively causing severe alopecia in 100% of patients"],"a":0,"e":"Imatinib mesylate inhibits the inactive conformation of BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase generated by the t(9;22) Philadelphia translocation in CML (and also inhibits c-KIT in GIST and PDGFR). Trastuzumab is a humanised IgG1 monoclonal antibody against the extracellular domain of HER2/neu (ErbB2); because myocardial HER2 signalling mediates stress survival, trastuzumab causes Type II (typically reversible) left ventricular dysfunction, necessitating baseline and q3-month echocardiogram/MUGA LVEF surveillance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00588","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry I","topic":"Supercritical Fluid Extraction (SCFE) vs Soxhlet","q":"When isolating heat-sensitive volatile terpenoids, astaxanthin, or decaffeinating coffee beans industrially, why is Supercritical Fluid Extraction (SCFE) using carbon dioxide (T_c = 31.1 C, P_c = 73.8 bar) preferred over traditional hot-solvent Soxhlet extraction?","o":["Supercritical CO2 combines gas-like low viscosity/high diffusivity with liquid-like solvating density just above mild ambient temperature (31.1 C), extracting thermolabile lipophilic compounds rapidly and depressurising cleanly with zero toxic organic solvent residue","Supercritical CO2 operates only above 350 C, pyrolysing plant cellulose into bio-oil","Soxhlet extraction uses zero solvent and runs at -78 C","Supercritical CO2 is a strongly polar aqueous acid that hydrolyses all glycosides"],"a":0,"e":"Above its critical point (31.1 C and 73.8 bar / 72.8 atm), CO2 becomes a supercritical fluid whose density (and polarity/solvating power, tunable by pressure or small ethanol cosolvent) rivals organic solvents like hexane while retaining low viscosity and zero surface tension for deep matrix penetration. Upon pressure release, CO2 evaporates completely as a harmless gas, leaving solvent-free, thermally undegraded botanical extracts.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00589","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry II","topic":"Podophyllotoxin vs Etoposide Mechanism Switch","q":"Natural aryltetralin lignan podophyllotoxin (from Podophyllum hexandrum rhizomes) is too toxic for systemic oncology and is restricted to topical warts, whereas its semisynthetic glycoside derivative etoposide is a cornerstone intravenous agent for testicular cancer and small-cell lung cancer. How does semisynthetic modification alter its molecular mechanism?","o":["Natural podophyllotoxin inhibits microtubule assembly by binding tubulin; epimerisation at C-4, 4\u0027-O-demethylation, and beta-D-glucopyranoside conjugation in etoposide abolishes tubulin binding and converts it into a Topoisomerase II poison generating lethal double-strand DNA breaks","Etoposide reverts back to tubulin polymerisation blockade in the stomach","Podophyllotoxin inhibits DNA Topoisomerase I; etoposide acts purely as a folic acid analogue","Both compounds act exclusively as cell-wall beta-lactamase inhibitors"],"a":0,"e":"Native podophyllotoxin binds tubulin at the colchicine site to block microtubule polymerisation (used topically as podofilox for condylomata acuminata). Semisynthetic conversion to etoposide (and teniposide)—4\u0027-demethylation + C-4 epimerisation + bulky cyclic acetal beta-D-glucoside attachment—eliminates anti-tubulin activity and transforms the molecule into a Topoisomerase II inhibitor that stabilises the enzyme-DNA cleavable complex, causing cytotoxic double-strand breaks in late S/G2 phase.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00590","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Microbiology","topic":"Bacterial Growth Curve Phases \u0026 Antibiotic Susceptibility","q":"In a batch culture bacterial growth curve (Lag, Log/Exponential, Stationary, and Death phases), during which phase are bacteria MOST susceptible to cell-wall-active beta-lactam antibiotics (e.g., penicillin G, ceftriaxone), and why?","o":["Exponential (Log) phase — cells are dividing at maximum balanced rate with active peptidoglycan cross-linking (transpeptidation), triggering rapid autolysin-mediated lysis","Death phase — all viable cells have already lysed","Stationary phase — nutrient depletion halts cell division and creates tolerant persister cells","Lag phase — cells are dormant and have zero peptidoglycan synthesis"],"a":0,"e":"Beta-lactam antibiotics bind Penicillin-Binding Proteins (PBPs) to inhibit peptidoglycan cross-linking during active cell-wall synthesis and division, tipping the balance toward cell-wall autolysins and osmotic lysis. Because cell division and peptidoglycan turnover peak during the Exponential (Log) phase, bactericidal killing by beta-lactams is maximal here; non-dividing Lag or Stationary phase persisters are phenotypically tolerant (which is why bacteriostatic drugs arresting cells out of Log phase can antagonise penicillins in meningitis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00591","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Engineering","topic":"Falling-Film \u0026 Wiped-Film Evaporators","q":"When concentrating thermolabile biological insulin solutions, vitamin syrups, or delicate botanical extracts on an industrial scale, why is a vacuum Falling-Film or Wiped-Film Evaporator chosen over an open steam-jacketed kettle evaporator?","o":["Wiped-film evaporators operate at 10 atm pressure to raise water boiling point to 180 C","Gravity/mechanical wipers spread the feed into an ultra-thin turbulent liquid film down heated tubes under vacuum, achieving high heat-transfer coefficients and very short residence times (seconds) at low boiling temperatures without charring","Open steam-jacketed kettles achieve 0.5-second residence times at 180 C without oxidation","Falling-film evaporators deliberately stagnate liquid at the bottom for 12 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Open jacketed pans hold large liquid volumes for long periods at \u003e=100 C, causing caramelisation, hydrolysis, and potency loss. In Falling-Film and Agitated Thin-Film (Wiped-Film) evaporators, liquid flows rapidly as a thin film along vertical heated surfaces under reduced pressure (vacuum lowering boiling point to ~35-55 C), completing concentration in a single pass within seconds—minimising thermal exposure and fouling even for viscous extracts.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00592","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Instrumental Methods of Analysis","topic":"The Nitrogen Rule in Organic Mass Spectrometry","q":"An unknown alkaloid isolated from a medicinal plant exhibits a molecular ion peak [M]+ at m/z = 289 (odd nominal mass) in its Electron Impact mass spectrum (e.g., atropine, C17H23NO3). According to the Nitrogen Rule in mass spectrometry, what does an ODD nominal molecular weight prove about the elemental composition of a neutral C, H, N, O, S, halogen compound?","o":["The neutral molecule contains zero nitrogen atoms","The compound must contain at least two bromine isotopes","The neutral molecule MUST contain an ODD number of nitrogen atoms (e.g., 1, 3, 5 N atoms)","The neutral molecule MUST contain an EVEN number of nitrogen atoms (e.g., 2, 4, 6 N atoms)"],"a":2,"e":"Because nitrogen has an even atomic mass (14) yet an ODD valence (3)—unlike C (mass 12, valence 4), O (mass 16, valence 2), and S (mass 32, valence 2)—neutral organic molecules containing C, H, O, S, and halogens always have EVEN nominal molecular weights if they have 0 or an EVEN number of nitrogen atoms (2, 4...), and ODD nominal molecular weights if and only if they contain an ODD number of nitrogen atoms (1, 3, 5...). Thus m/z 289 confirms 1 (or odd) nitrogen atom.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00593","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Instrumental Methods of Analysis","topic":"Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) vs Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC)","q":"A pharmaceutical hydrate batch shows an endothermic event at 115 C on Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC) followed by a sharp melt at 210 C. How does coupling DSC with Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) definitively distinguish whether the 115 C endotherm represents dehydration of crystal water versus a solid-solid polymorphic transition?","o":["TGA tracks sample mass vs temperature: crystal dehydration causes a quantitative stoichiometric weight loss step at ~115 C, whereas a solid-solid polymorphic phase transition shows a DSC enthalpy peak with ZERO mass change on TGA","TGA measures UV absorbance while DSC measures pH","A polymorphic transition always causes 50% weight loss on TGA while dehydration shows zero weight loss","Both TGA and DSC measure only liquid refractive index"],"a":0,"e":"DSC (and DTA) monitors heat-flow/temperature differences associated with physical or chemical enthalpy transitions—such as melting, glass transition (T_g), crystallisation, dehydration, and polymorphic conversion—even when no mass escapes. TGA continuously weighs the sample during heating: loss of volatile solvent/water of crystallisation produces an exact quantitative mass-loss step (confirming monohydrate/dihydrate stoichiometry), whereas pure polymorphic transitions, melting, and T_g exhibit zero TGA weight loss.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00594","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"High vs Low Hepatic Extraction Ratio (EH) Drugs","q":"Propranolol and verapamil are high-hepatic-extraction drugs (E_H ~ 0.75-0.85, low oral bioavailability F ~ 15-25% in healthy adults), whereas warfarin and phenytoin are low-extraction drugs (E_H \u003c 0.1). In a patient with decompensated liver cirrhosis and portosystemic shunting, how does oral bioavailability (F) and intravenous clearance (CL_H) of high-E_H propranolol behave?","o":["CL_H is perfusion/blood-flow limited (dependent primarily on hepatic blood flow Q_H), while portosystemic shunting and reduced first-pass extraction cause oral bioavailability F (= 1 - E_H) to surge dramatically (3- to 5-fold), risking severe toxicity unless oral doses are markedly lowered","Oral bioavailability drops to zero while IV clearance triples","Both oral F and IV CL_H depend exclusively on plasma protein binding f_u with zero dependence on Q_H","High-E_H drugs never undergo hepatic first-pass metabolism"],"a":0,"e":"From the well-stirred venous-equilibration hepatic model, for high-extraction drugs (f_u * CL_int \u003e\u003e Q_H), systemic hepatic clearance approaches hepatic blood flow (CL_H ~ Q_H), largely insensitive to modest changes in protein binding or intrinsic enzyme activity. However, oral bioavailability F = F_abs * F_g * (1 - E_H) is tiny in healthy subjects (e.g., 0.15) due to massive first-pass extraction; in cirrhosis with portosystemic shunts bypassing hepatocytes, E_H drops and oral F spikes sharply, causing exaggerated bradycardia/hypotension from standard oral propranolol/verapamil doses.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00595","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Quality Assurance","topic":"Process Capability Index (Cpk) \u0026 Control Charts","q":"In GMP Continued Process Verification (Stage 3 Process Validation), a tablet weight monitoring team calculates a Process Capability Index C_pk = 1.67 on a Shewhart X-bar / R control chart, versus C_pk = 0.72 on an older press line. What does C_pk \u003e= 1.33 signify about process centering and variation relative to specification limits?","o":["Control charts replace the need for specification limits completely","C_pk = 1.67 means 67% of tablets fail weight variation immediately","C_pk measures only tablet hardness in Newtons without using specification limits","C_pk \u003e= 1.33 indicates the process is well-centred with tight natural variation (+/- 4 sigma or better within nearest specification limit), yielding very low defect/OOS probability; C_pk \u003c 1.0 (0.72) warns the process mean or spread encroaches on specification limits"],"a":3,"e":"Process Capability Index C_pk = min[(USL - mean)/(3*sigma), (mean - LSL)/(3*sigma)] evaluates both how narrow the process spread (sigma) is AND how well centred the mean sits between Upper and Lower Specification Limits (unlike C_p, which ignores off-centering). A C_pk = 1.33 corresponds to 4-sigma clearance (~63 defects per million), whereas C_pk \u003c 1.0 (\u003c3 sigma) signals high risk of Out-of-Specification batches requiring corrective engineering action.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00596","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Practice","topic":"Primary, Secondary \u0026 Tertiary Biomedical Literature","q":"In hospital Drug Information Centre (DIC) practice, match each biomedical literature category to its correct representative examples: 1) Primary literature, 2) Secondary literature, 3) Tertiary literature.","o":["1-Original peer-reviewed RCTs, cohort studies \u0026 case reports (e.g., NEJM, Lancet, Indian Journal of Pharmacology); 2-Indexing \u0026 abstracting bibliographic databases (e.g., PubMed/MEDLINE, Embase, Scopus); 3-Synthesised textbooks, compendia \u0026 pharmacopoeias (e.g., Martindale, Goodman \u0026 Gilman, Micromedex, IP)","1-PubMed/MEDLINE; 2-Goodman \u0026 Gilman; 3-Original clinical case reports","1-Martindale \u0026 IP; 2-NEJM original RCTs; 3-PubMed/MEDLINE indexing","1-Unverified social media blogs; 2-Newspaper ads; 3-Manufacturer promotional flyers"],"a":0,"e":"Systematic drug information retrieval progresses from Tertiary sources (fast background overview: textbooks, drug monographs like Martindale/Lexicomp/AHFS/IP), through Secondary databases (indexing/abstracting tools locating primary articles: PubMed/MEDLINE, Embase, IPA), to Primary literature (unfiltered original research papers, RCTs, and ADR case reports providing the newest evidence).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00597","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation","q":"An acetate buffer for a topical solution is prepared containing 0.20 M sodium acetate and 0.02 M acetic acid (pKa = 4.76 at 25 °C). Using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, what is the resulting pH of the buffer?","o":["4.76","5.76","3.76","6.76"],"a":1,"e":"For a weak acid and its conjugate base salt, the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation states: pH = pKa + log10([Salt] / [Acid]). Substituting pKa = 4.76, [Salt] = 0.20 M, and [Acid] = 0.02 M: pH = 4.76 + log10(0.20 / 0.02) = 4.76 + log10(10) = 4.76 + 1.00 = 5.76.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00598","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Micellization, Krafft Point, and Cloud Point","q":"When heating an aqueous solution of a nonionic polyoxyethylated surfactant (such as Polysorbate 80 / Tween 80) during autoclave sterilization, the clear micellar solution suddenly turns turbid above a critical temperature. What is this phenomenon called and what causes it?","o":["Cloud point; dehydration of hydrophilic polyoxyethylene ether chains weakens hydrogen bonding with water, causing micellar phase separation","Krafft point; insoluble crystalline surfactant melts into soluble ionic micelles above this temperature","Critical Micelle Concentration (CMC); surface tension drops abruptly to zero at 100 °C","Eutectic point; surfactant precipitates irreversibly as a solid salt"],"a":0,"e":"Nonionic surfactants containing polyoxyethylene (PEO) chains owe their water solubility to hydrogen bonding between ether oxygens and water molecules. Heating above a characteristic temperature—the Cloud point—breaks these hydrogen bonds and dehydrates the PEO headgroups, causing the surfactant to become less soluble and separate into a cloudy surfactant-rich phase (typically reversible on cooling). Conversely, the Krafft point applies to ionic surfactants: the minimum temperature at which surfactant solubility equals the CMC so micelles can form.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00599","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Noyes-Whitney Dissolution Equation","q":"According to the modified Noyes-Whitney dissolution equation, dM/dt = (D · A / h) · (Cs - Cb), which formulation strategy increases dissolution rate specifically by increasing saturation solubility (Cs) in the stagnant diffusion layer without altering the particle surface area (A)?","o":["Increasing the total volume of dissolution medium from 500 mL to 900 mL","Increasing paddle rotation speed from 50 rpm to 100 rpm in the dissolution vessel","Converting a poorly soluble weak acid/base drug into a water-soluble pharmaceutical salt (e.g., phenytoin sodium or diclofenac potassium)","Micronizing the crystalline free-acid drug powder in a fluid energy jet mill"],"a":2,"e":"In the Noyes-Whitney equation, D is the diffusion coefficient, A is effective surface area, h is diffusion layer thickness, Cs is saturation solubility at the solid-liquid interface, and Cb is bulk concentration. Forming a water-soluble salt alters the microenvironmental pH inside the stagnant diffusion layer (h), elevating Cs orders of magnitude above the free acid/base and dramatically accelerating dissolution rate even at identical particle surface area A.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00600","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"SAR of Reversible Cholinesterase Inhibitors","q":"Both physostigmine and neostigmine are reversible carbamate acetylcholinesterase inhibitors, yet only physostigmine effectively reverses central anticholinergic delirium (e.g., atropine overdose), while neostigmine is preferred for peripheral myasthenia gravis. What structural difference explains this selectivity?","o":["Physostigmine lacks the carbamate ester group required for peripheral neuromuscular junction binding","Neostigmine contains a strongly acidic sulfonic acid moiety repelled by nicotinic receptors","Neostigmine is an irreversible organophosphate that crosses the BBB too slowly","Physostigmine contains a tertiary amine (partially unionized at physiological pH) that crosses the blood-brain barrier, whereas neostigmine contains a permanently charged quaternary ammonium group"],"a":3,"e":"Physostigmine is a natural pyrroloindole alkaloid possessing a tertiary amine (pKa ~7.9), leaving an appreciable unionized lipophilic fraction at pH 7.4 that readily traverses the blood-brain barrier to restore central acetylcholine in anticholinergic toxicity. Neostigmine (and pyridostigmine) was synthetically engineered with a permanent quaternary ammonium cation [-N+(CH3)3], conferring high water solubility, poor BBB penetration (minimal CNS side effects), and direct weak nicotinic agonist activity at the neuromuscular junction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00601","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"SAR of Fluoroquinolones","q":"Which structural moiety on ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin is directly responsible both for binding bacterial DNA gyrase (via Mg2+ coordination) and for forming insoluble chelates with oral calcium, iron, zinc, or aluminum-magnesium antacids?","o":["The coplanar C-3 carboxylic acid and C-4 keto oxygen (beta-keto acid system)","The C-7 piperazinyl ring nitrogen alone","The N-1 cyclopropyl or ethyl substituent","The C-6 fluorine atom alone"],"a":0,"e":"All clinically active fluoroquinolones share an essential coplanar 4-oxo-3-carboxylic acid (beta-keto acid) pharmacophore on the bicyclic quinolone core. Inside the bacterial cell, these adjacent oxygens coordinate a catalytic Mg2+ ion to form a water-Mg2+ bridge with DNA gyrase/topoisomerase IV; in the gastrointestinal lumen, the same beta-keto acid chelates polyvalent cations (Al3+, Mg2+, Ca2+, Fe2+, Zn2+) from antacids or supplements, drastically lowering oral bioavailability unless separated by 2–4 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00602","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"Ester vs Amide Local Anesthetics","q":"Assertion (A): Lidocaine (an amide local anesthetic) has a substantially longer plasma stability and much lower risk of allergic hypersensitivity than procaine (an ester local anesthetic). Reason (R): The two ortho-methyl groups on lidocaine\u0027s 2,6-xylidine aromatic ring sterically shield the amide bond from rapid hydrolysis, and amide metabolism does not liberate para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA).","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Procaine (an ester of para-aminobenzoic acid and diethylaminoethanol) is rapidly hydrolyzed by plasma pseudocholinesterase, releasing PABA—a well-known hapten responsible for hypersensitivity reactions. In contrast, lidocaine features an intrinsically stabler amide linkage flanked by two bulky ortho-methyl groups (2,6-dimethylphenyl / xylidine) that sterically protect the carbonyl from hydrolysis, requiring slower hepatic CYP450 N-dealkylation without generating PABA.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00603","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"Nucleoside Analogue Antivirals","q":"Why does acyclovir (acycloguanosine) exhibit high selective toxicity toward Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) and Varicella-Zoster Virus (VZV)-infected cells while sparing uninfected host cells?","o":["Acyclovir lacks a 3\u0027-OH group only after cleavage by host hepatic CYP3A4","Initial monophosphorylation of acyclovir requires viral thymidine kinase (~200-fold higher affinity than host kinase), followed by host conversion to acyclovir triphosphate which inhibits viral DNA polymerase and causes chain termination","Acyclovir directly dissolves viral lipid envelopes only at elevated fever temperatures","Host cells actively pump acyclovir out via MRP4 before any viral entry occurs"],"a":1,"e":"Acyclovir is an acyclic guanosine prodrug lacking the 2\u0027 and 3\u0027 carbons/hydroxyl of deoxyribose. Its selectivity rests on two viral barriers: (1) HSV/VZV-encoded thymidine kinase converts acyclovir to acyclovir monophosphate far more efficiently than mammalian thymidine kinase, trapping it inside infected cells; host kinases then generate acyclovir triphosphate; and (2) acyclovir triphosphate preferentially inhibits viral DNA polymerase and, lacking a 3\u0027-OH group, acts as an obligate DNA chain terminator.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00604","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Neuromuscular Blocking Agents","q":"A 26-year-old man with 45% total body surface area burns sustained 10 days ago requires emergency endotracheal intubation. Why is succinylcholine strictly avoided in this patient, and which antidote treats succinylcholine-triggered malignant hyperthermia?","o":["Succinylcholine causes immediate severe hypokalemia reversed by neostigmine; malignant hyperthermia is treated with atropine","Burns destroy plasma pseudocholinesterase, converting succinylcholine into rocuronium; treat malignant hyperthermia with physostigmine","Extrajunctional nicotinic acetylcholine receptor upregulation after burns causes massive lethal K+ efflux (hyperkalemia) with succinylcholine; malignant hyperthermia is treated with intravenous dantrolene","Succinylcholine is inactivated by burn wound exudate; treat malignant hyperthermia with protamine sulfate"],"a":2,"e":"Following major thermal burns (\u003e24–48 hours post-injury), denervation, crush trauma, or spinal cord injury, skeletal muscle fibers express abundant extrajunctional nicotinic ACh receptors across the entire sarcolemma. Depolarization by succinylcholine opens all these channels simultaneously, causing abrupt catastrophic K+ efflux, severe hyperkalemia, and cardiac arrest (non-depolarizing agents like rocuronium are used instead). Additionally, succinylcholine (with volatile anesthetics) can trigger malignant hyperthermia in RYR1-susceptible individuals, definitively treated with IV dantrolene (ryanodine receptor Ca2+-release blocker).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00605","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antiparkinsonian Drugs","q":"When levodopa is administered with carbidopa, adding a peripheral catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) inhibitor such as entacapone reduces \u0027wearing-off\u0027 motor fluctuations in Parkinson\u0027s disease. Which metabolic pathway does entacapone block?","o":["Central MAO-A degradation of serotonin inside raphe nuclei","Peripheral aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylation (handled by carbidopa)","Nigrostriatal dopamine D2 receptor internalization","Peripheral O-methylation of levodopa into inactive 3-O-methyldopa (3-OMD), prolonging levodopa plasma half-life and brain entry"],"a":3,"e":"When peripheral DOPA decarboxylase (AADC) is blocked by carbidopa, levodopa metabolism shifts predominantly toward catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT), converting levodopa into 3-O-methyldopa (3-OMD), which competes with levodopa for large neutral amino acid (LNAA) transport across the blood-brain barrier. Entacapone selectively inhibits peripheral COMT, increasing levodopa\u0027s plasma half-life and AUC and smoothing \u0027end-of-dose wearing-off\u0027 episodes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00606","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Incretin-Based Antidiabetic Drugs","q":"A 69-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and Stage 4 CKD (eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73 m²) requires an oral DPP-4 inhibitor added to basal insulin without needing any renal dose adjustment. Which DPP-4 inhibitor is predominantly excreted unchanged via the biliary/fecal route and requires zero dose reduction regardless of eGFR?","o":["Linagliptin","Sitagliptin","Saxagliptin","Vildagliptin"],"a":0,"e":"Most DPP-4 inhibitors (sitagliptin, saxagliptin, alogliptin, vildagliptin) rely substantially on renal clearance and require dose reductions (typically 50% to 75% reduction) or caution in moderate-to-severe CKD. Linagliptin uniquely exhibits high plasma protein binding and ~80–85% hepatobiliary/enterohepatic fecal elimination (\u003c5% renal excretion), permitting a single fixed 5 mg once-daily dose across all stages of renal impairment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00607","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Targeted Molecular Oncology","q":"Imatinib mesylate revolutionized chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) therapy by selectively occupying the ATP-binding pocket of which constitutively active fusion tyrosine kinase generated by the Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22) translocation?","o":["BRAF V600E serine-threonine kinase","BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase (as well as c-KIT and PDGFR)","Vascular endothelial growth factor receptor-2 (VEGFR-2) exclusively","Poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase-1 (PARP-1)"],"a":1,"e":"The reciprocal translocation t(9;22)(q34;q11) creates the Philadelphia chromosome encoding the chimeric BCR-ABL fusion oncoprotein with unregulated constitutive tyrosine kinase activity driving myeloid proliferation in CML. Imatinib competes at the ATP-binding site of BCR-ABL (stabilizing its inactive conformation) to halt downstream proliferative signaling and induce apoptosis, and also inhibits c-KIT (gastrointestinal stromal tumors) and PDGFR.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00608","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy \u0026 Phytochemistry","topic":"Cardiac Aglycone Structures","q":"Chemically, how do cardenolide cardiac aglycones (e.g., digitoxigenin from Digitalis) differ from bufadienolide aglycones (e.g., scillarenin from White Squill) at the C-17 position of the steroidal nucleus?","o":["Cardenolides possess a beta-oriented C-3 sugar and alpha-oriented C-14 OH, whereas bufadienolides lack the C-14 OH","Cardenolides contain an aromatic A-ring with zero lactone moiety, whereas bufadienolides contain a basic tropane nitrogen","Cardenolides are C23 steroids with a five-membered alpha,beta-unsaturated gamma-lactone (butenolide) ring, whereas bufadienolides are C24 steroids with a six-membered doubly unsaturated alpha-pyrone lactone ring","Cardenolides are C21 pregnanes, whereas bufadienolides are C30 triterpenes"],"a":2,"e":"All cardiac glycoside aglycones share a cis-trans-cis A/B/C/D steroid ring junction with a 14-beta-hydroxyl group and a 17-beta unsaturated lactone ring essential for Na+/K+-ATPase inhibition. Cardenolides (Digitalis, Strophanthus, Thevetia) have 23 carbons with a 5-membered monounsaturated lactone (positive Legal and Kedde tests), whereas bufadienolides (Urginea/Scilla, Bufo toad venom) have 24 carbons with a 6-membered doubly unsaturated delta-lactone (2-pyrone ring).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00609","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy \u0026 Phytochemistry","topic":"WHO / IP Herbal Standardization Parameters","q":"During quality control evaluation of powdered crude botanicals as per Indian Pharmacopoeia / WHO guidelines, which two respective parameters detect contamination with sand/siliceous earthy matter and quantify mucilage swelling capacity in Isabgol (Plantago ovata) husk?","o":["Total ash only and Foaming Index","Water-soluble ash and Bitter Value","Sulfated ash and Hemolytic Index","Acid-insoluble ash (residue remaining after boiling total ash in dilute HCl) and Swelling Index (volume in mL occupied by 1 g drug in water after 4 h)"],"a":3,"e":"Total ash captures both physiological mineral salts (carbonates/oxalates/phosphates) and extraneous matter; boiling total ash with dilute HCl dissolves physiological salts, leaving Acid-insoluble ash—primarily silica, sand, and earthy soil adulterants. Meanwhile, mucilage-rich bulk laxatives such as Isabgol (Plantago ovata seeds/husk) are standardized by the Swelling Index, measuring the mL volume attained by 1 g of drug shaken in water over 4 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00610","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy \u0026 Phytochemistry","topic":"Artemisinin (Qinghaosu)","q":"Artemisinin, isolated from the aerial parts of Artemisia annua (sweet wormwood), belongs to which terpenoid class, and which unique functional group is essential for its rapid schizontocidal antimalarial activity?","o":["Sesquiterpene lactone containing an internal 1,2,4-trioxane endoperoxide (-O-O-) bridge","Quinoline alkaloid containing a vinyl quinuclidine ring","Tetracyclic triterpenoid containing a spiroketal side chain","Monoterpene alcohol containing a primary allylic hydroxyl group"],"a":0,"e":"Artemisinin is a C15 sesquiterpene lactone incorporating an unusual 1,2,4-trioxane endoperoxide (-O-O-) bridge. Inside the Plasmodium food vacuole, ferrous iron (Fe2+) liberated during hemoglobin digestion cleaves the endoperoxide bridge via a Fenton-like reaction, generating carbon-centered free radicals that alkylate parasite proteins and heme, rapidly killing asexual blood-stage parasites (deoxyartemisinin lacking the peroxide bridge is completely inactive).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00611","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Ionization Techniques in Mass Spectrometry","q":"Why are Electrospray Ionization (ESI) and Matrix-Assisted Laser Desorption/Ionization (MALDI) classified as \u0027soft\u0027 ionization techniques preferred over 70 eV Electron Impact (EI) for analyzing polar thermolabile drugs and monoclonal antibodies?","o":["Soft ionization requires volatile non-polar analytes exclusively in GC-MS","Soft ionization transfers ions into the gas phase with minimal excess internal energy, preserving intact protonated/deprotonated molecular ions ([M+H]+ / [M-H]- or multiply charged ions) without thermal decomposition","Soft ionization operates exclusively at 700 °C to shatter macromolecules into single atomic cations","Soft ionization produces 100% fragmentation with zero intact molecular ion peak"],"a":1,"e":"Conventional 70 eV Electron Impact (EI) requires gas-phase volatilization and imparts high energy (~70 eV vs ~10 eV bond energies), causing extensive fragmentation and destroying non-volatile/thermolabile molecules. Soft ionization techniques like ESI (generating singly or multiply charged ions directly from liquid droplets at atmospheric pressure, ideal for LC-MS/MS and large proteins within standard quadrupole m/z ranges) and MALDI gently desorb/ionize intact molecular species with minimal fragmentation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00612","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"System Suitability Testing (USP / IP)","q":"In an HPLC system suitability chromatogram for a fixed-dose combination tablet, Peak 1 elutes at tR1 = 6.0 min with a baseline peak width (W1) of 0.4 min, and Peak 2 elutes at tR2 = 7.5 min with a baseline peak width (W2) of 0.6 min. What is the chromatographic resolution (Rs) between the two peaks?","o":["6.0","1.5","3.0","0.75"],"a":2,"e":"The USP/IP chromatographic resolution equation using baseline peak widths is Rs = [2 × (tR2 - tR1)] / (W1 + W2). Substituting the values: Rs = [2 × (7.5 - 6.0)] / (0.4 + 0.6) = (2 × 1.5) / 1.0 = 3.0. Because Rs \u003e= 2.0 (baseline separation typically starts at Rs \u003e= 1.5), the separation comfortably meets compendial system suitability.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00613","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Pharmaceutical Grade Water Specifications","q":"According to pharmacopoeial standards (IP/USP/EP), what are the official acceptance limits for Bacterial Endotoxins and Total Organic Carbon (TOC) in Water for Injection (WFI)?","o":["Bacterial Endotoxins \u003c= 0.25 EU/mL; Total Organic Carbon (TOC) \u003c= 500 ppb (0.5 mg/L)","Bacterial Endotoxins \u003c= 5.0 EU/mL; Total Organic Carbon (TOC) \u003c= 5,000 ppb","Bacterial Endotoxins \u003c= 2.5 EU/mL; Total Organic Carbon (TOC) \u003c= 100 ppm","Purified Water and WFI have no endotoxin specification because both are boiled prior to use"],"a":0,"e":"Water for Injection (WFI) is the highest-purity pharmaceutical grade water used as vehicle for parenteral and sterile preparations. Compendia (IP/USP/EP) mandate Bacterial Endotoxins \u003c= 0.25 EU/mL (absent in Purified Water specs, which only controls microbial bioburden \u003c= 100 CFU/mL), TOC \u003c= 500 ppb (0.50 mg/L), conductivity \u003c= 1.3 µS/cm at 25 °C (Stage 1), and total aerobic microbial count \u003c= 10 CFU/100 mL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00614","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Biotechnology","topic":"Selection of Recombinant Clones","q":"A foreign gene is ligated into the multiple cloning site (MCS) within the lacZ-alpha sequence of a pUC19 plasmid and transformed into competent E. coli plated on ampicillin + IPTG + X-gal agar. Which colony phenotype identifies bacteria harboring the recombinant plasmid containing the foreign DNA insert?","o":["Red colonies, because IPTG converts ampicillin into phenol red","Blue colonies, because the foreign gene accelerates hydrolysis of X-gal into indigo dye","No colonies grow on ampicillin because the foreign insert destroys the ampR gene","White colonies, because inserting foreign DNA disrupts lacZ-alpha complementation so beta-galactosidase is inactive and cannot cleave X-gal"],"a":3,"e":"In blue-white screening (insertional inactivation of lacZ-alpha), ampicillin eliminates untransformed bacteria lacking the ampR plasmid. Intact non-recombinant pUC19 produces the LacZ-alpha peptide, which complements the host\u0027s omega fragment upon IPTG induction to form functional beta-galactosidase that cleaves chromogenic X-gal into an insoluble blue product (blue colonies). When foreign DNA inserts into the MCS inside lacZ-alpha, alpha-complementation is disrupted, leaving X-gal uncleaved and producing white recombinant colonies.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00615","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Biotechnology","topic":"Industrial Penicillin Fermentation","q":"During industrial submerged fed-batch fermentation of Penicillium chrysogenum for antibiotic manufacturing, why is phenylacetic acid (or potassium phenylacetate) fed continuously at controlled non-toxic levels during the idiophase (production phase)?","o":["It serves as the specific side-chain precursor incorporated by acyl-CoA:isopenicillin N acyltransferase to maximize selective biosynthesis of Benzylpenicillin (Penicillin G)","It inhibits fungal sporulation completely during the lag phase","It acts as the sole nitrogen source replacing corn steep liquor","It converts Penicillin G directly into semi-synthetic ampicillin inside the bioreactor"],"a":0,"e":"Penicillin is a secondary metabolite synthesized predominantly during the idiophase (stationary/slow-growth phase under controlled glucose/lactose limitation). Without exogenous side-chain precursor, P. chrysogenum produces a mixture of natural penicillins (e.g., Penicillin F, K, X). Feeding phenylacetic acid continuously (kept low to avoid fungal toxicity and oxidation) supplies phenylacetyl-CoA, directing acyltransferase enzyme specificity toward Benzylpenicillin (Penicillin G) at high yield (whereas phenoxyacetic acid yields Penicillin V).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00616","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy","topic":"Fluidized Bed Processor Configurations","q":"A formulator needs to apply a uniform, defect-free sustained-release ethylcellulose film coat onto 500-micron drug-layered sugar pellets (multiparticulates) for capsule filling. Which fluidized bed processor configuration provides the most uniform, rapid-drying, non-agglomerating film coat?","o":["High-shear planetary wet granulator with chopper blades","Bottom-spray Wurster column (inner partition cylinder creating organized cyclic particle flow past an upward concurrent spray nozzle)","Ball mill tumbling drum","Top-spray counter-current nozzle without partition"],"a":1,"e":"In a Wurster (bottom-spray) fluid-bed coater, a cylindrical partition sits inside the expansion chamber above an air distribution plate with higher airflow beneath the inner column. Pellets are drawn smoothly upward through the high-velocity zone in an organized curtain directly through an upward concurrent atomizing nozzle, then fall gently outside the partition as solvent/water evaporates—minimizing spray-drying loss and premature agglomeration while delivering highly uniform polymer coats on small pellets/granules.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00617","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy","topic":"Soft Gelatin Capsules (Softgels)","q":"During accelerated stability storage at 40 °C / 75% RH, a soft gelatin capsule containing an oily herbal extract and trace aldehyde impurities fails USP disintegration and dissolution testing due to formation of a thin, tough, water-insoluble transparent skin (pellicle) on the inside of the shell. What chemical reaction caused this defect, and how is it prevented or tested?","o":["Titanium dioxide opacifier migrated into the fill oil; prevented by omitting plasticizers","Excess glycerin evaporated completely into the fill oil; prevented by filling 50% pure water","Aldehyde impurities (or auto-oxidized PEG peroxides/formaldehyde) cross-link gelatin lysine amino groups into insoluble amide/imine networks; prevented by removing aldehyde sources/adding glycine-citric acid scavengers and evaluated using Tier-2 enzyme (pepsin/pancreatin) dissolution media","Gelatin hydrolyzed completely into amino acids; prevented by storing in open unsealed jars"],"a":2,"e":"Gelatin cross-linking (pellicle formation) is a classic softgel and hard-gelatin stability failure triggered by trace aldehydes (formaldehyde, furfural from cellulosic/starch excipients, or oxidative breakdown of PEG/polysorbates) and high heat/humidity, which covalently bridge lysine epsilon-amino groups on adjacent gelatin strands. This rubbery insoluble membrane blocks in vitro dissolution in pure water/buffer—prompting USP \u003c711\u003e Tier-2 dissolution testing with pepsin (gastric pH) or pancreatin (intestinal pH), alongside formulation fixes using aldehyde-free excipients or amine/acid scavengers like glycine + citric acid.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00618","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy","topic":"Glass Containers for Parenterals","q":"Which pharmacopoeial glass type consists of highly resistant borosilicate glass and is recommended for buffered, alkaline, and repeated-sterilization aqueous parenteral preparations because it exhibits minimal alkali leaching?","o":["Type NP glass (general-purpose soda-lime glass)","Type III glass (regular soda-lime glass)","Type II glass (surface-treated soda-lime glass)","Type I glass (neutral borosilicate glass)"],"a":3,"e":"Compendia (IP/USP) classify glass containers via the Powdered Glass and Water Attack tests into four grades: Type I (borosilicate neutral glass, lowest thermal expansion and highest hydrolytic resistance throughout its bulk—suitable for all injectables, including alkaline/buffered solutions); Type II (de-alkalized sulfur-treated soda-lime glass—surface resistance only, limited to acidic/neutral aqueous parenterals); Type III (regular soda-lime—restricted to non-aqueous parenterals or dry powders); and Type NP (non-parenteral oral/topical use).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00619","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Wagner-Nelson vs Loo-Riegelman Methods","q":"When determining the first-order oral absorption rate constant (ka) and percent drug absorbed versus time from plasma concentration-time data, when must the Loo-Riegelman method be used instead of the Wagner-Nelson method?","o":["When the drug exhibits two-compartment (biexponential) disposition after IV administration, requiring micro-rate constants (k12, k21, k10) from an IV reference study","When the drug follows a simple one-compartment linear model without tissue distribution phase","When only cumulative urinary excretion data are available without plasma concentrations","When the drug undergoes zero-order IV infusion without oral absorption"],"a":0,"e":"The Wagner-Nelson method estimates cumulative fraction absorbed [(Cp + ke·AUC0-t) / (ke·AUC0-inf)] assuming a one-compartment open model and requires no prior IV data. However, if the drug distributes appreciably into a peripheral tissue compartment (two-compartment model with biexponential IV decline), Wagner-Nelson yields inaccurate absorption estimates; the Loo-Riegelman method incorporates peripheral compartment mass balance using k12, k21, and k10 obtained from an IV bolus study in the same subjects.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00620","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Flip-Flop Kinetics","q":"A drug exhibits an intrinsic elimination half-life of 2.0 hours (ke = 0.347 h^-1) after an IV bolus dose. When formulated as an oral matrix extended-release tablet, the terminal log-linear decline phase shows an apparent half-life of 12.0 hours (slope = -0.058 h^-1 / 2.303). What pharmacokinetic phenomenon explains this prolonged apparent terminal half-life?","o":["Permanent irreversible binding of the matrix tablet to plasma albumin","Flip-flop kinetics, in which the release/absorption rate constant (ka) is much slower than the elimination rate constant (ka \u003c\u003c ke), causing the terminal slope to reflect ka rather than ke","Saturation of hepatic glucuronidation exclusively during oral dosing","Sudden doubling of renal glomerular filtration rate after oral administration"],"a":1,"e":"In the Bateman equation for oral one-compartment kinetics, plasma concentration declines according to the smaller (slower) of the two rate constants (ka and ke). Normally ka \u003e\u003e ke, so the terminal log-linear slope measures ke. In sustained-release oral tablets or IM depot injections engineered for slow release, ka becomes rate-limiting (ka \u003c ke)—termed \u0027flip-flop kinetics\u0027—so the terminal phase actually measures the absorption/release rate constant ka, while the initial ascending phase reflects ke (confirmed only by comparing against an IV reference dose).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00621","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Michaelis-Menten Kinetics","q":"A patient receiving phenytoin (which follows nonlinear Michaelis-Menten elimination kinetics) has an estimated maximum elimination rate (Vmax) of 500 mg/day and a Michaelis constant (Km) of 5.0 mg/L. Assuming complete bioavailability (F = 1), what daily maintenance dosing rate (R, in mg/day) is required to achieve a target steady-state plasma concentration (Css) of 15.0 mg/L?","o":["1,500 mg/day","500 mg/day","375 mg/day","250 mg/day"],"a":2,"e":"At steady state under Michaelis-Menten saturation kinetics, the dosing rate equals the elimination rate: R = (Vmax × Css) / [F × (Km + Css)]. Substituting Vmax = 500 mg/day, Km = 5.0 mg/L, Css = 15.0 mg/L, and F = 1: R = (500 × 15.0) / (5.0 + 15.0) = 7,500 / 20.0 = 375 mg/day. Notice that increasing Css further toward Vmax requires only tiny dose increments because enzymes approach saturation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00622","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics I","topic":"Cosolvency (Dielectric Constant) vs Hydrotropy","q":"When solubilising poorly water-soluble drugs in aqueous oral or parenteral vehicles without surfactants, how does \u0027Cosolvency\u0027 (using ethanol, propylene glycol, or PEG 400) differ mechanistically from \u0027Hydrotropy\u0027 (using high concentrations of sodium benzoate or nicotinamide)?","o":["Cosolvents lower the bulk dielectric constant (epsilon) and disrupt water\u0027s hydrogen-bonded structure to favour non-polar drug solvation, whereas hydrotropes form non-covalent planar pi-stacking/molecular complexes with the drug at high hydrotrope concentration without forming true surfactant micelles","Cosolvents raise the dielectric constant of water above 150; hydrotropes covalently esterify the drug","Both cosolvency and hydrotropy require heating the drug to 250 C in mineral oil","Hydrotropy relies strictly on polymeric cross-linking into solid beads"],"a":0,"e":"Pure water has a high dielectric constant (~78.5 at 25 C) that squeezes out nonpolar drugs. Water-miscible cosolvents (ethanol ~24, propylene glycol ~32, PEG 400 ~12) reduce the bulk polarity/dielectric constant toward the solute\u0027s optimal requirement (log-linear solubilisation). Hydrotropes (concentrated water-soluble organic salts/amides like sodium benzoate, sodium salicylate, urea, nicotinamide) lack long alkyl surfactant tails (no true CMC micelles) and enhance solubility via self-association and planar donor-acceptor/pi-stacking complexes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00623","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Physical Pharmaceutics II","topic":"Arrhenius Equation Graphical Extrapolation","q":"During accelerated stability prediction of an oral vitamin syrup tested at 40 C, 50 C, and 60 C, an analyst plots log10(k) against 1/T (in Kelvin) using the Arrhenius equation. What physical constant is derived from the negative slope of this linear plot, and under what critical condition does Arrhenius extrapolation to 25 C FAIL?","o":["Slope = -K_m / V_max; Arrhenius equation applies strictly to zero-order reactions only","Slope = +t_90 x C_0; extrapolation fails only if the syrup is packaged in amber glass","Slope equals 0.693 at all temperatures regardless of activation energy","Slope = -E_a / (2.303 * R), yielding Activation Energy (E_a); extrapolation fails if elevated temperatures change the degradation mechanism/order, melt/evaporate vehicle components, or drive phase transitions (e.g., hydrate loss or Maillard onset not occurring at 25 C)"],"a":3,"e":"Taking base-10 logarithm of k = A * exp(-E_a / RT) yields log10(k) = log10(A) - [E_a / (2.303 * R)] * (1/T), so slope = -E_a / (2.303 * R) (where R = 8.314 J/mol*K). Extrapolating k back to 25 C (298 K) assumes the exact same single reaction pathway and E_a operate from 25 C to 60 C. Non-Arrhenius breakdown occurs if heating melts semisolid bases, denatures proteins, dehydrates crystal hydrates, breaks emulsions, or shifts relative humidity drastically.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00624","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy I","topic":"Troubleshooting Film-Coating Defects","q":"During aqueous HPMC film coating of engraved tablet cores in a perforated coating pan, match each visual film defect to its primary processing root cause: 1) Orange peel (roughness), 2) Bridging of monogram/intagliation, 3) Picking / Sticking.","o":["1-Overly rapid drying or high solution viscosity preventing sprayed droplets from spreading/coalescing smoothly on tablet surface; 2-High internal film cohesive stress + insufficient plasticiser pulling the film tight across engraved logo valleys; 3-Excessive spray rate / over-wetting causing adjacent tablets to adhere and pull patches of film off","1-Over-polishing with carnauba wax; 2-Subcoating talc excess; 3-Sealing shellac cracking","1-Mottling from dye migration in core; 2-Air entrapment in die; 3-Worn upper punch tip","1-Excessive plasticiser; 2-Insufficient pan rotation only; 3-Too little coating solution applied"],"a":0,"e":"Film-coating defect root causes: (1) Orange peel results when atomised droplets dry prematurely before levelling/coalescing (high inlet air temp, gun too far from bed, or high polymer viscosity); (2) Bridging occurs when shrinking film tensile stress exceeds film-core adhesion across debossed logos (remedied by adding plasticiser like PEG/triacetin, lowering polymer molecular weight, or optimising logo bevel angle); (3) Picking/sticking happens when over-wetting leaves tacky surfaces that stick to pan baffles or each other.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00625","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy II","topic":"Six Sigma Methodology \u0026 DMAIC Lifecycle","q":"In pharmaceutical Total Quality Management (TQM) and Six Sigma process excellence, what defect rate benchmark defines true \u0027Six Sigma\u0027 quality performance (accounting for the empirical 1.5-sigma long-term process mean shift), and what five sequential phases constitute the DMAIC improvement framework?","o":["\u003c= 60 defects per hundred units; Dilute, Mix, Autoclave, Incubate, Count","\u003c= 3.4 defects per million opportunities (DPMO); Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control","\u003c= 50% defects; Drug, Market, Advertise, Import, Close","\u003c= 3,400 defects per million opportunities; Design, Manufacture, Audit, Inspect, Certify"],"a":1,"e":"Six Sigma is a rigorous data-driven quality methodology aiming to minimise process variability so specification limits sit +/- 6 standard deviations from the target mean—corresponding (with a 1.5-sigma long-term drift allowance) to \u003c= 3.4 defects per million opportunities (99.99966% defect-free). Existing processes are improved systematically via DMAIC: Define (project charter/CTQs), Measure (baseline capability), Analyze (root causes), Improve (DoE/optimisation), and Control (SPC/standardisation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00626","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry I","topic":"SAR of Anticonvulsant Cyclic Ureides","q":"Many classical anticonvulsants share a cyclic ureide-like backbone (-CO-NH-CO-). How does the steric size of substituents at the tetrahedral carbon (C-5 in hydantoins/barbiturates vs C-3 in succinimides) govern clinical seizure-type selectivity between tonic-clonic and absence seizures?","o":["At least one bulky aromatic phenyl substituent (e.g., 5,5-diphenyl in phenytoin or 5-ethyl-5-phenyl in phenobarbital) confers voltage-gated Na+ channel blockade against generalised tonic-clonic/focal seizures, whereas small aliphatic alkyl chains (2-ethyl-2-methyl in ethosuximide) confer thalamic T-type Ca2+ channel blockade against absence seizures","Bulky 5,5-diphenyl rings selectively treat absence seizures ONLY; small methyl groups block Na+ channels exclusively","Adding a quaternary ammonium salt at N-3 maximises CNS anticonvulsant potency","Removing both carbonyl oxygens increases anticonvulsant selectivity tenfold"],"a":0,"e":"In anticonvulsant cyclic ureides (hydantoins, barbiturates, succinimides, oxazolidinediones), lipophilic aromatic substitution (e.g., two phenyl rings in phenytoin, phenyl+ethyl in phenobarbital) matches the hydrophobic pore binding site of inactivated voltage-gated Na+ channels—preventing high-frequency firing in focal and tonic-clonic seizures (phenytoin actually worsens absence seizures!). Conversely, replacing aryl rings with small alkyl groups (ethyl/methyl in ethosuximide, dimethyl in trimethadione) shifts activity to thalamic T-type Ca2+ channels controlling 3-Hz spike-and-wave absence seizures.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00627","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry II","topic":"SAR Evolution from Captopril to Enalaprilat \u0026 Fosinopril","q":"Captopril was the first orally active ACE inhibitor, yet its sulfhydryl (-SH) group caused penicillamine-like adverse effects (dysgeusia/metallic taste, maculopapular rash, neutropenia, and proteinuria). Which bioisosteric replacement of the zinc-binding ligand eliminated thiol toxicities in second-generation ACE inhibitors (enalaprilat, lisinopril, ramipril)?","o":["Adding two free thiol groups to double catalytic Zn2+ chelation","Replacing the terminal carboxylic acid with a cationic quaternary ammonium group","Replacing the sulfhydryl (-SH) group with a weaker-binding carboxylic acid (-COOH) or phosphinic acid (-PO2H-) zinc-binding group while compensating affinity via an extra phenethyl hydrophobic substituent occupying the S1\u0027 subsite","Replacing proline completely with an aromatic nitrobenzene ring"],"a":2,"e":"Captopril coordinates the catalytic Zn2+ ion of angiotensin-converting enzyme via a potent sulfhydryl (-SH) ligand—responsible for metallic taste, rash, and immune-complex proteinuria. Substituting -SH with a carboxylic acid (enalaprilat, lisinopril, ramiprilat, perindoprilat) or phosphinic acid (fosinoprilat) eliminated thiol toxicity; because carboxylate binds Zn2+ less tightly than -SH, chemists appended a phenethyl/aminoacyl side chain to engage the hydrophobic S1 subsite, restoring nanomolar affinity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00628","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry III","topic":"SAR of Azole Antifungals (Imidazoles vs Triazoles)","q":"In the medicinal chemistry of systemic azole antifungals, replacing ketoconazole\u0027s imidazole ring with two 1,2,4-triazole rings and a 2,4-difluorophenyl substituent yielded fluconazole. How does this structural change improve selectivity, water solubility, and CNS penetration?","o":["Fluconazole requires gastric acid pH 1 for absorption because it is completely water-insoluble","Triazole rings block fungal cell-wall chitin synthase directly without touching CYP51","Ketoconazole\u0027s imidazole gives 100% CSF penetration and zero human CYP inhibition","The less-basic 1,2,4-triazole N-4 lone pair coordinates fungal CYP51 (lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase) haem iron far more selectively than human CYP3A4/steroidogenic CYPs (eliminating ketoconazole\u0027s antiandrogenic adrenal suppression), while compact polar structure confers high aqueous solubility and \u003e70% CSF penetration"],"a":3,"e":"Ketoconazole\u0027s strongly basic imidazole N-3 binds mammalian hepatic CYP3A4 and gonadal/adrenal steroid CYPs indiscriminately, causing severe DDIs, hepatotoxicity, and gynaecomastia/adrenal insufficiency, while needing gastric acid to dissolve. Switching to the weaker-base 1,2,4-triazole (N-4 coordination to fungal CYP51 haem Fe3+) in fluconazole sharply raises fungal vs mammalian selectivity, while its small MW (~306 Da) and polar triazole/OH groups yield ~90% pH-independent oral bioavailability, low protein binding (~12%), and excellent CSF penetration for cryptococcal meningitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00629","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry III","topic":"Structure-Based (SBDD) vs Ligand-Based (LBDD) Drug Design","q":"A medicinal chemistry team discovers a novel membrane receptor without an available X-ray crystal, NMR, or cryo-EM 3D structure, yet has potency data for 45 structurally diverse active and inactive small-molecule ligands. Which CADD strategy is feasible here versus when a high-resolution target crystal structure IS available?","o":["Use Ligand-Based Drug Design (LBDD: pharmacophore mapping and 3D-QSAR CoMFA steric/electrostatic fields) when the 3D receptor structure is unknown; use Structure-Based Drug Design (SBDD: receptor-grid molecular docking \u0026 scoring) when a 3D target structure is available","Use SBDD molecular docking without any target coordinates by guessing random bond lengths","Use 3D-QSAR CoMFA strictly for inorganic heavy-metal limit tests","Both SBDD and LBDD require crystallising the patient\u0027s liver in ethanol"],"a":0,"e":"CADD splits into two complementary branches: (1) Structure-Based Drug Design (SBDD) exploits experimental 3D target coordinates (PDB X-ray/cryo-EM/NMR) for binding-site identification, molecular docking, and de novo ligand growing; (2) Ligand-Based Drug Design (LBDD) works solely from known active/inactive ligands when receptor 3D coordinates are absent—aligning bioactive conformations to derive 3D pharmacophores (H-bond donors/acceptors, hydrophobic/aromatic centres) and 3D-QSAR (CoMFA/CoMSIA) contour maps.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00630","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology I","topic":"5-HT Receptor Subtypes \u0026 Therapeutic Ligands","q":"Match each clinical serotonin receptor agonist/antagonist to its specific 5-HT receptor subtype and therapeutic indication: 1) Buspirone, 2) Sumatriptan, 3) Ondansetron, 4) Prucalopride.","o":["1-5-HT1A partial agonist (non-sedating anxiolytic for GAD); 2-5-HT1B/1D agonist (acute migraine abortive); 3-5-HT3 ligand-gated cation-channel antagonist (chemotherapy/post-op antiemetic); 4-Selective 5-HT4 agonist (prokinetic for chronic idiopathic constipation)","1-GABA-A agonist; 2-Dopamine D2 agonist; 3-Histamine H2 antagonist; 4-Opioid mu agonist","1-5-HT3 antagonist; 2-5-HT4 agonist; 3-5-HT1A partial agonist; 4-5-HT1B/1D agonist","1-5-HT2A irreversible agonist; 2-5-HT3 agonist; 3-5-HT1B antagonist; 4-5-HT7 inverse agonist"],"a":0,"e":"All 5-HT receptors are GPCRs EXCEPT 5-HT3 (which is a pentameric ligand-gated Na+/K+/Ca2+ ion channel). Clinically: Buspirone is a 5-HT1A partial agonist treating generalised anxiety without sedation/dependence (takes 1-2 weeks); Sumatriptan activates 5-HT1B/1D to abort migraine; Ondansetron blocks vagal/CTZ 5-HT3 ion channels to prevent CINV/PONV; Prucalopride activates myenteric 5-HT4 receptors enhancing colonic peristalsis in chronic constipation (avoiding cisapride\u0027s hERG QT toxicity).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00631","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Postpartum Haemorrhage (PPH) Drug Contraindications","q":"A 29-year-old woman with severe preeclampsia (BP 176/112 mmHg) and persistent bronchial asthma develops uterine atony and postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) immediately after vaginal delivery despite intravenous oxytocin infusion. Why are BOTH intramuscular methylergometrine AND intramuscular carboprost (15-methyl PGF2-alpha) contraindicated here, and what uterotonic alternative is safe?","o":["Administer high-dose salbutamol IV infusion to contract the uterus","Methylergometrine causes potent alpha-adrenergic/5-HT systemic vasoconstriction risking hypertensive stroke/eclampsia, while carboprost causes severe PGF2-alpha-mediated bronchospasm in asthma; administer misoprostol (or carbetocin/tranexamic acid + uterine massage)","Methylergometrine causes fatal bronchospasm exclusively while carboprost drops BP to 40/20 mmHg","Administer intravenous magnesium sulfate bolus 20 g to contract the uterus violently"],"a":1,"e":"Second-line uterotonics for oxytocin-refractory PPH carry distinct organ contraindications: ergot alkaloids (methylergometrine/ergometrine) stimulate vascular alpha-1/5-HT2 receptors producing marked systemic hypertension and coronary vasospasm—strictly contraindicated in hypertension, preeclampsia, and heart disease. Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2-alpha) potently constricts bronchial smooth muscle—contraindicated in active bronchial asthma. In a patient with BOTH preeclampsia and asthma, PGE1 analogue misoprostol (rectal/sublingual) + IV tranexamic acid (within 3 h per WOMAN trial) + mechanical tamponade/massage are safe.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00632","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology III","topic":"Pharmacokinetic Boosting with Low-Dose Ritonavir","q":"Low-dose ritonavir (100 mg) is co-formulated with HIV protease inhibitors (e.g., darunavir/ritonavir, lopinavir/ritonavir) as well as with the SARS-CoV-2 main protease inhibitor nirmatrelvir (Paxlovid). What is the pharmacological purpose of adding low-dose ritonavir?","o":["Chelate-binds viral RNA polymerase via magnesium ions","Acts purely as an osmotic tablet disintegrant in the stomach","Acts as a potent mechanism-based (irreversible) inhibitor of intestinal and hepatic CYP3A4 (and P-gp) to slow first-pass and systemic clearance of the companion antiviral, raising trough exposure and dosing convenience—with major DDI risk against co-prescribed CYP3A4 substrates","Directly induces hepatic CYP3A4 tenfold to clear viral particles faster"],"a":2,"e":"At low sub-antiretroviral booster doses (100 mg), ritonavir functions solely as a pharmacokinetic enhancer (\u0027pharmacoenhancer\u0027)—potently and irreversibly inhibiting gut-wall and hepatic CYP3A4 (and P-gp) metabolism of companion protease inhibitors (darunavir, atazanavir, lopinavir, or nirmatrelvir), markedly boosting bioavailability, half-life, and trough/IC90 ratios. Clinicians must screen ALL concomitant medications because ritonavir simultaneously spikes levels of CYP3A4 substrates (simvastatin, amiodarone, tacrolimus, midazolam).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00633","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry I","topic":"Gelatin (Type A vs Type B) \u0026 Chitosan","q":"In pharmaceutical and marine pharmacognosy, how do Type A and Type B Gelatin differ in source pretreatment and isoelectric point (pI), and from which marine biopolymer is Chitosan derived for haemostatic dressings and mucoadhesive nanoparticles?","o":["Type A gelatin uses acid-pretreated pig skin (pI ~7-9); Type B gelatin uses alkali/lime-pretreated calf bones/hide (pI ~4.7-5.2); Chitosan is obtained by alkaline N-deacetylation of chitin from crustacean shells (shrimp/crab)","Type A gelatin is extracted from brown seaweed; Type B is synthetic polyvinylpyrrolidone; Chitosan comes from pine resin","Both Type A and Type B gelatins have pI 1.0 and come from bacterial cell walls","Chitosan is prepared by acid hydrolysis of starch amylopectin"],"a":0,"e":"Collagen hydrolysis yields two pharmaceutical gelatins: Type A (acid-cured pork skin, minimal amide hydrolysis, basic pI ~7.0-9.0) and Type B (lime/alkali-cured ossein bone/hide, converting glutamine/asparagine to acidic Glu/Asp, acidic pI ~4.7-5.2 — critical when choosing polymer pairs for complex coacervation!). Chitosan is a cationic linear polysaccharide (beta-1,4-linked D-glucosamine \u0026 N-acetyl-D-glucosamine) obtained via partial alkaline deacetylation of marine crustacean shell chitin, prized for mucoadhesion, antimicrobial, and haemostatic properties.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00634","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy and Phytochemistry II","topic":"Shinoda Test \u0026 Rutin (Vitamin P) Bioflavonoid","q":"When screening an alcoholic extract of Ruta graveolens or Fagopyrum esculentum (Buckwheat) for flavonoids such as rutin and quercetin, adding magnesium ribbon turnings followed by dropwise concentrated HCl produces a vivid pink-to-magenta or crimson colour. Name this specific identification test and the classic vascular pharmacological role of rutin.","o":["Borntrager\u0027s test; rutin acts as a potent central anticholinergic","Keller-Kiliani test; rutin acts as a volatile terpene counter-irritant","Vitali-Morin test; rutin induces uterine smooth muscle spasm","Shinoda test (flavonoid reduction to anthocyanidin-like flavylium salts); rutin decreases abnormal capillary permeability and fragility (\u0027Vitamin P\u0027 factor)"],"a":3,"e":"The Shinoda (cyanidin) test reduces flavones, flavonols, and flavanones with nascent hydrogen (Mg + conc. HCl) into brightly coloured pink/magenta/red benzopyrylium (flavylium) cations—standard diagnostic screening for flavonoids (and ammonia vapour turns flavonoid spots yellow). Rutin (quercetin-3-O-rutinoside) and hesperidin reduce capillary fragility/permeability and oxidative microvascular leakage in chronic venous insufficiency and haemorrhoids.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00635","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Microbiology","topic":"Dry-Heat Depyrogenation vs Steam Autoclaving","q":"Why does standard moist-heat steam autoclaving (121 C for 15-30 minutes) sterilise empty parenteral glass vials of all viable bacterial spores yet FAIL to render them pyrogen-free, and what validated dry-heat depyrogenation cycle destroys bacterial endotoxins?","o":["Gram-negative bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS/endotoxin) is heat-stable and survives 121 C steam autoclaving intact; dry-heat depyrogenation tunnels operate at \u003e= 250 C for 30-60 minutes (achieving \u003e= 3-log endotoxin reduction) to thermally incinerate/destroy LPS","Autoclaving converts harmless Gram-positive spores into LPS endotoxins","Endotoxins evaporate cleanly at 100 C in steam autoclaves; dry heat at 250 C melts borosilicate glass","Depyrogenation is achieved by rinsing vials with tap water containing 1% phenol"],"a":0,"e":"Sterilisation (killing live organisms/spores) is distinct from depyrogenation (removing/destroying fever-inducing Gram-negative LPS Lipid A fragments). Because LPS is remarkably thermostable and unaffected by 121 C autoclaving, parenteral Type I glass vials are passed through continuous dry-heat depyrogenation tunnels at 250-300 C for \u003e=30 min (validated with spiked E. coli Control Standard Endotoxin [CSE] demonstrating \u003e=3-log10 reduction via LAL/BET assay), while WFI removes endotoxins by distillation/ultrafiltration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00636","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Engineering","topic":"SS 316L Austenitic Stainless Steel","q":"In pharmaceutical WFI loops, bioreactors, and tablet granulators, why is SS 316L stainless steel strictly specified over standard SS 304?","o":["SS 316L dissolves slightly in Water for Injection to add nutritional iron","SS 316L contains 2-3% Molybdenum (enhancing resistance to chloride pitting and crevice corrosion) AND extra-low carbon (\u003c 0.03%, \u0027L\u0027 grade) preventing chromium carbide precipitation during welding (avoiding intergranular weld decay)","SS 316L replaces all iron and chromium with pure copper and zinc","SS 316L contains 10% carbon making reactors completely magnetic and brittle"],"a":1,"e":"Both SS 304 and SS 316 are austenitic Cr-Ni steels (~18% Cr, 8-12% Ni), but pharmaceutical-contact equipment specifies SS 316L (ASME BPE): (1) addition of 2-3% Mo reinforces the passive Cr2O3 surface layer against halide/chloride pitting and acidic drug corrosion; (2) \u0027L\u0027 (Low carbon \u003c=0.03%) prevents chromium carbide formation at grain boundaries during orbital welding (\u0027sensitisation\u0027), preserving corrosion resistance at weld seams without rouge/leaching.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00637","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Instrumental Methods of Analysis","topic":"Stokes Shift \u0026 Fluorescence Quenching","q":"In spectrofluorimetry (used for trace assays of riboflavin, quinine, and thiamine/thiochrome), why is the emitted fluorescence wavelength ALWAYS longer than the excitation absorption wavelength (Stokes shift), and why does fluorescence intensity become non-linear or drop at excessively high analyte concentrations?","o":["Fluorescence emits higher-energy X-rays than excitation; high concentrations boost intensity exponentially","Stokes shift occurs only because the quartz cuvette absorbs all emitted photons","Excited electrons undergo rapid non-radiative vibrational relaxation to the lowest vibrational level of S1 before emitting a lower-energy (longer-wavelength) photon; at high concentrations, self-absorption (\u0027inner-filter effect\u0027) and collisional quenching cause negative deviation from linearity","Excitation occurs from triplet T1 directly to ground state; fluorescence is strictly independent of pH and oxygen"],"a":2,"e":"After absorbing an excitation photon (S0 -\u003e excited vibrational level of S1), the molecule rapidly dissipates excess vibrational energy as heat (internal conversion/vibrational relaxation in ~10^-12 s) to reach the S1 ground vibrational state. Radiative emission back to S0 therefore carries less energy (longer wavelength lambda_em \u003e lambda_ex — Stokes shift). Linearity holds only in dilute solutions (absorbance \u003c0.05); at higher concentrations, primary/secondary inner-filter self-absorption and collisional/O2 quenching depress fluorescence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00638","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Instrumental Methods of Analysis","topic":"Refractive Index (RI) vs Evaporative Light Scattering Detector (ELSD)","q":"When analysing excipients or APIs lacking UV-absorbing chromophores (such as sucrose, lactose, PEGs, or aminoglycosides) by HPLC, why can an Evaporative Light Scattering Detector (ELSD) operate smoothly with mobile-phase gradient elution, whereas a Refractive Index (RI) detector is restricted strictly to isocratic elution?","o":["RI detectors burn organic solvents in a hydrogen flame; ELSD requires pure metallic mercury columns","RI detectors achieve 1,000-fold higher sensitivity than fluorescence detectors during steep gradients","ELSD responds only to compounds absorbing UV light at 254 nm","ELSD nebulises and evaporates the volatile mobile phase leaving dry solute aerosol particles whose scattered light is measured independently of solvent refractive index, whereas RI measures bulk refractive index difference between eluent and reference cell—drifting wildly if mobile-phase composition changes during a gradient"],"a":3,"e":"Non-chromophoric carbohydrates, lipids, surfactants, and aminoglycosides require universal HPLC detectors. An RI detector continuously compares bulk refractive index of column effluent vs stagnant reference solvent—so any solvent-ratio shift during gradient elution (or minor temperature fluctuation) causes massive baseline drift, restricting RI to stable isocratic runs. ELSD nebulises effluent in warm N2 gas to vaporise volatile solvents/buffers completely, measuring laser light scattered by residual non-volatile solute particles—compatible with gradient elution and superior in sensitivity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00639","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Apparent Volume of Distribution (Vd) Calculation \u0026 Dialysability","q":"A 500 mg intravenous bolus dose of a basic drug yields an extrapolated initial plasma concentration C_0 = 2.0 mg/L (one-compartment model). Calculate the apparent Volume of Distribution (V_d), and state what this V_d implies about tissue distribution and haemodialysis efficacy in overdose.","o":["V_d = 250 L (far exceeding ~42 L total body water) — indicates extensive extravascular tissue/intracellular sequestration, rendering haemodialysis largely ineffective at removing total body drug","V_d = 4.0 L — indicates the drug stays strictly trapped inside plasma proteins, making haemodialysis maximally effective","V_d = 1,000 L — indicates pure blood-compartment retention","V_d = 0.004 L — proves immediate 100% glomerular excretion"],"a":0,"e":"Apparent Volume of Distribution V_d = IV Dose / C_0 = 500 mg / (2.0 mg/L) = 250 L. Because a 70-kg adult has only ~3 L plasma, ~15 L extracellular fluid, and ~42 L total body water, V_d = 250 L proves the drug partitions heavily out of plasma into peripheral tissues/fat/lysosomes (similar to chloroquine, digoxin, or TCAs). Consequently, \u003c2% of body drug circulates in plasma at any time, making extracorporeal haemodialysis ineffective in overdose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00640","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Quality Assurance","topic":"ICH Q1E Extrapolation of Real-Time Shelf-Life","q":"Under the ICH Q1E Guideline (Evaluation of Stability Data), a solid oral tablet batch demonstrates 12 months of real-time long-term data (30 C / 75% RH) and 6 months of accelerated data (40 C / 75% RH) with NO significant change and virtually flat regression slopes (little or no variability). What is the MAXIMUM retest period or shelf-life that can be granted by extrapolation beyond the 12-month long-term dataset?","o":["36 months immediately from 6 months of accelerated data alone","24 months (per ICH Q1E rule for little/no change and no variability: up to min(2x, x + 12) = 24 months when x = 12 months real-time data)","60 months (5 years automatically from 12 months of real-time data)","12 months only — extrapolation beyond real-time data is strictly forbidden under ICH Q1E"],"a":1,"e":"ICH Q1E provides a clear decision tree for proposing shelf-life/retest period (y) from x months of long-term real-time data when 6-month accelerated data show no significant change: if long-term data show little or no change over time and little/no variability, the proposed shelf-life can extend up to 2x (but NOT exceeding x + 12 months)—so x = 12 months supports up to 24 months (and x = 24 months supports up to 36 months), subject to ongoing post-approval stability commitment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00641","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Practice","topic":"Fluoxetine-to-MAOI Washout Requirement","q":"A 36-year-old patient with refractory major depression being switched from oral fluoxetine 40 mg/day to an irreversible monoamine oxidase inhibitor (phenelzine/tranylcypromine) must observe a much longer drug-free washout period than when switching from sertraline or paroxetine. What minimum washout duration is mandatory after stopping fluoxetine, and why?","o":["3 days, identical to venlafaxine","24 hours, because fluoxetine is cleared completely in 2 hours","At least 5 weeks (35 days), because fluoxetine\u0027s active metabolite norfluoxetine has an exceptionally long elimination half-life of 7-15 days—shorter washout risks fatal serotonin syndrome","Zero washout — fluoxetine and phenelzine should be overlapped for 14 days"],"a":2,"e":"Most SSRIs (sertraline, paroxetine, citalopram, escitalopram) have half-lives ~1 day, requiring a standard 14-day (2-week) washout before starting an irreversible MAOI. Uniquely, fluoxetine (t1/2 ~4-6 days at steady state via CYP2D6 autoinhibition) is metabolised to active norfluoxetine with a t1/2 of 7-15 days—requiring ~5 weeks (4-5 half-lives) to clear completely before MAOI initiation to avert catastrophic serotonin toxicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00642","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Degree of Ionization and Absorption","q":"A weakly acidic oral NSAID has a pKa of 4.4. According to the pH-partition hypothesis and Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, approximately what percentage of the drug exists in its unionized, lipid-soluble absorbable form (HA) in gastric fluid at pH 1.4?","o":["90.9% unionized (9.1% ionized)","0.1% unionized (99.9% ionized)","50.0% unionized (50.0% ionized)","99.9% unionized (0.1% ionized)"],"a":3,"e":"For a weak acid (HA \u003c-\u003e H+ + A-), log10([A-] / [HA]) = pH - pKa = 1.4 - 4.4 = -3.0. Therefore, the ionized-to-unionized ratio [A-] / [HA] = 10^-3 = 0.001. The percentage unionized = [1 / (1 + 0.001)] × 100 = (1 / 1.001) × 100 = 99.90% (and 0.10% ionized), favoring passive diffusion across gastric/upper mucosal lipid membranes (though small-intestinal surface area still drives overall absorption).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00643","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Polymorphism and Amorphous Solids","q":"Chloramphenicol palmitate exists in three polymorphic forms (A, B, and C), among which metastable Form B exhibits substantially higher oral bioavailability than thermodynamically stable Form A. Why do metastable polymorphs and amorphous solids generally show higher apparent solubility and dissolution rate than the most stable crystalline polymorph?","o":["Metastable polymorphs and amorphous forms possess higher Gibbs free energy, weaker crystal lattice forces, and lower melting points, facilitating faster solvent-mediated disruption","Stable polymorphs have higher free energy and melt at lower temperatures than amorphous solids","Metastable polymorphs form permanent covalent hydrates with water in the crystal","Amorphous solids possess rigid long-range 3D crystal order that repels water"],"a":0,"e":"At a given temperature below the transition point, the thermodynamically stable polymorph has the lowest Gibbs free energy, strongest intermolecular lattice packing, highest melting point, and lowest solubility. Conversely, metastable polymorphs (like chloramphenicol palmitate Form B) and non-crystalline amorphous solids sit at higher free-energy states with lower lattice energy barriers to overcome during solvation—producing higher apparent solubility (\u0027spring\u0027 effect) and faster dissolution, though they require stabilizers (e.g., PVP/HPMC) to prevent spontaneous conversion back to the stable low-solubility polymorph (as famously occurred in the ritonavir Form II recall).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00644","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Inclusion Complexes","q":"How do beta-cyclodextrin and hydroxypropyl-beta-cyclodextrin (HP-beta-CD) enhance the aqueous solubility and chemical stability of poorly water-soluble drugs such as piroxicam or voriconazole?","o":["They lower the pH of water to 0.5 to protonate neutral drugs","They are cyclic oligosaccharides with a hydrophobic internal cavity that non-covalently encapsulates lipophilic drug moieties and a hydrophilic hydroxyl-rich outer surface facing water","They covalently esterify drug carboxylic acids into permanent prodrugs","They act as irreversible chelating agents analogous to EDTA for alkali metals"],"a":1,"e":"Cyclodextrins are truncated cone-shaped cyclic oligosaccharides composed of alpha-(1,4)-linked D-glucopyranose units (alpha = 6, beta = 7, gamma = 8 glucose units). Secondary and primary hydroxyl groups orient on the exterior (making the outer torus water-soluble), while skeletal carbons and glycosidic oxygens line a relatively apolar central cavity capable of reversible host-guest inclusion complexation with hydrophobic drug molecules—shielding labile bonds from hydrolysis and elevating apparent aqueous solubility.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00645","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"SAR of Sulfonamide-Derived Diuretics","q":"In the structure-activity relationship of benzothiadiazine (thiazide) diuretics such as hydrochlorothiazide, which two substituent features on the aromatic ring are essential for natriuretic activity at the distal convoluted tubule?","o":["An electron-withdrawing group (-Cl or -CF3) at C-6 and an unsubstituted sulfamoyl (-SO2NH2) group at C-7","A bulky N-benzyl group at C-7 replacing the sulfamoyl group and an amino group at C-6","A 5-membered lactone ring at C-3 without any sulfonamide moiety","An unsubstituted phenyl ring lacking both halogen and sulfamoyl groups"],"a":0,"e":"Classic benzothiadiazine diuretics (chlorothiazide, hydrochlorothiazide, bendroflumethiazide) strictly require an unsubstituted sulfamoyl (-SO2NH2) group at position 7 (free NH2 protons essential for binding) and an activating electron-withdrawing halogen or trifluoromethyl group (-Cl or -CF3) at position 6 (adjacent ortho to C-7). Saturation of the C-3,C-4 double bond (chlorothiazide -\u003e hydrochlorothiazide) increases potency ~10-fold, and lipophilic substitution at C-3 prolongs duration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00646","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"SAR of Tricyclic Antidepressants (TCAs)","q":"Imipramine and amitriptyline are tertiary amine tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) that undergo hepatic N-demethylation to secondary amine active metabolites (desipramine and nortriptyline, respectively). How does this conversion from tertiary to secondary amine alter transporter selectivity and clinical tolerability?","o":["Tertiary amines are inactive prodrugs lacking any affinity for SERT or NET","Secondary amines completely lose all monoamine reuptake inhibition and act purely as GABA agonists","Secondary amines exhibit greater selectivity for norepinephrine transporter (NET) inhibition over serotonin (SERT), with markedly reduced antimuscarinic, H1-sedative, and alpha-1 orthostatic adverse effects","Secondary amines cause tenfold greater anticholinergic dry mouth and urinary retention than tertiary amines"],"a":2,"e":"In tricyclic antidepressants (containing a non-planar butterfly-angled 6-7-6 tricyclic ring connected via a 3-carbon propyl bridge to a terminal amine), tertiary dimethylamines (amitriptyline, imipramine, clomipramine) strongly inhibit both SERT and NET while exhibiting high affinity for M1 muscarinic, H1 histamine, and alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. N-demethylation to secondary monomethylamines (nortriptyline, desipramine) shifts potency selectively toward NET inhibition and substantially reduces antimuscarinic, sedative, and hypotensive side effects—making nortriptyline better tolerated in older adults.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00647","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"SAR of Phenothiazine Antipsychotics","q":"Assertion (A): Shortening the three-carbon propylene linker between the N-10 phenothiazine nitrogen and the basic side-chain amine to a two-carbon ethylene linker (as in promethazine) abolishes antipsychotic D2-blocking efficacy while preserving strong H1-antihistaminic activity. Reason (R): Dopamine D2 receptor pharmacophore geometry requires a 3-carbon distance and a C-2 electron-withdrawing group (-Cl or -CF3) to orient the protonated amine and substituted aromatic ring cleanly inside the D2 binding pocket.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Classic phenothiazine SAR demonstrates strict topological separation between antihistamines and neuroleptics: promethazine has a branched 2-carbon ethylene spacer and no C-2 substituent, fitting the H1 receptor pocket cleanly with strong sedation/antiemesis but negligible antipsychotic activity. Extending the spacer to an unbranched 3-carbon propylene chain alongside a C-2 electron-withdrawing substituent (-Cl in chlorpromazine, -CF3 in trifluoperazine/fluphenazine) aligns the molecule with the dopamine D2 antagonist pharmacophore.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00648","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Medicinal Chemistry","topic":"SAR of H2 Antagonists and PPIs","q":"Why did replacing the imidazole ring and cyanoguanidine moiety of cimetidine with a furan/nitroethenediamine (ranitidine) or thiazole/sulfamoylamidine (famotidine) eliminate cimetidine\u0027s clinically troublesome CYP450 drug interactions and antiandrogenic gynecomastia?","o":["Furan and thiazole rings convert H2 blockers into irreversible covalent H+/K+-ATPase inhibitors","Cimetidine lacks any nitrogen atoms required for H2 receptor selectivity","Cimetidine covalently alkylates hepatic DNA while ranitidine is excreted unchanged by sweat glands","The imidazole ring of cimetidine coordinates directly to heme iron in hepatic CYP450 enzymes and acts as a weak androgen-receptor antagonist, whereas furan and thiazole heterocycles lack significant CYP heme binding and androgen affinity"],"a":3,"e":"Cimetidine\u0027s 4-methylimidazole heterocycle uses its sp2-hybridized lone-pair nitrogen to ligate the catalytic Fe3+ heme center of CYP1A2, CYP2C9, CYP2D6, and CYP3A4 (causing major toxicity with warfarin, phenytoin, and theophylline) and also exhibits weak antiandrogenic receptor binding (gynecomastia, galactorrhea). Medicinal chemistry bioisosteric replacement with a dimethylaminomethyl-substituted furan (ranitidine) or guanidinothiazole (famotidine) retained high H2-receptor affinity while abolishing CYP450 heme coordination and antiandrogenic effects.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00649","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Intravenous Anesthetic Induction Agents","q":"A 34-year-old trauma patient in hemorrhagic shock (BP 78/48 mmHg, HR 128/min) with acute bronchospasm requires rapid-sequence induction of anesthesia. Why is ketamine preferred over propofol in this unstable hypotensive asthmatic patient?","o":["Ketamine acts as an NMDA receptor antagonist that indirectly stimulates central sympathetic outflow (supporting BP/HR) and induces bronchodilation, whereas propofol causes marked arterial/venous vasodilation and hypotension","Propofol causes severe bronchospasm via muscarinic M3 agonism and lacks hypnotic effect","Ketamine is a pure GABAA agonist that drops blood pressure by 40% to reduce bleeding","Ketamine produces zero analgesia and causes severe respiratory depression even at sub-anesthetic doses"],"a":0,"e":"Ketamine non-competitively blocks excitatory glutamate NMDA receptors, producing \u0027dissociative anesthesia\u0027 with profound analgesia, preserved pharyngeal/laryngeal airway reflexes, bronchodilation, and central sympathetic stimulation (catecholamine release supporting blood pressure—ideal in hypovolemic shock or reactive airway disease, though avoided in elevated ICP/coronary ischemia without caution). By contrast, propofol potentiates GABAA receptors and markedly decreases systemic vascular resistance and myocardial contractility, risking severe hypotension in shock.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00650","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Calcineurin Inhibitors","q":"Both cyclosporine and tacrolimus prevent solid-organ transplant rejection by inhibiting calcineurin phosphatase in T-lymphocytes, yet they bind distinct intracellular immunophilins and differ slightly in non-renal adverse effects. Which pairing is accurate?","o":["Cyclosporine binds cyclophilin and tacrolimus binds FKBP-12, both blocking NFAT dephosphorylation and IL-2 transcription; gingival hyperplasia/hirsutism are characteristic of cyclosporine whereas post-transplant diabetes (NODAT) is more frequent with tacrolimus","Both bind mTOR directly to arrest T-cells in S-phase; both cause severe bone marrow aplasia as primary toxicity","Cyclosporine binds FKBP-12 and tacrolimus binds cyclophilin; both lack any nephrotoxicity","Both act as IL-2 receptor monoclonal antibodies outside the cell membrane without entering T-cells"],"a":0,"e":"Cyclosporine (binds cyclophilin) and tacrolimus (binds FK506-binding protein-12, FKBP-12) form immunophilin complexes that inhibit Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent calcineurin phosphatase, preventing dephosphorylation and nuclear translocation of NFAT—halting IL-2 transcription and T-cell proliferation. Both cause afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction/nephrotoxicity, hypertension, and neurotoxicity; however, cyclosporine uniquely causes gingival hyperplasia, hirsutism, and hyperlipidemia, whereas tacrolimus carries higher risk of new-onset diabetes after transplantation (NODAT) and alopecia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00651","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Drugs Affecting Bone Mineral Homeostasis","q":"A 64-year-old postmenopausal woman with osteoporosis (T-score -2.8) is prescribed oral alendronate 70 mg once weekly. By what cellular mechanism does alendronate reduce vertebral and hip fracture risk, and what administration instruction prevents severe pill esophagitis?","o":["Inhibits RANK ligand extracellularly like a monoclonal antibody; inject subcutaneously once every 6 months","Inhibits osteoclast farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase upon bone resorption, inducing osteoclast apoptosis; take first thing in the morning with a full glass of plain water and remain upright for \u003e= 30 minutes before food or other drugs","Blocks intestinal calcium absorption via vitamin D antagonism; crush tablet in orange juice","Stimulates osteoblast PTH receptors continuously; take at bedtime with milk and lie flat immediately"],"a":1,"e":"Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate, zoledronate) bind avidly to bone hydroxyapatite crystals and are internalized by actively resorbing osteoclasts, where they inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase (mevalonate pathway), disrupting prenylation of small GTPases and triggering osteoclast apoptosis. Because oral bioavailability is \u003c1% and bisphosphonates are directly caustic to esophageal mucosa, patients must take the intact tablet on an empty stomach with 180–240 mL plain water and sit/stand upright for at least 30 minutes before lying down or consuming food, coffee, or calcium.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00652","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Oxytocics vs Tocolytics","q":"A 27-year-old primigravida at 40 weeks of gestation requires pharmacological augmentation of weak uterine contractions during the first stage of labor. Why is intravenous oxytocin preferred over intramuscular ergometrine (methylergometrine) prior to delivery of the baby and placenta?","o":["Ergometrine relaxes the myometrium completely via beta-2 agonism","Ergometrine has a 10-second half-life requiring continuous IV infusion only","Low-dose oxytocin induces physiological rhythmic contractions with intervening relaxation periods (preserving placental blood flow), whereas ergometrine causes sustained tonic tetanic uterine spasm risking fetal asphyxia and uterine rupture","Oxytocin irreversibly closes the cervix whereas ergometrine dilates it"],"a":2,"e":"Oxytocin acts on Gq-coupled oxytocin receptors to trigger rhythmic, phasic fundal contractions separated by complete uterine relaxation intervals—allowing uteroplacental blood oxygenation between contractions during labor induction/augmentation. In contrast, ergometrine/methylergometrine produces prolonged, intense tonic (tetanic) contraction of both upper and lower uterine segments without relaxation, which would fatally compromise fetal oxygenation or rupture the uterus before delivery; therefore, ergometrine is restricted strictly to postpartum hemorrhage (PPH) prophylaxis/treatment AFTER placental delivery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00653","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy \u0026 Phytochemistry","topic":"Morphinan Alkaloid Pathway","q":"In Papaver somniferum (opium poppy), two molecules derived from which aromatic amino acid condense to form (S)-norcoclaurine and (S)-reticuline—the central branch-point intermediate leading via salutaridine and thebaine to codeine and morphine?","o":["L-Histidine (via histamine)","L-Ornithine (via putrescine)","L-Tryptophan (via tryptamine and secologanin)","L-Tyrosine (via dopamine and 4-hydroxyphenylacetaldehyde)"],"a":3,"e":"Morphinan benzylisoquinoline alkaloids (morphine, codeine, thebaine, papaverine) are biosynthesized from two molecules of L-tyrosine: one is hydroxylated/decarboxylated to dopamine, and the other undergoes transamination/decarboxylation to 4-hydroxyphenylacetaldehyde. Condensation via norcoclaurine synthase yields (S)-norcoclaurine, methylated to (S)-reticuline, epimerized to (R)-reticuline, cyclized via phenol oxidative coupling to salutaridine, and converted through thebaine and codeine into morphine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00654","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy \u0026 Phytochemistry","topic":"Steroidal Sapogenin Industrial Precursors","q":"Diosgenin, a steroidal sapogenin obtained from Dioscorea deltoidea / D. villosa tubers (and solasodine from Solanum khasianum), plays a pivotal industrial role in pharmaceutical chemistry. For which semi-synthetic hormone class does diosgenin serve as a key starting precursor via Marker degradation?","o":["Progesterone, corticosteroids (cortisone/prednisolone), and steroidal oral contraceptives","Insulin and recombinant human growth hormone","Beta-lactam penicillins and cephalosporins","Purine xanthine alkaloids such as caffeine and theophylline"],"a":0,"e":"Diosgenin (hydrolyzed from the steroidal saponin dioscin in Dioscorea yam tubers) contains an intact cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene steroid nucleus with a spiroketal side chain. Russell Marker\u0027s three-step degradation cleaves the spiroketal side chain of diosgenin into 16-dehydropregnenolone acetate (16-DPA)—a versatile industrial intermediate converted into progesterone, sex hormones, oral contraceptives, and corticosteroids.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00655","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy \u0026 Phytochemistry","topic":"Leaf Constants","q":"During quantitative microscopic authentication of a dried Cassia angustifolia (Senna) leaflet, a 1 mm² epidermal field reveals 20 stomata (S = 20) and 80 surrounding epidermal cells (E = 80). What is the Stomatal Index (SI) of this specimen?","o":["16.7%","20.0%","25.0%","80.0%"],"a":1,"e":"Stomatal Index (SI) is a species-specific quantitative leaf constant independent of leaf age or ecological size variation, defined by Salisbury\u0027s formula: SI = [S / (E + S)] × 100, where S is the number of stomata and E is the number of ordinary epidermal cells in the same unit area. Substituting S = 20 and E = 80: SI = [20 / (80 + 20)] × 100 = (20 / 100) × 100 = 20.0%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00656","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC) vs TGA","q":"During preformulation screening of a 1:1 physical mixture of an API and magnesium stearate, Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC) reveals disappearance of the pure API sharp melting endotherm at 168 °C accompanied by a broad new endothermic peak at 142 °C, while Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) shows zero mass loss between 25 °C and 175 °C. How should these combined thermal results be interpreted?","o":["Complete chemical combustion of the API into CO2 gas at 142 °C","Presence of 15% residual bound crystal water evaporating at 142 °C","Potential solid-state interaction, eutectic formation, or polymorphic conversion/incompatibility between API and excipient, rather than dehydration of a stoichiometric hydrate","Instrument failure because melting transitions require mass loss in TGA"],"a":2,"e":"DSC measures heat flow associated with physical or chemical transitions (melting, crystallization, glass transition, polymorphic shift, drug-excipient interaction), whereas TGA continuously tracks sample mass as temperature rises. True melting, polymorphic transitions, and drug-excipient eutectic/interaction shifts produce DSC thermal events with ZERO mass change in TGA—unlike hydrate/solvate desolvation or thermal decomposition, which cause concomitant step-wise weight loss in TGA.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00657","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Atomic Absorption Spectroscopy (AAS) vs Flame Photometry","q":"Why does Atomic Absorption Spectroscopy (AAS) require a element-specific Hollow Cathode Lamp (HCL) to quantify trace heavy metals (e.g., Pb, Cd, Zn), unlike UV-Visible molecular spectrophotometry which uses a continuous tungsten/deuterium lamp?","o":["AAS measures thermal emission from excited plasma without absorbing light","Hollow cathode lamps generate X-rays to ionize inner-shell K-electrons","Continuous deuterium lamps emit only infrared radiation unable to excite atoms","Free ground-state metal atoms in the flame/graphite furnace exhibit extremely narrow atomic absorption lines (~0.002–0.005 nm), requiring a sharp line-emission source matching the analyte\u0027s resonance wavelength"],"a":3,"e":"Atomic vapor absorption bands are orders of magnitude narrower (~0.002–0.005 nm) than the spectral bandpass (~0.2–2 nm) of standard monochromators used with continuous lamps; a continuous source would pass massive unabsorbed stray radiation across the slit, destroying Beer-Lambert sensitivity and linearity. Walsh\u0027s Hollow Cathode Lamp (cathode coated with the analyte element) emits razor-sharp characteristic atomic lines matching the exact absorption transition of ground-state analyte atoms in the atomizer.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00658","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Out-of-Specification (OOS) Investigation","q":"An HPLC assay of a commercial tablet batch returns 87.2% label claim against a specification of 90.0–110.0%. Before notifying QA or initiating a formal investigation, the QC analyst contemplates discarding the volumetric flasks and re-injecting fresh preparations until a passing result \u003e= 90% is obtained. Under USFDA / GMP OOS guidance, what is the ONLY compliant initial action?","o":["Retain all original test preparations, glassware, and chromatographic data intact; immediately notify the laboratory supervisor and initiate a documented Phase I Laboratory Investigation for assignable lab error","Release the batch if the visual tablet appearance looks acceptable","Discard the failing flasks silently and average three fresh passing injections","Delete the failing chromatographic peak integration from the audit trail and re-integrate manually"],"a":0,"e":"Under USFDA Guidance on Investigating Out-of-Specification (OOS) Test Results, repeatedly re-preparing or retesting samples until a passing value appears (\u0027testing into compliance\u0027) and deleting raw audit-trail data are severe cGMP data-integrity violations. Instead, the analyst must preserve all original solutions and glassware, promptly report the OOS to the supervisor, and conduct a documented Phase I Laboratory Assessment (checking calculations, instrument suitability, dilution math, standard prep). Only if a clear, documented assignable laboratory error is proven may the original OOS be invalidated; otherwise, the investigation escalates to Phase II (full manufacturing batch record review and CAPA).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00659","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Biotechnology","topic":"Oxygen Mass Transfer in Aerobic Fermentation","q":"In an aerobic stirred-tank bioreactor producing recombinant protein, oxygen transfer from gas bubbles to the liquid broth (OTR = kLa · [C* - CL]) limits cell density. Which operational changes increase the volumetric oxygen mass transfer coefficient (kLa)?","o":["Raising fermentation temperature to 85 °C to reduce oxygen solubility","Increasing Rushton turbine impeller agitation speed (breaking bubbles into smaller sizes with higher interfacial area \u0027a\u0027) and increasing sparger aeration rate","Turning off agitation completely and adding excess silicone antifoam blindly","Replacing the sparger with a closed unvented headspace at zero gas flow"],"a":1,"e":"Because oxygen is sparingly soluble in aqueous media (~7–8 mg/L at 37 °C), oxygen transfer rate (OTR = kLa · [C* - CL]) depends heavily on kLa—the product of the liquid-film mass transfer coefficient (kL) and gas-liquid interfacial area per unit volume (a). Increasing mechanical agitation (Rushton flat-blade turbine impellers) shears rising air bubbles into smaller diameters (dramatically expanding interfacial area \u0027a\u0027 and increasing bubble holdup time), while increasing air/O2-enriched sparging rate supplies fresh oxygen (note: excessive silicone antifoam promotes bubble coalescence, lowering kLa).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00660","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Biotechnology","topic":"Polysaccharide-Protein Conjugate Vaccines","q":"Pure bacterial capsular polysaccharide vaccines fail to elicit protective immunity or memory B-cells in infants under 2 years of age. How do conjugate vaccines (such as Haemophilus influenzae type b [Hib] or Pneumococcal Conjugate Vaccine [PCV]) overcome this limitation?","o":["Replacing polysaccharides entirely with unconjugated aluminum hydroxide alone","Administering the pure polysaccharide orally at high doses to bypass lymph nodes","Covently linking the capsular polysaccharide to a carrier protein (e.g., CRM197 or tetanus toxoid) enables B-cells to present carrier peptides via MHC-II to CD4+ helper T-cells, converting the response into T-cell-dependent immunity with affinity maturation and memory","Adding live virulent bacteria to induce septic shock"],"a":2,"e":"Pure bacterial polysaccharides are T-cell-independent type 2 (TI-2) antigens: they cross-link B-cell receptors directly without MHC-II peptide presentation to T-helper cells, yielding weak IgM responses, poor class switching, no immunological memory, and unresponsiveness in infants (\u003c2 years). Covalent conjugation of the polysaccharide to an immunogenic carrier protein (e.g., non-toxic diphtheria mutant CRM197, tetanus toxoid, or meningococcal outer membrane protein) lets polysaccharide-specific B-cells internalize the conjugate, process the carrier protein, and present peptides on MHC-II to CD4+ T-helper cells—triggering germinal center IgG class switching and durable memory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00661","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy","topic":"Lubrication Pitfalls and Superdisintegrants","q":"A final tablet blend containing 1.0% magnesium stearate and 4% croscarmellose sodium is mixed for 25 minutes instead of the validated 3 minutes in a V-blender before compression. Subsequent QC testing shows markedly reduced tablet crushing strength (hardness) and prolonged disintegration/dissolution times. What mechanism explains this batch failure?","o":["Magnesium stearate volatilizes completely out of the V-blender within 25 minutes","Over-blending increases tablet porosity to 95%, making tablets dissolve instantaneously","Croscarmellose sodium chemically cross-links magnesium stearate into insoluble stone","Prolonged high-shear blending smears hydrophobic magnesium stearate platelets into a continuous film over granule surfaces, blocking interparticulate bonding and preventing water wicking"],"a":3,"e":"Magnesium stearate is a boundary-type hydrophobic lubricant whose lamellar platelets shear and coat granule surfaces during mixing. While brief mixing (typically 2–5 minutes) provides adequate die-wall lubrication, excessive blending time (e.g., 25 minutes) spreads an unbroken hydrophobic lipid barrier across particles—weakening solid-solid cohesive bonding (lowering tablet tensile strength/hardness) and impeding aqueous wetting so superdisintegrants like croscarmellose sodium cannot rapidly wick water to swell and break the tablet.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00662","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy","topic":"Scale-Up of High-Shear Mixer Granulators","q":"When scaling up a wet granulation process in geometrically similar high-shear mixer granulators from a 10 L pilot bowl (impeller diameter D1 = 0.25 m) to a 160 L commercial bowl (impeller diameter D2 = 0.625 m), why must impeller rotational speed (RPM, N) be reduced rather than kept identical to pilot RPM?","o":["Keeping identical RPM in a larger bowl dramatically increases peripheral tip speed (pi·D·N) and centrifugal Froude number (N²·D/g), causing over-granulation, lump formation, and excess frictional heat","Larger bowls require negative vacuum RPM to spin backwards","Keeping identical RPM reduces shear force to zero in larger bowls","Granule binder viscosity drops to zero in commercial stainless-steel bowls"],"a":0,"e":"In high-shear wet granulation scale-up, mechanical energy imparted to the powder bed scales with impeller tip speed (v_tip = pi · D · N) and Froude number (Fr = N² · D / g, ratio of centrifugal to gravitational force). Increasing impeller diameter D by 2.5-fold at constant RPM (N) would multiply tip speed 2.5-fold and centrifugal force \u003e6-fold, causing excessive shear, dense over-granulated agglomerates, and thermal stress; therefore, RPM is scaled down proportionally (N2 = N1 · (D1/D2)^n, typically n = 0.5–1.0 depending on constant Fr or constant tip speed).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00663","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Industrial Pharmacy","topic":"Ophthalmic Antimicrobial Preservatives","q":"Benzalkonium chloride (BAC, typically 0.01% w/v) is the most widely used antimicrobial preservative in multi-dose ophthalmic drops. Which two clinical/formulation limitations restrict the use of benzalkonium chloride?","o":["Incompatibility (precipitation) with anionic drugs/soaps/nitrates and adsorption onto hydrophilic soft contact lenses causing corneal epithelial irritation","Inability to dissolve in water above 0.0001% and rapid evaporation at 25 °C","Complete loss of antimicrobial activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa unless heated to 121 °C","Converting neutral solutions into strongly acidic pH \u003c 1.0"],"a":0,"e":"Benzalkonium chloride is a cationic quaternary ammonium surfactant that disrupts bacterial cell membranes (especially effective against Pseudomonas aeruginosa when combined with 0.01–0.1% disodium EDTA). However, being cationic, it precipitates with large anionic drug molecules, soaps, salicylates, and nitrates, and binds/accumulates inside porous hydrogel soft contact lenses—causing ocular surface toxicity and punctate keratitis unless lenses are removed prior to instillation (waiting \u003e= 15 minutes before reinsertion) or preservative-free unit-dose vials are used.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00664","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Renal Clearance Mechanisms","q":"An organic acid antibiotic has an unbound plasma fraction (fu) of 0.50 in a patient with a normal glomerular filtration rate (GFR) of 120 mL/min. Pharmacokinetic analysis measures the drug\u0027s renal clearance (CL_R) as 300 mL/min. Which renal elimination mechanism must predominate for this drug?","o":["Glomerular filtration alone with zero tubular transport","Active tubular secretion (via OAT1/OAT3), because CL_R (300 mL/min) substantially exceeds filtration clearance fu × GFR (60 mL/min)","Passive net tubular reabsorption, because CL_R exceeds fu × GFR","Complete hepatic biliary excretion without any renal tubular transport"],"a":1,"e":"Renal clearance equals CL_R = (Rate of filtration + Rate of secretion - Rate of reabsorption) / Cp, where glomerular filtration clearance of unbound drug is fu × GFR = 0.50 × 120 mL/min = 60 mL/min. Because observed CL_R (300 mL/min) is fivefold higher than maximum filtration clearance (60 mL/min), active carrier-mediated tubular secretion (e.g., via proximal OAT1/OAT3 transporters, inhibitable by probenecid) must be the dominant renal elimination mechanism.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00665","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"FDA / ICH IVIVC Categories","q":"Which In Vitro–In Vivo Correlation (IVIVC) category represents a point-to-point relationship between the entire in vitro dissolution curve and the in vivo input/dissolution curve (obtained by Wagner-Nelson or Loo-Riegelman deconvolution), serving as the highest predictive level eligible for regulatory biowaivers?","o":["Level D qualitative rank-order only","Level C IVIVC","Level A IVIVC","Level B IVIVC"],"a":2,"e":"USFDA guidance defines four IVIVC levels: Level A is a point-to-point correlation between in vitro fraction dissolved and in vivo fraction absorbed/dissolved across the full time course, directly predicting plasma profiles from dissolution data and supporting regulatory biowaivers for major SUPAC manufacturing/site changes. Level B compares summary statistical moments (mean dissolution time vs mean residence time, non-unique); Level C correlates a single dissolution time point (e.g., t50%) to a single PK parameter (Cmax or AUC); Level D is only a qualitative rank-order.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00666","course":"B.Pharm","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Washout Period and Highly Variable Drugs (HVDs)","q":"In a single-dose, two-period crossover bioequivalence (BE) trial for a generic drug with an elimination half-life of 24 hours, what minimum washout interval is required between Period 1 and Period 2, and why are 3-way or 4-way replicate crossover designs used when intra-subject variability (%CV) exceeds 30%?","o":["Washout of 1 half-life (24 hours) is sufficient; replicate designs reduce sample size simply by doubling the dose","Washout \u003e= 24 hours; replicate designs eliminate the need to measure AUC","No washout is ever needed in crossover studies if fasting subjects drink water","Washout \u003e= 5–7 half-lives (at least 120–168 hours) to prevent carryover (\u003e5% Cmax); replicate designs measure true reference within-subject variability (sWR) for Reference-Scaled Average Bioequivalence (RSABE) of Highly Variable Drugs"],"a":3,"e":"To ensure virtually complete drug elimination (\u003e97–99%) and prevent pre-dose Period 2 carryover (\u003e5% of Cmax), regulatory guidelines (CDSCO, USFDA, EMA) mandate a washout period of at least 5 (ideally \u003e= 7) elimination half-lives—here \u003e= 120–168 hours (5–7 days). For Highly Variable Drugs (HVDs) exhibiting intra-subject %CV \u003e= 30% on Cmax or AUC, conventional 2×2 crossover studies require impractically huge sample sizes; 3-way (TRR/RTR/RRT) or 4-way (TRTR/RTRT) replicate designs quantify the reference product\u0027s within-subject standard deviation (sWR), permitting widened Reference-Scaled Average Bioequivalence (RSABE) acceptance limits.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00667","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Equipment \u0026 Workstation Safety","topic":"Pin Index Safety System \u0026 Oxygen Flush","q":"While mounting reserve E-cylinders on the anaesthesia workstation yoke, which Pin Index Safety System (PISS) hole positions correspond to Oxygen and Nitrous Oxide respectively, and what hazard occurs if the O2 flush valve is activated during the inspiratory phase of mechanical ventilation?","o":["O2: 2 and 5; N2O: 3 and 5; risk of pulmonary barotrauma from 35-75 L/min at 45-55 psi","O2: 3 and 5; N2O: 1 and 5; risk of hypercapnia from soda lime bypass","O2: 2 and 6; N2O: 3 and 6; risk of hypoxic gas mixture delivery","O2: 1 and 5; N2O: 2 and 5; risk of volatile anaesthetic overdose"],"a":0,"e":"In the PISS standard, Oxygen pin positions are 2 and 5, Nitrous Oxide are 3 and 5, and Medical Air are 1 and 5. The O2 flush valve bypasses both flowmeters and vaporizers to deliver 100% O2 at 35-75 L/min directly from the 45-55 psi intermediate pipeline/regulator circuit to the common gas outlet; activating it during mechanical inspiration prevents relief through the ventilator spill valve, risking severe barotrauma and diluting volatile agent concentration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00668","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Breathing Systems \u0026 Monitoring","topic":"Waveform Capnography Troubleshooting","q":"During general anaesthesia with a Bain (coaxial Mapleson D) breathing circuit under controlled ventilation, the capnogram baseline fails to return to 0 mmHg during inspiration (inspired CO2 rises to 18 mmHg) and EtCO2 climbs progressively despite fresh gas flow set at 100 mL/kg/min. Which mechanical circuit fault causes this acute rebreathing?","o":["Water condensation inside the side-stream capnograph Nafion tubing","Disconnection or fracture of the inner fresh-gas delivery tube at the machine end","Excessive fresh gas flow exceeding minute ventilation","Stuck-open adjustable pressure-limiting (APL) valve"],"a":1,"e":"In a Bain circuit, fresh gas travels down the narrow inner tube to the patient end while expired gas returns through the outer corrugated limb. If the inner tube detaches or fractures at the machine end, fresh gas short-circuits directly out the APL valve/reservoir bag, turning the entire outer limb into massive apparatus dead space and causing severe hypercapnic rebreathing (elevated inspiratory baseline on capnography).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00669","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Pharmacology \u0026 Physics of Volatile Anaesthetics","topic":"Variable-Bypass Vaporizers","q":"At 20 C and standard sea-level atmospheric pressure (760 mmHg), isoflurane has a saturated vapor pressure (SVP) of 240 mmHg. In a variable-bypass vaporizer, if 100 mL/min of carrier gas enters the vaporizing chamber and becomes fully saturated with isoflurane, what is the volume flow rate of pure isoflurane vapor exiting the chamber?","o":["31.6 mL/min","68.4 mL/min","46.2 mL/min","100.0 mL/min"],"a":2,"e":"By Dalton\u0027s law, vapor output from a fully saturated chamber is given by: Vapor output = Carrier gas flow x [SVP / (Barometric pressure - SVP)] = 100 x [240 / (760 - 240)] = 100 x (240 / 520) = 46.15 mL/min (~46.2 mL/min). Total flow leaving the chamber is 100 + 46.2 = 146.2 mL/min, which is then diluted by the bypass gas to achieve the dialed percentage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00670","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Airway Management \u0026 Difficult Airway Protocols","topic":"Cormack-Lehane Classification","q":"During direct laryngoscopy with a Macintosh blade, the anaesthesiologist visualizes only the epiglottis with no view of the arytenoids or vocal cords (Cormack-Lehane Grade III). Which airway adjunct should the anaesthesia technologist hand over immediately to facilitate blind-angled tracheal intubation?","o":["Uncuffed nasopharyngeal airway","Magill forceps without a stylet","Oropharyngeal Guedel airway","Gum elastic bougie (Eschmann tracheal tube introducer) with coude tip"],"a":3,"e":"In a Cormack-Lehane Grade III view (only epiglottis visible), an Eschmann gum elastic bougie with its 30-degree angled coude tip can be directed posteriorly beneath the epiglottis and anteriorly toward the glottis, providing tactile \u0027tracheal clicks\u0027 as the tip slides over tracheal rings and a distal \u0027hold-up\u0027 sign before railroading the endotracheal tube.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00671","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Emergencies \u0026 Crisis Management","topic":"Malignant Hyperthermia (MH)","q":"Twenty minutes after induction with succinylcholine and sevoflurane in an adolescent undergoing appendectomy, the anaesthesia workstation displays a steep, unexplained rise in EtCO2 from 36 to 78 mmHg despite doubling minute ventilation, along with sinus tachycardia (148 bpm), generalized muscle rigidity, and hot soda lime canister. What is the earliest reliable monitor sign and definitive pharmacologic treatment?","o":["Rapid unexplained rise in EtCO2 (hypermetabolism); discontinue volatile/succinylcholine, hyperventilate with 100% O2 at high FGF, and administer IV dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg","Widened QRS complex; administer IV lidocaine and atropine","Core hyperthermia \u003e 41 C; administer IV calcium chloride and diltiazem","Drop in SpO2; administer IV epinephrine 1 mg"],"a":0,"e":"Unexplained, rapid elevation of EtCO2 (accompanied by tachycardia and masseter spasm) is the earliest sensitive indicator of Malignant Hyperthermia (RYR1 mutation causing uncontrolled sarcoplasmic Ca2+ release and hypermetabolism); severe temperature rise is often a late sign. Immediate management: turn off volatile agents, hyperventilate 100% O2 at \u003e= 10 L/min FGF, and give IV dantrolene sodium 2.5 mg/kg (repeated up to 10 mg/kg+ until resolution).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00672","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Regional Anaesthesia \u0026 Pharmacology","topic":"Local Anaesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST)","q":"Shortly after an accidental intravascular injection during a brachial plexus block with bupivacaine, a 70 kg patient develops perioral numbness, tinnitus, generalized tonic-clonic seizures, and refractory ventricular fibrillation. Per ASRA guidelines, what is the specific rescue infusion and initial bolus dose?","o":["IV lidocaine 1.5 mg/kg bolus to suppress ventricular arrhythmias","IV 20% lipid emulsion (Intralipid) 1.5 mL/kg (~100 mL) bolus over 2-3 minutes followed by 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion","IV propofol 2 mg/kg bolus (using propofol\u0027s 10% lipid carrier)","IV vasopressin 40 units bolus plus amiodarone"],"a":1,"e":"Bupivacaine binds cardiac voltage-gated sodium channels avidly (\u0027fast-in, slow-out\u0027), causing severe refractory cardiotoxicity. ASRA LAST guidelines prescribe an immediate IV bolus of 20% lipid emulsion at 1.5 mL/kg (or 100 mL for \u003e=70 kg) followed by a 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion (Acting as a \u0027lipid sink\u0027 and metabolic shuttle) alongside modified ACLS (using small epinephrine doses \u003c= 1 mcg/kg).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00673","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Neuromuscular Blockade \u0026 Monitoring","topic":"Train-of-Four (TOF) Monitoring","q":"At the end of laparoscopic cholecystectomy under rocuronium relaxation, the anaesthesia technologist applies quantitative acceleromyography Train-of-Four (TOF) stimulation at the ulnar nerve (adductor pollicis). What minimum quantitative TOF ratio (T4/T1) must be achieved before safe tracheal extubation, and why is visual/tactile assessment insufficient alone?","o":["TOF ratio \u003e= 0.50; visual fade disappears at 0.20","TOF ratio \u003e= 0.70; single twitch height suffices for all non-depolarizing blockers","TOF ratio \u003e= 0.90; human eyes and fingers cannot reliably detect tactile fade once TOF ratio exceeds ~0.40, leaving patients at risk for pharyngeal dysfunction and aspiration","TOF count of 2 twitches without T4 recovery is adequate for extubation"],"a":2,"e":"Modern ASA/ESA neuromuscular monitoring guidelines mandate a quantitative Train-of-Four ratio (T4/T1) \u003e= 0.90 at the adductor pollicis prior to extubation. Subjective visual/tactile evaluation loses accuracy between TOF 0.40 and 0.90, yet values below 0.90 impair upper airway pharyngeal coordination, hypoxic ventilatory drive, and swallowing—causing postoperative residual curarization (PORC).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00674","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Regional Anaesthesia Complications","topic":"High / Total Spinal Anaesthesia","q":"Three minutes after receiving intrathecal hyperbaric bupivacaine for an inguinal hernia repair, a supine patient develops tingling in the fingers (C8-T1), muffled whisper, rapid drop in BP from 130/80 to 62/34 mmHg, HR 38 bpm, shallow diaphragmatic breaths, and loss of consciousness. What has occurred, and what are the immediate priorities?","o":["Acute local anaesthetic allergy; administer IM ketorolac and observe","Post-dural puncture headache; keep patient sitting upright for 2 hours","Vasovagal syncope; sit the patient upright immediately and give oral fluids","Total spinal anaesthesia (cephalad local anaesthetic spread blocking T1-T4 cardiac accelerators, C3-C5 phrenic roots, and brainstem); secure airway immediately (intubation + 100% O2), elevate legs, infuse rapid IV crystalloids, and administer IV epinephrine/ephedrine/atropine"],"a":3,"e":"Total spinal anaesthesia occurs when excessive cephalad intrathecal local anaesthetic blocks sympathetic outflow and T1-T4 cardioaccelerator fibers (profound hypotension + bradycardia), C3-C5 phrenic nerve roots (apnea), and medullary centers (unconsciousness). Immediate management is ABC resuscitation: bag-mask 100% O2 -\u003e rapid endotracheal intubation, rapid IV fluids, vasopressors/anticholinergics (epinephrine/ephedrine/atropine), and supportive ventilation until block regresses.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00675","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Gas Conditioning \u0026 Safety","topic":"Soda Lime Desiccation Hazards","q":"If oxygen is inadvertently left flowing through a circle-system CO2 absorber over the weekend, desiccating the soda lime (containing NaOH/KOH), which toxic breakdown products are generated upon subsequent use of desflurane/isoflurane versus sevoflurane?","o":["Carbon monoxide (CO) with desflurane/isoflurane (especially desflurane); Compound A (and exothermic fire risk) with sevoflurane","Nitric oxide with isoflurane; hydrogen sulfide with sevoflurane","Phosgene gas with sevoflurane; ozone with desflurane","Methane gas with all halogenated ethers"],"a":0,"e":"Desiccated strong-base CO2 absorbents (containing KOH/NaOH, especially older Baralyme or dry soda lime) degrade CHF2-containing volatiles—desflurane \u003e enflurane \u003e isoflurane—into toxic Carbon Monoxide (CO), risking occult intraoperative carboxyhemoglobinemia. Desiccated absorbent also degrades sevoflurane into nephrotoxic Compound A and can trigger extreme exothermic reactions/absorber fires.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00676","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Patient Monitoring \u0026 Bioinstrumentation","topic":"Dyshemoglobin Artifacts","q":"A patient exposed to benzocaine/dapsone develops severe central cyanosis and chocolate-brown arterial blood (methemoglobinemia 35%), while another patient rescued from a house fire has 30% carboxyhemoglobin (COHb). How does a standard two-wavelength (660 nm / 940 nm) fingertip pulse oximeter read in severe methemoglobinemia versus carbon monoxide poisoning?","o":["Methemoglobinemia reads 100%; CO poisoning reads 50%","Methemoglobinemia plateaus around ~85% regardless of true PaO2 (equal absorbance at 660 and 940 nm); CO poisoning reads falsely HIGH/NORMAL (~98-100%) because COHb mimics oxyhemoglobin at 660 nm—requiring multi-wavelength CO-oximetry for both","Both conditions accurately display true arterial SaO2 on standard two-wavelength SpO2 sensors","Methemoglobinemia reads 0% immediately; CO poisoning reads 20%"],"a":1,"e":"Conventional pulse oximeters emit red (660 nm) and infrared (940 nm) light and compute an R-ratio. Methemoglobin absorbs equally strongly at both 660 nm and 940 nm (R ≈ 1.0), forcing the SpO2 reading toward ~85% regardless of actual hypoxemia. Conversely, carboxyhemoglobin absorbs 660 nm red light similarly to oxyhemoglobin, producing a falsely reassuring SpO2 (~98-100%) during lethal CO poisoning. Both require multi-wavelength blood-gas CO-oximetry.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00677","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Post-Anaesthesia Care \u0026 Airway Emergencies","topic":"Laryngospasm \u0026 Negative-Pressure Pulmonary Edema","q":"Immediately after tracheal extubation in a muscular 24-year-old athlete, upper airway stridor progresses to complete silent airway obstruction (laryngospasm) with vigorous chest-wall inspiratory efforts against a closed glottis. Moments after relieving the spasm with jaw thrust and CPAP, the patient coughs up pink frothy sputum and SpO2 drops to 82% with bilateral alveolar infiltrates. What complication occurred, and what drug breaks refractory laryngospasm?","o":["TACO from 200 mL saline; IV protamine","Negative-Pressure (Post-Obstructive) Pulmonary Edema (NPPE) caused by extreme negative intrathoracic pressure pulling fluid across pulmonary capillaries; refractory laryngospasm is broken with IV succinylcholine (0.1-0.5 mg/kg) + positive-pressure 100% O2","Tension pneumothorax from spontaneous breathing; immediate bilateral chest tubes before O2","Fat embolism syndrome; IV heparin infusion"],"a":1,"e":"Forceful inspiratory efforts against an obstructed glottis (modified Mueller maneuver) generate extreme negative intrathoracic pressure (-50 to -100 cmH2O), boosting venous return to the right heart while increasing LV afterload and Starling transcapillary hydrostatic gradient—producing Negative-Pressure Pulmonary Edema (NPPE). Breaking refractory laryngospasm involves jaw thrust + Larson\u0027s notch pressure, CPAP with 100% O2, deepening anaesthesia (propofol), or small-dose IV succinylcholine (0.1-0.5 mg/kg), followed by PEEP/O2 support for NPPE.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00678","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Medical Gases \u0026 Patient Transport","topic":"Oxygen E-Cylinder Duration Calculation","q":"Prior to transporting an intubated ICU patient to CT scan using a Bain circuit at 10 L/min oxygen flow, the anaesthesia technologist checks the portable Oxygen E-cylinder pressure gauge (full cylinder = 2,000 psi containing ~660 liters; standard E-cylinder factor = 0.30 L/psi conservative or 0.33 L/psi exact). If the gauge reads 1,000 psi, using the standard 0.30 L/psi factor (leaving a small safety margin), how many minutes of oxygen supply remain?","o":["10 minutes","60 minutes","30 minutes","100 minutes"],"a":2,"e":"Because Oxygen remains a non-liquefied compressed gas at room temperature, cylinder pressure is directly proportional to remaining gas volume (Boyle\u0027s law). Remaining usable volume = Gauge Pressure (psi) x Cylinder Factor (0.30 L/psi for O2 E-cylinder) = 1,000 x 0.30 = 300 liters. At 10 L/min flow, remaining transport duration = 300 / 10 = 30 minutes (or ~33 min exact if full 660 L / 2 = 330 L).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00679","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Machine Checkout \u0026 Safety","topic":"Universal Negative-Pressure Leak Test","q":"During morning anaesthesia workstation pre-use checkout, why MUST the technologist perform the Universal Negative-Pressure Leak Test (using a suction bulb at the common gas outlet—first with all vaporizers OFF, then sequentially with each vaporizer ON) rather than relying solely on a positive-pressure circle-system leak test?","o":["The suction bulb calibrates the oxygen analyzer to 100%","Positive-pressure testing detects vaporizer leaks only if N2O is flowing at 10 L/min","Negative-pressure testing measures soda lime moisture content","A one-way check valve at the common gas outlet blocks positive breathing-circuit pressure from traveling backward, so ONLY a negative-pressure suction bulb test detects leaks inside flowmeters and vaporizers (tested individually with each dial ON)"],"a":3,"e":"Most modern anaesthesia machines incorporate a one-way outlet check valve between the vaporizers and common gas outlet to prevent backpressure artifacts. Consequently, pressurizing the circle breathing circuit tests ONLY downstream of the check valve and misses dangerous upstream low-pressure leaks (cracked flowmeter tubes, leaky vaporizer O-rings) that cause hypoxic gas mixtures or intraoperative awareness—detected only by the negative-pressure suction bulb test (remaining collapsed \u003e= 10 s) with each vaporizer turned ON individually.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00680","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Obstetric Anaesthesia Technology","topic":"Aortocaval Compression \u0026 Aspiration Prophylaxis","q":"Immediately after spinal anaesthesia for an elective Cesarean delivery at 39 weeks, a supine parturient develops acute hypotension (BP 68/36 mmHg), nausea, and fetal heart rate deceleration. Which immediate physical positioning maneuver relieves aortocaval compression, and which pre-operative oral antacid prevents chemical pneumonitis (Mendelson syndrome) without causing particulate lung injury if aspirated?","o":["15-degree left lateral uterine displacement (wedge under right hip or table tilt left) to decompress the IVC and aorta (alongside IV phenylephrine/ephedrine + fluid); oral non-particulate 0.3 M sodium citrate","Prone positioning; oral activated charcoal","Right lateral tilt (wedge under left hip); oral magnesium hydroxide particulate suspension","Steep head-up reverse Trendelenburg; oral milk of magnesia"],"a":0,"e":"Beyond 20 weeks gestation, the gravid uterus compresses the inferior vena cava (reducing venous return) and aorta against the spine when supine—compounded by spinal sympathectomy. Placing a 15-degree wedge under the RIGHT hip tilts the uterus leftward off the IVC/aorta, paired with phenylephrine infusion/bolus. Clear non-particulate 0.3 M sodium citrate (30 mL) rapidly buffers gastric pH \u003e 2.5 without causing severe granulomatous foreign-body pneumonitis if aspirated (unlike particulate antacids).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00681","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Airway Devices \u0026 Ventilation","topic":"Laryngeal Mask Airway Sizing, Cuff Limits \u0026 Contraindications","q":"An anaesthesia technologist prepares a Laryngeal Mask Airway (LMA Classic) for a 70 kg adult undergoing brief elective knee arthroscopy. What is the standard adult size and maximum recommended LMA cuff pressure, and in which clinical scenario is an LMA CONTRAINDICATED as the primary elective airway?","o":["Size 2; inflate to 120 cmH2O; contraindicated in fasting patients","Size 4 (Size 5 for \u003e 70-100 kg); maintain cuff pressure \u003c= 60 cmH2O to prevent pharyngeal mucosal ischemia and lingual/recurrent laryngeal nerve injury; contraindicated in non-fasted/full-stomach emergency surgery, severe GERD, or very high airway resistance/low compliance (\u003e 25 cmH2O)","Size 6; inflate until pilot balloon feels rock-hard; ideal for emergency bowel obstruction","Size 3; requires laryngoscope blade for insertion; seals completely against gastric regurgitation"],"a":1,"e":"Standard LMA weight sizing assigns Size 3 for 30-50 kg, Size 4 for 50-70 kg, and Size 5 for 70-100 kg. Overinflating LMA cuffs (\u003e 60 cmH2O) compresses pharyngeal mucosa and risks lingual, hypoglossal, or recurrent laryngeal nerve neuropraxia. Because supraglottic airways sit above the glottis and do not seal the trachea completely, they are contraindicated in full-stomach emergency cases (bowel obstruction, trauma, active vomiting), severe GERD, and high-inflation-pressure lungs (use cuffed ETT with RSI).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00682","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Ventilator Engineering","topic":"Ascending (Standing) vs Descending (Hanging) Bellows","q":"Why are modern anaesthesia workstation ventilators designed with an ASCENDING (standing) bellows rather than an older DESCENDING (hanging) weighted bellows?","o":["Descending bellows cannot deliver positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP)","Descending bellows deliver only 100% nitrous oxide","If a major breathing-circuit disconnection occurs, an ascending bellows visibly COLLAPSES and fails to refill (triggering immediate visual and low-volume alarms), whereas a weighted descending bellows continues dropping by gravity and entrains room air into the circuit—masking the disconnection","Ascending bellows eliminate the need for a soda lime CO2 absorber"],"a":2,"e":"An ascending bellows rises during passive patient exhalation only when returning circuit gas fills it; during a circuit disconnect, it sinks flat to the bottom of the housing immediately. Conversely, a weighted descending bellows falls downward during the expiratory phase even when disconnected, sucking ambient room air through the open hose—potentially delaying disconnect alarms and diluting anaesthetic gases.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00683","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Perioperative Pharmacology \u0026 RSI","topic":"Succinylcholine Hyperkalemia Contraindications","q":"A 30-year-old man who sustained 45% TBSA flame burns 14 days ago (and has an upper motor neuron paraplegia from an associated spinal injury) requires emergency endotracheal intubation via Rapid Sequence Induction (RSI). Why is SUCCINYLCHOLINE strictly contraindicated here, and what non-depolarizing RSI alternative provides comparable \u003c 60-second onset with an immediate reversal agent?","o":["Succinylcholine causes profound hypokalemia; substitute atracurium 0.2 mg/kg","Succinylcholine is safe 14 days post-burn as long as serum K+ is \u003c 4.0 mEq/L","Succinylcholine blocks only cardiac muscarinic receptors; substitute pancuronium","Denervation and major burn injury (\u003e 24-48 hours post-injury) cause massive upregulation of extrajunctional nicotinic acetylcholine receptors across skeletal muscle, precipitating lethal ACUTE HYPERKALEMIC CARDIAC ARREST upon succinylcholine administration; use Rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg (with Sugammadex 16 mg/kg available for rapid reversal)"],"a":3,"e":"Normally, succinylcholine transiently raises serum K+ by ~0.5 mEq/L. However, starting \u003e 24-48 hours after major burns, massive crush injury, upper/lower motor neuron denervation (SCI/stroke), or prolonged ICU immobility, fetal/extrajunctional gamma-subunit ACh receptors proliferate across the entire muscle membrane—triggering massive, refractory hyperkalemia (K+ \u003e 8-10 mEq/L) and ventricular fibrillation/asystole upon succinylcholine exposure. High-dose rocuronium (1.2 mg/kg) achieves 60-second intubating conditions and is encapsulated rapidly by sugammadex (16 mg/kg).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00684","course":"B.Sc Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Inhalational Anaesthetic Pharmacology","topic":"Additive MAC Calculation","q":"In a 40-year-old adult at sea level, 1.0 MAC of Sevoflurane is 2.0% and 1.0 MAC of Nitrous Oxide (N2O) is 104%. If the patient maintains a steady-state end-tidal gas mixture of 1.5% Sevoflurane together with 52% N2O in oxygen, what total combined MAC is being delivered?","o":["1.25 MAC","1.00 MAC","1.50 MAC","0.75 MAC"],"a":0,"e":"MAC values of co-administered inhalational anaesthetics are linearly additive. Sevoflurane fraction = 1.5% / 2.0% = 0.75 MAC. N2O fraction = 52% / 104% = 0.50 MAC. Total combined MAC = 0.75 + 0.50 = 1.25 MAC (well above 1.0 MAC, preventing intraoperative movement/awareness in \u003e 95% of patients).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00685","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Echocardiography \u0026 Doppler Hemodynamics","topic":"Aortic Stenosis Quantification","q":"During transthoracic echocardiography for exertional syncope, the cardiac care technologist measures an LVOT diameter of 2.0 cm (parasternal long-axis), an LVOT pulsed-wave Doppler VTI of 20 cm, and a continuous-wave Doppler aortic valve jet VTI of 80 cm. Using the continuity equation (pi = 3.14), what is the calculated Aortic Valve Area (AVA) and severity grade?","o":["1.25 cm2 (moderate aortic stenosis)","0.79 cm2 (severe aortic stenosis)","1.57 cm2 (mild aortic stenosis)","0.39 cm2 (normal valve area)"],"a":1,"e":"LVOT cross-sectional area = pi x (diameter/2)^2 = 3.14 x (1.0)^2 = 3.14 cm2. By conservation of stroke volume, AVA = (CSA_LVOT x VTI_LVOT) / VTI_AV = (3.14 x 20) / 80 = 62.8 / 80 = 0.785 cm2 (~0.79 cm2). An AVA \u003c 1.0 cm2 meets ASE criteria for severe aortic stenosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00686","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Cardiac Catheterization \u0026 Mechanical Circulatory Support","topic":"IABP Timing \u0026 Physiology","q":"When optimizing a 1:1 Intra-Aortic Balloon Pump (IABP) using the arterial pressure waveform in a patient with cardiogenic shock, at which exact points of the cardiac cycle must balloon inflation and deflation occur?","o":["Inflate during the P wave; deflate at the end of the T wave","Inflate continuously throughout systole; deflate during mid-diastole","Inflate immediately at the dicrotic notch (aortic valve closure); deflate just prior to systolic ejection (isovolumetric contraction)","Inflate at the peak of the systolic upstroke; deflate at the dicrotic notch"],"a":2,"e":"Balloon inflation immediately at the dicrotic notch (aortic valve closure) displaces blood proximally and distally, augmenting diastolic aortic pressure and coronary perfusion. Rapid deflation just before aortic valve opening creates a vacuum-like reduction in end-diastolic aortic pressure, lowering left ventricular afterload and myocardial oxygen demand (MVO2).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00687","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Cardiac Electrophysiology \u0026 Pacemakers","topic":"Pacemaker Malfunctions","q":"On continuous telemetry of a patient with a VVI permanent pacemaker set at 70 bpm, the cardiac care technologist sees regular ventricular pacing spikes every 857 ms that are NOT followed by a paced QRS complex or T wave, while intrinsic escape beats occur independently. Which pacemaker malfunction is demonstrated?","o":["Oversensing of T waves","Failure to output (battery depletion without spikes)","Pacemaker-mediated endless loop tachycardia","Failure to capture (loss of capture)"],"a":3,"e":"Pacing spikes occurring at the programmed rate (857 ms = 70 bpm) without eliciting a subsequent myocardial depolarization (QRS complex) represent failure to capture. Common causes include lead dislodgement, exit block, myocardial fibrosis/infarction at the lead tip, hyperkalemia, or insufficient pacing output amplitude.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00688","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Cardiac Catheterization Lab Hemodynamics","topic":"Fick Principle Calculation","q":"During right heart catheterization, a patient\u0027s measured oxygen consumption (VO2) is 250 mL/min. Blood gas analysis yields an arterial oxygen content (CaO2) of 190 mL/L (19.0 mL/dL) and a pulmonary artery mixed venous oxygen content (CvO2) of 140 mL/L (14.0 mL/dL). Using the Fick equation, what is the patient\u0027s Cardiac Output?","o":["5.0 L/min","6.25 L/min","3.5 L/min","4.0 L/min"],"a":0,"e":"By the Fick principle, Cardiac Output (L/min) = VO2 (mL/min) / [CaO2 - CvO2 (mL/L)]. Here, arteriovenous oxygen difference = 190 - 140 = 50 mL/L. Therefore, CO = 250 / 50 = 5.0 L/min. Unlike thermodilution, the Fick method remains accurate in severe tricuspid regurgitation and low-output states.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00689","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Interventional Cardiology \u0026 Cath Lab Technology","topic":"Coronary No-Reflow Phenomenon","q":"During primary PCI for an acute anterior STEMI, immediately after stent deployment in the proximal LAD, the artery appears angiographically patent without dissection or residual stenosis, yet antegrade contrast flow drops abruptly to TIMI grade 0-1 with chest pain and ST re-elevation (\u0027no-reflow\u0027 phenomenon). Which intracoronary vasodilator regimen is prepared in the cath lab to restore microvascular perfusion?","o":["Intracoronary ergonovine or acetylcholine","Intracoronary adenosine, sodium nitroprusside, or nicardipine/verapamil delivered distally via microcatheter","Intracoronary protamine sulfate to reverse heparin immediately","Intracoronary norepinephrine bolus"],"a":1,"e":"Coronary no-reflow results from distal atherothrombotic microembolization, endothelial swelling, and intense microvascular arteriolar spasm despite epicardial patency. Intracoronary arteriolar vasodilators—adenosine, sodium nitroprusside, nicardipine, or verapamil (often via a distal microcatheter/aspiration catheter)—dilate resistance arterioles and restore TIMI 3 flow.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00690","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Non-Invasive Cardiology (ECG \u0026 TMT)","topic":"Bruce Protocol \u0026 Ischemic ECG Criteria","q":"During a Bruce protocol Treadmill Test (TMT), which exercise-induced ECG finding is the classic standard criterion for a positive ischemic test, and which clinical condition is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to performing TMT?","o":["PR-interval shortening during sinus tachycardia; asymptomatic right bundle branch block","J-point elevation of 0.5 mm with rapid upsloping ST segment; mild controlled hypertension","\u003e= 1.0 mm horizontal or downsloping ST-segment depression measured 60-80 ms after the J-point; acute myocardial infarction within 2 days or symptomatic severe aortic stenosis","Isolated premature atrial contractions at peak exercise; stable exertional angina"],"a":2,"e":"Per ACC/AHA Exercise Testing Guidelines, \u003e= 1.0 mm (0.1 mV) horizontal or downsloping ST-segment depression at 60-80 ms post-J-point across \u003e= 3 consecutive beats is the standard positive ischemic criterion (downsloping carries highest specificity). Absolute contraindications include acute MI (\u003c 2 days), ongoing unstable angina, decompensated heart failure, uncontrolled arrhythmias, and symptomatic severe aortic stenosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00691","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Critical Care Echocardiography","topic":"Echocardiographic Signs of Cardiac Tamponade","q":"A hypotensive patient with a large circumferential pericardial effusion undergoes bedside transthoracic echocardiography. Which constellation of 2D, M-mode, and pulsed-wave Doppler findings confirms hemodynamically significant cardiac tamponade?","o":["Systolic anterior motion (SAM) of the anterior mitral leaflet only","Hyperdynamic LV systolic collapse in systole with collapsible IVC (\u003e 75% on sniff)","Isolated left ventricular hypertrophy with E/A ratio \u003e 2.0","Late-diastolic right atrial inversion, early-diastolic right ventricular free-wall collapse, dilated non-collapsing IVC (plethora), and \u003e 25% inspiratory drop in mitral inflow E-wave velocity"],"a":3,"e":"In cardiac tamponade, rising intrapericardial pressure exceeds intracardiac diastolic pressures—first collapsing the thin-walled right atrium in late diastole/early systole (most sensitive) and the right ventricle in early diastole (more specific), accompanied by IVC plethora (\u003c 50% inspiratory collapse) and exaggerated ventricular interdependence (\u003e 25% inspiratory fall in mitral E velocity, \u003e 40% tricuspid E rise).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00692","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Coronary Angiography \u0026 Fluoroscopic Projections","topic":"Left Coronary Artery Profiling","q":"During diagnostic left coronary angiography in the cardiac catheterization laboratory, which fluoroscopic C-arm angulation is famously termed the \u0027Spider View\u0027 and is optimal for profiling the Left Main Coronary Artery (LMCA) bifurcation into the LAD and Left Circumflex (LCx) arteries?","o":["LAO 45 degrees / Caudal 30 degrees","RAO 30 degrees / Cranial 30 degrees","LAO 30 degrees / Cranial 25 degrees","Straight AP Cranial 40 degrees"],"a":0,"e":"The LAO Caudal (\u0027Spider\u0027) projection (typically LAO 40-50 deg, Caudal 25-35 deg) looks up from below the diaphragm along the long axis of the left main stem, spreading the LMCA bifurcation cleanly into the proximal LAD (going upward/right) and proximal LCx/obtuse marginals (going downward/left) without vessel overlap.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00693","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Clinical Electrocardiography","topic":"Bazett\u0027s Corrected QT Interval (QTc)","q":"On a standard 25 mm/s surface ECG of a patient receiving amiodarone and ondansetron, the measured QT interval spans 10 small boxes (0.40 seconds = 400 ms) and the R-R interval spans 16 small boxes (0.64 seconds = 640 ms; HR ~94 bpm). Using Bazett\u0027s formula [QTc = QT / sqrt(RR in seconds)], what is the QTc?","o":["400 ms","500 ms","320 ms","625 ms"],"a":1,"e":"Bazett\u0027s formula normalizes QT for heart rate: QTc = QT / sqrt(RR in seconds). With QT = 0.40 s and RR = 0.64 s, sqrt(0.64) = 0.80 s. Therefore, QTc = 0.40 / 0.80 = 0.50 s = 500 ms. A QTc \u003e= 500 ms carries high risk for early afterdepolarizations and Torsades de Pointes polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00694","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Echocardiography \u0026 Pulmonary Hemodynamics","topic":"Modified Bernoulli Equation","q":"During transthoracic echocardiography in a patient with dyspnea (no right ventricular outflow tract or pulmonic valve stenosis), continuous-wave Doppler of the tricuspid regurgitation (TR) jet measures a peak velocity (Vmax) of 3.5 m/s. The inferior vena cava (IVC) measures 2.4 cm with \u003c 50% inspiratory collapse (estimated Right Atrial Pressure RAP = 15 mmHg). Using the simplified Bernoulli equation [P = 4V^2], what is the estimated Pulmonary Artery Systolic Pressure (PASP)?","o":["79 mmHg","29 mmHg","64 mmHg","49 mmHg"],"a":2,"e":"Using the modified Bernoulli equation, peak systolic transtricuspid pressure gradient = 4 x (V_TR)^2 = 4 x (3.5)^2 = 4 x 12.25 = 49 mmHg. Adding estimated Right Atrial Pressure (15 mmHg for a dilated, poorly collapsing IVC) gives RVSP = 49 + 15 = 64 mmHg. Without pulmonic stenosis, PASP equals RVSP (64 mmHg, indicating moderate-to-severe pulmonary hypertension).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00695","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Interventional Cardiology Physiology","topic":"Fractional Flow Reserve (FFR) \u0026 iFR","q":"During coronary angiography, an angiographically borderline (60%) mid-LAD lesion is interrogated with a pressure wire. Under maximal hyperemia induced by intravenous/intracoronary adenosine, what Fractional Flow Reserve (FFR = Pd / Pa) cutoff proves hemodynamically significant ischemia justifying PCI stenting (versus resting instantaneous wave-free ratio iFR cutoff)?","o":["FFR \u003c= 0.50 (iFR \u003c= 0.60)","FFR \u003e= 0.95 (iFR \u003e= 0.98)","FFR \u003c= 0.90 (iFR \u003c= 0.70)","FFR \u003c= 0.80 (iFR \u003c= 0.89)"],"a":3,"e":"Based on the FAME and DEFINE-FLAIR/iFR-SWEDEHEART trials, an FFR \u003c= 0.80 during maximal adenosine hyperemia (meaning distal coronary pressure drops by \u003e= 20% across the stenosis) or a resting non-hyperemic iFR \u003c= 0.89 confirms physiologically flow-limiting ischemia benefiting from PCI; lesions with FFR \u003e 0.80 are safely deferred to medical therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00696","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Cardiac Electrophysiology \u0026 Device Therapy","topic":"ICD Magnet Application","q":"A patient with a dual-chamber Implantable Cardioverter-Defibrillator (ICD) arrives in the emergency room receiving repeated painful inappropriate ICD shocks triggered by rapid atrial fibrillation (HR 175 bpm) rather than ventricular tachycardia. When a clinical doughnut magnet is taped securely over the ICD pulse generator while awaiting programmer reprogramming, what happens to device function?","o":["Both tachyarrhythmia shock therapy AND bradycardia pacing are permanently disabled, causing asystole","Tachyarrhythmia detection and high-voltage shocks/ATP are temporarily suspended while the magnet remains in place, WITHOUT disabling underlying bradycardia pacing","The ICD immediately delivers an asynchronous 36 J defibrillation shock","The device switches permanently into VOO 120 bpm overdrive pacing"],"a":1,"e":"Unlike permanent pacemakers (where magnet placement switches pacing to an asynchronous fixed rate such as DOO/VOO), placing a clinical magnet over an ICD closes a reed switch that ONLY suspends tachyarrhythmia detection and shock/ATP delivery for as long as the magnet sits over the can, leaving programmed bradycardia pacing unaffected. Removing the magnet restores full shock therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00697","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Ambulatory Cardiac Monitoring","topic":"Device Selection by Symptom Frequency","q":"A 58-year-old patient experiences unexplained recurrent syncopal episodes roughly once every 6-8 weeks, with a normal resting 12-lead ECG and normal echocardiogram. Why is a standard 24-hour Holter monitor low-yield here, and which ambulatory monitoring strategy provides the highest diagnostic yield?","o":["A 24-hour Holter captures symptoms only if they occur daily; infrequent syncope occurring every 1-2 months requires an extended-duration event/patch monitor (14-30 days) or an Implantable Loop Recorder (ILR, up to 3 years)","24-hour Holter is ideal for bi-monthly syncope","Repeat 10-second resting 12-lead ECGs monthly","Exercise treadmill testing in the acute syncope bay only"],"a":0,"e":"Continuous 24-48 hour Holter monitoring is effective for daily or frequent arrhythmias (PVC burden, daily palpitations). When unexplained syncope occurs infrequently (every few weeks or months), diagnostic yield of a 24-hour Holter is \u003c 5-10%; ACC/AHA/ESC syncope guidelines recommend prolonged ambulatory ECG patches (14-30 days) or subcutaneous Implantable Loop Recorders (monitoring continuously for up to 3 years with retrospective memory loop).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00698","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Cardiac Catheterization Shunt Oximetry","topic":"Qp:Qs Pulmonary-to-Systemic Flow Ratio","q":"During a right-heart catheterization oximetry run in a patient with a secundum Atrial Septal Defect (ASD), saturations are: Mixed Venous SVC/IVC average (SvO2) = 70%, Pulmonary Artery (SpaO2) = 84%, Systemic Arterial (SaO2) = 98%, and Pulmonary Venous (SpvO2) = 98%. Using the shunt equation [Qp:Qs = (SaO2 - SvO2) / (SpvO2 - SpaO2)], what is the Qp:Qs ratio?","o":["1.0 : 1 (no shunt)","2.0 : 1 (hemodynamically significant left-to-right shunt)","3.5 : 1 (Eisenmenger reversal)","1.4 : 1 (borderline)"],"a":1,"e":"Qp:Qs = (SaO2 - SvO2) / (SpvO2 - SpaO2) = (98 - 70) / (98 - 84) = 28 / 14 = 2.0 : 1 (pulmonary blood flow is twice systemic blood flow). A Qp:Qs \u003e= 1.5 : 1 with right ventricular volume overload is the standard hemodynamic indication for ASD device or surgical closure (provided pulmonary vascular resistance is not irreversibly elevated).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00699","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Emergency Defibrillation \u0026 Resuscitation Equipment","topic":"Biphasic vs Monophasic Energy \u0026 Pad Placement","q":"A patient with a left pectoral permanent pacemaker develops witnessed pulseless Ventricular Fibrillation in the cardiac step-down unit. Using a biphasic defibrillator, what initial energy range is recommended (if manufacturer unknown) and where must the anterior adhesive pad be placed relative to the left-sided pacemaker generator?","o":["360 J monophasic equivalent; place pad directly over the pacemaker can","120-200 J (or max 200 J if unknown biphasic dose); place pads at least 8 cm away from the implanted pacemaker generator (e.g., standard right infraclavicular + left mid-axillary apical, or anterior-posterior vector)","10 J synchronized shock; place both pads over the sternum","50 J biphasic; remove the pacemaker surgically before shocking"],"a":1,"e":"AHA ACLS guidelines specify manufacturer-recommended biphasic defibrillation energy (120-200 J, or maximum available 200 J if unknown) for pulseless VT/VF. Pads must NEVER be placed directly over a pacemaker/ICD generator (which blocks current and risks myocardial lead-tip burns or device damage); maintain at least 8 cm (approx. 3 inches) clearance using standard right-infraclavicular/left-apical or anterior-posterior placement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00700","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Echocardiography in Valvular Heart Disease","topic":"Pressure Half-Time (PHT) Method","q":"During continuous-wave Doppler echocardiography in a patient with rheumatic mitral stenosis, the mitral inflow deceleration tracing yields a Pressure Half-Time (PHT) of 220 milliseconds. Using Hatle\u0027s formula (MVA = 220 / PHT), what is the calculated Mitral Valve Area (MVA), and why is PHT unreliable immediately after Balloon Mitral Valvotomy (BMV)?","o":["MVA = 4.4 cm2; normal mitral valve area","MVA = 0.5 cm2; PHT requires multiplying 220 by 4.4","MVA = 1.0 cm2 (severe mitral stenosis \u003c= 1.5 cm2); PHT becomes inaccurate immediately after BMV due to acute shifts in left atrial/LV compliance (and/or iatrogenic ASD), requiring 2D planimetry of the mitral orifice instead","MVA = 2.0 cm2; PHT is unaffected by acute left atrial compliance changes"],"a":2,"e":"Hatle\u0027s empirical formula estimates Mitral Valve Area as MVA = 220 / PHT (ms) = 220 / 220 = 1.0 cm2 (severe MS; current guidelines define severe MS as MVA \u003c= 1.5 cm2, very severe \u003c= 1.0 cm2). However, PHT reflects not only valve area but also net atrioventricular compliance and driving gradients—rendering it invalid immediately (24-72 h) after BMV, in severe aortic regurgitation, or atrial septal defects, where direct parasternal short-axis 2D/3D planimetry is gold standard.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00701","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Interventional Cardiology Emergencies","topic":"Ellis Type III Coronary Perforation","q":"During rotational atherectomy and PCI of a heavily calcified proximal LAD lesion, angiography suddenly demonstrates free jetting contrast extravasation into the pericardial space (Ellis Type III coronary perforation) with BP plunging to 64/38 mmHg. What is the immediate life-saving cath-lab sequence?","o":["Withdraw all guidewires immediately and administer IV tenecteplase","Inject intracoronary adenosine and continue high-pressure non-compliant post-dilation","Transfer the patient out of the cath lab unmonitored without sealing the leak","Inflate a compliant angioplasty balloon at low pressure proximal to/across the perforation to tamponade bleeding immediately, perform emergent pericardiocentesis for hemodynamic tamponade, prepare a PTFE-covered stent (or microcoil for distal wire exit), and reverse heparin ONLY after removing catheters"],"a":3,"e":"Ellis Type III perforation (frank extravasation through \u003e= 1 mm tear into pericardium) rapidly causes fatal cardiac tamponade. Immediate low-pressure prolonged balloon inflation seals the leak while emergent pericardiocentesis (via subxiphoid echo-guided pigtail, autotransfusing blood if needed) restores hemodynamics, and a covered stent (often via ping-pong dual guide catheter) definitively seals proximal vessel tears. Never reverse heparin prematurely while balloons/wires sit in the coronary artery (risks catastrophic vessel thrombosis!).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00702","course":"B.Sc Cardiac Care Technology","subject":"Electrocardiography Techniques","topic":"Right-Sided (V4R) \u0026 Posterior (V7-V9) Leads","q":"A patient with acute inferior STEMI (ST elevation in II, III, aVF) presents with clear lung fields, hypotension (BP 82/54 mmHg), elevated JVP, and 2 mm ST depression with tall R waves in V1-V3. Where must the cardiac care technologist place leads V4R and V7-V9 respectively to diagnose right ventricular infarction and true posterior STEMI?","o":["V4R: right 5th intercostal space at the mid-clavicular line; V7-V9: horizontal 5th ICS level at left posterior axillary line (V7), left mid-scapular/tip of scapula line (V8), and left paraspinal border (V9)","V4R: left 5th ICS mid-axillary line; V7-V9: right posterior thorax","V4R: left 2nd ICS sternal border; V7-V9: right anterior chest wall","V4R: right 2nd ICS mid-clavicular line; V7-V9: epigastrium"],"a":0,"e":"In acute inferior MI, right-sided lead V4R (mirror of V4 at the right 5th ICS mid-clavicular line) showing \u003e= 1 mm ST elevation diagnoses Right Ventricular infarction—critically alerting clinicians to AVOID preload-reducing nitrates/morphine and treat hypotension with IV fluid boluses. Posterior leads V7, V8, and V9 placed horizontally at the 5th ICS level on the left back unmask true posterior STEMI (\u003e= 0.5 mm ST elevation) corresponding to reciprocal V1-V3 ST depression.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00703","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"Invasive Hemodynamic Monitoring","topic":"Dynamic Response \u0026 Damping","q":"In a septic shock ICU patient on norepinephrine, the radial arterial line monitor suddenly displays BP 88/72 mmHg (MAP 77 mmHg) with a slurred systolic upstroke and absent dicrotic notch, whereas a brachial cuff reads 118/66 mmHg. Performing a fast-flush (square-wave) test shows a sluggish return to baseline with ZERO post-flush oscillations. What is the artifact and its effect on systolic and diastolic readings?","o":["Resonant catheter whip; requires immediate replacement with a pulmonary artery catheter","Overdamped trace (commonly from air bubbles, clot, or kinked catheter); underestimates systolic and overestimates diastolic BP while MAP stays relatively accurate","Underdamped trace caused by overly stiff tubing; overestimates systolic and underestimates diastolic BP","Zeroing drift due to transducer placed 10 cm below the phlebostatic axis; decreases all pressures by 7.4 mmHg"],"a":1,"e":"A fast-flush square-wave test producing zero oscillations and a slow baseline return diagnoses an overdamped system (caused by air bubbles, micro-clots, compliant tubing, or partial kink). Overdamping blunts waveform peaks and troughs—falsely lowering systolic BP and raising diastolic BP (narrowing pulse pressure)—while preserving mean arterial pressure (MAP) reasonably well.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00704","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"Critical Care Acid-Base \u0026 Electrolytes","topic":"Albumin-Corrected Anion Gap \u0026 Winter\u0027s Formula","q":"An ICU patient with sepsis has serum Na+ 138 mEq/L, Cl- 104 mEq/L, HCO3- 12 mEq/L, serum albumin 2.0 g/dL (normal 4.0 g/dL), and measured arterial PaCO2 26 mmHg. Using Figge\u0027s albumin correction [Corrected AG = Measured AG + 2.5 x (4.0 - albumin)] and Winter\u0027s formula [Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 x HCO3-) + 8 +/- 2], what are the corrected anion gap and acid-base interpretation?","o":["Corrected AG = 27 mEq/L; mixed metabolic acidosis and primary respiratory alkalosis","Corrected AG = 17 mEq/L; concurrent primary respiratory acidosis","Corrected AG = 27 mEq/L; high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","Corrected AG = 22 mEq/L; uncompensated metabolic acidosis"],"a":2,"e":"Uncorrected AG = 138 - (104 + 12) = 22 mEq/L. Adjusting for hypoalbuminemia (each 1 g/dL drop below 4.0 lowers AG by 2.5 mEq/L): Corrected AG = 22 + 2.5 x (4.0 - 2.0) = 27 mEq/L. Winter\u0027s expected PaCO2 = (1.5 x 12) + 8 +/- 2 = 26 +/- 2 mmHg (24-28 mmHg). Since measured PaCO2 is 26 mmHg, respiratory compensation is appropriate.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00705","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"Advanced Hemodynamic Monitoring","topic":"Pulse Pressure Variation (PPV) \u0026 SVV","q":"An ICU patient on pressure-support ventilation with active spontaneous inspiratory efforts and irregular atrial fibrillation displays a Pulse Pressure Variation (PPV) of 22% on the arterial pulse-contour monitor. Why is PPV UNRELIABLE for predicting fluid responsiveness in this patient?","o":["Atrial fibrillation artificially clamps PPV below 3% regardless of preload status","PPV is valid only in patients receiving continuous veno-venous hemodialysis","PPV only exceeds 13% when cardiac output is \u003e 10 L/min","PPV/SVV require controlled positive-pressure ventilation (no spontaneous efforts), tidal volume \u003e= 8 mL/kg PBW, and regular sinus rhythm; arrhythmias and spontaneous breathing create false-positive beat-to-beat pulse pressure swings"],"a":3,"e":"Dynamic indices (PPV, SVV \u003e 12-13%) rely on cyclic, uniform changes in intrathoracic pressure induced by controlled mechanical breaths to test Frank-Starling preload dependence. Irregular R-R intervals in atrial fibrillation vary ventricular filling independently of respiration, and variable spontaneous efforts distort pleural pressure swings, invalidating PPV/SVV (requiring a Passive Leg Raise maneuver instead).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00706","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"ICU Renal Replacement Therapy","topic":"Regional Citrate Anticoagulation (RCA)","q":"A patient with ischemic hepatitis (AST/ALT \u003e 3,000 U/L) and septic AKI is undergoing CVVHDF with Regional Citrate Anticoagulation (RCA). After 12 hours, labs show systemic ionized calcium (iCa2+) 0.86 mmol/L, total serum calcium 2.75 mmol/L (11.0 mg/dL), total-to-ionized calcium ratio = 3.2 (\u003e 2.5), and worsening high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. What is occurring and why?","o":["Systemic citrate accumulation (citrate toxicity) because severe hepatic failure impairs conversion of citrate to bicarbonate","Inadequate circuit citrate dose causing dialyzer clotting","Excessive post-filter calcium chloride infusion causing hypercalcemic alkalosis","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia cross-reacting with trisodium citrate"],"a":0,"e":"Normally, citrate infused into the CRRT circuit is partially cleared across the hemofilter and the remainder is metabolized by the liver, kidney, and skeletal muscle into bicarbonate (1 mmol citrate -\u003e 3 mmol HCO3-). In severe liver failure or profound shock, un-metabolized citrate accumulates in systemic blood, binding ionized calcium (low systemic iCa2+ with rising total calcium -\u003e total/iCa2+ ratio \u003e 2.5) and causing high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00707","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"Invasive Cardiothoracic Monitoring","topic":"PCWP Measurement \u0026 Safety","q":"While measuring Pulmonary Capillary Wedge Pressure (PCWP) via a Swan-Ganz catheter in a cardiogenic shock patient, the critical care technologist inflates the balloon with 1.5 mL of air, records the end-expiratory PCWP waveform (18 mmHg), and notices that after releasing the syringe, the monitor trace remains flat/wedged rather than snapping back to a pulsatile pulmonary artery waveform. What is the immediate risk and mandatory corrective action?","o":["Inflate with an extra 1.5 mL air to push the catheter farther distally","Risk of pulmonary infarction or catastrophic pulmonary artery rupture; immediately confirm balloon deflation, pull the catheter back 2-3 cm until a normal phasic PA waveform returns, and notify the intensivist","Flush the distal lumen forcefully at 300 mmHg to clear the wedge trace","Leave the balloon inflated continuously to monitor continuous left atrial pressure"],"a":1,"e":"A persistent \u0027spontaneously wedged\u0027 trace after balloon deflation indicates distal catheter tip migration into a small pulmonary arteriole. Leaving the catheter wedged or overinflating/flushing forcefully risks pulmonary infarction or fatal pulmonary artery rupture (massive hemoptysis). The balloon must be passively deflated (never aspirate forcefully against resistance) and the catheter withdrawn slightly until crisp PA systolic/diastolic/dicrotic waveforms reappear.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00708","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"Shock \u0026 Tissue Perfusion Physiology","topic":"MAP \u0026 Oxygen Extraction Ratio (O2ER)","q":"A patient in cardiogenic shock has an arterial blood pressure of 86/50 mmHg, arterial oxygen saturation (SaO2) of 98%, and mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2) of 50%. Neglecting dissolved O2, what are the Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) and Oxygen Extraction Ratio [O2ER = (SaO2 - SvO2) / SaO2 x 100]?","o":["MAP = 58 mmHg; O2ER = 50.0% (hyperdynamic sepsis)","MAP = 68 mmHg; O2ER = 25.0% (normal)","MAP = 62 mmHg; O2ER = 49.0% (markedly elevated, indicating inadequate oxygen delivery relative to demand)","MAP = 74 mmHg; O2ER = 15.0% (cyanide toxicity)"],"a":2,"e":"MAP = (SBP + 2 x DBP) / 3 = (86 + 100) / 3 = 186 / 3 = 62 mmHg (below the standard \u003e= 65 mmHg resuscitation goal). Neglecting negligible dissolved O2, O2ER = [(SaO2 - SvO2) / SaO2] x 100 = [(98 - 50) / 98] x 100 = (48 / 98) x 100 = 49.0%. Normal O2ER is 25-30%; an O2ER of 49% confirms heightened tissue O2 extraction compensating for critically low cardiac output/DO2.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00709","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) Management","topic":"Prone Positioning (PROSEVA Protocol)","q":"A mechanically ventilated ICU patient with severe pneumonia-induced ARDS has a PaO2/FiO2 ratio of 110 mmHg despite 6 mL/kg PBW tidal volume and PEEP 14 cmH2O. Per the PROSEVA trial and Surviving Sepsis / ATS ARDS guidelines, what prone-positioning duration is recommended, and how does it reduce mortality?","o":["Prone positioning is contraindicated whenever PEEP exceeds 10 cmH2O","Prone for 4 hours only when PaO2/FiO2 \u003c 50 mmHg; increases cardiac compression against sternum","Prone for 2 hours daily; acts solely by draining upper airway secretions","Early prone positioning for \u003e= 16 consecutive hours per day; homogenizes dorsal-ventral pleural pressure gradients, recruits collapsed dorsal alveoli (reducing V/Q mismatch and ventilator-induced lung injury), while perfusion remains predominantly dorsal"],"a":3,"e":"In severe ARDS (PaO2/FiO2 \u003c 150 mmHg), early prone ventilation for \u003e= 16 hours/day significantly lowers mortality (PROSEVA). Turning the patient prone relieves cardiac and mediastinal weight from dorsal lung parenchyma, evens out ventral-to-dorsal transpulmonary pressure gradients for uniform alveolar recruitment, and matches ventilation to dorsal pulmonary perfusion (which remains predominantly dorsal regardless of posture).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00710","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"Sepsis \u0026 Shock Resuscitation","topic":"1-Hour Sepsis Bundle \u0026 Vasopressor Selection","q":"A 63-year-old patient with urosepsis remains hypotensive (BP 76/42 mmHg, MAP 53 mmHg, serum lactate 4.8 mmol/L) despite receiving 30 mL/kg of balanced crystalloid resuscitation. Per Surviving Sepsis Campaign (SSC) guidelines, which vasopressor is the mandatory FIRST-LINE agent, what is the target MAP, and within what timeframe must broad-spectrum IV antimicrobials be initiated?","o":["Norepinephrine; target MAP \u003e= 65 mmHg; blood cultures followed immediately by broad-spectrum IV antimicrobials within 1 hour","Phenylephrine; target MAP \u003e= 55 mmHg; antibiotics after 24-hour culture results","Dopamine; target MAP \u003e= 85 mmHg; antibiotics within 6 hours","Dobutamine monotherapy; target MAP \u003e= 90 mmHg; antibiotics within 12 hours"],"a":0,"e":"Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines recommend Norepinephrine (strong recommendation) as first-line vasopressor for septic shock, titrated to MAP \u003e= 65 mmHg (adding vasopressin up to 0.03 U/min or epinephrine if refractory). The SSC 1-Hour Bundle requires measuring lactate, obtaining blood cultures prior to antibiotics, administering broad-spectrum IV antimicrobials within 1 hour, and giving \u003e= 30 mL/kg crystalloid for hypotension or lactate \u003e= 4 mmol/L.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00711","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"ICU Neuro-Care, Analgesia \u0026 Delirium","topic":"ICU Delirium \u0026 Sedation Titration","q":"A mechanically ventilated ICU patient (day 4) develops fluctuating inattention, pulling at lines, Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale (RASS) score +2, and tests positive on CAM-ICU. Per SCCM PADIS guidelines, which sedation strategy reduces delirium duration and mechanical ventilation days compared with continuous midazolam infusion?","o":["Administer routine prophylactic haloperidol even in asymptomatic RASS 0 patients","Optimize analgesia first, transition to a non-benzodiazepine sedative (dexmedetomidine or propofol) titrated to light sedation (RASS 0 to -2), perform daily spontaneous awakening/breathing trials, and implement early mobilization (ABCDEF bundle)","Escalate continuous IV midazolam/lorazepam to achieve deep sedation (RASS -5)","Apply rigid 4-point physical restraints indefinitely without assessing pain"],"a":1,"e":"SCCM PADIS guidelines strongly favor analgesia-first sedation and non-benzodiazepine agents (dexmedetomidine or propofol) targeting light sedation (RASS 0 to -2) alongside the ABCDEF bundle. Benzodiazepines (midazolam, lorazepam) are independent risk factors for prolonged ICU delirium, delayed extubation, and long-term cognitive impairment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00712","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"Critical Care Toxicology \u0026 Metabolic Crises","topic":"Serum Osmolal Gap Calculation","q":"An unconscious ICU patient with severe high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis (pH 7.05) and envelope-shaped calcium oxalate crystals in urine has measured serum osmolality = 340 mOsm/kg, Na+ = 140 mEq/L, glucose = 90 mg/dL, and BUN = 14 mg/dL. Using Calculated Osmolality = 2(Na+) + (Glucose/18) + (BUN/2.8), what is the Osmolal Gap, and what emergency antidote blocks toxic metabolite formation?","o":["Osmolal gap = 290 mOsm/kg; flumazenil","Osmolal gap = 5 mOsm/kg; sodium polystyrene sulfonate","Osmolal gap = 50 mOsm/kg (\u003e 10 elevated); Fomepizole (alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor) plus hemodialysis","Osmolal gap = 10 mOsm/kg; N-acetylcysteine"],"a":2,"e":"Calculated osmolality = 2(140) + (90 / 18) + (14 / 2.8) = 280 + 5 + 5 = 290 mOsm/kg. Osmolal gap = Measured - Calculated = 340 - 290 = 50 mOsm/kg (normal \u003c 10). A markedly elevated osmolal gap alongside severe high-AG acidosis and calcium oxalate crystalluria points to ethylene glycol (or methanol) toxicity; immediate fomepizole (inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase to halt glycolic/oxalic or formic acid generation) and hemodialysis are lifesaving.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00713","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"Neuro-Critical Care","topic":"CPP Calculation \u0026 Osmotherapy","q":"A severe traumatic brain injury patient in the neuro-ICU has an external ventricular drain measuring ICP = 28 mmHg (treatment threshold \u003e= 22 mmHg) and radial arterial MAP = 80 mmHg. What is the Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP = MAP - ICP), and what Tier-1 bedside measures lower ICP without risking cerebral ischemia?","o":["CPP = 108 mmHg; lay head flat and hyperventilate aggressively to PaCO2 20 mmHg","CPP = 52 mmHg; infuse 5% dextrose in water (D5W) bolus rapidly","CPP = 28 mmHg; apply tight circumferential cervical collar","CPP = 52 mmHg (below target 60-70 mmHg); elevate head of bed 30 degrees with midline neutral neck, maintain PaCO2 35-38 mmHg (avoiding prolonged PaCO2 \u003c 30 mmHg), optimize sedation/analgesia, and administer IV mannitol or 3% hypertonic saline alongside vasopressor support for CPP"],"a":3,"e":"Cerebral Perfusion Pressure CPP = MAP - ICP = 80 - 28 = 52 mmHg (below Brain Trauma Foundation target of 60-70 mmHg). Tier-1 interventions include 30-degree head elevation with neutral head alignment (facilitating jugular venous drainage without tight collars), maintaining normocapnia (35-38 mmHg; prolonged hyperventilation \u003c 30 mmHg causes severe cerebral vasoconstriction and ischemia!), osmotherapy (mannitol 0.25-1 g/kg or hypertonic saline), and norepinephrine to restore CPP.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00714","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"ICU Vascular Access \u0026 Procedural Assistance","topic":"Ultrasound Guidance \u0026 Optimal Tip Position","q":"Following ultrasound-guided right internal jugular central venous catheterization, where should the catheter tip ideally sit on a post-procedure AP chest radiograph, and why is positioning the tip deeper inside the right ventricle or higher in the brachiocephalic vein hazardous?","o":["Mid-right ventricle; high brachiocephalic placement prevents all thrombosis","At the cavoatrial junction (lower third of Superior Vena Cava, roughly 2 cm below the tracheal carina); deep RA/RV tip risks arrhythmias and myocardial perforation/tamponade, whereas high brachiocephalic tip increases venous thrombosis and vessel erosion","Inside the left subclavian artery; lowers CVP variability","Above the clavicle in the mid-internal jugular vein"],"a":1,"e":"The optimal CVC tip lies parallel to the vessel wall at the lower SVC / cavoatrial junction (~2 cm below the carina, which marks the pericardial reflection). Advancing too deeply into the right atrium or ventricle irritates the myocardium (ectopy/VT) and risks cardiac perforation/tamponade, whereas leaving the tip high in the brachiocephalic or upper SVC against the wall promotes catheter-related thrombosis and vascular erosion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00715","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"Critical Care Hematology \u0026 Coagulation","topic":"Goal-Directed Blood Product Therapy","q":"A trauma ICU patient with ongoing diffuse surgical bleeding has a point-of-care Thromboelastography (TEG) tracing showing: markedly prolonged R-time (14 min), reduced Maximum Amplitude (MA = 36 mm), and normal LY30 (1%). Which targeted blood component therapy addresses the prolonged R-time and reduced MA respectively?","o":["Protamine for prolonged R-time; Tranexamic acid for low MA","Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP) to replace clotting factors for prolonged R-time; Platelet transfusion (and/or cryoprecipitate/fibrinogen if alpha-angle is also low) to restore clot strength for low MA","Packed RBCs alone for both R-time and MA","IV Vitamin K bolus for LY30 = 1%"],"a":1,"e":"In TEG: R-time (reaction time to initial fibrin formation) reflects soluble coagulation factor activity -\u003e prolonged R requires FFP (or heparin reversal if heparinized). Alpha-angle reflects fibrin cross-linking kinetics -\u003e low angle indicates fibrinogen deficiency (cryoprecipitate). Maximum Amplitude (MA) reflects platelet count/function (~80%) + fibrinogen (~20%) -\u003e low MA indicates platelet transfusion. Normal LY30 (\u003c 3%) excludes hyperfibrinolysis (which would call for tranexamic acid).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00716","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"Critical Care Organ Dysfunction Monitoring","topic":"Intra-Abdominal Pressure (IAP) Measurement","q":"A trauma ICU patient with severe hemorrhagic shock who received massive resuscitation develops oliguria (\u003c 15 mL/hr), rising peak airway pressures, and a tense, distended abdomen. Measuring Intra-Abdominal Pressure (IAP) via the Foley bladder catheter per WSACS guidelines (instilling \u003c= 25 mL sterile saline, zeroed at the mid-axillary line at end-expiration in the supine position) yields IAP = 24 mmHg. What defines Abdominal Compartment Syndrome (ACS) and its management?","o":["Sustained IAP \u003e 20 mmHg associated with new organ dysfunction/failure (here oliguria and elevated airway pressure); manage with NG/rectal decompression, fluid minimization, sedation/neuromuscular blockade, and emergent surgical decompressive laparotomy if refractory","Normal physiological post-resuscitation pressure requiring no intervention","IAP \u003e= 5 mmHg; infuse additional 4 L crystalloids","Instill 500 mL saline into the bladder and zero at the patient\u0027s forehead"],"a":0,"e":"Per WSACS consensus definitions, Intra-Abdominal Hypertension (IAH) is sustained IAP \u003e= 12 mmHg, while Abdominal Compartment Syndrome (ACS) is sustained IAP \u003e 20 mmHg accompanied by new organ failure (renal hypoperfusion, elevated intrathoracic/peak pressures, reduced venous return). Bladder measurement requires supine positioning, \u003c= 25 mL saline instillation (excessive volume artificially elevates detrusor pressure!), zeroing at mid-axillary line, and end-expiratory reading.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00717","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"ICU Airway Liberation \u0026 Extubation Safety","topic":"Cuff-Leak Test","q":"Prior to extubating an ICU patient who has been intubated for 9 days following inhalation injury, the team performs a quantitative Cuff-Leak Test on assist-control volume ventilation (set Vt = 500 mL; expired Vt with cuff deflated = 460 mL, giving a leak of only 40 mL [\u003c 110 mL or \u003c 10%]). What does this failed cuff-leak test indicate, and what ATS/CHEST guideline intervention reduces reintubation risk?","o":["Complete absence of laryngeal swelling; extubate immediately without steroids","High risk of post-extubation laryngeal edema and upper-airway stridor; delay extubation briefly and administer systemic IV corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone) at least 4-12 hours prior to a repeat extubation attempt","Ventilator circuit leak requiring immediate tracheostomy in all patients","Severe bronchospasm; inflate the ETT cuff to 60 cmH2O and extubate"],"a":1,"e":"Normally, deflating the ETT cuff allows inspired tidal volume to escape upward around the tube, dropping expired Vt measured by the ventilator by \u003e 110-130 mL (or \u003e 10-15%). A minimal cuff leak (\u003c 110 mL) warns of subglottic/laryngeal mucosal edema compressing the ETT wall. Per ATS/CHEST liberation guidelines, administering systemic corticosteroids (e.g., IV methylprednisolone 20-40 mg q4-6h) for \u003e= 4-12 hours significantly lowers post-extubation stridor and reintubation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00718","course":"B.Sc Critical Care Technology","subject":"ICU Fluid \u0026 Electrolyte Therapy","topic":"Free Water Deficit Calculation \u0026 Safe Correction Rate","q":"A 60 kg elderly male ICU patient (Total Body Water factor = 0.6) develops chronic hypernatremia with serum Na+ = 168 mEq/L due to osmotic diuresis and inadequate free-water intake. Using Free Water Deficit = TBW x [(Serum Na+ / 140) - 1], what is his estimated Free Water Deficit, and why must serum Na+ NOT be lowered faster than 10-12 mEq/L over 24 hours?","o":["1.8 liters; hypernatremia should be normalized to 140 mEq/L within 2 hours","12.0 liters; rapid lowering causes acute hyperkalemia","7.2 liters; rapid lowering of chronic hypernatremia drives water into brain cells containing accumulated organic osmolytes, causing fatal CEREBRAL EDEMA and seizures","3.6 liters; rapid lowering causes central pontine myelinolysis"],"a":2,"e":"Total Body Water (TBW) = 60 kg x 0.6 = 36 L (or 0.5 in elderly males/females depending on lean mass; using given factor 0.6 -\u003e 36 L). Free Water Deficit = 36 x [(168 / 140) - 1] = 36 x (1.20 - 1) = 36 x 0.20 = 7.2 liters. In chronic hypernatremia (\u003e 48 h), neurons generate intracellular idiogenic osmoles to maintain volume; lowering serum Na+ faster than 0.5 mEq/L/hr (\u003c= 10-12 mEq/L/24 h) causes osmotic water influx into neurons, leading to cerebral edema and herniation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00719","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Haemodialysis Principles \u0026 Complications","topic":"AAMI Water Standards \u0026 Chloramine Toxicity","q":"Three patients simultaneously undergoing haemodialysis in a dialysis unit develop acute dyspnoea, chest tightness, dark brown blood in the venous line, and acute haemolysis with methaemoglobinaemia. Failure of which component of the haemodialysis water treatment plant is MOST likely responsible?","o":["UV irradiator bulb failure causing mild biofilm growth over 3 months","Deioniser resin exhaustion causing trace magnesium elevation","Water softener resin over-regeneration without salt flushing","Granular Activated Carbon (GAC) tanks failing to remove chloramines/chlorine prior to the Reverse Osmosis (RO) membrane"],"a":3,"e":"Municipal water supplies use chlorine/chloramines as disinfectants. Because chloramines pass readily across Reverse Osmosis (RO) membranes, dual Granular Activated Carbon (GAC) beds in series (tested regularly with DPD chlorine/chloramine strips) are mandatory upstream of the RO unit. GAC exhaustion allows chloramines into dialysate, causing acute oxidative red-cell injury, methaemoglobinaemia, and massive haemolysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00720","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Principles of Hemodialysis","topic":"Urea Kinetic Modeling","q":"A patient on thrice-weekly maintenance hemodialysis has a pre-dialysis BUN of 84 mg/dL. At the end of a 4-hour session, the dialysis technologist reduces the blood pump speed to 100 mL/min for 15 seconds (slow-flow technique) before drawing the post-dialysis sample, which yields a BUN of 21 mg/dL. What is the patient\u0027s Urea Reduction Ratio (URR), and why is the slow-flow step necessary?","o":["75%; to eliminate vascular access recirculation artifact before sampling","65%; to prevent cardiopulmonary recirculation","25%; to prevent dialyzer clotting during blood return","80%; to accelerate urea rebound from skeletal muscle"],"a":0,"e":"URR = [(Pre-BUN - Post-BUN) / Pre-BUN] x 100 = [(84 - 21) / 84] x 100 = (63 / 84) x 100 = 75% (meeting KDOQI target \u003e= 65%). Slowing the blood pump to 50-100 mL/min for 15 seconds clears dialyzed blood from the arterial needle/limb so that access recirculation does not artificially dilute the post-BUN and falsely inflate URR/Kt/V.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00721","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Water Treatment \u0026 Engineering","topic":"AAMI / ISO 13959 Standards","q":"During morning water-room checks prior to initiating hemodialysis, the total chlorine test strip taken after the primary and secondary Granular Activated Carbon (GAC) tanks reads 0.3 mg/L (ppm). Why must hemodialysis NOT be started even though the Reverse Osmosis (RO) membrane sits downstream of the GAC tanks?","o":["Chloramines will permanently oxidize the dual-media sand sediment filter upstream","RO membranes cannot reject chloramines, which cause acute oxidative hemolytic anemia in patients","Chloramines trigger severe metabolic alkalosis by reacting with bicarbonate concentrate","AAMI/ISO 13959 permits up to 0.5 mg/L total chlorine in dialysate product water"],"a":1,"e":"Chloramines (monochloramine) are small, uncharged molecules that pass freely across reverse osmosis membranes. Under AAMI / ISO 13959 standards, total chlorine/chloramine in dialysis product water must remain \u003c 0.1 mg/L (ppm). Breakthrough above 0.1 mg/L causes oxidative denaturation of hemoglobin (Heinz bodies), methemoglobinemia, and catastrophic acute hemolysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00722","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Intradialytic Complications \u0026 Emergency Management","topic":"Venous Air Embolism","q":"Midway through a hemodialysis session using an internal jugular catheter, a loose arterial luer-lock connection aspirates a large bolus of air past a bypassed air detector. The patient suddenly cries out, develops chest pain, dyspnea, cyanosis, and a loud \u0027mill-wheel\u0027 precordial murmur. What is the immediate sequence of actions by the dialysis technologist?","o":["Increase ultrafiltration rate to draw air back across the dialyzer membrane and place the patient supine","Clamp only the arterial line, keep the blood pump running slowly, and place the patient in right lateral decubitus","Clamp the venous blood line, stop the blood pump, place the patient in left lateral decubitus with head down (Trendelenburg), and administer 100% oxygen","Flush the venous line with 200 mL saline and place the patient upright in high-Fowler position"],"a":2,"e":"Clamping the venous line and stopping the blood pump immediately prevents further air entry into the patient. Placing the patient in left lateral decubitus with Trendelenburg tilt (Durant maneuver) traps the air bubble in the right atrium/apex of the right ventricle away from the pulmonary outflow tract, while 100% oxygen promotes nitrogen washout and reduces bubble size.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00723","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Vascular Access \u0026 Cannulation","topic":"AVF Physical Examination","q":"During pre-cannulation physical examination of a mature brachiocephalic AV fistula, the dialysis technologist notes a tense, hyperpulsatile fistula segment that fails to collapse when the patient raises the arm above heart level, a high-pitched systolic-only bruit over the mid-vein, rising dynamic venous pressures during HD, and prolonged post-dialysis needle-site bleeding. What vascular access complication is indicated?","o":["Juxta-anastomotic arterial inflow stenosis","Normal high-flow mature AV fistula","Dialysis access steal syndrome without stenosis","Proximal venous outflow stenosis"],"a":3,"e":"In a normal AVF, raising the arm above the heart drains venous blood and collapses the fistula, and the thrill/bruit is soft and continuous (systolic + diastolic). Proximal venous outflow stenosis obstructs drainage, causing hyperpulsatility, failure to collapse on arm elevation, loss of the diastolic bruit component (high-pitched systolic bruit), high venous pressures, and prolonged hemostasis time.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00724","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Acute Dialysis \u0026 Neurological Complications","topic":"Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome (DDS)","q":"A 42-year-old man newly diagnosed with end-stage kidney disease (pre-dialysis BUN 175 mg/dL) undergoes his first hemodialysis session using a large high-flux dialyzer at Qb 380 mL/min for 4 hours. At hour 3, he develops severe headache, nausea, agitation, visual blurring, and generalized tonic-clonic seizures. What is the underlying mechanism and standard prevention strategy for initial HD sessions?","o":["Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome (cerebral edema due to delayed urea clearance across the blood-brain barrier); initiate gentle first sessions (short 2-hour duration, low Qb ~200 mL/min, smaller surface-area dialyzer)","Hypernatremic brain shrinkage; use low-sodium dialysate (125 mEq/L) and higher blood flow","Heparin-induced intracranial hemorrhage; switch immediately to peritoneal dialysis without stopping HD","Aluminium encephalopathy; increase dialysate calcium to 3.5 mEq/L"],"a":0,"e":"Rapid clearance of plasma urea during aggressive initial hemodialysis creates an osmotic gradient between brain parenchyma and plasma (\u0027reverse urea effect\u0027 alongside intracellular idiogenic osmoles), drawing water across the blood-brain barrier and causing acute cerebral edema (DDS). Prevention relies on targeting modest initial urea reduction (~30-40%) via short 2-hour sessions, low Qb (150-200 mL/min), and smaller dialyzers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00725","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Hemodialysis Prescription \u0026 Fluid Balance","topic":"Ultrafiltration Rate (UFR) Calculation","q":"A hemodialysis patient with an estimated dry weight of 60.0 kg presents with a pre-dialysis weight of 62.7 kg. During the 4-hour dialysis session, the patient will receive 300 mL of combined IV saline prime/rinseback and oral fluids. What total ultrafiltration (UF) volume and hourly UF rate (UFR) are required to return the patient to dry weight, and what is the UFR indexed to body weight?","o":["3,600 mL total; 900 mL/hr (15.0 mL/kg/hr)","3,000 mL total; 750 mL/hr (12.5 mL/kg/hr)","2,700 mL total; 675 mL/hr (11.25 mL/kg/hr)","2,400 mL total; 600 mL/hr (10.0 mL/kg/hr)"],"a":1,"e":"Interdialytic weight gain = 62.7 - 60.0 = 2.7 kg (2,700 mL). Adding the 300 mL intradialytic fluid administration gives Total UF Target = 2,700 + 300 = 3,000 mL. Over 4 hours, hourly UFR = 3,000 / 4 = 750 mL/hr. Indexed to 60 kg dry weight: 750 / 60 = 12.5 mL/kg/hr (remaining below the high-risk \u003e 13 mL/kg/hr threshold associated with myocardial stunning).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00726","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Peritoneal Dialysis (PD)","topic":"PD-Associated Peritonitis","q":"A patient on Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis (CAPD) presents with diffuse abdominal pain and a cloudy drained effluent bag after a 4-hour dwell. Per International Society for Peritoneal Dialysis (ISPD) guidelines, which effluent cell count finding confirms PD peritonitis, and what is the preferred route of empiric antibiotic delivery?","o":["Effluent WBC \u003e 10/uL with \u003e 80% lymphocytes; oral ciprofloxacin only","Effluent WBC \u003e 100/uL with \u003e 50% polymorphonuclear neutrophils (PMNs); intraperitoneal (IP) administration covering both Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms","Effluent RBC \u003e 500/uL with sterile fluid; immediate catheter removal before any antibiotics","Effluent protein \u003e 1 g/dL with normal WBC; IV vancomycin monotherapy"],"a":1,"e":"ISPD diagnostic criteria for PD peritonitis require \u003e= 2 of 3 features: (1) abdominal pain and/or cloudy effluent, (2) dialysis effluent WBC \u003e 100 cells/uL (0.1 x 10^9/L) with \u003e 50% PMNs after \u003e= 2-hour dwell, and (3) positive effluent culture. Empiric therapy is administered intraperitoneally (achieving high local peritoneal concentrations) covering Gram-positive (vancomycin/cefazolin) and Gram-negative (ceftazidime/cefepime/aminoglycoside) pathogens.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00727","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialyzer Biocompatibility \u0026 Reactions","topic":"Type A (Anaphylactic) First-Use \u0026 AN69-ACEi Reaction","q":"Four minutes after initiating hemodialysis with a new ethylene-oxide-sterilized dialyzer (in a patient taking enalapril), the patient develops acute bronchospasm, laryngeal stridor, urticaria, and hypotension (BP 68/40 mmHg). Which reaction is occurring, and why must the dialysis technologist NOT rinse back the blood in the extracorporeal circuit?","o":["Type B nonspecific dialyzer reaction; rinse back blood rapidly with 500 mL normal saline","Type A (anaphylactic) dialyzer reaction (mediated by IgE to residual EtO or bradykinin accumulation with AN69/ACEi); stop pump and clamp BOTH lines WITHOUT returning blood because returning circuit blood delivers additional allergen/bradykinin","Pyrogenic endotoxin reaction; reduce dialysate temperature to 34 C and continue dialysis","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia; administer protamine and continue HD"],"a":1,"e":"Type A dialyzer reactions occur within the first 5-20 minutes of dialysis and present as severe IgE-mediated anaphylaxis (residual ethylene oxide) or bradykinin-mediated angioedema/shock (negatively charged AN69 membranes activating Factor XII/kallikrein in patients on ACE inhibitors). Emergency protocol: immediately stop the blood pump, clamp arterial and venous lines, discard circuit blood WITHOUT returning it (to prevent further allergen bolus), and administer epinephrine, antihistamines, and corticosteroids.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00728","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Hemodialysis Machines \u0026 Proportioning Safety","topic":"Conductivity \u0026 Osmolality Failures","q":"If a hemodialysis machine\u0027s acid/bicarbonate concentrate proportioning pump fails, delivering severely hypotonic dialysate (conductivity \u003c 11.5 mS/cm) across a bypassed conductivity monitor, what catastrophic intradialytic complication develops rapidly in the patient?","o":["Hypernatremic cellular dehydration and hyperosmolality","Instantaneous clotting of all hollow fibers in the dialyzer","Massive acute hypo-osmolar hemolysis (\u0027cherry-red\u0027 translucent port-wine blood in the venous tubing, back/chest pain, and life-threatening hyperkalemia)","Severe metabolic alkalosis with hypocalcemic tetany only"],"a":2,"e":"Conductivity monitors primarily reflect total dialysate sodium/electrolyte concentration (normal 13.5-14.5 mS/cm). Severely hypotonic dialysate drives water rapidly across RBC membranes inside the dialyzer, causing osmotic swelling and massive hemolysis—producing translucent port-wine/cherry-red venous blood, acute flank/chest pain, and lethal extracellular potassium release (requiring immediate cessation WITHOUT returning hemolyzed blood!).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00729","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialyzer Engineering \u0026 Membrane Physics","topic":"High-Flux vs Low-Flux Dialyzers","q":"Why are blood and dialysate directed in COUNTERCURRENT flow inside a hollow-fiber dialyzer, and what additional water-purity safeguard becomes critical when switching a patient from a low-flux to a high-flux (Kuf \u003e= 20 mL/hr/mmHg, beta-2-microglobulin sieving coefficient \u003e 0.6) dialyzer?","o":["Countercurrent flow prevents dialyzer clotting; high-flux dialyzers require no RO water purification","Concurrent flow yields higher urea clearance; high-flux dialyzers reject all water flux","Countercurrent flow increases venous air bubble formation deliberately","Countercurrent flow maintains the highest continuous transmembrane concentration gradient from inlet to outlet; high-flux large pores allow middle-molecule clearance (beta-2-microglobulin) but increase risk of endotoxin \u0027backfiltration\u0027 from dialysate into blood, necessitating ultrapure dialysate"],"a":3,"e":"Countercurrent flow ensures fresh dialysate meets partially cleared blood at the venous end while concentrated blood meets used dialysate at the arterial end, maximizing diffusive urea clearance across the entire fiber length. High-flux membranes possess high hydraulic permeability (Kuf \u003e= 20) and large pores clearing beta-2-microglobulin, which creates distal pressure zones where dialysate moves backward into blood (backfiltration)—mandating ultrapure dialysate (\u003c 0.1 CFU/mL, endotoxin \u003c 0.03 EU/mL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00730","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Vascular Access Function \u0026 Surveillance","topic":"Two-Needle Urea-Based Recirculation","q":"Suspecting AV fistula recirculation due to poor dialysis adequacy, the dialysis technologist performs a two-needle urea recirculation study. Simultaneous samples yield Arterial line BUN (A) = 40 mg/dL, Venous line BUN (V) = 10 mg/dL, and Systemic arterial BUN after stopping pump/slow-flow (S) = 50 mg/dL. Using the formula [%R = (S - A) / (S - V) x 100], what is the access recirculation percentage?","o":["25%","10%","20%","40%"],"a":0,"e":"Access Recirculation (%) = [(S - A) / (S - V)] x 100 = [(50 - 40) / (50 - 10)] x 100 = (10 / 40) x 100 = 25%. Normally, non-recirculating AV fistulas have ~0% access recirculation; values \u003e 10% indicate retrograde mixing of dialyzed venous return directly back into the arterial needle (commonly from venous outflow stenosis or closely placed/reversed needles), severely reducing delivered Kt/V.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00731","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Intradialytic Emergencies \u0026 Symptom Management","topic":"Intradialytic Hypotension (IDH)","q":"At hour 3 of a 4-hour hemodialysis session (UFR 900 mL/hr), a diabetic patient complains of yawning, lightheadedness, nausea, and painful calf cramps; BP has dropped from 136/82 to 76/46 mmHg. What is the immediate priority intervention by the dialysis technologist?","o":["Increase ultrafiltration rate to 1,200 mL/hr and raise dialysate temperature to 38.5 C","Place the patient in Trendelenburg / flat supine position, temporarily turn OFF ultrafiltration (UF minimum/bypass), administer a 100-250 mL 0.9% normal saline IV bolus, and notify the physician","Immediately clamp and discard the entire extracorporeal blood circuit without fluid bolus","Administer IV furosemide 80 mg and keep the patient seated upright"],"a":1,"e":"Intradialytic hypotension (IDH) occurs when ultrafiltration rate outpaces plasma refilling from the interstitium in patients with impaired autonomic/cardiac reserve. Immediate rescue: stop/minimize UF (halting further intravascular volume loss), position supine/Trendelenburg to restore cerebral venous return, infuse a 100-250 mL isotonic 0.9% saline bolus, and consider preventive cool dialysate (35.0-35.5 C), dry weight reassessment, and avoiding intradialytic meals.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00732","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialyzer Reprocessing \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Total Cell Volume (TCV) Testing","q":"In a hemodialysis center performing automated dialyzer reprocessing per AAMI RD47 standards, a hollow-fiber dialyzer had an initial baseline priming/Total Cell Volume (TCV) of 100 mL when new. At what minimum remaining TCV MUST the dialyzer be permanently discarded, and what does a drop below this threshold signify?","o":["Below 30 mL (\u003c 30%); signifies high ultrafiltration coefficient","Below 98 mL (\u003c 98%); indicates normal membrane hydration","Below 80 mL (\u003c 80% of baseline TCV); signifies \u003e= 20% clotted/obstructed hollow fibers and unacceptable loss of solute clearance surface area","Below 50 mL (\u003c 50%); indicates excessive dialysate calcium"],"a":2,"e":"Under AAMI/KDOQI dialyzer reuse standards, Total Cell Volume (fiber bundle volume) correlates directly with functional surface area. When TCV falls below 80% of the initial baseline volume (i.e., \u003e 20% fiber clotting/loss), urea clearance drops significantly (\u003e ~10% drop in KoA) and the dialyzer must be discarded—alongside mandatory pressure-leak failure rejection and germicide residual checks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00733","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysate Electrolyte Management","topic":"Zero-Potassium Dialysate Hazards","q":"A chronic hemodialysis patient taking digoxin for atrial fibrillation presents with pre-dialysis serum potassium of 6.4 mEq/L. Why is using a 1.0-2.0 mEq/L potassium dialysate strongly preferred over a 0 mEq/L (potassium-free) dialysate?","o":["0 mEq/L dialysate causes acute hyperkalemia during dialysis","Digoxin prevents potassium from crossing cuprophane or polysulfone membranes","2.0 mEq/L dialysate prevents any potassium removal from patients with K+ \u003e 5.0 mEq/L","0 mEq/L dialysate creates an extreme blood-to-dialysate gradient causing precipitous serum K+ drop, myocardial repolarization instability, and lethal ventricular arrhythmias—especially precipitating digoxin toxicity"],"a":3,"e":"Zero-K+ dialysate produces an abrupt extracellular potassium depletion faster than intracellular K+ equilibration, steepening the transmembrane resting potential gradient, prolonging QTc, and triggering sudden cardiac death/VF—a risk amplified in digitalized patients because hypokalemia enhances digoxin binding to Na+/K+-ATPase. Using a 1.0-2.0 mEq/L K+ bath (or step-down profiling) safely lowers serum K+ without sudden arrhythmogenic shock.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00734","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Peritoneal Dialysis Prescription \u0026 Kinetics","topic":"High (Fast) Peritoneal Transporter Management","q":"A Peritoneal Equilibration Test (PET) shows a 4-hour dialysate-to-plasma (D/P) creatinine ratio of 0.88 (High/Fast transporter) with markedly reduced net ultrafiltration on standard long-dwell CAPD exchanges. What explains the ultrafiltration failure, and how should the PD regimen be modified?","o":["Hyperpermeable capillaries rapidly absorb dialysate glucose across the peritoneum, dissipating the osmotic gradient early in the dwell; switch to short rapid dwells via Automated Peritoneal Dialysis (APD cycler) and/or use slow-absorbed Icodextrin for the long daytime dwell","Creatinine cannot cross the peritoneal membrane in High transporters","Patient has low peritoneal surface area requiring larger 5-liter single dwells","Peritoneal membrane is impermeable to glucose; switch to 12-hour dextrose dwells"],"a":0,"e":"In High/Fast transporters (4-hr D/P creatinine \u003e 0.81), solutes (urea, creatinine) equilibrate rapidly, AND small dextrose molecules are absorbed quickly from the peritoneal cavity into blood—collapsing the crystalloid osmotic gradient early so fluid actually reabsorbs back into the patient during long 6-8 hour CAPD dwells. Short rapid exchanges on an APD cycler (plus colloid-osmotic Icodextrin for the long dwell) restore ultrafiltration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00735","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Hemodialysis Machine Safety \u0026 Alarms","topic":"Blood Leak Detector Alarm","q":"Midway through a hemodialysis treatment, the machine\u0027s optical Blood Leak Detector triggers a major alarm, stopping the blood pump and clamping the venous line. Inspection of the clear dialysate drain tubing shows a faint pinkish tinge, and a hemoglobin/blood reagent dipstick of the dialysate effluent turns strongly positive. What has occurred, and what must the technologist do?","o":["Chloramine breakthrough; switch dialyzer into sequential ultrafiltration mode","True dialyzer hollow-fiber membrane rupture allowing blood into the dialysate; DO NOT override—clamp lines, discard/replace the dialyzer and bloodlines (do not return blood if contaminated by dialysate/hemolysis), and notify the physician","Normal hydroxocobalamin clearance requiring no action","Air detector false alarm; override the blood leak alarm and continue at higher TMP"],"a":1,"e":"A positive hemoglobin dipstick on the spent dialysate confirms true blood entry across ruptured hollow fibers (unlike false-positive optical triggers from air bubbles, high bilirubin, rifampicin, or hydroxocobalamin, which test dipstick-negative). Because non-sterile dialysate can enter blood across the tear or cause bacterial/endotoxin contamination, never override a confirmed blood leak; replace the dialyzer and tubing safely.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00736","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Acute \u0026 High-Risk Hemodialysis","topic":"Heparin-Free Hemodialysis","q":"A patient 6 hours post-craniotomy with acute-on-chronic renal failure and hyperkalemia requires urgent 3-hour hemodialysis, but systemic heparinization is strictly contraindicated due to intracranial hemorrhage risk. How does the dialysis technologist prevent dialyzer clotting during Heparin-Free Hemodialysis?","o":["Run low blood flow (Qb 100 mL/min) and avoid all saline flushes","Use a 0% calcium dialysate with continuous IV protamine infusion into the arterial line","Flush out initial heparin-primed circuit completely with normal saline prior to connecting the patient, maintain high blood flow (Qb 300-350 mL/min if tolerated), and administer periodic 100-150 mL 0.9% saline flushes every 15-30 minutes (adding total flush volume to the UF goal)","Administer full-dose enoxaparin (LMWH) intravenously instead"],"a":2,"e":"Heparin-free hemodialysis avoids systemic anticoagulation in neurosurgical, pericarditic, or actively bleeding patients. Pre-rinsing the dialyzer with heparinized saline coats membranes briefly and is completely flushed out before blood entry; maintaining brisk Qb (\u003e= 300 mL/min) minimizes fiber stasis, while scheduled 100-150 mL normal saline flushes clear early fibrin strands (with total saline volume factored into the ultrafiltration calculation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00737","course":"B.Sc Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Water Treatment Engineering","topic":"Water Hardness Limits \u0026 Cation-Exchange Regeneration","q":"In a dialysis water treatment plant, municipal water passes through a cation-exchange Water Softener BEFORE reaching the Reverse Osmosis (RO) membranes. Which ions are exchanged for sodium by the softener resin, what is the maximum allowable effluent hardness per AAMI standards, and what clinical syndrome occurs if hard water reaches the patient?","o":["Sodium exchanged for aluminum; limit \u003c 100 ppm; causes dialysis dementia","Bicarbonate exchanged for chloride; causes severe metabolic alkalosis","Chloramines exchanged for potassium; limit \u003c 50 grains/gal; causes acute methemoglobinemia","Calcium (Ca2+) and Magnesium (Mg2+) exchanged for Sodium (Na+) (regenerated with NaCl brine); effluent hardness must be \u003c 1 grain/gallon (\u003c 17.1 ppm as CaCO3) to prevent RO membrane scaling and \u0027Hard Water Syndrome\u0027 (hypercalcemia, hypertension, nausea/vomiting)"],"a":3,"e":"Water softeners protect expensive thin-film composite RO membranes from irreversible calcium/magnesium carbonate mineral scaling by swapping divalent Ca2+ and Mg2+ ions for monovalent Na+ ions on resin beads (periodically regenerated with concentrated NaCl brine). Effluent total hardness must remain \u003c 1 grain/gallon (\u003c 17.1 mg/L as CaCO3); failure of both softener and RO exposes patients to acute hypercalcemia/hypermagnesemia (\u0027Hard Water Syndrome\u0027: nausea, vomiting, flushing, weakness, hypertension).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00738","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Trauma Life Support (ATLS Principles)","topic":"Immediately Life-Threatening Chest Injuries","q":"A 26-year-old motorcyclist arrives in the trauma bay with BP 74/46 mmHg, HR 138 bpm, SpO2 82%, distended neck veins, absent breath sounds and hyperresonance over the right hemithorax, and tracheal deviation to the left. What is the immediate priority intervention?","o":["Perform immediate needle decompression (or finger thoracostomy) of the right chest at the 4th/5th intercostal space anterior-to-midaxillary line, followed by tube thoracostomy","Perform immediate pericardiocentesis via the left subxiphoid approach","Administer 2 L rapid crystalloid bolus and perform endotracheal intubation before touching the chest","Obtain an urgent portable AP chest radiograph to confirm pneumothorax before any invasive procedure"],"a":0,"e":"Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis during the Breathing/Circulation primary survey and must never be delayed for chest radiography. Per ATLS 10th Edition, immediate needle decompression in adults is performed at the 4th or 5th intercostal space just anterior to the midaxillary line (or finger thoracostomy in trained hands), followed promptly by formal chest tube insertion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00739","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Thermal \u0026 Environmental Emergencies","topic":"Fluid Resuscitation Formulas","q":"A 70 kg adult sustains 40% Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) partial- and full-thickness flame burns and reaches the emergency department exactly 2 hours after the injury, having received no prior IV fluids. Using the classic Parkland formula (4 mL/kg/%TBSA of Ringer\u0027s lactate in the first 24 hours), what hourly infusion rate is required over the remaining 6 hours of the first 8-hour window?","o":["700 mL/hr","933 mL/hr","2,800 mL/hr","1,400 mL/hr"],"a":1,"e":"Total 24-hour Parkland volume = 4 mL x 70 kg x 40 = 11,200 mL. Half of this volume (5,600 mL) must be infused within the first 8 hours calculated from the TIME OF BURN, not hospital arrival. Because 2 hours have already elapsed, 5,600 mL must be infused over the remaining 6 hours: 5,600 / 6 = 933.3 mL/hr (~933 mL/hr), then titrated to urine output (0.5 mL/kg/hr).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00740","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Prehospital \u0026 Disaster Management","topic":"START Triage Algorithm","q":"During a railway derailment mass casualty response using the START (Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment) algorithm, an unresponsive non-ambulatory adult victim is initially apneic. After performing a single jaw-thrust maneuver to open the airway, the victim resumes spontaneous breathing at 20 breaths/min. Which triage tag must be assigned?","o":["Green (Minor / Walking Wounded)","Yellow (Delayed)","Red (Immediate)","Black (Deceased / Expectant)"],"a":2,"e":"In the START triage algorithm, if a non-ambulatory victim is apneic, the rescuer performs one attempt to position/open the airway. If spontaneous breathing resumes after airway opening, the victim is immediately tagged Red (Immediate) because they have an airway-dependent life threat requiring urgent intervention. Only victims who remain apneic after positioning are tagged Black.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00741","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Emergency Toxicology","topic":"Organophosphate Poisoning","q":"A 35-year-old agricultural worker is brought to the emergency department after intentional ingestion of monocrotophos (an organophosphate). He has pinpoint pupils, profuse frothy bronchorrhea, bilateral coarse crackles, HR 40 bpm, BP 82/54 mmHg, and muscle fasciculations. After airway suctioning and supplemental O2, what is the clinical endpoint for titrating escalating IV atropine boluses?","o":["Normalization of skeletal muscle fasciculations","Return of normal blood cholinesterase levels on lab assay","Miosis reversal alone even while pulmonary crackles persist","Clearing of tracheobronchial secretions (clear chest auscultation), HR \u003e 80 bpm, and systolic BP \u003e 80-90 mmHg (full atropinization)"],"a":3,"e":"Atropine competitively blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, reversing life-threatening bronchorrhea, bronchospasm, and bradycardia. Rapid incremental doubling doses of IV atropine are titrated strictly to drying of pulmonary secretions (clear lungs), HR \u003e 80 bpm, and SBP \u003e 80 mmHg—not pupillary dilation alone. Pralidoxime (2-PAM) is co-administered to reverse nicotinic effects (fasciculations/weakness) before enzyme \u0027aging\u0027.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00742","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Resuscitation Science (BLS \u0026 ACLS)","topic":"High-Quality CPR \u0026 Waveform Capnography","q":"During adult cardiac arrest resuscitation with an endotracheal tube in place, quantitative waveform capnography has been reading 14 mmHg during chest compressions and suddenly jumps to 42 mmHg while the compressor switches. Per AHA ACLS guidelines, what are the target manual compression depth/rate and what does this abrupt EtCO2 surge indicate?","o":["Depth 5-6 cm at 100-120/min with full recoil; abrupt jump to \u003e= 35-40 mmHg strongly suggests Return of Spontaneous Circulation (ROSC)","Depth 3-4 cm at 80-90/min; indicates esophageal tube dislodgement","Depth 5-6 cm at 100-120/min; indicates hyperventilation requiring 30 breaths/min","Depth \u003e 7 cm at 140/min; indicates tension pneumothorax"],"a":0,"e":"AHA ACLS specifies adult chest compressions at 5-6 cm (2-2.4 inches) depth, 100-120 compressions/min, full chest wall recoil, and minimal interruptions (\u003c10 s), with 1 breath every 6 seconds (10 breaths/min) once an advanced airway is placed. Because EtCO2 mirrors pulmonary blood flow (cardiac output) during arrest, a sudden sustained rise to \u003e= 35-40 mmHg is an early physiological indicator of ROSC.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00743","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Neurological \u0026 Head Trauma Care","topic":"Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)","q":"A pedestrian struck by a car opens his eyes only to painful nailbed pressure, emits incomprehensible groans, and exhibits abnormal slow flexion of both elbows across the chest (decorticate posturing) to supraorbital pressure. What is his exact Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score and TBI severity classification?","o":["GCS 9 (E3V2M4); Mild TBI","GCS 7 (E2V2M3); Severe TBI requiring immediate definitive airway (endotracheal intubation)","GCS 8 (E2V2M4); Moderate TBI managed with observation","GCS 6 (E2V1M3); Moderate TBI"],"a":1,"e":"GCS components: Eye opening to pressure/pain = E2; Verbal incomprehensible sounds (moans/groans without words) = V2; Motor abnormal flexion (decorticate rigidity) = M3. Total GCS = 2 + 2 + 3 = 7. A GCS \u003c= 8 defines Severe Traumatic Brain Injury (\u0027GCS 8, intubate\u0027) requiring prompt airway protection while avoiding hypoxia and hypotension.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00744","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Acute Medical \u0026 Shock Emergencies","topic":"First-Line Epinephrine Protocol","q":"Four minutes after receiving an intravenous dose of ceftriaxone in the emergency bay, a 28-year-old woman develops generalized urticaria, audible inspiratory stridor, diffuse wheezing, and BP 68/42 mmHg. What is the single most critical first-line drug, concentration, dose, and route?","o":["IV hydrocortisone 200 mg + IV chlorpheniramine 10 mg first, holding epinephrine unless cardiac arrest occurs","Rapid IV bolus of epinephrine 1 mg of 1:1000 undiluted","Intramuscular (IM) epinephrine 0.3-0.5 mg (0.3-0.5 mL of 1:1000 / 1 mg/mL solution) into the mid-anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis) immediately","Subcutaneous epinephrine 0.1 mg of 1:10,000 into the deltoid"],"a":2,"e":"IM epinephrine (0.01 mg/kg up to 0.5 mg in adults, 1:1000 = 1 mg/mL) injected into the vascular vastus lateralis (anterolateral thigh) achieves faster, more reliable peak plasma levels than subcutaneous or deltoid routes, reversing airway edema, bronchospasm, and vasodilation. Antihistamines and corticosteroids are strictly second-line adjuncts and never substitute for or delay epinephrine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00745","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Hemorrhagic Shock \u0026 Trauma Resuscitation","topic":"Balanced Hemostatic Resuscitation","q":"A polytrauma patient with an unstable open-book pelvic fracture and hemorrhagic shock triggers the Massive Transfusion Protocol (MTP). What balanced component ratio and antifibrinolytic timing reduce mortality, and which acute electrolyte disturbance from citrate preservative in banked blood must be corrected aggressively during rapid MTP?","o":["Crystalloid 6 L before any plasma; TXA contraindicated in pelvic fractures; treat hypermagnesemia","Whole blood filtered through leukoreduction only; avoid platelets until \u003e 20 units PRBC","10:1 PRBC:FFP ratio; TXA after 12 hours; treat hypercalcemia with calcitonin","Balanced 1:1:1 ratio of PRBC : FFP : Platelets, IV tranexamic acid (1 g over 10 min within 3 hours of injury), and monitor/correct citrate-induced HYPOCALCEMIA (ionized Ca2+)"],"a":3,"e":"Balanced damage-control resuscitation (PROPPR trial / ATLS 10th Ed.) targets a 1:1:1 unit ratio of PRBC : FFP : Platelets (or 1:1:1 equivalent apheresis) alongside early IV tranexamic acid (1 g within 3 hours of trauma per CRASH-2) to combat trauma-induced coagulopathy. Rapid transfusion of citrated blood products chelates ionized calcium (causing hypocalcemia, myocardial depression, and worse coagulopathy), requiring empiric IV calcium chloride/gluconate replacement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00746","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Pediatric Emergency Care (PALS)","topic":"Intraosseous (IO) Cannulation","q":"In an unresponsive, hypotensive 2-year-old child in decompensated shock where peripheral intravenous access cannot be established within 60-90 seconds, what is the preferred anatomical landmark for emergency Intraosseous (IO) needle insertion, and how do IO drug doses compare with IV doses?","o":["Anteromedial flat surface of the proximal tibia (1-2 cm below and medial to the tibial tuberosity, avoiding the epiphyseal growth plate); all resuscitation drugs and fluids use identical IV doses followed by a 5-10 mL normal saline flush","Mid-shaft sternum in infants \u003c 3 years; only epinephrine can be given IO","Posterior iliac crest exclusively; IO cannot deliver blood products","Lateral malleolus of the fibula; IO doses must be tripled"],"a":0,"e":"Per PALS guidelines, the flat anteromedial proximal tibia—1-2 cm distal and slightly medial to the tibial tuberosity, directed slightly caudad away from the physeal growth plate—is the classic pediatric IO landmark (alternatives include distal tibia and distal femur). The non-collapsible marrow venous plexus accepts all resuscitation medications, crystalloids, and blood products at identical IV doses (chased by a rapid 5-10 mL NS bolus).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00747","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Regional \u0026 Environmental Toxicology (India)","topic":"Russell\u0027s Viper Hemotoxic Envenomation","q":"A 38-year-old farmer in Maharashtra presents 2 hours after a Russell\u0027s viper bite on the ankle with progressive limb swelling, oozing from the fang marks, and a positive 20-Minute Whole Blood Clotting Test (20WBCT: blood remains unclotted liquid in a clean dry glass tube after 20 minutes). Per India\u0027s MoHFW Standard Treatment Guidelines for Snakebite, what is the correct initial Anti-Snake Venom (ASV) regimen?","o":["Give 1 vial of ASV intramuscularly around the bite site","Administer 10 vials of polyvalent ASV diluted in isotonic saline/dextrose IV over 1 hour (identical initial dose for adults and children) with IV epinephrine/antihistamine readiness for anaphylaxis, and avoid tourniquets or local incisions","Give 2 vials for children and 20 vials IM for adults","Apply a tight arterial tourniquet, incise the fang marks, and withhold ASV until PT/INR returns"],"a":1,"e":"A non-clotting 20WBCT is a sensitive, rapid bedside marker of venom-induced consumption coagulopathy (VICC) from Russell\u0027s viper or saw-scaled viper bites. Per MoHFW National Snakebite Management Protocol, the initial dose of Indian polyvalent ASV (neutralizing Cobra, Common Krait, Russell\u0027s viper, Saw-scaled viper) is 10 vials IV over 1 hour—identical for adults and children because snake venom load is independent of victim weight. Tight tourniquets and wound incisions cause necrosis/bleeding.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00748","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Acute Cardiac Emergencies","topic":"Synchronized Cardioversion vs Defibrillation","q":"A 60-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy arrives in the emergency room with regular wide-complex monomorphic Ventricular Tachycardia (HR 188 bpm), a palpable carotid pulse, BP 68/40 mmHg, diaphoresis, and acute confusion. Why MUST the emergency technologist engage the \u0027SYNC\u0027 button before delivering 100 J biphasic shock?","o":["Defibrillation mode is safer for all conscious patients with a pulse","Synchronized mode converts monomorphic VT into sinus bradycardia without electrical energy","Synchronization times shock delivery to the R-wave peak, avoiding the vulnerable ventricular repolarization period (T-wave) where an unsynchronized shock could precipitate Ventricular Fibrillation (R-on-T phenomenon)","Unsynchronized shock delivers 0 Joules if a pulse is present"],"a":2,"e":"In unstable tachyarrhythmias WITH a pulse (unstable SVT, AF/flutter, or monomorphic VT), immediate synchronized cardioversion (100 J biphasic for regular wide-complex VT) is indicated. Engaging \u0027SYNC\u0027 detects the QRS R-wave and avoids discharging during the relative refractory period (peak/downslope of the T wave), preventing iatrogenic Ventricular Fibrillation. (Unsynchronized defibrillation is reserved for pulseless VT/VF or polymorphic VT where QRS complexes vary wildly.)","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00749","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Orthopaedic \u0026 Pelvic Trauma","topic":"Pelvic Binder Landmark \u0026 Acute Compartment Syndrome","q":"In a blunt-trauma patient with an unstable \u0027open-book\u0027 pelvic fracture and hemorrhagic shock, where must a circumferential pelvic binder be centered, and in a patient with a tibial shaft fracture, what is the EARLIEST reliable clinical sign of acute compartment syndrome?","o":["Center binder high over the iliac crests; cool pale foot is the first sign","Center binder over mid-thigh; complete motor paralysis appears before pain","Center binder over the lumbar spine; absent distal dorsalis pedis pulse is the earliest sign","Center binder over the GREATER TROCHANTERS (with internal rotation of legs); severe pain out of proportion to injury and pain on passive stretch of compartment muscles are the earliest clinical signs (well before pulselessness)"],"a":3,"e":"Applying a pelvic binder over the greater trochanters (not high on the iliac wings, which can actually pry an open-book pelvis wider!) reduces pelvic volume and tamponades retroperitoneal venous bleeding. In acute compartment syndrome, capillary/venular ischemia precedes arterial occlusion—so intense pain out of proportion to injury and pain on passive stretch appear EARLY, whereas pulselessness and pallor are late signs indicating irreversible muscle necrosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00750","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Environmental Emergencies","topic":"Exertional Heat Stroke","q":"During a summer military drill in Rajasthan, a 24-year-old recruit collapses with delirium, incoherent speech, BP 84/50 mmHg, HR 142 bpm, and rectal core temperature of 41.6 C. What distinguishes Exertional Heat Stroke from heat exhaustion, and what is the primary emergency treatment?","o":["Core temp \u003e 40 C PLUS CNS dysfunction (delirium/seizures/coma) defines heat stroke; initiate IMMEDIATE rapid active cooling (cold/ice-water immersion or evaporative misting + fans + ice packs to neck/axilla/groin) targeting \u003c 39 C within 30 minutes alongside IV isotonic fluids","Warm blankets to prevent shivering before checking electrolytes","Core temp \u003e 40 C PLUS central nervous system (CNS) dysfunction defines heat stroke; administer IV paracetamol 1 g and dantrolene alone","Absence of sweating is mandatory for heat stroke; withhold cooling if sweating is present"],"a":0,"e":"Heat stroke is defined clinically by hyperthermia (\u003e 40 C) accompanied by CNS dysfunction (confusion, delirium, seizures, coma)—unlike heat exhaustion, where mental status remains intact. Sweating is frequently PRESENT in exertional heat stroke! Survival depends on rapid reduction of core temperature (\u0027cool first, transport/workup second\u0027) via ice-water immersion or evaporative cooling; antipyretics (paracetamol/NSAIDs) do NOT reset environmental heat overload and worsen hepatic/renal injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00751","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Neurological Emergencies","topic":"Stepwise Antiseizure Protocol","q":"An adult arrives actively convulsing for 8 minutes (generalized convulsive status epilepticus). Alongside airway positioning, high-flow O2, and an immediate fingerstick blood glucose check, what are the evidence-based first-line (0-5 min) and second-line (5-20 min) medications?","o":["First-line: IV phenytoin slow infusion over 45 min; Second-line: oral carbamazepine","First-line: IV lorazepam (0.1 mg/kg up to 4 mg) or IM midazolam (10 mg if no IV access); Second-line (if seizures persist): IV levetiracetam (60 mg/kg), fosphenytoin (20 mg PE/kg), or valproate (40 mg/kg)","First-line: IM atropine; Second-line: IV magnesium sulfate alone","First-line: IV propofol + succinylcholine paralysis without EEG; Second-line: oral diazepam"],"a":1,"e":"Per AES/ICMR Status Epilepticus guidelines, rapid benzodiazepines—IV lorazepam (4 mg, repeatable once), IV diazepam (10 mg), or IM midazolam (10 mg per RAMPART trial when IV access is delayed)—terminate ~65% of seizures when given promptly alongside checking for hypoglycemia. Benzodiazepine-refractory seizures transition immediately to IV levetiracetam (60 mg/kg, max 4,500 mg), fosphenytoin (20 mg PE/kg), or valproate (40 mg/kg, per ESETT trial).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00752","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Thoracic Trauma Care","topic":"Flail Chest \u0026 Pulmonary Contusion","q":"A 45-year-old driver involved in a high-speed steering-wheel impact has severe chest pain, tachypnea (RR 32/min), SpO2 87%, and an anterolateral chest segment moving INWARD during inspiration and OUTWARD during expiration (flail chest: ribs 4-7 fractured in two places each). What is the primary cause of his hypoxia and the cornerstone of early ED/ICU management?","o":["Mediastinal flutter alone; tightly wrap circumferential adhesive strapping/sandbags around the chest","Isolated rib pain; discharge home with oral NSAIDs","Underlying PULMONARY CONTUSION (alveolar hemorrhage/edema); prioritize aggressive multimodal analgesia (IV opioids/thoracic epidural/serratus block), supplemental O2 / NIV or intubation if failing, and JUDICIOUS fluid resuscitation to avoid worsening contusion edema","Diaphragmatic paralysis; infuse 4 L rapid saline bolus"],"a":2,"e":"Although paradoxical chest-wall movement increases work of breathing, the principal driver of severe hypoxemia in flail chest is the underlying PULMONARY CONTUSION (interstitial/alveolar hemorrhage and V/Q shunting). Per ATLS 10th Ed., management focuses on potent pain control (enabling deep inspiration/coughing), pulmonary toilet/O2/ventilatory support as needed, and careful euvolemic fluid balance—excessive crystalloids leak across injured alveolar capillaries and worsen ARDS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00753","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Airway Management and Emergency Pharmacology","topic":"Induction and Paralytic Agent Selection","q":"A 30-year-old construction worker rescued 36 hours after a building collapse with bilateral lower-limb crush injury develops acute hypoxic respiratory failure requiring Rapid Sequence Intubation (RSI). Serum potassium is 6.1 mEq/L and BP is 86/52 mmHg. Which induction and paralytic combination is safest?","o":["Midazolam high-dose with vecuronium and hyperventilation alone","Propofol with succinylcholine","Thiopental with succinylcholine","Ketamine (or etomidate) with rocuronium"],"a":3,"e":"Succinylcholine is strictly contraindicated in patients with hyperkalemia or \u003e24 hours after major crush injury, burns, or denervation because upregulation of extrajunctional acetylcholine receptors leads to massive potassium efflux and fatal hyperkalemic cardiac arrest. Rocuronium (1.0–1.2 mg/kg) provides rapid paralysis without raising potassium, and ketamine or etomidate preserves hemodynamic stability in hypotension.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00754","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Trauma Care and Resuscitation","topic":"Immediately Life-Threatening Chest Injuries","q":"A 28-year-old motorcyclist arrives after blunt chest trauma with BP 74/40 mmHg, HR 138/min, SpO2 82%, distended neck veins, absent breath sounds over the right hemithorax, and tracheal deviation to the left. According to ATLS 10th Edition guidelines, what is the preferred immediate anatomical site for needle thoracostomy in this adult?","o":["4th or 5th intercostal space, slightly anterior to the midaxillary line on the right","7th intercostal space, posterior axillary line on the right","Subxiphoid space directed toward the left shoulder","2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line on the left"],"a":0,"e":"Per ATLS 10th Edition, the preferred site for adult needle decompression of a tension pneumothorax was updated to the 4th or 5th intercostal space, slightly anterior to the midaxillary line on the affected side (where chest wall thickness is thinner than at the 2nd ICS MCL, reducing catheter kinking/failure), followed promptly by tube thoracostomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00755","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Trauma Care and Resuscitation","topic":"Burn Fluid Resuscitation","q":"A 70-kg adult with 40% total body surface area (TBSA) partial- and full-thickness flame burns arrives in the emergency department exactly 2 hours after the injury, having received no prehospital IV fluids. Using the Parkland formula (4 mL/kg/%TBSA of Lactated Ringer\u0027s), what hourly infusion rate should be set for the next 6 hours to complete the first 8-hour allotment?","o":["700 mL/hour (5,600 mL over 8 hours)","933 mL/hour (5,600 mL over the remaining 6 hours)","1,400 mL/hour (11,200 mL over 8 hours)","467 mL/hour (2,800 mL over 6 hours)"],"a":1,"e":"Total 24-hour Parkland requirement = 4 mL × 70 kg × 40 = 11,200 mL. Half (5,600 mL) must be administered in the first 8 hours calculated from the TIME OF BURN, not time of arrival. Since 2 hours have elapsed with no fluids, 5,600 mL must be infused over the remaining 6 hours: 5,600 / 6 = 933.3 mL/hour, titrated to urine output (0.5 mL/kg/h in adults).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00756","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Disaster Management and Prehospital EMS","topic":"START Triage Algorithm","q":"During a bus-collision mass casualty incident, an emergency technician assesses an adult passenger who cannot walk, has a respiratory rate of 24/min, has a palpable radial pulse (capillary refill \u003c 2 seconds), and is unable to obey simple verbal commands (\u0027squeeze my hand\u0027). Which START triage tag should be assigned?","o":["Green (Minor)","Yellow (Delayed)","Red (Immediate)","Black (Expectant/Deceased)"],"a":2,"e":"In the START (Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment) algorithm, after checking ambulation (Green), Respirations (R: \u003e30/min = Red), and Perfusion (P: absent radial pulse or cap refill \u003e2 s = Red), Mental status (M) is evaluated. Inability to follow simple commands indicates acute neurologic compromise and immediately assigns the Red (Immediate) tag.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00757","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Emergency Toxicology and Poisoning","topic":"Organophosphate Poisoning","q":"In the emergency resuscitation of a farmer with severe monocrotophos (organophosphate) poisoning presenting with bronchorrhea, pinpoint pupils, bradycardia, and muscle fasciculations, what is the definitive clinical endpoint for titrating intravenous atropine?","o":["Complete resolution of skeletal muscle fasciculations","Normalization of pupil size to \u003e6 mm bilateral mydriasis regardless of lung auscultation","Normalization of serum pseudocholinesterase levels","Clear lungs with cessation of bronchorrhea and bronchial secretions (alongside adequate heart rate and BP)"],"a":3,"e":"Hypoxia from bronchorrhea and bronchospasm is the leading cause of early death in organophosphate poisoning. Therefore, atropine is titrated (typically doubling doses every 5 minutes) until complete atropinization of the airways—clear chest on auscultation and dry bronchial secretions—with HR \u003e80/min and SBP \u003e80–90 mmHg. Pralidoxime (2-PAM) is given concurrently for nicotinic fasciculations/weakness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00758","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Cardiac Life Support and Resuscitation","topic":"Shockable Arrest Algorithms","q":"A 55-year-old man collapses in the ED triage area with monitored ventricular fibrillation (VF). High-quality CPR is ongoing. He receives a first biphasic shock (200 J), then 2 minutes of CPR, a second shock, IV epinephrine 1 mg, another 2 minutes of CPR, and a third 200 J shock—yet VF persists on rhythm check. Which antiarrhythmic bolus should be prepared and administered next?","o":["Amiodarone 300 mg IV/IO bolus (or lidocaine 1–1.5 mg/kg IV/IO)","Adenosine 6 mg rapid IV push","Atropine 1 mg IV bolus","Magnesium sulfate 2 g IV even with normal QT and serum magnesium"],"a":0,"e":"Under AHA ACLS Cardiac Arrest Guidelines, epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO is administered after the second shock (every 3–5 min), and an antiarrhythmic—amiodarone 300 mg IV/IO (second dose 150 mg) or lidocaine 1–1.5 mg/kg IV/IO—is given after the third shock for shock-refractory VF/pVT.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00759","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Neurotrauma and Critical Care Stabilization","topic":"Cerebral Perfusion Pressure Management","q":"Assertion (A): Permissive hypotension (targeting SBP 70–80 mmHg before surgical hemostasis) is contraindicated in a polytrauma patient with concurrent severe Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI). Reason (R): Cerebral perfusion pressure equals mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure (CPP = MAP - ICP), so systemic hypotension sharply reduces cerebral blood flow and worsens secondary ischemic brain injury. Choose the correct option.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Even a single episode of hypotension (SBP \u003c 90 mmHg) doubles mortality in severe TBI because elevated ICP compromises cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP - ICP). Therefore, ATLS 10th Edition and Brain Trauma Foundation guidelines recommend avoiding permissive hypotension in TBI and maintaining SBP \u003e= 100–110 mmHg (age-dependent).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00760","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Point-of-Care Emergency Ultrasound \u0026 Obstetric Emergencies","topic":"Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy \u0026 eFAST","q":"A 25-year-old woman arrives in the emergency bay after collapsing at home with acute lower abdominal pain, BP 72/44 mmHg, HR 134 bpm, pale conjunctivae, and a positive point-of-care urine beta-hCG test. Bedside FAST ultrasound reveals a large anechoic fluid stripe in Morison\u0027s pouch (hepatorenal recess) and the pouch of Douglas. What is the diagnosis and immediate priority?","o":["Uncomplicated early intrauterine pregnancy; discharge for outpatient OB clinic","Ruptured ectopic pregnancy with massive hemoperitoneum; initiate two large-bore IV lines, balanced blood transfusion/type-and-crossmatch, and emergent gynecologic surgery (laparoscopy/laparotomy)","Acute uncomplicated cystitis; oral nitrofurantoin","Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome; administer IV methotrexate and observe 48 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Any hypotensive woman of reproductive age with abdominal pain/syncope has a ruptured ectopic pregnancy until proven otherwise. Free intraperitoneal fluid in Morison\u0027s pouch alongside a positive pregnancy test confirms massive hemoperitoneum—an absolute contraindication to medical methotrexate therapy, requiring immediate hemorrhagic shock resuscitation and emergent surgical hemostasis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00761","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"HAZMAT \u0026 Chemical Burn Emergencies","topic":"Dry Powder Removal \u0026 High-Volume Water Irrigation","q":"A construction worker arrives in the emergency decontamination zone with dry powdered quicklime (calcium oxide, strong alkali) spilled across his wet forearms and face. What is the correct immediate decontamination sequence, and why must a dilute acidic solution (e.g., vinegar/acetic acid) NEVER be poured onto an alkali skin burn?","o":["Cover dry quicklime tightly with petrolatum ointment without washing","Pour dilute vinegar immediately onto the dry powder to neutralize pH","Put on PPE, brush away ALL visible dry powder FIRST (preventing exothermic hydration reaction with small amounts of water), remove contaminated clothing, and then irrigate copiously with continuous running water/saline for \u003e= 20-30 minutes; acid-base neutralization generates intense exothermic heat that adds thermal burns","Wash for exactly 30 seconds and apply dry gauze"],"a":2,"e":"Dry chemical powders—especially quicklime (CaO), lye pellets, and elemental metals—generate intense heat when mixed with small volumes of water. Therefore, dry powder must be brushed off the skin/clothing first (wearing PPE to protect staff), followed by prolonged high-volume continuous water/saline irrigation (checking litmus pH until neutral ~7.0). Attempting chemical neutralization (acid on alkali or vice versa) triggers an exothermic reaction that deepens tissue necrosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00762","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Post-Resuscitation Care","topic":"Temperature Control, Oxygenation \u0026 Neuroprognostication","q":"Following 18 minutes of CPR for out-of-hospital VF cardiac arrest, a 55-year-old man achieves Return of Spontaneous Circulation (ROSC) but remains comatose. Per AHA Post-Cardiac Arrest Care guidelines, which combination of oxygenation, temperature management, and neuroprognostication timing optimizes neurological survival?","o":["Hyperventilate to PaCO2 22 mmHg and keep MAP \u003c 55 mmHg","Routine immediate prophylactic therapeutic hypothermia to 26 C","Keep FiO2 1.0 (PaO2 \u003e 300 mmHg) for 24 hours; allow fever to 39 C; declare poor prognosis at 6 hours post-ROSC","Titrate FiO2 promptly to maintain normoxia (SpO2 92-98%, avoiding hyperoxia), keep PaCO2 35-45 mmHg and MAP \u003e= 65 mmHg, maintain active temperature control (32-37.5 C, strictly preventing fever \u003e 37.7 C), and delay formal neuroprognostication until \u003e= 72 hours after return to normothermia"],"a":3,"e":"Post-ROSC ischemic-reperfusion brain injury is worsened by hyperoxia (free-radical oxidative stress -\u003e titrate FiO2 down to SpO2 92-98%), hypocapnia (cerebral vasoconstriction -\u003e keep PaCO2 35-45 mmHg), hypotension (keep MAP \u003e= 65 mmHg), and fever (active temperature control 32-37.5 C for \u003e= 36-72 h). Because sedatives and post-arrest stunning confound early exams, multimodal neuroprognostication is delayed until \u003e= 72 hours post-normothermia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00763","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Medical Emergencies and Pharmacology","topic":"Anaphylaxis Resuscitation","q":"A 29-year-old man develops generalized urticaria, inspiratory stridor, wheezing, and BP 76/48 mmHg 5 minutes after receiving IV ceftriaxone in the emergency bay. What is the single most critical immediate pharmacological intervention, including exact dose, concentration, and route?","o":["Epinephrine 0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1:1,000 / 1 mg/mL) intramuscularly into the mid-anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis)","Salbutamol nebulization alone while observing BP for 15 minutes","Diphenhydramine 50 mg IV push followed by hydrocortisone 200 mg IV before giving epinephrine","Epinephrine 1.0 mg (1:10,000) rapid IV bolus push into a peripheral vein"],"a":0,"e":"Immediate intramuscular epinephrine (0.01 mg/kg up to 0.5 mg in adults, using 1:1,000 [1 mg/mL] solution) injected into the mid-anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis) is the universal first-line life-saving treatment for anaphylaxis. It achieves faster and higher peak plasma levels than subcutaneous or deltoid IM injection while avoiding the arrhythmia/hypertensive crisis risks of an unmonitored IV bolus (reserved for refractory shock/arrest as a dilute infusion).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00764","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Trauma Care and Resuscitation","topic":"Unstable Pelvic Ring Fracture","q":"A 34-year-old pedestrian struck by a truck arrives with BP 80/46 mmHg, HR 132/min, negative eFAST peritoneal windows, and an anteroposterior pelvic radiograph showing pubic symphysis diastasis \u003e3 cm (\u0027open-book\u0027 pelvic fracture). Where must the circumferential pelvic binder be centered, and what additional lower-limb maneuver reduces intrapelvic volume?","o":["Perform repeated vigorous anteroposterior and lateral pelvic compression tests every 10 minutes to assess stability","Centered over the GREATER TROCHANTERS bilaterally with the knees/ankles internally rotated and taped together","Applied only around the upper thighs below the lesser trochanters","Centered high over the iliac crests and costal margin with legs externally rotated"],"a":1,"e":"An \u0027open-book\u0027 pelvic ring injury causes massive retroperitoneal venous plexus bleeding. Applying a commercial pelvic binder (or folded bedsheet) centered squarely over the GREATER TROCHANTERS—combined with internal rotation of the lower extremities—closes the anterior pelvic ring, reduces true pelvic volume, and stabilizes clot formation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00765","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Emergency Toxicology and Envenomation","topic":"Snakebite Envenomation (India Protocol)","q":"A 42-year-old agricultural laborer presents 3 hours after a Russell\u0027s viper bite on his right ankle with progressive limb swelling, bleeding gums, and a 20-Minute Whole Blood Clotting Test (20WBCT) that remains completely liquid (unclotted) after 20 minutes. Under the MoHFW Standard Treatment Guidelines for Snakebite in India, what is the immediate definitive management?","o":["Administer 10 vials of Polyvalent Anti-Snake Venom (ASV) diluted in isotonic saline/5% dextrose IV over 1 hour while monitoring closely for anaphylaxis, plus tetanus toxoid","Apply a tight arterial tourniquet above the knee, incise the bite site, and apply suction","Administer 2 vials of ASV subcutaneously around the bite margin only","Withhold ASV until coagulation factor assays (PT/aPTT/D-dimer) return from an external laboratory 6 hours later"],"a":0,"e":"A positive (non-clotting) 20WBCT in a clean, dry glass tube confirms systemic venom-induced consumption coagulopathy (typical of Russell\u0027s or saw-scaled viper). Per India\u0027s MoHFW National Snakebite Management Protocol, the standard initial dose is 10 vials of reconstituted Polyvalent ASV infused IV over 1 hour (with epinephrine ready for acute ASV reactions), repeated at 6 hours if 20WBCT remains non-clotting.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00766","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Pediatric Emergency Care","topic":"Weight-Based PALS Calculations","q":"A 4-year-old boy weighing 16 kg presents in decompensated septic shock requiring intubation and resuscitation. Using standard PALS formulas (uncuffed ETT = Age/4 + 4; cuffed ETT = Age/4 + 3.5; initial isotonic fluid bolus = 20 mL/kg; first defibrillation shock = 2 J/kg), what are the correct cuffed ETT internal diameter, initial fluid bolus volume, and first defibrillation dose if VF occurs?","o":["3.5 mm cuffed ETT, 640 mL normal saline bolus, and 16 J first shock","4.5 mm cuffed ETT, 80 mL normal saline bolus, and 100 J first shock","4.5 mm cuffed ETT, 320 mL normal saline bolus, and 32 J (approx. 30 J) first shock","5.5 mm cuffed ETT, 160 mL normal saline bolus, and 64 J first shock"],"a":2,"e":"For a 4-year-old (16 kg): Cuffed ETT ID = (4/4) + 3.5 = 4.5 mm (uncuffed would be 4/4 + 4 = 5.0 mm). Initial isotonic crystalloid bolus in non-cardiogenic shock = 20 mL/kg × 16 kg = 320 mL over 5–10 min (reassessing for fluid overload). Initial PALS defibrillation dose = 2 J/kg × 16 kg = 32 J (followed by 4 J/kg = 64 J for subsequent shocks).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00767","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Mechanical Ventilation and Respiratory Emergencies","topic":"Lung-Protective Ventilation","q":"A 175-cm tall male trauma patient (Predicted Body Weight = 70.5 kg, actual weight 105 kg) develops severe ARDS (PaO2/FiO2 = 95 mmHg) after pulmonary contusion and massive transfusion. Which initial ventilator target best prevents ventilator-induced lung injury (barotrauma and volutrauma)?","o":["Tidal volume 840 mL (8 mL/kg actual body weight) to keep PaCO2 strictly \u003c= 35 mmHg","Zero PEEP with tidal volume 1,000 mL to recruit collapsed alveoli via high peak pressures","High-frequency manual bag-valve hyperventilation targeting plateau pressure 45 cmH2O","Tidal volume ~420 mL (6 mL/kg Predicted Body Weight), plateau pressure \u003c= 30 cmH2O, and titrated PEEP"],"a":3,"e":"Lung size correlates with height/sex (Predicted Body Weight, PBW), NOT actual adipose weight. Per the ARDSNet lung-protective protocol, setting tidal volume at 4–6 mL/kg PBW (~420 mL here), keeping plateau pressure \u003c= 30 cmH2O (and driving pressure \u003c 15 cmH2O), and applying adequate PEEP prevents alveolar overdistension and cyclic atelectrauma, reducing ARDS mortality while accepting permissive hypercapnia (pH \u003e= 7.20).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00768","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Neurological and Cerebrovascular Emergencies","topic":"Pre-Thrombolysis Stabilization","q":"A 63-year-old man arrives within 90 minutes of sudden right-sided hemiparesis and aphasia, with BP 198/114 mmHg and capillary glucose 112 mg/dL. Non-contrast CT head reveals no hemorrhage or acute hypodensity. Before administering IV alteplase (or tenecteplase), to what target must his blood pressure be gently lowered?","o":["Below 185/110 mmHg (using IV labetalol or nicardipine)","Wait 6 hours until BP normalizes spontaneously before ordering thrombolysis","Below 110/70 mmHg rapidly using IV sodium nitroprusside","Below 220/120 mmHg only, as 198/114 mmHg requires no lowering before thrombolysis"],"a":0,"e":"In acute ischemic stroke eligible for IV thrombolysis (within 4.5 hours), blood pressure MUST be lowered carefully to \u003c 185/110 mmHg prior to bolus administration (and maintained \u003c 180/105 mmHg for at least 24 hours post-infusion) using titratable IV agents like labetalol or nicardipine to reduce symptomatic intracerebral hemorrhage risk. (In non-thrombolysis patients, permissive hypertension up to 220/120 mmHg is tolerated).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00769","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Electrolyte and Metabolic Emergencies","topic":"Membrane Stabilization Mechanism","q":"Assertion (A): Intravenous 10% calcium gluconate is the immediate first-line emergency medication for a hyperkalemic patient (K+ 7.6 mEq/L) with widened QRS complexes, even though it does not lower serum potassium levels. Reason (R): Hyperkalemia reduces the resting membrane potential gradient, and IV calcium rapidly restores the electrical gradient by raising the cardiac threshold potential toward less negative values. Choose the correct option.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Elevated extracellular K+ makes the resting myocardial membrane potential less negative (closer to threshold), initially accelerating excitability (peaked T waves) and then inactivating Na+ channels (QRS widening, sine-wave pattern, VF/asystole). IV calcium gluconate (10 mL of 10% over 2–5 min) acts within 1–3 minutes to shift the threshold potential less negative, re-establishing the resting-to-threshold gradient while insulin/dextrose, nebulized beta-agonists, and elimination therapies lower serum K+.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00770","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Emergency Procedures and Clinical Skills","topic":"Anatomical Landmarks","q":"Match each emergency procedure with its standard anatomical landmark: (1) Surgical cricothyrotomy, (2) Tube thoracostomy (triangle of safety), (3) Subxiphoid pericardiocentesis, (4) Adult proximal tibial intraosseous (IO) access. Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Cricothyroid membrane; 2-Left xiphocostal angle; 3-Anteromedial tibia; 4-4th/5th ICS midaxillary line","1-Cricothyroid membrane between thyroid and cricoid cartilages; 2-4th/5th ICS anterior to midaxillary line bounded by pectoralis major and latissimus dorsi; 3-Left xiphocostal angle aimed toward left shoulder tip; 4-Anteromedial flat tibia ~2 cm distal and medial to tibial tuberosity","1-Left xiphocostal angle; 2-Cricothyroid membrane; 3-4th/5th ICS midaxillary line; 4-Anteromedial tibia","1-4th/5th ICS midaxillary line; 2-Cricothyroid membrane; 3-Anteromedial tibia; 4-Left xiphocostal angle"],"a":1,"e":"Accurate landmarking prevents iatrogenic injury in emergent \u0027can\u0027t intubate, can\u0027t oxygenate\u0027 and shock scenarios: surgical cricothyrotomy incises the cricothyroid membrane; chest tubes enter the 4th/5th ICS above the rib border in the triangle of safety; subxiphoid pericardiocentesis enters the left xiphocostal notch at ~30–45° toward the left shoulder; adult proximal tibial IO enters the flat anteromedial cortex ~2 cm distal/medial to the tibial tuberosity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00771","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Trauma Care and Resuscitation","topic":"Acute Pericardial Tamponade","q":"A 25-year-old man arrives 15 minutes after a single stab wound just medial to the left nipple. BP is 72/44 mmHg, HR 136/min, breath sounds are equal bilaterally, neck veins are markedly distended, heart sounds are muffled, and bedside subxiphoid eFAST shows a large pericardial effusion with right ventricular diastolic collapse. While alerting the cardiothoracic surgeon for emergent thoracotomy/pericardial window, what immediate bedside measures stabilize him?","o":["Administer IV furosemide 40 mg and nitroglycerin to reduce elevated JVP","Induce positive-pressure paralysis immediately without preload support or pericardial drainage prep","Administer a cautious isotonic crystalloid bolus to augment RV filling pressure and prepare for ultrasound-guided pericardiocentesis (or emergent ED thoracotomy if arrest occurs)","Insert a left-sided 36 Fr chest tube as sole treatment despite equal breath sounds"],"a":2,"e":"Acute traumatic cardiac tamponade restricts ventricular diastolic filling (Beck\u0027s triad: hypotension, muffled heart sounds, JVD; plus RV diastolic collapse on echo). Equal breath sounds exclude tension pneumothorax/massive hemothorax. A transient IV crystalloid bolus raises central venous pressure to overcome intrapericardial pressure while preparing emergent surgical pericardial decompression (or ultrasound-guided pericardiocentesis as a temporizing bridge if surgical delay occurs).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00772","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Trauma Care and Resuscitation","topic":"Damage Control Resuscitation (DCR)","q":"In Damage Control Resuscitation (DCR) for a polytrauma patient with Class IV hemorrhagic shock arriving 90 minutes post-injury, which combination of interventions directly counteracts the trauma \u0027lethal triad\u0027 (hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy)?","o":["Infusing 6 liters of room-temperature 0.9% normal saline before ordering any plasma or platelets","Giving prophylactic sodium bicarbonate 200 mEq instead of blood products","Administering TXA 48 hours after injury alongside cold uncrossmatched PRBCs alone","Balanced 1:1:1 transfusion of PRBCs, FFP, and platelets, early IV tranexamic acid (TXA 1 g over 10 min within 3 h), active blood/patient warming, and ionized calcium replacement"],"a":3,"e":"Excess cold crystalloid dilutes clotting factors and worsens hypothermia and hyperchloremic acidosis. Modern DCR limits crystalloids, activates a Massive Transfusion Protocol in a balanced 1:1:1 (PRBC:FFP:Platelet) ratio, administers Tranexamic Acid (1 g IV within 3 hours per CRASH-2, followed by 1 g over 8 h) to halt hyperfibrinolysis, actively warms fluids/patient, and replaces calcium chelated by citrate anticoagulant.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00773","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Environmental and Submersion Emergencies","topic":"Hypothermic Cardiac Arrest","q":"A mountaineer rescued from an icy river arrives pulseless in ventricular fibrillation with an esophageal core temperature of 26.5°C. After delivering one 200 J biphasic shock without return of spontaneous circulation while continuous CPR is ongoing, how should ACLS medications and rewarming be managed until core temperature reaches 30°C?","o":["Withhold IV epinephrine and antiarrhythmics until core temperature is rewarmed \u003e= 30°C, limit defibrillation attempts, and initiate aggressive active core rewarming (warmed IV fluids, heated humidified O2, or ECMO/CPB)","Administer double-dose epinephrine (2 mg IV) every 2 minutes while continuing ice packs","Deliver 15 consecutive stacked shocks without pausing for active internal rewarming","Pronounce death immediately after 10 minutes of CPR at 26.5°C because VF failed to convert on the first shock"],"a":0,"e":"Below 30°C, the hypothermic myocardium is largely refractory to defibrillation and drugs, while hepatic/renal drug metabolism slows dramatically—causing repeated epinephrine boluses to accumulate to toxic levels upon rewarming. Per AHA/ERC Hypothermic Arrest guidelines, limit shocks (1–3 attempts), withhold vasopressors/antiarrhythmics below 30°C, and prioritize continuous CPR plus active internal rewarming (ideally VA-ECMO/CPB if available) before pronouncing death (\u0027not dead until warm and dead\u0027).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00774","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Emergency Toxicology and Inhalation Injury","topic":"Carbon Monoxide and Cyanide Toxicity","q":"A 38-year-old firefighter rescued from an enclosed warehouse fire arrives confused with headache, soot around the nares, and severe metabolic acidosis (lactate 11.4 mmol/L), yet standard fingertip pulse oximetry reads SpO2 99% on room air. Which diagnostic test confirms the suspected toxin, and what immediate therapies target both carbon monoxide and suspected co-existing cyanide toxicity?","o":["Serum pseudocholinesterase level; administer atropine 2 mg IV","Arterial CO-oximetry (measuring carboxyhemoglobin %); administer 100% high-flow non-rebreather oxygen (or hyperbaric O2 if indicated) plus IV hydroxocobalamin for suspected cyanide toxicity","Standard two-wavelength pulse oximetry alone; remove oxygen because SpO2 is 99%","Capillary glucose check alone; administer oral activated charcoal"],"a":1,"e":"Standard two-wavelength fingertip pulse oximeters cannot distinguish carboxyhemoglobin (COHb) from oxyhemoglobin, reading falsely normal (98–100%) despite severe tissue hypoxia. Multi-wavelength CO-oximetry on an arterial/venous blood gas directly measures COHb. High-flow 100% FiO2 shortens COHb half-life from ~4–6 hours to ~60–90 minutes. Furthermore, enclosed-space fires burning plastics/synthetics release hydrogen cyanide (evidenced by severe lactic acidosis \u003e8–10 mmol/L), treated empirically with IV hydroxocobalamin (Vitamin B12a).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00775","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Cardiovascular Emergencies","topic":"Acute Aortic Dissection","q":"A 64-year-old hypertensive man presents with sudden tearing interscapular back pain, BP 218/118 mmHg, HR 104/min, and a CT angiogram confirming an acute Stanford Type A ascending aortic dissection. In what strict order must intravenous antihypertensive agents be administered?","o":["Administer IV thrombolytic alteplase plus aspirin and clopidogrel immediately","Administer IV sodium nitroprusside or hydralazine FIRST without beta-blockade","Administer IV beta-blocker FIRST (esmolol or labetalol) to reduce HR \u003c 60 bpm, and ONLY THEN add a titratable vasodilator (nicardipine or clevidipine) if SBP remains \u003e 100–120 mmHg","Lower BP gradually over 48 hours using oral amlodipine only"],"a":2,"e":"Anti-impulse therapy in acute aortic dissection aims to minimize aortic wall shear stress (dP/dt) and propagation of the false lumen. Administering a pure vasodilator first triggers baroreceptor-mediated reflex tachycardia and increased left ventricular ejection velocity (dP/dt), worsening dissection! Therefore, an IV beta-blocker (esmolol or labetalol) MUST be given first to drop HR \u003c= 60 bpm before adding nicardipine to reach SBP 100–120 mmHg, followed by emergent cardiothoracic repair for Type A.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00776","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Pediatric Emergency Care","topic":"Acute Epiglottitis Precautions","q":"Assertion (A): In an unvaccinated 4-year-old child sitting upright in the tripod position with high fever, drooling, dysphagia, and muffled voice (suspected acute epiglottitis), forceful supine positioning, IV placement, or tongue-depressor pharyngeal inspection must be avoided in the triage bay. Reason (R): Agitation and crying markedly increase turbulent inspiratory airflow and can trigger acute complete laryngospasm and fatal airway obstruction of the edematous supraglottis. Choose the correct option.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Acute epiglottitis (classically Haemophilus influenzae type b) causes massive supraglottic edema that barely maintains a pinpoint airway. Keeping the child calm in the parent\u0027s lap in their preferred upright tripod posture while summoning senior anesthesia/ENT specialists to secure the airway in a controlled setting (operating theater) prevents crying-induced negative inspiratory collapse and sudden asphyxiation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00777","course":"B.Sc Emergency \u0026 Trauma Care Technology","subject":"Neurotrauma and Critical Care Stabilization","topic":"GCS, MAP, and CPP Calculations","q":"A patient with severe head trauma opens his eyes only to supraorbital pressure (E2), makes incomprehensible groaning sounds (V2), and exhibits abnormal decorticate flexion posturing of the upper limbs to pain (M3). His invasive arterial BP is 110/65 mmHg and his intracranial pressure (ICP) bolt reads 22 mmHg. What are his exact Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score and Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP)?","o":["GCS = 6/15 (E2V2M2); MAP = 80 mmHg; CPP = 102 mmHg","GCS = 9/15 (E3V2M4); MAP = 65 mmHg; CPP = 43 mmHg","GCS = 8/15 (E2V2M4); MAP = 87.5 mmHg; CPP = 65.5 mmHg","GCS = 7/15 (E2V2M3 -\u003e intubate); MAP = 80 mmHg; CPP = 58 mmHg"],"a":3,"e":"GCS components: Eye opening to pain = 2, Verbal incomprehensible sounds = 2, Motor abnormal flexion (decorticate) = 3 -\u003e Total GCS = 2 + 2 + 3 = 7/15 (GCS \u003c= 8 mandates definitive endotracheal intubation). MAP = [SBP + 2(DBP)] / 3 = [110 + 2(65)] / 3 = 240 / 3 = 80 mmHg. CPP = MAP − ICP = 80 − 22 = 58 mmHg (slightly below the Brain Trauma Foundation target of 60–70 mmHg).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00778","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Clinical Coding \u0026 Disease Classification","topic":"Principal Diagnosis \u0026 Code Structure","q":"A patient is admitted to the emergency ward for acute pulmonary edema, which after inpatient echocardiography and workup is established to be caused by an acute anterior wall ST-elevation myocardial infarction. Under WHO ICD-10 inpatient morbidity coding guidelines, how is the \u0027principal diagnosis\u0027 defined, and which letter chapter in ICD-10 is reserved for provisional/emerging diseases such as COVID-19 (U07.1)?","o":["The condition established after study to be chiefly responsible for occasioning the patient\u0027s admission; Chapter XXII (Codes for special purposes, U00-U85)","The patient\u0027s longest-standing chronic comorbidity; Chapter XXI","The first symptom written by the triage nurse; Chapter Z","The complication with the highest hospital billing tariff; Chapter XVIII"],"a":0,"e":"In inpatient health information management, the principal diagnosis is defined as the main condition diagnosed at the end of the episode of care—established after study—to be chiefly responsible for the patient\u0027s need for admission/investigation. Chapter XXII (U00-U85) in WHO ICD-10 is reserved for special purposes and provisional assignment of new diseases of uncertain etiology (e.g., U07.1).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00779","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Hospital Statistics \u0026 Health Informatics","topic":"Bed Occupancy Rate \u0026 ALOS","q":"During a 30-day month, a 200-bed tertiary care hospital records 5,400 inpatient service days and 900 total discharges (including deaths). What are the Bed Occupancy Rate (BOR) and Average Length of Stay (ALOS) for that month?","o":["BOR = 90.0%; ALOS = 4.5 days","BOR = 90.0%; ALOS = 6.0 days","BOR = 85.0%; ALOS = 5.4 days","BOR = 95.0%; ALOS = 6.7 days"],"a":1,"e":"Maximum available bed-days = Bed complement x Days in period = 200 x 30 = 6,000 bed-days. BOR = (Total inpatient service days / Maximum available bed-days) x 100 = (5,400 / 6,000) x 100 = 90.0%. ALOS = Total inpatient service days / Total discharges (including deaths) = 5,400 / 900 = 6.0 days.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00780","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Health Data Governance, Medico-Legal \u0026 ABDM","topic":"NMC Regulations \u0026 ABDM Architecture","q":"A hospital Medical Records Department is updating its archival policy in India. How long must routine adult Inpatient (IPD) medical records be retained under NMC (formerly MCI) Code of Ethics regulations compared with Medico-Legal Case (MLC) records, and what data exchange standard and consent mechanism govern sharing via the Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM)?","o":["IPD: 1 year; MLC: 3 years; ABDM uses unencrypted CSV push without patient consent","IPD: 6 months; MLC: 2 years; ABDM stores all clinical records permanently in a single central NHA database","IPD: minimum 3 years from commencement of treatment; MLC: preserved permanently/until final legal disposal; ABDM uses HL7 FHIR R4 with explicit electronic patient consent via Consent Manager","IPD: 10 years; MLC: 5 years; ABDM uses DICOM only with verbal doctor-to-doctor consent"],"a":2,"e":"Under NMC (MCI) Professional Conduct, Etiquette and Ethics Regulations, hospitals must maintain indoor patient records in standard proforma for at least 3 years from commencement of treatment (and furnish them within 72 hours upon request), whereas MLC records, birth/death registers, and pending litigation files are preserved indefinitely/permanently. ABDM uses federated architecture (no central clinical repository) exchanging structured data in HL7 FHIR R4 format only after explicit electronic patient consent through a Consent Manager.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00781","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Health Informatics \u0026 Interoperability Standards","topic":"LOINC, SNOMED CT, and DICOM","q":"Match the healthcare interoperability standard (List I) with its primary functional domain (List II): List I: 1. LOINC, 2. SNOMED CT, 3. DICOM, 4. HL7 FHIR. List II: A. Medical imaging storage and PACS transmission, B. Universal identifiers for laboratory tests and clinical observations, C. RESTful API web-based exchange of healthcare resources, D. Comprehensive polyhierarchical clinical terminology for diagnoses, findings, and procedures.","o":["1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D","1-D, 2-B, 3-C, 4-A","1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C","1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C"],"a":3,"e":"LOINC (Logical Observation Identifiers Names and Codes) provides universal codes for lab assays and clinical measurements (1-B). SNOMED CT is the global reference clinical terminology covering anatomy, diseases, and procedures (2-D). DICOM (Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine) governs radiology/pathology imaging storage and PACS transfer (3-A). HL7 FHIR enables modern RESTful JSON/XML clinical data exchange used in ABDM (4-C).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00782","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Hospital Mortality \u0026 Quality Statistics","topic":"Gross vs Net (Institutional) Death Rate","q":"During a calendar month, a hospital records 1,000 total inpatient discharges (including 35 total inpatient deaths). Upon review, 10 of those 35 deaths occurred within 48 hours of admission. What are the Gross Death Rate and the Net (Institutional) Death Rate respectively?","o":["Gross = 3.50%; Net = 2.53%","Gross = 3.50%; Net = 2.50%","Gross = 2.50%; Net = 3.50%","Gross = 3.63%; Net = 2.53%"],"a":0,"e":"Gross Death Rate = (Total deaths / Total discharges including deaths) x 100 = (35 / 1,000) x 100 = 3.50%. Net (Institutional) Death Rate excludes deaths occurring \u003c 48 hours after admission from BOTH numerator and denominator: [(35 - 10) / (1,000 - 10)] x 100 = (25 / 990) x 100 = 2.525% (~2.53%).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00783","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Healthcare Law, Ethics \u0026 Confidentiality","topic":"Patient Privacy \u0026 Statutory Exceptions","q":"The human resources manager of a private company visits a hospital Medical Records Department requesting a copy of an employee\u0027s psychiatric inpatient discharge summary to verify sick-leave eligibility, without bringing signed patient authorization or a court order. What is the legally compliant HIM action?","o":["Provide the diagnosis code only under the hospital\u0027s internal administrative discretion","Refuse disclosure; medical records are strictly confidential and cannot be released to an employer or third party without explicit written informed consent from the patient or a valid court/statutory order","Release a redacted summary immediately to protect the patient\u0027s employment status","Release the record verbally over the phone without handing over paper copies"],"a":1,"e":"Under NMC Professional Conduct Regulations, the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 (India), and healthcare privacy law, all clinical records—especially sensitive psychiatric data—are privileged. Disclosure to employers, insurers, or family members of a competent adult requires explicit written patient authorization or a valid judicial/statutory mandate (such as notifiable infectious diseases or MLC requisitions).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00784","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Healthcare Reimbursement \u0026 Casemix Systems","topic":"Case Mix Index (CMI)","q":"In hospital health information analytics and insurance reimbursement (DRG-based systems), how is a hospital\u0027s Case Mix Index (CMI) defined and interpreted?","o":["Total number of surgical procedures divided by total ICU beds","Ratio of total hospital nurses to licensed beds","The average DRG relative weight across all discharged inpatients (Sum of DRG weights / Total discharges), reflecting overall clinical severity, complexity, and resource intensity","Percentage of outpatient visits converted to inpatient admissions"],"a":2,"e":"Case Mix Index (CMI) = Sum of DRG relative weights for all discharges / Total number of discharges. A CMI \u003e 1.0 indicates a patient population more clinically complex and resource-intensive than the national/baseline average, directly influenced by accurate ICD/procedure coding of comorbidities and complications (CC/MCC).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00785","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Electronic Health Records (EHR) \u0026 Legal Integrity","topic":"Amendments, Addenda \u0026 Audit Trails","q":"Two days after electronically signing an inpatient progress note, a resident discovers that a medication allergy was documented on the wrong patient\u0027s chart in the hospital EHR. Under NABH and medico-legal HIM documentation standards, how must this error be corrected?","o":["Delete the signed note completely from the database so no trace remains","Ask the IT administrator to silently overwrite the timestamp and original text","Leave the incorrect allergy unaddressed in the chart to avoid drawing attention","Create a time-stamped, author-attributed electronic addendum/amendment explaining the error while preserving the original entry and an immutable system audit trail"],"a":3,"e":"Legal medical record integrity requires that signed entries—electronic or paper—never be erased, obliterated, or silently overwritten. In an EHR, corrections are entered as a linked, time-stamped addendum/amendment clearly identifying the author and reason for change, while maintaining the original text and an unalterable audit trail per NABH IMS standards and the Indian Evidence/Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam digital record rules.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00786","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Hospital Utilization \u0026 Obstetric Statistics","topic":"Cesarean Section Rate \u0026 Bed Turnover Rate","q":"During a calendar month, a hospital records 400 total deliveries (including 100 cesarean sections) across its obstetric unit, and 800 total inpatient discharges (including deaths) across its 200-bed facility. What are the Total Cesarean Section Rate and the monthly Bed Turnover Rate (BTR)?","o":["Cesarean Section Rate = 25.0%; BTR = 4.0 patients/bed","Cesarean Section Rate = 20.0%; BTR = 2.0 patients/bed","Cesarean Section Rate = 40.0%; BTR = 0.25 patients/bed","Cesarean Section Rate = 25.0%; BTR = 8.0 patients/bed"],"a":0,"e":"Total Cesarean Section Rate = (Total C-section deliveries / Total deliveries) x 100 = (100 / 400) x 100 = 25.0%. Monthly Bed Turnover Rate (BTR) = Total discharges (including deaths) / Bed complement = 800 / 200 = 4.0 patients per bed for that month (indicating each hospital bed served an average of 4 patients during the month).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00787","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Digital Health \u0026 ABDM Ecosystem (India)","topic":"ABHA ID, HPR \u0026 HFR","q":"In India\u0027s Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM) digital health architecture operated by the National Health Authority (NHA), what are the three foundational registries/identifiers linking patients, doctors/allied professionals, and hospitals across interoperable EHRs?","o":["10-digit PAN, MCI Paper Register, and Census Code","14-digit ABHA (Ayushman Bharat Health Account) number for patients, Healthcare Professionals Registry (HPR) for doctors/nurses/allied health professionals, and Health Facility Registry (HFR) for hospitals/clinics/labs","12-digit Aadhaar stored directly inside public discharge PDFs without encryption","16-digit credit card token, ICD-10 index, and PACS AE Title"],"a":1,"e":"ABDM builds federated digital health interoperability around: (1) a randomly generated 14-digit ABHA number (plus user-friendly ABHA address) uniquely identifying patients to link longitudinal health records with consent; (2) HPR, a comprehensive verified registry of medical, nursing, AYUSH, and allied healthcare practitioners; and (3) HFR, the national repository of public and private healthcare facilities.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00788","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Clinical Coding \u0026 ICD-10 Conventions","topic":"Dagger (†) and Asterisk (*) Coding","q":"A medical coder is coding an inpatient discharge diagnosis of \u0027Type 1 diabetes mellitus with proliferative diabetic retinopathy\u0027 using WHO ICD-10 Volume 1 \u0026 2. Under the ICD-10 Dagger (†) and Asterisk (*) dual classification convention, which rule governs the dagger code (E10.3†) versus the asterisk code (H36.0*)?","o":["The asterisk (*) code is used alone for primary mortality statistics; the dagger (†) code is optional","The dagger (†) code identifies the underlying etiology (E10.3†) and MUST be used as the primary statistical code, whereas the asterisk (*) code identifies the anatomical manifestation (H36.0*) and may NEVER be used alone","Both dagger and asterisk codes are omitted whenever a complication is present","Asterisk codes apply only to surgical procedures and never to medical diagnoses"],"a":1,"e":"WHO ICD-10 uses the dagger-and-asterisk convention to represent conditions having both an underlying generalized disease (etiology, marked †) and a localized organ manifestation (marked *). The dagger code is always mandatory and serves as the primary code for single-condition morbidity/mortality tabulation, while the asterisk code provides supplementary organ-specific detail and can never stand alone.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00789","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Health Data Privacy, Research Governance \u0026 Security","topic":"De-Identification \u0026 Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)","q":"A surgical resident requests the Hospital Information Management department to export a spreadsheet of 2,500 ICU sepsis patients—including patient names, Aadhaar/ABHA numbers, exact birthdates, mobile numbers, and full residential addresses—to a personal USB drive for a retrospective research paper without Institutional Ethics Committee (IEC) approval. How must the HIM officer respond?","o":["Export all identifiable fields immediately to support academic publishing","Print the 2,500 full charts on paper instead of using a USB drive","Deny raw identifiable export; require formal Institutional Ethics Committee (IEC) approval/waiver, enforce Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), and release only a de-identified/anonymized dataset (stripping direct and quasi-identifiers) over secure encrypted institutional storage","Replace patient names with their mobile phone numbers so the file is \u0027anonymous\u0027"],"a":2,"e":"Under ICMR National Ethical Guidelines for Biomedical and Health Research, ABDM Health Data Management Policy, and India\u0027s Digital Personal Data Protection (DPDP) principles, secondary research access requires IEC clearance, minimum-necessary de-identification (removing direct identifiers like names, phone numbers, MRNs, ABHA/Aadhaar, and generalizing dates/zip codes to prevent re-identification), and secure RBAC-governed infrastructure rather than unencrypted personal USB drives.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00790","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Vital Statistics \u0026 Mortality Documentation","topic":"WHO Form 4 / 4A Causal Sequencing","q":"When auditing a Medical Certificate of Cause of Death (MCCD, Form 4/4A under India\u0027s Civil Registration System following WHO ICD-10 standards), where MUST the certifying physician enter the \u0027Underlying Cause of Death\u0027 (the disease or injury that initiated the lethal chain of morbid events), and which entry is invalid as a sole Part I cause?","o":["In Part II alongside incidental conditions; pneumonia is invalid","Top line (a) of Part I; acute myocardial infarction is invalid","Only on the hospital discharge billing summary","On the LOWEST completed line of Part I (following the causal chain backward from immediate cause on line \u0027a\u0027); modes of dying such as \u0027cardiorespiratory arrest\u0027 or \u0027heart failure\u0027 alone without an underlying etiology are invalid"],"a":3,"e":"In WHO Form 4/4A, Part I traces the direct causal sequence backward: Line (a) = immediate/terminal disease (e.g., Acute renal failure), due to Line (b) = antecedent cause (e.g., Septic shock), due to Line (c) = Underlying Cause of Death (e.g., Perforated appendicitis). Part II lists other significant contributing conditions (e.g., Diabetes). Nonspecific mechanisms/modes of death (\u0027cardiorespiratory arrest\u0027, \u0027asphyxia\u0027) lack epidemiological utility and must never appear without their underlying etiology.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00791","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Hospital Census \u0026 Departmental Statistics","topic":"Average Daily Census (ADC) \u0026 ICU Occupancy","q":"A 300-bed multi-specialty hospital (including a 10-bed surgical ICU) records 7,200 total hospital inpatient service days during a 30-day month, of which 300 inpatient service days occurred in the 10-bed ICU. What are the hospital\u0027s Average Daily Inpatient Census (ADC) and the ICU\u0027s Bed Occupancy Rate for that month?","o":["Hospital ADC = 240 patients/day; ICU Occupancy = 100.0%","Hospital ADC = 210 patients/day; ICU Occupancy = 100.0%","Hospital ADC = 300 patients/day; ICU Occupancy = 80.0%","Hospital ADC = 240 patients/day; ICU Occupancy = 90.0%"],"a":0,"e":"Average Daily Inpatient Census (ADC) = Total inpatient service days in period / Number of calendar days in period = 7,200 / 30 = 240 patients/day. Maximum ICU bed-days = 10 ICU beds x 30 days = 300 bed-days. ICU Bed Occupancy Rate = (300 / 300) x 100 = 100.0%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00792","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Public Health Registries \u0026 Statutory Reporting (India)","topic":"RBD Act 1969 \u0026 ICD-O-3 Oncology Coding","q":"A tertiary cancer center\u0027s Medical Records and Registry department is reviewing statutory compliance in India. Under the Registration of Births and Deaths (RBD) Act, 1969, within how many days must institutional births and deaths be reported to the local Registrar without late penalty, and which specialized WHO classification system is used by ICMR-NCDIR Hospital-Based Cancer Registries (HBCR) to code both tumor anatomic site and histologic behavior?","o":["90 days; SNOMED CT only without morphology behavior digits","21 days; ICD-O-3 (International Classification of Diseases for Oncology, 3rd Ed.) coding Topography (C-codes) and Morphology (histology + behavior /0, /1, /2, /3, /6)","7 days; CPT-4 surgical codes","365 days; DSM-5 classification"],"a":1,"e":"Under India\u0027s RBD Act 1969 (and 2023 amendment), births and deaths occurring in hospitals must be reported by the medical officer/institution within 21 days. For oncology registries under ICMR-NCDIR (NCRP), ICD-O-3 provides a dual-axis code: 4-character Topography (anatomical primary site, e.g., C50.9) and 5-digit Morphology (4-digit cell type + 1-digit behavior code: /0 benign, /1 uncertain, /2 in situ, /3 primary malignant, /6 metastatic).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00793","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Medical Record Quality \u0026 NABH Standards","topic":"Discharge Summary Content \u0026 Delinquency Tracking","q":"Per NABH Accreditation Standards for Hospitals (Information Management System - IMS \u0026 AAC chapters), when MUST an inpatient Discharge Summary be provided to the patient/family, and which core clinical elements are mandatory?","o":["Contains solely ICD-10 numerical codes without plain-language medication instructions","Mailed 60 days after discharge; contains only hospital bill and room number","Provided at the time of discharge (signed by the treating team); must include reason for admission, significant findings/diagnosis, procedures/treatments performed, condition at discharge, discharge medication instructions, follow-up schedule, and urgent warning signs","Issued only upon RTI legal application"],"a":2,"e":"NABH AAC/IMS standards require a comprehensive, legible, authenticated Discharge Summary delivered AT THE TIME OF DISCHARGE (including for DAMA/LAMA patients) detailing clinical summary, investigations, interventions, medication reconciliation, follow-up plan, and emergency red-flag instructions. In HIM record management, incomplete inpatient charts remaining unsigned past the institutional deadline (typically 15-30 days) are tracked as the \u0027record delinquency rate.\u0027","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00794","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Healthcare Quality \u0026 Infection Surveillance Metrics","topic":"CLABSI Rate per 1,000 Central-Line Days","q":"During a quarter, a 20-bed medical ICU records 400 patient admissions, 2,500 total central-line days, and 5 laboratory-confirmed Central Line-Associated Bloodstream Infections (CLABSIs). What is the standardized ICU CLABSI rate reported for ICMR/NABH quality benchmarking?","o":["5.0 per 1,000 central-line days","0.20 per 1,000 central-line days","1.25 per 100 admissions","2.0 per 1,000 central-line days"],"a":3,"e":"Standardized device-associated HAI rates adjust for duration of device exposure rather than raw admissions: CLABSI Rate = (Number of CLABSI episodes / Total central-line days) x 1,000 = (5 / 2,500) x 1,000 = 2.0 infections per 1,000 central-line days (analogous to VAP per 1,000 ventilator-days and CAUTI per 1,000 catheter-days).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00795","course":"B.Sc Health Information Management","subject":"Master Patient Index (MPI) \u0026 Data Integrity","topic":"Overlay (Commingled) vs Duplicate Medical Records","q":"In a hospital EHR Master Patient Index (MPI), Patient X\u0027s penicillin allergy and abdominal CT scan are inadvertently entered under Patient Y\u0027s existing Medical Record Number (MRN) because both share the same common name—creating an \u0027OVERLAY (commingled) record\u0027—whereas Patient Z accidentally gets two separate MRNs (\u0027DUPLICATE record\u0027). Why is an overlay record far more acutely hazardous than a duplicate record, and how must HIM resolve it?","o":["Duplicate records are more dangerous; delete both MRNs permanently","An overlay mixes two distinct patients\u0027 clinical data inside one chart (risking wrong-patient surgery, drug allergy harm, or blood transfusion fatality); HIM must perform immediate EHR \u0027chart split/unmerge\u0027 verification using \u003e= 2-3 unique identifiers (full name, DOB, gender, ABHA/mobile), freeze affected orders, and notify treating clinicians","Overlays only affect hospital billing invoices and require no clinical notification","Simply rename Patient Y\u0027s chart to Patient X without separating prior notes"],"a":1,"e":"While duplicate MRNs fragment one patient\u0027s history across two charts (resolved by careful record merging), an OVERLAY (commingled) record contaminates one patient\u0027s chart with another human\u0027s blood group, allergies, imaging, and pathology—posing immediate lethal patient-safety risk. HIM MPI governance must halt erroneous clinical orders, alert clinicians, audit every timestamped encounter using \u003e= 2-3 independent identifiers, and execute a formal chart split.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00796","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Clinical Chemistry \u0026 Quality Management","topic":"Westgard Multirules","q":"On a Levey-Jennings chart for serum creatinine QC (Level 1 and Level 2), the technologist observes that Level 1 exceeds +2.4 SD and Level 2 on the same analytical run exceeds +2.6 SD. Under Westgard multirule criteria, which rule is violated and what type of analytical error does it signify?","o":["2_2s rejection rule; systematic error (calibration shift or reagent deterioration)","10_x warning rule; acceptable statistical variance","R_4s rejection rule; random imprecision between levels","1_2s rejection rule; random pipetting error"],"a":0,"e":"When two control observations across different levels within the same run (or two consecutive runs of the same level) both exceed the same +2 SD or -2 SD limit, the 2_2s rejection rule is violated. This indicates a systematic error (bias/shift), requiring calibration check, reagent inspection, and withholding patient results.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00797","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Molecular Diagnostics","topic":"Real-Time Quantitative PCR (qPCR)","q":"During a SYBR Green real-time PCR assay, a clinical sample yields a Cycle Threshold (Ct) of 22, while the No-Template Control (NTC) shows a late sigmoidal curve with Ct = 36. Post-amplification melt-curve analysis shows a single peak at Tm = 84.5 C for the clinical sample and a single peak at Tm = 72.0 C for the NTC. How should the technologist interpret the NTC signal?","o":["High genomic DNA template overload in the NTC well","Primer-dimer formation in the NTC (lower Tm than specific amplicon); the clinical sample peak at 84.5 C is valid","Probe hydrolysis failure due to Taq polymerase degradation","Specific target amplicon contamination in the NTC; reject the entire run"],"a":1,"e":"SYBR Green binds any double-stranded DNA nonspecifically. Short primer-dimers melt at a noticeably lower temperature (72.0 C) than the longer, specific target amplicon (84.5 C). Because the NTC shows only a low-Tm primer-dimer peak and no 84.5 C peak, there is no target contamination and the patient result (Ct 22, Tm 84.5 C) is valid.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00798","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Immunohematology \u0026 Advanced Blood Banking","topic":"ABO Discrepancies \u0026 Rare Phenotypes","q":"A 32-year-old woman scheduled for surgery types as Group O on routine forward grouping (no agglutination with anti-A or anti-B) and Group O on reverse grouping (4+ agglutination with A1 cells and B cells). However, her serum causes 4+ agglutination with all Group O donor red cells at immediate spin and AHG phase, and her red cells show 0 agglutination with Ulex europaeus lectin (anti-H). Which blood group phenotype is this, and what blood can she safely receive?","o":["Acquired B phenomenon; transfuse Group A PRBCs","A2 subgroup with anti-A1; transfuse Group O PRBCs","Bombay (Oh) phenotype lacking H antigen with potent anti-H; transfuse only Oh (Bombay) blood","Cold autoantibody interference; pre-warm Group O PRBCs for transfusion"],"a":2,"e":"Individuals with the Bombay (Oh, hh) phenotype cannot synthesize the precursor H antigen on RBCs (hence negative with anti-H Ulex europaeus lectin) and produce naturally occurring, complement-binding IgM/IgG anti-H in addition to anti-A and anti-B. Because all normal O, A, B, and AB donor RBCs express H antigen, they are incompatible; only rare Oh donor or autologous blood is safe.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00799","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Clinical Enzymology","topic":"Macroamylasemia vs Acute Pancreatitis","q":"An asymptomatic 48-year-old man on routine health screening has a persistently elevated serum amylase of 520 U/L (reference \u003c100 U/L), normal serum lipase (32 U/L), normal abdominal ultrasound, and an extremely low urinary amylase-to-creatinine clearance ratio (ACCR \u003c 1%). Which laboratory phenomenon explains these findings?","o":["Salivary gland adenocarcinoma secreting low-molecular-weight amylase","Diabetic ketoacidosis causing increased renal clearance of amylase","Acute necrotizing pancreatitis with renal tubular necrosis","Macroamylasemia (immunoglobulin-bound polymeric amylase complexes too large for glomerular filtration)"],"a":3,"e":"Macroamylase forms when pancreatic or salivary amylase binds circulating IgA or IgG (\u003e 150-200 kDa). Because the high-molecular-weight complex cannot cross the glomerular basement membrane, serum amylase remains persistently elevated while urinary amylase is low (ACCR \u003c 1%, vs \u003e 5% in acute pancreatitis) and serum lipase stays normal.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00800","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Diagnostic Flow Cytometry \u0026 Immunopathology","topic":"Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria (PNH)","q":"A patient with Coombs-negative intravascular hemolysis, morning dark urine, and hepatic vein thrombosis undergoes peripheral blood flow cytometry. Which surface marker abnormality on granulocytes and red blood cells confirms Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria (PNH)?","o":["Loss or marked deficiency of GPI-anchored complement regulatory proteins CD55 and CD59 (and absent FLAER binding on neutrophils/monocytes)","Absence of CD34 and MPO on bone marrow myeloblasts","Overexpression of CD19, CD20, and CD5 on B-lymphocytes","Decreased expression of Spectrin and Ankyrin on osmotic fragility testing"],"a":0,"e":"PNH arises from an acquired somatic PIGA gene mutation in hematopoietic stem cells, halting synthesis of the glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor. Consequently, cells lack GPI-linked complement inhibitors CD55 (DAF) and CD59 (MIRL), rendering RBCs susceptible to complement-mediated lysis; flow cytometry using anti-CD55/CD59 and FLAER (fluorescein-labeled proaerolysin) on granulocytes is the diagnostic gold standard.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00801","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Hemostasis \u0026 Coagulation Diagnostics","topic":"aPTT Mixing Studies","q":"A patient\u0027s isolated prolonged aPTT (68 s; normal 26-36 s) corrects completely to 33 s immediately upon 1:1 mixing with normal pooled plasma, but prolongs again to 58 s after incubating the 1:1 mixture at 37 C for 2 hours. What is the most likely hemostatic abnormality?","o":["Pre-analytical heparin contamination of the collection tube","Time- and temperature-dependent acquired Factor VIII inhibitor (autoantibody)","Congenital Factor VIII deficiency (Hemophilia A)","Factor VII deficiency affecting the extrinsic pathway"],"a":1,"e":"Factor VIII inhibitors are typically IgG autoantibodies that neutralize Factor VIII in a time- and temperature-dependent manner. Therefore, an immediate 1:1 mixing study may falsely appear to correct, whereas incubating the mixture at 37 C for 1-2 hours allows the antibody to inactivate the added Factor VIII, causing re-prolongation of the aPTT.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00802","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Analytical Instrumentation \u0026 Spectrophotometry","topic":"Beer-Lambert Law \u0026 Calibration Filters","q":"On a UV-visible clinical chemistry spectrophotometer, a standard serum blank/calibrator reads 10.0% Transmittance (%T). Using the Beer-Lambert relationship [A = 2 - log10(%T)], what is the corresponding Absorbance (A), and which solid optical standard is routinely used to verify WAVELENGTH accuracy?","o":["Absorbance = 0.100; neutral density glass filter","Absorbance = 0.500; tungsten filament lamp","Absorbance = 1.000; holmium oxide or didymium glass filter","Absorbance = 2.000; potassium dichromate solution"],"a":2,"e":"Absorbance A = -log10(T) = 2 - log10(%T). For %T = 10.0%, log10(10) = 1.0, so A = 2 - 1.0 = 1.000. In clinical chemistry instrumentation QA, holmium oxide (sharp UV-visible peaks) and didymium glass filters check wavelength accuracy, whereas potassium dichromate or neutral density filters verify photometric (absorbance) linearity/accuracy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00803","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Clinical Protein Chemistry","topic":"SPEP Pathological Patterns","q":"A 38-year-old patient with generalized anasarca and 6.2 g/day proteinuria undergoes Serum Protein Electrophoresis (SPEP). The densitometer tracing shows a markedly diminished albumin peak, a prominently elevated, sharp alpha-2 globulin peak, and reduced gamma-globulin levels. Which condition produces this characteristic SPEP profile?","o":["Acute-phase bacterial pneumonia alone","Decompensated hepatic cirrhosis (beta-gamma bridging)","Multiple myeloma (IgG kappa)","Nephrotic syndrome (selective retention of large alpha-2-macroglobulin while smaller albumin and IgG are lost in urine)"],"a":3,"e":"In nephrotic syndrome, glomerular damage leaks low- and intermediate-molecular-weight proteins (albumin ~66 kDa, transferrin, IgG) into urine while selectively retaining high-molecular-weight alpha-2-macroglobulin (~725 kDa) whose hepatic synthesis increases—producing low albumin, low gamma, and a towering alpha-2 peak.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00804","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Laboratory Biostatistics \u0026 Method Evaluation","topic":"Predictive Values \u0026 Disease Prevalence","q":"A rapid screening ELISA has a fixed analytical sensitivity of 95% and specificity of 95%. When the laboratory deploys this exact same assay in a low-prevalence general community screening clinic (prevalence 1%) versus a high-prevalence tertiary referral clinic (prevalence 40%), how do the Positive Predictive Value (PPV) and Negative Predictive Value (NPV) change?","o":["In the low-prevalence (1%) population, PPV drops markedly while NPV rises; in the high-prevalence (40%) population, PPV is much higher","In the low-prevalence population, PPV rises to 99% while NPV drops to 16%","PPV remains constant at 95% regardless of disease prevalence","Sensitivity and specificity drop to 50% in the low-prevalence clinic"],"a":0,"e":"Sensitivity and specificity are intrinsic test parameters independent of disease prevalence. By contrast, Positive Predictive Value [PPV = TP / (TP + FP)] depends strongly on pre-test prevalence: at 1% prevalence with 95% specificity, false positives outnumber true positives (~5% vs ~0.95%, yielding PPV ~16%!), whereas at 40% prevalence PPV exceeds 92%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00805","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Immunochemistry \u0026 Automated Immunoassays","topic":"High-Dose Hook Effect","q":"A patient with a massive pituitary macroadenoma and classic galactorrhea has serum prolactin measured on a one-step chemiluminescent sandwich immunoassay, returning a paradoxically low-normal result of 24 ng/mL (or a hydatidiform mole patient shows falsely low beta-hCG). Suspecting the \u0027High-Dose Hook Effect,\u0027 how does the laboratory scientist confirm and obtain the true concentration?","o":["Add exogenous mouse IgG to precipitate heterophile antibodies only","Perform a 1:10 and 1:100 serial dilution of the serum with assay diluent and re-assay; a dramatic surge in calculated concentration (e.g., to \u003e 5,000 ng/mL) confirms the hook effect","Repeat the undiluted sample on the same analyzer without modification","Heat-inactivate the serum at 80 C for 30 minutes prior to re-running"],"a":1,"e":"In a one-step immunometric sandwich assay, extreme analyte excess (e.g., prolactin \u003e 1,000-5,000 ng/mL or beta-hCG in molar pregnancy) saturates solid-phase capture antibodies and labeled detection antibodies separately, preventing \u0027sandwich\u0027 bridge formation and yielding a falsely low signal (\u0027hook effect\u0027). Serial dilution (1:10, 1:100) restores optimal antibody-to-antigen stoichiometry and unmasks the true massive elevation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00806","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Diagnostic Cytogenetics \u0026 Molecular Oncology","topic":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)","q":"A 44-year-old man with massive splenomegaly has WBC 145,000/uL displaying the full myeloid maturation spectrum (myelocytes, metamyelocytes, bands, neutrophils), 6% basophils, and markedly reduced Leukocyte Alkaline Phosphatase (LAP) score. Which chromosomal translocation and molecular assay confirm the diagnosis and monitor Major Molecular Response (MMR \u003c= 0.1% IS) during tyrosine kinase inhibitor therapy?","o":["t(8;14) MYC rearrangement; monitored by serum protein electrophoresis","JAK2 V617F point mutation only; monitored by periodic bone marrow reticulin stain alone","t(9;22)(q34;q11) Philadelphia chromosome creating the BCR-ABL1 fusion oncogene; diagnosed by FISH/karyotype and monitored longitudinally by quantitative RT-qPCR on the International Scale (IS)","t(15;17)(q24;q21) PML-RARA; monitored by osmotic fragility"],"a":2,"e":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is defined by the balanced reciprocal translocation t(9;22)(q34;q11) (Philadelphia chromosome), fusing BCR on chromosome 22 with ABL1 on chromosome 9 to produce a constitutively active p210 tyrosine kinase. FISH/karyotyping confirms the translocation, while peripheral blood quantitative RT-qPCR for BCR-ABL1 transcripts standardized to the International Scale (IS) tracks deep molecular responses (MMR \u003c= 0.1% IS).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00807","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Laboratory Quality Management (ISO 15189 / NABL)","topic":"Pre-Analytical Errors \u0026 Delta Checks","q":"Across the total testing process in clinical diagnostic laboratories, which phase accounts for the LARGEST proportion (~60-70%) of all laboratory errors, and what is the primary purpose of an automated Laboratory Information System (LIS) \u0027Delta Check\u0027?","o":["Analytical phase; calibrates spectrophotometer cuvettes automatically","Post-analytical phase; deletes critical panic values before clinician notification","Post-analytical phase; verifies centrifuge RPM","Pre-analytical phase (e.g., patient misidentification, hemolysis, wrong tube, insufficient volume); a Delta Check compares a patient\u0027s current result with their recent previous result to flag specimen mislabeling (\u0027Wrong Blood in Tube\u0027) or sudden analytical shift"],"a":3,"e":"Studies across ISO 15189 / NABL laboratories consistently demonstrate that 60-70% of errors arise in the pre-analytical phase (patient prep, identification, phlebotomy, tube selection, transport). An LIS Delta Check compares a current analyte (especially stable parameters like MCV, creatinine, or sodium) against the patient\u0027s prior value within a defined time window to catch sample mix-ups (\u0027Wrong Blood in Tube\u0027) or IV-fluid contamination before release.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00808","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Clinical Immunology \u0026 Autoimmune Serology","topic":"HEp-2 Indirect Immunofluorescence (IIF) Patterns","q":"During HEp-2 cell Indirect Immunofluorescence (IIF) ANA screening, Serum A shows a HOMOGENEOUS (diffuse) nuclear fluorescence including strong staining of condensed metaphase chromosomes, whereas Serum B shows 46 discrete dotted speckles aligned along the metaphase plate. Which autoantibody and clinical syndrome correlate with Serum A and Serum B respectively?","o":["Serum A: Anti-dsDNA / anti-histone (Systemic Lupus Erythematosus / drug-induced lupus); Serum B: Anti-centromere (Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis / CREST syndrome)","Serum A: Anti-centromere (CREST); Serum B: Anti-mitochondrial (PBC)","Serum A: Anti-Scl-70 nucleolar; Serum B: c-ANCA (granulomatosis with polyangiitis)","Serum A: Anti-transglutaminase; Serum B: Anti-CCP (rheumatoid arthritis)"],"a":0,"e":"On HEp-2 IIF, diffuse staining of interphase nuclei AND condensed chromatin on mitotic metaphase plates defines the Homogeneous pattern (anti-dsDNA, anti-nucleosome, anti-histone -\u003e SLE and drug-induced lupus). Discrete uniform speckles clustering along the centromeres of mitotic chromosomes define the Centromere pattern (AC-3 -\u003e limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis / CREST syndrome).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00809","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Clinical Chemistry Instrumentation","topic":"Direct vs Indirect Ion-Selective Electrodes (ISE)","q":"A patient with newly diagnosed IgG multiple myeloma (total serum protein 13.5 g/dL) and lipemia (triglycerides 2,400 mg/dL) has serum Na+ = 122 mEq/L measured on a central-lab automated chemistry analyzer (Indirect ISE), yet measured serum osmolality is normal (286 mOsm/kg) and a whole-blood arterial gas analyzer (Direct ISE) reads Na+ = 138 mEq/L. Why did the central-lab Indirect ISE produce pseudohyponatremia?","o":["IgG paraprotein destroys glass sodium membranes in Direct ISE electrodes","Indirect ISE predilutes a fixed total serum volume (assuming 93% water / 7% solids); excessive lipids/proteins displace plasma water fraction downward (electrolyte exclusion effect), causing the fixed dilution factor to underestimate true aqueous Na+ concentration","Direct ISE binds sodium to triglycerides causing falsely elevated results","Hyperglycemia shifted intracellular water out of erythrocytes"],"a":1,"e":"Normally plasma is ~93% water and ~7% lipids/proteins. Indirect ISE pipettes a fixed volume of whole plasma and dilutes it ~1:20-1:30 assuming 93% water; when severe hypertriglyceridemia or hyperproteinemia expands the solid non-aqueous volume to 15-20%, less plasma water is aspirated per aliquot, yielding falsely low calculated Na+ (\u0027pseudohyponatremia\u0027 with normal osmolality). Direct ISE measures undiluted plasma water activity directly, reporting true physiological Na+ (138 mEq/L).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00810","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Renal \u0026 Body Fluid Chemistry","topic":"Fractional Excretion of Sodium (FENa)","q":"An oliguric patient with acute kidney injury (off diuretics) has spot Urine Na+ = 10 mEq/L, Plasma Na+ = 140 mEq/L, Urine Creatinine = 100 mg/dL, and Plasma Creatinine = 2.0 mg/dL. Using FENa (%) = [(UNa x PCr) / (PNa x UCr)] x 100, what is the Fractional Excretion of Sodium and its diagnostic interpretation?","o":["FENa = 0.014%; consistent with loop diuretic effect","FENa = 7.14%; consistent with post-renal obstruction","FENa = 0.14% (\u003c 1%); consistent with prerenal azotemia (intact tubular sodium avidity)","FENa = 1.40%; consistent with intrinsic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN)"],"a":2,"e":"FENa (%) = [(UNa x PCr) / (PNa x UCr)] x 100 = [(10 x 2.0) / (140 x 100)] x 100 = (20 / 14,000) x 100 = 0.14%. A FENa \u003c 1% indicates avid aldosterone/angiotensin-II-mediated proximal and distal tubular sodium reabsorption characteristic of prerenal hypoperfusion (whereas ATN damages tubular epithelial reabsorption, raising FENa \u003e 2%).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00811","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Endocrine Clinical Chemistry","topic":"Biotin Interference in Thyroid Function Tests","q":"An asymptomatic euthyroid woman taking high-dose over-the-counter Biotin (10 mg/day for hair loss) has thyroid tests performed on a streptavidin-biotin automated chemiluminescent platform: TSH \u003c 0.01 mIU/L (very low) and Free T4 = 3.8 ng/dL (markedly elevated), falsely mimicking severe hyperthyroidism. Why does excess biotin push sandwich TSH falsely DOWN and competitive Free T4 falsely UP?","o":["Biotin converts T4 into reverse T3 inside the vacutainer tube","Biotin precipitates Free T4 out of serum directly","Biotin physically stimulates thyroid follicular TSH receptors in vivo","Free biotin saturates streptavidin binding sites on solid-phase beads: in a sandwich assay (TSH), fewer labeled complexes bind giving a FALSELY LOW signal, whereas in a competitive assay (Free T4), reduced labeled-T4 binding produces a FALSELY HIGH inverse signal"],"a":3,"e":"Many commercial immunoassays use biotinylated capture antibodies anchored to streptavidin-coated magnetic beads. Supraphysiologic serum biotin occupies streptavidin sites, blocking capture complex attachment. Because signal is directly proportional to analyte in sandwich assays (TSH, troponin, PTH), blocked binding causes falsely LOW TSH; in competitive assays (FT4, FT3, vitamin D) where signal is inversely proportional to analyte, blocked tracer binding mimics high analyte—falsely elevating FT4/FT3 and mimicking Graves\u0027 disease! Holding biotin \u003e= 48-72 hours or using non-biotinylated assays resolves the error.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00812","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Automated Hematology \u0026 Peripheral Smear Correlation","topic":"EDTA-Dependent Pseudothrombocytopenia","q":"An asymptomatic preoperative patient has an automated CBC platelet count of 32,000/uL from a K2EDTA tube, triggering a platelet-clump analyzer flag. Peripheral smear confirms large platelet aggregates at the feathered edge and platelet satellitism around neutrophils. When the specimen is redrawn into a 3.2% sodium citrate (blue-top) tube, the analyzer reports 220,000/uL. What multiplier corrects for the 9:1 citrate liquid dilution, and what is the true platelet count?","o":["Multiply by 1.1 (10/9) -\u003e 242,000/uL, confirming benign EDTA-dependent pseudothrombocytopenia","No correction needed; transfuse 1 unit single-donor platelets immediately","Multiply by 2.0 -\u003e 440,000/uL","Multiply by 0.9 -\u003e 198,000/uL"],"a":0,"e":"EDTA-dependent pseudothrombocytopenia occurs when IgM/IgG cold autoantibodies bind cryptic GPIIb/IIIa epitopes exposed by EDTA calcium chelation, clumping platelets so impedance/optical counters undercount them (or misclassify giant clumps as WBCs). Redrawing into 3.2% sodium citrate prevents clumping; because citrate adds 1 part liquid anticoagulant to 9 parts whole blood, multiplying the raw citrate count by 10/9 (~1.11) yields the true platelet count (~242,000/uL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00813","course":"B.Sc Medical Laboratory Sciences","subject":"Clinical Molecular Diagnostics \u0026 Genomics","topic":"Sequencing Read Depth \u0026 Phred Q30 Score","q":"In clinical Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) for solid-tumor somatic oncology panels (detecting low-abundance mutations at 5% Variant Allele Frequency, VAF), what does a Phred Quality Score of Q30 signify, and why is much higher sequencing read depth (e.g., \u003e= 500x) required compared with inherited germline testing (~30-100x)?","o":["Somatic tumors require 1x whole-genome coverage exclusively","Q30 signifies 99.9% base-call accuracy (1 in 1,000 error probability); somatic tumor samples contain mixed normal/stromal DNA and tumor heterogeneity, requiring deep coverage (\u003e= 500x) to detect low-VAF (~5%) actionable variants reliably","Q30 equals 50% base error rate; somatic panels need lower depth (5x) than germline","Q30 measures DNA extraction yield in ng/uL; depth refers only to PCR cycle number"],"a":1,"e":"Phred quality score Q = -10 log10(P_error), so Q20 = 99% (1 in 100 error) and Q30 = 99.9% base accuracy (1 in 1,000 error). While germline heterozygous variants sit near 50% VAF (reliably called at 30-100x depth), FFPE tumor biopsies contain admixed normal cells and subclones, requiring \u003e= 500x unique read depth so a 5% VAF driver mutation (e.g., EGFR T790M) yields sufficient supporting reads above sequencing noise.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00814","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Systematic Bacteriology \u0026 Antimicrobial Susceptibility","topic":"Staphylococcus aureus Resistance Testing","q":"A catalase-positive, tube-coagulase-positive Gram-positive coccus isolated from a wound swab is tested on Mueller-Hinton agar per CLSI M100 guidelines to detect mecA-mediated methicillin resistance (MRSA). Which antimicrobial disk and interpretive zone diameter confirm MRSA?","o":["Vancomycin 30 ug disk; zone \u003c= 15 mm","Penicillin 10 units disk; zone \u003c= 28 mm","Cefoxitin 30 ug disk; zone \u003c= 21 mm","Novobiocin 5 ug disk; zone \u003c= 16 mm"],"a":2,"e":"Per CLSI M100, the cefoxitin 30 ug disk is the preferred phenotypic surrogate for detecting mecA-mediated oxacillin/methicillin resistance (PBP2a) in S. aureus because cefoxitin is a more potent inducer of the mecA gene than oxacillin. A zone diameter \u003c= 21 mm at 16-18 hours confirms MRSA (\u003e= 22 mm is MSSA).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00815","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Diagnostic Mycobacteriology \u0026 NTEP","topic":"Molecular vs Smear Diagnostics","q":"Under India\u0027s National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP), a sputum specimen from a presumptive pulmonary TB patient is negative on Ziehl-Neelsen (ZN) microscopy (using 20% H2SO4 decolorizer) but positive on CBNAAT (GeneXpert MTB/RIF) showing \u0027MTB detected, Rifampicin resistance detected\u0027. Why was CBNAAT more sensitive than ZN smear, and which genetic locus confers this rifampicin resistance?","o":["CBNAAT requires 10^6 bacilli/mL and targets the katG gene","CBNAAT detects mycobacterial mycolic acids and targets the embB gene","ZN smear failed because 20% H2SO4 is too weak for M. tuberculosis; CBNAAT targets inhA promoter","CBNAAT detects ~131 CFU/mL (vs ~10,000 bacilli/mL for ZN smear) and targets the 81-bp RRDR of the rpoB gene"],"a":3,"e":"Ziehl-Neelsen smear microscopy requires ~10,000 acid-fast bacilli/mL of sputum for reliable detection, whereas cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification testing (CBNAAT / Xpert MTB/RIF) has a limit of detection near 113-131 CFU/mL. It amplifies an 81-base-pair rifampicin resistance-determining region (RRDR) of the rpoB gene (encoding RNA polymerase beta-subunit), which accounts for \u003e95% of rifampicin resistance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00816","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Medical Mycology","topic":"Cryptococcus neoformans","q":"A CSF sample from an immunocompromised patient with meningitis demonstrates spherical, narrow-based budding yeast cells surrounded by a wide refractile halo on India ink wet mount. Which pair of confirmatory biochemical/culture findings characterizes Cryptococcus neoformans?","o":["Urease-positive; brown-black pigmented colonies on Niger seed (birdseed) agar due to phenoloxidase","Urease-positive; broad-based budding yeast at 37 C and tuberculate macroconidia at 25 C","Urease-negative; germ tube positive at 37 C","Urease-negative; pseudohyphae with chlamydospores on cornmeal agar"],"a":0,"e":"Cryptococcus neoformans possesses a thick glucuronoxylomannan polysaccharide capsule (producing the clear halo on India ink/nigrosin negative staining), produces urease, and expresses laccase (phenoloxidase), which converts caffeic acid in Niger seed (birdseed) agar into melanin, yielding characteristic brown-black colonies.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00817","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Clinical Virology \u0026 Serology","topic":"Hepatitis B Serological Profiles","q":"A pre-employment blood donor screening panel returns: HBsAg = Negative, Anti-HBs = Positive (145 mIU/mL), IgM Anti-HBc = Negative, Total Anti-HBc (IgG) = Positive, and HBV DNA = Undetectable. How must the medical microbiologist interpret this serological profile?","o":["Chronic active Hepatitis B carrier state with high infectivity","Immunity secondary to resolved natural Hepatitis B infection","Immunity secondary to Hepatitis B vaccination","Acute Hepatitis B infection in the serological \u0027window period\u0027"],"a":1,"e":"Vaccination contains only recombinant HBsAg surface protein, eliciting Anti-HBs positivity alone with negative Anti-HBc. By contrast, natural infection exposes the immune system to the viral core antigen (HBcAg) as well; therefore, HBsAg negative + Anti-HBs positive + IgG Anti-HBc positive confirms immunity following recovery from past natural HBV infection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00818","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Diagnostic Parasitology \u0026 Vector-Borne Diseases","topic":"Visceral Leishmaniasis (Kala-Azar)","q":"A 29-year-old man from rural Bihar presents with 6 weeks of high fever, dark skin pigmentation, massive nontender splenomegaly, and pancytopenia. Bone marrow aspirate smears stained with Giemsa demonstrate oval intracellular amastigotes within macrophages, each containing a nucleus and a perpendicular rod-shaped kinetoplast (LD bodies). Which rapid serological assay is deployed under India\u0027s National Kala-Azar Elimination Programme?","o":["Paul-Bunnell heterophile agglutination test","Widal tube agglutination test","rK39 immunochromatographic dipstick test (detecting antibodies against a 39-amino-acid kinesin-related recombinant antigen)","NS1 antigen ELISA"],"a":2,"e":"Visceral leishmaniasis (Kala-azar), caused by Leishmania donovani transmitted by the sandfly Phlebotomus argentipes, is diagnosed microscopically by visualizing intracellular amastigotes (Leishman-Donovan bodies) in splenic/bone marrow aspirates or serologically by the rK39 rapid dipstick test (\u003e95% sensitivity/specificity in immunocompetent Indian patients under NVBDCP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00819","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Antimicrobial Resistance \u0026 Diagnostic Stewardship","topic":"ESBL \u0026 Carbapenemase Confirmation (CLSI)","q":"A multidrug-resistant Klebsiella pneumoniae urine isolate shows resistance to third-generation cephalosporins and meropenem. Per CLSI M100 phenotypic guidelines, how is Extended-Spectrum Beta-Lactamase (ESBL) confirmed on Mueller-Hinton agar, and what does a positive mCIM paired with a positive eCIM (\u003e= 5 mm EDTA zone increase) identify?","o":["ESBL: positive D-zone test with clindamycin; mCIM+/eCIM+: OXA-48-like carbapenemase","ESBL: resistance to vancomycin; mCIM+/eCIM+: AmpC chromosomal hyperproduction only","ESBL: \u003e= 2 mm decrease with clavulanate; mCIM+/eCIM+: Class A KPC serine carbapenemase","ESBL: \u003e= 5 mm increase in zone diameter for ceftazidime-clavulanate (or cefotaxime-clavulanate) vs cephalosporin disk alone; mCIM+/eCIM+: Metallo-beta-lactamase (e.g., NDM-1)"],"a":3,"e":"In the CLSI ESBL confirmatory combined-disk test, adding clavulanic acid (10 ug) inhibits Class A ESBLs, increasing the ceftazidime or cefotaxime inhibition zone by \u003e= 5 mm compared to the disk alone. For carbapenemase detection, a positive modified Carbapenem Inactivation Method (mCIM) plus a \u003e= 5 mm zone restoration with EDTA (eCIM, which chelates Zn2+ at the active site) confirms a Class B metallo-beta-lactamase such as NDM-1 (highly prevalent in India).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00820","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Diagnostic Bacteriology \u0026 Bloodstream Infections","topic":"Aseptic Venipuncture \u0026 Two-Set Volume","q":"In an adult patient with suspected infective endocarditis or sepsis, what is the standard blood volume per blood culture set, optimal skin antisepsis before venipuncture, and rationale for collecting at least two sets from separate venipuncture sites prior to antibiotics?","o":["20 mL per set (10 mL aerobic + 10 mL anaerobic bottle); 70% isopropyl alcohol followed by 2% chlorhexidine-in-alcohol allowed to dry 30-60 s; two separate venipunctures maximize sensitivity and distinguish true bacteremia from skin-flora contamination","2 mL into a single aerobic bottle; 70% alcohol wiped wet immediately; single draw from existing peripheral IV line","50 mL per bottle; drawn only 24 hours after starting broad-spectrum IV meropenem","5 mL into EDTA tube first; povidone-iodine wiped off immediately after 5 seconds"],"a":0,"e":"Blood volume is the single most important determinant of blood culture sensitivity: adults require 20-30 mL per set (10 mL aerobic + 10 mL anaerobic) across 2-3 separate peripheral venipunctures prior to antibiotics. Proper skin antisepsis (2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol dried fully) keeps contamination \u003c 3%, while dual-site positivity helps interpret skin commensals like Coagulase-Negative Staphylococci.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00821","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Enteric Infections \u0026 Public Health Microbiology","topic":"Time-Course of Diagnostic Specimens","q":"A 19-year-old student presents on Day 4 of high step-ladder fever, headache, and relative bradycardia with suspected Enteric Fever (Salmonella enterica serotype Typhi). Which microbiological specimen offers the highest diagnostic sensitivity during the FIRST week of illness (mnemonic BASU), and what biochemical feature distinguishes Salmonella Typhi on Triple Sugar Iron (TSI) agar?","o":["Widal test alone on Day 4; positive oxidase test","Blood culture (~80-90% sensitivity in Week 1); alkaline/acid (K/A) slant/butt with trace speck of H2S and NO gas","Urine culture; lactose-fermenting pink colonies on MacConkey agar","Stool culture; acid/acid slant/butt with abundant gas"],"a":1,"e":"Following the classic BASU timeline for enteric fever: Blood culture is maximally sensitive in Week 1 (~80-90%), Antibodies (Widal) rise in Week 2, and Stool/Urine cultures become positive mainly in Weeks 2-3. On TSI agar, non-lactose-fermenting S. Typhi yields an alkaline (red) slant, acid (yellow) butt (K/A), NO gas, and a characteristic faint trace of black H2S at the slant-butt junction (vs abundant gas + heavy H2S in S. Paratyphi B/Typhimurium).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00822","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Laboratory Biosafety \u0026 Containment","topic":"Class II Type A2 vs Type B2 BSC","q":"Why is a Biosafety Cabinet (BSC) Class II Type A2 suitable for routine BSL-2/BSL-3 sputum mycobacteriology processing, yet UNSAFE when manipulating volatile toxic chemicals or volatile radionuclides alongside infectious agents?","o":["Class II Type A2 protects only the sample and provides zero personnel protection","Class II Type A2 operates under positive pressure relative to the laboratory","Class II Type A2 recirculates ~70% of HEPA-filtered air inside the cabinet (HEPA traps particulates/microbes but NOT volatile chemical vapors, which build up to explosive/toxic levels and re-enter the room); volatile toxic chemicals require a 100% hard-ducted exterior-exhaust Class II Type B2 BSC","Class II Type A2 blows unfiltered room air directly onto the specimen"],"a":2,"e":"A Class II Type A2 BSC protects personnel (inward air curtain), product (vertical HEPA laminar downflow), and environment (exhaust HEPA), recirculating ~70% of air and exhausting ~30% back into the lab (or via canopy thimble). Because HEPA filters capture particulates/bacteria/fungi (0.3 um) but CANNOT trap volatile chemical vapors or gases, volatile toxins require a 100% total-exhaust hard-ducted Class II Type B2 BSC.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00823","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Diagnostic Anaerobic Bacteriology","topic":"Clostridioides difficile Infection (CDI)","q":"A 68-year-old inpatient develops frequent watery diarrhea (Bristol type 7) 10 days after completing clindamycin therapy. Under IDSA/ESCMID diagnostic stewardship guidelines, how should the microbiology laboratory test for Clostridioides difficile Infection (CDI), and why are formed stools rejected?","o":["Test formed stool routinely for \u0027test of cure\u0027 using Gram stain alone","Perform routine anaerobic stool culture alone without toxin detection","Culture stool on MacConkey agar aerobically for 24 hours","Accept ONLY unformed watery stools (taking the shape of the container) and apply a two-step algorithm: sensitive Glutamate Dehydrogenase (GDH) antigen EIA screen (or NAAT) paired with specific Toxin A/B EIA to distinguish active toxigenic CDI from asymptomatic colonization"],"a":3,"e":"Because asymptomatic colonization with C. difficile occurs in 5-15% of hospitalized patients, testing formed stools or repeating tests for cure yields high false-positive treatment. Two-step algorithms pair high-sensitivity GDH screening (detecting all C. difficile) with high-specificity Toxin A/B EIA (confirming active toxin production, arbitrated by NAAT/tcdB PCR if GDH+/Toxin-).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00824","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Clinical Mycology","topic":"Mucorales (Mucormycosis) vs Aspergillus","q":"A KOH wet mount and histopathology section of necrotic nasal turbinate tissue from an uncontrolled diabetic ketoacidosis patient with black palatal eschar show BROAD, ribbon-like, pauciseptate (aseptate) fungal hyphae branching irregularly at wide 90-degree angles with angioinvasion. Which fungal group is identified, and why is voriconazole ineffective?","o":["Mucorales (e.g., Rhizopus/Mucor causing Rhino-Orbito-Cerebral Mucormycosis); intrinsically resistant to voriconazole—requires urgent IV liposomal amphotericin B (followed by posaconazole/isavuconazole) plus surgical debridement","Pneumocystis jirovecii cysts; treated with caspofungin alone","Aspergillus fumigatus (narrow septate 45-degree branching); voriconazole is first-line","Candida albicans pseudohyphae; treated with fluconazole monotherapy"],"a":0,"e":"Rapid microscopic distinction between Aspergillus (uniform narrow 3-5 um septate hyphae with acute 45-degree dichotomous branching) and Mucorales (broad 10-20 um ribbon-like aseptate/pauciseptate hyphae with 90-degree right-angle branching) is life-saving in India\u0027s DKA/post-COVID ROCM patients. Mucorales are intrinsically resistant to voriconazole and echinocandins, requiring immediate liposomal amphotericin B, metabolic control, and radical debridement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00825","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Rickettsial \u0026 Zoonotic Serology (India)","topic":"Scrub Typhus \u0026 Leptospirosis Diagnostics","q":"During post-monsoon surveillance in India, two patients present with acute fever, thrombocytopenia, and transaminitis: Patient 1 has a painless black necrotic skin eschar in the axilla (suspected Scrub Typhus, Orientia tsutsugamushi), while Patient 2 has conjunctival suffusion, calf tenderness, and jaundice after wading through floodwater (suspected Leptospirosis). Which pair of laboratory diagnostic tests serves as the recommended confirmatory/reference serology for Patient 1 and Patient 2 respectively?","o":["Paul-Bunnell test for Scrub Typhus; Widal test for Leptospirosis","Scrub Typhus IgM ELISA targeting the recombinant 56-kDa antigen (or Weil-Felix OX-K screening) for Patient 1; Microscopic Agglutination Test (MAT) using live Leptospira serovars (or IgM ELISA) for Patient 2","Cold agglutinin test for Scrub Typhus; Sabin-Feldman dye test for Leptospirosis","Blood culture on MacConkey agar for Orientia; Ziehl-Neelsen stain for Leptospira"],"a":1,"e":"Scrub typhus (Orientia tsutsugamushi, transmitted by Leptotrombidium chigger mites) is an obligate intracellular bacterium that does not grow on cell-free routine agar; ICMR recommends IgM ELISA against the immunodominant 56-kDa outer membrane protein (with Weil-Felix OX-K as low-cost cross-reacting screen). Leptospirosis serological gold standard is the Microscopic Agglutination Test (MAT) demonstrating four-fold titer rise or \u003e= 1:400 titer against live serovars (complemented by darkfield/PCR in Week 1 and IgM ELISA).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00826","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Antimicrobial Susceptibility \u0026 Resistance Testing","topic":"Inducible Clindamycin Resistance (D-Zone Test)","q":"A Staphylococcus aureus pus isolate tests RESISTANT to erythromycin (15 ug) and apparently SUSCEPTIBLE to clindamycin (2 ug) on standard disk diffusion. When the two disks are placed 15-26 mm edge-to-edge on Mueller-Hinton agar per CLSI M100, the clindamycin zone exhibits sharp flattening on the side facing the erythromycin disk (forming a \u0027D\u0027 shape). How must the microbiologist report clindamycin?","o":["Report erythromycin as SUSCEPTIBLE","Report clindamycin as SUSCEPTIBLE since it inhibited growth away from erythromycin","Report clindamycin as RESISTANT (positive D-zone test indicating erm-mediated inducible MLSB resistance, which risks spontaneous constitutive mutants and treatment failure if clindamycin is used)","Repeat the test using a cefoxitin disk at 42 C"],"a":2,"e":"In isolates carrying inducible erm (erythromycin ribosome methylation) genes, erythromycin diffuses through agar to induce methylase expression, conferring cross-resistance to clindamycin and producing a blunted D-shaped clindamycin zone (\u0027positive D-test\u0027). Because constitutive erm mutants rapidly emerge during clindamycin therapy in vivo, CLSI M100 mandates reporting clindamycin as RESISTANT whenever the D-test is positive.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00827","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Retrovirus \u0026 Blood-Borne Viral Diagnostics","topic":"4th-Generation Combo Assay \u0026 Strategy III","q":"How does a 4th-generation HIV-1/2 combination immunoassay shorten the diagnostic \u0027window period\u0027 compared with 3rd-generation antibody-only assays, and how many reactive tests using distinct antigens/principles are required to diagnose HIV in an asymptomatic individual under NACO (India) Strategy III?","o":["4th-generation detects gp120 DNA; Strategy III requires 1 reactive rapid test only","4th-generation lengthens the window period to 6 months; Strategy III uses 2 identical ELISA kits","4th-generation detects CD4 cell count; Strategy III requires Western Blot on every sample before counseling","4th-generation simultaneously detects HIV-1 p24 capsid antigen (appearing ~2-3 weeks post-exposure) AND anti-HIV-1/2 IgM/IgG antibodies; NACO Strategy III requires 3 sequentially reactive tests using different antigen preparations/principles"],"a":3,"e":"During acute HIV infection, viral RNA appears first (~10 days), followed by p24 capsid protein (~14-21 days) prior to seroconversion of anti-HIV antibodies (~3-6 weeks). 4th-generation Ag/Ab combo assays detect both p24 antigen and HIV-1/2 antibodies simultaneously, closing the window period substantially. In ICTCs across India, NACO Strategy III requires three different reactive rapid/ELISA assays (A1+, A2+, A3+) using distinct antigens/principles to confirm HIV infection in asymptomatic persons.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00828","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Diagnostic Parasitology \u0026 Opportunistic Infections","topic":"Cryptosporidium Identification","q":"A 33-year-old man with advanced HIV (CD4 count 48 cells/uL) presents with profuse, non-bloody, cholera-like watery diarrhea lasting 3 weeks. Routine bacterial stool cultures and saline/iodine wet mounts are negative. Staining a formalin-ethyl-acetate concentrated stool smear with Kinyoun modified Ziehl-Neelsen (1% H2SO4 decolorizer) reveals numerous spherical 4-6 um bright pink-red acid-fast oocysts containing 4 sporozoites. Which pathogen is confirmed?","o":["Cryptosporidium parvum / hominis","Entamoeba histolytica cysts","Isospora (Cystoisospora) belli (25-30 um oval oocysts)","Giardia lamblia trophozoites"],"a":0,"e":"Cryptosporidium species cause severe protracted watery diarrhea in AIDS patients and are poorly seen on routine saline/iodine wet mounts (often mistaken for yeast). Using Kinyoun modified acid-fast stain (mild 1% H2SO4 decolorizer), Cryptosporidium oocysts appear as small, round 4-6 um bright pink-red acid-fast structures against a blue/green background—distinct from Cyclospora (8-10 um) and Cystoisospora belli (large oval 25-30 um containing two sporoblasts).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00829","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Diagnostic Bacteriology and Antimicrobial Testing","topic":"Macrolide-Lincosamide-Streptogramin B Resistance","q":"A wound swab yields Staphylococcus aureus that tests resistant to erythromycin (15 µg) and appears susceptible to clindamycin (2 µg) on routine Kirby-Bauer disk diffusion. When the two disks are placed 18 mm apart edge-to-edge on Mueller-Hinton agar, a flattened (\u0027D\u0027-shaped) zone of inhibition appears on the clindamycin zone adjacent to the erythromycin disk. How must the lab report clindamycin?","o":["Report clindamycin as INTERMEDIATE and add vancomycin disk synergy testing","Report clindamycin as RESISTANT (positive D-test indicating inducible erm-mediated MLSB resistance)","Report clindamycin as SUSCEPTIBLE because only the msrA efflux pump is present","Ignore the flattening as an agar thickness artifact and repeat on MacConkey agar"],"a":1,"e":"Per CLSI M100 guidelines, placement of erythromycin (15 µg) and clindamycin (2 µg) disks 15–26 mm apart edge-to-edge detects inducible MLSB resistance encoded by erm genes. Erythromycin induces methylase expression, flattening the adjacent clindamycin zone into a \u0027D\u0027 shape. Clinical treatment with clindamycin risks selecting constitutive resistant mutants and therapeutic failure, so clindamycin must be reported as resistant.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00830","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Clinical Mycobacteriology","topic":"Molecular Detection under NTEP","q":"A 35-year-old HIV-positive man with productive cough and weight loss undergoes sputum Cartridge-Based Nucleic Acid Amplification Test (CBNAAT / GeneXpert MTB/RIF) at a district hospital. The result reads \u0027MTB detected, Rifampicin resistance detected\u0027. Mutations in which specific Mycobacterium tuberculosis gene are targeted by this assay to detect rifampicin resistance?","o":["pncA gene encoding pyrazinamidase","katG and inhA promoter genes","81-bp core region of the rpoB gene (encoding the beta subunit of RNA polymerase)","embB gene encoding arabinosyltransferase"],"a":2,"e":"CBNAAT (GeneXpert MTB/RIF) is an automated real-time hemi-nested PCR assay endorsed by WHO and India\u0027s NTEP that simultaneously detects M. tuberculosis complex DNA and mutations within the 81-base-pair rifampicin resistance-determining region (RRDR) of the rpoB gene in under 2 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00831","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Systematic Bacteriology","topic":"Selective and Differential Media","q":"Match each pathogen with its characteristic selective/differential medium and colony appearance: (1) Vibrio cholerae, (2) Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi, (3) Corynebacterium diphtheriae, (4) Brucella melitensis. Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Red colonies with black center on XLD; 2-Yellow colonies on TCBS; 3-Castaneda biphasic medium; 4-Black colonies on Tellurite agar","1-Yellow colonies on TCBS; 2-Black colonies on Tellurite agar; 3-Castaneda biphasic medium; 4-Red colonies with black center on XLD","1-Black colonies on Tellurite agar; 2-Yellow colonies on TCBS; 3-Red colonies with black center on XLD; 4-Castaneda biphasic medium","1-Yellow sucrose-fermenting colonies on TCBS agar; 2-Red colonies with black H2S center on XLD agar; 3-Black colonies on Potassium Tellurite Blood Agar; 4-Castaneda biphasic blood culture medium"],"a":3,"e":"Vibrio cholerae ferments sucrose on Thiosulfate-Citrate-Bile Salts-Sucrose (TCBS) agar to produce flat yellow colonies (unlike green non-sucrose-fermenting V. parahaemolyticus). Salmonella produces red colonies with black H2S centers on XLD agar; C. diphtheriae reduces tellurite to black metallic tellurium on Potassium Tellurite Blood Agar; Brucella is classically isolated using Castaneda biphasic medium to reduce subculture aerosol hazard.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00832","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Hospital Infection Control and Sterilization","topic":"Biological Indicators","q":"A central sterile supply department (CSSD) operates both a gravity-displacement steam autoclave (121°C for 15 minutes at 15 psi) and an ethylene oxide (EtO) gas sterilizer for heat-sensitive endoscopes. Which pair of biological indicator organisms is standard for validating these two sterilizers respectively?","o":["Geobacillus stearothermophilus for steam autoclave; Bacillus atrophaeus for ethylene oxide","Clostridium tetani for steam autoclave; Staphylococcus aureus for ethylene oxide","Bacillus atrophaeus for steam autoclave; Geobacillus stearothermophilus for ethylene oxide","Geobacillus stearothermophilus for dry heat oven; Escherichia coli for ethylene oxide"],"a":0,"e":"Biological indicators represent the gold standard for sterilization efficacy testing because bacterial endospores are far more resistant than vegetative microbes. Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores validate moist heat (steam autoclaves) and hydrogen peroxide plasma sterilizers, whereas Bacillus atrophaeus (formerly B. subtilis var. niger) spores validate ethylene oxide (EtO) gas and dry-heat hot-air ovens.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00833","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"General Microbiology and Quantitative Techniques","topic":"Viable Plate Count","q":"A broth culture of Escherichia coli is serially diluted in 10-fold steps. Exactly 0.1 mL of the 10^-4 dilution is spread onto a nutrient agar plate and incubated overnight at 37°C, yielding 145 discrete colonies. What was the viable bacterial concentration in the original undiluted broth?","o":["1.45 × 10^5 CFU/mL","1.45 × 10^7 CFU/mL","1.45 × 10^8 CFU/mL","1.45 × 10^6 CFU/mL"],"a":1,"e":"Using the standard viable count formula: CFU/mL = (Number of colonies) / (Volume plated in mL × Dilution factor). Here, CFU/mL = 145 / (0.1 × 10^-4) = 145 / 10^-5 = 145 × 10^5 = 1.45 × 10^7 CFU/mL. Note that 145 colonies lies cleanly within the countable range of 30–300 colonies per plate.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00834","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Medical Mycology","topic":"Mucormycosis vs Aspergillosis","q":"A 54-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetic ketoacidosis develops black necrotic palatal eschar and orbital proptosis. A 10% KOH mount of necrotic nasal debridement tissue reveals broad, ribbon-like, aseptate/pauciseptate hyphae branching predominantly at wide (90-degree) angles. Which fungal group is implicated?","o":["Histoplasma capsulatum","Aspergillus fumigatus","Mucorales (e.g., Rhizopus or Mucor species causing mucormycosis)","Candida albicans"],"a":2,"e":"Rhino-orbital-cerebral mucormycosis is strongly associated with diabetic ketoacidosis and iron overload. On KOH mount and H\u0026E/GMS tissue sections, Mucorales appear as broad (5–20 µm), ribbon-like, aseptate or pauciseptate hyphae with irregular diameters and right-angle (90°) branching, invading blood vessels to cause hemorrhagic necrosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00835","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Medical Parasitology","topic":"Malaria Diagnosis","q":"Examination of a Giemsa-stained thin peripheral blood smear from a febrile traveler shows normal-sized erythrocytes without Schüffner\u0027s dots, containing multiple delicate ring-form trophozoites per RBC, appliqué (accolé) forms, double chromatin dots, and crescent-shaped (banana-shaped) gametocytes. Which species is present?","o":["Plasmodium malariae","Plasmodium vivax","Plasmodium ovale","Plasmodium falciparum"],"a":3,"e":"Plasmodium falciparum infects RBCs of all ages without causing cell enlargement or Schüffner\u0027s stippling. Characteristic diagnostic features on thin smear include high parasitemia with multiple rings per RBC, marginal appliqué/accolé rings, double chromatin dots (\u0027headphone\u0027 appearance), and pathognomonic banana/crescent-shaped gametocytes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00836","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Clinical Virology and Serology","topic":"Hepatitis B Serological Profiles","q":"Assertion (A): During the \u0027window period\u0027 of acute Hepatitis B virus (HBV) infection, IgM anti-HBc may be the sole positive serological marker detected in serum. Reason (R): HBsAg has cleared below the detection limit while protective anti-HBs has not yet risen to detectable titers. Choose the correct option.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Between the disappearance of serum HBsAg and the appearance of measurable anti-HBs (the serological window period), acute resolving HBV infection can be missed if only HBsAg and anti-HBs are tested. IgM anti-HBc remains positive throughout this gap and is essential for diagnosing acute HBV infection and screening donated blood.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00837","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Systematic Bacteriology","topic":"Streptococcus pneumoniae Identification","q":"A sputum culture from a patient with lobar pneumonia grows alpha-hemolytic, mucoid \u0027draughtsman\u0027 colonies on sheep blood agar showing Gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci. Which two confirmatory bench tests reliably distinguish Streptococcus pneumoniae from commensal viridans streptococci?","o":["Optochin susceptibility (inhibition zone \u003e= 14 mm around a 5 ug ethylhydrocupreine disk) AND positive Bile Solubility (clearing/lysis of suspension in 10% sodium deoxycholate)","Catalase positivity and coagulase positivity","Bacitracin resistance and CAMP test positivity","Growth in 6.5% NaCl broth and positive PYR test"],"a":0,"e":"Both S. pneumoniae and viridans streptococci are catalase-negative, alpha-hemolytic Gram-positive cocci. S. pneumoniae is uniquely susceptible to optochin (zone \u003e= 14 mm with 5 ug disk in 5% CO2) and bile-soluble (sodium deoxycholate activates pneumococcal autolysin LytA, rapidly clearing turbid broth or lysing colonies on blood agar), whereas viridans streptococci are optochin-resistant and bile-insoluble.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00838","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Clinical Virology \u0026 Arboviral Surveillance (India)","topic":"Dengue Fever Diagnostic Window","q":"Two patients present with acute high fever, retro-orbital pain, myalgia, and thrombocytopenia (45,000/uL) during a post-monsoon Dengue outbreak in Delhi: Patient A is on Day 2 of fever, while Patient B is on Day 8 of fever. Under India\u0027s NVBDCP guidelines, which confirmatory ELISA assay is optimal for Patient A and Patient B respectively?","o":["Patient A: Dengue IgG ELISA alone; Patient B: Blood film for microfilaria","Patient A (Days 1-5): Dengue NS1 Antigen ELISA (or RT-PCR during acute viremia); Patient B (Day 5 onward): Dengue IgM Capture ELISA (MAC-ELISA)","Patient A: Dengue IgM ELISA (always 100% positive on Day 1); Patient B: NS1 antigen (peaks at Week 4)","Widal test for both patients"],"a":1,"e":"During acute Dengue viremia (Days 1-5 of fever), viral RNA and secreted Non-Structural Protein-1 (NS1) circulate at high levels before seroconversion—making NS1 ELISA or RT-PCR optimal in Week 1. From Day 5 onward, viremia/NS1 wanes while anti-Dengue IgM rises sharply, making NIV Pune / NVBDCP-approved MAC-ELISA the confirmatory test from Day 5 onward.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00839","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Diagnostic Bacteriology \u0026 Quantitative Urine Culture","topic":"Kass Significant Bacteriuria \u0026 1 uL Calibrated Loop","q":"A clean-catch midstream urine sample is plated onto CLED (Cystine-Lactose-Electrolyte-Deficient) agar using a calibrated 0.001 mL (1 uL) inoculating loop. After 24 hours at 37 C, the plate yields 120 identical yellow lactose-fermenting colonies of Escherichia coli. What is the quantitative bacterial count in CFU/mL, and how does this meet Kass criteria for significant bacteriuria?","o":["120 CFU/mL (pure urethral flora)","1.2 x 10^5 CFU/mL (120 x 1,000 = 120,000 CFU/mL \u003e= 10^5 CFU/mL Kass threshold for significant bacteriuria in clean-catch midstream urine)","1.2 x 10^4 CFU/mL (always requires 10,000x multiplier)","1.2 x 10^3 CFU/mL (insignificant contamination)"],"a":1,"e":"Because a 0.001 mL (1 uL) calibrated loop delivers 1/1000th of 1 mL onto the CLED agar plate, each colony equals 1,000 CFU/mL: 120 colonies x 1,000 = 120,000 (1.2 x 10^5) CFU/mL, exceeding Kass\u0027s classic \u003e= 10^5 CFU/mL threshold for significant midstream bacteriuria (note CLED inhibits Proteus swarming and supports both Gram-positives and Gram-negatives).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00840","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Diagnostic Bacteriology and Antimicrobial Testing","topic":"ESBL vs AmpC Phenotypic Detection","q":"A urinary isolate of Klebsiella pneumoniae shows resistance to ceftazidime and cefotaxime on routine disk diffusion. On the CLSI Combined Disk Test using cefotaxime (30 µg) alone versus cefotaxime/clavulanic acid (30/10 µg), the inhibition zone expands from 14 mm (cefotaxime alone) to 23 mm (cefotaxime + clavulanate). What resistance mechanism is confirmed?","o":["Efflux-mediated fluoroquinolone resistance","Metallo-beta-lactamase (NDM-1) production","Extended-Spectrum Beta-Lactamase (ESBL) production (zone increase \u003e= 5 mm with clavulanate)","AmpC beta-lactamase hyperproduction (which is NOT inhibited by clavulanic acid)"],"a":2,"e":"Per CLSI guidelines, an increase of \u003e= 5 mm in the inhibition zone diameter for either ceftazidime or cefotaxime tested in combination with clavulanic acid versus the antimicrobial disk alone confirms Extended-Spectrum Beta-Lactamase (ESBL, Ambler Class A) production (here 23 − 14 = 9 mm).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00841","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Clinical Bacteriology and Bloodstream Infections","topic":"Blood Culture Collection and DTP","q":"An ICU patient with a tunneled central venous catheter (CVC) develops new-onset fever. Paired equal-volume blood cultures are drawn simultaneously from the CVC hub and a peripheral venipuncture after 2% chlorhexidine-70% alcohol skin antisepsis. The CVC bottle flags positive for Staphylococcus epidermidis at 11 hours, while the peripheral bottle flags positive with the identical organism at 15 hours. What does this 4-hour Differential Time to Positivity (DTP) indicate?","o":["Simple skin contamination of the peripheral bottle only","Sterile catheter colonization without bacteremia","False-positive automated fluorescence from chlorhexidine carryover","Catheter-Related Bloodstream Infection (CRBSI), because the CVC culture flagged \u003e= 2 hours earlier than the simultaneous peripheral culture"],"a":3,"e":"When equal volumes of blood are drawn simultaneously from a central venous catheter and a peripheral vein into an automated continuous-monitoring blood culture system, a Differential Time to Positivity (DTP) \u003e= 2 hours (CVC bottle flagging at least 2 hours before the peripheral bottle) indicates a much higher bacterial inoculum inside the catheter lumen, confirming Catheter-Related Bloodstream Infection (CRBSI).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00842","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Hospital Infection Control and Biosafety","topic":"Color-Coded Waste Segregation","q":"According to India\u0027s Biomedical Waste Management (BMW) Rules (2016 and 2018 amendments), into which color-coded containers must (a) used plastic IV tubing/catheters/syringes without needles and (b) discarded broken glass ampoules/slides be segregated respectively?","o":["Red bag for contaminated recyclable plastics; Blue cardboard box/puncture-proof container for glassware","Blue box for plastics; White puncture-proof container for intact glass bottles","Yellow bag for plastics; Red bag for broken glassware","White translucent container for plastics; Yellow bag for broken glass slides"],"a":0,"e":"Under India\u0027s BMW Rules: Yellow bags receive anatomical waste, blood-soaked dressings, expired medicines, and pre-treated (autoclaved) microbiology cultures; Red bags receive contaminated recyclable plastic disposables (tubing, catheters, vacutainers, syringes without needles); White translucent puncture-proof containers receive metallic sharps (needles, blades); Blue puncture-proof boxes/containers receive broken/discarded glassware and metallic body implants.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00843","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Systematic Bacteriology","topic":"Clostridium perfringens Identification","q":"A Gram-positive, boxcar-shaped, non-motile, spore-forming anaerobe isolated from a gas gangrene wound produces a double zone of hemolysis on sheep blood agar and stormy fermentation in litmus milk. When streaked onto egg-yolk agar where half the plate is spread with anti-alpha-toxin serum, opalescence appears ONLY on the untreated half. What is this reaction and which enzyme is neutralized?","o":["Positive CAMP test; neutralization of Group B streptococcal hemolysin","Positive Nagler\u0027s reaction; neutralization of Clostridium perfringens alpha-toxin (lecithinase C / phospholipase C)","Positive Weil-Felix reaction; neutralization of rickettsial lipopolysaccharide","Positive Elek\u0027s gel precipitation test; neutralization of diphtheria exotoxin"],"a":1,"e":"Clostridium perfringens alpha-toxin is a phospholipase C (lecithinase) that hydrolyzes lecithin in egg-yolk agar into insoluble diglyceride, creating an opaque halo around colonies. Pre-coating half the plate with specific C. perfringens antitoxin inhibits opalescence on that half (positive Nagler\u0027s reaction), confirming C. perfringens.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00844","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Diagnostic Immunology and Serology","topic":"Serial Two-Fold Serum Dilution","q":"In a quantitative tube agglutination test (e.g., Widal TO titration), the technologist prepares an initial 1:20 serum dilution in Tube 1 and performs serial two-fold dilutions across Tubes 2 through 7 (discarding excess volume from Tube 7 before adding equal antigen). Visible agglutination is observed in Tubes 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5, while Tubes 6 and 7 remain negative (turbid without clumping). What is the reported antibody titer?","o":["160 (1:160)","640 (1:640)","320 (1:320)","80 (1:80)"],"a":2,"e":"Starting at 1:20 in Tube 1 and doubling the dilution in each subsequent tube yields: Tube 1 = 1:20, Tube 2 = 1:40, Tube 3 = 1:80, Tube 4 = 1:160, Tube 5 = 1:320, Tube 6 = 1:640, Tube 7 = 1:1280. Because antibody titer is defined as the reciprocal of the highest serum dilution showing visible positive agglutination (Tube 5), the titer is 320 (1:320).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00845","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Zoonotic and Rickettsial Microbiology","topic":"Scrub Typhus (Orientia tsutsugamushi)","q":"A 33-year-old farmer from Tamil Nadu presents in October with 8 days of high fever, headache, myalgia, mild thrombocytopenia, elevated hepatic transaminases, and a painless black necrotic crusted eschar in the groin fold. Standard blood cultures remain sterile. Which organism, diagnostic test, and antimicrobial therapy apply?","o":["Leptospira interrogans; diagnosed by Widal test; treated with metronidazole","Rickettsia prowazekii (louse-borne); diagnosed by cold agglutinin test; treated with IV ceftriaxone","Coxiella burnetii; diagnosed by acid-fast sputum smear; treated with amoxicillin","Orientia tsutsugamushi (transmitted by trombiculid mite chiggers); diagnosed by Scrub Typhus IgM ELISA (or PCR from eschar); treated with doxycycline"],"a":3,"e":"Scrub typhus, caused by the obligate intracellular bacterium Orientia tsutsugamushi and transmitted by larval trombiculid mites (Leptotrombidium chiggers), is a leading cause of acute undifferentiated febrile illness in rural India. An eschar is pathognomonic when present; IgM ELISA (or eschar PCR) confirms the diagnosis (Weil-Felix OX-K has lower sensitivity), and doxycycline (or azithromycin in pregnancy) is rapidly curative—whereas beta-lactams are completely ineffective.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00846","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Applied and Food Microbiology","topic":"Bacterial Food Poisoning Syndromes","q":"Match each foodborne pathogen with its classic vehicle, incubation period, and toxin mechanism: (1) Staphylococcus aureus, (2) Bacillus cereus (emetic type), (3) Clostridium botulinum, (4) Clostridium perfringens. Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Dairy/cream pastries (1–6 h), heat-stable enterotoxin stimulating vagal emetic center; 2-Reheated fried rice (1–5 h), heat-stable cereulide; 3-Improperly canned/honey foods (12–36 h), heat-labile neurotoxin blocking ACh release; 4-Temperature-abused meat/gravy (8–16 h), sporulation enterotoxin causing watery diarrhea","1-Improperly canned foods (12–36 h); 2-Temperature-abused meat (8–16 h); 3-Cream pastries (1–6 h); 4-Reheated fried rice (1–5 h)","1-Temperature-abused meat (8–16 h); 2-Cream pastries (1–6 h); 3-Reheated fried rice (1–5 h); 4-Improperly canned foods (12–36 h)","1-Reheated fried rice (1–5 h); 2-Improperly canned foods (12–36 h); 3-Cream pastries (1–6 h); 4-Honey in infants only"],"a":0,"e":"Short-incubation (\u003c6 h) vomiting syndromes result from preformed heat-stable toxins: S. aureus enterotoxin in salted/creamy foods and B. cereus emetic toxin (cereulide) in reheated rice. C. perfringens requires ingestion of large vegetative numbers in reheated meat/gravy that sporulate in the gut (8–16 h diarrhea). C. botulinum produces a heat-labile neurotoxin cleaving SNARE proteins to block presynaptic acetylcholine release (12–36 h descending flaccid paralysis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00847","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Diagnostic Bacteriology and Antimicrobial Testing","topic":"MRSA and VISA Detection","q":"Per CLSI M100 standards, which disk-diffusion surrogate antibiotic and interpretive zone diameter accurately detect mecA-mediated Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA), and why must vancomycin susceptibility NOT be reported by routine Kirby-Bauer disk diffusion?","o":["Oxacillin (1 µg) disk with zone \u003e= 25 mm confirms MRSA; vancomycin disk diffusion is the gold standard for VISA","Cefoxitin (30 µg) disk with zone \u003c= 21 mm confirms MRSA; vancomycin disk diffusion fails to differentiate Vancomycin-Intermediate S. aureus (VISA, MIC 4–8 µg/mL) from susceptible strains, requiring MIC testing","Penicillin G (10 U) disk confirms MRSA; vancomycin degrades at 35°C","Vancomycin (30 µg) disk with zone \u003c= 15 mm confirms MRSA; cefoxitin detects only ESBLs"],"a":1,"e":"Cefoxitin (30 µg) is a far stronger inducer of the mecA regulatory system (PBP2a) than oxacillin and is less affected by salt/temperature variations; a zone \u003c= 21 mm after 16–18 hours at 33–35°C predicts mecA-mediated MRSA (zone \u003e= 22 mm = MSSA). Conversely, because vancomycin is a large glycopeptide that diffuses poorly in agar, disk diffusion cannot separate VISA (MIC 4–8 µg/mL) from VSSA (MIC \u003c= 2 µg/mL), mandating broth microdilution or gradient-strip MIC.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00848","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Systematic Bacteriology","topic":"Enteric Fever (Salmonella Typhi) Diagnostics","q":"A 19-year-old college student presents on Day 4 (first week) of continuous step-ladder fever, headache, and relative bradycardia, having taken no prior antibiotics. Which microbiological specimen yields the highest diagnostic sensitivity during the first week of suspected enteric fever?","o":["Urine culture alone on Day 4","Stool culture using MacConkey agar alone on Day 4","Blood culture (or bone marrow aspirate culture if antibiotics were already started)","Single Widal tube agglutination test alone on Day 4"],"a":2,"e":"Enteric fever diagnostics follow the classic BASU timeline: Blood culture has maximal sensitivity (~80–90%) during Week 1 (bacteremic phase) before antibiotics are given; Antibodies (Widal) rise significantly only in Week 2; Stool and Urine cultures become progressively positive in Weeks 2 and 3 (and in chronic gallbladder carriers). Bone marrow culture maintains high yield even after antibiotic initiation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00849","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Systematic Bacteriology","topic":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae","q":"Why is Mycoplasma pneumoniae invisible on routine Gram staining, inherently resistant to all penicillins and cephalosporins, and characterized by \u0027fried-egg\u0027 colonies on PPLO agar?","o":["It expresses plasmid-encoded NDM-1 carbapenemase that hydrolyzes crystal violet","It possesses an extra-thick waxy mycolic acid outer capsule that blocks crystal violet","It forms intracellular endospores inside alveolar macrophages","It completely lacks a peptidoglycan cell wall and instead stabilizes a triple-layered cytoplasmic membrane with cholesterol/sterols"],"a":3,"e":"Mycoplasma species are the smallest free-living bacteria and uniquely lack a peptidoglycan cell wall. Consequently, they do not retain Gram stain dyes, are pleomorphic, grow into agar with a dense central core and translucent peripheral zone (\u0027fried-egg\u0027 colonies on sterol-enriched PPLO agar), and exhibit intrinsic resistance to all beta-lactams and glycopeptides (requiring macrolides, tetracyclines, or fluoroquinolones).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00850","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Zoonotic and Spirochetal Microbiology","topic":"Weil\u0027s Disease Diagnostics","q":"During post-monsoon flooding in Kerala, a 36-year-old paddy worker presents with 7 days of fever, conjunctival suffusion, severe calf myalgia, jaundice, and oliguric acute kidney injury (Weil\u0027s syndrome). Which specialized medium isolates Leptospira interrogans, what is the serovar-specific gold-standard reference test, and why is direct dark-field microscopy of blood/urine alone discouraged?","o":["EMJH (Ellinghausen-McCullough-Johnson-Harris) semisolid medium; Microscopic Agglutination Test (MAT) using live leptospires on paired sera; dark-field microscopy suffers high false-positives from fibrin strands/Brownian artifacts and low sensitivity","Lowenstein-Jensen medium; Elek\u0027s gel test; dark-field stains only spores","Thayer-Martin agar; Paul-Bunnell heterophile test; dark-field lyses red blood cells","Sabouraud dextrose agar; Weil-Felix OX-2 test; dark-field requires oil immersion fluorescence"],"a":0,"e":"Leptospires are tightly coiled spirochetes with hooked ends requiring fatty-acid-enriched EMJH or Fletcher\u0027s medium (incubated at 28–30°C for up to 4–6 weeks). The Microscopic Agglutination Test (MAT)—testing patient serum against live serovar panels—is the serovar-specific gold standard (alongside IgM ELISA/PCR in acute care). Direct dark-field microscopy of clinical blood/urine is prone to misinterpreting fibrin threads or cellular debris (\u0027pseudospirochetes\u0027) as leptospires.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00851","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Hospital Infection Control and Sterilization","topic":"Rideal-Walker Phenol Coefficient","q":"In a Rideal-Walker phenol coefficient assay against Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi, the test phenolic disinfectant kills the suspension in 7.5 minutes (surviving at 5.0 minutes) at a 1:500 dilution, whereas standard pure phenol achieves the identical killing endpoint (surviving at 5.0 min, killed at 7.5 min) at a 1:100 dilution. What is the Phenol Coefficient of the test disinfectant?","o":["0.2 (test disinfectant is one-fifth as potent as phenol)","5.0 (test disinfectant is 5 times more potent than phenol under test conditions)","400 (difference between the two dilution denominators)","500 (direct reciprocal of the test dilution)"],"a":1,"e":"The Rideal-Walker Phenol Coefficient is defined as the dilution denominator of the test disinfectant divided by the dilution denominator of standard phenol that kills Salmonella Typhi in the same exposure time (7.5 min, while surviving 5 min): Phenol Coefficient = 500 / 100 = 5.0. Values \u003e 1.0 indicate greater bactericidal potency than phenol in the absence of organic matter (whereas Chick-Martin adds 3% yeast organic load).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00852","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Medical Parasitology","topic":"HIV-Associated Diarrhea","q":"A stool specimen from an HIV-infected patient (CD4 count 45 cells/µL) with profuse chronic watery diarrhea is examined by modified Kinyoun cold acid-fast staining. Which morphological finding specifically identifies Cryptosporidium parvum rather than Cystoisospora belli?","o":["Operculated trematode eggs measuring 140 × 60 µm","Non-acid-fast trophozoites with ingested erythrocytes measuring 20–40 µm","Small spherical pink-red acid-fast oocysts measuring 4–6 µm in diameter (containing 4 naked sporozoites)","Large elongated elliptical oocysts measuring 25–30 µm containing 1 or 2 sporoblasts"],"a":2,"e":"On modified cold acid-fast (Kinyoun) staining, all three opportunistic coccidia stain pink-red but differ strikingly in size and shape: Cryptosporidium oocysts are small, round spheres (4–6 µm) immediately infective upon passage; Cyclospora oocysts are medium spheres (8–10 µm) showing blue-green UV autofluorescence; Cystoisospora (formerly Isospora) belli oocysts are large oval/elliptical structures (25–30 × 12–15 µm).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00853","course":"B.Sc Medical Microbiology","subject":"Molecular Microbiology and Diagnostics","topic":"Cycle Threshold (Ct) Interpretation","q":"Assertion (A): In a properly controlled quantitative real-time RT-PCR (qRT-PCR) assay, a nasopharyngeal sample with a Cycle Threshold (Ct) value of 16 contains a substantially higher initial viral RNA load than a sample with a Ct value of 34. Reason (R): Ct represents the amplification cycle at which reporter fluorescence crosses the detection threshold; starting with more target nucleic acid copies requires fewer exponential doubling cycles to reach threshold. Choose the correct option.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Because PCR amplifies DNA exponentially (~2^n under 100% efficiency), every ~3.32 Ct difference corresponds to roughly a 10-fold (1 log10) change in starting target copy number. Thus Ct 16 reflects roughly 10^5-fold more target RNA than Ct 34, assuming equivalent specimen quality and internal housekeeping gene control.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00854","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Electroencephalography (EEG)","topic":"EEG Polarity \u0026 Phase Reversal","q":"On a standard longitudinal bipolar (\u0027double banana\u0027) EEG montage using clinical polarity convention (Input 1 negative relative to Input 2 produces an upward deflection), Channel 1 (Fp1-F3) shows a downward spike deflection, while Channel 2 (F3-C3) simultaneously shows an upward spike deflection. Where is the maximal surface-negative epileptiform focus located?","o":["Left frontopolar electrode (Fp1)","Right frontal electrode (F4)","Midline central electrode (Cz)","Left frontal electrode (F3)"],"a":3,"e":"Odd numbers in the International 10-20 system denote the left hemisphere. In Channel 1 (Fp1-F3), F3 is Input 2 (G2); a surface-negative potential at F3 makes G1 (Fp1) relatively positive, causing a downward deflection. In Channel 2 (F3-C3), F3 is Input 1 (G1); a negative potential at F3 makes G1 negative relative to G2, causing an upward deflection. Opposing deflections meeting at a shared electrode represent a negative phase reversal localizing to F3.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00855","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Nerve Conduction Studies \u0026 EMG","topic":"Demyelinating vs Axonal NCS Criteria","q":"A patient with ascending areflexic weakness undergoes motor nerve conduction studies. Median motor testing shows distal latency 8.4 ms (ULN 4.4 ms), forearm conduction velocity 26 m/s (LLN 49 m/s), prolonged F-wave latency (52 ms), and a 62% drop in compound muscle action potential (CMAP) amplitude with \u003e30% duration prolongation between wrist and elbow stimulation (non-entrapment segment). Which pathophysiological pattern is present?","o":["Acquired demyelinating neuropathy with conduction block and temporal dispersion","Normal physiological Martin-Gruber anastomosis","Primary axonal motor neuropathy","Neuromuscular junction postsynaptic defect (myasthenia gravis)"],"a":0,"e":"Markedly prolonged distal latency (\u003e130% ULN), severe conduction velocity slowing (\u003c70% LLN), prolonged/absent F-waves, abnormal temporal dispersion (\u003e30% CMAP duration increase), and \u003e50% proximal-to-distal CMAP amplitude drop across a non-entrapment forearm segment are classic electrodiagnostic hallmarks of acquired demyelinating polyneuropathy (such as AIDP/CIDP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00856","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Evoked Potentials \u0026 Intraoperative Neuromonitoring","topic":"Pattern-Reversal VEP","q":"A 27-year-old woman with unilateral painful blurring of vision and a relative afferent pupillary defect in the left eye undergoes monocular full-field checkerboard pattern-reversal Visual Evoked Potential (PR-VEP) testing. The right eye shows a P100 latency of 101 ms, whereas the left eye shows a well-formed waveform with P100 latency of 136 ms (upper limit 115 ms). What does this VEP abnormality indicate?","o":["Bilateral occipital cortical infarction","Left optic nerve demyelination (consistent with retrobulbar optic neuritis)","Refractive error in the right eye","Bitemporal chiasmal compression"],"a":1,"e":"Pattern-reversal VEP generates a prominent positive peak at ~100 ms (P100) over the occipital cortex (Oz). Unilateral marked prolongation of P100 latency (here 136 ms vs 101 ms in the fellow eye, interocular difference 35 ms) with relatively preserved waveform morphology is the classic electrophysiologic signature of pre-chiasmal optic nerve demyelination in optic neuritis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00857","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Clinical Electroencephalography","topic":"Hyperventilation \u0026 Generalized Epilepsy","q":"During a routine outpatient EEG of a 7-year-old girl with brief 10-second episodes of behavioral arrest and staring without post-ictal confusion, 3 minutes of vigorous hyperventilation provokes an abrupt generalized, bisynchronous 3 Hz spike-and-slow-wave discharge lasting 12 seconds. Which epilepsy syndrome and pharmacological effect are confirmed?","o":["West syndrome (infantile spasms); managed with carbamazepine","Lennox-Gastaut syndrome; slow 1.5 Hz spike-and-wave during deep sleep only","Childhood Absence Epilepsy (typical absence seizures); exquisitely sensitive to hyperventilation and treated with ethosuximide or valproate","Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy; 6 Hz phantom spike-and-wave pattern"],"a":2,"e":"Abrupt onset and offset of generalized, frontally predominant 3 Hz (2.5-4 Hz) spike-and-slow-wave discharges triggered reliably by hyperventilation (hypocapnic cerebral vasoconstriction) on a normal interictal background is the electroclinical hallmark of Childhood Absence Epilepsy. Ethosuximide (T-type Ca2+ channel blocker) is first-line for isolated absence seizures; carbamazepine/phenytoin worsen absence seizures.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00858","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Brainstem \u0026 Somatosensory Evoked Potentials","topic":"Wave I-V Anatomical Generators","q":"In a click-evoked Brainstem Auditory Evoked Potential (BAEP) study, which anatomical structure generates Wave V, and what interpeak latency abnormality on the symptomatic side characterizes a cerebellopontine angle vestibular schwannoma compressing the auditory nerve?","o":["Wave V: superior olivary complex; shortened I-V interpeak latency","Wave V: cochlear hair cells; prolonged III-V interpeak latency","Wave V: primary auditory cortex (Heschl\u0027s gyrus); absent Wave I with normal Wave III","Wave V: inferior colliculus (midbrain); prolonged I-III interpeak latency (with preserved Wave I and delayed or absent Waves III and V)"],"a":3,"e":"Standard BAEP generators follow the E.COLI mnemonic: Wave I = distal CN VIII, Wave II = proximal CN VIII/cochlear nucleus, Wave III = superior olivary complex (lower pons), Wave IV = lateral lemniscus, and Wave V = inferior colliculus (midbrain). A retrocochlear lesion at the cerebellopontine angle (vestibular schwannoma) slows conduction between distal CN VIII (Wave I) and the pons (Wave III), prolonging the I-III and I-V interpeak latencies.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00859","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Neuromuscular Junction Electrophysiology","topic":"Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome (LEMS) vs Myasthenia Gravis","q":"A 60-year-old smoker with proximal lower-limb weakness and dry mouth undergoes ulnar nerve Repetitive Nerve Stimulation (RNS) recording over the abductor digiti minimi. Baseline CMAP amplitude is markedly low (1.2 mV), 3 Hz low-frequency RNS produces a 18% decrement, and immediately after 10 seconds of maximal voluntary isometric contraction, the CMAP amplitude surges to 4.1 mV (+242% increment). Which neuromuscular disorder is confirmed?","o":["Presynaptic Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome (LEMS, antibodies against P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels)","Botulism with complete irreversible blockade of post-exercise facilitation","Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (AIDP)","Postsynaptic Myasthenia Gravis (anti-AChR antibody positive)"],"a":0,"e":"LEMS is a presynaptic NMJ disorder (often paraneoplastic with small-cell lung cancer) caused by autoantibodies against P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels on the motor nerve terminal. Resting CMAP amplitudes are very low and decrement at 2-3 Hz, but brief maximal exercise (10 s) or high-frequency RNS (20-50 Hz) drives Ca2+ accumulation in the nerve terminal, producing a dramatic \u003e 100% CMAP incremental facilitation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00860","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Intraoperative Neurophysiological Monitoring (IONM)","topic":"TcMEP vs SSEP Tract Specificity","q":"During surgical rod distraction for thoracic kyphoscoliosis correction under total intravenous anaesthesia (propofol/remifentanil), bilateral lower-limb Transcranial Electrical Motor Evoked Potentials (TcMEPs) abruptly drop by \u003e 80% while upper-limb TcMEPs and bilateral tibial Somatosensory Evoked Potentials (SSEPs) remain unchanged at baseline. Which vascular/tract injury does this isolated IONM change indicate?","o":["Generalized propofol overdose affecting both arms and legs equally","Selective anterior spinal cord (corticospinal motor tract) ischemia—such as anterior spinal artery compromise or mechanical distraction—sparing the posterior dorsal columns","Isolated peripheral sural nerve compression","Bilateral occipital cortical stroke"],"a":1,"e":"SSEPs traverse the dorsal columns (supplied by paired posterior spinal arteries), whereas TcMEPs traverse the anterolateral corticospinal tracts (supplied by the single anterior spinal artery). Preserved upper-limb TcMEPs and stable tibial SSEPs alongside sudden bilateral lower-limb TcMEP loss rules out anaesthetic suppression and alerts the surgical team immediately to anterior spinal cord ischemia/distraction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00861","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Clinical Electroencephalography","topic":"Electrocerebral Inactivity (ECI) Recording Standards","q":"Per American Clinical Neurophysiology Society (ACNS) minimum technical standards for recording an EEG to confirm Electrocerebral Inactivity (ECI / brain death), what minimum display sensitivity, inter-electrode distance, and continuous artifact-free recording duration are mandatory?","o":["Sensitivity 50 uV/mm; ear-clip reference only; duration 10 minutes","Sensitivity increased to 2 uV/mm; inter-electrode distances \u003e= 10 cm (covering standard 10-20 sites); minimum 30 minutes recording with absence of cerebral activity (\u003e 2 uV) and no reactivity to intense somatosensory/auditory/visual stimuli","Sensitivity 10 uV/mm; inter-electrode distance 3 cm; duration 5 minutes","Sensitivity 7 uV/mm; bipolar longitudinal chain with 5 cm spacing; duration 15 minutes"],"a":1,"e":"Confirming ECI requires rigorous technical sensitivity so low-voltage cortical activity is not missed: sensitivity \u003e= 2 uV/mm for at least 30 minutes, double-distance inter-electrode spacing (\u003e= 10 cm, e.g., Fp1-C3, C3-O1) to detect tiny potential gradients, impedances 100-10,000 ohms, simultaneous ECG monitoring to identify pulse/ECG artifact, and documented lack of reactivity to noxious, auditory, and photic stimuli.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00862","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Needle Electromyography (EMG)","topic":"Denervation vs Chronic Reinnervation Potentials","q":"During needle electromyography (EMG) of the tibialis anterior 6 months after an L5 radiculopathy, resting insertional evaluation reveals sparse fibrillation potentials, while minimal voluntary contraction demonstrates giant (high-amplitude \u003e 5 mV), long-duration (\u003e 15 ms), polyphasic Motor Unit Action Potentials (MUAPs) firing rapidly with reduced recruitment. What physiological process produced these giant polyphasic MUAPs?","o":["Primary inflammatory myopathy (polymyositis)","Normal physiological muscle architecture","Chronic neurogenic reinnervation via collateral axonal sprouting from surviving motor axons to adjacent denervated muscle fibers","End-plate noise from needle placement near the neuromuscular junction"],"a":2,"e":"Following partial axonal loss, surviving intact motor axons sprout collateral branches to adopt nearby denervated muscle fibers, increasing fiber density and spatial dispersion within each surviving motor unit. On needle EMG, this manifests months later as enlarged, prolonged, polyphasic (\u0027neurogenic\u0027) MUAPs with a fast-firing, reduced-recruitment interference pattern.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00863","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Polysomnography \u0026 Sleep Electrophysiology","topic":"Obstructive vs Central Sleep Apnea","q":"During overnight diagnostic Polysomnography (PSG) of a 52-year-old obese snoring man, the nasal thermistor/pressure channel shows repeated 20-second drops in airflow \u003e= 90% from baseline accompanied by persistent paradoxical chest and abdominal RIP belt respiratory effort and 5% oxygen desaturations, yielding an Apnea-Hypopnea Index (AHI) of 38 events/hour. How are these events classified?","o":["Mild Obstructive Sleep Apnea (AHI 5-14/hr)","Mild Central Sleep Apnea (CSA)","Normal physiological REM sleep atonia","Severe Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA; persistent respiratory effort against an occluded upper airway, AHI \u003e= 30/hr)"],"a":3,"e":"Per AASM Scoring Manual criteria, an apnea (\u003e= 90% airflow drop for \u003e= 10 s) with continued or increasing inspiratory effort (thoracoabdominal RIP belts) defines an Obstructive Apnea, whereas absent respiratory effort defines Central Apnea. Adult OSA severity is graded by AHI: 5-14.9 = Mild, 15-29.9 = Moderate, and \u003e= 30 events/hour = Severe (here 38/hr).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00864","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"EEG Instrumentation, Filters \u0026 Artifacts","topic":"High-Frequency (Low-Pass) Filter Distortion","q":"While reviewing an EEG heavily contaminated by frontalis/temporalis muscle (EMG) artifact, an electrophysiology technician lowers the High-Frequency (Low-Pass) Filter (HFF) from the standard 70 Hz down to 15 Hz. What dangerous diagnostic distortion does a 15 Hz HFF create on true cerebral waveforms?","o":["Attenuates and rounds out fast sharp epileptiform spikes (\u003c 70 ms duration) so they mimic benign slow waves or disappear, risking missed seizure foci","Converts normal posterior alpha rhythm into 30 Hz beta bursts","Amplifies 50 Hz AC line noise 10-fold","Eliminates slow delta waves below 1 Hz"],"a":0,"e":"Epileptiform spikes (20-70 ms duration) contain high-frequency components (15-50 Hz). Although lowering the HFF to 15 Hz visually smooths muscle EMG artifact (\u003e 30 Hz), it simultaneously blunts, attenuates, and broadens real cortical spikes so they masquerade as harmless theta/delta waves. Standard routine EEG HFF must remain at 70 Hz (or \u003e= 35 Hz only cautiously while checking unfiltered traces).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00865","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Focal Entrapment Neuropathies (NCS)","topic":"Sensory Comparison Studies","q":"A typist with nocturnal hand paresthesias in the thumb, index, and middle fingers has borderline-normal routine median motor distal latency (4.1 ms) and index-finger sensory peak latency (3.5 ms). Which internal comparison Nerve Conduction Study is MOST sensitive for confirming mild focal median neuropathy at the wrist (Carpal Tunnel Syndrome)?","o":["Phrenic nerve motor latency","Ring-finger (digit 4) median-versus-ulnar antidromic sensory latency comparison (or median-vs-ulnar palmar mixed study over identical distance; \u003e= 0.4-0.5 ms relative delay in median peak confirms CTS)","Sural sensory nerve conduction study in the lower leg","Blink reflex supraorbital stimulation"],"a":1,"e":"In early/mild Carpal Tunnel Syndrome, sensory fibers traversing the carpal tunnel are affected first, yet absolute latencies may still fall within broad population reference limits. Internal side-by-side comparison within the SAME hand over identical distance—specifically stimulating median vs ulnar sensory fibers to Digit 4 (ring finger, dual innervation) or trans-carpal mixed palmar stimulation—eliminates age/limb-temperature confounding; a median-to-ulnar latency difference \u003e= 0.4-0.5 ms confirms CTS with \u003e 90% sensitivity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00866","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"ICU Continuous EEG Monitoring","topic":"Lateralized Periodic Discharges (LPDs / PLEDs)","q":"A 46-year-old man admitted to the neuro-ICU with fever, behavioral confusion, and hemorrhagic hyperintensity in the left medial temporal lobe on brain MRI undergoes continuous EEG monitoring. The tracing demonstrates high-amplitude sharp-and-slow complexes repeating stereotypically every 1.2 seconds exclusively over the left temporal electrodes (F7, T3/T7, T5/P7). Which ACNS EEG pattern is this, and what classic infection does it strongly suggest?","o":["14- and 6-Hz positive bursts; normal adolescent drowsiness","Generalized 3 Hz spike-and-wave; Childhood Absence Epilepsy","Lateralized Periodic Discharges (LPDs, formerly PLEDs); strongly associated with acute focal destructive cortical pathology, classically Herpes Simplex Virus-1 (HSV-1) encephalitis","Normal posterior dominant alpha rhythm; metabolic hepatic coma"],"a":2,"e":"Lateralized Periodic Discharges (LPDs, formerly termed PLEDs) consist of stereotyped sharp/polyphasic complexes occurring at regular 0.5-2.0 Hz intervals confined to one hemisphere. They signify acute or subacute structural cortical irritation—most commonly acute ischemic stroke, focal tumor, or, when localized over the temporal lobe in a febrile encephalopathic patient, Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV-1) encephalitis—carrying high risk for focal seizures.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00867","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Nerve Conduction Studies (Late Responses)","topic":"F-Wave vs H-Reflex","q":"How do the neural pathway and optimal electrical stimulus intensity differ between an F-wave study and a tibial H-reflex (Hoffmann reflex) study in electrodiagnostic testing?","o":["F-wave is a sensory-motor reflex requiring submaximal stimulation; H-reflex is purely motor requiring supramaximal stimulation","F-wave uses SUPRAMAXIMAL stimulation traveling antidromically and orthodromically purely along motor axons (assessing proximal motor segments/roots in almost any limb muscle); H-reflex uses SUBMAXIMAL stimulation activating Ia sensory afferents -\u003e spinal synapse -\u003e alpha motor efferents (evaluating S1 radiculopathy over the soleus)","Both F-wave and H-reflex disappear completely with maximal voluntary relaxation","H-reflex has identical fixed latency regardless of stimulus intensity up to 100 mA"],"a":1,"e":"An F-wave is a non-synaptic pure motor late response generated when a SUPRAMAXIMAL shock travels antidromically up motor axons to backfire a small fraction (~1-5%) of anterior horn cells, returning down the same motor axons (variable latency/morphology, obtainable in virtually any distal muscle). Conversely, the H-reflex is a true monosynaptic Ia-afferent-to-alpha-motoneuron reflex elicited optimally by low-intensity SUBMAXIMAL pulses (which selectively stimulate large Ia sensory fibers before antidromic motor collision extinguishes the reflex at supramaximal levels), primarily testing the S1 arc via the tibial-soleus pathway.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00868","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Autonomic \u0026 Small-Fiber Neurophysiology","topic":"Heart Rate Variability (E:I Ratio) vs Sympathetic Skin Response (SSR)","q":"A diabetic patient with orthostatic hypotension, anhydrosis of the feet, and burning small-fiber pain (but normal routine motor/sensory nerve conduction velocities) is referred to the neurophysiology lab for autonomic testing. Which division of the autonomic nervous system is evaluated by the Heart Rate Expiratory-to-Inspiratory (E:I) ratio during deep breathing versus the Sympathetic Skin Response (SSR) recorded from the palm and sole?","o":["E:I ratio tests somatic alpha-motor fibers; SSR tests dorsal column proprioception","E:I deep-breathing heart rate variability tests PARASYMPATHETIC (vagal cardiovagal) function; Sympathetic Skin Response (SSR) evaluates unmyelinated POSTGANGLIONIC SYMPATHETIC cholinergic sudomotor (sweat gland) C-fibers","Both E:I ratio and SSR test exclusively myelinated A-alpha motor fibers","SSR evaluates parasympathetic salivation; E:I ratio tests sympathetic vasoconstriction only"],"a":1,"e":"Routine NCS tests only large myelinated nerve fibers, missing pure small-fiber and autonomic neuropathies. Sinus arrhythmia during paced deep breathing (6 breaths/min, E:I R-R ratio) is mediated predominantly by parasympathetic vagal efferents. Meanwhile, Sympathetic Skin Response (SSR) records slow electrodermal sweat potential changes across eccrine glands on palms/soles after sensory shock or deep inspiration, assessing unmyelinated sympathetic cholinergic C-fibers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00869","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Nerve Conduction Studies Quality Control","topic":"Cold Limb Temperature Artifact","q":"A patient enters the electrodiagnostic lab from freezing winter weather with a hand skin temperature of 25 C. If the technologist runs median motor and sensory Nerve Conduction Studies WITHOUT warming the limb to \u003e= 32 C first, what combined artifactual changes occur in distal latency, conduction velocity, and CMAP/SNAP amplitude?","o":["Complete electrical silence mimicking Guillain-Barre syndrome","No change in sensory conduction; only F-waves disappear","Shortened latency, faster velocity, and markedly decreased amplitude","Prolonged distal latency, slowed conduction velocity (~1.5-2.0 m/s per 1 C drop), AND paradoxically INCREASED CMAP/SNAP amplitude and prolonged potential duration (due to delayed sodium channel inactivation)"],"a":3,"e":"Cold limb temperature slows voltage-gated sodium channel opening AND closing (inactivation). Delayed opening prolongs distal latency and slows conduction velocity by ~1.5-2.4 m/s per 1 C drop (risking false-positive diagnosis of carpal tunnel or demyelinating neuropathy!), while delayed inactivation widens action potential duration and paradoxically INCREASES CMAP/SNAP amplitudes. Always warm limbs to 32-34 C using warm packs/heat lamps before recording.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00870","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Sleep Medicine \u0026 Multiple Sleep Latency Test (MSLT)","topic":"Narcolepsy Electrophysiological Diagnosis","q":"A 22-year-old college student with irresistible daytime sleep attacks, sleep paralysis, and knees buckling when laughing (cataplexy) completes a full-night Polysomnography (\u003e= 6 hours sleep, AHI = 1.8/hr ruling out OSA) followed the next morning by a 5-nap Multiple Sleep Latency Test (MSLT). Which MSLT electrodiagnostic threshold confirms Narcolepsy?","o":["Mean Sleep Latency \u003c= 8.0 minutes AND \u003e= 2 Sleep-Onset REM Periods (SOREMPs, entering REM within 15 minutes of sleep onset across the MSLT naps/preceding PSG)","Continuous 3 Hz spike-and-wave discharges during N1 sleep","Mean Sleep Latency \u003e 15 minutes with 0 REM periods","Mean Sleep Latency = 12 minutes with N3 slow-wave sleep in all 5 naps"],"a":0,"e":"Per ICSD-3 / AASM criteria, after ruling out sleep deprivation and obstructive sleep apnea on overnight PSG (and tapering REM-suppressing SSRIs/stimulants \u003e= 2 weeks beforehand), Narcolepsy (Type 1 with cataplexy/low CSF hypocretin-1, or Type 2) is confirmed on a 5-nap daytime MSLT (2-hour intervals) by Mean Sleep Latency \u003c= 8.0 minutes and \u003e= 2 SOREMPs (including a SOREMP on the preceding night\u0027s PSG).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00871","course":"B.Sc Neuro Electrophysiology","subject":"Focal Neuropathy Nerve Conduction Studies","topic":"Elbow Flexion Positioning Standardization","q":"When performing ulnar motor nerve conduction studies across the elbow (stimulating below-elbow and above-elbow, recording abductor digiti minimi), why MUST the elbow be flexed to 70-90 degrees rather than fully extended at 0 degrees?","o":["Full extension stretches the ulnar nerve tightly by 5 cm, creating false fast velocity","In full elbow extension, the ulnar nerve becomes slack and redundant behind the medial epicondyle—so surface skin distance underestimates true nerve length, producing a FALSELY SLOW across-elbow conduction velocity","Elbow flexion paralyzes the median nerve temporarily to prevent volume conduction","The ulnar nerve exits the cubital tunnel only when flexed 90 degrees"],"a":1,"e":"During full elbow extension, the ulnar nerve folds slackly in the retrocondylar groove, meaning surface tape measurement between below-elbow and above-elbow sites is shorter than true axonal length (underestimating distance / time -\u003e falsely slow across-elbow velocity!). Flexing the elbow to 70-90 degrees (minimum 10 cm across-elbow segment) pulls the nerve taut closely matching skin contour, ensuring accurate velocity (abnormal \u003c 50 m/s or \u003e 10 m/s drop vs forearm).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00872","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radiopharmacy \u0026 Radiation Dosimetry","topic":"Effective Half-Life","q":"A radiopharmaceutical labeled with Technetium-99m (physical half-life Tp = 6.0 hours) clears from the target organ with a biological half-life (Tb) of 3.0 hours. What is the effective half-life (Teff) of the radiopharmaceutical in that organ?","o":["4.5 hours","9.0 hours","2.0 hours","1.5 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Effective decay combines physical decay and biological clearance in parallel: 1/Teff = 1/Tp + 1/Tb, or Teff = (Tp x Tb) / (Tp + Tb). Substituting Tp = 6.0 h and Tb = 3.0 h gives Teff = (6.0 x 3.0) / (6.0 + 3.0) = 18 / 9 = 2.0 hours. Note that Teff is always shorter than both Tp and Tb.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00873","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radiopharmaceutical Quality Control","topic":"Radionuclidic \u0026 Chemical Purity","q":"After eluting a 99Mo/99mTc generator in the hot lab, how does the nuclear medicine technologist assay 99Mo radionuclidic breakthrough in a dose calibrator, and what happens clinically if Al3+ chemical breakthrough exceeds 10 ppm?","o":["Measure unshielded vial on Tc-99m setting; high Al3+ causes thyroid stunning","Measure eluate with a Geiger-Muller probe at 1 meter; high Al3+ causes F-18 defluorination in bone","Perform instant thin-layer chromatography (ITLC) in saline only; high Al3+ increases glomerular filtration","Place the eluate vial inside a 6 mm lead shield canister in the dose calibrator to attenuate 140 keV Tc-99m photons while counting 740/780 keV Mo-99 photons; high Al3+ causes Tc-99m sulfur colloid clumping and RBC agglutination"],"a":3,"e":"Because a 6 mm lead pot absorbs virtually all 140 keV photons from Tc-99m while transmitting ~50% of the high-energy 740/780 keV gamma photons from Mo-99, it allows accurate quantification of Mo-99 breakthrough (limit \u003c 0.15 uCi Mo-99 per mCi Tc-99m at administration). Excess Al3+ ion (\u003e 10 ug/mL = 10 ppm) causes flocculation of Tc-99m sulfur colloid (trapping in lung capillaries) and agglutination of RBCs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00874","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Nuclear Medicine Instrumentation","topic":"Coincidence Detection Physics","q":"In Fluorine-18 FDG PET imaging, why does a PET scanner achieve markedly higher system sensitivity than a conventional gamma camera without requiring physical lead collimators?","o":["Positron-electron annihilation produces two opposing 511 keV photons at ~180 degrees detected simultaneously by opposing crystal pairs (electronic collimation)","PET uses NaI(Tl) crystals with a 200-nanosecond coincidence window to accept scattered photons","F-18 has a 6-hour half-life allowing 10-fold higher injected patient activity","F-18 emits 140 keV single photons that pass straight through tungsten crystals"],"a":0,"e":"When a positron emitted by F-18 (t1/2 = 109.8 min) annihilates with an electron, two 511 keV gamma photons are emitted nearly 180 degrees apart. Opposing fast scintillation detectors (e.g., LSO/LYSO) register a true coincidence event along the line of response within a narrow timing window (4-12 ns), providing \u0027electronic collimation\u0027 and eliminating absorptive lead septa.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00875","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Clinical Nuclear Medicine Procedures","topic":"Renal Radiopharmaceuticals","q":"A 4-year-old boy with recurrent febrile urinary tract infections undergoes radionuclide imaging to detect acute pyelonephritis and focal renal cortical scarring, followed later by a diuretic renogram to evaluate suspected ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) obstruction in a poorly functioning hydronephrotic kidney. Which pair of Tc-99m radiopharmaceuticals is optimal for these two studies respectively?","o":["Tc-99m sulfur colloid for cortical scarring; Tc-99m MDP for diuretic renography","Tc-99m DMSA (proximal tubular cortical binding) for cortical scarring; Tc-99m MAG3 (high extraction efficiency via tubular secretion) for diuretic renography","Tc-99m DTPA for cortical scarring; Tc-99m DMSA for dynamic diuretic renography","Tc-99m pertechnetate for cortical scarring; Tc-99m sestamibi for diuretic renography"],"a":1,"e":"Tc-99m DMSA (dimercaptosuccinic acid) binds tightly (~40-60%) to proximal renal tubular cells, offering high-resolution static cortical imaging and accurate differential split renal function for pyelonephritis/scarring. Tc-99m MAG3 (mercaptoacetyltriglycine) is cleared rapidly via proximal tubular secretion with a high extraction fraction (~50-60% vs ~20% for glomerular DTPA), providing superior target-to-background renograms in neonates and impaired kidneys.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00876","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Nuclear Counting Statistics \u0026 Radiation Detection","topic":"Standard Deviation \u0026 Percent Error","q":"During a radioiodine thyroid uptake measurement, a well/probe scintillation counter accumulates exactly 10,000 net counts in 1 minute. Applying Poisson counting statistics, what are the standard deviation (sigma) and the percentage standard deviation (%SD) of this measurement?","o":["sigma = 1,000 counts; %SD = 10.0%","sigma = 10 counts; %SD = 0.1%","sigma = 100 counts; %SD = 1.0%","sigma = 50 counts; %SD = 0.5%"],"a":2,"e":"Radioactive decay follows a Poisson distribution where variance equals the total counts N, so standard deviation sigma = sqrt(N) = sqrt(10,000) = 100 counts. Percentage standard deviation (%SD) = (sigma / N) x 100 = (100 / 10,000) x 100 = 1.0% (requiring 10,000 counts to achieve 1% statistical precision).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00877","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radionuclide Therapy \u0026 Radiation Protection","topic":"AERB Therapeutic Discharge \u0026 Delay-Tank Criteria","q":"A patient with differentiated papillary thyroid carcinoma receives 100 mCi (3.7 GBq) of oral I-131 for post-thyroidectomy remnant ablation in India. Which physical emission properties describe I-131, and why must this patient be housed in an AERB-approved isolation ward connected to delay-and-decay sewage tanks?","o":["Isomeric transition 140 keV gamma emitter; discharged immediately with lead collar","Pure alpha emitter (t1/2 = 6.0 hours); discharged once urine is clear","Pure positron emitter (t1/2 = 110 min); released immediately home without isolation","Beta-minus emitter (606 keV max, therapeutic) plus 364 keV gamma photon (t1/2 = 8.02 days); isolation required until retained activity drops to \u003c= 30 mCi (1.11 GBq) or dose rate at 1 m meets AERB release limit (\u003c= 50 uSv/h)"],"a":3,"e":"I-131 has a physical half-life of 8.02 days, delivering high-LET short-range beta-minus therapy (606 keV max) while emitting penetrating 364 keV gamma photons that create external public exposure hazards. Under AERB (India) Radiation Safety Guidelines for Nuclear Medicine, doses \u003e 30 mCi (1.11 GBq) require inpatient isolation with dedicated delay-and-decay urinary waste tanks until residual activity \u003c= 30 mCi (\u003c= 50 uSv/h at 1 meter).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00878","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Gamma Camera Instrumentation \u0026 Collimators","topic":"Parallel-Hole vs Pinhole \u0026 Medium-Energy Collimators","q":"A nuclear medicine technologist prepares to acquire an Indium-111 (171 keV and 245 keV) octreotide SPECT scan, followed later by a Tc-99m (140 keV) pinhole thyroid scan. Why must a Medium-Energy All-Purpose (MEAP) collimator be mounted instead of a Low-Energy High-Resolution (LEHR) collimator for In-111, and how should the pinhole collimator be positioned relative to the thyroid?","o":["LEHR septa are too thin for 245 keV photons, causing severe septal penetration (\u0027star artifact\u0027) that degrades contrast; position the pinhole aperture CLOSE to the thyroid to maximize geometric magnification and resolution","LEHR blocks 140 keV photons completely; place the pinhole 50 cm away from the neck for magnification","In-111 emits 511 keV annihilation photons requiring electronic collimation without any lead","MEAP increases sensitivity 10-fold over LEHR for Tc-99m; pinhole produces inverted but minified images close up"],"a":0,"e":"Collimator septal thickness must scale with gamma photon energy. Using a thin-septum LEHR collimator (designed for \u003c= 140 keV Tc-99m) with In-111 (171/245 keV) or Ga-67 (300 keV) allows high-energy photons to penetrate lead septa, creating star artifacts and blurred images—requiring thicker-septum Medium-Energy collimators. Conversely, pinhole collimators magnify small superficial organs (thyroid/parathyroid) with highest resolution when positioned very close to the skin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00879","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Cardiovascular Nuclear Medicine","topic":"Pharmacologic Vasodilator Stress","q":"A patient unable to exercise on a treadmill is scheduled for a Regadenoson Tc-99m Sestamibi myocardial perfusion scan, but admits drinking two cups of strong black coffee 3 hours before arriving at the nuclear medicine department. Why must the vasodilator stress test be postponed?","o":["Caffeine accelerates Tc-99m physical half-life from 6 hours to 1 hour","Caffeine is a competitive adenosine receptor antagonist that blocks regadenoson-induced A2A coronary hyperemia, producing a false-negative myocardial perfusion scan","Coffee triggers severe coronary spasm when mixed with Tc-99m sestamibi","Caffeine causes acute nephrotoxicity when combined with sestamibi"],"a":1,"e":"Regadenoson (selective A2A receptor agonist), adenosine, and dipyridamole induce coronary steal/hyperemia via A2A receptors. Methylxanthines (caffeine in coffee, tea, cola, chocolate; theophylline) competitively antagonize adenosine receptors, blunting flow heterogeneity between normal and stenotic coronary territories and causing false-negative ischemia results—requiring \u003e= 12-24 hours of caffeine abstinence prior to testing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00880","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"PET/CT Quantitative Imaging","topic":"SUVbw Calculation \u0026 Hyperglycemia Artifact","q":"On an F-18 FDG PET/CT scan of a 70 kg (70,000 g) fasting patient, the decay-corrected injected F-18 FDG activity at scan time is 350 MBq (350,000 kBq), and a pulmonary nodule measures 35 kBq/mL (assuming tissue density = 1 g/mL). What is the lesion\u0027s body-weight Standardized Uptake Value (SUVbw), and how does uncontrolled hyperglycemia (\u003e 200 mg/dL) affect tumor SUV?","o":["SUVbw = 1.0; hyperglycemia artificially doubles tumor FDG uptake","SUVbw = 14.0; hyperglycemia has zero effect on FDG biodistribution","SUVbw = 7.0; hyperglycemia lowers tumor SUV because endogenous glucose competitively inhibits FDG via GLUT transporters and triggers insulin-driven skeletal muscle FDG uptake","SUVbw = 0.7; hyperglycemia increases brain SUV 5-fold"],"a":2,"e":"SUVbw = Tissue activity concentration (kBq/mL) / [Injected activity (kBq) / Body weight (g)] = 35 / (350,000 / 70,000) = 35 / 5 = 7.0. Elevated blood glucose (\u003e 180-200 mg/dL) competes directly with F-18 FDG for GLUT-1/GLUT-3 transport and hexokinase phosphorylation in tumors while stimulating endogenous insulin, shifting FDG into skeletal/cardiac muscle and degrading tumor-to-background contrast.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00881","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Clinical Nuclear Medicine Imaging","topic":"Acute Cholecystitis \u0026 Morphine Augmentation","q":"A patient with right upper quadrant pain undergoes Tc-99m mebrofenin hepatobiliary scintigraphy (HIDA scan) after fasting 6 hours. At 60 minutes, hepatic uptake and common bile duct-to-duodenal excretion are prompt, but the gallbladder is NOT visualized. Assuming no morphine allergy and patent common bile duct excretion, why is IV morphine sulfate (0.04 mg/kg) administered at 60 minutes?","o":["To accelerate urinary excretion of Tc-99m mebrofenin","To stimulate gallbladder contraction and empty bile rapidly (like CCK/sincalide)","To dissolve cholesterol gallstones chemically inside the cystic duct","To contract the sphincter of Oddi, raising intrabiliary pressure to drive radiotracer into the gallbladder if the cystic duct is patent—so persistent non-visualization 30 minutes later confirms acute cholecystitis"],"a":3,"e":"Failure to visualize the gallbladder at 60 minutes can represent acute cholecystitis (cystic duct obstruction) OR chronic cholecystitis/prolonged fasting stasis. Administering low-dose IV morphine (0.04 mg/kg) transiently constricts the sphincter of Oddi, increasing common bile duct pressure and forcing tracer into a patent gallbladder within 30 minutes (shortening the study from 4 hours to 90 minutes). Persistent non-visualization 30 minutes post-morphine confirms acute cholecystitis (~95% specificity).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00882","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radiation Safety \u0026 Decontamination in Nuclear Medicine","topic":"Major Spill Containment \u0026 Wipe Surveys","q":"If a vial containing 50 mCi (1.85 GBq) of Tc-99m or I-131 shatters on the hot-lab floor (a major radioactive spill), what is the correct directional wiping technique during containment/decontamination, and what immediate personnel steps are mandatory?","o":["Alert nearby staff, cover spill with disposable absorbent pads wearing double gloves/shoe covers, wipe strictly from the OUTSIDE perimeter INWARD toward the center to prevent spreading contamination, survey all personnel with a GM meter, and notify the Radiation Safety Officer (RSO)","Mop vigorously from the center outward toward the hallway to dilute activity","Leave the spill unguarded until it decays over 10 half-lives without informing the RSO","Sweep dry broken glass immediately with a bare broom and flush down a regular sink"],"a":0,"e":"Per AERB Nuclear Medicine Radiation Safety protocols, major spills require immediate warning/access control, PPE (double gloves, shoe covers, lab coat), covering liquids with absorbent paper, and wiping/scrubbing from the clean outer perimeter inward toward the center so radioactivity is never tracked outward. Contaminated glass is handled with tongs into shielded decay waste, personnel are surveyed before exiting, and the RSO documents wipe-test clearance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00883","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Skeletal \u0026 Oncologic Nuclear Medicine","topic":"Metastatic \u0027Superscan\u0027 Recognition","q":"A 70-year-old man with advanced metastatic prostate carcinoma undergoes a whole-body Tc-99m MDP bone scan. On initial inspection, the technologist notices unusually crisp, diffusely intense symmetric radiotracer uptake throughout the skull, spine, ribs, and pelvis, virtually zero soft-tissue background, and COMPLETELY ABSENT (\u0027missing\u0027) visualization of both kidneys and the urinary bladder. What does this pattern represent?","o":["Completely normal high-quality bone scan in an elderly patient","A \u0027Metastatic Superscan\u0027—widespread diffuse osteoblastic bone metastases avidly sequestered nearly all administered Tc-99m MDP, leaving insufficient tracer for renal excretion or soft-tissue background","Free Tc-99m pertechnetate contamination in the MDP kit","Acute bilateral renal artery thrombosis with normal bones"],"a":1,"e":"Normally, ~40-50% of injected Tc-99m MDP localizes in bone while the rest clears via the kidneys, making kidneys and bladder clearly visible on a normal 3-hour bone scan. In diffuse osteoblastic metastases (prostate/breast cancer) or severe metabolic bone disease (renal osteodystrophy/hyperparathyroidism), hyperactive bone acts as a sink (\u0027superscan\u0027), producing deceptively symmetric bright axial uptake alongside \u0027absent kidney sign\u0027 and ultra-low soft-tissue activity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00884","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radiopharmacy Dose Preparation","topic":"Tc-99m Radioactive Decay Compensation","q":"A patient is scheduled to receive 20 mCi (740 MBq) of Tc-99m MDP (physical half-life = 6.0 hours) at 11:00 AM. At 05:00 AM (6.0 hours prior to injection), the calibrated Tc-99m MDP stock vial concentration is 40 mCi/mL. What volume must the nuclear medicine technologist draw into the unit-dose syringe at 05:00 AM so that exactly 20 mCi remains at 11:00 AM?","o":["0.50 mL","0.25 mL","1.00 mL","2.00 mL"],"a":2,"e":"Because 6.0 hours between 05:00 AM and 11:00 AM equals exactly 1.0 physical half-life of Tc-99m, activity drawn at 05:00 AM will decay by 50% by 11:00 AM. Therefore, 05:00 AM required activity = 20 mCi x 2 = 40 mCi. At a 05:00 AM vial concentration of 40 mCi/mL, Volume = 40 mCi / (40 mCi/mL) = 1.00 mL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00885","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radiopharmacy and Radionuclide Production","topic":"99Mo-99mTc Generator System","q":"In a 99Mo-99mTc generator (99Mo t1/2 = 66 h; 99mTc t1/2 = 6.0 h), what type of radioactive equilibrium is established, approximately when does daughter 99mTc activity reach its maximum after complete elution, and what is the USP/AERB limit for 99Mo breakthrough at patient administration?","o":["Transient equilibrium; ~66 hours post-elution; \u003c 1.5 µCi of 99Mo per mCi of 99mTc","Secular equilibrium; ~6 hours post-elution; \u003c 15 µCi of 99Mo per mCi of 99mTc","No equilibrium; ~12 hours post-elution; \u003c 10 µCi of 99Mo per mCi of 99mTc","Transient equilibrium; ~23 hours post-elution; \u003c 0.15 µCi of 99Mo per mCi of 99mTc"],"a":3,"e":"Because the parent 99Mo half-life (66 h) is only about 11 times longer than the daughter 99mTc half-life (6.0 h), the pair achieves transient equilibrium, with maximum 99mTc buildup occurring at t_max = ln(λd/λp)/(λd - λp) ≈ 22.9 hours (~23 h). To avoid unnecessary patient beta-radiation dose from 99Mo, USP/AERB strictly limits 99Mo breakthrough to \u003c 0.15 µCi per mCi of 99mTc (and Al3+ \u003c 10 µg/mL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00886","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radiation Physics in Nuclear Medicine","topic":"Half-Life Decay and Dispensing Volume","q":"A multi-dose vial of 18F-FDG (physical half-life = 110 minutes) is calibrated at 08:00 AM with a concentration of 740 MBq/mL. A patient requires an exact injected activity of 370 MBq at 11:40 AM (220 minutes later). What volume must the technologist draw from the vial at 11:40 AM?","o":["2.0 mL","0.5 mL","4.0 mL","1.0 mL"],"a":0,"e":"Elapsed time = 11:40 AM − 08:00 AM = 220 minutes = exactly 2 half-lives (220 / 110 = 2). The specific concentration decays by (1/2)^2 = 1/4, dropping from 740 MBq/mL to 740 / 4 = 185 MBq/mL at 11:40 AM. Volume needed = Prescribed Activity / Current Concentration = 370 MBq / 185 MBq/mL = 2.0 mL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00887","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Nuclear Medicine Instrumentation","topic":"Pulse Height Analyzer and Collimators","q":"During 99mTc-MDP bone scintigraphy (140 keV photopeak), why does the technologist set the gamma camera Pulse Height Analyzer (PHA) to a symmetric 20% energy window (126–154 keV) rather than leaving a wide-open energy window?","o":["To reject lower-energy Compton-scattered photons generated within the patient that degrade image contrast and spatial resolution","To amplify the gamma-ray energy inside the NaI(Tl) crystal above 511 keV","To eliminate septal penetration by increasing collimator hole diameter electronically","To convert 140 keV photons directly into positrons before reaching the PMTs"],"a":0,"e":"Compton scattering within the patient deflects photons away from their original line of travel and reduces their energy below 140 keV. A narrow 15–20% PHA window centered on the 140 keV photopeak (126–154 keV) accepts true unscattered primary events while rejecting lower-energy scattered photons, preserving contrast and spatial resolution.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00888","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"PET/CT Imaging and Molecular Diagnostics","topic":"Patient Preparation and Artifacts","q":"A 42-year-old lymphoma patient arrives for an 18F-FDG PET/CT scan with a fasting capillary blood glucose of 260 mg/dL, having eaten a sweet breakfast 1 hour earlier. Why must the technologist consult the nuclear medicine physician to reschedule or normalize glucose rather than injecting 18F-FDG immediately?","o":["Insulin induces brown adipose tissue thermogenesis that mimics hepatic metastases","Hyperglycemia and postprandial insulin drive competitive inhibition at tumor GLUT receptors and redirect FDG into skeletal muscle and myocardium, causing false-negative tumor results","High blood glucose chemically cleaves the 18F atom from deoxyglucose in the bloodstream, creating free fluoride bone uptake","Elevated glucose triggers annihilation of 511 keV photons inside the liver before coincidence detection"],"a":1,"e":"18F-FDG competes directly with endogenous D-glucose for GLUT transporters and hexokinase phosphorylation. Postprandial hyperglycemia and elevated insulin drastically lower tumor-to-background ratio by competing with tumor uptake and shunting FDG into insulin-sensitive skeletal muscle, myocardium, and adipose tissue. Guidelines recommend fasting 4–6 hours and blood glucose \u003c150–200 mg/dL (without IV rapid insulin immediately prior to injection).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00889","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Clinical Nuclear Medicine Procedures","topic":"Diuretic Renography","q":"A 4-year-old boy with right-sided hydronephrosis and mild renal impairment undergoes diuretic renography with 99mTc-MAG3 and IV furosemide (1 mg/kg). After furosemide administration, the right renal pelvic time-activity curve shows a rising/plateau pattern with a washout half-time (T1/2) of 28 minutes, while the left kidney washes out with a T1/2 of 6 minutes. What is the correct interpretation?","o":["Bilateral non-obstructed dilated collecting systems","Right kidney normal washout; left kidney obstructed","Right-sided mechanical urinary tract obstruction; normal unobstructed left kidney","Cortical scarring only; MAG3 cannot evaluate drainage"],"a":2,"e":"99mTc-MAG3 (mercaptoacetyltriglycine) is cleared primarily by proximal tubular secretion (~90%) and provides superior target-to-background images in children and impaired kidneys compared with 99mTc-DTPA (cleared by GFR). After furosemide, a washout T1/2 \u003c 10 minutes excludes significant obstruction (normal left kidney), 10–20 minutes is indeterminate, and \u003e 20 minutes (here 28 min) indicates high-grade mechanical obstruction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00890","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radionuclide Therapy and Radiation Safety","topic":"Isolation Ward and Discharge Criteria","q":"A 30-year-old woman with papillary thyroid carcinoma is scheduled for high-dose 131I ablation (3.7 GBq / 100 mCi) in an AERB-approved isolation ward. She is currently breastfeeding her 8-month-old infant. What is the mandatory instruction regarding breastfeeding and radiation safety?","o":["Pause breastfeeding for 24 hours only, pump and discard milk once, and resume nursing immediately after discharge","Give potassium iodide (Lugol\u0027s solution) to the infant daily while continuing breastfeeding during ablation","Administer 131I as an outpatient with no isolation because 3.7 GBq emits only alpha particles","Breastfeeding must be completely discontinued for this child (several weeks prior to 131I if possible) and never resumed after 131I administration; isolate patient in a delay-tank-equipped ward until exposure rate meets AERB discharge limits"],"a":3,"e":"131I concentrates avidly in lactating breast tissue (increasing maternal breast radiation dose) and is excreted in breast milk, posing extreme risk of thyroid ablation/carcinogenesis in the infant. Breastfeeding must be permanently stopped for the current child before 131I therapy (ideally 4–6 weeks prior to allow mammary involution). High-dose 131I (\u003e1.1 GBq / 30 mCi) requires inpatient isolation with dedicated delay tanks for excreta until dose rate at 1 m falls below AERB discharge thresholds.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00891","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radiopharmacy and Biodistribution","topic":"Mechanisms of Localization","q":"Match each 99mTc-labeled radiopharmaceutical with its primary mechanism of physiological localization: (1) 99mTc-MAA, (2) 99mTc-MDP, (3) 99mTc-Sulfur Colloid, (4) 99mTc-Sestamibi. Which matching sequence is correct?","o":["1-Pulmonary capillary blockade; 2-Chemisorption onto hydroxyapatite crystals; 3-Reticuloendothelial phagocytosis (Kupffer cells); 4-Passive lipophilic diffusion and mitochondrial retention","1-Capillary blockade; 2-RES phagocytosis; 3-Chemisorption onto hydroxyapatite; 4-Active Na+/I- symporter transport","1-Chemisorption onto hydroxyapatite; 2-Capillary blockade; 3-Mitochondrial retention; 4-RES phagocytosis","1-RES phagocytosis; 2-Mitochondrial retention; 3-Capillary blockade; 4-Chemisorption onto hydroxyapatite"],"a":0,"e":"99mTc-Macroaggregated Albumin (MAA, 10–90 µm particles) transiently embolizes \u003c0.1% of pulmonary precapillary arterioles/capillaries for lung perfusion imaging. 99mTc-MDP binds bone mineral via chemisorption onto hydroxyapatite crystals in areas of osteoblastic turnover. 99mTc-Sulfur Colloid is phagocytosed by RES macrophages (Kupffer cells in liver/spleen). 99mTc-Sestamibi is a lipophilic cation retained inside negatively charged mitochondria (myocardium and parathyroid adenomas).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00892","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Hot-Lab Quality Control \u0026 Dosimetry","topic":"Constancy, Linearity, Accuracy \u0026 Geometry Tests","q":"In hot-lab quality control of a pressurized ionization-chamber Dose Calibrator per AERB/NRC regulations, what does the QUARTERLY LINEARITY test verify, and how is it performed?","o":["Daily +/- 5% stability using a single long-lived Cs-137 source","Accurate proportional response across the entire clinical activity range (from high multi-hundred mCi eluate down to \u003c 30 uCi patient/breakthrough levels), tested via serial decay of a high-activity Tc-99m vial over 48-72 hours or calibrated graded lead sleeves","Energy photopeak FWHM resolution of a NaI(Tl) crystal","Differences in reading between plastic syringes and glass vials of varying liquid fill volumes"],"a":1,"e":"Dose calibrator QA comprises four distinct tests: (1) Daily Constancy (reproducibility with long-lived Cs-137/Co-57 check source), (2) Annual/Installation Accuracy (comparison against NIST/BARC-traceable standard), (3) Quarterly Linearity (verifying chamber ionization current remains linear without saturation or electronic nonlinearity from ~200-500 mCi down to ~10-30 uCi using Tc-99m decay or Calicheck sleeve attenuators), and (4) Geometry (correcting volume/container differences between syringes and vials).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00893","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Theranostics \u0026 Targeted Radionuclide Therapy","topic":"Lu-177 DOTATATE Nephroprotection","q":"During Lutetium-177 DOTATATE (Peptide Receptor Radionuclide Therapy, PRRT) for metastatic somatostatin-receptor-positive neuroendocrine tumors, why is an intravenous infusion of positively charged amino acids (L-lysine and L-arginine) administered before, during, and after the radiopharmaceutical?","o":["To stimulate tumor somatostatin receptor internalization","To chelate Lu-177 out of liver metastases","To competitively block proximal renal tubular reabsorption of the small radiolabeled peptide via the megalin/cubilin endocytic receptor, markedly reducing radiation dose to the radiosensitive renal cortex","To accelerate Lu-177 physical beta-decay half-life from 6.7 days to 6 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Low-molecular-weight radiolabeled peptides like Lu-177 DOTATATE are filtered freely by glomeruli and partially reabsorbed by proximal convoluted tubule cells via negatively charged megalin/cubilin receptors, exposing the renal cortex to high-dose beta radiation (497 keV max, t1/2 = 6.7 days). Co-infusing cationic L-lysine and L-arginine saturates megalin binding sites, cutting renal radiation absorbed dose by ~40-50% (note: monitor serum K+ and give antiemetics because lysine/arginine can cause nausea and hyperkalemia).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00894","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Clinical Nuclear Medicine Procedures","topic":"Myocardial Perfusion SPECT","q":"A 59-year-old obese man undergoes stress/rest 99mTc-Sestamibi gated SPECT. Both stress and rest images demonstrate reduced radiotracer counts in the inferior wall, but gated cine views reveal normal inferior wall motion and normal systolic wall thickening (\u003e40%). What is the most likely explanation for this finding?","o":["Reversible multi-vessel myocardial ischemia","Left anterior descending (LAD) territory stunning","Acute transmural inferior wall myocardial infarction","Diaphragmatic soft-tissue attenuation artifact rather than a true myocardial infarction scar"],"a":3,"e":"ECG-gated SPECT evaluates regional wall motion and systolic wall thickening simultaneously with perfusion. A true transmural infarction scar produces a fixed perfusion defect accompanied by akinesis/hypokinesis and absent systolic wall thickening. Conversely, a fixed inferior defect in a male (or anterior defect in a female from breast tissue) with preserved normal wall motion and thickening is classic for soft-tissue attenuation artifact—confirmable with prone imaging or CT attenuation correction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00895","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radiation Protection in Nuclear Medicine","topic":"Inverse Square Law and Lead HVL","q":"An unshielded syringe containing 99mTc (140 keV, HVL in lead ≈ 0.27 mm) produces an exposure rate of 400 µSv/h at a distance of 0.5 m. If the technologist steps back to 2.0 m AND places the syringe inside a lead shield of thickness 0.54 mm (2 HVLs), what is the resulting exposure rate?","o":["6.25 µSv/h","25.0 µSv/h","12.5 µSv/h","100.0 µSv/h"],"a":0,"e":"First apply the Inverse Square Law: moving from 0.5 m to 2.0 m increases distance by a factor of 4, reducing exposure rate by (0.5 / 2.0)^2 = 1/16 -\u003e 400 / 16 = 25 µSv/h. Next apply shielding attenuation for 2 Half-Value Layers (0.54 mm / 0.27 mm = 2 HVLs): 25 × (1/2)^2 = 25 / 4 = 6.25 µSv/h.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00896","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Clinical Nuclear Medicine Procedures","topic":"Thyroid Uptake and Scan Interpretation","q":"A 34-year-old woman presents 2 weeks after an upper respiratory viral illness with tender neck pain, palpitations, suppressed TSH (\u003c0.01 mIU/L), and elevated free T4. A 99mTc-pertechnetate thyroid scan shows near-absent thyroid visualization (high background activity), and her 24-hour 131I Radioactive Iodine Uptake (RAIU) is 1.2% (normal 10–30%). What is the diagnosis and implication for therapy?","o":["Toxic autonomous hot thyroid adenoma; schedule surgical lobectomy","Subacute (de Quervain\u0027s) thyroiditis with destructive hormone release; treat symptomatically (beta-blockers/NSAIDs), NOT with 131I ablation or thionamides","Graves\u0027 disease; proceed immediately with high-dose 131I therapy","Hypothyroidism; initiate lifelong levothyroxine immediately"],"a":1,"e":"Thyrotoxicosis accompanied by painful tender thyroid and markedly suppressed RAIU (\u003c2–5%) plus faint/absent pertechnetate trapping indicates destructive thyroiditis (subacute viral de Quervain\u0027s thyroiditis releasing preformed colloid stores), rather than active de novo hormone synthesis. Neither antithyroid drugs (methimazole/carbimazole) nor 131I therapy works when trapping is suppressed; supportive NSAIDs/corticosteroids and propranolol are indicated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00897","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radiopharmacy and Quality Control","topic":"Instant Thin Layer Chromatography (ITLC)","q":"A nuclear pharmacy technologist tests a newly reconstituted 99mTc-MDP vial using an ITLC-SG strip developed in acetone (where bound 99mTc-MDP and hydrolyzed-reduced Tc colloid stay at the origin Rf = 0, while free pertechnetate 99mTcO4- migrates to the solvent front Rf = 1.0). The origin measures 840,000 cpm and the solvent front measures 160,000 cpm. If the patient were injected with this batch, what biodistribution artifact would appear and should the vial be released?","o":["16% bound 99mTc-MDP; release the vial immediately because 84% free pertechnetate improves bone contrast","84% free pertechnetate; release only for brain perfusion scanning","16% free 99mTcO4- impurity (RCP = 84%, below USP \u003e=90% limit); reject the vial because injecting it would cause prominent unwanted uptake in the thyroid, stomach, and salivary glands","0% impurity; release the vial because acetone dissolves hydrolyzed colloid into bone"],"a":2,"e":"Total counts = 840,000 + 160,000 = 1,000,000 cpm. Free 99mTcO4- migrated to the solvent front = 160,000 / 1,000,000 = 16% impurity (maximum RCP \u003c= 84%, failing the \u003e= 90% USP standard for 99mTc-MDP). Injecting excess free pertechnetate causes prominent uptake in the thyroid gland, gastric mucosa, choroid plexus, and salivary glands, degrading skeletal scan quality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00898","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radionuclide Therapy and Theranostics","topic":"177Lu-DOTATATE Therapy","q":"During 177Lu-DOTATATE Peptide Receptor Radionuclide Therapy (PRRT) for somatostatin-receptor-positive metastatic neuroendocrine tumors, why is an intravenous solution of positively charged amino acids (L-lysine and L-arginine) infused starting 30 minutes prior to and continuing for 4 hours with the radiopharmaceutical?","o":["To prevent acute anaphylactic reactions to gadolinium contrast","To chelate free 177Lu directly in the bloodstream for rapid hepatobiliary clearance","To upregulate somatostatin SSTR2 receptor density on normal liver parenchyma","To competitively inhibit megalin/cubilin-mediated proximal renal tubular reabsorption of radiolabeled peptides, reducing radiation nephrotoxicity"],"a":3,"e":"Small radiolabeled somatostatin analogs like 177Lu-DOTATATE are filtered by the glomeruli and partially reabsorbed by megalin/cubilin receptors in the renal proximal convoluted tubules, exposing the radiosensitive renal cortex to high beta-radiation dose. Co-infusing cationic amino acids (L-lysine and L-arginine) competitively saturates tubular reabsorption, reducing renal absorbed dose by ~40–50%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00899","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Nuclear Medicine Instrumentation","topic":"Detector Crystals and Reconstruction","q":"Match each nuclear imaging instrumentation term with its functional role: (1) LYSO/LSO scintillator crystal, (2) Time-of-Flight (TOF) PET, (3) OSEM iterative reconstruction, (4) Center-of-Rotation (COR) calibration. Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Fast decay time and high stopping power for 511 keV coincidence photons; 2-Measures picosecond arrival difference of opposing photons to localize annihilation along the line of response; 3-Reduces streak artifacts and improves SNR compared with filtered backprojection; 4-Aligns gamma camera mechanical and electronic axes to prevent SPECT ring/blurring artifacts","1-Aligns SPECT mechanical/electronic axes; 2-Fast 511 keV PET scintillator; 3-Measures picosecond arrival difference; 4-Reduces streak artifacts","1-Fast 511 keV PET scintillator; 2-Aligns SPECT axes; 3-Measures picosecond arrival difference; 4-Reduces streak artifacts","1-Measures picosecond arrival difference; 2-Reduces streak artifacts; 3-Aligns SPECT axes; 4-Fast 511 keV PET scintillator"],"a":0,"e":"LSO/LYSO crystals replaced older NaI(Tl) and BGO in modern PET because of their high effective Z/density and ultra-short decay time (~40 ns), enabling Time-of-Flight (TOF) picosecond timing that improves image SNR (especially in large patients). OSEM (Ordered-Subsets Expectation Maximization) iteratively refines projections without FBP streak artifacts, while periodic SPECT COR calibration ensures concentric projection alignment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00900","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Clinical Nuclear Medicine Procedures","topic":"Ventilation-Perfusion (V/Q) Lung Scan","q":"A pregnant patient with suspected acute pulmonary embolism and a completely normal chest radiograph undergoes a V/Q lung scan. Ventilation SPECT (99mTc-DTPA aerosol) demonstrates uniform bilateral ventilation, while Perfusion SPECT (99mTc-MAA) shows three large, wedge-shaped, pleural-based defects in the right lower and left upper lobes. How is this V/Q scan interpreted, and what MAA particle precaution applies in severe pulmonary hypertension?","o":["Normal/low-probability scan because ventilation is normal; give 5,000,000 MAA particles in pulmonary hypertension","High-probability for pulmonary embolism (multiple large segmental V/Q mismatches with clear CXR); in severe pulmonary hypertension, reduce the number of administered MAA particles (e.g., to ~100,000–150,000) to avoid worsening right ventricular afterload","Exclusively non-diagnostic because 99mTc-MAA cannot cross the alveolar membrane","Matched COPD pattern; diagnose bullous emphysema"],"a":1,"e":"Per modified PIOPED II criteria, two or more large (\u003e75% of a segment) wedge-shaped perfusion defects with preserved ventilation and a clear chest X-ray (V/Q mismatch) indicate high probability for pulmonary embolism. Because 99mTc-MAA particles physically occlude precapillary arterioles, standard adult doses use 200,000–700,000 particles, whereas in severe pulmonary hypertension, right-to-left shunts, or pediatrics, particle count must be reduced (~100,000–150,000) to preserve safety.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00901","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Clinical Nuclear Medicine Procedures","topic":"Meckel\u0027s Diverticulum vs GI Bleed Scan","q":"A 3-year-old boy presents with recurrent painless bright-red rectal bleeding and anemia. A Meckel\u0027s diverticulum scan is ordered using 99mTc-sodium pertechnetate after pre-medication with an H2-receptor antagonist (famotidine/ranitidine). What constitutes a positive Meckel\u0027s scan, and why is the H2 blocker given?","o":["Focal uptake solely inside the urinary bladder; H2 blockers prevent renal excretion","Diffuse hepatic uptake at 24 hours; H2 blockers block Kupffer cell phagocytosis","A focal hotspot in the right lower quadrant appearing simultaneously with normal gastric mucosa and persisting/intensifying over 30–60 min; H2 blockers inhibit intraluminal secretion of pertechnetate by ectopic gastric cells so activity stays concentrated inside the diverticulum","Immediate rapid movement of radiotracer from the colon into the esophagus; H2 blockers accelerate peristalsis"],"a":2,"e":"Symptomatic Meckel\u0027s diverticula harbor ectopic gastric mucosa that secretes acid causing painless ileal ulceration. Mucoid/parietal cells take up 99mTc-pertechnetate at the exact same time as the native stomach (5–15 min) and appear as a fixed focal activity in the RLQ. Pre-treating with an H2 blocker reduces pertechnetate washout from the mucosal cells into the bowel lumen, boosting target-to-background contrast.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00902","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"PET/CT Imaging and Molecular Diagnostics","topic":"Standardized Uptake Value (SUVbw)","q":"A 70-kg patient undergoes an 18F-FDG PET/CT scan with a decay-corrected administered activity of 350 MBq (350,000 kBq). On the workstation ROI placed over a solitary pulmonary nodule, the decay-corrected tissue activity concentration measures 35.0 kBq/mL. Assuming tissue density = 1 g/mL, what is the patient\u0027s body-weight Standardized Uptake Value (SUVbw) for this nodule?","o":["5.0","35.0","0.14","7.0"],"a":3,"e":"SUVbw = [Tissue Activity Concentration (kBq/mL)] / [Injected Activity (kBq) / Body Weight (g)]. Here, uniform whole-body concentration = 350,000 kBq / 70,000 g = 5.0 kBq/g (5.0 kBq/mL). Therefore, SUVbw = 35.0 / 5.0 = 7.0 (indicating intense hypermetabolism relative to uniform whole-body distribution).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00903","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Clinical Nuclear Medicine Procedures","topic":"Parathyroid Adenoma Localization","q":"In dual-phase (15-minute early vs 2-hour delayed) 99mTc-Sestamibi scintigraphy for localizing a hyperfunctioning parathyroid adenoma, what differential washout mechanism causes the adenoma to stand out brightly on delayed images?","o":["99mTc-Sestamibi washes out rapidly from normal thyroid tissue while being retained avidly inside the abundant mitochondria of parathyroid adenoma oxyphil cells","99mTc-Sestamibi does not enter the thyroid on early 15-minute images","Normal thyroid tissue takes 4 hours to accumulate Sestamibi while adenomas take 1 minute","99mTc-Sestamibi is trapped via sodium-iodide symporter organification exclusively in parathyroid chief cells"],"a":0,"e":"99mTc-Sestamibi is a lipophilic monovalent cation accumulated passively in proportion to blood flow and negative mitochondrial membrane potential. Initially (10–15 min), both normal thyroid parenchyma and hypervascular parathyroid adenomas show intense uptake. By 2–3 hours, normal thyroid tissue clears Sestamibi via P-glycoprotein/washout, whereas mitochondria-rich oxyphil cells in the parathyroid adenoma retain Sestamibi brightly (often paired with SPECT/CT or 99mTc-pertechnetate thyroid subtraction).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00904","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radiopharmacy and Radionuclide Production","topic":"18F Production and Positron Range","q":"Which nuclear reaction produces 18F-fluoride in a medical cyclotron, and why does 18F provide superior PET spatial resolution compared with higher-energy positron emitters such as 68Ga or 82Rb?","o":["14N(alpha,n)18F; 18F has a 6-hour half-life and zero positron range","18O(p,n)18F on an enriched [18O]H2O water target; 18F emits lower-energy positrons (E_max = 0.635 MeV) that travel a much shorter mean range (~0.6 mm) in tissue before annihilation","99Mo(p,n)18F; 18F emits 140 keV gamma rays directly without positron annihilation","18O(n,p)18F in a nuclear reactor; 18F emits positrons with 3.5 MeV energy and 10 mm range"],"a":1,"e":"Proton bombardment of 18O-enriched water in a biomedical cyclotron drives the 18O(p,n)18F reaction, producing aqueous [18F]fluoride (t1/2 = 109.8 min). Because 18F positrons have low kinetic energy (E_max = 0.635 MeV), they thermalize over a very short distance (~0.6 mm mean range in water) before annihilating with an electron, minimizing positron-range blurring compared with 68Ga (E_max = 1.90 MeV) or 82Rb (E_max = 3.35 MeV).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00905","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Radiation Protection in Nuclear Medicine","topic":"Decontamination Protocols","q":"While drawing up 99mTc-MDP in the hot lab, a technologist accidentally drops a vial, splashing liquid onto the floor and onto her bare forearm. After alerting co-workers and covering the floor spill with absorbent pads, how should she decontaminate her forearm skin and clean the floor?","o":["Scrub the forearm vigorously with hot water, stiff wire brush, and concentrated bleach until bleeding","Apply a lead apron directly over the wet spill and leave the department without surveying","Wash the forearm gently with lukewarm water and mild soap from periphery inward (never hot water or abrasive scrubbing that opens pores/skin); mop the floor from the outer perimeter toward the center while monitoring with a GM survey meter","Sweep the liquid spill outward from the center toward the hallway with a dry broom"],"a":2,"e":"Skin decontamination removes external radionuclide safely without compromising the epidermal barrier: hot water causes vasodilation and abrasive scrubbing creates micro-abrasions that allow internal uptake! Gentle lukewarm soap-and-water washing (checked repeatedly with a survey meter) protects the skin. Area spills are cleaned from outside-inward to prevent spreading the footprint, with radioactive waste bagged, labeled, and stored for decay.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00906","course":"B.Sc Nuclear Medicine Technology","subject":"Clinical Nuclear Medicine Procedures","topic":"HIDA, Brain Death, DaTscan, and Infection Imaging","q":"Match each nuclear medicine study with its classic diagnostic finding: (1) 99mTc-DISIDA/HIDA cholescintigraphy, (2) 99mTc-HMPAO/ECD brain perfusion scan, (3) 123I-Ioflupane (DaTscan) SPECT, (4) 99mTc-HMPAO-labeled WBC / 67Ga scan. Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Non-visualization of gallbladder; 2-Loss of putaminal uptake; 3-Absent intracranial perfusion; 4-Focal osteomyelitis","1-Absent intracranial perfusion; 2-Non-visualization of gallbladder; 3-Focal osteomyelitis; 4-Loss of putaminal uptake","1-Loss of putaminal uptake; 2-Focal osteomyelitis; 3-Non-visualization of gallbladder; 4-Absent intracranial perfusion","1-Persistent non-visualization of gallbladder at 4 hours (or 30 min post-morphine) confirming acute cystic duct obstruction/cholecystitis; 2-\u0027Empty light bulb\u0027 / absent intracranial perfusion confirming brain death; 3-Loss of comma-shaped putaminal dopamine-transporter uptake distinguishing Parkinsonian syndromes from essential tremor; 4-Focal leukocytic accumulation confirming osteomyelitis or prosthetic infection"],"a":3,"e":"HIDA cholescintigraphy diagnoses acute cholecystitis when bile enters the duodenum but fails to enter the gallbladder due to cystic duct obstruction. Lipophilic 99mTc-HMPAO/ECD crosses the intact blood-brain barrier; absence of intracerebral activity with preserved scalp perfusion (\u0027empty light bulb\u0027 / \u0027hot nose\u0027 sign) confirms brain death. DaTscan binds presynaptic striatal dopamine transporters (degenerating posteriorly in the putamen first in Parkinson\u0027s disease). Labeled WBCs localize active neutrophilic infection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00907","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations \u0026 Communication","topic":"WHO 5 Moments for Hand Hygiene","q":"According to the WHO \"My 5 Moments for Hand Hygiene\" framework taught in the INC Revised B.Sc Nursing curriculum, which sequence accurately lists all five mandatory moments?","o":["Before touching a patient; Before clean/aseptic procedure; After body fluid exposure risk; After touching a patient; After touching patient surroundings","Before wearing sterile gown; After autoclaving; Before taking BP; After pharmacy pickup; Before shift end","Before administering oral tablets only; After touching clean linen; After visiting cafeteria; Before discharge; After cleaning floor","Only before entering the ward; Before meals; After removing gloves; Before charting; After shift handover"],"a":0,"e":"The WHO 5 Moments protect both the patient and the healthcare worker/environment: (1) Before touching a patient, (2) Before a clean/aseptic procedure, (3) After body fluid exposure risk, (4) After touching a patient, and (5) After touching patient surroundings.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00908","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health (Medical-Surgical) Nursing I \u0026 II","topic":"IV Fluid \u0026 Drug Drip Rate Calculation","q":"A physician prescribes 1,000 mL of Normal Saline (0.9% NaCl) to infuse intravenously over 8 hours using a macrodrip IV tubing set with a drop factor of 15 drops/mL. What flow rate in drops per minute (gtt/min) should the nurse set?","o":["15 drops/min","31 drops/min (approx. 31.25 gtt/min)","62 drops/min","125 drops/min"],"a":1,"e":"Flow rate (drops/min) = (Total Volume in mL x Drop Factor in drops/mL) / (Total Time in minutes) = (1,000 x 15) / (8 x 60) = 15,000 / 480 = 31.25, rounded to 31 drops/min.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00909","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research, Statistics, Management \u0026 Informatics","topic":"Sampling Techniques","q":"A nursing researcher wants equal representation of B.Sc Nursing students across all four academic years (1st, 2nd, 3rd, and 4th year) by dividing the student roster into year-wise subgroups and randomly selecting 25 students from each subgroup. Which sampling method is being used?","o":["Convenience (accidental) sampling","Purposive (judgemental) sampling","Stratified random sampling","Snowball sampling"],"a":2,"e":"Stratified random sampling divides a heterogeneous population into homogeneous strata (here, academic years 1-4) and draws a probability random sample from each stratum, reducing sampling error and ensuring subgroup representation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00910","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Conducting System of the Heart","q":"In a healthy adult at rest, why is there a physiological delay of approximately 0.1 seconds at the atrioventricular (AV) node before the cardiac impulse enters the bundle of His?","o":["To allow complete ventricular diastole before atrial contraction begins","To permit complete atrial systole and ventricular filling prior to ventricular contraction","To increase the intrinsic pacemaker firing rate above 100 beats per minute","To prevent retrograde conduction through the coronary sinus"],"a":1,"e":"The sinoatrial (SA) node initiates impulses at 60–100 beats/min, which travel across the atria via internodal pathways to the AV node. Because AV nodal cells have smaller diameters and fewer gap junctions, conduction slows, creating a physiological delay (~0.1 s, reflected in the PR interval). This pause ensures the atria finish contracting and ejecting blood (atrial kick) into the ventricles before ventricular systole begins.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00911","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Juxtaglomerular Apparatus and RAAS","q":"A patient develops acute hypovolaemia following gastrointestinal haemorrhage, leading to reduced renal afferent arteriolar stretch and decreased sodium chloride delivery to the macula densa. Which physiological response is immediately triggered by the juxtaglomerular apparatus?","o":["Inhibition of antidiuretic hormone secretion from the posterior pituitary","Secretion of atrial natriuretic peptide to promote diuresis","Vasodilation of the efferent arteriole to lower glomerular filtration rate","Release of renin from juxtaglomerular cells to activate the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system"],"a":3,"e":"Decreased renal perfusion pressure at the afferent arteriole (baroreceptor mechanism) and reduced NaCl delivery sensed by macula densa cells stimulate juxtaglomerular (JG) granular cells to secrete renin. Renin cleaves angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, which ACE converts to angiotensin II, causing systemic vasoconstriction, efferent arteriolar constriction to preserve GFR, and aldosterone release for Na+ and water retention.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00912","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Nasogastric Tube Verification","q":"At an OSCE station, a student nurse prepares to administer the first bolus enteral feed through a newly inserted Ryle\u0027s (nasogastric) tube in an alert adult patient. Which combination represents the safest, evidence-based verification practice before starting feeding?","o":["Confirm radiographic (X-ray) tip location for initial placement and verify gastric aspirate pH \u003c= 5.5 before subsequent feeds","Submerge the distal end of the tube in a cup of water to check for bubbling","Inject 20 mL of air while auscultating the epigastrium for a whooshing sound alone","Check only the external centimetre marking at the nostril without aspirating gastric contents"],"a":0,"e":"Per Potter \u0026 Perry and INC Nursing Foundations skills guidelines, radiographic confirmation is the gold standard to verify initial nasogastric tube tip placement in the stomach and exclude inadvertent tracheobronchial placement. Prior to each subsequent intermittent feed or medication, checking aspirate pH (\u003c= 5.5 indicates gastric acidity) alongside verifying the external nostril mark ensures continued safe positioning.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00913","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Intravenous Therapy","q":"A physician orders 1,000 mL of 0.9% Normal Saline to be infused intravenously over 8 hours for a dehydrated adult patient. If the standard macrodrip infusion set delivers 15 drops/mL, at what rate (rounded to the nearest whole number) should the nurse regulate the infusion?","o":["21 drops/min","31 drops/min","63 drops/min","42 drops/min"],"a":1,"e":"Using the standard IV flow rate formula: Drops/min = (Total Volume in mL × Drop Factor in drops/mL) ÷ (Time in hours × 60 minutes). Substituting the values: (1000 × 15) ÷ (8 × 60) = 15,000 ÷ 480 = 31.25 drops/min, which rounds to 31 drops/min.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00914","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Pressure Injury Risk Assessment","q":"A bedridden postoperative patient is assessed on admission using the Braden Scale and obtains a total score of 11. How should the nurse interpret this score and prioritize care?","o":["No risk for pressure injury because higher scores indicate severe skin breakdown","Mild risk requiring only nutritional supplementation without repositioning","High risk for pressure injury; implement 2-hourly repositioning, moisture barrier protection, and pressure-redistribution surface","Low risk for pressure injury; reassess once weekly"],"a":2,"e":"The Braden Scale evaluates six subscales (sensory perception, moisture, activity, mobility, nutrition, and friction/shear) with total scores ranging from 6 to 23. Lower scores signify greater risk: \u003c= 9 indicates very high risk, 10–12 high risk, 13–14 moderate risk, and 15–18 mild/at-risk. A score of 11 mandates intensive preventive nursing interventions including scheduled turning, offloading heels, moisture control, and pressure-relieving mattresses.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00915","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Medical-Surgical)","topic":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease","q":"A 64-year-old man with severe COPD is brought to the emergency ward with acute dyspnoea, drowsiness, SpO2 83% on room air, and ABG showing pH 7.28, PaCO2 64 mmHg, and PaO2 49 mmHg. Which oxygen delivery strategy and SpO2 target should the nurse implement?","o":["Start nasal cannula at 8 L/min immediately without pulse oximetry monitoring","Administer 100% oxygen via non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min targeting SpO2 100%","Withhold all supplemental oxygen until PaCO2 normalizes spontaneously","Deliver controlled low-flow oxygen via a 24% or 28% Venturi mask targeting SpO2 88%–92%"],"a":3,"e":"In acute COPD exacerbation with hypercapnic (Type II) respiratory failure, uncontrolled high-concentration oxygen can worsen hypercapnia through worsening ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch (loss of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction), the Haldane effect, and reduced respiratory drive. Controlled oxygen via a Venturi mask (24%–28%) titrated to a target SpO2 of 88%–92% corrects life-threatening hypoxaemia while minimizing worsening hypercapnic acidosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00916","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Medical-Surgical)","topic":"Intercostal Chest Drainage","q":"While assessing a postoperative thoracotomy patient with a water-seal intercostal chest drainage (ICD) system connected to low suction, the nurse observes vigorous, continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber. What is the nurse\u0027s most appropriate interpretation and immediate action?","o":["Continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber is always expected; document as normal","There is an air leak in the pleural space or drainage tubing; systematically check tubing connections from insertion site to unit","Clamp the chest tube immediately with two rubber-tipped forceps for 30 minutes to stop the leak","Raise the drainage bottle above the patient\u0027s chest level to equalize pressure"],"a":1,"e":"In a water-seal ICD unit, gentle tidaling (fluctuation of fluid level with respiration) in the water-seal chamber is normal, and gentle continuous bubbling belongs in the suction-control chamber—not the water-seal chamber. Continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber signifies an air leak, either from an unresolved bronchopleural fistula/pneumothorax or a loose connection/cracked tubing requiring systematic inspection and securing of junctions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00917","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Medical-Surgical)","topic":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA)","q":"A patient with Type 1 diabetes is admitted in diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) with blood glucose 460 mg/dL, serum K+ 2.9 mEq/L, pH 7.18, and severe dehydration. After initiating 0.9% Normal Saline infusion, why must the nurse hold the prescribed IV regular insulin infusion and notify the physician immediately?","o":["Administering insulin when serum potassium is \u003c 3.3 mEq/L shifts remaining extracellular K+ into cells and risks fatal hypokalaemic arrhythmias","Hyperglycaemia below 500 mg/dL does not require insulin therapy in DKA","IV regular insulin should never be used in DKA because subcutaneous NPH insulin acts faster","Normal saline alone lowers blood glucose to normal within 15 minutes without insulin"],"a":0,"e":"Insulin drives potassium intracellularly along with glucose. If baseline serum K+ is \u003c 3.3 mEq/L in DKA, starting IV insulin before potassium replacement can precipitate severe hypokalaemia, cardiac dysrhythmias, and respiratory muscle weakness. Fluid resuscitation with isotonic saline comes first, followed by IV KCl repletion until K+ \u003e= 3.3 mEq/L before starting regular insulin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00918","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Medical-Surgical)","topic":"Post-Thyroidectomy Complications","q":"Twelve hours after a total thyroidectomy, a 34-year-old woman complains of tingling around her mouth and fingertips. When the nurse inflates a blood pressure cuff on her arm for 3 minutes, her wrist and metacarpophalangeal joints flex spasmodically. Which complication has developed, and which emergency medication must be readily available at the bedside?","o":["Postoperative haemorrhage; IV tranexamic acid","Acute hypocalcaemia from parathyroid injury; IV 10% calcium gluconate","Thyroid storm; IV propranolol","Recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy; IV dexamethasone"],"a":1,"e":"Circumoral paraesthesia and a positive Trousseau sign (carpopedal spasm induced by BP cuff inflation above systolic pressure) are classic manifestations of acute hypocalcaemia resulting from accidental removal, trauma, or transient ischaemia of the parathyroid glands during thyroidectomy. Severe hypocalcaemia can progress to tetany, laryngospasm, and seizures; IV 10% calcium gluconate (and an emergency tracheostomy tray) must be kept at the bedside.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00919","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Cardiac Glycosides","q":"An elderly patient with heart failure receiving oral digoxin and furosemide reports anorexia, nausea, and seeing yellow-green halos around ward lights. Which electrolyte imbalance most strongly predisposes this patient to digoxin toxicity, and what pre-administration assessment is mandatory?","o":["Hypercalcaemia; measure respiratory rate for 30 seconds","Hypernatraemia; check radial pulse for 15 seconds","Hypokalaemia; auscultate apical pulse for 1 full minute","Hypermagnesaemia; check capillary refill time"],"a":2,"e":"Loop diuretics such as furosemide cause renal potassium wasting. Because potassium and digoxin compete for the same binding site on Na+/K+-ATPase, hypokalaemia markedly increases digoxin binding and precipitates toxicity (anorexia, GI upset, xanthopsia/visual halos, bradyarrhythmias, or ventricular ectopy). The nurse must auscultate the apical pulse for 1 full minute before each dose (holding and notifying the prescriber if adult HR \u003c 60 bpm) and monitor serum potassium and digoxin levels.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00920","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Anticoagulant Infusion Calculation","q":"A patient with acute pulmonary embolism is prescribed a continuous intravenous heparin infusion at 1,200 units/hour. The pharmacy supplies a premixed bag containing 25,000 units of heparin in 250 mL of 5% Dextrose. At what rate in mL/hour should the nurse program the infusion pump?","o":["6 mL/hr","18 mL/hr","24 mL/hr","12 mL/hr"],"a":3,"e":"First determine the concentration: 25,000 units ÷ 250 mL = 100 units/mL. Then divide the ordered hourly dose by the concentration: 1,200 units/hr ÷ 100 units/mL = 12 mL/hr. Independent double-checking of high-alert anticoagulants like heparin with another registered nurse is standard safety practice.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00921","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Weight-Based Oral Dosing","q":"A 15 kg toddler with acute otitis media is prescribed oral amoxicillin at 40 mg/kg/day in two equally divided doses every 12 hours. The available oral suspension is labelled 250 mg/5 mL. How many millilitres (mL) should the nurse administer per dose?","o":["6 mL","12 mL","9 mL","3 mL"],"a":0,"e":"Step 1: Calculate total daily dose = 15 kg × 40 mg/kg/day = 600 mg/day. Step 2: Divide into two equal doses = 600 mg ÷ 2 = 300 mg per dose. Step 3: Calculate volume using concentration 250 mg/5 mL (50 mg/mL): (300 mg ÷ 250 mg) × 5 mL = 6 mL per dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00922","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing II","topic":"Nephrotic Syndrome","q":"A 5-year-old boy is admitted with periorbital puffiness progressing to generalized oedema (anasarca), frothy urine, 4+ proteinuria, serum albumin 1.8 g/dL, and elevated serum cholesterol. Oral prednisolone is initiated. Which nursing monitoring measure is the most accurate daily indicator of fluid retention status?","o":["Measuring mid-upper arm circumference every evening","Checking daily morning weight on the same scale before breakfast along with strict intake-output charting","Monitoring capillary blood glucose once a week","Encouraging unrestricted dietary sodium to prevent hyponatraemia"],"a":1,"e":"Minimal-change nephrotic syndrome is characterized by massive proteinuria, hypoalbuminaemia, oedema, and hyperlipidaemia, responding primarily to oral prednisolone. Daily body weight measured at the same time each morning on the same scale with similar clothing, paired with accurate intake-output and abdominal girth measurement, provides the most reliable bedside index of oedema accumulation or diuresis. Nurses also monitor closely for infection (e.g., spontaneous bacterial peritonitis) due to immunoglobulin loss and steroid therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00923","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing I","topic":"Mood Stabilizers","q":"A 30-year-old patient with Bipolar I Disorder maintained on lithium carbonate (serum level 0.9 mEq/L last month) presents after 4 days of severe gastroenteritis with coarse hand tremors, ataxia, slurred speech, and vomiting. Which physiological mechanism explains this presentation, and what key health teaching prevents recurrence?","o":["Excessive dietary sodium intake accelerated lithium excretion; advise a strictly salt-free diet","High fluid intake diluted serum lithium; advise restricting water to 500 mL/day","Dehydration and sodium loss increased proximal renal tubular reabsorption of lithium; advise maintaining adequate hydration and normal salt intake","Lithium caused irreversible extrapyramidal rigidity; advise stopping all psychiatric medications permanently"],"a":2,"e":"Lithium has a narrow therapeutic window (0.6–1.2 mEq/L maintenance; toxicity \u003e= 1.5 mEq/L). Because renal proximal tubules handle lithium similarly to sodium, dehydration, sodium depletion, diarrhoea/vomiting, thiazide diuretics, or NSAIDs trigger compensatory Na+ and lithium reabsorption, causing serum lithium accumulation. Coarse tremors, ataxia, dysarthria, and GI distress require holding lithium, checking serum lithium and renal function, and teaching the patient to maintain steady hydration and normal dietary sodium.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00924","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing I","topic":"Therapeutic Communication","q":"During an inpatient mental health assessment, a depressed client looks down and says, \u0027Nobody cares if I live or die; I am just a burden to my family.\u0027 Which response by the nurse demonstrates therapeutic communication?","o":["\u0027Let\u0027s talk about your lunch menu instead so you don\u0027t feel sad.\u0027","\u0027Don\u0027t talk like that—your family loves you and everything will get better soon.\u0027","\u0027Why do you keep thinking you are a burden when they visit you?\u0027","\u0027It sounds like you are feeling overwhelmed and hopeless right now. Are you having thoughts of hurting yourself?\u0027"],"a":3,"e":"Reflecting the client\u0027s underlying feelings validates emotional pain without judgement, and asking directly and empathetically about suicidal ideation opens essential safety assessment without implanting the idea. Non-therapeutic blocks—such as false reassurance, probing \u0027why\u0027 questions, or changing the subject—shut down communication and miss acute suicide risk.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00925","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Severe Preeclampsia and Eclampsia Prophylaxis","q":"A primigravida at 36 weeks gestation with BP 166/112 mmHg, 3+ proteinuria, severe frontal headache, and epigastric pain is started on magnesium sulphate (Pritchard regimen) for seizure prophylaxis. Prior to administering the scheduled 4-hourly IM maintenance dose, the nurse finds absent patellar reflexes and a respiratory rate of 10 breaths/min. What should the nurse do immediately?","o":["Withhold magnesium sulphate, maintain airway/oxygen, notify the obstetrician, and prepare IV 10% calcium gluconate (10 mL)","Give an additional 4 g IV loading dose to deepen central nervous system sedation","Administer half the maintenance dose of magnesium sulphate and recheck in 1 hour","Administer furosemide IV rapidly without assessing urine output"],"a":0,"e":"Loss of deep tendon (patellar) reflexes (typically at serum Mg2+ ~8–10 mEq/L) followed by respiratory depression (\u003c 16 breaths/min, often \u003c 12/min) are hallmark signs of impending magnesium toxicity. Before every maintenance dose of MgSO4, the nurse must confirm: (1) patellar reflexes present, (2) respiratory rate \u003e= 16/min, and (3) urine output \u003e= 30 mL/hr. If toxicity develops, hold MgSO4 immediately, support ventilation, and administer the specific antidote: 10 mL of 10% calcium gluconate slowly IV over 3–10 minutes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00926","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Active Management of Third Stage of Labour (AMTSL)","q":"At a labour room OSCE station, immediately after delivery of a live singleton newborn and palpating the abdomen to rule out a second twin, what is the first essential pharmacological step of Active Management of Third Stage of Labour (AMTSL) per MoHFW Dakshata / WHO guidelines?","o":["Administer methylergometrine 0.2 mg IV to all mothers routinely regardless of blood pressure","Administer 10 IU Oxytocin intramuscularly within 1 minute of birth","Pull the umbilical cord briskly before any uterine contraction occurs","Perform immediate uterine curettage prior to placental separation"],"a":1,"e":"AMTSL significantly reduces postpartum haemorrhage due to uterine atony. Its core components per WHO and MoHFW Dakshata guidelines are: (1) confirming no second twin and administering Oxytocin 10 IU IM within 1 minute of delivery; (2) controlled cord traction (Brandt-Andrews manoeuvre) with simultaneous suprapubic counter-traction during a uterine contraction; and (3) checking uterine tone and massaging the uterus after placental delivery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00927","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP)","q":"During a community health clinic, a 42-year-old adult presents with productive cough for 3 weeks, evening rise of temperature, and unexplained weight loss. Under India\u0027s National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP), which diagnostic and treatment approach applies if drug-sensitive pulmonary TB is confirmed?","o":["Wait 6 weeks before sputum testing; treat with intermittent thrice-weekly DOT regimen","Treat empirically with single-drug isoniazid monotherapy for 3 months","Obtain sputum for microscopy/NAAT (CBNAAT/Truenat) now; initiate daily weight-band fixed-dose combination (2HRZE + 4HRE) with Nikshay Poshan Yojana support","Isolate the patient in a sanatorium for 6 months without oral therapy"],"a":2,"e":"Under NTEP, anyone with cough \u003e= 2 weeks (along with fever, night sweats, or weight loss) is a presumptive pulmonary TB case requiring prompt sputum examination (two sputum samples for smear microscopy and/or rapid molecular NAAT such as CBNAAT/Truenat to detect MTB and rifampicin resistance). Drug-sensitive TB is treated with a daily weight-band FDC regimen: 2 months intensive phase (HRZE) followed by 4 months continuation phase (HRE), alongside nutritional DBT under Nikshay Poshan Yojana.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00928","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing I","topic":"Biomedical Waste Management Rules (2016)","q":"According to the Biomedical Waste Management Rules (2016, India), into which colour-coded container must used intravenous tubing, urinary catheters, and syringes without fixed needles be segregated at the point of generation?","o":["Yellow non-chlorinated plastic bag","White translucent puncture-proof, leak-proof container","Blue cardboard box with blue marking","Red non-chlorinated plastic bag or container"],"a":3,"e":"Under India\u0027s BMW Management Rules (2016), contaminated recyclable plastic waste—including IV sets/tubing, catheters, urine bags, and syringes without needles—goes into the Red bag/container for autoclaving/microwaving and recycling. Yellow is for anatomical, soiled cotton/dressings, and chemical/expired cytotoxic waste; White translucent puncture-proof containers are for sharps/needles; Blue is for glassware and metallic implants.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00929","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Informatics and Technology","topic":"Medication Safety Technologies","q":"A hospital implements Barcode Medication Administration (BCMA) integrated with the Electronic Health Record (EHR). During medication rounds, the barcode scanner alerts the nurse of a dose mismatch because two 250 mg tablets were scanned instead of one 500 mg tablet. Which action upholds safe nursing informatics practice?","o":["Pause administration, verify the original prescription in the EHR, and resolve the discrepancy before giving the medication","Override the BCMA alert routinely to save time during busy shifts","Ask another patient to confirm the tablet strength","Switch off the bedside scanner and document administration manually from memory"],"a":0,"e":"BCMA strengthens the \u0027Rights\u0027 of medication administration (right patient, drug, dose, route, time) by electronically matching patient wristband barcodes with unit-dose medication barcodes against the EHR order. Alert fatigue and routine workarounds/overrides negate patient safety safeguards; any discrepancy requires pausing and verifying the primary order before administration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00930","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Critical Care)","topic":"Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) Interpretation","q":"A 58-year-old patient in septic shock exhibits deep, rapid respirations (28 breaths/min). Arterial blood gas (ABG) analysis shows: pH 7.26, PaCO2 28 mmHg, HCO3- 12 mEq/L, and PaO2 88 mmHg. How should the nurse interpret this ABG result?","o":["Uncompensated respiratory acidosis","Partially compensated metabolic acidosis","Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis","Fully compensated metabolic alkalosis"],"a":1,"e":"Normal ABG reference ranges are pH 7.35–7.45, PaCO2 35–45 mmHg, and HCO3- 22–26 mEq/L. Here, pH (7.26) is acidaemic (\u003c 7.35) and HCO3- (12 mEq/L) is markedly reduced, identifying metabolic acidosis as the primary disorder (lactic acidosis in sepsis). PaCO2 (28 mmHg) is also below normal because the lungs are hyperventilating to blow off CO2 as compensation, yet pH remains below 7.35—defining partially compensated metabolic acidosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00931","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Medical-Surgical)","topic":"Respiratory Alkalosis","q":"A 22-year-old student arrives in the emergency room breathing at 34 breaths/min during an acute panic attack, complaining of dizziness and tingling around her lips and fingers. ABG reveals pH 7.52, PaCO2 24 mmHg, and HCO3- 23 mEq/L. What is the acid-base disturbance and the mechanism behind her paraesthesias?","o":["Acute metabolic alkalosis causing severe hyperkalaemia","Compensated respiratory acidosis causing cerebral vasodilation","Acute uncompensated respiratory alkalosis causing increased calcium binding to albumin and lowered ionized Ca2+","Metabolic acidosis from lactic acid accumulation"],"a":2,"e":"Rapid hyperventilation blows off CO2 (PaCO2 24 mmHg \u003c 35 mmHg), raising arterial pH to 7.52 (\u003e 7.45) while HCO3- remains normal (23 mEq/L), indicating acute uncompensated respiratory alkalosis. As extracellular H+ falls, hydrogen ions dissociate from serum albumin, exposing negatively charged binding sites that bind free ionized calcium (Ca2+), producing functional hypocalcaemia symptoms (circumoral numbness, paraesthesia, carpopedal spasm) and cerebral vasoconstriction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00932","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Critical Care)","topic":"Electrical Therapy in Arrhythmias","q":"A patient on telemetry suddenly develops wide-complex ventricular tachycardia with no palpable carotid pulse and unresponsiveness. When preparing the biphasic defibrillator, which mode must the nurse ensure is selected before delivering the shock?","o":["Standby mode for 5 minutes to await spontaneous conversion","Transcutaneous pacing mode at 60 beats/min","Synchronized mode ON at 50 Joules to avoid the T wave","Unsynchronized defibrillation mode (Sync OFF) at 120–200 Joules immediately alongside continuous CPR"],"a":3,"e":"Pulseless ventricular tachycardia (pVT) and ventricular fibrillation (VF) are shockable cardiac arrest rhythms managed identically under ACLS algorithms with immediate unsynchronized defibrillation (biphasic 120–200 J per device recommendation) and minimal interruptions in chest compressions. Synchronized cardioversion (Sync ON) is reserved for unstable tachyarrhythmias WITH a pulse (e.g., SVT, atrial flutter, unstable VT with pulse) because synchronized discharge waits for an R wave and would fail to shock VF/chaotic pVT.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00933","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"High-Quality Adult CPR","q":"At a BLS OSCE station, a nursing student initiates single-rescuer CPR on an unresponsive adult without an advanced airway after confirming absent breathing and carotid pulse. Which performance parameters meet AHA Basic Life Support standards?","o":["Compress 5–6 cm deep at 100–120/min allowing full chest recoil, with 30:2 compression-to-ventilation ratio","Deliver 10 rescue breaths before starting chest compressions","Compress 3–4 cm deep at 80/min with 15:2 compression-to-ventilation ratio","Compress \u003e 7 cm deep at 150/min without allowing chest recoil"],"a":0,"e":"High-quality adult BLS requires C-A-B sequencing: chest compressions at a rate of 100–120/min, depth of 5–6 cm (2–2.4 inches), complete chest wall recoil after each compression (to maximize coronary and venous filling), minimizing pauses (\u003c 10 seconds), and a 30:2 compression-to-ventilation ratio until an advanced airway is placed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00934","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Medical-Surgical)","topic":"Severe Hyperkalaemia","q":"A 52-year-old man with end-stage renal disease who missed two haemodialysis sessions presents with muscle weakness, serum potassium 7.1 mEq/L, and an ECG showing tall peaked T waves with QRS widening. Which prescribed medication must the nurse administer first to immediately protect the heart from ventricular fibrillation?","o":["Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate","Intravenous 10% calcium gluconate slowly with continuous ECG monitoring","Subcutaneous NPH insulin alone without dextrose","Oral furosemide tablet"],"a":1,"e":"In severe hyperkalaemia with ECG changes (peaked T waves, PR prolongation, widened QRS, sine-wave pattern), IV 10% calcium gluconate acts within 1–3 minutes to antagonize potassium-induced cardiac membrane depolarization and stabilize the threshold potential, preventing fatal arrhythmias. Although calcium gluconate does not lower serum K+, it buys critical time for K+-shifting agents (IV regular insulin with dextrose, nebulized salbutamol) and definitive K+ removal (haemodialysis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00935","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Neurological Nursing)","topic":"Cushing\u0027s Triad and Herniation Risk","q":"A patient with traumatic brain injury exhibits a rising blood pressure from 130/80 to 188/68 mmHg, heart rate dropping from 88 to 48 bpm, and irregular Cheyne-Stokes respirations. Which pathophysiological event does this triad signify, and which positioning should the nurse maintain?","o":["Hypovolaemic shock; place in steep Trendelenburg position","Neurogenic shock; encourage vigorous coughing and deep Valsalva manoeuvres","Cushing\u0027s triad indicating severe raised intracranial pressure (ICP) and impending brainstem herniation; keep head of bed at 30 degrees with neck in neutral midline alignment","Autonomic dysreflexia; flex hips acutely to 90 degrees"],"a":2,"e":"Cushing\u0027s triad—widening pulse pressure (hypertension), bradycardia, and irregular respirations—is a late sign of severely elevated ICP compressing brainstem centres. Nursing measures to optimize cerebral venous outflow and avoid further ICP spikes include elevating the head of bed 30°, maintaining neutral head/neck alignment, avoiding hip flexion/Valsalva/unnecessary suctioning, preventing hyperthermia/hypoxia, and preparing osmotic therapy (IV mannitol or 3% hypertonic saline).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00936","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology \u0026 Critical Care Nursing","topic":"Weight-Based Vasopressor Infusion","q":"A 60 kg patient in shock is prescribed a continuous IV dopamine infusion at 5 mcg/kg/min. The pharmacy supplies 400 mg of dopamine diluted in 250 mL of 5% Dextrose. At what rate in mL/hour should the nurse set the infusion pump?","o":["5.6 mL/hr","45.0 mL/hr","22.5 mL/hr","11.25 mL/hr"],"a":3,"e":"Step 1: Calculate required dose per hour = 5 mcg/kg/min × 60 kg × 60 min/hr = 18,000 mcg/hr = 18 mg/hr. Step 2: Determine solution concentration = 400 mg ÷ 250 mL = 1.6 mg/mL. Step 3: Calculate infusion rate = 18 mg/hr ÷ 1.6 mg/mL = 11.25 mL/hr.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00937","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Oral Anticoagulants (Warfarin)","q":"A patient discharged on oral warfarin after mechanical mitral valve/DVT management asks whether she must completely eliminate spinach and other green leafy vegetables from her diet. What is the best evidence-based nursing response, and which reversal agent treats severe warfarin toxicity?","o":["Maintain a consistent daily intake of vitamin K-rich foods without sudden spikes or drops while monitoring INR; reversal agent is Vitamin K1 (phytonadione) and PCC/FFP","Eliminate all vitamin K foods permanently; reversal agent is protamine sulphate","Monitor aPTT daily at home; reversal agent is idarucizumab","Double the warfarin dose whenever eating vegetables; reversal agent is naloxone"],"a":0,"e":"Warfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase, reducing synthesis of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X. Sudden large increases in dietary vitamin K antagonize warfarin and lower INR, whereas sudden elimination raises INR and bleeding risk; thus, consistent moderate intake is advised. Excessive INR or major bleeding is reversed with Vitamin K1 (phytonadione) plus 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC) or fresh frozen plasma (FFP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00938","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Glycopeptide Antibiotics","q":"Ten minutes after starting an intravenous vancomycin infusion for MRSA bacteraemia, the patient develops intense flushing of the face, neck, and upper chest with generalized pruritus, but has clear lungs and no wheezing or angioedema. On inspection, the infusion was accidentally programmed to run over 20 minutes. What is the nurse\u0027s priority action?","o":["Continue the infusion at the same speed because flushing is benign","Pause the vancomycin infusion immediately, notify the prescriber, administer prescribed antihistamine if needed, and resume at a slower infusion rate (\u003e= 60–90 minutes) after symptom resolution","Document a lifelong true IgE anaphylactic allergy and discard all glycopeptides permanently","Administer rapid IV epinephrine 1 mg undiluted as for cardiac arrest"],"a":1,"e":"Rapid vancomycin infusion triggers non-IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation and histamine release (\u0027Red Man Syndrome\u0027 / vancomycin infusion reaction), manifesting as upper-body erythema and pruritus. Stopping the infusion immediately halts histamine release; once symptoms subside (often aided by diphenhydramine), the drug can typically be resumed safely at a slower rate (over at least 60–90 minutes, or 10 mg/min) with adequate dilution.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00939","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Indwelling Foley Catheter Care","q":"At a clinical nursing OSPE station assessing Catheter-Associated Urinary Tract Infection (CAUTI) prevention, which nursing practice breaches sterile closed-drainage maintenance?","o":["Securing the Foley catheter to the thigh/abdomen to prevent urethral traction","Performing daily meatal hygiene with soap and water and reviewing catheter necessity every day","Disconnecting the catheter-tubing junction routinely twice daily to irrigate with normal saline","Keeping the urinary drainage bag below bladder level without resting on the floor"],"a":2,"e":"Hospital infection control and Potter \u0026 Perry CAUTI bundles mandate keeping the indwelling urinary drainage system strictly closed and sterile. Routine bladder irrigation or disconnecting the catheter-to-tubing junction introduces ascending pathogens directly into the bladder and significantly increases CAUTI risk; irrigation is reserved only for specific obstructions (e.g., postoperative blood clots) using strict aseptic technique.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00940","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Oxygen Transport","q":"During vigorous exercise or acute febrile sepsis, skeletal muscle tissues exhibit elevated temperature, increased PaCO2, and decreased pH (acidaemia). How do these local conditions affect the oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve and tissue oxygen delivery?","o":["Prevent haemoglobin from binding oxygen in pulmonary alveoli completely","Shift the curve to the left, increasing haemoglobin\u0027s affinity for oxygen and reducing unloading","Convert haemoglobin permanently into methaemoglobin","Shift the curve to the right (Bohr effect), decreasing haemoglobin\u0027s affinity for oxygen and facilitating oxygen unloading to tissues"],"a":3,"e":"Increased H+ (low pH), elevated PaCO2, fever, and increased 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG) shift the oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve to the right (the Bohr effect). A rightward shift lowers haemoglobin\u0027s affinity for O2 at peripheral capillary PO2 levels, promoting greater oxygen release to meet heightened tissue metabolic demand.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00941","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Pathophysiology \u0026 Adult Health Nursing I","topic":"Hepatic Cirrhosis and Portal Hypertension","q":"In a patient with decompensated alcoholic cirrhosis presenting with tense ascites and pedal oedema, which combined pathophysiological mechanism primarily drives peritoneal fluid accumulation?","o":["Elevated portal venous hydrostatic pressure, splanchnic vasodilation with RAAS activation, and hypoalbuminaemia reducing plasma oncotic pressure","Increased glomerular filtration rate causing pure osmotic diuresis","Increased hepatic albumin synthesis raising plasma oncotic pressure","Excessive biliary excretion of sodium into the duodenum"],"a":0,"e":"Fibrotic nodularity in cirrhosis obstructs sinusoidal blood flow, causing portal hypertension (\u003e 12 mmHg) and nitric-oxide-mediated splanchnic arterial vasodilation. Decreased effective arterial blood volume activates RAAS and ADH, promoting avid renal Na+ and water retention, while impaired hepatocyte albumin synthesis lowers plasma colloid oncotic pressure—transuding fluid into the peritoneal cavity (ascites).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00942","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)","q":"An 18-month-old child with unrepaired Tetralogy of Fallot begins crying intensely during venipuncture and suddenly develops deep central cyanosis, rapid breathing, and limpness (\u0027Tet\u0027 spell). What is the immediate priority positioning intervention by the nurse?","o":["Place the child flat supine with legs extended straight","Place the child in the knee-chest (squatting) position while keeping the child calm and administering blow-by oxygen","Hold the child upright with feet hanging down below the bed","Perform vigorous chest physiotherapy immediately"],"a":1,"e":"During a hypercyanotic (\u0027Tet\u0027) spell, infundibular spasm increases right ventricular outflow resistance, worsening right-to-left shunting of deoxygenated blood across the VSD into the aorta. Placing the child in the knee-chest position kinks femoral arteries and increases systemic vascular resistance (SVR), which opposes right-to-left shunting and forces more blood through the pulmonary artery for oxygenation, alongside calming, oxygen, IV morphine, and fluid bolus/phenylephrine as prescribed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00943","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Maintenance Fluid Calculation","q":"A 24 kg child admitted with acute appendicitis is kept nil per os (NPO) prior to surgery. Using the standard Holliday-Segar method (100 mL/kg for the first 10 kg, 50 mL/kg for the next 10 kg, and 20 mL/kg for each remaining kg), what is the child\u0027s total 24-hour maintenance fluid requirement?","o":["1,240 mL/day","2,400 mL/day","1,580 mL/day","1,480 mL/day"],"a":2,"e":"Using the Holliday-Segar formula for a 24 kg child: First 10 kg × 100 mL/kg = 1,000 mL; next 10 kg × 50 mL/kg = 500 mL; remaining 4 kg (24 - 20) × 20 mL/kg = 80 mL. Summing all components: 1,000 + 500 + 80 = 1,580 mL/day (approximately 65.8 mL/hr).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00944","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing II","topic":"Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS)","q":"Five days after increasing parenteral haloperidol dosage for acute psychosis, a 29-year-old man develops a temperature of 40.3°C, severe generalized \u0027lead-pipe\u0027 muscular rigidity, mutism, diaphoresis, labile blood pressure (164/102 mmHg), and serum creatine kinase (CK) 14,500 U/L. Which life-threatening syndrome has developed, and what is the first nursing action?","o":["Serotonin syndrome from lithium monotherapy; give fluoxetine","Mild akathisia; encourage vigorous treadmill exercise","Tardive dyskinesia; switch immediately to chlorpromazine","Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS); withhold all antipsychotics immediately, notify the team, initiate active cooling/IV hydration, and prepare dantrolene or bromocriptine"],"a":3,"e":"NMS is a rare, potentially fatal idiosyncratic reaction to dopamine D2 receptor antagonists (especially high-potency first-generation antipsychotics) characterized by the tetrad of hyperthermia, severe \u0027lead-pipe\u0027 rigidity, autonomic instability, and altered consciousness with markedly elevated CK/rhabdomyolysis and leukocytosis. Immediate cessation of the offending antipsychotic, aggressive supportive cooling and hydration (to prevent acute kidney injury from myoglobinuria), and specific pharmacotherapy (dantrolene sodium and/or bromocriptine) are essential.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00945","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing II","topic":"Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT)","q":"Which pre- and post-procedure nursing protocol is essential for a patient scheduled for modified Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT) for severe catatonic depression?","o":["Keep NPO for 6–8 hours, verify informed consent, have the patient void, remove dentures/metallic hairpins, prepare anticholinergic/anaesthetic/succinylcholine, and position laterally post-ECT until gag reflex returns","Administer full morning doses of oral anticonvulsants just before ECT to deepen seizure threshold","Give a full breakfast 1 hour prior to ECT and keep dentures in place","Restrain the patient tightly in prone position during recovery without checking orientation"],"a":0,"e":"Modified ECT uses a short-acting IV anaesthetic (e.g., propofol/methohexital), a depolarizing muscle relaxant (succinylcholine) to prevent fractures, and an anticholinergic (glycopyrrolate/atropine) to blunt vagal bradycardia and secretions. Pre-ECT nursing checks include NPO 6–8 hours (aspiration prevention), signed informed consent, empty bladder, removal of dentures/jewellery/hairpins, and holding anticonvulsants/lithium. Post-ECT care includes lateral recovery position, airway/oxygen monitoring, and gentle reorientation for transient confusion/amnesia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00946","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Eclampsia Seizure Management","q":"A woman at 37 weeks gestation with severe preeclampsia suddenly develops a generalized tonic-clonic eclamptic seizure in the antenatal ward. During the active convulsion, which nursing action is safest and highest priority?","o":["Give 200 mL of oral water immediately to prevent dehydration","Turn her onto her left lateral side, protect her head/limbs from injury without restraining movements, clear secretions after the convulsion, give high-flow oxygen, and administer prescribed IV/IM magnesium sulphate","Place her supine with pillows under her knees and wait to call the obstetrician","Force a metal spoon or padded tongue blade between her clenched teeth"],"a":1,"e":"During an active eclamptic seizure, turning the woman to the left lateral decubitus position relieves aortocaval compression (improving uteroplacental perfusion) and allows saliva/vomitus to drain out, preventing aspiration. Padding bed rails and guiding rather than forcibly restraining limbs prevents fractures/dislocations; never pry open clenched jaws, which breaks teeth and causes oral trauma. Immediately follow with suctioning, oxygen, and MgSO4 loading dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00947","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Rh Isoimmunization Prevention","q":"An Rh-negative mother with a negative Indirect Coombs Test (ICT) delivers a healthy Rh-positive newborn (Direct Coombs Test negative). Within what maximum postpartum window should the nurse administer 300 mcg of Anti-D immunoglobulin intramuscularly to prevent Rh alloimmunization in future pregnancies?","o":["Within 12 months postpartum","Administer to the newborn rather than the mother","Within 72 hours of delivery","Only before conception of the first pregnancy"],"a":2,"e":"Foetomaternal haemorrhage during delivery can expose an unsensitized Rh-negative mother to Rh-positive foetal erythrocytes. Administering 300 mcg (1,500 IU) of Anti-D immunoglobulin IM to the mother within 72 hours of birth (in addition to routine antenatal prophylaxis at 28 weeks and after sensitizing events like abortion or amniocentesis) clears foetal D-positive RBCs before maternal memory B-cell sensitization occurs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00948","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Dengue Fever Clinical Phases","q":"On Day 5 of dengue fever, as a 26-year-old patient\u0027s high fever begins to subside, he develops persistent vomiting, severe right upper quadrant abdominal pain, gum bleeding, and a haematocrit rising by 22% with platelets 38,000/mm3. What does this clinical shift represent, and which analgesic must be strictly avoided?","o":["Full recovery phase; avoid paracetamol","Typhoid relapse; administer aspirin 650 mg","Mild viral resolution; discharge home without follow-up","Critical plasma leakage phase (\u0027Dengue with Warning Signs\u0027) requiring urgent inpatient IV fluid titration; strictly avoid NSAIDs/aspirin and intramuscular injections"],"a":3,"e":"In dengue, defervescence (Days 3–7) marks entry into the critical phase when increased capillary permeability can precipitate hypovolaemic dengue shock syndrome. Persistent vomiting, abdominal pain, mucosal bleeding, clinical fluid accumulation, hepatomegaly, and rising haematocrit with falling platelets are NVBDCP/WHO warning signs requiring inpatient isotonic fluid management. Aspirin, ibuprofen/diclofenac (NSAIDs), and IM injections are contraindicated due to severe bleeding and haematoma risk; paracetamol is used for fever.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00949","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing I","topic":"Screening Test Validity","q":"A state health department selects an initial community screening test for a serious, treatable disease where missing true cases (false negatives) would be life-threatening. Which test characteristic is most critical for this initial first-line screening tool?","o":["High sensitivity (high true-positive rate, minimizing false negatives)","Low cost with 10% true-positive detection","High prevalence of disease in healthy controls","High specificity only, regardless of how many true cases are missed"],"a":0,"e":"Sensitivity [TP ÷ (TP + FN)] measures a test\u0027s ability to correctly identify individuals who truly have the disease. For an initial community screening test targeting a serious, treatable condition, high sensitivity is prioritized to minimize false negatives so affected persons are captured and referred for a subsequent high-specificity confirmatory diagnostic test.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00950","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Ethics, Legal Issues and Professionalism","topic":"Patient Autonomy and Informed Refusal","q":"A mentally alert, fully competent 40-year-old adult with gastrointestinal bleeding (Hb 6.8 g/dL) clearly refuses a blood transfusion on personal religious grounds even after the physician explains the risk of death. Which nursing action upholds the INC Code of Ethics and bioethical principles?","o":["Ask the ward clerk to sign consent on the patient\u0027s behalf","Respect the patient\u0027s informed refusal under the ethical principle of autonomy, verify comprehension without coercion, notify the physician, and document signed refusal while offering non-blood alternatives","Restrain the patient and transfuse under paternalism","Administer the blood transfusion covertly while the patient is asleep to preserve life"],"a":1,"e":"Under bioethics and the Indian Nursing Council (INC) Code of Ethics, a competent adult possesses the legal and ethical right to self-determination (autonomy), including informed refusal of life-saving interventions once risks, benefits, and alternatives are clearly explained. Coercing or covertly administering treatment constitutes battery and violates autonomy; the nurse advocates for alternative medical options (e.g., crystalloids, erythropoietin, iron, haemostatics) and ensures formal documentation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00951","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Bias Control in Randomized Controlled Trials","q":"In a nursing Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT) comparing a novel wound dressing against standard gauze, researchers use sequentially numbered, opaque, sealed envelopes (SNOSE) managed by an off-site coordinator prior to enrolment, and ensure neither the patient nor the outcome assessor knows which dressing was applied. What biases do these two safeguards respectively prevent?","o":["Allocation concealment prevents selection bias at enrolment; blinding prevents performance and detection (measurement) bias after assignment","Both prevent only recall bias in retrospective cohorts","Allocation concealment replaces informed consent; blinding eliminates attrition completely","SNOSE prevents statistical Type I error solely"],"a":0,"e":"Allocation concealment (e.g., SNOSE or central computer randomization) shields the upcoming group assignment from the recruiting clinician until the participant is formally enrolled, preventing selection bias. Blinding (masking participants, care providers, and/or outcome assessors) after randomization prevents differential co-interventions (performance bias) and subjective outcome rating distortion (detection/ascertainment bias).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00952","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Oncology Nursing)","topic":"Febrile Neutropenia","q":"A leukaemia patient 9 days post-chemotherapy has an absolute neutrophil count (ANC) of 320 cells/mm3 and develops a single oral temperature of 38.5°C. Which nursing order set and precaution is urgent and safe?","o":["Wait 48 hours for culture sensitivity results before giving any antibiotic","Place the patient in a shared room with a patient who has active varicella","Obtain two sets of blood cultures (peripheral and central line) immediately, initiate prescribed broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within 1 hour, and strictly avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories, or fresh raw flowers","Check rectal temperature every hour and give a rectal paracetamol suppository"],"a":2,"e":"Febrile neutropenia (ANC \u003c 500 cells/mm3 with single temperature \u003e= 38.3°C or \u003e= 38.0°C sustained for 1 hour) is an oncological emergency with rapid progression to septic shock because inflammatory signs like pus are blunted. Blood cultures must be drawn promptly without delaying empirical broad-spectrum anti-pseudomonal IV antibiotics beyond 60 minutes. Rectal procedures risk mucosal micro-tears and Gram-negative translocation; strict hand hygiene and avoiding stagnant water/fresh flowers reduce opportunistic fungal/bacterial exposure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00953","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Haematology)","topic":"Acute Haemolytic Transfusion Reaction","q":"At an OSCE station, 10 minutes after starting a packed red blood cell (PRBC) transfusion, the patient develops chills, fever (39.1°C), severe lower back pain, hypotension (86/54 mmHg), and dark red urine. What is the nurse\u0027s very first immediate action?","o":["Slow the blood infusion to 10 drops/min and give oral paracetamol","Flush the existing blood administration tubing with 20 mL saline to clear the vein","Complete the remaining unit slowly after giving an antihistamine","Stop the blood transfusion immediately, disconnect the blood tubing at the cannula hub, and maintain IV patency using fresh 0.9% Normal Saline tubing"],"a":3,"e":"Fever, rigors, lumbar/flank pain, hypotension, and haemoglobinuria signal an acute haemolytic transfusion reaction (usually ABO incompatibility). The absolute first step is stopping the transfusion immediately to limit further incompatible RBCs, replacing the blood set directly at the catheter hub with fresh 0.9% Normal Saline tubing (never flushing blood trapped inside the old tubing into the patient), monitoring vital signs, notifying the physician and blood bank, and returning the blood bag, tubing, and post-reaction blood/urine specimens.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00954","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Acute Epiglottitis","q":"An unvaccinated 4-year-old child presents with abrupt high fever, muffled (\u0027hot-potato\u0027) voice, marked drooling, dysphagia, and sits upright leaning forward with chin thrust out (tripod position). Which nursing action MUST be strictly avoided while awaiting senior airway support?","o":["Attempting to inspect the posterior pharynx using a wooden tongue depressor or obtaining a forceful throat swab","Administering humidified blow-by oxygen gently if tolerated","Allowing the child to remain calm in the parent\u0027s lap in an upright position","Having emergency intubation and tracheostomy equipment ready at the bedside"],"a":0,"e":"Acute epiglottitis (classically caused by Haemophilus influenzae type b) causes rapid supraglottic oedema. The classic \u00274 Ds\u0027—drooling, dysphagia, dysphonia, and distress—with tripod posturing warn of imminent airway occlusion. Visualizing the throat with a tongue depressor, forcing the child supine, or agitating the child for painful procedures can trigger sudden vagal/laryngospastic complete airway obstruction and respiratory arrest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00955","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF) Circulation","q":"Which anatomical pathway accurately traces cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) flow from its primary site of production in the lateral ventricles back into the venous circulation?","o":["Lateral ventricles -\u003e cerebral aqueduct -\u003e third ventricle -\u003e fourth ventricle -\u003e dural sinuses","Lateral ventricles -\u003e interventricular foramina (Monro) -\u003e third ventricle -\u003e cerebral aqueduct -\u003e fourth ventricle -\u003e foramina of Luschka and Magendie -\u003e subarachnoid space -\u003e arachnoid villi","Fourth ventricle -\u003e third ventricle -\u003e lateral ventricles -\u003e central canal -\u003e thoracic duct","Subarachnoid space -\u003e choroid plexus -\u003e foramen magnum -\u003e internal carotid artery"],"a":1,"e":"CSF is secreted primarily by the choroid plexuses within the ventricles. From the lateral ventricles, CSF passes through the paired interventricular foramina of Monro into the third ventricle, descends via the cerebral aqueduct of Sylvius into the fourth ventricle, exits through the lateral foramina of Luschka and median foramen of Magendie into the subarachnoid space, and is reabsorbed through arachnoid granulations (villi) into the superior sagittal dural venous sinus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00956","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Cardiac Output Regulation","q":"A postoperative patient receives an appropriate 500 mL isotonic crystalloid bolus for mild hypovolaemia, increasing venous return and end-diastolic ventricular volume, which boosts stroke volume. Which physiological principle accounts for this rise in cardiac output?","o":["Haldane effect on carbon dioxide unloading","Laplace\u0027s law of alveolar surface tension","Frank-Starling law of the heart (greater myocardial fibre stretch within physiological limits yields stronger systolic contraction)","Poiseuille\u0027s law of airway resistance"],"a":2,"e":"According to the Frank-Starling law, increasing venous return (preload) stretches ventricular cardiomyocytes during diastole, optimizing actin-myosin cross-bridge overlap and calcium sensitivity so the heart contracts more forcefully and ejects a larger stroke volume. However, excessive fluid overload in a failing ventricle overstretches sarcomeres beyond the optimal plateau, causing stroke volume to fall and pulmonary congestion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00957","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Surgical Asepsis","q":"At a surgical asepsis OSCE station, a nursing student performs open sterile gloving prior to a bedside sterile dressing. Which action maintains strict surgical asepsis while donning the second glove?","o":["Touch the outer fingers of the first glove against the sterile wrapper edge to adjust fit","Use the bare left hand to pull up the outer surface of the right glove","Blow air into the glove fingers to open them quickly","Slip the sterile-gloved fingers of the dominant hand underneath the folded outer cuff of the second glove without touching the inner cuff or bare wrist"],"a":3,"e":"Open sterile gloving follows the \u0027sterile-to-sterile, non-sterile-to-non-sterile\u0027 rule. The bare hand grasps only the folded inner cuff (skin-contact side) of the first glove. Once the first hand is sterile-gloved, its fingers slide beneath the everted outer cuff (sterile side) of the second glove, keeping the thumb abducted away from the ungloved wrist to prevent contamination.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00958","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations \u0026 Pharmacology","topic":"Short-Duration Loading Infusion Rate","q":"An obstetrician orders a loading dose of 4 g Magnesium Sulphate diluted in 100 mL of 0.9% Normal Saline to be infused over exactly 20 minutes via a volumetric infusion pump. At what rate (mL/hour) must the nurse set the pump?","o":["300 mL/hr","150 mL/hr","100 mL/hr","200 mL/hr"],"a":0,"e":"Electronic volumetric pumps are programmed in mL/hour. Convert 20 minutes to hours: 20 min ÷ 60 min/hr = 1/3 hour. Rate (mL/hr) = Total Volume (mL) ÷ Time (hr) = 100 mL ÷ (1/3 hr) = 100 × 3 = 300 mL/hr. Setting the pump with a 100 mL volume-to-be-infused (VTBI) limit prevents accidental over-infusion after 20 minutes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00959","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)","q":"During neurological assessment of a head-injury patient, the nurse observes that the patient opens eyes only to painful pressure (supraorbital/nailbed), utters inappropriate isolated words, and pushes the nurse\u0027s hand away toward the painful stimulus (localizes pain). What is the total Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score?","o":["14","10","12","8"],"a":1,"e":"Using standard adult GCS domains: Eye opening (E1 none, E2 to pressure/pain, E3 to sound, E4 spontaneous) = 2; Verbal response (V1 none, V2 incomprehensible sounds, V3 inappropriate words, V4 confused, V5 oriented) = 3; Motor response (M1 none, M2 extension, M3 abnormal flexion, M4 normal flexion/withdrawal, M5 localizing pain, M6 obeys commands) = 5. Total GCS = 2 + 3 + 5 = 10 (moderate head injury range 9–12).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00960","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Neurology)","topic":"Myasthenia Gravis Crises","q":"A 35-year-old woman with Myasthenia Gravis on oral pyridostigmine presents with progressive ptosis, dysphagia, weak cough, shallow respirations, and SpO2 88%, alongside severe abdominal cramping, hypersalivation, miosis, and muscle fasciculations after taking extra tablets. What is the nurse\u0027s priority intervention alongside preparing ventilatory support?","o":["Encourage oral fluids to clear thick secretions","Administer double doses of pyridostigmine immediately","Recognize cholinergic crisis (anticholinesterase excess), hold pyridostigmine, protect airway/prepare intubation, and have IV atropine ready","Perform vigorous chest percussion without airway protection"],"a":2,"e":"Both myasthenic crisis (under-dosing/infection) and cholinergic crisis (excessive acetylcholinesterase inhibition) cause life-threatening respiratory muscle weakness. Cholinergic crisis is distinguished by muscarinic excess (\u0027SLUDGE\u0027: salivation, lacrimation, urination, diaphoresis, GI cramping, emesis, miosis, bronchorrhoea) and nicotinic fasciculations. Airway/mechanical ventilation is paramount in both; in cholinergic crisis, anticholinesterases must be withheld and atropine administered for muscarinic toxicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00961","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Endocrinology)","topic":"SIADH vs Diabetes Insipidus","q":"Two days after transsphenoidal pituitary surgery, a patient develops lethargy and confusion. Lab tests reveal serum Na+ 121 mEq/L, low serum osmolality (254 mOsm/kg), urine osmolality 640 mOsm/kg, urine specific gravity 1.032, and no peripheral oedema. Which disorder is present, and what is the primary nursing intervention?","o":["Acute tubular necrosis; administer oral potassium chloride","Adrenal crisis; infuse 2,000 mL 5% Dextrose in water rapidly","Diabetes Insipidus; encourage unlimited free water intake","Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone (SIADH); implement strict fluid restriction (e.g., 800–1,000 mL/day), seizure precautions, daily weights, and cautious sodium correction"],"a":3,"e":"SIADH results from excessive ADH release causing inappropriate renal free-water retention, dilutional euvolaemic hyponatraemia, low serum osmolality, and inappropriately concentrated urine (high urine osmolality and specific gravity \u003e 1.030). Primary management includes strict fluid restriction, daily weights, neuro/seizure precautions, and cautious 3% hypertonic saline only for severe symptomatic hyponatraemia (avoiding rapid overcorrection \u003e 8–10 mEq/L in 24 h to prevent osmotic demyelination syndrome).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00962","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Neurology)","topic":"Autonomic Dysreflexia","q":"A hospitalized patient with a T4 spinal cord injury suddenly develops a severe throbbing headache, BP 214/118 mmHg, heart rate 46 bpm, nasal congestion, and profuse sweating above the nipple line. What is the nurse\u0027s immediate sequence of actions?","o":["Raise the head of the bed to 90 degrees (high Fowler\u0027s), loosen tight clothing/binders, and immediately check for a kinked Foley catheter/bladder distension or faecal impaction","Lower the head of the bed flat supine and administer IV atropine","Ignore the blood pressure because bradycardia indicates normal rest","Place the patient in Trendelenburg position and wait 1 hour"],"a":0,"e":"Autonomic dysreflexia is a hypertensive emergency occurring in spinal cord injuries at or above T6 when a noxious stimulus below the lesion triggers massive uninhibited sympathetic vasoconstriction below T6 and baroreceptor-mediated vagal bradycardia/vasodilation above. Sitting the patient upright uses orthostasis to lower blood pressure immediately while the nurse locates and relieves the trigger—most commonly urinary retention/kinked catheter or bowel impaction (using anaesthetic jelly before disimpaction).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00963","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Gastrointestinal)","topic":"Acute Pancreatitis","q":"A 48-year-old man with gallstone-induced acute pancreatitis has severe epigastric pain radiating straight through to the back and bluish-grey bruising in the flanks (Grey Turner\u0027s sign). Which serum test is most specific for confirming pancreatitis, and which position best eases his pain?","o":["Serum alkaline phosphatase; flat prone position","Serum lipase (\u003e 3 times upper normal limit); sitting upright leaning forward or side-lying with knees flexed","Urine ketones; brisk ambulation","Serum creatinine kinase; Trendelenburg position"],"a":1,"e":"Serum lipase is more specific to pancreatic acinar injury than serum amylase and remains elevated longer (7–14 days). Grey Turner\u0027s sign (flank ecchymosis) and Cullen\u0027s sign (periumbilical ecchymosis) reflect retroperitoneal haemorrhage in severe necrotizing pancreatitis. Leaning forward or assuming a knee-chest/foetal position reduces tension on the retroperitoneal peritoneum and eases pain alongside NPO rest, aggressive IV fluids, and analgesia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00964","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Aminoglycoside Toxicity Monitoring","q":"A 67-year-old woman receiving intravenous gentamicin for complicated pyelonephritis reports new ringing in both ears (tinnitus) and dizziness, while her urine output drops to 20 mL/hr and serum creatinine rises from 0.9 to 2.1 mg/dL. What is the nurse\u0027s priority action before the next scheduled gentamicin dose?","o":["Administer oral furosemide concurrently to boost gentamicin clearance","Advise the patient that tinnitus always resolves while continuing full doses","Hold the upcoming gentamicin dose, notify the prescriber immediately, monitor trough serum gentamicin levels and renal function, and maintain hydration","Infuse the next dose faster over 5 minutes to maintain peak killing"],"a":2,"e":"Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, amikacin) carry boxed risks of reversible/irreversible nephrotoxicity (proximal tubular necrosis) and irreversible CN VIII ototoxicity (vestibular vertigo and cochlear tinnitus/hearing loss). Rising creatinine, oliguria, and tinnitus warrant holding the next dose immediately, notifying the prescriber to obtain trough drug levels and adjust/switch therapy, and avoiding concurrent loop diuretics (furosemide) which synergistically worsen ototoxicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00965","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Biguanides (Metformin)","q":"A patient with Type 2 diabetes taking metformin 1,000 mg twice daily is scheduled for a contrast-enhanced CT angiogram. Which perioperative medication precaution prevents fatal metformin-associated lactic acidosis (MALA)?","o":["Switch metformin immediately to high-dose aspirin","Instruct the patient to restrict all oral/IV hydration for 24 hours before contrast","Double the metformin dose on the morning of the CT scan","Withhold metformin at the time of or prior to iodinated contrast administration and resume only after 48 hours once normal renal function (creatinine/eGFR) is confirmed"],"a":3,"e":"Metformin is excreted unchanged by the kidneys. If iodinated radiocontrast triggers transient contrast-associated acute kidney injury, metformin accumulates and inhibits hepatic gluconeogenesis from lactate, precipitating life-threatening lactic acidosis. Holding metformin at/before contrast exposure, ensuring adequate hydration, and rechecking renal function 48 hours post-procedure before restarting safeguards the patient.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00966","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology \u0026 Critical Care Nursing","topic":"Norepinephrine Titration Calculation","q":"An ICU patient in distributive shock is prescribed a continuous IV norepinephrine infusion at 8 mcg/min. The pharmacy supplies 4 mg (4,000 mcg) of norepinephrine mixed in 250 mL of 5% Dextrose. At what rate in mL/hour should the nurse set the infusion pump?","o":["30 mL/hr","24 mL/hr","48 mL/hr","15 mL/hr"],"a":0,"e":"Step 1: Determine hourly ordered dose = 8 mcg/min × 60 min/hr = 480 mcg/hr. Step 2: Determine bag concentration = 4,000 mcg ÷ 250 mL = 16 mcg/mL. Step 3: Calculate infusion rate = 480 mcg/hr ÷ 16 mcg/mL = 30 mL/hr. Norepinephrine should ideally infuse via a dedicated central venous line with continuous arterial/NIBP monitoring.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00967","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis","q":"A 4-week-old male infant presents with forceful, non-bilious projectile vomiting immediately after every feed, remains eager to feed afterward, and has a palpable olive-shaped right upper quadrant mass with visible gastric peristaltic waves. Which acid-base disturbance is expected, and what is the nurse\u0027s preoperative priority before pyloromyotomy?","o":["Hyperkalaemic acidosis; give oral sodium bicarbonate","Hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis; correct dehydration and electrolyte imbalances with IV fluids before surgery","Respiratory acidosis; start high-calorie solid cereal feeds","Metabolic acidosis; rush directly to operating theatre within 10 minutes without IV access"],"a":1,"e":"Hypertrophy of the circular pyloric muscle obstructs gastric emptying, causing repeated loss of H+ and Cl- rich gastric juice (non-bilious because obstruction is proximal to the ampulla of Vater). Vomiting triggers hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with volume depletion. Pyloromyotomy (Ramstedt procedure) is semi-elective and MUST await full IV rehydration and correction of alkalosis/electrolytes to prevent postoperative apnoea and arrhythmias.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00968","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Intussusception","q":"A 9-month-old infant is admitted with intermittent episodes of inconsolable screaming while drawing knees up to the abdomen, a sausage-shaped right upper quadrant mass, and dark red mucoid (\u0027currant jelly\u0027) stools. After confirmation by abdominal ultrasound, the stable infant undergoes pneumatic (air) enema reduction. Which observation indicates successful reduction?","o":["Development of fever 39.8°C and tachycardia","Sudden rigid board-like abdomen with absent bowel sounds","Passage of normal brown stool, cessation of colicky pain, and disappearance of the abdominal mass","Persistent bloody mucoid stools and worsening bilious vomiting"],"a":2,"e":"Intussusception occurs when a proximal bowel segment (commonly ileum) telescopes into a distal segment (colon), causing mesenteric venous congestion, mucosal sloughing (\u0027currant jelly\u0027 stool), and intermittent colicky pain. In haemodynamically stable children without peritonitis/perforation, ultrasound-guided pneumatic or hydrostatic enema successfully reduces the telescoping segment—evidenced clinically by comfort, disappearance of the mass, and passage of normal brown stool.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00969","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing II","topic":"Serotonin Syndrome","q":"Twelve hours after a patient on fluoxetine (SSRI) is started on oral linezolid for cellulitis, he develops agitation, fever (39.4°C), profuse diaphoresis, tachycardia, dilated pupils, hyperactive bowel sounds, hyperreflexia, and sustained bilateral ankle clonus. Which drug toxicity is present, and what is the primary management?","o":["Lithium withdrawal; restart high-dose tramadol","Anticholinergic toxicity; give oral benztropine","Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome; administer intravenous haloperidol","Serotonin syndrome due to serotonergic drug interaction; discontinue fluoxetine and linezolid immediately, provide cooling, IV fluids, benzodiazepines, and prepare cyproheptadine"],"a":3,"e":"Linezolid possesses weak reversible MAO-inhibiting activity; combining it with an SSRI (fluoxetine) precipitates acute serotonin excess. Unlike Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (slower onset over days, \u0027lead-pipe\u0027 rigidity, hyporeflexia), serotonin syndrome develops rapidly (\u003c 24 h) with neuromuscular hyperexcitability—clonus (spontaneous/inducible), hyperreflexia, tremor, mydriasis, and hyperactive bowel sounds. Stopping all serotonergic agents, benzodiazepine sedation, cooling, and 5-HT2A antagonism (cyproheptadine) treat the syndrome.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00970","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing I","topic":"Atypical Antipsychotics (Clozapine)","q":"A client with treatment-resistant schizophrenia taking clozapine for 6 weeks reports sore throat, malaise, and a temperature of 38.6°C during an outpatient follow-up. Which laboratory check and immediate precaution are mandatory?","o":["Hold clozapine provisionally and immediately obtain a Complete Blood Count with Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC) to rule out life-threatening agranulocytosis","Double the clozapine dose at bedtime to aid sleep","Reassure the client that sore throat is a harmless extrapyramidal symptom","Check serum lithium level and continue clozapine without interruption"],"a":0,"e":"Clozapine carries a well-known risk of idiosyncratic agranulocytosis (severe neutropenia, typically peaking in the first 3–6 months), presenting initially with flu-like symptoms, fever, sore throat, or oral ulcers. Regular scheduled ANC monitoring is mandatory, and any sign of infection requires immediate CBC/ANC evaluation and holding clozapine until neutropenia/agranulocytosis is ruled out.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00971","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Shoulder Dystocia","q":"During a cephalic vaginal delivery in a mother with gestational diabetes, the foetal head delivers and immediately retracts tightly against the perineum (\u0027turtle sign\u0027), with failure of the anterior shoulder to deliver despite gentle downward traction. While calling for obstetric/neonatal help, what is the immediate first-line external manoeuvre?","o":["Apply vigorous downward fundal pressure while pulling hard on the foetal head","Perform the McRoberts manoeuvre (hyperflexing both maternal thighs tightly onto the abdomen) combined with firm suprapubic pressure","Administer a rapid bolus of IV oxytocin to strengthen pushing","Place the mother\u0027s legs flat and straight off the end of the bed"],"a":1,"e":"Shoulder dystocia (\u0027turtle sign\u0027) is an obstetric emergency where the anterior foetal shoulder impacts behind the maternal symphysis pubis, risking foetal hypoxia and brachial plexus injury. The McRoberts manoeuvre (hyperflexing maternal hips/thighs onto the abdomen) flattens the lumbosacral lordosis and rotates the symphysis pubis cephalad, freeing the impacted shoulder in most cases when paired with directed suprapubic pressure above the pubic bone (Mazzanti/Rubin external pressure).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00972","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Abruptio Placentae","q":"A woman at 35 weeks gestation with chronic hypertension arrives with sudden severe constant abdominal pain, dark red vaginal bleeding, a tense, board-like tender uterus, and foetal bradycardia. Which obstetric emergency is most likely, and which systemic complication must the nurse monitor closely alongside shock?","o":["Placenta praevia; gestational diabetes","Abruptio placentae (premature placental separation); disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) and hypovolaemic shock","Normal latent phase labour; hyperemesis gravidarum","Vasa praevia; physiological anaemia"],"a":1,"e":"Painful third-trimester dark vaginal bleeding with a rigid, hypertonic, tender uterus and foetal distress—especially with hypertension or trauma—strongly indicates abruptio placentae (premature separation of a normally implanted placenta). Concealed retroplacental bleeding can cause shock disproportionate to visible blood loss, and thromboplastin release into maternal circulation precipitates consumptive coagulopathy (DIC) and acute kidney injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00973","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Pneumoconioses","q":"A 46-year-old worker from a sandstone quarry presents to an occupational health clinic with progressive exertional dyspnoea, chronic cough, and a chest radiograph showing nodular upper-zone opacities with \u0027eggshell\u0027 calcification of hilar lymph nodes. Which occupational lung disease is present, and which secondary infection requires heightened screening?","o":["Byssinosis from cotton dust; leptospirosis","Bagassosis from mouldy sugarcane; malaria","Silicosis from crystalline silica inhalation; heightened susceptibility to pulmonary tuberculosis (\u0027silicotuberculosis\u0027)","Asbestosis from shipyard insulation; dengue"],"a":2,"e":"Inhalation of free crystalline silica dust (quartz) in mining, stone-cutting, pottery, and slate-pencil industries causes silicosis, characterized radiographically by upper-lobe fibrotic nodules and classic \u0027eggshell\u0027 peripheral calcification of hilar lymph nodes. Silica impairs alveolar macrophage bactericidal function, making workers highly prone to pulmonary tuberculosis (silicotuberculosis), necessitating regular sputum screening alongside wet-drilling dust suppression and PPE.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00974","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing I","topic":"Levels of Prevention","q":"A community health nurse organizes cervical Pap smear screening camps for women aged 30–65 years and refers those with precancerous CIN lesions for prompt colposcopy and thermal ablation. Which level of prevention does this nursing activity exemplify?","o":["Tertiary prevention (rehabilitation)","Primary prevention","Primordial prevention","Secondary prevention (early diagnosis and prompt treatment)"],"a":3,"e":"Screening asymptomatic individuals (Pap smear, VIA, mammography, hypertension/diabetes screening) to detect pathology at an early subclinical stage and initiate prompt curative/preventive treatment halts progression and belongs to Secondary Prevention. Primordial prevents emergence of risk factors; Primary acts pre-pathogenesis (health promotion, vaccination); Tertiary limits established disability and restores function.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00975","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Management and Leadership","topic":"Transformational Leadership","q":"A nursing superintendent inspires ward nurses with a shared vision for zero pressure injuries, mentors newly graduated staff professionally, encourages creative evidence-based problem solving, and empowers bedside nurses during quality huddles. Which leadership style is demonstrated?","o":["Transformational leadership","Laissez-faire leadership","Autocratic (authoritarian) leadership","Passive management-by-exception"],"a":0,"e":"Transformational leadership motivates followers through idealized influence (role modelling), inspirational motivation (shared vision), intellectual stimulation (innovation/EBP), and individualized consideration (mentorship). Research consistently links transformational nursing leadership with higher nurse job satisfaction, retention, and improved patient safety outcomes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00976","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Gastrointestinal Surgery)","topic":"Colostomy Assessment and Pouch Sizing","q":"At a surgical nursing OSPE station, a student assesses a newly created end sigmoid colostomy 24 hours postoperatively and prepares to apply a new ostomy wafer. Which finding requires urgent surgical notification, and how should the wafer opening be sized?","o":["Mild initial stoma oedema; cut wafer opening smaller so it pinches the mucosa","Pale, dusky, or bluish-black dry stoma (indicating ischaemia); cut a healthy stoma\u0027s wafer opening 1.5–3 mm (1/8 inch) larger than the stoma diameter","Presence of small amount of serosanguinous mucus; apply povidone-iodine ointment inside the wafer","Moist, beefy-red stoma; cut wafer opening 2 cm wider than the stoma"],"a":1,"e":"A viable postoperative stoma should appear moist and pink to beefy-red due to rich mesenteric vascularity (mild early oedema is normal). A pale, mottled, dusky, purple, or black stoma indicates compromised arterial/venous perfusion and impending necrosis requiring immediate surgeon notification. When sizing a pouch skin barrier, cutting the opening 1.5–3 mm (1/8 inch) larger than the stoma prevents mucosal constriction while minimizing peristomal skin exposure to caustic effluent.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00977","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Orthopaedics)","topic":"Acute Compartment Syndrome","q":"Six hours after closed reduction and plaster casting of a tibial shaft fracture, a 28-year-old man complains of intense, worsening lower-leg pain unrelieved by IV morphine and severe pain when the nurse gently extends his toes passively, though his pedal pulse is still palpable. What is the nurse\u0027s priority action?","o":["Reassure him because palpable pedal pulses rule out compartment syndrome","Elevate the casted leg high above heart level on three pillows and apply ice tightly","Suspect acute compartment syndrome, keep the extremity at heart level (not high above the heart), notify the orthopaedic surgeon immediately to split/bivalve the cast, and prepare for fasciotomy","Administer another opioid dose and wait until morning rounds"],"a":2,"e":"Severe escalating pain out of proportion to the injury (unrelieved by opioids) and sharp pain on passive stretch of compartment muscles are the earliest hallmark signs of acute compartment syndrome; pulselessness and pallor are LATE signs indicating irreversible ischaemia. Immediate cast loosening/bivalving and surgical fasciotomy save the limb. Elevating the leg high above heart level decreases arterial inflow pressure gradient and worsens ischaemia—keep the limb at heart level.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00978","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations (Microbiology \u0026 Infection Control)","topic":"Airborne vs Droplet Precautions","q":"An adult with cavitary pulmonary tuberculosis (sputum AFB positive) is admitted to an inpatient ward. Which transmission-based isolation and personal protective equipment (PPE) protocol is required for nursing staff entering the room?","o":["Droplet precautions with a loose cloth mask placed 3 metres from the patient","Standard precautions only with an open shared ward bed","Airborne precautions: place patient in a negative-pressure Airborne Infection Isolation Room (AIIR), keep door closed, and wear a fit-tested N95 particulate respirator","Contact precautions with gown and gloves only; no mask needed"],"a":2,"e":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis, measles, and varicella spread via tiny airborne droplet nuclei (\u003c 5 microns) that remain suspended in air currents over distance. Airborne precautions require a negative-pressure isolation room (\u003e= 12 air changes/hour for new facilities), keeping the door closed, staff wearing a fit-tested N95 (or higher) respirator, and placing a surgical mask on the patient if transport outside the room is unavoidable.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00979","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Alveolar Mechanics and Surfactant","q":"Which alveolar cells secrete pulmonary surfactant, and what critical mechanical function does surfactant perform during expiration?","o":["Ciliated pseudostratified epithelial cells; drives hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","Type I alveolar cells; increases surface tension to collapse smaller alveoli","Alveolar macrophages; produces mucociliary mucus exclusively","Type II pneumocytes; reduces alveolar surface tension to prevent end-expiratory collapse (atelectasis) and decrease work of breathing"],"a":3,"e":"Pulmonary surfactant—a phospholipid-protein complex rich in dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC)—is synthesized and secreted by Type II alveolar epithelial cells (pneumocytes) beginning around 24–28 weeks gestation and reaching functional maturity by ~34–36 weeks. By lowering liquid-air surface tension in proportion to decreasing radius (Laplace\u0027s law), surfactant keeps smaller alveoli patent at end-expiration and increases lung compliance. Deficiency in preterm neonates causes Neonatal Respiratory Distress Syndrome (NRDS).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00980","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Short-Term Blood Pressure Regulation","q":"When a recovering postoperative patient stands up quickly from a supine position, venous pooling transiently lowers arterial stretch in the carotid sinus and aortic arch. Which immediate autonomic reflex prevents orthostatic syncope?","o":["Decreased baroreceptor firing via CN IX and CN X disinhibiting the medullary vasomotor centre, increasing sympathetic vasoconstriction and heart rate","Immediate aldosterone transcription acting within 2 seconds","Suppression of all sympathetic tone to skeletal muscle arterioles","Increased baroreceptor firing stimulating vagal bradycardia and systemic vasodilation"],"a":0,"e":"High-pressure stretch receptors in the carotid sinus (afferent CN IX / glossopharyngeal) and aortic arch (afferent CN X / vagus) normally inhibit the medullary sympathetic vasomotor centre when arterial pressure is adequate. When standing drops arterial pressure, baroreceptor firing decreases, triggering immediate sympathetic activation (arteriolar vasoconstriction, venoconstriction, increased heart rate and contractility) and parasympathetic withdrawal within seconds.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00981","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations \u0026 Medical-Surgical Nursing","topic":"Modified Allen\u0027s Test","q":"At an ICU skills OSCE station, before performing a radial artery puncture for arterial blood gas (ABG) sampling, the nurse compresses both radial and ulnar arteries while the patient clenches and opens the fist until the palm blanches. Which step and result confirm safe collateral perfusion to proceed?","o":["Release radial artery pressure first; palm remains white for 45 seconds","Release ulnar artery pressure while keeping the radial artery compressed; the palm flushes pink within 5–15 seconds","Release neither artery and puncture the brachial vein instead","Perform puncture immediately if the palm stays cyanotic after 30 seconds"],"a":1,"e":"The modified Allen\u0027s test verifies adequate ulnar collateral blood supply to the hand before puncturing or cannulating the radial artery (in case radial spasm or thrombosis occurs). After occluding both arteries and blanching the palm, releasing ONLY the ulnar artery while maintaining radial occlusion should restore palmar blush within 5–15 seconds (positive/normal test). Delayed or absent flushing (\u003e 15 s) contraindicates radial puncture on that wrist.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00982","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Critical Care)","topic":"Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP)","q":"A septic patient on a vasopressor infusion has a blood pressure reading of 92/56 mmHg. Using the standard Mean Arterial Pressure formula [MAP = (SBP + 2 × DBP) ÷ 3], what is the patient\u0027s MAP?","o":["58 mmHg","68 mmHg","64 mmHg","74 mmHg"],"a":1,"e":"Because diastole occupies approximately two-thirds of the cardiac cycle at normal resting heart rates, Mean Arterial Pressure is weighted toward diastolic pressure: MAP = (SBP + 2 × DBP) ÷ 3. Substituting SBP = 92 mmHg and DBP = 56 mmHg: MAP = (92 + 112) ÷ 3 = 204 ÷ 3 = 68 mmHg, which meets the Surviving Sepsis Campaign organ perfusion target of MAP \u003e= 65 mmHg.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00983","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Postural Drainage and Chest Physiotherapy","q":"A patient with bronchiectasis is prescribed nebulized salbutamol, chest percussion, and postural drainage twice daily. Which scheduling sequence is safest for the nurse to follow?","o":["Perform postural drainage immediately after a full lunch to aid digestion","Give oral sedatives before drainage and place head down immediately after drinking milk","Administer prescribed bronchodilator/nebulization first, perform postural drainage 1–2 hours after meals (or before meals), and provide oral hygiene afterward","Perform vigorous percussion directly over bare fractured ribs"],"a":2,"e":"Administering prescribed bronchodilators or mucolytic nebulization 15–30 minutes before chest physiotherapy opens airways and loosens tenacious secretions. Scheduling postural drainage before meals or at least 1–2 hours postprandially prevents nausea, gastroesophageal reflux, and pulmonary aspiration during head-down (Trendelenburg) positioning; post-procedure mouth care removes foul sputum taste.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00984","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Endocrinology)","topic":"Pheochromocytoma","q":"A 38-year-old woman admitted with suspected adrenal pheochromocytoma experiences paroxysmal surges of BP 218/124 mmHg, severe pounding headache, palpitations, and drenching sweats. Which bedside nursing precaution and preoperative drug sequence are mandatory before adrenalectomy?","o":["Administer IV dopamine infusion for headache","Encourage high-caffeine drinks and strenuous Valsalva exercises","Perform deep abdominal palpation every shift to assess tumour size; give beta-blocker monotherapy first","Strictly avoid abdominal palpation (post a bedside notice) to prevent sudden catecholamine surge, and initiate alpha-adrenergic blockade (e.g., phenoxybenzamine) BEFORE adding a beta-blocker"],"a":3,"e":"Pheochromocytoma is a catecholamine-secreting tumour of the adrenal medulla. Mechanical pressure from abdominal palpation can trigger massive release of epinephrine and norepinephrine, precipitating fatal hypertensive crisis, stroke, or arrhythmias. Preoperatively, alpha-blockade (phenoxybenzamine/prazosin) MUST precede beta-blockade (propranolol) because starting a beta-blocker first blocks beta-2-mediated vasodilation while leaving alpha-1 vasoconstriction unopposed, causing paradoxical severe hypertension.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00985","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Endocrinology)","topic":"Addisonian Crisis (Acute Adrenal Insufficiency)","q":"A patient with systemic lupus who abruptly stopped long-term oral prednisolone presents with severe weakness, vomiting, BP 74/44 mmHg, blood glucose 46 mg/dL, serum Na+ 124 mEq/L, and serum K+ 6.0 mEq/L. Which emergency treatment holds highest priority?","o":["Immediate intravenous hydrocortisone (100 mg IV bolus) and rapid 0.9% Normal Saline with Dextrose resuscitation","Subcutaneous regular insulin 20 units","Oral spironolactone and potassium chloride","Fluid restriction to 500 mL/day with IV furosemide"],"a":0,"e":"Abrupt withdrawal of chronic corticosteroids causes secondary adrenal crisis (acute glucocorticoid/mineralocorticoid deficiency), manifesting as refractory hypotension/shock, hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, and hyperkalaemia (in primary Addison\u0027s). Immediate IV hydrocortisone (which provides both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid activity) paired with rapid isotonic saline + dextrose infusion restores vascular tone, volume, and blood glucose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00986","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Gastrointestinal)","topic":"Oesophageal Variceal Tamponade","q":"A patient with massive bleeding oesophageal varices has a Sengstaken-Blakemore balloon tamponade tube inserted in the ICU. Why must the nurse tape a pair of heavy-duty scissors visibly at the head of the bed at all times?","o":["To trim the patient\u0027s fingernails during prolonged ICU stay","To immediately cut across all tube lumens to deflate the balloons and pull out the tube if the gastric balloon migrates upward and causes acute airway obstruction","To cut the traction rope gradually every hour to increase balloon pressure","To open enteral nutrition pouches faster"],"a":1,"e":"If the oesophageal balloon ruptures or traction pulls an inflated gastric balloon upward into the hypopharynx, it can immediately occlude the trachea and cause asphyxiation. Keeping heavy scissors taped at the bedside enables the nurse to transect all balloon ports instantaneously, deflating the tube so it can be rapidly removed to restore airway patency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00987","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Endocrinology)","topic":"Myxoedema Coma","q":"An elderly woman with untreated hypothyroidism develops myxoedema coma following cold exposure, presenting with hypothermia (34.1°C), bradycardia (44 bpm), hypoventilation, and stupor. Which warming and pharmacological nursing care is safest?","o":["Administer high-dose morphine for comfort","Force warm oral liquids immediately despite stupor","Support airway/ventilation, warm gradually and passively with warm blankets, and administer prescribed IV levothyroxine along with IV hydrocortisone","Apply rapid active electric heating blankets at maximum setting to warm within 15 minutes"],"a":2,"e":"Myxoedema coma is life-threatening decompensated hypothyroidism featuring hypothermia, respiratory acidosis from hypoventilation, bradycardia, and hyponatraemia. Rapid external warming with electric heating pads causes abrupt peripheral vasodilation and cardiovascular collapse (\u0027rewarming shock\u0027); passive/gradual warming with blankets is safer. IV levothyroxine restores metabolic drive, paired with empiric IV hydrocortisone to cover potential coexisting adrenal insufficiency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00988","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Antiepileptic Drugs (Phenytoin)","q":"A patient with status epilepticus is prescribed an IV loading dose of phenytoin 1,000 mg. Which intravenous preparation and infusion precaution must the nurse follow?","o":["Mix phenytoin in 5% Dextrose and push rapidly over 2 minutes","Advise the patient to stop brushing teeth permanently if gums swell","Administer phenytoin intramuscularly into the deltoid routinely","Dilute phenytoin ONLY in 0.9% Normal Saline (never Dextrose), infuse slowly (\u003c= 50 mg/min in adults) with an inline filter while monitoring ECG/BP, and flush the line with Normal Saline before and after"],"a":3,"e":"Phenytoin is poorly water-soluble and precipitates immediately into crystals if mixed with acidic Dextrose solutions. It must be diluted strictly in 0.9% Normal Saline, infused through a large vein via an inline 0.22-micron filter at \u003c= 50 mg/min (to prevent propylene-glycol-induced hypotension, bradyarrhythmias, and Purple Glove Syndrome), and flushed with NS before and after. For chronic oral therapy, meticulous oral hygiene/gum massage mitigates gingival hyperplasia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00989","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Loop Diuretics (Furosemide)","q":"A patient with acute kidney injury receives high-dose IV furosemide (120 mg) pushed rapidly over 30 seconds and immediately complains of ringing in the ears and muffled hearing. What adverse effect occurred, and how should future IV doses be administered?","o":["Loop-diuretic-induced ototoxicity (stria vascularis ion-transport disruption); infuse future high doses slowly (e.g., \u003c= 20–40 mg/min) and avoid concurrent aminoglycosides","Harmless transient tinnitus requiring faster bolus pushing","Hypocalcaemic tetany solely","Permanent hyperkalaemia requiring oral potassium supplements"],"a":0,"e":"Rapid IV injection of high-dose loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide)—especially in renal impairment or with concurrent aminoglycosides—can cause transient or permanent sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus by altering endolymph electrolyte composition in the cochlear stria vascularis. Administering IV furosemide slowly (\u003c= 20–40 mg/min or via controlled infusion) minimizes peak inner-ear toxicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00990","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology \u0026 Critical Care Nursing","topic":"Nitroglycerin Infusion Calculation","q":"A patient with unstable angina is prescribed an intravenous nitroglycerin infusion starting at 20 mcg/min. The pharmacy supplies a premixed glass bottle containing 50 mg (50,000 mcg) of nitroglycerin in 250 mL of 5% Dextrose. At what rate in mL/hour should the nurse program the infusion pump?","o":["20 mL/hr","6 mL/hr","12 mL/hr","3 mL/hr"],"a":1,"e":"Step 1: Convert ordered dose to mcg/hour = 20 mcg/min × 60 min/hr = 1,200 mcg/hr. Step 2: Calculate solution concentration = 50,000 mcg ÷ 250 mL = 200 mcg/mL. Step 3: Calculate pump rate = 1,200 mcg/hr ÷ 200 mcg/mL = 6 mL/hr. Non-PVC (polyethylene/glass) tubing is used because standard PVC tubing adsorbs nitroglycerin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00991","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Hirschsprung\u0027s Disease","q":"A full-term newborn fails to pass meconium during the first 48 hours of life and develops progressive abdominal distension and bilious vomiting; rectal examination produces an explosive gush of foul-smelling gas and liquid stool (Hirschsprung\u0027s disease). Three days later, the baby develops high fever, lethargy, and explosive foul watery diarrhoea. Which complication must the nurse recognize immediately?","o":["Simple lactose intolerance managed with oral ORS at home","Normal postoperative recovery","Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis (HAEC), a surgical/medical emergency requiring immediate NPO status, IV fluid resuscitation, rectal decompression, and broad-spectrum IV antibiotics","Benign breastmilk stool transition"],"a":2,"e":"Congenital aganglionic megacolon (Hirschsprung\u0027s disease) lacks myenteric/submucosal ganglion cells in the distal colon, causing functional obstruction and failure to pass meconium within 24–48 hours. Stasis and bacterial overgrowth can trigger Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis (HAEC)—presenting with fever, abdominal distension, lethargy, and explosive foul-smelling diarrhoea—which carries high mortality from sepsis and bowel perforation without urgent IV resuscitation, decompression, and antibiotics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00992","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing II","topic":"Wilms\u0027 Tumour (Nephroblastoma)","q":"A 3-year-old child is admitted with a firm, smooth, painless unilateral flank/abdominal mass that does not cross the midline, suspected to be a Wilms\u0027 tumour (nephroblastoma). Which nursing safety sign and precaution is mandatory prior to nephrectomy?","o":["Place a \u0027Do Not Palpate Abdomen\u0027 sign above the bed and handle the child gently during bathing and turning","Palpate the abdominal mass every 4 hours to track tumour growth","Administer vigorous enemas twice daily before imaging","Encourage rough contact play with other toddlers"],"a":0,"e":"Wilms\u0027 tumour (nephroblastoma) is an encapsulated embryonal renal malignancy. Because the tumour capsule is thin and vascular, repeated or rough abdominal palpation can rupture the capsule, spilling malignant cells into the peritoneal cavity (upstaging the tumour and worsening prognosis) or causing massive haemorrhage. Posting a \u0027No Abdominal Palpation\u0027 sign and careful handling protect the child preoperatively.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00993","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing II","topic":"Anorexia Nervosa and Refeeding Syndrome","q":"A 17-year-old female admitted with severe anorexia nervosa (BMI 13.8 kg/m2) is started on aggressive high-calorie enteral feeding. Within 48 hours, she develops generalized weakness, peripheral oedema, arrhythmias, and serum phosphate drops to 1.2 mg/dL alongside hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia. Which complication occurred, and how is it prevented?","o":["Dumping syndrome; double caloric intake immediately","Serotonin syndrome; withhold all oral hydration","Acute hyperparathyroidism; administer high-dose furosemide","Refeeding syndrome (insulin-driven intracellular shift of phosphate, potassium, and magnesium); prevent via \u0027start low, go slow\u0027 caloric advancement, thiamine supplementation, and daily electrolyte/ECG monitoring"],"a":3,"e":"During starvation, intracellular phosphate, K+, and Mg2+ stores become severely depleted despite normal baseline serum levels. Rapid carbohydrate refeeding spikes insulin secretion, driving glucose, phosphate, potassium, and magnesium abruptly into cells and causing profound hypophosphataemia (ATP depletion -\u003e heart failure, respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis, arrhythmias). Gradual caloric advancement, prophylactic thiamine, and close electrolyte replacement prevent fatal refeeding syndrome.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00994","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing I","topic":"Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs)","q":"A patient with atypical depression prescribed an irreversible Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitor (MAOI, e.g., tranylcypromine/phenelzine) asks which dietary items and over-the-counter drugs must be strictly avoided to prevent a life-threatening hypertensive crisis. Which list should the nurse emphasize?","o":["Aged cheeses, cured/smoked meats, fermented soy products, pickled foods, red wine/tap beer, and sympathomimetic decongestants (e.g., pseudoephedrine)","Fresh steamed rice, apples, and plain milk","Vitamin D tablets and plain water","Boiled potatoes, plain bread, and fresh pears"],"a":0,"e":"MAOIs inhibit intestinal and hepatic monoamine oxidase-A, preventing first-pass breakdown of dietary tyramine. Ingesting tyramine-rich aged, fermented, or pickled foods (or taking indirect sympathomimetics like pseudoephedrine/phenylephrine) triggers massive norepinephrine release from adrenergic terminals, causing acute hypertensive crisis (occipital headache, stiff neck, diaphoresis, stroke risk, treated with IV phentolamine).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00995","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Cardiotocography (CTG) Interpretation","q":"During active labour augmented with IV oxytocin, the cardiotocograph (CTG) shows repetitive smooth, gradual drops in foetal heart rate that begin AFTER the peak of each uterine contraction and recover only AFTER the contraction finishes (late decelerations). What is the underlying cause and immediate nursing sequence?","o":["Maternal fever solely; place mother supine flat","Uteroplacental insufficiency (foetal hypoxaemia); immediately stop oxytocin infusion, turn mother to left lateral position, administer IV crystalloid bolus, apply oxygen per protocol, and notify the obstetrician","Benign foetal head compression; increase oxytocin infusion rate","Normal acceleration pattern; encourage vigorous Valsalva pushing"],"a":1,"e":"Late decelerations mirror contraction timing with a delayed onset, nadir after the contraction peak, and delayed return to baseline—indicating uteroplacental insufficiency and foetal hypoxia during uterine contractions. Immediate intrauterine resuscitation stops uterine hyperstimulation (turn off oxytocin), maximizes maternal venous return and placental blood flow (left lateral position + IV fluid bolus), optimizes maternal oxygenation, and alerts the obstetrician for rapid evaluation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00996","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"HELLP Syndrome","q":"A pregnant woman at 34 weeks gestation with preeclampsia develops right upper quadrant/epigastric pain, nausea, and dark urine. Laboratory tests show haemoglobin 8.1 g/dL with schistocytes, elevated LDH (920 U/L), AST 240 U/L, ALT 210 U/L, and platelets 62,000/mm3. Which syndrome is diagnosed, and what is the definitive treatment?","o":["Hyperemesis gravidarum; delay delivery until 42 weeks","Acute fatty liver of normal term; discharge for outpatient iron","HELLP syndrome (Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets); stabilize BP and administer MgSO4 seizure prophylaxis followed by expedited delivery","Physiological anaemia of pregnancy; oral folic acid alone"],"a":2,"e":"HELLP syndrome is a severe, life-threatening variant of preeclampsia characterized by microangiopathic Haemolysis (schistocytes, high LDH/indirect bilirubin), Elevated Liver enzymes (AST/ALT elevation from periportal necrosis/subcapsular haematoma causing RUQ pain), and Low Platelets (\u003c 1,00,000/mm3). Because maternal morbidity (hepatic rupture, DIC, abruption, eclampsia) escalates rapidly, definitive management is maternal stabilization (antihypertensives, MgSO4, platelet support if needed) and prompt delivery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00997","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Vaccination During Pregnancy","q":"A 24-year-old woman in her second trimester attends an antenatal clinic for immunization screening. Which vaccine is safe and recommended during pregnancy, versus which category of vaccines is strictly contraindicated?","o":["MMR (Measles-Mumps-Rubella) is recommended; Td (Tetanus-diphtheria) is contraindicated","BCG and live Rubella must be given routinely at 28 weeks gestation","Varicella live vaccine is safe in first trimester; inactivated influenza is contraindicated","Td/Tdap (inactivated toxoid) and inactivated seasonal influenza are safe/recommended during pregnancy; live attenuated vaccines (e.g., MMR, Varicella, BCG) are contraindicated"],"a":3,"e":"Inactivated vaccines and toxoids—such as Td (under India\u0027s UIP antenatal schedule), Tdap, and inactivated influenza—are safe in pregnancy and confer vital transplacental passive immunity to the newborn against neonatal tetanus and pertussis. Conversely, live attenuated replicating vaccines (MMR/Rubella, Varicella, BCG, Yellow Fever) carry theoretical risk of foetal viraemia/congenital infection and are contraindicated during pregnancy (advise avoiding conception for 1 month after MMR).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00998","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing I","topic":"Epidemic Curves","q":"When plotting an epidemic curve (cases by date/hour of onset) during a community outbreak, all 62 cases cluster rapidly into a single sharp peak within one incubation period with no secondary waves, tracing back to contaminated paneer served at a single wedding lunch. Which type of epidemic curve does this depict?","o":["Point-source (common-source, single-exposure) epidemic","Slow-moving secular trend","Propagated (person-to-person) epidemic","Endemic seasonal baseline"],"a":0,"e":"A point-source (common-source, single-exposure) epidemic curve exhibits a steep upslope and fairly rapid downslope forming a single unimodal peak compressed within one minimum-to-maximum incubation period of the causative agent, because all susceptible individuals were exposed simultaneously to the same contaminated source (e.g., food poisoning). By contrast, a propagated epidemic shows multiple progressively wider/taller peaks separated by incubation intervals due to person-to-person spread.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q00999","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Threats to Internal Validity","q":"During a 2-week hospital study testing whether coloured wall posters increase hand-hygiene compliance, a researcher stands visibly at the ICU foot-end holding a clipboard. Hand-hygiene compliance jumps from 45% to 88% in both intervention and control ICUs immediately, then drops back to 48% once the observer leaves. Which research bias explains this temporary surge?","o":["Recall bias","Hawthorne effect (subject reactivity from awareness of being observed)","Maturation bias","Instrumentation decay"],"a":1,"e":"The Hawthorne effect occurs when study participants alter their behaviour simply because they know they are being observed or enrolled in a study, independent of the true efficacy of the experimental intervention. Unobtrusive observation, automated dispenser counters, or longer habituation periods help mitigate reactivity bias in nursing quality studies.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01000","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Informatics and Technology","topic":"Telenursing \u0026 eSanjeevani Guidelines","q":"A Community Health Officer / nurse at an Ayushman Arogya Mandir facilitates an eSanjeevani teleconsultation between a rural patient and a specialist physician. Which practice adheres to India\u0027s Telemedicine Practice Guidelines (2020) and nursing informatics standards?","o":["Delete all teleconsultation clinical notes immediately after disconnecting","Skip patient identification and allow bystanders to record the video consultation on personal phones","Verify patient identity, obtain explicit/implied informed consent for teleconsultation, maintain clinical confidentiality, and recognize that Schedule X narcotics/habit-forming drugs cannot be prescribed via routine teleconsultation","Allow an unregistered clerk to prescribe antibiotics independently"],"a":2,"e":"Under India\u0027s Telemedicine Practice Guidelines (MoHFW / Board of Governors) and eSanjeevani workflows, clinicians must verify two patient identifiers, document consent, safeguard health data privacy, and follow drug-list restrictions (Prohibited list drugs such as Schedule X narcotics and anticancer agents cannot be prescribed via telemedicine). Complete digital documentation in the EHR is mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01001","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Critical Care)","topic":"Central Venous Catheter (CVC) Removal","q":"At an ICU OSPE station, the nurse prepares to remove a non-tunnelled internal jugular central venous catheter (CVC). Which patient position and breathing instruction during catheter withdrawal prevent venous air embolism?","o":["Position left-side up and ask the patient to pant rapidly","Leave the insertion site uncovered to air-dry for 24 hours","Sit upright at 90 degrees and ask the patient to inhale deeply rapidly during pull","Place supine/slight Trendelenburg, instruct the patient to perform a Valsalva manoeuvre (exhale gently against closed glottis/hold breath) during withdrawal, and immediately seal the site with a sterile airtight occlusive dressing"],"a":3,"e":"During deep inspiration or upright posture, central venous pressure becomes negative relative to atmospheric air, risking fatal air entrainment along the subcutaneous catheter tract. Placing the patient supine or slight Trendelenburg and having them perform a Valsalva manoeuvre elevates intrathoracic/central venous pressure above atmospheric pressure while the CVC is smoothly withdrawn, followed immediately by firm pressure and an airtight petroleum/occlusive dressing for 24–72 hours. (If air embolism is suspected, place in left lateral Trendelenburg—Durant\u0027s manoeuvre).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01002","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Neurology)","topic":"Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS)","q":"A 26-year-old man admitted 10 days after acute Campylobacter diarrhoea develops symmetric ascending leg weakness, absent deep tendon reflexes, and new difficulty swallowing saliva (bulbar weakness), though his pulse oximetry still reads 97% on room air. Which bedside nursing assessment is the most sensitive indicator of impending respiratory failure requiring elective intubation?","o":["Serial measurement of Forced Vital Capacity (FVC), Negative Inspiratory Force (NIF), cough strength, and bulbar swallowing/gag function","Checking lower-limb Babinski sign once daily","Waiting until SpO2 drops below 80% before calling the physician","Measuring abdominal girth every 12 hours"],"a":0,"e":"In Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS), progressive demyelination ascends to involve intercostal muscles, diaphragm, and bulbar cranial nerves. Pulse oximetry remains deceptively normal until late alveolar hypoventilation and hypercapnic arrest occur. Serial bedside pulmonary mechanics—declining Forced Vital Capacity (\u003c 15–20 mL/kg), weak Negative Inspiratory Force (worse than -20 to -30 cmH2O), weak cough, and pooling oral secretions—alert the team early for safe elective intubation before emergency arrest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01003","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Nutrition \u0026 Critical Care)","topic":"Total Parenteral Nutrition (TPN)","q":"A patient receiving continuous central-line Total Parenteral Nutrition (TPN) containing 25% Dextrose runs out of the current bag while the replacement TPN bag is delayed in pharmacy for 2 hours. What temporary IV fluid must the nurse hang at the same infusion rate?","o":["No fluid; clamp the central line completely for 2 hours","10% Dextrose in water (D10W) temporarily until the new TPN bag arrives","Lactated Ringer\u0027s solution","0.9% Normal Saline"],"a":1,"e":"Continuous high-concentration dextrose TPN stimulates high endogenous pancreatic insulin output. Abruptly stopping TPN or switching to dextrose-free crystalloid (0.9% NS or RL) leaves high circulating insulin unopposed, causing sudden severe rebound hypoglycaemia. Hanging 10% Dextrose (D10W) temporarily at the same hourly rate maintains blood glucose safely while awaiting the next TPN bag, accompanied by frequent capillary blood glucose monitoring.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01004","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Countercurrent Multiplier Mechanism","q":"Which nephron segment actively reabsorbs Na+, K+, and Cl- via the NKCC2 cotransporter while remaining impermeable to water, thereby diluting tubular fluid and establishing the medullary osmotic gradient?","o":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Descending thin limb of the loop of Henle","Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle","Inner medullary collecting duct under ADH"],"a":2,"e":"The thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle actively pumps Na+, K+, and 2Cl- out of the lumen via the luminal NKCC2 cotransporter while remaining completely impermeable to water. This \u0027diluting segment\u0027 drives the countercurrent multiplier system that generates hyperosmotic medullary interstitium for ADH-mediated urine concentration—and is the exact site inhibited by loop diuretics such as furosemide.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01005","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Cerebellar Function","q":"During a neurological exam, a patient exhibits a broad-based staggering gait, overshoots the examiner\u0027s finger on the finger-to-nose test (dysmetria), shows an intention tremor that worsens near the target, and cannot perform rapid alternating pronation-supination movements. Which brain structure is impaired?","o":["Primary visual cortex (occipital lobe)","Substantia nigra of the basal ganglia","Medullary respiratory centre","Cerebellum"],"a":3,"e":"The cerebellum coordinates posture, balance, muscle tone, and smooth precision of voluntary movement. Cerebellar lesions produce classic ipsilateral coordination deficits: truncal/gait ataxia, dysmetria (past-pointing), dysdiadochokinesia (impaired rapid alternating movements), intention tremor (accentuated as the limb approaches a target), and nystagmus. By contrast, basal ganglia/substantia nigra disease causes resting \u0027pill-rolling\u0027 tremor, bradykinesia, and cogwheel rigidity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01006","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Biochemistry \u0026 Nutrition","topic":"Vitamin B12 Absorption","q":"Following total gastrectomy or surgical resection of the terminal ileum, a patient develops megaloblastic anaemia and dorsal column peripheral neuropathy due to Vitamin B12 deficiency. Why does total gastrectomy impair Vitamin B12 absorption?","o":["Gastric parietal cells secrete Intrinsic Factor, which is required to bind Vitamin B12 for receptor-mediated uptake in the terminal ileum","The stomach normally stores 50 years of Vitamin B12 inside chief cells","Pancreatic lipase destroys Vitamin B12 without gastric pepsin","Vitamin B12 can only be absorbed across the buccal mucosa"],"a":0,"e":"Dietary Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) binds Intrinsic Factor (IF)—a glycoprotein secreted by gastric parietal cells—forming an IF-B12 complex absorbed specifically in the terminal ileum. Total gastrectomy (eliminating parietal cells), autoimmune destruction of parietal cells (pernicious anaemia), or terminal ileal resection prevents oral B12 uptake, requiring lifelong parenteral (IM cyanocobalamin/hydroxocobalamin) or very high-dose replacement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01007","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Surgical Wound Dressing","q":"At a surgical nursing OSCE station, a student performs sterile saline cleansing of a clean, closed abdominal laparotomy incision. Which swabbing direction maintains surgical asepsis?","o":["Wipe from the surrounding outer skin inward toward the incision using a single gauze swab repeatedly","Clean from the least contaminated area (directly along the incision line top-to-bottom in one stroke per sterile swab) outward toward the periphery, discarding each swab after one pass","Blow gently across the wound to dry saline faster","Scrub back and forth across the drain site and the clean incision with the same swab"],"a":1,"e":"Sterile wound care always moves from the cleanest area to the least clean (more contaminated) area to avoid dragging skin flora or drain exudate into the healing incision. Using one sterile saline-moistened swab per downward single stroke along the incision, then parallel outward strokes on each side—and cleaning any drain site separately afterward—prevents surgical site infection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01008","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations \u0026 Adult Health Nursing","topic":"Hourly Urine Output Adequacy","q":"A 60 kg postoperative adult has a total urine output of 96 mL recorded over a 4-hour period via Foley catheter. What is the patient\u0027s hourly urine output in mL/kg/hr, and how should the nurse interpret it?","o":["0.8 mL/kg/hr (adequate renal perfusion)","1.6 mL/kg/hr (polyuria)","0.4 mL/kg/hr (oliguria, below the minimum adult threshold of 0.5 mL/kg/hr)","0.2 mL/kg/hr (severe anuria)"],"a":2,"e":"Step 1: Calculate average hourly urine output = 96 mL ÷ 4 hours = 24 mL/hr. Step 2: Divide by patient body weight = 24 mL/hr ÷ 60 kg = 0.4 mL/kg/hr. Because minimum adequate adult urine output is \u003e= 0.5 mL/kg/hr (30 mL/hr in a 60 kg adult), 0.4 mL/kg/hr indicates oliguria—prompting immediate catheter patency check, volume status assessment, and physician notification to prevent prerenal acute kidney injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01009","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Cleansing Enema Administration","q":"While administering a warm tap-water/normal-saline cleansing enema to a patient positioned in left lateral Sims\u0027 position, the patient complains of sudden abdominal cramping. What should the nurse do immediately?","o":["Raise the enema bag 120 cm higher to finish faster","Instruct the patient to bear down forcefully while solution is infusing","Push the rectal tube 20 cm deeper immediately","Temporarily lower the enema container or clamp the tubing for 30–60 seconds while encouraging slow deep breathing, then resume slowly at a lower height"],"a":3,"e":"Left lateral Sims\u0027 position aligns fluid flow naturally with the descending and sigmoid colon. Rapid distension or overly high bag elevation (\u003e 45 cm above the anus) triggers painful bowel spasms and premature expulsion. Clamping the tubing or lowering the container briefly allows peristaltic spasm to relax while deep breathing eases sphincter discomfort before resuming gently.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01010","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Oncology)","topic":"Tumour Lysis Syndrome (TLS)","q":"Twenty-four hours after initiating induction chemotherapy for high-grade Burkitt lymphoma, a patient develops oliguria and muscle cramps. Labs show uric acid 13.8 mg/dL, potassium 6.3 mEq/L, phosphate 7.4 mg/dL, and calcium 6.9 mg/dL. Which oncological emergency has occurred, and which nursing order must be implemented immediately?","o":["Tumour Lysis Syndrome (TLS); initiate aggressive potassium-free IV hydration, administer prescribed allopurinol or rasburicase, institute continuous ECG monitoring, and manage hyperkalaemia","Disseminated candidiasis; infuse potassium chloride 40 mEq/hr","SIADH; restrict all IV fluids to 400 mL/day","Hypercalcaemic crisis; administer IV calcium chloride rapidly to normal asymptomatic calcium"],"a":0,"e":"Rapid chemotherapy-induced lysis of high-turnover malignant cells releases massive intracellular potassium, phosphate, and nucleic acids (metabolized to uric acid), while excess phosphate precipitates serum calcium (causing secondary hypocalcaemia)—producing the TLS tetrad: hyperuricaemia, hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia, and hypocalcaemia, which threatens fatal arrhythmias and uric-acid/calcium-phosphate acute kidney injury. Aggressive K+-free IV hydration, urate-lowering therapy (allopurinol/rasburicase), and cardiac monitoring are vital.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01011","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Critical Care)","topic":"Neurogenic Shock","q":"Two hours after sustaining a high thoracic (T3) spinal cord injury in a road crash, a patient has BP 76/44 mmHg, heart rate 44 beats/min, warm dry skin below the clavicles, and no significant blood loss on trauma ultrasound. Which type of shock explains this haemodynamic profile?","o":["Anaphylactic shock from food allergy","Neurogenic (distributive) shock caused by loss of sympathetic vasomotor tone leaving unopposed parasympathetic vagal activity","Cardiogenic shock from acute mitral stenosis","Hypovolaemic haemorrhagic shock"],"a":1,"e":"Spinal cord injuries at or above T6 disrupt descending sympathetic pathways from the brainstem to sympathetic ganglia, causing peripheral arteriolar/venous vasodilation (warm, dry skin instead of cold clammy vasoconstricted skin) and loss of sympathetic cardioaccelerator input, leaving unopposed vagal tone that produces bradycardia alongside hypotension. Careful fluid resuscitation, vasopressors (norepinephrine), and atropine for symptomatic bradycardia restore spinal perfusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01012","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Orthopaedics)","topic":"Skeletal Traction Care","q":"A patient with a comminuted femoral shaft fracture is placed in balanced skeletal traction via a Steinmann pin. Which nursing principle is essential during every shift?","o":["Tighten bedsheets over the traction ropes to stabilize them","Rest the traction weights gently on the floor at night so the patient sleeps comfortably","Ensure weights hang freely off the bed without touching the floor or bedframe, keep ropes aligned in pulley grooves, monitor neurovascular status, and perform aseptic pin-site care","Remove the skeletal weights during bed-making every morning"],"a":2,"e":"Continuous uninterrupted line-of-pull is mandatory for effective bone alignment and muscle-spasm reduction in skeletal traction. If weights rest on the floor, touch the footboard, or are removed without an orthopaedic order, traction counter-force vanishes, causing painful muscle spasm and fracture malalignment. Routine neurovascular checks (5 Ps) and aseptic pin-site care prevent osteomyelitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01013","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Nephrology)","topic":"Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome (DDS)","q":"During the final hour of a patient\u0027s very first haemodialysis session for severe chronic uraemia (pre-dialysis BUN 148 mg/dL), the patient suddenly develops severe headache, nausea, restlessness, blurred vision, and generalized twitching. What complication is occurring, and what is the immediate nursing action?","o":["Hypoglycaemia solely; discharge home right away","Normal completion fatigue; increase dialyzer blood flow rate to maximum","Air embolism from high potassium; infuse 1,000 mL distilled water IV","Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome (cerebral oedema from rapid blood-urea clearance exceeding CSF clearance); stop or slow dialysis immediately, notify the nephrologist, protect airway/seizure precautions, and prepare prescribed IV mannitol"],"a":3,"e":"Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome (DDS) occurs most often during a patient\u0027s initial haemodialysis sessions when blood urea nitrogen (BUN) drops much faster than urea can diffuse out of brain cells across the blood-brain barrier. The resulting osmotic gradient draws free water into brain tissue, causing acute cerebral oedema, confusion, nausea, and seizures. Slowing/stopping dialysis and administering hypertonic mannitol/saline prevent herniation; prevention uses shorter, gentler initial sessions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01014","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Ophthalmology)","topic":"Retinal Detachment","q":"A patient with high myopia reports sudden flashing lights (photopsia), a shower of floaters, and a painless dark \u0027curtain\u0027 moving across his right visual field, undergoing emergency vitrectomy with intraocular gas tamponade. Which postoperative instruction is critical while the intraocular gas bubble remains in the eye?","o":["Maintain the prescribed head-down/prone positioning so the gas bubble floats upward to press against the retinal tear, and strictly avoid air travel or high altitudes until the surgeon confirms gas resorption","Lift 20 kg weights immediately to resume fitness","Sleep flat on the back and fly home by commercial aircraft the next morning","Rub the operated eye vigorously to relieve itching"],"a":0,"e":"Sudden painless photopsia, floaters, and a visual-field curtain signify rhegmatogenous retinal detachment. After pars plana vitrectomy with expansile gas tamponade (e.g., SF6 or C3F8), maintaining prescribed face-down/positioned posture allows the buoyant gas bubble to tamponade the posterior retinal break. Crucially, decreased cabin atmospheric pressure during air travel causes the trapped intraocular gas bubble to expand rapidly, spiking intraocular pressure to blinding levels—flying (and nitrous oxide anaesthesia) is strictly contraindicated until full absorption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01015","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Naloxone Rescue Monitoring","q":"A postoperative patient receiving IV morphine develops respiratory rate 6 breaths/min, pinpoint pupils, and unresponsiveness. The nurse administers prescribed IV naloxone, and within 2 minutes the patient awakens breathing at 16/min. Why must the nurse continue close respiratory monitoring for several hours afterward?","o":["Morphine is immediately converted to paracetamol by naloxone","Naloxone has a shorter elimination half-life (30–60 minutes) than morphine, so fatal recurrent respiratory depression (\u0027renarcotization\u0027) can return once naloxone wears off","Naloxone permanently destroys all opioid receptors for 7 days","Naloxone causes delayed hyperglycaemic coma 4 hours later"],"a":1,"e":"Naloxone is a competitive mu-opioid receptor antagonist with rapid onset (1–2 min IV) but a relatively brief duration of action (30–60 min). Because morphine and sustained-release/long-acting opioids persist in plasma much longer than naloxone, respiratory depression frequently recurs (\u0027renarcotization\u0027) as naloxone clears, requiring continuous pulse oximetry/RR monitoring and repeat naloxone boluses or infusion as prescribed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01016","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"ACE Inhibitors (Enalapril / Ramipril)","q":"A hypertensive patient started on oral enalapril 3 weeks ago develops a persistent hacking dry cough, and today arrives with acute swelling of the lips and tongue. Which mediator accumulation explains both reactions, and what is the safer antihypertensive class alternative?","o":["Histamine deficiency; increase the enalapril dose","Dopamine accumulation; switch immediately to lisinopril (another ACE inhibitor)","Bradykinin accumulation due to ACE (kininase II) inhibition; assess airway urgently for angioedema, discontinue enalapril permanently, and transition to an Angiotensin II Receptor Blocker (ARB, e.g., losartan) once stabilized","Aldosterone excess; add spironolactone while continuing enalapril"],"a":2,"e":"Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (identical to kininase II) normally degrades vasodilatory bradykinin. ACE inhibitors (enalapril, ramipril, lisinopril) elevate respiratory mucosal bradykinin and substance P, causing a characteristic non-productive dry cough (~10–20% of patients) and, rarely, life-threatening non-allergic angioedema of the lips, tongue, and larynx. Angioedema requires immediate airway protection and permanent ACEi avoidance; ARBs (losartan, telmisartan) block AT1 receptors without inhibiting bradykinin breakdown.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01017","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology \u0026 Critical Care Nursing","topic":"Continuous IV Insulin Infusion","q":"A 70 kg adult in diabetic ketoacidosis (serum K+ 4.4 mEq/L) is prescribed a continuous IV regular insulin infusion at 0.1 units/kg/hour. The ICU pharmacy prepares 100 units of regular insulin in 100 mL of 0.9% Normal Saline. At what rate in mL/hour should the nurse set the infusion pump?","o":["3.5 mL/hr","14 mL/hr","70 mL/hr","7 mL/hr"],"a":3,"e":"Step 1: Calculate ordered hourly insulin dose = 70 kg × 0.1 units/kg/hr = 7 units/hr. Step 2: Calculate bag concentration = 100 units ÷ 100 mL = 1 unit/mL. Step 3: Calculate infusion pump rate = 7 units/hr ÷ 1 unit/mL = 7 mL/hr. Prime the IV tubing with ~20 mL of the insulin solution to saturate plastic tubing binding sites before connecting to the patient.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01018","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing II","topic":"Kawasaki Disease","q":"A 3-year-old boy has had persistent fever (39.5°C) for 6 days, bilateral non-purulent conjunctivitis, cracked erythematous lips with a \u0027strawberry\u0027 tongue, cervical lymphadenopathy, and swollen hands/feet (Kawasaki disease). Which therapy initiated within 10 days of illness onset prevents the most serious cardiac complication, and what immunization adjustment is required afterward?","o":["High-dose Intravenous Immunoglobulin (IVIG) plus aspirin to prevent coronary artery aneurysms; defer live attenuated vaccines (MMR and Varicella) for 11 months after high-dose IVIG","Oral penicillin alone; give live MMR vaccine tomorrow","Oral corticosteroids as sole therapy; give BCG booster immediately","Diuretics only; no vaccine adjustment needed"],"a":0,"e":"Kawasaki disease is an acute medium-vessel systemic vasculitis in young children whose primary morbidity/mortality stems from coronary artery aneurysms and myocardial infarction. Administering high-dose IVIG (2 g/kg) plus anti-inflammatory/antiplatelet aspirin within the first 10 days reduces aneurysm risk from ~25% to \u003c 5%. Because transferred IVIG antibodies neutralize live measles/varicella vaccine viruses, live MMR and Varicella vaccines must be deferred for 11 months following high-dose IVIG.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01019","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Acute Viral Bronchiolitis (RSV)","q":"A 4-month-old infant is admitted with RSV bronchiolitis, exhibiting copious nasal secretions, wheezing, subcostal retractions, respiratory rate 62 breaths/min, and SpO2 89% on room air. Which nursing care bundle represents evidence-based first-line management?","o":["Continuous high-dose oral sedatives to slow respiratory rate","Gentle superficial nasal saline suctioning prior to feeds, humidified supplemental oxygen to maintain SpO2 \u003e= 90%–92%, hydration support (IV/NG if RR \u003e 60–70/min), and Contact/Droplet precautions","Routine systemic corticosteroids, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and aggressive chest percussion","Force large 200 mL bottle feeds while tachypnoeic at 62/min"],"a":1,"e":"Infants under 6 months are obligate nose-breathers; clearing thick RSV nasal secretions gently with saline drops and bulb/catheter suctioning immediately eases airway resistance before feeding or sleep. Because bronchiolitis is viral and self-limiting, supportive oxygenation (target SpO2 \u003e= 90%–92%), careful hydration (avoiding oral feeding when RR \u003e 60–70/min due to aspiration risk), and Contact/Droplet isolation are standard; routine antibiotics, steroids, and chest physiotherapy show no benefit.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01020","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Weight-Based Antipyretic Suspension Calculation","q":"An 18 kg child with high fever and joint pain is prescribed oral ibuprofen suspension at 10 mg/kg/dose. The available oral suspension is labelled 100 mg/5 mL. How many millilitres (mL) should the nurse measure for a single dose?","o":["12.0 mL","6.0 mL","9.0 mL","4.5 mL"],"a":2,"e":"Step 1: Calculate ordered dose = 18 kg × 10 mg/kg = 180 mg. Step 2: Calculate suspension volume from concentration 100 mg/5 mL (20 mg/mL): (180 mg ÷ 100 mg) × 5 mL = 1.8 × 5 = 9.0 mL. Always shake liquid suspensions thoroughly before measuring with a calibrated oral syringe.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01021","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing II","topic":"Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD)","q":"A patient hospitalized with Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD) wakes from a nightmare trembling, sweating, staring past the nurse, and shouting as if reliving a severe vehicle crash (acute dissociative flashback). Which trauma-informed nursing approach is safest?","o":["Apply four-point leather restraints right away without speaking","Shout loudly to stop dreaming immediately","Approach silently from behind and grab the patient\u0027s shoulders firmly to wake him","Remain calm within clear frontal view at a respectful distance, speak in a gentle soothing voice, avoid sudden unexpected touch, and use present-moment grounding cues (\u0027You are safe in the hospital; feel your feet on the floor and look at the blue wall\u0027)"],"a":3,"e":"During a PTSD flashback, the patient temporarily loses orientation to the present and experiences the traumatic threat as happening now. Sudden physical contact or approaching from behind can trigger defensive fight-or-flight reactions. Trauma-informed nursing care maintains non-threatening frontal visibility, soft calm reassurance, and sensory grounding (focusing on breathing, feet on the floor, room objects) to restore present reality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01022","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing I","topic":"Korsakoff Syndrome","q":"A hospitalized client with alcohol-related Korsakoff psychosis cannot recall what he had for breakfast, yet cheerfully tells the nurse an elaborate, completely false story about having just returned from a meeting with the city mayor. How should the nurse interpret and respond to this symptom?","o":["Deliberate manipulative lying; scold the client firmly for dishonesty","Confabulation (unconscious filling of severe memory gaps with fabricated accounts without intent to deceive); neither confront harshly nor argue, gently provide brief factual orientation and maintain a structured routine","Acute visual hallucination; give haloperidol 10 mg IV immediately","Normal creative storytelling requiring no thiamine or supervision"],"a":1,"e":"Korsakoff syndrome (chronic sequela of untreated thiamine deficiency / Wernicke encephalopathy) causes severe anterograde and retrograde amnesia with intact immediate alertness. Confabulation is an unconscious defense mechanism to mask embarrassing memory loss—not intentional lying. Arguing or shaming increases frustration and agitation; calm redirection and consistent environmental cues/calendars support safety.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01023","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy","q":"A 26-year-old woman with 7 weeks of amenorrhoea and a positive urine pregnancy test arrives pale and dizzy with sudden sharp right lower-quadrant abdominal pain, referred right shoulder-tip pain, BP 82/50 mmHg, and pulse 128 bpm. Which life-threatening condition explains the shoulder pain, and what is the nurse\u0027s priority?","o":["Ruptured tubal ectopic pregnancy causing haemoperitoneum (blood irritating the diaphragm/phrenic nerve -\u003e shoulder-tip pain); immediately establish two large-bore IV lines with crystalloids, cross-match blood, keep NPO, and prepare for emergency laparotomy/laparoscopy","Normal round ligament stretch; discharge with oral antacids","Hyperemesis gravidarum; encourage large oral meals","Uncomplicated urinary tract infection; prescribe oral nitrofurantoin"],"a":0,"e":"Classic triad of ectopic pregnancy (amenorrhoea, lower abdominal pain, vaginal spotting) progressing to haemorrhagic shock and referred shoulder-tip pain (Kehr\u0027s sign: free intraperitoneal blood pooling beneath the diaphragm irritating C3–C5 phrenic nerve fibres) signals a ruptured ectopic pregnancy. Immediate large-bore IV volume resuscitation, blood typing/cross-matching, NPO status, and rapid surgical haemostasis save the woman\u0027s life.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01024","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Bishop Scoring System","q":"Prior to scheduling induction of labour at 41 weeks gestation, the obstetric team calculates a Bishop score of 3 (firm, posterior, 0 cm dilated, 20% effaced, -3 station). Which clinical interpretation and pre-induction step does a low Bishop score (\u003c= 5) indicate?","o":["Immediate forceps delivery is indicated at 0 cm dilatation","Cervix is unfavourable/unripe; pre-induction cervical ripening (e.g., prostaglandin E2/E1 or mechanical Foley catheter balloon) is indicated before oxytocin induction to reduce failed induction/Caesarean risk","Bishop score assesses newborn Apgar status after birth","Cervix is highly favourable; start high-dose oxytocin immediately without cervical ripening"],"a":1,"e":"The modified Bishop score evaluates five pelvic parameters—cervical dilatation, effacement, consistency, position, and foetal head station (maximum score 13). A score \u003e= 8 predicts high vaginal delivery success with oxytocin induction alone, whereas a score \u003c= 5 indicates an unfavourable cervix requiring pharmacological (dinoprostone gel/insert or low-dose misoprostol) or mechanical (transcervical Foley catheter) cervical ripening first.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01025","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Acute Uterine Inversion","q":"Following premature cord traction on a fundally implanted placenta, the uterine fundus turns inside-out and protrudes into the vagina with the placenta still attached, accompanied by sudden maternal neurogenic and haemorrhagic shock (BP 70/40 mmHg). Which emergency sequence is correct?","o":["Wait 2 hours to see if the uterus reverts spontaneously","Administer IV ergometrine immediately to clamp the cervix tightly around the inverted uterus","Leave the attached placenta undisturbed initially, call emergency obstetric/anaesthesia help, start rapid IV resuscitation, discontinue any oxytocin, and assist immediate manual replacement of the uterus (Johnson manoeuvre) BEFORE giving uterotonics","Pull harder on the placenta to tear it off inside the vagina"],"a":2,"e":"In acute puerperal uterine inversion, removing an attached placenta before replacement triggers torrential haemorrhage, while giving uterotonics (oxytocin/ergometrine) before replacement contracts the cervical ring, trapping the inverted fundus outside. Immediate manual replacement upward along the pelvic axis (Johnson manoeuvre, under uterine relaxation if needed) MUST precede placental separation and subsequent uterotonic infusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01026","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Herd Immunity and Basic Reproduction Number (R0)","q":"Why does achieving community-wide interruption of measles transmission (herd immunity) require approximately 95% immunization coverage, significantly higher than diseases with a lower basic reproduction number (R0)?","o":["Measles vaccine provides immunity for only 2 weeks","Only adults over 60 years transmit measles","Measles spreads exclusively through contaminated drinking water","Measles has a very high R0 (approx. 12–18 secondary cases per infectious person in a fully susceptible population), so a very high proportion of immune individuals is needed to reduce the effective reproduction number below 1"],"a":3,"e":"The theoretical herd immunity threshold is given by HIT = 1 - (1 / R0). Because airborne measles is extraordinarily contagious (R0 ≈ 12–18), 1 - (1/15) ≈ 93%–95% of the population must possess immunity to break chains of transmission and indirectly protect unimmunized infants or immunocompromised individuals.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01027","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Organic Dust Lung Diseases","q":"A worker employed for 12 years in the carding and spinning section of a cotton textile mill reports chest tightness, wheezing, and cough that are consistently worst on Monday mornings upon returning to work after the Sunday holiday. Which occupational lung disease is characteristic?","o":["Byssinosis (\u0027Monday morning chest tightness\u0027)","Coal worker\u0027s pneumoconiosis","Bagassosis","Asbestosis"],"a":0,"e":"Byssinosis results from chronic inhalation of cotton, flax, or hemp fibre dust (with bacterial endotoxins) in textile mills. Its hallmark clinical feature is \u0027Monday morning fever/chest tightness\u0027—acute dyspnoea and airway constriction upon re-exposure after a weekend break, which initially subsides mid-week before progressing to chronic irreversible obstructive airway disease.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01028","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Probability vs Non-Probability Sampling","q":"A nurse researcher studying compassion fatigue across a tertiary hospital divides all 600 staff nurses into four mutually exclusive departments (ICU, Emergency, Paediatrics, General Wards) and then uses a computer random number generator to select 25% of nurses from each department. Which sampling method is used?","o":["Convenience sampling","Stratified random sampling (proportionate probability sampling)","Quota non-probability sampling","Snowball sampling"],"a":1,"e":"Stratified random sampling divides the accessible population into homogeneous subgroups (strata) based on a key variable (e.g., clinical unit), then applies simple random sampling within every stratum. This guarantees proportional representation of smaller high-acuity units (like ICU) while preserving probability-based generalizability and reducing sampling error.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01029","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations \u0026 Pharmacology","topic":"Low-Molecular-Weight Heparin (Enoxaparin)","q":"At a pharmacology OSCE station, a student prepares to administer a prefilled syringe of enoxaparin (LMWH) 40 mg subcutaneously for DVT prophylaxis. Which technique prevents local bruising, haematoma, and under-dosing?","o":["Pull back plunger for 10 seconds to check blood return and apply a hot water bottle","Inject directly into a surgical scar near the umbilicus","Do NOT expel the prefilled nitrogen air bubble; pinch a skinfold in the anterolateral/posterolateral abdomen \u003e= 5 cm from the umbilicus, insert at 90 degrees, inject slowly without aspirating, and do NOT massage afterward","Expel the small air bubble from the prefilled syringe, inject into the deltoid, aspirate, and massage firmly"],"a":2,"e":"Prefilled LMWH syringes contain a measured nitrogen/air bubble calibrated by the manufacturer to clear dead space in the needle hub so the full dose is delivered and to seal the drug inside subcutaneous fat without tracking backward. Injecting \u003e= 5 cm away from the umbilicus at 90° without aspirating or massaging minimizes capillary rupture and abdominal wall haematoma.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01030","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Ethics, Legal Issues and Professionalism","topic":"Advance Directives","q":"A patient with progressive ALS asks the nurse to explain the difference between a \u0027Living Will\u0027 and a \u0027Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (Healthcare Proxy)\u0027. Which explanation is accurate?","o":["A Living Will is a financial bank document, whereas a Healthcare Proxy is a hospital discharge summary","A Living Will specifies a person\u0027s own written medical treatment preferences (e.g., mechanical ventilation, CPR) if they lose decision-making capacity, whereas a Healthcare Proxy designates a trusted surrogate to make medical decisions on their behalf during incapacity","Both documents automatically allow relatives to override a fully alert, competent patient right now","Neither document requires witnessing or formal legal validity"],"a":1,"e":"Advance directives allow competent adults to guide future care before incapacity occurs. A Living Will records specific treatment wishes (such as withholding/withdrawing life support in terminal illness), whereas a Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare names a surrogate decision-maker who can apply the patient\u0027s values to unforeseen clinical situations. Neither overrides a currently competent patient\u0027s wishes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01031","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Endocrinology)","topic":"Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State (HHS)","q":"A 72-year-old woman with Type 2 diabetes and a recent stroke presents with severe lethargy, dry wrinkled skin, BP 84/52 mmHg, blood glucose 880 mg/dL, effective serum osmolality 348 mOsm/kg, arterial pH 7.37, and negative urine/serum ketones. Which crisis is present, and what is the very first nursing priority?","o":["Diabetic ketoacidosis; withhold IV fluids and push IV sodium bicarbonate","SIADH; restrict fluids to 500 mL/day","Hypoglycaemic coma; give IV 50% Dextrose","Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State (HHS); establish large-bore IV access for immediate aggressive isotonic 0.9% Normal Saline volume resuscitation while monitoring K+ and neurological status"],"a":3,"e":"HHS occurs typically in older Type 2 diabetics who retain enough endogenous insulin to suppress lipolysis and ketogenesis (normal pH \u003e= 7.30, absent/minimal ketones) yet develop extreme hyperglycaemia (\u003e 600 mg/dL), osmotic diuresis, profound dehydration (8–10 L deficit), and serum hyperosmolality (\u003e 320 mOsm/kg) causing obtundation/coma. Immediate aggressive isotonic crystalloid resuscitation restores renal/cerebral perfusion before initiating low-dose IV insulin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01032","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Cardiology)","topic":"Post-MI Discharge Counselling","q":"A 54-year-old man recovering from an uncomplicated myocardial infarction asks the nurse when it is safe to resume sexual activity and whether he can take sildenafil with his prescribed sublingual nitroglycerin. Which discharge guidance is accurate?","o":["Sexual activity is generally safe once able to climb two flights of stairs briskly without chest pain or dyspnoea (usually ~4–6 weeks post-MI); NEVER combine sildenafil/tadalafil (PDE-5 inhibitors) with nitroglycerin due to fatal hypotension","Resume vigorous exertion on discharge day 1 while fasting","Avoid sexual activity permanently for life; take sildenafil together with nitroglycerin before intercourse","Take three nitroglycerin tablets simultaneously with sildenafil to boost blood flow"],"a":0,"e":"Sexual intercourse requires roughly 3–5 METs of cardiac workload—comparable to briskly climbing two flights of stairs or walking at ~3–4 mph without angina or dyspnoea. Most uncomplicated post-MI patients safely resume sexual activity around 4–6 weeks once passing exercise tolerance. Crucially, combining nitrates (nitroglycerin, isosorbide) with PDE-5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil, vardenafil) causes synergistic cGMP-mediated vasodilation and refractory, potentially fatal hypotension.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01033","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (ENT)","topic":"Ménière\u0027s Disease","q":"A 42-year-old patient diagnosed with Ménière\u0027s disease experiences recurrent attacks of severe spinning vertigo, unilateral tinnitus, ear fullness, and fluctuating hearing loss. Which long-term dietary change and acute-attack nursing safety measure are recommended?","o":["High-sodium diet with extra coffee; walk quickly during vertigo","Low-sodium diet avoiding caffeine, alcohol, and nicotine; during an acute vertigo attack, lie still in a quiet darkened room with eyes closed or fixed on a stationary object and keep side rails up","Unlimited fluid surges with energy drinks; perform rapid head-shaking exercises during vomiting","High-dose aminoglycoside ear drops daily at home"],"a":1,"e":"Ménière\u0027s disease results from endolymphatic hydrops (excess fluid pressure in the inner-ear labyrinth). Restricting dietary sodium (~1,500–2,000 mg/day) and avoiding vasoconstrictors/stimulants (caffeine, nicotine, alcohol) reduces endolymph volume and attack frequency alongside diuretics/betahistine. During acute vertigo, immobility in a quiet dim room, fall precautions, and vestibular suppressants/antiemetics prevent injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01034","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Neural and Chemical Control of Breathing","q":"In normal resting physiology, what is the primary direct chemical stimulus activating central chemoreceptors on the ventral surface of the medulla oblongata to increase ventilation?","o":["Mild fall in arterial PaO2 from 95 to 82 mmHg","Stretch of alveolar walls during shallow inspiration","Elevation in arterial PaCO2 diffusing across the blood-brain barrier to lower cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) pH (increased H+ concentration)","Rise in serum bicarbonate solely"],"a":2,"e":"Central medullary chemoreceptors are exquisitely sensitive to hydrogen ion (H+) changes in brain interstitial fluid/CSF. Because blood H+ ions cross the blood-brain barrier poorly whereas lipid-soluble CO2 diffuses rapidly and hydrates into carbonic acid (H2CO3 -\u003e H+ + HCO3-), elevated arterial PaCO2 is the chief physiologic driver of minute ventilation. Peripheral carotid and aortic bodies respond principally when PaO2 falls below ~60 mmHg.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01035","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Portal-Systemic Venous Anastomoses","q":"A patient with advanced hepatic cirrhosis and portal hypertension presents with dilated tortuous veins radiating outward around the umbilicus (\u0027caput medusae\u0027) and bleeding oesophageal varices. Which anatomical mechanism explains these collateral vessels?","o":["Inflammation of the thoracic lymphatic duct","Complete occlusion of the renal veins","Obstruction of superior mesenteric arterial inflow into the spleen","Elevated portal venous pressure shunting blood backward through valveless portosystemic anastomoses—paraumbilical veins (caput medusae) and left gastric-to-oesophageal veins (varices)"],"a":3,"e":"Because the hepatic portal venous system lacks valves, fibrotic intrahepatic resistance in cirrhosis raises portal pressure (\u003e 12 mmHg) and forces retrograde flow into systemic venous collaterals at key portocaval junctions: (1) lower oesophagus (left gastric vein -\u003e azygos/oesophageal veins = oesophageal varices), (2) umbilicus (recanalized paraumbilical veins -\u003e superficial epigastric veins = caput medusae), and (3) rectum (superior -\u003e middle/inferior rectal veins = anorectal varices).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01036","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Microbiology and Infection Control","topic":"Moist Heat Sterilization (Autoclave)","q":"Which standard gravity-displacement steam autoclave temperature, pressure, and biological spore indicator confirm destruction of heat-resistant bacterial endospores on surgical instruments?","o":["121°C at 15 psi (103 kPa) for 15–20 minutes; Geobacillus stearothermophilus spore strip","37°C incubator for 24 hours; Candida albicans","60°C dry heat for 10 minutes; Staphylococcus aureus swab","100°C at atmospheric pressure for 5 minutes; Escherichia coli strip"],"a":0,"e":"Pressurized saturated steam in an autoclave achieves 121°C at 15 psi for 15–20 minutes (or 134°C at ~30 psi for 3–4 minutes in pre-vacuum autoclaves), coagulating microbial proteins and killing all vegetative organisms and spores. The gold-standard biological monitoring indicator for steam autoclaves uses heat-resistant endospores of Geobacillus stearothermophilus (incubated post-cycle to verify zero growth).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01037","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Intradermal Tuberculin (Mantoux) Test","q":"At a nursing skills OSCE station, a student administers 0.1 mL of Purified Protein Derivative (PPD) for a Mantoux tuberculin skin test on the inner forearm. Which technique and follow-up reading rule are exact?","o":["Read results only at 14 days by measuring bruising","Insert bevel-up at 5–15 degrees intradermally until a crisp 6–10 mm pale wheal forms, do NOT massage or bandage, and measure transverse induration (palpable raised firmness, NOT redness) in mm at 48–72 hours","Insert at 45 degrees subcutaneously and circle with thick marker directly over the wheal","Insert needle at 90 degrees into muscle, massage firmly, and measure redness (erythema) after 6 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Intradermal Mantoux injection places 0.1 mL PPD just beneath the epidermis of the ventral forearm at a 5°–15° angle (bevel up), creating a tense 6–10 mm bleb/wheal without massaging (which disperses antigen) or adhesive bandaging. At 48–72 hours, the nurse palpates and measures ONLY the transverse diameter of firm induration across the forearm in millimetres—ignoring surrounding flat erythema.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01038","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Critical Care)","topic":"Dobutamine Infusion Calculation","q":"A 50 kg patient with acute decompensated heart failure is prescribed a continuous IV dobutamine infusion at 10 mcg/kg/min. The pharmacy supplies 250 mg (250,000 mcg) of dobutamine in 250 mL of 5% Dextrose. At what rate in mL/hour should the nurse set the infusion pump?","o":["60 mL/hr","15 mL/hr","30 mL/hr","45 mL/hr"],"a":2,"e":"Step 1: Calculate ordered dose per hour = 10 mcg/kg/min × 50 kg × 60 min/hr = 30,000 mcg/hr (30 mg/hr). Step 2: Determine bag concentration = 250,000 mcg ÷ 250 mL = 1,000 mcg/mL (1 mg/mL). Step 3: Calculate infusion rate = 30,000 mcg/hr ÷ 1,000 mcg/mL = 30 mL/hr.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01039","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Postmortem Nursing Care","q":"Immediately after a physician pronounces death in an uncomplicated medical ward case (non-medico-legal), why should the nurse elevate the head slightly on a small pillow, close the eyelids gently, and replace dentures promptly?","o":["To accelerate rigor mortis within 10 minutes","To preserve arterial blood pressure for organ harvest","To prevent bacterial growth indefinitely without refrigeration","To prevent blood pooling/discolouration in the face (livor mortis) and align facial/body posture naturally before rigor mortis stiffens muscles (typically 2–4 hours after death)"],"a":3,"e":"Prompt respectful postmortem care prepares the body for family viewing before rigor mortis (ATP-depletion muscle stiffening beginning 2–4 hours postmortem) fixes jaw or limb positions. Placing the body supine with a small pillow elevating the head/shoulders prevents dependent livor mortis facial discolouration; gently closing eyelids and replacing dentures preserves natural facial contours. (In medico-legal/autopsy cases, tubes, catheters, and dressings remain untouched per legal protocol).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01040","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Nephrology)","topic":"Prerenal vs Intrinsic (ATN) AKI","q":"A septic ICU patient treated with prolonged hypotension and gentamicin develops persistent oliguria despite fluid boluses. Urinalysis shows muddy brown granular casts, urine sodium 56 mEq/L, fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) 3.4%, and urine osmolality 295 mOsm/kg. Which AKI category do these findings confirm?","o":["Intrinsic renal Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN) with impaired tubular sodium reabsorption and concentrating ability","Normal physiological diuresis","Prerenal AKI (reversible hypoperfusion)","Postrenal ureteric stone obstruction solely"],"a":0,"e":"In prerenal AKI, intact tubules avidly conserve sodium and water (urine Na+ \u003c 20 mEq/L, FENa \u003c 1%, concentrated urine osmolality \u003e 500 mOsm/kg, bland sediment). When ischaemia or nephrotoxins (gentamicin, contrast) damage tubular epithelium—causing Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN)—sloughed tubular cells form \u0027muddy brown\u0027 granular casts and damaged tubules fail to reabsorb Na+ or concentrate urine (urine Na+ \u003e 40 mEq/L, FENa \u003e 2%, isotonic urine ~300 mOsm/kg).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01041","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Orthopaedics \u0026 Neurotrauma)","topic":"Halo Traction Vest Care","q":"A patient with an unstable C4 cervical fracture is immobilized in a Halo fixation ring and thoracic vest. Which bedside safety measure and moving technique are mandatory?","o":["Loosen the skull pins manually if the patient has mild headache","Keep the specialized halo wrench taped directly onto the anterior vest at all times for emergency CPR chest access, never lift by the metal struts, and inspect skull pin sites daily","Apply tight gauze wrapping over the anterior vest buckles","Pull on the vertical metal halo bars to help the patient sit up in bed"],"a":1,"e":"A rigid halo thoracic vest obscures the sternum. Keeping the manufacturer\u0027s wrench taped visibly to the vest ensures rapid removal of the anterior vest shell if emergency CPR/chest compressions are required. Lifting or pulling the halo ring/struts risks dislodging cranial pins and causing catastrophic cervical spinal cord transection; always support the vest/trunk during log-rolling and monitor pin sites for erythema/drainage or loosening.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01042","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (ENT \u0026 Critical Care)","topic":"Accidental Tracheostomy Decannulation","q":"Forty-eight hours after surgical tracheostomy creation (immature tract \u003c 7 days), the patient coughs violently and the entire tracheostomy tube pops out onto the chest, causing immediate respiratory distress. What is the safest bedside nursing response?","o":["Wait 20 minutes to see if the patient breathes through the collapsed stoma alone","Force the rigid outer cannula blindly downward without an obturator or visualization","Call for emergency airway team immediately, keep a sterile tracheal dilator/haemostat and smaller spare tube + obturator ready at bedside, cover the fresh stoma gently if needed while ventilating via bag-mask over the mouth/nose (if upper airway patent), and avoid forcing a tube into a false subcutaneous passage","Remove the inner cannula only and discard it"],"a":2,"e":"During the first 5–7 days post-tracheostomy, the tissue tract between skin and trachea is immature and collapses rapidly if the tube dislodges. Blindly forcing a tracheostomy tube without tract maturity frequently creates a lethal false passage into pretracheal soft tissues (causing subcutaneous emphysema and asphyxia). Call airway experts immediately for controlled re-insertion/orotracheal intubation while supporting oxygenation via bag-valve-mask (occluding the stoma gently if upper airway is open) or gently holding the stoma open with a sterile tracheal dilator if trained.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01043","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Oncology)","topic":"Superior Vena Cava (SVC) Syndrome","q":"A patient with right upper-lobe small-cell lung carcinoma develops progressive facial and periorbital oedema, plethora/cyanosis of the upper torso, engorged jugular and anterior chest-wall veins, and worsening dyspnoea when raising both arms above the head (Pemberton\u0027s sign). Which oncological emergency is present, and what IV access precaution applies?","o":["Acute left ventricular failure; give rapid 2,000 mL saline bolus into right arm","Simple allergic conjunctivitis; give oral antihistamine and place supine","Pulmonary tuberculosis; perform immediate vigorous chest percussion","Superior Vena Cava (SVC) syndrome due to mediastinal tumour compression; elevate head of bed, monitor airway/respiration, prepare urgent radiotherapy/steroids/stenting, and AVOID placing IV infusions or BP cuffs in the congested upper extremities (prefer lower-extremity IV access if needed)"],"a":3,"e":"Obstruction of the thin-walled Superior Vena Cava by mediastinal malignancy or catheter thrombosis blocks venous return from the head, neck, and upper limbs—producing facial/arm oedema, jugular/chest-wall venous collaterals, stridor/dyspnoea, and neurological confusion from cerebral venous congestion. Elevating the head of bed reduces hydrostatic pressure; avoiding upper-extremity venipunctures/IVs prevents severe extravasation, thrombosis, and erratic drug delivery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01044","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Cardiology)","topic":"Permanent Pacemaker Monitoring","q":"On cardiac telemetry 12 hours after dual-chamber pacemaker insertion, the nurse observes regular vertical pacemaker spikes at 70/min that are NOT followed by any QRS complex, accompanied by a patient pulse of 34 bpm and dizziness. Which pacemaker malfunction is occurring?","o":["Failure to capture (pacemaker fires an electrical spike, but the myocardium fails to depolarize)","Normal sinus rhythm at 70 bpm","Normal pacemaker demand capture","Oversensing inhibition solely"],"a":0,"e":"In \u0027failure to capture\u0027, the pacemaker generator discharges a visible pacing spike on the ECG, but no P wave or wide QRS complex immediately follows—often caused by lead dislodgement, battery depletion, fibrosis at the electrode tip, or severe acidosis/hyperkalaemia. Immediate bedside assessment for perfusion/pulse, external transcutaneous pacing readiness, and cardiologist notification to increase milliamperage (mA) output or reposition the lead are required. (Teach patients to avoid raising the implant-side arm above shoulder level for 4–6 weeks).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01045","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology \u0026 Critical Care Nursing","topic":"Torsades de Pointes","q":"A patient receiving IV haloperidol, ondansetron, and amiodarone with serum potassium 2.9 mEq/L develops a prolonged QTc interval (550 ms) followed by episodes of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia twisting smoothly around the isoelectric baseline (Torsades de Pointes) with a palpable weak pulse. Which first-line intravenous medication specifically stabilizes this arrhythmia?","o":["IV haloperidol 10 mg","IV Magnesium Sulphate (2 g IV slowly) while correcting hypokalaemia and discontinuing QT-prolonging medications","Oral digoxin tablet","IV amiodarone bolus (further prolongs QT)"],"a":1,"e":"Drug-induced QT prolongation (haloperidol, ondansetron, fluoroquinolones, macrolides, Class IA/III antiarrhythmics) combined with hypokalaemia or hypomagnesaemia predisposes to early afterdepolarizations triggering Torsades de Pointes. Intravenous Magnesium Sulphate (2 g IV over 10–15 min, or faster if pulseless alongside defibrillation) is the specific first-line therapy to suppress early afterdepolarizations, even when baseline serum magnesium is normal.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01046","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Anticholinergic Poisoning (Datura / Atropine)","q":"An adolescent brought to the emergency ward after accidental ingestion of Datura stramonium seeds is flushed, febrile (39.6°C), agitated/delirious, with dry mucous membranes, absent bowel sounds, urinary retention, and widely dilated unreactive pupils. Which toxidrome is present, and which specific cholinesterase inhibitor crosses the blood-brain barrier to reverse both CNS delirium and peripheral toxicity if severe?","o":["Cholinergic organophosphate poisoning; IV atropine","Beta-blocker toxicity; IV glucagon","Anticholinergic toxidrome; IV physostigmine (under ECG monitoring, alongside cooling, benzodiazepines, and bladder decompression)","Opioid overdose; IV naloxone"],"a":2,"e":"Datura alkaloids (atropine, scopolamine, hyoscyamine) block muscarinic receptors centrally and peripherally, producing the classic anticholinergic toxidrome: \u0027hot as a hare, dry as a bone, red as a beet, blind as a bat, mad as a hatter\u0027 plus urinary retention/ileus. Physostigmine is a lipid-soluble tertiary amine acetylcholinesterase inhibitor capable of crossing the blood-brain barrier to reverse both central delirium and peripheral signs (unlike quaternary neostigmine/pyridostigmine, which cannot cross the BBB).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01047","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Safe Vancomycin Infusion Pump Rate","q":"A patient with MRSA osteomyelitis is prescribed 1,000 mg of intravenous vancomycin diluted in 250 mL of 0.9% Normal Saline to be infused slowly over 2 hours (120 minutes) via an electronic infusion pump. At what rate in mL/hour should the nurse set the pump?","o":["500 mL/hr","250 mL/hr","62.5 mL/hr","125 mL/hr"],"a":3,"e":"Rate (mL/hr) = Total Volume (mL) ÷ Infusion Duration (hours) = 250 mL ÷ 2 hours = 125 mL/hr. Checking the drug delivery speed confirms 1,000 mg ÷ 120 minutes = 8.33 mg/min, safely below the recommended \u003c= 10 mg/min maximum infusion limit to prevent histamine-mediated Red Man Syndrome.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01048","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing II","topic":"Acute Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (APSGN)","q":"An 8-year-old boy presents 2 weeks after skin impetigo with puffy eyelids, headache, BP 146/96 mmHg, oliguria, and smoky tea-coloured urine showing RBC casts and mild (1+) proteinuria (APSGN). Which nursing monitoring priority prevents the most serious acute neurological complication?","o":["Monitor blood pressure every 2–4 hours alongside neurological checks (headache, visual changes, seizures) for hypertensive encephalopathy, maintain strict intake-output/daily weight, and restrict sodium/potassium during oliguria","Encourage unlimited salty snacks and high-potassium fruit juices during oliguria","Push 1,000 mL IV Normal Saline bolus rapidly","Administer high-dose oral prednisolone immediately as first-line cure"],"a":0,"e":"Acute Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (APSGN) causes immune-complex glomerular inflammation, reduced GFR, sodium/water retention, smoky haematuria, RBC casts, and hypertension. Sudden severe hypertension can precipitate hypertensive encephalopathy (headache, vomiting, blurred vision, seizures), making frequent BP/neurological monitoring, antihypertensives/loop diuretics, and fluid/sodium/potassium restriction during oliguria vital.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01049","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Congenital Hypothyroidism","q":"A 3-week-old neonate diagnosed on newborn screening with congenital hypothyroidism (prolonged jaundice, large posterior fontanelle, umbilical hernia, macroglossia, constipation) is started on oral levothyroxine tablets. What essential administration instruction must the nurse teach the parents?","o":["Discontinue therapy automatically at 6 months once jaundice clears","Crush the daily tablet and mix it into a few millilitres of breastmilk or plain water via syringe/spoon immediately before feeding, SEPARATING administration from soy formula, iron, or calcium supplements by at least 4 hours","Mix crushed levothyroxine inside a full bottle of soy formula or together with iron/calcium drops","Give the medication only when the baby feels sleepy once a month"],"a":1,"e":"Early lifelong levothyroxine replacement (initiated ideally within the first 2 weeks of life) prevents irreversible neurodevelopmental intellectual disability in congenital hypothyroidism. Tablets must be crushed fresh and given in a small spoonful/syringe of water or breastmilk so the entire dose is swallowed (never inside a whole feeding bottle, which may not be finished), and separated from soy protein, iron, and calcium—all of which bind levothyroxine in the gut and block absorption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01050","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Weight-Based Oral Macrolide Calculation","q":"A 16 kg child with atypical community-acquired pneumonia is prescribed oral azithromycin at 10 mg/kg for Day 1. The available oral suspension concentration is 200 mg/5 mL. How many millilitres (mL) should the nurse administer?","o":["6.0 mL","8.0 mL","4.0 mL","2.0 mL"],"a":2,"e":"Step 1: Calculate prescribed Day 1 dose = 16 kg × 10 mg/kg = 160 mg. Step 2: Calculate suspension volume using 200 mg/5 mL (40 mg/mL): (160 mg ÷ 200 mg) × 5 mL = 0.8 × 5 = 4.0 mL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01051","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing II","topic":"Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)","q":"A hospitalized client with Borderline Personality Disorder tells the day-shift nurse, \u0027You are the only compassionate nurse in this hospital; the night-shift nurse is cruel and incompetent,\u0027 while simultaneously complaining to the night-shift nurse about the day nurse. How should the nursing team manage this defence mechanism (\u0027splitting\u0027)?","o":["Rotate only one nurse permanently 24 hours a day","Isolate the client in seclusion for 48 hours as punishment","Agree with the client that the night-shift nurse is incompetent to maintain rapport","Recognize \u0027splitting\u0027 (polarizing staff into all-good vs all-bad), hold frequent team huddles to ensure consistent limit-setting and unified care plans across all shifts, and avoid defensive argument"],"a":3,"e":"Splitting is a primitive ego defence mechanism common in Borderline Personality Disorder wherein individuals cannot integrate positive and negative qualities in a single person, polarizing ward staff into \u0027all-good rescuers\u0027 and \u0027all-bad persecutors\u0027. Staff conflict is prevented by open inter-shift communication, consistent behavioural expectations, neutral empathy, and firm non-punitive boundaries across the entire multidisciplinary team.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01052","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing I","topic":"Disulfiram (Antabuse) Therapy","q":"A client with alcohol dependence beginning supervised oral disulfiram (Antabuse) therapy asks which everyday products must be avoided besides alcoholic beverages. Which health teaching is vital?","o":["Strictly avoid ALL hidden ethanol sources—including alcohol-based mouthwashes, cough/cold elixirs, topical alcohol-rich colognes/aftershaves, and cooking vinegars/sauces—and maintain abstinence for at least 14 days after discontinuing disulfiram","Avoid only beer and whisky; alcohol-containing mouthwash, cough syrups, and fermented sauces are completely harmless","Stop disulfiram for 12 hours before attending a party with alcohol","Drink one small glass of wine daily to build tolerance safely"],"a":0,"e":"Disulfiram irreversibly inhibits hepatic aldehyde dehydrogenase. Even tiny amounts of absorbed ethanol—from cough syrups, mouthwash, sauces, or transdermal aftershaves—cause rapid acetaldehyde buildup resulting in severe flushing, throbbing headache, projectile vomiting, hypotension, arrhythmias, and collapse. Because new enzyme synthesis takes up to 2 weeks, alcohol avoidance must continue for 14 days after the final disulfiram dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01053","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Amniotic Fluid Embolism (AFE)","q":"During tumultuous labour immediately after amniotomy, a multiparous woman suddenly gasps for air, turns deeply cyanotic, becomes unresponsive with cardiac arrest, and begins bleeding profusely from her IV catheter sites within minutes. Which rare obstetric catastrophe is occurring, and what is the immediate response?","o":["Mild vasovagal fainting; offer sips of orange juice","Amniotic Fluid Embolism (anaphylactoid syndrome of pregnancy) triggering cardiopulmonary collapse and fulminant DIC; initiate immediate CPR/ACLS with left uterine displacement, airway intubation/oxygen, massive blood-component transfusion protocol, and perimortem delivery if needed","Simple urinary retention; insert Foley catheter only","Normal second-stage bearing down; encourage stronger pushing"],"a":1,"e":"Amniotic fluid embolism occurs when amniotic fluid/foetal debris enters maternal venous sinuses, provoking an anaphylactoid pulmonary vasospasm, acute right-to-left heart failure, profound hypoxia/cardiac arrest, and fulminant Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC). Immediate high-quality CPR with manual left uterine displacement, oxygenation/ventilation, aggressive blood/FFP/cryoprecipitate/platelet resuscitation, and expedited delivery are life-saving.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01054","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Chorioamnionitis (Intra-Amniotic Infection)","q":"Twenty-four hours after spontaneous rupture of membranes at 37 weeks gestation, a labouring woman develops a fever of 38.6°C, maternal pulse 116 bpm, foetal baseline heart rate 174 bpm (foetal tachycardia), uterine fundal tenderness, and foul-smelling amniotic fluid. Which diagnosis and management apply?","o":["Normal latent labour; discharge home for bed rest","Placenta praevia; delay delivery by 4 weeks with steroids only","Chorioamnionitis (intra-amniotic infection); initiate broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (e.g., ampicillin plus gentamicin) immediately and expedite delivery","Benign epidural warmth; withhold antibiotics until postpartum"],"a":2,"e":"Chorioamnionitis is an ascending polymicrobial bacterial infection of the amniotic cavity and membranes following prolonged membrane rupture. Diagnostic features include maternal fever (\u003e= 38.0°C) plus maternal/foetal tachycardia (\u003e 100 and \u003e 160 bpm), uterine tenderness, maternal leukocytosis, and purulent/foul liquor. Prompt intrapartum broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (covering GBS, E. coli, anaerobes) and delivery prevent maternal sepsis and neonatal sepsis/brain injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01055","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Community Health Nursing","topic":"Injectable Contraceptive DMPA (Antara Programme)","q":"A postpartum mother chooses the injectable progestin contraceptive DMPA (Depot Medroxyprogesterone Acetate 150 mg IM) under India\u0027s MoHFW Antara programme. Which counselling point regarding injection interval, bleeding pattern, and fertility return is accurate?","o":["Administer IM every 12 months; causes immediate return of fertility within 48 hours of stopping","Massage the deltoid injection site vigorously immediately to speed up release","Give IV slowly every week","Administer 150 mg deep IM every 3 months (90 days); explain that irregular spotting initially followed by amenorrhoea is expected and harmless, and fertility return may take ~6–10 months after the last injection"],"a":3,"e":"Under India\u0027s Antara programme, DMPA (150 mg) is injected deep IM (gluteal/deltoid, without massaging) every 3 months (13 weeks). High progestin suppression commonly produces irregular light spotting in early cycles and eventual amenorrhoea (which reduces anaemia and is medically safe). Because depot microcrystals clear slowly, ovulation/fertility returns after a typical lag of 6–10 months post-last dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01056","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing I","topic":"Case-Control vs Cohort Studies","q":"A public health nurse epidemiologist wants to investigate environmental risk factors for a rare paediatric bone sarcoma (incidence 3 per million children per year) within a modest 1-year budget. Which observational study design and primary measure of association are most suitable?","o":["Case-Control study (comparing children diagnosed with the sarcoma against matched disease-free controls for past exposures) computing the Odds Ratio (OR)","Prospective cohort study measuring Incidence Rate directly","Randomized controlled drug trial","Ecological correlation solely"],"a":0,"e":"Case-control studies start with outcome status (cases vs controls) and look backward for prior exposures, making them fast, cost-effective, and ideally suited for rare diseases or diseases with long latency periods where following millions of healthy subjects prospectively in a cohort study would be unfeasible. Because total population at risk is not followed from baseline, case-control studies estimate Relative Risk via the Odds Ratio (OR).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01057","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Mass Casualty Triage (START System)","q":"At the scene of a multi-coach railway accident using the START (Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment) colour-coded system, which patient receives a RED (Immediate) triage tag?","o":["A victim with catastrophic open cranial vault avulsion, no pulse, and no spontaneous breathing even after airway opening","An adult with severe chest trauma, respiratory rate 34/min, weak radial pulse (capillary refill \u003e 2 s), and survivable shock requiring urgent stabilization","A conscious passenger with a closed distal radius fracture and stable vitals","A walking passenger with minor forearm abrasions"],"a":1,"e":"In mass casualty START triage, limited resources prioritize maximum lives saved: RED (Immediate) tags go to victims with life-threatening yet salvageable compromise of airway, breathing (RR \u003e 30/min), perfusion (absent radial pulse/capillary refill \u003e 2 s), or altered mental status; YELLOW (Delayed) goes to serious non-life-threatening injuries (closed fractures); GREEN (Minor) goes to walking wounded; BLACK (Expectant/Deceased) goes to apnoeic pulseless or non-survivable trauma.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01058","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Reliability Assessment","q":"A final-year B.Sc Nursing student administers a newly translated 18-item Likert-scale questionnaire measuring \u0027Nurse Job Satisfaction\u0027 once to 120 ward nurses and wants to verify whether all 18 items consistently measure the same underlying construct. Which statistical reliability index is appropriate?","o":["Mann-Whitney U statistic","Cohen\u0027s kappa solely","Cronbach\u0027s alpha (internal consistency coefficient, typically acceptable \u003e= 0.70)","Chi-square goodness-of-fit"],"a":2,"e":"Cronbach\u0027s alpha evaluates internal consistency reliability—how closely items within a multi-item Likert scale correlate with one another as a unified construct—from a single administration (values \u003e= 0.70 are generally acceptable, \u003e= 0.80 strong). By contrast, Test-retest correlation assesses stability across two separate time points, and Cohen\u0027s kappa evaluates categorical inter-rater agreement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01059","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)","q":"At an infection-control OSCE station exiting an isolation room after caring for a patient on Contact + Airborne precautions, in what safest order must the student doff (remove) soiled PPE to avoid self-contamination?","o":["Gown first while keeping soiled gloves on -\u003e touch front of N95 respirator with bare hands","N95 respirator first -\u003e gloves -\u003e goggles -\u003e gown","Remove all items simultaneously by pulling over the face","Gloves first (most contaminated) -\u003e goggles/face shield -\u003e gown (turning inside-out) -\u003e N95 respirator last outside the room (touching only rear straps), followed immediately by hand hygiene"],"a":3,"e":"WHO/CDC doffing removes the most heavily contaminated items first while protecting mucous membranes and respiratory tract until last. Inside the room/ante-area: remove gloves (glove-to-glove/skin-to-skin), remove goggles/face shield from rear straps, unfasten and peel gown inward turning contaminated front inside-out, perform hand hygiene, exit and close door, then remove N95 respirator from rear elastic straps last without touching the contaminated filter front, finishing with hand hygiene.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01060","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Ethics, Legal Issues and Professionalism","topic":"Elements of Nursing Malpractice (Negligence)","q":"A nurse administers an antibiotic despite a clearly documented severe penicillin allergy on the wristband and chart, resulting in anaphylactic shock and anoxic brain injury. Which four legal elements must all be proven in court to establish professional nursing malpractice?","o":["Duty owed to the patient, Breach of the standard of care, Proximate causation linking the breach to injury, and Actual damages/harm","Only breach of hospital dress code","Motive, criminal intent, arrest, and fine","Written apology, gossip, shift change, and salary deduction"],"a":0,"e":"Under civil tort law applicable to nursing malpractice (professional negligence), a plaintiff must prove all four elements: (1) Duty (nurse-patient care relationship existed), (2) Breach of duty (failing to verify documented allergy fell below reasonable nursing standards), (3) Proximate causation (the wrongful drug directly caused the anaphylaxis), and (4) Damages (measurable injury/brain damage occurred). Missing even one element (e.g., catching a near-miss before harm occurs) means malpractice damages are not established.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01061","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Vascular \u0026 GI)","topic":"Acute Mesenteric Ischaemia","q":"A 74-year-old man with chronic atrial fibrillation (non-adherent to anticoagulation) develops sudden agonizing periumbilical abdominal pain, nausea, and one loose stool. Surprisingly, on physical examination his abdomen is soft, flat, and only mildly tender without guarding (\u0027pain out of proportion to exam findings\u0027). Which vascular emergency must the nurse suspect immediately?","o":["Acute cystitis solely","Acute mesenteric arterial embolism/ischaemia; keep strictly NPO, initiate IV fluids, avoid oral food/laxatives, and alert the physician for urgent CT angiography and vascular/surgical intervention","Simple functional constipation; give an enema and discharge","Benign gastroesophageal reflux; offer spicy food"],"a":1,"e":"In atrial fibrillation, a left atrial thrombus can embolize to the superior mesenteric artery (SMA). Early mucosal ischaemia causes excruciating visceral pain while the serosa/peritoneum is not yet inflamed—producing the classic red flag of \u0027severe abdominal pain out of proportion to benign abdominal physical findings\u0027. Rapid diagnosis via CT angiography and urgent revascularization/anticoagulation before bowel gangrene and peritonitis develop dramatically lowers mortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01062","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Rheumatology \u0026 Immunology)","topic":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)","q":"A 26-year-old woman newly diagnosed with Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) presents with a butterfly malar facial rash, symmetric polyarthralgia, and mild proteinuria. Which lifestyle and self-monitoring instruction helps prevent severe SLE flares?","o":["Stop all medications immediately as soon as joint pain eases","Take high-dose oral sulfonamide antibiotics prophylactically every month","Avoid direct ultraviolet sun exposure by using broad-spectrum sunscreen (SPF \u003e= 30) and protective hats/clothing, adhere to prescribed hydroxychloroquine (with periodic eye exams), report fever/oedema/foamy urine promptly, and never abruptly stop corticosteroids","Sunbathe daily for 2 hours at noon to fade the malar rash"],"a":2,"e":"Ultraviolet (UV) radiation induces keratinocyte apoptosis and autoantigen exposure, triggering both cutaneous and systemic SLE flares (including lupus nephritis). Strict photoprotection, consistent hydroxychloroquine therapy (with regular ophthalmologic retinal screening), vigilant infection/renal symptom reporting (foamy urine, hypertension, weight gain), and gradual steroid tapering prevent fatal renal and adrenal complications.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01063","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Musculoskeletal \u0026 Pharmacology)","topic":"Gout Management and Allopurinol Safety","q":"A 50-year-old man recovering from recurrent acute gouty arthritis of the first metatarsophalangeal joint (podagra) is started on daily oral allopurinol for chronic uric acid lowering. Which patient education point is most critical for safety and efficacy?","o":["Take allopurinol only for 2 days during an acute swollen attack and stop","Take high-dose aspirin 1,000 mg daily to speed uric acid excretion","Avoid water intake so urine remains concentrated","Drink 2–3 litres of fluids daily to prevent uric acid kidney stones, limit purine-rich organ meats/shellfish/alcohol, do not alter allopurinol abruptly during acute attacks, and stop drug and report ANY skin rash immediately"],"a":3,"e":"Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase to reduce uric acid production. Maintaining 2–3 L/day hydration prevents urate nephrolithiasis; abruptly starting or stopping allopurinol during an acute flare mobilizes joint urate crystals and worsens pain. Crucially, allopurinol can rarely cause severe cutaneous hypersensitivity / Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS/TEN/DRESS), so any new rash requires immediate discontinuation and medical review.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01064","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Corneal Blink Reflex","q":"When testing the corneal blink reflex with a wisp of sterile cotton in an unconscious neuro-ICU patient, which cranial nerves mediate the afferent sensory limb and the efferent motor blink limb respectively?","o":["Afferent CN V1 (Ophthalmic division of Trigeminal nerve); Efferent CN VII (Facial nerve to orbicularis oculi)","Afferent CN III (Oculomotor); Efferent CN VI (Abducens)","Afferent CN II (Optic); Efferent CN III (Oculomotor)","Afferent CN VII (Facial); Efferent CN V3 (Mandibular)"],"a":0,"e":"Touching the cornea gently with a sterile cotton wisp stimulates free sensory nerve endings of the nasociliary branch of the ophthalmic trigeminal division (CN V1—afferent), relaying to the pons and triggering bilateral contraction of orbicularis oculi via the facial nerve (CN VII—efferent). Absent corneal reflex signals pontine/cranial nerve dysfunction and requires prophylactic lubricating gel/taping to prevent exposure keratitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01065","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Starling Capillary Filtration Forces","q":"A child with severe kwashiorkor and an adult with nephrotic syndrome both develop marked generalized pitting oedema with serum albumin of 1.6 g/dL. Which alteration in capillary Starling forces primarily drives this interstitial fluid accumulation?","o":["Increased red blood cell haemoglobin concentration","Markedly decreased plasma colloid oncotic (osmotic) pressure reducing inward fluid reabsorption, shifting net Starling filtration outward into the interstitium","Complete paralysis of arterial baroreceptors","Elevated plasma colloid oncotic pressure pulling extra water into capillaries"],"a":1,"e":"Plasma proteins—principally albumin (~75%–80% of plasma oncotic force)—exert inward colloid osmotic (oncotic) pressure opposing outward capillary hydrostatic pressure. When severe malnutrition or massive proteinuria drops serum albumin below ~2.0 g/dL, inward oncotic pull collapses, allowing hydrostatic pressure to drive net fluid transudation into interstitial spaces faster than lymphatics can drain it, producing generalized pitting oedema.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01066","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Applied Microbiology and Infection Control","topic":"Spaulding Classification System","q":"According to Spaulding\u0027s classification for reprocessing reusable medical equipment, how are flexible gastrointestinal endoscopes and laryngoscope blades (contacting intact mucous membranes without entering sterile tissue) categorized and processed?","o":["Non-critical items requiring autoclave incineration","Critical items requiring only soap-and-water wiping","Semi-critical items requiring at minimum High-Level Disinfection (HLD) (e.g., 2% glutaraldehyde or ortho-phthalaldehyde) or sterilization","Single-use items that must never be cleaned before sterilization"],"a":2,"e":"Spaulding\u0027s framework matches infection risk to reprocessing rigour: Critical items (entering sterile tissue/vasculature, e.g., scalpels, surgical forceps) require full Sterilization; Semi-critical items (contacting mucous membranes or non-intact skin, e.g., endoscopes, laryngoscope blades, respiratory circuits) require High-Level Disinfection (eliminating all microbes except high numbers of bacterial spores) or sterilization; Non-critical items (intact skin, e.g., BP cuffs) require low/intermediate disinfection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01067","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Sterile Urine Culture from Indwelling Catheter","q":"At a clinical skills OSCE station, a nurse needs to obtain a sterile urine specimen for culture and sensitivity from an adult patient who already has an indwelling Foley catheter in place. Which technique yields an uncontaminated diagnostic sample?","o":["Open the spigot at the bottom of the gravity drainage bag and pour urine into the sterile container","Remove the entire Foley catheter and insert a brand new one purely for routine culture","Disconnect the catheter-to-drainage-tubing junction completely to catch dripping urine","Clamp the drainage tubing briefly below the sampling port if needed, scrub the catheter\u0027s needleless/rubber sampling port with 70% alcohol (allow to dry), aspirate urine sterilely via syringe, and unclamp tubing afterward"],"a":3,"e":"Urine sitting inside a gravity drainage bag rapidly develops heavy bacterial colonization and yields false-positive polymicrobial culture results. Always collect fresh urine directly from the disinfected sampling port on the proximal drainage tubing using aseptic syringe aspiration (temporarily clamping distal tubing \u003c= 15–30 minutes only if urine is absent, and immediately unclamping afterward to prevent urinary stasis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01068","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I \u0026 Pharmacology","topic":"IV Potassium Chloride Infusion Rate","q":"A patient with serum potassium 2.7 mEq/L is prescribed 40 mEq of Potassium Chloride (KCl) diluted in 1,000 mL of 0.9% Normal Saline to infuse at 10 mEq/hour via a peripheral volumetric infusion pump. At what rate in mL/hour should the nurse program the pump?","o":["250 mL/hr","100 mL/hr","150 mL/hr","400 mL/hr"],"a":0,"e":"Step 1: Calculate solution concentration = 40 mEq ÷ 1,000 mL = 0.04 mEq/mL (or 4 mEq per 100 mL). Step 2: Calculate pump rate = Ordered mEq/hr ÷ concentration = 10 mEq/hr ÷ 0.04 mEq/mL = 250 mL/hr (equivalently, 10 mEq is one-quarter of 40 mEq, so 1,000 mL ÷ 4 = 250 mL/hr). Never administer KCl via IV push; peripheral KCl rate should ordinarily not exceed 10 mEq/hr without ICU cardiac monitoring.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01069","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Physical Restraint Safety Standards","q":"When non-pharmacological de-escalation fails and a time-limited physician order is obtained for soft wrist restraints to prevent an agitated ICU patient from self-extubating, which nursing practice ensures physical and legal safety?","o":["Keep a standing PRN restraint order active for 30 days without reassessment","Use quick-release slip knots tied ONLY to the stationary bedframe (never movable side rails), pad wrists allowing two fingers underneath, assess circulation/skin/needs every 15–30 minutes, and release/reassess every 2 hours","Tie restraints tightly in double square knots directly to the movable side rails","Leave all four side rails raised simultaneously as a substitute for assessment"],"a":1,"e":"Restraints are a last resort requiring an active time-limited physician order (never PRN). Tying quick-release slip knots to the non-moving bedframe (rather than movable side rails) prevents limb traction injury if a rail is lowered and allows instantaneous emergency release during a code or fire. Frequent neurovascular checks, padding, two-finger slack, hydration/toileting, and 2-hourly release prevent pressure ulcers, nerve palsy, and asphyxiation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01070","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Cardiology)","topic":"Acute Pericarditis","q":"Ten days after a viral upper respiratory illness, a 29-year-old man presents with sharp retrosternal pleuritic chest pain that worsens when lying flat and markedly improves when sitting upright and leaning forward. Auscultation reveals a scratchy high-pitched pericardial friction rub along the left sternal border, and ECG shows diffuse concave-upward ST elevations with PR-segment depression. Why must systemic anticoagulants be avoided?","o":["Anticoagulants abolish the friction rub within seconds","Pericarditis requires urgent emergency fibrinolysis","In acute pericarditis, systemic anticoagulation risks life-threatening haemorrhage into the inflamed pericardial sac leading to cardiac tamponade; treat instead with NSAIDs and colchicine","Anticoagulants cause immediate coronary plaque rupture"],"a":2,"e":"Acute pericarditis presents with positional pleuritic pain (relieved leaning forward, worsened supine/inspiration), a triphasic/biphasic scratchy friction rub heard best at the left lower sternal border on held expiration, and diffuse concave-upward ST elevation with PR depression (unlike localized convex STEMI with reciprocal depression). Standard therapy uses high-dose NSAIDs (ibuprofen/aspirin) plus colchicine; anticoagulants/thrombolytics risk haemorrhagic pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01071","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Cardiorespiratory)","topic":"Right-Sided Heart Failure / Cor Pulmonale","q":"A 66-year-old patient with severe chronic emphysema and pulmonary hypertension develops progressive bilateral pitting ankle oedema, jugular venous distension (JVD) at 45 degrees, tender hepatomegaly, ascites, and nocturnal weight gain, while lung auscultation shows no bibasilar crackles. Which syndrome explains these findings?","o":["Isolated left renal artery stenosis","Acute pericardial constriction from hypothyroidism","Isolated left ventricular systolic pulmonary oedema","Right-sided heart failure (Cor Pulmonale) secondary to hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction increasing right ventricular afterload and causing systemic venous congestion"],"a":3,"e":"Chronic alveolar hypoxia in COPD causes sustained pulmonary arterial vasoconstriction and vascular remodelling (pulmonary hypertension), overloading the right ventricle (Cor Pulmonale). Right ventricular failure backs venous blood backward into the systemic venous circulation—producing JVD, hepatomegaly, hepatojugular reflux, ascites, and dependent peripheral pitting oedema—whereas pulmonary crackles characterize left-sided heart failure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01072","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Nephrology)","topic":"Peritonitis \u0026 Drain Troubleshooting","q":"A patient performing Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis (CAPD) calls the renal clinic reporting cloudy, turbid drained dialysate effluent alongside mild diffuse abdominal pain and fever (38.2°C). What is the nurse\u0027s priority clinical interpretation and instruction?","o":["Cloudy peritoneal effluent is the earliest hallmark of infectious PD peritonitis; instruct the patient to bring the cloudy effluent bag immediately for cell count, Gram stain, and culture, and prepare prescribed intraperitoneal antibiotics","Cloudy effluent is harmless high-protein clearance; continue exchanges at home without evaluation","Flush the Tenckhoff catheter forcefully with boiling tap water","Remove the surgical Tenckhoff catheter at home immediately"],"a":0,"e":"In peritoneal dialysis, drained effluent should always be clear and pale straw-yellow. Cloudy/opaque effluent (WBC \u003e 100/mm3 with \u003e 50% neutrophils)—often accompanied by abdominal pain, rebound tenderness, and fever—signals PD peritonitis (commonly from touch contamination with Staphylococcus epidermidis/aureus). Rapid effluent sampling and empirical intraperitoneal antibiotics preserve peritoneal membrane function and prevent sepsis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01073","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Neurotrauma)","topic":"Spinal Shock vs Neurogenic Shock","q":"Immediately after an acute C6 spinal cord contusion, a patient develops complete flaccid paralysis, absent deep tendon reflexes, loss of bowel/bladder tone, and an absent bulbocavernosus reflex below the injury level, though blood pressure stabilizes at 112/70 mmHg. Three weeks later, the bulbocavernosus reflex returns and lower-limb muscle tone turns spastic. What do these two phases represent?","o":["Permanent brain death followed by recovery","Spinal shock (transient physiological concussion/loss of all spinal reflexes below the lesion) resolving into upper motor neuron spasticity once spinal reflex arcs recover","Hypovolaemic shock solely","Autonomic dysreflexia progressing to coma"],"a":1,"e":"Spinal shock is a temporary state of complete neurological depression below an acute spinal cord injury—manifesting as flaccid areflexia, atonic bladder/bowel, and absent sacral reflexes (bulbocavernosus/anal wink)—distinct from neurogenic shock (which describes hypotension + bradycardia from sympathetic loss). Return of the bulbocavernosus reflex signals resolution of spinal shock and allows true prognosis assessment as spastic UMN reflexes emerge.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01074","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (GI \u0026 Respiratory Procedures)","topic":"Post-Bronchoscopy / EGD Recovery","q":"A patient returns to the recovery area after flexible bronchoscopy performed under conscious sedation and topical pharyngeal lignocaine spray, asking eagerly for a glass of water because his throat feels dry. What MUST the nurse verify before offering any oral fluids?","o":["Wait strictly 24 hours NPO regardless of swallowing ability","Give ice cream immediately to soothe throat soreness","Verify full return of the gag and swallow reflexes alongside alert level of consciousness (testing carefully with a tongue depressor/cotton swab) to prevent silent aspiration","Check only peripheral pedal pulses before giving water"],"a":2,"e":"Topical pharyngeal anaesthetics (lignocaine spray/jelly) numb protective laryngeal and pharyngeal sensory reflexes for ~1–3 hours after bronchoscopy or upper GI endoscopy (EGD). Offering water or food while the throat remains numb causes painless silent pulmonary aspiration and pneumonia. Keep NPO in lateral/semi-Fowler\u0027s position until full alertness and intact gag/swallow reflexes are confirmed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01075","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology \u0026 Emergency Nursing","topic":"Organophosphate Pesticide Poisoning","q":"A farmworker exposed while spraying monocrotophos (an organophosphate insecticide) arrives with pinpoint pupils (miosis), profuse frothy bronchorrhoea, wheezing, heart rate 40 bpm, involuntary urination/diarrhoea, and muscle fasciculations. Alongside PPE decontamination and frequent airway suctioning, which antidote regimen and clinical endpoint guide emergency treatment?","o":["Oral activated charcoal only without IV antidotes","IV naloxone until pupils dilate","IV physostigmine to further inhibit cholinesterase","IV Atropine (titrated upward until tracheobronchial secretions clear and lungs dry—\u0027atropinization\u0027, NOT relying on pupil dilation alone) PLUS IV Pralidoxime (2-PAM) to reactivate acetylcholinesterase"],"a":3,"e":"Organophosphates irreversibly phosphorylate acetylcholinesterase, flooding muscarinic (DUMBELS: diarrhoea, urination, miosis, bronchorrhoea/bradycardia, emesis, lacrimation, salivation) and nicotinic (fasciculations, paralysis) synapses. High-dose IV atropine reverses life-threatening muscarinic bronchorrhoea/bronchospasm/bradycardia—titrated specifically to clear lung crackles/secretions, dry axillae, and HR \u003e 80–90 bpm—while IV pralidoxime (2-PAM) cleaves the organophosphate before \u0027aging\u0027 to restore nicotinic neuromuscular strength.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01076","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT)","q":"A patient receiving unfractionated IV heparin for 6 days has platelet count drop from 2,30,000/mm3 to 78,000/mm3 (\u003e 50% fall) accompanied by a new deep vein thrombosis (Hit Type II). What immediate nursing and collaborative actions are mandatory?","o":["Discontinue ALL heparin immediately (including heparin catheter flushes and LMWH), notify the physician to start a non-heparin anticoagulant (e.g., argatroban or fondaparinux), and avoid routine platelet transfusion","Double the IV heparin infusion rate to dissolve the new clot","Start high-dose warfarin monotherapy immediately without direct thrombin inhibitor cover","Switch from unfractionated heparin to low-molecular-weight heparin (enoxaparin) and transfuse 6 units of platelets"],"a":0,"e":"Type II HIT is an immune-mediated prothrombotic disorder driven by IgG antibodies against platelet factor 4 (PF4)-heparin complexes, activating platelets and causing catastrophic arterial/venous thrombosis despite thrombocytopenia. Every source of heparin—including central-line heparin flushes and cross-reactive LMWH—must stop immediately, substituting a direct thrombin inhibitor (argatroban/bivalirudin) or fondaparinux. Prophylactic platelet transfusions add fuel to thrombosis and are avoided unless severe active bleeding occurs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01077","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing \u0026 Pharmacology","topic":"Weight-Based Loading Dose Calculation","q":"A 12 kg child with suspected bacterial meningitis is prescribed an initial IV loading dose of ampicillin at 100 mg/kg. After reconstitution per manufacturer instructions, the vial concentration is 250 mg/mL. How many millilitres (mL) should the nurse draw up from the vial?","o":["2.4 mL","4.8 mL","3.6 mL","6.0 mL"],"a":1,"e":"Step 1: Calculate total prescribed loading dose = 12 kg × 100 mg/kg = 1,200 mg. Step 2: Divide by reconstituted vial concentration (250 mg/mL): 1,200 mg ÷ 250 mg/mL = 4.8 mL. Further dilute and infuse slowly over 15–30 minutes per institutional paediatric IV monograph.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01078","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Coarctation of the Aorta","q":"A 6-year-old boy evaluated for exertional leg fatigue and recurrent nosebleeds is found to have blood pressure 148/90 mmHg with bounding radial/brachial pulses in both arms, whereas femoral pulses are weak and delayed (radio-femoral delay) with BP 88/54 mmHg in both legs. Which congenital heart defect is most likely?","o":["Isolated pulmonary valve stenosis","Atrial septal defect (ASD)","Coarctation of the aorta (aortic narrowing distal to the left subclavian artery)","Patent foramen ovale"],"a":2,"e":"Coarctation of the aorta narrows the aortic lumen typically near the ductus arteriosus just distal to the left subclavian artery. Consequently, blood pressure and pulses proximal to the narrowing (head and upper limbs) are markedly elevated, while distal perfusion (femoral arteries and lower limbs) shows weak, delayed pulses, lower BP (\u003e 20 mmHg gradient), and cool extremities. Four-extremity BP assessment is a hallmark nursing check.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01079","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Infant Developmental Milestones","q":"During a well-baby clinic assessment, which fine motor and psychosocial developmental milestones should a typically developing 9-to-10-month-old infant demonstrate?","o":["Copies a circle and ties shoelaces","Runs up stairs unassisted and speaks in 4-word sentences","Shows zero head control when pulled to sit","Uses a thumb-forefinger pincer grasp to pick up small objects, transfers toys hand-to-hand, sits steadily unsupported, and exhibits stranger anxiety"],"a":3,"e":"By 9–10 months, infants sit unsupported steadily, crawl/pull to stand, demonstrate object permanence, show stranger anxiety (healthy attachment discrimination), say \u0027mama/dada\u0027 nonspecifically, and transition from palmar grasp to a mature thumb-index finger pincer grasp (making small household objects a choking hazard requiring parent safety counselling).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01080","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing I","topic":"Concentrated Infant Drop Calculation","q":"A 6 kg infant with post-immunization fever (38.8°C) is prescribed oral paracetamol at 15 mg/kg/dose. The available concentrated paediatric paracetamol drops are labelled 100 mg/mL. How many millilitres (mL) should the nurse measure with the calibrated dropper?","o":["0.9 mL","0.45 mL","1.5 mL","0.6 mL"],"a":0,"e":"Step 1: Calculate ordered dose = 6 kg × 15 mg/kg = 90 mg. Step 2: Calculate drop volume using 100 mg/mL concentration: 90 mg ÷ 100 mg/mL = 0.9 mL. Always double-check concentrated infant drops (100 mg/mL) against standard syrup (120–250 mg/5 mL) to prevent accidental hepatotoxic overdose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01081","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing II","topic":"Catatonia Nursing Care","q":"A young woman with catatonic schizophrenia lies mute and immobile for hours, holding her raised arm rigidly wherever the examiner places it (waxy flexibility), and refuses all food and fluids. What is the highest-priority nursing care focus alongside preparing prescribed benzodiazepines (lorazepam) or ECT?","o":["Argue loudly demanding she move immediately","Prevent life-threatening physical complications of immobility and refusal—monitoring strict intake-output, nutrition/hydration, passive ROM/DVT prophylaxis, skin repositioning, and bladder/bowel output (avoiding forcing fluids orally while stuporous)","Force 500 mL milk rapidly down her throat while supine","Leave her completely undisturbed for 48 hours until she speaks spontaneously"],"a":1,"e":"Catatonic stupor and waxy flexibility (cerea flexibilitas) place patients at high medical risk for severe dehydration, electrolyte imbalance, pressure ulcers, venous thromboembolism/PE, urinary retention, and rhabdomyolysis. Priority nursing care maintains hydration/nutrition safely (IV/enteral as ordered, never forcing oral fluids in stupor), 2-hourly turning, passive ROM, and close monitoring while lorazepam/ECT treats the catatonia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01082","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing II","topic":"Antisocial Personality Disorder","q":"A client diagnosed with Antisocial Personality Disorder (history of childhood conduct disorder, repeated deceitfulness, impulsivity, and lack of remorse) uses superficial charm to persuade a new nurse to bend ward rules and grant special privileges. Which nursing approach is most therapeutic?","o":["Ignore all rule violations completely","Grant special exceptions to avoid conflict and build friendship","Maintain firm, clear, consistent unit rules and behavioural limits agreed upon by the entire team, confront manipulative rule-breaking neutrally with immediate consequences, and avoid power struggles","Engage in personal self-disclosure to win loyalty"],"a":2,"e":"Clients with Antisocial Personality Disorder exploit inconsistencies among staff to bypass accountability. Clear, matter-of-fact expectations, unified team enforcement of unit rules, neutral redirection of flattery/guilt-tripping, and natural consequences teach responsibility without moralizing or punitive anger.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01083","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Antenatal Corticosteroids","q":"A woman at 30 weeks gestation is admitted with Preterm Premature Rupture of Membranes (PPROM) confirmed by sterile speculum exam (clear pooling fluid, no contractions, no fever, normal FHR). Why does the obstetric team prescribe IM dexamethasone (6 mg q12h × 4 doses) and latency antibiotics while avoiding digital per-vaginal (PV) examinations?","o":["Digital PV exams are done hourly in PPROM to stimulate labour","Dexamethasone accelerates cervical dilatation to 10 cm within 2 hours","Dexamethasone stops all uterine bleeding immediately","Antenatal corticosteroids accelerate foetal lung surfactant synthesis (reducing neonatal RDS, intraventricular haemorrhage, and NEC), latency antibiotics prolong pregnancy and prevent infection, and avoiding digital PV exams prevents ascending chorioamnionitis"],"a":3,"e":"Between 24 and 34 weeks gestation in threatened preterm birth or PPROM without chorioamnionitis, a single course of antenatal corticosteroids (IM betamethasone or dexamethasone) significantly boosts foetal Type II pneumocyte surfactant maturation—cutting neonatal mortality, Respiratory Distress Syndrome (RDS), intraventricular haemorrhage, and necrotizing enterocolitis. Sterile speculum assessment replaces digital PV examinations in PPROM because digital fingers push vaginal flora directly up into the open amniotic cavity, shortening latency and triggering chorioamnionitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01084","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Obstetrical Nursing","topic":"Postpartum Bladder Distension","q":"Six hours after a normal vaginal delivery with episiotomy, the nurse palpates the mother\u0027s uterine fundus 3 cm above the umbilicus, deviated far to the right of midline, and feels it softening with heavier lochia rubra. What is the most likely immediate cause and nursing intervention?","o":["A full, distended urinary bladder displacing the uterus upward and rightward, impairing uterine contraction; assist the mother to void immediately (or perform sterile straight catheterization if unable) and recheck fundal firmness","Normal physiological uterine involution","Deep vein thrombosis in the right calf","Twins overlooked after birth"],"a":0,"e":"Postpartum diuresis, perineal oedema/pain, and epidural/pudendal analgesia frequently cause urinary retention in the first 6–12 hours after delivery. An overfilled bladder pushes the uterus upward above the umbilicus and laterally (usually to the right), preventing myometrial fibres from contracting tightly and triggering secondary atonic postpartum haemorrhage. Emptying the bladder immediately restores midline firm involution.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01085","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Child Health Nursing","topic":"Medical Contraindications to Breastfeeding","q":"Which clinical situation represents a TRUE absolute contraindication to breastfeeding / feeding breastmilk, versus a condition where breastfeeding CAN safely proceed with appropriate neonatal prophylaxis?","o":["Caesarean delivery is a lifelong contraindication to breastfeeding","Classic infant galactosaemia (inability to metabolize galactose) or maternal active cytotoxic chemotherapy is a true contraindication; whereas maternal Hepatitis B positivity is NOT a contraindication once the newborn receives Hepatitis B vaccine + HBIG at birth","Maternal Hepatitis B surface antigen positivity is an absolute contraindication; classic infant galactosaemia allows exclusive breastfeeding","Maternal mild common cold is an absolute contraindication; active untreated maternal Ebola is safe"],"a":1,"e":"Very few true contraindications to breastfeeding exist: classic infant galactosaemia (risking liver failure, cataracts, E. coli sepsis from breastmilk lactose/galactose), active maternal cytotoxic chemotherapy/radiopharmaceuticals, active herpetic lesions directly on the breast, or untreated active smear-positive TB until \u003e= 2 weeks of ATT (expressed milk can be given if breast lesion-free). By contrast, maternal Hepatitis B (with birth HepB + HBIG), maternal common colds, and post-C-section recovery safely permit breastfeeding.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01086","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing I","topic":"Hookworm Infection Prevention","q":"In an agricultural village with high prevalence of iron-deficiency anaemia linked to hookworm (Ancylostoma duodenale / Necator americanus) infection, which primary prevention measure directly blocks entry of infective filariform larvae into human hosts?","o":["Avoiding cooked green vegetables","Applying topical insect repellent against Culex mosquitoes","Wearing closed protective footwear/chappals outdoors AND using sanitary latrines to eliminate open defecation in soil","Boiling drinking water only while walking barefoot in defecated fields"],"a":2,"e":"Hookworm ova passed in human faeces hatch in warm moist soil into infective third-stage filariform larvae, which penetrate intact skin of bare feet and migrate via bloodstream/lungs to the small intestine, causing chronic occult blood loss and severe iron-deficiency anaemia. Sanitary latrine use stops soil contamination while closed footwear blocks larval skin penetration, complemented by biannual albendazole deworming.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01087","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Contraceptive Efficacy Metric","q":"In family planning epidemiology, what does the \u0027Pearl Index\u0027 measure, and does a lower or higher Pearl Index indicate a more effective contraceptive method?","o":["Percentage of postpartum women attending ANC","Average birth weight of neonates in grams; higher is better","Number of clinic visits per year; higher means better efficacy","Number of unintended pregnancies per 100 woman-years of contraceptive exposure; a LOWER Pearl Index indicates greater contraceptive effectiveness"],"a":3,"e":"The Pearl Index quantifies contraceptive failure rate: Pearl Index = (Total accidental pregnancies × 1,200) ÷ (Total months of exposure among all users), expressed as failures per 100 woman-years. Highly effective methods like Cu-T 380A or implants have a Pearl Index \u003c 1 per 100 woman-years, whereas less reliable methods have higher Pearl Indices.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01088","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Levels of Measurement (NOIR)","q":"A nursing researcher records four patient variables: (1) ABO blood group; (2) Braden ulcer stage (I, II, III, IV); (3) body temperature in degrees Celsius; and (4) 24-hour urine output in millilitres. Which ordered sequence matches Nominal, Ordinal, Interval, and Ratio scales respectively?","o":["(1) Nominal, (2) Ordinal, (3) Interval, (4) Ratio","All four variables are Ratio scales","(1) Ordinal, (2) Nominal, (3) Ratio, (4) Interval","(1) Ratio, (2) Interval, (3) Ordinal, (4) Nominal"],"a":0,"e":"Stevens\u0027 four measurement levels determine valid statistical operations: Nominal classifies mutually exclusive categories without rank (ABO blood group); Ordinal orders ranked categories with unequal/non-metric intervals (ulcer stages I–IV); Interval uses equal numerical units without a true absolute zero (0°C does not mean absence of heat); Ratio possesses equal intervals AND a meaningful true zero allowing ratio statements (0 mL urine means complete absence of urine, and 2,000 mL is twice 1,000 mL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01089","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Ventrogluteal Intramuscular Landmarking","q":"At an injection-skills OSCE station, a nursing student demonstrates how to locate the ventrogluteal intramuscular injection site (preferred in adults to avoid the sciatic nerve and superior gluteal vessels) on the patient\u0027s left hip. Which anatomical landmarking is exact?","o":["Inject 2 cm directly below the umbilicus","Place the heel of the RIGHT hand over the left greater trochanter with thumb pointing toward the groin, index finger pointing toward the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS), and middle finger spread posteriorly along the iliac crest—injecting inside the V-triangle formed between index and middle fingers","Inject directly over the popliteal fossa behind the knee","Divide the buttock into quadrants and inject into the lower inner quadrant near the sacrum"],"a":1,"e":"The ventrogluteal site targets the gluteus medius and minimus away from major nerves and blood vessels. Using the opposite hand (right hand for left hip; left hand for right hip), place the palm heel on the greater trochanter, point thumb anteriorly toward the groin, point index finger toward the ASIS, and abduct the middle finger posteriorly along the iliac crest—the centre of the V-triangle is the safe 90° IM site.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01090","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Ethics, Legal Issues and Professionalism","topic":"Veracity and Fidelity","q":"A postoperative patient asks her primary nurse honestly whether her biopsy showed malignancy, and the nurse ensures the physician meets with her promptly to share truthful diagnostic results rather than fabricating a false cheerful assurance. Later, the nurse returns precisely at 3:00 PM as promised to assist with ambulation. Which pair of ethical principles does the nurse uphold?","o":["Paternalism and negligence","Justice and whistleblowing only","Veracity (duty to tell the truth without deception) and Fidelity (faithfulness to commitments and promises made to patients)","Non-maleficence and battery"],"a":2,"e":"Veracity obligates healthcare professionals to communicate truthfully and refrain from deception (supporting informed patient autonomy), while Fidelity obligates nurses to honour professional promises, maintain trust, and remain faithful to patient commitments (such as returning at an agreed time for pain relief or ambulation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01091","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing I (Nephrology \u0026 Urology)","topic":"Acute Pyelonephritis","q":"A 28-year-old woman presents with high fever (39.3°C), shaking chills, nausea/vomiting, dysuria, and sharp flank pain with marked costovertebral angle (CVA) tenderness on gentle fist percussion (acute pyelonephritis). What is the nurse\u0027s priority sequencing of diagnostic cultures and prescribed IV ceftriaxone?","o":["Give only oral cranberry juice without antibiotics","Administer IV ceftriaxone immediately first, then draw blood and urine cultures 6 hours later","Perform bladder irrigation with Betadine","Obtain prescribed urine culture and blood cultures FIRST, then administer the first dose of IV ceftriaxone promptly alongside IV hydration and antipyretics"],"a":3,"e":"High fever, rigors, flank pain, nausea/vomiting, and CVA tenderness distinguish upper-tract acute pyelonephritis from afebrile lower cystitis. Collecting sterile urine and blood cultures BEFORE the first antibiotic dose prevents false-negative sterilization of specimens while still enabling rapid empirical IV broad-spectrum antimicrobial initiation to prevent urosepsis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01092","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Adult Health Nursing II (Hepatology)","topic":"Diagnostic/Therapeutic Paracentesis \u0026 SBP","q":"A cirrhotic patient with tense ascites develops mild fever, diffuse abdominal tenderness, and worsening confusion, and is scheduled for diagnostic and large-volume (5 L) therapeutic paracentesis. Which pre-procedure safety check and post-procedure volume replacement prevent iatrogenic complications?","o":["Instruct the patient to void/empty the urinary bladder immediately before paracentesis (to prevent bladder perforation), measure abdominal girth/weight, and administer prescribed IV Albumin during/after large-volume (\u003e 4–5 L) drainage to prevent circulatory dysfunction","Place the patient supine flat during high-volume drainage without BP checks","Infuse 5 L of potassium chloride rapidly","Have the patient drink 2 litres of water and keep the urinary bladder completely full before needle insertion"],"a":0,"e":"Before abdominal paracentesis, emptying the urinary bladder lowers the dome away from the infraumbilical needle track, preventing bladder perforation. When removing large ascites volumes (\u003e= 4–5 L), intravascular fluid rapidly re-accumulates into the peritoneum causing post-paracentesis circulatory dysfunction and hepatorenal injury—prevented by IV albumin infusion. Ascitic PMN count \u003e= 250 cells/mm3 confirms Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP) requiring IV third-generation cephalosporin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01093","course":"B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Pharmacology II","topic":"Metronidazole Drug Interactions","q":"A patient prescribed oral metronidazole 400 mg three times daily for amoebic liver abscess/giardiasis should receive which essential medication safety counselling?","o":["Take double doses with warfarin without monitoring INR","Strictly avoid ALL alcohol during treatment and for at least 48–72 hours after the last dose (to prevent a disulfiram-like reaction of flushing, vomiting, and palpitations), and expect a harmless metallic taste or dark urine","Drink beer with each tablet to mask the bitter taste","Stop metronidazole after 1 day as soon as stool softens"],"a":1,"e":"Metronidazole can provoke an unpleasant disulfiram-like acetaldehyde reaction (facial flushing, severe nausea/vomiting, abdominal cramps, headache, tachycardia) if combined with alcohol during therapy or within 48–72 hours post-completion. Nurses also counsel patients on common benign metallic taste, harmless reddish-brown urine darkening, and potentiation of warfarin anticoagulation (requiring close INR monitoring).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01094","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilisation, Disinfection \u0026 OT Protocols","topic":"Steam Sterilisation Parameters \u0026 Biological Indicators","q":"In a hospital CSSD, standard gravity-displacement steam autoclaving is run at 121 C (15 psi) for 15-20 minutes (or 134 C for 3-4 min in prevacuum). Which biological indicator spore strip is placed inside the most challenging surgical pack to verify true sterilisation?","o":["Clostridium tetani vegetative cells","Staphylococcus aureus ATCC 25923","Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores","Bacillus atrophaeus (subtilis var. niger) spores"],"a":2,"e":"Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores are highly heat-resistant and serve as the gold-standard biological indicator for steam autoclaving and hydrogen peroxide plasma sterilisation. Bacillus atrophaeus spores are used for dry heat (hot air oven) and ethylene oxide (EtO) gas sterilisation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01095","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"CSSD \u0026 Sterilization Techniques","topic":"Pre-Vacuum Autoclave Testing","q":"At the start of the morning shift in the CSSD, the OT technologist runs a Bowie-Dick test pack in an empty pre-vacuum steam sterilizer at 134 C for 3.5 minutes. Upon opening the pack, the central area of the chemical indicator sheet remains pale/unconverted while the perimeter turns dark black. What does this result indicate?","o":["Excessive superheated steam drying out the outer edges","Successful air removal and adequate steam penetration","Sterilizer failure to kill Bacillus atrophaeus spores","Air entrapment or vacuum pump leak preventing central steam penetration"],"a":3,"e":"The Bowie-Dick test specifically evaluates the efficacy of the vacuum pump and chamber seal to remove air from porous loads in pre-vacuum steam autoclaves. Residual air pockets migrate to the center of the test pack, preventing steam contact and leaving the center unconverted, requiring maintenance before sterilizing surgical loads.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01096","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"OT Equipment \u0026 Instrumentation","topic":"Monopolar Diathermy Safety","q":"During an open laparotomy using monopolar electrocautery, the OT technologist places a split-pad dispersive (return) electrode on the patient\u0027s anterior thigh. Shortly after skin prep, the ESU Return Electrode Monitoring (REM) alarm triggers and cuts off output. Which scenario best explains why REM prevents a patient pad-site burn?","o":["REM detects elevated skin-to-pad impedance when partial pad detachment reduces contact area, preventing high current density","REM decreases generator voltage to zero whenever tissue desiccation occurs at the active pencil tip","REM senses leakage of 50 Hz alternating current from the surgical table ground","REM converts monopolar current automatically into bipolar current upon high impedance"],"a":0,"e":"Split (dual-section) dispersive electrodes allow the REM circuit to pass a small interrogation current between the two halves and continuously measure contact impedance. If prep solution pooling, hair, or movement causes partial detachment, reduced surface area raises current density and impedance rises, prompting REM to alarm and disable RF output.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01097","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"OT Environment \u0026 Infection Control","topic":"HVAC Parameters \u0026 BMW Rules 2016","q":"Per NABH OT standards and India\u0027s Biomedical Waste Management Rules 2016, which combination correctly describes conventional operating theatre air filtration/pressure and the disposal container for discarded surgical scalpel blades?","o":["Positive pressure with 5 ACH; red chlorinated plastic bag","Positive pressure with HEPA filtration (\u003e=20 ACH); puncture-proof white translucent container","Neutral pressure with UVGI only; blue puncture-proof cardboard box","Negative pressure with 10 ACH; yellow non-chlorinated plastic bag"],"a":1,"e":"Standard operating theatres require positive pressure relative to corridors (+2.5 to +10 Pa) with \u003e=20 total air changes per hour (ACH) through terminal HEPA filters (99.97% efficiency at 0.3 microns) to prevent airborne contamination. Under BMW Rules 2016 (India), sharp metal objects such as scalpel blades and needles go into puncture-proof, leak-proof, tamper-proof white translucent containers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01098","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Positioning \u0026 Patient Safety","topic":"Lithotomy \u0026 Trendelenburg Positioning","q":"While positioning a patient in standard high lithotomy with candy-cane/boot stirrups for an abdominoperineal resection, which peripheral nerve is MOST vulnerable to compression injury against the lateral aspect of the fibular head if the lateral calf rests tightly against the stirrup bar?","o":["Pudendal nerve","Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","Common peroneal (fibular) nerve","Obturator nerve"],"a":2,"e":"The common peroneal nerve winds superficially around the neck of the fibula on the lateral knee. In lithotomy positioning, external rotation or direct pressure of the lateral proximal calf against unpadded metal stirrup posts compresses the common peroneal nerve, producing postoperative foot drop and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01099","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Materials, Sutures \u0026 Instruments","topic":"Synthetic Absorbables \u0026 Needle Point Geometry","q":"During gastrointestinal anastomosis and skin closure, which combination accurately describes the absorption mechanism of synthetic absorbable sutures (such as Polyglactin 910 / Polydioxanone) and the needle point geometry preferred for bowel versus tough dermis?","o":["Enzymatic proteolysis; reverse-cutting needle for bowel, taper-point needle for skin","Oxidative dissolution; trocar-point needle for intestinal mucosa, taper-point needle for palmar fascia","Phagocytosis by neutrophils only; blunt-tip needle for skin, conventional cutting needle for blood vessels","Non-enzymatic hydrolysis (minimal tissue reaction); taper-point (round-bodied) needle for bowel, reverse-cutting needle for skin"],"a":3,"e":"Synthetic absorbable polymers (Polyglactin 910, Polyglycolic acid, Polydioxanone) undergo predictable non-enzymatic hydrolysis, provoking far less inflammatory reaction than natural catgut (which is degraded by leukocytic enzymatic proteolysis). Taper-point needles spread soft visceral tissue (bowel, myocardium, vessels) without slicing, whereas reverse-cutting needles (cutting edge on the outer curvature) resist tissue cutout in tough skin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01100","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Specialized OT Procedures \u0026 Equipment","topic":"Tourniquet Pressure \u0026 Deflation Physiology","q":"After 105 minutes of continuous femoral pneumatic tourniquet inflation (300 mmHg) during total knee arthroplasty, the surgeon instructs the OT team to deflate the tourniquet. Which physiological constellation characteristically occurs within the first 2-5 minutes after tourniquet release?","o":["Transient systemic hypotension, transient rise in EtCO2 (washout of ischemic CO2/lactate), hyperkalemia, and mild core hypothermia","Acute pulmonary edema due to sudden surge in left ventricular afterload","Immediate drop in arterial lactate and rise in core body temperature by 2 C","Marked systemic hypertension, metabolic alkalosis, and sudden drop in EtCO2"],"a":0,"e":"Tourniquet deflation restores perfusion to an ischemic, acidotic, cooled limb. Reactive hyperemia and sudden central venous washout of accumulated CO2, lactic acid, and potassium produce transient hypotension, an abrupt spike in EtCO2 (10-15 mmHg), transient metabolic acidosis/hyperkalemia, and a 0.5-1.0 C core temperature drop.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01101","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Minimally Invasive \u0026 Laparoscopic OT Technology","topic":"CO2 Insufflation Physiology","q":"Why is medical-grade Carbon Dioxide (CO2) selected over Oxygen or Nitrous Oxide for creating laparoscopic pneumoperitoneum, and what is the standard safe intra-abdominal pressure limit in healthy adults?","o":["CO2 prevents hypercapnia completely; maintain 20-25 mmHg","CO2 is non-combustible (safe with electrocautery) and highly soluble in blood (minimizing fatal gas embolism risk); maintain 12-15 mmHg","CO2 is insoluble in blood so it stays trapped in the abdomen; maintain 5 mmHg","CO2 supports electrocautery sparks safely; maintain 25-30 mmHg"],"a":1,"e":"CO2 neither supports combustion (unlike O2 or N2O, which can ignite bowel methane/hydrogen during diathermy) nor persists as persistent intravascular gas bubbles because of its high blood solubility and rapid pulmonary elimination. Standard adult pneumoperitoneum is maintained at 12-15 mmHg; pressures \u003e 15-20 mmHg compress the inferior vena cava, dropping preload/cardiac output and impairing diaphragmatic excursion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01102","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Decontamination \u0026 High-Level Disinfection","topic":"Flexible Endoscope Reprocessing","q":"Under the Spaulding classification of medical devices, a flexible colonoscope or bronchoscope that contacts intact mucous membranes without penetrating sterile tissue is categorized as what level of item, and what minimum microbiological standard is required after thorough enzymatic cleaning?","o":["Critical item; dry-heat oven at 170 C for 2 hours","Semi-critical item; 70% isopropyl alcohol wipe only without channel flushing","Semi-critical item; High-Level Disinfection (HLD) using e.g. 2% alkaline glutaraldehyde (20-45 min) or 0.55% ortho-phthalaldehyde (OPA, 12 min), followed by sterile/filtered water rinse and alcohol-assisted drying","Non-critical item; low-level wiping with quaternary ammonium for 1 minute"],"a":2,"e":"Per Spaulding\u0027s scheme, devices contacting intact mucous membranes or non-intact skin (e.g., flexible GI endoscopes, laryngoscope blades, respiratory circuits) are semi-critical items requiring at least High-Level Disinfection (eliminating all vegetative bacteria, mycobacteria, fungi, and viruses, though not necessarily high numbers of bacterial spores). Dry heat at 170 C destroys heat-labile fiberoptic endoscopes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01103","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Perioperative Surgical Safety \u0026 Governance","topic":"Surgical Count Reconciliation","q":"During the \u0027Sign Out\u0027 closing count after an exploratory laparotomy, the scrub and circulating OT technologists discover that one radiopaque abdominal laparotomy sponge is unaccounted for after two recounts of the instrument table, kick buckets, and linen bags. What is the mandatory next action?","o":["Wait until the patient wakes up in PACU and order a CT scan the next morning","Document \u0027count unresolved\u0027 in the register and allow immediate skin closure to minimize anesthesia time","Open a new sterile pack to replace the missing sponge in the final tally","Notify the operating surgeon immediately, pause wound closure, re-explore the peritoneal cavity, and obtain an intraoperative plain radiograph of the surgical site before the patient leaves the OT"],"a":3,"e":"Retention of a foreign body (gossypiboma) is a preventable \u0027never event.\u0027 Under WHO Surgical Safety Checklist and AORN/NABH count policies, any unresolved sponge, needle, or instrument count mandates immediate notification of the surgeon, halting closure, systematic cavity re-exploration, room/trash search, and an intraoperative X-ray (since surgical sponges carry radiopaque barium markers) prior to leaving the operating theatre.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01104","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"OT Electrical Safety \u0026 Biomedical Engineering","topic":"Line Isolation Monitor (LIM) \u0026 Microshock","q":"In an operating theatre equipped with an ungrounded isolated power system and Line Isolation Monitor (LIM), why can a tiny leakage current of just 100 microamperes (0.1 mA) cause fatal ventricular fibrillation in a patient with a central venous saline catheter or epicardial pacing wires, and how does the LIM respond when a single first fault occurs?","o":["Direct myocardial contact bypasses high skin resistance (microshock threshold ~10-100 uA triggers VF); LIM alarms when total potential fault current exceeds 2-5 mA WITHOUT shutting off power on the first fault so life-saving surgery can continue safely","Macroshock threshold is 1 uA across dry intact skin; LIM increases voltage to 440 V","Isolation transformers convert AC power into 12 V DC battery output","100 uA causes superficial skin burns only; LIM cuts off all OT power immediately on the first fault"],"a":0,"e":"Intact dry skin provides high resistance (~10,000+ ohms), requiring ~100 mA externally (macroshock) for VF. However, an indwelling epicardial pacing lead or fluid-filled central catheter bypasses skin resistance directly to the myocardium, where tiny leakage currents of 50-100 uA can induce ventricular fibrillation (microshock). OT isolation transformers prevent a single ground fault from completing a shock circuit; the LIM alarms at 2-5 mA fault hazard while preserving uninterrupted surgical power until the faulty device is unplugged.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01105","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"CSSD Low-Temperature Sterilization","topic":"Packaging Compatibility \u0026 Cycle Aborts","q":"A CSSD technician loads a delicate rigid arthroscopic camera lens into a low-temperature Hydrogen Peroxide (H2O2) Gas Plasma sterilizer (STERRAD, \u003c 50 C) wrapped in standard woven cotton linen and cellulose paper peel-pouches with a paper count sheet inside. The sterilizer immediately aborts the cycle. Why did the cycle abort, and what packaging material is required?","o":["H2O2 requires \u003e 134 C steam to penetrate cotton; use metal canisters only","Cellulose (cotton, paper, linen) absorbs hydrogen peroxide vapor, depleting the sterilant and causing cycle abort; use non-woven polypropylene wraps and Tyvek-Mylar pouches exclusively","Arthroscopes must be sterilized only with boiling water for 10 minutes","Tyvek pouches block plasma radiofrequency waves; wrap in wet gauze"],"a":1,"e":"Hydrogen peroxide gas plasma sterilizes heat- and moisture-labile instruments rapidly (\u003c 75 min, no toxic aeration needed) using vaporized H2O2 energized into low-temperature plasma, monitored by Geobacillus stearothermophilus biological indicators. Cellulose materials (linen, cotton, paper wrappers, cardboard, gauze) rapidly absorb H2O2 and water, preventing adequate chamber concentration and triggering cycle cancellation—requiring synthetic Tyvek pouches and polypropylene wraps.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01106","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"OT Crisis \u0026 Risk Management","topic":"Fire Triad \u0026 Airway Fire Algorithm","q":"During an open tracheostomy with monopolar cautery and supplemental 100% oxygen delivered via the endotracheal tube, flames suddenly erupt from the airway stoma. Per the ASA Operating Room Fire Algorithm, what is the IMMEDIATE first simultaneous sequence of actions?","o":["Pour alcohol over the drapes and increase oxygen flow to blow out the flame","Apply a dry surgical sponge directly over the burning ETT cuff while keeping O2 at 10 L/min","Simultaneously stop the flow of all airway gases (disconnect breathing circuit from ETT), remove the burning endotracheal tube immediately, pour sterile saline/water into the airway to extinguish embers, and remove flaming drapes","Leave the ETT in place and increase sevoflurane to 8% before calling fire services"],"a":2,"e":"In an airway fire, the oxidizer-enriched gas stream (O2/N2O) acts as a blowtorch inside the trachea. The ASA Airway Fire Protocol demands immediate simultaneous cessation of airway gas flow (turn off O2/N2O and disconnect circuit), prompt removal of the burning ETT (to stop melting plastic/blowtorch injury), extinguishing residual embers with sterile saline, and re-establishing ventilation by mask/reintubation once the fire is out.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01107","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Asepsis, Scrubbing \u0026 Sterile Technique","topic":"Closed-Gloving Technique","q":"Immediately after drying hands and forearms on a sterile towel and donning a sterile wrap-around surgical gown, why does the scrub technologist keep both hands retracted INSIDE the knitted stockinette gown cuffs while performing the \u0027closed-gloving\u0027 technique?","o":["Keeping hands inside cuffs warms the latex to prevent allergies","Because stockinette cuffs are considered sterile indefinitely even when exposed to air","Closed-gloving is only for replacing a torn glove mid-surgery","Because bare skin must NEVER touch the exterior sterile surface of the gown or gloves; keeping fingers inside the gown cuffs allows sterile-to-sterile glove manipulation until the glove completely covers the non-sterile porous stockinette cuff"],"a":3,"e":"In closed-gloving (used for initial sterile gowning), hands remain tucked inside the gown sleeves so the outside of the sterile glove is handled only through the sterile gown fabric—preventing bare-skin contact with the exterior glove and completely enclosing the porous stockinette cuff (which rapidly loses barrier sterility once touched or dampened). Open-gloving is used for sterile procedures without a gown or glove replacement (assisted gloving).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01108","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Instrumentation \u0026 Specialty Devices","topic":"Fogarty Embolectomy Catheter \u0026 Suction Tips","q":"When preparing a Fogarty arterial embolectomy catheter on the sterile back table for an acute femoral artery thromboembolism, what medium MUST be used to test and inflate the delicate latex balloon (and never air), and which multi-fenestrated suction tip prevents bowel wall entrapment during deep peritoneal lavage?","o":["Inflate balloon strictly with sterile normal saline (eliminating air embolism risk if the balloon ruptures intravascularly); Poole abdominal sump suction tip","Inflate balloon with 10% formalin; Frazier neurosurgical suction tip","Inflate balloon with pure oxygen; cell-saver high-vacuum cannula","Inflate balloon with pressurized room air; Yankauer tonsil suction tip"],"a":0,"e":"A Fogarty embolectomy balloon must ALWAYS be tested and inflated with sterile isotonic saline (using the exact manufacturer-specified volume syringe) because an intravascular balloon rupture filled with air risks distal arterial air embolism. For deep abdominal/pelvic fluid evacuation, a Poole suction tip features a perforated outer sheath surrounding the inner lumen so omentum/bowel serosa cannot plug the tip or suffer suction trauma.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01109","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Perioperative Thermoregulation \u0026 Fluid Technology","topic":"Inadvertent Perioperative Hypothermia \u0026 Rapid Blood Warmers","q":"During a major pelvic tumor resection requiring 8 units of 4 C stored PRBCs and 4 L of crystalloids, the patient\u0027s core esophageal temperature drops to 34.1 C, accompanied by diffuse nonsurgical microvascular oozing. Why does rapid un-warmed transfusion worsen surgical bleeding, and what is the safe upper temperature setpoint of a dedicated blood warmer to avoid thermal RBC hemolysis?","o":["Cold blood prevents wound infection; warm blood only in a standard microwave oven","Hypothermia (\u003c 35 C) impairs platelet thromboxane release and slows coagulation cascade enzyme kinetics (forming the \u0027lethal triad\u0027 with acidosis and coagulopathy); dedicated countercurrent blood warmers safely warm blood to 37-41 C (never \u003e 42-43 C, which causes thermal hemolysis)","Hypothermia accelerates clotting factor enzymes; blood warmers heat PRBCs to 55 C","Hypothermia causes hypercoagulability; warm blood to 48 C"],"a":1,"e":"Every 1 C drop in core temperature impairs platelet adhesion/aggregation and reduces coagulation protease activity by ~10%, driving refractory microvascular bleeding (\u0027lethal triad\u0027: hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy) plus higher surgical site infection rates. Dedicated blood warmers with air-trap detectors safely warm blood to 37-41 C; exceeding ~42-43 C (or microwaving/hot-water bath immersion) causes acute thermal RBC hemolysis and hyperkalemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01110","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Energy \u0026 Hemostasis Technology","topic":"Bipolar Electrosurgery vs Ultrasonic Harmonic Scalpel","q":"How do Bipolar Electrocautery and an Ultrasonic Harmonic Scalpel (55.5 kHz) differ from Monopolar Diathermy regarding dispersive return pads and electrical current passage through the patient\u0027s torso?","o":["Both bipolar and ultrasonic scalpels require a thigh dispersive return pad","Bipolar cautery passes RF current ONLY between the two forceps tips (requiring NO dispersive pad and minimizing pacemaker EMI), while the Ultrasonic Harmonic Scalpel uses high-frequency mechanical blade vibration (55.5 kHz) with ZERO electrical current passing through the patient","Ultrasonic scalpels pass 500 V DC current to the patient table","Bipolar cautery causes 10 cm of lateral thermal spread compared with monopolar"],"a":1,"e":"In bipolar electrosurgery, one tine serves as active electrode and the other as return electrode, confining current strictly to tissue grasped between the tips—eliminating distant return pads and minimizing pacemaker electromagnetic interference. Ultrasonic shears convert electrical energy via piezoelectric transducers into 55,500 Hz mechanical blade vibration, coapting vessels via protein hydrogen-bond denaturation at lower temperatures (~60-100 C) with zero electrical current entering the patient.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01111","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Preoperative Skin Preparation \u0026 OT Fire Prevention","topic":"Chlorhexidine-Alcohol Pooling \u0026 Vapor Ignition","q":"Prior to an emergency laparotomy, the surgical team preps the abdomen with 2% chlorhexidine gluconate in 70% isopropyl alcohol (CHG-IPA), leaving wet solution pooled in the umbilicus and soaking into the cloth sheet beneath the patient\u0027s flanks. Why MUST the OT team remove soaked linens and wait at least 3 minutes for complete evaporation before applying adhesive drapes and activating monopolar cautery?","o":["CHG requires 30 minutes of wet pooling to kill Gram-positive skin flora","Wet alcohol neutralizes local anaesthesia injections","Trapped flammable alcohol vapors beneath impervious drapes can ignite instantaneously from a single electrosurgical spark, causing catastrophic OR flash burns and chemical pooling dermatitis","Alcohol becomes explosive only after it has completely evaporated"],"a":2,"e":"70% isopropyl alcohol is highly flammable and burns with an almost invisible pale-blue flame. If wet CHG-IPA pools in body hollows (umbilicus, groin) or saturates under-drapes, enclosed flammable vapor pockets ignite violently upon ESU activation, while prolonged wet skin contact also causes chemical burns. Waiting \u003e= 3 minutes (longer in hair-bearing areas) until completely dry and discarding soaked towels prevents surgical fires.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01112","course":"B.Sc Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Specialized OT Safety \u0026 ENT Surgery","topic":"CO2 Laser Airway Surgery Safety","q":"During microlaryngeal surgery using a Carbon Dioxide (CO2, 10,600 nm) laser, which OT safety bundle accurately protects the patient\u0027s airway, eyes, and staff from laser ignition and plume hazards?","o":["Standard PVC endotracheal tube inflated with air at FiO2 1.0 (with 50% N2O)","Turn off all OT overhead lights and use cloth paper drapes soaked in alcohol","Use clear unshielded reading glasses for staff since 10,600 nm is invisible","Laser-resistant metallic/shielded ETT with cuff inflated using sterile saline + methylene blue indicator, inspired FiO2 \u003c 30% (air-O2 blend, NO N2O), wet saline eye pads on patient + wavelength-specific safety goggles for staff, and dedicated ULPA smoke evacuator"],"a":3,"e":"A CO2 laser beam strikes tissue water superficially (risking corneal injury, so clear/polycarbonate CO2-rated goggles protect staff and wet eye pads protect patient) and readily perforates standard PVC/silicone ETTs. Laser-safe metallic/wrapped tubes filled with dyed saline (to douse sparks and alert cuff puncture), minimal FiO2 (\u003c 30%, strictly avoiding oxidizer N2O), wet towels around the surgical site, and active smoke evacuation (capturing aerosolized HPV/viral DNA) are mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01113","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Clinical Optometry \u0026 Refraction","topic":"Working Distance Neutralisation","q":"An optometrist performs static retinoscopy at a working distance of 67 cm (0.67 m) and achieves neutralisation in both principal meridians using a +3.50 D spherical lens. What is the patient’s net refractive error after deducting the working distance lens?","o":["+2.00 D sphere (Hypermetropia)","-2.00 D sphere (Myopia)","+5.00 D sphere","+3.50 D sphere"],"a":0,"e":"Working distance dioptric power = 1 / 0.67 m = +1.50 D. Net refractive error = Gross Retinoscopy Lens - Working Distance Power = +3.50 D - (+1.50 D) = +2.00 D sphere.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01114","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Clinical Refraction \u0026 Optometric Optics","topic":"Streak Retinoscopy","q":"A streak retinoscopy is performed at a working distance of 67 cm (working distance lens power = +1.50 D). Using loose trial lenses without a working-distance lens in place, the optometrist neutralizes the vertical streak (testing the horizontal meridian) with +3.00 D and the horizontal streak (testing the vertical meridian) with +1.50 D. What is the net refractive correction in minus-cylinder form?","o":["+3.00 DS / -1.50 DC x 90","+1.50 DS / -1.50 DC x 180","+1.50 DS / -1.50 DC x 90","0.00 DS / -1.50 DC x 180"],"a":1,"e":"Subtracting the +1.50 D working distance gives net horizontal meridian power = +3.00 - 1.50 = +1.50 D, and net vertical meridian power = +1.50 - 1.50 = 0.00 D. In plus-cylinder form this is 0.00 DS / +1.50 DC x 90 (axis 90 gives 0.00 D vertically and +1.50 D horizontally). Transposing to minus-cylinder form yields +1.50 DS / -1.50 DC x 180.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01115","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Contact Lens Practice","topic":"Corneal Hypoxia","q":"A 22-year-old university student wearing conventional low-water-content HEMA soft contact lenses for 14 hours daily presents with mild evening blur and haloes. Slit-lamp biomicroscopy reveals epithelial microcysts, faint vertical stromal striae, and 2.5 mm of superior limbal vascularization encroaching onto the clear cornea. What is the primary pathophysiologic mechanism and best lens-based management?","o":["Mechanical lid wiper epitheliopathy; increase lens diameter and steepen base curve","Hyperosmotic tear evaporation; switch to a high-water-content HEMA lens with thicker center","Chronic corneal hypoxia from low Dk/t; temporarily cease wear and refit with a high-Dk/t silicone hydrogel lens","Acanthamoeba keratitis; switch immediately to daily disposable HEMA lenses"],"a":2,"e":"Epithelial microcysts, vertical Descemet striae (indicating \u003e=5% stromal edema), and limbal neovascularization \u003e1.5-2.0 mm are classic hallmarks of chronic corneal hypoxia caused by low oxygen transmissibility (Dk/t). Management requires halting wear until resolution, then refitting into a high-Dk/t silicone hydrogel material (meeting Holden-Mertz criteria) with reduced wear time.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01116","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Optometric Instrumentation \u0026 Clinical Procedures","topic":"Goldmann Applanation Tonometry","q":"During Goldmann applanation tonometry (GAT), the optometrist observes overly wide, thick semicircular fluorescein mires before aligning their inner margins. How will these thick mires affect the measured intraocular pressure (IOP), and how should high regular corneal astigmatism (\u003e3.00 D) be compensated?","o":["Thick mires overestimate IOP; align prism white 0 mark with the minus cylinder axis","Thick mires underestimate IOP; align prism red mark with the steeper meridian","Thick mires have no effect on IOP; perform applanation without fluorescein","Thick mires overestimate IOP; align prism red mark with the minus cylinder axis (or average horizontal and vertical readings)"],"a":3,"e":"When excessive fluorescein creates thick mires, the inner edges overlap prematurely before reaching the true 3.06 mm applanation diameter, falsely overestimating IOP (excessively thin mires underestimate IOP). For corneal astigmatism \u003e 3.00 D, aligning the red line on the prism holder with the minus cylinder axis (43 degrees from the flat meridian) or averaging 0-degree and 90-degree measurements eliminates astigmatic error.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01117","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Binocular Vision \u0026 Orthoptics","topic":"Vergence Dysfunctions","q":"A 19-year-old engineering student complains of asthenopia, frontal headaches, and intermittent horizontal diplopia after 20 minutes of reading. Orthoptic evaluation reveals distance orthophoria, near exophoria of 14 prism diopters, receded Near Point of Convergence (NPC break 16 cm / recovery 22 cm), reduced positive fusional vergence at near, and a low AC/A ratio. What is the diagnosis and gold-standard first-line management?","o":["Convergence insufficiency; prescribe office-based vergence/accommodative vision therapy with home reinforcement (or base-in prism if refractory)","Convergence excess; prescribe +2.50 D bifocal add for near","Accommodative spasm; prescribe cycloplegic atropine drops daily","Divergence excess; prescribe minus overcorrection lenses"],"a":0,"e":"Convergence Insufficiency (CI) is characterized by near exophoria significantly greater than distance phoria, receded NPC (\u003e 10 cm), low near positive fusional vergence (failing Sheard\u0027s criterion), and low AC/A ratio. Per CITT (Convergence Insufficiency Treatment Trial) evidence, office-based vergence/accommodative therapy with home reinforcement is the most effective treatment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01118","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Ocular Disease \u0026 Glaucoma Diagnostics","topic":"Humphrey Visual Field (HVF) Interpretation","q":"While reviewing a 24-2 SITA-Standard Humphrey Visual Field printout, the optometrist notes False Positives = 28%, abnormally high foveal sensitivity (44 dB), and \u0027white scotomas\u0027 on the grayscale. Why is this field unreliable, and which true glaucomatous defect classically respects the horizontal raphe?","o":["Uncorrected presbyopia; central cecocentral scotoma","Patient was \u0027trigger-happy\u0027 (pressing button without stimulus), artificially inflating sensitivity and masking real defects; Roenne\u0027s nasal step and arcuate (Bjerrum) scotoma","High fixation losses from trial lens rim artifact; homonymous quadrantanopia","Patient was sleepy (\u0027cloverleaf\u0027 pattern); bitemporal hemianopia"],"a":1,"e":"False-positive errors \u003e 15% occur when a \u0027trigger-happy\u0027 patient responds in the absence of a projected stimulus, producing unrealistically high decibel sensitivities (\u0027white scotomas\u0027) and falsely normal Mean Deviation (MD). Because retinal nerve fibers arch from the optic disc without crossing the horizontal temporal raphe, early glaucomatous defects (nasal step, paracentral/arcuate Bjerrum scotoma) strictly respect the horizontal midline.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01119","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Ophthalmic Optics \u0026 Dispensing","topic":"Prentice\u0027s Rule","q":"A myopic patient with an interpupillary distance (PD) of 60 mm receives spectacles containing -6.00 DS lenses OU, but the optical centers were erroneously edged at 70 mm (each optical center decentered 5 mm temporally relative to the visual axis). Using Prentice\u0027s rule (P = c x F), what unwanted prismatic effect is induced in EACH eye?","o":["3.00 prism diopters Base-Out (BO) in each eye","1.20 prism diopters Base-Out (BO) in each eye","3.00 prism diopters Base-In (BI) in each eye","30.0 prism diopters Base-In (BI) in each eye"],"a":2,"e":"By Prentice\u0027s rule, induced prism P = c (decentration in cm) x F (lens power in diopters) = 0.5 cm x 6.00 D = 3.00 prism diopters per eye (6.00 PD total). Because a minus (concave) lens acts as two prisms apex-to-apex at the optical center, displacing the optical center temporally places the visual axis on the nasal side of the optical center, where thickness increases nasally—inducing Base-In (BI) prism.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01120","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Pediatric Optometry \u0026 Amblyopia","topic":"Anisometropic Amblyopia","q":"A 5-year-old boy referred from school vision screening has cycloplegic refraction of OD +4.75 DS and OS +0.50 DS, orthophoria on cover test, normal fundi, and best-corrected visual acuity of OD 6/24 (improving to 6/12 with isolated single optotypes) and OS 6/6. What explains the better single-letter acuity, and what is the primary initial management?","o":["Congenital optic atrophy; immediate strabismus surgery","Malingering; reassure parents with no glasses needed","Deprivation amblyopia; prescribe tinted sunglasses","Crowding phenomenon typical of anisometropic amblyopia; prescribe full optical correction first (optical adaptation), followed by part-time patching or atropine penalization of the left eye if residual amblyopia persists"],"a":3,"e":"Uncorrected unilateral hyperopic anisometropia (\u003e 1.50 D) causes chronic unilateral defocus in the right eye, producing anisometropic amblyopia without strabismus. Amblyopic eyes characteristically score better on isolated single optotypes than crowded linear charts (\u0027crowding phenomenon\u0027). Per PEDIG trials, full refractive correction alone resolves or improves acuity over 12-18 weeks, followed by 2-6 hours/day occlusion or weekend atropine of the fellow eye.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01121","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Low Vision Rehabilitation","topic":"Kestenbaum\u0027s Rule","q":"A 74-year-old patient with dry age-related macular degeneration has best-corrected distance visual acuity of 6/60 (20/200) and wants to read standard 1M newsprint. Applying Kestenbaum\u0027s rule (reciprocal of the Snellen acuity fraction) to estimate the starting high-plus spectacle reading add, what dioptric add power and focal working distance are predicted?","o":["+10.00 D add at 10 cm working distance","+2.50 D add at 40 cm working distance","+20.00 D add at 5 cm working distance","+5.00 D add at 20 cm working distance"],"a":0,"e":"Kestenbaum\u0027s rule estimates the dioptric add required to read 1M (equivalent to 6/15 or 20/50 at 40 cm) by taking the reciprocal of the distance Snellen fraction: 1 / (6/60) = 60/6 = +10.00 D (or 200/20 = +10.00 D). A +10.00 D lens has a focal length of 1 / 10 = 0.10 m = 10 cm, providing 2.5x relative distance magnification compared with a 25 cm reference distance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01122","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Ocular Instrumentation","topic":"Keratometry Principles","q":"In a standard manual Bausch \u0026 Lomb keratometer measuring the central ~3.0 mm anterior corneal curvature, what is the optical purpose of the internal doubling prism, and what standardized fictitious refractive index is used to convert corneal radius of curvature (r) into corneal dioptric power?","o":["Magnifies the retinal fovea 15x; refractive index 1.490","Renders mire alignment independent of small involuntary patient eye movements (saccades); standardized refractive index 1.3375","Eliminates chromatic aberration; refractive index 1.523","Measures posterior corneal curvature exclusively; refractive index 1.376"],"a":1,"e":"Because the cornea acts as a convex first-surface mirror constantly moving with microsaccades, a keratometer uses a doubling prism (Wollaston/bi-prism) so both reflected mires move identically together—allowing precise mire-edge contact alignment regardless of small eye movements. Power is calculated via F = (n - 1) / r using the calibrated keratometric index n = 1.3375 (accounting roughly for posterior corneal negative power).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01123","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Ocular Neuro-Anatomy \u0026 Clinical Examination","topic":"Swinging Flashlight Test","q":"During a swinging flashlight test in a dimly lit room, shining a bright penlight into the left eye (OS) causes brisk constriction of both pupils. When the light is swung rapidly to the right eye (OD), BOTH pupils paradoxically dilate; swinging back to OS causes brisk re-constriction. Which lesion is consistent with this finding, and which condition does NOT cause an RAPD?","o":["Left CN III efferent palsy; optic neuritis does not cause RAPD","Right Horner syndrome; central retinal artery occlusion does not cause RAPD","Right optic nerve lesion (e.g., retrobulbar optic neuritis, ischemic optic neuropathy, or severe right retinal detachment) producing a right Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect (Marcus Gunn pupil); dense unilateral cataract or refractive error does NOT cause RAPD","Bilateral symmetric glaucoma; unilateral optic nerve compression never causes RAPD"],"a":2,"e":"Paradoxical bilateral pupillary dilation when light moves from the intact left eye to the right eye diagnoses a right Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect (RAPD / Marcus Gunn pupil), indicating asymmetric afferent conduction impairment in the right optic nerve or extensive retina. Crucially, media opacities like dense mature cataract or high refractive error scatter light onto wide areas of healthy retina and do NOT produce an RAPD.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01124","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Contact Lens Optics \u0026 Prescribing","topic":"Spectacle-to-Corneal Plane Power Conversion","q":"A patient has a manifest spectacle prescription of -10.00 DS OU measured at a vertex distance of 12 mm (0.012 m). Using the exact vertex distance formula [Fc = Fs / (1 - d x Fs)], what spherical contact lens power is required at the corneal plane?","o":["-11.36 D","-10.00 D","-7.50 D","-8.93 D"],"a":3,"e":"When moving a lens closer to the eye (d = 0.012 m), Fc = Fs / (1 - d x Fs) = -10.00 / [1 - (0.012 x -10.00)] = -10.00 / (1 + 0.12) = -10.00 / 1.12 = -8.93 D (commonly ordered as -9.00 DS). High myopes require LESS minus power in contact lenses than spectacles, whereas high hyperopes require MORE plus power at the corneal plane.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01125","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Visual Psychophysics \u0026 Clinical Diagnostics","topic":"Ishihara Plates vs Farnsworth Panel D-15","q":"A patient starting ethambutol therapy for tuberculosis requires baseline and monthly color vision monitoring to detect early optic neuropathy (which often begins with blue-yellow / tritan or red-green defects). Why is the Farnsworth Panel D-15 (or Lanthony desaturated D-15) superior to relying solely on standard Ishihara pseudoisochromatic plates?","o":["Ishihara plates screen ONLY for congenital red-green (protan/deutan) deficiencies and miss blue-yellow (tritan) defects, whereas Farnsworth D-15 arrangement testing detects and classifies protan, deutan, AND acquired tritan defects","Ishihara plates detect only blue-yellow tritanopia and miss red-green defects","Farnsworth D-15 measures intraocular pressure simultaneously","Ishihara plates require full cycloplegia before administration"],"a":0,"e":"Ishihara pseudoisochromatic plates are specifically engineered to screen congenital X-linked red-green (protan and deutan) deficiencies and lack tritan plates. Acquired optic nerve and retinal diseases (ethambutol toxicity, glaucoma, diabetic retinopathy) frequently produce blue-yellow (tritan) confusion axes (Kollner\u0027s rule), requiring Farnsworth D-15, FM 100-Hue, or HRR plates.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01126","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Ocular Surface \u0026 Tear Film Diagnostics","topic":"Tear Break-Up Time (TBUT) vs Schirmer Test","q":"A 38-year-old IT professional with burning eyes and fluctuating blur after prolonged computer use has a normal Schirmer I test (16 mm wetting in 5 minutes), but Fluorescein Tear Break-Up Time (TBUT) is 4 seconds bilaterally (normal \u003e= 10 s), and slit-lamp exam shows capped, turbid meibomian gland orifices along the lid margins. Which dry eye subtype is confirmed?","o":["Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Evaporative Dry Eye secondary to Meibomian Gland Dysfunction (MGD) causing lipid-layer tear film instability","Pure aqueous tear deficiency (Sjogren syndrome)","Congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction"],"a":1,"e":"Under TFOS DEWS II criteria, a rapid TBUT (\u003c 10 seconds) paired with normal aqueous tear volume (Schirmer \u003e= 10 mm) and inspissated meibomian glands diagnoses Evaporative Dry Eye due to MGD—where deficient meibum lipids allow rapid aqueous evaporation between blinks (exacerbated by reduced blink rate during digital screen work). Management includes warm compresses, lid hygiene, lipid-containing tears, and blink training.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01127","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Geometrical \u0026 Visual Optics","topic":"Spherical Equivalent (SE) Calculation","q":"A patient\u0027s manifest refraction is +2.00 DS / -3.00 DC x 180. What is the Spherical Equivalent (SE) of this prescription, and which optical feature of the Conoid of Sturm does the Spherical Equivalent lens place onto the retina?","o":["+0.50 DS; places the posterior vertical focal line onto the retina","-1.00 DS; places the anterior horizontal focal line onto the retina","+0.50 DS; places the Circle of Least Confusion onto the retina","+3.50 DS; eliminates all astigmatism completely"],"a":2,"e":"Spherical Equivalent SE = Sphere + (Cylinder / 2) = +2.00 + (-3.00 / 2) = +2.00 - 1.50 = +0.50 DS. Dioptrically, the SE represents the arithmetic mean power of the two principal meridians (+2.00 D horizontally and -1.00 D vertically), positioning the Circle of Least Confusion—the narrowest, least-distorted waist of the Conoid of Sturm—directly on the retina.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01128","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Posterior Segment \u0026 Retinal Imaging","topic":"NPDR vs Proliferative DR (PDR) \u0026 DME","q":"During dilated fundus examination and Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT) of a 55-year-old diabetic patient, which retinal finding definitively transitions the staging from Non-Proliferative Diabetic Retinopathy (NPDR) to Proliferative Diabetic Retinopathy (PDR), and what OCT sign confirms center-involved Diabetic Macular Edema (DME)?","o":["Arteriovenous nicking defines PDR; drusen beneath the RPE confirm DME","Microaneurysms and dot-blot hemorrhages alone define PDR; optic disc cupping on OCT","Hard exudates and cotton-wool spots alone define PDR; choroidal thinning on OCT","Neovascularization at the optic disc (NVD) or elsewhere on the retina (NVE) defines PDR; intraretinal/subretinal fluid cysts with increased central subfield retinal thickness on macular OCT confirm DME"],"a":3,"e":"Microaneurysms, dot-blot hemorrhages, hard exudates, cotton-wool spots (nerve fiber layer infarcts), venous beading, and IRMA characterize NPDR (scored via the ETDRS 4-2-1 rule for severe NPDR). The appearance of fragile abnormal new vessels—NVD or NVE (or vitreous/preretinal hemorrhage)—defines Proliferative Diabetic Retinopathy (PDR). Macular OCT quantifies intraretinal hyporeflective cysts and central subfield thickness to diagnose DME.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01129","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Anterior Segment \u0026 Glaucoma Screening","topic":"Van Herick Technique \u0026 Gonioscopy","q":"Before instilling tropicamide/phenylephrine for a dilated fundus exam in a 62-year-old hyperopic woman (+4.50 DS), the optometrist performs the Van Herick slit-lamp technique (60-degree optic section at the temporal limbus). The dark aqueous space between the posterior cornea and anterior iris measures \u003c 1/4 of the corneal thickness (Van Herick Grade 1). What is the mandatory clinical action?","o":["WITHHOLD mydriatic dilation and refer/perform dark-room indentation gonioscopy to rule out a critically narrow occludable angle and prevent precipitating acute angle-closure crisis","Proceed immediately with 10% phenylephrine dilation since IOP is currently 16 mmHg","Repeat Van Herick at a 0-degree coaxial angle","Instill pilocarpine 4% immediately for routine cataract screening"],"a":0,"e":"The Van Herick test estimates peripheral anterior chamber depth relative to peripheral corneal thickness using a narrow 60-degree beam just inside the temporal limbus. Grade 1 (\u003c 1/4 corneal thickness) indicates a critically narrow, potentially occludable iridocorneal angle where pharmacologic pupillary dilation (especially mid-dilation pupillary block) can precipitate sight-threatening Acute Primary Angle-Closure (APAC)—mandating gonioscopy before dilation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01130","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Ocular Biometry \u0026 Cataract Co-Management","topic":"SRK Formula Error Sensitivity","q":"Using the classic SRK relationship [IOL Power P = A - 2.5(L) - 0.9(K)], if a handheld contact A-scan ultrasound probe inadvertently indents the cornea, artificially shortening the measured Axial Length (L) by 1.0 mm (e.g., reading 22.5 mm instead of true 23.5 mm), approximately how much postoperative refractive error (surprise) will result?","o":["~0.90 D surprise","~2.50 D to 3.00 D myopic refractive surprise (IOL power overestimated by 2.50 D)","~0.25 D surprise","~10.0 D hyperopic surprise"],"a":1,"e":"In P = A - 2.5L - 0.9K, axial length (L) carries a 2.5x multiplier! Corneal compression during contact A-scan underestimates true L by 1.0 mm, causing the formula to calculate an IOL power that is ~2.50 D (clinically ~2.50-3.00 D at the spectacle plane) TOO STRONG, leaving the pseudophakic patient unexpectedly myopic postoperatively. Optical biometry (IOLMaster/Lenstar) or immersion A-scan eliminates corneal indentation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01131","course":"B.Sc Optometry","subject":"Contact Lens Practice","topic":"Tear Lens Optics \u0026 Fluorescein Patterns","q":"A patient has flat-K = 42.00 D, steep-K = 42.00 D, and spectacle refraction -4.00 DS (vertex-negligible). If the optometrist fits a spherical Rigid Gas Permeable (RGP) lens steeper than K at a base curve of 42.50 D (0.50 D steeper), what fluorescein pattern appears centrally and what final RGP lens power is required by the SAM-FAP rule?","o":["Central dark touch with bright edge pooling; final RGP power -3.50 DS","Central pooling; final RGP power -3.50 DS","Central bright green fluorescein pooling (apical clearance) with mid-peripheral bearing; SAM (Steep Add Minus) creates a +0.50 D tear lens so final RGP power must be -4.50 DS","Uniform zero-fluorescein alignment; final RGP power -4.00 DS"],"a":2,"e":"Fitting an RGP steeper than the cornea (42.50 D on 42.00 D cornea) vaults the central cornea, accumulating fluorescein centrally (bright green pooling) with mid-peripheral ring touch. The trapped convex tear meniscus adds +0.50 D of refractive power; per SAM-FAP (\u0027Steep Add Minus, Flat Add Plus\u0027), adding -0.50 D to the -4.00 DS refraction yields an RGP power of -4.50 DS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01132","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Bypass (CPB) Physiology","topic":"Goal-Directed Perfusion","q":"An adult patient undergoing coronary artery bypass grafting under normothermic cardiopulmonary bypass (37 C) has a Body Surface Area (BSA) of 1.8 m2. If the targeted perfusion Cardiac Index (CI) is 2.4 L/min/m2, what arterial pump flow rate should the perfusionist set, and what indexed oxygen delivery (DO2i) threshold protects against CPB-associated acute kidney injury?","o":["4.80 L/min; DO2i \u003e= 150 mL/min/m2","3.60 L/min; DO2i \u003e= 180 mL/min/m2","2.40 L/min; DO2i \u003e= 400 mL/min/m2","4.32 L/min; DO2i \u003e= 280 mL/min/m2"],"a":3,"e":"Target CPB pump flow = BSA x Target Cardiac Index = 1.8 m2 x 2.4 L/min/m2 = 4.32 L/min. Under goal-directed perfusion guidelines, maintaining indexed oxygen delivery (DO2i = pump flow index x CaO2 x 10) \u003e= 270-280 mL/min/m2 prevents critical tissue dysoxia and significantly lowers the risk of postoperative acute kidney injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01133","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Anticoagulation \u0026 Coagulation Management in CPB","topic":"Heparin Resistance","q":"Prior to aortic cannulation for CPB, a patient who had been on an intravenous unfractionated heparin infusion for 6 days receives 400 IU/kg of unfractionated heparin. Three minutes later, the Activated Clotting Time (ACT) is 305 seconds (target \u003e= 480 seconds), and an additional 100 IU/kg bolus only raises ACT to 320 seconds. What is the most likely mechanism and definitive treatment?","o":["Acquired Antithrombin III (AT-III) deficiency causing heparin resistance; administer AT-III concentrate or Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP)","Protamine cross-reactivity; administer 50 mg protamine sulfate before bypass","Platelet factor 4 depletion; start CPB immediately because ACT \u003e 300 s is adequate","Excessive hemodilution; decrease prime volume in the venous reservoir"],"a":0,"e":"Unfractionated heparin requires Antithrombin III (AT-III) as a cofactor to inhibit thrombin and Factor Xa. Prolonged preoperative heparin infusions deplete circulating AT-III, producing heparin resistance (failure to reach ACT \u003e= 480 s despite \u003e= 400-500 IU/kg heparin). Administering recombinant/pooled AT-III concentrate or FFP restores AT-III levels and rapidly achieves therapeutic ACT.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01134","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"CPB Hardware \u0026 Extracorporeal Circuits","topic":"Gas Exchange Control on CPB","q":"During stable normothermic CPB using a microporous polypropylene hollow-fiber membrane oxygenator, an inline arterial blood gas reveals PaO2 = 210 mmHg and PaCO2 = 54 mmHg (alpha-stat). Which adjustment on the oxygenator gas blender specifically lowers PaCO2 without changing pump flow or PaO2?","o":["Decrease the blender FiO2 from 0.70 to 0.40","Increase the sweep gas flow rate (L/min)","Increase the arterial roller pump occlusion","Decrease the sweep gas flow rate (L/min)"],"a":1,"e":"In a hollow-fiber membrane oxygenator, CO2 elimination is driven by the concentration gradient across the membrane and is directly controlled by the sweep gas flow rate (analogous to alveolar minute ventilation). Increasing sweep gas flow flushes CO2 from the gas phase and lowers arterial PaCO2, whereas FiO2 on the blender controls PaO2.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01135","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Myocardial Protection \u0026 Cardioplegia","topic":"Aortic Insufficiency \u0026 Retrograde Cardioplegia","q":"During aortic valve replacement in a patient with severe aortic regurgitation (AR), why is standard aortic root antegrade cardioplegia ineffective alone, and what maximum coronary sinus perfusion pressure must the perfusionist NOT exceed when delivering retrograde cardioplegia?","o":["Antegrade cardioplegia causes immediate coronary artery spasm; keep retrograde pressure at 120 mmHg","Severe AR prevents aortic cross-clamping; retrograde cardioplegia is delivered via the pulmonary artery at 60 mmHg","Antegrade cardioplegia regurgitates across the incompetent aortic valve causing left ventricular distension and poor coronary flow; keep retrograde coronary sinus pressure \u003c= 40 mmHg (never \u003e 50 mmHg)","Antegrade cardioplegia bypasses the left main ostium completely; maintain retrograde pressure at 80-100 mmHg"],"a":2,"e":"In severe aortic regurgitation, pressurized cardioplegia infused into the aortic root leaks backward through the incompetent valve, failing to build coronary ostial perfusion pressure while dangerously distending the arrested left ventricle. Direct handheld coronary ostial cannulation or retrograde delivery into the coronary sinus (strictly limiting line pressure to 30-40 mmHg to prevent coronary sinus rupture or intramyocardial hemorrhage) ensures safe arrest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01136","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"CPB Hemodilution \u0026 Prime Calculations","topic":"Resultant On-Bypass Hematocrit","q":"A 70 kg adult patient (estimated blood volume = 70 mL/kg) has a pre-bypass hematocrit (Hct) of 40%. If the CPB circuit is primed entirely with 1,500 mL of clear crystalloid solution (0% RBCs), what is the predicted resultant hematocrit immediately after complete mixing on bypass (ignoring urine output and surgical blood loss)?","o":["34.0%","27.2%","24.5%","30.6%"],"a":3,"e":"Patient Blood Volume (PBV) = 70 kg x 70 mL/kg = 4,900 mL. Red cell volume = PBV x Hct = 4,900 x 0.40 = 1,960 mL. Total diluted circulating volume = PBV + Prime Volume = 4,900 + 1,500 = 6,400 mL. Predicted on-CPB Hct = (1,960 / 6,400) x 100 = 30.625% (~30.6%).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01137","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation (ECMO)","topic":"Differential Hypoxemia (Harlequin Syndrome)","q":"A patient supported on peripheral femoral Veno-Arterial (VA) ECMO for cardiogenic shock and severe ARDS shows recovering left ventricular ejection on echocardiography. However, the right radial arterial line ABG reveals PaO2 = 44 mmHg (SpO2 78%), while the lower extremities have SpO2 99%. What causes this \u0027Harlequin (North-South) syndrome,\u0027 and how is it corrected?","o":["Native LV ejects poorly oxygenated blood from diseased lungs into the proximal aorta/arch, competing against retrograde oxygenated femoral ECMO blood; optimize ventilator FiO2/PEEP, or convert to V-AV ECMO / central-axillary cannulation","Oxygenator membrane failure; increase ECMO sweep gas FiO2 to 1.0 only","Right femoral arterial cannula thrombosis; clamp the venous drainage line","Excessive ECMO pump flow causing cerebral hyperoxia; decrease ECMO RPM immediately"],"a":0,"e":"In peripheral femoral VA-ECMO, as native LV function recovers while pulmonary gas exchange remains poor, the LV ejects desaturated blood into the aortic root, perfusing the coronaries and right innominate artery (causing cerebral/upper-body hypoxia) while retrograde ECMO flow oxygenates the descending aorta. Management includes maximizing ventilator recruitment/FiO2, adding an internal jugular venous return limb (V-AV ECMO) to pre-oxygenate pulmonary blood, or relocating arterial return to the axillary/central aorta.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01138","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Hypothermic CPB \u0026 Acid-Base Physiology","topic":"Alpha-Stat vs pH-Stat","q":"During hypothermic cardiopulmonary bypass (28 C) in an adult undergoing valve surgery, how does alpha-stat blood gas management differ physiologically from pH-stat management?","o":["Alpha-stat adds CO2 to maintain temperature-corrected pH at 7.40, causing luxury cerebral vasodilation","Alpha-stat maintains blood gas pH 7.40 and PaCO2 40 mmHg uncorrected (as measured at 37 C), preserving cerebral autoregulation and intracellular enzyme ionization","Alpha-stat is strictly contraindicated in adult coronary surgery","pH-stat lowers PaCO2 to 15 mmHg to induce severe cerebral vasoconstriction"],"a":1,"e":"As blood cools, CO2 solubility rises and water neutral pH rises (~0.015 pH/C). Alpha-stat keeps the sample at pH 7.40 / PaCO2 40 mmHg uncorrected at 37 C (allowing natural alkaline shift in vivo), which preserves the imidazole alpha-ionization state of histidine, normal coupling of cerebral blood flow to metabolic demand, and cerebral autoregulation (minimizing microembolic load in adults). pH-stat adds CO2 to keep in vivo pH 7.40, increasing cerebral blood flow for homogeneous pediatric cooling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01139","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"CPB Crisis Management \u0026 Perfusion Safety","topic":"Massive Arterial Air Embolism","q":"Despite safety alarms, a large bolus of air passes through the arterial pump head and enters the ascending aortic cannula during normothermic CPB (massive arterial air embolism). What is the immediate perfusionist and surgical rescue sequence?","o":["Administer protamine sulfate immediately while keeping the arterial pump running","Elevate the patient\u0027s head 45 degrees upright to trap air in the carotid sinus","Stop the arterial pump immediately, clamp arterial and venous lines, place the patient in steep Trendelenburg (head-down), aspirate air from the aortic root/cannula, cool the patient, and perform temporary retrograde cerebral perfusion via the superior vena cava (SVC) followed by 100% O2 / hyperbaric therapy","Increase arterial pump flow to 6 L/min to push air through the capillary bed quickly"],"a":2,"e":"Massive arterial air embolism is an acute surgical emergency. Stopping the pump and clamping lines halts further air delivery; steep Trendelenburg prevents buoyancy-driven cerebral ascent; venting the aortic root and administering cold retrograde cerebral perfusion (RCP) via the SVC flushes air bubbles backward out of cerebral arteries via the aortic arch vent; hypothermia + 100% O2/hyperbaric oxygen shrink bubble volume and preserve neurons.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01140","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Pharmacological Management on CPB","topic":"Protamine Dosing \u0026 Adverse Reactions","q":"Following successful weaning from CPB, titration indicates 22,000 IU of active circulating heparin remains in the patient. Using the standard reversal ratio of 1.0 mg protamine sulfate per 100 IU of remaining active heparin, what total protamine dose is required, and how is a Type I hypotensive reaction prevented during administration?","o":["2,200 mg; administer directly into the left atrium","22 mg; administer as a rapid 5-second arterial bolus","110 mg; administer simultaneously with a 500 mg heparin flush","220 mg; administer slowly via peripheral/central venous infusion over 10-15 minutes to avoid histamine-mediated systemic vasodilation"],"a":3,"e":"At 1.0 mg protamine per 100 IU active heparin, 22,000 / 100 = 220 mg protamine sulfate is required (avoiding gross excess protamine, which itself acts as a mild anticoagulant). Rapid bolus injection triggers mast-cell histamine release causing sudden peripheral vasodilation and hypotension (Type I reaction), prevented by slow controlled infusion over 10-15 minutes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01141","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Ultrafiltration \u0026 Blood Conservation on CPB","topic":"Arteriovenous MUF Technique","q":"Immediately after weaning a 6 kg infant from cardiopulmonary bypass following congenital heart repair, the perfusionist initiates Arteriovenous Modified Ultrafiltration (MUF) for 12 minutes prior to protamine administration. How does MUF operate, and what clinical benefits does it achieve?","o":["Draws blood from the aortic cannula through a hemoconcentrator and returns warmed, concentrated blood directly to the right atrium—raising hematocrit, clearing excess interstitial edema and inflammatory cytokines, and improving ventricular/pulmonary compliance","Infuses unwashed crystalloid cardioplegia into the cerebral circulation","Is performed ONLY before aortic cross-clamping prior to starting CPB","Draws blood from the IVC and discards RBCs to induce severe hemodilution"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike Conventional Ultrafiltration (CUF, performed during CPB), Modified Ultrafiltration (MUF) occurs immediately after termination of CPB while cannulae remain in place (before protamine). Blood flows from the aortic line via a roller pump through the ultrafilter (removing plasma water and inflammatory mediators) and returns warmed to the right atrium—rapidly raising hematocrit, decreasing myocardial/pulmonary edema, and reducing postoperative blood transfusions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01142","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"CPB Pump Mechanics \u0026 Hardware","topic":"Occlusion Calibration \u0026 Centrifugal Backflow Hazard","q":"Compare the primary mechanical safety risks of a positive-displacement Roller Pump versus a non-occlusive Centrifugal Pump in the arterial CPB position:","o":["Roller pumps stop pumping automatically if distal arterial line pressure rises to 500 mmHg","Over-occluded roller pumps cause severe RBC hemolysis, tubing spallation, and line rupture against a clamped outlet; non-occlusive centrifugal pumps are afterload-sensitive and allow rapid RETROGRADE patient exsanguination or air aspiration if RPM drops to zero without clamping the arterial line","Roller pumps allow retrograde exsanguination if stopped; centrifugal pumps generate unlimited positive pressure against a clamped arterial line","Centrifugal pumps require manual fluid-drop occlusion calibration before every case"],"a":1,"e":"A roller pump is positive-displacement: over-occlusion crushes RBCs (hemolysis) and sheds PVC/silicone micro-particles (spallation), while pumping against an obstructed arterial line builds catastrophic pressure until tubing bursts. A non-occlusive centrifugal pump creates kinetic vortex pressure without tubing compression (low hemolysis, self-limiting pressure against occlusion), but because its pathway is open, stopping RPM without immediately clamping the arterial line causes rapid retrograde siphonage from the aorta back into the venous reservoir!","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01143","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Perioperative Autologous Blood Salvage","topic":"Cell Salvage Contraindications","q":"During a complex redo cardiac-thoracic and abdominal operation using an intraoperative Cell Saver (autologous blood recovery system), in which scenario MUST the perfusionist immediately turn OFF cell-saver suction and avoid reinfusing the salvaged blood?","o":["Low hematocrit \u003c 25% in a Jehovah\u0027s Witness patient accepting continuous cell salvage","High-volume clean orthopedic hip arthroplasty bleeding","Gross enteric/fecal bacterial contamination of the surgical field OR active application of microfibrillar collagen (Avitene), topical thrombin, or povidone-iodine irrigants into the pooled blood","Clean mediastinal bleeding after protamine administration when heparinized cardiotomy suction is off"],"a":2,"e":"Cell salvage washing removes plasma, free hemoglobin, heparin, and fat, returning washed autologous PRBCs. However, washing does NOT sterilize bacteria or fully eliminate microfibrillar collagen (Avitene) fibers, topical bovine thrombin, povidone-iodine, polymethylmethacrylate monomer, or hypotonic water—reinfusion risks septic shock, disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), or hemolysis. (Clean post-protamine mediastinal bleeding can safely be aspirated into the heparinized cell-saver reservoir.)","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01144","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Special Anticoagulation on CPB","topic":"Bivalirudin Anticoagulation for CPB","q":"A patient with acute Type II Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT, platelets 42,000/uL, positive anti-PF4/heparin assay) requires urgent coronary artery bypass grafting on CPB using intravenous Bivalirudin instead of unfractionated heparin. Which critical CPB circuit management rule MUST the perfusionist follow during bivalirudin bypass because bivalirudin is cleaved proteolytically by thrombin in stagnant blood?","o":["Reverse bivalirudin immediately post-CPB with protamine sulfate 1 mg per 100 units","Maintain ACT \u003c 150 seconds throughout CPB","Administer low-molecular-weight heparin (enoxaparin) simultaneously as backup","Eliminate all stagnant blood zones (clamp/flush unused lines, recirculate cardioplegia/reservoir volume continuously, and flush salvaged/graft blood with citrate or avoid pooling stagnant blood in the cardiotomy) because localized thrombin cleavage consumes bivalirudin and causes unexpected clot formation in static areas"],"a":3,"e":"Bivalirudin is a direct thrombin inhibitor (~25 min half-life) cleared ~80% by proteolytic cleavage by thrombin itself and ~20% renally, with NO reversal agent (protamine does NOT reverse bivalirudin!). Whenever blood sits stagnant (e.g., pooled pericardial blood, static venous reservoir side-ports, or clamped bypass grafts), local thrombin rapidly cleaves and exhausts bivalirudin, leading to focal thrombosis inside static cavities—requiring continuous recirculation and citrate-anticoagulated cell salvage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01145","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Extracorporeal Life Support (VV-ECMO)","topic":"VV-ECMO Access Recirculation","q":"A patient on femoral-to-internal-jugular Veno-Venous (VV) ECMO for severe ARDS remains hypoxemic (arterial SaO2 79%) despite 5.0 L/min ECMO flow and sweep FiO2 1.0, while the PRE-oxygenator venous drainage blood turns bright red (SvO2 92%). What mechanical problem is occurring, and how is it corrected?","o":["VV-ECMO recirculation—newly oxygenated return blood is immediately aspirated back into the venous drainage cannula due to close tip proximity or excessive pump speed; increase distance between drainage and return cannula tips (or use dual-lumen Avalon cannula) and modestly reduce excessive ECMO flow if appropriate","Harlequin differential hypoxemia; convert to peripheral femoral VA-ECMO","Oxygenator membrane thrombosis; decrease ECMO flow to 0.5 L/min","Cardiac tamponade; clamp the oxygenator sweep gas"],"a":0,"e":"In VV-ECMO, if the return cannula tip sits too close to the drainage cannula ports (or pump RPM creates excessive suction), freshly oxygenated blood bypasses the right ventricle and loops straight back into the drainage limb (\u0027recirculation\u0027). The telltale sign is bright-red, high-saturation blood entering the PRE-oxygenator drainage line (SvO2 \u003e 80-85%) paired with low patient arterial SaO2—corrected by pulling the cannulae farther apart (\u003e= 10-15 cm) or slightly lowering pump RPM.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01146","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"CPB Thermal Management \u0026 Neuroprotection","topic":"Preventing Cerebral Hyperthermia \u0026 Gaseous Microemboli","q":"During rewarming on CPB from 28 C back toward normothermia, why do STS/SCA/AmSECT perfusion guidelines mandate keeping the arterial outlet blood temperature \u003c= 37.0 C, rewarming slowly (\u003c= 0.5 C/min), and restricting the arterial-to-venous / water-to-blood gradient?","o":["Slow rewarming induces severe metabolic acidosis and hyperkalemia","Excessive arterial blood temperature (\u003e 37.0-37.5 C) causes cerebral hyperthermia exacerbating ischemic neuronal injury, while steep temperature gradients (\u003e 4-10 C) decrease gas solubility and precipitate dissolved N2/O2 out of solution as dangerous GASEOUS MICROEMBOLI","Rapid warming to 40 C accelerates hemoglobin oxygen affinity and cools the brain","Arterial blood temperature must reach 42 C to denature residual heparin"],"a":1,"e":"Warm blood holds less dissolved gas than cold blood (Henry\u0027s law). Rapid rewarming with an excessive heater-cooler water-to-blood gradient forces dissolved gas out of solution as gaseous microemboli in the arterial line/brain. Furthermore, because arterial outlet temperature tracks perfused cerebral temperature closely (often warmer than bladder/rectal temperature!), exceeding 37.0 C causes cerebral hyperthermia and worse postoperative neurocognitive deficits.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01147","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Bypass Instrumentation","topic":"Hollow-Fiber Membrane Oxygenators","q":"In a modern microporous polypropylene hollow-fiber membrane oxygenator used for routine adult cardiopulmonary bypass, how are blood and sweep gas arranged, and which parameter independently regulates arterial PaCO2?","o":["Sweep gas flows inside the hollow fibers while blood flows outside; increasing sweep gas flow rate lowers PaCO2","Blood flows inside the hollow fibers while sweep gas flows outside; increasing FiO2 lowers PaCO2","Both blood and sweep gas mix directly inside the fibers; increasing pump RPM lowers PaCO2","Sweep gas flows outside the fibers; increasing blood temperature lowers PaCO2 independently of sweep flow"],"a":0,"e":"Modern hollow-fiber oxygenators pass sweep gas through the lumen of thousands of microporous polypropylene fibers while venous blood flows extraluminally around the fibers (maximizing mixing and minimizing pressure drop). Because CO2 diffusion across the membrane is rapid and flow-limited by the concentration gradient, PaCO2 is controlled by the sweep gas flow rate, whereas PaO2 is controlled primarily by blender FiO2.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01148","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Clinical Perfusion and Anticoagulation","topic":"Heparin Resistance","q":"A 62-year-old man with unstable angina who received an unfractionated heparin infusion for 5 days preoperatively undergoes redo CABG. After receiving 400 IU/kg of IV unfractionated heparin, his Activated Clotting Time (ACT) rises only from 128 s to 290 s; an additional 200 IU/kg raises ACT to 310 s (target \u003e= 480 s). What is the most appropriate next step before going on bypass?","o":["Administer protamine sulfate 50 mg to reverse heparin tachyphylaxis and restart","Proceed immediately onto CPB because 600 IU/kg of heparin is sufficient regardless of ACT","Administer Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP) or Antithrombin III (AT-III) concentrate to restore cofactor levels","Give tranexamic acid 2 g IV bolus to prolong the kaolin ACT"],"a":2,"e":"Heparin exerts its anticoagulant effect by binding Antithrombin III (AT-III) and accelerating thrombin/Factor Xa inhibition 1000-fold. Prolonged preoperative heparin therapy depletes circulating AT-III, causing heparin resistance (ACT \u003c 400–480 s despite high heparin doses). Administering 2 units of FFP or recombinant/purified AT-III concentrate restores AT-III and rapidly raises ACT into the safe CPB range.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01149","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Perfusion Physiology and Calculations","topic":"Dilutional Hematocrit on CPB","q":"A 60-kg adult female has an estimated blood volume (EBV) of 65 mL/kg and a pre-bypass hematocrit of 36%. The CPB circuit is primed with 1,300 mL of crystalloid solution (zero red cells). Assuming no blood loss or transfusion prior to initiation, what will her calculated resultant hematocrit be immediately after mixing on CPB?","o":["22.5%","31.5%","18.0%","27.0%"],"a":3,"e":"First compute Patient EBV = 60 kg × 65 mL/kg = 3,900 mL. Red cell volume (RCV) = 3,900 mL × 0.36 = 1,404 mL. Total circulating volume on CPB = EBV + Prime = 3,900 + 1,300 = 5,200 mL. Resultant Hct = (1,404 / 5,200) × 100 = 27.0%, which remains above the critical transfusion threshold (typically \u003c21–24%).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01150","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Myocardial Protection and Cardioplegia","topic":"Electromechanical Arrest Mechanisms","q":"How does the elevated extracellular potassium concentration (typically 15–25 mEq/L) in cardioplegia solutions produce rapid diastolic arrest of the myocardium?","o":["It shifts the resting membrane potential to a less negative value (~ -50 mV), inactivating fast voltage-gated sodium channels","It hyperpolarizes the sarcolemma to -120 mV, preventing calcium-induced calcium release","It chelates extracellular magnesium to halt actin-myosin cross-bridge cycling in systole","It directly blocks beta-1 adrenergic receptors and depletes intracellular ATP stores"],"a":0,"e":"According to the Nernst equation, raising extracellular K+ depolarizes the myocyte resting membrane potential from approximately -90 mV toward -50 mV. At this depolarized potential, fast voltage-gated Na+ channels remain in the inactivated state, preventing action potential propagation and arresting the heart in diastole (minimizing ATP consumption). In Del Nido cardioplegia, lidocaine and magnesium further reduce Na+ and Ca2+ influx.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01151","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Pediatric and Specialized Perfusion","topic":"Acid-Base Management Strategies","q":"A 3-month-old infant with transposition of the great arteries undergoes cooling to 18°C for Deep Hypothermic Circulatory Arrest (DHCA). During cooling, the perfusionist adds CO2 to the sweep gas to maintain temperature-corrected arterial pH at 7.40 and PaCO2 at 40 mmHg. Which strategy is being used and what is its primary rationale in pediatric DHCA?","o":["Alpha-stat management; preserves cerebral autoregulation and prevents luxury perfusion","pH-stat management; induces cerebral vasodilation, promoting faster and more homogeneous brain cooling","pH-stat management; causes cerebral vasoconstriction to minimize microembolic load","Alpha-stat management; shifts the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve rightward via alkalosis"],"a":1,"e":"As blood cools, CO2 solubility rises and uncorrected PaCO2 drops. pH-stat management adds exogenous CO2 to keep temperature-corrected pH 7.40 and PaCO2 40 mmHg, producing hypercapnic cerebral vasodilation, counteracting cold-induced leftward shift of the oxyhemoglobin curve, and achieving uniform brain cooling in infants prior to DHCA (often transitioning to alpha-stat before arrest or in adults to limit embolic load).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01152","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"CPB Crisis Management and Safety","topic":"Massive Arterial Air Embolism","q":"During normothermic CPB for mitral valve replacement, the venous reservoir empties and a bolus of macroscopic air passes past the arterial filter into the aortic cannula. What is the immediate sequence of actions by the perfusionist and surgeon?","o":["Leave the arterial pump running while aspirating air from the venous oxygenator inlet","Administer protamine immediately and discontinue anticoagulation while keeping the patient upright","Immediately stop the arterial pump, clamp arterial and venous lines, place the patient in steep Trendelenburg, and initiate retrograde cerebral perfusion via the SVC","Increase arterial pump flow to flush air quickly through the cerebral capillaries into the venous reservoir"],"a":2,"e":"Massive arterial air embolism is a catastrophic perfusion emergency. The perfusionist must immediately stop the arterial pump and clamp the arterial and venous lines to prevent further air entry, communicate with the team, place the patient in steep Trendelenburg (head-down) to trap buoyant air away from the cerebral circulation, de-air the circuit, and collaborate with the surgeon to perform retrograde cerebral perfusion (RCP) via the SVC with aortic root venting, cooling, 100% FiO2, and consideration of hyperbaric oxygen therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01153","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Mechanical Circulatory Support","topic":"Balloon Timing and Waveform Analysis","q":"While troubleshooting an Intra-Aortic Balloon Pump (IABP) set to 1:2 assist ratio on the arterial pressure trace, the perfusionist observes that the assisted end-diastolic pressure is HIGHER than the unassisted end-diastolic pressure, and the subsequent assisted systolic peak is reduced. Which timing error is occurring and how should it be corrected?","o":["Early deflation; delay deflation slightly closer to the R wave","Late inflation; advance inflation to coincide with the dicrotic notch","Early inflation; delay inflation until the dicrotic notch","Late deflation; advance deflation earlier so it completes immediately before the onset of systole"],"a":3,"e":"Optimal IABP deflation occurs immediately prior to ventricular ejection (just before the upstroke of systole), creating a \u0027dip\u0027 where assisted aortic end-diastolic pressure is lower than unassisted end-diastolic pressure (afterload reduction). Late deflation leaves the balloon partially inflated as the aortic valve opens, raising end-diastolic pressure above unassisted levels, obstructing LV ejection, and increasing myocardial oxygen demand.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01154","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"CPB Circuit Safety \u0026 Filtration","topic":"Arterial Line Filter (ALF) \u0026 Purge Line","q":"Where is the 20-40 micron Arterial Line Filter (ALF) positioned in a standard CPB circuit, and why does its top dome incorporate a small purge line connected back to the venous/cardiotomy reservoir?","o":["Positioned in the arterial line DISTAL to the arterial pump and membrane oxygenator as the final safeguard against gaseous microemboli and particulate debris; the purge line continuously vents trapped microbubbles safely back to the filtered venous reservoir","Positioned inside the sweep-gas inlet tubing to filter nitrogen","Positioned exclusively on the cardioplegia waste line","Positioned upstream of the venous reservoir; purge line injects air into the patient aorta"],"a":0,"e":"The 20-40 um screen Arterial Line Filter sits downstream of both the arterial pump and membrane oxygenator (either standalone or integrated into the oxygenator housing) to capture microbubbles, platelet aggregates, spallation fragments, and calcific debris before blood reaches the ascending aorta. Its top purge port (with a one-way check valve) vents buoyant air microbubbles continuously or intermittently back to the venous cardiotomy reservoir.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01155","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Cardioplegia Delivery Systems","topic":"4:1 Blood-to-Crystalloid Cardioplegia Concentration","q":"A perfusionist runs a 4:1 blood-to-crystalloid cardioplegia system (4 parts patient oxygenated blood at K+ = 4.0 mEq/L mixed with 1 part concentrated crystalloid cardioplegia solution containing K+ = 104.0 mEq/L). What is the final delivered potassium (K+) concentration entering the coronary arteries?","o":["54.0 mEq/L","24.0 mEq/L","12.0 mEq/L","36.0 mEq/L"],"a":1,"e":"In a 4:1 ratio (total 5 parts: 80% blood + 20% crystalloid), Final [K+] = (0.80 x Blood [K+]) + (0.20 x Crystalloid [K+]) = (0.80 x 4.0) + (0.20 x 104.0) = 3.2 + 20.8 = 24.0 mEq/L—ideal for rapid depolarizing diastolic electromechanical cardiac arrest (typical induction target 20-30 mEq/L).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01156","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Advanced Mechanical Circulatory Support (VA-ECMO)","topic":"LV Distension \u0026 Aortic Valve Non-Opening","q":"A patient on peripheral femoral VA-ECMO (flow 4.5 L/min) for fulminant myocarditis has an arterial line pulse pressure \u003c 5 mmHg (flat waveform), rising PCWP (28 mmHg), pink frothy pulmonary edema, and echocardiography showing a dilated, akinetic LV with a closed aortic valve and swirling \u0027spontaneous echo contrast\u0027 (thrombus risk). What hemodynamic mechanism causes LV distension on VA-ECMO, and how is it relieved?","o":["Excessive preload depletion; increase ECMO flow to 7.0 L/min","Retrograde arterial ECMO flow elevates aortic root afterload above the weak LV\u0027s systolic ejection capacity while bronchial/Thebesian venous return continues filling the LV; relieve via inotropes, careful ECMO flow reduction, IABP, or active LV venting (Impella \u0027ECMELLA\u0027, atrial septostomy, or surgical LA/LV vent)","Right ventricular hypercontractility; clamp the ECMO arterial return cannula","Hypovolemia; infuse 3 L albumin into the left ventricle"],"a":1,"e":"Unlike surgical CPB with an open LV vent, peripheral VA-ECMO pumps blood backward up the aorta, dramatically increasing LV afterload. A severely stunned LV cannot overcome aortic pressure to open the aortic valve (pulse pressure \u003c 10 mmHg), yet residual pulmonary/Thebesian blood keeps filling the LV—raising LVEDP/PCWP, causing hemorrhagic pulmonary edema and LV stasis thrombosis. Mechanical LV unloading (Impella, IABP, atrial septostomy, or LA/PA vent) restores decompression.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01157","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Bypass Instrumentation","topic":"Roller Pump Occlusion Setting","q":"During pre-bypass setup of a twin-roller arterial pump, the perfusionist performs the vertical fluid-column drop test (75 cm / 30 inches saline column in 1/2-inch tubing) to set occlusion. What is the target drop rate, and what complications result from over-occluding versus under-occluding the rollers?","o":["Target a rapid drop of 50 cm/min; over-occlusion stops the centrifugal vortex, whereas under-occlusion triggers protamine anaphylaxis","Target zero drop (100% tight lock); over-occlusion improves platelet preservation, whereas under-occlusion causes air embolism","Target a slow drop of ~1–2.5 cm/min (\u0027just non-occlusive\u0027); over-occlusion causes severe mechanical hemolysis and tubing spallation, whereas under-occlusion causes inaccurate forward flow and retrograde regurgitation","Target 10 cm/sec drop; occlusion has no effect on hemolysis or forward flow accuracy"],"a":2,"e":"Standard arterial roller pump calibration sets the rollers \u0027just non-occlusive\u0027 so a 75-cm vertical fluid column drops slowly at approximately 1–2.5 cm/min (or 1 cm in 30–60 s). Tight over-occlusion crushes erythrocytes (raising plasma free hemoglobin), accelerates PVC/silicone tubing fatigue, and sheds plastic micro-particles (spallation). Gross under-occlusion permits retrograde slip across the pump head, causing overestimated flow display and aortic-to-reservoir regurgitation if the pump slows.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01158","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Clinical Perfusion and Anticoagulation","topic":"Catastrophic Protamine Reactions","q":"Two minutes after slow initiation of IV protamine following successful weaning from CPB, a patient with a history of daily NPH insulin use develops precipitous systemic hypotension (BP 55/30 mmHg), severe bronchospasm, massive right ventricular distension, and pulmonary artery pressures rising from 28/14 to 68/38 mmHg. Which protamine reaction is occurring and what is the immediate response?","o":["Heparin rebound; administer 30,000 IU additional heparin without stopping protamine","Normal expected protamine effect; double the protamine infusion rate to finish reversal faster","Type I mild transient histamine vasodilation; administer high-dose nitroglycerin and ignore PA pressures","Type III catastrophic pulmonary vasoconstriction (thromboxane A2/complement-mediated); stop protamine immediately, give 100% O2, support RV/systemic perfusion with epinephrine/vasopressors/milrinone/inhaled NO, and re-heparinize to resume CPB if refractory"],"a":3,"e":"Catastrophic protamine reaction (Type III) involves heparin-protamine complexes activating complement and releasing thromboxane A2, producing acute pulmonary arterial hypertension, RV failure, bronchospasm, and systemic collapse (risk increased with NPH insulin, fish allergy, or prior vasectomy). Protamine must be stopped immediately; the team ventilates with 100% O2, treats RV failure/vasoplegia (epinephrine, norepinephrine, milrinone, inhaled nitric oxide), and re-heparinizes to crash back onto CPB if hemodynamic collapse persists.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01159","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Perfusion Physiology and Calculations","topic":"Indexed Oxygen Delivery (DO2i)","q":"During normothermic CPB for a patient with a Body Surface Area (BSA) of 1.80 m², arterial pump flow is set at a Cardiac Index of 2.4 L/min/m² (4.32 L/min). Arterial blood gas shows Hb = 9.0 g/dL, SaO2 = 100% (1.00), and PaO2 = 200 mmHg. Using CaO2 = (1.34 × Hb × SaO2) + (0.003 × PaO2) and DO2i = CI × CaO2 × 10, what is the patient\u0027s indexed oxygen delivery (DO2i)?","o":["303.8 mL/min/m² (above the critical ~270–280 mL/min/m² threshold)","180.5 mL/min/m² (critically below threshold)","546.9 mL/min/m² (supra-normal)","216.0 mL/min/m² (severe oxygen debt)"],"a":0,"e":"First compute arterial oxygen content: CaO2 = (1.34 × 9.0 × 1.00) + (0.003 × 200) = 12.06 + 0.60 = 12.66 mL O2/dL. Converting dL to L multiplies by 10: DO2i = Cardiac Index (2.4 L/min/m²) × 12.66 mL/dL × 10 = 303.84 mL/min/m². Goal-Directed Perfusion maintains DO2i \u003e 270–280 mL/min/m² to prevent anaerobic lactic acidosis and CPB-associated acute kidney injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01160","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Mechanical Circulatory Support","topic":"Peripheral VA-ECMO Complications","q":"A patient on peripheral femoro-femoral Veno-Arterial (VA) ECMO for cardiogenic shock shows improving left ventricular ejection (pulsatile arterial line), severe bilateral pulmonary consolidation, a right radial arterial PaO2 of 44 mmHg (SpO2 76%), and a left femoral arterial PaO2 of 380 mmHg. Which phenomenon explains this differential hypoxemia and how is it corrected?","o":["Harlequin (North-South) syndrome: native LV ejects deoxygenated blood from failing lungs to the aortic arch/coronaries while ECMO oxygenates only the lower body; optimize ventilator/PEEP or add an upper-body venous return cannula (V-AV ECMO)","Differential arterial spasm from protamine; infuse papaverine into the right radial artery","Oxygenator fiber plasma leakage; immediately clamp the femoral arterial cannula","Recirculation artifact of VV-ECMO; increase femoral arterial pump RPM to 5,000"],"a":0,"e":"As native LV function recovers against poorly ventilated lungs on femoral VA-ECMO, hypoxic blood from the LV meets retrograde oxygenated ECMO blood in the descending aorta (the watershed/mixing zone), leaving the coronaries and right brachiocephalic/cerebral circulation hypoxic (\u0027Harlequin\u0027 or \u0027North-South\u0027 syndrome). Monitoring right radial ABGs/SpO2 detects it early; fixes include optimizing lung recruitment/FiO2, increasing ECMO flow if feasible, converting to V-AV ECMO (splitting arterial return to the internal jugular vein), or central/axillary cannulation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01161","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"CPB Crisis Management and Safety","topic":"Vacuum-Assisted Venous Drainage (VAVD)","q":"When utilizing Vacuum-Assisted Venous Drainage (VAVD) on a closed hard-shell venous reservoir during minimally invasive cardiac surgery, why must negative vacuum pressure be strictly regulated (typically kept milder than -40 to -60 mmHg) and paired with a positive/negative pressure relief valve?","o":["Excessive negative pressure reverses the polarity of the ultrasonic bubble detector","Excessive negative pressure causes venous line chattering/cavitation, increases hemolysis, and can pull air across the microporous oxygenator fibers into the blood phase if arterial pump flow stops","VAVD eliminates the need for systemic heparinization during bypass","Positive pressure inside the hard-shell reservoir is needed to force blood retrograde up the venous cannula"],"a":1,"e":"VAVD applies controlled sub-atmospheric suction (-20 to -50 mmHg, rarely \u003e -60 mmHg) to the sealed hard-shell venous reservoir to augment drainage through small cannulae. Excessive vacuum risks venous collapse/chattering, RBC shear hemolysis, and—if the arterial pump pauses or reservoir outlets become occluded—retrograde suction of gas across microporous oxygenator membranes into the arterial blood phase. Dedicated relief valves prevent dangerous over-pressurization or excessive vacuum.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01162","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Clinical Perfusion and Anticoagulation","topic":"Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT)","q":"A 57-year-old man with acute Type II Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT, platelet count 38,000/µL, positive anti-PF4/heparin antibody) requires urgent mechanical aortic valve replacement on CPB. Unfractionated heparin is contraindicated, so bivalirudin is selected. Which critical perfusionist precaution applies specifically to bivalirudin use on CPB?","o":["Use routine cell-saver heparinized saline wash for all shed mediastinal blood","Administer protamine sulfate immediately at the end of CPB to neutralize bivalirudin within 2 minutes","Avoid blood stagnation in the cardiotomy reservoir, venous bag, and cardioplegia lines (which are continuously flushed/recirculated), because bivalirudin is rapidly cleaved by thrombin/plasma proteases in stagnant blood","Prime the CPB circuit with 10,000 IU of low-molecular-weight heparin (enoxaparin) as a bridge"],"a":2,"e":"Bivalirudin is a reversible direct thrombin inhibitor (t1/2 ~25 min) cleared by proteolytic cleavage (by thrombin itself) and ~20% renal excretion. Where blood sits stagnant—such as clamped cardioplegia lines, unused vents, or unagitated cardiotomy reservoirs—bivalirudin is locally degraded and thrombus forms rapidly! Perfusionists must maintain continuous recirculation or flush stagnant lines with saline/bivalirudin and avoid heparin in all flushes/cell-saver solutions (using citrate in cell salvage).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01163","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Blood Conservation and Filtration","topic":"Cell Saver Mechanics","q":"When shed pericardial blood is processed through a centrifugal autotransfusion device (cell saver) and washed with 0.9% normal saline before reinfusion, which blood component is preserved and which components are discarded into the waste bag?","o":["Red blood cells and heparin are preserved together without dilution","Only plasma fibrinogen and platelets are preserved; red blood cells are lysed","Whole blood with intact clotting factors and platelets is preserved; only free hemoglobin is removed","Packed red blood cells (hematocrit ~50–60%) are preserved; platelets, clotting factors, plasma proteins, free hemoglobin, and heparin are washed away"],"a":3,"e":"Centrifugal cell salvage separates heavier RBCs in a Latham/disk bowl and washes them with isotonic saline, yielding autologous washed packed RBCs (Hct 50–60%) free of cellular debris, fat/lipid microemboli, activated cytokines, plasma free hemoglobin, and heparin. However, platelets, coagulation factors, and plasma proteins are also discarded—so massive cell-saver reinfusion requires monitoring for dilutional coagulopathy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01164","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"CPB Crisis Management and Safety","topic":"Alarms and Servoregulation","q":"Match each CPB circuit safety device with its primary protective function: (1) Ultrasonic arterial line bubble detector, (2) Venous reservoir level sensor, (3) Arterial line pressure transducer, (4) One-way purge valve on arterial line filter. Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Detects gaseous emboli and servoregulates arterial pump stop/clamp; 2-Alarms or slows arterial pump before reservoir empties; 3-Prevents circuit blowout or aortic dissection from downstream clamp/kink; 4-Prevents retrograde air entrainment if line pressure turns negative","1-Detects gaseous emboli; 2-Prevents circuit blowout; 3-Prevents reservoir emptying; 4-Measures activated clotting time","1-Prevents circuit blowout from kink; 2-Detects gaseous emboli; 3-Prevents retrograde air entrainment; 4-Prevents reservoir emptying","1-Prevents reservoir emptying; 2-Prevents retrograde air entrainment; 3-Detects gaseous emboli; 4-Prevents circuit blowout"],"a":0,"e":"Layered CPB safety engineering relies on servoregulated sensors: the ultrasonic bubble detector senses air distal to the oxygenator/filter and halts the pump; the level sensor guards against reservoir depletion; the arterial pressure transducer triggers high-pressure alarm/cutoff before tubing disconnection or dissection; and a one-way valve on the arterial filter purge line stops air from being sucked backward into the filter during negative pressure excursions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01165","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Bypass Instrumentation","topic":"Centrifugal Arterial Pumps","q":"Why must a perfusionist ALWAYS clamp the arterial line immediately whenever a centrifugal arterial blood pump slows to zero RPM during cardiopulmonary bypass, unlike a calibrated twin-roller pump?","o":["Centrifugal pumps contain internal one-way mechanical valves that jam unless clamped","A centrifugal pump is non-occlusive; stopping its impeller allows massive retrograde exsanguination from the patient\u0027s high-pressure aorta backward into the venous reservoir","A centrifugal pump reverses direction automatically to pump air forward at zero RPM","A centrifugal pump creates infinite positive occlusion that bursts the aortic cannula at zero RPM"],"a":1,"e":"Constrained-vortex centrifugal pumps impart kinetic energy without occluding the tubing. Consequently, they are preload- and afterload-dependent (requiring an ultrasonic flow probe to measure true forward flow) and non-occlusive: if power fails or RPM drops below arterial pressure head, blood rapidly siphons backward from the patient\u0027s aorta through the pump head into the open venous reservoir unless an arterial clamp or one-way valve closes the line.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01166","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Pediatric and Specialized Perfusion","topic":"Selective Antegrade Cerebral Perfusion (SACP)","q":"A 61-year-old man undergoes emergency repair of an acute Type A aortic dissection involving the aortic arch. After cooling to 24°C on CPB via right axillary artery cannulation, systemic circulation is arrested while the innominate and left common carotid arteries are snared/perfused for Selective Antegrade Cerebral Perfusion (SACP). What typical SACP flow rate, right radial perfusion pressure target, and neuromonitoring checks guide safe cerebral perfusion?","o":["Measure only femoral arterial pressure during innominate clamping","Flow 100 mL/kg/min at radial pressure 140 mmHg to force collateral perfusion","Flow ~10–15 mL/kg/min maintaining right radial arterial pressure ~50–70 mmHg, with bilateral cerebral oximetry (NIRS) showing symmetric oxygenation (alerting for left-hemisphere drop if Circle of Willis is incomplete)","Zero flow through the axillary cannula during arrest, maintaining water-to-blood temperature gradient of 20°C"],"a":2,"e":"Unilateral SACP via the right axillary/innominate artery perfuses the right carotid and vertebral territories at ~10–15 mL/kg/min (targeting right radial pressure ~50–70 mmHg at moderate hypothermia 22–28°C). Bilateral cerebral NIRS oximetry is vital: if the Circle of Willis is incomplete, left-hemisphere rSO2 drops precipitously compared with the right, prompting the surgeon to add a direct left common carotid perfusion cannula (bilateral SACP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01167","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"CPB Crisis Management and Safety","topic":"Acute Oxygenator Failure","q":"Midway through a 3-hour complex valve operation on CPB, the continuous inline venous/arterial saturation monitor shows arterial SaO2 dropping from 100% to 74%, pre-oxygenator pressure surges from 180 to 410 mmHg (high transmembrane pressure gradient), and blood exiting the oxygenator appears dark. What is the immediate troubleshooting and definitive corrective sequence?","o":["Turn off the arterial pump indefinitely while keeping the aortic cross-clamp applied at normothermia","Add 2,000 mL of cold saline directly into the gas exhaust port of the oxygenator","Increase sweep gas flow to 25 L/min across the obstructed oxygenator while administering protamine","Verify gas blender FiO2, sweep flow, and tubing connections (switch to backup emergency O2 tank); if gas supply is intact and transmembrane gradient/hypoxemia persists, cool the patient, prepare a primed parallel backup oxygenator, briefly stop pump/clamp lines, and execute sterile rapid oxygenator change-out"],"a":3,"e":"Acute oxygenator failure presents as severe hypoxemia/hypercapnia and/or rising transmembrane pressure drop (due to fibrin/thrombus deposition or moisture \u0027wetting out\u0027 of fibers). First rule out correctable gas-supply failure (disconnected sweep line, empty wall O2, faulty blender -\u003e switch to backup O2 cylinder). If confirmed intrinsic oxygenator failure/clotting, alert the surgeon, lower metabolic O2 demand (mild cooling/anesthesia), prime a backup oxygenator, and perform a coordinated \u003c60–90 s clamp-and-cut swap.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01168","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Perfusion Physiology and Calculations","topic":"Rewarming Rate and Gradients","q":"Per STS/SCA/AmSECT clinical practice guidelines for temperature management during CPB, why must the perfusionist restrict the arterial outlet blood temperature to \u003c= 37.0°C and keep the heat-exchanger water-to-blood temperature gradient \u003c= 10°C (typically 4–8°C) during rewarming?","o":["Because gas solubility drops as blood warms (Henry\u0027s Law -\u003e gaseous microemboli outgassing) and arterial blood \u003e37°C causes cerebral hyperthermia, worsening ischemic neuronal injury","Because keeping blood below 37°C induces permanent ventricular fibrillation","Because rapid rewarming increases heparin half-life tenfold, preventing protamine reversal","Because water warmer than 37°C melts the polypropylene hollow fibers instantly"],"a":0,"e":"Per Henry\u0027s Law, dissolved O2 and N2 become less soluble as blood temperature increases; a large water-to-blood gradient (\u003e10°C) forces rapid localized warming and precipitates dissolved gases into gaseous microemboli. Additionally, because the brain warms rapidly with high arterial perfusion, arterial outlet temperatures exceeding 37.0°C cause unrecognized cerebral hyperthermia that accelerates excitotoxic injury and postoperative neurocognitive decline.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01169","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Myocardial Protection and Cardioplegia","topic":"4:1 Blood-to-Crystalloid Delivery","q":"A perfusionist delivers cold 4:1 blood cardioplegia (4 parts oxygenated blood to 1 part hyperkalemic crystalloid) through a dual-tubing roller pump at a total combined delivery flow rate of 250 mL/min for 4 minutes (1,000 mL total induction dose). If the crystalloid bag contains potassium at 80 mEq/L, what are the individual blood and crystalloid flow rates, and how many mEq of potassium come from the crystalloid component in that 1,000 mL dose?","o":["Blood = 200 mL/min, Crystalloid = 50 mL/min; 80.0 mEq K+ from crystalloid","Blood = 200 mL/min, Crystalloid = 50 mL/min; 16.0 mEq K+ from crystalloid","Blood = 125 mL/min, Crystalloid = 125 mL/min; 40.0 mEq K+ from crystalloid","Blood = 225 mL/min, Crystalloid = 25 mL/min; 8.0 mEq K+ from crystalloid"],"a":1,"e":"A 4:1 ratio comprises 4 + 1 = 5 equal parts. Blood flow = (4/5) × 250 mL/min = 200 mL/min; Crystalloid flow = (1/5) × 250 mL/min = 50 mL/min. Over 4 minutes, total crystalloid volume infused = 50 mL/min × 4 min = 200 mL (0.20 L). Potassium delivered from the crystalloid bag = 0.20 L × 80 mEq/L = 16.0 mEq (yielding ~19–20 mEq/L final mixed [K+] when combined with ~3.2 mEq from 800 mL blood).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01170","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Clinical Perfusion and Surgical Coordination","topic":"Aortic Regurgitation on CPB","q":"A patient with severe aortic regurgitation (AR) undergoes aortic valve replacement. Immediately after initiating CPB and applying the aortic cross-clamp, antegrade aortic root cardioplegia is attempted, and the left ventricle rapidly balloons/distends with rising pulmonary artery pressure. Why is this happening and what is the corrective strategy?","o":["Increase antegrade aortic root cardioplegia pressure to 250 mmHg to force the aortic cusps shut","The mitral valve is stenotic and preventing blood from leaving the aorta","Aortic root pressure regurgitates across the incompetent aortic valve directly into the arrested LV; stop root cardioplegia, immediately insert an active LV vent (via right superior pulmonary vein), open the aorta, and deliver direct coronary ostial cardioplegia","Excessive venous drainage is sucking the LV inward; clamp the venous line"],"a":2,"e":"In significant aortic insufficiency, infusing antegrade cardioplegia into the aortic root (or high systemic CPB perfusion once the heart fibrillates/arrests) forces fluid backward through the incompetent valve into the flaccid LV. Acute LV distension stretches myocardial fibers, elevates subendocardial wall tension (impairing coronary perfusion), and causes pulmonary edema. Prompt LV venting (right superior pulmonary vein or LV apex) plus retrograde coronary sinus and/or direct hand-held coronary ostial cardioplegia protects the myocardium.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01171","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Blood Conservation and Filtration","topic":"Intravascular Hemolysis on CPB","q":"Two hours into a prolonged valve replacement, the perfusionist observes pink-to-port-wine red discoloration of urine in the Foley catheter tubing, rising serum potassium (5.8 mEq/L), and elevated arterial line pressure across a small 18 Fr arterial cannula. Which mechanism explains this complication and what circuit/clinical actions should be taken?","o":["Normal metabolism of propofol infusion; ignore urine color and restrict IV fluids","Isolated platelet consumption without RBC lysis; administer protamine on bypass","Allergic reaction to PVC tubing plasticizer only","Mechanical intravascular hemolysis (high plasma free hemoglobin) from excessive shear stress at the high-velocity arterial cannula/suction; reduce cardiotomy suction vacuum/air mixing, verify roller occlusion, avoid excessive line pressure, and stimulate alkaline diuresis (mannitol/furosemide/bicarbonate) to protect renal tubules"],"a":3,"e":"Pink/red urine with hyperkalemia signals hemoglobinuria from plasma free hemoglobin exceeding haptoglobin binding capacity (~100–150 mg/dL). High shear stress from an undersized arterial cannula (\u003e250–300 mmHg line pressure), over-occluded roller pumps, or vigorous air-mixing cardiotomy suction lyses RBCs. Free hemoglobin precipitates in acidic renal tubules causing ATN; correcting mechanical trauma, promoting brisk urine output (mannitol/fluids), and urinary alkalinization protect kidney function.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01172","course":"B.Sc Perfusion Technology","subject":"Pediatric and Specialized Perfusion","topic":"Lesion-Specific CPB Strategies","q":"Match each congenital cardiac lesion/procedure with its key perfusion consideration: (1) Tetralogy of Fallot with major aortopulmonary collaterals (MAPCAs), (2) Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Connection (TAPVC), (3) Interrupted Aortic Arch repair, (4) Bidirectional Glenn (superior cavopulmonary shunt). Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Aggressive left-heart venting to handle heavy bronchial/collateral venous return; 2-Bicaval venous cannulation with careful snaring and meticulous de-airing of pulmonary venous confluence; 3-Dual arterial pressure monitoring (right radial + femoral) and dual arterial perfusion or SACP; 4-Maintain passive SVC-to-PA venous return without systemic ventricular-PA connection and avoid hyperventilation-induced high PVR post-CPB","1-Dual arterial pressure monitoring; 2-Passive SVC-to-PA return; 3-Aggressive LV venting for MAPCAs; 4-Pulmonary venous confluence de-airing","1-Bicaval cannulation for TAPVC; 2-Avoid high PVR in Glenn; 3-Aggressive LV venting for MAPCAs; 4-Dual arterial pressure monitoring","1-Aggressive LV venting; 2-Dual arterial pressure monitoring; 3-Passive SVC-to-PA return; 4-Bicaval snaring"],"a":0,"e":"Cyanotic TOF patients develop extensive MAPCAs that flood the LA/LV on CPB, requiring vigorous LV venting. TAPVC repair opens the posterior left atrium/pulmonary venous confluence, requiring bicaval cannulation and thorough de-airing. Arch reconstruction requires simultaneous upper (right radial) and lower (femoral/umbilical) arterial monitoring to ensure distal perfusion. Bidirectional Glenn relies on low pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) for passive SVC flow to the lungs post-bypass.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01173","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Clinical Medicine - Cardiology","topic":"NSTEMI Initial Management","q":"A 58-year-old man with diabetes mellitus presents with 50 minutes of crushing retrosternal chest pain radiating to the jaw. ECG shows 1.5 mm horizontal ST-segment depression in V4-V6 without ST elevation, and high-sensitivity Troponin I is elevated at 480 ng/L. Under ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows, which acute pharmacological and invasive strategy is indicated?","o":["Immediate intravenous tenecteplase fibrinolysis plus oral aspirin","Chewed aspirin (300-325 mg), P2Y12 inhibitor (ticagrelor/clopidogrel), anticoagulation (heparin/enoxaparin), high-intensity statin, and GRACE risk stratification for early coronary angiography (\u003c24 h)","Sublingual nifedipine alone and discharge for outpatient exercise stress test","Intravenous streptokinase followed by warfarin monotherapy"],"a":1,"e":"ST depression with positive troponin defines NSTEMI. Dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin + P2Y12 inhibitor), parenteral anticoagulation, and high-intensity statin with early invasive angiography (within 24 hours for high GRACE score/rising troponin) represent standard care. Fibrinolytics (tenecteplase/streptokinase) are contraindicated in NSTEMI because they increase bleeding and mortality without clinical benefit.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01174","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Endocrinology \u0026 Acute Care Medicine","topic":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA)","q":"A 24-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes is admitted with Kussmaul breathing, blood glucose 460 mg/dL, arterial pH 7.18, serum HCO3- 10 mEq/L, positive serum ketones, and serum potassium 3.0 mEq/L. After initiating 0.9% NaCl fluid resuscitation, what is the critical next step before starting intravenous regular insulin?","o":["Start IV insulin bolus 0.1 U/kg immediately to drive glucose and potassium down rapidly","Administer 100 mEq IV sodium bicarbonate to normalize arterial pH before fluids","Hold IV insulin and administer IV potassium chloride (KCl) first until serum K+ is \u003e= 3.3 mEq/L","Give subcutaneous long-acting insulin glargine and restrict potassium intake"],"a":2,"e":"Despite total body potassium depletion in DKA, initial serum K+ may be low (\u003c 3.3 mEq/L). Starting insulin immediately drives potassium intracellularly via Na+/K+-ATPase activation, precipitating severe hypokalemia, ventricular arrhythmias, or respiratory muscle paralysis. Therefore, insulin MUST be delayed while IV KCl (20-30 mEq/hr) is infused until serum K+ \u003e= 3.3 mEq/L.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01175","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Clinical Pharmacology \u0026 Therapeutics","topic":"Renal vs Hepatic Drug Clearance","q":"A 68-year-old patient with Stage 4 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73 m2) requires empiric parenteral antibiotic coverage for community-acquired pyelonephritis/bacteremia while awaiting cultures. Which intravenous beta-lactam requires NO dose reduction in isolated severe renal impairment due to dual biliary-renal elimination?","o":["Meropenem","Cefepime","Piperacillin-tazobactam","Ceftriaxone"],"a":3,"e":"Ceftriaxone is eliminated approximately 40-50% via biliary excretion and 50-60% via the kidneys; consequently, no dose adjustment is required in isolated renal dysfunction (even eGFR \u003c 30 mL/min) unless concurrent severe hepatic failure is present. By contrast, meropenem, cefepime, and piperacillin-tazobactam are predominantly renally cleared and require dose/interval reduction to prevent drug accumulation and neurotoxicity (e.g., cefepime encephalopathy/seizures).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01176","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Internal Medicine - Pulmonology","topic":"Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP)","q":"A 68-year-old man presents with fever, productive cough, and right lower-lobe consolidation on chest radiograph. Exam and labs show new-onset confusion, BUN 28 mg/dL (urea ~10 mmol/L), respiratory rate 32 breaths/min, and BP 86/56 mmHg. What is his CURB-65 score, and what disposition and empiric antibiotic regimen are indicated?","o":["CURB-65 = 5; admit to Intensive Care Unit (ICU) and initiate IV beta-lactam (ceftriaxone/ampicillin-sulbactam) plus IV macrolide (azithromycin) or respiratory fluoroquinolone","CURB-65 = 2; discharge home on oral amoxicillin monotherapy","CURB-65 = 4; admit to medical ward on oral azithromycin alone","CURB-65 = 3; outpatient follow-up in 48 hours with oral doxycycline"],"a":0,"e":"CURB-65 awards 1 point each for: Confusion (+1), Urea \u003e 7 mmol/L / BUN \u003e 19 mg/dL (+1), Respiratory rate \u003e= 30/min (+1), Blood pressure SBP \u003c 90 or DBP \u003c= 60 mmHg (+1), and Age \u003e= 65 years (+1). With all 5 criteria present (CURB-65 = 5, 30-day mortality \u003e 25-40%), urgent ICU admission and dual IV empiric therapy (beta-lactam + macrolide or respiratory fluoroquinolone) are required.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01177","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Gastroenterology \u0026 Hepatology","topic":"Ascites \u0026 Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP)","q":"A 52-year-old man with decompensated alcoholic cirrhosis develops diffuse abdominal tenderness and low-grade fever. Diagnostic paracentesis reveals ascitic fluid albumin 1.4 g/dL (simultaneous serum albumin 3.2 g/dL), total WBC 680/mm3 with 60% polymorphonuclear leukocytes (absolute PMN = 408/mm3). What are the SAAG interpretation and definitive diagnosis/treatment?","o":["SAAG = 1.8 g/dL (rules out portal hypertension); tuberculous peritonitis requiring ATT alone","SAAG = 1.8 g/dL (\u003e= 1.1 g/dL confirms portal hypertension); Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (PMN \u003e= 250/mm3) requiring IV third-generation cephalosporin (cefotaxime/ceftriaxone) plus IV albumin","SAAG = 0.8 g/dL; peritoneal carcinomatosis requiring immediate laparotomy","SAAG = 4.6 g/dL; normal sterile cirrhotic ascites requiring diuretics only"],"a":1,"e":"Serum-Ascites Albumin Gradient (SAAG) = Serum albumin - Ascitic albumin = 3.2 - 1.4 = 1.8 g/dL (\u003e= 1.1 g/dL signifies portal hypertension with ~97% accuracy). An ascitic absolute PMN count \u003e= 250 cells/mm3 (here 408/mm3) confirms Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP) regardless of culture status, warranting immediate IV cefotaxime/ceftriaxone plus IV albumin (1.5 g/kg day 1, 1.0 g/kg day 3) to prevent hepatorenal syndrome.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01178","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Clinical Neurology \u0026 Stroke Care","topic":"IV Thrombolysis Blood Pressure Thresholds","q":"A 62-year-old man arrives 2 hours after sudden onset of right-sided hemiplegia and expressive aphasia. Non-contrast CT head excludes hemorrhage, NIHSS is 14, and blood pressure is 196/108 mmHg. Before initiating IV alteplase (0.9 mg/kg), to what threshold must his blood pressure be lowered safely using IV labetalol or nicardipine?","o":["Below 120/80 mmHg immediately with sublingual nifedipine","Below 140/90 mmHg using IV nitroprusside bolus","Below 185/110 mmHg prior to bolus (and maintained \u003c 180/105 mmHg during and for 24 hours after infusion)","Below 220/120 mmHg only; IV alteplase can start immediately at 196/108 mmHg"],"a":2,"e":"Per AHA/ASA and ICMR Acute Stroke guidelines, patients eligible for IV thrombolysis (within 4.5 hours) must have BP lowered smoothly with titratable IV agents (labetalol or nicardipine) to \u003c 185/110 mmHg before alteplase initiation, and kept \u003c 180/105 mmHg for at least 24 hours to minimize symptomatic intracranial hemorrhage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01179","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Nephrology \u0026 Fluid-Electrolyte Emergencies","topic":"ECG Changes \u0026 Emergency Cardiac Stabilization","q":"A 56-year-old woman with missed hemodialysis presents with severe weakness, serum K+ = 7.2 mEq/L, and an ECG showing peaked T waves, absent P waves, and a widened QRS complex (150 ms). What is the immediate FIRST medication that must be administered intravenously to prevent imminent ventricular fibrillation/asystole?","o":["IV 0.9% NaCl with 20 mEq KCl","Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate (Kayexalate) suspension","IV furosemide 40 mg alone and wait 4 hours for urine output","IV 10% Calcium Gluconate (10 mL = 1 g over 2-3 minutes) to immediately stabilize the cardiac myocyte membrane threshold potential"],"a":3,"e":"When hyperkalemia produces ECG conduction changes (peaked T waves, PR prolongation, flattened P waves, widened QRS, sine wave), IV calcium (10% calcium gluconate 10 mL over 2-3 min) is the immediate first-line drug—restoring the resting-to-threshold potential gradient within 1-3 minutes (lasting 30-60 min) without altering serum K+, buying time for insulin-dextrose/albuterol intracellular shifting and hemodialysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01180","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Obstetrics \u0026 Gynaecology Clinical Practice","topic":"Magnesium Sulfate in Eclampsia","q":"Assertion (A): Intravenous or intramuscular magnesium sulfate (Zuspan/Pritchard regimen) is the drug of choice for preventing and controlling eclamptic seizures, superior to phenytoin and diazepam. Reason (R): Because magnesium is renally cleared, patients must have hourly checks for presence of patellar deep tendon reflexes (earliest lost at 7-10 mEq/L), respiratory rate \u003e= 12-16/min, and urine output \u003e= 30 mL/hr, with 10% IV calcium gluconate ready as antidote.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":1,"e":"Both statements are clinically true: the Magpie and Eclampsia Collaborative Trials proved magnesium sulfate superior to phenytoin/diazepam for seizure prevention/control (Assertion A), and clinical toxicity monitoring—patellar reflexes, respiratory rate, and urine output \u003e= 30 mL/hr with IV calcium gluconate bedside antidote—is mandatory (Reason R). However, R describes safety monitoring of magnesium toxicity rather than explaining why MgSO4 outperforms phenytoin/diazepam.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01181","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Hematology \u0026 Anticoagulation","topic":"Warfarin-Associated Intracranial Hemorrhage","q":"A 71-year-old man taking warfarin for mechanical mitral valve develops an acute 35 mL intracerebral hemorrhage with INR = 4.6. Which immediate reversal regimen normalizes Vitamin K-dependent coagulation factors (II, VII, IX, X) MOST rapidly without fluid overload?","o":["Four-factor Prothrombin Complex Concentrate (4F-PCC) IV immediately PLUS slow IV Vitamin K 10 mg","Oral Vitamin K 2.5 mg alone and repeat INR in 24 hours","Protamine sulfate 50 mg IV bolus","Subcutaneous Vitamin K 10 mg with 2 L normal saline"],"a":0,"e":"In warfarin-associated major/intracranial hemorrhage, 4-factor PCC delivers concentrated Factors II, VII, IX, and X in a small volume, reversing INR within 10-15 minutes (far faster and without the volume overload/TRALI risks of FFP). Slow IV Vitamin K (10 mg over 10-30 min) is co-administered to sustain endogenous hepatic factor synthesis once PCC factors clear.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01182","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Gastroenterology \u0026 Hepatology Emergencies","topic":"Acute Esophageal Variceal Hemorrhage","q":"A 50-year-old man with Child-Pugh B cirrhosis presents with massive hematemesis, BP 82/50 mmHg, HR 118 bpm, and hemoglobin 6.3 g/dL. Following airway assessment, large-bore IV access, and crystalloid resuscitation, which combined pharmacological, transfusion, and endoscopic strategy reduces mortality in acute variceal hemorrhage?","o":["Oral propranolol bolus during active hypotension and withhold antibiotics","Restrictive PRBC transfusion targeting Hb 7-8 g/dL, immediate IV octreotide/terlipressin (splanchnic vasoconstrictor), prophylactic IV ceftriaxone, and urgent upper GI endoscopy with endoscopic variceal ligation (EVL) within 12 hours","High-dose IV heparin infusion to prevent portal vein thrombosis","Aggressive PRBC transfusion to target Hb \u003e 12 g/dL and delay endoscopy for 72 hours"],"a":1,"e":"In acute variceal bleeding, over-transfusion (targeting Hb \u003e 9-10 g/dL) paradoxically raises portal venous pressure and triggers rebleeding; randomized trials support a restrictive transfusion threshold (Hb \u003c 7 g/dL, target 7-8 g/dL). Immediate splanchnic vasoconstriction (octreotide or terlipressin) + short-course prophylactic IV ceftriaxone (preventing SBP/bacteremia) + EVL within 12 hours significantly lower mortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01183","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Pulmonology \u0026 Vascular Medicine","topic":"Acute Pulmonary Embolism (PE) Workup","q":"Five days after total knee arthroplasty, a 64-year-old woman develops acute pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, SpO2 89%, HR 116 bpm, and asymmetric right calf swelling (+4 cm). Her Wells score for Pulmonary Embolism is 7.5 (\u0027PE likely\u0027 / high clinical probability). Which diagnostic and initial therapeutic step is indicated?","o":["Wait 48 hours for bilateral lower-limb repeat ultrasound before ordering CTPA","Perform spirometry and discharge on oral azithromycin","Proceed directly to CT Pulmonary Angiography (CTPA) (bypassing D-dimer, which cannot safely exclude high-probability PE) and initiate empiric therapeutic anticoagulation (LMWH/UFH) while awaiting imaging if no bleeding contraindication exists","Order a quantitative D-dimer first and exclude PE without imaging if D-dimer is mildly elevated"],"a":2,"e":"D-dimer has high negative predictive value ONLY in low- or intermediate-probability patients (Wells score \u003c= 4). In a patient with high pre-test probability (Wells \u003e 4 / \u0027PE likely\u0027, especially post-major orthopedic surgery with DVT signs and tachycardia), D-dimer wastes time and carries unacceptable false-negative risk—mandating direct CTPA (or V/Q scan if severe renal failure/contrast allergy) and immediate empiric anticoagulation while awaiting confirmation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01184","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Clinical Pharmacology \u0026 Infectious Diseases","topic":"Rifampicin CYP450 Enzyme Induction","q":"A 29-year-old woman living with HIV (on Tenofovir-Lamivudine-Dolutegravir) and taking combined oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) is newly diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis and started on standard NTEP HRZE therapy containing Rifampicin. Because Rifampicin is a potent CYP450 (CYP3A4) and UGT1A1 enzyme INDUCER, what clinical adjustments are required?","o":["Discontinue rifampicin immediately for all patients on contraception","Replace dolutegravir with oral ketoconazole","Halve the dolutegravir dose due to drug accumulation toxicity; OCP efficacy increases","Double the dolutegravir dose to 50 mg TWICE daily (BID) to compensate for accelerated hepatic clearance, and advise non-hormonal/barrier contraception because rifampicin rapidly clears estrogen/progestin causing OCP failure"],"a":3,"e":"Rifampicin is one of the strongest known inducers of hepatic cytochrome P450 (CYP3A4, CYP2C9) and UGT1A1 glucuronidation enzymes. It sharply lowers plasma concentrations of dolutegravir (requiring an extra 50 mg dose 12 hours apart -\u003e 50 mg BID under NTEP/NACO guidelines), oral contraceptives (causing unintended pregnancy -\u003e use barrier/IUD methods), warfarin, tacrolimus, and azole antifungals.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01185","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Rheumatology \u0026 Neuro-Ophthalmic Emergencies","topic":"Giant Cell (Temporal) Arteritis","q":"A 73-year-old woman presents with new-onset throbbing right temporal headache, jaw pain while chewing (jaw claudication), scalp tenderness when combing hair, ESR 96 mm/hr, and two brief episodes of transient right-eye vision loss (amaurosis fugax) today. Temporal artery biopsy cannot be scheduled for 3 days. What is the mandatory immediate management?","o":["Start high-dose corticosteroids IMMEDIATELY (IV methylprednisolone 500-1,000 mg/day for 3 days given visual symptoms, or high-dose oral prednisolone 60 mg/day) today, and proceed with temporal artery biopsy within 1-2 weeks","Prescribe oral sumatriptan and discharge for routine outpatient ophthalmology follow-up in 1 month","Start oral methotrexate monotherapy as sole acute induction","Withhold all steroids until the temporal artery biopsy is performed in 3 days so histology is unaltered"],"a":0,"e":"Giant Cell Arteritis (GCA) in patients \u003e 50 years with jaw claudication and amaurosis fugax carries imminent risk of permanent ischemic optic neuropathy (AAION) and contralateral blindness. High-dose corticosteroids (IV pulse methylprednisolone when visual symptoms are present) MUST be initiated immediately without waiting for biopsy—arteritic histopathological changes remain diagnostic for at least 1-2 weeks after starting steroids.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01186","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Endocrine Emergencies","topic":"Acute Adrenal Crisis","q":"A 48-year-old man on chronic prednisolone for rheumatoid arthritis abruptly stopped his tablets 4 days ago during acute gastroenteritis. He arrives with BP 70/42 mmHg unresponsive to 2 L normal saline and norepinephrine, serum Na+ 125 mEq/L, K+ 5.8 mEq/L, and blood glucose 50 mg/dL. What is the diagnosis and immediate lifesaving treatment?","o":["Pheochromocytoma crisis; administer IV phenoxybenzamine","Acute Adrenal Crisis; immediately administer IV Hydrocortisone 100 mg bolus (followed by 200 mg/24 hr) plus rapid IV 0.9% normal saline with 5% dextrose (D5NS) without waiting for lab cortisol confirmation","Myxedema coma; give IV levothyroxine alone without steroids","SIADH; impose strict fluid restriction and wait 48 hours for cosyntropin stimulation results"],"a":1,"e":"Catecholamine-refractory distributive shock accompanied by hyponatremia, hyperkalemia (if primary, or hyponatremia/hypoglycemia in secondary HPA suppression after abrupt steroid withdrawal), and hypoglycemia during intercurrent illness defines Acute Adrenal Crisis. Treatment must NEVER wait for ACTH/cortisol results: give IV hydrocortisone 100 mg stat (or dexamethasone 4 mg IV if simultaneous cosyntropin test is drawn) + aggressive isotonic saline and dextrose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01187","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Neurology \u0026 Clinical Toxicology","topic":"Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) vs Serotonin Syndrome","q":"A hospitalized patient started on haloperidol 5 days ago develops fever (40.2 C), autonomic instability, CK 18,000 U/L, and diffuse \u0027lead-pipe\u0027 muscular rigidity with hyporeflexia, whereas a second patient combining fluoxetine and linezolid develops agitation, fever (39.5 C), hyperreflexia, and sustained inducible ankle clonus within 6 hours. Which pair correctly matches diagnosis and specific targeted pharmacotherapy?","o":["Patient 1: Serotonin Syndrome (cyproheptadine); Patient 2: NMS (dantrolene)","Patient 1: Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (stop antipsychotic; supportive cooling + IV dantrolene and/or bromocriptine); Patient 2: Serotonin Syndrome (stop serotonergic drugs; benzodiazepines + cyproheptadine)","Both patients have Malignant Hyperthermia; administer IV physostigmine","Patient 1: Anticholinergic toxicity; Patient 2: Opioid withdrawal"],"a":1,"e":"NMS (dopamine D2 blockade from antipsychotics/metoclopramide) evolves over days with bradykinesia, generalized \u0027lead-pipe\u0027 rigidity, hyporeflexia, autonomic lability, and markedly elevated CK -\u003e treated by stopping the neuroleptic, cooling, hydration, dantrolene, and bromocriptine (dopamine agonist). Serotonin syndrome (e.g., SSRI + linezolid/MAOI) erupts over hours with neuromuscular excitability—hyperreflexia, ocular/ankle clonus, and myoclonus -\u003e treated with benzodiazepines and 5-HT2A antagonist cyproheptadine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01188","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Internal Medicine and Acute Care","topic":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA)","q":"A 24-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes presents with Kussmaul breathing, abdominal pain, blood glucose 490 mg/dL, arterial pH 7.12, serum HCO3- 9 mEq/L, and serum potassium 2.9 mEq/L. After initiating 0.9% NaCl fluid resuscitation, what is the critical next step regarding insulin and potassium?","o":["Switch immediately to 5% dextrose with half-normal saline before measuring repeat electrolytes","Start high-dose IV regular insulin immediately without potassium replacement","Hold insulin initially and infuse IV potassium chloride (20–40 mEq/h) until serum K+ is \u003e= 3.3 mEq/L, then start regular insulin infusion","Administer IV sodium bicarbonate 100 mEq bolus to correct pH to 7.35 before giving potassium"],"a":2,"e":"Although total-body potassium is depleted in DKA, initial serum K+ may appear normal or low. Because insulin drives extracellular K+ rapidly intracellularly, starting insulin when serum K+ \u003c 3.3 mEq/L can precipitate severe hypokalemia and fatal ventricular arrhythmias. Potassium must be repleted first until K+ \u003e= 3.3 mEq/L before starting IV regular insulin (0.1 U/kg/h).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01189","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Cardiology and Critical Care","topic":"Inferior STEMI with Right Ventricular Infarction","q":"A 60-year-old man presents with 2 hours of crushing chest pain, BP 82/50 mmHg, HR 64/min, elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP), and clear lung fields on auscultation. His 12-lead ECG shows 3 mm ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, and a right-sided ECG shows 1.5 mm ST elevation in lead V4R. Which management choice is appropriate and which class of drugs must be strictly avoided?","o":["Administer sublingual nitroglycerin and IV furosemide immediately to lower JVP","Withhold IV fluids and initiate immediate dopamine plus high-dose nitroprusside","Administer IV metoprolol and morphine sulfate to reduce preload and heart rate","Administer cautious 0.9% NaCl fluid boluses to maintain RV preload and arrange urgent reperfusion; strictly avoid nitrates and diuretics"],"a":3,"e":"Inferior STEMI complicated by Right Ventricular (RV) infarction (confirmed by ST elevation in V4R) presents with the classic triad of hypotension, elevated JVP (with Kussmaul sign), and clear lung fields. The stunned right ventricle is critically preload-dependent; cautious normal saline boluses augment LV filling, while venodilators/diuretics (nitrates, morphine, furosemide) crash preload and worsen cardiogenic shock.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01190","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry and Critical Care Interpretation","topic":"Anion Gap and Winter\u0027s Formula","q":"A toxic-appearing patient has the following arterial blood gas and electrolyte values: pH = 7.22, PaCO2 = 24 mmHg, HCO3- = 10 mEq/L, Na+ = 138 mEq/L, Cl- = 102 mEq/L. Calculate the serum anion gap and apply Winter\u0027s formula (Expected PaCO2 = 1.5 × [HCO3-] + 8 ± 2) to classify the acid-base disorder.","o":["Anion gap = 26 mEq/L; expected PaCO2 = 21–25 mmHg -\u003e High anion gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","Anion gap = 36 mEq/L; expected PaCO2 = 21–25 mmHg -\u003e Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","Anion gap = 16 mEq/L; expected PaCO2 = 30–34 mmHg -\u003e Mixed metabolic acidosis and respiratory acidosis","Anion gap = 26 mEq/L; expected PaCO2 = 12–16 mmHg -\u003e Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis"],"a":0,"e":"Serum Anion Gap = Na+ − (Cl− + HCO3−) = 138 − (102 + 10) = 138 − 112 = 26 mEq/L (elevated above normal 8–12 mEq/L, indicating High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis). Using Winter\u0027s formula: Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × 10) + 8 ± 2 = 23 ± 2 = 21–25 mmHg. Because the measured PaCO2 (24 mmHg) lies squarely within 21–25 mmHg, respiratory compensation is appropriate without a superimposed respiratory disorder.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01191","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Infectious Diseases and National Health Programmes","topic":"Severe Malaria Management","q":"A 28-year-old woman in her second trimester of pregnancy presents with high-grade fever, altered sensorium (GCS 10/15), jaundice, and a blood smear showing 8% Plasmodium falciparum parasitemia. According to Indian NVBDCP / ICMR and WHO guidelines, what is the first-line antimalarial regimen?","o":["Oral mefloquine monotherapy immediately via nasogastric tube","Intravenous artesunate (2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, and 24 hours, then once daily) followed by a full 3-day oral ACT course once able to tolerate orally","Intramuscular artemether only on day 1 followed by primaquine 45 mg single dose","Oral chloroquine 25 mg/kg divided over 3 days"],"a":1,"e":"Under India\u0027s National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (NVBDCP) and WHO guidelines, IV artesunate (2.4 mg/kg IV at 0, 12, 24 hours and then every 24 hours for at least 24 hours until the patient can tolerate oral therapy) is the treatment of choice for severe falciparum malaria in ALL adults and pregnant women in all trimesters, followed by a complete 3-day oral Artemisinin-based Combination Therapy (ACT) course.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01192","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Clinical Pharmacology","topic":"First-Line Antitubercular Therapy (ATT)","q":"A 40-year-old man on the intensive phase of daily HRZE antitubercular therapy under NTEP complains of progressive blurring of vision and inability to distinguish red from green colors on Ishihara plates. Which drug should be withheld immediately for suspected retrobulbar optic neuritis?","o":["Isoniazid","Pyrazinamide","Ethambutol","Rifampicin"],"a":2,"e":"Ethambutol causes dose- and duration-dependent retrobulbar optic neuritis, classically presenting first with loss of red-green color discrimination and reduced visual acuity. Prompt discontinuation usually allows visual recovery, whereas continued therapy can lead to permanent optic atrophy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01193","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"General Surgery and Perioperative Care","topic":"Hollow Viscus Perforation","q":"A 48-year-old chronic NSAID user develops sudden, severe generalized abdominal pain starting in the epigastrium 4 hours ago. On examination, he lies motionless with BP 94/60 mmHg, HR 118/min, board-like abdominal rigidity, rebound tenderness, and absent bowel sounds. An erect chest radiograph demonstrates a crescent of free air beneath the right hemidiaphragm. What is the immediate priority?","o":["Perform immediate outpatient colonoscopy to inspect the duodenal mucosa","Administer oral barium swallow to localize the ulcer perforation site","Give oral antacids and IM diclofenac and observe for 24 hours","Keep NPO, insert wide-bore IV access for crystalloid resuscitation, place nasogastric tube, administer IV PPI and broad-spectrum antibiotics, and obtain urgent surgical consultation for repair"],"a":3,"e":"Pneumoperitoneum (free air under the diaphragm) with generalized peritonitis and board-like rigidity indicates hollow viscus perforation, most commonly a perforated peptic ulcer. Immediate management comprises NPO status, aggressive IV crystalloid resuscitation, nasogastric decompression, empiric broad-spectrum IV antibiotics covering enteric flora, IV proton pump inhibitor, and urgent surgical repair (e.g., Graham omental patch).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01194","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Eclampsia Management","q":"A 23-year-old primigravida at 36 weeks gestation with eclampsia is receiving maintenance magnesium sulfate per the Pritchard regimen (5 g IM every 4 hours after loading). Prior to the next dose, her deep tendon (patellar) reflexes are absent, respiratory rate is 10/min, and urine output over the past 4 hours was 60 mL total. What immediate action is required?","o":["Withhold the next magnesium sulfate dose immediately and administer IV 10% calcium gluconate (10 mL / 1 g) slowly over 3 minutes","Increase maintenance magnesium sulfate to 2 g/hour IV infusion","Give IV diazepam 10 mg bolus to stimulate respiratory drive","Administer the next 5 g IM magnesium sulfate dose alongside IV furosemide"],"a":0,"e":"Magnesium is excreted solely by the kidneys, so oliguria (\u003c25–30 mL/h) predisposes to hypermagnesemia. Toxicity progresses sequentially: loss of patellar reflexes (~8–10 mEq/L), respiratory depression (\u003c12–16/min at ~12 mEq/L), and cardiac arrest (\u003e15 mEq/L). Absent reflexes plus hypoventilation mandate stopping MgSO4 immediately and giving the physiological antagonist, 10 mL of 10% calcium gluconate IV slowly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01195","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Cardiovascular \u0026 Vascular Emergencies","topic":"Acute Aortic Dissection Management","q":"A 61-year-old man with longstanding uncontrolled hypertension develops abrupt, maximal-at-onset \u0027tearing\u0027 chest pain radiating to the interscapular back, BP 204/112 mmHg in the right arm vs 168/92 mmHg in the left arm, HR 104 bpm, and a widened mediastinum on chest X-ray (CT angiogram confirms Stanford Type A aortic dissection). What is the mandatory initial pharmacological sequence?","o":["Load with aspirin 325 mg, clopidogrel 600 mg, and IV heparin bolus immediately","Administer IV beta-blocker FIRST (esmolol or labetalol) to reduce heart rate (\u003c 60 bpm) and ventricular ejection velocity (dP/dt), THEN add IV nicardipine/nitroprusside if needed to achieve SBP 100-120 mmHg alongside emergent cardiothoracic surgical repair","Immediate IV sodium nitroprusside first, followed later by beta-blocker if tachycardia develops","Immediate IV tenecteplase fibrinolysis"],"a":1,"e":"In acute aortic dissection, sheer aortic wall stress (dP/dt) drives tear propagation. Giving a pure vasodilator (nitroprusside/nicardipine) BEFORE beta-blockade induces baroreceptor reflex tachycardia and increased LV contractility, accelerating dissection propagation! Therefore, IV esmolol or labetalol MUST be given first (HR target \u003c= 60 bpm), followed by vasodilator to target SBP 100-120 mmHg. Type A dissections require emergent surgical repair; antiplatelets/heparin/fibrinolytics are catastrophic.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01196","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Toxicology \u0026 Hepatology","topic":"Acetaminophen (Paracetamol) Overdose","q":"A 23-year-old woman presents 5 hours after an acute intentional ingestion of 24 g of paracetamol (acetaminophen). Her 4-hour post-ingestion serum acetaminophen concentration plots well above the Rumack-Matthew treatment line (220 mcg/mL at 4 h). What toxic metabolite causes centrilobular hepatic necrosis, and how does early N-acetylcysteine (NAC) prevent liver failure?","o":["Salicylic acid; NAC alkalinizes the urine","Ammonia; NAC chelates hepatic copper","NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine) generated by CYP2E1 once hepatic glutathione is depleted; NAC serves as a cysteine precursor replenishing hepatic glutathione and directly detoxifying NAPQI (maximally effective within 8 hours)","Formic acid; NAC inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase"],"a":2,"e":"In therapeutic doses, paracetamol is glucuronidated/sulfated safely and the tiny fraction oxidized by CYP2E1 to toxic NAPQI is conjugated by hepatic glutathione. Massive overdose saturates glucuronidation/sulfation and exhausts glutathione, allowing NAPQI to covalently bind hepatocyte proteins (zone 3 centrilobular necrosis). Plotting \u003e= 4-hour levels on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram guides IV/oral N-acetylcysteine, which restores glutathione and prevents hepatotoxicity if started within 8 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01197","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Infectious Diseases \u0026 Tropical Medicine (India)","topic":"Severe Plasmodium falciparum Cerebral Malaria","q":"A 34-year-old man from Odisha presents with fever, altered sensorium (GCS 10), jaundice, hypoglycemia, and a peripheral smear showing 8% Plasmodium falciparum ring-stage parasitemia. Per India\u0027s National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (NVBDCP) guidelines for severe malaria, what is the first-line antimalarial regimen?","o":["Oral primaquine monotherapy for 14 days","IV metronidazole plus oral doxycycline alone","Oral chloroquine 600 mg base stat","IV Artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, and 24 hours, then once daily until the patient can tolerate a full 3-day oral Artemisinin-based Combination Therapy (ACT) course"],"a":3,"e":"Large randomized trials (SEAQUAMAT/AQUAMAT) and NVBDCP India guidelines establish parenteral IV (or IM) Artesunate (2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 hours, then daily; 3.0 mg/kg for children \u003c 20 kg) as superior to IV quinine in severe P. falciparum malaria (reducing mortality by ~35% with zero quinine-induced hypoglycemia/cinchonism), followed by a complete 3-day oral ACT once oral intake resumes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01198","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Nephrology and Urology","topic":"Pre-Renal Azotemia vs Acute Tubular Necrosis","q":"A 67-year-old man admitted 4 days ago for septic shock after urosepsis develops oliguria (urine output 180 mL/24 h) and serum creatinine rising from 1.0 to 3.4 mg/dL despite adequate volume resuscitation. Laboratory studies show urine Na+ = 54 mEq/L, Fractional Excretion of Sodium (FENa) = 3.2%, urine osmolality = 305 mOsm/kg, and urine microscopy showing muddy brown granular casts. What is the diagnosis and primary management principle?","o":["Intrinsic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN); maintain euvolemia (avoid fluid overload), discontinue nephrotoxins, adjust renal-cleared drug doses, and monitor for dialysis indications","Minimal change nephrotic syndrome; initiate high-dose oral prednisolone","Acute post-obstructive uropathy; start oral thiazide diuretic without renal ultrasound","Pre-renal azotemia; administer 4 liters of rapid 0.9% NaCl bolus immediately"],"a":0,"e":"In ischemic/septic ATN, damaged tubular epithelial cells lose sodium-reabsorbing and urine-concentrating capacity, producing urine Na+ \u003e 40 mEq/L, FENa \u003e 2%, isosthenuric urine osmolality (~300 mOsm/kg), and pathognomonic muddy brown granular/epithelial cell casts. Once euvolemic, aggressive fluid boluses cause pulmonary edema; management is supportive (nephrotoxin avoidance, dose adjustment, electrolyte/volume balance, RRT if refractory).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01199","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology","topic":"Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP)","q":"A 52-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis and tense ascites presents with fever (38.4°C), diffuse abdominal tenderness, and mild hepatic encephalopathy. Diagnostic paracentesis yields slightly turbid fluid with total WBC 620/mm³ (82% polymorphonuclear neutrophils = 508 PMNs/mm³) and SAAG 1.6 g/dL. What is the diagnosis and evidence-based initial treatment?","o":["Secondary surgical bowel perforation; withhold antibiotics until Gram stain turns positive","Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (PMN \u003e= 250/mm³); start IV third-generation cephalosporin (cefotaxime/ceftriaxone) plus IV albumin (1.5 g/kg day 1, 1.0 g/kg day 3)","Uncomplicated portal hypertension ascites; treat solely with oral spironolactone and furosemide","Tuberculous peritonitis solely; begin empirical ATT without third-generation cephalosporins"],"a":1,"e":"An ascitic fluid absolute PMN count \u003e= 250 cells/mm³ (here 508/mm³) establishes the diagnosis of Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP) and mandates immediate empiric IV third-generation cephalosporin therapy (covering E. coli, Klebsiella, and Streptococci) without waiting for culture. Co-administering IV albumin (1.5 g/kg on day 1 and 1.0 g/kg on day 3) markedly reduces hepatorenal syndrome and mortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01200","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Pediatrics and Neonatology","topic":"Holliday-Segar Maintenance Formula","q":"A 7-year-old child weighing 24 kg is admitted NPO following acute appendectomy after initial dehydration has been corrected. Using the Holliday-Segar (4-2-1) formula for pediatric maintenance IV fluid, what hourly infusion rate should be prescribed?","o":["96 mL/hour","48 mL/hour","64 mL/hour","74 mL/hour"],"a":2,"e":"Under the Holliday-Segar 4-2-1 rule: 4 mL/kg/h for the first 10 kg (10 × 4 = 40 mL/h) + 2 mL/kg/h for the next 10 kg (10 × 2 = 20 mL/h) + 1 mL/kg/h for every kg above 20 kg (4 × 1 = 4 mL/h). Total hourly maintenance rate = 40 + 20 + 4 = 64 mL/hour (1,536 mL/24 h).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01201","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Hematology and Transfusion Medicine","topic":"Acute Hemolytic Transfusion Reaction","q":"Ten minutes after starting a unit of packed red blood cells for postoperative anemia, a 45-year-old woman develops severe rigor, lumbar flank pain, fever (39.1°C), BP 82/48 mmHg, and reddish-brown urine in her Foley catheter bag. What is the immediate priority sequence?","o":["Administer IV protamine sulfate and wait 2 hours before notifying the blood bank","Slow the blood infusion rate by half and give IV diphenhydramine + acetaminophen while finishing the unit","Flush the existing blood tubing rapidly with 50 mL saline and switch to a second unit from the same fridge","STOP the transfusion immediately, keep IV vein open with NEW normal saline tubing (do not flush the blood line), verify patient/bag labels, send blood/urine + blood bag to the blood bank, and infuse IV 0.9% NaCl to protect renal perfusion"],"a":3,"e":"Fever, flank pain, shock, and hemoglobinuria within minutes of initiating PRBCs indicate an Acute Hemolytic Transfusion Reaction (most often clerical ABO incompatibility causing complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis, DIC, and ATN). The transfusion must be stopped IMMEDIATELY; existing blood tubing is disconnected (never flushed, which infuses another 10–20 mL of incompatible blood), a new saline line maintains renal perfusion (targeting urine output \u003e=1–2 mL/kg/h), and samples/bag are returned for DAT and crossmatch re-check.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01202","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Rheumatology and Clinical Immunology","topic":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)","q":"In a young woman evaluated for malar rash, polyarthritis, and proteinuria, which autoantibody is MOST specific for idiopathic Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE), and which autoantibody classically fluctuates with lupus nephritis disease activity?","o":["Anti-Smith (anti-Sm) is most specific; Anti-double-stranded DNA (anti-dsDNA) correlates with lupus nephritis activity","Anti-Jo-1 is most specific; Anti-mitochondrial antibody correlates with nephritis","Rheumatoid factor is most specific; Anti-Scl-70 correlates with nephritis","Anti-histone is most specific for idiopathic SLE; Anti-centromere correlates with nephritis"],"a":0,"e":"While ANA has \u003e95% sensitivity for SLE, anti-Smith (directed against snRNP core proteins) has the highest specificity (~99%) for SLE. Anti-dsDNA is also highly specific (~95%) and rising titers (often paired with falling C3/C4 complement) correlate closely with active lupus nephritis and flares. Anti-histone characterizes drug-induced lupus (e.g., isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01203","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Pulmonology and Respiratory Medicine","topic":"Spirometry Interpretation","q":"A 50-year-old stone-quarry worker with progressive exertional dyspnea and dry cough undergoes pulmonary function testing: FEV1 = 58% predicted, FVC = 56% predicted, FEV1/FVC ratio = 0.84 (84%), Total Lung Capacity (TLC) = 60% predicted, and DLCO = 52% predicted. Which ventilatory pattern do these results demonstrate?","o":["Extrapulmonary chest wall restriction with normal alveolar-capillary gas diffusion (normal DLCO)","Intrinsic parenchymal restrictive ventilatory defect (consistent with interstitial lung disease / silicosis)","Normal spirometry with isolated upper-airway vocal cord dysfunction","Severe obstructive airway disease (COPD)"],"a":1,"e":"A reduced Total Lung Capacity (TLC \u003c 80% predicted) confirms true restrictive lung disease, accompanied on spirometry by symmetrically reduced FVC and FEV1 with a preserved or elevated FEV1/FVC ratio (\u003e0.70–0.80, here 0.84). Additionally, a reduced DLCO (52% predicted) points to intrinsic parenchymal/interstitial pathology (such as silicosis in a quarry worker) rather than extrapulmonary chest-wall/neuromuscular restriction (where DLCO corrected for alveolar volume is normal).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01204","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Gastroenterology and Emergency Medicine","topic":"Esophageal Variceal Hemorrhage","q":"A 49-year-old man with known cirrhosis and esophageal varices presents with massive hematemesis (BP 88/54 mmHg, HR 116/min, Hb 6.4 g/dL). After airway protection and cautious crystalloid resuscitation, which pharmacological and endoscopic bundle is indicated?","o":["Aggressive PRBC transfusion to target Hb \u003e 12 g/dL, oral beta-blocker propranolol immediately, and delay endoscopy for 72 hours","Oral proton pump inhibitor alone without vasoactive or antibiotic therapy","Restrictive PRBC transfusion (target Hb 7–8 g/dL), IV octreotide (or terlipressin), prophylactic IV ceftriaxone, and urgent endoscopic variceal band ligation (EVL) within 12 hours","High-dose IV heparin and immediate Sengstaken-Blakemore balloon placement before endoscopy"],"a":2,"e":"In acute variceal hemorrhage, over-transfusion increases portal venous pressure and rebleeding; a restrictive PRBC target (Hb 7–8 g/dL) improves survival. Immediate splanchnic vasoconstrictors (IV octreotide or terlipressin) reduce portal pressure, prophylactic IV ceftriaxone prevents SBP/bacterial sepsis, and urgent upper GI endoscopy within 12 hours allows definitive Endoscopic Variceal Ligation (EVL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01205","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Community Medicine and Preventive Healthcare","topic":"Universal Immunization Programme (UIP India)","q":"Match each vaccine in India\u0027s Universal Immunization Programme (UIP) with its scheduled age and route/site: (1) BCG, (2) Pentavalent (DPT+HepB+Hib), (3) Measles-Rubella (MR-1), (4) Fractional IPV (fIPV). Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-At birth ID left arm; 2-9 months SC right arm; 3-6, 10, 14 weeks IM thigh; 4-5 years oral","1-9 months SC right arm; 2-At birth ID left arm; 3-6, 10, 14 weeks IM thigh; 4-16 months oral drops","1-6, 10, 14 weeks IM thigh; 2-At birth ID left arm; 3-9 months SC right arm; 4-At birth IM gluteal","1-At birth, 0.1 mL (0.05 mL \u003c1 mo) intradermal left upper arm; 2-6, 10, 14 weeks, 0.5 mL IM anterolateral mid-thigh; 3-9 completed months, 0.5 mL subcutaneous right upper arm; 4-6 \u0026 14 weeks (plus 9 mo booster), 0.1 mL intradermal right upper arm"],"a":3,"e":"Under India\u0027s UIP: BCG is given at birth (0.05 mL up to 1 month, 0.1 mL beyond 1 month) intradermally in the left upper arm; Pentavalent is given at 6, 10, 14 weeks IM in the anterolateral mid-thigh; MR-1 is given at 9 completed months (with Vitamin A and JE-1 in endemic districts) subcutaneously in the right upper arm; fractional IPV (0.1 mL) is given intradermally in the right upper arm at 6 and 14 weeks (and 9 months per updated UIP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01206","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Cardiology and Critical Care","topic":"Atrial Fibrillation Management","q":"A 71-year-old woman with hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus presents with new-onset atrial fibrillation (HR 118/min, BP 136/82 mmHg, no chest pain or pulmonary edema). What is her CHA2DS2-VASc score, and what long-term stroke prevention strategy is indicated?","o":["CHA2DS2-VASc = 4 (Age 65–74 [+1], Female [+1], HTN [+1], Diabetes [+1]); initiate long-term oral anticoagulation (DOAC or warfarin)","CHA2DS2-VASc = 1; withhold anticoagulation if rate is controlled below 100/min","CHA2DS2-VASc = 6; perform immediate unsynchronized defibrillation at 360 J","CHA2DS2-VASc = 2; prescribe aspirin 75 mg monotherapy as equivalent to anticoagulation"],"a":0,"e":"CHA2DS2-VASc assigns 1 point each for CHF, Hypertension, Age 65–74, Diabetes, Vascular disease, and Female sex, and 2 points each for Age \u003e= 75 and prior Stroke/TIA. This 71-year-old woman scores 4 (well above the \u003e=2 threshold in men / \u003e=3 in women), mandating oral anticoagulation (DOAC preferred over warfarin in non-valvular AF) regardless of whether rate or rhythm control is used.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01207","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Internal Medicine and Acute Care","topic":"Thyroid Storm","q":"A 36-year-old woman with untreated Graves\u0027 disease presents post-appendectomy with temperature 40.2°C, HR 164/min, agitation/delirium, and jaundice (Burch-Wartofsky score \u003e 60, Thyroid Storm). Why MUST propylthiouracil (PTU) or methimazole be administered AT LEAST 1 hour BEFORE Lugol\u0027s iodine / potassium iodide solution?","o":["Thionamides destroy circulating free T3 instantaneously within 5 minutes","Administering iodine before blocking thyroid peroxidase with a thionamide supplies substrate for massive new thyroid hormone synthesis (Jod-Basedow phenomenon), worsening thyroid storm; thionamide first ensures iodine solely blocks hormone release (Wolff-Chaikoff effect)","Iodine chemically precipitates propranolol in the stomach if given first","Lugol\u0027s iodine induces acute adrenal insufficiency unless PTU is given first"],"a":1,"e":"Thyroid storm management requires strict sequencing: (1) Beta-blocker (propranolol) immediately to control HR and reduce peripheral T4-\u003eT3 conversion, (2) Thionamide (PTU preferred acutely because it also inhibits peripheral T4-\u003eT3 deiodination) to block thyroid peroxidase organification, (3) Iodine (Lugol\u0027s/SSKI) delayed \u003e=1 hour AFTER the thionamide to inhibit preformed hormone release without fueling new hormone synthesis, and (4) Hydrocortisone + active cooling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01208","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry and Fluid-Electrolyte Medicine","topic":"Hyperglycemia-Corrected Sodium","q":"A patient with hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) presents with a measured serum sodium of 126 mEq/L and serum glucose of 600 mg/dL. Using the standard Katz correction factor (add 1.6 mEq/L to measured Na+ for every 100 mg/dL rise in glucose above 100 mg/dL), what is the patient\u0027s corrected serum sodium concentration?","o":["126 mEq/L (no correction needed in hyperglycemia)","118 mEq/L (severe true hyponatremia)","134 mEq/L (translactional/hyperosmolar shift, true corrected Na+ is near-normal/mildly low)","142 mEq/L (severe hypernatremia)"],"a":2,"e":"Elevated extracellular glucose draws water out of cells osmotically, diluting measured serum sodium without true total-body sodium excess/deficit relative to water loss. Applying Katz\u0027s formula: Corrected Na+ = Measured Na+ + 1.6 × [(Glucose − 100) / 100] = 126 + 1.6 × [(600 − 100) / 100] = 126 + (1.6 × 5) = 126 + 8 = 134 mEq/L. (Note: at glucose \u003e400 mg/dL, some clinicians also check the Hillier 2.4 factor, yielding 138 mEq/L, confirming absence of true severe hyponatremia).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01209","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Neurology and Critical Care","topic":"Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS)","q":"A 31-year-old man develops symmetric ascending lower-limb weakness, paresthesias, and absent deep tendon reflexes 12 days after watery diarrhea. Lumbar puncture reveals CSF protein 142 mg/dL with only 2 lymphocytes/µL (albuminocytologic dissociation). Which bedside parameter best detects impending respiratory failure before arterial hypoxia appears, and what are the evidence-based disease-modifying therapies?","o":["Serial serum creatine kinase levels; treat with pyridostigmine","Peak expiratory flow once weekly; wait for hypercapnic coma before ICU transfer","Pulse oximetry SpO2 alone; treat with high-dose oral prednisolone monotherapy","Serial Forced Vital Capacity (FVC \u003c 15–20 mL/kg) and Negative Inspiratory Force (NIF worse than -30 cmH2O) (\u002720/30/40 rule\u0027); treat with IVIG or plasmapheresis (NOT corticosteroids)"],"a":3,"e":"In Guillain-Barré syndrome, neuromuscular diaphragmatic fatigue often progresses silently until sudden respiratory arrest; pulse oximetry remains normal until alveolar hypoventilation is critical! Frequent bedside FVC (\u003c20 mL/kg), NIF (\u003c30 cmH2O magnitude), and maximal expiratory pressure (\u003c40 cmH2O) guide timely elective intubation. High-dose IVIG or plasma exchange equally hasten recovery, whereas corticosteroids are ineffective (and oral steroids worsen outcomes) in GBS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01210","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Clinical Pharmacology and Toxicology","topic":"Serotonin Syndrome vs NMS","q":"A 28-year-old patient taking fluoxetine receives IV linezolid for MRSA pneumonia and within 6 hours develops agitation, diaphoresis, tachycardia, hyperthermia (39.4°C), hyperactive bowel sounds, mydriasis, and inducible ankle/ocular clonus with hyperreflexia. Which diagnosis and specific antidote apply?","o":["Serotonin Syndrome; stop serotonergic agents, provide cooling/benzodiazepines, and administer cyproheptadine (5-HT2 antagonist)","Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS); administer bromocriptine and dantrolene for lead-pipe rigidity and hyporeflexia","Anticholinergic toxidrome; administer haloperidol","Malignant Hyperthermia; administer IV atropine"],"a":0,"e":"Linezolid is a reversible monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI); combined with an SSRI (fluoxetine), it rapidly precipitates Serotonin Syndrome within hours. Hallmark neuromuscular features distinguishing Serotonin Syndrome from NMS are HYPERREFLEXIA, inducible/spontaneous CLONUS, and hyperactive bowel sounds (whereas NMS develops over days after dopamine-blockers with \u0027lead-pipe\u0027 rigidity, hyporeflexia/bradyreflexia, and markedly elevated CK). Treatment includes stopping offending drugs, cooling, benzodiazepines, and cyproheptadine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01211","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Orthopedics and Trauma Surgery","topic":"Acute Compartment Syndrome","q":"Eight hours after closed reduction and below-knee casting of a tibial shaft fracture, a 26-year-old man complains of escalating, severe throbbing leg pain unrelieved by IV morphine. His dorsalis pedis pulse is still palpable (BP 120/78 mmHg), and passive dorsiflexion of his great toe elicits excruciating calf pain. What is the immediate priority?","o":["Split and remove the cast and circumferential dressings down to skin immediately, place the limb at heart level (do NOT elevate above heart), and call orthopedics for urgent compartment pressure measurement (delta-P \u003c= 30 mmHg) / emergent fasciotomy","Elevate the leg high on three pillows, apply ice packs, and wait until the dorsalis pedis pulse disappears before removing the cast","Administer higher-dose PCA morphine and reassure the patient that palpable pulses exclude compartment syndrome","Tighten an elastic crepe bandage over the cast to reduce swelling"],"a":0,"e":"Acute compartment syndrome occurs when intracompartmental pressure compromises capillary perfusion long before arterial occlusion occurs—so distal pulses remain palpable until late irreversible ischemia! Pain out of proportion and severe pain on PASSIVE STRETCH of the involved compartment muscles are the earliest reliable clinical indicators. Immediate cast/padding bivalving down to skin drops pressure significantly, positioning at heart level preserves arteriolar inflow (excessive elevation worsens ischemia), and delta-P (DBP − compartment pressure) \u003c= 30 mmHg mandates emergent fasciotomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01212","course":"B.Sc Physician Assistant / Physician Associate","subject":"Pediatrics and Neonatology","topic":"Physiological vs Pathological Hyperbilirubinemia","q":"A term neonate delivered 14 hours ago develops visible scleral and facial jaundice with a total serum bilirubin of 11.5 mg/dL (unconjugated 11.0 mg/dL, conjugated 0.5 mg/dL) and a positive direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test. Why is this classified as PATHOLOGICAL rather than physiological jaundice?","o":["Jaundice appearing within the first 24 hours of life (alongside positive Coombs/hemolysis) is always pathological and requires urgent phototherapy/hemolysis workup to prevent acute bilirubin encephalopathy (kernicterus)","Physiological jaundice always peaks at 12 hours of life above 15 mg/dL","Conjugated bilirubin of 0.5 mg/dL indicates complete biliary atresia","Term newborns should never have unconjugated bilirubin above 1.0 mg/dL at 14 hours"],"a":0,"e":"Physiological neonatal jaundice typically appears after 24–36 hours of life, peaks around days 3–5 (\u003c12–15 mg/dL in term infants), and resolves by 1–2 weeks. Any visible jaundice in the FIRST 24 HOURS of life, rapid bilirubin rise (\u003e5 mg/dL/day), direct/conjugated bilirubin \u003e2 mg/dL (or \u003e20% of total), or prolonged jaundice \u003e14 days is pathological—most commonly ABO/Rh hemolytic disease within the first 24 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01213","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Prosthetic Gait Analysis \u0026 Checkout","topic":"Stance vs Swing Phase Deviations","q":"A unilateral left transfemoral amputee demonstrates marked lateral trunk bending toward the prosthetic (left) side during mid-stance on the prosthetic limb. Which prosthetic or anatomical factor is the MOST likely cause of this stance-phase deviation?","o":["Prosthesis is excessively long with overly high knee extension friction","Insufficient lateral socket wall adduction (inadequate support for femur/gluteus medius) or a prosthesis that is too short","Excessive heel cushion stiffness causing foot slap at heel strike","Medially inset prosthetic foot creating a varus moment"],"a":1,"e":"Lateral trunk bending (Trendelenburg-type lean) toward the prosthetic side during stance reduces the external hip adduction torque required of the hip abductors. Prosthetic causes include inadequate lateral wall adduction failing to maintain the femur in adduction for optimal gluteus medius length-tension, a painful lateral distal femur/distal lateral socket pressure, or a prosthesis that is too short.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01214","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Transfemoral Prosthetics","topic":"Quadrilateral vs Ischial Containment (IC) Socket","q":"How does an Ischial Containment (IC / narrow-ML) transfemoral prosthetic socket biomechanically differ from a traditional Quadrilateral socket in controlling the femur and pelvis during stance phase?","o":["The Quadrilateral socket eliminates the anterior Scarpa\u0027s triangle bulge to reduce anteroposterior pressure","The IC socket widens the mediolateral dimension and supports the ischial tuberosity strictly outside on a flat horizontal posterior shelf","The IC socket has a narrower mediolateral (ML) dimension that encapsulates the ischial tuberosity and medial ischial ramus inside the socket wall, locking the femur in physiological adduction for better gluteus medius leverage","The IC socket relies exclusively on distal end-bearing over the cut femoral shaft"],"a":2,"e":"A Quadrilateral socket uses a narrow AP dimension (Scarpa\u0027s bulge pushing the ischium backward onto a flat horizontal posterior shelf) with a relatively wide ML dimension that allows lateral femoral drifting. By contrast, an Ischial Containment (IC) socket narrows the ML dimension and raises the medial/posterior wall to enclose the ischium and ramus (\u0027bony lock\u0027), maintaining femoral adduction and optimizing abductor stability.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01215","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Prosthetic Knee \u0026 Foot Components","topic":"Polycentric (4-Bar) Knee Biomechanics","q":"A 40-year-old active unilateral knee-disarticulation amputee requires a mechanical prosthetic knee that maximizes stance-phase stability without lengthening the thigh segment excessively when sitting, while enhancing toe clearance in swing phase. Why is a polycentric (4-bar linkage) knee unit ideally suited for this prescription?","o":["It locks rigidly in 0 degrees extension during both stance and swing phases","Its 4-bar linkage places the Instantaneous Center of Rotation (ICR) high and posterior at heel strike (geometric stance stability) and physically shortens the shin relative to the thigh during mid-swing flexion (improving toe clearance)","It requires continuous external electrical battery power to bend during sitting","Its axis sits 4 cm anterior to the TKA alignment line to encourage rapid knee flexion at heel strike"],"a":1,"e":"A 4-bar polycentric knee creates a moving Instantaneous Center of Rotation (ICR) positioned superior and posterior to the anatomical knee at full extension—keeping the GRF well anterior to the ICR for high stance stability with minimal hip extensor effort. During swing flexion, linkage geometry folds and shortens the effective shin length (improving swing toe clearance) and keeps the distal thigh cosmetic when seated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01216","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Partial Foot \u0026 Ankle Disarticulation Prosthetics","topic":"End-Bearing \u0026 Suspension Design","q":"A patient with a traumatic forefoot injury undergoes a classic Syme\u0027s ankle disarticulation preserving the plantar heel pad over the distal tibia/fibula. What primary weight-bearing advantage does Syme\u0027s amputation offer over a standard transtibial amputation, and what socket design accommodates the bulbous distal malleolar flare during donning?","o":["Complete absence of end-bearing requiring a thigh lacer; open-ended distal socket","True distal end-bearing on the tough heel pad (eliminating need for proximal patellar-tendon cuff) combined with self-suspension above the bulbous distal end via a medial removable window or expandable inner liner","Requires a motorized ankle joint to replace lost gastrocnemius push-off; standard PTB supracondylar strap","Allows barefoot walking indefinitely on rough terrain without any prosthetic foot"],"a":1,"e":"Preserving the specialized, shock-absorbing plantar heel fat pad over the distal tibia allows Syme\u0027s amputees to bear full body weight directly through the residual limb end (even taking brief indoor steps without a prosthesis). Because the distal malleolar flare is wider than the supramalleolar ankle, a medial removable door/window (Canadian Syme\u0027s) or double-wall expandable liner permits donning while locking securely for suspension.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01217","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Prosthetic Componentry \u0026 Functional K-Levels","topic":"SACH vs Dynamic-Response (ESAR) Foot","q":"A 33-year-old traumatic unilateral transtibial amputee works outdoors on uneven construction terrain, walks at variable speeds, and participates in recreational jogging (Medicare Functional Classification Level K3-K4). Which prosthetic foot category is most appropriate compared with a basic Solid Ankle Cushion Heel (SACH) foot?","o":["Rigid wood-keel SACH foot only","Single-axis foot with locked rigid heel bumper","Non-articulated solid cosmetic rubber foot","Dynamic-Response / Energy-Storing-and-Releasing (ESAR) carbon-fiber foot"],"a":3,"e":"Energy-Storing-and-Releasing (ESAR / dynamic-response) carbon-fiber feet deflect elastically during mid-to-late stance loading and release stored strain energy at pre-swing push-off, reducing energy expenditure, improving step-length symmetry, and adapting to variable cadence and uneven terrain (ideal for K3-K4 ambulators). SACH feet lack dynamic forefoot return and are suited to K1-K2 limited household/community walkers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01218","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Transtibial Prosthetic Alignment \u0026 Gait","topic":"Early-Stance Knee Buckling (Excessive Knee Flexion)","q":"During dynamic alignment checkout of a new transtibial PTB prosthesis, the patient experiences sudden excessive knee flexion (feeling of knee instability/buckling) immediately between heel strike and foot flat. Which prosthetic alignment or component error causes this early-stance knee flexion moment?","o":["Heel cushion is too stiff/hard, foot is set in excessive dorsiflexion, or socket is aligned too far anteriorly over the foot","Socket brim is trimmed too low posteriorly","Prosthetic foot is inset too far medially","Heel cushion is too soft, foot is set in excessive plantarflexion, or socket is placed too far posteriorly"],"a":0,"e":"At heel strike/loading response, an excessively stiff heel cushion resists plantarflexion, holding the tibia angled forward and shifting the Ground Reaction Force (GRF) vector POSTERIOR to the knee joint center—generating an external knee flexion moment (buckling) and excessive anterior-distal tibial pressure. Excessive dorsiflexion or anterior socket displacement produces the exact same posterior-to-knee GRF shift.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01219","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Prosthetic Suspension Technologies","topic":"Elevated Vacuum Suspension (EVS)","q":"A bilateral dysvascular transtibial amputee experiences significant daily residual-limb volume shrinkage (\u0027milking\u0027 and distal pistoning) with a silicone shuttle-lock liner. How does upgrading to an Elevated Vacuum Suspension (EVS) system address both pistoning and residual limb volume fluctuation?","o":["EVS relies on a metal pin screwed into the distal tibial cortex","EVS actively draws a controlled subatmospheric vacuum (-15 to -20 inHg) between a total-surface-bearing socket and liner, maintaining intimate total-contact fit, minimizing pistoning, and drawing interstitial fluid to stabilize limb volume","EVS eliminates the need for any sealing suspension sleeve or liner","EVS inflates positive air pressure around the tibia to shrink limb diameter further"],"a":1,"e":"Elevated Vacuum Suspension uses a mechanical or electronic pump with a sealing sleeve/liner to pull active negative pressure inside the socket. Unlike distal pin-lock suspension (which pulls distally on soft tissue causing \u0027milking\u0027), EVS creates uniform circumferential negative pressure that virtually eliminates vertical pistoning, improves proprioception, and stabilizes daily limb fluid volume.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01220","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Transfemoral Prosthetic Gait Checkout","topic":"Terminal Swing Impact \u0026 Medial Heel Whip","q":"During dynamic checkout of a unilateral transfemoral prosthesis with a constant-friction mechanical knee, the prosthetist observes two swing-phase deviations: (1) an abrupt audible snap as the shin reaches full extension just before heel strike (\u0027terminal swing impact\u0027), and (2) the prosthetic heel swings MEDIALLY at toe-off (\u0027medial heel whip\u0027). Which mechanical/alignment faults cause these two deviations respectively?","o":["Excessive knee friction causes terminal swing impact; internally rotated knee axis causes medial heel whip","Overly short prosthesis causes terminal swing impact; plantarflexed foot causes medial heel whip","Insufficient knee friction / overly taut extension aid causes terminal swing impact; EXCESSIVE EXTERNAL rotation of the prosthetic knee axis (or external socket rotation on donning) causes medial heel whip","Weak hip abductors cause terminal swing impact; loose ankle bolt causes medial heel whip"],"a":2,"e":"Terminal swing impact occurs when the prosthetic shin swings forward too rapidly and slams into the extension stop because knee friction/extension dampening is insufficient or the extension-assist spring is too strong. A medial heel whip (heel swinging medially as the knee flexes in early swing) occurs when the knee bolt axis is aligned in excessive EXTERNAL rotation (often from donning the socket externally rotated).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01221","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Upper Limb Prosthetics","topic":"Dual-Control \u0026 Elbow-Lock Cable Mechanics","q":"In a transhumeral (above-elbow) body-powered prosthesis equipped with a mechanical locking elbow and a dual-control cable system, which sequence of prosthetic-side shoulder motions is required to LOCK or UNLOCK the elbow unit before flexing the forearm or opening the terminal hook?","o":["Elbow flexion and hook opening occur simultaneously with one motion without an elbow lock","Contralateral neck lateral bending pulls the elbow-lock cable","Forearm pronation rotates a ratchet inside the humeral socket","Simultaneous ipsilateral shoulder depression, extension, and slight abduction pulls the anterior elbow-lock cable (~1.25 cm excursion) to alternately lock and unlock the elbow; once locked, glenohumeral flexion/scapular abduction operates the terminal device"],"a":3,"e":"Transhumeral body-powered prostheses use a two-cable dual-control setup. The anterior elbow-lock cable is tensioned by a quick downward/backward movement—ipsilateral shoulder depression, extension, and slight abduction—cycling an alternator mechanism (\u0027pull to lock, pull again to unlock\u0027). With the elbow unlocked, glenohumeral flexion flexes the elbow; once locked at the desired angle, the same flexion cable opens the split hook.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01222","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Pre-Prosthetic Care \u0026 Residual Limb Management","topic":"Elastic Compression Bandaging","q":"While wrapping a healing transtibial residual limb with a 10 cm elastic bandage prior to prosthetic casting, what wrapping pattern and tension gradient must be used to promote conical shaping and avoid ischemic tourniquet injury?","o":["Oblique FIGURE-OF-8 turns with MAXIMUM tension applied distally over the stump end, progressively decreasing tension proximally, and never ending with tight horizontal circular turns","Tight circular circumferential turns with highest tension at the proximal thigh","Leave the distal end completely unwrapped and wrap tightly around the popliteal crease","Wrap only once per week without re-wrapping"],"a":0,"e":"Effective stump bandaging uses oblique figure-of-8 turns rather than horizontal circular turns (which act like a venous tourniquet, causing distal bulbous edema and skin necrosis). Tension must be greatest distally to assist lymphatic/venous return and shape a smooth conical residual limb, tapering progressively looser proximally, and re-wrapped every 4-6 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01223","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Advanced Upper Limb Prosthetics","topic":"Targeted Muscle Reinnervation (TMR)","q":"In an upper-limb transhumeral or shoulder-disarticulation amputee undergoing Targeted Muscle Reinnervation (TMR), what dual clinical and prosthetic benefits are achieved by transferring severed residual median, ulnar, and radial nerves into spared motor nerve branches of proximal muscles (e.g., pectoralis or biceps/triceps)?","o":["Converts a myoelectric prosthesis into a purely passive cosmetic arm","Creates distinct, physiologically intuitive surface EMG signal sites enabling simultaneous multi-joint myoelectric control (elbow, wrist, hand) AND provides functional motor targets for regenerating axons to prevent painful neuromas and reduce phantom limb pain","Requires permanent systemic immunosuppression like hand transplantation","Completely eliminates the need for any socket or harness by growing a biological hand"],"a":1,"e":"TMR surgically denervates redundant proximal muscle segments and reinnervates them with major amputated peripheral nerves (median, radial, ulnar). The target muscle acts as a biological amplifier producing independent, intuitive surface EMG signals (thinking \u0027close hand\u0027 activates the median-reinnervated muscle site for seamless control), while giving severed sensory/motor nerve endings a healthy end-organ target—markedly decreasing symptomatic neuromas and phantom limb pain.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01224","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Transtibial Gait \u0026 Alignment Checkout","topic":"Knee Hyperextension (Genu Recurvatum)","q":"During gait checkout of a unilateral transtibial amputee wearing a PTB prosthesis, the knee snaps backward into hyperextension (genu recurvatum) during mid-to-late stance, accompanied by painful pressure on the anterior-distal tibia and posterior-proximal popliteal brim. Which prosthetic alignment or component fault causes this deviation?","o":["Excessive 15 degrees of initial socket flexion","Prosthetic foot placed in 10 degrees of out-toeing","Heel cushion too soft, foot set in excessive plantarflexion, socket aligned too far posteriorly over the foot, or worn-out forefoot keel (\u0027drop-off\u0027)","Heel cushion too stiff, foot excessively dorsiflexed, or socket aligned too far anteriorly"],"a":2,"e":"Setting the foot in excessive plantarflexion, using an overly soft heel cushion, placing the socket too far posteriorly relative to the foot, or lacking forefoot keel resistance shifts the Ground Reaction Force (GRF) vector excessively ANTERIOR to the knee joint center during stance—generating an external knee extension moment that snaps the knee into hyperextension (genu recurvatum).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01225","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"High-Level Lower Limb Prosthetics","topic":"Canadian Hip Disarticulation Alignment","q":"In a Canadian-style hip disarticulation prosthesis (for a patient with zero residual hip musculature), how must the mechanical hip joint and prosthetic knee joint be aligned relative to the sagittal weight-bearing Trochanter-Knee-Ankle (TKA) reference line to achieve passive upright stance stability?","o":["Hip joint placed posterior to the weight line; knee joint placed anterior to the weight line","Mechanical hip joint positioned ANTERIOR to the weight-bearing line (resting backward against a posterior extension stop) and mechanical knee joint positioned POSTERIOR to the weight line, creating passive extension moments at both hip and knee during stance","Both hip and knee joints aligned directly on the anterior toe tip","Hip joint locked permanently at 45 degrees flexion for walking"],"a":1,"e":"Without hip extensors or knee extensors, the Canadian hip disarticulation prosthesis relies entirely on sagittal alignment geometry. Placing the hip axis ANTERIOR to the weight line causes gravity/GRF to push the socket backward against a posterior hip extension bumper (stabilizing the hip passively), while placing the knee axis POSTERIOR to the weight line keeps the GRF anterior to the knee (passive knee extension stability).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01226","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Extremity Prosthetics","topic":"Patellar Tendon Bearing (PTB) Biomechanics","q":"In a Patellar Tendon Bearing (PTB) transtibial prosthetic socket, which pair consists exclusively of pressure-TOLERANT anatomical areas capable of accepting weight-bearing loads?","o":["Fibular head and hamstring tendons","Tibial crest and fibular head","Anterior distal end of tibia and patella","Patellar tendon and medial tibial flare"],"a":3,"e":"The PTB socket concentrates axial and stabilizing forces onto pressure-tolerant soft-tissue/flat bony regions: the patellar tendon (between inferior pole of patella and tibial tubercle), medial tibial flare, pretibial muscle compartment, gastrocnemius-soleus belly, and shaft of the fibula. Relief must be provided over sharp bony prominences and superficial nerves.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01227","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Prosthetic Gait Analysis and Alignment","topic":"Sagittal Plane Alignment Errors","q":"During dynamic alignment of a transtibial prosthesis with a SACH foot, the amputee exhibits excessive knee flexion immediately after heel strike (loading response) and complains of feeling as though the knee is buckling forward. Which prosthetic alignment or component error most likely explains this deviation?","o":["Prosthetic foot placed too far posterior relative to the socket (or excessive socket flexion / overly firm SACH heel cushion)","Excessive plantarflexion of the prosthetic foot","Prosthetic foot placed too far anterior relative to the socket","Overly soft SACH heel cushion"],"a":0,"e":"At heel strike, a posteriorly inset foot, excessive socket flexion (\u003e5°), dorsiflexed foot, or an overly stiff SACH heel cushion prevents rapid plantarflexion of the foot toward the floor, shifting the ground reaction force vector further posterior to the knee joint axis and generating an excessive external knee-flexion moment (instability/buckling).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01228","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Extremity Prosthetics","topic":"Ischial Containment vs Quadrilateral Sockets","q":"Compared with a traditional quadrilateral transfemoral socket, how does an Ischial Containment (IC / Narrow M-L) socket improve pelvic stability and reduce lateral trunk bending during single-limb stance?","o":["It eliminates total contact distally to allow vertical pistoning during midstance","It encloses the ischial tuberosity and medial ramus within a narrow mediolateral profile, creating a bony lock that maintains the femur in anatomical adduction","It widens the mediolateral dimension and lowers the lateral wall below the greater trochanter","It provides a wide horizontal posterior shelf on which the ischial tuberosity rests externally while abducting the femur"],"a":1,"e":"The Ischial Containment (IC) socket has a narrower mediolateral than anteroposterior dimension and brings the medial brim superiorly and medially to encapsulate the ischial tuberosity and descending ramus. Combined with lateral wall contouring, this bony lock prevents lateral shifting of the socket, holds the femur in physiological adduction (preserving gluteus medius length-tension), and minimizes Trendelenburg/lateral trunk lean.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01229","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Spinal Orthotics","topic":"CTLSO vs TLSO Selection","q":"A 12-year-old girl (pre-menarchal, Risser sign 1) presents with Adolescent Idiopathic Scoliosis (AIS) measuring a 32-degree right thoracic curve with the apex at T6. Which spinal orthosis is biomechanically indicated to control a curve with an apex above T7–T8?","o":["Boston underarm TLSO (Thoracolumbosacral Orthosis)","Charleston nighttime bending brace alone for a high T6 apex","Milwaukee CTLSO (Cervicothoracolumbosacral Orthosis)","Soft elastic lumbosacral corset"],"a":2,"e":"Standard bracing guidelines for progressive AIS in skeletally immature patients (Risser 0–2, Cobb angle 25–40 degrees) dictate selecting a CTLSO (Milwaukee brace) when the curve apex lies above T7–T8, because underarm TLSOs (like the Boston brace) terminate below the axilla and cannot apply effective transverse corrective pads above T7–T8.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01230","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Extremity Orthotics","topic":"Ground Reaction AFO (GRAFO)","q":"A 10-year-old child with spastic diplegic cerebral palsy walks in crouch gait with persistent knee flexion throughout stance due to weak triceps surae (calf) and hamstring tightness, but has sufficient passive ankle dorsiflexion to neutral and no fixed knee flexion contracture \u003e10°. How does a rigid Ground Reaction AFO (GRAFO) with an anterior tibial shell stabilize the knee in midstance?","o":["By applying an anteriorly directed force at the calf to induce knee flexion","By unlocking the knee joint at terminal stance to allow rapid knee flexion","By dynamically plantarflexing the ankle into 30 degrees equinus during swing phase only","By blocking ankle dorsiflexion beyond neutral and applying a posteriorly directed force at the proximal anterior tibia, shifting the ground reaction force anterior to the knee axis to create an external knee-extension moment"],"a":3,"e":"In crouch gait, weak plantarflexors fail to restrain forward tibial progression over the planted foot, keeping the ground reaction force (GRF) posterior to the knee. A rigid GRAFO locks the ankle near neutral/slight plantarflexion and uses a stiff anterior tibial shell to arrest tibial advancement in midstance, redirecting the GRF anterior to the knee joint and generating a passive knee-extension moment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01231","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Materials Science and Fabrication Technology","topic":"Thermoplastics vs Thermosetting Resins","q":"During fabrication of a definitive transfemoral socket, why can a polypropylene check socket be spot-relieved later using a heat gun, whereas a cured carbon-fiber/acrylic resin laminated definitive socket cannot be reshaped by reheating?","o":["Polypropylene is a thermoplastic polymer whose linear/branched chains soften reversibly on heating, whereas acrylic/epoxy resin is a thermosetting polymer that forms irreversible covalent cross-links during curing","Acrylic resin is a thermoplastic elastomer that only softens below 0°C","Carbon fiber reacts with water vapor in the positive cast to form ceramic bonds, whereas polypropylene is metallic","Polypropylene undergoes irreversible vulcanization upon cooling, whereas acrylic resin melts at 60°C"],"a":0,"e":"Thermoplastics (polypropylene, polyethylene, copolymer, PETG) soften repeatedly when heated above their glass-transition/melting range and harden upon cooling, allowing post-fabrication heat relief or flaring. Thermosetting lamination resins (acrylic, polyester, epoxy) undergo exothermic chemical polymerization creating permanent 3D covalent cross-links; reheating causes charring/delamination rather than plastic remolding.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01232","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Clinical Prescription and Componentry","topic":"Pylon Length Calculation","q":"A K3-level unilateral transtibial amputee has a sound-side medial tibial plateau (MTP) to floor height (with shoe) of 460 mm. On the prosthetic side, the socket MTP-to-distal-base height (including 4-hole pyramid adapter) is 215 mm, and the dynamic-response carbon-fiber foot build height (including foot shell, shoe heel compensation, and distal pyramid receiver) is 125 mm. If each tube clamp engages 30 mm of pylon overlap at both the proximal and distal ends (60 mm total overlap inside clamps), what exact total length of 30 mm titanium pylon must be cut?","o":["240 mm","180 mm","120 mm","150 mm"],"a":1,"e":"First find the exposed gap between the proximal and distal adapter faces: Required total height (460 mm) − Socket height (215 mm) − Foot build height (125 mm) = 120 mm exposed pylon span. Because 30 mm of the pylon seats inside the proximal tube clamp and 30 mm seats inside the distal tube clamp (60 mm total insertion), total pylon cut length = 120 mm + 30 mm + 30 mm = 180 mm.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01233","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Transfemoral Prosthetic Gait Analysis","topic":"Abducted Gait (Widened Walking Base)","q":"A unilateral right transfemoral amputee walks with a persistently widened base of support (right prosthetic leg held abducted away from midline throughout BOTH stance and swing phases). Which prosthetic alignment or socket-fit fault is MOST commonly responsible for this abducted gait?","o":["Prosthesis is 2 cm too short with loose lateral wall","Excessive 15-degree internal rotation of the prosthetic foot","Prosthesis is too long, OR painful pressure on the medial pubic/adductor ramus from an improperly contoured medial brim (prompting the patient to abduct away from medial pain)","Too little knee extension friction during late swing"],"a":2,"e":"An abducted gait (wide walking base) in transfemoral amputees commonly stems from: (1) a prosthesis that is too long (patient abducts to clear the floor without vaulting), (2) a high or sharp medial socket wall pinching the adductor roll/ischial-pubic ramus (patient abducts the leg away from the painful medial brim), (3) insufficient lateral wall adduction/support, or (4) hip abduction contracture/insecurity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01234","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Partial Foot \u0026 Midfoot Amputation Prosthetics","topic":"Forefoot Lever Arm Loss \u0026 Equinovarus Prevention","q":"A 57-year-old diabetic patient undergoes a Chopart (midtarsal) amputation preserving only the talus and calcaneus, leaving the gastrocnemius-soleus unopposed by anterior dorsiflexors. Without specialized prosthetic management, what progressive deformity develops, and how does a Chopart clamshell AFO-prosthesis restore late-stance push-off?","o":["Pes planus; treat with isolated medial heel wedge","Calcaneovalgus deformity; treat with soft open-toed sandal only","Genu recurvatum; treat with a simple silicone toe cap only","Severe EQUINOVARUS deformity with painful anterior distal weight-bearing breakdown; prevented and restored using a rigid custom ankle-encompassing clamshell prosthesis (or custom carbon-fiber AFO with anterior tibial shell and full-length toe filler) to block plantarflexion and reinstate the forefoot lever arm"],"a":3,"e":"Chopart disarticulation removes all midfoot/forefoot bones along with the tibialis anterior insertion, leaving the powerful Achilles tendon unopposed—rapidly pulling the calcaneus into equinovarus so the patient walks painfully on the anterior-inferior scar. Even a simple toe filler slips off without ankle control; only an ankle-spanning rigid clamshell or anterior-shell carbon AFO-prosthesis stabilizes equinus and restores the anterior lever arm for smooth third-rocker progression.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01235","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Advanced Prosthetic Technology","topic":"Stance-Control Hydraulic Yielding","q":"How does a Microprocessor-Controlled Knee (MPK, such as a hydraulic C-Leg) allow a transfemoral amputee to descend stairs step-over-step and walk down steep ramps safely compared with a conventional single-axis constant-friction mechanical knee?","o":["Real-time ankle/shin load sensors and knee-angle gyroscopes sample 50-100 times/second and instantaneously increase hydraulic flexion resistance (\u0027stance-yielding\u0027) under weight-bearing—allowing controlled gradual knee bend without sudden collapse","It permanently locks the knee rigidly at 0 degrees extension during ramp descent","It disconnects the prosthetic foot completely during downward steps","It generates active upward stair-climbing torque solely from a solar battery"],"a":0,"e":"Conventional mechanical constant-friction knees buckle if loaded while flexed, forcing amputees to descend stairs one step at a time with a straight knee. An MPK continuously monitors sagittal moment, axial load, and knee angular velocity at 50-100 Hz; during ramp or step-over-step stair descent, high hydraulic flexion resistance yields smoothly under body weight (\u0027stance-yielding\u0027) while switching rapidly to low resistance once unweighted in swing—dramatically reducing stumbles and falls.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01236","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Extremity Prosthetics","topic":"Silicone Liner Pin-Lock vs Cuff Suspension","q":"A 68-year-old transtibial amputee with severe rheumatoid arthritis affecting both hands (weak pinch/grip) and unstable daily residual limb volume fluctuations from congestive heart failure needs a prosthetic suspension system. Which option balances secure suspension, ease of donning, and volume accommodation best?","o":["Ischial weight-bearing pelvic band with hip joint for a transtibial amputee","Supracondylar strap/cuff suspension (or supracondylar PTB-SC wedge) over a Pelite liner with adjustable prosthetic socks rather than a tight roll-on pin-lock or pure suction valve socket","Total-contact bare-skin suction socket with zero sock accommodation","Tight roll-on silicone liner with distal shuttle-lock requiring forceful finger unrolling and a fixed-volume suction valve"],"a":1,"e":"Patients with severe hand arthritis struggle to invert and roll on tight silicone/gel liners without tearing them or injuring fragile skin, and patients with large daily edema fluctuations cannot maintain an airtight seal in a pure suction socket. A Pelite foam liner with prosthetic ply socks and a simple supracondylar cuff (or PTB-SC self-suspending medial wedge) easily accommodates volume changes and requires minimal hand dexterity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01237","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Extremity Prosthetics","topic":"Microprocessor-Controlled Knees (MPK)","q":"What functional capability distinguishes a Microprocessor-Controlled Hydraulic Knee (MPK) from a conventional constant-friction mechanical single-axis knee in a K3 transfemoral amputee?","o":["It requires zero bench alignment because software rotates the thigh socket automatically","It generates active motorized hip extension from an external lithium battery without requiring residual hip muscles","Real-time sensor feedback dynamically increases hydraulic stance flexion resistance during stumble perturbations, ramp descent, and step-over-step stair descent while adapting swing damping to walking speed","It uses a rigid manual locking pin that permanently prevents knee flexion throughout swing phase"],"a":2,"e":"MPKs sample knee angle, angular velocity, and axial/moment strain gauges 50–100+ times per second to instantaneously adjust hydraulic valve resistance. In stance, yielding resistance allows controlled knee flexion (\u0027riding the knee\u0027) down stairs/slopes and automatically locks against buckling during a stumble; in swing, it adapts cadence continuously from slow to brisk walking.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01238","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Upper Extremity Orthotics","topic":"Radial, Ulnar, Median, and Erb\u0027s Palsy Splints","q":"Match each upper-limb nerve injury with its classic deformity and indicated orthosis: (1) High radial nerve palsy, (2) Low ulnar nerve palsy, (3) Low median nerve palsy, (4) Upper brachial plexus (Erb\u0027s C5–C6) palsy. Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Wrist drop -\u003e Anticlaw lumbrical bar; 2-Ulnar claw -\u003e Opponens C-bar; 3-Ape hand -\u003e Airplane splint; 4-Waiter\u0027s tip -\u003e Cock-up splint","1-Waiter\u0027s tip; 2-Wrist drop; 3-Ulnar claw hand; 4-Ape hand","1-Anticlaw lumbrical bar; 2-Opponens C-bar; 3-Dynamic wrist/MCP extension; 4-Waiter\u0027s tip","1-Wrist drop -\u003e Dynamic wrist/MCP extension orthosis; 2-Ulnar claw hand (4th–5th MCP hyperextension) -\u003e Anticlaw lumbrical-bar orthosis; 3-Ape hand (loss of thumb opposition) -\u003e C-bar / opponens orthosis; 4-Waiter\u0027s tip -\u003e Airplane abduction/external-rotation orthosis"],"a":3,"e":"Orthotic management prevents overstretching of paralyzed muscles and contracture of unopposed antagonists: radial palsy (wrist drop) uses a wrist/MCP extension assist; ulnar clawing (4th–5th MCP hyperextension from intrinsic paralysis) uses a dorsal lumbrical bar blocking MCP hyperextension so extrinsic extensors can extend the IP joints; median thenar paralysis uses a C-bar/opponens splint to preserve the first web space; Erb\u0027s C5–C6 palsy uses an airplane splint (or shoulder-abduction orthosis) to rest the deltoid/external rotators.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01239","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Extremity Orthotics","topic":"Reciprocating Gait Orthosis (RGO)","q":"A 9-year-old boy with L1 myelomeningocele (preserved iliopsoas 3/5, absent quadriceps and gluteus maximus) is prescribed an Isocentric Reciprocating Gait Orthosis (RGO) with a thoracic extension. How does the RGO\u0027s mechanical hip linkage facilitate reciprocal step-through ambulation?","o":["A dual-cable or pivot-bar linkage couples hip extension on the stance side with simultaneous contralateral hip flexion, preventing bilateral simultaneous hip flexion/collapse","Pneumatic hip motors automatically lift both feet together in a swing-through hop","It unlocks both knee joints during midstance to encourage crouch gait","It relies solely on active gluteus maximus contraction on both sides simultaneously"],"a":0,"e":"In L1–T10 paraplegia lacking hip extensors, bilateral unlocked hip joints would cause jackknifing collapse. The RGO joins right and left hip joints via dual Bowden cables or an isocentric rocker/pivot bar behind the pelvis: extending the stance hip (by shifting trunk backward) mechanically drives flexion of the contralateral swing hip while preventing both hips from flexing at once.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01240","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Extremity Prosthetics","topic":"Canadian Hip Disarticulation Alignment","q":"In the biomechanical alignment of a Canadian hip disarticulation prosthesis, how are the mechanical hip and knee axes positioned relative to the sagittal weight-bearing line (TKA line) to guarantee passive stance-phase stability?","o":["Prosthetic hip joint is mounted ANTERIOR to the weight-bearing line (with an extension bumper/stop) and prosthetic knee joint is mounted POSTERIOR to the weight-bearing line","Both hip and knee joints are mounted far posterior to the weight-bearing line","Prosthetic hip joint is mounted posterior to the weight-bearing line and knee joint anterior to it","Both hip and knee joints are locked permanently at 30 degrees of flexion"],"a":0,"e":"Because a hip-disarticulation amputee has zero active hip or knee musculature on the prosthetic side, passive stability is engineered geometrically: placing the hip joint axis ANTERIOR to the weight-bearing line forces the hip into extension against the posterior extension stop during stance, while placing the knee axis POSTERIOR to the line forces the knee into extension until heel-off.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01241","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"CAD/CAM and Digital Prosthetics","topic":"Digital Residual Limb Capture","q":"A prosthetist captures a transtibial residual limb using a non-contact structured-light 3D scanner rather than a hand-tensioned plaster wrap cast. Before carving the positive foam model via CNC milling, what critical CAD software modification must be applied and why?","o":["Apply targeted global circumference reduction (soft-tissue pre-compression) and anatomical patellar-tendon/tibial-flare indentations, because non-contact optical scanning records completely unloaded soft tissue","Expand the entire scan volume uniformly by +10% to compensate for laser shrinkage","Delete the fibular head relief entirely because 3D scanners flatten bony prominences automatically","Skip all alignment markers because CNC milling automatically aligns the foot pyramid"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike hand-tensioned plaster casting—where the clinician compresses soft tissue and manually indents the patellar tendon and flares during setting—non-contact optical/laser 3D scanners capture resting, uncompressed skin volume. Without software pre-compression (global volume reduction over soft-tissue regions plus localized weight-bearing indentations and bony relief additions), the milled socket would fit far too loosely and cause pistoning.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01242","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Prosthetic Biomechanics","topic":"Terminal Stance Torque Calculation","q":"During terminal stance in a 75-kg transtibial amputee, the vertical ground reaction force (GRF ≈ 750 N) acts at the prosthetic forefoot center of pressure 16 cm (0.16 m) anterior to the ankle joint axis. What is the external ankle dorsiflexion moment (torque), and what happens to that moment if an overly short prosthetic foot keel reduces the forefoot lever arm to 12 cm (0.12 m)?","o":["46.9 N·m initially; rises to 62.5 N·m with the short keel, causing hyperextension","120 N·m initially; drops to 90 N·m with the short keel, causing premature late-stance \u0027drop-off\u0027","1,200 N·m initially; drops to 900 N·m causing vaulting","750 N·m initially; remains unchanged regardless of keel length"],"a":1,"e":"Moment = Force × Perpendicular Lever Arm. Initially: 750 N × 0.16 m = 120 N·m. With a shortened 12 cm (0.12 m) forefoot lever arm: 750 N × 0.12 m = 90 N·m (a 25% loss of anterior support). Insufficient terminal-stance keel stiffness or too short a keel removes the anterior tibia-restraining support prematurely, causing the center of mass to plunge downward rapidly (\u0027drop-off\u0027) before contralateral heel strike.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01243","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Upper Extremity Prosthetics","topic":"Muenster vs Northwestern Self-Suspending Sockets","q":"In a patient with a very short transradial (below-elbow) amputation, how does the classical Muenster self-suspending socket achieve harness-free suspension, and what functional range-of-motion trade-off does it impose?","o":["Permits 0 to 150 degrees unrestricted elbow extension and flexion via open anterior cubital window","Suspends via a pelvic waist belt and Bowden cable across the shoulder","Encapsulates the olecranon posteriorly and humeral epicondyles mediolaterally with a high, tightly contoured anterior cubital trimline set in ~35° pre-flexion, restricting full elbow extension and terminal flexion beyond ~105°","Leaves the elbow completely uncovered distally and relies on an axillary figure-of-8 chest strap"],"a":2,"e":"The Muenster socket was developed for very short transradial residual limbs requiring self-suspension without a shoulder harness (e.g., myoelectric systems). Its snug anteroposterior compression between the pre-flexed cubital fold (~35°) and encapsulated olecranon locks onto the distal humerus/epicondyles, inherently trading off full terminal elbow extension and hyperflexion (unlike the Northwestern socket, which relies primarily on mediolateral supracondylar suspension with lower AP trimlines).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01244","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Lower Extremity Orthotics","topic":"Rearfoot and Forefoot Posting","q":"A 38-year-old runner presents with medial ankle pain (posterior tibial tendinopathy), flexible pes planus, and excessive compensatory subtalar pronation during stance driven by a 6-degree flexible forefoot varus deformity. Which foot orthosis modification brings the supporting surface up to the inverted forefoot/rearfoot to limit excessive pronation?","o":["A LATERAL rearfoot valgus wedge raising the outer lateral border of the calcaneus","A 3-cm heel lift on the affected side only","A rigid forefoot rocker with zero arch contact and a lateral heel flare","A MEDIAL forefoot and rearfoot varus post (medial wedge) combined with a firm contoured medial longitudinal arch support"],"a":3,"e":"In rearfoot or forefoot varus, the medial side of the calcaneus or 1st metatarsal head sits higher off the ground when the subtalar joint is in neutral; to reach the floor during stance, the subtalar joint undergoes excessive compensatory pronation, straining the plantar fascia and tibialis posterior. Adding a MEDIAL varus post (wedge) supports the elevated medial border, maintaining neutral alignment and unloading medial structures.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01245","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Materials Science and Interface Technology","topic":"Silicone vs Urethane vs TPE Gel Liners","q":"A 46-year-old diabetic transtibial amputee has a thin, heavily scarred anterior tibial crest with an adherent split-thickness skin graft that repeatedly blisters inside a firm silicone liner despite accurate socket relief. Which interface liner elastomer specifically redistributes peak pressures hydrostatically across adherent bony prominences?","o":["Polyurethane (urethane) liner, which exhibits true hydrostatic flow under compressive load to equalize interface pressures across irregular scars","Thin cotton stockinette alone without foam or gel","Unlined rigid carbon-fiber inner wall without socks or liner","Firm cross-linked silicone with a rough high-friction fabric outer coating directly on skin"],"a":0,"e":"Polyurethane liners uniquely deform and flow hydrostatically away from high-pressure peaks (like an adherent scarred tibial crest) toward adjacent softer areas, dramatically reducing focal shear and peak pressure gradients compared with stiffer cross-linked silicone (which resists longitudinal pistoning well for high-activity limbs) or mineral-oil TPE gel (often favored for dry sensitive skin).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01246","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Clinical Prescription and Componentry","topic":"Uneven Terrain Compliance","q":"A 41-year-old K3 unilateral transtibial amputee works as a horticulturalist in hilly, rocky, and muddy rural terrain. When fitted temporarily with a rigid-keel SACH foot, he reports painful lateral socket pressure and feeling tipped over whenever stepping onto slanted stones. Which prosthetic foot design directly resolves this multi-planar instability?","o":["Rigid Syme low-profile rubber block foot","Multi-axis (or split-keel carbon-fiber dynamic-response) prosthetic foot permitting controlled inversion, eversion, and transverse rotation","Pure sagittal Single-Axis foot with rigid mediolateral ankle blocks","Fixed-keel SACH foot with an even stiffer wooden core"],"a":1,"e":"A rigid SACH or purely sagittal Single-Axis foot cannot invert or evert when contacting sloped ground, transmitting large coronal-plane torque directly up the pylon into the transtibial socket walls (causing lateral fibular-head or medial flare discomfort and loss of balance). A multi-axis foot or split-keel dynamic-response carbon-fiber ESR foot accommodates tri-planar terrain compliance while maintaining K3 energy return.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01247","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Spinal Orthotics","topic":"Biofeedback Spinal Orthoses","q":"A 73-year-old woman with postmenopausal osteoporosis and chronic thoracic hyperkyphosis (T6–T9) experiences postural fatigue and backache. Why does a lightweight dynamic biofeedback thoracolumbar orthosis (such as Spinomed or Weighted Kypho-Orthosis) outperform a rigid total-contact plastic TLSO jacket for long-term osteoporotic kyphosis?","o":["Weighted kypho-orthoses press the shoulders further forward into flexion to stretch the sternum","Spinomed orthoses act exclusively by fusing thoracic vertebrae surgically without straps","The contoured dorsal aluminum stay and shoulder/pelvic straps gently cue proprioceptive biofeedback whenever she slouches, stimulating active thoracic extensor muscle contraction and preventing paraspinal disuse atrophy","A rigid total-contact plastic jacket strengthens abdominal and paraspinal muscles faster by completely eliminating trunk motion"],"a":2,"e":"Rigid full-contact TLSOs restrict trunk movement, rapidly weakening already fragile osteoporotic erector spinae muscles via disuse atrophy and often reducing respiratory compliance in elderly patients. Dynamic biofeedback orthoses (Spinomed, PTS/WKO) provide subtle posterior tensile/proprioceptive cues as the trunk flexes forward, prompting active back-extensor recruitment that measurably improves muscle strength, posture, and vital capacity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01248","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Upper Extremity Orthotics","topic":"Static Progressive vs Dynamic Splinting","q":"A patient 10 weeks post-distal humerus fracture has a firm, non-edematous, 40-degree elbow flexion contracture that feels hard at end-range. When fabricating a mobilization orthosis, how does a Static Progressive (turnbuckle/gear-ratchet) elbow orthosis differ mechanically from a rubber-band/spring Dynamic orthosis?","o":["Static progressive orthoses force the joint into 40 degrees MORE flexion rather than extension","Static progressive orthoses apply constant high-velocity elastic oscillation 60 times per minute","Dynamic elastic splints use rigid inelastic turnbuckles that cannot be adjusted by the patient","Static progressive orthoses hold the stiff joint at a fixed maximal tolerable end-range using inelastic components (stress relaxation), allowing incremental manual advancement without continuous painful elastic recoil"],"a":3,"e":"Dense mature scar/capsular contractures respond well to Static Progressive splinting (using inelastic turnbuckles, hook-and-loop straps, or ratchet hinges): the patient adjusts the angle to the end of comfortable stretch, and as collagen fibers undergo stress relaxation over minutes, tension drops to comfortable levels before the patient ratchets slightly further. Dynamic splints use elastic springs/bands (creep), which exert continuous force across shorter wear bouts.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01249","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Materials Science and Fabrication Technology","topic":"Fiber Orientation Mechanics","q":"During vacuum-assisted carbon-fiber/acrylic lamination of a high-activity prosthetic socket, match each fiber layup orientation with the primary mechanical stress it resists: (1) Longitudinal (0°) unidirectional carbon tape, (2) ±45° biaxial braided carbon sleeve, (3) Circumferential (90°) hoop wraps at the proximal brim/distal base, (4) Inner/outer Nylon or Perlon stockinette layers. Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Axial bending and longitudinal cantilever moments; 2-Torsional twisting shear moments; 3-Radial hoop/bursting expansion; 4-Resin wicking/distribution and smooth cosmetic surface finish","1-Torsional twisting shear; 2-Radial hoop expansion; 3-Resin wicking; 4-Axial bending","1-Resin wicking; 2-Axial bending; 3-Torsional twisting shear; 4-Radial hoop expansion","1-Radial hoop expansion; 2-Axial bending; 3-Resin wicking; 4-Torsional twisting shear"],"a":0,"e":"Anisotropic composite strength depends on fiber alignment along principal stress axes: 0° longitudinal unidirectional carbon fibers maximize bending stiffness along the socket wall; ±45° braided sleeves carry torsional twisting loads during stance pivot; 90° circumferential fibers resist radial hoop splitting at brims and distal attachment blocks; Nylon/Perlon stockinettes facilitate uniform vacuum resin flow and prevent rough carbon fiber exposure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01250","course":"B.Sc Prosthetics \u0026 Orthotics","subject":"Pedorthics and Shoe Modifications","topic":"Internal vs External Shoe Elevation","q":"An adult patient with a healed tibial malunion has a true structural leg-length discrepancy (LLD) of 32 mm (right leg shorter than left). Standard pedorthic practice leaves ~6 mm uncompensated to aid swing-phase toe clearance (targeting 26 mm total lift), limits internal in-shoe heel lifts to a maximum of 8 mm (to prevent heel slippage out of the shoe counter), and applies the remainder as an external sole build-up tapered toward the toe. How much external heel lift must be added to the sole of the right shoe?","o":["8 mm external heel lift only (leaving 24 mm uncompensated)","18 mm external heel lift (combined with 8 mm internal lift = 26 mm total)","26 mm external heel lift (combined with 8 mm internal lift = 34 mm total)","32 mm external heel lift with zero internal lift"],"a":1,"e":"Total desired compensation = 32 mm − 6 mm clearance allowance = 26 mm. Because an internal heel wedge thicker than ~8–10 mm pushes the calcaneus out of the shoe\u0027s heel counter and tightens the vamp, 8 mm is placed internally and the remaining 26 mm − 8 mm = 18 mm is added externally to the outsole (typically tapered from 18 mm at the heel to ~10–12 mm at the ball/toe with a rocker contour so gait rollover remains smooth).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01251","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiation Physics \u0026 CT Technology","topic":"CTDIvol and Helical Pitch","q":"On a multi-detector CT scanner operating without automatic tube current modulation (fixed 120 kVp and 200 mAs per rotation), a chest CT protocol has a pitch of 1.0 and a CTDIvol of 15.0 mGy. If the radiographer increases the pitch to 1.5 while keeping kVp, mAs, and collimation unchanged, what is the new CTDIvol?","o":["22.5 mGy","12.5 mGy","10.0 mGy","15.0 mGy"],"a":2,"e":"In helical CT with fixed mAs (without effective mAs compensation), Volume CT Dose Index is defined as CTDIvol = CTDIw / pitch. Here CTDIw = 15.0 x 1.0 = 15.0 mGy. Increasing pitch to 1.5 spreads the helical beam over greater table travel per rotation, giving CTDIvol = 15.0 / 1.5 = 10.0 mGy (a 33.3% dose reduction).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01252","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiation Protection \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Personnel Monitoring \u0026 Shielding","q":"According to Atomic Energy Regulatory Board (AERB, India) radiation safety guidelines for diagnostic fluoroscopy and interventional radiology staff using a single Thermoluminescent Dosimeter (TLD) badge and a 0.5 mm lead-equivalent apron, where must the TLD badge be worn?","o":["On the dominant wrist outside the lead apron","On the trouser waistband underneath the lead apron","At chest level outside the lead apron to record worst-case exposure","At chest level underneath the lead apron"],"a":3,"e":"Under AERB Safety Code for Medical Diagnostic X-ray Equipment and Practices (AERB/RF-MED/SC-3), when a single TLD chest badge is issued, it must be worn at chest level underneath the protective lead apron to estimate effective whole-body dose to vital trunk organs. (If a second collar badge is issued, it is worn outside at the collar for lens/thyroid estimation.)","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01253","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Magnetic Resonance Imaging","topic":"Spatial Misregistration Artifacts","q":"On a 3.0 T T1-weighted spin-echo abdominal MRI, a bright rim appears on one side of the kidney-perinephric fat interface and a dark signal-void band appears on the opposite side. Which imaging direction contains this artifact, and how should the technologist adjust parameters to reduce its pixel width?","o":["Frequency-encoding direction; increase receiver bandwidth","Phase-encoding direction; decrease receiver bandwidth and increase FOV","Slice-selection direction; increase echo time (TE)","Frequency-encoding direction; increase magnetic field strength from 3.0 T to 7.0 T"],"a":0,"e":"Type 1 chemical shift artifact occurs along the frequency-encoding (readout) axis because water and fat protons differ in Larmor frequency by ~3.5 ppm (~440 Hz at 3.0 T), causing fat signal to be spatially misregistered relative to water. Increasing the receiver bandwidth increases the Hz/pixel ratio, compressing the frequency offset into fewer pixels.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01254","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Specialized Radiographic Physics","topic":"Target-Filter Selection \u0026 Heel Effect","q":"In screen-film and dedicated digital mammography (25-32 kVp), why is the X-ray tube cathode positioned over the chest wall side of the breast rather than the nipple side?","o":["The anode side has higher beam intensity to penetrate the thin anterior nipple tissue","The anode heel effect produces greater X-ray intensity on the cathode side, matching the thicker posterior chest wall breast tissue","It doubles the focal spot size over the pectoral muscle to improve microcalcification resolution","Cathode-chest-wall alignment eliminates characteristic X-rays from molybdenum"],"a":1,"e":"Because X-rays emitted toward the anode side travel through a greater thickness of the tungsten/molybdenum target (self-absorption), beam intensity is higher on the cathode side (anode heel effect). Placing the cathode over the thicker posterior chest wall and anode over the thinner anterior nipple region equalizes detector exposure and geometric sharpness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01255","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Digital Radiography \u0026 Image Processing","topic":"Exposure Index (EI) \u0026 Dose Creep","q":"A radiographer switches from screen-film to indirect flat-panel Digital Radiography (DR) and notices a pelvis radiograph appears bright and clinically pleasing on the PACS monitor, yet the Exposure Index (EI) is markedly elevated (Deviation Index +4.5). Conversely, a low-mAs portable chest DR shows grainy, salt-and-pepper noise (quantum mottle). Why does DR mask overexposure visually?","o":["Flat-panel amorphous silicon detectors permanently burn out whenever mAs exceeds 20 mAs","Quantum mottle is caused by excessive mAs saturating the cesium iodide scintillator","Automatic histogram rescaling and lookup table (LUT) processing normalize display brightness over a wide dynamic range, hiding \u0027dose creep\u0027 unless EI is audited","Overexposure decreases the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), making images appear excessively dark on PACS"],"a":2,"e":"Digital radiography possesses a wide linear dynamic range combined with automatic exposure data recognition (histogram rescaling) that adjusts displayed image optical density regardless of excess dose. Consequently, technicians receive crisp low-noise images when overexposing patients (\u0027dose creep\u0027)—detectable only via the Exposure Index / Deviation Index—whereas underexposure produces visible quantum mottle from insufficient photon statistics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01256","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Contrast Media \u0026 Patient Care in Radiology","topic":"Metformin \u0026 Post-Contrast Acute Kidney Injury","q":"A 66-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus taking metformin (1000 mg twice daily) is referred for a contrast-enhanced CT abdomen with IV low-osmolar iodinated contrast. His serum creatinine today yields an eGFR of 26 mL/min/1.73 m2. Per ACR/ESUR contrast safety guidelines, what is the appropriate metformin and hydration management if contrast CT proceeds?","o":["Double the metformin dose immediately after CT to prevent hyperglycemic osmotic diuresis","Continue metformin uninterrupted; restrict oral and IV fluids for 12 hours","Substitute IV gadolinium-based contrast for routine abdominal CT angiography without checking eGFR","Discontinue metformin at the time of or prior to contrast administration, hydrate with isotonic 0.9% NaCl, and withhold metformin for 48 hours until renal function is re-evaluated as stable"],"a":3,"e":"In patients with eGFR \u003c 30 mL/min/1.73 m2 (or acute kidney injury), iodinated contrast carries a risk of precipitating contrast-associated AKI, causing metformin accumulation and potentially fatal lactic acidosis. Guidelines mandate isotonic saline hydration, temporarily holding metformin at the time of the procedure, and restarting it after 48 hours only once renal function stability is confirmed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01257","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Ultrasound \u0026 Doppler Physics","topic":"Nyquist Limit \u0026 Aliasing","q":"During a carotid duplex ultrasound across a tight internal carotid artery stenosis, the pulsed-wave (PW) Doppler spectral waveform cuts off at the top of the scale and wraps around to appear at the bottom of the baseline (aliasing). Which adjustment on the ultrasound console directly eliminates aliasing by raising the Nyquist limit?","o":["Increase the Pulse Repetition Frequency (PRF) / velocity scale (or switch to a lower-frequency transducer / Continuous-Wave Doppler)","Increase the wall filter to 500 Hz and raise overall B-mode gain","Decrease the Pulse Repetition Frequency (PRF) / velocity scale","Increase the Doppler angle from 60 degrees to 90 degrees"],"a":0,"e":"Aliasing occurs in pulsed-wave Doppler when the Doppler frequency shift exceeds the Nyquist limit (PRF / 2). Increasing the PRF (velocity scale) raises the Nyquist limit; alternatively, lowering transducer transmit frequency, shifting the baseline, decreasing sample gate depth, or switching to Continuous-Wave (CW) Doppler (no aliasing limit) resolves wrap-around.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01258","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"MRI Safety \u0026 Clinical Protocols","topic":"ACR Safety Zones \u0026 Cryogen Quench","q":"During an evening 1.5 T MRI scan, a loud hissing sound erupts from the magnet bore, white fog rapidly fills the magnet room, and the oxygen monitor alarm sounds as a superconducting quench occurs. Which immediate safety hazard threatens the patient inside the magnet room, and why must the magnet room door (Zone IV) open OUTWARD or have pressure-relief venting?","o":["Acute 50 Hz electrical shock from static magnetic field collapse","Rapid boil-off of liquid helium into massive volumes of gas (~700:1 expansion) can displace oxygen (causing asphyxiation/frostbite) and overpressurize Zone IV, jamming inward-opening doors if the quench vent pipe fails","Sudden gravitational drop inside Zone III requiring immediate ferric stretcher entry","Ionizing gamma-ray burst; door opens inward to seal radiation inside"],"a":1,"e":"During a quench, superconducting coils lose superconductivity and rapidly boil off liquid helium (-269 C), expanding ~700-750-fold in gas volume. If any helium leaks into Zone IV instead of exiting through the external quench vent, positive room pressure can slam inward-opening doors shut and rapidly displace oxygen, risking asphyxiation and cryogenic burns—requiring immediate patient evacuation and outward-opening/vented doors.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01259","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Computed Tomography Protocols","topic":"Hounsfield Units \u0026 Windowing","q":"On a non-contrast head CT obtained for suspected acute ischemic stroke versus a thoracic CT for interstitial lung disease, which Hounsfield Unit (HU) Window Width (WW) and Window Level (WL) strategies are used respectively?","o":["Brain stroke: wide WW (1500 HU), low WL (-600 HU); Lung: narrow WW (80 HU), WL (+35 HU)","Brain stroke: narrow WW (~80 HU) centered around WL (+35 to +40 HU) to accentuate subtle gray-white matter contrast; Lung parenchyma: wide WW (~1500 HU) centered around WL (-600 HU)","Both brain and lung use identical bone window WW 2500 / WL +500","Water attenuation is set to +1000 HU and air to 0 HU"],"a":1,"e":"In CT grayscale display (water = 0 HU, air = -1000 HU, cortical bone ~ +1000 HU), a narrow Window Width concentrates all 256 display shades across a tight HU span, maximizing contrast resolution between tissues with similar attenuation (e.g., gray matter ~37 HU vs white matter ~28 HU using WW ~80 / WL ~35-40 HU). Wide WW (~1500 HU) centered at aerated lung (~ -600 HU) displays both air-filled alveoli and dense pulmonary vessels simultaneously.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01260","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Advanced MRI Clinical Applications","topic":"Acute Cerebral Infarction vs T2 Shine-Through","q":"A 65-year-old man undergoes emergency brain MRI 2 hours after sudden left-sided weakness. Trace Diffusion-Weighted Imaging (DWI, b = 1000 s/mm2) reveals a hyperintense (bright) wedge-shaped lesion in the right MCA territory. How must the corresponding Apparent Diffusion Coefficient (ADC) map appear to confirm TRUE acute cytotoxic edema rather than \u0027T2 shine-through\u0027?","o":["Bright (hyperintense) on the ADC map","Dark (hypointense) on the ADC map, reflecting true restricted intracellular water diffusion","Isointense to CSF on both T1 and ADC","Blooming signal void only on T1 post-contrast"],"a":1,"e":"Because DWI is inherently T2-weighted with superimposed diffusion-sensitizing gradients, lesions with very long T2 relaxation times (subacute infarcts, vasogenic edema, epidermoid cysts) can look bright on b=1000 DWI (\u0027T2 shine-through\u0027) while appearing BRIGHT on the ADC map. Acute ischemic cytotoxic edema causes Na+/K+-ATPase failure and cell swelling, truly restricting Brownian motion—appearing BRIGHT on DWI and DARK on ADC.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01261","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"X-Ray Physics \u0026 Tube Construction","topic":"Line Focus Principle \u0026 15% kVp Rule","q":"How does angling the rotating tungsten anode target (e.g., 12 degrees) implement the Line Focus Principle in a diagnostic X-ray tube, and what exposure adjustment satisfies the \u002715% Rule\u0027 when halving mAs to reduce patient dose?","o":["Eliminates Bremsstrahlung radiation entirely; double kVp to halve mAs","Increases effective focal spot larger than actual focal spot; decrease kVp by 15%","Produces a SMALL effective focal spot (sharper spatial resolution) while spreading electron heat across a MUCH LARGER actual focal track; increasing kVp by 15% permits halving mAs while maintaining equivalent receptor exposure","Decreases heat capacity of the anode; increase SID by 15%"],"a":2,"e":"By tilting the anode face (Line Focus Principle: Effective focal spot = Actual focal spot x sin[target angle]), the tube achieves high heat loading across a broad actual target area while projecting a small effective focal spot toward the patient for sharp detail. By the empirical 15% Rule, raising kVp by 15% increases X-ray beam penetrability and receptor exposure equivalent to doubling mAs—allowing mAs to be halved and lowering patient entrance skin dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01262","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"CT Dosimetry \u0026 Radiation Protection","topic":"Dose-Length Product (DLP) to Effective Dose Conversion","q":"A single-phase helical CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis displays a CTDIvol of 12.0 mGy over a total scanned Z-axis length of 40 cm. Using the standard adult abdomen-pelvis conversion coefficient k = 0.015 mSv/(mGy.cm), what are the Dose-Length Product (DLP) and estimated Effective Dose (E)?","o":["DLP = 480 mGy.cm; Effective Dose = 48.0 mSv","DLP = 300 mGy.cm; Effective Dose = 4.5 mSv","DLP = 120 mGy.cm; Effective Dose = 1.8 mSv","DLP = 480 mGy.cm; Effective Dose = 7.2 mSv"],"a":3,"e":"DLP = CTDIvol (mGy) x Scan Length (cm) = 12.0 mGy x 40 cm = 480 mGy.cm (representing total radiation energy imparted along the scan range). Effective Dose E = DLP x k = 480 x 0.015 = 7.2 mSv.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01263","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Interventional Radiology \u0026 Fluoroscopy Safety","topic":"Fluoroscopic Peak Skin Dose \u0026 Tube Orientation","q":"During a prolonged 90-minute complex coronary/neuro-interventional fluoroscopy procedure, cumulative Air Kerma approaches 3.5 Gy (exceeding the ~2 Gy threshold for transient erythema). Which C-arm geometric setup minimizes BOTH patient skin entrance dose and operator head/eye scatter dose?","o":["Position the X-ray tube ABOVE the patient table, pull the image intensifier far away from the patient, and use continuous high-dose cine acquisition","Position the X-ray tube UNDER the patient table, move the image receptor/flat-panel detector as CLOSE to the patient as possible, use lowest-rate pulsed fluoroscopy + Last-Image-Hold (LIH) + tight collimation, and rotate C-arm angles periodically to spread skin entrance dose","Remove the 2.5 mm Al beam filtration and zoom to maximal electronic magnification throughout","Stand directly adjacent to the under-table X-ray tube port without ceiling-suspended lead glass"],"a":1,"e":"Deterministic skin injuries (transient erythema ~2 Gy, epilation ~3 Gy, dermal necrosis \u003e 12-15 Gy) occur at the beam entrance surface. Placing the X-ray tube under the table keeps primary scatter directed downward/under the table (dramatically reducing operator eye/neck dose), while keeping the detector close to the patient (reducing SID/air gap and automatic brightness control mA), using pulsed fluoroscopy, LIH, collimation, and varying gantry angles protects both patient skin and staff.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01264","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"MRI Contrast Media \u0026 Safety","topic":"Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis (NSF)","q":"Why do ACR Contrast Media guidelines strongly restrict older non-ionic linear Group I Gadolinium-Based Contrast Agents (e.g., gadodiamide) in patients with eGFR \u003c 30 mL/min/1.73 m2, whereas Macrocyclic Group II agents (e.g., gadoterate meglumine, gadobutrol) carry virtually zero risk of Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis (NSF)?","o":["Macrocyclic ligands enclose the toxic free Gd3+ ion in a rigid, cage-like molecular ring with far higher thermodynamic and kinetic stability, preventing free gadolinium dissociation and tissue deposition during prolonged renal retention","Linear GBCAs are excreted 100% by the lungs within 5 minutes","Macrocyclic GBCAs contain radioactive technetium rather than gadolinium","Macrocyclic GBCAs cannot cross the glomerular basement membrane"],"a":0,"e":"Free Gd3+ ion is highly toxic and triggers profibrotic fibrocyte activation (NSF) if released in vivo (transmetallation) during delayed renal clearance in severe CKD/AKI. Open-chain linear Group I chelates dissociate much more readily than rigid, pre-organized ring-shaped Macrocyclic Group II chelates, making Group II GBCAs extraordinarily safe even in advanced renal failure when MRI contrast is clinically essential.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01265","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Computed Tomography Artifacts \u0026 Reconstruction","topic":"Beam Hardening Artifact (Hounsfield Bar)","q":"On a non-contrast axial CT of the posterior fossa, a dark hypoattenuating band (\u0027Hounsfield bar\u0027) stretches horizontally across the pons between the two dense petrous temporal bones, mimicking brainstem infarction. What physical mechanism produces this beam-hardening artifact, and how is it minimized?","o":["Monochromatic X-ray amplification by bone; lower kVp to 60 kVp","Polychromatic X-ray low-energy photons are preferentially attenuated by the dense petrous ridges, increasing the mean energy (\u0027hardening\u0027) of the surviving beam along that path so tissues appear falsely hypodense; mitigated by increasing kVp, thin slices, bow-tie prefiltration, and dual-energy/beam-hardening correction algorithms","Patient respiration blurring the brainstem; use cardiac gating","Detector ring miscalibration causing a concentric bull\u0027s-eye ring around isocenter"],"a":1,"e":"Diagnostic CT tubes emit a polychromatic X-ray spectrum. Passing through high-Z dense cortical bone (petrous pyramids, shoulder girdle) selectively strips low-energy photons, raising the effective energy of the transmitted beam so subsequent brain tissue attenuates proportionally less—reconstructed as dark streak bands between two dense objects. Higher kVp, beam filtration, iterative beam-hardening software, and dual-energy virtual monochromatic imaging suppress the artifact.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01266","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiation Protection in Special Populations","topic":"Fetal Radiation Thresholds \u0026 Shielding Updates","q":"A 28-year-old woman who is 10 weeks pregnant undergoes an urgent single-view abdominal radiograph (estimated fetal absorbed dose = 2.0 mGy) for suspected ureteric calculus. Per ICRP Publication 84 and AAPM/AERB guidance, how does this dose compare with the deterministic fetal malformation/neurocognitive threshold, and why do modern guidelines emphasize tight beam collimation over placing a lead contact apron directly over the abdomen during pelvic imaging?","o":["Any diagnostic X-ray in the first trimester guarantees 100% fetal teratogenesis","X-rays do not penetrate maternal abdominal fat at 80 kVp","2.0 mGy is ~50 times BELOW the ~100 mGy deterministic threshold for malformations/intellectual disability (never justifying pregnancy termination); contact shielding inside the primary field risks obscuring pathology or triggering Automatic Exposure Control (AEC) dose spikes, making tight collimation and low-dose protocols paramount","2.0 mGy exceeds the 1 mGy abortion threshold; lead apron must cover the entire iliac fossa during AP pelvis X-ray"],"a":2,"e":"ICRP 84 establishes that fetal deterministic risks (malformations, growth restriction, severe intellectual impairment) do not occur below ~100 mGy—a threshold far above single diagnostic radiographs (1-3 mGy) or even a standard single-phase abdominal CT (~10-25 mGy). Furthermore, AAPM/NCRP updates advise against routine patient contact shielding over the pelvis/gonads because shields can cover AEC chambers (spiking mAs) or obscure target anatomy, while shielding adds negligible protection against internal patient scatter.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01267","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiation Physics \u0026 X-Ray Equipment","topic":"X-Ray Tube Geometry","q":"A radiographer is preparing to take an anteroposterior (AP) radiograph of the thoracic spine (T1 to T12) and an AP radiograph of the femur. To achieve uniform radiographic density using the anode heel effect, how should the patient be oriented relative to the X-ray tube?","o":["Place the thicker anatomical region (lower thoracic spine T12 and proximal thigh/hip) under the anode end of the X-ray tube","Use the shortest possible source-to-image distance (SID) and largest focal spot so the anode heel effect is maximized evenly","Angle the central ray 25 degrees toward the anode to reverse photon intensity across the cathode","Place the thicker anatomical region (lower thoracic spine T12 and proximal thigh/hip) under the cathode end of the X-ray tube"],"a":3,"e":"Due to the angled tungsten target face of the anode, X-ray photons emitted toward the anode side must traverse a greater thickness of target material before exiting, resulting in self-absorption and reduced beam intensity (up to ~45% difference across the field). Consequently, intensity is higher on the cathode side. Placing thicker, more attenuating anatomy (lower thoracic spine/diaphragm, proximal femur) under the cathode end produces a more balanced image exposure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01268","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiation Physics \u0026 X-Ray Equipment","topic":"X-Ray Production Mechanisms","q":"In a conventional diagnostic X-ray tube with a tungsten target (K-shell electron binding energy = 69.5 keV) operated at 60 kVp, which statement accurately describes the X-ray emission spectrum reaching the patient?","o":["Only Bremsstrahlung X-rays and L/M-shell low-energy photons are produced; no tungsten K-characteristic X-rays can be generated","Approximately 50% of the diagnostic beam consists of discrete 69.5 keV K-characteristic X-ray peaks","Filament thermionic electrons collide directly with tungsten nuclei to emit monoenergetic 60 keV Bremsstrahlung photons","Increasing mAs increases the maximum photon energy above 60 keV without changing photon quantity"],"a":0,"e":"To eject an inner K-shell electron from tungsten (binding energy 69.5 keV) and produce K-characteristic X-rays, the incident projectile electron kinetic energy must equal or exceed 69.5 keV (requiring \u003e=70 kVp). At 60 kVp, no K-characteristic photons can be created; the useful diagnostic beam consists entirely of polyenergetic Bremsstrahlung radiation (maximum 60 keV), as low-energy L/M-shell photons (~10-12 keV) are absorbed by inherent/added filtration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01269","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiographic Positioning \u0026 Special Procedures","topic":"Thoracic Radiography Positioning","q":"A trauma patient who cannot stand is suspected of having a small free-flowing left-sided pleural effusion (\u003c150 mL) that is equivocal on a supine AP chest radiograph. Which horizontal-beam decubitus projection is specifically indicated to confirm layering of the left pleural fluid?","o":["Left lateral decubitus (patient lying on the left side with horizontal X-ray beam)","Lordotic AP projection with 20 degrees cephalad tube angulation","Dorsal decubitus with the left arm elevated overhead","Right lateral decubitus (patient lying on the right side with horizontal X-ray beam)"],"a":0,"e":"To detect free-flowing pleural fluid (which layers dependently by gravity), the side of interest must be placed DOWN against the table/cart: thus a left lateral decubitus (left side down, horizontal AP/PA beam) demonstrates fluid layering along the dependent left lateral chest wall. Conversely, to demonstrate small free air (pneumothorax), the affected side is placed UP (e.g., right lateral decubitus for left pneumothorax).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01270","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"CT, MRI, Ultrasound \u0026 Nuclear Imaging","topic":"Computed Tomography Attenuation","q":"On a calibrated non-contrast cranial CT scan, which set of Hounsfield Unit (HU) values accurately represents fat, pure water, acute parenchymal haemorrhage, and dense cortical bone respectively?","o":["Fat: -500 HU; Water: +10 HU; Acute haemorrhage: +150 HU; Cortical bone: +200 HU","Fat: -100 to -50 HU; Water: 0 HU; Acute haemorrhage: +50 to +80 HU; Cortical bone: +1000 HU","Fat: +30 HU; Water: 0 HU; Acute haemorrhage: -80 HU; Cortical bone: +1000 HU","Fat: 0 HU; Water: +20 HU; Acute haemorrhage: -40 HU; Cortical bone: +400 HU"],"a":1,"e":"The Hounsfield Unit scale is normalized to pure water (0 HU) and air (-1000 HU) via HU = 1000 x (mu_tissue - mu_water) / mu_water. Adipose tissue has lower linear attenuation than water (-100 to -50 HU), clotted acute blood appears hyperdense (+50 to +80 HU due to concentrated globin protein), and dense cortical bone measures around +1000 HU (or higher).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01271","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"CT, MRI, Ultrasound \u0026 Nuclear Imaging","topic":"Multidetector Helical CT Parameters","q":"A 64-slice multidetector CT scanner operates with 64 active detector rows of 0.625 mm width each (total collimated beam width = 40 mm) and a table feed of 60 mm per 360-degree gantry rotation. What is the helical pitch, and how does changing from pitch 1.0 to this pitch affect patient radiation dose if mAs and kVp remain constant?","o":["Pitch = 2.4; patient radiation dose remains unchanged regardless of helical overlap","Pitch = 0.67; patient radiation dose decreases by 33% due to narrower beam overlap","Pitch = 1.5; patient radiation dose decreases because adjacent X-ray helices are separated by gaps","Pitch = 1.5; patient radiation dose increases proportionally by 50% due to faster table speed"],"a":2,"e":"For multidetector CT (MDCT), pitch = Table movement per 360-degree rotation / Total collimated beam width (N x T) = 60 mm / (64 x 0.625 mm) = 60 / 40 = 1.5. When pitch increases above 1.0 at fixed mAs and kVp, the patient passes through the X-ray beam faster with gaps between consecutive helical turns, so patient dose decreases (Dose is proportional to 1/pitch unless automatic tube current modulation compensates via effective mAs).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01272","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"CT, MRI, Ultrasound \u0026 Nuclear Imaging","topic":"CT Grayscale Display","q":"A 69-year-old man presents within 2 hours of sudden left-sided weakness. On the standard non-contrast head CT (Window Width 80 HU, Window Level 40 HU), the right MCA territory appears nearly normal. To maximize visual contrast resolution for subtle early parenchymal hypoattenuation and loss of gray-white differentiation (insular ribbon sign), how should the technologist adjust the stroke window settings?","o":["Widen the Window Width (e.g., to 1500 HU) and lower Window Level to -600 HU","Keep Window Width at 400 HU and set Window Level to -100 HU","Widen the Window Width (e.g., to 2500 HU) and raise Window Level to +500 HU","Narrow the Window Width (e.g., to 30-40 HU) while keeping Window Level near brain parenchyma (~35-40 HU)"],"a":3,"e":"Decreasing Window Width (WW) compresses the full 256 shades of display grayscale across a smaller range of Hounsfield Units centered on the Window Level (WL), sharply increasing displayed contrast between gray matter (~35-40 HU), white matter (~25-30 HU), and edematous ischemic cortex. A dedicated stroke window (WW ~30-40 HU, WL ~35-40 HU) enhances detection of subtle early ischemic hypoattenuation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01273","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"CT, MRI, Ultrasound \u0026 Nuclear Imaging","topic":"Diffusion-Weighted Imaging (DWI)","q":"A 59-year-old woman presents 2.5 hours after acute onset of aphasia and right hemiparesis. On emergency brain MRI, the left insular cortex and lentiform nucleus appear markedly hyperintense on Diffusion-Weighted Imaging (DWI, b = 1000 s/mm2), markedly hypointense on the Apparent Diffusion Coefficient (ADC) map, and still nearly isointense on FLAIR. What pathophysiological process accounts for this DWI/ADC appearance?","o":["Cytotoxic oedema from Na+/K+-ATPase pump failure causes intracellular water shifting and restricted Brownian diffusion of water protons","Vasogenic oedema from blood-brain barrier breakdown increases extracellular free water mobility (T2 shine-through)","Acute intraparenchymal haemorrhage causing T2* magnetic susceptibility blooming without diffusion restriction","Increased cerebral blood flow hyperperfusion washing out deoxyhaemoglobin"],"a":0,"e":"Within minutes of cerebral ischaemia, ATP depletion causes Na+/K+-ATPase pump failure, driving water osmotically from the extracellular space into swollen neurons and glia (cytotoxic oedema). Intracellular organelle crowding and tortuous narrowed extracellular pathways restrict random Brownian diffusion of water molecules, producing high signal on DWI (b=1000) and low ADC values. Absence of FLAIR hyperintensity (\u003c4.5 h DWI-FLAIR mismatch) further indicates hyperacute stroke.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01274","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"CT, MRI, Ultrasound \u0026 Nuclear Imaging","topic":"MRI Safety Architecture","q":"A ward nurse pushes an unscreened standard ferromagnetic steel oxygen cylinder past the MRI technologist console directly into the 3.0 Tesla scanner magnet room to assist a hypoxic patient, resulting in a severe projectile accident against the bore. According to ACR MR Safe Practices, which zone boundary was breached without mandatory ferromagnetic screening, and what emergency action risks cryogenic asphyxiation if misused?","o":["Zone II to Zone III boundary requires no screening; turning off the wall power breaker instantaneously collapses the 3.0 T B0 field","Zone III to Zone IV boundary was breached; activating the emergency magnet quench button vents liquid helium outside, though vent-pipe failure can release asphyxiating cold helium gas into Zone IV","Zone IV is the public waiting lobby; opening the RF Faraday cage door eliminates the fringe magnetic field","Zone I to Zone II boundary was breached; pressing the table-stop button immediately de-energises the static B0 superconducting field"],"a":1,"e":"Under the ACR 4-Zone MRI Safety model, Zone I is public, Zone II is the screening/reception area, Zone III is the physically restricted control/prep area with \u003e5 gauss risk where non-MR personnel must be supervised, and Zone IV is the magnet room itself housing the always-on superconducting static magnetic field (B0). Only an emergency quench removes B0 rapidly by boiling off liquid helium, which carries frostbite, pressure, and asphyxiation hazards if the quench vent fails, so Zone IV must be evacuated immediately during a quench.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01275","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"CT, MRI, Ultrasound \u0026 Nuclear Imaging","topic":"MRI Nuclear Precession Physics","q":"Hydrogen-1 (1H) protons have a gyromagnetic ratio of 42.58 MHz/Tesla. What is the exact Larmor precessional frequency required for radiofrequency (RF) excitation of hydrogen protons inside a clinical 3.0 Tesla MRI scanner?","o":["42.58 MHz","63.87 MHz","127.74 MHz","14.19 MHz"],"a":2,"e":"According to the Larmor equation, precessional frequency (omega_0) = gyromagnetic ratio (gamma) x external static magnetic field strength (B_0). At B_0 = 3.0 T, omega_0 = 42.58 MHz/T x 3.0 T = 127.74 MHz. Resonance occurs only when the applied RF pulse matches this exact frequency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01276","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"CT, MRI, Ultrasound \u0026 Nuclear Imaging","topic":"Diagnostic Ultrasound Instrumentation","q":"A sonographer must perform a targeted thyroid ultrasound (depth 1-3 cm) immediately after completing a transabdominal pelvic scan in an obese adult (depth 14 cm). Which transducer choice and physical rationale apply to the thyroid examination?","o":["Keep the 2.5 MHz curvilinear transducer because low frequency minimizes acoustic attenuation and improves superficial thyroid spatial resolution","Use a 1 MHz phased-array cardiac transducer to maximize beam penetration through the strap muscles","Increase overall receiver gain on a 3.5 MHz probe to physically shorten the spatial pulse length","Switch from a 2-5 MHz curvilinear transducer to a high-frequency 7.5-12 MHz linear array transducer because shorter wavelengths yield superior axial spatial resolution at shallow depths despite higher attenuation"],"a":3,"e":"Higher ultrasound frequency yields shorter wavelengths and shorter spatial pulse lengths, directly improving axial resolution (ability to distinguish closely spaced structures along the beam axis). Although attenuation (~0.5-1 dB/cm/MHz in soft tissue) limits deep penetration at 7.5-12 MHz, superficial organs like the thyroid, breast, tendons, and carotid/DVT veins lie within 1-4 cm and benefit from a high-frequency linear array probe.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01277","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"CT, MRI, Ultrasound \u0026 Nuclear Imaging","topic":"Doppler Ultrasound Physics","q":"During pulsed-wave spectral Doppler evaluation of a tight internal carotid artery stenosis, the systolic peak velocity wraps around from the top of the spectral display into the bottom negative baseline (aliasing). Which adjustment directly raises the Nyquist limit to eliminate aliasing while maintaining accurate Doppler velocity measurement?","o":["Increase the Pulse Repetition Frequency (PRF / velocity scale), shift the baseline downward, or switch to a lower-frequency transducer while keeping Doppler angle at or below 60 degrees","Adjust the Doppler beam angle to 85-90 degrees relative to vessel flow to maximize cosine theta accuracy","Switch from pulsed-wave spectral Doppler to continuous-wave B-mode harmonic imaging","Decrease the Pulse Repetition Frequency (PRF) by half and increase sample volume gate depth"],"a":0,"e":"Aliasing occurs in pulsed-wave Doppler whenever the Doppler frequency shift exceeds the Nyquist limit (PRF / 2). Raising the PRF (velocity scale) directly increases the Nyquist limit. Shifting the zero baseline downward dedicates more of the dynamic range to forward flow, and lowering the transmitted transducer frequency reduces the Doppler shift for a given blood velocity (delta f = 2 f0 v cos theta / c). Maintaining the Doppler insonation angle \u003c=60 degrees prevents large velocity errors from rapid changes in cos theta near 90 degrees.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01278","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"CT, MRI, Ultrasound \u0026 Nuclear Imaging","topic":"Ultrasound Image Artifacts","q":"On right upper quadrant abdominal ultrasonography, Lesion X in the gallbladder lumen is an echogenic focus casting a dark anechoic band deep to it, whereas Lesion Y in the adjacent liver is an anechoic, thin-walled round structure with a bright hyperechoic band deep to it. Which pairing correctly explains Lesion X and Lesion Y?","o":["Lesion X: biliary sludge causing speed-propagation error; Lesion Y: air in bile ducts causing clean acoustic enhancement","Lesion X: gallstone causing posterior acoustic shadowing due to high attenuation/reflection; Lesion Y: simple hepatic cyst causing posterior acoustic enhancement due to low fluid attenuation","Lesion X: simple hepatic cyst causing edge refraction artifact; Lesion Y: calcified gallstone causing ring-down comet-tail artifact","Lesion X: hepatic haemangioma causing mirror-image artifact; Lesion Y: cholesterol polyp causing reverberation artifact"],"a":1,"e":"Calcified gallstones strongly reflect and absorb ultrasound energy, leaving virtually no sound beam to penetrate deeper tissues and thus casting a clean dark posterior acoustic shadow. Conversely, clear fluid inside a simple cyst attenuates ultrasound far less than surrounding solid hepatic parenchyma; consequently, echoes returning from tissue deep to the cyst are stronger than adjacent parenchyma at the same depth, producing posterior acoustic enhancement (increased through-transmission).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01279","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiographic Positioning \u0026 Special Procedures","topic":"Iodinated Contrast Media Safety","q":"A 67-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus taking metformin 1000 mg twice daily is scheduled for a contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen for suspected mesenteric ischaemia. Pre-procedure serum creatinine yields an eGFR of 26 mL/min/1.73 m2. According to ACR Contrast Media guidelines, which protocol is indicated?","o":["Give oral N-acetylcysteine alone without IV fluids and double the metformin dose after the scan","Substitute gadopentetate dimeglumine (Group I linear GBCA) IV for the CT scan to avoid renal clearance completely","Administer prophylactic isotonic IV 0.9% saline hydration, use low- or iso-osmolar non-ionic iodinated contrast if clinically essential, and withhold metformin at the time of contrast administration for 48 hours until renal function is re-evaluated","Administer high-osmolar ionic diatrizoate contrast rapidly and continue metformin uninterrupted to control blood glucose"],"a":2,"e":"In patients with eGFR \u003c30 mL/min/1.73 m2 (or acute kidney injury), intravenous iodinated contrast carries elevated risk of post-contrast acute kidney injury (PC-AKI), which can impair renal clearance of metformin and precipitate fatal metformin-associated lactic acidosis (MALA). Per ACR guidelines, isotonic saline volume expansion is the primary proven prophylaxis, non-ionic low- or iso-osmolar contrast should be used at the minimum diagnostic volume, and metformin must be held at the time of the procedure for 48 hours and restarted only after confirming stable renal function.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01280","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiographic Positioning \u0026 Special Procedures","topic":"Contrast Reaction Emergencies","q":"Two minutes after intravenous injection of non-ionic iodinated contrast media for a CT pulmonary angiogram, a 34-year-old woman develops stridor, diffuse urticaria, audible wheezing, and hypotension (BP 76/44 mmHg, SpO2 88%). After calling the emergency code team and initiating high-flow oxygen and IV fluid resuscitation, what is the single most critical first-line medication and route of administration?","o":["Subcutaneous atropine 0.6 mg to raise blood pressure","Oral cetirizine 10 mg with observation for 30 minutes","Intravenous hydrocortisone 100 mg alone as monotherapy","Intramuscular epinephrine (adrenaline) 1:1000 (0.3-0.5 mg / 0.3-0.5 mL) into the anterolateral thigh"],"a":3,"e":"Life-threatening acute contrast-induced anaphylaxis/anaphylactoid shock with airway compromise (stridor/bronchospasm) and hypotension requires immediate intramuscular epinephrine 1:1000 (1 mg/mL) at 0.3 mg (0.3 mL, up to 0.5 mg in adults) injected into the mid-anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis). Epinephrine rapidly reverses bronchospasm (beta-2), laryngeal oedema and vasodilation (alpha-1), and further mast cell degranulation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01281","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"CT, MRI, Ultrasound \u0026 Nuclear Imaging","topic":"MRI Contrast Agents","q":"Assertion (A): Group I linear gadolinium-based contrast agents (such as gadodiamide and gadopentetate dimeglumine) are contraindicated in patients with severe chronic kidney disease (eGFR \u003c 30 mL/min/1.73 m2) or acute kidney injury. Reason (R): Impaired renal excretion prolongs gadolinium residence time, and linear chelates have lower kinetic stability than macrocyclic chelates, facilitating release of toxic free Gd3+ ions that trigger Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis (NSF). Choose the correct option.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis (NSF) is a rare, debilitating fibrosing disorder of skin, joints, and internal organs linked to gadolinium exposure in severe renal failure (eGFR \u003c30 mL/min/1.73 m2 or dialysis). Group I linear non-ionic/ionic chelates bind Gd3+ in an open chain with weaker kinetic stability (higher transmetallation rate) than cage-like Group II macrocyclic agents (e.g., gadobutrol, gadoteridol, gadoterate meglumine), explaining why Group I agents are contraindicated in severe renal impairment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01282","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiation Physics \u0026 X-Ray Equipment","topic":"Dedicated Mammography Physics","q":"Why does a dedicated mammography X-ray tube utilize a Molybdenum target with a thin Molybdenum filter (Mo/Mo) at low tube potential (25-32 kVp) together with firm breast compression?","o":["Mo K-characteristic X-rays (17.5 and 19.6 keV) and the 20 keV Mo K-absorption edge maximize photoelectric soft-tissue contrast, while compression reduces thickness, scatter, motion blur, and glandular dose","Mo/Mo filtration eliminates all photons below 40 keV to harden the beam for bone penetration","Tungsten K-characteristic X-rays at 69.5 keV maximize Compton scatter in dense breast parenchyma","Breast compression increases geometric magnification blur so microcalcifications appear larger"],"a":0,"e":"Because glandular breast tissue, adipose tissue, and microcalcifications differ slightly in effective atomic number and density, mammography requires low-energy photons (~17-20 keV) where photoelectric absorption predominates to maximize subject contrast. A Mo target emits K-characteristic photons at 17.5 keV and 19.6 keV, and a Mo filter (K-edge 20.0 keV) selectively transmits this narrow band while attenuating both higher-energy Bremsstrahlung (\u003e20 keV) and useless very-low-energy (\u003c15 keV) skin-dose photons. Vigorous compression creates uniform thickness, reduces tissue thickness and scatter, brings structures closer to the detector, immobilizes motion, and lowers mean glandular dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01283","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiographic Positioning \u0026 Special Procedures","topic":"Gastrointestinal Contrast Studies","q":"A 45-year-old man with severe retching and chest pain is referred for a fluoroscopic oesophagogram to rule out Boerhaave syndrome (oesophageal perforation). He also has a mild swallowing cough suggesting possible aspiration risk. Which oral contrast agent should be selected?","o":["Standard high-density barium sulfate suspension","Water-soluble low-osmolar non-ionic iodinated contrast (e.g., iohexol)","Lipiodol oil-based iodinated contrast","High-osmolar ionic diatrizoate meglumine (Gastrografin) regardless of aspiration risk"],"a":1,"e":"Barium sulfate is strictly contraindicated when gastrointestinal or oesophageal perforation is suspected because extravasation of insoluble barium into the mediastinum or peritoneal cavity causes severe granulomatous chemical mediastinitis or peritonitis. While hyperosmolar water-soluble agents (Gastrografin) avoid barium peritonitis, if aspirated into the lungs they draw fluid across the alveolar membrane and trigger acute pulmonary oedema; therefore, low-osmolar non-ionic water-soluble iodinated contrast (such as iohexol) is safest when both perforation and aspiration are concerns.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01284","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiation Protection (AERB) \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"AERB Radiation Dose Limits","q":"According to the Atomic Energy Regulatory Board (AERB) radiation protection standards in India, what are the annual whole-body effective dose limits for an adult radiation worker and a member of the general public respectively?","o":["Radiation worker: 150 mSv/year whole body; Public: 15 mSv/year","Radiation worker: 50 mSv/year averaged over 5 years (maximum 100 mSv in any single year); Public: 5 mSv/year","Radiation worker: 20 mSv/year averaged over 5 consecutive years (maximum 30 mSv in any single year); Public: 1 mSv/year","Radiation worker: 5 mSv/year strictly; Public: 0.1 mSv/year"],"a":2,"e":"AERB follows ICRP recommendations with an Indian statutory refinement for single-year occupational exposure: effective dose for occupational radiation workers must not exceed 20 mSv/year averaged over a 5-year block (100 mSv total in 5 years), with a maximum ceiling of 30 mSv in any single year. For members of the general public, the effective dose limit is 1 mSv/year (and once pregnancy is declared by a female worker, the equivalent dose to the surface of the abdomen/fetus is restricted to 1-2 mSv for the remainder of pregnancy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01285","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiation Protection (AERB) \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Personnel Radiation Monitoring","q":"During an OSCE station on radiation safety in an interventional fluoroscopy suite in India, a radiologic technologist is handed a single BARC/AERB CaSO4:Dy Thermoluminescent Dosimeter (TLD) chest badge and a 0.5 mm Pb-equivalent wrap-around lead apron. Where must the single TLD badge be worn, and how frequently is it processed?","o":["Pinned outside the lead apron on the hip pocket facing away from the X-ray tube; processed once every 2 years","Attached to the ankle cuff below the lead apron; replaced daily after each procedure","Left inside the control console drawer during fluoroscopy; processed monthly by visual inspection","At chest level underneath the lead apron; processed quarterly (every 3 months) by an AERB-accredited laboratory"],"a":3,"e":"Under AERB personnel monitoring protocol in India, the CaSO4:Dy TLD chest badge (used for whole-body effective dose estimation) is worn at chest level UNDERNEATH the protective lead apron so it records the actual attenuated dose reaching vital trunk organs (if a second collar badge is issued for high-dose fluoroscopy, that second badge is worn outside at the collar to monitor lens/thyroid dose). TLD badges are exchanged and read quarterly (every 3 months) and must never be left inside the X-ray room or worn during personal medical X-rays.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01286","course":"B.Sc Radiology \u0026 Imaging Technology","subject":"Radiation Protection (AERB) \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Structural Shielding Calculations","q":"In planning structural shielding for a diagnostic X-ray room, the unshielded primary radiation dose rate at a controlled barrier location is 80 mGy/h. If the Half-Value Layer (HVL) of standard concrete at the operating beam quality is 3.0 cm, what minimum thickness of concrete is required to attenuate the dose rate to 10 mGy/h?","o":["9.0 cm","12.0 cm","24.0 cm","6.0 cm"],"a":0,"e":"Each Half-Value Layer (HVL) reduces X-ray beam intensity/dose rate by 50% (factor of 1/2). Starting from 80 mGy/h: after 1 HVL = 40 mGy/h; after 2 HVLs = 20 mGy/h; after 3 HVLs = 10 mGy/h (80 / 2^3 = 10). Therefore, 3 HVLs are required: 3 x 3.0 cm = 9.0 cm of concrete.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01287","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"External Beam Radiotherapy (EBRT) Dosimetry","topic":"SSD Technique Using PDD","q":"A patient is prescribed 200 cGy per fraction to a depth of 10 cm using a single anterior 6 MV photon field (10 x 10 cm2) at 100 cm SSD. The linear accelerator is calibrated to deliver 1.00 cGy/MU at dmax (1.5 cm) for a 10 x 10 cm2 field at 100 cm SSD. Given PDD(10 cm, 10x10) = 67.0% and no wedge or tray factors, how many Monitor Units (MU) are required?","o":["134 MU","299 MU","200 MU","267 MU"],"a":1,"e":"For an SSD setup, MU = Prescribed Dose / [Calibrated Output x Output Factor x (PDD/100) x Wedge/Tray Factors]. Here Output Factor = 1.00 (reference 10x10 cm2 field), PDD/100 = 0.670, so MU = 200 / (1.00 x 1.00 x 0.670) = 298.5 MU, rounded to 299 MU.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01288","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Brachytherapy Physics \u0026 Technology","topic":"Brachytherapy Radionuclides","q":"Match the clinical radiotherapy/brachytherapy radionuclide (List I) with its correct physical half-life (List II): List I: 1. Iridium-192 (HDR), 2. Cobalt-60 (Teletherapy/HDR), 3. Cesium-137 (historical LDR), 4. Iodine-125 (permanent seed). List II: A. 59.4 days, B. 30.1 years, C. 73.8 days, D. 5.26 years.","o":["1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A","1-C, 2-B, 3-D, 4-A","1-C, 2-D, 3-B, 4-A","1-A, 2-B, 3-D, 4-C"],"a":2,"e":"Iridium-192 (HDR stepping source, mean gamma energy ~0.38 MeV) has a half-life of 73.8 days (1-C). Cobalt-60 (1.17 \u0026 1.33 MeV) has a half-life of 5.26 years (2-D). Cesium-137 (0.662 MeV) has a half-life of 30.1 years (3-B). Iodine-125 (28 keV mean photon energy) has a half-life of 59.4 days (4-A).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01289","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Clinical Radiobiology","topic":"The 4 Rs of Radiobiology \u0026 Alpha/Beta Ratio","q":"Assertion (A): Conventional small-dose fractionation (1.8-2.0 Gy/fraction) preferentially spares late-responding normal tissues (such as spinal cord) relative to rapidly proliferating squamous cell carcinomas. Reason (R): Late-responding normal tissues have a low alpha/beta ratio (2-3 Gy) and a broader shoulder on the cell survival curve, conferring greater sublethal damage repair per small fraction.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"In the Linear-Quadratic (LQ) model, late-responding normal tissues possess low alpha/beta ratios (~2-3 Gy), reflecting a curvy cell-survival shoulder and high capacity for sublethal damage repair between daily fractions. Most epithelial tumors have high alpha/beta ratios (~10 Gy), making their survival curves steeper and less sensitive to fraction-size sparing—so small fractions widen the therapeutic ratio.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01290","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Electron Beam Radiotherapy Physics","topic":"Surface Dose \u0026 Practical Range","q":"Unlike megavoltage photon beams where higher beam energy increases skin sparing, how does surface (skin) percentage depth dose behave as clinical electron beam energy increases from 6 MeV to 18 MeV, and what is the approximate practical range (Rp in cm of water) of a 12 MeV electron beam?","o":["Surface dose drops to 0% at 18 MeV; Rp equals E x 2 cm","Surface dose decreases sharply from ~90% to ~30%; Rp for 12 MeV is ~24 cm","Surface dose remains identical at 100% across all energies; Rp for 12 MeV is ~3 cm","Surface dose INCREASES with higher electron energy (from ~75-80% at 6 MeV to ~90-95% at 18 MeV); Rp for 12 MeV is ~6 cm (approx. E/2)"],"a":3,"e":"In clinical electron beams, lower-energy electrons undergo wider large-angle multiple Coulomb scattering near the surface, increasing oblique path length and fluence at dmax relative to the surface—resulting in LOWER surface dose (~75-80% at 6 MeV) than high-energy electrons (~90-95% at 18 MeV, which scatter less laterally). The practical range in water is approximately Rp (cm) = Energy (MeV) / 2 = 12 / 2 = 6 cm (with therapeutic 80-90% depth around E/3 to E/4).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01291","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiotherapy Quality Assurance \u0026 AERB Standards","topic":"Daily QA Tolerance Limits","q":"During morning daily quality assurance on a clinical medical linear accelerator (non-SRS/SBRT standard conformal mode) per AAPM TG-142 and AERB protocols, what are the standard acceptable daily tolerance limits for photon X-ray output constancy, Optical Distance Indicator (ODI), and localizing lasers?","o":["Output constancy: +/- 3%; ODI: +/- 2 mm; Lasers: +/- 2 mm","Output constancy: +/- 0.1%; ODI: +/- 0.2 mm; Lasers: +/- 0.1 mm","Output constancy: +/- 5%; ODI: +/- 5 mm; Lasers: +/- 4 mm","Output constancy: +/- 10%; ODI: +/- 10 mm; Lasers: +/- 5 mm"],"a":0,"e":"Under AAPM TG-142 and AERB acceptance/routine QA guidelines for standard conventional radiotherapy LINACs, daily dosimetric output constancy tolerance is +/- 3% (monthly +/- 2%), while daily mechanical checks for Optical Distance Indicator (ODI at isocenter), localizing lasers, and collimator/light-radiation field coincidence are +/- 2 mm (+/- 1 mm for SRS/SBRT).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01292","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiation Treatment Planning","topic":"Wedge Filters \u0026 Hinge Angle","q":"A treatment planner sets up an equally weighted two-field wedged pair of 6 MV photon beams intersecting at a hinge angle of 90 degrees to treat a superficial parotid/maxillary target volume uniformly without creating a hot spot at the shallow overlap apex. Using the standard geometric formula [Wedge angle = 90 - (Hinge angle / 2)], which wedge angle is optimal?","o":["15 degrees","45 degrees","30 degrees","60 degrees"],"a":1,"e":"When two equally weighted photon beams intersect at hinge angle theta, the isodose curves tilt to compensate for missing tissue/beam overlap if Wedge Angle W = 90 deg - (theta / 2). With a 90-degree hinge angle, W = 90 - (90 / 2) = 45 degrees (with the thick heels of both wedges facing each other toward the shallow apex) to produce flat, homogeneous target isodoses.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01293","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Advanced Radiotherapy Delivery Techniques","topic":"Inverse Planning \u0026 Patient-Specific QA","q":"In Intensity-Modulated Radiotherapy (IMRT) and Volumetric Modulated Arc Therapy (VMAT), how does treatment planning differ from 3D-CRT, and what pre-treatment quality assurance metric (per AAPM TG-218) evaluates agreement between calculated and measured complex fluence maps?","o":["Forward planning with static lead blocks; verified only by visual light-field check","Manual hand calculation of SSD only; no patient-specific QA required","Inverse optimization (computer optimizes MLC leaf motion/beamlet weights from physician-defined PTV/OAR dose-volume constraints); verified via patient-specific portal/phantom dosimetry using Gamma Index analysis (standard action tolerance \u003e= 95% of points with gamma \u003c= 1 at 3% dose difference / 2 mm distance-to-agreement)","Uses low-energy orthovoltage X-rays with fixed circular cones"],"a":2,"e":"IMRT/VMAT employs inverse planning: planners specify target dose objectives and Organs-At-Risk (OAR) dose-volume constraints, and optimization algorithms modulate dynamic MLC segments (sliding window/step-and-shoot or continuous arc gantry/dose-rate modulation). Because complex MLC apertures introduce small-field dosimetric uncertainty, AAPM TG-218 recommends pre-treatment patient-specific QA using Gamma Index (\u003e= 95% pass at 3%/2 mm, 10% low-dose threshold).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01294","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"External Beam Treatment Planning","topic":"Adjacent Field Skin Gap Calculation","q":"Two adjacent posterior spinal photon fields of lengths L1 = 20 cm and L2 = 30 cm are both treated at 100 cm SSD. To prevent a dangerous spinal cord overlap hot spot at depth d = 5.0 cm below the skin, what surface skin gap (S) must be left between the two light-field borders so their diverging 50% beam edges intersect exactly at 5.0 cm depth?","o":["2.50 cm","0.50 cm","5.00 cm","1.25 cm"],"a":3,"e":"By similar triangles for two diverging fields meeting at depth d: Surface Gap S = S1 + S2 = d x [(L1 / (2 x SSD1)) + (L2 / (2 x SSD2))]. Here S = 5.0 x [(20 / 200) + (30 / 200)] = 5.0 x (0.10 + 0.15) = 5.0 x 0.25 = 1.25 cm. Leaving zero gap creates a severe overdose triangle deeper than the skin over the spinal cord.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01295","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Clinical Oncology \u0026 Organ-at-Risk (OAR) Tolerances","topic":"Malignant Spinal Cord Compression \u0026 Cord Tolerance","q":"A patient with metastatic lung adenocarcinoma develops acute progressive paraparesis and urinary retention from T6 epidural Malignant Spinal Cord Compression (MSCC) confirmed on MRI. Which immediate medical and radiotherapy sequence is indicated, and what is the conventional QUANTEC/TD5/5 maximum tolerance dose (in 2 Gy/fraction equivalents) for the full-thickness thoracic spinal cord?","o":["Administer immediate IV dexamethasone bolus + urgent palliative EBRT (e.g., 20 Gy/5 fractions or 30 Gy/10 fractions) after surgical evaluation; conventional spinal cord tolerance Dmax is ~45-50 Gy (at 2 Gy/fraction, \u003c0.2% myelopathy risk at 50 Gy)","Watchful waiting for 4 weeks; cord tolerance is 25 Gy","Delay steroids until biopsy; cord tolerance is 80 Gy","Single fraction 30 Gy directly to the entire thoracic spinal cord"],"a":0,"e":"MSCC is a true oncologic emergency requiring immediate IV dexamethasone (to reduce vasogenic cord edema) and prompt neurosurgical/radiation oncology intervention (urgent palliative EBRT within 24 h for non-surgical candidates). Under QUANTEC/Emami guidelines, keeping cumulative spinal cord Dmax \u003c= 45-50 Gy (at 1.8-2.0 Gy/fraction) keeps late radiation myelopathy risk \u003c 0.2%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01296","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiotherapy Treatment Planning Principles","topic":"ICRU Reports 50, 62 \u0026 83 Definitions","q":"Per ICRU Reports 50 and 62 in external beam radiotherapy planning, which volume adds a geometric margin specifically for day-to-day patient setup uncertainties and mechanical beam variations onto the Internal Target Volume (ITV) / Clinical Target Volume (CTV)?","o":["Gross Tumor Volume (GTV)","Planning Target Volume (PTV)","Treated Volume (TV)","Organs at Risk (OAR)"],"a":1,"e":"ICRU volume hierarchy: GTV = visible/palpable/imaging-demonstrable gross tumor; CTV = GTV plus subclinical microscopic tumor spread; ITV = CTV plus Internal Margin (IM) accounting for physiological internal organ motion (breathing, bladder filling); PTV = geometrical concept adding a Setup Margin (SM) to CTV/ITV to ensure the CTV actually receives the prescribed dose despite daily setup and machine uncertainties.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01297","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Brachytherapy Dosimetry \u0026 Source Decay","topic":"Iridium-192 Decay \u0026 Dwell Time Adjustment","q":"An HDR brachytherapy afterloader is loaded with a new Iridium-192 stepping source (physical half-life = 73.8 days) calibrated at 10.0 Ci (370 GBq) on Day 0, requiring 120 seconds total dwell time for a standard intracavitary gynecologic plan. Exactly 147.6 days (2 half-lives) later, what is the remaining Ir-192 activity, and what total dwell time is required to deliver the identical prescribed dose?","o":["2.5 Ci; 120 seconds","1.25 Ci; 960 seconds","2.5 Ci; 480 seconds","5.0 Ci; 240 seconds"],"a":2,"e":"After n = 147.6 / 73.8 = 2.0 half-lives, Activity A(t) = A0 x (0.5)^n = 10.0 x (0.5)^2 = 2.5 Ci (one-fourth of initial activity). Because delivered dose = Dose Rate x Time, reducing source activity by 4-fold requires increasing total dwell time by 4-fold: 120 s x 4 = 480 seconds to deliver the exact same prescribed dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01298","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"IGRT \u0026 Stereotactic Radiotherapy (SBRT/SRS)","topic":"Cone-Beam CT (CBCT) \u0026 Flattening-Filter-Free (FFF) Beams","q":"During Lung Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy (SBRT, 18 Gy x 3 fractions) on a modern LINAC, why does the radiotherapy technologist acquire a pre-treatment kilovoltage Cone-Beam CT (kV-CBCT) and utilize a Flattening-Filter-Free (FFF) 6 MV photon beam?","o":["kV-CBCT delivers a 10 Gy therapeutic boost; FFF widens the flat beam penumbra for large whole-pelvis fields","kV-CBCT replaces CT simulation entirely; FFF converts X-rays into 6 MeV electrons","FFF beams decrease dose rate to 50 MU/min to spare skin","kV-CBCT provides volumetric 3D soft-tissue and bony target registration (enabling tight PTV margins and 6-DOF couch correction), while removing the flattening filter (FFF) increases central-axis dose rate (e.g., 1,400-2,400 MU/min) and cuts head scatter, dramatically shortening breath-hold/treatment time"],"a":3,"e":"High-dose-per-fraction SBRT requires millimeter precision and rapid delivery to minimize intrafraction organ motion. Gantry-mounted kV-CBCT visualizes 3D tumor/soft-tissue anatomy prior to beam-on for 6-degree-of-freedom alignment. Removing the attenuating metallic flattening filter (FFF mode) yields a forward-peaked beam with 2-4x higher dose rate (1,400-2,400 MU/min) and lower out-of-field head leakage/scatter—shrinking high-MU SBRT delivery times.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01299","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Gynecologic Brachytherapy Planning","topic":"ICRU Report 38 / Manchester System","q":"In Manchester/ICRU intracavitary tandem-and-ovoids brachytherapy for carcinoma of the uterine cervix, how is prescription reference \u0027Point A\u0027 anatomically defined?","o":["2 cm superior to the external cervical os (or mucous membrane of the lateral vaginal fornix/flange) along the intrauterine tandem axis, and 2 cm lateral to the uterine canal","At the center of the Foley catheter balloon filled with 7 mL contrast","On the posterior vaginal wall 0.5 cm anterior to the rectal mucosa","5 cm superior to the vaginal introitus and 5 cm lateral to midline"],"a":0,"e":"In the classic Manchester/ICRU-38 system, Point A lies 2 cm superior to the lateral vaginal fornix / external cervical os flange along the central tandem plane and 2 cm lateral perpendicular to the tandem—approximating the paracervical triangle where the uterine artery crosses the ureter. Point B lies 5 cm lateral from the patient midline at the same craniocaudal level (representing pelvic sidewall/obturator lymph nodes).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01300","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiation Shielding \u0026 Bunker Design","topic":"Tenth-Value Layer (TVL) Calculations","q":"When designing primary concrete radiation shielding for a 6 MV LINAC bunker under NCRP 151 / AERB guidelines, if the required transmission factor (B) through the wall is 0.001 (a 1,000-fold reduction in dose rate), how many Tenth-Value Layers (TVLs) of concrete are needed, and approximately how many Half-Value Layers (HVLs) equal 1 TVL?","o":["10 TVLs; 1 TVL = 2 HVLs","3 TVLs; 1 TVL = ~3.32 HVLs","2 TVLs; 1 TVL = 5 HVLs","1 TVL; 1 TVL = 10 HVLs"],"a":1,"e":"Each Tenth-Value Layer (TVL) attenuates photon beam intensity by a factor of 10 (transmission B = 10^-n). To achieve B = 0.001 = 10^-3, exactly n = 3 TVLs are required. Because attenuation by 10 equals (1/2)^x = 1/10 -\u003e x = log10(10)/log10(2) = 1 / 0.3010 = 3.322, 1 TVL equals ~3.32 HVLs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01301","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Clinical Radiotherapy Techniques \u0026 Motion Management","topic":"Deep Inspiration Breath-Hold (DIBH)","q":"A 44-year-old woman with left-sided early-stage breast cancer following breast-conserving surgery is simulated and treated using Deep Inspiration Breath-Hold (DIBH) optical surface/spirometry gating instead of free-breathing. What anatomical shift during DIBH reduces long-term radiation-induced cardiac mortality?","o":["DIBH compresses the left lung completely so the heart receives 100% of the prescription dose","Inspiration pulls the heart anteriorly closer to the left chest wall to boost target dose","Full lung inflation expands the thoracic cavity, displacing the heart and Left Anterior Descending (LAD) coronary artery posteriorly, inferiorly, and medially AWAY from the left tangential photon fields","DIBH slows the patient\u0027s heart rate to 20 bpm during beam-on"],"a":2,"e":"During free-breathing left breast/chest-wall tangential radiotherapy, the anterior heart apex and distal LAD frequently sit close to or within the posterior tangent field edge (increasing late ischemic heart disease risk linearly with mean heart dose). Deep Inspiration Breath-Hold inflates the left lung, depressing the diaphragm and pulling the heart/LAD away from the chest wall—often reducing mean heart dose and V25 by \u003e 50%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01302","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiation Physics and Linear Accelerator Technology","topic":"Treatment Head Components","q":"In a medical linear accelerator (LINAC) producing megavoltage X-rays, what is the primary physical purpose of removing the conical flattening filter to operate in Flattening Filter Free (FFF) mode during Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT)?","o":["Creates a perfectly uniform flat beam profile at 10 cm depth across a 40 × 40 cm field","Converts bremsstrahlung photons into monoenergetic electrons at the target","Eliminates the need for primary collimators and multileaf collimators (MLC)","Substantially increases dose rate (reducing beam-on time) and reduces out-of-field head scatter"],"a":3,"e":"High-energy bremsstrahlung X-rays exiting the target are strongly forward-peaked. A traditional conical flattening filter attenuates the central axis to yield a flat profile at 10 cm depth, but drastically cuts output and generates head scatter. Removing it in FFF mode yields 2–4× higher dose rates (e.g., 1400–2400 MU/min), markedly shortening SBRT/SRS delivery times and decreasing out-of-field scatter and leakage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01303","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiobiology in Radiation Oncology","topic":"Alpha/Beta Ratio","q":"Why does hyperfractionation (smaller dose per fraction, e.g., 1.15–1.2 Gy twice daily to a slightly higher total dose) improve the therapeutic ratio in head-and-neck squamous cell carcinoma compared with conventional 2 Gy/day fractionation?","o":["Late-responding normal tissues have a low alpha/beta ratio (~2–3 Gy) and show greater repair sparing as fraction size decreases compared with tumors (alpha/beta ~10 Gy)","Twice-daily fractions completely eliminate tumor hypoxia without reoxygenation","Smaller fraction sizes prevent sublethal DNA damage repair in spinal cord neurons while accelerating repair in tumor cells","Late-responding normal tissues have a high alpha/beta ratio (~15 Gy) and repopulate faster during twice-daily irradiation"],"a":0,"e":"In the Linear-Quadratic (LQ) model, tissues with a low alpha/beta ratio (e.g., spinal cord, brain, kidney ~2–3 Gy) have a curvier survival shoulder and are highly sensitive to changes in dose per fraction. Reducing fraction size below 2 Gy preferentially spares late-responding normal tissues relative to acute-responding tumors (alpha/beta ~10 Gy), widening the therapeutic window.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01304","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Clinical Dosimetry and Treatment Planning","topic":"Monitor Unit (MU) Calculations","q":"A patient is treated with a single 6 MV photon field at 100 cm SSD to a prescribed dose of 200 cGy at 8 cm depth. The LINAC is calibrated to deliver 1.00 cGy/MU at dmax for a 10 × 10 cm reference field at 100 cm SSD. Given Sc,p (total scatter factor) = 1.02, PDD(8 cm) = 75.0%, and wedge factor (WF) = 0.65, how many Monitor Units (MU) must be delivered?","o":["308 MU","402 MU","261 MU","195 MU"],"a":1,"e":"For an SSD setup: MU = Prescribed Dose / [Output × Sc,p × (PDD/100) × WF]. Substituting the given parameters: MU = 200 / (1.00 × 1.02 × 0.75 × 0.65) = 200 / 0.49725 = 402.2 MU (rounded to 402 MU).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01305","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Clinical Radiotherapy Techniques","topic":"Electron Depth Dose Characteristics","q":"A post-mastectomy chest wall scar and dermal lymphatics extending from the skin surface (0 cm) to a depth of 2.5 cm require en-face electron irradiation to encompass the target within the \u003e=90% isodose line while minimizing lung dose. Using standard clinical rules of thumb (R90 ≈ E/4 cm, Rp ≈ E/2 cm) and surface-dose considerations, which electron energy and bolus combination is most appropriate?","o":["20 MeV electrons without bolus","4 MeV electrons without bolus","9 MeV or 12 MeV electrons with a 0.5–1.0 cm tissue-equivalent bolus","6 MeV electrons with a 3 cm lead shield over the target"],"a":2,"e":"Using R90 ≈ E/4 cm (or E/3.2 cm), a 12 MeV beam places R90 at ~3.0–3.8 cm and practical range Rp at ~6 cm. Because electron beams exhibit modest skin sparing (surface dose ~75–90% depending on energy), placing 0.5–1.0 cm of tissue-equivalent bolus raises the skin surface dose to ~95–100% and pulls the 90% isodose line up by the bolus thickness, covering 0–2.5 cm while sparing deeper lung.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01306","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Brachytherapy Technology","topic":"Cervical Cancer HDR Brachytherapy","q":"A patient with FIGO Stage IIB squamous cell carcinoma of the uterine cervix undergoes CT-guided HDR Ir-192 tandem-and-ovoid brachytherapy following 45 Gy EBRT. On orthogonal/CT reconstruction, how is classical Manchester Point A geometrically defined?","o":["On the posterior wall of the Foley catheter balloon filled with 7 mL radiopaque contrast","At the anterior surface of the posterior vaginal wall opposite the ovoids","5 cm superior along the tandem axis and 5 cm lateral to the midline pelvic wall","2 cm superior along the intrauterine tandem axis from the external cervical os (tandem flange/mucosa of lateral fornix) and 2 cm lateral perpendicular to the tandem"],"a":3,"e":"Manchester Point A is defined 2 cm superior along the intrauterine tandem axis from the external cervical os (flange/mucosa of lateral vaginal fornix) and 2 cm lateral in the plane of the uterus—anatomically representing the paracervical triangle where the uterine artery crosses the ureter. Today, 3D IGABT optimizes HR-CTV D90 while respecting Point A and OAR D2cc constraints.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01307","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiation Protection and Regulatory Compliance","topic":"Occupational Dose Limits and TVL Shielding","q":"According to the Atomic Energy Regulatory Board (AERB) of India safety directives (aligned with ICRP recommendations), what is the occupational effective dose limit for a radiation worker in a radiotherapy facility, and what additional limit applies once a worker declares pregnancy?","o":["20 mSv/year averaged over 5 consecutive years (max 30 mSv in any single year); 1 mSv to the embryo/fetus over the remainder of pregnancy","1 mSv/year for occupational workers; 0.1 mSv to the fetus","5 mSv/year averaged over 5 years; 5 mSv to the fetus","50 mSv/year averaged over 5 years (max 100 mSv in a single year); 10 mSv to the fetus"],"a":0,"e":"AERB (India) specifies an occupational whole-body effective dose limit of 20 mSv/year averaged over a 5-year block (100 mSv in 5 years), with a maximum of 30 mSv in any single year. For a declared pregnant radiation worker, the equivalent dose to the surface of the abdomen (fetus) must not exceed 1 mSv for the remainder of the pregnancy (same as the annual public dose limit of 1 mSv/year).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01308","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Simulation, Immobilization and IGRT","topic":"Target Volume Margins","q":"During CT simulation for lung SBRT using 4D-CT respiration-correlated imaging, the radiation oncologist contours the tumor across all 10 breathing phases to create an envelope accounting for respiratory excursion, and then adds an additional geometric margin for daily patient setup uncertainty. Which two ICRU volumes correspond to these steps respectively?","o":["Clinical Target Volume (CTV) followed by Organ at Risk (OAR)","Internal Target Volume (ITV) followed by Planning Target Volume (PTV)","Treated Volume followed by Irradiated Volume","Gross Tumor Volume (GTV) followed by Clinical Target Volume (CTV)"],"a":1,"e":"Per ICRU Reports 62 and 83, the Internal Target Volume (ITV) adds an Internal Margin (IM) to the CTV to account for physiological internal motion (such as breathing excursion captured on 4D-CT). The Planning Target Volume (PTV) adds a Setup Margin (SM) around the ITV (or CTV) to account for mechanical and daily patient positioning uncertainties.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01309","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Quality Assurance in Radiotherapy","topic":"Periodic QA Schedule","q":"Match the LINAC Quality Assurance (QA) check with its standard AAPM TG-142 / AERB required frequency and tolerance: (1) Daily QA, (2) Monthly QA, (3) Annual QA. Which matching sequence is correct?","o":["1-Wedge transmission constancy; 2-Absolute calibration (±1%); 3-Output constancy (±3%) \u0026 laser alignment","1-Flatness/symmetry \u0026 TRS-398 calibration; 2-Output constancy (±3%) \u0026 lasers; 3-Door interlock","1-Photon/electron output constancy (±3%), laser alignment, door interlock \u0026 audio-visual monitor; 2-Output constancy (±2%), light-radiation field coincidence, wedge transmission constancy; 3-Absolute calibration (TRS-398/TG-51 ±1%), flatness/symmetry, radiation isocenter","1-Absolute calibration (±1%); 2-Door interlock \u0026 audio-visual monitor; 3-Light-radiation field coincidence"],"a":2,"e":"AAPM TG-142 and AERB QA protocols stratify checks by clinical risk and drift speed: Daily checks verify output constancy (±3%), lasers, ODI, and patient safety interlocks before treatment; Monthly checks tighten output constancy (±2%) and verify light-radiation coincidence, jaw/MLC accuracy, and accessory factors; Annual checks perform reference TRS-398/TG-51 dosimetry (±1%), PDD/profiles, and mechanical/radiation isocenter star-shots.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01310","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Clinical Radiotherapy Setup \u0026 Beam Modifiers","topic":"Eliminating Skin Sparing for Superficial Targets","q":"Following mastectomy for inflammatory breast carcinoma with dermal lymphatic invasion, the radiation oncologist prescribes 1.0 cm tissue-equivalent bolus over the chest-wall scar during 6 MV photon radiotherapy. What dosimetric purpose does placing bolus directly on the skin achieve?","o":["Acts as a lead block to reduce scar dose to 0%","Increases skin sparing by pushing dmax 10 cm deeper into the lung","Converts 6 MV photons into heavy alpha particles","Pulls the megavoltage photon buildup region (dmax ~1.5 cm for 6 MV) forward onto the skin surface, raising skin/scar dose to ~95-100% by eliminating the skin-sparing effect"],"a":3,"e":"Open 6 MV photon beams spare the epidermis/dermis (surface dose ~25-40%) because secondary electrons build up to peak dose at dmax = 1.5 cm. When tumor involves skin, surgical scar, or superficial nodes, placing 0.5-1.0 cm tissue-equivalent bolus directly against the skin (eliminating air gaps) shifts the buildup region into the bolus so the patient\u0027s skin receives near-100% therapeutic dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01311","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiation Physics \u0026 Protection","topic":"Point Source Exposure Rate Scaling","q":"During a brachytherapy radiation survey, a point source produces an exposure rate of 400 mR/hr at a distance of 0.5 meters. Applying the Inverse Square Law [I2 = I1 x (d1 / d2)^2], what is the exposure rate at a distance of 2.0 meters?","o":["25 mR/hr","100 mR/hr","12.5 mR/hr","50 mR/hr"],"a":0,"e":"Moving from d1 = 0.5 m to d2 = 2.0 m increases distance by a factor of 4 (d2 / d1 = 4). By the Inverse Square Law, intensity drops by 4^2 = 16-fold: I2 = 400 x (0.5 / 2.0)^2 = 400 x (1/4)^2 = 400 / 16 = 25 mR/hr—demonstrating why stepping back from a source dramatically reduces staff dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01312","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiotherapy Radiation Emergencies","topic":"Stuck HDR Ir-192 Source Protocol","q":"Upon completion of an HDR Ir-192 vaginal cylinder fraction, the afterloader console displays \u0027Source Retraction Failure\u0027 and the independent room area radiation monitor continues flashing red. What is the mandatory emergency response sequence by the radiotherapy technologist and medical physicist?","o":["Reset the console power breaker without checking the room radiation monitor","Press the console emergency retract button, enter the room immediately carrying a calibrated survey meter, attempt manual hand-crank retraction into the safe; if still exposed, use long-handled forceps to remove the applicator/transfer tube and place it into the emergency lead storage pig, evacuate the patient immediately, secure the room, and notify the RSO","Pick up the bare Ir-192 source cable directly with gloved hands to inspect the weld","Leave the patient inside the bunker for 1 hour to let Ir-192 decay completely"],"a":1,"e":"A stuck 10 Ci Ir-192 HDR source delivers extreme local dose rates to the patient every second. Pre-rehearsed AERB/AAPM emergency procedures mandate: (1) trigger console emergency retraction, (2) enter immediately with survey meter (never without a meter!), (3) manually crank the drive cable back, or (4) rapidly remove the applicator containing the source using long-handled forceps (inverse-square protection; NEVER touch with bare/gloved fingers!) into the bedside lead emergency container, verify zero room activity, evacuate the patient, and alert the RSO.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01313","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Advanced Radiotherapy Delivery Techniques","topic":"Volumetric Modulated Arc Therapy (VMAT)","q":"Which combination of three parameters is continuously and simultaneously modulated by a LINAC during Volumetric Modulated Arc Therapy (VMAT) delivery?","o":["Patient couch vertical height, electron applicator size, and source-to-tray distance","Flattening filter thickness, bending magnet current, and target tungsten density","Gantry rotation speed, multileaf collimator (MLC) leaf positions, and dose rate (MU/min)","Beam energy (6 MV to 18 MV mid-arc), SSD (100 to 150 cm), and physical wedge angle"],"a":2,"e":"Volumetric Modulated Arc Therapy (VMAT) achieves highly conformal dose painting around concave targets by continuously varying three synchronized parameters as the gantry rotates around the patient: (1) gantry angular velocity, (2) dynamic MLC leaf aperture shape/speed, and (3) variable LINAC dose rate—reducing Monitor Units and treatment time compared with static-field IMRT.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01314","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Clinical Dosimetry and Treatment Planning","topic":"Tissue-Maximum Ratio (TMR) Calculations","q":"A pelvic tumor is treated isocentrically at 100 cm SAD using a 6 MV photon field (12 × 12 cm at isocenter, depth = 10 cm). The LINAC is calibrated to deliver 1.00 cGy/MU at isocenter (100 cm SAD) at dmax for a 10 × 10 cm reference field. Given total scatter factor Sc,p (12 × 12 cm) = 1.025 and TMR (10 cm, 12 × 12 cm) = 0.780 (no wedge or tray), how many Monitor Units (MU) are required to deliver 180 cGy to the isocenter?","o":["176 MU","281 MU","144 MU","225 MU"],"a":3,"e":"For an isocentric (SAD) setup calibrated at SAD at dmax, distance correction is unity and PDD is replaced by Tissue-Maximum Ratio (TMR): MU = Prescribed Dose / (Output_SAD × Sc,p × TMR). Here, MU = 180 / (1.00 × 1.025 × 0.780) = 180 / 0.7995 = 225.14 MU (225 MU).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01315","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Particle Therapy and Emerging Modalities","topic":"Bragg Peak and SOBP","q":"Why does modulated Spread-Out Bragg Peak (SOBP) proton therapy offer a major dosimetric advantage over megavoltage X-ray IMRT for pediatric craniospinal irradiation or skull-base chordoma?","o":["Protons deposit maximal energy near the end of their finite range (Bragg peak) with virtually zero exit dose beyond the target, markedly reducing low-to-moderate integral dose to anterior normal organs","Protons require no immobilization because they automatically track tumor motion in air","Protons undergo pair production in bone, sparing the skull while delivering high exit dose to the heart and thyroid","Proton beams have zero entrance skin dose and higher RBE (20×) than alpha particles"],"a":0,"e":"Heavy charged particles (protons) lose energy inversely proportional to velocity squared (Bethe-Bloch), peaking sharply just before stopping (Bragg peak). Stacking weighted energies creates a uniform Spread-Out Bragg Peak (SOBP) across the tumor depth while delivering essentially zero exit dose distal to the target—sparing anterior heart, lungs, thyroid, and growing pediatric tissues from integral bath dose and secondary malignancy risk.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01316","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Brachytherapy Technology","topic":"Stuck Source Emergency Protocol","q":"At the end of an HDR Ir-192 intracavitary fraction, the console radiation monitor alarms and the room area monitor remains high, indicating the source failed to retract into the shielded safe. What is the correct emergency sequence for the radiotherapy technologist?","o":["Spray water on the afterloader and apply a 2-mm lead apron over the Ir-192 source capsule","Press the console/wall emergency-off button, enter with a calibrated survey meter, attempt manual hand-crank retraction; if unsuccessful, use long-handled forceps to remove the applicator into the lead emergency container, evacuate the patient, secure the room, and notify RSO/AERB","Turn off the main hospital electricity breaker and leave the patient inside the bunker for 74 days (one Ir-192 half-life)","Immediately run in without a survey meter, pull the source wire with bare gloved hands, and place the patient back on the waiting bench"],"a":1,"e":"Every HDR Ir-192 room must have a posted emergency procedure, calibrated portable survey meter, manual crank, long-handled forceps/wire cutters, and shielded lead bail-out container. The technologist presses Emergency Off, verifies radiation status with a survey meter, tries manual retraction (keeping time brief and distance maximal), and—if stuck—removes the entire applicator with long-handled forceps into the lead pig, removes the patient, surveys both patient and room, locks the bunker, and notifies the RSO and AERB.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01317","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Clinical Radiotherapy Techniques","topic":"Intracranial SRS Principles","q":"A 54-year-old patient with a solitary 1.8 cm brain metastasis located 1.5 cm from the optic chiasm is scheduled for single-fraction Stereotactic Radiosurgery (SRS) to 20 Gy. Which physical and dosimetric principles characterize safe SRS delivery?","o":["Use a single large parallel-opposed AP/PA photon pair with 2 cm PTV margin to homogenize skull dose","Deliver SRS using 6 MeV electrons without CT/MRI fusion","Use multiple non-coplanar arcs/beams with sub-millimeter IGRT/frame immobilization to achieve a steep dose fall-off outside the target while keeping single-fraction optic chiasm Dmax \u003c= 8–10 Gy","Prescribe 60 Gy in 1 fraction to overcome tumor radioresistance regardless of chiasm dose"],"a":2,"e":"SRS delivers an ablative single (or 1–5) fraction using high-resolution MRI/CT fusion, rigid stereotactic frame or frameless CBCT/6-DOF robotic couch sub-millimeter localization, and multiple converging non-coplanar beams/arcs (e.g., VMAT, CyberKnife, Gamma Knife) to create rapid isotropic dose fall-off—protecting serial critical structures such as the optic nerves/chiasm (single-fraction Dmax constraint ~8–10 Gy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01318","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Clinical Radiotherapy Techniques","topic":"Extended SSD and Beam Spoiler Physics","q":"During myeloablative Total Body Irradiation (TBI, e.g., 12 Gy in 6 fractions) delivered at 350–400 cm extended SSD with 6 MV photons, why is a 1–2 cm thick acrylic (Perspex) beam spoiler placed immediately in front of the patient alongside customized lung blocks/compensators?","o":["Extended SSD converts photons into thermal neutrons, and the spoiler slows them down","The acrylic spoiler blocks all 6 MV photons from reaching the pelvis while doubling kidney dose","Lung blocks ensure the lungs receive 20 Gy higher dose than the skeleton to sterilize leukemic cells","The acrylic spoiler generates forward-scattered secondary electrons to raise skin/superficial bone marrow dose, while lung compensators keep lung dose \u003c= 8–10 Gy to prevent fatal interstitial pneumonitis"],"a":3,"e":"Megavoltage photons exhibit skin sparing at dmax, which would underdose superficial ribs, sternum, and leukemic skin/subcutaneous sanctuaries during TBI. Placing an acrylic beam spoiler ~10–30 cm upstream of the patient restores surface dose (~90–95%) via contaminant electrons. Simultaneously, low-density lungs transmit ~15–20% more radiation and are highly radiosensitive; custom lung blocks/compensators cap lung dose at ~8–10 Gy to prevent radiation pneumonitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01319","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Clinical Dosimetry and Treatment Planning","topic":"Head and Neck IMRT Constraints","q":"A patient with locally advanced oropharyngeal carcinoma is planned for 70 Gy in 35 fractions using bilateral neck IMRT. Which QUANTEC conventional-fractionation constraints must the dosimetrist and technologist verify for the spinal cord and parotid glands to prevent myelopathy and permanent xerostomia?","o":["Spinal cord Dmax \u003c= 45–50 Gy; mean dose \u003c 26 Gy to at least one parotid gland (or \u003c 20 Gy to both parotids combined)","Spinal cord Dmax \u003c= 70 Gy; mean parotid dose \u003c= 60 Gy","Spinal cord mean dose \u003c= 10 Gy; parotid Dmax \u003c= 5 Gy","Spinal cord V60 \u003c 50%; parotid mean dose \u003e= 40 Gy"],"a":0,"e":"Under QUANTEC recommendations for 2 Gy/fx conventional radiotherapy, keeping spinal cord maximum point dose (with a 3–5 mm PRV margin) \u003c= 45–50 Gy limits radiation myelopathy risk to \u003c 0.2%. Maintaining mean dose \u003c 26 Gy to at least one parotid (or \u003c 20 Gy if both are spared) preserves \u003e= 25% baseline salivary flow and prevents severe chronic xerostomia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01320","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiation Physics and Dosimetry Instrumentation","topic":"Clinical Dosimeters","q":"Match each radiotherapy dosimeter with its primary clinical application: (1) Farmer-type 0.6 cc thimble ionization chamber, (2) LiF:Mg,Ti TLD / Al2O3:C OSLD, (3) Radiochromic (Gafchromic EBT) film, (4) Electronic Portal Imaging Device (EPID). Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Reference absolute photon/electron output calibration in water phantom (TRS-398/TG-51); 2-In-vivo patient skin/pacemaker dosimetry and personnel monitoring; 3-High-spatial-resolution 2D planar IMRT/SRS dose verification without darkroom processing; 4-Patient setup verification and portal/transit dosimetry","1-Reference absolute calibration; 2-Portal imaging; 3-In-vivo pacemaker dosimetry; 4-Personnel monitoring","1-In-vivo skin dosimetry; 2-Reference absolute calibration in water; 3-Patient setup verification; 4-2D planar film verification","1-High-resolution 2D SRS verification; 2-Portal imaging; 3-Reference absolute calibration; 4-Personnel monitoring"],"a":0,"e":"The 0.6 cc waterproof Farmer ionization chamber is the international reference standard for absolute beam calibration per IAEA TRS-398/AAPM TG-51. TLDs/OSLDs are compact, tissue-equivalent passive detectors ideal for in-vivo patient measurements and monthly personnel badges. Radiochromic EBT film self-develops via polymerization with sub-millimeter resolution for IMRT/SRS QA. EPIDs (amorphous silicon flat panels) verify daily patient anatomy and transit fluence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01321","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiation Physics","topic":"Photoelectric vs Compton Effect","q":"Why do megavoltage therapy photons (e.g., 6 MV) produce near-uniform absorbed dose per unit mass in cortical bone and soft tissue, whereas 80 kVp diagnostic X-rays show markedly higher absorption in bone?","o":["At 6 MV, photons pass through bone without any interaction whatsoever","At 6 MV, Compton scattering predominates (independent of atomic number Z and proportional to electron density), whereas at 80 kVp the photoelectric effect predominates (proportional to Z^3/E^3, favoring high-Z calcium in bone)","At 80 kVp, Compton scattering is proportional to Z^4","At 6 MV, pair production completely stops inside soft tissue before reaching bone"],"a":1,"e":"Photoelectric probability varies roughly as Z^3/E^3, causing high-Z cortical bone (Z_eff ~13.8 vs soft tissue ~7.4) to absorb far more kilovoltage X-rays—useful for diagnostic bone-soft-tissue contrast, but historically causing osteoradionecrosis in orthovoltage therapy. In the megavoltage range (~100 keV–10 MeV), Compton scattering dominates; its mass attenuation coefficient is independent of Z and depends only on electrons per gram (nearly identical for bone and soft tissue).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01322","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Clinical Dosimetry and Treatment Planning","topic":"Wedge Filters and Hinge Angle","q":"Two 6 MV photon fields intersect perpendicularly at a hinge angle of 90 degrees to treat an anterior maxillary sinus tumor. Using the standard wedge-hinge relationship (Wedge Angle = 90° - Hinge Angle/2), which wedge angle and heel orientation create a homogeneous dose distribution across the target volume?","o":["45-degree wedges on both beams with the thick wedge HEELS placed adjacent to each other (toward the acute beam-intersection side)","15-degree wedges with the thin toes placed adjacent to each other","60-degree wedges oriented perpendicular to the plane of intersection","45-degree wedges with the thin TOES placed adjacent to each other"],"a":0,"e":"When two beams intersect at hinge angle phi = 90°, their unweighted overlap creates an intense hot spot near the narrow intersection side and underdoses the broad far side. Applying Wedge Angle = 90° − (90°/2) = 45° wedges with the thick HEELS facing the intersection side (closest to each other) attenuates the overlap region and tilts the isodose curves parallel to the bisector for uniform target coverage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01323","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiobiology in Radiation Oncology","topic":"BED and EQD2 Calculations","q":"A patient with medically inoperable Stage I non-small-cell lung cancer receives SBRT to 50 Gy in 5 fractions (10 Gy per fraction). Assuming tumor alpha/beta = 10 Gy, calculate the Biological Effective Dose (BED10) and the Equivalent Dose in 2-Gy fractions (EQD2) using BED = D × [1 + d/(alpha/beta)] and EQD2 = BED / [1 + 2/(alpha/beta)].","o":["BED10 = 75.0 Gy; EQD2 = 62.5 Gy","BED10 = 50.0 Gy; EQD2 = 50.0 Gy","BED10 = 100.0 Gy; EQD2 = 83.3 Gy","BED10 = 216.7 Gy; EQD2 = 180.6 Gy"],"a":2,"e":"With D = 50 Gy, d = 10 Gy/fx, and alpha/beta = 10 Gy: BED10 = 50 × [1 + (10 / 10)] = 50 × 2 = 100.0 Gy10 (meeting the \u003e=100 Gy10 benchmark associated with \u003e90% local control in lung SBRT). Dividing by the 2-Gy relative effectiveness factor (1 + 2/10 = 1.2): EQD2 = 100.0 / 1.2 = 83.3 Gy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01324","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Simulation, Immobilization and IGRT","topic":"Deep Inspiration Breath-Hold (DIBH)","q":"A 44-year-old woman with left-sided early breast cancer post-lumpectomy undergoes CT simulation and whole-breast tangential radiotherapy using Deep Inspiration Breath-Hold (DIBH) with optical surface/spirometric tracking. What anatomical shift achieved by DIBH dramatically reduces long-term ischemic heart disease risk?","o":["Deep inspiration pulls the heart anteriorly against the chest wall to keep it motionless","DIBH doubles the biological alpha/beta ratio of cardiomyocytes","Holding breath eliminates photon attenuation inside breast adipose tissue","Deep lung expansion pushes the heart and left anterior descending (LAD) coronary artery posteriorly, inferiorly, and medially out of the left tangential photon fields"],"a":3,"e":"During deep inspiration, expansion of the left lung and downward excursion of the diaphragm displace the pericardium and LAD coronary artery inferiorly and posteriorly away from the anterior chest wall and tangential field borders, often reducing mean heart dose and maximum LAD dose by 40–60%+ compared with free-breathing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01325","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Advanced Radiotherapy Delivery Techniques","topic":"Spine SBRT Precision Requirements","q":"A patient with an isolated T8 vertebral body metastasis abutting the epidural space is treated with Spine SBRT (24 Gy in 2 fractions). Because the spinal cord lies within 2 mm of the high-dose PTV, what setup verification and immobilization requirements are mandatory prior to each fraction?","o":["Rigid near-body immobilization (e.g., vacuum cushion), pre-treatment CBCT with 6-degree-of-freedom (6-DOF) translational and rotational couch correction (\u003c1 mm / \u003c1° tolerance), and intra-fraction motion monitoring","Single posterior direct electron beam at 6 MeV","Weekly 2D MV portal films without rotational pitch/roll/yaw correction","No immobilization with 3 cm isotropic CTV-to-PTV margin"],"a":0,"e":"Spine SBRT features an ultra-steep dose gradient (~10% per mm) between the vertebral target and adjacent spinal cord. Even a 1–2° rotational pitch/roll error or 2 mm shift can overdose the cord and cause radiation myelopathy; therefore, rigid vacuum immobilization, volumetric 3D CBCT matching with 6-DOF robotic couch correction (plus mid-fraction verification/FFF fast delivery) are essential.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01326","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Clinical Radiotherapy Techniques","topic":"Metastatic Spinal Cord Compression (MSCC)","q":"A 62-year-old man with metastatic lung adenocarcinoma develops acute mid-thoracic back pain, progressive bilateral leg weakness (unable to stand since this morning), and urinary retention. What is the immediate emergency workflow prior to and alongside urgent radiotherapy?","o":["Administer spinal manipulation physiotherapy first without MRI or steroids","Administer immediate IV dexamethasone loading dose, obtain urgent whole-spine MRI to localize compression levels, arrange prompt surgical decompression evaluation, and deliver urgent palliative RT (e.g., 20 Gy/5 fx or 8 Gy/1 fx)","Give oral calcium supplements alone and re-evaluate in 1 month","Defer imaging for 2 weeks and book routine 35-fraction radical IMRT"],"a":1,"e":"Metastatic Spinal Cord Compression (MSCC) is a true oncologic emergency where ambulatory prognosis depends heavily on baseline neurologic status and speed of decompression (\u003c24–48 h). Immediate IV dexamethasone reduces vasogenic cord edema, urgent whole-spine MRI defines all epidural levels (skip lesions occur in ~20–30%), neurosurgical evaluation assesses stability/decompression (Patchell trial), and urgent palliative RT is initiated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01327","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Simulation, Immobilization and IGRT","topic":"HU-to-Electron-Density Calibration","q":"Why must a radiotherapy department calibrate its CT simulator with an electron-density phantom (HU-to-RED curve) before calculating clinical treatment plans, and why is an uncorrected daily kV Cone-Beam CT (CBCT) generally NOT used directly for primary dose calculation without scatter correction or density mapping?","o":["CT Hounsfield Units measure magnetic resonance T1 relaxation times rather than X-ray attenuation","Electron-density phantoms convert 6 MV photons into diagnostic kV X-rays","Treatment planning algorithms require accurate mapping from CT Hounsfield Units to tissue Relative Electron Density (RED) for heterogeneity corrections; wide-cone CBCT suffers substantial X-ray scatter and cupping/HU inaccuracies","CBCT has zero scatter because it uses a single narrow fan slice"],"a":2,"e":"Modern collapsed-cone/Monte Carlo dose engines compute megavoltage attenuation using electron/mass density derived from a scanner-specific HU-to-RED calibration curve (measured with tissue-equivalent inserts from lung to cortical bone). Unlike narrow-collimated fan-beam planning CT, wide-cone CBCT accepts large amounts of scattered radiation onto the flat-panel detector, distorting HU linearity (cupping artifacts) unless scatter-corrected or bulk-overridden for adaptive replanning.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01328","course":"B.Sc Radiotherapy Technology","subject":"Radiation Physics and Linear Accelerator Technology","topic":"Cobalt-60 vs Megavoltage LINAC","q":"Match each teletherapy characteristic with either Cobalt-60 (Co-60) or 6 MV Medical LINAC: (1) Monoenergetic gamma rays at 1.17 and 1.33 MeV (mean 1.25 MeV) with dmax = 0.5 cm, (2) Broad geometric penumbra caused by ~1.5–2.0 cm cylindrical source diameter, (3) Polyenergetic bremsstrahlung photon spectrum with dmax = 1.5 cm and ~1–2 mm focal spot, (4) Continuous radioactive decay (t1/2 = 5.26 years) requiring periodic timer adjustment and eventual source replacement. Which sequence is correct?","o":["1-Cobalt-60; 2-6 MV LINAC; 3-6 MV LINAC; 4-6 MV LINAC","1-6 MV LINAC; 2-6 MV LINAC; 3-Cobalt-60; 4-6 MV LINAC","1-6 MV LINAC; 2-Cobalt-60; 3-Cobalt-60; 4-Cobalt-60","1-Cobalt-60; 2-Cobalt-60; 3-6 MV LINAC; 4-Cobalt-60"],"a":3,"e":"Co-60 decays (t1/2 = 5.26 y) emitting 1.17 and 1.33 MeV gamma photons (dmax ~0.5 cm), has a relatively large 1.5–2.0 cm physical source diameter creating wider geometric penumbra, and loses ~1.1% output per month. Conversely, a 6 MV LINAC accelerates electrons onto a tungsten target to emit polyenergetic bremsstrahlung X-rays (mean ~2 MeV, dmax ~1.5 cm) from a tiny ~1–2 mm focal spot, yielding crisp beam edges and zero radioactive decay.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01329","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Mechanical Ventilation","topic":"ARDSNet Protocol","q":"A 50-year-old man (height 170 cm, actual body weight 95 kg) is intubated for severe ARDS. Using the ARDSNet formula for males [PBW = 50 + 0.91 x (height in cm - 152.4)], what initial target tidal volume (Vt) corresponds to 6 mL/kg PBW?","o":["396 mL","660 mL","480 mL","570 mL"],"a":0,"e":"PBW = 50 + 0.91 x (170 - 152.4) = 50 + (0.91 x 17.6) = 50 + 16.016 = 66.0 kg. Lung-protective tidal volume is indexed to PBW (which correlates with lung size, not adipose mass): 6 mL/kg x 66.0 kg = 396 mL (~400 mL), keeping plateau pressure \u003c= 30 cmH2O and driving pressure \u003c 15 cmH2O.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01330","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Ventilator Graphics \u0026 Troubleshooting","topic":"Peak vs Plateau Pressure Gradient","q":"A patient on volume-controlled ventilation (Vt 420 mL, square flow) triggers a high-pressure alarm as Peak Inspiratory Pressure (PIP) rises from 24 to 42 cmH2O. An end-inspiratory hold maneuver shows Plateau Pressure (Pplat) remains unchanged at 20 cmH2O. What is the physiological abnormality and primary cause?","o":["Decreased chest wall compliance from intra-abdominal hypertension","Increased airway resistance (PIP - Pplat = 22 cmH2O) due to bronchospasm, mucus plugging, or kinked endotracheal tube","Decreased static lung compliance due to acute tension pneumothorax","Excessive PEEP causing alveolar overdistension"],"a":1,"e":"PIP reflects both resistive pressure (flow x airway resistance) and elastic recoil pressure (Pplat). An inspiratory hold stops gas flow, eliminating resistive pressure so Pplat reflects alveolar compliance alone. A widened PIP-Pplat gradient (\u003e 5 cmH2O; here 22 cmH2O) with stable Pplat isolates the problem to elevated airway resistance (secretions, bronchospasm, tube biting/kinking).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01331","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Critical Care Respiratory Therapy","topic":"Spontaneous Breathing Trial (SBT)","q":"Ten minutes into a T-piece Spontaneous Breathing Trial (SBT) at FiO2 0.40, an intubated COPD patient has a respiratory rate of 32 breaths/min and a spontaneous tidal volume of 250 mL. What is the patient\u0027s Rapid Shallow Breathing Index (RSBI), and what does it predict?","o":["RSBI = 0.128 breaths/min/L; high likelihood of successful extubation","RSBI = 320 breaths/min/L; administer intravenous neuromuscular blocker","RSBI = 128 breaths/min/L (\u003e105 threshold); high risk of weaning failure requiring resumption of ventilatory support","RSBI = 80 breaths/min/L; proceed immediately to extubation"],"a":2,"e":"RSBI = Respiratory frequency (f, breaths/min) / Tidal volume (Vt in liters). Converting 250 mL to 0.25 L gives RSBI = 32 / 0.25 = 128 breaths/min/L. Per Yang and Tobin criteria, an RSBI \u003e 105 breaths/min/L indicates rapid shallow breathing fatigue and strongly predicts weaning/extubation failure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01332","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Physiology \u0026 Blood Gases","topic":"Alveolar Gas Equation \u0026 A-a Gradient","q":"A 40-year-old patient breathing room air (FiO2 = 0.21) at sea level (barometric pressure = 760 mmHg, water vapor pressure = 47 mmHg) has an ABG showing PaO2 = 60 mmHg and PaCO2 = 40 mmHg (respiratory quotient R = 0.8). Using the Alveolar Gas Equation, what is the Alveolar-Arterial (A-a) O2 gradient, and what mechanism of hypoxemia does it exclude?","o":["A-a gradient = 90 mmHg; confirms high-altitude hypoxia","A-a gradient = 10 mmHg; confirms pure hypoventilation","A-a gradient = 20 mmHg; rules out V/Q mismatch","A-a gradient = 40 mmHg (elevated); excludes pure hypoventilation or low inspired PO2 as the sole cause"],"a":3,"e":"PAO2 = FiO2 x (PB - PH2O) - (PaCO2 / R) = 0.21 x (760 - 47) - (40 / 0.8) = (0.21 x 713) - 50 = 149.7 - 50 = 99.7 mmHg (~100 mmHg). A-a gradient = PAO2 - PaO2 = 100 - 60 = 40 mmHg (normal for age 40 is \u003c= 14 mmHg). An elevated A-a gradient indicates V/Q mismatch, right-to-left shunt, or diffusion impairment, excluding pure hypoventilation (which has a normal A-a gradient).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01333","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Non-Invasive Ventilation \u0026 Oxygen Therapy","topic":"BiPAP Initiation \u0026 Oxygen Target","q":"A 66-year-old conscious patient with acute COPD exacerbation is tachypneic (RR 28/min) with ABG pH 7.26, PaCO2 68 mmHg, PaO2 54 mmHg, and HCO3- 30 mEq/L. Which Non-Invasive Positive Pressure Ventilation (BiPAP) strategy and SpO2 target should the respiratory therapist initiate?","o":["BiPAP starting at IPAP 12 cmH2O / EPAP 4 cmH2O (providing 8 cmH2O pressure support to augment tidal volume and clear CO2), titrating FiO2 to target SpO2 88-92%","CPAP 5 cmH2O with non-rebreather mask targeting SpO2 100%","BiPAP IPAP 6 cmH2O / EPAP 14 cmH2O targeting SpO2 \u003e= 98%","High-flow nasal cannula at 15 L/min FiO2 1.0 without ABG reassessment"],"a":0,"e":"BiPAP (bilevel PAP) is first-line therapy for acute hypercapnic acidemic COPD exacerbation (pH 7.25-7.35). The IPAP-EPAP gradient (pressure support = 8 cmH2O here) unloads fatigued respiratory muscles, boosts tidal volume, and reduces PaCO2, while EPAP (4-5 cmH2O) counterbalances intrinsic auto-PEEP. Controlled O2 targeting SpO2 88-92% prevents worsening hypercapnia from V/Q hypoxic vasoconstriction reversal and the Haldane effect.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01334","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Pulmonary Function Testing (PFT)","topic":"Obstructive vs Restrictive Patterns","q":"Which pulmonary function test profile performed in the PFT lab definitively distinguishes a pure restrictive ventilatory defect from an obstructive ventilatory defect?","o":["Increase in FEV1 by \u003e= 12% and \u003e= 200 mL post-salbutamol","Reduced FVC (\u003c 80% predicted), reduced TLC (\u003c 80% predicted), and normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio (\u003e= 0.75-0.80)","FEV1/FVC ratio \u003c 0.70 with increased Total Lung Capacity (TLC \u003e 120% predicted)","Scooped (\u0027coved\u0027) expiratory flow-volume loop with prolonged forced expiratory time"],"a":1,"e":"A pure restrictive ventilatory defect (e.g., interstitial lung disease, kyphoscoliosis, neuromuscular weakness) proportionally reduces both FEV1 and FVC, leaving the FEV1/FVC ratio normal or elevated (\u003e= 0.75-0.80), while confirmatory lung volume testing shows Total Lung Capacity (TLC) \u003c 80% predicted (or \u003c LLN).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01335","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Mechanical Ventilation Waveforms","topic":"Auto-PEEP (Intrinsic PEEP)","q":"In an intubated status asthmaticus patient on volume-controlled ventilation (RR 22/min, Vt 450 mL, I:E 1:1.5, set PEEP 5 cmH2O), the ventilator flow-time waveform shows that expiratory flow fails to return to zero baseline before the next inspiration begins, and an end-expiratory hold measures Total PEEP = 15 cmH2O (Auto-PEEP = 10 cmH2O). Which ventilator adjustment MOST effectively reduces Auto-PEEP?","o":["Prolong inspiratory pause to 1.0 second","Increase tidal volume to 700 mL","Decrease respiratory rate (e.g., to 12-14/min) and increase inspiratory flow rate (shortening Ti) to lengthen expiratory time (Te), alongside bronchodilator therapy","Increase respiratory rate to 28/min and lower inspiratory flow rate"],"a":2,"e":"Auto-PEEP (dynamic hyperinflation) occurs when severe airflow obstruction and insufficient expiratory time (Te) prevent complete alveolar emptying, elevating intrathoracic pressure and risking hypotension/barotrauma. Prolonging Te—by lowering respiratory rate, reducing minute ventilation (permissive hypercapnia), and increasing peak inspiratory flow (decreasing Ti)—allows fuller emptying.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01336","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Respiratory Pharmacology \u0026 Aerosol Therapy","topic":"Hypertonic Saline vs N-Acetylcysteine","q":"When administering nebulized N-acetylcysteine (Mucomyst) or 7% hypertonic saline to mobilize thick viscid secretions in a patient with bronchiectasis and bronchial hyperreactivity, what biochemical mechanism thins mucus for each agent, and what mandatory precaution prevents acute airway constriction?","o":["Both agents act as direct beta-2 agonists; no pretreatment needed","N-acetylcysteine breaks disulfide bonds in mucin glycoproteins while hypertonic saline osmotically draws water into airway surface liquid; administer a short-acting beta-2 agonist (salbutamol) BEFOREHAND to prevent drug-induced bronchospasm","Hypertonic saline cleaves DNA strands; administer IV atropine beforehand","N-acetylcysteine coats cilia with lipid surfactant; administer oral steroids immediately afterward"],"a":1,"e":"N-acetylcysteine is a classic mucolytic whose free sulfhydryl group cleaves disulfide (S-S) bonds linking mucin polymers, reducing viscosity. Hypertonic saline (3-7%) acts as a mucokinetic/hydrating agent via osmotic water influx into periciliary fluid. Because both can trigger irritant bronchospasm, pretreating with an inhaled SABA (salbutamol/albuterol) is standard respiratory therapy protocol.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01337","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Medical Gas \u0026 Oxygen Therapy","topic":"Venturi (Air-Entrainment) Mask","q":"A patient with severe COPD and chronic CO2 retention has an erratic, rapid inspiratory flow pattern (peak inspiratory flow ~50 L/min) and requires a tightly controlled FiO2 of 0.28 (28%). Why does a Venturi (air-entrainment) mask achieve a stable, precise FiO2 regardless of the patient\u0027s breathing pattern compared with a simple nasal cannula?","o":["A Venturi mask stores 100% O2 in a 1-liter non-rebreathing reservoir bag","A Venturi mask absorbs exhaled nitrogen via soda lime granules","A Venturi mask delivers only 2 L/min total gas flow so room air cannot enter","A high-velocity oxygen jet accelerates through a narrow nozzle (Bernoulli principle), entraining a fixed ratio of room air to generate total gas flow exceeding the patient\u0027s peak inspiratory demand"],"a":3,"e":"A Venturi (air-entrainment) mask is a high-flow, fixed-performance device. Oxygen forced through a restricted orifice creates a subatmospheric lateral pressure (Bernoulli effect) that entrains room air in a fixed air-to-O2 ratio (e.g., 10:1 for 28% FiO2). Total output flow (\u003e 40-60 L/min) meets or exceeds peak inspiratory flow, preventing variable ambient dilution seen with low-flow nasal cannulae.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01338","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Artificial Airway Management","topic":"Cuff Pressure Management","q":"During routine ICU respiratory therapy rounds, a cuff manometer connected to an intubated patient\u0027s high-volume, low-pressure endotracheal tube pilot balloon reads 46 cmH2O. Why must the cuff pressure be adjusted strictly to 20-30 cmH2O rather than leaving it at 46 cmH2O or deflating it below 15 cmH2O?","o":["Pressures \u003e 30 cmH2O impair tracheal mucosal capillary blood flow (risking ischemia, ulceration, and tracheal stenosis), while pressures \u003c 20 cmH2O allow microaspiration of subglottic secretions and VAP","Cuff pressure has no impact on tracheal mucosa if the tube is PVC","High-volume low-pressure cuffs require 60 cmH2O to seal during PEEP 5 cmH2O","Pressures \u003e 30 cmH2O improve oxygenation; \u003c 15 cmH2O causes ventilator barotrauma"],"a":0,"e":"Tracheal mucosal capillary perfusion pressure averages ~25-35 mmHg (~30-40 cmH2O). Sustaining cuff pressure \u003e 30 cmH2O compresses mucosal capillaries, causing ischemic necrosis, tracheomalacia, tracheoesophageal fistula, or post-intubation tracheal stenosis. Conversely, \u003c 20 cmH2O permits pooling and silent microaspiration of contaminated subglottic secretions, driving ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01339","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Ventilator Mechanics \u0026 Hemodynamics","topic":"Static Compliance (Cstat) \u0026 Airway Resistance (Raw)","q":"An adult patient on volume-controlled ventilation with constant square-wave inspiratory flow of 60 L/min (1.0 L/s) has Vt = 500 mL (0.50 L), PIP = 35 cmH2O, Plateau Pressure (Pplat) = 25 cmH2O, and total PEEP = 5 cmH2O. What are the Static Compliance (Cstat) and Airway Resistance (Raw)?","o":["Cstat = 50 mL/cmH2O; Raw = 5 cmH2O/L/s","Cstat = 25 mL/cmH2O; Raw = 10 cmH2O/L/s","Cstat = 16.7 mL/cmH2O; Raw = 30 cmH2O/L/s","Cstat = 20 mL/cmH2O; Raw = 20 cmH2O/L/s"],"a":1,"e":"Static Compliance Cstat = Vt / (Pplat - total PEEP) = 500 mL / (25 - 5) cmH2O = 500 / 20 = 25 mL/cmH2O (markedly reduced from normal 50-100 mL/cmH2O). Airway Resistance Raw = (PIP - Pplat) / Flow (L/s) = (35 - 25) / 1.0 = 10 cmH2O/L/s (elevated above normal intubated Raw ~3-7 cmH2O/L/s).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01340","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Neonatal \u0026 Pediatric Respiratory Care","topic":"Surfactant Deficiency \u0026 Bubble CPAP / INSURE","q":"A 29-week-gestation preterm neonate (1,150 g) develops expiratory grunting, nasal flaring, subcostal retractions, and cyanosis within 30 minutes of birth. Chest radiograph reveals low lung volumes with diffuse bilateral reticulogranular (\u0027ground-glass\u0027) opacities and air bronchograms. What is the primary pathophysiology, and what respiratory therapy strategy prevents both alveolar collapse and Retinopathy of Prematurity (ROP)?","o":["Transient tachypnea of the newborn; routine prophylactic nitric oxide at 80 ppm","Meconium aspiration chemical pneumonitis; target SpO2 100% with hyperventilation","Pulmonary surfactant deficiency (Neonatal RDS) causing high alveolar surface tension and atelectasis; initiate early nasal CPAP (5-7 cmH2O) with selective exogenous surfactant (INSURE/LISA) while titrating FiO2 to target SpO2 90-95%","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia; initiate aggressive bag-mask ventilation at 40 cmH2O"],"a":2,"e":"Preterm infants \u003c 32-34 weeks lack adequate dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (surfactant) from immature Type II pneumocytes, raising alveolar surface tension (LaPlace\u0027s law) and causing diffuse end-expiratory microatelectasis (ground-glass CXR with air bronchograms). Early nasal CPAP maintains Functional Residual Capacity, paired with rescue exogenous surfactant (INSURE or LISA) and strict SpO2 targeting (90-95%) to prevent hyperoxic Retinopathy of Prematurity (ROP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01341","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) Analysis \u0026 Quality Control","topic":"Air Bubbles \u0026 Delayed Room-Temperature Storage","q":"Compare the pre-analytical ABG error produced by leaving a large UNEXPELLED AIR BUBBLE in a heparinized arterial blood syringe versus leaving a bubble-free plastic syringe sitting un-iced at room temperature for 45 minutes:","o":["Air bubble lowers PaO2 and raises PaCO2; delayed room-temp storage raises PaO2 and lowers PaCO2","Air bubble (PO2 ~150, PCO2 ~0) falsely LOWERS PaCO2 (raising pH) and shifts PaO2 toward 150 mmHg; delayed room-temp leukocyte metabolism consumes O2 (falsely LOW PaO2) and generates CO2/lactate (falsely HIGH PaCO2, LOW pH)","Both errors produce identical false metabolic alkalosis","Plastic syringes require liquid nitrogen freezing before blood gas insertion"],"a":1,"e":"Ambient air has PO2 ~150 mmHg and PCO2 ~0.3 mmHg. An unexpelled air bubble equilibrates gases: PaCO2 drops (falsely raising pH) and PaO2 rises toward 150 mmHg (or drops if patient PaO2 was \u003e 150 on high FiO2!). Conversely, in a sealed un-cooled syringe left \u003e 30 min, live leukocytes/reticulocytes metabolize O2 and glucose—dropping PaO2 (especially severe in hyperleukocytosis \u0027leukocyte larceny\u0027), raising PaCO2, and lowering pH.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01342","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Humidity \u0026 Bland Aerosol Therapy","topic":"Isothermic Saturation Boundary \u0026 HME Contraindications","q":"In an intubated ICU patient whose upper airway heating/humidifying mucosa is bypassed, what is the target absolute humidity delivered at the carina (Isothermic Saturation Boundary, 37 C, 100% RH), and in which scenario is a passive Heat and Moisture Exchanger (HME) CONTRAINDICATED in favor of an active heated humidifier?","o":["Target 0 mg/L dry medical air prevents ventilator-associated pneumonia","Target 10 mg/L; HME is ideal for thick bloody secretions","Target 100 mg/L; HME adds zero mechanical dead space or airway resistance","Target 44 mg/L (minimum \u003e= 33 mg/L per ISO standards for artificial airways); HME is CONTRAINDICATED in patients with thick/copious/bloody secretions (occlusion risk), core hypothermia (\u003c 32 C), large cuff leaks, or very small tidal volumes (high dead space)"],"a":3,"e":"Normally, inspired gas reaches BTPS (37 C, 100% RH, 44 mg/L water vapor) at the Isothermic Saturation Boundary (~5 cm below the carina). Intubation bypasses the nasopharynx, shifting the ISB distally and causing ciliary paralysis/inspissated mucus plugs unless active heated humidifiers deliver 33-44 mg/L (37 C). Passive HMEs trap exhaled heat/moisture but clog rapidly with thick/bloody secretions, add dead space (worsening hypercapnia in low-Vt ARDS), and fail in hypothermia or bronchopleural leaks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01343","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Acute Hypoxemic Respiratory Failure","topic":"ROX Index Prediction","q":"A 58-year-old man with severe bilateral viral pneumonia is receiving High-Flow Nasal Cannula (HFNC) at 60 L/min and FiO2 0.70. After 6 hours, his SpO2 is 91% and respiratory rate is 34 breaths/min with persistent accessory muscle use. Calculate his ROX index [(SpO2 / FiO2) / RR], and determine what this score predicts:","o":["ROX = 3.82 (\u003c 3.85 threshold); high risk of HFNC failure—escalate promptly toward endotracheal intubation rather than delaying","ROX = 5.40; continue current settings unchanged for 48 hours","ROX = 1.30; switch immediately to low-flow nasal cannula at 2 L/min","ROX = 8.42; high probability of HFNC success, reduce flow to 20 L/min"],"a":0,"e":"ROX Index = (SpO2 / FiO2) / Respiratory Rate. Here: (91 / 0.70) / 34 = 130 / 34 = 3.82. Per Roca et al. validation thresholds, ROX \u003e= 4.88 predicts HFNC success, whereas ROX \u003c 3.85 at 6 hours (or \u003c 2.85 at 2 hours / \u003c 3.85 at 12 hours) strongly predicts HFNC failure; failing to intubate promptly increases mortality from patient self-inflicted lung injury (P-SILI) and emergent arrest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01344","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Respiratory Monitoring \u0026 Capnography","topic":"Shark-Fin Plateau vs Sudden Flatline Zero","q":"On continuous ICU capnography of an intubated asthmatic patient, the waveform initially exhibits a slanting upward Phase III plateau (\u0027shark-fin\u0027 pattern), and then suddenly drops abruptly to 0 mmHg (flatline) during mechanical ventilation. What do the \u0027shark-fin\u0027 waveform and the subsequent abrupt drop to 0 mmHg signify respectively?","o":["Shark-fin: soda lime exhaustion; abrupt zero drop: mild hypothermia","Shark-fin: uneven alveolar emptying due to bronchospasm/airway obstruction; abrupt drop to 0 mmHg: complete ETT dislodgement (extubation/esophageal), total circuit disconnection, complete mucus-plug occlusion, or sudden cardiac arrest","Shark-fin: normal alveolar emptying; abrupt zero drop: mild sedation awakening","Shark-fin: cardiogenic oscillations; abrupt zero drop: successful weaning"],"a":1,"e":"A steep sloping Phase II-III \u0027shark-fin\u0027 capnogram reflects asynchronous emptying of lung units with prolonged time constants caused by bronchospasm (asthma/COPD) or partial tube kinking. An abrupt drop of EtCO2 to zero is a critical red-flag emergency indicating zero exhaled alveolar CO2 reaching the sensor—immediately check ETT position/patency, ventilator tubing connection, and patient pulse!","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01345","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Specialty Medical Gases","topic":"Thorpe-Tube Flowmeter Correction Factor","q":"A patient with severe post-extubation upper-airway stridor is started on 70% Helium / 30% Oxygen (70:30 Heliox) via a non-rebreathing mask connected to a standard OXYGEN-calibrated Thorpe-tube flowmeter. When the flowmeter ball reads 10 L/min, applying the standard 70:30 Heliox correction factor (1.6x; vs 1.8x for 80:20 Heliox), what is the ACTUAL total Heliox flow rate delivered to the patient?","o":["6.25 L/min","18.0 L/min","16.0 L/min","10.0 L/min"],"a":2,"e":"Because Helium has a density roughly one-seventh that of Nitrogen/Oxygen, low-density Heliox passes faster past the float of a standard O2-calibrated flowmeter. Multiplying the indicated flowmeter reading by the density correction factor (1.6 for 70:30 Heliox; 1.8 for 80:20 Heliox) gives Actual Flow = 10 x 1.6 = 16.0 L/min. Lower gas density reduces the Reynolds number, converting turbulent upper-airway flow into low-resistance laminar flow.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01346","course":"B.Sc Respiratory Therapy","subject":"Advanced Mechanical Ventilation","topic":"Driving Pressure (Delta P)","q":"A patient with severe ARDS on volume-controlled ventilation (Vt 380 mL) has Plateau Pressure (Pplat) = 29 cmH2O and PEEP = 10 cmH2O (Driving Pressure Delta P = 19 cmH2O). After increasing PEEP from 10 to 14 cmH2O recruits collapsed dorsal alveoli and improves respiratory compliance, Pplat decreases to 27 cmH2O (reducing Driving Pressure from 19 to 13 cmH2O). Why is this reduction in Driving Pressure associated with lower mortality?","o":["Increasing PEEP always increases Driving Pressure linearly in recruitable lungs","Driving Pressure equals PIP minus PEEP regardless of airway resistance","Driving Pressure \u003e 20 cmH2O maximizes surfactant production","Driving Pressure (Delta P = Pplat - PEEP = Vt / Crs) reflects cyclic parenchymal strain applied to the functional \u0027baby lung\u0027; reducing Delta P below 15 cmH2O indicates improved respiratory compliance via alveolar recruitment and predicts lower ARDS mortality"],"a":3,"e":"Amato et al. demonstrated that Driving Pressure (Delta P = Pplat - PEEP = Vt / static compliance) indexes tidal volume directly to the functional aerated lung volume (\u0027baby lung\u0027), correlating more strongly with ARDS survival than Vt or PEEP alone. Keeping Delta P \u003c 15 cmH2O (preferably \u003c= 13-14 cmH2O) minimizes cyclic barotrauma/volutrauma; when raising PEEP recruits lung units and drops Delta P, recruitment is beneficial (whereas if PEEP raises Delta P, overdistension is occurring!).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01347","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Machine and Medical Gases","topic":"Cylinder Identification and PISS","q":"When mounting an E-type medical gas cylinder onto the yoke of an anaesthesia workstation, what are the standard Pin Index Safety System (PISS) pin numbers and Indian/ISO cylinder body-shoulder color coding for Oxygen and Nitrous Oxide?","o":["Oxygen: pins 2-5, black body with white shoulder; Nitrous oxide: pins 3-5, blue body and shoulder","Oxygen: pins 3-5, blue body; Nitrous oxide: pins 2-5, black body with white shoulder","Oxygen: pins 1-5, grey body; Nitrous oxide: pins 2-6, yellow body","Oxygen: pins 4-6, brown body; Nitrous oxide: pins 1-4, green body"],"a":0,"e":"Under ISO/Indian standard color coding and the Pin Index Safety System (PISS), medical oxygen E-cylinders have a black body with a white shoulder and PISS pin configuration 2-5 (full pressure ~137 bar / 1900-2000 psi). Nitrous oxide cylinders are French blue throughout with PISS pin configuration 3-5 (liquefied gas at ~50 bar / 745 psi).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01348","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Workstation Check","topic":"Vaporizer and Leak Testing","q":"During the pre-operative check of a circle breathing system, the anaesthesia technician observes that the soda lime granules in the absorber canister have turned deep violet/purple throughout the upper and lower chambers. What does this indicate and what action is required?","o":["Oxygen flow is set too high, which will normalize after induction","The CO2 absorbent is exhausted and must be replaced before starting anaesthesia to prevent hypercapnia","The absorber is newly activated and ready for a 12-hour case","Water condensation has occurred and the canister should be shaken only"],"a":1,"e":"Soda lime contains ethyl violet pH indicator dye that changes from white to violet/purple as calcium hydroxide reacts with carbonic acid and becomes exhausted. Using exhausted absorbent causes rebreathing of CO2 and severe intraoperative hypercapnia, so fresh absorbent must be refilled.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01349","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Monitoring in Anaesthesia","topic":"Capnogram Waveform Interpretation","q":"Immediately after endotracheal intubation in an asthmatic patient, the capnograph displays a normal-height ETCO2 trace that exhibits a markedly prolonged, upsloping Phase II-III (\u0027shark-fin\u0027 appearance). What does this waveform signify?","o":["Venous air embolism","Accidental esophageal intubation","Severe bronchospasm or partial airway obstruction causing delayed alveolar emptying","Complete disconnection of the breathing circuit"],"a":2,"e":"A \u0027shark-fin\u0027 capnogram (slanted, upsloping Phase III plateau without a flat alveolar plateau) reflects uneven ventilation-perfusion emptying due to obstructive airway resistance such as acute bronchospasm, COPD, or a kinked/partially obstructed endotracheal tube.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01350","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Breathing Circuits","topic":"Bain Circuit (Mapleson D) Check","q":"Before using a coaxial Bain (Mapleson D) circuit, the anaesthesia technician performs the Pethick test by occluding the patient end, filling the reservoir bag with oxygen, releasing the occlusion, and pressing the oxygen flush valve. If the reservoir bag collapses rapidly instead of staying full, what has been confirmed?","o":["Disconnection or crack in the inner fresh-gas tube","Obstruction of the outer corrugated expiratory limb","Competent APL (pop-off) valve at the patient end","Intact inner fresh-gas delivery tube (Venturi effect draws gas from the bag)"],"a":3,"e":"In the Pethick test, activating the O2 flush creates high-velocity flow out of the narrow inner tube at the patient end, generating a Venturi (entrainment) sub-atmospheric pressure that sucks gas out of the reservoir bag in the outer limb, causing the bag to collapse. If the inner tube is disconnected or broken at the machine end, the bag fails to collapse (or inflates further).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01351","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Regional and General Anaesthesia Emergencies","topic":"Malignant Hyperthermia and Local Anesthetic Toxicity","q":"Five minutes after a brachial plexus block with bupivacaine, a patient develops circumoral numbness, tinnitus, generalized tonic-clonic seizures, and wide-complex bradycardia. Beyond airway management and benzodiazepines for seizures, which specific antidote must the anaesthesia technician immediately bring and prepare?","o":["20% intravenous lipid emulsion (Intralipid)","Intravenous dantrolene sodium","Intravenous sugammadex","Intravenous protamine sulfate"],"a":0,"e":"Local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST), especially from cardiotoxic bupivacaine, presents with CNS excitation (tinnitus, metallic taste, seizures) followed by cardiovascular collapse. Immediate 20% lipid emulsion therapy (1.5 mL/kg IV bolus followed by 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion) acts as a \u0027lipid sink\u0027 and restores myocardial energetics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01352","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Airway Management Equipment","topic":"Macintosh vs Miller Laryngoscope Blades","q":"When preparing airway equipment for a 6-year-old child undergoing elective tonsillectomy, what is the estimated uncuffed endotracheal tube (ETT) internal diameter using the standard age-based formula, and where is the tip of a curved Macintosh blade placed during laryngoscopy?","o":["5.5 mm uncuffed ETT; Macintosh blade tip placed beneath the vocal cords","5.5 mm uncuffed ETT [(age/4) + 4]; Macintosh blade tip placed in the vallecula","7.0 mm uncuffed ETT; Macintosh blade tip placed into the cricoid ring","4.0 mm uncuffed ETT; Macintosh blade tip placed posterior to the epiglottis to lift it directly"],"a":1,"e":"Using Cole\u0027s/standard pediatric formula, uncuffed ETT internal diameter (mm) = (Age in years / 4) + 4 = (6 / 4) + 4 = 5.5 mm (cuffed ETT would be ~0.5 mm smaller, i.e., 5.0 mm). The curved Macintosh blade tip rests in the vallecula (base of tongue anterior to epiglottis), indirectly lifting the epiglottis via tension on the hyoepiglottic ligament.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01353","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Machine and Vaporizers","topic":"Desflurane Vaporizer (Tec 6)","q":"Why does desflurane require an electrically heated, pressurized vaporizer (such as the Tec 6) rather than a conventional unheated variable-bypass vaporizer used for sevoflurane or isoflurane?","o":["Desflurane reacts explosively with stainless steel unless cooled below 0 degrees C","Desflurane is a non-volatile solid at room temperature requiring 100 degrees C melting","Desflurane has a very low boiling point (22.8-23.5 degrees C) near operating room temperature and high saturated vapor pressure (~669 mmHg at 20 degrees C), which would cause uncontrolled boiling and cooling-induced output drops in a standard vaporizer","Desflurane has a boiling point of 98 degrees C and cannot vaporize without boiling water"],"a":2,"e":"Desflurane boils at ~23 degrees C (close to ambient OT temperature) with a very high vapor pressure (~669 mmHg at 20 degrees C). In a variable-bypass vaporizer, minor ambient temperature swings would cause erratic boiling and massive lethal over-delivery. The Tec 6 heats desflurane to 39 degrees C under ~2 atmospheres (~1500 mmHg) and electronically meters pure vapor into the fresh gas stream.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01354","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Pharmacology for Anaesthesia Technicians","topic":"Succinylcholine Contraindications","q":"A 30-year-old paraplegic patient (T6 spinal cord injury sustained 3 weeks ago) is scheduled for emergency debridement. The anaesthesia technician checks the induction tray containing succinylcholine. Why must succinylcholine be avoided in this patient, and what alternative rapid-onset relaxant should be prepared?","o":["Succinylcholine is inactivated by paraplegic plasma cholinesterase; prepare atracurium","Succinylcholine causes irreversible malignant hypothermia exclusively in spinal injury; prepare pancuronium","Succinylcholine triggers severe bronchospasm only via histamine release; prepare vecuronium 0.01 mg/kg","Upregulation of extrajunctional nicotinic acetylcholine receptors causes massive potassium efflux and lethal hyperkalemic cardiac arrest; prepare high-dose rocuronium (1.0-1.2 mg/kg) with sugammadex available"],"a":3,"e":"Between ~48 hours and several months after major thermal burns, denervation/spinal cord injury, crush trauma, or prolonged ICU immobility, immature extrajunctional ACh receptors proliferate across skeletal muscle membranes. Depolarization by succinylcholine opens all these channels simultaneously, causing massive K+ efflux and cardiac arrest. High-dose rocuronium (1.2 mg/kg) provides comparable rapid-sequence intubation onset safely.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01355","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Monitoring in Anaesthesia","topic":"Beer-Lambert Law and Dyshemoglobinemias","q":"A patient receiving topical benzocaine spray before awake bronchoscopy develops central cyanosis unresponsive to 100% oxygen, chocolate-brown arterial blood, and a pulse oximeter reading stuck around 85% despite a PaO2 of 380 mmHg on ABG. What physical principle explains the fixed 85% SpO2 reading?","o":["Methemoglobin absorbs both 660 nm (red) and 940 nm (infrared) wavelengths nearly equally (absorption ratio ~1.0), forcing standard two-wavelength oximeters toward ~85%","Carboxyhemoglobin shifts the 660 nm absorption identical to oxyhemoglobin, producing a falsely high reading of 100%","Fetal hemoglobin completely blocks infrared 940 nm transmission, yielding 0% reading","Severe hypothermia causes vasoconstriction that locks pulse oximetry at 85%"],"a":0,"e":"Standard pulse oximeters use Beer-Lambert spectrophotometry at 660 nm (red) and 940 nm (infrared). Methemoglobin (oxidized Fe3+ heme induced by benzocaine, prilocaine, or dapsone) has nearly equal extinction coefficients at both wavelengths (R = 1.0), which corresponds on calibration curves to an SpO2 of ~85% regardless of actual arterial oxygenation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01356","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Monitoring in Anaesthesia","topic":"Peripheral Nerve Stimulator and Train-of-Four","q":"When monitoring recovery from non-depolarizing neuromuscular blockade (e.g., rocuronium) at the ulnar nerve / adductor pollicis using a quantitative accelerometer, what minimum Train-of-Four (TOF) ratio (T4/T1) confirms adequate recovery to prevent postoperative residual curarization before extubation?","o":["TOF ratio \u003e= 0.90 (90%)","TOF count of 1 twitch with absent T2-T4","Zero twitches (post-tetanic count = 0)","TOF ratio \u003e= 0.40 (40%)"],"a":0,"e":"Current ASA/ESAIC neuromuscular monitoring guidelines mandate a quantitative Train-of-Four ratio (T4/T1) \u003e= 0.90 at the adductor pollicis before tracheal extubation. Lower ratios (such as the older 0.70 threshold) leave patients vulnerable to pharyngeal muscle weakness, upper airway obstruction, impaired hypoxic ventilatory drive, and pulmonary aspiration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01357","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Machine Safety Features","topic":"Fail-Safe Valve and Proportioning System","q":"What are the distinct protective functions of the Oxygen Failure Protection Device (fail-safe valve) and the mechanical/pneumatic Proportioning System (e.g., Link-25) on a modern anaesthesia workstation?","o":["The fail-safe valve cuts off or reduces nitrous oxide flow if oxygen supply pressure drops, while the proportioning system prevents setting an N2O:O2 flow ratio that delivers \u003c25% oxygen","Both devices turn off the volatile vaporizer whenever blood pressure drops below 80 mmHg","The fail-safe valve boosts nitrous oxide pressure to 100% if oxygen runs out","The proportioning system allows 100% pure nitrous oxide delivery for rapid induction"],"a":0,"e":"The oxygen supply failure protection device (fail-safe valve) senses upstream O2 pressure (~45-55 psi) and shuts off N2O (and other hypoxic gases) if O2 pressure falls below ~20-25 psi. Downstream at the flowmeters, the proportioning system (such as Ohmeda Link-25 chain or Drager pneumatic ratio controller) mechanically couples the O2 and N2O control knobs to enforce at least 25% O2 concentration (max 3:1 N2O:O2).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01358","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Airway Management Equipment","topic":"Laryngeal Mask Airway (LMA) Safety","q":"After insertion of a classic cuffed Laryngeal Mask Airway (LMA) for an elective hernia repair, what maximum intracuff pressure should be maintained using a cuff manometer, and which patient group has a key contraindication to elective LMA use?","o":["Inflate cuff to 150 cm H2O for a tight seal; ideal for acute intestinal obstruction with active vomiting","Keep cuff pressure \u003c= 60 cm H2O; contraindicated in non-fasted patients or those with severe GERD/full stomach at high risk of pulmonary aspiration","Keep cuff pressure at 0 cm H2O with cuff completely deflated; contraindicated in all fasting adults","Inflate with 100 mL of air regardless of LMA size"],"a":1,"e":"Overinflating an LMA cuff above 60 cm H2O compresses pharyngeal mucosa against cervical structures, causing postoperative sore throat and lingual, hypoglossal, or recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy. Because LMAs do not seal the trachea against gastric regurgitation, full-stomach/high aspiration risk patients require cuffed endotracheal intubation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01359","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Regional Anaesthesia Setup","topic":"Spinal Needle Design and PDPH","q":"When setting up a spinal (subarachnoid) anaesthesia tray for a young obstetric patient undergoing cesarean delivery, why is a 25G or 27G pencil-point needle (Whitacre or Sprotte) preferred over a larger cutting-bevel Quincke needle?","o":["Pencil-point needles have a sharper cutting edge that enlarges the dural hole for faster block onset","Cutting Quincke needles cannot penetrate the ligamentum flavum","Pencil-point needles separate rather than slice dural collagen fibers, markedly reducing CSF leakage and post-dural puncture headache (PDPH)","Whitacre needles prevent high spinal spread by neutralizing hyperbaric bupivacaine"],"a":2,"e":"Non-cutting, atraumatic pencil-point needles (Whitacre, Sprotte) part longitudinal dural fibers transiently, allowing elastic recoil and rapid closure once withdrawn, which drastically lowers PDPH incidence compared with bevel-cutting Quincke needles of the same gauge.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01360","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Invasive Hemodynamic Monitoring","topic":"Phlebostatic Axis Zeroing","q":"A supine patient\u0027s invasive arterial blood pressure transducer is accidentally mounted 10 cm BELOW the phlebostatic axis (4th intercostal space, mid-anterior-posterior chest level). Given the hydrostatic equivalence of mercury and water/blood (1.36 cm H2O = 1 mmHg), how will this leveling error distort the displayed arterial BP?","o":["It will falsely UNDERESTIMATE arterial BP by approximately -7.4 mmHg","It has zero effect on invasive BP as long as the monitor was zeroed to air","It will falsely OVERESTIMATE arterial BP by +13.6 mmHg","It will falsely OVERESTIMATE arterial BP by approximately +7.4 mmHg"],"a":3,"e":"Every 10 cm vertical error adds or subtracts 10 / 1.36 = 7.35 (~7.4) mmHg (or ~2 mmHg per inch) due to hydrostatic fluid column pressure. When the transducer is positioned 10 cm BELOW the heart (phlebostatic axis), the extra fluid column presses down onto the diaphragm, producing a falsely elevated reading by ~7.4 mmHg.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01361","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Crisis Management","topic":"Recognition and Emergency Cart Protocol","q":"Twenty minutes after induction with sevoflurane and succinylcholine, a young muscular patient develops abrupt masseter spasm, unexplained tachycardia, a steep rise in ETCO2 from 36 to 74 mmHg despite increased minute ventilation, and rapidly rising core temperature (39.4 degrees C). While the anaesthesiologist calls for help, what immediate machine and medication actions must the anaesthesia technician execute?","o":["Shut off the sevoflurane vaporizer, hyperventilate with 100% O2 at \u003e=10 L/min fresh gas flow (inserting activated charcoal filters or changing circuit/absorber), and immediately reconstitute IV dantrolene sodium (2.5 mg/kg initial bolus) from the MH cart","Administer IV succinylcholine repeat dose to relax the masseter spasm","Increase sevoflurane to 4% to deepen anaesthesia and warm the patient further","Switch off oxygen and give IV atropine to treat tachycardia"],"a":0,"e":"Sudden unexplained hypercapnia (steep ETCO2 rise), masseter rigidity, tachycardia, and hyperthermia after volatile agents or succinylcholine herald fulminant Malignant Hyperthermia. Immediate survival depends on discontinuing all triggering agents, flushing/hyperventilating with 100% O2 at high fresh gas flow (\u003e=10 L/min, using charcoal filters or clean circuit), and rapidly reconstituting IV dantrolene (2.5 mg/kg bolus, repeated until controlled) alongside active cooling and hyperkalemia/acidosis treatment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01362","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Inhalational Anaesthesia Pharmacology","topic":"Factors Altering MAC","q":"Which combination of physiological and pharmacological factors DECREASES a patient\u0027s Minimum Alveolar Concentration (MAC) requirement for volatile inhalational anaesthetics?","o":["Hyperthyroidism and acute amphetamine administration","Advanced elderly age, hypothermia, acute alcohol intoxication, pregnancy, and concurrent opioid/dexmedetomidine administration","Male sex and elevated barometric pressure alone","Infancy (peak at ~6 months), hyperthermia (\u003e40 degrees C), chronic alcohol abuse, and hypernatremia"],"a":1,"e":"MAC decreases by ~6% per decade after age 40, drops ~5% per degree C of hypothermia, and is lowered by pregnancy, acute ethanol/opioid/benzodiazepine/alpha-2 agonist coadministration, hyponatremia, and severe hypoxia/hypotension. Failure to reduce vaporizer dial settings accordingly causes deep anaesthetic overdose and hypotension.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01363","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Machine and OT Safety","topic":"Active vs Passive Scavenging Systems","q":"During pre-operative inspection of an active Waste Anaesthetic Gas Scavenging System (AGSS) connected to central vacuum suction, why is an open-interface reservoir or negative-pressure relief valve essential between the APL valve and the hospital suction line?","o":["To pump nitrous oxide back into the oxygen flowmeter","To increase fresh gas flow automatically to 20 L/min","To prevent excessive vacuum suction from collapsing the breathing circuit reservoir bag or transmitting negative pressure directly to the patient\u0027s lungs","To heat desflurane inside the reservoir bag"],"a":2,"e":"Active AGSS systems connect to high-flow hospital vacuum lines. Without an open scavenging interface or negative-pressure relief valve (and positive-pressure relief valve in closed interfaces in case the disposal line kinks/occludes), vacuum suction can suck gas out of the patient\u0027s lungs/circuit (causing negative-pressure pulmonary edema/atelectasis), while an occluded line causes severe barotrauma.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01364","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Airway Management and Ventilation","topic":"Nitrous Oxide Diffusion into ETT Cuffs","q":"During a 4-hour abdominal surgery maintained with 66% Nitrous Oxide (N2O) and oxygen, an air-filled endotracheal tube (ETT) cuff pressure rises steadily from 22 cm H2O to 48 cm H2O. Why does this occur and why is \u003e30 cm H2O dangerous?","o":["Carbon dioxide freezes inside the pilot balloon","Airway secretions leak upward into the cuff inflating it; \u003e30 cm H2O cools the trachea","Oxygen evaporates out of the cuff causing collapse; \u003e30 cm H2O softens tracheal cartilage","N2O diffuses rapidly across the PVC cuff wall down its concentration gradient faster than nitrogen leaves; pressures \u003e30 cm H2O exceed tracheal mucosal capillary perfusion pressure, causing ischemia, sore throat, or stenosis"],"a":3,"e":"Nitrous oxide is ~34x more blood/membrane-soluble than nitrogen and diffuses rapidly into any air-filled compliant cavity (ETT cuff, middle ear, pneumothorax, bowel). Because N2O enters the cuff far faster than N2 exits, cuff pressure escalates above tracheal mucosal capillary perfusion pressure (~25-30 cm H2O), necessitating periodic cuff-manometer checks (maintaining 20-30 cm H2O) or inflating with N2O/O2 mixture or saline per institutional protocol.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01365","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Difficult Airway Management","topic":"Gum-Elastic Bougie (Eschmann Introducer)","q":"During direct laryngoscopy, only the tip of the epiglottis is visible (Cormack-Lehane Grade III view). When the operator advances a coude-tip gum-elastic bougie beneath the epiglottis, which two tactile signs confirm correct endotracheal placement over accidental esophageal placement?","o":["Palpable \u0027tracheal clicks\u0027 as the angled tip bumps across anterior tracheal cartilaginous rings, followed by \u0027distal hold-up\u0027 when reaching smaller bronchi at ~25-35 cm","Loud gurgling belch from the stomach","Smooth effortless passage all the way to 50 cm with zero resistance","Immediate drop of ETCO2 to zero after tube railroading"],"a":0,"e":"The angled coude tip of an Eschmann gum-elastic bougie produces distinct palpable vibrations (\u0027tracheal clicks\u0027) as it slides over the C-shaped anterior tracheal cartilage rings, and encounters gentle mechanical resistance (\u0027hold-up\u0027) at ~24-40 cm upon reaching the carina/mainstem bronchus—neither of which occurs in the smooth, floppy esophagus (where it advances \u003e40-50 cm unimpeded). Caution: gentle advancement prevents bronchial perforation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01366","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Post-Anaesthesia Care Unit (PACU)","topic":"Modified Aldrete Scoring System","q":"Which five physiological parameters (each scored 0, 1, or 2 for a maximum of 10 points) are evaluated in the Modified Aldrete Scoring System to determine safe discharge from the Post-Anaesthesia Care Unit (PACU) to a general surgical ward?","o":["Heart rate only, ECG rhythm, Core temperature, Weight, and Ambulation distance","Activity (limb movement), Respiration, Circulation (BP relative to pre-op baseline), Consciousness, and Oxygen Saturation (SpO2)","Pupil size, Capillary refill, Blood glucose, Bowel sounds, and Drain color","Pain score, Urine output, Serum hemoglobin, Temperature, and Appetite"],"a":1,"e":"The Modified Aldrete Score replaces skin color from the original 1970 Aldrete score with pulse oximetry SpO2, assessing: (1) Activity, (2) Respiration, (3) Circulation (BP within +/-20% of baseline = 2), (4) Consciousness, and (5) O2 Saturation (SpO2 \u003e92% on room air = 2). A total score \u003e=9 (with no 0s in critical domains and stable pain/PONV/bleeding) generally clears ward transfer.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01367","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Monitoring in Anaesthesia","topic":"Infrared Anaesthetic Gas Analyzer \u0026 Additive MAC","q":"On the anaesthesia monitor\u0027s infrared respiratory gas analyzer, a middle-aged adult\u0027s end-tidal sevoflurane reads 1.0% (sevoflurane 1 MAC = 2.0%) while concurrently receiving 52.5% end-tidal Nitrous Oxide (N2O 1 MAC = 105%). What is the combined additive total MAC fraction delivered to the patient?","o":["1.5 MAC","2.0 MAC","1.0 MAC (0.5 MAC sevoflurane + 0.5 MAC N2O)","0.5 MAC"],"a":2,"e":"Minimum Alveolar Concentration (MAC) values of inhaled anaesthetics are approximately additive: Total MAC = (ET Sevoflurane / MAC_sevo) + (ET N2O / MAC_N2O) = (1.0% / 2.0%) + (52.5% / 105%) = 0.5 + 0.5 = 1.0 MAC. Modern gas analyzers compute and display this age-adjusted total MAC automatically.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01368","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Airway Management Techniques","topic":"Sellick Maneuver (Cricoid Pressure)","q":"When assisting an anaesthesiologist with Sellick\u0027s maneuver (cricoid pressure) during Rapid Sequence Induction in a full-stomach emergency laparotomy patient, which anatomical structure is pressed posteriorly, and when is pressure released?","o":["The thyroid cartilage (Adam\u0027s apple) is pressed laterally to the left before induction","Release cricoid pressure immediately before inserting the laryngoscope blade","The hyoid bone is pulled anteriorly toward the chin during extubation","The cricoid cartilage (the only complete circumferential tracheal cartilage ring) is pressed posteriorly against the C6 vertebral body (~10 N awake, ~30 N after loss of consciousness) and held until cuffed ETT placement and ETCO2 confirmation"],"a":3,"e":"Unlike the C-shaped tracheal rings or open-posterior thyroid cartilage, the cricoid cartilage forms a complete signet-ring below the thyroid cartilage (around C6). Pressing it backward compresses the upper esophagus between the rigid cricoid ring and C6 vertebral body to limit passive gastric regurgitation, remaining applied until cuff inflation and capnographic confirmation (unless it impairs laryngoscopic view or active vomiting occurs, when it must be released immediately).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01369","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Thoracic Anaesthesia Equipment","topic":"Double-Lumen Endobronchial Tube (DLT)","q":"When preparing equipment for one-lung ventilation during either a left or right thoracotomy, why is a LEFT-sided Robertshaw Double-Lumen Tube (DLT) preferred over a right-sided DLT in most clinical situations?","o":["Because the right mainstem bronchus is very short with the right upper lobe (RUL) bronchus taking off early (~2-2.5 cm from the carina), so a right DLT cuff easily occludes RUL ventilation unless precisely aligned, whereas the left mainstem bronchus is longer (~4.5-5 cm) with a wider margin of safety","Because the left lung has no upper lobe bronchus","Because right-sided DLTs lack an endobronchial blue cuff","Because left-sided DLTs can only ventilate the right lung and never isolate the left lung"],"a":0,"e":"Anatomically, the left mainstem bronchus averages 4.5-5.0 cm before dividing into upper and lower lobes, giving a generous margin of safety for cuff positioning. By contrast, the right mainstem bronchus is short (~2.0-2.5 cm), and the right upper lobe bronchus originates immediately near the carina, risking RUL collapse/hypoxemia if a right-sided DLT shifts by even a few millimeters (so right DLTs are reserved primarily for left mainstem lesions/pneumonectomy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01370","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Pharmacology and Infection Control","topic":"Propofol Lipid Emulsion Asepsis","q":"Why do infection control and pharmacology guidelines mandate strict aseptic swabbing of propofol ampoule necks/vial stoppers and discarding opened propofol syringes or infusions within 6 hours (for opened vials/syringes drawn up for bolus) to 12 hours (for continuous dedicated spiked infusions)?","o":["Propofol turns into succinylcholine if kept at room temperature beyond 6 hours","Propofol is formulated in an aqueous soybean-oil/egg-phosphatide/glycerol lipid emulsion that supports rapid, exponential bacterial and fungal growth without antimicrobial preservatives in standard vials, risking lethal nosocomial sepsis","Propofol evaporates completely into nitrous oxide gas after 6 hours","Propofol crystallizes into solid kidney stones inside the syringe after 6 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Propofol (1% or 2%) is poorly water-soluble and dissolved in a nutrient-rich white lipid emulsion (10% soybean oil, egg lecithin, glycerol) which acts as an ideal culture medium for Staphylococcus aureus, Gram-negative bacilli, and Candida if contaminated during drawing up. Strict aseptic technique, single-patient syringe use, and discarding within 6 hrs (or max 12 hrs for dedicated spiked ICU infusions) prevent fatal bloodstream infections.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01371","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Medical Gas Supply Systems","topic":"Vacuum Insulated Evaporator (VIE) \u0026 Pipeline Pressure","q":"In a tertiary hospital\u0027s central medical gas plant, what is the standard nominal working pressure in the wall pipeline outlets for Oxygen, Nitrous Oxide, and Medical Air (4-bar), and approximately how many liters of gaseous oxygen at room temperature are produced by 1 liter of cryogenic liquid oxygen from a Vacuum Insulated Evaporator (VIE)?","o":["Pipeline pressure ~0.5 bar (7 psi); 1 liter of liquid O2 produces 1 liter of gas","Pipeline pressure ~20 bar; liquid O2 is stored at +50 degrees C","Pipeline pressure ~4 bar (~50-60 psi / 400 kPa); 1 liter of liquid O2 expands to ~840-860 liters of gaseous O2","Pipeline pressure ~137 bar (2,000 psi); 1 liter of liquid O2 produces 10 liters of gas"],"a":2,"e":"Liquid oxygen stored in a Vacuum Insulated Evaporator (VIE) at approximately -160 to -183 degrees C under ~5-10 bar vaporizes through superheater coils to yield ~842-860 times its liquid volume in gaseous O2 at ambient conditions, regulated down to ~4 bar (400-415 kPa, ~55-60 psi) throughout hospital wall Schrader/DISS/NIST outlets (whereas surgical pneumatic tools use ~7-8 bar air).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01372","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Post-Anaesthesia Care Unit (PACU)","topic":"Opioid-Induced Respiratory Depression vs Residual Paralysis","q":"Fifteen minutes after arrival in the PACU following abdominal surgery, a patient becomes deeply somnolent with pinpoint pupils, a slow respiratory rate of 6 breaths/min, deep tidal volumes, and SpO2 87% on room air despite an intact TOF ratio of 0.96. After airway jaw-thrust, supplemental O2, and titrated IV naloxone (40 mcg increments), the patient breathes 16/min and wakes up. Why must PACU monitoring continue closely for at least 1-2 hours after naloxone?","o":["Because pinpoint pupils indicate malignant hyperthermia rather than opioids","Because naloxone permanently paralyzes the diaphragm after 30 minutes","Because naloxone converts morphine into succinylcholine","Because naloxone has a shorter elimination half-life (~30-60 minutes) than morphine or fentanyl/hydromorphone, risking recurrent life-threatening hypoventilation (\u0027re-narcotization\u0027) once naloxone wears off"],"a":3,"e":"Slow respiratory rate with preserved/deep tidal volume, somnolence, and miosis alongside TOF \u003e=0.90 points to opioid-induced respiratory depression rather than residual neuromuscular blockade (which causes rapid shallow panting/inability to lift head). Small titrated IV naloxone boluses (20-40 mcg) restore respiratory drive without triggering acute pain/hypertensive crisis, but naloxone\u0027s brief 30-60 min duration requires vigilant PACU observation to catch re-narcotization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01373","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Workstation \u0026 Medical Gas Supply","topic":"ISO/India Cylinder Color Coding \u0026 Pin Index Safety System","q":"When mounting a reserve E-type Oxygen cylinder onto the yoke of an anaesthesia workstation in India, which ISO color code and Pin Index Safety System (PISS) pin positions confirm it is medical Oxygen?","o":["Black body with white shoulder; PISS pins 2 and 5","French blue body and shoulder; PISS pins 3 and 5","All-white body with green neck; PISS pins 2 and 6","Grey body with black-and-white checkerboard shoulder; PISS pins 1 and 5"],"a":0,"e":"Under ISO 32 / Indian Standards for medical gas cylinders, Oxygen cylinders have a black body with a white shoulder and use PISS pin positions 2 and 5 (full E-cylinder ~137 bar / 1,900–2,000 psi containing ~660 L). Nitrous oxide is blue (pins 3 and 5), and medical air has a grey/black body with black-and-white quartered shoulder (pins 1 and 5).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01374","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Workstation \u0026 Medical Gas Supply","topic":"Hypoxia Prevention \u0026 Low-Pressure Leak Check","q":"During the pre-use check of an anaesthesia machine with a mechanical Link-25 proportioning system, you turn the Nitrous Oxide (N2O) flow control knob up to 6 L/min while the Oxygen flow knob is at its minimum setting. What should the technician observe?","o":["The Oxygen flow automatically increases proportionally to maintain at least 25% FiO2 (e.g., \u003e= 2 L/min O2 with 6 L/min N2O), preventing delivery of a hypoxic gas mixture","The N2O flowmeter shuts off completely unless O2 flow exceeds 10 L/min","Both flowmeters drop to zero and the APL valve vents room air","The machine delivers 100% N2O until the oxygen flush valve is manually pressed"],"a":0,"e":"The Link-25 (hypoxic guard) mechanically/pneumatically links the N2O and O2 flow-control valves (3:1 max ratio) so that increasing N2O automatically drives up O2 flow—or decreasing O2 lowers N2O—guaranteeing a minimum 25% oxygen concentration (O2 \u003e= 2 L/min when N2O is 6 L/min).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01375","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Patient Monitoring \u0026 Perioperative Vigilance","topic":"Capnogram Waveform Interpretation","q":"Immediately after laryngoscopy and endotracheal intubation, the patient is connected to the anaesthesia circuit and manually ventilated. Over the first 5 manual breaths, the capnograph shows a flat zero line (EtCO2 = 0 mmHg) with no visible square-wave plateau, although SpO2 still reads 98%. What is the most urgent diagnosis to exclude?","o":["Normal hyperventilation immediately after preoxygenation","Unrecognized esophageal intubation (or complete circuit disconnection/tracheal tube obstruction)","Mild post-induction bronchospasm","Exhausted soda lime canister in the circle absorber"],"a":1,"e":"Continuous waveform capnography is the gold standard to confirm tracheal intubation. Complete absence of CO2 waveforms after 4–6 breaths (apart from transient 1–2 small decreasing waves if carbonated air was in the stomach) signifies esophageal intubation, complete breathing circuit disconnect, or total airway obstruction—and SpO2 can remain \u003e95% for minutes after preoxygenation before catastrophic desaturation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01376","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Workstation \u0026 Medical Gas Supply","topic":"Cylinder Gas Volume \u0026 Duration Calculation","q":"You are preparing to transport an intubated patient from the OT to the ICU using a standard E-type Oxygen cylinder (full capacity ~660 L at 2,000 psi; conversion factor ~0.33 L/psi). The pressure gauge currently reads 1,000 psi, and the transport ventilator/Bain circuit requires 6 L/min of O2. Approximately how many minutes of oxygen supply remain?","o":["110 minutes (approx. 660 L remaining / 6 L/min)","165 minutes (approx. 990 L remaining / 6 L/min)","55 minutes (approx. 330 L remaining / 6 L/min)","25 minutes (approx. 150 L remaining / 6 L/min)"],"a":2,"e":"Because Oxygen remains a compressed non-liquefied gas at room temperature, cylinder volume is directly proportional to gauge pressure (Boyle’s law). At 1,000 psi (half of 2,000 psi), remaining volume = 1,000 × 0.33 = 330 L. At 6 L/min flow, remaining duration = 330 / 6 = 55 minutes (in practice, swap cylinders before reaching ~200–500 psi safety reserve).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01377","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Breathing Circuits \u0026 Vaporizers","topic":"Mapleson D (Bain) \u0026 Soda Lime Exhaustion","q":"Which Mapleson breathing system is coaxial and most efficient for CONTROLLED mechanical ventilation, and what capnographic abnormality indicates exhausted soda lime in a Circle breathing system?","o":["Mapleson D (Bain circuit) for controlled ventilation; inspired CO2 baseline rises above 0 mmHg (with ethyl violet indicator turning purple)","Mapleson A (Magill circuit) for controlled ventilation; end-tidal CO2 drops to 0 mmHg","Mapleson F (Jackson-Rees) exclusively for obese adults \u003e 100 kg; peak airway pressure falls to zero","Mapleson B for spontaneous breathing only; inspired oxygen rises to 100%"],"a":0,"e":"The Mapleson D (and its coaxial Bain modification) is the most efficient Mapleson circuit for controlled ventilation (requiring fresh gas flow ~70–100 mL/kg/min to prevent rebreathing), whereas Mapleson A (Magill) is most efficient for spontaneous breathing. In a Circle system, soda lime exhaustion allows CO2 rebreathing, raising the inspired capnogram baseline above 0 mmHg while ethyl violet turns purple/violet.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01378","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Airway Management \u0026 Perioperative Emergencies","topic":"Acute Laryngospasm Response","q":"Thirty seconds after awake extubation following tonsillectomy, a child develops high-pitched inspiratory stridor progressing to silent chest with paradoxical \"see-saw\" thoracoabdominal movement and rapidly falling SpO2 (84%). What immediate assistance must the anaesthesia technician provide?","o":["Immediately remove the face mask and lay the child prone without airway support","Administer IV Neostigmine 2.5 mg to stimulate diaphragmatic contraction","Re-insert an oral Guedel airway forcefully through clenched teeth without oxygen","Ensure suctioning of oropharyngeal secretions, assist with 100% oxygen via tight face mask with gentle continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) and jaw thrust/Larson maneuver, and immediately draw up/keep Succinylcholine and Atropine ready"],"a":3,"e":"Complete laryngospasm causes silent airway obstruction with paradoxical chest-abdomen rocking. Management follows stepwise escalation: clear airway irritants (blood/secretions), apply jaw thrust + Larson’s maneuver (bilateral pressure at the laryngospasm notch behind earlobes) with 100% O2 and gentle CPAP, deepen anaesthesia (propofol) if needed, and prepare low-dose IV/IM succinylcholine (plus atropine for pediatric bradycardia) if refractory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01379","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Breathing Circuits \u0026 Vaporizers","topic":"Vaporizer Color Coding \u0026 Desflurane Physics","q":"Which volatile anaesthetic requires a specialized electrically heated, pressurized vaporizer (Tec 6 heated to ~39°C at 2 atm) because its boiling point is close to room temperature (22.8°C), and what is its standard agent-specific color code?","o":["Desflurane — Blue","Sevoflurane — Yellow","Halothane — Red","Isoflurane — Purple"],"a":0,"e":"Desflurane (color-coded blue) has a boiling point of 22.8°C and high saturated vapor pressure (~669 mmHg at 20°C), meaning it would boil unpredictably in a conventional variable-bypass vaporizer. Therefore, it uses a dual-circuit electrically heated (39°C), pressurized (~2 atm) vaporizer (Tec 6). Sevoflurane is yellow, Isoflurane is purple, and Halothane is red.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01380","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Regional Anaesthesia \u0026 Perioperative Support","topic":"Spinal Anaesthesia Preparation \u0026 Monitoring","q":"You are assisting with a subarachnoid (spinal) block for an elective lower-segment cesarean section. Which tray preparation and immediate post-block monitoring protocol prevents post-dural puncture headache (PDPH), chemical arachnoiditis, and unrecognized maternal hypotension?","o":["Check blood pressure once every 30 minutes after spinal injection while keeping the parturient completely flat supine","Provide a small-gauge pencil-point needle (e.g., 25G/27G Whitacre), allow chlorhexidine-alcohol skin prep to dry completely before needle insertion, apply left uterine tilt, and cycle NIBP every 1–2 minutes initially with ephedrine/phenylephrine ready","Provide an 18G Tuohy cutting needle for intrathecal injection and wipe excess povidone-iodine directly onto the needle tip","Draw up 5% hyperbaric bupivacaine diluted with non-sterile tap water"],"a":1,"e":"Small-gauge (25G–27G) non-cutting pencil-point needles (Whitacre/Sprotte) separate dural fibers rather than cutting them, markedly lowering PDPH incidence. Letting chlorhexidine-alcohol dry completely prevents neurotoxic chemical arachnoiditis. Left lateral tilt relieves aortocaval compression and 1–2 min BP cycling detects rapid sympathetic blockade hypotension early.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01381","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Airway Management \u0026 Perioperative Emergencies","topic":"Malignant Hyperthermia Crisis Protocol","q":"Ten minutes after induction with Sevoflurane and Succinylcholine for an elective surgery, the patient develops masseter muscle rigidity, unexplained tachycardia (HR 138/min), and a rapid exponential rise in EtCO2 from 35 to 78 mmHg despite increased minute ventilation. Which emergency protocol must be initiated immediately?","o":["Administer a repeat dose of Succinylcholine 100 mg IV to break jaw stiffness","Wrap the patient in warm forced-air blankets and infuse 2 L of heated Ringer’s lactate","Suspect Malignant Hyperthermia: turn off Sevoflurane immediately, hyperventilate with 100% O2 at \u003e= 10 L/min fresh gas flow (replace circuit/insert charcoal filters if available), prepare IV Dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg bolus, and initiate active cooling","Increase Sevoflurane concentration to 4% to relax the masseter spasm and decrease minute ventilation"],"a":2,"e":"Masseter spasm after succinylcholine plus unexplained rapid EtCO2 elevation and tachycardia are classic early hallmarks of Malignant Hyperthermia (hypermetabolic crisis before late fever). Immediately discontinue all volatile triggers, switch to non-triggering IV anaesthesia (TIVA), flush with 100% O2 \u003e=10 L/min, administer IV Dantrolene (2.5 mg/kg initial bolus, repeating until signs subside), and treat hyperkalemia/acidosis/hyperthermia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01382","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Regional Anaesthesia Equipment","topic":"Safe Motor-Response Current Thresholds","q":"When assisting with a peripheral nerve block using a nerve stimulator (1-2 Hz, 0.1 ms pulse), the operator elicits a brisk distal motor twitch at 1.0 mA and dials the current down toward target threshold. Why must local anesthetic NEVER be injected if strong motor twitches persist at \u003c= 0.2 mA?","o":["Nerve stimulators lose electrical charge below 0.5 mA","A twitch at 0.1 mA proves the needle is 5 cm away from the nerve","Local anesthetics only work when injected directly inside the nerve fascicle at 0.05 mA","A persistent motor twitch at \u003c= 0.2 mA strongly suggests hazardous intraneural (intra-fascicular) needle tip placement, risking permanent ischemic/mechanical nerve injury upon injection; target optimal motor response is ~0.3-0.5 mA"],"a":3,"e":"In peripheral nerve stimulation (with negative/black cathode lead on the insulated needle), approaching the epineurium elicits target distal muscle contraction at ~0.3-0.5 mA (disappearing when current drops below 0.2 mA or immediately upon injecting 1-2 mL D5W/saline/LA). Persistent motor response at \u003c=0.2 mA—or high injection opening pressure \u003e15-20 psi or severe lancinating paresthesia—warns of intraneural placement requiring slight needle withdrawal before injection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01383","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Monitoring in Anaesthesia","topic":"Sudden Stepwise ETCO2 Drop","q":"Midway through pelvic tumor resection under controlled mechanical ventilation (constant tidal volume and respiratory rate), the capnograph displays an ABRUPT stepwise drop in ETCO2 from 37 mmHg down to 11 mmHg over 3 breaths, while airway pressures and breathing circuit connections remain completely intact, accompanied by sudden hypotension and tachycardia. What catastrophic event does this capnogram signify?","o":["Massive pulmonary thromboembolism, venous air embolism, or abrupt severe drop in cardiac output / impending cardiac arrest (markedly increased alveolar dead space)","Accidental hyperventilation from switching tidal volume down by 50%","Exhaustion of soda lime granules in the absorber","Severe acute bronchospasm without hemodynamic change"],"a":0,"e":"When minute ventilation is fixed and the circuit is intact, ETCO2 reflects pulmonary blood flow delivering CO2 to ventilated alveoli. An abrupt precipitous drop in ETCO2 (with persistent waveform rather than zero disconnection line) signals acute massive dead-space ventilation from pulmonary thromboembolism, venous air/fat/CO2 embolism, or acute cardiac output collapse/cardiac arrest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01384","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Breathing Circuits","topic":"Jackson-Rees Circuit (Mapleson F)","q":"Why is a Mapleson F (Jackson-Rees modification of Ayre\u0027s T-piece) circuit widely used for infants and children \u003c20 kg, and what Fresh Gas Flow (FGF) rate relative to minute ventilation (MV) is required during spontaneous breathing to prevent CO2 rebreathing?","o":["It has a heavy weighted pop-off valve at the facemask; requires FGF equal to 0.5x MV","It is lightweight, valveless with minimal dead space and very low resistance; requires FGF of 2.5 to 3 times minute ventilation (minimum ~3-4 L/min) to flush exhaled CO2","It can only be used in adults \u003e80 kg at 1 L/min FGF","It contains two unidirectional dome valves and soda lime; requires FGF of 0.2 L/min"],"a":1,"e":"The Jackson-Rees (Mapleson F) T-piece adds an open-ended 500 mL reservoir bag (with adjustable tail bleed valve) to Ayre\u0027s T-piece. Having zero mechanical valves keeps expiratory resistance and work of breathing negligible for infants, but because there is no CO2 absorber, high fresh gas flow (2.5-3x MV, usually \u003e=3-4 L/min) is essential to prevent rebreathing of alveolar gas.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01385","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Machine Safety","topic":"Oxygen Flowmeter Downstream Position","q":"On a conventional multi-gas anaesthetic workstation flowmeter bank, why is the Oxygen flowmeter tube always placed DOWNSTREAM (closest to the common gas outlet—on the right side in standard machines) relative to Nitrous Oxide and Medical Air flowmeters?","o":["To allow vaporizers to be mounted upstream of the oxygen cylinder yoke","To force nitrous oxide to boil inside the oxygen tube","So that if a crack or seal leak develops in an upstream N2O or Air glass rotameter tube, N2O/Air leaks outward into the room rather than oxygen leaking out—preventing delivery of a hypoxic gas mixture to the patient","Because oxygen floats upward only on the right side of glass tubes"],"a":2,"e":"If the O2 flowmeter were upstream of N2O, a cracked O2 glass tube or leaky O-ring could vent O2 out into the room while downstream N2O continued flowing into the common manifold—delivering a lethal hypoxic mixture despite an apparently normal O2 bobbin reading. Placing O2 strictly last (downstream, closest to the manifold exit) ensures any upstream N2O leak spares O2 concentration, and any O2 tube leak still leaves downstream O2 flowing last (note: an O2 tube crack below the bobbin still drops O2 flow, so O2 analyzers remain mandatory!).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01386","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Regional Anaesthesia Setup","topic":"Lidocaine-Epinephrine Epidural Test Dose","q":"After threading a lumbar epidural catheter for labor analgesia, the anaesthesiologist injects a standard 3 mL test dose of 1.5% lidocaine with 1:200,000 epinephrine (45 mg lidocaine + 15 mcg epinephrine). Within 45 seconds, the ECG monitor shows heart rate jumping from 82 to 118/min (+36 bpm) and systolic BP rising by 25 mmHg, without dense leg motor block. What does this response signify?","o":["Normal safe epidural placement ready for 20 mL bupivacaine bolus","Anaphylaxis to sterile saline flush","Accidental intrathecal (subarachnoid) placement causing total spinal block","Accidental intravascular (epidural vein) catheter placement—do NOT inject the main local anesthetic bolus; withdraw and re-site the catheter"],"a":3,"e":"The standard epidural test dose combines 45 mg lidocaine (detecting accidental subarachnoid placement via rapid dense motor/sensory block within 3-5 minutes) and 15 mcg epinephrine (detecting accidental intravenous placement into engorged epidural veins via \u003e=10-20 bpm heart rate increase and/or \u003e=15 mmHg SBP spike within 30-60 seconds, unless blunted by beta-blockers or labor pain). Recognizing intravascular placement prevents catastrophic LAST.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01387","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Preoperative Preparation","topic":"ASA Preoperative Fasting (NPO) Guidelines","q":"According to ASA Preoperative Fasting Guidelines for healthy patients undergoing elective surgery under general anaesthesia or sedation, what are the minimum recommended fasting durations for clear liquids, breast milk (infants), light solid meal / infant formula, and heavy fried/fatty meal?","o":["Clear liquids: 2 hours; Breast milk: 4 hours; Infant formula / non-human milk / light meal: 6 hours; Fatty/fried/heavy meal: 8 hours","Clear liquids: 0 hours (drink up to induction); Breast milk: 1 hour; Solid meal: 2 hours","Clear liquids: 12 hours; Breast milk: 12 hours; Light meal: 24 hours; Fatty meal: 48 hours","All oral intake strictly forbidden for 18 hours before any elective procedure"],"a":0,"e":"ASA fasting guidelines follow the 2-4-6-8 rule to minimize pulmonary aspiration while preventing dehydration and hypoglycemia: clear liquids (water, black tea/coffee, pulp-free clear juice) = 2 hrs; breast milk = 4 hrs; infant formula, cow\u0027s milk, and light solid meals (toast/crackers) = 6 hrs; fried, fatty, or meat-heavy meals (which delay gastric emptying) = 8 hrs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01388","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Breathing Circuits","topic":"Stuck-Open Expiratory Unidirectional Valve","q":"Midway through a laparoscopic surgery on a circle breathing system, the capnograph shows a progressive rise in INSPIRED baseline CO2 (rising from 0 mmHg to 18 mmHg) even though the soda lime canister was freshly filled white just 30 minutes ago. Visual inspection reveals condensation droplets pinning the EXPIRATORY unidirectional dome valve open. Why does a stuck-open expiratory valve cause inspired CO2 rebreathing?","o":["Soda lime turns into pure carbon dioxide when wet","During each mechanical inspiration, previously exhaled CO2-rich gas sitting in the expiratory limb flows backward past the stuck-open expiratory valve directly back into the patient\u0027s inspiratory Y-piece, bypassing the soda lime absorber","Fresh oxygen enters the room backward through the expiratory valve","The inspiratory valve melts the endotracheal tube cuff"],"a":1,"e":"Circle systems rely on competent inspiratory and expiratory unidirectional dome valves to force exhaled gas solely through the soda lime canister. If the expiratory (or inspiratory) valve sticks open—often from moisture condensation—exhaled alveolar gas in the corrugated tubing shuttles back and forth into the Y-piece on inspiration without passing through soda lime, elevating inspired baseline CO2 (\u003e0 mmHg on capnography) and causing hypercapnia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01389","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Machine Safety","topic":"Agent-Specific Keyed Fillers and Interlock","q":"What two mechanical engineering safeguards on modern anesthesia workstations prevent (1) pouring Sevoflurane into an Isoflurane vaporizer and (2) turning ON two volatile vaporizers simultaneously?","o":["(1) Pin Index Safety System (PISS) pins 2-5 and (2) soda lime ethyl violet indicator","(1) Universal funnel pouring and (2) dual-vaporizer boosting switch","(1) Color-coded, geometrically keyed agent-specific bottle-filler adaptors (yellow sevoflurane, purple isoflurane) and (2) the mechanical vaporizer Interlock exclusion bar/pin system","(1) Link-25 oxygen chain and (2) scavenging negative-pressure valve"],"a":2,"e":"Misfilling a vaporizer with a different volatile agent having higher vapor pressure (e.g., halothane/isoflurane into desflurane or vice versa) causes lethal anesthetic overdose or insufficient anesthesia. Keyed agent-specific collars/adapters physically mate only with the matching color-coded vaporizer (yellow = sevoflurane, purple = isoflurane, blue = desflurane, red = halothane), while the Selectatec/Drager interlock bar physically locks all other vaporizers at zero whenever one dial is turned on.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01390","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Preoperative Airway Assessment","topic":"Modified Mallampati Classification","q":"When performing a Modified Mallampati airway screening examination before surgery, how must the patient be positioned and instructed, and what structures are visible in a difficult-airway Mallampati Class IV?","o":["Sitting upright with head in neutral position, mouth opened maximally, and tongue fully protruded WITHOUT phonation (\u0027ah\u0027); Class IV shows ONLY the hard palate (neither soft palate, uvula, nor faucial pillars visible)","Lying flat supine with neck flexed, whispering \u0027eee\u0027 loudly; Class IV shows entire tonsils and uvula","Sitting with mouth closed breathing through the nose; Class IV shows full soft palate and faucial pillars","Standing bent forward 90 degrees; Class IV shows the vocal cords directly"],"a":0,"e":"Modified Mallampati (Samsoon \u0026 Young) grading requires the patient seated upright at eye level, head neutral, opening wide and protruding the tongue maximally WITHOUT saying \u0027ah\u0027 (phonation elevates the soft palate and falsely improves the score!). Class I: soft palate, fauces, uvula, pillars visible; Class II: soft palate, fauces, uvula; Class III: soft palate and base of uvula only; Class IV: hard palate only (higher risk of difficult laryngoscopy/mask ventilation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01391","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Neuroanaesthesia and Fluid Therapy","topic":"Avoiding Hypotonic Dextrose in Neurosurgery","q":"When preparing maintenance IV fluid bags for an adult craniotomy for traumatic subdural hematoma/brain tumor, why must hypotonic fluids such as 5% Dextrose in Water (D5W) or half-normal saline (0.45% NaCl) be AVOIDED in favor of isotonic 0.9% normal saline or balanced isotonic crystalloids?","o":["0.9% normal saline causes massive brain shrinkage down to zero intracranial pressure within 2 minutes","D5W freezes inside central venous catheters at operating room temperatures","Balanced isotonic fluids lower serum sodium to 110 mEq/L","Once dextrose is metabolized, D5W acts as pure hypotonic free water that crosses the blood-brain barrier and worsens cerebral edema and intracranial hypertension (while hyperglycemia also exacerbates ischemic neuronal injury)"],"a":3,"e":"Although D5W looks iso-osmolar in the bag (~252-278 mOsm/L), rapid cellular uptake and metabolism of glucose leaves pure hypo-osmolar free water that shifts across the blood-brain barrier into brain parenchyma, aggravating cerebral edema and raised ICP. Moreover, perioperative hyperglycemia worsens anaerobic lactic acidosis in ischemic brain tissue—making glucose-free isotonic crystalloids (0.9% NaCl, osmolarity ~308 mOsm/L, or Plasma-Lyte) standard in neuroanaesthesia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01392","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Pharmacology for Anaesthesia Technicians","topic":"Neostigmine + Glycopyrrolate vs Sugammadex","q":"When preparing routine reversal of vecuronium or atracurium at the end of surgery using the acetylcholinesterase inhibitor Neostigmine, why MUST an anticholinergic (Glycopyrrolate or Atropine) be drawn up and co-administered—and why does Sugammadex NOT require an anticholinergic?","o":["Neostigmine increases acetylcholine at ALL cholinergic synapses—without glycopyrrolate to block muscarinic receptors, it causes severe bradycardia/asystole, bronchospasm, and salivation; Sugammadex selectively encapsulates rocuronium/vecuronium directly without altering acetylcholine","Neostigmine causes severe tachycardia \u003e200/min unless glycopyrrolate slows the heart","Sugammadex is a mixture of atropine and succinylcholine","Glycopyrrolate reverses skeletal muscle paralysis directly at the nicotinic motor endplate"],"a":0,"e":"Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase non-selectively, boosting ACh at both nicotinic neuromuscular junctions (desired reversal) and muscarinic parasympathetic sites (undesired bradycardia/AV block, bronchoconstriction, bronchorrhea, GI cramps). Co-administering glycopyrrolate (matching neostigmine\u0027s onset without crossing the blood-brain barrier) blocks muscarinic effects cleanly. Sugammadex is a modified gamma-cyclodextrin that irreversibly chelates steroidal relaxants (rocuronium/vecuronium) in plasma—requiring no anticholinergic.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01393","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Oxygen Therapy Equipment","topic":"Venturi (HAFOE) Mask vs Non-Rebreather Mask","q":"A postoperative COPD patient with chronic CO2 retention requires tightly controlled 28% FiO2 oxygen therapy regardless of fluctuations in his breathing rate or inspiratory flow. Which oxygen mask operates on the Bernoulli air-entrainment principle to deliver a precise, fixed FiO2?","o":["Uncapped nasal cannula at 10 L/min","Venturi mask (High Air Flow Oxygen Enrichment / HAFOE mask using the color-coded 28% white jet adapter at its specified L/min O2 flow)","Non-Rebreather Reservoir Mask (NRBM) at 15 L/min","Simple Hudson plastic facemask at 2 L/min"],"a":1,"e":"Simple masks and nasal cannulae are variable-performance devices whose delivered FiO2 swings wildly depending on the patient\u0027s peak inspiratory flow and respiratory rate. A Venturi (HAFOE) mask forces oxygen through a narrow precision orifice at high velocity, entraining a fixed, predictable ratio of room air via the Bernoulli effect so total gas flow exceeds the patient\u0027s peak inspiratory demand—guaranteeing exact FiO2 (24%, 28%, 35%, 40%, 60%) for hypercapnic COPD patients.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01394","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Airway Management \u0026 Perioperative Emergencies","topic":"RSI Preparation \u0026 Preoxygenation","q":"At an OSCE station preparing for Rapid Sequence Induction (RSI) in an emergency intestinal obstruction patient with a full stomach, which preparation and induction assistance protocol is essential?","o":["Inflate the stomach gently with 25 cmH2O bag-mask positive pressure before administering muscle relaxant","Leave suction disconnected until after the vocal cords are visualized","Confirm working wide-bore Yankauer suction turned ON under the pillow, preoxygenate with 100% O2 via tight mask for 3–5 min (targeting EtO2 \u003e 85–90%), prepare rapid-onset induction agent + Succinylcholine (or high-dose Rocuronium) with styletted ETT, and avoid routine vigorous bag-mask ventilation prior to intubation unless hypoxic","Give only 50% N2O/O2 during preoxygenation to speed induction"],"a":2,"e":"RSI minimizes gastric aspiration risk during the interval from loss of airway reflexes to cuffed tracheal tube placement. High-flow 100% O2 preoxygenation denitrogenates functional residual capacity (EtO2 \u003e85–90%), providing safe apnea time without needing positive-pressure bag-mask ventilation (which insufflates the stomach), while immediate live suction prevents aspiration if regurgitation occurs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01395","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Airway Management \u0026 Perioperative Emergencies","topic":"ETT Cuff Pressure Monitoring \u0026 Nitrous Oxide Diffusion","q":"Following endotracheal intubation for a 3-hour surgery maintained with Nitrous Oxide (N2O) and Isoflurane, what target cuff pressure range must be maintained using a cuff manometer, and why does cuff pressure tend to climb intraoperatively when N2O is used?","o":["5–10 cmH2O; Isoflurane freezes the pilot balloon valve","60–80 cmH2O; Nitrous oxide shrinks the cuff over time causing massive leaks","100 cmH2O; manual finger-palpation of the pilot balloon reliably guarantees exact pressure","20–30 cmH2O; Nitrous oxide diffuses across the PVC cuff faster than nitrogen leaves, progressively increasing cuff volume and risking tracheal mucosal ischemia if unchecked"],"a":3,"e":"Standard high-volume low-pressure ETT cuffs should be maintained at 20–30 cmH2O: below 20 cmH2O risks silent microaspiration of subglottic secretions, while above 30 cmH2O exceeds tracheal capillary perfusion pressure (~25–30 mmHg), causing mucosal necrosis and post-intubation stenosis. Because N2O is 34× more soluble/diffusible than nitrogen, it enters air-filled cuffs rapidly, raising pressure unless monitored or filled with saline/N2O-O2.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01396","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Pediatric \u0026 Specialty Anaesthesia Support","topic":"Cole Formula \u0026 Pediatric ETT Depth","q":"You are preparing the airway trolley for a 4-year-old child scheduled for tonsillectomy. Using standard pediatric formulas (Uncuffed ID = [Age/4] + 4; Cuffed ID = [Age/4] + 3.5; Oral lip depth = [Age/2] + 12 or 3 × ETT ID), what cuffed ETT internal diameter (ID) and approximate oral insertion depth at the lips should be prepared?","o":["4.5 mm ID cuffed ETT (with 4.0 and 5.0 mm backups); ~14 cm depth at the lips","3.0 mm ID cuffed ETT; ~9 cm depth at the lips","6.5 mm ID cuffed ETT; ~21 cm depth at the lips","7.5 mm ID cuffed ETT; ~23 cm depth at the lips"],"a":0,"e":"For a 4-year-old: Uncuffed ETT ID = (4/4) + 4 = 5.0 mm; for a modern micro-cuffed ETT, subtract 0.5 mm → (4/4) + 3.5 = 4.5 mm ID (always keep half-size smaller and larger ready). Estimated oral insertion depth at the teeth/lips = (4/2) + 12 = 14 cm (or ~3 × 4.5 = 13.5–14 cm), confirmed by bilateral breath sounds.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01397","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Regional Anaesthesia \u0026 Perioperative Support","topic":"LAST Recognition \u0026 Lipid Emulsion Rescue","q":"Two minutes after an ultrasound-guided supraclavicular brachial plexus block using 30 mL of 0.5% Bupivacaine, the patient reports metallic taste, tinnitus, and perioral numbness, followed suddenly by generalized tonic-clonic seizures and wide-complex ventricular arrhythmia. After stopping injection and securing airway/oxygenation, which specific antidote must be retrieved and prepared immediately?","o":["IV Lidocaine 100 mg bolus to suppress the ventricular arrhythmia","20% Intravenous Lipid Emulsion (Intralipid): 1.5 mL/kg initial IV bolus followed by 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion (plus benzodiazepine for seizures; avoid high-dose epinephrine/lidocaine/propofol)","IV Flumazenil 0.5 mg bolus","IV Naloxone 2 mg bolus"],"a":1,"e":"Bupivacaine is highly lipophilic and cardiotoxic; inadvertent intravascular injection causes CNS excitation (tinnitus, circumoral paresthesia, seizures) followed by refractory cardiovascular collapse (LAST). ASRA guidelines mandate immediate 20% Lipid Emulsion (\"lipid sink\" + metabolic rescue: 1.5 mL/kg bolus over 2–3 min, then 0.25 mL/kg/min), benzodiazepines for seizures, and modified ACLS (small \u003c=1 mcg/kg epinephrine doses, avoid vasopressin/CCBs/beta-blockers/local anaesthetics).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01398","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Pre-Anaesthetic Evaluation \u0026 Pharmacology","topic":"ASA Physical Status \u0026 NPO Fasting Intervals","q":"Under American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) / ISA preoperative fasting guidelines for healthy elective surgical patients, what are the minimum recommended fasting durations for (1) clear liquids, (2) human breast milk, and (3) infant formula / light solid meal?","o":["Clear liquids: 8 hours; Breast milk: 12 hours; Light meal: 24 hours","All liquids and solids require uniform strict 18-hour fasting","Clear liquids: 2 hours; Breast milk: 4 hours; Infant formula / light meal: 6 hours (heavy/fried/fatty meal: 8 hours)","Clear liquids: 15 minutes; Breast milk: 1 hour; Fried meal: 2 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Standard evidence-based \"2-4-6-8\" preoperative NPO guidelines allow clear water/apple juice/black tea up to 2 hours before anaesthesia (reducing dehydration/hypoglycemia), breast milk up to 4 hours, non-human milk/infant formula/light toast-type meal up to 6 hours, and fried/fatty or meat meals \u003e= 8 hours due to delayed gastric emptying.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01399","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Patient Monitoring \u0026 Perioperative Vigilance","topic":"Peak Inspiratory Pressure (PIP) vs Plateau Pressure","q":"During volume-controlled mechanical ventilation under general anaesthesia, the Peak Inspiratory Pressure (PIP) suddenly jumps from 18 to 38 cmH2O, triggering the high-pressure alarm, while an inspiratory-hold maneuver shows Plateau Pressure (Pplat) remains normal at 15 cmH2O (widened PIP–Pplat gradient = 23 cmH2O). Which category of problem is causing the alarm?","o":["Decreased lung/chest wall compliance (e.g., tension pneumothorax or severe pulmonary edema)","Complete breathing circuit disconnection at the Y-piece","Empty oxygen pipeline pressure","Increased airway resistance (e.g., kinked/bitten endotracheal tube, thick mucus plug in ETT, or acute bronchospasm)"],"a":3,"e":"PIP reflects BOTH airway resistance and alveolar compliance, whereas Plateau Pressure (measured at zero airflow during an inspiratory pause) reflects purely alveolar/chest-wall compliance. A high PIP with a normal Pplat (gradient \u003e 5 cmH2O) pinpoints an AIRWAY RESISTANCE issue (kinked ETT, secretions, bronchospasm). Conversely, when both PIP and Pplat rise together, compliance has fallen (pneumothorax, pulmonary edema, mainstem intubation, abdominal insufflation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01400","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Patient Monitoring \u0026 Perioperative Vigilance","topic":"Train-of-Four (TOF) \u0026 Residual Neuromuscular Blockade","q":"At the end of abdominal surgery using Rocuronium, quantitative Train-of-Four (TOF) stimulation at the ulnar nerve / adductor pollicis shows 4 twitches with visible fade and a TOF ratio (T4/T1) of 0.55. Why is it unsafe to extubate immediately, and what TOF ratio is required before safe tracheal extubation?","o":["TOF ratio \u003c 0.90 indicates significant residual neuromuscular blockade risking pharyngeal dysfunction, airway obstruction, and aspiration; administer appropriate reversal (Neostigmine + Glycopyrrolate, or Sugammadex) and verify TOF ratio \u003e= 0.90 before extubation","TOF ratio 0.55 represents complete recovery; extubate immediately without reversal","Monitor TOF on the diaphragm because adductor pollicis recovers slowest and is irrelevant","Administer an extra dose of Succinylcholine to neutralize Rocuronium"],"a":0,"e":"Even when patients appear awake or lift an arm briefly, a TOF ratio \u003c 0.90 at the adductor pollicis leaves upper airway pharyngeal muscles and hypoxic ventilatory drive impaired—causing postoperative respiratory failure and aspiration. Modern anaesthesia standards require quantitative TOF ratio \u003e= 0.90 (after neostigmine/glycopyrrolate or sugammadex reversal) prior to safe extubation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01401","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Workstation \u0026 Medical Gas Supply","topic":"Waste Anaesthetic Gas Scavenging System (AGSS)","q":"Where does the Waste Anaesthetic Gas Scavenging System (AGSS) collect excess inhalational gases from the anaesthesia workstation, and what are the NIOSH recommended occupational exposure limits for halogenated agents (when used with N2O) and Nitrous Oxide?","o":["Vents gas directly onto the surgeon’s head via the oxygen flush valve; no exposure limits exist","Collects vented gas from the APL (pop-off) valve and ventilator pressure-relief valve; NIOSH limits are \u003c= 0.5 ppm for halogenated agents (\u003c= 2 ppm if alone) and \u003c= 25 ppm for N2O","Collects from the soda lime exhaust and discharges back into the OT air-conditioning return duct","Collects gas directly from the patient’s arterial line; NIOSH limits are \u003c= 500 ppm for halogenated agents"],"a":1,"e":"AGSS interfaces collect excess gas exiting the Adjustable Pressure-Limiting (APL) valve during manual/spontaneous ventilation and the ventilator relief valve during mechanical ventilation, routing it safely outside the building. NIOSH limits ceiling exposure to \u003c= 2 ppm for halogenated agents alone, \u003c= 0.5 ppm when combined with N2O, and \u003c= 25 ppm TWA for N2O.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01402","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Patient Monitoring \u0026 Perioperative Vigilance","topic":"Arterial Line Transducer Leveling \u0026 Zeroing","q":"During an invasive radial arterial line setup in a supine patient, the pressure transducer is inadvertently mounted 13.6 cm BELOW the phlebostatic axis (4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line). How will this positioning error affect the displayed arterial blood pressure reading?","o":["Displayed blood pressure remains completely accurate as long as the stopcock was zeroed once at floor level","Only heart rate will be inaccurate while systolic and diastolic pressures remain exact","Displayed blood pressure will read falsely HIGH by approximately 10 mmHg due to hydrostatic fluid column pressure","Displayed blood pressure will read falsely LOW by approximately 10 mmHg"],"a":2,"e":"Every 1.36 cm of vertical height difference between the transducer and the phlebostatic axis introduces a 1 mmHg hydrostatic pressure error (10 cm = ~7.4 mmHg; 13.6 cm = 10 mmHg). If the transducer sits BELOW the heart, the added fluid column increases pressure, yielding falsely HIGH BP readings; if ABOVE the heart, BP reads falsely LOW.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01403","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Anaesthesia Machine Engineering","topic":"Pressure-Reducing Regulator and Yoke Check Valves","q":"On an anaesthesia machine\u0027s high-pressure oxygen cylinder yoke assembly, what are the distinct roles of (1) the Pressure-Reducing Valve (Regulator) and (2) the unidirectional Yoke Check Valve?","o":["(1) Steps down high variable cylinder pressure (~137 bar / 2,000 psi) to a safe constant ~3-3.5 bar (~45 psi) so pipeline pressure (~50-55 psi) takes precedence and flowmeters stay steady; (2) prevents gas from leaking out of an empty yoke or transfilling between twin cylinders","(1) Boosts cylinder pressure to 10,000 psi; (2) injects liquid sevoflurane into the yoke","(1) Filters carbon dioxide from oxygen cylinders; (2) heats nitrous oxide to 100 degrees C","Both valves simply turn the machine power switch on and off"],"a":0,"e":"Without pressure regulators, falling tank pressure would continuously drop flowmeter delivery and damage downstream delicate glass rotameters. Setting cylinder regulator output slightly lower (~45 psi) than central wall pipeline pressure (~50-55 psi) also ensures the machine preferentially consumes central pipeline gas when both are open (reminder: close backup E-cylinder after checking unless pipeline fails!). Unidirectional yoke check valves block hazardous high-pressure transfilling or back-leakage from an open cylinder into an empty yoke.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01404","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Inhalational Anaesthesia Pharmacology","topic":"Blood-Gas Partition Coefficient","q":"Why do Desflurane (blood-gas partition coefficient = 0.42) and Sevoflurane (0.65) produce dramatically faster anaesthetic induction and postoperative awakening than older agents like Halothane (2.30) or Isoflurane (1.40)?","o":["Lower blood-gas solubility means the drug binds irreversibly to hemoglobin forever","Higher blood-gas partition coefficients produce faster induction and emergence","Desflurane and sevoflurane are injected intravenously rather than inhaled","Lower blood-gas solubility means blood saturates rapidly (less volatile agent dissolves into blood as a \u0027sink\u0027), so alveolar and brain partial pressures equilibrate and wash out much faster"],"a":3,"e":"Inhalational anesthetic depth tracks partial pressure (PA -\u003e Pa -\u003e Pbrain), NOT total dissolved mass. Agents with a high blood-gas partition coefficient dissolve heavily into blood—requiring huge uptake before arterial partial pressure rises (slow induction/slow emergence). Insoluble agents like desflurane (0.42) and sevoflurane (0.65) saturate blood quickly, accelerating both FA/FI equilibration and awakening.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01405","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Monitoring in Anaesthesia","topic":"Bispectral Index (BIS) Interpretation","q":"When using a forehead Bispectral Index (BIS) processed-EEG monitor during total intravenous anaesthesia (TIVA) with muscle relaxation, what BIS value range targets adequate surgical hypnosis (minimizing intraoperative awareness while avoiding burst suppression), and how does adding IV Ketamine affect the BIS display?","o":["Target BIS 40 to 60 for general anaesthesia; Ketamine paradoxically INCREASES high-frequency EEG beta/gamma power and can elevate BIS values (\u003e60-75) despite deepening dissociation/anaesthesia","BIS directly measures serum propofol concentration in mg/L from forehead sweat","Target BIS 95 to 100 for deep surgical anaesthesia; Ketamine drops BIS instantly to 0","Target BIS 0 to 10 (complete flatline burst suppression) for every elective hernia case"],"a":0,"e":"BIS translates frontal EEG frequency/phase into a 0-100 dimensionless scale (100 = awake, 40-60 = surgical general anesthesia, \u003c40 = deep excessive hypnosis/burst suppression). Crucially, electrocautery interference, EMG muscle activity (if unparalyzed), and NMDA-antagonist Ketamine (which generates fast beta/gamma EEG waves) falsely elevate BIS scores—whereas hypothermia, cerebral hypoperfusion, and deep propofol/volatile agents lower BIS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01406","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Obstetric Anaesthesia","topic":"Aortocaval Compression and Spinal Hypotension","q":"Immediately after administering intrathecal hyperbaric bupivacaine for an elective term cesarean section, the parturient lies flat supine and her blood pressure plummets from 118/74 to 74/42 mmHg with nausea and fetal bradycardia. Which positioning adjustment and first-line alpha-1 vasopressor rapidly restore maternal BP and uteroplacental perfusion?","o":["Administer IV furosemide 40 mg to reduce preload","Place a 15-degree left uterine displacement wedge under the right hip (relieving gravid aortocaval compression), infuse crystalloid co-load, and administer titrated IV Phenylephrine (or ephedrine if bradycardic)","Sit the patient upright at 90 degrees immediately while injecting 20 mL more spinal bupivacaine","Tilt the patient onto her right hip pressing the uterus onto the IVC, and give IV nitroglycerin"],"a":1,"e":"Spinal sympathectomy ablates lower-body vasomotor tone; combined with gravid uterine compression of the inferior vena cava and aorta in the flat supine position, venous return and cardiac output crash rapidly. A 15-degree left lateral tilt (wedge under right flank/hip) relieves IVC compression, while phenylephrine (pure alpha-1 agonist—preferred over ephedrine due to less placental transfer/fetal acidosis unless maternal HR \u003c60) + IV fluid co-loading restores BP.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01407","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Paediatric Anaesthesia Emergencies","topic":"Acute Laryngospasm Rescue Sequence","q":"Shortly after tracheal extubation during light plane of anaesthesia in a 4-year-old boy following tonsillectomy, the child develops high-pitched inspiratory stridor progressing rapidly to silent chest, paradoxical chest-abdomen \u0027rocking\u0027, flat ETCO2 trace, and SpO2 falling to 72% (acute complete laryngospasm). What is the stepwise rescue sequence?","o":["Administer IV neostigmine and glycopyrrolate to break vocal cord spasm","Force a metal stylet blindly through closed spastic vocal cords immediately without CPAP or relaxant","Remove airway secretions/stimuli, apply jaw thrust with firm pressure in Larson\u0027s notch (behind earlobes) plus tight 100% O2 CPAP via bag-mask; if persistent, deepen with IV propofol (0.5-1 mg/kg) or administer small-dose IV succinylcholine (0.1-0.5 mg/kg; 2-4 mg/kg IM if no IV) with atropine ready for bradycardia","Switch off oxygen and wait 10 minutes for spontaneous awakening"],"a":2,"e":"Laryngospasm is reflex glottic closure triggered by blood/secretions or airway manipulation during Stage 2 light anesthesia. Stepwise rescue starts with clearing pharyngeal secretions, vigorous jaw thrust + Larson\u0027s pressure point (anteriorly between mandibular condyle and mastoid) + gentle sustained 100% O2 CPAP (avoiding high-pressure gastric insufflation). Refractory spasm/desaturation requires IV propofol or low-dose succinylcholine (plus atropine if severe hypoxic bradycardia develops) to relax the vocal cords before negative-pressure pulmonary edema or hypoxic arrest occurs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01408","course":"Diploma in Anaesthesia Technology","subject":"Invasive Monitoring Preparation","topic":"Modified Allen\u0027s Test","q":"When performing a Modified Allen\u0027s test prior to radial artery cannulation for invasive blood pressure monitoring, both the radial and ulnar arteries are compressed while the patient clenches the fist until the palm blanches white, and then ONLY the ulnar artery is released while the hand relaxes slightly. What capillary refill time confirms adequate collateral ulnar perfusion?","o":["Immediate cyanotic blue discoloration of the fingertips","Blush returning after 45 seconds","No color return until the radial artery is released","Brisk palmar flushing/blush returning across the palm and thumb within \u003c= 5 to 7 seconds (whereas delay \u003e 10-15 seconds indicates inadequate collateral flow)"],"a":3,"e":"In the Modified Allen\u0027s test, releasing ulnar compression alone tests whether the ulnar artery supplies the superficial and deep palmar arches adequately should the cannulated radial artery thrombose. Return of normal pink palmar perfusion within \u003c=5-7 seconds (up to \u003c10 s) indicates a normal/positive collateral circulation; \u003e10-15 seconds delay warns of poor collateral flow (prompting Doppler/pulse-ox plethysmography check or choosing an alternative site).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01409","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Water Treatment","topic":"AAMI Water Standards and Chloramine Monitoring","q":"During a morning hemodialysis shift, two patients simultaneously develop sudden dyspnea, chest pain, and dark red-brown blood in the venous bloodline after 45 minutes of treatment. Chloramine testing of the RO product water shows 0.25 mg/L (above the 0.1 mg/L AAMI limit). Which component of the water treatment plant has failed?","o":["Dual activated carbon adsorption tanks","Ultraviolet (UV) bacterial irradiator","Multimedia sediment sand filter","Water softener resin regeneration unit"],"a":0,"e":"Granular activated carbon (GAC) tanks arranged in series (worker and polisher) remove chlorine and chloramines from municipal feed water. Breakthrough of chloramines (\u003e0.1 mg/L) crosses the dialyzer membrane and causes acute oxidative hemolysis with methemoglobinemia and Heinz body anemia, necessitating immediate cessation of dialysis without returning blood.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01410","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Hemodialysis Procedure and Complications","topic":"Air Embolism Management","q":"The ultrasonic air detector alarms and the dialysis machine clamps the venous line after microbubbles enter the venous tubing in a patient sitting upright. The patient suddenly coughs and becomes hypotensive. What is the immediate sequence of actions by the dialysis technician?","o":["Clamp the venous line manually, stop the blood pump, place the patient in Trendelenburg (head-down) left lateral decubitus position, and administer 100% oxygen","Turn off the air detector alarm, restart the blood pump to flush the air, and elevate the head of the bed to 60 degrees","Place the patient in right lateral decubitus position with head elevated and flush the venous line with normal saline","Immediately remove both fistula needles without clamping the bloodlines"],"a":0,"e":"In suspected air embolism, stopping the blood pump and clamping the venous line prevents further air entry. Placing the patient in left lateral decubitus with Trendelenburg tilt (Durant maneuver) traps air in the apex of the right ventricle away from the pulmonary artery outflow tract while 100% O2 accelerates nitrogen washout.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01411","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Principles and Adequacy","topic":"Ultrafiltration Rate Calculation","q":"A patient\u0027s dry weight is 62.0 kg and pre-dialysis weight is 64.6 kg. During a 4-hour hemodialysis session, the patient will receive 200 mL of IV normal saline as priming/rinseback fluid and drinks 200 mL of tea. What total ultrafiltration (UF) goal and hourly UF rate should be programmed?","o":["Total UF 3400 mL; hourly UF rate 850 mL/hr","Total UF 3000 mL; hourly UF rate 750 mL/hr","Total UF 2600 mL; hourly UF rate 650 mL/hr","Total UF 2200 mL; hourly UF rate 550 mL/hr"],"a":1,"e":"Interdialytic weight gain is 64.6 - 62.0 = 2.6 kg (2600 mL). Adding intradialytic fluid intake (200 mL saline rinseback + 200 mL oral tea = 400 mL) yields a total UF target of 2600 + 400 = 3000 mL. Over 4 hours, the hourly UF rate is 3000 / 4 = 750 mL/hr.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01412","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Vascular Access Management","topic":"AVF Cannulation and Monitoring","q":"When cannulating a mature radiocephalic arteriovenous fistula using the rope-ladder technique, how should the arterial and venous needles be spaced and oriented?","o":["Space needle tips at least 5 cm apart; point the venous needle antegrade (toward the heart) and keep arterial needle at least 3 cm from the anastomosis","Insert both needles at the exact same puncture site every session; point the venous needle retrograde toward the hand","Space needles 1 cm apart to reduce circuit length; point both needles toward the anastomosis","Always place the arterial needle proximal to the venous needle in the direction of venous return"],"a":0,"e":"Proper rope-ladder cannulation rotates sites along the vessel length with needle tips separated by \u003e=5 cm (or \u003e=2.5-5 cm depending on bevel direction) to prevent access recirculation and aneurysm formation. The venous needle always points antegrade with blood flow toward the heart, and the arterial needle is placed \u003e=3 cm away from the surgical anastomosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01413","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Peritoneal Dialysis and Reprocessing","topic":"Hepatitis B and Hepatitis C Isolation","q":"Under Indian MoHFW / Pradhan Mantri National Dialysis Programme infection control guidelines, what is mandatory regarding hemodialysis machine allocation and dialyzer reuse for a patient who tests positive for Hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg)?","o":["Dialyze on the same machine as Hepatitis C-positive patients without changing staff gloves","Reuse the dialyzer in the common reprocessing room after formalin soaking","Dialyze in a dedicated isolation room using a dedicated HBsAg-positive machine and never reuse the dialyzer","Use a shared machine with routine bleach wipe-down between patients and reuse the dialyzer up to 10 times"],"a":2,"e":"Hepatitis B virus is highly infectious in the dialysis environment and survives on surfaces for up to 7 days. HBsAg-positive patients require a separate isolation room/area, a dedicated machine, dedicated staff/equipment, and single-use (no reuse) dialyzers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01414","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysate Proportioning and Monitoring","topic":"Conductivity Alarm and Osmotic Hemolysis","q":"A hemodialysis machine displays a persistently low dialysate conductivity alarm (11.2 mS/cm; normal ~13.5-14.5 mS/cm) and automatically enters bypass mode. If a technician improperly overrides or bypasses this alarm while dialyzing a patient with severely hypo-osmolar dialysate, what life-threatening complication results?","o":["Purely metabolic alkalosis without red blood cell injury","Acute dialysate-induced hypernatremia with thirst and hyperosmolar coma","Rapid spontaneous clotting of the arterial bloodline only","Massive acute intravascular hemolysis with hyperkalemia and cerebral edema"],"a":3,"e":"Dialysate conductivity reflects total dissolved electrolytes, predominantly sodium (~135-142 mEq/L). Low conductivity (\u003c12.5 mS/cm) indicates hypo-osmolar dialysate (e.g., acid/bicarbonate concentrate exhausted or proportioning pump failure). Water shifts rapidly into red blood cells and brain cells, causing catastrophic intravascular hemolysis, lethal hyperkalemia, and cerebral edema.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01415","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Hemodialysis Procedure and Complications","topic":"Blood Leak Detector Alarm","q":"During mid-dialysis, the machine\u0027s optical blood leak detector triggers an alarm and stops the blood pump, though the effluent dialysate line looks clear to the naked eye. What is the correct verification step before deciding whether to discard the extracorporeal blood circuit?","o":["Sample the spent dialysate effluent from the dialyzer outlet using a urine blood/hemoglobin dipstick (Hemastix) and inspect for microbubbles or optical sensor fouling","Increase the blood pump speed to 450 mL/min to clear the optical sensor","Switch off the blood leak detector for the remainder of the session","Immediately return the blood to the patient without testing because clear effluent proves a false alarm"],"a":0,"e":"Microscopic hollow-fiber ruptures can leak small amounts of blood into the dialysate before pink discoloration is visible. Testing effluent with a hemoglobin reagent strip confirms or excludes a true blood leak; if positive, dialysis must be stopped and the blood in the circuit MUST NOT be returned due to bacterial/endotoxin contamination from non-sterile dialysate.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01416","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Principles and Adequacy","topic":"Urea Reduction Ratio (URR)","q":"A chronic hemodialysis patient has a pre-dialysis BUN of 80 mg/dL and a properly drawn post-dialysis BUN of 24 mg/dL. What is the Urea Reduction Ratio (URR), and does it meet the minimum KDOQI adequacy benchmark (\u003e=65%)?","o":["URR = 56%; borderline inadequate","URR = 70%; meets minimum KDOQI adequacy benchmark","URR = 30%; inadequate dialysis","URR = 85%; exceeds standard target"],"a":1,"e":"Urea Reduction Ratio (URR) = [(Pre-BUN - Post-BUN) / Pre-BUN] x 100 = [(80 - 24) / 80] x 100 = (56 / 80) x 100 = 70%. KDOQI guidelines recommend a minimum delivered URR of \u003e=65% (corresponding to single-pool Kt/V \u003e=1.2) for thrice-weekly hemodialysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01417","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Peritoneal Dialysis","topic":"PD-Associated Peritonitis","q":"A continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD) patient presents with abdominal pain and a cloudy drained dialysate bag. Under ISPD guidelines, what peritoneal effluent white blood cell count threshold confirms PD-associated peritonitis?","o":["Isolated red blood cell count \u003e10/microL without leukocytes","WBC count \u003e5,000 cells/microL with \u003e90% lymphocytes","WBC count \u003e100 cells/microL with \u003e50% polymorphonuclear neutrophils (PMNs) after \u003e=2 hours dwell","Any effluent glucose \u003c50 mg/dL regardless of WBC count"],"a":2,"e":"Per International Society for Peritoneal Dialysis (ISPD) guidelines, PD peritonitis is diagnosed when at least two of three criteria are present: (1) clinical features (abdominal pain and/or cloudy effluent); (2) effluent WBC \u003e100/microL (0.1 x 10^9/L) with \u003e50% PMNs after a dwell of \u003e=2 hours; (3) positive dialysate culture/Gram stain.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01418","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Anticoagulation in Dialysis","topic":"Heparin-Free Dialysis and Protamine Reversal","q":"A patient with acute upper gastrointestinal bleeding requires urgent 3-hour hemodialysis. Which circuit modification helps prevent dialyzer clotting during heparin-free hemodialysis?","o":["Replace the dialyzer every 20 minutes prophylactically without saline flushing","Reduce blood flow rate to 100 mL/min to minimize shear stress","Administer double-dose unfractionated heparin bolus into the venous drip chamber","Maintain high blood flow rate (300-400 mL/min as tolerated) and perform periodic 100-200 mL normal saline flushes into the arterial pre-dialyzer port every 15-30 minutes (accounting for flush volume in UF)"],"a":3,"e":"During heparin-free dialysis for active bleeding or recent surgery, high blood pump speeds reduce transit time through the dialyzer and periodic saline flushes (e.g., 100-250 mL every 15-30 min) wash away nascent fibrin strands while allowing technician inspection of fiber darkness. Extra saline flush volume must be added to the ultrafiltration goal.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01419","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Hemodialysis Procedure and Complications","topic":"Intradialytic Hypotension (IDH)","q":"Two hours into hemodialysis with a high ultrafiltration rate (1,100 mL/hr), a patient develops yawning, diaphoresis, calf cramps, and a sudden blood pressure drop from 134/82 mmHg to 82/50 mmHg. What is the immediate priority sequence by the dialysis technician?","o":["Place the patient in Trendelenburg (legs elevated) position, turn ultrafiltration off (or to minimum), infuse a 100-200 mL normal saline bolus, and notify the nephrologist/nurse","Increase the ultrafiltration rate to finish the session faster","Administer IV furosemide 80 mg and keep the patient sitting bolt upright","Clamp both arterial and venous needles immediately without returning blood"],"a":0,"e":"Intradialytic hypotension (IDH) occurs when ultrafiltration exceeds plasma vascular refilling rate. Immediate management restores central venous return via Trendelenburg positioning (unless dyspneic), halts ultrafiltration, and expands intravascular volume with 100-250 mL isotonic normal saline (avoiding routine hypertonic saline/albumin unless prescribed), followed by dry-weight/dialysate temperature reassessment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01420","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Hemodialysis Procedure and Complications","topic":"Type A Anaphylactoid Dialyzer Reaction","q":"Within 3 minutes of initiating hemodialysis on a new patient taking enalapril (ACE inhibitor), using a newly primed polyacrylonitrile (AN69) or ethylene-oxide-sterilized dialyzer, the patient develops acute wheezing, facial angioedema, laryngeal stridor, and hypotension. What critical circuit action must accompany emergency epinephrine/airway care?","o":["Stop the blood pump immediately, clamp both lines, and DO NOT return the blood in the extracorporeal circuit to the patient","Flush the entire dialyzer blood volume rapidly back into the patient using 500 mL saline","Continue dialysis at a slower blood flow rate of 150 mL/min","Switch the dialysate bath to zero-potassium without stopping the pump"],"a":0,"e":"A Type A (anaphylactoid/hypersensitivity) dialyzer reaction typically strikes within the first 5-20 minutes due to residual ethylene oxide sensitization or bradykinin accumulation on negatively charged AN69 membranes in patients on ACE inhibitors. Returning the blood in the circuit infuses a massive bolus of sensitized allergen/bradykinin and can cause fatal anaphylactic arrest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01421","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysate Prescription and Chemistry","topic":"Dialysate Potassium Concentration","q":"Why do modern nephrology safety guidelines strongly discourage using a 0 mEq/L (\u0027zero-K\u0027) or 1 mEq/L potassium dialysate bath routinely—especially in elderly cardiac patients or those taking digoxin?","o":["Rapidly steep serum-to-dialysate potassium gradients trigger sudden hypokalemic membrane instability, QT prolongation, and fatal ventricular arrhythmias or digoxin toxicity","Potassium cannot cross high-flux polysulfone membranes unless dialysate K+ is 0 mEq/L","Zero-potassium dialysate solidifies bicarbonate precipitate inside the dialyzer","Zero-potassium dialysate causes acute hyperkalemic sine-wave arrest"],"a":0,"e":"Using 0-1 mEq/L K+ baths creates a massive diffusive potassium gradient during the first hour of dialysis, causing abrupt extracellular potassium depletion, myocardial repolarization heterogeneity, QTc prolongation, and sudden cardiac death (especially in patients on digoxin, where hypokalemia potentiates Na+/K+-ATPase inhibition). Standard baths use 2.0-3.0 mEq/L K+.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01422","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Vascular Access Management","topic":"Venous Needle Dislodgement (VND)","q":"Why might a hemodialysis machine\u0027s venous pressure monitor FAIL to alarm immediately if the venous access needle accidentally pulls completely out of a patient\u0027s AV fistula at a blood flow rate of 350 mL/min?","o":["Because high flow resistance through the narrow 15G/16G venous needle tip itself sustains positive back-pressure inside the drip chamber above a widely set lower alarm limit, allowing exsanguination within minutes unless the access site is kept uncovered and secured","Because venous pressure monitors only measure dialysate temperature","Because arterial pressure automatically stops the blood pump when a venous needle dislodges","Because blood instantly clots inside the dislodged needle within 2 seconds"],"a":0,"e":"Much of the positive venous pressure measured at the venous drip chamber arises from hydraulic resistance across the narrow needle lumen rather than intra-fistula venous pressure (~15-30 mmHg). If the lower venous pressure alarm limit is set loosely (e.g., 40-50 mmHg below baseline), dislodgement causes only a small pressure drop (~20 mmHg) that fails to trigger the alarm while the pump ejects 350 mL/min onto the floor. Hence, keeping the access uncovered (never buried under blankets), butterfly taping, and strict narrow lower alarm windows (within 10-20 mmHg) are vital.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01423","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialyzer Reprocessing","topic":"Total Cell Volume (TCV) Test","q":"When reprocessing a hollow-fiber dialyzer for reuse in the same Hepatitis B-negative patient, what minimum percentage of the original baseline Total Cell Volume (TCV / fiber bundle volume) must remain on automated or manual volume testing for the dialyzer to pass clearance validation?","o":["Total cell volume does not affect solute clearance","\u003e= 80% of original baseline TCV (and intact pressure-hold leak test)","10% of original baseline TCV","\u003e= 50% of original baseline TCV"],"a":1,"e":"Clotted hollow fibers reduce effective membrane surface area and small-solute clearance. AAMI/KDOQI reuse standards require that a reprocessed dialyzer retain at least 80% of its original baseline Total Cell Volume (TCV, correlating with \u003c10% loss of urea clearance) and pass an air/fluid pressure-holding membrane integrity test before germicide filling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01424","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Hemodialysis Procedure and Complications","topic":"Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome (DDS)","q":"A newly diagnosed end-stage kidney disease patient with baseline BUN 185 mg/dL develops headache, nausea, restlessness, and a generalized tonic-clonic seizure near the end of an aggressive first 4-hour hemodialysis session using a large high-flux dialyzer at Qb 400 mL/min. What complication occurred and how could it have been prevented?","o":["Acute aluminum toxicity encephalopathy; prevent by doubling dialysate calcium","Purely benign first-use anxiety; treat by increasing Qb to 500 mL/min","Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome (acute cerebral edema from rapid extracellular urea clearance); prevent with short initial sessions (2 hours), lower blood flow (Qb 150-200 mL/min), and smaller dialyzer surface area","Hyperkalemic cardiac standstill; prevent by extending the first session to 8 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Because urea crosses the blood-brain barrier more slowly than across the dialyzer membrane, overly aggressive initial hemodialysis in severely uremic patients creates an osmotic gradient pulling water into brain parenchyma (Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome). Prevention relies on gentle initiation: short duration (~2 hrs), low Qb (150-200 mL/min), smaller low-efficiency dialyzer, and targeting \u003c=30-40% urea reduction initially.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01425","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Water Treatment","topic":"AAMI Bacterial and Endotoxin Limits","q":"Under current AAMI/ISO 23500 standards for standard hemodialysis water, what are the maximum allowable limits and action levels for heterotrophic bacterial colony-forming units (CFU/mL) and bacterial endotoxins (EU/mL)?","o":["Sterility is never tested if the water looks clear to the naked eye","Bacteria: max 1,000 CFU/mL; Endotoxin: max 10 EU/mL","Bacteria: max 10,000 CFU/mL; Endotoxin: max 50 EU/mL","Bacteria: max \u003c100 CFU/mL (action level 50 CFU/mL); Endotoxin: max \u003c0.25 EU/mL (action level 0.125 EU/mL)"],"a":3,"e":"Because each hemodialysis patient is exposed to ~120-180 liters of dialysate across a semipermeable membrane per session, strict AAMI/ISO 23500 microbiological standards mandate \u003c100 CFU/mL (with disinfection triggered at the 50 CFU/mL action level) and \u003c0.25 EU/mL endotoxin (action level 0.125 EU/mL) via Limulus Amebocyte Lysate (LAL) testing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01426","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Vascular Access Management","topic":"Look, Listen, Feel Assessment","q":"During pre-cannulation \u0027Look, Listen, Feel\u0027 assessment of a forearm radiocephalic AV fistula, the technician palpates a strong, forceful, hyper-pulsatile \u0027water-hammer\u0027 pulse with loss of the continuous diastolic thrill and hears a high-pitched whistling systolic-only bruit. What access pathology does this indicate?","o":["Venous outflow stenosis downstream from the examined segment (impending fistula thrombosis)","Complete total thrombosis with zero arterial inflow","Superior vena cava dilation with low pressure","Completely normal, well-matured AV fistula ready for aggressive cannulation"],"a":0,"e":"A healthy mature AV fistula exhibits a soft, easily compressible vessel with a continuous systolic-diastolic thrill (\u0027purring\u0027) and low-pitched continuous bruit due to low downstream venous resistance. A downstream venous outflow stenosis blocks drainage, creating high intra-access pressure characterized by a bounding hyper-pulsatile (\u0027water-hammer\u0027) pulse, loss of diastolic thrill, and high-pitched systolic bruit, warranting urgent fistulogram/angioplasty.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01427","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysate Proportioning and Chemistry","topic":"Two-Concentrate Proportioning Chemistry","q":"Why are hemodialysis concentrates supplied in TWO separate containers—Acid concentrate (A) and Bicarbonate concentrate (B)—that are diluted with RO water separately inside the dialysis machine right before entering the dialyzer?","o":["Combining Part A and Part B before dilution creates explosive chlorine gas","Mixing concentrated calcium and magnesium salts (in Part A) directly with concentrated sodium bicarbonate (in Part B) causes immediate precipitation of insoluble calcium carbonate and magnesium carbonate scale","Part B contains concentrated heparin that degrades if mixed with acetic acid","Single-jug concentrates freeze at room temperature unless split into two jugs"],"a":1,"e":"Acid concentrate (Part A) contains NaCl, KCl, CaCl2, MgCl2, dextrose, and acetic/citric acid (low pH), while Part B contains NaHCO3. At high concentrate concentrations and alkaline pH, Ca2+ and Mg2+ react with HCO3- to precipitate solid white CaCO3/MgCO3 crystals that clog proportioning pumps and dialyzer fibers; diluting each separately in RO water keeps concentrations below solubility limits.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01428","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Adequacy and Monitoring","topic":"Access Recirculation Calculation","q":"Suspecting accidental needle reversal or fistula stenosis in a patient with falling Kt/V, the technician performs a two-needle recirculation study using the low-flow/stop-flow peripheral BUN method: Arterial line BUN (A) = 40 mg/dL, Venous line BUN (V) = 10 mg/dL, and Systemic Peripheral BUN (S) = 50 mg/dL. What is the percentage of access recirculation [(S - A) / (S - V) x 100]?","o":["0% access recirculation (normal)","50% access recirculation","25% access recirculation (severely abnormal; investigate for reversed needles or stenosis)","10% access recirculation (acceptable borderline)"],"a":2,"e":"Access Recirculation (%) = [(S - A) / (S - V)] x 100 = [(50 - 40) / (50 - 10)] x 100 = (10 / 40) x 100 = 25%. Normally, a non-stenotic AV fistula with properly oriented and spaced needles has \u003c5-10% recirculation; 25% indicates significant mixing of freshly dialyzed venous blood directly back into the arterial needle (often due to reversed lines or severe outflow stenosis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01429","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Modalities and Transport Physics","topic":"Hemodialysis vs Hemofiltration (CVVH)","q":"In Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy (CRRT) in the ICU, how does Continuous Venovenous Hemofiltration (CVVH) clear solutes differently from standard diffusive Hemodialysis (HD), and which solute size benefits most from CVVH?","o":["CVVH uses purely passive diffusion without any ultrafiltration or replacement fluid","Hemodialysis clears large proteins better than small urea molecules via osmosis","CVVH removes only red blood cells and platelets while retaining urea","CVVH uses convection (\u0027solvent drag\u0027 driven by high ultrafiltration transmembrane pressure with sterile replacement fluid), achieving superior clearance of middle-molecular-weight solutes (e.g., beta-2 microglobulin, cytokines)"],"a":3,"e":"Diffusion (concentration-gradient movement across a semipermeable membrane in standard HD/CVVHD) excels at removing small molecules (urea 60 Da, creatinine, K+), whose rapid Brownian motion slows dramatically as molecular weight rises. Convection (solvent drag across high-flux membranes in CVVH) sweeps water and dissolved solutes up to the membrane pore cutoff together, markedly enhancing middle-molecule clearance (500-40,000 Da) while requiring sterile pre- or post-filter replacement fluid.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01430","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Vascular Access Infection Control","topic":"Catheter-Related Bloodstream Infection (CRBSI) Prevention","q":"When connecting a patient with a right internal jugular tunneled cuffed hemodialysis catheter, which antiseptic protocol and physical barrier precaution are mandatory to prevent catheter-related bloodstream infection (CRBSI)?","o":["Both technician and patient wear surgical masks, use sterile gloves, scrub catheter hubs/caps vigorously with \u003e0.5% (2%) chlorhexidine in 70% isopropyl alcohol (or povidone-iodine if allergic) and allow complete drying before connecting","Open catheter caps bare-handed and wipe hubs quickly with dry cotton gauze","Apply petroleum jelly ointment into the catheter lumen after every session","Leave catheter clamps open while removing end-caps to check back-bleeding"],"a":0,"e":"CRBSI is a leading cause of morbidity in catheter-dependent hemodialysis patients. CDC/KDOQI Core Interventions mandate masks for staff and patient (preventing nasopharyngeal Staphylococcal droplet seeding), sterile gloves, \u0027scrub-the-hub\u0027 with 2% chlorhexidine gluconate in 70% alcohol (allowing full dry contact time), and keeping clamps closed whenever caps are off to prevent fatal air embolism.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01431","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Adequacy and Sampling","topic":"Slow-Flow / Stop-Pump Technique","q":"At the completion of a 4-hour hemodialysis session, why must the dialysis technician follow the KDOQI \u0027slow-flow / stop-pump\u0027 protocol (turning dialysate flow/UF off, slowing blood pump to 50-100 mL/min for 15 seconds, then sampling from the arterial bloodline port) before drawing the post-dialysis BUN specimen?","o":["To clear access-recirculated dialyzed blood from the arterial needle tubing so systemic arterial blood is sampled without falsely depressing post-BUN and overestimating Kt/V","To draw blood from the venous return port after the dialyzer","To wait 4 hours for total body urea rebound before disconnecting the patient","To deliberately dilute the sample with residual priming saline"],"a":0,"e":"If blood is drawn from the arterial port at full 350-400 mL/min pump speed, any AV access recirculation mixes freshly dialyzed venous blood into the arterial sample—artificially lowering post-BUN and falsely inflating Kt/V and URR. Slowing Qb to 50-100 mL/min for 15 seconds (or stopping the pump for 20 s) eliminates access recirculation while limiting cardiopulmonary rebound errors (waiting 15-30 min is impractical routinely).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01432","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysate Monitoring and Safety","topic":"Overheated vs Cool Dialysate","q":"If a dialysis machine\u0027s heating thermostat fails and its high-temperature safety thermistor fails to trigger bypass, exposing blood inside the dialyzer to dialysate at 45 degrees C, what acute life-threatening complication occurs?","o":["Purely isolated hypoglycemia without red cell injury","Severe thermal red blood cell hemolysis (delayed or acute RBC fragmentation, port-wine bloodlines, and lethal hyperkalemia)","Immediate frostbite clotting of the arterial fistula needle","Rapid improvement in hemodynamic blood pressure stability"],"a":1,"e":"Red blood cell membranes denature and fragment rapidly at temperatures \u003e42-43 degrees C (normal dialysate range 35.0-37.5 degrees C; high-temp alarm triggers bypass around 39-41 degrees C). Overheated dialysate causes catastrophic intravascular hemolysis and hyperkalemia (sometimes continuing for hours after exposure due to heat-damaged RBCs), requiring immediate cessation without returning blood. Conversely, mildly cool dialysate (35.5 C) helps stabilize BP in hypotension-prone patients.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01433","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Peritoneal Dialysis","topic":"Dextrose Concentrations vs Icodextrin","q":"Why is 7.5% Icodextrin (a large-molecular-weight glucose polymer) superior to standard 1.5% dextrose PD solution for achieving sustained net ultrafiltration during a long 8-to-12-hour nocturnal CAPD dwell?","o":["Because icodextrin crosses blood vessels 10 times faster than glucose","Because icodextrin turns dialysate into a solid gel inside the abdomen","Because icodextrin is slowly absorbed primarily via peritoneal lymphatics rather than rapidly diffusing across peritoneal capillaries, preserving a sustained colloid osmotic gradient throughout long dwells","Because icodextrin contains 50% ethanol to dissolve peritoneal fibrin"],"a":2,"e":"Standard dextrose PD solutions (1.5%, 2.5%, 4.25%) rely on crystalloid osmosis; during long dwells (especially in high/fast peritoneal transporters), small glucose molecules rapidly absorb across capillaries, dissipating the osmotic gradient and causing fluid reabsorption. Large icodextrin polymers remain in the peritoneal cavity and draw fluid steadily by colloid osmosis over 8-16 hours, while reducing systemic glucose load.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01434","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Hemodialysis Procedure and Complications","topic":"Severe Intradialytic Muscle Cramps","q":"During the final 30 minutes of hemodialysis, a patient repeatedly develops painful calf and foot muscle cramps every session, often accompanied by falling blood pressure near the end of treatment. Beyond immediate UF pause and isotonic normal saline bolus for acute relief, what underlying prescription issue should the technician flag to the nephrologist?","o":["The dialysate temperature is too cold at 36 degrees C and should be raised to 42 degrees C","The patient needs zero-potassium dialysate immediately","The blood pump speed is too low and must be raised to 600 mL/min","The prescribed dry weight may be set too low (or ultrafiltration rate/interdialytic weight gain is excessive), requiring dry-weight upward reassessment and sodium/fluid restriction counseling"],"a":3,"e":"Recurrent late-session muscle cramps alongside intradialytic hypotension strongly suggest excessive intravascular volume depletion below the patient\u0027s true physiological dry weight (often as lean body mass increases with better nutrition) or overly steep ultrafiltration rates (\u003e10-13 mL/kg/hr). Stopping/reducing UF and giving 100 mL 0.9% NaCl relieves acute spasm, while clinical dry-weight re-evaluation prevents recurrence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01435","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Principles \u0026 Mechanics of Hemodialysis","topic":"Ultrafiltration Rate Calculation","q":"A chronic hemodialysis patient has a prescribed dry weight of 60.0 kg and arrives for a 4-hour dialysis session weighing 62.8 kg. Including 200 mL (0.2 kg) for expected IV rinseback fluid, what is the total ultrafiltration (UF) goal and the resulting hourly UF rate?","o":["3,000 mL total UF; 750 mL/hour (12.5 mL/kg/hr)","3,200 mL total UF; 800 mL/hour (13.3 mL/kg/hr)","2,800 mL total UF; 1,400 mL/hour (23.3 mL/kg/hr)","2,600 mL total UF; 650 mL/hour (10.8 mL/kg/hr)"],"a":0,"e":"Interdialytic weight gain = 62.8 - 60.0 = 2.8 kg (2,800 mL). Adding 200 mL rinseback fluid gives a total UF goal of 2,800 + 200 = 3,000 mL. Divided over 4 hours, the hourly UF rate = 3,000 / 4 = 750 mL/hr, which equals 750 / 60 = 12.5 mL/kg/hr (within the generally recommended \u003c=10–13 mL/kg/hr safety threshold).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01436","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Complications \u0026 Emergencies","topic":"Venous Air Embolism","q":"During hemodialysis, the venous air detector alarm triggers and you observe foam/air bubbles entering the venous return line as the patient suddenly develops coughing, dyspnea, chest tightness, and cyanosis. What is the correct immediate sequence of actions?","o":["Increase blood pump speed to flush the air rapidly through the lungs and sit the patient upright","Clamp the venous blood line, stop the blood pump, place patient in left lateral decubitus and Trendelenburg (head-down) position, administer 100% oxygen, and call the physician","Administer 500 mL normal saline rapidly through the air-filled venous line before stopping the pump","Turn off the air detector alarm, clamp only the arterial line, and place patient in right lateral semi-Fowler position"],"a":1,"e":"In venous air embolism, immediately clamping the venous line and stopping the blood pump prevents further air entry. Placing the patient in left lateral decubitus with head down (Durant maneuver) traps air in the apex of the right ventricle away from the pulmonary artery outflow tract, while 100% O2 aids nitrogen washout and oxygenation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01437","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Water Treatment \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"Chloramine Testing \u0026 Microbiological Standards","q":"Under AAMI/KDOQI hemodialysis water standards, why must total chlorine/chloramines be tested after the primary carbon tank BEFORE every dialysis shift, and what is the maximum allowable chloramine concentration in product water?","o":["Chloramines cause metabolic alkalosis; maximum allowable limit is \u003c 2.0 mg/L (ppm)","Chloramines cause dialyzer membrane calcification; maximum allowable limit is \u003c 5.0 mg/L (ppm)","Chloramines cross the dialyzer membrane and cause acute hemolytic anemia and methemoglobinemia; maximum allowable limit is \u003c 0.1 mg/L (ppm)","Chloramines raise dialysate potassium levels; testing is required only once per year"],"a":2,"e":"Chloramines are small molecules that freely cross the semipermeable dialyzer membrane, oxidizing hemoglobin and causing severe acute hemolytic anemia and methemoglobinemia. Two granular activated carbon beds in series (worker and polisher) remove chlorine/chloramines; water is tested before every shift (and every 4 hours) to ensure chloramines remain \u003c 0.1 mg/L (free chlorine \u003c 0.5 mg/L).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01438","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Vascular Access Management","topic":"Pre-Cannulation Assessment \u0026 Needle Placement","q":"At an OSPE station for AV fistula cannulation, you assess the patient’s mature radiocephalic AVF. Which pre-cannulation and needle-placement practice follows KDOQI standards?","o":["Inspect for redness/swelling, palpate a continuous thrill, auscultate a low-pitched continuous bruit; insert venous needle pointing toward the heart and space arterial and venous needle tips \u003e= 5 cm apart using rope-ladder site rotation","Cannulate the exact same pinhole scar every session at a 90-degree angle with both needles 1 cm apart","Point the venous needle distally toward the hand and place the arterial needle directly on top of the surgical anastomosis","Apply a tight blood pressure cuff on the fistula arm throughout dialysis to enhance venous return"],"a":0,"e":"Every AVF must undergo \"Look, Listen, Feel\" assessment before cannulation (a bounding pulse without thrill or a high-pitched systolic-only bruit suggests outflow stenosis). Using rope-ladder rotation prevents pseudoaneurysms; keeping needle tips \u003e=5 cm apart (and \u003e=3–5 cm from the anastomosis) with the venous needle antegrade (toward heart) prevents access recirculation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01439","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Complications \u0026 Emergencies","topic":"Intradialytic Hypotension (IDH) Management","q":"Two hours into a 4-hour hemodialysis session, a diabetic patient complains of yawning, lightheadedness, and calf cramps; BP has dropped from 136/82 mmHg to 84/50 mmHg with HR 104/min. What is the immediate appropriate bedside intervention?","o":["Immediately return blood and inject 50 mL of 10% potassium chloride intravenously","Increase ultrafiltration rate to finish the session earlier and elevate the head of the bed to 75 degrees","Administer 40 mg IV furosemide and increase dialysate temperature to 38.5°C","Reduce or pause ultrafiltration (set UF to minimum), place patient in Trendelenburg position if no respiratory distress, administer a 100–250 mL bolus of 0.9% normal saline, and reassess vital signs"],"a":3,"e":"Intradialytic hypotension occurs when UF exceeds plasma refilling rate. Stopping/minimizing UF immediately halts further intravascular volume removal, Trendelenburg positioning augments venous return, and a small 100–250 mL isotonic saline bolus restores preload. Dialysate temperature may also be lowered (cool dialysate 35.5–36°C) to improve vascular tone.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01440","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Infection Control \u0026 Dialyzer Reprocessing","topic":"Dialyzer Reuse \u0026 HBsAg Isolation","q":"In a dialysis unit practicing dialyzer reprocessing, a patient tests positive for Hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg), while a Hepatitis-negative patient’s reprocessed hollow-fiber dialyzer shows a Total Cell Volume (TCV) of 74% of baseline. How must the technician manage both situations?","o":["Strictly prohibit dialyzer reuse and use a dedicated isolation room/machine for the HBsAg-positive patient; discard the reprocessed dialyzer with TCV 74% because TCV must be \u003e= 80% of baseline","Reuse the HBsAg-positive patient’s dialyzer after double peracetic acid soaking; use the 74% TCV dialyzer by increasing blood flow by 20%","Share the HBsAg-positive patient’s machine with HCV-positive patients after 10-minute water rinse; discard only when TCV falls below 50%","Reuse both dialyzers up to a fixed 15 uses regardless of TCV or serology status"],"a":0,"e":"Because Hepatitis B virus is highly infectious and environmentally stable, guidelines prohibit dialyzer reuse for HBsAg-positive patients and mandate dedicated machines, supplies, and isolation areas. For eligible patients, AAMI standards require a minimum Total Cell Volume (fiber bundle volume) \u003e= 80% of original priming volume and a passed pressure leak test before reuse.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01441","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Complications \u0026 Emergencies","topic":"Type A Dialyzer Reaction","q":"Within 7 minutes of starting dialysis with a new ethylene-oxide-sterilized dialyzer, a patient develops pruritus, generalized urticaria, wheezing, laryngeal tightness, and severe hypotension. What is the critical circuit-handling rule when terminating dialysis in this Type A reaction?","o":["Stop the blood pump immediately, clamp both arterial and venous lines, and DO NOT return the blood in the extracorporeal circuit to the patient; administer epinephrine, oxygen, and supportive care","Rinse all blood from the dialyzer back into the patient using 300 mL saline so hemoglobin is not lost","Continue dialysis at a slower blood flow of 100 mL/min and give oral paracetamol","Increase dialysate sodium to 150 mEq/L while keeping the same dialyzer running"],"a":0,"e":"Type A (hypersensitivity/anaphylactic) dialyzer reactions are IgE/complement-mediated reactions (classic causes: residual ethylene oxide, AN69 membranes with ACE inhibitors, contaminated dialyzers). Because the blood inside the tubing and dialyzer contains high concentrations of the allergen/activated mediators, returning it can trigger fatal anaphylactic shock. Clamp lines and discard the blood-filled circuit immediately.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01442","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Principles \u0026 Mechanics of Hemodialysis","topic":"Kt/V \u0026 Post-Dialysis BUN Sampling","q":"To calculate delivered single-pool Kt/V (KDOQI minimum target \u003e= 1.2 per session for thrice-weekly hemodialysis) without false elevation caused by access recirculation, how must the technician draw the post-dialysis blood urea nitrogen (BUN) sample?","o":["Stop dialysate flow (or set to bypass), turn UF off, reduce blood pump speed to 50–100 mL/min for 15 seconds, then draw blood from the arterial sampling port before rinsing back","Draw blood 15 minutes after completely rinsing back the circuit with 300 mL normal saline","Draw blood from the venous port while the blood pump continues running at 400 mL/min","Draw blood from the arterial port immediately after injecting a heparin bolus at 350 mL/min pump speed"],"a":0,"e":"The KDOQI slow-flow/stop-pump method stops dialysate clearance and slows the blood pump to 50–100 mL/min for 15 seconds, clearing recirculated dialyzed blood from the arterial needle segment so the arterial port sample reflects true systemic arterial BUN (yielding accurate spKt/V \u003e= 1.2, recommended target 1.4).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01443","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Anticoagulation \u0026 Special Dialysis Protocols","topic":"Saline Flushing Protocol","q":"A patient with an acute gastrointestinal bleed requires urgent hemodialysis. The nephrologist orders heparin-free dialysis. Which combination of circuit preparation and intradialytic monitoring is standard for heparin-free hemodialysis?","o":["Slow blood flow down to 120 mL/min to prevent shear stress and avoid any saline flushes","Flush out heparinized priming saline with plain normal saline before connecting, maintain brisk blood flow (300–350 mL/min as tolerated), flush the dialyzer with 100–150 mL normal saline every 30 minutes (adding flush volume to UF target), and inspect drip chambers for clotting","Use calcium-rich dialysate and warm the venous drip chamber to 40°C","Administer a standard 5,000 IU systemic heparin bolus at the start and omit maintenance infusion"],"a":1,"e":"In heparin-free dialysis, any heparin used during priming is flushed to waste before patient connection. High blood flow (300–350 mL/min) minimizes stasis, periodic 100–150 mL normal saline flushes every 15–30 minutes clear fibrin/platelets and allow visual inspection of fibers/chambers, and total flush volume must be added to the UF goal to keep fluid balance neutral.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01444","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Water Treatment","topic":"Water Softener Resin Regeneration","q":"In a hemodialysis water treatment plant, why is the sodium-ion-exchange Water Softener positioned UPSTREAM of the Reverse Osmosis (RO) membranes, and how is exhausted softener resin regenerated?","o":["It removes chlorine and chloramines; regenerated using sulfuric acid","It adds calcium and magnesium into RO product water to harden it","It exchanges hard calcium and magnesium ions for sodium ions to prevent irreversible mineral scale fouling on the delicate RO membranes; regenerated automatically using concentrated sodium chloride (brine) solution","It sterilizes endotoxins; regenerated using boiling steam"],"a":2,"e":"Municipal feed water contains dissolved calcium and magnesium (\u0027hardness\u0027) that precipitate as hard mineral scale on thin-film composite RO membranes, destroying product water flow. Water softener cation-exchange resin beads swap Ca2+ and Mg2+ for Na+ ions; during scheduled backwash/regeneration (typically overnight), concentrated NaCl brine flushes off bound Ca2+/Mg2+ and recharges the resin with sodium.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01445","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysate Preparation and Microbiology","topic":"Liquid Bicarbonate Bacterial Proliferation","q":"Why must pre-mixed liquid sodium bicarbonate dialysate concentrate containers NEVER be topped off and must any unused mixed liquid bicarbonate be discarded within 24 hours (whereas dry bicarbonate powder cartridges are preferred)?","o":["Unlike acidic Part A concentrate, neutral/alkaline liquid bicarbonate lacks antimicrobial acidity and rapidly supports proliferation of Gram-negative waterborne bacteria and endotoxins","Liquid bicarbonate turns into solid potassium chloride after 24 hours","Liquid bicarbonate explodes if exposed to room fluorescent lighting","Topping off bicarbonate dilutes RO water conductivity above 50 mS/cm"],"a":0,"e":"Acid concentrate (Part A) has a low pH (~2.5-3.0) that inhibits microbial growth, whereas bicarbonate concentrate (Part B, pH ~8.0) is an ideal medium for halophilic/waterborne Gram-negative bacteria (Pseudomonas, Stenotrophomonas) that generate pyrogenic endotoxins. Thus, AAMI standards require single-use dry bicarbonate cartridges (Bibag) or discarding unused liquid Part B within 24 hours with thorough container disinfection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01446","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Vascular Access Management","topic":"Catheter Lumen Locking Volume","q":"At the end of a hemodialysis session using a dual-lumen tunneled dialysis catheter whose printed priming volumes are 1.8 mL (arterial) and 2.0 mL (venous), why must the technician flush with 10 mL normal saline first and then instill EXACTLY 1.8 mL and 2.0 mL of heparin (or citrate) locking solution?","o":["Instilling half of the lumen volume leaves the distal catheter tip un-locked and prone to clot occlusion; exact printed priming volume should be used","Saline flushing is skipped so residual clotted blood mixes with heparin inside the catheter","Instilling 10 mL of concentrated heparin per lumen is safer to anticoagulate the patient overnight","Instilling more than the exact printed internal lumen volume pushes concentrated heparin directly out the catheter tip into the right atrium/systemic circulation, causing dangerous systemic anticoagulation and hemorrhage"],"a":3,"e":"Catheter locking solutions (e.g., 1,000-5,000 U/mL heparin or 4% trisodium citrate) are intended to fill only the internal catheter lumen between dialysis sessions. After briskly flushing both ports with 10 mL 0.9% saline (clearing blood cleanly), instilling the exact printed lumen priming volume (1.8 mL arterial, 2.0 mL venous) prevents systemic bolus spillover that could trigger GI/intracranial hemorrhage or hypocalcemic arrhythmias with high-concentration citrate.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01447","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Adequacy and Quality","topic":"Determinants of Delivered Kt/V","q":"In the dimensionless dialysis adequacy index spKt/V (where K = dialyzer urea clearance, t = treatment time, V = urea distribution volume), what is the minimum KDOQI delivered spKt/V target per session for thrice-weekly hemodialysis, and how does shortening a patient\u0027s prescribed 240-minute session by 45 minutes every visit affect adequacy?","o":["Minimum delivered spKt/V \u003e= 1.2 (prescribed target 1.4); cutting 45 minutes reduces \u0027t\u0027 directly by ~19%, frequently causing underdialysis (Kt/V \u003c 1.2), uremic malnutrition, and higher mortality","Minimum spKt/V \u003e= 5.0; blood flow rate has zero impact on K","Minimum spKt/V \u003e= 0.4; shortening time improves Kt/V by increasing V","Shortening time is automatically compensated by lowering blood pump speed to 150 mL/min"],"a":0,"e":"KDOQI Hemodialysis Adequacy guidelines recommend a minimum delivered single-pool Kt/V of 1.2 (prescribing 1.4 to provide a safety margin) for thrice-weekly dialysis. Because Kt/V scales linearly with duration (t) and clearance (K, driven by blood/dialysate flow and dialyzer KoA), early patient sign-offs or clotting downtime directly slash delivered Kt/V, increasing hospitalization and mortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01448","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Peritoneal Dialysis","topic":"CAPD Outflow Failure Troubleshooting","q":"A CAPD patient reports that fresh 2.0 L dialysate infuses easily by gravity, but only 400 mL drains out slowly (\u0027one-way outflow obstruction\u0027) without abdominal pain or cloudy effluent. What are the most common mechanical causes to check first?","o":["Acute bacterial peritonitis requiring immediate 28-day ICU ventilation","Severe fecal impaction/constipation compressing the catheter tip, fibrin clot strands obstructing side holes, or catheter tip migration out of the true pelvis (checked via abdominal X-ray)","Immediate total bilateral nephrectomy failure","Too low a blood pump speed on the RO machine"],"a":1,"e":"Good inflow with sluggish/incomplete outflow (\u0027ball-valve\u0027 one-way obstruction) in CAPD is most frequently caused by sigmoid colon fecal distension trapping/displacing the Tenckhoff catheter tip upward out of the pelvis, intraluminal fibrin strands, or omental wrapping. Assessing bowel habits (laxatives), inspecting for fibrin (intraperitoneal heparin/alteplase per physician), and obtaining an abdominal radiograph for catheter tip position are primary steps.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01449","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysate Prescription and Chemistry","topic":"Dialysate Calcium Concentration","q":"Under KDIGO Chronic Kidney Disease-Mineral and Bone Disorder (CKD-MBD) guidelines, what standard dialysate calcium concentration is recommended for most maintenance hemodialysis patients to avoid positive calcium loading and vascular calcification while preventing severe hypocalcemia?","o":["10.0 mEq/L (5.0 mmol/L)","0 mEq/L (zero-calcium bath routinely)","2.5 mEq/L (1.25 mmol/L) [range 2.5-3.0 mEq/L / 1.25-1.50 mmol/L]","5.0 mEq/L (2.5 mmol/L) routine bath"],"a":2,"e":"KDIGO CKD-MBD guidelines recommend using a dialysate calcium concentration between 1.25 and 1.50 mmol/L (2.5 to 3.0 mEq/L), most commonly 2.5 mEq/L (1.25 mmol/L), to maintain neutral calcium balance. Higher baths (3.5 mEq/L) promote metastatic vascular/valvular calcification and adynamic bone disease, whereas \u003c2.0 mEq/L risks intradialytic hypotension, tetany, and QTc prolongation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01450","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Water Treatment","topic":"RO Percent Salt Rejection Calculation","q":"During the daily morning water-plant log check, feed water entering the Reverse Osmosis (RO) unit measures Total Dissolved Solids (TDS) of 400 ppm, and RO product (permeate) water measures 12 ppm. What is the RO system\u0027s Percent Salt Rejection, and how does it compare with typical AAMI/manufacturer operational benchmarks (\u003e= 90-95%)?","o":["3.0% salt rejection (failed membrane)","88.0% salt rejection (requires immediate membrane replacement)","92.0% salt rejection","97.0% salt rejection (normal healthy RO membrane performance)"],"a":3,"e":"RO Percent Rejection = [(Feed TDS - Product TDS) / Feed TDS] x 100 = [(400 - 12) / 400] x 100 = (388 / 400) x 100 = 97.0%. Healthy thin-film composite RO membranes reject \u003e=95-98% of dissolved ions; a steady drop below 90% rejection warns of chemical oxidation (chlorine damage), scale fouling, or O-ring bypass leakage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01451","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysate Chemistry","topic":"Bicarbonate vs Acetate Dialysate","q":"Why has modern chronic hemodialysis almost universally replaced traditional acetate-buffered dialysate with physiological bicarbonate-buffered dialysate?","o":["Acetate accumulates faster than hepatic metabolism in high-efficiency dialysis, causing peripheral vasodilation, myocardial depression, intradialytic hypotension, hypoxemia, and nausea—all markedly reduced by bicarbonate","Acetate cannot cross standard low-flux dialyzer membranes","Acetate causes solid aluminum precipitation inside the patient\u0027s brain","Bicarbonate eliminates the need for RO water treatment completely"],"a":0,"e":"In older acetate dialysis, sodium acetate diffused into the blood to be metabolized by liver/muscle into bicarbonate. With high-efficiency dialyzers, acetate influx exceeded metabolic capacity, inducing nitric-oxide/adenosine-mediated systemic vasodilation, negative inotropy, hypotension, headache, and vomiting (\u0027acetate intolerance\u0027). Direct physiological bicarbonate dialysate corrects metabolic acidosis smoothly with superior hemodynamic stability.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01452","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Vascular Access Management","topic":"Synthetic Arteriovenous Graft (AVG) Care","q":"Compared with a native autogenous radiocephalic arteriovenous fistula (AVF), how do a synthetic polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) AV graft\u0027s maturation time, cannulation technique, and long-term complication risks differ?","o":["A PTFE graft takes 12 months to mature and has zero risk of thrombosis or infection","A standard PTFE AV graft can typically be cannulated earlier (~2-4 weeks once swelling resolves vs 6-12 weeks for AVF), requires verifying flow direction from arterial to venous end, and carries higher rates of thrombosis and hard-to-eradicate prosthetic infection","A PTFE graft requires no aseptic skin preparation because plastic cannot grow bacteria","A PTFE graft develops true endothelial remodeling identical to native veins within 24 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Because PTFE grafts do not rely on venous wall arterialization, standard grafts are typically ready for rope-ladder cannulation in 2-4 weeks (or within 24-72 hrs for early-cannulation multilayer grafts) after surgical edema subsides. However, synthetic grafts carry markedly higher pseudoaneurysm, venous-anastomotic intimal hyperplasia/thrombosis, and persistent bacterial biofilm infection rates than native AVFs—requiring strict surgical-grade skin antisepsis and careful mapping of arterial-to-venous loop flow.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01453","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Hemodialysis Procedure","topic":"Saline Rinseback Protocol","q":"At the end of a hemodialysis session, why MUST the extracorporeal blood circuit be returned to the patient using sterile 0.9% normal saline rinseback (typically 150-250 mL) rather than pushing blood back with air?","o":["Saline rinseback adds 10 grams of hemoglobin to each red cell","Air rinseback is slower than saline rinseback","Air rinseback carries an extreme risk of fatal venous air embolism if the clamp timing slips by even a fraction of a second, whereas saline rinseback safely displaces blood back into the patient","Air rinseback freezes red blood cells inside the dialyzer"],"a":2,"e":"Older obsolete \u0027air rinseback\u0027 techniques pushed circuit blood back using room air—causing numerous fatal venous air emboli whenever operators blinked or failed to clamp before air reached the venous needle. Modern AAMI/KDOQI safety mandates closed-system sterile isotonic saline rinseback (with active air-detector protection and careful UF accounting of rinseback volume).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01454","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Peritoneal Dialysis","topic":"Peritoneal Equilibration Test (PET)","q":"A peritoneal dialysis patient\u0027s standardized 4-hour Peritoneal Equilibration Test (PET) reveals a High (Fast) peritoneal membrane transporter profile (4-hour D/P creatinine \u003e 0.81 and rapid glucose absorption). Why does this patient achieve far better ultrafiltration on short frequent nocturnal Automated Peritoneal Dialysis (APD) cycler exchanges than on long 6-8 hour manual CAPD dwells?","o":["High transporters have completely impermeable peritoneal membranes that block water movement","APD cyclers pump blood directly across a synthetic hollow-fiber dialyzer","Manual CAPD exchanges cannot drain fluid unless a patient runs on a treadmill","High transporters equilibrate small solutes rapidly, so glucose diffuses out of the peritoneum fast—meaning short frequent APD dwells capture peak osmotic ultrafiltration before the osmotic gradient collapses and fluid gets reabsorbed"],"a":3,"e":"High/fast peritoneal transporters achieve rapid urea/creatinine equilibration (good small-solute clearance), but absorb dialysate dextrose rapidly across highly vascularized peritoneum. During long manual CAPD dwells, loss of the dextrose osmotic gradient causes net fluid reabsorption (UF failure). Automated Peritoneal Dialysis (APD) delivers multiple short 1-2 hr nocturnal cycler exchanges (plus icodextrin for the long daytime dwell if needed), maximizing UF.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01455","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Hemodialysis Pressure Monitoring","topic":"Excessive Negative Arterial Pressure","q":"When increasing the blood pump speed (Qb) to 400 mL/min on a 17G arterial fistula needle, the prepump arterial pressure monitor drops sharply to -290 mmHg and triggers a low-arterial-pressure alarm. Beyond checking for a kinked line or needle bevel against the vessel wall, why must sustained negative arterial pressures below -250 mmHg be corrected rather than ignored?","o":["Extreme negative suction pulls red blood cells violently (mechanical shear hemolysis), draws dissolved air/microbubbles out of solution (\u0027cavitation\u0027) or across tubing joints, and reduces true delivered blood flow below the pump display","Negative arterial pressure -290 mmHg improves dialyzer urea clearance by 50%","It causes positive hypertension inside the patient\u0027s brain","It cools the dialysate down to 20 degrees C"],"a":0,"e":"In a blood pump running at high speed through too small a needle (e.g., 17G at 400 mL/min) or partially occluded arterial fistula, severe negative prepump pressure (\u003c -200 to -250 mmHg) collapses the roller-pump tubing segment (meaning actual Qb is far lower than the console\u0027s RPM readout), subjects erythrocytes to shear hemolysis, and induces microbubble cavitation or air suction through loose connections.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01456","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Complications \u0026 Emergencies","topic":"Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome (DDS)","q":"A newly diagnosed chronic kidney disease patient with pre-dialysis BUN 175 mg/dL undergoes his very first hemodialysis session using a large high-flux dialyzer at 400 mL/min blood flow for 4 hours. Near hour 3, he develops severe throbbing headache, nausea, blurred vision, restlessness, and a generalized seizure. What complication occurred, and how is it prevented in subsequent initial sessions?","o":["Type B dialyzer reaction; prevent by giving 10,000 IU heparin bolus","Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome (acute cerebral edema from rapid extracellular urea clearance); prevent by prescribing short initial sessions (e.g., 2 hours), low blood flow (150–200 mL/min), and a smaller surface-area dialyzer","Chronic aluminum bone disease; prevent by increasing dialysate temperature to 39°C","Intradialytic hypertension; prevent by using 0 mEq/L potassium dialysate"],"a":1,"e":"In severely uremic patients undergoing rapid aggressive initial dialysis, urea clears quickly from plasma across the dialyzer but lags across the blood-brain barrier, creating an osmotic gradient that pulls water into brain cells (cerebral edema = DDS). Prevention uses gentle low-efficiency initial sessions (\"low and slow\": 2 hr, Qb 150–200 mL/min, small dialyzer, +/- IV mannitol).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01457","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Principles \u0026 Mechanics of Hemodialysis","topic":"Dialysate Conductivity Alarms \u0026 Hypotonic Hemolysis","q":"Thirty minutes into hemodialysis, the machine triggers a LOW dialysate conductivity alarm (reading 10.5 mS/cm; normal ~13.5–14.5 mS/cm) and automatically diverts dialysate to bypass. Why must the technician NEVER bypass or override a low-conductivity alarm?","o":["Low conductivity triggers venous air embolism via the RO membrane","Low conductivity indicates hyperkalemic dialysate that freezes the blood pump rollers","Low conductivity indicates hypotonic dialysate (excess water or empty concentrate jug), which causes massive osmotic water entry into RBCs resulting in acute fatal intravascular hemolysis and hyperkalemia","Low conductivity causes dialyzer membrane pores to expand and leak plasma proteins"],"a":2,"e":"Dialysate electrical conductivity directly reflects total ion concentration (primarily sodium). If an acid/bicarbonate concentrate container runs empty or proportioning fails, dialysate becomes hypotonic (low conductivity). Exposing blood to hypotonic dialysate causes immediate osmotic RBC swelling and lysis (\"port-wine\" blood in venous tubing), back/chest pain, and lethal hyperkalemic cardiac arrest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01458","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Principles \u0026 Mechanics of Hemodialysis","topic":"Urea Reduction Ratio (URR) Calculation","q":"A chronic hemodialysis patient’s pre-dialysis BUN is 80 mg/dL, and the correctly sampled post-dialysis BUN is 24 mg/dL. What is the delivered Urea Reduction Ratio (URR), and does it meet the KDOQI minimum adequacy target (\u003e= 65%)?","o":["URR = 30%; No, it fails adequacy targets","URR = 80%; Yes, it meets adequacy","URR = 56%; No, dialysis time must be doubled","URR = 70%; Yes, it exceeds the minimum KDOQI target of \u003e= 65%"],"a":3,"e":"URR (%) = [(Pre-BUN - Post-BUN) / Pre-BUN] × 100 = [(80 - 24) / 80] × 100 = (56 / 80) × 100 = 70%. KDOQI guidelines recommend a minimum URR of 65% (and target ~70%, corresponding roughly to spKt/V \u003e= 1.2–1.4) for thrice-weekly hemodialysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01459","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Vascular Access Management","topic":"Tunneled Catheter Asepsis \u0026 Locking Protocol","q":"At an OSCE station for initiating dialysis via a tunneled cuffed internal jugular catheter, which step is mandatory BEFORE connecting bloodlines and AFTER completing rinseback?","o":["Before connecting: scrub hubs with 2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol and allow to dry, then aspirate and discard the existing catheter lock (3–5 mL) from each lumen to confirm patency and remove concentrated heparin; After dialysis: flush with 10 mL saline and instill the exact printed lumen priming volume of lock solution","Instill 5 mL of concentrated heparin (5,000 IU/mL) into a 1.8 mL lumen to ensure the entire tip is coated","Leave catheter clamps open while uncapping hubs so air enters freely to equalize pressure","Inject the old heparin lock straight into the patient with a 10 mL flush before checking patency"],"a":0,"e":"Strict catheter care prevents CRBSI and systemic anticoagulation: mask both staff/patient, scrub hubs with CHG-alcohol, aspirate and discard the old lock + clots (never flush an old lock into the patient!), and at termination instill ONLY the exact lumen volume printed on the catheter limb (e.g., 1.8 mL arterial, 2.0 mL venous) because excess spills directly into the right atrium.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01460","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Principles \u0026 Mechanics of Hemodialysis","topic":"Arterial vs Venous Pressure Alarm Troubleshooting","q":"During hemodialysis at Qb 350 mL/min, the PRE-PUMP arterial pressure suddenly plunges to -290 mmHg (alarming very negative) while venous pressure drops slightly and the arterial tubing flattens/chatters. What is the most likely site and cause of this problem?","o":["Clotting inside the venous drip chamber filter distal to the dialyzer","Inflow obstruction between the patient’s arterial needle and the blood pump (e.g., arterial needle bevel against vessel wall, kinked arterial bloodline, patient bent arm, or systemic hypotension/access inflow stenosis)","High dialysate temperature triggering bypass","Infiltrated venous return needle causing a hematoma at the venous site"],"a":1,"e":"The pre-pump arterial pressure monitor sits between the patient access and the roller blood pump, so it reads negative suction pressure (normally -80 to -200 mmHg). An excessively negative reading (\u003c-250 mmHg) with line chatter means the pump is pulling against an inflow block upstream of the pump (needle bevel suctioned onto vessel wall, kinked arterial line, tight clamp, or hypotension).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01461","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Principles \u0026 Mechanics of Hemodialysis","topic":"Dual-Concentrate Bicarbonate Dialysate Chemistry","q":"Why does modern bicarbonate hemodialysis require TWO separate liquid concentrates (an Acid \"A\" jug and a Bicarbonate \"B\" jug) proportioned independently with RO water inside the machine rather than premixing them into one single concentrate jug?","o":["Separate jugs allow the machine to run without any RO water","Mixing them in one jug raises dialysate potassium to 10 mEq/L","In a single concentrated jug, calcium and magnesium ions from the Acid part would react with bicarbonate to form insoluble calcium/magnesium carbonate precipitate, clogging tubing and altering electrolytes","Bicarbonate concentrate is flammable when mixed with sodium chloride"],"a":2,"e":"Part A (Acid concentrate) contains NaCl, KCl, CaCl2, MgCl2, dextrose, and a small amount of acetic/citric acid; Part B contains NaHCO3 (and sometimes NaCl). If mixed at high concentration, Ca2+ and Mg2+ precipitate rapidly with HCO3- as chalky carbonate scale. Keeping them separate until diluted ~35–45× with RO water inside the proportioning chamber maintains solubility at physiological pH ~7.2–7.4.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01462","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Complications \u0026 Emergencies","topic":"Blood Leak Detector Alarm \u0026 Dialyzer Rupture","q":"Midway through dialysis, the optical blood leak detector alarms and stops the blood pump. Inspecting the spent dialysate outflow line reveals a faint pink tinge, and a hemoglobin dipstick test on the dialysate sample turns strongly positive. What is the mandatory action?","o":["Override the blood leak sensor and rinse all blood back into the patient using 400 mL saline","Increase transmembrane pressure (TMP) to +400 mmHg to push blood back across the broken fibers","Turn off dialysate flow and continue ultrafiltration only through the ruptured dialyzer","Confirm dialyzer membrane rupture; clamp the venous line immediately, DO NOT return the blood in the circuit to the patient (because non-sterile dialysate/endotoxins may have crossed into the blood compartment), discard the circuit, and restart with a new dialyzer if ordered"],"a":3,"e":"A confirmed true blood leak (red/pink effluent or positive blood dipstick on spent dialysate) signifies hollow-fiber rupture. Because dialysate is non-sterile (and may harbor bacteria/endotoxins), especially when TMP fluctuates, blood in the ruptured dialyzer may be contaminated—so blood must NOT be returned to the patient (clamp and discard circuit immediately).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01463","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Peritoneal Dialysis \u0026 Home Therapies","topic":"PD-Associated Peritonitis Diagnosis","q":"A Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis (CAPD) patient brings in a drained effluent bag that appears cloudy and complains of diffuse abdominal pain. Which peritoneal effluent laboratory finding confirms ISPD diagnostic criteria for peritonitis, and what must be done before starting antibiotics?","o":["Effluent WBC count \u003e 100 cells/µL with \u003e 50% polymorphonuclear neutrophils (PMNs); collect effluent for cell count, Gram stain, and inoculate 5–10 mL into aerobic/anaerobic blood culture bottles BEFORE intraperitoneal antibiotics","Effluent potassium \u003e 5 mEq/L; perform immediate surgical removal of the Tenckhoff catheter in all cases","Effluent WBC count \u003e 5 cells/µL with 100% lymphocytes; discard the cloudy bag immediately before sampling","Effluent glucose \u003e 500 mg/dL; wait 7 days for culture results before giving any antibiotics"],"a":0,"e":"Per International Society for Peritoneal Dialysis (ISPD) guidelines, PD peritonitis is diagnosed by \u003e=2 of: (1) abdominal pain/cloudy effluent, (2) effluent WBC \u003e 100/µL (after \u003e=2 hr dwell) with \u003e 50% PMNs, (3) positive effluent Gram stain or culture. Inoculating effluent directly into blood culture bottles maximizes pathogen yield before empiric intraperitoneal antibiotics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01464","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Principles \u0026 Mechanics of Hemodialysis","topic":"Diffusion vs Convection (Ultrafiltration)","q":"In comparing conventional hemodialysis (HD) versus hemodiafiltration (HDF) / hemofiltration, how do diffusion and convection differ in clearing small solutes (urea, potassium) versus middle molecules (beta-2 microglobulin)?","o":["Diffusion (solute movement down a concentration gradient) primarily clears small low-molecular-weight solutes like urea and potassium; Convection (\"solvent drag\" driven by transmembrane pressure ultrafiltration) enhances clearance of larger middle molecules like beta-2 microglobulin","Convection only clears sodium ions while diffusion clears albumin and IgG","Diffusion requires high transmembrane pressure (+500 mmHg) while convection occurs with zero ultrafiltration","Both diffusion and convection clear red blood cells across intact high-flux membranes"],"a":0,"e":"Diffusion moves small molecules rapidly along a concentration gradient (blood to dialysate), governed by blood/dialysate flow rates and membrane area, but diffusion velocity drops sharply for larger molecules. Convection drags solutes along with bulk water movement across high-flux pores under a hydrostatic pressure gradient (TMP), substantially boosting middle-molecule removal (e.g., beta-2 microglobulin in HDF).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01465","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Water Treatment","topic":"Mixed-Bed Deionizer Exhaustion Hazard","q":"When a mixed-bed Deionization (DI) resin tank is used as a polishing/backup unit in a dialysis water plant and becomes chemically exhausted without dual-stage resistivity alarms and automatic bypass, what acute hazard can occur in the product water?","o":["Exhausted DI resins automatically sterilize all bacterial endotoxins","Exhausted DI resins can suddenly release previously bound ions back into product water in concentrated boluses (\u0027dumping\u0027 toxic fluoride, aluminum, or extreme acidic/alkaline pH spikes causing hemolysis/encephalopathy)","Exhausted DI resins turn water into sterile isotonic 0.9% normal saline","Exhausted DI resins increase water hardness harmlessly without changing pH"],"a":1,"e":"Deionizer tanks use H+ cation and OH- anion resins to polish trace ions down to high resistivity (\u003e=1 megohm-cm). Once anion or cation capacity exhausts asymmetrically, incoming ions displace weakly bound fluoride or aluminum in concentrated spikes while swinging product pH violently (e.g., pH \u003c4 or \u003e10)—requiring continuous dual-tank (worker-polisher) resistivity monitors (\u003e=1 megohm-cm) and immediate water bypass on alarm.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01466","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Principles and Adequacy","topic":"Ultrafiltration Coefficient (Kuf) and TMP","q":"A high-flux dialyzer has a labeled in-vitro Ultrafiltration Coefficient (Kuf) of 40 mL/hr/mmHg. If the prescribed hourly ultrafiltration rate (UFR) is 800 mL/hr (neglecting plasma oncotic pressure correction), approximately what Transmembrane Pressure (TMP) is generated across the dialyzer membrane?","o":["0.05 mmHg","32,000 mmHg","20 mmHg (800 / 40)","200 mmHg"],"a":2,"e":"Transmembrane Pressure (TMP) relates to Ultrafiltration Rate (UFR) and dialyzer water permeability (Kuf) via UFR = Kuf x TMP. Rearranging: TMP = UFR / Kuf = 800 mL/hr / 40 mL/hr/mmHg = 20 mmHg (note: modern volumetric UF-controlled machines adjust TMP automatically, whereas low-flux dialyzers with Kuf ~5 require ~160 mmHg TMP for the same 800 mL/hr removal).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01467","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Vascular Access Management","topic":"Buttonhole vs Rope-Ladder Cannulation","q":"Which vascular access type is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to the constant-site \u0027buttonhole\u0027 cannulation technique, and what is the primary complication if buttonhole scab removal asepsis is suboptimal in a native AV fistula?","o":["Synthetic PTFE arteriovenous grafts (AVG) are an absolute contraindication; suboptimal scab disinfection in native AVFs causes severe Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia and septic endocarditis","Native radiocephalic AV fistulas are contraindicated; buttonhole causes zero infections","Both PTFE grafts and native AVFs require buttonhole cannulation with sharp needles","Buttonhole cannulation causes instant acute chloramine hemolysis"],"a":0,"e":"Buttonhole cannulation creates a single cylindrical fibrous scar tunnel into a native AVF using blunt needles after initial track formation. Never use buttonhole in synthetic PTFE grafts because repeated single-spot punctures shred graft fabric, forming pseudoaneurysms and persistent graft infections. Even in native AVFs, failing to thoroughly disinfect before AND after picking the scab seeds S. aureus bloodstream infections—making rope-ladder rotation preferred by KDOQI in most patients.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01468","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Dialysis Water Treatment and Complications","topic":"Hard-Water Syndrome","q":"Following maintenance on a clinic\u0027s water softener brine tank and an RO bypass malfunction, three hemodialysis patients simultaneously develop acute nausea, severe vomiting, intense burning/flushing of the skin, headache, and sudden marked hypertension (BP 210/118 mmHg). Water hardness testing reveals elevated Ca2+ and Mg2+ in the dialysate. What syndrome is occurring and what is the immediate technician action?","o":["Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome; switch to hypertonic 4.25% dextrose bath","Chloramine oxidative hemolysis; increase dialysate calcium bath to 3.5 mEq/L","Simple anxiety; administer oral calcium carbonate tablets","Hard-Water Syndrome (acute dialysate-induced hypercalcemia and hypermagnesemia); stop dialysis immediately, clamp lines, DO NOT return contaminated blood, and notify the nephrologist"],"a":3,"e":"When both the water softener and RO membrane fail or bypass simultaneously, high concentrations of dissolved calcium and magnesium enter the dialysate and diffuse rapidly into patients\u0027 blood, causing acute hypercalcemia and hypermagnesemia (\u0027Hard-Water Syndrome\u0027—nausea, vomiting, warmth/flushing, severe hypertension, lethargy, and cardiac arrhythmias). Dialysis must be halted immediately and the water plant audited.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01469","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Peritoneal Dialysis","topic":"Exit-Site Infection Prophylaxis","q":"Under International Society for Peritoneal Dialysis (ISPD) Catheter-Related Infection guidelines, what daily topical prophylactic regimen at the healed Tenckhoff catheter exit site is recommended to prevent Staphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas exit-site infections and subsequent peritonitis?","o":["Daily cleansing with antibacterial soap/clean water (or chlorhexidine), drying thoroughly, and applying topical Mupirocin (or Gentamicin) cream/ointment at the catheter exit site","Submerging the catheter exit site daily in stagnant public swimming pool water","Packing raw unsterile turmeric powder around the exit-site sinus","Using only dry cotton swabs once every 30 days without washing"],"a":0,"e":"Exit-site and subcutaneous tunnel infections are major precursors to refractory bacterial PD peritonitis. ISPD guidelines strongly recommend daily exit-site washing, thorough drying, catheter immobilization to prevent mechanical tugging trauma, and daily topical antibiotic cream/ointment (mupirocin for S. aureus nasal/exit-site carriers, or topical gentamicin providing dual Gram-positive and Pseudomonas coverage—avoiding petroleum/oil excipients that degrade polyurethane catheters if using polyurethane!).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01470","course":"Diploma in Dialysis Technology","subject":"Hemodialysis Complications","topic":"Recognizing Circuit Thrombosis","q":"During heparin-free hemodialysis, the technician notices the blood in the venous drip chamber turning very dark, visible fibrin clots forming on the drip-chamber mesh screen, and a rising Transmembrane Pressure (TMP) with falling venous return pressure (or rising post-pump arterial pressure). What must the technician do if the dialyzer completely clots?","o":["Forcefully flush 500 mL of high-pressure saline through the clotted dialyzer back into the patient","Stop the blood pump immediately, clamp lines, and DO NOT attempt to flush or rinse back a completely clotted dialyzer/venous chamber (discard the clotted circuit safely to prevent massive pulmonary embolism)","Crush the clot inside the venous drip chamber with bare fingers so it passes through the mesh","Increase blood pump speed to 500 mL/min to blow the clot into the fistula"],"a":1,"e":"Darkening blood, rising pre-dialyzer pressure/TMP, and visible fibrin strands indicate progressive dialyzer thrombosis. Once a hollow-fiber dialyzer or venous drip chamber is solidly clotted, attempting high-pressure saline rinseback or squeezing the chamber dislodges thromboemboli past the filter straight into the patient\u0027s venous circulation and pulmonary arteries—causing acute pulmonary embolism or fistula occlusion. Discard the clotted set and re-prime a fresh circuit if dialysis continues.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01471","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Electrocardiography Instrumentation","topic":"Precordial Lead Placement","q":"Where should chest leads V1, V2, and V4 be positioned when recording a standard adult 12-lead ECG?","o":["V1: 5th intercostal space right midclavicular line; V2: 5th intercostal space left sternal border; V4: 6th intercostal space midaxillary line","V1: 2nd intercostal space right sternal border; V2: 2nd intercostal space left sternal border; V4: 4th intercostal space anterior axillary line","V1: 4th intercostal space right sternal border; V2: 4th intercostal space left sternal border; V4: 5th intercostal space left midclavicular line","V1: 4th intercostal space left sternal border; V2: 4th intercostal space right sternal border; V4: 5th intercostal space left midaxillary line"],"a":2,"e":"Correct anatomical landmarks using the sternal angle of Louis (which marks the 2nd rib) are: V1 in the 4th intercostal space (ICS) at the right sternal edge, V2 in the 4th ICS at the left sternal edge, V3 midway between V2 and V4, V4 in the 5th ICS at the left midclavicular line, V5 at the same horizontal level as V4 on the anterior axillary line, and V6 at the same horizontal level on the midaxillary line.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01472","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ECG Technical Troubleshooting","topic":"Limb Lead Reversal Recognition","q":"On reviewing a freshly printed 12-lead ECG, the technician observes an inverted P wave, inverted QRS complex, and inverted T wave in lead I, a positive P/QRS in aVR, and normal progressive R-wave growth across precordial leads V1 to V6. What is the most likely cause?","o":["60 Hz AC electrical interference","Right leg (RL) and left leg (LL) electrode reversal","True mirror-image dextrocardia","Right arm (RA) and left arm (LA) limb electrode reversal"],"a":3,"e":"Reversal of RA and LA electrodes flips lead I upside down (negative P, QRS, T) and swaps aVR with aVL (making aVR upright), while precordial chest leads V1-V6 remain completely normal because Wilson\u0027s central terminal is unchanged. In true dextrocardia, R-wave progression across V1-V6 is reversed (decreasing R wave from V1 to V6).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01473","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Special ECG Procedures","topic":"Treadmill Testing Termination Criteria","q":"During stage 2 of a Bruce protocol treadmill exercise test (TMT), a 55-year-old man develops a 15 mmHg drop in systolic blood pressure below his baseline standing pressure accompanied by substernal chest tightness and 2.5 mm horizontal ST depression in V4-V6. What should the ECG technician and supervising physician do?","o":["Terminate the exercise test immediately, seat or lay the patient down, monitor ECG/BP, and initiate recovery protocol","Encourage the patient to finish stage 2 to achieve target heart rate of 85% predicted maximum","Give oral water during running and wait for systolic BP to drop by 30 mmHg","Switch the paper speed to 50 mm/sec and continue up to stage 3"],"a":0,"e":"A drop in systolic blood pressure \u003e10 mmHg below baseline despite an increase in workload, especially when accompanied by angina or ischemic ST-segment depression, is an absolute indication to terminate exercise stress testing immediately due to impending myocardial ischemia or left ventricular dysfunction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01474","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Arrhythmia Recognition","topic":"Ventricular Tachycardia vs Artifacts","q":"A patient receiving IV haloperidol and amiodarone develops syncope on telemetry. The ECG strip shows a baseline QTc of 560 ms followed by a rapid, irregular wide-complex tachycardia whose QRS peaks appear to twist continuously around the isoelectric baseline. Which rhythm is this, and what urgent drug is indicated if the patient has a pulse?","o":["Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia; intravenous amiodarone bolus","Torsades de pointes (polymorphic VT); intravenous magnesium sulfate","Atrial fibrillation with aberrancy; intravenous digoxin","Sinus arrhythmia; oral beta-blocker"],"a":1,"e":"Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia occurring in the setting of a prolonged QT interval (\u003e500 ms) with undulating QRS axis (\u0027twisting of the points\u0027) is Torsades de pointes. First-line pharmacotherapy in a hemodynamically stable/pulsatile episode is IV magnesium sulfate (1-2 g) to suppress early afterdepolarizations, along with stopping QT-prolonging drugs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01475","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Electrolyte and Metabolic ECG Patterns","topic":"Hyperkalemia Progression","q":"An ECG technician records a STAT 12-lead ECG on a missed-dialysis CKD patient and observes tall, narrow, symmetrically peaked (\u0027tented\u0027) T waves in V2-V4, flattening/loss of P waves, PR prolongation, and widening QRS complexes merging into a sine-wave pattern. What electrolyte emergency does this represent?","o":["Hypercalcemia with shortened QT interval and Osborn J waves","Acute hypocalcemia with isolated QT prolongation","Severe progressive hyperkalemia requiring immediate calcium gluconate stabilization","Severe hypokalemia with prominent U waves"],"a":2,"e":"Rising extracellular potassium progressively alters cardiac depolarization/repolarization: early peaked symmetrical T waves (5.5-6.5 mEq/L), followed by P-wave flattening/loss and PR prolongation (6.5-7.5 mEq/L), QRS widening and merging into a sine wave (\u003e8.0 mEq/L), preceding ventricular fibrillation or asystole. Immediate escalation for IV calcium gluconate membrane stabilization is vital.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01476","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Arrhythmia Recognition","topic":"Second-Degree AV Block","q":"On a continuous lead II rhythm strip, the technician notes a constant PR interval of 0.16 seconds on every conducted sinus beat, followed intermittently by a P wave that fails to conduct a QRS complex (3:2 and 4:3 conduction) with a wide QRS morphology. Which conduction abnormality is present?","o":["Sinus arrest without AV block","First-degree AV block","Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach) second-degree AV block","Mobitz Type II second-degree AV block (infranodal / high risk for progression to complete heart block)"],"a":3,"e":"Mobitz Type II second-degree AV block is characterized by constant PR intervals before and after randomly dropped QRS complexes (non-conducted P waves), usually arising below the AV node (His-Purkinje system) with a wide QRS and high risk of sudden progression to complete heart block.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01477","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Electrocardiography Instrumentation","topic":"Right-Sided and Posterior ECG Leads","q":"A cardiologist orders posterior chest leads (V7, V8, V9) to evaluate suspected posterior wall myocardial infarction (isolated ST depression in V1-V3). Where must the technician place electrodes V7, V8, and V9?","o":["Horizontally along the 5th intercostal space (level of V4/V6): V7 at left posterior axillary line, V8 at left midscapular line, V9 at left paraspinal border","Along the right chest mirror-image of V1-V3 in the 4th intercostal space","In the 2nd intercostal space above the clavicles posteriorly","On the right posterior axillary line, right midscapular line, and right paraspinal line"],"a":0,"e":"Posterior leads V7-V9 continue the horizontal plane of V4-V6 posteriorly around the left thorax: V7 at the left posterior axillary line, V8 at the left midscapular line (inferior scapular angle), and V9 at the left paraspinal (paravertebral) border. Right-sided leads (V3R-V6R) mirror left precordial positions onto the right chest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01478","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Special ECG Procedures","topic":"Patient Preparation and Artifact Prevention","q":"When hooking up a 24-hour Holter monitor on a male patient with dense chest hair for palpitations evaluation, which preparation and patient instruction protocol ensures diagnostic, artifact-free recordings?","o":["Shave small electrode sites if needed, abrade skin lightly with fine prep tape and clean with alcohol (let dry), secure lead loops with tape, advise against showering/getting unit wet, and instruct to log exact symptoms and times in the diary","Set the monitor to record only when the patient presses the event button, turning off continuous recording","Instruct the patient to remove the electrodes during sleep and during morning showers","Apply electrodes directly over dry chest hair with extra gel without skin preparation"],"a":0,"e":"High skin-electrode impedance and motion tugging are the top causes of Holter baseline wander and false-pause/pseudo-VT artifacts. Proper clipping/shaving, gentle stratum corneum abrasion, alcohol degreasing, strain-relief lead looping, keeping the recorder dry, and maintaining a time-stamped activity/symptom diary ensure accurate correlation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01479","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ECG Calculations and Calibration","topic":"Half-Standard Voltage and 50 mm/s Speed Errors","q":"A technician notices that an ECG printout shows a tall 20 mm calibration box (instead of 10 mm) at the start of each row and a paper speed stamped \u002750 mm/s\u0027. If a QRS complex measures 16 mm tall and 6 small boxes (6 mm) wide on this printout, what are the true voltage and true QRS duration?","o":["True voltage = 1.6 mV; true QRS duration = 0.12 seconds (120 ms)","True voltage = 0.8 mV (would be 8 mm at standard 10 mm/mV); true QRS duration = 0.12 seconds (120 ms)","True voltage = 1.6 mV; true QRS duration = 0.24 seconds (240 ms)","True voltage = 3.2 mV; true QRS duration = 0.06 seconds (60 ms)"],"a":1,"e":"Double gain (20 mm/mV) doubles vertical amplitude, so a 16 mm deflection equals 16 / 20 = 0.8 mV (equivalent to 8 mm at standard 10 mm/mV). Double paper speed (50 mm/s) halves the time per millimeter from 0.04 s to 0.02 s, so 6 mm width equals 6 x 0.02 s = 0.12 s (120 ms).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01480","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Myocardial Ischemia and Infarction ECG","topic":"Coronary Artery Territory Mapping","q":"A 12-lead ECG recorded from a patient with acute chest pain shows 3 mm ST-segment elevation in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6 with reciprocal ST depression in leads III and aVF. Which myocardial wall and coronary vessel are most likely involved?","o":["Anteroseptal wall exclusively; isolated septal perforator","Inferior wall; right coronary artery (RCA)","High lateral and apical-lateral left ventricular wall; left circumflex (LCx) or obtuse marginal / diagonal branch","Right ventricular free wall; conus branch"],"a":2,"e":"Leads I and aVL face the high lateral wall, while V5 and V6 face the low lateral/apical-lateral wall of the left ventricle—typically perfused by the left circumflex artery (LCx) or its obtuse marginal branches (and first diagonal of LAD). Reciprocal depression often appears in the opposite inferior leads (III, aVF).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01481","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Arrhythmia Recognition","topic":"Atrial Fibrillation vs Atrial Flutter","q":"An ECG rhythm strip at standard 25 mm/sec paper speed shows no discrete P waves, an undulating fibrillatory baseline, and irregularly irregular narrow QRS complexes. Counting across a full 6-second interval (30 large boxes), the technician counts 11 QRS complexes. What is the rhythm and estimated average ventricular rate?","o":["Sinus tachycardia at 110 beats/min","Atrial fibrillation with an average ventricular rate of 55 beats/min","Atrial flutter with fixed 2:1 conduction at 150 beats/min","Atrial fibrillation with an average ventricular rate of 110 beats/min"],"a":3,"e":"Irregularly irregular R-R intervals lacking organized P waves define atrial fibrillation. Because R-R intervals vary constantly, the 300/large-box method is inaccurate; instead, count the number of QRS complexes in a 6-second strip (30 large boxes, since 30 x 0.20 s = 6.0 s) and multiply by 10: 11 x 10 = 110 beats/min.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01482","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Conduction Abnormalities","topic":"Right vs Left Bundle Branch Block","q":"Which ECG criteria accurately identify complete Right Bundle Branch Block (RBBB) on a 12-lead ECG?","o":["QRS duration \u003e=120 ms, triphasic rSR\u0027 (\u0027rabbit-ear\u0027 M-shaped) pattern in lead V1, and wide slurred S wave in leads I and V6","Short PR interval (\u003c0.12 s) with slurred delta upstroke and normal terminal QRS","QRS duration \u003e=120 ms, deep broad QS/rS wave in V1, and broad notched monophasic R wave without Q waves in leads I, aVL, and V6","QRS duration \u003c80 ms, narrow R wave in V1, and peaked T waves in V2-V4"],"a":0,"e":"In complete RBBB (QRS \u003e=120 ms), initial septal depolarization proceeds normally (small r in V1, small q in V6), followed by left ventricular depolarization (S in V1, R in V6), and finally delayed slow right ventricular depolarization producing a terminal tall R\u0027 in V1 (rSR\u0027) and a wide slurred terminal S wave in lateral leads I and V6.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01483","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Pacemaker ECG Interpretation","topic":"Failure to Capture vs Failure to Sense","q":"While reviewing a telemetry strip of a patient with a temporary transvenous ventricular pacemaker (VVI mode), the ECG technician sees sharp vertical pacemaker spikes occurring regularly at 70/min, but half of the spikes are followed by a flat baseline with NO resulting QRS complex or T wave. What pacemaker malfunction does this represent?","o":["Failure to sense (undersensing)","Failure to capture (loss of capture)","Oversensing causing inappropriate inhibition","Normal dual-chamber DDD tracking"],"a":1,"e":"Failure to capture occurs when the pacemaker delivers an electrical impulse (visible pacing spike) during a non-refractory period of the cardiac cycle, yet the myocardium fails to depolarize (no wide paced QRS follows the spike)—often due to lead dislodgement, exit block, battery depletion, or severe hyperkalemia/acidosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01484","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Electrocardiography Instrumentation","topic":"Diagnostic vs Monitor Mode Filters","q":"Why must a diagnostic 12-lead ECG machine be set to standard diagnostic bandwidth (0.05-150 Hz) rather than narrow \u0027monitor mode\u0027 (0.5-40 Hz) when evaluating a patient for acute myocardial ischemia?","o":["40 Hz low-pass filtering eliminates only 50 Hz AC interference without affecting R-wave height","0.05 Hz filtering intentionally removes all P waves to highlight QRS complexes","A 0.5 Hz high-pass filter causes artificial ST-segment elevation or depression distortion, while a 40 Hz low-pass filter attenuates sharp pacemaker spikes and notched QRS amplitudes","Monitor mode doubles the paper speed to 50 mm/sec automatically"],"a":2,"e":"The low-frequency ST segment lies below 0.5 Hz; raising the high-pass filter cutoff from 0.05 Hz to 0.5 Hz (used on bedside monitors to suppress respiratory baseline wander) introduces phase distortion that can create false J-point/ST-segment shifts mimicking STEMI or NSTEMI. Similarly, a 40 Hz low-pass filter clips high-frequency QRS peaks and pacemaker spikes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01485","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ECG Calculations and Measurements","topic":"Bazett Corrected QT (QTc) Calculation","q":"A patient\u0027s 12-lead ECG at 25 mm/sec shows a regular R-R interval of 0.64 seconds (16 small boxes) and a measured QT interval of 0.40 seconds (10 small boxes). Using Bazett\u0027s formula (QTc = QT / sqrt(RR)), what is the corrected QT interval (QTc)?","o":["0.32 seconds (320 ms) — shortened","0.625 seconds (625 ms) — severely prolonged","0.40 seconds (400 ms) — normal","0.50 seconds (500 ms) — prolonged"],"a":3,"e":"Bazett\u0027s formula corrects the measured QT interval for heart rate: QTc = QT / sqrt(RR in seconds). Here, sqrt(0.64) = 0.80, so QTc = 0.40 / 0.80 = 0.50 seconds (500 ms), which exceeds normal limits (\u003c450 ms men, \u003c460 ms women) and indicates heightened risk for Torsades de pointes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01486","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Pre-Excitation and Arrhythmias","topic":"Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) Pattern","q":"A 22-year-old athlete\u0027s screening ECG shows sinus rhythm at 72/min with a shortened PR interval of 0.09 seconds (\u003c120 ms), a slurred initial upstroke of the QRS complex (\u0027delta wave\u0027), and QRS duration of 0.13 seconds. Which ECG pattern is this, and why are AV-nodal blocking drugs (like IV verapamil or adenosine) avoided if this patient develops pre-excited atrial fibrillation?","o":["Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) pattern; blocking the AV node channels all rapid atrial impulses down the accessory bundle of Kent, risking ventricular fibrillation","Complete left bundle branch block; AV blockers worsen QTc prolongation","First-degree AV block; AV blockers shorten the PR interval dangerously","Lown-Ganong-Levine syndrome; AV blockers cause sinus arrest only"],"a":0,"e":"WPW syndrome results from an accessory atrioventricular bypass tract (bundle of Kent) that depolarizes the ventricles early, creating a short PR (\u003c0.12 s), slurred QRS upstroke (delta wave), and widened QRS. In atrial fibrillation with WPW, AV-nodal blockers (verapamil, diltiazem, beta-blockers, digoxin, adenosine) abolish AV nodal competition and accelerate 1:1 conduction down the accessory pathway into VF.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01487","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ST-Segment Differential Diagnosis","topic":"Acute Pericarditis vs Acute STEMI","q":"A 29-year-old man presents with sharp pleuritic chest pain relieved by leaning forward. His 12-lead ECG reveals diffuse concave-upward (\u0027saddle-shaped\u0027) ST-segment elevation across leads I, II, aVL, aVF, and V2-V6, accompanied by diffuse PR-segment depression and reciprocal ST depression with PR elevation in lead aVR, with NO focal reciprocal ST depression elsewhere. What diagnosis fits this ECG?","o":["Acute isolated inferior wall STEMI","Acute pericarditis (Stage 1)","Posterior wall myocardial infarction","Brugada Type 1 channelopathy"],"a":1,"e":"Stage 1 acute pericarditis causes diffuse epicardial inflammation across non-contiguous coronary territories (both limb and precordial leads except aVR/V1), producing concave-upward ST elevation, PR-segment depression (atrial injury), and reciprocal PR elevation/ST depression in aVR, without the localized convex ST elevation, reciprocal lead depression, or pathological Q waves typical of STEMI.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01488","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Cardiopulmonary ECG Patterns","topic":"Acute Pulmonary Embolism ECG Findings","q":"When recording an emergency ECG in a post-operative orthopedic patient with sudden dyspnea, pleuritic pain, and hypoxia (suspected acute pulmonary embolism), what is the MOST COMMON ECG finding overall, and what classic (though less sensitive) triad reflects acute cor pulmonale?","o":["Marked sinus bradycardia is most common; tall U waves in V2-V3","Osborn J waves in all leads","Sinus tachycardia is most common; the classic McGinn-White S1Q3T3 pattern (deep S in lead I, Q wave in lead III, inverted T in lead III) plus V1-V4 T-wave inversions","Complete heart block is most common; diffuse PR depression in V5-V6"],"a":2,"e":"Sinus tachycardia is the most frequent ECG abnormality in acute pulmonary embolism. Acute right ventricular strain can additionally produce right axis shift, new incomplete/complete RBBB, precordial V1-V4 T-wave inversion, and the classic S1Q3T3 pattern (S wave in lead I, Q wave and T-wave inversion in lead III).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01489","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ECG Technical Troubleshooting","topic":"AC Interference vs Somatic Tremor vs Loose Lead","q":"While recording a bedside 12-lead ECG in an ICU ward in India, the technician sees a uniform, thick, fuzzy, saw-toothed baseline oscillation at exactly 50 cycles per second across all limb leads, obscuring P waves. What is the cause and appropriate technical correction?","o":["Loose V4 chest electrode only; replace only the V4 sticker","Atrial flutter at 300/min; immediately start synchronized cardioversion","Parkinsonian somatic muscle tremor; warm the room with a blanket","50 Hz alternating-current (AC) electrical interference; check ECG machine earthing/grounding, move patient cable away from power cords/bed motors, unplug unnecessary nearby electrical devices, and enable the 50 Hz notch filter if needed"],"a":3,"e":"Uniform, regular 50 Hz (in India/Europe; 60 Hz in North America) sinusoidal thickening of the ECG baseline stems from AC powerline electromagnetic interference or poor machine/RL grounding. Correcting grounding, separating patient lead wires from wall AC cords/electric beds, ensuring low electrode impedance, and engaging the AC notch filter eliminates it cleanly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01490","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Inherited Arrhythmia ECG Patterns","topic":"Brugada Type 1 ECG Pattern","q":"A 34-year-old man who survived unexplained nocturnal agonal breathing and syncope has a resting 12-lead ECG showing a right-bundle-branch-like rSr\u0027 morphology in leads V1 and V2 with \u003e=2 mm coved ST-segment elevation descending smoothly into an inverted symmetrical T wave (with normal QT interval). What ECG syndrome is this, and where are V1-V2 sometimes shifted if ordered to unmask a concealed pattern?","o":["Type 1 Brugada ECG pattern (high risk for polymorphic VT/VF); V1 and V2 may be moved upward to the 2nd or 3rd intercostal space (right/left sternal borders)","Hypokalemia; repeat ECG after IV potassium chloride","Left ventricular hypertrophy strain; move V5-V6 to the right shoulder","Benign early repolarization; move V1-V2 down to the 7th intercostal space"],"a":0,"e":"Type 1 Brugada pattern—coved ST elevation \u003e=2 mm in \u003e=1 right precordial lead (V1-V2) followed by a negative T wave—reflects right ventricular outflow tract (RVOT) sodium-channel dysfunction (SCN5A) and carries high sudden cardiac death risk (requiring ICD evaluation and fever control). Because the RVOT lies superiorly, placing V1-V2 higher in the 2nd and 3rd ICS increases diagnostic sensitivity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01491","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ECG Axis and Hypertrophy","topic":"Quadrant Method Axis Determination","q":"Using the standard frontal-plane quadrant method, a 12-lead ECG shows a predominantly upright (positive) QRS complex in Lead I, a predominantly negative (rS) QRS in Lead aVF, and a predominantly negative (rS) QRS in Lead II. What is the mean QRS electrical axis?","o":["Normal QRS axis (between -30 degrees and +90 degrees)","Pathologic Left Axis Deviation (between -30 degrees and -90 degrees)","Right Axis Deviation (between +90 degrees and +180 degrees)","Extreme Northwest (\u0027No Man\u0027s Land\u0027) axis (between -90 degrees and +/-180 degrees)"],"a":1,"e":"When Lead I is positive (0 deg hemisphere) and Lead aVF is negative (-90 deg hemisphere), the axis lies in the left upper quadrant (0 to -90 deg). Checking Lead II (+60 deg, whose perpendicular is -30 deg) distinguishes physiological/borderline 0 to -30 deg (Lead II positive) from true pathologic Left Axis Deviation (-30 to -90 deg, where Lead II is predominantly negative, e.g., left anterior fascicular block).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01492","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Electrolyte and Systemic ECG Patterns","topic":"Hypothermia ECG Findings","q":"An elderly homeless patient found outdoors in winter with a core body temperature of 29.5 degrees C has a 12-lead ECG showing sinus bradycardia at 40/min, prolonged PR/QT intervals, fine shivering somatic tremor artifact, and a prominent positive \u0027camel-hump\u0027 deflection at the junction of the QRS complex and ST segment in V3-V6. What is this characteristic deflection?","o":["Delta wave of Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome","Epsilon wave of arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy","Osborn wave (J wave) characteristic of moderate-to-severe hypothermia","Pathological Q wave of chronic transmural infarction"],"a":2,"e":"Below ~32 degrees C, hypothermia slows cardiac conduction (bradycardia, prolonged PR/QRS/QT) and accentuates epicardial Ito potassium current notch, producing characteristic positive J-point humps (Osborn J waves) best seen in lateral precordial and inferior leads whose height correlates inversely with core temperature and resolves upon rewarming.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01493","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Pediatric Electrocardiography","topic":"Normal Newborn vs Adult ECG","q":"When recording a 12-lead ECG on a healthy 2-week-old full-term newborn, which combination of findings is physiologically NORMAL for age (and would be abnormal if seen in a 40-year-old adult)?","o":["Resting heart rate 45/min, Left Axis Deviation (-60 degrees), and deep QS in V1","PR interval 0.32 seconds with complete AV dissociation","QRS duration 180 ms with coved Brugada ST elevation","Resting heart rate 120-160/min, Right Axis Deviation (+90 to +160 degrees), dominant tall R wave in V1, and inverted T waves in V1-V3"],"a":3,"e":"In utero, the fetal right ventricle pumps against high pulmonary vascular resistance, so healthy neonates exhibit physiologic right ventricular dominance: resting sinus rate 110-160/min, right axis deviation (+90 to +180 deg), tall R \u003e S in V1, and T-wave inversion across V1-V3 (\u0027juvenile T-wave pattern\u0027 persisting into childhood/adolescence; upright T in V1 after day 7 of life up to age 7 actually suggests RVH!).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01494","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Myocardial Ischemia and Infarction ECG","topic":"Wellens Syndrome","q":"A 56-year-old man who experienced chest pain 3 hours earlier is now completely pain-free when an ECG technician records his resting 12-lead ECG prior to a scheduled Treadmill Exercise Test (TMT). The ECG reveals normal R-wave progression and deeply symmetrically inverted (or biphasic) T waves in V2-V4 with minimal ST elevation. Why MUST the technician immediately alert the physician and NOT proceed with treadmill exercise testing?","o":["This represents Wellens syndrome (critical proximal LAD coronary stenosis); exercise stress testing can precipitate massive acute anterior myocardial infarction or sudden cardiac arrest","This is caused solely by V2-V3 electrode reversal","This indicates acute benign pericarditis requiring oral ibuprofen during TMT","This represents normal athlete\u0027s heart; exercise testing should be performed at double speed"],"a":0,"e":"Wellens syndrome presents in a pain-free interval after unstable angina with characteristic Type A (biphasic +/-) or Type B (deep symmetrical inverted) T waves in V2-V3 (often V1-V4) without pathological Q waves or significant troponin rise. It signifies critical \u003e90% proximal Left Anterior Descending (LAD) stenosis; provocative TMT stress testing is strictly contraindicated due to high risk of acute anterior STEMI and ventricular fibrillation, requiring urgent coronary angiography.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01495","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Pacemaker ECG Interpretation","topic":"Clinical Magnet Application Effects","q":"When a standard clinical ring magnet is placed directly over a patient\u0027s Permanent Pacemaker (PPM) versus an Implantable Cardioverter-Defibrillator (ICD) while recording an ECG, what distinct effect occurs in each device?","o":["In a PPM, the magnet switches pacing to asynchronous fixed-rate mode (VOO/DOO/AOO, ignoring intrinsic beats) to test capture; in an ICD, the magnet suspends anti-tachycardia shocks/ATP WITHOUT altering underlying pacing mode","In a PPM, the magnet permanently turns off all electrical pacing forever; in an ICD, it triggers immediate 36 J defibrillation shocks","In both PPM and ICD, the magnet erases battery charge immediately","In a PPM, the magnet increases sensitivity so low-voltage P waves inhibit pacing"],"a":0,"e":"Placing a clinical magnet closes the internal reed switch (or Hall sensor). In a conventional pacemaker, this disables sensing and forces fixed asynchronous pacing at a manufacturer-specific magnet rate (useful to verify battery life and capture when intrinsic rhythm suppresses pacing, or during cautery in pacemaker-dependent patients). In an ICD, a magnet suspends tachyarrhythmia shock/ATP delivery (e.g., to prevent inappropriate electrocautery shocks) while leaving bradycardia pacing unchanged.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01496","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Electrocardiographic Anatomy \u0026 Lead Systems","topic":"Precordial (Chest) Lead Placement","q":"During an OSCE practical check on standard 12-lead ECG acquisition, which anatomical landmark specification is correct for placing precordial leads V1, V2, V4, and V6?","o":["V1: 5th ICS right midclavicular line; V2: 5th ICS left sternal border; V4: 6th ICS anterior axillary line; V6: 7th ICS midaxillary line","V1: 4th ICS right sternal border; V2: 4th ICS left sternal border; V4: 5th ICS left midclavicular line; V6: left midaxillary line at same horizontal level as V4","V1: 2nd ICS right sternal border; V2: 2nd ICS left sternal border; V4: 4th ICS midclavicular line; V6: posterior scapular line","V1: 3rd ICS right parasternal; V2: 4th ICS right parasternal; V4: xiphoid process; V6: left posterior axillary line"],"a":1,"e":"Per AHA/ACC ECG standardization: locate the sternal angle (Angle of Louis) to identify the 2nd rib/2nd ICS, count down to the 4th ICS for V1 (right sternal edge) and V2 (left sternal edge), place V4 at the 5th ICS left midclavicular line, V3 midway between V2 and V4, and V5 (anterior axillary) and V6 (midaxillary) horizontally aligned with V4.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01497","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ECG Measurement \u0026 Rhythm Analysis","topic":"Heart Rate Calculation (Regular vs Irregular)","q":"On a standard ECG recorded at 25 mm/s, a patient’s rhythm is irregularly irregular (atrial fibrillation with absent P waves). You count exactly 13 QRS complexes within a 30-large-box (6-second) interval on the rhythm strip. What is the estimated ventricular rate?","o":["78 beats/minute","65 beats/minute","130 beats/minute","195 beats/minute"],"a":2,"e":"At 25 mm/s paper speed, 5 large (5-mm) boxes equal 1 second, so 30 large boxes equal 6 seconds. Multiplying the number of QRS complexes in 6 seconds by 10 gives beats per minute: 13 × 10 = 130 bpm. In irregular rhythms like AF, the 300/large-box rule on a single R-R interval is unreliable.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01498","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ECG Troubleshooting \u0026 Artifacts","topic":"Right Arm–Left Arm (RA–LA) Electrode Swap","q":"A routine preoperative ECG shows inverted P, QRS, and T waves in Lead I and an upright P wave and upright QRS in Lead aVR. However, the precordial leads V1 through V6 demonstrate normal R-wave progression (R amplitude increasing from V1 to V5). What is the most likely explanation?","o":["True mirror-image dextrocardia with situs inversus","Acute high lateral myocardial infarction requiring urgent thrombolysis","Left Arm and Left Leg (LA–LL) electrode swap","Right Arm and Left Arm (RA–LA) limb electrodes were swapped; repeat the ECG after correcting limb lead placement"],"a":3,"e":"Swapping RA and LA inverts Lead I completely (negative P, QRS, T) and swaps aVR with aVL (making aVR upright). Unlike true dextrocardia—in which R-wave amplitude progressively diminishes across V1–V6 (reverse R-wave progression)—RA–LA reversal leaves the chest leads untouched, preserving normal precordial R-wave progression.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01499","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Clinical ECG Patterns \u0026 Acute Coronary Syndromes","topic":"Inferior STEMI \u0026 Right Ventricular Extension","q":"A 58-year-old man presents with crushing retrosternal chest pain and diaphoresis. His initial 12-lead ECG shows 3 mm ST-segment elevation in Leads II, III, and aVF (with ST elevation in III \u003e II) and reciprocal ST depression in I and aVL. Which additional ECG lead must the technician immediately record before the physician administers nitrates?","o":["Right-sided chest lead V4R (5th intercostal space, right midclavicular line) to screen for right ventricular infarction","Right-sided lead V1R placed over the right scapula","Repeat limb leads at 5 mm/mV gain without any chest leads","Lewis lead exclusively on the left thigh to measure QT dispersion"],"a":0,"e":"Inferior STEMI (II, III, aVF), especially when ST elevation in Lead III exceeds Lead II, strongly suggests proximal right coronary artery occlusion with potential right ventricular (RV) infarction. Recording V4R (5th ICS right midclavicular line; \u003e=1 mm ST elevation is diagnostic) alerts clinicians to avoid preload-reducing nitrates/morphine that can precipitate severe hypotension.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01500","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ECG Equipment \u0026 Technical Standards","topic":"Gain and Paper Speed Settings","q":"On a standard 12-lead ECG, what dimensions should the rectangular calibration pulse display at baseline settings, and when should the ECG technician switch to half-standardization (5 mm/mV)?","o":["20 mm height × 2 mm width; switch to 5 mm/mV only when recording Lead aVR","10 mm height × 5 mm width (at 10 mm/mV and 25 mm/s); switch to 5 mm/mV when tall QRS complexes overlap adjacent lead tracings in severe ventricular hypertrophy","5 mm height × 10 mm width (at 5 mm/mV and 50 mm/s); switch to 5 mm/mV for microvoltages in pericardial effusion","10 mm height × 1 mm width; switch to 5 mm/mV whenever heart rate exceeds 80 bpm"],"a":1,"e":"At standard calibration (10 mm/mV gain, 25 mm/s speed), a 1 mV, 0.20-second calibration pulse measures 10 mm (2 large boxes) tall and 5 mm (1 large box) wide. When massive QRS voltages (e.g., severe LVH) clip or collide across channels, switching to half-gain (5 mm/mV) keeps complexes readable—and must be clearly labeled on the tracing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01501","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ECG Troubleshooting \u0026 Artifacts","topic":"50 Hz AC Interference \u0026 Wandering Baseline","q":"An ECG tracing shows a uniform, dense, sawtooth-like thickening of the baseline at 50 cycles per second across all leads in an ICU bed. Which corrective action specifically targets this artifact?","o":["Apply thick ultrasound gel across the entire chest wall connecting V1 through V6 continuously","Switch paper speed to 10 mm/s and turn off all skin preparation","Check machine earth grounding, unplug or distance non-essential AC equipment (such as motorized bed plugs/infusion pumps if safe), untangle lead wires from power cords, and ensure clean electrode contact","Ask the patient to hold a deep breath for 30 seconds while tightening the limb clamps painfully"],"a":2,"e":"In India, mains AC frequency is 50 Hz, producing regular 50-cycles/sec fuzzy/thick baseline interference when there is poor grounding, dried electrode gel, or electromagnetic coupling from nearby AC power cords/motorized beds. Ensuring proper grounding, separating ECG cables from AC cords, and re-prepping electrodes resolves it cleanly (with the 50 Hz notch filter as adjunct).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01502","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Clinical ECG Patterns \u0026 Arrhythmias","topic":"Complete Heart Block \u0026 Critical Panic Alerts","q":"A 72-year-old man complaining of near-syncope has a 12-lead ECG showing regular P waves at 88/min and completely independent, regular wide QRS complexes at 34/min with constantly varying PR intervals (no consistent relationship between P waves and QRS). What rhythm is this, and what must the ECG technician do immediately?","o":["First-degree AV block; tell the patient to walk home and follow up in outpatient clinic next week","Sinus bradycardia with normal conduction; delete the tracing as muscle artifact","Wenckebach (Mobitz Type I) second-degree block; discharge immediately without informing the doctor","Third-degree (complete) AV block; keep the patient supine/monitored, do not leave them unattended, notify the emergency physician immediately, and ensure transcutaneous pacing pads/crash cart are accessible"],"a":3,"e":"Regular atrial P waves (88/min) and slower regular ventricular escape QRS complexes (34/min) marching independently with variable PR intervals define complete (3rd-degree) heart block (AV dissociation). With a slow wide-complex ventricular escape and syncope risk, the patient can deteriorate into asystole/cardiac arrest at any moment—requiring immediate clinician alert and pacing readiness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01503","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ECG Measurement \u0026 Rhythm Analysis","topic":"PR, QRS, and QTc Intervals","q":"At standard 25 mm/s paper speed, what are the normal ranges for the PR interval and QRS duration, and which corrected QT (QTc via Bazett’s formula) threshold signals high risk for Torsades de Pointes?","o":["PR: 0.12–0.20 s (3–5 small boxes); QRS: \u003c 0.12 s (\u003c 3 small boxes); QTc \u003e 500 ms carries high risk of Torsades de Pointes","PR: 0.04–0.08 s (1–2 small boxes); QRS: 0.20–0.30 s; QTc \u003c 350 ms carries high risk of Torsades de Pointes","PR: 0.12–0.20 s; QRS: \u003c 0.12 s; QTc is unaffected by heart rate so Bazett’s formula multiplies QT by 2","PR: 0.24–0.40 s; QRS: 0.14–0.18 s; QTc \u003e 250 ms carries high risk of Torsades de Pointes"],"a":0,"e":"Normal adult PR interval is 120–200 ms (3–5 small 0.04-s boxes) and normal QRS duration is \u003c120 ms (\u003c3 small boxes). Bazett’s formula corrects QT for heart rate (QTc = QT / sqrt(RR in seconds)); QTc \u003e 500 ms markedly increases risk of polymorphic VT (Torsades de Pointes).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01504","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Stress Testing (TMT) \u0026 Ambulatory Monitoring","topic":"Mason-Likar Lead Placement \u0026 Termination Criteria","q":"While assisting a physician during a Bruce-protocol Treadmill Test (TMT) using Mason-Likar modified torso leads, which finding constitutes an ABSOLUTE indication to terminate the exercise test immediately?","o":["Occasional isolated atrial premature beats (APCs) in an asymptomatic patient","A drop in systolic blood pressure \u003e= 10 mmHg below baseline despite increasing workload accompanied by ischemia/angina, or sustained ventricular tachycardia","Mild leg fatigue with systolic BP rising appropriately from 120 to 156 mmHg","Heart rate reaching 75% of age-predicted maximum without symptoms or ST change"],"a":1,"e":"In TMT, limb electrodes are moved to the torso (Mason-Likar: subclavicular fossae and lower abdomen/iliac crests) to minimize motion noise. Absolute AHA termination criteria include \u003e=10 mmHg SBP drop from baseline with ischemia, moderate-to-severe angina, sustained VT, ST elevation \u003e=1.0 mm in non-Q-wave leads, cyanosis/pallor, CNS symptoms (ataxia/near-syncope), or patient request to stop.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01505","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Chamber Enlargement ECG","topic":"Left Ventricular Hypertrophy (LVH) Criteria","q":"On a standard-calibrated (10 mm/mV) 12-lead ECG of a 60-year-old hypertensive patient, the S wave in V1 measures 18 mm deep and the R wave in V5 measures 24 mm tall (accompanied by asymmetric downsloping ST depression and T-wave inversion in V5-V6). Using Sokolow-Lyon criteria, does this meet Left Ventricular Hypertrophy (LVH) criteria?","o":["Yes; this meets Right Ventricular Hypertrophy criteria exclusively","No; asymmetric T inversion in V5-V6 excludes LVH","Yes; S(V1) + R(V5) = 42 mm (\u003e= 35 mm threshold), confirming LVH with repolarization (\u0027strain\u0027) abnormality","No; Sokolow-Lyon requires S(V1) + R(V5) \u003e= 80 mm"],"a":2,"e":"Sokolow-Lyon voltage criteria for LVH in adults (\u003e35-40 years) state: S wave depth in V1 + tallest R wave in V5 or V6 \u003e= 35 mm (3.5 mV), or R in aVL \u003e= 11 mm. Here 18 + 24 = 42 mm exceeds 35 mm, and lateral ST depression with asymmetric T-wave inversion (\u0027LV strain\u0027) reflects chronic pressure-overload subendocardial repolarization changes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01506","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Chamber Enlargement ECG","topic":"P-Mitrale vs P-Pulmonale","q":"A patient with severe rheumatic mitral stenosis has a resting ECG demonstrating a broad, notched (\u0027M-shaped\u0027) P wave lasting 0.14 seconds (3.5 small boxes) in Lead II and a prominent deep negative terminal P-wave deflection (\u003e1 mm deep x \u003e1 mm wide) in Lead V1. Which atrial abnormality does this represent?","o":["Right atrial enlargement (P pulmonale)","Sinus arrest with junctional escape","Hyperkalemia with absent P waves","Left atrial abnormality / enlargement (P mitrale)"],"a":3,"e":"Left atrial depolarization occurs later and posteriorly; enlargement prolongs total P duration \u003e=120 ms (3 small boxes) creating a bifid notched M-shaped P wave in Lead II (\u0027P mitrale\u0027, classically seen in mitral stenosis/hypertension) and an accentuated negative terminal P deflection in V1 (Morris index: width \u003e=40 ms x depth \u003e=1 mm). Right atrial enlargement produces tall peaked P waves \u003e=2.5 mm in II/III/aVF (\u0027P pulmonale\u0027).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01507","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Arrhythmia Recognition","topic":"AV Nodal Reentrant Tachycardia (AVNRT)","q":"A 26-year-old woman presents with sudden-onset regular palpitations at 188 beats/min. Her 12-lead ECG reveals a strictly regular narrow-complex tachycardia (QRS 80 ms) with NO visible P waves preceding the QRS, and a tiny terminal pseudo-r\u0027 deflection at the end of the QRS in V1 (and pseudo-S wave in II/III/aVF) that was absent on her previous baseline sinus ECG. What is this rhythm?","o":["Typical Atrioventricular Nodal Reentrant Tachycardia (AVNRT)","Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (Torsades de pointes)","Atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response","Third-degree complete AV block"],"a":0,"e":"Typical (slow-fast) AVNRT causes simultaneous antegrade ventricular activation and retrograde atrial activation, burying the inverted retrograde P wave inside or at the terminal edge of the QRS complex—producing a subtle terminal pseudo-r\u0027 in V1 and pseudo-S in inferior leads at regular rates of 150-220/min (terminating abruptly with vagal maneuvers or IV adenosine).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01508","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Drug Effects on ECG","topic":"Digoxin Effect vs Digoxin Toxicity","q":"A patient taking oral digoxin for heart failure has an ECG showing gentle, scooped, concave-upward (\u0027Salvador Dali mustache\u0027) ST-segment depression in V4-V6 with shortened QT interval, versus another patient on digoxin presenting with nausea, yellow vision, and alternating beat-to-beat QRS axis reversals in ventricular tachycardia (bidirectional VT). How are these two ECG findings interpreted?","o":["Bidirectional VT is a normal benign finding in patients taking digoxin","Scooped ST depression with shortened QT reflects benign therapeutic \u0027digitalis effect\u0027 (does NOT prove toxicity); bidirectional VT (and PAT with block or frequent PVCs) indicates life-threatening digitalis toxicity","Digoxin always prolongs the QT interval to \u003e600 ms","Scooped ST depression proves lethal digoxin overdose requiring immediate dialysis"],"a":1,"e":"Therapeutic digoxin alters phase 2-3 repolarization, producing characteristic downsloping scooped (\u0027hockey-stick\u0027 or \u0027Salvador Dali mustache\u0027) ST depression with shortened QT—seen at normal serum levels and NOT an indication to stop digoxin. Conversely, digoxin toxicity (often precipitated by hypokalemia or renal failure) manifests with increased automaticity + AV block: atrial tachycardia with 2:1 block, Wenckebach/complete block, frequent bigeminal PVCs, and classic bidirectional VT.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01509","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Electrocardiography Instrumentation","topic":"Mirror-Image Dextrocardia ECG","q":"A 20-year-old patient\u0027s 12-lead ECG shows an inverted P wave and inverted QRS in Lead I, positive P/QRS in aVR, AND progressively diminishing R-wave amplitude from V1 toward V6 (reverse R-wave progression; tall R in V1 shrinking to tiny rS in V6). After verifying limb electrodes are correctly placed, what should the ECG technician do next to record a diagnostic mirror-corrected ECG?","o":["Discard the machine as broken without notifying the physician","Place all chest leads V1-V6 along the left lumbar spine","Confirm mirror-image dextrocardia, reverse the RA and LA limb leads, and move precordial chest leads V1-V6 to their mirror-image right-chest positions (V1R-V6R, clearly labeling the printout \u0027Dextrocardia / Right-Sided Leads\u0027)","Increase paper speed to 100 mm/s only"],"a":2,"e":"Unlike simple RA-LA arm lead reversal (where chest leads V1-V6 retain normal R-wave growth), true mirror-image dextrocardia exhibits BOTH inverted Lead I/upright aVR AND reverse precordial R-wave progression (R amplitude decreases from V1 to V6 as the heart points to the right). Repeating the ECG with RA/LA swapped and precordial leads placed across the right hemithorax (V1 at 4th ICS left sternal border -\u003e V2 right sternal border -\u003e V4R-V6R) normalizes waveforms so ischemia can be assessed accurately.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01510","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Special ECG Procedures","topic":"Absolute Contraindications to TMT","q":"Before placing a patient onto the treadmill for a Bruce protocol Exercise Stress Test (TMT), the technician checks the resting baseline ECG and clinical referral. Which clinical condition is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to performing an exercise treadmill test?","o":["Stable chronic effort angina \u003e6 months after uncomplicated stenting","Controlled essential hypertension (BP 132/82 mmHg) on oral amlodipine","Mild asymptomatic first-degree AV block (PR 0.22 s)","Acute myocardial infarction within the past 2 days, high-risk unstable angina, severe symptomatic aortic stenosis, or acute aortic dissection"],"a":3,"e":"Per ACC/AHA Exercise Testing guidelines, absolute contraindications to TMT include acute MI (\u003c2 days), ongoing unstable angina, uncontrolled cardiac arrhythmias with hemodynamic compromise, severe symptomatic aortic stenosis, decompensated heart failure, acute pulmonary embolism/myocarditis/pericarditis, and acute aortic dissection—because exercise surges myocardial O2 demand and precipitates sudden cardiac arrest or rupture.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01511","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Electrocardiography Basics","topic":"Standard Paper Speed and Voltage Equivalents","q":"On standard ECG graph paper recorded at 25 mm/sec paper speed and 10 mm/mV gain calibration, what duration and voltage does ONE small 1 mm x 1 mm box represent?","o":["0.04 seconds (40 ms) horizontally and 0.1 mV vertically (so 5 small boxes = 0.20 s and 0.5 mV)","0.01 seconds horizontally and 0.5 mV vertically","0.20 seconds (200 ms) horizontally and 1.0 mV vertically","1.0 second horizontally and 10.0 mV vertically"],"a":0,"e":"At standard 25 mm/sec horizontal speed, 1 second equals 25 mm (5 large 5 mm boxes), so each large box = 0.20 s (200 ms) and each small 1 mm box = 0.04 s (40 ms). At standard 10 mm/mV vertical gain, 10 small boxes = 1.0 mV, so each small 1 mm box = 0.1 mV and each large 5 mm box = 0.5 mV.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01512","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Arrhythmia Recognition","topic":"Accelerated Idioventricular Rhythm (AIVR)","q":"Thirty minutes after successful primary PCI / thrombolysis for an acute inferior STEMI, telemetry shows a regular wide-complex rhythm (QRS 140 ms) at a rate of 76 beats/min with AV dissociation and fusion beats, while the patient remains completely comfortable and normotensive (BP 124/78 mmHg). What rhythm is this, and why is aggressive antiarrhythmic suppression (e.g., lidocaine) usually AVOIDED?","o":["Sinus tachycardia with normal narrow QRS","Accelerated Idioventricular Rhythm (AIVR, rate ~50-110/min)—a benign marker of successful coronary reperfusion that typically resolves spontaneously without antiarrhythmic suppression","Third-degree complete heart block at 28/min requiring atropine bolus","Rapid malignant Ventricular Tachycardia (\u003e180/min) requiring immediate unsynchronized 200 J shock"],"a":1,"e":"AIVR (\u0027slow VT\u0027) arises from enhanced ventricular automaticity at rates between 50 and 110 bpm (faster than an idioventricular escape at 20-40/min, slower than true VT \u003e100-120/min). Commonly seen shortly after coronary reperfusion in acute MI, AIVR is hemodynamically stable and transient; suppressing the ventricular pacemaker with lidocaine/amiodarone if underlying sinus/AV conduction is slow can precipitate asystole.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01513","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ST-Segment Differential Diagnosis","topic":"Benign Early Repolarization (BER)","q":"A routine pre-employment ECG of an asymptomatic 23-year-old marathon runner shows 1.5 mm concave-upward ST-segment elevation in V3-V5 with a distinct \u0027fish-hook\u0027 notch at the J point (junction of QRS and ST segment), tall upright T waves, and NO reciprocal ST depression anywhere. What normal physiological variant does this represent?","o":["Left bundle branch block","Acute hyperkalemia sine wave","Benign Early Repolarization (BER)","Acute anterior wall transmural STEMI"],"a":2,"e":"Benign Early Repolarization is a common normal ECG variant in healthy young adults and athletes, displaying concave-upward ST elevation (most prominent in V2-V5), characteristic J-point slurring or \u0027fish-hook\u0027 notching, symmetric tall concordant T waves, and absence of reciprocal ST depression or evolving Q waves (whereas acute STEMI typically shows dynamic convex ST changes and reciprocal depression).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01514","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Pacemaker ECG Interpretation","topic":"Three-Letter / Five-Letter NBG Code","q":"In the standardized NBG pacemaker nomenclature code (Position I: chamber paced; Position II: chamber sensed; Position III: response to sensing), what does \u0027VVI\u0027 signify?","o":["Ventricle paced, Ventricle sensed, Inhibited by a sensed intrinsic ventricular beat","Vena cava paced, Valve sensed, Immediately shocked","Both Atrium and Ventricle paced continuously regardless of intrinsic beats","Atrium paced, Ventricle sensed, Triggered pacing only"],"a":0,"e":"In the NBG code: Position I = chamber(s) paced (A, V, D = dual); Position II = chamber(s) sensed (A, V, D, O = none); Position III = response to sensed event (I = inhibited, T = triggered, D = dual). Thus VVI paces the ventricle on demand when intrinsic ventricular activity is absent, senses intrinsic ventricular beats, and inhibits firing when the patient\u0027s own QRS appears.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01515","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Electrocardiography Instrumentation","topic":"Reducing Impedance and Avoiding Mixed Electrode Gels","q":"While setting up continuous cardiac telemetry or a 12-lead ECG, why should the technician wipe oily skin/body lotion with alcohol (letting it dry), lightly abrade dry dead stratum corneum, and NEVER mix disposable Ag/AgCl electrodes from two different brands or dried-out open pouches?","o":["Dried-out gel patches record higher-resolution P waves","Wet alcohol under the sticker improves electrical conductivity permanently","Mixing two electrode brands improves precordial voltage by 200%","Removing oil/dead skin lowers high skin-electrode impedance, while mixing dissimilar electrode metals/gel compositions creates unequal half-cell polarization potentials that cause severe baseline wander and false ST shifts"],"a":3,"e":"High epidermal impedance from lotions, sweat, or dry keratin creates noise and baseline wander; cleaning/gentle prepping and allowing alcohol to evaporate gives clean contact. Using mixed electrode manufacturers or dried gel alters half-cell electrochemical offset potentials between leads, driving amplifier saturation and severe baseline drift.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01516","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Pediatric Electrocardiography","topic":"Right Ventricular Hypertrophy (RVH) in Children","q":"On a 12-lead ECG of a 2-year-old child with suspected Tetralogy of Fallot, which finding in Lead V1 is a classic, high-specificity indicator of pathologic Right Ventricular Hypertrophy (RVH) rather than normal childhood physiology?","o":["An UPRIGHT positive T wave in Lead V1 (coupled with abnormal qR or pure tall R pattern in V1 and persistent right axis deviation)","Normal sinus arrhythmia varying with respiration","Inverted negative T waves across V1, V2, and V3","Leftward QRS axis of +45 degrees"],"a":0,"e":"After the first week of life (day 7) until adolescence (~7-12 years), the T wave in Lead V1 is physiologically INVERTED (\u0027juvenile pattern\u0027). An upright positive T wave in V1 between 1 week and ~6-8 years of age—especially paired with a qR pattern, monophasic tall R \u003e98th percentile in V1, or persistent RAD—strongly indicates pathologic RV pressure overload/hypertrophy (e.g., Tetralogy of Fallot, severe pulmonary stenosis, PPHN).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01517","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Clinical ECG Patterns \u0026 Metabolic Emergencies","topic":"Hyperkalemia ECG Progression","q":"A chronic kidney disease patient who missed two dialysis sessions arrives weak in the ED. His 12-lead ECG reveals tall, symmetric, narrow-based \"tented\" T waves across the precordial leads, flattened P waves, and a widening QRS complex (160 ms). What electrolyte crisis do these findings indicate, and what is the technician’s immediate duty?","o":["Mild hypocalcemia; schedule routine outpatient echocardiography next month","Severe hyperkalemia progressing toward sine-wave pattern and cardiac arrest; keep patient on continuous monitor/defibrillator and alert the emergency physician STAT (for IV Calcium gluconate membrane stabilization)","Normal athletic variant; discharge patient home immediately","Hypokalemia; ignore the T waves because U waves are absent"],"a":1,"e":"Hyperkalemia produces a classic progressive ECG sequence: tall narrow peaked T waves -\u003e PR prolongation and loss of P waves -\u003e progressive QRS widening -\u003e sine-wave merging of QRS and T -\u003e VF/asystole. Because QRS widening signifies imminent cardiac arrest, immediate STAT physician alert for IV calcium gluconate (plus insulin-dextrose/albuterol/dialysis) is life-saving.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01518","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ECG Measurement \u0026 Rhythm Analysis","topic":"QRS Frontal Axis Quadrant Method","q":"Using the rapid frontal-plane quadrant method on a 12-lead ECG, a patient’s QRS complex is predominantly POSITIVE (upright) in Lead I, predominantly NEGATIVE (deep S \u003e R) in Lead aVF, and predominantly NEGATIVE in Lead II. How is the QRS frontal axis classified?","o":["Normal QRS axis (-30 to +90 degrees)","Right Axis Deviation (+90 to +180 degrees)","Pathological Left Axis Deviation (axis more negative than -30 degrees)","Extreme / Northwest axis (-90 to ±180 degrees)"],"a":2,"e":"When Lead I is net positive (0° pole) and Lead aVF is net negative (away from +90°), the axis lies between 0° and -90°. Checking Lead II (+60°) distinguishes physiological/normal (-30° to 0°, where Lead II remains positive) from true pathological Left Axis Deviation (\u003c-30°, where Lead II is predominantly negative, e.g., left anterior fascicular block).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01519","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Electrocardiographic Anatomy \u0026 Lead Systems","topic":"Posterior Chest Leads (V7, V8, V9)","q":"A chest-pain patient’s standard 12-lead ECG shows deep horizontal ST-segment depression, tall R waves, and upright T waves in Leads V1–V3 without ST elevation elsewhere. Suspecting acute posterior wall STEMI, where should the ECG technician place posterior leads V7, V8, and V9?","o":["All in the 5th intercostal space horizontal plane: V7 at left posterior axillary line, V8 at left midscapular line (inferior scapular tip), and V9 at left paraspinal border","Along the right anterior clavicular border at the 2nd intercostal space","On the dorsal surfaces of both wrists and left ankle","At the 8th intercostal space bilaterally along the posterior lumbar spine"],"a":0,"e":"Anterior V1–V3 ST depression with tall R waves can represent the mirror-image reciprocal of acute posterior STEMI. Placing V7 (5th ICS left posterior axillary line), V8 (5th ICS left midscapular line), and V9 (5th ICS left paraspinal border)—often swapping V4–V6 cables and relabeling the strip—directly detects \u003e=0.5 mm ST elevation diagnostic of posterior STEMI.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01520","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"ECG Measurement \u0026 Rhythm Analysis","topic":"QTc Interval Calculation (Bazett Formula)","q":"On a standard 25 mm/s ECG tracing, a patient receiving IV haloperidol has a measured QT interval of 11 small boxes (0.44 seconds) and a regular R-R interval of 16 small boxes (0.64 seconds). Using Bazett’s formula (QTc = QT / √RR), what is the QTc interval?","o":["688 ms (0.688 s)","352 ms (0.352 s — short QT)","440 ms (0.44 s — normal)","550 ms (0.55 s — critically prolonged \u003e 500 ms)"],"a":3,"e":"At 25 mm/s, 1 small box = 0.04 s: QT = 11 × 0.04 = 0.44 s (440 ms), and RR = 16 × 0.04 = 0.64 s. Taking √0.64 = 0.80 s, Bazett’s QTc = 0.44 / 0.80 = 0.55 s = 550 ms. Values \u003e 500 ms indicate severe repolarization delay with high risk of Torsades de Pointes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01521","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Stress Testing (TMT) \u0026 Ambulatory Monitoring","topic":"24-Hour Holter Hookup \u0026 Patient Instructions","q":"When hooking up a 5-lead 24-hour ambulatory Holter ECG monitor for a patient with intermittent palpitations, which combination of technical preparation and patient counseling ensures a clean, interpretable recording?","o":["Clip chest hair if needed, abrade/clean skin with alcohol and let dry, place electrodes over stable bony/intercostal landmarks avoiding large pectoral muscle bellies, tape stress loops on lead wires, instruct patient to keep a symptom/activity diary and press the event button during symptoms without bathing/showering","Tell the patient to remove all chest electrodes during sleep and reconnect them in the morning","Apply thick ultrasound gel under standard reusable limb clamps and wrap tightly with crepe bandages","Instruct the patient to swim and take hot tub baths while wearing the non-waterproof Holter unit"],"a":0,"e":"Holter recordings fail most often from motion artifact and loose leads. Proper skin abrasion lowers impedance, placing leads away from thick muscle bellies reduces EMG tremor, strain-relief tape loops prevent tugging, and pairing event-button presses with a written time-stamped symptom diary allows accurate rhythm-symptom correlation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01522","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Clinical ECG Patterns \u0026 Arrhythmias","topic":"Failure to Capture vs Failure to Sense","q":"A patient with a permanent VVI ventricular pacemaker reports recurrent dizziness. His 12-lead ECG reveals regular vertical pacemaker spikes at 70/min that are NOT followed by any QRS complex or T wave, while an independent slow native escape rhythm occurs at 36/min. Which pacemaker malfunction is present?","o":["Oversensing — pacemaker inhibits all spikes completely","Failure to capture (loss of capture) — pacing stimuli fail to depolarize the myocardium (requiring immediate clinician escalation)","Runaway atrial tachycardia","Normal dual-chamber DDD tracking"],"a":1,"e":"In failure to capture, the pacemaker fires an electrical spike (visible vertical line on ECG), but the myocardium fails to depolarize (no wide paced QRS/T follows), due to lead dislodgement, exit block, battery depletion, hyperkalemia, or myocardial ischemia. Failure to sense (undersensing), by contrast, fires spikes inappropriately right after or atop native QRS/T waves.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01523","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Clinical ECG Patterns \u0026 Arrhythmias","topic":"Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) Pattern","q":"A 22-year-old athlete’s resting 12-lead ECG in sinus rhythm shows a short PR interval (0.09 s), a slurred initial upstroke at the beginning of the QRS complex (\"delta wave\"), and a widened QRS duration (0.14 s). Which diagnosis explains this ECG, and why is flagging it clinically critical if he later develops atrial fibrillation?","o":["Left anterior fascicular block; treat immediately with IV verapamil","Sinus arrhythmia; completely benign with no arrhythmia risk","Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) pre-excitation via an accessory pathway (Bundle of Kent); if atrial fibrillation develops, AV nodal blockers (adenosine, verapamil, diltiazem, digoxin) can precipitate ventricular fibrillation by accelerating conduction down the accessory pathway","First-degree AV block; administer high-dose IV digoxin"],"a":2,"e":"WPW syndrome features the triad of short PR (\u003c0.12 s), slurred QRS upstroke (delta wave) from early ventricular pre-excitation across the Bundle of Kent, and wide QRS (\u003e=0.12 s). In pre-excited AF, blocking the AV node channels 300+ atrial impulses/min directly down the accessory pathway with no decremental delay, risking degenerate VF (requiring procainamide or cardioversion instead).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01524","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Clinical ECG Patterns \u0026 Arrhythmias","topic":"Left vs Right Bundle Branch Block (LBBB vs RBBB)","q":"On a 12-lead ECG with QRS duration \u003e= 0.12 seconds (3 small boxes) in sinus rhythm, which precordial lead findings specifically distinguish complete Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB) from Right Bundle Branch Block (RBBB)?","o":["LBBB: tall rSR’ in V1 with narrow QRS \u003c 0.08 s; RBBB: deep Q waves in V5–V6","LBBB and RBBB show identical tall upright R waves in V1 and deep S waves in V6","LBBB: absent P waves with sawtooth baseline; RBBB: short PR \u003c 0.10 s","LBBB: deep broad QS or rS in V1 with broad, notched/monophasic R wave (no Q waves) in I, aVL, V5–V6; RBBB: rSR’ (\"rabbit ears\") in V1 with wide slurred S waves in I and V6"],"a":3,"e":"Complete bundle branch blocks require QRS \u003e= 120 ms in supraventricular rhythm. Using the classic \"WiLLiaM / MaRRoW\" mnemonic: LBBB shows a negative \"W\" (QS/rS) in V1 and broad notched \"M\" R-wave in V6 (lateral leads lose normal septal Q waves); RBBB shows an \"M\" (rSR’) in V1 and slurred terminal S wave (\"W\") in I and V6.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01525","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Clinical ECG Patterns \u0026 Acute Coronary Syndromes","topic":"Acute Pericarditis vs Acute STEMI","q":"A 29-year-old man with sharp pleuritic chest pain relieved by leaning forward has an ECG showing widespread concave-upward (\"saddle-shaped\") ST-segment elevation across Leads I, II, aVL, aVF, and V2–V6, accompanied by diffuse PR-segment depression (and PR elevation in aVR), with NO reciprocal ST depression. Which condition produces this pattern?","o":["Acute pericarditis (Stage I)","Wellens Type A proximal LAD critical stenosis","Right bundle branch block","Acute localized inferior wall STEMI"],"a":0,"e":"Acute pericarditis causes diffuse epicardial and atrial inflammation across non-coronary territories: widespread concave-upward ST elevation in almost all leads (except aVR and V1) paired with PR-segment depression (PR elevation in aVR) and Spodick’s sign (downsloping TP segment), lacking the territorial convex ST elevation and reciprocal ST depression typical of STEMI.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01526","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Conduction Abnormalities","topic":"Left Anterior Fascicular Block (LAFB)","q":"Which ECG criteria establish isolated Left Anterior Fascicular Block (LAFB) in the absence of inferior myocardial infarction or LVH?","o":["Normal axis (+45 degrees) with delta waves in all leads","Marked Left Axis Deviation (-45 degrees to -90 degrees), small qR pattern in Leads I and aVL, small rS pattern in Leads II, III, and aVF, and QRS duration \u003c 120 ms","Marked Right Axis Deviation (+120 degrees), tall R in V1, and QRS 160 ms","PR interval 0.30 seconds with dropped beats"],"a":1,"e":"Blockade of the thin left anterior fascicle forces initial depolarization inferiorly/rightward via the posterior fascicle (producing initial small r waves in II/III/aVF and small q waves in I/aVL), followed by delayed superior/leftward activation (tall R in I/aVL, deep S in II/III/aVF)—shifting the mean QRS axis left of -45 degrees while keeping QRS duration \u003c120 ms (usually 90-110 ms).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01527","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Arrhythmia Recognition","topic":"Multifocal Atrial Tachycardia (MAT)","q":"A 68-year-old man with severe COPD exacerbation and hypoxia has an irregularly irregular narrow-complex tachycardia at 126 beats/min. Close inspection of Lead II reveals clear discrete P waves preceding every QRS, with at least THREE distinct P-wave shapes/axes, varying PR intervals, and an isoelectric baseline between waves. What rhythm is this?","o":["Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia with fixed PR interval","Multifocal Atrial Tachycardia (MAT)","Typical counter-clockwise atrial flutter"],"a":2,"e":"Multifocal Atrial Tachycardia (MAT) is defined by ventricular rate \u003e100/min, \u003e=3 distinct P-wave morphologies in a single lead, irregular PP/PR/RR intervals, and a flat isoelectric baseline—driven by triggered/abnormal automaticity across multiple irritable atrial foci secondary to hypoxia, hypercapnia, hypokalemia/hypomagnesemia, or theophylline/beta-agonist toxicity in severe COPD. Treatment targets the underlying pulmonary/electrolyte crisis (NOT cardioversion).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01528","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Myocardial Ischemia and Infarction ECG","topic":"Sgarbossa Criteria in LBBB","q":"Normally, complete Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB) exhibits \u0027appropriate discordance\u0027 (ST segments shifting opposite to the terminal QRS deflection). According to the Sgarbossa (and Smith-modified Sgarbossa) criteria, which ECG finding in a chest-pain patient with LBBB strongly indicates acute coronary occlusion (STEMI equivalent)?","o":["Appropriate mild discordant ST elevation of 1.5 mm in V1-V2 opposite a 25 mm deep S wave","Isolated notched broad R wave in V6 without concordant ST shifts","Heart rate 72/min with normal PR 0.16 s","Concordant ST-segment elevation \u003e= 1 mm in a lead with a positive QRS complex, OR concordant ST-segment depression \u003e= 1 mm in V1-V3, OR excessively discordant ST elevation (\u003e= 5 mm or \u003e= 25% of preceding S-wave depth)"],"a":3,"e":"Because uncomplicated LBBB creates baseline secondary ST-T discordance mimicking ischemia, the Sgarbossa criteria identify true superimposed acute myocardial infarction: (1) concordant ST elevation \u003e=1 mm in leads with positive QRS (5 pts), (2) concordant ST depression \u003e=1 mm in V1, V2, or V3 (3 pts), and (3) discordant ST elevation \u003e=5 mm (2 pts; or Smith-modified ST/S ratio \u003c= -0.25, improving sensitivity/specificity for emergency PCI activation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01529","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Electrocardiography Instrumentation","topic":"Amputees and Torso-Placed Limb Leads","q":"When recording a 12-lead ECG on a patient with bilateral lower-limb below-knee amputations and heavy forearm casts/bandages preventing wrist/ankle electrode placement, where should the limb electrodes be placed, and what documentation is essential?","o":["Place the four limb electrodes symmetrically on the upper arms/shoulders (infraclavicular fossae) and lower abdomen/hips (anterior iliac crests) equidistant from the heart, and clearly mark the printout \u0027Modified Torso Limb Leads\u0027","Place both leg electrodes onto the chest right next to V1 and V2","Leave the LL and RL cables disconnected in midair","Wrap electrodes loosely over the dry plaster casts"],"a":0,"e":"Standard limb leads follow Einthoven\u0027s equilateral triangle because limbs act largely as simple volume-conductor extensions. In bilateral amputees, tremors, or cast-covered limbs, placing RA/LA on the deltoid/infraclavicular shoulders and RL/LL on the lower torso/anterior iliac crests (Mason-Likar torso modification) acquires clean signals—but because torso placement can slightly shift frontal QRS axis and mask minor inferior Q waves, the technician MUST document the modified placement on the ECG.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01530","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Electrolyte ECG Patterns","topic":"Hypocalcemia vs Hypercalcemia","q":"How do severe HYPOCALCEMIA versus severe HYPERCALCEMIA characteristically alter the ST segment and corrected QT (QTc) interval on a 12-lead ECG?","o":["Hypocalcemia prolongs the QTc interval by stretching out a long flat isoelectric ST segment (normal narrow T wave); Hypercalcemia shortens the QTc interval so the T wave begins almost immediately after the QRS","Hypocalcemia shortens QTc to 250 ms; Hypercalcemia prolongs QTc to 650 ms","Both conditions eliminate all P waves and produce tall U waves","Calcium levels never affect cardiac action potential plateau phase 2 or QT duration"],"a":0,"e":"Extracellular calcium governs the duration of Phase 2 (slow inward Ca2+ plateau) of the ventricular myocyte action potential. Low serum Ca2+ delays Phase 2 closure, prolonging the flat ST segment and lengthening QTc (unlike hypokalemia, which creates prominent U waves and T-flattening). High serum Ca2+ accelerates Phase 2 repolarization, pulling the T wave right onto the QRS and shortening QTc (sometimes producing J-point Osborn-like notches).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01531","course":"Diploma in ECG Technology","subject":"Special ECG Procedures","topic":"24-Hour Holter vs Extended Event/Patch Loop Recorder","q":"A 38-year-old woman reports brief unexplained episodes of sudden racing palpitations and near-syncope occurring only ONCE every 10 to 14 days, with normal resting 12-lead ECG and echocardiogram. Why is a 14-to-30-day External Loop Recorder / Adhesive Patch Monitor superior to a standard 24-hour Holter monitor here?","o":["A 24-hour Holter has very low diagnostic yield for infrequent fortnightly symptoms, whereas a 14-to-30-day patch/loop recorder continuously captures retrospective-and-prospective rhythm strips when symptoms occur over weeks","A 24-hour Holter cannot record ventricular rates above 100/min","Patch loop recorders record coronary artery angiograms continuously","A 24-hour Holter only works on sleeping hospitalized patients"],"a":0,"e":"Ambulatory ECG selection matches device window to symptom frequency: daily/frequent symptoms suit a 24-48 hour continuous Holter monitor; weekly/monthly episodes (\u003c1/week) have poor capture rates (\u003c10-15%) on 24-hour Holter and require a 14-day adhesive ECG patch or 30-day external loop recorder (or implantable loop recorder for rare syncope every few months).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01532","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Prehospital Trauma Care","topic":"Tension Pneumothorax","q":"An ambulance EMT attends a 28-year-old motorcyclist with blunt chest trauma who has severe respiratory distress, BP 74/48 mmHg, distended neck veins, tracheal deviation to the left, and absent breath sounds over the right hemithorax. What is the immediate life-saving intervention before transport?","o":["Immediate needle decompression of the right chest (2nd ICS midclavicular line or 4th/5th ICS anterior axillary line) followed by tube thoracostomy at hospital","Apply a tight circumferential elastic bandage around the right chest wall","Initiate rapid positive-pressure bag-valve-mask ventilation without chest decompression","Obtain a portable AP chest X-ray on scene before touching the chest"],"a":0,"e":"Hypotension with absent breath sounds, JVD, and contralateral tracheal shift is a clinical diagnosis of tension pneumothorax—never delay decompression for radiography. Immediate needle thoracostomy (or finger thoracostomy per ATLS 10th edition: 4th/5th ICS anterior-to-midaxillary line in adults, or 2nd ICS MCL) converts tension into a simple pneumothorax until formal chest tube insertion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01533","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Cardiac Life Support","topic":"Adult Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation","q":"According to standard adult Basic Life Support (BLS) guidelines, what are the recommended chest compression depth, rate, and compression-to-ventilation ratio for a single rescuer before an advanced airway is placed?","o":["Depth 4 cm, rate 60-80/min, continuous compressions without rescue breaths","Depth 5-6 cm (2-2.4 inches), rate 100-120/min, ratio 30:2","Depth \u003e7 cm, rate \u003e140/min, ratio 5:1","Depth 2-3 cm, rate 80-90/min, ratio 15:2"],"a":1,"e":"High-quality adult CPR requires compressing the lower half of the sternum at least 5 cm (2 inches) and not exceeding 6 cm (2.4 inches), at 100-120 compressions/min, allowing full chest recoil, minimizing interruptions, and delivering 30 compressions to 2 breaths prior to advanced airway placement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01534","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Disaster Management and Triage","topic":"START Triage System","q":"At a highway bus crash mass casualty scene, an EMT assesses a 35-year-old passenger who cannot walk, has a respiratory rate of 36/min, capillary refill of 1.5 seconds, and obeys simple commands. Using the START (Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment) algorithm, which triage tag color should be assigned?","o":["Black (Expectant / Deceased)","Yellow (Delayed)","Red (Immediate)","Green (Minor)"],"a":2,"e":"In the START adult triage algorithm: Step 1 checks ambulation (unable to walk -\u003e not Green). Step 2 checks respirations (spontaneous breathing \u003e30/min immediately classifies the patient as Red / Immediate priority without needing further delay, as tachypnea \u003e30/min signals acute respiratory or shock compromise).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01535","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Burns and Fluid Resuscitation","topic":"Parkland Formula Calculation","q":"A 70 kg adult sustains partial- and full-thickness flame burns over the entire anterior trunk (18%) and entire right upper limb (9%), totaling 27% TBSA, arriving in the ED 1 hour after injury. Using the classic Parkland formula (4 mL/kg/%TBSA of Ringer\u0027s Lactate), how much fluid should be infused over the remaining 7 hours of the first 8-hour window?","o":["1890 mL total in first 8 hours (~270 mL/hr over the remaining 7 hours)","2700 mL total in first 8 hours (~385 mL/hr over the remaining 7 hours)","7560 mL total in first 8 hours (~1080 mL/hr over the remaining 7 hours)","3780 mL total in first 8 hours (~540 mL/hr over the remaining 7 hours)"],"a":3,"e":"Total 24-hour Parkland requirement = 4 mL x 70 kg x 27% = 7560 mL. Half of this (3780 mL) is delivered in the first 8 hours calculated from the time of burn injury (not hospital arrival). Since 1 hour has elapsed, 3780 mL is given over the remaining 7 hours (3780 / 7 = 540 mL/hr), adjusted to urine output 0.5 mL/kg/hr.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01536","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Medical Emergencies","topic":"Snakebite and Organophosphate Poisoning","q":"A 40-year-old farmer presents after ingesting monocrotophos (organophosphate insecticide) with pinpoint pupils, profuse bronchorrhea, bilateral wheezes, bradycardia (44/min), and fasciculations. After airway suctioning and initiating IV atropine boluses, which clinical sign defines adequate atropinization?","o":["Clear lungs on auscultation with cessation of bronchorrhea, dry axillae, heart rate \u003e80/min, and systolic BP \u003e80 mmHg","Heart rate \u003e160/min with urinary retention and delirium","Isolated pupillary dilation (mydriasis) even if coarse lung crackles persist","Complete cessation of skeletal muscle fasciculations using atropine alone"],"a":0,"e":"Muscarinic respiratory secretions (bronchorrhea/bronchospasm) are the primary cause of death in acute OP poisoning. Adequate atropinization endpoints are clear chest (no crackles/rhonchi), heart rate \u003e80 bpm, systolic BP \u003e80 mmHg, dry axillae, and dilated pupils—with lung clearance being the most critical therapeutic target.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01537","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Medical Emergencies","topic":"Anaphylaxis Management","q":"Minutes after receiving an antibiotic injection in a primary clinic, a 25-year-old woman (60 kg) develops generalized urticaria, hoarseness, inspiratory stridor, wheezing, and BP 78/48 mmHg. What is the immediate first-line drug, dose, concentration, and route?","o":["Salbutamol nebulization alone without epinephrine","Epinephrine 0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1:1000 / 1 mg/mL) intramuscularly into the mid-anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis)","Epinephrine 1:10,000 subcutaneously into the deltoid muscle","Chlorpheniramine 10 mg IV followed by hydrocortisone 200 mg IV as sole initial drugs"],"a":1,"e":"Intramuscular epinephrine (0.01 mg/kg, max 0.5 mg per dose in adults, i.e., 0.3-0.5 mL of 1:1000 concentration) injected into the mid-anterolateral thigh is the mandatory, life-saving first-line therapy for anaphylaxis, achieving rapid peak plasma levels to reverse bronchospasm, laryngeal edema, and vasodilation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01538","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Neurological Emergencies","topic":"Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)","q":"During primary assessment of a head-injured patient, the EMT observes that the patient opens his eyes only to painful nailbed pressure, utters incomprehensible moaning sounds without words, and withdraws his arm briskly away from a painful stimulus (no localization). What is the patient\u0027s total Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score?","o":["GCS 9 (E2 + V2 + M5)","GCS 10 (E3 + V3 + M4)","GCS 8 (E2 + V2 + M4)","GCS 7 (E1 + V2 + M4)"],"a":2,"e":"Standard GCS components: Eye opening to pain = E2 (spontaneous=4, speech=3, pain=2, none=1); Verbal response with incomprehensible sounds/moans = V2 (oriented=5, confused=4, inappropriate words=3, incomprehensible sounds=2, none=1); Motor normal flexion/withdrawal from pain = M4 (obeys=6, localizes=5, normal flexion/withdrawal=4, abnormal flexion=3, extension=2, none=1). Total = 2 + 2 + 4 = 8 (indicating severe TBI/coma threshold requiring airway protection consideration).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01539","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Prehospital Trauma Care","topic":"Tourniquet and Pelvic Binder Application","q":"A pedestrian struck by a truck has pelvic instability, perineal bruising, and hemorrhagic shock (BP 76/44 mmHg). When applying a commercial pelvic circumferential compression device (pelvic binder) in the ambulance, over which exact anatomical landmarks must the binder be centered?","o":["Directly over the bilateral greater trochanters of the femurs and pubic symphysis","High around the iliac crests and lower lumbar spine","Mid-abdomen over the umbilicus to compress the abdominal aorta","Around the distal femurs just above the knee joints"],"a":0,"e":"An open-book pelvic fracture causes life-threatening retroperitoneal venous and bone hemorrhage. Centering the pelvic binder over the greater trochanters (not the iliac crests) provides the optimal biomechanical lever arm to reduce pelvic ring volume, tamponade bleeding, and stabilize fracture fragments, often paired with gentle internal rotation taping of the legs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01540","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Neurological Emergencies","topic":"BE-FAST and Stroke Mimics","q":"An ambulance crew responds to a 64-year-old diabetic man who suddenly developed right facial droop, slurred speech, and right arm weakness 45 minutes ago. Before rapid transport to a thrombolysis-capable stroke center, what mandatory bedside point-of-care test must the EMT perform immediately?","o":["Oral aspirin 325 mg administration on scene before neuroimaging","Serum lipid profile and HbA1c measurement","Rapid lowering of BP from 170/96 mmHg to 110/70 mmHg using sublingual nifedipine","Point-of-care capillary blood glucose measurement to rule out hypoglycemia as an acute stroke mimic"],"a":3,"e":"Hypoglycemia frequently mimics acute focal stroke (hemiparesis, aphasia) and causes irreversible neuronal injury if untreated, while simultaneously being an exclusion check required before IV thrombolysis. Performing immediate bedside glucometry and documenting \u0027last known well\u0027 time are mandatory prehospital stroke steps.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01541","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Obstetric and Pediatric Emergencies","topic":"Shoulder Dystocia and Cord Prolapse","q":"During emergency ambulance transport of a multiparous woman at 38 weeks gestation whose membranes just ruptured, a pulsating umbilical cord prolapses into the vaginal introitus. What immediate actions must the EMT take while rushing to the obstetric hospital?","o":["Keep a sterile gloved hand in the vagina gently elevating the fetal presenting part off the cord, place the mother in knee-chest or exaggerated Trendelenburg position, give oxygen, and avoid pushing the cord back into the uterus","Forcefully push the protruding cord back up past the cervix and ask the mother to bear down","Apply downward fundal pressure to speed up vaginal delivery","Clamp and cut the pulsating umbilical cord immediately in the ambulance"],"a":0,"e":"Umbilical cord prolapse causes fatal fetal asphyxia from mechanical compression of the cord between the fetal head and maternal pelvis. Relieving pressure manually (sterile gloved fingers pushing the presenting part upward until emergency cesarean delivery) combined with knee-chest or steep Trendelenburg positioning and keeping the cord moist/warm preserves fetal oxygenation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01542","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Cardiac Life Support","topic":"Shockable vs Non-Shockable Rhythms","q":"During an ambulance cardiac arrest resuscitation, the AED/monitor rhythm check reveals organized narrow-complex electrical activity at 52/min, but palpation confirms NO carotid pulse (Pulseless Electrical Activity). What is the immediate ACLS priority?","o":["Deliver an immediate unsynchronized 200 J biphasic shock","Do NOT deliver a shock; resume immediate high-quality chest compressions, administer epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO every 3-5 minutes, and aggressively search for reversible H\u0027s and T\u0027s","Administer IV amiodarone 300 mg bolus before starting chest compressions","Stop resuscitation immediately because organized ECG waves mean the heart is beating"],"a":1,"e":"Pulseless Electrical Activity (PEA) and asystole are non-shockable arrest rhythms; defibrillation interrupts CPR needlessly and offers zero benefit. Immediate continuous CPR, early IV/IO epinephrine (1 mg every 3-5 min), and identifying reversible causes (Hypovolemia, Hypoxia, Hydrogen ion/acidosis, Hypo-/Hyperkalemia, Hypothermia; Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis pulmonary/coronary) are essential.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01543","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Environmental and Toxicological Emergencies","topic":"Indian Venomous Snakebite Protocol","q":"A farm laborer in rural Maharashtra is bitten on the right ankle by a Russell\u0027s viper and arrives at an ambulance stabilization point 20 minutes later. Which prehospital first-aid intervention is recommended under Indian MoHFW Standard Treatment Guidelines?","o":["Reassure the patient, immobilize the entire bitten limb gently with a splint at or slightly below heart level, remove constricting rings/anklets, and transport rapidly for Anti-Snake Venom (ASV)","Tie a tight arterial rope tourniquet above the knee to completely stop blood flow","Make deep cruciate incisions over the fang marks with a blade and suck out the venom","Pack the bitten ankle in crushed ice and give oral alcohol to calm pain"],"a":0,"e":"Indian MoHFW / WHO SEARO snakebite guidelines emphasize \u0027Do it R.I.G.H.T.\u0027: Reassurance, Immobilization (like a fractured limb, avoiding excessive movement that speeds lymphatic venom spread), Getting to hospital rapidly for polyvalent Anti-Snake Venom (ASV), and Telling the doctor about symptoms. Tight arterial tourniquets, incisions, suction, and ice cause tissue necrosis, severe bleeding (especially with viper venom coagulopathy), and gangrene.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01544","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Environmental and Toxicological Emergencies","topic":"Exertional Heat Stroke","q":"During an outdoor summer marathon, a 29-year-old runner collapses with core rectal temperature 41.2 degrees C, hot flushed skin, tachycardia 136/min, and acute delirium/disorientation. What defines this emergency and what is the primary goal of immediate care?","o":["Mild dehydration; administer antipyretic paracetamol 1,000 mg orally as sole therapy","Heat cramps; give oral salt tablets and let the runner walk off the field","Heat stroke (hyperthermia \u003e40 degrees C with CNS dysfunction); initiate immediate aggressive cooling (cold-water immersion or ice packs/evaporative misting + fans) targeting \u003c39 degrees C alongside airway/IV support","Heat exhaustion; keep the patient wrapped in warm blankets during slow transport"],"a":2,"e":"Core temperature \u003e40 degrees C (104 degrees F) accompanied by central nervous system dysfunction (confusion, seizures, coma) defines life-threatening heat stroke, distinguishing it from heat exhaustion (intact mental status). Immediate rapid cooling (\u0027cool first, transport second\u0027 when immersion/cooling equipment is on site) prevents multi-organ failure, rhabdomyolysis, and DIC.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01545","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Pediatric Emergencies","topic":"Intraosseous (IO) Access Landmarks","q":"A 2-year-old child in hypovolemic shock from severe gastroenteritis is unresponsive with failed peripheral IV attempts after 90 seconds. When inserting an emergency intraosseous (IO) needle into the preferred proximal tibia site, what is the exact anatomical landmark to avoid the epiphyseal growth plate?","o":["Anteromedial flat surface of the proximal tibia, 1 to 2 cm (one fingerbreadth) below and medial to the tibial tuberosity, directed perpendicular or slightly inferiorly away from the growth plate","Directly into the center of the knee joint space above the patella","Posterolateral aspect of the fibular head","Directly into the tibial tuberosity epiphysis angled 45 degrees upward toward the knee"],"a":0,"e":"Per PALS guidelines, when peripheral IV access is not achieved rapidly in critically ill children, intraosseous cannulation is indicated. The preferred pediatric site is the flat anteromedial cortex of the proximal tibia, 1-2 cm distal and medial to the tibial tuberosity, avoiding the proximal epiphyseal growth plate (angulating slightly caudad/away from the joint).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01546","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Environmental and Toxicological Emergencies","topic":"Carbon Monoxide Poisoning","q":"An EMT rescues a conscious family from a closed room with a faulty charcoal brazier (angithi) complaining of throbbing headache, dizziness, and nausea. Pulse oximetry reads 99% on room air. Why must immediate 100% FiO2 via a tight-fitting non-rebreather mask be started despite the 99% SpO2 reading?","o":["Carboxyhemoglobin turns pulse oximeters down to 50% immediately","Room air is sufficient if SpO2 is \u003e95% regardless of smoke exposure","100% oxygen causes fatal CO retention unless the patient is first given 21% air","Standard pulse oximeters misread carboxyhemoglobin as oxyhemoglobin (falsely normal ~99%); 100% oxygen competitively displaces CO from hemoglobin, slashing COHb elimination half-life from ~300 minutes to ~75 minutes"],"a":3,"e":"Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin with \u003e200x greater affinity than oxygen, blocking O2 delivery and shifting the oxyhemoglobin curve left. Because carboxyhemoglobin absorbs 660 nm red light similarly to oxyhemoglobin, standard two-wavelength SpO2 reads falsely normal (~98-100%). Immediate 100% normobaric oxygen drops COHb half-life from ~4-5 hours on room air to ~60-90 minutes (and ~20-30 min in hyperbaric O2).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01547","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Airway Emergencies","topic":"Choking Management in Infants vs Adults","q":"A 7-month-old infant suddenly chokes on a piece of banana, turning cyanotic with a silent ineffective cough and wide-eyed distress while still conscious. What is the immediate emergency maneuver?","o":["Deliver alternating cycles of 5 sharp back slaps (prone head-down over forearm) and 5 two-finger chest thrusts on the lower sternum until expelled or unresponsive","Perform forceful abdominal Heimlich thrusts below the xiphoid process","Hold the infant upside down by the ankles and shake vigorously","Insert a gloved index finger blindly down the pharynx to sweep the object out"],"a":0,"e":"In conscious choking infants (\u003c1 year), abdominal thrusts (Heimlich maneuver) are contraindicated because their large, unprotected liver and spleen sit below the ribcage and risk fatal rupture. Instead, support the jaw/head in a prone downward-angled position for 5 back blows between the scapulae, then flip supine for 5 chest thrusts (same landmark as infant CPR), avoiding blind finger sweeps.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01548","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Environmental and Toxicological Emergencies","topic":"Hypoxic Cardiac Arrest Sequence","q":"Lifeguards pull an unresponsive, apneic, pulseless 18-year-old swimmer from a river onto the riverbank. In submersion/drowning cardiac arrest (which is primarily hypoxic), how does resuscitation differ from standard adult sudden out-of-hospital cardiac arrest?","o":["Deliver compression-only hands-only CPR without rescue breaths","Prioritize immediate airway opening and rescue ventilations/oxygenation (A-B-C sequence with 2-5 initial breaths) alongside chest compressions, without wasting time on abdominal thrusts to drain lung water","Perform 5 minutes of Heimlich abdominal thrusts to empty swallowed stomach water before giving breaths","Delay CPR for 10 minutes until cervical spine X-rays are obtained"],"a":1,"e":"Drowning arrest is driven by asphyxial hypoxia rather than primary coronary ischemia. AHA/ERC drowning guidelines recommend opening the airway and delivering rescue breaths immediately (A-B-C traditional sequence with 100% O2 when available) combined with standard 30:2 CPR. Abdominal thrusts are NOT used to drain water because they fail to clear alveoli and trigger lethal gastric regurgitation/aspiration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01549","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Prehospital Trauma Care","topic":"ATLS Hemorrhagic Shock Classification","q":"A 32-year-old trauma victim with a closed femur fracture and splenic injury has HR 128/min, BP 86/62 mmHg (narrowed pulse pressure), respiratory rate 32/min, anxious/confused mental status, and urine output 10 mL/hr. According to ATLS hemorrhagic shock classification, which class of shock and estimated blood volume loss does this represent?","o":["Class II hemorrhage (15-30% loss) where systolic hypotension never occurs","Class IV hemorrhage (\u003e40% loss) characterized by normal pulse rate 70/min","Class III hemorrhage (31-40% blood volume loss; ~1,500-2,000 mL in a 70 kg adult) requiring balanced blood product transfusion plus hemostasis","Class I hemorrhage (\u003c15% loss; \u003c750 mL) needing oral hydration only"],"a":2,"e":"In ATLS 10th edition, Class III hemorrhagic shock (31-40% circulating volume loss) marks the threshold where compensatory vasoconstriction fails and systolic BP drops (\u003c90 mmHg), accompanied by tachycardia (\u003e120/min), tachypnea (30-35/min), oliguria (5-15 mL/hr), and confusion/anxiety, mandating immediate crystalloid moderation and packed RBC/balanced transfusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01550","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Cardiac Emergencies","topic":"Prehospital Aspirin and Nitroglycerin Safety","q":"An ambulance EMT attends a 59-year-old man with crushing substernal chest pain radiating to the jaw, BP 138/84 mmHg, HR 82/min, and anterior ST elevation on the 12-lead ECG obtained at 6 minutes. The patient reports taking sildenafil (Viagra) 8 hours earlier. Which medication should be GIVEN immediately, and which MUST BE WITHHELD?","o":["Give chewable non-enteric-coated aspirin (162-325 mg) immediately; WITHHOLD sublingual nitroglycerin due to recent PDE-5 inhibitor use","Give sublingual nitroglycerin 0.4 mg x 3 doses immediately; withhold aspirin","Give IV streptokinase in the moving ambulance without hospital notification","Withhold both aspirin and oxygen while delaying ECG until hospital arrival"],"a":0,"e":"Non-enteric-coated chewable aspirin (162-325 mg) reduces ACS mortality and should be given promptly if no allergy/active bleeding exists. However, nitrates (nitroglycerin) combined with phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors (sildenafil/vardenafil within 24 hrs or tadalafil within 48 hrs) cause synergistic cGMP-mediated vasodilation and refractory, potentially fatal hypotension.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01551","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Trauma and Burns","topic":"Deep Tissue Myonecrosis and Rhabdomyolysis","q":"A lineman touches a 11,000-volt overhead power cable and falls 3 meters onto grass. In the ambulance, he has only small 2 cm charred entry/exit wounds on his right palm and left foot, but passes dark tea-colored urine and shows premature ventricular contractions on telemetry. Why must this patient receive aggressive crystalloid resuscitation (targeting higher urine output 1-1.5 mL/kg/hr) and spinal immobilization rather than standard 2% TBSA burn care?","o":["Small skin wounds guarantee zero internal organ or muscle damage","Tea-colored urine indicates normal concentrated sweat only","High-voltage injuries cause purely superficial 1st-degree sunburn requiring no IV fluids","High-voltage current travels along low-resistance nerves/vessels/bone-muscle interfaces causing massive hidden deep myonecrosis, rhabdomyolysis (myoglobinuric AKI), ventricular arrhythmias, and potential fall/tetanic spinal fractures"],"a":3,"e":"External cutaneous burn TBSA severely underestimates deep thermal injury in high-voltage (\u003e1,000 V) electrocution (\u0027tip of the iceberg\u0027 effect). Deep muscle necrosis releases massive myoglobin (tea-colored urine) and potassium, risking acute tubular necrosis and arrhythmias, while fall trauma/tetanic contractions risk spine fractures—requiring spinal precautions, continuous ECG telemetry, and aggressive isotonic hydration targeting 1.0-1.5 mL/kg/hr urine output.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01552","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Neurological Emergencies","topic":"Prehospital Status Epilepticus","q":"An EMT arrives at a home where a 22-year-old man has been having continuous generalized tonic-clonic convulsions for 7 minutes without regaining consciousness (status epilepticus). What is the safest immediate prehospital management sequence?","o":["Protect the head from trauma, clear surrounding hazards, place in lateral recovery position once safe, suction secretions, give high-flow O2, check capillary blood glucose, and administer IM/IN midazolam (or IV lorazepam) per protocol","Pour cold water into his mouth and give oral phenytoin tablets immediately","Restrain all four limbs rigidly flat on his back to stop convulsions","Force a wooden tongue depressor or metal spoon forcefully between his clenched teeth"],"a":0,"e":"Status epilepticus (continuous seizure \u003e=5 minutes) is a time-critical neurological emergency. Prehospital priorities include protecting the patient from injury without restraining limbs, maintaining lateral airway drainage/suction, ruling out reversible hypoglycemia via bedside glucometry, and administering a first-line benzodiazepine (IM midazolam 10 mg if \u003e40 kg, intranasal midazolam, or IV lorazepam).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01553","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Prehospital Trauma Care","topic":"Open Pneumothorax (Sucking Chest Wound)","q":"A stab wound victim has a 3 cm defect on the right anterior chest wall that makes an audible sucking/bubbling sound with each inspiration, causing severe dyspnea. When applying a sterile petroleum gauze occlusive dressing in the ambulance, why is it taped on ONLY THREE sides (or replaced with a commercial vented chest seal)?","o":["To keep the wound dry for cosmetic healing","To act as a one-way flutter valve that blocks air from entering the pleural space during inhalation while allowing trapped pleural air to escape during exhalation, preventing conversion into a lethal tension pneumothorax","Because tape will not stick to the fourth side due to rib curvature","To allow room air to enter freely during inhalation"],"a":1,"e":"An open chest wound larger than ~2/3 the tracheal diameter equilibrates intrathoracic and atmospheric pressure (\u0027sucking chest wound\u0027), collapsing the lung. Taping a non-porous occlusive dressing on three sides (leaving the dependent edge untaped) or using a vented chest seal prevents external air entry on inspiration while letting positive-pressure expiratory air escape—whereas taping all four sides hermetically can trap ongoing parenchymal air leaks and precipitate tension pneumothorax.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01554","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Prehospital Trauma Care","topic":"Acute Cardiac Tamponade","q":"A 26-year-old man stabbed in the left parasternal 4th intercostal space arrives in shock (BP 76/54 mmHg), HR 132/min, markedly distended jugular neck veins, muffled distant heart sounds, and CLEAR equal bilateral breath sounds with midline trachea. What life-threatening condition is most likely?","o":["Simple superficial pectoral muscle laceration","Left-sided tension pneumothorax","Acute cardiac tamponade (Beck\u0027s triad)","Isolated massive left hemothorax"],"a":2,"e":"Penetrating anterior precordial trauma (\u0027cardiac box\u0027) presenting with Beck\u0027s triad—hypotension (narrow pulse pressure), elevated JVP/distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds—plus CLEAR, equal bilateral breath sounds and a midline trachea indicates acute pericardial tamponade restricting ventricular diastolic filling. Rapid trauma-center transport for FAST/echocardiography and pericardiocentesis or emergency thoracotomy is essential.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01555","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Obstetric Emergencies","topic":"Prehospital Eclamptic Seizure Management","q":"An ambulance is called for a 34-week pregnant woman actively seizing at home with BP 184/118 mmHg and 3+ pedal edema (eclampsia). Beyond airway suctioning, 100% oxygen, and preventing maternal trauma, how should she be positioned post-ictally and which anticonvulsant is specific first-line therapy?","o":["Prone face-down position; immediate IV phenytoin as sole agent","Flat supine position on her back; oral nifedipine during active convulsions","Trendelenburg head-down position; high-dose IV oxytocin infusion","Left lateral decubitus tilt (~15-30 degrees) to relieve inferior vena cava compression and prevent aspiration; Magnesium sulfate (4 g IV slow loading over 15-20 min or Pritchard 10 g IM)"],"a":3,"e":"In late pregnancy, lying flat supine compresses the inferior vena cava and aorta against the lumbar spine (supine hypotensive syndrome), slashing uteroplacental blood flow and worsening maternal shock. Left lateral tilt restores venous return and prevents aspiration, while parenteral magnesium sulfate is superior to phenytoin/diazepam for halting and preventing recurrent eclamptic seizures.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01556","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Toxicological Emergencies","topic":"Ventilation Priority and Titrated Naloxone","q":"An EMT finds a 27-year-old man unresponsive with pinpoint pupils (miosis), cyanotic lips, SpO2 76%, and slow shallow breathing at 4 breaths/min (palpable carotid pulse 64/min) beside empty morphine ampoules. What is the immediate priority sequence?","o":["Open the airway and immediately initiate assisted bag-valve-mask (BVM) ventilations with high-flow oxygen first, then administer naloxone (intranasal, IM, or IV) titrated to restore adequate spontaneous breathing","Start chest compressions immediately despite a strong carotid pulse","Administer IV flumazenil as first-line opioid antagonist","Inject a massive 10 mg IV bolus of naloxone before opening the airway or assisting breaths"],"a":0,"e":"In opioid overdose with a palpable pulse and severe bradypnea/apnea, hypoxic brain injury kills within minutes unless oxygenation and ventilation are restored immediately via jaw-thrust and BVM rescue breaths. Naloxone (0.4-2 mg IV/IM/IN, titrated to adequate respiratory rate \u003e=12/min rather than abrupt full wakefulness) reverses mu-opioid respiratory depression without precipitating violent acute withdrawal or pulmonary edema.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01557","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Environmental Emergencies","topic":"Gentle Handling and Rewarming Precautions","q":"A mountaineer rescued from snow exposure is stuporous with a core temperature of 27.5 degrees C, slow radial pulse 36/min, and weak slow respirations. Why must the EMS crew handle the patient with extreme gentleness during extrication and wet-clothing removal, avoiding rough jostling or cold extremity massage?","o":["Cold skin bones shatter on gentle touch","The cold myocardium (\u003c28-30 degrees C) is exquisitely irritable; rough physical movement or sudden return of cold acidic extremity blood (\u0027afterdrop\u0027) can precipitate refractory ventricular fibrillation","Gentle handling intentionally keeps core temperature below 25 degrees C","Massage melts ice crystals inside the carotid artery"],"a":1,"e":"Below 28-30 degrees C, myocardial conduction slows and the fibrillation threshold drops sharply. Rough movement, aggressive endotracheal jostling without preoxygenation, or vigorous rubbing of cold limbs flushes cold, acidotic peripheral blood centrally (\u0027core temperature afterdrop\u0027) and triggers refractory ventricular fibrillation. Remove wet clothes gently by cutting, insulate, warm the trunk actively, and apply the \u0027not dead until warm and dead\u0027 rule.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01558","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Basic \u0026 Advanced Cardiac Life Support","topic":"High-Quality Chest Compressions","q":"You find an unresponsive 55-year-old man on the roadside who is not breathing normally and has no carotid pulse after a 7-second check. While your partner activates EMS and fetches the AED, you initiate single-rescuer adult CPR. Which compression parameters meet AHA BLS standards?","o":["Rate 60–80 compressions/min, depth 2–3 cm, lean slightly on chest between strokes, 15:2 ratio","Deliver 2 minutes of rescue breaths alone before checking pulse again","Rate 100–120 compressions/min, depth 5–6 cm (2–2.4 inches), allow complete chest recoil between compressions, 30:2 compression-to-ventilation ratio, and keep interruptions \u003c 10 seconds","Rate 140–160 compressions/min, depth \u003e 8 cm, 5:1 compression-to-ventilation ratio"],"a":2,"e":"AHA 2020 Adult BLS guidelines specify chest compressions at 100–120/min, depth 5–6 cm (at least 2 inches, not \u003e2.4 inches), full chest wall recoil to permit venous return, minimal pauses (\u003c10 seconds), and a 30:2 ratio prior to advanced airway placement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01559","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Trauma Care \u0026 Prehospital Resuscitation","topic":"Tension Pneumothorax Decompression","q":"A 30-year-old blunt chest trauma patient arrives in severe respiratory distress with BP 76/48 mmHg, HR 132/min, SpO2 82%, distended neck veins, absent breath sounds and hyperresonance over the right hemithorax, and tracheal shift to the left. What is the immediate life-saving step?","o":["Perform immediate endotracheal intubation with high PEEP (15 cmH2O) without decompressing the chest","Order a portable supine chest X-ray and wait for radiology reporting before intervening","Administer 2 L rapid IV fluid bolus alone while placing the patient in left lateral position","Immediate needle decompression (4th or 5th intercostal space, anterior-to-midaxillary line in adults per ATLS 10th ed, or 2nd ICS midclavicular line per local protocol) followed by tube thoracostomy—do NOT delay for chest X-ray"],"a":3,"e":"Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis requiring immediate decompression during the Breathing (B) step of ATLS primary survey; delaying for radiography can result in obstructive cardiac arrest. ATLS 10th Edition recommends the 4th/5th ICS just anterior to the midaxillary line in adults (2nd ICS MCL remains an alternative, especially in children), followed promptly by formal chest tube insertion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01560","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Trauma Care \u0026 Prehospital Resuscitation","topic":"Life-Threatening Extremity Hemorrhage","q":"An industrial worker sustains a deep mid-thigh laceration with bright red pulsatile arterial bleeding that soaks through direct pressure dressings. You decide to apply a commercial windlass tourniquet. Where should it be positioned and tightened?","o":["Place 5–7 cm (2–3 inches) proximal to the wound (never over the knee/hip joint), tighten until bleeding stops completely and distal pulse disappears, secure the windlass, and record the exact time of application","Loosen the tourniquet for 5 minutes every 10 minutes even during active arterial bleeding","Place directly over the knee joint distal to the wound so venous return is slowed","Apply loosely enough that a strong pedal pulse remains palpable to prevent ischemia"],"a":0,"e":"For life-threatening extremity hemorrhage unresponsive to direct pressure, apply a wide commercial tourniquet 5–7 cm proximal to the bleeding site (avoiding joints), tighten until hemorrhage ceases and distal pulses vanish, lock the rod, and clearly mark the application time (e.g., on the strap or patient forehead) for the receiving surgical team.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01561","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Medical \u0026 Environmental Emergencies","topic":"Parkland Burn Resuscitation Formula","q":"A 70 kg adult arrives in the ED 1 hour after sustaining 40% Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) deep partial- and full-thickness flame burns. Using the classic Parkland formula (4 mL/kg/%TBSA of Ringer’s Lactate), what is the total 24-hour fluid volume, and how much should be infused during the FIRST 8 hours post-burn?","o":["2,800 mL total over 24 hours; infuse all 2,800 mL as a single 15-minute bolus","11,200 mL total over 24 hours; infuse 5,600 mL over the first 8 hours calculated from the time of burn (i.e., over the remaining 7 hours)","5,600 mL total over 24 hours; infuse 2,800 mL over the first 8 hours from ED arrival","11,200 mL total over 24 hours; infuse 2,800 mL in the first 8 hours and 8,400 mL in the next 16 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Parkland formula: 4 mL × 70 kg × 40 (%TBSA) = 11,200 mL of Ringer’s Lactate in the first 24 hours. Half (5,600 mL) is administered in the first 8 hours measured from the TIME OF INJURY (subtracting any prehospital fluid already given), and the remaining 5,600 mL over the subsequent 16 hours, titrated to urine output (0.5–1 mL/kg/hr in adults).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01562","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Disaster Management \u0026 Triage","topic":"START Triage Algorithm","q":"During a Mass Casualty Incident using the START (Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment) adult triage algorithm, a non-ambulatory victim has a respiratory rate of 24/min, a palpable radial pulse (capillary refill \u003c 2 seconds), and is unable to obey simple verbal commands (e.g., \"squeeze my fingers\"). Which triage tag color must be assigned?","o":["Green (Minor / Walking Wounded)","Black (Deceased / Expectant)","Red (Immediate / Priority 1)","Yellow (Delayed / Priority 2)"],"a":2,"e":"Under START triage (RPM: Respirations \u003e30, Perfusion cap refill \u003e2 s or absent radial pulse, Mental status unable to follow simple commands), failing ANY single physiologic threshold assigns the patient to Red (Immediate). Because this patient cannot follow simple commands, they are tagged Red despite normal respiratory rate and radial pulse.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01563","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Medical \u0026 Environmental Emergencies","topic":"Acute Anaphylaxis Management","q":"A 24-year-old man develops generalized urticaria, lip swelling, hoarse voice, wheezing, and BP 78/46 mmHg 10 minutes after multiple wasp stings. Which emergency intervention takes top priority?","o":["Nebulized Salbutamol as monotherapy without epinephrine","Subcutaneous Epinephrine 0.1 mg of 1:10,000 into the forearm after giving oral hydrocortisone","Intravenous Diphenhydramine alone while keeping the patient seated upright to observe for 30 minutes","Intramuscular Epinephrine 0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1:1000) into the mid-anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis), supine positioning with legs elevated, high-flow oxygen, and rapid IV 0.9% saline"],"a":3,"e":"IM Epinephrine 1:1000 (0.3–0.5 mg in adults, 0.01 mg/kg in children max 0.3 mg) into the mid-anterolateral thigh achieves rapid peak plasma levels via rich vastus lateralis blood supply, reversing airway edema, bronchospasm, and vasodilation. Patients with hypotension must lie supine (or recovery position if vomiting) because sudden standing causes \"empty ventricle syndrome\" cardiac arrest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01564","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Basic \u0026 Advanced Cardiac Life Support","topic":"Defibrillation vs Synchronized Cardioversion","q":"An ambulance/ED patient is conscious but diaphoretic and hypotensive (BP 76/50 mmHg) with a regular monomorphic wide-complex tachycardia at 180/min and a weak carotid pulse. When preparing the biphasic defibrillator for electrical therapy, which mode is required and why?","o":["SYNCHRONIZED cardioversion (engage SYNC button and confirm R-wave sensing markers) so the shock lands on the QRS complex and avoids the vulnerable T-wave period (R-on-T inducing ventricular fibrillation)","Transcutaneous pacing at 40 beats/min without sedation","Unsynchronized defibrillation at 360 J because all wide-complex tachycardias must be treated identically to VF","Turn off the ECG monitor leads and discharge pads blindly in AED mode"],"a":0,"e":"Unstable tachycardia WITH a pulse (SVT, AF/flutter, or monomorphic VT with pulse) requires synchronized cardioversion (after brief procedural sedation if awake) so energy discharges precisely on the R wave. Unsynchronized shocks are reserved for pulseless arrest rhythms (VF, pulseless VT) or polymorphic VT where the defibrillator cannot reliably track R waves.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01565","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Medical \u0026 Environmental Emergencies","topic":"Acute Stroke Prehospital \u0026 ED Triage","q":"An EMT responds to a 64-year-old man who developed sudden right facial droop, arm weakness, and slurred speech (FAST positive) 45 minutes ago. Before alerting the stroke-ready hospital and expediting non-contrast CT head, which point-of-care test must be performed immediately to exclude a reversible stroke mimic?","o":["Infuse IV nimodipine to drop systolic BP rapidly to 100 mmHg","Capillary blood glucose (CBG) check to rule out acute hypoglycemia, while avoiding routine aggressive lowering of mildly elevated blood pressure","Perform a bedside lumbar puncture before transporting the patient","Administer 300 mg oral aspirin immediately in the ambulance before any brain imaging"],"a":1,"e":"Hypoglycemia is the most important rapid mimic of acute focal stroke and must be checked immediately via fingerstick CBG. Aspirin/thrombolytics must NEVER be given before non-contrast CT excludes intracranial hemorrhage, and permissive hypertension is maintained (do not drop BP unless \u003e220/120 mmHg, or \u003e185/110 mmHg if thrombolysis candidate) to preserve ischemic penumbra perfusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01566","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Toxicology \u0026 Envenomation Emergencies","topic":"Indian National Snakebite Protocol","q":"Under the Government of India (MoHFW) National Snakebite Management Protocol, what is the recommended prehospital first aid for a farmer bitten on the ankle by a suspected venomous snake?","o":["Reassure the patient, immobilize the bitten limb with a splint at or slightly below heart level (\"Do it R.I.G.H.T.\"), and transport rapidly to a facility equipped with Anti-Snake Venom (ASV) and 20-minute Whole Blood Clotting Test (20WBCT) capability","Tie a tight rope tourniquet above the knee and make cruciate razor incisions over the fang marks to suck out venom","Encourage the patient to walk briskly to keep circulation moving and apply hot herbal poultices","Administer routine prophylactic intramuscular ASV into the bite site in the ambulance without monitoring"],"a":0,"e":"MoHFW \"Do it R.I.G.H.T.\" stands for Reassure, Immobilize like a fractured limb (without tight arterial tourniquets), Get to Hospital immediately, and Tell the doctor of any systemic symptoms. Tourniquets, incision, suction, cautery, and exertion accelerate tissue necrosis, bleeding, or systemic venom absorption. ASV is strictly given intravenously under hospital observation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01567","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Pediatric Emergencies","topic":"Acute Viral Croup (Laryngotracheobronchitis)","q":"A 2-year-old boy with 2 days of rhinorrhea and low-grade fever awakens at 2 AM with a harsh seal-like barking cough, hoarse voice, and inspiratory stridor at rest. He sits upright clinging to his mother, crying whenever approached. What is the safest prehospital approach?","o":["Force the child flat on the stretcher and insert a metal tongue depressor to inspect the epiglottis","Administer adult-dose oral codeine cough suppressant","Keep the child calm in the mother\u0027s lap, administer blow-by humidified oxygen if hypoxic, avoid painful agitation or throat/tongue-depressor examination, and prepare nebulized epinephrine + systemic corticosteroid (dexamethasone) per medical control","Perform immediate blind finger sweep of the posterior pharynx"],"a":2,"e":"Viral croup (parainfluenza) causes subglottic mucosal edema presenting with a barking cough and inspiratory stridor. Crying and agitation sharply increase turbulent inspiratory airflow and dynamic extrathoracic airway collapse; keeping the child comfortable in a parent\u0027s arms, avoiding unnecessary painful IV sticks or oral tongue-blade probing (which can also trigger fatal laryngospasm if occult epiglottitis exists), and giving dexamethasone + nebulized epinephrine for stridor at rest preserves airway patency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01568","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Prehospital Trauma Care","topic":"Trauma Airway Opening","q":"An unconscious motorcyclist thrown from his bike is breathing noisily with snoring upper-airway obstruction while wearing a helmet. Before definitive airway placement, which manual maneuver opens the airway while maintaining neutral cervical spine alignment?","o":["Rotating the head 90 degrees to the left side","Head-tilt chin-lift maneuver extending the occiput maximally backward","Hyperflexing the chin forcefully onto the sternum","Modified jaw-thrust maneuver (lifting the angles of the mandible anteriorly without extending the neck) paired with manual in-line cervical stabilization"],"a":3,"e":"In any unconscious trauma victim with potential cervical spine injury, the head-tilt chin-lift extends the neck and risks spinal cord transection. The modified jaw-thrust displaces the mandible and attached tongue anteriorly away from the posterior pharyngeal wall while a second rescuer maintains neutral manual in-line cervical stabilization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01569","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Prehospital Trauma Care","topic":"Amputated Part Transport Protocol","q":"A factory worker suffers a complete clean guillotine amputation of his right thumb at a lathe. After controlling stump hemorrhage with direct sterile pressure and elevation, how must the EMT package the severed thumb for transport to a microsurgical replantation center?","o":["Rinse gently with sterile normal saline if grossly dirty, wrap in sterile saline-moistened gauze, seal inside a watertight plastic bag, and place that bag inside a second container filled with ice-and-water slurry (avoiding direct ice contact)","Submerge the bare thumb directly into crushed dry ice","Drop the thumb directly into a bucket of unsterile ice water or 10% formalin","Leave the thumb uncooled in a warm dry cardboard box at 35 degrees C"],"a":0,"e":"Cooling slows cellular metabolism and extends warm ischemia time (~6 hrs muscle/12 hrs digit warm vs ~12-24 hrs cooled). However, direct contact with ice or freezing solutions causes frostbite endothelial crystallization that destroys microvascular replantation viability; sealing the saline-dampened wrapped part inside a waterproof bag resting on ice water preserves ~4 degrees C safely without tissue freezing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01570","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Endocrine and Metabolic Emergencies","topic":"Parenteral Dextrose vs Glucagon \u0026 Thiamine","q":"An ambulance responds to an unresponsive, diaphoretic 48-year-old man with chronic alcohol use disorder whose capillary blood glucose is 24 mg/dL. He has NO gag reflex. Why must oral glucose paste be avoided, and what two parenteral medications are indicated once IV access is secured (or IM glucagon if IV fails)?","o":["Administer 10 units of regular insulin subcutaneously","Oral paste risks aspiration when airway reflexes are absent; administer IV Thiamine 100 mg (alongside/prior to) IV Dextrose (D10W/D25W/D50W bolus) to correct hypoglycemia and prevent precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy","Give IM epinephrine 0.5 mg as primary glucose replacement","Force oral sugar water down his throat with a syringe; avoid thiamine completely"],"a":1,"e":"Oral glucose gel is strictly restricted to conscious patients able to swallow and protect their airway. In an unresponsive hypoglycemic patient with chronic alcoholism/malnutrition, IV dextrose rapidly restores cerebral glucose (or 1 mg IM glucagon if no IV line), while IV thiamine (vitamin B1) 100 mg prevents acute glucose-driven depletion of thiamine cofactors that precipitates Wernicke encephalopathy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01571","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Prehospital Trauma Care","topic":"Open Abdominal Evisceration","q":"A stab-wound trauma victim has loops of small intestine protruding visibly through an anterior abdominal laceration (abdominal evisceration). What is the mandatory prehospital wound care protocol?","o":["Pour 100% povidone-iodine and hydrogen peroxide directly onto the exposed bowel loops","Cover the bowel with dry absorbent cotton wool so it absorbs peritoneal fluid","Do NOT push the bowel back inside; gently cover the exposed viscera with large sterile dressings thoroughly moistened with warm normal saline, place an occlusive plastic barrier over top, flex knees slightly if no pelvic/spine trauma, and transport urgently","Push all protruding bowel loops forcefully back into the abdominal cavity and suture the skin shut"],"a":2,"e":"Attempting to push exposed contaminated bowel loops back into the peritoneal cavity introduces severe fecal/environmental peritonitis and risks mesenteric tearing or strangulation. Exposed serosa must be kept moist and warm with sterile saline-soaked gauze covered by an occlusive barrier (preventing desiccation and hypothermia) until surgical washout and repair in the OT.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01572","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Hazardous Materials and Burns","topic":"Dry Chemical Powder Exposure","q":"A cement/chemical factory worker is covered in dry powdered quicklime (calcium oxide) across his back and arms. Before initiating prolonged copious water irrigation, what critical first step prevents an exothermic thermal reaction on the skin?","o":["Dab the powder lightly with a damp wet cloth using 50 mL of water","Apply topical petroleum jelly directly over the dry quicklime powder","Leave the dry powder untouched on skin during a 2-hour ambulance ride","Don PPE/gloves and gently brush off all visible loose dry chemical powder from the skin and cut away contaminated clothing FIRST, then flush immediately with massive high-volume running water"],"a":3,"e":"Dry powdered chemicals such as quicklime (CaO), dry sodium hydroxide, or elemental metals react exothermically when dampened with small amounts of water—generating intense heat that adds thermal burns onto deep alkali burns. Brushing away dry powder first (wearing protective gloves/mask) and removing contaminated clothing before high-volume continuous water flushing eliminates the reaction safely.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01573","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Cardiac Life Support","topic":"Two-Rescuer Infant CPR Metrics","q":"When two trained EMTs perform CPR on an apneic, pulseless 5-month-old infant prior to advanced airway placement, what compression technique, depth, and compression-to-ventilation ratio are recommended by AHA guidelines?","o":["Two-thumb encircling hands technique on the lower sternum just below the nipple line, depth ~1/3 AP chest diameter (~4 cm / 1.5 inches), ratio 15:2","One-handed heel compressions at 7 cm depth, ratio 30:2","Two-finger compressions over the xiphoid tip, ratio 5:1","Continuous compressions at 160/min without ventilations"],"a":0,"e":"In two-rescuer infant/child BLS, because pediatric arrest is predominantly respiratory/hypoxic, the compression-to-ventilation ratio shifts from 30:2 (single rescuer) to 15:2. The two-thumb encircling hands technique generates superior coronary perfusion pressure and consistent depth (~1/3 anterior-posterior chest diameter, ~4 cm in infants, ~5 cm in children) at 100-120/min.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01574","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Prehospital Trauma Care","topic":"Flail Chest Management","q":"After a steering-wheel chest impact, a 44-year-old driver has severe chest pain, tachypnea (34/min), SpO2 87%, and a free-floating anterior-lateral chest wall segment that sucks INWARD during inspiration and bulges OUTWARD during expiration (flail chest). What is the modern evidence-based emergency care, and what outdated practice must be AVOIDED?","o":["Wrap tight elastic bandages tightly around the entire ribcage to splint the flail segment","Administer high-flow oxygen, titrated analgesia, careful fluid balance, and CPAP/intubation with PPV if respiratory failure develops; AVOID tight circumferential chest taping or heavy sandbagging","Infuse 4 liters of rapid normal saline bolus immediately to boost pulmonary edema","Place the patient face-down with a 10 kg sandbag strapped to the sternum"],"a":1,"e":"Flail chest (\u003e=2 consecutive ribs fractured in \u003e=2 places) produces paradoxical motion, yet hypoxia stems primarily from underlying pulmonary contusion and pain-induced hypoventilation. Effective pain relief, oxygenation, cautious crystalloids (preventing contusion edema), and positive-pressure ventilation (\u0027internal pneumatic stabilization\u0027) when needed are standard; circumferential taping/sandbags restrict vital capacity and worsen atelectasis/pneumonia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01575","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Environmental Emergencies","topic":"Frostbite Field Management","q":"A high-altitude trekker presents at a remote aid post (12 hours from definitive hospital transport via cold jeep) with white, waxy, hard, numb frostbitten toes. Why must the rescue EMT NOT rub the toes with snow/hands and NOT thaw them in warm water if there is any risk of refreezing during evacuation?","o":["Direct holding over an open campfire flame thaws toes fastest without burns","Puncture all hemorrhagic blisters in the field with unsterile pins","Rubbing shears delicate ice-crystal-filled cells mechanically, and a freeze-thaw-refreeze cycle causes devastating vascular thrombosis and gangrene; protect gently, pad between toes, and perform rapid 37-39 degrees C warm-water immersion ONLY once sustained warmth is guaranteed","Rubbing with snow melts deep capillary clots safely"],"a":2,"e":"Intracellular/extracellular ice crystals make frozen tissues mechanically brittle—rubbing or walking on thawed feet causes severe shear necrosis. Furthermore, thawing followed by refreezing during cold transport multiplies endothelial damage and tissue loss far worse than staying frozen until reaching continuous definitive warmth (rapid rewarming in circulating 37-39 C water bath for 15-30 min with analgesia).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01576","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Obstetric Emergencies","topic":"Prehospital Management of Atonic PPH","q":"After a home birth, an ambulance crew finds a mother pale, tachycardic (124/min), BP 86/54 mmHg, with brisk vaginal bleeding and a soft, boggy uterus palpated above the umbilicus. Beyond rapid transport, two large-bore IV crystalloid lines, high-flow oxygen, and warming, what immediate physical maneuver helps arrest uterine atony?","o":["Vigorous transabdominal uterine fundal massage (cupping the fundus while supporting the lower uterine segment above the symphysis), encouraging breastfeeding/uterotonics per protocol, and emptying a distended bladder","Pack tight dry cotton gauze loosely into the vagina without massage","Place the mother sitting upright at 90 degrees with legs dangling","Pull vigorously on the remaining perineal tissue to invert the uterus"],"a":0,"e":"Uterine atony accounts for ~70-80% of primary postpartum hemorrhage. Immediate brisk fundal massage stimulates myometrial contraction to constrict spiral arterioles; supporting the lower segment avoids uterine inversion, and emptying a full bladder removes mechanical displacement preventing contraction—alongside supine/leg-elevated shock positioning, IV fluids, oxytocin/misoprostol/tranexamic acid per medical control, and rapid hospital pre-notification.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01577","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Toxicological Emergencies","topic":"Acquired Methemoglobinemia","q":"A chemical plant worker exposed to aniline dyes/nitrites arrives alert-to-lethargic with slate-gray central cyanosis that fails to improve on 100% non-rebreather oxygen, SpO2 fixed at 85%, normal PaO2 (290 mmHg) on ABG, and dark chocolate-brown venous/arterial blood. Which specific antidote reverses this condition?","o":["Intravenous atropine 2 mg boluses","Intravenous hydroxocobalamin (Cyanokit) exclusively for cyanide","High-dose oral activated charcoal alone","Intravenous Methylene Blue (1-2 mg/kg slowly over 5 minutes; noting caution/avoidance in known G6PD deficiency)"],"a":3,"e":"Oxidizing agents (aniline dyes, nitrites, dapsone, benzocaine) oxidize hemoglobin\u0027s ferrous (Fe2+) iron to ferric (Fe3+) methemoglobin, which cannot bind O2 and turns blood chocolate-brown with an ~85% SpO2 plateau. IV Methylene Blue serves as an electron-carrier cofactor for NADPH-dependent methemoglobin reductase to rapidly reduce Fe3+ back to functional Fe2+ (ineffective and hemolytic in severe G6PD deficiency, where high-dose ascorbic acid/exchange transfusion is used).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01578","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Medical Emergencies","topic":"Life-Threatening Status Asthmaticus","q":"An EMT transports a 20-year-old asthmatic who was initially wheezing loudly and speaking short phrases, but now becomes exhausted, lethargic, unable to utter single words, with SpO2 84% and a \u0027silent chest\u0027 (virtually absent audible wheezes despite visible chest effort). What does the sudden disappearance of wheezing indicate?","o":["Critical life-threatening bronchoconstriction with dangerously minimal air movement (\u0027silent chest\u0027) signaling impending respiratory arrest—prepare immediate continuous bronchodilators, systemic steroids/epinephrine/MgSO4 per protocol, and BVM/airway readiness","Normal sleepiness after bronchodilator inhalation","Resolution of bronchospasm showing the patient is cured","Pneumothorax is ruled out completely"],"a":0,"e":"Wheezes require turbulent airflow across narrowed airways; when airway obstruction worsens to extreme exhaustion and near-total airflow cessation, wheezing vanishes into a \u0027silent chest\u0027 accompanied by altered mental status, bradycardia/pulsus paradoxus, and hypercapnia. This is an ominous pre-arrest sign requiring immediate aggressive bronchodilation, parenteral steroids/epinephrine (if anaphylaxis/refractory) or IV MgSO4, and assisted ventilation readiness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01579","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Toxicology \u0026 Envenomation Emergencies","topic":"Organophosphate (OP) Toxicity Management","q":"A farmworker is brought to the emergency bay smelling of pesticide with pinpoint pupils (miosis), profuse frothing/salivation, coarse wet lung crackles (bronchorrhea), vomiting, fasciculations, and heart rate 42/min. What is the correct immediate resuscitation and antidote strategy?","o":["Give a single small 0.2 mg IM dose of Atropine only if pupils dilate","Don gloves/gown (prevent secondary staff exposure), remove contaminated clothing and wash skin with soap/water, suction airway/give 100% O2, administer rapid escalating IV Atropine boluses until bronchorrhea clears (\"atropinization\"), and prepare IV Pralidoxime (2-PAM)","Give IV Flumazenil and withhold oxygen until gastric lavage is finished","Induce vomiting with ipecac syrup and perform immediate mouth-to-mouth rescue breathing without barrier protection"],"a":1,"e":"Organophosphates irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase, causing lethal cholinergic crisis (bronchorrhea/bronchospasm/bradycardia). Staff must wear PPE and wash contaminated skin/clothes to avoid absorbing pesticide. Clear secretions and oxygenate, then give escalating IV Atropine (e.g., 2 mg doubling every 5 min) titrated to clear lungs (dry chest, HR \u003e80, SBP \u003e80—NOT pupil size alone!), plus Pralidoxime to reactivate cholinesterase before aging.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01580","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Neurological \u0026 Trauma Assessment","topic":"Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) Scoring","q":"Following a motorcycle accident without a helmet, a patient opens his eyes ONLY to firm nailbed/trapezius squeeze, speaks confused disoriented sentences, and brings his hand up directly to push away the examiner’s hand during supraorbital pressure. What is his total Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score and TBI severity?","o":["GCS 13/15 (E3 + V4 + M6) — Mild Traumatic Brain Injury","GCS 8/15 (E1 + V3 + M4) — Severe Traumatic Brain Injury","GCS 11/15 (E2 + V4 + M5) — Moderate Traumatic Brain Injury","GCS 9/15 (E2 + V2 + M5) — Moderate Traumatic Brain Injury"],"a":2,"e":"Eye opening to pressure/pain = E2; Confused conversation = V4; Localizing painful stimulus above the clavicle = M5. Total = 2 + 4 + 5 = 11/15. Head injury severity is classified as Mild (13–15), Moderate (9–12), and Severe (\u003c= 8, traditionally indicating need for definitive airway protection).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01581","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Basic \u0026 Advanced Cardiac Life Support","topic":"Infant Choking Rescue Protocol","q":"An 8-month-old infant chokes on a piece of fruit, turns cyanotic, and cannot cry or cough (severe airway obstruction) while still conscious. Which emergency maneuver must the EMT perform?","o":["Perform a blind finger sweep deep inside the infant’s posterior pharynx","Perform forceful subdiaphragmatic abdominal thrusts (Heimlich maneuver)","Hold the infant upside down by the ankles and shake vigorously","Deliver cycles of 5 firm back slaps (infant supported prone/head-down along forearm) alternating with 5 chest thrusts (2 fingers over lower sternum just below intermammary line) until the object expels or infant becomes unresponsive"],"a":3,"e":"In responsive infants (\u003c 1 year), AHA BLS guidelines mandate alternating 5 back slaps and 5 chest thrusts while supporting the head and jaw in a head-down prone/supine sandwich along the rescuer’s forearm/thigh. Abdominal thrusts are strictly contraindicated in infants \u003c 1 year because their relatively large, unprotected liver and spleen risk fatal rupture.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01582","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Basic \u0026 Advanced Cardiac Life Support","topic":"Reversible Causes of Cardiac Arrest (5 H’s \u0026 5 T’s)","q":"During ACLS resuscitation of an adult in Pulseless Electrical Activity (PEA) — where a monitor shows organized wide-complex electrical activity at 50/min without a palpable pulse — the EMT focuses on high-quality CPR, Epinephrine 1 mg IV q3–5 min, and searching for the \"5 H’s and 5 T’s.\" Which clinical scenario specifically matches a reversible \"H\" vs \"T\" cause?","o":["Missed hemodialysis sessions with peaked T-waves prior to arrest → Hyperkalemia (\"H\"); penetrating precordial stab wound with muffled heart sounds and Beck’s triad → Cardiac Tamponade (\"T\")","Shockable ventricular fibrillation → automatically requires immediate synchronized cardioversion at 50 J","Simple ankle sprain → Tension pneumothorax (\"T\")","Asystole → immediate unsynchronized 200 J defibrillation shock"],"a":0,"e":"Non-shockable rhythms (PEA and Asystole) do NOT respond to defibrillation; survival depends on continuous high-quality CPR, IV epinephrine, and rapidly reversing the 5 H’s (Hypovolemia, Hypoxia, Hydrogen ion/acidosis, Hypo/Hyperkalemia, Hypothermia) and 5 T’s (Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade cardiac, Toxins, Thrombosis pulmonary, Thrombosis coronary).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01583","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Trauma Care \u0026 Prehospital Resuscitation","topic":"Open-Book Pelvic Fracture Stabilization","q":"A pedestrian struck at high speed by a truck arrives with pelvic pain, perineal bruising, and hypotension (BP 82/50 mmHg, HR 128/min). How should the EMT stabilize the suspected unstable pelvic ring fracture to reduce retroperitoneal venous hemorrhage?","o":["Avoid repeated \"pelvic rocking\" stress exams; apply a commercial pelvic binder (or folded bedsheet) centered precisely over the GREATER TROCHANTERS (not the iliac crests), tighten firmly, internally rotate and tape the knees/ankles together, and assist with early Tranexamic Acid (TXA) \u0026 fluid/blood resuscitation","Repeatedly press down hard on both iliac wings every 5 minutes to monitor crepitus","Wrap the pelvic binder high around the umbilical abdomen above the iliac crests","Insert a rigid Foley catheter immediately despite urethral meatus blood"],"a":0,"e":"Unstable \"open-book\" pelvic fractures expand pelvic volume and bleed massively from disrupted posterior venous plexuses. Repeated pelvic rocking dislodges fragile clots. Centering a circumferential pelvic binder over the GREATER TROCHANTERS (level with pubic symphysis) and binding knees/ankles closed reduces pelvic volume and tamponades venous bleeding.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01584","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Medical \u0026 Environmental Emergencies","topic":"Exertional / Classic Heat Stroke Resuscitation","q":"During an Indian summer heatwave (45°C), a highway construction worker collapses with confusion, slurred speech, rectal temperature 41.4°C, HR 136/min, and BP 88/54 mmHg. What distinguishes this diagnosis from simple heat exhaustion, and what is the primary emergency treatment?","o":["Delay all cooling for 60 minutes until brain MRI rules out stroke","CNS dysfunction (confusion/encephalopathy) + core temp \u003e 40°C defines HEAT STROKE; initiate immediate rapid active cooling (cold/ice-water immersion or continuous lukewarm misting + high-speed fans + ice packs to neck/axillae/groin) targeting \u003c 39°C alongside IV isotonic fluids","Wrap the patient in warm blankets to prevent shivering while giving hot oral tea","Administer IV Paracetamol 1 g and Aspirin 325 mg as the definitive therapy without external cooling"],"a":1,"e":"Heat stroke is defined by hyperthermia (core temp \u003e 40°C) WITH central nervous system dysfunction (delirium, seizures, coma), differentiating it from heat exhaustion (intact mental status). Mortality correlates directly with duration above critical thermal maximum; rapid conductive/evaporative cooling (\"cool first, transport/CT second\" if safe) is life-saving. Antipyretics fail because hypothalamic set-point is not elevated and risk hepatic/renal toxicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01585","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Vascular Access \u0026 Resuscitation Skills","topic":"Emergency IO Cannulation Sites \u0026 Confirmation","q":"During resuscitation of a patient in decompensated hemorrhagic shock, two attempts at peripheral IV cannulation fail within 90 seconds. You prepare for Intraosseous (IO) access using a powered drill driver. What is the classic proximal tibial insertion landmark and confirmation method?","o":["Use IO access exclusively for normal saline; epinephrine and blood products cannot be given via IO","Mid-shaft posterior calf muscle away from bone","Flat anteromedial surface of the proximal tibia, ~1–2 cm distal and medial to the tibial tuberosity (avoiding the epiphyseal growth plate in children); confirm by needle standing upright without support, aspiration of marrow/blood (if seen), and rapid 10 mL saline flush without subcutaneous swelling","Directly into the center of the patellar tendon over the knee joint space"],"a":2,"e":"The anteromedial proximal tibia (1–2 cm medial/distal to the tibial tuberosity in adults; 1–2 cm distal to tuberosity on the flat medial cortex in children to spare the growth plate) offers thin cortical bone and non-collapsible venous sinusoids. Proper placement allows ALL ACLS medications, fluids, and blood products at identical IV doses (using a pressure bag for rapid flow).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01586","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Medical \u0026 Environmental Emergencies","topic":"Severe Hypoglycemia in Unconscious Patients","q":"An ambulance is called for a 58-year-old insulin-treated diabetic man found unresponsive with diaphoresis and decorticate posturing; glucometer reads \"LOW\" (\u003c 25 mg/dL) and he has an absent gag reflex. What is the safest immediate glucose-restoration intervention?","o":["Administer 250 mL of 50% dextrose without checking vein patency and leave him unattended","Pour sweetened orange juice and glucose powder down his throat while supine","Inject 20 units of regular insulin subcutaneously to help glucose enter cells","Protect airway in lateral recovery/sniffing position and administer 25 g IV Dextrose (50 mL of 50% dextrose or 250 mL of 10% dextrose), OR 1 mg IM Glucagon if IV access is unavailable—NEVER pour oral sugar water into an unconscious patient’s mouth"],"a":3,"e":"Severe hypoglycemia (\u003c40 mg/dL with altered consciousness) causes permanent neuronal necrosis if prolonged. Because an unresponsive patient lacks protective airway reflexes, oral liquids/gels risk fatal aspiration. Give 25 g IV dextrose (D10W aliquots or D50W through a patent large-bore IV, avoiding extravasation tissue necrosis) or IM/intranasal glucagon if no IV access, and recheck CBG in 15 min.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01587","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Obstetric \u0026 Pediatric Emergencies","topic":"Uterine Atony Stabilization (108 Ambulance Protocol)","q":"An ambulance EMT responds to a woman 30 minutes after a home delivery who has soaked 4 pads with bright-red vaginal bleeding, BP 80/48 mmHg, HR 126/min, and a soft, boggy uterine fundus palpable above the umbilicus (uterine atony). Which initial stabilization bundle aligns with MoHFW PPH guidelines?","o":["Perform firm bimanual/fundal uterine massage, empty the urinary bladder if distended, establish two large-bore IVs with warmed isotonic crystalloid, administer Oxytocin 10 IU IM (or IV infusion per protocol) + Tranexamic Acid 1 g IV within 3 hours, and apply Non-Pneumatic Anti-Shock Garment (NASG) if available during rapid transfer","Give IV Methylergometrine rapidly to a patient with severe preeclampsia/hypertension without checking BP","Place the patient upright at 90 degrees and delay transport until bleeding stops spontaneously","Pack the uterine cavity tightly with unsterile rags and withhold IV fluids to avoid pulmonary edema"],"a":0,"e":"Uterine atony accounts for ~70–80% of PPH. Immediate EMS/first-line stabilization combines mechanical contraction (fundal massage, emptying a full bladder that prevents contraction), first-line uterotonic (Oxytocin 10 IU IM/IV), early Tranexamic Acid (1 g IV within 3 hr per WOMAN trial/WHO), two large-bore IV lines, oxygen, and NASG during urgent transfer to an FRU/CEmOC center.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01588","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Pediatric Emergencies","topic":"Compensated vs Hypotensive Pediatric Shock","q":"A 3-year-old boy with 3 days of profuse watery diarrhea presents lethargic with HR 164/min, cool mottled extremities, capillary refill 4.5 seconds, weak thready radial pulses, and BP 88/60 mmHg (normal systolic BP for age ~90 mmHg). How should the EMT classify his circulatory status?","o":["Isolated mild anxiety needing oral sips only","Compensated hypovolemic shock requiring immediate aggressive isotonic fluid resuscitation—hypotension in children is a LATE, ominous pre-arrest sign after \u003e25-30% blood/fluid loss","Completely normal stable hydration because systolic BP is not below 70 mmHg","Warm distributive anaphylactic shock"],"a":1,"e":"Children possess robust sympathetic vasoconstriction and cardiac rate reserve that preserve normal arterial blood pressure despite severe intravascular volume depletion. Relying on BP to rule out shock in pediatrics is a fatal error: persistent tachycardia, sluggish capillary refill (\u003e2 s), cool mottled skin, weak peripheral pulses, and altered mental status establish compensated shock requiring immediate 20 mL/kg isotonic crystalloid boluses before abrupt hypotensive collapse occurs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01589","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Prehospital Trauma Care","topic":"Two-Rescuer Motorcycle Helmet Removal","q":"A motorcyclist wearing a tight full-face helmet is unconscious with noisy gurgling airway obstruction and requires immediate suctioning and bag-mask ventilation. When removing the helmet in the field, what is the mandatory two-rescuer technique?","o":["Leave the full-face visor locked shut over the vomiting mouth permanently","Pull the helmet straight upward solo with one rapid yank by the chin guard","Rescuer 1 supports the occiput and mandible from below to hold strict neutral in-line C-spine stabilization while Rescuer 2 cuts/unbuckles the chin strap, spreads the helmet sides laterally clear of the ears, and gently rotates/slides the helmet off (tilting backward slightly over the nose) before transferring head control","Flex the neck 60 degrees forward onto the chest while pulling the helmet off"],"a":2,"e":"Full-face helmets block airway suctioning, BVM mask seal, and proper cervical collar application. Removing a helmet requires two trained providers: one stabilizes the head/neck from below (gripping jaw and occiput through the bottom opening) while the second removes glasses, unfastens the strap, widens the cheek pads laterally, and slides the helmet off without axial neck bending, immediately followed by in-line stabilization and collar sizing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01590","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Toxicological Emergencies","topic":"Cyanide Toxicity in Enclosed Fires","q":"A firefighter rescues an unconscious victim from a burning synthetic-upholstery warehouse with soot around the nares, bright pink/normal skin, profound lactic acidosis (lactate 12 mmol/L), hypotension, and cardiovascular collapse despite 100% oxygen. Suspecting hydrogen cyanide inhalation alongside carbon monoxide, which antidote binds cyanide safely WITHOUT inducing methemoglobinemia?","o":["High-dose oral activated charcoal via NG tube","Intravenous atropine 5 mg","Intravenous Amyl nitrite / Sodium nitrite alone (risks worsening hypoxia via methemoglobinemia in concurrent CO poisoning)","Intravenous Hydroxocobalamin (vitamin B12a, Cyanokit, 5 g IV infusion)"],"a":3,"e":"Combustion of polyurethane foams, plastics, wool, and nylon releases hydrogen cyanide (which blocks mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase Complex IV, halting aerobic ATP synthesis and causing severe lactic acidosis \u003e8-10 mmol/L). In fire victims who also carry high carboxyhemoglobin, nitrites induce methemoglobinemia—catastrophically compounding functional anemia—whereas IV hydroxocobalamin directly chelates cyanide into non-toxic cyanocobalamin excreted in urine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01591","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Musculoskeletal Trauma","topic":"Posterior Hip Dislocation Assessment","q":"A front-seat car passenger whose knees struck the dashboard in a head-on collision presents with severe right hip pain and a visibly shortened right leg held fixed in hip flexion, adduction, and INTERNAL rotation. Which nerve injury must the EMT specifically check for distally before splinting in position of comfort?","o":["Sciatic nerve injury (testing ankle dorsiflexion/great-toe extension [peroneal division] and plantarflexion/foot sensation)","Axillary nerve injury (regimental badge numbness)","Phrenic nerve paralysis","Radial nerve injury (wrist drop)"],"a":0,"e":"Posterior hip dislocation (~90% of traumatic hip dislocations, classic \u0027dashboard injury\u0027) drives the femoral head posteriorly into the sciatic nerve in 10-20% of cases—most commonly injuring its lateral common peroneal fibers (foot drop, weak EHL/ankle dorsiflexion, dorsal foot numbness). Splint in position of comfort with pillows/vac-splint and alert the trauma team immediately because closed reduction within \u003c6 hours prevents femoral head avascular necrosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01592","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Cardiac Life Support","topic":"Symptomatic Bradycardia Algorithm","q":"A 70-year-old man complains of lightheadedness and chest discomfort with HR 32/min (third-degree complete AV block with wide QRS escape), BP 76/48 mmHg, and diaphoresis. IV atropine 1 mg is given with zero improvement in heart rate or blood pressure. What is the next immediate ACLS intervention?","o":["Place the patient in knee-chest position and withhold all pacing","Initiate Transcutaneous Pacing (TCP) immediately (with analgesia/sedation as tolerated) OR start an IV dopamine or epinephrine infusion while preparing transvenous pacing","Administer IV amiodarone 300 mg bolus immediately","Deliver an unsynchronized 200 J defibrillation shock"],"a":1,"e":"Under the AHA 2020 ACLS Symptomatic Bradycardia algorithm, first-line IV atropine was updated to 1 mg (max 3 mg) for unstable bradycardia—though infranodal Mobitz II and complete 3rd-degree AV blocks with wide escape rhythms frequently fail atropine. When atropine is ineffective, immediately initiate Transcutaneous Pacing (confirming electrical AND mechanical femoral/carotid pulse capture!) or chronotropic dopamine (5-20 mcg/kg/min) / epinephrine (2-10 mcg/min) infusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01593","course":"Diploma in Emergency Medical Technology","subject":"Trauma and Infectious First Aid","topic":"Category III Dog Bite Wound Management","q":"A child suffers multiple deep transdermal lacerations on the forearm from a stray dog bite (WHO Category III exposure). What immediate wound-care intervention reduces rabies transmission risk by \u003e50-80% before Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG) and anti-rabies vaccine administration, and how should suturing be handled?","o":["Cauterize the wounds with hot iron rods","Apply red chili paste, lime, and turmeric directly onto the wound without water","Immediate thorough washing and flushing of all wounds with soap and running water for at least 15 minutes followed by povidone-iodine virucidal irrigation; AVOID tight primary suturing unless surgically unavoidable (and only AFTER local RIG infiltration)","Tightly suture and close all puncture wounds immediately in the ambulance without washing or RIG"],"a":2,"e":"Under WHO and India\u0027s National Rabies Control Programme (NCDC) guidelines, prompt vigorous 15-minute soap-and-running-water irrigation mechanically flushes and lipid-enveloped-lyses rabies virus from the wound before peripheral nerve entry, followed by povidone-iodine/virucidal antiseptic. Primary suturing traumatizes tissue and drives virus deeper unless unavoidable (e.g., severe facial bleeding), in which case wound edges must first be infiltrated thoroughly with RIG and sutured loosely.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01594","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Environmental Sanitation and Water Supply","topic":"Horrock\u0027s Apparatus Test","q":"Using Horrock\u0027s apparatus to disinfect a community well holding 45,500 liters of water, the blue color first appears in the 4th white cup (each cup increment equals 2 g of bleaching powder per 455 liters). How much fresh bleaching powder is needed to chlorinate this well?","o":["1600 grams","200 grams","400 grams","800 grams"],"a":3,"e":"In Horrock\u0027s test, if the 4th cup shows a distinct blue starch-iodide color, 4 x 2 g = 8 g of bleaching powder is required per 455 liters (100 gallons) of water. For 45,500 liters (which is 100 x 455 liters), the total bleaching powder required is 8 g x 100 = 800 grams.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01595","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Vector Control and Entomology","topic":"Larval Entomological Indices","q":"During a sanitary survey of 100 houses in an urban ward, a Health Inspector inspects 400 water-holding containers and finds Aedes aegypti larvae in 30 containers distributed across 20 houses. What is the Breteau Index (BI)?","o":["30","20","7.5","15"],"a":0,"e":"Breteau Index (BI) is defined as the number of positive containers per 100 houses inspected: (30 positive containers / 100 houses) x 100 = 30. (Note: House Index here is 20%, and Container Index is 30/400 = 7.5%). A Breteau Index \u003e20 (or \u003e50 in older thresholds) signals high dengue transmission risk requiring urgent source reduction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01596","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Epidemiological Surveillance and Immunization","topic":"Vaccine Vial Monitor (VVM) and Cold Chain","q":"During a routine PHC cold-chain inspection, the Health Inspector checks the Ice-Lined Refrigerator (ILR) temperature log and inspects a pentavalent (DPT-HepB-Hib) vaccine vial whose Vaccine Vial Monitor (VVM) inner square is darker than the outer circle. What must be done with this vial and what is the required ILR temperature range?","o":["Keep the vial for outreach sessions because it has not been frozen","Discard the pentavalent vial immediately because it has reached the discard point; maintain ILR at +2 degrees C to +8 degrees C","Place the pentavalent vial in the deep freezer compartment to restore its potency","Use the vial within 24 hours because only OPV is heat-sensitive; maintain ILR at -20 degrees C"],"a":1,"e":"When the inner square of a VVM matches or becomes darker than the outer reference circle (Stage 3 or 4), cumulative heat exposure has degraded potency and the vial must be discarded immediately. Under the Universal Immunization Programme (UIP), all vaccines stored in an ILR—especially freeze-sensitive T-series/pentavalent/HepB/PCV vaccines—must be kept strictly between +2 degrees C and +8 degrees C.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01597","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Food Hygiene and Sanitation","topic":"Milk Pasteurization Verification","q":"Which laboratory test is used by a Sanitary / Food Inspector to confirm that cow\u0027s milk has been adequately pasteurized?","o":["Benedict\u0027s reducing sugar test","Widal agglutination test","Phosphatase test (alkaline phosphatase must be absent/negative)","Methylene blue reduction test (MBRT) alone for thermal destruction"],"a":2,"e":"Raw milk contains the heat-labile enzyme alkaline phosphatase, which is destroyed at the exact time-temperature combination required to kill Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Coxiella burnetii during pasteurization (e.g., 72 degrees C for 15 seconds HTST). A negative phosphatase test confirms adequate pasteurization and absence of raw milk adulteration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01598","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Occupational and Rural Sanitation","topic":"Residual Chlorine Testing","q":"After chlorinating drinking water with a 1-hour contact period under normal non-epidemic conditions, what is the recommended minimum free residual chlorine level at the consumer tap/point of use, and which test differentiates free from combined chlorine?","o":["2.5 mg/L (ppm); Fouchet\u0027s test","5.0 mg/L (ppm); Horrock\u0027s starch-iodide test","0.05 mg/L (ppm); Nessler\u0027s reagent test","0.5 mg/L (ppm); Orthotolidine-Arsenite (OTA) test (or DPD method)"],"a":3,"e":"A minimum free residual chlorine concentration of 0.5 mg/L after a 1-hour contact period ensures effective disinfection against bacterial pathogens and safeguards against post-treatment contamination in distribution pipes. The OTA test (or modern DPD colorimetric test) distinguishes free residual chlorine (immediate yellow/pink color) from slower-reacting combined chloramines.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01599","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Environmental Sanitation and Water Supply","topic":"Slow Sand vs Rapid Sand Filtration","q":"In a municipal slow sand (biological) water filtration bed, what is the \u0027vital layer\u0027 (Schmutzdecke) that forms on the top surface of the sand bed and removes \u003e98% of bacteria, organic matter, and ammonia?","o":["A slimy gelatinous zoogleal biological film composed of algae, plankton, diatoms, and bacteria","A pressurized synthetic reverse osmosis polyamide membrane","A layer of coarse crushed anthracite coal and liquid alum floc","A galvanized iron mesh screen impregnated with bleaching powder"],"a":0,"e":"The Schmutzdecke (\u0027dirty skin\u0027) is the biologically active gelatinous surface layer that matures over the top 2-3 cm of a slow sand filter during ripening. It traps suspended particles, oxidizes ammoniacal nitrogen to nitrates, and eliminates up to 99.9% of waterborne bacteria.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01600","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Rural Sanitation and Excreta Disposal","topic":"RCA Water-Seal Latrine Standards","q":"When supervising construction of a rural RCA-type pour-flush water-seal sanitary latrine, what minimum horizontal safe distance should be maintained between the leach pit and an existing shallow drinking water handpump/well, and what is the primary purpose of the 2 cm water seal?","o":["At least 1 meter away; the water seal heats sewage to 80 degrees C","At least 15 meters (50 feet) away and preferably at a lower elevation; the water seal prevents access of flies and blocks foul odor escape","At least 500 meters away; the water seal adds chlorine automatically","Distance does not matter in sandy soil; the water seal increases methane pressure"],"a":1,"e":"In Indian public health sanitation norms (RCA latrine / Swachh Bharat), the latrine pit should be situated at least 15 meters (50 feet) from wells/drinking water sources (and never uphill/higher than the water source) to prevent fecal coliform seepage. A 20 mm (2 cm) water seal in the S-trap prevents flies from breeding/contacting excreta and stops sewer gases from entering the cubicle.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01601","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Epidemiological Surveillance","topic":"S, P, and L Reporting Forms","q":"Under India\u0027s Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (IDSP / IHIP), which weekly reporting forms correspond respectively to health workers (syndromic), medical officers (presumptive), and diagnostic laboratories (confirmed cases)?","o":["Form M (Malaria), Form T (TB), Form D (Dengue)","Form A (Acute), Form B (Bacterial), Form C (Chronic)","Form S (Syndromic), Form P (Presumptive), Form L (Laboratory confirmed)","Form 1 (Birth), Form 2 (Death), Form 3 (Migration)"],"a":2,"e":"IDSP operates a three-tier weekly disease surveillance structure: Form S is filled by peripheral health workers/MPWs based on syndromes (fever, diarrhea, jaundice, AFP); Form P is filled by Medical Officers/clinicians for presumptive clinical diagnoses; Form L is filled by laboratories reporting microbiologically/serologically confirmed pathogens.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01602","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Vector Control and Entomology","topic":"Chemical and Biological Larvicides","q":"When treating clean domestic stored drinking-water containers (cement tanks/sumps) in an urban area with active Aedes aegypti breeding where source emptying is not feasible, which organophosphate larvicide is approved by NCVBDC at 1 ppm (1 mg/L) without rendering water unsafe?","o":["Crude diesel / kerosene oil pouring into drinking water","Paris green (copper acetoarsenite) dust","DDT 50% wettable powder","Temephos (Abate) 50% EC at 1 ppm"],"a":3,"e":"Temephos (Abate) has very low mammalian toxicity and is recommended by WHO and India\u0027s NCVBDC at 1 ppm (1 mg active ingredient per liter, e.g., 1 mL of 50% EC diluted and dosed per 500 L) specifically for potable water storage tanks to control Aedes and Anopheles larvae for several weeks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01603","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Occupational Health and Industrial Hygiene","topic":"Occupational Lung Diseases","q":"During an occupational health inspection of a cotton textile spinning mill, several workers report chest tightness, dyspnea, and cough that are characteristically worst on Monday mornings after the weekend break and improve later in the week (\u0027Monday morning fever\u0027). Which occupational lung disease is this?","o":["Byssinosis (caused by cotton dust inhalation)","Bagassosis (caused by moldy sugarcane residue Thermoactinomyces sacchari)","Silicosis (caused by free crystalline silica dust)","Asbestosis (caused by chrysotile/crocidolite fibers)"],"a":0,"e":"Byssinosis occurs in textile workers exposed to raw cotton, flax, or hemp dust (especially carding/opening rooms). Grade 1/2-1 typically manifests as \u0027Monday chest tightness/dyspnea\u0027 upon re-exposure after a 48-hour weekend away from dust, progressing over years to chronic irreversible airway obstruction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01604","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Environmental Sanitation and Water Supply","topic":"WHO and BIS Drinking Water Standards","q":"Under WHO Guidelines for Drinking-water Quality and BIS (IS 10500) standards in India, what is the maximum permissible count of Escherichia coli (or thermotolerant coliform bacteria) in a 100 mL sample of treated potable drinking water?","o":["50 per 100 mL","0 per 100 mL (must not be detectable in any 100 mL sample)","10 per 100 mL","100 per 100 mL"],"a":1,"e":"E. coli (and thermotolerant fecal coliforms) serves as the definitive indicator of recent fecal contamination and potential presence of enteric pathogens (Salmonella, Shigella, Vibrio, hepatitis A). Both WHO and BIS IS 10500:2012 mandate ZERO E. coli / thermotolerant coliforms per 100 mL in any potable water supply.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01605","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Epidemiological Outbreak Investigation","topic":"Food-Specific Attack Rate Calculation","q":"After a village wedding feast, 48 guests develop abrupt vomiting and abdominal cramps within 3 hours. Out of 60 guests who ate kheer (milk dessert), 45 fell ill; out of 40 guests who did NOT eat kheer, only 3 fell ill. What are the Attack Rate among kheer eaters, Attack Rate among non-eaters, and Relative Risk (RR) for kheer?","o":["Ate kheer = 93.8%; Did not eat kheer = 6.2%; Relative Risk = 15.1","Ate kheer = 45%; Did not eat kheer = 3%; Relative Risk = 15.0","Ate kheer = 75%; Did not eat kheer = 7.5%; Relative Risk = 10.0","Ate kheer = 75%; Did not eat kheer = 12.5%; Relative Risk = 6.0"],"a":2,"e":"Attack Rate among exposed (ate kheer) = (45 / 60) x 100 = 75%. Attack Rate among unexposed (did not eat kheer) = (3 / 40) x 100 = 7.5%. Relative Risk (RR) = 75% / 7.5% = 10.0—strongly incriminating kheer (likely preformed heat-stable Staphylococcus aureus enterotoxin given the short 3-hour incubation and vomiting).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01606","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Solid Waste Management","topic":"Composting Methods in India","q":"In municipal sanitary engineering in India, how does the \u0027Bangalore method\u0027 of composting organic refuse and night soil differ from the \u0027Indore method\u0027?","o":["The Bangalore method is an anaerobic trench composting process without regular turning (requiring ~4-6 months), whereas the Indore method is an aerobic pit/windrow process with periodic turning","The Bangalore method burns all plastic waste in open drums, whereas the Indore method uses steam autoclaving","The Bangalore method requires daily mechanical turning every 2 hours","Both methods dump untreated sewage directly into rivers"],"a":0,"e":"Developed by Acharya at IISc Bangalore, the Bangalore method layers refuse and night soil/sludge in trenches sealed with earth to undergo slow anaerobic decomposition (~4-6 months), minimizing fly breeding and odor without manual turning. The Indore method uses aerobic decomposition with regular turning every few weeks (~2-3 months completion).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01607","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Food Hygiene and Adulteration","topic":"Toxic Food Adulterants","q":"During a market raid following cases of bilateral pitting leg edema, glaucoma, and diarrhea (epidemic dropsy), a Food/Health Inspector tests suspected mustard oil with concentrated nitric acid and observes a distinct orange-red to crimson-brown ring at the junction. Which toxic adulterant does this indicate?","o":["Metanil yellow dye","Boric acid preservative","Chalk powder (calcium carbonate)","Argemone mexicana seed oil (containing sanguinarine)"],"a":3,"e":"Adulteration of mustard oil with Argemone mexicana oil (sanguinarine toxin) causes epidemic dropsy—characterized by bilateral pitting edema, cutaneous erythema/sarcoids, cardiac failure, and raised IOP/glaucoma. On nitric acid testing, shaking mustard oil with conc. HNO3 yields a characteristic orange-red/crimson layer in the acid phase.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01608","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Immunization Programme Management","topic":"Open Vial Policy (28-Day Rule)","q":"At the end of an outreach immunization session, a health worker returns partially used multi-dose vials of Pentavalent, OPV, IPV, and reconstituted Measles-Rubella (MR) vaccines in an intact vaccine carrier. Under India\u0027s MoHFW Open Vial Policy, how must these opened vials be handled?","o":["Return opened Pentavalent, OPV, and IPV vials to the ILR for reuse up to 28 days (if cold chain, VVM, expiry, and septum sterility are intact), but DISCARD reconstituted MR after 4 hours","Freeze opened IPV vials at -20 degrees C until next week\u0027s clinic","Discard all opened Pentavalent and OPV vials immediately after every single session","Keep reconstituted MR in the ILR for 28 days alongside OPV"],"a":0,"e":"India\u0027s MoHFW Open Vial Policy allows opened multi-dose liquid vials of OPV, DPT, TT/Td, Hepatitis B, Pentavalent, IPV, PCV, and liquid Rota (where applicable per product) to be reused in subsequent sessions for up to 28 days provided expiry date is valid, VVM has not reached discard point, cold chain was maintained, and aseptic septum technique was followed. However, reconstituted lyophilized vaccines (BCG, Measles/MR, JE) lack preservatives and MUST be discarded within 4 hours of reconstitution (or at session end, whichever is earlier) to prevent toxic shock syndrome.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01609","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Environmental Sanitation and Water Supply","topic":"Fluoride Limits and Nalgonda Defluoridation","q":"In an endemic fluorosis district of Rajasthan where borewell water shows fluoride at 4.2 mg/L (exceeding the BIS/WHO upper limit of 1.5 mg/L), which indigenous Indian method developed by NEERI uses sequential addition of lime and alum to precipitate excess fluoride?","o":["Boiling water in open aluminum vessels for 5 minutes","Nalgonda technique (adding lime/bleaching powder followed by alum, flocculation, and sedimentation)","Schmutzdecke slow sand biological filtration","Horrock\u0027s starch-iodide technique"],"a":1,"e":"Developed by NEERI (National Environmental Engineering Research Institute) for endemic fluorosis belts in India, the Nalgonda technique adds calculated doses of lime (Ca(OH)2) and alum (aluminum sulfate) with bleaching powder, followed by rapid mixing, flocculation, sedimentation, and filtration—co-precipitating excess fluoride down to the safe 0.5-1.0 mg/L range.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01610","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Sewage and Wastewater Sanitation","topic":"Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD)","q":"In municipal wastewater engineering, what does \u0027BOD5\u0027 (5-day Biochemical Oxygen Demand measured at 20 degrees C) measure, and what BOD level classifies raw domestic sewage as \u0027strong\u0027?","o":["The pH change caused by boiling sewage for 5 days","The amount of inorganic silica sand settled in 5 hours","The amount of dissolved oxygen consumed by aerobic microorganisms to decompose biodegradable organic matter over 5 days at 20 degrees C; strong sewage typically has BOD \u003e=300 mg/L","The concentration of free chlorine left after 5 minutes; strong sewage has \u003c1 mg/L"],"a":2,"e":"BOD5 measures the biodegradable organic load of sewage via the oxygen (mg/L) consumed by aerobic bacteria over 5 days in the dark at 20 degrees C. Raw sewage is categorized as weak (~100 mg/L), medium (~200 mg/L), or strong (\u003e=300 mg/L); treated effluent discharged into inland surface waters in India must typically meet BOD \u003c=30 mg/L (or \u003c=10-20 mg/L per NGT/CPCB norms).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01611","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Vector Control and Zoonoses","topic":"Flea Indices in Plague Prevention","q":"During a port-health rodent surveillance survey, a Health Inspector traps 50 domestic rats (Rattus rattus) and collects 200 rat fleas, of which 125 are identified as Xenopsylla cheopis. What is the Xenopsylla cheopis (specific flea) index, and does it exceed the critical threshold (\u003e1.0) indicating plague transmission hazard?","o":["X. cheopis index = 62.5 (percentage, not an index)","X. cheopis index = 0.4 (safe below threshold)","X. cheopis index = 4.0 (that is the total flea index, not specific index)","X. cheopis index = 2.5 (exceeds the critical threshold of 1.0; requires immediate anti-flea insufflation before anti-rodent trapping)"],"a":3,"e":"The Specific Flea Index (Cheopis index) = Total number of Xenopsylla cheopis fleas collected / Total number of rats examined = 125 / 50 = 2.5. (Total flea index = 200 / 50 = 4.0). A Cheopis index \u003e1.0 represents high potential for plague transmission; critically, anti-flea insecticide dusting (e.g., malathion/pyrethroid) MUST precede rodenticide poisoning so starving fleas do not jump from dead rats onto humans.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01612","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Air Hygiene and Thermal Comfort","topic":"Kata Thermometer","q":"Which specialized alcohol-bulb thermometer is used by sanitary and industrial hygiene inspectors to measure very low indoor air velocities and the combined \u0027cooling power\u0027 of air (temperature, humidity, and air movement) on the human body?","o":["Kata thermometer (dry and wet Kata)","Anemometer vane exclusively for wind speeds \u003e50 km/hr","Maximum-minimum Six\u0027s thermometer","Sling psychrometer only"],"a":0,"e":"Developed by Leonard Hill, the Kata thermometer is an alcohol thermometer with a large bulb heated to 38 degrees C (above body temperature) whose cooling time from 38 to 35 degrees C (100 to 95 degrees F) measures air cooling power (millicalories/cm2/sec) and subtle indoor air currents (\u003c1-2 m/s) in workplaces and wards. Standard comfort cooling power is \u003e=6 (dry Kata) and \u003e=20 (wet Kata).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01613","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Food Hygiene and Meat Inspection","topic":"Post-Mortem Carcass Condemnation","q":"During post-mortem inspection at a municipal abattoir, a Sanitary Inspector incises the masseter, tongue, and cardiac muscles of a pig carcass and finds numerous pearly-white, fluid-filled bladder-like cysts (~5-10 mm) with an invaginated scolex (\u0027measly pork\u0027). What parasite is this, and what public health action is required?","o":["Ascaris lumbricoides adult worms; wash with cold tap water and sell normally","Cysticercus cellulosae (larval stage of Taenia solium); condemn the heavily infested carcass immediately to prevent human taeniasis and neurocysticercosis","Toxoplasma gondii oocysts visible to the naked eye as 5 cm cysts","Cysticercus bovis (larval stage of Taenia saginata); stamp as Grade-A prime pork"],"a":1,"e":"Pearly-white cysticerci in porcine skeletal/cardiac/lingual muscles represent Cysticercus cellulosae, the larval stage of pork tapeworm Taenia solium (\u0027measly pork\u0027). Ingestion of undercooked infested pork causes human intestinal taeniasis, and fecal-oral ingestion of T. solium eggs causes severe human neurocysticercosis (a leading cause of acquired epilepsy in India). Heavily infested carcasses must be condemned and safely destroyed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01614","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Occupational Health and Industrial Hygiene","topic":"Permissible Noise Exposure Limits","q":"During an industrial hygiene inspection of a bottling factory, what is the standard WHO/NIOSH recommended 8-hour time-weighted average noise exposure threshold above which hearing conservation and ear protection are required, and at which audiometric frequency does noise-induced hearing loss (NIHL) classically first notch?","o":["140 dB(A) continuous exposure for 8 hours; dip at 250 Hz","45 dB(A) quiet library threshold; dip at 125 Hz","85 dB(A) for an 8-hour workday; classic early audiometric dip at 4,000 Hz (4 kHz)","115 dB(A) for 16 hours; conductive loss at 500 Hz"],"a":2,"e":"Repeated exposure to \u003e=85 dB(A) over an 8-hour shift damages outer hair cells of the basal turn of the cochlea, manifesting initially as a sensorineural audiometric \u0027boiler-maker\u0027s notch\u0027 at 4,000 Hz (4 kHz; occasionally 3-6 kHz) before spreading to speech frequencies (500-2,000 Hz). Above 115 dB(A) unprotected exposure is prohibited, and \u003e=140 dB causes immediate acoustic rupture.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01615","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Environmental Sanitation and Water Supply","topic":"Temporary vs Permanent Hardness","q":"In community water chemistry, which dissolved salts cause \u0027temporary\u0027 versus \u0027permanent\u0027 hardness of water, and how is permanent hardness softened on a large scale?","o":["Temporary hardness is caused by calcium and magnesium bicarbonates (removed by simple boiling or lime); permanent hardness is caused by calcium and magnesium sulfates, chlorides, and nitrates (removed by Permutit/zeolite base-exchange or soda-lime)","Temporary hardness is caused by sodium chloride; permanent hardness is removed by 5-minute boiling","Temporary hardness is caused by dissolved oxygen; permanent hardness is removed by sand filtration","Both temporary and permanent hardness are caused exclusively by iron oxide and removed by chlorination"],"a":0,"e":"Carbonate (temporary) hardness comes from Ca(HCO3)2 and Mg(HCO3)2, which decompose on boiling into insoluble carbonates. Non-carbonate (permanent) hardness stems from Ca/Mg sulfates, chlorides, and nitrates that survive boiling and require Base-Exchange (Zeolite/Permutit sodium-calcium ion exchange) or washing soda/lime treatment. Moderately hard water (~100-300 mg/L CaCO3) is acceptable, while overly soft water (\u003c50 mg/L) can corrode lead/copper pipes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01616","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Food Hygiene and Sanitation","topic":"Milk Pasteurization Time-Temperature Regimes","q":"Which time-temperature holding combinations correctly define the three standard methods of milk pasteurization inspected by Sanitary and Food Safety Officers (Holder/Vat, High-Temperature Short-Time [HTST], and Ultra-High Temperature [UHT])?","o":["Holder/Vat: 100 degrees C for 2 hours; HTST: 40 degrees C for 10 minutes; UHT: 25 degrees C for 24 hours","Holder/Vat: 37 degrees C for 30 minutes; HTST: 55 degrees C for 15 seconds; UHT: 72 degrees C for 30 minutes","All three methods heat milk to exactly 80 degrees C for 5 minutes without cooling","Holder/Vat: 63-66 degrees C for 30 minutes; HTST: 72 degrees C for at least 15 seconds (rapidly cooled to \u003c=4 degrees C); UHT: 135-150 degrees C for 1-5 seconds"],"a":3,"e":"Pasteurization eliminates non-spore-forming milkborne pathogens (Mycobacterium bovis/tuberculosis, Brucella, Salmonella, Coxiella burnetii) while preserving nutritional quality. Standard industrial regimes are Holder/Vat batch (~63-65 C x 30 min), continuous HTST (72 C x \u003e=15 s followed by immediate chilling to \u003c=4 C), and sterile aseptic UHT (135-150 C x 1-5 s).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01617","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Communicable Disease Control","topic":"Cholera Fecal and Vomitus Disinfection","q":"During an acute watery diarrhea (cholera) outbreak in a flood relief camp without connected sewerage, how must a Sanitary Inspector supervise disinfection of patient stool and vomitus collected in bedpans/buckets before pit disposal?","o":["Mix thoroughly with an equal volume of 5% cresol (or 2% bleaching powder / 1-2% sodium hypochlorite solution), break up any solid lumps, and allow at least 2 hours of contact time before burying or sanitary disposal","Rinse directly into the community drinking water pond immediately","Sprinkle dry talcum powder on top and discard after 10 seconds","Pour 70% ethyl alcohol over the stool and ignite it"],"a":0,"e":"Cholera stool (\u0027rice-water stool\u0027) teems with Vibrio cholerae (\u003e10^7-10^8 CFU/mL). Effective concurrent disinfection requires mixing feces/vomitus thoroughly with an equal volume of potent chemical disinfectant (e.g., 5% cresol/Lysol, freshly prepared 5-10% bleaching powder milk-of-lime, or 1-2% hypochlorite—noting organic matter consumes chlorine, so cresol/phenolics or higher-strength lime/bleach with \u003e=1-2 hour contact time is standard) prior to deep burial or sanitary latrine disposal.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01618","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Vital Statistics and Health Legislation","topic":"Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969","q":"Under India\u0027s Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969 (and CRS guidelines), within what statutory time limit must a live birth, stillbirth, or death be reported to the local Registrar without late fee/delayed-registration magistrate order?","o":["Within 24 hours only","Within 21 days from the date of occurrence","Within 1 year","Within 90 days"],"a":1,"e":"Under the Registration of Births and Deaths (RBD) Act, 1969 (and 2023 Amendment maintaining the 21-day reporting window), every birth, stillbirth, and death in India must be reported to the local Registrar within 21 days of occurrence (within 21-30 days incurs nominal late fee; \u003e30 days to 1 year requires written permission and affidavit; \u003e1 year requires First-Class Magistrate/SDM order).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01619","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"School Health and Institutional Sanitation","topic":"School Hygiene Norms in India","q":"During a school health sanitary inspection in India, what minimum classroom floor area per child and what type of desk-bench geometry are recommended to prevent overcrowding and spinal postural strain?","o":["2 sq ft per student and \u0027plus-type\u0027 desks spaced 50 cm apart horizontally","50 sq ft per student with standing-only lecterns for 8 hours","Minimum 10 sq ft floor area per student (max 40 students/classroom) and \u0027minus-type\u0027 desks (where the desk edge slightly overlaps the seat front vertically)","Single shared unisex latrine for 500 students"],"a":2,"e":"Indian School Health Committee sanitary standards recommend a maximum of 40 students per classroom with \u003e=10 sq ft floor space per pupil, light entering from the left side (or cross-ventilation), separate urinal/latrine blocks for boys and girls, safe drinking water, and \u0027minus-type\u0027 desks (vertical plumb line from desk inner edge falls slightly onto the bench seat) so students sit upright without slouching forward.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01620","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Environmental Sanitation \u0026 Waste Management","topic":"BMW Color Coding \u0026 Disposal Pathways","q":"During a sanitary inspection of a Primary Health Centre under India’s Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules (2016), into which color-coded containers must (A) blood-soaked cotton swabs, (B) contaminated plastic IV tubing/syringes without needles, and (C) used hypodermic needles/scalpel blades be segregated?","o":["(A) Blue box; (B) White puncture-proof container; (C) Yellow bag","(A) Red bag; (B) Yellow bag; (C) Blue cardboard box","(A) Black municipal bin; (B) White container; (C) Red bag","(A) Yellow non-chlorinated plastic bag; (B) Red non-chlorinated plastic bag; (C) White translucent puncture-proof, leak-proof container"],"a":3,"e":"Per India’s BMW Management Rules 2016: Yellow bags hold anatomical waste, blood-soaked dressings/swabs, and expired drugs (for incineration/plasma pyrolysis/deep burial); Red bags hold contaminated recyclable plastics like IV sets, catheters, and syringes without needles (for autoclaving + shredding); White puncture-proof translucent containers hold sharps (needles, blades); Blue boxes hold broken/unbroken glassware and metallic implants.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01621","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Water Supply, Quality \u0026 Sanitation","topic":"Horrocks Apparatus Chlorine Demand Test","q":"Using Horrocks’ apparatus (6 white cups containing 200 mL sample water each, dosed incrementally with stock chlorine solution), you add starch-iodide indicator after 30 minutes and observe that White Cup #4 is the FIRST cup to show a distinct blue color. How many grams of fresh bleaching powder are required to chlorinate a village well containing 4,550 liters (1,000 gallons) of water?","o":["80 grams (since 4th cup = 4 × 2 g = 8 g per 455 L; × 10 = 80 g)","20 grams total for 4,550 liters","40 grams (4 × 1 g per 455 L)","8 grams total for the entire 4,550-liter well"],"a":0,"e":"In Horrocks’ test, the number of the first white cup showing a definite blue color with starch-iodide multiplied by 2 gives the grams of bleaching powder (with ~25% available chlorine) needed per 455 liters (100 gallons) of water. Here, Cup #4 → 4 × 2 = 8 g per 455 L. For 4,550 L (10 × 455 L), total dose = 8 × 10 = 80 grams.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01622","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Water Supply, Quality \u0026 Sanitation","topic":"BIS IS 10500 Residual Chlorine \u0026 Coliform Limits","q":"According to BIS IS 10500:2012 Indian Drinking Water Standards (and Park’s PSM), what is the minimum free residual chlorine required at the consumer tap after a 1-hour contact period during routine supply (and during waterborne outbreaks), and what is the permissible limit for thermotolerant E. coli per 100 mL sample?","o":["Minimum 0.01 mg/L routine; E. coli up to 50 per 100 mL","Minimum 0.2 mg/L routine (0.5 mg/L during outbreaks); E. coli must be 0 per 100 mL","Zero free chlorine required if turbidity is \u003c 5 NTU; E. coli \u003c= 5 per 100 mL","Minimum 5.0 mg/L routine; E. coli up to 10 per 100 mL"],"a":1,"e":"BIS IS 10500 specifies minimum 0.2 mg/L (ppm) free residual chlorine at the point of delivery after sufficient contact time (1 hour), raised to 0.5 mg/L when viral/enteric outbreaks threaten or post-contamination risk exists. Microbiologically, thermotolerant E. coli / fecal coliforms must be undetectable (0 CFU/100 mL) in all drinking water samples.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01623","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Food Hygiene \u0026 Disease Surveillance","topic":"Staphylococcal Food Poisoning Outbreak","q":"Thirty-two hostel students develop sudden violent nausea, projectile vomiting, and abdominal cramps 3 hours after eating cream-filled pastries stored at room temperature for 8 hours; none have high fever. As the investigating Sanitary Inspector, which pathogen is most likely responsible and what targeted control steps are indicated?","o":["Clostridium botulinum toxin; simply re-boil the pastries for 2 minutes and serve them again","Salmonella Typhi infection; prescribe mass prophylactic ciprofloxacin without inspecting the kitchen","Preformed heat-stable Staphylococcus aureus enterotoxin; collect leftover pastry/vomitus samples, inspect kitchen food handlers for skin boils/paronychia/nasal carriage, discard contaminated batch, and enforce cold-holding \u003c 5°C (or hot-holding \u003e 60°C) with IDSP notification","Hepatitis A virus; focus solely on chlorinating bathing water"],"a":2,"e":"A 1–6 hour incubation period with severe vomiting and minimal/no fever after dairy/cream/custard items held in the temperature danger zone (5–60°C) is classic for preformed heat-stable S. aureus enterotoxin. Investigation pairs food/clinical sampling with handler skin/wound screening, strict cold-chain/hygiene enforcement, and IDSP outbreak reporting.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01624","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Medical Entomology \u0026 Vector Control","topic":"Larval Indices (House, Container \u0026 Breteau Index)","q":"During an urban dengue larval survey, a Health Inspector checks 100 houses containing 250 water-holding containers (coolers, tires, drums). Twenty houses are positive, and 35 containers harbor Aedes aegypti larvae. What is the Breteau Index (BI), and what does an index above 20 indicate under NCVBDC guidelines?","o":["Breteau Index = 55; requires draining municipal underground sewers exclusively","Breteau Index = 14%; requires only indoor residual DDT spraying on bedroom ceilings","Breteau Index = 20%; indicates zero risk of dengue transmission","Breteau Index = 35 (positive containers per 100 houses inspected); BI \u003e 20 indicates high dengue transmission risk requiring immediate source reduction (\"Dry Day\"), temephos larviciding, and targeted space spraying"],"a":3,"e":"Breteau Index (BI) = (Number of positive containers / Number of houses inspected) × 100 = (35 / 100) × 100 = 35. (House Index = 20%, Container Index = 35/250 = 14%). Under NCVBDC (formerly NVBDCP), BI \u003e 20 (or HI \u003e 5–10%) signifies high vector density and active outbreak threat, demanding weekly dry-day container emptying, temephos (1 ppm) in non-drainable containers, and focal fogging if cases occur.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01625","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Immunization Cold Chain \u0026 Public Health Logistics","topic":"Cold Chain Audit, VVM Staging \u0026 Shake Test","q":"During an immunization cold-chain audit at a sub-centre, the ILR temperature log shows a brief drop to -2°C overnight, and one vial of Pentavalent vaccine shows a Vaccine Vial Monitor (VVM) at Stage 2 while another OPV vial shows Stage 3 (inner square matches outer circle). How should the Health Inspector manage both vials?","o":["Discard the Stage 3 OPV vial immediately (unusable heat exposure); perform the \"Shake Test\" on the freeze-sensitive Pentavalent vial compared to a purposefully frozen-and-thawed control vial and discard if rapid sedimentation/flocculation confirms freeze damage","Use the Stage 3 OPV vial first on the same day and freeze the Pentavalent vial solid to preserve potency","Use both vials normally because ILR temperatures down to -10°C are safe for Pentavalent vaccine","Discard all Stage 1 and Stage 2 vials and keep only Stage 4 vials"],"a":0,"e":"Under India’s UIP Cold Chain guidelines: ILRs must stay strictly at +2°C to +8°C. VVM Stage 1 \u0026 2 are usable (Stage 2 used first before expiration); Stage 3 (inner square = outer ring) and Stage 4 (darker) MUST be discarded. Freeze-sensitive adsorbed vaccines (Pentavalent, Hep-B, Td, IPV, PCV) damaged at sub-zero temperatures lose potency and cause sterile abscesses—verified via the WHO Shake Test (damaged vials settle rapidly with granular flakes).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01626","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Food Hygiene \u0026 Disease Surveillance","topic":"Phosphatase Test \u0026 Methylene Blue Reduction Test","q":"At a food-safety practical station testing a dairy’s High-Temperature Short-Time (HTST) pasteurized milk (72°C for 15 seconds), which test specifically confirms that pasteurization reached adequate temperature/time—and what result indicates properly pasteurized milk?","o":["Alkaline Phosphatase test; a NEGATIVE phosphatase result confirms complete pasteurization because the enzyme is destroyed at pasteurization temperatures","Orthotolidine test showing 2.0 mg/L residual chlorine in milk","Alkaline Phosphatase test; a strongly POSITIVE result proves pasteurization succeeded","Methylene Blue Reduction Test (MBRT) turning colorless within 15 minutes proves sterile pasteurized milk"],"a":0,"e":"Raw milk naturally contains alkaline phosphatase, which has slightly higher thermal resistance than non-spore-forming pathogens (e.g., Mycobacterium bovis, Coxiella burnetii). Proper pasteurization (Holder 63°C/30 min or HTST 72°C/15 s) inactivates it; therefore, a negative phosphatase test verifies complete pasteurization and absence of raw-milk admixture. MBRT assesses bacterial load in raw milk (\u003c30 min decolorization = very poor quality).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01627","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Epidemic Control \u0026 Public Health Legislation","topic":"Acute Waterborne Cholera Cluster Control","q":"Twelve residents in a peri-urban slum develop sudden profuse painless \"rice-water\" diarrhea and severe dehydration within 24 hours; inspection reveals a leaking drinking water pipeline running beside an overflowing open drain. Which integrated public health containment package must the Health Inspector coordinate immediately?","o":["Administer single-dose oral albendazole to all residents as primary cholera treatment","Notify IDSP immediately, collect rectal swabs/stool in Cary-Blair (or alkaline peptone water) transport medium before antibiotics, shut off/repair the leaking line and issue alternate safe/boiled water, super-chlorinate supply (\u003e= 0.5 mg/L free chlorine), establish ORS/ORT corners, and disinfect latrines/fomites with bleaching powder","Spray indoor pyrethrum fogging in every house and isolate patients without repairing the water pipe","Wait 14 days for final culture sensitivity before recommending water boiling or ORS distribution"],"a":1,"e":"Suspected cholera demands urgent IDSP reporting and immediate empirical public health action without awaiting lab confirmation: Cary-Blair/alkaline peptone water transport preserves Vibrio cholerae, cutting/repairing cross-contaminated pipelines plus providing super-chlorinated (\u003e=0.5 mg/L) or boiled water halts transmission, while ORT corners prevent dehydration deaths and disinfection blocks fecal-oral spread.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01628","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Environmental Sanitation \u0026 Occupational Health","topic":"Sanitary Latrine Design \u0026 Occupational Noise Limits","q":"During rural sanitation and factory health inspections in India, what are the standard specifications for (1) the water-seal trap depth and minimum distance of a leach-pit sanitary latrine from a drinking water well, and (2) the maximum permissible occupational noise exposure for an 8-hour work shift under the Factories Act?","o":["No water seal required; pit located \u003e= 3 meters from well; 105 dB(A) 8-hour limit","15 cm water-seal depth; pit located directly beside the well; 110 dB(A) 8-hour limit","2 cm (20 mm) water-seal depth; pit located \u003e= 15 meters (50 feet) downhill from drinking water source; 90 dB(A) max 8-hour limit under Factories Act (with hearing protection/conservation \u003e= 85 dB(A))","0.2 cm water-seal depth; pit located 2 meters uphill from the well; 120 dB(A) 8-hour limit"],"a":2,"e":"In an RCA/sanitary water-seal latrine, a 2 cm (20 mm) water seal blocks foul gases and prevents flies from accessing excreta, while siting the pit \u003e=15 m (50 ft) away and at a lower elevation than any well/handpump prevents groundwater contamination. Under the Indian Factories Act, 90 dB(A) is the statutory 8-hour TWA permissible limit (and \u003e=85 dB(A) triggers hearing conservation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01629","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Vector Control and Entomology","topic":"Indoor Residual Spraying (IRS) and LLINs","q":"Why is Indoor Residual Spraying (IRS) with synthetic pyrethroids or DDT applied specifically to the INSIDE walls and eaves of rural houses rather than spraying outdoor bushes/forests to control rural malaria vectors (such as Anopheles culicifacies) in India?","o":["Because IRS instantly kills aquatic mosquito larvae in deep underground borewells","Because outdoor trees absorb pyrethroids and convert them into vitamin D","Because Anopheles mosquitoes only live inside refrigerator compressors","Because primary Indian malaria vectors are endophilic/endophagic—they rest on interior walls after taking a blood meal indoors, absorbing a lethal contact dose from the residual insecticide film over several months"],"a":3,"e":"IRS coats interior walls, ceilings, and eaves up to ~3 meters with a long-acting residual insecticide. Blood-fed female Anopheles mosquitoes typically rest quietly on indoor walls to digest blood and mature eggs; contact with the sprayed wall delivers a lethal dose before the ~10-14 day extrinsic incubation period of Plasmodium completes, interrupting community malaria transmission.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01630","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Environmental Sanitation and Water Supply","topic":"Breakpoint Chlorination Curve","q":"During community water chlorination, why does adding small initial doses of chlorine fail to produce sustained FREE residual chlorine until the \u0027breakpoint\u0027 is crossed?","o":["Initial chlorine is consumed by oxidizing reducing impurities (Fe2+, Mn2+, H2S) and reacting with ammonia to form combined chloramines, which are subsequently destroyed/oxidized at the breakpoint dip—only after the breakpoint does free available residual chlorine appear","Ammonia prevents water from ever dissolving chlorine gas","Initial chlorine evaporates immediately unless the water is boiled at 100 degrees C","Free chlorine forms first and then turns into calcium carbonate at the breakpoint"],"a":0,"e":"On the classic breakpoint chlorination curve: Zone 1 reduces inorganic matter; Zone 2 forms combined chloramines (rising total residual); Zone 3 oxidizes chloramines into nitrogen gas/trichloride causing total residual to DIP down to a trough (the \u0027breakpoint\u0027, representing complete satisfaction of chlorine demand); Zone 4 beyond the breakpoint yields potent, fast-acting FREE residual chlorine (HOCl / OCl-) linearly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01631","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Sanitary Housing and Environment","topic":"Accepted Standards of Overcrowding","q":"Under standard public health housing criteria in India (Park\u0027s PSM accepted persons-per-room norms), what is the maximum acceptable number of persons in a 2-room house versus a 3-room house (counting infants \u003c12 months as 0 and children 1-10 years as 0.5), and at what age does lack of sex separation constitute overcrowding?","o":["2-room house: max 6 persons; 3-room house: max 9 persons; infants count as 2 persons","2-room house: max 3 persons; 3-room house: max 5 persons; overcrowding exists if 2 persons \u003e9 years of age (not married couples) of opposite sexes must sleep in the same room","2-room house: max 10 persons; 3-room house: max 15 persons; sex separation at age 25","2-room house: max 1 person; 3-room house: max 2 persons; no sex-separation rule"],"a":1,"e":"Accepted persons-per-room standards: 1 room = 2 persons, 2 rooms = 3 persons, 3 rooms = 5 persons, 4 rooms = 7 persons, \u003e=5 rooms = 10 persons (+2 for each additional room; baby \u003c12 months not counted, child 1-10 years = half unit). Sex separation overcrowding occurs if two individuals \u003e9 years (not husband and wife) of opposite sexes share a sleeping room. Floor space should ideally be \u003e=50-100 sq ft per person.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01632","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Communicable Disease Control","topic":"Quarantine vs Isolation \u0026 Yellow Fever IHR Rules","q":"In public health outbreak control, how is the duration of QUARANTINE for healthy exposed contacts determined (distinct from ISOLATION of symptomatic cases), and how long is Yellow Fever international quarantine/vaccination validity delay under WHO IHR?","o":["Quarantine applies only to dead bodies; lasts strictly 6 months for all diseases","Quarantine equals the minimum period of communicability (1 hour) after symptom resolution","Quarantine applies to healthy exposed contacts and equals the MAXIMUM incubation period of the disease (e.g., 6 days for Yellow Fever; Yellow Fever 17D vaccine certificate becomes valid 10 days after vaccination and lasts for life)","Isolation and quarantine are identical terms with no distinction in public health"],"a":2,"e":"Isolation separates sick/infected individuals during the period of communicability, whereas Quarantine restricts healthy exposed contacts for the longest/maximum incubation period to see if disease develops. Under IHR (2005), travelers arriving in India from Yellow Fever-endemic countries without a valid vaccine certificate (valid 10 days post-vaccination, now lifelong validity per 2016 WHO amendment) are quarantined up to 6 days (Yellow Fever max incubation period).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01633","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Food Hygiene and Catering Sanitation","topic":"Microbial Danger Zone Temperature","q":"During a sanitary inspection of a hospital canteen kitchen, what temperature range is defined as the \u0027Food Temperature Danger Zone\u0027 in which foodborne bacteria (e.g., Salmonella, Clostridium perfringens, S. aureus) multiply rapidly, and what holding temperatures keep cold and hot cooked foods safe?","o":["Danger Zone: +80 degrees C to +120 degrees C; keep cold milk at +25 degrees C","Bacteria grow fastest at -18 degrees C inside deep freezers","Danger Zone: -20 degrees C to 0 degrees C; keep cooked rice at room temperature (30 degrees C) for 12 hours","Danger Zone: +5 degrees C to +60 degrees C (41-140 degrees F); keep cold food \u003c= 5 degrees C and hot cooked food \u003e= 60-65 degrees C (cooking core \u003e= 70-75 degrees C)"],"a":3,"e":"Per WHO \u0027Five Keys to Safer Food\u0027 and FSSAI Schedule 4 hygiene norms, pathogenic bacteria proliferate rapidly between +5 degrees C and +60 degrees C. Cooked food must NOT sit in this danger zone for \u003e2 hours; perishables should be chilled \u003c=5 degrees C (refrigerator) or frozen \u003c= -18 degrees C, cooked thoroughly to \u003e=70-75 degrees C core temperature, and held hot \u003e=60 degrees C until served.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01634","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Disaster Sanitation and Mortuary Hygiene","topic":"Sanitary Burial and Water Source Protection","q":"During post-flood disaster sanitation management, when mass temporary burial of unidentified bodies is required near a relief camp, what minimum burial depth and horizontal distance from wells/springs/streams must a Sanitary Inspector enforce?","o":["Burial depth at least 1.5-2 meters (5-6 feet) above the water table, and at least 30-50 meters away from drinking water wells/streams (preferably downhill from water sources)","Shallow burial 30 cm deep directly beside the camp drinking water handpump","Dispose of bodies directly into flowing drinking-water rivers","Bury bodies directly inside the active groundwater well shaft"],"a":0,"e":"WHO/NDMA disaster sanitation guidelines recommend burial at least 1.5 meters deep (above groundwater table) and \u003e=30 meters (up to 50-250 m depending on soil permeability) away from drinking water sources, properly mapped/tagged for forensic identification, while noting that trauma victims generally pose minimal epidemic risk except in specific outbreaks (cholera/viral hemorrhagic fevers) where fecal/body-fluid leakage must be contained.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01635","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Wastewater Sanitation","topic":"Oxidation Pond (Waste Stabilization Pond)","q":"How does a shallow municipal Oxidation Pond (Waste Stabilization Pond, ~1-1.5 meters deep) purify raw organic sewage naturally in sunny tropical climates like India?","o":["Through a mutually beneficial symbiosis: aerobic bacteria oxidize organic sewage into CO2 and nutrients, while photosynthetic algae consume CO2 and sunlight to generate dissolved oxygen that sustains the aerobic bacteria","By freezing sewage under artificial ice sheets","By injecting pure chlorine gas directly into raw feces without settling","By boiling sewage using geothermal vents at 100 degrees C"],"a":0,"e":"Oxidation (stabilization) ponds provide economical, low-energy sewage treatment in warm sunny climates over ~10-15 days detention: bacteria break down organic matter (lowering BOD) into CO2, ammonia, and phosphates; algae (Chlorella, Scenedesmus) utilize sunlight and those nutrients to release oxygen for bacterial respiration, while intense solar UV + alkaline daytime pH kill \u003e99% of enteric pathogens.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01636","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Medical Entomology","topic":"Anopheles vs Culex vs Aedes Larvae and Adults","q":"During larval dipping surveillance of stagnant water collections, a Health Inspector inspects live mosquito larvae in a white enamel tray. Which morphological and postural features distinguish ANOPHELES larvae from CULEX and AEDES larvae?","o":["Anopheles larvae have a long slender siphon tube hanging vertically downward","Anopheles larvae lack a respiratory siphon tube and float horizontally parallel to the water surface, whereas Culex and Aedes larvae possess a distinct breathing siphon tube and hang at an angle head-down from the surface","Adult Anopheles females rest with bodies completely parallel to the wall and plain unspotted wings","Aedes larvae float completely horizontally without any siphon tube"],"a":1,"e":"Field identification separates major Indian vector genera: Anopheles larvae have no breathing siphon and rest parallel to the water film (adults have spotted wings, rest inclined at ~45 deg to walls, and female palpi equal proboscis length); Culex and Aedes larvae breathe via a prominent posterior siphon tube while hanging head-down (adults rest hunchbacked/parallel to walls; Aedes bears striking black-and-white \u0027tiger\u0027 bands).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01637","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Environmental Sanitation and Water Supply","topic":"NEERI Double-Pot Chlorination Method","q":"In a rural village relying on an open dug well (~4,500 liters capacity) where daily manual chlorination is impractical during a diarrhea-prone monsoon season, how does the NEERI Double-Pot method provide continuous safe chlorination for ~2-3 weeks?","o":["Two sealed plastic bottles filled with kerosene float on top of the well","Two iron pots heat bleaching powder over charcoal beside the well","A smaller perforated inner earthen pot filled with a moist 1 kg bleaching powder + 2 kg coarse sand mixture sits inside a larger perforated outer pot, suspended ~1 meter below water level to slowly diffuse chlorine steadily","Raw bleaching powder is poured dry once every 2 years without sand mixing"],"a":2,"e":"Developed by NEERI (Nagpur) for rural Indian wells, the Double-Pot system places a moist paste of 1 kg fresh bleaching powder + 2 kg coarse sand inside a small pot (with 1 cm holes) nested inside a larger pot (with 1 cm holes), lowered by rope ~1 m below the water surface. Controlled diffusion maintains safe residual chlorine for ~2-3 weeks in a household/small community well serving ~20 persons.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01638","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Food Hygiene and Safety","topic":"Clostridium botulinum Hazard in Blown Tins","q":"During a routine grocery and hostel kitchen inspection, the Sanitary Inspector finds several hermetically sealed metal tins of canned vegetables and fish whose ends are visibly swollen and bulging outward (\u0027hard swells / blown tins\u0027). What is the mandatory rule regarding these cans?","o":["Hammer the bulged lid flat and donate it to a school hostel","Boil the swollen tin for 2 minutes, open it, and taste a small spoonful to check flavor","Refrigerate the blown tin for 1 week to see if the swelling goes down","Condemn and destroy the swollen cans immediately WITHOUT opening or tasting the contents, because gas production inside anaerobic sealed cans may harbor lethal Clostridium botulinum neurotoxin"],"a":3,"e":"Swollen/bulging hermetically sealed cans (\u0027hard swells\u0027 or \u0027springers\u0027 failing to stay flat) indicate anaerobic bacterial fermentation and gas accumulation—potentially from heat-resistant spores of Clostridium botulinum producing one of the deadliest biological neurotoxins (even tasting a tiny drop can cause fatal descending bulbar and respiratory paralysis). Such tins must be seized, punctured/denatured, and disposed of safely.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01639","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Occupational Health Legislation","topic":"ESI Act 1948 and Factories Act 1948","q":"Under India\u0027s Employees\u0027 State Insurance (ESI) Act, 1948, which statutory benefits are provided to insured factory/establishment workers and their families, and what minimum age under the Factories Act, 1948 prohibits hazardous factory employment of children?","o":["ESI provides comprehensive Medical, Sickness (including extended sickness for 34 chronic diseases), Maternity (26 weeks), Disablement, Dependants\u0027, and Funeral benefits; Factories Act prohibits employment of children below 14 years","ESI covers only CEOs earning \u003eINR 5 lakh/month","ESI provides only free yearly dental whitening; Factories Act sets minimum child factory age at 8 years","Factories Act requires 100-hour workweeks without rest intervals"],"a":0,"e":"The ESI Act, 1948 is India\u0027s cornerstone social security scheme providing full cashless medical care, sickness cash benefit (70% wage for 91 days; extended sickness up to 2 years at 80% wage for 34 specified diseases like TB/cancer), 26 weeks paid maternity benefit, temporary/permanent disablement pensions, and dependants\u0027 pensions. Under the Factories Act (and Child Labour Amendment Act), no child \u003c14 years may work in any factory (adolescents 14-18 require fitness certification and hazardous-process restrictions).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01640","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Immunization Cold Chain Management","topic":"Ice Pack Conditioning","q":"Before placing freeze-sensitive vaccines (Pentavalent, Hepatitis B, PCV, Td) inside an outreach vaccine carrier alongside 4 ice packs taken straight out of a -20 degrees C deep freezer, why MUST the health worker \u0027condition\u0027 the ice packs on a table until condensation droplets form and ice rattles inside when shaken?","o":["Conditioning melts all ice completely into 30 degrees C warm water","Hard-frozen ice packs straight from -20 degrees C can freeze adjacent adjuvant-containing vaccines below 0 degrees C inside the carrier, irreversibly destroying their potency; conditioning brings ice-pack surface temperature safely to ~0 degrees C","Hard-frozen ice packs explode inside plastic carriers unless thawed completely","Conditioning sterilizes the outer plastic of the ice pack with UV light"],"a":1,"e":"Ice packs pulled directly from a -20 C deep freezer remain well below 0 C and will freeze aluminum-adjuvanted T-series/HepB/PCV/Pentavalent vaccines sitting against them inside a well-insulated carrier—causing adjuvant clumping and loss of immunogenicity. Laying ice packs out at room temperature until frost melts into water droplets and the ice core visibly moves/rattles on shaking (\u0027conditioning\u0027) equilibrates them at 0 C, safely holding carrier temps at +2 to +8 C for 12-48 hrs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01641","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Environmental Sanitation \u0026 Air Quality","topic":"National Air Quality Index (AQI) \u0026 Respirable Particulates","q":"Under India’s Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) National Air Quality Index (AQI), which of the 8 monitored pollutants poses the highest risk of penetrating deep into pulmonary alveoli and entering the systemic circulation to trigger cardiopulmonary mortality?","o":["Atmospheric Nitrogen gas (N2, 78% of air)","Coarse dust \u003e 100 micrometers trapped in nasal vibrissae","PM2.5 (fine particulate matter \u003c= 2.5 micrometers in aerodynamic diameter)","Water vapor humidity alone"],"a":2,"e":"India’s National AQI tracks 8 criteria pollutants: PM10, PM2.5, NO2, SO2, CO, O3, NH3, and Lead (Pb). While particles \u003e10 µm are filtered in the nasopharynx and PM10 reaches bronchi, ultra-fine PM2.5 bypasses mucociliary clearance to deposit in terminal bronchioles/alveoli and cross the alveolar-capillary membrane, driving COPD, asthma, stroke, and ischemic heart disease.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01642","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Environmental Sanitation \u0026 Waste Management","topic":"Composting Methods (Bangalore vs Indore)","q":"When advising a small municipality on hygienic organic refuse and night-soil composting without mechanical turning equipment, how does the \"Bangalore Method\" differ operationally from the \"Indore Method\"?","o":["The Bangalore method is an ANAEROBIC trench method (alternate layers of refuse and night soil covered with earth and left undisturbed for 4–6 months without turning), whereas the Indore method is an AEROBIC process requiring periodic turning","The Bangalore method requires daily forced compressed-air blowing and hourly turning","Both methods involve burning solid waste in open unlined dumps","The Indore method seals waste hermetically underwater for 10 years"],"a":0,"e":"Developed by the Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR), the Bangalore anaerobic trench method alternates ~15 cm refuse and ~5 cm night soil/sludge layers, topped with a 25 cm earth seal—thermophilic heat (~60°C) destroys pathogens/fly larvae within 4–6 months with minimal odor or labor. The Indore aerobic method layers materials in pits/windrows and turns them every ~15 days for faster 2–3 month composting.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01643","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Communicable Disease Control \u0026 Zoonoses","topic":"Category III Dog Bite Post-Exposure Prophylaxis","q":"A child arrives at a Primary Health Centre 1 hour after multiple deep bleeding stray-dog bites on the leg (WHO Category III exposure). Which immediate wound-care and immunization protocol conforms to National Rabies Control Programme (NCDC/MoHFW India) guidelines?","o":["Give only Tetanus Toxoid and advise that RIG is unnecessary because the skin bled","Inject RIG directly into the gluteal buttock far away from the bite wound without washing","Cauterize the bite wounds with red-hot chili paste and tight sutures, and wait 10 days to observe the stray dog before giving vaccine","Wash wounds immediately with soap and running water for \u003e= 15 minutes followed by povidone-iodine/alcohol antiseptic (avoid tight suturing), infiltrate Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG) directly into and around all bite wounds, and administer Intradermal (ID) Anti-Rabies Vaccine (updated Thai Red Cross 2-2-2-0-2 on Days 0, 3, 7, 28)"],"a":3,"e":"Rabies is ~100% fatal once clinical symptoms appear, making immediate Category III PEP vital: thorough 15-minute soap-and-water irrigation physically flushes ~90% of virus; antiseptics inactivate residual virus; RIG (Equine 40 IU/kg or Human 20 IU/kg) MUST be infiltrated locally into/around the wound to neutralize virus before neural entry (never delay 10 days for stray dogs!), paired with full ARV course.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01644","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Epidemiology \u0026 Outbreak Surveillance","topic":"Food-Specific Attack Rate \u0026 Case Fatality Rate","q":"During an outbreak investigation at a community feast attended by 500 villagers, 250 people ate a specific paneer dish; of those 250 consumers, 100 developed acute watery diarrhea within 12 hours, and 2 patients died. What are the Food-Specific Attack Rate (for the paneer dish) and the Case Fatality Rate (CFR)?","o":["Attack Rate = 40% (100/250); Case Fatality Rate = 2% (2/100)","Attack Rate = 50% (250/500); Case Fatality Rate = 0.8% (2/250)","Attack Rate = 20% (100/500); Case Fatality Rate = 0.4% (2/500)","Attack Rate = 2% (2/100); Case Fatality Rate = 40% (100/250)"],"a":0,"e":"Food-Specific Attack Rate = (Ill among those who ate the food / Total who ate the food) × 100 = (100 / 250) × 100 = 40%. Case Fatality Rate (CFR) measures disease severity among diagnosed cases = (Deaths from the disease / Total cases of the disease) × 100 = (2 / 100) × 100 = 2%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01645","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Food Hygiene \u0026 Disease Surveillance","topic":"Argemone Oil, Kesari Dal \u0026 Metanil Yellow Toxicity","q":"During an FSSAI food-safety market raid, which adulterant-disease-test triad is accurately matched?","o":["Kesari dal (Lathyrus sativus) → Night blindness → detected by Benedict’s urine test","Argemone mexicana oil in mustard oil → Epidemic Dropsy (bilateral pitting edema, diarrhea, glaucoma, cardiac failure) → detected by Nitric Acid test producing orange-red/crimson color","Calcium carbide on bananas → Scurvy → detected by Gram stain","Metanil yellow in turmeric powder → Goiter → detected by Adding pure water turning blue"],"a":1,"e":"Sanguinarine alkaloid in Argemone mexicana seed oil contaminates/adulterates mustard oil, causing capillary permeability and Epidemic Dropsy (edema, erythematous rash, glaucoma, high-output heart failure); concentrated nitric acid turns the sample orange-red/crimson. Kesari dal (BOAA toxin) causes Neurolathyrism (spastic paraparesis), while Metanil yellow in turmeric turns magenta-pink with concentrated HCl.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01646","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Medical Entomology \u0026 Vector Control","topic":"Urban vs Rural Malaria Vectors \u0026 Blood Smear Protocol","q":"While investigating a cluster of fever cases in an urban construction ward under NCVBDC malaria surveillance, where does the primary urban malaria vector (Anopheles stephensi) breed, and what is the distinct purpose of preparing BOTH a thick and a thin blood smear on the same glass slide?","o":["Breeds exclusively in polluted septic sewage drains; THIN smear detects parasites faster than thick smear","Transmits Dengue virus; thick smear identifies microfilariae only","Breeds in clean stored water (overhead tanks, curing tanks, cisterns, wells); THICK smear has higher sensitivity to detect presence of malaria parasites, whereas THIN smear preserves RBC morphology to confirm Plasmodium species (P. falciparum vs P. vivax)","Bites strictly at midday indoors only; thick smear must be fixed with methanol before staining"],"a":2,"e":"Unlike Culex (polluted drains), Anopheles stephensi breeds in clean overhead water tanks, masonry curing tanks, and cisterns (controlled via mosquito-proof lids, temephos, or larvivorous Gambusia/Poecilia fish). On blood slides, the unfixed dehemoglobinized thick film concentrates 20–30× more blood for sensitive detection, while the methanol-fixed thin film preserves RBCs/parasite rings for exact species ID.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01647","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Environmental Sanitation \u0026 Waste Management","topic":"Septic Tank Design \u0026 Effluent Disposal","q":"During a sanitary inspection of a new rural primary school toilet block, you check the newly built septic tank. Which design and maintenance specification is mandatory for effective anaerobic digestion and safe effluent disposal?","o":["24-hour hydraulic retention time, liquid depth ~1.2–1.5 m with \u003e= 30 cm freeboard air space above scum layer, periodic desludging every 2–3 years, and effluent routed into a covered subsurface soakage pit/dispersion trench (NEVER directly into open street drains)","1-hour retention time with effluent discharged directly into an open roadside storm drain","Pour 2 kg of bleaching powder and caustic soda into the septic tank weekly to kill all bacteria","Fill the tank completely to the concrete roof slab with zero air space"],"a":0,"e":"A sanitary septic tank relies on anaerobic bacteria over a 24-hour retention period to settle solids (sludge) and partially digest organic matter; \u003e=30 cm air space vents/accommodates gases and scum; effluent still contains viable pathogens/parasite eggs and must percolate safely through soil via a covered soakage pit/subsurface trench (\u003e=15 m from wells).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01648","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Public Health Legislation \u0026 Vital Statistics","topic":"Registration of Births and Deaths (RBD) Act \u0026 MMR/IMR","q":"As a Sanitary/Health Inspector assisting the Local Registrar under India’s Registration of Births and Deaths (RBD) Act, a family approaches you regarding a home birth and an infant death that occurred in the ward. What is the statutory time limit for normal registration without late fee, and what are the correct denominators for Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) and Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR)?","o":["21 days from date of event for both birth and death; IMR = infant deaths (\u003c 1 year) per 1,000 live births; MMR = maternal deaths per 100,000 live births","6 months from date of event; IMR per 100,000 total population; MMR per 1,000 pregnant women","7 days for births and 1 year for deaths; both use mid-year total population as denominator","90 days without penalty; MMR counts only deaths occurring in tertiary hospitals"],"a":0,"e":"Under the RBD Act 1969 (and amendments), every live birth, stillbirth, and death must be reported within 21 days to the Registrar for free registration. In public health statistics, IMR expresses deaths under 1 year of age per 1,000 live births in that year, whereas MMR expresses maternal deaths (during pregnancy or within 42 days of termination) per 100,000 live births.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01649","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Communicable Disease Control \u0026 Zoonoses","topic":"Sputum Collection \u0026 Airborne Infection Control","q":"At a peripheral NTEP Designated Microscopy Centre (DMC), a patient with 2 weeks of productive cough needs sputum testing for pulmonary tuberculosis. Which specimen-collection and airborne infection-control protocol protects staff and waiting patients?","o":["Require 7 consecutive daily sputum specimens pooled into one unsealed glass jar","Collect sputum inside an unventilated crowded OPD waiting room using open paper cups","Collect a single pure saliva swab inside the closed laboratory microscope room with AC recirculation","Collect two sputum specimens (one \"on-the-spot\" and one early morning deep-cough sample) in clean wide-mouth screw-capped leak-proof containers inside an open, well-ventilated outdoor sputum collection area away from crowds; maintain cross-ventilation (\u003e= 12 ACH) in clinics and provide surgical mask + cough etiquette instruction"],"a":3,"e":"Under India’s NTEP (formerly RNTCP), presumptive pulmonary TB screening uses two sputum specimens (1st spot, 2nd early-morning mucopurulent sample; or two spot samples \u003e=1 hr apart if same-day) for AFB microscopy / CBNAAT-Truenat. Coughing generates infectious droplet nuclei (\u003c5 µm); therefore sputum collection MUST occur outdoors in sunlight/open air away from others, while clinics maintain \u003e=12 ACH and respiratory hygiene.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01650","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Wastewater and Excreta Disposal","topic":"Sanitary Septic Tank Design \u0026 Operation","q":"When inspecting a septic tank serving a rural PHC or school without municipal sewerage, what minimum liquid retention period, mosquito-proofing detail, and safe effluent disposal method are required under Indian public health engineering standards?","o":["Minimum 24-hour liquid retention time (anaerobic sludge digestion), submerged inlet/outlet T-baffles, vent pipe screened with mosquito-proof wire mesh, and effluent discharged into an underground soakage pit/dispersion trench (never open street drains)","Leave the top manhole uncovered so mosquitoes breed freely","Use harsh phenyl/carbolic acid daily inside the septic tank to kill all anaerobic bacteria","Retention time 15 minutes; discharge raw effluent directly into open roadside storm drains"],"a":0,"e":"A sanitary septic tank relies on \u003e=24-hour retention for heavy solids to settle as sludge while anaerobic bacteria digest organic waste, forming a floating scum layer trapped by submerged T-baffles. Adding soap/phenyl/strong disinfectants into a septic tank kills beneficial anaerobic flora and causes failure; periodic desludging every 2-3 years prevents overflow, and mosquito-screened vent pipes prevent Culex breeding while effluent percolates safely into a soakage pit.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01651","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Hospital \u0026 Community Disinfection","topic":"Phasing Out Formalin Fumigation","q":"Why have modern MoHFW India / CDC infection control guidelines phased out traditional Potassium Permanganate + Formalin gas fumigation of hospital wards, isolation rooms, and operating theatres?","o":["Formalin evaporates completely in 2 seconds with zero health risk","Formaldehyde gas is a known human carcinogen, severe ocular/respiratory irritant, leaves toxic residue, and penetrates poorly compared with thorough detergent-disinfectant wet wiping/mopping or validated Hydrogen Peroxide Vapor (HPV) fogging","Formalin fumigation multiplies MRSA bacteria by 1,000%","Formalin gas turns white hospital walls permanently bright blue"],"a":1,"e":"Mixing formalin with potassium permanganate releases toxic, carcinogenic formaldehyde vapors (IARC Group 1 carcinogen) posing severe acute mucosal and long-term occupational cancer risks to healthcare workers while failing to clean organic soil. Current national HIC guidelines mandate mechanical detergent cleaning + EPA-registered wet surface disinfection (or enclosed no-touch Hydrogen Peroxide Vapor/mist systems only after manual cleaning when indicated).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01652","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Food Hygiene and Toxicology","topic":"Neurolathyrism (Khesari Dal / Lathyrus sativus)","q":"In a drought-prone agricultural belt, poor laborers consuming \u003e30% of their daily diet as cheap \u0027Khesari dal\u0027 (Lathyrus sativus) develop irreversible spastic paraparesis of both legs (\u0027no-stick / one-stick / two-stick / crawling\u0027 gait stages—neurolathyrism). Which water-soluble neurotoxin causes this, and which simple household processing step removes \u003e80-90% of the toxin?","o":["Sanguinarine alkaloid; freezing for 1 hour removes it","Aflatoxin B1 from Aspergillus flavus; deep-frying in mustard oil destroys it","Beta-oxalyl-amino-alanine (BOAA / ODAP); steeping the dehusked seeds in abundant hot water for 2 hours and discarding the steep water completely (or parboiling) removes the water-soluble toxin","Botulinum toxin; adding raw sugar neutralizes it"],"a":2,"e":"Lathyrus sativus (grass pea / Khesari dal) survives severe drought and contains the excitotoxic glutamate analog BOAA (beta-N-oxalyl-L-alpha,beta-diaminopropionic acid / ODAP), causing upper motor neuron corticospinal tract degeneration (neurolathyrism). Because BOAA is water-soluble, soaking/steeping in hot water and draining off the water (or parboiling + vitamin C rich diet + low-BOAA varieties) eliminates ~80-90% of the neurotoxin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01653","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Medical Entomology and Vector Control","topic":"Phlebotomus argentipes Sandfly Control","q":"When supervising vector control in a Kala-azar (visceral leishmaniasis) endemic block of Bihar/Jharkhand, why is synthetic pyrethroid Indoor Residual Spraying (IRS) focused on the LOWER 6 feet (1.8 meters) of indoor damp mud walls and cattle sheds, combined with plastering wall cracks?","o":["Because the vector sandfly (Phlebotomus argentipes) is a weak nocturnal hopper that breeds in damp dark organic soil crevices and seldom flies/rests higher than 6 feet above the floor","Because sandflies live exclusively inside 100-foot tall palm tree crowns","Because sandfly larvae swim inside open municipal overhead drinking-water tanks","Because pyrethroid spray evaporates above 6 feet into ozone"],"a":0,"e":"Phlebotomus argentipes (hairy-winged tiny sandfly, ~2-3 mm, lacking aquatic larval stage) lays eggs in moist, dark, organic soil/mud cracks near human dwellings and cattle sheds, and adults move by short hops close to the ground (\u003c6 ft). Targeted alpha-cypermethrin IRS up to 6 ft wall height plus sealing cracks/crevices with mud-lime plaster breaks Kala-azar transmission efficiently under India\u0027s elimination programme.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01654","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Rural Water Supply Sanitation","topic":"Criteria of a Sanitary Dug Well","q":"Which engineering features define a \u0027Sanitary Well\u0027 capable of protecting groundwater from surface runoff and user rope-bucket contamination?","o":["Open mud-lined step-well allowing villagers to walk down into the water to bathe and wash clothes","Located 2 meters downhill from a leaking unlined pit latrine","Zero parapet wall flush with muddy ground so rainwater washes directly into the well","Impermeable brick/cement lining \u003e= 6 meters (20 ft) deep, raised parapet wall \u003e= 70-75 cm high, sloping concrete apron \u003e= 1 meter wide draining into a lined pucca drain, watertight cover with handpump (eliminating individual ropes/buckets), and \u003e= 15 meters from contamination sources"],"a":3,"e":"Open step-wells and unlined shallow dug wells transmit dracunculiasis, cholera, typhoid, and hepatitis A via dirty footwear and contaminated household ropes/buckets. A Sanitary Well seals the top 6 m casing against shallow contaminated seepage, uses a \u003e=70-75 cm parapet and \u003e=1 m sloped apron to divert spilled water away, and closes the wellhead with a clean handpump.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01655","course":"Diploma in Health Inspector / Sanitary Inspector","subject":"Occupational Health and Industrial Hygiene","topic":"Occupational Lead Poisoning (Plumbism)","q":"During an inspection of a lead-acid battery recycling unit, workers exhibit microcytic anemia with punctate basophilic stippling of RBCs, a bluish-purple Burtonian line along the gingival margin, abdominal colic, and bilateral wrist drop. Which industrial hygiene control is MOST effective at the workplace level alongside medical chelation/removal from exposure?","o":["Wet mopping/HEPA vacuuming (banning dry sweeping), local exhaust ventilation at smelting/soldering stations, mandatory PPE/showers before eating, and periodic Blood Lead Level (BLL) screening","Measuring only room ambient noise in decibels","Dry sweeping lead dust with brooms every morning and letting workers eat lunch at the smelting bench","Substituting inorganic lead dust with metallic mercury"],"a":0,"e":"Inorganic lead exposure occurs primarily by inhaling lead fumes/dust and ingesting hand-to-mouth dust during breaks. Dry sweeping re-aerosolizes toxic lead dust; engineering controls (local exhaust hoods, wet cleaning/HEPA vacuuming), strict handwashing/changing clothes before meals, and periodic biological monitoring (Blood Lead Levels, ZPP/delta-ALA) prevent irreversible peripheral neuropathy (wrist drop) and nephropathy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01656","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization and Disinfection","topic":"Autoclave Monitoring","q":"Before running the first surgical instrument load in a pre-vacuum steam autoclave each morning, an OT technician performs a Bowie-Dick test. What specific parameter does the Bowie-Dick test evaluate?","o":["Destruction of Geobacillus stearothermophilus bacterial spores","Adequacy of air removal and steam penetration in a pre-vacuum sterilizer","Proteinaceous soil left on laparoscopic instruments after ultrasonic washing","Residual ethylene oxide gas concentration inside wrapped packs"],"a":1,"e":"The Bowie-Dick test is a daily diagnostic test performed on an empty pre-vacuum (prevac) steam sterilizer at 134 degrees C for 3.5 minutes to detect air leaks, inadequate vacuum pump function, or non-condensable gases that prevent uniform steam penetration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01657","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Instrumentation and Equipment","topic":"Monopolar vs Bipolar Cautery","q":"When preparing a patient with a right total hip metallic prosthesis for abdominal laparotomy using monopolar electrocautery, where should the OT technician place the adhesive dispersive (patient return) pad?","o":["Cut the adhesive pad in half so it fits neatly over the calf muscle","Directly over the right hip surgical scar and metallic prosthesis to ground the implant","On a clean, dry, hairless, well-vascularized muscular area like the left anterior thigh close to the surgical site, avoiding the right hip prosthesis","Over a bony prominence such as the iliac crest or patella for firm adhesion"],"a":2,"e":"Monopolar current travels from the active electrode through the patient to the dispersive pad. Placing the pad over well-vascularized muscle (e.g., left thigh) minimizes impedance and heat concentration, while avoiding metallic implants, bony prominences, scar tissue, and excess hair prevents alternate-site or pad-site thermal burns.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01658","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Operating Theatre Protocols","topic":"WHO Surgical Safety Checklist","q":"During closure of the peritoneum in an exploratory laparotomy, the scrub technician and circulating nurse discover a discrepancy of one abdominal mop (laparotomy sponge) in the closing count. What is the mandatory protocol action?","o":["Close the skin first and order a routine chest X-ray in the recovery ward the next morning","Subtract one sponge from the initial count sheet to match the final tray","Document the missing mop as a clerical counting error if the surgeon feels confident nothing was left inside","Immediately inform the surgeon, halt closure, search the operative field, drapes, and kick-bucket, and obtain an intraoperative radiograph if still unaccounted for"],"a":3,"e":"Any count discrepancy must be announced immediately to the surgical team so closure is paused, a methodical recount and search of the wound, sterile field, floor, and kick-buckets is conducted, and an intraoperative X-ray (detecting the radiopaque marker in surgical mops) is obtained before closing if the item remains missing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01659","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Patient Positioning and OT Environment","topic":"Lithotomy Position Complications","q":"While positioning a patient in the dorsal lithotomy position using candy-cane or Allen stirrups for a transurethral resection, improper lateral pressure of the stirrup bar against the fibular head most commonly injures which nerve?","o":["Common peroneal (fibular) nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve at the popliteal fossa"],"a":0,"e":"In lithotomy positioning, the common peroneal nerve wraps superficially around the neck/head of the fibula on the lateral aspect of the knee. Unpadded pressure against lateral stirrup poles compresses this nerve, resulting in postoperative foot drop and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01660","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Endoscopic and Laparoscopic Equipment","topic":"High-Level Disinfection","q":"When performing manual high-level disinfection (HLD) of a flexible cystoscope using 2% alkaline glutaraldehyde (Cidex) after thorough enzymatic pre-cleaning, what critical post-soaking step is essential before using the instrument on the next patient?","o":["Immerse the fiberoptic cable in boiling water for 5 minutes","Thoroughly rinse the exterior and internal channels with sterile water and dry with filtered air to prevent chemical mucosal burns","Wipe only the exterior handle with dry gauze and leave residual glutaraldehyde inside the lumen for ongoing sterility","Rinse with unfiltered tap water and store wet in a sealed dark box"],"a":1,"e":"Glutaraldehyde is toxic and causes severe chemical cystitis, proctitis, or ocular irritation if residue remains on an endoscope. After HLD immersion (typically 20 minutes at 20-25 degrees C for vegetative bacteria/viruses/mycobacteria), devices must be thoroughly rinsed with sterile water (and channels flushed) followed by drying.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01661","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization and Disinfection","topic":"Ethylene Oxide (EtO) Sterilization","q":"Why must heat- and moisture-sensitive polymer catheters and cameras sterilized with 100% Ethylene Oxide (EtO) gas undergo a dedicated mechanical aeration cycle before surgical use?","o":["To rehydrate dried blood left inside instrument lumens","To coat the surgical tubing with an antimicrobial silicone layer","To remove residual toxic, carcinogenic, and tissue-irritating EtO gas absorbed into plastics and rubber","To cool down instruments from 134 degrees C steam sterilization temperature"],"a":2,"e":"EtO is an alkylating gas effective at 37-55 degrees C for heat-sensitive items, but it penetrates plastics and elastomers. Residual EtO causes severe chemical burns, hemolysis, airway inflammation, and occupational carcinogenicity/mutagenicity; therefore, heated mechanical aeration (typically 8-12 hours at 50-60 degrees C) is mandatory before release.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01662","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Instrumentation and Equipment","topic":"Tourniquet Pressure and Safe Inflation Time","q":"When applying a pneumatic tourniquet to the upper arm of an adult with baseline systolic BP of 120 mmHg for orthopedic forearm surgery, what is the standard safe cuff pressure range and maximum continuous single-inflation duration before alerting the surgeon?","o":["50-100 mmHg above systolic BP (~170-220 mmHg for upper limb); alert surgeon at 60 minutes and max ~90-120 minutes","400 mmHg fixed pressure for all adult limbs; continuous inflation safe up to 6 hours","30 mmHg below systolic BP to allow venous pooling; no time limit","Inflate directly over bare skin without under-cast padding to prevent cuff slipping"],"a":0,"e":"Upper-extremity pneumatic tourniquets typically require 50-100 mmHg above systolic BP (or LOP + safety margin, generally \u003c=250 mmHg; lower limbs ~100-150 mmHg above SBP or \u003c=300-350 mmHg). Prolonged ischemia risks neuropraxia and rhabdomyolysis, so the team is alerted at 60 minutes and safe upper-limb continuous duration generally stays within 90-120 minutes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01663","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Patient Positioning and OT Environment","topic":"Positive Pressure Ventilation and HEPA Filtration","q":"According to Indian NABH / CDC operating theatre HVAC standards for conventional sterile OTs, what pressure relationship, minimum total air changes per hour (ACH), and terminal filter type are required?","o":["Negative pressure (-5 Pa) relative to corridors, 4 ACH, and coarse mesh pre-filters only","85% relative humidity at 30 degrees C with recirculated un-filtered ward air","Zero pressure differential with open exterior windows for natural cross-ventilation","Positive pressure relative to adjacent corridors (+2.5 to +15 Pa), \u003e=20 ACH (at least 4 fresh air changes), and 99.97% efficiency HEPA filters (0.3 micron)"],"a":3,"e":"Sterile operating theatres maintain positive pressure relative to corridors/sub-sterile areas so airborne contaminants flow outward when doors open, with \u003e=20 total air changes per hour (minimum 4 outdoor ACH), temperature 18-24 degrees C (typically 21 +/- 3 degrees C), relative humidity 20-60% (typically 30-60%), and terminal HEPA filtration (99.97% at 0.3 microns; ultra-clean orthopedic/implant OTs often use laminar airflow).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01664","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization and Disinfection","topic":"Ultrasonic Cleaning Principles","q":"How does an ultrasonic washer remove microscopic blood and tissue debris from serrated clamps and box-locks, and what critical pre-step must be done before placing instruments into the ultrasonic bath?","o":["By high-frequency sound waves generating collapsing microscopic vacuum bubbles (cavitation); grossly visible soil must be pre-rinsed/cleaned enzymatically first and instruments opened","By UV-C light irradiation; leave all hinged joints locked shut","By 70% isopropyl alcohol immersion; mix stainless steel and aluminum/chrome instruments together in one basket","By boiling water at 121 degrees C with high-pressure steam; instruments should be tightly ratcheted closed"],"a":0,"e":"Ultrasonic cleaners emit 20-80 kHz acoustic waves that create microscopic cavitation bubbles whose implosion dislodges soil from hinges and serrations. Instruments must first have gross organic debris removed (enzymatic pre-wash), hinges/box-locks opened, dissimilar metals separated to prevent galvanic corrosion, and non-immersible power drills/optics excluded.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01665","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Asepsis and Surgical Scrubbing","topic":"Sterile Gown Boundaries and Contamination","q":"Once a surgical scrub technician has donned a sterile gown and closed-method sterile gloves, which regions of the gown are considered sterile during the operation?","o":["Only the front from chest/axillary level down to the sterile table/waist level, plus the sleeves from 2 inches above the elbow down to the cuff","The entire front and back of the gown from neck collar to shoe covers","Only the gloved hands; the sleeves above the wrist are considered unsterile","The underarm axillary fold and back tie area where the circulating nurse fastens the gown"],"a":0,"e":"Under Association of periOperative Registered Nurses (AORN) aseptic principles, a sterile gown is sterile only in front from mid-chest to the level of the sterile field (waist/table top) and along the sleeves from 2 inches above the elbow to the cuff. The neckline, shoulders, axillae, back, and anything below waist/table level are unsterile.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01666","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization and Disinfection","topic":"Event-Related Sterility","q":"While opening a steam-autoclaved orthopedic instrument tray wrapped in double blue surgical wrap, the OT technician notices damp water droplets inside the outer wrapper (\u0027wet pack\u0027) and a torn corner seal. Even though the external autoclave tape stripes turned dark brown/black, what must the technician do?","o":["Place the damp tray in front of an electric fan for 15 minutes until dry and proceed","Reject the entire tray as contaminated, re-clean/re-wrap, and re-sterilize because moisture wicking breaches the sterile barrier","Use the inner instruments if the chemical indicator strip inside looks okay","Wipe the wet wrapper with 70% alcohol and transfer instruments onto the sterile trolley"],"a":1,"e":"Under modern event-related sterility standards, sterility depends on an intact, dry barrier rather than calendar time. Moisture inside or on a sterilized pack (\u0027wet pack\u0027) allows microbial capillary wicking (\u0027strike-through\u0027) from the outside environment into the sterile contents; external tape only confirms heat exposure, not sterility. The tray must be rejected and reprocessed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01667","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Endoscopic and Laparoscopic Equipment","topic":"CO2 Pneumoperitoneum Safety","q":"When setting up the laparoscopic CO2 insufflator for an adult laparoscopic cholecystectomy, what is the standard target intra-abdominal pressure limit, and what physiological hazard occurs if pressure is inadvertently set to 22-25 mmHg?","o":["Standard target 2-4 mmHg; pressures \u003e20 mmHg cause spontaneous systemic hypothermia only","Use compressed room air instead of CO2 to eliminate all hemodynamic effects","Standard target 12-15 mmHg (starting at low 1-2 L/min flow); pressures \u003e20 mmHg compress the inferior vena cava, sharply reduce venous return/cardiac output, impair renal perfusion, and elevate peak airway pressures","Standard target 30-35 mmHg; high pressure improves hepatic venous outflow"],"a":2,"e":"Adult laparoscopic surgery uses medical-grade CO2 (non-combustible and highly blood-soluble) initiated at low flow (1-2 L/min) to verify intraperitoneal Veress/trocar placement, then maintained at 12-15 mmHg. Excessive pneumoperitoneum (\u003e20 mmHg) compresses the IVC and abdominal aorta, decreasing preload and cardiac output while splinting the diaphragm and increasing risk of CO2 embolism or subcutaneous emphysema.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01668","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Operating Theatre Protocols","topic":"Fire Triad in the Operating Room","q":"During an awake tracheostomy or facial lesion excision under monitored anesthesia care with nasal cannula oxygen at 6 L/min, the surgeon prepares to use a monopolar cautery pencil immediately after the skin was prepped with 70% isopropyl alcohol-chlorhexidine. What two critical fire hazards exist here?","o":["Saline-soaked gauze sponges plus room air","Using a cold scalpel blade over dry povidone-iodine skin prep","Bipolar forceps under water irrigation","Un-dried pooled alcohol vapor (fuel) plus open oxygen enrichment (oxidizer) under drapes, creating an extreme flash-fire risk when cautery sparks (ignition)"],"a":3,"e":"The surgical fire triad consists of oxidizer (O2/N2O enriched tenting under drapes), fuel (alcohol-based prep fumes, dry drapes, hair), and ignition source (ESU cautery, laser, fiberoptic light cable). Alcohol preps must dry completely for at least 3 minutes without pooling in skin creases/umbilicus, drapes must prevent O2 tenting, and open O2 must be reduced/stopped before activating cautery near the head/neck.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01669","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization and Disinfection","topic":"Gravity Displacement vs Pre-Vacuum Parameters","q":"What are the standard minimum exposure temperature, pressure, and holding time parameters for wrapped surgical instrument packs in a conventional gravity-displacement steam autoclave versus a dynamic air-removal (pre-vacuum) steam sterilizer?","o":["Gravity displacement: 121 degrees C (250 degrees F) at 15 psi for 30 minutes; Pre-vacuum: 132-134 degrees C (270-273 degrees F) at ~27-30 psi for 3-4 minutes","Gravity displacement: 100 degrees C at 0 psi for 10 minutes; Pre-vacuum: 180 degrees C for 1 second","Gravity displacement: 134 degrees C for 2 hours; Pre-vacuum: 60 degrees C for 15 minutes","Both gravity and pre-vacuum cycles operate at 121 degrees C for 3 minutes"],"a":0,"e":"Saturated steam under pressure destroys heat-resistant bacterial spores rapidly when air is fully displaced. Standard wrapped pack parameters are 121 degrees C at 15 psi (103 kPa gauge) for 30 min in gravity-displacement autoclaves, and 132-134 degrees C (~27-30 psi) for 3-4 min (followed by vacuum drying) in pre-vacuum autoclaves.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01670","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Supplies and Sutures","topic":"Needle Point Geometry","q":"When setting up the Mayo stand for an intestinal anastomosis and subsequent skin closure, which needle point configurations should the scrub technician hand to the surgeon respectively?","o":["Round-bodied taper-point needle for bowel anastomosis; reverse-cutting (or conventional cutting) triangular-tip needle for skin closure","Blunt-point liver needle for subcuticular skin closure; cutting needle for hepatic parenchyma","Reverse-cutting needle for delicate bowel/vascular anastomosis; taper-point needle for thick palmar skin","Spatulated microsurgical corneal needle for abdominal fascia"],"a":0,"e":"Round-bodied taper-point needles gently spread soft parenchymal, seromuscular bowel, and vascular wall fibers without slicing them, minimizing suture-hole bleeding or anastomotic tearing. Tough, dense collagenous dermis (skin) requires a sharp triangular cutting or reverse-cutting needle (with third cutting edge on the outer convex curvature to prevent tissue cutout).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01671","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization and Disinfection","topic":"Spaulding Classification","q":"Under the Spaulding Classification system for medical device reprocessing, how are surgical hemostats/vascular clamps versus flexible gastrointestinal endoscopes/laryngoscope blades categorized, and what minimum processing level does each require?","o":["Hemostats: Critical items requiring sterilization; Flexible endoscopes/laryngoscope blades: Semi-critical items requiring at least High-Level Disinfection (HLD)","Hemostats: Non-critical requiring soap and water; Endoscopes: Non-critical requiring dry dusting","Hemostats: Semi-critical requiring 70% alcohol wipe; Endoscopes: Critical requiring dry-heat oven at 170 degrees C","Both hemostats and flexible gastroscopes require low-level quaternary ammonium wiping only"],"a":0,"e":"Earle Spaulding classified devices by infection risk: Critical items enter sterile tissue or the vascular system (surgical instruments, implants, cardiac catheters) and MUST be sterilized; Semi-critical items contact intact mucous membranes or non-intact skin (flexible endoscopes, laryngoscope blades, respiratory circuits) and require at least High-Level Disinfection (or sterilization if heat-stable); Non-critical items contact only intact skin (BP cuffs, stethoscopes) and need intermediate/low-level disinfection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01672","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization and Disinfection","topic":"Spore Strip Organism Selection","q":"Which standard bacterial spore species are used respectively in biological indicators (BIs) to validate steam autoclaves (and hydrogen peroxide plasma) versus Ethylene Oxide (EtO) and dry-heat sterilizers?","o":["Staphylococcus aureus for steam; Escherichia coli for EtO","Geobacillus stearothermophilus for steam/H2O2 plasma; Bacillus atrophaeus (formerly B. subtilis var. niger) for EtO and dry heat","Mycobacterium tuberculosis for steam; Pseudomonas aeruginosa for dry heat","Bacillus atrophaeus for steam; Geobacillus stearothermophilus for 70% alcohol wipes"],"a":1,"e":"Biological indicators challenge sterilizers with the most resistant non-pathogenic spores for that modality: thermophilic Geobacillus stearothermophilus (incubated at 55-60 degrees C) is most resistant to moist heat/steam and vaporized H2O2, whereas Bacillus atrophaeus (incubated at 35-37 degrees C) is most resistant to Ethylene Oxide gas and dry heat.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01673","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Asepsis and Preoperative Prep","topic":"Clean-to-Dirty Prepping Principle","q":"When prepping the abdomen of a patient scheduled for an exploratory laparotomy who also has an existing draining ileostomy on the right lower quadrant, how must the OT technician perform the antiseptic skin preparation?","o":["Start directly over the ileostomy stoma and scrub across the clean midline incision with the same sponge","Scrub back and forth randomly across the entire abdomen using one single sponge","Prepping from the clean midline incision line outward to the periphery, discarding each sponge at the edge, and prepping the ileostomy stoma LAST with a separate sponge","Skip skin antisepsis around the abdomen because sterile drapes cover the skin"],"a":2,"e":"Surgical skin antisepsis always progresses from the cleanest area (intended incision site) outward to the less clean periphery using friction, never returning a peripheral sponge back toward the incision center. Heavily contaminated areas (stomas, open wounds, umbilicus, anus, sinuses) are prepped last or isolated with separate sponges to prevent dragging enteric flora onto the surgical incision.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01674","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Orthopaedic OT Procedures","topic":"PMMA Bone Cement Mixing Safety","q":"During cemented hip hemiarthroplasty in an elderly patient, the scrub technician mixes liquid methylmethacrylate monomer with polymer powder. What occupational safety precaution applies to mixing, and what acute intraoperative complication must the team monitor closely when the surgeon pressurizes cement into the femoral canal?","o":["Mix over an open flame to speed polymerization; monitor solely for hyperglycemia","Use bare ungloved hands to feel cement warmth; monitor for hypertension only","Pour excess liquid monomer directly into the patient\u0027s urinary catheter","Mix in a closed vacuum mixing system to minimize toxic monomer vapor exposure (and avoid contact lens wear if open mixing); monitor for Bone Cement Implantation Syndrome (sudden hypotension, hypoxemia, arrhythmia)"],"a":3,"e":"Liquid methylmethacrylate monomer is volatile, neurotoxic/respiratory-irritant, and penetrates soft contact lenses and standard latex gloves (requiring double-gloving/closed vacuum mixing with fume extraction). During femoral pressurization, marrow fat/air/debris embolization causes Bone Cement Implantation Syndrome (BCIS)—characterized by abrupt hypotension, desaturation, pulmonary hypertension, and possible cardiac arrest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01675","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Operating Theatre Safety","topic":"Neutral Zone Passing Technique","q":"To prevent percutaneous needlestick and scalpel injuries between the scrub technician and surgeon during an operation, what standardized engineering/work-practice technique should be used whenever passing sharp instruments?","o":["Hands-free Neutral Zone (placing the scalpel or loaded needle holder in a designated basin/kidney tray or magnetic mat and announcing \u0027sharp\u0027 rather than hand-to-hand passing)","Passing uncapped #11 scalpels blade-first directly into the surgeon\u0027s palm while they look at the monitor","Recapping used suture needles with both hands before handing them back","Leaving loose scalpel blades scattered beneath gauze sponges on the Mayo stand"],"a":0,"e":"Direct hand-to-hand passing of scalpels and suture needles is a major source of occupational bloodborne pathogen exposure (HBV, HCV, HIV) in the OR. Using a designated hands-free Neutral Zone (transfer tray or magnetic pad) with clear verbal notification ensures only one person touches a sharp instrument at any moment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01676","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Asepsis and Surgical Scrubbing","topic":"Hand Scrubbing and Rinsing Posture","q":"During surgical hand scrubbing and rinsing at the scrub sink prior to entering the operating theatre, how must the scrubbed technician hold their hands and forearms while rinsing off antiseptic suds?","o":["Hold fingertips pointed upward above elbow level so rinse water runs downward from clean fingertips toward the less-clean elbows, passing each arm through the water stream in one direction","Point fingertips downward toward the sink drain so elbow water runs over the fingers","Shake hands vigorously at waist level to flick off excess water drops","Dry hands first with a non-sterile paper towel before stepping into the OT"],"a":0,"e":"Surgical hand antisepsis maintains a cleanliness gradient from fingertips (cleanest) to elbows (least clean). Holding hands higher than elbows throughout scrubbing, rinsing (passing fingertips-to-elbows one way under water without backing up), and walking into the OT ensures gravity drains water away from sterile hands rather than washing elbow skin flora onto fingertips.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01677","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization and Disinfection","topic":"Type 1 Process Indicator vs Type 5 Integrator","q":"Before handing surgical instruments from a newly opened steam-autoclaved tray to the surgeon, the scrub technician checks the internal chemical strip placed inside the center of the tray. Why is an ISO Type 5 Integrating Indicator required inside every pack in addition to external Type 1 autoclave tape?","o":["External Type 1 tape only proves the outside of the pack was exposed to heat/process, whereas an internal Type 5 integrator reacts to ALL critical sterilization variables (time, temperature, and saturated steam moisture) inside the pack","Type 5 integrators replace the need for cleaning instruments before autoclaving","External Type 1 tape confirms the interior of the tray stayed sterile for 10 years","Type 1 tape counts the exact number of bacterial spores killed inside the instruments"],"a":0,"e":"Per ISO 11140-1, Type 1 process indicators (autoclave tape stripes on wrappers) merely differentiate processed from unprocessed packages and turn dark even in dry heat or incomplete cycles. Type 5 integrating indicators (placed inside the center/most challenging spot of each tray) respond cumulatively to all critical parameters (time, temperature, steam presence) correlated with biological spore kill.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01678","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Endoscopic and Laparoscopic Equipment","topic":"Veress Needle Safety Checks","q":"Before handing a reusable Veress insufflation needle to the surgeon for closed laparoscopic abdominal entry, what functional check must the OT technician perform on the needle tip?","o":["Connect the Veress needle directly to the wall vacuum suction port","Verify the spring-loaded blunt inner stylet retracts smoothly upon pressing against a hard sterile surface and snaps forward immediately when released, and flush the lumen with saline to confirm patency","Glue the safety shield permanently in the retracted position","Sharpen the inner blunt stylet with a diamond file so it cuts fascia faster"],"a":1,"e":"A Veress needle protects bowel and aorta/iliac vessels via a spring-loaded blunt hollow stylet that retracts as the sharp outer bevel pierces fascia/peritoneum and springs forward instantly once resistance drops inside the peritoneal cavity. Testing spring action, stopcock integrity, and lumen patency beforehand prevents catastrophic retroperitoneal vascular or bowel perforation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01679","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Urological OT Procedures","topic":"Monopolar vs Bipolar TURP Irrigation","q":"An OT technician prepares irrigation bags for a transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP). Why must isotonic 0.9% normal saline NEVER be used with traditional MONOPOLAR resectoscopes, and what maximum height above the patient\u0027s bladder should irrigation bags be hung to reduce TURP syndrome risk?","o":["Electrolyte-containing 0.9% saline disperses monopolar electrical current (preventing cutting/coagulation), so non-conductive 1.5% glycine is used instead; hang bags \u003c=60 cm above bladder level","0.9% saline dissolves the stainless steel resectoscope sheath immediately; hang bags 3 meters high","1.5% glycine conducts monopolar current faster than saline; hang bags on the floor","Distilled sterile water is always the safest high-volume irrigation fluid for TURP"],"a":0,"e":"Monopolar TURP requires electrically non-conductive irrigation (1.5% glycine, sorbitol, or mannitol) because ionic 0.9% saline conducts and disperses RF current away from the loop (whereas bipolar TURP specifically enables safe 0.9% saline irrigation!). Keeping irrigation bag height \u003c=60 cm above the prostate minimizes hydrostatic venous absorption of hypotonic glycine and reduces TURP syndrome (dilutional hyponatremia, cerebral edema, visual disturbances).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01680","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Specimen Handling and OT Documentation","topic":"Frozen Section vs Routine Histopathology","q":"During a Whipple pancreaticoduodenectomy, the surgeon hands the scrub technician a pancreatic neck resection margin biopsy marked \u0027STAT Frozen Section\u0027 to check for tumor clearance before reconstruction. How must the circulating technician transport this specimen to pathology?","o":["Store the specimen in 70% ethyl alcohol overnight before sending","Submerge the specimen immediately in 10% neutral buffered formalin","Place the specimen FRESH (unfixed, without formalin, optionally on saline-dampened Telfa/gauze so it does not desiccate—never submerged) in a clearly labeled dry sterile container and rush immediately to the pathology lab","Freeze the specimen directly in liquid nitrogen inside the OT trash bin"],"a":2,"e":"Intraoperative frozen section requires rapid cryostat freezing of fresh, unfixed tissue at -20 degrees C so the pathologist can report margin status within 15-20 minutes. Placing a frozen-section biopsy in formalin fixes proteins, delays freezing, alters ice-crystal architecture, and ruins rapid frozen sectioning (whereas routine permanent histopathology specimens require 10% buffered formalin at \u003e=10:1 volume ratio).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01681","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Instrumentation Care","topic":"Normal Saline Pitting Corrosion","q":"Why must surgical instruments NOT be wiped with or left soaking in 0.9% normal saline on the sterile back table or during post-operative soaking prior to decontamination?","o":["Normal saline melts tungsten-carbide needle holder inserts","Normal saline permanently turns stainless steel bright purple","Normal saline causes instruments to float out of ultrasonic cleaners","Chloride ions in normal saline chemically attack the protective chromium-oxide passivation layer of stainless steel, causing irreversible pitting corrosion and rusting"],"a":3,"e":"Blood and 0.9% sodium chloride contain corrosive chloride ions that break down the thin passive chromium-oxide surface film on martensitic/austenitic stainless steel, producing microscopic pitting corrosion and stiff box-locks. During surgery, instruments should be wiped with sterile distilled water (or moistened sponge) rather than saline, and post-op soaked in pH-neutral enzymatic detergent solution.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01682","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Operation Theatre Procedures \u0026 Patient Safety","topic":"WHO Surgical Safety Checklist","q":"During the WHO Surgical Safety Checklist workflow in the operating theatre, when is the \"Time Out\" phase conducted, and which critical verification occurs at that moment?","o":["Immediately before skin incision; the whole team pauses to confirm patient identity, marked surgical site, procedure, antibiotic prophylaxis, and critical anticipated events","After skin closure before transferring to recovery; only instrument and sponge counts are tallied","In the surgical ward the night before surgery; only the ward nurse signs the consent form","Before induction of anaesthesia; solely the anaesthesia technician checks pulse oximetry"],"a":0,"e":"The WHO Surgical Safety Checklist has three distinct pauses: \"Sign In\" (before induction of anaesthesia), \"Time Out\" (immediately before skin incision, with surgeon, anaesthetist, and OT nurse/technician confirming identity, procedure, site/side, prophylactic antibiotics within 60 min, and essential imaging), and \"Sign Out\" (before patient leaves OT, confirming counts, specimen labeling, and equipment issues).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01683","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization, CSSD \u0026 Infection Control","topic":"Autoclave Validation \u0026 Biological Indicators","q":"An OT technician is validating a prevacuum (dynamic-air-removal) steam autoclave load containing wrapped surgical instrument sets. Which combination of cycle parameters and biological indicator organism is standard for confirming sterilization efficacy?","o":["100°C boiling water for 10 minutes validated with Staphylococcus aureus vegetative cells","132–134°C for 4 minutes (or 121°C for 15–30 min in gravity cycle) validated with Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores","160°C dry heat for 2 minutes validated with Escherichia coli cultures","134°C steam cycle validated with Bacillus atrophaeus strips incubated for 1 hour"],"a":1,"e":"Standard prevacuum steam sterilization runs at 132–134°C for 4 minutes (gravity displacement runs at 121°C at 15 psi for 15–30 minutes). Geobacillus stearothermophilus is the highly heat-resistant non-pathogenic spore former used as the biological indicator for steam autoclaves and hydrogen peroxide plasma sterilizers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01684","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Equipment \u0026 Instrumentation","topic":"Monopolar Dispersive Electrode Safety","q":"Before a total abdominal hysterectomy using monopolar electrocautery, the OT technician prepares to apply the single-use adhesive dispersive return pad (patient plate). Which site and preparation practice is safest to prevent pad-site thermal burns?","o":["Cut the adhesive pad in half so it fits neatly over a small patch of the lower leg","Apply directly over the iliac crest bony prominence using extra alcohol-based skin prep underneath the gel","Apply firmly over a clean, dry, hair-clipped, well-vascularized muscular area (such as the anterior/lateral thigh) close to the surgical site, avoiding bony prominences and metal implants","Place over a scarred area on the calf distal to a metallic knee prosthesis"],"a":2,"e":"Safe monopolar dispersive electrode placement requires full contact on clean, dry, muscular, well-perfused tissue close to the operative field so current density remains low and heat dissipates. Bony prominences, scars, excessive hair, fluid pooling, and metallic implants increase electrical impedance and risk severe full-thickness burns.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01685","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Asepsis \u0026 Surgical Assisting","topic":"Boundaries of the Sterile Gown","q":"While assisting as a scrub technician wearing a sterile gown and gloves during laparotomy, you need to step back momentarily while the circulating nurse adjusts an overhead light. Which regions of your gowned body are considered sterile, and where must your hands be kept?","o":["Front of the gown from chest level to sterile-table/waist level, and sleeves from 2 inches above the elbow to the cuff; keep hands clasped at chest/waist level within view","Entire front and back of the gown from neck to knees; hands may rest comfortably behind the back","Only the gloved fingers; the cuffs and forearms above the wrist are considered unsterile","Front of the gown including the axillary regions and below the waist down to mid-thigh"],"a":0,"e":"In standard OR aseptic protocol, only the anterior gown from chest (mid-sternum/nipple line) down to waist/sterile-field level and the sleeves from 2 inches above the elbow to the cuff are considered sterile. Neckline, shoulders, axillae, back, and areas below the sterile field level are unsterile; hands must never drop below waist level or go behind the back.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01686","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Operation Theatre Procedures \u0026 Patient Safety","topic":"Surgical Sponge Count Discrepancy","q":"During abdominal wall closure at the end of an emergency laparotomy, the scrub technician and circulating nurse perform the closing sponge count and discover that one 10×10 cm radiopaque gauze sponge is missing. What is the immediate mandatory action?","o":["Document \"count presumed correct\" if the surgeon states visually that the abdomen looks clear","Complete skin closure first so the patient is not kept under anaesthesia, then check the laundry bag in recovery","Open a new sterile pack of 5 sponges to replace the missing item in the tally sheet","Immediately notify the surgeon, halt wound closure, re-count all sponges, systematically search the operative field, drapes, floor, and kick bucket, and obtain an intraoperative X-ray if still unaccounted for"],"a":3,"e":"An unresolved count discrepancy is a critical patient safety event (never event if retained). The scrub/circulating team must inform the surgeon immediately, pause closure, repeat the count, thoroughly inspect the wound, sterile field, floor, and waste receptacles, and take an intraoperative radiograph (using the sponge radiopaque filament) before closure if not found.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01687","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization, CSSD \u0026 Infection Control","topic":"OR Ventilation \u0026 Air Quality","q":"To prevent airborne microbial contamination and surgical site infections (SSIs) in a conventional Class B/modular operating room under NABH/NCDC guidelines, how should the room pressure gradient and filtration system be maintained relative to adjacent corridors?","o":["Positive air pressure (+2.5 Pa or higher) relative to corridors, minimum 15–20 air changes per hour, supplied through terminal HEPA filters (99.97% efficiency at 0.3 µm)","5 air changes per hour using coarse pre-filters only, with temperature maintained at 32°C","Negative air pressure (-5 Pa) relative to corridors to pull fresh air through cracks around the doors","Zero pressure differential with openable windows and split domestic air conditioners recirculating room air"],"a":0,"e":"Operating theatres must maintain positive pressure relative to adjacent semi-restricted corridors (minimum +2.5 Pa) so air flows outward when doors open, preventing contaminated corridor air from entering. Air is delivered at \u003e=15–20 ACH (typically 20–25 ACH) via HEPA filters capturing 99.97% of particles \u003e=0.3 microns, at 18–22°C and 40–60% relative humidity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01688","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Equipment \u0026 Instrumentation","topic":"Pneumatic Tourniquet Safety","q":"An adult patient (baseline systolic BP 120 mmHg) is undergoing knee arthroscopy with a pneumatic thigh tourniquet. Which setup and monitoring practice minimizes the risk of tourniquet-induced nerve palsy and ischemic muscle injury?","o":["Pad the proximal thigh smoothly, apply cuff snugly, inflate to ~100–150 mmHg above systolic BP (typically 250–300 mmHg for lower limb), prevent prep solution pooling under cuff, and alert surgeon at 90–120 minutes continuous inflation","Inflate every thigh tourniquet routinely to 450 mmHg regardless of patient blood pressure for up to 4 hours continuously","Place the cuff directly over the fibular head without padding so skin slip is avoided","Allow aqueous povidone-iodine to soak beneath the tourniquet cuff to keep the limb cool"],"a":0,"e":"Safe pneumatic tourniquet use requires soft wrinkle-free padding, barrier protection so prep liquids do not seep under the cuff (chemical burns), lowest effective occlusion pressure (limb occlusion pressure or ~100–150 mmHg above SBP for lower limb, ~50–100 mmHg above SBP for upper limb), and time alerts around 90–120 minutes to consider deflation/reperfusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01689","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization, CSSD \u0026 Infection Control","topic":"Blood Spill Decontamination","q":"Following an orthopedic surgery, there is a large blood spill on the OT floor. Hospital infection control policy requires fresh 1% sodium hypochlorite solution for large blood spills. You have a stock bottle labeled 5% sodium hypochlorite. How should you prepare 1,000 mL (1 liter) of 1% working solution?","o":["Mix 100 mL of 5% sodium hypochlorite stock with 900 mL of hot alcohol","Mix 200 mL of 5% sodium hypochlorite stock with 800 mL of clean water","Mix 20 mL of 5% sodium hypochlorite stock with 980 mL of clean water","Mix 500 mL of 5% sodium hypochlorite stock with 500 mL of clean water"],"a":1,"e":"Using C1V1 = C2V2: 5% × V1 = 1% × 1000 mL → V1 = 1000 / 5 = 200 mL of 5% stock solution, diluted with 800 mL of water (1:4 ratio) to make 1,000 mL of 1% (10,000 ppm) sodium hypochlorite. Apply over absorbent material for 10–20 minutes contact time while wearing PPE.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01690","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Equipment \u0026 Instrumentation","topic":"Suture Classification","q":"During vascular anastomosis, the surgeon requests a synthetic, monofilament, non-absorbable suture on a swaged taper-point needle to minimize tissue drag and bacterial harboring. Which suture material matches this specification?","o":["Braided Silk","Polyglactin 910 (Vicryl)","Polypropylene (Prolene)","Chromic Catgut"],"a":2,"e":"Polypropylene is a synthetic, monofilament, non-absorbable suture with low tissue reactivity, smooth passage through vessel walls, and minimal bacterial wicking, making it the gold standard for vascular anastomoses on a swaged taper-point needle.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01691","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Asepsis and Sterile Field","topic":"Sterile Drape Placement Integrity","q":"While unfolding and placing a sterile fenestrated abdominal drape over the patient\u0027s prepped abdomen, the scrub technician accidentally drops the drape 5 cm too far caudally, partially covering the prepped midline incision site. What is the ONLY aseptically correct action?","o":["Cut a new hole in the misplaced drape with unsterile bandage scissors","Pull the drape 5 cm upward toward the xiphoid so the fenestration lines up properly","Lift the edge off the floor below table level and tuck it back onto the sterile field","Leave the misplaced drape in place or discard/remove it completely and apply a fresh sterile drape—NEVER slide or pull the drape upward toward the incision site"],"a":3,"e":"Once a sterile drape makes contact with the patient\u0027s skin, shifting or pulling it back toward the incision drags unsterile peripheral skin flora and lower-edge organisms directly onto the prepared surgical wound. Similarly, any portion of a drape hanging below the sterile table/operating bed edge is unsterile and must never be lifted back up.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01692","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic and Microsurgical OT","topic":"Pre-Case Phaco Setup and Priming","q":"Before cataract surgery begins, an OT technician sets up the phacoemulsification machine and ophthalmic operating microscope. Which pre-incision verification steps are essential for patient ocular safety?","o":["Prime and tune the phaco/irrigation-aspiration handpiece with BSS using the sterile test chamber to purge all air bubbles and verify vacuum/ultrasound self-test; balance the microscope arm with magnetic locks and verify coaxial red-reflex illumination","Skip priming so air bubbles enter the anterior chamber for better lens visualization","Use tap water instead of Balanced Salt Solution (BSS) for intraocular irrigation","Lock the microscope oculars at fixed 0-diopter focus without checking surgeon interpupillary distance or sterile drapes"],"a":0,"e":"Purging air from phacoemulsification/I\u0026A tubing using Balanced Salt Solution (BSS) in a sterile test chamber ensures stable anterior chamber fluidics, prevents air-bubble corneal endothelial trauma, and validates piezoelectric ultrasound tuning before eye entry. Balancing the operating microscope ensures smooth X-Y/focus pedal movement without mechanical drift during capsulorhexis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01693","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Cardiovascular and Vascular OT","topic":"Fogarty Embolectomy Balloon Testing","q":"Before handing a Fogarty arterial embolectomy balloon catheter to the vascular surgeon for an acute femoral thromboembolectomy, how must the scrub technician test balloon integrity, and which vascular clamp design prevents crushing atherosclerotic intima?","o":["Clamp the silicone catheter with bare metal hemostats without rubber shod boots","Test-inflate the latex balloon gently with sterile normal saline (NOT air) to the exact labeled syringe volume to check symmetry/leaks, then deflate completely; prepare atraumatic fine-serrated DeBakey or Satinsky clamps","Inflate the balloon forcibly with 10 mL of room air until stretched to double size","Use heavy crushing Kocher clamps directly across the femoral artery"],"a":1,"e":"Testing a Fogarty embolectomy balloon with sterile normal saline (never air, which risks arterial air embolism if the balloon ruptures inside the vessel) confirms smooth symmetric inflation and intact seal at manufacturer-specified volume. Atraumatic DeBakey/Satinsky vascular clamps grip arteries gently without fracturing intimal plaques or causing thrombosis, whereas bare metal hemostats shred delicate catheters unless rubber-shodded.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01694","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Operating Theatre Safety","topic":"Airway and Ocular Laser Precautions","q":"During laryngeal papilloma ablation using a CO2 laser in the operating theatre, which combination of safety measures is mandatory to protect the patient, surgical team, and airway?","o":["Ordinary PVC endotracheal tube inflated with pure nitrous oxide","Standard clear prescription eyeglasses only, dry alcohol-soaked towels around the airway, and 100% FiO2","Wavelength-specific optical-density eye protection for all personnel, moistened saline gauze/shields over the patient\u0027s eyes and surrounding tissues, laser-resistant ETT with cuff inflated with methylene-blue-tinted saline, lowered FiO2 (\u003c30% without N2O), and dedicated ULPA smoke evacuator","Turn off the smoke evacuator so viral aerosol clears naturally into the corridor"],"a":2,"e":"CO2 lasers (10,600 nm) cause irreversible corneal burns, ignite dry PVC tubes in oxygen-enriched airways (airway fire), and aerosolize infectious HPV/viral DNA in laser plume. Safety mandates wavelength-matched laser goggles, wet protective drapes/eye shields, specialized foil/metal laser-resistant ETTs with saline-filled cuffs (tinted with methylene blue to signal cuff puncture immediately), FiO2 \u003c=30% (air/O2 or heliox, avoiding N2O), and high-efficiency ULPA plume evacuation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01695","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization and Disinfection","topic":"Hydrogen Peroxide Gas Plasma (STERRAD)","q":"When preparing delicate arthroscopic cameras and battery-powered light cables for low-temperature Hydrogen Peroxide Gas Plasma sterilization (e.g., STERRAD), why MUST synthetic polypropylene or Tyvek pouches be used instead of standard paper-linen wraps or cotton gauze?","o":["Polypropylene melts at 45 degrees C to vacuum-seal the camera","Cotton gauze turns H2O2 plasma into toxic ethylene oxide","Paper wraps ignite spontaneously into flames inside a plasma chamber","Cellulose (paper, cotton, linen, wood) absorbs hydrogen peroxide vapor, causing cycle cancellation and inadequate sterilant concentration inside the load"],"a":3,"e":"Hydrogen peroxide gas plasma operates dry at low temperatures (~45-55 C) in ~45-75 minutes with non-toxic water/oxygen byproducts (requiring zero aeration!). However, cellulose-based materials (linen towels, paper-plastic peel pouches, cardboard, wood) strongly absorb H2O2 vapor, dropping chamber concentration and aborting the cycle—so only non-woven polypropylene wraps and Tyvek/Mylar pouches are compatible, and loads must be bone-dry.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01696","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Patient Safety and Thermoregulation","topic":"Preventing Inadvertent Intraoperative Hypothermia","q":"During a 3-hour open abdominal resection in a 19-degree C OT, the patient\u0027s core esophageal temperature drops to 34.8 degrees C. Beyond shivering discomfort, which surgical complications are significantly increased by mild inadvertent intraoperative hypothermia (\u003c36.0 degrees C), and what is the most effective preventive method?","o":["Increased surgical site infection (vasoconstriction/impaired neutrophil killing), platelet dysfunction with greater blood loss, and delayed drug metabolism; prevent with active forced-air warming blankets and pre-warmed IV/irrigation fluids","Decreased bleeding and zero risk of wound infection; prevent by pouring ice-cold saline into the abdomen","Rapid accelerated clearance of vecuronium and morphine; no warming needed","Permanent hyperthermia and malignant hypertension; prevent by removing all blankets"],"a":0,"e":"Even mild perioperative hypothermia (\u003c36.0 C) impairs platelet aggregation and coagulation cascade enzymes (increasing transfusion need), causes subcutaneous vasoconstriction/tissue hypoxia (tripling surgical site infection rates), prolongs neuromuscular blocker/anesthetic elimination, and triggers postoperative shivering/cardiac ischemia. Active convective forced-air warming blankets plus warmed IV/irrigation fluids prevent core hypothermia far better than passive cotton sheets.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01697","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Instrumentation","topic":"Self-Retaining vs Handheld Retractors","q":"When preparing surgical instrument sets for an open laparotomy versus a lumbar laminectomy, which self-retaining retractors are standardly included for maintaining hands-free deep exposure respectively?","o":["Finochietto rib spreader for carpal tunnel release","Balfour (or Bookwalter) retractor for abdominal laparotomy; Gelpi or Weitlaner (or Taylor/Adson-Beckman) self-retaining retractor for lumbar spine laminectomy","Senn rake retractor for deep aortic clamping; Kocher toothed forceps as bladder retractor","Gelpi micro-retractor for huge adult abdominal retraction; Morris handheld retractor for hands-free exposure"],"a":1,"e":"Balfour (with lateral wire blades and central bladder blade) and table-mounted Bookwalter/Omni-Tract systems provide wide hands-free abdominal exposure during laparotomy. Deep narrow orthopedic/spine incisions (laminectomy, perineal/hip dissection) rely on ratcheted self-retaining Gelpi (sharp single-prong tips) or Weitlaner (blunt/sharp multi-prong hinged blades) retractors—always padding wound edges and periodically releasing tension to prevent nerve/muscle ischemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01698","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Operating Theatre Sanitation","topic":"Between-Case and Terminal OT Cleaning","q":"Immediately after a contaminated laparotomy finishes and the patient leaves the OR, which environmental cleaning protocol prevents aerosolizing floor pathogens and ensures effective surface disinfection before the next case?","o":["Skip cleaning between cases unless visible blood exceeds 500 mL","Dry-sweep the floor briskly with a straw broom to stir dust into the HVAC vents, then spray 100% alcohol mist","Never dry-sweep; wipe high horizontal surfaces/OR lights/boom/table pads first, then wet-mop the floor from clean outer perimeter inward toward the central operating table using fresh hospital-grade disinfectant (or 2-bucket/microfiber method), strictly observing required wet contact time","Mop the central bloody operating table zone first and spread that mop water outward toward the sterile supply cabinets"],"a":2,"e":"Dry brooms and dry dusting loft settled Staphylococcus and Gram-negative spores back into sterile air. Between-case and terminal OT disinfection proceeds top-to-bottom and cleanest-to-dirtiest (perimeter inward toward the OR table/spill zone, cleaning blood/body fluid spills first with 1% hypochlorite), using lint-free microfiber cloths/mops and allowing full manufacturer wet-contact kill time before setup.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01699","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Neurosurgery OT Procedures","topic":"Mayfield 3-Pin Skull Clamp Safety","q":"When assisting the neurosurgeon with positioning an adult patient in a 3-pin Mayfield cranial skull clamp for a posterior fossa craniotomy in the prone position, what pin-placement zone must be avoided, what standard adult clamping force is applied, and what critical ocular precaution is required?","o":["Use 60 lbs pin force on a 6-month-old infant\u0027s unfused fontanelle","Leave the Mayfield articulating joints unlocked during the craniotomy","Place pins directly into the thin temporal bone and tighten to 200 lbs; tape eyelids over hard foam blocks pressing directly onto the globes","Avoid the thin squamous temporal bone, frontal/mastoid sinuses, and superficial temporal artery (place pins along the sweatband frontal-parietal-occipital zone); tighten adult torque screw to ~60-80 lbs (270-360 N); ensure eyes are completely free of orbital compression to prevent postoperative visual loss"],"a":3,"e":"Mayfield pins must engage thick cortical bone along the superior temporal line/\u0027sweatband\u0027 region, strictly avoiding thin temporal squama (epidural hematoma risk), frontal/mastoid air sinuses (pneumocephalus/CSF leak), and neurovascular bundles. Adult torque is typically 60-80 lbs (much lower 20-40 lbs or padded horseshoe in young children). In prone neurosurgery, zero pressure on the globes (checked periodically) prevents central retinal artery occlusion and ischemic optic neuropathy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01700","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Operating Theatre Engineering","topic":"LED Surgical Light Specifications","q":"Why must modern operating theatre overhead surgical lights have a high Color Rendering Index (CRI \u003e= 90-95) and infrared-filtered (\u0027cold\u0027) light output, and why is an online UPS required alongside the hospital diesel generator?","o":["High CRI accurately reveals subtle tissue color changes (cyanosis, ischemia, arterial vs venous bleeding), cold-light prevents tissue desiccation/thermal drying, and an online UPS bridges the 5-15 second transfer gap before the diesel generator stabilizes","Low CRI (30) turns all tissues monochrome green for better contrast; hot halogen rays dry exposed bowel deliberately","UPS backup is unnecessary because surgeons can finish open heart surgery using smartphone flashlights","Surgical lights should emit intense ultraviolet UV-C radiation directly onto open wounds during laparotomy"],"a":0,"e":"Surgical luminaires require 40,000-160,000 lux illumination, shadow dilution via overlapping multi-faceted reflectors, CRI \u003e=90-95 (and R9 red rendering) so surgeons distinguish dusky ischemic bowel or cyanosis accurately, and cold LED/IR-filtering (\u003c2 C tissue rise) to prevent visceral drying. An online UPS guarantees zero-millisecond seamless illumination during grid failure while backup diesel generators crank up.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01701","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Perioperative Blood and Fluid Management","topic":"Safe Blood Warming Temperature Limit","q":"During massive intraoperative hemorrhage requiring rapid transfusion of refrigerated packed red blood cells (stored at 2-6 degrees C), why must ONLY an FDA/CE-approved medical blood warmer set \u003c= 41-42 degrees C be used—never a microwave oven or unmonitored hot water bath?","o":["Cold 4 degrees C blood should always be infused directly into the right atrium via central line at 100 mL/min without warming","Unregulated heating or temperatures exceeding 42-43 degrees C cause acute thermal hemolysis of red blood cells, releasing lethal free potassium and hemoglobin into the circulation","Medical blood warmers cool blood down to -10 degrees C before transfusion","Microwaving blood bags kills latent HIV and hepatitis viruses safely"],"a":1,"e":"Rapid infusion of ice-cold 4 C blood causes profound systemic hypothermia, coagulopathy, and ventricular fibrillation. Dedicated counter-current or dry-heat plate blood warmers equipped with audible over-temperature shutoff alarms maintain outflow \u003c=37-41 degrees C (strictly \u003c42 C); microwaving or hot tap-water immersion creates hot spots \u003e45 C that lyse RBCs and trigger fatal hyperkalemia/renal tubular injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01702","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Instrumentation and Equipment","topic":"Insulated Safety Holster and Capacitive Coupling","q":"When the surgeon sets down an active monopolar electrosurgical pencil between uses during a surgical procedure, where MUST the scrub technician ensure the pencil is placed, and why is laying it loosely on the sterile drapes strictly forbidden?","o":["Balanced loosely across the patient\u0027s bare chest skin","Tucked inside the surgeon\u0027s unsterile pocket","Inside a rigid, non-conductive insulated plastic safety holster secured on the sterile field; laying it on drapes risks accidental foot-pedal/button activation causing severe patient skin burns or surgical drape fires","Coiled tightly around the metallic Balfour retractor handle"],"a":2,"e":"Inadvertent leaning against the hand-switch or stepping on the ESU foot pedal while a monopolar pencil rests on paper/cloth drapes is a leading cause of operating room fires and occult third-degree patient burns. Placing the tip inside a visible, rigid non-conductive safety holster and verifying audible activation tones eliminates accidental thermal injury; coiling wires around metal clamps induces dangerous electromagnetic eddy currents.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01703","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Operation Theatre Procedures \u0026 Patient Safety","topic":"Surgical Fire Triad \u0026 Alcohol Prep Hazard","q":"A patient is undergoing excision of a facial lesion under monitored anaesthesia care with nasal cannula O2 at 6 L/min. The surgical site is prepped with 2% chlorhexidine in 70% isopropyl alcohol, and monopolar cautery is brought onto the sterile field. Which precaution prevents an operating room surgical fire?","o":["Increase nasal O2 flow to 15 L/min to blow away cautery sparks","Use dry gauze sponges directly over the active cautery tip to wipe away blood","Immediately cover wet alcohol prep with adhesive plastic drapes to trap vapors safely underneath","Allow alcohol-based prep to dry completely (\u003e= 3 minutes) without pooling before applying drapes, minimize/discontinue open O2 (FiO2 \u003c 30%) or switch to air when activating cautery near the head/neck, and keep sterile saline readily available"],"a":3,"e":"The surgical fire triad consists of Oxidizer (O2/N2O), Ignition source (ESU/laser/light cord), and Fuel (alcohol prep fumes, drapes, dry gauze). Draping over wet alcohol traps explosive invisible vapors, and open O2 enriches the field under head/neck drapes. Full drying (\u003e=3 min), stopping/lowering O2 before cautery activation, and wet sponges/saline prevent ignition.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01704","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization, CSSD \u0026 Infection Control","topic":"Bowie-Dick Test Protocol","q":"Every morning before loading surgical instrument trays into a prevacuum (dynamic-air-removal) steam sterilizer in CSSD, the technician runs a Bowie-Dick test pack in an otherwise empty chamber (134°C for 3.5 minutes). What specific malfunction does a failed (non-uniform color change) Bowie-Dick sheet detect?","o":["Inadequate vacuum air removal or an air leak in the chamber allowing residual air pockets that block steam penetration","Biological spore resistance in dry-heat ovens","Incorrect pH of glutaraldehyde soaking trays","Insufficient ethylene oxide aeration time"],"a":0,"e":"The Bowie-Dick test is NOT a sterilization test for microbes; it is a daily functional test specifically for prevacuum steam sterilizers to verify vacuum pump efficiency and absence of air leaks. Residual trapped air inside the center of the porous test pack prevents saturated steam contact, leaving an incomplete/pale central color change on the indicator sheet (requiring machine lockout/repair).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01705","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Asepsis \u0026 Surgical Assisting","topic":"Lithotomy \u0026 Supine Arm Positioning Safety","q":"While positioning an anaesthetized patient into dorsal lithotomy for gynecological surgery and abducting the arms onto armboards, which two positioning rules prevent common peroneal nerve palsy and brachial plexus stretch injury?","o":["Pad the lateral fibular heads against stirrup pressure and raise/lower both legs simultaneously; keep arms abducted \u003c 90 degrees with palms supinated/neutral and elbows padded","Rest the lateral knees firmly against unpadded metal stirrup posts and raise one leg fully before lifting the other; abduct arms to 135 degrees overhead","Hyperextend the hips past 120 degrees and pronate the forearms tightly against metal table rails","Keep legs strapped tightly to unpadded stirrups for \u003e 4 hours without repositioning"],"a":0,"e":"In lithotomy, pressure from stirrup bars against the lateral aspect of the proximal fibula compresses the common peroneal nerve (causing foot drop), while lifting/lowering legs asymmetrically strains the sacroiliac joint and hip. On armboards, limiting abduction to \u003c90° with forearm supination/neutral position and ulnar groove padding prevents brachial plexus stretch and ulnar neuropathy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01706","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization, CSSD \u0026 Infection Control","topic":"Ethylene Oxide (ETO) vs Hydrogen Peroxide Gas Plasma","q":"When sterilizing heat- and moisture-sensitive laparoscopic cameras and fiberoptic cables in CSSD, what critical safety distinction separates Ethylene Oxide (ETO) sterilization from Hydrogen Peroxide (H2O2) gas plasma sterilization?","o":["ETO requires mandatory mechanical aeration (8–12 hours) to remove toxic residual gas before patient use; H2O2 gas plasma requires no aeration (byproducts are water and oxygen) but CANNOT process cellulose/linen drapes or long narrow lumens outside validated limits","H2O2 gas plasma requires 24 hours of heated aeration because it leaves carcinogenic residue, whereas ETO items can be used immediately out of the chamber","Both ETO and H2O2 plasma require cotton linen wraps and liquid glutaraldehyde immersion","ETO operates at 134°C steam pressure while H2O2 plasma boils instruments in water"],"a":0,"e":"ETO is an alkylating gas effective for complex lumens at 37–55°C, but absorbed ETO causes severe mucosal/tissue chemical burns and toxicity—necessitating dedicated mechanical aeration for 8–12 hours. H2O2 gas plasma (STERRAD) is rapid (~28–75 min) with non-toxic water/oxygen byproducts, but cellulose (paper, cotton, linen) absorbs H2O2 and aborts the cycle, so only polypropylene Tyvek/non-woven wraps are permitted.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01707","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Asepsis \u0026 Surgical Assisting","topic":"Frozen Section vs Routine Histopathology Specimens","q":"During breast conservation surgery, the surgeon excises a sentinel lymph node and requests an urgent intraoperative FROZEN SECTION while also removing a routine benign lipoma for standard histopathology. How must the OT technician prepare each specimen container?","o":["Send the frozen-section lymph node FRESH and UNFIXED immediately (dry or on saline-moistened gauze—NEVER in formalin); immerse the routine lipoma in 10% neutral buffered formalin (~10:1 fixative-to-tissue volume); verify patient ID, exact site/laterality, and time on both labels","Place both specimens immediately into 10% formalin before sending to pathology","Freeze the sentinel node directly in liquid nitrogen inside the OT wash basin and send the lipoma in 70% alcohol","Wait until the end of the entire surgical list (4 hours later) to send both unfixed dry specimens together"],"a":0,"e":"Formalin fixes proteins and prevents cryostat freezing/immunohistochemistry or microbiology cultures—putting a frozen-section or culture specimen into formalin ruins urgent intraoperative diagnosis. Routine histology specimens, conversely, degrade via autolysis if left unfixed, so they require prompt immersion in 10% neutral buffered formalin (10× tissue volume) with verified two-identifier and laterality labeling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01708","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Equipment \u0026 Instrumentation","topic":"CO2 Insufflator Safety \u0026 Pneumoperitoneum","q":"While establishing pneumoperitoneum via a Veress needle for laparoscopic cholecystectomy, what initial insufflator flow and target intra-abdominal pressure settings are standard, and what sudden monitor change signals suspected CO2 gas embolism?","o":["Start immediately at 40 L/min flow and 30 mmHg pressure; sudden rise in SpO2 to 100% signals CO2 embolism","Start at low flow (1–2 L/min) verifying low opening pressure (\u003c 8 mmHg), then maintain working pressure at 12–15 mmHg; sudden abrupt drop in EtCO2, hypotension, tachycardia, and \"mill-wheel\" murmur signal CO2 embolism","Use pure Nitrogen gas at 25 mmHg; isolated hypertension signals embolism","Leave the insufflator cylinder valve closed and infuse room air via manual bulb"],"a":1,"e":"Starting CO2 insufflation at low flow (1–2 L/min) avoids massive intravascular gas injection if the Veress needle accidentally enters a vessel, and low initial intra-abdominal pressure (\u003c8 mmHg) confirms free peritoneal space before raising to 12–15 mmHg. CO2 embolism produces an air-lock in the right heart/pulmonary artery, causing abrupt EtCO2 collapse (no pulmonary perfusion), shock, and hypoxemia—requiring immediate deflation, left lateral head-down (Durant) position, and 100% O2.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01709","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Equipment \u0026 Instrumentation","topic":"Atraumatic Clamps \u0026 Self-Retaining Retractors","q":"During an exploratory laparotomy, the surgeon asks for an ATRAUMATIC fenestrated grasping forceps to hold the delicate appendix/fallopian tube without crushing tissue, and a SELF-RETAINING abdominal wall retractor. Which pair of instruments should the scrub technician pass?","o":["Kocher heavy toothed clamp and Langenbeck handheld retractor","Towel clip (Backhaus) and periosteal elevator","Babcock tissue forceps and Balfour abdominal retractor","Allis sharp-toothed clamp and Deaver handheld retractor"],"a":2,"e":"Babcock forceps feature smooth, rounded, fenestrated atraumatic jaws specifically designed to encircle and hold delicate tubular structures (appendix, bowel, ureter, fallopian tube) without crushing. The Balfour retractor is a self-retaining frame retractor with lateral blades and a central bladder blade used to maintain abdominal exposure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01710","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Operation Theatre Procedures \u0026 Patient Safety","topic":"Estimated Blood Loss (EBL) Gravimetric Calculation","q":"At the end of a myomectomy, the surgical suction canister contains 1,350 mL of fluid, and the scrub technician documented using 500 mL of normal saline irrigation into the pelvis. Additionally, 3 abdominal lap sponges are fully saturated (~100 mL each) and 6 standard 4×4 gauze sponges are soaked (~10 mL each). What is the net Estimated Blood Loss (EBL)?","o":["1,710 mL","850 mL","2,210 mL","1,210 mL"],"a":3,"e":"Net suctioned blood = Total suction volume (1,350 mL) - Irrigation used (500 mL) = 850 mL. Blood in sponges = (3 × 100 mL) + (6 × 10 mL) = 300 + 60 = 360 mL. Total EBL = 850 + 360 = 1,210 mL. Accurate tracking alerts the team promptly for blood transfusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01711","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization, CSSD \u0026 Infection Control","topic":"Critical vs Semi-Critical Instrument Reprocessing","q":"According to the Spaulding classification for medical device reprocessing, how must (1) a rigid laparoscope entering the sterile peritoneal cavity be processed versus (2) a flexible gastroscope contacting intact gastrointestinal mucosa?","o":["Laparoscope (Critical item) requires STERILIZATION (or validated HLD only if sterilization is incompatible, though steam/plasma sterilization is preferred); Gastroscope (Semi-critical item) requires at minimum High-Level Disinfection (e.g., 2% glutaraldehyde for 20 min or OPA) after leak testing and manual enzymatic brushing","Both scopes are Non-critical items requiring only wiping with 70% alcohol swabs for 10 seconds","Gastroscope must be autoclaved at 134°C while the laparoscope only needs soap and tap water","Neither scope requires manual pre-cleaning before chemical soaking"],"a":0,"e":"Spaulding classification defines items entering sterile tissue/vascular system (laparoscopes, trocars, arthroscopes) as Critical (requiring sterilization via autoclavable optics, H2O2 plasma, or ETO), and items contacting intact mucous membranes (flexible GI endoscopes, laryngoscope blades) as Semi-critical (requiring meticulous manual cleaning + High-Level Disinfection).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01712","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Instrumentation","topic":"Babcock vs Allis vs Kocher Forceps","q":"During an appendectomy or salpingectomy, which ratcheted grasping forceps has smooth, rounded, fenestrated non-toothed jaws designed to cradle delicate tubular organs (appendix, ureter, fallopian tube, intestine) WITHOUT crushing them?","o":["Babcock forceps","Crile serrated hemostatic artery forceps","Allis sharp-toothed grasping forceps","Kocher heavy-toothed crushing clamp"],"a":0,"e":"Babcock forceps feature flared, smooth, rounded fenestrated blades that encircle and hold delicate tubular structures (appendix, fallopian tubes, bowel wall, ureter) gently with minimal mucosal/serosal trauma. By contrast, Allis forceps have sharp interlocking teeth (for fascia/tissue being excised), and Kocher clamps have heavy 1x2 interlocking teeth and transverse serrations for tough fascia, bone fragments, or tissue destined for removal.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01713","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Thoracic and Postoperative OT Care","topic":"Underwater Seal Drainage Physics","q":"When setting up a single- or three-chamber underwater seal intercostal chest drain (ICD) at the end of a thoracotomy, how deep below the sterile water surface should the drainage stem tip sit, where must the drainage bottle be positioned relative to the patient\u0027s chest, and what happens if an assistant clamps the chest tube during transport in a patient with an active air leak?","o":["Submerge tip ~2 cm below water level, always keep bottle BELOW chest level (never lifted above chest!), and DO NOT clamp a bubbling tube during transport because trapping an ongoing air leak precipitates lethal tension pneumothorax","Submerge tip 25 cm deep; lift bottle onto the patient\u0027s pillow; clamp every chest tube tightly during elevator transfer","Leave the tube open to room air without water seal; place bottle higher than the head","Connect the chest tube directly to wall oxygen at 15 L/min"],"a":0,"e":"A 2 cm sterile water seal acts as a low-resistance one-way valve: air/blood escapes during expiration/coughing (\u003e +2 cm H2O) while negative inspiratory intrapleural pressure (-5 to -8 cm H2O) cannot suck room air backward. Keeping the system upright and strictly below chest level prevents retrograde fluid siphoning into the pleural space, and avoiding routine clamping prevents rapid tension pneumothorax when visceral pleural air leaks exist.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01714","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Endoscopic and Laparoscopic Equipment","topic":"Laparoscope Fogging and Fiberoptic Fire Hazard","q":"At the end of a laparoscopic cholecystectomy, the surgeon disconnects the fiberoptic light cable from the laparoscope while the high-intensity xenon/LED light source is still powered ON at 100% brightness and sets the glowing cable end onto the paper surgical drape. Within seconds, smoke and charring appear. What mandatory safety habit prevents this OT hazard?","o":["Keep the light source permanently locked at 100% intensity between cases","Switch the light source to STANDBY (or power off) BEFORE disconnecting the fiberoptic cable from the scope—never lay an energized glowing light cable tip on drapes or patient skin","Immerse the hot fiberoptic glass bundle immediately into cold acetone","Wrap the glowing tip inside dry cotton gauze"],"a":1,"e":"High-intensity xenon (300 W) and high-output LED cold-light sources concentrate intense radiant energy at the distal fiberoptic cable tip—reaching temperatures \u003e200-300 degrees C at close contact despite \u0027cold-light\u0027 infrared filtering—capable of igniting surgical drapes or causing full-thickness patient skin burns within 3-10 seconds. Placing the console on Standby before detaching the cable eliminates this fire hazard completely.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01715","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Orthopaedic OT Procedures","topic":"C-Arm Sterile Draping and Radiation Discipline","q":"During an orthopedic femur intramedullary nailing requiring frequent switches between AP and lateral C-arm fluoroscopy, how must the C-arm be draped and rotated to preserve surgical site asepsis?","o":["Turn off all room lights permanently so the C-arm moves in the dark","Drape only the floor wheels and let the unsterile image intensifier brush against the surgeon\u0027s gown","Cover the C-arm C-bow, image intensifier, and X-ray tube with dedicated sterile transparent plastic C-arm drapes secured with sterile bands, and keep the sterile technician guarding the lower/lateral clearance zone whenever rotating the C-arm underneath or over the table","Leave the C-arm completely undraped and wipe it with dry gauze every hour"],"a":2,"e":"C-arm fluoroscopy arcs close to the open orthopedic incision and sterile instrument tables. Sterile transparent barrier bags over the receiver, X-ray tube, and C-arch (plus careful awareness that anything below table level remains unsterile even if draped) prevent catastrophic prosthetic/implant infections when swinging between AP and horizontal lateral views.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01716","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Sterilization and Disinfection","topic":"Immediate-Use Steam Sterilization (IUSS)","q":"Under NABH and AORN standards, when is Immediate-Use Steam Sterilization (IUSS, formerly called \u0027flash\u0027 sterilization) permissible in an operating theatre, and which surgical items should NEVER undergo routine IUSS?","o":["Recommended for sterilizing disposable plastic syringes for reuse","Permissible without prior enzymatic cleaning or brushing if steam temperature is 134 degrees C","Permissible as the standard daily shortcut for 100% of orthopedic knee implants to save inventory cost","Permissible ONLY in urgent unplanned emergencies (e.g., dropping an irreplaceable instrument mid-case with no sterile backup) after full decontamination; NEVER use for routine convenience/instrument shortages and avoid for implantable hardware"],"a":3,"e":"IUSS (unwrapped/rigid-container rapid 132-134 C cycle without full drying or long-term barrier packaging) carries high risk of contamination during hot wet transfer and zero shelf-life. It is restricted strictly to unexpected emergency recovery of a unique dropped instrument—never to compensate for inadequate instrument sets, and never for implants (unless life-threatening emergency with concurrent biological indicator spore monitoring and rapid-readout quarantine).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01717","course":"Diploma in Operation Theatre Technology","subject":"Surgical Supplies and Sutures","topic":"Monofilament vs Braided Multifilament Sutures","q":"When closing a contaminated laparotomy wound or performing vascular anastomosis, why does a smooth synthetic MONOFILAMENT suture (e.g., polydioxanone [PDS] or polypropylene) carry lower surgical site infection risk than a braided MULTIFILAMENT suture (e.g., silk)?","o":["Braided multifilament sutures harbor bacteria inside microscopic interstices between braided fibers (\u0027wicking\u0027 capillarity) shielded from host leukocytes, whereas smooth single-strand monofilaments resist bacterial harbor","Silk monofilament dissolves completely within 2 hours of skin closure","Monofilament polypropylene requires zero knots and melts spontaneously to seal fascia","Braided sutures automatically release broad-spectrum vancomycin"],"a":0,"e":"Braided multifilament sutures (silk, braided polyester, polyglactin) provide superior handling and knot security, but bacteria penetrate microscopic crevices between braided strands where macrophages cannot reach—inducing chronic stitch sinuses/abscesses in contaminated wounds. Smooth single-strand monofilaments (PDS, poliglecaprone, nylon, polypropylene) eliminate wicking capillarity (though requiring extra square-knot throws due to memory/slipperiness).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01718","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Optics and Refraction","topic":"Streak Retinoscopy Calculation","q":"An ophthalmic technician performs streak retinoscopy at a working distance of 67 cm (0.67 m) without a working-distance lens in place. Neutralization is achieved with +2.50 DS in the vertical meridian and +3.50 DS in the horizontal meridian. What is the patient\u0027s net spherocylindrical prescription in minus-cylinder form?","o":["+3.50 DS / -1.00 DC x 180","+2.00 DS / -1.00 DC x 180","+4.00 DS / -1.00 DC x 90","+2.00 DS / -1.00 DC x 90"],"a":1,"e":"A working distance of 67 cm requires subtracting +1.50 D (1 / 0.67 m = 1.50 D). Subtracting +1.50 D from the gross neutralizing lenses gives net powers of +1.00 D in the vertical meridian (90 degrees) and +2.00 D in the horizontal meridian (180 degrees). In minus-cylinder form, the sphere equals the more plus power (+2.00 DS), the cylinder is -1.00 DC, and the axis lies along the more plus meridian (axis 180, so the -1.00 D cylinder power acts 90 degrees away in the vertical meridian to yield +1.00 D).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01719","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Instruments and Diagnostics","topic":"Goldmann Applanation Tonometry","q":"When performing Goldmann applanation tonometry, the technician notices that the two green semicircles (mires) appear excessively thick and wide. How does excessively thick fluorescein tear film affect the IOP reading?","o":["It causes an artificially low (underestimated) IOP reading","It only changes the astigmatic axis without altering IOP","It causes an artificially high (overestimated) IOP reading","It has no effect because Goldmann tonometry is independent of tear meniscus width"],"a":2,"e":"Goldmann applanation tonometry relies on the Imbert-Fick principle balanced by corneal rigidity and tear film surface tension. Excess fluorescein creates wide, thick mires that increase meniscus adhesion/surface tension and cause the technician to apply more force to align inner edges, yielding a falsely elevated IOP reading.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01720","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Cataract Pre-op Diagnostics","topic":"A-Scan Ultrasonography and IOL Power","q":"During contact applanation A-scan biometry for cataract surgery, an inexperienced technician presses the ultrasound probe firmly against the cornea, indenting the anterior chamber by 0.5 mm. If this erroneous axial length is used in the SRK formula (P = A - 2.5L - 0.9K), what refractive surprise will result postoperatively?","o":["Purely against-the-rule corneal astigmatism with spherical equivalence unchanged","Zero refractive error because keratometry compensates for axial length error","Postoperative hyperopia of approximately +1.50 D due to underestimation of IOL power","Overestimation of IOL power leading to postoperative myopia (approximately -1.25 D to -1.50 D myopic shift)"],"a":3,"e":"Corneal indentation artificially shortens the measured axial length (L) by 0.5 mm. In the SRK formula (P = A - 2.5L - 0.9K), a shorter L increases the calculated IOL power (by ~2.5 x 0.5 = +1.25 D). Implanting a stronger-than-needed IOL focuses light in front of the true retina, producing postoperative myopia (myopic surprise).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01721","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ocular Anatomy and Clinical Assessment","topic":"Ishihara Pseudoisochromatic Plates","q":"When screening a railway job applicant for congenital red-green color vision deficiency using Ishihara 38-plate pseudoisochromatic charts, under what illumination and viewing conditions should the test be conducted?","o":["Under natural daylight (or daylight-equivalent illumination) at 75 cm distance, held perpendicular to the line of sight, allowing 3 seconds per plate","In a completely dark refraction room using a monochromatic red penlight at 25 cm","Under dim yellow tungsten bulb light at 3 meters with pinhole occluder","Using a green filter over the right eye only at 6 meters"],"a":0,"e":"Ishihara plates are calibrated for natural daylight (or standard daylight-spectrum illuminants around 6500 K) held at 75 cm perpendicular to the visual axis. Tungsten/incandescent light shifts the spectral reflectance and allows red-green anomalous individuals to pass falsely.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01722","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Pharmacology and Emergencies","topic":"Alkali vs Acid Ocular Injury","q":"A construction worker splashes wet lime (calcium hydroxide) into his right eye and arrives at the eye clinic in severe blepharospasm. What is the single most critical first step before checking visual acuity or slit-lamp examination?","o":["Neutralize the alkali directly with dilute vinegar (acetic acid) drops","Copious immediate irrigation of the eye and conjunctival fornices with Ringer\u0027s lactate or normal saline (after topical anesthetic and double eversion to remove lime particles) until pH normalizes to 7.0-7.4","Instill topical atropine and patch the eye firmly before referring to the ophthalmologist","Measure Snellen visual acuity and Goldmann IOP first to document baseline medico-legal status"],"a":1,"e":"Alkali burns (such as lime/cement) cause rapid liquefactive necrosis and penetrate deeply into the cornea and anterior chamber. Immediate, prolonged irrigation (at least 30 minutes / 1-2 L isotonic fluid) and mechanical sweeping of retained particulate lime from the upper/lower fornices is the #1 determinant of ocular survival; visual acuity assessment must wait until irrigation begins or completes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01723","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Spectacle Optics and Dispensing","topic":"Optical Center and Prentice\u0027s Rule","q":"Using Prentice\u0027s rule, if a patient\u0027s right spectacle lens has a power of +5.00 D sphere and its optical center is mistakenly decentered 4 mm (0.4 cm) temporally relative to the patient\u0027s pupil, how much prismatic effect is induced and in which base direction?","o":["0.2 prism diopters Base Out","1.25 prism diopters Base In","2.0 prism diopters Base Out","2.0 prism diopters Base In"],"a":2,"e":"Prentice\u0027s rule states: Induced Prism (Delta) = Lens Power (D) x Decentration (cm). Here, 5.00 D x 0.4 cm = 2.0 prism diopters. Because a plus (convex) lens is thickest at its optical center, temporal decentration of the optical center places the thicker lens base temporally—inducing Base Out prism.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01724","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Instruments and Diagnostics","topic":"Illumination Techniques","q":"Which slit-lamp biomicroscopy illumination technique directs a wide beam onto the temporal limbus so light undergoes total internal reflection across the cornea, highlighting subtle central corneal edema, nebular opacities, or foreign bodies against a dark pupil background?","o":["Direct focal optic section (thin parallelepiped)","Specular reflection","Indirect retroillumination from the fundus (red reflex)","Sclerotic scatter"],"a":3,"e":"In sclerotic scatter, the illumination arm is decoupled and a bright slit beam is focused sharply at the limbus. Light travels internally across the corneal stroma by total internal reflection, illuminating the entire limbal ring like a halo while scattering brightly at any central corneal opacity or stromal edema.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01725","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Glaucoma Diagnostics","topic":"Humphrey Visual Field Reliability Indices","q":"On a Humphrey 24-2 SITA-Standard visual field printout, the technician notes 35% False Positive errors, an abnormally bright \u0027cloverleaf\u0027 or white-out sensitivity plot (\u0027trigger-happy\u0027 pattern), and Mean Deviation (MD) of +4.5 dB. What does this indicate?","o":["An unreliable test due to an anxious patient pressing the response button when no stimulus was presented; requires re-instruction and repeat testing","Severe cataract causing diffuse depression across all quadrants","True glaucomatous optic neuropathy with superior arcuate scotoma","Markedly elevated false-negative rate due to patient fatigue"],"a":0,"e":"High False Positive rates (\u003e15% in SITA strategies) occur when a patient presses the clicker during silent catch trials without a visual stimulus (\u0027trigger-happy\u0027). This produces abnormally high threshold sensitivities (white scotoma plot) and falsely positive Mean Deviation values, invalidating glaucoma progression analysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01726","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Optics and Refraction","topic":"Near Addition Calculation","q":"An emmetropic 50-year-old tailor whose habitual near working distance is 40 cm (requiring 2.50 D of accommodation) has a measured binocular amplitude of accommodation of 2.00 D. Leaving half of his amplitude in reserve for comfortable sustained near work, what near reading addition (+ add) should be prescribed?","o":["+0.50 DS","+1.50 DS","+2.50 DS","+3.50 DS"],"a":1,"e":"At a 40 cm working distance, total dioptric demand = 1 / 0.40 m = +2.50 D. Keeping half of the patient\u0027s 2.00 D amplitude in reserve leaves 1.00 D of usable comfortable accommodation. Required near addition = Total Demand - Usable Accommodation = 2.50 D - 1.00 D = +1.50 DS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01727","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ocular Anatomy and Clinical Assessment","topic":"Pinhole Visual Acuity Test","q":"A patient\u0027s uncorrected distance visual acuity on the Snellen chart is 6/36 in the right eye, and improves to 6/9 when viewing through a 1.2 mm pinhole occluder. What does this pinhole improvement confirm?","o":["The patient has a dense mature cataract blocking all axial light rays","The patient is malingering and has normal emmetropic optics","The visual reduction is primarily due to an uncorrected refractive error (or mild regular corneal irregularity) rather than retinal or optic nerve pathology","The patient has advanced optic atrophy or central macular degeneration"],"a":2,"e":"A pinhole (1-1.2 mm aperture) blocks peripheral unfocused light rays and admits only paraxial rays, dramatically increasing depth of focus and minimizing the blur circle on the retina. Significant improvement (\u003e2 Snellen lines) indicates refractive error, whereas failure to improve or worsening with a pinhole points toward media opacity, macular disease, or optic neuropathy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01728","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Optics and Keratometry","topic":"Keratometry Interpretation","q":"A keratometer measures a patient\u0027s right cornea as K1 = 42.00 D at 180 degrees and K2 = 44.50 D at 90 degrees. How much corneal astigmatism is present and how is it classified?","o":["2.50 D of against-the-rule (ATR) corneal astigmatism","0.50 D of spherical myopia","43.25 D of oblique astigmatism","2.50 D of with-the-rule (WTR) corneal astigmatism"],"a":3,"e":"Corneal astigmatism equals the dioptric difference between the two principal meridians: 44.50 D - 42.00 D = 2.50 D. Because the vertical meridian (90 degrees, +/- 30 deg) is steeper (44.50 D) and the horizontal meridian (180 degrees) is flatter (42.00 D), it is classified as with-the-rule (WTR) astigmatism—typical of younger eyes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01729","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Contact Lens Practice","topic":"Fluorescein Fit Assessment","q":"During an RGP contact lens trial fitting with fluorescein and cobalt-blue filter illumination, the ophthalmic technician observes bright central green pooling (excessive apical clearance) surrounded by a dark 360-degree mid-peripheral bearing ring and tight edge seal. What does this fluorescein pattern indicate?","o":["A steep fit (base curve radius is too short/steep); flatten the base curve to improve tear exchange","A flat fit (base curve radius is too long/flat); steepen the base curve","An ideal aligned alignment fit requiring no adjustment","Inside-out soft hydrogel lens inversion"],"a":0,"e":"In RGP fitting, fluorescein pools brightly wherever there is a thick tear gap between lens and cornea, and appears dark/black where the lens touches the cornea. Bright central apical pooling with a dark peripheral touch ring and minimal edge lift signifies a steep base curve (vaulting centrally and sealing peripherally), which traps debris and causes corneal hypoxia unless flattened.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01730","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Instruments and Diagnostics","topic":"OCT Image Quality Optimization","q":"While acquiring a Spectral-Domain OCT retinal nerve fiber layer (RNFL) scan on a 68-year-old glaucoma patient, the technician notices a low signal strength of 3/10 and horizontal dropout bands across the B-scan despite clear media on slit-lamp exam. What simple pre-scan adjustments most effectively restore signal strength?","o":["Turn off room lights completely and press the scanner lens directly onto the cornea","Instill preservative-free artificial tear drops, ask the patient to blink fully right before capture, re-align the chinrest/working distance to center the pupil, and re-acquire","Dilate the pupil with topical pilocarpine 2%","Switch the scan protocol to Goldmann applanation mode"],"a":1,"e":"Tear film breakup from dry eye or staring during OCT alignment scatters the near-infrared superluminescent diode beam and drops signal strength sharply, while blinking creates horizontal black B-scan dropouts. Lubricating drops, deliberate pre-capture blinking, and centered pupil/working-distance alignment restore crisp signal strength (\u003e=6-7/10).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01731","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ocular Infection Control","topic":"Tonometer Prism Disinfection","q":"After measuring IOP in a patient with suspected viral conjunctivitis (epidemic keratoconjunctivitis), how must the Goldmann applanation tonometer biprism tip be disinfected before examining the next patient to prevent both adenovirus transmission and chemical corneal burns?","o":["Wipe once with a dry tissue paper and reuse immediately","Leave the prism soaking in 70% alcohol and place it wet directly onto the next patient\u0027s cornea","Remove and clean the prism tip, soak in 1:10 dilute sodium hypochlorite (~5,000 ppm) or 3% hydrogen peroxide for 5-10 minutes, rinse thoroughly with sterile water/saline, and dry completely before reuse","Boil the acrylic biprism tip in a 134 degrees C autoclave for 30 minutes"],"a":2,"e":"Non-enveloped Adenovirus (causing epidemic keratoconjunctivitis outbreaks in eye clinics) is resistant to brief alcohol wipes; soaking the removable tonometer tip in 1:10 diluted bleach (5,000 ppm NaOCl) or 3% H2O2 for 5-10 minutes inactivates adenovirus and HIV/HBV. Thorough sterile water rinsing and complete drying are mandatory so residual bleach or alcohol does not cause severe toxic corneal epithelial sloughing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01732","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Optics and Refraction","topic":"Spherical Equivalent Calculation","q":"An ophthalmic technician needs to fit a patient with a spherical soft contact lens based on a spectacle prescription of -4.00 DS / -1.50 DC x 180. What is the Spherical Equivalent (SE) of this prescription?","o":["-3.25 DS","-2.50 DS","-5.50 DS","-4.75 DS"],"a":3,"e":"Spherical Equivalent (SE) places the circle of least confusion on the retina and is calculated as: SE = Sphere + (Cylinder / 2). Here, SE = -4.00 + (-1.50 / 2) = -4.00 + (-0.75) = -4.75 DS (note: vertex distance adjustment is also considered for powers \u003e= +/-4.00 D when dispensing contact lenses).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01733","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Optics and Refraction","topic":"Jackson Cross Cylinder (JCC)","q":"When performing subjective refraction with a +/-0.25 D Jackson Cross Cylinder (JCC) using minus trial cylinders, in what sequence should cylinder axis and power be refined, and how is the JCC handle oriented when refining the AXIS?","o":["Refine AXIS first with the JCC handle aligned directly parallel to the trial cylinder axis (so the JCC principal meridians straddle 45 degrees on either side), rotating the minus cylinder axis toward the red/minus dot of the clearer flip","Skip axis refinement whenever cylinder power is \u003c= -2.00 DC","Refine AXIS with the JCC red/white dots aligned parallel to the trial cylinder axis","Refine POWER first with the JCC handle at 90 degrees to the axis, then ignore axis alignment"],"a":0,"e":"In JCC subjective refraction, cylinder AXIS is always refined BEFORE power (because an off-axis cylinder alters effective power). For axis refinement, the JCC handle is aligned along the trial cylinder\u0027s axis, placing the red (minus) and white (plus) cylinder power axes at +/-45 degrees; flipping compares two oblique combinations and the trial minus cylinder axis is chased toward the red dot of the preferred choice until both flips look equally blurred. Power is then refined with the dots aligned along the axis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01734","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Orthoptics and Binocular Vision","topic":"Cover-Uncover vs Alternate Cover Test","q":"During an ocular motility examination at 6 meters, the patient fixates on a 6/18 Snellen letter. When the technician covers the right eye, the uncovered left eye makes an immediate outward (temporal) refixation movement to take up fixation. When the occluder is removed, no movement is needed if the left eye holds fixation. What does this Cover-Uncover finding demonstrate?","o":["Bilateral sixth nerve palsy with zero refixation","Manifest left esotropia (a heterotropia where the left eye was turned inward prior to covering the right eye)","Manifest left exotropia (eye turned outward)","Purely latent orthophoria with zero strabismus"],"a":1,"e":"In the Cover-Uncover test, the examiner watches the UNCOVERED eye as the fellow eye is occluded. If the uncovered left eye moves temporally (outward) to pick up fixation, it must have been resting nasally (turned inward = esotropia) under binocular viewing conditions. By contrast, an outward movement of an eye ONLY when uncovered in the Alternate Cover test (with no movement of the fellow eye during Cover-Uncover) indicates latent esophoria.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01735","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Diagnostics","topic":"Schirmer I Test Protocol","q":"When performing a standard Schirmer I test (without topical anesthetic) using a 5 x 35 mm Whatman No. 41 filter paper strip hooked over the lateral third of the lower eyelid margin for 5 minutes, what wetting length indicates normal versus severe aqueous-deficient dry eye?","o":["Normal: \u003e=10-15 mm wetting in 5 minutes; Severe aqueous deficiency: \u003c=5 mm wetting in 5 minutes","Normal: 0-2 mm wetting; Severe deficiency: \u003e30 mm wetting","Test duration is 30 seconds with strip placed directly onto the central cornea","Strip is soaked in fluorescein dye and held above the eyebrow"],"a":0,"e":"In the Schirmer I test (measuring basal + reflex tear secretion without anesthetic), the bent 5 mm tip of a Whatman #41 strip is placed at the junction of the middle and lateral third of the lower fornix (avoiding corneal touch) with eyes gently closed or blinking normally for 5 minutes. Wetting \u003e=10-15 mm is normal, 6-10 mm borderline, and \u003c=5 mm confirms severe aqueous-deficient dry eye (e.g., Sjogren syndrome).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01736","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Retinal Imaging and Diagnostics","topic":"Patient Preparation and Adverse Reaction Safety","q":"Before injecting 10% or 20% intravenous sodium fluorescein for Fundus Fluorescein Angiography (FFA) in a diabetic retinopathy patient, what routine benign side effects should the patient be counseled about, and what essential safety equipment must be immediately present in the FFA room?","o":["Inject the dye directly into the vitreous cavity instead of a peripheral vein","Counsel that urine will turn black permanently; no emergency drugs are ever required because fluorescein is a vitamin","Counsel that skin and urine will temporarily turn bright yellow-orange for 24-36 hours (plus transient nausea); keep an emergency resuscitation crash cart with epinephrine, oxygen, and IV access ready for rare anaphylaxis","Instruct the patient that vision will remain completely blind for 7 days"],"a":2,"e":"Sodium fluorescein rapidly stains skin/mucosa yellowish and is renally excreted, turning urine bright neon yellow-orange for 24-36 hours; transient nausea occurs in ~3-5% of patients during rapid bolus injection. Because severe anaphylactic reactions/bronchospasm (~1 in 1,900-20,000) can occur, written consent, allergy/asthma screening, patent IV access, and an emergency crash cart (epinephrine, antihistamines, steroids, O2) are mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01737","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Optics and Refraction","topic":"Duochrome (Bichrome) Red-Green Test","q":"At the end of monocular subjective refraction in a 26-year-old myope wearing -3.00 DS in the trial frame, the patient reports that the black optotypes on the GREEN background look significantly sharper and darker than those on the RED background during the Duochrome test. What spherical lens adjustment is indicated?","o":["Prescribe -3.00 DS immediately because green clearer proves ideal emmetropia","Rotate the cylinder axis by 90 degrees","Add -0.50 DS (increase minus power to -3.50 DS)","Add +0.25 DS (or reduce minus power to -2.75 DS) until red and green letters appear equally sharp/clear (avoiding over-minusing)"],"a":3,"e":"Due to ocular longitudinal chromatic aberration, shorter-wavelength green light refracts more strongly and focuses slightly ahead of longer-wavelength red light. If letters on GREEN appear clearer, the red focus is behind the retina—meaning the eye is over-minused (or under-plussed), so adding +0.25 D shifts both foci forward until balanced equally (\u0027RAM-GAP\u0027: Red Add Minus, Green Add Plus).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01738","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Glaucoma Diagnostics","topic":"Total Internal Reflection at Cornea-Air Interface","q":"Why is the anterior chamber iridocorneal drainage angle (trabecular meshwork) invisible on direct slit-lamp viewing without placing a contact goniolens on the cornea?","o":["Light rays originating from the angle strike the inner tear-air corneal interface at an angle steeper than the critical angle (~46 degrees) and undergo Total Internal Reflection back into the eye; a goniolens replaces the air interface with glass/acrylic of matching refractive index","The crystalline lens blocks all peripheral iris light","Tear film absorbs 100% of trabecular meshwork wavelengths","The cornea is completely opaque at the limbus to visible light"],"a":0,"e":"Because the corneal stroma/tear film (n ~1.376) transitions abruptly to air (n = 1.00), rays coming from the peripheral anterior chamber angle exceed the ~46-degree critical angle and suffer Total Internal Reflection at the anterior corneal surface. Applying a contact goniolens (n ~1.49-1.52, coupled with viscous methylcellulose or tears) eliminates the cornea-air interface so light exits smoothly into the mirror/prism for visualization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01739","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Glaucoma Diagnostics","topic":"Central Corneal Thickness (CCT) and IOP","q":"A patient with suspected normal-tension glaucoma has a Goldmann applanation IOP of 17 mmHg in both eyes, but ultrasonic pachymetry measures a thin Central Corneal Thickness (CCT) of 465 microns (normal average ~545 microns). How does this thin cornea affect the measured Goldmann IOP?","o":["A CCT of 465 microns indicates severe corneal edema and pseudopapilledema","A thin cornea bends more easily under the tonometer prism, causing Goldmann tonometry to UNDERESTIMATE true intraocular pressure (meaning actual IOP is higher than 17 mmHg, and thin CCT is itself an independent glaucoma risk factor)","CCT has zero influence on applanation force in Goldmann tonometry","A thin cornea causes Goldmann tonometry to markedly OVERESTIMATE true IOP"],"a":1,"e":"Goldmann applanation tonometry was calibrated assuming an average CCT of ~520-550 microns. Thinner corneas (\u003c500 microns, e.g., post-LASIK or naturally thin) offer less structural resistance to applanation, yielding falsely LOW IOP readings, whereas abnormally thick corneas (\u003e600 microns) require more force and yield falsely HIGH IOP readings. Additionally, the OHTS study proved thin CCT independently predicts conversion from ocular hypertension to POAG.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01740","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Ultrasonography","topic":"Opaque Media Posterior Segment Evaluation","q":"A 65-year-old diabetic man presents for cataract surgery evaluation with light-perception vision and a completely white, mature intumescent cataract in his right eye preventing any view of the retina on indirect ophthalmoscopy or OCT. Which diagnostic test is mandatory preoperatively to rule out a concealed total retinal detachment or vitreous hemorrhage?","o":["Automated Humphrey 30-2 static perimetry","Corneal specular microscopy","Ocular B-scan ultrasonography (high-frequency ~10 MHz two-dimensional ultrasound)","Ishihara pseudoisochromatic color plates"],"a":2,"e":"Whenever opaque ocular media (mature cataract, dense corneal scar, hyphema, or dense vitreous hemorrhage) blocks optical visualization of the posterior segment, 10 MHz B-scan ultrasonography provides real-time two-dimensional cross-sectional imaging of the vitreous, retina, choroid, and optic nerve—detecting retinal detachment, posterior vitreous detachment, tumors (retinoblastoma/melanoma), or occult posterior staphyloma before cataract surgery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01741","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Low Vision Rehabilitation","topic":"Kestenbaum\u0027s Rule Calculation","q":"A patient with age-related macular degeneration has a best-corrected distance visual acuity of 6/60 (20/200) and wishes to read standard 1M (newsprint) text. Applying Kestenbaum\u0027s Rule (reciprocal of distance Snellen acuity), what initial equivalent dioptric reading addition (+ add) is estimated?","o":["+40.00 DS","+1.00 DS","+2.50 DS","+10.00 DS (approximately 2.5x reference magnification at 10 cm focal distance)"],"a":3,"e":"Kestenbaum\u0027s Rule estimates the starting dioptric near add needed to read 1M print (at focal distance 1 / Add) as the reciprocal of the Snellen fraction: for 6/60 (= 1/10) or 20/200 (= 1/10), the reciprocal is 60/6 = +10.00 D (yielding a 10 cm working distance and 10/4 = 2.5x rated magnification under the D/4 convention).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01742","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Neuro-Ophthalmic Assessment","topic":"Swinging Flashlight Test (RAPD)","q":"During a swinging flashlight test in a dimly lit room, shining a bright penlight into the left eye causes brisk constriction of BOTH pupils. When the examiner immediately swings the light across to the right eye, BOTH pupils paradoxically DILATE. What finding is present, and which structures can cause it?","o":["Right Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect (RAPD / Marcus Gunn pupil), caused by asymmetric right optic nerve pathology (e.g., optic neuritis, ischemic optic neuropathy) or extensive retinal detachment—NOT by an uncomplicated cataract","Left third cranial nerve (oculomotor) efferent palsy","Uncomplicated right senile cortical cataract alone","Bilateral normal physiological pupillary hippus"],"a":0,"e":"Because consensual and direct pupillary light reflexes share the efferent Edinger-Westphal / CN III pathway, when light swings from the intact left eye to an impaired right afferent pathway, reduced retinal/optic nerve input causes BOTH pupils to redilate relative to the strong consensual drive from the left eye—confirming a right RAPD (Marcus Gunn pupil). Uncomplicated cataracts scatter light into the retina and NEVER produce an RAPD.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01743","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Diagnostic Instrumentation","topic":"Goldmann Applanation Tonometry (GAT)","q":"During Goldmann Applanation Tonometry (GAT) under the cobalt-blue slit-lamp filter, the ophthalmic technician notices very wide, excessively thick bright-green semicircular fluorescein mires after aligning their inner edges. How does excessive fluorescein affect the IOP reading, and how should the prism be disinfected afterward?","o":["Mire thickness has no effect on IOP; wipe dry with unsterile tissue paper only","Excessively thick mires cause a falsely HIGH IOP reading; wipe and soak the prism tip in 1:10 dilute sodium hypochlorite (or 70% isopropyl alcohol per protocol) followed by sterile water/saline rinse and complete drying before next use","Align the outer edges of the mires instead of the inner edges when fluorescein is excessive","Excessively thick mires cause a falsely LOW IOP reading; boil the plastic prism in an autoclave at 134°C"],"a":1,"e":"In GAT (Imbert-Fick principle), ideal mire width is ~1/10 of the applanated circle diameter (~0.3 mm). Excess fluorescein widens the meniscus, causing overestimation (falsely high IOP) when inner edges meet, whereas insufficient fluorescein yields falsely low IOP. Prism tips must be disinfected (e.g., 1:10 bleach soak 5–10 min to inactivate adenovirus/HSV, thoroughly rinsed and dried to prevent corneal chemical burn).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01744","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ocular Emergencies \u0026 Triage","topic":"Alkali Chemical Burn of the Eye","q":"A construction worker rushes into the ophthalmic casualty clinic with intense pain, blepharospasm, and chalky white lime (calcium hydroxide) particles in his right eye after a mortar splash 10 minutes ago. What is the ABSOLUTE FIRST action before recording visual acuity or performing a full slit-lamp exam?","o":["Instill atropine drops and wait 45 minutes for ophthalmologist arrival before flushing","Patch the eye tightly with antibiotic ointment and ask the patient to return next morning","Instill topical anaesthetic if available and immediately begin copious continuous irrigation with normal saline or Ringer’s lactate (or clean water) for 15–30 minutes, everting eyelids to sweep out retained lime particles until conjunctival pH reaches 7.0–7.4","Record Snellen visual acuity first, then instill weak acetic acid drops to chemically neutralize the alkali"],"a":2,"e":"Alkali burns (lime, ammonia, lye) penetrate corneal stroma rapidly via saponification of cell membranes—every second of delay deepens limbal stem cell ischemia. Immediate, copious irrigation (at least 1–2 liters over 15–30 min) with double lid eversion and particulate removal until pH is 7.0–7.4 is the sole priority overriding visual acuity measurement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01745","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Clinical Optics \u0026 Refraction","topic":"Streak Retinoscopy Working Distance Compensation","q":"During streak retinoscopy in a dark room at a working distance of 67 cm (2/3 meter) without a working-distance lens in the trial frame, you achieve neutrality in both principal meridians using a +3.50 D spherical lens (gross retinoscopy = +3.50 D). What is the patient’s NET spherical refractive error?","o":["+2.50 DS (hypermetropia; subtract +1.00 D working distance)","+5.00 DS (hypermetropia; add +1.50 D working distance)","-2.00 DS (myopia)","+2.00 DS (hypermetropia; subtract +1.50 D working distance)"],"a":3,"e":"Working distance dioptric correction = 1 / distance in meters = 1 / 0.67 m = +1.50 D. Without a +1.50 D retinoscopy lens in place, the net refractive error equals Gross Retinoscopy minus Working Distance: +3.50 D - (+1.50 D) = +2.00 DS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01746","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Diagnostic Instrumentation","topic":"A-Scan Ultrasound Biometry \u0026 Keratometry","q":"While performing contact (applanation) A-scan ultrasound biometry before cataract surgery, if the technician presses the ultrasound probe too firmly against the cornea, what measurement error and postoperative refractive surprise will result?","o":["Falsely SHORT axial length measurement → overestimation of IOL power → unintended postoperative MYOPIA","Falsely flat keratometry → no change in IOL power","Falsely thick crystalline lens → permanent corneal perforation","Falsely LONG axial length measurement → underestimation of IOL power → unintended postoperative HYPEROPIA"],"a":0,"e":"Excessive probe pressure indents the cornea, shortening the measured anterior chamber depth and total axial length (note: a 0.1 mm axial length error alters IOL power by ~0.25–0.30 D). A falsely short axial length causes formulas (SRK/T, Barrett) to recommend too strong an IOL power, leaving the patient unexpectedly myopic postoperatively. Immersion A-scan or optical biometry eliminates corneal indentation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01747","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ocular Emergencies \u0026 Triage","topic":"Acute Primary Angle-Closure Crisis","q":"A 60-year-old hypermetropic woman presents with sudden excruciating right eye pain, ipsilateral headache, nausea/vomiting, colored halos around lights, hazy cornea, shallow anterior chamber, and a mid-dilated fixed pupil with stony-hard globe (IOP 56 mmHg). What action by the ophthalmic technician would be DANGEROUS?","o":["Preparing the Nd:YAG laser room for definitive laser peripheral iridotomy once cornea clears","Instilling Tropicamide 0.8% / Phenylephrine 5% mydriatic drops to perform dilated fundus examination before alerting the ophthalmologist","Documenting presenting visual acuity and checking IOP gently without pressing hard on the globe","Leaving the eye undilated and immediately alerting the ophthalmologist for urgent systemic acetazolamide/mannitol and topical hypotensive therapy"],"a":1,"e":"Acute primary angle-closure crisis occurs when pupillary block traps aqueous behind the iris, bowing it forward to occlude the trabecular meshwork. Instilling mydriatic/cycloplegic drops (tropicamide/phenylephrine/atropine) worsens iris crowding and spikes IOP catastrophically, accelerating irreversible optic nerve ischemia. Immediate urgent ophthalmology escalation for medical IOP lowering and YAG peripheral iridotomy is mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01748","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Community Ophthalmology \u0026 Visual Acuity Screening","topic":"Blindness Definition \u0026 Pinhole Visual Acuity Test","q":"Under the National Programme for Control of Blindness and Visual Impairment (NPCB\u0026VI) / WHO updated criteria in India, what presenting visual acuity in the better eye defines \"blindness,\" and what does a 2-line improvement on Snellen testing through a 1-mm pinhole indicate?","o":["Presenting visual acuity \u003c 6/18 in the worse eye; pinhole improvement indicates dense central cataract or macular scar","Presenting visual acuity \u003c 6/12 in the better eye; pinhole worsens vision in pure myopia","Presenting visual acuity \u003c 3/60 in the better eye (or visual field \u003c 10°); pinhole improvement indicates uncorrected refractive error","No perception of light (NPL) only; pinhole cannot improve any ametropia"],"a":2,"e":"India’s NPCB\u0026VI aligned its definition of blindness with WHO criteria as presenting visual acuity \u003c 3/60 (or central visual field \u003c 10°) in the better eye. During screening, a pinhole restricts light to paraxial rays, reducing blur circles; improvement of \u003e= 2 Snellen lines confirms refractive error (ametropia), whereas lack of improvement or worsening suggests media opacity or retinal/optic nerve disease.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01749","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Diagnostic Instrumentation","topic":"Van Herick Angle Grading \u0026 Retroillumination","q":"Before dilating a 55-year-old patient’s pupils for fundus examination, you perform the Van Herick technique at the temporal limbus using a narrow slit beam angled at 60°. You observe that the peripheral anterior chamber space (dark optical interval between posterior cornea and anterior iris) is less than 1/4 of the corneal thickness (Grade 1). What does this signify?","o":["Normal corneal endothelial keratic precipitates best viewed by specular reflection","Wide-open deep anterior chamber angle (Grade 4); safe to instill strong atropine drops immediately","Posterior vitreous detachment requiring immediate retinal cryopexy","Critically narrow / occludable anterior chamber angle (high risk of angle closure); DO NOT dilate—refer to the ophthalmologist for gonioscopy first"],"a":3,"e":"The Van Herick slit-lamp method compares peripheral anterior chamber depth to corneal thickness (CT) at the limbus: Grade 4 (\u003e=1 CT, wide open), Grade 3 (1/4–1/2 CT), Grade 2 (1/4 CT, narrow), Grade 1 (\u003c1/4 CT, dangerously narrow/angle closure likely). Grade 1 or \u003c=2 warrants withholding mydriatics until gonioscopy confirms safety.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01750","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic OT \u0026 Instrument Sterilization","topic":"Toxic Anterior Segment Syndrome (TASS) Prevention","q":"An ophthalmic OT technician is reprocessing phacoemulsification handpieces and Simcoe irrigation-aspiration cannulas between cataract cases. Which instrument-processing error is a well-known cause of Toxic Anterior Segment Syndrome (TASS) outbreaks?","o":["Inadequate rinsing of enzymatic detergents or residual ophthalmic viscoelastic devices (OVD) inside narrow cannula lumens before sterilization","Keeping intraocular instruments strictly segregated from general surgical instruments","Using single-use disposable phaco tips discarded after each case","Using sterile distilled/deionized water for final lumen flushing"],"a":0,"e":"TASS is an acute sterile postoperative anterior segment inflammation triggered by non-infectious toxic contaminants entering the eye. Residual enzymatic detergent, dried OVD denatured during autoclaving, bacterial endotoxin from ultrasonic bath water, or improper glutaraldehyde residues inside cannulas are major causes—prevented by immediate distilled-water flushing and rigorous multi-volume rinsing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01751","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Spectacle Optics and Dispensing","topic":"Bifocal Near Addition Verification","q":"While neutralizing a patient\u0027s bifocal spectacles on a manual lensometer, the technician measures the distance upper portion as -1.50 DS / -0.50 DC x 180, and when shifting the lens upward to focus the lower near reading segment (with the same cylinder), the sphere drum reads +0.75 DS. What is the Near Reading Addition (+ Add) of this bifocal?","o":["+0.75 DS","+2.25 DS","+1.50 DS","-0.75 DS"],"a":1,"e":"The bifocal Near Addition (+ Add) equals the algebraic difference between the spherical power measured through the near segment and the spherical power of the distance carrier along the same meridian: Add = (+0.75 DS) - (-1.50 DS) = +2.25 DS. (In high-plus lenses \u003e+5.00 D, front-surface neutralization by turning the temples toward the examiner minimizes back-vertex measurement error).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01752","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Glaucoma Diagnostics","topic":"Van Herick Slit-Lamp Technique","q":"Before instilling mydriatic drops (tropicamide/phenylephrine) for a dilated fundus exam, the ophthalmic technician directs a narrow slit-lamp beam perpendicular to the temporal limbus at a 60-degree illumination angle and sees the dark peripheral anterior chamber space is LESS THAN 1/4 of the adjacent corneal thickness (Van Herick Grade 1). What must the technician do?","o":["Record the anterior chamber as deeply open (Grade 4) and proceed with dilation","Switch to Goldmann applanation tonometry using thick fluorescein mires","Do NOT dilate the pupil blindly; alert the ophthalmologist immediately for gonioscopy because a Grade 1 narrow peripheral chamber carries high risk of precipitating acute primary angle-closure glaucoma","Instill double-strength atropine 1% drops immediately"],"a":2,"e":"Van Herick grading compares peripheral anterior chamber depth (PACD) to corneal thickness (CT) at the temporal limbus: Grade 4 (\u003e=1 CT, wide open), Grade 3 (1/4-1/2 CT), Grade 2 (1/4 CT), Grade 1 (\u003c1/4 CT, dangerously narrow/occludable), Grade 0 (slit/closed). Instilling mydriatics in a Van Herick Grade 0-1 eye before gonioscopy/iridotomy can precipitate an acute angle-closure crisis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01753","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Clinical Visual Assessment","topic":"Snellen Metric vs Imperial and Low-Vision Steps","q":"A patient seated at 6 meters cannot read the top 6/60 letter on a standard Snellen chart even when moved forward to 1 meter (1/60). In standardized clinical ophthalmology documentation, what is the correct descending sequence of sub-Snellen visual acuity tests performed next?","o":["No further testing is possible; record immediately as total blindness (NPL)","Pinhole 6/6 -\u003e Color vision -\u003e Schirmer test","Hand Movements -\u003e 6/6 Snellen -\u003e Ishihara plates -\u003e Amsler grid","Counting Fingers (CF at documented distance, e.g., CF at 2 feet/0.5 m) -\u003e Hand Movements (HM) -\u003e Perception of Light (PL) with Projection of Rays (PR in 4 quadrants) -\u003e No Perception of Light (NPL)"],"a":3,"e":"If a patient fails to read the 6/60 optotype even at 1 meter (1/60), visual acuity is stepped down methodically in a well-lit room (with fellow eye completely occluded): Counting Fingers (recording exact distance), then Hand Movements close to the face, then Perception of Light (shining bright penlight while fellow eye is tightly covered) plus Projection of Rays (assessing intact peripheral retinal function across superior, inferior, nasal, temporal quadrants), and finally NPL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01754","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Contact Lens Practice","topic":"Acanthamoeba Keratitis Prevention","q":"A soft contact lens wearer presents with agonizing ocular pain out of proportion to mild initial signs, photophobia, radial perineuritis, and a central ring-shaped stromal infiltrate after habitually rinsing her lenses and lens case with domestic tap water and swimming while wearing lenses. Which devastating corneal pathogen is classically linked to tap-water contact lens hygiene errors?","o":["Acanthamoeba species (free-living protozoan amoeba)","Varicella-zoster virus","Demodex folliculorum eyelid mite","Molluscum contagiosum poxvirus"],"a":0,"e":"Acanthamoeba cysts thrive in domestic tap water, well water, swimming pools, and hot tubs, adhering strongly to soft hydrogel lenses and lens cases. Rinsing lenses/cases with tap water, making homemade saline, topping off old solution, or swimming/showering in lenses causes sight-threatening Acanthamoeba keratitis (characterized by severe pain out of proportion, radial keratoneuritis, and late ring infiltrate).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01755","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Clinical Visual Assessment","topic":"Farnsworth-Munsell D-15 / 100-Hue Test","q":"Why is the Farnsworth D-15 (or Farnsworth-Munsell 100-Hue) cap-arrangement test preferred over standard Ishihara plates when monitoring a patient on ethambutol or evaluating acquired macular/glaucomatous optic nerve color loss?","o":["Ishihara plates only screen congenital red-green (protan/deutan) defects, whereas Farnsworth cap arrangement tests detect BOTH red-green AND acquired blue-yellow (tritan) defects while quantifying severity","Ishihara plates detect blue-yellow tritanopia far better than Farnsworth tests","Farnsworth D-15 requires injecting IV sodium fluorescein before cap sorting","Farnsworth D-15 measures axial length in millimeters"],"a":0,"e":"Standard 38-plate Ishihara charts test only X-linked congenital red-green deficiencies (protan/deutan) and miss blue-yellow (tritan) color defects commonly seen in glaucoma, diabetic retinopathy, and early drug optic neuropathies. Farnsworth D-15 / 100-Hue tests require arranging subtle pastel color caps in chromatic sequence, plotting distinct protan, deutan, or tritan confusion axes and quantifying total error score.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01756","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Contact Lens and Spectacle Optics","topic":"High-Power Lens Vertex Conversion","q":"A high-myopic patient has a spectacle refraction of -10.00 DS at a vertex distance of 12 mm (0.012 m) in front of the cornea. Using the vertex distance conversion formula [Fc = Fs / (1 - d * Fs)], what spherical contact lens power (at 0 mm vertex distance) provides equivalent retinal focus?","o":["Approximately -9.00 DS (exact -8.93 D; less minus required on the cornea)","Approximately -11.25 DS (more minus required on the cornea)","Exactly -10.00 DS (vertex distance has zero effect above 4.00 D)","Approximately -5.00 DS"],"a":0,"e":"Moving a diverging minus spectacle lens closer to the eye (from 12 mm onto the cornea) increases its effective diverging power at the eye—therefore LESS minus contact lens power is needed! Using Fc = Fs / (1 - d*Fs) = -10.00 / [1 - (0.012 x -10.00)] = -10.00 / (1 + 0.12) = -10.00 / 1.12 = -8.93 D (rounded to -9.00 D). (Conversely, a high-plus hyperope needs MORE plus power in contact lenses!).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01757","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Diagnostics","topic":"Fluorescein vs Rose Bengal / Lissamine Green","q":"After minor corneal trauma, the ophthalmologist suspects a tiny full-thickness corneal perforation and asks the technician to assist with a Seidel test using concentrated 2% sodium fluorescein under cobalt-blue slit-lamp illumination. What positive Seidel finding confirms an active aqueous humor leak?","o":["Pink-red staining of mucous threads in the inferior fornix","A bright glowing yellow-green stream/rivulet of diluted fluorescein flowing downward through the dark orange/brown concentrated dye pool over the wound","Complete absence of any fluorescence under cobalt-blue light","Uniform dry dark blue spots across the upper eyelid"],"a":1,"e":"Concentrated fluorescein appears dark orange-brown; when leaking aqueous humor dilutes it locally along the corneal wound, it fluoresces intensely bright green under cobalt-blue light (\u0027waterfall\u0027 stream = Seidel positive, requiring immediate shield without pressure and surgical repair). By contrast, Rose Bengal and Lissamine Green stain dead/devitalized epithelial cells and mucus-deficient zones in dry eye or HSV dendritic ulcer borders.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01758","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Retinal Diagnostics","topic":"Amsler Grid Examination","q":"When instructing an age-related macular degeneration (AMD) patient on how to check for new metamorphopsia (wavy/distorted lines) or central scotomas using a standard 10 x 10 cm Amsler Grid, what testing conditions must be followed?","o":["Test ONE eye at a time (covering the fellow eye), wearing habitual near reading glasses under good lighting at ~33 cm distance while staring steadily at the central fixation dot","Test both eyes together from 6 meters away without reading glasses","Move the eyes rapidly across every corner instead of fixating on the central dot","Use a completely dark room with a red filter"],"a":0,"e":"Binocular viewing allows the healthy fellow eye to mask unilateral macular distortion, so strict monocular occlusion is mandatory. Held at 33 cm (subtending ~20 degrees centered on the fovea, 1 degree per 5 mm square) with proper near correction, any newly curved/wavy lines (metamorphopsia) or missing boxes (scotoma) signal urgent subretinal fluid/choroidal neovascularization needing prompt OCT and anti-VEGF therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01759","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Clinical Visual Assessment","topic":"Pelli-Robson Contrast Sensitivity Chart","q":"A 61-year-old patient with early nuclear/posterior subcapsular cataract and mild optic neuropathy reads 6/6 on the standard high-contrast black-on-white Snellen chart, yet complains bitterly of \u0027washed-out\u0027 vision and difficulty seeing steps in dim fog. Which specialized chart held at 1 meter tests fading low-contrast letters of constant large size to quantify this disability?","o":["Goldmann tonometer prism scale","Standard 6-meter illuminated black-on-white Snellen chart","Pelli-Robson Contrast Sensitivity Chart (measuring log contrast sensitivity across triplets of progressively fainter gray letters)","Keratometer mire rings"],"a":2,"e":"Standard Snellen charts test only maximum ~100% high-contrast spatial resolution, missing early functional impairment in glaucoma, optic neuritis, diabetic retinopathy, and early cataract glare. The Pelli-Robson chart presents large constant-size letters arranged in triplets decreasing by 0.15 log contrast units per triplet down to faint gray, sensitively quantifying real-world contrast loss.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01760","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Spectacle Dispensing","topic":"Distance PD vs Near PD","q":"When measuring a patient\u0027s Interpupillary Distance (PD) for dispensing bifocal or progressive addition spectacles, how does Near PD (at 40 cm) compare with Distance PD (at infinity), and why is precise measurement critical in high-power lenses?","o":["Distance PD and Near PD are always identical because eyeballs cannot rotate horizontally","PD only matters for zero-power plano sunglasses","Near PD is always 6 mm WIDER than Distance PD due to divergence","Near PD is typically 2 to 4 mm NARROWER than Distance PD because the eyes converge nasally during near focus; inaccurate PD decentration induces unwanted horizontal prism (diplopia/asthenopia)"],"a":3,"e":"During near fixation at 40 cm, medial rectus convergence shifts both pupils nasally, making Near PD ~2-4 mm narrower than Distance PD (hence bifocal reading segments are inset nasally ~1.5-2 mm per eye). Aligning optical centers accurately to monocular PDs prevents induced base-in/base-out horizontal prismatic errors per Prentice\u0027s rule, especially in high-diopter prescriptions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01761","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Pharmacology","topic":"Phenylephrine vs Tropicamide vs Atropine","q":"Which diagnostic dilating drop produces pupillary mydriasis purely via alpha-1 adrenergic dilator-muscle stimulation WITHOUT paralyzing ciliary accommodation (zero cycloplegia), and carries risk of acute hypertensive crisis or arrhythmias in elderly cardiovascular patients (especially at 10% strength)?","o":["Phenylephrine (2.5% preferred; 10% high-risk systemic vasoconstriction)","Atropine sulfate 1% (longest-acting 7-14 day muscarinic cycloplegic)","Pilocarpine 2% (parasympathomimetic miotic)","Cyclopentolate 1% (6-24 hr cycloplegic)"],"a":0,"e":"Phenylephrine directly stimulates alpha-1 receptors on the iris dilator pupillae, dilating the pupil cleanly for ophthalmoscopy while leaving ciliary accommodation intact (zero cycloplegia). Systemic absorption via nasolacrimal mucosa—particularly with 10% concentration in infants or elderly hypertensives—can trigger severe blood pressure spikes, tachycardia, or myocardial ischemia, so 2.5% with punctal occlusion is safer.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01762","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Diagnostic Instrumentation","topic":"Ishihara Pseudoisochromatic Plates","q":"When screening a candidate for congenital red-green color deficiency using Ishihara pseudoisochromatic plates, which testing condition is required for valid results?","o":["Allow the candidate 60 seconds to trace every plate slowly using red-tinted sunglasses","Hold plates at 75 cm perpendicular to line of sight under natural daylight (or daylight-balanced illuminant), allow \u003c= 3 seconds per plate, with patient wearing near reading correction if presbyopic (never tinted lenses)","Use Ishihara plates primarily to diagnose acquired blue-yellow tritan defects from glaucoma","Perform the test in a pitch-dark room under a dim yellow tungsten bulb at 3 meters distance"],"a":1,"e":"Ishihara plates screen congenital X-linked red-green (protan/deutan) deficiencies. Accuracy requires uniform daylight illumination (artificial yellow bulbs alter hue discrimination), 75 cm viewing distance tilted perpendicular to gaze, near spectacles if needed, and \u003c= 3 seconds response time per plate (long staring allows edge-brightness guessing).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01763","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Diagnostic Instrumentation","topic":"Humphrey Visual Field Reliability Indices","q":"On a Humphrey 24-2 SITA Standard visual field printout for a glaucoma suspect, the False Positive rate is 32% (\"XX\"), the Mean Deviation is unusually high (+4.5 dB) with \"white scotomas\" on the grayscale, and the foveal threshold reads 52 dB. How should the ophthalmic technician interpret this test?","o":["High false-negative rate caused by miosis from pilocarpine","Normal supranormal optic nerve function proving glaucoma is completely absent","Unreliable test (\"trigger-happy\" patient clicking the response button even when no stimulus was presented; False Positives \u003e 15% invalidates the printout)—re-instruct patient and repeat the visual field","Classic \"cloverleaf\" field proving dense bitemporal hemianopia"],"a":2,"e":"In Humphrey SITA perimetry, False Positives \u003e 15% (or Fixation Losses \u003e 20%) mark the field unreliable (\"XX\"). Trigger-happy patients press the button without seeing a light, creating unrealistically high sensitivities (\"white scotomas\" on grayscale, foveal threshold \u003e 40 dB, positive MD) that mask genuine glaucomatous field defects.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01764","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Clinical Optics \u0026 Dispensing Optometry","topic":"Prentice’s Rule Calculation","q":"On a lensmeter verification check, the optical center of a patient’s +6.00 DS spectacle lens sits 5 mm (0.5 cm) ABOVE (superior to) the patient’s pupillary center. Applying Prentice’s Rule (Δ = c × F), what induced prismatic effect does the eye experience?","o":["1.2 Prism Diopters (Δ) Base-Down","30.0 Prism Diopters (Δ) Base-Up","3.0 Prism Diopters (Δ) Base-Down","3.0 Prism Diopters (Δ) Base-Up"],"a":3,"e":"By Prentice’s Rule, Induced Prism (Δ) = decentration in centimeters (c) × lens power in diopters (F) = 0.5 cm × 6.00 D = 3.0 Δ. Because a plus (convex) lens is thickest at its optical center (acting as two prisms base-to-base at the optical center), when the optical center sits superior to the visual axis, the prism base lies ABOVE the pupil = Base-Up.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01765","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ocular Emergencies \u0026 Triage","topic":"Open-Globe Injury (Penetrating Trauma) Protocol","q":"A mechanic struck in the left eye by a high-speed metal fragment while hammering presents with reduced vision, a shallow/flat anterior chamber, a teardrop-shaped pupil peaked toward 4 o’clock, and a dark knuckle of uveal tissue visible at the limbus. Which action MUST BE STRICTLY AVOIDED?","o":["Measuring intraocular pressure (tonometry), applying a tight pressure eye patch, instilling ointments/unprescribed drops, or forcefully irrigating the globe—instead place a rigid protective Fox shield and refer STAT for surgical repair","Keeping the patient NPO and giving systemic antiemetics/analgesics as ordered","Obtaining gentle non-contrast orbital CT (avoiding MRI due to possible metallic foreign body)","Placing a non-contacting rigid protective shield taped to the orbital rim"],"a":0,"e":"Peaked/teardrop pupil, flat anterior chamber, and prolapsed iris/uvea are pathognomonic of an open-globe injury. Any external pressure—including Goldmann/Schiotz tonometry, lid squeezing, tight patching, or forceful irrigation—can extrude intraocular contents (lens, vitreous, retina) causing irreversible blindness. Protect with a rigid Fox shield resting on orbital bone, keep NPO, prevent vomiting/Valsalva, and escalate STAT.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01766","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Diagnostic Instrumentation","topic":"Schirmer I Test \u0026 Tear Break-Up Time (TBUT)","q":"At an OSPE station assessing a patient with burning, gritty eyes, you perform a Schirmer I test (Whatman #41 filter paper folded 5 mm at the lateral lower lid fornix WITHOUT topical anaesthetic for 5 minutes) and Tear Film Break-Up Time (TBUT). Which pair of results confirms aqueous deficiency and tear-film instability?","o":["Schirmer I test performed after 10 minutes with thick antibiotic ointment applied beforehand","Schirmer I strip wetting \u003c 10 mm in 5 minutes (severe \u003c 5 mm) and TBUT \u003c 10 seconds","Schirmer I strip wetting 15 mm in 1 minute and TBUT \u003e 15 seconds","Schirmer I strip wetting \u003e= 25 mm in 5 minutes and TBUT \u003e 20 seconds"],"a":1,"e":"Schirmer I (without anaesthetic) measures combined basal and reflex aqueous tear secretion: normal wetting over 5 minutes is \u003e= 10–15 mm; \u003c 10 mm indicates aqueous tear deficiency (\u003c 5 mm severe). In TBUT, sodium fluorescein is instilled and observed under cobalt-blue slit-lamp light after a blink; first dry dark spot appearing in \u003c 10 seconds indicates tear-film instability (meibomian/mucin deficiency).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01767","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ocular Emergencies \u0026 Triage","topic":"Central Retinal Artery Occlusion (CRAO)","q":"A 68-year-old hypertensive man with atrial fibrillation presents 45 minutes after sudden, completely painless profound vision loss (hand movements only) in his right eye. Pupil exam shows a marked Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect (RAPD), and fundus screening reveals a pale milky-white ischemic retina with a bright \"cherry-red spot\" at the fovea. What emergency diagnosis is this?","o":["Allergic vernal keratoconjunctivitis","Chronic open-angle glaucoma needing routine glasses update","Central Retinal Artery Occlusion (CRAO) — an acute retinal stroke emergency requiring immediate ophthalmologist alert (and systemic stroke/cardioembolic evaluation)","Uncomplicated senile immature cataract"],"a":2,"e":"CRAO causes acute painless monocular blindness, RAPD, attenuated arterioles (\"box-carring\"), and generalized inner-retinal ischemic pallor sparing the foveola—where underlying choroidal circulation shines through as the classic \"cherry-red spot.\" Retinal ganglion cells suffer irreversible necrosis within ~90–120 minutes, demanding immediate ophthalmic/stroke emergency escalation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01768","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Clinical Optics \u0026 Refraction","topic":"Duochrome (Bichrome) Red-Green Test","q":"At the end of monocular subjective refraction with a -2.50 DS trial lens, the patient views the Duochrome (Red-Green) chart and reports that the black optotypes appear sharper and clearer on the GREEN background than on the Red background. What spherical adjustment should the optometrist/ophthalmic technician make?","o":["Add -0.25 DS (increase minus power to -2.75 DS) until Green becomes even darker","Rotate the cylinder axis by 90 degrees immediately","Keep -2.50 DS because Green clearer than Red is the final endpoint for young myopes","Add +0.25 DS (i.e., reduce minus power to -2.25 DS; \"Green Add Plus — GAP\") because the eye is currently over-minused/hyperopic"],"a":3,"e":"Chromatic aberration focuses shorter-wavelength Green light slightly in front of longer-wavelength Red light. When Green letters look sharper, the focal interval sits behind the retina (over-minused or under-plussed)—requiring Plus power (\"GAP: Green Add Plus\") until Red and Green appear equally sharp. Conversely, if Red is clearer, Add Minus (\"RAM\").","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01769","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Contact Lenses \u0026 Anterior Segment Care","topic":"Microbial \u0026 Acanthamoeba Keratitis Prevention","q":"A college student wearing monthly soft contact lenses presents with severe eye pain, photophobia, and a central white corneal infiltrate after sleeping in her lenses and rinsing her lens case in tap water. Beyond immediate lens removal and urgent ophthalmologist referral for corneal scraping, what hygiene practices must be corrected?","o":["Strictly prohibit rinsing lenses or storage cases with tap/well/bottled water (major risk for devastating Acanthamoeba keratitis), prohibit overnight sleeping/swimming in lenses, and use fresh sterile disinfecting solution daily without \"topping off\"","Switch to boiling the soft hydrogel lenses in tap water and reusing old case solution","Clean contact lenses using saliva when multipurpose solution runs out","Advise continuing lens wear over the ulcer to act as a bandage while instilling steroid drops"],"a":0,"e":"Overnight wear (hypoxia/microtrauma -\u003e Pseudomonas aeruginosa) and tap-water exposure (Acanthamoeba cysts in domestic water/biofilm) are the primary preventable causes of vision-threatening contact lens keratitis. Patients must immediately discontinue wear, bring lenses/case for culture, avoid empiric steroids before workup, and strictly use fresh sterile solutions (never water or saliva).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01770","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Diagnostic Instrumentation","topic":"With-the-Rule vs Against-the-Rule Corneal Astigmatism","q":"Keratometry on a young patient’s right cornea reads K1 = 41.50 D @ 180° (horizontal meridian) and K2 = 44.50 D @ 90° (vertical meridian). How much corneal astigmatism is present, and what type is it?","o":["Zero corneal astigmatism (spherical cornea)","3.00 D of With-the-Rule (WTR) corneal astigmatism (vertical meridian steeper than horizontal)","3.00 D of Against-the-Rule (ATR) corneal astigmatism","86.00 D of Oblique astigmatism"],"a":1,"e":"Corneal astigmatism magnitude equals |K2 - K1| = |44.50 - 41.50| = 3.00 D. Because the vertical meridian (90° ± 30°) has greater refractive power (steeper curvature, smaller radius) than the horizontal meridian (180° ± 30°), it is classified as With-the-Rule (WTR) astigmatism (corrected by minus cylinder around axis 180° or plus cylinder around 90°).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01771","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Optics and Refraction","topic":"Stenopaeic Slit Refraction","q":"When refracting a patient with an irregular corneal scar or early cortical cataract where streak retinoscopy reflexes are dull and distorted, how does the ophthalmic technician use a 1 mm Stenopaeic Slit placed in the trial frame to determine astigmatic refraction?","o":["Rotate the slit across 180 degrees until visual acuity is sharpest (locating one principal meridian), neutralize that meridian with spherical lenses, then rotate the slit 90 degrees to neutralize the second meridian and convert into a spherocylindrical prescription","Place the slit horizontally only and measure IOP with Goldmann prism","Use the slit as a direct ophthalmoscope to examine the optic disc cup","Place red and green filters simultaneously over both eyes"],"a":0,"e":"A stenopaeic slit (0.5-1.0 mm narrow aperture) isolates rays entering along the meridian parallel to the slit\u0027s length while blocking perpendicular rays—eliminating Sturm\u0027s conoid blur along that single meridian. By finding the sharpest axis, neutralizing with spheres, repeating 90 degrees away, and constructing an optical cross, the technician calculates exact sphere, cylinder, and axis even when irregular media obscures retinoscopy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01772","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Corneal Diagnostics","topic":"Placido / Scheimpflug Topographic Red Flags","q":"A 19-year-old man with chronic eye rubbing from vernal keratoconjunctivitis presents with rapidly worsening myopic astigmatism. Streak retinoscopy shows a swirling \u0027scissoring\u0027 reflex, Munson\u0027s sign (V-shaped lower lid indentation on downgaze) is positive, and corneal topography shows localized inferior corneal steepening (\u003e48.0 D) with skewed radial axes and focal corneal thinning. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Congenital red-green protanopia","Central serous chorioretinopathy","Keratoconus (progressive non-inflammatory corneal ectasia)","Primary open-angle glaucoma"],"a":2,"e":"Progressive bilateral (often asymmetric) stromal thinning and conical corneal protrusion define Keratoconus—strongly linked to vigorous chronic eye rubbing in allergic vernal keratoconjunctivitis. Early clues include scissors reflex on retinoscopy, Fleischer iron ring, Vogt striae, Munson\u0027s sign, and inferior-temporal steepening/posterior elevation on Pentacam/Placido topography—prompting Corneal Collagen Cross-Linking (CXL) to halt progression.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01773","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Glaucoma and Neuro-Ophthalmic Perimetry","topic":"Glaucomatous Horizontal Midline vs Chiasmal Vertical Midline","q":"On automated Humphrey 24-2 perimetry printouts, how does a classic glaucomatous nerve-fiber-bundle field defect (e.g., Roenne nasal step or Bjerrum arcuate scotoma) differ topographically from a pituitary macroadenoma chiasmal field defect?","o":["Glaucomatous defects respect the HORIZONTAL midline (reflecting superior/inferior retinal nerve fiber bundle anatomy at the horizontal raphe), whereas chiasmal lesions produce bitemporal hemianopia respecting the VERTICAL midline","Glaucomatous defects always respect the vertical midline cleanly across both eyes","Pituitary tumors produce an isolated superior nasal step respecting the horizontal raphe","Glaucoma causes complete homonymous hemianopia in all four quadrants symmetrically"],"a":0,"e":"Retinal nerve fiber axons arch above and below the macula to meet at the temporal horizontal raphe—never crossing the horizontal midline. Consequently, glaucomatous ganglion cell loss yields paracentral scotomas, Seidel/Bjerrum arcuate defects, and nasal steps strictly obeying the HORIZONTAL meridian. By contrast, crossing nasal retinal fibers at the optic chiasm and post-chiasmal tracts obey the VERTICAL meridian (bitemporal or homonymous hemianopias).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01774","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Ophthalmic Emergencies","topic":"Retrobulbar Hemorrhage Management","q":"Immediately after a retrobulbar/peribulbar local anesthetic block (or blunt orbital trauma), a patient develops tense proptosis, severe deep orbital pain, stony-hard globe to palpation, IOP 56 mmHg, visual acuity dropping to hand movements, and a new Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect (RAPD). What emergency sight-saving procedure must be performed immediately (\u003c60-90 minutes)?","o":["Firm pressure eye patching with tight elastic head bandage","Delay all intervention for 24 hours until elective orbital CT scan reports return","Instill topical pilocarpine 2% drops and send the patient home","Immediate bedside Lateral Canthotomy and Inferior Cantholysis to decompress the orbit and prevent permanent ischemic central retinal artery/optic nerve blindness"],"a":3,"e":"The orbit is an enclosed rigid bony compartment bounded anteriorly by the orbital septum and tarsal ligaments. Rapid retrobulbar arterial bleeding creates Orbital Compartment Syndrome—choking off retinal/optic nerve perfusion within 60-90 minutes. Immediate lateral canthotomy plus cutting the inferior crus of the lateral canthal tendon (cantholysis) allows the globe to advance anteriorly, dropping intraorbital/intraocular pressure instantly to save sight.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01775","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Spectacle Optics and Dispensing","topic":"Geneva Lens Measure (Lens Clock)","q":"What does a three-pronged Geneva Lens Measure (Lens Clock) physically measure when pressed perpendicularly against the front and back surfaces of a crown-glass spectacle lens (calibrated index n = 1.523), and how does it identify a spherocylindrical lens?","o":["It measures the surface curvature power (diopters) of each individual surface—a spherical surface gives the same reading in all meridians as rotated, whereas a toric/cylindrical surface shows varying power between two principal meridians 90 degrees apart","It measures the patient\u0027s axial corneal length in millimeters","It measures intraocular pressure directly through the closed eyelid","It measures progressive corridor pupillary distance directly"],"a":0,"e":"A Geneva lens clock uses two fixed outer pins and a central spring-loaded movable pin to gauge surface sagitta/radius of curvature calibrated to crown glass (n = 1.523). Summing front-surface and back-surface readings estimates total lens power (requiring index correction for polycarbonate n = 1.586 or high-index plastics), and rotating the clock 180 degrees reveals whether cylinder was ground on the front or back (minus-cylinder) surface.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01776","course":"Diploma in Ophthalmic Technology","subject":"Lacrimal System Assessment","topic":"ROPLAS Test and Lacrimal Syringing","q":"A 55-year-old woman presents with persistent epiphora (watering) in her right eye. On gentle digital pressure over the medial canthus below the medial palpebral ligament (ROPLAS test), mucopurulent discharge regurgitates through the lower punctum; during sterile saline syringing via the lower punctum, fluid immediately regurgitates back through the UPPER punctum without reaching the throat. Where is the anatomical obstruction?","o":["Completely patent normal nasolacrimal drainage system","Complete obstruction distal to the common canaliculus—specifically at the Nasolacrimal Duct (NLD) / lacrimal sac junction (chronic dacryocystitis)","Obstruction inside the lower canaliculus alone (fluid would reflux out the SAME lower punctum only)","Blocked retinal central vein"],"a":1,"e":"During lacrimal syringing via the lower canaliculus: (1) free clear fluid tasted in the throat = patent system; (2) immediate clear reflux out the SAME lower punctum = lower canalicular block; (3) delayed/mucopurulent reflux out the OPPOSITE upper punctum alongside positive ROPLAS (Regurgitation On Pressure over Lacrimal Sac) confirms fluid reached the sac and hit a blocked Nasolacrimal Duct (NLD), typical of chronic dacryocystitis (contraindicating elective intraocular cataract surgery until treated via DCR/DCT!).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01777","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiation Protection and Safety","topic":"Occupational Dose Limits and Personnel Monitoring","q":"According to Atomic Energy Regulatory Board (AERB) India guidelines, what is the occupational effective dose limit for radiation workers averaged over 5 consecutive years, and where should a single TLD badge be worn when using a lead apron?","o":["50 mSv/year averaged over 5 years (max 100 mSv in a single year); worn outside the lead apron on the wrist","5 mSv/year averaged over 5 years; worn outside the lead apron on the collar only","20 mSv/year averaged over 5 years (max 30 mSv in a single year); worn inside the lead apron at chest level","1 mSv/year averaged over 5 years; worn inside the lead apron at the ankle"],"a":2,"e":"AERB follows ICRP limits specifying 20 mSv/year averaged over 5 consecutive years (100 mSv in 5 years, with no single year exceeding 30 mSv in Indian AERB regulations). A single TLD badge for whole-body effective dose estimation is worn at chest level underneath the protective lead apron.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01778","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Computed Tomography and Contrast Media","topic":"Contrast Media Adverse Reactions","q":"A 61-year-old diabetic patient scheduled for contrast-enhanced CT abdomen has serum creatinine 1.9 mg/dL (eGFR 38 mL/min/1.73 m2) and takes metformin 1000 mg twice daily. What is the safest radiology department protocol?","o":["Administer double-dose high-osmolar ionic contrast without hydration","Instruct the patient to take an extra dose of metformin immediately before CT to prevent hyperglycemia","Proceed without checking renal function or notifying the radiologist because oral drugs never interact with contrast","Use low/iso-osmolar contrast with IV hydration if contrast is essential, and withhold metformin at the time of contrast for 48 hours until renal function is reassessed"],"a":3,"e":"Patients with reduced renal function (eGFR 30-44 mL/min/1.73 m2) receiving intravascular iodinated contrast are at risk for contrast-associated acute kidney injury, which can impair metformin clearance and precipitate lactic acidosis. Standard radiology safety guidelines recommend hydration, minimizing contrast volume with low/iso-osmolar agents, and withholding metformin for 48 hours pending renal reassessment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01779","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning","topic":"Wrist and Carpal Projections","q":"A patient presents with anatomical snuffbox tenderness after falling on an outstretched hand, and routine PA/lateral wrist views are equivocal. How should the hand and X-ray tube be positioned to obtain a dedicated PA scaphoid view that eliminates foreshortening?","o":["Position the wrist in ulnar deviation with the central ray angled 10-15 degrees proximally (toward the elbow)","Perform a carpal tunnel tangential (Gaynor-Hart) projection only","Position the wrist in radial deviation with the central ray angled 30 degrees distally","Rotate the forearm into full supination with a 45-degree lateral tilt"],"a":0,"e":"In neutral PA wrist positioning, the scaphoid tilts palmarly and appears foreshortened. Ulnar deviation opens the radial carpal space and elongates the scaphoid parallel to the image receptor, and angling the central ray 10-15 degrees proximally (or elevating the hand 20 degrees in the modified Stecher view) projects the scaphoid waist clearly without overlap.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01780","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"MRI Safety and Cross-Sectional Imaging","topic":"ACR MRI Four-Zone Safety System","q":"In the ACR four-zone MRI safety layout, in which zone does the static magnetic field (\u003e5 Gauss / 0.5 mT fringe field) pose an immediate ferromagnetic projectile hazard, requiring all patients, staff, and crash carts to be fully screened and MR-safe/conditional before entry?","o":["Administrative billing desk outside the radiology department","Zone IV (the magnet room itself), controlled via Zone III","Zone I (general public waiting corridor)","Zone II (patient reception and screening interview desk)"],"a":1,"e":"Zone IV is the MRI scanner room containing the active superconducting magnet (always on, typically 1.5 T or 3.0 T), where ferromagnetic objects (oxygen cylinders, wheelchairs, scissors) become lethal projectiles. Zone III is the physically restricted control/staging vestibule guarding access into Zone IV after thorough Zone II screening.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01781","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning","topic":"Pneumoperitoneum Detection","q":"A critically ill, non-ambulatory patient with suspected perforated peptic ulcer cannot stand or sit upright for an erect chest radiograph. Which horizontal-beam radiographic projection should the radiographer perform to demonstrate free intraperitoneal air?","o":["Right lateral decubitus abdomen with vertical central ray","Supine AP abdomen with 30-degree caudal tube angulation","Left lateral decubitus abdomen (patient lying on left side for 10 minutes before exposure using a horizontal beam)","Prone PA abdomen after oral barium administration"],"a":2,"e":"In the left lateral decubitus position, free intraperitoneal air rises to the highest point—between the lateral border of the liver and the right lateral abdominal wall/diaphragm—where it contrasts sharply against homogeneous liver density rather than blending with gastric/splenic flexure gas. Keeping the patient on the left side for 5-10 minutes allows trapped air bubbles to ascend.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01782","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiation Protection and Safety","topic":"ALARA in Interventional Fluoroscopy","q":"When operating a mobile C-arm fluoroscope in the operating theatre, which geometric and technical configuration minimizes patient entrance skin radiation dose and scatter to staff?","o":["Keep the X-ray tube under the table (as far from the patient as possible), position the image intensifier/detector as close to the patient as possible, use pulsed fluoroscopy, tight collimation, and last-image hold","Place the X-ray tube directly touching the patient\u0027s anterior skin and raise the detector 50 cm above the table","Use continuous high-dose magnification mode with wide-open shutters throughout the procedure","Disable last-image hold and record continuous cine loops for static screw checks"],"a":0,"e":"By the inverse square law, placing the X-ray tube far from the patient (under-table) dramatically lowers entrance skin dose, while keeping the flat-panel detector/image intensifier close to the patient reduces both patient dose (via Automatic Brightness Control) and lateral scatter to the operator\u0027s eyes/thyroid. Pulsed fluoroscopy, tight collimation, and last-image hold further slash cumulative dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01783","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Computed Tomography Physics","topic":"CT Pitch Calculation and Beam Hardening","q":"A 64-slice helical CT scanner operates with a total collimated X-ray beam width of 40 mm (64 x 0.625 mm) and advances the patient table by 60 mm per 360-degree gantry rotation. What is the CT pitch factor, and how does it affect patient radiation dose compared with pitch 1.0 (holding mAs constant)?","o":["Pitch = 2.4; doubles patient radiation dose","Pitch = 0.67; increases patient radiation dose by 50%","Pitch = 1.5; increases patient radiation dose by 50%","Pitch = 1.5; reduces patient radiation dose by one-third compared with pitch 1.0"],"a":3,"e":"Helical CT pitch = Table travel per 360-degree gantry rotation / Total collimated beam width = 60 mm / 40 mm = 1.5. Because pitch \u003e1.0 spreads the helical X-ray projections over a larger z-axis volume without overlapping (assuming fixed mAs rather than effective mAs), patient radiation dose is inversely proportional to pitch (1 / 1.5 = 0.67, i.e., a 33% dose reduction).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01784","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Digital Radiography Systems","topic":"Photostimulable Phosphor (PSP) Plate Maintenance","q":"A Computed Radiography (CR) cassette has been sitting unused in the X-ray department storage rack for 5 days before being used for a pediatric abdomen radiograph. Why should the radiographer run the CR plate through a dedicated secondary erase cycle just before exposing the patient?","o":["Because PSP plates continuously absorb ambient background/scatter radiation over time, creating fog noise unless erased prior to use","To recharge the wireless Wi-Fi battery inside a passive CR cassette","Because unexposed CR plates lose their mechanical lead backing after 48 hours","Because the barium fluorohalide phosphor freezes below 25 degrees C and needs heating"],"a":0,"e":"Photostimulable phosphor (PSP) imaging plates (BaFBr:Eu2+) remain sensitive to ambient natural and departmental scatter radiation even inside closed cassettes. Any cassette stored unused for \u003e=24-48 hours accumulates background fog or residual ghost latent signals and must undergo a bright-light erasure cycle before clinical exposure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01785","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Physics and Equipment","topic":"Bremsstrahlung vs Characteristic Radiation","q":"In a standard rotating tungsten-anode diagnostic X-ray tube (tungsten K-shell electron binding energy = 69.5 keV), what minimum tube potential must be applied before K-characteristic X-rays can be produced, and which interaction generates the majority (~80-85%) of the diagnostic X-ray beam at 80 kVp?","o":["Characteristic photons make up 100% of the diagnostic beam at all kVp settings","At least 70 kVp is required for tungsten K-characteristic X-rays; Bremsstrahlung (braking) radiation accounts for the majority (~80-85%) of photons at 80 kVp","140 kVp is the minimum for tungsten K-characteristic X-rays; Compton scatter generates the primary tube beam","30 kVp is sufficient for tungsten K-characteristic X-rays; photoelectric effect creates 90% of tube output"],"a":1,"e":"To eject a tungsten K-shell electron (binding energy 69.5 keV), projectile electrons must possess kinetic energy \u003e69.5 keV, requiring \u003e=70 kVp. Even at 80-100 kVp, continuous-spectrum Bremsstrahlung radiation (resulting from deceleration of projectile electrons near tungsten nuclei) contributes ~80-90% of the X-ray beam.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01786","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning","topic":"Shoulder Trauma Series","q":"A patient arrives in the trauma bay after an electric shock/seizure holding his right arm locked in internal rotation with suspected posterior shoulder dislocation. Why must the radiographer avoid forcing external rotation for a standard AP external-rotation view and instead perform an AP neutral plus Scapular Y (PA oblique) or Velpeau axillary view?","o":["Scapular Y view uses 150 kVp to penetrate the sternum","Posterior shoulder dislocations are best seen by placing the patient prone on the injured shoulder","Forcing external rotation can cause an iatrogenic proximal humeral fracture or worsen neurovascular injury; the Scapular Y view confirms anterior versus posterior humeral head dislocation safely with the arm immobilized","External rotation is avoided only because it makes the clavicle disappear"],"a":2,"e":"Posterior shoulder dislocation (classically following seizure or electrocution) often locks the lesser tuberosity or creates a reverse Hill-Sachs impaction against the posterior glenoid rim. Forcing external rotation risks fracturing the humeral neck. Rotating the patient 45-60 degrees into an anterior oblique (Scapular Y lateral) keeps the arm safely in a sling while clearly demonstrating whether the humeral head sits posterior (behind) or anterior (subcoracoid) to the glenoid Y intersection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01787","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Contrast Media and Special Procedures","topic":"Esophageal Perforation Contrast Selection","q":"A patient presents with severe retrosternal pain and subcutaneous emphysema after forceful vomiting (suspected Boerhaave esophageal perforation) and is ordered an emergency esophagogram. Which contrast agent is initially indicated, and why is barium sulfate avoided first?","o":["Thin barium sulfate suspension first because it seals esophageal tears rapidly","Oil-based Lipiodol by mouth","Hyperosmolar ionic Gastrografin deliberately into the tracheobronchial tree in suspected tracheoesophageal fistula","Water-soluble iodinated contrast (ideally low-osmolar non-ionic iso-osmolar agent if aspiration risk exists) first, because extravasated barium sulfate causes severe chemical/granulomatous mediastinitis"],"a":3,"e":"When esophageal or bowel perforation is suspected, barium sulfate is contraindicated initially because non-absorbable barium leaking into the mediastinum or peritoneum provokes severe fibrosing mediastinitis/peritonitis. Water-soluble iodinated contrast is rapidly absorbed if extravasation occurs (using low-osmolar non-ionic contrast if aspiration is also a concern, since hyperosmolar Gastrografin causes pulmonary edema if aspirated).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01788","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiation Biology and Protection","topic":"Law of Bergonie and Tribondeau","q":"According to the Law of Bergonie and Tribondeau in diagnostic radiation biology, which biological characteristics make cells/tissues MOST radiosensitive to ionizing radiation?","o":["High mitotic activity, long actively dividing future, and undifferentiated/immature cell morphology (e.g., lymphocytes, bone marrow stem cells, spermatogonia, intestinal crypts)","Calcified cortical bone osteocytes","Cells in G0 resting phase with zero DNA replication","Completely post-mitotic, highly specialized mature cells (e.g., adult cortical neurons and skeletal muscle fibers)"],"a":0,"e":"Formulated in 1906, the Law of Bergonie and Tribondeau states that cellular radiosensitivity is directly proportional to reproductive/mitotic rate and inversely proportional to degree of differentiation. Consequently, embryonic/fetal tissues, hematopoietic stem cells, lymphocytes, intestinal epithelium, and gonadal germ cells are highly radiosensitive, whereas mature neurons and muscle are radioresistant.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01789","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Physics and Equipment","topic":"Half-Value Layer (HVL) and Total Filtration","q":"Why do AERB / ICRP diagnostic radiology equipment standards require a minimum total filtration of 2.5 mm aluminum (Al) equivalent for general-purpose X-ray tubes operating above 70 kVp, and what does Half-Value Layer (HVL) measure?","o":["Filtration removes useless low-energy (\u0027soft\u0027) X-ray photons that would otherwise be absorbed in the patient\u0027s skin without reaching the detector; HVL measures X-ray beam quality/penetrability","Filtration doubles entrance skin dose to sharpen bone edges; HVL measures tube filament current (mA)","Filtration blocks all photons above 100 keV; HVL measures darkroom safelight brightness","Filtration eliminates the need for lead aprons; HVL measures patient body mass"],"a":0,"e":"Low-energy X-ray photons (\u003c20 keV) cannot penetrate the patient to contribute to the image and merely deposit unnecessary radiation dose in superficial skin/tissue. Adding aluminum filtration (total \u003e=2.5 mm Al above 70 kVp) hardens the beam and reduces skin dose. Half-Value Layer (HVL)—the thickness of aluminum reducing beam intensity by 50%—is the standard QC metric of beam quality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01790","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning","topic":"Acute Hip Fracture Lateral Projection","q":"A 78-year-old woman arrives from the emergency room with a shortened, externally rotated right leg after a fall, and the AP pelvis X-ray is suspicious for an acute femoral neck fracture. Which lateral hip projection is indicated, and why is the routine frog-leg (modified Cleaves) lateral strictly AVOIDED?","o":["Perform a horizontal-beam cross-table (Danelius-Miller) lateral projection with the unaffected hip flexed/elevated out of the beam; frog-leg abduction/external rotation risks displacing a non-displaced fracture or tearing retinacular blood supply","Perform a bilateral frog-leg lateral by forcefully abducting both thighs 45 degrees","Stand the patient upright for a weight-bearing lateral hip view","Roll the patient directly onto the painful fractured right hip for a prone oblique"],"a":0,"e":"In acute hip trauma, forceful abduction and external rotation required for a frog-leg lateral can convert an impacted/non-displaced femoral neck fracture into a displaced fracture, jeopardizing femoral head vascularity (avascular necrosis) or causing severe pain. The cross-table (Danelius-Miller) lateral images the injured hip in situ with the grid cassette vertical at the iliac crest and the uninjured leg raised out of the field.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01791","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Image Quality and Geometric Factors","topic":"Geometric Unsharpness (Penumbra)","q":"Which combination of geometric factors minimizes geometric unsharpness (penumbra) and produces the sharpest trabecular bone detail on an extremity radiograph?","o":["Maximum tube angulation of 45 degrees with large OID","Small effective focal spot size, minimum Object-to-Image Distance (OID), and maximum practical Source-to-Image Distance (SID)","Removing the cassette far below the tabletop while shortening SID","Large focal spot size, large OID (air gap), and very short 50 cm SID"],"a":1,"e":"Geometric unsharpness (penumbra) obeys Ug = (Focal spot size x OID) / SOD (where SOD = SID - OID). Therefore, sharpness is maximized when the focal spot is small (e.g., 0.6 mm fine focus), the body part lies directly against the image receptor (minimal OID), and SID is adequately large (e.g., 100-180 cm).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01792","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Pediatric Radiography","topic":"Eliminating Involuntary Motion Blur","q":"When taking an AP chest radiograph of an agitated, crying 10-month-old infant, which exposure adjustment best prevents motion unsharpness while keeping mAs constant?","o":["Use the lowest mA station (25 mA) with a long 0.5-second exposure time","Decrease kVp to 40 kVp and triple the exposure duration","Select the highest available mA station paired with the shortest possible exposure time (e.g., \u003c5-10 ms), use a Pigg-O-Stat or sandbag/velcro immobilizer, and expose at peak inspiration","Ask the unshielded parent to hold the infant inside the primary X-ray beam without a lead apron"],"a":2,"e":"In infants and young children who cannot hold their breath, rapid respiration and crying cause severe motion blur—the #1 cause of pediatric repeat exposures. Selecting the highest mA allows the shortest millisecond exposure time to \u0027freeze\u0027 cardiac/respiratory motion for the same total mAs, combined with safe mechanical immobilization and lead-apron/thyroid shielding if any caregiver assists.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01793","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Special Radiographic Procedures","topic":"Hysterosalpingography (HSG) Scheduling","q":"During which phase of a woman\u0027s regular 28-day menstrual cycle should a Hysterosalpingography (HSG) for infertility tubal-patency evaluation be scheduled, and why?","o":["Immediately after unprotected intercourse on day 14 without pregnancy exclusion","During active heavy menstrual bleeding (days 1-3) to flush the tubes","On day 24 of the luteal phase right before next expected menstruation","Between days 7 and 10 after the onset of menstruation (follicular phase after bleeding stops and before ovulation), avoiding early pregnancy irradiation and venous intravasation"],"a":3,"e":"Scheduling HSG during days 7-10 of a 28-day cycle (after menstrual flow has completely ceased and before ovulation, following the \u002710-day rule\u0027 with abstinence or negative urine beta-hCG) ensures the patient is not pregnant, prevents retrograde flushing of menstrual endometrium/clots into the peritoneal cavity, and avoids contrast intravasation into raw bleeding vessels.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01794","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiation Physics \u0026 Protection","topic":"Occupational Dose Limits","q":"As per the Atomic Energy Regulatory Board (AERB) Safety Code for Medical Diagnostic X-Ray Equipment in India, what is the permissible annual occupational effective dose limit averaged over 5 consecutive years, and where should a single chest TLD badge be worn when using a lead apron?","o":["20 mSv/year (max 30 mSv in any single year); TLD worn at chest level underneath the lead apron","5 mSv/year (max 15 mSv in any single year); TLD worn on the wrist underneath the lead gloves","50 mSv/year (max 100 mSv in any single year); TLD worn outside the lead apron over the shoulder","1 mSv/year (max 5 mSv in any single year); TLD worn at waist level outside the lead apron"],"a":0,"e":"AERB prescribes an occupational effective dose limit of 20 mSv/year averaged over 5 consecutive years, not exceeding 30 mSv in any single year. For routine diagnostic radiography with a single TLD badge, it is worn at chest level underneath the lead apron to estimate whole-body shielded trunk dose (a second collar badge outside the apron is added in high-dose fluoroscopy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01795","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiation Physics \u0026 Protection","topic":"Inverse Square Law","q":"During portable bedside radiography, the measured scatter exposure rate at 100 cm from the patient is 48 mR/hour. If the radiographer steps back to a distance of 200 cm from the source without changing exposure factors, what will the exposure rate be?","o":["6 mR/hour","12 mR/hour","24 mR/hour","96 mR/hour"],"a":1,"e":"By the inverse square law, I1 / I2 = (d2 / d1)^2. Doubling the distance from 100 cm to 200 cm reduces intensity by a factor of 2^2 = 4. Thus, I2 = 48 / 4 = 12 mR/hour, illustrating why distance is one of the most effective radiation protection measures.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01796","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning \u0026 Trauma Imaging","topic":"Cervical Spine Trauma Protocol","q":"An unconscious 28-year-old motorcyclist arrives on a spine board with a rigid cervical collar in place after a high-speed collision. The trauma team orders cervical spine X-rays in the resuscitation bay. Which projection must the radiographer acquire FIRST without removing the collar or moving the neck?","o":["Lateral flexion and extension dynamic views with collar removed","Swimmer lateral projection as the sole initial view before supine AP","Horizontal-beam cross-table lateral projection visualizing C1 through the C7-T1 junction","Erect AP axial oblique projections with 45-degree body rotation"],"a":2,"e":"In suspected cervical spine trauma, a horizontal-beam cross-table lateral radiograph is always obtained first while maintaining full cervical immobilization. Complete visualization from C1 down to the C7-T1 intervertebral disc space is mandatory before clearing or repositioning the patient.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01797","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning \u0026 Anatomy","topic":"PA Chest Projection Criteria","q":"At an OSPE station reviewing an adult PA chest radiograph, which set of findings confirms technically optimal positioning and full inspiration?","o":["180 cm SID, medial clavicular ends equidistant from spinous processes, scapulae rotated laterally out of lung fields, 10 posterior ribs visible above diaphragm","100 cm SID, medial clavicles overlapping the spine asymmetrically, scapulae projected over apices, 6 anterior ribs visible above diaphragm","180 cm SID, hands placed behind the head, 7 posterior ribs visible above diaphragm, lordotic clavicular angulation","120 cm SID, chin depressed over upper lung fields, elbows tucked tightly against ribs, 8 posterior ribs visible"],"a":0,"e":"A standard PA chest radiograph uses 180 cm (72 inches) SID to minimize cardiac magnification, rolling shoulders forward (hands on hips, elbows forward) to clear scapulae from the lung fields, zero rotation (equidistant sternoclavicular joints relative to spinous process), and full inspiration demonstrating at least 10 posterior (or 6 anterior) ribs above the diaphragm.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01798","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiation Physics \u0026 Protection","topic":"15% kVp Rule","q":"An abdominal radiograph technique uses 70 kVp and 40 mAs. To reduce patient entrance skin dose while maintaining equivalent receptor exposure in a digital system, the radiographer increases kVp by 15% (to ~81 kVp). What adjustment should be made to mAs, and what happens to subject contrast?","o":["Double mAs to 80 mAs; subject contrast increases sharply due to photoelectric absorption","Keep mAs at 40 mAs; subject contrast remains completely unchanged","Quarter mAs to 10 mAs; spatial resolution increases due to reduced focal spot blur","Halve mAs to 20 mAs; subject contrast decreases slightly due to higher beam penetration"],"a":3,"e":"According to the 15% rule, increasing kVp by 15% roughly doubles the X-ray beam intensity reaching the image receptor, equivalent to doubling mAs. Therefore, mAs must be halved (from 40 to 20 mAs) to keep exposure index constant, substantially lowering patient dose while slightly reducing subject contrast (more Compton scatter relative to photoelectric effect).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01799","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Equipment \u0026 Image Quality","topic":"Anode Heel Effect","q":"When performing an AP thoracic spine radiograph on a patient with a markedly thicker lower thorax and upper abdomen compared to the upper cervical-thoracic region, how should the patient be oriented relative to the X-ray tube axis to compensate for unequal tissue attenuation?","o":["Position the thicker lower thoracic/caudal end under the cathode side and the thinner upper thoracic end under the anode side","Position the thicker lower thoracic end under the anode side and the thinner upper end under the cathode side","Rotate the tube 90 degrees so the anode-cathode axis runs transversely across the shoulders","Use a shorter SID of 60 cm with a narrow collimator perpendicular to the anode heel"],"a":0,"e":"Due to the anode heel effect, X-ray beam intensity is greater on the cathode side (up to ~120%) and lower on the anode side (~75%) because photons emitted toward the anode must traverse more target material. Placing the thicker lower thoracic region under the higher-intensity cathode side balances image receptor exposure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01800","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Contrast Media \u0026 Special Procedures","topic":"Acute Anaphylactoid Contrast Reaction","q":"Two minutes after intravenous injection of non-ionic iodinated contrast for a CT abdomen, a 45-year-old patient develops facial flushing, inspiratory stridor, diffuse wheezing, and BP drops to 76/48 mmHg. What is the immediate priority pharmacological intervention once the infusion is stopped and emergency code is called?","o":["Intravenous Chlorpheniramine alone while completing the contrast scan rapidly","Intramuscular Epinephrine 1:1000 (0.3 to 0.5 mg) into the anterolateral thigh, high-flow oxygen, and IV 0.9% saline bolus","Oral Cetirizine 10 mg with head-up 60-degree sitting posture","Intravenous Furosemide 40 mg to accelerate renal clearance of the contrast medium"],"a":1,"e":"Stridor, bronchospasm, and hypotension indicate severe anaphylaxis. Immediately stop contrast, maintain airway/oxygen, elevate legs if tolerated, give rapid IV normal saline, and administer IM Epinephrine 1:1000 (0.3–0.5 mg) into the mid-anterolateral thigh as the definitive life-saving first-line drug.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01801","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiation Physics \u0026 Protection","topic":"Protective Aprons \u0026 Pregnancy Screening","q":"Under AERB radiation safety norms for medical diagnostic X-ray facilities, what minimum lead equivalence is specified for protective aprons used in routine radiography versus fluoroscopy/interventional procedures, and what mandatory check precedes abdominal/pelvic radiography in women of reproductive age?","o":["0.05 mm Pb minimum for all procedures; proceed without LMP inquiry if kVp is below 80","0.10 mm Pb minimum for fluoroscopy; avoid lead aprons entirely during mobile ward X-rays","0.25 mm Pb minimum for routine radiography (0.35–0.5 mm Pb for fluoroscopy); verify Last Menstrual Period (LMP) and pregnancy status before exposure","2.0 mm Pb minimum for routine portable aprons; perform routine beta-hCG blood test on every female patient regardless of age"],"a":2,"e":"AERB stipulates a minimum lead equivalence of 0.25 mm Pb for general diagnostic radiography aprons and 0.35–0.5 mm Pb for higher-dose fluoroscopy/interventional suites. Before irradiating the abdomen or pelvis of any woman of childbearing age, staff must verify LMP and screen for possible pregnancy to avoid unintended fetal exposure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01802","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning","topic":"AP Axial Lordotic Chest Projection","q":"On a routine PA chest radiograph, a suspicious nodular opacity is partially obscured behind the right clavicle and first rib. How should the radiographer position the patient (or angle the X-ray tube) for an AP Axial (Apical Lordotic) chest projection to project the clavicles above the lung apices?","o":["Place the patient in a left lateral decubitus position","Perform a PA expiration chest view with chin tucked tightly onto the sternum","Angle the central ray 30 degrees caudad while keeping the patient leaning forward","Place the patient standing ~30 cm in front of the upright bucky leaning backward so shoulders/upper back rest against the grid (15-20 degree lordotic tilt) with a horizontal central ray at midsternum—or supine with 15-20 degree cephalad tube angle"],"a":3,"e":"In the AP Axial (Lindblom / Lordotic) chest projection, leaning the patient backward (15-20 deg) or angling the beam 15-20 degrees cephalad in a supine patient projects the clavicles superiorly above the pulmonary apices and first ribs, unmasking apical tuberculosis, Pancoast tumors, and right middle lobe atelectasis/interlobar effusions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01803","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Equipment and Exposure Control","topic":"AEC Phototimer Sensor Selection","q":"When performing a standard upright PA chest radiograph using Automatic Exposure Control (AEC), which ionization chamber(s) should be activated, and what happens if the radiographer accidentally leaves ONLY the center chamber active?","o":["Activate the two lateral (outer) chambers positioned over the aerated lungs; activating only the center chamber over the dense mediastinum/thoracic spine causes overexposure of the lungs","Activate only the center chamber over the spine; activating outer chambers causes mediastinal burnout","AEC works identically regardless of whether the patient stands centered over the detector or 20 cm off-center","Activate all three chambers for an AP lateral finger radiograph"],"a":0,"e":"AEC terminates X-ray exposure once a preset radiation dose reaches the selected ionization chamber(s). For a PA chest radiograph, the two upper outer chambers lie directly behind aerated lung parenchyma (the organ of primary interest); selecting only the dense central thoracic spine/mediastinum chamber delays termination, overexposing the lungs and inflating patient dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01804","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Special Radiographic Procedures","topic":"Seldinger Technique","q":"What is the correct sequential order of steps in the classic Seldinger technique for percutaneous femoral arterial angiography access?","o":["Advance catheter directly through skin without needle -\u003e inject contrast -\u003e insert guidewire last","Needle puncture of vessel -\u003e advance flexible guidewire through needle -\u003e withdraw needle while holding wire -\u003e advance vascular sheath/catheter over guidewire -\u003e withdraw guidewire","Cut down surgically on artery -\u003e insert rigid metal trocar -\u003e remove artery","Inject 100 mL barium into femoral vein -\u003e insert wire through vein"],"a":1,"e":"Described by Sven Ivar Seldinger in 1953, percutaneous vascular catheterization replaces hazardous surgical cutdown: (1) puncture vessel with bevel needle until pulsatile blood return, (2) thread soft-tipped guidewire into lumen, (3) remove needle while maintaining wire control/compression, (4) slide dilator/sheath/angiographic catheter over the wire into the vessel, and (5) remove wire leaving catheter in place.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01805","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning","topic":"Cervical vs Lumbar Oblique Projections","q":"Which anatomical structures are specifically demonstrated in a 45-degree oblique Cervical Spine projection versus a 45-degree oblique Lumbar Spine projection?","o":["Oblique Cervical Spine demonstrates the intervertebral (neural) foramina and pedicles; Oblique Lumbar Spine demonstrates the \u0027Scottie dog\u0027 appearance of the zygapophyseal joints and pars interarticularis (spondylolysis)","Oblique Cervical Spine shows the odontoid dens; Oblique Lumbar Spine shows the sacrum only","Both demonstrate only the spinous processes in profile","Oblique Cervical Spine requires 0-degree rotation and 45-degree lateral tilt"],"a":0,"e":"Because cervical intervertebral foramina project anterolaterally at ~45 degrees, a 45-degree oblique cervical radiograph (with 15 deg cephalad ray for AP oblique / 15 deg caudad for PA oblique) opens the neural foramina clearly to assess nerve root impingement. In the lumbar spine, 45-degree obliques profile the zygapophyseal (facet) joints and \u0027Scottie dog\u0027 pars interarticularis (neck of dog) to detect spondylolysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01806","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiation Protection and Safety","topic":"AERB / ICRP Fetal Dose Limits","q":"According to AERB / ICRP radiation protection regulations, once a pregnant female radiographer formally declares her pregnancy to the Radiological Safety Officer (RSO), what is the maximum equivalent dose limit to the embryo/fetus for the remainder of the pregnancy, and where is the dedicated fetal TLD badge worn?","o":["0.01 mSv lifetime limit; TLD worn on the shoe","50 mSv per trimester; pregnancy automatically requires permanent termination from radiography","1 mSv for the remainder of the pregnancy (analogous to public limit); fetal TLD worn at waist/abdomen level UNDERNEATH the lead apron","20 mSv per month; fetal TLD worn outside the lead apron on the forehead"],"a":2,"e":"AERB and ICRP specify that once pregnancy is declared, working conditions must ensure the fetus receives no more than 1 mSv equivalent dose over the remainder of the pregnancy (protecting the unborn child similarly to a member of the general public). A second fetal dosimeter badge is worn at the maternal abdomen/waist underneath the protective lead apron to monitor fetal exposure directly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01807","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Computed Tomography Image Display","topic":"Brain vs Bone Window Settings","q":"When displaying a non-contrast head CT to detect subtle early acute ischemic stroke (loss of grey-white matter differentiation) versus a linear skull fracture, how must the CT Window Width (WW) and Window Level (WL) be adjusted?","o":["Set Window Width to 10 HU and Window Level to +1000 HU for grey-white matter","Use a lung window (WW 1500 HU, WL -600 HU) to detect subarachnoid hemorrhage","Use a single wide bone window (WW 3000 HU) to evaluate subtle cerebral cortex edema","Use a NARROW brain window (WW ~70-100 HU, WL ~35-40 HU; or narrower \u0027stroke window\u0027 WW ~30-40 HU) for parenchyma, and switch to a WIDE bone window (WW ~2000-4000 HU, WL ~400-700 HU) for skull fractures"],"a":3,"e":"Window Width (WW) controls grayscale contrast (narrower WW = higher contrast between similar densities), while Window Level (WL) centers the display on the target tissue\u0027s average Hounsfield Unit. Because gray matter (~35-45 HU) and white matter (~25-32 HU) differ by only ~10 HU, a narrow brain/stroke window centered at ~35-40 HU maximizes contrast, whereas cortical bone (+1000 HU) requires a wide high-level bone window to avoid solid white saturation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01808","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Physics","topic":"Photoelectric Absorption vs Compton Scatter","q":"In diagnostic radiography (30-140 kVp), how do Photoelectric Absorption and Compton Scattering differ in their effect on radiographic subject contrast and occupational scatter dose?","o":["Photoelectric absorption depends strongly on tissue atomic number (Z^3) and produces high subject contrast between bone/iodine and soft tissue; Compton scatter depends primarily on mass density (independent of Z), degrades image contrast via fog, and is the main source of occupational scatter to staff","Compton scatter produces sharp high-contrast bone edges without fog; Photoelectric absorption causes all occupational scatter","Photoelectric absorption occurs only above 1.022 MeV (pair production threshold)","Both interactions occur exclusively inside the X-ray tube glass envelope"],"a":0,"e":"Photoelectric absorption completely removes incident photons in inner-shell interactions proportional to Z^3/E^3, differentiating high-Z bone (Z ~13.8) or iodinated/barium contrast (Z = 53/56) sharply from soft tissue (Z ~7.4). Compton scattering deflects outer-shell photons in all directions with reduced energy—degrading contrast at the receptor and scattering outward from the patient to irradiate fluoroscopy personnel.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01809","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Special Radiographic Procedures","topic":"Intravenous Urography (IVU) Sequence","q":"During a standard Intravenous Urography (IVU) study using non-ionic water-soluble iodinated contrast after reviewing normal baseline KUB and eGFR, what is the purpose and timing of the immediate 1-to-3-minute film versus the 5-to-15-minute films?","o":["The 1-to-3-minute film demonstrates the nephrogram phase (renal parenchymal enhancement and symmetry/size), whereas 5-to-15-minute films demonstrate the pyelogram/excretory phase (calyces, renal pelvis, and ureters)","The 1-minute film shows only the urinary bladder full of contrast","The 15-minute film demonstrates the arterial abdominal aorta exclusively","Both films are taken 24 hours before injecting IV contrast"],"a":0,"e":"Immediately following bolus IV iodinated contrast injection, glomerular filtration concentrates contrast inside renal cortical tubules (1-3 minute nephrogram phase, best for renal contours and asymmetric delayed uptake in obstruction/renovascular disease). Over 5-15 minutes, contrast drains into the collecting system (pyelogram phase) delineating calyceal blunting, pelviureteric junction obstruction, or ureteric calculi, followed by full-bladder and post-void films.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01810","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning","topic":"Tangential Patella (Sunrise / Merchant) View","q":"Which radiographic projection places the X-ray beam tangentially through the patellofemoral joint space to demonstrate vertical/longitudinal patellar fractures and lateral patellar subluxation without femoral condyle superimposition?","o":["Tangential (\u0027Sunrise\u0027 / Skyline / Merchant) projection with the knee flexed ~40-45 degrees (or Settegast prone flexion)","Lateral lumbar spine projection","AP pelvis frog-leg view","Routine AP weight-bearing knee with 0-degree central ray"],"a":0,"e":"On standard AP knee films, the patella superimposes directly over the distal femur, hiding subtle vertical fractures and patellofemoral tracking abnormalities. The tangential Skyline/Merchant/Sunrise projection directs the central ray parallel to the patellar articular facets with controlled knee flexion (~40-45 deg in Merchant to prevent reducing subluxation), opening the patellofemoral joint space cleanly (avoiding forced deep flexion in acute comminuted trauma).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01811","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Computed Tomography Dosimetry","topic":"CTDIvol and Dose-Length Product (DLP)","q":"A spiral chest CT scan uses a Volume CT Dose Index (CTDIvol) of 8.0 mGy across an anatomical scan length (z-axis coverage) of 35 cm. What is the Dose-Length Product (DLP) for this acquisition, and how does needlessly extending the scan range into the pelvis affect DLP?","o":["DLP = 800 mGy*cm","DLP = 280 mGy*cm (8.0 x 35); unnecessarily extending scan length increases DLP and effective patient dose proportionally","DLP = 4.37 mGy*cm; increasing scan length decreases DLP","DLP = 0.23 mGy/cm; scan length has zero impact on total patient radiation"],"a":1,"e":"Dose-Length Product (DLP, in mGy*cm) quantifies total radiation output delivered over the entire scanned z-axis volume: DLP = CTDIvol (mGy) x Scan Length (cm) = 8.0 x 35 = 280 mGy*cm (which can be multiplied by region-specific k-conversion factors to estimate Effective Dose in mSv). Avoiding unnecessary scan \u0027overscan\u0027 beyond clinical boundaries directly minimizes DLP.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01812","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"MRI Physics and Artifacts","topic":"Chemical Shift Spatial Misregistration","q":"On a 3.0 Tesla T2-weighted abdominal MRI, the technologist observes a bright crescentic rim on one border of the kidney and a dark shadow rim on the opposite border at the renal cortex-perinephric fat interface along the frequency-encoding axis. What artifact is this, and which parameter adjustment reduces it?","o":["Patient respiratory ghosting; double the slice thickness to 20 mm","Aliasing (wrap-around) artifact; reduce by shrinking the Field of View (FOV) further","Chemical shift artifact (Type 1 spatial misregistration from the ~3.5 ppm Larmor frequency difference between fat and water protons); reduce it by INCREASING receiver bandwidth (or using fat suppression)","RF zipper artifact from an open MRI door; increase echo time TE to 500 ms"],"a":2,"e":"Fat protons precess ~3.5 ppm (~440 Hz at 3.0 T; ~220 Hz at 1.5 T) slower than water protons. Because spatial position along the frequency-encoding (readout) axis is mapped strictly by precession frequency, fat pixels shift slightly relative to water pixels, leaving a dark void on one side and bright overlapping signal on the opposite side of fat-water boundaries. Widening receiver bandwidth increases Hz/pixel, shrinking the pixel shift.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01813","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Specialized Imaging Modalities (Mammography)","topic":"Mammography Compression \u0026 Target/Filter","q":"Why is firm, uniform breast compression combined with low-kVp (25–32 kVp) characteristic X-ray spectra (e.g., Molybdenum/Rhodium target-filter) essential in diagnostic mammography?","o":["Compression increases breast thickness to amplify Compton scatter and requires 120 kVp for high penetration","Low kVp prevents photoelectric absorption so only dense bone absorbs the beam","Compression eliminates the need for an image receptor grid and permits 5-second exposures","Compression reduces tissue thickness, scatter radiation, motion unsharpness, and mean glandular dose while low kVp enhances photoelectric contrast between microcalcifications, fat, and fibroglandular tissue"],"a":3,"e":"Vigorous breast compression creates uniform thickness, lowers object-to-image distance (improving spatial resolution), immobilizes the breast, reduces scatter, and decreases mean glandular dose. Operating at 25–32 kVp with Mo/Mo, Mo/Rh, or W/Rh target-filter pairs maximizes differential photoelectric absorption across tissues of similar atomic number.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01814","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Digital Radiography \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"CR Imaging Plate Erasure \u0026 Double Exposure","q":"A Computed Radiography (CR) knee radiograph demonstrates a faint superimposed outline of a lumbar spine from an earlier examination alongside the current knee anatomy. What caused this ghosting artifact, and how is it prevented?","o":["Incomplete erasure of the CR photostimulable phosphor (PSP) imaging plate prior to reuse; perform a full primary erasure cycle (and erase plates unused for \u003e 48 hours before exposure)","Static electricity discharge inside a wet chemical processor","Using an automatic exposure control (AEC) chamber at 180 cm SID","Excessive kVp causing anode blooming on the collimator blades"],"a":0,"e":"CR photostimulable phosphor plates retain trapped electrons if the reader erasure lamp fails or an erasure cycle is bypassed, causing a residual latent (\"ghost\" or double-exposure) image to superimpose on the next study. Plates must undergo complete bright-light erasure after reading and pre-exposure erasure if stored unused for \u003e24–48 hours (to clear background scatter/cosmic radiation fog).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01815","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning \u0026 Trauma Imaging","topic":"Pneumoperitoneum in Non-Ambulatory Patients","q":"A 52-year-old man with severe epigastric pain and hypotension from a suspected perforated duodenal ulcer is too unstable to sit or stand upright for an erect chest radiograph. Which abdominal projection should the radiographer perform to demonstrate small amounts of free intraperitoneal air?","o":["Right lateral decubitus (right side down) immediately without waiting, projecting air over the gastric fundus","Left lateral decubitus (left side down, horizontal X-ray beam) after keeping the patient in that position for 10–15 minutes so free air rises over the non-dependent right lateral liver margin","Supine AP pelvis angled 30 degrees caudad","Prone PA abdomen with vertical X-ray beam centered at L4"],"a":1,"e":"When an erect PA chest radiograph is unsafe, a left lateral decubitus abdominal view (left side down, right side up) using a horizontal beam allows free peritoneal gas to ascend between the homogeneous right lobe of the liver and the right lateral abdominal wall—providing sharp contrast unlike the gas-filled stomach/splenic flexure on the left.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01816","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning \u0026 Anatomy","topic":"Parietoacanthial (Waters) Projection","q":"At an OSPE station for paranasal sinus imaging, how is the patient’s head positioned for a PA Parietoacanthial (Waters) projection, and where must the petrous ridges appear on a technically acceptable radiograph?","o":["Patient erect facing bucky with chin extended so Orbitomeatal Line (OML) forms a 37-degree angle to the image receptor (MML perpendicular); petrous ridges projected immediately inferior to the floors of the maxillary sinuses","OML perpendicular (0 degrees) to image receptor; petrous ridges filling the lower third of the orbits","Patient supine with Infraorbitomeatal Line parallel to the floor; petrous ridges superimposed over the maxillary antra","Head rotated 45 degrees laterally; petrous ridges projected above the frontal sinuses"],"a":0,"e":"In the erect Waters (parietoacanthial) sinus view—erect posture is mandatory to demonstrate air-fluid levels—the chin touches the bucky so the Mentomeatal Line (MML) is perpendicular and the OML is angled 37° to the IR. This drops the dense petrous pyramids cleanly below the maxillary sinus floors (opening mouth further visualizes the sphenoid sinus).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01817","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiation Physics \u0026 Protection","topic":"Total Permanent Tube Filtration","q":"Under AERB/ICRP safety standards for medical diagnostic X-ray tubes operating above 70 kVp, what is the minimum required total filtration (inherent plus added) in millimeters of Aluminum equivalent, and why is it mandatory?","o":["2.5 mm Al equivalent; selectively absorbs low-energy (\"soft\") X-ray photons that would otherwise be absorbed in superficial skin without contributing to the diagnostic image","0.25 mm Al equivalent; increases soft X-rays to darken bone structures","10.0 mm Lead equivalent; blocks all characteristic radiation above 70 kVp","0.5 mm Copper equivalent; eliminates Compton scatter inside the patient"],"a":0,"e":"Diagnostic X-ray tubes operating \u003e70 kVp require at least 2.5 mm Al total filtration (inherent tube glass/oil filtration plus added aluminum plate). Aluminum preferentially attenuates low-energy bremsstrahlung photons (\u003c20 keV) that cannot penetrate the patient to reach the detector, thereby lowering entrance skin dose while hardening the beam.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01818","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Contrast Media \u0026 Special Procedures","topic":"Metformin \u0026 Renal Function Screening","q":"A 63-year-old diabetic man taking oral Metformin is scheduled for an elective contrast-enhanced CT abdomen. His latest lab report shows serum creatinine 2.4 mg/dL (eGFR 26 mL/min/1.73 m²). What precaution must the radiology team take regarding Metformin and IV iodinated contrast?","o":["Withhold water intake for 24 hours before the scan to concentrate contrast in the kidneys","Proceed with high-osmolar ionic contrast immediately because Metformin prevents nephrotoxicity","Alert the radiologist before injecting contrast; if IV contrast proceeds in a patient with eGFR \u003c 30 mL/min/1.73 m² (or AKI), withhold Metformin at the time of contrast and for 48 hours afterward, hydrate appropriately, and recheck renal function before restarting Metformin","Double the Metformin dose just before the scan to prevent contrast-induced hyperglycemia"],"a":2,"e":"Although metformin itself is not nephrotoxic, if contrast-associated acute kidney injury occurs in patients with severe CKD (eGFR \u003c30 mL/min/1.73 m²), metformin clearance drops and life-threatening lactic acidosis can develop. Radiology protocol requires flagging eGFR \u003c30/AKI to the radiologist, ensuring IV hydration/non-contrast alternatives if feasible, holding metformin for 48 hours post-contrast, and confirming stable creatinine before resumption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01819","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Specialized Imaging Modalities (CT Scan)","topic":"Hounsfield Units (CT Numbers) \u0026 Helical Pitch","q":"On a standard non-contrast CT brain scan, what are the approximate Hounsfield Unit (HU) values for (1) Water/CSF, (2) Air, and (3) Acute intracranial hemorrhage, and how does increasing helical pitch from 1.0 to 1.5 (with fixed mAs) affect patient radiation dose?","o":["Water -500 HU, Air -50 HU, Acute blood +1000 HU; pitch has zero effect on radiation dose","Water +1000 HU, Air 0 HU, Acute blood -100 HU; increasing pitch to 1.5 doubles patient radiation dose","Water 0 HU, Air +500 HU, Acute blood -80 HU; increasing pitch reduces slice thickness to 0.1 mm","Water ~0 HU, Air -1000 HU, Acute blood +50 to +80 HU; increasing pitch to 1.5 decreases patient radiation dose"],"a":3,"e":"The Hounsfield scale calibrates pure water at 0 HU and air at -1000 HU; acute clotted blood appears hyperdense (+50 to +80 HU), fat is -50 to -100 HU, and cortical bone is ~+1000 HU. Helical pitch = table travel per 360° rotation / total collimated beam width; raising pitch \u003e 1 stretches the helix, scanning faster and reducing radiation dose (assuming constant tube current).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01820","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning","topic":"Swimmer\u0027s Lateral (Twining) Projection","q":"On a routine lateral cervical spine trauma radiograph, the dense humeral heads and shoulders completely obscure the C7-T1 intervertebral junction. How must the radiographer position the patient\u0027s arms for a Swimmer\u0027s (Twining) lateral cervicothoracic projection?","o":["Raise the arm/shoulder closest to the image receptor straight overhead (180 degrees flexion) and depress the opposite shoulder downward and slightly anteriorly, directing the horizontal central ray through C7-T1","Cross both arms tightly in front of the sternum at shoulder height","Shrug both shoulders maximally upward toward the ears","Rotate the entire spine into a supine AP lordotic position"],"a":0,"e":"In a standard lateral C-spine film, superimposition of both muscular shoulders blocks visualization of C7 and T1 (a notorious site of missed cervical spine fractures!). Separating the shoulders vertically—raising the IR-side arm fully overhead and pulling the remote shoulder downward/slightly forward (with 3-5 deg caudad beam angle if needed)—separates the humeral shadows and profiles C7-T1 clearly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01821","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Physics and Equipment","topic":"Anode Heat Units (HU) Calculation","q":"A radiographer performs an exposure using 100 kVp, 200 mA, and 0.5 seconds (100 mAs) on a modern three-phase 12-pulse / high-frequency X-ray generator (waveform factor = 1.4). Using the formula HU = kVp x mAs x waveform factor, how many Heat Units (HU) are deposited in the X-ray tube anode?","o":["14,000 HU","10,000 HU","7,000 HU","28,000 HU"],"a":0,"e":"Heat Units (HU) measure thermal energy deposited at the anode target: HU = kVp x mA x seconds x generator constant (1.0 for single-phase, 1.35 for 3-phase 6-pulse, 1.4 or 1.41 for 3-phase 12-pulse / high-frequency). Here: 100 kVp x 100 mAs x 1.4 = 14,000 HU. Exceeding single-exposure or anode-housing cooling charts cracks or pits the tungsten-rhenium target.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01822","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Special Radiographic Procedures","topic":"Postoperative T-Tube Cholangiography","q":"During a postoperative T-tube cholangiogram (~7-10 days after common bile duct exploration) to confirm biliary patency prior to T-tube removal, why is meticulous elimination of all air bubbles from the contrast syringe and connecting tubing mandatory?","o":["Air bubbles dissolve stainless steel surgical clips","Air bubbles injected into the common bile duct appear as round radiolucent filling defects on fluoroscopy that closely mimic retained radiolucent gallstones","Air bubbles lower the patient\u0027s serum bilirubin immediately","Air bubbles permanently solidify iodinated contrast into concrete"],"a":1,"e":"When injecting dilute water-soluble iodinated contrast slowly under low gravity/syringe pressure (avoiding forceful injection that triggers cholangitic sepsis!), any trapped air bubble forms a discrete round filling defect inside the contrast column—indistinguishable at first glance from a residual common bile duct calculus unless distinguished by gravity movement on tilting. Flushing tubing bubble-free prevents false-positive re-exploration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01823","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiographic Positioning","topic":"AP Mortise Ankle Projection","q":"To obtain a true AP Mortise projection of the ankle joint that opens the entire medial, superior, and lateral tibiotalar joint spaces free of distal fibular superimposition, how must the patient\u0027s leg and foot be rotated?","o":["Externally (laterally) rotate the foot 45 degrees","Plantarflex the toes downward 60 degrees with 0-degree rotation","Internally (medially) rotate the entire extended lower leg and foot 15 to 20 degrees until the intermalleolar line lies parallel to the image receptor","Flex the knee 90 degrees in a lateral decubitus position"],"a":2,"e":"Because the lateral malleolus lies ~15-20 degrees posterior to the medial malleolus anatomically, a straight 0-degree AP ankle superimposes the distal fibula over the lateral talus. Rotating the whole leg medially 15-20 degrees aligns both malleoli parallel to the image receptor, revealing the symmetric ankle mortise ring to detect subtle talar tilt, osteochondral dome lesions, and distal tibiofibular syndesmosis widening.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01824","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"MRI Safety and Contrast Media","topic":"Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis (NSF)","q":"A dialysis-dependent CKD stage 5 patient (eGFR 8 mL/min/1.73 m2) is referred for a contrast-enhanced MRI. Which rare, debilitating systemic complication is linked to unstable Group I linear Gadolinium-Based Contrast Agents (GBCAs) in severe renal failure, and what is the current radiology safety protocol?","o":["Immediate anaphylactoid bone dissolution; give IV calcium chloride","Acute radiation necrosis from MRI X-ray photons","Contrast-induced hyperthyroidism; premedicate with Lugol\u0027s iodine","Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis (NSF—progressive thickening/fibrosis of skin, joints, and organs); avoid Group I linear GBCAs in eGFR \u003c30 or AKI, evaluate non-contrast MRI alternatives first, and use low-dose stable Group II macrocyclic GBCAs only when clinically indispensable"],"a":3,"e":"In severe renal failure (eGFR \u003c30 mL/min/1.73 m2 or dialysis/AKI), prolonged GBCA half-life allows unstable linear chelates (gadodiamide, gadopentetate, gadoversetamide—ACR Group I) to dissociate free toxic Gd3+ ions into tissues, triggering irreversible Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis (NSF). Modern ACR/ESUR guidelines prohibit Group I linear agents in severe CKD and favor non-contrast sequences or stable Group II macrocyclic agents when essential.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01825","course":"Diploma in X-Ray / Radiography","subject":"Radiation Protection and Safety","topic":"Inverse Square Law Staff Scatter Protection","q":"During a bedside mobile ICU chest radiograph, the radiographer steps back from 1 meter to 3 meters away from the patient (the primary source of scatter radiation) before pressing the exposure switch. By what factor does the radiographer\u0027s scatter radiation exposure rate decrease due to distance alone?","o":["It decreases by a factor of 9 (to 1/9th, or an ~89% reduction)","It decreases by a factor of 2 (to 1/2)","Distance has no effect on scatter radiation intensity in air","It decreases by a factor of 3 (to 1/3rd)"],"a":0,"e":"By the Inverse Square Law (I2 / I1 = [d1 / d2]^2), increasing distance threefold from 1 m to 3 m reduces exposure intensity to (1 / 3)^2 = 1/9th (~11% of the original dose rate, an 89% dose reduction). Combining \u003e=2 m cord distance, wearing a 0.25-0.5 mm Pb-equivalent apron, and standing at 90 degrees to the primary beam maximally protects staff.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01826","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology \u0026 Blood Banking","topic":"Vacutainer Anticoagulants \u0026 Order of Draw","q":"A phlebotomist is collecting blood for Blood Culture, PT/INR (Citrate), Serum Electrolytes, CBC (EDTA), and Fasting Glucose (Fluoride-Oxalate). What is the correct CLSI Order of Draw to prevent additive cross-contamination (especially K+ EDTA carryover into serum/coagulation tubes)?","o":["Lavender (EDTA) -\u003e Grey (Fluoride) -\u003e Light Blue (Citrate) -\u003e Serum -\u003e Blood Culture","Blood Culture -\u003e Light Blue (3.2% Sodium Citrate) -\u003e Red/Gold Serum (Clot Activator/SST) -\u003e Lavender (EDTA) -\u003e Grey (Sodium Fluoride/Potassium Oxalate)","Serum -\u003e Blood Culture -\u003e Lavender (EDTA) -\u003e Light Blue -\u003e Grey","Light Blue (Citrate) -\u003e Lavender (EDTA) -\u003e Blood Culture -\u003e Grey -\u003e Serum"],"a":1,"e":"The standard CLSI Order of Draw prevents false hyperkalaemia, false hypocalcaemia, and false PT/aPTT prolongation: (1) Blood culture bottles (sterile), (2) Citrate (light blue), (3) Serum/SST (red/gold), (4) Heparin (green), (5) EDTA (lavender), (6) Glycolytic inhibitor (grey, sodium fluoride/potassium oxalate). Drawing EDTA before serum/citrate contaminates the needle/stopper with K+ and Ca2+-chelating EDTA, causing spurious hyperkalaemia and severe hypocalcaemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01827","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Beer-Lambert Law Calculation","q":"In an enzymatic glucose assay (GOD-POD method), the standard glucose solution (concentration = 100 mg/dL) gives an absorbance of 0.40 at 505 nm against a reagent blank, while the patient serum sample gives an absorbance of 0.52. Assuming linearity under Beer-Lambert law, what is the patient serum glucose concentration?","o":["208 mg/dL","112 mg/dL","130 mg/dL","76.9 mg/dL"],"a":2,"e":"Under Beer-Lambert Law (A = epsilon x c x l): Concentration of Test = (Absorbance of Test / Absorbance of Standard) x Concentration of Standard = (0.52 / 0.40) x 100 mg/dL = 1.3 x 100 = 130 mg/dL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01828","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Anticoagulants \u0026 Vacutainers","q":"A phlebotomist inadvertently draws a lavender-top (K2EDTA) tube before a green-top (lithium heparin) tube using the same needle assembly without discarding. Which pre-analytical electrolyte artifact is most likely to appear in the heparin plasma sample?","o":["Falsely elevated sodium and falsely decreased chloride","Falsely decreased magnesium and falsely elevated bicarbonate","Falsely decreased potassium and falsely elevated calcium","Falsely elevated potassium and markedly decreased calcium"],"a":3,"e":"K2EDTA contains dipotassium ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid. Even trace carryover of K2EDTA into a heparin or serum tube introduces exogenous potassium (falsely elevating K+) and chelates divalent cations such as calcium and magnesium (causing spuriously low Ca2+ and Mg2+ and inhibiting alkaline phosphatase).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01829","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology \u0026 Blood Banking","topic":"Anticoagulant-Preservative Solutions","q":"In an Indian blood centre licensed under Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, what is the maximum approved shelf life and storage temperature for Whole Blood collected in CPDA-1, compared with Packed Red Blood Cells suspended in SAGM additive solution?","o":["CPDA-1 Whole Blood: 35 days at 2-6 C; SAGM PRBC: 42 days at 2-6 C","CPDA-1 Whole Blood: 35 days at 20-24 C; SAGM PRBC: 42 days at 2-6 C","CPDA-1 Whole Blood: 21 days at 2-6 C; SAGM PRBC: 35 days at 2-6 C","CPDA-1 Whole Blood: 28 days at 4-8 C; SAGM PRBC: 42 days at 4-8 C"],"a":0,"e":"Citrate-phosphate-dextrose-adenine (CPDA-1) preserves red cell ATP via adenine and sustains glycolysis via dextrose, extending shelf life at 2-6 C to 35 days (vs 21 days for CPD). Additive solution SAGM (saline-adenine-glucose-mannitol) maintains membrane integrity and extends PRBC shelf life at 2-6 C to 42 days.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01830","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Chemical Examination of Urine","q":"A 64-year-old man with bone pain has a trace protein result on a routine urine reagent strip (tetrabromophenol blue), yet 24-hour urinary total protein measured by sulfosalicylic acid (SSA) turbidimetry is 3.8 g/day. On heating an acidified urine sample, turbidity appears at 50 C, clears completely at 100 C, and reappears upon cooling. What explains the initial dipstick discrepancy?","o":["Alkaline urine destroyed the monoclonal light chains before dipstick immersion","Urine reagent strips selectively detect albumin and are insensitive to monoclonal immunoglobulin light chains","Sulfosalicylic acid precipitates only Tamm-Horsfall mucoprotein and not immunoglobulins","High urinary glucose interfered with the bromophenol blue indicator pad"],"a":1,"e":"The protein error of indicators principle on urine dipsticks (tetrabromophenol blue) is highly sensitive to albumin but insensitive to globulins and Bence Jones proteins (monoclonal kappa or lambda free light chains). Acid-precipitation tests like SSA and the classic heat-coagulation test (precipitating at 40-60 C, dissolving at 100 C) detect Bence Jones proteinuria.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01831","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Reticulocyte Production Index (RPI)","q":"A patient with normocytic anemia has a hematocrit (Hct) of 15% (normal reference Hct = 45%) and a raw peripheral blood reticulocyte count of 6.0%. Given a reticulocyte maturation correction factor of 2.5 days at Hct 15%, what is the Reticulocyte Production Index (RPI), and how is the bone marrow response classified?","o":["RPI = 2.4; brisk hemolytic response","RPI = 2.0; adequate hyperproliferative marrow response","RPI = 0.8; inadequate (hypoproliferative) bone marrow response","RPI = 5.0; polycythemic marrow hyperplasia"],"a":2,"e":"First, Corrected Reticulocyte Count (CRC) = Raw Retic% x (Patient Hct / Normal Hct) = 6.0% x (15 / 45) = 2.0%. Next, RPI = CRC / Maturation Factor = 2.0 / 2.5 = 0.8. Because an RPI \u003c 2.0 in an anemic patient indicates failure of the marrow to mount an adequate compensatory erythropoietic surge, the anemia is hypoproliferative.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01832","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Histopathology \u0026 Cytotechniques","topic":"Routine Paraffin Embedding \u0026 H\u0026E Staining","q":"During routine histopathology tissue processing prior to hematoxylin and eosin (H\u0026E) staining, what is the standard fixative-to-tissue volume ratio for 10% neutral buffered formalin (NBF), and what is the primary purpose of xylene in the clearing step?","o":["2:1 volume ratio; xylene hydrates the tissue to accept aqueous hematoxylin directly","5:1 volume ratio; xylene decalcifies dense cortical bone specimens","1:1 volume ratio; xylene precipitates nuclear chromatin prior to eosin staining","15:1 to 20:1 volume ratio; xylene removes dehydrating alcohol and is miscible with molten paraffin wax"],"a":3,"e":"Adequate fixation in 10% NBF requires a fixative volume 15 to 20 times (minimum 10:1) the tissue volume to prevent autolysis and pH drop. Because absolute ethanol and paraffin wax are immiscible, a clearing agent (xylene) displaces ethanol and acts as an intermediate solvent miscible with molten paraffin (58-60 C) while rendering tissue translucent.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01833","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Parasitology","topic":"Malaria Microscopy","q":"When preparing Giemsa-stained peripheral blood smears for suspected malaria under NCVBDC (India) laboratory protocol, how do the fixation step and diagnostic utility differ between the thick smear and the thin smear on the same glass slide?","o":["Fix the thick smear with absolute methanol for species identification; leave the thin smear unfixed for parasite concentration","Leave the thick smear unfixed so RBCs lyse (dehemoglobinize) for high-sensitivity screening; fix only the thin smear in absolute methanol to preserve RBC morphology for Plasmodium species identification","Heat-fix both smears over a Bunsen flame and stain at pH 4.0","Fix both smears in 10% formalin before applying Leishman stain"],"a":1,"e":"In malaria microscopy, the thick smear is NOT fixed in methanol so that water in the aqueous Giemsa stain (buffered to pH 7.2) lyses erythrocytes, concentrating 20-30 times more blood per microscopic field for maximum sensitivity. Conversely, the thin smear is fixed in absolute methanol to preserve intact RBC size, Schuffner\u0027s dots, and Maurer\u0027s clefts for accurate species identification.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01834","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate (ESR)","q":"While setting up a Westergren Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate (ESR) test, a technician dilutes venous blood 4:1 with 3.8% trisodium citrate, but places the Westergren pipette in a rack tilted 4 degrees off vertical on an uneven benchtop. How will this technical error affect the 1-hour ESR reading?","o":["Markedly falsely elevates the ESR (up to 30% higher) because RBCs settle rapidly along the lower wall while plasma rises along the upper wall","Causes immediate intravascular-type hemolysis in the pipette","Falsely decreases the ESR because red cells adhere to the tilted glass wall","Produces no measurable effect unless tilt exceeds 30 degrees"],"a":0,"e":"Strictly vertical tube alignment (+/- 1 degree) is critical in the Westergren ESR method. Even a small 3-degree tilt accelerates erythrocyte sedimentation markedly (Boycott effect) because settling red cells slide down the lower glass wall while displaced plasma flows upward unimpeded along the upper side, falsely elevating ESR by up to 30%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01835","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Friedewald Equation","q":"A fasting lipid panel yields Total Cholesterol = 240 mg/dL, HDL-Cholesterol = 40 mg/dL, and Triglycerides = 200 mg/dL. Using the Friedewald formula, what is the calculated LDL-Cholesterol, and above what triglyceride concentration does this formula become invalid?","o":["180 mg/dL; invalid when TG \u003e 150 mg/dL","160 mg/dL; invalid when TG \u003e 400 mg/dL","200 mg/dL; invalid only when TG \u003e 1,000 mg/dL","120 mg/dL; invalid when TG \u003e 200 mg/dL"],"a":1,"e":"In mg/dL, the Friedewald equation estimates VLDL-C as Triglycerides / 5, so LDL-C = Total Cholesterol - HDL-C - (TG / 5) = 240 - 40 - (200 / 5) = 200 - 40 = 160 mg/dL. When fasting triglycerides exceed 400 mg/dL (or chylomicrons/type III dysbetalipoproteinemia are present), the TG/5 ratio overestimates VLDL-C and direct homogeneous LDL-C assay is required.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01836","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Blood Banking \u0026 Transfusion Technology","topic":"Major Crossmatch Phases","q":"In pre-transfusion major crossmatching (mixing recipient serum with donor red blood cells), why is the 37 C incubation followed by the Anti-Human Globulin (AHG / Indirect Coombs) phase mandatory rather than relying solely on an immediate-spin room-temperature crossmatch?","o":["Immediate spin detects only platelet antibodies","Immediate spin works only for Fresh Frozen Plasma compatibility","The 37 C and AHG phases are required to detect incomplete, non-agglutinating IgG warm alloantibodies (such as anti-D, anti-Kell, anti-Kidd, anti-Duffy) capable of causing severe hemolytic transfusion reactions","AHG serum neutralizes cold IgM anti-A and anti-B antibodies"],"a":2,"e":"Room-temperature immediate spin detects IgM ABO incompatibilities, whereas clinically significant warm-reacting IgG antibodies (Rh, Kell, Duffy, Kidd) sensitize RBCs at 37 C without forming visible direct agglutination bridges. Adding polyspecific/anti-IgG AHG reagent after washing crosslinks bound IgG molecules, revealing incompatibility before transfusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01837","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"HbA1c Biological Limitations","q":"A patient with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus and hereditary spherocytosis (chronic hemolytic anemia with reticulocytosis) has fasting plasma glucose readings consistently around 195 mg/dL, yet his HbA1c returns at 5.1%. What accounts for this falsely low HbA1c, and which alternative test reflects 2-3 week glycemic control independently of RBC lifespan?","o":["Iron overload converts HbA1c into fetal HbF; repeat EDTA tube freeze-thaw","Elevated bilirubin blocks enzymatic glucose oxidase; measure serum insulin","Hemolysis prolongs RBC survival to 240 days; measure urine ketones","Accelerated erythrocyte turnover (shortened RBC lifespan) reduces glycation exposure time; measure serum fructosamine (glycated albumin) alongside plasma glucose/OGTT"],"a":3,"e":"HbA1c reflects cumulative non-enzymatic glycation of hemoglobin over an assumed ~120-day erythrocyte lifespan. Any condition that shortens RBC survival (hemolytic anemia, acute blood loss, recent PRBC transfusion, splenomegaly, erythropoietin therapy) lowers HbA1c spuriously. Serum fructosamine (glycated serum proteins/albumin) reflects 2-3 week glycemic control independent of RBC turnover.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01838","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology \u0026 Body Fluids","topic":"Meningitis CSF Profiles","q":"A lumbar puncture CSF specimen from a young adult with 2 weeks of low-grade fever, headache, and cranial nerve palsy is clear-to-straw-colored and forms a delicate fibrinous \u0027cobweb coagulum\u0027 upon standing undisturbed. Microscopy shows 260 WBCs/uL (85% lymphocytes), CSF protein is 210 mg/dL, CSF glucose is 24 mg/dL (simultaneous blood glucose 105 mg/dL), and CSF ADA is elevated. Which meningitis etiology matches this CSF profile?","o":["Tuberculous meningitis (TBM)","Acute pyogenic pneumococcal meningitis","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Acute viral (aseptic) meningitis"],"a":0,"e":"Tuberculous meningitis produces high CSF protein (100-500 mg/dL) that precipitates fibrinogen into a classic \u0027cobweb\u0027 pellicle/coagulum on standing, combined with lymphocytic pleocytosis (typically 100-500/uL), markedly depressed CSF glucose (\u003c 40% of plasma glucose), and elevated adenosine deaminase (ADA).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01839","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology \u0026 Blood Banking","topic":"Weak D (Du) Testing \u0026 RhIG Prophylaxis","q":"Under Indian DGHS blood bank standards, why MUST a donor blood unit that shows no agglutination with anti-D reagent on immediate-spin testing undergo an Indirect Antiglobulin (AHG / Weak D) test before being labeled \u0027RhD-Negative\u0027?","o":["To neutralize anti-D antibodies inside the donor\u0027s plasma","Donor RBCs carrying weak D (quantitative reduction of D epitopes) can stimulate anti-D alloimmunization if transfused to a truly RhD-negative recipient","To detect ABO subgroup A2","To verify platelet viability in CPDA-1 bags"],"a":1,"e":"Weak D (formerly Du) variants express fewer D antigen sites per erythrocyte, failing to agglutinate directly with routine anti-D at immediate spin but reacting at the 37 C AHG phase. Because weak-D donor RBCs possess intact D epitopes capable of immunizing a truly D-negative patient (especially women of childbearing potential), any D-negative donor unit must test negative on Weak D (AHG) testing before being labeled RhD-negative.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01840","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Neubauer Improved Hemocytometer","q":"For a manual Total Leukocyte Count (TLC) using Turk\u0027s fluid at a 1:20 blood dilution in an improved Neubauer counting chamber (depth = 0.1 mm), a technician counts a total of 80 WBCs across the 4 large outer 1 mm x 1 mm corner squares (total counted area = 4 mm2; total volume = 0.4 uL). What is the patient\u0027s total WBC count per microliter (uL)?","o":["1,600 cells/uL","8,000 cells/uL","4,000 cells/uL","16,000 cells/uL"],"a":2,"e":"WBC count (cells/uL) = (Cells counted x Dilution factor) / Volume counted (uL). Volume of 4 corner squares = 4 mm2 x 0.1 mm depth = 0.4 mm3 (0.4 uL). Substituting: (80 x 20) / 0.4 = 1,600 / 0.4 = 4,000 cells/uL (standard shortcut: cells in 4 squares x 50 = 80 x 50 = 4,000).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01841","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Direct vs Indirect Bilirubin \u0026 Phototherapy","q":"A heel-prick capillary serum sample from a jaundiced neonate sits uncovered on a brightly lit laboratory bench under fluorescent lights for 2 hours before total bilirubin analysis using the Jendrassik-Grof diazo method. How does light exposure alter the measured bilirubin, and why is an accelerator (caffeine-benzoate or methanol) required to measure TOTAL bilirubin?","o":["Bilirubin is completely photostable for 48 hours on an open sunlit bench","Light converts conjugated bilirubin into hemoglobin; accelerator chelates calcium","Light increases bilirubin; accelerator precipitates albumin","Light photo-oxidizes/isomerizes bilirubin causing FALSELY LOW results (samples must be shielded from light in amber/foil wrap); an accelerator is required to dissociate hydrophobic unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin from albumin so it can react with diazotized sulfanilic acid"],"a":3,"e":"Bilirubin is highly photosensitive (the same blue-light ~450-460 nm photoisomerization mechanism used therapeutically in neonatal phototherapy!); uncovered specimens degrade rapidly, falsely lowering values by 30-50% in hours. In the van den Bergh / Jendrassik-Grof reaction, water-soluble conjugated (direct) bilirubin reacts directly in aqueous buffer, whereas albumin-bound unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin requires caffeine-sodium benzoate or methanol to disrupt hydrogen bonding and react for Total Bilirubin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01842","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Histopathology Special Stains","topic":"Special Stains Matching","q":"Match the special histopathology stain (List I) with its primary diagnostic target/finding (List II): List I: 1. Periodic Acid-Schiff (PAS), 2. Perl\u0027s Prussian Blue, 3. Congo Red (polarized light), 4. Masson\u0027s Trichrome. List II: A. Collagen fibers (blue/green) vs muscle (red), B. Ferric hemosiderin iron (Prussian blue), C. Glycogen, fungal walls, and basement membranes (magenta), D. Amyloid fibrils (apple-green birefringence).","o":["1-C, 2-B, 3-D, 4-A","1-D, 2-A, 3-C, 4-B","1-C, 2-D, 3-B, 4-A","1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D"],"a":0,"e":"PAS oxidizes 1,2-glycol groups to aldehydes stained magenta by Schiff reagent—highlighting glycogen, glomerular basement membranes, and fungi (1-C). Perl\u0027s Prussian Blue uses potassium ferrocyanide + HCl to stain ferric Fe3+ hemosiderin deep blue (2-B). Congo Red yields pathognomonic apple-green birefringence of amyloid under crossed polarizers (3-D). Masson\u0027s Trichrome differentiates blue/green collagen from red smooth muscle/cytoplasm (4-A).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01843","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Urine Preservation and Cast Microscopy","q":"Match each urinary sediment finding or preservative with its specific clinical or laboratory correlation: 1. Red Blood Cell (RBC) casts 2. White Blood Cell (WBC) casts 3. Waxy casts 4. Hydrochloric acid (6N HCl) preservative for 24-hour urine Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Lower UTI cystitis - Nephrotic syndrome - Normal dehydration - Routine culture and sensitivity","Acute glomerulonephritis - Acute pyelonephritis - Chronic renal failure / tubular stasis - Catecholamines / VMA / calcium estimation","Renal calculi - Glomerular basement membrane rupture - Acute tubular necrosis only - Microscopic cast preservation","Nephrotic syndrome - Bladder carcinoma - Acute pyelonephritis - Uric acid crystallization"],"a":1,"e":"(1) RBC casts form when glomerular bleeding allows red cells to become embedded in Tamm-Horsfall mucoprotein within nephrons, hallmarking acute glomerulonephritis; (2) WBC casts localize inflammation to the renal parenchyma (pyelonephritis/interstitial nephritis); (3) Broad, refractile waxy casts represent degenerated cellular/granular casts seen in severe chronic renal failure; (4) Concentrated/6N HCl stabilizes 24-hour urine catecholamines, VMA, and calcium (while destroying casts and cells).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01844","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Chemical Examination of Urine","q":"In Benedict’s semi-quantitative test for urine reducing substances, a technician heats 5 mL of Benedict’s qualitative reagent with 8 drops (0.5 mL) of urine in a boiling water bath for 5 minutes and observes a brick-red precipitate with clear supernatant. What is the approximate reducing sugar concentration, and which non-glucose substance can also yield a positive result?","o":["Negative; uric acid","Trace (\u003c 0.5 g/dL); ketone bodies","4+ (\u003e 2.0 g/dL); galactose or lactose (non-glucose reducing sugars)","1+ (0.5 g/dL); albumin"],"a":2,"e":"Benedict’s test relies on reduction of cupric (Cu2+) ions in alkaline citrate solution to insoluble cuprous oxide (Cu2O). Colour grading: Blue = negative, Green = Trace (\u003c0.5%), Green-yellow = 1+ (0.5%), Yellow = 2+ (1.0%), Orange = 3+ (1.5%), Brick-red precipitate = 4+ (\u003e2.0 g/dL). Unlike glucose-oxidase dipsticks, Benedict’s test detects ALL reducing substances including galactose, fructose, lactose, and high-dose vitamin C.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01845","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF) Analysis","q":"A lumbar puncture CSF sample from a young adult with subacute fever and neck stiffness appears clear to slightly opalescent. After standing undisturbed in a test tube for 12 hours, a delicate spider-web (\"cobweb\") coagulum forms. Cell count is 240/µL (85% lymphocytes), CSF protein is 180 mg/dL, and CSF glucose is 24 mg/dL (simultaneous blood glucose 96 mg/dL). What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Normal CSF with traumatic tap","Acute aseptic viral meningitis","Acute pyogenic (bacterial) meningitis","Tuberculous meningitis (TBM)"],"a":3,"e":"Tuberculous meningitis classically shows elevated opening pressure, lymphocytic pleocytosis (100–500/µL), markedly elevated protein (\u003e100–500 mg/dL), low CSF glucose (\u003c40% of blood glucose), and formation of a fine fibrin \"cobweb\" coagulum upon standing due to high fibrinogen content (ideal for ZN staining of AFB).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01846","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Semen Examination Protocol","q":"In routine semen analysis, what is the maximum normal liquefaction time at 37°C after ejaculation, and which supravital stain is used to differentiate live (unstained) spermatozoa from dead (pink/red-stained) spermatozoa when motility is very low?","o":["60 minutes; Eosin-Nigrosin stain","12 hours; Sudan Black B stain","5 minutes; Giemsa stain","4 hours; Ziehl-Neelsen stain"],"a":0,"e":"Freshly ejaculated semen coagulates immediately and normally liquefies completely within 15–30 minutes (maximum 60 minutes) at 37°C or room temperature via prostatic serine proteases (PSA). In severe asthenozoospermia, the WHO Eosin-Nigrosin vitality test distinguishes live spermatozoa (intact plasma membrane excludes eosin, appearing white against a dark nigrosin background) from dead spermatozoa (damaged membrane takes up eosin and stains pink/red).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01847","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Anticoagulant Selection","q":"Match each diagnostic test with its standard anticoagulant/tube colour and mechanism of action: 1. Complete Blood Count (CBC) \u0026 HbA1c 2. Prothrombin Time (PT) \u0026 aPTT 3. Westergren ESR 4. Plasma Glucose Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Double oxalate (Wintrobe) - Potassium oxalate alone - Heparin (Green) - Plain clot activator (Red)","K2/K3 EDTA (Lavender, chelates Ca2+) - 3.2% Trisodium citrate 9:1 (Light blue, binds Ca2+) - 3.8% or 3.2% Trisodium citrate 4:1 (Black) - Sodium fluoride + Potassium oxalate (Grey, inhibits enolase)","3.2% Citrate 4:1 - K2 EDTA - Plain serum tube - Acid Citrate Dextrose (Yellow)","Heparin (Green, activates antithrombin) - EDTA (Lavender) - Sodium fluoride (Grey) - 3.2% Citrate 9:1 (Light blue)"],"a":1,"e":"Lavender-top K2/K3 EDTA (1.5 mg/mL blood) irreversibly chelates calcium while preserving RBC/WBC/platelet morphology for CBC and HbA1c. Light-blue 3.2% trisodium citrate at 9 parts blood : 1 part anticoagulant reversibly binds calcium for PT/aPTT. Westergren ESR uses 4 parts blood : 1 part trisodium citrate (Black top). Grey-top NaF/potassium oxalate inhibits the glycolytic enzyme enolase to prevent ex vivo glucose consumption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01848","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Improved Neubauer Chamber","q":"For a manual Total Leukocyte Count (TLC), anticoagulated blood is diluted 1:20 with Turk’s fluid and charged onto an Improved Neubauer counting chamber (depth = 0.1 mm). A total of 160 leukocytes are counted across the four large outer corner squares (each 1 mm × 1 mm). What is the patient’s TLC?","o":["4,000 cells/µL","16,000 cells/µL","8,000 cells/µL","32,000 cells/µL"],"a":2,"e":"Volume of 4 large corner squares = 4 × (1 mm × 1 mm × 0.1 mm depth) = 0.4 mm³ (0.4 µL). Using TLC = (Cells counted × Dilution factor) / Volume counted: (160 × 20) / 0.4 = 3,200 / 0.4 = 8,000 cells/µL (8.0 × 10⁹/L). Equivalently, Cells counted × 50 = 160 × 50 = 8,000/µL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01849","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Reticulocyte Enumeration","q":"An anaemic patient’s peripheral smear stained supravitally with New Methylene Blue shows an observed reticulocyte count of 4.0%. If the patient’s packed cell volume (PCV/hematocrit) is 22.5% (assuming a normal reference PCV of 45%), what is the Corrected Reticulocyte Count (CRC)?","o":["1.0%","4.0%","8.0%","2.0%"],"a":3,"e":"In anaemia, reduced circulating mature RBCs artificially elevate the percentage of reticulocytes. The Corrected Reticulocyte Count adjusts for hematocrit: CRC (%) = Observed Reticulocyte % × (Patient PCV / Normal Reference PCV) = 4.0 × (22.5 / 45) = 4.0 × 0.5 = 2.0%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01850","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"G6PD Deficiency Screening","q":"A male patient develops acute intravascular hemolysis after taking primaquine. In the laboratory, Brewer’s Methemoglobin Reduction Test is performed: the patient’s test tube remains dark chocolate-brown (matching the positive control) after 3 hours of incubation with sodium nitrite, dextrose, and methylene blue, and a supravital stain reveals denatured hemoglobin inclusions along the RBC membrane. What do these findings confirm?","o":["G6PD deficiency; Heinz bodies","Iron deficiency anaemia; Pappenheimer bodies","Beta-thalassemia trait; Cabot rings","Normal G6PD enzyme activity; Howell-Jolly bodies"],"a":0,"e":"In Brewer’s Methemoglobin Reduction Test, sodium nitrite oxidizes hemoglobin to brown methemoglobin. Normal RBCs use G6PD-generated NADPH (coupled with methylene blue) to reduce methemoglobin back to bright red oxyhemoglobin. In G6PD deficiency, NADPH generation fails, so the tube remains brown. Oxidative denaturation precipitates hemoglobin as Heinz bodies, visible only on supravital stains (methyl violet/brilliant cresyl blue), not Romanowsky stains.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01851","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Blood Banking and Immunohaematology","topic":"Crossmatching","q":"Which pairing of blood components is tested in a MAJOR crossmatch prior to issuing packed red blood cells?","o":["Recipient red blood cells + Anti-A/Anti-B antisera","Donor red blood cells + Recipient serum/plasma","Donor red blood cells + Donor serum","Donor serum/plasma + Recipient red blood cells"],"a":1,"e":"The major crossmatch mixes washed donor red blood cells (from the blood bag segment) with recipient serum/plasma across saline (room temperature), albumin/LISS (37°C), and anti-human globulin (AHG/Coombs) phases to detect recipient antibodies capable of destroying the transfused donor RBCs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01852","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Blood Banking and Immunohaematology","topic":"Coombs Test Principles","q":"Assertion (A): The Direct Antiglobulin Test (DAT) is the test of choice on cord blood to confirm Hemolytic Disease of the Newborn (HDN) or in suspected autoimmune hemolytic anaemia. Reason (R): DAT detects incomplete IgG antibodies or complement (C3d) that have already sensitized the patient’s red blood cells in vivo, without requiring a 37°C in vitro incubation step. Choose the correct option:","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"DAT directly washes the patient’s/newborn’s RBCs to remove unbound plasma proteins and adds Anti-Human Globulin (Coombs reagent). Agglutination confirms in vivo coating of RBCs by maternal IgG (HDN), autoantibodies (warm AIHA), or drug-induced antibodies/complement. In contrast, IAT (requiring 37°C incubation) detects free unbound antibodies in serum (used for maternal antibody screening, weak D/Du testing, and crossmatching).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01853","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Blood Banking and Immunohaematology","topic":"Blood Component Storage Requirements","q":"Which storage temperature, agitation requirement, and shelf life correctly apply to Random Donor Platelet (RDP) concentrates versus Packed Red Blood Cells (PRBC) in CPDA-1 anticoagulant?","o":["Platelets: +20°C to +24°C stationary for 42 days; PRBC (CPDA-1): +2°C to +6°C for 7 days","Platelets: -18°C for 5 days; PRBC (CPDA-1): +20°C to +24°C for 24 hours","Platelets: +20°C to +24°C with continuous gentle agitation for 5 days; PRBC (CPDA-1): +2°C to +6°C for 35 days","Platelets: +2°C to +6°C stationary for 35 days; PRBC: -30°C for 1 year"],"a":2,"e":"Under Drugs and Cosmetics Rules (India) and NBTC standards, platelet concentrates are stored at +20°C to +24°C on a flat-bed platelet agitator (to maintain pH \u003e= 6.2 and prevent clumping/shape loss) for up to 5 days. Whole blood and PRBCs in CPDA-1 are stored in blood bank refrigerators at +2°C to +6°C for 35 days (42 days with SAGM additive solution).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01854","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Spectrophotometry and Beer-Lambert Law","q":"During serum glucose estimation by the GOD-POD enzymatic colorimetric method at 505 nm (instrument zeroed with reagent blank), the absorbance of the Standard (concentration = 100 mg/dL) is 0.30 and the absorbance of the patient Test sample is 0.45. Assuming linearity, what is the patient’s serum glucose concentration?","o":["200 mg/dL","120 mg/dL","66.7 mg/dL","150 mg/dL"],"a":3,"e":"According to Beer-Lambert Law, Absorbance (A) = ε · c · l, so absorbance is directly proportional to concentration when path length (l) and molar absorptivity (ε) are constant. Therefore, Conc_Test = (A_Test / A_Std) × Conc_Std = (0.45 / 0.30) × 100 mg/dL = 1.5 × 100 = 150 mg/dL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01855","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Internal Quality Control (IQC)","q":"While reviewing the morning Internal Quality Control (IQC) Levey-Jennings chart for serum creatinine before releasing patient reports, the lab technologist observes that two consecutive control runs on Level-1 QC both fell above +2 SD (at +2.3 SD and +2.5 SD), while the previous day’s control was at +1.2 SD. According to Westgard multi-rules, which rule is violated and what type of error does it indicate?","o":["2_2s rule rejection; indicates systematic error (shift/bias) requiring troubleshooting before releasing patient results","1_2s rule warning only; release patient results without checking","R_4s rule rejection; indicates pure random pipetting error only","4_1s rule warning; recalibrate only after 10 consecutive runs"],"a":0,"e":"Westgard’s 1_2s rule is a warning rule that triggers inspection of other rules. When two consecutive control measurements exceed the same +2 SD (or -2 SD) limit, the 2_2s rejection rule is violated. Rules sensitive to systematic error (shift or trend/drift, e.g., failing lamp, deteriorated reagent, or calibration drift) include 2_2s, 4_1s, and 10_x, whereas 1_3s and R_4s detect random error.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01856","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Serum Bilirubin Estimation","q":"In the Jendrassik-Grof (or Malloy-Evelyn) diazo method for serum bilirubin estimation, why is an accelerator (caffeine-benzoate or methanol) required for Total Bilirubin measurement but omitted for Direct Bilirubin, and why must the blood sample be protected from bright light?","o":["Conjugated bilirubin requires methanol to precipitate proteins; light increases bilirubin concentration by hemolysis","Unconjugated bilirubin is water-insoluble and bound to albumin, requiring the accelerator to split hydrogen bonds to react with diazotized sulfanilic acid; light photo-oxidizes bilirubin causing falsely low results","The accelerator prevents interference from serum creatinine; light causes false elevation of direct bilirubin","Direct bilirubin measures biliverdin only; light converts urobilinogen into bilirubin"],"a":1,"e":"Water-soluble conjugated (direct) bilirubin reacts rapidly with Ehrlich’s diazo reagent (diazotized sulfanilic acid) in aqueous medium without an accelerator. Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin is tightly bound to albumin with internal hydrogen bonding, requiring caffeine-benzoate (Jendrassik-Grof) or 50% methanol (Malloy-Evelyn) to dissociate it so Total Bilirubin can react. Serum bilirubin degrades rapidly on exposure to UV/daylight (photo-isomerization/oxidation), necessitating amber/wrapped tubes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01857","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"Ziehl-Neelsen (ZN) Acid-Fast Staining","q":"During Ziehl-Neelsen (ZN) staining of a sputum smear for Mycobacterium tuberculosis under NTEP microscopy guidelines, which sequence of primary stain, decolorizer, and counterstain is used, and why is heating applied during primary staining?","o":["0.1% Methylene blue with heating -\u003e 5% HCl -\u003e 1% Carbol fuchsin; heat removes peptidoglycan","Auramine-O with boiling -\u003e 95% ethanol -\u003e Malachite green; heat dissolves spores","1% Carbol fuchsin with intermittent heating (5 min) -\u003e 20% H2SO4 (or 25% H2SO4) decolorizer -\u003e 0.1% Methylene blue counterstain; heat helps penetrate the lipid-rich mycolic acid cell wall","Crystal violet cold -\u003e Gram iodine -\u003e Acetone-alcohol -\u003e Safranin; heat fixes lipids"],"a":2,"e":"Mycobacteria possess a thick, hydrophobic cell wall rich in mycolic acids that resists standard Gram dyes. Heating drives phenolic 1% carbol fuchsin into the cell wall; once stained, bacilli resist decolorization by strong acid (20%–25% sulfuric acid under NTEP; 5% H2SO4 for M. leprae), appearing as slender, slightly curved red/pink beaded rods against a blue methylene blue background.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01858","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"Enterobacteriaceae Identification","q":"A lactose-fermenting Gram-negative bacillus isolated on MacConkey agar from a urine specimen yields the following IMViC reaction profile: Indole positive, Methyl Red positive, Voges-Proskauer negative, and Simmons Citrate negative (+ + - -). Which organism is most consistent with this profile?","o":["Proteus mirabilis","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Escherichia coli"],"a":3,"e":"Escherichia coli is a lactose fermenter (pink colonies on MacConkey agar) with the classic IMViC pattern + + - - (Indole +, Methyl Red +, Voges-Proskauer -, Citrate -). In contrast, Klebsiella pneumoniae typically produces mucoid pink colonies with the opposite IMViC pattern - - + +.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01859","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Parasitology","topic":"Malaria Microscopy","q":"On examining a Giemsa-stained thin peripheral blood smear from a patient with severe fever, the technician observes normal-sized RBCs containing multiple delicate ring-form trophozoites, marginal \"appliqué/accolé\" forms, double chromatin dots, and crescent-shaped (banana-shaped) gametocytes. What is the utility of the paired thick smear, and which Plasmodium species is identified?","o":["Thick smear increases sensitivity for screening and parasite density estimation (lysed RBCs); Plasmodium falciparum","Thick smear preserves RBC size; Plasmodium malariae","Thick smear detects Schüffner dots; Plasmodium ovale","Thick smear determines RBC morphology; Plasmodium vivax"],"a":0,"e":"In malaria microscopy, the dehemoglobinized thick film concentrates 10–20× more blood per field for sensitive screening and parasite counting against WBCs, whereas the methanol-fixed thin film preserves RBC morphology for species identification. Multiple rings per normal-sized RBC, double-dot/accolé rings, and banana/crescent-shaped gametocytes are diagnostic of Plasmodium falciparum (whereas P. vivax causes enlarged RBCs with Schüffner’s dots and amoeboid trophozoites).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01860","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Parasitology","topic":"Parasite Concentration Methods","q":"Why is the Formol-Ether (or Formol-Ethyl Acetate) sedimentation technique preferred over Zinc Sulfate (sp. gr. 1.18) centrifugal flotation when screening stool samples for operculated trematode eggs (e.g., Fasciola) or unfertilized Ascaris lumbricoides eggs?","o":["Formol-ether lyses all protozoan cysts so only helminth worms remain","Heavy eggs and operculated eggs fail to float in standard zinc sulfate solution and/or collapse/rupture in hypertonic salt, whereas formol-ether sediments all ova and cysts cleanly while dissolving faecal fats","Formol-ether increases the specific gravity of water to 1.50","Zinc sulfate only recovers trophozoites and destroys Ascaris eggs"],"a":1,"e":"Zinc sulfate flotation (specific gravity 1.18) concentrates light cysts and eggs at the top meniscus, but heavy eggs (unfertilized Ascaris, Taenia, Schistosoma, and operculated trematode/Diphyllobothrium eggs) sink to the bottom or become distorted by hypertonic zinc sulfate. Formol-ether sedimentation fixes parasites in formalin, extracts faecal lipids/debris into the ether layer, and concentrates all cysts and heavy/operculated ova intact in the bottom sediment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01861","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Histopathology and Cytology","topic":"Fixation Principles","q":"What is the recommended minimum fixative-to-tissue volume ratio for routine surgical biopsy fixation in 10% Neutral Buffered Formalin (NBF), and what is its primary chemical mechanism of action?","o":["1:1 ratio; dehydrates tissues by lipid extraction","50:1 ratio; freezes cellular enzymes reversibly","15:1 to 20:1 ratio; forms methylene cross-links between amino groups of tissue proteins to prevent autolysis and putrefaction","2:1 ratio; precipitates nucleic acids by extreme acidity (pH 2.0)"],"a":2,"e":"Routine histopathology uses 10% Neutral Buffered Formalin (a 4% w/v formaldehyde solution buffered to pH 7.0–7.2 to prevent acid formalin hematin pigment) at a fixative-to-tissue volume ratio of 15–20:1. Formaldehyde forms additive methylene cross-links between lysine/protein amino groups, halting enzymatic autolysis and bacterial putrefaction while preserving tissue architecture.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01862","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Histopathology and Cytology","topic":"Haematoxylin and Eosin (H\u0026E) Staining","q":"In regressive Harris Haematoxylin and Eosin (H\u0026E) staining of paraffin-embedded tissue sections, what is the exact purpose of dipping the slides briefly in 1% acid alcohol followed by rinsing in alkaline tap water (or Scott’s tap water substitute) prior to eosin staining?","o":["Acid alcohol stains collagen pink; tap water precipitates eosin inside nuclei","Acid alcohol removes paraffin wax; tap water dehydrates the section before DPX mounting","Acid alcohol clears the tissue; tap water prevents xylene evaporation","Acid alcohol selectively differentiates (removes excess haematoxylin from cytoplasm/background), and alkaline water \"blues\" the nuclear chromatin from reddish-brown to crisp blue-purple"],"a":3,"e":"In regressive H\u0026E staining, sections are initially overstained with Harris haematoxylin. A brief dip in 1% acid alcohol (1% HCl in 70% ethanol) decolorizes cytoplasm and background mucin while leaving nuclear chromatin bound. Rinsing in mildly alkaline water (Scott’s tap water / running tap water) converts the soluble red aluminium-hematein lake into an insoluble crisp blue-purple nuclear stain (\"bluing\") before counterstaining cytoplasm/collagen pink with eosin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01863","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology \u0026 Coagulation","topic":"Sodium Citrate Tube Fill \u0026 Polycythemia Adjustment","q":"A patient with polycythemia vera (Hematocrit = 66%) has a light-blue-top 3.2% trisodium citrate tube drawn cleanly to the standard fill mark (9:1 whole blood-to-citrate ratio), yet PT and aPTT return markedly prolonged despite normal liver function and no anticoagulants. Why did polycythemia falsely prolong PT and aPTT, and how must the technician resolve it?","o":["Reduced plasma fraction leaves excess unbound citrate in the plasma that chelates the CaCl2 reagent added during PT/aPTT testing; reduce the citrate volume in the collection tube proportionally per CLSI H21 formula before redrawing","High hematocrit destroys Factor VIII mechanically during centrifugation; switch to a purple EDTA tube","Excess RBCs release thromboplastin accelerating clotting; dilute plasma 1:10 with saline","Polycythemia increases fibrinogen 10-fold, blocking optical clot detectors"],"a":0,"e":"A standard 9:1 citrate tube assumes ~55-60% plasma volume. When Hematocrit exceeds 55%, plasma volume shrinks sharply so a disproportionate amount of free trisodium citrate remains in plasma—binding the fixed calcium chloride added by the coagulometer and spuriously prolonging PT and aPTT (the exact same mechanism occurs when a normal-Hct citrate tube is underfilled \u003c 90%!). Per CLSI H21, citrate volume must be adjusted using C = (1.85 x 10^-3) x (100 - Hct) x V_blood.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01864","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Mentzer Index \u0026 Beta-Thalassemia Trait","q":"A 24-year-old asymptomatic woman undergoes pre-marital CBC screening: Hb = 11.2 g/dL, MCV = 64 fL, RBC count = 6.2 x 10^12/L (6.2 million/uL), and RDW = 13.1% (normal). Calculating the Mentzer Index (MCV / RBC in millions/uL), what is the index value, most likely diagnosis, and confirmatory laboratory test?","o":["Mentzer Index = 10.3 (\u003c 13); confirms severe Iron Deficiency Anemia","Mentzer Index = 10.3 (\u003c 13); favors Beta-Thalassemia Trait, confirmed by elevated HbA2 (\u003e 3.5%) on HPLC / Hb electrophoresis","Mentzer Index = 19.4 (\u003e 13); confirms Megaloblastic Anemia","Mentzer Index = 0.09; confirms Aplastic Anemia"],"a":1,"e":"Mentzer Index = MCV (fL) / RBC count (millions/uL) = 64 / 6.2 = 10.3 (\u003c 13). In beta-thalassemia trait, compensatory erythroid hyperplasia maintains a high/elevated RBC count despite microcytosis (producing uniform microcytes -\u003e normal RDW and Mentzer \u003c 13), confirmed by HbA2 \u003e 3.5% on cation-exchange HPLC. In iron deficiency anemia, marrow output falls (low RBC count -\u003e Mentzer \u003e 13) alongside high anisocytosis (elevated RDW).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01865","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology \u0026 Microscopy","topic":"Urinary Casts \u0026 Dysmorphic RBCs","q":"During microscopic examination of centrifuged urinary sediment in a patient with dark tea-colored urine and periorbital edema, which formed element is MOST specific for active GLOMERULONEPHRITIS?","o":["Calcium oxalate envelope crystals","Hyaline casts alone after vigorous exercise","Red Blood Cell (RBC) casts and \u003e 5% acanthocytes (dysmorphic RBCs)","Triple phosphate (\u0027coffin-lid\u0027) crystals"],"a":2,"e":"When erythrocytes leak across an inflamed glomerular basement membrane, they become distorted (dysmorphic RBCs / acanthocytes) and get embedded in Tamm-Horsfall mucoprotein within distal tubules/collecting ducts—forming pathognomonic Red Blood Cell casts diagnostic of glomerular bleeding (glomerulonephritis). White cell casts indicate pyelonephritis/interstitial nephritis, while muddy-brown granular casts indicate ATN.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01866","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Chemical Examination for Proteinuria","q":"While performing the Heat and Acetic Acid test on a clear urine sample, heating the upper third of the test tube produces distinct white cloudiness. When 3–5 drops of 3% glacial acetic acid are added, the cloudiness completely disappears with slight effervescence. How should the laboratory technician report this result?","o":["Trace proteinuria (1+ albumin)","Bence-Jones proteinuria","Massive nephrotic proteinuria (4+)","Negative for protein (initial turbidity was caused by precipitated phosphates or carbonates)"],"a":3,"e":"Heating alkaline or neutral urine can precipitate amorphous phosphates or carbonates, producing a false-positive white cloud. Adding 3%–5% acetic acid dissolves phosphates (clearing the cloudiness) and carbonates (clearing with CO2 effervescence), whereas true albumin/globulin coagulates permanently or intensifies upon acidification.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01867","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Chemical Examination for Ketone Bodies","q":"In Rothera’s test for urine ketone bodies (using ammonium sulfate, sodium nitroprusside, and liquor ammonia), a purple/violet ring at the junction detects acetoacetic acid and acetone. Which predominant ketone body in severe Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) is NOT detected by Rothera’s test or standard nitroprusside strips?","o":["Beta-hydroxybutyrate","Acetone","Pyruvic acid","Acetoacetic acid"],"a":0,"e":"Sodium nitroprusside in alkaline medium reacts strongly with acetoacetate and weakly with acetone to form a purple ring, but does NOT react with beta-hydroxybutyrate (which lacks a true ketone carbonyl group). During early DKA treatment, beta-hydroxybutyrate oxidizes back to acetoacetate, which can paradoxically make urine Rothera’s/dipstick tests remain positive even as clinical acidosis improves.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01868","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Pleural Fluid Analysis","q":"Laboratory analysis of a patient’s pleural fluid and paired serum shows: Pleural fluid protein = 4.2 g/dL, Serum protein = 6.8 g/dL; Pleural fluid LDH = 310 U/L, Serum LDH = 250 U/L (upper limit of normal serum LDH = 200 U/L). Based on Light’s criteria, how is this pleural effusion classified?","o":["Indeterminate because glucose was not measured","Exudate (consistent with tuberculosis, pneumonia, or malignancy)","Transudate (consistent with congestive heart failure or cirrhosis)","Chylothorax based solely on protein ratio"],"a":1,"e":"According to Light’s criteria, a pleural effusion is an EXUDATE if ANY ONE of the following three criteria is met: (1) Pleural fluid/serum protein ratio \u003e 0.5 (here 4.2 / 6.8 = 0.62); (2) Pleural fluid/serum LDH ratio \u003e 0.6 (here 310 / 250 = 1.24); (3) Pleural fluid LDH \u003e 2/3 of the upper limit of normal serum LDH (here \u003e 133 U/L). All three criteria are met, confirming an exudate.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01869","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Cyanmethemoglobin (Drabkin’s) Method","q":"In the ICSH-recommended Cyanmethemoglobin (Drabkin’s) method for haemoglobin estimation at 540 nm, which chemicals convert haemoglobin into stable cyanmethemoglobin (HiCN), and which abnormal haemoglobin derivative is NOT converted by Drabkin’s reagent?","o":["Sodium metabisulfite and formalin; carboxyhemoglobin (HbCO)","Acetic acid and copper sulfate; methemoglobin","Potassium ferricyanide and potassium cyanide; sulfhemoglobin","Hydrochloric acid and distilled water; fetal haemoglobin (HbF)"],"a":2,"e":"In Drabkin’s reagent (buffered with KH2PO4/NaHCO3), potassium ferricyanide oxidizes Fe2+ of haemoglobin to Fe3+ (forming methemoglobin), and potassium cyanide supplies CN- ions to form stable cyanmethemoglobin (HiCN), measured spectrophotometrically at 540 nm. It measures oxyhemoglobin, deoxyhemoglobin, carboxyhemoglobin, and methemoglobin, but NOT sulfhemoglobin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01870","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Wintrobe Red Cell Indices Calculation","q":"A patient’s complete blood count reveals: Haemoglobin (Hb) = 9.0 g/dL, Packed Cell Volume (PCV/Hct) = 30%, and RBC count = 5.0 million/µL. Calculate the MCV, MCH, and MCHC respectively.","o":["MCV = 120 fL; MCH = 36 pg; MCHC = 30 g/dL","MCV = 60 fL; MCH = 30 pg; MCHC = 18 g/dL","MCV = 90 fL; MCH = 30 pg; MCHC = 33.3 g/dL","MCV = 60 fL; MCH = 18 pg; MCHC = 30 g/dL"],"a":3,"e":"MCV = (PCV% × 10) / RBC (millions/µL) = (30 × 10) / 5.0 = 60 fL (microcytic; normal 80–100 fL). MCH = (Hb g/dL × 10) / RBC (millions/µL) = (9.0 × 10) / 5.0 = 18 pg (hypochromic; normal 27–32 pg). MCHC = (Hb g/dL × 100) / PCV% = (9.0 × 100) / 30 = 30 g/dL (hypochromic; normal 32–36 g/dL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01871","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Romanowsky (Leishman) Staining","q":"After staining a peripheral blood smear with Leishman stain, the technician inspects the slide microscopically and notices that all red blood cells look bright red/orange, eosinophil granules are overly brilliant, and leukocyte nuclei appear pale blue and washed out. What is the most likely technical cause?","o":["The buffered diluting water was too acidic (pH \u003c 6.4), or staining time after dilution was too short","The buffered diluting water was too alkaline (pH \u003e 7.4)","Too much Leishman stain was poured during the fixation phase","The blood smear was made too thick with high reticulocytes"],"a":0,"e":"Romanowsky stains (Leishman, Giemsa, Wright) require buffered water at pH 6.8 (7.0–7.2 for malaria parasites) for balanced binding of basic methylene blue/azure B (nuclei/basophilic cytoplasm) and acidic eosin (RBCs/eosinophil granules). Acidic buffer (pH \u003c 6.4) increases eosin uptake and suppresses basic dye binding, yielding overly pink/red RBCs and pale washed-out leukocyte nuclei.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01872","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Coagulation Screening Panel","q":"A 10-year-old boy with recurrent hemarthroses (joint bleeds) after minor trauma has the following coagulation profile: Platelet count = 2,60,000/µL (normal), Bleeding Time = 4 minutes (normal), Prothrombin Time (PT) = 12.5 seconds (normal), and aPTT = 68 seconds (prolonged; control 30 sec), which corrects on 1:1 mixing with normal pooled plasma. Which factor deficiency is most consistent with these findings?","o":["Factor VII deficiency (extrinsic pathway)","Factor VIII or Factor IX deficiency (Haemophilia A or B, intrinsic pathway)","Factor XIII (fibrin stabilizing factor) deficiency","Severe thrombocytopenia"],"a":1,"e":"An isolated prolonged aPTT with normal PT, normal platelet count, and normal bleeding time localizes the defect to the intrinsic coagulation pathway (Factors VIII, IX, XI, or XII). Hemarthroses in a young male plus correction on a 1:1 mixing study confirm a factor deficiency—most commonly Haemophilia A (Factor VIII) or Haemophilia B (Factor IX).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01873","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Osmotic Fragility Test (OFT)","q":"In the Osmotic Fragility Test (OFT) using graded hypotonic NaCl solutions (0.85% down to 0.10%), a patient’s red blood cells begin lysing early at 0.65% NaCl (normal onset ~0.45%–0.50%), whereas another patient’s RBCs resist lysis until 0.30% NaCl (decreased fragility). Which pair of conditions matches increased versus decreased osmotic fragility respectively?","o":["Increased fragility: Polycythemia vera; Decreased fragility: Normal newborn cord blood","Increased fragility: Beta-thalassemia major; Decreased fragility: Hereditary spherocytosis","Increased fragility: Hereditary spherocytosis; Decreased fragility: Thalassemia / Iron deficiency anaemia (leptocytes/target cells)","Increased fragility: Sickle cell anaemia; Decreased fragility: Autoimmune hemolytic anaemia with spherocytes"],"a":2,"e":"Osmotic fragility reflects the surface-area-to-volume ratio of RBCs. Spherocytes (in hereditary spherocytosis and warm AIHA) have reduced surface area relative to volume, so they swell and burst in milder hypotonic saline (increased osmotic fragility, shifted to the right). Conversely, flat hypochromic cells and target cells (in thalassemia, iron deficiency anaemia, and sickle cell disease) have excess membrane surface area relative to volume and withstand lower NaCl concentrations before lysing (decreased fragility).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01874","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Blood Banking and Immunohaematology","topic":"ABO Grouping Discrepancies","q":"During routine blood grouping, a patient’s RBCs show no agglutination with Anti-A, Anti-B, or Anti-AB (appearing Group O on forward grouping), yet the patient’s serum strongly agglutinates A-cells, B-cells, AND Group O pooled control cells at room temperature and 37°C/AHG. Adding Ulex europaeus lectin (Anti-H) shows NO agglutination with the patient’s RBCs. What is the patient’s blood group, and what blood can they safely receive?","o":["Group O Rh-positive; can safely receive standard Group O packed RBCs","Subgroup A2 with Anti-A1; can receive Group A2 blood","Cold autoagglutinin disease; warm blood to 37°C and transfuse Group O","Bombay (Oh) phenotype; has potent Anti-H in serum and can ONLY receive autologous or Bombay (Oh) donor blood"],"a":3,"e":"Individuals with the rare Bombay (Oh, hh) phenotype lack FUT1-encoded H transferase and cannot synthesize H antigen (the precursor for A and B antigens). Forward grouping mimics Group O, but because their RBCs lack H antigen, they do NOT react with Anti-H lectin (Ulex europaeus) and their serum contains naturally occurring, complement-binding Anti-H (plus Anti-A and Anti-B) that lyses standard Group O RBCs. Only Oh blood or autologous blood is compatible.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01875","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Blood Banking and Immunohaematology","topic":"Mandatory Donor Screening in India","q":"Under the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules (India) and National Blood Transfusion Council (NBTC) standards, which FIVE Transfusion-Transmitted Infections (TTIs) must be mandatorily screened on every single unit of donated blood before release?","o":["HIV 1 \u0026 2, Hepatitis B (HBsAg), Hepatitis C (Anti-HCV), Syphilis, and Malaria","HIV 1 \u0026 2, CMV, EBV, Malaria, and Dengue","Hepatitis B, Hepatitis E, Chikungunya, Syphilis, and Brucellosis","HIV 1 \u0026 2, Hepatitis A, Dengue, Typhoid, and Tuberculosis"],"a":0,"e":"In India, every donated unit must test non-reactive/negative for five mandatory TTIs before labelling and issue: (1) HIV 1 \u0026 2 (antibodies/antigen-antibody ELISA/CLIA or NAT), (2) Hepatitis B (HBsAg), (3) Hepatitis C (Anti-HCV), (4) Syphilis (VDRL/RPR/TPHA), and (5) Malaria (microscopy or validated rapid antigen test).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01876","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT)","q":"A non-pregnant adult undergoes a standard 75 g Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT) after an 8–12 hour overnight fast. Venous plasma glucose results are: Fasting = 114 mg/dL; 2-hour post-load = 178 mg/dL. According to WHO criteria, how should this result be interpreted?","o":["Normal glucose tolerance","Impaired Fasting Glucose (IFG) and Impaired Glucose Tolerance (IGT) — Prediabetes","Diabetes Mellitus","Renal glycosuria"],"a":1,"e":"WHO diagnostic thresholds for non-pregnant adults: Normal FPG \u003c 110 mg/dL (ADA \u003c 100 mg/dL) and 2-hr PG \u003c 140 mg/dL; Diabetes Mellitus is FPG \u003e= 126 mg/dL OR 2-hr PG \u003e= 200 mg/dL. Here, FPG 114 mg/dL falls in the IFG range (110–125 mg/dL WHO; 100–125 mg/dL ADA) and 2-hr PG 178 mg/dL falls in the IGT range (140–199 mg/dL), indicating prediabetes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01877","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Diagnostic Organ Enzymes","q":"Match each serum enzyme/biomarker profile with its most characteristic clinical condition: 1. Markedly elevated Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP) and Gamma-Glutamyl Transferase (GGT) with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia 2. Serum AST \u003e ALT with De Ritis (AST/ALT) ratio \u003e 2.0 3. Elevated Serum Lipase (more specific than Amylase) \u003e 3× upper limit of normal 4. Cardiac Troponin I (cTnI) rising at 3–6 hours and remaining elevated for 7–10 days Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Bone rickets - Acute viral hepatitis - Liver cirrhosis - Pulmonary embolism","Acute viral hepatitis - Bone Paget disease - Chronic renal failure - Skeletal muscle cramp","Obstructive/cholestatic liver disease - Alcoholic hepatitis - Acute pancreatitis - Acute myocardial infarction","Alcoholic hepatitis - Obstructive jaundice - Acute myocardial infarction - Acute pancreatitis"],"a":2,"e":"(1) Simultaneous elevation of ALP and GGT confirms hepatobiliary cholestasis/biliary obstruction (GGT differentiates liver ALP from bone ALP); (2) AST/ALT \u003e 2 (with absolute levels typically \u003c 300 U/L due to pyridoxal-5-phosphate depletion affecting ALT) is classic for alcoholic hepatitis; (3) Serum lipase is more sensitive and specific than amylase for acute pancreatitis; (4) Cardiac Troponin I is the gold-standard biomarker for myocardial necrosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01878","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Serum Potassium Pre-Analytical Artifacts","q":"An electrolyte panel run on an Ion-Selective Electrode (ISE) analyzer from an outpatient’s blood sample reports: Na+ = 139 mEq/L, K+ = 8.9 mEq/L, and Total Calcium = 1.8 mg/dL, even though the patient is asymptomatic with a normal ECG. What pre-analytical error most likely occurred during phlebotomy?","o":["The analyzer’s sodium electrode failed calibration","The patient drank 500 mL of plain water 1 hour before testing","The specimen was transported on an ice pack for 5 minutes","The phlebotomist drew blood into a K2/K3 EDTA (lavender-top) tube first and poured part of it into the serum tube, or contaminated the needle with EDTA"],"a":3,"e":"Severe spurious hyperkalemia (e.g., K+ \u003e 7–8 mEq/L) paired with markedly low calcium (Ca2+ \u003c 3–4 mg/dL) in an asymptomatic patient is the hallmark signature of K2/K3 EDTA contamination (violating the CLSI order of draw or pouring EDTA blood into a plain tube). Potassium salts in EDTA spike K+ massively while EDTA chelates serum calcium, yielding a dangerously false hypocalcemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01879","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Serum Creatinine Assay","q":"In the Jaffe reaction for serum creatinine estimation, creatinine reacts with picric acid in an alkaline medium (NaOH) to form an orange-red Janovski complex measured around 500–520 nm. Why is kinetic Jaffe measurement preferred over end-point Jaffe, and which endogenous/drug substances act as positive \"pseudo-creatinine\" chromogens?","o":["Kinetic reading minimizes interference from slow-reacting non-creatinine chromogens; ketoacids (acetoacetate), glucose, ascorbic acid, and cephalosporins falsely elevate creatinine","Picric acid converts creatinine into ammonia measured at 340 nm","Kinetic reading eliminates interference from urea; triglycerides falsely lower creatinine to zero","End-point Jaffe is specific only for cystatin C; bilirubin increases creatinine tenfold"],"a":0,"e":"The classic Jaffe alkaline picrate reaction lacks absolute specificity: proteins, glucose, ascorbic acid, ketone bodies (acetoacetate), and cephalosporin antibiotics react as non-creatinine chromogens causing falsely high results. Reading absorbance kinetically between ~20 and 80 seconds (after fast-reacting proteins/interferents stabilize or before slow ones react) improves specificity compared to end-point measurement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01880","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"Gram Staining Mechanism","q":"Assertion (A): In Gram staining, Gram’s iodine acts as a mordant that forms an insoluble crystal violet–iodine (CV-I) complex inside bacterial cells, and acetone-alcohol serves as the critical differentiating decolorizer. Reason (R): Over-aged (\u003e 24–48 hours) or antibiotic-damaged Gram-positive bacteria may stain pink (Gram-negative / Gram-variable) because their degraded peptidoglycan cell walls fail to retain the CV-I complex during decolorization. Choose the correct option:","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is NOT the physiological explanation of A, though both principles are true","Both A and R are true, and R directly explains the role of the mordant","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Assertion (A) correctly states the reagents and roles in Gram staining (Crystal Violet primary stain -\u003e Gram’s Iodine mordant -\u003e Acetone-Alcohol decolorizer -\u003e Safranin/dilute carbol fuchsin counterstain). Reason (R) is also true: thick cross-linked peptidoglycan shrinks upon acetone-alcohol dehydration to trap the large CV-I complex, so older cultures with autolytic wall thinning lose CV-I and take up safranin (appearing Gram-variable). However, R describes an age/wall-damage artifact rather than explaining why iodine acts as a mordant in A.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01881","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"Gram-Positive Cocci Identification","q":"A Gram-positive coccus arranged in grape-like clusters grown from a pus swab on nutrient agar yields vigorous oxygen effervescence when touched to a drop of 3% H2O2 on a glass slide. Subsequent testing shows positive clumping on a slide coagulase test and a firm fibrin clot in rabbit/human plasma after 4 hours in a tube coagulase test. Which organism is identified, and why must blood agar colonies NOT be used directly for the catalase test?","o":["Staphylococcus epidermidis; blood agar turns H2O2 acidic","Staphylococcus aureus; RBCs in blood agar contain intrinsic catalase that causes false-positive bubbling","Streptococcus pyogenes; blood agar inhibits bacterial catalase","Enterococcus faecalis; plasma lyses streptococci"],"a":1,"e":"Catalase-positive Gram-positive cocci in clusters belong to Staphylococcus (Streptococci/Enterococci are catalase-negative). Positive slide coagulase (clumping factor/bound coagulase) and tube coagulase (extracellular free coagulase converting fibrinogen to fibrin via coagulase-reacting factor) confirm Staphylococcus aureus (differentiating it from coagulase-negative S. epidermidis/S. saprophyticus). Never pick colonies from blood agar for catalase testing because trace sheep/human RBCs possess peroxidase/catalase that falsely bubbles with 3% H2O2.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01882","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"Kirby-Bauer Disk Diffusion Method","q":"When performing routine Kirby-Bauer disk diffusion Antimicrobial Susceptibility Testing (AST) for non-fastidious bacteria per CLSI guidelines, which agar medium, agar depth, and inoculum turbidity standard must be used?","o":["MacConkey agar; 2 mm depth; 2.0 McFarland standard","Sabouraud dextrose agar; 4 mm depth; 5.0 McFarland standard","Mueller-Hinton agar (pH 7.2–7.4); 4 mm uniform depth; 0.5 McFarland turbidity standard (~1.5 × 10^8 CFU/mL)","Nutrient agar; 8 mm depth; 0.1 McFarland standard"],"a":2,"e":"Kirby-Bauer AST requires Mueller-Hinton agar (low in sulfonamide/trimethoprim inhibitors like thymidine, controlled Ca2+/Mg2+ for aminoglycosides) poured to a precise 4 mm depth (too thin gives falsely large zones/false susceptibility; too thick gives falsely small zones/false resistance) and standardized with a 0.5 McFarland barium sulfate turbidity standard (~1.5 × 10^8 CFU/mL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01883","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Parasitology","topic":"Diagnostic Helminth Ova and Specimen Collection","q":"Match each intestinal helminth with its hallmark diagnostic egg morphology or preferred collection method: 1. Enterobius vermicularis (Pinworm) 2. Trichuris trichiura (Whipworm) 3. Hookworm (Ancylostoma duodenale / Necator americanus) 4. Taenia species Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Rhabditiform larva in CSF - Spine-bearing egg in sputum - Plano-convex egg in stool flotation - Ciliated miracidium in blood","Barrel-shaped egg with bipolar plugs - Mammillated decorticated egg - Operculated golden egg - Crescent-shaped gametocyte","Segmented 4-cell egg in urine - Plano-convex egg in sputum - Radially striated egg in blood - Bipolar plugged egg in CSF","Plano-convex (\"D-shaped\") egg collected via morning perianal NIH cellophane tape swab - Barrel-shaped bile-stained egg with bipolar mucus plugs - Oval non-bile-stained egg with clear space between shell and segmented 4-blastomere embryo - Spherical brown egg with thick radially striated embryophore containing 6-hooked oncosphere"],"a":3,"e":"(1) Enterobius female deposits asymmetric plano-convex eggs on perianal skin at night, best detected by early-morning NIH/Scotch tape swab (rarely found in routine stool); (2) Trichuris eggs are classic barrel-shaped, bile-stained with prominent translucent polar plugs; (3) Hookworm eggs are thin-shelled, colourless (non-bile-stained), containing a 4–8 cell segmented morula with a clear sub-shell space; (4) Taenia eggs have a thick, brown, radially striated embryophore housing a hexacanth oncosphere.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01884","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Histopathology and Cytology","topic":"Bone and Calcified Tissue Decalcification","q":"A fixed bone trephine biopsy is placed in decalcifying solution prior to paraffin embedding. Which chemical test on the spent decalcifying fluid confirms complete decalcification (end-point) without damaging tissue by bending/probing, and which decalcifying agent best preserves nucleic acids and antigens for immunohistochemistry (IHC)?","o":["Calcium oxalate chemical test (ammonium hydroxide + saturated ammonium oxalate showing NO white precipitate); neutral EDTA chelating solution","Rothera’s nitroprusside test; 10% formaldehyde alone","Biuret test; absolute xylene","Benedict’s copper reduction test; 20% sulfuric acid"],"a":0,"e":"Leaving bone too long in strong mineral acids (like 5%–10% nitric acid) destroys nuclear basophilia and antigens; probing with needles damages morphology. The chemical calcium oxalate end-point test neutralizes spent acid fluid with ammonium hydroxide and adds saturated ammonium oxalate: absence of white calcium oxalate turbidity/precipitate after 30 minutes confirms complete decalcification. Though slow, neutral EDTA (pH 7.0–7.4) gently chelates Ca2+ while maximally preserving nuclear staining, DNA/RNA, and IHC epitopes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01885","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Histopathology and Cytology","topic":"Papanicolaou (Pap) Staining","q":"When preparing a conventional cervical Pap smear for exfoliative cytology, why must the glass slide be immersed IMMEDIATELY (within seconds) into 95% ethyl alcohol (or sprayed with fixative) before drying, and what are the cytoplasmic stains used in the Papanicolaou sequence?","o":["To lyse all squamous epithelial cells; Sudan III and Oil Red O","To prevent irreversible air-drying artifact (nuclear swelling and loss of chromatin crispness); Orange G-6 (OG-6) and Eosin Azure (EA-36 / EA-50)","To decalcify cervical mucus; Congo Red and Masson Trichrome","To promote rapid air-drying for better adhesion; Giemsa and Leishman stain"],"a":1,"e":"In conventional Pap cytology, even brief air-drying causes cellular shrinkage, nuclear enlargement/smudging, and false eosinophilia (\"air-drying artifact\") that mimics atypia. Immediate wet fixation in 95% ethanol preserves crisp chromatin structure. The classic Pap stain uses Harris haematoxylin (nuclei blue), Orange G-6 (stains keratinized mature/malignant squamous cytoplasm bright orange), and EA-36/EA-50 (polychrome eosin/light green staining superficial cells pink and intermediate/parabasal cells blue-green).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01886","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Tests for Bile Pigments, Bile Salts, and Urobilinogen","q":"Match each biochemical urine test for hepatobiliary evaluation with its reagent/principle and positive clinical result: 1. Fouchet’s test (with BaCl2 precipitate) 2. Hay’s sulfur flower test 3. Ehrlich’s aldehyde test Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3):","o":["Detects glucose (brick-red) - Detects albumin (white cloud) - Detects bile salts (red ring)","Detects blood (orthotolidine blue) - Detects urobilinogen (sulfur sinks) - Detects bilirubin (yellow precipitate)","Detects conjugated bilirubin (ferric chloride in trichloroacetic acid turns blue-green) - Detects bile salts (sulfur powder sinks to bottom due to lowered surface tension) - Detects urobilinogen (cherry-red colour with p-dimethylaminobenzaldehyde, elevated in hemolytic/hepatocellular jaundice)","Detects urobilinogen (blue-green) - Detects bilirubin (sulfur floats) - Detects ketones (purple ring)"],"a":2,"e":"In obstructive jaundice, urine contains conjugated bilirubin (Fouchet’s test: BaCl2 adsorbs bilirubin and Fouchet’s reagent oxidizes it to green biliverdin) and bile salts (Hay’s test: bile salts lower surface tension causing sulfur powder to sink), while urobilinogen is absent. In hemolytic jaundice, urine bilirubin is absent (\"acholuric\") while urobilinogen is markedly increased (Ehrlich’s test: cherry-red colour).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01887","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Microscopic and Gross Sputum Findings","q":"On microscopic examination of thick mucoid sputum from a patient with severe bronchial asthma, the technician observes twisted mucin casts of small bronchioles alongside hexagonal bipyramidal crystals derived from eosinophil granules. What are these two classic findings called?","o":["Aschoff bodies and Psammoma bodies","Sulfur granules and Asteroid bodies","Negri bodies and Heinz bodies","Curschmann’s spirals and Charcot-Leyden crystals"],"a":3,"e":"Asthmatic sputum characteristically contains abundant eosinophils, Creola bodies (desquamated ciliated epithelial clusters), Curschmann’s spirals (whorled mucous plugs moulded in terminal bronchioles), and Charcot-Leyden crystals (slender, needle/bipyramidal eosinophil lysophospholipase crystals).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01888","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Westergren vs Wintrobe ESR","q":"When setting up a Westergren ESR test (pipette 300 mm long, 2.5 mm bore, graduated 0–200 mm using 4:1 blood-to-3.2%/3.8% sodium citrate), which technical error or RBC morphology causes a FALSELY LOW ESR reading?","o":["Sickle cells / Poikilocytosis / Polycythemia (or laboratory temperature \u003c 18°C)","Tilting the Westergren tube at a 10-degree angle instead of keeping it strictly vertical","Elevated plasma fibrinogen and immunoglobulins (rouleaux formation)","Testing at high ambient room temperature (35°C) with anaemia"],"a":0,"e":"ESR depends on rouleaux formation (accelerated by asymmetrical plasma proteins like fibrinogen and globulins, anaemia, warm room temperature, and even slight tube tilting—which dramatically increases sedimentation). Conversely, abnormally shaped RBCs (sickle cells, spherocytes, acanthocytes) cannot stack into rouleaux, and increased RBC concentration (polycythemia) or cold temperatures (\u003c18°C) cause low ESR.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01889","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Sickle Cell Anaemia Screening and Confirmation","q":"A tribal-area adolescent screened for anaemia tests positive on a 2% sodium metabisulfite slide sickling test (RBCs become crescent/sickle-shaped under coverslip hypoxia). Subsequent Cation-Exchange HPLC / Alkaline Haemoglobin Electrophoresis shows: HbA = 58%, HbS = 39%, HbA2 = 2.5%, and HbF = 0.5%. How should the laboratory report this finding?","o":["Normal adult haemoglobin pattern (HbAA)","Heterozygous Sickle Cell Trait (HbAS)","Homozygous Sickle Cell Disease (HbSS)","Beta-thalassemia major"],"a":1,"e":"Sodium metabisulfite deoxygenates haemoglobin and induces sickling in BOTH Sickle Cell Trait (HbAS) and Sickle Cell Disease (HbSS), so it cannot differentiate them. Definitive Hb electrophoresis/HPLC distinguishes them: HbAS shows both HbA (55%–65%) and HbS (35%–45%, with HbA \u003e HbS), whereas HbSS shows predominant HbS (80%–95%) and elevated HbF with zero HbA (in untransfused patients).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01890","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Absolute Eosinophil Count (AEC)","q":"To determine an Absolute Eosinophil Count (AEC), EDTA blood is diluted 1:10 with Dunger’s diluting fluid (which lyses RBCs and non-eosinophil WBCs while staining eosinophil granules bright red-orange) and charged onto an Improved Neubauer chamber (depth = 0.1 mm). Across all 9 large squares (total area = 9 mm²; volume = 0.9 µL), 36 eosinophils are counted. What is the patient’s AEC?","o":["200 cells/µL","800 cells/µL","400 cells/µL","3,600 cells/µL"],"a":2,"e":"Volume counted across all 9 large squares of an Improved Neubauer chamber = 9 mm² × 0.1 mm = 0.9 µL. Using AEC = (Cells counted × Dilution factor) / Volume counted = (36 × 10) / 0.9 = 360 / 0.9 = 400 cells/µL (within normal adult reference range 40–440 cells/µL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01891","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Perls’ Prussian Blue Iron Stain","q":"In Perls’ Prussian Blue cytochemical stain on an unstained bone marrow aspirate smear (using potassium ferrocyanide and dilute hydrochloric acid), what do bright blue-stained granules inside macrophages versus blue granules forming a ring around \u003e 1/3 of an erythroblast nucleus indicate?","o":["Lipid droplets; Gaucher storage cells","Marrow glycogen; Auer rods of acute myeloid leukemia","Extracellular amyloid; Reed-Sternberg cells","Marrow haemosiderin iron stores (absent in iron deficiency anaemia); Ringed sideroblasts (characteristic of sideroblastic anaemia / MDS)"],"a":3,"e":"Perls’ reaction converts ferric iron (Fe3+) in haemosiderin into insoluble blue ferric ferrocyanide (Prussian blue). Macrophage haemosiderin reflects reticuloendothelial iron stores (gold standard to confirm iron deficiency anaemia when 0, or anaemia of chronic disease when increased). Iron-overloaded mitochondria encircling \u003e= 1/3 of the erythroblast nuclear perimeter (\u003e= 5 granules) define ringed sideroblasts.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01892","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Blood Banking and Immunohaematology","topic":"Weak D (Du) Testing","q":"A blood donor’s RBCs show no agglutination with Anti-D at immediate spin or 37°C, but show 2+ agglutination after adding Anti-Human Globulin (AHG/Coombs serum) during Weak D (Du) testing. How MUST this donated unit be labelled and issued by the blood bank?","o":["Label as RhD-POSITIVE (never transfuse to an RhD-negative recipient, as Weak D donor cells can immunize them to produce Anti-D)","Discard the unit as Bombay phenotype","Label as RhD-negative and transfuse to an RhD-negative woman of childbearing age","Label as Group O universal donor without Rh status"],"a":0,"e":"Weak D (formerly Du) represents a quantitative reduction of D antigen sites on the RBC surface requiring the sensitive IAT/AHG phase for detection. Under NBTC/AABB rules, all apparently D-negative DONOR units must undergo Weak D testing: if Weak D positive, the donor unit carries D antigen and MUST be labelled RhD-positive to prevent alloimmunizing an Rh-negative recipient.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01893","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Friedewald Equation","q":"A fasting patient’s lipid profile yields: Total Cholesterol = 240 mg/dL, HDL-Cholesterol = 45 mg/dL, and Triglycerides (TG) = 200 mg/dL. Using the Friedewald equation, what is the calculated LDL-Cholesterol, and above what TG threshold does this formula become invalid?","o":["195 mg/dL; invalid if TG \u003e= 150 mg/dL","155 mg/dL; invalid if TG \u003e= 400 mg/dL","115 mg/dL; invalid if TG \u003e= 200 mg/dL","165 mg/dL; valid at all TG levels"],"a":1,"e":"In mg/dL, Friedewald’s formula estimates VLDL-C = Triglycerides / 5, and LDL-C = Total Cholesterol - HDL-C - (TG / 5). Here, VLDL-C = 200 / 5 = 40 mg/dL, so LDL-C = 240 - 45 - 40 = 155 mg/dL. When TG \u003e= 400 mg/dL (or in chylomicronemia/Type III hyperlipoproteinemia), the TG:cholesterol ratio in VLDL/chylomicrons alters and direct homogeneous LDL assay must be used instead.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01894","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Serum Urea vs Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN)","q":"In automated clinical chemistry analyzers using the enzymatic Urease–Glutamate Dehydrogenase (GLDH) kinetic UV method at 340 nm, what is measured, and what conversion factor relates Blood Urea (mg/dL) to Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN, mg/dL)?","o":["Reduction of NAD+ to NADH (increase at 540 nm); Urea = BUN × 0.5","Direct reaction with picric acid; Urea equals BUN numerically","Oxidation of NADH to NAD+ (decrease in absorbance at 340 nm); Urea = BUN × 2.14 (since MW of urea is 60 and two nitrogens weigh 28)","Formation of yellow diazo complex; BUN = Urea × 2.14"],"a":2,"e":"Urease hydrolyzes urea [CO(NH2)2, MW 60] into NH3 and CO2. Glutamate dehydrogenase (GLDH) then couples NH3 with alpha-ketoglutarate and NADH -\u003e glutamate + NAD+, causing a measurable decrease in NADH absorbance at 340 nm. Because 60 g of urea contains 28 g of elemental nitrogen (60 / 28 = 2.14), Serum Urea (mg/dL) = BUN × 2.14 (and BUN = Urea / 2.14 or × 0.467).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01895","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) Pre-Analytical Handling","q":"An arterial blood sample for ABG analysis is collected in a plastic syringe containing excess liquid heparin (diluting the sample), with a large air bubble trapped inside, and sits on the bench at 30°C for 45 minutes before analysis. Which combined artifacts will distort the ABG result?","o":["Air bubbles increase PaCO2 to 100 mmHg; delayed storage raises pH to 7.80","Plastic syringes eliminate all metabolic glycolysis completely","Excess heparin causes spurious hyperkalemia; delayed storage increases glucose","Excess liquid heparin falsely LOWERS PaCO2/HCO3-; air bubbles shift PaO2 toward ~150 mmHg and lower PaCO2; delayed room-temperature storage lowers pH and PaO2 while raising PaCO2 via leukocyte metabolism"],"a":3,"e":"Proper ABG collection uses pre-heparinized dry balanced lithium-heparin syringes (avoiding excess liquid heparin dilution), expels all air bubbles immediately (room air has pO2 ~150 mmHg and pCO2 ~0.3 mmHg), and analyzes within 15 min (or transports in ice-water slurry for up to 30 min) to halt ongoing O2 consumption and CO2/lactic acid generation by leukocytes/reticulocytes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01896","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Serum Protein Electrophoresis (SPEP)","q":"A 64-year-old man with bone pain, anaemia, and hypercalcemia has Total Serum Protein = 10.6 g/dL and Serum Albumin = 3.1 g/dL (markedly reversed A:G ratio). Cellulose acetate / agarose Serum Protein Electrophoresis (SPEP) at pH 8.6 reveals a tall, sharp, narrow-based peak (\"church spire\" M-spike) in the gamma region. What does this SPEP pattern indicate?","o":["Monoclonal gammopathy (e.g., Multiple Myeloma) due to clonal proliferation of plasma cells secreting a single immunoglobulin","Nephrotic syndrome (elevated alpha-2 macroglobulin with severe hypoalbuminemia)","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Polyclonal gammopathy (chronic liver cirrhosis with beta-gamma bridging)"],"a":0,"e":"At pH 8.6, serum proteins separate into 5 bands from anode (+) to cathode (-): Albumin (fastest), Alpha-1, Alpha-2, Beta, and Gamma. A narrow, tall, homogeneous \"M-spike\" in the gamma (or beta-gamma) zone paired with high total protein, reversed A:G ratio (\u003c1.0), CRAB symptoms, and often urinary Bence-Jones light chains confirms a monoclonal gammopathy such as Multiple Myeloma (confirmed by immunofixation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01897","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"Classification of Bacterial Culture Media","q":"Match each bacteriological culture medium with its primary functional classification and target organism: 1. Chocolate agar 2. Thiosulfate Citrate Bile Salts Sucrose (TCBS) agar 3. Selenite F broth 4. Lowenstein-Jensen (LJ) medium Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Indicator medium for Corynebacterium - Enrichment broth for Neisseria - Selective medium for Pseudomonas - Enriched medium for Clostridium","Enriched medium (hemin X and NAD V factors) for Haemophilus influenzae \u0026 Neisseria - Selective/differential medium for Vibrio cholerae (yellow sucrose-fermenting colonies) - Liquid enrichment broth for Salmonella/Shigella from stool - Selective egg-based solid medium with malachite green for Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Transport medium for viruses - Selective medium for Staphylococci - Basal medium - Anaerobic indicator medium","Selective medium for Gram-positives - Enrichment broth for anaerobes - Enriched medium for fungi - Differential medium for Streptococcus"],"a":1,"e":"Heating blood agar lyses RBCs to release intracellular Factor X (hemin) and Factor V (NAD) while inactivating NADase, creating Chocolate agar for fastidious Haemophilus and Neisseria. TCBS inhibits Gram-positives/coliforms and grows yellow Vibrio cholerae colonies. Selenite F is a liquid enrichment broth suppressing coliforms to favour Salmonella/Shigella. LJ medium uses malachite green to inhibit contaminants during slow M. tuberculosis growth.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01898","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"Laboratory Diagnosis of Enteric Fever","q":"Assertion (A): Blood culture (e.g., in brain-heart infusion or automated broth) is the gold-standard diagnostic test during the FIRST week of suspected enteric (typhoid) fever, whereas the Widal tube agglutination test typically becomes positive only from the second week onward. Reason (R): A single elevated Widal titre in an endemic country like India must be interpreted cautiously because prior TAB vaccination, past infection, or cross-reacting non-typhoidal febrile illnesses (anamnestic response) can produce false-positive titres. Choose the correct option:","o":["Both A and R are true, and R explains why a single Widal test is less reliable than blood culture/rising paired titres","Both A and R are true, but R claims Widal is superior in week 1","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Recall the BASU mnemonic for Typhoid diagnostics: Week 1 = Blood culture (~90% sensitive before antibiotics); Week 2 = Agglutination (Widal O and H antibodies appear day 7–10); Week 3 = Stool culture; Week 4 = Urine culture. Because India is typhoid-endemic, baseline O/H titres exist in healthy populations and non-specific fevers (malaria/dengue) can trigger an anamnestic rise, making a 4-fold rise in paired sera or blood culture far more diagnostic.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01899","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"Diagnostic Fungal Techniques","q":"A CSF sample from an immunocompromised HIV patient shows encapsulated budding yeast cells surrounded by clear halos on an India Ink negative stain, while a separate vaginal swab yields creamy yeast colonies that form short hyphal extensions without constriction at the origin after 2 hours incubation at 37°C in human serum (Reynolds-Braude Germ Tube test). Identify the two fungi respectively:","o":["Histoplasma capsulatum and Candida tropicalis","Aspergillus fumigatus and Mucor","Cryptococcus neoformans (India Ink halo) and Candida albicans (Germ tube positive)","Trichophyton rubrum and Cryptococcus neoformans"],"a":2,"e":"India Ink (or Nigrosin) provides a dark background that highlights the thick mucopolysaccharide capsule of Cryptococcus neoformans in CSF as a bright refractile halo around budding yeasts. The Germ Tube (Reynolds-Braude) test rapidly differentiates Candida albicans (and C. dubliniensis) from non-albicans Candida species by demonstrating true germ tubes without constriction at the mother-cell junction after 2–3 hours in serum at 37°C.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01900","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"Syphilis Serology (VDRL / RPR)","q":"In the VDRL (Venereal Disease Research Laboratory) / RPR non-treponemal slide flocculation test for syphilis using cardiolipin–lecithin–cholesterol antigen, what causes a false-negative \"Prozone phenomenon\" in undiluted serum from a patient with secondary syphilis, and how is it resolved in the lab?","o":["Lack of complement in patient serum; add guinea pig complement","Excess antigen destroys antibodies; heat serum at 100°C","Bacterial contamination; filter through 0.22 micron filter","Excessively high antibody titre prevents lattice formation in undiluted serum; resolved by performing serial 2-fold serum dilutions (1:2, 1:4, 1:8, etc.)"],"a":3,"e":"In secondary syphilis (or HIV co-infection), overwhelming anti-cardiolipin (reagin) antibody excess saturates all antigen binding sites, preventing visible cross-linked flocculation lattice formation (\"prozone phenomenon\") on neat serum. Diluting the serum serially restores the optimal zone of equivalence, turning the test strongly reactive. (Note: VDRL requires heating serum at 56°C for 30 min to inactivate complement, whereas RPR uses unheated serum with charcoal particles).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01901","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Parasitology","topic":"Entamoeba histolytica vs Giardia lamblia","q":"On examining a fresh warm normal-saline stool wet mount from a patient with bloody mucoid dysentery, the technician sees actively motile amoebic trophozoites exhibiting directional pseudopodia and containing phagocytosed host red blood cells inside their cytoplasm, while an iodine mount shows spherical cysts with 1–4 nuclei and rounded cigar-shaped chromatoid bars. Which pathogen is confirmed?","o":["Entamoeba histolytica","Entamoeba coli (non-pathogenic commensal)","Giardia lamblia","Balantidium coli"],"a":0,"e":"Ingested RBCs (erythrophagocytosis) within an actively motile trophozoite are pathognomonic for pathogenic Entamoeba histolytica (distinguishing it morphologically from non-invasive E. dispar/E. coli). Its mature infective cyst is quadrinucleate (\u003c=4 nuclei) with a central karyosome and rounded chromatoid bars, whereas commensal E. coli cysts have up to 8 nuclei with splintered chromatoid bars.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01902","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Parasitology","topic":"Lymphatic Filariasis Microscopy","q":"In India’s endemic regions, why should peripheral blood smears for microfilariae be collected between 10:00 PM and 2:00 AM (unless a daytime DEC provocation test is performed), and how are sheathed microfilariae of Wuchereria bancrofti distinguished microscopically from Brugia malayi?","o":["Because microfilariae exhibit nocturnal periodicity (circulating in peripheral blood at night when Culex mosquitoes bite); W. bancrofti has smooth curves and a pointed tail tip FREE of nuclei, whereas B. malayi has kinky curves and TWO discrete terminal nuclei at the tail tip","Because mosquitoes only breed at midnight; W. bancrofti is unsheathed while B. malayi has no nuclei","Because microfilariae survive only below 15°C; W. bancrofti has nuclei extending to the tail tip","Because daytime blood lyses Giemsa stain; both species have identical tail tips"],"a":0,"e":"During daytime, W. bancrofti and B. malayi microfilariae retreat into pulmonary capillaries; between 10 PM and 2 AM they surge into peripheral blood (\"nocturnal periodicity\") coinciding with nocturnal vector feeding (or 45 min after oral 100 mg DEC provocation during daytime). On Giemsa/JSB thick smear, W. bancrofti shows graceful curves and a nucleus-free tail tip, whereas B. malayi shows secondary kinks and 2 distinct terminal/subterminal tail nuclei.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01903","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Histopathology and Cytology","topic":"Paraffin Processing and Sectioning Artifacts","q":"While cutting 4-micron paraffin tissue ribbons on a rotary microtome, the histotechnician notices alternating thick-and-thin horizontal bands (\"Venetian blind\" chatter) across the sections and longitudinal score lines splitting the ribbon. What are the most likely mechanical causes and corrective actions?","o":["Too much DPX mountant; wash slide in absolute alcohol","Loose knife/block chuck clamp, excessive clearance angle, or overly hard/brittle tissue causing chatter plus a nicked/dirty knife edge causing longitudinal tears; tighten clamps, adjust clearance angle (3°–8°), surface-decalcify/cool block on ice, and shift to a fresh blade","Formol-ether concentration error; repeat fixation in saline","Insufficient hematoxylin time; re-stain in eosin for 10 minutes"],"a":1,"e":"In rotary microtomy, horizontal vibration ripples (\"chatter\" / Venetian blind effect) arise from loose blade/cassette holders, steep knife clearance angle, rapid cutting stroke, or over-dehydrated/calcified brittle blocks (fixed by tightening clamps, setting 3°–8° bevel/clearance angle, cooling/hydrating block face). Longitudinal splits/scratches across every section indicate a nicked blade edge or hard calcium/suture particle caught on the knife edge.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01904","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Histopathology and Cytology","topic":"Diagnostic Special Stains","q":"Match each special histopathology stain with its target tissue constituent and characteristic diagnostic colour: 1. Periodic Acid-Schiff (PAS) 2. Masson’s Trichrome 3. Congo Red (viewed under polarized light) 4. Fite-Faraco stain Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Melanin (blue) - Iron (green) - Mucin (black) - Collagen (purple)","Elastic fibres (black) - Neutral lipids (red) - Reticulin fibres (silver black) - Calcium salts (orange)","Glycogen, fungal cell walls, \u0026 glomerular basement membrane (magenta/bright pink) - Collagen fibres (blue/green) vs muscle/cytoplasm (red) - Amyloid deposits (apple-green birefringence) - Mycobacterium leprae (red acid-fast bacilli using weak acid \u0026 peanut-oil/xylene protection)","Collagen (apple-green) - Amyloid (magenta) - Glycogen (blue) - Fungal hyphae (yellow)"],"a":2,"e":"(1) PAS oxidizes 1,2-glycols to aldehydes reactive with Schiff reagent, staining glycogen (diastase-sensitive), neutral mucins, basement membranes, and fungi magenta; (2) Masson’s Trichrome differentiates blue/green collagen from red smooth muscle/keratin in fibrosis evaluation; (3) Congo Red binds beta-pleated sheets of amyloid producing pathognomonic apple-green birefringence under polarized light; (4) Fite-Faraco protects delicate M. leprae cell-wall lipids with peanut oil/xylene and uses weak 5% H2SO4 decolorization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01905","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Physical Examination of Urine","q":"A technician measures urine specific gravity using a urinometer calibrated at 15°C. The urine sample is at 24°C and gives a raw urinometer reading of 1.015. What is the temperature-corrected specific gravity, and what clinical condition is indicated if a patient’s serial urine specific gravity remains fixed at 1.010 (isosthenuria)?","o":["1.012; Diabetes insipidus","1.018 (adding 0.001 for every 3°C above calibration temperature); End-stage chronic renal failure (loss of renal concentrating and diluting ability)","1.015; Acute cystitis","1.024; Nephrotic syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Urinometer readings require temperature correction: add 0.001 for every 3°C above the instrument’s calibration temperature (here 24°C - 15°C = +9°C -\u003e +0.003 -\u003e 1.015 + 0.003 = 1.018), and subtract 0.001 for every 3°C below (plus subtract 0.003 per 1 g/dL protein and 0.004 per 1 g/dL glucose). Fixed specific gravity around 1.010—matching protein-free glomerular ultrafiltrate—is isosthenuria, signifying severe chronic renal tubular damage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01906","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Faecal Occult Blood Testing (FOBT)","q":"Why must a patient avoid red meat, turnips/horseradish, aspirin, and high-dose Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) for 48–72 hours prior to a traditional chemical (Guaiac or Benzidine/Orthotolidine) Faecal Occult Blood Test (FOBT)?","o":["Red meat and plant peroxidases cause FALSE-POSITIVE pseudoperoxidase reactions, whereas Vitamin C (a reducing agent) causes FALSE-NEGATIVE results","Red meat causes false-negative results; Vitamin C turns the stool bright blue","Both red meat and Vitamin C only affect bacterial culture","Aspirin permanently destroys human haemoglobin inside the colon"],"a":0,"e":"Chemical FOBTs rely on the pseudoperoxidase activity of heme converting H2O2 + chromogen (guaiac/benzidine) to a blue-green oxidized dye. Dietary animal myoglobin/haemoglobin (red meat) and vegetable peroxidases (horseradish, turnips, broccoli) yield false positives, while NSAIDs induce gastric micro-bleeding. Conversely, strong antioxidants like Vitamin C (\u003e250 mg/day) inhibit dye oxidation, yielding false negatives. (Immunochemical FIT tests using anti-human globin antibodies eliminate dietary interference).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01907","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Pathology","topic":"Synovial Fluid Analysis","q":"Joint aspirate from an acutely swollen, red, painful first metatarsophalangeal (great toe) joint shows thin watery viscosity (string test \u003c 2 cm), poor friable mucin clot, WBC count 32,000/µL (85% neutrophils), and needle-shaped intracellular crystals exhibiting STRONG NEGATIVE birefringence (yellow when aligned parallel to the slow axis of the red compensator) under polarized light. Which crystal and diagnosis are confirmed?","o":["Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate (CPPD) crystals; Pseudogout","Calcium oxalate envelope crystals; Septic arthritis","Cholesterol rhomboid notched plates; Osteoarthritis","Monosodium urate (MSU) crystals; Acute Gouty Arthritis"],"a":3,"e":"Synovial fluid in acute gout reveals inflammatory turbidity (decreased hyaluronic acid -\u003e poor string test and friable Ropes mucin clot) and needle-shaped Monosodium Urate (MSU) crystals showing strong negative birefringence (yellow parallel to compensator axis, blue perpendicular). In contrast, CPPD crystals of Pseudogout are rhomboid/rod-shaped with weak POSITIVE birefringence (blue when parallel).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01908","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"EDTA-Induced Pseudothrombocytopenia","q":"An automated 5-part haematology analyzer flags a low platelet count of 28,000/µL on an EDTA blood sample from an asymptomatic pre-employment checkup patient with no bruising or bleeding. On reviewing the Leishman-stained peripheral smear, the technician sees large platelet clumps at the feathered edge and platelet satellitism around neutrophils. What is the correct laboratory action?","o":["Recognize EDTA-induced pseudothrombocytopenia; do NOT release the spurious low count, request a fresh sample collected in 3.2% Sodium Citrate (light-blue) or Heparin tube, re-run promptly, and multiply the citrate platelet count by 1.1 (to correct for 9:1 dilution)","Release 28,000/µL immediately as critical thrombocytopenia requiring platelet transfusion","Vortex the EDTA tube for 5 minutes and report the average","Dilute the same EDTA sample 1:10 with distilled water"],"a":0,"e":"In ~0.1% of individuals, naturally occurring cold-reactive IgG/IgM autoantibodies recognize cryptic GPIIb/IIIa epitopes exposed when EDTA chelates calcium, causing platelet clumping or neutrophil satellitism so the analyzer miscounts clumps as WBCs. Never release a critical low platelet count without smear review! Re-collecting in 3.2% trisodium citrate (9 parts blood : 1 part citrate -\u003e multiply reading by 1.1) resolves the artifact.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01909","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Mentzer Index Calculation","q":"An asymptomatic young adult undergoing pre-marital screening has the following CBC: Hb = 11.2 g/dL, MCV = 62 fL, RBC count = 5.6 million/µL, and RDW = 13.4% (normal). Calculate the Mentzer Index and identify the most likely diagnosis.","o":["Mentzer Index = 5.5; favours Megaloblastic Anaemia","Mentzer Index = 11.1 (\u003c 13); favours Beta-Thalassemia Trait","Mentzer Index = 14.5; favours Aplastic Anaemia","Mentzer Index = 18.0 (\u003e 13); favours Iron Deficiency Anaemia"],"a":1,"e":"Mentzer Index = MCV (in fL) / RBC count (in millions/µL). Here, 62 / 5.6 = 11.07 (≈ 11.1). Because beta-thalassemia trait maintains high marrow erythropoiesis (high RBC count despite small microcytes and normal RDW), Mentzer Index \u003c 13 strongly suggests thalassemia trait (confirmed by HbA2 \u003e 3.5% on HPLC), whereas in Iron Deficiency Anaemia both RBC count and MCV fall with elevated RDW (Mentzer \u003e 13).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01910","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Haematology","topic":"Acute Leukemia Cytochemistry","q":"A peripheral blood and bone marrow aspirate smear shows 65% blasts with delicate chromatin, prominent nucleoli, and occasional slender azurophilic needle-like cytoplasmic inclusions (Auer rods). On cytochemical staining, the blasts are strongly positive for Myeloperoxidase (MPO) and Sudan Black B (SBB). Which diagnosis is confirmed?","o":["Infectious mononucleosis","Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)","Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)","Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)"],"a":2,"e":"Auer rods (fused primary azurophilic granules) and \u003e= 3% blast positivity for Myeloperoxidase (MPO) or Sudan Black B (SBB) are definitive hallmarks of myeloid lineage, confirming Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) over ALL. In contrast, lymphoblasts in ALL are MPO/SBB-negative, lack Auer rods, and often display coarse \"block\" positivity with Periodic Acid-Schiff (PAS).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01911","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Blood Banking and Immunohaematology","topic":"ABO vs Rh Hemolytic Disease of the Newborn","q":"Assertion (A): ABO Hemolytic Disease of the Newborn (HDN) frequently occurs in a firstborn Group A or B infant of a Group O mother, whereas RhD HDN rarely affects a first pregnancy unless the mother was previously sensitized. Reason (R): Group O individuals naturally possess preformed IgG anti-A,B antibodies capable of crossing the placenta during a first pregnancy, whereas anti-D is an immune IgG antibody formed only after prior exposure to RhD-positive RBCs. Choose the correct option:","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Group O mothers naturally carry IgG anti-A,B antibodies (unlike Group A/B individuals whose anti-B/anti-A are predominantly pentameric IgM that cannot cross the placenta), enabling mild-to-moderate ABO HDN (with spherocytes and weak/variable DAT) even in the first pregnancy. Conversely, anti-D requires primary alloimmunization from a prior Rh+ delivery, abortion, or transfusion, making RhD HDN classically affect subsequent Rh+ pregnancies.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01912","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Blood Banking and Immunohaematology","topic":"Plasma Component Preparation and Indications","q":"Match each frozen blood component with its principal coagulation contents and storage/thawing standard: 1. Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP) 2. Cryoprecipitate (Anti-Haemophilic Factor) Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2):","o":["Stored at +2°C to +6°C for 35 days - Thawed at 60°C water bath","Contains platelets only at +22°C - Contains concentrated RBCs in CPDA-1 at +4°C","Lacks Factors V and VIII - Rich only in Albumin and IgG","Prepared within 6–8 hours of collection, stored at \u003c= -30°C (up to 1 year), contains ALL stable and labile coagulation factors (including V \u0026 VIII), thawed at 30–37°C and transfused within 24 hrs (ideally 4–6 hrs) - Cold-insoluble precipitate obtained by thawing FFP at +4°C, rich in Factor VIII, von Willebrand factor, Fibrinogen, and Factor XIII"],"a":3,"e":"FFP separated and frozen rapidly within 6–8 hours preserves labile Factors V and VIII alongside all plasma clotting proteins (stored at -30°C for 12 months; -18°C to -30°C shorter shelf life per NBTC). Slowly thawing FFP at +1°C to +6°C leaves behind Cryoprecipitate, concentrated in Fibrinogen (\u003e=150 mg/unit), Factor VIII (\u003e=80 IU/unit), vWF, and Factor XIII (used in hypofibrinogenemia/DIC/massive transfusion).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01913","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Molar Solution Preparation","q":"How many grams of analytical-grade Sodium Chloride (NaCl, Molecular Weight = 58.5 g/mol) must be weighed and dissolved in distilled water to make exactly 500 mL of a 0.2 M NaCl solution?","o":["5.85 grams","11.7 grams","2.925 grams","29.25 grams"],"a":0,"e":"Mass required (g) = Molarity (mol/L) × Molecular Weight (g/mol) × Volume (L). Here, Volume = 500 mL = 0.5 L. Therefore, Mass = 0.2 × 58.5 × 0.5 = 0.1 × 58.5 = 5.85 grams of NaCl made up to 500 mL final volume in a volumetric flask.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01914","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Chylomicron and VLDL Differentiation","q":"A fasting serum/plasma sample appears milky lipemic (Triglycerides = 1,400 mg/dL). After leaving the tube upright undisturbed at 4°C in a refrigerator for 18 hours, the technician observes a thick, creamy white floating layer at the top meniscus with a completely crystal-clear infranatant below. Which lipoprotein abnormality does this demonstrate?","o":["Elevated HDL only","Elevated Chylomicrons only (Type I hyperlipoproteinemia)","Elevated LDL only (Type IIa)","Elevated VLDL only (Type IV hyperlipoproteinemia)"],"a":1,"e":"Due to very low density (\u003c 0.95 g/mL) and large particle size, Chylomicrons float overnight at 4°C to form a distinct creamy top layer. In Type I (lipoprotein lipase deficiency), only chylomicrons are elevated, leaving the underlying infranatant clear. In Type IV (elevated VLDL), the entire plasma remains uniformly turbid without a creamy top layer; Type V shows BOTH a creamy top layer and a turbid infranatant.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01915","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Serum Calcium Anticoagulant Restriction","q":"Why must blood specimens collected in EDTA, Sodium Citrate, or Potassium Oxalate tubes be REJECTED for Serum Calcium and Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP) estimation?","o":["They convert calcium into insoluble glucose complexes","They falsely double serum calcium to \u003e 20 mg/dL","EDTA, citrate, and oxalate chelate/precipitate Ca2+ ions (yielding falsely undetectable calcium) and also strip essential divalent metal cofactors (Mg2+/Zn2+) inhibiting Alkaline Phosphatase","They only interfere with serum bilirubin"],"a":2,"e":"Total serum calcium (o-cresolphthalein complexone or Arsenazo III methods) and ionized calcium require plain serum (red/yellow SST) or lithium-heparin plasma collected without prolonged tourniquet venous stasis. EDTA, citrate, and oxalate bind/precipitate Ca2+ and chelate Mg2+/Zn2+ cofactors required by Alkaline Phosphatase (and Mg2+ itself).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01916","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Clinical Biochemistry","topic":"Glycated Haemoglobin (HbA1c)","q":"A 45-year-old diabetic man with hereditary spherocytosis (chronic hemolytic anaemia with RBC lifespan reduced to ~35 days) has fasting blood glucose readings consistently around 190–210 mg/dL, yet his HbA1c returns surprisingly low at 5.2%. What explains this discrepancy?","o":["HbA1c measures plasma glucose from the previous 24 hours only","Non-enzymatic glycation of haemoglobin accelerates during hemolysis","High blood glucose destroys HbA1c in the EDTA tube","HbA1c reflects average blood glucose over the normal ~120-day RBC lifespan; accelerated RBC turnover/hemolysis reduces erythrocyte exposure time to glucose, causing a FALSELY LOW HbA1c"],"a":3,"e":"Glucose irreversibly and non-enzymatically binds to the N-terminal valine of beta-globin chains proportionally to ambient blood glucose over the 8–12 week (120-day) RBC lifespan. Conditions shortening RBC survival (hemolytic anaemia, recent acute haemorrhage, recent blood transfusion, hypersplenism, erythropoietin therapy) yield falsely low HbA1c (fructosamine/glycated albumin is used instead), whereas iron/B12 deficiency anaemia prolongs mean RBC age and falsely elevates HbA1c.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01917","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"Rapid Biochemical Identification Tests","q":"Match each diagnostic bacteriological test with its hallmark positive reaction and classic target pathogen: 1. Kovacs Oxidase test (tetramethyl-p-phenylenediamine) 2. Christensen’s Urease agar test 3. Triple Sugar Iron (TSI) agar showing Alkaline slant / Acid butt (K/A) with abundant black H2S and gas 4. CAMP test on sheep blood agar Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Deep purple colour within 10 sec (Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Vibrio cholerae) - Bright pink/magenta colour (Proteus species, Helicobacter pylori) - Non-lactose fermenter producing H2S (Salmonella enterica / Proteus) - Arrowhead zone of enhanced beta-hemolysis with S. aureus (Streptococcus agalactiae / Group B Strep)","Deep purple (E. coli) - Yellow slant/butt (Pseudomonas) - Arrowhead hemolysis (S. pneumoniae) - Pink urease (S. aureus)","Bright yellow (E. coli) - Blue citrate (Klebsiella) - Red indole ring (Shigella) - Green optochin zone (S. pyogenes)","H2S blackening (Vibrio) - Pink urease (E. coli) - Purple oxidase (S. aureus) - Arrowhead hemolysis (Enterococcus)"],"a":0,"e":"(1) Cytochrome c oxidase oxidizes TMPD reagent to deep purple within 10 seconds (positive in Pseudomonas, Vibrio, Neisseria; negative in Enterobacteriaceae); (2) Urease hydrolyzes urea to alkaline ammonia turning phenol red bright pink (rapid in Proteus and H. pylori); (3) TSI K/A with H2S indicates glucose fermentation only plus H2S production (Salmonella Typhimurium/Paratyphi B; S. Typhi gives K/A with small H2S without gas); (4) CAMP factor of Group B Streptococcus synergizes with S. aureus beta-lysin forming an arrowhead zone.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01918","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"NTEP Sputum AFB Microscopy Grading","q":"When examining a Ziehl-Neelsen (ZN) stained sputum smear under a 100× oil-immersion objective according to India’s NTEP (Revised National TB Programme) grading table, the technician counts an average of 3 Acid-Fast Bacilli (AFB) per oil-immersion field after examining 50 fields. How should this smear be graded?","o":["Scanty","2+","3+","1+"],"a":1,"e":"NTEP ZN microscopy grading criteria (100× oil-immersion): Negative = 0 AFB in 100 fields; Scanty = 1–9 AFB in 100 fields (report exact count); 1+ = 10–99 AFB in 100 fields (examine 100 fields); 2+ = 1–10 AFB per field in at least 50 fields; 3+ = \u003e 10 AFB per field in at least 20 fields. An average of 3 AFB/field over 50 fields falls squarely in 2+.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01919","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae","q":"On staining a throat-swab smear from a suspected diphtheria patient with Albert’s stain (Toluidine blue + Malachite green followed by Albert’s iodine), how do Corynebacterium diphtheriae bacilli appear microscopically, and which media are used for rapid growth and selective isolation?","o":["Gram-negative kidney-shaped diplococci; Thayer-Martin medium","Encapsulated lancet diplococci; Bile esculin agar","Green slender pleomorphic bacilli in \"Chinese letter / cuneiform\" V/L arrangements with bluish-black Babes-Ernst metachromatic (volutin) granules; Loeffler’s serum slope (rapid 6–8 hr growth) and Potassium Tellurite blood agar (black colonies)","Red beaded acid-fast rods; Lowenstein-Jensen medium"],"a":2,"e":"Albert’s stain highlights polyphosphate energy reserves (Babes-Ernst / metachromatic / volutin granules) as dark bluish-black dots at the poles of green-stained club-shaped C. diphtheriae rods arranged in Chinese-letter/cuneiform patterns. Loeffler’s serum slope promotes rapid granule development in 6–8 hours, while Potassium Tellurite blood agar selectively yields grey-black colonies in 48 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01920","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Microbiology","topic":"Blood Culture Collection and Processing","q":"In adult suspected bacteraemia/sepsis, which blood culture collection practice maximizes pathogen recovery while minimizing skin-contaminant (e.g., Staphylococcus epidermidis) false positives?","o":["Inject blood directly onto a dry MacConkey agar plate at the bedside","Draw 1 mL of blood from an existing peripheral IV cannula after wiping briefly with plain tap water","Refrigerate blood culture bottles at 4°C overnight before placing in the incubator","Disinfect a fresh peripheral venipuncture site sequentially with 70% alcohol followed by 2% chlorhexidine-alcohol (or tincture of iodine) allowing complete drying, collect 10–20 mL blood per culture set (aerobic + anaerobic bottles at ~1:5 to 1:10 blood-to-broth ratio) prior to antibiotics, and incubate immediately at 35–37°C"],"a":3,"e":"Blood volume is the single most critical factor for blood culture sensitivity (10–20 mL per adult set; 1–5 mL in infants). Two-step skin antisepsis (allowing chlorhexidine/iodine full contact drying time without re-palpating the vein with ungloved/non-sterile fingers) prevents skin commensal contamination, and 1:5–1:10 dilution in SPS-containing broth neutralizes serum complement/lysozyme/antibiotics. Never refrigerate blood culture bottles!","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01921","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Parasitology","topic":"Diagnostic Urine and Cyst Parasitology","q":"Microscopic examination of a centrifuged terminal-stream urine deposit from a patient with painless terminal haematuria reveals large oval non-operculated eggs (140 × 50 µm) possessing a prominent sharp TERMINAL SPINE. Which trematode causes this finding?","o":["Schistosoma haematobium","Paragonimus westermani","Schistosoma mansoni","Fasciolopsis buski"],"a":0,"e":"Schistosoma haematobium adult worms inhabit the vesical venous plexus around the urinary bladder, laying eggs with a characteristic sharp TERMINAL spine in urine (optimal collection: midday/terminal urine between 10 AM and 2 PM). By contrast, S. mansoni eggs (in stool) have a prominent LATERAL spine, and S. japonicum eggs have a small rudimentary lateral knob.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01922","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Medical Parasitology","topic":"Coccidian Parasites in Immunocompromised Hosts","q":"To detect Cryptosporidium parvum oocysts (4–6 µm spherical acid-fast bodies) in concentrated stool smears from an HIV-positive patient with chronic watery diarrhoea, which modified acid-fast decolorizer concentration is used in the Modified Ziehl-Neelsen (Kinyoun) stain instead of the harsh 20% sulfuric acid used for Mycobacterium tuberculosis?","o":["Potassium hydroxide 20%","0.5% to 1% Sulfuric acid (H2SO4) or 1% acid-alcohol (weak acid decolorization)","Absolute acetone for 10 minutes","50% concentrated hydrochloric acid"],"a":1,"e":"Coccidian oocysts (Cryptosporidium parvum 4–6 µm, Cyclospora cayetanensis 8–10 µm, Cystoisospora belli 25–30 µm oval) are weakly acid-fast compared to mycobacteria. Using standard 20% H2SO4 strips the carbol fuchsin dye completely from oocysts; therefore, Modified ZN/Kinyoun uses gentle 0.5%–1% H2SO4 (for ~30–60 sec), staining oocysts bright pink-red against a blue/green background.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01923","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Histopathology and Cytology","topic":"Frozen Section (Cryostat) Technique","q":"When a surgeon sends a fresh breast/tumour margin specimen for rapid intraoperative Frozen Section diagnosis (within 10–15 minutes), why must the tissue NEVER be placed in 10% formalin or processed through xylene, and at what cryostat chamber temperature is fresh tissue embedded in OCT compound typically sectioned?","o":["Formalin explodes at -20°C; cryostat operates at +37°C","Xylene freezes the microtome knife; cryostat operates at -196°C continuously","Formalin and xylene take hours and dissolve lipids; rapid freezing in OCT at -20°C to -25°C solidifies water/tissue instantly for immediate cryostat cutting and rapid H\u0026E staining (also essential for Oil Red O lipid stains)","Formalin is avoided only because it stains nuclei blue prematurely"],"a":2,"e":"Frozen sectioning bypasses slow 12–24 hr fixation/dehydration/clearing/paraffin infiltration by rapidly freezing fresh unfixed tissue in Optimal Cutting Temperature (OCT) water-soluble medium inside a refrigerated cryostat (-20°C to -25°C; colder -30°C for fatty breast tissue). Besides 15-minute intraoperative tumour margin/sentinel node evaluation, frozen sections preserve neutral lipids (for Oil Red O/Sudan stains) and labile enzymes normally extracted by alcohol/xylene.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01924","course":"DMLT - Medical Laboratory Technology","subject":"Histopathology and Cytology","topic":"Antigen Retrieval and Peroxidase Blocking","q":"During Immunohistochemistry (IHC) on Formalin-Fixed Paraffin-Embedded (FFPE) tissue sections using a Horseradish Peroxidase (HRP)–DAB detection system, what is the purpose of (a) Heat-Induced Epitope Retrieval (HIER) in citrate/Tris-EDTA buffer and (b) incubating sections in 3% Hydrogen Peroxide (H2O2) before adding the primary antibody?","o":["(a) Dissolves glass slides; (b) Bleaches melanin completely to white","(a) Fixes fresh unfixed antigens; (b) Enhances endogenous RBC catalase positivity","(a) Removes paraffin wax instead of xylene; (b) Stains nuclei blue","(a) Unmasks protein epitopes cross-linked by formalin methylene bridges so antibodies can bind; (b) Quenches endogenous tissue/RBC peroxidase activity to prevent non-specific brown DAB background staining"],"a":3,"e":"Formalin fixation forms methylene cross-links that sterically mask antigenic epitopes. Microwave/pressure-cooker HIER (in pH 6.0 citrate or pH 9.0 Tris-EDTA) breaks cross-links and restores immunoreactivity. Prior to HRP-conjugated secondary detection with 3,3′-diaminobenzidine (DAB, which turns brown with H2O2), blocking endogenous pseudoperoxidase in RBCs/neutrophils/macrophages with 3% H2O2 prevents false-positive brown background.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01925","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Acute Coronary Syndrome","q":"A 58-year-old man presents 2 hours after substernal chest pain with BP 84/56 mmHg, pulse 54/min, raised JVP with Kussmaul sign, clear lung fields, and ST elevation in leads II, III, aVF (III \u003e II) plus V4R. Which immediate intervention is most appropriate?","o":["Rapid isotonic normal saline bolus and avoidance of preload-reducing nitrates","Emergency pericardiocentesis before coronary angiography","Immediate intravenous metoprolol followed by IV morphine","Intravenous nitroglycerin infusion and oral furosemide"],"a":0,"e":"ST elevation in II, III, aVF with III \u003e II and V4R ST elevation alongside hypotension, elevated JVP, Kussmaul sign, and clear lungs indicates acute inferior wall MI with right ventricular infarction (RVMI). RVMI depends critically on adequate RV preload to maintain left ventricular filling; therefore, isotonic crystalloid volume loading and primary PCI are indicated while nitrates, diuretics, and morphine must be avoided.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01926","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Bile Duct Injury","q":"On postoperative day 4 after laparoscopic cholecystectomy, a 42-year-old woman develops bilious drain output of 450 mL/day and mild jaundice. MRCP shows complete transection of the common hepatic duct 1 cm below the confluence with clips on the distal stump (Strasberg E2). What is the definitive surgical management once sepsis and biloma are controlled?","o":["Percutaneous transhepatic biliary drainage as permanent standalone therapy","Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy using the Hepp-Couinaud approach","End-to-end primary choledochocholedochostomy over a T-tube","Endoscopic biliary sphincterotomy with plastic stenting alone"],"a":1,"e":"Strasberg Type E2 represents a complete transection of the common hepatic duct less than 2 cm from the hilar confluence. Definitive reconstruction after controlling sepsis and delineating biliary anatomy is a tension-free, mucosa-to-mucosa Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy (often via the Hepp-Couinaud left duct extension).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01927","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Hypertensive Disorders of Pregnancy","q":"A 26-year-old primigravida with eclampsia on the Pritchard regimen of magnesium sulfate develops absent deep tendon reflexes, respiratory rate of 10/min, and urine output of 18 mL/hr over the last 2 hours. What is the immediate antidote and first action?","o":["Administer IV sodium bicarbonate 50 mEq and hyperventilate","Administer IV naloxone 0.4 mg and start labetalol infusion","Stop magnesium sulfate infusion and administer 10 mL of 10% calcium gluconate IV slowly over 3 minutes","Continue half-dose magnesium sulfate and give IV furosemide 40 mg"],"a":2,"e":"Loss of patellar reflexes (around 8-12 mg/dL) followed by respiratory depression (\u003c12/min, around 12-15 mg/dL) indicates magnesium toxicity, often precipitated by oliguria because magnesium is renally excreted. Immediate management requires stopping magnesium sulfate, supporting airway/breathing, and giving 1 g (10 mL of 10% solution) IV calcium gluconate slowly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01928","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Neonatal Persistent Pulmonary Hypertension","q":"A term neonate delivered through thick meconium-stained amniotic fluid develops severe hypoxemia on mechanical ventilation with a pre-ductal SpO2 of 91% and post-ductal SpO2 of 76%. Echocardiography shows normal structural cardiac anatomy with right-to-left shunting across the ductus arteriosus and tricuspid regurgitation jet velocity indicating suprasystemic pulmonary artery pressure. Which targeted pulmonary vasodilator is first-line?","o":["Oral propranolol titrated to heart rate","High-dose intravenous dexamethasone","Intravenous indomethacin to close the ductus arteriosus","Inhaled nitric oxide (iNO) at 20 ppm"],"a":3,"e":"A pre-ductal to post-ductal SpO2 gradient \u003e5-10% with structurally normal heart and right-to-left PDA shunt confirms PPHN secondary to meconium aspiration syndrome. Inhaled nitric oxide (20 ppm) selectively dilates ventilated pulmonary vasculature via cGMP without causing systemic hypotension and is the gold-standard targeted therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01929","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Compartment Syndrome","q":"Six hours after closed reduction and casting of a tibial shaft fracture, a 30-year-old man complains of severe escalating pain unrelieved by IV opioids, with excruciating pain on passive toe extension. Distal dorsalis pedis pulse is palpable. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Remove the cast and padding completely, keep limb at heart level, and prepare for urgent fasciotomy","Elevate the leg above heart level and apply ice packs for 4 hours","Reassure the patient because palpable distal pulses exclude compartment syndrome","Wait for paresthesia or motor paralysis before splitting the cast"],"a":0,"e":"Pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch are the earliest and most reliable clinical signs of acute compartment syndrome; palpable distal pulses do NOT rule it out because compartment pressures exceed capillary perfusion pressure long before occluding major arteries. All circumferential dressings/casts must be bivalved and removed down to skin, the limb kept at heart level (not elevated), and urgent fasciotomy performed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01930","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Acute Liver Failure (ALF)","q":"A 26-year-old woman develops acute liver failure from antitubercular therapy (isoniazid-rifampicin hepatotoxicity). Ten days after onset of jaundice, she develops Grade III hepatic encephalopathy, INR 4.2, and serum total bilirubin 21.4 mg/dL (serum creatinine 1.1 mg/dL, arterial pH 7.36). According to King\u0027s College Hospital criteria for non-acetaminophen ALF, what is the prognostic implication and management?","o":["Meets \u003e= 3 criteria (unfavourable aetiology, jaundice-to-encephalopathy \u003e 7 days, INR \u003e 3.5, bilirubin \u003e 17.5 mg/dL); urgent liver transplantation listing plus IV N-acetylcysteine","Restart antitubercular drugs at half-dose immediately","Administer IV vitamin K 10 mg daily and wait 4 weeks for INR normalisation","Does not meet transplant criteria because arterial pH is \u003e 7.30; manage with oral lactulose alone"],"a":0,"e":"For non-acetaminophen acute liver failure, King\u0027s College criteria for urgent liver transplantation are INR \u003e 6.5 alone OR \u003e= 3 of the following 5 variables: (1) Age \u003c10 or \u003e40 years; (2) Unfavourable aetiology (non-A/non-B hepatitis, halothane, idiosyncratic drug reactions); (3) Jaundice-to-encephalopathy interval \u003e 7 days; (4) INR \u003e 3.5; (5) Serum bilirubin \u003e 17.5 mg/dL (300 micromol/L). She meets 4 criteria, indicating high mortality without transplant; early IV N-acetylcysteine also improves transplant-free survival in early non-acetaminophen ALF.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01931","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Acute Colonic Pseudo-Obstruction","q":"A 74-year-old man recovering in the ICU 4 days after hip fracture surgery develops massive painless abdominal distension without peritonitis. Contrast CT abdomen shows marked colonic dilatation with a caecal diameter of 11.5 cm and NO mechanical obstructing lesion or pneumoperitoneum. Electrolytes (K+, Mg2+) are corrected and a rectal tube fails after 48 hours. What is the pharmacological therapy of choice (under telemetry with atropine at bedside)?","o":["High-dose oral polyethylene glycol purgative","Slow IV Neostigmine 2.0 mg over 3-5 minutes","Oral loperamide and morphine PCA escalation","Emergency total abdominal colectomy with end ileostomy"],"a":1,"e":"Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome) causes massive non-mechanical colonic dilatation (risking caecal perforation if diameter \u003e= 12 cm). Once mechanical obstruction, ischaemia/perforation, and electrolyte abnormalities/offending opioids/anticholinergics are addressed, slow IV Neostigmine (2 mg over 3-5 min under continuous ECG monitoring with atropine ready for bradycardia) achieves rapid colonic decompression in \u003e80-90% of patients; endoscopic decompression is used if neostigmine fails or is contraindicated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01932","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Assisted Reproductive Technology Complications","q":"Eight days after hCG trigger and oocyte retrieval for IVF, a 29-year-old woman with PCOS presents with tense ascites, dyspnoea, bilateral ovarian enlargement (11 cm), haematocrit 49%, serum albumin 2.4 g/dL, Na+ 128 mEq/L, and oliguria (urine output 20 mL/h). Which therapeutic combination is essential for severe OHSS?","o":["Withhold anticoagulation and restrict all fluid intake to \u003c 500 mL/day","Immediate high-dose IV furosemide 80 mg BID as sole therapy without fluids or anticoagulation","Isotonic crystalloid/colloid volume optimisation, LMWH thromboprophylaxis, and ultrasound-guided paracentesis for tense ascites/oliguria","Emergency bilateral oophorectomy to remove hyperstimulated cysts"],"a":2,"e":"Severe OHSS is driven by VEGF-mediated capillary hyperpermeability leading to massive third-spacing (ascites, pleural effusion), intravascular haemoconcentration (Hct \u003e 45%), hypercoagulability (arterial/venous thrombosis risk), and prerenal oliguria. Management requires careful isotonic fluid/albumin resuscitation, mandatory LMWH thromboprophylaxis, and ultrasound-guided paracentesis to relieve abdominal compartment pressure and restore renal perfusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01933","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Kawasaki Disease","q":"A 3-year-old boy with complete Kawasaki disease receives IVIG (2 g/kg over 12 hours) and high-dose aspirin on day 6 of illness. Forty-eight hours after completion of the IVIG infusion, his fever spikes to 39.2 degrees C persistently with ongoing irritability and conjunctival injection (IVIG resistance). What is the recommended next step in management?","o":["Wait 4 weeks before reconsidering anti-inflammatory therapy","Stop aspirin and discharge on oral paracetamol alone","Switch immediately to IV vancomycin and meropenem for 14 days without immunomodulation","Administer a second dose of IVIG (2 g/kg) and/or adjunctive corticosteroid (pulse methylprednisolone) or infliximab, with repeat echocardiography"],"a":3,"e":"Persistent or recrudescent fever \u003e= 36 hours after completing the initial 2 g/kg IVIG infusion defines IVIG-resistant (refractory) Kawasaki disease (~10-20% of cases), which carries a substantially higher risk of coronary artery aneurysms. Standard rescue is a second infusion of IVIG (2 g/kg) and/or pulse IV methylprednisolone (30 mg/kg/day for 3 days) or TNF-alpha blockade (infliximab), accompanied by serial echocardiographic surveillance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01934","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Pheochromocytoma","q":"A 39-year-old woman with a 4.5 cm right adrenal pheochromocytoma (markedly elevated plasma metanephrines, paroxysmal BP 210/120 mmHg, HR 118/min) is scheduled for laparoscopic adrenalectomy in 3 weeks. Which preoperative pharmacological sequence is MANDATORY to prevent fatal intraoperative hypertensive crisis and pulmonary oedema?","o":["Initiate alpha-adrenergic blockade (phenoxybenzamine or prazosin/doxazosin) plus liberal salt/fluid expansion for 10-14 days FIRST, adding a beta-blocker ONLY after adequate alpha blockade","Perform fine-needle aspiration biopsy of the adrenal tumour on the morning of surgery","Give no antihypertensives preoperatively to preserve surgical catecholamine responsiveness","Start high-dose IV propranolol on Day 1 for heart rate control, adding phenoxybenzamine 1 day before surgery"],"a":0,"e":"In pheochromocytoma, initiating a beta-blocker BEFORE alpha-blockade blocks beta-2-mediated peripheral vasodilation while leaving alpha-1-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed, precipitating malignant hypertension, acute LV failure, and pulmonary oedema. Standard preparation starts oral alpha-blockade (phenoxybenzamine or selective alpha-1 blocker) for 10-14 days with high salt/fluid intake to restore contracted plasma volume, followed by beta-blockade for tachycardia/arrhythmias (meeting Roizen criteria).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01935","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Opportunistic Pulmonary Infections","q":"A 48-year-old man with acute myeloid leukaemia and prolonged profound neutropenia (ANC 80/mm3) develops pleuritic chest pain, fever unresponsive to broad-spectrum antibacterials, and minor haemoptysis. High-resolution CT chest reveals two solid pulmonary nodules each surrounded by a rim of ground-glass attenuation (\u0027CT halo sign\u0027). What does this sign represent pathologically and which antifungal is first-line?","o":["Simple healed tuberculosis granuloma; no antifungal required","Early angioinvasive pulmonary aspergillosis with peri-lesional alveolar haemorrhage; start IV/oral Voriconazole (or isavuconazole)","Pulmonary oedema; IV furosemide alone","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia; high-dose oral fluconazole"],"a":1,"e":"In severely neutropenic patients, the \u0027CT halo sign\u0027—a macronodule surrounded by a perimeter of ground-glass opacity representing haemorrhagic infarction from fungal angioinvasion—is an early hallmark of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis (whereas the \u0027air-crescent sign\u0027 typically appears later during neutrophil recovery as necrotic lung retracts). Prompt empirical therapy with Voriconazole (or isavuconazole) significantly improves survival.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01936","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Fat Embolism Syndrome (FES)","q":"Thirty-six hours after sustaining a closed bilateral femoral shaft fracture managed initially with skin traction, a 25-year-old man develops sudden tachypnoea, hypoxaemia (PaO2 52 mmHg on room air), confusion, fever (38.8 degrees C), tachycardia (128/min), thrombocytopenia (92,000/mm3), and non-palpable petechiae across the anterior axillary folds and conjunctivae. Chest X-ray shows diffuse bilateral \u0027snowstorm\u0027 infiltrates. What is the diagnosis and primary management?","o":["Delirium tremens; oral diazepam and continued prolonged traction for 6 weeks","Massive pulmonary thromboembolism; immediate systemic alteplase thrombolysis","Fat Embolism Syndrome (FES); respiratory/critical care support (O2/ventilation as needed) and early definitive fracture fixation","Meningococcaemia; immediate intrathecal penicillin"],"a":2,"e":"Development within 24-72 hours of long-bone/pelvic fractures of the classic triad—respiratory insufficiency, neurological dysfunction, and axillary/subconjunctival/chest petechiae (Gurd\u0027s Major criteria)—plus tachycardia, fever, and thrombocytopenia diagnoses Fat Embolism Syndrome. Management is intensive supportive oxygenation/ventilation and prompt fracture stabilisation (limiting ongoing marrow fat embolisation); systemic heparin and routine high-dose steroids lack proven mortality benefit and carry risks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01937","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Juvenile Nasopharyngeal Angiofibroma (JNA)","q":"A 14-year-old boy presents with progressive unilateral nasal obstruction and recurrent severe spontaneous epistaxis for 8 months. Nasal endoscopy shows a lobulated, reddish-pink vascular mass filling the posterior nasal cavity and nasopharynx. Contrast-enhanced CT shows an intensely enhancing mass widening the sphenopalatine foramen with anterior bowing of the posterior wall of the maxillary sinus (Holman-Miller sign). What is the critical diagnostic rule and standard preoperative adjunct?","o":["Pack the nose with bismuth iodoform paraffin paste for 6 months until spontaneous regression","Treat with 60 Gy external-beam radiotherapy as routine first-line therapy for all Stage I tumours","Perform immediate outpatient punch biopsy under local anaesthesia before imaging","Avoid outpatient punch biopsy (risks torrential haemorrhage); perform preoperative angiography and transarterial embolisation 24-48 hours before surgical excision"],"a":3,"e":"Recurrent massive epistaxis in an adolescent male with a posterior nasal/nasopharyngeal vascular mass, sphenopalatine foramen widening, and the Holman-Miller sign (antral sign) is diagnostic of Juvenile Nasopharyngeal Angiofibroma (JNA). Because JNA consists of thin-walled endothelial-lined vascular spaces lacking a muscular coat, office biopsy causes life-threatening bleeding and is contraindicated. Standard treatment is preoperative feeding-vessel embolisation (commonly internal maxillary artery) 24-48 hours prior to endoscopic/open resection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01938","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Infective Endocarditis","q":"A 52-year-old man with Enterococcus faecalis native aortic valve endocarditis has received appropriate IV ampicillin plus ceftriaxone for 7 days. Transoesophageal echocardiography shows severe acute aortic regurgitation with pulmonary oedema refractory to diuretics, plus a 14 mm mobile vegetation and a perivalvular aortic root abscess with new-onset PR prolongation (260 ms). What is the definitive management?","o":["Urgent cardiac surgical valve replacement and debridement of the aortic root abscess alongside ongoing IV antibiotics","Add oral aspirin and clopidogrel to shrink the 14 mm vegetation","Continue medical antibiotic therapy alone for 6 weeks and defer surgery","Implant a permanent pacemaker alone without addressing the aortic valve"],"a":0,"e":"This patient meets multiple Class I indications for urgent/early surgery in infective endocarditis: (1) Valve dysfunction causing refractory heart failure/pulmonary oedema; (2) Locally uncontrolled infection—paravalvular aortic root abscess with conduction block; and (3) Large mobile vegetation (\u003e10 mm) at high embolic risk. Delaying surgery in aortic root abscess with acute severe AR carries catastrophic mortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01939","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Organophosphate Poisoning","q":"A 30-year-old farmer admitted after chlorpyrifos (organophosphate) ingestion was successfully atropinised and given pralidoxime on Day 1, with complete resolution of bronchorrhoea and miosis. On Day 3, while fully conscious and without excessive secretions, he abruptly develops inability to lift his head off the pillow (neck flexor weakness), proximal limb weakness, cranial nerve palsies, and rapidly falling tidal volumes (SpO2 85%). What is the diagnosis and critical intervention?","o":["Myasthenia gravis; oral prednisolone 60 mg daily","Intermediate syndrome; immediate endotracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation (unresponsive to extra atropine or pralidoxime)","Delayed organophosphate-induced neuropathy (OPIDN); IV pyridostigmine bolus","Rebound muscarinic cholinergic crisis; double the atropine infusion rate without intubation"],"a":1,"e":"Intermediate syndrome occurs 24-96 hours after acute organophosphate poisoning (after cholinergic crisis resolves and before OPIDN at 2-4 weeks) due to prolonged nicotinic receptor desensitisation/dysfunction at the neuromuscular junction. It manifests as cranial nerve palsies, neck flexor weakness, proximal limb weakness, and acute respiratory muscle paralysis. Crucially, intermediate syndrome does NOT respond to atropine (which blocks muscarinic receptors only) or delayed oximes; prompt mechanical ventilation is life-saving.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01940","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis","q":"A 24-year-old man with type 1 diabetes presents with Kussmaul breathing, blood glucose 460 mg/dL, arterial pH 7.12, HCO3- 9 mEq/L, positive serum ketones, and serum potassium 2.9 mEq/L. After starting isotonic normal saline, what is the most appropriate next step regarding insulin and potassium therapy?","o":["Hold insulin infusion temporarily and administer IV potassium chloride 20-40 mEq/L until serum K+ rises above 3.3 mEq/L","Start an immediate IV regular insulin bolus of 0.15 U/kg before checking or replacing potassium","Administer IV sodium bicarbonate 100 mEq bolus to raise pH above 7.30 before potassium replacement","Give oral potassium citrate syrup and start subcutaneous glargine immediately"],"a":0,"e":"In DKA, total body potassium is severely depleted despite variable serum levels. When initial serum K+ is \u003c3.3 mEq/L, starting insulin drives potassium intracellularly and precipitates fatal hypokalemic ventricular arrhythmias or respiratory muscle paralysis. Insulin must be withheld while IV KCl (20-40 mEq/hr via fluid) restores serum K+ \u003e=3.3 mEq/L.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01941","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Acute Pancreatitis","q":"A 48-year-old man with gallstone acute necrotizing pancreatitis develops persistent fever (38.9 degrees C), leukocytosis, and gas bubbles within a well-demarcated retroperitoneal collection on contrast CT at week 4 of illness. He is hemodynamically stable on broad-spectrum carbapenems. What is the optimal modern surgical management strategy?","o":["Withhold all drainage permanently and continue antibiotics alone for 6 months","Routine prophylactic surgical debridement in week 1 of sterile necrosis","Step-up approach starting with CT-guided percutaneous or endoscopic ultrasound-guided drainage, reserving minimally invasive necrosectomy only if clinical failure persists","Immediate emergency open laparotomy and total pancreatectomy on the day of CT"],"a":2,"e":"Gas within walled-off pancreatic necrosis at week 4 confirms infected necrosis. Per the PANTER trial and WSES/IAP guidelines, a delayed (\u003e=4 weeks) step-up approach—beginning with percutaneous catheter drainage or endoscopic transluminal drainage, followed by VARD or endoscopic necrosectomy only if sepsis persists—significantly lowers morbidity and mortality compared with upfront open necrosectomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01942","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Ectopic Pregnancy","q":"Which of the following hemodynamically stable patients with an unruptured tubal ectopic pregnancy is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to systemic methotrexate therapy?","o":["A patient with minimal free fluid confined to the pouch of Douglas and stable hemoglobin 11.8 g/dL","A patient who desires future fertility and can adhere to day-4 and day-7 beta-hCG follow-up","A patient with a 2.5 cm unruptured ectopic mass, absent fetal cardiac activity, beta-hCG 1,800 mIU/mL, and normal LFT/RFT","A patient with an adnexal gestational sac of 2.8 cm showing positive fetal cardiac activity, beta-hCG 3,200 mIU/mL, and serum creatinine 2.1 mg/dL"],"a":3,"e":"Renal insufficiency (elevated creatinine impairs methotrexate clearance, risking severe pancytopenia/mucositis) is an absolute contraindication to methotrexate, alongside hemodynamic instability, breastfeeding, immunodeficiency, active peptic ulcer, hepatic dysfunction, and intrauterine co-pregnancy. Positive embryonic cardiac activity and high beta-hCG (\u003e5000 mIU/mL) are also major predictors of medical failure/relative contraindications favoring laparoscopy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01943","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Febrile Neutropenia","q":"A 7-year-old boy undergoing induction chemotherapy for acute lymphoblastic leukemia presents with a single oral temperature of 38.6 degrees C, HR 128/min, BP 98/64 mmHg, and absolute neutrophil count (ANC) of 180/mm3. After obtaining blood cultures from peripheral vein and central venous catheter, what is the immediate next step?","o":["Administer empiric IV antipseudomonal beta-lactam monotherapy (e.g., cefepime or piperacillin-tazobactam) within 60 minutes of presentation","Administer intramuscular filgrastim alone and observe without antimicrobials","Discharge home on oral amoxicillin-clavulanate and recheck CBC after 72 hours","Wait for blood culture growth and susceptibility results before starting any antibiotics"],"a":0,"e":"Febrile neutropenia (single temperature \u003e=38.3 degrees C or \u003e=38.0 degrees C for 1 hour with ANC \u003c500/mm3) is an oncologic emergency due to rapid progression to Pseudomonas or Gram-negative sepsis. High-risk pediatric patients require immediate hospitalization and IV empiric antipseudomonal monotherapy (cefepime, piperacillin-tazobactam, or meropenem) within the golden hour after cultures.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01944","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Acute Epiglottitis vs Ludwig Angina","q":"A 36-year-old man with neglected lower molar caries develops bilateral brawny submandibular swelling, trismus, elevated tongue touching the palate, muffled hot-potato voice, and inspiratory stridor. What is the safest airway management plan before surgical incision and drainage?","o":["Blind nasotracheal intubation without topical anesthesia","Awake fiberoptic nasotracheal intubation in the operating theatre with surgical tracheostomy team scrubbed and ready","Rapid sequence induction with propofol and succinylcholine in the emergency room ward","Immediate insertion of a supraglottic laryngeal mask airway (LMA) as definitive airway"],"a":1,"e":"Ludwig angina causes rapid cellulitic edema of the submandibular and sublingual spaces, displacing the tongue posteriorly and creating a high risk of complete airway collapse upon loss of pharyngeal muscle tone during general anesthesia. Awake fiberoptic nasal intubation with preserved spontaneous breathing and surgical tracheostomy backup ready is the gold standard.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01945","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Toxic Alcohol Ingestion","q":"A 40-year-old man is brought in comatose 6 hours after ingesting radiator antifreeze. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.06, HCO3- 7 mEq/L, anion gap 28 mEq/L, measured serum osmolality 348 mOsm/kg (calculated 292 mOsm/kg -\u003e osmolar gap 56), acute kidney injury (Cr 2.8 mg/dL), and envelope-shaped and needle-shaped calcium oxalate crystals in urine microscopy. What is the specific alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor and definitive toxin-removal modality?","o":["Oral activated charcoal 100 g and IV sodium bicarbonate alone","IV N-acetylcysteine alone without dialysis","IV Fomepizole (or ethanol if fomepizole unavailable) to block toxic metabolite formation, PLUS urgent haemodialysis (plus pyridoxine and thiamine)","IV naloxone infusion with supportive observation"],"a":2,"e":"Ethylene glycol poisoning initially causes a high osmolar gap, then is metabolised by alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) into glycolic and oxalic acids—producing severe high anion gap metabolic acidosis, AKI, and urinary calcium oxalate crystals. Immediate ADH blockade with IV Fomepizole (preferred over ethanol due to predictable kinetics and no CNS depression) halts further toxic acid generation, while urgent haemodialysis clears parent ethylene glycol and glycolate and corrects acidosis/AKI.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01946","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Necrotising Soft Tissue Infections","q":"A 58-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes presents with rapidly worsening scrotal and perineal pain out of proportion to early erythema, fever (39.4 degrees C), hypotension, dusky skin patches with dishwater-grey exudate, and palpable subcutaneous crepitus tracking along Colles\u0027 fascia toward the anterior abdominal wall. What is the single most critical intervention to prevent mortality?","o":["Topical mupirocin ointment with oral amoxicillin-clavulanate","Wait 48 hours for MRI pelvis and culture sensitivity before operating","Incision and small stab-drainage under local anaesthesia in the OPD","Immediate emergency radical surgical debridement of all necrotic tissue PLUS broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (vancomycin + piperacillin-tazobactam/carbapenem + clindamycin for antitoxin effect)"],"a":3,"e":"Fournier\u0027s gangrene is a fulminant polymicrobial necrotising fasciitis of the perineal/genital region spreading rapidly along fascial planes (Colles\u0027 and Scarpa\u0027s fascia). Every hour of surgical delay sharply increases mortality; immediate aggressive operative debridement down to bleeding healthy tissue (with planned second-look debridement at 24 h) combined with broad-spectrum IV antibiotics including Clindamycin (suppresses streptococcal/clostridial exotoxin synthesis) is mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01947","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome (TTTS)","q":"A 29-year-old woman with a monochorionic diamniotic (MCDA) twin gestation at 21 weeks presents with rapidly increasing fundal height. Ultrasound shows Twin A (\u0027donor\u0027) with oligohydramnios (maximum vertical pocket 1.2 cm) and an empty non-visible bladder, while Twin B (\u0027recipient\u0027) has severe polyhydramnios (MVP 10.5 cm) and abnormal umbilical artery Doppler (absent end-diastolic flow) without hydrops (Quintero Stage III TTTS). What is the definitive gold-standard fetal therapy?","o":["Fetoscopic laser photocoagulation of the placental arteriovenous vascular anastomoses","Serial maternal oral indomethacin until 34 weeks","Expectant ultrasound monitoring every 4 weeks without intervention","Immediate Caesarean delivery at 21 weeks"],"a":0,"e":"TTTS complicates ~10-15% of MCDA twin pregnancies due to unbalanced unidirectional intertwin blood flow across deep placental arteriovenous anastomoses (oligohydramnios/polyhydramnios sequence: MVP \u003c= 2 cm in donor and \u003e= 8 cm in recipient). For Quintero Stage II-IV TTTS between 16 and 26 weeks, selective fetoscopic laser photocoagulation (Solomon technique) is superior to serial amnioreduction, addressing the root vascular cause and maximising dual/single survival without severe neurologic injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01948","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Urea Cycle Disorders","q":"A full-term male neonate appears well for 36 hours, then develops lethargy, poor feeding, tachypnoea, seizures, and coma after starting breast milk. Arterial blood gas shows respiratory alkalosis (pH 7.51, PaCO2 24 mmHg), normal anion gap, blood glucose 84 mg/dL, very low BUN (2 mg/dL), plasma ammonia 920 micromol/L, and markedly elevated urinary orotic acid. What is the most likely diagnosis and emergency management?","o":["Diabetic ketoacidosis; IV regular insulin infusion","X-linked Ornithine Transcarbamylase (OTC) deficiency; stop all protein, infuse high-rate IV glucose/lipids, IV sodium benzoate/phenylacetate + arginine, and initiate urgent haemodialysis","Pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency; ketogenic high-protein diet","Congenital hypothyroidism; oral levothyroxine alone"],"a":1,"e":"Severe neonatal hyperammonaemia with respiratory alkalosis (central hyperventilation triggered by ammonia), absence of ketoacidosis/hypoglycaemia, low BUN, and elevated urinary orotic acid (from carbamoyl phosphate shunting into pyrimidine synthesis) is classic for Ornithine Transcarbamylase (OTC) deficiency—the commonest urea cycle defect. Emergency management stops catabolism/protein (high-glucose IV + intralipid), gives nitrogen scavengers (sodium benzoate + sodium phenylacetate) + IV arginine, and initiates urgent haemodialysis/CRRT (when ammonia \u003e 400-500 micromol/L) to prevent irreversible cerebral oedema.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01949","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Brainstem Death Determination","q":"A 34-year-old man with catastrophic traumatic brain injury has irreversible coma of known cause, normal core temperature (36.8 degrees C), normotension, normal electrolytes, and absence of sedatives/neuromuscular blockers, with all brainstem reflexes absent. During formal apnoea testing (after preoxygenation with 100% O2 and disconnecting the ventilator while delivering 4-6 L/min O2 via tracheal cannula), what arterial PaCO2 endpoint without respiratory effort confirms a positive apnoea test?","o":["PaCO2 \u003e= 60 mmHg (or a rise of \u003e= 20 mmHg above normal baseline PaCO2) with no observed spontaneous breathing","PaCO2 \u003e= 35 mmHg after 30 seconds of disconnection","PaO2 \u003c 40 mmHg regardless of PaCO2","Presence of spinal plantar withdrawal reflexes rules out brain death"],"a":0,"e":"Under India\u0027s Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act (THOTA) and international neurological determination guidelines, once prerequisites are met and all brainstem reflexes (pupillary, corneal, oculocephalic, oculovestibular, gag, cough) are absent, the apnoea test provides hypercapnic medullary drive using apnoeic oxygenation. Complete absence of respiratory movements despite PaCO2 rising to \u003e= 60 mmHg (or \u003e= 20 mmHg above baseline in chronic CO2 retainers) confirms apnoea. Notably, spinally mediated reflexes (e.g., Lazarus sign, plantar withdrawal) do NOT preclude brain death.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01950","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Cerebrovascular Malformations","q":"A 9-year-old girl presents with recurrent transient ischaemic attacks precipitated by crying and hyperventilation. Brain MRI/MRA and Digital Subtraction Angiography (DSA) demonstrate progressive bilateral stenosis/occlusion of the distal supraclinoid internal carotid arteries and proximal M1/A1 segments, surrounded by a dense basal network of dilated lenticulostriate collaterals resembling a \u0027puff of smoke\u0027, plus leptomeningeal enhancement/hyperintensity (\u0027ivy sign\u0027) on FLAIR. What is the diagnosis and standard surgical strategy?","o":["Takayasu arteritis confined to the renal arteries; bilateral nephrectomy","Berry aneurysm rupture; endovascular coiling","Moyamoya disease; surgical cerebral revascularisation (direct STA-MCA bypass or indirect EDAS/encephalo-duro-arterio-synangiosis)","Dural arteriovenous fistula; systemic alteplase"],"a":2,"e":"Idiopathic bilateral progressive steno-occlusion of the terminal internal carotid arteries and Circle of Willis with compensatory basal lenticulostriate collaterals (\u0027puff of smoke\u0027 = Moyamoya) and the FLAIR \u0027ivy sign\u0027 (slow cortical collateral flow) defines Moyamoya disease. Hyperventilation induces hypocapnic vasoconstriction and TIAs in children. Definitive management to prevent major strokes is surgical revascularisation (indirect synangiosis such as EDAS/EMS in young children or direct STA-MCA bypass in adults/older children).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01951","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Spinal Emergencies","q":"A 41-year-old man with chronic low-back pain lifts a heavy crate and develops acute bilateral sciatica, perineal (\u0027saddle\u0027) numbness, painless urinary retention (post-void residual 650 mL with overflow dribbling), and loss of voluntary anal sphincter tone. What is the mandatory emergency diagnostic and surgical pathway?","o":["Elective outpatient X-ray lumbar spine after 2 weeks","Epidural steroid injection without MRI","Oral NSAIDs, muscle relaxants, and physiotherapy review in 6 weeks","Immediate emergency MRI lumbosacral spine followed by urgent surgical decompression (discectomy/laminectomy, ideally within 24-48 hours)"],"a":3,"e":"Acute central disc herniation (commonly L4-L5 or L5-S1) compressing lumbosacral nerve roots below the conus medullaris causes Cauda Equina Syndrome: saddle anaesthesia, bladder/bowel dysfunction (retention/overflow incontinence), reduced anal tone, and bilateral radiculopathy/weakness. It is a true orthopaedic/neurosurgical emergency requiring immediate MRI confirmation and urgent surgical decompression within 24-48 hours to preserve bladder, bowel, and sexual function.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01952","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Retinopathy of Prematurity (ROP)","q":"Under India\u0027s National Neonatology Forum / RBSK guidelines, preterm neonates with gestational age \u003c= 34 weeks or birth weight \u003c= 2,000 g (or 34-36 weeks with risk factors) undergo first Retinopathy of Prematurity (ROP) screening by 3-4 weeks of postnatal age (\u0027Day 30 rule\u0027). If screening reveals Zone I Stage 3 ROP with \u0027Plus disease\u0027 (venous dilatation and arteriolar tortuosity at the posterior pole), what is the required management?","o":["Urgent retinal ablative therapy within 48-72 hours (laser photocoagulation of avascular retina or intravitreal anti-VEGF)","Systemic oral corticosteroids","Routine re-examination after 6 months","Immediate bilateral enucleation"],"a":0,"e":"Type 1 (high-risk pre-threshold) ROP—including any stage ROP in Zone I with Plus disease, Zone I Stage 3 without Plus, or Zone II Stage 2/3 with Plus disease—progresses rapidly to cicatricial traction retinal detachment (Stage 4-5) and irreversible childhood blindness. Urgent treatment within 48-72 hours with indirect diode/argon laser photocoagulation to the avascular peripheral retina (or intravitreal anti-VEGF for aggressive posterior/Zone I ROP) prevents blindness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01953","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Dermatology","topic":"Leprosy Reactions","q":"A 30-year-old man with lepromatous leprosy (LL) who started MB-MDT 4 months ago develops high fever, crops of painful erythematous evanescent subcutaneous nodules over the extensor forearms and shins, acute ulnar neuritis, arthritis, and iridocyclitis (Type 2 lepra reaction / Erythema Nodosum Leprosum). What is the correct principle regarding MB-MDT and systemic anti-inflammatory treatment?","o":["Stop MB-MDT permanently and treat with oral fluconazole","Continue full MB-MDT without interruption AND initiate systemic prednisolone (or clofazimine high-dose / thalidomide in refractory male patients)","Treat with topical emollients alone without systemic therapy","Administer oral thalidomide routinely to pregnant/childbearing-age females without contraception"],"a":1,"e":"Type 2 lepra reaction (ENL) is a Type III immune-complex hypersensitivity occurring in multibacillary (BL/LL) patients with high bacillary load. MB-MDT must ALWAYS be continued uninterrupted during both Type 1 and Type 2 reactions. Severe ENL (with neuritis, iritis, orchitis, ulceration) requires prompt systemic corticosteroids (prednisolone), with high-dose clofazimine or thalidomide (highly effective in male/non-childbearing patients, strictly contraindicated in pregnancy due to phocomelia) as steroid-sparing agents.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01954","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Inherited Channelopathies","q":"A 33-year-old Southeast Asian man is brought after surviving an out-of-hospital polymorphic ventricular tachycardia / ventricular fibrillation arrest during sleep following a febrile illness. Baseline echocardiogram and coronary angiogram are normal. ECG recorded during fever shows coved ST-segment elevation \u003e= 2 mm followed by an inverted T wave in leads V1 and V2 (Type 1 Brugada pattern), caused by loss-of-function SCN5A sodium channel mutation. Which definitive therapy and drug precaution are indicated?","o":["Long-term oral flecainide to suppress ventricular ectopy","Coronary artery bypass grafting","Implantable Cardioverter-Defibrillator (ICD), aggressive antipyretics during fever, and strict avoidance of Class IA/IC sodium-channel blockers (e.g., flecainide, procainamide)","Oral amiodarone alone without ICD"],"a":2,"e":"Symptomatic Type 1 Brugada syndrome (history of aborted cardiac arrest or arrhythmic syncope with coved \u003e= 2 mm ST elevation in V1-V2) carries high sudden cardiac death risk (classically nocturnal vagal-predominant VF) and requires ICD placement (quinidine or epicardial RVOT ablation are used for electrical storms). Because fever and sodium-channel blockers unmask/aggravate SCN5A loss-of-function, aggressive fever treatment and strict avoidance of Class IA/IC antiarrhythmics (procainamide, flecainide, ajmaline) are mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01955","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Chronic Hyponatremia Correction","q":"A 54-year-old malnourished man with chronic alcohol use disorder presents with confusion and serum sodium 108 mEq/L of unknown (\u003e48 hour) duration. Six hours after starting 3% hypertonic saline, his serum sodium rises to 118 mEq/L with sudden brisk aquaresis (urine output 500 mL/hr). What is the most appropriate immediate intervention to prevent osmotic demyelination syndrome (ODS)?","o":["Give IV furosemide 40 mg and restrict all free water intake","Administer IV mannitol 20% to accelerate free water diuresis","Continue 3% saline until serum sodium reaches 130 mEq/L within the next 6 hours","Stop 3% saline immediately, administer IV D5W and IV desmopressin (DDAVP) to re-lower serum sodium toward a \u003c=6-8 mEq/L net 24-hour rise"],"a":3,"e":"Patients with chronic hyponatremia, alcoholism, malnutrition, or hypokalemia are at extreme risk for osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis) if serum sodium increases by \u003e8 mEq/L in any 24-hour period (already +10 mEq/L in 6 hours here). Sudden spontaneous aquaresis (rapidly falling ADH) causes dangerous overcorrection, requiring immediate cessation of hypertonic saline plus IV D5W and desmopressin (DDAVP) clamp to re-lower sodium back to target.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01956","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Post-Thyroidectomy Complications","q":"Four hours after total thyroidectomy for a large multinodular goiter, a 45-year-old woman in the surgical recovery ward develops severe neck tightness, rapidly expanding anterior neck swelling, agitation, and inspiratory stridor with SpO2 dropping to 86%. What is the immediate life-saving bedside action?","o":["Immediately open the skin sutures/clips and strap muscle layer at the bedside to evacuate the clot before attempting intubation","Apply a tight circumferential elastic pressure dressing over the neck wound","Administer nebulized racemic epinephrine and IV hydrocortisone and observe for 1 hour","Send the patient to the radiology suite for an urgent Doppler ultrasound of the neck"],"a":0,"e":"An expanding post-thyroidectomy hematoma compresses the venous and lymphatic drainage of the larynx, causing rapid supraglottic edema and fatal airway obstruction. Waiting for imaging or trying to intubate over a compressed, deviated trachea fails; opening the skin and deep strap muscle layers immediately at the bedside (\u0027SCOOP\u0027 protocol) decompresses the airway so definitive intubation can succeed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01957","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Postpartum Hemorrhage (PPH)","q":"A 30-year-old multipara with chronic bronchial asthma and mild gestational hypertension (BP 152/96 mmHg) develops persistent atonic postpartum hemorrhage after vaginal delivery despite bimanual uterine massage, IV oxytocin infusion, and IV tranexamic acid 1 g. Which second-line uterotonic is safest to administer next?","o":["Misoprostol 800-1000 mcg (sublingual or rectal) while avoiding both methylergometrine and carboprost","Intramuscular methylergometrine (Methergine) 0.2 mg","Intramuscular carboprost tromethamine (15-methyl PGF2-alpha) 250 mcg","High-dose IV magnesium sulfate infusion"],"a":0,"e":"In atonic PPH refractory to oxytocin, drug comorbidities govern second-line selection: methylergometrine (ergot alkaloid) is contraindicated in hypertension/preeclampsia due to severe vasospasm and hypertensive crisis, while carboprost (PGF2-alpha) is contraindicated in bronchial asthma due to severe bronchospasm. Misoprostol (PGE1 analog) is safe in both hypertension and asthma, alongside uterine balloon tamponade if bleeding persists.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01958","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Acute Bacterial Meningitis","q":"A 4-year-old unimmunized child presents with high fever, neck stiffness, photophobia, and lethargy. Lumbar puncture yields cloudy CSF with 1,800 WBC/mm3 (92% neutrophils), glucose 18 mg/dL, protein 210 mg/dL, and Gram-positive diplococci on Gram stain. Beyond IV ceftriaxone plus vancomycin, what adjunctive therapy reduces severe sensorineural hearing loss if given before or with the first antibiotic dose?","o":["Intravenous acyclovir for 21 days","Intravenous dexamethasone (0.15 mg/kg/dose every 6 hours for 2-4 days)","Routine therapeutic hyperventilation to PaCO2 \u003c25 mmHg","Oral rifampicin prophylaxis for the patient"],"a":1,"e":"Adjunctive IV dexamethasone (0.15 mg/kg q6h) administered 10-20 minutes before or concurrently with the first parenteral antibiotic dose blunts the CSF inflammatory cytokine surge triggered by bacterial lysis and significantly decreases severe neurologic sequelae and sensorineural deafness in Hib and pneumococcal meningitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01959","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Acute Primary Angle-Closure Crisis","q":"A 62-year-old hyperopic woman develops sudden severe right eye pain, colored halos around lights, vomiting, visual acuity reduced to hand movements, corneal edema, shallow anterior chamber, mid-dilated non-reactive pupil, and IOP of 58 mmHg. Which initial medical regimen and definitive procedure are indicated?","o":["Oral ibuprofen alone with outpatient review in 2 weeks","Topical atropine 1% eye drops and immediate phacoemulsification without lowering IOP","IV acetazolamide, topical timolol/apraclonidine, hyperosmotic IV mannitol if needed, and pilocarpine once IOP drops below 40 mmHg, followed by bilateral laser peripheral iridotomy","Urgent warm compresses and topical cyclopentolate to dilate the pupil fully"],"a":2,"e":"Acute primary angle-closure crisis results from pupillary block in shallow hyperopic eyes. Rapid aqueous suppression (IV acetazolamide, topical beta-blocker/alpha-agonist) and osmotic dehydration (IV mannitol) lower IOP enough to relieve iris sphincter ischemia so pilocarpine can constrict the pupil and pull the peripheral iris away from the trabecular meshwork. Definitive management is laser peripheral iridotomy (LPI) in the affected eye and prophylactically in the fellow eye.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01960","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Primary Aldosteronism (Conn Syndrome)","q":"A 42-year-old man with resistant hypertension (on 4 drugs) and spontaneous hypokalaemia (K+ 2.9 mEq/L) has a markedly elevated plasma Aldosterone-to-Renin Ratio (ARR \u003e 30 with suppressed plasma renin activity) and fails to suppress plasma aldosterone after an IV saline infusion test. Contrast CT abdomen shows a 1.3 cm left adrenal nodule and a normal-appearing right adrenal gland. Before recommending unilateral laparoscopic adrenalectomy, which test is essential in patients \u003e 35-40 years to avoid operating on a non-functioning incidentaloma?","o":["24-hour urinary 5-HIAA","FNA biopsy of the left adrenal nodule","Immediate empirical bilateral adrenalectomy","Bilateral Adrenal Venous Sampling (AVS) to confirm unilateral aldosterone lateralisation"],"a":3,"e":"In Primary Aldosteronism, adrenal CT alone misclassifies lateralisation in up to 35-40% of adults \u003e35-40 years because non-functioning adrenal incidentalomas are common while bilateral idiopathic adrenal hyperplasia (treated medically with spironolactone/eplerenone) may coexist with an incidentaloma. Bilateral Adrenal Venous Sampling (AVS) is the gold standard to prove unilateral excess aldosterone secretion before surgical adrenalectomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01961","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal Stromal Tumour (GIST)","q":"A 58-year-old man evaluated for upper GI bleeding has a 6 cm well-circumscribed submucosal mass in the gastric fundus with central ulceration on EUS. Biopsy shows spindle cells strongly positive for CD117 (c-KIT) and DOG1 (PDGFRA/KIT signalling). Which surgical and systemic principle applies to localised resectable gastric GIST?","o":["Wedge/segmental gastric resection with macroscopically negative margins (R0) and intact pseudocapsule WITHOUT routine lymphadenectomy; adjuvant Imatinib for high-risk features","Neoadjuvant FOLFIRINOX followed by pelvic radiotherapy","Radical total gastrectomy with obligatory D2 lymph node dissection in all cases","Simple endoscopic mucosal enucleation rupturing the capsule"],"a":0,"e":"GISTs arise from interstitial cells of Cajal and express CD117 (c-KIT, ~95%) and DOG1. Because GIST spreads haematogenously/peritoneally and almost never metastasises to lymph nodes, wedge resection with clear margins (1-2 cm) avoiding tumour rupture/spillage—WITHOUT formal lymphadenectomy—is curative surgery. Tyrosine kinase inhibition with Imatinib (targeting KIT/PDGFRA) is used as neoadjuvant cytoreduction for borderline/unresectable disease and as adjuvant therapy (3 years) for high-risk resected GIST (large size, high mitotic index, rupture).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01962","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Acute Puerperal Uterine Inversion","q":"Immediately after vigorous cord traction on a fundally implanted placenta prior to uterine contraction, a 24-year-old primipara develops severe hypotension (66/38 mmHg), bradycardia, and a smooth, beefy-red haemorrhagic mass protruding from the introitus with the uterine fundus no longer palpable per abdomen (acute complete uterine inversion). What is the crucial sequence of manual replacement and uterotonics?","o":["Forcibly peel off the attached placenta at the introitus first, then pull down","Stop all uterotonics (give tocolytic if cervical constriction ring present), immediately push the inverted fundus upward manually along the vaginal axis (Johnson manoeuvre) WITH placenta still attached if unseparated, and administer oxytocin ONLY AFTER repositioning","Perform immediate abdominal hysterectomy in the labour room","Administer IV ergometrine and oxytocin bolus FIRST before attempting replacement"],"a":1,"e":"Acute uterine inversion causes severe neurogenic (vagal) and haemorrhagic shock with a mass in the vagina/introitus and absent abdominal fundus (\u0027cupping\u0027). Immediate manual replacement (Johnson\u0027s manoeuvre: cupping the fundus with palm and pressing upward toward the umbilicus along the long axis of the vagina, or O\u0027Sullivan\u0027s hydrostatic method) must be performed immediately BEFORE cervical ring contraction traps the fundus. If the placenta is still attached, leave it attached during replacement to avoid torrential bleeding; uterotonics are given ONLY AFTER the uterus is restored to prevent immediate re-inversion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01963","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Congenital Diaphragmatic Hernia (CDH)","q":"A term newborn with an antenatal diagnosis of left-sided Congenital Diaphragmatic Hernia (Bochdalek hernia) develops severe cyanosis and respiratory distress immediately after birth, with a scaphoid abdomen, shifted heart sounds to the right, and bowel loops visible in the left hemithorax on chest X-ray. What is the critical delivery-room resuscitation rule and surgical timing?","o":["Rush straight from delivery room to operating theatre within 30 minutes before cardiopulmonary stabilisation","Insert a left-sided intercostal chest drain into the bowel loops","Immediate endotracheal intubation (STRICTLY AVOID bag-and-mask ventilation) + wide-bore orogastric decompression; delay surgical repair until pulmonary hypertension and haemodynamics stabilise","Immediate vigorous bag-and-mask positive-pressure ventilation for 10 minutes in the delivery room"],"a":2,"e":"In Congenital Diaphragmatic Hernia (90% posterolateral Bochdalek, 80% left-sided), bag-and-mask ventilation forces air down the oesophagus into herniated intrathoracic stomach and bowel loops, acutely compressing the contralateral lung and heart. Delivery-room protocol mandates immediate endotracheal intubation (low peak pressures \u003c= 25 cmH2O) + Replogle/orogastric suction decompression. Because mortality stems from pulmonary hypoplasia and persistent pulmonary hypertension (PPHN), surgical repair is delayed (24-72+ h) until cardiorespiratory stability is achieved.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01964","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"One-Lung Ventilation (OLV)","q":"Twenty minutes after initiating left-sided One-Lung Ventilation (OLV) via a left-sided double-lumen tube (DLT) in the right lateral decubitus position for a right thoracotomy, SpO2 drops progressively from 98% to 84% with stable blood pressure. After increasing FiO2 to 1.0, what is the immediate diagnostic and physiological troubleshooting sequence?","o":["Clamp the dependent left lung lumen and ventilate only the open surgical right lung at tidal volume 1,000 mL","Administer IV sodium nitroprusside to dilate pulmonary vessels globally","Immediately pull out the DLT 5 cm blindly without bronchoscopy","Confirm DLT position via fibreoptic bronchoscopy, suction secretions, apply recruitment manoeuvre + PEEP to the dependent (ventilated) left lung, and apply CPAP (2-5 cmH2O with O2) to the non-dependent right lung"],"a":3,"e":"Acute hypoxaemia during OLV is most often caused by DLT malposition (dislodgement during lateral positioning) or shunt through the non-ventilated lung. Standard stepwise management: (1) FiO2 1.0; (2) Fibreoptic bronchoscopy to verify DLT placement and clear mucus plugs; (3) Recruit and optimise PEEP (5-8 cmH2O) to the dependent ventilated lung; (4) Apply low-level CPAP (2-5 cmH2O O2) to the non-dependent surgical lung (and alert surgeon to clamp pulmonary artery if pneumonectomy/refractory).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01965","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Blunt Thoracic Aortic Injury","q":"A 31-year-old unrestrained driver involved in a high-speed frontal deceleration crash has chest pain and a widened mediastinum (\u003e8 cm), left apical pleural cap, obscured aortic knob, and rightward deviation of the nasogastric tube on supine chest X-ray. Contrast-enhanced CT angiography (CTA) demonstrates an intimal flap and a focal 2 cm pseudoaneurysm just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery (aortic isthmus) with periaortic haematoma. What is the preferred definitive repair in an anatomically suitable patient?","o":["Thoracic Endovascular Aortic Repair (TEVAR) alongside strict anti-impulse beta-blocker control (HR \u003c 80, SBP 100-110 mmHg)","Oral aspirin and outpatient repeat CT in 6 months","Immediate insertion of a left chest tube directly into the mediastinal haematoma","Systemic anticoagulation with IV heparin infusion"],"a":0,"e":"Rapid deceleration creates maximal shear stress at the aortic isthmus (just distal to the left subclavian artery, where the mobile arch is tethered by the ligamentum arteriosum), causing blunt thoracic aortic injury. CTA is diagnostic (Grade III pseudoaneurysm). Strict anti-impulse control (esmolol/labetalol) lowers wall shear stress, and Thoracic Endovascular Aortic Repair (TEVAR) is standard first-line repair with dramatically lower paraplegia and mortality than open thoracotomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01966","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Acute Septic Arthritis of Hip","q":"A 5-year-old boy presents with acute right hip pain for 24 hours, refusal to bear weight, hip held in flexion-abduction-external rotation, temperature 39.1 degrees C, WBC 16,400/mm3, and ESR 58 mm/h (CRP 8.5 mg/dL). Ultrasound demonstrates a moderate right hip joint effusion. Applying Kocher\u0027s criteria (all 4 predictors present -\u003e \u003e93-99% probability of septic arthritis), what is the mandatory immediate treatment?","o":["Intra-articular triamcinolone injection into the hip joint","Diagnostic ultrasound-guided hip aspiration followed by urgent surgical arthrotomy/washout AND IV empiric anti-staphylococcal antibiotics","Skin traction alone for 3 weeks without antibiotics","Oral ibuprofen alone for presumed benign transient synovitis and discharge"],"a":1,"e":"Kocher\u0027s 4 criteria for distinguishing paediatric septic arthritis of the hip from transient synovitis are: (1) Fever \u003e 38.5 C; (2) Non-weight-bearing; (3) ESR \u003e 40 mm/h; (4) Serum WBC \u003e 12,000/mm3 (plus CRP \u003e 2.0 mg/dL). With all 4 criteria positive, septic arthritis probability exceeds 93-99%. Because purulent enzymes and tamponade destroy articular cartilage and femoral head blood supply within 24-48 hours, urgent surgical drainage (arthrotomy/washout) plus IV antibiotics is mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01967","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Orbital Infections","q":"A 10-year-old boy with ethmoid sinusitis develops acute fever (39.3 degrees C), periorbital erythema, axial proptosis, chemosis, severe pain on eye movement, restricted abduction/elevation, visual acuity drop to 6/24, and a right relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD). How is this differentiated from preseptal cellulitis, and what is the immediate management?","o":["Preseptal cellulitis; oral amoxicillin-clavulanate outpatient for 5 days","Allergic conjunctivitis; topical olopatadine drops","Post-septal Orbital Cellulitis (evidenced by proptosis, ophthalmoplegia, visual loss, RAPD); urgent contrast CT orbits/sinuses, broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftriaxone + metronidazole), and surgical drainage if subperiosteal/orbital abscess","Thyroid-associated orbitopathy; oral carbimazole alone"],"a":2,"e":"Preseptal cellulitis involves eyelid tissues anterior to the orbital septum and preserves vision, pupillary reflexes, and full extraocular motility without proptosis. Proptosis, painful ophthalmoplegia, decreased acuity, and RAPD signify post-septal Orbital Cellulitis (most commonly secondary to ethmoid sinusitis), risking optic nerve compression, cavernous sinus thrombosis, and meningitis. Urgent contrast CT, hospital admission with IV broad-spectrum antibiotics, and ENT/ophthalmic drainage for abscesses or visual compromise are mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01968","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Psychiatry","topic":"Treatment-Resistant Schizophrenia","q":"A 29-year-old man with treatment-resistant schizophrenia started on Clozapine 8 weeks ago presents with high fever and sore throat. Complete blood count shows total WBC 1,200/mm3 and Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC) 380/mm3 (agranulocytosis). What is the mandatory action regarding Clozapine?","o":["Reduce Clozapine dose by 50% and recheck CBC in 2 weeks","Continue Clozapine with oral amoxicillin and paracetamol","Switch to Chlorpromazine today and restart Clozapine next month once ANC normalises","Stop Clozapine IMMEDIATELY and permanently (never rechallenge), isolate/culture, start broad-spectrum IV antibiotics for febrile neutropenia, and consider G-CSF"],"a":3,"e":"Clozapine is the gold-standard antipsychotic for treatment-resistant schizophrenia and suicidality in schizophrenia, but carries a ~0.8-1% risk of idiosyncratic agranulocytosis (ANC \u003c 500/mm3, highest in the first 3-6 months), necessitating mandatory regular ANC monitoring. Severe neutropenia/agranulocytosis requires IMMEDIATE permanent discontinuation of clozapine (strictly contraindicated from future rechallenge), prompt febrile neutropenia sepsis workup/antibiotics, and haematology/filgrastim (G-CSF) support.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01969","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Mechanical Complications of Acute MI","q":"Five days after an acute anterior STEMI (late presentation without reperfusion), a 63-year-old man suddenly develops refractory cardiogenic shock, pulmonary oedema, and a new loud harsh holosystolic murmur with thrill maximal at the left lower sternal border. Pulmonary artery catheterisation reveals a marked step-up in oxygen saturation from the right atrium (62%) to the right ventricle (86%), and bedside echo shows a 1.5 cm defect in the apical interventricular septum. What is the diagnosis and urgent management?","o":["Post-MI Ventricular Septal Rupture (VSR); afterload reduction (IV nitroprusside if BP tolerates) + Intra-Aortic Balloon Pump (IABP)/mechanical circulatory support + urgent surgical VSR repair","Dressler syndrome; oral ibuprofen 600 mg TID","Acute papillary muscle rupture with severe mitral regurgitation (no RA-to-RV O2 step-up); IV fluid bolus alone","Free wall rupture with tamponade; oral warfarin"],"a":0,"e":"At 3-5 days post-transmural MI, coagulative necrosis weakens the myocardium, causing mechanical complications. A new holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border WITH a left-to-right shunt (\u003e7-10% oxygen saturation step-up from right atrium to right ventricle/pulmonary artery) confirms Ventricular Septal Rupture (unlike papillary muscle rupture/acute MR, which shows large \u0027v\u0027 waves in PCWP without an O2 step-up). Immediate medical/mechanical afterload reduction (IABP + vasodilators if tolerated) reduces left-to-right shunting as a bridge to urgent surgical repair.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01970","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)","q":"A 46-year-old man (ideal body weight 70 kg) with severe community-acquired pneumonia develops bilateral diffuse alveolar infiltrates, normal echocardiogram, and PaO2/FiO2 ratio of 92 mmHg on PEEP 14 cm H2O. Which mechanical ventilation and adjunctive strategy has proven mortality benefit?","o":["Routine nitric oxide inhalation for 14 days at tidal volume 10 mL/kg","Tidal volume 420 mL (6 mL/kg IBW), plateau pressure \u003c=30 cm H2O, adequate PEEP, and early prone positioning for 12-16 hours/day","Zero PEEP (ZEEP) with high-dose IV methylprednisolone 2 g/day alone","Tidal volume 840 mL (12 mL/kg IBW) to normalize PaCO2 to 32 mmHg"],"a":1,"e":"In severe ARDS (PaO2/FiO2 \u003c=100 mmHg, or \u003c150 mmHg per PROSEVA trial), ARDSNet lung-protective ventilation (low tidal volume 4-8 mL/kg predicted body weight targeting 6 mL/kg = 420 mL, plateau pressure \u003c=30 cm H2O, permissive hypercapnia) combined with early prolonged prone positioning (12-16 hrs/day) improves V/Q matching, dorsal alveolar recruitment, and survival.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01971","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Acute Mesenteric Ischemia","q":"A 72-year-old woman with untreated atrial fibrillation presents with sudden, agonizing periumbilical abdominal pain out of proportion to minimal abdominal tenderness on palpation, accompanied by vomiting, leukocytosis (21,000/mm3), and serum lactate 4.8 mmol/L. Which diagnostic modality and definitive management are indicated?","o":["Immediate colonoscopy with aggressive air insufflation","Oral barium meal follow-through over 24 hours","Urgent triphasic CT angiography of the abdomen (without delaying for oral contrast) followed by heparin anticoagulation and emergency SMA embolectomy/laparotomy","Outpatient proton pump inhibitor therapy and elective endoscopy next week"],"a":2,"e":"Sudden \u0027pain out of proportion to physical exam\u0027 in a patient with atrial fibrillation is the classic hallmark of acute superior mesenteric artery (SMA) embolism. Triphasic CT angiography (CTA) rapidly confirms arterial cutoff/bowel ischemia without oral contrast delay, guiding immediate systemic anticoagulation, resuscitation, and surgical embolectomy/endovascular revascularization plus resection of non-viable bowel.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01972","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Shoulder Dystocia","q":"Immediately after delivery of the fetal head in a gestational diabetic mother, the head retracts tightly against the perineum (\u0027turtle sign\u0027) and gentle downward traction fails to deliver the anterior shoulder. What are the recommended initial maneuvers, and what action is strictly CONTRAINDICATED?","o":["Cutting the umbilical cord immediately before attempting shoulder delivery","Immediate vacuum re-application to the fetal head","Vigorous downward traction on the fetal head plus strong uterine fundal pressure","McRoberts maneuver (sharp hyperflexion of maternal hips onto abdomen) combined with firm suprapubic pressure; fundal pressure is strictly contraindicated"],"a":3,"e":"McRoberts maneuver flattens the sacral promontory and rotates the symphysis pubis cephalad, freeing the impacted anterior shoulder in \u003e40% of cases alone and \u003e50-60% when paired with suprapubic pressure (Mazzanti/Rubin I) to adduct the shoulder. Fundal pressure impacts the anterior shoulder even harder behind the symphysis pubis and increases uterine rupture and brachial plexus avulsion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01973","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Infantile Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis","q":"A 4-week-old male infant presents with progressive non-bilious projectile vomiting after feeds, visible gastric peristalsis, and a palpable olive-shaped epigastric mass. Labs show Na+ 132 mEq/L, K+ 2.8 mEq/L, Cl- 84 mEq/L, HCO3- 34 mEq/L. What is the mandatory preoperative management before Ramstedt pyloromyotomy?","o":["Withhold oral feeds, give isotonic normal saline boluses/maintenance (0.9% NaCl with 5% dextrose + KCl once urine output is confirmed) to correct hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis prior to surgery","Administer IV sodium bicarbonate to correct the pH","Rush directly to the operating theatre within 30 minutes without IV fluids or electrolyte correction","Feed oral ORS immediately and schedule surgery only at 1 year of age"],"a":0,"e":"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is a medical-metabolic emergency first, NOT a surgical emergency. Gastric HCl loss and secondary hyperaldosteronism create classic hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis; operating before restoring intravascular volume, chloride, and potassium risks severe postoperative respiratory depression/apnea under anesthesia. Once urine flow is established, add KCl to D5-0.9% NaCl until HCO3- \u003c26-28 and Cl- \u003e100 mEq/L.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01974","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Dermatology and Venereology","topic":"Stevens-Johnson Syndrome / Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (SJS/TEN)","q":"Two weeks after starting carbamazepine for trigeminal neuralgia, a 34-year-old man develops fever, painful hemorrhagic crusting of the lips, bilateral purulent conjunctivitis, and dusky targetoid macules progressing to flaccid blisters with sheet-like epidermal detachment across 38% of his body surface area (positive Nikolsky sign). What is the diagnosis and top priority intervention?","o":["Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome; apply topical potent fluorinated steroid ointment under occlusion","Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN, \u003e30% BSA detachment); immediately discontinue carbamazepine permanently and admit to an ICU/burn unit for meticulous wound care, fluid resuscitation, and ophthalmologic care","Erythema multiforme minor; continue carbamazepine at half dose and give oral acyclovir","Fixed drug eruption; reassure and discharge with oral cetirizine"],"a":1,"e":"Epidermal detachment \u003c10% BSA defines SJS, 10-30% is SJS/TEN overlap, and \u003e30% BSA with severe hemorrhagic mucositis (oral, ocular, genital) defines Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN). Prompt recognition and immediate permanent cessation of the culprit drug (carbamazepine/allopurinol/lamotrigine/sulfa) coupled with burn-center supportive care (thermoregulation, non-adherent dressings, IV fluids, ophthalmology synechiae prevention) saves lives.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01975","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Allergic Bronchopulmonary Aspergillosis (ABPA)","q":"A 34-year-old woman with poorly controlled asthma expectorates golden-brown mucus plugs. HRCT chest reveals central (proximal) varicose bronchiectasis with high-attenuation mucoid impaction (\u0027finger-in-glove\u0027 sign). Blood tests show peripheral eosinophilia (1,100/mm3), total serum IgE 2,800 IU/mL (\u003e1,000 IU/mL), strongly positive Aspergillus fumigatus-specific IgE (\u003e0.35 kUA/L), and positive Aspergillus-specific IgG. What is the diagnosis and standard treatment?","o":["Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease; salmeterol inhaler alone","Pulmonary tuberculosis; standard HRZE antitubercular regimen","Allergic Bronchopulmonary Aspergillosis (ABPA); systemic oral Prednisolone taper PLUS oral Itraconazole (steroid-sparing antifungal)","Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis; immediate lobectomy without steroids"],"a":2,"e":"Asthma (or cystic fibrosis) with elevated Aspergillus-specific IgE, total serum IgE \u003e 1,000 IU/mL, eosinophilia \u003e 500/mm3, positive Aspergillus IgG/skin prick test, and central bronchiectasis/high-attenuation mucus meets ISHAM diagnostic criteria for ABPA. First-line therapy suppresses hypersensitivity with systemic Prednisolone (4-month taper) combined with oral Itraconazole (or voriconazole) to reduce fungal antigenic burden and steroid requirement, preventing progression to fibrotic bronchiectasis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01976","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Wilson Disease","q":"A 17-year-old boy presents with acute jaundice, ascites, coagulopathy (INR 2.6), and mild dysarthria. Labs show total bilirubin 22.0 mg/dL, AST 145 U/L, ALT 72 U/L (AST:ALT \u003e 2), paradoxically very LOW serum alkaline phosphatase (24 U/L; ALP-to-bilirubin ratio \u003c 4), haemoglobin 7.6 g/dL with elevated reticulocytes and NEGATIVE direct Coombs test, low serum ceruloplasmin (8 mg/dL), and brownish Kayser-Fleischer rings on slit-lamp exam. What is the diagnosis and urgent definitive management for fulminant liver failure?","o":["Autoimmune hepatitis; high-dose IV methylprednisolone alone","Hereditary spherocytosis; immediate splenectomy","Acute hepatitis B; oral entecavir alone","Acute Wilson disease (ATP7B copper overload); urgent evaluation for Liver Transplantation (plus plasmapheresis/albumin dialysis bridge) rather than chelation alone"],"a":3,"e":"Acute Wilson disease (ATP7B mutation impairing biliary copper excretion) classically presents in children/young adults with acute liver failure, Coombs-negative intravascular haemolytic anaemia (from sudden copper release lysing RBCs), AST \u003e ALT, and a strikingly low ALP-to-total-bilirubin ratio (\u003c 4) alongside Kayser-Fleischer rings and low ceruloplasmin. While stable/chronic Wilson disease is treated with chelators (D-penicillamine, trientine) and zinc, acute Wilsonian fulminant liver failure with coagulopathy/encephalopathy is fatal without urgent Liver Transplantation (bridged with plasmapheresis/CRRT to remove free copper).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01977","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"CSF Pressure Disorders","q":"A 26-year-old obese woman taking minocycline for acne presents with daily throbbing headaches worse on waking, transient visual obscurations on bending forward, pulsatile tinnitus, horizontal diplopia (false-localising CN VI palsy), and bilateral Grade III papilloedema. Brain MRI and MR venography (excluding venous sinus thrombosis) are completely normal, and lumbar puncture in the lateral decubitus position shows opening pressure 34 cmH2O with normal CSF cell count/protein/glucose. What is the diagnosis and first-line management?","o":["Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension (Pseudotumour cerebri); discontinue minocycline, initiate structured weight loss + oral Acetazolamide (consider optic nerve sheath fenestration/shunt if vision rapidly worsens)","Glioblastoma multiforme; urgent craniotomy","Cluster headache; 100% oxygen and sumatriptan alone","Chronic tuberculous meningitis; 12 months of HRZE"],"a":0,"e":"Modified Dandy criteria for Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension (IIH): symptoms/signs of raised ICP (headache, papilloedema, CN VI palsy), normal neurological exam otherwise, normal MRI/MRV (empty sella/flattened posterior globes allowed, no mass/CVST), CSF opening pressure \u003e 25 cmH2O in lateral decubitus, and normal CSF composition. Stopping precipitants (tetracyclines/minocycline, isotretinoin/vitamin A, steroid withdrawal), weight reduction (5-10%), and Acetazolamide (carbonic anhydrase inhibitor reducing CSF production) preserve visual fields; surgical CSF shunting or optic nerve sheath fenestration is reserved for progressive visual loss.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01978","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Iatrogenic Bile Duct Injury","q":"During laparoscopic cholecystectomy for acute cholecystitis, a Strasberg Type E2 injury occurs (complete transection of the common hepatic duct \u003c 2 cm below the hilar confluence with segmental tissue loss), recognised intraoperatively on cholangiography. What is the definitive surgical principle for durable long-term biliary patency?","o":["Primary end-to-end sutured anastomosis over a T-tube under tension","Tension-free, mucosa-to-mucosa Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy performed by an experienced hepatobiliary surgeon (or place drains and refer immediately if HPB expertise unavailable—avoiding primary end-to-end ductal anastomosis)","Simple ligation of the proximal hepatic duct and abdominal closure","Immediate Whipple pancreaticoduodenectomy"],"a":1,"e":"Major bile duct transection with tissue loss/diathermy ischaemia (Strasberg Class E) carries a \u003e50-80% stricture rate if repaired by primary end-to-end choledochocholedochostomy because of tenuous axial blood supply and tension. Definitive reconstruction is a well-vascularised, tension-free, mucosa-to-mucosa Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy (Hepp-Couinaud extension onto the left hepatic duct if high hilar injury) by an HPB specialist; if unavailable intraoperatively, place wide subhepatic drains without further dissection and refer immediately.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01979","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Posterior Urethral Valves (PUV)","q":"A newborn male with antenatal oligohydramnios and bilateral hydroureteronephrosis fails to void properly, has a poor dribbling urinary stream, palpable distended bladder, serum creatinine 2.6 mg/dL, and metabolic acidosis. Voiding cystourethrogram (VCUG) demonstrates a dilated, elongated posterior urethra (\u0027keyhole sign\u0027), trabeculated bladder, and vesicoureteral reflux. What is the immediate stabilisation and definitive treatment?","o":["Open radical cystectomy","Oral tamsulosin monotherapy without catheterisation","Immediate urethral catheter drainage (small feeding tube without balloon) + fluid/electrolyte/sepsis stabilisation, followed by endoscopic transurethral valve fulguration/ablation","Immediate bilateral nephrectomy on day 1"],"a":2,"e":"Posterior Urethral Valves (Type I mucosal folds from verumontanum) are the commonest cause of congenital lower urinary tract obstruction in boys, causing the classic VCUG \u0027keyhole sign\u0027, renal dysplasia, and pulmonary hypoplasia. Immediate priority is bladder decompression (5F-6F infant feeding tube/catheter, monitoring post-obstructive diuresis and electrolytes) and treating urosepsis, followed once stable by primary endoscopic transurethral valve ablation (or temporary vesicostomy in tiny premature infants).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01980","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Intrauterine Adhesions (Asherman Syndrome)","q":"A 28-year-old woman develops secondary amenorrhoea and cyclical lower abdominal pain 4 months after an aggressive dilatation and curettage (D\u0026C) for postpartum retained placental products. Serum FSH, LH, TSH, and prolactin are normal, and progesterone + oestrogen-progesterone withdrawal tests fail to induce bleeding. Hysterosalpingography shows irregular intrauterine filling defects. What is the diagnosis and gold-standard treatment?","o":["Sheehan syndrome; lifelong thyroxine and hydrocortisone alone","Premature ovarian insufficiency; lifelong combined oral contraceptives","MRKH syndrome (Mullerian agenesis); McIndoe vaginoplasty","Asherman syndrome (intrauterine synechiae); hysteroscopic adhesiolysis (using cold scissors) followed by temporary intrauterine barrier/stent and cyclical oestrogen therapy"],"a":3,"e":"Endometrial basalis trauma (classically post-partum/post-abortal D\u0026C or genital TB in India) destroys endometrium and creates fibrous intrauterine adhesions (Asherman syndrome), causing secondary amenorrhoea/hypomenorrhoea unresponsive to oestrogen-progesterone challenge despite normal gonadotropins. Gold-standard diagnosis and treatment is direct hysteroscopic adhesiolysis with cold microscissors (avoiding thermal energy), followed by short-term Foley balloon/IUD barrier and high-dose cyclical oestrogen to promote endometrial regeneration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01981","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Clinical Chorioamnionitis (Triple I)","q":"A 27-year-old woman with PPROM at 31 weeks develops maternal fever (38.9 degrees C), baseline fetal tachycardia (176 bpm), maternal tachycardia (116/min), uterine fundal tenderness, and purulent malodorous amniotic fluid. What is the mandatory management?","o":["Immediate broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (Ampicillin + Gentamicin, adding Clindamycin/Metronidazole if Caesarean delivery) AND prompt delivery regardless of gestational age","Administer IV nifedipine tocolysis to delay delivery by 1 week","Give oral erythromycin and continue expectant management until 34 weeks","Wait for amniotic fluid culture results in 72 hours before intervening"],"a":0,"e":"Maternal fever \u003e= 38.0-39.0 C accompanied by fetal tachycardia, maternal leucocytosis, uterine tenderness, or purulent/foul liquor diagnoses clinical Chorioamnionitis (Intrauterine Infection/Inflammation — Triple I). Chorioamnionitis is an absolute contraindication to expectant management or tocolysis at ANY gestational age: immediate broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (ampicillin + gentamicin, plus anaerobic coverage if CS) and expedited delivery (induction/augmentation if reassuring for vaginal birth, or CS for obstetric indications) prevent maternal septic shock and fetal sepsis/PVL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01982","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Neonatal Cholestasis","q":"A 5-week-old thriving infant presents with persistent jaundice since week 2 of life, dark tea-stained urine staining diapers, and persistently pale clay-coloured (acholic) stools. Total bilirubin is 9.8 mg/dL with DIRECT (conjugated) bilirubin 7.2 mg/dL and markedly elevated GGT (580 U/L). Fasting abdominal ultrasound shows an absent/atretic gallbladder and \u0027triangular cord sign\u0027 at the porta hepatis; HIDA scan shows good hepatic uptake with no bowel excretion at 24 hours. Following confirmatory intraoperative cholangiogram/biopsy, what surgery MUST be performed ideally before 60 days of age?","o":["Urgent endoscopic retrograde sphincterotomy alone","Kasai hepatic portoenterostomy (Roux-en-Y)","Elective cholecystectomy at age 5 years","Oral ursodeoxycholic acid monotherapy without surgery for 1 year"],"a":1,"e":"Persistent neonatal jaundice \u003e 2-3 weeks with elevated direct bilirubin (\u003e1-2 mg/dL or \u003e20% of total), acholic stools, dark urine, high GGT, and triangular cord sign/non-excreting HIDA points to Extrahepatic Biliary Atresia—the commonest indication for paediatric liver transplantation. Performing a Kasai hepatic portoenterostomy BEFORE 60 days (ideally 30-45 days) of life achieves the highest rates of bile drainage (~60-80%) and delays/avoids cirrhosis; success drops dramatically after 90 days.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01983","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Glycogen Storage Diseases","q":"A 3-month-old infant presents with profound generalised hypotonia (\u0027floppy infant\u0027), macroglossia, mild hepatomegaly, feeding difficulty, and severe congestive heart failure. Chest X-ray shows massive globular cardiomegaly, and ECG reveals a characteristically SHORT PR interval (0.07 s) with huge QRS voltage complexes across all leads. Serum CK is elevated, and leukocyte/dried blood spot assay confirms lysosomal acid alpha-1,4-glucosidase (acid maltase) deficiency. What is the diagnosis and specific disease-modifying treatment?","o":["Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA Type 1); oral prednisolone","Von Gierke disease (GSD Type I); frequent uncooked cornstarch feeds alone","Infantile Pompe disease (Glycogen Storage Disease Type II); lifelong IV Enzyme Replacement Therapy with recombinant alglucosidase alfa","Duchenne muscular dystrophy; immediate cardiac transplantation at 3 months"],"a":2,"e":"Acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency causes lysosomal glycogen accumulation in cardiac, skeletal, and smooth muscle—Infantile Pompe Disease (GSD Type II). Unlike other GSDs (which primarily affect liver or skeletal muscle), infantile Pompe produces massive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, severe hypotonia, macroglossia, and a hallmark ECG of short PR interval with giant QRS voltages. Early Enzyme Replacement Therapy (alglucosidase alfa / avalglucosidase alfa) dramatically improves cardiomyopathy and ventilator-free survival.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01984","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Refeeding Syndrome","q":"A severely malnourished patient (BMI 13.8 kg/m2 after 3 weeks of bowel obstruction and starvation) is started rapidly on full-goal 2,200 kcal/day parenteral nutrition. Forty-eight hours later, he develops acute heart failure, pulmonary oedema, generalised weakness, arrhythmias, and serum phosphate 0.9 mg/dL, K+ 2.5 mEq/L, and Mg2+ 1.0 mg/dL. What is the hallmark electrolyte abnormality of Refeeding Syndrome, and how should nutrition have been initiated?","o":["Hyperkalaemia; restrict all electrolytes and double dextrose infusion","Hyperphosphataemia from tissue catabolism; start at 45 kcal/kg/day with loop diuretics alone","Hyponatraemia alone; give 3% hypertonic saline without checking phosphate","Severe hypophosphataemia (driven by insulin-mediated intracellular shift and ATP synthesis); start slowly at 10 kcal/kg/day (or 5 kcal/kg/day if extreme), give prophylactic IV Thiamine FIRST, and aggressively replete phosphate, K+, and Mg2+"],"a":3,"e":"During starvation, intracellular phosphate, potassium, magnesium, and thiamine are severely depleted despite normal baseline serum levels. Abrupt carbohydrate refeeding triggers a sharp insulin surge driving glucose, phosphate, K+, and Mg2+ intracellularly for ATP/glycogen synthesis—causing hallmark severe HYPOPHOSPHATAEMIA (respiratory muscle failure, rhabdomyolysis, cardiomyopathy, haemolysis), hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, sodium/water retention, and acute Wernicke encephalopathy. Prevention uses slow caloric initiation (~10 kcal/kg/day increasing over 4-7 days), pre-feed IV thiamine, and close electrolyte replacement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01985","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Congenital Thoracic Malformations","q":"A 21-year-old man with recurrent left lower-lobe pneumonias since childhood undergoes contrast-enhanced CT angiography of the chest, revealing a non-functioning multicystic mass in the left posterior basal segment that lacks communication with the tracheobronchial tree and receives a large feeding artery arising directly from the descending thoracic aorta, draining via the left inferior pulmonary vein. What is the diagnosis and standard treatment?","o":["Intralobar Bronchopulmonary Sequestration; surgical lobectomy/segmentectomy (or endovascular embolisation of the systemic feeder)","Extralobar sequestration (which drains into systemic azygos/IVC veins, not pulmonary vein); oral antibiotics alone","Bronchogenic cyst; bronchoscopic aspiration alone","Congenital lobar emphysema; needle decompression"],"a":0,"e":"Bronchopulmonary sequestration is a dysplastic mass of non-functioning lung tissue lacking normal tracheobronchial connection and supplied by an anomalous systemic artery (usually descending thoracic or abdominal aorta). Intralobar sequestration (~75%) shares the same visceral pleura as the lower lobe (typically left posterior basal), presents with recurrent pneumonia in adolescents/young adults, and drains into the PULMONARY veins (whereas Extralobar sequestration has its own visceral pleura, presents in infancy, and drains into SYSTEMIC veins such as azygos/hemiazygos/IVC). Resection after ligating the systemic artery prevents fatal haemorrhage and recurrent sepsis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01986","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Viral CNS Infections","q":"A 44-year-old man presents with 3 days of fever, bizarre personality changes, olfactory hallucinations, aphasia, and focal-to-bilateral tonic-clonic seizures. Brain MRI (FLAIR and DWI) shows asymmetric T2/FLAIR hyperintensity and restricted diffusion involving the bilateral (left \u003e right) medial temporal lobes, hippocampi, insular cortex, and cingulate gyrus, sparing the lentiform nucleus. CSF shows 85 lymphocytes/mm3 and 420 RBCs/mm3. What is the diagnosis and urgent therapy?","o":["Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis; IV alteplase","Herpes Simplex Virus-1 (HSV-1) Encephalitis; start IV Acyclovir (10 mg/kg q8h for 14-21 days) immediately while awaiting CSF HSV PCR","Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy; oral steroids","Japanese encephalitis (which preferentially involves bilateral thalami and basal ganglia); supportive care alone"],"a":1,"e":"Asymmetric limbic involvement—medial temporal lobes, hippocampus, insula, and inferior frontal/cingulate cortex, characteristically sparing the basal ganglia (lentiform nucleus)—combined with fever, altered behaviour/seizures, and haemorrhagic lymphocytic CSF is classic for HSV-1 encephalitis. Because HSV-1 is the most common treatable cause of sporadic fatal encephalitis, empirical IV Acyclovir (10 mg/kg IV q8h) must be initiated immediately without waiting for PCR.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01987","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Giant Cell Tumour (GCT) of Bone","q":"A 29-year-old woman (closed growth plates) presents with progressive knee pain and swelling. Knee radiographs reveal an eccentric, purely lytic, expansile \u0027soap-bubble\u0027 lesion located in the EPIPHYSIS-metaphysis of the distal femur extending right up to the subchondral articular surface, without periosteal reaction or matrix calcification. Biopsy shows numerous multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells in a mononuclear stromal background (RANKL positive). What is the diagnosis and standard surgical treatment?","o":["Giant Cell Tumour (Osteoclastoma) of bone; extended intralesional curettage with high-speed burr, local adjuvants (phenol/cryotherapy/argon), and bone cement (PMMA) packing (denosumab for unresectable/axial cases)","Osteosarcoma; immediate above-knee amputation","Unicameral simple bone cyst; observation alone","Multiple myeloma; total knee arthroplasty without biopsy"],"a":0,"e":"Giant Cell Tumour (GCT) occurs almost exclusively in skeletally mature young adults (20-40 years, closed physes) as an eccentric epiphyseal-metaphyseal lytic (\u0027soap-bubble\u0027) lesion abutting subchondral bone—most commonly distal femur, proximal tibia, and distal radius. Though histologically benign, it is locally aggressive with ~1-3% \u0027benign pulmonary metastases\u0027. Extended intralesional curettage + burring + adjuvant + PMMA cement reconstruction preserves the native joint while minimising recurrence (denosumab targets RANKL in unresectable/sacral lesions).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01988","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Central Serous Chorioretinopathy (CSCR)","q":"A 36-year-old male corporate executive with high stress using a topical corticosteroid cream for facial eczema presents with sudden painless central blurring, micropsia (objects look smaller), and mild hypermetropic shift in his right eye. OCT macula shows a dome-shaped serous neurosensory retinal detachment at the fovea, and fundus fluorescein angiography (FFA) shows an early pinpoint hyperfluorescent leak rising upward and spreading laterally like a \u0027smokestack\u0027. What is the MOST critical initial step in management?","o":["Perform immediate panretinal photocoagulation","Administer intravitreal ganciclovir","Discontinue ALL exogenous corticosteroids (topical, inhaled, systemic) and observe initially (as most acute CSCR resolves spontaneously in 3 months; focal laser/PDT if chronic)","Start high-dose oral prednisolone 60 mg/day immediately"],"a":2,"e":"Central Serous Chorioretinopathy (CSCR) classically affects young-to-middle-aged men with Type-A personality/stress or exogenous corticosteroid exposure (oral, topical, nasal, inhaled), producing serous macular elevation, micropsia, hyperopic shift, and \u0027smokestack\u0027 or \u0027inkblot\u0027 leakage on FFA. Crucially, unlike most inflammatory retinal diseases, steroids WORSEN CSCR; stopping all corticosteroids immediately and observing for 3 months leads to spontaneous resolution in 80-90% (half-dose photodynamic therapy or focal laser is used if persistent \u003e3-4 months).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01989","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Non-Ischaemic Acute Coronary Mimics","q":"A 64-year-old postmenopausal woman presents 2 hours after learning of the sudden death of her son with acute substernal chest pain, dyspnoea, anterior ST-segment elevation in V2-V5, and mild troponin I elevation (disproportionately modest for the extent of wall motion abnormality). Urgent coronary angiography shows completely normal epicardial coronary arteries, while left ventriculography demonstrates akinesia/ballooning of the LV apex and mid-segments with hypercontractile basal segments (LVEF 30%). What is the diagnosis and key haemodynamic precaution if hypotension develops?","o":["Anterior STEMI from plaque rupture; immediate IV streptokinase thrombolysis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; lifelong ICD without coronary angiography","Viral myocarditis; immediate high-dose cyclophosphamide","Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy; check echo for dynamic LV outflow tract (LVOT) obstruction before giving positive inotropes (treat LVOTO with fluids + beta-blockers rather than dobutamine)"],"a":3,"e":"Takotsubo (stress/apical ballooning) cardiomyopathy classically affects postmenopausal women following severe emotional or physical stress, mimicking anterior STEMI with ST elevations and transient apical akinesia/basal hyperkinesia extending beyond a single coronary territory, normal coronaries on angiography, and spontaneous LVEF recovery within weeks. Crucially, hypercontractile basal segments can create dynamic LVOT obstruction (~15-20%): giving catecholamines/inotropes (dobutamine/dopamine) worsens LVOTO and shock—requiring bedside echo first (treating LVOTO with fluids and short-acting beta-blockers).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01990","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP)","q":"A 52-year-old man with decompensated alcoholic cirrhosis and tense ascites presents with mild confusion, low-grade fever (38.0 degrees C), and diffuse abdominal tenderness. Diagnostic paracentesis shows ascitic fluid PMN count 480 cells/mm3, total protein 0.9 g/dL, and serum creatinine 1.6 mg/dL. Beyond IV cefotaxime (2 g q8h), which adjunctive therapy prevents hepatorenal syndrome and reduces mortality?","o":["Intravenous human albumin (1.5 g/kg on day 1 and 1.0 g/kg on day 3)","Oral neomycin monotherapy without systemic antibiotics","Large-volume 8-liter paracentesis without colloid replacement","High-dose oral furosemide 160 mg/day and spironolactone 400 mg/day"],"a":0,"e":"An ascitic neutrophil (PMN) count \u003e=250 cells/mm3 confirms Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP). Alongside third-generation cephalosporins (IV cefotaxime or ceftriaxone), Sort\u0027s protocol of weight-based IV albumin (1.5 g/kg at diagnosis and 1.0 g/kg on day 3) significantly reduces Type 1 hepatorenal syndrome and inpatient mortality, particularly when serum creatinine \u003e1.0 mg/dL, BUN \u003e30 mg/dL, or bilirubin \u003e4 mg/dL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01991","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Fournier Gangrene","q":"A 58-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes presents with agonizing scrotal and perineal pain, fever 39.2 degrees C, BP 82/52 mmHg, dusky violaceous perineal skin with hemorrhagic bullae, foul dishwater-gray exudate, and palpable subcutaneous crepitus extending toward the inguinal canal. What is the mandatory immediate management?","o":["Wait 48 hours for MRI soft-tissue demarcation before surgical incision","Immediate resuscitation, empiric broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (covering aerobes, anaerobes, and MRSA plus clindamycin for antitoxin effect), and emergent surgical exploration with wide radical debridement without delaying for CT/MRI","Outpatient oral cephalexin and warm sitz baths with review after 5 days","Apply topical mupirocin ointment and perform needle aspiration only"],"a":1,"e":"Fournier gangrene is a rapidly lethal polymicrobial necrotizing fasciitis of the genitalia and perineum (spreading along Colles and Scarpa fasciae at up to 2-3 cm/hr). Clinical diagnosis mandates immediate emergent surgical debridement back to bleeding, viable tissue plus broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (e.g., piperacillin-tazobactam or meropenem + vancomycin + clindamycin); delaying surgery for imaging dramatically increases mortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01992","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Rh Alloimmunization Prophylaxis","q":"An unsensitized RhD-negative mother (Indirect Coombs Test negative) delivers an RhD-positive term neonate via cesarean section complicated by manual removal of an adherent placenta. How much standard anti-D immunoglobulin should be given initially, within what postpartum window, and which laboratory test detects excessive fetomaternal hemorrhage requiring additional anti-D vials?","o":["300 mcg IM only if the Indirect Coombs Test is already strongly positive (1:64)","No anti-D is needed after cesarean section if routine 28-week dose was given","300 mcg IM within 72 hours of delivery; perform Kleihauer-Betke acid-elution test (or flow cytometry) to quantify fetal RBC volume and add 300 mcg per 30 mL fetal whole blood (15 mL fetal RBCs)","50 mcg oral tablet at 6 weeks postpartum; perform Direct Coombs test on the mother"],"a":2,"e":"Routine postpartum prophylaxis for an unsensitized RhD-negative mother delivering an RhD-positive infant is 300 mcg anti-D immunoglobulin IM within 72 hours (covering up to 30 mL of fetal whole blood / 15 mL packed fetal RBCs). Because manual placental removal risks large fetomaternal hemorrhage, the Kleihauer-Betke acid-elution assay quantifies fetal hemoglobin-containing erythrocytes so extra anti-D doses can be administered.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01993","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Necrotizing Enterocolitis (NEC)","q":"A 29-week preterm neonate (birth weight 1,100 g) on day 10 of life develops abdominal distension, bilious gastric residuals, bloody stools, lethargy, and apnea. Supine abdominal radiograph reveals linear intramural gas bubbles along the bowel wall (pneumatosis intestinalis) without free intraperitoneal air. What is the diagnosis and immediate management?","o":["Benign cow-milk protein allergy; switch to oral soy formula without antibiotics","Physiological meconium plug; increase formula feeding volumes immediately","Duodenal atresia; perform immediate barium enema","Necrotizing Enterocolitis (Bell Stage II); make NPO, place nasogastric suction decompression, start broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, total parenteral nutrition, and obtain serial supine/left-lateral decubitus abdominal radiographs"],"a":3,"e":"Pneumatosis intestinalis (intramural bowel gas produced by bacterial fermentation) in an ill preterm infant with bloody stools and bilious aspirates is pathognomonic for Bell Stage II Necrotizing Enterocolitis (NEC). Immediate medical management requires bowel rest (NPO), continuous NG decompression, broad-spectrum parenteral antibiotics, fluid/cardiorespiratory support, and serial abdominal radiographs (every 6-8 hrs) with surgical standby for perforation (pneumoperitoneum = Stage IIIB).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01994","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Psychiatry","topic":"Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS)","q":"Four days after starting high-dose IM haloperidol for acute mania, a 31-year-old man develops temperature 40.4 degrees C, profuse diaphoresis, labile blood pressure, mutism, generalized \u0027lead-pipe\u0027 muscular rigidity, hyporeflexia, leukocytosis, and serum creatine kinase (CK) 18,400 U/L. What is the diagnosis and primary management?","o":["Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS); stop haloperidol immediately, provide aggressive IV hydration/cooling, and consider dantrolene or bromocriptine","Serotonin syndrome; administer IV cyproheptadine and increase haloperidol","Simple anticholinergic toxicity; administer physostigmine without hydration","Acute malignant catatonia; treat with higher-dose haloperidol depot injection"],"a":0,"e":"Dopamine D2 receptor blockade by potent antipsychotics (like haloperidol) triggers Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS), evolving over days with hyperthermia, autonomic instability, severe \u0027lead-pipe\u0027 rigidity, suppressed reflexes, and massive rhabdomyolysis (elevated CK). Treatment requires immediate discontinuation of the antipsychotic, aggressive IV fluids (preventing myoglobinuric AKI), cooling, and dantrolene (muscle relaxant) or bromocriptine (dopamine agonist).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01995","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Juvenile Nasopharyngeal Angiofibroma (JNA)","q":"A 14-year-old boy presents with recurrent, profuse, unprovoked unilateral left epistaxis and progressive nasal blockage. Nasal endoscopy shows a smooth, lobulated reddish-pink mass filling the posterior choana, and contrast CT shows a hypervascular mass widening the sphenopalatine foramen with anterior bowing of the posterior wall of the maxillary sinus (Holman-Miller sign). What diagnostic/therapeutic rule applies?","o":["Start long-term intranasal corticosteroid spray for allergic antrochoanal polyp","Never perform an office/outpatient incisional biopsy due to risk of catastrophic hemorrhage; proceed with MRI/angiography, preoperative arterial embolization, and complete surgical excision","Treat with high-dose oral fluconazole for rhinosporidiosis","Perform an immediate outpatient punch biopsy in the OPD clinic under local spray"],"a":1,"e":"Juvenile Nasopharyngeal Angiofibroma (JNA) is a histologically benign yet locally aggressive vascular tumor arising almost exclusively in adolescent males near the sphenopalatine foramen, classically demonstrating the Holman-Miller (antral bowing) sign on CT. Because its thin-walled vascular channels lack a muscular coat, office biopsy precipitates torrential, life-threatening bleeding; diagnosis is made clinically and radiologically before preoperative embolization and endoscopic/open resection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01996","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Pericardial and Myocardial Haemodynamics","q":"A 46-year-old man with treated pulmonary tuberculosis 4 years ago presents with progressive ascites, peripheral oedema, elevated JVP rising on inspiration (Kussmaul\u0027s sign), rapid \u0027y\u0027 descent, pericardial knock, and dense pericardial calcification on lateral chest X-ray. Cardiac catheterisation shows equalisation of LV and RV diastolic pressures (18 mmHg), a \u0027dip-and-plateau\u0027 (square-root) ventricular pressure trace, and DISCORDANT inspiratory changes in LV and RV systolic pressures (ventricular interdependence). What is the diagnosis and definitive curative treatment?","o":["Cardiac amyloidosis (restrictive cardiomyopathy); permanent pacemaker alone","Dilated cardiomyopathy; heart transplantation as routine first-line","Chronic Constrictive Pericarditis; complete surgical Pericardiectomy","Acute viral myocarditis; oral NSAIDs alone"],"a":2,"e":"In India, tuberculosis remains a major cause of chronic constrictive pericarditis. Encasement by a rigid calcified pericardium equalises end-diastolic pressures in all four chambers, yields a \u0027dip-and-plateau\u0027 (square-root) curve, and forces ventricular interdependence (during inspiration, increased venous return to RV shifts the septum into LV, causing LV systolic pressure to fall while RV systolic pressure rises—\u0027discordance\u0027, whereas restrictive cardiomyopathy shows parallel \u0027concordant\u0027 changes and LV \u003e RV EDP by \u003e5 mmHg). Complete surgical pericardiectomy is curative.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01997","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Rare Diffuse Parenchymal Lung Diseases","q":"A 39-year-old male smoker presents with progressive dyspnoea and dry cough. HRCT chest demonstrates bilateral geographic ground-glass opacities with superimposed interlobular and intralobular septal thickening (\u0027crazy-paving\u0027 pattern). Bronchoalveolar lavage yields opaque, milky fluid containing amorphous lipoproteinaceous globules that stain strongly positive with Periodic Acid-Schiff (PAS), and serum anti-GM-CSF autoantibodies are positive. What is the diagnosis and gold-standard treatment?","o":["Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia; IV pentamidine","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis; oral pirfenidone","Pulmonary oedema; IV furosemide alone","Autoimmune Pulmonary Alveolar Proteinosis (PAP); Whole-Lung Lavage (under general anaesthesia via double-lumen ETT) +/- inhaled GM-CSF"],"a":3,"e":"Autoimmune PAP (~90% of adult PAP) is caused by neutralising IgG autoantibodies against Granulocyte-Macrophage Colony-Stimulating Factor (GM-CSF), impairing alveolar macrophage catabolism of surfactant. It classically presents with \u0027crazy-paving\u0027 HRCT, milky PAS-positive diastase-resistant BAL fluid, and high anti-GM-CSF antibodies (plus risk of Nocardia infection). Standard definitive therapy for hypoxaemic/symptomatic disease is sequential Whole-Lung Lavage with warm saline via a double-lumen endotracheal tube (and supplemental inhaled/SC GM-CSF; avoid steroids which worsen surfactant accumulation and opportunistic infection).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01998","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Neuroendocrine Tumours","q":"A 54-year-old man with a midgut (ileal) neuroendocrine tumour and multiple hepatic metastases presents with episodic facial flushing, watery diarrhoea, wheezing, elevated 24-hour urinary 5-HIAA, and severe tricuspid regurgitation with pulmonary stenosis (normal left-sided mitral/aortic valves). He is scheduled for hepatic cytoreduction/resection. Why are left-sided heart valves typically spared, and what prophylaxis prevents fatal intraoperative \u0027carcinoid crisis\u0027?","o":["Pulmonary endothelial monoamine oxidase inactivates serotonin in the lungs before blood reaches the left heart; administer perioperative IV/SC Octreotide (somatostatin analogue)","Serotonin is produced only in the left atrium; give oral warfarin alone","The aortic valve is immune to fibrosis because it lacks endothelium; give IV epinephrine bolus preoperatively","Hepatic metastases bypass the right heart; give IV dopamine routinely"],"a":0,"e":"Metastatic midgut carcinoid tumours secrete serotonin and vasoactive peptides directly into the systemic venous circulation (bypassing hepatic first-pass inactivation once liver metastases exist), causing endocardial fibrosis of the RIGHT-sided valves (tricuspid regurgitation, pulmonary stenosis) before pulmonary monoamine oxidase degrades serotonin prior to the left heart (unless a PFO or bronchial carcinoid exists). Surgical tumour manipulation or catecholamines can trigger catastrophic vasoplegic/bronchospastic Carcinoid Crisis, prevented and treated with perioperative Octreotide infusion (avoiding beta-agonists/epinephrine).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q01999","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Enterocutaneous Fistula (ECF)","q":"Eight days after laparotomy for adhesiolysis, a 46-year-old man develops a high-output (900 mL/24 h) bilious enterocutaneous fistula draining through the midline wound, with surrounding skin excoriation and fever. Following the \u0027SNAP\u0027 protocol (Sepsis/Skin, Nutrition, Anatomy, Plan), what is the immediate priority in week 1-2, and which factor guarantees failure of spontaneous non-operative closure?","o":["Keep NPO without any parenteral nutrition for 6 weeks","Control intra-abdominal sepsis/abscess, protect perifistular skin, correct fluid/electrolytes, and establish nutrition (TPN/enteral) BEFORE delaying definitive surgery \u003e= 6-12 weeks; distal bowel obstruction prevents spontaneous closure","Give oral loperamide alone without fluid replacement or skin care","Perform immediate re-laparotomy on Day 8 through dense hostile inflammatory adhesions to resect the fistula"],"a":1,"e":"Immediate re-operation between days 7 and 42 in a hostile \u0027frozen\u0027 inflammatory abdomen causes catastrophic inadvertent enterotomies and recurrent fistulae. Management follows SNAP: (1) Sepsis drainage \u0026 Skin barrier/wound manager; (2) Nutrition \u0026 electrolyte resuscitation (high-output proximal ECFs typically need TPN +/- octreotide); (3) Anatomy delineation (fistulogram/CT); and (4) Plan definitive single-stage resection \u003e= 6-12+ weeks later if spontaneous closure fails due to FRIENDS factors (Foreign body, Radiation, Infection/IBD, Epithelialisation, Neoplasm, Distal obstruction, Short tract \u003c2 cm).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02000","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Marjolin\u0027s Ulcer","q":"A 52-year-old man who sustained deep flame burns over his lower leg 22 years ago presents with a chronic unstable scar that broke down 6 months ago into a foul-smelling, rapidly growing ulcer with raised, everted, indurated edges fixed to underlying fascia, and two firm 2 cm ipsilateral inguinal lymph nodes. What is the diagnosis and standard diagnostic/surgical approach?","o":["Benign hypertrophic keloid; intralesional triamcinolone injections","Basal cell carcinoma; topical imiquimod cream","Marjolin\u0027s ulcer (well-differentiated Squamous Cell Carcinoma arising in a chronic burn scar); multiple edge/base biopsies, nodal FNAC, and wide local excision with \u003e= 2 cm margins (+/- therapeutic lymphadenectomy if node-positive)","Venous stasis ulcer; 4-layer compression bandaging without biopsy"],"a":2,"e":"Marjolin\u0027s ulcer is an aggressive cutaneous malignancy—predominantly Squamous Cell Carcinoma—arising decades after chronic wounds, old burn scars, venous ulcers, or chronic osteomyelitis sinuses (Marjolin\u0027s ulcer behaves more aggressively with higher nodal metastasis ~20-35% than sun-induced SCC because dense scar tissue initially delays recognition before deep/lymphatic invasion). Multiple quadrant edge biopsies confirm SCC, followed by wide local excision (\u003e= 2 cm margins, or amputation if bone/neurovascular bundle invaded) and therapeutic regional lymph node dissection if cytology-positive.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02001","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Disorders of Sex Development (DSD)","q":"A 17-year-old phenotypically normal tall female presents with primary amenorrhoea and absent breast development (Tanner Stage I breasts, normal female external genitalia, present uterus and vagina on ultrasound). Serum FSH is markedly elevated (78 IU/L), oestradiol is very low, and peripheral blood karyotype reveals 46,XY (Swyer syndrome / pure gonadal dysgenesis). Alongside cyclic oestrogen-progestogen replacement for puberty/bone health, what surgical intervention is mandatory promptly upon diagnosis?","o":["Total hysterectomy leaving the streak gonads in situ","Delay gonadectomy until age 35 because Y-bearing streak gonads are benign","Give clomiphene citrate for ovulation induction","Immediate bilateral laparoscopic gonadectomy (excision of streak gonads) due to ~30% risk of malignant gonadoblastoma / dysgerminoma"],"a":3,"e":"In 46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis (Swyer syndrome, often SRY mutation), absent AMH and testosterone allow normal Mullerian development (uterus/vagina present) with bilateral dysgenetic streak gonads and hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. Any dysgenetic gonad harbouring Y-chromosome material (GBY locus) carries a 25-35% risk of developing gonadoblastoma and malignant dysgerminoma early in life; therefore, immediate bilateral gonadectomy upon diagnosis is mandatory (leaving the uterus intact to carry future donor-oocyte pregnancy with HRT!).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02002","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Peripartum Cardiomyopathy (PPCM)","q":"A 31-year-old previously healthy woman at 37 weeks of gestation presents with acute orthopnoea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, bilateral basal crackles, elevated JVP, S3 gallop, and echocardiography showing new-onset dilated LV with LVEF 32% and no other identifiable cause of heart failure (Peripartum Cardiomyopathy). Which antihypertensive/afterload-reducing regimen is safe BEFORE delivery, and which standard HFrEF drugs MUST be avoided until AFTER delivery?","o":["Use Hydralazine + Isosorbide dinitrate (+ furosemide and beta-1 selective blocker e.g. metoprolol); strictly AVOID ACE inhibitors, ARBs, Sacubitril-Valsartan, and Spironolactone until postpartum","Give 3 litres of IV normal saline bolus and IV ergometrine","Start Enalapril + Sacubitril-Valsartan + Spironolactone immediately at 37 weeks antenatally","Perform immediate forced vaginal delivery with ergometrine prophylaxis in third stage"],"a":0,"e":"Peripartum cardiomyopathy is a diagnosis of exclusion presenting with systolic HF (LVEF \u003c 45%) in the final month of pregnancy or first 5 months postpartum. Antenatally, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, ARNIs, and aldosterone antagonists are contraindicated due to fetal renal dysplasia, oligohydramnios, and teratogenicity; afterload reduction uses Hydralazine + Nitrates alongside cautious loop diuretics and metoprolol (plus anticoagulation if LVEF \u003c 30-35%). Postpartum, full HFrEF GDMT (and bromocriptine per ESC considerations) is initiated, and ergometrine is strictly avoided as it triggers acute hypertensive pulmonary oedema.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02003","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Spinal Muscular Atrophy (SMA Type 1)","q":"A 3-month-old infant presents with progressive severe hypotonia (\u0027frog-leg\u0027 posture), absent deep tendon reflexes, weak cry, bell-shaped chest with paradoxical diaphragmatic breathing, normal extraocular movements with bright alert social smile, and visible fine tongue fasciculations. Serum creatine kinase (CK) is normal. Which genetic defect causes this condition (Werdnig-Hoffmann disease / SMA Type 1)?","o":["X-linked dystrophin gene deletion (Duchenne muscular dystrophy)","Autosomal recessive homozygous deletion of exon 7 in the SMN1 (Survival Motor Neuron 1) gene on chromosome 5q13","Lysosomal acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency (Pompe disease)","Trisomy 18 (Edwards syndrome)"],"a":1,"e":"Anterior horn motor neuron degeneration in SMA Type 1 (Werdnig-Hoffmann disease) produces severe symmetrical lower motor neuron weakness (areflexia, frog-leg hypotonia, tongue fasciculations, intercostal weakness with spared diaphragm) while preserving cortical alertness, facial expression, and extraocular movements, with normal/mildly elevated CK. ~95% are caused by homozygous deletion of SMN1 exon 7 on chromosome 5q13, now treatable with SMN-targeted therapies (nusinersen, risdiplam, onasemnogene abeparvovec).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02004","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Inherited Renal Tubulopathies","q":"A 13-year-old girl presents with chronic fatigue, carpopedal muscle cramps, and salt craving. BP is normal-to-low (98/62 mmHg). Labs show K+ 2.7 mEq/L, HCO3- 32 mEq/L, arterial pH 7.48 (hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis), serum Mg2+ 1.1 mg/dL (hypomagnesaemia), elevated renin/aldosterone, and markedly LOW urinary calcium excretion (hypocalciuria, urine Ca/Cr ratio \u003c 0.04). Which autosomal recessive tubulopathy mimics chronic thiazide diuretic use?","o":["Bartter syndrome (loop of Henle NKCC2/ROMK defect, which causes hypercalciuria and nephrocalcinosis)","Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome)","Gitelman syndrome (inactivating mutation of the distal convoluted tubule Na+-Cl- cotransporter, NCC)","Liddle syndrome (gain-of-function ENaC causing severe hypertension and low renin/aldosterone)"],"a":2,"e":"Gitelman syndrome (SLC12A3 mutation impairing distal convoluted tubule NaCl cotransporter NCC, mimicking thiazide therapy) presents in late childhood/adolescence with normotensive hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis, prominent HYPOMAGNESAEMIA, and HYPOCALCIURIA (unlike Bartter syndrome, which impairs thick ascending limb NKCC2 mimicking loop diuretics, presenting earlier with polyhydramnios/failure to thrive and HYPERCALCIURIA/nephrocalcinosis). Management uses high-salt/K+ diet, oral Mg2+ and KCl supplements, and amiloride/spironolactone.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02005","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"CIED Intraoperative Management","q":"A pacemaker-dependent patient with an Implantable Cardioverter-Defibrillator (ICD) is scheduled for urgent abdominal surgery requiring monopolar electrocautery near the umbilicus (electromagnetic interference risk). When a standard ring magnet is placed directly over an ICD versus a conventional permanent pacemaker, what is the expected device response?","o":["Over a conventional pacemaker, a magnet turns off all pacing completely","Over an ICD, a magnet always switches pacing to asynchronous VOO mode and leaves shocks active","Over an ICD, a magnet permanently erases all stored device programming","Over an ICD, a magnet suspends ONLY tachyarrhythmia detection/shocks WITHOUT changing pacing mode (pacing remains inhibited by cautery unless reprogrammed to DOO/VOO!); over a conventional pacemaker, a magnet forces asynchronous fixed-rate pacing (DOO/VOO)"],"a":3,"e":"Understanding CIED magnet physics is critical in perioperative anaesthesia: placing a magnet over an ICD suspends antitachycardia therapies (preventing inappropriate shocks from electrocautery electromagnetic interference) while leaving bradycardia pacing unchanged! Therefore, a pacemaker-dependent patient with an ICD STILL risks asystole from cautery oversensing unless the ICD is formally reprogrammed to asynchronous (DOO/VOO) preoperatively (or bipolar cautery/short bursts with dispersive pad placed far away). Over a standard pacemaker, a magnet switches pacing to asynchronous mode.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02006","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease Imaging","q":"A 21-year-old man with chronic right lower quadrant pain, non-bloody diarrhoea, and perianal fistulae undergoes MR Enterography (MRE). T1 post-contrast and T2 sequences reveal segmental terminal ileal wall thickening with stratified \u0027target\u0027 mural hyperenhancement, engorged vasa recta perpendicular to the bowel wall (\u0027comb sign\u0027), and fibrofatty mesenteric proliferation separating bowel loops (\u0027creeping fat\u0027). What do the \u0027comb sign\u0027 and stratified mural enhancement indicate, and why is MRE preferred over repeated CT enterography?","o":["Active transmural inflammatory Crohn\u0027s disease (rather than chronic burnt-out fibrotic stricture); MRE achieves equivalent sensitivity while avoiding cumulative ionising radiation in young patients requiring serial scans","Carcinoid tumour exclusively; MRE cannot detect fistulae","Ulcerative colitis confined to the rectum; MRE requires high-dose barium","Ischaemic colitis; MRE gives higher radiation than CT"],"a":0,"e":"Engorged hypervascular mesenteric vasa recta (\u0027comb sign\u0027), stratified mural hyperenhancement, restricted diffusion, and mesenteric oedema on MR Enterography specifically signify ACTIVE acute inflammatory Crohn\u0027s disease amenable to anti-inflammatory/biologic therapy (rather than quiescent fibrotic strictures requiring endoscopic dilation/resection). Because IBD patients are young and require repeated lifelong imaging, radiation-free MRE is preferred over CT enterography.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02007","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Benign Bone Tumours","q":"A 16-year-old boy complains of aching pain in his right shin that becomes severe at NIGHT and is dramatically relieved within 20-30 minutes by taking oral ibuprofen or aspirin. Thin-section CT of the tibia demonstrates a well-circumscribed 9 mm (\u003c1.5 cm) radiolucent intracortical nidus with central punctate calcification surrounded by dense fusiform reactive cortical sclerosis. What is the diagnosis and standard minimally invasive curative treatment?","o":["Osteoblastoma (typically \u003e 2 cm in posterior spinal elements and less responsive to NSAIDs); radical amputation","Osteoid osteoma; percutaneous CT-guided Radiofrequency Ablation (RFA) of the central nidus","Stress fracture; cast immobilisation for 6 months without imaging","Brodie\u0027s subacute osteomyelitis abscess (which shows a \u0027penumbra sign\u0027 on T1 MRI and serpiginous cloaca); lifelong IV antibiotics"],"a":1,"e":"Prostaglandin (PGE2)-secreting Osteoid Osteoma classically affects adolescents/young adults with nocturnal bone pain exquisitely relieved by NSAIDs/salicylates and a \u003c1.5-2 cm radiolucent cortical nidus (usually femur/tibia) surrounded by intense reactive cortical sclerosis. Thin-slice CT is the modality of choice to visualise the nidus, and CT-guided percutaneous Radiofrequency Ablation (RFA) destroys the nidus with \u003e90-95% single-session cure and minimal morbidity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02008","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Forearm Fracture-Dislocations","q":"A 26-year-old man shields his head from a direct blow with a stick and presents with forearm deformity and inability to extend his thumb and metacarpophalangeal joints (preserving wrist radial extension). Forearm radiographs show a fracture of the proximal third of the ulna with anterior dislocation of the radial head (Bado Type I Monteggia fracture-dislocation). What is the classic associated nerve injury, and what is the primary operative step in adults?","o":["Median nerve palsy; fix the radial head first and leave the ulna unplated","Ulnar nerve palsy; treat in a sling without ulnar reduction","Posterior Interosseous Nerve (PIN) palsy; anatomical Open Reduction and Internal Fixation (ORIF) of the ulna with plate (which usually reduces the radial head spontaneously)","Anterior interosseous nerve palsy; excise the radial head immediately in all acute cases"],"a":2,"e":"Monteggia fracture-dislocation = fracture of the proximal/middle ulna + dislocation of the radial head at the proximal radioulnar joint (contrast with Galeazzi = distal radius shaft fracture + distal radioulnar joint dislocation). Anterior/lateral radial head displacement stretches the Posterior Interosseous Nerve (PIN) as it enters the arcade of Frohse, causing finger/thumb MCP drop while sparing ECRL wrist extension. In adults, anatomical plate ORIF restoring ulnar length and alignment reduces the radial head spontaneously (open radial head reduction only if interposed annular ligament blocks reduction).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02009","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Psychiatry","topic":"Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT)","q":"A 58-year-old man with life-threatening catatonic psychotic depression and active suicidal refusal of all food/fluids is referred for urgent modified Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT). Which statement accurately reflects current psychiatric/anaesthetic guidelines regarding contraindications to ECT and the highest-risk relative condition?","o":["ECT must never be given in psychotic depression","Pregnancy and cardiac pacemakers are absolute contraindications to ECT","Age \u003e 55 years is an absolute contraindication to ECT","There are NO absolute contraindications to ECT; space-occupying intracranial lesions with raised intracranial pressure (ICP), recent MI/stroke, and unstable aneurysm are high-risk relative situations requiring stabilisation/medical ICP lowering prior to ECT"],"a":3,"e":"Modern modified ECT (with short-acting anaesthetic + succinylcholine muscle relaxation + 100% oxygenation) has NO absolute contraindications when weighed against life-threatening psychiatric emergencies (malignant catatonia, severe suicidality, refractory psychotic depression). However, conditions sensitive to the brief ictal surge in blood pressure and cerebral blood flow—especially intracranial mass lesions with raised ICP/herniation risk, recent myocardial infarction (\u003c4-6 weeks), recent intracranial haemorrhage, and unstable vascular aneurysms—are high-risk relative situations requiring neurosurgical/cardiology optimisation (e.g., steroids/mannitol, esmolol) beforehand. Pregnancy and pacemakers are NOT contraindications.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02010","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Polymorphic Ventricular Tachycardia","q":"A 67-year-old woman taking oral clarithromycin, ondansetron, and haloperidol (serum K+ 2.8 mEq/L, Mg2+ 1.6 mg/dL) experiences recurrent syncope. Telemetry shows sinus bradycardia with QTc 580 ms, premature ventricular beats (\u0027R-on-T\u0027), and runs of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia whose QRS axis twists sinusoidally around the isoelectric baseline (Torsades de Pointes), reverting spontaneously to sinus rhythm. What is the immediate first-line antiarrhythmic drug?","o":["IV Magnesium Sulphate (2 g bolus over 1-2 min, followed by infusion) EVEN if serum magnesium is normal, plus stopping QT-prolonging drugs, repleting K+ to 4.5-5.0 mEq/L, and overdrive pacing/isoproterenol if pause-dependent","IV Procainamide 1 g bolus (Class IA drug that prolongs QT)","IV Amiodarone 300 mg bolus (which further prolongs QTc)","IV Digoxin loading"],"a":0,"e":"Acquired Long QT Syndrome (precipitated by macrolides, antipsychotics, ondansetron, fluoroquinolones, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, bradycardia) triggers early afterdepolarisations causing Torsades de Pointes (TdP). Pulseless/unstable TdP requires immediate unsynchronised defibrillation; stable/recurrent TdP is treated immediately with IV Magnesium Sulphate (2 g bolus) regardless of baseline serum Mg2+ (suppressing L-type calcium early afterdepolarisations without shortening QT), stopping offending drugs, raising K+ to 4.5-5.0 mEq/L, and accelerating heart rate via temporary overdrive pacing or isoproterenol (for pause-dependent acquired TdP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02011","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Severe Cerebral Malaria","q":"A 28-year-old man from Odisha presents with high fever, coma (GCS 7), generalized seizures, dark cola-colored urine, blood glucose 42 mg/dL, and peripheral blood smear showing 14% Plasmodium falciparum ring-stage parasitemia. Beyond immediate IV 25% dextrose for hypoglycemia and airway protection, what is the definitive antimalarial treatment?","o":["Oral chloroquine phosphate loading dose via nasogastric tube","Intravenous artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, and 24 hours, then once daily until oral step-down ACT","High-dose IV dexamethasone plus routine osmotic mannitol","Intramuscular sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine monotherapy"],"a":1,"e":"Under WHO and India NVBDCP/NCVBDC guidelines, severe falciparum malaria (impaired consciousness, convulsions, hypoglycemia, hemoglobinuria, hyperparasitemia \u003e5-10%) is treated immediately with parenteral IV artesunate (2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, and 24 h, then q24h for \u003e=24 h until able to tolerate a full 3-day oral artemisinin-based combination therapy). Trials proved artesunate cuts mortality by ~35% over IV quinine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02012","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Acute Testicular Torsion","q":"A 15-year-old boy awakens with sudden severe right testicular pain and nausea 3 hours ago. Examination reveals a swollen, exquisitely tender, high-riding right testis lying horizontally, an absent right cremasteric reflex, and pain that worsens on scrotal elevation (negative Prehn sign). What is the most appropriate immediate management?","o":["Wait 24 hours to see if spontaneous detorsion occurs","Prescribe 10 days of oral ciprofloxacin and scrotal support for presumed epididymitis","Emergent surgical scrotal exploration, detorsion, and bilateral testicular orchidopexy without delaying for ultrasound if operating room is immediately available","Schedule an outpatient scrotal Doppler ultrasound for the next morning"],"a":2,"e":"Acute testicular torsion is a surgical emergency with a narrow 4-to-6-hour golden salvage window (\u003e90% viability \u003c6 hrs, dropping sharply after 12 hrs). High-riding transverse lie, absent cremasteric reflex, sudden onset, and negative Prehn sign confer high clinical probability (TWIST score), mandating immediate surgical exploration, detorsion, and bilateral orchidopexy (because the underlying bell-clapper deformity is bilateral) without waiting for imaging delays.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02013","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Placenta Previa Management","q":"A 29-year-old G2P1 woman at 33 weeks gestation presents with sudden-onset painless bright red vaginal bleeding (~300 mL). Her uterus is soft and non-tender, fetal heart rate is 142/min reassuring, and fetal head is high and unengaged. What examination MUST be strictly avoided on admission, and what initial imaging confirms the diagnosis?","o":["Perform immediate amniotomy with Kocher forceps","Perform an immediate forceful digital cervical dilation check in casualty; obtain pelvic X-ray","Insert a Cook cervical ripening balloon immediately","Strictly avoid digital per-vaginal (PV) examination; perform transabdominal/transperineal ultrasound first to rule out placenta previa"],"a":3,"e":"Painless, causeless, recurrent bright-red bleeding in the third trimester with a soft, non-tender uterus and malpresentation/unengaged presenting part is classic for placenta previa. Digital vaginal/cervical palpation can disrupt the low-lying placental cotyledons and unleash catastrophic maternal-fetal hemorrhage (\u0027never do a PV in APH until placenta previa is excluded by ultrasound\u0027).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02014","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Duct-Dependent Congenital Heart Disease","q":"A 6-day-old term neonate presents with sudden lethargy, poor feeding, tachypnea, gray mottled skin, severe metabolic acidosis (pH 7.08), hepatomegaly, and impalpable bilateral femoral pulses (brachial BP 82/50 mmHg vs calf BP unrecordable). Which life-saving medication must be started immediately while preparing cardiology transfer?","o":["Intravenous Prostaglandin E1 (alprostadil) continuous infusion to reopen and maintain ductus arteriosus patency (with apnea monitoring)","High-dose oral digoxin and propranolol alone","Intravenous indomethacin to close the ductus arteriosus rapidly","Immediate 100% FiO2 hyperoxia targeting PaO2 \u003e300 mmHg"],"a":0,"e":"Sudden cardiogenic shock at 5-14 days of life with absent femoral pulses, differential blood pressure, and severe lactic acidosis signals closure of the ductus arteriosus in a duct-dependent systemic obstructive lesion (critical coarctation of aorta, interrupted aortic arch, or hypoplastic left heart syndrome). Immediate IV PGE1 (0.05-0.1 mcg/kg/min) reopens the PDA to restore lower-body/systemic perfusion (monitoring closely for PGE1-induced apnea and hypotension).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02015","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Massive Transfusion Protocol (MTP)","q":"A trauma patient in hemorrhagic shock receives 8 units of packed RBCs rapidly over 45 minutes alongside crystalloids, and subsequently develops diffuse microvascular oozing, prolonged QT interval, and refractory hypotension despite adequate filling pressures. Which transfusion strategy and specific electrolyte replacement address these complications?","o":["Administer IV potassium chloride bolus and protamine sulfate","Activate balanced 1:1:1 ratio transfusion (PRBC:FFP:Platelets) plus early tranexamic acid, active warming, and IV calcium chloride/gluconate to reverse citrate-induced hypocalcemia","Avoid calcium completely because stored blood is hypercalcemic","Infuse 10 more liters of cold 0.9% normal saline before ordering any FFP or platelets"],"a":1,"e":"Rapid transfusion of PRBCs alone without plasma/platelets induces dilutional coagulopathy, while citrate anticoagulant in stored blood products chelates serum ionized calcium—causing acute hypocalcemia, impaired myocardial contractility, prolonged QTc, and worsened clotting. Balanced 1:1:1 damage-control resuscitation, active warming (avoiding the hypothermia-acidosis-coagulopathy lethal triad), and IV calcium replacement restore hemostasis and cardiac output.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02016","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH)","q":"A 3-week-old breech-delivered female newborn has a palpable \u0027clunk\u0027 when the flexed hip is gently abducted with anterior pressure over the greater trochanter (positive Ortolani maneuver) and asymmetric thigh creases. Which diagnostic imaging study and initial treatment are indicated at this age?","o":["AP pelvis plain radiograph only (as ultrasound cannot penetrate neonatal cartilage) and double diapering","Immediate open surgical pelvic osteotomy in the neonatal nursery","Dynamic hip ultrasonography (Graf method) and immediate Pavlik harness (maintaining hip flexion ~90-100 degrees and gentle abduction)","Wait until age 18 months to start walking before taking any X-ray or splinting"],"a":2,"e":"Because the femoral head epiphysis does not ossify until 4-6 months of age, plain radiographs are unreliable in neonates; dynamic hip ultrasound (Graf alpha angle \u003e=60 degrees normal) directly visualizes the cartilaginous femoral head and acetabular labrum. Early reducible neonatal DDH is treated non-operatively with a Pavlik harness (avoiding extreme \u003e60 deg abduction to prevent avascular necrosis of the femoral head and femoral nerve palsy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02017","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Infiltrative Cardiomyopathies","q":"A 74-year-old man with bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome and lumbar spinal stenosis presents with HFpEF (LVEF 54%). Echocardiography shows severe concentric LV thickening (17 mm septal wall) and \u0027apical sparing\u0027 longitudinal strain (\u0027cherry-on-top\u0027), YET 12-lead ECG paradoxically reveals LOW-VOLTAGE QRS complexes (\u0027voltage-mass mismatch\u0027). Serum/urine immunofixation and serum free light-chain kappa/lambda ratio are completely normal (excluding AL amyloidosis), while Technetium-99m pyrophosphate (Tc-99m PYP) bone scintigraphy demonstrates Grade 3 intense myocardial uptake. What is the diagnosis and disease-modifying therapy?","o":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; high-dose digoxin (strictly avoid digoxin in amyloidosis due to fibril toxicity)","Severe calcific aortic stenosis; emergency PBMV","Light-chain (AL) amyloidosis; immediate bortezomib-cyclophosphamide-dexamethasone without PYP","Transthyretin Cardiac Amyloidosis (ATTR-CM); Tafamidis (TTR tetramer stabiliser) plus genetic testing to differentiate wild-type from hereditary variant ATTR"],"a":3,"e":"HFpEF with thickened LV walls + low QRS voltage on ECG (\u0027voltage-mass mismatch\u0027) + apical-sparing strain + carpal tunnel/biceps tendon rupture/spinal stenosis strongly suggests Cardiac Amyloidosis. Once clonal plasma cell dyscrasia (AL amyloidosis) is excluded by normal serum free light chains and SPEP/UPEP with immunofixation, Grade 2-3 myocardial uptake on Tc-99m PYP/DPD scintigraphy non-invasively confirms ATTR Cardiac Amyloidosis without needing endomyocardial biopsy! Tafamidis reduces mortality and HF hospitalisations (and digoxin/CCBs are avoided due to heightened toxicity).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02018","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Pulmonary Hypertension","q":"A 51-year-old man remains severely dyspnoeic 6 months after an acute pulmonary embolism despite uninterrupted therapeutic rivaroxaban. Echocardiogram estimates PASP 68 mmHg, right-heart catheterisation shows mean PAP 42 mmHg, PAWP 10 mmHg, and PVR 6.5 Wood units (pre-capillary PH). Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) lung scan demonstrates multiple large wedge-shaped mismatched segmental perfusion defects, confirmed on pulmonary angiography as organised proximal thrombofibrotic webs. What is the potentially CURATIVE first-line treatment?","o":["Surgical Pulmonary Endarterectomy (PEA) at an expert centre (+ lifelong anticoagulation; Riociguat if inoperable or persistent post-PEA PH)","Stop anticoagulation and give IV alteplase thrombolysis at 6 months","Start sildenafil monotherapy alone without surgical evaluation","Immediate bilateral lung resection"],"a":0,"e":"WHO Group 4 Chronic Thromboembolic Pulmonary Hypertension (CTEPH) develops in ~2-4% of PE survivors when thrombi organise into fibrotic luminal obstructions. A V/Q scan is the screening test of choice (a normal V/Q scan virtually excludes CTEPH, outperforming CTPA for distal webs). Unlike Group 1 PAH, proximal CTEPH is uniquely potentially CURABLE by surgical Pulmonary Endarterectomy (PEA) alongside lifelong anticoagulation (with Riociguat [soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator] and balloon pulmonary angioplasty for inoperable/residual disease).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02019","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Anti-NMDA Receptor Encephalitis","q":"A 21-year-old previously healthy woman develops acute behavioural psychosis, memory loss, catatonia, seizures, rhythmic orofacial grimacing/dyskinesias (\u0027chewing movements\u0027), central hypoventilation, and marked autonomic instability over 2 weeks. Brain MRI is unremarkable, CSF shows mild lymphocytic pleocytosis with oligoclonal bands, and CSF IgG antibodies against the GluN1 subunit of the NMDA receptor are positive. Which pelvic neoplasm must be actively searched for (and removed promptly if found) alongside first-line immunotherapy (IV steroids + IVIG/PLEX)?","o":["Uterine fibroid alone","Mature or immature Ovarian Teratoma (pelvic ultrasound/MRI)","Choriocarcinoma of the cervix","Follicular thyroid adenoma"],"a":1,"e":"Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis classically presents in young women/children with subacute psychiatric changes, movement disorders (orofacial dyskinesias/dystonia), seizures, autonomic storms, and hypoventilation. Roughly 40-50% of adult female patients harbour an Ovarian Teratoma expressing ectopic neural NMDA receptors. Prompt tumour resection PLUS first-line immunotherapy (high-dose IV methylprednisolone + IVIG or plasmapheresis, escalating early to rituximab/cyclophosphamide if needed) yields dramatic recovery in \u003e75-80%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02020","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Retroperitoneal Sarcoma","q":"A 54-year-old man presents with progressive abdominal fullness. Contrast CT abdomen/pelvis shows a 19 cm well-circumscribed retroperitoneal mass containing fat and enhancing soft-tissue septa displacing the left kidney anteriorly and abutting the descending colon, with no liver or lung metastases (suspected retroperitoneal liposarcoma). Which biopsy approach and definitive surgical principle are oncologically sound?","o":["Immediate preoperative external-beam radiotherapy without tissue diagnosis","Transperitoneal laparoscopic incisional wedge biopsy followed by simple piecemeal enucleation","Image-guided retroperitoneal (posterior/flank) core needle biopsy (avoiding transperitoneal open biopsy) followed by planned En-Bloc R0 resection (including adherent kidney/colon if required to achieve clear margins)","Observation until bowel obstruction occurs"],"a":2,"e":"Retroperitoneal sarcomas (most commonly liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma) require coaxial CT/USG-guided core needle biopsy via a RETROPERITONEAL flank route (confirming sarcoma subtype/grade and excluding lymphoma/germ cell tumour, while avoiding transperitoneal needle/open biopsy that seeds the peritoneal cavity). Complete En-Bloc surgical resection with negative microscopic margins (R0)—often sacrificing contiguous involved/enveloped organs (nephrectomy, colectomy)—is the sole curative modality; piecemeal debulking guarantees local recurrence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02021","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Short Bowel Syndrome (SBS)","q":"A 42-year-old man undergoes extensive small-bowel resection for midgut ischaemia leaving 110 cm of jejunum anastomosed to the ascending colon (terminal ileum and ileocaecal valve resected). Beyond needing parenteral Vitamin B12 supplementation, why is he at high risk of calcium oxalate nephrolithiasis and cholesterol gallstones?","o":["Preserving the colon prevents any oxalate absorption","Terminal ileum is the sole site of intrinsic factor secretion","Jejunal hyperabsorption of bilirubin precipitates stones directly in the renal pelvis","Bile salt malabsorption (due to terminal ileum loss) leaves unabsorbed fatty acids in the colon that bind luminal Ca2+, freeing dietary oxalate for hyperabsorption (enteric hyperoxaluria) and depleting the bile salt pool causing lithogenic bile"],"a":3,"e":"The terminal ileum is uniquely responsible for active reabsorption of Vitamin B12-intrinsic factor complex AND conjugated bile salts (\u003e95% enterohepatic circulation). Loss of \u003e100 cm of ileum depletes the bile acid pool, causing cholesterol-supersaturated gallstones and fat malabsorption; unabsorbed luminal fatty acids saponify dietary calcium in the colon, leaving free oxalate to be hyperabsorbed across colonic mucosa—producing enteric hyperoxaluria and calcium oxalate kidney stones (when colon is in continuity!).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02022","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Fertility Preservation in Breast Cancer","q":"A 28-year-old nulliparous woman newly diagnosed with Stage IIA, ER-positive/PR-positive invasive breast carcinoma is scheduled to start gonadotoxic cyclophosphamide-based adjuvant chemotherapy in 3 weeks and wishes to preserve fertility. Which rapid ovarian stimulation protocol maximises oocyte yield without delaying chemotherapy or exposing the ER+ tumour to supraphysiological oestradiol surges?","o":["Random-start controlled ovarian stimulation with gonadotropins PLUS concurrent Letrozole (aromatase inhibitor) for oocyte or embryo cryopreservation","High-dose clomiphene citrate alone for 6 months","Standard follicular-phase-only stimulation delaying chemotherapy by 2 months with high-dose exogenous oestradiol","Routine bilateral oophorectomy prior to chemotherapy"],"a":0,"e":"Embryo or mature oocyte cryopreservation is the gold-standard fertility preservation method before alkylating (cyclophosphamide) chemotherapy. Random-start protocols initiate gonadotropins at any menstrual cycle day, completing oocyte retrieval within ~2 weeks without delaying oncology care. Co-administering Letrozole (aromatase inhibitor) keeps serum oestradiol near physiological levels throughout stimulation, protecting ER-positive breast tumours without impairing mature oocyte yield.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02023","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (CAIS)","q":"A 17-year-old phenotypic female presents with primary amenorrhoea, well-developed breasts (Tanner Stage V), absent axillary and pubic hair, a short blind-ending vaginal pouch, absent uterus/cervix, bilateral inguinal/labial testes, serum testosterone in the normal male range, and a 46,XY karyotype (Complete Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome — X-linked androgen receptor mutation). Unlike 46,XY Swyer syndrome (where streak gonads are removed immediately), when is prophylactic bilateral gonadectomy performed in CAIS?","o":["Remove testes immediately at birth in infancy","Wait until AFTER pubertal breast/bone maturation is complete (late adolescence/early adulthood, ~18-20 years), then perform bilateral gonadectomy followed by oestrogen-only HRT","Never remove the testes at any age and give progestin-only pills","Perform total hysterectomy and vaginectomy"],"a":1,"e":"In CAIS (AR mutation), fetal testes secrete normal AMH (regressing uterus/upper vagina) and normal male testosterone (which cannot act on androgen receptors, leaving absent sexual hair and female external phenotype), while peripheral aromatisation of testosterone to oestradiol induces natural female breast development and peak bone mass! Because germ cell malignancy risk in CAIS testes is negligible before puberty (\u003c1-2% prior to age 20, unlike Swyer streak gonads), testes are retained until pubertal growth/feminisation completes, then excised followed by oestrogen-only HRT (no progestin needed since uterus is absent).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02024","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Histiocytic Disorders","q":"A 2-year-old boy presents with persistent refractory seborrhoea-like scaly rash on the scalp and groin, chronic purulent bilateral ear discharge unresponsive to antibiotics, polyuria/polydipsia (central diabetes insipidus), and well-demarcated \u0027punched-out\u0027 lytic skull lesions without reactive sclerosis on radiograph. Biopsy of the skin/bone lesion demonstrates histiocytes positive for CD1a and Langerin (CD207) with tennis-racket-shaped Birbeck granules on electron microscopy. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia","Multiple myeloma","Langerhans Cell Histiocytosis (LCH)","Congenital syphilis"],"a":2,"e":"Langerhans Cell Histiocytosis (clonal proliferation driven commonly by BRAF V600E mutations) classically affects young children with refractory \u0027seborrhoeic\u0027 dermatitis, chronic draining otitis media/mastoiditis, punched-out osteolytic skull/bone lesions (\u0027floating teeth\u0027), hepatosplenomegaly, and central diabetes insipidus (posterior pituitary infiltration). Diagnosis requires CD1a + CD207 (Langerin) positivity (and Birbeck granules); multisystem disease is treated with vinblastine + prednisolone (+ BRAF inhibitor if refractory).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02025","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Inherited Glomerular Basement Membrane Disorders","q":"A 14-year-old boy with persistent microscopic haematuria and episodic gross haematuria since childhood develops progressive bilateral high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss, worsening proteinuria, and conical protrusion of the anterior lens surface (\u0027anterior lenticonus\u0027). Electron microscopy of his renal biopsy demonstrates alternating thinning and thickening with lamellation and \u0027basket-weave\u0027 splitting of the glomerular basement membrane (GBM). Which gene/protein is mutated in the commonest X-linked form, and what therapy slows progression to ESRD?","o":["PKD1; tolvaptan","NPHS1 (nephrin); cyclophosphamide","COL4A1; high-dose oral prednisolone for 12 months","COL4A5 (alpha-5 chain of Type IV collagen); early ACE inhibitor / ARB nephroprotection (steroids are ineffective)"],"a":3,"e":"Alport syndrome results from mutations in Type IV collagen (80-85% X-linked COL4A5 affecting males severely; remainder autosomal COL4A3/COL4A4), disrupting basement membranes of glomeruli, cochlea, and lens capsule—producing haematuria progressing to CKD/ESRD, high-frequency SNHL, and pathognomonic anterior lenticonus (plus dot-and-fleck retinopathy), with GBM \u0027basket-weave\u0027 lamellation on EM. Early RAAS blockade (ACEi/ARB) significantly delays kidney failure, whereas immunosuppression/steroids are ineffective.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02026","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Postoperative Visual Loss (POVL)","q":"After a 7-hour prone lumbar instrumented spine fusion complicated by 2.4 litres of blood loss, prolonged hypotension, and 6 litres of crystalloid infusion, a 58-year-old obese man awakens in recovery with painless bilateral complete visual loss, sluggish pupils, and initially normal-appearing optic discs (Posterior Ischaemic Optic Neuropathy — PION). Which intraoperative preventive measures reduce the risk of POVL from ischaemic optic neuropathy versus central retinal artery occlusion?","o":["Position head in neutral/slightly elevated level above heart on Mayfield pins/foam WITHOUT external eyeball pressure, avoid prolonged Trendelenburg tilt, maintain normotension/haemoglobin, and use colloids/blood to limit facial/orbital venous congestion","Pack the eyeballs tightly down into a narrow horseshoe headrest in steep Trendelenburg position","Induce deliberate severe hypotension (MAP 45 mmHg) for 7 hours with 10 litres of crystalloid","Administer high-dose IV furosemide at closure to restore optic nerve circulation"],"a":0,"e":"Postoperative Visual Loss (POVL) following prolonged (\u003e6 h) prone spine surgery is most often caused by Posterior Ischaemic Optic Neuropathy (PION: bilateral, painless, normal initial disc, associated with obesity, male sex, Wilson frame/head-down position, massive blood loss \u003e1-2 L, and excessive crystalloid venous congestion) rather than unilateral Central Retinal Artery Occlusion (from direct orbital compression on horseshoe headrests). Prevention aligns head neutrally at/above heart level without globe pressure, stages excessively long procedures, limits crystalloid oedema, and maintains MAP/perfusion pressure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02027","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Carotid Body Tumour (Paraganglioma)","q":"A 45-year-old man living at high altitude presents with a slow-growing, painless, pulsatile swelling at the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid at the level of the hyoid bone that moves freely side-to-side horizontally but CANNOT be moved vertically (Fontaine\u0027s sign). MRI shows a hypervascular mass with \u0027salt-and-pepper\u0027 flow voids, and CTA shows splaying of the internal and external carotid arteries at the carotid bifurcation (\u0027lyre sign\u0027). What is the diagnosis and critical preoperative warning?","o":["Carotid body tumour (chemodectoma / paraganglioma); STRICTLY AVOID incisional/FNAC biopsy (risks catastrophic haemorrhage/carotid injury) and screen plasma/urine metanephrines before surgical excision","Branchial cleft cyst; immediate outpatient incisional biopsy","Schwannoma of the vagus nerve (which displaces both carotid vessels together rather than splaying the bifurcation); simple aspiration","Tuberculous cervical lymphadenitis; empirical HRZE without imaging"],"a":0,"e":"Carotid body paragangliomas arise from neural crest chemoreceptor cells at the carotid bifurcation (more common in chronic hypoxia/high altitude and SDHx mutations), demonstrating horizontal-only mobility (Fontaine\u0027s sign), \u0027salt-and-pepper\u0027 MRI appearance (high-flow serpiginous signal voids + hyperintense slow flow/haemorrhage), and classic splaying of the ICA and ECA (\u0027lyre sign\u0027). Diagnostic biopsy is contraindicated due to extreme vascularity; preoperative catecholamine screening (+ selective preoperative embolisation for large Shamblin II/III tumours) precedes subadventitial excision.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02028","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"HIV Intracranial Mass Lesions","q":"A 36-year-old man with advanced HIV (CD4 count 25 cells/mm3) presents with subacute confusion, right hemiparesis, and headaches. Contrast MRI brain reveals a SINGLE periventricular, avidly enhancing mass infiltrating the corpus callosum (\u0027butterfly\u0027 pattern) with marked restricted diffusion and high perfusion; Thallium-201 SPECT / FDG-PET is intensely hypermetabolic, and CSF EBV PCR is positive (serum Toxoplasma IgG negative). What is the diagnosis, and how does it contrast with Cerebral Toxoplasmosis?","o":["Primary CNS Lymphoma (EBV-driven); whereas Cerebral Toxoplasmosis typically causes multiple ring-enhancing lesions in the basal ganglia/corticomedullary junction (eccentric target sign) with positive Toxoplasma IgG and hypometabolic SPECT/PET","Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (non-enhancing JC virus white matter lesion); no biopsy needed","Cryptococcoma; single-dose fluconazole","Cerebral Toxoplasmosis; start empirical pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine for 6 weeks before considering lymphoma"],"a":0,"e":"In advanced HIV (CD4 \u003c 100), the two major enhancing mass lesions are Cerebral Toxoplasmosis and EBV-associated Primary CNS Lymphoma (PCNSL). Multiple ring-enhancing basal ganglia/grey-white junction lesions with \u0027eccentric target sign\u0027 and positive Toxoplasma IgG receive an empirical 14-day pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine trial; conversely, a solitary/periventricular corpus-callosum-crossing hypermetabolic lesion with positive CSF EBV PCR (especially if Toxoplasma IgG-negative or failing a 2-week toxo trial) indicates PCNSL requiring stereotactic biopsy (avoiding steroids prior to biopsy!) and high-dose methotrexate-based therapy + ART.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02029","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Scaphoid Fracture","q":"A 23-year-old motorcyclist falls onto an outstretched dorsiflexed hand and presents with focal tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox, pain over the scaphoid tubercle, and pain on axial loading of the thumb. Initial dedicated scaphoid-series radiographs on Day 1 appear completely normal. Why MUST you NOT discharge him with a simple diagnosis of \u0027wrist sprain\u0027, and what vascular anatomy puts proximal-pole scaphoid fractures at high risk of avascular necrosis?","o":["Apply a volar plaster slab leaving the thumb free for 3 days only","Immobilise in a thumb spica splint/cast and obtain early MRI (or repeat X-rays at 10-14 days) because non-displaced scaphoid fractures are often radiographically occult initially; the proximal pole relies on retrograde intraosseous blood supply entering distally from the dorsal carpal branch of the radial artery","Perform immediate excision of the entire scaphoid bone on Day 1","Reassure and remove all support because normal Day-1 X-rays exclude scaphoid fracture completely; blood enters exclusively at the proximal pole"],"a":1,"e":"The scaphoid\u0027s blood supply enters predominantly distally/dorsally (dorsal carpal branch of radial artery at the waist/tubercle) and flows RETROGRADELY to the proximal pole. Consequently, scaphoid waist/proximal fractures sever blood flow to the proximal fragment, risking non-union and avascular necrosis (~30% in waist, up to 100% in proximal pole). Because up to 15-20% of acute scaphoid fractures are invisible on Day-1 X-rays, clinical snuffbox tenderness mandates thumb-spica immobilisation and early MRI (or 10-14 day repeat radiographs).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02030","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Post-Thyroidectomy Nerve Injury","q":"Immediately upon extubation after a difficult total thyroidectomy for locally invasive goitre, a 46-year-old woman develops loud inspiratory stridor, severe suprasternal retractions, and falling SpO2 (78%) with a near-normal phonation whisper. Fibreoptic laryngoscopy shows both vocal cords immobile in the median/paramedian position with a chink \u003c 2 mm (bilateral abductor vocal fold paralysis). Contrast this with unilateral RLN palsy and state the immediate emergency management.","o":["Bilateral RLN palsy leaves both cords adducted in the paramedian position causing acute life-threatening airway obstruction; immediately secure airway (reintubation/emergency tracheostomy) rather than giving racemic epinephrine alone (later laser posterior cordotomy/arytenoidectomy if permanent)","Unilateral RLN palsy causes complete aphonia and fatal stridor, whereas bilateral RLN palsy causes painless hoarseness needing speech therapy only","Administer IV calcium gluconate alone assuming hypocalcaemia without securing the airway","Wait 6 hours in recovery for spontaneous recovery while SpO2 is 78%"],"a":0,"e":"All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid (supplied by external branch of SLN) are innervated by the Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (including the sole abductor, posterior cricoarytenoid). Per Semon\u0027s law, abductor fibres are lost earlier/more severely, leaving bilateral paralysed vocal folds in the paramedian position—preserving some voice while critically narrowing the glottic airway and causing immediate life-threatening inspiratory stridor upon extubation (whereas unilateral RLN palsy causes hoarseness/breathy voice with adequate airway). Immediate reintubation or tracheostomy saves life.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02031","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Psychiatry","topic":"Serotonin Syndrome (Hunter Criteria)","q":"A 51-year-old man taking sertraline 150 mg/day for depression is started on IV Linezolid for hospital-acquired MRSA pneumonia. Within 24 hours he develops agitation, diaphoresis, temperature 39.4 degrees C, HR 132/min, hyperactive bowel sounds, generalised hyperreflexia, spontaneous ocular clonus, and sustained bilateral ankle clonus (\u003e5 beats; Hunter Serotonin Toxicity Criteria met). Beyond immediately stopping linezolid and sertraline, cooling, and IV benzodiazepines, what is the specific serotonin receptor antagonist?","o":["IV Dantrolene + Bromocriptine (specific for Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome)","IV Haloperidol 10 mg bolus","Oral/NG Cyproheptadine (5-HT2A antagonist: 12 mg loading dose, then 2 mg q2h until response)","Oral Propranolol + Sumatriptan"],"a":2,"e":"Linezolid is an oxazolidinone antibiotic with potent reversible monoamine oxidase inhibition (MAOI) activity; co-administering it with SSRIs/SNRIs/tramadol precipitates acute Serotonin Syndrome within \u003c24 hours. Diagnosis via Hunter Serotonin Toxicity Criteria centres on neuromuscular excitation—clonus (spontaneous, inducible, or ocular), hyperreflexia, agitation, diaphoresis, hyperthermia, and diarrhoea (unlike NMS, which shows lead-pipe rigidity and bradyreflexia). Management stops serotonergics immediately, provides cooling/hydration + benzodiazepines (diazepam/lorazepam), and adds Cyproheptadine (5-HT2A/5-HT1A antagonist) in moderate-to-severe cases.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02032","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Acute Ischemic Stroke Thrombolysis","q":"A 64-year-old man presents 90 minutes after sudden onset of left hemiparesis and dysarthria (NIHSS 14). Non-contrast head CT excludes intracranial hemorrhage, and he has no bleeding contraindications to IV alteplase, but his BP is 202/114 mmHg. What is the mandatory blood pressure management before and after initiating IV alteplase?","o":["Withhold alteplase permanently and give IV mannitol 20%","Give sublingual nifedipine 10 mg to drop BP abruptly to 110/70 mmHg","Administer IV alteplase immediately at 202/114 mmHg without lowering BP","Lower BP gently using titratable IV labetalol or nicardipine to strictly \u003c 185/110 mmHg BEFORE alteplase bolus, and maintain \u003c 180/105 mmHg for at least 24 hours post-infusion"],"a":3,"e":"To minimize symptomatic intracerebral hemorrhage after IV fibrinolysis (alteplase or tenecteplase within 4.5 hrs), AHA/ASA guidelines mandate BP \u003c185/110 mmHg prior to thrombolysis using smooth titratable parenteral agents (IV labetalol boluses or nicardipine/clevidipine infusion), followed by strict maintenance \u003c180/105 mmHg for 24 hours. Conversely, in non-thrombolyzed ischemic stroke, permissive hypertension up to 220/120 mmHg preserves penumbral perfusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02033","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Massive Hemothorax","q":"A 27-year-old man with a left stab wound to the 6th intercostal space presents in shock (BP 80/50 mmHg) with dullness and absent breath sounds over the left chest. After large-bore IV crystalloid/blood resuscitation and insertion of a 32F left tube thoracostomy, 1,650 mL of frank dark blood drains immediately, followed by 300 mL over the next hour. What is the definitive next step?","o":["Immediate surgical exploration via emergent left anterolateral thoracotomy (with autotransfusion if available) without clamping the chest tube","Clamp the chest tube immediately to tamponade intrathoracic bleeding","Observe conservatively in the surgical ward for 24 hours","Remove the chest tube and apply a pressure bandage"],"a":0,"e":"Massive hemothorax is defined under ATLS by immediate evacuation of \u003e=1,500 mL of blood upon chest tube insertion (or \u003e1/3 blood volume) OR ongoing hemorrhage \u003e200 mL/hr for 2-4 hours (or persistent blood transfusion requirement to maintain perfusion). This indicates major pulmonary hilar, intercostal, or internal mammary vessel injury requiring emergent thoracotomy alongside balanced resuscitation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02034","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Acute Puerperal Uterine Inversion","q":"Immediately following vigorous fundal pressure and premature umbilical cord traction during the third stage of labor, a 24-year-old woman develops sudden profound hypotension (BP 68/40 mmHg) disproportionate to modest bleeding, absent uterine fundus on abdominal palpation, and a smooth firm reddish-purple mass protruding through the introitus with the placenta still attached. What is the correct immediate sequence?","o":["Pull off the attached placenta forcefully first, then give high-dose IV oxytocin while leaving the uterus inverted","Call for help/anesthesia, start rapid IV resuscitation, STOP uterotonics temporarily, perform immediate Johnson manual replacement (pushing the inverted fundus upward along the pelvic axis BEFORE removing the attached placenta), then remove placenta and give uterotonics","Apply strong downward traction on the protruding mass","Administer IV methylergometrine immediately to constrict the cervical ring around the inverted fundus"],"a":1,"e":"Acute uterine inversion triggers severe neurogenic (vagal) and hemorrhagic shock. Removing an adherent placenta BEFORE repositioning provokes torrential bleeding from open sinuses, while giving oxytocin/ergots tightens the cervical constriction ring and traps the inverted fundus. Immediate Johnson maneuver (cupping the fundus upward toward the umbilicus—with tocolytics like terbutaline/nitroglycerin/halothane if the cervical ring is tight) replaces the uterus first, followed by manual placental separation and uterotonics to hold tone.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02035","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Acute Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (APSGN)","q":"An 8-year-old boy presents with puffiness around the eyes, tea-colored (grossly hematuric) urine, oliguria, and BP 138/92 mmHg two weeks after recovering from impetigo skin lesions. Urinalysis shows 3+ blood, dysmorphic RBCs, RBC casts, and 1+ protein; ASO/anti-DNase B titers are elevated and serum C3 complement is markedly depressed (C4 normal). What is the diagnosis and expected prognosis?","o":["Minimal change nephrotic syndrome; treat immediately with 12 weeks of oral prednisolone","IgA nephropathy characterized by persistently elevated C3","Acute Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (APSGN); self-limiting in \u003e95% of children with supportive sodium/fluid restriction, loop diuretics/antihypertensives (C3 normalizes within 6-8 weeks)","Henoch-Schonlein purpura nephritis requiring urgent cyclophosphamide"],"a":2,"e":"Classic nephritic syndrome (cola-colored urine, RBC casts, edema, hypertension, oliguria) occurring 1-3 weeks after Group A beta-hemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis or pyoderma (impetigo, where anti-DNase B is especially sensitive) with transient alternative-pathway hypocomplementemia (low C3, normal C4) confirms APSGN. Management is supportive (fluid/salt restriction, furosemide, CCBs for hypertension); corticosteroids are NOT indicated, and \u003e95% recover completely with C3 normalizing by 6-8 weeks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02036","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Thyroid Storm (Thyrotoxic Crisis)","q":"A 35-year-old woman with Graves disease develops acute thyroid storm (temperature 40.1 degrees C, HR 158/min with AFib, delirium, vomiting) precipitated by lobar pneumonia. Why MUST propylthiouracil (PTU) be administered at least 1 hour BEFORE giving Lugol\u0027s iodine or saturated solution of potassium iodide (SSKI)?","o":["Iodine is administered first to boost peripheral conversion of T4 to T3","Because PTU instantly reverses atrial fibrillation within 5 seconds","Because iodine chemically destroys PTU tablets in the stomach","To block thyroid peroxidase-mediated organification of newly administered iodine first—giving iodine before thionamide blockade provides substrate for massive new T4/T3 synthesis (Jod-Basedow worsening)"],"a":3,"e":"Thyroid storm therapy follows a strict sequence: (1) Beta-blocker (propranolol) to control adrenergic surge and peripheral T4-\u003eT3 conversion; (2) Thionamide (PTU preferred initially over methimazole because PTU also inhibits peripheral 5\u0027-deiodinase T4-\u003eT3 conversion); (3) Wait \u003e=1 hour before (4) Iodine (Lugol\u0027s/SSKI) to inhibit hormone release via Wolff-Chaikoff effect without feeding new hormone synthesis; plus (5) Hydrocortisone (blocks T4-\u003eT3 conversion and treats relative adrenal insufficiency), cooling (paracetamol—NEVER aspirin, which displaces T4 from TBG!), and treating the trigger.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02037","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Sigmoid Volvulus Management","q":"A 68-year-old bedridden man presents with massive abdominal distension, obstipation, and a giant inverted U-shaped air-filled colonic loop rising from the pelvis toward the right upper quadrant (\u0027coffee-bean\u0027 sign) on abdominal X-ray. He is afebrile, hemodynamically stable, and has NO peritoneal rebound tenderness or leukocytosis. What is the initial treatment of choice?","o":["Flexible sigmoidoscopic decompression and placement of a soft rectal decompression tube (followed by elective sigmoid resection during the same admission to prevent recurrence)","Immediate emergency total abdominal colectomy with permanent ileostomy","High-volume warm soapsuds enema with blind rigid proctoscope forcing","Discharge on oral senna laxatives"],"a":0,"e":"In uncomplicated, viable sigmoid volvulus (absence of fever, peritonitis, leukocytosis, acidosis, or free air), urgent flexible sigmoidoscopy allows direct visualization of mucosal viability and gentle detorsion/decompression (\u003e70-80% success), leaving a soft flatus tube temporarily. Because recurrence exceeds 50-60%, semi-elective sigmoid resection with primary anastomosis is advised during the index hospitalization; if mucosal ischemia/peritonitis is present, proceed straight to emergency laparotomy (Hartmann procedure).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02038","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Valvular Emergencies","q":"A 39-year-old man with Streptococcus viridans native aortic valve endocarditis abruptly develops fulminant pulmonary oedema, cardiogenic shock (BP 78/42 mmHg, narrow pulse pressure 36 mmHg), HR 128/min, a soft short early diastolic decrescendo murmur, soft S1, and premature mitral valve closure on echocardiography with normal LV end-diastolic diameter (Acute Severe Aortic Regurgitation). Why is an Intra-Aortic Balloon Pump (IABP) STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED while preparing for emergency valve replacement, and why should beta-blockers be avoided?","o":["Acute AR always produces a wide pulse pressure \u003e 100 mmHg like chronic AR","Diastolic inflation of the IABP directly forces blood backward through the incompetent aortic valve worsening LV overload/shock, and beta-blockers block compensatory tachycardia needed to limit diastolic regurgitation time; bridge with IV vasodilator/inotropes to EMERGENCY aortic valve surgery","Beta-blockers at high doses improve cardiac output in acute AR by lengthening diastole","IABP cures acute AR permanently; wait 6 weeks of antibiotics before surgery"],"a":1,"e":"Unlike chronic compensated AR (dilated compliant LV, wide pulse pressure, Austin Flint murmur), Acute Severe AR floods an unprepared non-compliant LV, spiking LVEDP abruptly, closing the mitral valve prematurely (soft S1), shortening the diastolic murmur, and narrowing pulse pressure. Emergency aortic valve replacement is mandatory: IABP inflates during diastole and catastrophically worsens regurgitant volume into the LV (absolute contraindication!), while beta-blockers prolong diastole and abolish compensatory tachycardia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02039","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Mixed Cryoglobulinaemic Vasculitis","q":"A 52-year-old man with untreated chronic Hepatitis C (HCV RNA positive) presents with palpable purpura on the legs, symmetric arthralgias, mononeuritis multiplex, nephritic syndrome (creatinine 2.4 mg/dL, RBC casts, 3.2 g/day proteinuria), strongly positive Rheumatoid Factor, and profoundly depressed serum C4 (\u003c 2 mg/dL) with mildly reduced C3. Renal biopsy shows Membranoproliferative Glomerulonephritis (MPGN) with PAS-positive intraluminal \u0027hyaline thrombi\u0027 and subendothelial IgM/IgG deposits. What is the diagnosis and dual-pronged treatment?","o":["Minimal change disease; low-dose aspirin alone","Polyarteritis nodosa (classic HBV-associated); lifelong IV vancomycin","HCV-associated Type II Mixed Cryoglobulinaemic Vasculitis; Direct-Acting Antivirals (e.g., sofosbuvir/velpatasvir) to eradicate HCV PLUS Rituximab/corticosteroids (+/- PLEX) for severe glomerulonephritis/mononeuritis","Henoch-Schonlein purpura; oral penicillin"],"a":2,"e":"Chronic Hepatitis C stimulates B-cell clonal monoclonal IgM kappa rheumatoid factor directed against polyclonal IgG (Type II Mixed Cryoglobulinaemia), causing Meltzer\u0027s triad (palpable purpura, arthralgia, weakness), mononeuritis multiplex, markedly low C4, and MPGN with PAS+ cryoglobulin thrombi. Curative therapy targets the antigenic trigger with pan-genotypic Direct-Acting Antivirals (DAAs), combined with Rituximab + corticosteroids (and plasmapheresis if rapidly progressive/life-threatening) to halt active immune-complex vasculitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02040","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Autoimmune Hepatitis (AIH)","q":"A 29-year-old woman (viral hepatitis A/B/C/E, Wilson, and drug hepatotoxicity excluded) presents with fatigue, jaundice, AST 640 U/L, ALT 780 U/L, selectively elevated serum IgG (2,900 mg/dL), positive ANA (1:320) and Anti-Smooth Muscle Antibody (ASMA, anti-F-actin). Liver biopsy demonstrates dense lymphoplasmacytic portal/periportal infiltrate with \u0027interface hepatitis\u0027 (piecemeal necrosis) and hepatocyte rosettes. What is the diagnosis and standard immunosuppressive regimen?","o":["Acute hepatitis E; lifelong entecavir","Type 2 Autoimmune Hepatitis (anti-LKM1/anti-LC1 positive); oral ursodeoxycholic acid monotherapy","Primary biliary cholangitis; oral obeticholic acid alone","Type 1 Autoimmune Hepatitis; systemic Glucocorticoids (prednisolone) combined with Azathioprine (added after checking TPMT/NUDT15 and bilirubin improvement) as steroid-sparing maintenance"],"a":3,"e":"Young female with marked transaminitis, selectively elevated serum IgG, positive ANA/ASMA (Type 1 AIH; whereas Type 2 affects children with anti-LKM1), and biopsy interface hepatitis rich in plasma cells confirms Autoimmune Hepatitis. Standard induction/maintenance uses Prednisolone (or budesonide in non-cirrhotics) + Azathioprine (introduced ~2 weeks later once cholestasis improves and TPMT/NUDT15 checked), achieving remission in \u003e80% and preventing cirrhosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02041","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Pancreatic Pseudocyst","q":"Eight weeks after an episode of acute gallstone pancreatitis, a 45-year-old man presents with early satiety, postprandial fullness, and vomiting. CECT abdomen reveals a homogeneous, well-encapsulated 9 cm fluid collection with a thick mature fibrous wall (no solid necrotic debris or gas) in the lesser sac compressing the posterior gastric wall. Which intervention is the preferred first-line drainage modality?","o":["Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS)-guided transgastric Cystogastrostomy (stenting into stomach)","Wait 5 more years despite gastric outlet compression","Immediate distal pancreatectomy with splenectomy","Emergency percutaneous catheter drainage on Day 3 of acute pancreatitis before wall maturation"],"a":0,"e":"Symptomatic, enlarging, or obstructing pancreatic pseudocysts (\u003e= 4-6 weeks old with a mature \u003e3-5 mm fibrous wall and absent solid necrosis) are drained internally into the GI tract. EUS-guided endoscopic cystogastrostomy/cystoduodenostomy is preferred first-line over surgical cystogastrostomy and external percutaneous drainage (which carries higher pancreaticocutaneous fistula rates), achieving \u003e90% resolution with minimal morbidity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02042","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Rapidly Enlarging Thyroid Mass","q":"A 64-year-old woman with a 10-year history of Hashimoto\u0027s thyroiditis presents with a painless, rapidly enlarging, stony-hard thyroid mass causing tracheal compression over 5 weeks. Instead of proceeding straight to radical thyroidectomy, core needle biopsy is performed and reveals CD20-positive Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (Primary Thyroid Lymphoma), NOT anaplastic carcinoma. Why was core biopsy critical, and what is the primary curative treatment?","o":["Give high-dose oral levothyroxine alone to shrink the lymphoma","Primary Thyroid Lymphoma is exquisitely chemosensitive and radiosensitive—cured with R-CHOP immunochemotherapy + involved-site radiotherapy WITHOUT extensive surgical resection","Treat immediately with radioactive iodine (I-131)","Radical total thyroidectomy with laryngectomy is mandatory for thyroid lymphoma"],"a":1,"e":"Both Anaplastic Thyroid Carcinoma and Primary Thyroid Lymphoma (strongly associated with chronic Hashimoto\u0027s autoimmune thyroiditis) present in older adults as rapidly growing compressing neck masses. Because FNAC often fails to distinguish small-cell/lymphoma subtypes from anaplastic carcinoma, core needle biopsy with flow cytometry/IHC is mandatory: DLBCL of the thyroid is treated with systemic Rituximab-CHOP chemotherapy + radiotherapy (surgery plays NO therapeutic role beyond biopsy/airway relief if critical).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02043","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia Variants","q":"A 33-year-old woman presents 2 years after a normal term vaginal delivery with irregular vaginal bleeding and an enlarged vascular uterine mass. Paradoxically, serum beta-hCG is only mildly elevated (180 IU/L), while immunohistochemistry of endometrial curettings demonstrates intermediate extravillous trophoblast cells strongly positive for Human Placental Lactogen (hPL) and Mel-CAM, with very low hCG staining (Placental Site Trophoblastic Tumour — PSTT). Why is Hysterectomy primary treatment rather than single-agent methotrexate?","o":["PSTT responds 100% to single-dose methotrexate alone without surgery","PSTT is cured by suction and evacuation alone","PSTT is relatively chemoresistant to standard methotrexate/EMA-CO compared with choriocarcinoma; Total Hysterectomy (+ lymph node sampling, preserving ovaries in young Stage I patients) is primary curative treatment","Oral combined oral contraceptives resolve PSTT"],"a":2,"e":"Placental Site Trophoblastic Tumour (arising from intermediate trophoblasts, secreting high hPL with disproportionately low/modest serum beta-hCG, often years after a term non-molar birth) is biologically distinct from choriocarcinoma: it grows slowly, metastasises to lymphatics later, and is relatively chemoresistant. Stage I disease confined to the uterus requires primary Total Hysterectomy (retaining ovaries unless metastatic/postmenopausal), reserving multi-agent EMA-EP chemotherapy for metastatic or \u003e48-month-interval disease.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02044","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (POI)","q":"A 29-year-old woman (normal puberty, prior regular cycles) presents with 7 months of secondary amenorrhoea, severe hot flushes, and vaginal dryness (pregnancy ruled out; prolactin and TSH normal). Serum FSH measured twice 5 weeks apart is 52 IU/L and 58 IU/L (\u003e25-40 IU/L) with low serum oestradiol (\u003c 20 pg/mL). Beyond checking Karyotype, FMR1 premutation (Fragile X), and adrenal/thyroid autoantibodies, what is the essential long-term endocrine management?","o":["Withhold all oestrogen until age 55","Give lifelong clomiphene citrate 150 mg daily","Perform immediate bilateral oophorectomy","Physiological Oestrogen-Progestogen Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT) continued until the average age of natural menopause (~50-51 years) to prevent osteoporosis, cardiovascular disease, and cognitive decline"],"a":3,"e":"Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (cessation of ovarian function before age 40: \u003e= 4 months oligo/amenorrhoea + elevated FSH \u003e 25 IU/L on two occasions \u003e= 4 weeks apart) requires evaluation for chromosomal mosaicism (karyotype), FMR1 premutation (~2-5% of sporadic POI), and autoimmune polyglandular thyroid/21-hydroxylase antibodies. Crucially, unlike postmenopausal HRT (\u003e50 yrs), young women with POI MUST receive systemic oestrogen + cyclic/continuous progestin (protecting intact uterus) until age ~50 to prevent premature osteoporotic fractures, ischaemic heart disease, and dementia (noting ~5-10% may still intermittently ovulate!).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02045","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Classic Galactosaemia (GALT Deficiency)","q":"A 6-day-old full-term breastfed newborn develops vomiting, lethargy, hepatomegaly, conjugated jaundice, coagulopathy, bilateral \u0027oil-drop\u0027 cataracts on red reflex testing, and Gram-negative Escherichia coli neonatal sepsis. Urine tests POSITIVE for reducing substances (Benedict\u0027s test) yet NEGATIVE on glucose-oxidase dipstick. What is the diagnosis and immediate mandatory dietary action?","o":["Classic Galactosaemia (galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase [GALT] deficiency); IMMEDIATELY stop breastfeeding and all cow\u0027s-milk lactose formulas and switch to a lactose-free Soy-based formula","Biliary atresia; continue cow\u0027s milk formula","Hereditary fructose intolerance (which manifests only after weaning onto fruit/sucrose); continue exclusive breastfeeding","Physiological breastmilk jaundice; increase breastfeeds only"],"a":0,"e":"Accumulation of galactose-1-phosphate and galactitol in GALT deficiency damages the liver, renal proximal tubules (Fanconi aminoaciduria), lens (\u0027oil-drop\u0027 cataract), and impairs neutrophil bactericidal activity—predisposing specifically to fulminant neonatal E. coli sepsis upon starting breastmilk/lactose feeds! Non-glucose reducing substance in urine + E. coli sepsis + jaundice + cataracts demands immediate withdrawal of ALL breastmilk and dairy/lactose formulas and switching to soy/elemental galactose-free formula (confirmed by RBC GALT enzyme assay before any RBC transfusion).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02046","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome (WAS)","q":"A 9-month-old boy presents with persistent bloody diarrhoea, petechiae, severe refractory atopic-like eczema, and recurrent otitis media and pneumococcal pneumonia. CBC shows platelets 18,000/mm3 with unusually SMALL platelet size (Mean Platelet Volume [MPV] 4.2 fL), low serum IgM, elevated IgA and IgE, and poor polysaccharide antibody response (X-linked recessive WAS gene mutation). What is the diagnosis and only curative therapy?","o":["Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura (which exhibits LARGE megathrombocytes/high MPV); oral prednisolone alone","Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome (WAS); Haematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation (HSCT) (plus irradiated CMV-negative blood products, IVIG, and strict avoidance of live vaccines)","Bruton X-linked agammaglobulinaemia (normal platelets); oral amoxicillin alone","Severe eczema with cow\u0027s milk allergy alone"],"a":1,"e":"Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome (X-linked WAS protein cytoskeleton defect) presents in boys with the classic triad of (1) thrombocytopenia with characteristic SMALL platelets (low MPV, distinguishing it from large-platelet ITP/Bernard-Soulier); (2) eczema; and (3) combined T/B-cell immunodeficiency (low IgM, high IgA/IgE, poor anti-polysaccharide response, high lymphoma/autoimmunity risk). Allogeneic HSCT (or gene therapy) is curative, supported by IVIG/antibiotic prophylaxis and irradiated CMV-negative transfusions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02047","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Targeted Temperature Management and Neuroprognostication","q":"A 54-year-old man achieves Return of Spontaneous Circulation (ROSC) after 18 minutes of CPR for out-of-hospital VF arrest and remains comatose in the ICU. Targeted temperature control (preventing fever \u003c= 37.5 C or maintaining 32-36 C for 24 h) is initiated using propofol and fentanyl infusions. At 24 hours post-ROSC, he still has absent motor response. According to AHA/ERC Post-Cardiac Arrest guidelines, when should formal multimodal neurological prognostication (clinical exam, EEG, SSEP, neuroimaging) be performed before considering withdrawal of life-sustaining therapy?","o":["Induce deliberate hyperthermia to 39.5 degrees C to awaken the cortex faster","Withdraw life support immediately at 12 hours post-ROSC based on absent corneal reflex under propofol sedation","Defer formal prognostication until AT LEAST 72 hours after ROSC (or \u003e= 72 hours after return to normothermia once sedatives/neuromuscular blockers are fully cleared)","Declare brain death based solely on a single dilated pupil at 6 hours post-ROSC"],"a":2,"e":"Hypothermia and critical-illness hepatic/renal dysfunction dramatically delay clearance of sedatives (propofol, midazolam, fentanyl) and neuromuscular blockers, confounding early neurological exams. AHA/ERC guidelines mandate active fever prevention (\u003c= 37.5 C) and deferring definitive multimodal neuroprognostication (absent pupillary/corneal reflexes, bilateral absent N20 SSEP waves, burst-suppression/status epilepticus EEG, diffuse anoxic MRI/CT changes, NSE) until AT LEAST 72 hours post-ROSC/normothermia off sedation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02048","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Extra-Axial Brain Tumours","q":"A 52-year-old woman with focal seizures undergoes brain MRI demonstrating a well-circumscribed, broad-based EXTRA-AXIAL mass over the right frontoparietal convexity showing intense homogeneous contrast enhancement, a tapering peripheral dural enhancement (\u0027dural tail sign\u0027), a thin rim of CSF separating the mass from buckled adjacent cortex (\u0027CSF cleft sign\u0027), and adjacent calvarial hyperostosis on CT (histology: psammoma bodies and whorls). What is the diagnosis?","o":["Metastatic melanoma (intra-axial corticomedullary junction)","Oligodendroglioma (intra-axial cortical mass with coarse calcification)","Glioblastoma multiforme (intra-axial with central necrosis and ring enhancement)","Meningioma"],"a":3,"e":"Meningioma (arachnoid cap cell neoplasm, commonest benign extra-axial intracranial tumour, female predominance, progesterone receptor positive, NF2 association) classically exhibits a broad dural base, intense homogeneous enhancement, a \u0027dural tail\u0027 (reactive dural thickening/enhancement), inward cortical buckling with a CSF/vascular cleft confirming extra-axial location, and adjacent bone hyperostosis + calcification (histological whorls and psammoma bodies).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02049","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Visceral Artery Aneurysms","q":"A 28-year-old multiparous woman planning pregnancy undergoes CT angiography for mild abdominal pain, revealing an incidental 2.2 cm saccular aneurysm of the distal splenic artery (commonest visceral artery aneurysm). Why is prophylactic endovascular embolisation/stent-graft (or surgical repair) strongly indicated BEFORE pregnancy even at 2.2 cm diameter (unlike asymptomatic males where threshold is \u003e= 3 cm)?","o":["Pregnancy-related hypervolaemia, portal hypertension, and hormonal medial degeneration carry a \u003e 25-70% maternal-fetal mortality risk from spontaneous rupture in the third trimester (regardless of aneurysm size in childbearing women)","Splenic artery aneurysms shrink spontaneously during pregnancy","Wait until splenic rupture occurs in the third trimester before intervening","Treat medically with oral warfarin indefinitely"],"a":0,"e":"Splenic artery aneurysm is the most common visceral/splanchnic artery aneurysm (~60%), unusually showing female predominance (4:1, linked to multiparity and portal hypertension). While asymptomatic non-pregnant adults are often observed until \u003e= 3.0 cm, ALL splenic artery aneurysms (any size!) in women of childbearing age or pregnant women carry catastrophic third-trimester rupture risk (\u0027double-rupture phenomenon\u0027 into lesser sac then free peritoneum, with high maternal/fetal mortality) and require prophylactic transcatheter coil embolisation/covered stent or surgical repair.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02050","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Acute Achilles Tendon Rupture","q":"A 38-year-old recreational badminton player (who recently received a fluoroquinolone course) feels a sudden sharp \u0027kick\u0027 and hears an audible \u0027pop\u0027 at the back of his right heel while pushing off, then cannot stand on his right tiptoe. With the patient prone and feet hanging off the bed, squeezing the right calf produces NO passive plantarflexion of the foot (whereas the left foot plantarflexes normally), and a palpable tendon gap exists 4 cm above the calcaneus. What is this clinical test, and why can weak active plantarflexion still mislead clinicians?","o":["Lachman test for ACL tear; knee arthroscopy","Positive Simmonds-Thompson calf-squeeze test confirming complete Achilles tendon rupture; intact deep flexors (tibialis posterior, flexor hallucis/digitorum longus, peroneals) preserve weak active plantarflexion, causing ~20% missed diagnoses","Finkelstein test for de Quervain tenosynovitis; thumb spica cast","Negative Thompson test confirming intact Achilles tendon; discharge as simple ankle sprain"],"a":1,"e":"Acute Achilles tendon rupture (classically 3-6 cm proximal to the calcaneal insertion—watershed hypovascular zone, precipitated by eccentric loading, fluoroquinolones, or local steroid injections) produces a palpable gap and a positive Simmonds-Thompson test (absent passive plantarflexion on calf squeeze). Because tibialis posterior, FHL, FDL, and peronei remain intact, patients can still weakly plantarflex against minimal resistance—making calf-squeeze testing + dynamic ultrasound crucial to avoid chronic neglected gap.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02051","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Leukocoria Differential Diagnosis","q":"A healthy 6-year-old boy is brought for unilateral yellow-white pupillary reflex (xanthocoria/leukocoria) and strabismus in the right eye. Indirect ophthalmoscopy shows anomalous \u0027light-bulb\u0027 aneurysmal retinal telangiectatic vessels and massive bright yellow intraretinal/subretinal lipid exudates with exudative retinal detachment. Crucially, B-scan ultrasonography and MRI show NO solid intraocular mass and NO intralesional calcification. What is the diagnosis and standard globe-sparing treatment?","o":["Retinoblastoma; immediate enucleation without further review","Retinopathy of prematurity in a 6-year-old term boy; high-flow oxygen","Coats\u0027 disease (idiopathic retinal telangiectasia); laser photocoagulation or cryotherapy to ablate leaking telangiectatic vessels (+/- intravitreal anti-VEGF) to preserve the eye","Congenital cataract; simple IOL implantation alone"],"a":2,"e":"Coats\u0027 disease is an idiopathic, non-hereditary, typically unilateral (95%) disorder predominantly affecting boys (peak 4-8 years) featuring abnormal retinal capillary telangiectasias (\u0027light-bulb\u0027 dilatations) leaking massive yellow subretinal lipid exudates—making it the most frequent pseudoretinoblastoma causing unilateral leukocoria. Absence of a calcified solid mass on B-scan/CT/MRI distinguishes it from retinoblastoma; early ablative laser/cryotherapy + anti-VEGF controls exudation and saves the globe.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02052","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Psychiatry","topic":"Valproate-Induced Hyperammonaemic Encephalopathy (VHE)","q":"A 35-year-old man with bipolar disorder whose sodium valproate dose was recently increased (and topiramate added for migraines) develops progressive lethargy, confusion, vomiting, asterixis, and stupor. Brain MRI is normal, serum valproate level is within therapeutic range (85 mcg/mL), AST/ALT/bilirubin are completely NORMAL, yet plasma ammonia is markedly elevated at 185 micromol/L. What is the diagnosis and specific metabolic rescue alongside stopping valproate?","o":["Not valproate toxicity because LFTs and valproate serum levels are normal; double the valproate dose","Benign sedation; add oral lorazepam 4 mg","Acute hepatic necrosis; immediate liver transplantation","Valproate-induced Hyperammonaemic Encephalopathy (VHE); stop valproate immediately and administer IV/oral L-Carnitine (plus lactulose/rifaximin)"],"a":3,"e":"Valproate depletes mitochondrial carnitine and inhibits carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I (the rate-limiting urea cycle enzyme), impairing hepatic ammonia detoxification and causing acute hyperammonaemic encephalopathy EVEN WITH completely normal transaminases, normal bilirubin, and therapeutic serum valproate levels (risk amplified by topiramate carbonic-anhydrase co-administration or occult heterozygous OTC deficiency). Stopping valproate and administering L-carnitine (restoring beta-oxidation and urea-cycle N-acetylglutamate) rapidly reverses encephalopathy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02053","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP)","q":"A 34-year-old woman presents with fluctuating confusion, fever (38.4 degrees C), petechiae, hemoglobin 7.2 g/dL, platelets 11,000/mm3, serum creatinine 1.9 mg/dL, markedly elevated LDH, indirect hyperbilirubinemia, normal PT/aPTT, and numerous fragmented RBCs (schistocytes) on peripheral smear. What is the emergency life-saving therapy, and what routine transfusion MUST be avoided unless catastrophic hemorrhage occurs?","o":["Immediate Therapeutic Plasma Exchange (PLEX) with corticosteroids (and caplacizumab/rituximab per protocol); AVOID routine prophylactic platelet transfusion","High-dose IV heparin infusion for presumed DIC","Immediate transfusion of 12 units of pooled platelets without plasma exchange","Immediate splenectomy prior to hematology workup"],"a":0,"e":"Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (schistocytes, high LDH, low haptoglobin) with severe thrombocytopenia, fever, neurologic fluctuation, and renal dysfunction alongside normal PT/aPTT (distinguishing it from DIC) establishes Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP, severe ADAMTS13 deficiency \u003c10%). Daily Therapeutic Plasma Exchange (removes anti-ADAMTS13 autoantibodies and replenishes ADAMTS13) plus glucocorticoids reduces mortality from \u003e90% to \u003c15%. Routine platelet transfusions \u0027feed the fire\u0027 of microvascular VWF-platelet thrombi, precipitating fatal stroke or myocardial infarction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02054","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Retroperitoneal Hematoma Zones","q":"During damage-control laparotomy for a pelvic fracture after a high-speed motor vehicle collision (blunt trauma), the surgeon identifies a stable, non-expanding Zone 3 pelvic retroperitoneal hematoma (without active intraperitoneal rupture) while Zone 1 and Zone 2 are normal. What is the correct intraoperative surgical management of a blunt Zone 3 pelvic hematoma?","o":["Incise the posterior peritoneum widely over the pelvis immediately to hunt for torn venous plexuses","Do NOT open or explore the blunt pelvic retroperitoneal hematoma; apply preperitoneal pelvic packing / external pelvic binder and proceed to urgent interventional angiography/embolization","Explore Zone 3 routinely in all blunt pelvic fractures just like Zone 1","Leave the abdomen open without packing or pelvic stabilization"],"a":1,"e":"Blunt Zone 3 pelvic retroperitoneal hematomas stem mainly from low-pressure sacral/pelvic venous plexuses and cancellous fracture surfaces tamponaded by the intact peritoneum. Opening a stable blunt Zone 3 hematoma releases tamponade and unleashes catastrophic, uncontrollable pelvic venous exsanguination! Instead, stabilize the pelvic ring, pack if needed (preperitoneal packing), and perform angiographic embolization (whereas Zone 1 central hematomas and ALL penetrating retroperitoneal hematomas mandate surgical exploration).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02055","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Complete Hydatidiform Mole","q":"A 23-year-old primigravida at 11 weeks amenorrhea presents with severe hyperemesis, dark prune-juice vaginal bleeding with grape-like vesicles, BP 154/96 mmHg, uterine size corresponding to 20 weeks gestation, serum beta-hCG 240,000 mIU/mL, and a \u0027snowstorm\u0027 intrauterine echogenic pattern with bilateral ovarian theca-lutein cysts on ultrasound. What is the primary definitive management?","o":["Emergency bilateral oophorectomy for the theca-lutein cysts","Immediate sharp metallic D\u0026C curettage followed by immediate discharge with no hCG follow-up","Suction evacuation (vacuum aspiration) with IV oxytocin started after cervical dilation/evacuation initiation, Rh anti-D prophylaxis if Rh-negative, and strict serial weekly beta-hCG follow-up with reliable contraception","Medical induction with prostaglandin misoprostol alone without suction evacuation"],"a":2,"e":"Preeclampsia \u003c20 weeks, hyperemesis, oversized uterus, sky-high beta-hCG, bilateral theca-lutein cysts (driven by hCG hyperstimulation), and snowstorm USG confirm Complete Hydatidiform Mole. Suction curettage is the safest evacuation method (lower perforation and trophoblastic embolization risk than sharp curettage or uterotonic induction); theca-lutein cysts regress spontaneously once beta-hCG normalizes, and weekly beta-hCG surveillance detects persistent gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) early.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02056","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Kawasaki Disease","q":"A 3-year-old boy has had persistent high fever (39.5 degrees C) for 6 days unresponsive to antipyretics, accompanied by bilateral non-purulent bulbar conjunctival injection sparing the limbus, bright red cracked lips and strawberry tongue, swollen erythematous palms/soles, a polymorphous truncal rash, and a single 2 cm tender right anterior cervical lymph node. What is the diagnosis and urgent treatment to prevent coronary artery aneurysms?","o":["Scarlet fever; administer oral amoxicillin alone without echocardiography","Measles; treat with vitamin A and discharge without cardiac evaluation","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis; start methotrexate monotherapy","Kawasaki disease; administer single-dose IV Immunoglobulin (IVIG 2 g/kg over 10-12 hours) plus oral aspirin within 10 days of fever onset and obtain baseline echocardiography"],"a":3,"e":"Fever \u003e=5 days plus \u003e=4 of 5 principal clinical criteria (bilateral non-exudative conjunctivitis, oral mucosal changes, polymorphous rash, peripheral extremity edema/erythema, cervical lymphadenopathy \u003e=1.5 cm) diagnoses Kawasaki disease (medium-vessel vasculitis). Administering IVIG 2 g/kg plus anti-inflammatory aspirin ideally within 10 days of fever onset reduces coronary artery aneurysm incidence from ~25% down to \u003c4%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02057","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Massive Hemoptysis Stabilization","q":"A 50-year-old man with post-tuberculosis right upper lobe bronchiectasis/aspergilloma coughs up 450 mL of bright red blood over 2 hours, becoming hypoxic and tachypneic. While preparing emergent airway protection and urgent Bronchial Artery Embolization (BAE), how should the patient be positioned immediately?","o":["Right lateral decubitus position (bleeding RIGHT lung DOWN) to prevent blood from spilling across the carina into the healthy left lung","Left lateral decubitus position (bleeding right lung UP) so gravity drains blood into the healthy left lung","Prone Trendelenburg position","Flat supine position with head extended downward"],"a":0,"e":"In massive/life-threatening hemoptysis (\u003e300-600 mL/24 hrs), death results from asphyxiation due to clot flooding of alveoli rather than exsanguination. Positioning the known bleeding lung DEPENDENT (right-side down for right-sided bleeding) uses gravity to confine blood within the affected lung while preserving gas exchange in the healthy upper lung—alongside large-bore single-lumen (\u003e=8.0 mm) ETT or selective mainstem intubation/bronchial blocker and urgent Bronchial Artery Embolization (since 90% of massive hemoptysis arises from high-pressure bronchial arteries).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02058","course":"DNB - Diplomate of National Board","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis (SCFE)","q":"An obese 13-year-old boy presents with a 3-week limp and dull medial left knee/thigh pain. Passive flexion of the left hip causes obligate external rotation and abduction (positive Drehmann sign), and frog-leg lateral pelvis X-ray shows posterior-inferior slippage of the femoral epiphysis failing to intersect Klein\u0027s line. What is the standard surgical management for stable SCFE?","o":["Observation with 6 months of vigorous running physiotherapy","Immediate strict non-weight-bearing and urgent single central cannulated screw fixation in situ WITHOUT forceful closed reduction","Immediate vigorous closed reduction under general anesthesia to anatomically snap the epiphysis back before pinning","Total hip arthroplasty immediately at age 13"],"a":1,"e":"Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis (SCFE) occurs via Salter-Harris Type I shearing through the hypertrophic physis in peripubertal adolescents (often obese or hypothyroid/hypogonadal) and frequently presents as referred medial knee pain via the obturator nerve. Forceful closed reduction tears the delicate posterior-superior retinacular vessels, causing catastrophic Avascular Necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head; gentle in-situ single-screw epiphysiodesis stabilizes the slip safely (with prophylactic contralateral pinning considered in high-risk/endocrinopathy cases).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02059","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Size Reduction \u0026 Sieving Standards","q":"According to Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) powder classification in PCI ER-2020 Pharmaceutics, if all particles pass through sieve No. 85 and not more than 40% pass through sieve No. 120, how is the powder classified?","o":["Coarse powder","Moderately coarse powder","Fine powder","Very fine powder"],"a":2,"e":"Under IP standards: Coarse powder = all pass #10, \u003c=40% pass #44; Moderately coarse = all pass #22, \u003c=40% pass #60; Moderately fine = all pass #44, \u003c=40% pass #85; Fine powder = all pass #85, \u003c=40% pass #120; Very fine powder = all pass #120.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02060","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Pharmaceutical Calculations (Alligation)","q":"A pharmacist needs to prepare 500 mL of 70% v/v alcohol by mixing 95% v/v alcohol with 50% v/v alcohol. Using the alligation alternate method, what volumes of 95% and 50% alcohol are required?","o":["350 mL of 95% alcohol and 150 mL of 50% alcohol","250 mL of 95% alcohol and 250 mL of 50% alcohol","277.8 mL of 95% alcohol and 222.2 mL of 50% alcohol","222.2 mL of 95% alcohol and 277.8 mL of 50% alcohol"],"a":3,"e":"Using alligation alternate: parts of 95% = |70 - 50| = 20 parts; parts of 50% = |95 - 70| = 25 parts. Total parts = 20 + 25 = 45 parts. Volume of 95% = (20/45) x 500 = 222.22 mL. Volume of 50% = (25/45) x 500 = 277.78 mL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02061","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law \u0026 Ethics","topic":"Drug Schedules (India)","q":"Under the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 (India), match each Schedule to its regulatory requirement: 1) Schedule H1, 2) Schedule X, 3) Schedule G, 4) Schedule M.","o":["1-Separate register for 3 years (Rx + red Rx box, 3rd/4th gen antibiotics \u0026 ATT), 2-Narcotic/psychotropic drugs requiring 2-year duplicate Rx retention, 3-Caution label \"Dangerous to take except under medical supervision\", 4-GMP for premises \u0026 equipment","1-Biological products only, 2-Schedule C sera, 3-Veterinary medicines, 4-Pack sizes","1-List of ayurvedic poisons, 2-OTC antacids, 3-Blood bank standards, 4-Clinical trials","1-GMP requirements, 2-Cosmetics standards, 3-Ophthalmic preparations, 4-Life period of drugs"],"a":0,"e":"In Indian Pharmacy Jurisprudence: Schedule H1 restricts indiscriminate dispensing of 3rd/4th-generation cephalosporins, carbapenems, anti-TB drugs, and certain habit-forming drugs (red Rx box, separate register preserved for 3 years); Schedule X covers habit-forming psychotropic/narcotic drugs (Rx in duplicate, preserved for 2 years); Schedule G carries the mandatory caution label; Schedule M specifies Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02062","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Tablet Processing Defects","q":"During high-speed rotary compression of paracetamol tablets, the upper crown separates cleanly from the main tablet body immediately after ejection. Which combination of root cause and corrective action MOST accurately addresses this defect (capping)?","o":["Insufficient disintegrant concentration; add extra sodium starch glycolate intragranularly","Air entrapment and excessive fine powder in granules; reduce turret speed, apply pre-compression, and decrease fines below 10-15%","Excessive moisture in granules causing adherence to punch faces; increase drying temperature and polish punch tips","Uneven dye migration during film drying; slow down spray rate and increase pan speed"],"a":1,"e":"Capping is the partial or complete separation of the top or bottom crown of a tablet horizontally due to trapped air expanding upon decompression or excessive fines causing poor interparticulate bonding. Applying pre-compression allows air escape, reducing turret speed increases dwell time, and controlling fines/moisture improves plastic deformation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02063","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Alligation Alternate Method","q":"A compounding pharmacist needs to prepare 500 mL of 50% v/v alcohol using available stocks of 70% v/v alcohol and 20% v/v alcohol. Using the alligation alternate method, what volumes of 70% v/v and 20% v/v alcohol are required respectively?","o":["200 mL of 70% v/v and 300 mL of 20% v/v","250 mL of 70% v/v and 250 mL of 20% v/v","300 mL of 70% v/v and 200 mL of 20% v/v","350 mL of 70% v/v and 150 mL of 20% v/v"],"a":2,"e":"By alligation alternate: parts of stronger alcohol (70%) = desired (50) - weaker (20) = 30 parts; parts of weaker alcohol (20%) = stronger (70) - desired (50) = 20 parts. Total = 50 parts. For 500 mL: 70% alcohol = (30/50) x 500 = 300 mL; 20% alcohol = (20/50) x 500 = 200 mL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02064","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Limit Test for Iron (Indian Pharmacopoeia)","q":"In the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) limit test for iron, thioglycollic acid reacts with iron in an ammoniacal medium to produce a purple-red colour. What is the specific purpose of adding iron-free citric acid prior to making the solution alkaline with ammonia?","o":["To precipitate heavy metal impurities such as lead and arsenic as insoluble citrates","To oxidise all ferrous ions completely to ferric ions because only ferric ions react with thioglycollic acid","To convert the purple-red ferrous thioglycollate complex into a colourless standard blank","To form a soluble complex with iron so that it does not precipitate as ferric hydroxide upon addition of ammonia"],"a":3,"e":"In the IP limit test for iron, thioglycollic acid reduces ferric (Fe3+) to ferrous (Fe2+) iron and forms a soluble purple-red coordination complex (ferrous thioglycollate) in alkaline ammoniacal medium. Citric acid chelates/complexes iron in solution, preventing precipitation of iron as ferric hydroxide when ammonia is added.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02065","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Isoniazid Chemistry \u0026 Toxicity","q":"Isoniazid (isonicotinic acid hydrazide) frequently causes peripheral neuropathy in malnourished or slow-acetylator patients on antitubercular therapy. Which chemical mechanism explains this adverse effect and justifies routine vitamin supplementation?","o":["Isoniazid condenses with pyridoxal-5\u0027-phosphate to form an inactive hydrazone excreted in urine, depleting vitamin B6","Isoniazid undergoes N-dealkylation to form neurotoxic cyanide metabolites","Isoniazid inhibits dihydrofolate reductase selectively in peripheral Schwann cells","Isoniazid irreversibly chelates dietary vitamin B12 in the ileal lumen preventing intrinsic factor binding"],"a":0,"e":"The free hydrazide group (-CONHNH2) of isoniazid reacts with the aldehyde group of pyridoxal-5\u0027-phosphate (active Vitamin B6) to form a Schiff base / hydrazone that is rapidly excreted renally, and also inhibits pyridoxine phosphokinase. Concomitant pyridoxine (Vitamin B6, 10-50 mg/day) prevents peripheral neuropathy without impairing antimycobacterial activity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02066","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Alkaloid Identification Tests","q":"During pharmacognostic evaluation of Belladonna leaf (Atropa belladonna), an evaporated alkaloidal residue is treated with fuming nitric acid, evaporated to dryness on a water bath, and moistened with alcoholic potassium hydroxide to yield a bright violet colour. Name this specific chemical test and the alkaloid class detected.","o":["Murexide test for purine alkaloids (caffeine/theobromine)","Vitali-Morin test for tropane alkaloids (hyoscyamine/atropine)","Van Urk test for indole alkaloids of ergot (ergometrine)","Thalleioquin test for quinoline alkaloids of cinchona (quinine)"],"a":1,"e":"The Vitali-Morin reaction is characteristic of tropane ester alkaloids containing tropic acid (atropine, hyoscyamine, hyoscine/scopolamine) found in Atropa belladonna and Datura stramonium. Nitration of the aromatic ring followed by methanolic/alcoholic KOH forms a violet-coloured Meisenheimer-type complex.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02067","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Vaccine Vial Monitor (VVM) Interpretation","q":"At a Primary Health Centre outreach session, a pharmacist inspects an oral polio vaccine (OPV) vial before administration and observes that the inner square of the Vaccine Vial Monitor (VVM) is darker than the surrounding outer reference circle. What action MUST be taken?","o":["Discard the vial immediately and report heat exposure because the VVM has reached Stage 4 (beyond discard point)","Administer the vaccine immediately during the same session because Stage 4 indicates peak potency","Place the vial in a deep freezer at -20 C for 24 hours to reverse the VVM colour change","Use the vial only for booster doses in children above 2 years of age"],"a":0,"e":"A Vaccine Vial Monitor (VVM) measures cumulative heat exposure. Stage 1 (inner square white) and Stage 2 (inner square lighter than outer circle) are usable if within expiry. Once the inner square matches (Stage 3) or becomes darker than (Stage 4) the outer circle, the vaccine has reached or exceeded the discard point and MUST be discarded. VVM colour change is irreversible.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02068","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Organophosphate Poisoning Management","q":"A 32-year-old agricultural worker is brought to the emergency department 90 minutes after accidental dermal and inhalational exposure to malathion, presenting with profuse bronchorrhoea, pinpoint pupils, bradycardia (44/min), and skeletal muscle fasciculations. Alongside atropine, intravenous pralidoxime (2-PAM) is administered. What specific therapeutic role does pralidoxime fulfil that atropine alone cannot?","o":["Chelate-binds circulating malathion in plasma to enhance renal clearance without affecting cholinesterase","Directly blocks central and peripheral muscarinic M3 receptors in bronchial smooth muscle to dry secretions","Reactivates phosphorylated acetylcholinesterase before enzyme \u0027aging\u0027 occurs, reversing nicotinic skeletal muscle fasciculations and weakness","Stimulates presynaptic alpha-2 adrenergic receptors to inhibit acetylcholine release"],"a":2,"e":"Atropine competitively blocks muscarinic receptors (reversing bronchorrhoea, bronchospasm, and bradycardia) but has NO effect on nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction. Pralidoxime (an oxime) nucleophilically cleaves the phosphate-ester bond on acetylcholinesterase prior to irreversible dealkylation (\u0027aging\u0027), restoring enzyme activity and resolving nicotinic respiratory muscle paralysis/fasciculations.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02069","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Pressurised Metered-Dose Inhaler (pMDI) Counselling","q":"During an OSCE counselling station, a community pharmacist dispenses a budesonide pressurised metered-dose inhaler (pMDI) with a valved holding chamber (spacer) to a newly diagnosed asthma patient. Which counselling instruction is ESSENTIAL after each inhaled corticosteroid dose?","o":["Rinse the mouth and gargle thoroughly with water and spit it out to prevent oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia","Take a rapid, forceful inhalation before pressing the canister and swallow the post-dose gargle water","Actuate two puffs simultaneously into the spacer and inhale both within a single breath","Wash the plastic spacer in boiling water daily and dry it inside with a lint towel"],"a":0,"e":"Local deposition of inhaled corticosteroids (budesonide, fluticasone) in the oropharynx causes mucosal immunosuppression leading to oral thrush (candidiasis) and hoarseness (dysphonia). Using a spacer reduces oropharyngeal deposition, and rinsing/gargling with water and spitting out immediately removes residual steroid.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02070","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Hypertension in Diabetes Mellitus","q":"A 54-year-old man with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus has a clinic blood pressure of 152/94 mmHg and a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) of 180 mg/g. Serum potassium is 4.2 mEq/L and eGFR is 72 mL/min/1.73 m2. Which antihypertensive class is preferred as initial therapy per Indian Standard Treatment Guidelines?","o":["Oral methyldopa as first-line monotherapy for diabetic nephropathy","A loop diuretic (furosemide) as monotherapy to accelerate urinary albumin clearance","A non-selective beta-blocker (propranolol) as monotherapy because it masks hypoglycaemic tachycardia beneficially","An ACE inhibitor (e.g., enalapril/ramipril) or an ARB (e.g., telmisartan/losartan) to reduce intraglomerular pressure and albuminuria"],"a":3,"e":"In patients with hypertension, diabetes, and albuminuria (UACR \u003e= 30 mg/g), an ACE inhibitor or ARB is first-line because blocking angiotensin II dilates the glomerular efferent arteriole, lowering intraglomerular filtration pressure, slowing diabetic nephropathy progression, and reducing proteinuria. Serum creatinine and potassium should be monitored 2-4 weeks after initiation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02071","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law and Ethics","topic":"Regulatory Schedules for Retail Dispensing","q":"Under the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 (India), match each regulatory schedule to its principal requirement: 1) Schedule G, 2) Schedule H1, 3) Schedule M, 4) Schedule X.","o":["1-Medical supervision caution label; 2-Red Rx box \u0026 separate 3-year register for specified antibiotics/anti-TB drugs; 3-Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP); 4-Duplicate prescription retained for 2 years with NRx symbol","1-Red Rx box \u0026 3-year register; 2-Duplicate prescription retained for 2 years; 3-Medical supervision caution label; 4-Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP)","1-Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP); 2-Medical supervision caution label; 3-Duplicate prescription retained for 2 years; 4-Red Rx box \u0026 3-year register","1-Duplicate prescription retained for 2 years; 2-Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP); 3-Red Rx box \u0026 3-year register; 4-Medical supervision caution label"],"a":0,"e":"Under the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945: Schedule G requires a caution label (\u0027dangerous to take except under medical supervision\u0027); Schedule H1 covers restricted third/fourth-generation antibiotics, anti-TB, and certain habit-forming drugs requiring a red Rx box and a separate register kept for 3 years; Schedule M specifies GMP for manufacturing premises; Schedule X covers narcotic/psychotropic substances requiring prescriptions in duplicate (one copy retained for 2 years) and NRx warning.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02072","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Pharmaceutical Milling Principles","q":"A pharmaceutical unit needs to micronise a thermolabile crystalline antibiotic down to 2-5 micrometres without mechanical moving-part contamination or frictional heat build-up. Which size-reduction equipment and operating principle is MOST suitable?","o":["Cutter mill operating by shearing action of sharp rotating blades","Fluid energy mill (jet mill) operating by interparticulate impact and attrition in high-velocity compressed air/nitrogen","Edge runner mill operating by heavy granite stone crushing","Hammer mill operating at high rotor speed with steel hammers"],"a":1,"e":"A fluid energy mill (microniser/jet mill) suspends powder particles in high-velocity compressed gas jets inside a toroidal chamber; size reduction occurs solely through particle-on-particle impact and attrition. Expansion of compressed gas absorbs frictional heat (cooling effect), and the absence of moving grinding parts prevents metallic contamination—ideal for thermolabile, sterile, or micronised APIs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02073","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Dry Heat Sterilisation (Hot Air Oven)","q":"In hospital and industrial pharmacy, anhydrous fixed oils, glycerine, and dry glassware are sterilised in a Hot Air Oven because steam cannot penetrate non-aqueous liquids. What is the standard Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) holding cycle at 160 C and the corresponding biological indicator spore?","o":["100 C for 30 minutes, validated using Pseudomonas diminuta","160 C for 15 minutes, validated using Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores","160 C for 2 hours (120 minutes), validated using Bacillus atrophaeus (formerly B. subtilis var. niger) spores","121 C for 15 minutes, validated using Clostridium sporogenes vegetative cells"],"a":2,"e":"Dry heat sterilisation kills microorganisms by oxidative denaturation and requires higher temperatures and longer exposure than moist heat: standard IP/BP cycles are 160 C for 2 hours, 170 C for 1 hour, or 180 C for 30 minutes. Spores of Bacillus atrophaeus (ATCC 9372) exhibit high resistance to dry heat and ethylene oxide, serving as the official biological indicator.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02074","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Paediatric Dose Estimation","q":"The standard adult oral dose of an analgesic is 500 mg. Using Young\u0027s formula for paediatric posology, what is the calculated dose for a 4-year-old child?","o":["250 mg","166.7 mg","100 mg","125 mg"],"a":3,"e":"Young\u0027s formula calculates paediatric dose from age (in years, typically 1-12 years): Child Dose = [Age / (Age + 12)] x Adult Dose = [4 / (4 + 12)] x 500 mg = (4 / 16) x 500 mg = 0.25 x 500 mg = 125 mg.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02075","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Limit Test for Arsenic (Gutzeit Apparatus)","q":"During the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) limit test for arsenic in the Gutzeit apparatus, arsine gas (AsH3) passes up the glass tube through a plug of cotton wool moistened with lead acetate solution before contacting mercuric chloride paper. What is the specific function of the lead acetate cotton wool?","o":["To trap any interfering hydrogen sulphide (H2S) gas as black lead sulphide so it does not falsely stain the mercuric chloride paper","To catalyse the reduction of pentavalent arsenic to trivalent arsine","To oxidise arsine gas into elemental metallic arsenic before reaching the paper","To absorb moisture so that stannated hydrochloric acid remains concentrated"],"a":0,"e":"In the Gutzeit limit test for arsenic, zinc and stannated HCl reduce arsenic (via potassium iodide and stannous chloride converting As5+ to As3+) into volatile arsine gas (AsH3), which reacts with mercuric chloride paper to produce a yellow-to-brown stain. Trace sulphide impurities in reagents/samples liberate H2S gas that would also blacken/stain the mercuric chloride paper; lead acetate cotton wool reacts with H2S to form insoluble PbS, preventing false-positive staining.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02076","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Biguanides — Metformin","q":"Metformin is the first-line oral antidiabetic agent in Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus because it suppresses hepatic gluconeogenesis and improves peripheral insulin sensitivity without causing hypoglycaemia or weight gain. In which clinical situation is metformin strictly contraindicated due to increased risk of fatal lactic acidosis?","o":["Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) with normal hepatic and renal function","Severe renal impairment with eGFR \u003c 30 mL/min/1.73 m2 or acute hypoxic/septic shock","Mild essential hypertension controlled on amlodipine","Newly diagnosed obese Type 2 diabetes with eGFR 85 mL/min/1.73 m2"],"a":1,"e":"Metformin is excreted unchanged exclusively by the kidneys via glomerular filtration and active tubular secretion (OCT2/MATE). When eGFR falls below 30 mL/min/1.73 m2 (or during tissue hypoxia/sepsis/hepatic failure), metformin accumulates, inhibits mitochondrial respiratory chain complex I, impairs hepatic lactate clearance, and precipitates metformin-associated lactic acidosis (MALA).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02077","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Borntrager\u0027s vs Modified Borntrager\u0027s Test","q":"A crude extract of Aloe (Aloe barbadensis) fails to give a pink-red colour in the standard Borntrager\u0027s test (dilute acid + ether + ammonia), whereas Senna leaf gives a positive pink-red ammoniacal layer. Why is the Modified Borntrager\u0027s test (using ferric chloride and HCl) required for Aloe?","o":["Aloe contains volatile monoterpenes that mask the ammonia indicator unless chelated with iron","Aloe contains cardiac steroidal glycosides lacking an aromatic anthracene ring","Aloe contains anthraquinone C-glycosides (barbaloin/aloin) resistant to simple dilute-acid hydrolysis, requiring oxidative cleavage with FeCl3/HCl to liberate free aloe-emodin","Aloe contains cyanogenic glycosides that require alkaline copper oxidation"],"a":2,"e":"Senna and Rhubarb contain O-glycosides of anthraquinones (sennosides) whose C-O-sugar bond hydrolyses readily with dilute H2SO4/HCl, yielding free anthraquinones extractable into ether/chloroform to turn ammonia pink-red (Borntrager\u0027s test). Aloe contains C-glycosides (barbaloin/aloin) with a stable C-C glycosidic bond that requires oxidative hydrolysis with FeCl3 + concentrated HCl (Modified Borntrager\u0027s test) to release free aloe-emodin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02078","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Absorbable Surgical Catgut","q":"From which biological source is absorbable surgical catgut prepared, and why MUST it NOT be sterilised by moist heat (autoclaving)?","o":["Prepared from regenerated cellulose of cotton linters; autoclaving causes instant caramelisation","Prepared from fibroin protein of Bombyx mori silkworm cocoons; autoclaving melts the silk wax coating","Prepared from polyglycolic acid polymers; autoclaving turns it into non-absorbable nylon","Prepared from purified collagen of the submucosal layer of sheep/goat small intestine; autoclaving denatures/hydrolyses collagen destroying tensile strength"],"a":3,"e":"Surgical catgut is an absorbable natural suture derived from collagen ribbons of the intestinal submucosa of healthy sheep or goats (or serosa of cattle). Because collagen is a heat-labile protein that denatures into gelatin on boiling/autoclaving (losing tensile strength), catgut is sterilised chemically or by gamma irradiation (Co-60) in tubing fluid (e.g., isopropyl alcohol). Treatment with chromium salts (chromic catgut) slows enzymatic absorption by macrophages.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02079","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Cardiac Conduction System \u0026 Electrocardiogram (ECG)","q":"On a standard surface electrocardiogram (ECG), which electrical cardiac event is represented by the P wave, and what does the PR interval (0.12-0.20 s) primarily reflect?","o":["P wave represents atrial depolarisation; PR interval reflects conduction time from the SA node through the atria and physiological delay at the AV node","P wave represents ventricular repolarisation; PR interval reflects Bundle of His automaticity alone","P wave represents atrial repolarisation; PR interval reflects aortic valve closure","P wave represents ventricular depolarisation; PR interval reflects Purkinje fibre repolarisation"],"a":0,"e":"In a normal sinus ECG: the P wave corresponds to atrial depolarisation initiated by the sinoatrial (SA) node; the PR interval (start of P to start of QRS, normal 0.12-0.20 s) measures atrial conduction plus the critical physiological slowing at the AV node allowing complete ventricular filling; the QRS complex reflects ventricular depolarisation (masking atrial repolarisation); and the T wave reflects ventricular repolarisation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02080","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Biomedical Waste Management Rules, 2016 (India)","q":"Under India\u0027s Biomedical Waste Management Rules, 2016 (and 2018 amendment), match each waste item to its mandatory colour-coded segregation container: 1) Blood-soaked gauze \u0026 expired cytotoxic drugs, 2) Contaminated plastic IV tubing \u0026 disposable syringes without needles, 3) Used hypodermic needles \u0026 scalpel blades, 4) Broken glass ampoules \u0026 vials.","o":["1-White container; 2-Blue box; 3-Red bag; 4-Yellow bag","1-Yellow bag; 2-Red bag; 3-White translucent puncture-proof container; 4-Blue cardboard box / puncture-proof container","1-Red bag; 2-Yellow bag; 3-Blue box; 4-White container","1-Black general waste bin; 2-Blue box; 3-Yellow bag; 4-Red bag"],"a":1,"e":"Per Biomedical Waste Management Rules, 2016 (MoEFCC, India): Yellow non-chlorinated plastic bags receive infectious soiled dressings, anatomical waste, chemical waste, and discarded/expired medicines (incineration/deep burial); Red bags receive contaminated recyclable plastics such as IV sets, catheters, gloves, and syringes without fixed needles (autoclave + shredding); White translucent puncture-proof leak-proof containers receive metallic sharps (needles, scalpels); Blue-marked puncture-proof boxes receive glassware and metallic body implants.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02081","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"First-Line Antitubercular Therapy Regimen \u0026 Toxicities","q":"A 38-year-old man newly diagnosed with drug-sensitive pulmonary tuberculosis is initiated on the NTEP daily fixed-dose combination (2HRZE / 4HRE). After 6 weeks, he complains of blurred vision and difficulty distinguishing red from green traffic signals. Which first-line antitubercular drug is MOST likely responsible for this adverse effect?","o":["Isoniazid (causing orange-coloured sputum)","Pyrazinamide (causing hyperuricaemia and arthralgia)","Ethambutol (causing dose-dependent retrobulbar optic neuritis)","Rifampicin (causing harmless red-orange tear discolouration)"],"a":2,"e":"Under India\u0027s National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP), drug-sensitive TB is treated with 2 months of Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide, and Ethambutol (HRZE) followed by 4 months of HRE. Ethambutol causes dose- and duration-dependent retrobulbar optic neuritis presenting earliest as loss of red-green colour discrimination (dyschromatopsia) and decreased visual acuity, requiring prompt discontinuation of ethambutol to prevent irreversible vision loss.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02082","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Opioid Overdose \u0026 Antagonists","q":"A 26-year-old man is brought to casualty unconscious with a respiratory rate of 6 breaths/min, cyanosis, and pinpoint pupils after intravenous morphine overdose. Following intravenous naloxone 0.4 mg, he regains consciousness and normal breathing within 2 minutes. Why MUST the patient remain under close observation for several hours after initial reversal?","o":["Naloxone causes delayed pulmonary oedema only 4 hours post-injection in all patients","Naloxone converts morphine into normorphine, which is ten times more sedating after 2 hours","Naloxone permanently destroys mu-opioid receptors, triggering delayed status epilepticus","Naloxone has a much shorter elimination half-life (30-60 minutes) than morphine, creating high risk of recurrent respiratory depression (renarcotisation)"],"a":3,"e":"Naloxone is a pure competitive opioid receptor antagonist that rapidly reverses mu-receptor-mediated respiratory depression, miosis, and coma. However, its plasma half-life (30-60 minutes) is substantially shorter than that of morphine (2-4 hours) or methadone (24+ hours), so patients must be monitored closely and given repeat doses or continuous infusion if respiratory depression recurs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02083","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy and Management","topic":"WHO Reduced-Osmolarity ORS \u0026 Zinc Therapy","q":"A mother asks a community pharmacist for Oral Rehydration Salts (ORS) for her 3-year-old child with acute watery diarrhoea and mild dehydration. What is the total osmolarity and sodium-glucose composition of the standard WHO/UNICEF reduced-osmolarity ORS (per litre of clean drinking water), and what adjuvant micronutrient should be co-dispensed?","o":["245 mOsm/L (Na+ 75 mEq/L, glucose 75 mmol/L, citrate 10 mmol/L, K+ 20 mEq/L); co-dispense oral Zinc 20 mg/day for 10-14 days","150 mOsm/L (sucrose-only without sodium); co-dispense ciprofloxacin syrup","311 mOsm/L (Na+ 90 mEq/L, glucose 111 mmol/L); co-dispense oral loperamide 2 mg","400 mOsm/L hypertonic solution; co-dispense activated charcoal"],"a":0,"e":"WHO/UNICEF reduced-osmolarity ORS (NaCl 2.6 g, KCl 1.5 g, trisodium citrate 2.9 g, anhydrous glucose 13.5 g per litre) yields 245 mOsm/L (Na+ 75 mEq/L, glucose 75 mmol/L), exploiting intact intestinal SGLT1 sodium-glucose cotransport while reducing stool output compared with the older 311 mOsm/L formula. MoHFW/IAP guidelines also recommend oral Zinc (20 mg/day for 10-14 days in children \u003e=6 months; 10 mg/day in \u003c6 months) to shorten duration and prevent recurrence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02084","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Hospital and Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Physical-Chemical Incompatibilities","q":"While compounding a Total Parenteral Nutrition (TPN) bag in a hospital sterile admixture unit, concentrated calcium gluconate and potassium phosphate are inadvertently added directly next to each other before dilution in a high-pH amino acid/dextrose base, resulting in cloudy white microcrystals. What precipitate has formed and why is this hazardous if infused?","o":["Heparin-protamine complex causing immediate systemic anticoagulation","Insoluble dibasic calcium phosphate (CaHPO4) precipitate, which can cause fatal pulmonary microvascular embolism upon intravenous infusion","Fat emulsion creaming due to dextrose polymerisation","Soluble sodium chloride crystals that dissolve harmlessly in venous blood"],"a":1,"e":"Calcium and phosphate ions have limited solubility together, especially as pH rises (favouring dibasic HPO4^2- over monobasic H2PO4-), at higher temperatures, or when added sequentially in concentrated form. Insoluble calcium phosphate crystals can occlude pulmonary capillaries, causing severe respiratory distress or fatal pulmonary embolism. Pharmacists prevent this by using calcium gluconate (less dissociated than CaCl2), adding phosphate first, mixing thoroughly before adding calcium last, and checking solubility curves with a 0.22/1.2-micron in-line filter.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02085","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Peptic Ulcer Disease \u0026 GERD","q":"A 45-year-old patient prescribed oral omeprazole 20 mg once daily for erosive gastro-oesophageal reflux disease reports minimal symptom relief because he takes the capsule immediately before bedtime on an empty stomach. Why should omeprazole be taken 30-60 minutes BEFORE the first meal (breakfast) of the day?","o":["Food accelerates hepatic CYP2C19 metabolism to double systemic half-life","Omeprazole requires gastric lipid micelles from dinner to dissolve its enteric coating in the stomach","PPIs are short-half-life prodrugs that require post-prandial acid secretion to activate H+/K+-ATPase proton pumps into canalicular membranes so the active sulfenamide can irreversibly bind active pumps","Night-time administration converts omeprazole directly into ranitidine in the colon"],"a":2,"e":"Omeprazole has a short plasma half-life (~1-1.5 hours) and inhibits only actively secreting parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase proton pumps. Taking the enteric-coated capsule 30-60 minutes before breakfast ensures peak plasma drug absorption coincides with meal-stimulated translocation of dormant proton pumps to the secretory canaliculus and acid-driven conversion of the prodrug to its active sulfenamide.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02086","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law and Ethics","topic":"Drugs and Magic Remedies (Objectionable Advertisements) Act, 1954","q":"An Ayurvedic/herbal marketer prints newspaper pamphlets claiming a \u0027guaranteed permanent 7-day cure for diabetes mellitus, cataract, and cancer\u0027 via a proprietary capsule. Under which Indian statute is this advertisement a punishable offence?","o":["Patent Act, 1970","Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act, 1960","Poisons Act, 1919","Drugs and Magic Remedies (Objectionable Advertisements) Act, 1954"],"a":3,"e":"The Drugs and Magic Remedies (Objectionable Advertisements) Act, 1954 prohibits misleading advertisements of drugs claiming diagnosis, cure, mitigation, treatment, or prevention of specified diseases/conditions listed in its Schedule (such as diabetes, cancer, cataract, blindness, epilepsy, sexual impotence) as well as advertisements promoting \u0027magic remedies\u0027 (talismans, mantras, kavachas).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02087","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Hard Gelatin Capsule Sizes \u0026 Moisture Control","q":"In pharmaceutical manufacturing of hard gelatin capsules (standard sizes 000, 00, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5), which capsule size holds the LARGEST fill volume and what equilibrium moisture range keeps gelatin shells neither brittle nor tacky?","o":["Size 000 (~1.37 mL) is the largest and Size 5 (~0.13 mL) is the smallest; shell moisture must be maintained at 12-16%","Size 5 (~1.37 mL) is the largest and Size 000 (~0.13 mL) is the smallest; shell moisture must be maintained at 0-2%","All sizes 000 to 5 have identical 0.50 mL internal volume differing only in cap colour","Size 0 is the largest (~2.50 mL) and Size 000 is the smallest; shell moisture must be maintained at 35-45%"],"a":0,"e":"Hard gelatin capsule sizes run inversely to their numerical designation: Size 000 is the largest (~1.37 mL), followed by 00 (~0.95 mL), 0 (~0.68 mL), 1 (~0.50 mL), 2 (~0.37 mL), 3 (~0.30 mL), 4 (~0.21 mL), and Size 5 is the smallest (~0.13 mL). Hard gelatin shells normally contain 12-16% bound moisture; dropping below ~10% makes shells brittle and prone to cracking, whereas exceeding ~18% causes softening and distortion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02088","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Sodium Chloride Equivalent (E-Value) Calculation","q":"A hospital pharmacist compounds 100 mL of a 1% w/v pilocarpine hydrochloride ophthalmic solution (sodium chloride equivalent E = 0.24). Assuming 0.9% w/v NaCl is isotonic with tears, how much additional sodium chloride must be added to render this 100 mL solution isotonic?","o":["1.14 g","0.66 g","0.90 g","0.24 g"],"a":1,"e":"100 mL of 1% w/v pilocarpine HCl contains 1.0 g of drug. Its sodium chloride equivalent contribution = 1.0 g x 0.24 = 0.24 g NaCl. Because 100 mL of pure isotonic saline requires 0.90 g NaCl, the additional NaCl required = 0.90 g - 0.24 g = 0.66 g.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02089","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Limit Test for Chloride \u0026 Sulphate (IP)","q":"In the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) limit test for chloride, why is dilute nitric acid added prior to silver nitrate (AgNO3) in the Nessler cylinder, and why does the IP limit test for sulphate employ ethanolic potassium sulphate inside the barium sulphate reagent?","o":["Dilute HNO3 prevents precipitation of interfering silver salts (carbonate, phosphate, oxalate) keeping only AgCl opalescence; ethanolic K2SO4 provides fine BaSO4 seed nuclei while alcohol prevents supersaturation for uniform turbidity","Dilute HNO3 reduces Ag+ to metallic black silver; K2SO4 oxidises sulphides to elemental yellow sulphur","Dilute HNO3 buffers pH to 10.5 to dissolve AgCl; K2SO4 precipitates interfering calcium as CaSO4","Both dilute HNO3 and ethanolic K2SO4 act purely as antimicrobial preservatives for stored Nessler cylinders"],"a":0,"e":"In the IP limit test for chloride, neutral/alkaline media allow AgNO3 to precipitate silver carbonate, silver phosphate, or silver oxalate; acidifying with dilute HNO3 dissolves those salts so opalescence reflects only insoluble AgCl. In the IP sulphate limit test, Barium Sulphate Reagent contains ethanolic BaCl2 + trace K2SO4: the trace K2SO4 seeds uniform microcrystalline BaSO4 nuclei increasing sensitivity, while alcohol lowers BaSO4 solubility to prevent supersaturation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02090","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"8-Aminoquinolines (Primaquine)","q":"A 24-year-old man recovering from acute Plasmodium vivax malaria (treated with chloroquine) is prescribed a 14-day course of primaquine to prevent relapse. Three days later he develops dark cola-coloured urine, jaundice, pallor, and Heinz bodies on peripheral smear. Deficiency of which erythrocyte enzyme MUST be screened prior to primaquine therapy per Indian NVBDCP guidelines?","o":["UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1A1","Erythrocyte acetylcholinesterase","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD)","Pyruvate kinase"],"a":2,"e":"Primaquine (an 8-aminoquinoline) eliminates dormant hepatic hypnozoites of P. vivax and P. ovale (radical cure) and has gametocytocidal activity against P. falciparum. However, its oxidative metabolites deplete reduced glutathione in red blood cells lacking G6PD (which generates NADPH via the hexose monophosphate shunt), precipitating acute intravascular haemolysis, haemoglobinuria, and Heinz-body formation. G6PD screening is essential before 14-day daily primaquine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02091","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Hydrolysable vs Condensed Tannins","q":"During pharmacognostic evaluation of crude astringent drugs such as Terminalia chebula (Myrobalan) and Acacia catechu (Black Catechu), which specific test confirms the presence of true tannins (versus pseudotannins), and what colour reaction distinguishes hydrolysable from condensed tannins with ferric chloride (FeCl3)?","o":["Dragendorff\u0027s test (orange-red precipitate); FeCl3 gives colourless solution","Fehling\u0027s test (brick-red Cu2O); FeCl3 gives emerald green with hydrolysable tannins","Shinoda test (magnesium + HCl magenta colour); FeCl3 gives bright orange with all tannins","Goldbeater\u0027s skin test (brown-black staining of ox-intestine membrane); FeCl3 gives blue-black with hydrolysable tannins and brownish-green with condensed tannins"],"a":3,"e":"True tannins (high molecular weight polyphenols) precipitate proteins and convert animal hide into leather, detected specifically by the Goldbeater\u0027s skin test (a small piece of ox-intestine membrane soaked in HCl, dipped in tannin extract, washed, and treated with FeSO4 turns brown-black). With FeCl3 reagent, hydrolysable (gallotannin/ellagitannin) drugs yield a blue-black colour whereas condensed (catechin/proanthocyanidin) tannins yield a brownish-green colour.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02092","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Physicochemical Evaluation — Ash Values","q":"When testing crude root/rhizome powders such as Rauwolfia serpentina or Zingiber officinale according to Indian Pharmacopoeia standards, which ash determination specifically quantifies adhering earthy matter, sand, and siliceous contamination?","o":["Acid-insoluble ash (residue remaining after boiling total ash in dilute hydrochloric acid)","Volatile ether-soluble extractive value","Sulphated ash (residue after ignition with concentrated sulphuric acid)","Water-soluble ash (difference between total ash and water-insoluble residue)"],"a":0,"e":"Total ash measures physiological ash (plant calcium/potassium salts) plus non-physiological contaminants. Boiling total ash with dilute HCl dissolves physiological carbonates/phosphates/oxides while leaving silica, sand, and earthy soil undissolved—making acid-insoluble ash the specific pharmacopoeial index of earthy/siliceous adulteration. Water-soluble ash helps detect substitution with previously water-exhausted drugs (e.g., exhausted ginger/tea).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02093","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Juxtaglomerular Apparatus \u0026 RAAS Activation","q":"Which specialised renal cells secrete the proteolytic enzyme renin into systemic circulation, and which three physiological stimuli trigger its release?","o":["Loop of Henle thin descending limb epithelium; stimulated by parasympathetic vagal acetylcholine","Granular juxtaglomerular (JG) cells in the afferent arteriole wall; stimulated by decreased afferent arteriolar perfusion pressure, decreased NaCl delivery to the macula densa, and beta-1 adrenergic sympathetic stimulation","Podocytes of Bowman\u0027s capsule; stimulated by hypernatraemia and atrial natriuretic peptide","Principal cells of the cortical collecting duct; stimulated by high serum aldosterone"],"a":1,"e":"Modified smooth muscle (granular JG) cells in the afferent glomerular arteriole synthesise and secrete renin in response to: (1) renal baroreceptor stretch reduction (low blood pressure/perfusion), (2) low tubular NaCl sensed by adjacent macula densa cells of the thick ascending limb/DCT, and (3) sympathetic beta-1 adrenoceptor activation. Renin cleaves hepatic angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, which pulmonary ACE converts to vasoconstrictor/aldosterone-stimulating angiotensin II.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02094","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Biochemistry and Clinical Pathology","topic":"Diabetes Mellitus Diagnostic Criteria","q":"According to ICMR / WHO / ADA diagnostic criteria for Diabetes Mellitus in an asymptomatic adult (requiring confirmation on a repeat test), which set of venous plasma glucose and glycated haemoglobin (HbA1c) values crosses the threshold for diagnosing overt Diabetes Mellitus?","o":["Fasting plasma glucose \u003e= 100 mg/dL, OR 2-hour OGTT \u003e= 140 mg/dL, OR HbA1c \u003e= 5.7%","Fasting plasma glucose \u003e= 90 mg/dL, OR random glucose \u003e= 120 mg/dL, OR HbA1c \u003e= 5.0%","Fasting plasma glucose \u003e= 126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L), OR 2-hour post-75 g OGTT plasma glucose \u003e= 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L), OR HbA1c \u003e= 6.5%","Fasting plasma glucose \u003e= 180 mg/dL only, with HbA1c irrelevant in diagnosis"],"a":2,"e":"Diabetes Mellitus is diagnosed by any of: Fasting Plasma Glucose (after \u003e=8 h fast) \u003e= 126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L), 2-h plasma glucose during a 75 g Oral Glucose Tolerance Test \u003e= 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L), HbA1c \u003e= 6.5% (using NGSP-certified assay), or a random plasma glucose \u003e= 200 mg/dL with classic hyperglycaemic symptoms (polyuria, polydipsia, unexplained weight loss).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02095","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Emergency Contraception (MoHFW Family Planning)","q":"A 25-year-old woman visits a community pharmacy 18 hours after unprotected intercourse seeking an Emergency Contraceptive Pill (ECP) containing levonorgestrel 1.5 mg. Which counselling point regarding window of efficacy and primary mechanism is accurate?","o":["It provides full contraceptive protection for all subsequent episodes of unprotected intercourse during the next month","Take 1.5 mg every day for 14 days; it acts by inducing uterine contraction to expel an implanted blastocyst","Wait at least 7 days after intercourse before taking the tablet so fertilisation can finish","Take the single 1.5 mg dose as soon as possible within 72 hours of unprotected intercourse; it acts primarily by inhibiting or delaying ovulation (LH surge) and will NOT disrupt an already implanted pregnancy"],"a":3,"e":"Under India\u0027s MoHFW Family Planning guidelines, single-dose levonorgestrel 1.5 mg is most effective the sooner it is taken (ideally within 72 hours) after unprotected intercourse. It suppresses the pre-ovulatory LH surge to prevent or delay ovulation; it is NOT an abortifacient and is ineffective once implantation has occurred, nor does it protect against subsequent unprotected intercourse or STIs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02096","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Beta-Adrenergic Receptor Antagonists","q":"A 50-year-old man with well-controlled persistent bronchial asthma develops acute severe wheezing and dyspnoea 3 days after starting oral propranolol for essential tremor (or timolol eye drops for glaucoma). What receptor-level mechanism precipitated his bronchospasm?","o":["Blockade of bronchial smooth muscle beta-2 adrenoceptors removes sympathetic bronchodilator tone, triggering unopposed bronchoconstriction","Selective antagonism of alpha-1 adrenoceptors causing pulmonary oedema","Release of leukotriene B4 via cyclooxygenase-2 activation exclusively","Direct stimulation of bronchial muscarinic M3 receptors by propranolol"],"a":0,"e":"Propranolol and timolol are non-selective (beta-1 + beta-2) adrenoceptor antagonists. Even systemic absorption of ophthalmic timolol via the nasolacrimal duct blocks airway beta-2 receptors that mediate smooth muscle relaxation and bronchodilation, leaving parasympathetic vagal bronchoconstriction unopposed and precipitating severe status asthmaticus. Non-selective beta-blockers are contraindicated in asthma.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02097","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Aminoglycoside Pharmacology \u0026 Toxicity","q":"Gentamicin and amikacin require active oxygen-dependent transport across the bacterial inner membrane and exhibit concentration-dependent bactericidal killing. Which molecular target and pair of dose-limiting organ toxicities characterise aminoglycosides?","o":["Inhibit fungal ergosterol 14-alpha-demethylase; cause QT prolongation and adrenal suppression","Bind irreversibly to the 30S bacterial ribosomal subunit causing mRNA misreading; cause proximal tubular nephrotoxicity (usually reversible) and vestibular/cochlear ototoxicity (often irreversible)","Inhibit bacterial cell wall D-Ala-D-Ala cross-linking; cause pseudomembranous colitis exclusively","Bind 50S ribosomal peptidyl transferase; cause dose-independent grey-baby syndrome and aplastic anaemia"],"a":1,"e":"Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin, streptomycin) bind the 30S (and 30S-50S interface) ribosomal subunit to inhibit initiation and induce codon misreading, yielding rapid bactericidal effect (ineffective against strict anaerobes due to O2-dependent uptake). They accumulate in renal proximal tubular epithelial cells (causing reversible non-oliguric AKI) and inner ear endolymph/perilymph (causing irreversible hair-cell ototoxicity), plus neuromuscular blockade at high doses.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02098","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy and Management","topic":"Look-Alike Sound-Alike (LASA) Medicines","q":"During an OSCE medication-safety audit of a retail/hospital pharmacy shelf, an inspector notices that \u0027hydralazine 25 mg\u0027 and \u0027hydroxyzine 25 mg\u0027 blister packs are stored alphabetically right next to each other in identical white boxes. Which WHO/NABH-aligned dispensing safeguard BEST prevents LASA (Look-Alike, Sound-Alike) dispensing errors?","o":["Physically separate LASA pairs onto non-adjacent shelves, apply Tall-Man lettering labels (e.g., hydrALAZINE vs hydrOXYzine) with high-alert shelf stickers, and mandate double-check verification against indication before handing over","Store both drugs in the same bin sorted purely by manufacturer price","Replace written drug names with unlabelled numerical bin codes only","Dispense whichever drug is closer to expiry without verifying the prescriber\u0027s indication"],"a":0,"e":"LASA errors (such as hydrALAZINE vs hydrOXYzine, predniSONE vs prednisoLONE, vinCRIStine vs vinBLAStine) are major preventable medication hazards. NABH and WHO Patient Safety Solutions recommend physical separation (never storing LASA pairs side-by-side alphabetically), Tall-Man lettering to accentuate differing syllables, colour-coded alert flags, barcode scanning, and confirming clinical indication with the patient/prescription prior to dispensing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02099","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Hospital and Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"ABC-VED Matrix Analysis","q":"A hospital pharmacist performs an ABC (Annual Usage Value) and VED (Vital, Essential, Desirable) combined matrix inventory analysis for the central drug store. Which drug category combination demands the STRICTEST top-management control—maintaining zero stock-outs while simultaneously enforcing tight purchasing and low buffer capital tie-up?","o":["Category CD (Low annual expenditure AND Desirable non-essential drugs)","Category BD (Moderate annual expenditure AND Desirable drugs)","Category AV (High annual expenditure AND Vital life-saving drugs)","Category CE (Low annual expenditure AND Essential drugs only)"],"a":2,"e":"In hospital pharmacy ABC-VED matrix analysis, Category I includes AV, AE, AD, BV, and CV items (high cost OR vital clinical criticality). Among these, \u0027A\u0027 items (~10-15% of items accounting for ~70% of annual drug budget) combined with \u0027V\u0027 (Vital drugs whose absence halts patient care/survival, e.g., emergency ICU/life-saving biologics) represent Category AV—requiring the highest supervisory scrutiny to avoid both fatal stock-outs and excess capital lock-up.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02100","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Iron-Deficiency Anaemia Pharmacotherapy","q":"A 26-year-old woman with microcytic hypochromic iron-deficiency anaemia (Hb 8.4 g/dL) is prescribed oral ferrous sulfate tablets. Which dietary/medication counselling instruction maximises gastrointestinal iron absorption while preparing her for expected benign effects?","o":["Always take simultaneously with a glass of milk and an aluminium hydroxide antacid to enhance duodenal DMT1 uptake","Stop the tablets permanently on day 2 if stools turn dark green-black","Co-administer immediately with oral ciprofloxacin and calcium carbonate at breakfast","Take on an empty stomach (or with vitamin C / citrus juice) 1-2 hours apart from antacids, calcium supplements, milk, and tea/coffee; expect harmless dark/black-coloured stools"],"a":3,"e":"Oral iron is absorbed primarily in the duodenum/upper jejunum in the soluble ferrous (Fe2+) state. Ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) and gastric acidity maintain iron in the soluble Fe2+ state enhancing absorption, whereas antacids (raising pH), dairy calcium, phytates, and tea/coffee tannins form insoluble complexes that markedly impair absorption (and iron chelates fluoroquinolones/tetracyclines). Unabsorbed iron oxidises in the gut producing benign dark/black stools.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02101","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law and Ethics","topic":"Statutory Provision on Dispensing by Unregistered Persons","q":"Under Section 42 of the Pharmacy Act, 1948 (India), who is legally permitted to compound, prepare, mix, or dispense any medicine on the prescription of a medical practitioner?","o":["Only a Registered Pharmacist whose name is entered in the State Pharmacy Council register (subject to statutory exemption for a medical practitioner dispensing to their own patients)","Only a pharmaceutical sales representative holding a wholesale licence","Any retail medical store owner holding a GST registration regardless of pharmacy qualification","Any hospital ward boy or receptionist after 1 month of informal on-the-job training"],"a":0,"e":"Section 42 of the Pharmacy Act, 1948 (\u0027Dispensing by unregistered persons\u0027) mandates that no person other than a Registered Pharmacist shall compound, prepare, mix, or dispense any medicine on the prescription of a medical practitioner (except a medical practitioner dispensing for their own patients or hospital patients under statutory exemptions). Violation attracts imprisonment up to 6 months and/or fine under Section 42(2).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02102","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Hospital and Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Intravenous Infusion Drip Rate Calculation","q":"A physician orders 1,000 mL of 0.9% normal saline to be infused intravenously over 8 hours using a standard macrodrip infusion set calibrated at 15 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate in drops per minute (rounded to the nearest whole drop)?","o":["16 drops/min","31 drops/min","125 drops/min","63 drops/min"],"a":1,"e":"Flow rate (drops/min) = [Total Volume (mL) x Drop Factor (drops/mL)] / [Time (hours) x 60 min/h] = (1,000 x 15) / (8 x 60) = 15,000 / 480 = 31.25 drops/min, which rounds to 31 drops/min.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02103","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Dilution of Stock Solutions (C1V1 = C2V2)","q":"A pharmacist needs to dispense 240 mL of a 1:4,000 w/v potassium permanganate antiseptic soak starting from a 1% w/v (1:100) stock solution. What volume of the 1% w/v stock solution must be measured and diluted with purified water to 240 mL?","o":["24.0 mL","2.4 mL","6.0 mL","0.6 mL"],"a":2,"e":"Convert both strengths to the same ratio/percentage: 1% w/v = 1 g in 100 mL (0.01 g/mL), and 1:4,000 w/v = 1 g in 4,000 mL (0.025% w/v). Using C1 x V1 = C2 x V2: (1/100) x V1 = (1/4,000) x 240 -\u003e V1 = (240 x 100) / 4,000 = 240 / 40 = 6.0 mL of stock diluted to 240 mL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02104","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Weight-Based Paediatric Posology","q":"The usual adult dose of an oral antibiotic is 300 mg. Applying Clark\u0027s rule (based on body weight in pounds) for a child weighing 25 lb, what is the calculated paediatric dose?","o":["75 mg","25 mg","100 mg","50 mg"],"a":3,"e":"Clark\u0027s rule calculates paediatric dose from child\u0027s body weight in pounds assuming an average adult weight of 150 lb: Child Dose = (Weight in lb / 150) x Adult Dose = (25 / 150) x 300 mg = (1/6) x 300 mg = 50 mg.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02105","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Organic Nitrates \u0026 PDE-5 Inhibitors","q":"A 59-year-old man with stable angina pectoris who took oral sildenafil 50 mg three hours ago develops chest tightness and takes sublingual glyceryl trinitrate (nitroglycerin) 0.5 mg. Within 10 minutes he collapses with blood pressure 68/40 mmHg and reflex tachycardia. What biochemical mechanism explains this life-threatening drug interaction?","o":["Nitric oxide from nitroglycerin activates soluble guanylyl cyclase to raise vascular smooth muscle cGMP while sildenafil blocks PDE-5 degradation of cGMP, causing synergistic profound vasodilation","Sildenafil inhibits hepatic CYP3A4 causing sublingual nitroglycerin to convert into intravenous epinephrine","Nitroglycerin stimulates vascular alpha-1 receptors while sildenafil blocks beta-1 cardiac pacemaker channels","Both drugs inhibit angiotensin-converting enzyme simultaneously in pulmonary capillaries"],"a":0,"e":"Organic nitrates release nitric oxide (NO), activating guanylyl cyclase to synthesise cyclic GMP (cGMP), which relaxes vascular smooth muscle. Phosphodiesterase-5 (PDE-5) inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil, vardenafil) prevent breakdown of cGMP. Co-administration causes massive intracellular cGMP accumulation, severe venodilation/arteriodilation, and potentially fatal hypotension—making nitrates strictly contraindicated within 24 hours of sildenafil (48 hours of tadalafil).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02106","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Insulin Analogues Pharmacokinetics","q":"A patient with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus uses subcutaneous insulin lispro before meals and insulin glargine once daily at bedtime. Which pharmacokinetic and administration rule accurately distinguishes these two insulin analogues?","o":["Insulin lispro has rapid onset (5-15 min) given immediately before/with meals; insulin glargine is an acidic clear solution that microprecipitates subcutaneously to provide ~24-hour peakless basal release and MUST NOT be mixed in the same syringe with other insulins","Insulin lispro lasts 24 hours without a peak; insulin glargine must be given intravenously for postprandial glucose spikes","Both insulin lispro and insulin glargine are cloudy protamine suspensions that should be shaken vigorously and mixed in one syringe","Insulin glargine has an onset of 5 minutes and peaks sharply at 45 minutes causing nocturnal surges"],"a":0,"e":"Insulin lispro (LysB28, ProB29) dissociates rapidly from hexamers to monomers upon SC injection (onset 5-15 min, peak 1-2 h, duration 3-5 h), controlling mealtime glucose spikes. Insulin glargine is formulated as an acidic (pH 4.0) clear solution that neutralises at tissue pH 7.4 to form subcutaneous microprecipitates releasing insulin steadily over ~24 hours without a pronounced peak; mixing it with neutral insulins alters its pH and destroys its protracted release profile.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02107","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unfractionated Heparin (UFH) Monitoring \u0026 Reversal","q":"A postoperative patient receiving a continuous intravenous infusion of unfractionated heparin (UFH) for acute deep vein thrombosis develops frank haematuria with an activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) \u003e 120 seconds. Which laboratory test monitors UFH, and which specific antidote immediately neutralises circulating heparin?","o":["Monitored by aPTT; reversed immediately by slow intravenous protamine sulfate (~1 mg per 100 units of active UFH)","Monitored by platelet count only; reversed by intravenous desmopressin","Monitored by bleeding time; reversed by intravenous tranexamic acid exclusively","Monitored by INR; reversed immediately by oral vitamin K1 alone"],"a":0,"e":"Unfractionated heparin binds antithrombin III to accelerate inactivation of Factors IIa (thrombin) and Xa, requiring routine monitoring with aPTT (target typically 1.5-2.5x control). In acute haemorrhage, stopping UFH and administering strongly basic protamine sulfate slowly IV (~1 mg per 100 units of UFH remaining from the last 2-3 hours) ionically binds acidic heparin into an inactive complex (rapid infusion risks hypotension/anaphylactoid reactions).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02108","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Hospital and Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Pharmacovigilance Programme of India (PvPI)","q":"Under the Pharmacovigilance Programme of India (PvPI) launched by the Ministry of Health \u0026 Family Welfare, which institution serves as the National Coordination Centre (NCC) collecting Individual Case Safety Reports (ICSRs) from Adverse Drug Reaction Monitoring Centres (AMCs) across the country?","o":["Pharmacy Council of India (PCI), New Delhi","Indian Pharmacopoeia Commission (IPC), Ghaziabad","National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority (NPPA), New Delhi","Central Institute of Medicinal and Aromatic Plants (CIMAP), Lucknow"],"a":1,"e":"Since April 2011, the Indian Pharmacopoeia Commission (IPC) in Ghaziabad, Uttar Pradesh has functioned as the National Coordination Centre (NCC) for PvPI under CDSCO/MoHFW. IPC-PvPI collects suspected ADR reports via regional AMCs, Vigiflow, the ADR PvPI mobile app, and toll-free helpline (1800-180-3024), forwarding validated ICSRs to the WHO-Uppsala Monitoring Centre (VigiBase) for global signal detection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02109","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Hospital and Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Generic Substitution vs Therapeutic Interchange","q":"In a tertiary hospital operating a closed Hospital Formulary System approved by the Pharmacy and Therapeutics (P\u0026T) Committee, a prescription orders oral omeprazole 20 mg, whereas the formulary stocks only pantoprazole 40 mg. Dispensing pantoprazole under an existing P\u0026T-approved standing protocol exemplifies which pharmacy practice, and how does it differ from generic substitution?","o":["Generic substitution (because omeprazole and pantoprazole are identical molecules); therapeutic interchange changes only tablet colour","Pharmacokinetic bio-enhancement requiring no medical staff policy","Therapeutic interchange (substituting a chemically distinct drug of the same therapeutic class under pre-approved P\u0026T Committee medical staff protocol), whereas generic substitution dispenses the exact same active chemical entity in a bioequivalent product","Unauthorised illegal dispensing because hospital P\u0026T committees cannot approve formulary equivalents"],"a":2,"e":"Generic substitution replaces a prescribed brand with an approved bioequivalent product containing the identical API, strength, and dosage form. Therapeutic interchange (therapeutic substitution) replaces a prescribed non-formulary drug with a chemically different agent in the same pharmacological class deemed clinically equivalent—permitted ONLY within a formal institutional formulary policy pre-authorised by the P\u0026T Committee and medical staff (with prescriber notification/opt-out as applicable).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02110","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy and Management","topic":"Standard Latin Prescription Abbreviations","q":"When interpreting prescription Signatura instructions in community pharmacy, match each Latin abbreviation to its correct English dispensing instruction: 1) h.s. (hora somni), 2) a.c. (ante cibos), 3) p.c. (post cibos), 4) q.i.d. (quater in die).","o":["1-Half strength; 2-At bedtime; 3-Twice daily; 4-As directed","1-Every hour; 2-After meals; 3-Before meals; 4-Every fourth day","1-Subcutaneously; 2-With food; 3-On empty stomach; 4-Once daily","1-At bedtime; 2-Before meals; 3-After meals; 4-Four times a day"],"a":3,"e":"Standard Latin prescription abbreviations: h.s. (hora somni) = at bedtime; a.c. (ante cibos) = before meals; p.c. (post cibos) = after meals; q.i.d. (quater in die) = four times daily (contrast with q.d.s. / q.4h = every 4 hours, which equates to 6 doses/24 h). Accurate translation prevents dosing errors during patient counselling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02111","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Measles-Rubella (MR) \u0026 Vitamin A Prophylaxis","q":"Under India\u0027s Universal Immunisation Programme (UIP), at what ages are the two routine doses of Measles-Rubella (MR) vaccine administered, by what route/site, and what first oral dose of Vitamin A accompanies the 9-month MR visit?","o":["MR-1 at 9 completed months (9-12 months) and MR-2 at 16-24 months, 0.5 mL subcutaneously in the right upper arm; accompanied by Vitamin A 1,00,000 IU (1 mL) orally","MR-1 at 14 weeks and MR-2 at 5 years, orally 2 drops; Vitamin A 10,00,000 IU IV","MR-1 at 6 months only, 1.0 mL deep gluteal IM; no Vitamin A given until 10 years","MR-1 at birth and MR-2 at 6 weeks, 0.1 mL intradermally in left thigh; Vitamin A 5,000 IU IM"],"a":0,"e":"Under India\u0027s UIP: Measles-Rubella (MR) vaccine is given as 0.5 mL subcutaneously in the right upper arm at 9 completed months to 12 months (MR-1) and 16-24 months (MR-2), reconstituted with manufacturer diluent and used within 4 hours. At 9 months, infants simultaneously receive the 1st oral dose of Vitamin A (1 lakh IU = 1 mL), followed by 2 lakh IU (2 mL) every 6 months up to 5 years of age.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02112","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"NP-NCD Population-Based Screening at HWCs","q":"Under India\u0027s National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD, formerly NPCDCS) delivered through Ayushman Arogya Mandirs (Health \u0026 Wellness Centres), population-based screening is conducted for all individuals aged 30 years and above targeting which five major NCD conditions?","o":["Malaria, Kala-azar, Lymphatic filariasis, Dengue, and Chikungunya","Hypertension, Diabetes Mellitus, Oral cancer, Breast cancer, and Cervical cancer","Poliomyelitis, Diphtheria, Pertussis, Tetanus, and Measles","Acute appendicitis, Cholelithiasis, Renal calculi, Cataract, and Varicose veins"],"a":1,"e":"Under MoHFW\u0027s NP-NCD / Comprehensive Primary Health Care (CPHC) operational guidelines, community-based (CBAC) and opportunistic screening for adults \u003e=30 years targets five high-burden NCDs: Hypertension (BP check), Diabetes Mellitus (random blood glucose), Oral cancer (oral visual examination), Breast cancer (clinical breast examination), and Cervical cancer (Visual Inspection with Acetic Acid — VIA).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02113","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Drinking Water Chlorination \u0026 Residual Chlorine","q":"During community drinking-water disinfection by chlorination beyond the breakpoint, what minimum free residual chlorine concentration and contact time are recommended to ensure safe potability, and which test differentiates free available chlorine from combined chloramines?","o":["0.01 mg/L free chlorine after 5 seconds; Benedict\u0027s copper reduction test","Minimum 50 mg/L free chlorine after 1 minute contact; Widal agglutination test","Minimum 0.5 mg/L (ppm) free residual chlorine after at least 1 hour (60 min) contact period; Orthotolidine-Arsenite (OTA) test","10 mg/L combined chloramines with zero free chlorine; Schick test"],"a":2,"e":"Effective water chlorination requires adding chlorine past the \u0027breakpoint\u0027 (where ammonia/organic chlorine demand is satisfied) so that free residual chlorine (HOCl/OCl-) persists at \u003e=0.5 mg/L (0.5 ppm) for a minimum 1-hour contact period (higher \u003e=1.0-2.0 mg/L during outbreaks/emergencies). The Orthotolidine-Arsenite (OTA) test (or DPD method) measures rapid yellow colour from free chlorine separately from slower combined chlorine and false nitrite/iron interference.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02114","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Status Epilepticus Pharmacotherapy","q":"A 30-year-old man is brought to the emergency ward in continuous generalised tonic-clonic seizures lasting 12 minutes without regaining consciousness between convulsions (status epilepticus). After securing airway and checking capillary blood glucose, what is the FIRST-LINE emergency drug class and subsequent second-line maintenance anti-seizure option?","o":["Oral carbamazepine 200 mg crushed via nasogastric tube as sole first-line rescue","Subcutaneous epinephrine 1 mg followed by oral ethosuximide","Intravenous haloperidol 10 mg bolus as sole anticonvulsant","Immediate parenteral benzodiazepine (IV lorazepam 4 mg or IV diazepam / IM midazolam) to abort acute seizures, followed by IV levetiracetam, fosphenytoin/phenytoin, or valproate"],"a":3,"e":"In convulsive status epilepticus (\u003e5 minutes), immediate first-line rescue (0-20 min) is a fast-acting parenteral benzodiazepine—IV lorazepam (preferred for longer CNS persistence), IV diazepam, or IM/buccal midazolam if IV access is delayed—to enhance GABA-A inhibition rapidly. This is followed by a second-line non-benzodiazepine loading dose (IV levetiracetam, fosphenytoin/phenytoin, or sodium valproate) to prevent seizure recurrence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02115","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law and Ethics","topic":"Bonded vs Non-Bonded Manufactory","q":"Under the Medicinal and Toilet Preparations (Excise Duties) Act, 1955 and Rules, 1956 (India), what is the key statutory distinction between a \u0027Bonded Manufactory\u0027 and a \u0027Non-Bonded Manufactory\u0027 producing alcoholic medicinal preparations?","o":["In a Bonded Manufactory, rectified spirit is received and stored in bond WITHOUT prior payment of excise duty (duty is paid only upon removal of finished goods from the bonded warehouse); in a Non-Bonded Manufactory, excise duty is paid upfront BEFORE purchasing rectified spirit","Non-Bonded Manufactories are exempt from all Drug Rules and never pay any duty","Bonded Manufactories require no excise officer oversight or bond execution; Non-Bonded Manufactories operate inside State Prisons","In a Bonded Manufactory, only non-alcoholic Ayurvedic churnas are manufactured; Non-Bonded Manufactories export narcotics exclusively"],"a":0,"e":"Under the Medicinal and Toilet Preparations (Excise Duties) Act, 1955: a Bonded Manufactory executes a security bond and operates under Excise Officer supervision/locks, obtaining spirit duty-free into a bonded spirit store and paying duty only when finished preparations leave the bonded warehouse. A Non-Bonded Manufactory obtains rectified spirit only after full advance payment of excise duty, without requiring permanent on-site excise officer locking.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02116","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law and Ethics","topic":"Statutory Definitions — Misbranded, Adulterated \u0026 Spurious Drugs","q":"Under Sections 17, 17A, and 17B of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940 (India), match each illegal drug category to its defining statutory scenario: 1) Misbranded drug (Sec. 17), 2) Adulterated drug (Sec. 17A), 3) Spurious drug (Sec. 17B).","o":["1-Counterfeit imitation of another brand; 2-False/misleading label claim; 3-Decomposed putrid substance","1-Label bears false/misleading claim or is coloured/coated to conceal damage; 2-Consists of filthy/putrid/decomposed substance or packed in a container leaching toxic material; 3-Purports to be a product of a manufacturer of whom it is not truly a product, or bears a fictitious company name/counterfeit imitation","1-Toxic leaching container; 2-Fictitious manufacturer name; 3-Concealed tablet damage by coating","1-Registered generic formulation; 2-Hospital formulary preparation; 3-Pharmacopoeial reference standard"],"a":1,"e":"Under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940: Section 17 defines Misbranded drugs (coloured/coated to hide damage, deceptive labelling, false therapeutic claims); Section 17A defines Adulterated drugs (filthy/putrid/decomposed content, insanitary preparation, poisonous container, or sub-standard admixture); Section 17B defines Spurious drugs (counterfeit/imitation sold under another drug\u0027s/manufacturer\u0027s name or claiming a fictitious manufacturer).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02117","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Emulsion Instability Phenomena","q":"Upon standing, a liquid paraffin oral emulsion separates into two visible layers: in Batch A, gentle shaking immediately restores a uniform white emulsion, whereas in Batch B, droplets have fused into a clear separate oil layer that fails to re-emulsify on shaking. Identify the respective instability phenomena in Batch A and Batch B.","o":["Batch A exhibits reversible creaming (governed by Stokes\u0027 law); Batch B exhibits irreversible cracking (coalescence of the internal phase)","Batch A exhibits irreversible phase inversion; Batch B exhibits reversible flocculation","Batch A exhibits Ostwald ripening; Batch B exhibits reversible sedimentation","Both batches exhibit stable microemulsion equilibrium"],"a":0,"e":"Creaming is the upward (or downward) migration of intact dispersed droplets under gravity/density difference per Stokes\u0027 law without rupture of the emulsifying film; shaking readily redisperses creamed globules. Cracking (breaking) occurs when the protective interfacial film is destroyed (by incompatible electrolytes, extreme temperature, microbial growth, or wrong HLB), causing irreversible coalescence into separate continuous oil and water layers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02118","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Proof Spirit \u0026 Over-Proof Calculation","q":"Under Indian excise and pharmacopoeial alcoholimetry standards, Proof Spirit corresponds to 57.1% v/v ethanol in water (100 degrees proof; multiplier = 1.753). What is the proof strength of 95% v/v rectified spirit expressed in degrees Over Proof (O.P.)?","o":["16.5 degrees Over Proof (O.P.)","37.9 degrees Over Proof (O.P.)","66.5 degrees Over Proof (O.P.)","95.0 degrees Under Proof (U.P.)"],"a":2,"e":"Degrees Proof = (% v/v ethanol x 1.753) - 100. For 95% v/v rectified spirit: (95 x 1.753) - 100 = 166.535 - 100 = +66.535 degrees, i.e. 66.5 degrees Over Proof (O.P.), meaning 100 volumes of 95% v/v spirit yield 166.5 volumes of proof spirit when diluted with water.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02119","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Classification of Ointment Bases","q":"In topical pharmaceutical compounding, match each semisolid ointment base class to its standard representative example: 1) Hydrocarbon (Oleaginous) base, 2) Absorption base, 3) Water-removable (O/W emulsion) base, 4) Water-soluble (greaseless) base.","o":["1-Bentonite magma; 2-Kaolin paste; 3-Zinc oxide paste; 4-Liquid paraffin","1-Macrogol (PEG) ointment; 2-White soft paraffin; 3-Wool fat; 4-Hydrophilic ointment","1-Hydrophilic ointment; 2-PEG 4000; 3-White soft paraffin; 4-Beeswax","1-White soft paraffin (petrolatum); 2-Wool fat (anhydrous lanolin); 3-Hydrophilic ointment (emulsifying ointment + water); 4-Macrogol (PEG 400 + PEG 4000) ointment"],"a":3,"e":"Ointment bases fall into four USP/IP classes: (1) Hydrocarbon/Oleaginous bases (white/yellow soft paraffin, hard paraffin) are occlusive, anhydrous, and non-washable; (2) Absorption bases (anhydrous lanolin/wool fat, hydrophilic petrolatum) absorb aqueous solutions forming W/O emulsions; (3) Water-removable bases (O/W creams/emulsifying ointments) wash off easily with water; (4) Water-soluble bases (Macrogols/PEGs blending liquid PEG 400 and solid PEG 3350/4000) contain no fats and dissolve cleanly in water.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02120","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Limit Test for Heavy Metals (IP)","q":"In the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) limit test for heavy metals (Method A), the test solution and standard lead solution react with freshly prepared hydrogen sulphide (or thioacetamide) reagent to form a brownish colour. At what exact pH buffer is this reaction carried out, and why is that pH critical?","o":["pH 3.5 (dilute acetic acid / ammonium acetate buffer); prevents precipitation of interfering zinc/iron/alkaline-earth hydroxides while ensuring complete precipitation of brown-black heavy metal sulphides (PbS)","pH 1.0 (concentrated HCl); precipitates all calcium and magnesium chlorides","pH 11.0 (concentrated ammonia buffer); converts lead into colourless plumbite ions","pH 7.4 (phosphate buffer); dissolves lead sulphide into a bright red solution"],"a":0,"e":"In the IP limit test for heavy metals, maintaining pH 3.0-4.0 (specifically pH 3.5 using dilute acetic acid/ammonia or acetate buffer) ensures quantitative formation of colloidal brown-black heavy metal sulphides (chiefly PbS) from trace Group II/heavy-metal contaminants while suppressing precipitation of iron, aluminium, or alkaline-earth hydroxides/sulphides that occur at neutral/alkaline pH.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02121","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Non-Aqueous Titration of Weakly Basic Drugs","q":"When assaying ephedrine hydrochloride (a weak organic base formulated as a halide salt) by non-aqueous titration in glacial acetic acid using 0.1 M perchloric acid (HClO4), why MUST mercuric acetate solution be added before titration?","o":["Mercuric acetate converts interfering halide ions (Cl-) into unionised, undissociated mercuric chloride (HgCl2) while liberating free acetate ions that titrate cleanly with perchloric acid","Mercuric acetate oxidises ephedrine into benzoic acid to sharpen the phenolphthalein endpoint","Mercuric acetate supplies extra water to increase dielectric constant above 80","Mercuric acetate precipitates perchloric acid as insoluble mercury perchlorate"],"a":0,"e":"Halide anions (Cl-, Br-, I-) are too weakly basic in glacial acetic acid (and HCl is strongly acidic/interfering), giving indistinct endpoints with perchloric acid. Adding mercuric acetate [Hg(CH3COO)2] quantitatively binds Cl- into non-dissociated HgCl2 and releases stoichiometric acetate ions (the strongest base in glacial acetic acid), allowing sharp titration by 0.1 M HClO4 using crystal violet or potentiometric detection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02122","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Pharmacopoeial Analytical Constants of Fixed Oils","q":"In the quality control of fixed oils such as castor oil, arachis oil, and linseed oil, what chemical property is quantified by the \u0027Iodine Value\u0027 versus the \u0027Saponification Value\u0027?","o":["Iodine Value measures the degree of unsaturation (grams of iodine absorbed by 100 g of oil); Saponification Value measures mg of KOH needed to neutralise free acids and saponify esters in 1 g of oil (inversely proportional to mean fatty acid molecular weight)","Iodine Value measures volatile terpene content; Saponification Value measures moisture content via Karl Fischer","Iodine Value measures free alkali contamination; Saponification Value directly measures double bonds","Both values measure solely acid-insoluble ash content"],"a":0,"e":"Iodine Value (grams of I2 absorbed per 100 g fat/oil via Wijs or Hanus method) quantifies olefinic double bonds (distinguishing non-drying oils \u003c100 like olive/arachis from drying oils \u003e120 like linseed oil). Saponification Value (mg KOH required to completely hydrolyse esters and neutralise free fatty acids in 1 g fat) varies inversely with average fatty-acid chain length (short-chain fats like coconut oil/butter have high saponification values \u003e240).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02123","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Asafoetida (Ferula foetida) Identification","q":"When boiled with hydrochloric acid, filtered, and made alkaline with strong ammonia solution, an alcoholic extract of Asafoetida (Ferula foetida oleo-gum-resin) exhibits a striking bright blue fluorescence under UV/daylight. Which phytoconstituent is liberated to give this positive test?","o":["Sennoside A from anthraquinone dimers","Combined umbelliferone (7-hydroxycoumarin) released from ferulic acid esters","Atropine from tropic acid esters","Eugenol from schizogenous oil glands"],"a":1,"e":"Asafoetida contains ~40-65% resin rich in asaresinotannol esters of ferulic acid. Acid hydrolysis converts ferulic acid/umbelliferone precursors into free umbelliferone (7-hydroxycoumarin), which ionises in ammoniacal alkaline solution to produce vivid blue fluorescence—distinguishing genuine Asafoetida from Colophony or Galbanum adulterants (and giving a red colour on fractured surface with 50% H2SO4/HNO3).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02124","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Oxygen-Haemoglobin Dissociation Curve","q":"During strenuous skeletal muscle exercise, local metabolic changes shift the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve to the RIGHT (Bohr effect), promoting oxygen unloading to active tissues. Which combination of physiological changes shifts the curve to the right?","o":["Decreased PCO2, increased blood pH (alkalosis), hypothermia, and presence of fetal haemoglobin (HbF)","Decreased body temperature and hyperventilation-induced respiratory alkalosis","Increased PCO2, decreased blood pH (acidosis), increased temperature, and increased erythrocyte 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG)","Carbon monoxide (CO) binding and complete absence of 2,3-BPG"],"a":2,"e":"A rightward shift raises P50 (lowering haemoglobin\u0027s affinity for O2 so more O2 is released at a given tissue PO2). Factors driving a rightward shift (CADET face right: CO2, Acid/low pH, 2,3-DPG/BPG, Exercise, Temperature) stabilise the taut (T) deoxyhaemoglobin conformation. Conversely, alkalosis, hypothermia, low CO2, low 2,3-BPG, CO, and HbF shift the curve left.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02125","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Biochemistry and Clinical Pathology","topic":"Differential Diagnosis of Jaundice","q":"A 56-year-old man presents with painless progressive icterus, intense pruritus, dark tea-coloured urine, and pale clay-coloured stools. Laboratory LFTs show: total bilirubin 9.4 mg/dL (direct/conjugated 7.8 mg/dL), alkaline phosphatase (ALP) 680 U/L, elevated GGT, and positive urine bilirubin with absent urine urobilinogen. Which pattern of jaundice do these findings confirm?","o":["Isolated vitamin B12 megaloblastic anaemia","Pre-hepatic haemolytic jaundice","Physiological Gilbert syndrome (isolated defective hepatic UGT conjugation)","Post-hepatic obstructive (cholestatic) jaundice"],"a":3,"e":"Biliary obstruction prevents conjugated (water-soluble direct) bilirubin from entering the intestine, causing pale acholic stools and absent urinary urobilinogen; conjugated bilirubin regurgitates into blood and spills into urine (dark urine positive for bilirubin), while biliary canalicular pressure strongly induces ALP and GGT. By contrast, haemolytic jaundice shows predominantly unconjugated (indirect) hyperbilirubinaemia, normal ALP, absent urine bilirubin, and elevated urobilinogen.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02126","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Rabies Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP)","q":"A 19-year-old student sustains multiple deep transdermal dog-bite lacerations on his calf from an unvaccinated stray dog (WHO/NCDC Category III exposure). Alongside thorough 15-minute soap-and-running-water wound washing and povidone-iodine antisepsis, what is the MANDATORY immediate immunoprophylaxis protocol under India\u0027s National Rabies Control Programme?","o":["Infiltrate Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG / ERIG or HRIG) locally into and around ALL bite wounds PLUS administer anti-rabies vaccine (either ID 2-site 0.1 mL on days 0, 3, 7, 28 or IM 0, 3, 7, 14, 28 at a distant site); avoid tight primary wound suturing","Administer RIG solely into the gluteal muscle 14 days after completing the vaccine series","Give oral ciprofloxacin for 5 days and observe the stray dog without any vaccine or RIG","Administer only a single tetanus toxoid injection and suture the bite tightly immediately"],"a":0,"e":"Category III bites (single/multiple transdermal bites, scratches, or saliva on broken skin/mucosa) require ALL three steps urgently: (1) Immediate 15-min soap-and-water wash + antiseptic; (2) Passive neutralisation by infiltrating calculated ERIG (40 IU/kg) or HRIG (20 IU/kg) as completely as anatomically feasible into/around every wound (never beyond day 7 of first vaccine dose); (3) Active vaccination via updated Thai Red Cross intradermal schedule (0.1 mL at 2 sites on Days 0, 3, 7, 28) or Essen IM schedule (Days 0, 3, 7, 14, 28) in deltoid, never in the gluteal region.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02127","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Vector Identification \u0026 Breeding Habits","q":"During an urban dengue and chikungunya outbreak inspection, health workers eliminate stagnant clean rainwater inside tyres, flower-pot trays, and overhead tanks targeting a day-biting mosquito with white-striped legs (\u0027tiger mosquito\u0027). Which vector species is being targeted?","o":["Phlebotomus argentipes","Aedes aegypti","Anopheles culicifacies","Culex quinquefasciatus"],"a":1,"e":"Aedes aegypti (and Ae. albopictus) are artificial clean-water container breeders that bite predominantly during daytime (early morning and late afternoon) and transmit Dengue, Chikungunya, Zika, and Yellow Fever. By contrast, Anopheles transmits Malaria, Culex quinquefasciatus breeds in polluted drains transmitting Bancroftian lymphatic filariasis/JE, and Phlebotomus argentipes (sandfly) transmits Visceral Leishmaniasis (Kala-azar).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02128","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antithyroid Thioamides (Carbimazole vs Propylthiouracil)","q":"A 26-year-old woman at 7 weeks\u0027 gestation with newly diagnosed Graves\u0027 hyperthyroidism is prescribed propylthiouracil (PTU) for the first trimester (with plan to switch to carbimazole/methimazole in the second trimester). Why is PTU preferred over methimazole/carbimazole specifically during the first trimester and thyroid storm, and what serious adverse reaction requires immediate reporting of sore throat and fever?","o":["PTU avoids methimazole/carbimazole-associated first-trimester embryopathy (aplasia cutis/choanal atresia) and uniquely inhibits peripheral 5\u0027-deiodinase (T4-to-T3 conversion) in thyroid storm; both drugs carry a rare life-threatening risk of agranulocytosis","PTU is preferred throughout all nine months solely because it has zero hepatotoxicity; it causes harmless red urine","Carbimazole directly destroys fetal thyroid tissue in week 1 via radioactive iodine emission","PTU stimulates thyroid peroxidase to raise free T4 levels safely"],"a":0,"e":"Both methimazole/carbimazole and PTU inhibit thyroid peroxidase-mediated iodination and iodotyrosine coupling. Methimazole/carbimazole carries higher risk of first-trimester congenital anomalies (aplasia cutis, tracheo-oesophageal fistula), so PTU is preferred during organogenesis (first trimester) and then switched back to methimazole/carbimazole in the 2nd trimester due to PTU\u0027s severe hepatotoxicity risk. PTU also inhibits peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion (advantageous in thyroid storm). Patients on any thioamide must stop the drug and check WBC count immediately if fever or sore throat develops (agranulocytosis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02129","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Allopurinol-Azathioprine Interaction \u0026 Colchicine","q":"A 51-year-old kidney-transplant recipient maintained on azathioprine for immunosuppression develops hyperuricaemia and gouty tophi, and is prescribed full-dose allopurinol 300 mg/day without azathioprine dose reduction. Three weeks later he develops severe pancytopenia. What enzymatic interaction caused this toxicity, and which drug is instead used acutely to abort gouty inflammation via microtubule inhibition?","o":["Allopurinol induces CYP2C9 causing azathioprine clearance to triple; methotrexate treats acute gouty attacks","Azathioprine converts allopurinol into febuxostat in renal tubules","Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, blocking inactivation of azathioprine\u0027s active metabolite 6-mercaptopurine (6-MP) and causing severe myelosuppression; colchicine inhibits beta-tubulin polymerisation in neutrophils during acute gout","Allopurinol chelates serum calcium; probenecid inhibits microtubule assembly"],"a":2,"e":"Azathioprine is converted to 6-mercaptopurine (6-MP), which is inactivated to 6-thiouric acid by xanthine oxidase. Allopurinol (and febuxostat) potently inhibits xanthine oxidase, causing 6-MP/thioguanine nucleotides to accumulate massively and precipitate life-threatening bone marrow suppression (requiring \u003e=75% azathioprine dose reduction or avoiding combination). For acute gout flares, colchicine binds tubulin to prevent microtubule polymerisation, halting neutrophil chemotaxis and inflammasome activation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02130","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacotherapy of Open-Angle Glaucoma","q":"Latanoprost 0.005% ophthalmic drops (once nightly) are widely prescribed as first-line monotherapy for primary open-angle glaucoma. By what primary mechanism does latanoprost lower intraocular pressure (IOP), and what distinctive local cosmetic side effects should patients be counselled about?","o":["Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor halting aqueous humour secretion completely; causes immediate mydriasis","Muscarinic antagonist relaxing trabecular meshwork; causes permanent acute angle-closure attack","Beta-2 agonist constricting ciliary muscle; causes severe corneal calcium deposits","Prostaglandin F2-alpha analogue increasing uveoscleral aqueous humour outflow; causes gradual darkening of iris pigmentation (melanin increase) and thickening/lengthening of eyelashes"],"a":3,"e":"Prostaglandin F2-alpha analogues (latanoprost, travoprost, bimatoprost) activate FP receptors in the ciliary muscle and extracellular matrix, remodelling collagen via MMPs to markedly increase uveoscleral outflow of aqueous humour with minimal systemic side effects. Patients should be forewarned about gradual irreversible iris browning (especially in hazel/mixed-colour eyes), periocular skin darkening, and hypertrichosis (lengthened/darkened eyelashes).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02131","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy and Management","topic":"Pill-Count Adherence Assessment","q":"A hypertensive patient is dispensed a 30-tablet pack of atenolol 50 mg (instructed to take 1 tablet once daily). On Day 25 following dispensing, the patient returns for follow-up with 10 tablets still remaining in the strip. Using the pill-count method, what is the patient\u0027s medication adherence percentage?","o":["80%","83.3%","66.7%","33.3%"],"a":0,"e":"Tablets actually consumed = Dispensed (30) - Remaining (10) = 20 tablets. Expected tablets over 25 elapsed days (1 tablet/day) = 25 tablets. Adherence (%) = (Tablets taken / Expected tablets) x 100 = (20 / 25) x 100 = 80%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02132","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy and Management","topic":"Acute Drug-Induced Anaphylaxis","q":"Minutes after receiving a parenteral antibiotic, a patient develops acute generalised urticaria, inspiratory stridor, bronchospasm, and blood pressure 72/44 mmHg (anaphylaxis). At an OSCE emergency station, what is the FIRST-LINE drug, concentration, route, and anatomical injection site?","o":["Chlorpheniramine maleate 10 mg orally with a glass of water","Epinephrine (adrenaline) 1:1,000 (1 mg/mL), 0.3-0.5 mg (0.3-0.5 mL) intramuscularly into the anterolateral mid-thigh (vastus lateralis)","Hydrocortisone 100 mg subcutaneously into the forearm as sole initial therapy","Epinephrine 1:1,000, 5.0 mg rapid IV bolus push in a conscious patient"],"a":1,"e":"Intramuscular epinephrine (1:1,000 = 1 mg/mL; adult dose 0.3-0.5 mg = 0.3-0.5 mL, repeated every 5-15 min as needed) injected into the anterolateral mid-thigh (vastus lateralis, yielding faster and higher peak absorption than deltoid or SC routes) is the mandatory first-line treatment for anaphylaxis—reversing alpha-1 vasodilation/oedema and beta-2 bronchospasm while stabilising mast cells. Antihistamines and corticosteroids are secondary adjuncts and NEVER replace prompt IM epinephrine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02133","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Hospital and Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Cytotoxic Drug Reconstitution Engineering Controls","q":"A hospital oncology pharmacy technician prepares intravenous doxorubicin and cisplatin infusions. Why MUST hazardous cytotoxic antineoplastic agents be reconstituted inside a vertical-airflow Class II Type B2 Biological Safety Cabinet (BSC) or negative-pressure isolator rather than a standard horizontal Laminar Airflow Workstation (LAFW)?","o":["Horizontal LAFWs blow HEPA-filtered air outward directly toward the operator\u0027s face exposing staff to toxic cytotoxic aerosols, whereas Class II Type B2 BSCs provide vertical laminar flow, an inward front air-curtain, and 100% HEPA-filtered external exhaust protecting both product sterility and operator","Horizontal LAFWs cannot filter 0.3-micron bacteria even for non-hazardous TPN","Class II Type B2 BSCs heat cytotoxic vials to 121 C during compounding","Horizontal LAFWs automatically neutralise cisplatin DNA alkylation inside the syringe"],"a":0,"e":"Standard horizontal LAFWs maintain ISO Class 5 product sterility for non-hazardous IV admixtures by blowing sterile air horizontally across the work surface out the front—which would propel mutagenic/teratogenic chemotherapy aerosols directly onto the compounder. Hazardous cytotoxic preparations require negative-pressure Containment Aseptic Compounding Isolators or Class II Type B2 BSCs (100% total exhaust through HEPA filters outside the building) to simultaneously ensure product sterility and personnel/environmental safety.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02134","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Bronchial Asthma Stepwise Management (GINA)","q":"A 22-year-old college student with mild intermittent/persistent asthma (daytime wheeze twice weekly) currently uses only an as-needed salbutamol (SABA) inhaler. According to modern GINA asthma pharmacotherapy guidelines, why is SABA-only monotherapy no longer recommended, and what is the preferred Track 1 Step 1-2 regimen?","o":["SABA-only therapy relieves bronchoconstriction without treating underlying eosinophilic airway inflammation (increasing exacerbation/mortality risk); as-needed low-dose ICS-formoterol (e.g., budesonide-formoterol) provides both rapid bronchodilation and anti-inflammatory steroid coverage","SABA-only therapy cures airway remodelling permanently within 2 weeks; switch to oral theophylline monotherapy","SABA-only therapy causes fatal bradycardia; replace with oral prednisolone 40 mg daily lifelong","Switch immediately to inhaled tiotropium monotherapy without corticosteroids"],"a":0,"e":"GINA guidelines advise against SABA-only treatment in adults/adolescents because isolated beta-2 agonism masks progressive eosinophilic airway inflammation, promotes beta-receptor downregulation, and leaves patients vulnerable to severe exacerbations. Low-dose budesonide-formoterol (ICS + fast-onset, long-acting LABA formoterol) used as-needed delivers immediate bronchodilation (~1-3 min onset via formoterol) paired with anti-inflammatory ICS, slashing severe exacerbations by \u003e60% compared with SABA alone.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02135","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Uncomplicated Acute Cystitis (ICMR STG)","q":"A 29-year-old non-pregnant woman presents with dysuria, urinary frequency, and suprapubic discomfort without fever or flank pain (uncomplicated acute cystitis). Per ICMR/NHSRC Standard Treatment Guidelines, oral nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily for 5 days (or fosfomycin) is preferred over fluoroquinolones. In which clinical situation is nitrofurantoin INEFFECTIVE/CONTRAINDICATED?","o":["Patients allergic to oral penicillin","Second trimester pregnancy (non-G6PD-deficient) with asymptomatic bacteriuria","Acute pyelonephritis (due to poor renal parenchymal/systemic tissue concentrations) OR severe renal impairment with CrCl \u003c 30 mL/min (inadequate urinary drug concentration and increased neuro/pulmonary toxicity)","Isolated uncomplicated E. coli lower cystitis with eGFR 95 mL/min/1.73 m2"],"a":2,"e":"Nitrofurantoin achieves high bactericidal concentrations exclusively in the lower urinary tract (bladder urine) while maintaining negligible serum/renal-parenchymal levels—so it MUST NOT be used for pyelonephritis or urosepsis. Furthermore, when CrCl falls below 30 mL/min, urinary excretion drops below therapeutic MIC while systemic accumulation increases risk of peripheral neuropathy and pulmonary fibrosis (also avoid near term \u003e=38 weeks pregnancy or G6PD deficiency).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02136","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law and Ethics","topic":"CCSEA (formerly CPCSEA) \u0026 Animal Ethics Regulations","q":"In India, preclinical pharmacological and toxicological experimentation on laboratory animals is regulated under the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act, 1960 by which statutory national committee (requiring Institutional Animal Ethics Committee [IAEC] protocol approval and adherence to Replacement, Reduction, and Refinement)?","o":["Pharmacy Council of India (PCI)","National Medical Commission (NMC)","Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI)","Committee for Control and Supervision of Experiments on Animals (CCSEA, formerly CPCSEA)"],"a":3,"e":"Constituted under Section 15 of the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act, 1960, CCSEA (Committee for Control and Supervision of Experiments on Animals, formerly CPCSEA) registers animal facilities, mandates Institutional Animal Ethics Committees (IAECs) with CCSEA nominees to review small-animal protocols, directly approves large-animal studies, and enforces Russell and Burch\u0027s 3Rs (Replacement, Reduction, Refinement).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02137","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Size Reduction Equipment","q":"A ball mill is used in a pharmaceutical manufacturing unit for fine grinding of a brittle crystalline drug. If the mill operates above its critical rotational speed, which phenomenon occurs and how does it affect size reduction?","o":["Balls remain centrifuged against the inner wall of the cylinder, resulting in negligible size reduction","Feed material melts due to excessive impact without hitting the mill wall","Balls accumulate at the bottom of the cylinder and slide without impact","Balls are carried to the top and fall in a cascade, maximizing attrition"],"a":0,"e":"A ball mill achieves size reduction by a combination of impact and attrition as the balls are lifted up the rotating cylinder wall and fall onto the material. At or above the critical speed, centrifugal force equals or exceeds gravitational force, causing the balls to cling to the inner wall of the cylinder and rotate with it without cascading, so practically no grinding occurs. Optimal grinding is typically achieved at 60% to 80% of the critical speed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02138","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Quality Control Tests for Tablets","q":"According to the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP), what is the standard disintegration test limit for plain uncoated tablets and sugar-coated tablets, respectively, at 37 ± 2 °C in water?","o":["5 minutes for uncoated tablets and 60 minutes for sugar-coated tablets","15 minutes for uncoated tablets and 60 minutes for sugar-coated tablets","30 minutes for uncoated tablets and 45 minutes for sugar-coated tablets","15 minutes for uncoated tablets and 30 minutes for sugar-coated tablets"],"a":1,"e":"Under the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) disintegration test for tablets and capsules, plain uncoated tablets must disintegrate within 15 minutes, film-coated tablets within 30 minutes, and sugar-coated tablets within 60 minutes when tested in water (or specified medium) at 37 ± 2 °C using the basket-rack assembly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02139","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Sterilization Techniques","q":"A batch of aqueous isotonic dextrose intravenous infusion is sterilized by saturated steam under pressure at 121 °C (15 psi) for 15 minutes. Which microorganism\u0027s spores serve as the official biological indicator to validate this moist heat sterilization cycle?","o":["Clostridium sporogenes","Bacillus pumilus","Geobacillus stearothermophilus","Bacillus atrophaeus"],"a":2,"e":"Moist heat sterilization in an autoclave (commonly 121 °C at 15 psi for 15-20 minutes) destroys microorganisms by coagulating and denaturing essential cellular proteins. Spores of Geobacillus stearothermophilus (formerly Bacillus stearothermophilus) possess high thermal resistance to saturated steam (D121 value around 1.5-2.0 minutes) and are the standard biological indicator for validating moist heat sterilization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02140","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Limit Test for Iron (IP)","q":"In the Indian Pharmacopoeia limit test for iron, citric acid is added to the test and standard solutions prior to making the solution alkaline with ammonia and adding thioglycollic acid. What is the specific chemical purpose of adding citric acid?","o":["To mask chloride and sulfate anions present in the sample","To oxidize ferrous ions to ferric ions so the purple complex forms faster","To reduce thioglycollic acid into dithiodiglycollic acid","To form a soluble complex with iron and prevent precipitation of ferric hydroxide in ammoniacal medium"],"a":3,"e":"In the IP limit test for iron, thioglycollic acid reduces ferric (Fe3+) to ferrous (Fe2+) ions and reacts with Fe2+ in an ammoniacal medium to yield a pale pink to deep reddish-purple ferrous thioglycollate complex. Because iron precipitates as insoluble ferric or ferrous hydroxide upon addition of ammonia, iron-free citric acid is added first to chelate iron as a soluble citrate complex, keeping it in solution at alkaline pH.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02141","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Non-Aqueous Titrations","q":"During the non-aqueous assay of ephedrine hydrochloride using standardized perchloric acid in glacial acetic acid, mercuric acetate is added to the reaction flask before titration. Why is mercuric acetate required?","o":["It converts the weakly basic chloride ion into unionized mercuric chloride so it does not interfere with perchloric acid titration","It increases the dielectric constant of glacial acetic acid to promote aqueous ionization","It neutralizes excess acetic anhydride present as a dehydrating agent","It acts as a visual redox indicator at the endpoint"],"a":0,"e":"Hydrochloride salts of weak bases (such as ephedrine HCl) cannot be titrated directly with perchloric acid in glacial acetic acid because the displaced chloride ion (Cl-) acts as a competing base and HCl formed interferes with the sharpness of the endpoint. Adding mercuric acetate converts the halide ions into unionized, undissociated mercuric chloride (HgCl2) while liberating equivalent acetate ions (CH3COO-), which behave as strong bases in glacial acetic acid and are quantitatively titrated with perchloric acid.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02142","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Antitubercular Drugs","q":"Isoniazid, a first-line antitubercular drug, belongs chemically to which heterocyclic carboxylic acid hydrazide class?","o":["Pyrazine-2-carboxamide","Isonicotinic acid hydrazide (pyridine-4-carboxylic acid hydrazide)","2-Aminobenzimidazole hydrazide","Nicotinic acid hydrazide (pyridine-3-carboxylic acid hydrazide)"],"a":1,"e":"Isoniazid (INH) is chemically isonicotinic acid hydrazide, consisting of a pyridine ring substituted at the 4-position with a carbohydrazide (-CONHNH2) group. It is a prodrug activated by mycobacterial catalase-peroxidase (KatG) to form an adduct with NAD that inhibits InhA, blocking mycolic acid cell wall synthesis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02143","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Alkaloid Identification Tests","q":"An aqueous extract of tea leaves gives a negative precipitate with Mayer\u0027s and Dragendorff\u0027s reagents, yet produces a distinct purple coloration when evaporated with potassium chlorate and hydrochloric acid followed by exposure to ammonia vapor. Which constituent and chemical test explain this observation?","o":["Morphine giving a positive Marquis test","Quinine giving a positive Thalleioquin test","Caffeine giving a positive Murexide test","Atropine giving a positive Vitali-Morin test"],"a":2,"e":"Caffeine is a purine (pseudoalkaloid) derived from xanthine and typically fails to yield precipitates with general alkaloidal reagents like Mayer\u0027s (potassium mercuric iodide) or Dragendorff\u0027s reagent. Instead, purine alkaloids are identified by the Murexide test: heating with KClO3 and concentrated HCl oxidizes caffeine to tetramethylalloxantin, which turns purple (murexide) upon exposure to ammonia vapor.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02144","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Anthraquinone Glycosides","q":"Why does Aloe (aloin) fail to respond to the standard Borntrager\u0027s test unless first treated with ferric chloride and dilute hydrochloric acid (modified Borntrager\u0027s test), whereas Senna responds directly to the standard Borntrager\u0027s test?","o":["Aloin is a cyanogenetic glycoside requiring enzymatic hydrolysis by emulsin","Aloin contains only free anthranols without any sugar moiety","Senna contains saponin aglycones that directly dissolve in ammonia without extraction","Aloin is a C-glycoside resistant to dilute acid hydrolysis alone, whereas sennosides are O-glycosides"],"a":3,"e":"Sennosides A and B in Senna (Cassia angustifolia) are dianthrone O-glycosides whose glycosidic C-O bonds are readily cleaved by boiling with dilute mineral acid in the standard Borntrager\u0027s test, releasing free anthraquinones that turn rose-pink/red in ammonia. In contrast, barbaloin (aloin) in Aloe is a C-glycoside (glucose attached directly to the carbon atom at C-10) that resists simple dilute acid hydrolysis and requires oxidative cleavage with FeCl3 and HCl (modified Borntrager\u0027s test) to release aloe-emodin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02145","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy \u0026 Physiology","topic":"Cardiac Conduction System and ECG","q":"In the normal human heart, what is the primary physiological purpose of the ~0.1-second conduction delay at the atrioventricular (AV) node?","o":["To allow complete atrial contraction and ventricular filling before ventricular systole begins","To initiate simultaneous contraction of both the atria and the ventricles","To prolong ventricular repolarization and prevent T-wave inversion","To allow aortic and pulmonary semilunar valves to close before atrial systole"],"a":0,"e":"The AV node conducts electrical impulses slowly (approximately 0.05 m/s) because of smaller fiber diameter and fewer gap junctions, creating a physiological delay of about 0.1 seconds (reflected in the PR interval on an ECG). This delay ensures that the atria finish contracting and empty blood into the ventricles before the ventricles begin contracting.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02146","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy \u0026 Physiology","topic":"Urine Formation Physiology","q":"Which tubular segment establishes the hyperosmotic renal medullary interstitium by actively transporting Na+, K+, and 2Cl- ions out of the filtrate while remaining impermeable to water?","o":["Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle","Proximal convoluted tubule","Thin descending limb of the loop of Henle","Collecting duct in the presence of ADH"],"a":0,"e":"The thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle is known as the diluting segment because it actively reabsorbs Na+, K+, and 2Cl- via the luminal NKCC2 symporter while lacking aquaporins and remaining impermeable to water. This active solute extrusion into the renal medulla without water reabsorption generates the osmotic gradient essential for the countercurrent multiplier system.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02147","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"National Immunization Schedule (UIP India)","q":"Under India\u0027s Universal Immunization Programme (UIP), which combination of vaccines is recommended for a newborn within 24 hours of institutional delivery?","o":["BCG, Pentavalent-1, and Fractional Inactivated Polio Vaccine (fIPV-1)","BCG, Oral Polio Vaccine zero dose (OPV-0), and Hepatitis B birth dose","Pentavalent-1, Rotavirus-1, and Measles-Rubella (MR-1)","OPV-0, DPT-1, and Japanese Encephalitis-1"],"a":1,"e":"Under India\u0027s Universal Immunization Programme (MoHFW), a newborn at birth (ideally within 24 hours for Hepatitis B) receives BCG (intradermal, up to 1 year if missed), OPV-0 (oral, within first 15 days), and Hepatitis B birth dose (intramuscular, within 24 hours of birth) to protect against tuberculosis, poliomyelitis, and perinatal hepatitis B transmission.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02148","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Contraceptive Efficacy Metrics","q":"In a family planning clinic study evaluating a barrier contraceptive, 100 couples used the method consistently for 24 months (total 2,400 woman-months of exposure), during which 6 unintended pregnancies occurred. What is the Pearl Index (per 100 woman-years)?","o":["6.0 per 100 woman-years","12.0 per 100 woman-years","3.0 per 100 woman-years","1.5 per 100 woman-years"],"a":2,"e":"The Pearl Index measures contraceptive failure rate per 100 woman-years (1,200 woman-months) using the formula: Pearl Index = (Number of accidental pregnancies × 1,200) / Total months of exposure. Substituting the values: (6 × 1,200) / 2,400 = 7,200 / 2,400 = 3.0 pregnancies per 100 woman-years.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02149","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Anticholinesterase Poisoning","q":"A 32-year-old agricultural worker is brought to the emergency department 90 minutes after accidental ingestion of parathion, presenting with pinpoint pupils, profuse salivation, bronchorrhea, and muscle fasciculations. Alongside atropine, intravenous pralidoxime (2-PAM) is administered. What is the primary mechanism of pralidoxime in this setting?","o":["It irreversibly chelates parathion in the gastrointestinal lumen to prevent systemic absorption","It competitively antagonizes muscarinic M3 receptors in bronchial smooth muscle","It directly blocks nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction without affecting acetylcholinesterase","It reactivates phosphorylated acetylcholinesterase by removing the organophosphate moiety before enzyme aging occurs"],"a":3,"e":"Organophosphates like parathion (converted to paraoxon) phosphorylate the serine hydroxyl group at the esteratic site of acetylcholinesterase (AChE). Pralidoxime (2-PAM), an oxime, binds to the anionic site of AChE and nucleophilically attacks the phosphorus atom, cleaving the phosphate-enzyme bond and restoring AChE activity—provided administration occurs before \u0027aging\u0027 (dealkylation) renders the bond irreversible. Because pralidoxime restores neuromuscular junction function (relieving fasciculations and respiratory muscle paralysis) where atropine has no effect, both drugs are used together.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02150","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Renin-Angiotensin System Inhibitors","q":"A hypertensive patient started on ramipril develops a persistent, non-productive dry cough that resolves only after switching to losartan. Which mediator accumulation is primarily responsible for ACE inhibitor-induced dry cough?","o":["Bradykinin and substance P","Norepinephrine","Angiotensin II","Aldosterone"],"a":0,"e":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) is identical to kininase II, the enzyme responsible for degrading bradykinin into inactive peptides. Inhibition of ACE elevates local bradykinin and substance P concentrations in the bronchial mucosa, sensitizing C-fiber airway sensory nerves and triggering a persistent dry cough (and rarely angioedema). Angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs) such as losartan block AT1 receptors without inhibiting ACE/kininase II, so bradykinin does not accumulate.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02151","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","q":"Why are aminoglycoside antibiotics such as gentamicin and amikacin clinically ineffective against obligate anaerobic bacteria like Bacteroides fragilis?","o":["Anaerobic bacteria lack 30S ribosomal subunits required for aminoglycoside binding","Transport of aminoglycosides across the bacterial inner membrane is an oxygen-dependent active process","Aminoglycosides are rapidly hydrolyzed by anaerobic beta-lactamases","Aminoglycosides require low intracellular pH found only in aerobic organisms"],"a":1,"e":"Entry of polar, polycationic aminoglycosides across the bacterial cytoplasmic membrane requires an electron-transport-chain-driven proton motive force that depends on oxygen (energy-dependent phase I). Because obligate anaerobes lack oxidative phosphorylation, they cannot actively transport aminoglycosides intracellularly to reach the 30S ribosomal subunit, conferring intrinsic resistance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02152","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Parts of a Prescription and Latin Abbreviations","q":"On a prescription, the Signatura reads: \u00271 tab p.o. b.i.d. p.c. × 7d\u0027. What instructions should the community pharmacist write on the dispensing label for the patient?","o":["Take 1 tablet by mouth once daily at bedtime for 7 days","Take 1 tablet by mouth every 4 hours as needed for 7 days","Take 1 tablet by mouth twice daily after meals for 7 days","Take 1 tablet by mouth twice daily before meals for 7 days"],"a":2,"e":"In standard prescription terminology: \u0027p.o.\u0027 (per os) means by mouth/orally; \u0027b.i.d.\u0027 (bis in die) means twice daily; and \u0027p.c.\u0027 (post cibum) means after meals. Therefore, the patient should be instructed to take 1 tablet orally twice daily after meals for 7 days.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02153","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Inventory Control Techniques","q":"A hospital pharmacy applies combined ABC-VED matrix analysis to its annual drug inventory. Which composite category includes high-annual-consumption-value drugs that are also clinically Vital (life-saving, zero stock-out tolerance), requiring direct top-management monitoring and minimal safety buffer delays?","o":["Category III (CD)","Category II (BE, BD, CE)","Category IV (BD, CD)","Category I (AV, AE, AD, BV, CV)"],"a":3,"e":"In ABC-VED coupling, ABC classifies items by annual usage value (A = ~70% value, B = ~20%, C = ~10%) while VED classifies by clinical criticality (Vital, Essential, Desirable). Category I encompasses all A-items and all V-items (AV, AE, AD, BV, CV)—among which AV items represent the highest cost and life-saving criticality requiring strict oversight, tight EOQ/ROP scheduling, and zero stock-outs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02154","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Inhaler Technique Counselling","q":"A 40-year-old woman with persistent asthma is prescribed a budesonide pressurized metered-dose inhaler (pMDI) twice daily. Which post-inhalation instruction is essential during patient counselling to minimize the risk of oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia?","o":["Rinse the mouth and gargle with water and spit it out immediately after each inhalation","Actuate two puffs rapidly during a single shallow breath without shaking","Hold the breath for 2 seconds and immediately drink hot milk","Store the MDI canister in the freezer between doses"],"a":0,"e":"A substantial fraction of inhaled corticosteroid emitted from a pMDI deposits in the oropharynx, causing local immunosuppression that predisposes to oral thrush (candidiasis) and hoarseness (dysphonia). Rinsing the mouth and gargling with water—and spitting it out rather than swallowing—after each dose removes residual steroid and dramatically reduces these local adverse effects. Using a spacer also lowers oropharyngeal deposition.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02155","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Biochemistry \u0026 Clinical Pathology","topic":"Glycemic Biomarkers","q":"A patient\u0027s HbA1c value is reported as 7.8%. Which biochemical principle explains why HbA1c reflects average blood glucose control over the preceding 8 to 12 weeks?","o":["Renal tubular reabsorption of glycosylated albumin occurs over a 90-day half-life","Non-enzymatic glycation of the N-terminal valine of hemoglobin beta-chains persists for the ~120-day lifespan of erythrocytes","Insulin-dependent hexokinase irreversibly phosphorylates hemoglobin proportionally to fasting plasma glucose","Hepatic glucokinase synthesizes HbA1c exclusively during postprandial hyperglycemia"],"a":1,"e":"HbA1c is formed by slow, non-enzymatic attachment of glucose to the N-terminal valine residue of the beta-globin chains of hemoglobin A (forming an unstable Schiff base that undergoes Amadori rearrangement to a stable ketoamine). Because circulating red blood cells have an average lifespan of ~120 days, the fraction of HbA1c reflects integrated mean blood glucose over the preceding 2 to 3 months.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02156","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Biochemistry \u0026 Clinical Pathology","topic":"Liver Function Tests (LFT)","q":"A 48-year-old woman presents with pruritus, clay-colored stools, and dark urine. Laboratory tests show total serum bilirubin 9.4 mg/dL, direct (conjugated) bilirubin 7.6 mg/dL, markedly elevated serum alkaline phosphatase (ALP), and positive urine bilirubin with absent urine urobilinogen. Which type of jaundice is most consistent with these findings?","o":["Gilbert syndrome","Physiological neonatal jaundice","Obstructive (post-hepatic / cholestatic) jaundice","Hemolytic (pre-hepatic) jaundice"],"a":2,"e":"Extrahepatic or intrahepatic biliary obstruction prevents conjugated bilirubin from entering the intestine, causing regurgitation of water-soluble conjugated (direct) bilirubin into the blood and excretion into urine (dark urine). Lack of intestinal bilirubin prevents urobilinogen and stercobilinogen formation, resulting in clay-colored (acholic) stools and absent urine urobilinogen, alongside marked ALP elevation due to biliary epithelial induction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02157","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Tuberculosis Management (NTEP India)","q":"A 35-year-old man newly diagnosed with drug-sensitive pulmonary tuberculosis is initiated on the NTEP daily fixed-dose combination (HRZE) along with oral pyridoxine 10–25 mg daily. Pyridoxine is co-prescribed primarily to prevent which drug-related toxicity?","o":["Pyrazinamide-induced hyperuricemia","Ethambutol-induced retrobulbar optic neuritis","Rifampicin-induced red-orange discoloration of body fluids","Isoniazid-induced peripheral neuropathy"],"a":3,"e":"Isoniazid inhibits pyridoxine phosphokinase and forms hydrazone complexes with pyridoxal phosphate, increasing renal excretion of vitamin B6 and depleting active pyridoxal-5\u0027-phosphate required for GABA and neurotransmitter synthesis. Supplementation with pyridoxine prevents or treats isoniazid-induced peripheral neuropathy (paresthesias in hands and feet), especially in malnourished, diabetic, pregnant, or HIV-infected patients.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02158","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Acute Severe Bronchial Asthma","q":"A 26-year-old man with known asthma arrives in the emergency room with severe dyspnea, inability to speak in full sentences, respiratory rate 30/min, SpO2 89% on room air, and silent chest on auscultation. Which initial pharmacotherapy regimen is most appropriate alongside controlled supplemental oxygen?","o":["Nebulized salbutamol plus ipratropium bromide and systemic corticosteroids (hydrocortisone/prednisolone)","Oral theophylline loading dose alone without bronchodilators","Propranolol intravenous infusion to reduce tachycardia","Oral montelukast monotherapy and inhaled salmeterol"],"a":0,"e":"In acute severe asthma exacerbation, first-line emergency pharmacotherapy combines controlled oxygen (target SpO2 93-95%), repeated/continuous nebulized short-acting beta-2 agonist (salbutamol) plus a short-acting muscarinic antagonist (ipratropium bromide) for additive bronchodilation, and early systemic corticosteroids (oral prednisolone or IV hydrocortisone) to resolve airway inflammation and reduce hospitalization/relapse.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02159","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Iron Deficiency Anemia","q":"A pregnant woman prescribed oral ferrous sulfate tablets for iron deficiency anemia asks how to maximize iron absorption. Which dietary or co-administration advice is pharmacologically sound?","o":["Crush the enteric-coated iron tablet in hot black tea to improve dissolution","Take the tablet with orange juice or ascorbic acid (vitamin C) and separate it by at least 2 hours from calcium supplements or tea","Take the tablet together with milk and an aluminum-magnesium antacid to enhance duodenal solubility","Take the tablet simultaneously with ciprofloxacin to increase chelation and absorption"],"a":1,"e":"Dietary and supplemental iron is absorbed primarily in the duodenum and proximal jejunum in the ferrous (Fe2+) state. Ascorbic acid (vitamin C) enhances oral iron absorption by reducing ferric iron (Fe3+) to the more soluble ferrous state and forming a soluble iron-ascorbate chelate at duodenal pH. Conversely, calcium, antacids (raising gastric pH), tannins in tea/coffee, and phytates inhibit iron absorption and should be separated by at least 2 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02160","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law \u0026 Ethics","topic":"Schedules to the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules","q":"Under the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 (India), which Schedule mandates maintaining a separate register recording the name and address of the prescriber and patient along with drug quantity supplied, preserved for 3 years, specifically introduced to curb indiscriminate sale of third/fourth-generation antibiotics and anti-TB drugs?","o":["Schedule G","Schedule K","Schedule H1","Schedule X"],"a":2,"e":"Schedule H1 was inserted into the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 (notified in 2013, effective 2014) to regulate third- and fourth-generation antibiotics, certain habit-forming drugs, and anti-tuberculosis drugs. Its packaging bears a prominent red-bordered \u0027Rx\u0027 warning box, and retail pharmacists must maintain a separate Schedule H1 register recording prescriber name, patient name, drug name, and quantity dispensed, retained for 3 years for Drug Inspector inspection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02161","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law \u0026 Ethics","topic":"Statutory Schedules for Facilities and Standards","q":"Under the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945, which Schedule specifies the list of minimum equipment and operational requirements for the efficient running of a retail pharmacy?","o":["Schedule M","Schedule P","Schedule C","Schedule N"],"a":3,"e":"Schedule N of the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 prescribes the minimum equipment, premises standards, dispensing area specifications, and books/apparatus required for the efficient running of a pharmacy. Adherence to Schedule N is mandatory for licensing and operating a retail/dispensing pharmacy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02162","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Functional Roles of Tablet Excipients","q":"In compressed tablet formulation, match each excipient to its primary functional role: 1) Microcrystalline cellulose (Avicel), 2) Croscarmellose sodium, 3) Magnesium stearate, 4) Colloidal silicon dioxide (Aerosil).","o":["1-Direct-compression diluent/dry binder; 2-Superdisintegrant; 3-Hydrophobic die-wall lubricant (excessive blending delays dissolution); 4-Glidant reducing interparticulate friction to improve hopper flow","1-Hydrophobic lubricant; 2-Direct-compression diluent; 3-Antioxidant; 4-Chelating agent","1-Superdisintegrant; 2-Hydrophobic lubricant; 3-Glidant; 4-Film-coating plasticiser","1-Antimicrobial preservative; 2-Sweetener; 3-Superdisintegrant; 4-Enteric polymer"],"a":0,"e":"Each tablet excipient serves a distinct manufacturing function: Microcrystalline cellulose (MCC) acts as a plastically deforming direct-compression diluent and dry binder; Croscarmellose sodium (cross-linked CMC) swells rapidly as a superdisintegrant; Magnesium stearate lubricates die walls and punch tips (must be blended briefly at 0.25-1% because hydrophobic coating impairs wetting and tablet hardness); Colloidal silicon dioxide (Aerosil) coats granule surfaces as a glidant enhancing powder flow.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02163","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Infant Paediatric Dose Estimation","q":"The standard adult dose of an oral medication is 150 mg. Using Fried\u0027s rule (specifically designed for infants under 2 years of age based on age in months), what is the calculated dose for an 8-month-old infant?","o":["12 mg","8 mg","15 mg","4 mg"],"a":1,"e":"Fried\u0027s rule estimates infant dose (under 24 months): Infant Dose = [Age in months / 150] x Adult Dose. Substituting Age = 8 months and Adult Dose = 150 mg: (8 / 150) x 150 mg = 8 mg.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02164","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Wet Granulation vs Dry Granulation (Slugging / Roller Compaction)","q":"Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) hydrolyses rapidly in contact with water into salicylic and acetic acids, and effervescent vitamin C granules react prematurely if exposed to aqueous binders and oven drying. Which granulation process is specifically chosen to produce free-flowing granules WITHOUT liquids or heat?","o":["Aqueous wet granulation with 10% starch paste and tray drying at 70 C","Hot-steam fluidised-bed granulation at 90 C","Dry granulation (slugging on heavy-duty rotary presses or roller compaction via Chilsonator followed by milling and screening)","Spray congealing in boiling water"],"a":2,"e":"Dry granulation compacts cohesive dry powder blends under high pressure into large flat slugs (slugging) or dense ribbons between counter-rotating rollers (roller compaction / Chilsonator), then mills and screens them into uniform free-flowing granules—completely eliminating aqueous/alcoholic solvents and thermal drying steps required in wet granulation, protecting moisture- and heat-sensitive drugs such as aspirin, effervescent salts, and beta-lactam antibiotics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02165","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Systemic vs Non-Systemic Antacids","q":"Why are commercial antacid suspensions commonly formulated as a fixed combination of aluminium hydroxide and magnesium hydroxide (often with simethicone) rather than sodium bicarbonate alone?","o":["Aluminium hydroxide lowers gastric pH to 0.5 while magnesium hydroxide stops all intestinal peristalsis","Simethicone neutralises 100 mEq of HCl directly via rapid bicarbonate liberation","Sodium bicarbonate permanently heals duodenal ulcers in 1 dose without systemic absorption","Non-systemic Al(OH)3 (slow-acting, constipating) and Mg(OH)2 (fast-acting, osmotic laxative) balance bowel motility without causing systemic metabolic alkalosis, Na+ overload, or CO2 rebound acid secretion seen with sodium bicarbonate, while simethicone breaks gas bubbles"],"a":3,"e":"Sodium bicarbonate is a systemic antacid: excess soluble Na+ and HCO3- are absorbed causing metabolic alkalosis, fluid retention/hypertension, and CO2 gastric distension with acid rebound. Combining poorly absorbed non-systemic antacids—magnesium hydroxide (rapid acid neutralisation, osmotic diarrhoea) and aluminium hydroxide (sustained buffering, astringent constipation)—yields prompt, prolonged buffering with balanced GI motility, while silicone polymer simethicone lowers surface tension of gas bubbles to relieve flatulence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02166","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Complexometric Titration (IP Assay of MgSO4 / Ca-Gluconate)","q":"In the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) complexometric assay of Magnesium Sulphate using 0.05 M disodium edetate (EDTA) and Mordant Black II (Eriochrome Black T) indicator, what buffer system maintains pH 10, and what colour change signals the endpoint?","o":["Ammonia-ammonium chloride buffer (pH 10); indicator shifts from wine-red (Mg-indicator complex) to pure sky-blue (free indicator) once EDTA chelates all Mg2+","0.1 M NaOH alone (pH 13); indicator shifts from yellow to dark brown precipitate","Glacial acetic acid-perchloric acid buffer (pH 2); indicator shifts from colourless to bright pink","Phosphate buffer (pH 7.0); indicator shifts from blue to brick-red cuprous oxide"],"a":0,"e":"Complexometric titration of Mg2+ with hexadentate EDTA requires alkaline pH ~10 (ammonia-ammonium chloride buffer) so EDTA exists predominantly in its chelating tetra-anionic/tri-anionic forms and Eriochrome Black T (Mordant Black II) binds Mg2+ cleanly to give a wine-red complex. At the equivalence point, stronger EDTA displaces the indicator from Mg2+, liberating free uncomplexed indicator which turns pure sky-blue.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02167","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Botanical Source, Family \u0026 Principal Phytoconstituent","q":"Match each important medicinal plant drug to its botanical family and hallmark alkaloid/diterpenoid: 1) Cinchona bark (Cinchona calisaya), 2) Opium latex (Papaver somniferum), 3) Madagascar periwinkle (Catharanthus roseus), 4) Pacific yew bark (Taxus brevifolia).","o":["1-Papaveraceae (morphine); 2-Rubiaceae (quinine); 3-Taxaceae (paclitaxel); 4-Apocynaceae (vincristine)","1-Rubiaceae (quinine/quinidine); 2-Papaveraceae (morphine/codeine); 3-Apocynaceae (vincristine/vinblastine); 4-Taxaceae (paclitaxel)","1-Apocynaceae (reserpine); 2-Zingiberaceae (curcumin); 3-Rubiaceae (emetine); 4-Loganiaceae (strychnine)","1-Solanaceae (atropine); 2-Lamiaceae (menthol); 3-Apiaceae (fennel); 4-Fabaceae (senna)"],"a":1,"e":"Pharmacognosy botanical classification: Cinchona spp. (Rubiaceae) yield quinoline alkaloids quinine and quinidine; Papaver somniferum (Papaveraceae) yields benzylisoquinoline/phenanthrene alkaloids morphine, codeine, and papaverine; Catharanthus roseus (Apocynaceae) yields dimeric indole alkaloids vincristine and vinblastine; Taxus brevifolia (Taxaceae) yields the pseudo-alkaloid diterpenoid paclitaxel.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02168","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Acacia vs Tragacanth Pharmaceutical Incompatibilities","q":"When compounding emulsions or suspensions using natural plant gums, why can unheated Acacia (Gum Arabic) mucilage discolour or degrade easily oxidisable drugs (such as adrenaline, phenols, or vitamin C), and how does Tragacanth differ in aqueous behaviour?","o":["Acacia is a pure synthetic hydrocarbon polymer; Tragacanth contains cyanogenic glycosides","Acacia is completely insoluble in cold water; Tragacanth is a strong proteolytic enzyme","Acacia naturally contains oxidase/peroxidase enzymes (inactivated by brief heating at 100 C) that oxidise phenolic/labile drugs; Tragacanth consists of water-soluble tragacanthin plus water-insoluble bassorin that swells into a thick thixotropic gel rather than forming a true clear solution","Acacia lowers pH to 1.0 hydrolysing esters; Tragacanth liberates free metallic mercury"],"a":2,"e":"Acacia (dried gummy exudate from Acacia senegal, Fabaceae) dissolves completely in cold water (~1:2) to yield low-viscosity solutions, but raw acacia contains active oxidase/peroxidase enzymes causing brown/pink oxidation of phenols, morphine, epinephrine, and tannins unless heat-inactivated (e.g., 100 C for 30 min). Tragacanth (Astragalus gummifer) contains ~30% soluble tragacanthin and ~60-70% bassorin (which swells into high-viscosity suspending mucilage without dissolving completely).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02169","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Vitamin K-Dependent Coagulation Factors","q":"Which quartet of hepatic procoagulant clotting factors depends on Vitamin K hydroquinone as a cofactor for post-translational gamma-carboxylation of glutamic acid residues (and is therefore inhibited by warfarin)?","o":["Von Willebrand factor, plasminogen, tissue factor, and thromboxane A2","Factors XI, XII, prekallikrein, and high-molecular-weight kininogen","Factors I (fibrinogen), V, VIII, and XIII","Factors II (prothrombin), VII, IX, and X (along with anticoagulant Proteins C and S)"],"a":3,"e":"Vitamin K (reduced hydroquinone KH2) is required by hepatic gamma-glutamyl carboxylase to add an extra carboxyl group to N-terminal glutamate residues on Factors II, VII, IX, and X (and endogenous anticoagulants Proteins C and S), enabling Ca2+-mediated phospholipid membrane binding. Warfarin inhibits Vitamin K Epoxide Reductase (VKOR), blocking regeneration of active KH2 and depleting functional Factors II, VII, IX, and X.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02170","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Biochemistry and Clinical Pathology","topic":"Pre-Renal Azotaemia vs Intrinsic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN)","q":"A 68-year-old man with severe gastroenteritis and orthostatic hypotension presents with oliguria, dry mucous membranes, Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) 54 mg/dL, serum creatinine 1.8 mg/dL (BUN:Cr ratio = 30:1), urine specific gravity 1.028, and a bland urine sediment without muddy-brown casts. Which acute kidney injury pattern do these biochemical results indicate?","o":["Pre-renal azotaemia (hypovolaemia/hypoperfusion causing intact tubules to avidly reabsorb Na+, water, and urea disproportionately relative to creatinine)","Intrinsic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN) with tubular necrosis","Post-renal bilateral ureteric stone obstruction exclusively","Nephrotic syndrome with massive lipiduria"],"a":0,"e":"In pre-renal azotaemia (renal hypoperfusion from dehydration, haemorrhage, or heart failure), renal tubular cells remain structurally intact and respond to low perfusion/ADH/angiotensin II by avidly reabsorbing water, sodium (FENa \u003c 1%, concentrated urine \u003e1.020), and passive urea—raising BUN disproportionately compared with creatinine (BUN:Cr ratio \u003e 20:1). Conversely, intrinsic ATN damages tubular reabsorption (BUN:Cr ~ 10-15:1, FENa \u003e 2%, muddy-brown granular casts).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02171","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Weekly Iron and Folic Acid Supplementation (WIFS) \u0026 IDD Control","q":"Under India\u0027s Weekly Iron and Folic Acid Supplementation (WIFS / Anemia Mukt Bharat) programme for school-going and out-of-school adolescents (10-19 years), what exact weekly blue tablet composition and biannual deworming regimen are administered, and what minimum iodine level is mandated in iodised salt at the consumer level?","o":["10 mg elemental iron + 5 mg vitamin B12 daily; \u003e= 500 ppm iodine","60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid weekly (with albendazole 400 mg every 6 months); \u003e= 15 ppm iodine at consumer household level (\u003e= 30 ppm at production)","200 mg elemental iron IV monthly; 0 ppm iodine","5 mg folic acid alone without iron; 1 ppm iodine"],"a":1,"e":"Under MoHFW\u0027s Anemia Mukt Bharat / WIFS guidelines, adolescents aged 10-19 years receive one blue IFA tablet weekly containing 60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid alongside biannual deworming (albendazole 400 mg). Under the National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP / FSSAI), edible salt must contain \u003e= 30 ppm iodine at the manufacturing point and not less than 15 ppm at the consumer/retail distribution level.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02172","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"NACO First-Line Antiretroviral Therapy (TLD Regimen)","q":"Under India\u0027s National AIDS Control Organisation (NACO) guidelines, what is the preferred once-daily fixed-dose combination (TLD regimen) for treatment-naive adults living with HIV-1?","o":["Abacavir + Nevirapine + Ritonavir monotherapy","Acyclovir + Oseltamivir + Ribavirin","Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF 300 mg) + Lamivudine (3TC 300 mg) + Dolutegravir (DTG 50 mg)","Zidovudine + Stavudine + Indinavir"],"a":2,"e":"NACO/WHO guidelines establish TLD—Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (NRTI, 300 mg) + Lamivudine (NRTI, 300 mg) + Dolutegravir (Integrase Strand Transfer Inhibitor, 50 mg)—as the preferred first-line single-tablet daily regimen due to rapid viral suppression, high genetic barrier to resistance, minimal drug interactions (note: double DTG to 50 mg twice daily if co-administered with rifampin!), and improved tolerability over efavirenz/stavudine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02173","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Levodopa + Carbidopa Combination in Parkinson\u0027s Disease","q":"Why is levodopa almost always formulated together with carbidopa (e.g., 100 mg / 25 mg) in Parkinson\u0027s disease rather than administered alone?","o":["Carbidopa crosses the blood-brain barrier rapidly to stimulate D2 receptors directly without levodopa","Carbidopa inhibits peripheral COMT exclusively inside skeletal muscle","Carbidopa converts dopamine back into tyrosine in the stomach","Carbidopa inhibits peripheral aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AAAD) WITHOUT crossing the blood-brain barrier, reducing peripheral conversion of levodopa to dopamine (decreasing nausea, hypotension, arrhythmias) while increasing CNS levodopa delivery ~4-fold"],"a":3,"e":"Oral levodopa given alone undergoes \u003e95% premature decarboxylation in gut mucosa and liver by aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AAAD), generating peripheral dopamine that cannot cross the BBB yet causes severe nausea/vomiting (CTZ outside BBB), orthostatic hypotension, and cardiac arrhythmias. Carbidopa (and benserazide) inhibits peripheral AAAD without entering the CNS, cutting required levodopa dose by ~75%, blunting peripheral side effects, and preventing pyridoxine (Vitamin B6) from accelerating peripheral levodopa breakdown.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02174","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Metoclopramide Extrapyramidal Toxicity vs Ondansetron","q":"An 18-year-old woman given intravenous metoclopramide for acute gastroenteritis-related vomiting develops sudden painful neck twisting (torticollis), jaw muscle spasm, and upward eye deviation (oculogyric crisis) 4 hours later. What receptor mechanism caused this acute dystonic reaction, and which drug class rapidly reverses it?","o":["Central nigrostriatal dopamine D2 receptor blockade by metoclopramide (which crosses the BBB); reverse rapidly with parenteral anticholinergic/antihistamine (benztropine, trihexyphenidyl, or diphenhydramine/promethazine)","GABA-A inhibition; reverse with intravenous flumazenil","Muscarinic M3 agonism; reverse with intravenous neostigmine","Peripheral 5-HT3 blockade by metoclopramide; reverse with oral ondansetron"],"a":0,"e":"Metoclopramide is a prokinetic antiemetic that blocks D2 receptors in the CTZ and enhances GI acetylcholine release, but unlike domperidone (which poorly crosses the BBB) or ondansetron (selective 5-HT3 antagonist), metoclopramide penetrates the CNS and blocks striatal D2 receptors—precipitating acute dystonia/oculogyric crisis especially in children and young adults. Restoring striatal cholinergic-dopaminergic balance with parenteral anticholinergic (benztropine/biperiden) or diphenhydramine/promethazine reverses dystonia rapidly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02175","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Systemic Glucocorticoid Withdrawal \u0026 HPA Axis Suppression","q":"A 40-year-old patient with nephrotic syndrome who has taken oral prednisolone 40 mg/day for 4 months abruptly stops his tablets on his own and presents 5 days later with severe weakness, nausea, abdominal pain, hyponatraemia, and refractory hypotension. Why MUST prolonged (\u003e2-3 weeks) systemic corticosteroid therapy always be tapered gradually?","o":["Prednisolone accumulates in adipose tissue and crystallises in renal tubules unless tapered","Exogenous glucocorticoids suppress hypothalamic CRH and pituitary ACTH via negative feedback, causing adrenocortical atrophy—abrupt cessation precipitates acute secondary adrenal insufficiency (adrenal crisis)","Gradual tapering induces hepatic CYP3A4 so prednisolone converts into endogenous cortisol","Abrupt cessation causes sudden rebound hypersecretion of aldosterone leading to malignant hypertension"],"a":1,"e":"Supraphysiological systemic corticosteroids administered for \u003e2-3 weeks exert continuous negative feedback on the hypothalamus (CRH) and anterior pituitary (ACTH), leading to hypofunction and atrophy of the adrenal cortex (zona fasciculata). Abrupt withdrawal leaves the patient unable to mount endogenous cortisol during baseline or stress states, causing fatigue, hypoglycaemia, GI distress, and vascular collapse. Gradual tapering allows HPA axis recovery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02176","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy and Management","topic":"Acute Hypoglycaemia Management (\u0027Rule of 15\u0027)","q":"During an OSCE diabetes counselling station, a conscious patient on insulin reports sweating, tremor, and palpitations with a capillary glucometer reading of 56 mg/dL. Applying the standard \u0027Rule of 15\u0027, what exact rescue instruction should the pharmacist give?","o":["Drink 500 mL of sugar-free diet soda and go to sleep immediately","Eat a large bar of dark chocolate with buttered toast because dietary fat speeds gastric glucose absorption","Immediately take 15 g of fast-acting simple carbohydrate (e.g., 3-4 glucose tablets, 3 teaspoons of table sugar dissolved in water, or 120-150 mL fruit juice—avoiding chocolate/ice cream), recheck blood glucose in 15 minutes, and repeat 15 g if still \u003c 70 mg/dL","Inject 10 units of rapid-acting insulin lispro immediately and wait 2 hours"],"a":2,"e":"For conscious mild-to-moderate hypoglycaemia (BG \u003c 70 mg/dL), the ADA/ICMR \u002715-15 Rule\u0027 directs taking 15 g of pure rapid-absorbing carbohydrate (glucose tablets, glucose/sucrose water, or juice), waiting 15 minutes, and retesting blood glucose—repeating 15 g until BG \u003e= 70 mg/dL, followed by a complex snack/meal if the next meal is \u003e1 hour away. High-fat foods (chocolate, pastry, ice cream) slow gastric emptying and delay glucose recovery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02177","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy and Management","topic":"Transdermal Fentanyl Patch Counselling \u0026 Disposal","q":"When counselling a palliative oncology caregiver on a fentanyl transdermal patch (changed every 72 hours), which two safety instructions regarding patch disposal and local heat exposure are ESSENTIAL to prevent fatal opioid overdose?","o":["Soak in a hot sauna bath daily while wearing the patch on broken skin","Cut the used patch into four pieces and discard loosely in an open kitchen bin; apply a heating pad directly over the patch to double analgesia","Place two patches simultaneously on day 1 for breakthrough pain","Fold the used patch in half with sticky adhesive sides pressed firmly together before safe disposal/flushing per local protocol (because significant residual fentanyl remains), and NEVER apply heating pads, hot water bottles, or electric blankets over the patch"],"a":3,"e":"After 72 hours of wear, a fentanyl transdermal patch still retains 28-84% of its original fentanyl content—enough to fatally overdose a toddler or pet if chewed or stuck to skin—so used patches must immediately be folded sticky-sides-together and disposed securely. Moreover, external heat (heating pads, hot tubs, saunas, fever) vasodilates dermal capillaries and accelerates transdermal fentanyl flux dangerously, risking lethal respiratory depression.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02178","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Hospital and Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Unit-Dose Drug Distribution System (UDDS)","q":"Why is the Centralised/Decentralised Unit-Dose Drug Distribution System (UDDS)—where medications are dispensed by the pharmacy in single-dose, ready-to-administer packages covering a 24-hour supply per patient cassette—considered safer and more economical than the Complete Floor Stock System?","o":["Eliminates nursing medication preparation errors, permits prospective pharmacist review of every order, minimises ward drug pilferage/expiration waste, and provides accurate patient-level billing and credit for unused doses","Stores bulk 1,000-tablet jars of narcotics unlocked in every patient room","Increases ward inventory capital tie-up fivefold without pharmacist oversight","Requires nurses to compound all sterile IV antibiotics on open ward tables"],"a":0,"e":"In UDDS, each medication dose is individually packaged, labelled (drug name, strength, lot, expiry), and placed in patient-specific cassettes for \u003c=24 hours after pharmacist verification of the medication order. Compared with bulk Ward Floor Stock, UDDS markedly lowers medication administration errors, frees nursing time, eliminates ward-level bulk stockpiling/pilferage/spoilage, and credits returned unopened unit doses directly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02179","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Acute Coronary Syndrome (STEMI) Immediate Pharmacotherapy","q":"A 58-year-old man presents to casualty with 45 minutes of crushing retrosternal chest pain, diaphoresis, and ST-segment elevation in leads V2-V5 (anterior STEMI). Alongside urgent ECG/reperfusion activation, which immediate oral antiplatelet loading instruction is standard first-line care?","o":["Administer oral warfarin 10 mg monotherapy without aspirin","Chew and swallow 300-325 mg of non-enteric-coated aspirin immediately (for rapid buccal/gastric platelet COX-1 blockade), followed by a P2Y12 inhibitor loading dose (clopidogrel or ticagrelor/prasugrel)","Give intravenous vitamin K1 10 mg and withhold all antiplatelets for 48 hours","Swallow a single 75 mg enteric-coated aspirin tablet whole with a fatty meal"],"a":1,"e":"In suspected Acute Coronary Syndrome (STEMI/NSTEMI), immediate administration of non-enteric-coated aspirin 300-325 mg chewed (achieving faster systemic/portal absorption and complete platelet thromboxane A2 suppression within 15-30 minutes vs \u003e2-3 hours for swallowed enteric-coated tablets) reduces mortality substantially and is paired with a P2Y12 receptor inhibitor (ticagrelor, prasugrel, or clopidogrel) plus anticoagulation and rapid primary PCI or fibrinolysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02180","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Hypothyroidism \u0026 Levothyroxine Counselling","q":"A 42-year-old woman taking oral levothyroxine 75 mcg daily for primary hypothyroidism continues to have an elevated TSH (14 mIU/L) despite claiming full adherence. Medication history reveals she swallows her levothyroxine tablet every morning together with her calcium carbonate + ferrous sulfate supplements and a cup of milk coffee. What pharmacokinetic counselling resolves this apparent treatment failure?","o":["Double the levothyroxine dose immediately and keep taking it with iron and calcium","Crush levothyroxine inside aluminium hydroxide gel to boost solubility","Take levothyroxine on an empty stomach with plain water 30-60 minutes before breakfast, and separate calcium carbonate, iron supplements, antacids, and soy/coffee by at least 4 hours to prevent insoluble gut chelation/binding","Switch levothyroxine to be taken immediately after a heavy high-fibre dinner with milk"],"a":2,"e":"Oral levothyroxine (T4) requires normal gastric acidity for tablet dissolution and has ~70-80% fasting bioavailability. Polyvalent cations (calcium carbonate, ferrous sulfate, aluminium/magnesium antacids, sucralfate), bile acid sequestrants, soy protein, and coffee bind levothyroxine in the GI tract, slashing absorption and causing persistently elevated TSH. Spacing cation supplements \u003e=4 hours apart and taking levothyroxine fasting with water restores euthyroidism cleanly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02181","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law and Ethics","topic":"Rule 65 Conditions of Retail Pharmacy Licence","q":"Under Rule 65 of the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 (India), what statutory requirement governs the retail sale and supply of prescription medicines (Schedules H, H1, X) at a licensed retail chemist/pharmacy (Form 20/21)?","o":["Only Schedule X drugs require a Registered Pharmacist; Schedule H antibiotics may be sold by any shop assistant","The Registered Pharmacist\u0027s certificate must be locked hidden in a bank vault rather than displayed","Retail pharmacies may operate indefinitely without a Registered Pharmacist as long as a wholesale dealer visits monthly","Supply of drugs on prescription MUST be conducted under the personal supervision of a Registered Pharmacist whose original registration certificate is conspicuously displayed on the premises"],"a":3,"e":"Under Rule 65(2) and Rule 65(15) of the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 (aligned with Section 42 of the Pharmacy Act, 1948), retail supply of any drug compounded or dispensed against a prescription—and retail sale of Schedule H/H1/X drugs—must occur under the personal supervision of a qualified Registered Pharmacist (whose name is entered in the State Pharmacy Register and endorsed on the retail licence), with the registration certificate displayed prominently at the premises.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02182","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Hard Gelatin Capsules","q":"In pharmaceutical manufacturing of hard gelatin capsules for human oral use, which capsule size number corresponds to the largest fill capacity (~1.37 mL) and what is the standard equilibrium moisture content of empty hard gelatin shells?","o":["Size 000 is the largest; moisture content is 12–15% w/w","Size 000 is the largest; moisture content is 25–30% w/w","Size 5 is the largest; moisture content is 2–4% w/w","Size 0 is the largest; moisture content is 12–15% w/w"],"a":0,"e":"Standard human hard gelatin capsule sizes run inversely from 000 (largest, ~1.37 mL capacity) through 00, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, down to 5 (smallest, ~0.13 mL). Hard gelatin shells normally contain 12–15% w/w moisture acting as a plasticizer; if moisture drops below ~10%, shells become brittle and crack, whereas above ~16–18% they soften and distort.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02183","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Emulsions and HLB System","q":"A formulator wants to prepare a stable oil-in-water (O/W) topical cream and compares a surfactant of HLB 4.3 (Span 80) against one of HLB 15.0 (Tween 80). Which statement accurately reflects surfactant selection and emulsion instability?","o":["Tween 80 (HLB 15.0) favors O/W emulsions; creaming is reversible upon shaking whereas cracking (coalescence) is irreversible","Span 80 (HLB 4.3) favors O/W emulsions; creaming is irreversible whereas cracking is reversible","Tween 80 (HLB 15.0) favors W/O emulsions; phase inversion improves stability permanently","Span 80 (HLB 4.3) and Tween 80 (HLB 15.0) both yield exclusively O/W emulsions regardless of phase volume ratio"],"a":0,"e":"According to Griffin\u0027s Hydrophile-Lipophile Balance (HLB) scale, predominantly lipophilic surfactants (HLB 3–6, such as Span 80 at 4.3) stabilize water-in-oil (W/O) emulsions, while hydrophilic surfactants (HLB 8–16, such as Tween 80 at 15.0) stabilize oil-in-water (O/W) emulsions. Creaming is the upward or downward migration of intact dispersed droplets due to density differences and is reversible by shaking; cracking (breaking) results from film rupture and coalescence into separate continuous oil and water layers, which is irreversible.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02184","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Suppositories","q":"A pharmacist prepares 12 rectal suppositories using a 2.0 g cocoa butter mold, each containing 200 mg of zinc oxide (displacement value = 5.0). Assuming no overage is included, what weight of cocoa butter is required for the batch?","o":["21.60 g","23.52 g","24.00 g","12.00 g"],"a":1,"e":"Total cocoa butter capacity of 12 blank 2.0 g molds = 12 × 2.0 g = 24.00 g. Total weight of zinc oxide required = 12 × 0.200 g = 2.40 g. Since the displacement value (DV) of zinc oxide in cocoa butter is 5.0 (meaning 5.0 g of ZnO displaces 1.0 g of cocoa butter), the weight of cocoa butter displaced = 2.40 g / 5.0 = 0.48 g. Therefore, cocoa butter needed = 24.00 g - 0.48 g = 23.52 g.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02185","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Limit Test for Arsenic (Gutzeit Method, IP)","q":"In the Indian Pharmacopoeia Gutzeit limit test for arsenic, arsine gas (AsH3) generated in the bottle passes through a plug of cotton wool moistened with lead acetate solution before reaching the mercuric chloride paper. What is the specific function of the lead acetate cotton wool?","o":["To catalyze the reduction of pentavalent arsenic to trivalent arsenic","To absorb excess hydrochloric acid vapors and generate nascent hydrogen","To trap any interfering hydrogen sulfide (H2S) gas as insoluble black lead sulfide so it does not stain the mercuric chloride paper","To oxidize arsine gas into non-volatile arsenate ions"],"a":2,"e":"During reduction of arsenic with granulated zinc and stannated hydrochloric acid, trace sulfide impurities in the sample or reagents evolve hydrogen sulfide (H2S) gas, which would otherwise react with mercuric chloride (HgCl2) paper to produce a false dark stain. Passing the evolved gas stream through lead acetate cotton wool traps H2S selectively as insoluble lead sulfide (PbS), allowing pure arsine (AsH3) to pass unimpeded and produce a yellow-to-brown stain on the HgCl2 paper.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02186","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Complexometric Titrations","q":"During the IP complexometric assay of Magnesium Sulfate (MgSO4·7H2O) using 0.05 M disodium edetate (EDTA) and Eriochrome Black T (Mordant Black II) indicator, why must the solution be buffered to pH 10 with ammonia-ammonium chloride buffer?","o":["Both EDTA stability constant with Mg2+ and the indicator\u0027s color transition from wine-red to sky-blue require alkaline pH ~10","At pH 10, Mg2+ precipitates completely as Mg(OH)2 before EDTA is added","Acidic pH destroys disodium EDTA by hydrolyzing its carboxylate arms","Eriochrome Black T acts as a redox indicator only in strongly acidic pH \u003c 2"],"a":0,"e":"EDTA binds divalent cations like Mg2+ effectively only in its deprotonated anionic forms (HY3-/Y4-) prevalent at alkaline pH (~10), ensuring a sufficiently high conditional stability constant for quantitative 1:1 chelation. Additionally, Eriochrome Black T is a pH-sensitive metallochromic dye whose free uncomplexed form is blue between pH 7 and 11 (giving a sharp wine-red Mg-indicator complex to pure blue free-indicator endpoint at pH 10).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02187","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Salicylates","q":"A pharmacy quality control technician adds neutral ferric chloride solution to an alcoholic solution of freshly synthesized aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) and observes an immediate deep violet coloration. What does this result indicate?","o":["Complete purity of acetylsalicylic acid with zero phenolic groups","Successful formation of acetylsalicylic acid sodium salt","Presence of benzoic acid formed by decarboxylation","Presence of free salicylic acid impurity resulting from incomplete acetylation or hydrolytic degradation"],"a":3,"e":"Pure aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) has its phenolic hydroxyl group masked as an acetate ester and therefore does not react with neutral ferric chloride (FeCl3) solution. When aspirin absorbs moisture and hydrolyzes to salicylic acid and acetic acid (or contains unreacted salicylic acid), the exposed free phenolic -OH group forms a deep violet iron(III)-salicylate coordination complex with FeCl3.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02188","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Cardiac Glycosides (Digitalis)","q":"An extract of Digitalis purpurea leaves is dissolved in glacial acetic acid containing a trace of ferric chloride, and concentrated sulfuric acid is carefully layered down the side of the test tube. A reddish-brown ring appears at the junction while the upper acetic acid layer slowly turns bluish-green. What chemical moiety is detected by this Keller-Killiani test?","o":["2-Deoxy sugars (specifically digitoxose) attached to the cardiac glycoside","Volatile monoterpene aldehydes","Condensed catechin tannins","Tropane alkaloid ester moiety"],"a":0,"e":"The Keller-Killiani test is the classic diagnostic test for 2-deoxy sugars (such as D-digitoxose) found in cardiac glycosides like digitoxin and digoxin. Concentrated sulfuric acid hydrolyzes the glycosidic bond and dehydrates the deoxy sugar, which reacts with glacial acetic acid and trace ferric chloride to yield a reddish-brown interface and a characteristic bluish-green upper acetic acid layer.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02189","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Quinoline Alkaloids (Cinchona)","q":"When an aqueous acidic extract of Cinchona bark is treated sequentially with bromine water and dilute ammonium hydroxide, an emerald-green coloration develops. What is the name of this specific test and which alkaloid class does it identify?","o":["Shinoda test for flavone glycosides","Thalleioquin test for quinoline alkaloids (quinine and quinidine)","Guignard test for cyanogenetic glycosides","Vitali-Morin test for tropane alkaloids"],"a":1,"e":"The Thalleioquin test is specific for methoxylated quinoline alkaloids obtained from Cinchona bark (Cinchona calisaya / C. succirubra), notably quinine and quinidine. Oxidation with bromine water followed by addition of excess ammonia yields the green quinonoid pigment thalleioquin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02190","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy \u0026 Physiology","topic":"Gas Transport in Blood","q":"During vigorous skeletal muscle exercise, local tissue pCO2 rises, pH falls, and temperature increases. How do these changes alter the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve (Bohr effect) and oxygen delivery?","o":["The curve shifts to the left, increasing hemoglobin\u0027s affinity for O2 and retaining oxygen in blood","Hemoglobin converts irreversibly to methemoglobin to release more O2","The curve shifts to the right, decreasing hemoglobin\u0027s affinity for O2 and enhancing oxygen unloading to tissues","The curve shifts downward without changing P50, blocking carbon dioxide transport"],"a":2,"e":"The Bohr effect describes how elevated pCO2, decreased pH (increased H+), increased temperature, and higher 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG) stabilize the taut (T) deoxy state of hemoglobin, shifting the O2-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the right (increasing P50). This reduced affinity facilitates O2 unloading precisely where metabolically active tissues need it most.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02191","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy \u0026 Physiology","topic":"Gastric Secretions and Vitamin B12 Absorption","q":"Autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells reduces secretion of Intrinsic Factor (Castle\u0027s intrinsic factor), leading to impaired absorption of vitamin B12 at which specific gastrointestinal site and causing which type of anemia?","o":["Descending colon; aplastic pancytopenia","Stomach body; sickle-cell hemolytic anemia","Duodenum; microcytic hypochromic iron-deficiency anemia","Terminal ileum; pernicious (megaloblastic) anemia"],"a":3,"e":"Gastric parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete both hydrochloric acid and Intrinsic Factor (IF), a glycoprotein that binds dietary vitamin B12 (cobalamin) in the upper small intestine and protects it until receptor-mediated endocytosis occurs in the terminal ileum. Lack of IF prevents ileal B12 absorption, impairing DNA synthesis in erythroid precursors and causing megaloblastic (pernicious) anemia with hypersegmented neutrophils.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02192","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Cold Chain and Vaccine Vial Monitor (VVM)","q":"At an immunization clinic, a pharmacist inspects a vial of Pentavalent (DPT-HepB-Hib) vaccine stored in an Ice-Lined Refrigerator (ILR). Which Vaccine Vial Monitor (VVM) stage mandates discarding the vial immediately, and why must Pentavalent vials never be placed directly on frozen ice packs?","o":["Stage 3 or 4 (inner square matches or is darker than outer circle); Pentavalent is freeze-sensitive and freezing destroys aluminum-adjuvanted potency","Stage 1 (inner square much lighter than outer circle); freezing only affects live attenuated BCG","Stage 2 (inner square slightly darker than white but still lighter than outer circle); freezing activates toxoid hyperimmunity","Only when the outer blue circle turns white; Pentavalent should be stored at -20 °C"],"a":0,"e":"A VVM indicates cumulative heat exposure: in Stage 1 and Stage 2, the inner square remains lighter than the outer reference circle, so the vaccine is usable (use Stage 2 first under EEFO). In Stage 3 (equal color) and Stage 4 (inner square darker than outer circle), the discard point is reached and the vaccine must NOT be used. Furthermore, aluminum-adjuvanted \u0027T-series\u0027 and Hepatitis B vaccines (DPT, Td, Pentavalent, HepB, PCV, IPV) are freeze-sensitive and lose potency irreversibly if frozen (\u003c0 °C), requiring strict storage at +2 °C to +8 °C.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02193","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Diarrheal Disease Management","q":"According to the current WHO/UNICEF standard formula for reduced-osmolarity Oral Rehydration Salts (ORS) reconstituted in 1 liter of clean drinking water, what is its total osmolarity and why is glucose included alongside sodium chloride?","o":["150 mOsm/L; glucose solely improves palatability without aiding electrolyte transport","245 mOsm/L; glucose facilitates coupled Na+ and water absorption across the intestinal brush border via SGLT1 even during secretory diarrhea","311 mOsm/L; glucose inhibits intestinal motility like loperamide","450 mOsm/L; hypertonic glucose draws water into the gut lumen to flush pathogens"],"a":1,"e":"The current WHO reduced-osmolarity ORS (20.5 g sachet per 1 L water) yields a total osmolarity of 245 mOsm/L (Na+ 75, glucose 75, Cl- 65, K+ 20, citrate 10 mmol/L). Co-transport of Na+ and glucose via the apical sodium-glucose linked transporter (SGLT1) remains intact even during severe toxin-mediated secretory diarrhea (such as cholera), driving passive osmotic water absorption while avoiding the osmotic diarrhea seen with excessive sugar.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02194","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Potassium-Sparing Diuretics","q":"A 59-year-old man with heart failure develops painful bilateral gynecomastia and mild hyperkalemia 4 months after adding spironolactone 25 mg daily to his regimen. Switching to which selective mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist avoids anti-androgenic gynecomastia while preserving aldosterone blockade?","o":["Amiloride","Triamterene","Eplerenone","Chlorthalidone"],"a":2,"e":"Spironolactone non-selectively binds androgen and progesterone receptors in addition to mineralocorticoid receptors, frequently causing gynecomastia, breast tenderness, and impotence in men (and menstrual irregularities in women). Eplerenone is a selective mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist with negligible affinity for androgen and progesterone receptors, eliminating anti-androgenic gynecomastia while maintaining survival benefits in HFrEF (though potassium monitoring remains mandatory).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02195","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Paracetamol (Acetaminophen) Toxicity","q":"A 22-year-old woman presents 6 hours after ingesting 18 g of paracetamol (acetaminophen) in a self-harm attempt. Intravenous N-acetylcysteine (NAC) is initiated promptly. How does N-acetylcysteine prevent centrilobular hepatic necrosis?","o":["It acts as a competitive antagonist at peripheral prostaglandin EP3 receptors","It chelates unchanged paracetamol in the renal collecting duct","It irreversibly inhibits hepatic glucuronidation and sulfation pathways","It supplies cysteine to replenish hepatic glutathione stores, detoxifying the reactive CYP2E1 metabolite NAPQI"],"a":3,"e":"At therapeutic doses, paracetamol is mainly conjugated with glucuronide and sulfate; a small fraction oxidized by CYP2E1 forms the toxic electrophile N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI), which is safely detoxified by hepatic glutathione (GSH). In overdose, conjugation pathways saturate and GSH is depleted, allowing NAPQI to bind covalently to hepatocyte proteins and cause centrilobular necrosis. N-acetylcysteine provides sulfhydryl/cysteine groups to restore GSH and also directly conjugates NAPQI.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02196","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Lignocaine with Adrenaline","q":"Lignocaine (lidocaine) is frequently formulated with adrenaline (1:200,000) for infiltrative dental and minor surgical procedures, yet this combination is strictly avoided for ring blocks of fingers, toes, or the tip of the nose. What explains both its general benefit and this specific anatomical contraindication?","o":["Adrenaline causes local vasoconstriction that prolongs anesthesia and reduces systemic toxicity, but risks ischemic necrosis in end-artery tissues lacking collateral circulation","Adrenaline directly accelerates Na+ channel blockade in C-fibers, but causes immediate anaphylaxis in digital nerves","Adrenaline alkalizes tissue pH to prevent lignocaine ionization, but dissolves digital bone cartilage","Adrenaline increases hepatic blood flow to clear lignocaine faster, except in peripheral extremities"],"a":0,"e":"Adding a dilute vasoconstrictor (adrenaline 1:100,000 to 1:200,000) constricts local arterioles via alpha-1 receptors, keeping lignocaine at the nerve site longer, prolonging block duration, reducing surgical bleeding, and slowing systemic absorption (lowering CNS/cardiac toxicity risk). However, in anatomical regions supplied by end-arteries with minimal collateral blood supply (digits, toes, pinna, tip of nose, penis), intense vasoconstriction can precipitate prolonged tissue ischemia and gangrene.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02197","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Look-Alike Sound-Alike (LASA) Drugs","q":"To minimize Look-Alike Sound-Alike (LASA) dispensing errors on pharmacy shelves and computer pick-lists between \u0027hydralazine\u0027 and \u0027hydroxyzine\u0027 or \u0027prednisone\u0027 and \u0027prednisolone\u0027, which standardized safety intervention is recommended by ISMP and patient safety authorities?","o":["Apply Tall Man lettering (e.g., hydrALAZINE vs hydrOXYzine) and physically separate LASA pairs on storage shelves with warning labels","Store both LASA drugs immediately side-by-side alphabetically in identical unlabelled bins","Dispense solely from memory without barcode scanning or independent double-checks","Substitute brand names randomly without informing the prescriber"],"a":0,"e":"Tall Man lettering capitalizes the dissimilar letter segments in confused drug name pairs (such as hydrALAZINE / hydrOXYzine, predniSONE / predniSOLONE, DOPamine / DOBUTamine) to draw visual attention and prevent confirmation-bias misreading. Combining Tall Man lettering with physical shelf separation, distinct auxiliary LASA alerts, and barcode verification substantially reduces dispensing errors.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02198","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Inventory Control Calculations","q":"A pharmacy chain estimates annual demand (D) for a fast-moving insulin pen pack at 2,000 units. The fixed ordering cost (S) is ₹100 per order, and the annual inventory holding cost (H) is ₹10 per unit per year. What is the Economic Order Quantity (EOQ) per order?","o":["100 units","200 units","2,000 units","400 units"],"a":1,"e":"The Wilson Economic Order Quantity formula is EOQ = sqrt[(2 × D × S) / H]. Substituting D = 2,000 units/year, S = ₹100/order, and H = ₹10/unit/year: EOQ = sqrt[(2 × 2,000 × 100) / 10] = sqrt[400,000 / 10] = sqrt(40,000) = 200 units per order (resulting in 2,000 / 200 = 10 orders per year).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02199","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Pediatric Fever Counselling","q":"A parent requests an over-the-counter aspirin tablet to reduce high fever in their 6-year-old child recovering from chickenpox (varicella). Why must the community pharmacist decline aspirin and recommend paracetamol instead?","o":["Aspirin has zero antipyretic effect in pediatric patients due to immature COX-2","Aspirin causes permanent dental enamel staining in children under 8 years","Aspirin use during viral infections such as varicella or influenza in children is strongly associated with Reye\u0027s syndrome (acute encephalopathy and fatty liver degeneration)","Paracetamol prevents viral replication directly whereas aspirin promotes bacterial superinfection"],"a":2,"e":"Administering salicylates (aspirin) to children and adolescents (\u003c16–18 years) with febrile viral illnesses—especially influenza and varicella—carries a well-documented risk of Reye\u0027s syndrome, a rare but potentially fatal condition featuring mitochondrial injury, acute non-inflammatory encephalopathy, cerebral edema, and hepatic microvesicular steatosis. Weight-based paracetamol (or ibuprofen if well-hydrated and non-varicella/non-dengue) is recommended instead.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02200","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Biochemistry \u0026 Clinical Pathology","topic":"Cardiac Biomarkers in Myocardial Infarction","q":"A 58-year-old man treated for an acute ST-elevation myocardial infarction 4 days ago develops recurrent crushing chest pain. Which cardiac enzyme/biomarker profile best distinguishes a new acute reinfarction at day 4 from persistent elevation due to the initial event?","o":["Total serum alkaline phosphatase (ALP) elevation","Serum amylase and lipase elevation","A solitary elevation in serum Troponin I without serial comparison, since Troponin normalizes within 6 hours","A secondary spike in serum CK-MB (which normally peaks at 18–24 hours and normalizes within 48–72 hours), whereas cardiac Troponin I remains elevated for 7–10 days"],"a":3,"e":"Cardiac Troponin I and T are the gold-standard sensitive and specific biomarkers for myocardial injury, rising within 3–6 hours and remaining elevated for 7–14 days—making single troponin values ambiguous for detecting early reinfarction on day 4. In contrast, CK-MB rises in 4–6 hours, peaks around 18–24 hours, and returns to baseline within 48–72 hours; thus, a fresh re-elevation of CK-MB (or a \u003e= 20% rise in serial troponin values) clearly signals acute reinfarction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02201","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Biochemistry \u0026 Clinical Pathology","topic":"Chemical Examination of Urine","q":"A breastfed neonate\u0027s urine gives a positive brick-red precipitate with Benedict\u0027s qualitative test, yet a glucose oxidase urine dipstick strip reads completely negative. What biochemical explanation accounts for this discrepancy?","o":["Benedict\u0027s test detects any reducing sugar (such as galactose or lactose in galactosemia/lactosuria), whereas glucose oxidase strips are strictly specific for D-glucose","Glucose oxidase strips react only with sucrose and fructose","The neonate\u0027s urine contains excess urea which reduces glucose oxidase","Benedict\u0027s reagent detects ketone bodies (acetoacetate) selectively"],"a":0,"e":"Benedict\u0027s reagent contains alkaline cupric sulfate (Cu2+), which is reduced to insoluble red-orange cuprous oxide (Cu2O) by any sugar with a free aldehyde or ketone group (reducing sugars: glucose, galactose, fructose, lactose, maltose) as well as non-sugar reducing substances like ascorbic acid. By contrast, commercial dipsticks use glucose oxidase, which exclusively oxidizes beta-D-glucose—making a Benedict-positive / dipstick-negative pattern a classic screening clue for galactosemia or lactosuria in neonates.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02202","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Peptic Ulcer Disease","q":"A 44-year-old man prescribed oral omeprazole 20 mg once daily for duodenal ulcer reports persistent daytime heartburn because he takes the capsule right at bedtime on a full stomach. What counselling adjustment optimizes omeprazole efficacy?","o":["Double the dose only when pain occurs immediately after dinner","Take omeprazole 30 to 60 minutes before the first meal (breakfast) of the day so peak drug levels coincide with meal-stimulated active H+/K+-ATPase proton pumps","Take omeprazole simultaneously with an aluminum hydroxide antacid suspension","Crush the enteric-coated pellets and mix them in hot milk at bedtime"],"a":1,"e":"Proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole, pantoprazole, rabeprazole, esomeprazole) are acid-labile prodrugs requiring enteric protection; after intestinal absorption, they accumulate in parietal cell canaliculi and convert to active sulfenamide forms that covalently inhibit only actively secreting H+/K+-ATPase pumps. Because fasting stimulates recruitment of pumps into the canalicular membrane upon food intake, taking the PPI 30–60 minutes before breakfast ensures maximal active pumps are exposed during peak plasma drug concentration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02203","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Hypertension in Pregnancy","q":"A 29-year-old woman at 18 weeks of gestation with chronic hypertension (BP 154/96 mmHg) needs antihypertensive initiation. Which agent is safe and guideline-recommended in pregnancy, and why are ACE inhibitors/ARBs strictly contraindicated?","o":["Furosemide monotherapy is first-line to expand maternal plasma volume","Losartan is preferred in the second and third trimesters; methyldopa causes fetal renal agenesis","Labetalol or methyldopa (or extended-release nifedipine) is preferred; ACE inhibitors/ARBs cause fetal renal dysplasia, oligohydramnios, and skull hypoplasia","Enalapril is first-line because it reduces maternal proteinuria safely; labetalol is teratogenic"],"a":2,"e":"Oral labetalol, extended-release nifedipine, and methyldopa have well-established maternal and fetal safety profiles and serve as first-line antihypertensives in pregnancy (ICMR STW / ACOG / ESC guidelines). Conversely, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, and direct renin inhibitors are strictly contraindicated throughout pregnancy—particularly in the 2nd and 3rd trimesters—because fetal angiotensin II blockade impairs fetal renal perfusion, causing renal tubular dysplasia, anuria, oligohydramnios, pulmonary hypoplasia, and cranial ossification defects.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02204","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Hypothyroidism Management","q":"A patient newly diagnosed with primary hypothyroidism is started on levothyroxine 50 mcg daily. Which counselling instruction and follow-up monitoring interval are clinically appropriate?","o":["Take immediately after a high-fiber breakfast together with calcium carbonate and ferrous sulfate","Stop levothyroxine as soon as fatigue improves after 2 weeks","Recheck serum TSH after 48 hours to adjust the maintenance dose","Take with plain water on an empty stomach 30–60 minutes before breakfast, separate by 4 hours from iron/calcium supplements, and recheck serum TSH after 6–8 weeks"],"a":3,"e":"Oral levothyroxine absorption (~70–80% when fasting) is significantly impaired by food, dietary fiber, soy, coffee, and polyvalent cations (calcium, iron, aluminum/magnesium antacids, sucralfate) that form insoluble complexes in the gut lumen; therefore, it should be taken on an empty stomach 30–60 minutes before breakfast and separated by at least 4 hours from mineral supplements. Because levothyroxine has a long plasma half-life (~7 days), steady-state pituitary-thyroid feedback stabilizes after ~6–8 weeks, which is the proper interval for TSH re-evaluation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02205","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law \u0026 Ethics","topic":"Pharmacy Council of India (PCI)","q":"Under the Pharmacy Act, 1948, which statutory body is empowered to frame Education Regulations prescribing the minimum standard of education required for qualification as a pharmacist and to approve pharmacy courses/examinations across India?","o":["Pharmacy Council of India (PCI)","Drugs Technical Advisory Board (DTAB)","Central Drugs Laboratory (CDL), Kolkata","National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority (NPPA)"],"a":0,"e":"Under Section 10 and Section 12 of the Pharmacy Act, 1948, the Central Council—known as the Pharmacy Council of India (PCI)—frames the Education Regulations (ER) prescribing minimum curriculum, practical training, and institutional standards required for registration as a pharmacist, and inspects/approves courses and examinations nationwide (while State Pharmacy Councils maintain the registers of pharmacists).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02206","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law \u0026 Ethics","topic":"Prohibited Advertisements","q":"Under the Drugs and Magic Remedies (Objectionable Advertisements) Act, 1954 (India), advertising a drug or \u0027magic remedy\u0027 to the general public claiming to diagnose, cure, mitigate, or prevent which group of scheduled conditions is strictly prohibited?","o":["Standard WHO ORS packets promoted solely by a generic government health awareness campaign","Diabetes, cancer, cataract, leprosy, and sexual impotence (among the 54 scheduled diseases/conditions)","Topical cosmetic skin moisturization without therapeutic claims","Mild seasonal common cold symptoms only"],"a":1,"e":"Section 3(d) and the Schedule to the Drugs and Magic Remedies (Objectionable Advertisements) Act, 1954 prohibit public advertisements claiming cure, treatment, or prevention of 54 specified serious or chronic diseases/conditions—including diabetes, cancer, cataract, blindness, deafness, leprosy, tuberculosis, epilepsy, and sexual impotence—to protect the public from quackery and self-medication harm.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02207","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Simple Syrup IP (66.7% w/w) Self-Preservation","q":"Simple Syrup IP contains 66.7% w/w sucrose in purified water and resists bacterial and fungal spoilage at room temperature without any added chemical preservative, whereas a 30% w/w sucrose solution ferments rapidly. What physical mechanism explains the self-preserving stability of Simple Syrup IP?","o":["Sucrose chemically converts into ethyl alcohol and formaldehyde immediately upon boiling","Sucrose chelates atmospheric oxygen completely out of the glass bottle","Near-saturated 66.7% w/w sucrose exerts extremely high osmotic pressure (low water activity), dehydrating/plasmolysing contaminating bacteria, yeasts, and moulds","Simple Syrup IP maintains pH 1.5, destroying all bacterial peptidoglycan"],"a":2,"e":"According to the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP), Simple Syrup (66.7% w/w; USP 85% w/v) is nearly saturated with sucrose, leaving minimal free water (low water activity a_w) and exerting high osmotic pressure that draws water out of microbial cells (plasmolysis), halting growth. However, temperature fluctuations can cause surface condensation that dilutes the top layer—so diluted or medicated syrups (\u003c60% w/w) still require preservatives such as parabens or benzoic acid.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02208","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Compounding Eutectic Powder Mixtures","q":"When compounding a divided dusting powder prescription containing camphor, menthol, and talc, triturating camphor and menthol directly together in a porcelain mortar immediately produces a damp oily liquid. What physical phenomenon occurred, and how should the pharmacist compound this prescription cleanly as a dry free-flowing powder?","o":["Eutectic mixture formation (depression of melting point below room temperature); triturate each eutectic ingredient separately with an inert adsorbent diluent (light magnesium carbonate, kaolin, or starch) before lightly blending together","Hygroscopic deliquescence from atmospheric CO2; heat the mortar at 150 C to evaporate camphor","Irreversible esterification; discard the prescription and dispense as an emulsion","Polymorphic explosion; dissolve both powders in concentrated sulphuric acid"],"a":0,"e":"Certain low-melting organic compounds (camphor, menthol, thymol, phenol, aspirin, salol) form eutectic mixtures whose combined melting point drops below ambient room temperature, liquefying upon intimate trituration. In extemporaneous powder dispensing, the pharmacist either triturates each eutectic component separately with an inert absorbent powder (light magnesium carbonate/oxide, kaolin, or talc/starch) and blends gently by spatulation without heavy pressure, or deliberately liquefies them together and adsorbs the liquid onto the diluent.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02209","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Concentrate-to-Ratio Dilution Calculation","q":"A clinic requires 600 mL of a 1:2,000 w/v benzalkonium chloride antiseptic wash prepared from a 10% w/v stock concentrate. What volume of the 10% w/v concentrate must be diluted with purified water to 600 mL?","o":["30.0 mL","0.3 mL","6.0 mL","3.0 mL"],"a":3,"e":"1:2,000 w/v means 1 g per 2,000 mL = 0.05% w/v (0.5 mg/mL). For 600 mL, total benzalkonium chloride needed = 600 mL x (1 g / 2,000 mL) = 0.30 g (300 mg). Since the 10% w/v stock contains 10 g per 100 mL (0.10 g/mL), required stock volume = 0.30 g / (0.10 g/mL) = 3.0 mL diluted to 600 mL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02210","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Diazotisation Titration (IP Assay of Sulfonamides/Procaine)","q":"In the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) quantitative assay of primary aromatic amine drugs such as sulfamethoxazole, dapsone, or procaine hydrochloride by diazotisation titration, why is the acidic solution cooled below 15 C during titration with 0.1 M sodium nitrite (NaNO2), and what external indicator signals the endpoint?","o":["Cooling (\u003c15 C) prevents thermal decomposition of the unstable aryl diazonium salt and loss of volatile nitrous acid; external starch-iodide paste/paper turns instantaneously blue upon contact with the first drop of excess nitrous acid","Cooling prevents EDTA hydrolysis; Eriochrome Black T turns sky-blue","Cooling accelerates conversion into phenol gas; phenolphthalein turns bright crimson","Cooling keeps silver chloride dissolved; potassium chromate forms brick-red Ag2CrO4"],"a":0,"e":"Primary aromatic amines (Ar-NH2) react stoichiometrically 1:1 with nitrous acid (NaNO2 + HCl) to form aryl diazonium chlorides (Ar-N2+ Cl-). Keeping the reaction mixture chilled in an ice bath (0-15 C) prevents both volatilisation of nitrous acid and hydrolysis of the diazonium salt to phenols + N2 gas. At the equivalence point, the first slight excess of nitrous acid oxidises iodide (I-) on external starch-iodide paper/paste to free iodine (I2), producing an immediate dark-blue starch-iodine complex (or sharp dead-stop electrometric endpoint).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02211","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Chelating Agents in Heavy Metal Poisoning","q":"Match each specific pharmaceutical chelating antidote to the primary metal toxicity it treats clinically: 1) Dimercaprol (BAL), 2) D-Penicillamine, 3) Deferoxamine, 4) Sodium calcium edetate (CaNa2EDTA).","o":["1-Cyanide poisoning; 2-Paracetamol toxicity; 3-Warfarin bleeding; 4-Heparin overdose","1-Acute arsenic, inorganic mercury \u0026 gold poisoning; 2-Copper overload (Wilson\u0027s disease); 3-Acute iron poisoning; 4-Lead poisoning (often combined with BAL in lead encephalopathy)","1-Acute iron poisoning; 2-Lead encephalopathy; 3-Copper overload; 4-Cyanide poisoning","1-Copper overload; 2-Iron poisoning; 3-Arsenic toxicity; 4-Organophosphate poisoning"],"a":1,"e":"Heavy metal chelating specificity: Dimercaprol (British Anti-Lewisite, dithiol in oil IM) chelates arsenic, inorganic mercury, and gold; D-Penicillamine (oral thiol) promotes urinary copper excretion in Wilson\u0027s disease (hepatolenticular degeneration); Deferoxamine (parenteral siderophore) selectively binds free ferric iron (Fe3+) in acute iron toxicity/transfusion haemosiderosis without depleting Mg2+/Ca2+; CaNa2EDTA chelates lead without stripping plasma calcium (unlike Na2EDTA).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02212","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Glycoside \u0026 Volatile Oil Botanical Sources","q":"Match each official Indian Pharmacopoeia crude drug to its botanical source, family, and major active constituent: 1) Senna leaf, 2) Peppermint oil, 3) Cinnamon bark, 4) Clove flower buds.","o":["1-Syzygium aromaticum (eugenol); 2-Cassia angustifolia (sennosides); 3-Mentha piperita (menthol); 4-Cinnamomum zeylanicum (cinnamaldehyde)","1-Aloe barbadensis (aloin); 2-Eucalyptus globulus (cineole); 3-Zingiber officinale (zingiberene); 4-Carum carvi (carvone)","1-Cassia angustifolia (Leguminosae — sennosides A \u0026 B); 2-Mentha piperita (Lamiaceae — L-menthol); 3-Cinnamomum zeylanicum (Lauraceae — cinnamaldehyde); 4-Syzygium aromaticum (Myrtaceae — eugenol)","1-Digitalis purpurea (digoxin); 2-Foeniculum vulgare (anethole); 3-Coriandrum sativum (linalool); 4-Elettaria cardamomum (cineole)"],"a":2,"e":"Standard IP botanical origins: Tinnevelly Senna consists of dried leaflets of Cassia angustifolia (Leguminosae/Fabaceae) rich in anthraquinone dianthrone glycosides (sennosides A and B); Peppermint oil is steam-distilled from Mentha piperita (Lamiaceae, \u003e=50% menthol); Ceylon Cinnamon inner bark comes from Cinnamomum zeylanicum/verum (Lauraceae, cinnamaldehyde); Clove consists of dried flower buds of Syzygium aromaticum/Eugenia caryophyllus (Myrtaceae, 70-90% eugenol).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02213","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Diagnostic Trichomes \u0026 Calcium Oxalate Crystals","q":"During powdered microscopy of crude drugs, which diagnostic microscopic feature uniquely identifies Strychnos nux-vomica seed powder versus Urginia/Scilla (Squill) and Belladonna leaf respectively?","o":["Belladonna shows unicellular curved warty trichomes with cystoliths of calcium carbonate","All three crude drugs show identical rosette calcium oxalate clusters","Nux vomica shows oil droplets only with zero lignified structures; Squill shows cubic starch grains only","Nux vomica shows bent, lignified warty covering trichomes with thickened pitted bases; Squill shows bundles of needle-shaped acicular calcium oxalate raphides; Belladonna shows microsphenoidal (sandy) calcium oxalate crystals"],"a":3,"e":"Diagnostic cell inclusions and trichomes authenticate powdered botanicals: Strychnos nux-vomica seed epidermis exhibits characteristic closely packed, sharply bent lignified trichomes staining pink-red with phloroglucinol + HCl; Squill (Urginea indica/maritima) and Ipecac contain bundles of sharp needle-like acicular raphides of calcium oxalate; Atropa belladonna mesophyll cells are packed with dark microsphenoidal (\u0027crystal sand\u0027) calcium oxalate (whereas cystoliths of CaCO3 characterise Cannabis/Urticaceae, and rosettes characterise Clove/Rhubarb).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02214","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Spirometric Lung Volumes \u0026 Capacities","q":"Which pulmonary volume and derived lung capacities CANNOT be measured directly by a standard handheld or wet-bell spirometer (requiring helium dilution, nitrogen washout, or body plethysmography instead)?","o":["Residual Volume (RV), Functional Residual Capacity (FRC = ERV + RV), and Total Lung Capacity (TLC = VC + RV)","Inspiratory Capacity (IC = TV + IRV)","Tidal Volume (TV) and Inspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV)","Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV) and Vital Capacity (VC)"],"a":0,"e":"A spirometer measures only volumes of air that can be inhaled or exhaled at the mouth (TV, IRV, ERV, IC, and Vital Capacity VC = IRV + TV + ERV). Even after maximal forced expiration, ~1,100-1,200 mL of air remains trapped inside alveoli and conducting airways—the Residual Volume (RV)—preventing alveolar collapse. Consequently, RV and any capacity incorporating RV (FRC = ERV + RV; TLC = VC + RV) require gas dilution or body plethysmography.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02215","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Cholera Pathophysiology \u0026 Treatment","q":"During a monsoon flood outbreak, a 28-year-old man presents with sudden painless, profuse \u0027rice-water\u0027 diarrhoea, sunken eyes, feeble pulse, and severe dehydration caused by Vibrio cholerae enterotoxin. What is the cellular mechanism of cholera toxin, and what is the primary emergency treatment alongside single-dose oral doxycycline 300 mg?","o":["Cholera toxin inhibits guanylyl cyclase; give oral loperamide 16 mg immediately","Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates Gs-alpha, constitutively activating adenylyl cyclase to raise enterocyte cAMP and drive massive CFTR Cl- and water secretion; immediate aggressive IV Ringer\u0027s lactate + WHO reduced-osmolarity ORS rehydration","Cholera toxin blocks enterocyte SGLT1 completely so ORS is futile; give IV furosemide","Vibrio cholerae invades colonic submucosa causing bloody mucoid dysentery; give IV metronidazole alone"],"a":1,"e":"Cholera toxin A-subunit permanently ADP-ribosylates the Gs-alpha regulatory subunit in intestinal crypt/villous cells, locking adenylyl cyclase active -\u003e high intracellular cAMP -\u003e massive apical CFTR chloride secretion and passive Na+/water efflux (\u0027rice-water\u0027 stools). Crucially, Na+-glucose cotransport (SGLT1) remains completely intact, making rapid IV Ringer\u0027s lactate (for severe shock) + oral WHO ORS life-saving, while single-dose doxycycline 300 mg (or azithromycin in children/pregnancy) halves stool volume and shedding duration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02216","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Succinylcholine \u0026 Malignant Hyperthermia","q":"Minutes after anaesthesia induction with halothane/sevoflurane and intravenous succinylcholine, a 22-year-old surgical patient develops masseter muscle rigidity, rapidly rising end-tidal CO2 (hypercarbia), tachycardia, generalised skeletal muscle stiffness, and fever climbing to 40.5 C (malignant hyperthermia). What genetic channel defect triggers this crisis, and what is the ONLY specific life-saving intravenous antidote?","o":["Excessive hepatic CYP2E1 induction; administer IV N-acetylcysteine","Blockade of presynaptic acetylcholine synthesis; administer IV neostigmine immediately","Gain-of-function mutation in skeletal muscle sarcoplasmic reticulum ryanodine receptor (RYR1) causing uncontrolled Ca2+ release and hypermetabolism; discontinue triggering agents immediately and administer IV Dantrolene sodium","IgE mast-cell degranulation exclusively; administer oral cetirizine"],"a":2,"e":"Malignant hyperthermia is a pharmacogenetic skeletal muscle crisis triggered by volatile halogenated anaesthetics and/or the depolarising neuromuscular blocker succinylcholine in patients with RYR1 (or CACNA1S) mutations. Uncontrolled sarcoplasmic reticulum Ca2+ dumping drives sustained contraction, massive ATP/O2 consumption, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia, lactic acidosis, and extreme hyperthermia. Immediately halt triggers, hyperventilate with 100% O2, cool the patient, and give IV Dantrolene (2.5 mg/kg bolus repeated as needed), which directly binds RYR1 to block Ca2+ release.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02217","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Teratogenicity of Antiseizure Medications in Pregnancy","q":"A 23-year-old woman with newly diagnosed generalised epilepsy is planning pregnancy within the next year. Which antiseizure medication carries the HIGHEST risk of major congenital malformations (neural tube defects such as spina bifida, craniofacial defects, and neurodevelopmental IQ impairment) and should be AVOIDED in women of childbearing potential unless alternatives fail?","o":["Folic acid 5 mg supplementation","Lamotrigine","Levetiracetam","Sodium valproate (valproic acid)"],"a":3,"e":"Large pregnancy registries (EURAP/NAAPR) consistently show sodium valproate carries the highest major congenital malformation risk (~8-11%, notably neural tube defects/spina bifida, hypospadias, cardiac defects) plus up to 30-40% risk of neurodevelopmental/autism-spectrum impairment in exposed offspring. Therefore CDSCO/EMA/MHRA strongly advise avoiding valproate in females of childbearing potential in favour of safer options like lamotrigine or levetiracetam (with preconception folic acid).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02218","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Sucralfate Mucosal Cytoprotection","q":"Sucralfate (basic aluminium salt of sucrose octasulfate) protects peptic ulcer craters locally without systemic absorption. Why MUST sucralfate be taken on an empty stomach and separated from antacids, H2 blockers, or proton pump inhibitors (as well as fluoroquinolones/digoxin/phenytoin)?","o":["Sucralfate requires gastric acid (pH \u003c 4) to polymerise into a viscous negatively charged adhesive gel binding positively charged proteins on the ulcer base (antacids/PPIs raise pH \u003e 4 preventing activation), and its aluminium gel adsorbs/chelates co-administered drugs blocking their absorption","Sucralfate increases gastric acid secretion tenfold unless neutralised by PPIs","Sucralfate is an enteric-coated prodrug requiring alkaline duodenal bile at pH 8 to dissolve","Sucralfate converts into omeprazole only when mixed with aluminium hydroxide antacids"],"a":0,"e":"At gastric pH \u003c 4, sucralfate loses aluminium ions and cross-links into a sticky, negatively charged polyanionic paste that electrostatically adheres to positively charged necrotic tissue proteins at the ulcer crater for ~6 hours, shielding it from acid and pepsin while stimulating local prostaglandins. Co-administering antacids or PPIs raises gastric pH \u003e 4 and prevents sucralfate gel activation; furthermore, sucralfate binds fluoroquinolones, tetracyclines, phenytoin, warfarin, and digoxin in the gut—requiring a 2-hour separation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02219","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy and Management","topic":"NSAID Risk Counselling in Peptic Ulcer \u0026 CKD Patients","q":"At a community pharmacy counter, a 66-year-old man with Stage 3 Chronic Kidney Disease (eGFR 42 mL/min) and a history of duodenal ulcer bleeding asks for OTC oral diclofenac 50 mg TID for knee osteoarthritis pain. Which safe triage and counselling response should the pharmacist provide?","o":["Recommend oral aspirin 650 mg QID because salicylates heal duodenal ulcers","Advise AGAINST oral systemic NSAIDs (diclofenac/ibuprofen) because COX-1/COX-2 prostaglandin inhibition risks recurrent GI haemorrhage and acute-on-chronic renal failure; suggest topical diclofenac gel or low-dose paracetamol (within \u003c=3 g/day limit) with physician follow-up","Dispense oral ketorolac 10 mg QID for 30 days as a \u0027kidney-safe\u0027 alternative","Dispense diclofenac 50 mg TID plus ibuprofen 400 mg TID together for additive pain relief"],"a":1,"e":"Oral NSAIDs inhibit gastroprotective PGE2/PGI2 (precipitating recurrent GI ulcer bleeding) and block renal vasodilatory prostaglandins that maintain afferent glomerular perfusion in CKD, precipitating acute-on-chronic kidney injury and fluid overload. Topical NSAIDs (e.g., diclofenac gel, achieving high local joint concentration with minimal systemic exposure) and judicious paracetamol (avoiding hepatotoxic excess) alongside non-pharmacological physiotherapy and physician review constitute safe pharmacist triage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02220","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy and Management","topic":"Counter-Sale Requests for Antibiotics in Viral URTIs","q":"A young adult requests a 3-day strip of azithromycin 500 mg over the counter WITHOUT a prescription for a 2-day history of clear watery rhinorrhoea, sneezing, and mild scratchy throat (afebrile, no purulent tonsillar exudate). What is the pharmacist\u0027s legally and clinically sound action?","o":["Dispense azithromycin 500 mg without prescription to maintain customer loyalty","Advise taking a single 250 mg ciprofloxacin tablet prophylactically","Decline OTC antibiotic sale (citing Schedule H/H1 prescription law and viral aetiology of the common cold), explain that antibiotics do not cure viral infections and fuel antimicrobial resistance (AMR), and offer symptomatic relief (warm saline gargles, hydration, paracetamol/antihistamine)","Dispense oral amoxicillin-clavulanate instead because penicillins treat rhinovirus faster"],"a":2,"e":"Acute watery rhinorrhoea/coryza without fever or bacterial criteria is overwhelmingly viral (\u003e90%, rhinovirus/coronavirus). Selling antibiotics without a valid RMP prescription violates Rules 65/97 (Schedules H/H1) of the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 and accelerates India\u0027s antimicrobial resistance crisis (macrolide-resistant S. pneumoniae and Mycoplasma). Community pharmacists play a frontline AMR stewardship role by refusing non-prescription antibiotics and offering safe symptomatic care and red-flag return advice.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02221","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Hospital and Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Concentrated Potassium Chloride (KCl) Ward-Stock Removal","q":"Under NABH Accreditation Standards and WHO/ISMP High-Alert Medication safety practices in Indian hospitals, why MUST concentrated Potassium Chloride (KCl, e.g. 15% w/v 10 mL ampoules) be removed from general inpatient ward floor stock?","o":["Because concentrated KCl evaporates rapidly into explosive potassium vapour","Because concentrated KCl freezes solid at room temperature in ward refrigerators","Because KCl is completely ineffective intravenously and only works topically","Because accidental intravenous push of undiluted concentrated KCl causes immediate fatal hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest (asystole/VF); pharmacies must instead supply commercially premixed diluted KCl IV bags or prepare dilute infusions centrally"],"a":3,"e":"Concentrated KCl (2 mEq/mL) ampoules closely resemble sterile water or 0.9% NaCl ampoules in ward drawers; inadvertent undiluted IV bolus injection spikes extracellular cardiac K+, depolarising resting membrane potential and causing rapid lethal cardiac arrest. Consequently, NABH and WHO High-Alert Medication protocols prohibit stocking concentrated KCl vials on general nursing units (restricting them to central pharmacy compounding/sealed ICU emergency boxes with high-alert auxiliary labels) and favour ready-to-use premixed KCl infusions (e.g., 20-40 mEq/L).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02222","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Hypertensive Emergency vs Hypertensive Urgency","q":"A 57-year-old man presents with BP 228/132 mmHg, severe headache, confusion, blurred vision, and bilateral papilloedema on fundoscopy (hypertensive encephalopathy — Hypertensive Emergency). What is the safe BP reduction target in the first hour, and why MUST punctured sublingual nifedipine capsules be strictly avoided?","o":["Use titratable IV antihypertensives (e.g., IV labetalol or nicardipine) to lower Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) by no more than 20-25% in the first 1-2 hours; sublingual nifedipine causes abrupt, uncontrollable BP plunges precipitating cerebral ischaemia, stroke, or myocardial infarction","Give IV epinephrine infusion to stabilise cerebral perfusion","Drop BP instantaneously to 110/70 mmHg within 5 minutes using sublingual nifedipine capsules","Administer oral hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg and send the patient home for 1 week"],"a":0,"e":"Hypertensive Emergency (markedly elevated BP \u003e180/120 mmHg WITH acute end-organ damage: encephalopathy, papilloedema, acute pulmonary oedema, ACS, aortic dissection, AKI) requires ICU admission and controlled parenteral titration (IV labetalol, nicardipine, esmolol, or clevidipine/nitroglycerin). In chronic hypertensives, cerebral autoregulation shifts rightward, so lowering MAP too rapidly (\u003e25% in hour 1) starves the brain of perfusion—which is why erratic, non-titratable sublingual immediate-release nifedipine is banned.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02223","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Outpatient Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP) — ICMR STG","q":"A previously healthy 32-year-old outpatient in India presents with 4 days of high fever, productive rust-coloured cough, pleuritic chest pain, and right lower-zone consolidation on chest X-ray (CURB-65 score = 0). Per ICMR Antimicrobial Guidelines, what oral antibiotic is preferred over empiric levofloxacin/moxifloxacin, and why are respiratory fluoroquinolones avoided as routine first-line CAP therapy in India?","o":["Oral Gentamicin capsules; fluoroquinolones cause dental fluorosis in adults","Oral Amoxicillin (or Amoxicillin-clavulanate +/- Macrolide/Doxycycline if atypical coverage needed) for 5-7 days; respiratory fluoroquinolones (levofloxacin/moxifloxacin) are core NTEP MDR-TB drugs whose empirical use delays/masks pulmonary tuberculosis diagnosis and promotes fluoroquinolone-resistant TB","Oral Vancomycin monotherapy; fluoroquinolones cause immediate anaphylaxis in all Indians","Oral Metronidazole 400 mg TID; fluoroquinolones have zero activity against Gram-negative rods"],"a":1,"e":"In a high-TB-burden country like India, ICMR and NTEP strongly caution against routine empiric use of respiratory fluoroquinolones (levofloxacin, moxifloxacin) for mild outpatient CAP/respiratory infections: fluoroquinolones temporarily suppress M. tuberculosis symptoms (delaying TB diagnosis) and select lethal fluoroquinolone resistance, compromising Group A MDR-TB regimens. High-dose oral amoxicillin (targeting S. pneumoniae) +/- macrolide/doxycycline (atypicals) is standard first-line outpatient CAP therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02224","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law and Ethics","topic":"Statutory Licence Forms for Retail, Wholesale \u0026 Manufacture","q":"Under the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 (India), match each statutory licence Form to its exact purpose: 1) Forms 20 \u0026 21, 2) Forms 20B \u0026 21B, 3) Form 20F, 4) Forms 25 \u0026 28.","o":["1-Hospital clinical trial permission; 2-Pharmacist registration; 3- Narcotic opium cultivation; 4-Import of cosmetics","1-Manufacture of parenterals; 2-Retail sale of Schedule X; 3-Wholesale export; 4-Retail chemist non-C/C1","1-Retail sale of drugs (non-Schedule C/C1 in Form 20; Schedule C/C1 biologicals in Form 21); 2-Wholesale distribution (non-Schedule C/C1 in 20B; Schedule C/C1 in 21B); 3-Retail sale of Schedule X drugs; 4-Manufacture of drugs (non-C/C1 in Form 25; Schedule C/C1 biologicals/parenterals in Form 28)","1-Form 20F wholesale; 2-Form 25 retail; 3-Form 21 manufacture; 4-Form 20B clinical trial"],"a":2,"e":"Under Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945: Retail licences are issued in Form 20 (general/H/H1 drugs excluding C/C1/X) and Form 21 (Schedule C/C1 biologicals/sera/vaccines/antibiotics); Wholesale licences in Form 20B and 21B; Schedule X retail in Form 20F (wholesale in 20G); Manufacturing licences in Form 25 (general drugs) and Form 28 (Schedule C/C1 biologicals \u0026 parenterals, with Form 28B for large-volume parenterals/sera/rDNA).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02225","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law and Ethics","topic":"Essential Narcotic Drugs (ENDs) for Palliative Care","q":"The 2014 Amendment to India\u0027s Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances (NDPS) Act, 1985 created a uniform national category called \u0027Essential Narcotic Drugs\u0027 (ENDs) to improve palliative pain relief for cancer patients through Recognised Medical Institutions (RMIs). Which list comprises the six notified ENDs in India?","o":["Aspirin, Paracetamol, Ibuprofen, Diclofenac, Tramadol, and Ketorolac","Heroin (diacetylmorphine), Cocaine, LSD, MDMA, Psilocybin, and Ketamine","Diazepam, Alprazolam, Zolpidem, Phenobarbital, Midazolam, and Lorazepam","Morphine, Fentanyl, Methadone, Oxycodone, Codeine, and Hydrocodone"],"a":3,"e":"Before 2014, fragmented state-by-state NDPS quota/transport licences severely restricted oral morphine for terminal cancer pain in India. The NDPS (Amendment) Act, 2014 empowered the Central Government to notify six Essential Narcotic Drugs (ENDs)—Morphine, Fentanyl, Methadone, Oxycodone, Codeine, and Hydrocodone—under unified nationwide rules permitting Recognised Medical Institutions (RMIs) to stock and dispense them for legitimate pain and palliative care.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02226","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Official Sieve Standards (IP)","q":"In the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) classification of powders by sieve size, what does a \u0027Sieve Number\u0027 represent, and which sieve pair defines a \u0027Moderately Fine Powder\u0027?","o":["Number of meshes per 25.4 mm (1 inch) linear length; all particles pass through Sieve No. 44 and not more than 40% pass through Sieve No. 85","Number of wires per square centimeter; all particles pass through Sieve No. 22 and not more than 40% through Sieve No. 60","Diameter of each square aperture in millimeters; all particles pass through Sieve No. 10 and not more than 40% through Sieve No. 22","Percentage of powder retained on a 100-mesh screen; all particles pass through Sieve No. 120"],"a":0,"e":"Under the Indian Pharmacopoeia, a sieve number indicates the number of meshes in a length of 25.4 mm (1 inch) in each transverse direction parallel to the wires. Official IP powder grades specify upper and lower sieve limits: Coarse (10/44), Moderately Coarse (22/60), Moderately Fine (44/85 — all pass No. 44, \u003c= 40% pass No. 85), Fine (all pass No. 85), and Very Fine (all pass No. 120).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02227","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Sterile Filtration and Integrity Testing","q":"A heat-labile vitamin B-complex parenteral solution cannot withstand autoclaving and is sterilized by aseptic filtration. Which membrane filter pore size is standard for bacterial sterilization, and which non-destructive test confirms its integrity before and after filtration?","o":["0.45 µm pore size cellulose filter; validated by the Friability test","0.22 µm pore size membrane filter; validated by the Bubble Point test (or forward flow/diffusion test)","5.0 µm sintered glass G1 filter; validated by the Disintegration test","1.2 µm depth asbestos pad; validated by the Sedimentation volume test"],"a":1,"e":"Sterilizing-grade membrane filters (commonly PES, PVDF, or nylon) have a nominal pore rating of 0.22 µm (0.2 µm), capable of retaining Brevundimonas diminuta under standard bacterial challenge testing. Filter integrity before and after sterile filtration is verified non-destructively using the Bubble Point test, which measures the minimum gas pressure needed to displace wetting fluid from the largest pores—a drop in bubble point pressure indicates a torn membrane or oversized pore.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02228","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Syrups and Preservation","q":"According to the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP), Simple Syrup contains 66.7% w/w of sucrose in purified water. Why does Simple Syrup IP resist microbial fermentation without added chemical preservatives, whereas dilute sucrose solutions spoil rapidly?","o":["Sucrose undergoes spontaneous hydrolysis into bactericidal benzoic acid","66.7% w/w sucrose forms micelles that trap and lyse fungal spores","Near-saturation sucrose exerts high osmotic pressure leaving negligible free water (low water activity) to support microbial growth","Sucrose lowers the pH of purified water below 1.5, killing all bacteria and yeasts"],"a":2,"e":"Simple Syrup IP (66.7% w/w, or ~85% w/v USP) is nearly saturated with sucrose at room temperature, leaving virtually no free unbound water (very low water activity, aw) for bacteria, yeasts, or molds to utilize. The high osmotic pressure plasmolyzes contaminating microorganisms. Conversely, dilute syrups contain excess free water and ferment rapidly unless an antimicrobial preservative (e.g., methylparaben/propylparaben or sodium benzoate) is added.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02229","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Limit Tests for Chloride and Sulfate (IP)","q":"In the Indian Pharmacopoeia limit test for chlorides, dilute nitric acid is added prior to silver nitrate, whereas in the limit test for sulfates, ethanolic potassium sulfate (seeding reagent) is added to the barium chloride reagent. What are their respective chemical purposes?","o":["Dilute HNO3 chelates iron impurities; ethanolic K2SO4 prevents lead sulfide blackening","Dilute HNO3 reduces Ag+ to metallic silver; ethanolic K2SO4 dissolves BaSO4 completely","Dilute HNO3 neutralizes Cl- to HCl gas; ethanolic K2SO4 acts as a pH 10 buffer","Dilute HNO3 prevents precipitation of interfering silver salts (e.g., carbonate/phosphate) and makes AgCl insoluble; ethanolic K2SO4 provides fine BaSO4 nuclei for uniform turbidity and slows supersaturation"],"a":3,"e":"In the IP chloride limit test, dilute nitric acid acidifies the medium to dissolve interfering anions (carbonates, phosphates, oxalates) that would otherwise precipitate with AgNO3, while ensuring AgCl remains insoluble and yields uniform opalescence. In the IP sulfate limit test, the barium sulfate reagent contains BaCl2, acetic acid, and alcohol with a trace of potassium sulfate (seeding reagent): ethanol reduces BaSO4 solubility/prevents supersaturation while trace K2SO4 seeds uniform microcrystalline BaSO4 turbidity for reproducible visual comparison.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02230","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Redox Titrations","q":"In an iodometric assay (such as assay of copper sulfate or sodium hypochlorite) where liberated iodine (I2) is titrated with standardized sodium thiosulfate (Na2S2O3), why is freshly prepared starch mucilage indicator added only near the endpoint (when the solution turns pale straw-yellow) rather than at the beginning of the titration?","o":["High iodine concentrations form an irreversible water-insoluble dark blue starch-iodine complex, causing sluggish or inaccurate endpoints","Iodine oxidizes starch into glucose only when the solution is pale straw-yellow","Starch reacts with excess potassium iodide to precipitate insoluble potassium starchate","Starch instantly reduces thiosulfate into elemental sulfur if added early"],"a":0,"e":"In indirect iodometric titrations, excess iodide reduces the oxidizing analyte to liberate stoichiometric I2, which is titrated with Na2S2O3 (forming tetrathionate S4O6^2-). If starch is added at the start when free [I2/I3-] is high, iodine binds tightly within the amylose helix to form a sparingly dissociable blue complex that resists rapid reaction with thiosulfate, causing a dragged-out/over-shot endpoint. Adding starch just before equivalence—when the solution fades to pale yellow—ensures a sharp blue-to-colorless transition.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02231","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Dental Products and Antacids","q":"How does topical sodium fluoride (NaF) protect dental enamel against acid-mediated caries, and why is magnesium hydroxide commonly combined with aluminum hydroxide gel in oral antacid suspensions?","o":["Fluoride replaces hydroxyl ions in hydroxyapatite to form harder, acid-resistant fluorapatite; laxative Mg(OH)2 offsets constipating Al(OH)3","Fluoride dissolves enamel calcium completely; Mg(OH)2 slows gastric emptying permanently","Fluoride buffers saliva to pH 2.0; Al(OH)3 and Mg(OH)2 both cause severe osmotic diarrhea synergistically","Fluoride chelates dietary sucrose; Mg(OH)2 inhibits H2-histamine receptors"],"a":0,"e":"Topical and systemic fluoride ions exchange with OH- groups in dental enamel hydroxyapatite [Ca10(PO4)6(OH)2] to yield fluorapatite [Ca10(PO4)6F2], which possesses lower acid solubility and enhances remineralization while inhibiting bacterial enolase. In antacid formulations, aluminum hydroxide is slow-acting and causes constipation, whereas magnesium hydroxide acts rapidly and produces osmotic diarrhea; combining them provides balanced acid neutralization with minimal bowel disturbance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02232","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Analytical Constants of Fixed Oils","q":"Which analytical constant of a pharmaceutical fixed oil (such as castor oil, arachis oil, or linseed oil) quantifies the degree of unsaturation (number of double bonds) in its constituent fatty acids, expressed as grams of iodine absorbed per 100 g of fat?","o":["Acid value","Iodine value","Saponification value","Ester value"],"a":1,"e":"The Iodine value (determined by Wijs or Hanus method) is defined as the number of grams of iodine absorbed by 100 g of fat or fixed oil under specified conditions, directly measuring fatty acid unsaturation (drying oils like linseed oil have high iodine values \u003e 150; non-drying oils like olive/castor oil have values \u003c 90).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02233","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Identification of True Tannins","q":"A pharmacy student immerses a strip of ox-intestine membrane (Goldbeater\u0027s skin) in 2% HCl, rinses it, places it in a dilute aqueous extract of Pale Catechu (Uncaria gambir) for 5 minutes, washes it, and treats it with 1% ferrous sulfate solution, observing a dark brown-black stain on the membrane. What does this Goldbeater\u0027s skin test specifically confirm?","o":["Presence of neutral mucilage polysaccharides like acacia","Presence of simple low-molecular-weight pseudotannins such as chlorogenic acid alone","Presence of true tannins (molecular weight 500–3,000 Da capable of tanning animal hide protein into leather)","Presence of volatile phenylpropanoids like eugenol"],"a":2,"e":"True tannins (hydrolyzable and condensed tannins, MW ~500–3,000 Da) possess sufficient polyphenolic groups to cross-link collagen proteins in animal skin/membrane (Goldbeater\u0027s skin, prepared from ox intestine) and resist rinsing, forming a dark brown/black ferric-tannate stain upon immersion in 1% FeSO4. Pseudotannins (e.g., gallic acid, chlorogenic acid, simple catechins) have lower molecular weight and fail to tan Goldbeater\u0027s skin permanently.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02234","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy \u0026 Physiology","topic":"Excitation-Contraction Coupling","q":"During skeletal muscle contraction, calcium ions released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum bind directly to which regulatory protein on the thin filament to expose myosin-binding sites on actin?","o":["Myosin light-chain kinase","Dystrophin","Tropomyosin directly without troponin","Troponin C"],"a":3,"e":"Action potential propagation down T-tubules triggers Ca2+ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum terminal cisternae via ryanodine receptors. Released Ca2+ binds specifically to the Troponin C (TnC) subunit of the troponin complex on thin filaments, inducing a conformational shift that pulls tropomyosin away from the active myosin-binding sites on F-actin so cross-bridge cycling can proceed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02235","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy \u0026 Physiology","topic":"Coagulation Cascade and Vitamin K","q":"Vitamin K acts as an essential cofactor for hepatic gamma-glutamyl carboxylase to activate which exact set of procoagulant clotting factors?","o":["Factors II (prothrombin), VII, IX, and X","Factors XI, XII, prekallikrein, and HMWK","Von Willebrand factor and Factor III (tissue factor)","Factors I (fibrinogen), V, VIII, and XIII"],"a":0,"e":"Reduced vitamin K (hydroquinone) serves as a cofactor for hepatic gamma-glutamyl carboxylase, which adds a second carboxyl group to N-terminal glutamic acid residues (forming Gla domains) on procoagulant Factors II, VII, IX, and X (as well as anticoagulant Proteins C and S). These negatively charged Gla domains chelate Ca2+ ions to anchor clotting factors onto platelet phospholipid membranes; warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKOR), halting this activation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02236","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Biomedical Waste Management Rules, 2016 (India)","q":"Under India\u0027s Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules, 2016 (as amended), into which color-coded container should discarded/expired cytotoxic or general medicines, soiled cotton dressings, and contaminated plastic IV tubing (without needles) be segregated, respectively?","o":["Red bag for expired cytotoxic medicines; Yellow bag for unbroken glass ampoules","Yellow bag for expired/discarded medicines and soiled cotton dressings; Red bag for contaminated recyclable plastic IV tubing","Black general municipal bin for all soiled dressings and expired antibiotics","Blue puncture-proof box for soiled cotton dressings; White translucent container for expired medicines"],"a":1,"e":"Under India\u0027s BMW Management Rules, 2016: Yellow bags/containers receive human/animal anatomical waste, soiled blood-contaminated cotton/dressings, and discarded/expired or cytotoxic medicines (for incineration/plasma pyrolysis); Red bags receive contaminated recyclable plastics (IV sets, catheters, syringes without fixed needles, urine bags); White translucent puncture-proof containers receive sharps/needles/scalpels; Blue puncture-proof boxes receive glassware and metallic implants.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02237","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Vector-Borne Diseases in India","q":"Which arthropod vector transmits Dengue and Chikungunya in India, characteristically bites during daytime hours, and breeds predominantly in clean, stagnant domestic water containers?","o":["Female Anopheles culicifacies","Culex quinquefasciatus","Aedes aegypti","Phlebotomus argentipes"],"a":2,"e":"Aedes aegypti (the tiger mosquito, recognized by white stripes on its legs and thorax) is the primary vector for Dengue, Chikungunya, Zika, and Yellow Fever. Unlike Anopheles and Culex, Aedes aegypti is an aggressive daytime biter that breeds in small clean domestic water collections (desert coolers, flower pots, overhead tanks, discarded tires).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02238","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Parenteral Anticoagulants","q":"A hospitalized patient receiving an intravenous infusion of unfractionated heparin (UFH) for massive pulmonary embolism develops retroperitoneal hemorrhage with an aPTT \u003e 120 seconds. Which antidote rapidly neutralizes UFH, and by what chemical mechanism?","o":["Vitamin K1 (phytonadione); restores hepatic carboxylation of Factors II, VII, IX, and X within 5 minutes","Tranexamic acid; blocks antithrombin III directly","Idarucizumab; monoclonal antibody fragment binding heparin chains","Protamine sulfate; positively charged basic peptide binds electrostatically to negatively charged acidic heparin to form an inactive stable 1:1 complex"],"a":3,"e":"Unfractionated heparin is a strongly anionic sulfated mucopolysaccharide that accelerates antithrombin III inhibition of thrombin (IIa) and Factor Xa (~1000-fold). Protamine sulfate is a strongly basic (arginine-rich, cationic) low-molecular-weight protein that binds UFH ionically (1 mg protamine neutralizes ~100 units of UFH), instantaneously abolishing anticoagulant activity (administered slowly IV to avoid hypotension/anaphylactoid reactions).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02239","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Oral Antidiabetic Agents","q":"A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes taking glimepiride and acarbose (an alpha-glucosidase inhibitor) experiences an episode of symptomatic hypoglycemia (blood glucose 52 mg/dL). Why must he be treated with pure glucose/dextrose tablets rather than ordinary cane sugar (sucrose) or fruit juice rich in sucrose?","o":["Acarbose inhibits intestinal brush-border sucrase and alpha-glucosidases, blocking hydrolysis of sucrose into absorbable glucose and fructose, whereas pure glucose is absorbed directly","Sucrose reacts with acarbose in the stomach to form toxic lactic acid","Acarbose covalently destroys sodium-glucose cotransporters unless high-dose sucrose is co-ingested","Pure glucose stimulates endogenous insulin release less effectively than sucrose"],"a":0,"e":"Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors (acarbose, voglibose, miglitol) competitively inhibit intestinal brush-border enzymes (maltase, isomaltase, sucrase, glucoamylase) that cleave complex carbohydrates and disaccharides (including sucrose) into monosaccharides. While acarbose does not cause hypoglycemia alone, when combined with insulin or sulfonylureas (glimepiride), hypoglycemia must be corrected with oral glucose/dextrose (monosaccharide absorbed directly via SGLT1) or IV dextrose—because sucrose absorption is delayed by sucrase inhibition.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02240","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Broad-Spectrum Antibiotics","q":"Unlike oxytetracycline or demeclocycline, why is doxycycline generally safe without dose adjustment in patients with severe renal insufficiency, yet still avoided in pregnant women and children under 8 years of age?","o":["Doxycycline is 100% cleared by alveolar exhalation, and causes fetal kernicterus by displacing bilirubin","Doxycycline is excreted predominantly via biliary/intestinal fecal elimination rather than kidneys, but still chelates calcium in developing fetal/pediatric teeth and bone","Doxycycline undergoes rapid hydrolysis in renal tubules, and causes gray baby syndrome in children","Doxycycline is converted to nephrotoxic epianhydrotetracycline in pediatric liver"],"a":1,"e":"Most tetracyclines rely heavily on glomerular filtration and accumulate in renal failure (worsening azotemia). Doxycycline (and minocycline) is lipophilic and eliminated largely unchanged via bile and direct intestinal secretion into feces, requiring no renal dose adjustment. However, as a tetracycline, doxycycline binds Ca2+ in calcifying tissues—causing permanent yellow-brown tooth discoloration, enamel hypoplasia, and transient bone growth suppression in fetuses and children \u003c 8 years.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02241","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Auxiliary Warning Labels","q":"When dispensing an extemporaneously reconstituted amoxicillin oral pediatric suspension and an adult alprazolam tablet prescription, which respective auxiliary labels are mandatory on the containers?","o":["Amoxicillin suspension: \u0027For external use only\u0027; Alprazolam: \u0027Keep in freezer at -20 °C\u0027","Amoxicillin suspension: \u0027Stop immediately once fever subsides\u0027; Alprazolam: \u0027Take only with strong black coffee\u0027","Amoxicillin suspension: \u0027Shake well before use\u0027 \u0026 \u0027Store in refrigerator / Discard unused portion after 7–14 days\u0027; Alprazolam: \u0027May cause drowsiness; avoid alcohol and driving\u0027","Amoxicillin suspension: \u0027Chew thoroughly before swallowing\u0027; Alprazolam: \u0027Take with abundant grapefruit juice and alcohol\u0027"],"a":2,"e":"Reconstituted oral antibiotic suspensions contain settled insoluble particles that require thorough shaking before each measured dose for uniformity, and beta-lactam suspensions degrade hydrolytically over 7–14 days, requiring refrigerated storage (unless product-specific label states room temp) and discarding leftovers. Benzodiazepines (alprazolam) depress the CNS and synergize dangerously with ethanol, mandating warnings against driving, operating machinery, and alcohol intake.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02242","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Reorder Level and Safety Stock Calculations","q":"A pharmacy stocks a fast-moving antihypertensive strip with an average daily consumption of 40 strips, maximum daily consumption of 50 strips, average supplier lead time of 6 days, and maximum lead time of 10 days. What are the Reorder Level (ROL) and the Safety Stock?","o":["Reorder Level = 240 strips; Safety Stock = 500 strips","Reorder Level = 400 strips; Safety Stock = 160 strips","Reorder Level = 300 strips; Safety Stock = 60 strips","Reorder Level = 500 strips; Safety Stock = 260 strips"],"a":3,"e":"Reorder Level (ROL) is set to cover peak demand during the longest possible delivery delay: ROL = Maximum daily consumption × Maximum lead time = 50 strips/day × 10 days = 500 strips. Safety Stock (buffer stock) equals the excess held above expected average lead-time usage: Safety Stock = ROL - (Average daily consumption × Average lead time) = 500 - (40 × 6) = 500 - 240 = 260 strips.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02243","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Blood Pressure Measurement in Community Pharmacy","q":"During a community pharmacy hypertension screening camp, an obese adult (arm circumference 40 cm) has blood pressure measured using a standard small adult cuff (bladder width too narrow) while sitting with legs crossed and the arm hanging below heart level. How do these technical errors affect the recorded blood pressure reading?","o":["They produce a falsely elevated (overestimated) blood pressure reading","They produce a falsely low (underestimated) systolic reading by 20–30 mmHg","They eliminate Korotkoff sounds completely across all cuff pressures","They affect only the pulse rate without altering systolic or diastolic pressure"],"a":0,"e":"Standard AHA/ICMR BP measurement guidelines require resting quietly for 5 minutes, uncrossed feet flat on the floor, back supported, arm supported at right-atrial (heart) level, and a cuff bladder covering ~80% of arm circumference. Using an undersized cuff on a large arm (\u0027cuff hypertension\u0027), letting the arm hang below heart level (hydrostatic column effect), and crossing legs each artificially elevate recorded systolic and diastolic blood pressure readings.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02244","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Biochemistry \u0026 Clinical Pathology","topic":"Renal Function Tests (RFT)","q":"A 60-year-old man with severe gastroenteritis and dehydration presents with oliguria, BUN 48 mg/dL, serum creatinine 1.6 mg/dL (BUN:Cr ratio = 30:1), urine sodium 10 mEq/L, fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) 0.4%, and high urine osmolality (640 mOsm/kg) without casts. Which etiology of acute kidney injury do these laboratory findings indicate?","o":["Chronic end-stage diabetic glomerulosclerosis","Pre-renal azotemia due to renal hypoperfusion with intact tubular sodium and water reabsorption","Intrinsic acute tubular necrosis (ATN) with tubular epithelial sloughing","Post-renal bilateral ureteric obstruction with zero tubular concentrating ability"],"a":1,"e":"In volume depletion/hypoperfusion (pre-renal AKI), intact renal tubules respond to aldosterone and ADH by avidly reabsorbing sodium (urine Na+ \u003c 20 mEq/L, FENa \u003c 1%) and water (concentrated urine \u003e 500 mOsm/kg). Because urea follows passive proximal water reabsorption whereas creatinine is not reabsorbed, BUN rises disproportionately faster than creatinine, yielding a BUN:Cr ratio \u003e 20:1—rapidly reversible with isotonic fluid resuscitation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02245","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Biochemistry \u0026 Clinical Pathology","topic":"Lipid Profile Interpretation","q":"A fasting patient\u0027s serum lipid panel reports Total Cholesterol = 240 mg/dL, HDL-Cholesterol = 40 mg/dL, and Triglycerides = 200 mg/dL. Using the Friedewald formula, what is the calculated LDL-Cholesterol concentration (in mg/dL)?","o":["200 mg/dL","120 mg/dL","160 mg/dL","180 mg/dL"],"a":2,"e":"The Friedewald equation (valid for fasting samples with triglycerides \u003c 400 mg/dL) estimates VLDL-cholesterol as Triglycerides / 5 (in mg/dL): LDL-C = Total Cholesterol - HDL-C - (Triglycerides / 5). Substituting the values: LDL-C = 240 - 40 - (200 / 5) = 240 - 40 - 40 = 160 mg/dL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02246","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Rheumatoid Arthritis (DMARDs)","q":"A 46-year-old woman with active rheumatoid arthritis brings a new prescription for oral methotrexate 15 mg once weekly plus folic acid 5 mg once weekly (on a different day). During dispensing verification and counselling, why is confirming the \u0027once-weekly\u0027 schedule critical?","o":["Daily methotrexate dosing causes acute hyperkalemia within 12 hours","Folic acid must be omitted because it completely blocks methotrexate\u0027s anti-inflammatory action","Methotrexate has a 2-hour elimination half-life requiring daily dosing for efficacy","Inadvertent daily dosing of methotrexate for rheumatoid arthritis causes fatal pancytopenia, mucositis, and hepatotoxicity"],"a":3,"e":"Low-dose methotrexate (7.5–25 mg) for rheumatoid arthritis or psoriasis is strictly administered ONCE WEEKLY (all tablets on a single designated day of the week). Accidental daily administration is a classic high-alert \u0027never event\u0027 medication error that rapidly precipitates severe bone marrow suppression (pancytopenia/agranulocytosis), ulcerative stomatitis, GI bleeding, and hepatotoxicity. Co-prescribing folic acid (on non-methotrexate days) markedly lowers GI/hepatic/mucosal toxicity without reducing antirheumatic efficacy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02247","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Gout Management","q":"A 54-year-old man presents to the clinic with his first acute, severely painful, erythematous first metatarsophalangeal joint swelling (acute podagra) and serum uric acid 9.2 mg/dL. He is not currently on any urate-lowering therapy. What is the safest immediate pharmacotherapeutic approach?","o":["Treat the acute inflammation first with an NSAID (e.g., indomethacin/naproxen), low-dose colchicine, or a short corticosteroid course; do NOT initiate allopurinol alone during an acute flare","Start high-dose allopurinol 300 mg/day immediately as monotherapy without anti-inflammatory cover","Give intravenous calcium gluconate to dissolve monosodium urate crystals","Administer low-dose aspirin 75 mg/day to enhance renal urate excretion"],"a":0,"e":"Acute gout flares are driven by monosodium urate crystal-induced NLRP3 inflammasome/neutrophil inflammation and require rapid anti-inflammatory treatment with full-dose NSAIDs (if no CKD/PUD), low-dose colchicine (within 24–36 h), or systemic/intra-articular corticosteroids. Initiating urate-lowering therapy (allopurinol/febuxostat) during an acute flare without anti-inflammatory prophylaxis causes abrupt serum urate fluctuations that mobilize joint crystals and worsen/prolong the acute attack (conversely, if a patient is already taking chronic allopurinol, it should NOT be stopped during a flare).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02248","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Epilepsy in Women of Childbearing Potential","q":"A 23-year-old newly married woman planning pregnancy requires initiation of maintenance antiepileptic therapy. Why is sodium valproate (valproic acid) avoided as first-line therapy in women of childbearing potential whenever safer alternatives (such as levetiracetam or lamotrigine) are suitable?","o":["Valproate completely blocks placental glucose transport causing acute maternal hypoglycemia","Valproate carries the highest risk of major congenital malformations—particularly neural tube defects (spina bifida)—and impaired postnatal neurodevelopment","Valproate causes irreversible fetal deafness via eighth cranial nerve toxicity exclusively","Valproate induces CYP3A4 so strongly that it causes spontaneous maternal hepatic necrosis in 90% of pregnancies"],"a":1,"e":"Sodium valproate is the most teratogenic antiepileptic drug (~10% major congenital malformation rate and up to 30–40% risk of neurodevelopmental/autism-spectrum/IQ deficits), characteristically causing neural tube defects (spina bifida, meningomyelocele), cleft palate, and hypospadias. Regulatory agencies (CDSCO, MHRA, EMA, FDA) restrict valproate use in women of childbearing potential unless alternatives fail and strict pregnancy-prevention + high-dose folic acid protocols are in place; lamotrigine or levetiracetam carry substantially lower fetal risk.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02249","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law \u0026 Ethics","topic":"NPPA and Essential Medicines Pricing","q":"Under India\u0027s Drugs (Prices Control) Order (DPCO), 2013 issued under the Essential Commodities Act, 1955, which authority fixes and monitors the ceiling prices of scheduled formulations listed in the National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM)?","o":["Indian Pharmacopoeia Commission (IPC)","Pharmacy Council of India (PCI)","National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority (NPPA)","Central Drugs Research Institute (CDRI)"],"a":2,"e":"The National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority (NPPA), under the Department of Pharmaceuticals (Ministry of Chemicals and Fertilizers), enforces DPCO 2013—calculating ceiling prices of scheduled NLEM formulations using market-based pricing (simple average price of brands holding \u003e= 1% market share plus 16% retailer margin) and monitoring overcharging and non-scheduled annual price cap limits (10%).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02250","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law \u0026 Ethics","topic":"Control of Narcotics and Essential Narcotic Drugs","q":"Under the Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances (NDPS) Act, 1985 (as amended in 2014 in India), why were six opioids—including morphine, fentanyl, methadone, oxycodone, codeine, and hydrocodone—designated as \u0027Essential Narcotic Drugs (ENDs)\u0027?","o":["To permit unrestricted poppy cultivation without government supervision","To allow over-the-counter sale of oral morphine without a prescription in all retail shops","To reclassify opioids as herbal food supplements exempt from record-keeping","To create a uniform central licensing/stocking framework for Recognized Medical Institutions (RMIs) improving medical access for cancer pain and palliative care while preventing illicit diversion"],"a":3,"e":"Before the 2014 NDPS Amendment, fragmented state-level multi-license rules severely restricted oral morphine availability for terminal cancer pain. Designating six Essential Narcotic Drugs (ENDs) shifted regulatory authority to uniform Central Government rules allowing Recognized Medical Institutions (RMIs) to procure, stock, and dispense palliative opioids through a streamlined single-window State Drug Controller approval with strict patient/stock registers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02251","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Elixirs vs Linctuses vs Tinctures","q":"In extemporaneous compounding of monophasic oral liquids, what distinguishes an Elixir from a Linctus regarding vehicle composition and patient administration?","o":["An Elixir is a clear, sweetened, flavoured hydro-alcoholic solution (requiring careful alcohol-water balance so neither alcohol-soluble actives nor sucrose precipitate); a Linctus is a viscous demulcent cough liquid sipped slowly undiluted without water to soothe throat mucosa","An Elixir is a solid compressed lozenge; a Linctus is an intravenous large-volume parenteral","An Elixir must always contain 95% pure ethanol with zero water; a Linctus must be diluted in 500 mL boiling water before swallowing","Both Elixirs and Linctuses are strictly external liniments applied with friction"],"a":0,"e":"Elixirs are clear, sweetened, aromatic hydro-alcoholic liquids (~4-40% alcohol + glycerin/sorbitol/syrup) formulated to solubilise both water- and alcohol-soluble actives; adding strong aqueous syrup carelessly precipitates hydrophobic actives, whereas excess alcohol precipitates sucrose/salts. Linctuses (e.g., Codeine Linctus) are thick, syrupy demulcent oral preparations containing honey/syrup/tolu balsam intended to be sipped slowly undiluted without chasing with water so the soothing film coats the inflamed pharynx.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02252","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) Disintegration Test for Enteric-Coated Tablets","q":"When performing the Indian Pharmacopoeia (IP) disintegration test on enteric-coated pantoprazole or diclofenac tablets, what two sequential immersion stages MUST all six tested tablets pass at 37 +/- 2 C?","o":["Remain intact with zero signs of disintegration, cracking, or softening in 0.1 M HCl (acid stage) for 2 hours, then disintegrate completely within 60 minutes in mixed phosphate buffer pH 6.8","Disintegrate completely within 15 minutes in 0.1 M HCl without using any buffer stage","Remain intact in pH 6.8 phosphate buffer for 4 hours and disintegrate only at pH 10.5","Disintegrate within 3 minutes in purified water at 20 C (standard effervescent test)"],"a":0,"e":"Enteric coatings (cellulose acetate phthalate, HPMCP, Eudragit L100) protect acid-labile drugs (PPIs, erythromycin) from gastric acid or shield stomach mucosa from irritating NSAIDs. Per IP, six enteric-coated tablets are tested in 0.1 M HCl for 2 hours (without discs) and must show no disintegration or cracks allowing contents to escape; the medium is then replaced with mixed phosphate buffer pH 6.8 (with discs), where all tablets must disintegrate within 60 minutes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02253","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Milliequivalent (mEq) Calculation","q":"A hospital parenteral nutrition order calls for 40 mEq of potassium chloride (KCl, molecular weight = 74.5 g/mol, valence = 1) added to a 1-L IV bag. How many milliequivalents (mEq) of K+ are contained in 20 mL of a 15% w/v KCl stock solution?","o":["20.1 mEq","40.3 mEq","80.5 mEq","15.0 mEq"],"a":1,"e":"15% w/v KCl equals 15 g per 100 mL = 150 mg/mL. Therefore, 20 mL contains 20 x 150 mg = 3,000 mg of KCl. Using mEq = [Weight (mg) x Valence] / Molecular Weight = (3,000 x 1) / 74.5 = 40.27 mEq (~40.3 mEq, confirming standard clinical ~2 mEq/mL concentration).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02254","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Iodimetry vs Iodometry (IP Assays)","q":"In pharmacopoeial redox assays, how does Iodimetry (used for Ascorbic Acid) differ from Iodometry (used for Copper Sulphate or available chlorine in Bleaching Powder), and why is starch indicator added ONLY near the endpoint in Iodometry?","o":["Iodimetry is direct titration of reducing analytes with standard I2 solution; Iodometry is indirect titration where an oxidising analyte liberates stoichiometric I2 from excess KI for titration against sodium thiosulfate—adding starch late prevents irreversible trapping of I2 inside amylose helices","Iodimetry uses sodium thiosulfate directly; Iodometry uses potassium permanganate without iodide","Starch is added at the very start of Iodometry to precipitate copper iodide immediately","Both iodimetry and iodometry are non-aqueous perchloric acid titrations"],"a":0,"e":"In Iodimetry, a reducing drug (e.g., ascorbic acid, sodium metabisulfite) is titrated directly with a standard 0.05 M I2 solution. In Iodometry, an oxidising agent (e.g., Cu2+, ClO-, Fe3+) reacts with excess potassium iodide in acid to liberate an equivalent amount of free I2, which is back-titrated with standard sodium thiosulfate (Na2S2O3). Adding starch mucilage early in high I2 concentrations forms an insoluble, sluggish starch-iodine complex causing dragged endpoints; waiting until the solution fades to pale straw-yellow ensures a sharp blue-to-colourless disappearance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02255","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Phenytoin vs Carbamazepine Adverse Effects \u0026 PK","q":"Which pair accurately contrasts the characteristic chronic adverse effect/metabolic profile of phenytoin (hydantoin class) versus carbamazepine (iminostilbene class)?","o":["Phenytoin causes gingival hyperplasia, hirsutism, coarse facial features, and folate-deficiency megaloblastic anaemia; carbamazepine exhibits CYP3A4 auto-induction (requiring dose escalation after 2-4 weeks) and SIADH-induced hyponatraemia","Phenytoin causes auto-induction and pure SIADH without gingival changes; carbamazepine causes severe tooth-gum overgrowth exclusively","Both drugs are excreted 100% unchanged by the kidneys with zero CYP450 enzyme induction","Phenytoin causes hyperkalaemia exclusively; carbamazepine causes severe gingival overgrowth in 90% of patients"],"a":0,"e":"Chronic phenytoin therapy classically causes gingival hyperplasia (overgrowth via fibroblast/PDGF upregulation, reduced by meticulous oral hygiene), hirsutism, coarsening of facial features, osteomalacia (vitamin D catabolism), and megaloblastic anaemia. Carbamazepine induces its own CYP3A4 metabolism (\u0027auto-induction\u0027) over the first 2-4 weeks—halving its initial t1/2 from ~30 h to ~12-17 h—and potentiates ADH/V2 action causing hyponatraemia (SIADH), diplopia, and ataxia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02256","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Cardiotonic, Antihypertensive, Expectorant \u0026 Antitussive Crude Drugs","q":"Match each official crude drug to its botanical origin, family, and therapeutic class: 1) Digitalis leaf, 2) Rauwolfia root, 3) Liquorice root, 4) Ipecacuanha root/rhizome.","o":["1-Papaver somniferum (analgesic); 2-Taxus brevifolia (anticancer); 3-Aloe vera (purgative); 4-Ephedra sinica (bronchodilator)","1-Atropa belladonna (mydriatic); 2-Cassia angustifolia (laxative); 3-Zingiber officinale (carminative); 4-Cinchona officinalis (antimalarial)","1-Digitalis purpurea (Scrophulariaceae/Plantaginaceae — cardiotonic); 2-Rauwolfia serpentina (Apocynaceae — antihypertensive/tranquilliser); 3-Glycyrrhiza glabra (Leguminosae — expectorant/demulcent); 4-Cephaelis ipecacuanha (Rubiaceae — emetic/amoebicide/expectorant)","1-Cephaelis ipecacuanha (cardiotonic); 2-Digitalis purpurea (emetic); 3-Rauwolfia serpentina (demulcent); 4-Glycyrrhiza glabra (antihypertensive)"],"a":2,"e":"Standard Indian Pharmacopoeia / PCI Pharmacognosy classifications: Digitalis purpurea (Scrophulariaceae/Plantaginaceae) yields cardenolide glycosides (digitoxin/digoxin); Rauwolfia serpentina (Apocynaceae) yields indole alkaloids (reserpine/ajmalicine); Glycyrrhiza glabra (Leguminosae) yields glycyrrhizin saponin (demulcent/expectorant); Cephaelis ipecacuanha (Rubiaceae) yields isoquinoline alkaloids (emetine/cephaeline).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02257","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Diagnostic Morphology of Pharmaceutical Starches","q":"Under light microscopy (x400) stained with dilute iodine, which microscopic starch granule morphology specifically differentiates Potato starch from Maize (corn) starch and Rice starch?","o":["All botanical starches display identical uniform 25-micron spherical granules","Potato starch shows tiny 2-micron compound spheres without striations; Maize starch shows large 100-micron eccentric oval shells","Rice starch shows giant lenticular grains with radial cracks; Wheat starch shows needle-shaped raphide bundles","Potato starch shows large, ovoid/pear-shaped simple granules with an eccentric point hilum and distinct concentric striations; Maize starch shows polyhedral/angular granules with a central stellate/radiate cleft hilum; Rice starch shows tiny polyhedral compound aggregates"],"a":3,"e":"Microscopic differentiation of official IP pharmaceutical starches: Potato starch (Solanum tuberosum) exhibits large (30-100 micron) ovoid granules with an eccentric hilum at the narrow end and clear shell-like striations; Maize starch (Zea mays) shows medium (10-30 micron) polyhedral/sub-spherical granules with a distinct 2-to-5-rayed star-shaped central hilum and no striations; Wheat starch (Triticum aestivum) shows bimodal large lenticular + small rounded grains; Rice starch (Oryza sativa) forms minute (2-10 micron) angular compound clusters.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02258","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy and Physiology","topic":"Autonomic Control of Pupil Diameter","q":"Which pair of autonomic receptors and iris smooth muscles governs pupillary constriction (miosis) versus pupillary dilation (mydriasis), explaining why topical phenylephrine dilates the pupil WITHOUT causing cycloplegia (loss of near accommodation)?","o":["Parasympathetic M3 muscarinic stimulation contracts the circular sphincter pupillae (miosis) and ciliary muscle (accommodation); sympathetic alpha-1 adrenergic stimulation contracts the radial dilator pupillae (mydriasis without ciliary muscle paralysis)","Alpha-1 stimulation contracts the circular sphincter (miosis); M3 stimulation contracts the radial muscle (mydriasis)","Histamine H1 contracts the lens directly while dopamine dilates the iris","Beta-2 stimulation contracts the sphincter pupillae; nicotinic receptors contract the cornea"],"a":0,"e":"Parasympathetic oculomotor (CN III) fibres release acetylcholine onto M3 receptors on both the circular iris sphincter (causing miosis) and the ciliary muscle (relaxing zonular tension for near-vision accommodation). Thus, muscarinic antagonists (atropine, tropicamide) cause BOTH mydriasis and cycloplegia. By contrast, sympathetic alpha-1 agonists (phenylephrine) contract the radial dilator pupillae to produce pure mydriasis while sparing ciliary M3 accommodation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02259","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Biochemistry and Clinical Pathology","topic":"Friedewald Equation for LDL-Cholesterol","q":"A fasting lipid profile returns: Total Cholesterol = 240 mg/dL, HDL-Cholesterol = 40 mg/dL, and Triglycerides (TG) = 200 mg/dL. Using the Friedewald equation (valid because TG \u003c 400 mg/dL), what is the calculated LDL-Cholesterol (mg/dL)?","o":["200 mg/dL","160 mg/dL","120 mg/dL","80 mg/dL"],"a":1,"e":"The Friedewald formula estimates VLDL-cholesterol as TG / 5 (in mg/dL, valid when fasting TG \u003c 400 mg/dL) and calculates: LDL-C = Total Cholesterol - HDL-C - (TG / 5) = 240 - 40 - (200 / 5) = 200 - 40 = 160 mg/dL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02260","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Multidrug Therapy (MDT) for Multibacillary Leprosy","q":"A 35-year-old man diagnosed with multibacillary (MB) leprosy (\u003e5 hypopigmented anaesthetic skin lesions and thickened ulnar nerves) is started on the 12-month NLEP/WHO Uniform Multidrug Therapy (MDT) blister pack. Which three antimicrobials compose adult MB-MDT, and what harmless reversible dermatological counselling is needed for clofazimine?","o":["Metronidazole + Albendazole + Chloroquine; counsel green sweat","Streptomycin + Gentamicin + Vancomycin; counsel irreversible hair regrowth","Rifampicin (600 mg monthly supervised) + Clofazimine (300 mg monthly + 50 mg daily) + Dapsone (100 mg daily); counsel that clofazimine causes reversible reddish-brown/mahogany skin discolouration and ichthyosis (while dapsone requires monitoring for haemolysis/methaemoglobinaemia)","Isoniazid + Pyrazinamide + Ethambutol; counsel permanent blue-grey teeth staining"],"a":2,"e":"Under NLEP (India) and WHO Uniform MDT guidelines, Multibacillary (MB) leprosy is treated for 12 months with Rifampicin 600 mg once monthly (potent bactericidal against M. leprae), Clofazimine 300 mg once monthly + 50 mg daily (bacteriostatic + anti-inflammatory against Type 2 ENL reaction), and Dapsone 100 mg daily. Warning patients beforehand about clofazimine\u0027s reversible brownish-red skin pigmentation prevents non-adherence; dapsone carries G6PD haemolysis, agranulocytosis, and dapsone hypersensitivity risk.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02261","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Misoprostol (PGE1 Analogue) — Gastroprotection \u0026 Pregnancy Contraindication","q":"A 30-year-old woman taking chronic naproxen for ankylosing spondylitis is evaluated for NSAID gastroprotection. Why MUST oral misoprostol (a prostaglandin E1 analogue) be strictly contraindicated if she is pregnant or not using reliable contraception, whereas a proton pump inhibitor (omeprazole) is preferred?","o":["Misoprostol causes severe irreversible constipation and maternal bradycardia","Omeprazole stimulates uterine contractions stronger than oxytocin","Misoprostol inhibits parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase irreversibly causing fetal hypothyroidism","Misoprostol stimulates powerful uterine smooth muscle contractions and cervical ripening, risking miscarriage, premature labour, or congenital Moebius sequence defects (and also frequently causes osmotic diarrhoea)"],"a":3,"e":"Misoprostol (methyl PGE1 analogue) protects NSAID-exposed gastric mucosa by suppressing basal acid secretion and stimulating mucus/bicarbonate production and mucosal blood flow. However, because PGE1 potently stimulates myometrial contraction and cervical ripening (clinically used in obstetric induction/medical termination of pregnancy/PPH), it is strictly contraindicated in pregnancy (risking abortion, uterine rupture, and Moebius vascular-disruption defects), and causes dose-limiting diarrhoea/abdominal cramps—making PPIs far better tolerated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02262","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Beta-Blocker + Non-DHP Calcium Channel Blocker Contraindication","q":"A 63-year-old man with chronic stable angina taking metoprolol succinate 50 mg/day still experiences exertional chest pain, and his physician considers adding a calcium channel blocker (CCB). Why is adding amlodipine (a long-acting dihydropyridine) safe and complementary, whereas adding verapamil or diltiazem (especially IV) is hazardous?","o":["Verapamil and diltiazem strongly depress SA/AV nodal conduction and myocardial contractility—combining them with a beta-blocker causes additive severe bradycardia, high-grade AV block, and decompensated heart failure; long-acting vascular-selective amlodipine lacks AV nodal depression while metoprolol blunts reflex tachycardia","Verapamil causes severe reflex tachycardia unless combined with high-dose atenolol","Amlodipine slows AV nodal conduction twice as strongly as verapamil","Metoprolol prevents oral absorption of verapamil completely inside the gut"],"a":0,"e":"Non-dihydropyridine CCBs (verapamil \u003e diltiazem) exhibit potent negative chronotropic, dromotropic (AV node), and inotropic effects. Co-administration with beta-blockers produces dangerous pharmacodynamic synergism leading to sinus arrest, complete heart block, and cardiogenic shock. Conversely, dihydropyridine CCBs (amlodipine, nifedipine) are predominantly arteriolar vasodilators with minimal cardiac nodal depression; pairing amlodipine with a beta-blocker improves coronary/peripheral vasodilation while the beta-blocker prevents reflex sympathetic tachycardia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02263","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Amphotericin B Mechanism \u0026 Toxicities","q":"A 47-year-old immunocompromised patient with rhino-orbital-cerebral mucormycosis receives intravenous conventional amphotericin B deoxycholate and develops rigors/fever during infusion, rising serum creatinine, hypokalaemia (K+ 2.8 mEq/L), and hypomagnesaemia. What is amphotericin B\u0027s antifungal mechanism, and how does switching to Liposomal Amphotericin B mitigate these toxicities?","o":["Inhibits fungal squalene epoxidase; Liposomal form doubles renal tubular toxicity","Binds fungal membrane ergosterol forming transmembrane aqueous pores that leak K+ and intracellular ions causing fungal cell death; Liposomal Amphotericin B targets ergosterol selectively while markedly reducing renal tubular cholesterol binding (lowering nephrotoxicity, electrolyte wasting, and infusion rigors)","Inhibits fungal mitotic spindle formation; Liposomal form turns urine orange","Inhibits fungal beta-1,3-glucan synthase; Liposomal form is taken orally with zero systemic absorption"],"a":1,"e":"Polyene macrolide amphotericin B binds fungal membrane ergosterol with high avidity (forming barrel-stave transmembrane pores leaking K+ and killing Mucorales, Aspergillus, Candida, and Leishmania), but cross-binds human cholesterol in renal tubular membranes causing afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction, distal RTA, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, anaemia, and cytokine-mediated infusion chills (\u0027shake and bake\u0027). Liposomal encapsulation preferentially delivers drug to fungal cell walls/MPS while shielding renal tubules, sharply cutting nephrotoxicity and infusion reactions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02264","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy and Management","topic":"Otic (Ear) Drops Instillation Technique","q":"During an OSCE counselling station, a community pharmacist demonstrates how to instil antibiotic ear drops into a 2-year-old toddler versus an adult patient. Which pinna-pulling direction and pre-instillation check are correct to straighten the external auditory canal and prevent dizziness?","o":["Use ice-cold drops straight from the refrigerator; pull the pinna downward and forward in adults","Pull the pinna upward and backward in a 2-year-old toddler, and insert the dropper tip deeply against the eardrum","Warm the bottle in the palm for 1-2 minutes first (cold drops trigger caloric vestibular vertigo); pull the pinna DOWNWARD and BACKWARD in a child \u003c 3 years, and UPWARD and BACKWARD in an adult","Pack dry cotton tightly inside the canal before dropping medication so drops stay outside"],"a":2,"e":"Instilling cold otic drops stimulates semicircular canal endolymph convection causing acute caloric vertigo/nausea—so bottles should always be warmed gently in the hands before use (never microwaved/boiled). Anatomically, in children under 3 years the cartilaginous external auditory canal slants upward/inward, so pulling the earlobe DOWNWARD and BACKWARD straightens it; in older children (\u003e3 years) and adults, pulling the upper pinna UPWARD and BACKWARD straightens the canal while keeping the dropper tip sterile above the meatus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02265","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy and Management","topic":"Nicotine Replacement Therapy (NRT) — \u0027Chew and Park\u0027 Technique","q":"A smoker motivated to quit tobacco asks how to use 2 mg nicotine polacrilex chewing gum dispensed at the pharmacy. Which counselling instruction (\u0027chew and park\u0027 technique) ensures proper buccal absorption and avoids hiccups, nausea, and therapeutic failure?","o":["Chew vigorously and continuously like regular bubble gum swallowing all saliva rapidly","Drink a cold glass of lemon juice or black coffee simultaneously to speed nicotine absorption","Swallow the entire gum piece whole with milk to avoid bad breath","Chew slowly until a peppery/tingling taste appears, then \u0027park\u0027 the gum between the cheek and gum for buccal mucosal absorption until the taste fades (repeating over ~30 min); avoid coffee, fruit juice, or soda for 15 minutes before and during use because acid impairs buccal nicotine uptake"],"a":3,"e":"Nicotine (a weak base) must remain unionised in slightly alkaline saliva to cross the buccal mucosa directly into systemic venous circulation (bypassing high hepatic first-pass metabolism). Rapid continuous chewing releases nicotine too fast, causing swallowing into the stomach—triggering GI cramping, hiccups, heartburn, and loss via hepatic first-pass inactivation. \u0027Chew and park\u0027 over 30 minutes maximises buccal absorption, while avoiding acidic beverages (coffee, cola, citrus juice) within 15 min prevents saliva acidification and ionisation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02266","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Hospital and Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Medication Reconciliation (BPMH)","q":"A 70-year-old man admitted for elective knee arthroplasty brings his home medication strips (atorvastatin 20 mg OD, metformin 500 mg BID, and levothyroxine 50 mcg OD), yet the inpatient medication chart lists only paracetamol, enoxaparin, and a newly added pantoprazole alongside an accidental duplicate omeprazole order. Which structured hospital pharmacy process prevents such unintended omissions and therapeutic duplications across admission, transfer, and discharge?","o":["Formal Medication Reconciliation comparing a Best Possible Medication History (BPMH from \u003e=2 reliable sources) against current inpatient/discharge orders and resolving discrepancies with the prescriber","Dispensing whichever drugs are cheapest in the ward floor stock without checking home strips","Replacing all home medications automatically with double-strength antibiotics","Waiting until 30 days after discharge to ask the patient what tablets he took"],"a":0,"e":"Transitions of care (admission, ICU-to-ward transfer, hospital discharge) account for \u003e50% of hospital medication errors. NABH and WHO High 5s Medication Reconciliation protocol mandates compiling a structured Best Possible Medication History (BPMH) verified via \u003e=2 sources (patient/caregiver interview, physical strips, prior prescriptions/pharmacy records), systematically comparing it against active orders to catch unintended omissions (e.g., levothyroxine), duplications (dual PPIs), or dose discrepancies, and resolving them immediately with the physician.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02267","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Acute Migraine Pharmacotherapy (Sumatriptan)","q":"A 54-year-old man with a history of coronary artery disease (prior inferior MI with stent) and poorly controlled hypertension presents with acute pulsating unilateral migraine headache, photophobia, and nausea. Why is oral or subcutaneous sumatriptan CONTRAINDICATED in this patient, and what alternative acute abortive therapy should be considered?","o":["Sumatriptan has zero vascular effects and is completely safe in recent myocardial infarction","Sumatriptan (5-HT1B/1D agonist) can trigger coronary vasospasm and acute myocardial ischaemia/hypertensive crisis; manage acutely with NSAIDs (e.g., naproxen/ibuprofen if GI/renal safe) or gepants/paracetamol + antiemetic","Sumatriptan induces acute bronchial asthma via beta-2 blockade; give oral aspirin + clopidogrel exclusively","Sumatriptan causes severe orthostatic hypotension and bradycardia; give IV ergotamine instead"],"a":1,"e":"Triptans (sumatriptan, rizatriptan, zolmitriptan) selectively activate 5-HT1B/1D receptors on intracranial meningeal blood vessels and trigeminal nerve endings (inhibiting CGRP/substance P release), but 5-HT1B receptors also mediate coronary artery vasoconstriction (~10-20% narrowing). Thus all triptans (and ergotamine/dihydroergotamine) are strictly contraindicated in ischaemic heart disease, Prinzmetal vasospastic angina, cerebrovascular disease, peripheral vascular disease, and uncontrolled hypertension.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02268","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Acute Exacerbation of COPD (GOLD Guidelines)","q":"A 68-year-old chronic smoker with severe COPD presents with acute worsening dyspnoea, increased sputum volume, and purulent yellow sputum (SpO2 84% on room air). Alongside nebulised salbutamol + ipratropium and targeted oral antibiotics (amoxicillin-clavulanate/doxycycline), what oxygen target and systemic corticosteroid duration are recommended by GOLD guidelines?","o":["Inhaled fluticasone monotherapy once weekly without bronchodilators","High-flow 100% non-rebreather oxygen targeting SpO2 100% PLUS oral prednisolone 60 mg/day for 6 months","Controlled supplemental oxygen titrated strictly to target SpO2 88-92% (avoiding excessive hyperoxia/CO2 retention) PLUS oral prednisolone 40 mg/day for a short 5-day course","Zero oxygen supplementation PLUS intravenous morphine 10 mg q4h"],"a":2,"e":"In acute COPD exacerbations (Type II hypercapnic respiratory failure risk), uncontrolled high-flow O2 driving SpO2 toward 100% worsens V/Q mismatch (abolishing hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction), suppresses hypoxic ventilatory drive, and induces Haldane-effect CO2 narcosis—so controlled Venturi/nasal O2 is titrated to SpO2 88-92% with ABG monitoring. Systemic corticosteroids (prednisolone 40 mg/day orally for 5 days, per REDUCE trial/GOLD) shorten recovery and prevent relapse as effectively as 14-day courses with far less hyperglycaemia/adrenal suppression.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02269","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law and Ethics","topic":"Statutory Duties \u0026 Code of Pharmaceutical Ethics","q":"Under the Pharmacy Practice Regulations, 2015 notified by the Pharmacy Council of India (PCI), which professional conduct rule binds every Registered Pharmacist in India?","o":["Pharmacists may substitute a completely different pharmacological class drug without informing the doctor or hospital policy","Pharmacists are prohibited from counselling patients on drug administration or side effects","Pharmacists may legally pay 20% secret commission to physicians for directing prescriptions","Pharmacists must maintain strict patient confidentiality, provide medication counselling/ADR monitoring, avoid clandestine arrangements/commissions with medical practitioners, and never alter prescribed ingredients without prescriber consent"],"a":3,"e":"PCI\u0027s Pharmacy Practice Regulations, 2015 codify clinical/community duties (patient medication profiles, counselling, ADR reporting, rational drug use) alongside ethical conduct: protecting patient confidentiality, prohibiting secret kickbacks/commissions or rebates with prescribers, banning misleading self-promotion, and strictly forbidding unauthorised omission or substitution of prescribed ingredients.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02270","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law and Ethics","topic":"Import Licences (Forms 10, 10A, and 11)","q":"Under Part IV of the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945 (India), which statutory Import Licence Form issued by the Central Licensing Authority (CDSCO) permits an institution or laboratory to import small quantities of non-approved or investigational drugs solely for examination, test, or analysis (NOT for commercial sale)?","o":["Form 11 (Test Licence)","Form 20B (Wholesale licence)","Form 10 (Commercial import licence for Schedule C/C1 \u0026 general drugs)","Form 25 (Domestic manufacturing licence)"],"a":0,"e":"Under Rule 33-35 of the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945: Form 11 is the dedicated Test Licence issued by CDSCO authorising import of small quantities of drugs (including new drugs/reference standards) strictly for examination, test, or analytical research without commercial sale. Form 10 (and Form 10A for Schedule X) grants commercial import permission for registered drugs, while Forms 20B and 25 are domestic wholesale and manufacturing licences respectively.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02271","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Drying Equipment in Tablet Granulation","q":"A tablet manufacturing unit switches wet-granule drying from a static hot-air tray dryer to a Fluidized Bed Dryer (FBD). Which major processing advantage does the FBD provide over static tray drying?","o":["Drying strictly by vacuum sublimation below -40 °C over 48 hours","Rapid, uniform convective drying in 20–45 minutes that minimizes intergranular solute/dye migration and case-hardening","Simultaneous dry-powder blending and tablet compression inside the drying bowl","Complete elimination of airflow so wet granules remain stationary in thick beds"],"a":1,"e":"In a Fluidized Bed Dryer (FBD), heated filtered air passes upward through a perforated bottom plate at sufficient velocity to suspend (\u0027fluidize\u0027) wet granules in turbulent motion, maximizing gas-solid surface contact. This dries batches 10–15 times faster (typically 20–45 minutes vs 10–24 hours in tray ovens) at uniform temperatures, minimizing soluble binder/dye migration to the surface (preventing tablet mottling) and avoiding hard outer crusts (case-hardening).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02272","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Quality Control Tests for Tablets","q":"During an IP friability test of uncoated tablets using a Roche friabilator rotated at 25 rpm for 4 minutes (100 revolutions), what is the maximum permissible percentage weight loss for the batch to pass?","o":["Not more than 10.0%","Not more than 5.0%","Not more than 1.0% (with no broken, cracked, or cleaved tablets)","Zero weight loss (0.00%) strictly"],"a":2,"e":"The IP/USP friability test evaluates tablet resistance to abrasion and shock during packaging and transport. De-dusted tablets (10 tablets if unit weight \u003e 650 mg, or sample \u003e= 6.5 g if \u003c= 650 mg) are tumbled in the Roche friabilator at 25 ± 1 rpm for 4 minutes (100 drops of 6 inches). A maximum mean weight loss of \u003c= 1.0%—with zero cracked, split, or broken tablets—is acceptable for most compressed uncoated tablets.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02273","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutics","topic":"Dry Heat Sterilization and Depyrogenation","q":"Which pharmaceutical materials are appropriately sterilized using a Dry Heat Hot Air Oven (e.g., 160 °C for 2 hours or 170 °C for 1 hour), and at what minimum dry-heat temperature are glass ampoules routinely depyrogenated?","o":["gelatin suppositories and hydroalcoholic elixirs; depyrogenation at 121 °C","Live attenuated viral vaccines; depyrogenation at 100 °C","Aqueous 5% dextrose IV bags and soft plastic PVC tubing; depyrogenation at 60 °C","Anhydrous fixed oils, liquid paraffin, thermostable powders, and empty glass vials; depyrogenation requires \u003e= 250 °C (e.g., 250 °C for 30–60 min)"],"a":3,"e":"Dry heat sterilization kills microorganisms via oxidative destruction of cellular constituents and is reserved for moisture-impermeable or anhydrous materials—fixed oils (arachis/sesame oil vehicles), petrolatum, zinc oxide/talc dusting powders, surgical instruments, and borosilicate glassware (standard IP cycles: 160 °C for 2 h, 170 °C for 1 h, or 180 °C for 30 min). Bacterial endotoxins (LPS) are heat-stable and require high-temperature dry-heat depyrogenation tunnels (\u003e= 250 °C for \u003e= 30 min) to destroy pyrogenicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02274","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Limit Test for Lead (IP)","q":"In the Indian Pharmacopoeia limit test for lead (method using dithizone), potassium cyanide and ammonium citrate are added to the ammoniacal test and standard solutions before extracting with dithizone in chloroform. What is the appearance of the positive lead-dithizonate complex, and why is potassium cyanide added?","o":["Violet/cherry-red chloroform layer; potassium cyanide masks interfering metal ions (e.g., Cu2+, Zn2+, Ni2+) as stable soluble cyanide complexes","Sky-blue aqueous layer; potassium cyanide precipitates lead sulfate selectively","Colorless vapor; potassium cyanide neutralizes chloroform","Bright yellow aqueous precipitate; potassium cyanide oxidizes lead to PbO2"],"a":0,"e":"In the IP limit test for lead, dithizone (diphenylthiocarbazone, green in chloroform) reacts with Pb2+ in an alkaline ammoniacal medium (pH 8.5–10) to form a cherry-red/violet chloroform-soluble lead dithizonate complex whose color intensity is compared against a standard lead solution. Because other trace metals (Cu, Zn, Fe, Bi) also react with dithizone, ammonium citrate keeps Fe/Al in solution as citrates while potassium cyanide complexes Cu2+, Zn2+, and Ni2+ into stable non-extractable cyanides (hydroxylamine HCl prevents oxidation of dithizone).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02275","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Precipitation Titrations (Argentometry)","q":"When assaying ammonium chloride or sodium chloride by Volhard\u0027s argentometric back-titration (adding excess AgNO3 and back-titrating unreacted Ag+ with standardized ammonium thiocyanate in nitric acid using ferric alum indicator), why is nitrobenzene (or dibutyl phthalate) added before back-titrating chloride?","o":["To coat the precipitated AgCl particles so AgCl (higher Ksp than AgSCN) does not react with thiocyanate and cause a fading, inaccurate endpoint","To oxidize Fe3+ indicator to Fe2+ before equivalence","To raise the solution pH above 10 for potassium chromate precipitation","To dissolve silver chloride completely into free silver ions"],"a":0,"e":"In Volhard\u0027s back-titration for chlorides, silver thiocyanate (AgSCN, Ksp ≈ 1.0×10^-12) is actually less soluble than silver chloride (AgCl, Ksp ≈ 1.8×10^-10). Therefore, after all excess Ag+ is consumed, added SCN- reacts slowly with solid AgCl precipitate (AgCl + SCN- -\u003e AgSCN + Cl-), stealing thiocyanate and causing a fading ferric-thiocyanate red endpoint. Adding a dense immiscible organic liquid like nitrobenzene (or filtering off AgCl) coats and isolates the AgCl precipitate from the aqueous phase, giving a sharp, stable endpoint.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02276","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmaceutical Chemistry","topic":"Antileprotic Drugs","q":"Dapsone, a cornerstone drug in multidrug therapy (MDT) for leprosy alongside rifampicin and clofazimine, belongs chemically to which class and inhibits which microbial pathway?","o":["Nitroimidazole; generates cytotoxic nitro-radicals only in obligate anaerobes","4,4\u0027-Diaminodiphenyl sulfone; competitively inhibits bacterial dihydropteroate synthase (folate synthesis) via PABA antagonism","Phenazine dye; intercalates mycobacterial ribosomal RNA","4-Aminoquinoline; inhibits plasmodial heme polymerase"],"a":1,"e":"Dapsone is chemically 4,4\u0027-diaminodiphenyl sulfone (DDS). Structurally analogous to para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) and sulfonamides, it competitively inhibits dihydropteroate synthase in Mycobacterium leprae, blocking folic acid biosynthesis. Clinically, it can induce methemoglobinemia and dose-dependent hemolytic anemia—especially severe in patients with Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02277","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Identification of Acacia vs Tragacanth","q":"A pharmacognosy student tests two powdered pharmaceutical gums: Sample X gives a blue color with benzidine and hydrogen peroxide (due to an endogenous oxidase enzyme) and does NOT stain pink with ruthenium red; Sample Y swells into a thick mucilage whose particles stain pink with ruthenium red and gives no blue color with benzidine-H2O2. Identify Samples X and Y.","o":["Sample X is Agar and Sample Y is Gelatin","Sample X is Tragacanth and Sample Y is Acacia","Sample X is Acacia (Gum Arabic) and Sample Y is Tragacanth","Sample X is Starch and Sample Y is Beeswax"],"a":2,"e":"Acacia (Acacia senegal / A. arabica) is completely water-soluble (forms clear mucilage without swelling residue), contains a natural peroxidase/oxidase enzyme that turns benzidine + H2O2 blue (a key test for avoiding incompatibility with oxidizable drugs like morphine/adrenaline unless heated to inactivate oxidase), and tests negative with ruthenium red. Tragacanth (Astragalus gummifer) contains ~60–70% water-insoluble swelling bassorin, stains pink with ruthenium red, and lacks oxidase.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02278","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacognosy","topic":"Indole Alkaloids (Ergot)","q":"Ergot consists of the dried sclerotium of the fungus Claviceps purpurea developed on rye plants. When its indole alkaloids (ergometrine, ergotamine) are treated with Van Urk\u0027s reagent (p-dimethylaminobenzaldehyde in sulfuric acid with trace ferric chloride), what characteristic color develops?","o":["Brick-red precipitate","Emerald green color with bromine water","Bright yellow fluorescence with ammonia only","Deep blue to violet-blue color"],"a":3,"e":"Ergot alkaloids share an ergoline indole ring system (lysergic acid derivatives: water-soluble oxytocic ergometrine and water-insoluble antimigraine ergotamine). With Van Urk\u0027s reagent (p-dimethylaminobenzaldehyde in H2SO4 containing trace FeCl3), the unsubstituted C-2 position of the indole nucleus condenses to yield a characteristic deep blue coloration—used both qualitatively and colorimetrically in pharmacopoeial assays.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02279","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy \u0026 Physiology","topic":"Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF) Circulation","q":"Where is cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) primarily secreted, and through which anatomical structures is it reabsorbed back into the dural venous blood?","o":["Secreted by the choroid plexuses in the cerebral ventricles; reabsorbed through arachnoid villi (granulations) into the superior sagittal venous sinus","Secreted by the pineal gland; reabsorbed directly into the thoracic lymphatic duct","Secreted by spinal dorsal root ganglia; reabsorbed into the hepatic portal vein","Secreted by Purkinje cells of the cerebellum; reabsorbed via the Eustachian tube"],"a":0,"e":"Approximately 500 mL/day of clear CSF (total volume ~125–150 mL) is actively secreted by specialized ependymal cells and fenestrated capillaries forming the choroid plexuses inside the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles. CSF circulates through the interventricular foramina, cerebral aqueduct, and subarachnoid space around the brain and spinal cord, and drains down a pressure gradient via arachnoid villi/granulations into the superior sagittal dural venous sinus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02280","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Human Anatomy \u0026 Physiology","topic":"Calcium and Phosphate Homeostasis","q":"In response to a drop in ionized serum calcium, Parathyroid Hormone (PTH) is released from chief cells of the parathyroid glands. How does PTH regulate serum calcium and serum phosphate levels via its direct renal and skeletal actions?","o":["Increases serum Ca2+ (stimulates osteoclastic bone resorption, distal tubular Ca2+ reabsorption, and renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase for calcitriol) while decreasing serum phosphate (inhibits proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption)","Decreases serum Ca2+ and increases serum phosphate by depositing hydroxyapatite into bone","Increases both serum Ca2+ and serum phosphate by blocking proximal phosphate excretion","Lowers serum Ca2+ exclusively by inhibiting intestinal vitamin D absorption"],"a":0,"e":"PTH is the primary defender against hypocalcemia: (1) in bone, it acts on osteoblasts/RANKL to stimulate osteoclastic Ca2+ and PO4^3- mobilization; (2) in the kidney, it enhances distal convoluted tubule Ca2+ reabsorption, inhibits proximal tubular Na+-phosphate cotransporters (causing phosphaturia and lowering serum phosphate so Ca×PO4 does not precipitate), and activates 1-alpha-hydroxylase to convert 25(OH)D3 into active calcitriol [1,25(OH)2D3], which boosts gut Ca2+ absorption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02281","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Disinfection of Drinking Water","q":"In community water supply sanitation, what is meant by \u0027breakpoint chlorination\u0027, and what minimum free residual chlorine level is recommended after a 1-hour contact period for routine bacteriological safety?","o":["Precipitation of all dissolved calcium hardness using sodium chloride; 10 mg/L","The point at which chlorine demand (oxidation of ammonia, organic matter, and microbes) is fully satisfied so free residual chlorine begins appearing; minimum \u003e= 0.5 mg/L (0.5 ppm) after 1 hour","Adding chlorine solely until chloramines form with zero free chlorine; \u003e= 50 mg/L","The temperature at which chlorine boils out of water; 0.0 mg/L residual"],"a":1,"e":"When chlorine is added to water, it first reacts with reducing agents, organic matter, and ammonia (forming combined chloramines), and then oxidizes chloramines until the \u0027breakpoint\u0027 is reached—beyond which further chlorine persists as potent bactericidal free residual chlorine (HOCl / OCl-). Public health guidelines recommend at least 0.5 mg/L (ppm) free residual chlorine after 1 hour contact (verified by Orthotolidine-Arsenite [OTA] or DPD test) to guard against recontamination in distribution pipes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02282","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Social Pharmacy","topic":"Micronutrient Deficiency Control in India","q":"Under India\u0027s National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP) and FSSAI regulations, what are the minimum statutory iodine levels mandated in iodized edible salt at the manufacturing/production level and at the consumer household level, respectively?","o":["Not less than 500 ppm at production level and 250 ppm at consumer level","Not less than 5 ppm at production level and 1 ppm at consumer level","Not less than 30 ppm at production level and not less than 15 ppm at consumer level","Iodine is added only to infant milk formula, not table salt"],"a":2,"e":"To prevent goiter, cretinism, hypothyroidism, and impaired cognitive development under NIDDCP, potassium iodate (KIO3, more stable in tropical humid climates than KI) is fortified into edible salt at \u003e= 30 ppm at the manufacturing site so that despite storage and transport losses, at least \u003e= 15 ppm iodine reaches the consumer household level (providing ~150 µg iodine/day at ~10 g daily salt intake).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02283","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimuscarinic Toxicity","q":"A 17-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department agitated and hallucinating after ingesting Datura stramonium seeds. Examination reveals temperature 39.4 °C, flushed dry skin, markedly dilated non-reactive pupils, absent bowel sounds, and urinary retention. Which specific cholinergic drug reverses both central delirium and peripheral muscarinic manifestations?","o":["Pralidoxime (2-PAM)","Neostigmine","Scopolamine","Physostigmine (slow IV under ECG monitoring)"],"a":3,"e":"Datura stramonium contains atropine, hyoscyamine, and scopolamine, producing a classic anticholinergic toxidrome (\u0027hot as a hare, dry as a bone, red as a beet, blind as a bat, mad as a hatter\u0027). Physostigmine is a tertiary-amine reversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitor capable of crossing the blood-brain barrier to restore synaptic acetylcholine at both central and peripheral muscarinic receptors (given cautiously only in severe delirium/life-threatening toxicity without QRS prolongation/TCA co-ingestion).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02284","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antianginal Pharmacotherapy","q":"A 61-year-old man with chronic stable angina using isosorbide mononitrate asks why his physician schedules a daily 10-hour nitrate-free overnight interval, and whether he can take sildenafil occasionally. What should the pharmacist explain?","o":["A daily 8–12 hour nitrate-free interval prevents vascular nitrate tolerance; sildenafil is strictly contraindicated with nitrates because dual cGMP elevation causes refractory life-threatening hypotension","Nitrate-free intervals prevent reflex bradycardia; sildenafil blocks beta-1 receptors in the heart","Continuous 24-hour nitrate exposure is mandatory to eliminate headache; sildenafil safely lowers nitrate dose requirements","Taking sildenafil 5 minutes before sublingual nitroglycerin prevents myocardial infarction"],"a":0,"e":"Continuous uninterrupted nitrate exposure rapidly induces vascular tolerance (attributed to mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase-2 inactivation, superoxide oxidative stress, and counter-regulatory neurohormonal activation); scheduling an asymmetric dosing regimen with an 8–12 hour nitrate-free period daily restores responsiveness. Moreover, nitrates stimulate nitric oxide-mediated cGMP synthesis while PDE-5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil, vardenafil) block cGMP degradation—producing synergistic profound vasodilation and fatal cardiovascular collapse (nitrates must be separated by \u003e= 24 hours from sildenafil/vardenafil and \u003e= 48 hours from tadalafil).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02285","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Prokinetic and Antiemetic Agents","q":"A 21-year-old woman treated with intravenous metoclopramide for chemotherapy-related nausea develops acute torticollis and oculogyric crisis (acute dystonia). Why does domperidone rarely cause such extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS) despite also blocking dopamine D2 receptors?","o":["Metoclopramide irreversibly destroys substantia nigra neurons within one dose","Domperidone poorly crosses the blood-brain barrier (acting peripherally in the gut and chemoreceptor trigger zone outside the BBB), whereas metoclopramide readily penetrates the CNS to block nigrostriatal D2 receptors","Domperidone is metabolized instantly in the stomach before entering systemic blood","Domperidone acts exclusively as a 5-HT3 agonist without any D2 receptor affinity"],"a":1,"e":"Both metoclopramide and domperidone antagonize D2 receptors in the gastrointestinal myenteric plexus (prokinetic action) and the area postrema / chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ, which lies outside the blood-brain barrier). However, metoclopramide crosses the BBB readily and blocks striatal D2 receptors, causing acute dystonia, akathisia, or tardive dyskinesia (especially in young females/children; acute dystonia is treated with IV/IM diphenhydramine, promethazine, or benztropine). Domperidone barely crosses the BBB, sparing the striatum (though high IV/oral doses carry QT-prolongation risk).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02286","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Weight-Based Pediatric Dose Conversion","q":"A pediatrician prescribes amoxicillin oral suspension 40 mg/kg/day in 2 divided doses (every 12 hours) for a child weighing 15 kg. The pharmacy stocks reconstituted amoxicillin suspension labeled 250 mg/5 mL. How many milliliters should the parent administer per dose?","o":["4.8 mL per dose (twice daily)","12.0 mL per dose (twice daily)","6.0 mL per dose (twice daily)","3.0 mL per dose (twice daily)"],"a":2,"e":"Total daily dose = 40 mg/kg/day × 15 kg = 600 mg/day. Divided into 2 equal doses (every 12 hours), each single dose = 600 mg / 2 = 300 mg. Using the stock concentration (250 mg in 5 mL, i.e., 50 mg/mL): Volume per dose = (300 mg / 250 mg) × 5 mL = 1.2 × 5 mL = 6.0 mL per dose twice daily.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02287","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Drug-Alcohol Interactions","q":"When dispensing a 7-day course of oral metronidazole 400 mg three times daily for amebic liver abscess, the community pharmacist advises the patient to strictly avoid alcohol during therapy and for at least 48–72 hours after the last dose. What adverse interaction does this counselling prevent?","o":["Irreversible chelation of metronidazole into inactive calcium salts","Rapid crystallization of metronidazole in the renal pelvis","Sudden hyperkalemic cardiac arrest via aldosterone blockade","A disulfiram-like reaction (intense facial flushing, throbbing headache, nausea/vomiting, tachycardia, and hypotension) due to aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibition"],"a":3,"e":"Metronidazole (and tinidazole, cefoperazone, chlorpropamide) inhibits hepatic aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH), blocking oxidation of ethanol-derived acetaldehyde to acetate. Co-ingestion of alcoholic beverages or alcohol-containing elixirs causes toxic acetaldehyde accumulation, precipitating an unpleasant and potentially dangerous disulfiram-like reaction (flushing, palpitations, nausea, vomiting, dyspnea, hypotension). Avoiding alcohol during treatment and for 48–72 hours post-completion prevents this reaction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02288","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Community Pharmacy \u0026 Management","topic":"Markup vs Gross Profit Margin","q":"A community pharmacy purchases a digital blood glucose monitor kit for ₹400 and prices it for retail sale using a 25% markup on cost. What are the retail selling price and the resulting gross profit margin percentage (based on selling price)?","o":["Selling price = ₹500; Gross profit margin = 20%","Selling price = ₹533.33; Gross profit margin = 25%","Selling price = ₹480; Gross profit margin = 16.7%","Selling price = ₹500; Gross profit margin = 25%"],"a":0,"e":"With a 25% markup on purchase cost (₹400): Profit = 0.25 × ₹400 = ₹100, so Selling Price = ₹400 + ₹100 = ₹500. Gross Profit Margin is expressed as a percentage of Selling Price: Gross Margin (%) = (Gross Profit / Selling Price) × 100 = (₹100 / ₹500) × 100 = 20%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02289","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Biochemistry \u0026 Clinical Pathology","topic":"Red Cell Indices Calculation","q":"A patient\u0027s complete blood count reports Hematocrit (PCV) = 30% and Red Blood Cell (RBC) count = 5.0 × 10^6 /µL. Calculate the Mean Corpuscular Volume (MCV) and classify the erythrocyte morphology (normal MCV reference range: 80–100 fL).","o":["MCV = 150 fL; megaloblastic anemia","MCV = 60 fL; microcytic anemia","MCV = 85 fL; normocytic anemia","MCV = 166.7 fL; macrocytic anemia"],"a":1,"e":"Mean Corpuscular Volume (MCV) in femtoliters (fL) is calculated as: MCV = [Hematocrit (%) × 10] / RBC count (in millions/µL). Substituting Hct = 30% and RBC = 5.0 million/µL: MCV = (30 × 10) / 5.0 = 300 / 5.0 = 60 fL. Because MCV \u003c 80 fL, the red blood cells are microcytic (typical of iron deficiency anemia or thalassemia trait).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02290","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Biochemistry \u0026 Clinical Pathology","topic":"Serum Anion Gap and Metabolic Acidosis","q":"A 24-year-old man with uncontrolled type 1 diabetes presents with Kussmaul deep breathing, arterial pH 7.18, pCO2 22 mmHg, serum Na+ 136 mEq/L, Cl- 96 mEq/L, and HCO3- 10 mEq/L. What is his calculated serum anion gap and acid-base diagnosis?","o":["Anion gap = 12 mEq/L; normal anion gap hyperchloremic acidosis","Anion gap = 10 mEq/L (normal); respiratory alkalosis","Anion gap = 30 mEq/L (elevated); partially compensated high anion gap metabolic acidosis (DKA)","Anion gap = 40 mEq/L; uncompensated metabolic alkalosis"],"a":2,"e":"Serum Anion Gap = [Na+] - ([Cl-] + [HCO3-]) = 136 - (96 + 10) = 136 - 106 = 30 mEq/L (normal ~8–12 mEq/L without K+). Combined with low arterial pH (7.18), markedly reduced HCO3- (10 mEq/L), and compensatory hyperventilation lowering pCO2 (22 mmHg), this confirms partially compensated High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis (HAGMA) driven by unmeasured ketoacid anions (beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate) in diabetic ketoacidosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02291","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Malaria Treatment (National Drug Policy, India)","q":"A 30-year-old man in India is diagnosed by microscopy with uncomplicated Plasmodium vivax malaria (with normal G6PD enzyme activity). According to the National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (NVBDCP / ICMR) guidelines, why is a 14-day course of primaquine added after 3 days of chloroquine?","o":["Primaquine is required because all P. vivax strains in India are 100% chloroquine-resistant","Primaquine prevents chloroquine-induced hypoglycemia","Chloroquine clears liver hypnozoites while primaquine treats cerebral malaria exclusively","Primaquine eliminates dormant hepatic hypnozoites to achieve radical cure and prevent relapse, whereas chloroquine clears only asexual blood-stage parasites"],"a":3,"e":"Plasmodium vivax and P. ovale form dormant liver stages (hypnozoites) that cause late relapses weeks to months after blood-stage clearance. Under India\u0027s NVBDCP/ICMR malaria guidelines, uncomplicated P. vivax is treated with 3 days of oral chloroquine (25 mg/kg total over 3 days) to clear erythrocytic schizonts, paired with 14 days of primaquine (0.25 mg/kg/day) for radical cure of hepatic hypnozoites—after screening/precaution for G6PD deficiency (since primaquine causes oxidant hemolysis in G6PD-deficient patients and is contraindicated in pregnancy/infants).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02292","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Ischemic Stroke Secondary Prevention","q":"A 65-year-old hypertensive man presents with sudden right-sided weakness and slurred speech of 6 hours\u0027 duration (outside the IV thrombolysis window). Before initiating aspirin for acute non-cardioembolic ischemic stroke and secondary prevention, which mandatory diagnostic step must first be completed?","o":["Non-contrast CT (NCCT) scan of the brain to definitively rule out acute intracranial hemorrhage","Serum troponin measurement alone without brain imaging","Starting warfarin INR 3.0 immediately in the ambulance","Immediate lumbar puncture followed by heparin bolus before any imaging"],"a":0,"e":"Clinical examination alone cannot reliably distinguish acute ischemic stroke from intracerebral hemorrhage (ICH). Therefore, an urgent non-contrast head CT (NCCT) is mandatory prior to administering any antiplatelet (aspirin/clopidogrel) or thrombolytic/anticoagulant agent; giving aspirin in an unrecognized hemorrhagic stroke catastrophically expands hematoma bleeding. Once hemorrhage is excluded, oral aspirin (160–325 mg loading, then 75–150 mg/day) plus high-intensity statin are initiated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02293","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Scabies Management","q":"A family is prescribed permethrin 5% cream for classic Sarcoptes scabiei infestation. Which counselling instruction is essential to ensure eradication and prevent rapid reinfestation?","o":["Apply only to the itchy visible papules for 15 minutes and rinse immediately","Apply thoroughly from neck to soles (including interdigital webs, under nails, and skin folds) at bedtime, wash off after 8–14 hours, treat all close household contacts simultaneously, and wash bedding/clothes in hot water","Take permethrin 5% cream orally with milk once daily for 5 days","Treat only the symptomatic index patient and reassure them that post-scabetic itching stops within 1 hour of application"],"a":1,"e":"Permethrin 5% cream (a synthetic pyrethroid paralyzing mite voltage-gated Na+ channels) must be applied evenly over all skin surfaces from the neck down to the soles (plus scalp/face sparing eyes/mouth in infants/elderly), left on for 8–14 hours overnight before washing off, with a second application 7 days later commonly advised. Simultaneous treatment of asymptomatic close household/sexual contacts and hot-water laundering (or sealing in plastic bags \u003e= 72 h) of linens prevents ping-pong reinfestation; patients should also know mild hypersensitivity pruritus may persist for 2–4 weeks after mites are killed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02294","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law \u0026 Ethics","topic":"Offences and Statutory Definitions","q":"Under Section 17B of India\u0027s Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940, how is a \u0027Spurious Drug\u0027 legally defined?","o":["A drug solely lacking an unexpired manufacturing license renewal fee","A drug stored in a container made of uncolored amber glass","A drug imported/manufactured under a name belonging to another drug, an imitation/substitute resembling another drug to deceive, or bearing the name of a fictitious or non-existent manufacturer","A genuine drug manufactured in full compliance with IP whose label uses a slightly smaller font size"],"a":2,"e":"The Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940 distinguishes three grave quality offences: Section 17 (Misbranded: false/misleading label claims, unprescribed color, misleading design), Section 17A (Adulterated: filthy/putrid/decomposed material, unsanitary manufacture, toxic container leaching, added substandard filler reducing quality), and Section 17B (Spurious: counterfeit/imitation drug sold under another drug\u0027s name, substituted wholly/partly, or falsely purporting to be manufactured by another or fictitious company).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02295","course":"D.Pharm","subject":"Pharmacy Law \u0026 Ethics","topic":"Code of Pharmaceutical Ethics (PCI)","q":"According to the Code of Pharmaceutical Ethics framed by the Pharmacy Council of India (PCI), how must a pharmacist respond upon noticing a suspected therapeutic error or excessive overdose on a physician\u0027s prescription?","o":["Unilaterally alter the active drug molecule to a different therapeutic class without consulting the prescriber","Tell the patient loudly in the waiting area that the doctor is incompetent","Dispense the suspected overdose silently without questioning the physician","Refrain from alarming the patient or publicly criticizing the prescriber; instead contact the prescriber discreetly to verify and clarify the prescription before dispensing"],"a":3,"e":"Under the PCI Code of Pharmaceutical Ethics (Pharmacist in relation to his job and medical profession), a pharmacist has a dual duty: safeguard patient safety by never knowingly dispensing an unsafe error, while upholding interprofessional trust—never commenting critically to the patient on a prescriber\u0027s competence, never entering into secret commission arrangements, and never substituting ingredients without prescriber consent. Discreetly contacting the prescriber to resolve dose/drug concerns protects both patient and professional ethics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02296","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery, Gynaecology \u0026 Community Health Nursing","topic":"Active Management of Third Stage of Labour (AMTSL)","q":"Immediately after the delivery of a term newborn in a district hospital labour room, and after ruling out a second undiagnosed twin by abdominal palpation, what is the first-line uterotonic and dose administered by the GNM midwife under Active Management of Third Stage of Labour (AMTSL)?","o":["Oxytocin 10 IU intramuscularly (IM) within 1 minute of birth","Magnesium sulphate 4 g IV bolus to contract the uterus","Methylergometrine 2 mg IV bolus regardless of maternal blood pressure","Misoprostol 2,000 mcg orally 2 hours after placental delivery"],"a":0,"e":"Per INC/MoHFW SBA and WHO AMTSL guidelines, the core step is administering Oxytocin 10 IU IM within 1 minute of delivery (after excluding a multiple gestation), followed by controlled cord traction during uterine contraction and uterine tone assessment after placental delivery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02297","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Fundamentals of Nursing \u0026 First Aid","topic":"Routes \u0026 Angles of Parenteral Injections","q":"During a nursing OSPE station on parenteral medication administration, what are the correct standard needle insertion angles relative to the skin surface for Intramuscular (IM), Subcutaneous (SC), and Intradermal (ID) injections respectively?","o":["IM: 15 degrees; SC: 90 degrees; ID: 60 degrees","IM: 90 degrees; SC: 45 degrees (or 90 degrees with short 4-8 mm insulin needle in pinched fold); ID: 10-15 degrees","IM: 30 degrees; SC: 30 degrees; ID: 45 degrees","IM: 45 degrees; SC: 10 degrees; ID: 90 degrees"],"a":1,"e":"Standard injection angles ensure deposition into the intended tissue plane: IM reaches deep muscle at 90 degrees, SC reaches adipose tissue at 45 degrees (or 90 degrees with ultra-short insulin pen needles), and ID places a tiny wheal just beneath the epidermis at 10-15 degrees bevel up (e.g., Mantoux/BCG).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02298","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations (including First Aid)","topic":"Snakebite First Aid","q":"A farmworker is bitten on the right lower leg by an unidentified snake and arrives at a primary clinic 20 minutes later. Which first-aid intervention by the GNM nurse aligns with MoHFW/NHSRC Standard Treatment Guidelines?","o":["Apply a tight arterial tourniquet above the knee and incise the fang marks to suck out venom","Reassure the patient, immobilize the right leg in a neutral position using a splint, keep the patient still, and arrange urgent transfer for anti-snake venom (ASV) assessment","Ask the patient to walk briskly to promote circulation and prevent clotting","Apply ice packs directly onto the bite site and administer aspirin"],"a":1,"e":"MoHFW/NHSRC snakebite guidelines emphasize \u0027Do it RIGHT\u0027: Reassure the victim (anxiety increases heart rate and venom spread), Immobilize the bitten limb gently with a splint (like a fracture, avoiding tight compression), Get to Hospital immediately, and Tell the doctor of any symptoms. Tourniquets, wound incisions, suction, ice, and herbal pastes cause local tissue necrosis, bleeding, and delay definitive ASV therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02299","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Intramuscular Injection Technique","q":"At a practical OSPE station, a GNM student is instructed to administer an irritating intramuscular iron preparation using the Z-track technique in the ventrogluteal site. Which sequence of actions is correct?","o":["Use a 1/2-inch 26-gauge needle without displacing tissue and apply a warm compress immediately","Pinch the skin upward, insert needle at 45 degrees, inject rapidly, and massage vigorously after withdrawal","Pull the skin and subcutaneous tissue laterally 2.5–3.5 cm, insert needle at 90 degrees, inject slowly, wait 10 seconds, withdraw needle, release skin, and do not massage","Insert needle at 15 degrees into the dermis until a bleb forms"],"a":2,"e":"The Z-track technique creates a zigzag path through tissue planes so irritating or staining drugs (like iron dextran) remain sealed deep inside the muscle and cannot leak back into subcutaneous tissue. Displacing skin laterally before inserting at 90 degrees, waiting 10 seconds post-injection, withdrawing, and then releasing tissue without massaging prevents skin discolouration and sterile abscesses.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02300","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Acute Myocardial Infarction","q":"A 56-year-old man arrives in the emergency unit with crushing retrosternal chest pain radiating to the left jaw for 40 minutes, diaphoresis, BP 134/82 mmHg, HR 96 bpm, and SpO2 96% on room air. Which immediate nursing action holds highest priority alongside establishing IV access?","o":["Place the patient flat supine and administer high-flow oxygen at 15 L/min despite SpO2 96%","Encourage the patient to ambulate to relieve chest tightness before ECG","Administer sublingual nitroglycerin without checking blood pressure or recent sildenafil use","Obtain a 12-lead ECG within 10 minutes and administer chewed aspirin 300 mg (if no allergy/bleeding contraindication)"],"a":3,"e":"Rapid 12-lead ECG acquisition (within 10 minutes of arrival) is essential to identify ST-elevation MI (STEMI) for timely reperfusion (thrombolysis or primary PCI), while chewed non-enteric-coated aspirin inhibits platelet aggregation immediately. Routine supplemental oxygen is not indicated when SpO2 \u003e= 90% because hyperoxia may cause coronary vasoconstriction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02301","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Fluid Management","q":"A postoperative patient is prescribed 500 mL of Ringer\u0027s Lactate over 5 hours using a microdrip (paediatric/burette) infusion set calibrated at 60 drops/mL. What should the drip rate be set to?","o":["100 drops/min","75 drops/min","50 drops/min","125 drops/min"],"a":0,"e":"Using Drops/min = (Volume in mL × Drop Factor) ÷ (Time in hours × 60 min): (500 × 60) ÷ (5 × 60) = 30,000 ÷ 300 = 100 drops/min. Notably, with a 60 drops/mL microdrip set, drops per minute always equals mL per hour (500 mL ÷ 5 hr = 100 mL/hr).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02302","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Alcohol Withdrawal Syndrome and Delirium Tremens","q":"Seventy-two hours after abrupt cessation of heavy alcohol use, a hospitalized 44-year-old man develops agitation, disorientation, visual hallucinations of insects crawling on the bed, profuse sweating, coarse tremors, and heart rate 126 bpm. Which pharmacological order should the nurse prioritize?","o":["Rapid infusion of 10% Dextrose alone without vitamins","Intravenous benzodiazepine (e.g., diazepam/lorazepam) for withdrawal control and parenteral thiamine (Vitamin B1) prior to any glucose infusion","High-dose haloperidol monotherapy as first-line treatment for withdrawal seizures","Oral disulfiram 250 mg immediately to deter alcohol craving"],"a":1,"e":"The presentation indicates Delirium Tremens (DTs), a medical emergency occurring 48–96 hours after alcohol cessation. Benzodiazepines are first-line therapy to cross-react at GABA receptors, control autonomic hyperactivity, and prevent seizures. Parenteral thiamine MUST be administered before dextrose-containing fluids to prevent precipitating acute Wernicke encephalopathy in thiamine-depleted alcohol-dependent patients.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02303","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Extrapyramidal Side Effects (EPS)","q":"Eighteen hours after receiving intramuscular haloperidol for acute psychosis, a young adult develops sudden upward deviation of the eyes (oculogyric crisis) and painful neck spasm (torticollis). Which medication is indicated for immediate relief of this extrapyramidal reaction?","o":["Intravenous sodium valproate","Oral fluoxetine","Parenteral anticholinergic such as promethazine or benztropine","Additional intramuscular haloperidol"],"a":2,"e":"Acute dystonia (oculogyric crisis, torticollis, opisthotonos, or laryngospasm) is an early extrapyramidal side effect caused by nigrostriatal dopamine D2 receptor blockade creating relative cholinergic excess. Rapid administration of a parenteral anticholinergic/antihistamine agent (such as IM/IV promethazine, diphenhydramine, or benztropine, followed by oral trihexyphenidyl) restores dopaminergic-cholinergic balance and relieves spasms promptly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02304","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Neonatal Hyperbilirubinaemia","q":"A term neonate with unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia is placed under LED phototherapy. Which nursing action is essential to ensure safety and accuracy during treatment?","o":["Apply thick oil lotion over the skin to prevent dryness under the lights","Keep the baby fully clothed in woollens to avoid hypothermia","Stop breastfeeding completely and replace with plain water feeds","Shield the neonate\u0027s eyes with opaque eye patches, cover the genitalia, monitor temperature, and turn off phototherapy lights while collecting blood for serum bilirubin"],"a":3,"e":"Phototherapy converts unconjugated bilirubin into water-soluble photo-isomers excreted in bile and urine. Essential nursing precautions include shielding eyes with opaque patches (to prevent retinal injury), covering genitalia, maximizing exposed skin area (only diaper), monitoring axillary temperature and hydration/stool output, and switching off phototherapy lights during blood sampling because light degrades bilirubin in the collection tube causing falsely low lab results.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02305","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"IMNCI Dehydration Assessment and Plan B","q":"A 10-month-old infant (weight 8 kg) presents with acute watery diarrhoea for 2 days. On assessment, the infant is restless and irritable, has sunken eyes, drinks ORS eagerly, and an abdominal skin pinch goes back slowly (\u003c 2 seconds). Under IMNCI guidelines, what is the classification and immediate fluid management?","o":["Some dehydration (Plan B); administer 600 mL of WHO low-osmolarity ORS (75 mL/kg) over 4 hours plus oral zinc 20 mg/day for 14 days","Some dehydration; withhold all breastfeeds for 24 hours until stools stop","Severe dehydration (Plan C); give only plain glucose water orally","No dehydration (Plan A); send home without ORS"],"a":0,"e":"Two or more signs among restlessness/irritability, sunken eyes, eager drinking (thirst), and slow skin pinch (\u003c 2 s) classify the child as \u0027Some Dehydration\u0027 under IMNCI. Plan B recommends WHO low-osmolarity ORS (245 mOsm/L) at 75 mL/kg over 4 hours (8 kg × 75 mL = 600 mL), continued breastfeeding, and zinc supplementation (20 mg/day for children \u003e= 6 months for 14 days) to reduce duration and recurrence of diarrhoea.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02306","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"WHO Modified Partograph","q":"While plotting a primigravida\u0027s active labour progress on a WHO modified partograph, the nurse notes that the cervical dilatation plot has moved to the right of the Alert Line and is approaching the Action Line (4 hours to the right of the Alert Line). What does crossing the Alert Line signify?","o":["Normal rapid descent of the foetal head requiring no further observation","Warning of slower-than-expected labour progress (\u003c 1 cm/hour) requiring closer clinical reassessment and readiness for referral/obstetric intervention","Complete third stage of labour","Immediate need for routine forceps delivery at 4 cm dilatation"],"a":1,"e":"On the WHO modified partograph (started in active phase at 4 cm), the Alert Line represents an expected minimum cervical dilatation rate of 1 cm/hour. When the plot crosses to the right of the Alert Line, it serves as an early warning of prolonged/slow labour, prompting evaluation of uterine contractions, maternal hydration, bladder status, foetal heart rate, and timely referral from a peripheral centre to an FRU before reaching the Action Line.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02307","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Oral Rehydration Therapy","q":"A mother asks the community health nurse how to prepare and store a standard 20.5 g packet of WHO Oral Rehydration Salts (ORS) at home for her 2-year-old child with diarrhoea. Which instruction is accurate?","o":["Give the entire 1 litre in 15 minutes using a feeding bottle","Boil the water again after mixing the ORS powder to sterilize the salts","Dissolve one full packet in 1 litre of clean drinking water (boiled and cooled if needed), give small frequent sips by spoon, and discard any leftover solution after 24 hours","Dissolve one packet in 250 mL of warm milk and keep it for 72 hours"],"a":2,"e":"A standard 20.5 g WHO low-osmolarity ORS packet must be completely dissolved in exactly 1 litre (1,000 mL) of clean drinking water to achieve the correct sodium (75 mmol/L) and glucose (75 mmol/L) osmolarity (245 mOsm/L). Reconstituted ORS should be covered, used within 24 hours, never boiled after mixing (which concentrates electrolytes), and given slowly by cup and spoon (if the child vomits, wait 10 minutes and resume more slowly).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02308","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations (including First Aid)","topic":"Thermal Scald Burn First Aid","q":"A homemaker sustains boiling-water scald burns over her forearm and hand. Which immediate first-aid measure should the GNM nurse perform and teach?","o":["Apply ice cubes directly onto the blisters for 30 minutes","Break all intact blisters immediately with a pin","Apply toothpaste, turmeric paste, or butter to soothe the pain","Hold the burned forearm under cool (not ice-cold) clean running water for 15–20 minutes, gently remove rings/bangles before swelling develops, and cover loosely with a clean sterile non-adherent cloth"],"a":3,"e":"Immediate irrigation with cool running tap water for 15–20 minutes dissipates residual thermal energy, limits burn depth progression, and reduces pain/oedema. Constricting jewellery must be removed early before oedema causes digital ischaemia, and intact blisters serve as a sterile biological dressing so they should not be punctured.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02309","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Parkland Burn Resuscitation Formula","q":"A 70 kg adult arrives immediately after sustaining 40% total body surface area (TBSA) partial- and full-thickness flame burns. Using the Parkland formula (4 mL × weight in kg × %TBSA of Ringer\u0027s Lactate over 24 hours), how much fluid should be infused during the FIRST 8 hours?","o":["5,600 mL","11,200 mL","8,400 mL","2,800 mL"],"a":0,"e":"Step 1: Calculate total 24-hour Ringer\u0027s Lactate requirement = 4 mL × 70 kg × 40 = 11,200 mL. Step 2: Per the Parkland formula, half (50%) of the total 24-hour volume is infused in the first 8 hours from the time of burn: 11,200 mL ÷ 2 = 5,600 mL (at 700 mL/hr if initiated at hour 0), with the remaining 5,600 mL given over the subsequent 16 hours, titrated to maintain adult urine output of 0.5 mL/kg/hr.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02310","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Acute Decompensated Heart Failure and Pulmonary Oedema","q":"A 65-year-old woman with ischaemic cardiomyopathy wakes at night with severe orthopnoea, anxiety, SpO2 84%, BP 172/96 mmHg, bilateral coarse crackles, and pink frothy sputum. Which immediate positioning and emergency medication sequence should the nurse execute?","o":["Encourage oral fluids to thin frothy sputum","Place in High Fowler\u0027s position with legs dangling over the bedside, administer high-flow oxygen, and give prescribed IV furosemide and nitroglycerin","Place in left lateral Trendelenburg; withhold diuretics","Place flat supine with legs elevated; infuse 1,000 mL 0.9% NaCl rapidly"],"a":1,"e":"Pink frothy sputum, crackles, and severe hypoxaemia indicate acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema from left ventricular failure. High Fowler\u0027s positioning with dependent legs pools venous blood peripherally, decreasing preload and easing diaphragmatic excursion. High-flow oxygen/non-invasive ventilation improves oxygenation, while IV loop diuretics (furosemide) and venodilators (nitroglycerin) rapidly reduce pulmonary venous congestion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02311","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Acute Hypoglycaemia","q":"An alert hospitalized patient with Type 2 diabetes on insulin becomes diaphoretic and tremulous with a capillary blood glucose of 48 mg/dL. Which immediate intervention follows the standard \u0027Rule of 15\u0027?","o":["Inject another 10 units of regular insulin immediately","Give a high-fat chocolate bar or buttered toast first to delay absorption","Administer 15–20 g of fast-acting oral carbohydrate (e.g., 3–4 glucose tablets or 150 mL fruit juice/sugar water) and recheck blood glucose in 15 minutes","Force oral glucose water even if the patient loses consciousness"],"a":2,"e":"For conscious, able-to-swallow patients with hypoglycaemia (\u003c 70 mg/dL), the \u0027Rule of 15\u0027 directs giving 15–20 g of simple, rapidly absorbed carbohydrate and rechecking blood glucose after 15 minutes (repeating if still \u003c 70 mg/dL), followed by a complex carb/protein snack once normalized. If the patient is unconscious or has impaired swallowing, oral fluids are contraindicated due to aspiration risk—use IV 25%/50% dextrose or IM glucagon instead.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02312","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Tracheostomy Suctioning","q":"At a tracheostomy care OSPE station, the GNM nurse prepares to suction thick secretions from an adult patient\u0027s tracheostomy tube. Which technique prevents mucosal trauma and severe suction-induced hypoxaemia?","o":["Apply continuous suction while inserting the catheter forcefully down to resistance","Suction continuously for 45 seconds without removing the catheter","Reuse the same non-sterile catheter for multiple shifts after rinsing in tap water","Pre-oxygenate with 100% O2, insert sterile catheter without suction, and apply intermittent suction only during withdrawal for no more than 10–15 seconds per pass"],"a":3,"e":"Applying suction during catheter insertion strips tracheal mucosa and removes alveolar oxygen prematurely. Safe tracheostomy suctioning requires pre-oxygenation (30–60 s), inserting a sterile catheter gently without suction until just past the tube tip (or gentle resistance, withdrawing 1–2 cm), and applying intermittent suction while rotating and withdrawing for \u003c= 10–15 seconds total per pass, allowing adequate recovery between passes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02313","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Bipolar Mania Nursing Care","q":"A hospitalized client in an acute manic episode paces constantly, talks rapidly, is easily distracted by ward noise, and refuses to sit at the dining table for meals, resulting in weight loss. What is the most effective nursing nutrition strategy?","o":["Provide portable, high-calorie, high-protein \u0027finger foods\u0027 (e.g., sandwiches, boiled eggs, bananas, milkshakes) in a quiet, low-stimulus area","Withhold food until hyperactivity ceases completely","Give large amounts of caffeinated coffee to calm the client","Insist that the client sit silently for 45 minutes in the crowded dining hall before eating"],"a":0,"e":"Clients in acute mania expend immense energy yet cannot sustain attention long enough to finish formal seated meals in stimulating environments. High-calorie, high-protein finger foods and nutrient-dense drinks allow safe caloric and fluid intake while pacing, paired with a calm, low-stimulus milieu to prevent exhaustion and dehydration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02314","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Paradoxical Suicide Risk Window","q":"A patient admitted for severe major depression who previously lacked energy to get out of bed appears noticeably energized 12 days after starting an antidepressant, smiles calmly, says \u0027My worries are finally over,\u0027 and gives his watch and wallet to his roommate. How should the nurse interpret this sudden change?","o":["Expected harmless euphoria requiring no intervention","Acute heightened suicide risk as energy returns before depressive hopelessness resolves, combined with classic behavioural warning signs; initiate continuous 1:1 observation and immediate suicide reassessment","Full remission of depression; downgrade observation and prepare immediate unsupervised discharge","Drug-induced Parkinsonism"],"a":1,"e":"During the first 2–4 weeks of antidepressant therapy, psychomotor energy often improves before mood and cognitive hopelessness lift, creating a high-risk window for acting on suicidal thoughts. Sudden inexplicable calm after deep despair (\u0027my worries are over\u0027) and giving away prized possessions are classic covert indicators of an imminent suicide plan requiring immediate 1:1 safety precautions and psychiatric evaluation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02315","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"IMNCI Pneumonia Classification","q":"A 14-month-old child presents to a clinic with cough, fever, respiratory rate 54 breaths/min, lower chest wall indrawing, and audible stridor even while sitting calmly in the mother\u0027s lap. Under IMNCI guidelines, what is the classification and immediate action?","o":["Mild croup; perform vigorous chest percussion","Pneumonia; give oral amoxicillin only at home","Severe Pneumonia or Very Severe Disease; administer first dose of appropriate antibiotic, prevent low blood sugar, and refer urgently to hospital","No pneumonia (cough or cold); send home with honey"],"a":2,"e":"Under IMNCI (2 months–5 years), fast breathing (\u003e= 40/min in 12–59 months) indicates Pneumonia, whereas the presence of any general danger sign OR stridor in a calm child upgrades classification to the pink/red row: \u0027Severe Pneumonia or Very Severe Disease\u0027 (note: in updated MoHFW IMNCI/WHO pneumonia revision, chest indrawing alone without danger signs/hypoxaemia can be managed with oral amoxicillin in outpatient settings, whereas stridor in a calm child or general danger signs unequivocally mandates urgent referral after the first antibiotic dose).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02316","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Intravenous Analgesic Dosing","q":"A 12 kg child with postoperative pain is prescribed intravenous paracetamol at 15 mg/kg/dose. The available IV paracetamol vial is labelled 10 mg/mL (1,000 mg in 100 mL). What exact volume should the nurse draw up and infuse over 15 minutes?","o":["15 mL","12 mL","24 mL","18 mL"],"a":3,"e":"Step 1: Calculate ordered dose = 12 kg × 15 mg/kg = 180 mg. Step 2: Calculate volume using concentration 10 mg/mL: 180 mg ÷ 10 mg/mL = 18 mL. Drawing the exact pediatric dose into a syringe/burette rather than hanging the full 100 mL adult bottle prevents accidental 10-fold IV paracetamol overdose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02317","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Placenta Praevia","q":"A multiparous woman at 33 weeks gestation presents with sudden, painless, bright-red vaginal bleeding. On gentle abdominal palpation, the uterus is soft and non-tender, the foetal head is high and floating, and foetal heart rate is 142 bpm. Which examination is strictly contraindicated before ultrasound localization?","o":["Digital per-vaginal (PV) examination","Non-invasive abdominal ultrasound (transabdominal)","Checking maternal vital signs and blood group/cross-match","External foetal heart rate auscultation"],"a":0,"e":"Painless, causeless, bright-red third-trimester bleeding with a soft, relaxed uterus and unengaged presenting part strongly suggests placenta praevia (unlike abruptio placentae, which typically presents with painful dark bleeding and a rigid, tender uterus). Performing a digital PV examination before ultrasound rules out placenta praevia can disrupt placental attachment over the internal os and provoke catastrophic haemorrhage (\u0027No PV in APH\u0027).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02318","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Umbilical Cord Prolapse","q":"Immediately after spontaneous rupture of membranes in a woman at 6 cm cervical dilatation, the foetal heart rate drops abruptly to 78 bpm and the nurse feels a pulsating loop of umbilical cord in the vagina. While calling for emergency Caesarean delivery, what must the nurse do immediately?","o":["Attempt to push the prolapsed cord back high into the uterine cavity","Insert a sterile gloved hand into the vagina to manually elevate the presenting foetal part off the cord, position the mother in knee-chest or Trendelenburg position, and administer oxygen","Clamp and cut the pulsating umbilical cord immediately before delivery","Place the mother in high Fowler\u0027s position and administer oxytocin"],"a":1,"e":"Umbilical cord prolapse causes acute cord compression between the presenting foetal part and maternal pelvis, cutting off foetal oxygenation. The nurse must keep gloved fingers inside the vagina continuously lifting the foetal head upward off the cord (combined with knee-chest or steep Trendelenburg positioning and stopping any oxytocin infusion) until emergency Caesarean delivery is accomplished.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02319","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"National Leprosy Eradication Programme (NLEP)","q":"A patient newly diagnosed with multibacillary leprosy under the National Leprosy Eradication Programme (NLEP) starts Multidrug Therapy (MDT) containing rifampicin, clofazimine, and dapsone. Which finding is a cardinal diagnostic sign of leprosy, and what harmless drug side effect should the nurse forewarn the patient about to preserve adherence?","o":["Acute blistering rash; lifelong permanent deafness from dapsone","Painful genital ulcer; blue-green nails from rifampicin","Hypopigmented/erythematous anaesthetic skin patch with thickened peripheral nerve; harmless reddish-orange urine from rifampicin and brownish skin discolouration from clofazimine","High-grade continuous fever; black stools from rifampicin"],"a":2,"e":"Cardinal signs of leprosy include: (1) hypopigmented or reddish skin patch with definite loss of sensation, (2) thickened/enlarged peripheral nerve with sensory loss or muscle weakness, and (3) acid-fast bacilli on slit-skin smear. Counselling patients beforehand that once-monthly supervised rifampicin turns urine/tears/sweat reddish-orange and clofazimine causes reversible brownish skin pigmentation prevents unnecessary anxiety and treatment default.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02320","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Rabies Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP)","q":"A child presents 30 minutes after a deep, bleeding stray dog bite on the leg (WHO Category III exposure). Along with anti-rabies vaccine and local infiltration of rabies immunoglobulin (RIG), what is the single most critical immediate local wound care measure?","o":["Suture the deep wound tightly right away and apply red chilli/lime paste","Administer rabies immunoglobulin exclusively into the gluteal muscle far from the bite","Cover the unwashed wound with an occlusive plaster only","Wash and flush the wound thoroughly with soap and running water for at least 15 minutes, followed by povidone-iodine antiseptic, and avoid tight suturing"],"a":3,"e":"Immediate mechanical and chemical wound toilet—vigorous flushing with soap and running water for \u003e= 15 minutes followed by virucidal antiseptic (povidone-iodine or 70% alcohol)—physically removes and inactivates rabies virus at the entry site, reducing rabies risk dramatically. In Category III bites, RIG must be infiltrated directly into and around all wounds (not solely distant IM), and wounds should not be tightly sutured unless unavoidable after RIG infiltration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02321","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Medication Error Reporting","q":"After accidentally administering 50 mg of an antihypertensive instead of the prescribed 25 mg, the nurse assesses the patient\u0027s blood pressure, notifies the physician, and completes an internal hospital incident report. What should the nurse document in the patient\u0027s medical progress record?","o":["Objective facts only: medication and dose given, time, physician notified, vital signs/patient assessment, and interventions implemented—without mentioning the incident report","\u0027Medication error made by night nurse; formal incident report #402 submitted to nursing superintendent\u0027","Blame the pharmacy technician in the nursing notes","Delete the medication entry from the MAR so no one notices"],"a":0,"e":"The patient\u0027s medical record is a legal clinical document containing factual care events (what occurred, assessments, provider notification, and monitoring/interventions). Incident/occurrence reports are confidential administrative quality-improvement tools and must NOT be referenced or stapled inside the patient\u0027s chart; falsifying or deleting records is illegal and unethical.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02322","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)","q":"Four days after hip arthroplasty, a patient develops unilateral right calf swelling, warmth, redness, and tenderness. While awaiting venous Doppler ultrasound and physician orders, which nursing action is strictly contraindicated?","o":["Keeping the patient on bed rest with the affected leg gently elevated","Massaging the painful right calf deeply to relieve muscle cramping","Monitoring respiratory rate, chest pain, and SpO2 for signs of pulmonary embolism","Measuring and comparing bilateral calf circumferences"],"a":1,"e":"Unilateral calf swelling, warmth, erythema, and pain post-orthopaedic surgery strongly suggest lower-extremity Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT). Massaging or compressing the affected limb vigorously risks dislodging the fresh venous thrombus into the pulmonary circulation, causing a potentially fatal pulmonary embolism (PE).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02323","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations (including First Aid)","topic":"Choking Management in Adults vs Infants","q":"A 7-month-old infant suddenly chokes on a piece of solid food, becomes silent (unable to cry or cough), and turns cyanotic while still conscious. Which emergency first-aid sequence should the nurse perform?","o":["Hold the infant upside down by the ankles and shake vigorously","Perform forceful upward abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre) below the xiphoid","Deliver alternating cycles of 5 firm back blows (between the shoulder blades, head lower than chest) and 5 chest thrusts (two fingers on lower half of sternum)","Sweep blindly deep inside the infant\u0027s throat with an index finger"],"a":2,"e":"In a conscious infant (\u003c 1 year) with severe foreign-body airway obstruction, abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre) are contraindicated because the infant\u0027s relatively large, unprotected liver can rupture. Instead, support the infant\u0027s jaw/neck prone along the forearm (head lower than trunk) for 5 back slaps, then turn supine for 5 chest thrusts until the object is expelled or the infant becomes unresponsive (when CPR begins).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02324","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Non-Invasive Blood Pressure Measurement","q":"At a vital signs OSPE station, a student measures blood pressure on an obese adult\u0027s upper arm using a narrow paediatric/small adult cuff while the patient\u0027s arm hangs unsupported below heart level. How will these two technical errors distort the reading?","o":["Both errors produce a falsely high blood pressure reading","Both errors produce a falsely low blood pressure reading","Small cuff produces falsely low BP; unsupported arm produces no change","Blood pressure cannot be measured unless the patient stands upright"],"a":0,"e":"Accurate sphygmomanometry requires a cuff bladder width of ~40% of mid-upper arm circumference (length 80%) and supporting the arm at heart (right atrial) level. A cuff that is too narrow requires higher inflation pressure to compress the brachial artery, yielding falsely elevated BP; similarly, an unsupported arm hanging below heart level adds hydrostatic column pressure (~0.77 mmHg/cm) plus isometric muscle tension, further inflating the reading.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02325","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Thyroid Storm (Thyrotoxic Crisis)","q":"A patient with poorly controlled Graves\u0027 disease develops pneumonia and presents with fever 40.4°C, heart rate 166 bpm, severe agitation, and vomiting (thyroid storm). Which medication MUST be strictly avoided for fever control, and why is an antithyroid drug (e.g., propylthiouracil) given at least 1 hour BEFORE Lugol\u0027s iodine?","o":["Avoid beta-blockers; give aspirin 650 mg immediately","Avoid paracetamol; give iodine first to stimulate T4 synthesis","Avoid IV fluids; give oral thyroxine","Avoid aspirin/salicylates (which displace T4 from thyroid-binding globulin and worsen free hormone levels); give PTU first to block new hormone synthesis before iodine blocks hormone release"],"a":3,"e":"In thyroid storm, propylthiouracil (PTU) is given first to inhibit thyroid peroxidase (blocking new hormone synthesis) and peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion. Waiting ~1 hour before giving potassium iodide (Lugol\u0027s solution) prevents the gland from using the iodine load as substrate to synthesize even more thyroid hormone (Jod-Basedow phenomenon). Paracetamol and external cooling treat hyperpyrexia, whereas salicylates (aspirin) are contraindicated because they displace T4/T3 from plasma binding proteins, raising free active hormone.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02326","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"24-Hour Intake Calculation","q":"Over a 24-hour shift, a surgical patient receives 1,500 mL of IV 0.9% Normal Saline, two 250 mL enteral tube feedings, four 100 mL sterile water tube flushes, and three 50 mL IV antibiotic piggyback infusions. What is the patient\u0027s total 24-hour fluid intake?","o":["2,550 mL","2,750 mL","2,150 mL","2,400 mL"],"a":0,"e":"Sum all parenteral and enteral liquid volumes administered over 24 hours: Continuous IV Normal Saline = 1,500 mL; Enteral feedings (2 × 250 mL) = 500 mL; Water flushes (4 × 100 mL) = 400 mL; IV piggyback antibiotics (3 × 50 mL) = 150 mL. Total 24-hour intake = 1,500 + 500 + 400 + 150 = 2,550 mL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02327","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Cardiac Tamponade","q":"Following blunt chest trauma, a patient develops acute dyspnoea, narrow pulse pressure with hypotension (82/66 mmHg), distended jugular veins, and muffled heart sounds on auscultation. Which life-threatening emergency does Beck\u0027s triad indicate, and what definitive bedside procedure should the nurse prepare for?","o":["Acute inferior myocardial infarction; immediate diuresis with furosemide","Cardiac tamponade; urgent ultrasound-guided pericardiocentesis","Bacterial endocarditis; routine oral antibiotics only","Tension pneumothorax; oral bronchodilators"],"a":1,"e":"Beck\u0027s triad—hypotension (low cardiac output), jugular venous distension (impaired right ventricular filling), and muffled/distant heart sounds (fluid/blood dampening acoustic transmission)—characterizes cardiac tamponade, often accompanied by pulsus paradoxus (\u003e 10 mmHg inspiratory SBP drop). Rapid accumulation of fluid/blood in the non-compliant pericardial sac restricts ventricular filling; emergent pericardiocentesis (or surgical pericardial window) restores cardiac output immediately.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02328","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Post-Gastrectomy Dumping Syndrome","q":"Two weeks after partial gastrectomy (Billroth II), a patient reports dizziness, sweating, palpitations, and crampy diarrhoea 20 minutes after eating a sweet breakfast with hot tea. Which dietary modification should the nurse teach to prevent dumping syndrome?","o":["Drink 500 mL of sweetened juice together with each large meal","Stand upright and jog immediately after finishing meals","Eat small, frequent meals high in protein and moderate fat while avoiding simple sugars, drink liquids 30–60 minutes between meals (not during meals), and lie down for 20–30 minutes after eating","Consume exclusively high-sugar snacks every hour"],"a":2,"e":"Early dumping syndrome occurs 15–30 minutes postprandially when hyperosmolar chyme rapidly empties from the surgically reduced stomach into the jejunum, drawing fluid into the bowel lumen (causing bloating, diarrhoea, and hypovolaemia/vasomotor dizziness). Slowing gastric emptying by eating small dry meals rich in protein/complex carbs/fat, avoiding concentrated sugars, separating fluids from meals, and reclining briefly after eating effectively prevents episodes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02329","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD)","q":"A hospitalized client with Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) washes his hands 40 times daily until the skin is raw and chapped, causing him to miss meals and group therapy. Which initial nursing intervention is most therapeutic?","o":["Assist the client in washing his hands 50 times so he finishes faster","Lock the bathroom door abruptly and physically forbid all handwashing immediately","Ridicule the compulsion to show it is irrational","Allow structured time for the handwashing ritual initially while protecting skin integrity, gradually set collaborative limits, and teach anxiety-management techniques"],"a":3,"e":"Compulsive rituals in OCD serve as maladaptive attempts to neutralize intense obsessional anxiety. Abruptly blocking or forbidding rituals without alternative coping skills induces overwhelming panic. Therapeutic nursing care acknowledges the anxiety, protects physical health (emollients, ensuring meal nutrition), schedules structured times for rituals initially, and gradually tapers ritual time alongside relaxation and Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02330","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Auditory Hallucinations","q":"A patient with schizophrenia suddenly stares at the ceiling, covers his ears, and mutters angrily, \u0027No, I won\u0027t do it—stop telling me!\u0027 Which nursing response addresses immediate safety while maintaining reality orientation?","o":["\u0027I do not hear the voices, though I can see they are upsetting you. Can you tell me what the voices are commanding you to do right now?\u0027","\u0027Look closer—let\u0027s ask the person in the ceiling why they are shouting at you.\u0027","\u0027You are lying for attention; nobody is speaking.\u0027","\u0027Ignore them completely; it is illegal to talk to voices in the ward.\u0027"],"a":0,"e":"Command hallucinations instructing self-harm or violence toward others carry high safety risk. The nurse must first present reality gently without arguing (\u0027I do not hear the voices\u0027), validate the patient\u0027s emotional distress, and ask directly what the voices are saying so appropriate safety precautions (1:1 observation, de-escalation, PRN medication) can be instituted immediately.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02331","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Febrile Seizures","q":"An 18-month-old toddler brought to the outpatient clinic with high fever (39.6°C) suddenly experiences a 90-second generalized tonic-clonic febrile seizure. What is the correct immediate nursing action and accurate post-seizure parent education?","o":["Restrain the child\u0027s jerking limbs firmly and sponge with ice-cold alcohol","Protect the child from injury, place in lateral recovery position once safe, loosen neck clothing, time the seizure, give prescribed antipyretic once alert, and reassure parents that brief simple febrile seizures do not cause brain damage","Force a spoon between the teeth and immerse the convulsing child in an ice tub","Start lifelong phenobarbitone immediately for a single 90-second simple febrile seizure"],"a":1,"e":"Simple febrile seizures (\u003c 15 minutes, generalized, once in 24 hours in children 6 months–5 years without CNS infection) are benign and do not cause neurological damage. Immediate care mirrors general seizure first aid: cushion the head, turn to lateral recovery position to protect the airway, time the event, avoid oral objects or restraint, evaluate fever source, and educate parents calmly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02332","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Paediatric Digoxin Dose Calculation","q":"A 10 kg infant with ventricular septal defect and heart failure is prescribed oral digoxin 10 mcg/kg once daily. The available paediatric digoxin elixir concentration is 50 mcg/mL (0.05 mg/mL). After auscultating the apical pulse for 1 full minute (HR 126 bpm), how many mL should the nurse draw up using a calibrated oral syringe?","o":["4.0 mL","1.0 mL","2.0 mL","0.5 mL"],"a":2,"e":"Step 1: Calculate ordered dose = 10 kg × 10 mcg/kg = 100 mcg. Step 2: Divide by concentration (50 mcg/mL) = 100 mcg ÷ 50 mcg/mL = 2.0 mL. In infants, always auscultate the apical pulse for 1 full minute prior to digoxin and hold/verify with the prescriber if infant HR \u003c 90–110 bpm (age-specific threshold).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02333","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Involution and Lochia Assessment","q":"During postnatal ward rounds, which chronological sequence and colour change represents normal physiological lochia progression across the first 2–3 weeks postpartum?","o":["Greenish-yellow foul-smelling discharge from Day 2 onward","Lochia alba (white) -\u003e Lochia serosa (pink) -\u003e Lochia rubra (red)","Continuous bright-red bleeding with large clots for 6 weeks","Lochia rubra (bright red, Days 1–4) -\u003e Lochia serosa (pinkish-brown, Days 5–9) -\u003e Lochia alba (yellowish-white, Day 10 onward)"],"a":3,"e":"Normal puerperal uterine involution sheds decidual tissue and blood progressively: Lochia rubra (red, Days 1–4, mainly blood/decidua), Lochia serosa (pinkish-brown, Days 5–9, serous exudate/leukocytes/fewer RBCs), and Lochia alba (creamy white-yellow, ~Days 10–14 up to 3–6 weeks, predominantly leukocytes and mucus). Sudden reversion from serosa/alba back to bright-red bleeding or foul odour signals secondary PPH, retained placenta, or endometritis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02334","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Lactation Mastitis","q":"Two weeks postpartum, a lactating mother develops fever (38.7°C), malaise, and a painful, warm, red, wedge-shaped indurated area on her left breast (acute mastitis). Along with prescribed antistaphylococcal antibiotics and analgesia, what essential lactation advice should the nurse give?","o":["Stop breastfeeding completely on both breasts and bind the chest tightly","Continue frequent breastfeeding or regular milk expression from the affected breast (starting on the affected or comfortable side to ensure complete emptying) alongside warm compresses before feeds","Apply ice-cold tight binders to suppress all lactation permanently","Discard expressed breastmilk forever after a single episode of mastitis"],"a":1,"e":"Lactational mastitis usually follows milk stasis and cracked nipples allowing Staphylococcus aureus entry. Effective milk drainage—frequent breastfeeding (infant is not harmed by milk from mastitis) or gentle pumping/hand expression—is essential to resolve stasis, prevent breast abscess formation, and preserve milk supply while prescribed antibiotics clear infection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02335","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Safe Drinking Water Chlorination","q":"Following chlorination of a community drinking water source (after a 1-hour contact period), what minimum free residual chlorine level measured by the orthotolidine-arsenite (OTA) or DPD test indicates microbiologically safe water under routine Indian public health norms?","o":["0.5 mg/L","0.05 mg/L","10.0 mg/L","5.0 mg/L"],"a":0,"e":"After meeting the chlorine demand of organic matter and allowing a 1-hour contact period, a free residual chlorine concentration of \u003e= 0.5 mg/L (0.5 parts per million) ensures continuous disinfection against vegetative waterborne pathogens (such as Vibrio cholerae, Salmonella Typhi, and E. coli) in routine drinking water supplies (rising to 0.7–1.0+ mg/L during outbreaks/emergency superchlorination).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02336","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Lymphatic Filariasis Elimination","q":"In a district endemic for lymphatic filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti), which dual community nursing strategy combines transmission interruption with disability management for patients having chronic lower-limb elephantiasis?","o":["Surgical amputation of all swollen limbs and isolation","Annual supervised Mass Drug Administration (DEC + albendazole, or IDA triple regimen in designated blocks) for eligible residents, paired with daily limb washing with soap/water, drying, elevation, and skin care","Withholding all drugs from asymptomatic microfilaria carriers","Single dose of chloroquine taken monthly without limb care"],"a":1,"e":"India\u0027s Lymphatic Filariasis Elimination strategy combines annual Mass Drug Administration (MDA) using diethylcarbamazine (DEC) + albendazole (or ivermectin + DEC + albendazole [IDA]) to clear microfilariae from asymptomatic carriers and halt Culex mosquito transmission, alongside Morbidity Management and Disability Prevention (MMDP)—simple daily soap-and-water washing, interdigital drying, antifungal care, limb elevation, and exercises to prevent acute adenolymphangitis attacks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02337","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Burn Surface Area Estimation","q":"Using the Wallace Rule of Nines for an adult patient who sustained deep partial-thickness burns over the entire anterior trunk and the entire surface of both upper extremities (front and back), what is the estimated Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) burned?","o":["45%","18%","36%","27%"],"a":2,"e":"According to Wallace\u0027s Rule of Nines in adults: Head \u0026 neck = 9%; each entire upper extremity = 9% (so both upper limbs = 18%); anterior trunk = 18%; posterior trunk = 18%; each entire lower extremity = 18%; perineum = 1%. Adding entire anterior trunk (18%) + both upper limbs (9% + 9% = 18%) yields 36% TBSA.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02338","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations (including First Aid)","topic":"Chemical Eye Injury","q":"A laboratory worker accidentally splashes strong sodium hydroxide (alkali) cleaner into his right eye. What is the immediate first-aid priority by the nurse?","o":["Apply a tight pressure patch over the unwashed eye and wait 2 hours","Rub the cornea briskly with dry gauze","Instil weak vinegar/acetic acid drops to neutralize the alkali","Irrigate the affected eye immediately and continuously from inner to outer canthus with copious clean water or 0.9% Normal Saline for at least 15–30 minutes while protecting the unaffected left eye"],"a":3,"e":"Alkali chemicals penetrate ocular tissues rapidly via liquefactive necrosis and can cause permanent blindness within minutes. Immediate, continuous, copious irrigation with Normal Saline or clean running water from inner to outer canthus (so runoff does not contaminate the unaffected eye) for \u003e= 15–30 minutes—while everting eyelids and removing contact lenses—is the decisive sight-saving intervention.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02339","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Subcutaneous Insulin Administration","q":"At a medication administration OSPE station, the GNM nurse prepares to inject regular insulin subcutaneously into a thin adult patient\u0027s abdomen using a 6 mm insulin syringe needle. Which technique is correct?","o":["Pinch a skinfold gently, insert at 45–90 degrees into subcutaneous tissue, inject steadily without aspirating, withdraw, and do NOT massage the site; rotate injection spots systematically within the abdomen","Use the exact same 1 cm spot daily to minimize pain","Inject directly into the deltoid muscle deeply and rub with warm water","Aspirate for 10 seconds and massage the abdomen vigorously after withdrawal"],"a":0,"e":"Routine aspiration is unnecessary and discouraged for subcutaneous insulin/heparin injections because it increases tissue trauma and bruising. Massaging accelerates unpredictable rapid absorption into capillaries (risking sudden hypoglycaemia), and failing to rotate spots causes lipohypertrophy (which impairs insulin absorption). Rotating systematically \u003e= 1 cm apart within the abdominal region ensures consistent pharmacokinetics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02340","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma","q":"A 62-year-old woman presents with sudden agonizing right eye pain, headache, nausea/vomiting, blurred vision with rainbow-coloured halos around lights, a hazy cornea, a mid-dilated fixed pupil, and a stony-hard eyeball on palpation. Which medication MUST the nurse withhold/avoid while preparing prescribed miotics (pilocarpine) and IV acetazolamide/mannitol?","o":["Oral acetazolamide","Atropine or cyclopentolate (mydriatic/anticholinergic eye drops)","Topical pilocarpine 2% drops","Intravenous mannitol"],"a":1,"e":"Acute angle-closure glaucoma is an ophthalmic emergency caused by sudden pupillary block obstructing aqueous humour outflow through the trabecular meshwork, spiking intraocular pressure (IOP) and threatening irreversible optic nerve blindness. Anticholinergic/mydriatic drops (atropine, tropicamide, cyclopentolate) dilate the pupil further, thickening the peripheral iris and worsening angle closure; miotics (pilocarpine), carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (acetazolamide), and osmotic agents (mannitol) rapidly lower IOP.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02341","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Oral Tablet Calculation","q":"A hypertensive patient is prescribed oral metoprolol tartrate 37.5 mg once daily. The ward stock supplies scored 25 mg tablets. How many tablets should the nurse administer for a single dose?","o":["2.5 tablets","1 tablet","1.5 tablets","2 tablets"],"a":2,"e":"Using the standard medication dosage formula: Number of tablets = Desired Dose ÷ Available Dose per tablet = 37.5 mg ÷ 25 mg/tablet = 1.5 tablets (one whole tablet plus one half of a scored tablet). Always verify apical pulse and blood pressure prior to administering beta-blockers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02342","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Haemodialysis Vascular Access","q":"A patient with chronic kidney disease on maintenance haemodialysis has a matured arteriovenous (AV) fistula in the left forearm. Which nursing precaution and shift assessment preserve fistula patency?","o":["Measure blood pressure on the left arm hourly and draw blood from the fistula vein for routine CBC","Sleep exclusively on the left arm to cushion the fistula","Apply tight elastic compression bandages over the fistula constantly","Strictly avoid BP cuffs, venipunctures, IV cannulas, and tight watches/sleeves on the left arm, and palpate for a thrill and auscultate for a bruit every shift"],"a":3,"e":"An AV fistula is a high-flow surgical anastomosis between an artery and vein essential for life-sustaining haemodialysis. External compression (BP cuff inflation, tourniquets, tight sleeves, sleeping on the arm) or routine venipuncture/IV access can cause fistula thrombosis, haematoma, or infection. Assessing a palpable buzzing vibration (\u0027thrill\u0027) and audible whooshing sound (\u0027bruit\u0027) confirms patency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02343","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Antitubercular Drug Toxicity Counselling","q":"A patient taking the daily HRZE antitubercular regimen (Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol) reports numbness and tingling (\u0027pins and needles\u0027) in both feet, while another patient reports blurred vision and difficulty distinguishing red from green. Which two drugs respectively explain these toxicities, and what vitamin prevents isoniazid neuropathy?","o":["Isoniazid causes peripheral neuropathy (prevented/treated with Pyridoxine/Vitamin B6); Ethambutol causes retrobulbar optic neuritis (requiring immediate vision review and stopping ethambutol)","Ethambutol causes neuropathy; Streptomycin causes green urine; give Vitamin K","Pyrazinamide causes optic neuritis; Isoniazid causes orange tears; give Vitamin D","Rifampicin causes neuropathy; Pyrazinamide causes red-green blindness; give Vitamin C"],"a":0,"e":"Isoniazid interferes with pyridoxal phosphate metabolism, causing dose-dependent peripheral neuropathy (stocking-glove paraesthesias), prevented by daily pyridoxine (Vitamin B6) supplementation (10–25 mg/day; higher in pregnancy/diabetes/malnutrition). Ethambutol can cause retrobulbar optic neuritis manifesting as reduced visual acuity and red-green colour blindness—requiring prompt ophthalmic evaluation and discontinuation of ethambutol to avoid permanent blindness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02344","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Psychological Defense Mechanisms","q":"After being sharply reprimanded by the nursing supervisor for a documentation mistake, a ward staff member stays silent in the office, then walks to the ward pantry and angrily yells at a junior nursing student over a minor tea spill. Which ego defense mechanism is illustrated?","o":["Regression","Displacement","Reaction formation","Sublimation"],"a":1,"e":"Displacement redirects pent-up unacceptable emotions (such as anger or frustration) from a threatening, powerful target (the supervisor) onto a safer, less threatening substitute target (the junior student). By contrast, Sublimation channels impulses into socially constructive work, Reaction formation expresses the exact opposite feeling, and Regression reverts to earlier developmental behaviour.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02345","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Delirium vs Dementia","q":"A previously alert, oriented 76-year-old woman who underwent hip surgery 2 days ago suddenly becomes disoriented at night (\u0027sundowning\u0027), picks at imaginary threads on the bedsheet, alternates between agitation and drowsiness, and has a newly positive urinary nitrite/leukocyte test. Which condition is most likely, and what is the nursing priority?","o":["Malingering; ignore the confusion","Schizophrenia onset; isolate in a dark silent room with physical restraints","Acute delirium precipitated by urinary tract infection/postoperative stress; treat the underlying medical cause (UTI/hypoxia/meds), maintain safety, and provide calm reorientation","Irreversible late-stage Alzheimer\u0027s dementia; arrange long-term psychiatric institutionalization immediately"],"a":2,"e":"Delirium is an acute, fluctuating disturbance in attention, awareness, and cognition (often worse at night, with altered sleep-wake cycle and visual/tactile hallucinations) triggered by reversible physiological insults such as UTI, sepsis, hypoxia, electrolyte imbalance, or anticholinergic drugs. Unlike dementia (insidious, chronic progressive decline with intact consciousness early on), delirium resolves rapidly once the underlying trigger is treated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02346","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Acute Laryngotracheobronchitis (Croup)","q":"A 2-year-old child wakes at 2 AM with a harsh \u0027barking\u0027 seal-like cough, hoarse cry, and inspiratory stridor at rest following 2 days of mild coryza (viral croup). Along with keeping the child calm in the mother\u0027s arms and giving oral/IM dexamethasone, nebulized epinephrine is administered. What critical post-nebulization nursing observation is required before discharge?","o":["Administer high-dose oral sedatives to suppress cough reflex completely","Perform immediate blind throat suctioning deeply","Discharge home 5 minutes after nebulized epinephrine because effects last 48 hours","Observe the child closely for at least 3–4 hours after nebulized epinephrine for rebound airway oedema/stridor recurrence as the vasoconstrictive effect wears off"],"a":3,"e":"Viral laryngotracheobronchitis (croup, typically parainfluenza virus) causes subglottic oedema manifesting as barking cough, hoarseness, and inspiratory stridor. Corticosteroids (dexamethasone) reduce mucosal inflammation over several hours, whereas nebulized epinephrine rapidly shrinks subglottic oedema via alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction for ~1–2 hours. Children must be monitored for 3–4 hours post-epinephrine to detect rebound stridor before safe discharge.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02347","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Neonatal Sepsis Antibiotic Calculation","q":"A 3 kg term neonate with suspected early-onset neonatal sepsis is prescribed IV/IM gentamicin at 4 mg/kg/dose once daily. The available paediatric vial contains 10 mg/mL (20 mg in 2 mL). What exact volume in mL should the nurse draw up for one dose?","o":["1.2 mL","0.6 mL","3.0 mL","2.4 mL"],"a":0,"e":"Step 1: Calculate ordered dose = 3 kg × 4 mg/kg = 12 mg. Step 2: Calculate required volume using concentration 10 mg/mL: 12 mg ÷ 10 mg/mL = 1.2 mL. Using a 1 mL or 2 mL syringe with clear graduations prevents neonatal dosing errors.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02348","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Expected Date of Delivery (EDD)","q":"A pregnant woman with regular 28-day menstrual cycles reports that the first day of her Last Menstrual Period (LMP) was 10 January 2026. Using Naegele\u0027s rule, what is her Expected Date of Delivery (EDD)?","o":["17 November 2026","17 October 2026","10 October 2026","03 October 2026"],"a":1,"e":"Naegele\u0027s rule estimates a 280-day (40-week) gestation by adding 9 calendar months and 7 days to the first day of the LMP (equivalently, subtracting 3 months, adding 7 days, and adding 1 year). Here: Day 10 + 7 days = 17; January + 9 months = October (2026). Thus, the EDD is 17 October 2026.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02349","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Hyperemesis Gravidarum","q":"A primigravida at 10 weeks gestation is admitted for hyperemesis gravidarum with intractable vomiting, 6% body weight loss, dry mucous membranes, and 3+ ketonuria. Alongside NPO gut rest and IV antiemetics, which micronutrient MUST be infused intravenously BEFORE giving dextrose-containing fluids?","o":["Vitamin E","Vitamin K","Thiamine (Vitamin B1) to prevent precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy","Oral iron tablets immediately"],"a":2,"e":"Prolonged intractable vomiting in hyperemesis gravidarum depletes maternal thiamine (Vitamin B1) reserves. Infusing dextrose-containing IV fluids without first replenishing thiamine consumes the tiny remaining B1 stores as a cofactor in glucose metabolism, risking acute Wernicke encephalopathy (ataxia, ophthalmoplegia, confusion). Always administer parenteral thiamine prior to or with initial glucose-containing infusions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02350","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Dengue Vector Control","q":"During an urban dengue prevention drive, why does the community health nurse emphasize observing a weekly \u0027Dry Day\u0027—emptying, scrubbing, and drying desert coolers, flower-pot trays, discarded tyres, and uncovered water storage containers?","o":["Anopheles mosquitoes breed solely inside dry sand","Dengue virus multiplies directly inside dry metal containers without mosquitoes","Culex mosquitoes bite exclusively at noon in clean coolers","Aedes aegypti is a day-biting mosquito that breeds primarily in small artificial collections of clean stagnant domestic water, with eggs maturing in ~7 days"],"a":3,"e":"Aedes aegypti (the primary dengue and chikungunya vector) is an anthropophilic, daytime-biting (\u0027tiger\u0027) mosquito whose eggs adhere to container walls and hatch in small domestic/peridomestic clean water collections (coolers, tyres, flower pots, overhead tanks). Because the aquatic egg-to-adult cycle takes roughly 7–10 days, weekly emptying, scrubbing (to dislodge eggs), and drying breaks the vector life cycle effectively.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02351","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Endemic Fluorosis Prevention","q":"In an Indian village with excess groundwater fluoride (\u003e 1.5 mg/L) causing dental mottling and skeletal fluorosis, the community nurse demonstrates the Nalgonda technique of domestic water defluoridation. Which chemical pair is added sequentially to raw water with stirring, flocculation, and settling?","o":["Lime (calcium hydroxide) and Alum (aluminium sulphate), along with bleaching powder for disinfection","Potassium permanganate and table salt","Baking soda and vinegar","Copper sulphate and urea"],"a":0,"e":"Developed by NEERI (Nagpur), the Nalgonda technique is a simple, low-cost domestic/community defluoridation method widely taught in Indian community health nursing. Sequential addition of bleaching powder (disinfection), lime (alkalinity adjustment), and alum (coagulation/flocculation), followed by rapid mixing, slow flocculation, 1-hour sedimentation, and decanting removes excess fluoride as insoluble aluminium-fluoride complexes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02352","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Acute Pulmonary Embolism (PE)","q":"On postoperative Day 5 after pelvic surgery, an immobilized patient suddenly develops sharp pleuritic chest pain, tachypnoea (32/min), tachycardia (126 bpm), haemoptysis, anxiety, and SpO2 84% with relatively clear breath sounds. What is the nurse\u0027s immediate priority while alerting the emergency physician?","o":["Perform vigorous abdominal massage","Elevate the head of the bed (high/semi-Fowler\u0027s), apply high-flow supplemental oxygen immediately, monitor continuous ECG/vitals, and prepare for urgent diagnostic imaging (CTPA) and anticoagulation","Assist the patient to walk briskly down the corridor","Lower the head flat and administer oral aspirin alone"],"a":1,"e":"Sudden unexplained dyspnoea, pleuritic chest pain, tachypnoea, tachycardia, hypoxaemia, and haemoptysis in an immobilized postoperative patient are classic indicators of acute pulmonary embolism (PE). Raising the head of the bed eases diaphragmatic excursion, immediate high-flow oxygen treats acute V/Q-mismatch hypoxaemia, and urgent physician escalation enables CT pulmonary angiography and rapid anticoagulation/thrombolysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02353","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations (including First Aid)","topic":"Exertional Heat Stroke","q":"During an intense summer heatwave, a construction worker collapses with core temperature 41.2°C, confusion, and seizures (heat stroke). Which immediate first-aid intervention is most effective while arranging emergency transport?","o":["Force iced water orally while unconscious and convulsing","Wait 2 hours for spontaneous sweating","Move to shade, remove outer clothing, initiate rapid active cooling (cold water immersion or tepid misting with fanning and ice packs over neck, axillae, and groin) while protecting the airway","Give oral paracetamol 1,000 mg and cover with heavy woollen blankets"],"a":2,"e":"Heat stroke (core temperature \u003e 40°C with central nervous system dysfunction—delirium, stupor, or seizures) is a thermoregulatory failure causing multi-organ cellular necrosis unless core temperature is lowered rapidly. Cold-water immersion or vigorous evaporative cooling (wetting skin + fanning + ice packs over large vessels in neck, axillae, and groin) lowers core temperature promptly; oral antipyretics (paracetamol/aspirin) are ineffective because the hypothalamic set-point is NOT elevated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02354","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Ophthalmic Medication Instillation","q":"At an ophthalmic medication OSPE station, a GNM student must administer both timolol eye drops and erythromycin eye ointment into the same eye. Which technique and sequence are correct?","o":["Hold the eyelids shut tightly without waiting between medications","Apply thick ointment first, then instil drops directly onto the centre of the cornea","Touch the dropper tip firmly against the eyeball to steady the hand","Instil the liquid eye drops FIRST into the lower conjunctival sac (never onto the cornea), apply gentle pressure over the inner canthus (nasolacrimal punctum) for 1–2 minutes to limit systemic absorption, wait 5 minutes, and apply ointment last"],"a":3,"e":"Liquid eye drops must always precede ointments because oily ointment coats the eye and blocks aqueous drop absorption. Dropping into the lower conjunctival fornix avoids painful corneal reflex blinking, maintaining sterility prevents bottle-tip contamination, waiting 5 minutes prevents washout, and pressing the nasolacrimal duct blocks systemic absorption of drugs like timolol (preventing systemic beta-blockade bradycardia/bronchospasm).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02355","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Oral Liquid Solution Calculation","q":"A patient with diuretic-induced hypokalaemia is ordered 30 mEq of oral potassium chloride liquid. The pharmacy supplies potassium chloride elixir labelled 20 mEq per 15 mL. How many millilitres (mL) of elixir should the nurse measure and dilute in cold juice/water?","o":["22.5 mL","10 mL","15 mL","30 mL"],"a":0,"e":"Using Desired ÷ Have × Volume: (30 mEq ÷ 20 mEq) × 15 mL = 1.5 × 15 mL = 22.5 mL. Always dilute oral potassium chloride elixir in at least 120 mL of water or fruit juice and administer after meals to minimize severe gastric mucosal irritation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02356","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Chest Tube Disconnection","q":"While transferring a pneumothorax patient in the ward, the chest tube accidentally pulls loose from the water-seal drainage system tubing, leaving the distal end open to room air. What must the nurse do immediately?","o":["Clamp the chest tube permanently for 4 hours without reconnecting","Immediately submerge the open sterile distal tip of the chest tube 2–3 cm below the surface of a bottle of sterile water or Normal Saline to re-establish a temporary water seal while obtaining a new sterile drainage unit","Leave the open tube exposed to room air while walking to fetch another unit","Push the contaminated floor-touched connector directly back in without cleaning"],"a":1,"e":"An open chest tube lets atmospheric air rush straight into the negative-pressure pleural cavity, collapsing the lung; conversely, prolonged clamping when an active pleural air leak exists causes fatal tension pneumothorax. Submerging the open end 2–3 cm into sterile water/Normal Saline immediately restores a one-way water seal (allowing trapped pleural air to bubble out without letting room air enter) until a sterile replacement system is connected.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02357","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Acute Peritonitis / Perforated Ulcer","q":"A patient with chronic NSAID use develops sudden agonizing abdominal pain, fever, tachycardia, absent bowel sounds, rebound tenderness, and generalized \u0027board-like\u0027 abdominal wall rigidity, with pneumoperitoneum under the right hemidiaphragm on erect X-ray. Which nursing intervention is contraindicated?","o":["Positioning comfortably with knees slightly flexed while preparing for emergency laparotomy","Placing a nasogastric tube on low suction as ordered","Administering a soapsuds enema, oral laxative, or heating pad to the abdomen","Keeping the patient strictly NPO and initiating IV fluids/prescribed broad-spectrum antibiotics"],"a":2,"e":"Free air under the diaphragm and a rigid, silent, rebound-tender abdomen signal a perforated hollow viscus with generalized peritonitis—a surgical emergency. Laxatives, enemas, and oral intake stimulate peristalsis and worsen faecal/gastric spillage into the peritoneal cavity, while heat increases vasodilation and perforation spread. NPO status, NG decompression, IV fluid/electrolyte/antibiotic resuscitation, and urgent surgery are indicated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02358","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Nephrolithiasis (Renal Calculi)","q":"A patient is admitted with severe colicky flank pain radiating to the groin, nausea, and microscopic haematuria due to a 4 mm ureteric calculus. Why must the nurse instruct the patient to void exclusively through a fine gauze/mesh urine strainer?","o":["To measure urine specific gravity accurately","To prevent urinary tract infection automatically","To filter out red blood cells before discarding urine","To capture any spontaneously passed calculus so the laboratory can analyse its chemical composition and guide recurrence prevention"],"a":3,"e":"Most ureteric stones \u003c 5 mm pass spontaneously with adequate analgesia (NSAIDs/opioids), medical expulsive therapy (tamsulosin), and hydration (2.5–3 L/day unless obstructed). Straining all voided urine captures the passed stone for compositional analysis (calcium oxalate, uric acid, struvite, cystine), enabling targeted dietary and pharmacological prophylaxis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02359","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Postoperative Paralytic Ileus","q":"Forty-eight hours after an open laparotomy, a patient has uniform abdominal distension, no passage of flatus, mild diffuse discomfort, nausea, and completely absent bowel sounds in all four quadrants on auscultation (with serum K+ 3.0 mEq/L). What is the most likely postoperative condition and appropriate nursing care?","o":["Postoperative paralytic ileus aggravated by hypokalaemia; maintain NPO/NG decompression as ordered, correct serum potassium, and encourage early safe ambulation","Early mechanical volvulus; give high-fibre solid meals immediately","Acute appendicitis; administer magnesium hydroxide laxative","Normal oral feeding readiness; remove IV access"],"a":0,"e":"Paralytic (adynamic) ileus is temporary cessation of coordinated peristalsis after bowel handling, opioids, peritonitis, or hypokalaemia—characterized by diffuse abdominal distension, absent flatus, and silent/absent bowel sounds (unlike early mechanical obstruction, which produces hyperactive high-pitched tinkling rushes). Treatment includes NPO rest, NG decompression if vomiting, correcting hypokalaemia, minimizing opioids, and early ambulation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02360","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Osteoporosis Therapy (Alendronate)","q":"A postmenopausal woman prescribed weekly oral alendronate (bisphosphonate) for severe osteoporosis asks how to take the tablet safely. Which instruction prevents severe pill-induced oesophagitis and ensures absorption?","o":["Take concurrently with calcium and iron supplements","Take first thing in the morning on an empty stomach with a full glass (200 mL) of plain water ONLY, and remain sitting or standing upright for at least 30–60 minutes before eating or lying down","Take at bedtime with a glass of milk and lie flat immediately","Crush and chew the tablet with orange juice"],"a":1,"e":"Oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate) have \u003c 1% oral bioavailability (completely blocked by food, coffee, juice, milk, or calcium) and are fiercely caustic to oesophageal mucosa if retained in the lower oesophagus. Taking intact with plain water on an empty stomach and staying upright \u003e= 30–60 minutes protects the oesophagus and maximizes bone uptake.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02361","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Conversion Disorder","q":"After witnessing a severe family conflict, a 21-year-old woman abruptly loses the ability to move both legs despite completely normal MRI, nerve conduction studies, and deep tendon reflexes—yet appears remarkably calm and unconcerned about her sudden paralysis (\u0027la belle indifférence\u0027). Which therapeutic nursing approach is best?","o":["Order an electric wheelchair permanently on Day 1","Laugh at the normal MRI to embarrass her into walking","Accept her physical symptom neutrally without arguing or reinforcing excessive secondary gain, maintain routine self-care expectations safely, and explore underlying stressors gradually","Accuse her loudly of faking paralysis to get attention"],"a":2,"e":"Conversion disorder (Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder) produces genuine involuntary motor or sensory deficits incompatible with neurological pathology following acute psychological stress, sometimes accompanied by \u0027la belle indifférence\u0027 (unusual emotional unconcern). Unlike malingering, symptoms are unconscious. Nurses provide safe supportive physical therapy while shifting focus gently from physical deficits to verbalizing emotional distress without humiliating or rewarding disability.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02362","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Acute Panic Attack","q":"A hospitalized client suddenly develops intense terror, chest tightness, palpitations, trembling, and hyperventilation, crying out \u0027I am going to die!\u0027 (acute panic attack). What is the nurse\u0027s immediate priority intervention?","o":["Deliver a detailed 30-minute lecture on autonomic nervous system anatomy","Ask the client to fill out a 50-item psychological questionnaire immediately","Leave the client alone in the room so they can calm down privately","Stay calmly with the client in a quiet low-stimulus environment, speak in short simple clear sentences, and guide slow abdominal/pursed-lip breathing"],"a":3,"e":"During panic-level anxiety, perceptual field narrows severely and cognitive problem-solving fails, so complex explanations or questionnaires overwhelm the client further. Remaining physically present provides immediate reassurance and safety, while a calm, quiet setting, brief concrete phrases, and paced slow breathing reverse hyperventilation-induced respiratory alkalosis and restore self-control.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02363","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Tricyclic Antidepressants (Amitriptyline)","q":"An elderly male patient started on amitriptyline (a tricyclic antidepressant) for chronic neuropathic pain and depression reports dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, and inability to pass urine since morning with lower abdominal fullness. Which drug-induced mechanism explains these symptoms?","o":["Strong peripheral muscarinic anticholinergic blockade causing urinary retention, constipation, xerostomia, and blurred vision","Acute renal stone obstruction solely","Dopamine hyperstimulation","Cholinergic excess (SLUDGE)"],"a":0,"e":"Tricyclic antidepressants (amitriptyline, imipramine) block muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, alpha-1 adrenergic receptors (orthostatic hypotension), and cardiac sodium channels (QRS prolongation in overdose). In older men with benign prostatic hyperplasia, anticholinergic detrusor relaxation and sphincter contraction frequently precipitate acute urinary retention—requiring bladder scan/catheterization and prescriber review (often switching to an SSRI/SNRI).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02364","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Acute Rheumatic Fever","q":"A 10-year-old boy presents 3 weeks after an untreated sore throat with painful swollen knees and ankles that shift from joint to joint (migratory polyarthritis), a new apical pansystolic murmur (carditis), fever, and elevated ASO titre. Along with acute anti-inflammatory care and bed rest during active carditis, what long-term prophylactic regimen prevents recurrent rheumatic heart valve damage?","o":["Immediate surgical splenectomy","Regular long-term intramuscular Benzathine Penicillin G (every 3–4 weeks) or daily oral penicillin V prophylaxis for years as per guidelines","A single 3-day course of oral amoxicillin only","Lifelong monthly oral vitamin C alone"],"a":1,"e":"Acute Rheumatic Fever is an autoimmune post-streptococcal sequela of untreated Group A beta-haemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis. Major Jones criteria include carditis, migratory polyarthritis, Sydenham chorea, erythema marginatum, and subcutaneous nodules. Preventing recurrent streptococcal infections—and progressive rheumatic valvular heart disease—requires uninterrupted secondary prophylaxis with IM Benzathine Penicillin G every 3–4 weeks (duration based on carditis/valvular involvement).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02365","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Weight-Based Oral Corticosteroid Calculation","q":"A 20 kg child recovering from an acute asthma exacerbation is prescribed oral prednisolone at 1.5 mg/kg/day as a single morning dose with breakfast. Available prednisolone tablets are 10 mg each. How many tablets should the nurse administer?","o":["4 tablets","2 tablets","3 tablets","1.5 tablets"],"a":2,"e":"Step 1: Calculate total prescribed dose = 20 kg × 1.5 mg/kg = 30 mg. Step 2: Divide by strength per tablet = 30 mg ÷ 10 mg/tablet = 3 tablets. Giving oral corticosteroids in the morning with food mimics endogenous cortisol circadian rhythm and minimizes gastric irritation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02366","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Myelomeningocele (Spina Bifida)","q":"A newborn delivered with an exposed, thin-walled lumbosacral myelomeningocele sac is awaiting surgical closure. Which preoperative nursing care protocol prevents sac rupture, meningitis, and latex sensitization?","o":["Take rectal temperatures every 2 hours","Apply talcum powder to dry out the translucent sac","Place supine directly on the sac and cover with dry cotton wool","Position the infant prone (or side-lying with hips slightly flexed), cover the sac immediately with sterile non-adherent dressing moistened with warm 0.9% Normal Saline, avoid diapering tightly over the sac, and use strictly latex-free supplies"],"a":3,"e":"In myelomeningocele, meninges and neural elements protrude through an open vertebral defect. Placing the newborn supine or applying dry gauze tears the fragile membrane, causing CSF leakage and fatal meningitis. Prone positioning with warm sterile saline-soaked dressings keeps the sac moist and intact, avoiding rectal/perineal contamination and using latex-free gloves/catheters prevents severe latex allergy common in spina bifida.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02367","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Leopold\u0027s Manoeuvres (Obstetric Grips)","q":"During an antenatal abdominal examination after asking the pregnant woman at 36 weeks to empty her bladder, the nurse places both flat hands over the uterine fundus and palpates a broad, soft, irregular, non-ballotable mass (foetal breech). Which Leopold\u0027s manoeuvre is being performed?","o":["First Leopold\u0027s manoeuvre (Fundal grip)","Fourth Leopold\u0027s manoeuvre (Pelvic grip)","Second Leopold\u0027s manoeuvre (Lateral/Umbilical grip)","Third Leopold\u0027s manoeuvre (Pawlik\u0027s grip)"],"a":0,"e":"Leopold\u0027s four obstetric grips determine foetal lie, presentation, and engagement: (1) First/Fundal grip palpates the uterine fundus to identify whether the soft, irregular breech or hard, round, ballotable head occupies the fundus; (2) Second/Lateral grip locates the smooth curved foetal back versus small irregular limbs; (3) Third/Pawlik\u0027s grip grasps the lower pole above the symphysis to assess presenting part and mobility; (4) Fourth/Pelvic grip faces the mother\u0027s feet to assess engagement and cephalic prominence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02368","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Meconium-Stained Amniotic Fluid (MSAF)","q":"A term newborn is delivered through thick meconium-stained amniotic fluid and is limp, apnoeic, and has poor tone (non-vigorous). According to updated NRP/MoHFW NSSK neonatal resuscitation guidelines, what is the recommended immediate approach?","o":["Administer sodium bicarbonate IV before clearing the mouth","Provide warmth, dry/stimulate, gently clear visible secretions from mouth and nose if obstructing the airway, and initiate bag-and-mask Positive Pressure Ventilation (PPV) promptly within the Golden Minute if not breathing","Delay breathing assistance for 5 minutes to perform routine deep endotracheal intubation and tracheal suctioning on every non-vigorous infant","Hang the baby upside down by the ankles and slap the back"],"a":1,"e":"Updated ILCOR/NRP and MoHFW NSSK guidelines no longer recommend routine immediate laryngoscopy and endotracheal suctioning for non-vigorous newborns born through meconium-stained amniotic fluid, as evidence showed no reduction in Meconium Aspiration Syndrome and harmful delays in oxygenation. Instead, place under radiant warmer, quickly clear mouth/nose if obstructed, dry/stimulate, and start PPV within 60 seconds if apnoeic/gasping.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02369","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing \u0026 Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Cervical Cancer Screening in India","q":"Under India\u0027s National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD) at Primary Health Centres, a 38-year-old woman undergoes cervical cancer screening via Visual Inspection with 3%–5% Acetic Acid (VIA). Which finding constitutes a VIA-positive screen requiring cryotherapy/thermal ablation or colposcopy referral?","o":["Presence of mild menstrual spotting alone","Clear mucoid cervical discharge only","Distinct, well-defined opaque white (\u0027acetowhite\u0027) area appearing near the squamocolumnar junction (transformation zone) 1 minute after applying acetic acid","Uniformly smooth pink cervix with no colour change"],"a":2,"e":"Acetic acid (3%–5%) reversibly coagulates intracellular nuclear proteins. Because dysplastic precancerous cervical cells (CIN) possess high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic density, applying dilute acetic acid turns abnormal transformation-zone epithelium distinctly opaque white (\u0027acetowhite\u0027) within 1 minute. VIA enables low-cost, point-of-care \u0027screen-and-treat\u0027 referral for women aged 30–65 years every 5 years.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02370","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing I","topic":"Infectious Agent Characteristics","q":"In communicable disease epidemiology, which term specifically measures the proportion of clinically apparent cases that result in severe clinical manifestations or death (reflecting disease severity, often indexed by Case Fatality Rate)?","o":["Endemicity","Pathogenicity","Infectivity","Virulence"],"a":3,"e":"Epidemiological agent properties are distinct: Infectivity is the capacity to enter and multiply in a susceptible host (Attack Rate); Pathogenicity is the proportion of infected hosts who develop overt clinical disease; and Virulence measures the severity of clinical disease among clinically ill cases (proportion of severe/fatal cases, reflected in Case Fatality Rate).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02371","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Enteric Fever (Typhoid) Control","q":"Following a cluster of Salmonella Typhi enteric fever cases linked to a street food stall, a food handler is identified as an asymptomatic chronic gallbladder carrier shedding bacilli in stool. Which public health nursing interventions control transmission effectively?","o":["Temporarily exclude the chronic carrier from food handling until treated/cleared per medical protocol, enforce strict handwashing and sanitary excreta disposal, protect drinking water, and promote Typhoid Conjugate Vaccine (TCV)","Advise boiling all metal utensils in milk","Allow the untreated carrier to continue preparing cold salads bare-handed","Prescribe only oral ORS without treating water/sanitation"],"a":0,"e":"Roughly 2%–5% of typhoid patients become chronic biliary carriers (\u0027Typhoid Mary\u0027 pattern), shedding Salmonella Typhi intermittently in faeces. Because typhoid transmits via the faecal-oral \u00275 Fs\u0027 (food, fingers, flies, fomites, faeces), excluding carriers from food preparation until cured, chlorinating water, fly/sanitation control, and TCV vaccination break the chain of transmission.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02372","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Medical Gas Cylinder Safety","q":"In an Indian hospital ward, what is the standard colour coding of a medical Oxygen cylinder, and why must oil or petroleum grease NEVER be applied to oxygen cylinder valves or regulators?","o":["Green body with red shoulder; oil blocks pulse oximeter light","Black body with a White shoulder; compressed pure oxygen reacts violently with hydrocarbons (oil/grease) causing spontaneous ignition and explosion","Grey body with yellow shoulder; oil freezes oxygen into solid ice","Blue body with blue shoulder; oil makes the valve too slippery to close"],"a":1,"e":"Under Indian medical gas cylinder standards (IS 3933 / BIS), medical Oxygen cylinders have a Black body and a White shoulder (Nitrous Oxide is Blue; Carbon Dioxide is Grey). High-pressure oxygen is a powerful oxidizer; even trace amounts of oil, petroleum jelly, or hand grease on threads/regulators can ignite spontaneously under rapid compression friction, causing catastrophic fire or explosion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02373","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Anatomy and Physiology)","topic":"Cardiac Conduction System","q":"During the normal cardiac cycle, where does the electrical impulse experience a physiological delay of approximately 0.1 seconds to allow complete atrial emptying before ventricular contraction?","o":["Sinoatrial (SA) node","Bundle of His","Atrioventricular (AV) node","Purkinje fibres"],"a":2,"e":"The atrioventricular (AV) node located in the lower interatrial septum slows impulse conduction by roughly 0.09–0.12 seconds. This physiological delay ensures that the atria finish contracting and ejecting blood into the ventricles before ventricular systole begins.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02374","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Anatomy and Physiology)","topic":"Renal Physiology","q":"A postoperative patient develops hypovolemia, triggering increased release of Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH) from the posterior pituitary. On which segments of the nephron does ADH primarily act to increase water permeability via aquaporin-2 channels?","o":["Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle","Proximal convoluted tubule and Bowman capsule","Descending limb of the loop of Henle","Late distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct"],"a":3,"e":"ADH (arginine vasopressin) binds to V2 receptors on principal cells of the late distal convoluted tubule and collecting ducts, stimulating insertion of aquaporin-2 water channels into the apical membrane. This increases water reabsorption into the hypertonic medullary interstitium, producing concentrated urine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02375","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Microbiology)","topic":"Moist Heat Sterilization","q":"In a hospital Central Sterile Supply Department (CSSD), which combination represents the standard operating parameters and biological indicator used to validate routine gravity-displacement autoclaving of surgical linen and instruments?","o":["121°C at 15 psi for 15–20 minutes; Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores","134°C at 5 psi for 3 minutes; Staphylococcus aureus vegetative cells","100°C at atmospheric pressure for 30 minutes; Clostridium tetani spores","160°C at 15 psi for 60 minutes; Bacillus atrophaeus spores"],"a":0,"e":"Routine steam sterilization (autoclaving) requires saturated steam at 121°C under 15 lbs/sq inch (psi) pressure for at least 15–20 minutes holding time. Spores of the thermophilic bacterium Geobacillus stearothermophilus serve as the gold-standard biological indicator for steam autoclaves.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02376","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Microbiology)","topic":"Biomedical Waste Management Rules 2016","q":"After completing an intravenous cannulation in the ward, a GNM nurse has a blood-stained plastic IV tubing set, an intact glass ampoule, and a metallic stylet needle. According to the Biomedical Waste Management Rules (India, 2016), into which colour-coded containers should these three items be segregated respectively?","o":["Yellow bag (IV tubing), Red bag (glass ampoule), Blue box (needle)","Red bag (IV tubing), Blue puncture-proof box (glass ampoule), White translucent puncture-proof container (needle)","Yellow bag (IV tubing), White translucent container (glass ampoule), Red bag (needle)","Red bag (IV tubing), Yellow bag (glass ampoule), Black bag (needle)"],"a":1,"e":"Under the BMW Management Rules 2016 (India), contaminated recyclable plastic waste such as IV tubing, catheters, and syringes without needles goes into the Red bag; broken or intact glassware including medicine vials and ampoules goes into the Blue puncture-proof box; metallic sharps including needles and stylets go into the White translucent puncture-proof, leak-proof container.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02377","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Intravenous Fluid Calculation","q":"A physician prescribes 1,000 mL of 0.9% Normal Saline to be infused intravenously over 8 hours. If the available adult macrodrop IV administration set has a drop factor of 15 drops/mL, at what rate (rounded to the nearest whole number) should the nurse regulate the infusion?","o":["42 drops/minute","21 drops/minute","31 drops/minute","63 drops/minute"],"a":2,"e":"Using the formula: Drops/min = (Total volume in mL × Drop factor) / (Time in hours × 60). Here, (1,000 × 15) / (8 × 60) = 15,000 / 480 = 31.25 drops/min, which rounds to 31 drops/minute.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02378","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations (First Aid)","topic":"Envenomation First Aid","q":"A 28-year-old agricultural worker is brought to a rural health camp 20 minutes after being bitten on the right ankle by an unidentified snake. He is anxious with mild local swelling. Which first-aid measure should the nurse perform immediately prior to urgent hospital transfer?","o":["Apply a tight arterial tourniquet above the knee to halt venom spread","Make a cruciate incision over the fang marks and apply suction","Reassure the patient, immobilize the entire right leg with a splint below heart level, and arrange rapid transport","Wash the wound vigorously with potassium permanganate and apply ice packs"],"a":2,"e":"Per the MoHFW Standard Treatment Guidelines for Snakebite in India (\"Do it R.I.G.H.T.\" protocol), immediate first aid consists of Reassurance, Immobilization of the bitten limb using a splint/sling without compressing arterial flow, Getting to a hospital urgently for Anti-Snake Venom (ASV), and Telling the doctor about evolving symptoms.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02379","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Diabetes Mellitus Emergencies","q":"A 22-year-old man with type 1 diabetes presents with nausea, abdominal pain, deep rapid respirations (Kussmaul breathing), and fruity breath odour. Lab results show blood glucose 460 mg/dL, serum K+ 3.1 mEq/L, pH 7.12, and urine ketones +++. Before initiating the continuous regular insulin infusion, what is the priority nursing and medical action?","o":["Restrict all intravenous fluids to prevent cerebral edema","Administer NPH intermediate-acting insulin subcutaneously","Administer sodium bicarbonate IV bolus immediately to correct pH to 7.40","Start 0.9% Normal Saline resuscitation and initiate potassium chloride replacement as serum K+ is below 3.3 mEq/L"],"a":3,"e":"In DKA, initial priority is restoring intravascular volume with 0.9% Normal Saline. Because insulin drives extracellular potassium into cells, giving insulin when serum K+ is \u003c3.3 mEq/L can precipitate life-threatening hypokalemia and fatal cardiac arrhythmias; therefore, potassium replacement must be started before insulin infusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02380","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Thoracic Procedures","q":"While assessing a patient with a left-sided intercostal chest tube (ICD) connected to a single-bottle water-seal drainage system for pneumothorax on day 3, the nurse observes continuous, vigorous bubbling in the water-seal chamber even during expiration and breath-holding. What does this finding indicate and what should the nurse do first?","o":["An air leak in the system or persistent bronchopleural fistula; inspect all tubing connections from insertion site to drainage bottle","Normal lung re-expansion; document and continue routine observation","Tube obstruction by a blood clot; milk the chest tube vigorously","Excessive suction pressure; clamp the chest tube immediately with two hemostats"],"a":0,"e":"In a water-seal chamber, intermittent bubbling during coughing or expiration is expected initially as pleural air escapes. Continuous bubbling throughout the respiratory cycle indicates a persistent air leak—either an external connection leak or a bronchopleural air leak. The nurse must systematically inspect the dressing and tubing connections to locate and seal any external leak.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02381","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease","q":"A 65-year-old man with severe COPD and chronic CO2 retention is admitted with an acute exacerbation and SpO2 of 84%. Which oxygen delivery device allows the nurse to deliver the most precise, fixed low-concentration FiO2 (24%–28%) to target an SpO2 of 88%–92%?","o":["Nasal cannula at 8 L/min","Venturi mask with colour-coded jet adapter","Simple face mask at 6 L/min","Non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min"],"a":1,"e":"A Venturi mask is a high-flow fixed-performance oxygen delivery system that entrains room air in a precise ratio through colour-coded adapters (e.g., blue 24%, white 28%), ensuring exact FiO2 regardless of the patient’s ventilatory pattern and preventing worsening hypercapnia in COPD.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02382","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Postoperative Thyroidectomy Care","q":"Six hours after a total thyroidectomy, a patient reports tingling around the mouth and fingertips, and tapping over the facial nerve anterior to the ear elicits ipsilateral facial twitching. Which electrolyte imbalance is occurring, and which emergency medication must the nurse prepare?","o":["Hyperkalemia; IV insulin with dextrose","Hyponatremia; 3% hypertonic saline","Hypocalcemia; IV calcium gluconate 10%","Hypomagnesemia; IV potassium chloride"],"a":2,"e":"Inadvertent parathyroid injury or ischemia during thyroidectomy causes acute hypoparathyroidism and hypocalcemia. Perioral paresthesia and a positive Chvostek sign (facial twitching on tapping the facial nerve) warn of impending tetany and laryngospasm, requiring immediate IV 10% calcium gluconate (alongside an emergency tracheostomy tray at the bedside).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02383","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Glaucoma Emergencies","q":"A 58-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with sudden severe right eye pain, blurred vision with coloured halos around lights, nausea, a mid-dilated non-reactive pupil, and a stony-hard eyeball on palpation. Which medication is strictly CONTRAINDICATED in this patient?","o":["Intravenous Mannitol 20%","Intravenous Acetazolamide 500 mg","Topical Pilocarpine 2% eye drops","Topical Atropine 1% eye drops"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical picture is acute angle-closure glaucoma caused by pupillary block obstructing aqueous humour outflow through the trabecular meshwork. Mydriatics/cycloplegics such as atropine dilate the pupil further, crowding the peripheral iris into the iridocorneal angle and dangerously spiking intraocular pressure (IOP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02384","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Increased Intracranial Pressure (ICP)","q":"Assertion (A): In a head-injured patient with rising intracranial pressure (ICP), the appearance of systolic hypertension with widened pulse pressure, bradycardia, and irregular respirations indicates impending brainstem herniation. Reason (R): Elevating the head of the bed to 30 degrees with the neck in neutral alignment promotes cerebral venous drainage and helps lower ICP. Choose the correct option:","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":1,"e":"Assertion (A) accurately describes Cushing triad—a late, pre-terminal sign of severe ICP elevation reflecting medullary ischemia and impending uncal/tonsillar herniation. Reason (R) is also a true nursing intervention (30° head-of-bed elevation in neutral midline facilitates jugular venous outflow), but R is a therapeutic measure rather than the physiological explanation of why Cushing triad occurs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02385","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Thermal Burn Resuscitation","q":"A 60 kg adult sustains 40% total body surface area (TBSA) deep partial-thickness flame burns at 8:00 AM and reaches the burn unit at 10:00 AM without prior IV fluids. Using the Parkland formula (4 mL/kg/%TBSA of Ringer’s Lactate), at what hourly rate should fluids be infused from 10:00 AM to 4:00 PM?","o":["800 mL/hour","400 mL/hour","600 mL/hour","1,200 mL/hour"],"a":0,"e":"Total 24-hour fluid requirement = 4 mL × 60 kg × 40 = 9,600 mL of Ringer’s Lactate. Half of this (4,800 mL) must be infused within the first 8 hours calculated from the TIME OF BURN (8:00 AM to 4:00 PM). Since 2 hours have already elapsed before arrival at 10:00 AM, 4,800 mL must be infused over the remaining 6 hours: 4,800 / 6 = 800 mL/hour.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02386","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Blood Transfusion Safety","q":"Ten minutes after starting a packed RBC transfusion, a patient develops chills, fever, lower back pain, hypotension, and dark red urine. What is the FIRST immediate action the nurse must take?","o":["Slow the transfusion rate to 10 drops/minute and administer oral paracetamol","Stop the transfusion immediately, disconnect the blood tubing, and keep the IV line patent with 0.9% Normal Saline via new tubing","Administer IV furosemide before stopping the blood unit","Flush the existing blood tubing rapidly with 5% dextrose to clear the cannula"],"a":1,"e":"The patient exhibits classic signs of an acute hemolytic transfusion reaction (often ABO incompatibility). The absolute first priority is to stop the transfusion immediately to prevent further incompatible blood from entering the circulation, replace the blood set with new tubing infusing 0.9% Normal Saline to maintain venous access and renal perfusion, then notify the physician/blood bank and send the blood bag plus fresh patient blood/urine samples.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02387","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing","topic":"Mood Stabilizers","q":"A 34-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder taking lithium carbonate 900 mg/day develops persistent vomiting and diarrhoea during summer heat, followed by coarse hand tremors, slurred speech, and ataxia. Which serum electrolyte disturbance most directly precipitates this condition, and what is the normal maintenance therapeutic serum lithium range?","o":["Hypercalcemia; therapeutic lithium range 0.1–0.3 mEq/L","Hypophosphatemia; therapeutic lithium range 1.5–2.0 mEq/L","Hyponatremia; therapeutic lithium range 0.6–1.2 mEq/L","Hyperkalemia; therapeutic lithium range 2.0–2.5 mEq/L"],"a":2,"e":"Lithium is handled by the proximal renal tubule similarly to sodium. Volume depletion and hyponatremia (from vomiting, diarrhoea, sweating, or thiazide diuretics) increase proximal tubular reabsorption of both sodium and lithium, precipitating lithium toxicity (coarse tremor, ataxia, dysarthria). Normal maintenance serum lithium is 0.6–1.2 mEq/L (toxicity generally occurs \u003e1.5 mEq/L).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02388","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing","topic":"Antipsychotic Adverse Effects","q":"Two hours after receiving intramuscular haloperidol 5 mg for acute agitation, a young man develops painful neck twisting (torticollis), upward rolling of the eyes (oculogyric crisis), and tongue protrusion. Which drug should the nurse anticipate administering immediately?","o":["Oral Haloperidol 10 mg stat","Intravenous Naloxone","Intramuscular Flumazenil","Intravenous or intramuscular Promethazine or Trihexyphenidyl/Benztropine"],"a":3,"e":"Haloperidol (a high-potency typical antipsychotic) blocks nigrostriatal D2 receptors, creating relative cholinergic excess that manifests as acute dystonia (torticollis, oculogyric crisis, opisthotonos). Rapid relief is achieved with parenteral anticholinergic/antihistaminic agents such as promethazine (25–50 mg IM/IV) or benztropine/diphenhydramine, followed by oral trihexyphenidyl.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02389","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing","topic":"Ego Defense Mechanisms","q":"Match the following clinical examples with the corresponding ego defense mechanism: 1. A patient angry with his strict ward doctor shouts at the student nurse instead. 2. A person with strong unconscious hostility towards a colleague treats that colleague with exaggerated friendliness. 3. A student who fails an examination blames the invigilator for plotting against him. 4. A smoker claims he smokes only to relieve work stress and help him concentrate. Select the correct sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Displacement - Reaction formation - Projection - Rationalization","Displacement - Sublimation - Denial - Intellectualization","Projection - Displacement - Rationalization - Reaction formation","Regression - Reaction formation - Projection - Compensation"],"a":0,"e":"(1) Displacement redirects emotions from a threatening target (doctor) to a safer substitute (student nurse); (2) Reaction formation replaces unacceptable impulses (hostility) with the opposite behaviour (exaggerated friendliness); (3) Projection attributes one’s own unacceptable feelings/blame onto others; (4) Rationalization creates logical-sounding excuses to justify unacceptable behaviour.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02390","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Cyanotic Congenital Heart Disease","q":"A 10-month-old infant with Tetralogy of Fallot suddenly becomes intensely cyanotic, irritable, and tachypneic after crying vigorously during venipuncture. What is the nurse’s immediate first positioning and supportive intervention?","o":["Perform deep endotracheal suctioning for 30 seconds continuously","Place the infant in a knee-chest position, administer 100% oxygen, and prepare morphine/propranolol as ordered","Place the infant in Trendelenburg position and administer IV furosemide","Place the infant flat in supine position and encourage vigorous oral feeding"],"a":1,"e":"During a hypercyanotic (\"tet\") spell, right ventricular outflow tract spasm increases right-to-left shunting across the VSD. Placing the infant in the knee-chest position increases systemic vascular resistance (kinking femoral arteries), thereby decreasing right-to-left shunting and forcing more blood into the pulmonary circulation. High-flow oxygen, calming the child, and morphine (to relax infundibular spasm and reduce respiratory drive) or IV propranolol are standard management.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02391","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Neonatal Resuscitation (NSSK)","q":"A term newborn delivered through clear amniotic fluid is limp and not breathing at birth. After drying, warming, positioning the head in slight extension, and gentle tactile stimulation within the first 30 seconds, the baby remains apneic with a heart rate of 76 beats/minute. What is the immediate next action per NSSK/NRP guidelines?","o":["Start chest compressions immediately at 3:1 ratio","Slap the soles repeatedly for another 60 seconds before ventilating","Initiate positive pressure ventilation (bag-and-mask) with room air (21% O2) at 40–60 breaths/minute","Administer endotracheal epinephrine 1:10,000"],"a":2,"e":"Under NSSK/NRP guidelines, if a newborn remains apneic/gasping or has a heart rate \u003c 100 bpm after the initial steps (30 seconds), effective positive pressure ventilation (PPV) via bag-and-mask at 40–60 breaths/minute is the single most critical intervention. For term and \u003e=35-week newborns, PPV is started with room air (21% oxygen).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02392","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Diarrhoeal Diseases","q":"A 2-year-old boy weighing 12 kg is brought with watery diarrhoea for 2 days. He is irritable, has sunken eyes, drinks ORS eagerly, and his abdominal skin pinch goes back slowly (\u003c 2 seconds). Under WHO/IMNCI Plan B (\"Some Dehydration\"), how much low-osmolarity ORS should be given over the first 4 hours, and what is the recommended daily zinc dose?","o":["600 mL ORS over 4 hours; Zinc 10 mg/day for 14 days","360 mL ORS over 4 hours; Zinc 5 mg/day for 10 days","1,200 mL ORS over 4 hours; Zinc 20 mg/day for 5 days","900 mL ORS over 4 hours; Zinc 20 mg/day for 14 days"],"a":3,"e":"The child exhibits two or more signs of \"Some Dehydration\" (restless/irritable, sunken eyes, drinks eagerly, slow skin pinch), requiring Treatment Plan B. Standard Plan B volume is 75 mL/kg over 4 hours: 75 × 12 kg = 900 mL. Children aged 6 months and older receive elemental Zinc 20 mg once daily for 14 days (infants 2–6 months receive 10 mg/day for 14 days).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02393","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Third Stage of Labour","q":"According to MoHFW/WHO guidelines, which combination accurately describes the three core components of Active Management of the Third Stage of Labour (AMTSL)?","o":["Administration of Oxytocin 10 IU IM within 1 minute of birth (after ruling out second twin), controlled cord traction, and uterine tone assessment/massage after placental delivery","Administration of Oxytocin 40 IU IV push before shoulder delivery, manual removal of placenta, and vaginal packing","Waiting 30 minutes for spontaneous placental separation, then administering Misoprostol 200 mcg orally","Immediate cord clamping within 10 seconds, forceful fundal pressure (Kristeller manoeuvre), and oral methylergometrine"],"a":0,"e":"AMTSL reduces postpartum haemorrhage by \u003e60% and comprises: (1) Uterotonic administration—Oxytocin 10 IU IM within 1 minute of baby’s delivery after palpating the abdomen to exclude an undiagnosed twin; (2) Controlled cord traction (CCT) with counter-traction above the symphysis pubis during uterine contraction; (3) Uterine massage/tone monitoring immediately after placental delivery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02394","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Hypertensive Disorders of Pregnancy","q":"A woman at 36 weeks gestation with eclampsia received a loading dose of Magnesium Sulfate (4 g IV + 10 g IM, Pritchard regimen). Four hours later, prior to the next 5 g IM maintenance dose, the nurse notes: respiratory rate 10 breaths/min, absent deep tendon (patellar) reflexes, and urine output 18 mL/hour. What is the correct nursing action?","o":["Administer the 5 g IM maintenance dose on schedule to prevent recurrent seizures","Withhold the maintenance dose of Magnesium Sulfate, notify the physician immediately, and prepare IV Calcium Gluconate 10% (10 mL)","Give IV furosemide 40 mg and administer half-dose magnesium sulfate","Double the IV oxytocin infusion to expedite delivery"],"a":1,"e":"Safe continuation of maintenance Magnesium Sulfate requires ALL three parameters: respiratory rate \u003e= 16/min (some protocols \u003e= 12/min; 10/min is frank respiratory depression), present patellar reflexes, and urine output \u003e= 30 mL/hr (or \u003e= 100 mL over 4 hours). Loss of patellar reflex and respiratory depression signify magnesium toxicity, requiring immediate withholding of magnesium and slow IV administration of the antidote, 10 mL of 10% Calcium Gluconate.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02395","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations (including First Aid)","topic":"Epistaxis (Nosebleed) First Aid","q":"A schoolchild develops sudden active bleeding from the anterior nasal septum (epistaxis) after minor trauma. Which immediate first-aid posture and compression technique should the GNM nurse perform?","o":["Tilt the child\u0027s head far backward while lying flat supine","Pack dry cotton wool loosely and tell the child to blow the nose forcefully every 2 minutes","Have the child sit upright leaning slightly FORWARD, pinch the soft lower fleshy part of the nostrils firmly against the septum for 10–15 continuous minutes while breathing through the mouth, and apply a cold compress over the nasal bridge","Squeeze only the hard bony upper bridge of the nose for 10 seconds"],"a":2,"e":"Over 90% of nosebleeds arise from Little\u0027s area (Kiesselbach\u0027s plexus) on the anterior cartilaginous septum. Sitting upright and leaning slightly forward prevents swallowed blood from dripping down the posterior pharynx (which triggers nausea, vomiting, and airway aspiration). Continuous direct pressure on the soft lower alae of the nose for 10–15 uninterrupted minutes allows stable clot formation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02396","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Otic (Ear) Drop Instillation","q":"At a paediatric/adult medication OSPE station, how must the nurse straighten the external auditory canal before instilling room-temperature eardrops in a 2-year-old toddler versus an adult?","o":["Pull the pinna upward and backward for both toddlers and adults","For a child \u003c 3 years (2-year-old), pull the pinna DOWNWARD and BACKWARD; for an adult/child \u003e 3 years, pull the pinna UPWARD and BACKWARD","Pull the pinna straight forward toward the nose for all ages","Instil ice-cold drops straight from the refrigerator rapidly"],"a":1,"e":"Because the external auditory canal in infants and children under 3 years slopes upward and is largely cartilaginous, pulling the auricle/pinna gently downward and backward straightens the canal for medication entry. In children \u003e 3 years and adults, the canal curves downward and forward, requiring pulling upward and backward. Warming the bottle in the hands prevents cold-induced vestibular vertigo/vomiting.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02397","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Short IV Antibiotic Macrodrip Rate","q":"A patient is prescribed 100 mL of IV ceftriaxone piggyback to infuse over 30 minutes using a standard macrodrip infusion set with a drop factor of 15 drops/mL. At what rate (drops/min) should the nurse regulate the drip?","o":["25 drops/min","33 drops/min","100 drops/min","50 drops/min"],"a":3,"e":"Using Drops/min = (Volume in mL × Drop Factor) ÷ Time in minutes: (100 mL × 15 drops/mL) ÷ 30 minutes = 1,500 ÷ 30 = 50 drops/min.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02398","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Status Asthmaticus","q":"A young adult admitted with status asthmaticus initially had loud bilateral expiratory wheezes and tachypnoea (34/min). Two hours later, the nurse observes exhaustion, diaphoresis, SpO2 dropping to 82%, and on auscultation hears a \u0027silent chest\u0027 (almost absent breath sounds and no audible wheezing). What does this change signify?","o":["Critical worsening with extreme bronchospasm, mucus plugging, and minimal air movement (\u0027silent chest\u0027) signalling imminent respiratory arrest; call rapid-response/intubation team immediately while maintaining high-flow oxygen and bronchodilators","Normal sleep recovery","Complete resolution of bronchospasm; prepare for discharge","Mild dehydration only"],"a":0,"e":"Wheezing requires airflow across narrowed airways. When severe status asthmaticus progresses to near-complete bronchial occlusion and respiratory muscle fatigue, airflow drops so severely that wheezing disappears—producing a deceptive \u0027silent chest\u0027. This is an emergency red flag for impending hypercapnic/hypoxic respiratory arrest requiring immediate airway/ventilatory support, continuous nebulized beta-2 agonists/ipratropium, IV corticosteroids, and IV magnesium sulphate.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02399","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Percutaneous Liver Biopsy","q":"Which coagulation check, breathing instruction during needle insertion, and immediate post-procedure positioning prevent haemorrhage after a percutaneous liver biopsy?","o":["Place flat prone on the abdomen and apply hot packs","Verify normal PT/INR and platelet count beforehand; instruct patient to exhale and hold breath at end-expiration during needle pass; position on the RIGHT side (right lateral decubitus) with a small pillow beneath the costal margin for 2–4 hours afterward","Skip coagulation tests; hold breath at full inspiration; walk around the ward immediately","Check blood glucose only; inhale deeply during insertion; lie on left side afterward"],"a":1,"e":"Because the liver is richly vascular, normal clotting parameters (PT/INR, platelets) must be verified before biopsy. Holding breath at end-expiration elevates the diaphragm and immobilizes the liver, preventing diaphragmatic/hepatic laceration during needle entry. Afterward, lying on the right side compresses the liver capsule smoothly against the chest wall/pillow to tamponade bleeding while monitoring vitals and right-shoulder/abdominal pain.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02400","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Total Hip Arthroplasty (Posterior Approach)","q":"Two days after a posterior-approach total hip replacement, which postoperative positioning and movement instruction prevents prosthesis dislocation?","o":["Sleep exclusively twisted onto the operated side without pillows","Cross legs at knees when sitting and bend over at the waist to tie shoelaces","Keep legs slightly abducted using an abduction pillow when turning, avoid hip flexion \u003e 90 degrees, avoid crossing legs (adduction), and avoid internal rotation","Sit in very low soft sofas with knees higher than hips"],"a":2,"e":"After posterior hip arthroplasty, the posterior joint capsule is healing; flexing the hip \u003e 90° (low chairs, leaning far forward to tie shoes), adducting (crossing legs/ankles), or internally rotating the leg can pop the femoral head prosthesis posteriorly out of the acetabulum. Using a raised toilet seat, keeping an abduction wedge/pillow during bed turning, and avoiding extreme flexion prevent dislocation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02401","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Post-TURP Continuous Bladder Irrigation (CBI)","q":"Four hours after Transurethral Resection of the Prostate (TURP), a patient with a 3-way Foley catheter receiving Continuous Bladder Irrigation (CBI) complains of sudden severe suprapubic bladder spasms and urge to void, while drainage turns dark bright-red and slows down with visible clots. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Stop the bladder irrigation completely to rest the bladder","Pull firmly on the catheter balloon without deflating it","Give high-dose oral diuretics without checking patency","Check tubing for kinks/clot obstruction, increase the CBI normal saline flow rate to flush out blood before clots solidify (or irrigate manually per order if completely blocked), and monitor vitals/pain"],"a":3,"e":"Post-TURP Continuous Bladder Irrigation (0.9% Normal Saline via 3-way Foley) keeps urine pale pink/light cherry and prevents obstructive blood clots. Sudden painful bladder fullness and slowed bright-red output indicate clot retention/tubing occlusion. Checking for kinks and increasing CBI rate clears developing clots (or performing gentle prescribed manual irrigation with 30–50 mL sterile NS if occluded), while persistent bright-red bleeding/shock requires surgeon notification.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02402","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Oral Iron Therapy Counselling","q":"A patient prescribed oral ferrous sulphate 200 mg twice daily for iron-deficiency anaemia returns anxious because her stools have turned dark greenish-black, though she feels much more energetic and has no abdominal pain or dizziness. What should the nurse explain?","o":["Reassure her that dark greenish-black stools are an expected, harmless effect of unabsorbed oral iron; advise taking iron with Vitamin C (citrus/amla) away from tea, coffee, milk, or antacids, and encourage fibre/fluids for constipation","Double the dose until stools turn bright red","Stop iron immediately because dark stools always mean massive gastric ulcer bleeding","Take iron tablets strictly with hot milk tea and calcium tablets"],"a":0,"e":"Unabsorbed oral iron forms dark iron-sulphide compounds in the colon, turning stools harmlessly dark green or black (unlike true melena, which is tarry, sticky, foul-smelling, and accompanied by GI bleeding symptoms/positive occult blood). Ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) reduces ferric to ferrous iron boosting absorption, whereas tannins (tea/coffee), calcium/milk, and antacids impair uptake.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02403","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Thought Process Abnormalities","q":"During a Mental Status Examination, a client in acute mania speaks rapidly and continuously: \u0027I bought a red car, red like the roses on my dress, dressing up for the Queen of Hearts, my heart beats fast like drums!\u0027 Which thought-process abnormality is demonstrated?","o":["Catatonic mutism","Flight of ideas","Echopraxia","Thought blocking"],"a":1,"e":"Flight of ideas—characteristic of acute mania—involves rapid, pressured speech that shifts abruptly from one topic to another, yet retains superficial logical, phonetic (clang), or word-play links between consecutive phrases (car -\u003e red -\u003e roses -\u003e dress -\u003e Queen of Hearts -\u003e heart). By contrast, loosening of associations (derailment) in schizophrenia breaks even superficial connections between ideas, and thought blocking is an abrupt mid-sentence silence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02404","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Akathisia vs Psychotic Agitation","q":"Four days after starting high-dose risperidone for schizophrenia, a patient paces the corridor relentlessly, shifts weight from foot to foot while standing, and states miserably, \u0027My legs feel restless inside and I just cannot sit still.\u0027 How should the nurse interpret and manage this?","o":["Malingering; apply physical ankle restraints","Worsening psychotic aggression; request doubling the risperidone dose immediately","Antipsychotic-induced akathisia (an extrapyramidal symptom of subjective inner motor restlessness); avoid escalating antipsychotic dose, notify the psychiatrist to lower/switch antipsychotic or add a beta-blocker (propranolol) or benzodiazepine","Acute dystonia; give extra haloperidol IM"],"a":2,"e":"Akathisia is a distressing subjective urge to keep moving (pacing, rocking, inability to sit still) caused by antipsychotic D2 blockade, commonly mistaken for psychotic agitation. Escalating the antipsychotic dose worsens akathisia and increases distress/suicide risk! Management includes reducing/switching the antipsychotic and prescribing propranolol (first-line) or a short-term benzodiazepine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02405","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Malingering vs Somatic Symptom Disorder","q":"A defendant facing criminal court sentencing intentionally feigns severe back paralysis and memory loss solely to avoid imprisonment, and walks normally when unaware anyone is watching. Which clinical classification describes this conscious behaviour?","o":["Somatic symptom disorder","Conversion disorder (unconscious neurological deficit)","Obsessive-compulsive disorder","Malingering (intentional, conscious production of false physical/psychological symptoms motivated by external incentives)"],"a":3,"e":"Malingering is NOT a true mental disorder—it is the deliberate, conscious fabrication or gross exaggeration of symptoms motivated directly by identifiable external rewards (evading criminal prosecution/military duty, obtaining financial compensation or narcotics). By contrast, in Conversion Disorder and Somatic Symptom Disorder, symptoms are experienced genuinely without conscious feigning.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02406","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Sickle Cell Vaso-Occlusive Crisis","q":"A 7-year-old boy with sickle cell disease is admitted crying with severe throbbing pain and swelling in both knees and elbows following a febrile illness and dehydration (vaso-occlusive crisis). Which nursing intervention MUST be strictly avoided?","o":["Applying ice packs or cold compresses directly onto the painful swollen joints","Scheduled opioid/non-opioid analgesia for pain control","Monitoring oxygenation and treating underlying infection promptly","Aggressive oral/IV hydration to reduce blood viscosity and erythrocyte sickling"],"a":0,"e":"In a sickle cell vaso-occlusive crisis, deoxygenated/dehydrated haemoglobin S polymerizes, making RBCs rigid and obstructing microvasculature causing ischaemic tissue pain. Management centres on aggressive hydration, effective analgesia, oxygenation if hypoxic, and warm compresses/gentle heat. Cold compresses or ice packs induce local vasoconstriction, slowing capillary flow and worsening sickling and ischaemia!","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02407","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Divided IV Antibiotic Calculation","q":"A 14 kg infant with acute bacterial meningitis is prescribed IV cefotaxime at 200 mg/kg/day in 4 equally divided doses every 6 hours. How many milligrams (mg) should the nurse administer per single dose?","o":["1,400 mg","700 mg","2,800 mg","350 mg"],"a":1,"e":"Step 1: Calculate total daily dose = 14 kg × 200 mg/kg/day = 2,800 mg/day. Step 2: Divide into 4 equal q6h doses = 2,800 mg ÷ 4 = 700 mg per single dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02408","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Postoperative Cleft Lip Repair (Cheiloplasty)","q":"An infant returns to the paediatric surgical ward immediately after cleft lip repair (cheiloplasty). Which postoperative positioning and suture-line care prevent wound dehiscence?","o":["Give a hard rubber pacifier to soothe crying","Place the infant prone (face-down) on the abdomen so saliva drains","Position supine or side-lying (never prone), use soft elbow restraints to keep hands/toys away from the lip, maintain a Logan bow/protective tape if ordered, cleanse suture line gently with saline after feeds, and avoid pacifiers or straws","Scrub dry crusts off the sutures with alcohol swabs"],"a":2,"e":"After cleft lip repair, suture-line tension or rubbing against bed linens (prone position), fingers, pacifiers, or straws can disrupt delicate facial sutures and ruin cosmetic/functional healing. Supine/side-lying posture, soft elbow restraints (removed periodically under direct supervision for skin checks/ROM), gentle saline cleansing after syringe/Haberman feeds, and prompt comforting to minimize crying preserve wound integrity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02409","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Four Stages of Labour","q":"In obstetrical nursing, which exact clinical events define the beginning and end of the Second Stage of normal labour?","o":["From the birth of the baby until delivery of the placenta and membranes","From delivery of the placenta until 6 weeks postpartum","From onset of true labour contractions until 4 cm cervical dilatation","From full cervical dilatation (10 cm) until the complete expulsion/birth of the baby"],"a":3,"e":"Normal labour is divided into four stages: First stage (onset of true regular contractions with progressive effacement/dilatation up to full 10 cm dilatation); Second stage (from 10 cm full dilatation to delivery of the foetus); Third stage (from birth of baby to complete expulsion of placenta and membranes); and Fourth stage (first 1–2 hours of immediate postpartum maternal stabilization and uterine-tone monitoring).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02410","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Vasa Praevia","q":"Immediately after amniotomy (artificial rupture of membranes) in a woman with a velamentous cord insertion, painless bright-red vaginal bleeding occurs and the foetal heart rate plunges instantaneously from 140 bpm to 65 bpm while maternal blood pressure and pulse remain completely normal. What emergency is occurring?","o":["Vasa praevia (rupture of unsupported foetal umbilical vessels crossing the membranes over the internal os, causing rapid foetal exsanguination); prepare immediately for emergency Caesarean delivery and neonatal blood resuscitation","Maternal urinary bladder infection","Benign cervical show","Uterine rupture with massive maternal shock"],"a":0,"e":"In vasa praevia, unprotected foetal umbilical vessels run across the amniotic membranes over the cervical os. When membranes rupture, tearing of these vessels spills FOETAL blood—producing painless vaginal bleeding paired with catastrophic acute foetal bradycardia/sinusoidal trace while maternal vitals remain stable (since total foetal blood volume is only ~80–100 mL/kg). Immediate emergency Caesarean delivery and neonatal resuscitation are required to save the baby.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02411","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Rh Alloimmunization in Early Pregnancy Loss","q":"A 23-year-old unsensitized Rh-negative woman (Indirect Coombs Test negative) undergoes manual vacuum aspiration for an incomplete spontaneous miscarriage at 10 weeks gestation. Should she receive Anti-D immunoglobulin, even though the pregnancy did not reach full term?","o":["Only if the woman develops fever","Yes; sensitizing foetomaternal bleeding occurs in miscarriages, medical termination of pregnancy (MTP), and ectopic pregnancy—administer Anti-D immunoglobulin (50–100 mcg in first trimester \u003c 12 weeks; 300 mcg \u003e= 12 weeks) IM within 72 hours","No; Anti-D is needed only after full-term vaginal delivery","Give oral iron tablets instead of Anti-D"],"a":1,"e":"Foetal Rh(D) antigens are expressed on erythrocytes as early as 6 weeks gestation. Any sensitizing event—spontaneous abortion, induced MTP, ectopic pregnancy, molar pregnancy, amniocentesis, or abdominal trauma—can expose an unsensitized Rh-negative mother to D-positive foetal cells. Administering Anti-D IM within 72 hours prevents anti-D antibody formation and protects future pregnancies from erythroblastosis foetalis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02412","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing I","topic":"Isolation vs Quarantine","q":"In communicable disease control (Park\u0027s PSM), how does \u0027Quarantine\u0027 differ from \u0027Isolation\u0027 regarding whom it applies to and for how long?","o":["Quarantine applies to sick confirmed patients for 1 day; Isolation applies to healthy travellers forever","Isolation separates confirmed infected/sick persons for the period of communicability, whereas Quarantine restricts healthy exposed contacts for the longest (maximum) incubation period of the disease","Both terms mean identical ICU hospitalization","Quarantine applies exclusively to veterinary animals"],"a":1,"e":"Isolation separates clinically ill or infected individuals from healthy people for as long as they remain contagious (period of communicability). Quarantine restricts the freedom of movement of apparently healthy individuals (or animals/ships) who have been exposed to a communicable disease for a duration equal to the longest known incubation period—so if they develop illness, they cannot transmit it before detection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02413","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Japanese Encephalitis (JE) Control","q":"In a Japanese Encephalitis (JE)-endemic district of eastern India, which mosquito vector, animal amplifying host, and routine UIP childhood vaccination schedule protect children against Acute Encephalitis Syndrome (AES)?","o":["Phlebotomus sandfly; cats; Td booster","Aedes aegypti; stray dogs; Oral BCG","Culex tritaeniorhynchus/vishnui mosquitoes breeding in flooded paddy fields; pigs as amplifying hosts; two doses of live JE vaccine (SA-14-14-2) at 9 months (JE-1) and 16–24 months (JE-2) subcutaneously in left upper arm","Anopheles stephensi; cows; Pentavalent"],"a":2,"e":"Japanese Encephalitis virus is maintained in an enzootic cycle involving ardeid waterbirds (reservoirs), pigs (amplifying hosts developing high viraemia), and Culex vishnui-group mosquitoes (especially C. tritaeniorhynchus) breeding in irrigated rice paddies—spilling over to humans as dead-end hosts. In UIP-designated JE-endemic districts, children receive 0.5 mL live SA-14-14-2 JE vaccine SC (left upper arm) at 9–12 months and 16–24 months alongside keeping pigsties away from homes and using bed nets.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02414","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Safe Body Mechanics","q":"When lifting a heavy box of ward IV fluids from floor level, which ergonomic body-mechanics principle protects the nurse\u0027s lumbar spine from disc injury?","o":["Twist the torso rapidly while holding the box at full arm\u0027s length","Lift suddenly with a quick jerk using back extensors","Keep feet together, lock knees straight, and bend sharply forward from the waist","Stand with feet shoulder-width apart for a wide base of support, bend at the hips and knees (keeping back straight), hold the load close to the body\u0027s centre of gravity, and pivot feet rather than twisting the spine"],"a":3,"e":"Safe body mechanics recruit powerful quadriceps and gluteal leg muscles rather than fragile lumbar paraspinal muscles and intervertebral discs. A wide base of support lowers the centre of gravity for stability, keeping the load close minimizes torque lever arm on L4–L5/L5–S1 discs, and pivoting with feet prevents rotational shear injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02415","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Anatomy and Physiology)","topic":"Ventricular System and CSF Circulation","q":"Through which anatomical structure does cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) pass from the third ventricle into the fourth ventricle before exiting into the subarachnoid space?","o":["Cerebral aqueduct (Aqueduct of Sylvius)","Interventricular foramen of Monro","Central canal of the spinal cord","Foramen of Magendie"],"a":0,"e":"CSF produced mainly by the choroid plexus flows from the lateral ventricles through the paired interventricular foramina of Monro into the third ventricle, then travels down the narrow cerebral aqueduct (Aqueduct of Sylvius) in the midbrain to enter the fourth ventricle, and finally exits via the median foramen of Magendie and lateral foramina of Luschka into the subarachnoid space.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02416","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Anatomy and Physiology)","topic":"Gastric Secretion and Absorption","q":"Which gastric cells secrete Intrinsic Factor, and in which segment of the gastrointestinal tract is the Intrinsic Factor–Vitamin B12 complex absorbed?","o":["Chief (peptic) cells; duodenum","Parietal (oxyntic) cells; terminal ileum","G-cells; jejunum","Mucous neck cells; ascending colon"],"a":1,"e":"Gastric parietal (oxyntic) cells located in the body and fundus of the stomach secrete both hydrochloric acid (HCl) and Intrinsic Factor (IF). IF binds dietary vitamin B12 (cobalamin) and protects it from proteolysis until receptor-mediated endocytosis occurs in the terminal ileum; deficiency leads to pernicious (megaloblastic) anaemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02417","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Microbiology)","topic":"Gell and Coombs Hypersensitivity Classification","q":"Match each clinical condition with its primary Gell and Coombs hypersensitivity mechanism: 1. Penicillin-induced systemic anaphylaxis 2. ABO-incompatible blood transfusion hemolysis 3. Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis / Serum sickness 4. Positive Mantoux (Tuberculin) skin test induration at 48–72 hours Select the correct sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Type IV (T-cell delayed) - Type II (Cytotoxic) - Type III (Immune complex) - Type I (IgE-mediated)","Type I (IgE-mediated) - Type III (Immune complex) - Type II (Cytotoxic) - Type IV (T-cell delayed)","Type I (IgE-mediated) - Type II (Antibody-mediated cytotoxic) - Type III (Immune complex-mediated) - Type IV (T-cell delayed)","Type II (Cytotoxic) - Type I (IgE-mediated) - Type IV (Delayed) - Type III (Immune complex)"],"a":2,"e":"(1) Systemic anaphylaxis is classic Type I immediate hypersensitivity via IgE-sensitized mast cell/basophil degranulation; (2) ABO transfusion hemolysis is Type II IgM/IgG-mediated complement lysis of RBCs; (3) Serum sickness and PSGN involve circulating antigen-antibody immune complex deposition (Type III); (4) The Mantoux tuberculin test and contact dermatitis are Type IV CD4+ Th1 cell-mediated delayed hypersensitivity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02418","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Microbiology)","topic":"Chemical Disinfection and Cold Sterilization","q":"A flexible fibreoptic bronchoscope cannot withstand steam autoclaving. Which liquid chemical agent is standardly used in hospital endoscopy units for high-level disinfection (20 minutes immersion) or chemical sterilization (10 hours immersion) after thorough enzymatic cleaning?","o":["0.5% Cetrimide with chlorhexidine","70% Isopropyl alcohol","1% Sodium hypochlorite","2% Alkaline Glutaraldehyde (activated solution)"],"a":3,"e":"Spaulding’s classification categorizes flexible endoscopes as semi-critical instruments requiring high-level disinfection (HLD). Activated 2% alkaline glutaraldehyde (Cidex) is non-corrosive to lenses, rubber, and plastics, achieving HLD in 20 minutes and sporicidal chemical sterilization in 10 hours (with adequate ventilation and thorough sterile water rinsing afterward).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02419","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Enteral Tube Feeding","q":"After inserting a nasogastric (Ryle’s) tube measured using the NEX method (nose tip to earlobe to xiphoid process), which bedside and diagnostic checks provide the most reliable confirmation of correct intragastric placement before starting enteral feeding?","o":["Testing aspirated gastric fluid pH (\u003c= 5.5) and confirming tip position below the diaphragm on chest/upper abdominal X-ray when available","Submerging the open proximal end of the tube in a bowl of water to look for absence of bubbles","Injecting 30 mL of air while auscultating a \"whoosh\" over the epigastrium alone","Asking the sedated patient to speak a full sentence"],"a":0,"e":"Radiographic confirmation (X-ray) is the gold standard for verifying NG tube tip position in the stomach below the diaphragm, while aspirate pH testing (pH \u003c= 5.5) is the safest recommended bedside check prior to each feed. Air insufflation auscultation (\"whoosh test\") is unreliable because sounds from a malpositioned bronchial or esophageal tube transmit easily to the epigastrium.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02420","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Paediatric IV Fluid Calculation","q":"A paediatric patient is ordered 300 mL of 0.45% Dextrose-Normal Saline to be infused over 5 hours via a microdrop burette set (drop factor = 60 microdrops/mL). What should the infusion rate be in microdrops per minute?","o":["120 microdrops/minute","60 microdrops/minute","30 microdrops/minute","15 microdrops/minute"],"a":1,"e":"Hourly rate = 300 mL / 5 hours = 60 mL/hour. Using Microdrops/min = (Volume × 60) / (Time in hours × 60) = 300 / 5 = 60 microdrops/minute. With a standard 60 microdrops/mL set, the numerical value of microdrops/min always equals mL/hour.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02421","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations (First Aid)","topic":"Foreign Body Airway Obstruction (Choking)","q":"A 7-month-old infant accidentally inhales a piece of raw carrot, becomes silent, cannot cough or cry, and develops perioral cyanosis while still conscious. What is the correct immediate first-aid sequence?","o":["Perform a blind finger sweep deep into the infant’s pharynx","Hold the infant upside down by the ankles and shake gently","Deliver alternating cycles of 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades (head lower than trunk) and 5 chest thrusts over the lower sternum","Perform rapid subdiaphragmatic abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre)"],"a":2,"e":"In a conscious infant (\u003c 1 year) with severe foreign body airway obstruction, abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre) are CONTRAINDICATED because the infant’s relatively large liver and spleen lie unprotected below the ribcage and can rupture. Instead, support the jaw/head downward along the forearm and alternate 5 back slaps with 5 two-finger chest thrusts until the object is expelled or the infant becomes unresponsive (when CPR starts).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02422","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Acute Coronary Syndrome (STEMI)","q":"A 59-year-old man presents with crushing substernal chest pain radiating to the jaw, diaphoresis, ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF (inferior wall MI) with V4R elevation suggesting right ventricular infarction, and BP 82/54 mmHg. Which prescribed medication must the nurse question and HOLD before contacting the physician?","o":["Oxygen if SpO2 is 89%","Continuous 12-lead ECG monitoring","Chewable Aspirin 300 mg","Sublingual Nitroglycerin 0.5 mg"],"a":3,"e":"In inferior wall STEMI with right ventricular involvement (V4R ST-elevation) and hypotension (systolic BP \u003c 90 mmHg), cardiac output is preload-dependent. Nitroglycerin causes potent venodilation, drastically reducing right ventricular preload and precipitating refractory cardiogenic shock. Nitrates are also strictly contraindicated if the patient has taken a PDE-5 inhibitor (e.g., sildenafil) within 24–48 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02423","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Heart Failure Pharmacotherapy","q":"A 68-year-old woman taking oral digoxin 0.25 mg daily and furosemide 40 mg daily for chronic heart failure reports anorexia, nausea, and yellow-green halos around ward lights. Her apical pulse over one full minute is 52 beats/minute, and morning serum potassium is 2.9 mEq/L. What is the correct nursing action?","o":["Withhold the scheduled digoxin dose, notify the physician regarding suspected digoxin toxicity precipitated by furosemide-induced hypokalemia, and prepare potassium replacement","Administer IV atropine prophylactically and give digoxin","Give double dose of furosemide to relieve fluid retention","Administer digoxin now and recheck potassium tomorrow"],"a":0,"e":"Hypokalemia (K+ \u003c 3.5 mEq/L, commonly caused by loop diuretics like furosemide) increases digoxin binding to Na+/K+-ATPase, precipitating digoxin toxicity even at normal serum digoxin levels. Classic manifestations include GI symptoms (anorexia, nausea), xanthopsia (yellow-green visual halos), and bradyarrhythmias. Always count the apical pulse for 1 full minute and withhold digoxin if HR \u003c 60 bpm in adults.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02424","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Complications of Liver Cirrhosis","q":"A patient with decompensated alcoholic cirrhosis develops confusion, sleep-wake reversal, slurred speech, and a flapping hand tremor (asterixis) when arms are outstretched. The physician prescribes oral/NG lactulose 30 mL three times daily. What is the therapeutic mechanism of lactulose and the nursing target for stool frequency?","o":["Kills all gut bacteria directly; target 1 hard stool every 3 days","Colonic bacteria ferment lactulose into lactic/acetic acids, trapping ammonia (NH3) as non-absorbable ammonium (NH4+) excreted in stool; target 2–3 soft stools per day","Neutralizes gastric acid to prevent variceal bleeding; target constipation","Inhibits hepatic urea cycle enzymes; target watery diarrhoea \u003e10 stools/day"],"a":1,"e":"In hepatic encephalopathy, impaired hepatic urea-cycle clearance raises blood ammonia, causing astrocyte swelling. Lactulose (a non-absorbable disaccharide) is fermented by colonic flora to organic acids, lowering colonic pH and converting diffusible ammonia (NH3) into charged ammonium ions (NH4+) that cannot cross the mucosa and are expelled via osmotic laxation. Titrate dose to achieve 2–3 soft stools daily without causing dehydration/hypernatremia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02425","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Long-Bone Fracture Complications","q":"Thirty-six hours after sustaining a closed mid-shaft femur fracture from a road traffic accident, a 25-year-old man suddenly becomes restless and confused, with SpO2 dropping to 86%, tachypnea (32/min), tachycardia (124/min), fever, and pinpoint non-blanching petechiae across the anterior chest, neck, and axillae. What complication has developed?","o":["Osteomyelitis","Volkmann ischemic contracture","Fat Embolism Syndrome (FES)","Compartment syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Fat Embolism Syndrome typically arises 24–72 hours after long-bone (femur/tibia) or pelvic fractures when marrow fat globules enter torn venous sinusoids and trigger pulmonary microvascular occlusion and free-fatty-acid pneumonitis. The classic triad (Gurd’s criteria) is hypoxemia/respiratory distress, neurological changes (confusion/agitation), and a characteristic petechial rash over the upper chest, neck, axillae, and conjunctivae.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02426","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Oncologic Emergencies","q":"Assertion (A): Patients with high-grade lymphoma or acute leukemia starting cytotoxic chemotherapy are at risk of Tumor Lysis Syndrome (TLS), characterized by hyperuricemia, hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, and secondary hypocalcemia. Reason (R): Rapid malignant cell lysis releases intracellular potassium, phosphate, and purine nucleic acids into the bloodstream, and excess phosphate binds serum calcium to precipitate calcium phosphate crystals in renal tubules. Choose the correct option:","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"TLS is an oncologic emergency caused by massive lysis of tumour cells releasing intracellular K+ (causing hyperkalemia/arrhythmias), PO4^3- (causing hyperphosphatemia, which chelates ionized Ca2+ producing hypocalcemia/tetany and renal tubular precipitation), and nucleic acid purines metabolized by xanthine oxidase into uric acid (causing obstructive uric acid nephropathy). Prophylaxis includes aggressive IV hydration and allopurinol or rasburicase.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02427","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Artificial Airway Maintenance","q":"While performing sterile open suctioning on an adult patient with a cuffed tracheostomy tube, which set of parameters reflects safe evidence-based nursing practice?","o":["Instill 10 mL normal saline routinely before every suction pass; keep suction on continuously for 25 seconds","Use clean bare hands and reuse the same catheter over 24 hours","Wall suction pressure 200 mmHg; apply continuous suction while inserting catheter for 30 seconds","Pre-oxygenate with 100% O2 for 30–60 seconds; set suction pressure to 80–120 mmHg; insert without suction and apply intermittent suction only during withdrawal for \u003c= 10–15 seconds"],"a":3,"e":"Safe adult tracheostomy suctioning uses sterile technique, pre-oxygenation (hyperoxygenation) to prevent suction-induced hypoxemia, negative pressure limited to 80–120 mmHg (to avoid mucosal tearing and atelectasis), insertion without suction until resistance/cough, and intermittent suction while rotating and withdrawing within 10–15 seconds.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02428","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Cerebrovascular Accident (Stroke)","q":"A 62-year-old man arrives in the emergency room 90 minutes after sudden onset of right-sided hemiparesis and expressive aphasia. His capillary blood glucose is 118 mg/dL and BP is 168/92 mmHg. What is the single most urgent diagnostic investigation required BEFORE intravenous alteplase (tPA) can be considered?","o":["Immediate non-contrast CT scan of the brain to rule out intracranial haemorrhage","Carotid Doppler ultrasound","Lumbar puncture for CSF cytology","Electroencephalogram (EEG)"],"a":0,"e":"Although CT may appear normal in the first few hours of acute ischemic stroke, an urgent non-contrast CT head (target door-to-CT \u003c= 25 min) is mandatory to exclude intracerebral or subarachnoid haemorrhage before administering IV alteplase within the 4.5-hour window (provided BP \u003c 185/110 mmHg and no bleeding contraindications exist).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02429","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing","topic":"Alcohol Withdrawal and Delirium Tremens","q":"Three days after abrupt cessation of heavy daily alcohol intake, a 44-year-old man develops severe agitation, disorientation, visual hallucinations (seeing insects on the wall), diaphoresis, BP 172/102 mmHg, and pulse 128/min (Delirium Tremens). Along with titrated IV benzodiazepines (lorazepam/diazepam), which vitamin MUST be given parenterally BEFORE any intravenous dextrose solution?","o":["Vitamin C (Ascorbic acid)","Vitamin B1 (Thiamine)","Vitamin D (Cholecalciferol)","Vitamin K (Phytomenadione)"],"a":1,"e":"Chronic alcohol use depletes thiamine (Vitamin B1) stores. Administering IV dextrose before thiamine exhausts residual thiamine as a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, precipitating acute Wernicke encephalopathy (ataxia, ophthalmoplegia, confusion) and irreversible Korsakoff psychosis. Parenteral thiamine (100–500 mg IV/IM) must precede glucose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02430","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing","topic":"Psychiatric Drug Emergencies","q":"Four days after starting high-dose parenteral haloperidol and chlorpromazine for acute mania, a patient develops fever of 40.2°C, severe generalized \"lead-pipe\" muscular rigidity, fluctuating blood pressure, tachycardia, mutism, and markedly elevated serum Creatine Phosphokinase (CPK 14,500 U/L) with leukocytosis. What is the diagnosis and immediate priority?","o":["Malignant catatonia unrelated to drugs; double the haloperidol dose","Tardive dyskinesia; add oral risperidone","Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS); stop all antipsychotics immediately, institute aggressive cooling/IV hydration, and prepare dantrolene or bromocriptine","Akathisia; encourage vigorous walking"],"a":2,"e":"NMS is a rare, life-threatening idiosyncratic reaction to dopamine D2 receptor antagonists characterized by \"FEVER\": Fever (\u003e38°C/40°C), Encephalopathy (confusion/mutism), Vital sign instability (autonomic lability), Elevated CPK/WBC (rhabdomyolysis risk), and Rigidity (lead-pipe). Immediate withdrawal of the offending antipsychotic, ICU supportive care (cooling, hydration to prevent myoglobinuric AKI), and dantrolene (muscle relaxant) or bromocriptine (dopamine agonist) are required.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02431","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Neonatal Hyperbilirubinemia","q":"Which nursing intervention is essential while caring for a term newborn receiving continuous overhead blue-light phototherapy (450–460 nm) for unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia?","o":["Apply oil or moisturizing lotion generously to the baby’s skin before placing under the phototherapy lamp","Cover both eyes completely with opaque eye patches (removed briefly during feeds/eye checks) and cover the genitalia/diaper area, while encouraging frequent breastfeeding","Keep the infant fully clothed in thick blankets to prevent hypothermia","Discontinue breastfeeding completely and give plain water between feeds"],"a":1,"e":"Phototherapy photo-isomerizes unconjugated bilirubin in superficial skin capillaries into water-soluble lumirubin excreted in bile and urine. Maximum skin exposure (only small diaper covering genitalia and opaque eye shields protecting retinas from light injury) is required. Frequent breastfeeding (8–12 times/day) promotes hydration and bilirubin excretion in stools, and temperature must be monitored every 2–4 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02432","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Nephrotic Syndrome vs Glomerulonephritis","q":"A 4-year-old boy presents with generalized puffy face (periorbital edema worse in the morning), scrotal edema, ascites, normal blood pressure (96/62 mmHg), clear non-bloody urine with 4+ proteinuria, serum albumin 1.8 g/dL, and serum cholesterol 340 mg/dL. What is the diagnosis and standard initial medical therapy?","o":["Urinary tract infection; oral nitrofurantoin","Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis; antihypertensives and penicillin","Haemolytic uraemic syndrome; urgent platelet transfusion","Minimal change nephrotic syndrome; oral Prednisolone therapy with high-protein/no-added-salt diet during edema and infection prevention"],"a":3,"e":"Childhood nephrotic syndrome is defined by the tetrad of massive proteinuria (\u003e40 mg/m²/hr or 3+/4+ on dipstick), hypoalbuminemia (\u003c2.5 g/dL), generalized edema (anasarca), and hypercholesterolemia, typically with normotension and absence of gross hematuria (unlike acute glomerulonephritis, which presents with cola-coloured hematuria, RBC casts, hypertension, and oliguria). Minimal change disease responds in \u003e85% of children to daily oral prednisolone (2 mg/kg/day for 6 weeks followed by alternate-day taper).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02433","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Weight-Based Paediatric Drug Calculation","q":"A child weighing 15 kg is prescribed oral Amoxicillin at 40 mg/kg/day in 2 equally divided doses (every 12 hours). If the reconstituted Amoxicillin oral suspension is labelled 250 mg per 5 mL, how many millilitres (mL) should the nurse administer for EACH single dose?","o":["6 mL","3 mL","12 mL","5 mL"],"a":0,"e":"Total daily dose = 40 mg/kg/day × 15 kg = 600 mg/day. Single dose (every 12 hours, i.e., 2 doses/day) = 600 mg / 2 = 300 mg per dose. Volume required = (Desired dose / Available concentration) × Volume = (300 mg / 250 mg) × 5 mL = 1.2 × 5 mL = 6 mL per dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02434","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Placenta Previa vs Abruptio Placentae","q":"A 31-year-old multigravida at 34 weeks gestation is admitted with sudden onset of painless, bright-red vaginal bleeding after waking up. On abdominal palpation, the uterus is soft, relaxed, and non-tender, and the fetal head is high and floating with fetal heart rate 144 bpm. Which action is strictly CONTRAINDICATED during initial emergency assessment?","o":["Establishing two large-bore IV lines and sending blood for grouping and crossmatching","Performing a digital per-vaginal (PV) examination in the emergency room","Monitoring maternal vital signs, pad counts, and fetal heart rate continuously","Arranging an urgent bedside transabdominal obstetric ultrasound"],"a":1,"e":"Painless, causeless, recurrent bright-red vaginal bleeding in the third trimester with a soft non-tender uterus and unengaged presenting part is classic for Placenta Previa. A digital vaginal (PV) examination is strictly CONTRAINDICATED (\"No PV in APH until placenta previa is ruled out by ultrasound\") because the examining finger can tear the low-lying placenta or placental sinuses, triggering catastrophic haemorrhage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02435","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Rh Isoimmunization Prevention","q":"An Rh-negative primigravida with a negative Indirect Coombs Test (ICT) delivers an Rh-positive newborn whose cord blood Direct Coombs Test is negative. Within what optimal window should Anti-D immunoglobulin (300 mcg IM) be administered to the mother to prevent Rh alloimmunization in future pregnancies?","o":["Only if the baby develops jaundice on day 5","Only to the newborn infant within 24 hours","Within 72 hours of delivery","At the 6-week postnatal checkup"],"a":2,"e":"Unsensitized (ICT-negative) Rh-negative mothers who deliver an Rh-positive infant must receive 300 mcg of RhD (Anti-D) immunoglobulin intramuscularly into the deltoid as soon as possible and within 72 hours of birth (in addition to routine antenatal prophylaxis at 28 weeks and after sensitizing events like abortion/ectopic/amniocentesis). Anti-D clears fetal Rh-positive RBCs from maternal circulation before maternal B-cells mount an immune response.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02436","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Anatomy and Physiology)","topic":"Excitation-Contraction Coupling","q":"During skeletal muscle excitation-contraction coupling, calcium ions (Ca2+) released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum bind directly to which regulatory protein to expose myosin-binding sites on actin?","o":["Dystrophin","Acetylcholinesterase","Tropomyosin","Troponin C"],"a":3,"e":"Following acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction and depolarization along T-tubules, Ca2+ is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum and binds to Troponin C. This induces a conformational shift in the troponin-tropomyosin complex, uncovering active binding sites on F-actin so myosin heads can form cross-bridges and slide actin filaments inward.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02437","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Anatomy and Physiology)","topic":"Sensory Organs of Eye and Ear","q":"Match each specialized anatomical structure of the eye or ear with its primary physiological function: 1. Organ of Corti (cochlea) 2. Semicircular canals 3. Canal of Schlemm (scleral venous sinus) 4. Macula lutea / Fovea centralis Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Auditory mechanotransduction (hearing) - Detection of rotational/angular acceleration of head - Drainage of aqueous humour - Area of sharpest photopic (cone) visual acuity","Drainage of endolymph - Hearing high frequencies - Accommodation of lens - Night (rod) vision","Detection of linear gravity - Auditory transduction - Secretion of vitreous humour - Blind spot lacking photoreceptors","Equalization of middle ear pressure - Aqueous humour production - Colour vision - Balance during walking"],"a":0,"e":"(1) The Organ of Corti on the basilar membrane houses inner and outer hair cells for hearing; (2) Three perpendicular semicircular canals detect rotational head movements via crista ampullaris; (3) Aqueous humour produced by the ciliary body drains via the trabecular meshwork into the Canal of Schlemm; (4) The fovea centralis within the macula contains densely packed cones providing highest visual acuity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02438","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Anatomy and Physiology)","topic":"Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS)","q":"When renal perfusion pressure falls during haemorrhage, granular juxtaglomerular cells secrete renin, initiating the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS). Which enzyme converts Angiotensin I to potent vasoconstrictor Angiotensin II, and what is the primary renal effect of Aldosterone?","o":["Lipoprotein lipase; Aldosterone increases glomerular filtration of glucose","Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) mainly in pulmonary capillaries; Aldosterone promotes Na+ reabsorption and K+ excretion in distal convoluted tubule/collecting duct","Carbonic anhydrase in RBCs; Aldosterone promotes Na+ excretion and K+ retention","Renin directly in the liver; Aldosterone inhibits ADH release"],"a":1,"e":"Renin cleaves hepatic angiotensinogen into Angiotensin I, which is converted by pulmonary endothelial Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) into Angiotensin II. Angiotensin II causes systemic arteriolar vasoconstriction and stimulates adrenal cortex release of Aldosterone, which acts on principal cells of the late DCT/collecting duct to reabsorb Na+ (and water) while secreting K+ and H+.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02439","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Microbiology)","topic":"Bacterial Growth Curve","q":"In a closed batch culture system, during which phase of the bacterial growth curve do bacteria divide at their maximum constant geometric rate (making them most susceptible to beta-lactam cell-wall antibiotics), and in which subsequent phase do endospores typically begin forming as nutrients deplete?","o":["Lag phase (maximum division); Decline phase (sporulation)","Decline phase (maximum division); Log phase (sporulation)","Log / Exponential phase (maximum division); Stationary phase (sporulation)","Stationary phase (maximum division); Lag phase (sporulation)"],"a":2,"e":"During the Lag phase, bacteria adapt metabolically and increase in cell size without dividing. In the Log (exponential) phase, cells multiply at maximum constant rate—ideal for biochemical testing and bactericidal action of penicillin/cephalosporins. In the Stationary phase, nutrient exhaustion and toxic metabolite buildup equalize birth and death rates, triggering endospore and exotoxin/antibiotic production.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02440","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Microbiology)","topic":"CAUTI Prevention Bundle","q":"Which nursing practice is a core element of the evidence-based Catheter-Associated Urinary Tract Infection (CAUTI) maintenance bundle for a hospitalized patient with an indwelling Foley catheter?","o":["Elevate the urinary drainage bag above bladder level during transport so tubing stays straight","Maintain a sterile, continuously closed drainage system with the collection bag positioned below bladder level (never resting on the floor) and review daily for early catheter removal","Perform routine daily bladder irrigation with normal saline or chlorhexidine","Disconnect the catheter-tubing junction every 8 hours to obtain routine surveillance urine cultures"],"a":1,"e":"CAUTI prevention bundles emphasize aseptic insertion, keeping the drainage system closed and unobstructed, hanging the drainage bag below bladder level off the floor to prevent retrograde urine reflux, routine meatal hygiene with soap/water, and daily assessment of catheter necessity for prompt removal.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02441","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) Interpretation","q":"An adult patient admitted with severe acute exacerbation of asthma has the following Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) report: pH = 7.26 (normal 7.35–7.45), PaCO2 = 58 mmHg (normal 35–45 mmHg), and HCO3- = 25 mEq/L (normal 22–26 mEq/L). How should the nurse interpret this ABG?","o":["Fully compensated metabolic alkalosis","Uncompensated metabolic acidosis","Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis","Uncompensated respiratory acidosis"],"a":3,"e":"pH 7.26 indicates acidemia (\u003c 7.35). PaCO2 58 mmHg is elevated (\u003e 45 mmHg), moving in the opposite direction to pH (\"ROME\": Respiratory Opposite) and explaining the acidosis via hypoventilation/CO2 retention. Because HCO3- (25 mEq/L) remains within the normal range (22–26 mEq/L), renal metabolic compensation has not yet occurred—indicating uncompensated (acute) respiratory acidosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02442","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Subcutaneous Insulin Preparation","q":"A diabetic patient is prescribed 10 units of Regular (short-acting, clear) insulin and 20 units of NPH (intermediate-acting, cloudy) insulin subcutaneously before breakfast. Which syringe-drawing sequence prevents contaminating the Regular insulin vial with protamine/NPH suspension?","o":["Inject 20 units air into NPH vial -\u003e Inject 10 units air into Regular vial -\u003e Withdraw 10 units Regular (clear) first -\u003e Withdraw 20 units NPH (cloudy) second","Shake the NPH vial vigorously until foam forms, then draw cloudy before clear","Inject 10 units air into Regular vial -\u003e Inject 20 units air into NPH vial -\u003e Withdraw 20 units NPH -\u003e Withdraw 10 units Regular","Draw NPH and Regular in the same syringe with Glargine insulin"],"a":0,"e":"Follow the \"Clear before Cloudy\" rule (RN: Regular before NPH): first gently roll (never shake) the NPH vial, inject air into NPH (without touching liquid), inject air into Regular vial, invert and withdraw exact Regular (clear) units first, then withdraw NPH (cloudy) units up to total 30 units. Drawing clear first prevents protamine zinc from NPH entering the Regular vial and altering its rapid onset.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02443","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations (First Aid)","topic":"Electrical Injury First Aid","q":"A farm worker collapses after accidentally touching an energized bare 220V pump-set wire lying on damp ground. A nurse arrives first at the scene. What is the mandatory FIRST step before touching the victim?","o":["Start chest compressions while the wire is still resting on the victim’s leg","Switch off the main power supply / circuit breaker (or if impossible for low voltage, stand on dry insulating wood/rubber and push the wire away with a dry non-conductive wooden stick) before assessing CAB and C-spine","Grab the victim’s wet shirt immediately and pull him away from the wire","Pour buckets of cold water over the victim to cool electrical burns"],"a":1,"e":"Scene safety is absolute in electrocution: touching an energized victim or standing on wet conductive ground electrocutes the rescuer. Turn off the main power source first (for high-voltage downed lines, maintain safe distance \u003e= 10 m and call electricity authorities). Once de-energized, assess responsiveness/breathing/pulse, initiate CPR immediately for cardiac arrest, immobilize cervical spine if fall occurred, and treat hidden deep-tissue electrical burns/arrhythmias at hospital.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02444","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Life-Threatening Cardiac Arrhythmias","q":"A 61-year-old post-MI patient in the CCU suddenly loses consciousness, has no palpable carotid pulse, and the bedside ECG monitor displays chaotic, irregular, coarse fibrillatory waves with no identifiable P waves or QRS complexes (Ventricular Fibrillation). Along with starting high-quality CPR, what electrical therapy is indicated immediately?","o":["Synchronized cardioversion at 50 Joules","Transcutaneous pacing at 70 bpm only","Immediate unsynchronized biphasic defibrillation (e.g., 120–200 Joules per device manufacturer)","Carotid sinus massage and IV adenosine 6 mg"],"a":2,"e":"Ventricular fibrillation (VF) and pulseless ventricular tachycardia (pVT) are shockable cardiac arrest rhythms requiring immediate UNSYNCHRONIZED defibrillation (120–200 J biphasic; 360 J monophasic) alongside continuous high-quality CPR (100–120/min, depth 5–6 cm) and epinephrine 1 mg IV every 3–5 min. Because VF lacks organized QRS complexes, turning on \"SYNC\" mode prevents the defibrillator from firing!","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02445","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Venous Thromboembolism","q":"A patient discharged on oral Warfarin for lower-limb Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) returns with epistaxis, bleeding gums, and an International Normalized Ratio (INR) of 6.8 (target therapeutic range 2.0–3.0). Which specific pharmacological antidote reverses Warfarin-induced coagulopathy, and what dietary advice should the nurse reinforce?","o":["Protamine sulfate; completely eliminate all green vegetables permanently","Naloxone; double the warfarin dose on alternate days","Idarucizumab; take high-dose aspirin for pain","Vitamin K1 (Phytomenadione); maintain a CONSISTENT daily intake of Vitamin K-rich green leafy vegetables rather than erratic binges or total avoidance"],"a":3,"e":"Warfarin inhibits Vitamin K epoxide reductase, blocking synthesis of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X (monitored via PT/INR, target 2.0–3.0 for DVT/PE). Vitamin K1 (phytomenadione) is its specific antidote (with FFP/PCC for life-threatening bleeding). Patients should keep dietary Vitamin K (spinach, cabbage, broccoli) steady each week so INR remains stable.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02446","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Acute Pancreatitis","q":"A 42-year-old man admitted with severe epigastric pain radiating straight to the back, serum lipase 1,850 U/L, and persistent vomiting develops bluish discoloration around the umbilicus (Cullen’s sign) and across both flanks (Grey Turner’s sign). What do these physical signs signify, and what is the initial gastrointestinal nursing priority?","o":["Retroperitoneal haemorrhage from necrotizing pancreatitis; maintain strict NPO (nothing by mouth) with aggressive IV fluid resuscitation and pain relief","Superficial skin allergy; start high-fat oral diet immediately","Subcutaneous emphysema; apply hot water bottle to abdomen","Normal resolving bruising from gallstones; give oral laxatives"],"a":0,"e":"Cullen’s sign (periumbilical ecchymosis) and Grey Turner’s sign (flank ecchymosis) indicate retroperitoneal bleeding tracking along fascial planes in severe acute haemorrhagic/necrotizing pancreatitis. Priority care includes aggressive isotonic crystalloid resuscitation (to prevent hypovolemic shock/AKI), bowel/pancreatic rest (NPO initially until pain/ileus resolves), analgesia, and close ICU monitoring.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02447","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis (CAPD)","q":"During the drain phase of Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis (CAPD), the nurse observes that the drained peritoneal effluent in the bag is cloudy and turbid rather than clear pale-yellow, and the patient reports mild diffuse abdominal discomfort and low-grade fever. What is the priority nursing action?","o":["Warm the next dialysate bag in a microwave oven for 3 minutes before instilling","Suspect CAPD peritonitis, collect an effluent sample aseptically for cell count, Gram stain, and culture, and notify the nephrologist immediately for intraperitoneal antibiotics","Clamp the Tenckhoff catheter permanently without notifying the physician","Discard the bag immediately and reassure the patient that cloudy fluid is normal from protein loss"],"a":1,"e":"Cloudy drained dialysate effluent is the earliest and cardinal sign of PD-associated peritonitis (confirmed by effluent WBC \u003e 100/µL with \u003e50% neutrophils). Prompt aseptic collection of effluent for microscopy/culture and immediate empirical intraperitoneal antibiotics prevent catheter loss and sepsis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02448","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Cataract Surgery Nursing Care","q":"Following uncomplicated right-eye phacoemulsification cataract surgery with intraocular lens (IOL) implantation, which postoperative instruction prevents dangerous spikes in intraocular pressure (IOP) and wound dehiscence?","o":["Irrigate the operated eye daily with unboiled tap water","Sleep flat on the right (operative) side to apply gentle pressure to the eye","Avoid bending at the waist, heavy lifting (\u003e5 kg), coughing/straining at stool, and rubbing the eye; wear the protective eye shield at night and sleep on the back or unoperated left side","Perform vigorous Valsalva breathing exercises every hour"],"a":2,"e":"Bending forward below waist level, heavy lifting, straining during defecation (prevented with stool softeners), forceful coughing/vomiting, and squeezing/rubbing the globe raise intraocular pressure and risk anterior chamber collapse, iris prolapse, or hyphaema. Sleeping supine or on the non-operative side with a rigid eye shield protects the incision.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02449","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Spinal Cord Injury Emergencies","q":"A patient with a T4 spinal cord injury suddenly develops a severe throbbing headache, BP 214/118 mmHg, heart rate 46 bpm, nasal congestion, and profuse diaphoresis/flushing on the face and neck while the legs remain pale and cool. What is the FIRST immediate bedside action the nurse must take?","o":["Place the patient flat in Trendelenburg position to increase cerebral blood flow","Administer IV atropine immediately and ignore the catheter","Give a cold sponge bath to the lower extremities","Elevate the head of the bed to 90 degrees (high-Fowler’s sitting position), loosen tight clothing, and immediately check for a kinked Foley catheter / distended bladder"],"a":3,"e":"A T6-or-above spinal cord injury patient presenting with severe paroxysmal hypertension, pounding headache, reflex bradycardia, and sweating above the lesion is experiencing Autonomic Dysreflexia—a hypertensive emergency triggered by noxious stimuli below the injury (most commonly bladder distension from a blocked/kinked catheter, followed by faecal impaction). Sitting the patient upright immediately lowers BP orthostatically while locating and removing the noxious trigger.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02450","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Neuromuscular Disorders","q":"Assertion (A): In a patient with Myasthenia Gravis presenting with acute respiratory muscle weakness, transient improvement in muscle strength within 30–60 seconds after IV edrophonium (Tensilon) distinguishes myasthenic crisis (under-medication) from cholinergic crisis (anticholinesterase overdose). Reason (R): Atropine sulfate must be kept ready at the bedside during an edrophonium test to counteract severe muscarinic side effects such as bradycardia, bronchospasm, and hypersalivation. Choose the correct option:","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is NOT the explanation for why edrophonium differentiates the two crises","Both A and R are true, and R explains why myasthenic crisis occurs","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Assertion (A) is true: Edrophonium (ultra-short-acting acetylcholinesterase inhibitor) temporarily improves strength in myasthenic crisis (insufficient acetylcholine at nicotinic receptors) but worsens weakness/fasciculations in cholinergic crisis (excess acetylcholine causing depolarization block). Reason (R) is also true as an essential safety precaution (atropine reverses muscarinic SLUDGE/bradycardia toxicity), though R describes safety preparedness rather than the nicotinic mechanism stated in A.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02451","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing","topic":"Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT)","q":"Which pre-procedure preparation and pharmacotherapy protocol is standard for Modified Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT) in severe catatonia or treatment-resistant depression?","o":["Keep patient NPO for 6–8 hours, obtain informed consent, empty bladder, remove dentures/metallic hairpins, and administer pre-medication with anticholinergic (atropine/glycopyrrolate), short-acting IV anaesthetic (propofol/thiopentone), and succinylcholine muscle relaxant","Administer oral benzodiazepine 30 minutes before ECT to raise the seizure threshold","Restrain the patient tightly in prone position during the shock without anaesthesia","Allow a full breakfast 1 hour before ECT; administer haloperidol IM"],"a":0,"e":"Under India’s Mental Healthcare Act (2017), unmodified ECT is prohibited; all ECT must be modified with anaesthesia and muscle relaxation. Standard nursing prep includes NPO (aspiration prevention), informed consent, voiding, removing jewellery/dentures/pins, pre-ECT anticholinergic (reduces vagal bradycardia/secretions), IV induction agent, and succinylcholine (prevents fractures during tonic-clonic seizure), followed by lateral recovery positioning.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02452","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing","topic":"Anorexia Nervosa vs Bulimia Nervosa","q":"A 19-year-old college student with normal BMI (20.5 kg/m²) presents with dental enamel erosion on the lingual surfaces of upper teeth, bilateral painless parotid gland enlargement, calluses over the dorsal knuckles (Russell’s sign), and serum potassium 2.8 mEq/L with metabolic alkalosis. What eating disorder is most consistent with these findings?","o":["Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder","Bulimia Nervosa (recurrent binge eating and self-induced vomiting)","Pica","Restricting-type Anorexia Nervosa"],"a":1,"e":"Unlike Anorexia Nervosa (characterized by significantly low body weight, BMI \u003c 18.5 kg/m², amenorrhoea/lanugo, and fear of weight gain), patients with Bulimia Nervosa typically maintain normal or slightly above-normal weight while engaging in recurrent binge-eating episodes followed by compensatory purging. Self-induced vomiting causes gastric acid dental erosion, parotid sialadenosis, Russell’s sign (knuckle calluses from teeth), and hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02453","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing","topic":"Behavioural Psychotherapies","q":"Which behavioural therapy technique is considered the psychological treatment of choice for Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD), wherein the patient confronts anxiety-provoking contamination cues while refraining from performing compulsive washing rituals?","o":["Free association psychoanalysis","Flooding with physical restraints","Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP)","Aversion therapy"],"a":2,"e":"Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP)—a form of Cognitive Behavioural Therapy—gradually exposes the patient to obsession-triggering stimuli (e.g., touching a doorknob) while preventing the anxiety-relieving compulsion (e.g., handwashing). Over repeated sessions, conditioned anxiety habituates naturally without ritual performance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02454","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Congenital Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis vs Intussusception","q":"A 4-week-old male infant presents with forceful, non-bilious projectile vomiting immediately after every breastfeed, eager hunger after vomiting, visible left-to-right upper abdominal peristaltic waves, and a firm olive-shaped mass palpable in the epigastrium. Which diagnosis and electrolyte abnormality are expected?","o":["Intussusception; metabolic acidosis","Tracheoesophageal fistula; respiratory alkalosis","Hirschsprung disease; hypernatremic dehydration","Congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis; hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis"],"a":3,"e":"Hypertrophy of the pyloric sphincter typically presents at 3–6 weeks of age (more common in first-born males) with non-bilious projectile vomiting, visible gastric peristalsis, and a palpable \"olive\" mass. Persistent loss of gastric HCl and fluids leads to hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, which must be corrected with IV fluids/electrolytes before Ramstedt pyloromyotomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02455","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF)","q":"An 11-year-old girl develops migratory polyarthritis of the knees and ankles, a new pansystolic mitral regurgitation murmur, and subcutaneous nodules 3 weeks after an untreated Group A beta-hemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis. After acute anti-inflammatory management, what is the standard secondary prophylaxis to prevent recurrent Rheumatic Heart Disease?","o":["Intramuscular Benzathine Penicillin G (1.2 million units if \u003e27–30 kg; 600,000 units if \u003c=27–30 kg) every 3–4 weeks for prolonged years/adulthood","Oral amoxicillin for 7 days only","Annual influenza vaccine alone","Oral ciprofloxacin every 6 months"],"a":0,"e":"Acute Rheumatic Fever is diagnosed by Revised Jones Criteria (major criteria: Carditis, Arthritis, Chorea, Erythema marginatum, Subcutaneous nodules + evidence of preceding GAS infection). Secondary prophylaxis with deep IM Benzathine Penicillin G every 21–28 days (for at least 10 years or until age 40/lifelong in severe carditis) prevents recurrent streptococcal triggers and progressive valvular damage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02456","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Holliday-Segar Maintenance Fluid Calculation","q":"Using the Holliday-Segar formula (100 mL/kg for the first 10 kg, 50 mL/kg for the next 10 kg, and 20 mL/kg for each kilogram above 20 kg), what is the total 24-hour maintenance fluid requirement for a child weighing 24 kg?","o":["2,400 mL/24 hours","1,580 mL/24 hours","1,500 mL/24 hours","1,200 mL/24 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Applying Holliday-Segar weights: First 10 kg = 10 × 100 mL = 1,000 mL; Next 10 kg = 10 × 50 mL = 500 mL; Remaining 4 kg (24 - 20) = 4 × 20 mL = 80 mL. Total 24-hour maintenance fluid = 1,000 + 500 + 80 = 1,580 mL/24 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02457","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy","q":"A 27-year-old woman with 7 weeks of amenorrhoea and a positive urine pregnancy test presents to casualty with sudden severe right lower quadrant abdominal pain, dizziness, right shoulder-tip pain, BP 84/56 mmHg, pulse 126/min, marked pallor, abdominal guarding, and extreme cervical motion tenderness. What is the most likely diagnosis and immediate priority?","o":["Hyperemesis gravidarum; give IV metoclopramide only","Normal corpus luteum cyst; schedule routine ANC ultrasound next month","Ruptured ectopic pregnancy with hemoperitoneum; initiate two large-bore IV crystalloid infusions, blood crossmatch, and prepare for emergency laparotomy/laparoscopy","Threatened abortion; advise bed rest and discharge with oral progesterone"],"a":2,"e":"The classic triad of amenorrhoea, lower abdominal/pelvic pain, and vaginal bleeding (often minimal) accompanied by hemorrhagic shock, referred shoulder-tip pain (Kehr’s sign from diaphragmatic irritation by hemoperitoneum), and cervical excitation pain indicates a ruptured ectopic pregnancy. Immediate shock resuscitation and surgical hemostasis are life-saving.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02458","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations (including First Aid)","topic":"Drowning Resuscitation","q":"A teenager is pulled unconscious and non-breathing from a village pond (drowning). Once safely out of the water on a firm surface, what is the immediate resuscitation priority, and why must abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre) NOT be used?","o":["Leave the victim wet in cold wind without CPR for 15 minutes","Perform 10 forceful abdominal thrusts first to drain water from the stomach before checking breathing","Hang the victim upside down by the ankles for 10 minutes","Prioritize immediate oxygenation/rescue breathing alongside chest compressions (opening airway and delivering initial rescue breaths + 30:2 CPR); NEVER perform abdominal thrusts because lungs contain very little drainable water and thrusts provoke vomiting and fatal aspiration"],"a":3,"e":"Drowning causes primary hypoxic cardiac arrest; restoring alveolar oxygen via rescue breathing alongside high-quality CPR is the single most critical survival determinant (A-B-C oxygenation priority). Routine abdominal thrusts to \u0027drain water\u0027 are ineffective and dangerous—compressing a water-filled stomach forces gastric contents up into the pharynx and trachea, worsening aspiration and delaying CPR.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02459","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Nasogastric (Ryle\u0027s) Tube Length Measurement","q":"At a clinical nursing OSPE station prior to inserting a Ryle\u0027s (nasogastric) tube for gastric decompression, how does the student accurately measure the external length of tube to be inserted using the standard NEX method?","o":["From the tip of the patient\u0027s Nose -\u003e to the Earlobe -\u003e down to the Xiphoid process (NEX)","From the forehead hairline to the chin only","Always insert exactly 25 cm in every adult regardless of height","From the right wrist to the shoulder"],"a":0,"e":"Before lubricating and inserting a nasogastric tube, measuring from the tip of the Nose to the Earlobe and then down to the Xiphoid process (NEX) estimates the individualized anatomical distance through the nasopharynx and oesophagus into the stomach (~50–60 cm in average adults, marking the point with tape). Tip location must then be verified per protocol before feeding.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02460","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Macrodrip IV Flow Calculation","q":"A patient with dengue plasma leakage recovery is prescribed 1,800 mL of Ringer\u0027s Lactate over 12 hours using a macrodrip infusion set calibrated at 20 drops/mL. At how many drops/minute should the nurse regulate the drip?","o":["38 drops/min","50 drops/min","25 drops/min","75 drops/min"],"a":1,"e":"Using Drops/min = (Volume in mL × Drop Factor) ÷ (Time in hours × 60 min): (1,800 × 20) ÷ (12 × 60) = 36,000 ÷ 720 = 50 drops/min.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02461","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Acute Appendicitis","q":"A 19-year-old student presents with periumbilical pain migrating to the right lower quadrant (McBurney\u0027s point tenderness), anorexia, low-grade fever, and leukocytosis (suspected acute appendicitis) awaiting surgical evaluation. Which bedside intervention MUST the nurse strictly avoid?","o":["Keeping the patient NPO and starting prescribed maintenance IV fluids","Positioning in semi-Fowler\u0027s with knees flexed for comfort","Applying a warm heating pad/hot water bottle to the right lower quadrant or administering a laxative/enema","Monitoring vital signs and sudden relief of pain (which may signal rupture)"],"a":2,"e":"In acute appendicitis, applying local heat increases local blood flow and vascular congestion, accelerating suppuration and rupture; laxatives or enemas stimulate violent peristalsis, risking perforation and diffuse faecal peritonitis. An ice pack (never heat), NPO status, IV hydration, and close monitoring (note: sudden relief of severe pain followed by spreading rigidity suggests perforation!) are standard.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02462","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Acute Cholecystitis \u0026 Cholelithiasis","q":"A 44-year-old woman experiences recurrent right upper quadrant pain radiating to the right shoulder blade after fried meals, and stops breathing abruptly in pain when the examiner presses beneath her right costal margin during deep inspiration (positive Murphy\u0027s sign). Which dietary teaching and obstructive jaundice warning signs should the nurse provide?","o":["Eat raw fatty meats daily","Encourage high-fat butter/fried foods; pale clay-coloured stools are normal","Avoid all water intake","Follow a low-fat diet avoiding fried foods, rich ghee, and egg yolks (to prevent gallbladder contraction), and report yellowing of sclera, dark tea-coloured urine, or pale clay-coloured stools immediately"],"a":3,"e":"Dietary fats trigger duodenal cholecystokinin (CCK) release, forcing an inflamed/stone-filled gallbladder to contract against an obstructed cystic duct (biliary colic and positive Murphy\u0027s sign). A low-fat diet reduces painful spasms pre-cholecystectomy. Jaundice, dark bilirubinuria, and clay-coloured (acholic) stools signal common bile duct obstruction (choledocholithiasis) or cholangitis needing urgent intervention.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02463","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Mechanical Small-Bowel Obstruction","q":"A patient with prior abdominal surgeries develops colicky abdominal pain, bilious vomiting, and obstipation. On auscultation proximal to the suspected mechanical small-bowel obstruction, which characteristic bowel sound pattern is expected early in the course, and what is the primary bedside decompression measure?","o":["High-pitched, tinkling, hyperactive rushes (borborygmi) as bowel contracts vigorously against the blockage; initiate NPO, nasogastric suction decompression, and IV fluid/electrolyte replacement","Normal 10 clicks/min; discharge home","Completely silent abdomen from minute one; give oral castor oil","Soft continuous musical venous hum; start high-residue bran cereal"],"a":0,"e":"In early mechanical small-bowel obstruction (adhesions, hernia, volvulus), forceful peristalsis proximal to the occlusion produces high-pitched tinkling metallic sounds and rushes (transitioning to absent sounds only if prolonged ischaemia/gangrene or paralytic ileus develops). Immediate NPO rest, NG tube suction decompression (preventing vomiting, aspiration, and bowel-wall strangulation), and IV electrolyte correction stabilize the patient.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02464","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Parkinson\u0027s Disease Pharmacotherapy","q":"A 68-year-old man with Parkinson\u0027s disease taking oral levodopa-carbidopa reports that his morning pills fail to relieve his tremor and bradykinesia whenever he swallows them along with a high-protein breakfast of eggs and milk. What evidence-based medication-timing adjustment should the nurse teach?","o":["Crush levodopa into hot coffee","Take levodopa-carbidopa 30–60 minutes BEFORE meals (or with a small low-protein snack if nauseated) because large neutral dietary amino acids compete with levodopa transport across the intestine and blood-brain barrier","Double the levodopa dose every time he drinks milk","Stop levodopa permanently and eat high-protein meals hourly"],"a":1,"e":"Levodopa uses the Large Neutral Amino Acid (LNAA) active transporter to cross both the small intestinal mucosa and the blood-brain barrier. High-protein meals flood this transporter with competing dietary amino acids, causing erratic \u0027off\u0027 periods. Taking levodopa-carbidopa 30–60 minutes before protein-rich meals (or shifting major protein intake to evening dinner) markedly improves motor symptom control.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02465","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Post-Cataract Surgery Precautions","q":"Before discharging an elderly patient following right-eye cataract extraction and intraocular lens (IOL) implantation, which postoperative instruction protects the eye from intraocular pressure spikes and wound dehiscence?","o":["Sleep face-down on the operated right eye without a shield","Wash the eye vigorously with unboiled tap water and soap","Wear the protective eye shield at night, sleep on the back or unoperated left side, avoid bending below the waist, heavy lifting (\u003e 5 kg), straining at stool, or rubbing the eye, and report sudden severe eye pain or vision drop immediately","Lift heavy water buckets on Day 2 to regain strength"],"a":2,"e":"Following cataract extraction/IOL implantation, activities triggering Valsalva spikes or direct ocular pressure (bending head below waist, heavy lifting, coughing/straining at stool, rubbing, or sleeping on the operated side) elevate intraocular pressure and risk iris prolapse or wound leak. Using a night eye shield, prescribed antibiotic/steroid drops, stool softeners, and urgent reporting of severe pain/redness/vision loss (possible endophthalmitis) safeguard sight.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02466","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Systematic Desensitization","q":"A behavioural therapist treats a client with severe fear of elevators (claustrophobia) by first teaching progressive muscle relaxation, then pairing deep relaxation gradually with an anxiety hierarchy—from viewing elevator photos to riding one floor with support. Which behavioural therapy is being applied?","o":["Flooding (abrupt maximum exposure)","Electroconvulsive therapy","Aversion therapy","Systematic desensitization (reciprocal inhibition)"],"a":3,"e":"Developed by Joseph Wolpe, systematic desensitization pairs an incompatible physiological state (deep muscle relaxation) sequentially with a graded hierarchy of phobic stimuli from least to most frightening—extinguishing conditioned panic through reciprocal inhibition. By contrast, Flooding exposes the client abruptly to the highest-intensity fear stimulus without gradual steps.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02467","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Tardive Dyskinesia (TD)","q":"A 45-year-old client treated with chlorpromazine/haloperidol for 5 years develops repetitive involuntary lip-smacking, chewing movements, worm-like tongue protrusion (\u0027fly-catcher\u0027 tongue), and choreiform finger writhing. Which late-onset adverse effect is occurring, and why should routine anticholinergics (e.g., trihexyphenidyl) NOT be added to treat it?","o":["Tardive Dyskinesia (striatal dopamine D2 receptor supersensitivity); adding anticholinergics typically WORSENS tardive dyskinesia—notify the psychiatrist to taper/switch to an atypical antipsychotic (e.g., quetiapine/clozapine) or VMAT2 inhibitor","Acute mania; increase haloperidol dose to 30 mg/day","Lithium toxicity; give IV normal saline","Acute dystonia; trihexyphenidyl cures lip-smacking immediately"],"a":0,"e":"Tardive Dyskinesia (TD) emerges after months to years of dopamine-antagonist exposure due to nigrostriatal D2 receptor upregulation and supersensitivity, assessed regularly via the Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale (AIMS). Unlike acute dystonia or Parkinsonism, TD is aggravated by anticholinergics (trihexyphenidyl/benztropine) and requires gradual antipsychotic reduction/switching (clozapine/quetiapine) or VMAT2 inhibitors (valbenazine/deutetrabenazine).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02468","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health Nursing","topic":"Bipolar I vs Bipolar II Disorder","q":"A client with recurrent major depressive episodes experiences a 5-day period of elevated mood, decreased need for sleep, rapid speech, and increased goal-directed productivity, WITHOUT psychosis, severe occupational breakdown, or need for psychiatric hospitalization. Which mood disorder classification fits this presentation?","o":["Unipolar Dysthymia solely","Bipolar II Disorder (major depressive episode plus hypomanic episode \u003e= 4 days without psychosis or severe impairment)","Generalized Anxiety Disorder","Bipolar I Disorder (full mania with hospitalization/psychosis)"],"a":1,"e":"Bipolar I Disorder is defined by at least one full manic episode (\u003e= 7 days, or any duration if hospitalization or psychotic features occur). Bipolar II Disorder is characterized by at least one major depressive episode AND at least one hypomanic episode (lasting \u003e= 4 consecutive days, noticeable to others, but lacking psychotic features, hospitalization, or marked social/occupational impairment).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02469","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Acute Otitis Media (AOM)","q":"An 8-month-old infant presents for the third time with acute otitis media (fever, ear tugging, irritability, bulging red tympanic membrane); the mother admits propping a feeding bottle in the baby\u0027s mouth while the infant lies flat in the crib at night. Which anatomical factor explains this recurrence, and what preventive advice applies?","o":["Advise putting hot garlic oil into the ear canal nightly","Otitis media is caused exclusively by bathing water","The infant\u0027s Eustachian tube is shorter, wider, and more horizontal than an adult\u0027s, allowing pooled milk and nasopharyngeal bacteria to reflux easily into the middle ear; advise holding the infant semi-upright during feeds, never propping bottles in bed, and avoiding passive tobacco smoke","Infants have a longer, steeply vertical Eustachian tube"],"a":2,"e":"In infants and young toddlers, the Eustachian (auditory) tube is relatively short, wide, flaccid, and lies almost horizontally compared with the angled adult tube. Feeding flat supine with a propped bottle allows milk and respiratory pathogens to track directly into the middle ear cavity causing recurrent Acute Otitis Media. Semi-upright feeding, exclusive breastfeeding/no bottle-propping, smoke-free air, and PCV immunization prevent episodes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02470","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"IMNCI Plan B ORS Volume Calculation","q":"A 12 kg toddler with acute diarrhoea is classified under IMNCI as having \u0027Some Dehydration\u0027 (Plan B), requiring WHO low-osmolarity ORS at 75 mL/kg over the first 4 hours in the clinic. How many millilitres (mL) of ORS should the nurse prepare to administer over these 4 hours?","o":["750 mL","1,200 mL","600 mL","900 mL"],"a":3,"e":"Under IMNCI Plan B for Some Dehydration, the recommended initial 4-hour ORS volume is 75 mL/kg: 12 kg × 75 mL/kg = 900 mL given by frequent small sips from a cup/spoon (paired with continued breastfeeding and 20 mg oral zinc daily for 14 days, then reassessing dehydration status at 4 hours).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02471","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Beta-Thalassaemia Major","q":"A 9-year-old child with beta-thalassaemia major receiving regular packed red blood cell transfusions every 3–4 weeks has serum ferritin rising to 2,400 ng/mL. Which long-term complication threatens the heart, liver, and endocrine glands, and which therapy prevents it?","o":["Secondary iron overload (haemosiderosis / haemochromatosis from repeated RBC transfusions); initiate regular iron chelation therapy (e.g., oral deferasirox or parenteral desferrioxamine)","Severe iron deficiency; give double doses of oral iron syrup","Acute calcium toxicity; restrict all protein","Vitamin K overdose; perform splenectomy immediately without chelation"],"a":0,"e":"Because the human body has no active physiological pathway to excrete excess iron, chronic RBC transfusions in beta-thalassaemia major progressively deposit toxic free iron in the myocardium (causing fatal heart failure/arrhythmias), liver (cirrhosis), pituitary/pancreas/parathyroids (growth failure, diabetes, hypogonadism). Regular iron chelation (deferasirox, deferiprone, or desferrioxamine) guided by serial serum ferritin/MRI T2* prevents organ damage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02472","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"True Labour vs False Labour","q":"A primigravida at 38 weeks gestation arrives at the labour ward reporting uterine tightenings. Which clinical finding definitively confirms TRUE labour rather than false labour (Braxton Hicks contractions)?","o":["Irregular tightenings confined to the lower abdomen that disappear after walking or hydration","Regular uterine contractions increasing progressively in frequency, duration, and intensity (radiating from back to abdomen), accompanied by progressive cervical effacement and dilatation","Absence of any change in cervical length or os over 6 hours","Mild contractions relieved completely by a warm bath"],"a":1,"e":"The hallmark differentiating true labour from false labour is progressive cervical change (effacement and dilatation) driven by regular contractions that intensify and shorten in interval over time, unaffected by rest, ambulation, or hydration, and commonly accompanied by bloody show. Braxton Hicks contractions remain irregular, non-progressive, and produce zero cervical dilatation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02473","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Obstructed Labour \u0026 Threatened Uterine Rupture","q":"A multiparous woman in prolonged obstructed labour suddenly develops severe continuous abdominal pain between contractions, marked lower-segment tenderness, maternal tachycardia (124 bpm), and an oblique groove/ridge visible across the abdomen rising toward the umbilicus (Bandl\u0027s ring). Which emergency is imminent, and what must the nurse do immediately?","o":["Normal second-stage descent; increase IV oxytocin rate immediately","Normal third stage; apply firm fundal pressure","Threatened uterine rupture from obstructed labour; stop any oxytocin infusion immediately, call emergency obstetric/surgical team for urgent Caesarean delivery, establish two large-bore IV lines, and cross-match blood","False labour; send home to walk"],"a":2,"e":"A visible/palpable pathological retraction ring (Bandl\u0027s ring) between the thick upper active uterine segment and an overstretched, Paper-thin tender lower uterine segment signals obstructed labour on the verge of uterine rupture. Stopping oxytocin immediately, rapid IV resuscitation/cross-matching, and immediate Caesarean delivery prevent catastrophic uterine rupture, maternal exsanguination, and foetal death.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02474","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery \u0026 Paediatric Nursing","topic":"Pathological vs Physiological Neonatal Jaundice","q":"Fourteen hours after birth, a term newborn develops visible yellow discolouration of the face and upper chest (icterus). Why must this be classified as pathological jaundice requiring immediate investigation rather than benign physiological jaundice?","o":["Term babies normally turn yellow in the delivery room","Breastfeeding jaundice starts at hour 14 normally","Physiological jaundice always begins at 2 hours of birth","Visible jaundice appearing within the first 24 hours of life is ALWAYS pathological (e.g., Rh/ABO incompatibility, G6PD deficiency, or sepsis) and carries high risk of rapid hyperbilirubinaemia and kernicterus; physiological jaundice typically appears after 24–48 hours (Day 2–3)"],"a":3,"e":"Benign physiological neonatal jaundice never appears before 24 hours of age; it usually becomes visible on Day 2–3 (48–72 h), peaks around Day 3–5 (\u003c 12–15 mg/dL in term infants), and resolves by Day 7–10. Any jaundice visible \u003c 24 hours after birth, rising \u003e 5 mg/dL/day, persisting \u003e 14 days in term babies, or accompanied by pale stools/dark urine is pathological and requires urgent serum bilirubin, blood group/DCT, sepsis workup, and phototherapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02475","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing I","topic":"Pasteurization of Milk","q":"In community food hygiene (Park\u0027s PSM), what are the standard High-Temperature Short-Time (HTST) milk pasteurization temperature and duration, and which biochemical test verifies adequate pasteurization?","o":["72°C for at least 15 seconds followed by rapid cooling to \u003c= 4°C; verified by a negative Alkaline Phosphatase test","100°C for 60 minutes; Widal test","50°C for 5 seconds; Benedict\u0027s test","37°C for 10 seconds; VDRL test"],"a":0,"e":"HTST pasteurization heats milk rapidly to 72°C (161°F) for 15 seconds (compared with Holder method: 63°C–66°C for 30 minutes) and cools immediately to \u003c= 4°C, killing milkborne pathogens (M. bovis, Brucella, Salmonella, Coxiella) while preserving nutrition and taste. Because raw milk alkaline phosphatase is destroyed at exactly the same time-temperature threshold, a negative phosphatase test proves complete pasteurization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02476","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing II","topic":"Leptospirosis (Weil\u0027s Disease)","q":"Following heavy monsoon flooding in a coastal district, several paddy farmers and municipal drain cleaners present with high fever, severe calf-muscle tenderness, red eyes without pus (conjunctival suffusion), and jaundice (Leptospirosis). Which transmission route and preventive strategy apply?","o":["Transmitted by airborne coughing solely; wear ear plugs","Transmitted via skin abrasions or mucous membranes contacting floodwater/wet soil contaminated with infected rodent/animal urine; prevent using waterproof boots/gloves, rodent control, covering skin cuts, and weekly doxycycline chemoprophylaxis during high-risk flood exposures per MoHFW guidelines","Transmitted by raw seafood solely; avoid all cooked rice","Transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes; apply calamine lotion"],"a":1,"e":"Leptospira interrogans is excreted in the urine of rodents and domestic animals, surviving in warm alkaline floodwater and wet paddy soil. Walking barefoot or working with open abrasions allows spirochaetes to enter, causing fever, calf myalgia, conjunctival suffusion, and severe hepato-renal Weil\u0027s syndrome. Protective footwear/gloves, rodent sanitation, prompt penicillin/doxycycline therapy, and flood-outbreak chemoprophylaxis (doxycycline 200 mg weekly) prevent severe morbidity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02477","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Local Cold vs Heat Therapy","q":"A college athlete twists her ankle on the field 2 hours ago, presenting with acute swelling, bruising, and tenderness without fracture (acute sprain). Which local thermal application is appropriate during the first 24–48 hours?","o":["Immerse the ankle in hot boiling water for 1 hour immediately","Apply a high-heat electric pad continuously for 6 hours","Apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack intermittently (15–20 minutes at a time, with a cloth barrier to prevent frostbite) alongside Rest, Compression, and Elevation (RICE)","Massage deeply with hot liniment immediately"],"a":2,"e":"During the first 24–48 hours after acute musculoskeletal sprain/contusion, cold therapy causes local arteriolar vasoconstriction, reducing capillary bleeding, interstitial oedema, cellular metabolic demand, and nerve-conduction pain. Always wrap ice in a cloth/towel and limit applications to 15–20 minutes to prevent tissue ischaemia/frostbite. Heat or vigorous massage acutely increases vasodilation and haematoma swelling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02478","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Anatomy and Physiology)","topic":"Regulation of Erythropoiesis","q":"In adults, tissue hypoxia stimulates synthesis and release of the glycoprotein hormone Erythropoietin (EPO) to drive bone marrow RBC production. Which organ produces roughly 85%–90% of circulating Erythropoietin?","o":["Thymus gland","Anterior pituitary gland","Spleen (red pulp macrophages)","Kidneys (peritubular interstitial fibroblasts of the renal cortex)"],"a":3,"e":"Approximately 85%–90% of Erythropoietin is synthesized by peritubular capillary interstitial cells in the adult kidney (with ~10%–15% from the liver) in response to hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF). Consequently, patients with chronic kidney disease develop normocytic normochromic anaemia requiring exogenous recombinant EPO (epoetin alfa).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02479","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Anatomy and Physiology)","topic":"Ovarian and Menstrual Cycle Physiology","q":"In a healthy woman with a regular 28-day menstrual cycle, which acute hormonal event triggers ovulation around Day 14, and which hormone secreted thereafter by the corpus luteum prepares the secretory endometrium for implantation?","o":["Rapid mid-cycle Luteinizing Hormone (LH) surge triggered by positive estrogen feedback causes ovulation; Progesterone from the corpus luteum maintains the secretory endometrium","FSH drop causes ovulation; Oxytocin maintains the secretory endometrium","Prolactin surge triggers ovulation; Testosterone maintains secretory endometrium","hCG surge causes spontaneous ovulation; Estriol causes menstruation"],"a":0,"e":"Sustained high estradiol from the mature Graafian follicle switches feedback from negative to positive, triggering an abrupt anterior pituitary LH surge (peaking ~24–36 hours before follicular rupture/ovulation on Day 14). The residual follicle transforms into the corpus luteum, secreting Progesterone to convert proliferative endometrium into a vascular secretory lining.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02480","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Anatomy and Physiology)","topic":"Cranial Nerves Functional Anatomy","q":"Match each cranial nerve with its primary clinical function: 1. Trigeminal nerve (CN V) 2. Facial nerve (CN VII) 3. Vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) 4. Vagus nerve (CN X) Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Taste from posterior 1/3 of tongue - Mastication - Smell - Lateral eye movement","Facial touch/pain sensation \u0026 muscles of mastication - Muscles of facial expression \u0026 taste from anterior 2/3 of tongue - Hearing \u0026 equilibrium - Parasympathetic innervation of thoracic/abdominal viscera \u0026 gag/swallowing","Visual acuity - Eye abduction - Tongue movement - Shoulder shrug","Facial expression - Facial sensation - Pupillary constriction - Hearing"],"a":1,"e":"(1) CN V mediates facial sensation (ophthalmic, maxillary, mandibular) and motor mastication; (2) CN VII innervates facial expression muscles (impaired in Bell’s palsy) and anterior 2/3 tongue taste; (3) CN VIII mediates cochlear hearing and vestibular balance; (4) CN X supplies motor palate/pharynx/larynx and major parasympathetic fibres to heart, lungs, and GI tract.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02481","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Microbiology)","topic":"Immunoglobulin Classes","q":"Match each immunoglobulin class with its defining biological property: 1. IgG 2. IgM 3. Secretory IgA 4. IgE Select the correct match sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Mediates anaphylaxis - Mucosal immunity - Crosses placenta - Primary acute response","Pentamer in mucosa - Monomer in colostrum - Helminth defence - Secondary anamnestic response","Only antibody crossing the human placenta to protect newborns - Pentamer produced first in primary acute infection - Dimer guarding respiratory/GI mucosa and abundant in colostrum - Mediates Type I anaphylaxis and helminth defence via mast cells","Dimer in breastmilk - Crosses placenta - Monomer in B-cell receptor - Fixes complement in blood"],"a":2,"e":"IgG (~75% of serum Ig) is the sole class transported across the placenta via FcRn to confer passive fetal/neonatal immunity; IgM is a large pentamer (does not cross placenta—thus fetal IgM indicates congenital infection) appearing earliest in primary infection; Secretory IgA is a J-chain dimer with secretory component defending mucosal surfaces and breastmilk; IgE binds FcεRI on mast cells/basophils.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02482","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Bio-Sciences (Microbiology)","topic":"Occupational Exposure and Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP)","q":"While recapping a used hollow-bore needle carelessly, a student nurse sustains a deep finger prick from a patient with unknown HIV/HBV status. What is the immediate first-aid wound care and optimal initiation window for HIV Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) under NACO guidelines?","o":["Squeeze the finger forcefully to bleed it out and scrub with concentrated hypochlorite bleach; start PEP after 1 week","Take a single dose of azithromycin and cover with adhesive tape","Apply a tight rubber tourniquet at the wrist and wait 14 days for ELISA results","Wash the site immediately and gently under running water with soap (do NOT squeeze, suck, or apply bleach/antiseptic caustic agents); report immediately to the nodal officer so HIV PEP (28-day 3-drug ART) is started ideally within 2 hours and no later than 72 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Per NACO (India) PEP guidelines, wash percutaneous injuries immediately with mild soap and running water without squeezing or scrubbing (which increases tissue trauma and viral entry), never apply bleach/alcohol/caustics, evaluate source HIV/HBsAg/HCV status and nurse’s HBV vaccine immunity, and initiate 3-drug HIV PEP (e.g., TDF + 3TC + DTG for 28 days) ideally within 2 hours (ineffective if delayed \u003e 72 hours).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02483","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Cardiovascular Vital Signs","q":"A patient with atrial fibrillation has an apical heart rate of 108 beats/minute and a simultaneous radial pulse rate of 84 beats/minute. How should the nurse document this finding, and what causes the difference?","o":["Pulse deficit of 24 beats/minute; caused by weak, premature ventricular contractions that fail to generate enough stroke volume to transmit a palpable radial pulse wave","Water-hammer pulse; caused by fever","Pulsus alternans of 24; caused by aortic stenosis","Orthostatic hypotension of 24; caused by dehydration"],"a":0,"e":"Pulse deficit equals Apical Rate minus Radial Rate measured simultaneously for one full minute by two nurses (here 108 - 84 = 24 beats/minute). In atrial fibrillation or frequent premature beats, short diastolic filling time produces ineffective beats audible at the apex that fail to open the aortic valve strongly enough to create a peripheral radial pulse.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02484","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations","topic":"Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)","q":"During neurological assessment of a road-traffic accident patient, the nurse observes: eyes open ONLY to painful pressure on the trapezius/nailbed, verbal output consists only of incomprehensible groaning sounds, and the patient’s best motor response to pain is abnormal flexion of the arms toward the chest (decorticate posturing). What is the patient’s Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score, and what airway implication does it carry?","o":["GCS = 12 (E3 V4 M5); mild head injury","GCS = 7 (E2 + V2 + M3); severe coma requiring immediate airway protection / endotracheal intubation","GCS = 5 (E1 + V2 + M2); brain death","GCS = 9 (E2 + V3 + M4); moderate head injury"],"a":1,"e":"GCS scoring: Eye opening to pain = E2 (spontaneous=4, to speech=3, to pain=2, none=1); Verbal incomprehensible sounds = V2 (oriented=5, confused=4, inappropriate words=3, incomprehensible sounds=2, none=1); Motor abnormal flexion/decorticate = M3 (obeys=6, localizes=5, normal withdrawal=4, abnormal flexion=3, extension/decerebrate=2, none=1). Total = 2 + 2 + 3 = 7. Any GCS \u003c= 8 defines coma and warrants airway protection (\"less than 8, intubate\").","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02485","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundations (First Aid)","topic":"Chemical Eye Splash and Corrosive Ingestion","q":"A factory worker accidentally splashes caustic soda (alkali) into his left eye while a coworker swallows a mouthful of drain cleaner from an unlabelled bottle. What are the two correct immediate first-aid actions for these two victims respectively?","o":["Patch the splashed eye tightly without washing and wait for an ophthalmologist; insert a nasogastric tube forcefully","Instill dilute acetic acid drops into the eye to neutralize alkali; induce vomiting immediately with salt water for ingestion","Irrigate the splashed eye continuously for 15–30 minutes with copious normal saline/clean water flowing from inner to outer canthus; NEVER induce vomiting or give neutralizing vinegar for the corrosive ingestion","Apply butter to the eye and give activated charcoal in corrosive alkali ingestion"],"a":2,"e":"Alkali ocular burns cause rapid liquefactive necrosis and penetrate deeply; immediate, prolonged irrigation (\u003e= 15–30 min from inner to outer canthus so the unaffected right eye is protected) is vision-saving. In corrosive acid/alkali ingestion, inducing emesis re-exposes the esophagus and pharynx to burning chemical, and chemical neutralization generates exothermic heat worsening mucosal perforation; maintain airway and refer urgently.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02486","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding","q":"A 48-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis and portal hypertension vomits 700 mL of bright red blood (massive hematemesis), with BP 86/54 mmHg and pulse 128/min. After airway protection and two wide-bore IV lines for cautious crystalloid/packed RBC resuscitation, which vasoactive drug is initiated immediately alongside prophylactic IV antibiotics prior to urgent endoscopic variceal band ligation?","o":["Oral aspirin and ibuprofen","Oral lactulose tablet only with no IV drugs","IV heparin infusion","IV Octreotide (or Terlipressin) to reduce splanchnic blood flow and portal venous pressure"],"a":3,"e":"In suspected acute oesophageal variceal bleeding in a cirrhotic patient, immediate splanchnic vasoconstrictors—IV Octreotide (somatostatin analogue) or Terlipressin—rapidly lower portal venous pressure and control bleeding while preparing for endoscopic variceal ligation (EVL) within 12 hours, paired with prophylactic ceftriaxone to prevent spontaneous bacterial peritonitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02487","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Thoracic Trauma Emergencies","q":"A patient with blunt chest trauma develops sudden severe dyspnea, restlessness, distended neck veins (JVD), absent breath sounds and hyperresonance over the right hemithorax, tracheal deviation to the LEFT, and BP 74/46 mmHg. What is the immediate life-saving diagnosis and intervention prior to obtaining a chest X-ray?","o":["Right-sided Tension Pneumothorax; perform immediate needle decompression (thoracostomy) followed by tube thoracostomy (ICD) without delaying for X-ray","Pericardial tamponade; give 2 litres normal saline and observe","Pulmonary edema; administer IV furosemide and wait for portable chest X-ray","Simple rib fracture; apply tight adhesive chest strapping"],"a":0,"e":"A one-way pleural valve traps air under rising positive pressure in the right pleural space, collapsing the right lung and shifting the mediastinum/trachea to the opposite (left) side, kinking venae cavae and causing obstructive shock. Tension pneumothorax is a CLINICAL diagnosis—delaying for chest X-ray causes cardiac arrest. Immediate needle decompression (wide-bore cannula at 2nd ICS midclavicular or 4th/5th ICS anterior axillary line) converts it to a simple pneumothorax before ICD insertion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02488","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Thyroid Crisis (Thyroid Storm)","q":"A patient with untreated Graves’ disease develops fever of 40.5°C, heart rate 156/min, severe agitation, and delirium (Thyroid Storm) after an acute infection. To reduce fever, why must Aspirin (salicylates) be STRICTLY AVOIDED in favour of Paracetamol and external cooling?","o":["Aspirin causes severe hypothyroidism within 10 minutes","Salicylates displace thyroxine (T4) from thyroid-binding globulin (TBG), acutely increasing free circulating thyroid hormone and worsening the crisis","Aspirin stimulates TSH secretion from the pituitary","Aspirin blocks the absorption of beta-blockers"],"a":1,"e":"Thyroid storm is treated with PTU (preferred acutely as it blocks both hormone synthesis and peripheral T4-\u003eT3 conversion), followed 1 hour later by Lugol’s iodine/SSKI (blocks hormone release), propranolol (blocks beta-adrenergic storm and T4-\u003eT3), hydrocortisone, and paracetamol/cooling blankets. Aspirin/salicylates compete with T4/T3 for TBG binding, spiking free active thyroid hormone.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02489","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing I","topic":"Acute Hyperkalemia in Renal Failure","q":"A chronic kidney disease patient who missed two dialysis sessions has a serum potassium of 7.4 mEq/L, and the ECG monitor shows tall, narrow, peaked T waves with widening QRS complexes. Which medication must be administered FIRST intravenously to immediately stabilize the cardiac membrane?","o":["IV Spironolactone","Oral Sodium Polystyrene Sulfonate (Kayexalate) suspension","Slow IV 10% Calcium Gluconate (10 mL over 2–5 minutes with continuous ECG monitoring)","Subcutaneous Epinephrine"],"a":2,"e":"When severe hyperkalemia (\u003e 6.5 mEq/L) produces ECG changes (peaked T waves, prolonged PR, widened QRS, sine wave), the immediate first step is IV Calcium Gluconate 10%, which antagonizes potassium-induced cardiac membrane depolarization within 1–3 minutes (cardioprotection, though it does not lower serum K+). Next, shift K+ intracellularly (Regular Insulin 10 U + 25 g IV Dextrose, nebulized salbutamol) and eliminate K+ (loop diuretics/potassium binders/emergency haemodialysis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02490","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Acute Compartment Syndrome","q":"Six hours after closed reduction and application of a below-knee plaster cast for a tibial shaft fracture, a 24-year-old man complains of intense, escalating throbbing leg pain unrelieved by IV morphine, exacerbated severely by passive dorsiflexion of the toes, with tense swelling and numbness over the first webspace. What is the priority diagnosis and immediate nursing/orthopaedic intervention?","o":["Normal post-reduction cast soreness; elevate the leg high above heart level on 4 pillows and apply ice","Acute Compartment Syndrome; immediately bivalve/split the tight cast and underlying padding down to the skin, keep limb at heart level (not elevated), and prepare for urgent surgical fasciotomy","Deep vein thrombosis; administer oral warfarin and encourage walking","Pressure ulcer under heel; wait 48 hours until cast clinic follow-up"],"a":1,"e":"Pain out of proportion to injury—especially pain on passive stretching of muscles in the compartment—plus paresthesia are the earliest hallmarks of Acute Compartment Syndrome (pulselessness is a late, ominous sign). Splitting/bivalving both plaster and cotton padding relieves external pressure immediately, keeping the limb at heart level maintains arterial inflow (excessive elevation reduces arteriolar perfusion gradient), and urgent fasciotomy saves the limb.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02491","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Retinal Detachment","q":"A 56-year-old patient with high myopia reports sudden onset of flashes of light (photopsia), showers of black floating spots (\"cobwebs\"), followed by a painless dark \"curtain\" descending across the upper visual field of the left eye. Which ophthalmic emergency is occurring?","o":["Bacterial conjunctivitis","Allergic blepharitis","Senile mature cataract","Acute retinal detachment"],"a":3,"e":"Vitreous traction tearing the neurosensory retina causes photopsia (flashes) and floaters (vitreous pigment/blood), followed by painless progressive visual field loss described as a dark curtain or veil moving across vision as subretinal fluid detaches the retina from the retinal pigment epithelium. Urgent bed rest/bilateral eye patching (per specialist) and emergency vitreoretinal repair preserve macula/vision.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02492","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing II","topic":"Acute Bacterial Meningitis","q":"When examining a febrile young adult with severe headache, photophobia, and petechial rash suspected of meningococcal meningitis, flexing the patient’s neck passively causes involuntary flexion of both hips and knees. What is this sign called, and what isolation precaution is required for the first 24 hours of effective IV antibiotic therapy?","o":["Brudzinski’s neck sign; Droplet precautions (surgical mask within 1 metre, single/cohorted room) plus chemoprophylaxis for close contacts","Homan’s sign; Airborne negative-pressure isolation only","Babinski sign; Reverse protective isolation","Trousseau’s sign; Standard precautions with no mask"],"a":0,"e":"Involuntary hip/knee flexion upon passive neck flexion is Brudzinski’s sign (Kernig’s sign is pain/resistance on extending the knee with hip flexed 90°). Neisseria meningitidis spreads via respiratory droplets, mandating Droplet precautions until 24 hours after starting effective antibiotics (e.g., IV ceftriaxone) and rifampicin/ciprofloxacin/ceftriaxone prophylaxis for close contacts.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02493","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing","topic":"Antidepressant Drug Interactions","q":"Within 6 hours of accidentally taking fluoxetine (SSRI), linezolid, and tramadol together, a 30-year-old woman develops acute agitation, diaphoresis, shivering, dilated pupils, hyperactive bowel sounds, brisk hyperreflexia, and sustained ankle clonus. Which toxidrome has developed, and which specific serotonin antagonist may be used if supportive care and benzodiazepines are insufficient?","o":["Anticholinergic delirium; physostigmine","Serotonin Syndrome; Cyproheptadine (5-HT2A antagonist)","Lithium toxicity; haemodialysis","Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome; dantrolene"],"a":1,"e":"Excessive synaptic serotonin (from combining SSRIs, SNRIs, MAOIs, linezolid, tramadol, or triptans) produces rapid-onset (hours) neuromuscular excitability—specifically hyperreflexia and spontaneous/inducible clonus (Hunter criteria)—alongside autonomic hyperactivity (mydriasis, diaphoresis, hyperactive bowel sounds, tachycardia) and agitation. Management is immediate cessation of serotonergic agents, benzodiazepines, cooling, and oral/NG cyproheptadine. (Unlike NMS, which develops over days with antipsychotics, bradyreflexia, and lead-pipe rigidity).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02494","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing","topic":"Delirium vs Dementia","q":"Match each neurocognitive feature with either Delirium or Dementia: 1. Acute onset over hours/days with fluctuating course (\"sundowning\") 2. Clear level of consciousness and alertness until late stages 3. Marked impairment of sustained attention and sleep-wake cycle reversal 4. Insidious, slowly progressive memory impairment over months/years (usually irreversible) Select the correct sequence (1 - 2 - 3 - 4):","o":["Dementia - Delirium - Dementia - Delirium","Delirium - Delirium - Dementia - Dementia","Delirium - Dementia - Delirium - Dementia","Dementia - Dementia - Delirium - Delirium"],"a":2,"e":"Delirium is an acute, potentially reversible medical emergency (often from infection, hypoxia, metabolic derangement, or drug withdrawal) marked by fluctuating consciousness/attention, perceptual illusions/hallucinations, and sleep-wake disturbance. Dementia (e.g., Alzheimer’s or vascular dementia) has an insidious onset over months/years with intact alertness/consciousness early on and progressive decline in memory and executive function.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02495","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing","topic":"Therapeutic Communication","q":"An anxious preoperative patient says to the nurse, \"I am terrified I will never wake up after the surgery tomorrow.\" Which response demonstrates a therapeutic communication technique?","o":["\"Let’s talk about something happier—did you watch the cricket match?\"","\"Why do you feel so scared when this is a routine operation?\"","\"Don’t worry at all, everything will be completely fine—our surgeons never lose patients.\"","\"It sounds like you are feeling very frightened about going under anaesthesia tomorrow. Would you like to share what worries you most?\""],"a":3,"e":"Reflecting the patient’s expressed emotion and offering an open-ended invitation to explore concerns validates feelings and builds trust. False reassurance (\"everything will be fine\"), probing \"why\" questions (which make patients defensive), and changing the subject are classic non-therapeutic communication blocks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02496","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Upper Airway Emergencies in Children","q":"A 4-year-old unimmunized child arrives in acute respiratory distress sitting upright leaning forward on outstretched hands with chin thrust out (tripod position), high fever (39.6°C), muffled \"hot potato\" voice, dysphagia, and continuous drooling of saliva (Acute Epiglottitis). Which nursing/medical action is STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED in the emergency room?","o":["Inspecting the posterior pharynx directly with a tongue depressor or attempting throat swabbing in the casualty ward","Allowing the child to remain calm in the mother’s lap in a position of comfort","Alerting anaesthesia/ENT immediately for controlled endotracheal intubation in the operating room","Administering humidified blow-by oxygen gently"],"a":0,"e":"Acute epiglottitis (classically Haemophilus influenzae type b in unimmunized children) causes massive supraglottic swelling. Depressing the tongue, forcing the child supine, or agitating the child with painful venipuncture/throat swabs can trigger immediate reflex laryngospasm and complete fatal airway obstruction. Keep the child calm upright with parent, give blow-by O2, and secure the airway in OT before IV/cultures/ceftriaxone.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02497","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Kawasaki Disease","q":"A 3-year-old boy has had unremitting high fever for 7 days accompanied by bilateral non-purulent conjunctival injection, cracked red lips and \"strawberry tongue\", polymorphous trunk rash, swollen erythematous palms/soles, and a unilateral 2 cm cervical lymph node. Which first-line therapy started within 10 days of illness onset dramatically reduces the risk of coronary artery aneurysms?","o":["Oral prednisolone monotherapy without IVIG","High-dose Intravenous Immunoglobulin (IVIG 2 g/kg single infusion) plus oral Aspirin","Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate alone","Intramuscular benzathine penicillin"],"a":1,"e":"Kawasaki Disease is an acute medium-vessel vasculitis diagnosed by fever \u003e= 5 days plus \u003e= 4 of 5 mucocutaneous features (CRASH: Conjunctivitis bilateral non-exudative, Rash, Adenopathy cervical \u003e= 1.5 cm, Strawberry tongue/cracked lips, Hand/foot erythema/edema). Prompt IVIG (2 g/kg over 10–12 hrs) plus aspirin reduces coronary artery aneurysm incidence from ~20%–25% down to \u003c 4%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02498","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Child Health Nursing","topic":"Immediate Newborn Assessment","q":"At 1 minute after birth, a term newborn exhibits: heart rate = 132 beats/min, slow irregular breathing with weak cry, some flexion of extremities, grimace on gentle stimulation, and pink body with bluish hands and feet (acrocyanosis). What is the newborn’s 1-minute APGAR score?","o":["9","8","6","4"],"a":2,"e":"APGAR scoring (0–2 per component): Appearance (pink trunk, blue extremities/acrocyanosis) = 1; Pulse (132 bpm \u003e= 100) = 2; Grimace (facial grimace only, no vigorous cry/cough) = 1; Activity (some flexion of extremities) = 1; Respiratory effort (slow/irregular, weak cry) = 1. Total = 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 6 (moderate depression requiring airway clearing/stimulation/close observation and repeat scoring at 5 minutes).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02499","course":"GNM Nursing","subject":"Midwifery and Gynaecological Nursing","topic":"Pre-Induction Cervical Assessment","q":"Before inducing labour with oxytocin or prostaglandins, the obstetric team calculates a Bishop Score (0–13) to assess cervical favourability. Which FIVE clinical parameters comprise the Bishop Score?","o":["Uterine contraction frequency, pelvic outlet diameter, fetal weight, parity, and gestational age","Maternal blood pressure, fundal height, fetal heart rate, amniotic fluid index, and haemoglobin","Cervical dilatation, membrane status, urine protein, maternal temperature, and lochia colour","Cervical dilatation, cervical effacement, fetal station, cervical consistency, and cervical position"],"a":3,"e":"The Modified Bishop Score assesses five pelvic exam parameters: cervical dilatation (cm), cervical effacement (%/length), station of the presenting part (-3 to +2), cervical consistency (firm/medium/soft), and cervical position (posterior/mid/anterior). A score \u003e= 8 (or \u003e 6) indicates a favourable (\"ripe\") cervix likely to respond well to induction, whereas \u003c= 5 warrants cervical ripening (e.g., dinoprostone/misoprostol/Foley balloon).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02500","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Brachial Plexus \u0026 Peripheral Nerves","q":"A 34-year-old carpenter presents with numbness over the medial one-and-a-half fingers and dorsal medial hand following a displaced supracondylar fracture of the humerus involving the medial epicondyle. On examination, clawing of the 4th and 5th digits is LESS pronounced than expected. Which anatomical explanation accounts for this \"ulnar paradox\"?","o":["Proximal ulnar nerve injury denervates flexor digitorum profundus (medial half), reducing flexion at the distal interphalangeal joints","Distal ulnar nerve injury at Guyon canal spares the dorsal cutaneous branch but paralyses flexor carpi ulnaris","Sparing of the palmaris brevis prevents flexion deformity of the metacarpophalangeal joints","Collateral sprouting from the anterior interosseous nerve immediately compensates for lumbrical paralysis"],"a":0,"e":"In a proximal ulnar nerve lesion at the elbow, both the intrinsic hand muscles (3rd and 4th lumbricals, interossei) and the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus (FDP to ring and little fingers) are paralysed. Loss of FDP pull at the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints makes the clawing less severe than in a distal wrist lesion where FDP remains intact and unopposed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02501","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Inguinal Canal \u0026 Hernia","q":"Which anatomical structure forms the lateral boundary of Hesselbach (inguinal) triangle, distinguishing an indirect from a direct inguinal hernia?","o":["Lateral border of rectus abdominis","Inferior epigastric artery","Conjoint tendon (falx inguinalis)","Inguinal ligament"],"a":1,"e":"Hesselbach triangle is bounded laterally by the inferior epigastric vessels, medially by the lateral border of the rectus abdominis muscle, and inferiorly by the inguinal ligament. Indirect inguinal hernias emerge lateral to the inferior epigastric artery through the deep inguinal ring, whereas direct hernias protrude medial to it.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02502","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Cranial Nerves \u0026 Cavernous Sinus","q":"Select ALL structures that lie within the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus (rather than passing through its lumen alongside the internal carotid artery):","o":["Facial nerve (CN VII); Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Abducens nerve (CN VI); Internal carotid artery","Oculomotor nerve (CN III); Trochlear nerve (CN IV); Ophthalmic nerve (CN V1); Maxillary nerve (CN V2)","Mandibular nerve (CN V3); Optic nerve (CN II)"],"a":2,"e":"Embedded in the dural lateral wall of the cavernous sinus from superior to inferior are CN III (oculomotor), CN IV (trochlear), CN V1 (ophthalmic), and CN V2 (maxillary). CN VI (abducens) and the internal carotid artery with its sympathetic plexus traverse the lumen of the sinus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02503","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Cardiac Cycle \u0026 Pressure-Volume Loop","q":"A 58-year-old patient undergoing echocardiography has a left ventricular end-diastolic volume (EDV) of 140 mL and an end-systolic volume (ESV) of 84 mL. What are the stroke volume (SV) and ejection fraction (EF) respectively?","o":["SV = 224 mL; EF = 66%","SV = 56 mL; EF = 60%","SV = 84 mL; EF = 40%","SV = 56 mL; EF = 40%"],"a":3,"e":"Stroke Volume (SV) = EDV - ESV = 140 - 84 = 56 mL. Ejection Fraction (EF) = (SV / EDV) x 100 = (56 / 140) x 100 = 40%. Normal resting EF is typically 55-70%; 40% represents reduced systolic function.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02504","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Renal Clearance \u0026 GFR","q":"Assertion (A): Moderate constriction of the efferent arteriole by angiotensin II helps preserve glomerular filtration rate (GFR) during mild-to-moderate hypotension. Reason (R): Efferent arteriolar constriction increases glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure while decreasing renal plasma flow.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Moderate efferent arteriolar constriction raises resistance downstream of the glomerular capillaries, elevating glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure (PGC) and buffering GFR against reduced renal perfusion pressure, even though total renal blood/plasma flow declines (increasing filtration fraction). Note that extreme efferent constriction can eventually drop GFR by raising colloid osmotic pressure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02505","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Inborn Errors of Metabolism","q":"A 22-year-old male develops acute dark-coloured urine, pallor, and jaundice 48 hours after starting primaquine prophylaxis. Peripheral smear shows bite cells and supravital staining reveals Heinz bodies. Deficiency of which HMP shunt enzyme, and consequently failure to regenerate which cofactor, underlies this haemolysis?","o":["Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD); NADPH needed to maintain reduced glutathione","6-phosphogluconate dehydrogenase; NADH needed for methaemoglobin reductase","Pyruvate kinase; ATP needed for sodium-potassium pump","Transketolase; Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) needed for ribose synthesis"],"a":0,"e":"G6PD catalyses the first, rate-limiting step of the pentose phosphate (HMP) pathway, generating NADPH. In red blood cells (which lack mitochondria), NADPH is required by glutathione reductase to regenerate reduced glutathione (GSH), which detoxifies H2O2 generated by oxidant drugs such as primaquine, dapsone, and sulfonamides. Without GSH, haemoglobin precipitates as Heinz bodies and splenic macrophages pluck them out forming bite cells.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02506","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Apoptosis vs Necrosis","q":"During the intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway of apoptosis, release of cytochrome c into the cytosol activates which initiator caspase via the apoptosome?","o":["Caspase-3","Caspase-9","Caspase-8","Caspase-1"],"a":1,"e":"In the intrinsic pathway (triggered by DNA damage, growth factor withdrawal, or BH3-only sensor activation overcoming BCL2/BCL-XL), mitochondrial outer membrane permeabilisation via BAX/BAK releases cytochrome c. Cytosolic cytochrome c binds Apaf-1 to form the apoptosome, which activates initiator Caspase-9, subsequently cleaving executioner Caspase-3 and Caspase-7.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02507","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics","q":"A clinician wishes to rapidly achieve a target plasma concentration (Cp) of 10 mg/L for an intravenous antiarrhythmic drug with an apparent volume of distribution (Vd) of 50 L and bioavailability (F) of 1.0 (IV). What loading dose should be administered?","o":["50 mg","200 mg","500 mg","5,000 mg"],"a":2,"e":"Loading Dose (LD) = (Target Cp x Vd) / F = (10 mg/L x 50 L) / 1.0 = 500 mg. Clearance determines the maintenance dose rate, whereas volume of distribution determines the loading dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02508","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Biomedical Waste Management (India)","q":"Under India Biomedical Waste Management Rules (2016 \u0026 Amendments), match the waste item to its correct colour-coded container: 1) Soiled cotton swabs/dressings, 2) Intravenous tubing \u0026 vacutainers (without needle), 3) Scalpels/needles, 4) Discarded glass vials/ampoules.","o":["1-Black, 2-Red, 3-White, 4-Yellow","1-Red, 2-Yellow, 3-Blue, 4-White","1-Yellow, 2-Blue, 3-Red, 4-White","1-Yellow, 2-Red, 3-White (puncture-proof), 4-Blue (cardboard box with blue marking)"],"a":3,"e":"Under Indian BMW Management Rules 2016: Yellow = human anatomical, soiled infectious waste, expired medicines, microbiology waste; Red = contaminated recyclable plastics (IV sets, tubing, catheters, vacutainers, syringes without needles); White translucent puncture-proof = metallic sharps (needles, scalpels, blades); Blue = glassware (broken/unbroken vials, ampoules, slides) and metallic body implants.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02509","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine (PSM)","topic":"Screening Tests","q":"A new rapid antigen test has a sensitivity of 90% and specificity of 95%. If this test is applied sequentially in two populations—Population A (prevalence 20%) and Population B (prevalence 2%)—how does the Positive Predictive Value (PPV) change?","o":["PPV is substantially higher in Population A than in Population B, while sensitivity and specificity remain unchanged","PPV is higher in Population B because specificity has a greater effect at low prevalence","Sensitivity increases in Population A while specificity decreases","Both PPV and NPV remain identical across both populations"],"a":0,"e":"Sensitivity and specificity are intrinsic properties of a test (independent of disease prevalence). Positive Predictive Value (PPV) depends strongly on prevalence: as prevalence increases from 2% to 20%, the proportion of true positives among all positive test results rises sharply (fewer false positives relative to true positives). Conversely, Negative Predictive Value (NPV) rises as prevalence falls.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02510","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine (incl. Psychiatry \u0026 Dermatology)","topic":"Snakebite Envenomation Management (India)","q":"A 28-year-old farmer from rural Telangana arrives 3 hours after a bite on the ankle while sleeping on the floor. He has bilateral ptosis, ophthalmoplegia, pooling of secretions, and weak neck flexors, with minimal local swelling and a normal 20-minute whole blood clotting test (20WBCT). What is the most appropriate immediate pharmacological management alongside airway support?","o":["Administer polyvalent Anti-Snake Venom (ASV, 10 vials initial IV infusion) plus atropine followed by neostigmine trial, while avoiding local tourniquet/incision","Administer IV calcium gluconate and await 24-hour serial D-dimer before starting ASV","Give 2 vials of ASV subcutaneously around the bite site and apply a tight arterial tourniquet","Withhold ASV because 20WBCT is normal and administer tetanus toxoid alone"],"a":0,"e":"Bilateral ptosis, ophthalmoplegia, and bulbar/neck weakness with minimal local swelling and normal 20WBCT are classic for neurotoxic elapid envenomation (e.g., common krait/cobra). Per NHSRC/MoHFW Standard Treatment Guidelines for Snake Bite, systemic neurotoxicity is an absolute indication for immediate IV polyvalent ASV (initial dose 10 vials in India) even when 20WBCT is normal; an atropine + neostigmine trial reverses postsynaptic nicotinic blockade (cobra) while preparing for assisted ventilation if krait (presynaptic) paralysis progresses.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02511","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery (incl. Orthopaedics \u0026 Anaesthesia)","topic":"ATLS Primary Survey \u0026 Tension Pneumothorax","q":"A 26-year-old motorcyclist arrives in the emergency department after blunt chest trauma with BP 78/48 mmHg, HR 136/min, RR 34/min, SpO2 82%, distended neck veins, absent breath sounds and hyperresonance over the right hemithorax, and tracheal deviation to the left. What is the immediate next step in management?","o":["Administer 2 L hypertonic saline bolus and schedule delayed elective thoracoscopy","Immediate needle/finger decompression of the right pleural space followed by tube thoracostomy without waiting for a chest radiograph","Obtain an urgent upright chest X-ray and CT thorax to confirm pneumothorax size before intervention","Perform immediate pericardiocentesis via the left xiphocostal angle"],"a":1,"e":"Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis (hypotension/obstructive shock, JVD, absent breath sounds, hyperresonance, contralateral tracheal shift) and an immediately life-threatening cause of death during the Breathing (B) step of the primary survey. Decompression (needle thoracostomy or finger thoracostomy followed by intercostal chest drain) must be performed immediately before imaging.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02512","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics \u0026 Gynaecology","topic":"Postpartum Haemorrhage \u0026 Eclampsia","q":"A 24-year-old primigravida at 36 weeks gestation with BP 170/110 mmHg and 3+ proteinuria develops a generalised tonic-clonic seizure. What is the standard loading dose of Magnesium Sulphate under the Pritchard regimen, and which clinical signs must be monitored prior to each maintenance dose?","o":["2 g IM single dose; monitor blood glucose and platelet count prior to each dose","4 g IV loading followed by diazepam infusion as standard first-line maintenance","4 g IV slowly over 3-5 min + 10 g IM (5 g in each buttock); monitor patellar reflex, respiratory rate (\u003e=16/min), and urine output (\u003e=30 mL/hr)","10 g IV bolus over 1 min; monitor pupillary light reflex and serum sodium only"],"a":2,"e":"Under the Pritchard regimen for eclampsia (standard across India and DC Dutta Obstetrics), the loading dose of MgSO4 is 4 g IV (20% solution over 3-5 min) plus 10 g deep IM (5 g of 50% solution in each buttock), followed by 5 g IM every 4 hours in alternate buttocks. Before every maintenance dose, verify: (1) patellar tendon reflexes present, (2) respiratory rate \u003e= 16/min, and (3) urine output \u003e= 30 mL/hr (or \u003e= 100 mL over 4 hours). Calcium gluconate (10 mL of 10% IV) is the antidote for toxicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02513","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM)","q":"An 18-month-old child with Severe Acute Malnutrition (weight-for-height \u003c-3 SD, bilateral pitting oedema, and poor appetite) is admitted for stabilization. Why should intravenous fluid boluses and high-sodium ORS be avoided unless frank hypovolaemic shock is present, and which starter feed is initiated?","o":["SAM children are hyperkalaemic and hypernatraemic; initiate high-calorie F-100 and full-strength normal saline bolus immediately","SAM children lack pancreatic amylase only; give pure dextrose infusion with routine iron supplementation on Day 1","F-75 is reserved only for infants under 2 months; 18-month-old children require immediate iron-fortified F-100 on Day 1","Total body sodium is already elevated with risk of fluid-overload heart failure; initiate low-protein/low-sodium F-75 starter formula and ReSoMal for oral rehydration"],"a":3,"e":"In SAM, reductive adaptation impairs the Na+/K+-ATPase pump, causing intracellular sodium retention, low total body potassium/magnesium, and reduced cardiac reserve. Standard ORS or unnecessary IV boluses precipitate fatal pulmonary oedema and heart failure. Initial stabilization (Phase 1) uses F-75 (75 kcal/100 mL, 0.9 g protein/100 mL), ReSoMal (low Na+, high K+), and withholds iron until the rehabilitation phase (F-100) once infections settle.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02514","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Brachial Plexus","q":"A 26-year-old construction worker grabs an overhead scaffolding bar with his right hand while falling, causing forced hyperabduction of the right arm. Examination reveals wasting of the hypothenar eminence and interossei, clawing of the medial two digits, sensory loss along the ulnar border of the forearm and hand, and right-sided ptosis with miosis. Which root(s)/trunk of the brachial plexus are injured?","o":["Lower trunk (C8-T1)","Posterior cord (C5-T1)","Upper trunk (C5-C6)","Middle trunk (C7)"],"a":0,"e":"Forced hyperabduction of the arm stretches the lower trunk (C8-T1) of the brachial plexus, producing Klumpke palsy. Intrinsic muscles of the hand (supplied via ulnar and median T1 fibers) are paralyzed, resulting in a claw hand deformity due to unopposed action of long flexors and extensors. Sensory loss occurs along the C8-T1 dermatomes (medial forearm and hand). Concurrent traction on T1 preganglionic sympathetic fibers exiting to the superior cervical ganglion produces ipsilateral Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02515","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Circle of Willis","q":"A patient develops sudden painful diplopia, right-sided ptosis, a \u0027down and out\u0027 right eye position, and a dilated non-reactive right pupil. An aneurysm of which component of the Circle of Willis most characteristically compresses the affected cranial nerve?","o":["Anterior cerebral artery","Posterior communicating artery","Posterior inferior cerebellar artery","Anterior communicating artery"],"a":1,"e":"The oculomotor nerve (CN III) courses anteriorly between the posterior cerebral artery and superior cerebellar artery, passing directly adjacent to the posterior communicating artery (PComA) as it travels toward the cavernous sinus. A saccular aneurysm at the junction of the internal carotid artery and PComA compresses the superficial parasympathetic fibers of CN III first, causing pupillary dilation (mydriasis) along with ptosis and a \u0027down and out\u0027 gaze due to unopposed lateral rectus (CN VI) and superior oblique (CN IV) action.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02516","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Inguinal Canal","q":"During laparoscopic repair of a right groin hernia, the surgeon identifies the hernial sac protruding directly through the posterior wall of the inguinal canal medial to the inferior epigastric vessels and lateral to the rectus abdominis muscle. Which structure forms the inferior boundary of this anatomical triangle, and what type of hernia is present?","o":["Pectineal ligament; femoral hernia","Lacunar ligament; direct inguinal hernia","Inguinal ligament; direct inguinal hernia","Conjoint tendon; indirect inguinal hernia"],"a":2,"e":"Hesselbach (inguinal) triangle is bounded laterally by the inferior epigastric vessels, medially by the lateral border of the rectus abdominis (linea semilunaris), and inferiorly by the inguinal ligament (Poupart ligament). A direct inguinal hernia protrudes through a weakened transversalis fascia within Hesselbach triangle, lying medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, whereas an indirect inguinal hernia enters the deep inguinal ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02517","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Pelvic Floor","q":"Which component of the levator ani muscle forms a U-shaped muscular sling around the anorectal junction, maintaining the anorectal angle crucial for fecal continence?","o":["Superficial transverse perineal muscle","Ischiococcygeus (coccygeus)","Iliococcygeus","Puborectalis"],"a":3,"e":"The puborectalis muscle arises from the posterior aspect of the pubic body, passes posteriorly alongside the urethra/vagina and rectum, and loops behind the anorectal junction to join its contralateral partner. Tonic contraction of this U-shaped sling pulls the anorectal junction anteriorly, creating the approximately 90-degree anorectal angle that is essential for fecal continence; relaxation during defecation straightens this angle.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02518","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Pharyngeal Arches and Pouches","q":"Assertion (A): Failure of development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches results in congenital thymic aplasia and hypocalcemic tetany (DiGeorge syndrome). Reason (R): The third pharyngeal pouch gives rise to the thymus and inferior parathyroid glands, whereas the fourth pharyngeal pouch gives rise to the superior parathyroid glands.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"In DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion), defective neural crest migration into the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches impairs organogenesis of the thymus and parathyroid glands. The ventral wing of the third pharyngeal pouch forms the thymus and its dorsal wing forms the inferior parathyroid glands (which descend further along with the thymus). The dorsal wing of the fourth pharyngeal pouch forms the superior parathyroid glands. Absence of parathyroid hormone leads to neonatal hypocalcemic tetany, and thymic aplasia causes severe T-cell immunodeficiency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02519","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Lymphoid Organs","q":"During an OSPE histology station, a student examines an H\u0026E-stained section of an encapsulated, lobulated lymphoid organ lacking lymphoid follicles and germinal centers, and identifies concentric whorls of eosinophilic epithelial reticular cells in the paler central zone of each lobule. What is the identity of these concentric structures and the organ shown?","o":["Hassall corpuscles in the medulla of the thymus","Subcapsular sinuses in the cortex of a lymph node","MALT lymphoid nodules in the lamina propria of the palatine tonsil","Periarteriolar lymphoid sheaths in the white pulp of the spleen"],"a":0,"e":"The thymus is a primary lymphoid organ divided into incomplete lobules by connective tissue septa, each containing a darker peripheral cortex packed with immature thymocytes and a paler central medulla. Unlike lymph nodes and spleen, the thymus lacks B-cell lymphoid follicles and germinal centers. Its pathognomonic histological hallmark in the medulla is the presence of Hassall (thymic) corpuscles—concentric, acidophilic whorls of type VI epithelioreticular cells involved in thymic stromal lymphopoietin production and regulatory T-cell maturation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02520","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Cavernous Sinus","q":"A 42-year-old diabetic man with a squeezed furuncle on the upper lip develops high fever, periorbital edema, chemosis, and an isolated inability to abduct his right eye before other cranial nerve deficits appear. Why is this specific ocular motor nerve affected earliest in cavernous sinus thrombosis?","o":["The ophthalmic nerve (V1) supplies motor fibers directly to the lateral rectus muscle inside the sinus","The abducens nerve (CN VI) runs freely within the lumen of the cavernous sinus inferolateral to the internal carotid artery","The oculomotor nerve (CN III) lacks a dural sheath inside the lateral wall of the sinus","The trochlear nerve (CN IV) lies medial to the internal carotid artery inside the venous blood"],"a":1,"e":"Infection from the \u0027danger area of the face\u0027 (upper lip and nose) spreads via the valveless facial and superior ophthalmic veins into the cavernous sinus. Unlike cranial nerves III, IV, V1, and V2—which are embedded within the protective dural fibrous lateral wall of the cavernous sinus—the abducens nerve (CN VI) traverses directly through the central venous lumen inferolateral to the internal carotid artery, separated from venous blood only by endothelium. Consequently, CN VI is the most vulnerable and typically the earliest cranial nerve paralyzed in cavernous sinus thrombosis or internal carotid aneurysm.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02521","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Coronary Circulation","q":"A coronary angiogram performed prior to cardiac surgery shows the posterior interventricular (posterior descending) artery arising from the left circumflex artery, while the right coronary artery terminates before reaching the crux of the heart. Which statement accurately describes this patient\u0027s coronary anatomy?","o":["The patient has left coronary dominance, meaning the SA node is invariably supplied by the anterior interventricular artery","The patient has codominance, and the anterior interventricular artery arises from the right coronary artery","The patient has left coronary dominance, seen in approximately 10-15% of the population, and the AV node is likely supplied by the left circumflex artery","The patient has right coronary dominance, seen in approximately 85% of the population"],"a":2,"e":"Coronary dominance is defined by which coronary artery gives off the posterior interventricular artery (posterior descending artery, PDA) at the crux of the heart. In roughly 80-85% of individuals, the PDA arises from the right coronary artery (right dominance). In approximately 10-15% of individuals, the PDA originates from the left circumflex artery (left dominance), and in ~5% both contribute (codominance). Because the AV nodal artery usually arises from whichever artery supplies the crux, the AV node is supplied by the left circumflex artery in left-dominant hearts.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02522","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Frank-Starling Law of the Heart","q":"According to the Frank-Starling mechanism, an acute increase in venous return elevates stroke volume primarily by which cellular mechanism?","o":["Increasing heart rate via the Bainbridge reflex, shortening ventricular diastole","Decreasing total peripheral resistance, thereby lowering ventricular afterload at a constant preload","Increasing sympathetic norepinephrine release, which directly prolongs phase 2 of the ventricular action potential","Increasing end-diastolic ventricular volume, which optimizes actin-myosin overlap and increases troponin-C sensitivity to calcium"],"a":3,"e":"The Frank-Starling law states that within physiological limits, the heart pumps whatever volume of blood returns to it without needing extrinsic neural input. Greater venous return increases left ventricular end-diastolic volume (preload), stretching cardiac myocytes toward optimal sarcomere length (~2.2 micrometers). This optimizes actin-myosin cross-bridge overlap and increases myofilament Ca2+ sensitivity of troponin C (length-dependent activation), generating stronger systolic force and higher stroke volume.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02523","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Oxygen-Hemoglobin Dissociation Curve","q":"During strenuous skeletal muscle exercise, metabolically active tissues extract more oxygen from capillary blood. Which combination of local changes shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the right, increasing P50 and facilitating oxygen unloading?","o":["Increased PCO2, decreased pH, increased temperature, and increased 2,3-BPG","Increased fetal hemoglobin (HbF) proportion and carbon monoxide binding","Decreased hydrogen ion concentration and respiratory alkalosis","Decreased PCO2, increased pH, decreased temperature, and decreased 2,3-BPG"],"a":0,"e":"Active skeletal muscle produces CO2, lactic acid (lowering pH), and heat, while red blood cells increase 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG) during sustained hypoxia/exercise. All four factors stabilize the tense (T) deoxyhemoglobin state, shifting the sigmoidal O2-Hb dissociation curve to the right (increasing P50 from ~27 mmHg). Lower hemoglobin affinity for O2 at a given PO2 enhances O2 unloading in peripheral tissues (Bohr effect).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02524","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Glomerular Filtration Rate","q":"In a healthy adult, glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure (P_GC) is 60 mmHg, Bowman\u0027s capsule hydrostatic pressure (P_BC) is 18 mmHg, glomerular capillary colloid osmotic pressure (pi_GC) is 32 mmHg, and Bowman\u0027s capsule colloid osmotic pressure (pi_BC) is 0 mmHg. If the glomerular ultrafiltration coefficient (K_f) is 12.5 mL/min/mmHg, what are the Net Filtration Pressure (NFP) and the resulting GFR?","o":["NFP = 28 mmHg; GFR = 350 mL/min","NFP = 10 mmHg; GFR = 125 mL/min","NFP = 42 mmHg; GFR = 525 mL/min","NFP = 14 mmHg; GFR = 175 mL/min"],"a":1,"e":"Net Filtration Pressure across the glomerular filtration barrier is given by Starling\u0027s equation: NFP = (P_GC - P_BC) - (pi_GC - pi_BC) = (60 - 18) - (32 - 0) = 42 - 32 = 10 mmHg. Multiplying by the filtration coefficient K_f yields GFR = K_f x NFP = 12.5 mL/min/mmHg x 10 mmHg = 125 mL/min (equivalent to 180 L/day), matching normal physiological human GFR.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02525","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Electrocardiogram (ECG)","q":"During a physiology practical, a student records a lead II ECG at the standard paper speed of 25 mm/s and observes that every P wave is followed by a normal narrow QRS complex, but the PR interval consistently measures 7 small squares (7 mm). What is the duration of this PR interval, and what physiological conduction delay does it indicate?","o":["0.28 seconds (280 ms); complete (third-degree) AV block","0.14 seconds (140 ms); normal AV nodal conduction","0.28 seconds (280 ms); first-degree atrioventricular (AV) block","0.35 seconds (350 ms); bundle branch block"],"a":2,"e":"At a standard ECG paper speed of 25 mm/s, 1 small square (1 mm) equals 0.04 seconds (40 ms), and 1 large square (5 mm) equals 0.20 seconds (200 ms). A PR interval spanning 7 small squares measures 7 x 0.04 s = 0.28 seconds (280 ms). Because the normal PR interval is 0.12-0.20 seconds (3-5 small squares) and every P wave is consistently conducted to a QRS complex at a constant prolonged PR interval, this represents first-degree AV nodal conduction delay (block).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02526","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Visual Pathway Lesions","q":"A 38-year-old woman presents with progressive headaches, galactorrhea, and bumping into doorways on both sides. Visual perimetry demonstrates loss of vision in both temporal visual fields while both nasal visual fields are preserved. Which fibers are compressed and at what anatomical site?","o":["Left optic nerve anterior to the optic chiasm","Meyer loop (inferior optic radiation) fibers in the left temporal lobe","Uncrossed temporal retinal fibers in the right optic tract","Decussating nasal retinal fibers at the optic chiasm"],"a":3,"e":"Nasal retinal ganglion cell fibers (which receive light from the temporal visual fields) decussate at the optic chiasm, whereas temporal retinal fibers (receiving nasal visual fields) remain ipsilateral. A pituitary macroadenoma (such as a prolactinoma causing galactorrhea) expanding superiorly from the sella turcica compresses the central optic chiasm, selectively damaging decussating nasal fibers from both eyes and causing bitemporal hemianopia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02527","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF)","q":"Where is cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) primarily absorbed back into the systemic venous circulation, and how does normal CSF glucose compare with simultaneous plasma glucose?","o":["Arachnoid granulations into the superior sagittal sinus; CSF glucose is approximately 60% of plasma glucose","Foramen of Magendie into internal jugular lymphatics; CSF glucose equals zero","Ependymal lining of the central canal; CSF protein exceeds plasma protein","Choroid plexus capillaries; CSF glucose is twice plasma glucose"],"a":0,"e":"CSF is secreted mainly by the choroid plexus of the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles (~500 mL/day; total volume ~150 mL), circulates via the foramina of Luschka and Magendie into the subarachnoid space, and is reabsorbed through one-way valve-like arachnoid granulations (villi) into the dural venous sinuses, chiefly the superior sagittal sinus. Normal CSF glucose is roughly 50-70% (~60%) of simultaneous serum glucose (45-80 mg/dL), and normal CSF protein is very low (15-45 mg/dL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02528","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Spermatogenesis","q":"Assertion (A): Exogenous high-dose anabolic-androgenic steroid abuse suppresses spermatogenesis and causes testicular atrophy. Reason (R): High circulating androgens inhibit hypothalamic GnRH and pituitary LH/FSH secretion via negative feedback, lowering intratesticular testosterone and FSH stimulation of Sertoli cells.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Normal spermatogenesis requires very high local intratesticular testosterone (produced by Leydig cells under LH stimulation and concentrated in the seminiferous tubules by Sertoli cell-derived androgen-binding protein) together with FSH action on Sertoli cells. Exogenous anabolic steroids exert strong negative feedback on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary, suppressing LH and FSH release. Consequently, endogenous Leydig cell testosterone synthesis plummets, intratesticular testosterone falls below the threshold needed for spermatogenesis, and seminiferous tubules atrophy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02529","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) Cycle","q":"Which enzyme catalyzes the primary rate-limiting, irreversibly regulated step of the TCA cycle, and how is it allosterically regulated by cellular energy status?","o":["Citrate synthase; activated by NADH and succinyl-CoA, inhibited by ADP","Isocitrate dehydrogenase; activated by ADP and Ca2+, inhibited by ATP and NADH","Malate dehydrogenase; activated by ATP and acetyl-CoA, inhibited by Ca2+","Succinate dehydrogenase; activated by malonate and ATP, inhibited by FAD"],"a":1,"e":"Isocitrate dehydrogenase (converting isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate with generation of NADH and CO2) is the principal rate-limiting enzyme of the citric acid cycle. High cellular energy signals (ATP and NADH) allosterically inhibit it, whereas low-energy/high-demand signals (ADP and Ca2+ during muscle contraction) allosterically activate it to accelerate oxidative phosphorylation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02530","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Urea Cycle Disorders","q":"A 3-day-old male neonate develops lethargy, poor feeding, tachypnea with respiratory alkalosis, and seizures after starting breast milk. Laboratory testing reveals marked hyperammonemia (480 micromol/L), low plasma citrulline, elevated plasma glutamine, and markedly increased urinary orotic acid without megaloblastic anemia. Which enzyme deficiency is most likely?","o":["Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I (CPS-I)","Arginase","Ornithine transcarbamylase (OTC)","Argininosuccinate synthetase"],"a":2,"e":"Ornithine transcarbamylase (OTC) deficiency is the most common urea cycle disorder and is inherited in an X-linked recessive manner. Defective conversion of carbamoyl phosphate and ornithine to citrulline causes mitochondrial carbamoyl phosphate to spill into the cytosol, where it enters the pyrimidine synthesis pathway, producing excess orotic aciduria alongside severe hyperammonemia and low citrulline. Unlike hereditary orotic aciduria (UMPS deficiency), OTC deficiency presents with hyperammonemia and lacks megaloblastic anemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02531","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Ketogenesis","q":"During prolonged fasting or diabetic ketoacidosis, the liver synthesizes large quantities of acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate but cannot oxidize them for its own ATP production. Absence of which enzyme in hepatocytes accounts for this metabolic division of labor?","o":["Beta-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase","HMG-CoA lyase","Mitochondrial HMG-CoA synthase","Succinyl-CoA:acetoacetate CoA transferase (thiophorase)"],"a":3,"e":"Ketogenesis occurs exclusively in hepatic mitochondria via mitochondrial HMG-CoA synthase (the rate-limiting enzyme) and HMG-CoA lyase. However, hepatocytes lack succinyl-CoA:acetoacetate CoA transferase (thiophorase), which is required to activate acetoacetate to acetoacetyl-CoA for entry into the TCA cycle. Consequently, ketone bodies exported from the liver are selectively spared for extrahepatic tissues such as brain, heart, and skeletal muscle.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02532","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Water-Soluble Vitamin Deficiencies","q":"A 48-year-old man with chronic alcohol use disorder is brought to the emergency department with confusion and dehydration. Shortly after receiving an intravenous 5% dextrose infusion without vitamin supplementation, he develops horizontal nystagmus, bilateral sixth nerve palsy, and truncal ataxia. Decreased activity of which erythrocyte enzyme confirms the underlying vitamin deficiency?","o":["Erythrocyte transketolase","Erythrocyte glutathione reductase","Erythrocyte aminotransferase (AST/ALT)","Erythrocyte methylmalonyl-CoA mutase"],"a":0,"e":"Administering glucose before thiamine (vitamin B1) in a malnourished alcoholic patient depletes residual thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) reserves via glycolysis and pyruvate dehydrogenase, precipitating acute Wernicke encephalopathy (triad of encephalopathy, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia). TPP is an essential cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase, and transketolase (pentose phosphate pathway). Reduced erythrocyte transketolase activity (and marked increase upon in vitro TPP addition) is the classic biochemical test for thiamine deficiency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02533","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) and ELISA","q":"A diagnostic laboratory performs conventional PCR starting with 100 copies of a target viral double-stranded DNA sequence. Assuming 100% amplification efficiency per cycle, how many copies of the target DNA amplicon will be present after 10 thermal cycles?","o":["2,000 copies","102,400 copies","1,024,000 copies","10,240 copies"],"a":1,"e":"Each PCR cycle consists of denaturation (~94-95°C), primer annealing (~50-65°C), and extension (~72°C by thermostable Taq DNA polymerase), doubling the number of target DNA copies at 100% efficiency. Therefore, final copy number N = N0 x 2^n. With N0 = 100 and n = 10 cycles, 2^10 = 1,024, so N = 100 x 1,024 = 102,400 copies.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02534","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Collagen Synthesis and Cross-Linking","q":"Assertion (A): Menkes kinky-hair disease and dietary copper deficiency impair extracellular covalent cross-linking of collagen and elastin fibrils, predisposing to arterial aneurysms. Reason (R): Lysyl oxidase is an extracellular copper-dependent enzyme that oxidatively deaminates lysine and hydroxylysine residues to form reactive aldehydes for cross-linking.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"After tropocollagen is secreted into the extracellular space and cleaved by procollagen peptidases, lysyl oxidase catalyzes oxidative deamination of peptidyl lysine and hydroxylysine epsilon-amino groups to allysine and hydroxyallysine, which spontaneously condense to form covalent aldol and Schiff-base cross-links conferring tensile strength. Because lysyl oxidase requires Cu2+ as an essential cofactor, defective copper transport in Menkes disease (ATP7A mutation) weakens vascular collagen and elastin. By contrast, prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases act intracellularly and require vitamin C and Fe2+.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02535","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Bilirubin Metabolism","q":"An asymptomatic 22-year-old medical student is found to have mild fluctuating jaundice with total serum bilirubin 4.2 mg/dL, direct (conjugated) bilirubin 3.1 mg/dL, normal serum alkaline phosphatase and transaminases, and a grossly black-pigmented liver on biopsy due to lysosomal pigment deposition. Mutation of which transporter causes this condition?","o":["Complete absence of UGT1A1 (Crigler-Najjar syndrome type I)","OATP1B1/1B3 sinusoidal organic anion uptake transporters (Rotor syndrome)","MRP2 (ABCC2) canalicular multispecific organic anion transporter (Dubin-Johnson syndrome)","UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1A1 (UGT1A1) promoter (Gilbert syndrome)"],"a":2,"e":"Dubin-Johnson syndrome is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in ABCC2 (encoding MRP2), impairing ATP-dependent biliary excretion of conjugated bilirubin glucuronides across the hepatocyte canalicular membrane. Patients present with benign, predominantly conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, normal liver enzymes, and characteristic dark melanin-like lipofuscin pigment granules in centrilobular hepatocyte lysosomes producing a \u0027black liver\u0027.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02536","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Thrombosis and Virchow Triad","q":"Which triad of primary pathogenic factors constitutes Virchow triad predisposing to intravascular thrombosis, and which inherited defect is the most common cause of familial thrombophilia?","o":["Platelet depletion, fibrinolysis activation, and vasodilation; Protein C deficiency","Vasospasm, hyperbilirubinemia, and hypovolemia; Antithrombin III deficiency","Endothelial hyperplasia, leukocytosis, and anemia; Prothrombin G20210A mutation","Endothelial injury, abnormal blood flow (stasis/turbulence), and hypercoagulability; Factor V Leiden mutation"],"a":3,"e":"Virchow triad comprises (1) endothelial injury/dysfunction, (2) stasis or turbulent blood flow, and (3) hypercoagulability. Factor V Leiden—a single point mutation (Arg506Gln) rendering Factor Va resistant to cleavage and inactivation by activated Protein C—is the most frequent inherited thrombophilia in clinical practice, significantly increasing deep vein thrombosis risk.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02537","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)","q":"A 24-year-old woman presents with malar rash, oral ulcers, symmetric polyarthralgia, proteinuria (2.4 g/day), and RBC casts on urinalysis. Serology is positive for antinuclear antibodies (ANA). Which autoantibody is most specific for SLE, and which autoantibody titer best correlates with lupus nephritis disease activity?","o":["Anti-Smith (anti-Sm) is most specific; anti-double-stranded DNA (anti-dsDNA) correlates with lupus nephritis and disease flares","Anti-Ro (SS-A) is most specific; anti-U1 RNP correlates with lupus nephritis","Anti-centromere is most specific; anti-topoisomerase I correlates with lupus nephritis","Antinuclear antibody (ANA) is most specific; anti-histone correlates with lupus nephritis"],"a":0,"e":"In SLE, ANA is the most sensitive screening test (~95-99%) but lacks specificity. Anti-Smith (anti-Sm, directed against snRNP core proteins) has the highest specificity (\u003e95%) for SLE, though lower sensitivity (~20-30%). Anti-dsDNA is both highly specific for SLE and fluctuates with disease activity, immune-complex deposition, complement consumption, and active lupus nephritis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02538","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Hodgkin vs Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma","q":"A cervical lymph node biopsy from a 25-year-old woman with a mediastinal mass shows broad collagen bands dividing the node into nodules containing large binucleated cells with clear perinuclear retraction halos (\u0027lacunar\u0027 variants of Reed-Sternberg cells) in a polymorphous reactive background. Which immunohistochemical profile confirms classical Hodgkin lymphoma?","o":["CD20 strongly positive, CD45 positive, BCL2 positive, CD15 negative","CD15 positive, CD30 positive, PAX5 weakly positive, CD45 negative","CD138 positive, CD56 positive, CD15 negative, CD30 negative","CD3 positive, TdT positive, CD30 negative, CD15 negative"],"a":1,"e":"The description matches nodular sclerosis classical Hodgkin lymphoma (the most common subtype of Hodgkin lymphoma, classically affecting young adult females with anterior mediastinal involvement). Diagnostic Reed-Sternberg (RS) cells—derived from crippled germinal center B cells—and their lacunar variants display a characteristic immunophenotype: CD15+ and CD30+, weak nuclear PAX5+, and negative for pan-leukocyte CD45 and mature B-cell CD20 (with rare variable CD20 expression).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02539","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)","q":"Which balanced chromosomal translocation produces the Philadelphia chromosome in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), and what is its molecular consequence?","o":["t(14;18)(q32;q21), causing overexpression of anti-apoptotic BCL2","t(15;17)(q24;q21), generating a PML-RARA fusion transcription factor","t(9;22)(q34;q11), generating a constitutively active BCR-ABL1 tyrosine kinase (p210)","t(8;14)(q24;q32), juxtaposing MYC to the IgH enhancer"],"a":2,"e":"The Philadelphia chromosome is the shortened chromosome 22 resulting from reciprocal translocation t(9;22)(q34;q11), fusing the BCR gene on chromosome 22q11 with the ABL1 tyrosine kinase gene on chromosome 9q34. In CML, this produces the p210 BCR-ABL1 fusion protein with constitutive tyrosine kinase activity that drives myeloid proliferation and resists apoptosis, making it directly targetable by imatinib.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02540","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Pneumoconioses","q":"A 55-year-old former shipyard insulation worker presents with progressive exertional dyspnea. Chest imaging reveals bilateral lower-lobe interstitial fibrosis and calcified diaphragmatic pleural plaques, and sputum/lung biopsy demonstrates golden-brown beaded dumbbell-shaped rods on Prussian blue stain. Which complication is this patient at greatest risk for, and how does this contrast with silicosis?","o":["Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis; silicosis is caused by amphibole fibers","Progressive massive fibrosis and tuberculosis; silicosis predominantly involves lower lobes with ferruginous bodies","Caplan syndrome with rheumatoid nodules; silicosis causes calcified diaphragmatic pleural plaques","Bronchogenic carcinoma and malignant mesothelioma; silicosis predominantly involves upper lobes with eggshell hilar calcification and predisposes to tuberculosis"],"a":3,"e":"Asbestosis classically produces lower-lobe interstitial fibrosis, benign fibrocalcific pleural plaques (especially on the parietal pleura and diaphragm), and ferruginous (asbestos) bodies—asbestos fibers coated with iron-rich proteinaceous material forming golden-brown beaded rods positive on Prussian blue stain. It markedly elevates risk of bronchogenic carcinoma (especially with smoking) and malignant mesothelioma. By contrast, silicosis (crystalline silica inhalation in stone cutters/sandblasters) shows upper-lobe collagenous silicotic nodules, eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes, and impaired macrophage killing that increases susceptibility to Mycobacterium tuberculosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02541","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Glomerular Diseases","q":"Two 8-year-old boys present with periorbital edema. Child A develops dark cola-colored urine, hypertension, oliguria, low serum C3, and dysmorphic RBC casts 2 weeks after streptococcal pharyngitis. Child B has normotension, massive proteinuria (4.5 g/day), hypoalbuminemia, hyperlipidemia, and normal light microscopy of a renal biopsy. Which electron microscopic findings distinguish Child A from Child B?","o":["Child A: subepithelial electron-dense \u0027humps\u0027; Child B: diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes without immune deposits","Child A: tram-track splitting of glomerular basement membrane; Child B: mesangial IgA deposits","Child A: diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes; Child B: subendothelial \u0027wire-loop\u0027 deposits","Child A: linear IgG along GBM; Child B: subepithelial spikes on silver stain"],"a":0,"e":"Child A has acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN), a classic nephritic syndrome featuring hematuria, RBC casts, hypertension, and complement consumption (low C3); electron microscopy shows discrete subepithelial electron-dense immune-complex \u0027humps\u0027 with hypercellular glomeruli on light microscopy. Child B has minimal change disease (lipoid nephrosis), the commonest cause of childhood nephrotic syndrome, showing normal glomeruli on light microscopy, negative immunofluorescence, and diffuse effacement (flattening) of podocyte foot processes on electron microscopy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02542","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Colorectal Carcinogenesis","q":"Assertion (A): Loss of both alleles of the APC tumor suppressor gene (5q21) is an early initiating event in the classic colorectal adenoma-carcinoma sequence. Reason (R): Loss of functional APC prevents cytosolic degradation of beta-catenin, allowing beta-catenin nuclear translocation and transcriptional activation of MYC and cyclin D1.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"In the classic chromosomal instability (adenoma-carcinoma) pathway of colorectal cancer (and familial adenomatous polyposis), biallelic inactivation of the APC gene on chromosome 5q21 initiates mucosal hyperproliferation and early adenoma formation. Normally, APC forms part of the destruction complex that phosphorylates beta-catenin for proteasomal degradation. Without APC, beta-catenin accumulates, enters the nucleus, binds TCF, and upregulates growth-promoting genes (MYC, CCND1/cyclin D1), followed later by KRAS activation and SMAD4/TP53 loss.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02543","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Tuberculosis","q":"On H\u0026E examination of a lymph node biopsy from a patient with cervical lymphadenopathy and evening rise of temperature, a student observes central acellular pink granular necrosis surrounded by slipper-shaped activated macrophages, multinucleated giant cells with nuclei arranged in a peripheral horseshoe pattern, and an outer collar of lymphocytes. Which cytokine pathway is essential for forming these activated macrophages and maintaining granuloma integrity?","o":["Neutrophil elastase and complement C5a","CD4+ Th1 cell-derived IFN-gamma and macrophage-derived TNF-alpha","B-cell-derived IgE and mast cell histamine","Th2 cell-derived IL-4 and IL-5 recruiting eosinophils"],"a":1,"e":"Tuberculous granulomas are prototypes of immune-mediated delayed-type (Type IV) hypersensitivity characterized by central caseous necrosis, epithelioid histiocytes (activated macrophages), Langhans-type multinucleated giant cells (peripheral horseshoe nuclei), and a lymphocytic/fibroblastic rim. Macrophage IL-12 drives CD4+ Th1 differentiation; Th1 cells secrete IFN-gamma to activate macrophages into epithelioid cells capable of killing mycobacteria, while TNF-alpha recruits monocytes and maintains granuloma architecture (which is why anti-TNF biologics can reactivate latent TB).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02544","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Adrenergic Receptors","q":"In an experimental pharmacology demonstration, intravenous administration of epinephrine normally causes a sharp rise in mean arterial pressure. However, after pre-treating the subject with phentolamine (or phenoxybenzamine), the same dose of epinephrine produces a fall in blood pressure. What explains this phenomenon (Dale\u0027s vasomotor reversal)?","o":["Activation of presynaptic alpha-2 (Gi) receptors stimulates intense vascular smooth muscle contraction","Blockade of beta-1 (Gs) cardiac receptors unmasks parasympathetic M3 vasodilation","Blockade of vascular alpha-1 (Gq) receptors unmasks unopposed epinephrine-mediated beta-2 (Gs) skeletal muscle vasodilation","Blockade of vascular beta-2 receptors converts epinephrine into a pure muscarinic agonist"],"a":2,"e":"Epinephrine acts on alpha-1 (Gq -\u003e IP3/DAG -\u003e vasoconstriction), beta-1 (Gs -\u003e increased heart rate/contractility), and beta-2 (Gs -\u003e increased cAMP -\u003e vasodilation in skeletal muscle beds) receptors. At pressor doses, alpha-1 vasoconstriction predominates, raising blood pressure. Pretreatment with an alpha-adrenergic antagonist (phentolamine or phenoxybenzamine) blocks alpha-1-mediated vasoconstriction, leaving beta-2-mediated vasodilation unopposed and causing a net drop in blood pressure (\u0027epinephrine reversal\u0027).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02545","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Oral Anticoagulants (Warfarin)","q":"A 62-year-old man stabilized on warfarin for a mechanical mitral valve (target INR 2.5-3.5) starts a 7-day course of oral metronidazole for amoebic colitis and presents with epistaxis, hematuria, and an INR of 9.2 with hemodynamic instability. What explains this INR spike, and what is the most appropriate immediate reversal regimen?","o":["Metronidazole induces CYP2C9 metabolism of warfarin; administer oral protamine sulfate","Metronidazole inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase directly; administer subcutaneous enoxaparin","Metronidazole directly chelates Factor Xa; administer IV idarucizumab","Metronidazole inhibits CYP2C9 metabolism of S-warfarin; administer IV vitamin K1 plus 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC)"],"a":3,"e":"Warfarin is a racemic mixture whose more potent S-enantiomer is metabolized primarily by hepatic CYP2C9. Metronidazole inhibits CYP2C9, markedly increasing warfarin plasma levels and INR. For major or life-threatening warfarin-associated bleeding, immediate factor replacement with 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC, containing factors II, VII, IX, X, plus proteins C and S; or FFP if PCC is unavailable) combined with slow IV vitamin K1 (phytonadione 5-10 mg) provides both rapid and sustained INR reversal.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02546","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antitubercular Therapy (ATT)","q":"A patient receiving standard first-line HRZE antitubercular therapy (isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol) under NTEP develops nausea, right upper quadrant tenderness, jaundice, and serum ALT 420 U/L after 3 weeks. Which statement regarding the hepatotoxic potential and adverse effect profile of first-line ATT drugs is correct?","o":["Isoniazid, rifampicin, and pyrazinamide are hepatotoxic (with pyrazinamide carrying the highest dose-related hepatotoxicity risk), whereas ethambutol is non-hepatotoxic and causes retrobulbar optic neuritis","Pyrazinamide inhibits hepatic CYP450 enzymes, whereas isoniazid induces CYP3A4 and causes red-green color blindness","Rifampicin is non-hepatotoxic and prevents isoniazid-induced peripheral neuropathy","Ethambutol is the most hepatotoxic first-line ATT drug, whereas pyrazinamide causes orange-red discoloration of urine"],"a":0,"e":"Among first-line oral ATT agents, isoniazid (H), rifampicin (R), and pyrazinamide (Z) all cause drug-induced liver injury—pyrazinamide is generally the most hepatotoxic (and also causes hyperuricemia/arthralgia), isoniazid causes hepatocellular injury (and peripheral neuropathy prevented by pyridoxine), and rifampicin causes cholestatic/mixed injury plus potent CYP450 induction and harmless orange-red secretions. Ethambutol (E) is excreted renally, is non-hepatotoxic (safe to continue when bridging hepatotoxic drugs), and causes dose-dependent retrobulbar optic neuritis manifesting as decreased visual acuity and red-green color blindness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02547","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Aminoglycosides","q":"Assertion (A): Extended-interval (once-daily) high-dose aminoglycoside regimens achieve comparable or superior bactericidal efficacy with lower nephrotoxicity than divided multiple-daily dosing. Reason (R): Aminoglycosides exhibit concentration-dependent killing with a prolonged post-antibiotic effect, and megalin-mediated uptake into renal proximal tubular cells is saturable.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, amikacin, tobramycin) display concentration-dependent bactericidal activity (maximizing Cmax/MIC) and a prolonged post-antibiotic effect (PAE) against Gram-negative bacilli, allowing efficacy even when trough concentrations fall low before the next dose. Furthermore, proximal tubular uptake via apical megalin/cubilin receptors is saturable; a single daily peak limits total tubular accumulation, and low trough periods allow tubular clearance/recovery, reducing reversible non-oliguric acute tubular necrosis compared with persistently elevated troughs from multiple-daily dosing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02548","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"NSAIDs and Cyclooxygenase (COX) Inhibition","q":"Why does low-dose aspirin irreversibly inhibit platelet aggregation for the 7-10 day lifespan of circulating platelets, whereas selective COX-2 inhibitors (coxibs) increase cardiovascular thrombotic risk?","o":["Aspirin induces hepatic synthesis of prostacyclin, whereas coxibs block thromboxane A2 receptors","Aspirin irreversibly acetylates a serine residue in COX-1/COX-2 (blocking platelet TXA2 synthesis in anucleate platelets), whereas coxibs inhibit endothelial COX-2-derived PGI2 without blocking platelet COX-1-derived TXA2","Aspirin reversibly inhibits lipoxygenase, whereas coxibs deplete vitamin K-dependent clotting factors","Aspirin selectively inhibits endothelial COX-2, whereas coxibs irreversibly acetylate platelet COX-1"],"a":1,"e":"Aspirin covalently and irreversibly acetylates Ser530 of platelet COX-1; because platelets are anucleate and cannot synthesize new COX-1 enzyme, thromboxane A2 (TXA2)-mediated platelet aggregation remains inhibited for the entire platelet lifespan (~7-10 days). Selective COX-2 inhibitors (e.g., celecoxib, etoricoxib) spare gastric COX-1 prostaglandins (reducing peptic ulcer risk) but suppress vascular endothelial COX-2-mediated prostacyclin (PGI2, a vasodilator and platelet inhibitor) while leaving platelet COX-1 TXA2 unopposed, shifting hemostatic balance toward thrombosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02549","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Status Epilepticus","q":"A 30-year-old man with known epilepsy is brought to the emergency department in continuous generalized tonic-clonic convulsions lasting 12 minutes. After securing airway, breathing, circulation, and checking blood glucose (normal), intravenous access is established. Which drug is the preferred first-line initial agent, and why is it favored over intravenous diazepam?","o":["Intravenous ethosuximide; it selectively blocks T-type calcium channels in generalized tonic-clonic seizures","Intravenous phenobarbital; it has no respiratory depressant risk when given rapidly","Intravenous lorazepam; it provides a longer duration of anticonvulsant action in the brain due to slower redistribution from CNS","Intravenous phenytoin; it terminates status epilepticus instantly without causing hypotension"],"a":2,"e":"Parenteral benzodiazepines are the first-line initial therapy for convulsive status epilepticus because they rapidly potentiate GABA-A receptor-mediated chloride influx. Intravenous lorazepam (0.1 mg/kg, up to 4 mg) is preferred over diazepam when IV access is available because lorazepam is less lipophilic and redistributes more slowly out of the brain, maintaining anticonvulsant brain concentrations for hours (versus 15-30 minutes for diazepam), followed by a second-line agent such as IV levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, or sodium valproate if seizures continue.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02550","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiac Glycosides (Digoxin)","q":"A 60-year-old man weighing 70 kg with atrial fibrillation and rapid ventricular rate requires a rapid oral digoxin loading dose. The volume of distribution (Vd) of digoxin is 7 L/kg, target plasma concentration (Cp) is 1.5 mcg/L, and oral tablet bioavailability (F) is 0.75. What is the calculated oral loading dose (in micrograms)?","o":["735 mcg","551 mcg","140 mcg","980 mcg"],"a":3,"e":"Loading dose (LD) = (Vd x Cp) / F. First calculate total Vd = 7 L/kg x 70 kg = 490 L. Next calculate target body amount = Vd x Cp = 490 L x 1.5 mcg/L = 735 mcg (this would be the IV loading dose where F = 1). Dividing by oral bioavailability F = 0.75 gives oral LD = 735 / 0.75 = 980 mcg (clinically administered in divided aliquots while monitoring potassium and renal function, as hypokalemia precipitates digoxin toxicity).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02551","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Beta-Lactam Antibiotics (Penicillins)","q":"Five minutes after receiving intramuscular benzathine penicillin G, a 22-year-old woman develops generalized urticaria, inspiratory stridor, wheezing, and blood pressure 78/48 mmHg. Which antigenic determinant primarily drives penicillin allergy, and what is the immediate first-line drug, dose, and route of administration?","o":["Penicilloyl (major determinant); intramuscular epinephrine 0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1:1000) into the anterolateral thigh","Procaine moiety; subcutaneous epinephrine 0.1 mg (1:10,000) into the deltoid","Beta-lactamase ring; intravenous atropine 0.6 mg","Penilloate only; oral cetirizine 10 mg followed by IV hydrocortisone"],"a":0,"e":"Penicillin hypersensitivity is most commonly triggered when the beta-lactam ring opens and binds tissue proteins to form the penicilloyl hapten-protein conjugate (the \u0027major antigenic determinant\u0027, accounting for ~95% of epitopes; minor determinants include penicilloate/penilloate). Acute IgE-mediated (Type I) anaphylaxis requires immediate intramuscular epinephrine 0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1:1000 solution in adults; 0.01 mg/kg in children) into the mid-anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis), which achieves faster and more reliable peak plasma levels than subcutaneous injection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02552","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Bacterial Cell Wall and Toxins","q":"Which structural component of Gram-negative bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS) represents the biologically active endotoxin moiety that binds TLR4/MD-2 on host macrophages to trigger TNF-alpha and IL-1 release during septic shock?","o":["Peptidoglycan (murein) pentapeptide cross-bridge","Lipid A","Teichoic acid","O-specific polysaccharide side chain"],"a":1,"e":"Endotoxin is an integral lipopolysaccharide (LPS) component of the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria consisting of three domains: Lipid A, core polysaccharide, and O-antigen polysaccharide. The conserved hydrophobic Lipid A moiety is the toxic active center that binds CD14 and TLR4/MD-2 on macrophages, triggering NF-kappaB activation, release of TNF-alpha, IL-1beta, and IL-6, fever, DIC, and septic shock. Endotoxins are heat-stable and detected clinically via the Limulus amebocyte lysate (LAL) assay.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02553","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Malaria","q":"A Giemsa-stained thin peripheral blood smear from a patient returning from an endemic forest belt with high fever and altered sensorium shows normal-sized erythrocytes containing multiple delicate ring-form trophozoites, marginal \u0027accolé\u0027 (appliqué) rings, occasional double chromatin dots (\u0027headphone\u0027 forms), and crescent-shaped (banana-shaped) gametocytes. Which Plasmodium species is present?","o":["Plasmodium ovale","Plasmodium vivax","Plasmodium falciparum","Plasmodium malariae"],"a":2,"e":"Plasmodium falciparum infects erythrocytes of all ages without enlarging the host RBC. Key diagnostic features on Giemsa-stained thin smears include multiple ring forms per RBC, fine delicate cytoplasm rings, marginal/peripheral accole (applique) forms, double chromatin dots, Maurer clefts, absence of late trophozoites/schizonts in peripheral blood (due to deep microvascular sequestration via PfEMP1 knobs), and pathognomonic banana- or crescent-shaped gametocytes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02554","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)","q":"A 27-year-old healthcare worker presents 16 days after an unprotected needle-stick exposure from a source known to have untreated HIV, reporting mild fever, pharyngitis, and generalized lymphadenopathy. A 3rd-generation anti-HIV antibody-only ELISA is negative. Which diagnostic assay closes the window period to approximately 2 weeks by simultaneously detecting both viral antigen and host antibodies?","o":["CD4+ T-lymphocyte count by flow cytometry","Western blot assay detecting anti-gp120 antibodies alone","1st-generation viral lysate IgG ELISA","4th-generation HIV-1/2 Ag/Ab combination ELISA detecting p24 capsid antigen plus anti-HIV-1/2 IgM/IgG antibodies"],"a":3,"e":"During acute HIV infection (days 10-21 post-exposure), HIV RNA becomes detectable first (~7-10 days via NAAT/RT-PCR), followed by serum HIV p24 capsid antigen (~14 days) before seroconversion of anti-HIV antibodies (3rd-generation IgM/IgG ELISA typically turns positive at ~3-4 weeks). Fourth-generation HIV Ag/Ab combo assays detect both p24 antigen and anti-HIV-1/2 IgM/IgG antibodies, shortening the serologic window period to roughly 14-18 days.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02555","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Dengue Virus","q":"According to national NVBDCP/ICMR diagnostic guidelines for dengue fever, a patient presenting on Day 2 of high-grade fever with retro-orbital pain and myalgia is tested for dengue. Which test is most appropriate for confirming acute dengue infection on Day 2, and when does MAC-ELISA (IgM) become reliably positive?","o":["NS1 antigen ELISA (positive from Day 1 to Day 5 of illness); dengue IgM MAC-ELISA becomes detectable from Day 5 onwards","Dengue IgG ELISA alone on Day 2; NS1 antigen is absent in primary dengue","Weil-Felix OX-K agglutination on Day 2; dengue IgM peaks at Day 1","Dengue IgM MAC-ELISA on Day 2; NS1 antigen appears only after Day 10"],"a":0,"e":"During the early viremic febrile phase (Days 1-5), dengue viral non-structural protein 1 (NS1) antigen is secreted in high concentrations into blood and can be detected by NS1 ELISA (or RT-PCR). Anti-dengue IgM antibodies (detected by IgM antibody-capture ELISA / MAC-ELISA) typically rise to detectable levels around Day 4-5 of illness, peaking at ~2 weeks. Therefore, NS1 ELISA/RT-PCR is preferred in the first 5 days, whereas IgM MAC-ELISA is preferred from Day 5 onward.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02556","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Clostridioides difficile","q":"A 65-year-old hospitalized patient treated with clindamycin for aspiration pneumonia develops profuse watery diarrhea, abdominal cramps, leukocytosis (22,000/mm3), and yellow-white raised colonic plaques on sigmoidoscopy. How do the major virulence toxins of the causative anaerobic Gram-positive spore-forming bacillus damage enterocytes, and what is the preferred oral treatment?","o":["Shiga-like toxin cleaves 28S rRNA to halt protein synthesis; treat with oral ciprofloxacin","Toxin A (enterotoxin) and Toxin B (cytotoxin) glucosylate Rho family GTPases causing actin cytoskeleton disruption; treat with oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin","Heat-labile toxin increases cAMP via Gs activation; treat with oral clindamycin","Alpha-toxin (lecithinase) lyses enterocyte membranes; treat with IV gentamicin"],"a":1,"e":"Antibiotic exposure (classically clindamycin, broad-spectrum cephalosporins, or fluoroquinolones) disrupts normal colonic microbiota, allowing Clostridioides difficile overgrowth. C. difficile produces Toxin A (enterotoxin) and Toxin B (potent cytotoxin), both of which monoglucosylate Rho/Rac/Cdc42 GTPases, depolymerizing the actin cytoskeleton, disrupting tight junctions, and inducing enterocyte apoptosis and pseudomembrane formation (fibrin, mucin, necrotic debris, neutrophils). First-line therapy includes stopping the offending antibiotic and administering oral vancomycin or oral fidaxomicin (metronidazole is no longer preferred first-line).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02557","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Opportunistic Mycoses","q":"A patient with uncontrolled diabetic ketoacidosis develops black necrotic eschar over the hard palate, periorbital swelling, and ophthalmoplegia. KOH mount and histopathology of nasal sinus debridement reveal broad, ribbon-like, aseptate (pauciseptate) hyphae branching at wide right angles (90 degrees). How does this morphology distinguish the causative fungus from Aspergillus fumigatus?","o":["Mucorales (Rhizopus/Mucor) show broad aseptate right-angle (90-degree) hyphae, whereas Aspergillus shows narrow uniform septate hyphae branching dichotomously at acute (45-degree) angles","Aspergillus shows broad aseptate 90-degree hyphae, whereas Mucorales show narrow septate 45-degree hyphae","Mucorales form pseudohyphae with blastoconidia and germ tubes, whereas Aspergillus forms spherules with endospores","Both Mucorales and Aspergillus show narrow septate 45-degree hyphae and are treated identically with fluconazole"],"a":0,"e":"Rhino-orbital-cerebral mucormycosis (caused by Mucorales such as Rhizopus, Mucor, or Lichtheimia) classically affects patients with diabetic ketoacidosis, iron overload, or corticosteroid use. Histopathology and KOH mounts show broad (10-20 micrometer), ribbon-like, non-septate or pauciseptate hyphae with irregular right-angle (~90-degree) branching and angioinvasion. In contrast, Aspergillus species exhibit narrower (3-5 micrometer), uniform, regularly septate hyphae with dichotomous acute-angle (~45-degree) branching.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02558","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Sterilization and Disinfection","q":"What are the standard operating parameters for routine steam sterilization in a gravity-displacement autoclave, and which bacterial spores serve as its biological indicator?","o":["72°C for 15 seconds; Coxiella burnetii","160°C dry heat for 60 minutes; Bacillus atrophaeus (subtilis var. niger) spores","121°C at 15 psi pressure for 15-20 minutes; Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores","100°C free-flowing steam for 30 minutes; Clostridium tetani spores"],"a":2,"e":"Autoclaving uses saturated steam under pressure (moist heat) to coagulate and denature microbial proteins and enzymes. Standard holding conditions are 121°C at 15 psi (103 kPa above atmospheric pressure) for 15-20 minutes (or 134°C for 3 minutes in pre-vacuum flash cycles). Heat-resistant spores of the thermophilic bacterium Geobacillus stearothermophilus are the gold-standard biological indicator to verify sterilization efficacy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02559","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Postmortem Changes (Algor Mortis and Rigor Mortis)","q":"During a medicolegal examination in a temperate indoor room, an adult male cadaver has a rectal temperature of 90.9°F (32.7°C). Using Glaister\u0027s empirical formula assuming a normal antemortem rectal temperature of 98.4°F and an average cooling rate of 1.5°F per hour, what is the approximate time since death?","o":["3 hours","10 hours","7.5 hours","5 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Glaister\u0027s formula estimates postmortem interval from algor mortis as: Time since death (hours) = (Normal rectal temperature 98.4°F - Measured rectal temperature in °F) / 1.5°F per hour. Substituting the measured value: (98.4 - 90.9) / 1.5 = 7.5 / 1.5 = 5 hours. Clinically, algor mortis estimates must always be corroborated with rigor mortis (typically appearing at 1-2 hours, full by 12 hours) and postmortem lividity while accounting for ambient temperature, clothing, and body habitus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02560","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Organophosphate Poisoning","q":"A 35-year-old farmer is brought to the casualty after intentional ingestion of an agricultural insecticide, smelling strongly of garlic-kerosene. He exhibits pinpoint pupils, profuse bronchorrhea, salivation, diaphoresis, bradycardia, and diffuse skeletal muscle fasciculations. What is the toxic mechanism, and what are the distinct roles of atropine and pralidoxime (2-PAM) in his management?","o":["Irreversible phosphorylation of acetylcholinesterase; atropine reverses muscarinic effects (bronchorrhea/bradycardia), whereas pralidoxime reactivates acetylcholinesterase at nicotinic and muscarinic sites before enzyme \u0027aging\u0027","Direct muscarinic receptor agonism; atropine reverses nicotinic fasciculations, whereas pralidoxime blocks central GABA receptors","Blockade of presynaptic acetylcholine release; physostigmine is given with atropine to enhance ACh levels","Reversible inhibition of butyrylcholinesterase only; atropine reactivates acetylcholinesterase, whereas pralidoxime prevents atropine toxicity"],"a":0,"e":"Organophosphates covalently phosphorylate the serine hydroxyl group of acetylcholinesterase (AChE), causing massive accumulation of acetylcholine at muscarinic (DUMBBELLS: diarrhea, urination, miosis, bronchospasm/bronchorrhea, bradycardia, emesis, lacrimation, salivation), nicotinic (muscle fasciculations progressing to depolarizing paralysis), and CNS synapses. IV atropine competitively antagonizes muscarinic receptors—titrated aggressively until lung secretions clear (\u0027atropinization\u0027)—but does NOT reverse nicotinic neuromuscular paralysis. Pralidoxime (2-PAM) cleaves the phosphate-AChE bond to regenerate active enzyme at both nicotinic and muscarinic junctions, provided it is given early before irreversible dealkylation (\u0027aging\u0027) occurs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02561","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Medical Negligence and Consent Laws","q":"Under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), 2023 (which replaced the Indian Penal Code), which section specifically penalizes causing death by rash or negligent act by a registered medical practitioner while performing a medical procedure (corresponding to former IPC Section 304A), and which section protects emergency treatment performed in good faith without consent (corresponding to former IPC Section 92)?","o":["BNS Section 106(2) for hit-and-run medical negligence; BNS Section 28 for emergency act without consent","BNS Section 106(1) for medical negligence (up to 2 years imprisonment and fine); BNS Section 30 for emergency act in good faith without consent","BNS Section 88 for medical negligence; BNS Section 304A for emergency consent","BNS Section 103 for medical negligence; BNS Section 26 for emergency act without consent"],"a":1,"e":"Under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), 2023, Section 106(1) replaces IPC Section 304A for causing death by negligence: while general negligence carries up to 5 years imprisonment, a dedicated clause specifies that if the act is done by a registered medical practitioner while performing a medical procedure, punishment may extend to 2 years imprisonment with fine (requiring gross negligence under Jacob Mathew principles). Regarding consent exceptions: BNS Section 26 (former IPC 88) protects acts done in good faith with consent; BNS Section 28 (former IPC 90) invalidates consent given under fear, misconception, or by a child under 12 years; and BNS Section 30 (former IPC 92) protects emergency life-saving acts done in good faith when consent cannot be obtained.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02562","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Lead vs Arsenic Poisoning","q":"A 34-year-old battery-recycling factory worker presents with colicky abdominal pain, constipation, microcytic anemia with coarse basophilic stippling of erythrocytes, bilateral wrist drop, and a bluish-black stippled line along the gingival margin. Which enzymes are inhibited by this toxin, and how do the cutaneous and nail signs differ in chronic arsenic poisoning?","o":["Lead inhibits cytochrome c oxidase; chronic arsenic causes pure motor wrist drop without sensory neuropathy","Lead inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase; chronic arsenic causes Burtonian blue gum line and wrist drop","Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase; chronic arsenic poisoning causes \u0027raindrop\u0027 hyperpigmentation, palmar-plantar hyperkeratosis, and transverse white Mees\u0027 lines on nails","Arsenic inhibits ferrochelatase; chronic lead poisoning causes Mees\u0027 lines and garlic breath odor"],"a":2,"e":"Chronic inorganic lead poisoning (plumbism) inhibits delta-aminolevulinic acid dehydratase (ALAD) and ferrochelatase in heme synthesis, causing microcytic sideroblastic anemia with punctate basophilic stippling (accumulated undegraded rRNA due to pyrimidine-5\u0027-nucleotidase inhibition), abdominal colic, radial motor neuropathy (wrist drop), and a subepithelial lead sulfide gingival line (Burtonian line); treatment uses CaNa2EDTA or succimer (DMSA). Conversely, chronic arsenic poisoning inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase/lipoyl-dependent enzymes via sulfhydryl binding, producing mottled \u0027raindrop\u0027 skin pigmentation, palmar-plantar hyperkeratosis, transverse white nail bands (Aldrich-Mees\u0027 lines), and painful glove-and-stocking sensorimotor neuropathy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02563","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Autopsy Artifacts","q":"Assertion (A): During forensic autopsy of a suspected hanging or strangulation case, the brain and thoracic viscera must be eviscerated prior to dissecting the anterior neck structures. Reason (R): Dissecting the neck before draining cephalic and thoracic venous blood creates the Prinsloo-Gordon artifact—postmortem extravasation from engorged pharyngo-laryngeal venous plexuses that mimics antemortem neck bruising.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"The Prinsloo-Gordon artifact occurs when postmortem blood from congested neck and retropharyngeal/laryngeal venous plexuses leaks into soft tissues over the posterior cricoarytenoid/pharyngeal muscles or strap muscles during in situ neck dissection, closely mimicking antemortem vital bruising of manual strangulation. Opening the cranium and thorax and removing the brain and thoracic organs first drains blood from the neck vessels, creating a bloodless field for layered neck dissection that prevents this critical false-positive autopsy artifact.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02564","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Observational Studies","q":"In a 10-year prospective cohort study evaluating occupational silica exposure and chronic bronchitis, the annual incidence among exposed workers is 120 per 1,000 person-years, whereas among unexposed workers it is 15 per 1,000 person-years. What is the Attributable Risk Percent (AR%) among the exposed workers?","o":["92.5%","80.0%","75.0%","87.5%"],"a":3,"e":"Attributable Risk (AR) = Incidence in exposed minus Incidence in unexposed = 120 - 15 = 105 per 1,000 person-years. Attributable Risk Percent (AR%) = [(Ie - Iu) / Ie] x 100 = (105 / 120) x 100 = 87.5%, indicating the proportion of disease among exposed workers attributable to silica exposure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02565","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Universal Immunisation Programme (UIP)","q":"At a Primary Health Centre immunisation session, opened multi-dose vials returned to the ice-lined refrigerator (ILR) at the end of the session are inspected for reuse under the Government of India UIP Open Vial Policy (up to 28 days). Which of the following vaccines MUST be discarded within 4 hours of reconstitution and is NOT eligible for the 28-day Open Vial Policy?","o":["Measles-Rubella (MR) vaccine","Pentavalent (DPT-HepB-Hib) vaccine","Fractional-dose Inactivated Poliovirus Vaccine (fIPV)","Oral Polio Vaccine (OPV)"],"a":0,"e":"Under India\u0027s UIP Open Vial Policy, liquid multi-dose vaccines containing preservatives (OPV, Pentavalent, IPV/fIPV, Hep B, Td, PCV) can be reused for up to 28 days if stored at +2 to +8 degrees C without contamination, freeze damage, or expired VVM. Reconstituted freeze-dried vaccines (BCG, Measles-Rubella, Japanese Encephalitis) lack preservatives and must be discarded 4 hours after reconstitution (6 hours for JE/BCG as per manufacturer, maximum 4 hours for MR).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02566","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP)","q":"A 38-year-old man weighing 52 kg presents to a microscopy centre with cough for 3 weeks, evening rise of temperature, and weight loss. Sputum smear and CBNAAT confirm Mycobacterium tuberculosis sensitive to rifampicin. Under NTEP guidelines, what is the standard daily Fixed-Dose Combination (FDC) regimen for this patient?","o":["6 months of HRZES + 12 months of HRE (daily)","2 months of HRZE + 4 months of HRE (daily weight-band FDC)","2 months of HRZE + 4 months of HR (thrice-weekly intermittent)","2 months of Bedaquiline-Levofloxacin-Linezolid + 4 months of HR"],"a":1,"e":"Under India\u0027s National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP), all drug-sensitive TB patients receive a daily weight-band Fixed-Dose Combination regimen: 2 months (56 doses) of intensive phase with Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide, and Ethambutol (HRZE), followed by 4 months (112 doses) of continuation phase with Isoniazid, Rifampicin, and Ethambutol (HRE).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02567","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme","q":"A 26-year-old non-pregnant woman in Delhi presents with high-grade fever with chills and rigors. Peripheral blood smear shows ring forms and amoeboid trophozoites of Plasmodium vivax. According to the National Drug Policy on Malaria (NCVBDC, India), what is the recommended radical treatment?","o":["Artemether-Lumefantrine for 3 days + single-dose Primaquine 0.75 mg/kg","Artesunate + Sulfadoxine-Pyrimethamine for 3 days + Primaquine for 7 days","Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days + Primaquine 0.25 mg/kg/day for 14 days","Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days alone without Primaquine"],"a":2,"e":"Under India\u0027s National Drug Policy on Malaria, uncomplicated Plasmodium vivax malaria is treated with Chloroquine (25 mg base/kg divided over 3 days: 10, 10, 5 mg/kg) for blood schizontocidal action, combined with Primaquine (0.25 mg/kg daily for 14 days) to eliminate hepatic hypnozoites and prevent relapse, after ruling out pregnancy, infancy (\u003c1 year), and G6PD deficiency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02568","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Screening for Disease","q":"A new rapid screening assay for disease X is evaluated in a community survey of 1,000 individuals where true disease prevalence is 10% (100 diseased, 900 non-diseased). The test identifies 80 true positives among the diseased and produces 90 false positives among the non-diseased. What are the sensitivity and positive predictive value (PPV) of the test?","o":["Sensitivity 80.0%, PPV 90.0%","Sensitivity 90.0%, PPV 80.0%","Sensitivity 47.1%, PPV 80.0%","Sensitivity 80.0%, PPV 47.1%"],"a":3,"e":"Sensitivity = TP / (TP + FN) = 80 / 100 = 80.0%. Specificity = TN / (TN + FP) = 810 / 900 = 90.0%. Positive Predictive Value (PPV) = TP / (TP + FP) = 80 / (80 + 90) = 80 / 170 = 47.06% (47.1%), illustrating how low disease prevalence lowers PPV despite 90% specificity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02569","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Clinical Trial Metrics","q":"In a randomised controlled trial of a preventive statin therapy over 5 years, major cardiovascular events occurred in 25% of patients in the placebo group and 15% of patients in the statin group. How many patients need to be treated (NNT) for 5 years to prevent one major cardiovascular event?","o":["10","15","40","4"],"a":0,"e":"Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR) = Control Event Rate (CER) - Experimental Event Rate (EER) = 25% - 15% = 10% (0.10). Number Needed to Treat (NNT) = 1 / ARR = 1 / 0.10 = 10 patients.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02570","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Pneumoconioses","q":"A 48-year-old male who worked for 18 years in slate-pencil and stone-crushing units in Madhya Pradesh presents with progressive exertional dyspnoea and dry cough. Chest radiograph demonstrates upper-lobe nodular opacities and peripheral \u0027eggshell\u0027 calcification of hilar lymph nodes. Which complication is he at highest risk of developing?","o":["Pleural mesothelioma","Pulmonary tuberculosis (silicotuberculosis)","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Farmer\u0027s lung (hypersensitivity pneumonitis)"],"a":1,"e":"Chronic exposure to crystalline silica (0.5 to 3 microns) in stone-crushing, mining, and slate-pencil industries causes silicosis, classically presenting with upper-zone fibrotic nodules and \u0027eggshell\u0027 calcification of hilar lymph nodes. Impaired alveolar macrophage clearance markedly increases susceptibility to pulmonary tuberculosis (silicotuberculosis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02571","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Primary Angle-Closure Glaucoma","q":"A 62-year-old hypermetropic woman presents to the emergency room with sudden severe right eye pain, headache, vomiting, and coloured halos around lights after sitting in a dark cinema hall. Examination reveals visual acuity of hand movements, circumcorneal ciliary congestion, corneal oedema, shallow anterior chamber, a mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil, and stony hard eyeball on digital palpation. After administering IV mannitol and oral acetazolamide, what is the definitive procedure once the attack is broken?","o":["Nd:YAG laser peripheral iridotomy in the affected eye and prophylactic iridotomy in the fellow eye","Topical atropine 1% drops in both eyes","Immediate phacoemulsification in the fellow eye only","Systemic corticosteroids followed by tarsorrhaphy"],"a":0,"e":"Acute primary angle-closure crisis occurs due to pupillary block in predisposed shallow anterior chambers. Emergency medical IOP reduction uses hyperosmotic agents (IV mannitol), carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (acetazolamide), and topical pilocarpine (effective once ischaemic sphincter recovers). Definitive treatment is bilateral Nd:YAG laser peripheral iridotomy to bypass pupillary block, as the fellow eye has a high risk of an attack.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02572","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Diabetic Retinopathy","q":"A 56-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus for 16 years undergoes dilated fundus examination showing dot-and-blot haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots, venous beading, and neovascularisation at the optic disc (NVD) covering more than one-third of the disc area without centre-involving macular oedema. What is the gold-standard treatment to prevent severe visual loss?","o":["Grid laser photocoagulation to the fovea","Topical nepafenac eye drops","Panretinal photocoagulation (PRP)","Observation with 1-yearly follow-up"],"a":2,"e":"Presence of neovascularisation at the disc (NVD \u003e 1/3 disc area) defines high-risk Proliferative Diabetic Retinopathy (PDR). Panretinal photocoagulation (PRP) destroys hypoxic peripheral retina, reducing VEGF drive and preventing vitreous haemorrhage and tractional retinal detachment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02573","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Trachoma","q":"During a school eye screening programme, an 8-year-old child has upper tarsal conjunctival follicles (\u003e=5 follicles of size \u003e=0.5 mm) caused by Chlamydia trachomatis (serotypes A, B, Ba, C). Under the WHO SAFE strategy for trachoma control, what does the letter \u0027A\u0027 represent?","o":["Artificial tears for dry eye prophylaxis","Anterior lamellar keratoplasty","Antivirals (topical Acyclovir 3% ointment)","Antibiotics (single-dose oral Azithromycin 20 mg/kg, max 1 g)"],"a":3,"e":"The WHO SAFE strategy stands for Surgery (for trichiasis/entropion), Antibiotics (mass drug administration or targeted single-dose oral Azithromycin 20 mg/kg up to 1 g, or 1% tetracycline eye ointment for 6 weeks), Facial cleanliness, and Environmental improvement. Active trachoma features tarsal follicles (TF), while cicatricial stages show Arlt\u0027s line and Herbert\u0027s pits.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02574","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Infective Keratitis","q":"A 42-year-old farmer presents 10 days after a paddy-leaf injury to his left eye with mild pain disproportionate to signs, a dry greyish-white corneal ulcer with feathery hyphate margins, satellite lesions, and a thick immobile hypopyon. Potassium hydroxide (KOH) wet mount shows septate hyphae. Which topical agent is the drug of choice?","o":["Natamycin 5% eye drops","Acyclovir 3% eye ointment","Prednisolone acetate 1% eye drops","Moxifloxacin 0.5% eye drops"],"a":0,"e":"Trauma with vegetative matter, feathery margins, satellite lesions, and septate hyphae (commonly Aspergillus or Fusarium) are classic for fungal keratitis. Topical Natamycin 5% suspension is the first-line agent for filamentous fungal corneal ulcers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02575","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Chronic Suppurative Otitis Media (CSOM)","q":"A 24-year-old man presents with a 6-year history of scanty, foul-smelling, occasionally blood-stained left ear discharge and progressive hearing loss. Otoscopy shows a posterior-superior marginal perforation with pearly-white flakes in the attic and erosion of the outer attic wall (scutum). What is the definitive management?","o":["Conservative topical antibiotic ear drops and simple myringoplasty","Mastoidectomy (tympanomastoid exploration) to eradicate cholesteatoma","Oral antihistamines and nasal decongestants alone","Grommet (ventilation tube) insertion in the anteroinferior quadrant"],"a":1,"e":"Scanty, foul-smelling discharge, marginal/attic perforation, pearly-white cholesteatoma debris, and scutum erosion define unsafe (atticoantral/squamosal) CSOM. Because cholesteatoma causes progressive bone erosion and life-threatening intracranial complications (brain abscess, lateral sinus thrombophlebitis, meningitis), definitive surgical eradication via mastoidectomy is mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02576","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Peripheral Vestibular Disorders","q":"A 41-year-old woman experiences recurrent episodes of spontaneous rotatory vertigo lasting 2 to 4 hours, accompanied by nausea, left-sided roaring tinnitus, aural fullness, and fluctuating hearing loss. Pure-tone audiometry demonstrates a low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss in the left ear. Which underlying pathophysiological mechanism and initial maintenance therapy best fit this condition?","o":["Autoimmune ossicular fixation; sodium fluoride tablets","Cupulolithiasis of the posterior semicircular canal; Epley manoeuvre","Endolymphatic hydrops; salt restriction with betahistine and/or diuretics","Demyelination of the vestibular nerve; high-dose IV acyclovir"],"a":2,"e":"Meniere\u0027s disease presents with episodic vertigo (20 min to 12 hours), fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus, and aural fullness due to distension of the membranous labyrinth from endolymphatic hydrops. Long-term medical management includes a low-salt diet, betahistine (histamine H1 agonist/H3 antagonist improving labyrinthine microcirculation), and thiazide/acetazolamide diuretics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02577","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Epistaxis","q":"A 12-year-old boy presents with recurrent anterior epistaxis originating from Little\u0027s area on the anteroinferior nasal septum. Which four arteries anastomose to form Kiesselbach\u0027s plexus in this region?","o":["Posterior ethmoidal, inferior labial, maxillary, and superficial temporal arteries","Internal carotid, supraorbital, infraorbital, and posterior auricular arteries","Posterior ethmoidal, facial, lingual, and middle meningeal arteries","Anterior ethmoidal, sphenopalatine, greater palatine, and superior labial arteries"],"a":3,"e":"Kiesselbach\u0027s plexus in Little\u0027s area (anteroinferior cartilaginous septum) is the commonest site of epistaxis in children and young adults. It is formed by the anastomosis of the anterior ethmoidal artery (from ophthalmic/ICA), septal branch of sphenopalatine artery, greater palatine artery, and septal branch of superior labial artery (from ECA). First aid includes pinching the soft cartilaginous nose with head leaning forward (Trotter\u0027s method).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02578","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Assessment of Hearing","q":"A 28-year-old woman complains of progressive bilateral hearing loss that worsened during pregnancy, and mentions she hears better in noisy surroundings (paracusis Willisii). Tuning fork testing at 512 Hz shows Rinne test negative bilaterally (BC \u003e AC) and Weber test lateralised to the worse-hearing ear. Pure-tone audiometry reveals an air-bone gap with a dip in bone conduction at 2000 Hz. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Otosclerosis (stapedial)","Acoustic neuroma (vestibular schwannoma)","Noise-induced hearing loss","Presbycusis"],"a":0,"e":"Progressive conductive hearing loss in a young adult female exacerbated by pregnancy, paracusis Willisii, negative Rinne test, Weber lateralised to the worse ear, and Carhart\u0027s notch (a 2000 Hz bone-conduction dip) are classic features of stapedial otosclerosis. Surgical treatment is stapedotomy with prosthesis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02579","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA)","q":"A 19-year-old boy with type 1 diabetes mellitus presents with polyuria, abdominal pain, Kussmaul breathing, BP 92/58 mmHg, blood glucose 460 mg/dL, arterial pH 7.12, serum HCO3- 10 mEq/L, positive serum ketones, and serum potassium 2.9 mEq/L. After starting 0.9% normal saline resuscitation, what is the immediate next critical step regarding insulin and potassium?","o":["Switch immediately to subcutaneous insulin glargine and 5% dextrose","Hold IV regular insulin temporarily and administer IV KCl (20-30 mEq/L) until K+ \u003e= 3.3 mEq/L","Administer IV sodium bicarbonate 100 mEq immediately to normalise pH","Start high-dose IV insulin bolus (0.5 U/kg) immediately without potassium"],"a":1,"e":"In DKA, total body potassium is severely depleted despite variable serum levels. When initial serum K+ is \u003c3.3 mEq/L, initiating insulin drives potassium intracellularly and precipitates life-threatening hypokalaemic cardiac arrhythmias. Therefore, insulin must be held while giving IV KCl (20-30 mEq/L of IV fluid) until K+ rises \u003e=3.3 mEq/L.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02580","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Acute Coronary Syndrome","q":"A 60-year-old man presents with crushing retrosternal chest pain for 2 hours. BP is 84/54 mmHg, HR 62/min, JVP is elevated with Kussmaul\u0027s sign, and lungs are clear to auscultation. ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, and right-sided lead V4R shows 2 mm ST elevation. Which intervention is MOST appropriate for his hypotension and which drug must be avoided?","o":["Perform immediate pericardiocentesis; avoid clopidogrel","Administer IV nitroglycerin infusion; avoid normal saline","Administer IV 0.9% normal saline fluid bolus; avoid nitroglycerin and morphine","Administer IV furosemide 40 mg; avoid aspirin"],"a":2,"e":"Inferior wall STEMI with hypotension, clear lung fields, elevated JVP, and ST elevation in lead V4R indicates concomitant Right Ventricular (RV) myocardial infarction. RV cardiac output is preload-dependent; therefore, careful IV isotonic saline bolus restores RV filling pressure, whereas venodilators/preload-reducers such as nitrates, diuretics, and morphine can cause profound cardiovascular collapse.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02581","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Complications of Cirrhosis","q":"A 54-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis and tense ascites is admitted with fever (38.3 degrees C), diffuse abdominal tenderness, and mild asterixis. Diagnostic paracentesis reveals ascitic fluid total protein 0.8 g/dL, SAAG 1.6 g/dL, and polymorphonuclear (PMN) leukocyte count 480 cells/mm3 (Gram stain shows no organisms yet). What is the diagnosis and preferred empirical therapy?","o":["Uncomplicated portal hypertension ascites; large-volume paracentesis without antibiotics","Secondary bacterial peritonitis; emergency exploratory laparotomy","Tuberculous peritonitis; empirical 4-drug ATT alone","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis; IV Cefotaxime plus IV Albumin"],"a":3,"e":"An ascitic fluid absolute PMN count \u003e= 250 cells/mm3 confirms Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP) even before culture results (culture-negative neutrocytic ascites). Most commonly caused by Escherichia coli, SBP is treated empirically with a third-generation cephalosporin (e.g., IV Cefotaxime 2 g q8h for 5 days) plus IV Albumin (1.5 g/kg on day 1 and 1 g/kg on day 3) to prevent hepatorenal syndrome and reduce mortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02582","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Thyroid Disorders","q":"A 70-year-old woman with longstanding untreated primary hypothyroidism is brought to the ICU in winter with hypothermia (33.8 degrees C), bradycardia (44/min), altered sensorium, hypoventilation (PaCO2 58 mmHg), and hyponatraemia (Na+ 122 mEq/L). Along with supportive airway/warming care and IV levothyroxine, why MUST IV hydrocortisone be administered concurrently?","o":["To prevent acute adrenal crisis from coexisting adrenal insufficiency or accelerated cortisol clearance","To directly reverse hyponatraemia by inhibiting ADH synthesis immediately","To stimulate endogenous TSH secretion from the pituitary","To enhance peripheral conversion of T4 to reverse T3"],"a":0,"e":"In myxoedema coma, patients may have coexisting primary (autoimmune polyglandular) or secondary (pituitary) adrenal insufficiency, and rapid thyroid hormone replacement accelerates hepatic cortisol metabolism. Giving IV hydrocortisone (e.g., 100 mg IV q8h) before or alongside IV levothyroxine prevents precipitating acute adrenal crisis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02583","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Chronic Kidney Disease Emergencies","q":"A 58-year-old man with Stage 5 Chronic Kidney Disease misses two haemodialysis sessions and presents with generalised weakness. Serum potassium is 7.4 mEq/L and ECG shows tall peaked T waves, flattened P waves, and QRS widening (140 ms). Which agent should be administered FIRST?","o":["Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate (Kayexalate)","10% Calcium gluconate 10 mL IV over 2 to 5 minutes","Regular insulin 10 units IV with 25 g dextrose","Nebulised salbutamol 10 mg"],"a":1,"e":"QRS widening and peaked T waves indicate severe myocardial membrane instability from hyperkalaemia. The immediate first step is IV 10% calcium gluconate (10 mL over 2-5 min under ECG monitoring), which restores the resting-to-threshold potential gradient within 1-3 minutes without lowering serum K+. Shifting agents (insulin-dextrose, nebulised beta-2 agonists) and removal (haemodialysis, potassium binders) follow immediately after membrane stabilisation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02584","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Cerebrovascular Disease","q":"A 64-year-old man arrives in the emergency department 2 hours after sudden onset of right-sided hemiparesis and expressive aphasia (NIHSS 14). Non-contrast CT head shows no intracranial haemorrhage or established large infarction. BP is 196/114 mmHg, blood glucose is 128 mg/dL, and platelets are 210,000/mm3. What is the correct immediate step before administering IV alteplase (0.9 mg/kg)?","o":["Administer oral aspirin 300 mg plus clopidogrel 300 mg and withhold alteplase","Administer IV alteplase immediately without lowering BP to maintain cerebral perfusion","Lower BP cautiously with IV labetalol to \u003c 185/110 mmHg, then infuse IV alteplase within the 4.5-hour window","Wait for MRI brain diffusion-weighted imaging at 6 hours before deciding on thrombolysis"],"a":2,"e":"Patients presenting within 4.5 hours of acute ischaemic stroke symptom onset without haemorrhage on NCCT are candidates for IV thrombolysis (alteplase 0.9 mg/kg, max 90 mg: 10% bolus, 90% over 60 min). However, BP \u003e= 185/110 mmHg increases symptomatic intracranial haemorrhage risk and must first be gently lowered below 185/110 mmHg using IV labetalol or nicardipine (and maintained \u003c180/105 mmHg for 24 hours post-lysis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02585","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Appendix","q":"A 26-year-old man presents with a 6-day history of right iliac fossa pain and low-grade fever. He is haemodynamically stable without generalised peritonitis, and abdominal examination reveals a well-defined, firm, mildly tender 5 x 4 cm mass in the right iliac fossa. Ultrasound confirms an appendicular phlegmon (lump) without a drainable abscess cavity. What is the preferred initial management?","o":["Immediate emergency appendicectomy via Gridiron incision","Radiotherapy to the right iliac fossa","Right hemicolectomy with ileotransverse anastomosis","Conservative management with the Ochsner-Sherren regimen (IV fluids, broad-spectrum antibiotics, close monitoring)"],"a":3,"e":"When an appendicular mass/phlegmon forms after 4-5 days, the omentum and bowel loops wall off the inflamed appendix. Immediate surgery risks spreading infection, bleeding, and iatrogenic bowel injury (faecal fistula). Standard care is the conservative Ochsner-Sherren regimen (bowel rest/IV fluids, IV antibiotics, charting pulse/temperature/mass size), operating only if deterioration or abscess formation occurs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02586","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Thyroid Gland","q":"Twelve hours after subtotal thyroidectomy in an inadequately prepared patient with Graves\u0027 disease, she develops fever (40.2 degrees C), sinus tachycardia (156/min), agitation, and pulmonary oedema (thyroid storm). When initiating multi-drug therapy with propranolol, propylthiouracil (PTU), hydrocortisone, and Lugol\u0027s iodine, what is the critical timing rule for Lugol\u0027s iodine?","o":["Administer Lugol\u0027s iodine at least 1 hour AFTER propylthiouracil to prevent increased thyroid hormone synthesis","Give Lugol\u0027s iodine as monotherapy and avoid beta-blockers","Replace propylthiouracil with levothyroxine before giving iodine","Administer Lugol\u0027s iodine 2 hours BEFORE propylthiouracil to induce immediate Wolff-Chaikoff effect"],"a":0,"e":"In thyroid storm, PTU is preferred over methimazole because it both inhibits thyroid peroxidase-mediated hormone synthesis and blocks peripheral conversion of T4 to active T3. Inorganic iodine (Lugol\u0027s solution or SSKI) inhibits hormone release, but MUST be given at least 1 hour after PTU; giving iodine first supplies substrate for new thyroid hormone synthesis (Jod-Basedow effect) and worsens thyrotoxicosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02587","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Chest Trauma","q":"A 30-year-old motorcyclist sustains blunt right-sided chest trauma and arrives with severe dyspnoea, SpO2 78%, BP 74/46 mmHg, HR 138/min, distended neck veins, tracheal deviation to the left, absent breath sounds, and hyperresonance over the right hemithorax. What is the immediate life-saving action?","o":["Perform emergency endotracheal intubation and positive-pressure ventilation before decompression","Immediate needle decompression of the right pleural space without waiting for a chest radiograph","Insert a left-sided intercostal chest drain","Order a portable chest X-ray first to confirm lung collapse before intervention"],"a":1,"e":"Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis characterised by respiratory distress, obstructive shock (hypotension, distended neck veins), contralateral tracheal shift, absent breath sounds, and hyperresonance. Under ATLS guidelines, immediate needle (or finger) thoracostomy decompression (4th/5th intercostal space anterior-to-midaxillary line in adults, or 2nd ICS midclavicular line) must be performed BEFORE radiography, followed by tube thoracostomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02588","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Thermal Burns Resuscitation","q":"A 60-kg adult arrives in the emergency room 2 hours after sustaining deep partial-thickness and full-thickness flame burns involving 40% of total body surface area (TBSA). Using the Parkland formula (4 mL/kg/%TBSA of Ringer\u0027s Lactate), at what hourly rate should Ringer\u0027s Lactate be infused over the next 6 hours to complete the first 8-hour resuscitation volume?","o":["600 mL/hour","1,200 mL/hour","800 mL/hour","400 mL/hour"],"a":2,"e":"Parkland total 24-hour fluid requirement = 4 mL x 60 kg x 40% = 9,600 mL of Ringer\u0027s Lactate. Half of this total (4,800 mL) must be infused during the first 8 hours calculated FROM THE TIME OF BURN. Since 2 hours have already elapsed prior to arrival, 4,800 mL is infused over the remaining 6 hours = 4,800 / 6 = 800 mL/hour (titrated thereafter to urine output 0.5 mL/kg/h).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02589","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gallstones and Biliary Tree","q":"A 52-year-old woman with known gallstones presents with severe right upper quadrant pain, high-grade fever with rigors (39.5 degrees C), progressive jaundice, BP 82/50 mmHg, and confusion. Serum bilirubin is 6.8 mg/dL and alkaline phosphatase is 520 U/L. Ultrasound shows dilated common bile duct (14 mm) with a distal calculus. Beyond resuscitation and IV broad-spectrum antibiotics, what is the urgent definitive intervention?","o":["Oral ursodeoxycholic acid dissolution therapy","Elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy after 6 weeks without decompression","Watchful waiting with antispasmodics for biliary colic","Urgent biliary decompression via Endoscopic Retrograde Cholangiopancreatography (ERCP)"],"a":3,"e":"This patient exhibits Reynolds\u0027 pentad (Charcot\u0027s triad of RUQ pain, fever/rigors, jaundice PLUS hypotension and altered mental status), diagnostic of acute suppurative ascending cholangitis. While simple biliary colic is self-limiting and acute cholecystitis has a positive Murphy\u0027s sign without obstructive jaundice/sepsis, toxic cholangitis demands immediate fluid resuscitation, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and urgent biliary decompression (ERCP sphincterotomy/stenting, or PTBD if ERCP fails).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02590","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Groin Hernias","q":"A 62-year-old multiparous woman presents with an irreducible, tense, tender 3 cm swelling in the upper thigh located below and lateral to the pubic tubercle, accompanied by colicky abdominal pain and bilious vomiting. Which anatomical structure forms the sharp medial boundary of the femoral ring responsible for its high strangulation risk?","o":["Lacunar (Gimbernat\u0027s) ligament","Inguinal (Poupart\u0027s) ligament","Femoral vein","Iliopsoas fascia"],"a":0,"e":"A femoral hernia emerges through the femoral canal, lying below the inguinal ligament and below and lateral to the pubic tubercle (unlike an inguinal hernia, which lies above and medial to the pubic tubercle). Because the femoral ring is narrow and bounded medially by the sharp, rigid free edge of the lacunar (Gimbernat\u0027s) ligament, anteriorly by the inguinal ligament, posteriorly by the pectineal (Cooper\u0027s) ligament, and laterally by the femoral vein, femoral hernias carry the highest incidence of irreducibility and strangulation among groin hernias.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02591","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Eclampsia and Severe Preeclampsia","q":"A primigravida at 36 weeks of gestation develops generalised tonic-clonic eclamptic seizures and receives the Pritchard regimen loading dose of Magnesium Sulphate (4 g IV slow + 10 g IM). Four hours later, before administering the 5 g IM maintenance dose, her respiratory rate is 10 breaths/min, deep tendon (patellar) reflexes are absent, and urine output over 4 hours is 60 mL. What is the correct immediate action?","o":["Administer half the maintenance MgSO4 dose (2.5 g IM) immediately","Withhold the maintenance MgSO4 dose and administer 10% Calcium Gluconate 10 mL IV slowly","Give an additional 4 g IV MgSO4 bolus to prevent recurrent seizures","Administer IV furosemide 40 mg and continue full 5 g IM MgSO4"],"a":1,"e":"Before each maintenance dose of MgSO4 (5 g IM q4h in alternate buttocks), three clinical criteria must be met: patellar reflexes present, respiratory rate \u003e= 16/min (some protocols \u003e= 12-16/min), and urine output \u003e= 100 mL over 4 hours (or \u003e= 30 mL/h). Absent patellar reflexes with respiratory depression (10/min) and oliguria indicate magnesium toxicity; MgSO4 must be withheld immediately and its antidote, 10% Calcium Gluconate (10 mL IV over 3-10 min), administered.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02592","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Postpartum Haemorrhage (PPH)","q":"Which intervention is the single most effective component of Active Management of the Third Stage of Labour (AMTSL) recommended by WHO and MoHFW India for preventing atonic postpartum haemorrhage after every vaginal birth?","o":["Immediate intravenous ergometrine before delivery of the anterior shoulder","Routine manual removal of the placenta immediately after delivery","Administration of Oxytocin 10 IU IM within 1 minute of delivery of the baby","Episiotomy and prophylactic uterine tamponade"],"a":2,"e":"AMTSL reduces PPH by over 60%, and administration of a uterotonic—specifically Oxytocin 10 IU IM (or slow IV) within 1 minute of birth (after ruling out a second twin)—is its most critical component. Controlled cord traction and assessment of uterine tone (with uterine massage if atonic) complete AMTSL.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02593","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Ectopic Pregnancy","q":"A 27-year-old woman with 6 weeks of amenorrhoea presents with mild left iliac fossa pain and minimal spotting. Pulse is 78/min, BP 116/74 mmHg, Hb 11.8 g/dL, and serum beta-hCG is 1,850 IU/L. Transvaginal ultrasound shows an empty uterine cavity and a 2.4 cm unruptured left adnexal gestational sac with no fetal cardiac activity and no free fluid in the pouch of Douglas. LFT and RFT are normal. Which management is most appropriate?","o":["Dilatation and curettage of the uterine cavity","Oral mifepristone followed by misoprostol","Immediate emergency laparotomy and salpingectomy","Single-dose intramuscular Methotrexate (50 mg/m2) with serial day 4 and day 7 beta-hCG monitoring"],"a":3,"e":"This patient satisfies all criteria for medical management of tubal ectopic pregnancy with IM Methotrexate (50 mg/m2): haemodynamically stable, unruptured sac \u003c 3.5 cm, absent fetal cardiac activity, initial beta-hCG \u003c 5,000 IU/L (optimal \u003c 3,000 IU/L), no significant haemoperitoneum, normal hepatic/renal/haematological parameters, and reliable follow-up (expecting \u003e= 15% drop in beta-hCG between day 4 and day 7).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02594","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Placenta Praevia","q":"A 30-year-old gravida 3 para 2 woman at 33 weeks of gestation presents with sudden-onset, painless, bright-red vaginal bleeding (~200 mL) that occurred while resting. Pulse is 88/min, BP 114/72 mmHg. On abdominal palpation, the uterus is soft, non-tender, relaxed, with a breech presentation and normal fetal heart rate (142/min). What is the single most critical precaution in initial assessment?","o":["Strictly avoid digital per-vaginal examination; perform transabdominal/transvaginal ultrasound","Rupture the membranes artificially to induce labour","Perform an immediate digital vaginal examination in the labour ward to assess cervical dilatation","Administer IV oxytocin infusion to contract the uterus"],"a":0,"e":"Painless, causeless, recurrent bright-red vaginal bleeding in the third trimester with a soft, non-tender uterus and malpresentation/high presenting part is classic for placenta praevia. Digital per-vaginal (PV) examination can disrupt placental cotyledons covering the internal os and trigger catastrophic haemorrhage; hence PV examination is strictly contraindicated outside a double set-up in the operating theatre, and ultrasound localises the placenta safely.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02595","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Diarrhoeal Dehydration","q":"A 14-month-old toddler weighing 10 kg is brought with watery diarrhoea for 2 days. On IMNCI assessment, the child is restless and irritable, has sunken eyes, drinks ORS eagerly when offered, and an abdominal skin pinch goes back slowly (\u003c2 seconds). How is this dehydration classified and what fluid plan is indicated?","o":["Some dehydration; IV 5% dextrose 500 mL over 2 hours","Some dehydration; IMNCI Plan B giving 750 mL of low-osmolarity ORS over 4 hours","No dehydration; IMNCI Plan A giving 50-100 mL ORS after each loose stool","Severe dehydration; IMNCI Plan C giving 1,000 mL IV Ringer\u0027s Lactate"],"a":1,"e":"Under IMNCI, \u003e= 2 of the following—restless/irritable, sunken eyes, drinks eagerly/thirsty, skin pinch goes back slowly—classify the child as \u0027Some Dehydration\u0027. Treatment is IMNCI Plan B: 75 mL/kg of WHO low-osmolarity ORS over 4 hours in the clinic (75 x 10 kg = 750 mL), plus oral zinc 20 mg/day for 14 days (10 mg if \u003c6 months) and continued feeding.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02596","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Neonatal Hyperbilirubinaemia","q":"A full-term neonate (birth weight 3.1 kg, blood group A positive, mother O positive) develops visible icterus extending to the trunk at 14 hours of life with total serum bilirubin 13.5 mg/dL (direct 0.8 mg/dL). Intensive blue-green LED phototherapy (460-490 nm) is started immediately. By what primary mechanism does phototherapy eliminate unconjugated bilirubin?","o":["Chelation of bilirubin in the intestinal lumen to block enterohepatic circulation","Direct hepatic induction of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase enzyme","Structural photoisomerisation of unconjugated bilirubin into water-soluble lumirubin excreted in bile and urine","Conversion of indirect bilirubin into biliverdin inside erythrocytes"],"a":2,"e":"Jaundice appearing within 24 hours of life is always pathological (here likely ABO incompatibility). Phototherapy using blue-green light (460-490 nm) converts toxic, lipophilic unconjugated bilirubin in skin capillaries into water-soluble photoisomers—primarily lumirubin (via irreversible structural photoisomerisation, the main route of excretion) and configurational isomers—which are excreted in bile and urine without hepatic conjugation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02597","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Acute Rheumatic Fever","q":"A 10-year-old child in rural Uttar Pradesh presents with painful swelling of the right knee (monoarthritis) for 3 days, fever of 38.2 degrees C, ESR 42 mm/1st hour, prolonged PR interval on ECG, and an elevated ASO titre (480 IU/mL) following pharyngitis 3 weeks ago. According to the 2015 revised Jones criteria for moderate/high-risk populations (applicable to India), does this patient meet diagnostic criteria for initial Acute Rheumatic Fever?","o":["Yes; because elevated ASO titre alone is a Major criterion","No; ESR must be \u003e= 60 mm/1st hour and fever \u003e= 38.5 C in all risk groups","No; polyarthritis affecting at least 4 joints is mandatory as a Major criterion in all populations","Yes; meets 1 Major (monoarthritis) + 2 Minor (fever \u003e= 38.0 C, ESR \u003e= 30 mm/h, prolonged PR) + GAS evidence"],"a":3,"e":"Under the 2015 revised AHA Jones criteria for moderate-to-high risk populations (including India), monoarthritis or polyarthralgia qualifies as a Major criterion (whereas low-risk populations require polyarthritis). Furthermore, the minor thresholds are lowered to fever \u003e= 38.0 degrees C and ESR \u003e= 30 mm/h (or CRP \u003e= 3.0 mg/dL), plus prolonged PR interval. With positive ASO titre confirming preceding Group A Streptococcal infection, 1 Major + 3 Minor confirms Acute Rheumatic Fever.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02598","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM)","q":"A 2-year-old child weighing 7.5 kg presents with bilateral pitting pedal oedema, apathy, moon facies, \u0027flaky-paint\u0027 hyperpigmented desquamating dermatosis, sparse hypopigmented hair (\u0027flag sign\u0027), and a smooth enlarged liver. Which feature most reliably differentiates this condition (kwashiorkor) from marasmus?","o":["Nutritional bilateral pitting oedema with hypoalbuminaemia and fatty hepatomegaly","Severe loss of subcutaneous buccal fat pad (\u0027monkey facies\u0027)","Alertness and voracious appetite","Weight-for-height Z-score below -3 SD with preserved appetite"],"a":0,"e":"Kwashiorkor is characterised by bilateral pitting oedema, marked hypoalbuminaemia, fatty infiltration of the liver (hepatomegaly due to impaired VLDL/apolipoprotein synthesis), apathy, skin changes (flaky-paint dermatosis), and hair changes (flag sign). In contrast, marasmus presents with severe non-oedematous wasting, loss of subcutaneous fat (baggy-pants appearance, monkey facies), alertness, and good appetite.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02599","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Brainstem Vascular Syndromes","q":"A 58-year-old hypertensive man develops acute vertigo, dysphagia, hoarseness, ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature over the left face, contralateral loss of pain and temperature over the right trunk and limbs, left-sided ataxia, and left Horner syndrome, without tongue deviation or limb weakness. Occlusion of which artery and involvement of which motor nucleus explain the dysphagia and hoarseness?","o":["Anterior spinal artery; hypoglossal nucleus","Posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA); nucleus ambiguus","Superior cerebellar artery; trochlear nucleus","Basilar artery paramedian branches; facial motor nucleus"],"a":1,"e":"Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome results from occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA) or vertebral artery supplying the dorsolateral medulla. Dysphagia, hoarseness, and diminished ipsilateral gag reflex occur specifically due to ischemia of the nucleus ambiguus (which supplies somatic motor fibers via CN IX and X to pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles). Preservation of the medial medullary pyramids and hypoglossal nucleus explains the absence of contralateral hemiparesis or tongue deviation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02600","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Spinal Cord Tracts","q":"A young man sustains a stab wound causing clean hemisection of the right half of the spinal cord at the T8 vertebral level. Below the lesion, which combination of neurological deficits is expected?","o":["Bilateral flaccid paralysis of lower limbs with preserved pain and temperature sensation","Contralateral (left) spastic paralysis and loss of vibration/proprioception, with ipsilateral (right) loss of pain and temperature","Ipsilateral (right) spastic paralysis and loss of vibration/proprioception, with contralateral (left) loss of pain and temperature starting 1-2 segments below T8","Ipsilateral (right) loss of pain and temperature with preserved motor power and proprioception"],"a":2,"e":"In Brown-Séquard (cord hemisection) syndrome at T8: (1) interruption of the right lateral corticospinal tract (which already decussated in the medullary pyramids) causes ipsilateral UMN spastic paralysis below T8; (2) interruption of the right dorsal columns (fasciculus gracilis) causes ipsilateral loss of fine touch, vibration, and conscious proprioception below T8; and (3) interruption of the right lateral spinothalamic tract (which carries fibers from the left side that ascended 1-2 segments in Lissauer tract before decussating in the anterior white commissure) causes contralateral (left) loss of pain and temperature 1-2 dermatomes below the lesion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02601","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Cranial Nerve VII (Facial Nerve)","q":"A 34-year-old man presents with paralysis of both upper and lower muscles of facial expression on the right side, hyperacusis in the right ear, and loss of taste from the anterior two-thirds of the right side of the tongue, while Schirmer testing shows normal lacrimation in both eyes. Where is the facial nerve lesion localized?","o":["At the stylomastoid foramen distal to the chorda tympani","In the left primary motor cortex (supranuclear corticobulbar lesion)","Proximal to the geniculate ganglion inside the internal acoustic meatus","Within the facial canal distal to the geniculate ganglion (greater petrosal nerve branch) and proximal to the nerve to stapedius"],"a":3,"e":"Along the intrapetrous course of CN VII: the greater petrosal nerve (parasympathetic secretomotor to the lacrimal gland) branches off first at the geniculate ganglion; next comes the nerve to stapedius (dampens loud sounds); then the chorda tympani (taste to anterior two-thirds of tongue and secretomotor to submandibular/sublingual glands); and finally CN VII exits the stylomastoid foramen to supply facial expression muscles. Normal lacrimation with hyperacusis, taste loss, and ipsilateral facial palsy localizes the lesion distal to the geniculate ganglion and proximal to the nerve to stapedius.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02602","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Basal Ganglia","q":"A small lacunar hemorrhage destroying the left subthalamic nucleus causes sudden, violent, involuntary flinging movements of the right upper and lower extremities (contralateral hemiballismus). Disruption of which functional pathway explains this hyperkinetic state?","o":["Loss of excitatory glutamatergic drive from the subthalamic nucleus to the globus pallidus internus (GPi), reducing thalamic inhibition in the indirect pathway","Overactivation of GABAergic inhibition from the striatum to the globus pallidus externus","Loss of cerebellar dentate nucleus input to the contralateral red nucleus","Destruction of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta projecting to the caudate"],"a":0,"e":"The subthalamic nucleus (STN) is the sole glutamatergic (excitatory) nucleus of the basal ganglia and forms a key relay of the indirect pathway. Normally, STN excites the globus pallidus internus (GPi)/substantia nigra pars reticulata (SNr), which tonically inhibit the ventral anterior/ventrolateral thalamus to suppress unwanted movements. Destruction of the left STN removes excitatory input to the left GPi, disinhibiting the left thalamus and driving excessive right-sided motor cortex activity (contralateral hemiballismus).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02603","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Internal Capsule","q":"A hypertensive patient suffers a pure motor hemiparesis affecting the contralateral face, arm, and leg equally after rupture/occlusion of a lateral lenticulostriate artery (Charcot\u0027s artery of cerebral hemorrhage). Which subdivisions of the internal capsule carry the corticobulbar and corticospinal tracts, and from which parent vessel do the lateral lenticulostriate arteries arise?","o":["Anterior limb (corticospinal) and retrolentiform part (corticobulbar); posterior cerebral artery","Genu (corticobulbar) and posterior limb (corticospinal); middle cerebral artery","Posterior limb (auditory radiation) and anterior limb (corticobulbar); vertebral artery","Genu (optic radiation) and sublentiform part (corticospinal); anterior communicating artery"],"a":1,"e":"In horizontal section, the internal capsule has five parts: anterior limb (frontopontine and anterior thalamic radiation), genu (corticobulbar/corticonuclear fibers to cranial nerve nuclei), posterior limb (corticospinal tract in anterior two-thirds and superior thalamic radiation/sensory fibers posteriorly), retrolentiform part (optic radiation), and sublentiform part (auditory radiation). The lateral lenticulostriate arteries—penetrating end-arteries from the M1 segment of the middle cerebral artery—supply the genu and posterior limb, making them classic sites for lacunar strokes and hypertensive hemorrhage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02604","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Cerebellum","q":"From lateral to medial, which sequence lists the four deep cerebellar nuclei, and through which peduncle do the major efferent fibers from the dentate nucleus exit to reach the contralateral red nucleus and VL thalamus?","o":["Globose, dentate, fastigial, emboliform; superior cerebellar peduncle","Dentate, fastigial, globose, emboliform; middle cerebellar peduncle","Dentate, emboliform, globose, fastigial; superior cerebellar peduncle","Fastigial, globose, emboliform, dentate; inferior cerebellar peduncle"],"a":2,"e":"The four paired deep cerebellar nuclei are arranged from lateral to medial as Dentate (neocerebellum/cerebrocerebellum), Emboliform, Globose (together forming the interposed nucleus for spinocerebellum), and Fastigial (vestibulocerebellum), easily remembered by \u0027Don\u0027t Eat Greasy Food\u0027. Major efferents from the dentate and interposed nuclei exit via the superior cerebellar peduncle (brachium conjunctivum), decussate in the caudal midbrain (Wernekink commissure), and synapse in the contralateral red nucleus and ventrolateral (VL) thalamic nucleus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02605","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Blood-Brain Barrier vs Blood-CSF Barrier","q":"Assertion (A): Circulating macromolecules and polar drugs readily cross the fenestrated capillary endothelium inside the choroid plexus stroma, yet are still prevented from freely entering the ventricular cerebrospinal fluid. Reason (R): The structural diffusion gate of the blood-CSF barrier is formed by zonula occludens (tight junctions) between adjacent apical choroid plexus epithelial cells, unlike the blood-brain barrier where tight junctions seal non-fenestrated brain capillary endothelial cells.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"At the blood-brain barrier (BBB), continuous non-fenestrated brain capillary endothelial cells joined by high-resistance tight junctions (zonulae occludentes), surrounded by basement membrane, pericytes, and astrocytic end-feet, restrict paracellular entry. In contrast, capillaries within the choroid plexus villi are fenestrated and leaky; however, the overlying cuboidal choroid plexus ependymal/epithelial cells are sealed near their apical surfaces by tight junctions, constituting the true anatomical site of the blood-CSF barrier.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02606","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Pudendal Nerve and Alcock Canal","q":"Prior to performing an episiotomy repair, a clinician palpates a prominent bony landmark through the lateral vaginal wall to administer a transvaginal pudendal nerve block. Which bony landmark and adjacent ligament guide needle placement to anesthetize the S2-S4 nerve before it enters the pudendal (Alcock) canal?","o":["Pubic tubercle and inguinal ligament","Ischial tuberosity and sacrotuberous ligament only (anterior to the obturator foramen)","Sacral promontory and iliolumbar ligament","Ischial spine and sacrospinous ligament"],"a":3,"e":"The pudendal nerve (S2, S3, S4) exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, hooks posteriorly around the ischial spine and sacrospinous ligament (just medial to the internal pudendal vessels), and re-enters the perineum via the lesser sciatic foramen to run inside the pudendal (Alcock) canal in the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa. Palpating the ischial spine transvaginally and injecting local anesthetic just posterior-medial to it blocks the inferior rectal, perineal, and dorsal nerves of the clitoris/penis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02607","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Mediastinum and Thoracic Duct","q":"A patient develops left-sided chylothorax following thoracic esophageal surgery. In the posterior mediastinum, where does the thoracic duct lie relative to the thoracic aorta and azygos vein, at what vertebral level does it cross from right to left, and where does it terminate?","o":["Between the thoracic aorta (left) and azygos vein (right) posterior to the esophagus; crosses at T5; terminates at the left venous angle (junction of left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)","Anterior to the pericardium in the middle mediastinum; crosses at T10; terminates into the right atrium","Posterior to the vertebral bodies; crosses at L1; terminates into the azygos arch","Lateral to the left phrenic nerve; crosses at T2; terminates into the superior vena cava"],"a":0,"e":"Originating from the cisterna chyli at L1-L2, the thoracic duct enters the posterior mediastinum through the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm (T12) between the descending thoracic aorta on its left and the azygos vein on its right, lying immediately posterior to the esophagus. At the level of T5 (sternal angle plane), it crosses obliquely to the left behind the esophagus, ascends through the superior mediastinum into the root of the neck, and arches over the left subclavian artery to drain into the left jugulosubclavian venous angle. Injury below T5 typically causes right chylothorax, whereas injury above T5 causes left chylothorax.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02608","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Urine Concentration and Countercurrent Mechanism","q":"Administration of furosemide (a loop diuretic) abolishes the corticomedullary osmotic gradient and impairs both maximal urine concentration and maximal urine dilution. Inhibition of which nephron segment and transporter directly disrupts the single-effect step of the countercurrent multiplier?","o":["Proximal convoluted tubule; SGLT2 Na+-glucose cotransporter","Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle; Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2)","Cortical collecting duct principal cells; aquaporin-2 water channels","Thin descending limb of the loop of Henle; aquaporin-1 water channels"],"a":1,"e":"The thick ascending limb (TAL) of the loop of Henle is water-impermeable and actively reabsorbs Na+, K+, and Cl- into the medullary interstitium via the luminal NKCC2 cotransporter. This active solute transport without water generates the \u0027single effect\u0027 (~200 mOsm/L gradient at each level) amplified by countercurrent multiplication to reach ~1200 mOsm/L in the inner medulla, while simultaneously diluting tubular fluid entering the distal nephron. Blocking NKCC2 with furosemide collapses both diluting capacity and the medullary hyperosmolar gradient required for ADH-mediated water reabsorption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02609","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Renal Blood Flow and Clearance","q":"During a renal physiology clearance study using para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) at low plasma concentration, a subject has a urine PAH concentration (U_PAH) of 14 mg/mL, urine flow rate (V) of 1.5 mL/min, plasma PAH concentration (P_PAH) of 0.035 mg/mL, and hematocrit of 40% (0.40). Assuming complete renal extraction of PAH (effective RPF = C_PAH), what are the effective Renal Plasma Flow (RPF) and Renal Blood Flow (RBF)?","o":["RPF = 600 mL/min; RBF = 840 mL/min","RPF = 400 mL/min; RBF = 667 mL/min","RPF = 600 mL/min; RBF = 1,000 mL/min","RPF = 1,000 mL/min; RBF = 1,667 mL/min"],"a":2,"e":"PAH is both freely filtered and actively secreted by proximal tubular organic anion transporters so that virtually all PAH in plasma perfusing the kidneys is cleared in a single pass. Effective RPF = C_PAH = (U_PAH x V) / P_PAH = (14 mg/mL x 1.5 mL/min) / 0.035 mg/mL = 21 / 0.035 = 600 mL/min. Renal Blood Flow is then derived from RPF and hematocrit (Hct): RBF = RPF / (1 - Hct) = 600 / (1 - 0.40) = 600 / 0.60 = 1,000 mL/min.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02610","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Renal Tubular Acidosis (RTA)","q":"A 29-year-old woman with Sjögren syndrome presents with muscle weakness, bilateral nephrocalcinosis on ultrasound, normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis, serum potassium 2.8 mEq/L, and a persistently alkaline urine pH of 6.8 even during severe acidemia. Which tubular defect accounts for her findings?","o":["Excessive carbonic anhydrase activity in the thick ascending limb","Defective HCO3- reabsorption in the proximal convoluted tubule (Type 2 proximal RTA)","Aldosterone deficiency or resistance in principal/intercalated cells causing hyperkalemia (Type 4 RTA)","Impaired H+ secretion by alpha-intercalated cells in the cortical/medullary collecting duct (Type 1 distal RTA)"],"a":3,"e":"Type 1 (distal) renal tubular acidosis—classically associated with autoimmune disorders such as Sjögren syndrome or SLE—results from failure of collecting duct alpha-intercalated cells (H+-ATPase or H+/K+-ATPase) to secrete H+ into urine. Because new HCO3- cannot be generated and urine cannot be acidified below pH 5.5 (remaining \u003e5.5 even in acidemia), patients develop normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, hypokalemia (increased K+ wasting), and hypercalciuria with hypocitraturia causing calcium phosphate kidney stones and nephrocalcinosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02611","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Gastrointestinal Motility","q":"Which pacemaker cells generate the intrinsic basal electrical rhythm (slow waves) across the gastrointestinal smooth muscle, and which hormone initiates the interdigestive migrating motor complex (MMC) every ~90 minutes during fasting?","o":["Interstitial cells of Cajal; motilin","Submucosal Meissner plexus cells; secretin","Enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells; gastrin","Myenteric Auerbach neurons; cholecystokinin (CCK)"],"a":0,"e":"Interstitial cells of Cajal (ICCs), situated between the circular and longitudinal smooth muscle layers and connected via gap junctions, act as the electrical pacemakers of the GI tract by generating oscillating slow-wave depolarizations (frequency ~3/min in stomach, ~12/min in duodenum, ~8-9/min in ileum). During the fasting state, periodic surges of motilin from duodenal/jejunal M cells trigger Phase III of the migrating motor complex (MMC)—a \u0027housekeeper\u0027 wave sweeping undigested debris and bacteria from stomach to terminal ileum.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02612","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Gastric Acid Secretion","q":"Assertion (A): H2-receptor antagonists (such as famotidine) substantially blunt gastric acid secretion stimulated not only by histamine, but also by gastrin and vagal acetylcholine. Reason (R): Gastrin and acetylcholine stimulate parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase primarily via Gq-IP3/Ca2+ signaling, and a major component of gastrin\u0027s physiological action is mediated indirectly by releasing histamine from neighboring enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells, which acts via Gs-cAMP on parietal cells.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Three primary secretagogues stimulate gastric parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase: acetylcholine (M3 -\u003e Gq/Ca2+), gastrin (CCK-B -\u003e Gq/Ca2+), and histamine (H2 -\u003e Gs/cAMP). Because gastrin potently stimulates ECL cells to release histamine and because cAMP potentiates Ca2+-mediated acid secretion inside parietal cells, blocking H2 receptors markedly diminishes the maximal acid response to both gastrin and vagal acetylcholine as well as histamine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02613","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Pancreatic Exocrine Secretion","q":"When acidic chyme and partially digested fats/peptides enter the duodenum, two duodenal hormones coordinate pancreatic secretion. How do secretin and cholecystokinin (CCK) differ in their cellular targets and physiological effects on pancreatic juice, and which brush-border enzyme initiates the intraluminal zymogen cascade?","o":["Secretin stimulates pancreatic ductal cells (via cAMP) to secrete HCO3--rich watery fluid; CCK stimulates acinar cells (via IP3/Ca2+) to secrete digestive enzymes; enteropeptidase (enterokinase) activates trypsinogen to trypsin","Secretin stimulates acinar cells to secrete amylase; CCK inhibits gallbladder contraction; pepsin activates trypsinogen","Secretin stimulates parietal cells to secrete HCl; CCK stimulates ductal HCO3- secretion; chymotrypsin initiates the cascade","Both secretin and CCK act exclusively via somatostatin release to suppress pancreatic flow; salivary lipase activates procarboxypeptidase"],"a":0,"e":"Acid (H+) in the duodenum stimulates S cells to release secretin, which acts via Gs-cAMP on pancreatic centroacinar and ductal epithelial cells (driving CFTR Cl- efflux coupled to apical Cl-/HCO3- exchange) to produce an alkaline, bicarbonate-rich secretion that neutralizes gastric acid. Fatty acids and amino acids stimulate duodenal I cells to release CCK, which acts via Gq-IP3/Ca2+ on pancreatic acinar cells to release enzyme-rich zymogen granules (and contracts the gallbladder while relaxing the sphincter of Oddi). Duodenal brush-border enteropeptidase (enterokinase) cleaves trypsinogen into active trypsin, which then activates all other pancreatic zymogens.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02614","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Thyroid Gland","q":"A patient with multinodular goiter receives a large intravenous iodinated contrast load and transiently suppresses thyroid hormone synthesis (Wolff-Chaikoff effect). Which thyroid enzyme and apical transporter normally mediate iodide efflux into the colloid, oxidation, organification onto thyroglobulin, and coupling?","o":["Thyroid-binding globulin (TBG) protease in the colloid","Pendrin (apical Cl-/I- exchanger) and thyroid peroxidase (TPO)","Sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) alone without enzymatic oxidation","5\u0027-deiodinase type 1 inside the follicular lumen"],"a":1,"e":"Thyroid follicular cells actively take up circulating iodide across the basolateral membrane via the Na+/I- symporter (NIS), then transport iodide across the apical membrane into the follicular lumen via pendrin. Inside the colloid, membrane-bound thyroid peroxidase (TPO) uses H2O2 to catalyze three sequential steps: (1) oxidation of I- to reactive iodine, (2) organification (iodination of thyroglobulin tyrosyl residues to MIT and DIT), and (3) coupling of MIT/DIT into T3 and T4. Acute excess iodide inhibits TPO-mediated organification and NIS expression (Wolff-Chaikoff effect).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02615","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Adrenal Cortex","q":"A 10-day-old 46,XX newborn presents with ambiguous genitalia (clitoromegaly and labial fusion), persistent vomiting, dehydration, hypotension, hyponatremia (122 mEq/L), hyperkalemia (6.4 mEq/L), and elevated plasma renin activity. Serum 17-hydroxyprogesterone is markedly elevated. Deficiency of which adrenal enzyme explains both the salt-wasting crisis and female virilization?","o":["Aromatase (CYP19A1)","17-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP17A1)","21-hydroxylase (CYP21A2)","11-beta-hydroxylase (CYP11B1)"],"a":2,"e":"21-hydroxylase deficiency accounts for \u003e90% of congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) cases. Blocking 21-hydroxylation impairs synthesis of both aldosterone (causing renal Na+ loss, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and hypovolemic shock) and cortisol (removing negative feedback on pituitary ACTH). Excess ACTH drives adrenal hyperplasia and shunts accumulated 17-hydroxyprogesterone into adrenal androgen synthesis (DHEA, androstenedione, testosterone), causing virilization of 46,XX fetuses.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02616","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Calcium and Phosphate Homeostasis","q":"During an endocrine physiology case lab, a 52-year-old woman\u0027s fasting biochemical panel shows corrected serum calcium 12.1 mg/dL (elevated), serum phosphate 2.1 mg/dL (low), elevated intact PTH (148 pg/mL), elevated urinary cAMP, and elevated serum 1,25-(OH)2 vitamin D (calcitriol). Which physiological mechanism explains the low phosphate and elevated calcitriol?","o":["Humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy (PTHrP): intact PTH is markedly suppressed below normal","Vitamin D intoxication: excess exogenous vitamin D directly suppresses PTH and raises serum phosphate","Hypoparathyroidism: impaired distal Ca2+ reabsorption lowers calcitriol synthesis","Primary hyperparathyroidism: PTH inhibits proximal tubular Na+-phosphate cotransport (NaPi-IIa/IIc) and stimulates proximal tubular 1-alpha-hydroxylase"],"a":3,"e":"In primary hyperparathyroidism, autonomous parathyroid adenoma secretion of PTH raises serum calcium by enhancing osteoclastic bone resorption (via RANKL), increasing distal convoluted tubule Ca2+ reabsorption, and stimulating proximal tubular 1-alpha-hydroxylase to convert 25-(OH)D into active 1,25-(OH)2D (calcitriol), which enhances intestinal Ca2+ absorption. Simultaneously, PTH activates PKA/PKC via Gs/Gq in proximal tubule cells—raising urinary cAMP—and internalizes apical NaPi-IIa/IIc cotransporters, producing phosphaturia and hypophosphatemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02617","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Enzyme Kinetics and Inhibition","q":"In an enzyme kinetics practical, adding Drug X to an enzyme assay causes the Lineweaver-Burk (1/v vs 1/[S]) double-reciprocal plot to shift so that the y-intercept (1/Vmax) remains unchanged while the x-intercept (-1/Km) moves closer to zero (less negative). What type of inhibition does Drug X exhibit, and what happens to apparent Km and Vmax?","o":["Competitive inhibition; apparent Km increases while Vmax remains unchanged","Non-competitive inhibition; Vmax decreases while Km remains unchanged","Uncompetitive inhibition; both Km and Vmax decrease proportionally","Irreversible suicide inhibition; Vmax increases while Km decreases"],"a":0,"e":"On a Lineweaver-Burk plot, the y-intercept equals 1/Vmax and the x-intercept equals -1/Km. In competitive inhibition (e.g., statins on HMG-CoA reductase, methotrexate on DHFR), the inhibitor competes reversibly with substrate at the active site; high [S] can outcompete the inhibitor, so Vmax (and y-intercept 1/Vmax) is unchanged. However, more substrate is needed to achieve half-maximal velocity, increasing apparent Km and shifting -1/Km rightward toward zero.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02618","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Michaelis-Menten Kinetics","q":"An enzyme obeys Michaelis-Menten kinetics with a Vmax of 120 micromol/min and a Km of 4 mM. What is the initial reaction velocity (v0) when the substrate concentration [S] is 12 mM?","o":["90 micromol/min","30 micromol/min","60 micromol/min","100 micromol/min"],"a":0,"e":"According to the Michaelis-Menten equation, v0 = (Vmax x [S]) / (Km + [S]). Substituting Vmax = 120 micromol/min, [S] = 12 mM, and Km = 4 mM gives: v0 = (120 x 12) / (4 + 12) = 1440 / 16 = 90 micromol/min (i.e., 75% of Vmax when [S] = 3 x Km).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02619","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Inborn Errors of Amino Acid Metabolism","q":"A 6-month-old infant with fair skin, blue eyes, eczema, global developmental delay, and a \u0027musty\u0027 mousy body odor tests positive on Guthrie bacterial inhibition assay and shows dark green urine on ferric chloride addition. In classic phenylketonuria (PKU), which amino acid becomes nutritionally essential, and why must maternal PKU be strictly controlled before conception?","o":["Tryptophan becomes essential; accumulated phenylketones inhibit fetal urea cycle","Tyrosine becomes essential; high maternal phenylalanine crosses the placenta and causes fetal microcephaly, intellectual disability, and congenital heart defects","Methionine becomes essential; maternal tetrahydrobiopterin excess causes neonatal hypoglycemia","Cysteine becomes essential; accumulated homogentisic acid stains fetal cartilage"],"a":1,"e":"Classic PKU is caused by autosomal recessive deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH), preventing hydroxylation of phenylalanine to tyrosine (rendering tyrosine a conditionally essential dietary amino acid). Excess phenylalanine is transaminated to phenylpyruvate, phenyllactate, and phenylacetate (imparting a musty odor and reacting green with FeCl3). In pregnant women with poorly controlled PKU, high maternal phenylalanine is teratogenic across the placenta, causing maternal PKU syndrome (fetal microcephaly, growth restriction, intellectual disability, and congenital heart defects) even in genetically heterozygous fetuses.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02620","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Branched-Chain Amino Acid Disorders","q":"A 5-day-old neonate develops poor feeding, vomiting, alternating hypertonia and hypotonia, seizures, and urine smelling like burnt sugar or maple syrup. Deficiency of which mitochondrial enzyme complex causes this disorder, and which amino acids accumulate?","o":["Cystathionine beta-synthase; homocysteine and methionine","Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase; tyrosine and succinylacetone","Branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase (BCKDH) complex; isoleucine, leucine, and valine","Homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase; homogentisic acid"],"a":2,"e":"Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD) results from autosomal recessive deficiency of the branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase (BCKDH) complex, which requires five cofactors (thiamine pyrophosphate, lipoate, CoA, FAD, NAD+—identical to pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase). Blocked oxidative decarboxylation causes toxic accumulation of branched-chain amino acids (leucine, isoleucine, valine) and their alpha-ketoacids in blood and urine, producing cerebral edema and a sweet maple-syrup odor.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02621","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Sulfur-Containing Amino Acid Disorders","q":"A 12-year-old boy with tall stature, arachnodactyly, pectus excavatum, intellectual disability, and a history of deep vein thrombosis presents with bilateral inferonasal (downward and inward) ectopia lentis. High-dose pyridoxine (vitamin B6) significantly lowers his elevated plasma homocysteine and methionine. Which enzyme is defective, and how does the lens dislocation direction contrast with Marfan syndrome?","o":["Propionyl-CoA carboxylase; homocystinuria causes upward lens dislocation and megaloblastic anemia","Methionine synthase; homocystinuria causes upward lens dislocation and low plasma methionine","Fibrillin-1 (FBN1); homocystinuria causes aortic root dilation without thrombosis","Cystathionine beta-synthase (CBS); homocystinuria causes inferonasal (downward) lens dislocation, whereas Marfan syndrome causes superotemporal (upward) lens dislocation"],"a":3,"e":"Classic homocystinuria is caused by autosomal recessive cystathionine beta-synthase (CBS) deficiency, which uses pyridoxal phosphate (PLP, vitamin B6) to condense homocysteine and serine into cystathionine. Accumulation of homocysteine (and remethylated methionine) damages vascular endothelium (causing early arterial/venous thromboembolism), causes intellectual disability, and weakens zonular fibers (producing downward/inferonasal ectopia lentis). Marfan syndrome (autosomal dominant FBN1 mutation) shares marfanoid habitus but lacks intellectual disability/thrombophilia and characteristically shows upward/superotemporal lens dislocation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02622","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Glycogen Storage Diseases","q":"A 4-month-old infant presents with doll-like facies, massive hepatomegaly, severe fasting hypoglycemia that fails to respond to glucagon, lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia, and hypertriglyceridemia. Which enzyme is deficient, and why does hyperuricemia occur?","o":["Glucose-6-phosphatase (Von Gierke disease, GSD Type Ia); lactate competes with urate at renal proximal tubular organic acid transporters and excess G6P enters the HMP shunt increasing purine synthesis","Muscle glycogen phosphorylase (McArdle disease, GSD Type V); muscle cramps prevent hepatic gluconeogenesis","Acid alpha-glucosidase (Pompe disease, GSD Type II); lysosomal glycogen blocks renal uric acid excretion","Debranching enzyme (Cori disease, GSD Type III); glucagon stimulates excess glycogenolysis"],"a":0,"e":"Von Gierke disease (GSD Type Ia) results from deficiency of hepatic/renal glucose-6-phosphatase, blocking the final common step of both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. Trapped glucose-6-phosphate is shunted into (1) glycolysis—generating severe lactic acidosis (which competitively impairs renal urate excretion)—and (2) the pentose phosphate pathway—increasing ribose-5-phosphate, PRPP, de novo purine synthesis, and degradation to uric acid (hyperuricemia/gout). Because hepatocytes cannot export free glucose, blood glucose does not rise after glucagon or epinephrine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02623","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Sphingolipidoses (Lysosomal Storage Disorders)","q":"An Ashkenazi Jewish infant develops progressive developmental regression, exaggerated startle response to noise, muscular hypotonia, and a macular \u0027cherry-red spot\u0027 on fundoscopy, with NO hepatosplenomegaly. Which enzyme is deficient, and which sphingolipid accumulates in neuronal lysosomes?","o":["Sphingomyelinase; sphingomyelin (Niemann-Pick disease)","Hexosaminidase A; GM2 ganglioside (Tay-Sachs disease)","Beta-glucocerebrosidase; glucocerebroside (Gaucher disease)","Alpha-galactosidase A; ceramide trihexoside (Fabry disease)"],"a":1,"e":"Tay-Sachs disease is an autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disorder caused by hexosaminidase A (HEXA) deficiency, leading to GM2 ganglioside accumulation in neuronal lysosomes (appearing as onion-skin whorls on EM). Destruction of retinal ganglion cells surrounding the fovea exposes the underlying vascular choroid as a macular \u0027cherry-red spot\u0027. Crucially, unlike Niemann-Pick disease (which also shows a cherry-red spot), Tay-Sachs disease lacks hepatosplenomegaly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02624","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Galactose Metabolism","q":"Assertion (A): A neonate with classic galactosemia (galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase deficiency) who is fed breast milk develops jaundice, hepatomegaly, bilateral cataracts, positive urinary Benedict reducing-substance test, and negative urine glucose oxidase dipstick. Reason (R): Accumulated galactose is reduced by aldose reductase in the lens to osmotically active galactitol, while galactose-1-phosphate accumulation injures hepatocytes and renal tubules, and galactose is a reducing sugar not detected by glucose-specific oxidase strips.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"In classic galactosemia (GALT deficiency), lactose from breast milk or cow\u0027s milk formula provides galactose, which is phosphorylated by galactokinase to galactose-1-phosphate (Gal-1-P) but cannot proceed further. Gal-1-P traps inorganic phosphate and damages liver, kidney, and brain (predisposing also to neonatal E. coli sepsis), while excess galactose enters the polyol pathway via aldose reductase to form galactitol, drawing water into the lens and causing cataracts. Unabsorbed/excreted galactose reduces Cu2+ in Benedict\u0027s test (positive reducing substance) while remaining negative on glucose oxidase dipsticks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02625","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Porphyrias","q":"A 26-year-old woman given phenobarbital for seizures develops severe colicky abdominal pain, peripheral motor neuropathy, psychosis, hyponatremia, and urine that turns dark port-wine color upon standing in light, WITHOUT any cutaneous photosensitivity. Which heme synthesis enzyme is deficient, and why do cytochrome P450 inducers precipitate acute attacks?","o":["Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase; CYP450 inducers cause accumulation of photoactive uroporphyrins in skin","Ferrochelatase; CYP450 inducers cause protoporphyrin IX photosensitivity","Porphobilinogen (PBG) deaminase (hydroxymethylbilane synthase); CYP450 inducers increase hepatic heme demand, derepressing ALA synthase-1 and accumulating neurotoxic ALA and PBG","ALA dehydratase only; phenobarbital inhibits ALA synthase-1"],"a":2,"e":"Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP) is an autosomal dominant deficiency of porphobilinogen (PBG) deaminase (hydroxymethylbilane synthase). Because the block occurs early before tetrapyrrole ring formation, photosensitive porphyrins do NOT accumulate—explaining the absence of skin blistering. Drugs inducing hepatic CYP450 hemoproteins (barbiturates, phenytoin, sulfonamides, rifampicin) or fasting deplete the regulatory free heme pool, lifting negative feedback on rate-limiting ALA synthase-1 and flooding the body with neurotoxic delta-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and PBG (which polymerizes non-enzymatically in urine to dark porphobilin). IV hemin and glucose repress ALA synthase-1 to treat attacks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02626","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Microcytic Anemias","q":"A 24-year-old asymptomatic woman undergoes pre-marital screening and has Hemoglobin 11.2 g/dL, MCV 62 fL, RBC count 5.8 million/microL, RDW 12.8% (normal), and normal serum ferritin. Calculate her Mentzer index and identify the most likely diagnosis.","o":["Mentzer index = 19.3 (\u003e13); beta-thalassemia trait","Mentzer index = 19.3 (\u003e13); anemia of chronic disease","Mentzer index = 10.7 (\u003c13); iron deficiency anemia","Mentzer index = 10.7 (\u003c13); beta-thalassemia trait"],"a":3,"e":"The Mentzer index is calculated as MCV (in fL) divided by RBC count (in millions/microL): 62 / 5.8 = 10.69 (~10.7). A Mentzer index \u003c13 strongly favors thalassemia trait (where bone marrow compensates with erythroid hyperplasia producing numerous small RBCs, normal RDW, normal ferritin, and elevated HbA2 \u003e3.5% on HPLC/electrophoresis). In iron deficiency anemia, marrow erythropoiesis is substrate-limited, lowering RBC count disproportionately so MCV/RBC is typically \u003e13 with elevated RDW and low serum ferritin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02627","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Hemolytic Anemias","q":"A 16-year-old boy with mild splenomegaly, unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, pigmented gallstones, and spherocytes lacking central pallor on peripheral smear undergoes laboratory evaluation. Which findings are expected on MCHC, osmotic fragility testing, and flow cytometric eosin-5-maleimide (EMA) binding test?","o":["Increased MCHC, increased osmotic fragility (lysis at higher saline concentrations), and decreased EMA fluorescence binding","Normal MCHC, positive direct Coombs (DAT) test, and increased CD55/CD59","Target cells with decreased osmotic fragility and positive sickling test","Decreased MCHC, decreased osmotic fragility, and increased EMA fluorescence binding"],"a":0,"e":"Hereditary spherocytosis results from autosomal dominant (most commonly) defects in RBC vertical membrane-cytoskeleton tethering proteins—ankyrin, band 3, spectrin, or protein 4.2. Loss of unsupported membrane microvesicles in splenic cords reduces surface-area-to-volume ratio, producing dehydrated, rigid spherocytes with elevated MCHC (\u003e36 g/dL). Because spherocytes cannot swell further in hypotonic saline, they lyse earlier (increased osmotic fragility). Flow cytometry with eosin-5-maleimide (EMA, which binds band 3/Rh-associated proteins) shows reduced mean fluorescence intensity and is the modern high-specificity screening test, while direct Coombs test is negative (distinguishing it from warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02628","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Plasma Cell Neoplasms","q":"A 64-year-old man presents with worsening lower back pain, fatigue, serum calcium 11.8 mg/dL, serum creatinine 2.6 mg/dL, normocytic anemia with prominent rouleaux formation, punched-out lytic skull lesions without surrounding sclerosis, and a sharp IgG-kappa M-spike on serum protein electrophoresis. What cytokine drives osteoclastic bone resorption here, and what causes the renal failure (\u0027myeloma kidney\u0027)?","o":["PTH-mediated osteitis fibrosa cystica; immune-complex crescentic glomerulonephritis","IL-6/RANKL-mediated osteoclastic activation (with osteoblast inhibition by DKK1); obstructive distal tubular casts formed by filtered Bence Jones free light chains binding Tamm-Horsfall mucoprotein","EPO excess; amyloid deposition exclusively in renal arterioles without tubular casts","TNF-beta-driven osteoblastic sclerosis; uric acid crystals alone without light chains"],"a":1,"e":"In multiple myeloma, clonal plasma cells (constituting \u003e=10% of bone marrow cells, often showing eccentric \u0027clock-face\u0027 chromatin, perinuclear halo, Dutcher/Russell bodies) secrete cytokines and stimulate marrow stromal IL-6 and RANKL expression while suppressing osteoblasts via DKK1—causing purely lytic (\u0027punched-out\u0027) bone lesions, hypercalcemia, and normal alkaline phosphatase (so bone scans are often cold). Excess monoclonal free kappa/lambda light chains (Bence Jones proteins) are filtered by glomeruli, overwhelm proximal tubular reabsorption, and co-aggregate with Tamm-Horsfall uromodulin in distal/collecting tubules to form dense eosinophilic, fractured, waxy casts surrounded by multinucleated giant cells (\u0027myeloma cast nephropathy\u0027).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02629","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)","q":"A 33-year-old woman presents with gingival bleeding, ecchymoses, prolonged PT/aPTT, hypofibrinogenemia, and elevated D-dimer. Peripheral smear and bone marrow show hypergranular abnormal promyelocytes containing bundles of needle-shaped azurophilic Auer rods (\u0027faggot cells\u0027) and strong myeloperoxidase positivity. Which cytogenetic abnormality is diagnostic, and what targeted differentiation therapy reverses the maturation arrest?","o":["t(8;21)(q22;q22) producing RUNX1-RUNX1T1; imatinib mesylate","t(12;21)(p13;q22) in pre-B lymphoblasts; hydroxyurea","t(15;17)(q24;q21) producing PML-RARA fusion; all-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) plus arsenic trioxide","inv(16)(p13q22) with abnormal marrow eosinophils; rituximab"],"a":2,"e":"Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL, FAB M3) is characterized by t(15;17)(q24;q21), which fuses the PML gene on chromosome 15 with the retinoic acid receptor-alpha (RARA) gene on chromosome 17. The PML-RARA fusion oncoprotein represses retinoic acid-responsive myeloid differentiation genes, arresting blasts at the promyelocyte stage; release of procoagulant/fibrinolytic granules triggers life-threatening disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). Pharmacologic doses of all-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) combined with arsenic trioxide (ATO) degrade PML-RARA and force promyelocytes to differentiate into mature neutrophils.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02630","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Valvular Heart Disease and Endocarditis","q":"At autopsy of a young woman with systemic lupus erythematosus, the mitral valve demonstrates multiple small (1-4 mm), sterile, warty pink vegetations distributed indiscriminately on BOTH the atrial and ventricular surfaces of the valve leaflets and along the chordae tendineae without significant valvular destruction. What are these lesions called?","o":["Acute Staphylococcus aureus infective endocarditis vegetations","Acute rheumatic fever verrucae","Non-bacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis vegetations","Libman-Sacks endocarditis vegetations"],"a":3,"e":"Libman-Sacks (atypical verrucous) endocarditis occurs in SLE (and antiphospholipid syndrome) as sterile immune-complex fibrinous vegetations (1-4 mm) that classically appear on BOTH surfaces of the mitral or tricuspid leaflets, in valve pockets, and on chordae tendineae, with hematoxylin bodies and minimal leaflet perforation. In contrast, acute rheumatic fever verrucae are tiny (1-2 mm) sterile rows strictly along the lines of valve closure; NBTE forms larger sterile thrombi along lines of closure in hypercoagulable/advanced malignancy states; and bacterial infective endocarditis produces large, bulky, destructive friable vegetations.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02631","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Alcoholic Liver Disease","q":"A liver biopsy from a chronic heavy alcohol consumer with AST 190 U/L and ALT 72 U/L shows hepatocyte ballooning degeneration, neutrophilic satellitosis around necrotic hepatocytes in centrilobular Zone 3, pericellular \u0027chicken-wire\u0027 perisinusoidal fibrosis, and ropey eosinophilic intracytoplasmic inclusions (Mallory-Denk bodies). What intermediate filament protein forms Mallory-Denk bodies, and why does Zone 3 suffer the earliest injury?","o":["Ubiquitinated cytokeratins 8 and 18; Zone 3 (pericentral) has the highest CYP2E1 activity and lowest oxygen tension","Vimentin and amyloid fibrils; Zone 3 has the highest portal venous oxygenation","Alpha-1 antitrypsin polymers; Zone 1 (periportal) receives the lowest blood oxygen","Desmin filaments; Zone 2 (midzonal) has the highest glutathione stores"],"a":0,"e":"Alcoholic steatohepatitis features macrovesicular steatosis, hepatocyte ballooning, neutrophilic lobular inflammation, perivenular/perisinusoidal \u0027chicken-wire\u0027 fibrosis (activated Ito/hepatic stellate cells in space of Disse), and Mallory-Denk bodies—tangled, eosinophilic cytoplasmic aggregates composed of ubiquitinated intermediate filaments cytokeratin 8 and 18 (also seen in NASH, Wilson disease, and PBC). Centrilobular Zone 3 hepatocytes surround the central vein—the distal end of the hepatic acinus with the lowest oxygen tension and highest concentration of ethanol-metabolizing CYP2E1—making them maximally vulnerable to acetaldehyde toxicity, ROS, and hypoxia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02632","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Thyroid Neoplasms","q":"Fine-needle aspiration and subsequent thyroidectomy of a painless thyroid nodule in a 35-year-old woman with prior neck irradiation reveals branching papillae lined by cells with optically clear (\u0027Orphan Annie eye\u0027) nuclei, intranuclear pseudoinclusions, longitudinal nuclear grooves, and laminated concentric calcifications (psammoma bodies). Which molecular alteration and primary metastatic route characterize this tumor?","o":["RAS mutation or PAX8-PPARG fusion; spreads primarily hematogenously to bone and lungs","BRAF V600E mutation or RET/PTC rearrangement; spreads primarily via cervical lymphatics","RET germline point mutation in parafollicular C cells; amyloid stroma","TP53 inactivation with giant pleomorphic spindle cells; rapid local airway invasion"],"a":1,"e":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma (PTC) is the most common thyroid malignancy (~80-85%) and is classically linked to ionizing radiation exposure. Diagnosis rests on characteristic nuclear features—optically clear \u0027Orphan Annie eye\u0027 chromatin, intranuclear cytoplasmic pseudoinclusions, and nuclear grooves—often accompanied by psammoma bodies. Molecularly, PTC is driven by MAP kinase pathway activation via BRAF V600E point mutations or RET/PTC rearrangements, and it metastasizes predominantly via regional cervical lymphatics while carrying an excellent prognosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02633","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Esophageal Pathology","q":"Assertion (A): Chronic gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) predisposes to adenocarcinoma of the distal one-third of the esophagus via Barrett esophagus. Reason (R): Persistent acid-bile injury induces replacement of normal distal esophageal stratified squamous epithelium by specialized intestinal-type columnar epithelium containing Alcian blue-positive goblet cells, which can progress through dysplasia to adenocarcinoma.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Barrett esophagus is an adaptive intestinal metaplasia arising in the distal esophagus from chronic GERD: the normal non-keratinizing stratified squamous epithelium is replaced by columnar mucosa containing true mucin-rich goblet cells (staining blue with Alcian blue at pH 2.5). This metaplastic epithelium carries a significantly increased risk of stepwise progression through low-grade and high-grade dysplasia to distal esophageal adenocarcinoma (whereas smoking, alcohol, achalasia, and caustic strictures predispose mainly to mid/upper-third squamous cell carcinoma).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02634","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Amyloidosis","q":"A patient with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis and nephrotic-range proteinuria undergoes a renal biopsy showing amorphous acellular eosinophilic mesangial and capillary-wall deposits that stain salmon-pink with Congo red and exhibit apple-green birefringence under polarized light. Which precursor protein forms these fibrils, and what secondary structure gives amyloid its staining property?","o":["Beta-2 microglobulin retained during hemodialysis; globular beta-barrel without fibrillar structure","Immunoglobulin lambda light chains synthesized by plasma cells; alpha-helical coiled-coil conformation","Serum amyloid A (SAA) synthesized by hepatocytes in response to IL-6/IL-1; cross-beta-pleated sheet conformation","Transthyretin (TTR) synthesized by choroid plexus; triple-helix collagen conformation"],"a":2,"e":"Regardless of precursor protein, all amyloid deposits share an identical insoluble fibrillar ultrastructure (7.5-10 nm non-branching fibrils) arranged in a cross-beta-pleated sheet conformation, which intercalates Congo red dye to produce diagnostic apple-green birefringence under polarized light. In chronic inflammatory diseases (rheumatoid arthritis, tuberculosis, bronchiectasis, osteomyelitis), macrophage-derived IL-1 and IL-6 drive hepatic acute-phase production of Serum Amyloid A (SAA), which is partially proteolyzed into AA amyloid (secondary/reactive systemic amyloidosis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02635","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Serotonin (5-HT) Pharmacology","q":"Which combination accurately pairs a serotonergic drug with its target 5-HT receptor subtype and primary clinical indication?","o":["Sumatriptan: 5-HT3 agonist for migraine; Ondansetron: 5-HT1B antagonist for carcinoid flushing; Buspirone: 5-HT2C antagonist for emesis","Cyproheptadine: 5-HT1D agonist for acute migraine; Metoclopramide: pure 5-HT1A agonist for serotonin syndrome","Ketanserin: 5-HT4 agonist for gastroparesis; Prucalopride: 5-HT1A antagonist for anxiety; Ergotamine: selective 5-HT3 agonist","Sumatriptan: 5-HT1B/1D agonist for acute migraine; Ondansetron: 5-HT3 antagonist for chemotherapy-induced nausea/vomiting; Cyproheptadine: 5-HT2A antagonist for serotonin syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Triptans (sumatriptan, rizatriptan) selectively activate Gi-coupled 5-HT1B/1D receptors on intracranial blood vessels and trigeminal nerve endings, causing vasoconstriction and inhibiting vasoactive peptide (CGRP) release to abort acute migraine (contraindicated in coronary artery disease). Ondansetron blocks ligand-gated ion channel 5-HT3 receptors in the area postrema (CTZ) and vagal gut afferents to prevent emesis. Cyproheptadine blocks 5-HT2A (and H1) receptors and is the specific antidote for serotonin syndrome (hyperthermia, clonus, agitation from SSRI + MAOI/linezolid).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02636","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Prostaglandins and Leukotrienes","q":"A 68-year-old man with primary open-angle glaucoma treated with nightly ophthalmic drops develops progressive darkening of his light-colored iris and elongation/thickening of eyelashes after 4 months. Which drug class and mechanism account for this therapeutic and adverse effect?","o":["Latanoprost (PGF2alpha analogue); lowers intraocular pressure by increasing uveoscleral aqueous humor outflow","Timolol (non-selective beta-blocker); increases ciliary body aqueous production","Pilocarpine (muscarinic agonist); shrinks trabecular meshwork via sympathetic stimulation","Alprostadil (PGE1 analogue); closes the ductus arteriosus via smooth muscle constriction"],"a":0,"e":"Topical prostaglandin F2alpha analogues (latanoprost, travoprost, bimatoprost) are first-line therapy for open-angle glaucoma because they activate FP receptors in the ciliary muscle and remodel extracellular matrix to substantially increase uveoscleral outflow of aqueous humor. Characteristic local adverse effects include irreversible hyperpigmentation of the iris (increased melanin in stromal melanocytes), periorbital skin darkening, and hypertrichosis (elongated, thickened eyelashes).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02637","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Parkinson Disease Pharmacotherapy","q":"Assertion (A): Combining levodopa with carbidopa markedly increases the fraction of oral levodopa reaching the brain while reducing peripheral nausea, vomiting, and orthostatic hypotension. Reason (R): Carbidopa inhibits peripheral aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (DOPA decarboxylase) without crossing the blood-brain barrier, preventing premature conversion of levodopa to dopamine outside the CNS.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Dopamine itself cannot cross the blood-brain barrier (BBB), whereas its precursor levodopa is actively transported across the gut and BBB via L-type amino acid transporters. Given alone, ~95-99% of oral levodopa is decarboxylated in the gut/liver/periphery to dopamine—activating the area postrema (CTZ, which lies outside the BBB, causing nausea/vomiting) and peripheral vascular/cardiac receptors (causing orthostasis/arrhythmias). Carbidopa (or benserazide) inhibits peripheral DOPA decarboxylase without entering the CNS, dramatically increasing brain levodopa delivery (~4-fold dose reduction) and curbing peripheral dopaminergic toxicity (though central dyskinesias/psychosis may still occur).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02638","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Opioid Analgesics and Antagonists","q":"A 24-year-old man is brought in comatose with a respiratory rate of 5/min, pinpoint pupils (miosis), and needle track marks after intravenous heroin use. Intravenous naloxone rapidly restores his respiratory rate to 16/min, but 45 minutes later he again becomes somnolent and hypoventilating. Which receptor mechanism and pharmacokinetic property explain this clinical course?","o":["Opioids do not develop tolerance to respiratory depression, and naloxone induces hepatic opioid metabolism","Naloxone competitively antagonizes mu-opioid receptors but has a shorter plasma half-life (~30-60 minutes) than many opioids, allowing re-sedation (\u0027renarcotization\u0027)","Naloxone is an irreversible covalent mu-receptor blocker that wears off via new receptor synthesis in 45 minutes","Naloxone acts strictly at kappa receptors while heroin acts exclusively at delta receptors"],"a":1,"e":"Heroin (diacetylmorphine) and morphine cause coma, miosis (unblocked Edinger-Westphal nucleus parasympathetic tone, to which tolerance does NOT develop), and fatal medullary chemoreceptor desensitization (respiratory depression) primarily via Gi-coupled mu-opioid receptors. Naloxone is a pure competitive antagonist at mu, kappa, and delta receptors with high first-pass hepatic metabolism (hence given parenterally/intranasally) and a short half-life (30-60 min)—shorter than morphine, methadone, or sustained-release opioids—necessitating close observation and repeat dosing or infusion to prevent recurrent respiratory depression.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02639","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Mood Stabilizers (Lithium)","q":"A 36-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder maintained on lithium carbonate (serum trough level 0.9 mEq/L) is started on hydrochlorothiazide for hypertension and ibuprofen for knee pain. Two weeks later she develops coarse tremor, ataxia, dysarthria, and confusion with a serum lithium level of 2.6 mEq/L, alongside polyuria unresponsive to desmopressin. Why did her lithium level spike, and how does lithium cause nephrogenic diabetes insipidus?","o":["Thiazides induce CYP2D6 metabolism of lithium; lithium destroys hypothalamic supraoptic nuclei directly","Ibuprofen displaces lithium from plasma albumin binding; lithium blocks loop of Henle NKCC2","Thiazide-induced volume contraction and NSAID-reduced renal blood flow increase proximal tubular Na+ and Li+ reabsorption; lithium enters collecting duct principal cells via ENaC and impairs ADH-stimulated AQP2 insertion","Thiazides alkalinize urine causing lithium precipitation; lithium inhibits aldosterone synthase"],"a":2,"e":"Lithium is not bound to plasma proteins and is NOT metabolized by the liver; it is freely filtered at the glomerulus and handled by the proximal tubule almost identically to Na+ (~80% reabsorbed). Thiazide diuretics (via distal volume depletion triggering compensatory proximal Na+/Li+ reabsorption) and NSAIDs (inhibiting renal vasodilatory prostaglandins and lowering GFR) markedly reduce lithium clearance, precipitating neurotoxicity above its narrow therapeutic index (0.6-1.2 mEq/L). Additionally, lithium enters collecting duct principal cells through apical epithelial sodium channels (ENaC) and inhibits adenylyl cyclase/GSK-3beta signaling, downregulating aquaporin-2 (AQP2) and causing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (ameliorated by amiloride).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02640","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimetabolites and Alkylating Agents","q":"High-dose methotrexate regimens require timed administration of leucovorin (folinic acid), whereas high-dose cyclophosphamide/ifosfamide regimens require co-administration of mesna. What are the exact biochemical mechanisms of these two protective rescue agents?","o":["Leucovorin stimulates neutrophil G-CSF receptors; mesna competitively blocks bladder muscarinic receptors","Leucovorin directly inhibits thymidylate synthase; mesna alkalinizes hepatic CYP2B6 to block cyclophosphamide activation","Leucovorin chelates methotrexate in renal tubules; mesna inhibits xanthine oxidase to prevent tumor lysis","Leucovorin (N5-formyl-THF) bypasses methotrexate-inhibited dihydrofolate reductase to replenish reduced folates in normal cells; mesna provides sulfhydryl groups in urine to bind and detoxify urotoxic acrolein"],"a":3,"e":"Methotrexate competitively inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), blocking regeneration of tetrahydrofolate (THF) needed for thymidylate and purine synthesis; leucovorin (folinic acid, N5-formyl-THF) is already a reduced folate that bypasses the DHFR block to rescue normal bone marrow and GI mucosal cells. Cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide are hepatic prodrugs (activated by CYP450) that generate phosphoramide mustard (cytotoxic alkylator) and acrolein—which concentrates in urine to cause hemorrhagic cystitis; mesna (2-mercaptoethanesulfonate) concentrates in the bladder and conjugates acrolein via its free thiol (-SH) group into a non-toxic thioether.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02641","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Glycopeptides and MRSA Resistance","q":"How does vancomycin inhibit cell wall synthesis in methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA), how do vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE) evade it, and what causes \u0027Red Man syndrome\u0027 during rapid IV infusion?","o":["Binds D-Ala-D-Ala terminus of peptidoglycan pentapeptide to block transglycosylation; VRE alter target to D-Ala-D-Lac; IgE-independent direct mast cell degranulation releasing histamine","Inhibits 50S ribosomal translocation; VRE methylate 23S rRNA; bradykinin accumulation","Binds cell membrane ergosterol; VRE upregulate efflux pumps; complement C5a activation","Inhibits PBP2a transpeptidase directly; VRE produce beta-lactamase; IgE-mediated Type I anaphylaxis"],"a":0,"e":"MRSA resists beta-lactams via mecA-encoded PBP2a with low beta-lactam affinity. Vancomycin (a glycopeptide) bypasses PBPs entirely by binding directly to the D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of nascent muramyl pentapeptide chains, sterically blocking transglycosylase and transpeptidase cross-linking. VRE acquire vanA/vanB plasmids that replace D-Ala-D-Ala with D-Ala-D-lactate (D-Ala-D-Lac), losing a critical hydrogen bond. Rapid vancomycin infusion triggers non-IgE-mediated (anaphylactoid) direct mast cell and basophil histamine release (\u0027Red Man syndrome\u0027: flushing/pruritus of upper torso/neck), managed by slowing infusion (\u003e=60 min) and giving antihistamines.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02642","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimalarial Chemotherapy","q":"A 28-year-old man from Odisha is diagnosed with uncomplicated Plasmodium falciparum malaria and treated per India\u0027s national malaria guidelines with artemether-lumefantrine (ACT) plus a single low dose of primaquine on Day 2. What is the purpose of adding single-dose primaquine in P. falciparum infection, and why is a 14-day primaquine course used cautiously in P. vivax malaria?","o":["Primaquine clears P. falciparum blood schizonts rapidly; 14-day primaquine causes cinchonism in P. vivax","Single-dose primaquine is gametocytocidal (interrupting P. falciparum transmission); 14-day primaquine eradicates P. vivax liver hypnozoites (radical cure) but precipitates acute intravascular hemolysis in G6PD-deficient patients","Artemether targets liver hypnozoites exclusively; primaquine reverses chloroquine resistance in P. falciparum","Single-dose primaquine prevents cerebral sequestration of rings; 14-day primaquine causes irreversible retinal deposition"],"a":1,"e":"Under India\u0027s National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (NVBDCP/NCVBDC) guidelines, uncomplicated P. falciparum malaria is treated with a 3-day Artemisinin-based Combination Therapy (ACT: artesunate + sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine nationwide, or artemether-lumefantrine in northeastern states) plus a single gametocytocidal dose of primaquine (0.75 mg/kg) on Day 2 to block mosquito transmission (since ACTs clear asexual rings rapidly via heme-activated free radicals but do not kill mature P. falciparum gametocytes). For P. vivax, 14 days of primaquine (0.25 mg/kg/day) eliminates dormant hepatic hypnozoites to prevent relapse, but requires caution/G6PD screening because oxidative metabolites trigger acute hemolytic anemia (Heinz bodies, bite cells) in G6PD deficiency and is contraindicated in pregnancy/infants.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02643","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antipsychotic Drugs","q":"A 31-year-old man with treatment-resistant schizophrenia starting clozapine returns for routine weekly CBC monitoring showing WBC 2,100/mm3 and Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC) 450/mm3 (down from baseline ANC 3,200/mm3), while a second patient on haloperidol develops fever 40.2°C, lead-pipe rigidity, autonomic instability, and serum creatine kinase (CK) 18,500 U/L. What are the appropriate management actions for these two antipsychotic toxicities?","o":["Reduce clozapine dose by 25% and repeat CBC in 1 month; give propranolol for haloperidol-induced NMS","Switch clozapine to chlorpromazine immediately without stopping; treat haloperidol rigidity with succinylcholine","Discontinue clozapine immediately (agranulocytosis, ANC \u003c500/mm3) and avoid rechallenge; stop haloperidol (neuroleptic malignant syndrome) and administer dantrolene and/or bromocriptine","Continue clozapine with oral lithium to boost WBC; treat haloperidol toxicity with benztropine alone"],"a":2,"e":"Clozapine carries a ~1% risk of idiosyncratic agranulocytosis (ANC \u003c500/mm3), mandating strict baseline and periodic ANC monitoring; if ANC falls below 500/mm3 (or \u003c1000/mm3 with infection/severe neutropenia thresholds), clozapine must be discontinued immediately with hematology/G-CSF support and permanent avoidance of rechallenge. The second patient exhibits Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) from potent nigrostriatal/hypothalamic D2 blockade by high-potency typical antipsychotics (haloperidol): fever, lead-pipe rigidity, autonomic instability, altered mental status, and rhabdomyolysis (sky-high CK). Immediate management requires stopping the antipsychotic, aggressive cooling/hydration, and dantrolene (ryanodine receptor antagonist reducing sarcoplasmic Ca2+ release) plus bromocriptine (central D2 agonist).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02644","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Hepatitis B Virus (HBV)","q":"During serologic interpretation in a microbiology practical, a recovering acute hepatitis patient tests negative for HBsAg, negative for anti-HBs, negative for HBeAg, positive for anti-HBc IgM, and positive for anti-HBe. Which phase of Hepatitis B infection does this serologic profile represent?","o":["Resolved past Hepatitis B infection with lifelong immunity","Immunity following Hepatitis B vaccination alone","Chronic active Hepatitis B carrier with high infectivity","Serologic \u0027window period\u0027 of resolving acute Hepatitis B infection"],"a":3,"e":"During the serologic \u0027window period\u0027 of resolving acute HBV infection, circulating HBsAg has fallen below detectable thresholds (or formed immune complexes) before protective anti-HBs antibodies reach detectable titers. During this gap, anti-HBc IgM is the sole reliable diagnostic marker of acute HBV infection (often accompanied by seroconversion from HBeAg to anti-HBe indicating declining viral replication).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02645","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Rabies Virus","q":"A 12-year-old boy sustains multiple deep transdermal dog bites on his hand and forearm (WHO Category III exposure) from a stray dog that died 2 days later with hypersalivation. Which diagnostic intracytoplasmic inclusions are classically found in hippocampal pyramidal neurons and cerebellar Purkinje cells of the rabid animal, and what is the mandatory post-exposure prophylaxis for this previously unvaccinated child?","o":["Negri bodies; thorough soap-and-water wound washing, rabies immunoglobulin (RIG) infiltrated into and around the wounds, plus full anti-rabies vaccination series","Owl-eye intranuclear inclusions; tetanus toxoid alone without rabies immunoglobulin","Guarnieri bodies; wound suturing immediately with oral acyclovir","Councilman bodies; anti-rabies vaccine only without RIG"],"a":0,"e":"Rabies virus (a bullet-shaped, negative-sense ssRNA Lyssavirus) binds nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction and ascends via retrograde axonal transport (dynein) to the CNS, forming pathognomonic eosinophilic intracytoplasmic ribonucleoprotein inclusions called Negri bodies in hippocampal pyramidal cells and cerebellar Purkinje neurons. For WHO Category III bites (single/multiple transdermal bites or scratches, or licks on broken skin/mucous membranes) in an unvaccinated person, immediate soap-and-water washing (\u003e=15 minutes) + passive immunization with Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG—as much as anatomically feasible infiltrated directly into/around all wounds) + active cell-culture rabies vaccine (IM Essen 0-3-7-14-28 or updated Thai Red Cross intradermal 2-2-2-0-2) are mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02646","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Influenza Virus","q":"Assertion (A): Major global influenza pandemics arise exclusively from Influenza A viruses undergoing antigenic shift rather than Influenza B or C viruses. Reason (R): Influenza A possesses an 8-segmented negative-sense RNA genome and infects multiple animal reservoirs (avian, swine, human), enabling genetic reassortment of hemagglutinin (HA) or neuraminidase (NA) RNA segments when two distinct strains co-infect a single host cell.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Antigenic drift involves gradual point mutations in the HA/NA genes due to error-prone RNA-dependent RNA polymerase, causing seasonal epidemics in both Influenza A and B. Antigenic shift is an abrupt, major replacement of HA and/or NA subtypes via reassortment of the 8 segmented RNA pieces when avian/swine and human Influenza A strains co-infect a \u0027mixing vessel\u0027 (such as pigs). Because humans lack pre-existing immunity to the novel HA/NA subtype and Influenza B/C lack broad animal reservoirs for such reassortment, pandemics occur only with Influenza A antigenic shift.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02647","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Visceral Leishmaniasis (Kala-azar)","q":"A 28-year-old farmer from rural Bihar presents with 3 months of irregular fever, darkening of facial skin, marked weight loss, pancytopenia, hypergammaglobulinemia, and massive splenomegaly. Bone marrow aspirate reveals macrophages packed with round/oval aflagellate organisms containing a nucleus and a rod-shaped kinetoplast (LD bodies). Which sandfly vector transmits this parasite in India, and what rapid serologic test and first-line treatment are recommended under the national elimination program?","o":["Culex quinquefasciatus; thick nocturnal smear; diethylcarbamazine","Phlebotomus argentipes; rK39 immunochromatographic strip test; single-dose intravenous liposomal amphotericin B","Anopheles culicifacies; NS1 ELISA; chloroquine","Aedes aegypti; Widal test; ciprofloxacin"],"a":1,"e":"Visceral leishmaniasis (Kala-azar) in India is caused by Leishmania donovani and transmitted by the female sandfly Phlebotomus argentipes (inoculating flagellated metacyclic promastigotes that transform inside host reticuloendothelial macrophages into intracellular amastigotes / Leishman-Donovan bodies with a prominent kinetoplast). Under India\u0027s National Kala-azar Elimination Programme (NVBDCP), rapid field diagnosis uses the rK39 dipstick test (supplemented by splenic/bone marrow microscopy), and first-line treatment is single-dose IV liposomal amphotericin B (10 mg/kg), with miltefosine + paromomycin as alternatives.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02648","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Cestodes (Taenia solium)","q":"A strict vegetarian presents with new-onset focal seizures, and brain MRI shows multiple cystic parenchymal ring-enhancing lesions with eccentric hyperintense scolex dots (\u0027starry-sky\u0027 appearance of neurocysticercosis). How does the infective stage causing neurocysticercosis differ from that causing adult intestinal Taenia solium taeniasis?","o":["Neurocysticercosis is acquired by ingesting Taenia solium embryonated eggs (fecal-oral contamination or autoinfection), whereas intestinal taeniasis is acquired by eating undercooked pork containing Cysticercus cellulosae larvae","Neurocysticercosis is acquired by eating Cysticercus cellulosae in pork, whereas intestinal taeniasis is acquired by ingesting embryonated eggs","Both neurocysticercosis and intestinal taeniasis are acquired solely by skin penetration of cercariae","Neurocysticercosis is caused by Taenia saginata eggs only, never Taenia solium"],"a":0,"e":"For Taenia solium, humans are normally the definitive host when they ingest Cysticercus cellulosae larvae in undercooked pork, developing adult intestinal tapeworms (taeniasis). However, if a human (even a strict vegetarian exposed via contaminated raw vegetables/water or a tapeworm carrier via autoinfection) ingests Taenia solium embryonated eggs, humans act as accidental intermediate hosts: oncospheres hatch, penetrate the intestinal wall, and encyst as cysticerci in brain (neurocysticercosis), eye, or skeletal muscle. Notably, Taenia saginata eggs do NOT cause human cysticercosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02649","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Intestinal Nematodes","q":"Which infective larval stage penetrates intact human skin in both Ancylostoma duodenale (hookworm) and Strongyloides stercoralis infections, and which unique Strongyloides feature precipitates fatal hyperinfection syndrome in patients started on systemic corticosteroids?","o":["Rhabditiform (L1) larva; Ancylostoma causes internal autoinfection in the colon","Embryonated egg; Strongyloides adults live inside erythrocytes","Filariform (L3) larva; Strongyloides rhabditiform (L1) larvae can transform into infective L3 larvae inside the host gut causing internal autoinfection","Cercaria; Necator americanus multiplies in CSF"],"a":2,"e":"Both hookworms (Ancylostoma duodenale, Necator americanus) and Strongyloides stercoralis infect humans via skin penetration by soil-dwelling third-stage filariform (L3) larvae, followed by lung migration and small-intestinal maturation. Unlike hookworm (which passes eggs in stool), Strongyloides adult parthenogenetic females lay eggs that hatch into rhabditiform (L1) larvae in the intestinal mucosa; some L1 larvae mature prematurely into invasive L3 filariform larvae inside the gut, penetrating colonic mucosa or perianal skin (internal autoinfection). Corticosteroid-induced suppression of Th2/eosinophil immunity triggers massive Strongyloides hyperinfection and Gram-negative bacteremia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02650","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"CNS Fungal Infections","q":"A 35-year-old man with advanced HIV (CD4 count 35 cells/mm3) presents with subacute headache, fever, and cranial nerve VI palsy. CSF shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, low glucose, and high protein. India ink wet mount reveals round budding yeast cells surrounded by a wide, clear unstained halo. Which additional biochemical and tissue-staining features confirm Cryptococcus neoformans?","o":["Germ-tube positive in human serum, forms chlamydospores on cornmeal agar, and urease-negative","Thermotolerant mold showing narrow septate 45-degree branching hyphae on Sabouraud agar","Dimorphic mold at 25°C converting to broad-based budding yeast at 37°C without a polysaccharide capsule","Urease-positive, produces brown-black colonies on birdseed (Niger seed) agar, and capsule stains bright red with Mayer mucicarmine"],"a":3,"e":"Cryptococcus neoformans (acquired by inhaling aerosolized pigeon droppings) has a thick glucuronoxylomannan polysaccharide capsule—visualized as a clear refractile halo against black India ink/nigrosin in CSF and staining bright carmine-red on Mayer mucicarmine (plus cryptococcal capsular antigen lateral flow/latex agglutination, the most sensitive test). Key laboratory identifiers include rapid urease positivity (Christensen urea agar) and phenoloxidase conversion of caffeic acid on Niger seed (birdseed) agar into dark brown/black melanin-pigmented colonies.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02651","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Dimorphic and Subcutaneous Mycoses","q":"A gardener develops a painless papulonodular ulcer on his right index finger after a rose-thorn prick, followed by a linear chain of ascending subcutaneous nodules along the forearm lymphatics. Biopsy and culture at 37°C reveal pleomorphic elongated \u0027cigar-shaped\u0027 budding yeasts (with occasional asteroid bodies), converting to delicate hyphae bearing daisy-like rosette conidia at 25°C. Which organism is responsible?","o":["Sporothrix schenckii","Histoplasma capsulatum","Paracoccidioides brasiliensis","Blastomyces dermatitidis"],"a":0,"e":"Sporothrix schenckii is a thermally dimorphic fungus inoculated traumatically from soil, splinters, or rose thorns, classically causing lymphocutaneous sporotrichosis (ascending nodular lymphangitis). At 37°C/in tissue it forms small oval-to-cigar-shaped yeasts (sometimes surrounded by eosinophilic Splendore-Hoeppli material forming \u0027asteroid bodies\u0027), whereas at 25°C it grows as a mold with slender hyphae and flower-like/daisy-like clusters of conidia at conidiophore tips.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02652","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Hanging vs Strangulation","q":"At medicolegal autopsy of a deceased adult, the neck shows a deep, completely encircling horizontal ligature groove situated below the thyroid cartilage, accompanied by extensive subcutaneous hemorrhage, fractured thyroid cartilage, and absence of salivary dribbling. Do these findings favor suicidal hanging or homicidal ligature strangulation, and why?","o":["Suicidal hanging; salivary dribbling is exclusive to ligature strangulation","Homicidal ligature strangulation; strangulation classically produces a horizontal, complete ligature mark at or below the thyroid cartilage with marked soft-tissue bruising and no antemortem salivary dribbling","Suicidal hanging; hanging always produces a horizontal mark below the thyroid cartilage","Judicial hanging; judicial hanging never fractures neck structures"],"a":1,"e":"In suicidal hanging, body suspension pulls the noose upward toward the knot, creating an oblique, non-continuous (open at the knot) parchmentized ligature mark high on the neck above the thyroid cartilage, minimal subcutaneous bruising, and often a dry vertical trickle of saliva from the angle of the mouth opposite the knot (a vital sign from submandibular gland compression during life). By contrast, homicidal ligature strangulation typically produces a horizontal, continuous (complete) groove at or below the level of the thyroid cartilage, marked subcutaneous/muscle hemorrhage, frequent laryngeal/thyroid cartilage injury, intense facial petechiae, and no vertical salivary dribbling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02653","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Drowning","q":"Assertion (A): Detection of identical species of acid-resistant silica diatoms in intact long-bone marrow (femur) or sternum matching the drowning water source strongly supports antemortem drowning over postmortem disposal of a corpse into water. Reason (R): Only an active circulation during life can pump aspirated diatoms that cross ruptured alveolar-capillary membranes in the lungs into the systemic arterial blood and deposit them inside closed bone marrow cavities.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"When a live person inhales diatom-containing water during drowning, hydrostatic pressure and alveolar rupture allow microscopic unicellular algae (with acid-resistant silica frustules) to enter pulmonary capillaries, pass through the left heart, and embolize via systemic circulation to distant closed organs such as femoral/sternal bone marrow, kidney, and brain. A dead body thrown into water lacks beating cardiac circulation; water may passively enter the stomach or upper airways, but diatoms cannot reach intact distant bone marrow (unless extreme putrefaction/contamination occurs, which is why control water and strict acid-digestion precautions are taken).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02654","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Firearm Injuries","q":"At autopsy of a gunshot homicide, a circular wound in the right temporal scalp shows an abrasion collar, grease collar, and fine punctate unburnt gunpowder stippling (tattooing) extending over a 10 cm radius around the defect without soot blackening or singed hair, while the skull shows clean inner-table beveling. What do the tattooing and inner-table beveling indicate?","o":["Postmortem artifact unrelated to projectile direction","Contact-range shotgun exit wound with bullet leaving the right temple","Close/intermediate-range rifled firearm entry wound (roughly 30-90 cm for handgun) with bullet trajectory entering the right temple","Distant-range (\u003e2 meters) exit wound without gunpowder residue"],"a":2,"e":"An abrasion collar (contusion ring), grease/wipe collar, and gunpowder tattooing (unburnt/partially burnt propellant grains embedded in dermis that cannot be wiped off) are hallmarks of an entrance wound. At contact/near-contact range, flame singes hair and soot blackens skin; at intermediate/close range (~15-90 cm depending on barrel/ammunition, beyond soot/flame reach), only gunpowder tattooing reaches the skin; beyond ~1-2 m (distant range), neither soot nor tattooing is seen. Furthermore, a bullet passing through flat cranial bone creates a narrow punched-out defect on the impacted table and wider cratering (\u0027beveling\u0027) in the direction of travel—so inner-table beveling confirms an entry wound.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02655","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Methanol Poisoning","q":"A 40-year-old man presents with blurred \u0027snowfield\u0027 vision, optic disc hyperemia, and Kussmaul breathing 14 hours after drinking illicit hooch liquor. Serum labs show Na+ 140 mEq/L, Cl- 102 mEq/L, HCO3- 10 mEq/L, glucose 90 mg/dL, BUN 14 mg/dL, and measured serum osmolality 335 mOsm/kg. Calculate his serum anion gap and osmolar gap, and identify the toxic metabolite causing blindness.","o":["Anion gap = 18 mEq/L, osmolar gap = 45 mOsm/kg; formaldehyde directly causes hypocalcemia","Anion gap = 28 mEq/L, osmolar gap = 5 mOsm/kg; acetaldehyde causes optic neuritis","Anion gap = 12 mEq/L, osmolar gap = 5 mOsm/kg; oxalic acid precipitates in renal tubules","Anion gap = 28 mEq/L, osmolar gap = 45 mOsm/kg; formic acid (formate) inhibits retinal cytochrome oxidase"],"a":3,"e":"Serum anion gap = Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3-) = 140 - (102 + 10) = 28 mEq/L (high anion gap metabolic acidosis from accumulated formic acid). Calculated serum osmolality = (2 x Na+) + (Glucose / 18) + (BUN / 2.8) = (2 x 140) + (90 / 18) + (14 / 2.8) = 280 + 5 + 5 = 290 mOsm/kg. Therefore, osmolar gap = Measured osmolality - Calculated osmolality = 335 - 290 = 45 mOsm/kg (elevated \u003e10 due to circulating uncharged methanol). Alcohol dehydrogenase and aldehyde dehydrogenase convert methanol to formaldehyde and then formic acid, which inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase in the optic nerve and retina causing blindness; treatment uses fomepizole (or ethanol) plus folinic acid and hemodialysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02656","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Asphyxiant Poisons","q":"A jeweler collapses in his workshop with rapid onset of seizures, severe lactic acidosis (lactate 14 mmol/L), bright pink venous blood (high venous PO2), and a faint bitter-almond odor on gastric contents at casualty admission. Which cellular enzyme is inhibited, and what are the two standard specific antidotal strategies?","o":["Mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase (Complex IV, binding ferric Fe3+ heme a3); hydroxocobalamin (preferred) or methemoglobin-inducing sodium nitrite followed by sodium thiosulfate","Na+/K+-ATPase; intravenous atropine and pralidoxime","Hemoglobin ferrous Fe2+ oxygen-binding site; 100% hyperbaric oxygen alone","Complex I (NADH dehydrogenase); methylene blue and vitamin C"],"a":0,"e":"Cyanide binds avidly to the ferric (Fe3+) iron of cytochrome c oxidase (Complex IV) in mitochondria, halting aerobic oxidative phosphorylation despite normal arterial oxygen delivery. Because tissues cannot extract oxygen, venous PO2 approaches arterial PO2 (\u0027arterialization\u0027 of venous blood), producing severe anaerobic lactic acidosis and a bitter-almond odor (detected genetically by ~60% of individuals). Specific antidotes are hydroxocobalamin (vitamin B12a, which directly binds cyanide to form non-toxic renally excreted cyanocobalamin without impairing O2 carrying) or the traditional Lilly kit: amyl/sodium nitrite (oxidizing Hb Fe2+ to methemoglobin Fe3+ to pull cyanide off Complex IV) followed by sodium thiosulfate (providing sulfur for rhodanese to convert cyanide to thiocyanate).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02657","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Venomous Snakebites in India","q":"A 45-year-old agricultural laborer is bitten on the ankle while harvesting paddy in Maharashtra and presents 3 hours later with painful local swelling, bleeding from the bite site, and a bedside 20-minute Whole Blood Clotting Test (20WBCT) showing unclotted liquid blood when the clean glass tube is tilted. Which Indian snake family/species is most likely responsible, and what is the primary specific treatment?","o":["Sea snake (Hydrophiidae) causing pure myotoxicity without coagulopathy; tourniquet and incision-suction","Viperidae (Russell\u0027s viper or saw-scaled viper) causing consumption coagulopathy; immediate intravenous Indian polyvalent anti-snake venom (ASV)","Elapidae (common krait) causing isolated painless nocturnal neurotoxicity; oral neostigmine alone","Non-venomous rat snake; prophylactic intramuscular ASV test dose only"],"a":1,"e":"In India, the \u0027Big Four\u0027 medically important snakes are Russell\u0027s viper (Daboia russelii), saw-scaled viper (Echis carinatus), spectacled cobra (Naja naja), and common krait (Bungarus caeruleus). Viperid bites produce painful local swelling/blistering and hemotoxic consumption coagulopathy (defibrination/DIC) plus acute kidney injury; an incoagulable 20WBCT (blood remaining liquid in a clean, dry glass tube after 20 minutes) is a simple, definitive bedside indicator of systemic viperid envenomation requiring immediate IV Indian polyvalent ASV (typically 10 vials initially, same dose in adults and children, without intradermal test doses).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02658","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Dying Declaration and Criminal Responsibility","q":"Under the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA), 2023 and Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), 2023, which statements accurately describe the evidentiary rule for a dying declaration and the absolute age of criminal immunity (doli incapax)?","o":["Dying declarations are valid only when written in the declarant\u0027s own handwriting; BNS Section 20 grants conditional immunity under 7 years","BNS Section 21 grants absolute un-rebuttable criminal immunity to children aged 7 to 12 years; dying declaration is inadmissible unless recorded by a doctor","BSA Section 26(a) (former IEA Section 32(1)) makes a dying declaration admissible without oath/cross-examination (even if the declarant does not die immediately, though it becomes a statement only if they survive); BNS Section 20 grants absolute criminal immunity to a child under 7 years","BSA Section 26(a) requires mandatory oath and cross-examination by defense counsel; BNS Section 20 grants absolute immunity up to 18 years"],"a":2,"e":"Under Section 26(a) of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA), 2023 (corresponding to former Section 32(1) of the Indian Evidence Act, 1872), a statement made by a person (oral, written, or by signs/gestures) regarding the cause of their death or circumstances of the transaction resulting in death is admissible as substantive evidence without oath or cross-examination (under the maxim \u0027nemo moriturus praesumitur mentire\u0027), provided the declarant was in a fit mental state (compos mentis, certified by the attending doctor) and subsequently dies (if the person survives, it is not a dying declaration under Section 26(a) but remains usable as a previous statement for corroboration/contradiction). Under BNS 2023 Section 20 (former IPC 82), nothing is an offence committed by a child under 7 years (absolute doli incapax), whereas BNS Section 21 (former IPC 83) provides conditional/rebuttable immunity for ages 7 to under 12 depending on maturity of understanding.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02659","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","q":"A clinical trial comparing a new antihypertensive drug against standard therapy fails to detect a true 8 mmHg reduction in systolic blood pressure because the enrolled sample size was too small. Which statistical error occurred, and how is the probability of avoiding it defined?","o":["Type II (beta) error; avoided by decreasing the sample size","Type I (alpha) error; avoided by lowering the significance level to 0.01","Type I (alpha) error; avoided by increasing statistical power (1 - beta)","Type II (beta) error; avoided by increasing statistical power (1 - beta)"],"a":3,"e":"Failing to reject a false null hypothesis (missing a true difference, i.e., false-negative conclusion) is a Type II (beta) error. Statistical power (1 - beta, conventionally \u003e= 80%) is the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis; increasing sample size increases power and reduces beta error.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02660","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Measures of Dispersion and Estimation","q":"In a community blood pressure survey of 100 adults, the mean systolic blood pressure is 130 mmHg with a standard deviation (SD) of 15 mmHg. What is the Standard Error of the Mean (SEM)?","o":["1.5 mmHg","15.0 mmHg","0.15 mmHg","3.0 mmHg"],"a":0,"e":"Standard Error of the Mean (SEM) measures the precision of the sample mean as an estimate of the population mean and is calculated as SEM = SD / sqrt(n). Here, SEM = 15 / sqrt(100) = 15 / 10 = 1.5 mmHg (yielding a 95% confidence interval of approximately 130 +/- 1.96 x 1.5 = 127.06 to 132.94 mmHg).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02661","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"NP-NCD (formerly NPCDCS)","q":"Under the National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD) at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs (Sub-Health Centres / PHCs), universal population-based screening is initiated for hypertension, diabetes, and three common cancers (oral, breast, and cervical). At what minimum age is this routine adult screening recommended under the operational guidelines?","o":["18 years and above","30 years and above","45 years and above","60 years and above"],"a":1,"e":"Under India\u0027s MoHFW population-based screening guidelines (NP-NCD / Comprehensive Primary Health Care), individuals aged 30 years and above undergo systematic risk assessment (CBAC form by ASHA) and screening for hypertension, diabetes mellitus, oral cancer, breast cancer (clinical breast examination), and cervical cancer (VIA every 5 years for women 30-65 years).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02662","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"National AIDS Control Programme (NACP)","q":"A 32-year-old man newly diagnosed with HIV-1 infection (CD4 count 280 cells/mm3, HBsAg negative, normal renal function, no active tuberculosis) is referred to an ART centre under India\u0027s National AIDS Control Programme (NACP). What is the preferred NACO first-line single-tablet daily ART regimen?","o":["Abacavir (ABC) + Didanosine (ddI) + Lopinavir/ritonavir","Tenofovir (TDF) 300 mg + Lamivudine (3TC) 300 mg + Dolutegravir (DTG) 50 mg","Zidovudine (AZT) + Lamivudine (3TC) + Nevirapine (NVP)","Stavudine (d4T) + Lamivudine (3TC) + Indinavir (IDV)"],"a":1,"e":"Per NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, the preferred first-line ART regimen for all adults and adolescents (with normal renal function) is the fixed-dose TLD combination: Tenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate (300 mg) + Lamivudine (300 mg) + Dolutegravir (50 mg) once daily, owing to high genetic barrier to resistance, rapid viral suppression, and better tolerability.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02663","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"National Leprosy Eradication Programme (NLEP)","q":"A 35-year-old man presents with 7 hypopigmented, anaesthetic skin patches distributed asymmetrically over the trunk and limbs, along with thickened bilateral ulnar and common peroneal nerves. Slit-skin smear is negative for acid-fast bacilli. Under NLEP/WHO operational classification, how is this patient classified and treated?","o":["Multibacillary (MB) leprosy; Dapsone monotherapy for 24 months","Paucibacillary (PB) leprosy; single-dose Rifampicin monotherapy","Multibacillary (MB) leprosy; 3-drug MDT (Rifampicin, Clofazimine, Dapsone) for 12 months","Paucibacillary (PB) leprosy; 2-drug MDT (Rifampicin, Dapsone) for 6 months"],"a":2,"e":"Under WHO/NLEP operational rules, any patient with \u003e= 6 skin lesions (6 or more), OR \u003e 1 peripheral nerve trunk involved, OR a positive slit-skin smear is classified as Multibacillary (MB) leprosy—even if slit-skin smear is negative. Adult MB-MDT comprises monthly supervised Rifampicin 600 mg + Clofazimine 300 mg, plus daily self-administered Clofazimine 50 mg + Dapsone 100 mg for 12 months (12 blister packs).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02664","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Rabies Prophylaxis","q":"A 22-year-old previously unvaccinated man presents 2 hours after a stray dog bite on his right calf resulting in multiple deep, actively bleeding transdermal puncture wounds. After immediate 15-minute soap-and-running-water wound washing and povidone-iodine application, how should Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG) and anti-rabies vaccine be administered?","o":["Administer anti-rabies vaccine alone on Days 0, 3, 7, 14 without RIG since the wound bled","Inject full-dose RIG intramuscularly into the gluteus muscle and suture the wound tightly immediately","Give RIG only if the dog dies after 10 days of observation","Infiltrate RIG directly into and around all bite wounds (not into the gluteal region), and start anti-rabies vaccine at a distant site (deltoid) on Day 0"],"a":3,"e":"Multiple transdermal bleeding bites represent WHO Category III rabies exposure. Management requires: (1) Immediate vigorous soap-and-water washing for 15 minutes + antiseptic; (2) Passive immunisation with Equine/Human RIG (or rabies monoclonal antibody) infiltrated anatomically into and around the depth of all wounds to neutralise virus locally before neural entry; (3) Active vaccination (e.g., Updated Thai Red Cross intradermal 2-2-2-0-2 on Days 0, 3, 7, 28 or Essen IM) at a separate distant site (deltoid), avoiding primary wound suturing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02665","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Retinal Vascular Occlusions","q":"A 66-year-old hypertensive man with carotid atherosclerosis complains of sudden, painless, profound loss of vision in his right eye (hand movements only) starting 45 minutes ago. Examination shows a right relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD), diffuse ischaemic retinal whitening, attenuated arterioles with \u0027cattle-trucking\u0027 segmentation, and a bright \u0027cherry-red spot\u0027 at the macula. What is the diagnosis and immediate emergency aim?","o":["Central Retinal Artery Occlusion (CRAO); urgently lower IOP (ocular massage, IV acetazolamide, anterior chamber paracentesis) and carbogen inhalation to restore retinal perfusion","Central Retinal Vein Occlusion (CRVO); elective anti-VEGF injection after 4 weeks","Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment; immediate atropine drops and bed rest","Optic neuritis; oral doxycycline for 14 days"],"a":0,"e":"Sudden painless catastrophic monocular vision loss, RAPD, retinal pallor, arteriolar segmentation (\u0027cattle-trucking\u0027), and a macular cherry-red spot (thin foveola showing intact underlying choroidal circulation) define CRAO—an ophthalmic emergency with a ~90-100 minute retinal survival window. Immediate measures aim to dislodge the embolus and increase retinal perfusion pressure via intermittent ocular massage, IV/oral acetazolamide, anterior chamber paracentesis, and vasodilatory carbogen (95% O2 + 5% CO2), followed by urgent stroke/carotid workup.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02666","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma (POAG)","q":"A 58-year-old myopic man undergoing routine refraction is found to have IOP of 28 mmHg bilaterally, deep open angles on gonioscopy, vertical cup-to-disc ratio of 0.8 with inferior neuroretinal rim notching, and an arcuate (Bjerrum) scotoma with Roenne\u0027s nasal step on automated perimetry. Which topical medication class is the preferred first-line medical therapy to lower IOP?","o":["Topical mydriatic (Tropicamide 1% twice daily)","Prostaglandin F2-alpha analogues (e.g., Latanoprost 0.005% once at bedtime)","Topical corticosteroid (Fluorometholone 0.1%)","Topical cholinergic miotics (Pilocarpine 2% four times daily)"],"a":1,"e":"POAG causes asymptomatic progressive retinal ganglion cell loss, optic disc cupping/notching, and characteristic nerve-fibre-bundle field defects (paracentral scotoma, Seidel scotoma, Bjerrum arcuate scotoma, Roenne\u0027s nasal step) with open iridocorneal angles. Prostaglandin analogues (Latanoprost, Travoprost, Bimatoprost) increase uveoscleral outflow, achieve ~25-35% IOP reduction with once-daily dosing and minimal systemic side effects, making them first-line.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02667","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Strabismus Evaluation","q":"During an OSCE station on strabismus in a 5-year-old child, a penlight held at 33 cm shows the corneal light reflex centred on the right pupil, while on the left eye the reflex falls precisely on the nasal pupillary margin (2 mm nasal to the corneal centre). Which type and approximate magnitude of manifest squint is present in the left eye?","o":["Left esotropia of approximately 15 degrees (30 prism dioptres)","Left esotropia of approximately 45 degrees (90 prism dioptres)","Left exotropia of approximately 15 degrees (30 prism dioptres)","Right hypertropia of approximately 7 degrees (15 prism dioptres)"],"a":2,"e":"In the Hirschberg corneal light reflex test, if the eye turns outward (exotropia), the light reflex shifts nasally; if the eye turns inward (esotropia), the reflex shifts temporally. Each 1 mm of reflex decentration corresponds to ~7 degrees (~15 prism dioptres). A reflex at the pupillary margin (~2 mm nasal to centre) indicates ~15 degrees (30 PD) of exotropia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02668","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Retinoblastoma","q":"An 18-month-old child is brought with a white pupillary reflex (leukocoria) in the right eye and new-onset strabismus. B-scan ultrasonography reveals a dome-shaped intraocular mass with high-amplitude acoustic shadowing from intratumoral calcification. Histopathology of an enucleated specimen in this tumour characteristically demonstrates which feature?","o":["Call-Exner bodies","Birbeck granules on electron microscopy","Reed-Sternberg cells","Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes and Homer Wright rosettes"],"a":3,"e":"Retinoblastoma (RB1 tumour suppressor gene mutation on chromosome 13q14) is the most common primary intraocular malignancy of childhood, presenting classically with leukocoria (60%) and strabismus (20%). Intralesional calcification is pathognomonic on ultrasound/CT (MRI is preferred for optic nerve/intracranial staging without ionising radiation), and histology shows Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes (true photoreceptor differentiation around a lumen), Homer Wright rosettes, and fleurettes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02669","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Acute Paediatric Stridor","q":"A 4-year-old unvaccinated boy develops acute high fever (39.6 degrees C), muffled \u0027hot-potato\u0027 voice, severe dysphagia with drooling of saliva, inspiratory stridor, and sits leaning forward with his chin extended (tripod posture). What is the most critical rule during initial evaluation in the emergency room?","o":["Do NOT attempt tongue-depressor throat examination; secure the airway urgently in the operating theatre followed by IV ceftriaxone","Perform immediate rigid oesophagoscopy in the outpatient clinic","Depress the tongue immediately with a metallic spatula to swab the epiglottis","Administer oral amoxicillin and send the child home for lateral neck X-ray next morning"],"a":0,"e":"Acute epiglottitis (traditionally caused by Haemophilus influenzae type b) presents rapidly with toxic fever, drooling, dysphagia, muffled voice, and tripod posturing (lateral neck radiograph shows \u0027thumbprint sign\u0027 if taken safely, though imaging must never delay airway control). Agitating the child or inserting a tongue depressor can trigger sudden fatal laryngospasm and complete airway obstruction; immediate controlled intubation/tracheostomy readiness in the OT precedes IV third-generation cephalosporin therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02670","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Laryngeal Malignancy","q":"A 58-year-old chronic bidi smoker presents with progressive hoarseness of voice for 2 months without dysphagia or neck nodes. Flexible laryngoscopy shows an exophytic keratotic growth confined to the middle third of the left true vocal fold with normal vocal cord mobility (T1aN0M0). Biopsy confirms well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma. Why does glottic carcinoma rarely have early nodal metastasis, and what is the primary curative option?","o":["True vocal cords drain bilaterally to level V nodes; radical neck dissection is mandatory","True vocal cords lack significant lymphatic drainage; definitive single-modality radiotherapy or transoral laser cordectomy preserves voice","Glottic tumours are always radiosensitive lymphomas; R-CHOP chemotherapy is first-line","Vocal cords have rich submucosal lymphatics; total laryngectomy is required for T1a"],"a":1,"e":"Because the true vocal cords (glottis) have sparse/virtually absent lymphatic capillaries, glottic carcinoma presents early with persistent hoarseness and rarely metastasises to cervical lymph nodes at T1 stage. T1aN0M0 glottic squamous cell carcinoma has \u003e90% cure rates with single-modality organ-preserving therapy—either external-beam radiotherapy or transoral CO2 laser cordectomy—without elective neck dissection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02671","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Congenital Neck Swellings","q":"A 14-year-old boy presents with a painless, smooth, cystic 2.5 cm midline swelling just below the hyoid bone that moves upward both when he swallows and when he protrudes his tongue. After confirming a normal orthotopic thyroid gland on ultrasound, what is the standard definitive operation to prevent recurrence?","o":["Simple enucleation of the cyst wall preserving the hyoid bone intact","Incision and drainage followed by tetracycline sclerotherapy","Sistrunk operation: excision of the cyst, entire tract up to the foramen caecum, and the central body of the hyoid bone","Total thyroidectomy with central compartment neck dissection"],"a":2,"e":"A midline subhyoid swelling moving upward with both deglutition and tongue protrusion (due to attachment to the foramen caecum via the thyroglossal tract) is pathognomonic of a thyroglossal duct cyst. Because the tract passes closely through or behind the body of the hyoid bone, simple cystectomy carries a high (~50%) recurrence rate; the Sistrunk procedure excises the cyst, the central body of the hyoid bone, and a core of tongue-base tissue up to the foramen caecum.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02672","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"External Ear Infections","q":"A 68-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes mellitus presents with severe, throbbing nocturnal right ear pain unresponsive to analgesics, purulent otorrhoea, and right lower motor neuron facial nerve palsy. Otoscopy reveals granulation tissue at the floor of the external auditory canal at the bony-cartilaginous junction. Which causative organism and antibiotic strategy are most appropriate?","o":["Streptococcus pyogenes; oral phenoxymethylpenicillin for 7 days","Staphylococcus epidermidis; topical hydrocortisone drops alone for 5 days","Aspergillus niger; simple ear syringing with warm tap water","Pseudomonas aeruginosa; prolonged systemic anti-pseudomonal therapy (e.g., IV ciprofloxacin or piperacillin-tazobactam/cefepime) plus strict glycaemic control"],"a":3,"e":"Malignant (necrotising) otitis externa is an invasive skull-base osteomyelitis occurring predominantly in elderly diabetics and immunocompromised patients, classically presenting with severe nocturnal otalgia, EAC granulation tissue at the bony-cartilaginous junction, and cranial nerve palsies (CN VII first, then IX-XII). Pseudomonas aeruginosa is responsible in \u003e95% of cases and requires prolonged (6-8 weeks) systemic anti-pseudomonal therapy alongside glycaemic optimisation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02673","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Inflammatory Arthritis","q":"A 36-year-old woman presents with 4 months of symmetrical pain and swelling of the bilateral wrists, metacarpophalangeal (MCP), and proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints, sparing the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints, with early morning stiffness lasting 90 minutes. Serum anti-CCP antibody is strongly positive. Which disease-modifying antirheumatic drug (DMARD) is the anchor first-line therapy, and what supplement reduces its mucosal/haematological toxicity?","o":["Weekly Methotrexate with daily or next-day Folic acid supplementation","Allopurinol with colchicine prophylaxis","Oral glucosamine sulphate alone","Daily Hydroxychloroquine combined with lifelong high-dose prednisolone 40 mg/day"],"a":0,"e":"Symmetrical small-joint inflammatory polyarthritis (MCP, PIP, wrist; sparing DIP) \u003e6 weeks with positive anti-CCP (highly specific for RA) indicates Rheumatoid Arthritis. Methotrexate (weekly oral/SC) is the anchor first-line conventional synthetic DMARD, paired with folic acid supplementation to prevent stomatitis, cytopenias, and hepatotoxicity after baseline CBC, LFT, RFT, and viral hepatitis screening.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02674","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Crystal Arthropathies","q":"A 54-year-old obese man with hypertension on hydrochlorothiazide wakes up with excruciating pain, erythema, warmth, and swelling of the right first metatarsophalangeal joint (podagra). Synovial fluid aspiration shows intracellular needle-shaped crystals that are strongly negatively birefringent under polarised light. What is the correct immediate pharmacotherapy?","o":["Start IV furosemide to increase renal uric acid excretion","Start an NSAID (e.g., indomethacin/naproxen) or low-dose colchicine or corticosteroid for the acute flare; do NOT initiate allopurinol during the acute attack","Administer IV vancomycin plus ceftriaxone for presumed septic arthritis without analgesia","Start high-dose allopurinol 300 mg/day immediately as monotherapy without anti-inflammatories"],"a":1,"e":"Negatively birefringent needle-shaped monosodium urate (MSU) crystals confirm acute gout. Acute flares are treated promptly with NSAIDs, low-dose colchicine, or oral/intra-articular glucocorticoids. Initiating urate-lowering therapy (allopurinol/febuxostat) during an acute flare—without prior anti-inflammatory cover—causes rapid serum urate fluctuations that destabilise joint crystals and worsen/prolong the attack.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02675","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Glomerular Diseases","q":"A 7-year-old boy presents with periorbital puffiness, scrotal oedema, ascites, 24-hour urine protein of 4.2 g, serum albumin 1.8 g/dL, serum cholesterol 360 mg/dL, and normal C3/C4 complement levels. Renal biopsy shows normal glomeruli on light microscopy, negative immunofluorescence, and diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes on electron microscopy. What is the diagnosis and expected response to oral prednisolone?","o":["Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis; universally steroid-resistant","Goodpasture syndrome; anti-GBM positive linear IgG on immunofluorescence","Minimal Change Disease; highly steroid-responsive with complete remission in \u003e90% of children","Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis; requires cyclophosphamide pulse therapy"],"a":2,"e":"Minimal Change Disease (MCD) is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children. It demonstrates normal glomeruli on light microscopy, absent immune deposits on immunofluorescence, and diffuse podocyte foot-process effacement on electron microscopy. More than 90% of children achieve complete remission within 4-8 weeks of oral prednisolone therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02676","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)","q":"A 50-year-old man develops oliguria (urine output 200 mL/24 h) and serum creatinine rises from 1.0 to 3.2 mg/dL 48 hours after severe gastroenteritis and hypotension. Laboratory evaluation shows BUN/creatinine ratio 26:1, urine sodium 11 mEq/L, fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) 0.4%, urine osmolality 620 mOsm/kg, and hyaline casts on microscopy. Which diagnosis and management are indicated?","o":["Postrenal obstructive uropathy; urgent bilateral nephrostomy","Intrinsic Acute Tubular Necrosis; immediate loop diuretic infusion to force urine output","Acute interstitial nephritis; high-dose IV methylprednisolone","Prerenal azotaemia; prompt isotonic IV fluid resuscitation (0.9% NaCl or balanced crystalloid)"],"a":3,"e":"Intact tubular sodium and water avidity in hypovolaemic prerenal AKI produces BUN/Cr \u003e 20:1, urine Na+ \u003c 20 mEq/L, FENa \u003c 1%, concentrated urine (\u003e500 mOsm/kg), and benign hyaline casts. Prompt volume restoration with isotonic crystalloids reverses prerenal azotaemia before progression to ischaemic acute tubular necrosis (ATN).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02677","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Peripheral Neuropathies","q":"Two weeks after an episode of watery diarrhoea, a 29-year-old man develops symmetric ascending weakness of both lower limbs progressing to upper limbs over 4 days, global areflexia, and mild facial diplegia without a sensory level or sphincter dysfunction. CSF shows protein 110 mg/dL with 2 lymphocytes/mm3 (albuminocytologic dissociation). Along with serial Forced Vital Capacity (FVC) monitoring, which immunotherapy is indicated?","o":["Intravenous Immunoglobulin (IVIG 0.4 g/kg/day for 5 days) OR Plasmapheresis","High-dose IV methylprednisolone pulse monotherapy","IV acyclovir for 14 days","Oral pyridostigmine with neostigmine"],"a":0,"e":"Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy (Guillain-Barre syndrome, frequently triggered by Campylobacter jejuni) presents with rapidly progressive symmetric ascending flaccid paralysis, areflexia, and CSF albuminocytologic dissociation. Frequent FVC monitoring detects impending neuromuscular respiratory failure (intubate if FVC \u003c 15-20 mL/kg), and either IVIG or plasma exchange hastens recovery equally. Notably, corticosteroids are ineffective and NOT recommended in GBS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02678","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Liver Infections","q":"A 38-year-old man from Bihar with regular alcohol intake presents with 10 days of high fever, dull right upper quadrant pain radiating to the right shoulder, and intercostal tenderness over the lower right chest. Ultrasound shows a solitary 4.5 cm hypoechoic lesion in the posterior-superior right lobe of the liver, \u003e2 cm away from the liver capsule. Amoebic serology is positive. What is the first-line treatment?","o":["Urgent percutaneous cholecystostomy","Oral/IV Metronidazole (750 mg TID for 7-10 days) followed by a luminal amoebicide (paromomycin or diloxanide furoate)","Broad-spectrum antibiotics alone without metronidazole","Immediate emergency surgical laparotomy and right hepatectomy"],"a":1,"e":"An uncomplicated solitary right-lobe amoebic liver abscess (\u003c5 cm, no imminent rupture) responds dramatically within 48-72 hours to tissue amoebicidal therapy with Metronidazole (or Tinidazole), followed by a luminal cyst-eradicating agent (paromomycin or diloxanide furoate) to prevent relapse. Ultrasound-guided aspiration is reserved for abscesses \u003e5-10 cm with imminent rupture, left-lobe abscesses (risk of pericardial rupture), or failure of medical therapy after 72 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02679","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Obstructive Jaundice","q":"A 64-year-old man presents with painless, progressive deep jaundice, pale clay-coloured stools, dark urine, generalised pruritus, and a smooth, non-tender, palpable globular mass in the right hypochondrium moving with respiration. According to Courvoisier\u0027s law, what is the most likely underlying cause?","o":["Gilbert syndrome","Chronic calculous cholecystitis with a gallstone impacted in the common bile duct","Malignant distal biliary obstruction (e.g., carcinoma of the head of pancreas or periampullary tumour)","Acute viral hepatitis A"],"a":2,"e":"Courvoisier\u0027s law states that in a patient with obstructive jaundice, a palpable, distended, non-tender gallbladder is unlikely to be due to gallstones—because gallstone disease causes chronic cholecystitis with a fibrotic, shrunken, non-distensible gallbladder—and instead points toward malignant distal common bile duct obstruction (pancreatic head or periampullary carcinoma) stretching a previously normal gallbladder wall.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02680","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Acute Scrotum","q":"A 15-year-old boy wakes up 2 hours ago with sudden severe left scrotal pain and vomiting. Examination reveals a tender, swollen, high-riding left testis lying transversely (\u0027bell-clapper\u0027 deformity), absent left cremasteric reflex, and pain that does NOT decrease on elevating the scrotum (negative Prehn\u0027s sign). Urinalysis is normal. What is the immediate management?","o":["Immediate left radical inguinal orchidectomy without checking viability","Schedule an outpatient colour Doppler ultrasound 24 hours later","Oral ciprofloxacin for 10 days with scrotal elevation and ice packs","Emergency scrotal exploration, detorsion, and bilateral orchidopexy without delaying for imaging"],"a":3,"e":"Sudden severe scrotal pain in an adolescent with a high-riding transverse testis, absent cremasteric reflex, and negative Prehn\u0027s sign is testicular torsion until proven otherwise. Testicular salvage approaches 90-100% if detorted within 6 hours. When clinical suspicion is high, immediate surgical exploration (with contralateral orchidopexy because the bell-clapper anatomical anomaly is bilateral) must NEVER be delayed for Doppler ultrasound.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02681","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)","q":"A 67-year-old man with moderate-to-severe lower urinary tract symptoms, prostate volume 58 mL on ultrasound, and baseline serum PSA 3.2 ng/mL (benign DRE) is started on combination therapy with Tamsulosin 0.4 mg and Finasteride 5 mg daily. After 12 months of therapy, his serum PSA should be interpreted knowing that Finasteride typically causes what effect?","o":["Reduces serum PSA by approximately 50% (requiring doubling the measured value for prostate cancer screening)","Causes immediate acute urinary retention by contracting prostatic smooth muscle","Doubles serum PSA within 3 months","Has no effect on prostate volume or PSA"],"a":0,"e":"Tamsulosin (selective alpha-1A adrenoceptor antagonist) rapidly relaxes prostatic and bladder-neck smooth muscle (dynamic component) without altering PSA or prostate volume. Finasteride (5-alpha-reductase inhibitor) blocks conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, shrinking enlarged prostates (\u003e40 mL) over 6 months and lowering serum PSA by ~50% (so measured PSA after \u003e= 6 months is multiplied by 2 when screening for malignancy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02682","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Paediatric Upper Limb Fractures","q":"A 7-year-old child falls on an outstretched hand and presents with a painful swollen elbow. Radiographs confirm a completely displaced extension-type (Gartland Type III) supracondylar fracture of the humerus with posteromedial displacement. When asked to make an \u0027OK\u0027 sign with the thumb and index finger, the child cannot flex the interphalangeal joint of the thumb or distal interphalangeal joint of the index finger. Which nerve is most commonly injured in extension-type supracondylar fracture?","o":["Posterior interosseous nerve","Anterior interosseous nerve (branch of median nerve)","Suprascapular nerve","Axillary nerve"],"a":1,"e":"In extension-type supracondylar humerus fractures in children, the Anterior Interosseous Nerve (AIN, pure motor branch of the median nerve supplying flexor pollicis longus, pronator quadratus, and FDP to index/middle fingers) is the single most commonly injured nerve, producing inability to form an \u0027OK\u0027 sign (pinch flattens). Posteromedial displacement specifically places the radial nerve at risk as well, while the brachial artery must also be carefully checked.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02683","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Electrical Burns","q":"A 28-year-old electrician sustains a 11,000-volt high-voltage electrical contact injury with small entry and exit wounds on the right hand and left foot (total visible cutaneous burn \u003c 10% TBSA). Two hours later, his right forearm is tense and painful on passive finger extension, and his urine turns dark reddish-brown (myoglobinuria). Why is the standard Parkland formula inadequate here, and what is the priority management?","o":["Treat as uncomplicated haematuria with nitrofurantoin","Give oral fluids only because cutaneous TBSA is \u003c 10%","Deep muscular myonecrosis exceeds visible skin burn; maintain higher urine output (1-1.5 mL/kg/h) with IV crystalloids (+/- mannitol/bicarbonate) and perform urgent forearm fasciotomy","Administer IV gentamicin and delay fasciotomy for 48 hours"],"a":2,"e":"High-voltage electrical current travels along low-resistance neurovascular bundles and heats high-resistance bone, causing massive hidden deep muscle necrosis despite minimal skin wounds (\u0027iceberg effect\u0027). Rhabdomyolysis causes acute pigment nephropathy (requiring vigorous fluid resuscitation targeting urine output 1-1.5 mL/kg/h or 75-100 mL/h until urine clears, with close ECG/K+ monitoring) and compartment syndrome (pain on passive stretch, tense compartment) requiring emergency surgical fasciotomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02684","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM)","q":"A 28-year-old primigravida at 26 weeks of gestation undergoes screening for Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) at a government antenatal clinic using the MoHFW India / DIPSI single-step 75-g oral glucose challenge test (irrespective of last meal timing). Which 2-hour plasma glucose cutoff confirms the diagnosis of GDM, and what is the initial treatment?","o":["Fasting glucose \u003e= 70 mg/dL; start high-dose insulin glargine immediately","2-hour plasma glucose \u003e= 100 mg/dL; immediate Caesarean section at 34 weeks","2-hour plasma glucose \u003e= 200 mg/dL; start glibenclamide immediately","2-hour plasma glucose \u003e= 140 mg/dL; initiate Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) and physical exercise for 2 weeks before considering insulin/metformin"],"a":3,"e":"Under MoHFW India / DIPSI guidelines, a single-step 75-g oral glucose test given regardless of fasting state diagnoses GDM if the 2-hour venous plasma glucose is \u003e= 140 mg/dL (tested at first ANC visit and repeated at 24-28 weeks if initially negative). Initial management consists of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) and light physical activity for 2 weeks; pharmacotherapy (insulin, or metformin after 20 weeks per national guidelines) is added if 2-hour postprandial glucose remains \u003e= 120 mg/dL (fasting \u003e= 95 mg/dL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02685","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Rh Isoimmunisation","q":"A 25-year-old Rh-negative primigravida (husband Rh-positive) has a negative Indirect Coombs Test (ICT) at booking and again at 28 weeks, when she receives 300 mcg IM Anti-D immunoglobulin. At 39 weeks she delivers a healthy Rh-positive newborn with negative Direct Coombs Test on cord blood. What postpartum prophylaxis is indicated?","o":["Administer 300 mcg Anti-D immunoglobulin IM within 72 hours of delivery (adjusting upward only if Kleihauer-Betke shows fetomaternal haemorrhage \u003e 30 mL whole blood)","Administer oral RhoGAM tablets to the newborn baby","Withhold Anti-D unless the baby develops physiological jaundice","No postpartum Anti-D is needed because she received an antenatal dose at 28 weeks"],"a":0,"e":"An unsensitised Rh-negative mother (ICT negative) delivering an Rh-positive infant must receive 300 mcg of Anti-D IgG intramuscularly within 72 hours of delivery—regardless of having received the routine 28-week antenatal dose (whose half-life is ~21-24 days and covers only pregnancy until term). One 300 mcg dose neutralises up to 30 mL of fetal whole blood (15 mL fetal RBCs); larger fetomaternal bleeds detected on Kleihauer-Betke test require additional Anti-D.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02686","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Carcinoma Cervix","q":"A 48-year-old multiparous woman presents with postcoital bleeding and foul-smelling watery vaginal discharge. Speculum and bimanual/rectovaginal examination reveal a 4.5 cm friable exophytic cervical growth extending into the proximal left parametrium, not reaching the pelvic sidewall, with no hydronephrosis on imaging. Biopsy shows invasive squamous cell carcinoma (FIGO Stage IIB). What is the definitive standard treatment?","o":["Total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy alone","Concurrent chemoradiation (external-beam pelvic radiotherapy + weekly Cisplatin + intracavitary brachytherapy)","Wertheim\u0027s radical hysterectomy with pelvic lymphadenectomy alone","Cryotherapy of the cervix in the outpatient clinic"],"a":1,"e":"Parametrial invasion places cervical carcinoma into FIGO Stage IIB (locally advanced disease). Radical hysterectomy (Wertheim\u0027s) is reserved for early-stage disease without parametrial spread (Stages IA2, IB1, IB2, select IIA1) because surgery in Stage IIB fails to achieve clear lateral margins and requires adjuvant radiation anyway, compounding morbidity. Definitive concurrent chemoradiation (EBRT + weekly cisplatin radiosensitiser + brachytherapy) is the gold standard for Stage IIB-IVA.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02687","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Cyanotic Congenital Heart Disease","q":"An 11-month-old infant with Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF) begins crying vigorously after waking up, rapidly developing deep central cyanosis, tachypnoea, and lethargy; on auscultation, the previously loud pulmonary ejection systolic murmur has almost disappeared. What is the immediate physiological goal and bedside management?","o":["Administer IV furosemide and nitroprusside immediately to lower systemic blood pressure","Give oral digoxin loading dose and place flat in Trendelenburg position without oxygen","Place infant in knee-chest position (increases systemic vascular resistance), administer 100% oxygen, IV morphine, and isotonic fluid bolus to reduce right-to-left shunting","Perform emergency pericardiocentesis"],"a":2,"e":"During a hypercyanotic (\u0027Tet\u0027) spell, infundibular spasm increases right ventricular outflow tract resistance (causing the murmur to soften/disappear as pulmonary flow drops) and shunts deoxygenated blood right-to-left across the VSD. Immediate knee-chest positioning kinks femoral arteries to raise systemic vascular resistance (SVR), while calming/IV morphine relieves infundibular spasm and hyperpnoea, 100% O2 aids oxygenation, and IV fluids/propranolol/phenylephrine further restore pulmonary blood flow.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02688","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Chromosomal Syndromes","q":"A newborn with hypotonia, upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthic folds, Brushfield spots, single palmar crease, and a wide sandal gap is confirmed by karyotyping to have Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome). Which congenital heart defect is MOST characteristically associated with Down syndrome?","o":["Coarctation of the aorta","Total anomalous pulmonary venous connection (TAPVC)","Truncus arteriosus","Atrioventricular septal defect (endocardial cushion defect)"],"a":3,"e":"About 40-50% of infants with Down syndrome have congenital heart disease; an Atrioventricular Septal Defect (AVSD / endocardial cushion defect) is the most characteristic lesion (followed by VSD and secundum ASD). Down syndrome is also associated with duodenal atresia (\u0027double bubble\u0027 sign), Hirschsprung disease, congenital hypothyroidism, atlantoaxial instability, and increased leukaemia risk.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02689","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Midbrain Brainstem Syndromes","q":"A 61-year-old man presents with acute right-sided ptosis, a \u0027down and out\u0027 right eye with dilated pupil, and spastic hemiparesis of the left arm, leg, and lower left face (without tremor or ataxia). Occlusion of paramedian branches of the posterior cerebral artery affecting which midbrain structures produces this syndrome?","o":["Right crus cerebri (corticospinal/corticobulbar tracts) and exiting right oculomotor (CN III) nerve fascicles (Weber syndrome)","Right red nucleus and superior cerebellar peduncle decussation (Benedikt syndrome)","Right dorsal tectum (superior colliculi) causing upward gaze palsy (Parinaud syndrome)","Left medial lemniscus and dorsal spinocerebellar tract"],"a":0,"e":"Weber syndrome (ventral midbrain syndrome) results from infarction of the ventral midbrain tegmentum/basis pedunculi supplied by paramedian branches of the posterior cerebral artery. Involvement of the ipsilateral (right) oculomotor (CN III) fascicles causes ipsilateral ptosis, dilated non-reactive pupil, and lateral/inferior eye deviation, while damage to the adjacent crus cerebri (carrying descending corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers prior to medullary/pontine decussation) produces contralateral (left) hemiparesis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02690","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Pontine and Medullary Vascular Syndromes","q":"A patient develops ipsilateral lower motor neuron facial paralysis (inability to wrinkle the forehead or close the eye), ipsilateral lateral rectus palsy (medial strabismus), and contralateral hemiparesis sparing the face. Damage to which brainstem region and descending/exiting fibers produces this presentation (Millard-Gubler syndrome)?","o":["Medial medulla involving the hypoglossal (CN XII) nerve, pyramid, and medial lemniscus","Ventral caudal pons involving CN VI and CN VII fascicles along with the corticospinal tract","Midbrain periaqueductal gray involving the trochlear (CN IV) nucleus","Dorsolateral pons involving the spinal trigeminal nucleus and vestibular nuclei"],"a":1,"e":"Millard-Gubler syndrome is a ventral caudal pontine lesion (typically basilar artery paramedian/short circumferential branch infarct) damaging (1) exiting abducens (CN VI) fascicles -\u003e ipsilateral medial strabismus/loss of abduction, (2) intrapontine facial (CN VII) fascicles -\u003e ipsilateral complete LMN facial palsy, and (3) corticospinal fibers in the basis pontis -\u003e contralateral spastic hemiparesis of the limbs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02691","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Trigeminal Nerve (CN V)","q":"Through which skull base foramina do the ophthalmic (V1), maxillary (V2), and mandibular (V3) divisions of the trigeminal nerve exit the middle cranial fossa, and which nerves form the afferent and efferent limbs of the corneal blink reflex?","o":["V1: optic canal, V2: foramen ovale, V3: foramen spinosum; afferent = CN II, efferent = CN III","V1: foramen lacerum, V2: foramen rotundum, V3: jugular foramen; afferent = CN VII, efferent = V1","V1: superior orbital fissure, V2: foramen rotundum, V3: foramen ovale; corneal reflex afferent = V1 (nasociliary), efferent = CN VII (temporal/zygomatic branches to orbicularis oculi)","V1: superior orbital fissure, V2: infraorbital canal, V3: stylomastoid foramen; afferent = V2, efferent = V3"],"a":2,"e":"The three divisions of the trigeminal nerve exit the middle cranial fossa via: V1 (ophthalmic) through the superior orbital fissure, V2 (maxillary) through the foramen rotundum into the pterygopalatine fossa, and V3 (mandibular, carrying motor root to muscles of mastication) through the foramen ovale into the infratemporal fossa. Touching the cornea activates sensory nasociliary fibers of V1 (afferent limb), synapsing in the spinal/main sensory trigeminal nuclei and bilaterally projecting to facial motor nuclei to drive orbicularis oculi contraction via CN VII (efferent limb).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02692","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Posterior Skull Base Cranial Nerves","q":"A glomus jugulare tumor eroding the right jugular foramen produces loss of the right gag reflex, uvular deviation to the left, hoarseness (right vocal cord paralysis), loss of taste on the posterior one-third of the right tongue, and weakness shrugging the right shoulder and turning the head to the left. Which three cranial nerves pass through the jugular foramen and are compressed here?","o":["Trigeminal (CN V), glossopharyngeal (CN IX), and hypoglossal (CN XII) nerves","Vagus (CN X), accessory (CN XI), and hypoglossal (CN XII) nerves","Facial (CN VII), vestibulocochlear (CN VIII), and hypoglossal (CN XII) nerves","Glossopharyngeal (CN IX), vagus (CN X), and spinal accessory (CN XI) nerves"],"a":3,"e":"The jugular foramen transmits the glossopharyngeal (CN IX), vagus (CN X), and spinal accessory (CN XI) nerves alongside the internal jugular vein (and inferior petrosal sinus). Compression (Vernet syndrome) causes: CN IX loss -\u003e absent ipsilateral gag afferent and loss of general/taste sensation from posterior one-third of tongue; CN X loss -\u003e ipsilateral palatal droop with uvular deviation toward the intact left side and hoarseness; CN XI loss -\u003e ipsilateral trapezius weakness (shoulder shrug) and sternocleidomastoid weakness (inability to turn chin to opposite side).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02693","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Retroperitoneal Vascular Relations","q":"A thin 20-year-old woman presents with intermittent gross hematuria, left flank pain, and pelvic congestion. Contrast CT shows compression of a major vein in the narrow acute angle between the abdominal aorta posteriorly and the origin of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) anteriorly (nutcracker phenomenon). Which vein is compressed, and why does this predispose men with the same compression to a left-sided varicocele?","o":["Left renal vein; the left testicular (gonadal) vein drains perpendicularly into the left renal vein rather than directly into the IVC","Right renal vein; the right testicular vein drains into the portal vein","Inferior mesenteric vein; it empties into the left internal iliac vein","Splenic vein; it communicates directly with the pampiniform plexus"],"a":0,"e":"The left renal vein travels rightward across the midline anterior to the abdominal aorta and directly posterior to the origin of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA)—passing through the aortomesenteric angle—to enter the inferior vena cava. Marked loss of retroperitoneal fat narrows this angle, compressing the left renal vein (nutcracker syndrome) and raising venous pressure in its tributaries, notably the left gonadal (testicular/ovarian) vein, which drains at a 90-degree angle into the left renal vein (whereas the right gonadal vein drains obliquely directly into the IVC).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02694","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Ureter Anatomy and Relations","q":"Which three sites represent the normal anatomical constrictions where ureteric calculi most frequently lodge, and what is the critical surgical relation of the pelvic ureter in females (\u0027water under the bridge\u0027)?","o":["Aortic bifurcation, ischial spine, and external urethral meatus; the vaginal artery pierces the ureter","Pelviureteric junction (PUJ), crossing of the pelvic brim (bifurcation of common iliac artery), and vesicoureteric junction (VUJ); the uterine artery crosses anteriorly/superiorly over the ureter near the lateral vaginal fornix","Renal hilum, internal inguinal ring, and bladder neck; the ovarian artery passes inferior to the ureter","Mid-abdominal segment, obturator foramen, and prostatic urethra; the round ligament crosses posterior to the ureter"],"a":1,"e":"The three classic sites of physiological ureteric narrowing (stone impaction sites) are: (1) the pelviureteric junction (PUJ), (2) the pelvic brim where the ureter crosses anterior to the bifurcation of the common iliac artery (into external and internal iliac arteries), and (3) the vesicoureteric junction (VUJ, intramural oblique course through the detrusor—the narrowest site). During hysterectomy, ligation of the uterine artery ~1.5-2 cm lateral to the cervix places the ureter at high risk because the uterine artery crosses superiorly/anteriorly over the ureter (\u0027water under the bridge\u0027, analogously crossed by the vas deferens in males).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02695","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Urethral Trauma and Perineal Fascial Pouches","q":"A 30-year-old man falls astride a metal bar (straddle injury), rupturing his bulbar urethra inferior to the perineal membrane while Buck fascia is torn. Urine and blood extravasate into the scrotum, shaft of the penis, and anterior abdominal wall up to the clavicles, while sparing the thighs and posterior perianal triangle. Which fascial layer limits this spread?","o":["Superficial perineal fascia (Colles fascia) continuous with dartos fascia and Scarpa fascia, attached posteriorly to the perineal body/posterior edge of perineal membrane and laterally to the fascia lata","Deep perineal (Buck) fascia intact around the corpus spongiosum","Pelvic floor levator ani fascia confining urine to the retropubic space of Retzius","Transversalis fascia preventing spread above the umbilicus"],"a":0,"e":"Rupture of the bulbous (spongy) urethra with disruption of Buck fascia allows urine to escape into the superficial perineal pouch deep to Colles fascia (the membranous layer of superficial perineal fascia). Because Colles fascia attaches posteriorly to the posterior margin of the perineal membrane and laterally to the ischiopubic rami/fascia lata (Holden line), urine cannot track posteriorly into the ischiorectal fossa or downward into the thighs; instead, it follows the continuous fascial plane anteriorly through scrotal dartos fascia, penile superficial fascia, and Scarpa fascia onto the anterior abdominal wall.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02696","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Lymphatic Drainage of Pelvis and Gonads","q":"Assertion (A): Testicular seminoma and ovarian epithelial carcinoma metastasize primarily to the para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes near L1-L2, whereas carcinoma of the scrotal skin or lower third of the vagina metastasizes first to the superficial inguinal lymph nodes. Reason (R): Gonads develop embryologically on the posterior abdominal wall near the lumbar level and carry their neurovascular and lymphatic pedicles along the gonadal vessels during descent, whereas the scrotum and lower perineum derive from surface ectoderm/urogenital folds drained by superficial inguinal nodes.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Embryologically, the testes and ovaries originate from the urogenital ridges on the posterior abdominal wall and receive arterial supply from the abdominal aorta (L2) and lymphatic drainage to the para-aortic/pre-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes. During descent into the scrotum or pelvis, they retain these lumbar lymphatic channels along the gonadal vessels. Conversely, the scrotal wall, penile skin, vulva, distal vagina (below the hymen), and anal canal below the pectinate line drain to the superficial inguinal lymph nodes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02697","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Pterygopalatine Fossa","q":"On a dry human skull at an anatomy OSPE station, a probe is passed medially from the pterygopalatine fossa into the posterior nasal cavity (just posterior to the superior/middle meatus), and a second probe is passed posteriorly into the middle cranial fossa toward the trigeminal cave. Through which two openings do these probes pass, and which major artery traverses the first opening to cause severe posterior epistaxis?","o":["Inferior orbital fissure and foramen ovale (transmitting V3)","Pterygoid canal and incisive canal (transmitting the greater palatine artery)","Sphenopalatine foramen (transmitting the sphenopalatine artery into the nasal cavity) and foramen rotundum (transmitting V2 from the middle cranial fossa)","Stylomastoid foramen and carotid canal (transmitting the internal carotid artery)"],"a":2,"e":"The pterygopalatine fossa is a crucial deep neurovascular crossroads located inferior to the orbital apex between the maxilla anteriorly and the pterygoid process posteriorly. It communicates medially with the nasal cavity via the sphenopalatine foramen (transmitting the sphenopalatine artery—a branch of the 3rd part of the maxillary artery and the chief culprit in refractory posterior epistaxis—along with nasopalatine/posterior superior nasal nerves) and posteriorly with the middle cranial fossa via the foramen rotundum (transmitting the maxillary nerve, V2) and pterygoid (Vidian) canal.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02698","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Gastric and Colonic Motility","q":"Which vagovagal reflex mechanism allows the proximal stomach to accommodate a large meal with minimal rise in intragastric pressure, and what neurotransmitters mediate relaxation of the gastric fundus and internal anal sphincter?","o":["Gastric emptying propulsion; mediated exclusively by sympathetic norepinephrine onto beta-1 receptors","Segmental haustration; mediated by somatic pudendal acetylcholine","Migrating motor complex; mediated by motilin binding parietal H2 receptors","Receptive relaxation (adaptive accommodation); mediated by non-adrenergic non-cholinergic vagal enteric neurons releasing vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) and nitric oxide (NO)"],"a":3,"e":"Swallowing and esophageal/gastric distension trigger vagovagal inhibitory reflexes (receptive relaxation and gastric accommodation) in which preganglionic vagal fibers stimulate inhibitory enteric myenteric neurons releasing nitric oxide (NO) and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) to relax the gastric fundus and body smooth muscle without raising luminal pressure. Similarly, rectal distension triggers the rectoanal inhibitory reflex via enteric NO/VIP release to relax the smooth-muscle internal anal sphincter, whereas the striated external anal sphincter is under voluntary somatic pudendal nerve (S2-S4) control.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02699","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Digestion and Absorption","q":"A patient with Crohn disease undergoes surgical resection of 120 cm of the terminal ileum. Within months, he develops fat malabsorption (steatorrhea), cholesterol gallstones, and megaloblastic anemia. Failure to actively reabsorb which two substances in the terminal ileum explains this triad?","o":["Conjugated bile salts (via apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter, ASBT) and intrinsic factor-vitamin B12 complexes (via cubilin receptors)","Free fatty acids (chylomicrons) and duodenal iron (via DMT1)","Micellar cholesterol and gastric pepsinogen","Unconjugated bilirubin and intrinsic factor alone in the jejunum"],"a":0,"e":"Approximately 95% of secreted conjugated bile salts are actively reclaimed in the terminal ileum via the apical Na+-dependent bile acid transporter (ASBT) and returned via portal blood to the liver (enterohepatic circulation, recycling 6-8 times/day). Extensive terminal ileal resection depletes the bile acid pool faster than hepatic synthesis can compensate—impairing micellar solubilization of dietary lipids (steatorrhea) and supersaturating bile with cholesterol (cholesterol gallstones). Simultaneously, loss of terminal ileal cubilin-amnionless receptors prevents uptake of intrinsic factor-cobalamin complexes, causing vitamin B12-deficiency megaloblastic anemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02700","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Tubular Glucose Transport","q":"A patient with poorly controlled diabetes mellitus has a GFR of 120 mL/min, plasma glucose concentration of 400 mg/dL (4.0 mg/mL), and a renal tubular maximum for glucose (TmG) of 375 mg/min. Assuming plasma glucose is far above splay, what are the filtered load of glucose and the rate of urinary glucose excretion?","o":["Filtered load = 375 mg/min; urinary glucose excretion = 0 mg/min","Filtered load = 480 mg/min; urinary glucose excretion = 105 mg/min","Filtered load = 240 mg/min; urinary glucose excretion = 135 mg/min","Filtered load = 480 mg/min; urinary glucose excretion = 480 mg/min"],"a":1,"e":"Glucose is freely filtered at the glomerulus, so Filtered Load = GFR x Plasma Glucose = 120 mL/min x 4.0 mg/mL = 480 mg/min. Above the tubular transport maximum (TmG = 375 mg/min via proximal tubular SGLT2/SGLT1 cotransporters), all excess filtered glucose escapes reabsorption and is excreted in urine: Excretion rate = Filtered Load - TmG = 480 - 375 = 105 mg/min (producing osmotic diuresis and glucosuria).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02701","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Autoregulation of GFR and Renal Blood Flow","q":"Assertion (A): An acute rise in GFR increases NaCl delivery to the macula densa, triggering reflex vasoconstriction of the afferent arteriole to restore GFR back toward baseline (tubuloglomerular feedback). Reason (R): Increased NaCl uptake by macula densa cells via apical NKCC2 cotransporters increases ATP hydrolysis to adenosine, which acts on vascular A1 receptors to constrict the adjacent afferent arteriole while suppressing juxtaglomerular renin release.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) operates at the juxtaglomerular apparatus to stabilize single-nephron GFR and distal solute load across mean arterial pressures of ~80-180 mmHg. Elevated GFR delivers more NaCl to the cortical thick ascending limb/macula densa, enhancing Na+-K+-2Cl- (NKCC2) transport and Na+/K+-ATPase consumption; released ATP/adenosine activates A1 adenosine receptors on afferent arteriolar smooth muscle, causing afferent vasoconstriction (lowering glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure and GFR) while inhibiting renin secretion from granular JG cells.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02702","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Distal Nephron Sodium and Potassium Handling","q":"A 42-year-old man with an aldosterone-producing adrenal adenoma (Conn syndrome) has hypertension (172/102 mmHg), serum K+ 2.7 mEq/L, and metabolic alkalosis, yet demonstrates NO peripheral edema and exhibits normal daily urinary Na+ excretion matching his intake. Why does extracellular volume expansion plateau without persistent edema (\u0027aldosterone escape\u0027)?","o":["Aldosterone receptors on principal cells completely downregulate within 48 hours, causing hyperkalemia","ADH secretion stops completely, causing massive hypernatremia","Initial Na+ retention raises blood pressure and atrial stretch, stimulating atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) release and pressure natriuresis to restore Na+ balance while K+ and H+ wasting continue","Macula densa cells secrete excess renin to enhance proximal Na+ reabsorption"],"a":2,"e":"Aldosterone binds intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors in late distal/collecting duct principal cells (upregulating luminal ENaC, ROMK, and basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase) and alpha-intercalated cells (upregulating H+-ATPase). Initial Na+ and water retention expands ECF volume and raises arterial pressure; after ~2-3 L volume gain, atrial stretch triggers ANP secretion (increasing GFR and inhibiting collecting duct ENaC) alongside pressure natriuresis in proximal/medullary segments. Thus, urinary Na+ excretion escapes aldosterone to match intake (preventing edema), but high distal delivery of Na+ and flow continues to drive K+ and H+ secretion (persistent hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02703","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Respiratory Compensation in Metabolic Acidosis","q":"A patient with diabetic ketoacidosis has arterial blood gas values showing pH 7.22, HCO3- 12 mEq/L, and measured PaCO2 26 mmHg. Using Winter\u0027s formula, what is the expected compensatory PaCO2 range, and how should the measured PaCO2 of 26 mmHg be interpreted?","o":["Expected PaCO2 = 38 to 42 mmHg; uncompensated metabolic acidosis","Expected PaCO2 = 34 to 38 mmHg; concurrent respiratory alkalosis","Expected PaCO2 = 14 to 18 mmHg; concurrent respiratory acidosis","Expected PaCO2 = 24 to 28 mmHg (26 +/- 2 mmHg); appropriate respiratory compensation (simple metabolic acidosis)"],"a":3,"e":"In metabolic acidosis, acidemia stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid bodies) to increase alveolar ventilation (Kussmaul breathing), lowering PaCO2. Winter\u0027s formula predicts expected compensatory PaCO2 = (1.5 x [HCO3-]) + 8 +/- 2. Substituting [HCO3-] = 12 mEq/L gives (1.5 x 12) + 8 +/- 2 = 18 + 8 +/- 2 = 26 +/- 2 mmHg (range 24-28 mmHg). Because the measured PaCO2 of 26 mmHg lies squarely within this expected range, respiratory compensation is appropriate (no superimposed primary respiratory disorder).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02704","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Pulmonary Function Testing (Spirometry)","q":"In a pulmonary function laboratory, a 58-year-old smoker undergoes spirometry and helium dilution testing showing FEV1/FVC = 54% (reduced), markedly elevated Residual Volume (RV = 175% predicted), elevated Total Lung Capacity (TLC = 128% predicted), and a scooped/concave expiratory curve on the flow-volume loop. Why cannot simple spirometry measure RV or TLC directly, and which pattern do these results represent?","o":["Spirometry cannot measure air remaining in the lungs after maximal expiration (RV), requiring helium dilution, nitrogen washout, or body plethysmography; obstructive lung disease with air trapping (emphysema)","Body plethysmography measures anatomical dead space only; interstitial pulmonary fibrosis","Helium dilution directly measures dead space only; normal pulmonary function","Spirometry measures RV directly via forced inspiratory capacity; restrictive lung disease"],"a":0,"e":"A standard bell or flow spirometer records only dynamic air volumes that enter and exit the mouth (TV, IRV, ERV, IC, VC, FEV1, FVC); because Residual Volume (RV) remains trapped in alveoli after maximal expiration, RV (and consequently Functional Residual Capacity = ERV + RV and Total Lung Capacity = VC + RV) cannot be measured by simple spirometry and requires inert gas (helium) dilution, nitrogen washout, or Boyle\u0027s law body plethysmography. An FEV1/FVC ratio \u003c70% with elevated RV and TLC and a concave (\u0027scooped\u0027) expiratory flow-volume loop indicates obstructive lung disease with hyperinflation and air trapping (classic emphysema).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02705","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Posterior Pituitary (ADH / Vasopressin)","q":"A 30-year-old man recovering from pituitary transsphenoidal surgery develops polyuria (7 L/day) and polydipsia with serum Na+ 149 mEq/L and baseline urine osmolality 110 mOsm/kg. After 6 hours of supervised water deprivation, urine osmolality remains 130 mOsm/kg while plasma osmolality rises to 306 mOsm/kg; following subcutaneous desmopressin (DDAVP), urine osmolality jumps to 620 mOsm/kg. What is the diagnosis and underlying receptor mechanism?","o":["Primary psychogenic polydipsia; endogenous ADH raises urine osmolality \u003e600 mOsm/kg during water deprivation alone","Central (neurogenic) diabetes insipidus; exogenous desmopressin activates collecting duct basolateral V2 (Gs-cAMP) receptors to insert apical AQP2 water channels","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus; collecting duct V2 receptors fail to respond to DDAVP","Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH); water deprivation produces hyponatremia and hyperosmolar urine"],"a":1,"e":"During water deprivation, rising plasma osmolality (\u003e295 mOsm/kg) maximally stimulates hypothalamic supraoptic/paraventricular osmoreceptors to release ADH (arginine vasopressin). Failure to concentrate urine during dehydration (remaining \u003c300 mOsm/kg) rules out primary polydipsia and confirms diabetes insipidus. A \u003e50% surge in urine osmolality after desmopressin (a selective V2 agonist) proves that renal collecting duct basolateral V2 receptors (Gs -\u003e cAMP -\u003e PKA -\u003e apical AQP2 vesicle exocytosis) are intact and that endogenous hypothalamic-neurohypophyseal ADH secretion is deficient (central diabetes insipidus).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02706","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Neural Regulation of Blood Pressure","q":"A patient with an acute expanding epidural hematoma develops severe hypertension (BP 205/115 mmHg), progressive bradycardia (HR 42/min), and irregular Cheyne-Stokes/ataxic respirations (Cushing triad). What physiological sequence produces both the severe hypertension and the paradoxical bradycardia?","o":["Carotid body hypoxia inhibits sympathetic outflow, causing reflex tachycardia","Direct parasympathetic stimulation of the heart raises BP, followed by peripheral beta-2 vasodilation","Cerebral ischemia from raised ICP triggers intense medullary sympathetic vasoconstriction (raising BP to maintain cerebral perfusion pressure), which secondarily activates carotid/aortic baroreceptors (CN IX/X) to drive vagal bradycardia","Loss of baroreceptor afferents causes severe hypotension and sympathetic withdrawal"],"a":2,"e":"When intracranial pressure (ICP) approaches mean arterial pressure (MAP), cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP - ICP) falls critically. Brainstem ischemia stimulates the rostral ventrolateral medulla (vasomotor center) to unleash massive sympathetic alpha-1 peripheral vasoconstriction, driving systemic MAP up to preserve cerebral blood flow. This pronounced arterial hypertension stretches high-pressure baroreceptors in the carotid sinus (afferent CN IX) and aortic arch (afferent CN X), which project to the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) and activate nucleus ambiguus/dorsal motor vagal efferents—producing reflex bradycardia alongside medullary respiratory center compression (irregular breathing).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02707","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Lipoprotein Metabolism","q":"A 10-year-old boy presents with recurrent episodes of acute pancreatitis, eruptive xanthomas on his buttocks, lipemia retinalis, and fasting plasma that forms a thick creamy supernatant layer over clear infranatant when refrigerated overnight (serum triglycerides 2,400 mg/dL, normal LDL/HDL). Deficiency of lipoprotein lipase (LPL) or which obligatory apolipoprotein cofactor causes this Type I hyperlipoproteinemia?","o":["Apolipoprotein E (ApoE)","Apolipoprotein B-100 (ApoB-100)","Apolipoprotein A-I (ApoA-I)","Apolipoprotein C-II (ApoC-II)"],"a":3,"e":"Nascent chylomicrons (assembled with intestinal ApoB-48) and VLDL (hepatic ApoB-100) acquire ApoC-II and ApoE from HDL in plasma. ApoC-II is the indispensable cofactor that activates capillary endothelial lipoprotein lipase (LPL) in adipose tissue, skeletal muscle, and heart to hydrolyze core triacylglycerols into free fatty acids. Autosomal recessive deficiency of either LPL or ApoC-II (Fredrickson Type I familial chylomicronemia) prevents chylomicron clearance, producing milky lipemic serum with a creamy top layer, eruptive xanthomas, and severe pancreatitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02708","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Fatty Acid Oxidation","q":"An 18-month-old child develops vomiting, lethargy, seizures, and severe hypoketotic hypoglycemia (undetectable urine ketones) after a 20-hour viral-illness fast, with elevated plasma C8-C10 acylcarnitines and urinary dicarboxylic acids. Deficiency of which enzyme explains this presentation, and why is ketogenesis impaired?","o":["Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD); impaired mitochondrial beta-oxidation deprives hepatocytes of acetyl-CoA and ATP/NADH needed for gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I (CPT-I) activated by malonyl-CoA; excess ketone bodies cause ketoacidosis","Acetyl-CoA carboxylase; excess fatty acid synthesis blocks glycogenolysis","Hormone-sensitive lipase; inability to release free fatty acids from liver"],"a":0,"e":"MCAD deficiency is the most common inherited disorder of mitochondrial fatty acid beta-oxidation. During fasting, adipose lipolysis mobilizes fatty acids to the liver, but blocked beta-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acyl-CoAs prevents generation of acetyl-CoA (for ketogenesis and allosteric activation of pyruvate carboxylase in gluconeogenesis) and ATP/NADH—causing life-threatening hypoketotic hypoglycemia. Blocked beta-oxidation shunts fatty acids into microsomal omega-oxidation, excreting C6-C10 dicarboxylic acids in urine alongside accumulated C8-octanoylcarnitine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02709","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Purine Salvage Pathway","q":"A 4-year-old boy presents with choreoathetosis, spasticity, orange uric acid crystals (\u0027orange sand\u0027) in his diaper, hyperuricemia (11.4 mg/dL), and compulsive lip- and finger-biting self-mutilation. What is the X-linked enzyme defect, and why does it simultaneously accelerate de novo purine synthesis?","o":["Xanthine oxidase deficiency; hypoxanthine cannot be oxidized to uric acid","Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT) deficiency; unused PRPP accumulates and removes negative feedback on PRPP amidotransferase, driving excessive de novo purine synthesis and uric acid production","Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency; pentose phosphate pathway shutdown depletes PRPP","Adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency; excess dATP stimulates PRPP synthetase"],"a":1,"e":"Lesch-Nyhan syndrome is an X-linked recessive complete deficiency of hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT), which normally salvages hypoxanthine (to IMP) and guanine (to GMP) using phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate (PRPP). Failure to consume PRPP and reduced IMP/GMP levels both derepress and allosterically activate glutamine-PRPP amidotransferase (the committed rate-limiting enzyme of de novo purine synthesis). Excess purine nucleotides are degraded via xanthine oxidase to uric acid—causing hyperuricemia, gout, nephrolithiasis, and severe neurobehavioral self-mutilation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02710","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Purine Degradation and Pyrimidine Synthesis","q":"Why does autosomal recessive adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency cause severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) affecting both T and B lymphocytes, whereas UMPS deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia resistant to vitamin B12/folate?","o":["ADA deficiency blocks hepatic urea cycle detoxification; UMPS deficiency causes hyperammonemia","ADA deficiency prevents RNA capping; UMPS deficiency selectively impairs mitochondrial tRNA","ADA deficiency accumulates deoxyadenosine and dATP, which allosterically inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and halts lymphocyte DNA synthesis; UMPS deficiency blocks de novo UMP pyrimidine synthesis, impairing DNA replication in erythroid precursors (treated with oral uridine)","ADA deficiency overproduces uric acid crystals inside thymocytes; UMPS deficiency inhibits heme ferrochelatase"],"a":2,"e":"In ADA deficiency, failure to deaminate adenosine and deoxyadenosine to inosine/deoxyinosine causes massive intracellular accumulation of dATP in lymphoid precursors. High dATP potently inhibits ribonucleotide reductase (the enzyme converting NDPs to dNDPs), depleting the remaining dNTPs required for lymphocyte DNA replication and triggering T- and B-cell apoptosis (SCID). In hereditary orotic aciduria, defective bifunctional UMP synthase (UMPS: orotate phosphoribosyltransferase + OMP decarboxylase) blocks pyrimidine synthesis—causing urinary orotic acid crystals and megaloblastic anemia unresponsive to B12/folate, bypassed by oral uridine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02711","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Pentose Phosphate Pathway (HMP Shunt) and Hemoglobinopathies","q":"Mature erythrocytes lack mitochondria and rely entirely on the pentose phosphate pathway (HMP shunt) to prevent oxidative denaturation of hemoglobin into Heinz bodies. Which reducing equivalent produced by glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) directly powers glutathione reductase to detoxify H2O2?","o":["Ubiquinol (CoQH2)","NADH","FADH2","NADPH"],"a":3,"e":"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) catalyzes the first, rate-limiting oxidative step of the pentose phosphate pathway, generating NADPH. Because RBCs lack mitochondria (and malic enzyme/isocitrate dehydrogenase NADPH sources), G6PD is their sole source of NADPH. Glutathione reductase requires NADPH to reduce oxidized glutathione disulfide (GSSG) back to reduced glutathione (GSH), enabling glutathione peroxidase to neutralize reactive H2O2. Without sufficient NADPH during oxidative stress (fava beans, sulfa drugs, primaquine, infection), hemoglobin sulfhydryl groups cross-link into insoluble Heinz bodies, which splenic macrophages pluck out to form \u0027bite cells\u0027.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02712","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"DNA Replication and Repair","q":"During prokaryotic DNA replication, which enzyme removes the RNA primers on lagging-strand Okazaki fragments via its 5\u0027-to-3\u0027 exonuclease activity while filling the gap, and which human DNA repair pathway is defective in xeroderma pigmentosum?","o":["DNA polymerase I; nucleotide excision repair (NER) failing to excise UV-induced pyrimidine (thymine) dimers","DNA helicase (DnaB); mismatch repair (MLH1/MSH2)","DNA polymerase III; base excision repair (BER) failing to remove uracil","Topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase); non-homologous end joining (NHEJ)"],"a":0,"e":"In E. coli, DNA polymerase III synthesizes both leading and lagging (Okazaki) strands in the 5\u0027-\u003e3\u0027 direction with 3\u0027-\u003e5\u0027 proofreading exonuclease activity, whereas DNA polymerase I uniquely possesses an additional 5\u0027-\u003e3\u0027 exonuclease activity that degrades RNA primers ahead of itself while synthesizing replacement DNA (before DNA ligase seals the nick). In humans, xeroderma pigmentosum is an autosomal recessive defect in nucleotide excision repair (NER), leaving cells unable to remove bulky UV-induced cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers and causing extreme photosensitivity and early skin cancers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02713","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Regulation of Gene Expression","q":"Assertion (A): Maximal transcription of the E. coli lac operon occurs only when lactose is present AND glucose is absent. Reason (R): Allolactose (derived from lactose) binds and inactivates the LacI repressor protein to relieve negative control, while low glucose elevates intracellular cAMP so the cAMP-CAP (CRP) complex binds upstream of the promoter to positively recruit RNA polymerase.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"The E. coli lac operon exemplifies dual coordinate control: (1) Negative regulation: in the absence of lactose, the LacI repressor binds the operator to block RNA polymerase; when lactose is present, trace beta-galactosidase converts it to allolactose, which induces conformational release of LacI. (2) Positive regulation (catabolite repression): high glucose inhibits adenylyl cyclase, keeping cAMP low; only when glucose is absent does cAMP rise and complex with Catabolite Activator Protein (CAP/CRP) to strongly enhance RNA polymerase binding—yielding maximal lacZ/lacY/lacA expression.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02714","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Transcription and Post-Transcriptional Processing","q":"Which three co-/post-transcriptional modifications convert eukaryotic primary pre-mRNA (hnRNA) synthesized by RNA polymerase II into mature cytoplasmic mRNA, and what autoimmune disease features antibodies against snRNP spliceosomal proteins (anti-Sm)?","o":["Sigma-factor cleavage, Shine-Dalgarno insertion, and intron retention; myasthenia gravis","Addition of a 5\u0027 7-methylguanosine triphosphate cap, addition of a 3\u0027 poly(A) tail, and spliceosome-mediated intron excision via a lariat intermediate; systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)","Methylation of 28S rRNA, histone deacetylation, and Okazaki ligation; Goodpasture syndrome","Addition of a 3\u0027 CCA amino acid acceptor stem, 5\u0027 poly(U) tail, and Rho-dependent termination; rheumatoid arthritis"],"a":1,"e":"Eukaryotic RNA polymerase II transcribes protein-coding pre-mRNA (hnRNA), which undergoes: (1) 5\u0027 capping with 7-methylguanosine via a 5\u0027-5\u0027 triphosphate linkage (protecting from 5\u0027 exonucleases and aiding eIF4E ribosome recognition), (2) 3\u0027 cleavage and polyadenylation (poly-A tail added without a DNA template by poly-A polymerase at AAUAAA), and (3) nuclear splicing by the spliceosome (U1, U2, U4, U5, U6 snRNPs) excising GU-AG introns as a branched lariat intermediate (mutated in some beta-thalassemias). Anti-Smith (anti-Sm) antibodies in SLE target core proteins of these U-rich snRNPs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02715","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Quantitative Real-Time PCR (qPCR)","q":"In a real-time RT-qPCR experiment measuring an inflammatory cytokine mRNA normalized to GAPDH (housekeeping gene), an untreated control sample has cytokine Ct = 26 and GAPDH Ct = 18 (Delta-Ct_control = 8). A treated patient sample has cytokine Ct = 22 and GAPDH Ct = 17 (Delta-Ct_treated = 5). Using the 2^(-Delta-Delta-Ct) method (100% PCR efficiency), what is the fold change in cytokine mRNA expression in the treated sample relative to control?","o":["3-fold upregulation","8-fold downregulation (0.125-fold)","8-fold upregulation (2^3)","16-fold upregulation (2^4)"],"a":2,"e":"Using the Livak 2^(-Delta-Delta-Ct) relative quantification method: Delta-Ct_control = 26 - 18 = 8; Delta-Ct_treated = 22 - 17 = 5. Next, Delta-Delta-Ct = Delta-Ct_treated - Delta-Ct_control = 5 - 8 = -3. Fold change = 2^(-Delta-Delta-Ct) = 2^(-(-3)) = 2^3 = 8-fold increase (upregulation). Lower Ct values mean the fluorescence threshold was crossed 3 cycles earlier, corresponding to 2^3 = 8 times more starting mRNA.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02716","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Tumor Suppressor Genes","q":"A 14-month-old infant presents with bilateral leukocoria (white pupillary reflex) and retinal tumors containing Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes, and has an elevated lifetime risk of developing osteosarcoma. According to Knudson\u0027s two-hit hypothesis for the RB1 (13q14) tumor suppressor gene, how does this familial presentation differ from sporadic retinoblastoma, and what cell-cycle checkpoint does pRB regulate?","o":["RB1 acts as a dominant oncogene requiring only a single activating point mutation; pRB regulates G2-to-M mitosis","Sporadic cases present earlier and bilaterally due to autosomal dominant inheritance of MYCN amplification","Hereditary cases require two independent somatic hits in the same retinal cell; pRB promotes G1-to-S transition by phosphorylating CDK4","Hereditary cases inherit one germline mutant RB1 allele (\u0027first hit\u0027) and require only one somatic second hit, causing early bilateral tumors; hypophosphorylated pRB normally sequesters E2F to block G1-to-S progression"],"a":3,"e":"Knudson\u0027s two-hit hypothesis explains recessive loss-of-function of tumor suppressor genes at the cellular level: both alleles of RB1 (13q14) must be inactivated for tumorigenesis. In familial/hereditary retinoblastoma (~40%), one mutated allele is inherited in every somatic cell (\u0027first hit\u0027), so a single random somatic mutation/loss of heterozygosity (\u0027second hit\u0027) in retinal or osteoblast precursors produces early-onset, multifocal, bilateral retinoblastomas and later osteosarcomas. Normally, active hypophosphorylated pRB binds and inhibits E2F transcription factors, arresting cells at the G1/S restriction point until Cyclin D-CDK4/6 phosphorylates pRB.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02717","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Apoptosis Pathways and TP53","q":"In response to severe irreparable DNA double-strand breaks, wild-type p53 (\u0027guardian of the genome\u0027) transcriptionally upregulates BAX/BAK and PUMA to trigger intrinsic apoptosis. Which protein leaks from the mitochondrial intermembrane space to assemble the Apaf-1 apoptosome and activate which initiator caspase?","o":["Cytochrome c; activates initiator caspase-9 (whereas extrinsic Fas/TNF death receptor signaling activates initiator caspase-8)","Smac/DIABLO; activates caspase-8 directly via FADD","Apoptosis-inducing factor (AIF); activates caspase-1 inflammasome","Bcl-2; activates caspase-3 directly without an apoptosome"],"a":0,"e":"In the intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway of apoptosis, cellular stress/DNA damage activates p53, which induces pro-apoptotic BH3-only sensors (PUMA, NOXA) and pore-forming BAX/BAK while overriding anti-apoptotic BCL2/BCL-XL. Permeabilization of the outer mitochondrial membrane releases cytochrome c into the cytosol, where it binds Apaf-1 and dATP to form the apoptosome, activating initiator caspase-9 and downstream executioner caspases (3, 6, 7). Conversely, the extrinsic pathway uses FasL/TNF binding to Fas (CD95)/TNFR1 and adapter FADD to activate initiator caspase-8.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02718","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Paraneoplastic Syndromes","q":"A 62-year-old heavy smoker presents with a central hilar lung mass, proximal symmetric limb weakness that paradoxically improves with repeated muscle contraction, dry mouth, and diminished deep tendon reflexes (Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome), along with serum Na+ 121 mEq/L and urine osmolality 480 mOsm/kg. Biopsy shows sheets of small round-to-fusiform cells with scant cytoplasm, salt-and-pepper chromatin, and nuclear molding. Which tumor and autoantibody target are present?","o":["Lung adenocarcinoma; anti-Yo cerebellar Purkinje antibodies with hypertrophic osteoarthropathy","Small cell lung carcinoma (neuroendocrine); autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels (plus ectopic ADH causing SIADH)","Squamous cell lung carcinoma; autoantibodies against postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (plus PTHrP)","Thymoma; anti-MuSK antibodies with ectopic erythropoietin"],"a":1,"e":"Small cell lung carcinoma (SCLC) is a centrally located, smoking-associated high-grade neuroendocrine tumor (synaptophysin+, chromogranin+, Azzopardi effect, salt-and-pepper chromatin, nuclear molding) responsible for most lung-cancer paraneoplastic endocrinopathies and neurologic syndromes: SIADH (ectopic ADH -\u003e euvolemic hyponatremia), Cushing syndrome (ectopic ACTH), and Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (autoantibodies against presynaptic P/Q-type voltage-gated Ca2+ channels reducing ACh vesicle release, so repetitive stimulation facilitates Ca2+ buildup and transiently improves strength).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02719","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Hypersensitivity Reactions","q":"Ten days after receiving equine anti-venom serum, a patient develops fever, urticarial rash, arthralgia, generalized lymphadenopathy, and hypocomplementemia. Renal immunofluorescence reveals granular (\u0027lumpy-bumpy\u0027) IgG and C3 deposits along the glomerular basement membrane. Which hypersensitivity mechanism causes this syndrome, and how does its immunofluorescence pattern differ from Goodpasture syndrome?","o":["Type I (IgE-mediated) anaphylaxis; serum sickness is mediated solely by CD8+ T cells","Type IV (cell-mediated) hypersensitivity; Goodpasture syndrome shows negative immunofluorescence","Type III (immune-complex-mediated) hypersensitivity (serum sickness); Goodpasture syndrome is Type II antibody-mediated and shows smooth linear IgG staining along the GBM","Type II (cytotoxic) hypersensitivity; Goodpasture syndrome shows granular mesangial IgA deposits"],"a":2,"e":"Serum sickness (and the localized Arthus reaction) is the prototype of Type III immune-complex hypersensitivity: circulating antigen-antibody complexes (formed in slight antigen excess ~7-10 days after foreign serum or drugs) deposit in small vessel walls, synovium, and glomeruli, fixing complement (lowering serum C3/C4) and recruiting neutrophils to cause fibrinoid necrosis and granular (\u0027lumpy-bumpy\u0027) immunofluorescence. In contrast, Goodpasture syndrome is a Type II hypersensitivity reaction in which anti-GBM IgG autoantibodies directly bind alpha-3 chains of type IV collagen evenly along glomerular and alveolar basement membranes, producing crisp linear immunofluorescence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02720","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Transplant Rejection","q":"Minutes after vascular anastomoses are unclamped during a renal transplant, the donor kidney turns mottled cyanotic, becomes flaccid, and stops producing urine; biopsy shows widespread glomerular capillary fibrin thrombi and neutrophil infiltration of arteriolar walls. Another patient develops graft dysfunction 3 months post-transplant with biopsy showing interstitial lymphocytic infiltrate, tubulitis, and intimal endotheliitis. What mechanisms distinguish these two rejections?","o":["Hyperacute rejection: preformed circulating recipient anti-donor ABO/HLA antibodies (Type II); Acute cellular rejection: recipient CD8+/CD4+ T cells reacting to donor MHC (Type IV)","Hyperacute rejection: CD4+ Th1 delayed hypersensitivity; Acute rejection: preformed IgM antibodies","Both represent chronic rejection driven by TGF-beta smooth muscle intimal fibrosis","Hyperacute rejection: donor T cells attacking recipient tissues (graft-versus-host disease)"],"a":0,"e":"Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes to hours of revascularization when the recipient already possesses preformed anti-ABO or anti-HLA donor-specific antibodies (from prior pregnancies, transfusions, or transplants). Antibody binding to graft endothelium activates complement, causing endothelial necrosis, platelet-fibrin microthrombi, and hemorrhagic cortical infarction (prevented by pre-transplant ABO matching and crossmatch). Acute cellular (T-cell-mediated) rejection occurs weeks to months later (or anytime immunosuppression lapses), featuring CD8+ cytotoxic T-cell tubulitis and endotheliitis (responsive to high-dose steroids/antithymocyte globulin), whereas chronic rejection causes obliterative vascular intimal fibrosis and interstitial fibrosis/tubular atrophy over months to years.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02721","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Systemic Vasculitides","q":"A 48-year-old man presents with chronic purulent sinusitis, saddle-nose deformity, cavitary lung nodules with hemoptysis, and rapidly progressive crescentic glomerulonephritis (pauci-immune on immunofluorescence). Nasal/lung biopsy reveals geographic necrotizing granulomas with palisading histiocytes and small-vessel necrotizing vasculitis. Which ANCA specificity confirms this diagnosis, and how does it contrast with eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss)?","o":["Anti-dsDNA in polyarteritis nodosa (PAN); Churg-Strauss spares the respiratory tract completely","c-ANCA directed against elastase in giant cell arteritis; Churg-Strauss lacks eosinophilia","p-ANCA directed against MPO in Wegener granulomatosis; Churg-Strauss shows immune-complex linear GBM IgG","c-ANCA directed against proteinase 3 (PR3) in granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener); Churg-Strauss classically shows asthma, peripheral eosinophilia, eosinophil-rich granulomas, and p-ANCA (anti-myeloperoxidase, MPO)"],"a":3,"e":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA, formerly Wegener granulomatosis) is a necrotizing granulomatous small-vessel vasculitis triad affecting upper airways (sinusitis, septal perforation/saddle-nose), lower airways (bilateral cavitary nodules), and kidneys (pauci-immune crescentic GN); \u003e80-90% of patients are positive for cytoplasmic c-ANCA targeting neutrophil proteinase 3 (PR3). By contrast, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (EGPA/Churg-Strauss) features late-onset asthma, allergic rhinitis, marked peripheral eosinophilia, necrotizing granulomas with abundant eosinophils, and predominantly perinuclear p-ANCA targeting myeloperoxidase (MPO).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02722","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Atherosclerosis","q":"Assertion (A): Acute myocardial infarction most often results from disruption of an eccentric coronary atherosclerotic plaque with a large necrotic lipid core, abundant macrophage foam cells, and a thin fibrous cap rather than from a densely calcified thick-cap plaque causing fixed 80% stenosis. Reason (R): Activated plaque macrophages secrete matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) that degrade interstitial collagen in the fibrous cap, while T-cell IFN-gamma inhibits smooth muscle collagen synthesis, predisposing the thin cap to fissuring and occlusive thrombosis.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Stable plaques typically have a thick, collagen-rich smooth muscle fibrous cap and small lipid core, causing chronic stable exertional angina when luminal narrowing exceeds ~70-75%. In contrast, \u0027vulnerable\u0027 plaques—even those causing \u003c50% baseline stenosis—have a large, thrombogenic necrotic core rich in tissue factor and oxidized LDL foam cells beneath a thin, inflamed fibrous cap. Macrophage-derived matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) digest fibrous cap collagen while Th1-derived IFN-gamma suppresses smooth muscle cell repair/collagen deposition, causing cap rupture, acute platelet aggregation, and occlusive coronary thrombosis (STEMI).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02723","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Shock Pathophysiology","q":"A hypotensive febrile ICU patient with urosepsis undergoes invasive hemodynamic monitoring, showing cardiac output (CO) 7.8 L/min (elevated), systemic vascular resistance (SVR) 420 dyn*s/cm5 (markedly reduced), pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) 8 mmHg (low-normal), warm flushed extremities, and elevated mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2 82%). Which type of shock is present, and how do SVR and PCWP differ in cardiogenic shock?","o":["Early distributive (warm septic) shock; cardiogenic shock exhibits markedly elevated SVR and elevated PCWP (\u003e18 mmHg) with low cardiac output","Obstructive shock from cardiac tamponade; cardiogenic shock exhibits normal CO and low SVR","Cardiogenic shock; septic shock exhibits elevated SVR and elevated PCWP","Hypovolemic shock; cardiogenic shock exhibits low SVR and low PCWP"],"a":0,"e":"Early (hyperdynamic/\u0027warm\u0027) septic shock is a distributive shock driven by cytokine/nitric-oxide-mediated systemic arteriolar and venular vasodilation: SVR drops sharply, initially raising cardiac output (via reduced afterload and sympathetic tachycardia) while microvascular shunting/mitochondrial dysfunction reduces tissue O2 extraction (raising mixed venous SvO2 \u003e75%). Conversely, cardiogenic shock (pump failure, e.g., massive MI) presents with low cardiac output, elevated left-ventricular filling pressure (PCWP \u003e18 mmHg), reflex compensatory vasoconstriction (high SVR), cold clammy extremities, and low SvO2.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02724","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Immunohistochemistry (IHC) and Tumor Markers","q":"In an histopathology lab evaluation of an undifferentiated retroperitoneal malignant tumor, which immunohistochemical (IHC) marker panel accurately matches intermediate filaments/lineage markers to epithelial carcinomas, mesenchymal sarcomas, skeletal muscle rhabdomyosarcoma, and malignant melanoma?","o":["CD20 (carcinoma), S100 (lymphoma), Desmin (glial astrocytoma), and Chromogranin (squamous carcinoma)","Cytokeratin (carcinoma), Vimentin (mesenchymal sarcoma), Desmin/Myogenin (rhabdomyosarcoma), and S100/HMB-45/SOX10 (melanoma)","Vimentin (carcinoma), Cytokeratin (melanoma), CD45/LCA (sarcoma), and GFAP (rhabdomyosarcoma)","AFP (melanoma), Cytokeratin (glioblastoma), CD3 (mesothelioma), and PSA (sarcoma)"],"a":1,"e":"Immunohistochemistry uses lineage-specific intermediate filaments and proteins to classify poorly differentiated (\u0027anaplastic\u0027) tumors: Cytokeratins (pan-CK) identify epithelial carcinomas (and mesotheliomas); Vimentin marks mesenchymal/sarcoma cells (as well as renal cell carcinoma and melanoma); Desmin and nuclear MyoD1/Myogenin identify skeletal muscle differentiation (rhabdomyosarcoma); S100, HMB-45, Melan-A, and SOX10 identify neural crest-derived malignant melanoma; CD45 (leukocyte common antigen) identifies lymphomas; and GFAP identifies astrocytomas/gliomas.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02725","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) Drugs and Pregnancy Hypertension","q":"A 29-year-old woman at 14 weeks of gestation with chronic hypertension is taking enalapril (an ACE inhibitor), and also has a dry hacking cough. Why must enalapril be discontinued immediately in pregnancy, what causes the cough, and which oral antihypertensives are preferred in pregnancy?","o":["ACE inhibitors cause neonatal hyperkalemia only without teratogenicity; switch to atenolol and spironolactone","ACE inhibitors cause maternal pulmonary fibrosis only; cough is due to angiotensin II accumulation; switch to losartan (ARB)","ACE inhibitors cause fetal renal dysplasia, oligohydramnios, and skull hypoplasia (Potter sequence); cough results from accumulated bradykinin/substance P; switch to labetalol, nifedipine, or methyldopa","ACE inhibitors cause fetal neural tube defects via folate depletion; cough is due to aldosterone excess; switch to aliskiren"],"a":2,"e":"All RAAS blockers—ACE inhibitors (enalapril, ramipril), ARBs (losartan, telmisartan), and direct renin inhibitors (aliskiren)—are strictly contraindicated throughout pregnancy because they impair fetal renal perfusion and nephrogenesis, causing fetal renal tubular dysplasia, anuria, oligohydramnios, pulmonary hypoplasia, limb contractures, and cranial ossification defects. ACE also degrades bradykinin; inhibiting ACE elevates bradykinin and substance P in airways, causing dry cough and angioedema (avoided by ARBs in non-pregnant patients). Safe first-line oral antihypertensives in pregnancy are labetalol (alpha/beta blocker), extended-release nifedipine (DHP CCB), and alpha-methyldopa (central alpha-2 agonist).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02726","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antiarrhythmic Drugs","q":"A 64-year-old man on long-term oral amiodarone for recurrent ventricular tachycardia develops exertional dyspnea with bilateral interstitial lung infiltrates, elevated TSH with low free T4, slate-gray facial photodermatitis, and whorl-like corneal microdeposits. Which Vaughan-Williams class does amiodarone primarily belong to, and why does it rarely trigger torsades de pointes despite prolonging the QT interval?","o":["Class IV (calcium channel blocker); it blocks AV nodal A1 adenosine receptors exclusively","Class IC (flecainide-like); it is safe in structural heart disease post-MI","Class IA (sodium channel blocker); it induces SLE-like syndrome rather than thyroid dysfunction","Class III (potassium channel blocker prolonging Phase 3 repolarization and ERP, with additional Class I, II, and IV properties); it reduces transmural dispersion of repolarization across the myocardium"],"a":3,"e":"Amiodarone is classified as a Class III antiarrhythmic (blocking delayed rectifier I_Kr potassium channels to prolong action potential duration and effective refractory period), while also exhibiting Class I (Na+ channel), Class II (non-competitive beta-blockade), and Class IV (Ca2+ channel) actions. Despite prolonging the QT interval, amiodarone homogenizes repolarization across epicardial, M-cell, and endocardial layers (minimal transmural dispersion), making torsades de pointes very rare (\u003c1%) compared with sotalol or dofetilide. Its iodine-rich structure and extremely long half-life (25-100 days) produce pulmonary fibrosis (most fatal toxicity), hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism, corneal microdeposits, hepatotoxicity, and blue-gray skin discoloration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02727","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Hypolipidemic Drugs","q":"How do statins (e.g., atorvastatin, rosuvastatin) lower plasma LDL-cholesterol by 30-60%, and how do PCSK9 monoclonal antibodies (evolocumab, alirocumab) act synergistically with statins?","o":["Statins competitively inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, depleting hepatocyte cholesterol and triggering SREBP-2-mediated upregulation of hepatic LDL receptors; PCSK9 inhibitors prevent lysosomal degradation of LDL receptors so receptors recycle to the hepatocyte surface","Statins activate lipoprotein lipase via PPAR-alpha; PCSK9 inhibitors block intestinal NPC1L1 cholesterol absorption","Statins directly bind bile acids in the intestinal lumen; PCSK9 inhibitors inhibit microsomal triglyceride transfer protein","Statins inhibit hepatic VLDL ApoB synthesis directly without affecting LDL receptors; PCSK9 inhibitors activate CETP"],"a":0,"e":"Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase (rate-limiting enzyme of hepatic cholesterol biosynthesis). Reduced intracellular hepatocyte cholesterol activates sterol regulatory element-binding protein-2 (SREBP-2), upregulating transcription of LDL receptors on the hepatocyte basolateral surface to clear circulating LDL particles. However, SREBP-2 also upregulates PCSK9—a secreted protease that targets LDL receptors for lysosomal destruction. Monoclonal antibodies (evolocumab, alirocumab) neutralize PCSK9, dramatically increasing LDL receptor recycling and lowering LDL-C by an additional 50-60%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02728","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Heart Failure Pharmacotherapy","q":"Assertion (A): In heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF), the neprilysin inhibitor sacubitril must always be co-administered with an ARB (valsartan) rather than an ACE inhibitor, and clinicians should monitor NT-proBNP rather than BNP during therapy. Reason (R): Neprilysin degrades both natriuretic peptides (ANP/BNP) and vasoconstrictors (angiotensin II, bradykinin); co-administering sacubitril with an ACE inhibitor causes severe angioedema from dual bradykinin blockade, while sacubitril elevates BNP (neprilysin substrate) without elevating NT-proBNP (non-substrate).","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Neprilysin (neutral endopeptidase) breaks down beneficial vasodilatory/natriuretic peptides (ANP, BNP, CNP) as well as angiotensin II and bradykinin. Blocking neprilysin alone raises angiotensin II (requiring RAAS blockade); combining sacubitril with an ACE inhibitor (which also blocks bradykinin breakdown, as seen with omapatrilat) causes dangerous life-threatening angioedema, so a 36-hour washout is mandatory before switching from ACEi to sacubitril-valsartan (ARNI). Because active BNP is degraded by neprilysin, BNP levels rise pharmacologically on sacubitril, whereas inactive cleavage fragment NT-proBNP is NOT a neprilysin substrate and reliably reflects true cardiac wall stress.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02729","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antidiabetic Drugs","q":"A 54-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and HFrEF taking empagliflozin (an SGLT2 inhibitor) and metformin develops nausea, abdominal pain, and deep rapid breathing after 2 days of poor oral intake and gastroenteritis. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.18, HCO3- 11 mEq/L, anion gap 24 mEq/L, positive serum beta-hydroxybutyrate, and capillary blood glucose 168 mg/dL. What explains this metabolic crisis, and why is metformin also held in severe renal impairment (eGFR \u003c30 mL/min/1.73 m2)?","o":["Pioglitazone-induced fluid overload; metformin causes hepatotoxic cholestasis","SGLT2 inhibitor-associated euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis (glucosuria lowers plasma glucose while reduced insulin-to-glucagon ratio drives hepatic ketogenesis); metformin is renally cleared and accumulates to inhibit mitochondrial Complex I, risking lactic acidosis","Acarbose-induced malabsorption; metformin inhibits renal SGLT2 causing glycosuria","Sulfonylurea-induced hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state; metformin causes hypoglycemia via beta-cell K_ATP closure"],"a":1,"e":"SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin, canagliflozin) block proximal tubular glucose reabsorption, improving glycemic control, weight, and heart-failure/renal outcomes. However, during illness, dehydration, fasting, or surgery, ongoing renal glucose wasting keeps blood glucose near-normal (\u003c200-250 mg/dL) even as suppressed endogenous insulin and elevated glucagon/catecholamines drive unrestrained lipolysis and ketogenesis—producing euglycemic DKA. Metformin activates AMPK (suppressing hepatic gluconeogenesis) and is excreted unchanged by the kidneys; in severe renal failure (eGFR \u003c30), accumulation inhibits mitochondrial Complex I and gluconeogenic lactate clearance, precipitating metformin-associated lactic acidosis (MALA).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02730","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antithyroid Drugs","q":"Why is propylthiouracil (PTU) preferred over methimazole/carbimazole in BOTH thyroid storm and the first trimester of pregnancy, before switching to methimazole in the second trimester?","o":["PTU destroys thyroid follicles permanently via beta-radiation, whereas methimazole stimulates TSH","PTU has a longer half-life allowing once-weekly dosing without agranulocytosis risk","PTU additionally inhibits peripheral 5\u0027-deiodinase conversion of T4 to active T3 (ideal in thyroid storm) and avoids methimazole-associated aplasia cutis/embryopathy in the first trimester, whereas methimazole carries lower maternal hepatotoxicity risk after the first trimester","PTU is purely an iodide uptake blocker (NIS inhibitor) with zero hepatotoxicity, whereas methimazole blocks peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion"],"a":2,"e":"Both methimazole (active metabolite of carbimazole) and PTU inhibit thyroid peroxidase (blocking iodide oxidation, organification, and iodotyrosine coupling). However, PTU uniquely inhibits peripheral 5\u0027-deiodinase conversion of T4 to more potent T3—making it favored in acute thyroid storm (alongside beta-blockers, potassium iodide given \u003e=1 hour after PTU, and hydrocortisone). In early organogenesis (first trimester), methimazole/carbimazole carries a small risk of aplasia cutis congenita and choanal/esophageal atresia, so PTU is preferred in trimester 1 and switched to methimazole in trimesters 2-3 to minimize PTU\u0027s higher risk of fulminant maternal hepatotoxicity (both share rare agranulocytosis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02731","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Corticosteroids","q":"A patient with cerebral edema from a brain tumor requires a systemic corticosteroid with maximal anti-inflammatory potency, long biological half-life, and negligible mineralocorticoid (salt-retaining) activity. Which corticosteroid fits best, and why must chronic systemic glucocorticoid therapy (\u003e2-3 weeks) be tapered gradually rather than stopped abruptly?","o":["Prednisolone; it induces adrenal cortex hypertrophy rather than atrophy","Hydrocortisone; it has zero mineralocorticoid activity and does not suppress pituitary ACTH","Fludrocortisone; abrupt cessation causes acute Cushing syndrome","Dexamethasone (zero mineralocorticoid activity, ~25-30x hydrocortisone anti-inflammatory potency); abrupt cessation precipitates acute adrenal crisis due to chronic hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis suppression and adrenal cortical atrophy"],"a":3,"e":"Dexamethasone (and betamethasone) are long-acting synthetic glucocorticoids (biological t1/2 ~36-54 hours) with ~25-30 times the anti-inflammatory potency of hydrocortisone (cortisol) and essentially zero mineralocorticoid sodium/water-retaining activity—making dexamethasone ideal for vasogenic cerebral edema (and fetal lung maturation). Prolonged supraphysiologic glucocorticoid therapy suppresses hypothalamic CRH and anterior pituitary ACTH via negative feedback, leading to zona fasciculata/reticularis atrophy; abrupt withdrawal causes secondary adrenal insufficiency (hypotension, hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, shock).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02732","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Bronchial Asthma Pharmacotherapy","q":"A 26-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma is prescribed a single combination inhaler containing budesonide (inhaled corticosteroid) and formoterol (fast-onset long-acting beta-2 agonist) for both maintenance and reliever therapy (MART), and develops white curd-like plaques on her buccal mucosa. Why must LABAs never be used as monotherapy in asthma, and how is the mucosal adverse effect prevented?","o":["LABA monotherapy increases the risk of severe asthma exacerbations and asthma-related mortality (masking worsening airway inflammation without anti-inflammatory effect); rinse mouth and gargle with water after each inhalation (or use a spacer) to prevent oropharyngeal candidiasis","Formoterol has a slower onset than salmeterol; prevent thrush by stopping the inhaled corticosteroid completely","Budesonide causes acute beta-2 downregulation; inject IM epinephrine before every inhaler puff","LABAs cause irreversible bronchoconstriction via M3 agonism; treat thrush with oral theophylline"],"a":0,"e":"In bronchial asthma (unlike COPD), long-acting beta-2 agonists (formoterol, salmeterol) must ALWAYS be combined with an inhaled corticosteroid (ICS, e.g., budesonide, fluticasone) because LABA monotherapy relaxes bronchial smooth muscle without treating underlying eosinophilic airway inflammation, leading to beta-2 desensitization and higher risk of fatal asthma attacks. Formoterol has rapid onset (~1-3 min) plus 12-hour duration, enabling single-inhaler ICS-formoterol MART per GINA guidelines. Local ICS deposition causes dysphonia and oropharyngeal candidiasis (thrush), prevented by using a spacer device and rinsing/spitting with water immediately after inhalation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02733","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics","q":"An antibiotic following first-order kinetics has a volume of distribution (Vd) of 40 L and a systemic clearance (CL) of 5.54 L/hour in an adult patient. If the target steady-state plasma concentration (Css) is 10 mg/L, what are the elimination half-life (t1/2, using ln 2 = 0.693) and the required continuous intravenous maintenance infusion rate (in mg/hour)?","o":["t1/2 = 2.5 hours; infusion rate = 5.54 mg/hour","t1/2 = 5.0 hours; infusion rate = 55.4 mg/hour","t1/2 = 7.2 hours; infusion rate = 400 mg/hour","t1/2 = 5.0 hours; infusion rate = 400 mg/hour"],"a":1,"e":"Under first-order pharmacokinetics: (1) Elimination half-life t1/2 = (0.693 x Vd) / CL = (0.693 x 40 L) / 5.54 L/hour = 27.72 / 5.54 = 5.0 hours (steady state will be reached in ~4-5 half-lives, i.e., 20-25 hours without a loading dose). (2) At steady state, rate of drug administration equals rate of elimination: Maintenance Infusion Rate = CL x Css = 5.54 L/hour x 10 mg/L = 55.4 mg/hour.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02734","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Biomedical Waste Management (India)","q":"According to India\u0027s Biomedical Waste Management Rules (2016 and amendments), into which color-coded containers must (A) blood-soaked cotton swabs/anatomical tissue, (B) contaminated plastic IV tubing/urobags/catheters, (C) metallic hypodermic needles/scalpel blades, and (D) broken glass vials/ampoules be segregated respectively?","o":["(A) Black general waste bin, (B) Yellow bag, (C) Red bag, (D) White container","(A) Red bag, (B) Yellow bag, (C) Blue box, (D) White container","(A) Yellow bag, (B) Red bag, (C) White translucent puncture-proof container, (D) Blue puncture-proof box/container","(A) Blue box, (B) White container, (C) Yellow bag, (D) Red bag"],"a":2,"e":"Under India\u0027s Biomedical Waste Management Rules (2016, MoEFCC): Yellow non-chlorinated plastic bags receive infectious/anatomical/soiled cotton/gauze waste, expired cytotoxic drugs, and microbiology culture waste (treated by incineration/plasma pyrolysis/deep burial); Red bags receive contaminated recyclable plastic disposables (IV sets, tubing, catheters, syringes without needles, gloves) for autoclaving/microwaving then shredding; White translucent puncture-proof leak-proof containers receive metallic sharps (needles, scalpels, blades); and Blue puncture-proof boxes/containers receive broken/discarded glassware and metallic body implants.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02735","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Spaulding Classification and Chemical Sterilants","q":"According to Spaulding\u0027s classification of medical devices, how is a flexible fiberoptic gastroscope/colonoscope (which contacts intact mucous membranes without penetrating sterile tissue and is heat-labile) categorized, and what is the minimum recommended chemical processing?","o":["Critical item; low-level disinfection with quaternary ammonium compounds","Non-critical item; steam autoclaving at 134°C for 3 minutes","Critical item; wiping with 70% isopropyl alcohol for 30 seconds","Semi-critical item; high-level disinfection (e.g., 2% glutaraldehyde for 20 min, 0.55% ortho-phthalaldehyde, or peracetic acid)"],"a":3,"e":"Spaulding\u0027s scheme divides devices by infection risk: (1) Critical items entering sterile tissue or vasculature (surgical instruments, cardiac catheters, laparoscopes) require complete sterilization; (2) Semi-critical items contacting intact mucous membranes or non-intact skin (flexible GI endoscopes, laryngoscope blades, endotracheal tubes) require at least high-level disinfection (HLD)—which kills all vegetative bacteria, mycobacteria, fungi, and viruses except high numbers of bacterial spores—typically using 2% alkaline glutaraldehyde (20 min HLD; 10-12 hr for sporicidal sterilization), ortho-phthalaldehyde (OPA), or peracetic acid; (3) Non-critical items contacting intact skin (BP cuffs, stethoscopes) require low/intermediate-level disinfection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02736","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Beta-Lactamases and Carbapenem Resistance","q":"A ventilator-associated pneumonia isolate of Klebsiella pneumoniae is resistant to third-generation cephalosporins, piperacillin-tazobactam, and meropenem. On phenotypic testing, meropenem inhibition zones enlarge significantly in the presence of EDTA (chelating Zn2+) and show NO inhibition by avibactam or clavulanic acid. Which carbapenemase class is present?","o":["Ambler Class B metallo-beta-lactamase (e.g., NDM-1, VIM, IMP), which requires zinc ions at its active site and hydrolyzes all beta-lactams except aztreonam","Ambler Class A serine carbapenemase (KPC), which is strongly inhibited by avibactam","Ambler Class A extended-spectrum beta-lactamase (ESBL), which is inhibited by clavulanic acid and spares carbapenems","Ambler Class C AmpC cephalosporinase, which spares cefepime and meropenem"],"a":0,"e":"Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales (CRE) in India frequently harbor Ambler Class B metallo-beta-lactamases (MBLs), notably New Delhi metallo-beta-lactamase-1 (NDM-1). Unlike Class A/C/D serine beta-lactamases, MBLs utilize Zn2+ at their catalytic site—so they are inhibited by zinc chelators like EDTA (positive EDTA-meropenem synergy test) and are NOT inhibited by clavulanate, tazobactam, or avibactam. MBLs hydrolyze penicillins, cephalosporins, and carbapenems, uniquely sparing monobactams (aztreonam, though co-produced ESBL/AmpC often necessitates ceftazidime-avibactam + aztreonam).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02737","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Nipah Virus","q":"During a seasonal outbreak in Kerala, a 27-year-old man who consumed raw date palm sap presents with high fever, severe headache, myalgia, segmental myoclonus, rapid progression to acute encephalitis, and ARDS; two family caregivers subsequently develop similar symptoms. Which viral genus, natural animal reservoir, and diagnostic confirmation test characterize this Biosafety Level 4 pathogen?","o":["Rhabdovirus; stray dogs; Negri body brain biopsy","Henipavirus (Paramyxoviridae, enveloped negative-sense ssRNA); Pteropus fruit bats (flying foxes); RT-PCR of throat swab/CSF/urine and anti-Nipah IgM ELISA","Flavivirus; Aedes aegypti mosquito; NS1 antigen ELISA","Filovirus; multimammate rat (Mastomys); thick blood smear"],"a":1,"e":"Nipah virus is a Biosafety Level 4 (BSL-4) enveloped negative-sense ssRNA virus in the genus Henipavirus (family Paramyxoviridae). Pteropus fruit bats (flying foxes) are the natural asymptomatic reservoir, shedding virus in saliva/urine onto raw date palm sap or partially eaten fruits (with pigs acting as intermediate amplifying hosts in earlier Malaysian outbreaks, and documented human-to-human respiratory/body-fluid nosocomial transmission in Kerala/Bangladesh). Infection causes severe systemic vasculitis with acute encephalitis (myoclonus, brainstem signs) and atypical pneumonia/ARDS (40-75% case fatality). Diagnosis relies on RT-PCR and IgM ELISA under strict droplet/contact/airborne isolation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02738","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Scrub Typhus (Orientia tsutsugamushi)","q":"A 32-year-old soldier returning from scrub vegetation in Himachal Pradesh presents with 6 days of high fever, hepatosplenomegaly, bilateral conjunctival suffusion, and a painless black necrotic eschar with surrounding erythema over his axilla. Which obligate intracellular organism, arthropod vector, serologic heterophile agglutination reaction, and first-line antibiotic correspond to this illness?","o":["Leptospira interrogans; rat urine exposure; Weil-Felix OX-2 positive; vancomycin","Rickettsia prowazekii; human body louse; Weil-Felix OX-19 positive; gentamicin","Orientia tsutsugamushi; larval trombiculid mite (chigger, Leptotrombidium); Weil-Felix OX-K positive; doxycycline (or azithromycin in pregnancy)","Coxiella burnetii; cattle aerosols; Weil-Felix OX-K positive; penicillin G"],"a":2,"e":"Scrub typhus—a major cause of acute undifferentiated febrile illness across India—is caused by Orientia tsutsugamushi and transmitted by the bite of infected larval trombiculid mites (chiggers, genus Leptotrombidium, which serve as both vector and reservoir via transovarial transmission). A punched-out black eschar at the painless bite site (axilla, groin, waist) is virtually pathognomonic. Serology uses IgM ELISA/IFA (gold standard) and classically shows agglutination with Proteus mirabilis OX-K antigen on the Weil-Felix test (negative for OX-19 and OX-2). Oral/IV doxycycline is the drug of choice (azithromycin in pregnancy/children).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02739","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Coronaviruses (SARS-CoV-2)","q":"Which structural viral glycoprotein mediates SARS-CoV-2 attachment and entry into human respiratory and endothelial cells, which host receptor/protease does it engage, and how does the RT-PCR Cycle threshold (Ct) value relate to viral RNA load?","o":["Nucleocapsid (N) protein binding sialic acid; Ct value has no relation to amplification cycles","Envelope (E) protein binding CD4 receptor; Ct value is directly proportional to viral RNA load","Matrix (M) protein binding DPP4 (CD26) receptor; higher Ct = higher viral load","Spike (S) glycoprotein binding ACE2 receptor (primed by TMPRSS2 serine protease); Ct value is inversely proportional to viral RNA load (lower Ct = higher viral load)"],"a":3,"e":"SARS-CoV-2 is a large, enveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA coronavirus. Its surface homotrimeric Spike (S) glycoprotein receptor-binding domain attaches to host angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2) receptors, followed by proteolytic cleavage/priming by host TMPRSS2 (or endosomal cathepsins) for membrane fusion. In diagnostic real-time RT-PCR targeting conserved viral genes (e.g., RdRp, E, N, ORF1ab), the Cycle threshold (Ct) represents the number of amplification cycles needed to cross the fluorescence positivity threshold; therefore, Ct is inversely logarithmic to initial viral RNA quantity (a drop of ~3.3 cycles indicates roughly a 10-fold higher viral load).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02740","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Emerging Multidrug-Resistant Fungi","q":"An ICU patient with a central venous catheter develops candidemia due to an emerging yeast that grows at 42°C, frequently shows high resistance to fluconazole (and variable resistance to amphotericin B), is commonly misidentified by routine biochemical panels unless MALDI-TOF MS or sequencing is used, and persists on hospital bedrails despite standard quaternary ammonium disinfectants. Which pathogen is this, and what are the first-line drug class and environmental disinfectant?","o":["Candida auris; treat with an echinocandin (caspofungin/micafungin/anidulafungin) and disinfect surfaces with chlorine/bleach-based or sporicidal hydrogen peroxide agents","Candida albicans; treat with oral fluconazole and disinfect with quaternary ammonium wipes","Cryptococcus gattii; treat with ketoconazole and disinfect with 10% povidone-iodine","Malassezia furfur; treat with griseofulvin and disinfect with soap water"],"a":0,"e":"Candida auris is a globally emerging multidrug-resistant nosocomial yeast capable of causing severe invasive bloodstream/catheter infections and prolonged ICU outbreaks. Unlike most other Candida species, C. auris grows well at 40-42°C, lacks pseudohyphae, is often misidentified as Candida haemulonii/famata on older commercial biochemical systems (requiring MALDI-TOF MS or D1/D2/ITS sequencing), exhibits widespread fluconazole resistance (~90%), and survives weeks on dry surfaces. Echinocandins are first-line empiric therapy (with susceptibility testing), and contact isolation plus EPA/hospital-grade sporicidal (sodium hypochlorite/chlorine or hydrogen peroxide-peracetic acid) environmental disinfection—rather than quaternary ammonium compounds—is mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02741","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Hand Hygiene and Contact Precautions","q":"Assertion (A): When caring for a patient with Clostridioides difficile diarrhea or norovirus gastroenteritis, healthcare workers must wash hands with plain/antimicrobial soap and running water rather than relying solely on alcohol-based hand rubs (ABHR). Reason (R): Alcohols rapidly denature lipid envelopes and vegetative bacteria, but lack sporicidal activity against C. difficile endospores and have poor virucidal efficacy against non-enveloped viruses like norovirus, whereas soap-and-water friction mechanically removes them.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"While 60-80% alcohol-based hand rubs are the preferred routine method across WHO\u0027s Five Moments for Hand Hygiene when hands are not visibly soiled, alcohols do NOT kill bacterial endospores (C. difficile) and show markedly reduced activity against hydrophilic non-enveloped viruses (norovirus, enteroviruses, hepatitis A). Therefore, during C. difficile or norovirus outbreaks (and whenever hands are visibly soiled with blood/body fluids), vigorous soap-and-water handwashing for 40-60 seconds is required to physically detach and rinse away spores and non-enveloped virions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02742","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Traumatic Asphyxia (Perthes Syndrome)","q":"A 24-year-old factory worker is pinned under a collapsed heavy concrete slab across his chest and upper abdomen for 20 minutes during a stampede/industrial collapse. At autopsy, there is a sharp line of demarcation at the clavicles/upper chest separating pale lower skin from intense violaceous cyanosis, facial swelling, florid conjunctival hemorrhages, and pinpoint petechiae over the face, neck, and upper chest. What is this syndrome and its mechanism?","o":["Autoerotic asphyxia; hypoxia-induced arterial vasodilation in the lower limbs","Traumatic asphyxia (Perthes syndrome); sustained thoracic compression prevents chest expansion and forces high-pressure venous blood retrograde into valveless head, neck, and upper thoracic veins","Postmortem hypostasis; gravitational settling while prone after death","Café coronary syndrome; bolus impaction in the larynx causing vagal inhibition"],"a":1,"e":"Traumatic (crush) asphyxia—also known as Perthes syndrome—occurs when heavy mechanical compression of the thorax and upper abdomen (crowd stampedes, overturned vehicles, cave-ins) immobilizes respiratory excursions and sharply elevates intrathoracic pressure against a closed glottis. Because the superior vena cava and brachiocephalic/jugular veins lack effective valves against sudden high retrograde pressure, venous blood surges backward into the head, neck, and upper thorax, rupturing capillaries and venules to produce dramatic craniocervical violaceous congestion, florid petechiae, subconjunctival hemorrhage, and a sharp cervicothoracic demarcation line.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02743","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Ethanol Pharmacokinetics and Road Traffic Law","q":"A 70 kg adult male (Widmark distribution factor r = 0.68) rapidly consumes ethanol corresponding to 34 g of pure absolute alcohol, and zero-order hepatic elimination proceeds at 15 mg/100 mL/hour over 2 hours. Using Widmark\u0027s equation C0 = A / (W x r) (converted to mg/100 mL) minus elimination, what is his approximate blood alcohol concentration (BAC) at 2 hours, and how does it compare with the statutory driving limit under Section 185 of India\u0027s Motor Vehicles Act?","o":["Approximately 41.4 mg/100 mL (above the legal driving limit of 30 mg/100 mL)","Approximately 71.4 mg/100 mL (below the legal driving limit of 80 mg/100 mL)","Approximately 21.4 mg/100 mL (below the legal driving limit of 30 mg/100 mL)","Approximately 100 mg/100 mL (equal to the Indian legal driving limit of 100 mg/100 mL)"],"a":0,"e":"By Widmark\u0027s formula: theoretical peak BAC C0 (in g/L, equivalent to mg/mL) = A (g) / (W [kg] x r) = 34 / (70 x 0.68) = 34 / 47.6 = 0.7143 g/L = 71.4 mg/100 mL (mg%). Subtracting 2 hours of zero-order ethanol metabolism at 15 mg/100 mL/hour (2 x 15 = 30 mg/100 mL) yields 71.4 - 30 = 41.4 mg/100 mL. Under Section 185 of the Motor Vehicles Act, 1988 (India), driving with a BAC exceeding 30 mg per 100 mL of blood (0.03% BAC) is a punishable offence—so 41.4 mg/100 mL exceeds the legal threshold.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02744","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Toxic Alcohols","q":"A 36-year-old mechanic who drank sweet-tasting radiator antifreeze presents 10 hours later with flank pain, oliguric acute kidney injury, hypocalcemia, high anion gap metabolic acidosis, and high osmolar gap. Urine microscopy under polarized light reveals numerous birefringent octahedral (\u0027envelope-shaped\u0027) and needle/dumbbell-shaped crystals. Which metabolite causes these crystals and renal tubular necrosis, and what is the primary antidote?","o":["Acetic acid; administer naloxone","Formic acid; administer N-acetylcysteine","Oxalic acid (precipitating with calcium as calcium oxalate crystals in renal proximal tubules); inhibit alcohol dehydrogenase with fomepizole (or ethanol)","Hippuric acid; administer dimercaprol (BAL)"],"a":2,"e":"Ethylene glycol (antifreeze) initially produces CNS depression and a high osmolar gap, then is oxidized by alcohol dehydrogenase and aldehyde dehydrogenase through glycoaldehyde, glycolic acid (causing severe high anion gap metabolic acidosis), and ultimately oxalic acid. Oxalate chelates serum calcium (causing hypocalcemia and QT prolongation) and precipitates as birefringent calcium oxalate dihydrate (\u0027envelope-shaped\u0027) and monohydrate (needle/dumbbell) crystals inside renal proximal tubules, causing acute tubular necrosis and oliguric renal failure. Early administration of fomepizole (competitive alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor) or ethanol blocks toxic metabolite formation, with hemodialysis for severe acidosis/renal failure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02745","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Deliriant Poisons and NDPS Act","q":"A train passenger robbed after accepting spiked sweets is brought to casualty agitated, picking at imaginary objects in the air (carphologia/floccillation), with hot dry flushed skin, dilated non-reactive pupils, absent bowel sounds, urinary retention, and tachycardia (138/min). Which Indian toxic plant and active alkaloids cause this presentation, what is the specific antidote for severe central delirium, and which central statute regulates narcotic/psychotropic substances like opium and cannabis in India?","o":["Cannabis sativa (THC); flumazenil; Poisons Act, 1919 only","Strychnos nux-vomica (strychnine); atropine; Epidemic Diseases Act","Papaver somniferum (morphine); neostigmine; Drugs and Cosmetics Act only","Datura fastuosa/stramonium (hyoscine/scopolamine, atropine, hyoscyamine); physostigmine (tertiary amine AChE inhibitor crossing BBB); Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances (NDPS) Act, 1985"],"a":3,"e":"Datura (\u0027roadside poison\u0027 seeds containing tropane alkaloids—hyoscine/scopolamine, atropine, hyoscyamine) produces a classic antimuscarinic deliriant toxidrome (\u0027hot as a hare, blind as a bat, dry as a bone, red as a beet, mad as a hatter\u0027—mutism, picking at bedclothes/imaginary threads, urinary retention, ileus, mydriasis). Unlike neostigmine/pyridostigmine (quaternary amines restricted to the periphery), physostigmine is a tertiary amine reversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that crosses the blood-brain barrier to reverse both central delirium and peripheral anticholinergic signs (used cautiously if QRS is narrow). Separately, illicit production, possession, and trafficking of narcotic drugs (opium, heroin, coca, cannabis) and psychotropic substances are governed in India by the NDPS Act, 1985.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02746","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Child Sexual Abuse Laws (POCSO Act, 2012)","q":"Under the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012 in India, how is a \u0027child\u0027 defined, what legal duty is imposed on a registered medical practitioner under Section 19, and what is the penalty under Section 21 for failure to report?","o":["Any person below 18 years of age; mandatory duty under Section 19 to report any knowledge or suspicion of a POCSO offense to the Special Juvenile Police Unit (SJPU) or local police; failure to report is punishable under Section 21 with imprisonment up to 6 months and/or fine","Any female below 16 years of age; reporting is optional depending on parental consent; no legal penalty for doctors","Any person below 12 years of age; doctor must interrogate the child in an open courtroom first","Any person below 18 years of age; mandatory two-finger virginity test must be documented under Section 19"],"a":0,"e":"The POCSO Act, 2012 is gender-neutral and defines a \u0027child\u0027 as any person below 18 years of age. Section 19 imposes an explicit legal obligation on any person—including doctors and hospital personnel—who has apprehension or knowledge that an offense under the Act is likely to be or has been committed, to report it immediately to the Special Juvenile Police Unit (SJPU) or local police (even if the parents/patient request confidentiality). Under Section 21, failure to report is a criminal offense punishable by up to 6 months imprisonment and/or fine (up to 1 year for heads of institutions).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02747","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Sexual Assault Medicolegal Examination","q":"A 22-year-old woman reports sexual assault (defined under Section 63 of BNS, 2023, replacing IPC 375) and is brought for medicolegal examination under Section 184 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNSS), 2023 (replacing CrPC 164A). Which clinical and legal requirement is mandatory during her examination?","o":["Proceed with intimate genital examination even if the competent adult survivor explicitly refuses consent, because police requisition overrides consent","Obtain specific informed written consent from the survivor before examination/sample collection (within 24 hours of police information, report sent within 7 days), examine preferably by a female doctor, and strictly avoid the banned \u0027two-finger test\u0027","Delay medical care and emergency contraception until after the Magistrate records a statement","Perform per-vaginam two-finger laxity assessment to document habituation to sexual intercourse"],"a":1,"e":"Under BNSS 2023 Section 184 (former CrPC 164A), a sexual assault survivor is sent for examination by a registered medical practitioner within 24 hours of receiving information of the offense, and the reasoned report must be forwarded to the investigating officer within 7 days. Informed consent of the survivor (or a competent guardian if minor/incapable) is strictly mandatory—no forced examination can be conducted on a refusing competent adult—and refusal of forensic examination must never deny emergency medical treatment, STI prophylaxis, or emergency contraception. Per MoHFW guidelines and Supreme Court directives, the unscientific \u0027two-finger test\u0027 is prohibited.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02748","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Postmortem Lividity (Hypostasis)","q":"Assertion (A): Incising an area of dependent purplish skin discoloration at autopsy distinguishes postmortem hypostasis (livor mortis) from an antemortem subcutaneous contusion (bruise) even after lividity has become fixed. Reason (R): In postmortem hypostasis, blood remains confined within dilated dermal/subdermal capillaries and washes away cleanly on gentle rinsing after incision, whereas an antemortem bruise shows coagulated blood extravasated firmly into surrounding soft tissue stroma that cannot be washed away.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Before fixation (~6-12 hours postmortem), thumb pressure blanches postmortem lividity while failing to blanch a bruise. Once red cells settle/hemolyze and stain perivascular tissues (\u0027fixed\u0027 lividity), external pressure can no longer blanch lividity—making surgical incision the definitive test: in hypostasis, fluid blood oozes from cut intact vessels and washes off cleanly under water leaving pale dermis, whereas an antemortem contusion contains clotted blood infiltrated firmly into torn extravascular connective tissue (which also shows vital reaction/hemosiderin on microscopy). Additionally, lividity color provides toxicologic clues (cherry-red in CO/cyanide/cold, chocolate-brown in nitrites/methemoglobinemia).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02749","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Demographic Indicators","q":"Which demographic indicator represents the average number of daughters a newborn girl will bear during her lifetime assuming fixed age-specific fertility and mortality rates, and what value signifies exact population replacement (two-child norm)?","o":["Gross Reproduction Rate (GRR); GRR = 1","Total Fertility Rate (TFR); TFR = 1.0","Net Reproduction Rate (NRR); NRR = 1","Crude Birth Rate (CBR); CBR = 10 per 1,000"],"a":2,"e":"Net Reproduction Rate (NRR) measures the average number of daughters that would be born to a female if she passed through her childbearing years conforming to current age-specific fertility AND age-specific mortality rates. An NRR of 1 indicates that each generation of mothers exactly replaces itself (corresponding roughly to TFR ~2.1 in low-mortality settings).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02750","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Vital Health Indicators","q":"In a district with 20,000 live births in a calendar year, there were 25 maternal deaths (during pregnancy or within 42 days of termination from pregnancy-related causes) and 400 infant deaths within the first 28 days of life. What are the Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) and Neonatal Mortality Rate (NMR)?","o":["MMR 250 per 100,000 live births; NMR 40 per 1,000 live births","MMR 125 per 1,000 live births; NMR 20 per 100,000 live births","MMR 12.5 per 1,000 live births; NMR 200 per 100,000 live births","MMR 125 per 100,000 live births; NMR 20 per 1,000 live births"],"a":3,"e":"Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) = (Maternal deaths / Total live births) x 100,000 = (25 / 20,000) x 100,000 = 125 per 100,000 live births. Neonatal Mortality Rate (NMR) = (Deaths within 0-28 days / Total live births) x 1,000 = (400 / 20,000) x 1,000 = 20 per 1,000 live births.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02751","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Contraceptive Efficacy","q":"In a clinical follow-up study of a progestin-only pill, 200 women used the method for an average of 18 months each (3,600 woman-months of exposure), during which 9 accidental pregnancies occurred. What is the Pearl Index (failure rate per 100 woman-years)?","o":["3.0 per 100 woman-years","9.0 per 100 woman-years","1.5 per 100 woman-years","4.5 per 100 woman-years"],"a":0,"e":"Pearl Index = (Total accidental pregnancies x 1,200) / Total months of exposure = (9 x 1,200) / 3,600 = 10,800 / 3,600 = 3.0 pregnancies per 100 woman-years (equivalently, 3,600 months = 300 woman-years; 9 / 300 x 100 = 3.0).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02752","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Anaemia Mukt Bharat (AMB)","q":"Under the Government of India\u0027s Anaemia Mukt Bharat (AMB) strategy, what is the recommended daily prophylactic Iron and Folic Acid (IFA) tablet composition and duration for a non-anaemic pregnant woman starting from the second trimester?","o":["20 mg elemental iron + 100 mcg folic acid twice weekly for 3 months","60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid daily for 180 days antenatally (and 180 days postpartum)","60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid only during the first 8 weeks of gestation","100 mg elemental iron + 5 mg folic acid once weekly only"],"a":1,"e":"Under Anaemia Mukt Bharat (MoHFW India), all pregnant women receive daily one red IFA tablet containing 60 mg elemental iron and 500 mcg folic acid starting from the 4th month (second trimester) for at least 180 days during pregnancy, continued for 180 days postpartum. (For mild-to-moderate anaemia, two tablets daily are given therapeutically).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02753","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Viral Keratitis","q":"A 30-year-old man presents with painful watering, photophobia, and mild blurring in his right eye. Corneal sensation is markedly diminished, and fluorescein staining under cobalt-blue light reveals a linear branching epithelial ulcer with swollen terminal bulbs. Which treatment is indicated and which drug class is strictly contraindicated?","o":["Topical Natamycin 5% suspension; avoid atropine","Topical Prednisolone acetate 1% drops; avoid topical antivirals","Topical Acyclovir 3% eye ointment (or ganciclovir 0.15% gel); strictly avoid topical corticosteroids","Immediate corneal scraping and topical gentamicin fortified drops"],"a":2,"e":"A branching dendritic corneal ulcer with terminal bulbs and reduced corneal sensation is pathognomonic of Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) epithelial keratitis. First-line therapy is topical antiviral ointment/gel (Acyclovir 3% 5 times/day or Ganciclovir 0.15% gel). Topical corticosteroids are strictly contraindicated in active epithelial dendritic keratitis because they inhibit local immunity and convert a dendritic ulcer into a large amoeboid/geographic ulcer risking corneal perforation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02754","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Anterior Uveitis (Iridocyclitis)","q":"A 29-year-old man with chronic inflammatory low-back pain (HLA-B27 positive) presents with acute painful red eye, photophobia, circumcorneal ciliary congestion, small sluggish irregular pupil, aqueous flare and cells, and keratic precipitates on the inferior corneal endothelium. Along with frequent topical corticosteroids, why is a topical cycloplegic (homatropine/atropine) essential?","o":["Directly eradicates Chlamydia trachomatis in the anterior chamber","Lowers intraocular pressure by increasing trabecular outflow via miosis","Constricts the pupil to prevent acute angle-closure glaucoma","Relieves ciliary spasm pain and dilates the pupil to break/prevent posterior synechiae"],"a":3,"e":"Acute anterior uveitis (iridocyclitis) produces painful ciliary spasm, miotic pupil, aqueous cells/flare, and keratic precipitates. Topical cycloplegics (homatropine 2% or atropine 1%) serve three vital roles: (1) prevent or break fibrinous posterior synechiae between iris and anterior lens capsule (which can cause seclusio pupillae and secondary iris bombe glaucoma); (2) relieve ciliary spasm pain; and (3) reduce vascular permeability.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02755","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Optic Neuritis","q":"A 26-year-old woman presents with subacute unilateral blurred vision in her left eye over 3 days, pain worsened by extraocular movements, impaired red-green colour vision, and a left relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) with a normal optic disc (retrobulbar neuritis). MRI brain shows periventricular demyelinating white-matter plaques. Based on the Optic Neuritis Treatment Trial (ONTT), which corticosteroid regimen is appropriate and which is contraindicated?","o":["High-dose IV methylprednisolone (1 g/day for 3 days) followed by oral prednisolone taper; AVOID low-dose oral prednisolone monotherapy","Sub-Tenon triamcinolone injection alone","Low-dose oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day alone for 14 days; avoid IV steroids","Topical dexamethasone 0.1% drops every hour; avoid systemic steroids"],"a":0,"e":"The Optic Neuritis Treatment Trial (ONTT) established that high-dose IV methylprednisolone (1 g/day for 3 days followed by oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day for 11 days) accelerates visual recovery and temporarily delays clinically definite multiple sclerosis at 2 years. Conversely, standard-dose oral prednisolone ALONE doubled the rate of recurrent optic neuritis without improving final visual outcome and is contraindicated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02756","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Pupillary Abnormalities","q":"A 48-year-old man evaluated for tabes dorsalis (neurosyphilis) has bilateral small, irregular pupils that fail to constrict to bright direct or consensual light, yet briskly constrict when focusing on a near object (\u0027accommodation reflex present, light reflex absent\u0027). What is this pupillary abnormality called, and where is the classic lesion?","o":["Adie\u0027s tonic pupil; ciliary ganglion","Argyll Robertson pupil; dorsal midbrain (pretectal region)","Horner syndrome; oculosympathetic pathway","Marcus Gunn pupil; anterior optic nerve"],"a":1,"e":"Argyll Robertson pupil (\u0027prostitute\u0027s pupil\u0027 — accommodates but does not react) exhibits bilateral small, irregular pupils with light-near dissociation, classically seen in neurosyphilis (or dorsal midbrain lesions / diabetes). Interruption of pretectal-to-Edinger-Westphal fibres spares the more ventrally located near-reflex pathway.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02757","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Deep Neck Space Infections","q":"A 25-year-old man presents 5 days after acute tonsillitis with severe right-sided throat pain, drooling, \u0027hot-potato\u0027 muffled voice, marked trismus, and fever. Examination shows a tense unilateral bulge above the right tonsil pushing the tonsil medially and the uvula toward the left side. Beyond IV antibiotics and hydration, what is the immediate definitive procedure?","o":["Immediate bilateral guillotine tonsillectomy in the outpatient clinic","Emergency tracheostomy as routine first step in all cases","Needle aspiration or incision and drainage of the peritonsillar abscess at the point of maximum bulge (superolateral to the tonsil)","Oral amoxicillin alone for 5 days without drainage"],"a":2,"e":"Unilateral tonsillar swelling, trismus (medial pterygoid spasm), hot-potato voice, and contralateral uvular deviation define a peritonsillar abscess (quinsy)—pus between the tonsillar capsule and superior constrictor muscle. Prompt needle aspiration or incision and drainage (at the point of maximum fluctuance/intersection of a vertical line along anterior pillar and horizontal line from base of uvula) with IV penicillin/amoxicillin-clavulanate/clindamycin rapidly relieves pain and prevents parapharyngeal spread.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02758","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Fungal Rhinosinusitis","q":"A 55-year-old man with diabetic ketoacidosis and recent systemic corticosteroid exposure develops facial pain, black necrotic eschar over the right middle turbinate and hard palate, proptosis, and ophthalmoplegia. KOH mount of nasal scraping shows broad, non-septate (pauciseptate) ribbon-like hyphae branching at 90 degrees. What is the emergency management triad?","o":["Wait 2 weeks for fungal culture before starting therapy","Oral ciprofloxacin plus topical steroid nasal spray","Oral fluconazole monotherapy for 6 weeks without surgery","Urgent surgical debridement of necrotic tissue, systemic Liposomal Amphotericin B, and rapid reversal of DKA/immunosuppression"],"a":3,"e":"Broad, ribbon-like aseptate hyphae with right-angle (90-degree) branching in a ketoacidotic/immunocompromised host with black necrotic nasal/palatal eschar and orbital signs are pathognomonic for Rhino-Orbito-Cerebral Mucormycosis (Mucorales). Because hyphae angioinvade and cause thrombosis, systemic antifungals cannot penetrate dead tissue alone; immediate radical surgical debridement + IV Liposomal Amphotericin B (followed by posaconazole/isavuconazole) + metabolic correction (DKA reversal) are mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02759","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Nasal Polyposis","q":"A 14-year-old boy presents with progressive unilateral left-sided nasal obstruction (worse on expiration) and hyponasal voice. Anterior rhinoscopy and nasal endoscopy show a single, smooth, greyish-white trilobed polyp originating from the maxillary antrum and extending posteriorly into the nasopharynx. What is the diagnosis and standard treatment?","o":["Antrochoanal (Killian\u0027s) polyp; Functional Endoscopic Sinus Surgery (FESS) ensuring complete removal of its maxillary antral origin","Bilateral allergic ethmoidal polyps; lifelong topical steroids alone without addressing antral stalk","Rhinosporidiosis; oral dapsone alone","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma; preoperative embolisation"],"a":0,"e":"An antrochoanal (Killian\u0027s) polyp typically presents in children/young adults as a solitary, unilateral polyp arising from the maxillary sinus mucosa, exiting via the accessory ostium, and hanging into the posterior choana/nasopharynx (causing expiratory ball-valve obstruction). Unlike bilateral allergic ethmoidal polyps (seen in adults, associated with asthma/aspirin sensitivity), medical steroid therapy fails and FESS removing both the nasal/choanal mass and its antral attachment prevents recurrence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02760","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Impedance Audiometry (Tympanometry)","q":"A 6-year-old boy with mouth breathing and poor school attentiveness undergoes impedance audiometry. Otoscopy shows a dull, amber-coloured retracted tympanic membrane with air-fluid bubbles, and tympanometry shows a completely flat curve with no compliance peak (\u0027Type B\u0027 tympanogram) and normal ear canal volume. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Ossicular chain discontinuity","Otitis media with effusion (serous otitis media / glue ear)","Otosclerosis (stapedial fixation)","Eustachian tube obstruction with negative middle ear pressure alone"],"a":1,"e":"A flat Type B tympanogram (no compliance peak across pressure changes) with normal external ear canal volume indicates non-compressible fluid or a mass behind an intact tympanic membrane—classic for Otitis Media with Effusion (glue ear). (By contrast, Type B with a LARGE ear canal volume indicates a tympanic membrane perforation or patent grommet).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02761","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Cardiac Arrhythmias","q":"A 68-year-old woman with hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus presents with palpitations. ECG confirms persistent atrial fibrillation (irregularly irregular rhythm, absent P waves) with ventricular rate 86/min. Echocardiogram shows normal valves and LVEF 58%. Calculate her CHA2DS2-VASc score and select the appropriate stroke prophylaxis.","o":["CHA2DS2-VASc = 0; no antithrombotic therapy required","CHA2DS2-VASc = 1; aspirin 75 mg daily alone","CHA2DS2-VASc = 4; initiate long-term oral anticoagulation (DOAC such as apixaban/rivaroxaban or warfarin)","CHA2DS2-VASc = 2; dual antiplatelet therapy without anticoagulation"],"a":2,"e":"CHA2DS2-VASc scoring: Congestive heart failure (0), Hypertension (+1), Age \u003e= 75 (+2; 65-74 = +1), Diabetes (+1), Stroke/TIA (+2), Vascular disease (+1), Sex category female (+1). Here: Hypertension (1) + Age 68 [65-74] (1) + Diabetes (1) + Female sex (1) = 4. A score \u003e= 2 in men (\u003e= 3 in women) strongly indicates chronic oral anticoagulation (DOACs preferred over warfarin in non-valvular AF).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02762","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Portal Hypertension Emergencies","q":"A 49-year-old man with decompensated cirrhosis presents with massive haematemesis, BP 86/56 mmHg, HR 118/min, and Hb 6.2 g/dL. After securing two wide-bore IV lines and airway protection, which combination represents optimal acute medical and endoscopic management?","o":["High-dose IV PPI monotherapy without vasoactive drugs or antibiotics","Immediate surgical portacaval shunt before stabilisation","Aggressive PRBC transfusion to target Hb \u003e 12 g/dL, oral propranolol, and elective endoscopy after 72 hours","Restrictive PRBC transfusion (target Hb 7-8 g/dL), IV Terlipressin (or Octreotide), prophylactic IV Ceftriaxone, and urgent Endoscopic Variceal Band Ligation (EVL) within 12 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Acute variceal bleeding management requires: (1) Restrictive transfusion targeting Hb 7-8 g/dL (over-transfusion raises portal pressure and worsens rebleeding/mortality); (2) Immediate splanchnic vasoconstrictor (IV terlipressin or octreotide/somatostatin for 2-5 days); (3) Short-term prophylactic antibiotics (IV ceftriaxone 1 g/day for 5-7 days) to prevent SBP/bacterial infections and reduce mortality; and (4) Urgent endoscopic variceal band ligation within 12 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02763","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Calcium and Parathyroid Disorders","q":"A 61-year-old woman with squamous cell carcinoma of the lung presents with confusion, polyuria, constipation, QT shortening on ECG, and corrected serum calcium of 15.2 mg/dL (normal 8.5-10.5 mg/dL). What is the FIRST therapeutic step in acute hypercalcaemic crisis?","o":["Vigorous IV 0.9% normal saline hydration (200-300 mL/hour initially) to restore intravascular volume and promote calciuresis, followed by Calcitonin and IV Zoledronic acid","Immediate IV furosemide 80 mg before any IV fluids","Immediate IV sodium bicarbonate 200 mEq without saline","Oral hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg daily"],"a":0,"e":"Severe hypercalcaemia causes profound osmotic diuresis (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus) and volume depletion, which impairs GFR and increases proximal tubular calcium reabsorption. Aggressive IV 0.9% normal saline resuscitation is ALWAYS the first step to restore renal perfusion and calciuresis, combined with salmon calcitonin (rapid onset within 4-6 h, tachyphylaxis after 48 h) and IV bisphosphonate (zoledronic acid/pamidronate, onset 48-72 h).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02764","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Severe Cutaneous Adverse Reactions","q":"Two weeks after starting carbamazepine for trigeminal neuralgia, a 34-year-old woman develops fever, painful dusky purpuric macules, haemorrhagic crusting of the lips, bilateral conjunctivitis, and flaccid blisters with sheets of epidermal detachment involving 35% of her body surface area (Nikolsky sign positive). How is this classified and what is the primary management?","o":["Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS, \u003c10% BSA); continue carbamazepine at half-dose with antihistamines","Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN, \u003e30% BSA detachment); immediate permanent discontinuation of carbamazepine and specialised burn-ICU supportive/wound/ocular care","Erythema multiforme minor; topical permethrin cream","Staphylococcal Scalded Skin Syndrome; oral flucloxacillin while continuing carbamazepine"],"a":1,"e":"SJS/TEN spectrum classification is based on detachable epidermal Body Surface Area (BSA): SJS (\u003c10% BSA), SJS-TEN overlap (10-30% BSA), and TEN (\u003e30% BSA), all featuring mucocutaneous necrosis and positive Nikolsky sign. Immediate cessation of the offending drug (carbamazepine, allopurinol, lamotrigine, cotrimoxazole, phenytoin) and burn-unit care (thermoregulation, fluids, non-adherent dressings, ophthalmology consultation to prevent symblepharon) are life-saving.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02765","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Autoimmune Bullous Disorders","q":"A 42-year-old man presents with painful oral mucosal erosions followed by flaccid cutaneous blisters that rupture easily leaving raw crusted areas (positive Nikolsky and bulla-spread signs). Skin biopsy reveals suprabasal acantholysis with a \u0027row of tombstones\u0027 appearance, and direct immunofluorescence shows intercellular \u0027fishnet\u0027 IgG deposition. Which autoantigen and diagnosis match these findings?","o":["Bullous pemphigoid; autoantibodies against BP180/BP230 hemidesmosomes","Dermatitis herpetiformis; IgA deposits in dermal papillae tips","Pemphigus vulgaris; autoantibodies against Desmoglein 3 (and Desmoglein 1)","Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita; anti-type VII collagen antibodies"],"a":2,"e":"Pemphigus vulgaris is mediated by IgG autoantibodies targeting desmosomal cadherins (Desmoglein 3 in mucosal-dominant, Desmoglein 3 + 1 in mucocutaneous disease), causing intraepidermal suprabasal acantholysis, flaccid fragile blisters, oral erosions, positive Nikolsky sign, basal cells remaining attached (\u0027row of tombstones\u0027), and intercellular fishnet IgG/C3 on DIF.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02766","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Bipolar Disorder Pharmacotherapy","q":"A 44-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder maintained on lithium carbonate 900 mg/day is started on ibuprofen and hydrochlorothiazide for knee osteoarthritis and hypertension. Two weeks later she develops coarse hand tremors, dysarthria, ataxia, hyperreflexia, and confusion, with serum lithium level 3.1 mEq/L and serum creatinine 1.8 mg/dL. Beyond stopping lithium/NSAIDs/thiazide and starting IV 0.9% saline, what is the definitive treatment?","o":["Switch immediately to oral furosemide monotherapy","IV flumazenil bolus","Oral activated charcoal 50 g every 4 hours","Urgent haemodialysis"],"a":3,"e":"NSAIDs and thiazide diuretics reduce renal lithium clearance and precipitate lithium toxicity (therapeutic window 0.6-1.2 mEq/L). Severe neurotoxicity (ataxia, dysarthria, confusion, seizures) or serum lithium \u003e 2.5-4.0 mEq/L with renal impairment requires urgent haemodialysis, as lithium is a small, non-protein-bound ion readily cleared by dialysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02767","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Peripheral Arterial Disease","q":"A 31-year-old man who smokes 25 bidis daily presents with severe rest pain in his right foot, blackened ischaemic tips of two toes, absent dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses with normal popliteal/femoral pulses, and migratory superficial thrombophlebitis. Angiography shows segmental distal infrapopliteal arterial occlusions with \u0027corkscrew\u0027 collaterals and normal proximal aortoiliac vessels. What is the ONLY intervention proven to halt disease progression and prevent amputation?","o":["Complete and permanent cessation of all tobacco/nicotine exposure","Oral glucocorticoids for 6 months","Aorto-bifemoral bypass grafting","Lifelong high-dose warfarin anticoagulation"],"a":0,"e":"Buerger\u0027s disease (thromboangiitis obliterans) is a non-atherosclerotic segmental inflammatory occlusive disease of small- and medium-sized distal arteries and veins strongly linked to tobacco use in young male smokers. Because distal runoff is poor, bypass surgery is rarely feasible; complete abstinence from ALL tobacco and nicotine products is the single definitive measure that arrests progression and prevents major amputation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02768","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Fibroepithelial Breast Tumours","q":"A 46-year-old woman presents with a rapidly growing, 9 cm well-circumscribed, bosselated mass in the right breast with stretched shiny overlying skin and no palpable axillary lymph nodes. Core needle biopsy reveals a borderline/malignant phyllodes tumour with marked stromal hypercellularity and leaf-like epithelial clefts. What is the standard surgical management?","o":["Neoadjuvant endocrine therapy with letrozole alone","Wide local excision with clear surgical margins (\u003e= 1 cm), or simple mastectomy if breast-to-tumour ratio precludes clear margins, WITHOUT routine axillary lymph node dissection","Simple enucleation (shelling out) along the pseudocapsule","Modified radical mastectomy with routine Level I-III axillary lymph node dissection"],"a":1,"e":"Phyllodes tumours are fibroepithelial neoplasms (differing from fibroadenomas by hypercellular stroma and leaf-like projections) that have a high local recurrence rate if enucleated and spread predominantly haematogenously (lungs/bone) rather than via lymphatics (\u003c5% nodal spread). Therefore, wide local excision with \u003e= 1 cm histologically negative margins (or mastectomy for huge lesions) is mandatory, and routine axillary lymph node dissection is NOT indicated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02769","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastric Carcinoma","q":"A 60-year-old man with chronic Helicobacter pylori gastritis and weight loss has an ulceroproliferative lesion in the gastric antrum confirmed as diffuse-type (signet-ring cell) adenocarcinoma on endoscopic biopsy. On physical examination, a hard, non-tender palpable lymph node is noted in the left supraclavicular fossa (Troisier\u0027s sign). What is this node classically called and what does it signify?","o":["Sister Mary Joseph nodule; local benign umbilical hernia","Blumer\u0027s shelf; benign rectal prolapse","Virchow\u0027s node; haematogenous/lymphatic spread via the thoracic duct indicating inoperable/metastatic (M1) disease","Irish\u0027s node; early curable mucosal-only T1a gastric cancer"],"a":2,"e":"Virchow\u0027s node (Troisier\u0027s sign) is a metastatic left supraclavicular lymph node receiving drainage from the abdomen via the thoracic duct. Its presence in gastric carcinoma signifies distant metastatic (Stage IV / M1) disease, precluding curative gastrectomy and shifting management to systemic palliative chemotherapy/targeted therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02770","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infantile Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis (IHPS)","q":"A 4-week-old firstborn male infant presents with non-bilious projectile vomiting immediately after feeds, visible left-to-right upper abdominal peristalsis, and a palpable firm \u0027olive\u0027 mass in the epigastrium. Labs show Na+ 132 mEq/L, K+ 2.8 mEq/L, Cl- 84 mEq/L, HCO3- 34 mEq/L, and arterial pH 7.52 (hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis). What is the crucial preoperative priority before performing Ramstedt pyloromyotomy?","o":["Take the baby to emergency operating theatre immediately within 30 minutes without fluid correction","Administer IV sodium bicarbonate to neutralise paradoxical aciduria","Perform gastrojejunostomy immediately","Resuscitate with IV 0.45%/0.9% NaCl in dextrose plus KCl (once urinating) until serum Cl- \u003e 100 mEq/L and HCO3- \u003c 26 mEq/L before surgery"],"a":3,"e":"Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is NOT a surgical emergency—it is a medical resuscitation priority. Persistent loss of gastric HCl causes hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis (with paradoxical aciduria as the kidney retains Na+ by excreting H+ and K+). Operating before correcting alkalosis and hypokalaemia risks severe postoperative apnoea under general anaesthesia; hence IV saline + KCl resuscitation precedes Ramstedt\u0027s extramucosal pyloromyotomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02771","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Acute Paediatric Abdomen","q":"An 8-month-old infant presents with episodic paroxysms of inconsolable crying during which he draws his legs up to his abdomen every 15 minutes, followed by lethargy and \u0027red currant jelly\u0027 mucoid bloody stools. Examination shows a sausage-shaped mass in the right upper quadrant and an empty right iliac fossa (Dance\u0027s sign). Ultrasound shows a \u0027target\u0027 sign, and there is NO peritonitis or free air. What is the first-line definitive treatment?","o":["Ultrasound-guided hydrostatic (saline) or fluoroscopic pneumatic enema reduction","Immediate exploratory laparotomy and right hemicolectomy","Barium meal follow-through","Oral loperamide and broad-spectrum oral antibiotics at home"],"a":0,"e":"Ileocolic intussusception is the commonest cause of intestinal obstruction in infants aged 5-12 months (often post-viral Peyer\u0027s patch hyperplasia). In a haemodynamically stable infant without signs of peritonitis, bowel perforation, or shock, non-operative hydrostatic (saline under USG) or pneumatic (air) enema reduction is both diagnostic and therapeutic with \u003e80-90% success.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02772","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Splenectomy Care","q":"A 24-year-old man undergoes emergency splenectomy for a shattered Grade V splenic rupture following blunt abdominal trauma. To prevent Overwhelming Post-Splenectomy Infection (OPSI), against which three encapsulated organisms must he be vaccinated (ideally around Day 14 postoperatively)?","o":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis, Salmonella Typhi, and Vibrio cholerae","Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib), and Neisseria meningitidis","Clostridium tetani, Corynebacterium diphtheriae, and Bordetella pertussis","Staphylococcus aureus, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and Enterococcus faecalis"],"a":1,"e":"Splenectomy impairs opsonisation and clearance of polysaccharide-encapsulated bacteria, creating a lifelong risk of fulminant OPSI (most commonly caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae, followed by Hib and Neisseria meningitidis). In emergency trauma splenectomy, vaccines against all three organisms are administered ~14 days postoperatively for optimal antibody response (vs \u003e= 14 days preoperatively in elective splenectomy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02773","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Pelvic Organ Prolapse","q":"A 56-year-old sexually active woman who underwent total abdominal hysterectomy 8 years ago presents with a symptomatic Stage III vaginal vault prolapse protruding 3 cm beyond the hymen. Which surgical procedure provides the most durable anatomical restoration while preserving vaginal length and sexual function?","o":["Colpocleisis (Le Fort partial obliteration of the vagina)","Manchester-Fothergill amputation of the cervix","Abdominal or laparoscopic sacrocolpopexy (suspending the vaginal vault to the anterior longitudinal ligament of the sacral promontory using mesh)","Anterior colporrhaphy alone"],"a":2,"e":"Sacrocolpopexy (open, laparoscopic, or robotic) suspends the prolapsed vaginal apex to the sacral promontory (S1-S2 anterior longitudinal ligament) via a Y-shaped synthetic mesh, providing the highest long-term anatomical success rate while preserving vaginal axis and coital function in sexually active women.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02774","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS)","q":"A 27-year-old woman (BMI 29 kg/m2) presents with primary infertility for 2 years, oligomenorrhoea (cycles every 50-70 days), hirsutism, and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound (Rotterdam criteria met). Semen analysis and tubal patency (HSG) are normal. Beyond lifestyle weight reduction, which oral agent is currently recommended as FIRST-LINE for ovulation induction with higher live-birth rates and lower multiple pregnancy risk?","o":["Danazol","Combined oral contraceptive pills (ethinyl estradiol + levonorgestrel)","High-dose oral dexamethasone","Letrozole (aromatase inhibitor)"],"a":3,"e":"Per current international PCOS guidelines and large RCTs (PPCOS II), Letrozole (an aromatase inhibitor suppressing oestrogen negative feedback to increase pituitary FSH without antioestrogenic endometrial thinning or cervical mucus hostility) achieves significantly higher ovulation and live-birth rates and lower twin pregnancy rates than clomiphene citrate, making it first-line pharmacological ovulation induction in PCOS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02775","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Emergency Contraception","q":"A 26-year-old nulliparous woman in a monogamous relationship (no pelvic infection) presents 96 hours (4 days) after unprotected sexual intercourse seeking the MOST effective emergency contraception available that can also provide ongoing long-term contraception. Which option is best?","o":["Insertion of a Copper intrauterine device (CuT 380A) within 5 days (120 hours) of unprotected intercourse","Oral misoprostol 800 mcg","Single-dose Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg oral tablet","High-dose Depot Medroxyprogesterone Acetate (DMPA) injection"],"a":0,"e":"Insertion of a Copper IUD (CuT 380A) within 5 days (120 hours) of unprotected intercourse is the single most effective form of emergency contraception (\u003e99% effective, failure rate \u003c0.1%), acts via spermicidal copper ions and endometrial inflammatory anti-implantation effects, maintains efficacy in obese women, and provides up to 10 years of continuous reversible contraception.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02776","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Diphtheria","q":"An unimmunised 6-year-old boy presents with low-grade fever, sore throat, inspiratory stridor, massive tender cervical lymphadenopathy (\u0027bull-neck\u0027 appearance), and a tough greyish-white pseudomembrane over the tonsils and uvula that bleeds profusely when gently swabbed. What is the single most time-critical specific therapy to reduce mortality from myocarditis and neuritis?","o":["Surgical forceful stripping of the pseudomembrane in the clinic","Immediate administration of equine Diphtheria Antitoxin (after sensitivity test) PLUS IV penicillin G or erythromycin","Oral azithromycin alone while waiting 7 days for throat culture confirmation","High-dose IV acyclovir"],"a":1,"e":"An adherent pseudomembrane that bleeds on removal with \u0027bull-neck\u0027 cervical oedema is classic for respiratory diphtheria (Corynebacterium diphtheriae). Because diphtheria antitoxin neutralises only circulating unbound exotoxin (and cannot reverse toxin already bound to myocardium or peripheral nerves), antitoxin MUST be administered immediately on clinical suspicion without waiting for culture results, alongside antibiotics (penicillin/erythromycin) to eradicate bacilli and stop further toxin production.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02777","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Febrile Seizures","q":"An 18-month-old developmentally normal boy experiences a single generalised tonic-clonic convulsion lasting 4 minutes during a febrile viral upper respiratory illness (temperature 39.1 degrees C). Postictally he recovers completely within 15 minutes with normal neurological examination and no meningeal signs. What is the most appropriate management regarding neuroimaging, EEG, and long-term antiepileptic prophylaxis?","o":["Perform mandatory lumbar puncture in all children aged 18 months with simple febrile seizure","Start lifelong oral phenobarbitone or sodium valproate immediately","Reassure parents, treat fever/underlying illness, and do NOT perform routine EEG, MRI, or start continuous daily antiepileptic drugs","Perform urgent MRI brain and routine EEG before discharge"],"a":2,"e":"A brief (\u003c15 minutes), generalised seizure occurring once in 24 hours in a neurologically normal child aged 6 months to 5 years with fever (without CNS infection) defines a Simple Febrile Seizure. It carries an excellent prognosis without cognitive sequelae; routine EEG, neuroimaging, and continuous daily AEDs (whose side effects outweigh benefits) are NOT indicated. Lumbar puncture is indicated only if meningeal signs/CNS infection is suspected or in infants \u003c12 months lacking Hib/pneumococcal vaccines.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02778","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Acute Glomerulonephritis","q":"An 8-year-old boy presents with puffiness around the eyes, smoky cola-coloured urine, oliguria, and BP 138/92 mmHg two weeks after healing of pustular impetigo on his legs. Urinalysis shows dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts with 2+ proteinuria. Which serum complement profile is expected, and when should it normalise?","o":["Normal C3 with very low C4; normalises within 24 hours","Elevated C3 and elevated C4","Persistently low C3 and low C4 for \u003e 6 months","Low C3 with normal C4; C3 typically normalises within 6 to 8 weeks"],"a":3,"e":"APSGN (an acute nephritic syndrome 1-2 weeks post-pharyngitis or 2-3 weeks post-pyoderma/impetigo) activates the alternative complement pathway via nephritogenic Group A Streptococci, producing transient hypocomplementaemia with LOW C3 and NORMAL C4. C3 returns to normal within 6-8 weeks (persistent low C3 beyond 8-12 weeks suggests C3 glomerulopathy / MPGN and warrants renal biopsy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02779","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Fetal Circulation and Postnatal Remnants","q":"In fetal circulation, oxygenated blood returning from the placenta bypasses the hepatic sinusoids and flows directly into the inferior vena cava via the ductus venosus. Which adult anatomical structure is the obliterated fibrous remnant of the ductus venosus, and which structure derives from the umbilical vein?","o":["Ligamentum venosum (from ductus venosus) and ligamentum teres hepatis (from left umbilical vein)","Ligamentum arteriosum (from ductus venosus) and medial umbilical ligaments (from umbilical vein)","Falciform ligament (from ductus venosus) and coronary ligament (from umbilical vein)","Fossa ovalis (from ductus venosus) and urachus (from umbilical vein)"],"a":0,"e":"After birth, cessation of umbilical blood flow and rising arterial oxygen tension (plus reduced PGE2) trigger functional then anatomical closure of fetal shunts: (1) the left umbilical vein obliterates into the ligamentum teres hepatis (round ligament of liver, running in the free edge of the falciform ligament); (2) the ductus venosus obliterates into the ligamentum venosum (lying in the fissure between the left and caudate lobes); (3) the foramen ovale closes against septum secundum forming the fossa ovalis; (4) the ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum; and (5) the distal umbilical arteries become the medial umbilical ligaments.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02780","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Cardiac Embryology","q":"A cyanotic newborn shows an \u0027egg-on-a-string\u0027 cardiac silhouette on chest radiograph, with the aorta arising anteriorly from the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk arising posteriorly from the left ventricle (D-transposition of the great arteries). Failure of which embryonic process causes this defect?","o":["Premature closure of the foramen ovale in utero","Failure of the aorticopulmonary (conotruncal) septum to spiral 180 degrees during partitioning of the truncus arteriosus","Failure of neural crest cells to form the endocardial cushions of the atrioventricular canal","Excessive apoptosis of the septum primum producing an ostium secundum defect"],"a":1,"e":"During weeks 5-6, cardiac neural crest cells migrate into the truncal and bulbar ridges to form the aorticopulmonary septum, which normally spirals 180 degrees so that the aorta connects to the left ventricle and the pulmonary trunk connects to the right ventricle. If the aorticopulmonary septum grows straight down without spiraling, D-transposition of the great arteries (D-TGA) results—creating two parallel circuits incompatible with postnatal survival unless a mixing shunt (PDA, VSD, or ASD/PFO) is maintained.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02781","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Gastrointestinal Embryology","q":"A 2-year-old boy presents with painless bright-red rectal bleeding and severe iron-deficiency anemia. A technetium-99m pertechnetate scan highlights ectopic uptake in the right lower quadrant ~50 cm proximal to the ileocecal valve on the antimesenteric border of the ileum. Failure of obliteration of which embryonic duct causes this anomaly, and why does painless ulcer bleeding occur?","o":["Mesonephric (Wolffian) duct; heterotopic pancreatic enzymes digest the cecum","Thyroglossal duct; ectopic thyroid tissue undergoes infarction","Vitelline (omphalomesenteric) duct; ectopic acid-secreting gastric mucosa within Meckel diverticulum ulcerates adjacent ileal mucosa","Urachus (allantoic stalk); ectopic transitional epithelium erodes mesenteric vessels"],"a":2,"e":"Meckel diverticulum is the most common congenital GI anomaly, resulting from incomplete obliteration of the proximal vitelline (omphalomesenteric) duct connecting the embryonic midgut loop to the yolk sac. Following the \u0027Rule of 2s\u0027, it occurs in ~2% of the population, is ~2 inches long, lies within ~2 feet (~60 cm) proximal to the ileocecal valve on the antimesenteric border, often presents before age 2, and frequently contains heterotopic gastric mucosa (acid secretion causes painless ulceration and bleeding detected by Tc-99m pertechnetate \u0027Meckel scan\u0027) or pancreatic tissue.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02782","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Urogenital Embryology","q":"Assertion (A): A horseshoe kidney formed by fusion of the lower renal poles is typically trapped low in the abdomen at the L3-L4 vertebral level rather than ascending to its normal T12-L3 retroperitoneal position. Reason (R): During normal embryonic ascent from the pelvis, the fused isthmus of a horseshoe kidney hooks beneath the root of the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) arising from the abdominal aorta.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"The permanent metanephric kidneys develop in the pelvic region via reciprocal induction between the ureteric bud (forming collecting ducts, calyces, renal pelvis, and ureter) and the metanephric blastema (forming glomeruli through distal convoluted tubules). Normally, kidneys ascend to the lumbar region receiving successive aortic branches. When the lower poles fuse across the midline (horseshoe kidney, associated with Turner syndrome), the connecting isthmus catches under the origin of the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) at L3 during ascent, arresting the kidney in the lower abdomen.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02783","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Gastrointestinal Tract Histology","q":"At a histology practical exam, Slide 1 shows tall leaf-like villi with abundant mucous acini filling the submucosa (Brunner glands), whereas Slide 2 shows shorter villi, large aggregated mucosal/submucosal lymphoid nodules (Peyer patches) covered by M cells, and eosinophilic apical-granule cells (Paneth cells) at the crypt bases. Which two intestinal segments are represented?","o":["Slide 1: Jejunum; Slide 2: Duodenum","Slide 1: Ileum; Slide 2: Colon","Slide 1: Stomach fundus; Slide 2: Esophagus","Slide 1: Duodenum; Slide 2: Ileum"],"a":3,"e":"All three small-intestinal segments possess villi, microvilli, crypts of Lieberkühn, and Paneth cells (secreting lysozyme, alpha-defensins, and TNF-alpha at crypt bases). However, two regional hallmarks uniquely distinguish them: (1) Branched tubuloalveolar mucus-secreting Brunner glands in the submucosa (producing alkaline bicarbonate-rich mucus to neutralize gastric chyme) are pathognomonic for the duodenum; (2) large aggregated lymphoid follicles (Peyer patches) extending from the lamina propria into the submucosa along the antimesenteric border are diagnostic of the ileum (while the jejunum displays the tallest, most numerous plicae circulares and lacks both Brunner glands and prominent Peyer patches).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02784","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Chest Radiograph Mediastinal Contours","q":"While interpreting a standard posteroanterior (PA) and lateral chest radiograph in a clinical anatomy posting, a student traces the cardiovascular borders. Which structures normally form (1) the right cardiac border on PA view, (2) the left cardiac border (from top to bottom) on PA view, and (3) the anterior retrosternal cardiac border on lateral view?","o":["(1) Right atrium (and SVC above); (2) aortic knuckle, pulmonary trunk, left atrial appendage, left ventricle; (3) right ventricle","(1) Right ventricle; (2) superior vena cava and right atrium; (3) left atrium","(1) Left atrium; (2) ascending aorta and right ventricle; (3) left ventricle","(1) Inferior vena cava only; (2) descending aorta only; (3) pulmonary trunk only"],"a":0,"e":"On a standard PA chest radiograph: the right mediastinal/cardiac border is formed superiorly by the brachiocephalic vein/SVC and inferiorly by the right atrium (the right ventricle sits anteriorly and does NOT form the right or left lateral border on PA view). The left border consists from superior to inferior of the aortic arch (\u0027aortic knuckle\u0027), pulmonary artery segment, left atrial appendage (normally flat/slightly concave; bulges in mitral stenosis), and left ventricle. On a lateral radiograph, the right ventricle forms the anterior border behind the sternum, whereas the left atrium and left ventricle form the posterior cardiac border.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02785","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Intracranial Hemorrhage Neuroimaging","q":"A 22-year-old motorcyclist suffers a temporal bone fracture over the pterion, experiences a brief \u0027lucid interval\u0027, and then deteriorates rapidly. Non-contrast head CT shows a hyperdense, biconvex (lentiform) hematoma that stops sharply at the coronal and lambdoid sutures. Which vessel was lacerated, and why does this hematoma fail to cross cranial suture lines?","o":["Middle meningeal artery; blood accumulates in the potential epidural space between the inner skull table and tightly adherent periosteal dura mater, which inserts firmly at suture lines","Anterior cerebral artery; subarachnoid blood fills the basal cisterns","Superior sagittal bridging veins; subdural blood stops at sutures but crosses the falx cerebri","Sigmoid dural venous sinus; blood dissects freely across all cranial sutures"],"a":0,"e":"The pterion (H-shaped junction of frontal, parietal, squamous temporal, and greater wing of sphenoid) overlies the anterior division of the middle meningeal artery (branch of the maxillary artery entering via foramen spinosum). Fracture here lacerates the middle meningeal artery, stripping the outer periosteal layer of dura from the inner calvarium to create a high-pressure, lens-shaped (biconvex) epidural hematoma. Because periosteal dura is firmly anchored into cranial sutures, epidural hematomas do NOT cross suture lines (though they can cross midline dural reflections like the falx). Conversely, torn cerebral bridging veins create crescent-shaped (concave) subdural hematomas that cross suture lines freely but are limited by dural reflections (falx/tentorium).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02786","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Omental Bursa and Epiploic Foramen","q":"During emergency laparotomy for severe traumatic hepatic bleeding, a surgeon inserts an index finger through the epiploic foramen (of Winslow) and compresses the free edge of the lesser omentum between thumb and index finger (Pringle maneuver) to arrest hepatic inflow. Which three structures lie inside this compressed anterior boundary, and which major vein lies immediately posterior to the surgeon\u0027s finger?","o":["Left gastric artery, coronary vein, and esophageal branches anteriorly; azygos vein posteriorly","Proper hepatic artery, portal vein, and common bile duct inside the hepatoduodenal ligament anteriorly; inferior vena cava (IVC) posteriorly","Splenic artery, splenic vein, and pancreatic tail anteriorly; abdominal aorta posteriorly","Superior mesenteric artery, superior mesenteric vein, and uncinate process anteriorly; left renal vein posteriorly"],"a":1,"e":"The epiploic foramen (of Winslow) is the natural communication between the greater peritoneal sac and the lesser sac (omental bursa). Its boundaries are: Anteriorly, the free right margin of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) containing the portal triad—portal vein posteriorly, proper hepatic artery anteromedially, and common bile duct anterolaterally; Posteriorly, the retroperitoneal inferior vena cava (and right crus of diaphragm); Superiorly, the caudate lobe of the liver; Inferiorly, the first part of the duodenum. Compressing the hepatoduodenal ligament (Pringle maneuver) temporarily occludes hepatic arterial and portal venous inflow.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02787","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Auditory Physiology","q":"During a clinical physiology practical using a 512 Hz tuning fork, a patient with right ear otosclerosis demonstrates Weber test lateralizing to the RIGHT ear and a negative Rinne test (bone conduction \u003e air conduction) on the RIGHT side, with a positive Rinne (AC \u003e BC) on the LEFT. Why does Weber lateralize to the affected right ear, and which cochlear compartment contains the high-K+ fluid driving hair cell depolarization?","o":["Hyperpolarization of outer hair cells by Na+ influx; helicotrema CSF","Left cochlear nerve degeneration; scala vestibuli perilymph","Loss of ambient masking air-conducted noise makes bone-conducted vibration seem louder in the conductive-impaired right ear; scala media (cochlear duct) endolymph (+80 mV endocochlear potential secreted by stria vascularis)","Enhanced oval window amplification in the right ear; scala tympani perilymph"],"a":2,"e":"In right-sided conductive hearing loss (e.g., otosclerosis fixing the stapes footplate, cerumen impaction, middle ear effusion), bone conduction bypasses the middle ear directly to the cochlea; because ambient environmental masking noise transmitted via air is attenuated on the right, the patient perceives the bone-conducted 512 Hz tuning fork louder in the affected right ear (Weber lateralizes right) alongside right BC \u003e AC (Rinne negative). Mechanotransduction occurs when stereocilia deflect toward the kinocilium, opening tip-link K+ channels so high-K+ endolymph (~150 mM K+, +80 mV endocochlear potential generated by stria vascularis in the scala media) rushes into hair cells to depolarize them.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02788","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Vestibular Physiology","q":"In an awake patient with intact brainstem and vestibular pathways, irrigating the right external auditory canal with cold (30°C) water elicits horizontal nystagmus. Which direction does the fast corrective phase of nystagmus beat, and how do semicircular canals differ functionally from the utricle and saccule?","o":["No nystagmus occurs in an awake person with intact brainstem; saccule detects angular acceleration","Fast phase beats to the RIGHT (same side); semicircular canals detect linear gravity","Fast phase beats downward; utricle detects rotational yaw exclusively","Fast phase beats to the LEFT (opposite side: COWS mnemonic); semicircular canals detect angular acceleration (rotational head movements), whereas utricle and saccule detect linear acceleration and static head tilt"],"a":3,"e":"Thermal caloric testing induces convection currents in the horizontal semicircular canal endolymph: cold water causes endolymph to sink, reducing right vestibular afferent firing relative to the left, producing slow tonic eye drift toward the irrigated right ear (vestibulo-ocular reflex) followed by a rapid cortical corrective saccade toward the opposite LEFT ear (mnemonic COWS: Cold Opposite, Warm Same, referring to the fast phase). Anatomically, the three orthogonal semicircular canals (ampullary crista/cupula) transduce angular/rotational acceleration, whereas the utricle (horizontal macula) and saccule (vertical macula) contain calcium carbonate otoconia to sense linear acceleration and gravitational head tilt.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02789","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Chemical Senses (Olfaction and Gustation)","q":"Unlike most sensory neurons, olfactory receptor neurons continuously regenerate from basal stem cells and bypass the thalamus on their way to the primary olfactory cortex. Which intracellular signaling cascade mediates odorant transduction in olfactory cilia, and how is salty taste primarily transduced?","o":["Odorants bind G_olf-coupled receptors activating adenylyl cyclase III -\u003e cAMP -\u003e opens cyclic nucleotide-gated Na+/Ca2+ channels (and Ca2+-activated Cl- efflux); salty taste uses direct Na+ entry through amiloride-sensitive ENaC channels","Odorants use direct ionotropic mechanoreceptors; salty taste uses H+ proton channels exclusively","Odorants activate Gs -\u003e cGMP -\u003e closes Na+ channels causing hyperpolarization; salty taste uses gustducin GPCRs","Odorants bind tyrosine kinase receptors; salty taste uses voltage-gated K+ closure via IP3"],"a":0,"e":"Odorants dissolve in nasal mucus and bind GPCRs on olfactory cilia coupled to G_olf, activating adenylyl cyclase III to raise cAMP. cAMP opens cyclic nucleotide-gated (CNG) cation channels allowing Na+ and Ca2+ influx; Ca2+ further opens Ca2+-activated Cl- channels (ANO2/TMEM16B), causing Cl- efflux (because olfactory cilia maintain high intracellular Cl-) and strong depolarization. Olfactory mitral/tufted cells project via the lateral olfactory tract directly to the piriform/entorhinal cortex and amygdala without a thalamic relay. In taste buds, salty (Na+) enters directly via ENaC (sour uses H+ proton channels such as OTOP1, whereas sweet, umami, and bitter use T1R/T2R GPCRs coupled to gustducin/PLC-beta2).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02790","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Spinal Reflexes and Muscle Proprioceptors","q":"Assertion (A): During voluntary alpha motor neuron contraction of an extrafusal skeletal muscle, gamma motor neurons are simultaneously co-activated to maintain muscle spindle sensitivity across the entire range of muscle shortening. Reason (R): Gamma motor neurons innervate the contractile polar ends of intrafusal nuclear bag and nuclear chain fibers, keeping the central sensory region taut so Group Ia afferents do not go slack when extrafusal fibers shorten.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Muscle spindles lie in parallel with extrafusal skeletal muscle fibers and detect muscle length and rate of stretch (Group Ia primary annulospiral afferents and Group II secondary flower-spray afferents). If alpha motor neurons shortened extrafusal fibers alone, the parallel spindle would become slack and stop firing. Descending motor tracts therefore trigger alpha-gamma coactivation: gamma motor neurons contract the striated polar ends of intrafusal fibers, stretching the non-contractile equatorial sensory zone so Ia/II afferents remain responsive to stretch and load perturbations. By contrast, Golgi tendon organs sit in series at the musculotendinous junction (Group Ib afferents) to sense tension and drive autogenic inhibition.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02791","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Sleep Architecture and Electroencephalogram (EEG)","q":"On polysomnography of a healthy young adult, which pairing of sleep/wakefulness stage, characteristic EEG waveform, and neurochemical/physiological feature is accurate?","o":["Stage N3 NREM sleep: beta waves (14-30 Hz) with dreaming; Awake eyes-open alert: theta waves (4-7 Hz)","Stage N2 NREM sleep: sleep spindles (12-14 Hz) and K-complexes; Stage N3 NREM sleep: high-amplitude low-frequency delta waves (\u003c4 Hz); REM sleep: desynchronized low-voltage sawtooth waves, rapid eye movements, and pontine cholinergic-driven muscle atonia","Awake eyes-closed relaxed state: delta waves (1-3 Hz); Stage N1: gamma bursts; REM sleep: sleep spindles and maximal skeletal muscle tone","REM sleep: slow-wave delta oscillations with sleepwalking and night terrors"],"a":1,"e":"Normal sleep cycles every ~90 minutes between NREM and REM: Awake alert exhibits beta waves (13-30 Hz), transitioning to occipital alpha waves (8-13 Hz) with eyes closed. Stage N1 shows theta waves (4-7 Hz); Stage N2 (largest portion of adult sleep) is defined by sleep spindles and K-complexes; Stage N3 (slow-wave/deep sleep, when sleepwalking and night terrors occur) shows high-voltage delta waves (0.5-4 Hz). REM sleep (driven by pontine cholinergic PPRF/LDT/PPT neurons while locus coeruleus NE and raphe 5-HT fall silent) displays awake-like desynchronized EEG/sawtooth waves, vivid dreaming, penile/clitoral tumescence, variable heart rate/breathing, and glycinergic spinal alpha-motor inhibition (atonia).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02792","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Hypothalamic Integrative Functions","q":"A patient with a craniopharyngioma compressing the ventromedial hypothalamic nucleus develops hyperphagia and severe obesity, whereas lesioning of the lateral hypothalamic area produces aphagia and weight loss. Which other hypothalamic nuclei respectively regulate (1) circadian melatonin rhythm, (2) heat dissipation (sweating/cutaneous vasodilation), and (3) heat conservation/shivering?","o":["(1) Supraoptic nucleus; (2) ventromedial nucleus; (3) dorsomedial nucleus","(1) Paraventricular nucleus; (2) posterior hypothalamus; (3) anterior hypothalamus","(1) Suprachiasmatic nucleus; (2) anterior hypothalamus / preoptic area; (3) posterior hypothalamus","(1) Arcuate nucleus; (2) mammillary bodies; (3) lateral hypothalamus"],"a":2,"e":"Hypothalamic nuclei have tightly localized homeostatic roles: the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN, receiving retinohypothalamic melanopsin light input) is the master circadian pacemaker controlling pineal melatonin rhythm; the anterior hypothalamus/preoptic area senses core blood warmth and triggers heat dissipation (sweating, cutaneous vasodilation, panting—so anterior lesions cause hyperthermia); the posterior hypothalamus integrates cold defense (shivering, cutaneous vasoconstriction, piloerection—so posterior lesions cause poikilothermia/hypothermia); the ventromedial nucleus is the satiety center (stimulated by leptin); and the lateral hypothalamus is the hunger/feeding center (orexin/hypocretin).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02793","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Somatosensory Pain Pathways","q":"After bumping his shin against a bench, a medical student vigorously rubs the surrounding skin, noticeably dulling the throbbing pain. According to Melzack and Wall\u0027s Gate Control Theory of pain, how does mechanical rubbing inhibit ascending nociceptive transmission in the spinal dorsal horn?","o":["Mechanical pressure blocks peripheral voltage-gated Na+ channels in dorsal root ganglia permanently","Unmyelinated C fibers directly stimulate alpha motor neurons to block spinothalamic decussation","A-beta fibers cross immediately in the dorsal columns to inhibit the contralateral amygdala","Large myelinated A-beta mechanoreceptor afferents excite inhibitory interneurons in the substantia gelatinosa (Rexed lamina II), presynaptically inhibiting C and A-delta nociceptive transmission to projection neurons"],"a":3,"e":"Fast sharp pain travels via thinly myelinated A-delta fibers (glutamate) and slow burning/throbbing pain via unmyelinated C fibers (glutamate + substance P) into the spinal dorsal horn (laminae I, II, V) before ascending in the contralateral anterolateral spinothalamic tract. Tactile rubbing activates fast, large-diameter myelinated A-beta fibers whose collaterals excite enkephalinergic/GABAergic inhibitory interneurons in the substantia gelatinosa (lamina II)—closing the dorsal horn \u0027gate\u0027 on C/A-delta input (the same principle utilized by transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation, TENS).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02794","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation","q":"A patient in severe acute metabolic acidosis without respiratory compensation has arterial plasma [HCO3-] = 12 mEq/L and PaCO2 = 40 mmHg. Using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation with pKa = 6.1 and CO2 solubility coefficient alpha = 0.03 mEq/L/mmHg, what is the calculated arterial pH?","o":["7.10","7.22","6.80","7.40"],"a":0,"e":"The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation for the blood bicarbonate-CO2 buffer system is pH = 6.1 + log10([HCO3-] / (0.03 x PaCO2)). First calculate dissolved CO2: 0.03 x 40 mmHg = 1.2 mEq/L. Next take the ratio: 12 / 1.2 = 10. Since log10(10) = 1.0, pH = 6.1 + 1.0 = 7.10.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02795","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"High-Altitude Acclimatization and Acid-Base Compensation","q":"A trekker ascends rapidly to 4,200 meters in Ladakh. Low ambient PO2 stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors, driving hyperventilation (PaCO2 drops to 26 mmHg, arterial pH rises to 7.50), yet over 3-4 days arterial pH normalizes toward 7.42. How do the kidneys compensate during acclimatization, and how does prophylactic acetazolamide accelerate this process?","o":["Kidneys increase erythropoietin within 2 hours to buffer H+; acetazolamide acts as a loop diuretic on NKCC2","Proximal tubules decrease H+ secretion and excrete filtered HCO3- (lowering plasma HCO3-); acetazolamide inhibits proximal tubular carbonic anhydrase to induce bicarbonaturia and mild metabolic acidosis, stimulating central chemoreceptors to maintain ventilation","Distal alpha-intercalated cells retain more HCO3- and secrete excess NH4+; acetazolamide inhibits Na+/K+-ATPase in collecting ducts","Pulmonary capillaries secrete bicarbonate into alveoli; acetazolamide directly stimulates carotid body O2 sensors"],"a":1,"e":"High-altitude hypobaric hypoxia stimulates carotid body peripheral chemoreceptors, causing hyperventilation and acute respiratory alkalosis—which paradoxically brakes central medullary CO2/H+ chemoreceptors and limits nocturnal ventilation (acute mountain sickness). Over days, renal compensation lowers plasma HCO3- (~4-5 mEq/L drop per 10 mmHg chronic fall in PaCO2) by reducing proximal tubular H+ secretion and increasing urinary HCO3- excretion. Acetazolamide inhibits luminal/intracellular carbonic anhydrase in proximal convoluted tubules, forcing rapid renal HCO3- loss and metabolic acidosis that stimulates central chemoreceptors to sustain hyperventilation during sleep.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02796","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Diagnostic Serum Biomarkers","q":"A 56-year-old man presents 4 days after an episode of crushing chest pain with re-emerging chest discomfort, while another patient presents with isolated elevation of serum alkaline phosphatase (ALP 480 U/L) and bone pain. Which cardiac biomarker best detects re-infarction at Day 4, and which serum enzyme distinguishes hepatic cholestasis from Paget bone disease as the source of elevated ALP?","o":["Cardiac troponin I alone (returns to baseline in 6 hours); serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)","AST (specific exclusively to cardiac muscle); serum amylase","CK-MB (returns to baseline within 48-72 hours, so a repeat spike detects re-infarction, whereas cardiac troponins stay elevated 7-14 days); gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) or 5\u0027-nucleotidase (elevated in hepatobiliary cholestasis, normal in bone disease)","Myoglobin (stays elevated for 21 days); serum creatine kinase (CK-MM)"],"a":2,"e":"Although high-sensitivity cardiac troponin I and T (cTnI/cTnT) are the gold-standard markers for myocardial injury (rising in 3-6 hours and remaining elevated for 7-14 days), their prolonged elevation limits detection of early re-infarction at Days 3-7; CK-MB rises in 4-6 hours, peaks at 24 hours, and normalizes by 48-72 hours, making a secondary rise in CK-MB useful for detecting re-infarction. Because ALP originates mainly from liver canaliculi, bone osteoblasts, placenta, and intestine, simultaneous elevation of GGT or 5\u0027-nucleotidase confirms a hepatobiliary source (whereas normal GGT points to osteoblastic bone turnover).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02797","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Isoenzymes","q":"Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is a tetramer composed of H (heart) and M (muscle) subunits forming five isoenzymes (LDH-1 to LDH-5). Which isoenzyme predominates in normal human serum versus acute myocardial infarction/hemolytic anemia (\u0027flipped\u0027 pattern) and hepatocellular/skeletal muscle necrosis?","o":["Normal serum: LDH-5 \u003e LDH-1; MI: LDH-5 \u003e LDH-4; liver injury: LDH-1","Normal serum: LDH-3 exclusively; MI: LDH-4; liver injury: LDH-1","Normal serum: LDH-1 \u003e LDH-2; MI: LDH-3 \u003e LDH-2; skeletal muscle: LDH-2","Normal serum: LDH-2 (H3M1) \u003e LDH-1 (H4); MI/intravascular hemolysis: LDH-1 \u003e LDH-2; liver/skeletal muscle injury: LDH-5 (M4)"],"a":3,"e":"LDH tetramers combine H (LDHA/B-encoded heart-type, fastest anodal electrophoretic mobility) and M (muscle-type, slowest cathodal mobility) polypeptides: LDH-1 (H4) is enriched in myocardium and erythrocytes; LDH-2 (H3M1) is normally the most abundant serum isoenzyme; LDH-3 (H2M2) in lungs/lymphocytes/platelets; LDH-4 (H1M3) in kidney/placenta; and LDH-5 (M4) in skeletal muscle and liver. In late acute myocardial infarction or intravascular hemolysis, massive LDH-1 release reverses the normal serum ratio so LDH-1 \u003e LDH-2 (\u0027flipped LDH pattern\u0027).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02798","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Mitochondrial Inheritance","q":"A 22-year-old man develops subacute painless bilateral central vision loss (Leber hereditary optic neuropathy), and his maternal aunt and sister have milder optic atrophy, whereas none of his children inherit the condition when he marries an unaffected woman. What explains both the transmission pattern and the variable clinical severity among maternal relatives?","o":["Mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) maternal inheritance (all oocyte mitochondria derive from the mother) combined with heteroplasmy (varying proportions of mutant vs wild-type mtDNA partitioned into daughter cells/tissues)","X-linked recessive inheritance with skewed lyonization in females","Autosomal dominant inheritance with paternal genomic imprinting","Y-linked holandric inheritance affecting Complex II solely"],"a":0,"e":"Human mitochondrial DNA (16.5 kb circular double-stranded DNA encoding 13 oxidative phosphorylation subunits, 22 tRNAs, and 2 rRNAs) is transmitted exclusively from the mother because sperm mitochondria are eliminated after fertilization. Consequently, an affected mother transmits the mutation to all her offspring, whereas an affected father transmits it to none. Because each cell contains hundreds of mitochondria, random segregation of mixed mutant and wild-type mtDNA (heteroplasmy) during cell division produces variable phenotypic severity depending on whether high-ATP-demand tissues (retina/optic nerve, brain, skeletal/cardiac muscle) exceed the biochemical threshold.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02799","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders","q":"A 39-year-old man develops progressive chorea, dementia, and caudate nucleus atrophy; his father developed identical symptoms at age 52, and his grandfather at age 64. Which trinucleotide repeat expansion and molecular mechanism explain both this disorder and earlier onset in successive generations (anticipation)?","o":["CTG repeat expansion in DMPK (Myotonic dystrophy) causing loss of dystrophin","CAG polyglutamine repeat expansion in HTT (Huntington disease) undergoing polymerase slippage during paternal spermatogenesis","GAA intronic repeat expansion in FXN (Friedreich ataxia) causing autosomal dominant anticipation","CGG repeat hypermethylation silencing FMR1 (Fragile X syndrome) during paternal transmission"],"a":1,"e":"Huntington disease is an autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disorder caused by an unstable CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion (\u003e36-40 repeats) in exon 1 of the huntingtin (HTT) gene on chromosome 4p, producing a toxic gain-of-function polyglutamine tract that aggregates in striatal (caudate/putamen) GABAergic medium spiny neurons. DNA polymerase slippage during meiosis—especially during paternal spermatogenesis—lengthens the CAG tract across generations, causing earlier onset and more severe disease in offspring (genetic anticipation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02800","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Epigenetics and Genomic Imprinting","q":"Assertion (A): Deletion of the same chromosome 15q11-q13 region produces Prader-Willi syndrome when inherited on the paternal chromosome, whereas it produces Angelman syndrome when inherited on the maternal chromosome. Reason (R): Due to parent-of-origin epigenetic DNA methylation (genomic imprinting), Prader-Willi critical genes (e.g., SNRPN) are expressed exclusively from the paternal allele (maternal allele silenced), whereas UBE3A in brain is expressed exclusively from the maternal allele (paternal allele silenced).","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Genomic imprinting is an epigenetic phenomenon in which CpG methylation silences one parental allele during gametogenesis, leaving the organism functionally haploid at that locus. On chromosome 15q11-q13, SNRPN/NDN are maternally silenced and expressed only from the paternal allele—so paternal deletion (or maternal uniparental disomy) causes Prader-Willi syndrome (neonatal hypotonia, hyperphagia, obesity, hypogonadism). Conversely, UBE3A is paternally imprinted/silenced in neurons—so maternal 15q11-q13 deletion (or paternal uniparental disomy / UBE3A mutation) causes Angelman syndrome (ataxia, seizures, inappropriate happy laughter).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02801","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Proto-Oncogenes and Signal Transduction","q":"Which biochemical mechanism converts the KRAS proto-oncogene into a constitutively active dominant oncogene in pancreatic and colorectal adenocarcinomas?","o":["Missense point mutation (commonly at codon 12, 13, or 61) that impairs intrinsic GTPase activity and GAP-mediated GTP hydrolysis, locking RAS in its active GTP-bound state","Biallelic promoter hypermethylation silencing KRAS mRNA transcription","Reciprocal translocation fusing KRAS to the mitochondrial cytochrome c gene","Deletion of both KRAS alleles causing loss of G1/S checkpoint arrest"],"a":0,"e":"RAS family small monomeric G-proteins (KRAS, HRAS, NRAS) cycle between an inactive GDP-bound state and an active GTP-bound state that stimulates the RAF-MEK-ERK MAP kinase proliferation cascade. Point mutations at critical GTP-binding/catalytic codons (12, 13, or 61) abolish intrinsic GTPase activity and prevent GTPase-activating proteins (GAPs, such as neurofibromin/NF1) from hydrolyzing GTP to GDP—locking RAS permanently \u0027on\u0027 even in the absence of growth factor receptor signaling. Only a single mutated allele is needed for this dominant gain-of-function effect.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02802","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Non-Coding RNAs","q":"A biotechnology therapy uses synthetic double-stranded small interfering RNA (siRNA) to silence hepatic transthyretin mRNA (patisiran). Which cytosolic ribonuclease processes precursor dsRNA into ~21-23 nucleotide duplexes, and which multiprotein complex uses the guide strand to cleave target mRNA?","o":["Telomerase; exosome adds a poly(A) tail to silence translation","Drosha inside the cytoplasm; spliceosome cleaves mRNA introns","Dicer processes dsRNA into ~21-23 nt duplexes; RISC (RNA-induced silencing complex containing Argonaute-2) cleaves complementary target mRNA","RNase H inside mitochondria; 30S ribosome degrades mRNA"],"a":2,"e":"In RNA interference (RNAi), long double-stranded RNAs or nuclear-exported pre-miRNA hairpins (initially cleaved in the nucleus by Drosha-DGCR8 and exported via Exportin-5) are cleaved in the cytosol by the RNase III enzyme Dicer into ~21-23 nucleotide duplexes. One guide strand loads into the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC) containing Argonaute (Ago2): perfect sequence complementarity (typical of siRNA) triggers endonucleolytic mRNA cleavage, whereas partial 3\u0027-UTR complementarity (typical of endogenous miRNA) causes translational repression and mRNA deadenylation/destabilization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02803","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium","q":"In a large randomly mating population in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder affects 1 in 10,000 newborns (q^2 = 0.0001). What are the mutant allele frequency (q) and the unaffected heterozygous carrier frequency (2pq) in this population?","o":["q = 0.02 (2%); carrier frequency = 0.04 (4%)","q = 0.10 (10%); carrier frequency = 0.18 (18%)","q = 0.0001 (0.01%); carrier frequency = 0.0002 (0.02%)","q = 0.01 (1%); carrier frequency 2pq = 0.0198 (1.98%, or approximately 1 in 50)"],"a":3,"e":"Under Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium (p + q = 1 and p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = 1), affected homozygotes represent q^2 = 1 / 10,000 = 0.0001. Taking the square root gives the recessive allele frequency q = 0.01 (1%), and the wild-type allele frequency p = 1 - 0.01 = 0.99. Therefore, the heterozygous carrier frequency is 2pq = 2 x 0.99 x 0.01 = 0.0198 (1.98%, roughly 1 in 50 individuals)—illustrating why asymptomatic carriers are ~200 times more common than affected individuals.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02804","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Protein Synthesis and Post-Translational Modification","q":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae exotoxin and Pseudomonas aeruginosa exotoxin A halt eukaryotic host protein synthesis by catalyzing ADP-ribosylation of a unique modified histidine residue (diphthamide) on which translation factor, and which fat-soluble vitamin is required post-translationally to gamma-carboxylate glutamate residues of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X?","o":["Eukaryotic elongation factor 2 (eEF-2); vitamin K (hydroquinone cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase)","Peptidyl transferase (28S rRNA); vitamin C","Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase; vitamin B12","Eukaryotic initiation factor 4E (eIF4E); vitamin E"],"a":0,"e":"Both diphtheria toxin (fragment A) and Pseudomonas exotoxin A transfer an ADP-ribose group from NAD+ onto diphthamide on eukaryotic elongation factor 2 (eEF-2), blocking ribosomal translocation along mRNA and killing host cells (whereas Shiga toxin and ricin depurinate 28S rRNA). Separately, hepatic gamma-glutamyl carboxylase converts specific N-terminal glutamate (Glu) residues on prothrombin (II), VII, IX, X, Protein C, and Protein S into gamma-carboxyglutamate (Gla) residues—allowing Ca2+ chelation and phospholipid membrane binding—using reduced vitamin K hydroquinone (regenerated by VKORC1, the target of warfarin).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02805","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Bone Tumors","q":"A 16-year-old boy presents with progressive pain and swelling above his right knee. Radiograph reveals an aggressive, mixed lytic-sclerotic destructive lesion in the distal femoral metaphysis with periosteal elevation forming a \u0027Codman triangle\u0027 and \u0027sunburst\u0027 spicules. Biopsy shows pleomorphic spindle/polyhedral cells directly producing lace-like eosinophilic osteoid matrix. What is the diagnosis, and how does Ewing sarcoma differ in location and cytogenetics?","o":["Osteoid osteoma; Ewing sarcoma produces nocturnal pain relieved dramatically by aspirin","Osteosarcoma; Ewing sarcoma typically arises in the diaphysis of long bones with \u0027onion-skin\u0027 periosteal reaction, small round blue cells (Homer-Wright rosettes), and t(11;22)(q24;q12) EWSR1-FLI1 fusion","Chondrosarcoma; Ewing sarcoma arises in the metaphysis with malignant hyaline cartilage","Giant cell tumor (osteoclastoma); Ewing sarcoma arises in the epiphysis with \u0027soap-bubble\u0027 osteolysis"],"a":1,"e":"Osteosarcoma is the most common primary malignant bone tumor in adolescents/young adults (bimodal peak in teens and \u003e60s secondary to Paget disease/radiation), classically arising in the metaphysis around the knee (distal femur or proximal tibia). Radiographs show periosteal lifting (Codman triangle) and perpendicular \u0027sunburst\u0027 mineralization, while histology requires malignant mesenchymal cells directly depositing unmineralized osteoid. In contrast, Ewing sarcoma (children/teens) classically involves the diaphysis/metadiaphysis with concentric \u0027onion-skin\u0027 periosteal layering, sheets of CD99+ glycogen-rich small round blue cells forming Homer-Wright pseudorosettes, and t(11;22)(q24;q12) generating EWSR1-FLI1.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02806","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Metabolic Bone Diseases","q":"A 68-year-old man experiences increasing hat size, progressive sensorineural hearing loss, and bowing of the tibiae. Serum calcium and phosphate are normal, but serum alkaline phosphatase (ALP) is markedly elevated (980 U/L) with elevated urinary hydroxyproline/N-telopeptide. Bone biopsy during the sclerotic phase reveals thickened, chaotic trabeculae criss-crossed by prominent irregular cement lines creating a \u0027mosaic\u0027 (jigsaw-puzzle) pattern. What is the diagnosis, and which malignancy complicates it in ~1% of cases?","o":["Postmenopausal osteoporosis; Ewing sarcoma","Osteomalacia; multiple myeloma","Paget disease of bone (osteitis deformans); secondary osteosarcoma","Primary hyperparathyroidism (osteitis fibrosa cystica); chondrosarcoma"],"a":2,"e":"Paget disease of bone (osteitis deformans) progresses through three overlapping phases—osteolytic (hyperactive multinucleated osteoclasts), mixed osteoclastic-osteoblastic, and burnt-out osteosclerotic—resulting in structurally weak, enlarged, hypervascular bone (causing skull enlargement/hat-size increase, cranial nerve foraminal compression with deafness, tibial bowing, and high-output cardiac failure). Serum Ca2+ and PO4 are characteristically normal while osteoblastic ALP is sky-high. The pathognomonic histological hallmark is the \u0027mosaic\u0027 pattern of irregular cement lines in lamellar bone, and ~1% of polyostotic cases undergo sarcomatous transformation to secondary osteosarcoma.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02807","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Soft Tissue Neoplasms and GIST","q":"A well-circumscribed submucosal spindle-cell gastric tumor arising from the interstitial cells of Cajal stains strongly positive for CD117 (c-KIT) and DOG1. Which activating receptor tyrosine kinase mutation drives ~80% of gastrointestinal stromal tumors (GISTs), and which oral targeted inhibitor treats unresectable disease?","o":["t(X;18)(p11;q11) SS18-SSX fusion; rituximab","t(12;16) FUS-DDIT3 fusion; methotrexate","MDM2/CDK4 amplification on 12q13-15; trastuzumab","KIT (or PDGFRA) gain-of-function mutation; imatinib mesylate"],"a":3,"e":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumors (GISTs) are the most common mesenchymal neoplasms of the GI tract (stomach most frequent), originating from pacemaker interstitial cells of Cajal. Over 80% harbor activating mutations in KIT (CD117, ~75-80%) or PDGFRA (~5-10%), driving constitutive tyrosine kinase signaling; immunohistochemistry is characteristically positive for CD117, DOG1, and CD34. Unresectable or metastatic KIT/PDGFRA-driven GISTs respond dramatically to the tyrosine kinase inhibitor imatinib mesylate.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02808","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Primary CNS Tumors","q":"A 59-year-old man presents with rapid cognitive decline and a ring-enhancing frontal lobe mass crossing the corpus callosum (\u0027butterfly glioma\u0027). Resection shows GFAP-positive pleomorphic fibrillary astrocytes, serpentine necrosis bordered by palisading tumor nuclei (\u0027pseudopalisading necrosis\u0027), and glomeruloid microvascular proliferation. How does this tumor\u0027s molecular signature contrast with oligodendroglioma?","o":["Glioblastoma (CNS WHO Grade 4 astrocytoma) is commonly IDH-wildtype with EGFR amplification, TERT promoter mutation, and chromosome 10q/PTEN loss, whereas oligodendroglioma shows IDH1/2 mutation with whole-arm 1p/19q codeletion and \u0027fried-egg\u0027 perinuclear halos","Glioblastoma shows 1p/19q codeletion and psammoma whorls, whereas oligodendroglioma shows Rosenthal fibers and BRAF duplication","Glioblastoma arises from arachnoid cap cells with EMA positivity, whereas oligodendroglioma shows Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes","Both glioblastoma and oligodendroglioma are universally IDH-wildtype and GFAP-negative"],"a":0,"e":"IDH-wildtype glioblastoma (WHO Grade 4) is the most aggressive and common malignant primary adult brain tumor, classically infiltrating white-matter tracts across the corpus callosum (\u0027butterfly\u0027 appearance) with central necrosis and ring enhancement. Histopathologic hallmarks are GFAP-positive pleomorphic astrocytic cells, pseudopalisading necrosis, and microvascular (glomeruloid) endothelial proliferation, driven by EGFR amplification, TERT promoter mutation, +7/-10 (PTEN loss). In contrast, oligodendroglioma is defined molecularly by IDH1/2 mutation AND whole-arm 1p/19q codeletion, showing round uniform nuclei with clear cytoplasmic \u0027fried-egg\u0027 halos, delicate \u0027chicken-wire\u0027 capillaries, and calcifications.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02809","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Neurodegenerative and Prion Diseases","q":"At neuropathologic autopsy of a 78-year-old woman with progressive memory loss and cortical atrophy, silver staining of the hippocampus and neocortex reveals extracellular neuritic plaques with Congo red-positive amyloid cores alongside intraneuronal flame-shaped neurofibrillary tangles. Which two misfolded proteins compose these lesions, and which cerebral vascular complication shares the plaque protein?","o":["Extracellular A-beta (beta-amyloid cleaved from APP by beta- and gamma-secretases) in plaques and hyperphosphorylated intracellular tau in neurofibrillary tangles; cerebral amyloid angiopathy (causing lobar intracerebral hemorrhage)","Extracellular alpha-synuclein in plaques and TDP-43 in tangles; lacunar basal ganglia infarction","Cytoplasmic ubiquitin-positive Pick bodies and alpha-synuclein Lewy bodies; cavernous sinus thrombosis","Intranuclear huntingtin in plaques and PrPSc in tangles; Berry aneurysm rupture"],"a":0,"e":"Alzheimer disease is defined neuropathologically by two hallmark proteinopathies: (1) extracellular neuritic (senile) plaques containing A-beta42 amyloid cores derived from sequential cleavage of amyloid precursor protein (APP, chromosome 21—explaining early Alzheimer pathology in Down syndrome) by beta-secretase and gamma-secretase (presenilin 1/2), and (2) intracellular flame-shaped neurofibrillary tangles composed of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated tau protein (paired helical filaments). A-beta deposition within leptomeningeal and cortical arteriolar walls causes cerebral amyloid angiopathy, a major cause of spontaneous lobar intracerebral hemorrhage in older adults.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02810","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Demyelinating Diseases","q":"A 28-year-old woman presents with subacute monocular painful vision loss (optic neuritis) followed 6 months later by internuclear ophthalmoplegia and lower-limb paresthesias. Brain MRI shows hyperintense ovoid periventricular white-matter lesions oriented perpendicular to the lateral ventricles (\u0027Dawson fingers\u0027), and CSF electrophoresis shows discrete oligoclonal IgG bands absent from serum. Which CNS cell and immune process underlie active plaques?","o":["Schwann cell destruction in the peripheral nerves by anti-ganglioside antibodies (Guillain-Barré syndrome)","Autoimmune Th1/Th17 CD4+ T-cell and macrophage-mediated destruction of oligodendrocytes and CNS myelin (with relative axonal sparing initially), accompanied by intrathecal B-cell IgG synthesis","JC polyomavirus infection of oligodendrocytes causing multifocal demyelination without oligoclonal bands","Rapid osmotic myelinolysis of the central pons from overly fast hyponatremia correction"],"a":1,"e":"Multiple sclerosis (MS) is a chronic T-cell-mediated (Th1 IFN-gamma and Th17 IL-17) autoimmune demyelinating disorder of CNS white matter targeting myelin antigens (such as myelin basic protein) and oligodendrocytes—classically producing sharply demarcated gray-tan plaques around periventricular venules (\u0027Dawson fingers\u0027), optic nerves, brainstem (MLF lesion causing internuclear ophthalmoplegia), and spinal cord. Active plaques show myelin-laden macrophages (Luxol fast blue loss) and perivascular lymphocytic cuffs with relative preservation of axons, transitioning to gliosis in chronic plaques. Intrathecal clonal B-cell IgG production produces CSF oligoclonal bands.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02811","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Adrenal Medulla and Pituitary Tumors","q":"A 38-year-old man presents with paroxysmal headaches, palpitations, diaphoresis, and severe episodic hypertension (220/126 mmHg). Plasma free metanephrines are markedly elevated, and abdominal MRI identifies a 4-cm vascular right adrenal mass. Resection demonstrates nests of polygonal chromaffin cells surrounded by rich capillary networks and S100-positive sustentacular cells (\u0027Zellballen\u0027 pattern) staining positive for chromogranin and synaptophysin. Which rule classically summarizes its epidemiology, and why must alpha-blockade precede beta-blockade preoperatively?","o":["Adrenocortical carcinoma; beta-blockers cause hypoglycemia","Pituitary corticotroph adenoma (Crooke hyaline change); alpha-blockers prevent Nelson syndrome","Pheochromocytoma (\u002710% tumor\u0027: ~10% bilateral, ~10% extra-adrenal/paraganglioma, ~10% malignant, though \u003e25% are now recognized as hereditary); starting beta-blockers first blocks vasodilatory beta-2 receptors and triggers unopposed alpha-1 hypertensive crisis","Conn adenoma; alpha-blockade prevents hyperkalemia"],"a":2,"e":"Pheochromocytoma is a catecholamine-secreting neoplasm of adrenal medullary chromaffin cells (extra-adrenal sympathetic tumors are termed paragangliomas, often at the organ of Zuckerkandl). Histologically, polygonal cells form \u0027Zellballen\u0027 (cell balls) bounded by S100-positive sustentacular cells, staining positive for neuroendocrine markers (chromogranin A, synaptophysin) and turning dark brown with dichromate salts. Preoperatively, alpha-adrenergic blockade (phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin) MUST be established before beta-blockers (propranolol) to prevent unopposed alpha-1-mediated vasoconstriction and catastrophic hypertensive crisis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02812","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Diabetes Mellitus Pathogenesis and Complications","q":"Assertion (A): Type 2 diabetes mellitus shows pancreatic islet deposition of pink amorphous amyloid derived from islet amyloid polypeptide (amylin), whereas Type 1 diabetes characteristically shows lymphocytic insulitis and severe beta-cell depletion. Reason (R): In Type 2 diabetes, chronic peripheral insulin resistance drives compensatory hypersecretion of insulin and co-packaged amylin by beta cells until amylin misfolds into islet amyloid fibrils and beta cells fail, whereas Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune T-cell-mediated destruction of beta cells.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"In Type 1 diabetes mellitus (HLA-DR3/DR4 association, anti-GAD/ICA/insulin autoantibodies), autoreactive CD4+ and CD8+ T cells infiltrate islets (\u0027insulitis\u0027) and selectively destroy beta cells. In Type 2 diabetes mellitus, peripheral insulin resistance forces compensatory beta-cell hypersecretion of both insulin and co-secreted amylin (islet amyloid polypeptide, IAPP); excess amylin aggregates into extracellular Congo red-positive islet amyloid that replaces islets and accelerates beta-cell dysfunction. Both types share microvascular complications including diabetic nephropathy (hyaline arteriolosclerosis of BOTH afferent and efferent arterioles, diffuse mesangial sclerosis, and PAS-positive acellular Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02813","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN) Syndromes","q":"Which germline mutation and tumor triad characterize Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2B (MEN 2B) in contrast to MEN 1 (Wermer syndrome)?","o":["MEN 2B: BRAF V600E mutation causing papillary thyroid carcinoma and insulinoma; MEN 1: TP53 mutation","MEN 2B: APC mutation causing follicular thyroid carcinoma and colon polyps; MEN 1: BRCA1 mutation","MEN 2B: loss-of-function VHL mutation causing hemangioblastomas and renal cell carcinoma; MEN 1: RET mutation","MEN 2B: gain-of-function RET proto-oncogene mutation (10q11.2) causing medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, and mucosal neuromas/marfanoid habitus (without hyperparathyroidism); MEN 1: loss-of-function MEN1 (menin) tumor suppressor mutation causing parathyroid, pituitary, and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors"],"a":3,"e":"All MEN syndromes follow autosomal dominant inheritance: MEN 1 (Wermer syndrome) stems from inactivating mutations in the MEN1 (menin) tumor suppressor gene (11q13), causing the \u00273 Ps\u0027—Parathyroid hyperplasia/adenomas (most common), Pancreatic/duodenal neuroendocrine tumors (gastrinoma/Zollinger-Ellison, insulinoma), and Pituitary adenomas (prolactinoma). Both MEN 2A (Sipple: medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, parathyroid hyperplasia) and MEN 2B (medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, oral/GI mucosal neuromas, and marfanoid habitus—NO hyperparathyroidism) result from activating germline mutations in the RET receptor tyrosine kinase proto-oncogene (prophylactic thyroidectomy is indicated in childhood).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02814","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Monoclonal Antibodies","q":"A 50-year-old woman with HER2/neu-positive breast cancer develops reduced left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF 38%) while receiving trastuzumab, and a 44-year-old man with Crohn disease develops miliary tuberculosis after starting infliximab. Which targets and toxicity profiles explain these two biologics?","o":["Trastuzumab binds ERBB2 (HER2) receptor causing reversible type II cardiotoxicity (dilated cardiomyopathy, worsened with anthracyclines); infliximab neutralizes TNF-alpha, disrupting granuloma integrity and reactivating latent Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Trastuzumab binds VEGF-A causing hypertension and bowel perforation; infliximab binds CD20 causing progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy","Trastuzumab binds RANKL causing osteonecrosis of jaw; infliximab binds IgE causing anaphylaxis","Trastuzumab binds EGFR causing acneiform rash; infliximab inhibits calcineurin causing nephrotoxicity"],"a":0,"e":"Trastuzumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting the extracellular domain of HER2 (ERBB2) overexpressed in ~20% of breast cancers and some gastric cancers. Because cardiomyocytes rely on neuregulin-HER2/HER4 survival signaling, trastuzumab causes non-dose-dependent, usually reversible dilated cardiomyopathy/heart failure (synergistically worsened when combined concurrently with doxorubicin). Infliximab (chimeric anti-TNF-alpha mAb) and adalimumab neutralize TNF-alpha; because TNF-alpha is critical to maintain macrophage granulomas containing dormant M. tuberculosis, screening (TST/IGRA and chest X-ray) is mandatory before initiating anti-TNF biologics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02815","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors","q":"A 60-year-old man with metastatic melanoma treated with ipilimumab plus nivolumab develops severe watery bloody diarrhea (colitis), fatigue with low ACTH and low cortisol (hypophysitis), and elevated TSH. What immune checkpoints do these two monoclonal antibodies block, and what is the primary medical treatment for moderate-to-severe immune-related adverse events (irAEs)?","o":["Ipilimumab blocks CTLA-4 (enhancing T-cell priming by APC B7-CD28 costimulation in lymph nodes) and nivolumab blocks PD-1 (restoring effector T-cell killing in peripheral tumors); treat severe irAEs by holding checkpoint therapy and initiating systemic corticosteroids (e.g., prednisone/methylprednisolone)","Ipilimumab blocks CD28 and nivolumab blocks MHC class I; treat irAEs with recombinant IL-2","Ipilimumab blocks PD-L1 and nivolumab blocks CD20; treat irAEs with naloxone","Both drugs are cytotoxic microtubule poisons causing neutropenic colitis treated with broad-spectrum antibiotics alone"],"a":0,"e":"T-cell activation requires TCR recognition plus B7-CD28 costimulation; CTLA-4 outcompetes CD28 to brake early T-cell priming in lymph nodes, whereas tumor PD-L1 engages T-cell PD-1 to induce peripheral T-cell exhaustion. Blocking CTLA-4 (ipilimumab) or PD-1/PD-L1 (nivolumab, pembrolizumab, atezolizumab) unleashes potent antitumor T-cell immunity, but removes self-tolerance—precipitating autoimmune immune-related adverse events (irAEs: dermatitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis, hypophysitis, pneumonitis, myocarditis). Grade 2-4 irAEs require withholding immunotherapy and prompt systemic immunosuppression with corticosteroids (plus hormone replacement for endocrinopathies, or infliximab for steroid-refractory colitis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02816","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Targeted Small-Molecule Inhibitors","q":"Which pairing correctly matches a targeted non-antibody anticancer agent with its molecular target and signature clinical toxicity or indication?","o":["Venetoclax: ALK fusion inhibitor; Imatinib: 26S proteasome inhibitor","Bortezomib: reversible 26S proteasome inhibitor used in multiple myeloma (causing peripheral neuropathy and herpes zoster reactivation); Venetoclax: selective BCL-2 inhibitor inducing rapid apoptosis/tumor lysis syndrome in CLL","Osimertinib: BRAF V600E inhibitor causing squamous keratoacanthomas; Vemurafenib: ABL1 kinase inhibitor","Imatinib: EGFR T790M inhibitor causing severe diarrhea; Bortezomib: dihydrofolate reductase inhibitor"],"a":1,"e":"Bortezomib (and carfilzomib) inhibit the chymotrypsin-like activity of the 26S proteasome, blocking degradation of misfolded proteins and I-kappaB (suppressing NF-kappaB survival signaling) in immunoglobulin-producing multiple myeloma plasma cells—classically causing peripheral sensory neuropathy and varicella-zoster reactivation (requiring acyclovir prophylaxis). Venetoclax is an oral BH3-mimetic that selectively inhibits anti-apoptotic BCL-2 in CLL and AML, triggering brisk intrinsic mitochondrial apoptosis with high risk of tumor lysis syndrome (requiring ramp-up dosing, hydration, and allopurinol/rasburicase).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02817","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Acetaminophen (Paracetamol) Overdose","q":"A 23-year-old woman presents 6 hours after ingesting 18 grams of paracetamol (acetaminophen) in a self-harm attempt, with a 4-hour serum paracetamol level plotting above the Rumack-Matthew treatment line. Why does massive overdose cause centrilobular (Zone 3) hepatic necrosis, and how does N-acetylcysteine (NAC) prevent liver failure?","o":["Unchanged paracetamol precipitates in bile canaliculi; NAC alkalinizes urine to enhance renal excretion","CYP3A4 converts paracetamol to formic acid; NAC inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase","Saturation of glucuronidation and sulfation shunts paracetamol through hepatic CYP2E1 to form toxic NAPQI, depleting hepatic glutathione; NAC provides cysteine precursors to replenish glutathione and directly conjugate NAPQI","Paracetamol irreversibly acetylates hepatic COX-2; NAC stimulates hepatic regeneration via insulin receptors"],"a":2,"e":"At therapeutic doses, \u003e90% of paracetamol undergoes hepatic Phase II conjugation (glucuronidation and sulfation) to non-toxic metabolites, while \u003c10% is oxidized by CYP2E1 to reactive N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI), which is rapidly detoxified by glutathione (GSH) into mercapturic acid. In overdose, Phase II pathways saturate and Zone 3 CYP2E1 generates excess NAPQI; once hepatic GSH stores fall below ~30%, NAPQI covalently binds hepatocyte sulfhydryl proteins, causing centrilobular necrosis. N-acetylcysteine (NAC, most effective within 8-10 hours) donates cysteine to rapidly restore GSH, directly reduces/detoxifies NAPQI, and supports sulfation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02818","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Heavy Metal and Iron Antidotes","q":"A 3-year-old boy accidentally swallows ~20 adult ferrous sulfate tablets, developing hemorrhagic vomiting, metabolic acidosis, radiopaque gastric pills on X-ray, and shock. Which parenteral antidote specifically chelates free ferric (Fe3+) iron into water-soluble ferrioxamine (turning urine pink/vin-rose), and which oral chelator is preferred for chronic copper overload in Wilson disease?","o":["Intramuscular dimercaprol (BAL) for acute iron toxicity; Prussian blue for Wilson disease","Oral D-penicillamine for acute iron toxicity; deferoxamine for Wilson disease","Intravenous CaNa2EDTA for acute iron toxicity; fomepizole for Wilson disease","Intravenous deferoxamine for acute iron toxicity; D-penicillamine (or trientine) for Wilson disease"],"a":3,"e":"Deferoxamine (parenteral; oral deferasirox/deferiprone are used for chronic transfusional hemosiderosis) binds free plasma ferric iron (Fe3+)—sparing hemoglobin/cytochrome iron—to form water-soluble ferrioxamine excreted by the kidneys (giving characteristic pink/orange \u0027vin-rose\u0027 urine). For Wilson disease (ATP7B copper accumulation), D-penicillamine or trientine (plus oral zinc to block intestinal copper uptake via metallothionein) promotes urinary copper excretion. Dimercaprol (BAL) chelates arsenic/mercury/gold (and severe lead encephalopathy with EDTA), succimer (DMSA) treats mild-moderate lead/arsenic/mercury orally, and Prussian blue traps radioactive cesium and thallium in the gut.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02819","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Tricyclic Antidepressant (TCA) Overdose","q":"Assertion (A): Intravenous hypertonic sodium bicarbonate is the cornerstone emergency antidote for tricyclic antidepressant (amitriptyline) overdose manifesting with QRS prolongation (\u003e100 ms), terminal R wave in lead aVR, or ventricular arrhythmias. Reason (R): Hypertonic sodium bicarbonate overcomes TCA blockade of cardiac fast voltage-gated Na+ channels by supplying a high extracellular Na+ concentration gradient while serum alkalinization increases plasma protein binding and reduces the active unionized fraction of the weak-base TCA.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Tricyclic antidepressants (amitriptyline, imipramine, nortriptyline) cause lethal overdose via three mechanisms: (1) muscarinic blockade (delirium, mydriasis, dry flushed skin, urinary retention), (2) alpha-1 adrenergic blockade (refractory hypotension), and (3) Class IA-like inhibition of cardiac fast voltage-gated Na+ channels (widening QRS \u003e100 ms, deep S in I/aVL, tall terminal R in aVR, and ventricular tachycardia/fibrillation) plus GABA-A blockade (seizures). IV sodium bicarbonate combats cardiotoxicity via two mechanisms: increasing extracellular Na+ to outcompete TCA Na+-channel blockade, and raising blood pH (7.45-7.55) to shift the drug equilibrium and enhance albumin binding, lowering free cardiotoxic drug (importantly, physostigmine is CONTRAINDICATED in TCA overdose due to asystole risk).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02820","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Beta-Blocker and Calcium Channel Blocker Overdose","q":"A patient intentionally overdoses on metoprolol (a beta-1 blocker), presenting with heart rate 34/min and BP 68/40 mmHg refractory to atropine and IV fluids. Intravenous glucagon restores chronotropy and contractility. How does glucagon bypass beta-1 receptor blockade inside cardiac myocytes, and what additional antidote improves myocardial glucose uptake and contractility in severe calcium-channel blocker (verapamil) toxicity?","o":["Glucagon binds separate cardiac Gs-coupled glucagon receptors to activate adenylyl cyclase and raise intracellular cAMP independently of beta-1 receptors; high-dose insulin-euglycemia therapy (HIET) plus IV calcium gluconate/chloride for CCB toxicity","Glucagon directly opens beta-1 adrenergic receptor ion channels; naloxone for CCB toxicity","Glucagon inhibits cardiac phosphodiesterase-5; protamine sulfate for CCB toxicity","Glucagon blocks muscarinic M2 receptors on the SA node; flumazenil for CCB toxicity"],"a":0,"e":"In severe beta-blocker overdose, beta-1 receptors are competitively occupied so catecholamines struggle to raise cAMP. Glucagon activates its own distinct myocardial Gs-protein-coupled glucagon receptor, stimulating adenylyl cyclase -\u003e cAMP -\u003e PKA -\u003e L-type Ca2+ channel phosphorylation to restore heart rate and inotropy (often alongside high-dose insulin-euglycemia therapy). In severe calcium channel blocker (verapamil/diltiazem/amlodipine) overdose—which blocks L-type Ca2+ channels and impairs pancreatic beta-cell insulin release while causing shock/hyperglycemia—IV calcium salts provide extracellular Ca2+ drive and high-dose insulin (with dextrose) restores myocardial carbohydrate metabolism and inotropy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02821","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Quantal Dose-Response Curves","q":"In preclinical quantal dose-response testing of two investigational anticonvulsants, Drug A has a median effective dose (ED50) of 25 mg/kg and a median toxic dose (TD50) of 500 mg/kg, whereas Drug B has an ED50 of 40 mg/kg and a TD50 of 120 mg/kg. What are the Therapeutic Indices (TI) of Drug A and Drug B, and which drug has the wider margin of safety?","o":["Drug A TI = 0.05; Drug B TI = 0.33; Drug B has the wider safety margin","Drug A TI = 20; Drug B TI = 3; Drug A has the wider safety margin","Drug A TI = 475; Drug B TI = 80; Drug A has the wider safety margin","Drug A TI = 12.5; Drug B TI = 4.8; Drug A has the wider safety margin"],"a":1,"e":"The Therapeutic Index (TI) in quantal population dose-response studies is defined as the ratio of median toxic (or lethal) dose to median effective dose: TI = TD50 / ED50 (or LD50 / ED50 in animal lethality assays). For Drug A, TI = 500 / 25 = 20. For Drug B, TI = 120 / 40 = 3. A higher Therapeutic Index indicates a wider separation between therapeutic and toxic doses (like Drug A vs narrow-TI drugs such as digoxin, lithium, warfarin, or theophylline).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02822","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacogenomics and HLA-Linked Hypersensitivity","q":"Which pairing accurately matches a pharmacogenomic biomarker to the drug and life-threatening toxicity it predicts?","o":["NAT2 fast acetylator -\u003e isoniazid-induced systemic lupus; VKORC1 mutation -\u003e succinylcholine apnea","CYP2C19 poor metabolizer -\u003e enhanced clopidogrel antiplatelet efficacy; HLA-B*57:01 -\u003e warfarin bleeding","TPMT or NUDT15 deficiency -\u003e severe pancytopenia with azathioprine/6-mercaptopurine; HLA-B*57:01 -\u003e abacavir hypersensitivity syndrome; HLA-B*15:02 -\u003e Stevens-Johnson syndrome/TEN with carbamazepine (especially in East/South Asian ancestry)","G6PD deficiency -\u003e malignant hyperthermia with halothane; RYR1 mutation -\u003e primaquine hemolysis"],"a":2,"e":"Key clinically actionable pharmacogenomic tests include: (1) TPMT and NUDT15 (especially prevalent in Asian populations) variants impairing inactivation of thiopurines (azathioprine, 6-MP), risking profound bone marrow suppression; (2) HLA-B*57:01 mandatory screening prior to abacavir to prevent fatal systemic hypersensitivity; and (3) HLA-B*15:02 screening in Asian ancestry patients before carbamazepine/oxcarbazepine/phenytoin to prevent Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis (SJS/TEN).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02823","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Enteric Fever (Salmonella Typhi)","q":"A 19-year-old college student presents on Day 4 of high step-ladder fever, headache, relative bradycardia, and coated tongue before starting antibiotics. Which specimen provides the highest diagnostic yield in the first week of typhoid fever, how does the Widal test distinguish somatic O from flagellar H agglutination, and which antigen test screens chronic gallbladder carriers?","o":["Stool culture (~95% sensitive in Day 1-3); Widal H antibody appears first and disappears fastest; VDRL screens carriers","Throat swab in Week 1; Widal O forms fluffy clumps; Paul-Bunnell test screens carriers","Urine culture in Week 1; Widal detects IgM solely against Vi antigen; Weil-Felix screens carriers","Blood culture (~90% sensitive in Week 1, whereas stool/urine cultures peak in Weeks 2-3); Widal somatic O forms compact granular chalky clumps indicating recent/active infection while flagellar H forms loose fluffy cotton-wool clumps; Vi (capsular) antibody test screens carriers"],"a":3,"e":"Following Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi invasion across M cells/Peyer patches and reticuloendothelial multiplication, primary/secondary bacteremia peaks in Week 1—so blood culture (or bone marrow culture if already given antibiotics) gives the highest yield (~80-90%) in Week 1, while stool and urine cultures peak in Weeks 2-3 (mnemonic BASU: Blood 1st, Antibodies Widal 2nd, Stool 3rd, Urine 4th). On the Widal tube agglutination test (rising from Day 7-10), somatic TO antibodies appear earlier, decline faster, and form compact granular disc deposits, whereas flagellar TH/AH/BH antibodies persist longer after vaccination/past infection and form loose cotton-wool floccules. Anti-Vi antibody screening identifies asymptomatic chronic gallbladder carriers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02824","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Vibrio cholerae","q":"A patient with painless profuse \u0027rice-water\u0027 diarrhea and severe isotonic dehydration has a hanging-drop stool preparation showing rapid \u0027shooting-star\u0027 (darting) motility and oxidase-positive curved Gram-negative rods that form flat yellow colonies on TCBS agar. How does the A-B cholera enterotoxin produce massive secretory diarrhea?","o":["B subunit binds GM1 ganglioside on enterocytes; A1 subunit irreversibly ADP-ribosylates Gs-alpha, constitutively activating adenylyl cyclase -\u003e high cAMP -\u003e opening CFTR Cl- channels and inhibiting Na+/Cl- absorption","B subunit binds Gb3 globotriaosylceramide; A subunit cleaves 28S rRNA to halt enterocyte protein synthesis","Cytotoxin depolymerizes Rho-GTPase actin cytoskeleton causing pseudomembranes","Heat-stable STa toxin activates guanylyl cyclase -\u003e high cGMP"],"a":0,"e":"Vibrio cholerae is a halophilic, oxidase-positive, comma-shaped Gram-negative bacillus with a single polar flagellum (\u0027darting\u0027 or \u0027shooting-star\u0027 motility halted by specific antisera). It ferments sucrose to produce diagnostic yellow colonies on Thiosulfate-Citrate-Bile-Salts-Sucrose (TCBS) agar. Its phage-encoded AB5 cholera enterotoxin (choleragen) binds enterocyte GM1 ganglioside receptors via five B subunits; the internalized A1 catalytic subunit ADP-ribosylates Gs-alpha (inhibiting intrinsic GTPase), locking adenylyl cyclase active to elevate cAMP—driving massive CFTR Cl- secretion and osmotic Na+/water efflux without mucosal invasion or inflammation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02825","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Syphilis (Treponema pallidum)","q":"A 26-year-old man presents with a single painless, indurated genital ulcer (hard chancre) and non-tender inguinal lymphadenopathy. Dark-field microscopy shows slender corkscrew spirochetes with flexion-extension motility; RPR/VDRL is reactive at 1:32 and TPHA is positive. Six hours after receiving a single dose of IM benzathine penicillin G, he develops fever, rigors, myalgias, and transient intensification of rash. How is VDRL/RPR used after treatment, and what caused his acute post-penicillin febrile reaction?","o":["TPHA gives false positives in pregnancy and SLE while VDRL never gives biological false positives; reaction is Type III serum sickness","VDRL/RPR titers fall \u003e=4-fold within 6-12 months to monitor treatment response/reinfection (whereas TPHA remains lifelong positive); acute febrile episode is the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction caused by massive release of treponemal lipoproteins/pyrogens from lysed spirochetes (managed supportively with antipyretics, NOT penicillin allergy)","TPHA titers monitor cure while VDRL remains permanently positive; reaction is Type I IgE anaphylaxis requiring epinephrine","VDRL detects live treponemes directly in serum; reaction is Red Man syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Non-treponemal flocculation tests (VDRL, RPR) detect heterophile anti-cardiolipin/reagin antibodies: they can yield biological false positives (pregnancy, SLE/antiphospholipid, malaria, leprosy) and prozone false negatives in high-titer secondary syphilis, but quantitative VDRL/RPR titers correlate with disease activity and drop \u003e=4-fold after effective therapy. Specific treponemal tests (TPHA, TPPA, FTA-ABS) confirm infection and usually remain reactive for life. Within hours of penicillin treatment of early syphilis, rapid spirochetal lysis releases pyrogenic lipoproteins triggering TNF-alpha/IL-6/IL-8 surges—the self-limited Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction (treated with antipyretics/hydration, never mislabeled as penicillin allergy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02826","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Genital Ulcer Syndromes","q":"Patient 1 presents with multiple painful, soft, non-indurated bleeding genital ulcers and painful suppurative inguinal buboes; Gram stain shows pleomorphic Gram-negative coccobacilli in parallel \u0027school-of-fish\u0027 chains. Patient 2 presents with a painless beefy-red friable genital ulcer that bleeds easily without significant inguinal lymphadenopathy; Giemsa-stained tissue crush smear shows macrophages filled with bipolar \u0027safety-pin\u0027 intracellular bacilli (Donovan bodies). Identify the two causative pathogens.","o":["Patient 1: Herpes simplex virus-2; Patient 2: Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Patient 1: Chlamydia trachomatis L1-L3 (LGV); Patient 2: Treponema pallidum","Patient 1: Haemophilus ducreyi (chancroid); Patient 2: Klebsiella granulomatis (granuloma inguinale / donovanosis)","Patient 1: Klebsiella granulomatis; Patient 2: Haemophilus ducreyi"],"a":2,"e":"Genital ulcer syndromes are cleanly differentiated by pain, induration, adenopathy, and microscopy: (1) Chancroid (Haemophilus ducreyi, requiring factor X/hemin) produces painful, soft, undermined purulent ulcers with painful suppurative inguinal buboes and Gram-negative coccobacilli arranged in parallel \u0027school of fish\u0027 / \u0027railroad track\u0027 chains (treated with single-dose azithromycin or ceftriaxone); (2) Donovanosis / Granuloma inguinale (Klebsiella granulomatis) causes painless, beefy-red granulomatous ulcers without true regional buboes (\u0027pseudobuboes\u0027) and pathognomonic bipolar-staining \u0027safety-pin\u0027 Donovan bodies inside macrophages on Wright/Giemsa smear (treated with azithromycin/doxycycline).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02827","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Urethritis and Cervicitis","q":"A 24-year-old man presents with purulent urethral discharge showing Gram-negative kidney-shaped diplococci inside polymorphonuclear neutrophils, growing on Modified Thayer-Martin medium (containing vancomycin, colistin, nystatin, and trimethoprim) as oxidase-positive, glucose-fermenting (maltose-negative) colonies. Why is syndromic/empiric treatment routinely paired with doxycycline (or azithromycin) alongside ceftriaxone, and what developmental stage of Chlamydia trachomatis initiates infection?","o":["N. gonorrhoeae ferments sucrose and maltose equally; ceftriaxone treats Chlamydia better than azithromycin","Thayer-Martin selects for Chlamydia inclusion bodies; elementary body divides inside blood agar","Vancomycin in Thayer-Martin kills Chlamydia; Chlamydia reticulate body is the extracellular infectious spore","High rate of Chlamydia trachomatis co-infection (obligate intracellular bacterium lacking muramic acid peptidoglycan, resistant to beta-lactams); Chlamydia elementary body (metabolically inert infectious form) enters mucosal cells and converts to reticulate body"],"a":3,"e":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae is an oxidase-positive Gram-negative diplococcus that ferments glucose ONLY (unlike N. meningitidis, which ferments glucose and maltose) and is isolated from contaminated mucosal sites on Modified Thayer-Martin agar (vancomycin inhibits Gram-positives, colistin inhibits other Gram-negatives, nystatin inhibits yeast, trimethoprim suppresses Proteus swarming). Because up to 30-50% of gonorrhea patients harbor co-infection with Chlamydia trachomatis (serovars D-K)—an obligate intracellular organism lacking classical muramic acid cell-wall peptidoglycan and therefore resistant to ceftriaxone—dual therapy covers both. Chlamydia infects via the rigid, metabolically inert extracellular elementary body (EB), which transforms into the replicating intracellular reticulate body (RB) inside phagosomes (forming glycogen-rich inclusion bodies).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02828","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Brucellosis (Undulant Fever)","q":"A 40-year-old veterinarian/dairy worker who regularly consumes raw unpasteurized goat milk presents with 4 weeks of drenching sweats, undulating evening fever, sacroiliac joint pain, and hepatosplenomegaly. Bone marrow/blood inoculated into a Castaneda biphasic bottle yields tiny, slow-growing, non-motile, catalase/oxidase/urease-positive Gram-negative coccobacilli. Which organism is most likely, and what is the standard combination antibiotic regimen?","o":["Brucella melitensis; oral doxycycline for 6 weeks combined with streptomycin/gentamicin (for 2-3 weeks) or rifampicin (for 6 weeks)","Francisella tularensis; penicillin G monotherapy for 5 days","Yersinia pestis; cephalexin monotherapy","Coxiella burnetii; oral metronidazole"],"a":0,"e":"Brucellosis (Malta/Mediterranean/undulant fever) is an important Indian zoonosis caused most commonly in humans by Brucella melitensis (sheep/goats, unpasteurized dairy) or B. abortus (cattle). These small aerobic Gram-negative coccobacilli survive inside macrophages, require prolonged incubation (up to 4 weeks) in Castaneda biphasic blood culture medium (agar slant + broth in one bottle to avoid dangerous subculture aerosols in the lab), and are screened by Rose Bengal card test / Standard Agglutination Test (SAT; \u003e=1:160, using prozone dilution and 2-mercaptoethanol for IgG). Because monotherapy causes high relapse rates, treatment requires 6 weeks of doxycycline plus an aminoglycoside (streptomycin/gentamicin) or rifampicin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02829","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Anthrax (Bacillus anthracis)","q":"A wool-sorter develops a painless blackened necrotic cutaneous eschar surrounded by non-pitting edema (\u0027malignant pustule\u0027). Polychrome methylene blue staining of exudate shows chains of large rectangular (\u0027bamboo-stick\u0027) Gram-positive bacilli surrounded by a pink amorphous halo (McFadyean reaction), and blood agar grows non-hemolytic \u0027Medusa-head\u0027 frosted-glass colonies. What uniquely composes this organism\u0027s capsule, and what appearance does it produce on gelatin stab culture?","o":["Chitin-glucan polysaccharide capsule; beta-hemolytic mucoid colonies","Poly-D-glutamic acid polypeptide capsule (unlike polysaccharide capsules of most bacteria); \u0027inverted fir-tree\u0027 growth in gelatin stab","Mycolic acid cord factor; \u0027fried-egg\u0027 colonies on PPLO agar","Hyaluronic acid capsule; \u0027string-of-pearls\u0027 exclusively without penicillin"],"a":1,"e":"Bacillus anthracis is an aerobic, non-motile, spore-forming large Gram-positive bacillus with square-cut ends (\u0027boxcar\u0027/\u0027bamboo-stick\u0027 chains). Uniquely among pathogenic bacteria, its antiphagocytic capsule is composed of a polypeptide—poly-gamma-D-glutamic acid (encoded on plasmid pXO2, visualized as a purplish-pink McFadyean capsule on polychrome methylene blue), while plasmid pXO1 encodes edema factor (calmodulin-dependent adenylyl cyclase) and lethal factor (metalloprotease cleaving MAPK kinases). On nutrient agar it forms comma-like outgrowths (\u0027Medusa-head\u0027 colonies), shows \u0027string-of-pearls\u0027 swelling with low-dose penicillin, and liquefies gelatin slowly in an \u0027inverted fir-tree\u0027 pattern.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02830","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Leptospirosis (Weil Disease)","q":"Assertion (A): During monsoon flooding in India, wading through stagnant water contaminated with rodent urine predisposes individuals to severe icterohemorrhagic leptospirosis (Weil disease: fever, conjunctival suffusion, calf tenderness, jaundice, and acute kidney injury). Reason (R): Tightly coiled, hooked-end Leptospira interrogans spirochetes penetrate abraded skin or conjunctival mucosa, disseminate hematogenously to cause systemic vasculitis/endothelial injury in liver and kidney, and are confirmed serologically by the gold-standard Microscopic Agglutination Test (MAT) or IgM ELISA.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Leptospirosis is a major monsoon-associated zoonosis in coastal and agricultural regions of India. Rodents (and cattle/dogs) shed Leptospira interrogans in alkaline urine into flooded soil and water. Motile, tightly coiled spirochetes with characteristic umbrella-handle hooked ends penetrate waterlogged/abraded skin or mucous membranes, producing a biphasic illness culminating in Weil disease (conjunctival suffusion, severe calf myalgia, cholestatic jaundice with modest transaminases, thrombocytopenia, pulmonary hemorrhage, and acute kidney injury). Blood/CSF cultures in EMJH medium are positive in Week 1, urine cultures in Week 2+, and serologic confirmation uses IgM ELISA and the serovar-specific Microscopic Agglutination Test (MAT).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02831","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Personal Identification Methods","q":"Which forensic identification method relies on dermal papillae ridge patterns that appear around the 16th week of intrauterine life, remain unchanged throughout life, and cannot be permanently obliterated unless the dermis (\u003e1-2 mm depth) is destroyed; and which six regressive tooth changes form Gustafson\u0027s method for adult age estimation?","o":["Rugoscopy; Krogman cranial suture closure","Poroscopy only; Boyd-Hixon third-molar calcification only","Dactylography (Galton system: loops most common ~60-65%, whorls ~30-35%, arches ~5%, composite); Gustafson\u0027s 6 dental criteria: Attrition, Periodontosis, Secondary dentin, Cementum apposition, Root resorption, and Root transparency","Bertillonage anthropometry; Schour-Massler deciduous eruption chart"],"a":2,"e":"Dactylography (Henry-Galton fingerprint system) is the most reliable biometric personal identifier (even identical monozygotic twins have distinct fingerprints). Epidermal ridges reflect underlying dermal papillae patterns established in utero (12-16 weeks) and recover identically after superficial epidermal injury—only deep burns/scars or leprosy destroying the true dermis alter them (criminal attempts to abrade fingertips fail unless dermis is destroyed). After age 25, when epiphyseal union is complete, Gustafson\u0027s microscopic/macroscopic tooth-section method scores 6 age-related changes (0-3 each): Attrition, Periodontosis, Secondary dentin, Cementum apposition, Root resorption, and Root translucency/transparency (most reliable single criterion).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02832","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Skeletal Sex Determination and Stature Estimation","q":"A skeletonized femur recovered from an excavation measures 45.0 cm in maximum length, and the accompanying pelvis exhibits a narrow V-shaped subpubic angle (~65 degrees), deep narrow greater sciatic notch, absent preauricular sulcus, and heart-shaped pelvic inlet. Using the Trotter-Gleser adult male femur stature equation [Stature (cm) = 2.38 x Femur length (cm) + 61.41], what are the estimated sex and living stature?","o":["Male; approximately 145.0 cm","Female; approximately 168.5 cm","Female; approximately 176.2 cm","Male; approximately 168.5 cm"],"a":3,"e":"The pelvis is the single most accurate bone for forensic sex determination (~95% accuracy): a narrow V-shaped subpubic angle (\u003c70-75 degrees), narrow greater sciatic notch, absent preauricular sulcus, heart-shaped inlet, and taller/narrower sacrum indicate an adult MALE (whereas a female pelvis shows a wide U-shaped subpubic angle \u003e90 degrees, wide shallow sciatic notch, prominent preauricular sulcus, wider circular/oval inlet, and shorter wider sacrum). Applying the regression formula: Stature = (2.38 x 45.0) + 61.41 = 107.10 + 61.41 = 168.51 cm (~168.5 cm).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02833","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Smooth-Bore Shotgun Ballistics","q":"During autopsy of a victim shot with a smooth-bore 12-gauge shotgun, the chest wound shows a single large central circular defect (~4 cm diameter) with scalloped (\u0027rat-hole\u0027) margins surrounded by a few individual satellite pellet holes and plastic wad/cardboard cup recovered deep inside the thoracic cavity. Which firing distance range does this pattern signify, and what is the purpose of \u0027choking\u0027 a shotgun barrel?","o":["Close/near-contact to ~1-2 meters (\u0027rat-hole\u0027 transition zone where pellets begin dispersing slightly and wad enters the wound); choking constricts the terminal muzzle bore to reduce pellet spread and increase effective range","Distant range (\u003e10 meters); choking widens the muzzle to maximize immediate close-range spread","Rifled sniper bullet wound at 500 meters; choking imparts spin stabilization via helical grooves","Contact wound with intact outer-table skull beveling; choking eliminates gunpowder gases"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike rifled firearms (which have helical lands and grooves imparting gyroscopic spin to a single bullet), smooth-bore shotguns fire multiple lead pellets enclosed with a wad. At contact/very close range (\u003c0.5-1 m), pellets travel as a single compact mass (\u0027billiard-ball\u0027 effect) creating one round defect plus wad entry. Around ~1-2 meters (transition zone), the pellet column begins separating at its periphery, creating a central \u0027rat-hole\u0027 wound with scalloped margins and a few satellite pellet holes alongside the wad penetrating the wound. Beyond ~2-3 m, the wad drops away and individual pellet holes scatter widely (approximate spread in inches ~ distance in yards for cylinder-bore, reduced by terminal muzzle narrowing called \u0027choking\u0027).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02834","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Antemortem vs Postmortem Burns","q":"Assertion (A): Finding a \u0027pugilistic attitude\u0027 (boxer-like flexion of all limbs and spine) on a charred corpse recovered from a house fire does NOT prove that the victim was alive during the fire, whereas finding soot mixed with mucus deep below the vocal cords in the bronchi and blood carboxyhemoglobin \u003e10% strongly proves antemortem inhalation during the fire. Reason (R): Pugilistic attitude is a purely physicochemical postmortem/thermal heat-stiffening artifact caused by coagulation and shortening of muscle proteins (flexors overpowering extensors), regardless of whether death preceded the fire, whereas deep airway soot and carboxyhemoglobin require active respiration and circulation.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"A classic forensic trap is mistaking the \u0027pugilistic\u0027 (fencing/boxer) posture—or thermal extradural heat hematomas—for antemortem defense or trauma. Intense heat (\u003e65°C) denatures and coagulates skeletal muscle proteins, shrinking stronger flexor groups more than extensors; this happens equally in corpses burned after death and in fire victims. Conversely, proving the victim was alive when the fire started requires vital signs: carbon soot embedded in mucoid secretions deep in the trachea/bronchi below the glottis, elevated blood carboxyhemoglobin (\u003e10%, often 40-70%), and vital inflammatory erythema/protein-rich vesicles with a red base at burn margins.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02835","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Electrocution, Lightning, and Hypothermia","q":"At medicolegal autopsies of three environmental fatalities, Case 1 shows a firm pale crateriform skin lesion with raised edges and metallic copper/iron deposits at the point of contact with a 240V live wire; Case 2 shows transient reddish-brown fern-like branching skin markings on the shoulder after an outdoor thunderstorm; and Case 3 shows multiple pinpoint dark-brown hemorrhagic erosions in the gastric mucosa (\u0027Wischnewsky spots\u0027) in a homeless man found unclothed outdoors in sub-zero winter. Identify the three characteristic findings.","o":["Case 1: Flash burn; Case 2: Tardieu spots; Case 3: Curling ulcer","Case 1: Joule burn (endogenous electrical mark with metallization); Case 2: Lichtenberg / arborescent filigree figures (lightning); Case 3: Wischnewsky spots with paradoxical undressing (fatal hypothermia)","Case 1: Lichtenberg figure; Case 2: Joule burn; Case 3: Mallory-Weiss tear","Case 1: Contact gunshot abrasion collar; Case 2: Postmortem marbling; Case 3: Dieulafoy lesion"],"a":1,"e":"Each environmental death leaves signature forensic lesions: (1) Low-voltage domestic electrocution generates heat by tissue electrical resistance (Joule\u0027s law), forming a specific \u0027Joule burn\u0027 (electrical entry mark) with a collapsed pale center, raised hyperemic rim, honeycomb epidermal vacuolation, and microscopic electrode metallization; (2) Lightning strikes produce transient reddish-brown fern-leaf branching Lichtenberg figures (filigree burns) due to superficial capillary/RBC extravasation along skin moisture/static discharge lines; (3) Fatal hypothermia causes hemorrhagic gastric mucosal erosions (Wischnewsky spots) from cold stress and splanchnic microthrombosis, often accompanied by \u0027paradoxical undressing\u0027 and \u0027hide-and-die\u0027 behavior.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02836","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Seminal Stain Examination and Locard Principle","q":"During forensic laboratory examination of clothing swabs from a sexual assault case (guided by Locard\u0027s Exchange Principle), which test sequence accurately separates presumptive screening, microcrystal tests, and definitive confirmation of human semen—even in an azoospermic or vasectomized assailant?","o":["Presumptive: Lugol\u0027s iodine glycogen test alone; Confirmatory: Barberio crystal test even in animal semen","Presumptive: Takayama hemochromogen test; Microcrystal: Teichmann hemin crystals; Confirmatory: Kastle-Meyer phenolphthalein","Presumptive: Wood\u0027s lamp UV fluorescence and prostatic acid phosphatase (Brentamine fast blue); Microcrystal: Florence test (potassium tri-iodide forming dark-brown rhombic choline iodide crystals) and Barberio test (picric acid forming yellow needle-shaped spermine picrate crystals); Confirmatory: microscopic spermatozoa or human prostate-specific antigen (PSA / p30) plus STR DNA profiling","Presumptive: Ferric chloride test; Confirmatory: Florence test (specific exclusively for human spermatozoa)"],"a":2,"e":"Under Locard\u0027s Exchange Principle (\u0027every contact leaves a trace\u0027), seminal stains fluoresce bluish-white under filtered UV (Wood\u0027s lamp) and show high prostatic acid phosphatase activity (presumptive). Classic crystal tests include the Florence test (potassium iodide + iodine reacting with seminal choline to form dark-brown rhombic crystals) and Barberio test (saturated aqueous/alcoholic picric acid reacting with prostatic spermine to form yellow needle-shaped crystals—negative in animals, useful even in aspermia, though still presumptive). Definitive confirmation requires identifying intact spermatozoa microscopically OR detecting human seminal plasma markers PSA (p30)/semenogelin (critical in vasectomized/azoospermic perpetrators), followed by autosomal/Y-STR DNA profiling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02837","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Live Birth vs Stillbirth Assessment","q":"A newborn\u0027s body is recovered from a municipal drain 36 hours after delivery with early greenish abdominal putrefaction. At autopsy, the lungs are pinkish-mottled, crepitant, fill the pleural cavities overlapping the pericardium, float in water (positive hydrostatic test), and show air inside the stomach and duodenum (Breslau second-life test), while histology confirms uniform alveolar expansion without putrefactive gas bullae. Which finding most reliably indicates live birth, and what are the major false-positive causes of the hydrostatic floatation test?","o":["Air in the stomach (Breslau test) occurs exclusively from postmortem decomposition and never swallowing during live birth","Complete absence of ossification center at the lower end of the femur proves live birth","A positive hydrostatic test alone is 100% infallible even in advanced putrefaction; false positives never occur","Microscopic uniform alveolar expansion with flattened epithelium and vital reaction at umbilical cord/airway (confirming respiration); false-positive lung floatation occurs with putrefactive gas formation and artificial mouth-to-mouth/bag-valve resuscitation"],"a":3,"e":"Determining live birth vs stillbirth in alleged infanticide relies primarily on demonstrating postnatal respiration: unrespired fetal lungs are small, dark-red, liver-like, airless, and sink in water, whereas respired lungs expand to fill the thoracic cavity, feel spongy/crepitant, double in weight (Ploucquet test), show air in the stomach/duodenum from swallowing (Breslau second-life test), and float in the hydrostatic test. However, the hydrostatic test has two classic false positives—postmortem putrefaction gases and artificial resuscitation—and one false negative (atelectasis/pneumonia/vaginal crying failure). Therefore, histopathology confirming uniform alveolar duct expansion (distinct from irregular interstitial putrefactive gas cysts) plus vital umbilical stump inflammation/caput succedaneum is essential.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02838","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Systemic Hypertension","q":"A 54-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus and diabetic nephropathy (urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 420 mg/g, serum creatinine 1.3 mg/dL, K+ 4.3 mEq/L) has persistent clinic blood pressure of 156/94 mmHg on two separate visits despite lifestyle modification. Under ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows (STW) and NHSRC Hypertension STG, which first-line antihypertensive class is preferred and which combination must be avoided?","o":["Initiate an ACE inhibitor (e.g., enalapril/ramipril) or ARB (e.g., telmisartan/losartan); avoid combining an ACE inhibitor with an ARB simultaneously","Initiate oral clonidine monotherapy; avoid thiazide-like diuretics","Initiate an alpha-blocker (prazosin) as first-line; avoid calcium channel blockers","Combine enalapril PLUS telmisartan together for maximal antiproteinuric blockade"],"a":0,"e":"Per ICMR STW and NHSRC Hypertension guidelines, patients with diabetes and albuminuria/proteinuric CKD should receive an ACE inhibitor or ARB (plus a CCB or thiazide-like diuretic if two drugs are needed for BP \u003e= 160/100 or uncontrolled BP) to slow nephropathy progression. Dual RAAS blockade (combining an ACEi + ARB) increases hyperkalaemia, hypotension, and AKI without cardiovascular benefit and is contraindicated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02839","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Hypertensive Crisis","q":"A 60-year-old man with poorly controlled hypertension presents with severe headache, confusion, blurred vision, grade IV Keith-Wagener hypertensive retinopathy (papilloedema), and BP 228/134 mmHg (hypertensive encephalopathy). Which blood pressure reduction target and route are safest during the first 1 to 2 hours?","o":["Give IV nitroprusside to achieve systolic BP \u003c 90 mmHg within 30 minutes","Reduce Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) by no more than 20-25% over the first 1-2 hours using a titratable IV agent (e.g., labetalol or nicardipine); avoid sublingual nifedipine","Drop BP abruptly to 110/70 mmHg within 15 minutes using sublingual nifedipine capsules","Administer oral amlodipine 5 mg and discharge home immediately"],"a":1,"e":"Because chronic hypertensives have a rightward-shifted cerebral autoregulation curve, abrupt normalisation of BP in hypertensive encephalopathy causes watershed cerebral ischaemia, stroke, or myocardial infarction. Titratable IV antihypertensives (labetalol, nicardipine, clevidipine) should reduce MAP by at most 20-25% in the first 1-2 hours (to ~160/100 mmHg over 2-6 h). Sublingual nifedipine causes unpredictable precipitous hypotension and is contraindicated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02840","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Diabetic Foot","q":"A 57-year-old man with type 2 diabetes for 14 years presents with a painless, punched-out 2.5 cm plantar ulcer over the first metatarsal head surrounded by thick callus. Pedal pulses are bounding (ABPI 1.05), monofilament sensation is absent, and a sterile blunt metal probe inserted gently into the ulcer base strikes hard, gritty bone (\u0027probe-to-bone\u0027 test positive). Under ICMR/NHSRC Diabetic Foot STG, what does the positive probe-to-bone test indicate, and what is essential alongside antibiotics and debridement?","o":["High likelihood of underlying diabetic foot osteomyelitis (confirm with X-ray/MRI); strict mechanical offloading (e.g., total contact cast/therapeutic footwear)","Purely superficial fungal infection; apply topical clotrimazole and encourage barefoot walking","Critical limb-threatening arterial ischaemia; immediate above-knee amputation","Neuropathic Charcot joint; inject intra-articular triamcinolone"],"a":0,"e":"A painless ulcer over a weight-bearing plantar pressure point with absent 10-g Semmes-Weinstein monofilament sensation and palpable pulses defines a neuropathic diabetic foot ulcer. A positive probe-to-bone test strongly predicts underlying osteomyelitis (requiring imaging and prolonged bone-directed antimicrobial/surgical management). Mechanical offloading (total contact cast or removable walker) plus sharp callus debridement are mandatory for healing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02841","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Snakebite Envenomation","q":"A 34-year-old agricultural worker presents 3 hours after a viper bite on the ankle with progressive local limb swelling, oozing from the fang marks, and mild gingival bleeding. Two millilitres of venous blood placed in a clean dry glass test tube remains completely liquid and unclotted at 20 minutes (positive 20WBCT). According to India\u0027s MoHFW Standard Treatment Guidelines for Snakebite, what is the immediate management?","o":["Immobilise the limb without tight tourniquets and administer polyvalent Anti-Snake Venom (initial 10 vials IV over 1 hour, repeating 20WBCT every 6 hours)","Apply a tight arterial tourniquet above the knee and incise the bite site to suck out venom","Give a small 1-vial intradermal ASV skin test and withhold full-dose ASV for 24 hours","Administer IV tranexamic acid and fresh frozen plasma alone without ASV"],"a":0,"e":"Non-clotting blood on the 20-minute Whole Blood Clotting Test (20WBCT) confirms systemic venom-induced consumption coagulopathy from haemotoxic viperid snakes (Russell\u0027s viper or saw-scaled viper). Under MoHFW India STG, polyvalent ASV (initial 10 vials diluted in isotonic fluid over 1 hour, keeping epinephrine ready for anaphylaxis; NO routine intradermal test dose as it delays treatment and is unreliable) is given immediately, with repeat 20WBCT at 6 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02842","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Snakebite Envenomation","q":"A 22-year-old man sleeping on the floor in rural Maharashtra wakes up with abdominal pain, bilateral ptosis, ophthalmoplegia, dysphagia, and shallow breathing (single-breath count 8) without significant local swelling, suggestive of Common Krait (Bungarus caeruleus) neurotoxic envenomation. Alongside endotracheal intubation/assisted ventilation and polyvalent ASV (10 vials), why is an Atropine-Neostigmine challenge recommended in MoHFW STG for neurotoxic snakebites, and how do Cobra and Krait differ?","o":["Neurotoxic snakebites require 20WBCT positivity before ASV can be administered","Atropine is given alone to reverse ptosis in Krait bites without ventilation","Atropine + Neostigmine reverses post-synaptic nicotinic blockade seen in Cobra bites, whereas Krait causes pre-synaptic nerve-terminal destruction that may not respond and often requires prolonged mechanical ventilation","Neostigmine directly lyses Krait venom in the bloodstream without needing ASV"],"a":2,"e":"Elapid venoms cause descending neuromuscular paralysis (ptosis first, then bulbar/respiratory failure). Cobra neurotoxins block post-synaptic acetylcholine receptors competitively (reversible with IV atropine 0.6 mg followed by neostigmine 1.5 mg IV), whereas Common Krait (beta-bungarotoxin) damages pre-synaptic nerve terminals, often failing neostigmine and requiring mechanical ventilation until terminals regenerate. Because species identification is often uncertain, MoHFW STG recommends an empirical atropine-neostigmine trial in all neurotoxic bites alongside ASV and airway support.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02843","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Neonatal Hyperbilirubinaemia","q":"A 3-day-old term breastfed neonate (3.0 kg) with Rh incompatibility presents with extreme yellow staining of palms and soles, high-pitched shrill cry, poor sucking, lethargy alternating with irritability, and retrocollis/opisthotonos (arching of neck and back). Total serum bilirubin is 28.5 mg/dL (unconjugated 27.2 mg/dL). Under ICMR/NNF Neonatal Jaundice STG, what is the immediate definitive intervention?","o":["Oral phenobarbitone tablets and immediate cessation of breastfeeding permanently","Single-volume exchange transfusion with 5% dextrose","Phototherapy alone for 24 hours before considering exchange transfusion","Immediate double-volume exchange transfusion (160-170 mL/kg) while simultaneously administering intensive phototherapy"],"a":3,"e":"Lethargy, high-pitched cry, poor feeding, hypertonia, and retrocollis/opisthotonos indicate Acute Bilirubin Encephalopathy (kernicterus spectrum). In any neonate exhibiting neurological signs of acute bilirubin encephalopathy, an immediate double-volume exchange transfusion (~160 mL/kg, i.e., 2 x 80 mL/kg blood volume) is an emergency—regardless of exact TSB threshold—with continuous intensive phototherapy before and after the procedure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02844","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Major Trauma Resuscitation (STG)","q":"A 36-year-old pedestrian struck by a truck arrives with BP 76/48 mmHg, HR 132/min, negative eFAST (no haemoperitoneum or haemothorax), clear chest radiograph, and mechanical pelvic instability (\u0027open-book\u0027 pelvic fracture) with scrotal bruising. During the \u0027C\u0027 (Circulation) phase of the ATLS/NHSRC Major Trauma STG, where should a pelvic circumferential compression binder be centred to tamponade pelvic venous bleeding?","o":["Directly over the bilateral greater trochanters of the femora and pubic symphysis","High over the iliac crests at the level of the umbilicus","Around the mid-thighs just above the knee joints","Across the lower costal margin"],"a":0,"e":"Unstable open-book pelvic fractures tear the posterior pelvic venous plexus and bony cancellous surfaces, causing massive retroperitoneal haemorrhage. Applying a pelvic binder (or tied bedsheet) centred precisely at the level of the GREATER TROCHANTERS (and internal rotation/taping of ankles) closes the pelvic ring, reduces pelvic volume, and promotes clot tamponade.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02845","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Acute Rhinosinusitis (STG)","q":"A 28-year-old healthy woman presents with nasal congestion, clear-to-mucoid rhinorrhoea, mild maxillary heaviness, and low-grade fever (37.8 degrees C) for 4 days following a common cold, without worsening symptoms. According to ICMR/NHSRC Acute Sinusitis Standard Treatment Guidelines, what is the appropriate management?","o":["Symptomatic treatment only (normal saline nasal irrigation, steam inhalation, oral analgesics, short-course topical decongestant); NO antibiotics","Immediate 10-day course of oral azithromycin plus levofloxacin","Oral prednisolone 60 mg/day for 14 days","Urgent Functional Endoscopic Sinus Surgery (FESS)"],"a":0,"e":"More than 90% of acute rhinosinusitis cases lasting \u003c10 days are viral. Under ICMR/NHSRC Acute Sinusitis STG, antibiotics are withheld unless criteria for Acute Bacterial Rhinosinusitis (ABRS) are met: (1) persistent symptoms \u003e= 10 days without improvement; (2) severe onset (fever \u003e= 39 C + purulent discharge/facial pain for \u003e= 3-4 consecutive days); or (3) \u0027double sickening\u0027 (worsening after initial viral improvement at day 5-6), in which case amoxicillin or amoxicillin-clavulanate is first-line.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02846","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Osteoarthritis (OA) of Knee (STG)","q":"A 61-year-old obese woman (BMI 32 kg/m2) with peptic ulcer disease and Stage 3 CKD presents with chronic bilateral medial knee pain worse on climbing stairs and \u003c30 minutes of morning stiffness. Standing AP knee radiographs show medial joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and marginal osteophytes. Under ICMR/NHSRC OA Knee STG, what is the foundational first-line management?","o":["Chronic high-dose oral diclofenac + indomethacin combination","Structured weight reduction, quadriceps-strengthening exercises, knee-joint protection, and topical NSAID gel (avoiding chronic oral NSAIDs given CKD and ulcer risk)","Immediate bilateral total knee arthroplasty without conservative trial","Lifelong systemic oral prednisolone 20 mg/day"],"a":1,"e":"Core first-line therapy in all OA Knee guidelines (ICMR STW / NHSRC / OARSI) consists of patient education, weight loss (even 5-10% weight loss markedly lowers joint load), and quadriceps/hip-strengthening exercises. Pharmacologically, topical NSAIDs (diclofenac gel) or paracetamol are preferred before oral NSAIDs—especially critical here because chronic oral NSAIDs risk GI haemorrhage and acute-on-chronic kidney injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02847","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Recurrent Spontaneous Abortion (STG)","q":"A 29-year-old woman with three consecutive unexplained first-trimester spontaneous abortions at 8-10 weeks (normal parental karyotypes, normal TSH, and normal 3D ultrasound/hysteroscopy excluding uterine septum) tests strongly positive for Lupus Anticoagulant and IgG Anticardiolipin antibodies on two separate occasions 12 weeks apart. Under ICMR/NHSRC Recurrent Spontaneous Abortion STG, which antenatal regimen improves live-birth rates?","o":["Oral warfarin throughout the first trimester","Empirical progesterone vaginal pessaries alone without thromboprophylaxis","Low-dose Aspirin (75-150 mg/day) PLUS prophylactic Low-Molecular-Weight Heparin (LMWH) throughout pregnancy and 6 weeks postpartum","High-dose oral prednisolone 60 mg/day alone"],"a":2,"e":"Three consecutive early losses (or \u003e=1 fetal loss \u003e=10 weeks) with persistently positive antiphospholipid antibodies (\u003e=12 weeks apart) establish Obstetric Antiphospholipid Syndrome (APS). Combination therapy with low-dose aspirin (started preconception/positive test) plus prophylactic-dose LMWH/UFH (started upon intrauterine pregnancy confirmation) prevents placental thrombosis and trophoblast apoptosis, raising live-birth rates to \u003e70-80%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02848","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Acute Respiratory Infections (ARI) in Children (STG)","q":"A 10-month-old infant presents with cough and mild fever for 2 days. The child is alert, feeding well, has NO lower chest wall indrawing, NO stridor, and NO general danger signs, while the respiratory rate counted over 1 full minute is 54 breaths/minute. According to MoHFW India ARI / IMNCI Standard Treatment Guidelines, how is this classified and treated?","o":["No pneumonia (cough or cold); give cough suppressant syrup alone","Acute bronchiolitis; immediate oral prednisolone and ribavirin","Severe pneumonia; immediate hospital admission for IV ampicillin plus gentamicin","Pneumonia (fast breathing \u003e= 50/min for age 2-12 months); treat at home with oral Amoxicillin for 5 days and counsel for danger signs/follow-up in 2 days"],"a":3,"e":"Under revised MoHFW IMNCI / ARI guidelines, fast-breathing cutoffs are: \u003e= 60/min (\u003c2 months), \u003e= 50/min (2 to 12 months), and \u003e= 40/min (12 months to 5 years). Fast breathing (54/min in a 10-month-old) OR lower chest wall indrawing WITHOUT general danger signs is classified as \u0027Pneumonia\u0027 and managed outpatient with oral Amoxicillin (40-45 mg/kg/dose twice daily for 5 days) and mandatory 2-day reassessment (whereas danger signs or severe respiratory distress define \u0027Severe Pneumonia\u0027 requiring referral/IV antibiotics).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02849","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Alcohol Dependence and Withdrawal (STG)","q":"A 45-year-old man with chronic heavy alcohol dependence admitted for an orthopaedic fracture develops agitation, coarse tremors, vivid visual hallucinations (\u0027bugs crawling on walls\u0027), disorientation, BP 174/102 mmHg, HR 128/min, and diaphoresis 60 hours after his last drink. Blood glucose is 68 mg/dL. Under NHSRC/NIMHANS Alcohol Dependence STG, what is the critical sequence regarding Thiamine, Glucose, and Pharmacotherapy?","o":["Administer IV Thiamine BEFORE IV dextrose/glucose infusion, and titrate parenteral Benzodiazepines (e.g., diazepam/lorazepam) for Delirium Tremens","Use physical restraints without sedatives and give oral naltrexone","Administer 50% dextrose IV immediately without thiamine, and control agitation with haloperidol monotherapy","Give oral disulfiram 500 mg immediately to induce alcohol aversion"],"a":0,"e":"Delirium tremens (occurring 48-96 hours after alcohol cessation) is a life-threatening medical emergency treated primarily with symptom-triggered/loading parenteral Benzodiazepines (GABA-A agonists: diazepam, or lorazepam if cirrhotic). Because chronic alcoholics have depleted thiamine stores, infusing glucose before thiamine consumes remaining thiamine pyrophosphate in pyruvate dehydrogenase and can acutely precipitate Wernicke encephalopathy; hence parenteral Thiamine (100-500 mg IV) MUST precede or accompany glucose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02850","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Dengue Haemorrhagic Fever (NCVBDC STG)","q":"A 23-year-old man on Day 5 of confirmed Dengue fever develops persistent vomiting, severe abdominal pain, mild hepatomegaly, a haematocrit rise from 40% to 49%, and platelet count of 22,000/mm3, with NO mucosal or active bleeding and stable BP (110/74 mmHg). According to India\u0027s NCVBDC/MoHFW Dengue Guidelines, what is the correct management?","o":["Infuse 5% dextrose rapidly at 20 mL/kg/hour for 6 hours regardless of haematocrit","Admit for closely titrated isotonic IV crystalloid therapy (0.9% NaCl / Ringer\u0027s Lactate guided by serial haematocrit/urine output) and paracetamol; do NOT give prophylactic platelet transfusion","Administer IM diclofenac and oral aspirin for fever and bodyache","Transfuse 6 units of random-donor platelets immediately because platelets are \u003c 30,000/mm3"],"a":1,"e":"Abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, and rising haematocrit (+20%) with falling platelets signify Dengue with Warning Signs (critical plasma leakage phase). Cornerstone therapy is judicious isotonic crystalloid titration (starting ~5-7 mL/kg/h, stepping down to 3-5 then 2-3 mL/kg/h based on hourly urine output and serial haematocrit) to prevent shock without causing fluid overload. Prophylactic platelet transfusion is NOT indicated in non-bleeding patients (even at 10,000-20,000/mm3 unless \u003c10,000/mm3 or active haemorrhage).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02851","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Enteric (Typhoid) Fever","q":"A 20-year-old college student in Delhi presents on Day 5 of continuous step-ladder fever (39.4 degrees C), headache, dull abdominal pain, coated tongue, and relative bradycardia (HR 76/min). What is the diagnostic test of choice in the first week of illness, and which empiric antibiotic is recommended by ICMR Treatment Guidelines for uncomplicated outpatient enteric fever?","o":["Stool culture alone in week 1; oral vancomycin","Bone marrow biopsy as routine outpatient test; oral gentamicin","Blood culture (highest yield in week 1); oral Azithromycin (20 mg/kg/day up to 1 g/day for 7 days; IV Ceftriaxone if hospitalised)","Widal test alone on Day 5; oral ciprofloxacin monotherapy"],"a":2,"e":"During the first week (Days 1-7) of enteric fever (Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi/Paratyphi), blood culture has the highest diagnostic sensitivity (~60-80%, whereas Widal O/H antibodies rise significantly only in week 2 and have high false positivity in endemic India). Because fluoroquinolone (ciprofloxacin) resistance exceeds 80-90% in India, ICMR AMR guidelines recommend oral Azithromycin for uncomplicated outpatient disease and IV Ceftriaxone for severe/hospitalised cases.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02852","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Digoxin Toxicity","q":"A 72-year-old woman taking digoxin and furosemide for heart failure presents with nausea, vomiting, yellow-green halos around lights (xanthopsia), serum K+ 2.7 mEq/L, and bidirectional ventricular tachycardia (alternating with atrial tachycardia with 2:1 AV block) on ECG. Why did hypokalaemia precipitate toxicity, and what is the specific life-saving antidote?","o":["Hypermagnesaemia caused toxicity; administer IV amiodarone alone without Fab","Digoxin blocks beta-1 receptors; administer IV atropine and high-dose furosemide","Hypokalaemia accelerates hepatic digoxin clearance; administer IV calcium chloride bolus","Hypokalaemia reduces K+ competition at the Na+/K+-ATPase binding site increasing digoxin binding; administer Digoxin-specific Fab fragments (Digibind) + correct K+"],"a":3,"e":"Digoxin and potassium compete for the extracellular binding site on cardiac Na+/K+-ATPase; therefore, loop diuretic-induced hypokalaemia (and hypomagnesaemia/renal failure) dramatically enhances digoxin binding and toxicity. Classic signs include GI upset, xanthopsia, PAT with block, and bidirectional VT. Life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias or hyperkalaemia (\u003e5.0-5.5 mEq/L in acute overdose) mandate specific Digoxin immune Fab antibody fragments plus K+/Mg2+ correction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02853","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"SGLT2 Inhibitor Complications","q":"A 48-year-old man with type 2 diabetes taking empagliflozin (an SGLT2 inhibitor) and metformin undergoes emergency appendicectomy while fasting. Postoperatively he develops tachypnoea, nausea, arterial pH 7.18, HCO3- 11 mEq/L, anion gap 22 mEq/L, strongly positive serum beta-hydroxybutyrate, yet capillary blood glucose is only 168 mg/dL. What is the diagnosis and correct treatment?","o":["SGLT2 inhibitor-associated Euglycaemic DKA; stop empagliflozin, resuscitate with isotonic saline, and start IV regular insulin PLUS dextrose-containing IV fluids simultaneously","Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state; give 20 units insulin without dextrose","Not DKA because glucose is \u003c 250 mg/dL; withhold insulin and restart oral empagliflozin","Simple starvation ketosis; treat with oral sodium bicarbonate alone"],"a":0,"e":"SGLT2 inhibitors promote continuous renal glucosuria, lowering plasma glucose (\u003c200-250 mg/dL) even while insulinopenia, glucagon surge, fasting, or surgical stress trigger severe ketoacidosis (Euglycaemic DKA). Management requires stopping the SGLT2i, fluid resuscitation, potassium monitoring, and immediate IV insulin infusion CO-ADMINISTERED with dextrose (D5W/D10W) from the start to suppress ketogenesis without causing hypoglycaemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02854","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Antitubercular Drug Adverse Effects","q":"A 36-year-old patient on daily HRZE antitubercular therapy for 6 weeks complains of painless progressive blurring of central vision and difficulty distinguishing red and green traffic lights. Fundoscopy is initially normal, and visual field testing shows a central scotoma. Which drug is responsible and what is the required action?","o":["Rifampicin; reassure that orange-red tears always cause permanent colour blindness","Ethambutol (retrobulbar optic neuritis); discontinue Ethambutol immediately and refer for ophthalmology evaluation","Isoniazid; double the pyridoxine dose while continuing Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide; add oral allopurinol and continue all 4 drugs"],"a":1,"e":"Ethambutol causes dose- and duration-dependent retrobulbar optic neuritis (manifesting as reduced visual acuity, central scotoma, and red-green dyschromatopsia). Because vision loss can become irreversible if continued, Ethambutol must be stopped immediately upon visual symptoms. (By contrast, Isoniazid causes pyridoxine-preventable peripheral neuropathy, Rifampicin causes benign orange secretions and CYP induction, and Pyrazinamide causes hyperuricaemia/hepatotoxicity).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02855","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Visceral Leishmaniasis (Kala-Azar)","q":"A 28-year-old man from rural Bihar presents with 6 weeks of double-quotidian fever, darkening of facial skin, massive non-tender splenomegaly, hepatomegaly, pancytopenia, and polyclonal hypergammaglobulinaemia. Rapid rK39 immunochromatographic dipstick is positive, and bone marrow aspirate reveals intracellular amastigotes (LD bodies) with kinetoplasts inside macrophages. Under India\u0027s National Kala-Azar Elimination Programme, what is the first-line treatment?","o":["IV metronidazole for 10 days","Oral sodium stibogluconate for 7 days","Single-dose IV Liposomal Amphotericin B (10 mg/kg)","Oral chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days"],"a":2,"e":"Chronic fever, massive splenomegaly, pancytopenia, positive rK39 strip, and amastigote LD bodies (containing nucleus + rod-shaped kinetoplast) in a patient from Bihar confirm Visceral Leishmaniasis (Kala-Azar, caused by Leishmania donovani transmitted by Phlebotomus argentipes). Due to widespread pentavalent antimonial resistance in Bihar, India\u0027s NVBDCP first-line regimen is single-dose IV Liposomal Amphotericin B (10 mg/kg), with Miltefosine + Paromomycin as alternative.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02856","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Antimicrobial Stewardship","q":"A 64-year-old diabetic woman with urosepsis grows Extended-Spectrum Beta-Lactamase (ESBL)-producing Escherichia coli from two blood culture sets (resistant to ceftriaxone, cefotaxime, and ciprofloxacin; susceptible to meropenem and ertapenem). Based on ICMR Antimicrobial Guidelines and the MERINO trial, what is the most reliable definitive IV antibiotic?","o":["IV Ceftriaxone 2 g daily","Oral Cefixime 200 mg BID","IV Vancomycin monotherapy","IV Carbapenem (Meropenem or Ertapenem/Imipenem)"],"a":3,"e":"ESBL enzymes hydrolyse penicillins, first-, second-, and third-generation cephalosporins (ceftriaxone, cefotaxime, ceftazidime), and aztreonam, while remaining susceptible to carbapenems. For invasive ESBL bacteraemia/septic shock, carbapenems (meropenem, imipenem, or ertapenem) are the gold-standard therapy (superior to piperacillin-tazobactam for ESBL bloodstream infection per the MERINO trial).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02857","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Tetanus Prophylaxis in Wounds","q":"A 35-year-old farmer sustains a deep, soil-contaminated crush laceration of his foot on a rusty farm implement. He has never received tetanus immunisation (unknown/zero prior doses). Alongside thorough surgical wound toileting, what tetanus prophylaxis is required?","o":["Human Tetanus Immunoglobulin (HTIG 250-500 IU IM) AND Tetanus-diphtheria (Td) toxoid vaccine at two separate anatomical sites (plus completing the 3-dose series)","Td toxoid vaccine alone without HTIG","Only oral amoxicillin without Td or HTIG","HTIG alone without any toxoid vaccine"],"a":0,"e":"For a tetanus-prone (dirty, contaminated, devitalised, \u003e6 hours old, or crush) wound in a patient with \u003c3 prior tetanus toxoid doses or unknown vaccination history, BOTH passive immediate neutralisation with Human Tetanus Immunoglobulin (HTIG 250-500 IU IM) AND active immunisation with Td vaccine must be given immediately into different limbs using separate syringes, followed by completing the primary 3-dose Td schedule.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02858","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)","q":"A 66-year-old chronic smoker with COPD presents with increased dyspnoea and purulent sputum. On room air SpO2 is 80%; after starting controlled oxygen via 28% Venturi mask (target SpO2 88-92%), ABG shows pH 7.28, PaCO2 68 mmHg, PaO2 62 mmHg, HCO3- 31 mEq/L. He is conscious and able to protect his airway. What is the next best ventilatory step alongside bronchodilators, steroids, and antibiotics?","o":["Switch to 100% non-rebreather mask to push SpO2 to 100%","Initiate Non-Invasive Positive-Pressure Ventilation (BiPAP/NIV) while maintaining target SpO2 88-92%","Discharge on home salbutamol inhaler alone","Administer IV sodium bicarbonate 100 mEq and withhold NIV"],"a":1,"e":"In acute COPD exacerbation with hypercapnic respiratory acidosis (pH \u003c 7.35, specifically 7.25-7.35, and PaCO2 \u003e 45 mmHg) in a cooperative patient protecting their airway, Non-Invasive Ventilation (BiPAP/NIV) significantly reduces work of breathing, intubation rates, and mortality. Controlled oxygen targeting SpO2 88-92% prevents both lethal hypoxaemia and hyperoxia-induced worsening hypercapnia (Haldane effect / V/Q mismatch).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02859","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Acute Bacterial Meningitis","q":"A 24-year-old college student presents with 12 hours of high fever (39.6 degrees C), severe headache, photophobia, neck rigidity, and confusion (GCS 12/15). Because of altered mental status, a non-contrast CT head is ordered prior to lumbar puncture. What is the mandatory action BEFORE sending the patient to the CT scanner?","o":["Wait until CT head and lumbar puncture CSF results return 2 hours later before giving antibiotics","Perform immediate lumbar puncture without CT despite GCS 12/15 and papilloedema risk","Draw two sets of blood cultures and immediately administer IV Dexamethasone plus empiric IV Ceftriaxone + Vancomycin BEFORE CT/LP","Administer oral amoxicillin alone"],"a":2,"e":"Altered sensorium (GCS \u003c 15), papilloedema, focal deficits, or immunocompromised state warrant CT head before LP to exclude mass effect/herniation risk. However, delaying antimicrobials in suspected acute bacterial meningitis sharply increases mortality and neurological sequelae. Blood cultures must be drawn immediately followed at once by IV dexamethasone (before/with first antibiotic dose to reduce pneumococcal neurological sequelae) and empiric IV ceftriaxone + vancomycin (+ ampicillin if \u003e50 yrs/immunocompromised for Listeria) BEFORE going to CT.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02860","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Acute Intestinal Obstruction","q":"A 52-year-old woman with a prior midline laparotomy scar admitted for adhesive small bowel obstruction initially had colicky periumbilical pain. Six hours later, her pain becomes constant and severe, temperature rises to 38.6 degrees C, HR is 124/min, guarding and rebound tenderness appear over the lower abdomen, WBC rises to 19,500/mm3, and serum lactate is 4.2 mmol/L. What is the immediate surgical decision?","o":["Administer oral gastrografin challenge to relieve obstruction","Give IV morphine and discharge once pain settles","Continue conservative nasogastric decompression and IV fluids for another 48 hours","Proceed to emergency exploratory laparotomy immediately after rapid resuscitation (bowel strangulation)"],"a":3,"e":"Conversion from intermittent colicky pain to constant severe pain accompanied by fever, tachycardia, peritoneal signs (guarding/rebound), leucocytosis, and elevated lactate strongly signals bowel strangulation/ischaemia in small bowel obstruction. While uncomplicated adhesive SBO may trial conservative NG decompression, suspected strangulation is an absolute emergency requiring immediate laparotomy to prevent gangrene, perforation, and peritonitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02861","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Urolithiasis Emergencies","q":"A 44-year-old diabetic woman presents with acute left flank pain, fever (39.5 degrees C) with rigors, BP 84/54 mmHg, WBC 21,000/mm3, and serum creatinine 2.2 mg/dL. NCCT KUB shows an 8 mm obstructing calculus in the left proximal ureter with moderate-to-severe hydroureteronephrosis and perinephric fat stranding (obstructed infected kidney). Beyond fluid resuscitation and broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, what is the immediate urological intervention?","o":["Urgent decompression of the collecting system via retrograde Double-J (DJ) ureteric stent OR percutaneous nephrostomy (PCN), deferring stone extraction until sepsis resolves","Oral tamsulosin medical expulsive therapy alone for 4 weeks","Immediate ureteroscopic laser lithotripsy (URS) and stone fragmentation today","Immediate Extracorporeal Shock Wave Lithotripsy (ESWL) in the acute septic phase"],"a":0,"e":"An obstructing ureteric stone complicated by urosepsis (obstructive pyelonephritis/pyonephrosis) is a true urological emergency that can progress rapidly to fatal septic shock if pus remains trapped under pressure. Immediate decompression via cystoscopic DJ stent or ultrasound-guided PCN + IV antibiotics is mandatory; definitive stone removal (URS/ESWL/PCNL) must be deferred until infection is completely controlled because instrumentation/irrigation pressure pushes bacteria into the bloodstream.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02862","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Chronic Venous Insufficiency","q":"A 53-year-old traffic policeman with longstanding varicose veins presents with a shallow, sloping-edged, granulating ulcer over the medial gaiter area (just above the medial malleolus), surrounded by brownish haemosiderin pigmentation and lipodermatosclerosis. Before applying graduated four-layer compression bandaging (gold standard for venous ulcer healing), which mandatory bedside test must be performed?","o":["Immediate saphenofemoral high ligation before infection/oedema control","Ankle-Brachial Pressure Index (ABPI) via handheld Doppler to exclude concomitant peripheral arterial disease (compression safe if ABPI \u003e 0.8)","Biopsy of the ulcer edge to start immediate radiotherapy","Venous tourniquet Perthes test alone"],"a":1,"e":"Chronic venous hypertension classically causes gaiter-area (medial malleolar) ulcers with haemosiderin staining, venous eczema, and lipodermatosclerosis. Graduated multilayer compression bandaging promotes venous ulcer healing, but MUST NOT be applied before measuring ABPI with Doppler—if significant arterial insufficiency exists (ABPI \u003c 0.8, especially \u003c 0.5), tight compression induces severe limb ischaemia and necrosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02863","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Abruptio Placentae","q":"A 32-year-old G3P2 woman with severe chronic hypertension at 34 weeks presents with sudden severe continuous abdominal pain and scant dark vaginal bleeding. Pulse is 124/min, BP 82/54 mmHg, uterus is larger than gestational age, tense, \u0027woody\u0027 hard, and exquisitely tender with absent fetal heart sounds. Fibrinogen is 90 mg/dL, platelets 68,000/mm3, and PT/aPTT are prolonged. What is the primary diagnosis and immediate management priority?","o":["Subacute appendicitis; diagnostic laparoscopy leaving pregnancy undisturbed","False labour pains; discharge with oral analgesics","Concealed/mixed Abruptio Placentae with DIC and IUFD; aggressive blood/FFP/cryoprecipitate resuscitation and expeditious delivery (amniotomy + oxytocin if stable for vaginal birth, or CS if deteriorating)","Placenta praevia; expectant conservative Macafee regimen until 37 weeks"],"a":2,"e":"Severe continuous abdominal pain, dark scant bleeding, hypovolaemic shock disproportionate to visible blood loss, a tense \u0027woody\u0027 tender uterus, fetal demise, and consumptive coagulopathy (DIC) are hallmarks of severe Abruptio Placentae (premature separation of a normally sited placenta). Resuscitation with cross-matched blood, FFP/cryoprecipitate, and urgent delivery (amniotomy + oxytocin preferred in IUFD if coagulopathy/haemodynamics allow rapid vaginal birth; Caesarean if unstable/failed progress) stops ongoing thromboplastin release.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02864","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Preterm Prelabour Rupture of Membranes (PPROM)","q":"A 26-year-old primigravida at 30+2 weeks of gestation presents with confirmed watery vaginal leaking on sterile speculum examination (pooling of amniotic fluid, ferning positive). She is afebrile, uterus is non-tender, WBC is normal, CTG is reassuring, and she is NOT in active labour. Which expectant management bundle improves neonatal survival and reduces chorioamnionitis?","o":["Long-term oral tocolysis with nifedipine for 4 weeks","Routine digital cervical examination every 4 hours plus immediate induction of labour today","Prophylactic metronidazole + ciprofloxacin alone without corticosteroids","Antenatal corticosteroids (Betamethasone 12 mg IM q24h x 2 or Dexamethasone) PLUS latency antibiotics (IV ampicillin + oral azithromycin/erythromycin) and close maternal-fetal surveillance (avoiding digital PV exams)"],"a":3,"e":"In uncomplicated PPROM \u003c 34 weeks (without chorioamnionitis, abruption, or fetal distress), expectant management prolongs gestation safely using: (1) a single course of antenatal corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity/IVH reduction; (2) latency antibiotics (typically IV ampicillin + erythromycin/azithromycin followed by oral amoxicillin) to prolong latency and cut neonatal sepsis; and (3) strict avoidance of digital vaginal examinations (to prevent ascending infection).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02865","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Uterine Rupture","q":"A 29-year-old G2P1 woman with a history of one previous lower-segment Caesarean section undergoing trial of labour at 39 weeks suddenly complains of sharp suprapubic pain tearing through her contractions. Uterine contractions abruptly cease, CTG drops to prolonged severe fetal bradycardia (65 bpm), maternal tachycardia and hypotension develop, and on vaginal exam the previously engaged fetal head has receded upward (\u0027loss of station\u0027) with superficial fetal parts palpable per abdomen. What is the immediate step?","o":["Immediate emergency laparotomy for suspected uterine rupture alongside maternal resuscitation","Administer intramuscular terbutaline and observe for 2 hours","Perform forceps delivery in the labour room","Increase IV oxytocin infusion to restart uterine contractions"],"a":0,"e":"Sudden abdominal pain, abrupt cessation of uterine contractions, severe fetal bradycardia, maternal shock, loss of presenting part station, and easily palpable superficial fetal parts during TOLAC are pathognomonic of complete Uterine Rupture. Immediate emergency laparotomy (to deliver the fetus and perform uterine repair or hysterectomy) with simultaneous massive resuscitation is life-saving for both mother and fetus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02866","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Gestational Trophoblastic Disease","q":"A 23-year-old primigravida at 11 weeks of amenorrhoea presents with hyperemesis, vaginal passage of grape-like vesicles, a uterus corresponding to 18 weeks\u0027 size, bilateral 7 cm ovarian cysts (theca lutein cysts), and serum beta-hCG of 240,000 IU/L. Ultrasound shows a classic \u0027snowstorm\u0027 vesicular appearance with no fetus. Following suction evacuation, what is essential for follow-up surveillance and ovarian cyst management?","o":["Routine bilateral oophorectomy for the theca lutein cysts","Weekly serial serum beta-hCG monitoring until undetectable (then monthly) with reliable contraception for 6-12 months; leave theca lutein cysts alone as they regress spontaneously","Allow immediate conception next month without beta-hCG follow-up","Prophylactic hysterectomy in all primigravidas"],"a":1,"e":"Complete hydatidiform mole (typically 46,XX entirely paternal androgenetic origin) requires gentle suction evacuation (with IV oxytocin started once evacuation begins and anti-D if Rh-negative). Because ~15-20% can progress to persistent gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN), serial weekly serum beta-hCG until 3 consecutive normal values (then monthly for 6 months post-normalisation) plus reliable contraception (COCPs/barrier, avoiding pregnancy) are mandatory. Hyperstimulated theca lutein cysts regress spontaneously once hCG normalises and require surgery only if twisted/ruptured.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02867","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Thoracoabdominal Diaphragm","q":"Which pairing accurately lists the three major diaphragmatic openings, their classical vertebral levels, and the structures passing through each?","o":["Vena caval opening (T12): IVC and thoracic duct; Esophageal hiatus (T8): esophagus and left phrenic nerve; Aortic hiatus (T10): aorta and splanchnic nerves","Vena caval opening (T8): SVC and left vagus; Esophageal hiatus (T10): trachea; Aortic hiatus (L2): inferior mesenteric artery","Vena caval opening (T8, central tendon): IVC and right phrenic nerve; Esophageal hiatus (T10, right crus sling): esophagus, vagal trunks, and esophageal branches of left gastric vessels; Aortic hiatus (T12, behind median arcuate ligament): aorta, thoracic duct, and azygos vein","Vena caval opening (T10): IVC and azygos vein; Esophageal hiatus (T12): esophagus and sympathetic trunk; Aortic hiatus (T8): aorta"],"a":2,"e":"Remembered by \u0027I Ate 10 Eggs At 12\u0027 (IVC at T8, Esophagus at T10, Aorta at T12): (1) The caval opening lies in the central tendon at T8 (slightly right of midline) transmitting the inferior vena cava and right phrenic nerve branches; (2) the esophageal hiatus lies in a muscular sling of the right diaphragmatic crus at T10 transmitting the esophagus, anterior/posterior vagal trunks, and esophageal branches of the left gastric artery/vein; and (3) the aortic hiatus lies posterior to the median arcuate ligament at T12 transmitting the descending thoracic aorta, thoracic duct, and usually the azygos vein.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02868","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Tracheobronchial Tree and Bronchopulmonary Segments","q":"A bedridden supine stroke patient aspirates oropharyngeal secretions. Into which lung and specific bronchopulmonary segment is aspirated material most likely to gravitate first, and what anatomical unit permits surgical segmentectomy?","o":["Right lung, medial basal segment of right middle lobe; segments share no intersegmental connective tissue planes","Left lung, anterior basal segment; segmental pulmonary veins run centrally alongside each tertiary bronchus","Left lung (more horizontal left main bronchus), lingula; each segment is supplied exclusively by bronchial veins","Right lung (wider, shorter, and more vertical right main bronchus), superior (apical) segment of the right lower lobe (or posterior segment of right upper lobe); each bronchopulmonary segment is a pyramid-shaped unit aerated by a tertiary (segmental) bronchus with its own segmental pulmonary artery branch and intersegmental venous drainage"],"a":3,"e":"Because the right main bronchus is wider, shorter (~2.5 cm), and more nearly vertical (in line with the trachea) than the left, aspirated foreign bodies and secretions preferentially enter the right bronchial tree. In a supine patient, gravity directs material posteriorly into the superior (apical) segment of the right lower lobe (or posterior segment of the right upper lobe); when upright, it enters the posterior basal segment of the right lower lobe. Each of the ~10 bronchopulmonary segments per lung is functionally and surgically independent, centered on a tertiary (segmental) bronchus and segmental pulmonary artery branch, while pulmonary veins course in the intersegmental connective tissue septa.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02869","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Intercostal Spaces","q":"A 50-year-old man requires diagnostic and therapeutic pleural tapping (thoracocentesis) for a large right-sided pleural effusion. Where should the needle be inserted relative to the ribs in the midaxillary/scapular line, and why?","o":["Immediately along the SUPERIOR border of the lower rib (above the rib below), avoiding the main intercostal neurovascular bundle (arranged Vein-Artery-Nerve from top to bottom) tucked inside the subcostal groove along the inferior border of the upper rib","Immediately along the INFERIOR border of the upper rib, because the intercostal nerve runs along the superior border of the lower rib","Directly through the subcostal groove to puncture the internal thoracic artery","Between the external and internal intercostal muscles to drain the endothoracic fascia only"],"a":0,"e":"In each intercostal space, the main neurovascular bundle lies between the internal intercostal and innermost intercostal muscles, sheltered under the costal (subcostal) groove along the inferior border of the upper rib, ordered from superior to inferior as Vein, Artery, Nerve (\u0027VAN\u0027). Therefore, during thoracocentesis or chest tube insertion (within the \u0027triangle of safety\u0027), the needle/trocar is advanced directly over the superior margin of the lower rib to avoid lacerating the posterior intercostal artery/vein or injuring the intercostal nerve (only a tiny collateral branch runs along the upper border of the lower rib).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02870","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Pericardium and Pericardial Sinuses","q":"During open-heart surgery for cardiopulmonary bypass, a cardiac surgeon passes a finger horizontally behind the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk and anterior to the superior vena cava to place a vascular clamp across the arterial outflow trunks. Which pericardial reflection space is the surgeon using, and why does acute pericarditis refer sharp pain to the left shoulder tip?","o":["Transverse pericardial sinus; fibrous and parietal serous pericardium are innervated by somatic sensory fibers of the phrenic nerve (C3-C5), sharing spinal cord segments with the supraclavicular nerves","Transverse pericardial sinus; pericardial pain is carried by the left recurrent laryngeal nerve","Oblique pericardial sinus; visceral pericardium is innervated by the cardiac sympathetic plexus (T1-T4)","Costodiaphragmatic recess; parietal pleura is innervated by the vagus nerve (CN X)"],"a":0,"e":"Embryologic folding of the heart tube creates two serous pericardial recesses: (1) the transverse pericardial sinus—a horizontal tunnel bounded anteriorly by the arterial mesocardium (ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk) and posteriorly by the venous inflow (SVC and superior pulmonary veins), allowing surgeons to pass a finger/ligature behind the aorta and pulmonary trunk to cross-clamp them during bypass; and (2) the oblique pericardial sinus—a blind cul-de-sac posterior to the left atrium bounded by pulmonary veins and IVC. Because the phrenic nerve (C3-C5) supplies somatic pain fibers to the fibrous and parietal serous pericardium, pericarditis refers pain to the C3-C5 shoulder/trapezius ridge dermatome (supraclavicular nerves).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02871","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Superior Mediastinum and Arch of Aorta","q":"A 60-year-old man with a saccular aneurysm of the inferior concavity of the aortic arch (or severe left atrial enlargement in mitral stenosis, Ortner syndrome) develops progressive hoarseness and a weak, bovine cough. Laryngoscopy confirms left vocal fold paralysis in the paramedian position. Where does the injured nerve branch and hook in the thorax?","o":["Right recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch at T4","Left recurrent laryngeal nerve branches from the left vagus nerve on the anterior aspect of the aortic arch, hooks inferiorly beneath the arch immediately posterior/lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum, and ascends in the tracheoesophageal groove","Left sympathetic trunk branches beneath the pulmonary veins to innervate the cricothyroid muscle","Left phrenic nerve hooks under the azygos arch to supply the vocal folds"],"a":1,"e":"In the superior mediastinum, the left vagus nerve descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries across the anterolateral aortic arch, where it gives off the left recurrent laryngeal nerve (RLN). Because the 6th aortic arch on the left persists distally as the ductus/ligamentum arteriosum, the left RLN hooks beneath the aortic arch just lateral/posterior to the ligamentum arteriosum and ascends in the tracheoesophageal groove to innervate all intrinsic muscles of the left larynx EXCEPT the cricothyroid (external branch of superior laryngeal nerve). Compression by an aortic arch aneurysm, bronchial carcinoma, or enlarged left atrium/pulmonary artery (Ortner cardiovocal syndrome) causes left vocal cord paralysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02872","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Azygos Venous System","q":"A patient with bronchogenic carcinoma compressing the superior vena cava (SVC) proximal to the azygos arch develops dilated thoracic wall veins and relies on the azygos-hemiazygos system to return upper-body blood to the heart. How does the azygos vein terminate, and which major veins connect the azygos system to the inferior vena cava?","o":["The azygos vein empties into the coronary sinus; it connects with the IVC via hepatic veins","The azygos vein drains directly into the left brachiocephalic vein; inferiorly it connects with the portal vein via the coronary vein only","The azygos vein arches over the root of the right lung (at T4) to enter the posterior aspect of the SVC; inferiorly it connects with the IVC via the ascending lumbar veins and right subcostal vein","The hemiazygos vein arches over the left lung root to enter the left atrium; it has no communication with the IVC"],"a":2,"e":"The azygos vein originates around T12-L1 from the union of the right ascending lumbar vein and right subcostal vein (often with a direct communication to the posterior IVC), ascends in the posterior mediastinum to the right of the thoracic duct and vertebral bodies, receives right posterior intercostal veins plus left-sided hemiazygos (at T8) and accessory hemiazygos (at T7) veins, and arches anteriorly over the right main bronchus/hilum at T4 to drain into the posterior SVC. If the SVC is obstructed below the azygos entry, blood shunts downward via the azygos/ascending lumbar/iliac veins into the IVC.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02873","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Superficial Venous Drainage of Lower Limb","q":"During venous cutdown at the ankle for emergency fluid resuscitation and subsequent varicose vein stripping along the medial leg, which surface landmark reliably locates the great saphenous vein (GSV), where does it terminate, and which cutaneous nerve is most vulnerable to iatrogenic injury alongside it below the knee?","o":["Posterior to the lateral malleolus; enters the popliteal vein; sural nerve","Mid-inguinal point lateral to the femoral artery; enters the external iliac vein; obturator nerve","Posterior to the medial malleolus; enters the posterior tibial vein; tibial nerve","Anterior to the medial malleolus; ascends medial leg/thigh (a handbreadth posterior to medial patella) and pierces the cribriform fascia at the saphenous opening to enter the femoral vein; saphenous nerve (branch of femoral nerve)"],"a":3,"e":"The great saphenous vein (longest vein in the body) begins on the medial dorsal venous arch of the foot, passes consistently ANTERIOR to the medial malleolus (ideal landmark for emergency venesection), ascends obliquely across the medial surface of the distal tibia, passes behind the medial femoral/tibial condyles, ascends the anteromedial thigh, and pierces the cribriform fascia covering the saphenous opening (~3-4 cm inferolateral to the pubic tubercle) to join the femoral vein. Below the knee, the saphenous nerve (purely sensory terminal branch of the femoral nerve) runs intimately with the GSV—accounting for medial leg/foot paresthesia if injured during GSV harvesting or stripping.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02874","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Perforating Veins and Venous Muscle Pump","q":"A traffic policeman presents with tortuous superficial varicosities and chronic hyperpigmented stasis dermatitis (\u0027gaiter area\u0027 ulceration) just superior to the medial malleolus. Which perforating veins directly connect the posterior arch vein to the posterior tibial veins in this lower medial leg region, and what direction do their valves normally permit blood flow?","o":["Cockett ankle perforators (medial distal leg); one-way valves direct blood strictly from SUPERFICIAL veins into DEEP veins (assisted by the soleus/gastrocnemius calf muscle pump)","Hunterian perforators at the saphenofemoral junction; blood flows bidirectionally in health","Boyd perforators at the mid-thigh; valves direct blood from DEEP veins to SUPERFICIAL veins","Dodd perforators behind the lateral malleolus; valves direct blood downward into the dorsal venous arch"],"a":0,"e":"Incompetent valves in the saphenofemoral junction or perforating veins allow high-pressure deep venous blood (driven by calf muscle contraction) to regurgitate into superficial veins, causing varicosities, venous hypertension, and gaiter-area venous ulcers. Anatomically, perforators pierce deep fascia with valves oriented to allow flow ONLY from superficial -\u003e deep: Hunterian/Dodd perforators in the distal/mid-thigh (adductor canal), Boyd perforators just below the knee, and Cockett perforators (upper, middle, lower) in the distal medial leg above the medial malleolus connecting the posterior arch (Leonardo) vein to the posterior tibial veins.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02875","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Femoral Sheath and Femoral Canal","q":"Which structures occupy the three compartments of the femoral sheath from lateral to medial, which structure lies OUTSIDE the femoral sheath altogether, and what lies inside the medial femoral canal?","o":["Lateral: femoral nerve; Intermediate: femoral artery; Medial: femoral vein; saphenous nerve lies inside the femoral canal","Lateral: femoral artery; Intermediate: femoral vein; Medial (femoral canal): lymphatics and deep inguinal lymph node of Cloquet/Rosenmüller; the femoral nerve lies LATERAL and OUTSIDE the femoral sheath","Lateral: femoral vein; Intermediate: femoral canal; Medial: femoral artery; deep peroneal nerve lies outside","Lateral: obturator artery; Intermediate: great saphenous vein; Medial: femoral nerve"],"a":1,"e":"Within the femoral triangle (bounded by inguinal ligament, sartorius, and adductor longus), the femoral sheath is a downward fascial prolongation of transversalis fascia anteriorly and fascia iliaca posteriorly surrounding the proximal femoral vessels (mnemonic NAVEL from lateral to medial: Nerve [OUTSIDE sheath], Artery, Vein, Empty space/lymphatics). Its three compartments are: lateral (femoral artery and femoral branch of genitofemoral nerve), intermediate (femoral vein), and medial (femoral canal—dead space allowing femoral vein expansion, containing fat, lymphatics, and Cloquet\u0027s node, and serving as the route for femoral hernias below and lateral to the pubic tubercle).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02876","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Lymphatic Drainage of Lower Limb","q":"Assertion (A): An infected ulcer over the lateral side of the heel or fifth toe typically causes tender enlargement of the popliteal lymph nodes before reaching the deep inguinal lymph nodes, whereas an ulcer over the medial foot or great toe drains directly to the vertical group of superficial inguinal lymph nodes. Reason (R): Superficial lymphatics accompanying the small saphenous vein along the lateral foot and posterior calf drain into the popliteal lymph nodes (which then ascend along deep femoral vessels to deep inguinal nodes), whereas lymphatics following the great saphenous vein bypass the popliteal nodes directly to the vertical superficial inguinal group.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Superficial lymphatics of the lower limb mirror the two saphenous venous territories: (1) Medial/anterior vessels accompany the great saphenous vein from the medial toes, sole, and medial/anterior leg directly to the vertical (inferior) group of superficial inguinal lymph nodes (and from there through the cribriform fascia to deep inguinal and external iliac nodes); (2) Lateral foot/heel and posterolateral calf vessels accompany the small saphenous vein to synapse first in the popliteal lymph nodes in the popliteal fossa before draining along deep femoral vessels to the deep inguinal nodes (note: horizontal superficial inguinal nodes drain the lower abdominal wall below umbilicus, gluteal region, perineum, and anal canal below pectinate line).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02877","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Popliteal Fossa","q":"During posterior knee dissection in the anatomy hall, a student exposes the diamond-shaped popliteal fossa. Which muscles form its superolateral and inferomedial/inferolateral borders, and what is the order of the three major neurovascular structures from SUPERFICIAL (posterior) to DEEP (anterior) against the femur/knee capsule?","o":["Superolateral: sartorius; Inferolateral: popliteus; Superficial-to-deep: Common peroneal nerve -\u003e Popliteal artery -\u003e Popliteal vein","Superolateral: semimembranosus; Inferomedial: soleus; Superficial-to-deep: Popliteal artery -\u003e Popliteal vein -\u003e Tibial nerve","Superolateral: biceps femoris; Inferomedial/Inferolateral: medial and lateral heads of gastrocnemius (plus plantaris); Superficial-to-deep order: Tibial nerve -\u003e Popliteal vein -\u003e Popliteal artery","Superolateral: gracilis; Inferomedial: tibialis posterior; Superficial-to-deep: Popliteal vein -\u003e Tibial nerve -\u003e Popliteal artery"],"a":2,"e":"The popliteal fossa is bounded superomedially by semitendinosus and semimembranosus, superolaterally by biceps femoris, and inferiorly by the medial and lateral heads of gastrocnemius (assisted laterally by plantaris). From posterior (superficial, beneath popliteal fascia) to anterior (deep, resting directly on the popliteal surface of the femur, oblique popliteal ligament, and popliteus fascia), the neurovascular structures lie in the order Nerve -\u003e Vein -\u003e Artery (\u0027NVA\u0027: Tibial nerve most superficial, Popliteal vein intermediate, Popliteal artery deepest—making the popliteal artery vulnerable in posterior knee dislocation/supracondylar femoral fracture).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02878","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Autonomic Ganglia and Neurotransmitters","q":"Which neurotransmitter and postsynaptic receptor mediate transmission at (1) ALL preganglionic autonomic ganglia (both sympathetic and parasympathetic), (2) sympathetic postganglionic innervation of thermoregulatory eccrine sweat glands, and (3) sympathetic preganglionic innervation of adrenal medullary chromaffin cells?","o":["(1) Glutamate on NMDA; (2) Acetylcholine on N_M; (3) Epinephrine on M3","(1) Norepinephrine on alpha-1; (2) Norepinephrine on beta-2; (3) Epinephrine on beta-1","(1) Acetylcholine on M2; (2) Dopamine on D1; (3) Norepinephrine on alpha-2","(1) Acetylcholine on nicotinic N_N (N2) receptors; (2) Acetylcholine on muscarinic M3 receptors; (3) Acetylcholine on nicotinic N_N receptors"],"a":3,"e":"All preganglionic autonomic fibers (thoracolumbar T1-L2 sympathetic and craniosacral CN III, VII, IX, X, S2-S4 parasympathetic) release acetylcholine onto neuronal nicotinic (N_N) ligand-gated cation channels on postganglionic neurons—including adrenal medullary chromaffin cells, which function as modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons releasing ~80% epinephrine / ~20% norepinephrine into blood. While most sympathetic postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine onto adrenergic receptors, thermoregulatory eccrine sweat glands are the classic exception: sympathetic postganglionic fibers release acetylcholine onto muscarinic M3 receptors (hence atropine causes anhidrosis/hyperthermia).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02879","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Pupillary Light Reflex and Accommodation","q":"A patient with neurosyphilis exhibits small, irregular bilateral pupils that fail to constrict to bright light (absent direct and consensual light reflexes) yet constrict briskly when focusing on a near object (Argyll Robertson pupil: light-near dissociation). Which anatomical site is damaged to abolish the light reflex while sparing near accommodation?","o":["Dorsal midbrain pretectal nucleus / posterior commissure (interrupting the CN II -\u003e pretectal -\u003e bilateral Edinger-Westphal light reflex pathway while sparing the ventral cortical-Edinger-Westphal near-response pathway)","Optic nerve anterior to the chiasm (causing an afferent pupillary defect)","Ciliary ganglion in the orbit (causing tonic Adie pupil unresponsive to both light and near)","Cervical sympathetic chain (causing Horner syndrome with unilateral ptosis and anhidrosis)"],"a":0,"e":"In the pupillary light reflex, retinal ganglion cell fibers travel via CN II, partially decussate at the chiasm, bypass the lateral geniculate body to synapse in the midbrain pretectal nuclei, and project bilaterally (via the posterior commissure) to both Edinger-Westphal nuclei -\u003e CN III -\u003e ciliary ganglion -\u003e sphincter pupillae (giving equal direct and consensual constriction). By contrast, the near/accommodation-convergence triad (miosis, lens thickening, medial rectus convergence) descends from the visual/association cortex directly to the ventral Edinger-Westphal and oculomotor nuclei, bypassing the dorsal pretectal area. Dorsal midbrain lesions (neurosyphilis, pinealoma/Parinaud) selectively abolish light constriction while preserving near constriction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02880","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Respiratory Mechanics and Surfactant","q":"Using the Law of Laplace for a spherical alveolus (P = 2T / r), an alveolus of radius r = 0.01 cm (100 micrometers) has a surface tension T = 50 dynes/cm in the absence of pulmonary surfactant (as in severe neonatal respiratory distress syndrome), which drops to T = 5 dynes/cm when adequate surfactant is present. What are the collapsing pressures P (in dynes/cm2) without surfactant versus with surfactant?","o":["Without surfactant: 10,000 dynes/cm2 (~7.5 mmHg); With surfactant: 1,000 dynes/cm2 (~0.75 mmHg)","Without surfactant: 5,000 dynes/cm2; With surfactant: 500 dynes/cm2","Without surfactant: 1,000 dynes/cm2; With surfactant: 10,000 dynes/cm2","Without surfactant: 2,500 dynes/cm2; With surfactant: 250 dynes/cm2"],"a":0,"e":"For a sphere with a single air-liquid interface (an alveolus), Laplace\u0027s law states P = 2T / r. Without surfactant: P = (2 x 50 dynes/cm) / 0.01 cm = 100 / 0.01 = 10,000 dynes/cm2 (since 1 mmHg ~ 1,333 dynes/cm2, ~7.5 mmHg collapsing pressure per tiny alveolus). With surfactant (DPPC + SP-A/B/C/D secreted by Type II pneumocytes): P = (2 x 5) / 0.01 = 1,000 dynes/cm2 (~0.75 mmHg)—reducing collapsing pressure 10-fold, increasing lung compliance, preventing small alveoli from emptying into large alveoli, and keeping alveoli dry.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02881","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Gas Exchange and Alveolar Gas Equation","q":"A patient breathing room air (FiO2 = 0.21) at sea level (barometric pressure PB = 760 mmHg, water vapor pressure PH2O = 47 mmHg) has arterial blood gas values PaO2 = 60 mmHg, PaCO2 = 40 mmHg, and respiratory quotient R = 0.8. Using the simplified alveolar gas equation PAO2 = FiO2(PB - PH2O) - (PaCO2 / R), what are the calculated PAO2 and A-a gradient (PAO2 - PaO2), and what does this A-a gradient indicate?","o":["PAO2 ~ 150 mmHg; A-a gradient ~ 90 mmHg (normal)","PAO2 ~ 100 mmHg; A-a gradient ~ 40 mmHg (elevated, indicating V/Q mismatch, diffusion defect, or right-to-left shunt)","PAO2 ~ 100 mmHg; A-a gradient ~ 10 mmHg (normal, indicating pure hypoventilation)","PAO2 ~ 70 mmHg; A-a gradient ~ 10 mmHg (elevated due to high altitude)"],"a":1,"e":"Inspired tracheal PO2 = FiO2 x (PB - PH2O) = 0.21 x (760 - 47) = 0.21 x 713 = 149.73 (~150 mmHg). Subtracting alveolar CO2 dilution: PAO2 = 149.73 - (40 / 0.8) = 149.73 - 50 = 99.73 (~100 mmHg). Thus, the A-a gradient = PAO2 - PaO2 = 100 - 60 = 40 mmHg (normal young adult A-a gradient on room air is ~5-15 mmHg). An elevated A-a gradient proves impaired gas transfer across the lung (V/Q mismatch, shunt, or diffusion impairment) rather than pure hypoventilation or low inspired FiO2 (which preserve a normal A-a gradient).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02882","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Ventilation-Perfusion (V/Q) Matching","q":"A patient with acute hypoxemia fails to show any substantial rise in arterial PaO2 even after breathing 100% inspired oxygen (FiO2 = 1.0) via a tight non-rebreather mask. Which V/Q abnormality explains this refractory hypoxemia, and how do West Zone 1 and Zone 3 pressures differ in a healthy upright lung?","o":["High-altitude hypoventilation; Zone 2 has Pv \u003e PA \u003e Pa","Pure diffusion limitation, which never responds to 100% O2; Zone 3 has PA \u003e Pa \u003e Pv","True right-to-left intrapulmonary or intracardiac shunt (V/Q = 0, e.g., lobar atelectasis/pneumonia/ASD reversal), because shunted blood bypasses ventilated alveoli and mixes with oxygenated blood; in upright lungs, Zone 1 (apex) has PA \u003e Pa \u003e Pv whereas Zone 3 (base) has Pa \u003e Pv \u003e PA","Alveolar dead space from pulmonary embolism (V/Q = infinity), which fails to improve on 100% O2; Zone 1 has Pa \u003e Pv \u003e PA"],"a":2,"e":"In a true right-to-left shunt (V/Q = 0), mixed venous blood bypasses ventilated alveoli completely; breathing 100% O2 raises dissolved O2 minimally in already saturated blood from ventilated units (since hemoglobin is ~100% saturated), leaving PaO2 refractory to supplemental oxygen. By contrast, V/Q mismatch (low V/Q), hypoventilation, and diffusion defects respond robustly to 100% O2. In an upright person, gravity increases pulmonary arterial (Pa) and venous (Pv) hydrostatic pressures from apex to base: Zone 1 (apex, minimal/absent in health unless PEEP/hemorrhage occurs) has PA \u003e Pa \u003e Pv (alveolar pressure compresses capillaries); Zone 2 has Pa \u003e PA \u003e Pv (waterfall flow); and Zone 3 (base) has Pa \u003e Pv \u003e PA (continuous distended capillary flow).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02883","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Dynamic Airway Compression","q":"Assertion (A): In patients with emphysema, the equal pressure point (where airway luminal pressure equals surrounding pleural pressure) shifts upstream toward smaller non-cartilaginous bronchioles, causing premature airway collapse during forced expiration; pursed-lip breathing helps maintain expiratory flow. Reason (R): Loss of alveolar elastic recoil in emphysema reduces intraluminal driving pressure during expiration while positive pleural pressure compresses floppy bronchioles, whereas exhaling through pursed lips adds downstream back-pressure inside the airways to shift the equal pressure point back toward cartilage-supported larger airways.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"During forced expiration, positive intrapleural pressure (+20 to +40 cmH2O) compresses both alveoli and intrathoracic airways. Normally, alveolar elastic recoil keeps intraluminal airway pressure higher than pleural pressure until the \u0027equal pressure point\u0027 (EPP), which resides in larger cartilaginous bronchi that resist collapse. In emphysema, destruction of alveolar walls abolishes elastic recoil and radial traction, dropping intraluminal pressure earlier and shifting the EPP upstream into thin-walled, non-cartilaginous bronchioles—which collapse and limit expiratory flow. Pursed-lip breathing creates positive end-expiratory airway resistance at the mouth, raising intraluminal pressure downstream to stent small airways open.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02884","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Cardiovascular Responses to Dynamic Exercise","q":"During vigorous dynamic aerobic exercise (e.g., running on a treadmill), which combination of systemic hemodynamic changes occurs in a healthy young adult?","o":["Doubled diastolic blood pressure with unchanged systolic blood pressure and decreased a-v O2 difference","Complete cessation of coronary and cerebral blood flow due to sympathetic vasoconstriction","Markedly increased TPR, decreased cardiac output, and narrowed pulse pressure","Markedly decreased total peripheral resistance (TPR), increased systolic blood pressure and pulse pressure, unchanged or slightly decreased diastolic blood pressure, and markedly increased cardiac output and venous return"],"a":3,"e":"During dynamic exercise, sympathetic discharge increases heart rate and contractility while venoconstriction, skeletal muscle pump, and thoracic respiratory pump augment venous return and stroke volume—increasing cardiac output 4-5 fold. Simultaneously, local metabolic vasodilators (adenosine, K+, H+, CO2, lactate, NO) override alpha-1 vasoconstriction in active skeletal muscles (\u0027functional sympatholysis\u0027), causing total peripheral resistance (TPR) to fall dramatically. Consequently, systolic BP rises (higher stroke volume/CO), diastolic BP stays nearly constant or falls slightly (lower TPR), pulse pressure widens markedly, and skeletal muscle O2 extraction widens the arteriovenous O2 difference.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02885","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Fick Principle and Oxygen Consumption","q":"During maximal aerobic exercise on a cycle ergometer, an athlete achieves a cardiac output of 20 L/min, arterial oxygen content (CaO2) of 200 mL O2/L blood, and mixed venous oxygen content (CvO2) of 50 mL O2/L blood. Using the Fick equation, what is the athlete\u0027s whole-body oxygen consumption (VO2)?","o":["3,000 mL O2/min (3.0 L/min)","1,000 mL O2/min (1.0 L/min)","150 mL O2/min (0.15 L/min)","4,000 mL O2/min (4.0 L/min)"],"a":0,"e":"By the Fick principle, Cardiac Output (CO) = VO2 / (CaO2 - CvO2), which rearranges to VO2 = CO x (CaO2 - CvO2). Here, the arteriovenous oxygen content difference is 200 - 50 = 150 mL O2/L blood. Multiplying by CO = 20 L/min gives VO2 = 20 L/min x 150 mL/L = 3,000 mL O2/min = 3.0 L/min (a ~12-fold increase over the resting basal VO2 of ~250 mL/min).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02886","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Sleep-Wake Flip-Flop Switch and Narcolepsy","q":"A 21-year-old medical student presents with irresistible daytime sleep attacks, vivid hypnagogic hallucinations upon falling asleep, sleep paralysis on waking, and sudden episodes of bilateral knee buckling/loss of muscle tone triggered by hearty laughter (cataplexy). Multiple sleep latency testing shows sleep-onset REM periods (SOREMPs). Loss of which hypothalamic neuropeptide and neuronal population causes this disorder?","o":["Histamine produced by the tuberomammillary nucleus alone without lateral hypothalamic involvement","Orexin-A and Orexin-B (hypocretin-1/2) produced by neurons of the lateral hypothalamus","Melatonin produced by the pineal gland","GABA produced by the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO)"],"a":1,"e":"Narcolepsy Type 1 (with cataplexy, strongly associated with HLA-DQB1*06:02) results from autoimmune destruction of orexin (hypocretin)-producing neurons in the lateral and posterior hypothalamus. Normally, orexin stabilizes the monoaminergic arousal centers (locus coeruleus NE, raphe 5-HT, tuberomammillary histamine) against the sleep-promoting GABA/galanin neurons of the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO) and suppresses inappropriate REM intrusion into wakefulness. Loss of CSF orexin (\u003c110 pg/mL) causes unstable sleep-wake boundaries and intrusion of REM phenomena (atonia -\u003e cataplexy/sleep paralysis; dreaming -\u003e hypnagogic hallucinations) directly into wakefulness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02887","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Pulmonary Vascular Regulation","q":"Assertion (A): Localized alveolar hypoxia constricts pulmonary arterioles (Euler-Liljestrand hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction) to divert blood toward better-ventilated alveoli, whereas generalized chronic alveolar hypoxia in COPD causes diffuse pulmonary hypertension and right ventricular hypertrophy (cor pulmonale). Reason (R): Hypoxia inhibits oxygen-sensitive voltage-gated K+ (Kv) channels in pulmonary arterial smooth muscle cells—causing membrane depolarization, L-type Ca2+ influx, and vasoconstriction—opposite to systemic arterioles where hypoxia opens K_ATP channels and increases adenosine/NO to cause vasodilation.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike systemic vascular beds (coronary, cerebral, skeletal muscle), which vasodilate during hypoxia to boost O2 delivery, pulmonary resistance arterioles constrict in response to low alveolar PO2 (PAO2 \u003c70 mmHg). Hypoxia closes O2-sensitive Kv channels in pulmonary vascular smooth muscle, depolarizing the membrane, opening voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, and elevating intracellular Ca2+ (augmented by endothelin-1 and reduced NO). Locally, this optimizes V/Q matching by shunting blood away from poorly ventilated regions; globally (high altitude, COPD, OSA), diffuse vasoconstriction raises pulmonary vascular resistance and precipitates cor pulmonale.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02888","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Chemical Control of Breathing","q":"A 67-year-old man with severe chronic bronchitis (baseline PaCO2 58 mmHg, PaO2 52 mmHg) receives uncontrolled high-flow 100% oxygen via non-rebreather mask and becomes progressively drowsy with PaCO2 rising to 82 mmHg. Which three physiological mechanisms explain oxygen-induced hypercapnia, and what primarily drives resting minute ventilation in a normal healthy subject?","o":["Bicarbonate retention in alveoli; in healthy subjects, peripheral arterial H+ drives 90% of resting breathing","Hering-Breuer stretch receptor paralysis; central chemoreceptors respond directly to arterial PO2","Reversal of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction worsening V/Q dead space, the Haldane effect (oxygenated Hb releases CO2 and H+), and blunting of carotid body hypoxic drive; in healthy subjects, central medullary chemoreceptors responding to CSF H+ derived from arterial CO2 crossing the blood-brain barrier provide the primary minute-to-minute drive","Carotid body stimulation by 100% O2 causing hyperventilation; in healthy subjects, PaO2 \u003c100 mmHg is the sole respiratory drive"],"a":2,"e":"In healthy individuals, central chemoreceptors on the ventral medulla respond sensitively to small rises in arterial PaCO2 (CO2 rapidly diffuses across the BBB, hydrates via carbonic anhydrase to H2CO3 -\u003e H+ + HCO3-, lowering CSF pH because CSF has low protein buffering), whereas carotid bodies respond primarily to severe hypoxemia (PaO2 \u003c60 mmHg) and acute arterial pH changes. In chronic hypercapnic COPD patients (where CSF HCO3- adaptation has blunted central CO2 sensitivity), excessive uncontrolled O2 (targeting SpO2 88-92% instead of 100%) worsens hypercapnia via: (1) releasing hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction in poorly ventilated units (increasing physiologic dead space—largest factor), (2) Haldane effect (O2 binding Hb decreases Hb\u0027s carbamino/H+ buffering capacity, shifting CO2 into plasma), and (3) reduced peripheral hypoxic ventilatory drive.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02889","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Phase I vs Phase II Biotransformation","q":"Which combination accurately describes Phase I versus Phase II hepatic xenobiotic biotransformation reactions and their essential cofactors?","o":["Phase I uses S-adenosylmethionine solely; Phase II uses molecular O2 and FAD only","Phase I always produces completely non-toxic metabolites; Phase II exclusively occurs in renal glomeruli","Phase I uses UDP-glucuronic acid in lysosomes; Phase II uses mitochondrial CYP450 and NADH to make drugs more lipophilic","Phase I (functionalization: oxidation/reduction/hydrolysis, chiefly microsomal CYP450 monooxygenases using NADPH, O2, and heme iron) exposes/introduces polar groups (-OH, -NH2, -SH); Phase II (conjugation: glucuronidation via UDP-glucuronic acid, sulfation via PAPS, glutathione conjugation via GSH, acetylation via acetyl-CoA) increases water solubility for biliary/renal excretion"],"a":3,"e":"Hepatic xenobiotic detoxification occurs in smooth endoplasmic reticulum (microsomes) and cytosol in two phases: Phase I introduces or unmasks polar functional groups via oxidation, reduction, or hydrolysis—predominantly catalyzed by cytochrome P450 (CYP) heme-thiolate monooxygenases (RH + O2 + NADPH + H+ -\u003e ROH + H2O + NADP+), which can either detoxify or bioactivate prodrugs/procarcinogens (e.g., paracetamol -\u003e NAPQI, benzo[a]pyrene -\u003e epoxide). Phase II covalently conjugates large polar endogenous molecules—glucuronic acid (from UDPGA via UGT), sulfate (from PAPS), glutathione (via GST), glycine/taurine, acetyl (acetyl-CoA via NAT2), or methyl (SAM)—to render metabolites hydrophilic and excretable.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02890","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Cytochrome P450 Induction and Inhibition","q":"A woman stabilized on combined oral contraceptive pills (ethinyl estradiol, metabolized by CYP3A4) is prescribed rifampicin for tuberculosis and experiences breakthrough bleeding and unintended pregnancy, whereas a patient taking simvastatin (CYP3A4 substrate) develops severe rhabdomyolysis after drinking fresh grapefruit juice daily. How do rifampicin and grapefruit juice alter CYP3A4 biochemistry?","o":["Rifampicin binds nuclear pregnane X receptor (PXR) to transcriptionally INDUCE hepatic/intestinal CYP3A4 synthesis (accelerating estrogen clearance); grapefruit furanocoumarins (bergamottin) irreversibly (mechanism-based) INHIBIT intestinal enterocyte CYP3A4 (raising oral simvastatin bioavailability)","Rifampicin covalently destroys CYP3A4 heme; grapefruit juice induces hepatic CYP3A4 transcription","Both rifampicin and grapefruit juice inhibit renal tubular secretion without affecting CYP3A4","Rifampicin induces UDPGA depletion only; grapefruit juice accelerates gastric emptying"],"a":0,"e":"Nuclear xenobiotic receptors—such as Pregnane X Receptor (PXR, activated by rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, St. John\u0027s wort) and Constitutive Androstane Receptor (CAR, phenobarbital)—heterodimerize with RXR and bind xenobiotic response elements to transcriptionally upregulate CYP3A4, CYP2C9, and UGT enzymes over days-to-weeks, accelerating metabolism of co-administered drugs (contraceptives, warfarin, cyclosporine, protease inhibitors). Conversely, furanocoumarins (bergamottin, 6\u0027,7\u0027-dihydroxybergamottin) in grapefruit juice act as mechanism-based (\u0027suicide\u0027) inhibitors of intestinal CYP3A4, permanently inactivating enterocyte CYP3A4 until new enzyme is synthesized—spiking oral blood levels of high-first-pass CYP3A4 substrates (simvastatin, felodipine, cyclosporine, midazolam).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02891","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Ethanol Metabolism and Disulfiram","q":"Assertion (A): Acute binge ethanol ingestion in a fasting malnourished individual precipitates severe fasting hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and hepatic steatosis. Reason (R): Sequential hepatic oxidation of ethanol to acetaldehyde (via cytosolic alcohol dehydrogenase) and acetate (via mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase, ALDH2) consumes NAD+ and generates a markedly elevated NADH/NAD+ ratio, which drives pyruvate -\u003e lactate and oxaloacetate -\u003e malate (starving gluconeogenesis), while inhibiting beta-oxidation and promoting glycerol-3-phosphate triglyceride synthesis.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both cytosolic ADH and mitochondrial ALDH2 reduce NAD+ to NADH, flooding hepatocytes with reducing equivalents (high NADH/NAD+ ratio). High NADH shifts reversible dehydrogenases toward reduced products: (1) pyruvate -\u003e lactate (depleting gluconeogenic pyruvate and causing lactic acidosis, which also impairs renal urate excretion); (2) oxaloacetate -\u003e malate (halting gluconeogenesis -\u003e severe fasting hypoglycemia once glycogen is depleted, plus inhibiting the TCA cycle); and (3) DHAP -\u003e glycerol-3-phosphate while suppressing NAD+-dependent fatty acid beta-oxidation—driving hepatic triglyceride accumulation (fatty liver). Blocking ALDH2 (via disulfiram, metronidazole, or East Asian ALDH2*2 deficiency) accumulates acetaldehyde causing flushing, tachycardia, and vomiting.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02892","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Nucleotide Antimetabolites and Xanthine Oxidase","q":"A patient with gout taking allopurinol requires initiation of 6-mercaptopurine (6-MP) for acute lymphoblastic leukemia maintenance. Why must the dose of 6-MP (or azathioprine) be reduced by ~75% when co-administered with allopurinol, and what is the biochemical mechanism of allopurinol\u0027s metabolite oxypurinol?","o":["Allopurinol is oxidized by xanthine oxidase to alloxanthine (oxypurinol), which potently inhibits xanthine oxidase (suicide/tight-binding inhibition); because xanthine oxidase also catabolizes 6-mercaptopurine to inactive 6-thiouric acid, inhibiting it causes toxic accumulation of 6-MP and fatal myelosuppression","Allopurinol inhibits IMP dehydrogenase directly, depleting GMP","Allopurinol induces HGPRT to destroy 6-MP rapidly, requiring a higher 6-MP dose","Oxypurinol converts 6-MP into uric acid crystals inside renal tubules"],"a":0,"e":"Xanthine oxidase converts hypoxanthine -\u003e xanthine -\u003e uric acid. Allopurinol (a structural isomer of hypoxanthine) acts as a substrate for xanthine oxidase, which oxidizes it to oxypurinol (alloxanthine)—a tight-binding/suicide inhibitor that remains coordinated to the molybdenum cofactor of xanthine oxidase, raising soluble hypoxanthine/xanthine while lowering uric acid. However, xanthine oxidase is also a major catabolic pathway clearing 6-mercaptopurine (and its prodrug azathioprine); co-administering allopurinol (or febuxostat) without reducing 6-MP/azathioprine doses causes severe bone marrow aplasia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02893","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Thymidylate Synthase and Folate Trap","q":"A patient with colon adenocarcinoma receives 5-fluorouracil (5-FU) potentiated by leucovorin, while another patient with pernicious anemia (vitamin B12 deficiency) develops megaloblastic anemia despite adequate dietary folate intake. How does 5-FU block dTMP synthesis, and why does vitamin B12 deficiency trap folate in an unusable form?","o":["5-FU inhibits ribonucleotide reductase directly; vitamin B12 deficiency blocks dihydrofolate reductase","5-FU is converted to FdUMP, which forms a covalent ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and N5,N10-methylene-THF (stabilized by leucovorin) to block dUMP -\u003e dTMP; vitamin B12 deficiency blocks methionine synthase, trapping folate irreversibly as N5-methyl-THF (\u0027folate trap\u0027)","5-FU incorporates into mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma; vitamin B12 deficiency accumulates N10-formyl-THF","5-FU activates thymidine kinase; vitamin B12 is a cofactor for thymidylate synthase directly"],"a":1,"e":"De novo thymidylate (dTMP) synthesis requires thymidylate synthase transferring a methyl group from N5,N10-methylene-THF to dUMP (oxidizing THF to DHF, which DHFR regenerates). 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) is metabolically activated to 5-FdUMP, a suicide inhibitor that binds thymidylate synthase together with N5,N10-methylene-THF (increased by leucovorin/folinic acid) to lock the enzyme in an irreversible covalent ternary complex—causing \u0027thymineless cell death\u0027. Separately, methylene-THF reductase irreversibly reduces N5,N10-methylene-THF to N5-methyl-THF; the ONLY reaction recycling N5-methyl-THF back to free THF is vitamin B12 (methylcobalamin)-dependent methionine synthase (homocysteine -\u003e methionine). In B12 deficiency, folate accumulates uselessly as N5-methyl-THF (\u0027methyl-folate trap\u0027), starving dTMP and purine synthesis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02894","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Purine Salvage and Degradation Defects","q":"Which metabolic abnormality and clinical phenotype characterize autosomal recessive deficiency of purine nucleoside phosphorylase (PNP) versus adenine phosphoribosyltransferase (APRT) deficiency?","o":["PNP deficiency accumulates dATP causing B-cell and T-cell SCID equally; APRT deficiency causes cystine staghorn calculi","PNP deficiency blocks pyrimidine synthesis; APRT deficiency causes orotic aciduria","PNP deficiency accumulates dGTP (selectively toxic to T cells -\u003e severe T-cell immunodeficiency with low serum/urine uric acid and neurologic deficits); APRT deficiency channels unsalvaged adenine via xanthine oxidase into insoluble 2,8-dihydroxyadenine renal stones","PNP deficiency causes hyperuricemia and gout; APRT deficiency causes B-cell-only agammaglobulinemia"],"a":2,"e":"Purine nucleoside phosphorylase (PNP) cleaves inosine and guanosine/deoxyguanosine to hypoxanthine/guanine + ribose-1-phosphate. PNP deficiency blocks purine breakdown (lowering uric acid) and elevates deoxyguanosine, which T-lymphocytes preferentially phosphorylate to dGTP—inhibiting ribonucleotide reductase and causing selective severe T-cell immunodeficiency (often with normal/variable B cells and neurologic ataxia). By contrast, adenine phosphoribosyltransferase (APRT) salvages adenine to AMP; in APRT deficiency, xanthine oxidase oxidizes accumulated adenine into poorly soluble 2,8-dihydroxyadenine (2,8-DHA), forming radiolucent urinary stones that look like urate stones on routine tests but fail to dissolve with urine alkalinization (treated with allopurinol).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02895","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Phagocyte Respiratory Burst","q":"A 5-year-old boy with recurrent liver abscesses and pneumonia caused by catalase-positive Staphylococcus aureus and Aspergillus undergoes neutrophil functional testing. Flow cytometric dihydrorhodamine (DHR-123) assay shows NO green fluorescence shift after PMA stimulation, and the nitroblue tetrazolium (NBT) slide test remains yellow (fails to turn blue-black). Which enzyme complex is defective, and why are catalase-negative bacteria (like Streptococcus pyogenes) still killed normally?","o":["Glutathione reductase deficiency; neutrophils rely solely on nitric oxide synthase","Superoxide dismutase deficiency; catalase-negative bacteria cannot produce ATP","Myeloperoxidase (MPO) deficiency; catalase-positive bacteria lack superoxide dismutase","Phagocyte NADPH oxidase (NOX2 complex); catalase-negative bacteria generate their own metabolic H2O2, which host myeloperoxidase (MPO) uses inside the phagolysosome to produce bactericidal hypochlorous acid (HOCl)"],"a":3,"e":"Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD, most commonly X-linked CYBB/gp91phox mutation) impairs phagocyte NADPH oxidase, which normally transfers electrons from HMP-shunt NADPH to O2 to generate superoxide (O2-•) during the respiratory burst -\u003e converted by SOD to H2O2 -\u003e converted by myeloperoxidase (MPO) + Cl- into potent microbicidal hypochlorous acid (HOCl). Without NADPH oxidase, neutrophils fail to reduce yellow NBT dye to blue-black formazan or oxidize DHR-123 to fluorescent rhodamine. Catalase-negative organisms (Streptococcus) supply endogenous bacterial H2O2 for host MPO to make HOCl (\u0027self-destruction\u0027), whereas catalase-positive organisms (S. aureus, Burkholderia, Serratia, Nocardia, Aspergillus) destroy their own H2O2 with catalase and survive inside phagocytes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02896","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Antioxidant Defense Systems","q":"Which trace-element and vitamin pairing accurately matches the cellular antioxidant enzymes and non-enzymatic chain-breaking lipid-membrane antioxidants?","o":["Cytosolic SOD1 requires Cu/Zn and mitochondrial SOD2 requires Mn (converting O2-• -\u003e H2O2); Glutathione peroxidase requires Selenium (selenocysteine) to reduce H2O2/lipid hydroperoxides using GSH; Vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol) breaks lipid peroxidation chains in membranes and is regenerated by Vitamin C (ascorbate)","Catalase requires Magnesium in mitochondria; Vitamin B12 directly scavenges superoxide in lipid bilayers","Glutathione peroxidase requires Copper; Vitamin D regenerates oxidized alpha-tocopherol in cytosol","SOD2 requires Selenium; Catalase requires Zinc without heme; Vitamin K breaks lipid peroxidation chains"],"a":0,"e":"Cells neutralize reactive oxygen species sequentially: (1) Superoxide dismutases convert 2 O2-• + 2 H+ -\u003e H2O2 + O2 using Cu2+/Zn2+ in cytosol/extracellular space (SOD1/3; mutated in familial ALS) and Mn2+ in mitochondria (SOD2); (2) Peroxisomal catalase (a heme-iron enzyme) decomposes 2 H2O2 -\u003e 2 H2O + O2; and (3) Selenium-dependent glutathione peroxidase (GPx, containing selenocysteine) reduces both H2O2 and organic lipid hydroperoxides (ROOH) to water/alcohols while oxidizing 2 GSH -\u003e GSSG. In cell membranes and LDL, lipid-soluble alpha-tocopherol (Vitamin E) donates a hydrogen to lipid peroxyl radicals, forming a resonance-stabilized tocopheroxyl radical that cytosolic Vitamin C (ascorbate) reduces back to active Vitamin E.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02897","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Fenton Reaction, Lipid Peroxidation, and Ferroptosis","q":"In iron-overload states (hemochromatosis) or ischemia-reperfusion injury, free ferrous iron (Fe2+) reacts with hydrogen peroxide to generate the most indiscriminately damaging reactive oxygen species—the hydroxyl radical (•OH)—which initiates polyunsaturated fatty acid (PUFA) chain peroxidation. What are this iron-catalyzed reaction, the classic colorimetric lipid peroxidation byproduct assayed by TBARS, and the selenoenzyme that prevents iron-dependent ferroptosis?","o":["Haber-Weiss without transition metals; acetone; catalase","Fenton reaction (Fe2+ + H2O2 -\u003e Fe3+ + •OH + OH-); malondialdehyde (MDA); glutathione peroxidase-4 (GPX4)","Krebs-Henseleit reaction; acetoacetate; myeloperoxidase","Maillard glycation reaction; bilirubin; superoxide dismutase-1"],"a":1,"e":"Because cells lack an enzyme to specifically scavenge the hyper-reactive hydroxyl radical (•OH), preventing its formation is paramount. In the Fenton reaction, unbound ferrous iron (Fe2+) transfers an electron to H2O2 to form •OH + OH- + Fe3+ (and superoxide reduces Fe3+ back to Fe2+ in the iron-catalyzed Haber-Weiss cycle). •OH abstracts hydrogen from membrane PUFAs to trigger self-propagating lipid peroxidation, yielding cytotoxic aldehydes such as malondialdehyde (MDA, measured as thiobarbituric acid-reactive substances, TBARS) and 4-hydroxynonenal (4-HNE). GPX4 uniquely reduces membrane phospholipid hydroperoxides using GSH to suppress iron-dependent non-apoptotic cell death (ferroptosis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02898","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Nitric Oxide (NO) Biosynthesis and Peroxynitrite","q":"Nitric oxide synthase (NOS) converts which amino acid substrate and cofactors into nitric oxide (NO) and L-citrulline, and which potent nitrosating/oxidizing radical forms when excess NO reacts with superoxide (O2-•) during septic shock?","o":["L-glutamate + PLP; hydrogen sulfide","L-ornithine + CO2 + ATP; hypochlorous acid (HOCl)","L-arginine + O2 + NADPH (requiring tetrahydrobiopterin [BH4], FAD, FMN, and heme); peroxynitrite (ONOO-)","L-tyrosine + BH4; singlet oxygen"],"a":2,"e":"Endothelial (eNOS), neuronal (nNOS), and inducible macrophage (iNOS) nitric oxide synthases catalyze the 5-electron oxidation of a guanidino nitrogen of L-arginine using O2 and NADPH—plus five prosthetic/cofactor groups (FMN, FAD, heme, calmodulin [Ca2+-dependent in eNOS/nNOS], and tetrahydrobiopterin [BH4])—to produce NO and L-citrulline. NO activates soluble guanylyl cyclase (sGC) -\u003e cGMP -\u003e PKG -\u003e smooth muscle relaxation. During macrophage activation or ischemia-reperfusion, NO reacts at diffusion-limited rates with superoxide (O2-•) to form peroxynitrite (ONOO-), which nitrates protein tyrosine residues and damages lipids/DNA.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02899","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Respiratory Quotient (RQ) and Substrate Oxidation","q":"Complete aerobic oxidation of 1 mole of palmitic acid (C16H32O2) proceeds according to the balanced stoichiometric equation: C16H32O2 + 23 O2 -\u003e 16 CO2 + 16 H2O. What is the Respiratory Quotient (RQ) for pure palmitate oxidation, and how does it compare with pure glucose (C6H12O6) oxidation?","o":["Palmitate RQ = 1.44 (23 / 16); Glucose RQ = 0.80","Palmitate RQ = 1.00; Glucose RQ = 0.70","Palmitate RQ = 0.50; Glucose RQ = 1.20","Palmitate RQ = 0.70 (16 / 23); Glucose RQ = 1.00 (6 / 6)"],"a":3,"e":"The Respiratory Quotient (RQ) is defined as moles (or volume) of CO2 produced divided by moles (or volume) of O2 consumed at the cellular level: RQ = CO2 produced / O2 consumed. For palmitic acid (C16H32O2), RQ = 16 / 23 = 0.696 (~0.70)—lower than glucose because fatty acids are more reduced (oxygen-poor) and require extra O2 to oxidize their hydrogens to H2O. For glucose (C6H12O6 + 6 O2 -\u003e 6 CO2 + 6 H2O), RQ = 6 / 6 = 1.00 (proteins ~0.80; mixed diet ~0.82-0.85; lipogenesis from excess carbohydrate \u003e1.0).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02900","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Myocardial Infarction","q":"Five days after an acute transmural anterior myocardial infarction, a 63-year-old man suddenly develops hypotension, jugular venous distension, muffled heart sounds, and electromechanical dissociation (pulseless electrical activity) due to free-wall rupture and hemopericardium. Which inflammatory cell predominates in the infarcted myocardium at Days 4-7 to soften the necrotic wall via phagocytosis, and what color does unfixed necrotic myocardium show with triphenyltetrazolium chloride (TTC) staining?","o":["Macrophages (phagocytosing necrotic myocytes and weakening the wall prior to granulation tissue); pale/un-stained (white-gray) due to lactate dehydrogenase leakage, whereas viable myocardium stains brick-red","Eosinophils exclusively; viable myocardium stays unstained white on TTC","Neutrophils solely at Day 10; necrotic myocardium stains dark brick-red on TTC","Fibroblasts and dense collagen scar at 12 hours; necrotic myocardium stains bright blue"],"a":0,"e":"Myocardial infarction evolves chronologically: 0-4 hr (no gross/LM change, occasional wavy fibers at border); 4-12 hr (early coagulative necrosis, loss of LDH -\u003e pale unstained area on brick-red TTC); 1-3 days (dense neutrophilic infiltrate); 3-7 days (macrophages infiltrate to phagocytose dead myocytes—making Days 4-7 the peak window of maximal wall softening and mechanical rupture: ventricular free-wall rupture causing tamponade, interventricular septal rupture causing VSD, or posteromedial papillary muscle rupture causing acute MR); 7-14 days (vascularized granulation tissue); \u003e2 weeks to 2 months (dense collagen scar).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02901","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Cardiomyopathies","q":"A 19-year-old collegiate athlete collapses and dies suddenly during a basketball match. At autopsy, the heart shows massive asymmetric hypertrophy of the interventricular septum (septal-to-free-wall ratio \u003e1.5) compressing the left ventricular cavity into a \u0027banana-like\u0027 slit with an endocardial plaque beneath the anterior mitral leaflet. What is the hallmark histopathology and the most common mutated gene?","o":["Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM): four-chamber eccentric dilation with thinned walls; transthyretin (TTR) amyloid","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM): haphazard myocyte disarray with hypertrophied myofibers, branching bizarre nuclei, and interstitial fibrosis; autosomal dominant mutation in sarcomeric beta-myosin heavy chain (MYH7) or myosin-binding protein C (MYBPC3)","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy: fibrofatty replacement of RV; sarcomeric actin gain-of-function","Restrictive cardiomyopathy: non-dilated ventricles with endomyocardial eosinophilic fibrosis (Loeffler); dystrophin deletion"],"a":1,"e":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)—the leading cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes—is an autosomal dominant sarcomeric disease most frequently caused by missense mutations in MYH7 (beta-myosin heavy chain) or MYBPC3 (myosin-binding protein C). Grossly, asymmetric septal hypertrophy narrows the left ventricular outflow tract (causing systolic anterior motion [SAM] of the anterior mitral leaflet and a subaortic endocardial friction plaque). Microscopically, the pathognomonic finding is severe disorganization (\u0027myocyte disarray\u0027) of hypertrophied cardiomyocytes arranged in chaotic angles/whorls alongside interstitial fibrosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02902","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Rheumatic Fever and Rheumatic Heart Disease","q":"Myocardial histology from a child who died of acute rheumatic pancarditis shows discrete perivascular granulomatous foci of fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by lymphocytes, plasma cells, multinucleated giant cells, and plump activated macrophages with wavy ribbon-like central chromatin (\u0027caterpillar cells\u0027). What are these pathognomonic lesions and macrophages named, and what chronic mitral valve deformity develops years later?","o":["Verrucous nodules containing Negri bodies; Ebstein tricuspid displacement","Bracht-Wachter bodies containing foam cells; bicuspid aortic stenosis","Aschoff bodies containing Anitschkow (caterpillar) cells; chronic rheumatic valvulitis causes commissural fusion and chordal shortening producing a \u0027fish-mouth\u0027 (buttonhole) stenotic mitral valve","MacCallum plaques containing Reed-Sternberg cells; isolated pulmonary valve regurgitation"],"a":2,"e":"Acute rheumatic fever follows Group A beta-hemolytic Streptococcus (S. pyogenes) pharyngitis via Type II molecular mimicry (antibodies/CD4+ T cells against streptococcal M protein cross-reacting with cardiac myosin and valve glycoproteins). Histologically, acute rheumatic carditis is defined by Aschoff bodies (foci of collagen fibrinoid degeneration ringed by T cells, occasional plasma cells, Aschoff giant cells, and characteristic Anitschkow macrophages with central undulating \u0027caterpillar\u0027 chromatin), often accompanied by MacCallum endocardial plaques in the left atrium and fibrinous \u0027bread-and-butter\u0027 pericarditis. Chronic healing leads to fibrous thickening, calcification, commissural fusion, and \u0027fish-mouth\u0027 mitral stenosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02903","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Cholestatic and Viral Liver Diseases","q":"A 46-year-old woman with intense pruritus, xanthelasma, and elevated alkaline phosphatase has strongly positive anti-mitochondrial antibodies (AMA, anti-E2 pyruvate dehydrogenase) and liver biopsy showing lymphoplasmacytic granulomatous destruction of interlobular bile ducts (\u0027florid duct lesion\u0027). How do these features distinguish primary biliary cholangitis (PBC) from primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC)?","o":["PBC affects middle-aged women (intrahepatic small bile ducts destroyed by granulomatous \u0027florid duct lesions\u0027, AMA-positive); PSC affects men with ulcerative colitis (extrahepatic and large intrahepatic ducts showing concentric \u0027onion-skin\u0027 periductal fibrosis and \u0027beads-on-a-string\u0027 cholangiogram, p-ANCA-positive)","PBC shows \u0027onion-skin\u0027 fibrosis and p-ANCA positivity in men with Crohn disease; PSC is AMA-positive","PBC shows \u0027ground-glass\u0027 HBsAg-filled hepatocytes; PSC shows Mallory-Denk hyaline","Both PBC and PSC show negative ALP and exclusively affect hepatic veins (Budd-Chiari)"],"a":0,"e":"Primary biliary cholangitis (PBC) is an autoimmune T-cell-mediated destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts predominantly in middle-aged women (~9:1 F:M), serologically defined by \u003e90-95% anti-mitochondrial antibodies (AMA) and histologically by non-suppurative granulomatous \u0027florid duct lesions\u0027. Conversely, primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) predominates in younger/middle-aged men (~2:1 M:F) strongly associated with ulcerative colitis (~70%) and p-ANCA positivity; chronic fibro-obliterative cholangitis produces concentric \u0027onion-skin\u0027 fibrosis around medium/large intra- and extrahepatic bile ducts, yielding alternating strictures and dilations (\u0027beads-on-a-string\u0027) on MRCP/ERCP and high risk of cholangiocarcinoma.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02904","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Inherited Metabolic Liver Diseases","q":"A 52-year-old man presents with cirrhosis, new-onset diabetes mellitus, slate-gray/bronze skin hyperpigmentation, hypogonadism, and arthropathy of the 2nd/3rd MCP joints. Transferrin saturation is 85% and serum ferritin is 2,800 ng/mL; liver biopsy stained with Prussian blue shows dense golden-brown hemosiderin granules inside hepatocytes and bile duct epithelium. What is the defective gene/pathophysiology, and how does Wilson disease stain differently?","o":["HFE mutation increasing hepcidin levels; Wilson disease causes \u0027bronze diabetes\u0027","Autosomal dominant ATP7B mutation; hemochromatosis causes Kayser-Fleischer rings in Descemet membrane","Acquired alpha-1 antitrypsin Z-allele polymerization; stained with PAS-diastase","Autosomal recessive HFE (commonly C282Y) mutation reducing hepatic hepcidin expression -\u003e unregulated intestinal iron absorption via ferroportin (hereditary hemochromatosis); Wilson disease (ATP7B mutation) shows copper accumulation staining positively with rhodanine/orcein and low serum ceruloplasmin"],"a":3,"e":"Hereditary hemochromatosis (most often autosomal recessive HFE C282Y homozygosity) impairs hepatocyte iron-sensing and suppresses hepcidin synthesis. Without hepcidin to degrade duodenal enterocyte basolateral ferroportin, excess dietary iron is continuously absorbed and deposited as hemosiderin (staining bright blue on Perls Prussian blue) in liver (micronodular cirrhosis -\u003e high HCC risk), pancreas (\u0027bronze diabetes\u0027), skin, heart (cardiomyopathy), pituitary (hypogonadism), and joints (pseudogout). By contrast, Wilson disease (recessive ATP7B copper-transporting ATPase defect) impairs biliary copper excretion and ceruloplasmin incorporation—causing low serum ceruloplasmin, rhodanine/rubeanic-acid/orcein-positive hepatic copper, basal ganglia degeneration (lenticular), and Kayser-Fleischer corneal rings.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02905","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD)","q":"A 26-year-old man undergoes ileocecal resection for recurrent right lower quadrant pain, non-bloody diarrhea, and enteroenteric fistulas. Gross pathology shows patchy \u0027skip\u0027 segments of thickened \u0027hose-pipe\u0027 bowel wall with mesenteric \u0027creeping fat\u0027 and linear fissures creating a \u0027cobblestone\u0027 mucosal appearance, while microscopy reveals transmural lymphoid aggregates and non-caseating epithelioid granulomas. How do these findings differentiate Crohn disease from ulcerative colitis?","o":["Crohn disease causes discontinuous skip lesions anywhere from mouth to anus (commonly terminal ileum), transmural inflammation (fistulas/strictures/creeping fat), and non-caseating granulomas; ulcerative colitis begins in the rectum and spreads continuously proximally (limited to colon), with superficial mucosal/submucosal inflammation, crypt abscesses, pseudopolyps, and toxic megacolon risk","Crohn disease is always continuous starting at the rectum without granulomas; ulcerative colitis affects the terminal ileum with transmural fistulas","Crohn disease shows superficial mucosal ulceration only and p-ANCA positivity; ulcerative colitis shows ASCA positivity and creeping fat","Both Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis universally feature caseating Langhans granulomas with acid-fast bacilli"],"a":0,"e":"Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis are starkly distinct: Crohn disease (Th1/Th17, NOD2/CARD15 association, ASCA+) involves any GI segment (\u0027mouth to anus\u0027, sparing the rectum in ~50% and classically targeting terminal ileum/ileocecal region) with discontinuous skip lesions, full-thickness transmural inflammation (leading to deep knife-like fissures, cobblestone mucosa, strictures/\u0027string sign\u0027, fistulas, and creeping mesenteric fat), and non-caseating granulomas (~35%). Ulcerative colitis (p-ANCA+, smoking protective) invariably involves the rectum and extends proximally in a continuous distribution confined to the colon (except occasional \u0027backwash ileitis\u0027), with inflammation restricted to mucosa/submucosa, neutrophilic crypt abscesses, regenerating pseudopolyps, loss of haustra (\u0027lead-pipe\u0027 colon), and toxic megacolon.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02906","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Gastric Carcinoma","q":"A 44-year-old woman presents with early satiety, weight loss, a firm enlarged left supraclavicular lymph node (Virchow node), and bilateral solid ovarian masses. Upper GI endoscopy reveals a rigid, thickened, non-distensible stomach (\u0027linitis plastica / leather-bottle stomach\u0027) without a discrete fungating mass, and biopsy shows discohesive mucin-filled cells with peripherally compressed crescentic nuclei (\u0027signet-ring\u0027 cells). Loss of which cell adhesion molecule defines this diffuse gastric carcinoma?","o":["E-cadherin (CDH1 loss-of-function mutation); bilateral ovarian metastases represent Krukenberg tumors","APC tumor suppressor (beta-catenin loss); Krukenberg tumors arise exclusively from squamous esophageal cancer","HER2/neu amplification causing a polypoid antral mass with Sister Mary Joseph nodule solely","Helicobacter pylori CagA-driven glandular intestinal metaplasia forming cohesive mucinous glands (intestinal type)"],"a":0,"e":"Per Lauren\u0027s classification, gastric adenocarcinoma comprises two main types: (1) Intestinal type (associated with H. pylori chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, nitrosamines, blood group A; older males; cohesive gland-forming tumor creating an exophytic/ulcerated mass along the lesser curvature/antrum); and (2) Diffuse type (younger patients, no H. pylori/metaplasia precursor; driven by loss-of-function mutations in CDH1 encoding E-cadherin, causing cells to lose epithelial cohesion and infiltrate widely as PAS/mucicarmine-positive \u0027signet-ring\u0027 cells producing a rigid desmoplastic \u0027linitis plastica\u0027). Transcoelomic/lymphatic spread to bilateral ovaries forms Krukenberg tumors, and left supraclavicular nodal spread via the thoracic duct produces Troisier\u0027s sign (Virchow node).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02907","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Ovarian Neoplasms","q":"At an ovarian histopathology station, Slide A from a 16-year-old girl shows glomeruloid structures with a central blood vessel cuffed by malignant germ cells (\u0027Schiller-Duval bodies\u0027) and elevated serum AFP; Slide B from a 52-year-old woman shows small granulosa cells arranged around eosinophilic fluid-filled follicles (\u0027Call-Exner bodies\u0027) with inhibin positivity and endometrial hyperplasia; and Slide C shows sheets of large polygonal cells with clear glycogen cytoplasm and fibrous septa infiltrated by T-lymphocytes (\u0027fried-egg\u0027 appearance). Identify Slides A, B, and C.","o":["Slide A: Mature cystic teratoma; Slide B: Serous cystadenocarcinoma; Slide C: Choriocarcinoma","Slide A: Yolk sac (endodermal sinus) tumor; Slide B: Adult granulosa cell tumor; Slide C: Dysgerminoma","Slide A: Brenner tumor; Slide B: Krukenberg tumor; Slide C: Yolk sac tumor","Slide A: Dysgerminoma; Slide B: Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor; Slide C: Mucinous cystadenoma"],"a":1,"e":"Ovarian neoplasms feature iconic microscopic hallmarks: (1) Yolk sac (endodermal sinus) tumor—aggressive germ cell tumor in children/young women producing markedly elevated AFP and pathognomonic Schiller-Duval bodies (resembling primitive glomeruli) plus hyaline droplets; (2) Adult granulosa cell tumor—inhibin-positive sex cord-stromal tumor (FOXL2 mutation) forming microfollicular Call-Exner bodies (resembling Graafian follicles) and secreting estrogen (causing precocious puberty or postmenopausal bleeding/endometrial hyperplasia); and (3) Dysgerminoma (ovarian homolog of testicular seminoma)—OCT3/4+, PLAP+, c-KIT+ sheets of clear \u0027fried-egg\u0027 cells divided by lymphocytic fibrous septa, highly radiosensitive.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02908","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Cervical Neoplasia and HPV Oncogenesis","q":"Assertion (A): Persistent infection of the cervical transformation zone by high-risk Human Papillomavirus (HPV 16, 18) drives progression from CIN (HSIL) to invasive cervical squamous cell carcinoma. Reason (R): Viral integration disrupts the E2 regulatory gene and causes high-level expression of HPV E6 (which promotes ubiquitin-mediated degradation of p53) and E7 (which binds and inactivates hypophosphorylated pRB, releasing E2F and causing compensatory p16^INK4a overexpression).","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"High-risk mucosal HPV genotypes (especially HPV 16 -\u003e squamous cell carcinoma, and HPV 18 -\u003e endocervical adenocarcinoma) infect basal metaplastic cells at the squamocolumnar transformation zone, initially forming raisinoid perinuclear halo \u0027koilocytes\u0027 on Pap smear. When viral DNA integrates into host chromosomes, loss of the viral E2 transcriptional repressor leads to constitutive overexpression of two viral oncoproteins: E6 (recruits E6AP ubiquitin ligase to degrade p53, abolishing apoptosis/DNA arrest) and E7 (binds pRB to release E2F for S-phase entry while triggering strong compensatory diffuse block-positive p16^INK4a immunohistochemistry).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02909","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Gestational Trophoblastic Disease","q":"A 23-year-old pregnant woman at 13 weeks has uterine size larger than dates, hyperemesis gravidarum, serum beta-hCG \u003e200,000 mIU/mL, bilateral theca-lutein ovarian cysts, and a \u0027snowstorm\u0027 ultrasound without fetal parts. Evacuation yields diffuse grape-like edematous avascular villi with circumferential trophoblastic proliferation and absent fetal tissue. Which karyotype, p57 immunohistochemistry, and malignancy risk characterize this complete hydatidiform mole?","o":["45,X monosomy; elevated maternal AFP; zero risk of choriocarcinoma","69,XXY triploidy (dispermic fertilization of a normal ovum); positive p57^KIP2; fetal parts always present","46,XX (androgenetic diploidy from fertilization of an empty ovum lacking maternal DNA); negative p57^KIP2 staining (maternally imprinted/expressed gene absent); ~15-20% risk of persistent invasive mole and ~2% risk of choriocarcinoma","46,XY maternal-only parthenogenesis; absence of trophoblast proliferation; choriocarcinoma shows well-formed chorionic villi"],"a":2,"e":"A complete hydatidiform mole develops when an \u0027empty\u0027 ovum (lacking maternal nuclear chromosomes) is fertilized by a single sperm that duplicates (90% 46,XX) or two sperms (10% 46,XY)—resulting in purely paternal nuclear DNA. Consequently, p57^KIP2 (encoded by paternally imprinted, maternally expressed CDKN1C) is NEGATIVE in villous cytotrophoblasts and stroma. Diffuse hydropic swelling, absent fetal RBCs/parts, sky-high hCG, and marked circumferential trophoblastic hyperplasia carry ~15-20% risk of invasive mole and ~2% risk of choriocarcinoma (malignant biphasic cytotrophoblast + syncytiotrophoblast sheets completely LACKING chorionic villi, spreading hematogenously to lungs).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02910","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Prostatic Adenocarcinoma and Testicular Neoplasms","q":"A 72-year-old man presents with low back pain, elevated serum PSA (48 ng/mL), elevated alkaline phosphatase, and dense radiopaque (osteoblastic) lesions in the lumbar spine and pelvis. Needle core biopsy of a hard posterior peripheral-zone prostatic nodule shows crowded small back-to-back malignant glands with prominent nucleoli. Which two immunohistochemical findings definitively confirm invasive prostatic adenocarcinoma over benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)?","o":["Strong desmin and CD117 positivity with myoepithelial hyperplasia","Positive serum AFP and Schiller-Duval bodies in the anterior transition zone","Presence of an intact double-layered basal-and-luminal epithelium (strongly p63-positive) with corpora amylacea","Complete ABSENCE of basal cell layer (negative p63 and high-molecular-weight cytokeratin 34betaE12) alongside strong AMACR (P504S) positivity"],"a":3,"e":"Unlike benign prostatic hyperplasia (which arises in the inner periurethral transition zone and retains an intact outer basal cell layer), ~70-80% of prostatic adenocarcinomas arise in the posterior peripheral zone (palpable on digital rectal exam) and demonstrate loss of the basal myoepithelial layer—confirmed by negative p63 and 34betaE12 (HMWCK) immunostaining—paired with luminal overexpression of alpha-methylacyl-CoA racemase (AMACR/P504S) and prominent nucleoli. Graded by architectural patterns (Gleason score = primary + secondary pattern, reported in Grade Groups 1-5), prostate cancer classically produces osteoblastic (sclerotic) bone metastases in the axial skeleton via Batson paravertebral venous plexus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02911","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Calcineurin and mTOR Inhibitors","q":"A renal transplant recipient maintained on tacrolimus develops post-transplant diabetes mellitus and tremor, whereas another patient switched from cyclosporine avoids gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism. How do cyclosporine, tacrolimus, and sirolimus differ in their intracellular binding proteins and step of T-cell activation blocked?","o":["Cyclosporine binds cyclophilin and tacrolimus binds FKBP12 to inhibit calcineurin (blocking NFAT dephosphorylation and IL-2 transcription); sirolimus (rapamycin) binds FKBP12 to inhibit mTOR (blocking downstream IL-2 receptor-driven G1-to-S T-cell proliferation without nephrotoxicity)","Cyclosporine and tacrolimus inhibit mTOR directly; sirolimus inhibits calcineurin and causes severe nephrotoxicity","Sirolimus activates calcineurin to stimulate IL-2 synthesis; tacrolimus causes severe gingival hyperplasia","Cyclosporine inhibits IMP dehydrogenase; tacrolimus binds CD25 on IL-2 receptors"],"a":0,"e":"Calcineurin inhibitors block Signal 1-to-IL-2 gene transcription: cyclosporine binds cytosolic cyclophilin, whereas tacrolimus (FK506) binds FKBP12; both drug-immunophilin complexes inhibit the Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent phosphatase calcineurin, preventing dephosphorylation and nuclear translocation of NFAT—halting IL-2 transcription. Both share afferent arteriolar nephrotoxicity, hypertension, hyperkalemia, and neurotoxicity (tremor); however, cyclosporine uniquely causes gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism/hyperlipidemia, whereas tacrolimus causes higher rates of alopecia and new-onset diabetes mellitus. Sirolimus (and everolimus) also binds FKBP12, but inhibits mTOR rather than calcineurin—blocking IL-2-driven G1-\u003eS cell-cycle progression without calcineurin nephrotoxicity (though causing hyperlipidemia, thrombocytopenia, and impaired wound healing).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02912","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antiproliferative and Biologic Immunosuppressants","q":"Why does mycophenolate mofetil (MMF) selectively suppress T- and B-lymphocyte proliferation while largely sparing neutrophils, hepatocytes, and neurons, and what is the molecular target of basiliximab?","o":["MMF irreversibly alkylates DNA in all dividing cells; basiliximab blocks TNF-alpha","Mycophenolic acid reversibly inhibits inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase (IMPDH), blocking de novo guanosine nucleotide synthesis (lymphocytes lack an active purine salvage pathway and depend on de novo synthesis, unlike most somatic cells); basiliximab is a chimeric anti-CD25 (IL-2 receptor alpha-chain) monoclonal antibody","MMF activates adenosine deaminase; basiliximab blocks CTLA-4 to stimulate T cells","MMF inhibits thymidylate synthase solely; basiliximab inhibits calcineurin"],"a":1,"e":"Mycophenolate mofetil (prodrug hydrolyzed to mycophenolic acid) is a reversible, uncompetitive inhibitor of inosine-5\u0027-monophosphate dehydrogenase (IMPDH, specifically the inducible type II isoform in activated lymphocytes), halting de novo GMP/GTP synthesis. Because proliferating T and B cells depend almost exclusively on the de novo purine pathway—whereas most other cells use the HGPRT salvage pathway—MMF achieves lymphocyte-selective antiproliferation with minimal myelosuppression (chief toxicities: GI diarrhea, CMV risk, and teratogenicity). Basiliximab blocks CD25 (high-affinity IL-2Ralpha expressed only on activated T cells) for transplant induction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02913","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Class I Antiarrhythmic Drugs","q":"How do Class IA (procainamide/quinidine), Class IB (lidocaine/mexiletine), and Class IC (flecainide/propafenone) antiarrhythmics differ in their effects on ventricular action potential duration (APD) and effective refractory period (ERP), and why is lidocaine selectively effective in acute ischemic ventricular arrhythmias?","o":["All three Class I drugs act purely on AV nodal L-type Ca2+ channels without affecting Phase 0","IA shortens APD; IB prolongs QT interval severely; IC is first-line after acute myocardial infarction","IA prolongs APD/ERP (moderate Na+ block + K+ block); IB shortens APD (weakest Na+ block with fast unbinding, preferentially binding inactivated Na+ channels in depolarized ischemic myocardium); IC markedly slows Phase 0 conduction with minimal effect on APD (strongest use-dependent Na+ block, contraindicated in structural/post-MI heart disease)","IB has the slowest channel unbinding kinetics and widest QRS prolongation at resting heart rates"],"a":2,"e":"Class I antiarrhythmics block fast voltage-gated Na+ channels (decreasing Phase 0 upstroke slope Vmax) with distinct dissociation kinetics and K+ channel overlap: (1) Class IA (quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide) moderately slows Phase 0 and blocks K+ channels—prolonging APD, ERP, and QT interval (risk of torsades, plus procainamide lupus and quinidine cinchonism); (2) Class IB (lidocaine, mexiletine) unbinds rapidly (\u003c0.5 s) and blocks residual Na+ current, slightly shortening APD while selectively blocking inactivated Na+ channels in depolarized/ischemic ventricular tissue; (3) Class IC (flecainide, propafenone) unbinds very slowly, maximally slowing His-Purkinje conduction (widening QRS) without changing APD—and increases fatal arrhythmias in structural/post-MI hearts (CAST trial).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02914","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Supraventricular Antiarrhythmics","q":"A 29-year-old woman presents with regular narrow-complex paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVT) at 190/min unresponsive to carotid sinus massage. Rapid IV push of 6 mg adenosine (followed by saline flush) produces 4 seconds of transient AV block and restores normal sinus rhythm, accompanied by brief chest tightness and flushing. What is adenosine\u0027s cellular mechanism at the AV node, and how do theophylline and dipyridamole alter its effect?","o":["Inhibits Na+/K+-ATPase; requires slow 60-minute IV infusion to avoid Red Man syndrome","Activates Gs-coupled beta-1 receptors; has a plasma half-life of 12 hours","Blocks fast Na+ channels permanently; potentiated by theophylline and antagonized by dipyridamole","Activates Gi-coupled A1 purinergic receptors, opening I_KACh K+ efflux channels (hyperpolarizing AV nodal cells) and inhibiting L-type Ca2+ influx; antagonized by methylxanthines (theophylline/caffeine) and potentiated by dipyridamole (which blocks nucleoside uptake)"],"a":3,"e":"Endogenous purine nucleoside adenosine is the drug of choice for terminating AV nodal reentrant tachycardia (AVNRT/PSVT). Binding A1 Gi-coupled receptors on SA/AV nodal cells activates inward-rectifier K+ channels (hyperpolarizing the membrane) and lowers cAMP to suppress L-type Ca2+ currents—transiently halting AV nodal conduction. Because RBCs and endothelium clear adenosine via equilibrative nucleoside transporters and adenosine deaminase within \u003c10 seconds, it must be injected as a rapid proximal IV bolus with saline flush. Theophylline/caffeine competitively block A1 receptors (blunting adenosine), whereas dipyridamole inhibits cellular adenosine uptake (requiring drastic adenosine dose reduction to avoid prolonged asystole).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02915","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Parenteral Anticoagulants and HIT","q":"A 60-year-old man receiving intravenous unfractionated heparin (UFH) for deep vein thrombosis develops a \u003e50% drop in platelet count on Day 7 alongside new arterial limb thrombosis. How do UFH, enoxaparin (LMWH), and fondaparinux differ in their antithrombin-mediated inhibition of Factor IIa vs Factor Xa, and what is the immediate management of this heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT)?","o":["UFH (~15 kDa, \u003e=18 saccharides) bridges antithrombin to inhibit BOTH Factor IIa (thrombin) and Factor Xa equally (monitored by aPTT, fully reversed by protamine); LMWH (~5 kDa) predominantly inhibits Factor Xa and fondaparinux (pentasaccharide) inhibits Factor Xa exclusively; in HIT (IgG antibodies against PF4-heparin complexes activating platelets), stop ALL heparins immediately and start a non-heparin direct thrombin inhibitor (argatroban/bivalirudin) or fondaparinux","UFH inhibits Factor Xa exclusively while fondaparinux inhibits thrombin directly; in HIT, switch UFH immediately to enoxaparin or start warfarin monotherapy","LMWH is monitored routinely by INR; in HIT, give platelet transfusions and protamine sulfate","Fondaparinux requires \u003e=18 saccharide chains to bind thrombin; in HIT, restart low-dose UFH"],"a":0,"e":"Heparin\u0027s unique pentasaccharide sequence binds antithrombin III (AT-III), inducing a conformational change that accelerates Factor Xa inactivation ~1,000-fold. However, simultaneous inactivation of Factor IIa (thrombin) requires a longer heparin chain (\u003e=18 saccharide units) to physically bridge AT-III and thrombin: UFH contains long chains (1:1 anti-Xa:anti-IIa), shorter LMWHs inhibit Xa \u003e IIa (~3-4:1, anti-Xa assay if needed, partially reversed by protamine), and synthetic pentasaccharide fondaparinux inhibits Xa ONLY (zero IIa block, not reversed by protamine). Immune-mediated HIT Type II involves IgG antibodies binding platelet factor 4 (PF4)-heparin complexes, triggering intense platelet activation and paradoxical arterial/venous thrombosis—requiring immediate cessation of ALL heparins (including flushes/LMWH) and initiation of argatroban, bivalirudin, or fondaparinux (avoiding early warfarin alone due to Protein C depletion causing venous limb gangrene).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02916","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Direct Oral Anticoagulants (DOACs) and Reversal Agents","q":"Which pairing accurately matches an oral direct anticoagulant with its molecular target and specific FDA/CDSCO-approved reversal agent for life-threatening hemorrhage?","o":["Dabigatran: direct reversible thrombin (Factor IIa) inhibitor -\u003e reversed by idarucizumab (humanized Fab fragment); Apixaban / Rivaroxaban: direct Factor Xa inhibitors -\u003e reversed by andexanet alfa (recombinant modified Factor Xa decoy protein)","Apixaban: indirect antithrombin activator -\u003e reversed by idarucizumab; Dabigatran: GPIIb/IIIa inhibitor -\u003e reversed by tranexamic acid","Rivaroxaban: direct Factor IIa inhibitor -\u003e reversed by fresh frozen plasma alone; Dabigatran: irreversible Factor XII inhibitor","Dabigatran: direct Factor Xa inhibitor -\u003e reversed by protamine sulfate; Rivaroxaban: vitamin K antagonist -\u003e reversed by vitamin K1"],"a":0,"e":"Direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs) act directly without requiring antithrombin or vitamin K depletion, offering predictable pharmacokinetics without routine INR/aPTT monitoring: (1) Dabigatran etexilate (oral prodrug, ~80% renal clearance) directly inhibits both free and clot-bound thrombin (Factor IIa) and is specifically reversed within minutes by idarucizumab (monoclonal Fab binding dabigatran with ~350-fold higher affinity than thrombin); (2) Rivaroxaban, apixaban, and edoxaban directly inhibit Factor Xa (\u0027-xaban\u0027) and are specifically reversed by andexanet alfa (catalytically inactive recombinant Factor Xa decoy that sequesters Xa inhibitors, or 4-factor PCC when unavailable).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02917","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antiplatelet Drugs","q":"A patient undergoing percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) with drug-eluting stent placement receives dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin plus a P2Y12 ADP receptor antagonist). How does ticagrelor differ pharmacologically from clopidogrel and prasugrel, and what final common step of platelet aggregation do abciximab, eptifibatide, and tirofiban block?","o":["Clopidogrel is reversible with a 2-hour duration; eptifibatide inhibits platelet phosphodiesterase-3","Ticagrelor is a direct-acting, REVERSIBLE P2Y12 antagonist requiring NO hepatic CYP activation (unlike clopidogrel and prasugrel, which are prodrugs irreversibly blocking P2Y12, with clopidogrel dependent on CYP2C19); abciximab/eptifibatide/tirofiban block platelet GPIIb/IIIa (alphaIIb-beta3 integrin) receptors to prevent fibrinogen cross-linking","Ticagrelor requires CYP2C19 bioactivation and irreversibly acetylates COX-1; abciximab blocks vWF binding to GPIb","Prasugrel blocks thrombin PAR-1 receptors; tirofiban enhances prostacyclin synthesis"],"a":1,"e":"Thienopyridines (clopidogrel, prasugrel) are oral prodrugs requiring hepatic CYP activation (clopidogrel relies heavily on two-step CYP2C19 activation—impaired in CYP2C19*2/*3 poor metabolizers or by omeprazole) whose active metabolites irreversibly bind platelet P2Y12 ADP receptors for the platelet lifespan. In contrast, ticagrelor (and IV cangrelor) binds a distinct site on P2Y12 REVERSIBLY without requiring hepatic metabolic activation (can cause transient adenosine-like dyspnea/bradycardia). Downstream, all agonists converge on activating GPIIb/IIIa receptors to bind bivalent fibrinogen bridges—blocked directly by abciximab (Fab fragment), eptifibatide, and tirofiban.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02918","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antiepileptic Pharmacokinetics and Toxicities","q":"Assertion (A): Increasing a patient\u0027s daily maintenance dose of phenytoin modestly (e.g., from 300 mg/day to 400 mg/day) can produce a disproportionately large jump in plasma phenytoin concentration and precipitate cerebellar nystagmus and ataxia. Reason (R): Hepatic CYP2C9/2C19 hydroxylation of phenytoin is capacity-limited and saturates near the therapeutic plasma range (10-20 mcg/mL), shifting elimination from first-order to zero-order (Michaelis-Menten) kinetics.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Phenytoin blocks inactivated neuronal voltage-gated Na+ channels and follows capacity-limited Michaelis-Menten elimination: at low subtherapeutic plasma levels it exhibits first-order kinetics, but because its Km (~4-6 mcg/mL) lies near/below the therapeutic window (10-20 mcg/mL), hepatic CYP2C9/2C19 enzymes approach saturation. Once saturated (zero-order kinetics), clearance drops as concentration rises, so small dose increments cause steep, unpredictable spikes into toxicity (horizontal nystagmus, diplopia, ataxia, dysarthria; chronic gingival hyperplasia, coarsened facies, hirsutism, osteomalacia, megaloblastic anemia, and fetal hydantoin syndrome). Conversely, levetiracetam binds synaptic vesicle protein SV2A with linear kinetics and minimal drug interactions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02919","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antiepileptic Drugs","q":"An 8-year-old girl has 20-30 daily episodes of 10-second blank staring spells with lip smacking, no post-ictal confusion, and generalized 3-Hz spike-and-wave discharges on EEG triggered by hyperventilation. Which drug selectively inhibits thalamic T-type Ca2+ channels as first-line monotherapy for uncomplicated childhood absence epilepsy, and why is valproate avoided in women of childbearing age unless alternatives fail?","o":["Diazepam daily oral monotherapy; valproate causes SIADH exclusively","Phenytoin; valproate causes pure gingival hyperplasia only","Ethosuximide (selective thalamic T-type Ca2+ channel blocker); sodium valproate carries the highest antiepileptic teratogenic risk (neural tube defects such as spina bifida, craniofacial defects, and neurodevelopmental impairment) plus hepatotoxicity/pancreatitis","Carbamazepine (exacerbates absence seizures); valproate has zero teratogenicity"],"a":2,"e":"Typical childhood absence seizures arise from rhythmic thalamocortical oscillations driven by low-threshold T-type Ca2+ currents in thalamic relay neurons. Ethosuximide selectively blocks thalamic T-type Ca2+ channels and is first-line monotherapy for pure absence seizures (valproate is used if generalized tonic-clonic seizures coexist, as ethosuximide lacks efficacy for GTCS). Crucially, Na+-channel blockers like carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, and phenytoin can WORSEN absence/myoclonic seizures. Sodium valproate blocks Na+ channels, T-type Ca2+ channels, and inhibits GABA transaminase/HDAC, but carries the highest teratogenicity among ASMs (~10% major congenital malformations, especially neural tube defects, and lowered childhood IQ) plus idiosyncratic fatal hepatotoxicity (especially \u003c2 years old) and hyperammonemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02920","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Non-Linear Pharmacokinetics of Phenytoin","q":"A patient taking phenytoin (100% oral bioavailability assumed) has an individual Vmax of 500 mg/day and Km of 4 mg/L. Using the Michaelis-Menten steady-state dosing equation Dose Rate = (Vmax x Css) / (Km + Css), what daily maintenance dose is required to achieve a target steady-state plasma concentration (Css) of 16 mg/L (versus 250 mg/day which gives Css = 4 mg/L)?","o":["1,000 mg/day","200 mg/day","500 mg/day","400 mg/day"],"a":3,"e":"Using Michaelis-Menten steady-state kinetics: Dose Rate R = (Vmax x Css) / (Km + Css). Substituting Vmax = 500 mg/day, Km = 4 mg/L, and target Css = 16 mg/L yields R = (500 x 16) / (4 + 16) = 8,000 / 20 = 400 mg/day. Notice that when Css = Km = 4 mg/L, Dose Rate = 250 mg/day; increasing the daily dose by just 60% (from 250 to 400 mg/day) quadruples steady-state plasma concentration (from 4 to 16 mg/L), demonstrating non-linear saturation kinetics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02921","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Newer Antiepileptic Drugs","q":"Which pairing accurately matches a newer antiseizure drug to its mechanism and signature adverse effect or drug interaction?","o":["Vigabatrin: irreversible inhibitor of GABA transaminase (GABA-T) causing permanent bilateral concentric visual field constriction; Topiramate: blocks Na+ channels/AMPA/kainate, enhances GABA-A, and weakly inhibits carbonic anhydrase causing nephrolithiasis, weight loss, and paresthesias; Lamotrigine: plasma levels double and SJS/TEN risk spikes when co-administered with valproate (UGT1A4 inhibitor)","Vigabatrin: GABA-A agonist causing weight gain only; Topiramate: causes severe obesity and diabetes insipidus; Lamotrigine: induced 3-fold by valproate","Tiagabine: activates glutamate NMDA receptors; Topiramate: irreversible retinal toxicity; Lamotrigine: zero risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Gabapentin: blocks presynaptic GABA-B receptors directly; Vigabatrin: causes nephrolithiasis"],"a":0,"e":"Each second-generation antiseizure drug has distinctive mechanisms and warnings: (1) Vigabatrin (used in infantile spasms / West syndrome, especially tuberous sclerosis) irreversibly inhibits GABA transaminase, raising synaptic GABA but causing irreversible retinal toxicity (bilateral concentric visual field constriction in ~30% of long-term users, requiring baseline/periodic perimetry); (2) Topiramate inhibits carbonic anhydrase alongside Na+/AMPA/GABA actions, causing metabolic acidosis, calcium phosphate kidney stones, paresthesias, cognitive slowing (\u0027word-finding\u0027), acute angle-closure glaucoma, and weight loss; (3) Lamotrigine (Na+-channel blocker) undergoes UGT1A4 glucuronidation—valproate inhibits UGT1A4, doubling lamotrigine half-life/levels and sharply increasing life-threatening Stevens-Johnson syndrome/TEN unless titrated slowly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02922","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Clostridial Neurotoxins","q":"Both Clostridium tetani (tetanospasmin) and Clostridium botulinum (botulinum toxin) produce zinc-dependent metalloprotease neurotoxins that cleave SNARE vesicle-fusion proteins, yet tetanus causes spastic paralysis (trismus, risus sardonicus, opisthotonos) whereas botulism causes descending symmetric flaccid paralysis. What explains this opposite clinical outcome?","o":["Tetanospasmin undergoes retrograde axonal transport up motor neurons to CNS spinal Renshaw interneurons and cleaves synaptobrevin (VAMP), blocking inhibitory GABA and glycine release; botulinum toxin acts locally at peripheral cholinergic neuromuscular junctions (cleaving SNAP-25/synaptobrevin/syntaxin) to block excitatory acetylcholine release","Tetanospasmin blocks postsynaptic skeletal muscle nicotinic receptors; botulinum toxin activates spinal glycine receptors","Tetanospasmin stimulates excessive acetylcholine release at the NMJ; botulinum toxin enters the CNS to inhibit glutamate","Both toxins cleave postsynaptic dystrophin directly inside muscle sarcoplasm"],"a":0,"e":"Both neurotoxins are A-B zinc endopeptidases targeting SNARE proteins essential for synaptic vesicle exocytosis, but they localize to opposite synapses: (1) Tetanospasmin enters peripheral motor nerve terminals and travels via dynein-mediated retrograde axonal transport across the synapse into spinal cord inhibitory interneurons (Renshaw cells), where it cleaves synaptobrevin (VAMP) to abolish inhibitory glycine and GABA release—leaving alpha motor neurons disinhibited and firing continuously (spastic trismus/lockjaw, opisthotonos); (2) Botulinum toxin remains at peripheral cholinergic nerve terminals (cranial nerves -\u003e limbs -\u003e diaphragm), cleaving SNAP-25, syntaxin, or synaptobrevin to prevent excitatory acetylcholine release—causing descending symmetric flaccid paralysis without sensory loss or fever.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02923","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Clostridium perfringens (Gas Gangrene)","q":"A patient with a contaminated crush injury of the thigh develops painful crepitant myonecrosis with Gram-positive boxcar-shaped bacilli lacking visible spores in tissue smears. On laboratory media, anaerobic blood agar shows a double zone of hemolysis (inner complete beta zone + outer partial zone), litmus milk shows a \u0027stormy clot\u0027 fermentation, and half of an egg-yolk agar plate coated with anti-alpha-toxin antitoxin shows complete inhibition of opalescence (positive Nagler reaction). Which toxin mediates the Nagler reaction?","o":["Iota-toxin (ADP-ribosylating binary toxin)","Alpha-toxin (phospholipase C / lecithinase), which hydrolyzes egg-yolk lecithin into insoluble opaque phosphorylcholine and diglyceride and lyses RBC/muscle membranes","Theta-toxin (perfringolysin O) exclusively","Epsilon-toxin (pore-forming enterotoxin)"],"a":1,"e":"Clostridium perfringens (capsule+, non-motile, subterminal spores rarely seen in clinical specimens) is the principal cause of gas gangrene (clostridial myonecrosis). Its major lethal virulence factor is alpha-toxin—a zinc metallophospholipase C (lecithinase) that cleaves membrane phosphatidylcholine (lecithin) and sphingomyelin, causing massive myonecrosis, hemolysis, and microvascular thrombosis. In the diagnostic Nagler reaction, streaking C. perfringens across an egg-yolk agar plate with anti-alpha-toxin serum spread on one half produces an opaque pearly precipitate around colonies on the untreated half and complete clearing/inhibition on the antitoxin half. Perfringolysin O (theta-toxin) produces the inner clear zone of the classic \u0027target\u0027 double-zone hemolysis on blood agar.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02924","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Non-Spore-Forming Anaerobes","q":"A 36-year-old woman with a forgotten intrauterine contraceptive device (IUCD) for 6 years develops a slow-growing indurated pelvic mass with draining cutaneous sinus tracts discharging gritty yellow \u0027sulfur granules\u0027. Crush smear shows Gram-positive filamentous branching rods that are NON-acid-fast. Which anaerobic pathogen is present, and how is it treated compared with Bacteroides fragilis intra-abdominal abscess?","o":["Nocardia asteroides (obligate aerobe, partially acid-fast, treated with TMP-SMX); Bacteroides fragilis is treated with gentamicin monotherapy","Clostridium septicum; treated with metronidazole only for 3 days","Actinomyces israelii (treated with high-dose prolonged penicillin G or amoxicillin); Bacteroides fragilis is an obligate anaerobic Gram-negative bacillus (bile-resistant, catalase-positive, capsulated) treated with metronidazole or beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitors","Actinomyces israelii (treated with vancomycin + aminoglycoside); Bacteroides fragilis is acid-fast positive"],"a":2,"e":"Actinomyces israelii is a normal oral/GI/female genital commensal anaerobic Gram-positive filamentous branching bacterium (NOT a fungus, despite forming \u0027ray-fungus\u0027 colonies and yellow \u0027sulfur granules\u0027 of mineralized bacterial aggregates) that causes cervicofacial (\u0027lumpy jaw\u0027), thoracic, or IUCD-associated pelvic abscesses with sinus tracts breaching tissue planes; it is non-acid-fast and responds to prolonged penicillin G/amoxicillin (unlike Nocardia, which is an obligate aerobe, weakly acid-fast on 1% H2SO4 modified ZN, and treated with TMP-SMX). Bacteroides fragilis—the predominant colonic anaerobe—possesses an antiphagocytic polysaccharide capsule causing intra-abdominal abscesses and resists penicillin via beta-lactamase, requiring metronidazole, piperacillin-tazobactam, or carbapenems.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02925","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Borrelia Species (Lyme Disease and Relapsing Fever)","q":"How do Borrelia burgdorferi (Lyme disease) and Borrelia recurrentis (epidemic relapsing fever) differ in their arthropod vectors, microscopic visibility, and mechanism of recurrent febrile cycles?","o":["B. burgdorferi is transmitted by Ixodes (deer/hard) ticks (causing erythema migrans, Bell palsy, heart block, arthritis; cultured in Barbour-Stoenner-Kelly BSK medium); B. recurrentis is transmitted by human body louse Pediculus humanus corporis and causes cyclical fever spikes via programmed gene-conversion antigenic variation of surface Variable Major Proteins (Vmp); both large loose spirochetes are visible on Giemsa/Wright smears","B. burgdorferi is transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes; B. recurrentis is invisible on light microscopy and requires dark-field exclusively","B. burgdorferi uses antigenic variation of Vmp to cause weekly relapses; B. recurrentis causes targetoid erythema migrans via Ixodes ticks","Both are strictly obligate intracellular bacteria requiring McCoy cell culture and transmitted by sandflies"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike thin Treponema and Leptospira spirochetes (which require dark-field or silver stains), Borrelia species are large, loosely coiled spirochetes easily visualized on routine Giemsa/Wright-stained blood smears. B. burgdorferi is transmitted by Ixodes scapularis/ricinus hard ticks (requiring \u003e24-36 hr attachment; mouse/deer reservoir), grows in BSK medium, and causes early localized erythema migrans (\u0027bull\u0027s-eye\u0027 rash) -\u003e early disseminated bilateral Bell palsy/AV block -\u003e late oligoarthritis (treated with doxycycline/ceftriaxone). B. recurrentis is transmitted when crushed Pediculus humanus corporis body lice contaminate abraded skin, escaping neutralizing IgM repeatedly by switching surface Vmp lipoproteins through plasmid gene conversion (each switch producing a new febrile relapse).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02926","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Vincent Angina and Non-Venereal Treponematoses","q":"A malnourished patient with poor oral hygiene presents with painful bleeding necrotic gingival ulcers covered by a grayish pseudomembrane and foul halitosis (Vincent angina / acute necrotizing ulcerative gingivitis). Which synergistic organisms are seen on Gram/carbol fuchsin stain, and which Treponema subspecies causes non-venereal tropical Yaws?","o":["Leptospira biflexa plus Actinomyces; Borrelia duttonii causes Yaws","Streptococcus mutans alone; Treponema pallidum subsp. pallidum causes Pinta","Treponema carateum plus Prevotella; Treponema pallidum subsp. endemicum causes Yaws","Borrelia vincentii (spirochetes) plus Fusobacterium nucleatum (spindle-shaped fusiform Gram-negative bacilli); Treponema pallidum subsp. pertenue causes Yaws (skin-to-skin contact)"],"a":3,"e":"Vincent angina (trench mouth / acute necrotizing ulcerative gingivitis) is a classic fusospirochetal anaerobic symbiosis between spirochetes (Borrelia vincentii) and tapering spindle-shaped Gram-negative anaerobes (Fusobacterium nucleatum), readily seen on crystal violet or dilute carbol fuchsin smear and treated with penicillin/metronidazole plus oral debridement. Endemic non-venereal treponematoses—morphologically and serologically indistinguishable from T. pallidum subsp. pallidum (syphilis) on VDRL/TPHA—include Yaws (T. pallidum subsp. pertenue: raspberry-like papillomas and bone lesions, eliminated in India), Endemic syphilis/Bejel (subsp. endemicum), and Pinta (Treponema carateum: dyschromic skin macules only).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02927","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Spotted Fever and Typhus Group Rickettsiae","q":"A hiker presents with fever, severe headache, and a maculopapular-to-petechial rash that began on his wrists and ankles and spread centripetally to his trunk, palms, and soles after a tick bite. How does this presentation (Rocky Mountain spotted fever) differ in vector, rash distribution, and Weil-Felix reaction from epidemic typhus (Rickettsia prowazekii) and Q fever (Coxiella burnetii)?","o":["R. rickettsii is transmitted by Dermacentor (hard) ticks with centripetal rash involving palms/soles (OX-19/OX-2 positive); R. prowazekii is transmitted by human body louse Pediculus humanus corporis with centrifugal trunk-outward rash sparing palms/soles (OX-19 positive, capable of Brill-Zinsser recrudescence); Coxiella burnetii is transmitted via cattle/sheep birth-fluid aerosols without a rash or arthropod vector (Weil-Felix negative)","R. rickettsii is transmitted by fleas with centrifugal rash sparing palms/soles; Coxiella burnetii causes severe palmar petechiae via ticks","R. prowazekii is transmitted by chiggers with OX-K positivity; R. rickettsii is Weil-Felix OX-K strongly positive only","All three require arthropod vectors and produce identical Weil-Felix OX-19 positivity"],"a":0,"e":"Rickettsiae are obligate intracellular bacteria that proliferate in endothelial cells causing systemic vasculitis (note: Indian tick typhus by R. conorii similarly uses Rhipicephalus ticks and causes tache noire eschar + OX-19/OX-2 positivity). R. rickettsii (RMSF) uses Dermacentor ticks and spreads centripetally from wrists/ankles to palms, soles, and trunk. R. prowazekii (epidemic typhus, louse feces rubbed into scratch) spreads centrifugally from trunk outward, sparing palms/soles/face, and can reactivate decades later as milder Brill-Zinsser disease. Coxiella burnetii (Q fever) forms spore-like small cell variants inhaled in livestock placental/milk aerosols—causing atypical pneumonia, granulomatous hepatitis, and culture-negative endocarditis WITHOUT rash or Weil-Felix positivity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02928","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Ehrlichiosis and Anaplasmosis","q":"A patient with fever, headache, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and elevated transaminases 10 days after a tick bite (without a rash) has a Giemsa-stained peripheral blood buffy-coat smear showing mulberry-like intracytoplasmic bacterial microcolonies (\u0027morulae\u0027) inside MONOCYTES. Which pathogen and tick vector cause this infection, and which leukocyte hosts Anaplasma phagocytophilum morulae?","o":["Ehrlichia chaffeensis inside eosinophils; Babesia microti forms Maltese cross tetrads inside neutrophils","Ehrlichia chaffeensis (human monocytic ehrlichiosis, transmitted by Amblyomma americanum lone star tick); Anaplasma phagocytophilum forms morulae inside NEUTROPHILS (granulocytes, transmitted by Ixodes ticks)","Anaplasma phagocytophilum inside monocytes; Ehrlichia chaffeensis inside erythrocytes","Rickettsia prowazekii forms morulae inside platelets; transmitted by Xenopsylla fleas"],"a":1,"e":"Ehrlichia and Anaplasma are obligate intracellular Anaplasmataceae that replicate inside membrane-bound phagosomal vacuoles of circulating leukocytes, forming characteristic basophilic mulberry-shaped clusters called \u0027morulae\u0027 (distinct from Rickettsia, which escape vacuoles to multiply freely in endothelial cytosol). Ehrlichia chaffeensis (transmitted by Amblyomma americanum) infects MONOCYTES/macrophages, whereas Anaplasma phagocytophilum (transmitted by Ixodes ticks—same vector as Lyme/Babesia) infects NEUTROPHILS/granulocytes. Both cause febrile illness with leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, elevated LFTs, typically no rash, negative Weil-Felix, and respond promptly to doxycycline.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02929","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Lymphatic Filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti vs Brugia malayi)","q":"A patient from coastal Andhra Pradesh with recurrent chyluria and unilateral lower-limb lymphedema has a midnight (10 PM-2 AM) Giemsa-stained thick blood smear showing sheathed microfilariae with smooth graceful curves, distinct discrete body nuclei, and a pointed tail tip COMPLETELY FREE of nuclei. How does this identify Wuchereria bancrofti versus Brugia malayi, and what endosymbiont bacterium is targeted by adjunctive doxycycline?","o":["Onchocerca volvulus (sheathed microfilariae in peripheral blood); Borrelia endosymbiont","Loa loa (African eye worm) has nocturnal periodicity; Chlamydia endosymbiont","Wuchereria bancrofti has a hyaline sheath and an empty tail tip free of nuclei (whereas Brugia malayi has kinky curves and TWO discrete terminal nuclei at the tail tip); intracellular Wolbachia endosymbiont (essential for filarial embryogenesis and adult worm survival)","Brugia malayi has an empty tail tip free of nuclei (whereas W. bancrofti is unsheathed); Rickettsia endosymbiont"],"a":2,"e":"Both Wuchereria bancrofti (transmitted by Culex quinquefasciatus) and Brugia malayi (transmitted by Mansonia) exhibit nocturnal periodicity (microfilariae peak in peripheral blood around midnight, or 45 min after daytime 100 mg diethylcarbamazine [DEC] provocation). Microscopically, W. bancrofti microfilariae are sheathed with smooth curves, discrete somatic nuclei, and a tapered tail tip devoid of nuclei; B. malayi microfilariae are smaller, kinky, and display two characteristic terminal/subterminal tail nuclei. Most filarial nematodes (Wuchereria, Brugia, Onchocerca) harbor obligate intracellular Wolbachia bacteria—doxycycline kills Wolbachia, sterilizing and slowly killing adult worms alongside MDA (DEC + albendazole + ivermectin).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02930","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Cestodes (Echinococcus granulosus and Diphyllobothrium latum)","q":"A 35-year-old shepherd presents with right upper quadrant dull pain and a 9-cm hepatic cyst showing peripheral eggshell calcification and daughter cysts (\u0027spoke-wheel\u0027 / \u0027water-lily\u0027 sign) on ultrasound/CT. Why is blind percutaneous diagnostic aspiration of this cyst traditionally contraindicated without albendazole/scolicidal precaution, and which definitive host sheds the infective eggs?","o":["Spillage of antigenic hydatid fluid and protoscolices (\u0027hydatid sand\u0027) can trigger fatal Type I IgE-mediated anaphylactic shock and secondary peritoneal seeding; dogs (canids) are the definitive host (sheep/humans are intermediate hosts)","Cyst aspiration triggers acute biliary obstruction by Diphyllobothrium proglottids; freshwater copepods are the definitive host","Hydatid fluid causes acute hyperammonemic encephalopathy; pigs are the definitive host","Cyst fluid contains neurotoxic botulinum spores; cats are the definitive host"],"a":0,"e":"Echinococcus granulosus (dog tapeworm) uses dogs/canids as definitive hosts (harboring tiny 3-6 mm adult worms in intestine) and sheep/cattle (and accidental dead-end humans) as intermediate hosts upon ingesting eggs from dog feces. Oncospheres penetrate the gut and develop slowly into unilocular hydatid cysts (most often right hepatic lobe, then lung) composed of an outer acellular laminated membrane, inner nucleated germinal layer producing brood capsules and free protoscolices (\u0027hydatid sand\u0027), and host pericyst. Unprotected cyst rupture or needle puncture leaks highly allergenic hydatid fluid—risking fatal anaphylactic shock and disseminated peritoneal echinococcosis (hence PAIR therapy uses albendazole coverage and hypertonic saline/ethanol scolicidal instillation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02931","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Trematodes (Flukes)","q":"A young traveler returning from freshwater swimming in East Africa presents with painless terminal hematuria. Centrifuged urine microscopy reveals large non-operculated oval eggs (~150 x 60 micrometers) bearing a prominent TERMINAL spine. How does this species (Schistosoma haematobium) differ in egg morphology, venous plexus habitat, and malignancy association from Schistosoma mansoni and Clonorchis sinensis?","o":["S. haematobium passes lateral-spined eggs in stool causing colon cancer; S. mansoni causes bladder transitional cell carcinoma","S. haematobium passes operculated eggs ingested via raw crabs; Paragonimus westermani causes bladder cancer","All Schistosoma species are acquired by ingesting metacercariae encysted on watercress and are treated with albendazole","S. haematobium inhabits the vesical/pelvic venous plexus, passes terminal-spined eggs in urine, and predisposes to squamous cell carcinoma of the urinary bladder; S. mansoni inhabits the inferior mesenteric veins passing lateral-spined eggs in stool (causing periportal Pipestem fibrosis); Clonorchis sinensis passes small operculated flask-shaped eggs in stool and predisposes to cholangiocarcinoma"],"a":3,"e":"Unlike hermaphroditic liver/lung flukes (which produce operculated eggs and infect humans via ingested metacercariae), Schistosoma blood flukes are diecious (separate sexes) with non-operculated spined eggs and infect humans via skin penetration by fork-tailed cercariae from freshwater snails: (1) S. haematobium resides in vesical venous plexus -\u003e terminal-spined eggs in urine -\u003e chronic granulomatous cystitis, calcified bladder, and squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder; (2) S. mansoni resides in inferior mesenteric veins -\u003e prominent lateral-spined eggs in stool -\u003e Symmers clay-pipestem periportal fibrosis/portal hypertension; (3) S. japonicum -\u003e small round eggs with a rudimentary lateral knob; (4) Clonorchis sinensis (raw freshwater fish metacercariae) -\u003e operculated eggs and cholangiocarcinoma. Praziquantel is the drug of choice for all trematodes (except triclabendazole for Fasciola hepatica).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02932","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Intestinal Nematodes","q":"Assertion (A): In suspected Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm) infection causing nocturnal perianal pruritus in a child, routine stool microscopy for ova has very low sensitivity (\u003c5-10%), and an early-morning perianal cellophane tape (NIH swab) before bathing is the diagnostic method of choice. Reason (R): Gravid female pinworms migrate out of the anus onto perianal skin at night to deposit clusters of colorless, asymmetrical plano-convex (\u0027D-shaped\u0027) embryonated eggs that adhere to perianal folds rather than mixing uniformly in fecal matter.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Enterobius vermicularis (threadworm/pinworm) adult females migrate nocturnally out onto perianal skin to lay thousands of sticky, translucent, plano-convex (D-shaped) eggs containing coiled larvae (causing intense pruritus ani and direct finger-to-mouth autoinfection), then die. Because eggs are laid outside the lumen on perianal skin rather than inside feces, stool ova examination is frequently negative—requiring an early-morning transparent cellophane-tape / NIH swab pressed against perianal folds before washing/defecation. Contrast this with stool-ovum hallmarks of Ascaris lumbricoides (bile-stained oval fertilized eggs with thick mammillated albuminous coat; causes Loeffler eosinophilic pneumonitis during lung migration) and Trichuris trichiura (bile-stained barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar mucoid plugs; causes rectal prolapse).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02933","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Criminal Responsibility and Unsoundness of Mind","q":"A man with chronic schizophrenia kills his neighbor believing under an acute paranoid delusion that the neighbor is a demon about to murder him, and afterwards waits calmly beside the body without attempting to hide the weapon or flee. Another defendant with kleptomania steals an item during an \u0027irresistible impulse\u0027 while fully aware that stealing is illegal. Under Section 22 of BNS, 2023 (corresponding to former IPC Section 84, McNaughten Rule), how are these two defendants assessed legally?","o":["The first defendant meets legal insanity under BNS Section 22 (incapable of knowing the nature/wrongfulness of the act due to unsoundness of mind at the exact time of the act); the second defendant does NOT qualify because Indian law uses the cognitive McNaughten test and does NOT recognize \u0027irresistible impulse\u0027 alone as legal insanity","Both qualify automatically because any psychiatric diagnosis (medical insanity) confers full criminal immunity","Neither qualifies because killing under a delusion is always punished as culpable homicide","Only the kleptomania defendant qualifies under Durham\u0027s \u0027product rule\u0027 adopted in India"],"a":0,"e":"Section 22 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), 2023 (re-enacting IPC Section 84) codifies the 1843 McNaughten Rule: nothing is an offense done by a person who, at the time of doing it, by reason of unsoundness of mind, is incapable of knowing the nature of the act, or that he is doing what is either wrong or contrary to law. Medical insanity (having a psychiatric diagnosis) does NOT automatically equal legal insanity. Because BNS Section 22 tests purely cognitive impairment at the moment of the act, Indian criminal law does NOT accept \u0027irresistible impulse\u0027 (volitional impairment when the person knew the act was illegal) or Durham\u0027s product test as complete defenses.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02934","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Civil Responsibility, Testamentary Capacity, and MHCA 2017","q":"Which elements are required for valid testamentary capacity (making a valid will during a lucid interval or illness), and how does Section 115 of India\u0027s Mental Healthcare Act (MHCA), 2017 govern a patient brought to casualty after a non-fatal suicide attempt?","o":["Testamentary capacity requires a sound disposing mind (understanding the nature of making a will, extent of property, and rightful claims of dependents/heirs, free from insane delusions affecting disposition); MHCA Section 115 presumes severe stress in suicide attempts and shields the person from criminal trial/punishment while mandating care, treatment, and rehabilitation","Testamentary capacity requires the patient to be under age 18; MHCA Section 115 prohibits doctors from treating suicide survivors without Magistrate orders","Testamentary capacity requires continuous lifelong absence of any psychiatric history (lucid intervals invalid); MHCA Section 115 mandates 1 year imprisonment for attempted suicide","Testamentary capacity depends solely on physical ability to write; MHCA Section 115 replaces BNS Section 22 completely"],"a":0,"e":"For a valid will (testamentary capacity, Indian Succession Act), the testator must be an adult of sound disposing mind at the time of execution—even during a genuine \u0027lucid interval\u0027 in mental illness or on a deathbed—capable of understanding: (1) the nature and effect of a will, (2) the nature and extent of their property, and (3) the natural objects of bounty/relatives (and free from delusions influencing property distribution; a blind/aphasic person can still make a valid will by signs/mark if compos mentis). Under Section 115 of the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017, any person attempting suicide is presumed to be under severe stress and shall NOT be tried or punished under penal law (overriding old IPC 309), with the state obligated to provide care and rehabilitation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02935","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Battered Baby Syndrome (Caffey Syndrome) and Child Abandonment","q":"A 10-month-old infant is brought with drowsiness and seizures following a \u0027fall off a low sofa\u0027. Examination shows retinal hemorrhages, patterned bruises on the back/thighs, and a skeletal survey revealing healing posterior rib fractures, acute spiral humeral fracture, and classic metaphyseal corner (\u0027bucket-handle\u0027) fractures of the distal femur alongside acute-on-chronic subdural hematomas on CT. Which diagnosis explains this discrepancy between history and findings?","o":["Osteogenesis imperfecta alone (classic triad includes acute posterior rib and metaphyseal bucket-handle fractures with normal sclerae and patterned back bruises)","Non-accidental injury / Battered Baby Syndrome (Caffey syndrome) with Shaken Baby Syndrome (subdural hematoma + retinal hemorrhages + encephalopathy) and pathognomonic fractures of varying ages","Physiological Mongolian spots with benign infantile subdural hygroma","Congenital syphilis (Wimberger sign only without subdural/retinal bleeding)"],"a":1,"e":"Battered Baby Syndrome (non-accidental physical child abuse, described by Kempe/Caffey) is diagnosed when caregivers give a vague or inconsistent history (e.g., trivial low fall) that cannot explain severe multi-age injuries: fractures in different stages of healing (\u0027signature\u0027 metaphyseal corner/bucket-handle avulsions from violent yanking/twisting, posterior rib fractures from thoracic squeezing, spiral long-bone fractures in non-ambulatory infants), patterned bruises in protected areas (ears, torso, buttocks), and cigarette burns. Violent shaking adds the classic Abusive Head Trauma / Shaken Baby triad: subdural hemorrhage (torn bridging veins), bilateral multilayered retinal hemorrhages, and hypoxic/traumatic encephalopathy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02936","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Forensic Bloodstain Identification","q":"In a forensic serology laboratory examining a dried reddish-brown stain on a suspect\u0027s knife, Test 1 (phenolphthalein + H2O2) turns bright pink immediately; Test 2 (heated sodium hydroxide + pyridine + glucose reagent) yields pink feathery crystals under the microscope; and Test 3 uses anti-human serum in agar gel diffusion (Ouchterlony double diffusion) showing a crisp precipitin line. Identify Tests 1, 2, and 3 and their diagnostic specificity.","o":["Test 1: Luminol test exclusively; Test 2: Florence choline crystal test; Test 3: ABO blood grouping alone","Test 1: Teichmann test; Test 2: Kastle-Meyer test; Test 3: Acid phosphatase test","Test 1: Kastle-Meyer presumptive catalytic test (heme peroxidase-like activity); Test 2: Takayama hemochromogen confirmatory crystal test (superior to Teichmann hemin crystal test for old/heated stains); Test 3: Precipitin test confirming HUMAN species origin","Test 1: Benzidine confirmatory test for human DNA; Test 2: Barberio test; Test 3: Spectroscopic carboxyhemoglobin test"],"a":2,"e":"Forensic bloodstain analysis follows a strict hierarchy: (1) Presumptive screening tests exploit the peroxidase-like activity of hemoglobin\u0027s heme group to oxidize colorless dyes with H2O2 (Kastle-Meyer phenolphthalein turns pink; benzidine/orthotolidine turn blue; luminol emits blue-green chemiluminescence in darkness for washed stains—sensitive but can cross-react with plant peroxidases/bleach/copper); (2) Confirmatory microcrystal tests prove blood by forming characteristic heme derivatives—Teichmann (glacial acetic acid + NaCl -\u003e dark brown rhombic hemin crystals) and Takayama (pyridine + NaOH + glucose -\u003e pink feathery pyridine-hemochromogen crystals, more reliable on aged/laundered stains); (3) Species origin (human vs animal) is established by anti-human precipitin (ring/Ouchterlony gel diffusion) or immunochromatographic glycophorin A assays before STR DNA profiling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02937","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Forensic Hair Examination and DNA Profiling","q":"A single telogen hair shaft WITHOUT a follicular root bulb/sheath is recovered from the victim\u0027s clenched hand at a crime scene. Microscopically, the hair shows thin, narrow, discontinuous medulla occupying ~20% of shaft diameter (medullary index 0.20) and flattened imbricated cuticular scales. Does this indicate human or animal hair, and which DNA analysis succeeds on a rootless hair shaft?","o":["Human hair; autosomal STR PCR requires no nuclear DNA and yields unique paternal/maternal profiles from a dead keratinized shaft","Animal hair; ABO agglutination of hair shaft keratin confirms species","Animal hair (dogs/cattle have medullary index \u003c0.30); Y-STR nuclear DNA succeeds best on a rootless shaft","Human hair (human medullary index \u003c0.30 with fine uniform cortex pigment and flattened imbricated cuticle, whereas animal hair typically has medullary index \u003e0.50 and coronal/spinous scales); mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) sequencing succeeds on rootless shafts because keratinocytes contain abundant mtDNA despite lacking nuclear DNA"],"a":3,"e":"Microscopic trichology distinguishes human from non-human hair: Human hair has a narrow/absent medulla (medullary index = medulla diameter / shaft diameter \u003c0.30, usually \u003c1/3), broad cortex with fine pigment granules denser peripherally, and flattened overlapping (imbricated) cuticular scales; most animals show a wide continuous/vacuolated medulla (medullary index \u003e0.50), thin cortex with central pigment, and spinous or coronal scales. Because shed telogen hair shafts lack living nucleated root cells, nuclear autosomal STR and Y-STR DNA profiling usually fail; instead, high-copy-number maternal mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA hypervariable regions HVI/HVII) extracted from keratinized shafts permits maternal-lineage comparison.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02938","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Witnesses, Subpoena, and Perjury","q":"When a forensic medical doctor testifies in court regarding autopsy findings and gives professional opinion on cause of death, what category of witness is the doctor, what happens if the doctor willfully suppresses truth under oath, and which Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), 2023 sections define and punish giving false evidence (perjury, replacing IPC 191/193)?","o":["Expert witness (giving both observed facts and professional opinion; also acts as common witness for clinical facts observed); willful false statement under oath constitutes perjury, defined under BNS Section 227 and punished under BNS Section 229 (up to 7 years imprisonment + fine in judicial proceedings)","Common witness strictly barred from expressing any medical opinion; perjury is punished under BNS Section 106","Hostile witness automatically; perjury carries zero criminal liability for doctors","Dying declarant; perjury is defined under BSA Section 26(a)"],"a":0,"e":"A doctor summoned via subpoena (summons, with conduct money for civil cases; priority over non-emergency duties) serves dual roles: as a common witness for facts personally observed (e.g., wound size, vital signs) and as an EXPERT witness (permitted under BSA Section 39 / former IEA Section 45 to draw scientific inferences and opinions on cause/manner/time of death). Intentionally giving false evidence after taking oath or affirmation is perjury—defined in BNS 2023 Section 227 (former IPC 191; fabricating false evidence is BNS 228/IPC 192) and punished under BNS Section 229 (former IPC 193) with up to 7 years imprisonment and fine when committed in a judicial proceeding (up to 3 years in other cases).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02939","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Court Examination Sequence and Inquests","q":"During a murder trial in a Sessions Court, in what mandatory sequence is a medical witness examined under oath, during which stage are \u0027leading questions\u0027 (suggesting the desired answer) normally permitted without special court permission, and which BNSS 2023 section mandates a Magistrate inquest (former CrPC 176) for custodial deaths or suspicious deaths of women within 7 years of marriage?","o":["Oath -\u003e Examination-in-chief (by calling party; NO leading questions) -\u003e Cross-examination (by opposing counsel; LEADING QUESTIONS PERMITTED) -\u003e Re-examination (by calling party to clarify ambiguities) -\u003e Court questions; Magistrate inquest is governed by BNSS Section 196 (whereas routine Police inquest is BNSS Section 194)","Examination-in-chief -\u003e Court questions -\u003e Cross-examination; Police inquest is BNSS Section 196","Cross-examination -\u003e Examination-in-chief -\u003e Re-examination; leading questions permitted ONLY in examination-in-chief; Magistrate inquest is BNSS Section 194","Oath -\u003e Re-examination -\u003e Cross-examination; leading questions are strictly banned in ALL stages; Magistrate inquest is BNS Section 106"],"a":0,"e":"Under the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA, 2023; mirroring Indian Evidence Act), witness testimony follows a strict order: (1) Oath/affirmation, (2) Examination-in-chief by the party calling the witness (prosecution/plaintiff)—leading questions are prohibited unless permitted for introductory/undisputed facts or if the witness turns hostile; (3) Cross-examination by the adverse/defense party—leading questions ARE freely permitted to test accuracy, bias, and credibility; (4) Re-examination by the calling party strictly to clarify points raised in cross-examination; and (5) Judge/Court questions at any time. For unnatural deaths, routine Police inquest occurs under BNSS Section 194 (former CrPC 174), whereas mandatory Executive/Judicial Magistrate inquest occurs under BNSS Section 196 (former CrPC 176) for custodial deaths, disappearances/rape in custody, and suspicious death/suicide of a woman within 7 years of marriage (dowry death/Section 80 BNS).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02940","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Medical Certification of Cause of Death (MCCD) and Exhumation","q":"Assertion (A): On a standard Medical Certificate of Cause of Death (MCCD Form 4/4A), \u0027cardiorespiratory arrest\u0027 or \u0027heart failure\u0027 alone must NOT be entered as the underlying cause of death, and an underlying chronic condition that did NOT initiate the fatal sequence (e.g., controlled diabetes in a fatal road traffic head injury) belongs in Part II rather than Part I. Reason (R): Part I records the direct causal sequence from immediate cause (Line Ia) backward to the single underlying cause (lowest line of Part I) that initiated the morbid train of events, whereas Part II lists significant contributing conditions unrelated to the direct causal chain.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"WHO/ICMR Medical Certification of Cause of Death (MCCD) rules require distinguishing modes of dying (cardiorespiratory arrest, syncope, asphyxia—which are universal terminal events and forbidden as standalone causes) from specific pathological causes. Part I traces the direct causal sequence downward: Line (a) immediate cause (e.g., pulmonary embolism) due to Line (b) antecedent cause (e.g., deep vein thrombosis) due to Line (c) underlying cause (e.g., fractured femur from fall). Part II records other significant conditions contributing to death without being part of the Part I causal chain. (Note: In India, when foul play/poisoning is suspected after burial, exhumation can be ordered by a District/Sub-Divisional/Executive Magistrate under BNSS Section 196(3) with NO statutory time limit.)","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02941","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Fetal Age Estimation","q":"A stillborn fetus recovered for medicolegal examination has a crown-heel length of 35 cm, lanugo hair over the skin, and an ossification center present in the talus (alongside calcaneus) while the lower femoral epiphysis center is still absent. Using Haase\u0027s rule for fetal crown-heel length (in lunar months of 28 days each), what is the estimated gestational age, and has the fetus reached the forensic age of viability (7 lunar months)?","o":["9 lunar months (36 weeks); YES, confirmed by presence of talus alone","7 lunar months (28 weeks); YES, it has reached forensic viability (ossification center of talus appears at ~7 months, whereas lower femoral epiphysis appears at ~9 months)","3.5 lunar months (14 weeks); NO, below viability","5 lunar months (20 weeks); NO, it is non-viable"],"a":1,"e":"Haase\u0027s rule estimates fetal age in lunar months (28 days each) from crown-heel (vertex-heel) length: during the first 5 lunar months, Length (cm) = (Month)^2 (e.g., 1, 4, 9, 16, 25 cm); from the 6th to 10th lunar months, Length (cm) = Month x 5 (e.g., 6-\u003e30 cm, 7-\u003e35 cm, 8-\u003e40 cm, 9-\u003e45 cm, 10-\u003e50 cm). A crown-heel length of 35 cm divided by 5 gives 7 lunar months (28 weeks, 196 days—closely matching the classic ~210 days / 7-month forensic viability threshold), corroborated by ossification centers: calcaneus appears at ~5th month, talus at ~7th month, lower end of femur (Béclard center) at ~9th month (36 weeks), and cuboid/upper tibia at ~9-10th month.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02942","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP)","q":"A 69-year-old man presents with fever, productive cough, and right lower-zone consolidation on chest X-ray. He is newly confused (AMTS 6/10), blood urea is 54 mg/dL (BUN 25 mg/dL), respiratory rate is 32/min, and BP is 86/54 mmHg. Calculate his CURB-65 score and choose the appropriate site of care and empirical IV therapy.","o":["CURB-65 = 2; discharge home with oral amoxicillin monotherapy","CURB-65 = 3; outpatient nebulised salbutamol without antibiotics","CURB-65 = 5; admit to ICU and start IV beta-lactam (ceftriaxone or amoxicillin-clavulanate) PLUS IV macrolide (azithromycin) or respiratory fluoroquinolone","CURB-65 = 1; outpatient oral doxycycline alone"],"a":2,"e":"CURB-65 assigns 1 point each for: Confusion, Urea \u003e 42 mg/dL (BUN \u003e 19-20 mg/dL or \u003e7 mmol/L), Respiratory rate \u003e= 30/min, Blood pressure (SBP \u003c 90 or DBP \u003c= 60 mmHg), and age \u003e= 65 years. This patient meets all 5 criteria (CURB-65 = 5, severe CAP with \u003e20-30% mortality), requiring ICU admission and prompt dual IV empiric therapy (non-pseudomonal 3rd-gen cephalosporin/beta-lactamase inhibitor + macrolide or respiratory fluoroquinolone).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02943","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Acute Pancreatitis","q":"A 38-year-old man presents with 12 hours of severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, vomiting, serum lipase 1,420 U/L (\u003e3x upper limit of normal), and tachycardia (HR 114/min) after an alcohol binge. Ultrasound shows no gallstones and no biliary dilatation; CT is not yet performed (2 of 3 Revised Atlanta criteria met). What is the optimal initial management in the first 24 hours?","o":["Keep strictly NPO for 10 days on total parenteral nutrition and start prophylactic IV meropenem immediately","Perform immediate emergency ERCP sphincterotomy within 6 hours","Infuse 6 litres of 0.9% normal saline rapidly in 2 hours regardless of urine output","Goal-directed IV Ringer\u0027s Lactate resuscitation, opioid/multimodal analgesia, early oral/enteral feeding once pain/nausea settle, and NO routine prophylactic antibiotics"],"a":3,"e":"Acute pancreatitis is diagnosed by 2 of 3 Revised Atlanta criteria (typical epigastric pain, amylase/lipase \u003e= 3x ULN, characteristic imaging). Early care uses moderate goal-directed isotonic crystalloid—Ringer\u0027s Lactate is preferred over 0.9% NaCl as it reduces systemic inflammation (SIRS) and hyperchloraemic acidosis—plus analgesia and early oral/enteral nutrition (within 24-48 h as tolerated, preserving gut mucosal barrier). Prophylactic antibiotics are NOT indicated in sterile interstitial or necrotising pancreatitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02944","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","q":"A 28-year-old woman complains of fluctuating diplopia, bilateral ptosis worsening toward evening, and nasal regurgitation after prolonged chewing. Ice-pack test improves ptosis, repetitive nerve stimulation shows a decremental response, and serum anti-acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibodies are positive. Along with starting oral pyridostigmine and evaluating chest CT for thymoma, which antibiotic class MUST be avoided because it can precipitate a life-threatening myasthenic crisis?","o":["Aminoglycosides (e.g., gentamicin, amikacin) and fluoroquinolones","Penicillins (e.g., amoxicillin)","First-generation cephalosporins (e.g., cephalexin)","Oral nitrofurantoin"],"a":0,"e":"Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune postsynaptic AChR disorder presenting with fatigable ocular, bulbar, and proximal limb weakness with preserved reflexes. Drugs that impair neuromuscular transmission—notably aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, macrolides (telithromycin), magnesium sulphate, beta-blockers, and neuromuscular blockers—can precipitate acute respiratory failure (myasthenic crisis) and must be avoided.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02945","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Microcytic Anaemias","q":"An asymptomatic 24-year-old woman evaluated during antenatal screening has Hb 10.6 g/dL, MCV 60.0 fL, RBC count 6.0 million/mm3, normal RDW (13.2%), and normal serum ferritin. Calculate the Mentzer index (MCV / RBC count) and identify the most likely diagnosis.","o":["Mentzer index = 10.0 (\u003c 13); diagnostic of severe Iron Deficiency Anaemia requiring IV iron sucrose","Mentzer index = 10.0 (\u003c 13); suggestive of Beta-thalassaemia trait (confirm with HbA2 \u003e= 3.5% on HPLC)","Mentzer index = 17.6 (\u003e 13); suggestive of sideroblastic anaemia","Mentzer index = 36.0 (\u003e 13); suggestive of megaloblastic anaemia"],"a":1,"e":"Mentzer index = MCV (in fL) / RBC count (in millions/mm3) = 60.0 / 6.0 = 10.0. An index \u003c 13 (microcytosis with disproportionately elevated/preserved RBC count and normal RDW) strongly points to Beta-thalassaemia trait (confirmed by elevated HbA2 \u003e= 3.5% on HPLC/electrophoresis), whereas Iron Deficiency Anaemia has reduced erythropoiesis (Mentzer index \u003e 13, elevated RDW, low ferritin).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02946","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Zoonotic Bacterial Infections","q":"A 33-year-old sanitation worker in Mumbai presents 8 days after wading through monsoon floodwater with high fever, severe calf tenderness, non-purulent conjunctival suffusion, deep jaundice (bilirubin 11.2 mg/dL with only mild transaminase elevation), serum creatinine 3.4 mg/dL, and thrombocytopenia (58,000/mm3). What is the most likely diagnosis and antibiotic of choice for this severe presentation?","o":["Acute viral hepatitis A; oral rifampicin","Scrub typhus; oral ciprofloxacin","Severe Leptospirosis (Weil\u0027s disease); IV Ceftriaxone or IV Penicillin G","Severe vivax malaria; oral chloroquine alone"],"a":2,"e":"Monsoon floodwater exposure (rat-urine-contaminated water) followed by fever, marked calf myalgia, conjunctival suffusion, conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with modest transaminitis, AKI, and thrombocytopenia defines Weil\u0027s disease (icteric severe Leptospirosis due to Leptospira interrogans). Severe disease requires immediate IV Penicillin G or IV Ceftriaxone (whereas mild outpatient leptospirosis is treated with oral doxycycline).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02947","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Pericardial Diseases","q":"A 26-year-old man presents 1 week after a viral illness with sharp pleuritic retrosternal chest pain that worsens when lying supine and improves upon sitting upright and leaning forward. Auscultation reveals a triphasic scratchy pericardial friction rub, and ECG shows diffuse concave-upward ST-segment elevation with PR-segment depression across most leads (except aVR and V1, which show PR elevation). What is the first-line pharmacotherapy for acute idiopathic/viral pericarditis?","o":["Immediate IV thrombolysis with tenecteplase","Oral prednisolone monotherapy as routine first-line","Oral warfarin anticoagulation","High-dose NSAID (e.g., ibuprofen or aspirin) PLUS Colchicine for 3 months"],"a":3,"e":"Diffuse concave-upward (\u0027saddle-shaped\u0027) ST elevation without reciprocal ST depression (except aVR/V1) and PR-segment depression with positional pleuritic pain and pericardial rub confirm acute viral pericarditis. First-line therapy combines an NSAID (or high-dose aspirin) with Colchicine (for 3 months), which halves recurrence rates. Corticosteroids are avoided first-line because they increase relapse risk, and thrombolysis/anticoagulants risk haemorrhagic cardiac tamponade.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02948","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Peptic Ulcer Complications","q":"A 46-year-old man taking daily diclofenac for back pain develops sudden agonising epigastric pain spreading rapidly across the abdomen. He lies completely still; abdomen shows board-like rigidity and obliteration of liver dullness to percussion. Erect chest X-ray demonstrates free gas (crescentic air) under the right dome of the diaphragm. After IV fluid resuscitation, NG decompression, and IV antibiotics/PPI, what is the standard emergency surgical procedure?","o":["Laparoscopic or open peritoneal lavage and omental patch closure (modified Graham patch) of the perforated duodenal ulcer, followed by H. pylori eradication","Conservative outpatient oral omeprazole alone without surgery","Immediate total gastrectomy with oesophagojejunostomy","Emergency colonoscopy"],"a":0,"e":"Sudden generalised peritonitis, board-like rigidity, absent liver dullness, and pneumoperitoneum on erect chest X-ray confirm perforated peptic ulcer (most commonly anterior first-part duodenum). Standard emergency operation is thorough peritoneal toilet and simple closure reinforced with a vascularised omental plug (Cellan-Jones / Graham patch), followed by stopping NSAIDs and eradicating Helicobacter pylori.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02949","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Colorectal Carcinoma","q":"A 64-year-old man presents with unexplained fatigue, microcytic hypochromic iron-deficiency anaemia (Hb 7.8 g/dL), positive faecal occult blood, and a firm palpable mass in the right iliac fossa without altered bowel habits or obstruction. Contrast this with left-sided (sigmoid) colon cancer: why does caecal/ascending colon carcinoma typically present with anaemia rather than early intestinal obstruction?","o":["Caecal tumours produce erythropoietin-blocking antibodies; treated with Hartmann procedure","Wide caecal lumen and liquid faecal contents allow exophytic tumours to grow large and bleed chronically without obstructing; treated with radical right hemicolectomy","Left colon has liquid stool so right colon obstructs earlier; treated with sigmoid colectomy","Caecal carcinoma is always benign and needs only oral iron"],"a":1,"e":"The right colon (caecum/ascending) has a wide diameter and liquid ileal effluent, so polypoid/ulcerative cancers grow silently to large size causing chronic occult blood loss and unexplained iron-deficiency anaemia in older adults (mandating colonoscopy). Conversely, the narrower left/sigmoid colon with solid stools and annular stenosing tumours (\u0027apple-core\u0027 lesion) presents with altered bowel habits, haematochezia, and large bowel obstruction. Caecal cancer is treated with radical right hemicolectomy (ligating ileocolic, right colic, and right branch of middle colic vessels).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02950","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Inflammatory Breast Carcinoma","q":"A 47-year-old non-lactating woman presents with rapid onset over 4 weeks of diffuse erythema, warmth, oedema, and \u0027peau d\u0027orange\u0027 dimpling involving \u003e50% of the left breast skin, without fever or leucocytosis, and no improvement after 2 weeks of empirical oral antibiotics. Punch skin biopsy plus core needle biopsy reveals dermal lymphatic invasion by high-grade invasive ductal carcinoma (T4d). What is the correct treatment sequence?","o":["6 more weeks of IV vancomycin alone","Immediate primary mastectomy without chemotherapy","Neoadjuvant systemic chemotherapy (plus anti-HER2 if HER2+) FIRST, followed by modified radical mastectomy and post-mastectomy radiotherapy","Repeated incision and drainage for presumed breast abscess"],"a":2,"e":"Inflammatory Breast Cancer (T4d) mimics mastitis clinically (erythema, warmth, peau d\u0027orange due to tumour emboli blocking dermal lymphatics) and must be biopsied (core + full-thickness skin punch biopsy) when \u0027mastitis\u0027 occurs in non-lactating women or fails 1-2 weeks of antibiotics. Upfront surgery carries \u003e50% chest-wall recurrence and terrible outcomes; trimodality therapy MUST begin with Neoadjuvant Chemotherapy, followed by Modified Radical Mastectomy (if responsive) and locoregional radiotherapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02951","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Renal Neoplasms","q":"A 59-year-old male smoker presents with painless gross haematuria, dull left flank pain, haemoglobin 19.2 g/dL (secondary erythrocytosis), and a newly developed left-sided varicocele that fails to decompress when he lies supine. CECT abdomen reveals a 7.5 cm hypervascular enhancing mass in the left kidney with tumour thrombus extending into the left renal vein. What explains the non-emptying left varicocele, and what is the primary curative treatment for localised/locally advanced RCC?","o":["Primary testicular seminoma; inguinal orchidectomy","Benign renal angiomyolipoma; oral propranolol","Right renal vein compression of the aorta; systemic cisplatin chemotherapy alone","Left testicular vein drains directly at 90 degrees into the left renal vein (occluded by tumour thrombus); radical nephrectomy (+/- thrombectomy)"],"a":3,"e":"Renal Cell Carcinoma (clear cell adenocarcinoma) classically produces paraneoplastic syndromes (erythropoietin-mediated polycythaemia, PTHrP hypercalcaemia, Stauffer hepatic dysfunction) and has a propensity to invade the renal vein and IVC. Because the left gonadal (testicular) vein drains perpendicularly into the left renal vein, tumour thrombus occlusion produces an acute left varicocele that does NOT empty on lying down. Localised/locally advanced RCC is chemo- and radio-resistant; surgical radical (or partial for small T1) nephrectomy with tumour thrombectomy is curative.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02952","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Cutaneous Malignancies","q":"A 68-year-old fair-skinned outdoor worker presents with a slow-growing, painless, 1.5 cm ulcerated nodule near the medial canthus of the right eye (above the line joining the angle of the mouth to the ear lobule), featuring rolled, pearly, telangiectatic edges and central crusting. Biopsy shows nests of basaloid cells with peripheral palisading. What is the key biological behaviour and standard treatment?","o":["Locally invasive (\u0027rodent ulcer\u0027) with extremely rare (\u003c0.1%) metastasis; complete surgical excision with clear margins (or Mohs micrographic surgery near the eyelid)","Radiosensitive lymphoma; CHOP chemotherapy","Benign seborrhoeic keratosis; reassure without excision","Rapidly metastasises to regional cervical lymph nodes in \u003e60% of cases; prophylactic radical neck dissection"],"a":0,"e":"Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC) occurs predominantly on sun-exposed head/neck skin above the line from the oral commissure to the tragus/ear lobule, classically appearing as a pearly telangiectatic nodule/rodent ulcer with peripheral nuclear palisading on histology. While it causes destructive local invasion if neglected, metastasis is exceedingly rare; complete surgical excision with 3-5 mm margins (or tissue-sparing Mohs micrographic surgery around medial canthus/nose) is curative.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02953","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Tocolysis and Neuroprotection","q":"A 25-year-old primigravida at 30+4 weeks of gestation presents with painful regular uterine contractions (3 in 10 minutes), intact membranes, cervical dilatation 2 cm, and no chorioamnionitis or abruption. Which pharmacologic combination is indicated during the next 48 hours to improve neonatal survival and prevent cerebral palsy?","o":["Immediate IV oxytocin augmentation","Antenatal corticosteroids (betamethasone/dexamethasone) + short-term Nifedipine tocolysis (48 hours to complete steroid window) + IV Magnesium Sulphate for fetal neuroprotection","Oral misoprostol to accelerate preterm birth","Long-term oral ritodrine maintenance until 38 weeks without steroids"],"a":1,"e":"In uncomplicated spontaneous preterm labour \u003c32 weeks (with intact membranes and no infection/fetal compromise), short-course tocolysis (Nifedipine is preferred first-line for 48 hours—avoiding prolonged maintenance tocolysis) buys time for (1) Antenatal corticosteroids to reduce RDS, IVH, and NEC, and (2) IV Magnesium Sulphate (4 g loading + 1 g/h maintenance) for fetal neuroprotection (\u003c32 weeks), which significantly lowers cerebral palsy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02954","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Fetal Growth Restriction (FGR)","q":"A 30-year-old woman with severe preeclampsia and early-onset Fetal Growth Restriction (estimated fetal weight \u003c 3rd percentile) at 31 weeks undergoes serial fetal Doppler velocimetry. Previously, umbilical artery Doppler showed absent end-diastolic flow and middle cerebral artery showed reduced PI (\u0027brain-sparing\u0027 redistribution). Today, umbilical artery shows REVERSED end-diastolic flow and the Ductus Venosus shows an absent/reversed \u0027a\u0027-wave. What does the ductus venosus reversal signify and what is the management?","o":["Mild anaemia only; start maternal oral iron","Improved placental perfusion; continue pregnancy to 40 weeks","Decompensated myocardial dysfunction and imminent fetal acidaemia/stillbirth; give corticosteroids (if not already given) and expedite delivery (Caesarean section)","Benign physiological fetal sleep state; repeat Doppler in 4 weeks"],"a":2,"e":"In placental-insufficiency FGR, Doppler deterioration follows a sequence: elevated umbilical artery PI -\u003e MCA vasodilatation (\u0027brain-sparing\u0027) -\u003e absent/reversed umbilical end-diastolic flow -\u003e finally venous Doppler abnormalities (absent/reversed Ductus Venosus a-wave and umbilical vein pulsations), reflecting right-heart failure, acidosis, and imminent intrauterine fetal death within days. Delivery (typically by Caesarean section given poor fetal reserve) is urgently indicated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02955","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Uterine Leiomyoma (Fibroid)","q":"A 31-year-old primigravida at 24 weeks of gestation with a known 7 cm intramural uterine fibroid presents with acute severe localised abdominal pain over the fibroid, low-grade fever (37.9 degrees C), nausea, mild leucocytosis (14,000/mm3), and exquisite focal tenderness over the firm uterine swelling without vaginal bleeding or cervical change. Ultrasound shows a heterogeneous hypoechoic area with cystic spaces inside the fibroid and a live normal fetus. What is the diagnosis and treatment?","o":["Acute ruptured uterus; emergency hysterectomy","Chorioamnionitis; immediate misoprostol termination","Torsion of ovarian cyst; emergency myomectomy during pregnancy","Red (carneous) degeneration of fibroid; conservative management with bed rest, IV/oral hydration, and analgesics (avoiding myomectomy during pregnancy)"],"a":3,"e":"Rapid second-trimester fibroid growth outstripping its blood supply causes haemorrhagic infarction—Red (carneous) degeneration—presenting with acute focal pain, tenderness, low-grade fever, and mild leucocytosis. Crucially, treatment is purely conservative (analgesia, hydration, rest, resolving within 1-2 weeks); myomectomy during pregnancy (except rarely for a twisted pedunculated subserosal fibroid) risks uncontrollable haemorrhage and pregnancy loss.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02956","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Vaginitis and STIs","q":"A 26-year-old woman presents with profuse, frothy, greenish-yellow malodorous vaginal discharge, vulvar itching, and dysuria. Speculum exam reveals punctate haemorrhagic spots on the ectocervix (\u0027strawberry cervix\u0027), vaginal pH is 5.8, and wet-mount saline microscopy demonstrates pear-shaped motile flagellated protozoa. What is the correct management?","o":["Oral Metronidazole (2 g single dose or 500 mg BID x 7 days) AND mandatory simultaneous treatment of her sexual partner(s)","Oral fluconazole 150 mg single dose","Topical clotrimazole pessary for the patient alone without partner treatment","Oral doxycycline alone without treating partner"],"a":0,"e":"Frothy greenish-yellow discharge, elevated vaginal pH (\u003e4.5), strawberry cervix (colpitis macularis), and motile flagellated trophozoites on wet mount confirm Trichomonas vaginalis infection. Because trichomoniasis is a sexually transmitted infection, systemic oral Metronidazole (or Tinidazole) MUST be prescribed for BOTH the patient and all sexual partners (with abstinence until both complete therapy) to prevent reinfection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02957","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Neonatal Respiratory Distress","q":"A preterm male infant born at 29 weeks\u0027 gestation (birth weight 1,150 g) to a mother who received no antenatal steroids develops progressive tachypnoea, expiratory grunting, nasal flaring, subcostal retractions, and cyanosis within 2 hours of birth. Chest radiograph shows low lung volumes with diffuse bilateral reticulogranular (\u0027ground-glass\u0027) opacities and air bronchograms. What is the immediate respiratory management?","o":["Immediate needle thoracostomy bilaterally","Nasal Continuous Positive Airway Pressure (CPAP) plus early rescue intratracheal exogenous Surfactant (INSURE/LISA) if FiO2 requirement exceeds 0.30","IV furosemide bolus for presumed transient tachypnoea of newborn","Immediate high-volume 100% free-flow oxygen without CPAP and oral antibiotics"],"a":1,"e":"Surfactant deficiency in preterm neonates (\u003c34 weeks, especially without antenatal corticosteroids) causes alveolar collapse (atelectasis), presenting early with grunting, retractions, and \u0027ground-glass\u0027 reticulogranular lungs with air bronchograms (RDS / Hyaline Membrane Disease). Delivery-room/NICU stabilisation starts with early nasal CPAP (5-7 cmH2O) to maintain functional residual capacity, adding early selective rescue exogenous surfactant (via INSURE or LISA) if oxygen demand remains elevated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02958","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Acute Viral Bronchiolitis","q":"A previously healthy 4-month-old infant presents in winter with 2 days of rhinorrhoea followed by cough, tachypnoea (RR 58/min), subcostal retractions, diffuse bilateral expiratory wheezes and fine crackles, and SpO2 89% on room air (first episode of wheezing; likely RSV bronchiolitis). Which management plan is evidence-based?","o":["Immediate systemic oral dexamethasone for 5 days plus IV azithromycin","Scheduled nebulised salbutamol every 2 hours for 7 days regardless of response","Supportive care: gentle nasal saline suctioning, adequate hydration (NG/IV if unable to feed), and heated humidified oxygen to maintain SpO2 \u003e= 90-92%; avoid routine corticosteroids and antibiotics","Oral cough suppressant syrup (codeine)"],"a":2,"e":"Acute bronchiolitis (most commonly Respiratory Syncytial Virus in infants \u003c2 years, peaking 2-6 months) is a self-limiting viral inflammation/oedema of bronchioles. Large RCTs and AAP/IAP guidelines confirm that supportive care—oxygen when SpO2 \u003c90-92%, hydration, and superficial nasal suctioning—is the cornerstone; routine bronchodilators, systemic/inhaled corticosteroids, chest physiotherapy, and antibiotics (unless secondary bacterial infection) do NOT shorten hospital stay or improve outcomes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02959","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Abdominal Solid Tumours","q":"A thriving 3-year-old girl is brought after her mother noticed a large abdominal swelling while bathing her. Examination reveals a smooth, firm, non-tender left flank mass that does NOT cross the midline, mild hypertension, and microscopic haematuria (WT1/aniridia associations absent). Contrast CT confirms an intrarenal mass with the \u0027claw sign\u0027. What is the critical bedside examination precaution, and how does this tumour differ from neuroblastoma?","o":["Wilms tumour; crosses the midline routinely with elevated urinary VMA/HVA","Neuroblastoma; perform immediate open incisional biopsy in the OPD","Hepatoblastoma; always associated with opsoclonus-myoclonus","Wilms tumour (nephroblastoma); avoid repeated/vigorous abdominal palpation (risks tumour capsule rupture and peritoneal upstaging), whereas neuroblastoma typically crosses the midline and has calcifications"],"a":3,"e":"Wilms tumour (nephroblastoma, commonest childhood renal malignancy) typically presents in a well-appearing 2-4 year old as a smooth, unilateral flank mass that rarely crosses the midline and shows an intrarenal \u0027claw sign\u0027 on CT. Because the tumour pseudocapsule is thin and friable, excessive abdominal palpation (and transcutaneous biopsy before nephrectomy in resectable unilateral disease) risks capsule rupture, spilling tumour into the peritoneal cavity (upstaging to Stage III requiring whole-abdomen radiation). Neuroblastoma is extrarenal, ill-defined, crosses the midline, shows stippled calcifications, elevates urinary VMA/HVA, and causes \u0027raccoon eyes\u0027/opsoclonus-myoclonus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02960","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Vitamin D Deficiency Rickets","q":"A 15-month-old exclusively breastfed toddler with minimal sunlight exposure presents with delayed walking, frontal bossing, widened wrists, beaded costochondral junctions (\u0027rachitic rosary\u0027), and bow legs. Wrist radiograph shows widening, cupping, and fraying of the distal radial and ulnar metaphyses. Which biochemical profile is expected in active nutritional vitamin D deficiency rickets?","o":["Markedly elevated Serum Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP), low serum phosphate, normal or low serum calcium, and elevated PTH","Markedly elevated serum vitamin C and subperiosteal haemorrhages","Low Alkaline Phosphatase, high serum phosphate, and suppressed PTH","Isolated hypercalcaemia with suppressed ALP"],"a":0,"e":"In Vitamin D deficiency rickets, impaired intestinal calcium absorption triggers secondary hyperparathyroidism (elevated PTH), which mobilises bone calcium (often maintaining near-normal serum calcium until late stages) and causes phosphaturia (LOW serum phosphate) alongside markedly elevated osteoblastic Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP) — the most sensitive biochemical marker of active rickets. Radiographs show metaphyseal widening, cupping, and fraying with increased distance between epiphysis and metaphysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02961","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Water Purification","q":"During chlorination of a community well water supply using bleaching powder, chlorine demand is first satisfied, followed by a point where all chloramines are oxidised and any further added chlorine appears directly as free residual chlorine. What is this point called, and what minimum free residual chlorine level must be present at 1 hour contact time for safe drinking water?","o":["Saturation point; minimum 5.0 mg/L free chlorine for 5 minutes","Break-point chlorination; minimum 0.5 mg/L (0.5 ppm) free residual chlorine for 1 hour","Flash point; 0.01 mg/L free chlorine for 24 hours","Isoelectric point; 10.0 mg/L total combined chlorine"],"a":1,"e":"When chlorine is added to water, it first reacts with reducing substances and ammonia to form chloramines; as dosing increases, chloramines are oxidised until a trough is reached—the \u0027break-point\u0027. Beyond break-point chlorination, added chlorine exists as free residual chlorine (HOCl/OCl-), providing potent ongoing disinfection. WHO/Indian drinking water standards require \u003e= 0.5 mg/L free residual chlorine after a minimum 1-hour contact period (measured via Orthotolidine-Arsenite or DPD test; Horrocks apparatus estimates chlorine demand).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02962","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Biomedical Waste Management Rules (India)","q":"At an OSCE station on Biomedical Waste Management Rules (India), a nurse holds four items: (1) Blood-soaked cotton swabs; (2) Used plastic IV tubing and urinary catheter (without needle); (3) Used metallic hypodermic needle and scalpel blade; (4) Broken glass ampoule. Which colour-coded bin sequence matches items 1, 2, 3, and 4 respectively?","o":["1-Black, 2-Red, 3-Yellow, 4-Blue","1-Yellow, 2-Blue, 3-Red, 4-White","1-Yellow, 2-Red, 3-White (puncture-proof translucent), 4-Blue (puncture-proof box)","1-Red, 2-Yellow, 3-Blue, 4-Black"],"a":2,"e":"Under India\u0027s Biomedical Waste Management Rules (2016, amended 2018): Yellow bag holds human anatomical waste, soiled blood/pus-contaminated dressings/cotton, microbiology waste, and expired/discarded medicines; Red bag holds contaminated recyclable plastic waste (IV bottles/tubing, catheters, syringes without needles, gloves); White translucent puncture-proof container holds metallic sharps (needles, scalpels, blades); Blue puncture-proof box holds glassware (ampoules, vials) and metallic body implants.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02963","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Vitamin A Prophylaxis Programme","q":"Under India\u0027s National Vitamin A Prophylaxis Programme integrated with the Universal Immunisation Programme (UIP), a child receives 100,000 IU (1 lakh IU) at 9 months of age with the first Measles-Rubella dose, followed by 200,000 IU (2 lakh IU) every 6 months until 5 years of age. How many total doses and total cumulative IU of Vitamin A does a child receive by age 5 years?","o":["5 doses totalling 9 lakh IU (900,000 IU)","6 doses totalling 11 lakh IU (1,100,000 IU)","10 doses totalling 20 lakh IU (2,000,000 IU)","9 doses totalling 17 lakh IU (1,700,000 IU)"],"a":3,"e":"The UIP Vitamin A prophylaxis schedule consists of: Dose 1 at 9 months = 1 lakh IU (100,000 IU); Doses 2 to 9 at 16-18, 24, 30, 36, 42, 48, 54, and 60 months (8 doses x 2 lakh IU = 16 lakh IU). Total = 9 doses delivering 1 + 16 = 17 lakh IU (1,700,000 IU) to prevent nutritional blindness (xerophthalmia/keratomalacia) and reduce measles/diarrhoeal mortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02964","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Mass Casualty Triage","q":"During a railway mass-casualty disaster, four victims are assessed on-site using the 4-colour triage system: Victim A has an open femur fracture with stable vitals; Victim B is walking calmly with minor forearm abrasions; Victim C has airway stridor and SBP 72 mmHg (responsive); Victim D has catastrophic open cranial trauma with absent breathing after jaw-thrust and absent carotid pulse. Which colour tags apply to A, B, C, and D respectively?","o":["A-Yellow (Delayed), B-Green (Minimal), C-Red (Immediate), D-Black (Expectant/Deceased)","A-Red, B-Yellow, C-Green, D-Black","A-Yellow, B-Red, C-Black, D-Green","A-Green, B-Yellow, C-Black, D-Red"],"a":0,"e":"In disaster triage (START protocol), priority goes to doing the greatest good for the greatest number: Red (Priority I / Immediate) = life-threatening airway, breathing, or circulatory shock likely survivable with rapid intervention (Victim C); Yellow (Priority II / Delayed) = serious injuries like open long-bone fractures that can wait safely ~hours without immediate threat to life (Victim A); Green (Priority III / Minimal) = \u0027walking wounded\u0027 (Victim B); Black (Priority IV / Expectant) = dead or unsalvageable apnoeic/pulseless despite airway opening (Victim D).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02965","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Retinal Detachment","q":"A 54-year-old high myope notices bright flashes of light (photopsia) in his temporal visual field followed 2 days later by a sudden shower of black floaters and a painless dark \u0027curtain\u0027 descending over his vision. Slit-lamp shows pigment granules (\u0027Shafer\u0027s tobacco dust\u0027) in the anterior vitreous, and indirect ophthalmoscopy reveals an elevated, greyish, corrugated retina with a horseshoe retinal tear superotemporally. What is the diagnosis and urgent treatment?","o":["Posterior vitreous detachment alone without tear; reassure and review in 1 year","Rhegmatogenous Retinal Detachment; urgent surgical reattachment (scleral buckling, pneumatic retinopexy, or pars plana vitrectomy with laser/cryopexy)","Exudative retinal detachment from central serous retinopathy; oral prednisolone","Optic atrophy; vitamin B12 injections"],"a":1,"e":"High myopia predisposes to posterior vitreous detachment causing retinal traction (photopsia), tearing a retinal vessel (floaters + Shafer\u0027s tobacco dust), and allowing liquefied vitreous fluid through a full-thickness horseshoe break into the subretinal space—Rhegmatogenous Retinal Detachment (corrugated retina moving on eye movement). Urgent surgical repair (pneumatic retinopexy, scleral buckle, or pars plana vitrectomy) is required—especially urgently if the macula is still attached (\u0027macula-on\u0027) to preserve central vision.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02966","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Lens-Induced Glaucoma","q":"A 74-year-old rural woman with an untreated hypermature Morgagnian cataract presents with severe right eye pain, redness, IOP 48 mmHg, corneal oedema, a DEEP anterior chamber filled with heavy flare and iridescent white flocculent particles (macrophages engorged with lens protein), and an open iridocorneal angle on gonioscopy. What is the diagnosis and definitive treatment after medically lowering IOP?","o":["Phacomorphic glaucoma (shallow AC angle closure); immediate atropine drops alone","Primary open-angle glaucoma; lifelong timolol without cataract surgery","Phacolytic glaucoma (open-angle lens-protein obstruction); medical IOP control + topical steroids followed by prompt cataract extraction with IOL implantation","Fungal endophthalmitis; intravitreal voriconazole"],"a":2,"e":"Hypermature cataracts leak high-molecular-weight soluble lens proteins through an intact capsule, inciting a macrophage response that clogs trabecular meshwork pores—Phacolytic Glaucoma. Unlike Phacomorphic Glaucoma (intumescent swollen cataract pushing the iris forward to cause a SHALLOW anterior chamber and secondary angle closure), phacolytic glaucoma has a DEEP anterior chamber, open angle, and milky/white floccules. Definitive cure is prompt cataract surgery once IOP and inflammation are controlled medically.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02967","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Chemical Ocular Burns","q":"A 7-year-old boy accidentally splashes edible lime (\u0027chuna\u0027 / calcium hydroxide) paste into his right eye and arrives crying with blepharospasm. Why are alkali burns more destructive than acid burns, and what is the SINGLE most urgent first step before measuring visual acuity or waiting for slit-lamp evaluation?","o":["Pad and bandage the eye tightly and refer next morning","Alkalis cause superficial coagulative necrosis; check Snellen visual acuity and wait for pH paper first","Neutralise the alkali eye with dilute vinegar/acid drops","Alkalis cause liquefactive necrosis (saponifying cell membranes for deep penetration); IMMEDIATELY irrigate copiously with normal saline/Ringer\u0027s lactate (or clean water) for \u003e= 30 minutes and double-evert upper eyelid to remove retained lime particles"],"a":3,"e":"Chemical ocular injuries—especially alkali burns (lime/chuna, ammonia, lye)—are true emergencies because alkalis saponify fatty acids in cell membranes, causing liquefactive necrosis and rapid deep penetration into the cornea, limbal stem cells, and anterior chamber (whereas acids coagulate proteins creating a partial barrier). Immediate continuous irrigation for \u003e= 30 minutes (until conjunctival pH normalises ~7.0-7.4) with removal of trapped calcium hydroxide particles from the upper/lower fornices via double lid eversion MUST precede visual acuity testing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02968","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV)","q":"A 49-year-old woman experiences intense spinning vertigo lasting 20-30 seconds whenever she rolls onto her right side in bed or looks up at a high shelf, with NO hearing loss or tinnitus. Performing the right Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre reproduces vertigo after a 4-second latency with transient torsional upbeating nystagmus that fatigues on repetition. What is the diagnosis and definitive curative treatment?","o":["Right posterior semicircular canal BPPV (canalolithiasis); Epley canalith repositioning manoeuvre","Cerebellar stroke; emergency thrombolysis","Meniere\u0027s disease; low-salt diet and gentamicin ablation","Acute vestibular neuritis; high-dose oral prednisolone"],"a":0,"e":"Brief (\u003c1 minute) head-position-triggered rotatory vertigo without auditory symptoms, plus a positive Dix-Hallpike test showing latency (2-5 s), fatigable torsional upbeating geotropic nystagmus lasting \u003c1 min, confirms posterior semicircular canal BPPV (displaced utricular otoconia/canalolithiasis). The bedside Epley particle repositioning manoeuvre cures \u003e80-90% of patients immediately (pharmacotherapy/vestibular suppressants are discouraged except briefly for severe nausea).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02969","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Tracheobronchial Foreign Body","q":"A 2-year-old toddler had a sudden choking and coughing paroxysm while eating peanuts 6 hours ago. Now he has persistent unilateral wheeze and decreased breath sounds over the right hemithorax. Inspiratory chest X-ray looks near-normal, while an EXPIRATORY chest radiograph reveals hyperlucency and hyperinflation of the right lung with mediastinal shift to the left (ball-valve check-valve mechanism). What is the definitive diagnostic and therapeutic procedure?","o":["Flexible upper GI oesophagoscopy","Urgent rigid bronchoscopy under general anaesthesia to extract the peanut","Oral azithromycin and bronchodilator inhaler discharge","Wait 1 week for spontaneous coughing out of the peanut"],"a":1,"e":"Organic foreign bodies (peanuts—commonest in toddlers) lodge preferentially in the wider, more vertical right main bronchus, producing partial expiratory obstruction (\u0027check-valve\u0027 / ball-valve effect) with air trapping best seen on expiratory/lateral decubitus chest radiographs. Furthermore, peanut oils cause severe chemical arachidic bronchitis. Urgent rigid ventilating bronchoscopy is the gold standard for safe extraction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02970","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Deep Neck Space Infections","q":"A 42-year-old diabetic man develops rapidly progressive, tense, \u0027woody-hard\u0027 brawny bilateral submandibular and submental swelling 3 days after extraction of an infected lower third molar. His tongue is pushed upward and backward against the palate (\u0027double tongue\u0027 appearance), with drooling, trismus, and inspiratory stridor. What is the immediate life-saving priority?","o":["Routine rapid-sequence induction with muscle relaxant and blind direct laryngoscopy","Immediate needle aspiration of the parotid gland","Immediate airway protection (awake fibreoptic intubation or awake tracheostomy under local anaesthesia) PLUS broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (covering anaerobes/streptococci) and surgical decompression","Outpatient oral amoxicillin and warm saline gargles"],"a":2,"e":"Ludwig\u0027s angina is a rapidly spreading bilateral cellulitis of the submandibular, sublingual, and submental spaces (classically from mandibular second/third molar roots lying below the mylohyoid line), elevating the tongue posteriorly and causing fatal asphyxiation. Maintaining spontaneous ventilation via awake fibreoptic nasal intubation or awake tracheostomy (NEVER paralysing with muscle relaxants before securing the airway, as the swollen tongue/floor collapses the pharynx!) is the primary life-saving step alongside IV antibiotics and external drainage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02971","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Upper Limb Fractures","q":"A 65-year-old osteoporotic woman falls on an outstretched hand (FOOSH) and develops a painful \u0027dinner-fork\u0027 deformity at the wrist. Radiographs confirm a distal radius fracture ~2 cm proximal to the radiocarpal joint with dorsal displacement, dorsal angulation/tilt, radial shortening, and radial tilt (Colles\u0027 fracture). Which nerve compression complication is most frequently associated with Colles\u0027 fracture?","o":["Posterior interosseous nerve palsy","Axillary nerve palsy","Long thoracic nerve palsy","Median nerve compression (acute/delayed carpal tunnel syndrome)"],"a":3,"e":"Colles\u0027 fracture is an extra-articular distal radius fracture in osteoporotic women with dorsal displacement/tilt and radial deviation producing a classic \u0027dinner-fork\u0027 deformity (contrast with volarly displaced Smith\u0027s \u0027garden-spade\u0027 fracture). Comminution, haematoma, or volar-flexed casting in the Cotton-Loder position commonly compresses the Median nerve within the carpal tunnel (and other classic complications include malunion, EPL tendon rupture, and Sudeck\u0027s osteodystrophy/CRPS).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02972","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Spinal Tuberculosis","q":"A 32-year-old man presents with 4 months of dull mid-back pain, evening fever, weight loss, and a tender angular kyphotic gibbus over T10-T11 with intact lower-limb power and no bowel/bladder deficit. MRI thoracic spine shows paradiscal destruction of T10 and T11 vertebral bodies with narrowing of the intervening intervertebral disc space and a large bilateral paravertebral cold abscess. What is the cornerstone primary treatment?","o":["Standard daily 4-drug Antitubercular Therapy (HRZE/HRE under NTEP) plus spinal bracing and close neurological monitoring","Palliative spinal radiotherapy for presumed metastasis","Long-term oral ciprofloxacin monotherapy","Immediate radical corpectomy without antitubercular drugs"],"a":0,"e":"Paradiscal vertebral destruction involving two contiguous vertebrae WITH early narrowing/destruction of the intervertebral disc space and a paravertebral cold abscess is characteristic of Pott\u0027s spine (unlike spinal metastases, which destroy the posterior body/pedicles and SPARE the avascular disc space). In neurologically intact patients without severe progressive kyphosis/instability, daily NTEP Antitubercular Therapy plus spinal orthosis (\u0027middle path\u0027 regimen) is primary curative treatment; surgery is reserved for progressive/unresponsive Pott\u0027s paraplegia or severe instability.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02973","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Catatonia","q":"A 24-year-old woman with severe major depressive disorder stops eating, drinking, and speaking for 4 days. On examination, she exhibits mutism, stupor, negativism, posturing, and \u0027waxy flexibility\u0027 (maintaining limbs in awkward positions placed by the examiner). Metabolic/infectious/structural brain workup is normal. What is the first-line acute pharmacological test/treatment, and what is the definitive treatment if she remains refractory or develops autonomic instability (malignant catatonia)?","o":["Oral lithium carbonate alone without hydration","Parenteral Lorazepam (1-2 mg IV/IM challenge and scheduled dosing); Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT) for lorazepam-refractory or malignant catatonia","High-dose IM Haloperidol immediately as first-line","Wait 6 weeks for oral fluoxetine to take effect while withholding fluids"],"a":1,"e":"Stupor, mutism, negativism, posturing, and waxy flexibility (\u003e= 3 DSM-5 features) diagnose Catatonia (frequently associated with mood disorders as well as schizophrenia or medical conditions). Parenteral Lorazepam (benzodiazepine) produces dramatic rapid relief in 70-80% of cases. If benzodiazepines fail or malignant catatonia (fever, tachycardia, autonomic instability) develops, Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT) is the gold-standard life-saving intervention.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02974","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD)","q":"A 23-year-old university student spends 4 hours daily repeatedly checking door locks and washing his hands until raw because of intrusive, unwanted, distressing (\u0027ego-dystonic\u0027) thoughts that his family will die unless he performs the rituals exactly 12 times. What is the evidence-based first-line combined treatment?","o":["Long-term alprazolam monotherapy","Psychoanalysis alone without behavioural exposure","Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitor (SSRI, e.g., fluoxetine/fluvoxamine/sertraline, often at higher doses for 8-12 weeks) PLUS Cognitive Behavioural Therapy with Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP)","Low-dose haloperidol monotherapy"],"a":2,"e":"Recurrent intrusive ego-dystonic thoughts/urges (obsessions) neutralised by repetitive behaviours (compulsions) causing \u003e1 hour/day distress define OCD. First-line management combines an SSRI (titrated to higher anti-obsessional doses with an adequate 8-12 week trial; clomipramine is second-line due to anticholinergic/cardiac side effects) and Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP), which extinguishes anxiety without performing the ritual.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02975","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Parasitic Infestations","q":"A 19-year-old hostel student presents with intensely pruritic papules and excoriations worst at night over the finger web spaces, flexor wrists, axillae, and genitalia, with two roommates reporting similar nocturnal itching. Examination reveals thread-like grey burrows in the interdigital webs. What is the first-line topical treatment and essential household control measure?","o":["Apply 5% Permethrin solely to the visibly itchy spots for 10 minutes on the patient alone","Topical betamethasone monotherapy for 4 weeks","Oral albendazole single dose without treating contacts","5% Permethrin cream applied from neck to toe (left on 8-14 hours overnight, repeated in 7 days) AND simultaneous treatment of all close contacts/roommates plus hot-washing of bedding/clothes"],"a":3,"e":"Sarcoptes scabiei var. hominis causes classic nocturnal pruritus and burrows in finger webs, wrists, axillae, areolae, and genitalia. First-line treatment is 5% Permethrin cream applied thoroughly to ALL skin from jawline/neck down to toes overnight (including scalp/face in infants), repeated after 7 days (or oral ivermectin 200 mcg/kg x 2 doses 1 week apart). Simultaneous treatment of asymptomatic roommates/family contacts and laundering/isolating fomites prevents \u0027ping-pong\u0027 reinfestation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02976","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Papulosquamous Disorders","q":"A 34-year-old man presents with well-demarcated erythematous plaques topped by thick silvery-white micaceous scales over the extensor elbows, knees, and scalp, along with pinpoint bleeding upon scraping off the scales (Auspitz sign) and nail pitting. Why MUST oral/systemic corticosteroids be avoided in routine management of chronic plaque psoriasis?","o":["Tapering or withdrawing systemic corticosteroids frequently triggers severe rebound erythroderma or generalised pustular psoriasis (von Zumbusch)","Oral steroids cause immediate Auspitz-positive melanoma","Systemic steroids have zero effect on psoriasis plaques even transiently","Systemic steroids permanently cure psoriasis in 2 days"],"a":0,"e":"Psoriasis vulgaris presents with well-demarcated erythematous scaly plaques on extensors, Auspitz sign, Koebner isomorphism, and nail pitting/\u0027oil-drop\u0027 signs. Standard treatment uses topical corticosteroids + vitamin D analogues (calcipotriol) for limited disease and phototherapy (NB-UVB), methotrexate, acitretin, cyclosporine, or biologics for extensive/arthropathic disease. Systemic oral/parenteral corticosteroids are contraindicated in routine psoriasis because withdrawal precipitates severe rebound erythrodermic or life-threatening generalised pustular psoriasis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02977","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Shoulder Joint and Rotator Cuff Nerves","q":"A 28-year-old rugby player sustains an anterior-inferior shoulder dislocation and subsequently develops flattened contour of the shoulder, weakness abducting the arm beyond 15 degrees, and sensory loss over the lower half of the deltoid (\u0027regimental badge\u0027 area). Which nerve and anatomical space are compromised, and which muscle initiates the first 0-15 degrees of abduction?","o":["Long thoracic nerve (C5-C7) over serratus anterior; subscapularis initiates abduction","Axillary nerve (C5-C6) passing through the quadrangular space with the posterior circumflex humeral artery; supraspinatus (supplied by suprascapular nerve) initiates 0-15 degrees of abduction","Musculocutaneous nerve piercing coracobrachialis; teres minor initiates abduction","Radial nerve in the triangular interval; infraspinatus initiates abduction"],"a":1,"e":"The axillary nerve (posterior cord, C5-C6) winds posteriorly around the surgical neck of the humerus through the quadrangular space (bounded by teres minor, teres major, long head of triceps, and humeral shaft) alongside the posterior circumflex humeral vessels. Anterior/inferior glenohumeral dislocation or surgical neck fracture injures the axillary nerve, paralyzing deltoid (abduction 15-90 degrees) and teres minor while causing \u0027regimental badge\u0027 anesthesia via the upper lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm. Initial 0-15 degrees of abduction is preserved because supraspinatus is innervated by the suprascapular nerve.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02978","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Wrist and Hand Compartments","q":"A 45-year-old typist with hypothyroidism presents with nocturnal tingling and numbness over the palmar aspect of the lateral 3.5 digits and thenar wasting (loss of thumb opposition), yet cutaneous sensation directly over the central thenar eminence is completely preserved. Which structures traverse the carpal tunnel, and why is sensation over the central thenar palm spared?","o":["Nine extrinsic flexor tendons (4 FDS, 4 FDP, 1 FPL) plus the median nerve traverse the tunnel; the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve arises ~5 cm proximal to the wrist and passes SUPERFICIAL to the flexor retinaculum","Four FDS tendons and ulnar nerve traverse the tunnel; the recurrent motor branch arises inside the palm","Palmaris longus tendon and radial artery traverse the tunnel; the superficial radial nerve supplies the thenar palm","Eight flexor tendons and both median and ulnar nerves traverse the tunnel; ulnar nerve supplies thenar skin"],"a":0,"e":"The carpal tunnel—roofed by the transverse carpal ligament (flexor retinaculum) attaching to scaphoid/trapezium laterally and pisiform/hook of hamate medially—transmits 9 tendons (4 flexor digitorum superficialis, 4 flexor digitorum profundus, flexor pollicis longus) and the median nerve (FCR lies in its own separate lateral compartment; ulnar nerve/artery run superficially in Guyon canal). Because the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve branches off ~5 cm proximal to the flexor retinaculum and travels superficial to the tunnel, sensation over the thenar eminence is spared in classic carpal tunnel compression (whereas the recurrent thenar motor branch to opponens/abductor pollicis brevis/superficial FPB exits distal to the tunnel and is impaired).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02979","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Arm and Forearm Nerves","q":"A 32-year-old man fractures the midshaft of his right humerus in a road traffic accident and develops wrist drop (inability to extend wrist and MCP joints) and numbness over the first dorsal web space, while elbow extension against resistance remains relatively strong. Why is triceps elbow extension spared in a midshaft humeral (radial groove) fracture?","o":["Branches to the long and medial heads of triceps arise proximal to the spiral (radial) groove, whereas branches to wrist/finger extensors (ECRL/ECU/ED) arise within or distal to the groove","Triceps is innervated by the axillary nerve rather than the radial nerve","Midshaft humeral fractures injure only the posterior interosseous nerve at the supinator arcade","Triceps receives dual motor innervation from both musculocutaneous and ulnar nerves"],"a":0,"e":"Level localization of radial nerve lesions is classic clinical anatomy: (1) Axillary compression (\u0027crutch palsy\u0027) knocks out ALL triceps heads -\u003e loss of elbow extension + wrist drop + sensory loss; (2) Midshaft humerus fracture (spiral groove, with profunda brachii artery) spares the high branches to the long head (and often medial head) of triceps -\u003e preserved elbow extension + wrist drop + first dorsal web-space anesthesia; (3) Posterior interosseous nerve (PIN) entrapment at the radial head/arcade of Frohse spares extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL) and superficial sensory branch -\u003e finger drop WITHOUT complete wrist drop (radial deviation on wrist extension) and NO sensory loss.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02980","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Gluteal Region and Pelvis","q":"Following a misplaced deep intramuscular gluteal injection, a patient develops a positive Trendelenburg sign: when standing on the RIGHT leg alone, the LEFT (unsupported) side of the pelvis drops downward. Which nerve and muscles were injured on the weight-bearing right side, and which gluteal quadrant is safest for IM injections?","o":["Left inferior gluteal nerve supplying left gluteus maximus; lower inner quadrant is safest","Right sciatic nerve; upper inner quadrant is safest","Right superior gluteal nerve (L4-S1, exiting suprapiriform foramen) supplying right gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fasciae latae; upper outer (superolateral) quadrant is safest","Right obturator nerve; lower outer quadrant is safest"],"a":2,"e":"During single-leg stance on the right leg, the right hip abductors (gluteus medius and minimus, innervated by the right superior gluteal nerve exiting the greater sciatic foramen SUPERIOR to the piriformis) contract to hold the pelvis level and prevent the unsupported left hemipelvis from sagging. Damage to the right superior gluteal nerve causes the contralateral (left) pelvis to drop when standing on the injured right leg (positive Trendelenburg sign; \u0027sound side sags\u0027). To avoid the sciatic nerve, posterior femoral cutaneous nerve, and gluteal vessels (exiting infrapiriform/suprapiriform medially), IM injections are placed in the upper outer (superolateral) quadrant or ventrogluteal triangle.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02981","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Leg Compartments and Foot Arches","q":"A patient wearing a tight below-knee plaster cast develops high-stepping \u0027foot drop\u0027 (loss of ankle dorsiflexion and eversion) with numbness over the anterolateral leg and dorsum of the foot (including the first web space), while plantar flexion and ankle inversion remain intact. Which nerve is compressed and at what bony landmark?","o":["Tibial nerve passing behind the medial malleolus inside the tarsal tunnel","Superficial fibular nerve alone (which preserves dorsiflexion)","Deep fibular nerve alone inside the anterior compartment (which preserves eversion)","Common fibular (peroneal) nerve winding subcutaneously around the neck of the fibula"],"a":3,"e":"The common fibular (peroneal) nerve wraps laterally and superficially around the neck of the fibula before dividing into superficial fibular (lateral compartment evertors: fibularis longus/brevis + anterolateral leg/dorsal foot skin) and deep fibular (anterior compartment dorsiflexors: tibialis anterior, EHL, EDL + 1st dorsal web space skin) branches. Tight casts, lithotomy stirrups, or fibular neck fractures compress the common fibular nerve, abolishing BOTH dorsiflexion and eversion while sparing tibial nerve-mediated plantar flexion and tibialis posterior inversion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02982","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Larynx Functional Anatomy","q":"Which intrinsic laryngeal muscle is the SOLE abductor of the vocal folds (widening the rima glottidis for breathing, known as the \u0027safety muscle\u0027 of the larynx), and which muscle is uniquely supplied by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve?","o":["Posterior cricoarytenoid (sole abductor, supplied by recurrent laryngeal nerve); cricothyroid (tenses/elongates vocal folds to raise pitch, supplied by external laryngeal nerve)","Thyroarytenoid (sole abductor); posterior cricoarytenoid (supplied by external laryngeal nerve)","Transverse arytenoid (sole abductor); vocalis (supplied by internal laryngeal nerve)","Lateral cricoarytenoid (sole abductor); thyroarytenoid (supplied by external laryngeal nerve)"],"a":0,"e":"All intrinsic muscles of the larynx are innervated by the recurrent laryngeal nerve EXCEPT the cricothyroid (which is innervated by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve—risked during superior thyroid artery ligation—and tilts the thyroid cartilage forward/downward to tense the vocal cords for high-pitched speech). Only ONE paired muscle abducts (opens) the vocal cords to maintain an open airway: the posterior cricoarytenoid (\u0027safety muscle\u0027); lateral cricoarytenoids, transverse/oblique arytenoids, and thyroarytenoids all adduct or relax the folds. Internal laryngeal nerve provides purely sensory innervation above the vocal folds (afferent of cough reflex).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02983","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Pharynx and Esophagus","q":"An elderly man presents with halitosis, dysphagia, gurgling neck sounds, and regurgitation of undigested food eaten days earlier. Barium swallow reveals a posterior midline pharyngeal pouch (Zenker diverticulum) herniating through Killian dehiscence. Between which two muscular parts of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor does Killian dehiscence lie?","o":["Between the oblique fibers of the thyropharyngeus superiorly and the horizontal/transverse sphincter fibers of the cricopharyngeus inferiorly","Between the superior and middle pharyngeal constrictors (where stylopharyngeus enters)","Above the superior constrictor at the sinus of Morgagni (where auditory tube enters)","Between the cricopharyngeus and circular esophageal muscle (Laimer area)"],"a":0,"e":"The inferior pharyngeal constrictor has two parts: upper oblique fibers (thyropharyngeus, propelling food downward via pharyngeal plexus CN X) and lower horizontal fibers (cricopharyngeus, acting as the upper esophageal sphincter). Between these two parts posteriorly lies a triangular weak area devoid of longitudinal backing—Killian dehiscence. Uncoordinated failure of cricopharyngeus relaxation raises intrapharyngeal pressure, pushing mucosa/submucosa posteriorly out through Killian dehiscence as a false pulsion diverticulum (Zenker diverticulum, usually deflecting slightly left).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02984","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Extraocular Muscles","q":"During an extraocular muscle clinical exam, a student asks a patient to first look medially (adduct the eye) and then look DOWNWARD toward the tip of the nose to isolate the depressor action of which extraocular muscle, and how is this muscle\u0027s nerve tested when the eye is abducted instead?","o":["Inferior rectus (CN III); in adduction, depression tests medial rectus","Superior oblique (CN IV, trochlear nerve—depresses and intorts; isolated depressor in ADDUCTION); in ABDUCTION, depression tests the inferior rectus (CN III)","Superior rectus (CN III); in adduction, elevation tests lateral rectus","Inferior oblique (CN III—elevates and extorts; isolated in adduction); in abduction, elevation tests superior oblique"],"a":1,"e":"Because the orbital axis diverges laterally from the visual axis, recti and obliques pull at different angles depending on horizontal gaze: (1) Superior and inferior recti align with the visual axis when the eye is ABDUCTED (so in abduction, looking up isolates superior rectus and looking down isolates inferior rectus); (2) Superior and inferior obliques align along the visual axis when the eye is ADDUCTED (so in adduction, looking down isolates superior oblique [CN IV], and looking up isolates inferior oblique [CN III]). Thus CN IV palsy impairs downward gaze while adducted (difficulty walking downstairs/reading), causing compensatory head tilt AWAY from the paralyzed side to counter extorsion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02985","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Deep Cervical Fascia and Neck Spaces","q":"Assertion (A): Acute suppurative infections originating in the posterior pharynx or lower molar roots can track inferiorly through the neck to cause life-threatening descending necrotizing mediastinitis. Reason (R): The true retropharyngeal space (between buccopharyngeal fascia and alar fascia, extending to T1-T4) and the immediately posterior \u0027danger space\u0027 (between alar fascia and prevertebral fascia) provide uninterrupted loose areolar conduits extending directly down into the posterior mediastinum to the level of the diaphragm.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Deep cervical fascial planes dictate infection spread: the visceral/pretracheal fascia encloses the thyroid/trachea/esophagus (buccopharyngeal fascia posteriorly) and blends inferiorly with the fibrous pericardium in the anterior/middle mediastinum. Behind the buccopharyngeal fascia lies the retropharyngeal space (to ~T1-T4 where alar fascia fuses with visceral fascia), and immediately behind the thin alar fascia—anterior to prevertebral fascia—lies the \u0027danger space\u0027 (space 4 of Grodinsky-Holyoke), running uninterrupted from the skull base straight through the posterior mediastinum to the diaphragm. Perforation of the alar fascia allows rapid mediastinal dissemination.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02986","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Portal Venous System","q":"Which pairing accurately identifies the portal tributary and systemic venous counterpart at the three major clinical sites of portosystemic anastomosis in portal hypertension (esophageal varices, caput medusae, and anorectal varices)?","o":["Lower esophagus: splenic vein \u003c-\u003e pulmonary veins; Umbilicus: hepatic vein \u003c-\u003e internal thoracic vein; Anorectum: inferior mesenteric artery \u003c-\u003e internal pudendal vein","Lower esophagus: short gastric veins \u003c-\u003e coronary sinus; Umbilicus: renal vein \u003c-\u003e lumbar veins; Anorectum: superior rectal \u003c-\u003e ovarian vein","Lower esophagus: left gastric (coronary) vein (portal) \u003c-\u003e esophageal branches of azygos/hemiazygos veins (systemic); Umbilicus: paraumbilical veins (portal) \u003c-\u003e superficial/inferior epigastric veins (systemic); Anorectum: superior rectal vein (portal) \u003c-\u003e middle and inferior rectal veins (systemic)","Lower esophagus: right gastroepiploic vein \u003c-\u003e IVC; Umbilicus: cystic vein \u003c-\u003e great saphenous vein; Anorectum: middle rectal vein \u003c-\u003e portal vein"],"a":2,"e":"When portal venous pressure rises (\u003e10-12 mmHg in cirrhosis), valveless portosystemic collateral channels dilate at four classic sites: (1) Gastroesophageal junction—left gastric (and short gastric) portal veins anastomose with esophageal tributaries of the azygos/hemiazygos caval system (producing life-threatening bleeding esophageal varices); (2) Paraumbilical region—paraumbilical veins traveling in the ligamentum teres communicate with superior/inferior/superficial epigastric veins (\u0027caput medusae\u0027); (3) Anorectal junction—superior rectal vein (from IMV, portal) communicates with middle/inferior rectal veins (internal iliac/pudendal, systemic); and (4) Retroperitoneum (veins of Retzius) and bare area of liver (veins of Sappey).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02987","course":"MBBS","subject":"Anatomy","topic":"Extrahepatic Biliary Apparatus","q":"During laparoscopic cholecystectomy, the surgeon achieves the \u0027Critical View of Safety\u0027 by clearing fat and fibrous tissue within the cystohepatic (Calot) triangle to isolate the cystic artery and cystic duct. Which structures form the three boundaries of the modern surgical cystohepatic triangle, and from which parent vessel does the cystic artery most commonly arise inside it?","o":["Medially: proper hepatic artery; Inferiorly: duodenum; Superiorly: stomach; cystic artery arises from the splenic artery","Medially: inferior vena cava; Inferiorly: pancreatic duct; Superiorly: falciform ligament; cystic artery arises from the SMA","Medially: portal vein; Inferiorly: common bile duct; Superiorly: diaphragm; cystic artery arises from the left gastric artery","Medially: common hepatic duct; Inferiorly/laterally: cystic duct; Superiorly: inferior surface of the liver; cystic artery usually arises from the right hepatic artery"],"a":3,"e":"The modern surgical cystohepatic triangle (triangle of Calot) is bounded medially by the common hepatic duct, inferiorly (laterally) by the cystic duct, and superiorly by the inferior visceral surface of the liver (note: Calot\u0027s original 1891 description used the cystic artery as the superior border). In ~70-80% of individuals, the cystic artery originates from the right hepatic artery within this triangle (along with the cystic lymph node of Lund). Recognizing an anomalous caterpillar/Moynihan hump of the right hepatic artery looping near the gallbladder neck prevents catastrophic bile duct or hepatic arterial injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02988","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Resting Membrane Potential and Nernst Equation","q":"A mammalian neuron at 37°C has an intracellular potassium concentration [K+]i of 150 mEq/L and an extracellular potassium concentration [K+]o of 3.75 mEq/L (hypokalemia). Using the Nernst equation for a monovalent cation at 37°C [E_K = -61.5 x log10([K+]i / [K+]o)] with log10(40) ~ 1.60, what is the potassium equilibrium potential (E_K), and how does hypokalemia shift the resting membrane potential?","o":["E_K = -98.4 mV; hypokalemia hyperpolarizes the resting membrane potential (making it more negative)","E_K = -30.8 mV; hypokalemia has zero effect on E_K","E_K = +98.4 mV; hypokalemia triggers spontaneous depolarization","E_K = -61.5 mV; hypokalemia depolarizes the resting membrane potential"],"a":0,"e":"Using the Nernst equation for K+ (valence z = +1) at 37°C: E_K = -61.5 x log10([K+]i / [K+]o) = -61.5 x log10(150 / 3.75) = -61.5 x log10(40) = -61.5 x 1.60 = -98.4 mV (more negative than normal E_K of ~ -90 to -95 mV when [K+]o = 4.5 mEq/L). Because resting membrane potential sits closest to E_K (due to high resting K+ leak conductance), hypokalemia hyperpolarizes skeletal/cardiac membranes (note: in cardiac ventricular/Purkinje fibers, severe hypokalemia also paradoxically reduces I_Kr K+ channel conductance and prolongs repolarization, predisposing to U waves and torsades).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02989","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Excitation-Contraction Coupling","q":"How do skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, and smooth muscle differ in their sarcoplasmic reticulum Ca2+ release coupling and Ca2+-binding regulatory proteins during contraction?","o":["Skeletal muscle requires extracellular Ca2+ influx for every twitch; Smooth muscle uses troponin C exclusively","Skeletal muscle uses direct mechanical coupling between T-tubule DHPR (CaV1.1) and SR RyR1 (requiring NO extracellular Ca2+ influx) and binds Ca2+ to troponin C; Cardiac muscle uses Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release (extracellular Ca2+ entering L-type DHPR triggers SR RyR2) and binds Ca2+ to troponin C; Smooth muscle lacks troponin and instead binds Ca2+ to calmodulin to activate myosin light-chain kinase (MLCK)","Smooth muscle uses RyR1 mechanical coupling in T-tubules and activates troponin I","Cardiac muscle uses direct mechanical DHPR-RyR1 coupling without extracellular Ca2+; Skeletal muscle uses calmodulin-MLCK"],"a":1,"e":"The three muscle types have distinct E-C coupling architectures: (1) Skeletal muscle T-tubule voltage-sensing dihydropyridine receptors (DHPR) are physically tethered to sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR) ryanodine receptors (RyR1)—so depolarization mechanically pulls RyR1 open without needing extracellular Ca2+ influx, and Ca2+ binds troponin C to move tropomyosin; (2) Cardiac muscle relies on Phase 2 plateau Ca2+ influx through L-type Ca2+ channels (DHPR) to trigger SR RyR2 opening (Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release, CICR), also binding troponin C; (3) Smooth muscle lacks T-tubules and troponin altogether—Ca2+ enters via voltage/ligand-gated channels + IP3-mediated SR release, binds calmodulin, activates MLCK to phosphorylate myosin light chains (opposed by NO-cGMP-activated myosin light-chain phosphatase).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02990","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Neuromuscular Junction (NMJ) Transmission","q":"In an electrophysiology recording at the skeletal neuromuscular junction, a patient with fluctuating ptosis, diplopia, and proximal muscle fatigable weakness worsening at the end of the day demonstrates reduced amplitude of miniature end-plate potentials (MEPPs) at normal quantal frequency, plus a decremental compound muscle action potential (CMAP) on repetitive 3-Hz nerve stimulation that improves transiently after edrophonium. What explains the reduced MEPP amplitude?","o":["Deficiency of acetylcholinesterase in the synaptic cleft prolongs end-plate potentials","Autoantibodies against presynaptic P/Q-type voltage-gated Ca2+ channels reduce ACh quantum content per nerve impulse (Lambert-Eaton), raising MEPP frequency","Autoantibodies against postsynaptic nicotinic ACh receptors (N_M) at the motor end-plate reduce functional receptor density (myasthenia gravis), decreasing postsynaptic depolarization per spontaneously released ACh vesicle (quantum)","Botulinum toxin cleavage of SNAP-25 completely halts spontaneous vesicle fusion without affecting postsynaptic receptors"],"a":2,"e":"Each spontaneous fusion of a single presynaptic ACh synaptic vesicle produces a tiny ~0.5 mV depolarization at the motor end-plate called a miniature end-plate potential (MEPP). In myasthenia gravis (often associated with thymic hyperplasia/thymoma), IgG autoantibodies destroy postsynaptic N_M nicotinic ACh receptors via complement and receptor internalization—reducing the end-plate response to EVERY single vesicle (smaller MEPP amplitude) while presynaptic vesicle release frequency remains normal. Repetitive stimulation depletes readily releasable ACh vesicles, dropping end-plate potentials below firing threshold (decremental CMAP), whereas AChE inhibitors (edrophonium, pyridostigmine) prolong ACh dwell time to restore transmission.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02991","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Jugular Venous Pulse","q":"During a cardiovascular physiology lab analyzing simultaneous Wiggers cardiac cycle and jugular venous pressure (JVP) tracings, which event produces (1) the S1 heart sound, (2) the prominent JVP \u0027v\u0027 wave, (3) a \u0027cannon a wave\u0027 on JVP, and (4) the physiological/pathological S3 heart sound?","o":["(1) Opening snap of mitral valve; (2) tricuspid valve opening; (3) atrial fibrillation; (4) isovolumetric relaxation","(1) Semilunar valve closure; (2) rapid ventricular ejection; (3) first-degree AV block; (4) late diastolic atrial systole","(1) Aortic valve closure; (2) atrial contraction; (3) mitral stenosis; (4) atrial kick against a stiff ventricle (which actually causes S4)","(1) Mitral and tricuspid (AV) valve closure at onset of isovolumetric contraction; (2) passive right atrial filling against a closed tricuspid valve during late ventricular systole; (3) atrial contraction against a closed tricuspid valve in complete (3rd-degree) AV block/VT; (4) rapid passive ventricular filling in early diastole"],"a":3,"e":"Correlating the cardiac cycle and JVP: S1 marks closure of mitral/tricuspid valves at the start of isovolumetric ventricular contraction; S2 marks closure of aortic/pulmonic valves at onset of isovolumetric relaxation; S3 occurs during rapid early diastolic passive filling (normal in children/athletes/pregnancy, volume overload/HFrEF in older adults); S4 occurs in late diastole during atrial kick into a stiff non-compliant ventricle (LVH/AS). On JVP: \u0027a\u0027 wave = atrial contraction (absent in atrial fibrillation; giant \u0027a\u0027 in tricuspid stenosis/pulmonary HTN; irregular \u0027cannon a\u0027 when atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve in AV dissociation/complete heart block); \u0027c\u0027 wave = tricuspid bulging during isovolumetric RV contraction; \u0027x\u0027 descent = atrial relaxation; \u0027v\u0027 wave = passive atrial venous filling against closed tricuspid valve; \u0027y\u0027 descent = rapid RV emptying once tricuspid opens.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02992","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Hemodynamics and Poiseuille Law","q":"A vasoconstrictor agonist reduces the luminal radius (r) of an isolated resistance arteriole to half (50%) of its baseline radius while blood viscosity (eta), vessel length (L), and driving pressure gradient (Delta-P) remain constant. According to Poiseuille\u0027s law [R = (8 x eta x L) / (pi x r^4) and Q = Delta-P / R], by what factor do vascular resistance (R) and blood flow (Q) change?","o":["Resistance increases 16-fold; blood flow decreases to 1/16 (6.25%) of baseline","Resistance increases 8-fold; blood flow decreases to 1/8 (12.5%) of baseline","Resistance increases 4-fold; blood flow decreases to 1/4 (25%) of baseline","Resistance increases 2-fold; blood flow decreases to 1/2 (50%) of baseline"],"a":0,"e":"Under laminar flow described by Poiseuille\u0027s law, vascular resistance R is inversely proportional to the FOURTH power of vessel radius (R proportional to 1/r^4). Reducing radius r to 0.5r gives R_new = R_old / (0.5)^4 = R_old / (1/16) = 16 x R_old. Because blood flow Q = Delta-P / R at constant driving pressure, flow drops 16-fold to 1/16 (6.25%) of baseline—explaining why arterioles (the primary site of sympathetic and local radius modulation) exert dominant control over total peripheral resistance and organ perfusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02993","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Physiological Adaptations of Pregnancy","q":"A healthy 26-year-old primigravida at 30 weeks of gestation has Hb 10.8 g/dL, BP 102/64 mmHg, heart rate 88/min, and arterial PaCO2 30 mmHg. Which physiological mechanisms explain this combination of mild dilutional anemia, lowered diastolic blood pressure, and mild hypocapnia?","o":["Estrogen induces pulmonary fibrosis; fetal hemoglobin replaces maternal HbA","Plasma volume expands ~45-50% while RBC mass rises only ~20-30% (physiological hemodilution); progesterone/NO/relaxin lower systemic vascular resistance (increasing cardiac output ~30-50%); and progesterone stimulates central respiratory centers to increase tidal volume (hyperventilation aiding fetal CO2 offloading)","Plasma volume contracts by 30%; aldosterone is completely suppressed; and maternal PaCO2 rises to 50 mmHg","RBC mass decreases by 40% due to erythropoietin suppression; systemic vascular resistance doubles; and elevated diaphragm causes chronic hypercapnia"],"a":1,"e":"Normal pregnancy induces profound physiological adaptations: (1) RAAS activation expands plasma volume by ~45-50% outstripping the ~20-30% rise in erythropoietin-driven RBC mass, producing physiological anemia of pregnancy (and lowering blood viscosity); (2) Progesterone, prostacyclin, NO, and uteroplacental low-resistance shunting drop systemic vascular resistance (lowering MAP/DBP in mid-trimester despite 30-50% higher cardiac output and GFR); and (3) Progesterone directly sensitizes the medullary respiratory center to CO2, raising minute ventilation (via larger tidal volume, unchanged respiratory rate) and lowering maternal PaCO2 to ~28-32 mmHg to steepen the fetal-to-maternal placental CO2 diffusion gradient.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02994","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Menstrual Cycle Endocrinology","q":"A 28-year-old woman tracking ovulation notices a 0.4°C sustained rise in morning basal body temperature and thick, tacky non-ferning cervical mucus on Day 16 of a 28-day cycle. Which hormonal switch triggered ovulation ~24-36 hours earlier, and which hormone causes the post-ovulatory rise in basal body temperature?","o":["Falling progesterone triggers an FSH-only surge; estrogen causes the sustained luteal temperature rise","Inhibin B stimulates the LH surge; prolactin raises basal body temperature","Sustained high follicular estradiol (\u003e200 pg/mL for ~48 hours) switches feedback from negative to POSITIVE at the anterior pituitary/hypothalamus (kisspeptin AVPV) to trigger the mid-cycle LH surge; corpus luteum PROGESTERONE raises the hypothalamic thermoregulatory set-point","Testosterone suppresses GnRH pulsatility; hCG raises basal temperature in every non-pregnant cycle"],"a":2,"e":"During the early-to-mid follicular phase, moderate estrogen levels exert negative feedback on GnRH/FSH/LH (aided by granulosa-cell inhibin B suppressing FSH). As the dominant Graafian follicle matures, sustained high estradiol (\u003e200 pg/mL for ~36-50 hours) flips feedback to POSITIVE—increasing pituitary GnRH receptor sensitivity and AVPV kisspeptin firing to unleash the mid-cycle LH (and smaller FSH) surge, which triggers meiosis I completion and ovulation ~24-36 hours later (10-12 hr after LH peak). Following ovulation, the corpus luteum secretes progesterone (transforming endometrium to secretory phase, thickening cervical mucus without \u0027fern\u0027 crystallization, and acting on preoptic hypothalamic thermoregulatory neurons to elevate basal body temperature by ~0.3-0.5°C).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02995","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Lactation Physiology","q":"Assertion (A): Although maternal plasma prolactin rises progressively throughout pregnancy, copious milk secretion begins only 2-4 days after delivery of the placenta, and ongoing lactation suppresses ovulation (lactational amenorrhea). Reason (R): High placental estrogen and progesterone during pregnancy block prolactin\u0027s lactogenic action on mammary alveolar epithelium until placental expulsion removes that inhibition, while infant suckling stimulates prolactin (via inhibition of hypothalamic tuberoinfundibular dopamine) and oxytocin (milk ejection) while suppressing pulsatile GnRH release.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"During pregnancy, estrogen and progesterone stimulate mammary ductal and lobuloalveolar growth while high estrogen drives pituitary lactotroph hyperplasia and prolactin secretion—yet high placental progesterone/estrogen inhibit prolactin-induced casein/lactose synthesis at the mammary gland. Delivery of the placenta causes an abrupt drop in estrogen/progesterone, unleashing prolactin-mediated milk synthesis (galactopoiesis). Infant nipple suckling activates a neuroendocrine reflex that: (1) suppresses tuberoinfundibular hypothalamic dopamine (prolactin-inhibiting factor) to pulse prolactin, (2) triggers paraventricular/supraoptic oxytocin release contracting myoepithelial cells (milk let-down), and (3) inhibits arcuate GnRH pulsatility, suppressing LH/FSH and ovulation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02996","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Growth Hormone and IGF-1 Axis","q":"A 41-year-old man presents with enlarging hands and shoe size, coarse facial features, prognathism, macroglossia, carpal tunnel syndrome, and fasting hyperglycemia. Serum IGF-1 is elevated, and during an oral glucose tolerance test (75 g glucose), serum growth hormone (GH) fails to suppress below 1 ng/mL. Which direct metabolic actions of GH explain his hyperglycemia, and which hypothalamic peptide normally suppresses GH?","o":["GH increases peripheral glucose uptake like insulin; hypothalamic CRH inhibits GH","IGF-1 causes severe hypoglycemia during OGTT; hypothalamic dopamine stimulates GH","GH directly stimulates pancreatic beta-cell apoptosis without affecting peripheral insulin sensitivity; hypothalamic TRH inhibits GH","GH exerts direct anti-insulin actions (decreasing peripheral skeletal muscle glucose uptake, increasing hepatic gluconeogenesis, and stimulating adipose lipolysis), while skeletal linear/soft-tissue growth is mediated by hepatic IGF-1 (somatomedin C); hypothalamic somatostatin inhibits GH"],"a":3,"e":"Growth hormone (somatotropin) is secreted pulsatilely by anterior pituitary somatotrophs (stimulated by hypothalamic GHRH, ghrelin, deep N3 sleep, exercise, hypoglycemia, and arginine; inhibited by hypothalamic somatostatin and negative feedback from IGF-1/free fatty acids/hyperglycemia). GH acts via JAK2-STAT5 receptors to stimulate hepatic IGF-1 (promoting epiphyseal chondrogenesis and visceral/soft-tissue growth) while directly opposing insulin in metabolism (diabetogenic effect: lipolysis, hepatic gluconeogenesis, and insulin resistance in muscle/adipose). In healthy subjects, an oral glucose load suppresses GH (\u003c0.4-1 ng/mL); failure to suppress during OGTT plus elevated IGF-1 confirms acromegaly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02997","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Iron Homeostasis and Carbon Dioxide Transport","q":"In anemia of chronic inflammation, elevated IL-6 stimulates hepatic synthesis of hepcidin, trapping iron inside macrophages and duodenal enterocytes. Which transporter does hepcidin bind and degrade, and in RBC physiology, which exchanger mediates the Hamburger chloride shift in systemic venous capillaries?","o":["Hepcidin binds and induces internalization/degradation of basolateral FERROPORTIN (iron exporter); the Hamburger chloride shift uses Band 3 (AE1) Cl-/HCO3- anion exchanger on erythrocyte membranes","Hepcidin degrades duodenal cytochrome B (Dcytb); chloride shift uses SGLT1","Hepcidin degrades apical DMT1 (divalent metal transporter-1); chloride shift uses Na+/K+-ATPase","Hepcidin activates transferrin receptor-1 on enterocytes; chloride shift uses aquaporin-1"],"a":0,"e":"Non-heme dietary Fe3+ is reduced by apical Dcytb to Fe2+ and imported into duodenal enterocytes via DMT1, then exported into portal blood (and out of splenic/hepatic macrophages) exclusively via basolateral ferroportin before oxidation by hephaestin/ceruloplasmin onto transferrin. Hepcidin—the master iron-regulatory peptide hormone upregulated by iron overload and inflammatory IL-6 (and suppressed by hypoxia/erythropoiesis via erythroferrone)—binds ferroportin and triggers its lysosomal degradation, causing functional iron sequestration. Separately, ~70% of blood CO2 travels as plasma HCO3-: CO2 enters RBCs, forms H2CO3 -\u003e H+ + HCO3- via carbonic anhydrase, and HCO3- exits into plasma in exchange for Cl- via Band 3 (anion exchanger 1, AE1; \u0027Hamburger chloride shift\u0027, causing venous RBCs to swell slightly).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02998","course":"MBBS","subject":"Physiology","topic":"Coagulation Cascade and Fibrinolysis","q":"Which coagulation factor initiates the extrinsic pathway (measured by Prothrombin Time, PT) upon vascular injury, which factors are proteolytically inactivated by activated Protein C (with cofactor Protein S), and what does an elevated plasma D-dimer specifically prove?","o":["Von Willebrand factor initiates the extrinsic pathway; Protein S activates prothrombin; D-dimer measures PT prolongation","Tissue Factor (Factor III) complexing with Factor VIIa initiates the extrinsic pathway; activated Protein C/S inactivates Factors Va and VIIIa; D-dimer proves plasmin-mediated breakdown of cross-linked fibrin (Factor XIIIa-stabilized clot)","Factor VIII initiates the extrinsic pathway; Antithrombin III inactivates Factors Va and VIIIa; D-dimer measures platelet count","Factor XII (Hageman factor) initiates the extrinsic pathway; Protein C inactivates Factors IXa and XIa; D-dimer indicates fibrinogenolysis without clotting"],"a":1,"e":"In vivo coagulation is triggered primarily by exposed subendothelial Tissue Factor (Factor III) binding circulating Factor VIIa (extrinsic pathway, assessed by PT/INR), activating Factors X and IX. Thrombin (IIa) converts fibrinogen to fibrin, activates Factor XIII (transglutaminase cross-linking fibrin polymers), and binds endothelial thrombomodulin to activate vitamin K-dependent Protein C—which, aided by Protein S, proteolytically cleaves cofactors Va and VIIIa to prevent runaway thrombosis. During fibrinolysis, tPA converts plasminogen to plasmin, cleaving cross-linked fibrin exclusively into D-dimer fragments (elevated D-dimer proves both prior thrombin/Factor XIIIa clot formation and active fibrinolysis, e.g., DVT/PE/DIC).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q02999","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Hemoglobin Allostery and Protein Misfolding","q":"Where does 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG) bind on adult hemoglobin (HbA), why does fetal hemoglobin (HbF, alpha2-gamma2) exhibit higher oxygen affinity than HbA, and what secondary-structure transition characterizes prion (PrP^Sc) misfolding?","o":["2,3-BPG binds only oxygenated R-state hemoglobin; HbF has a hyperbolic Hill coefficient of 1.0 like myoglobin","2,3-BPG binds alpha-globin N-termini covalently; HbF lacks alpha-globin chains; PrP^Sc results from DNA point mutations in all sporadic CJD cases","2,3-BPG binds inside the central positively charged cavity (His/Lys residues) between the two beta-globin chains in the deoxy T-state; HbF gamma-globin replaces beta-His143 with neutral Ser143, lowering 2,3-BPG binding and shifting O2 affinity higher; PrP^C converts from predominantly alpha-helical to protease-resistant beta-sheet aggregates (PrP^Sc)","2,3-BPG binds directly to the heme Fe2+ sixth coordination site; HbF binds 2,3-BPG 10-fold more tightly; PrP^C converts from beta-sheet to alpha-helix"],"a":2,"e":"HbA (alpha2-beta2) displays positive cooperativity (sigmoidal O2 binding, Hill coefficient n ~2.8) as O2 binding breaks salt bridges to shift from taut (T) to relaxed (R) quaternary state. Negatively charged 2,3-BPG (from the Rapoport-Luebering RBC shunt) wedges inside the central cavity between the two beta-subunits (lined by positively charged Lys82, His2, His143) ONLY in the wider T-state, stabilizing deoxyhemoglobin and promoting O2 release. Because HbF (alpha2-gamma2) substitutes neutral Ser for positively charged His143 on gamma chains, it binds 2,3-BPG weakly, giving higher O2 affinity to extract O2 across the placenta. In prion diseases, normal alpha-helix-rich PrP^C refolds post-translationally (without primary amino acid sequence change) into insoluble, proteinase-K-resistant beta-sheet-rich PrP^Sc fibrils.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03000","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Mitochondrial Electron Transport Chain and Oxidative Phosphorylation","q":"In an isolated mitochondrial respirometry experiment with NADH and ADP/Pi, adding Drug X causes oxygen consumption (NADH oxidation) to surge maximally and core temperature to rise sharply while ATP synthesis drops to zero. Conversely, adding Oligomycin halts BOTH ATP synthesis and oxygen consumption until Drug X is added. Which classes of agents are Drug X and Oligomycin?","o":["Drug X is cyanide (Complex IV inhibitor); Oligomycin inhibits Complex II","Drug X is atractyloside (ANT inhibitor); Oligomycin activates Complex III","Drug X is rotenone (Complex I inhibitor); Oligomycin is an uncoupler","Drug X is an uncoupler/protonophore (e.g., 2,4-dinitrophenol, high-dose aspirin, or physiological brown-fat UCP1/thermogenin) that dissipates the inner-membrane H+ gradient as heat; Oligomycin inhibits the Fo proton channel of Complex V (ATP synthase)"],"a":3,"e":"Normally, electron transport (NADH -\u003e O2) and ATP synthesis are tightly coupled via the proton-motive force across the inner mitochondrial membrane. Oligomycin plugs the Fo proton channel of ATP synthase (Complex V): H+ cannot re-enter the matrix, so the back-pressure of the proton gradient halts BOTH ATP synthesis and ETC O2 consumption. Adding an uncoupler (2,4-DNP, salicylate toxicity, or endogenous brown-adipose thermogenin/UCP1) creates a proton leak bypassing ATP synthase—collapsing ATP synthesis while releasing the proton gradient back-pressure so ETC electron flow and O2 consumption run wildly unrestrained, liberating free energy as heat (hyperthermia/non-shivering thermogenesis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03001","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Cytosolic NADH Shuttles and Substrate-Level Phosphorylation","q":"Because the inner mitochondrial membrane is impermeable to NADH, cytosolic NADH from glycolysis transfers reducing equivalents via shuttles. Which tissues use the malate-aspartate shuttle versus the glycerol-3-phosphate shuttle, what is their ATP yield per cytosolic NADH, and which enzymes catalyze substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis and the TCA cycle?","o":["Malate-aspartate shuttle (liver, heart, kidney; regenerates mitochondrial NADH -\u003e ~2.5 ATP); Glycerol-3-phosphate shuttle (skeletal muscle, brain; reduces FAD to FADH2 -\u003e ~1.5 ATP); Substrate-level phosphorylation: phosphoglycerate kinase, pyruvate kinase (glycolysis), and succinyl-CoA synthetase (TCA)","Malate-aspartate shuttle yields 1.5 ATP via Complex II; Glycerol-3-phosphate yields 2.5 ATP via Complex I; hexokinase catalyzes substrate-level phosphorylation","Both shuttles transport NADH directly through porins yielding 3 ATP; isocitrate dehydrogenase catalyzes substrate-level phosphorylation","Carnitine shuttle transports cytosolic NADH; citrate synthase catalyzes substrate-level phosphorylation"],"a":0,"e":"In heart, liver, and kidney, the reversible malate-aspartate shuttle transfers cytosolic NADH electrons via malate into the matrix, regenerating mitochondrial NADH that enters Complex I (~2.5 ATP/NADH, making aerobic glucose yield ~32 ATP). In skeletal muscle and brain, the irreversible glycerol-3-phosphate shuttle transfers electrons from cytosolic glycerol-3-P dehydrogenase (NADH) to mitochondrial FAD-linked glycerol-3-P dehydrogenase, feeding CoQ directly (~1.5 ATP/NADH, ~30 ATP/glucose). Three human metabolic enzymes generate ATP/GTP directly via substrate-level phosphorylation without the ETC: phosphoglycerate kinase (1,3-BPG -\u003e 3-PG), pyruvate kinase (PEP -\u003e pyruvate), and succinyl-CoA synthetase/thiokinase (succinyl-CoA -\u003e succinate, GTP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03002","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Gluconeogenesis Regulation and Cori/Cahill Cycles","q":"During fasting, glucagon rises and activates hepatic cAMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA), flipping the bifunctional enzyme PFK-2/FBPase-2 to degrade fructose-2,6-bisphosphate (F-2,6-BP). How does this drop in F-2,6-BP reciprocally regulate hepatic glycolysis versus gluconeogenesis, and which obligate activator is required by mitochondrial pyruvate carboxylase?","o":["Falling F-2,6-BP removes potent allosteric ACTIVATION of PFK-1 (halting glycolysis) and relieves allosteric INHIBITION of fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase (accelerating gluconeogenesis); pyruvate carboxylase requires biotin and allosteric activation by acetyl-CoA","Falling F-2,6-BP inhibits glycogen phosphorylase; pyruvate carboxylase requires thiamine pyrophosphate","Glucagon increases hepatic F-2,6-BP via PKA phosphorylation of PFK-2; PEPCK is activated by AMP","Falling F-2,6-BP activates PFK-1 and inhibits FBPase-1; pyruvate carboxylase is inhibited by acetyl-CoA"],"a":0,"e":"Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate (F-2,6-BP) is the strongest physiological coordinator of hepatic glycolysis vs gluconeogenesis: it allosterically ACTIVATES PFK-1 (glycolysis) and INHIBITS fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase-1 (gluconeogenesis). During fasting, glucagon -\u003e PKA phosphorylates the bifunctional PFK-2/FBPase-2 enzyme, inactivating the PFK-2 kinase domain and activating the FBPase-2 phosphatase domain—depleting F-2,6-BP so PFK-1 shuts down and FBPase-1 turns on. Simultaneously, fatty acid beta-oxidation supplies ATP and acetyl-CoA, which is an obligate allosteric activator of biotin-dependent pyruvate carboxylase (converting pyruvate -\u003e oxaloacetate, followed by PEPCK -\u003e PEP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03003","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Fructose Metabolism Disorders","q":"A 6-month-old infant exclusively breastfed previously develops recurrent vomiting, lethargy, severe fasting/postprandial hypoglycemia, jaundice, hepatomegaly, and lactic acidosis immediately after weaning onto fruit purees and sucrose-sweetened cereal. Urine tests positive for reducing sugars (negative glucose oxidase). Deficiency of which enzyme explains this life-threatening phosphate-trapping disorder, and why is benign fructokinase deficiency asymptomatic?","o":["Galactokinase deficiency causes post-sucrose hypoglycemia; Aldolase A causes hepatomegaly","Aldolase B (fructose-1-phosphate aldolase) deficiency causing hereditary fructose intolerance: trapped fructose-1-phosphate depletes hepatic inorganic phosphate (Pi) and ATP, inhibiting glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis; fructokinase deficiency (essential fructosuria) leaves unmetabolized fructose to spill harmlessly into urine without trapping Pi","Fructokinase deficiency causes severe hypoglycemia; Aldolase B causes benign essential fructosuria","Sucrase-isomaltase deficiency causes phosphate trapping in liver"],"a":1,"e":"Dietary sucrose (glucose + fructose) and fruit juices deliver fructose, which hepatic fructokinase (ketohexokinase) rapidly phosphorylates using ATP to fructose-1-phosphate (F-1-P), bypassing PFK-1 regulation. In hereditary fructose intolerance (autosomal recessive Aldolase B deficiency), F-1-P cannot be cleaved to DHAP + glyceraldehyde; massive F-1-P accumulation sequesters intracellular inorganic phosphate (Pi) and depletes ATP—inhibiting glycogen phosphorylase and gluconeogenesis (severe hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia, hepatorenal injury). Elimination of fructose/sucrose/sorbitol restores health. Conversely, fructokinase deficiency prevents F-1-P formation altogether, so free fructose simply exits in urine (benign essential fructosuria).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03004","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Fat-Soluble Vitamins (A, D, E, K)","q":"A malnourished 4-year-old child in rural India presents with difficulty seeing at dusk (nyctalopia), dry conjunctiva with foamy triangular grayish-white temporal plaques (Bitot spots), and follicular hyperkeratosis. Which active vitamin A vitamer drives rhodopsin visual phototransduction versus epithelial glycoprotein differentiation, and what neurological disorder develops in severe vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol) deficiency (such as abetalipoproteinemia)?","o":["Retinoic acid forms rhodopsin; 11-cis-retinal regulates nuclear transcription; vitamin E deficiency causes night blindness","Retinol causes Bitot spots directly in excess; vitamin E deficiency causes pellagra","11-cis-retinal binds opsin in rod photoreceptors for night vision (isomerized by light to all-trans-retinal), whereas all-trans retinoic acid binds nuclear RAR/RXR transcription factors for epithelial differentiation; vitamin E deficiency causes spinocerebellar degeneration (ataxia, areflexia, loss of proprioception) and hemolytic anemia with acanthocytes","Beta-carotene directly activates phototransduction; vitamin E deficiency causes osteomalacia and rickets"],"a":2,"e":"Vitamin A comprises three active vitamers: (1) 11-cis-retinal combines with opsin to form rhodopsin in retinal rods; photon absorption isomerizes it to all-trans-retinal, activating transducin -\u003e PDE -\u003e closing cGMP-gated Na+/Ca2+ channels (hyperpolarization); (2) Retinol (transport form bound to RBP); and (3) Retinoic acid, which enters the nucleus and binds RAR/RXR to direct epithelial mucus-secreting differentiation (deficiency causes squamous metaplasia, xerophthalmia, Bitot spots, keratomalacia, and measles vulnerability; excess causes pseudotumor cerebri, hepatotoxicity, and isotretinoin teratogenicity). Vitamin E protects membrane PUFAs from peroxidation; deficiency (e.g., abetalipoproteinemia/MTP defect) mimics Friedreich ataxia (posterior column + spinocerebellar degeneration) with RBC membrane fragility/acanthocytosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03005","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Cofactor Roles of Vitamins B6, Biotin, and B12","q":"A bodybuilder consuming 12 raw egg whites daily develops alopecia, erythematous periorificial dermatitis, lactic acidosis, and organic aciduria due to egg-white avidin binding a water-soluble vitamin; meanwhile, a patient with pernicious anemia develops subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord with elevated serum methylmalonic acid. Which two cofactors and enzymatic pathways explain these findings?","o":["Niacin (B3) is bound by avidin; pyridoxine deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration with high methylmalonate","Thiamine (B1) is required by propionyl-CoA carboxylase; biotin deficiency elevates methylmalonic acid exclusively without dermatitis","Vitamin B6 (PLP) is bound by avidin; folate deficiency elevates serum methylmalonic acid","Biotin (B7, irreversibly bound by raw avidin) is required by all carboxylase enzymes (pyruvate carboxylase, acetyl-CoA carboxylase, propionyl-CoA carboxylase); Vitamin B12 (5\u0027-deoxyadenosylcobalamin) is required by mitochondrial methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (odd-chain fatty acid/branched-chain amino acid propionyl-CoA -\u003e succinyl-CoA), so B12 deficiency accumulates methylmalonyl-CoA impairing myelin synthesis"],"a":3,"e":"Raw egg white contains the glycoprotein avidin, which binds biotin (vitamin B7) with extraordinarily high affinity (Kd ~10^-15 M), blocking intestinal absorption (cooking denatures avidin). Biotin is the prosthetic CO2 carrier for ABC carboxylases (ATP + Biotin + CO2): pyruvate carboxylase, acetyl-CoA carboxylase, propionyl-CoA carboxylase, and beta-methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase. Downstream of propionyl-CoA carboxylase (catabolizing odd-chain fatty acids, Val, Ile, Met, Thr), methylmalonyl-CoA mutase requires adenosylcobalamin (B12) to form succinyl-CoA; thus B12 (unlike pure folate) deficiency elevates BOTH homocysteine and methylmalonic acid (MMA), incorporating abnormal branched/odd-chain fatty acids into CNS myelin (subacute combined degeneration of dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03006","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Zinc Metabolism and Deficiency Disorders","q":"A 7-month-old infant recently weaned from breast milk to cow\u0027s milk develops symmetric erythematous crusted plaques around the mouth, eyes, and perineum (acral/periorificial dermatitis), chronic diarrhea, alopecia, poor wound healing, and low serum alkaline phosphatase. Mutation in the intestinal apical zinc transporter SLC39A4 (ZIP4) causes this condition (acrodermatitis enteropathica). Which biochemical roles explain these manifestations?","o":["Zinc is an essential catalytic/structural cofactor for \u003e300 metalloenzymes—including alkaline phosphatase, carbonic anhydrase, alcohol dehydrogenase, matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs), DNA/RNA polymerases, Cu/Zn-SOD1, and zinc-finger transcription factors—required for skin barrier, immunity, gustation, and collagen remodeling","Zinc functions only as a redox-active cofactor in Fenton hydroxyl radical generation","Zinc deficiency impairs iron incorporation into protoporphyrin IX via ferrochelatase alone","Zinc is exclusively stored in thyroid colloid to catalyze iodide organification"],"a":0,"e":"Acrodermatitis enteropathica is an autosomal recessive defect in the enterocyte apical zinc importer ZIP4 (SLC39A4), manifesting classically when infants transition from high-bioavailability breast milk to cow\u0027s milk/cereal phytates. Zinc is a redox-inert Lewis-acid cofactor and structural stabilizer of zinc-finger DNA-binding transcription factors, DNA/RNA polymerases, alkaline phosphatase (so serum ALP drops markedly in severe zinc deficiency), carbonic anhydrase, MMPs (wound remodeling), Cu/Zn-SOD, and gustin (causing hypogeusia/anosmia, hypogonadism, and T-cell dysfunction). Oral elemental zinc supplementation rapidly reverses all lesions.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03007","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Serum Protein Electrophoresis and Acute Phase Reactants","q":"On routine serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP, pH 8.6), a patient with chronic pulmonary tuberculosis/inflammation shows a flattened albumin peak, markedly elevated alpha-1, alpha-2, and broad polyclonal gamma bands, and reduced serum transferrin and prealbumin (transthyretin). Which plasma protein is the primary driver of the alpha-1 band (whose deficiency causes panacinar emphysema), and why do albumin and transferrin fall during acute inflammation?","o":["IgG forms the beta-gamma bridge in acute inflammation; C-reactive protein (CRP) is a negative acute-phase reactant","Alpha-1 antitrypsin (A1AT, a positive acute-phase protease inhibitor) forms \u003e90% of the alpha-1 band; albumin, prealbumin (transthyretin), and transferrin are NEGATIVE acute-phase reactants whose hepatic synthesis is downregulated by IL-6/TNF-alpha so amino acids are redirected to CRP, fibrinogen, haptoglobin, ceruloplasmin, and SAA","Haptoglobin forms the albumin band; alpha-1 antitrypsin falls sharply during acute bacterial sepsis","Alpha-2 macroglobulin forms the alpha-1 band; albumin and transferrin are positive acute-phase reactants"],"a":1,"e":"At alkaline pH 8.6, serum proteins separate from anode (+) to cathode (-) into five classic fractions: Albumin (tallest sharp peak), Alpha-1 (chiefly alpha-1 antitrypsin—absence produces a flat alpha-1 band and unopposed neutrophil elastase panacinar emphysema/cirrhosis), Alpha-2 (haptoglobin, ceruloplasmin, alpha-2 macroglobulin—markedly elevated in nephrotic syndrome because large alpha-2 macroglobulin is retained), Beta (transferrin, C3, beta-lipoproteins; beta-gamma bridging in cirrhosis due to polyclonal IgA), and Gamma (immunoglobulins: narrow M-spike in myeloma vs broad hump in chronic infection/autoimmunity). During inflammation, IL-6/IL-1/TNF-alpha upregulate POSITIVE acute-phase reactants (CRP, ferritin, fibrinogen, hepcidin, A1AT, haptoglobin, ceruloplasmin, SAA) and downregulate NEGATIVE acute-phase reactants (albumin, prealbumin/transthyretin, transferrin, retinol-binding protein).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03008","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Acid-Base Titration and Isoelectric Point (pI)","q":"Aspartic acid has three ionizable groups with pKa1 (alpha-COOH) = 2.1, pKaR (beta-side-chain COOH) = 3.9, and pKa2 (alpha-NH3+) = 9.8. What is the isoelectric point (pI) of aspartic acid, and toward which electrode will it migrate during paper electrophoresis at pH 7.4?","o":["pI = 3.0; at pH 7.4 (above pI), it carries a net negative charge (-1) and migrates toward the ANODE (+)","pI = 5.95; at pH 7.4, it carries a net positive charge (+1) and migrates toward the CATHODE (-)","pI = 6.85; at pH 7.4, it remains completely stationary at the origin","pI = 3.0; at pH 7.4, it carries a net positive charge (+1) and migrates toward the CATHODE (-)"],"a":0,"e":"The isoelectric point (pI) is the pH at which a molecule bears zero net electrical charge (zwitterion state), calculated as the average of the two pKa values immediately flanking the neutral zwitterion species. For acidic aspartic acid: below pH 2.1 charge is +1; between 2.1 and 3.9 it is the neutral zwitterion (0); between 3.9 and 9.8 it is -1; above 9.8 it is -2. Thus pI = (pKa1 + pKaR) / 2 = (2.1 + 3.9) / 2 = 3.0. Whenever environmental pH \u003e pI (7.4 \u003e 3.0), amino acids/proteins are deprotonated to a net negative charge and migrate toward the positive anode (+).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03009","course":"MBBS","subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Fed-Fasting-Starvation Cycle","q":"Assertion (A): After 3-5 days of complete starvation, skeletal muscle protein breakdown (and urinary urea nitrogen excretion) markedly slows down compared with the first 24-48 hours of fasting, prolonging survival for weeks. Reason (R): Rising hepatic ketogenesis supplies acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate to the brain, reducing brain glucose demand from ~120 g/day to ~30-40 g/day and thereby reducing the need to catabolize muscle alanine/glutamine for hepatic and renal gluconeogenesis.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"During the first 24 hours of fasting, hepatic glycogenolysis maintains blood glucose; once liver glycogen is exhausted (~18-24 hours), gluconeogenesis from muscle-derived alanine/glutamine, adipose glycerol, and Cori lactate sustains the brain and RBCs—causing rapid initial muscle proteolysis and urinary nitrogen loss. By Days 3-7 of prolonged starvation, low insulin/high glucagon maximizes adipose lipolysis and hepatic ketone body output; the brain upregulates ketolytic enzymes (thiophorase) to derive ~60-70% of its ATP from ketone bodies (mainly beta-hydroxybutyrate), sparing skeletal muscle protein (while RBCs remain obligately glucose-dependent because they lack mitochondria).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03010","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Types of Necrosis","q":"At histopathology practical stations, Slide 1 (ischemic cerebral infarct) shows complete dissolution of tissue into a fluid-filled cystic space with lipid-laden microglia (gitter cells); Slide 2 (acute pancreatitis) shows chalky white peripancreatic deposits with shadowy adipocyte outlines surrounded by basophilic calcium precipitates; and Slide 3 (malignant hypertension renal arteriole) shows bright pink, smudgy eosinophilic proteinaceous rings inside vessel walls. Identify the three types of necrosis.","o":["Slide 1: Caseous necrosis; Slide 2: Gummatous necrosis; Slide 3: Liquefactive necrosis","Slide 1: Fibrinoid necrosis; Slide 2: Coagulative necrosis; Slide 3: Fat necrosis","Slide 1: Liquefactive necrosis; Slide 2: Enzymatic fat necrosis (saponification); Slide 3: Fibrinoid necrosis","Slide 1: Coagulative necrosis; Slide 2: Caseous necrosis; Slide 3: Gangrenous necrosis"],"a":2,"e":"Each type of necrosis reflects a distinct biochemical mechanism: (1) Hypoxic brain ischemia (and bacterial abscesses) undergoes LIQUEFACTIVE necrosis because abundant microglial/neutrophil hydrolytic enzymes completely digest dead tissue into viscous liquid/cysts (unlike heart, kidney, and spleen ischemia, which undergo coagulative necrosis with preserved ghost cell outlines); (2) Acute pancreatitis releases activated pancreatic lipases that split peripancreatic triglycerides into free fatty acids, which complex with Ca2+ (saponification) forming chalky white-blue deposits (FAT necrosis); (3) Immune-complex vasculitis, polyarteritis nodosa, and malignant hypertension damage vessel walls, allowing plasma proteins and fibrin to leak into smooth muscle media forming bright pink smudges (FIBRINOID necrosis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03011","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Wound Healing and Scar Pathology","q":"Eight months after ear piercing, a 22-year-old woman develops a firm, raised, smooth, hyperpigmented fibrous mass extending far beyond the original puncture borders onto the helix, which recurs aggressively after surgical excision; biopsy shows thick, glassy, eosinophilic (\u0027hyalinized\u0027) disorganized collagen bundles. How does a keloid differ from a hypertrophic scar, and what collagen shift occurs during normal scar maturation?","o":["Hypertrophic scars occur only on earlobes/presternum with thick glassy hyalinized collagen; keloids never recur","A keloid consists exclusively of elastic fibers; normal scar tensile strength reaches 100% of unwounded skin at Day 7","A keloid stays strictly within wound margins and regresses spontaneously within 6 months; normal healing shifts from Type I to Type III collagen","A keloid extends BEYOND the original wound margins, contains thick hyalinized/glassy type I/III collagen bundles, rarely regresses, and commonly recurs after excision; a hypertrophic scar stays CONFINED within original wound boundaries, shows parallel wavy collagen, and often flattens/regresses over time; normal wound healing shifts from initial granulation-tissue Type III collagen to mature tensile Type I collagen via zinc-dependent MMPs"],"a":3,"e":"During proliferative healing (Days 3-14), TGF-beta stimulates fibroblasts to deposit loose Type III collagen alongside angiogenesis (granulation tissue) and myofibroblast wound contraction. Over weeks to months, zinc-dependent matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) degrade Type III collagen and fibroblasts replace it with cross-linked Type I collagen (achieving ~70-80% of original skin tensile strength by ~3 months). Excessive collagen deposition yields either a hypertrophic scar (parallel collagen, confined to the original incision borders, tends to regress) or a keloid (common in darker skin, presternal/deltoid/earlobe sites, thick glassy acellular eosinophilic collagen bundles extending BEYOND wound margins like claw-like projections, high recurrence after simple excision).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03012","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Infarction and Effusions","q":"Which organ group characteristically develops RED (hemorrhagic) infarcts rather than WHITE (anemic) wedge-shaped infarcts following vascular occlusion, and what pleural fluid findings define an EXUDATE by Light\u0027s criteria?","o":["Red infarcts occur in loose spongy organs, organs with dual blood supply, or venous occlusion/reperfusion (lung, small intestine, testis/ovary), whereas white infarcts occur in solid organs with end-arterial circulation (heart, kidney, spleen); exudates show pleural/serum protein \u003e0.5, pleural/serum LDH \u003e0.6, or pleural LDH \u003e2/3 upper normal serum limit","White infarcts occur in small intestine and lung; transudates show high specific gravity (\u003e1.020) and abundant neutrophils","Red infarcts occur only in solid end-arterial myocardium without reperfusion; Light\u0027s criteria diagnose transudate when pleural LDH \u003e1000 U/L","Red infarcts occur exclusively in renal cortex and spleen; exudates show pleural/serum protein \u003c0.3 and low LDH"],"a":0,"e":"White (anemic) infarcts occur with arterial occlusions in dense solid organs lacking dual collateral flow (heart, kidney, spleen—forming pale wedge-shaped coagulative necrosis pointing toward the occluded vessel). Red (hemorrhagic) infarcts occur when blood seeps into necrotic tissue via: (1) venous torsion/occlusion (testis, ovary), (2) loose spongy parenchyma (lung), (3) dual/anastomosing blood supply (lung: pulmonary + bronchial arteries; small bowel: mesenteric arcades; liver), or (4) arterial reperfusion after thrombolysis. By Light\u0027s criteria, a pleural effusion is an inflammatory/malignant exudate (specific gravity \u003e1.020) if ANY ONE holds: pleural/serum protein \u003e0.5, pleural/serum LDH \u003e0.6, or pleural LDH \u003e2/3 upper limit of normal serum LDH.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03013","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)","q":"A 39-year-old man with severe acute pancreatitis develops acute refractory hypoxemia (PaO2/FiO2 \u003c150 mmHg) and bilateral symmetric non-cardiogenic pulmonary infiltrates (\u0027white-out lung\u0027) with normal PCWP (\u003c18 mmHg). Lung biopsy from a fatal case reveals congested alveolar capillaries, necrotic Type I pneumocytes, interstitial edema, and thick eosinophilic waxy bands lining alveolar ducts and alveoli (\u0027hyaline membranes\u0027). What is the histologic lesion and composition of these hyaline membranes?","o":["Bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia; hyaline membranes consist purely of aspirated lipid droplets","Diffuse alveolar damage (DAD, pathology of ARDS); hyaline membranes consist of fibrin-rich proteinaceous edema fluid mixed with necrotic alveolar epithelial cell debris","Emphysema; hyaline membranes consist of mucus plugs and eosinophil Charcot-Leyden crystals","Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis; hyaline membranes consist solely of PAS-positive surfactant without capillary injury"],"a":1,"e":"ARDS is triggered by indirect systemic inflammation (sepsis, acute pancreatitis, trauma, massive transfusion) or direct lung injury (aspiration, viral pneumonia, smoke), activating neutrophils/macrophages (TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-8) to injure alveolar capillary endothelium and Type I/II pneumocytes. Increased permeability floods alveoli with proteinaceous fluid and inactivates surfactant, leaving eosinophilic waxy \u0027hyaline membranes\u0027 (exuded fibrin + plasma proteins + necrotic epithelial debris) in the acute exudative phase, followed by Type II pneumocyte hyperplasia in the proliferative phase and interstitial fibrosis in the fibrotic phase.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03014","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Obstructive Airway Pathology","q":"Sputum cytology from a young patient during an acute status asthmaticus attack shows coiled basophilic mucinous casts of small bronchioles (Curschmann spirals), desquamated ciliated epithelial clusters (Creola bodies), and needle-shaped bipyramidal eosinophilic crystals (Charcot-Leyden crystals). Which protein forms Charcot-Leyden crystals, and what structural ciliary defect causes Kartagener syndrome bronchiectasis?","o":["Surfactant apoprotein A forms Charcot-Leyden crystals; alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency causes situs inversus","Immunoglobulin lambda light chains form Charcot-Leyden crystals; CFTR Cl- channel defect causes Kartagener syndrome","Galectin-10 (eosinophil lysophospholipase-binding protein) forms Charcot-Leyden crystals; autosomal recessive dynein arm defect causes immotile cilia in Kartagener syndrome (bronchiectasis, chronic sinusitis, situs inversus)","Major basic protein of neutrophils forms Charcot-Leyden crystals; collagen IV mutation causes Kartagener syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Allergic (atopic) bronchial asthma is driven by Th2 (IL-4 IgE class switch, IL-5 eosinophil recruitment, IL-13 mucus hypersecretion) airway inflammation with basement membrane thickening, goblet cell metaplasia, and bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy. Eosinophil degranulation releases galectin-10, which crystallizes into hexagonal bipyramidal Charcot-Leyden crystals alongside whorled Curschmann mucus spirals and Creola epithelial clumps. Separately, Kartagener syndrome (primary ciliary dyskinesia) results from mutations (e.g., DNAI1/DNAH5) disrupting ciliary dynein arms—impairing mucociliary clearance (bronchiectasis, sinusitis), embryonic nodal cilia rotation (situs inversus), and sperm flagella motility (male infertility).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03015","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Renal Cystic Diseases and Renal Cell Carcinoma","q":"A 58-year-old male smoker presents with painless gross hematuria, flank mass, left-sided varicocele, and unexplained polycythemia (Hb 19.2 g/dL). Radical nephrectomy reveals a golden-yellow upper-pole mass invading the left renal vein, composed of polygonal cells with abundant clear lipid- and glycogen-rich cytoplasm and delicate capillary networks. Which tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 3p is inactivated in \u003e80% of sporadic clear cell renal cell carcinomas, and why does polycythemia occur?","o":["WT1 gene on 11p13; excessive renin secretion directly stimulates bone marrow erythropoiesis","PKD1 gene on 16p13; autosomal dominant polycystin-1 mutation causes clear cell carcinoma exclusively","RB1 gene on 13q14; tumor directly produces PTHrP to stimulate RBC production","VHL (von Hippel-Lindau) tumor suppressor gene on 3p25; loss of pVHL prevents degradation of HIF-1alpha, driving constitutive transcription of VEGF (rich vascularity) and erythropoietin (paraneoplastic polycythemia)"],"a":3,"e":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma (RCC, ~70-80% of adult renal cancers; classically associated with smoking, obesity, and VHL syndrome) arises from proximal tubular epithelium and displays golden-yellow lipid/glycogen-rich clear cells that frequently invade the renal vein (occluding the left gonadal vein to cause a non-reducible left varicocele) and IVC. Loss/silencing of VHL on chromosome 3p prevents ubiquitin-proteasomal degradation of hypoxia-inducible factor-1alpha (HIF-1alpha), activating VEGF (angiogenesis), PDGF, and erythropoietin (EPO -\u003e paraneoplastic polycythemia). By contrast, autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) stems from PKD1 (16p, 85%) or PKD2 (4q) mutations causing bilateral cortical/medullary cysts, hepatic cysts, and Circle of Willis berry aneurysms.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03016","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Nephrolithiasis and Urothelial Carcinoma","q":"A patient with chronic Proteus mirabilis urinary tract infections develops a large branching radiopaque staghorn calculus filling the renal pelvis, while a second patient with hyperuricemia/tumor lysis develops a radiolucent stone that forms rhomboid/rosette crystals in acidic urine. Which chemical composition and microscopic crystal shape identify the infection-related staghorn stone?","o":["Struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate, MgNH4PO4·6H2O) forming rectangular \u0027coffin-lid\u0027 prism crystals in alkaline urine (urease-splitting bacteria hydrolyze urea to NH3 + OH-)","Calcium oxalate dihydrate forming hexagonal flat plates exclusively in alkaline urine","Uric acid forming coffin-lid prisms in strongly alkaline urine","Cystine forming envelope-shaped octahedral crystals in alkaline urine"],"a":0,"e":"Urinary calculi have diagnostic shapes, pH preferences, and X-ray visibility: (1) Calcium oxalate (~75-80%, radiopaque, envelope-shaped dihydrate / dumbbell monohydrate); (2) Struvite / Triple phosphate (magnesium ammonium phosphate, ~10-15%, radiopaque, classic large branching staghorn calculi, \u0027coffin-lid\u0027 prisms)—caused by urease-positive organisms (Proteus, Klebsiella, Staphylococcus saprophyticus, Ureaplasma) splitting urea into ammonia and alkalinizing urine (pH \u003e7.2); (3) Uric acid (~5-10%, RADIOLUCENT on plain X-ray, rhomboid/rosette crystals in ACIDIC urine, treated with hydration + potassium citrate alkalinization + allopurinol); and (4) Cystine (faintly radiopaque hexagonal plates in acidic urine, cystinuria).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03017","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Breast Carcinoma Molecular Subtypes and Paget Disease","q":"A 54-year-old woman presents with an eczematous, crusted, pruritic unilateral nipple ulceration unresponsive to topical steroids; nipple punch biopsy reveals large pale vacuolated intraepidermal cells (mucin-positive, HER2-positive) infiltrating the epidermis without dermal invasion at the nipple surface. What is this lesion (Paget disease of the breast), and which molecular breast cancer subtype carries the best prognosis versus strong association with BRCA1 mutations?","o":["Benign allergic eczema unrelated to underlying breast malignancy; Triple-negative has the best prognosis","Intraepidermal spread of underlying ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) or invasive breast carcinoma along lactiferous ducts to the nipple skin; Luminal A (ER+/PR+, HER2-, low Ki-67) has the best prognosis, whereas Triple-negative/basal-like (ER-/PR-/HER2-) strongly associates with BRCA1 mutations","Fibroadenoma stromal proliferation; Luminal A is exclusively BRCA1-mutated","Invasive lobular carcinoma with E-cadherin preservation; HER2+ subtype has the best prognosis without therapy"],"a":1,"e":"Paget disease of the nipple presents as unilateral erythematous scaling/crusting mimicking eczema, caused in \u003e95% of cases by malignant Paget cells (large clear-halo intraepidermal cells staining for mucin/PAS, CAM5.2, and usually HER2) migrating upward from underlying high-grade DCIS or invasive ductal carcinoma through lactiferous sinuses. Breast carcinoma molecular classification guides prognosis and therapy: Luminal A (ER+/PR+, HER2-, low proliferation) is the commonest (~50%) and has the best prognosis (tamoxifen/aromatase inhibitor responsive); HER2-enriched (ERBB2 amplified) is aggressive but targetable with trastuzumab; Basal-like/triple-negative (ER-/PR-/HER2-) occurs in younger women and BRCA1 mutation carriers (whereas invasive lobular carcinoma loses E-cadherin, growing in \u0027single-file\u0027 Indian-file cords).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03018","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Autoimmune Blistering Dermatoses","q":"Assertion (A): Pemphigus vulgaris presents with flaccid, easily ruptured blisters (positive Nikolsky sign) and frequent painful oral mucosal erosions, whereas bullous pemphigoid presents with tense, intact bullae (negative Nikolsky sign) and usually spares the oral mucosa. Reason (R): Pemphigus vulgaris involves IgG autoantibodies against epidermal desmosomal cadherins (desmoglein-3 and desmoglein-1) causing intraepidermal suprabasal acantholysis (\u0027row of tombstones\u0027 basal cells and fish-net intercellular IgG), whereas bullous pemphigoid targets hemidesmosomal BP180/BP230 proteins at the dermo-epidermal junction causing clean subepidermal separation (linear BMZ IgG/C3).","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"In pemphigus vulgaris (Type II hypersensitivity), IgG autoantibodies against desmoglein-3 (mucosa/skin) and desmoglein-1 break intercellular keratinocyte desmosomes, producing suprabasal intraepidermal acantholysis—leaving a single layer of basal cells attached to basement membrane (\u0027row of tombstones\u0027) and a reticular \u0027fish-net\u0027 IgG pattern on direct immunofluorescence; thin roofed intraepidermal blisters rupture readily (Nikolsky positive) and involve oral mucosa early. In bullous pemphigoid (elderly), IgG binds hemidesmosomal BPAG1 (BP230) and BPAG2 (BP180/type XVII collagen) along the basement membrane zone (linear IgG + C3), splitting the entire epidermis cleanly from the dermis into thick-roofed tense bullae (Nikolsky negative, eosinophil-rich).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03019","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Cutaneous Neoplasms","q":"A fair-skinned outdoor farmer presents with a pearly, rolled-border papule with surface telangiectasias and central ulceration (\u0027rodent ulcer\u0027) on his upper cheek. Excisional biopsy shows lobules of basaloid cells with prominent peripheral palisading of nuclei and stromal mucinous retraction artifacts. What is the diagnosis, and which single histologic feature is the strongest prognostic predictor in cutaneous malignant melanoma?","o":["Squamous cell carcinoma (keratin pearls); Clark anatomic level is stronger than Breslow depth","Seborrheic keratosis (horn pseudocysts); mitotic index alone","Basal cell carcinoma (nodular BCC, rarely metastasizes, driven by PTCH1 Hedgehog pathway mutations); Breslow thickness (vertical depth of invasion in millimeters from granular layer) is the strongest prognostic factor in melanoma","Actinic keratosis; diameter in centimeters alone"],"a":2,"e":"Basal cell carcinoma (BCC)—the most common human malignancy—arises on sun-exposed skin above the line from ear to mouth corner (e.g., upper lip/cheek/nose) from Hedgehog signaling activation (loss of PTCH1 tumor suppressor or activating SMO mutations; inherited in Gorlin nevoid BCC syndrome). Nodular BCC presents as a pearly telangiectatic nodule/rodent ulcer showing nests of basaloid cells with peripheral nuclear palisading and peritumoral clefting/retraction artifact, and virtually never metastasizes. Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma presents as scaly crusted plaques/ulcers (often lower lip/hands/Marjolin scar) with eosinophilic keratin pearls. In invasive melanoma (often BRAF V600E, S100/SOX10/HMB-45+), vertical Breslow thickness (mm) is the most powerful prognostic determinant.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03020","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pathology","topic":"Salivary Gland Neoplasms","q":"Which pairing accurately matches the two most common benign parotid tumors and the most common malignant salivary gland tumor with their defining histopathology?","o":["Warthin tumor (commonest malignant salivary tumor, linked to radiation); Pleomorphic adenoma (pure lymphoid stroma without cartilage)","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma (benign tumor of sublingual gland); Adenoid cystic carcinoma (contains oncocytic lymphoid germinal centers only)","Oncocytoma (commonest parotid tumor overall); Pleomorphic adenoma (never recurs even after enucleation)","Pleomorphic adenoma (commonest parotid tumor overall; biphasic epithelial ducts/myoepithelial cords embedded in chondromyxoid/cartilaginous stroma, PLAG1 rearrangement); Warthin tumor (papillary cystadenoma lymphomatosum; double-layered eosinophilic oncocytic epithelium over dense lymphoid follicles with germinal centers, strongly linked to smoking and often bilateral); Mucoepidermoid carcinoma (commonest malignant salivary tumor; mucous + epidermoid/squamous + intermediate cells, MAML2 fusion)"],"a":3,"e":"Most salivary gland tumors arise in the parotid (~70-80%, mostly benign, whereas minor/sublingual tumors are more often malignant). Pleomorphic adenoma (\u0027benign mixed tumor\u0027, ~60% of parotid neoplasms) exhibits epithelial/myoepithelial elements mixed with chondroid/myxoid stroma (PLAG1/HMGA2 rearrangements) and projects finger-like pseudopods through an incomplete capsule (requiring superficial parotidectomy rather than simple enucleation to prevent recurrence, with ~5% carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma risk). Warthin tumor occurs almost exclusively in the parotid of older male smokers (~10% bilateral) with bilayered eosinophilic oncocytic papillary epithelium over lymphoid germinal centers. Mucoepidermoid carcinoma (CRTC1-MAML2 fusion) is the commonest malignant salivary tumor, whereas adenoid cystic carcinoma displays \u0027Swiss-cheese\u0027 cribriform cylinders and marked perineural invasion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03021","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Diuretics","q":"A patient with recurrent idiopathic calcium oxalate nephrolithiasis and mild hypertension is started on chlorthalidone (a thiazide-like diuretic), whereas a patient with severe hypercalcemia of malignancy receives intravenous saline plus furosemide. Why do thiazides REDUCE urinary calcium excretion whereas loop diuretics INCREASE urinary calcium excretion, and how do both affect serum uric acid and potassium?","o":["Thiazides inhibit DCT NCC (increasing basolateral Na+/Ca2+ exchange and proximal volume-contraction Ca2+ reabsorption -\u003e hypocalciuria); loop diuretics inhibit TAL NKCC2 (abolishing the lumen-positive transepithelial voltage that drives paracellular Ca2+/Mg2+ reabsorption -\u003e hypercalciuria); BOTH cause hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and hyperuricemia (competing with urate via OAT1/3)","Thiazides inhibit TAL NKCC2 causing hypercalciuria; loop diuretics inhibit DCT NCC causing hypocalciuria; both cause hyperkalemia","Thiazides inhibit proximal carbonic anhydrase causing metabolic acidosis; loop diuretics cause hypouricemia","Thiazides block collecting duct ENaC causing hyperkalemia; loop diuretics increase lumen-positive voltage"],"a":0,"e":"Calcium handling sharply separates loop and thiazide diuretics (\u0027Loops Lose calcium\u0027): (1) Loop diuretics (furosemide, torsemide, bumetanide) inhibit NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, eliminating K+ back-diffusion via ROMK and collapsing the +10 mV lumen-positive electrical potential that normally pushes Ca2+ and Mg2+ paracellularly into blood—producing hypercalciuria (useful acutely with saline in hypercalcemia); (2) Thiazides (hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone, indapamide) inhibit Na+/Cl- cotransporter (NCC) in the early DCT, lowering intracellular Na+ to accelerate basolateral 3Na+/1Ca2+ exchange (boosting Ca2+ reabsorption) alongside proximal volume-contraction Ca2+ uptake—reducing calciuria and preventing calcium stones. Both deliver more Na+ and aldosterone-driven K+/H+ secretion distally (hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis) and compete with urate secretion (hyperuricemia/gout).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03022","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Peptic Ulcer and Acid-Suppressive Drugs","q":"Assertion (A): Oral proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole, pantoprazole) should be administered 30-60 minutes BEFORE breakfast rather than at bedtime on an empty stomach for optimal acid suppression, and they should NEVER be co-administered simultaneously with an H2-blocker before the meal. Reason (R): PPIs are acid-labile enteric-coated prodrugs absorbed in the small intestine that require acidic parietal cell secretory canaliculi (stimulated by a meal, not blocked by an H2-antagonist) to undergo protonation into active sulfenamide, which forms a covalent disulfide bond with active H+/K+-ATPase pumps.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"PPIs (omeprazole, esomeprazole, pantoprazole, rabeprazole) are weak-base prodrugs protected from premature gastric acid destruction by enteric coating. After duodenal absorption, they diffuse into parietal cell secretory canaliculi (pH \u003c2), where acid traps and rearranges them into reactive thiophilic sulfenamides that covalently bind Cys813 on actively inserted H+/K+-ATPase proton pumps—irreversibly suppressing acid secretion for ~24-48 hours until new pumps are synthesized. Taking the PPI 30-60 minutes before breakfast ensures peak plasma drug levels coincide with meal-stimulated maximal proton pump insertion into acidic canaliculi (whereas co-administering an H2 blocker reduces pump activation and impairs PPI conversion).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03023","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Hepatic Encephalopathy and Diarrheal/IBD Drugs","q":"A 56-year-old man with decompensated alcoholic cirrhosis develops asterixis (flapping tremor), reversal of sleep-wake cycle, and confusion (hepatic encephalopathy). He is treated with oral lactulose (titrated to 2-3 soft stools/day) plus rifaximin. How does lactulose lower blood ammonia levels?","o":["Colonic bacteria ferment non-absorbable lactulose into lactic and acetic acids, acidifying the colonic lumen to convert diffusible NH3 into poorly absorbed ammonium ions (NH4+, \u0027ion trapping\u0027) and accelerating fecal nitrogen excretion via osmotic catharsis","Lactulose absorbs directly into portal blood and chelates ammonia covalently","Lactulose directly activates hepatic urea cycle CPS-I inside hepatocyte mitochondria","Lactulose inhibits renal glutaminase to block urinary NH4+ excretion"],"a":0,"e":"Lactulose is a synthetic non-absorbable disaccharide (galactose + fructose) that passes intact to the colon, where resident bacteria ferment it to short-chain organic acids (lactic, acetic, formic acids). Lowering colonic luminal pH protonates lipophilic, freely absorbable ammonia (NH3) into charged, membrane-impermeant ammonium (NH4+), trapping nitrogen in feces while acidifying stool inhibits ammoniagenic coliforms and osmotic fluid retention speeds transit. Rifaximin (a minimally absorbed oral rifamycin) complements lactulose by suppressing gut ammonia-producing bacteria.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03024","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Anesthetics","q":"Which pharmacokinetic property determines the speed of induction and emergence of an inhaled volatile anesthetic, how does Minimum Alveolar Concentration (MAC) relate to potency, and how do propofol and ketamine differ as intravenous induction agents?","o":["Higher blood:gas partition coefficient produces faster induction; higher MAC indicates higher potency; Propofol blocks NMDA receptors","Lower blood:gas partition coefficient (e.g., desflurane, sevoflurane, nitrous oxide) produces FASTER induction and recovery; MAC is INVERSELY proportional to potency (1/MAC, determined by oil:gas lipid solubility); Propofol potentiates GABA-A (causing hypotension/apnea without analgesia) whereas Ketamine blocks NMDA glutamate receptors (dissociative anesthesia maintaining BP/airway reflexes)","Blood:gas coefficient determines potency only; Propofol causes emergence hallucinations exclusively","Lower oil:gas solubility produces higher potency; Ketamine causes profound myocardial depression and zero analgesia"],"a":1,"e":"For inhaled anesthetics, blood solubility (blood:gas partition coefficient) governs induction/emergence rate: agents with LOW blood:gas solubility (N2O 0.47, desflurane 0.42, sevoflurane 0.65) saturate blood rapidly and equilibrate alveolar-to-brain partial pressure quickly, giving rapid induction/recovery (whereas high-solubility halothane 2.3 dissolves extensively in blood first, inducing slowly). Potency is defined by MAC (alveolar concentration preventing movement to surgical incision in 50% of patients)—lower MAC means higher potency (governed by Meyer-Overton oil:gas lipid solubility). Among IV agents, propofol activates GABA-A (rapid smooth induction, antiemetic, marked vasodilation/hypotension, zero analgesia), whereas ketamine antagonizes NMDA receptors (dissociative analgesia, sympathetic stimulation preserving BP/bronchodilation, emergence nightmares).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03025","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Local Anesthetics","q":"A dentist injects 2% lidocaine (weak base, pKa ~7.9) directly into an acutely inflamed, purulent periapical abscess (tissue pH ~6.0), and the patient experiences almost no local analgesia. Why do local anesthetics fail in acidic infected tissue, which form of the drug actually blocks the voltage-gated Na+ channel pore, and what is the specific rescue infusion for bupivacaine-induced cardiac arrest (LAST)?","o":["Lidocaine blocks K+ channels preferentially in resting nerves; treat bupivacaine toxicity with naloxone","Acidic tissue accelerates unionized base formation; unionized base blocks Na+ channels from outside the axon; treat bupivacaine cardiotoxicity with IV amiodarone alone","Acidic tissue pH shifts equilibrium toward the charged ionized cation (BH+), preventing lipid-soluble unionized free base (B) from crossing the axonal membrane; inside the axoplasm, the ionized cation (BH+) binds the intracellular pore of inactivated/open voltage-gated Na+ channels; treat bupivacaine cardiotoxicity with 20% intravenous lipid emulsion (Intralipid)","Acidic pus directly hydrolyzes amide bonds instantly; treat bupivacaine cardiotoxicity with IV calcium channel blockers"],"a":2,"e":"Local anesthetics (esters like procaine/tetracaine metabolized by plasma pseudocholinesterase; amides like lidocaine/bupivacaine/ropivacaine metabolized by hepatic CYP450) are weak bases (pKa 7.6-8.9). Only the neutral unionized base (B) can diffuse across the lipophilic axonal membrane; once inside the axoplasm (pH ~7.2), it re-protonates into the active ionized cation (BH+), which enters the intracellular inner pore of open/inactivated voltage-gated Na+ channels (frequency/use-dependent block, hitting small/myelinated pain C and A-delta fibers earlier than thick A-alpha motor fibers). In acidic infected pus (pH \u003c6.5), virtually all drug stays trapped extracellularly as BH+ and cannot penetrate nerves. Lipophilic bupivacaine local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST: seizures -\u003e refractory arrhythmias) is treated with 20% IV lipid emulsion (\u0027lipid sink\u0027).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03026","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Systemic Antifungal Drugs","q":"Which pairing accurately matches four systemic antifungal classes to their fungal molecular targets and hallmark toxicities?","o":["Amphotericin B: inhibits beta-1,3-glucan synthase; Caspofungin: binds membrane ergosterol directly","Voriconazole: inhibits fungal DNA gyrase; Griseofulvin: binds ergosterol pores","Fluconazole: inhibits squalene epoxidase; Terbinafine: inhibits thymidylate synthase (like flucytosine)","Amphotericin B: binds ergosterol forming transmembrane aqueous pores (causing nephrotoxicity, distal RTA-1, hypokalemia/hypomagnesemia, infusion rigors); Voriconazole/Fluconazole: inhibit lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51, inhibiting CYP450; voriconazole causes visual blurring/photopsia); Caspofungin: inhibits 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase (cell wall); Terbinafine: inhibits squalene epoxidase"],"a":3,"e":"Antifungals exploit ergosterol, cell wall glucan, or nucleic acid differences: (1) Polyenes (Amphotericin B, nystatin) bind ergosterol to punch lethal ion pores (liposomal AmB reduces dose-limiting afferent vasoconstriction, tubular K+/Mg2+ wasting, and Type 1 RTA); (2) Azoles (fluconazole, itraconazole, voriconazole, posaconazole) inhibit fungal CYP450 14-alpha-demethylase blocking ergosterol synthesis (voriconazole is first-line for invasive aspergillosis, causing transient visual flashes/photopsia and hepatotoxicity); (3) Echinocandins (caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin) block cell-wall 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthesis; (4) Terbinafine blocks squalene epoxidase (accumulating toxic squalene in dermatophytes); (5) Flucytosine (5-FC) is deaminated to 5-FU inside fungi to inhibit thymidylate synthase; and (6) Griseofulvin disrupts mitotic spindles.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03027","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antiviral Drugs (Non-HIV)","q":"A bone-marrow transplant recipient develops acyclovir-resistant mucocutaneous HSV-1 lesions due to viral thymidine kinase (UL23) deficiency, while another transplant patient develops CMV retinitis treated with ganciclovir or foscarnet. Why does foscarnet remain effective against thymidine kinase-deficient HSV and UL97-mutant CMV, and how does oseltamivir act against influenza?","o":["Foscarnet is a pyrophosphate analogue that DIRECTLY inhibits viral DNA polymerase (requiring NO viral thymidine kinase or UL97 phosphorylation, unlike acyclovir/ganciclovir); oseltamivir inhibits influenza neuraminidase to prevent cleavage of sialic acid and block progeny virion release","Foscarnet requires UL97 kinase activation inside host neutrophils; oseltamivir blocks influenza M2 proton channel uncoating","Acyclovir directly inhibits host RNA polymerase II; oseltamivir inhibits influenza RNA-dependent RNA polymerase directly","Ganciclovir inhibits viral protease; foscarnet inhibits viral integrase"],"a":0,"e":"Acyclovir (HSV/VZV) must first be monophosphorylated selectively by viral thymidine kinase (TK) before host kinases convert it to acyclovir-triphosphate (viral DNA polymerase inhibitor \u0026 chain terminator); similarly, ganciclovir requires CMV UL97 protein kinase for initial phosphorylation (carrying dose-limiting myelosuppression). Consequently, TK-negative HSV and UL97-mutant CMV resist acyclovir/ganciclovir, yet remain susceptible to foscarnet (pyrophosphate mimic directly blocking viral DNA/RNA polymerase pyrophosphate binding site, causing nephrotoxicity and hypocalcemia/hypomagnesemia) and cidofovir. For Influenza A and B, oseltamivir/zanamivir block neuraminidase, trapping budding virions clumped to host sialic acid receptors.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03028","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antiretroviral Therapy (NACO TLD Regimen)","q":"A newly diagnosed 32-year-old adult with HIV-1 (HBV co-infected) is initiated under India\u0027s NACO national guidelines on the preferred first-line single-tablet TLD regimen: Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) + Lamivudine (3TC) + Dolutegravir (DTG). Which viral enzymes do these three drugs inhibit, what dual benefit do TDF + 3TC provide in HBV co-infection, and what signature toxicities require monitoring with TDF?","o":["TDF and 3TC are NRTIs inhibiting HIV reverse transcriptase (and HBV DNA polymerase, suppressing both HIV and HBV); Dolutegravir is an integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) blocking viral DNA integration into host chromosomes; monitor renal proximal tubulopathy (Fanconi syndrome) and bone mineral density loss with TDF","TDF inhibits HIV protease; Dolutegravir inhibits CCR5 entry; monitor severe pancreatitis with 3TC","3TC is a non-nucleoside RT inhibitor; Dolutegravir inhibits gp41 fusion; TDF causes severe hepatotoxicity via CYP3A4 boosting","TDF inhibits HIV integrase; Dolutegravir inhibits reverse transcriptase without divalent cation chelation"],"a":0,"e":"Under WHO and India\u0027s NACO guidelines, the preferred first-line adult ART regimen is Tenofovir (TDF, nucleotide RTI) + Lamivudine (3TC, nucleoside RTI) + Dolutegravir (DTG, second-generation INSTI with high genetic barrier to resistance). TDF and 3TC require intracellular phosphorylation to triphosphates/diphosphates to terminate HIV reverse transcriptase DNA synthesis AND suppress Hepatitis B viral polymerase (preventing hepatic flare if continued). Dolutegravir chelates Mg2+ at the active site of HIV integrase (prevented from chelating oral Ca2+/Fe2+/Mg2+ antacids by spacing doses). TDF requires baseline/periodic serum creatinine monitoring for proximal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi syndrome) and osteomalacia/osteopenia (avoided by tenofovir alafenamide, TAF).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03029","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Osteoporosis Pharmacotherapy","q":"A 67-year-old postmenopausal woman with osteoporosis (T-score -3.1) is prescribed weekly oral alendronate with strict instructions to take it on an empty stomach with a full glass of plain water and remain upright for at least 30 minutes. What cellular enzyme do nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates inhibit to trigger osteoclast apoptosis, and how does teriparatide\u0027s dosing paradox stimulate NEW bone formation instead of bone resorption?","o":["Inhibit vitamin D 1-alpha-hydroxylase; teriparatide inhibits osteoblast Wnt/beta-catenin signaling","Inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway (blocking prenylation of small GTPases and inducing osteoclast apoptosis; upright posture prevents pill-induced corrosive esophagitis); pulsatile once-daily low-dose subcutaneous teriparatide (recombinant PTH 1-34) preferentially activates osteoblasts over osteoclasts (whereas continuous high PTH causes bone resorption)","Bind calcium hydroxyapatite to stimulate osteoclastic acid secretion; alendronate must be taken with milk and calcium tablets immediately","Activate RANKL on osteoblasts to trigger osteoblast apoptosis; continuous infusion of teriparatide builds trabecular bone"],"a":1,"e":"Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate, zoledronate) bind avidly to bone hydroxyapatite, are endocytosed by resorbing osteoclasts, and inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase (blocking Ras/Rho/Rac prenylation, disrupting ruffled border formation, and inducing osteoclast apoptosis). Oral bisphosphonates have \u003c1% bioavailability (chelated by milk/food/calcium) and cause severe erosive esophagitis if lodged in the esophagus—mandating plain water and \u003e=30 min upright fasting. Rare chronic risks include osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical subtrochanteric femur fractures. Conversely, intermittent daily subcutaneous teriparatide (PTH 1-34) stimulates osteoblastic survival and Wnt/IGF-1 bone formation, whereas continuous PTH elevation (hyperparathyroidism) drives net osteoclastic resorption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03030","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Uterotonics and Tocolytics","q":"A 30-year-old woman with severe preeclampsia (BP 168/108 mmHg) and moderate persistent bronchial asthma develops uterine atony and postpartum hemorrhage immediately after delivery despite initial IV oxytocin infusion. Which uterotonic agent is CONTRAINDICATED because of her severe hypertension, and which prostaglandin uterotonic is CONTRAINDICATED because of her bronchial asthma?","o":["Ritodrine is first-line uterotonic; magnesium sulfate causes uterine tetany","Carboprost is contraindicated in hypertension only; ergometrine causes fatal bronchodilation","Methylergometrine/ergometrine (causes severe alpha-adrenergic/serotonergic systemic vasoconstriction and hypertensive crisis) is contraindicated in preeclampsia/hypertension; Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2alpha, causes potent bronchospasm) is contraindicated in bronchial asthma (use misoprostol/PGE1 or tranexamic acid + uterine massage instead)","Oxytocin is contraindicated in hypertension; misoprostol is strictly contraindicated in asthma"],"a":2,"e":"Active PPH management uses uterotonics tailored to comorbidities: (1) Oxytocin (first-line, contracts upper uterine segment rhythmically; excessive rapid IV bolus can cause hypotension/water intoxication via V2 cross-reactivity); (2) Methylergometrine (ergot alkaloid causing sustained tonic uterine contraction via 5-HT2/alpha-1 receptors)—contraindicated in hypertension, preeclampsia, and coronary disease due to severe vasospasm; (3) Carboprost (IM 15-methyl PGF2alpha)—potent FP-receptor bronchoconstrictor strictly contraindicated in active bronchial asthma; and (4) Misoprostol (PGE1 analogue—safe in both hypertension and asthma, causing transient shivering/pyrexia) alongside early IV tranexamic acid.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03031","course":"MBBS","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Dosing Regimens and Bioavailability","q":"A cardiovascular drug has a systemic clearance (CL) of 4.0 L/hour, an oral bioavailability (F) of 0.80 (80%), and a target average steady-state plasma concentration (Css,avg) of 10 mg/L. What oral maintenance dose must be administered every 6 hours (tau = 6 hours) to maintain this target steady-state concentration?","o":["50 mg every 6 hours","192 mg every 6 hours","240 mg every 6 hours","300 mg every 6 hours"],"a":3,"e":"For intermittent oral dosing at interval tau, steady-state balance requires: (F x Maintenance Dose) / tau = CL x Css,avg. Rearranging gives Maintenance Dose = (CL x Css,avg x tau) / F. Substituting CL = 4.0 L/hour, Css,avg = 10 mg/L, tau = 6 hours, and F = 0.80 yields: MD = (4.0 x 10 x 6) / 0.80 = 240 / 0.80 = 300 mg every 6 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03032","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Staphylococcus aureus Virulence and Toxins","q":"A 21-year-old woman using a high-absorbency vaginal tampon during menstruation develops sudden high fever (39.8°C), hypotension (BP 76/48 mmHg), diffuse erythematous macular \u0027sunburn-like\u0027 rash involving palms and soles, conjunctival hyperemia, and acute multiorgan dysfunction (elevated creatinine, ALT, thrombocytopenia). Blood cultures remain sterile. How does the causative Staphylococcus aureus exotoxin (TSST-1) trigger this toxic shock syndrome, and what is the role of staphylococcal Protein A?","o":["TSST-1 acts as a superantigen that directly cross-links the outside of MHC Class II on antigen-presenting cells to the V-beta region of T-cell receptors (activating ~20% of T cells nonspecifically to unleash massive IL-1, IL-2, IFN-gamma, and TNF-alpha cytokine storm); Protein A binds the Fc region of human IgG to block opsonization and phagocytosis","TSST-1 binds GM1 ganglioside to raise cAMP; Protein A binds complement C5a to attract neutrophils","TSST-1 blocks 60S eukaryotic ribosomes; Protein A is an outer-membrane LPS endotoxin","TSST-1 cleaves desmoglein-1 in the stratum granulosum; Protein A cleaves mucosal IgA1 exclusively"],"a":0,"e":"Staphylococcus aureus (catalase-positive, coagulase-positive Gram-positive cocci in grape-like clusters) deploys specialized virulence factors: (1) Toxic shock syndrome toxin-1 (TSST-1, and enterotoxins B/C) is a superantigen that bypasses normal peptide-groove antigen processing by bridging MHC Class II beta-chain and TCR V-beta chain externally—activating up to 5-20% of all CD4+ T cells into a massive TNF-alpha/IL-1/IL-2/IFN-gamma shock cascade without bacteremia; (2) Cell-wall Protein A binds the Fc portion of IgG backward, preventing complement fixation and phagocyte Fc-receptor opsonization; (3) Exfoliative toxins A/B cleave desmoglein-1 causing Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome (Nikolsky+); and (4) Panton-Valentine leukocidin (PVL) causes severe necrotizing MRSA pneumonia/furunculosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03033","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Streptococcus Species Identification","q":"In a clinical bacteriology practical, Isolate 1 is a catalase-negative, alpha-hemolytic lancet-shaped diplococcus that is optochin-sensitive and bile-soluble (positive Quellung reaction), whereas Isolate 2 is a catalase-negative beta-hemolytic coccus in chains that is bacitracin-resistant, hippurate hydrolysis-positive, and produces an arrowhead zone of enhanced hemolysis perpendicular to Staphylococcus aureus on blood agar (positive CAMP test). Identify Isolates 1 and 2.","o":["Isolate 1: Viridans streptococci; Isolate 2: Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A)","Isolate 1: Streptococcus pneumoniae; Isolate 2: Streptococcus agalactiae (Group B Streptococcus)","Isolate 1: Enterococcus faecalis; Isolate 2: Streptococcus pneumoniae","Isolate 1: Streptococcus pneumoniae; Isolate 2: Enterococcus faecium"],"a":1,"e":"Catalase-negative Gram-positive cocci in pairs/chains are systematically separated by hemolysis and confirmatory tests: (1) Alpha-hemolytic lancet-shaped diplococci sensitive to optochin (\u003c14 mm zone) and lysed by sodium deoxycholate (bile-soluble, with capsular swelling in Quellung test) = Streptococcus pneumoniae (separating it from optochin-resistant, bile-insoluble viridans streptococci); (2) Beta-hemolytic Group A S. pyogenes is bacitracin-sensitive and PYR-positive; (3) Beta-hemolytic Group B S. agalactiae (neonatal sepsis/meningitis) is bacitracin-resistant, hippurate-positive, and CAMP-test positive (CAMP factor synergizes with S. aureus beta-hemolysin to form a distinct arrowhead zone).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03034","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae and Listeria monocytogenes","q":"A throat swab from an unvaccinated child with a tough bleeding grayish-white pharyngeal pseudomembrane grows black colonies on potassium tellurite agar, and Albert staining shows green pleomorphic \u0027Chinese-letter\u0027 bacilli with bluish-black polar metachromatic (Babes-Ernst) granules. Which laboratory test proves exotoxin production in this isolate, and which foodborne Gram-positive bacillus uniquely exhibits \u0027tumbling\u0027 motility at 25°C and actin-rocket intercellular spread?","o":["Schick skin test on the agar plate; Clostridium difficile","CAMP arrowhead test; Bacillus cereus","Elek gel immunodiffusion precipitation test (encoded by beta-corynebacteriophage tox gene); Listeria monocytogenes (cold enrichment at 4°C, ActA actin polymerization)","Quellung capsular test; Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae"],"a":2,"e":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae displays club-shaped/cuneiform \u0027Chinese-letter\u0027 arrangements with polyphosphate volutin metachromatic (Babes-Ernst) granules on Albert/Neisser stain and reduces tellurite to gray-black colonies on potassium tellurite blood agar (Loeffler serum slope enhances granule visualization). Because non-toxigenic strains exist, toxigenicity (mediated by lysogenic beta-corynebacteriophage carrying the tox gene) is confirmed in vitro by the Elek gel immunoprecipitation test (antitoxin-soaked strip forming precipitin lines at 45 degrees) or PCR. Separately, Listeria monocytogenes (neonatal/pregnancy meningitis from unpasteurized dairy/deli meats) is a facultative intracellular Gram-positive rod showing umbrella/tumbling motility at 20-25°C (non-motile at 37°C), narrow beta-hemolysis, cold growth (4°C), and ActA-driven actin comet tails.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03035","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Mycobacterium leprae","q":"Under India\u0027s National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP), a sputum sample undergoes Cartridge-Based Nucleic Acid Amplification Testing (CBNAAT / GeneXpert MTB/RIF), simultaneously detecting M. tuberculosis DNA and rifampicin resistance within 2 hours. Mutation in which gene predicts rifampicin resistance, and how does lepromatous leprosy differ from tuberculoid leprosy on slit-skin smear and lepromin test?","o":["gyrA gene for rifampicin; tuberculoid leprosy shows Virchow globi and leonine facies","katG/inhA gene for rifampicin; lepromatous leprosy is strongly lepromin-positive with absent bacilli","pncA gene for rifampicin; M. leprae grows rapidly in 48 hours on Lowenstein-Jensen agar","rpoB gene (81-bp rifampicin resistance-determining region encoding RNA polymerase beta-subunit); lepromatous leprosy shows foamy Virchow macrophages packed with globi of acid-fast bacilli (high bacterial index, Th2 IL-4/IL-10 response) and NEGATIVE lepromin skin test, whereas tuberculoid leprosy shows well-formed granulomas, very few/absent AFB, and strongly POSITIVE lepromin test (Th1 response)"],"a":3,"e":"CBNAAT (GeneXpert MTB/RIF) amplifies an M. tuberculosis-specific sequence of the rpoB gene (which encodes the beta-subunit of bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase)—detecting TB and \u003e95% of rifampicin resistance mutations (a surrogate marker for MDR-TB) directly from sputum in ~2 hours (whereas solid Lowenstein-Jensen culture takes 4-8 weeks and MGIT liquid culture 1-2 weeks). In leprosy (Hansen disease, M. leprae—uncultivable on artificial media, grown in nine-banded armadillo/mouse footpad, 5% H2SO4 modified ZN), polar tuberculoid (TT) mounts strong CD4+ Th1 cell-mediated immunity (positive lepromin test, few hypopigmented anesthetic patches, paucibacillary), whereas polar lepromatous (LL) mounts weak CMI / Th2 humoral immunity (negative lepromin test, Virchow foamy histiocytes stuffed with cigar-bundle globi, leonine facies, multibacillary).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03036","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Nontuberculous Mycobacteria (NTM)","q":"A tropical aquarium keeper develops nodular lymphangitic skin lesions on his hand after cleaning a fish tank, yielding acid-fast bacilli that grow at 30-32°C and produce yellow-orange pigment ONLY after exposure to light. Into which Runyon group and species does this organism fall, and which NTM produces a painless necrotic cutaneous \u0027Buruli ulcer\u0027 via mycolactone exotoxin?","o":["Runyon Group I photochromogen: Mycobacterium marinum (fish-tank granuloma; M. kansasii is also a Group I photochromogen); Mycobacterium ulcerans causes Buruli ulcer","Runyon Group IV rapid grower: Mycobacterium abscessus; Mycobacterium fortuitum causes fish-tank granuloma","Runyon Group III non-photochromogen: Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare; Mycobacterium chelonae causes Buruli ulcer","Runyon Group II scotochromogen: Mycobacterium scrofulaceum; Mycobacterium avium causes Buruli ulcer"],"a":0,"e":"Runyon classified slow-growing (\u003e7 days) vs rapid-growing (\u003c7 days) nontuberculous mycobacteria by pigmentation: Group I Photochromogens (yellow/orange pigment ONLY when exposed to light: M. kansasii [pulmonary TB-like] and M. marinum [aquarium/swimming-pool granuloma growing best at 30-32°C]); Group II Scotochromogens (pigmented even in the dark: M. scrofulaceum, M. gordonae); Group III Non-photochromogens (unpigmented: Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare complex [MAC] causing disseminated infection in AIDS CD4 \u003c50); and Group IV Rapid growers (\u003c7 days: M. fortuitum, M. chelonae, M. abscessus). Mycobacterium ulcerans produces the plasmid-encoded polyketide toxin mycolactone, causing painless undermined Buruli ulcers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03037","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa","q":"A neutropenic leukemia patient develops rapidly progressive necrotic black cutaneous ulcers with surrounding erythema (\u0027ecthyma gangrenosum\u0027) and septic shock. Blood and lesion cultures yield oxidase-positive, non-lactose-fermenting, obligate aerobic Gram-negative rods producing a sweet grape-like odor and blue-green diffusible pigment on nutrient agar. Which two diffusible pigments are characteristic, and how does Pseudomonas exotoxin A kill host cells?","o":["Pyomelanin only; Exotoxin A stimulates adenylyl cyclase via Gs","Pyocyanin (blue-green) and pyoverdine/fluorescein (yellow-green fluorescent under UV); Exotoxin A catalyzes ADP-ribosylation of eukaryotic elongation factor-2 (eEF-2) to halt host protein synthesis (identical mechanism to diphtheria toxin)","Indole blue pigment only; Exotoxin A lyses RBCs via lecithinase","Violacein (purple) and prodigiosin (red); Exotoxin A cleaves SNARE proteins"],"a":1,"e":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a strictly aerobic, motile (single polar flagellum), oxidase-positive, non-fermenting Gram-negative bacillus thriving in moist hospital environments (ventilators, sinks, contact lens fluid). It produces sweet grape-like (aminoacetophenone) odor, grows at 42°C, and secretes blue-green pyocyanin (which generates ROS) and fluorescent yellow-green siderophore pyoverdine (plus rare pyorubin/pyomelanin). Virulence includes elastase/alkaline protease (causing perivascular bacterial invasion and hemorrhagic necrosis in ecthyma gangrenosum), mucoid alginate biofilms in cystic fibrosis, and Exotoxin A—which ADP-ribosylates diphthamide on eEF-2 identically to Corynebacterium diphtheriae toxin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03038","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Campylobacter jejuni and Helicobacter pylori","q":"A 24-year-old man develops ascending symmetric flaccid paralysis with areflexia and albuminocytologic dissociation in CSF (Guillain-Barré syndrome) 2 weeks after an acute episode of fever, abdominal cramps, and bloody diarrhea from eating undercooked poultry. Which curved microaerophilic Gram-negative rod triggered this autoimmune molecular mimicry, and how is it differentiated in the lab from Helicobacter pylori?","o":["Yersinia enterocolitica (grows at 4°C, triggers GBS exclusively); C. jejuni is catalase-negative","Helicobacter pylori (grows at 42°C, urease-negative); Campylobacter jejuni is strongly urease-positive","Campylobacter jejuni (gull-wing curved rods, microaerophilic, grows best at 42°C on Skirrow/Campy-BAP medium, oxidase-positive, hippurate-positive, UREASE-NEGATIVE); Helicobacter pylori is strongly UREASE-POSITIVE (converting urea to ammonia to neutralize gastric acid) and grows at 37°C","Vibrio parahaemolyticus (grows on 8% NaCl TCBS); H. pylori is oxidase-negative"],"a":2,"e":"Both Campylobacter jejuni and Helicobacter pylori are microaerophilic, oxidase- and catalase-positive, motile curved/spiral Gram-negative rods. However, C. jejuni (thermophilic, growing preferentially at 42°C on selective Skirrow/Butzler/Campy-BAP media, gull-wing pairs with darting motility, hippurate hydrolysis-positive) is UREASE-NEGATIVE, causes poultry-associated inflammatory bloody diarrhea, and shares lipooligosaccharide epitopes with peripheral nerve GM1/GD1a gangliosides—making it the commonest antecedent trigger of acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (Guillain-Barré syndrome) and reactive arthritis. Conversely, H. pylori grows at 37°C and produces abundant UREASE (basis of rapid urease CLO test and 13C/14C urea breath test) to buffer gastric acid.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03039","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Herpesviridae (HSV, VZV, EBV, CMV)","q":"Which pairing accurately matches human herpesviruses with their primary cellular site of lifelong latency and characteristic diagnostic laboratory finding?","o":["HSV-1: B lymphocytes; EBV: trigeminal ganglion; CMV: anterior horn spinal motor neurons","VZV: CD4+ T cells; EBV: monocytes; CMV: sensory dorsal root ganglia (Tzanck smear positive)","HHV-8: dorsal root ganglia; HSV-2: hepatocytes (Negri bodies)","HSV-1/HSV-2 \u0026 VZV: sensory dorsal root / trigeminal cranial nerve ganglia (multinucleated giant cells on Tzanck smear); EBV: memory B lymphocytes (via CD21/CR2 receptor; atypical Downey CD8+ T cells on smear \u0026 heterophile Paul-Bunnell/Monospot positivity); CMV: monocytes/macrophages/endothelial cells (large \u0027owl\u0027s eye\u0027 basophilic intranuclear inclusions)"],"a":3,"e":"All human herpesviruses are enveloped icosahedral dsDNA viruses establishing lifelong latency: alpha-herpesviruses (HSV-1 in trigeminal ganglion, HSV-2 in sacral ganglia, VZV in dorsal root/trigeminal ganglia) produce Cowdry type A intranuclear inclusions and multinucleated giant syncytia on Giemsa/Wright Tzanck smears of vesicular fluid. Beta-herpesvirus CMV stays latent in bone marrow myeloid progenitors/monocytes/endothelium and produces classic enlarged cytomegalic cells with prominent basophilic intranuclear (\u0027owl\u0027s eye\u0027) and cytoplasmic inclusions. Gamma-herpesvirus EBV infects oropharyngeal epithelium and B cells via CD21 (CR2), inducing polyclonal IgM heterophile antibodies (Paul-Bunnell/Monospot) and reactive cytotoxic CD8+ atypical lymphocytes (Downey cells).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03040","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Picornaviruses (Poliovirus, Coxsackievirus) and Paramyxoviruses (Measles)","q":"How does live-attenuated oral polio vaccine (Sabin OPV) interrupt wild poliovirus fecal-oral transmission more effectively than inactivated injectable polio vaccine (Salk IPV), and what fatal delayed CNS complication of measles virus occurs years after childhood infection due to persistent defective M-protein virus?","o":["Sabin OPV induces BOTH systemic IgG AND intestinal mucosal secretory IgA (blocking gut viral replication and community shedding, though carrying rare vaccine-associated paralytic polio/cVDPV reversion risk, whereas Salk IPV induces systemic IgG only); Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE)","Sabin OPV induces systemic IgG only; Guillain-Barré syndrome solely","Salk IPV induces superior secretory gut IgA and sheds protective virus to contacts; Reye syndrome","Both OPV and IPV induce identical intestinal secretory IgA; progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy"],"a":0,"e":"Poliovirus (non-enveloped positive-sense ssRNA Enterovirus) replicates in Peyer patches/gut mucosa before invading CNS anterior horn motor neurons. Injectable trivalent/fractional Salk IPV stimulates strong serum IgG (preventing viremia and paralytic poliomyelitis in the vaccinee without reversion risk) but elicits minimal intestinal IgA. Oral Sabin OPV replicates in enterocytes to induce BOTH serum IgG and gut secretory IgA—blocking subsequent wild-virus gut colonization and shedding—though rare neurovirulent reversion causes VAPP/VDPV (driving India\u0027s switch from tOPV to bOPV + fIPV). Years after wild measles (Warthin-Finkeldey multinucleated giant cells, Koplik spots), defective matrix (M) protein persistence causes fatal demyelinating SSPE.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03041","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Intestinal and Opportunistic Protozoa","q":"In a parasitology stool practical: Specimen 1 (dysentery with blood/mucus) shows actively motile trophozoites with finger-like pseudopodia containing ingested host erythrocytes and a central pinpoint karyosome; Specimen 2 (foul-smelling greasy steatorrhea) shows binucleated pear-shaped trophozoites with a sucking disc and \u0027falling-leaf\u0027 motility; and Specimen 3 (profuse watery diarrhea in an HIV patient with CD4 40/mm3) shows 4-6 micrometer spherical pink oocysts on modified Kinyoun acid-fast stain (1% H2SO4). Identify the three protozoa.","o":["Specimen 1: Entamoeba histolytica; Specimen 2: Giardia duodenalis (lamblia); Specimen 3: Cryptosporidium parvum (or hominis)","Specimen 1: Giardia lamblia; Specimen 2: Cryptosporidium; Specimen 3: Isospora (Cystoisospora) belli (30x12 micrometers oval)","Specimen 1: Entamoeba coli; Specimen 2: Trichomonas vaginalis; Specimen 3: Balantidium coli","Specimen 1: Balantidium coli; Specimen 2: Entamoeba histolytica; Specimen 3: Cyclospora cayetanensis (10 micrometers exclusively)"],"a":0,"e":"Each protozoan has definitive diagnostic microscopy: (1) Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites exhibit unidirectional pseudopodial motility, a single nucleus with central pinpoint karyosome, and pathognomonic erythrophagocytosis (ingested RBCs, absent in commensal Entamoeba coli, whose mature cyst has 8 nuclei vs 4 in E. histolytica) while causing flask-shaped colonic ulcers and \u0027anchovy-sauce\u0027 liver abscess; (2) Giardia duodenalis (lamblia) trophozoites are pear-shaped, bilaterally symmetric with two nuclei (\u0027owl-face\u0027), four pairs of flagella (\u0027falling-leaf\u0027 motility), and a ventral sucking disc covering duodenal microvilli; (3) Cryptosporidium (4-6 micrometer round oocysts—smaller than 8-10 micrometer Cyclospora and 25-30 micrometer oval Cystoisospora) stains bright red on modified ZN acid-fast stain.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03042","course":"MBBS","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Tissue and CNS Protozoa","q":"Case 1: An HIV-positive patient (CD4 55/mm3) develops focal seizures with multiple ring-enhancing basal ganglia lesions on brain MRI; biopsy shows crescentic tachyzoites and tissue cysts. Case 2: A healthy teenager who swam in a warm stagnant freshwater pond 4 days earlier develops fulminant fatal hemorrhagic meningoencephalitis with motile amoeboid trophozoites in purulent CSF (no bacteria on Gram stain). Identify both pathogens and their routes of infection.","o":["Case 1: Taenia solium; Case 2: Acanthamoeba castellanii (always chronic granulomatous amoebic encephalitis via contact lens only)","Case 1: Toxoplasma gondii (reactivation of latent bradyzoite tissue cysts; acquired via cat feces oocysts or undercooked cyst-containing meat; congenital triad = chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, diffuse intracranial calcifications); Case 2: Naegleria fowleri (primary amoebic meningoencephalitis via warm freshwater inhalation invading the olfactory neuroepithelium across the cribriform plate)","Case 1: Toxoplasma gondii (dogs are definitive host); Case 2: Balamuthia mandrillaris solely","Case 1: Trypanosoma brucei; Case 2: Entamoeba histolytica"],"a":1,"e":"Toxoplasma gondii uses felids (cats) as the definitive host shedding unsporulated oocysts in feces; humans acquire infection by ingesting sporulated oocysts, undercooked meat containing bradyzoite cysts, or transplacentally (congenital toxoplasmosis triad: chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, diffuse parenchymal intracranial calcifications—distinct from CMV periventricular calcifications). In AIDS (CD4 \u003c100), reactivation causes multiple ring-enhancing CNS lesions (treated with pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + folinic acid). Naegleria fowleri (\u0027brain-eating\u0027 free-living thermophilic flagellate-amoeba) enters the nose during warm freshwater swimming/nasal irrigation, crosses the cribriform plate along olfactory nerves, and causes rapidly fatal acute hemorrhagic primary amoebic meningoencephalitis (PAM).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03043","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Mechanical Injuries","q":"During medicolegal injury certification of an assault survivor, Wound A on the scalp shows irregular ragged margins, crushed hair bulbs, and intact nerves/blood vessels (\u0027tissue bridges\u0027) stretching across the wound depth; nearby, a forearm contusion appears greenish-yellow. What weapon mechanism caused Wound A, and what approximate age and hemoglobin pigment breakdown explain the greenish-yellow color of the bruise?","o":["Wound A is a grazing brush-burn abrasion; greenish-yellow color proves postmortem infliction","Wound A is a stab wound deeper than length; greenish-yellow bruise is \u003e3 weeks old (hemosiderin is bright green)","Wound A is a split/blunt LACERATION caused by blunt force crushing tissue against underlying skull bone (mimicking an incised wound superficially, distinguished by irregular margins, abraded edge, and intact tissue bridging); greenish-yellow bruise indicates roughly 4-7 days old (hemoglobin -\u003e biliverdin [green] -\u003e bilirubin [yellow] via macrophage heme oxygenase)","Wound A is a sharp-edged INCISED wound caused by a surgical scalpel; greenish-yellow bruise is \u003c6 hours old"],"a":2,"e":"Scalp, eyebrow, and shin lacerations over unyielding bone often split linearly and superficially resemble incised cuts (\u0027incised-looking lacerated wound\u0027). Careful inspection with a hand lens resolves them: an incised wound (sharp blade) cleanly severs hair shafts, blood vessels, and nerves evenly with sharp edges and zero bridging, whereas a blunt-force laceration crushes tissue, leaving uneven abraded/bruised margins, crushed hair bulbs, and intact elastic tissue/nerve/vessel bridges across the floor. In living bruises (except subconjunctival hemorrhage, which stays bright red because atmospheric oxygen diffuses through conjunctiva), RBC breakdown progresses: Red (fresh, 0-1 d) -\u003e Bluish-purple/black (1-3 d, deoxyHb) -\u003e Greenish (4-5 d, biliverdin) -\u003e Yellowish (6-10 d, bilirubin/hemosiderin) -\u003e normal (~2 weeks).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03044","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Sharp-Force Wounds (Homicidal vs Suicidal Patterns)","q":"At autopsy of a victim of sharp-force assault, the body exhibits a deep fatal incised wound across the left side of the neck that tapers at its right end (\u0027tailing\u0027) alongside multiple deep clean cuts across the palmar aspect of the fingers (grabbing the blade) and ulnar border of the left forearm. Contrast these with multiple superficial, parallel, closely grouped horizontal cuts on the volar left wrist sparing clothes. What do the forearm/finger cuts and wound tailing indicate?","o":["Suicidal self-infliction: palmar finger cuts are hesitation marks, and tailing marks the exact point where the blade first entered the skin","Accidental chop wounds from falling onto grass","Postmortem scavenging artifacts unrelated to manner of death","Homicidal assault: the palmar finger and ulnar forearm cuts are classic ACTIVE/PASSIVE DEFENSE wounds sustained while gripping the knife or shielding the face/neck, and the shallow \u0027tail\u0027 points toward the direction the blade exited (left-to-right stroke); superficial parallel volar wrist cuts are typical suicidal \u0027hesitation\u0027 (tentative) marks"],"a":3,"e":"Distinguishing homicidal from suicidal sharp-force deaths relies on wound distribution and morphology: (1) Defense wounds—pathognomonic of homicidal struggle—occur actively on the palms/flexor web spaces when grabbing a knife blade or passively along the extensor/ulnar borders of the forearms, backs of hands, and thighs when raised defensively (virtually absent in suicide); (2) Suicidal hesitation (tentative) cuts are multiple superficial, parallel, grouped incisions on accessible \u0027elective sites\u0027 (volar left wrist in right-handed persons, anterior neck above thyroid cartilage) with intact clothing pulled aside; and (3) Incised wounds are deeper/cleaner at the entry end and taper off into a shallow \u0027tail\u0027 where the blade lifts out, showing stroke direction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03045","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Head Trauma and Skull Fractures","q":"Assertion (A): When a moving head strikes a stationary flat floor (e.g., falling backward onto the occiput), cerebral contusions are typically minimal at the occipital impact site (coup) and far more severe in the frontal poles and inferior temporal lobes (contrecoup). Reason (R): Sudden deceleration of the skull against a flat surface creates transient negative pressure / cavitation and forces the brain forward so the frontal/temporal poles scrape forcefully against the irregular bony ridges of the anterior and middle cranial fossae (orbital plates and sphenoid wings), whereas a blow by a moving object to a stationary head produces predominantly coup injury.","o":["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false","Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true"],"a":0,"e":"Coup and contrecoup mechanics are fundamental in forensic neuropathology: (1) When a moving object (hammer, lathi) strikes a stationary head, maximal focal acceleration/inbending damages cortex directly beneath the impact (COUP injury); (2) When a moving head decelerates abruptly against a broad stationary surface (fall onto occiput), the smoother occipital concavity cushions the impact site while brain momentum/cavitation drives the frontal and temporal tips against the jagged orbital roofs, crista galli, and lesser wings of the sphenoid—producing severe contralateral CONTRECOUP contusions (notably, a fall onto the FRONTAL forehead rarely causes large occipital contrecoup injury because the occipital fossa is smooth).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03046","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Corrosive Poisons","q":"A young woman sustains facial and ocular burns from deliberate acid throwing (\u0027vitriolage\u0027, punished under BNS Section 124 / former IPC 326A/B), while a toddler accidentally swallows drain-cleaner caustic soda (NaOH). How do mineral acids and strong alkalis differ in tissue necrosis mechanism and depth of GI injury, and what urine change characterizes carbolic acid (phenol) poisoning?","o":["Strong mineral acids (H2SO4/HCl/HNO3) cause COAGULATIVE necrosis forming a dry firm eschar that somewhat limits deep penetration (brown-black charring with H2SO4, yellow xanthoproteic with HNO3); strong alkalis (NaOH/KOH) cause LIQUEFACTIVE (colliquative) necrosis with protein/lipid saponification causing deep transmural esophageal penetration and strictures; phenol (carbolic acid) produces \u0027carboluria\u0027 (urine turning dark olive-green/black on air exposure due to hydroquinone/pyrocatechol oxidation)","Acids cause liquefactive necrosis and deep esophageal perforation faster than alkalis; alkalis form a protective coagulative eschar","Nitric acid causes black charring and alkalis turn urine bright green","Both acids and alkalis should be neutralized immediately with oral vinegar or baking soda followed by gastric lavage"],"a":0,"e":"Corrosive injuries differ sharply by pH: Mineral acids precipitate proteins into a tough dry coagulative eschar (sulfuric acid dehydrates/chars tissues brownish-black; nitric acid gives yellow xanthoproteic staining; hydrochloric acid gives grayish-white mucosa) and tend to injure the stomach/pylorus heavily, whereas strong alkalis (sodium/potassium hydroxide) dissolve keratin, saponify fats, and cause deep liquefactive necrosis—penetrating deeply through the esophageal muscularis to cause mediastinitis and severe late esophageal strictures. Carbolic acid (phenol) causes painless white leathery mucosal burns, CNS/renal collapse, and dark smoky green-black \u0027carboluria\u0027. Gastric lavage, emesis, and acid-base neutralization (exothermic heat!) are CONTRAINDICATED in corrosive ingestion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03047","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Plant, Spinal, and Cardiac Poisons","q":"Which pairing accurately matches four classic Indian plant poisons with their active toxic mechanisms and diagnostic clinical features?","o":["Strychnos nux-vomica (strychnine): competitive antagonist at postsynaptic spinal glycine receptors causing painful opisthotonos/risus sardonicus convulsions WITH FULL CONSCIOUSNESS (mimicking tetanus, triggered by slightest touch/noise); Aconitum napellus (aconite): opens voltage-gated Na+ channels causing perioral tingling, alternating pupil dilation/constriction (\u0027hippus\u0027), and bidirectional/ventricular arrhythmias; Abrus precatorius (abrin): toxalbumin inhibiting 60S ribosomes (used historically in cattle \u0027sui\u0027 needles); Ricinus communis (ricin): toxalbumin depurinating 28S rRNA","Strychnine: blocks GABA-A in brain causing deep coma before seizures; Aconite: blocks Na+ channels causing bradycardia with fixed pinpoint pupils","Ricin: activates muscarinic receptors; Aconite: inhibits mitochondrial Complex IV","Abrus precatorius: acts as an organophosphate AChE inhibitor; Strychnine: causes painless descending flaccid paralysis"],"a":0,"e":"Indian forensic toxicology highlights four classic botanical toxins: (1) Strychnine (kuchila seeds) blocks inhibitory spinal Renshaw-cell glycine receptors—causing excruciating tonic-clonic spasms (opisthotonos, emprosthotonos, risus sardonicus) while the patient remains horrifyingly wide awake/conscious (differentiating from tetanus by rapid onset, absence of wound/trismus initially, and complete muscle relaxation between spasms; treated with IV diazepam/propofol in a dark quiet room); (2) Aconite (mitha zahar / monkshood) locks neuronal and cardiac Na+ channels open—causing numb tingling mouth/extremities, alternating miosis/mydriasis (hippus), and lethal ventricular arrhythmias; (3) Abrus precatorius (rati/gunja, scarlet-black seeds) abrin and (4) Ricinus communis (castor bean) ricin are RIP-II toxalbumins that depurinate 28S rRNA of 60S ribosomes to arrest protein synthesis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03048","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Agricultural Fumigants (Aluminum Phosphide)","q":"A 25-year-old farmer\u0027s son is brought 2 hours after ingesting two fresh 3-gram tablets of \u0027Celphos\u0027 (stored grain fumigant), complaining of epigastric burning, vomiting, and breathlessness. His breath and vomitus have a strong garlicky/decaying-fish odor; BP is 70/40 mmHg, ECG shows severe ST-T abnormalities and ventricular ectopy, and metabolic acidosis is profound. What gas is liberated on contact with gastric moisture, what is its cellular target, and what is true regarding a specific antidote?","o":["Carbon monoxide is liberated; treat with atropine and pralidoxime (2-PAM)","Phosphine (PH3) gas is liberated upon hydrolysis in gastric acid/moisture; it inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase (Complex IV) and triggers massive lipid peroxidation/myocardial toxicity causing refractory cardiogenic shock; there is NO specific antidote (avoid aqueous gastric lavage—if early, use coconut oil/liquid paraffin + aggressive vasopressors, IV MgSO4, and ECMO/supportive care)","Arsine gas is liberated; treat immediately with IV hydroxocobalamin as a 100% curative specific antidote","Sulfur dioxide gas is liberated; treat with immediate aggressive plain-water gastric lavage to maximize tablet dissolution"],"a":1,"e":"Aluminum phosphide (and zinc phosphide rodenticide) is one of the commonest fatal agricultural poisons in North/Central India. Exposure to gastric acid or water rapidly liberates toxic phosphine (PH3) gas (garlicky/rotten-fish odor), which inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase, collapses oxidative phosphorylation, and induces intense free-radical myocardial/vascular injury—causing refractory hypotension, toxic myocarditis, ARDS, hepatic/renal failure, and severe lactic acidosis. Crucially, plain water gastric lavage accelerates PH3 gas release (coconut oil/liquid paraffin/KMnO4 protocols have been used early to limit hydrolysis/absorption), and NO specific pharmacological antidote exists, making early ICU hemodynamic/MgSO4/ECMO support vital.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03049","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Professional Misconduct and Privileged Communication","q":"A bus driver undergoing medical evaluation is diagnosed with poorly controlled epilepsy and recurrent blackouts, yet refuses to inform his transport employer and insists the physician maintain absolute confidentiality. When is a doctor legally and ethically justified—or obligated—to disclose confidential patient information (\u0027privileged communication\u0027), and what constitutes \u0027infamous conduct\u0027 (professional misconduct)?","o":["Dichotomy (fee-splitting commission for referrals) is ethically encouraged under NMC regulations","A doctor may freely publish identifiable patient clinical photos on social media without consent if educational","Privileged communication permits/obligates disclosure in good faith to protect public safety/prevent imminent grave harm (such as notifying transport authorities after counseling an unfit epileptic commercial driver), statutory notifiable infectious diseases, suspected child abuse (POCSO Section 19), or under court order; infamous conduct includes the \u00276 As\u0027 (Adultery with patient, Abortion illegal, Alcohol/drug abuse on duty, Advertising improperly, Association with unqualified persons/dichotomy fee-splitting, Addiction/false certificates)","Confidentiality is absolute in every circumstance without exception, even when summoned by a Sessions Judge or for POCSO Section 19"],"a":2,"e":"Professional secrecy is a cornerstone of medical ethics, yet is NOT absolute in India: under the doctrine of privileged communication (qualified privilege, made bona fide only to the concerned authority with a legal/moral/social duty to receive it), a physician must breach confidentiality for: (1) court orders during testimony (BSA/evidence law grants NO absolute doctor-patient testimonial privilege in Indian courts), (2) notifiable infectious diseases (e.g., cholera, TB, diphtheria), (3) mandatory reporting under POCSO Section 19, and (4) grave public safety hazards (e.g., epileptic/color-blind pilot/bus driver refusing to stop driving). Serious professional misconduct (infamous conduct) adjudicated by State Medical Councils/NMC includes adultery/sexual misconduct with a patient, fee-splitting (dichotomy), issuing false medical certificates, improper self-advertisement, and covering unqualified practitioners.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03050","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Age Estimation by Teeth and Ossification","q":"During medicolegal age estimation of an adolescent/young adult in India, which combination of permanent dental eruption milestones and radiographic epiphyseal union timings is accurate?","o":["First permanent molar erupts at 12 years and third molar at 9 years; medial clavicle fuses earliest at 14 years","Mixed dentition never occurs simultaneously; all cranial sutures close completely by age 16","Temporary (milk) dentition totals 24 teeth erupting by 12 months; lower end of humerus fuses at 25 years","First permanent molar erupts at ~6 years, second permanent molar at ~12 years, and third molar (wisdom tooth) between 17-25 years; iliac crest apophysis fuses at ~19-20 years and medial end of clavicle fuses latest at ~21-22 years"],"a":3,"e":"Forensic age estimation integrates dental and skeletal maturity: (1) Temporary (deciduous) teeth total 20 (eruption begins ~6 months with lower central incisors and completes ~24-30 months with second deciduous molars); (2) Permanent teeth total 32 (1st molar \u00276-year molar\u0027 at ~6 yr, central/lateral incisors 7-8 yr, premolars/canines 9-12 yr, 2nd molar \u002712-year molar\u0027 at ~12-14 yr, 3rd molar variably at 17-25 yr; \u0027mixed dentition\u0027 spans ages 6-12 yr); (3) Radiographic epiphyseal fusion (Indian standards): elbow (lower humerus/head of radius) ~14-16 yr, wrist/distal radius-ulna ~18-19 yr, iliac crest ~19-20 yr, ischial tuberosity ~20-21 yr, and medial clavicular epiphysis ~21-22 yr (last long-bone epiphysis to fuse).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03051","course":"MBBS","subject":"Forensic Medicine \u0026 Toxicology","topic":"Salicylate (Aspirin) Poisoning and Ion Trapping","q":"A patient with acute aspirin (salicylic acid, weak acid pKa = 3.5) overdose is treated with intravenous sodium bicarbonate to alkalinize urine from a baseline urine pH of 5.5 to a target urine pH of 7.5. Using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation for a weak acid [log10([A-] / [HA]) = pH - pKa], what are the ionized-to-unionized ratios ([A-]/[HA]) at urine pH 5.5 versus pH 7.5, and by what factor does alkalinization increase ionized salicylate trapping?","o":["At pH 5.5: ratio = 100:1 (10^2); at pH 7.5: ratio = 10,000:1 (10^4); ionized/unionized ratio increases 100-fold, trapping charged salicylate in tubular urine and preventing passive back-diffusion","At pH 5.5: ratio = 1:100; at pH 7.5: ratio = 1:10,000; alkalinization promotes salicylate reabsorption into blood","At pH 5.5: ratio = 10:1; at pH 7.5: ratio = 100:1; ionized ratio increases 10-fold","At pH 5.5: ratio = 2:1; at pH 7.5: ratio = 4:1; ionized ratio increases 2-fold"],"a":0,"e":"Salicylic acid is a weak acid (pKa = 3.5). By Henderson-Hasselbalch, [A-]/[HA] = 10^(pH - pKa). At acidic urine pH 5.5: 10^(5.5 - 3.5) = 10^2 = 100:1. Raising urine pH by 2 full units to 7.5 via IV NaHCO3 yields: 10^(7.5 - 3.5) = 10^4 = 10,000:1—a 100-fold increase in the ionized-to-unionized ratio (reducing membrane-permeant unionized HA concentration 100-fold). Because charged A- cannot passively diffuse back across renal tubular lipid membranes, salicylate is \u0027ion-trapped\u0027 in urine and rapidly cleared (conversely, serum alkalinization keeps salicylate ionized in plasma, reducing CNS penetration; note: potassium must also be repleted so kidneys do not excrete H+ instead of HCO3-).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03052","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Megaloblastic Anaemia","q":"A 58-year-old strict vegan presents with glossitis, macrocytic anaemia (Hb 8.2 g/dL, MCV 116 fL), hypersegmented neutrophils, paraesthesias, loss of vibration and joint-position sense in both feet, and extensor plantar responses (subacute combined degeneration of spinal cord). Why is administering folic acid monotherapy without Vitamin B12 dangerous in this patient?","o":["Folic acid blocks absorption of dietary iron","Folic acid corrects the megaloblastic anaemia haematologically while masking and accelerating irreversible Vitamin B12-deficiency neurological degeneration","Vitamin B12 deficiency is always cured permanently by oral folic acid","Folic acid causes acute intravascular haemolysis in vegans"],"a":1,"e":"Vitamin B12 is required both for methionine synthase (folate trap) and for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (myelin synthesis). Giving high-dose folic acid alone bypasses the methylfolate trap to restore DNA synthesis and correct macrocytic anaemia, masking the diagnosis while myelin degeneration in the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts progresses irreversibly. Serum B12, methylmalonic acid (elevated specifically in B12 deficiency), and parenteral Vitamin B12 replacement are required.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03053","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Platelet Disorders","q":"A 30-year-old woman presents with petechiae, ecchymoses, and active menorrhagia/wet oral mucosal blood blisters. CBC shows Hb 10.4 g/dL, normal WBC count, and platelets 8,000/mm3; peripheral smear shows giant platelets without schistocytes or blasts, and PT/aPTT/fibrinogen are normal (HIV/HCV/H. pylori negative). What is the diagnosis and preferred initial treatment for active mucosal bleeding?","o":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation; IV heparin","Acute promyelocytic leukaemia; all-trans retinoic acid","Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura (ITP); systemic Corticosteroids (prednisolone/dexamethasone) PLUS IVIG (adding platelet transfusion only if life-threatening haemorrhage)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura; immediate plasma exchange"],"a":2,"e":"Isolated severe thrombocytopenia with normal RBC/WBC morphology, normal coagulation profile, and mucocutaneous bleeding (wet purpura = high risk of intracranial bleed) diagnoses primary ITP (autoantibodies against platelet GPIIb/IIIa). Wet mucosal bleeding with platelets \u003c10,000-20,000/mm3 warrants prompt systemic corticosteroids (e.g., high-dose dexamethasone 40 mg/day x 4 days) plus IVIG (1 g/kg) for rapid platelet rise (platelet transfusion is ineffective alone as antibodies destroy donor platelets, reserved for life-threatening bleeding).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03054","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Myeloproliferative Neoplasms","q":"A 42-year-old man presents with dragging left upper quadrant discomfort, massive splenomegaly extending 10 cm below the costal margin, WBC 185,000/mm3 showing full-spectrum myeloid maturation (\u0027myelocyte bulge\u0027) with basophilia and \u003c2% blasts, and low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) score. Cytogenetics/RT-PCR confirms t(9;22)(q34;q11) BCR-ABL1 fusion. What is the first-line targeted oral therapy?","o":["Urgent allogeneic bone marrow transplant before trying oral drugs","CHOP chemotherapy with rituximab","Watchful waiting without therapy until blast crisis","BCR-ABL1 Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor (Imatinib, Dasatinib, or Nilotinib)"],"a":3,"e":"Chronic-phase CML is driven by the constitutively active BCR-ABL1 tyrosine kinase from the Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22). Oral Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitors (Imatinib 400 mg/day or second-generation TKIs such as dasatinib/nilotinib) induce deep molecular remissions and near-normal life expectancy, making TKIs first-line therapy (while allogeneic stem-cell transplant is reserved for T315I-resistant/blast-phase disease failing TKIs).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03055","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Plasma Cell Dyscrasias","q":"A 66-year-old man presents with persistent thoracic back pain, anaemia (Hb 8.6 g/dL), serum calcium 12.1 mg/dL, serum creatinine 2.6 mg/dL, total serum protein 10.8 g/dL with albumin 2.8 g/dL (markedly elevated globulin gap), rouleaux formation on smear, and punched-out lytic lesions on skull X-ray. Serum protein electrophoresis shows a sharp monoclonal IgG spike and bone marrow shows 34% clonal plasma cells. Which drug class MUST be strictly avoided for his bone pain to prevent acute-on-chronic renal failure?","o":["NSAIDs (e.g., diclofenac, ibuprofen), plus avoiding dehydrated IV iodinated contrast","Bisphosphonates (adjusted for renal function) for bone disease","Oral paracetamol with adequate hydration","Bortezomib-based antimyeloma therapy"],"a":0,"e":"This patient fulfills CRAB myeloma-defining events (hyperCalcaemia, Renal insufficiency from light-chain cast nephropathy, Anaemia, lytic Bone lesions). Because glomerular perfusion is fragile and filtered free light chains precipitate with Tamm-Horsfall protein in tubules, NSAIDs (which constrict afferent arterioles via prostaglandin inhibition) and volume depletion/hypercalcaemia rapidly precipitate irreversible acute renal failure. Hydration, hypercalcaemia reversal, and bortezomib-based induction preserve renal function.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03056","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Adrenal Cortex Disorders","q":"A 34-year-old woman (not taking exogenous steroids) presents with central obesity, moon facies, wide violaceous abdominal striae (\u003e1 cm), proximal muscle weakness, easy bruising, and new-onset hypertension. What is the correct FIRST diagnostic step before ordering pituitary MRI or adrenal CT?","o":["Order an immediate contrast MRI brain before any biochemical testing","Confirm endogenous hypercortisolism biochemically using at least two first-line tests (1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test, late-night salivary cortisol, or 24-h urinary free cortisol), then check plasma ACTH","Check random 2 PM serum cortisol alone","Perform immediate bilateral adrenalectomy"],"a":1,"e":"After excluding exogenous glucocorticoid use, diagnosing Cushing\u0027s syndrome strictly follows a two-step approach: Step 1 confirms endogenous hypercortisolism using \u003e= 2 screening tests (overnight 1 mg dexamethasone suppression test, 24-hour urinary free cortisol, or late-night salivary cortisol). Only AFTER biochemical confirmation does Step 2 measure plasma ACTH to distinguish ACTH-independent adrenal causes (suppressed ACTH -\u003e adrenal CT) from ACTH-dependent causes (normal/high ACTH -\u003e pituitary MRI / high-dose DST / IPSS) to avoid chasing common incidentalomas.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03057","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone (SIADH)","q":"A 61-year-old male smoker with small-cell lung carcinoma is clinically euvolaemic (no oedema, normal JVP, moist mucosae, BP 122/78 mmHg) and alert, with serum Na+ 122 mEq/L, serum osmolality 254 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality 540 mOsm/kg, urine Na+ 68 mEq/L, low serum uric acid (2.1 mg/dL), and normal thyroid and adrenal function. What is the diagnosis and initial first-line treatment?","o":["Primary polydipsia (which has urine osmolality \u003c 100 mOsm/kg); 3% saline bolus immediately","Hypovolaemic hyponatraemia; rapid 3-litre 0.9% normal saline infusion (which risks worsening SIADH hyponatraemia)","Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone (SIADH); free-water fluid restriction (\u003c 800-1,000 mL/day) alongside treating the underlying lung tumour","Central diabetes insipidus; oral desmopressin"],"a":2,"e":"Hypotonic euvolaemic hyponatraemia with inappropriately concentrated urine (\u003e100 mOsm/kg), elevated urine sodium (\u003e30-40 mEq/L), hypouricaemia, and intact thyroid/adrenal/renal function confirms SIADH (commonly paraneoplastic from small-cell lung cancer). In mild/moderate asymptomatic or minimally symptomatic SIADH, oral fluid restriction (800-1,000 mL/day) is first-line (adding oral salt tablets/loop diuretic or tolvaptan/urea if refractory). Notably, isotonic 0.9% saline (308 mOsm/kg) can paradoxically WORSEN hyponatraemia in SIADH when urine osmolality (540 mOsm/kg) exceeds saline (\u0027desalination phenomenon\u0027).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03058","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Inherited Cystic Kidney Diseases","q":"A 38-year-old man whose father died of a sudden subarachnoid haemorrhage at age 45 presents with hypertension, flank fullness, episodic gross haematuria, bilaterally palpable lobulated kidneys, and multiple cortical cysts in both kidneys and the liver on ultrasound (ADPKD, PKD1 mutation). Which high-risk vascular extrarenal association warrants targeted MRA screening when a family history of aneurysm or warning headache is present, and which class slows cyst growth in rapid progressors?","o":["Thoracic aortic coarctation; chronic high-dose NSAIDs","Pulmonary arteriovenous fistula; oral corticosteroids","Renal artery fibromuscular dysplasia only; lifelong hydrochlorothiazide monotherapy","Intracranial saccular (berry) aneurysms of the Circle of Willis; ACEi/ARB for BP control plus Tolvaptan (V2 receptor antagonist) in rapid progressors"],"a":3,"e":"ADPKD (PKD1/PKD2 mutations) causes progressive bilateral renal cysts, hepatic cysts, mitral valve prolapse, colonic diverticula, and intracranial Circle of Willis saccular aneurysms (~8-10% prevalence, higher with family history of SAH/aneurysm—requiring MRA screening). Strict BP control with RAAS blockade (ACEi/ARB) and Tolvaptan (vasopressin V2 receptor antagonist inhibiting cAMP-driven cyst proliferation) slow eGFR decline in rapidly progressing ADPKD.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03059","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Decompensated Cirrhosis","q":"A 56-year-old man with Child-Pugh C cirrhosis is brought with day-night sleep reversal, slurred speech, asterixis (flapping tremor), and lethargy (West Haven Grade II-III hepatic encephalopathy) following 4 days of constipation and excessive furosemide intake (serum K+ 2.8 mEq/L, metabolic alkalosis). Alongside correcting hypokalaemia and identifying/treating any occult GI bleed or infection, what is the primary ammonia-lowering pharmacotherapy?","o":["Oral/NG Lactulose (titrated to achieve 2-3 soft bowel movements per day) PLUS Rifaximin for recurrent/overt encephalopathy (avoiding severe protein restriction)","IV Lorazepam 4 mg for sleep reversal","IV Flumazenil plus high-dose oral neomycin indefinitely","Strict zero-protein starvation diet for 14 days"],"a":0,"e":"Hepatic encephalopathy is triggered by precipitants (GI bleeding, infection/SBP, constipation, hypokalaemia/alkalosis increasing renal NH3 generation and NH3 crossing the blood-brain barrier, sedatives). Treatment addresses precipitants + Lactulose (non-absorbable disaccharide fermented by colonic bacteria into lactic/acetic acid, trapping NH3 as non-absorbable NH4+ and speeding transit to 2-3 soft stools/day) + Rifaximin (minimally absorbed antibiotic cutting urease-producing gut flora). Severe dietary protein restriction is AVOIDED as it worsens sarcopenia and muscle ammonia clearance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03060","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Malabsorption Syndromes","q":"A 22-year-old woman from Punjab presents with chronic bloating, foul-smelling greasy stools, refractory iron-deficiency anaemia, and intensely pruritic papulovesicular lesions on her elbows and knees (dermatitis herpetiformis). Which first-line serological test pair should be ordered BEFORE she eliminates gluten from her diet, and what must be checked alongside it to prevent a false-negative result?","o":["Serum IgE anti-wheat antibody alone","IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase (IgA anti-tTG) antibody PLUS total serum IgA level (to rule out selective IgA deficiency), followed by duodenal biopsy while on a gluten-containing diet","Stool ova and cyst examination alone","Start a strict gluten-free diet for 6 months FIRST, then test IgA anti-tTG"],"a":1,"e":"Coeliac disease (gluten-sensitive enteropathy, HLA-DQ2/DQ8) has \u003e95% sensitivity/specificity with IgA anti-tTG testing—provided the patient is consuming gluten AND does not have selective IgA deficiency (~2-3% of coeliac patients, requiring IgG-based deamidated gliadin peptide or IgG anti-tTG instead). Endoscopic distal duodenal biopsy (crypt hyperplasia, intraepithelial lymphocytosis, villous atrophy) confirms diagnosis prior to initiating a lifelong gluten-free diet.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03061","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Movement Disorders","q":"A 68-year-old man presents with insidious-onset asymmetric 4-6 Hz resting \u0027pill-rolling\u0027 tremor of the right hand (diminishing on purposeful reaching), cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia, micrographia, and shuffling festinating gait with reduced arm swing. Why is Levodopa routinely co-formulated with Carbidopa (a peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor)?","o":["Carbidopa inhibits MAO-B inside the substantia nigra","Carbidopa crosses the blood-brain barrier to directly stimulate D2 receptors","Carbidopa blocks peripheral conversion of levodopa to dopamine without crossing the blood-brain barrier, maximising CNS levodopa delivery and reducing peripheral nausea/hypotension","Carbidopa blocks central muscarinic receptors to treat tremor exclusively"],"a":2,"e":"Idiopathic Parkinson\u0027s disease results from loss of pigmented dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta with intracytoplasmic alpha-synuclein Lewy bodies. Levodopa (precursor crossing the BBB) is the most efficacious agent for bradykinesia and rigidity; adding Carbidopa (or benserazide), which does not cross the BBB, inhibits peripheral aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase, cutting required levodopa dose by ~75% and preventing peripheral dopamine-mediated nausea, vomiting, and arrhythmias.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03062","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Drug Overdose","q":"A 22-year-old woman presents 5 hours after intentionally ingesting 18 g of paracetamol (acetaminophen) tablets. She has mild nausea, normal initial transaminases, and her 4-hour serum paracetamol level plots above the treatment line on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram (195 mcg/mL). What is the mechanism and urgency of the specific antidote, N-Acetylcysteine (NAC)?","o":["Perform immediate liver transplantation today before giving NAC","Administer IV flumazenil and wait 24 hours before repeating paracetamol level","NAC directly chelates paracetamol in the stomach; delay until ALT rises \u003e 1,000 U/L","NAC replenishes hepatic glutathione stores to detoxify the hepatotoxic metabolite NAPQI; initiate IV/oral NAC immediately (maximal hepatoprotection within 8 hours of ingestion)"],"a":3,"e":"In paracetamol overdose, glucuronidation and sulphation pathways saturate, shunting excess drug through CYP2E1 into N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI), which depletes glutathione and causes centrilobular (Zone 3) hepatic necrosis. N-Acetylcysteine provides cysteine precursors to restore hepatic glutathione (and directly conjugates NAPQI). Starting NAC within 8 hours of acute ingestion prevents severe hepatotoxicity in nearly 100% of patients.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03063","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Benign Anorectal Disorders","q":"A 29-year-old woman with chronic constipation complains of sharp, tearing anal pain during and for 1-2 hours after defaecation with streaks of bright-red blood on stool. Inspection reveals a longitudinal mucosal tear at the posterior midline (6 o\u0027clock) with an external sentinel skin tag and internal sphincter spasm. Why is surgical lateral internal sphincterotomy (or topical GTN/diltiazem) effective in chronic anal fissure?","o":["Relieves internal anal sphincter hypertonia and local mucosal ischaemia at the posterior midline, allowing fissure healing","Eliminates external sphincter tone causing faecal incontinence routinely","Directly excises internal haemorrhoidal venous cushions","Cures underlying Crohn\u0027s perianal fistula in all cases"],"a":0,"e":"The posterior midline (6 o\u0027clock) anoderm has relatively poor blood supply and sustains mechanical shearing from hard stools; secondary internal anal sphincter spasm perpetuates ischaemia, preventing healing. First-line therapy includes stool softeners/fibre, warm sitz baths, and topical vasodilators (\u0027chemical sphincterotomy\u0027: 0.2% GTN or 2% diltiazem cream). Chronic refractory fissures respond to Lateral Internal Sphincterotomy (LIS, dividing the distal internal sphincter below the dentate line, preserving the external sphincter).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03064","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Venous Thromboembolism","q":"A 58-year-old woman with pelvic malignancy develops acute massive swelling of the entire left lower limb from thigh to toes, severe bursting pain, tense cyanotic bluish-purple skin discolouration, venous bullae, and diminished dorsalis pedis Doppler signal (phlegmasia cerulea dolens). Duplex scan confirms complete occlusive iliofemoral and collateral DVT. Beyond immediate IV unfractionated heparin and leg elevation, what urgent intervention prevents venous gangrene and amputation?","o":["Oral aspirin 75 mg daily and outpatient compression stocking alone","Urgent catheter-directed thrombolysis / pharmacomechanical thrombectomy (or surgical venous thrombectomy) to restore venous outflow, +/- fasciotomy","Immediate below-knee amputation without attempting venous recanalisation","High-dose IV furosemide diuresis alone"],"a":1,"e":"Phlegmasia cerulea dolens (\u0027painful blue leg\u0027) represents near-total occlusion of both deep and collateral iliofemoral veins, raising compartment/capillary hydrostatic pressure above arterial inflow and threatening venous gangrene, hypovolaemic shock, and limb loss (contrasting with \u0027phlegmasia alba dolens\u0027 / milk leg where collateral flow preserves skin perfusion). Immediate IV UFH, fluid resuscitation, and urgent catheter-directed thrombolysis/mechanical thrombectomy (or open Fogarty venous thrombectomy + fasciotomy if compartment syndrome) are limb-saving.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03065","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Congenital GI Anomalies","q":"A 2-year-old boy presents with acute painless maroon-coloured lower gastrointestinal bleeding (Hb 7.2 g/dL). Upper GI endoscopy and colonoscopy show no bleeding source. Suspecting a Meckel\u0027s diverticulum (remnant of the vitellointestinal/omphalomesenteric duct) containing ectopic gastric mucosa, which nuclear medicine scan is the diagnostic test of choice?","o":["Gallium-67 citrate scan","Iodine-131 whole-body thyroid scan","Technetium-99m (Tc-99m) pertechnetate scan (\u0027Meckel scan\u0027, enhanced with H2-blocker/pentagastrin)","Technetium-99m sulphur colloid liver-spleen scan"],"a":2,"e":"Meckel\u0027s diverticulum is a true congenital diverticulum on the antimesenteric border of the ileum (~2 feet from ileocaecal valve, ~2% population) resulting from incomplete obliteration of the vitellointestinal duct. Painless paediatric rectal bleeding occurs when ectopic gastric parietal mucosa secretes hydrochloric acid causing adjacent ileal ulceration. Tc-99m pertechnetate is avidly concentrated by gastric mucosal mucin-producing cells, making the \u0027Meckel scan\u0027 the non-invasive test of choice before segmental ileal resection (or wedge diverticulectomy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03066","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Breast Malignancy Presentations","q":"A 57-year-old woman presents with a 5-month history of unilateral, erythematous, scaling, and crusted erosion of the right nipple and areola that failed to heal after 6 weeks of topical corticosteroid cream prescribed for presumed eczema. Why is full-thickness wedge/punch biopsy of the nipple-areola complex plus bilateral mammography mandatory?","o":["Paget\u0027s disease is a benign viral wart cured by cryotherapy","Only bilateral nipple lesions ever harbour underlying breast carcinoma","Topical eczema commonly destroys the nipple unilaterally and requires oral antifungals","Unilateral persistent \u0027nipple eczema\u0027 represents Paget\u0027s disease of the nipple (intraepidermal adenocarcinoma cells associated with underlying DCIS or invasive breast carcinoma in \u003e 90%) until proven otherwise"],"a":3,"e":"Paget\u0027s disease of the breast presents as unilateral, persistent, crusting/oozing nipple-areolar erythema spreading outward from the nipple (whereas true eczema is usually bilateral, itchy, involves the areola first, and responds promptly to topical steroids). Histology shows large pale vacuolated intraepidermal Paget cells; \u003e90% have underlying DCIS or invasive ductal carcinoma, requiring mammography/MRI and oncological breast surgery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03067","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Urethral Trauma","q":"A 28-year-old man involved in a pelvic crush injury arrives with a distended painful bladder, blood at the external urethral meatus, perineal \u0027butterfly\u0027 bruising, and a high-riding prostate on rectal examination. Which diagnostic step and urinary diversion are safe, and what manoeuvre is strictly contraindicated?","o":["Strictly AVOID blind transurethral Foley catheterisation; perform a Retrograde Urethrogram (RUG) and establish bladder drainage via Suprapubic Cystostomy (SPC) if urethral disruption is confirmed","Perform immediate TURP in the emergency room","Administer IV furosemide 80 mg to force spontaneous micturition","Force a 16F Foley catheter blindly through the urethra immediately"],"a":0,"e":"Blood at the urethral meatus, inability to void, perineal/scrotal haematoma, and a high-riding prostate after pelvic fracture strongly indicate posterior (membranous) urethral disruption. Blind urethral Foley passage risks converting a partial tear into a complete transection, introducing infection into the pelvic haematoma, and worsening stricture/incontinence. Perform a Retrograde Urethrogram (RUG) first and divert urine via Suprapubic Cystostomy (SPC), with delayed endoscopic/open urethroplasty after ~3 months.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03068","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Congenital Biliary Anomalies","q":"A 19-year-old woman presents with intermittent right upper quadrant pain, mild obstructive jaundice, and a soft cystic mass in the right hypochondrium. MRCP demonstrates fusiform dilatation of the common bile duct sparing the intrahepatic ducts (Todani Type I choledochal cyst) with an anomalous pancreaticobiliary junction. Why is complete cyst excision with Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy mandatory rather than internal cyst-duodenostomy drainage?","o":["Internal drainage causes acute splenic rupture","Retained cyst wall carries a high lifelong risk (~10-30%) of malignant transformation into cholangiocarcinoma and recurrent cholangitis","Complete excision allows lifelong oral ursodeoxycholic acid discontinuation without restoring bile flow","Choledochal cysts spontaneously regress completely by age 30"],"a":1,"e":"Todani Type I (fusiform extrahepatic CBD dilatation, ~80-90% of choledochal cysts) frequently involves an anomalous pancreaticobiliary ductal junction allowing pancreatic enzyme reflux, chronic biliary epithelial injury, and high risk of cholangiocarcinoma. Simple internal drainage (cystoduodenostomy/cystojejunostomy) leaves the dysplastic cyst mucosa exposed, leading to strictures, stones, cholangitis, and malignancy; hence complete extrahepatic cyst excision + cholecystectomy + Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy is mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03069","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Retained Placenta","q":"Following vaginal delivery of a term baby and active management (10 IU oxytocin IM), the placenta fails to separate after 30 minutes despite repeat controlled cord traction, and moderate vaginal bleeding begins. The bladder is emptied and IV access/cross-matched blood are secured. What is the definitive procedure, and when must the obstetrician STOP attempting manual cleavage?","o":["Immediate abdominal hysterectomy in all cases before assessing separation","Forceful pulling on the umbilical cord in the labour ward without anaesthesia until the cord snaps","Manual Removal of Placenta (MRP) under adequate anaesthesia/analgesia using the ulnar border of the hand along the cleavage plane—stopping immediately if NO cleavage plane exists (morbidly adherent placenta accreta)","Administer oral misoprostol and wait 24 hours"],"a":2,"e":"If the placenta remains undelivered \u003e30 minutes after birth (retained placenta), controlled cord traction is stopped (forceful traction causes cord avulsion or uterine inversion). Under regional/general anaesthesia with antibiotic cover, one hand steadies the uterine fundus abdominally while the internal gloved hand follows the cord to locate the placental edge and gently sweeps along the decidua basalis cleavage plane with the ulnar border; if no plane exists (placenta accreta spectrum), STOP immediately to avoid catastrophic tearing and proceed to laparotomy/hysterectomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03070","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Breech Presentation","q":"A 26-year-old Rh-negative primigravida at 36+4 weeks of gestation has an uncomplicated singleton frank breech presentation, normal amniotic fluid index (12 cm), posterior upper-segment placenta, and reactive non-stress test. Which intervention should be offered to reduce Caesarean section rate, and what medication is mandatory post-procedure if she is unsensitised Rh-negative?","o":["Internal podalic version in the outpatient clinic","Oral misoprostol induction for assisted breech vaginal delivery at 36 weeks","Immediate elective Caesarean section at 36+4 weeks without discussing ECV","External Cephalic Version (ECV) under ultrasound/CTG monitoring (with short-term tocolysis) PLUS 300 mcg Anti-D immunoglobulin IM afterwards"],"a":3,"e":"For uncomplicated singleton breech at \u003e= 36 weeks (nulliparas at 36 wks, multiparas at 37 wks) without contraindications (placenta praevia, abruption, oligohydramnios, multiple pregnancy, prior classical scar, fetal compromise, ruptured membranes), External Cephalic Version (ECV) succeeds in ~50-60% and lowers Caesarean delivery rates. Because ECV can cause fetomaternal haemorrhage, Anti-D immunoglobulin must be administered to unsensitised Rh-negative mothers afterwards.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03071","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Endometriosis","q":"A 28-year-old nulliparous woman presents with progressive congestive dysmenorrhoea starting 2-3 days before menses, deep dyspareunia, and 3 years of primary infertility. Pelvic exam reveals a fixed retroverted uterus and tender nodularity along the uterosacral ligaments; transvaginal ultrasound shows a unilocular 5 cm ovarian cyst with diffuse low-level homogeneous \u0027ground-glass\u0027 internal echoes (endometrioma). What is the diagnosis, and why is hormonal suppression (COCPs/GnRH agonists) NOT used alone when pregnancy is desired?","o":["Pelvic endometriosis; medical hormonal therapies suppress ovulation/contraception and do not improve spontaneous fertility—requiring laparoscopic excision/ablation (protecting ovarian reserve) or ART","Polycystic ovary syndrome; letrozole dissolves chocolate cysts","Pelvic inflammatory disease; prolonged oral doxycycline cures endometriomas","Mucinous cystadenoma; lifelong combined oral contraceptives"],"a":0,"e":"Progressive dysmenorrhoea, deep dyspareunia, infertility, uterosacral nodularity, and \u0027ground-glass\u0027 ovarian endometrioma (\u0027chocolate cyst\u0027) confirm pelvic endometriosis. While hormonal suppression (continuous COCPs, dienogest/progestins, GnRH agonists) effectively controls endometriosis-associated pelvic pain, these agents block ovulation and do NOT enhance fertility after discontinuation; fertility management relies on conservative laparoscopic staging/adhesiolysis (careful cystectomy preserving AMH/ovarian reserve) and assisted reproductive technology (IUI/IVF).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03072","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Endometrial Hyperplasia","q":"A 49-year-old obese, perimenopausal multiparous woman with completed family presents with heavy irregular bleeding. Endometrial biopsy reveals Endometrial Hyperplasia WITH Atypia (Endometrial Intraepithelial Neoplasia / EIN). Why is total hysterectomy preferred over progestin observation here?","o":["Simple D\u0026C alone is permanently curative for EIN","Endometrial hyperplasia WITH atypia carries a high (~25-40%) risk of progression to or coexisting invasive endometrioid endometrial carcinoma, making total hysterectomy (+ BSO as indicated) the definitive treatment","High-dose unopposed oestrogen reverses endometrial atypia","Non-atypical and atypical hyperplasia have identical 0.1% cancer risk and need no follow-up"],"a":1,"e":"Under WHO classification, endometrial hyperplasia WITHOUT atypia carries low progression risk (\u003c5%) and responds well to progestin therapy (Levonorgestrel-IUS first-line, or oral progestins). Conversely, Endometrial Hyperplasia WITH Atypia (EIN) carries a ~25-40% risk of occult synchronous or subsequent invasive endometrial adenocarcinoma; therefore, total extrafascial hysterectomy (+ BSO in postmenopausal/perimenopausal women) is definitive treatment (high-dose progestins are reserved only for young patients strongly desiring fertility or medically unfit for surgery).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03073","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Measles (Rubeola)","q":"An unimmunised 2-year-old child presents with 4 days of high fever, hacking cough, coryza, bilateral conjunctivitis, and tiny bluish-white spots on an erythematous base on the buccal mucosa opposite the lower molars (Koplik spots), followed by a confluent maculopapular rash starting behind the ears. Along with supportive isolation and hydration, what specific micronutrient regimen is WHO/IAP-recommended to halve mortality and prevent corneal blindness?","o":["Single dose of Vitamin A 5,000 IU orally","IV acyclovir for 14 days","Oral Vitamin A 200,000 IU once daily for TWO consecutive days (Day 0 and Day 1), plus a third dose 2-4 weeks later if signs of vitamin A deficiency exist","Systemic oral prednisolone for 10 days"],"a":2,"e":"Fever + 3 Cs (cough, coryza, conjunctivitis) + pathognomonic Koplik spots + cephalocaudal maculopapular rash confirm Measles. Measles acutely depletes hepatic Vitamin A stores, precipitating xerophthalmia/keratomalacia and severe pneumonia/diarrhoea. WHO/IAP guidelines mandate two age-based oral Vitamin A doses on Day 0 and Day 1 (50,000 IU if \u003c6 mo; 100,000 IU if 6-11 mo; 200,000 IU if \u003e=12 mo), cutting measles mortality by ~50%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03074","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Croup (Acute Laryngotracheobronchitis)","q":"A 2-year-old boy presents at night with a harsh \u0027seal-like\u0027 barking cough, hoarse voice, low-grade fever (38.1 degrees C), mild suprasternal retractions, and audible inspiratory stridor at rest (without drooling or toxic tripod posture). AP neck radiograph shows subglottic narrowing (\u0027steeple sign\u0027). Which pharmacologic combination rapidly relieves subglottic oedema?","o":["Oral amoxicillin + salbutamol inhaler alone","Immediate IV ceftriaxone + blind oral intubation in the clinic","Codeine cough syrup","Single-dose oral/IM Dexamethasone (0.6 mg/kg) PLUS nebulised Epinephrine (for stridor at rest, observing 3-4 hours post-nebulisation for rebound)"],"a":3,"e":"Parainfluenza virus-induced subglottic mucosal oedema (croup / acute laryngotracheobronchitis) produces barking cough, hoarseness, stridor, and the \u0027steeple sign\u0027 (distinguished from bacterial epiglottitis by absence of drooling, dysphagia, and toxic tripod appearance). Single-dose Dexamethasone (0.15-0.6 mg/kg PO/IM) is effective across all severities; moderate-to-severe croup with stridor at rest additionally receives nebulised Epinephrine (L-epinephrine 1:1000 or racemic), observing 3-4 hours because epinephrine\u0027s vasoconstrictive effect wears off after ~2 hours before dexamethasone peaks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03075","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Acyanotic Congenital Heart Disease","q":"A 3-year-old girl born after maternal first-trimester rubella infection is asymptomatic but has bounding peripheral pulses, wide pulse pressure (105/45 mmHg), and a continuous \u0027machinery\u0027 (Gibson) murmur heard best in the left second intercostal space (infraclavicular area). Echocardiography confirms an isolated moderate Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA) with left-to-right shunting. What is the definitive treatment in a 3-year-old child?","o":["Percutaneous transcatheter device/coil closure of the PDA (or surgical ligation if unsuitable)","Wait until Eisenmenger syndrome develops before closing","Oral indomethacin or ibuprofen (effective only in preterm neonates during first 2 weeks of life)","Prostaglandin E1 (Alprostadil) infusion"],"a":0,"e":"PDA (classically associated with prematurity and congenital rubella syndrome) connects the proximal left pulmonary artery to the descending aorta distal to the left subclavian artery, producing a continuous machinery murmur, wide pulse pressure, and left-volume overload. While COX inhibitors (ibuprofen/indomethacin) or paracetamol close functional PDAs in preterm neonates, they are ineffective in term/older children; elective percutaneous transcatheter occluder device closure cures isolated PDA and prevents heart failure/endarteritis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03076","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Congenital Intestinal Obstruction","q":"A full-term 3-day-old male infant has failed to pass meconium since birth and develops progressive abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. Digital rectal examination reveals an empty tight rectum followed immediately by an explosive gush of foul-smelling liquid stool and flatus (\u0027blast sign\u0027). Contrast enema shows a narrow spastic rectosigmoid segment with a funnel-shaped transition zone to a dilated proximal colon. What is the gold-standard confirmatory test?","o":["Upper GI barium meal follow-through","Full-thickness or rectal suction biopsy demonstrating ABSENCE of ganglion cells in the submucosal (Meissner\u0027s) and myenteric (Auerbach\u0027s) plexuses with hypertrophied acetylcholinesterase-positive nerve trunks","Colonic transit study with oral lactulose","Sweat chloride test alone"],"a":1,"e":"Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon, often RET/EDNRB mutations, associated with Down syndrome) arises from failure of craniocaudal migration of neural crest cells into the distal colon (~75-80% rectosigmoid). Delayed meconium \u003e48 hours, bilious vomiting, and explosive \u0027blast sign\u0027 on rectal exam require confirmatory rectal biopsy (\u003e= 2 cm above dentate line to avoid normal physiological hypoganglionic zone) proving aganglionosis and nerve trunk hypertrophy before definitive pull-through surgery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03077","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Case-Control Studies","q":"In a hospital-based case-control study investigating oral tobacco chewing and oral squamous cell carcinoma, out of 100 biopsy-confirmed cases, 80 were tobacco chewers and 20 were non-chewers. Out of 100 age-matched controls without cancer, 40 were tobacco chewers and 60 were non-chewers. What is the Odds Ratio (OR) for oral cancer associated with tobacco chewing?","o":["8.0","2.0","6.0","4.0"],"a":2,"e":"In a 2x2 case-control table: Exposed cases (a) = 80, Exposed controls (b) = 40, Unexposed cases (c) = 20, Unexposed controls (d) = 60. Exposure Odds Ratio (OR) = (a x d) / (b x c) = (80 x 60) / (40 x 20) = 4,800 / 800 = 6.0 (indicating cases had 6 times the odds of tobacco-chewing exposure compared with controls).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03078","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Cold Chain Equipment and Vaccine Sensitivity","q":"During a cold-chain audit at a Primary Health Centre, you inspect a Deep Freezer (-15 to -25 degrees C) and an Ice-Lined Refrigerator (+2 to +8 degrees C), and find a Pentavalent (DPT-HepB-Hib) vial accidentally frozen solid inside the deep freezer. Which statement accurately reflects UIP cold-chain rules and the Shake Test?","o":["Thaw the frozen Pentavalent vial slowly in a warm water bath and use it if the VVM is white","Place Pentavalent, Hep B, and IPV in the deep freezer at -20 degrees C for long-term storage","Only BCG and Measles vaccines are freeze-sensitive and damaged by freezing","Freeze-sensitive T-series/adjuvanted vaccines (Pentavalent, Hep B, Td, IPV, PCV) must NEVER be placed in the deep freezer (only icepacks are frozen there at PHC); if frozen, discard Pentavalent directly as confirmed by failed Shake Test"],"a":3,"e":"At PHC level, the Deep Freezer (-15 to -25 C) is used solely to freeze icepacks (never to store UIP vaccines; heat-sensitive OPV is stored at -20 C only at state/regional stores). All vaccines at PHC are kept in the ILR (+2 to +8 C), keeping freeze-sensitive vaccines (Hep B, Pentavalent, Td, IPV, PCV) in the upper basket away from the evaporator coils. Freezing destroys aluminium-adjuvanted vaccines (proven by the Shake Test, where damaged vials sediment rapidly compared with an unfrozen control) and they MUST be discarded even if VVM is intact.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03079","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Heavy Metal Toxicity","q":"A 36-year-old man working for 9 years in an unventilated lead-acid battery recycling workshop presents with recurrent crampy abdominal colic, constipation, bilateral wrist drop (radial motor neuropathy), a bluish-black \u0027Burtonian line\u0027 on the gingival margin, microcytic anaemia with prominent basophilic stippling of RBCs, and elevated urinary delta-aminolevulinic acid (ALA). What is the mechanism of the haematological finding and the treatment for severe symptomatic plumbism?","o":["Inhibition of ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase (causing ring sideroblasts and basophilic stippling from inhibited pyrimidine-5\u0027-nucleotidase); remove from exposure + chelation therapy (oral DMSA/succimer or parenteral CaNa2EDTA + BAL)","Direct destruction of B12 intrinsic factor; monthly hydroxocobalamin","Inhibition of vitamin K epoxide reductase; IV vitamin K","Activation of bone marrow erythropoietin; oral iron sulphate monotherapy"],"a":0,"e":"Chronic inorganic lead poisoning (plumbism) inhibits delta-ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase in haem synthesis (elevating blood/urine ALA and free erythrocyte protoporphyrin/ZPP) and inhibits RBC pyrimidine-5\u0027-nucleotidase (retaining ribosomal RNA aggregates seen as basophilic stippling). Classic features include abdominal colic, Burtonian lead line, wrist/foot drop, and nephropathy. Removing exposure and chelating (oral succimer/DMSA for mild-moderate; parenteral BAL + CaNa2EDTA for severe/encephalopathic cases) is definitive.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03080","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Cerebellopontine Angle Tumours","q":"A 48-year-old woman presents with 2 years of slowly progressive unilateral right-sided hearing loss, high-pitched tinnitus, mild unsteadiness, markedly poor speech discrimination disproportionate to pure-tone thresholds, loss of the right corneal reflex, and hypoesthesia over the posterior-superior wall of the right external auditory canal (Hitselberger\u0027s sign). Which investigation is the gold standard to confirm the diagnosis?","o":["Non-contrast CT paranasal sinuses","Gadolinium-enhanced MRI of the internal auditory canal and cerebellopontine angle (vestibular schwannoma)","Caloric test alone followed by tympanoplasty","Pure-tone audiometry alone without neuroimaging"],"a":1,"e":"Vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma, benign Schwann cell tumour of the inferior vestibular nerve in the internal auditory canal/CP angle) presents with asymmetric/unilateral SNHL, tinnitus, retrocochlear poor speech discrimination (roll-over phenomenon), reduced corneal reflex (earliest CN V sign as tumour expands into CP angle), and Hitselberger\u0027s sign (sensory branch of CN VII compressed in IAC). Contrast-enhanced (gadolinium) MRI is the gold-standard diagnostic test.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03081","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Granulomatous and Fungal Nasal Masses","q":"A 26-year-old man from coastal Tamil Nadu who frequently bathes in stagnant freshwater village ponds presents with unilateral nasal obstruction and epistaxis. Anterior rhinoscopy reveals a friable, vascular, polypoidal pink mass attached to the nasal septum studded with tiny grey-white dots resembling a \u0027strawberry\u0027. Histopathology shows large thick-walled sporangia packed with numerous endospores. What is the diagnosis and standard treatment?","o":["Nasopharyngeal carcinoma; primary chemoradiation","Rhinophyma; oral isotretinoin","Rhinosporidiosis (Rhinosporidium seeberi); wide surgical excision with electrocautery of the attachment base (plus oral dapsone adjunct to reduce recurrence)","Allergic ethmoidal polyp; lifelong intranasal fluticasone spray alone"],"a":2,"e":"Rhinosporidiosis (caused by the aquatic protistan parasite Rhinosporidium seeberi, endemic in South/East India) presents as a bleeding strawberry-like polyp studded with white sporangia on the nasal septum/inferior turbinate. Medical antifungal therapy is largely ineffective because the organism cannot be cultured and resists standard antifungals; complete surgical excision with diathermy cauterisation of the base (to prevent autoinoculation of endospores) plus adjuvant dapsone prevents recurrence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03082","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Retinal Vein Occlusion","q":"A 65-year-old hypertensive man presents with sudden painless severe blurring of vision in his left eye (finger counting at 1 metre) and a positive RAPD. Fundoscopy reveals optic disc swelling, markedly dilated and tortuous retinal veins, cotton-wool spots, and extensive flame-shaped and blot haemorrhages across all four retinal quadrants (\u0027blood-and-thunder\u0027 or \u0027splashed-tomato\u0027 fundus). What is the diagnosis, and which dreaded complication typically develops around 3 months (\u002790-day glaucoma\u0027) in the ischaemic type?","o":["Retinitis pigmentosa; bilateral nyctalopia","Diabetic background retinopathy; cataract alone","Central Retinal Artery Occlusion; acute angle-closure pupillary block","Ischaemic Central Retinal Vein Occlusion (CRVO); Neovascular glaucoma (rubeosis iridis driven by retinal VEGF, prevented/treated with Panretinal Photocoagulation and anti-VEGF)"],"a":3,"e":"Four-quadrant flame haemorrhages, dilated tortuous veins, optic disc oedema, and cotton-wool spots produce the classic \u0027blood-and-thunder\u0027 fundus of CRVO. Severe retinal capillary non-perfusion (\u003e10 disc areas) with RAPD defines Ischaemic CRVO, which triggers massive VEGF release causing iris neovascularisation (rubeosis iridis) and refractory Neovascular Glaucoma ~90-100 days post-occlusion (\u002790-day glaucoma\u0027). Close gonioscopic follow-up, intravitreal anti-VEGF (for macular oedema/NVI), and prompt Panretinal Photocoagulation (PRP) prevent blind painful eye.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03083","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Sympathetic Ophthalmia","q":"A 24-year-old carpenter sustained a severe corneoscleral laceration with uveal prolapse in his right eye (no light perception, irreparably blind) 6 weeks ago. He now develops photophobia, ciliary congestion, mutton-fat keratic precipitates, and Dalen-Fuchs nodules in his previously normal LEFT (\u0027sympathising\u0027) eye. What is this condition, and within what time window after severe irreparable trauma can enucleating a blind exciting eye prevent it?","o":["Sympathetic ophthalmia (bilateral granulomatous panuveitis); preventive enucleation within 14 days (2 weeks) of injury—now treat urgently with high-dose systemic corticosteroids + immunosuppressants without removing an eye with residual vision","Phacoanaphylactic endophthalmitis; topical acyclovir drops","Acute angle-closure glaucoma; pilocarpine monotherapy","Bacterial conjunctivitis; ciprofloxacin ointment alone"],"a":0,"e":"Sympathetic ophthalmia is a rare delayed Type IV autoimmune granulomatous panuveitis triggered by exposure of sequestered uveal/retinal antigens following penetrating injury (especially ciliary body/uveal prolapse) to the \u0027exciting\u0027 eye, threatening blindness in the uninjured \u0027sympathising\u0027 eye (histology: Dalen-Fuchs nodules). Enucleating a hopelessly blind, painful exciting eye within 14 days of injury prevents sensitisation; once sympathetic ophthalmia begins, immediate high-dose systemic steroids + immunomodulators (azathioprine/cyclosporine/methotrexate) are mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03084","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Congenital Nasolacrimal Duct Obstruction (CNLDO)","q":"A healthy 4-month-old infant presents with persistent watery tearing (epiphora) and mild mucopurulent matting of the right eyelashes since 2 weeks of age, without photophobia, corneal enlargement, or hazy cornea. Gentle pressure over the lacrimal sac produces mucopurulent regurgitation from the puncta. What is the most common anatomical site of obstruction and appropriate first-line management at 4 months of age?","o":["Viral keratitis; topical acyclovir","Imperforate membrane at the distal end of the nasolacrimal duct (Valve of Hasner); Crigler hydrostatic lacrimal sac massage + intermittent topical antibiotic drops if discharge is purulent (spontaneous resolution in ~90% by 12 months)","Congenital glaucoma; immediate trabeculectomy","Obstruction at the superior canaliculus; immediate dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR) at 4 months"],"a":1,"e":"CNLDO results from delayed canalisation of the distal nasolacrimal duct at the Valve of Hasner (~90% open spontaneously within the first year). Normal corneal size/clarity and absent photophobia distinguish CNLDO from congenital glaucoma (buphthalmos, Haab striae, photophobia). At 4 months, conservative hydrostatic Crigler lacrimal sac massage (pressing downward over the sac to raise pressure and pop the membrane) + hygiene/antibiotic drops suffices; surgical probing is deferred until ~12 months if unresolved.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03085","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Bone and Joint Infections","q":"A 7-year-old boy presents with 3 days of high fever (39.4 degrees C), refusal to walk, and exquisite focal bony tenderness over the distal femoral metaphysis (while knee joint range of motion is minimally restricted with mild sympathetic effusion). Plain femur radiograph on Day 3 is completely normal; WBC is 17,500/mm3 and CRP is 12 mg/dL. What is the diagnosis, sensitive early imaging test, and immediate management?","o":["Rheumatoid arthritis; oral methotrexate","Osteosarcoma; immediate amputation","Acute haematogenous osteomyelitis (most commonly Staphylococcus aureus); MRI is most sensitive early—start empiric IV anti-staphylococcal antibiotics after blood cultures and drain subperiosteal abscess if present or unresponsive at 48 h","Normal growing pains because Day 3 X-ray shows no bone lysis; discharge with paracetamol"],"a":2,"e":"Acute haematogenous osteomyelitis localises to growing long-bone metaphyses (hairpin sluggish blood-flow loops around the distal femur/proximal tibia). Plain radiographs take 10-14 days to show periosteal reaction or lytic changes—so a normal Day-3 X-ray NEVER excludes osteomyelitis! Contrast MRI detects marrow oedema and subperiosteal pus within 24-48 hours. Immediate blood cultures + empiric IV antibiotics covering S. aureus (cloxacillin/cefazolin/vancomycin) +/- surgical decompression prevent chronic osteomyelitis and sequestrum formation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03086","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Shoulder Instability and Dislocation","q":"A 24-year-old cricketer falls onto an abducted, externally rotated arm and presents supporting his right elbow with the opposite hand. Examination reveals flattening of the normal rounded deltoid contour, anterior fullness below the clavicle, positive Dugas\u0027 and Hamilton\u0027s ruler tests, and numbness over a small patch on the lateral upper arm (\u0027regimental badge\u0027 area). Which nerve is injured and what bony glenoid/humeral defects occur in recurrent anterior shoulder dislocation?","o":["Ulnar nerve at Guyon\u0027s canal; Bennett\u0027s fracture","Musculocutaneous nerve; Colles\u0027 fracture","Radial nerve in spiral groove; posterior Monteggia lesion","Axillary nerve (C5-C6); Bankart lesion (anteroinferior glenoid labrum/rim detachment) and Hill-Sachs lesion (posterolateral humeral head impaction)"],"a":3,"e":"Anterior (\u003e95%) glenohumeral dislocation stretches the Axillary nerve winding around the surgical neck of the humerus, causing deltoid weakness and loss of sensation over the lateral deltoid (\u0027regimental badge\u0027 patch)—always documented before and after closed reduction (Kocher/Milch/Stimson). Impaction of the humeral head against the anteroinferior glenoid produces a Hill-Sachs posterolateral humeral head defect and a Bankart anteroinferior labroligamentous avulsion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03087","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Infective Endocarditis Prophylaxis","q":"A 42-year-old man with a mechanical mitral valve prosthesis is scheduled for dental root extraction involving gingival manipulation. Which antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended prior to the procedure, and which valvular condition does NOT require routine antibiotic prophylaxis anymore?","o":["Single dose oral Amoxicillin 2 g given 30-60 minutes before the procedure; mitral valve prolapse without prosthetic material does NOT require prophylaxis","Single dose oral ciprofloxacin 3 days after extraction","No prophylaxis for mechanical valves; give IV vancomycin for isolated MVP","Lifelong daily amoxicillin for 1 month; prosthetic valves do not need prophylaxis"],"a":0,"e":"Under AHA/ESC guidelines, infective endocarditis antibiotic prophylaxis (oral Amoxicillin 2 g—or clindamycin/azithromycin/cephalexin if penicillin-allergic—30-60 min pre-procedure) is restricted to highest-risk cardiac conditions undergoing dental procedures involving gingival tissue, periapical region, or oral mucosa perforation: (1) prosthetic cardiac valves/material; (2) prior infective endocarditis; (3) unrepaired cyanotic CHD or repaired CHD with residual shunts/conduits; and (4) cardiac transplant valvulopathy. Routine prophylaxis is NO longer recommended for mitral valve prolapse, bicuspid aortic valve, or calcific AS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03088","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Valvular Heart Disease","q":"A 27-year-old pregnant woman in India with childhood rheumatic fever presents with progressive exertional dyspnoea and orthopnoea. Auscultation at the cardiac apex in the left lateral decubitus position reveals a loud S1, an opening snap (OS) occurring very close to S2 (A2-OS interval 45 ms), and a long low-pitched rumbling mid-diastolic murmur. Echocardiography shows mitral valve area 0.8 cm2, pliable non-calcified leaflets (Wilkins score 6), no left atrial appendage thrombus, and trivial mitral regurgitation. What does the short A2-OS interval signify, and what is the procedure of choice?","o":["Mild mitral stenosis; reassure until age 50","Severe mitral stenosis (high left atrial pressure opens the valve earlier); Percutaneous Balloon Mitral Valvotomy (PBMV)","Trivial mitral stenosis; oral digoxin monotherapy","Severe aortic regurgitation; immediate mechanical aortic valve replacement"],"a":1,"e":"As rheumatic mitral stenosis worsens, left atrial pressure rises markedly, forcing the stenotic mitral valve open sooner after aortic closure—shortening the A2-to-Opening-Snap interval (\u003c60-70 ms) and lengthening the mid-diastolic murmur. For symptomatic severe MS (MVA \u003c= 1.5 cm2) with favourable valve morphology (Wilkins score \u003c= 8, pliable leaflets, minimal calcification/subvalvular fusion), absent LA thrombus, and \u003c moderate MR, Percutaneous Balloon Mitral Valvotomy (Inoue balloon) is the procedure of choice (safe even in pregnancy with abdominal shielding).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03089","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Pleural Effusion","q":"A 26-year-old man presents with low-grade fever, pleuritic right chest pain, and a moderate right pleural effusion. Serum total protein is 6.0 g/dL and serum LDH is 240 U/L (lab upper limit of normal 220 U/L). Thoracentesis yields straw-coloured fluid with protein 4.2 g/dL, LDH 300 U/L, 92% lymphocytes, and Adenosine Deaminase (ADA) 78 U/L. Calculate the pleural-to-serum protein and LDH ratios and identify the classification and most likely cause.","o":["Protein ratio 0.70 and LDH ratio 0.80 -\u003e Transudative pleural effusion; Congestive heart failure","Protein ratio 1.43 and LDH ratio 0.80 -\u003e Chylothorax","Protein ratio 0.70 (\u003e0.5) and LDH ratio 1.25 (\u003e0.6) -\u003e Exudative pleural effusion; Tuberculous pleuritis","Protein ratio 0.35 and LDH ratio 0.45 -\u003e Transudative pleural effusion; Nephrotic syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Light\u0027s criteria classify a pleural effusion as an EXUDATE if any one of three thresholds is met: (1) pleural/serum protein \u003e 0.5 (here 4.2 / 6.0 = 0.70); (2) pleural/serum LDH \u003e 0.6 (here 300 / 240 = 1.25); or (3) pleural LDH \u003e two-thirds of the upper limit of normal serum LDH (300 \u003e 146.7). In an Indian young adult, a lymphocytic exudate with elevated ADA (\u003e 40 U/L) has \u003e90% sensitivity/specificity for Tuberculous pleural effusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03090","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Viral Hepatitis","q":"A 29-year-old man recovering from acute icteric hepatitis (AST 980 U/L, ALT 1,240 U/L 2 weeks ago) undergoes a viral hepatitis panel showing: HBsAg NEGATIVE, Anti-HBs NEGATIVE, IgM Anti-HBc POSITIVE, and Anti-HAV IgM negative. How is this Hepatitis B serological profile interpreted?","o":["Complete absence of any Hepatitis B exposure","Resolved past Hepatitis B infection with lifelong immunity","Hepatitis B vaccine-induced immunity","Acute Hepatitis B infection in the serological \u0027window period\u0027 (HBsAg has cleared before detectable Anti-HBs appears; IgM Anti-HBc is the sole diagnostic marker)"],"a":3,"e":"During recovery from acute Hepatitis B, HBsAg can fall below detectable levels weeks before protective Anti-HBs becomes measurable—creating the \u0027core window period\u0027. During this gap, IgM Anti-HBc is the ONLY positive serological marker establishing acute/recent HBV infection. (By contrast, vaccine immunity shows ONLY Anti-HBs positive with negative Anti-HBc; resolved natural infection shows Anti-HBs positive + IgG Anti-HBc positive).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03091","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Nephrolithiasis Prevention","q":"A 40-year-old man with recurrent calcium oxalate renal stones has 24-hour urine testing showing idiopathic hypercalciuria (360 mg/day) and normal serum calcium/PTH. Alongside increasing fluid intake (\u003e2.5 L urine/day) and restricting dietary sodium, which advice regarding dietary calcium and pharmacotherapy prevents stone recurrence?","o":["Maintain NORMAL dietary calcium intake (1,000-1,200 mg/day) so calcium binds dietary oxalate in the gut, AND prescribe a Thiazide diuretic (e.g., chlorthalidone/hydrochlorothiazide)","Strictly eliminate all dietary calcium (\u003c 200 mg/day) to lower urinary calcium","Start loop diuretic (furosemide) daily to wash out stones","Start high-dose vitamin C (ascorbic acid 4 g/day) supplementation"],"a":0,"e":"A classic clinical pitfall is advising severe dietary calcium restriction for calcium oxalate stones: low gut calcium leaves dietary oxalate unbound, increasing intestinal oxalate absorption, hyperoxaluria, and MORE stones (plus osteopenia). Maintaining normal dietary calcium + low sodium + potassium citrate (if hypocitraturic) + Thiazide diuretics (which enhance distal tubular Ca2+ reabsorption to lower calciuria) effectively cuts stone recurrence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03092","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Pituitary Tumours","q":"A 43-year-old man presents with enlarging shoe and ring sizes over 5 years, coarse facial features, prognathism, macroglossia, bitemporal visual field loss, and carpal tunnel syndrome. Why is a random serum Growth Hormone (GH) level unreliable for diagnosis, and what is the gold-standard biochemical screening and confirmatory sequence before pituitary MRI?","o":["Check serum prolactin alone and start bromocriptine immediately","GH is secreted in pulsatile bursts; screen with serum Insulin-like Growth Factor-1 (IGF-1) FIRST, then confirm with a 75-g Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (failure of GH to suppress \u003c 1 ng/mL)","Random fasting GH \u003e 0.5 ng/mL alone confirms acromegaly; no OGTT needed","Perform insulin-induced hypoglycaemia tolerance test as first-line"],"a":1,"e":"Growth hormone is secreted episodically with wide physiological spikes (stress, sleep, fasting), making random GH useless for diagnosing acromegaly. Serum IGF-1 provides an integrated 24-hour measure of GH bioactivity and is the best initial screening test. Confirmation requires a 75-g oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT): hyperglycaemia normally suppresses GH (\u003c 1 ng/mL or \u003c 0.4 ng/mL on ultrasensitive assays), whereas somatotroph adenomas fail to suppress GH. Transsphenoidal surgical resection is first-line therapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03093","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Spinal Cord Compression","q":"A 50-year-old poorly controlled diabetic man with a recent furuncle on his forearm presents with 4 days of severe progressive mid-thoracic back pain, fever (39.0 degrees C), focal percussion tenderness over T6, bilateral lower-limb weakness (power 2/5), sensory level at T6, and urinary retention. What is the most likely diagnosis and emergency management?","o":["Lumbar canal stenosis; elective physiotherapy in 4 weeks","Diabetic polyneuropathy; oral pregabalin","Spinal Epidural Abscess (most commonly Staphylococcus aureus); emergency gadolinium-enhanced MRI spine, blood cultures, empiric IV Vancomycin + Ceftriaxone/Cefepime, and urgent neurosurgical decompression","Transverse myelitis; discharge on oral prednisolone alone without MRI"],"a":2,"e":"Back pain + fever + progressive neurological deficit (weakness/sensory level/sphincter dysfunction) in a diabetic or IV-drug-use host constitutes the classic triad of Spinal Epidural Abscess (haematogenous S. aureus seeding most common). Contrast MRI of the spine confirms the epidural collection, and immediate blood cultures + empiric broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (covering MRSA + Gram-negatives) + urgent surgical laminectomy/drainage are required to prevent permanent paraplegia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03094","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)","q":"A 62-year-old man receiving Hydralazine (and previously Procainamide) for cardiovascular disease develops symmetric arthralgias, myalgias, fever, and bilateral pleuritic chest pain with pleural effusions, without renal disease or CNS involvement. Serology shows positive ANA, strongly positive anti-histone antibodies, normal serum complement (C3/C4), and negative anti-dsDNA. What is the diagnosis and expected clinical course?","o":["Rheumatoid arthritis; start lifelong methotrexate","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis; urgent plasmapheresis","Idiopathic lupus nephritis Class IV; requires lifelong IV cyclophosphamide","Drug-induced lupus erythematosus; resolves within weeks to months upon discontinuing the offending drug"],"a":3,"e":"Drug-induced lupus (classically triggered by procainamide, hydralazine, isoniazid, minocycline, methyldopa, anti-TNF agents) presents with constitutional symptoms, arthralgia, and serositis (pleuritis/pericarditis), characteristically SPARING the kidneys and CNS, with anti-histone antibodies (\u003e95%), normal complement levels, and negative anti-dsDNA/anti-Sm. Symptoms resolve after withdrawing the culprit drug (short-course NSAIDs/low-dose steroids only if severe serositis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03095","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Salivary Gland Disorders","q":"A 36-year-old man complains of recurrent painful swelling beneath the right side of his lower jaw that swells noticeably just before and during meals (\u0027mealtime syndrome\u0027) and subsides over 1-2 hours afterwards. Bimanual palpation of the floor of the mouth reveals a firm, tender 6 mm stone along Wharton\u0027s duct, confirmed as a radiopaque calculus on an occlusal X-ray. Why do ~80% of salivary calculi occur in the submandibular gland/duct rather than the parotid?","o":["Submandibular saliva is more alkaline, thicker/mucinous with higher calcium-phosphate content, and drains uphill against gravity through a long tortuous Wharton\u0027s duct","Wharton\u0027s duct opens opposite the upper second molar with downward gravity flow","Submandibular gland produces purely serous saliva without mucin","Parotid saliva has higher mucin and calcium content than submandibular saliva"],"a":0,"e":"Approximately 80-90% of salivary stones occur in the submandibular system (Wharton\u0027s duct, opening at the sublingual papilla) because its mixed seromucinous secretion is viscous, alkaline, and calcium-rich, and must flow upward against gravity through a long, angulated duct around the mylohyoid muscle. Most (~80%) submandibular stones are radiopaque (whereas most parotid stones are radiolucent). Distal duct stones are removed via transoral sialolithotomy or sialoendoscopy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03096","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Aortic Aneurysms","q":"A 67-year-old asymptomatic male chronic smoker undergoes abdominal ultrasound screening revealing an infrarenal Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA) measuring 5.8 cm in maximal anteroposterior diameter (no tenderness or hypotension). What is the standard size threshold for recommending elective repair (EVAR or open) in an asymptomatic male with AAA?","o":["Immediate anticoagulation with warfarin to shrink the aneurysm","Maximal diameter \u003e= 5.5 cm in men (or \u003e= 5.0 cm in women, or rapid growth \u003e 0.5 cm in 6 months / \u003e 1 cm/year)","Elective repair at 3.2 cm in all men","Watchful waiting until diameter reaches \u003e= 8.0 cm"],"a":1,"e":"Based on the UK Small Aneurysm Trial and ADAM trial, annual rupture risk of infrarenal AAA rises sharply once diameter reaches \u003e= 5.5 cm in men (\u003e= 5.0 cm in women), exceeding elective procedural mortality. Small asymptomatic AAAs (3.0-5.4 cm in men) are safely managed with ultrasound surveillance, smoking cessation, and statins/BP control unless rapid expansion (\u003e0.5 cm/6 months), saccular morphology, or symptoms develop.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03097","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding","q":"A 35-year-old man presents after several bouts of violent retching and non-bloody vomiting following an alcohol binge, subsequently vomiting bright-red blood (haematemesis). He is haemodynamically stable (BP 118/76 mmHg, HR 88/min) with a soft non-tender abdomen and no subcutaneous emphysema. Upper GI endoscopy demonstrates a 1.5 cm longitudinal mucosal tear at the gastro-oesophageal junction without active spurting. What is the diagnosis and usual prognosis?","o":["Boerhaave syndrome (full-thickness oesophageal rupture); emergency thoracotomy","Bleeding oesophageal varices; immediate TIPS shunt","Mallory-Weiss syndrome (mucosal tear); \u003e 90% heal spontaneously with conservative PPI/antiemetic support (endoscopic haemoclip/epinephrine only if actively bleeding)","Gastric adenocarcinoma; total gastrectomy"],"a":2,"e":"Repeated retching/vomiting against a closed glottis creates high shear pressure lacerating the mucosa/submucosa at the lesser curvature/gastro-oesophageal junction (Mallory-Weiss tear, distinguished from full-thickness Boerhaave perforation by absence of chest/mediastinal pain, subcutaneous emphysema, or pleural effusion). Most (~90%) stop bleeding spontaneously and heal within 48-72 hours with supportive PPI and antiemetics; active bleeding is easily controlled via endoscopic clipping/thermal/epinephrine injection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03098","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Lactational Mastitis and Breast Abscess","q":"A 25-year-old lactating woman 4 weeks postpartum presents with fever (38.8 degrees C), malaise, and a painful, erythematous, fluctuant 4 cm lump in the upper outer quadrant of her right breast with intact overlying skin. Ultrasound confirms a unilocular lactational breast abscess (likely Staphylococcus aureus). Alongside oral cloxacillin/amoxicillin-clavulanate, what is the preferred modern drainage and breastfeeding advice?","o":["Ultrasound-guided needle aspiration (repeated as needed) with intact skin AND advise continuing breastfeeding/regular breast emptying from both breasts","Immediate permanent cessation of breastfeeding with oral bromocriptine","Radical wide surgical mastectomy","Oral azithromycin alone without draining the pus cavity"],"a":0,"e":"For lactational breast abscesses (\u003c5 cm with healthy overlying skin), repeated ultrasound-guided percutaneous needle aspiration (sent for culture) plus oral anti-staphylococcal antibiotics achieves excellent cure rates with superior cosmesis, minimal pain, and zero interruption of lactation compared with traditional Hilton incision-and-drainage (reserved for large multiloculated abscesses or necrotic overlying skin). Continued breastfeeding/milk expression prevents milk stasis and speeds resolution.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03099","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Primary Vaginal Hydrocele","q":"A 38-year-old man presents with a painless, smooth, ovoid scrotal swelling for 1 year. On examination, the swelling is fluctuant, brilliantly transilluminant, the testis cannot be palpated separately, and the examiner CAN \u0027get above the swelling\u0027 at the root of the scrotum. Which diagnosis and standard adult surgical repair match these findings?","o":["Indirect inguinoscrotal hernia; Lichtenstein mesh hernioplasty","Varicocele; high retroperitoneal Palomo ligation","Seminoma of the testis; needle aspiration in the OPD","Primary vaginal hydrocele; Lord\u0027s plication (for thin-walled small/moderate sac) or Jaboulay\u0027s eversion of the tunica vaginalis"],"a":3,"e":"Being able to \u0027get above the swelling\u0027 confirms a purely scrotal origin (excluding inguinoscrotal hernia), while positive transillumination and inability to separate the testis (unlike an encysted epididymal cyst/spermatocele, which sits separate/posterosuperior to a palpable testis) confirm a primary vaginal hydrocele. Definitive adult surgical treatment is Lord\u0027s plication (minimal dissection, low haematoma rate for thin sacs) or Jaboulay\u0027s eversion (excising/everting thick sacs behind the testis/cord).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03100","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Operative Hepatic Haemorrhage Control","q":"During emergency laparotomy for severe parenchymal liver laceration with brisk haemorrhage, the surgeon places a non-crushing vascular clamp across the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) via the foramen of Winslow (Pringle manoeuvre). Which vessels are occluded, and what does persistent torrential dark venous bleeding DESPITE an effective Pringle manoeuvre suggest?","o":["Occludes the Proper Hepatic Artery and Portal Vein; persistent massive dark bleeding suggests injury to the Retrohepatic IVC or Hepatic Veins","Occludes the Hepatic Veins exclusively; persistent bleeding suggests normal haemostasis","Occludes only the Cystic Artery; persistent bleeding suggests gastric ulcer","Occludes the Inferior Vena Cava and Superior Mesenteric Artery; persistent bleeding suggests splenic rupture"],"a":0,"e":"The Pringle manoeuvre compresses the hepatoduodenal ligament (containing the Portal Vein, Proper Hepatic Artery, and Common Bile Duct) to arrest inflow haemorrhage from liver parenchyma (~15-20 min intermittent clamping). If massive dark venous bleeding continues unabated despite complete inflow occlusion, it is a classic intraoperative sign of Retrohepatic Inferior Vena Cava or major Hepatic Vein laceration (requiring perihepatic packing or total hepatic vascular isolation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03101","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Pre-Induction Cervical Assessment","q":"A primigravida at 41+1 weeks of gestation is admitted for induction of labour (reactive NST, intact membranes). Vaginal examination shows: cervical dilatation 1 cm, effacement 40%, fetal head at -2 station, firm cervical consistency, and posterior cervical position. Calculate her Bishop score and select the appropriate initial induction step.","o":["Bishop score = 1; give intramuscular progesterone","Bishop score = 3 (unfavourable); perform pre-induction cervical ripening (prostaglandin E2/misoprostol or Foley balloon catheter) BEFORE starting oxytocin","Bishop score = 8 (favourable); proceed directly to amniotomy and oxytocin","Bishop score = 6 (favourable); perform immediate Caesarean section"],"a":1,"e":"Modified Bishop Score breakdown: Dilatation 1-2 cm = 1 pt; Effacement 40-50% = 1 pt; Station -2 = 1 pt; Consistency firm = 0 pts; Position posterior = 0 pts -\u003e Total = 3. A Bishop score \u003c 6 (especially \u003c= 3-4) indicates an unripe/unfavourable cervix where direct oxytocin/amniotomy carries high failed-induction Caesarean risk; hence cervical ripening with vaginal PGE2 (dinoprostone), low-dose misoprostol, or mechanical Foley balloon is indicated first.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03102","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Polyhydramnios","q":"A 30-year-old woman with poorly controlled gestational diabetes at 36 weeks has fundal height measuring 42 cm, tense shiny abdomen, fluid thrill, and difficulty palpating fetal parts. Ultrasound confirms severe polyhydramnios (Amniotic Fluid Index 28 cm, deepest vertical pocket 10 cm) and a structurally normal macrosomic fetus in unstable lie. During labour management, if artificial rupture of membranes is ever performed, why MUST it be done via controlled high/needle amniotomy in a stabilised longitudinal lie?","o":["Because rapid liquor drainage immediately prevents PPH","Because polyhydramnios protects against cord prolapse","Sudden uncontrolled gush of excess liquor risks umbilical cord prolapse and abruptio placentae (plus high risk of postpartum uterine atony after delivery)","To deliberately induce fetal transverse lie"],"a":2,"e":"Polyhydramnios (AFI \u003e 24-25 cm or single deepest vertical pocket \u003e= 8 cm; commonly associated with maternal diabetes, fetal GI atresias such as oesophageal/duodenal atresia, anencephaly, or twin-twin transfusion) overdistends the uterus and prevents engagement. Sudden spontaneous or unguarded rupture of membranes causes violent decompression, carrying extreme risk of umbilical cord prolapse, malpresentation, and abruptio placentae—and overstretching predisposes to severe atonic PPH.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03103","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Hyperemesis Gravidarum","q":"A 23-year-old primigravida at 10 weeks of gestation presents with 3 weeks of intractable vomiting, \u003e6% pre-pregnancy weight loss, dry mucous membranes, ketonuria (4+), hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis, and mild confusion with horizontal nystagmus. What is the single most critical vitamin supplement to administer IV BEFORE infusing dextrose-containing fluids?","o":["IV Vitamin K to prevent neural tube defects","High-dose Vitamin A (retinol) 200,000 IU","Oral Vitamin D alone","IV Thiamine (Vitamin B1, 100-500 mg) to prevent/treat Wernicke encephalopathy"],"a":3,"e":"Prolonged starvation and vomiting in severe Hyperemesis Gravidarum deplete thiamine stores. Administering IV dextrose before repleting Thiamine rapidly consumes residual thiamine pyrophosphate in carbohydrate metabolism and precipitates acute Wernicke encephalopathy (triad: confusion/encephalopathy, ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus, ataxia). Always give parenteral Thiamine before or with IV dextrose alongside antiemetics (doxylamine-pyridoxine, metoclopramide, ondansetron) and electrolyte correction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03104","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID)","q":"A 24-year-old sexually active woman presents with lower abdominal pain, mucopurulent cervical discharge, cervical motion tenderness, and bilateral adnexal tenderness (afebrile, not pregnant, able to tolerate oral fluids, no tubo-ovarian abscess on ultrasound). Which empiric outpatient antimicrobial regimen covers Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Chlamydia trachomatis, and pelvic anaerobes?","o":["Single-dose IM Ceftriaxone 500 mg PLUS oral Doxycycline 100 mg BID for 14 days PLUS oral Metronidazole 500 mg BID for 14 days (plus partner treatment)","Oral fluconazole 150 mg single dose","IM benzathine penicillin G alone","Oral amoxicillin monotherapy for 5 days"],"a":0,"e":"Acute PID requires low diagnostic threshold (uterine, adnexal, or cervical motion tenderness in sexually active young women) to prevent tubal-factor infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain. Current CDC/MoHFW syndromic regimens combine IM Ceftriaxone 500 mg single dose (for N. gonorrhoeae) + oral Doxycycline 14 days (for C. trachomatis) + oral Metronidazole 14 days (for anaerobes/BV), along with partner notification and treatment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03105","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Adnexal / Ovarian Torsion","q":"A 21-year-old unmarried woman presents with sudden-onset severe right iliac fossa pain, repeated vomiting, and low-grade fever. Pregnancy test is negative. Transvaginal/transabdominal Doppler ultrasound shows a 7 cm right ovarian dermoid cyst with an enlarged oedematous ovary, peripherally displaced follicles, twisted vascular pedicle (\u0027whirlpool sign\u0027), and absent venous/arterial flow. On emergency laparoscopy, the ovary appears bluish-purple and swollen after twisting 360 degrees twice. What is the modern standard surgical approach?","o":["Administer IV alteplase thrombolysis without laparoscopy","Urgent laparoscopic detorsion (untwisting the pedicle) and ovarian cystectomy to preserve ovarian parenchyma and fertility—even if the ovary initially looks dark/dusky (unless frankly necrotic/friable)","Conservative outpatient analgesia and repeat ultrasound in 2 weeks","Routine immediate salpingo-oophorectomy in all dark-appearing ovaries"],"a":1,"e":"Ovarian torsion (commonly triggered by benign mobile cysts such as mature cystic teratoma/dermoid) is a surgical emergency. Historically, surgeons feared thromboembolism or necrosis and excised bluish ovaries; modern evidence proves \u003e90% of bluish-purple ovaries recover normal follicular function after simple laparoscopic detorsion and cystectomy (and detorsion does not increase PE risk). Oophorectomy is reserved only if frankly necrotic/non-viable after detorsion or postmenopausal.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03106","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Congenital Hypothyroidism","q":"A 3-week-old term neonate presents with prolonged unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia, constipation, umbilical hernia, coarse dry skin, macroglossia, wide open anterior and posterior fontanelles, hypotonia, and hoarse cry. Newborn heel-prick screening shows TSH 140 mIU/L and very low free T4. Why MUST oral Levothyroxine (10-15 mcg/kg/day) be started immediately within the first 2-3 weeks of life?","o":["Delaying until 1 year of age gives identical neurocognitive outcomes","Early levothyroxine prevents Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Thyroid hormone is critical for early postnatal brain myelination and synaptogenesis; delaying treatment past 4-6 weeks causes irreversible intellectual disability (cretinism)","Levothyroxine is only needed cosmetically for the umbilical hernia"],"a":2,"e":"Congenital hypothyroidism (most commonly thyroid dysgenesis ~85%, or dyshormonogenesis) is the most common preventable cause of intellectual disability worldwide. Because clinical signs are often subtle at birth (masked partially by maternal T4), universal newborn TSH screening (48-72 h post-birth) and immediate high-dose Levothyroxine (10-15 mcg/kg/day crushed in breastmilk/water—never soy formula or iron concurrently!) within 2 weeks normalise IQ.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03107","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Acyanotic Congenital Heart Disease","q":"A term infant appears asymptomatic at birth, then at 6 weeks of age develops tachypnoea, sweating during feeds, poor weight gain, hepatomegaly, and a loud harsh pansystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border (perimembranous VSD). Why do symptoms of congestive heart failure in a large VSD typically manifest around 4 to 8 weeks of life rather than on Day 1?","o":["Ductus arteriosus closure triggers cyanosis in VSD","The VSD spontaneously enlarges 10-fold at 6 weeks in all infants","Maternal antibodies protect the myocardium until week 6","High neonatal pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) initially limits left-to-right shunting at birth; as PVR falls over 4-8 weeks, left-to-right shunt volume surges causing pulmonary overcirculation and heart failure"],"a":3,"e":"At birth, physiological elevation of pulmonary vascular resistance keeps right ventricular pressure close to left ventricular pressure, minimising left-to-right flow across a VSD. Over the first 4-8 weeks, PVR drops normally, unleashing massive left-to-right shunting, pulmonary venous congestion, and heart failure (treated initially with furosemide/spironolactone, ACEi, high-calorie feeds, and timely surgical/device closure for large VSDs with PAH or refractory CHF before irreversible Eisenmenger syndrome develops).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03108","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Intestinal Malrotation and Midgut Volvulus","q":"A previously healthy 5-day-old full-term breastfed neonate suddenly develops persistent dark-green BILIOUS vomiting, irritability, and mild abdominal tenderness with bloody stools. An urgent upper GI contrast series shows the duodenojejunal (DJ) flexure lying to the RIGHT of the midline with a classic \u0027corkscrew\u0027 tapering configuration. What is the diagnosis and immediate surgical emergency procedure?","o":["Intestinal malrotation with midgut volvulus; immediate emergency exploratory laparotomy and Ladd\u0027s procedure (detorsion counterclockwise, division of Ladd\u0027s bands, widening mesenteric base, appendicectomy)","Benign cow\u0027s milk protein allergy; switch to soy formula","Physiological gastro-oesophageal reflux; thicken feeds","Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis; wait 5 days for elective pyloromyotomy"],"a":0,"e":"Sudden BILIOUS (green) vomiting in a neonate is intestinal malrotation with midgut volvulus until proven otherwise—a true surgical catastrophe threatening total necrosis of the entire SMA-supplied midgut within hours. Upper GI series is diagnostic (abnormal DJ junction position and \u0027corkscrew\u0027 volvulus). Immediate resuscitation and emergency Ladd\u0027s procedure (counterclockwise detorsion, dividing peritoneal Ladd\u0027s bands, broadening the mesenteric root, placing small bowel on right and colon on left, + appendicectomy) save the bowel.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03109","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Neonatal Metabolic Emergencies","q":"A small-for-gestational-age (SGA) term neonate weighing 2.0 kg develops jitteriness, lethargy, and a brief multifocal clonic seizure at 4 hours of life; bedside glucometer and venous plasma glucose measure 22 mg/dL. What is the immediate emergency IV glucose bolus and maintenance Glucose Infusion Rate (GIR)?","o":["Withhold IV dextrose and give IM glucagon alone in an SGA infant with depleted glycogen stores","IV 10% Dextrose 2 mL/kg (200 mg/kg) bolus slowly over 1 minute, followed immediately by continuous 10% Dextrose infusion at GIR 6-8 mg/kg/min (NEVER use 25% or 50% hypertonic dextrose)","IV 50% Dextrose 10 mL/kg rapid push","Oral sugar water via dropper while seizing"],"a":1,"e":"Symptomatic neonatal hypoglycaemia (\u003c40-45 mg/dL, especially with seizures/jitteriness/apnoea) causes irreversible occipital-parietal neuronal injury. Immediate management gives 2 mL/kg of 10% Dextrose IV bolus (followed by continuous IV infusion at GIR 6-8 mg/kg/min, titrating up to 10-12 mg/kg/min if needed) with recheck at 15-30 min. Hyperosmolar 25-50% dextrose is strictly avoided in neonates because it causes rebound hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia and intraventricular haemorrhage/venous thrombosis; glucagon fails in SGA babies who have depleted hepatic glycogen.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03110","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Low Birth Weight Care","q":"In an Indian Special Newborn Care Unit (SNCU) and at home post-discharge, Kangaroo Mother Care (KMC) is recommended for clinically stable low-birth-weight neonates (\u003c 2,000 g). Which two core components define KMC, and what major clinical benefits does it achieve?","o":["Strict incubator isolation separating mother and baby with formula feeding","Cold-water sponging with intermittent bottle feeding","Early, prolonged, continuous skin-to-skin contact on the mother\u0027s/caregiver\u0027s chest AND exclusive breastfeeding; reduces hypothermia, severe nosocomial sepsis, and neonatal mortality","Prone sleeping on soft pillows away from caregiver"],"a":2,"e":"WHO/MoHFW India KMC guidelines define Kangaroo Mother Care by two essential pillars—early prolonged skin-to-skin contact (upright between the mother\u0027s breasts in a frog-like position) and exclusive breastfeeding (supported by early discharge and follow-up). KMC dramatically lowers hypothermia, hypoglycaemia, apnoea, severe infection/sepsis, and mortality in stable LBW/preterm infants while strengthening maternal bonding.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03111","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Lymphatic Filariasis Elimination (ELF)","q":"In an endemic district of Odisha under India\u0027s Elimination of Lymphatic Filariasis (ELF) programme (transmitted by Culex quinquefasciatus), when should peripheral blood smears be collected to detect Wuchereria bancrofti microfilariae, and which annual single-dose Mass Drug Administration (MDA) combination is used?","o":["Early morning sputum smear; HRZE for 6 months","Afternoon stool smear; oral metronidazole","Midday blood smear at 12:00 noon; daily chloroquine for 14 days","Night blood smear between 10:00 PM and 2:00 AM (nocturnal periodicity); single annual dose of DEC (Diethylcarbamazine) + Albendazole (or Triple-drug IDA: Ivermectin + DEC + Albendazole)"],"a":3,"e":"Wuchereria bancrofti microfilariae exhibit nocturnal periodicity (circulating maximally in peripheral blood between 10 PM and 2 AM while resting in pulmonary capillaries during the day; daytime rapid filarial antigen FTS cards can also detect adult worm antigen). India\u0027s NVBDCP/NCVBDC ELF strategy conducts annual single-dose MDA using DEC (6 mg/kg) + Albendazole (400 mg), or accelerated Triple-Drug Therapy (IDA: Ivermectin + DEC + Albendazole) in eligible districts, excluding children \u003c2 years, pregnant women, and severely ill persons.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03112","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Summary Measures of Population Health","q":"In Global Burden of Disease (GBD) assessments and health economics, which composite metric sums Years of Life Lost due to premature mortality (YLL) and Years Lost due to Disability (YLD), where 1 unit equals one lost year of healthy life?","o":["Disability-Adjusted Life Year (DALY)","Quality-Adjusted Life Year (QALY)","Sullivan\u0027s Health Expectancy Index (DFLE)","Physical Quality of Life Index (PQLI)"],"a":0,"e":"DALY = YLL (Years of Life Lost from premature death compared with standard life expectancy) + YLD (Years Lived with Disability, weighted by disability severity). One DALY represents one lost year of healthy life (hence public health programmes aim to minimise DALYs averted/lost, whereas QALYs measure healthy years gained in cost-utility analysis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03113","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Categorical Data Analysis","q":"A paediatrician compares post-vaccination fever proportions (Yes/No) between two independent small cohorts (n = 12 per group) in a 2x2 contingency table, where two of the four cells have EXPECTED frequencies of 2.5 and 3.0 (\u003c 5). Which statistical test is valid instead of Pearson\u0027s Chi-square test, and which test would apply if the binary outcome were paired in the SAME subjects before and after an intervention?","o":["Pearson Chi-square without correction regardless of expected cell \u003c 5; Kaplan-Meier for paired proportions","Fisher\u0027s Exact Test for the small independent 2x2 table; McNemar\u0027s test for paired binary outcomes","Unpaired Student\u0027s t-test for the small 2x2 table; One-way ANOVA for paired binary outcomes","Mann-Whitney U test for nominal Yes/No counts; Kruskal-Wallis for paired binary data"],"a":1,"e":"Pearson\u0027s Chi-square test approximates a continuous chi-squared distribution and becomes unreliable when \u003e20% of cells have expected frequencies \u003c 5 (or any cell \u003c 1). In small 2x2 tables with expected cell count \u003c 5, Fisher\u0027s Exact Test computes exact hypergeometric probabilities. When comparing paired/matched binary (Yes/No) proportions in the same individuals before/after treatment, McNemar\u0027s test (based on discordant pairs) is used.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03114","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Allergic Conjunctivitis","q":"A 9-year-old boy presents every summer with intense bilateral ocular itching, photophobia, and thick, stringy, ropy mucoid discharge. Eversion of the upper eyelid reveals giant flat-topped polygonal \u0027cobblestone\u0027 papillae on the upper tarsal conjunctiva, along with gelatinous limbal thickening and whitish Horner-Trantas dots at the limbus. What is the diagnosis and safe long-term maintenance therapy?","o":["Bacterial gonococcal conjunctivitis; IV ceftriaxone","Trachoma; oral azithromycin alone","Vernal Keratoconjunctivitis (VKC); topical dual-acting antihistamine/mast-cell stabiliser (e.g., olopatadine/ketotifen) + cold compresses, reserving short tapered topical soft steroids only for severe flare/shield ulcer under IOP monitoring","Primary open-angle glaucoma; latanoprost drops"],"a":2,"e":"VKC (spring catarrh) is a recurrent bilateral IgE- and Th2-mediated allergic conjunctivitis in boys aged 5-15 years, featuring intense itching, ropy discharge, giant \u0027cobblestone\u0027 tarsal papillae, and limbal Horner-Trantas dots (eosinophil aggregates). Long-term maintenance uses dual antihistamine/mast-cell stabilisers (olopatadine) and lubricants/cyclosporine/tacrolimus drops; unsupervised chronic topical steroid drops MUST be avoided as they cause devastating steroid-induced glaucoma and cataracts in children.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03115","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Degenerative Conjunctival Lesions","q":"A 45-year-old outdoor farmer in Rajasthan presents with a progressive wing-shaped fleshy fibrovascular fold growing from the nasal bulbar conjunctiva onto the cornea, causing with-the-rule astigmatism and encroaching toward the pupillary axis (firmly adherent to underlying tissue throughout, unable to pass a probe beneath its neck). Which surgical technique achieves the lowest recurrence rate?","o":["Simple probing under the lesion (which only passes in pseudopterygium)","Topical pilocarpine eye drops","Bare-sclera excision alone without graft","Surgical excision (avulsion/keratectomy) combined with Conjunctival-Limbal Autograft (using fibrin glue or sutures)"],"a":3,"e":"Pterygium is a UV-B-induced elastotic degeneration of subconjunctival tissue invading Bowman\u0027s layer onto the cornea (Stocker\u0027s iron line at the advancing head; firmly adherent at the limbus so a probe CANNOT pass underneath, unlike a post-inflammatory pseudopterygium). Bare-sclera excision carries ~40-60% recurrence; excision with a Conjunctival Autograft (or amniotic membrane graft/mitomycin-C) lowers recurrence to \u003c5%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03116","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Fungal Rhinosinusitis","q":"A 22-year-old immunocompetent atopic man with allergic rhinitis and nasal polyps undergoes FESS, revealing thick, tenacious, greenish-brown \u0027peanut-butter-like\u0027 allergic mucin filling expanded sinuses without tissue invasion. Preoperative non-contrast CT sinuses showed heterogeneous \u0027double-density\u0027 hyperattenuating areas inside opacified sinuses, and histopathology of mucin shows eosinophils, Charcot-Leyden crystals, and non-invading fungal hyphae. What is the diagnosis and key postoperative therapy?","o":["Allergic Fungal Rhinosinusitis (AFRS); complete surgical debridement/ventilation (FESS) PLUS postoperative systemic and topical Corticosteroids","Antrochoanal polyp; no follow-up needed","Acute invasive mucormycosis; urgent orbital exenteration and IV liposomal amphotericin B","Nasopharyngeal angiofibroma; preoperative embolisation"],"a":0,"e":"AFRS (Bent-Kuhn criteria: Type I hypersensitivity, nasal polyposis, characteristic CT \u0027double-density\u0027 hyperattenuation from heavy-metal/calcium binding in thick allergic mucin, eosinophilic mucin with Charcot-Leyden crystals, and non-invasive fungal hyphae) is an immune-mediated non-invasive fungal sinusitis in immunocompetent atopic hosts. Management requires FESS to evacuate all allergic mucin and ventilate sinuses, followed by systemic/topical corticosteroids (and immunotherapy) to prevent recurrence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03117","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Benign Vocal Fold Lesions","q":"A 29-year-old primary school teacher presents with chronic hoarseness and vocal fatigue worsening after long classes. Videolaryngoscopy reveals bilateral, small (\u003c3 mm), symmetric, pearly-white smooth nodules located precisely at the junction of the anterior one-third and posterior two-thirds of the free edge of both true vocal cords. What is the first-line treatment?","o":["Urgent external-beam radiotherapy","Voice rest and structured Speech/Voice Therapy (microlaryngoscopic excision only if persistent mature fibrotic nodules fail conservative voice therapy)","6 weeks of systemic oral prednisolone + broad-spectrum antibiotics","Immediate bilateral total cordectomy"],"a":1,"e":"Vocal nodules (\u0027singer\u0027s / screamer\u0027s / teacher\u0027s nodules\u0027) result from phonotrauma (voice abuse) at the midpoint of the membranous vocal fold—precisely the junction of the anterior 1/3 and posterior 2/3 of the entire vocal cord, where maximal mechanical vibratory impact occurs. Early oedematous nodules resolve completely with voice rest and speech therapy modifying vocal hygiene; cold-steel microlaryngoscopic excision is reserved only for persistent mature fibrotic nodules.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03118","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Primary Malignant Bone Tumours","q":"A 15-year-old boy presents with 3 months of progressive, deep, aching pain and a warm firm swelling just above the right knee, with markedly elevated serum alkaline phosphatase. Plain radiograph of the distal femur shows an ill-defined osteoblastic/osteolytic METAPHYSEAL lesion breaching the cortex, with perpendicular \u0027sunburst\u0027 spiculated periosteal reaction and a triangular subperiosteal cuff of new bone (\u0027Codman\u0027s triangle\u0027). What is the diagnosis and standard treatment sequence?","o":["Osteoid osteoma; oral ibuprofen alone","Ewing sarcoma; radiotherapy alone without chemotherapy","Conventional Osteosarcoma; image-guided core biopsy followed by Neoadjuvant Chemotherapy (MAP: methotrexate, doxorubicin, cisplatin), limb-salvage wide resection (or amputation), and adjuvant chemotherapy","Giant cell tumour; simple curettage"],"a":2,"e":"Conventional osteosarcoma peaks in adolescents around rapid-growth metaphyses (distal femur \u003e proximal tibia \u003e proximal humerus), producing malignant osteoid matrix, \u0027sunburst\u0027 periosteal spicules, Codman\u0027s triangle, and high ALP. Pre-biopsy staging (MRI entire femur for skip lesions + CT chest for lung metastases) and carefully planned biopsy (along the future surgical incision) precede neoadjuvant MAP chemotherapy, wide en-bloc limb-salvage resection with endoprosthesis, and postoperative chemotherapy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03119","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Compressive Neuropathies","q":"A 46-year-old woman with hypothyroidism complains of nocturnal burning pain and tingling in her right thumb, index, middle, and radial half of her ring finger that awakens her at night and improves when she shakes her hand (\u0027flick sign\u0027). Examination shows positive Phalen\u0027s wrist-flexion test, positive Tinel\u0027s percussion sign over the volar wrist, and early wasting of the thenar eminence (abductor pollicis brevis weakness) with spared sensation over the central palm. Why is palm sensation spared, and what structure is divided during surgical decompression?","o":["Median nerve is compressed at the elbow; transpose the ulnar nerve","Radial nerve supplies the thenar eminence; fuse the radiocarpal joint","Ulnar nerve supplies the central palm via Guyon\u0027s canal; divide the pronator teres","Palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve travels superficial to the flexor retinaculum; surgical division of the Transverse Carpal Ligament (flexor retinaculum) decompresses the median nerve"],"a":3,"e":"In Carpal Tunnel Syndrome, compression of the Median nerve beneath the Transverse Carpal Ligament (flexor retinaculum) causes paraesthesias in the lateral 3.5 digits and thenar muscle wasting, while sparing central palm sensation because the palmar cutaneous branch arises ~5 cm proximal to the wrist and passes SUPERFICIAL to the flexor retinaculum. Early/mild cases respond to nocturnal neutral wrist splinting and treating hypothyroidism/diabetes; progressive thenar wasting or refractory pain mandates open or endoscopic release of the transverse carpal ligament.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03120","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Superficial Fungal Infections","q":"A 30-year-old man with extensive, pruritic, poorly demarcated erythematous plaques with pustules and striae across the groin, buttocks, and trunk (\u0027tinea corporis et cruris\u0027) admits applying an over-the-counter fixed-dose cream containing clobetasol + clotrimazole + gentamicin for 3 months. KOH skin scraping shows numerous branching septate hyphae. What is the essential management principle?","o":["Immediately STOP all topical corticosteroid creams, counsel against OTC steroid-antifungal combinations, and treat with systemic oral Terbinafine or Itraconazole PLUS a plain topical azole/allylamine for 4-6 weeks","Switch to a stronger topical steroid (halobetasol) for 2 weeks","Apply topical acyclovir cream","Treat with oral amoxicillin alone for pustules"],"a":0,"e":"Misuse of potent topical corticosteroid combination creams is a major epidemic in India, suppressing local cell-mediated immunity and transforming classic annular tinea into extensive, atypical, steroid-modified \u0027tinea incognito\u0027 (with striae, atrophy, telangiectasia, and deep follicular Majocchi granuloma). Immediate cessation of topical steroids plus adequate-duration systemic antifungal therapy (oral itraconazole or terbinafine, guided by Indian recalcitrant Trichophyton mentagrophytes patterns) + plain topical antifungal and loose cotton hygiene is essential.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03121","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Delirium vs Dementia","q":"Two days after hip arthroplasty, a 76-year-old man who was previously cognitively intact becomes acutely disoriented, easily distracted (unable to recite months backward), fluctuates between agitation at night (\u0027sundowning\u0027) and drowsiness by day, and sees nonexistent people in the room (Confusion Assessment Method positive). Which feature distinguishes this state (delirium) from dementia, and what is the primary therapeutic priority?","o":["Fixed non-fluctuating memory loss; administer high-dose IV diazepam routinely","Acute onset with fluctuating course and impaired attention/consciousness; actively identify and reverse underlying medical precipitants (infection, urinary retention, hypoxia, pain, electrolyte imbalance, anticholinergic/opioid drugs) before using low-dose antipsychotics only for severe dangerous agitation","Insidious progressive onset over years with preserved consciousness; start lifelong donepezil alone","Chronic schizophrenia; start clozapine immediately"],"a":1,"e":"Delirium is an acute, fluctuating organic brain syndrome defined by disturbed attention and awareness (unlike dementia, which is chronic, insidious, and preserves alertness/attention until late stages). Management centres on finding and correcting underlying physiological triggers (UTI/pneumonia, urinary retention, faecal impaction, hypoxaemia, hyponatraemia, anticholinergic/benzodiazepine medications), non-pharmacological reorientation/sleep hygiene, and reserving low-dose haloperidol/quetiapine strictly for severe safety-threatening agitation (avoiding benzodiazepines except in alcohol/sedative withdrawal).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03122","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Cardiac Tumours","q":"A 44-year-old woman presents with intermittent positional syncope when standing upright, dyspnoea, low-grade fever, weight loss, elevated ESR, and a low-pitched mid-diastolic murmur at the apex with an early diastolic \u0027tumour plop\u0027 that changes intensity with body posture. Transthoracic echocardiography demonstrates a mobile, gelatinous 4 cm mass attached by a pedicle to the fossa ovalis of the left atrial septum prolapsing into the mitral orifice during diastole. What is the diagnosis and definitive treatment?","o":["Cardiac lipoma; annual echocardiographic observation","Rheumatic mitral stenosis; lifelong oral penicillin prophylaxis alone","Left atrial myxoma; urgent surgical excision (resecting the pedicle attachment from the interatrial septum) to prevent systemic embolisation and sudden valve obstruction","Infective endocarditis vegetation; 6 weeks of IV vancomycin alone"],"a":2,"e":"Atrial myxoma is the commonest primary cardiac tumour (~75-80% in the left atrium attached to the fossa ovalis). It produces a classic triad: (1) positional mechanical obstruction (\u0027tumour plop\u0027 mimicking mitral stenosis, syncope on standing); (2) systemic embolisation (stroke/limb ischaemia); and (3) IL-6-mediated constitutional symptoms (fever, arthralgia, high ESR). Urgent surgical resection is curative and prevents catastrophic emboli or sudden cardiac death.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03123","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Bradyarrhythmias and Conduction Blocks","q":"A 71-year-old man presents after two unprovoked syncopal episodes (Stokes-Adams attacks). Pulse is 34/min regular, BP is 94/58 mmHg, JVP shows intermittent prominent \u0027cannon a-waves\u0027, and S1 intensity varies beat-to-beat. ECG shows atrial rate 88/min and regular wide-QRS escape rhythm at 34/min with complete atrioventricular (AV) dissociation (P waves marching independently through QRS complexes). After ruling out reversible causes (drugs/hyperkalaemia/ischaemia), what is the definitive treatment?","o":["Oral amiodarone loading","Synchronised DC cardioversion 200 J","Lifelong oral digoxin and beta-blocker","Temporary transcutaneous/transvenous pacing bridge (with IV atropine/isoproterenol/epinephrine support if unstable) followed by Permanent Pacemaker implantation"],"a":3,"e":"Complete (third-degree) AV block produces complete AV dissociation, wide-complex slow infranodal escape rhythm, variable S1 intensity, and cannon \u0027a\u0027 waves whenever the right atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve. Symptomatic third-degree AV block without reversible causes requires urgent temporary pacing stabilisation (noting atropine often fails in infranodal wide-QRS block, needing transcutaneous/transvenous pacing or dopamine/epinephrine infusion) followed by Permanent Pacemaker implantation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03124","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Drug-Resistant Tuberculosis (NTEP)","q":"A 33-year-old man with previously treated pulmonary tuberculosis presents with cough and weight loss. Sputum CBNAAT (GeneXpert) detects Mycobacterium tuberculosis WITH rpoB gene mutation (Rifampicin-resistant TB). Under NTEP Programmatic Management of Drug-Resistant TB (PMDT) and WHO guidelines, what rapid molecular drug-susceptibility test guides regimen selection, and what modern all-oral regimen replaces older 24-month injectable kanamycin regimens?","o":["Second-line Line Probe Assay (SL-LPA / Xpert XDR) for fluoroquinolones and second-line drugs; all-oral Bedaquiline-based regimen (e.g., BPaLM: Bedaquiline, Pretomanid, Linezolid, Moxifloxacin if FQ-susceptible, or shorter all-oral bedaquiline regimen)","Oral ciprofloxacin + erythromycin for 3 months","24 months of daily IM Kanamycin monotherapy","Repeat standard 2HRZE + 4HRE regardless of rpoB resistance"],"a":0,"e":"When CBNAAT detects Rifampicin-resistant (RR/MDR) TB, NTEP PMDT performs First-line and Second-line Line Probe Assays (FL-LPA / SL-LPA) and liquid culture DST (MGIT) to detect fluoroquinolone and second-line drug resistance upfront. Injectable aminoglycosides (kanamycin/capreomycin) have been phased out in favour of safer, much more effective all-oral Bedaquiline-containing regimens (including 6-month BPaLM/BPaL or 9-11 month shorter all-oral regimens, monitoring QTc and linezolid myelosuppression/neuropathy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03125","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Chronic Pancreatitis","q":"A 46-year-old man from Kerala with chronic heavy alcohol and tobacco use presents with recurrent boring epigastric pain radiating to the back, weight loss, floating greasy foul-smelling stools difficult to flush (steatorrhoea), and newly diagnosed diabetes mellitus. Plain abdominal radiograph and CT show coarse pancreatic parenchymal calcifications along the L1-L2 vertebrae and a beaded, dilated main pancreatic duct (\u0027chain of lakes\u0027). Beyond complete alcohol and smoking cessation, what replaces his lost exocrine function?","o":["Strict zero-calorie fasting for 3 months","Oral Pancreatic Enzyme Replacement Therapy (enteric-coated lipase/pancreatin taken WITH meals and snacks) plus fat-soluble vitamin supplementation","Immediate total gastrectomy","Lifelong oral cholestyramine alone"],"a":1,"e":"Chronic pancreatitis causes progressive parenchymal fibrosis, intraductal calculi/calcifications, and \u0027chain of lakes\u0027 ductal ectasia; steatorrhoea and pancreatogenic (Type 3c) diabetes emerge once \u003e90% of acinar/islet function is lost. Alongside strict alcohol/smoking cessation and analgesia, steatorrhoea and malabsorption are treated with enteric-coated Pancreatic Enzyme Replacement Therapy (PERT) administered DURING meals/snacks and fat-soluble vitamin (A, D, E, K) monitoring.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03126","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Posterior Pituitary Disorders","q":"A 31-year-old woman 6 months after transsphenoidal pituitary surgery presents with polyuria (6.5 L/day) and intense polydipsia. During a supervised water deprivation test, serum Na+ rises to 148 mEq/L and plasma osmolality reaches 302 mOsm/kg while urine remains maximally dilute (urine osmolality 135 mOsm/kg). Following subcutaneous Desmopressin (DDAVP 2 mcg), urine osmolality surges by \u003e100% to 520 mOsm/kg. What is the diagnosis and treatment?","o":["Complete Nephrogenic Diabetes Insipidus (which shows \u003c 10% rise in Uosm after DDAVP); oral thiazide + amiloride","Primary polydipsia (where urine concentrates \u003e 600 mOsm/kg during dehydration alone); fluid restriction","Complete Central Diabetes Insipidus (AVP deficiency); oral/intranasal Desmopressin (DDAVP)","SIADH; 3% hypertonic saline"],"a":2,"e":"Failure to concentrate urine (Uosm \u003c 300 mOsm/kg) despite hyperosmolar dehydration (Posm \u003e 295-300 mOsm/kg) rules out primary polydipsia and confirms Diabetes Insipidus. A \u003e 50% rise in urine osmolality after exogenous Desmopressin (DDAVP) proves renal collecting-duct V2 receptors are intact and diagnoses Central (arginine vasopressin-deficient) Diabetes Insipidus, treated with oral/nasal Desmopressin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03127","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Tubulointerstitial Nephritis","q":"Ten days after starting oral amoxicillin-clavulanate and pantoprazole for sinusitis, a 39-year-old man develops low-grade fever, a pruritic morbilliform rash, arthralgias, peripheral eosinophilia, and serum creatinine rising from 0.9 to 2.7 mg/dL. Urinalysis reveals sterile pyuria, white blood cell (WBC) casts, mild proteinuria (\u003c1 g/day), and eosinophiluria. What is the diagnosis and primary first step?","o":["Drug-induced Acute Interstitial Nephritis (AIN); immediately discontinue both offending drugs (amoxicillin-clavulanate and pantoprazole) and consider short-course corticosteroids if renal recovery stalls","Acute tubular necrosis from contrast; start high-dose furosemide","Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis; continue amoxicillin for 4 more weeks","Ureteric stone obstruction; immediate nephrectomy"],"a":0,"e":"Allergic/drug-induced AIN (classically triggered by beta-lactams, PPIs, NSAIDs, sulfa/rifampicin, allopurinol) presents 1-3 weeks after drug exposure with AKI, sterile pyuria, WBC casts, and sometimes the classic hypersensitivity triad (fever, rash, eosinophilia/eosinophiluria—seen in \u003c30% overall). Immediate cessation of the culprit drug(s) is the cornerstone of therapy; early prednisolone accelerates recovery if creatinine fails to fall within days or biopsy shows heavy interstitial infiltrate.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03128","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"CSF Dynamics and Dementias","q":"A 70-year-old man presents with 8 months of progressive shuffling broad-based \u0027magnetic\u0027 gait (\u0027feet glued to the floor\u0027), urinary urgency/incontinence, and mild psychomotor slowing (Hakim-Adams triad). Brain MRI shows communicating ventriculomegaly (Evans index \u003e 0.30) with disproportionate Sylvian fissure enlargement and minimal cortical atrophy. Lumbar puncture shows normal opening pressure (14 cmH2O), and his gait improves markedly 2 hours after draining 40 mL of CSF. What is the diagnosis and definitive treatment?","o":["Subdural haematoma; burr-hole evacuation","Alzheimer disease; oral donepezil alone","Parkinson\u0027s disease; lifelong bromocriptine alone","Idiopathic Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus (NPH); ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt placement"],"a":3,"e":"Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus is a classic treatable cause of reversible dementia presenting with the triad of gait apraxia (often earliest and most responsive feature), urinary incontinence, and subcortical cognitive slowing (\u0027wet, wobbly, wacky\u0027), communicating ventriculomegaly out of proportion to sulcal atrophy, and normal CSF pressure. Improvement on timed gait testing following a large-volume (30-50 mL) CSF tap test predicts excellent response to Ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt diversion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03129","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Inherited Haemolytic Anaemias","q":"Two days after starting primaquine (or cotrimoxazole/dapsone) for vivax malaria radical cure, a 22-year-old man develops acute jaundice, dark red-brown urine (haemoglobinuria), back pain, Hb dropping from 14.0 to 7.8 g/dL, elevated reticulocytes, and peripheral blood smear showing \u0027bite cells\u0027 and supravital-stained Heinz bodies. A quantitative G6PD enzyme assay sent TODAY during the acute crisis returns \u0027borderline normal\u0027. Why can G6PD assay be falsely normal during acute haemolysis, and what is the management?","o":["Oxidative crisis selectively destroys older G6PD-deficient RBCs leaving young reticulocytes with higher G6PD levels; stop primaquine immediately, hydrate, transfuse if severe, and REPEAT G6PD assay 2-3 months after recovery","Perform emergency splenectomy","Borderline normal test today rules out G6PD deficiency permanently; continue primaquine at double dose","Give IV methylene blue immediately"],"a":0,"e":"X-linked G6PD deficiency impairs NADPH/glutathione regeneration, causing haemoglobin precipitation (Heinz bodies) and splenic pitting (\u0027bite cells\u0027) upon oxidative stress (primaquine, dapsone, rasburicase, fava beans, infections). Because acute haemolysis destroys older enzyme-depleted RBCs and triggers a surge of young reticulocytes (which contain much higher transient G6PD activity), testing during an acute episode often yields false-normal results—requiring repeat testing 2-3 months post-crisis. Immediate drug withdrawal and hydration prevent AKI from haemoglobinuria.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03130","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Congenital Lateral Neck Swellings","q":"A 22-year-old man presents with a painless, smooth, fluctuant, non-transilluminant lateral neck swelling present for 1 year along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle at the junction of its upper one-third and lower two-thirds (does NOT move on swallowing or tongue protrusion). Aspiration yields turbid fluid containing glistening cholesterol crystals. What is the diagnosis and standard treatment?","o":["Thyroglossal duct cyst; Sistrunk operation","Second branchial cleft cyst; complete surgical excision (tracing any tract passing between the internal and external carotid arteries to the tonsillar fossa)","Cystic hygroma; observation alone","Carotid body tumour; radiotherapy alone"],"a":1,"e":"Second branchial cleft cysts account for \u003e90% of branchial anomalies, classically presenting in young adults as a fluctuant swelling beneath the anterior border of SCM at the junction of upper 1/3 and lower 2/3, containing cholesterol crystals. Unlike thyroglossal cysts (midline, moving with tongue protrusion) or cystic hygroma (brilliantly transilluminant posterior triangle mass in infants), branchial cysts are non-transilluminant and cured by complete excision (noting a second-arch tract courses between the internal and external carotid arteries to the palatine tonsillar fossa).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03131","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastric Outlet Obstruction (GOO)","q":"A 56-year-old man presents with 6 weeks of voluminous, foul-smelling, NON-bilious vomiting containing undigested food eaten 12-24 hours earlier, weight loss, visible left-to-right epigastric peristalsis, and an audible succussion splash 5 hours after his last meal. Labs show hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with paradoxical aciduria. After NG decompression and IV 0.9% NaCl + KCl resuscitation, what diagnostic step is mandatory before planning definitive surgery/dilation?","o":["Immediate gastrojejunostomy without endoscopy","Immediate Ramstedt pyloromyotomy","Upper GI endoscopy with multiple biopsies from the pyloric/antral stricture to rule out gastric adenocarcinoma before assuming benign peptic ulcer scarring","Oral barium swallow alone followed by lifelong antacids"],"a":2,"e":"In adults \u003e40-50 years presenting with Gastric Outlet Obstruction (non-bilious stale food vomiting, succussion splash \u003e3-4 h post-meal, hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis), malignant antral/pancreatic obstruction accounts for \u003e50-70% of cases in modern practice (exceeding benign chronic duodenal ulcer stricture). After correcting dehydration and metabolic alkalosis with 0.9% saline + KCl and NG washouts, upper GI endoscopy + tissue biopsy MUST precede definitive therapy (D2 radical gastrectomy if malignant vs endoscopic balloon dilation/H. pylori eradication or vagotomy-gastrojejunostomy if benign).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03132","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Complete Rectal Prolapse (Procidentia)","q":"A 68-year-old multiparous woman complains of a full-thickness rectal mass protruding 7 cm through the anus during straining, accompanied by mucus discharge and faecal incontinence. Examination shows CONCENTRIC circular mucosal folds with a deep sulcus between the protruding cylinder and the anal verge. How does this differ from third-degree prolapsed internal haemorrhoids, and what is the preferred definitive repair in a surgically fit adult?","o":["Prolapsed internal haemorrhoids (which show concentric folds); Milligan-Morgan haemorrhoidectomy","Anal fissure; lateral internal sphincterotomy","Perianal condyloma acuminata; topical podophyllin","Complete rectal prolapse (concentric circular folds vs radial mucosal grooves in haemorrhoids); abdominal/laparoscopic rectopexy (e.g., ventral mesh rectopexy) in fit patients—reserving perineal Delorme/Altemeier procedures for frail elderly"],"a":3,"e":"Full-thickness rectal procidentia exhibits concentric circular mucosal rings protruding through a patulous sphincter (whereas prolapsed internal haemorrhoids display 3 primary cushions at 3, 7, and 11 o\u0027clock separated by radial longitudinal grooves). Fit adults achieve the lowest recurrence rates with abdominal/laparoscopic rectopexy (mobilisation and fixation to the sacral promontory, +/- sigmoid resection if severely constipated), whereas high-risk frail elderly undergo perineal Delorme mucosal sleeve resection or Altemeier perineal rectosigmoidectomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03133","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer (NMIBC)","q":"A 62-year-old textile-dye worker and smoker presents with painless total gross haematuria. Cystoscopy and complete Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumour (TURBT) reveal a high-grade urothelial carcinoma invading the lamina propria (pT1) with intact muscularis propria in the specimen and focal Carcinoma in Situ (CIS). What adjuvant intravesical therapy significantly reduces recurrence and progression to muscle-invasive disease?","o":["Intravesical Bacillus Calmette-Guerin (BCG) immunotherapy (6-week induction + maintenance)","External-beam radiotherapy alone without TURBT","Immediate radical cystectomy without considering BCG in all T1/CIS cases","Oral rifampicin + isoniazid for 6 months"],"a":0,"e":"High-grade pT1 and Carcinoma in Situ (CIS) are high-risk Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancers (NMIBC). After complete TURBT (plus re-TURBT at 2-6 weeks for T1 to confirm muscle isn\u0027t invaded), intravesical BCG (live attenuated Mycobacterium bovis eliciting local Th1 granulomatous anti-tumour immunity) induction and maintenance is the gold standard to prevent recurrence and muscle invasion (contraindicated if gross haematuria, traumatic catheterisation, or active UTI due to systemic BCG-osis risk).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03134","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Vasospastic Disorders","q":"A 24-year-old woman complains of symmetric episodic digital colour changes (blanching white -\u003e cyanotic blue -\u003e reactive hyperaemic red) triggered by cold water, without digital ulcers, sclerodactyly, or telangiectasia; nailfold capillaroscopy and ANA are normal. Which drug class is first-line pharmacotherapy if lifestyle warming fails, and which antihypertensive class worsens Raynaud\u0027s attacks?","o":["Immediate bilateral sympathectomy in all young females","Dihydropyridine Calcium Channel Blockers (e.g., amlodipine or extended-release nifedipine) are first-line; non-selective Beta-blockers worsen digital vasospasm","Propranolol is first-line; nifedipine worsens vasospasm","Oral ergotamine is first-line"],"a":1,"e":"Primary Raynaud\u0027s disease (benign symmetrical vasospasm without tissue necrosis, normal nailfold capillaries, negative ANA) responds to cold avoidance, smoking cessation, and dihydropyridine CCBs (nifedipine/amlodipine; adding PDE-5 inhibitors/IV prostacyclin for severe secondary Raynaud\u0027s digital ulcers in systemic sclerosis). Beta-blockers (especially non-selective), ergot alkaloids, sympathomimetics, and bleomycin/cisplatin exacerbate digital ischaemia and must be avoided.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03135","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Biophysical Profile (BPP)","q":"A 33-year-old woman with gestational hypertension at 36+5 weeks undergoes an antepartum Biophysical Profile (BPP). Ultrasound over 30 minutes reveals normal fetal tone (2 pts), normal gross body movements (2 pts), present fetal breathing movements (2 pts), and reactive NST (2 pts), yet single deepest amniotic fluid pocket measures 1.4 cm (oligohydramnios: 0 pts) — total BPP score = 8/10 with oligohydramnios. Which five variables comprise a complete BPP, and what does isolated oligohydramnios on BPP indicate?","o":["Fetal heart rate variability, umbilical Doppler, maternal BP, cervical length, and placenta grade; score 8/10 means repeat in 1 month","Only NST and AFI; score 8/10 always requires emergency hysterectomy","Fetal breathing, gross body movements, fetal tone, amniotic fluid volume, and Non-Stress Test (NST); score 8/10 with oligohydramnios indicates chronic uteroplacental insufficiency warranting delivery/close evaluation at 36+5 weeks (whereas 0-4/10 mandates immediate delivery)","Fetal weight, biparietal diameter, femur length, NST, and kick count; oligohydramnios is irrelevant"],"a":2,"e":"Manning\u0027s Biophysical Profile scores 5 indicators (2 points each, max 10): 4 acute CNS hypoxia-sensitive parameters (NST reactivity, fetal breathing, fetal movements, fetal tone—lost in reverse order of embryological development!) plus 1 chronic marker (Amniotic Fluid Volume: \u003e= 2 cm vertical pocket). Even with a total score of 8/10, abnormal amniotic fluid (0 pts for oligohydramnios) signals chronic placental compromise and warrants prompt delivery near/at term (~36-37 weeks).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03136","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Instrumental Vaginal Delivery","q":"A 24-year-old primigravida at 31+4 weeks of gestation in preterm second-stage labour has maternal exhaustion (fully dilated cervix, vertex at +2 station, ruptured membranes, OA position). Which instrumental delivery rule applies to Vacuum Extractor (Ventouse) versus Outlet Forceps in preterm deliveries (\u003c 34 weeks)?","o":["Face presentation (mento-anterior) is the ideal indication for vacuum cup placement","Vacuum extraction is strictly safer than forceps at 30-32 weeks","Vacuum extraction can be applied safely at 5 cm cervical dilatation and -3 unengaged station","Vacuum extraction is CONTRAINDICATED below 34 weeks\u0027 gestation (due to high risk of neonatal subgaleal haemorrhage, cephalhaematoma, and intraventricular haemorrhage); gentle outlet forceps may be used if indicated"],"a":3,"e":"Preterm fetuses (\u003c 34 weeks, and especially \u003c 32 weeks) have fragile cranial sutures, soft subgaleal vessels, and germinal matrix capillaries highly susceptible to vacuum-induced traction, making Ventouse contraindicated below 34 weeks (also contraindicated in face/breech presentations, fetal bleeding disorders, and unengaged head/incomplete dilatation). Forceps protect the preterm cranium in a rigid cradle if second-stage assisted delivery is required.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03137","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Lactational Amenorrhoea Method (LAM)","q":"A 25-year-old woman 4 months postpartum wishes to rely on the Lactational Amenorrhoea Method (LAM) for contraception. She feeds her infant exclusively on breast milk during the day, gives one bottle of infant formula every night, and has resumed menstrual spotting 2 weeks ago. Can she rely on LAM (~98% efficacy)?","o":["No; LAM requires ALL THREE criteria simultaneously—infant \u003c 6 months, COMPLETE amenorrhoea, AND exclusive/near-exclusive breastfeeding day and night without regular formula/supplements","No; LAM is ineffective during the first 6 weeks postpartum only","Yes; LAM remains 99% effective for 24 months regardless of menses or top-feeds","Yes; only the \u003c 6 months criterion matters"],"a":0,"e":"Lactational Amenorrhoea Method (LAM) achieves ~98% contraceptive protection ONLY when ALL three conditions are strictly fulfilled: (1) postpartum period \u003c 6 months; (2) complete absence of menstrual bleeding after day 56 postpartum; and (3) exclusive/full breastfeeding on demand day and night (suckling intervals \u003c= 4 h daytime, \u003c= 6 h nighttime, no formula/solid supplements). Once menses return OR supplemental formula is introduced, ovulation can precede next menses and an alternative method (PPIUCD, POP, barrier, DMPA) must begin immediately.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03138","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Vaginal Discharge Syndromes","q":"A 27-year-old woman presents with a thin, homogeneous, greyish-white vaginal discharge with a strong fishy odour worsened after intercourse. Speculum exam shows minimal vaginal mucosal inflammation, vaginal pH is 5.2, adding 10% KOH produces an intense fishy amine odour (positive \u0027whiff\u0027 test), and saline wet mount shows \u003e20% squamous epithelial cells coated with coccobacilli obscuring cell borders (\u0027clue cells\u0027). What is the diagnosis and first-line treatment?","o":["Atrophic vaginitis; topical oestrogen cream","Bacterial Vaginosis (Gardnerella vaginalis / anaerobes dysbiosis); oral or intravaginal Metronidazole (500 mg BID x 7 days) or Clindamycin","Trichomoniasis; mandatory treatment of all sexual partners","Vulvovaginal candidiasis; single-dose oral fluconazole 150 mg"],"a":1,"e":"Meeting \u003e= 3 of 4 Amsel criteria—(1) thin homogeneous grey-white adherent discharge; (2) vaginal pH \u003e 4.5; (3) positive KOH \u0027whiff\u0027 amine test; and (4) \u003e20% \u0027clue cells\u0027 on microscopy (or Nugent score \u003e= 7 on Gram stain)—confirms Bacterial Vaginosis (loss of H2O2-producing Lactobacillus replaced by Gardnerella/Mobiluncus/anaerobes). Treatment is oral/vaginal Metronidazole or Clindamycin (note: routine partner treatment does NOT reduce BV recurrence).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03139","course":"MBBS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Uterine Leiomyoma (Fibroids)","q":"A 31-year-old woman desiring future pregnancy presents with severe heavy menstrual bleeding (Hb 7.9 g/dL) and two early first-trimester miscarriages. Saline infusion sonohysterography and hysteroscopy show a solitary 3.0 cm intracavitary pedunculated SUBMUCOSAL leiomyoma (FIGO Type 0) distorting the endometrial cavity, with normal adnexa and no other fibroids. What is the definitive fertility-preserving surgical treatment?","o":["Total abdominal hysterectomy","Bilateral uterine artery embolisation as routine first-line in women desiring pregnancy","Hysteroscopic transcervical resection of the fibroid (hysteroscopic myomectomy)","Laparotomy and classical vertical uterine incision for a completely intracavitary Type 0 fibroid"],"a":2,"e":"Submucosal leiomyomas (FIGO Type 0 entirely intracavitary, Type 1 \u003c50% intramural) cause the heaviest menstrual bleeding and impair implantation/increase miscarriage. Hysteroscopic myomectomy (resectoscope morcellation/loop excision) completely removes Type 0/1 fibroids \u003c= 4-5 cm via the natural transcervical route without creating a transmural abdominal uterine scar—preserving fertility and avoiding mandatory future Caesarean section.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03140","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"IgA Vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein Purpura)","q":"Ten days after an upper respiratory infection, a 6-year-old boy develops symmetric non-blanching palpable purpuric macules and papules over his buttocks and extensor lower legs, painful swollen knees/ankles, colicky abdominal pain, and normal platelet count (310,000/mm3) and coagulation profile. What is the diagnosis, acute surgical complication to watch for, and mandatory follow-up?","o":["Acute rheumatic fever; lifelong benzathine penicillin","Immune thrombocytopenic purpura; immediate IVIG","Meningococcaemia; treat with oral prednisolone alone","Henoch-Schonlein Purpura (IgA vasculitis); monitor for ileoileal intussusception and perform serial urinalysis and blood pressure checks for 6 months to detect delayed HSP nephritis"],"a":3,"e":"HSP (IgA vasculitis) is the commonest childhood systemic small-vessel vasculitis, presenting with palpable purpura (buttocks/lower extremities) WITHOUT thrombocytopenia, arthritis/arthralgia, colicky abdominal pain (mucosal oedema/haemorrhage acting as lead point for ileoileal intussusception), and IgA glomerulonephritis. Uncomplicated cases need supportive analgesia/hydration (short oral prednisolone for severe abdominal pain/GI bleeding); because renal involvement may appear weeks to months later, serial urine dipstick + BP monitoring for 6 months is essential.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03141","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Paroxysmal Non-Epileptic Events","q":"A 16-month-old boy has repeated episodes always triggered immediately after being scolded or crying vigorously: he cries hard, holds his breath in forced expiration, turns deeply cyanotic around the lips, goes limp for 15 seconds with a brief upward eye roll, and wakes up alert within 30 seconds without postictal drowsiness. Interictal EEG and cardiac exam/ECG (normal QTc) are normal, while Hb is 9.2 g/dL (microcytic hypochromic). What is the diagnosis and effective therapy?","o":["Cyanotic breath-holding spells; parental reassurance (benign self-limiting by age 5-6 years) PLUS oral Iron supplementation for coexisting iron-deficiency anaemia (avoiding antiepileptic drugs)","Generalised tonic-clonic epilepsy; start lifelong oral sodium valproate","Long QT syndrome; immediate ICD","Tetralogy of Fallot hypercyanotic spell; emergency BT shunt"],"a":0,"e":"Breath-holding spells (cyanotic triggered by anger/frustration; pallid triggered by sudden fright/pain causing vagal bradycardia) occur in toddlers 6 months to 5 years, always preceded by crying/provocation BEFORE cyanosis/syncope (unlike epilepsy or TOF spells, and with normal ECG/QTc). They are benign and self-limiting without neurological sequelae; correcting coexisting iron-deficiency anaemia with oral iron significantly reduces spell frequency, and AEDs are unnecessary.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03142","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Neonatal Tetanus","q":"A 7-day-old term newborn delivered at home with an unsterile blade used to cut the umbilical cord (and cow-dung ash applied to the stump) to an unimmunised mother presents with sudden inability to suck/feed (trismus), tight facial grimace (risus sardonicus), and painful generalised opisthotonic spasms triggered by noise and touch. Beyond placing the infant in a dark, quiet NICU with airway/ventilation support and IV Diazepam for spasms, what specific toxin-neutralising and antimicrobial therapy is required?","o":["Wait 10 days for CSF bacterial culture before giving HTIG","Human Tetanus Immunoglobulin (HTIG 500 IU IM) to neutralise unbound tetanospasmin + IV Metronidazole (preferred over penicillin) + umbilical wound debridement","IV Ceftriaxone alone without HTIG","Oral DPT vaccine immediately as cure without immunoglobulin"],"a":1,"e":"Neonatal tetanus (Clostridium tetani, typically Days 3-14 post-unhygienic cord care in unimmunised mothers) blocks inhibitory GABA/glycine interneurons via tetanospasmin. Immediate IM HTIG neutralises circulating unbound toxin (before irreversible axonal binding), IV Metronidazole eradicates vegetative bacilli (preferred over Penicillin G, which is a GABA antagonist that can worsen spasms), benzodiazepines/MgSO4 control spasms, and active Td vaccination + clean delivery (\u00275 cleans\u0027) prevent it.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03143","course":"MBBS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Nutritional Iron Deficiency Anaemia","q":"A 2-year-old toddler weighing 12 kg who drinks 1.2 litres of cow\u0027s milk daily with minimal solid food presents with pallor, irritability, Hb 7.4 g/dL, MCV 61 fL, RDW 19.5%, and serum ferritin 6 ng/mL (Iron Deficiency Anaemia). Prescribing standard therapeutic oral elemental iron at 3 mg/kg/day, what daily dose of ELEMENTAL iron should be given, and how long must therapy continue AFTER haemoglobin normalises?","o":["120 mg elemental iron per day; give for 7 days total","3 mg elemental iron total per day; transfuse packed RBCs immediately","36 mg elemental iron per day; continue for an additional 2 to 3 months AFTER Hb normalises to replenish marrow/hepatic iron stores (observing reticulocytosis by day 5-7)","36 mg elemental iron per day; stop immediately on the day Hb reaches 11.0 g/dL"],"a":2,"e":"Standard therapeutic paediatric oral iron dose is 3-6 mg/kg/day of elemental iron (here 3 mg x 12 kg = 36 mg/day, given between meals with Vitamin C and reducing excessive cow\u0027s milk \u003c500 mL/day). Reticulocytes peak at days 5-10 and Hb rises ~1 g/dL every 2-4 weeks; stopping iron as soon as Hb normalises leaves ferritin stores depleted and causes rapid relapse—so therapy MUST continue 2-3 months beyond Hb normalisation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03144","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Measures of Disease Frequency","q":"In a stable population, a chronic non-fatal condition has a constant annual incidence rate (I) of 4 cases per 1,000 population per year. Following introduction of a life-prolonging medication that does not cure the disease, mean disease duration (D) doubles from 5 years to 10 years. Assuming steady state (P = I x D), how does the Prevalence (P) change?","o":["Prevalence drops to zero","Prevalence halves from 20 per 1,000 to 10 per 1,000","Prevalence remains unchanged at 4 per 1,000","Prevalence doubles from 20 per 1,000 to 40 per 1,000"],"a":3,"e":"In a stable population with low prevalence, Prevalence approximates Incidence x Mean Duration (P = I x D). Initially, P = 4 per 1,000/yr x 5 yrs = 20 per 1,000. When a therapy prolongs survival without curing the disease (doubling D to 10 yrs while I stays 4/1,000/yr), P = 4 x 10 = 40 per 1,000—a classic epidemiological paradox where improved treatment increases disease prevalence!","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03145","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Natural History of Disease","q":"According to the \u0027Iceberg Phenomenon\u0027 of disease in community medicine, what does the large submerged portion below the waterline represent, and which infectious disease is a classic EXCEPTION showing virtually no submerged subclinical iceberg?","o":["Subclinical, asymptomatic, undiagnosed, and carrier cases in the community; Rabies (and Tetanus/Measles) lacks a significant subclinical iceberg","Recovered cases only; Hepatitis B is an exception","Immunised individuals; Diabetes mellitus is an exception","Hospitalised ICU deaths; Hypertension is an exception"],"a":0,"e":"For most chronic (hypertension, diabetes, malnutrition) and infectious (polio, typhoid, COVID-19) conditions, physicians only see the floating \u0027tip\u0027 of clinically apparent cases while a vast submerged portion of asymptomatic, pre-symptomatic, mild, or carrier cases persists undetected in the community (targeted by screening). Diseases where nearly 100% of infections produce overt/fatal clinical disease—classically Rabies, Tetanus, and Measles—do NOT exhibit the iceberg phenomenon.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03146","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK)","q":"Under India\u0027s Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK), Mobile Health Teams and District Early Intervention Centres (DEICs) screen children from birth to 18 years for early identification and management of \u00274 Ds\u0027. Which combination accurately lists the 4 Ds of RBSK?","o":["Dehydration, Dementia, Depression, and Deafness only","Defects at birth, Deficiencies, Diseases, and Developmental delays including disabilities","Drug resistance, Dialysis, Disinfection, and Demography","Diabetes, Dengue, Diarrhoea, and Diphtheria"],"a":1,"e":"RBSK (under NHM India) covers 0-18 years (deliveries at public health facilities, Anganwadis, and government/aided schools) across 30+ selected health conditions grouped into the 4 Ds: (1) Defects at birth (NTD, cleft lip/palate, clubfoot, CHD, congenital cataract/deafness/hip dysplasia); (2) Deficiencies (SAM, anaemia, vitamin A/D, goitre); (3) Diseases (scabies, otitis media, rheumatic heart disease, reactive airway); and (4) Developmental delays including disabilities (vision, hearing, neuromotor, speech, autism, ADHD).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03147","course":"MBBS","subject":"Community Medicine","topic":"Organic Dust Lung Diseases","q":"A 39-year-old textile spinning-mill worker in Ahmedabad with 12 years of cotton-dust exposure complains of chronic cough and chest tightness that is reproducibly WORST on Monday mornings when returning to work after the weekend off (\u0027Monday morning fever\u0027), improving gradually through the week. Which occupational lung disease is this, and how does it differ from Bagassosis?","o":["Silicosis; whereas Byssinosis shows eggshell hilar calcification","Anthracosis; whereas Bagassosis is caused by coal dust","Byssinosis (cotton fibre dust); whereas Bagassosis is hypersensitivity pneumonitis caused by mouldy sugarcane residue (bagasse) containing Thermoactinomyces sacchari","Bagassosis (cotton dust); whereas Byssinosis is caused by asbestos"],"a":2,"e":"Inhalation of cotton, flax, or hemp dust in textile mills causes Byssinosis, classically producing \u0027Monday fever / Monday morning tightness\u0027 (endotoxin-mediated bronchospasm peaking on the first workday after weekend absence) before progressing to chronic airway obstruction. In contrast, Bagassosis is an extrinsic allergic alveolitis (hypersensitivity pneumonitis) seen in sugarcane-paper/cardboard workers inhaling Thermoactinomyces sacchari spores from damp bagasse (prevented by spraying with 2% propionic acid).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03148","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Primary Congenital Glaucoma (Buphthalmos)","q":"A 5-month-old infant is brought with excessive watery tearing (WITHOUT mucopurulent discharge), severe photophobia (hiding face in mother\u0027s shoulder), and blepharospasm. Examination under anaesthesia shows bilaterally enlarged hazy corneas (horizontal diameter 13.5 mm, \u0027buphthalmos\u0027), deep anterior chambers, elevated IOP (32 mmHg), and horizontal curvilinear breaks in Descemet\u0027s membrane (\u0027Haab\u0027s striae\u0027). What is the definitive treatment?","o":["Lifelong topical pilocarpine monotherapy without surgery","Topical steroid drops for presumed viral conjunctivitis","Crigler lacrimal sac massage alone for 12 months","Urgent surgical angle surgery (Goniotomy if cornea clear, or Trabeculotomy / combined Trabeculotomy-Trabeculectomy if cornea hazy)"],"a":3,"e":"Primary Congenital Glaucoma (trabeculodysgenesis / Barkan\u0027s membrane) stretches the pliable infant sclera and cornea \u003c3 years of age, presenting with the classic triad of epiphora (clear tears without sac regurgitation, distinguishing it from CNLDO!), photophobia, and blepharospasm, plus megalocornea (\u003e12 mm), corneal oedema, and horizontal Haab\u0027s striae. Medical drops serve only as a brief preoperative bridge; definitive treatment is ALWAYS surgical angle opening (goniotomy if clear view, trabeculotomy/trabeculectomy if cloudy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03149","course":"MBBS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Late Postoperative Cataract Complications","q":"A 64-year-old woman who regained 6/6 vision after uneventful phacoemulsification with posterior chamber IOL implantation 2 years ago returns with gradual painless return of blurry vision and glare (6/24). Dilated slit-lamp examination shows proliferating lens epithelial cells forming translucent \u0027Elschnig\u0027s pearls\u0027 and fibrous plaques across the intact posterior lens capsule behind the IOL, with a normal retina and macula. What is the definitive outpatient treatment?","o":["Nd:YAG laser posterior capsulotomy","Intravitreal anti-VEGF injection","Topical prednisolone drops for 6 months","Repeat phacoemulsification with IOL explantation"],"a":0,"e":"Posterior Capsular Opacification (PCO, \u0027after-cataract\u0027) is the commonest late complication of extracapsular cataract extraction/phacoemulsification, caused by migration and proliferation of residual anterior lens epithelial cells onto the posterior capsule (forming Elschnig pearls or Soemmering ring). A rapid, painless outpatient Nd:YAG laser posterior capsulotomy (photodisruption at 1064 nm) creates a clear central visual opening restoring 6/6 vision immediately.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03150","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Complications of Otitis Media","q":"A 6-year-old boy treated inadequately for acute otitis media 12 days ago returns with recurrence of high fever, throbbing earache, profuse creamy otorrhoea (\u0027reservoir sign\u0027), tenderness over Macewen\u0027s triangle, obliteration of the retroauricular crease with a fluctuant postauricular swelling pushing the pinna FORWARD and DOWNWARD, and sagging of the posterosuperior external auditory canal wall. What is the diagnosis and urgent management?","o":["Safe mucosal CSOM; schedule elective tympanoplasty in 6 months","Acute coalescent mastoiditis with subperiosteal abscess; hospitalise for IV broad-spectrum antibiotics (e.g., ceftriaxone), wide myringotomy, and cortical mastoidectomy (Schwartze operation) / abscess drainage","Otitis externa furuncle; oral antihistamines and ear drops alone","Meniere\u0027s disease; low-salt diet"],"a":1,"e":"Recurrent pain/fever \u003e2 weeks post-AOM, \u0027reservoir sign\u0027 (pus rapidly refilling EAC after mopping), posterosuperior EAC wall sagging (periosteitis of mastoid antrum), obliteration of the postauricular groove (unlike simple furunculosis/lymphadenitis), and downward/forward displacement of the pinna confirm acute coalescent mastoiditis with subperiosteal abscess. Urgent IV antibiotics + cortical mastoidectomy (exenterating coalescent mastoid air cells) prevent intracranial complications (sigmoid sinus thrombosis, meningitis, brain abscess).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03151","course":"MBBS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Obstructive Sleep Apnoea (OSA)","q":"A 49-year-old obese man (BMI 34 kg/m2, collar size 43 cm, Mallampati Class IV) with resistant hypertension presents with loud habitual snoring, witnessed breathing pauses during sleep, morning headaches, and excessive daytime sleepiness (Epworth Sleepiness Scale 16/24). Overnight attended polysomnography demonstrates an Apnoea-Hypopnoea Index (AHI) of 34 events/hour with nocturnal desaturations to 76%. What is the gold-standard first-line treatment?","o":["Nighttime benzodiazepines to deepen sleep","Total laryngectomy","Nasal Continuous Positive Airway Pressure (CPAP) during sleep alongside weight reduction and alcohol/sedative avoidance","Supplemental oxygen alone without positive airway pressure"],"a":2,"e":"OSA (AHI \u003e= 15/h, or \u003e= 5/h with daytime symptoms/hypertension) results from repetitive upper airway pharyngeal collapse during sleep, driving sympathetic surges, resistant hypertension, pulmonary hypertension, and cardiovascular morbidity. Nocturnal CPAP acts as a pneumatic splint keeping the pharynx patent throughout inspiration/expiration, eliminating apnoeas, restoring sleep architecture, and lowering BP (while weight loss helps long-term, and UPPP/mandibular advancement devices are alternatives in CPAP-intolerant/select patients).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03152","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (CTEV / Clubfoot)","q":"A newborn presents with bilateral rigid Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (clubfoot) exhibiting the four classic deformities remembered by the mnemonic CAVE (midfoot Cavus, forefoot Adductus, hindfoot Varus, and ankle Equinus). Under the gold-standard Ponseti serial manipulation and casting protocol, which deformity MUST be corrected FIRST, and which deformity is corrected LAST (often via percutaneous Achilles tenotomy)?","o":["Immediate extensive Turco posteromedial release surgery at 1 week of age","Do not start casting until age 3 years","Correct equinus first by forcefully dorsiflexing the foot before correcting cavus/varus (which creates a \u0027rocker-bottom\u0027 deformity)","Correct midfoot CAVUS first (by supinating the first metatarsal), followed by adductus and varus simultaneously via abduction around the talar head, and correct hindfoot EQUINUS LAST (percutaneous tendo-Achilles tenotomy)"],"a":3,"e":"Ponseti serial casting started within the first weeks of life cures \u003e90-95% of idiopathic clubfeet without major open surgery. Deformities are corrected strictly in the C-A-V-E sequence: (1) Cavus first (elevating the 1st metatarsal/supinating forefoot to align with hindfoot); (2) Forefoot Adductus and hindfoot Varus together by abducting the foot beneath the talus using counter-pressure on the lateral talar head; and (3) Equinus LAST via percutaneous Achilles tenotomy in ~85% of cases, followed by a foot abduction brace (Steenbeek/Denis Browne) to prevent relapse.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03153","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Proximal Femoral Fractures","q":"A 74-year-old woman slips in the bathroom and cannot bear weight on her right hip. Her right lower limb is shortened and moderately externally rotated (~30-45 degrees). Pelvic radiograph shows a completely displaced subcapital INTRACAPSULAR fracture of the right femoral neck (Garden Stage IV). Why is Hemiarthroplasty (or Total Hip Arthroplasty) preferred over closed screw osteosynthesis in an elderly patient with a displaced Garden III/IV fracture?","o":["Displaced intracapsular fractures disrupt the ascending retinacular branches of the medial femoral circumflex artery, creating high risk of femoral head Avascular Necrosis (AVN) and non-union—requiring prosthetic replacement","Intertrochanteric fractures have higher AVN risk than subcapital fractures","Intracapsular fractures never cause AVN and heal within 2 weeks with bed rest","Dynamic hip screw (DHS) is strictly superior to arthroplasty in elderly displaced subcapital fractures"],"a":0,"e":"Blood supply to the femoral head derives mainly from retinacular branches of the medial femoral circumflex artery ascending along the femoral neck capsule (plus absence of periosteum inside the joint capsule and synovial fibrinolysins bathing the fracture). Displaced (Garden III-IV) intracapsular neck fractures in older adults (\u003e60-65 years) carry 30-40% AVN and non-union rates with internal fixation; hence bipolar hemiarthroplasty (or total hip arthroplasty in active independent elderly) enables immediate pain-free mobilisation. (Extracapsular intertrochanteric fractures have rich cancellous blood supply and are fixed with DHS/PFN).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03154","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Pigmentary Disorders","q":"A 21-year-old woman presents with progressive, well-demarcated, symmetrically distributed chalk-white DEPIGMENTED macules over her dorsa of hands, periorbital skin, and shins, showing bright ivory-white enhancement under Wood\u0027s lamp examination with preserved pinprick sensation and normal scaling. Which auto-immune/therapeutic approach applies to generalised active vitiligo?","o":["Superficial Malassezia infection; single-dose oral fluconazole","Autoimmune destruction of epidermal melanocytes; topical potent corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus for face/folds) PLUS Narrowband UVB (NB-UVB 311 nm) phototherapy for widespread disease","Pityriasis alba; oral methotrexate","Paucibacillary tuberculoid leprosy (which shows anaesthetic hypopigmented plaques); 6 months MDT"],"a":1,"e":"Vitiligo features total loss of melanocytes (chalky-white depigmentation accentuated ivory-white under Wood\u0027s lamp, often showing Koebner phenomenon and associated autoimmune thyroid disease), unlike hypopigmented scaling patches of pityriasis versicolor/alba or anaesthetic hypopigmented patches of leprosy. Focal lesions respond to topical corticosteroids/tacrolimus (topical ruxolitinib/JAK inhibitors increasingly used), while generalised/active vitiligo responds best to Narrowband UVB (311 nm) phototherapy (+ oral mini-pulse steroids if rapidly spreading).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03155","course":"MBBS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Trauma- and Stressor-Related Disorders","q":"Four months after surviving a fatal bus accident, a 27-year-old man experiences recurrent distressing flashbacks and nightmares of the crash, avoids riding vehicles or passing the highway, suffers persistent hypervigilance and exaggerated startle response, and feels detached and emotionally numb. What is the diagnosis (duration \u003e 1 month) and evidence-based first-line treatment?","o":["Adjustment disorder; lifelong alprazolam monotherapy","Acute Stress Disorder (which is strictly \u003c 1 month duration); high-dose haloperidol","Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD); trauma-focused psychotherapy (Cognitive Processing Therapy, Prolonged Exposure, or EMDR) PLUS SSRI (sertraline/paroxetine; prazosin for trauma nightmares)","Panic disorder; immediate ECT"],"a":2,"e":"Exposure to life-threatening trauma followed for \u003e 1 month by four symptom clusters—(1) intrusion (flashbacks, nightmares); (2) avoidance; (3) negative alterations in cognition/mood; and (4) hyperarousal/reactivity—defines PTSD (symptoms lasting 3 days to \u003c 1 month are classified as Acute Stress Disorder). Trauma-focused CBT/EMDR and SSRIs (sertraline, paroxetine, venlafaxine) are first-line, with alpha-1 blocker prazosin helping trauma-related nightmares; benzodiazepines are avoided due to dependence and impaired fear extinction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03156","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Prosthodontics and Crown \u0026 Bridge","topic":"Ante\u0027s Law \u0026 Pier Abutment Management","q":"A patient requires a 5-unit fixed partial denture replacing the maxillary first premolar and maxillary first molar, using the canine, second premolar (pier abutment), and second molar as abutments. Why is a rigid 5-unit bridge prone to retainer cement failure here, and where should a non-rigid connector (stress breaker) ideally be positioned?","o":["Because Ante\u0027s law is violated unless all connectors are soldered rigidly at the gingival embrasure; place a non-rigid connector on the mesial aspect of the canine","Because pier abutments lack periodontal mechanoreceptors; place the keyway on the mesial aspect of the pier abutment so occlusal forces pull the key out of the keyway","Because porcelain-fused-to-metal cannot span five units; replace the pier abutment with an immediate cantilever from the canine","Because physiological tooth movement turns the middle pier abutment into a fulcrum creating tensile debonding forces at terminal retainers; place the keyway (female matrix) on the distal aspect of the pier abutment (second premolar) with the key (male patrix) on the mesial aspect of the distal pontic"],"a":3,"e":"When a middle pier abutment sits between two edentulous spaces, occlusal load on one end acts via a Class I lever/fulcrum at the pier abutment, generating extrusive tensile stresses on the opposite terminal retainer and breaking the cement seal. Placing a non-rigid connector (precision dovetail keyway on the distal of the pier abutment and male key on the mesial of the distal pontic) allows physiological mesial tipping of posterior teeth to seat the key more tightly into the keyway rather than unseating it.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03157","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopaedics","topic":"Steiner\u0027s Skeletal Jaw Relationship","q":"Lateral cephalometric tracing of a 13-year-old boy with a convex facial profile and 9 mm overjet reveals: SNA = 82 degrees, SNB = 75 degrees, and ANB = +7 degrees (Steiner norms: SNA 82 +/- 2 degrees, SNB 80 +/- 2 degrees, ANB 2 +/- 2 degrees), with a normal SN plane inclination. How is this skeletal discrepancy classified?","o":["Skeletal Class II malocclusion primarily due to mandibular retrognathism with an orthognathic maxilla","Skeletal Class II malocclusion primarily due to maxillary prognathism with a normal mandible","Skeletal Class I bimaxillary protrusion","Skeletal Class III malocclusion due to maxillary deficiency"],"a":0,"e":"Steiner\u0027s SNA (82 degrees) indicates a normally positioned maxilla relative to the anterior cranial base (S-N), whereas SNB (75 degrees, well below 80 +/- 2 degrees) demonstrates a retrognathic mandible. The resulting ANB difference of +7 degrees (\u003e4 degrees) confirms a Skeletal Class II jaw base discrepancy driven by mandibular retrusion, making functional/mandibular advancement orthopaedics relevant in a growing adolescent.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03158","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Le Fort Midface Fracture Classification","q":"In maxillofacial traumatology, match each Le Fort fracture type to its defining anatomical separation plane: 1) Le Fort I, 2) Le Fort II, 3) Le Fort III.","o":["1-Horizontal (Guérin) fracture above tooth apices detaching hard palate/alveolus from maxilla; 2-Pyramidal fracture through nasofrontal suture, medial orbital wall, infraorbital rim \u0026 zygomaticomaxillary suture; 3-Craniofacial disjunction through frontozygomatic sutures, orbital walls \u0026 zygomatic arches","1-Craniofacial disjunction through frontozygomatic sutures; 2-Horizontal fracture above maxillary apices; 3-Pyramidal fracture sparing zygomatic arches","1-Pyramidal fracture through infraorbital rim; 2-Craniofacial disjunction; 3-Isolated alveolar dentoalveolar fracture","1-Isolated nasal bone fracture; 2-Zygomaticomaxillary complex tripod fracture; 3-Horizontal Guérin fracture"],"a":0,"e":"Le Fort I (floating palate / Guérin) runs horizontally through the pyriform aperture, canine fossa, and pterygoid plates above the maxillary apices. Le Fort II (pyramidal) extends from the nasofrontal suture across the lacrimal/medial orbital floor, infraorbital rim/foramen, and zygomaticomaxillary suture to pterygoid plates. Le Fort III (craniofacial disjunction) separates the midface completely from the cranium via the nasofrontal suture, orbital walls, frontozygomatic sutures, and zygomatic arches.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03159","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Paediatric and Preventive Dentistry","topic":"Space Maintainers in Primary/Mixed Dentition","q":"A 5-year-old healthy child requires extraction of a non-restorable carious mandibular right primary second molar (tooth 85). Clinical and radiographic examination shows that the permanent mandibular right first molar (tooth 46) has not yet erupted through alveolar bone and its crown lies intraosseously beneath the mucosa. Which space maintainer is specifically indicated?","o":["Conventional band-and-loop space maintainer cemented onto the primary first molar alone","Intra-alveolar distal shoe space maintainer extending subgingivally to guide the mesial surface of erupting tooth 46","Nance palatal holding arch with acrylic button","Fixed lingual holding arch soldered to permanent first molar bands"],"a":1,"e":"When a primary second molar is lost prematurely before eruption of the adjacent permanent first molar (around age 6), the permanent first molar tends to tip mesially during intraosseous eruption, causing severe arch-length loss. A distal shoe (Roche/Willet) space maintainer uses an intra-alveolar vertical blade contacting the mesial surface of the unerupted permanent first molar to guide it into its correct occlusal position.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03160","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"Apexification Techniques","q":"A 9-year-old boy presents with pulp necrosis and a periapical radiolucency of tooth 11, which has an open blunderbuss apex and thin dentinal walls following trauma. Why is single- or two-visit Mineral Trioxide Aggregate (MTA) apical barrier apexification generally preferred over prolonged multi-month calcium hydroxide [Ca(OH)2] dressing changes?","o":["MTA requires 18-24 months of monthly re-instrumentation to form an apical barrier","Ca(OH)2 cannot neutralise bacterial lipopolysaccharide whereas MTA acts purely as a temporary non-setting paste","Prolonged Ca(OH)2 exposure degrades collagenous dentine matrix increasing cervical root fracture risk, whereas MTA provides an immediate biocompatible apical seal allowing earlier definitive coronal restoration","MTA induces rapid root lengthening and thickening of the entire root wall unlike Ca(OH)2"],"a":2,"e":"Traditional Ca(OH)2 apexification requires 6-18 months of repeated dressing changes, during which high alkalinity denatures organic dentine matrix proteins and significantly increases susceptibility to cervical root fracture in thin-walled immature teeth. Placing a 3-5 mm MTA (or Biodentine) apical plug creates an immediate bioactive, biocompatible seal so the canal can be reinforced promptly with bonded composite/obturating material (note: regenerative endodontics/revascularisation, not MTA apexification, aims for continued root lengthening/thickening).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03161","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Prosthodontics and Crown \u0026 Bridge","topic":"Hanau\u0027s Quint (Laws of Articulation)","q":"When fabricating bilateral balanced complete dentures according to Hanau\u0027s Quint and Thielemann\u0027s formula [Balanced Occlusion = (Incisal Guidance x Condylar Guidance) / (Plane of Occlusion x Compensating Curve x Cusp Height)], if a patient exhibits a steep protrusive Condylar Guidance (45 degrees) while Incisal Guidance is kept shallow (10 degrees) and Plane of Occlusion remains fixed, which adjustment restores balanced posterior contact during protrusion?","o":["Eliminate the Compensating Curve completely (flat occlusal plane) and use zero-degree monoplane teeth","Increase Incisal Guidance to 60 degrees with deep vertical incisal overlap","Decrease the Compensating Curve downward (reverse curve) while reducing Cusp Height","Increase the prominence of the Compensating Curve (Curve of Spee) and/or select higher-cusp posterior teeth (increase Cusp Height)"],"a":3,"e":"In Hanau\u0027s Quint (Thielemann\u0027s equation: CG x IG / PO x CC x CH = constant), Condylar Guidance (CG) and Incisal Guidance (IG) sit in the numerator as fixed/semi-fixed end-controlling factors, while Plane of Occlusion (PO), Compensating Curve (CC), and Cusp Height (CH) sit in the denominator. When a steep CG increases the numerator (causing Christensen\u0027s posterior disclusion gap on protrusion), the clinician compensates by increasing a denominator factor—most effectively steepening the Compensating Curve (Curve of Spee) and/or increasing posterior Cusp Height/inclination.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03162","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopaedics","topic":"Moment-to-Force (M/F) Ratio \u0026 Types of Tooth Movement","q":"A retraction force of 100 g is applied to a maxillary canine bracket located 10 mm coronal to the tooth\u0027s centre of resistance (CR), generating a tipping moment of 1,000 g-mm. To achieve pure bodily translation (M/F ratio ~ 10:1), what counter-moment must be applied at the bracket?","o":["1,000 g-mm in the opposite rotational sense (yielding M/F ratio 10:1)","0 g-mm because a single bracket force through the crown translates the root apex automatically","500 g-mm in the opposite rotational sense (yielding M/F ratio 5:1, which produces pure translation)","1,000 g-mm in the same rotational sense (doubling the tipping moment to 2,000 g-mm)"],"a":0,"e":"Because the orthodontic bracket lies ~10 mm coronal to the centre of resistance (CR), a 100 g distal force creates an undesired moment of force M_F = 100 g x 10 mm = 1,000 g-mm tipping the crown distally and root mesially. Applying an equal and opposite counter-moment (moment of a couple, M_C = 1,000 g-mm; M_C / F = 10:1) cancels net rotation around CR, producing pure bodily translation. By contrast, M/F ~ 5-7:1 produces controlled tipping, and M/F \u003e= 12:1 produces root torquing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03163","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Bilateral Sagittal Split Osteotomy (BSSO)","q":"A 22-year-old woman with severe skeletal Class II mandibular retrognathia is scheduled for a 7-mm mandibular advancement via Bilateral Sagittal Split Osteotomy (BSSO). Which neurosensory complication MUST be specifically highlighted during informed consent as the most frequent postoperative morbidity of BSSO, and why?","o":["Facial nerve frontal branch paralysis due to intraoral vestibular incision","Paresthesia/hypoesthesia of the lower lip and chin due to traction or injury to the inferior alveolar nerve (IAN) within the mandibular canal during osteotomy splitting and rigid fixation","Ipsilateral ptosis and miosis due to cervical sympathetic trunk transection","Permanent loss of taste on the posterior third of the tongue due to glossopharyngeal nerve transection"],"a":1,"e":"During BSSO (Trauner-Obwegeser / Dal Pont / Hunsuck modification), the sagittal split passes directly along the buccal side of the mandibular canal enclosing the inferior alveolar neurovascular bundle. Retraction at the lingula, chisel manipulation, segment mobilisation/advancement, and bicortical screw placement make transient (and occasionally persistent) neurosensory deficit of the inferior alveolar/mental nerve (lower lip and chin numbness) the most common complication of BSSO.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03164","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Paediatric and Preventive Dentistry","topic":"Avulsed Mature Permanent Incisor Management","q":"A 10-year-old boy arrives at the dental emergency clinic 35 minutes after complete avulsion of his mature permanent maxillary central incisor (closed apex), which was immediately placed in cold milk. There is no alveolar fracture. Following gentle saline rinsing and atraumatic replantation into the socket, what is the evidence-based IADT protocol for splinting and endodontic treatment?","o":["Perform immediate extraoral apicoectomy and keep the tooth dry for 2 hours prior to replantation","Apply a rigid wire-acrylic splint for 12 weeks; defer root canal therapy for 1 year regardless of symptoms","Apply a passive, flexible splint for 2 weeks; initiate root canal therapy within 7-10 days (before splint removal) using calcium hydroxide intracanal medication, alongside systemic antibiotic (doxycycline/amoxicillin) and tetanus check","Scrub the periodontal ligament thoroughly with a stiff brush and sodium hypochlorite before replantation and leave unsplinted"],"a":2,"e":"Per International Association of Dental Traumatology (IADT) guidelines for an avulsed permanent tooth with a closed apex and extraoral dry time \u003c60 minutes (stored in physiological medium like milk/HBSS): gently rinse debris with saline (preserving viable PDL cells), replant immediately, stabilise with a passive flexible splint for 2 weeks (allowing physiological micromotion to prevent ankylosis), prescribe systemic antibiotics (amoxicillin or doxycycline per age/weight) and verify tetanus status, and start RCT at 7-10 days to prevent inflammatory root resorption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03165","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"Sodium Hypochlorite (NaOCl) Extrusion Accident","q":"During root canal irrigation of a maxillary canine with a side-vented needle wedged tightly in the canal, the patient suddenly screams in severe burning pain, accompanied within minutes by profuse haemorrhagic oozing through the canal, rapid tense cheek/upper-lip swelling, and spreading infraorbital ecchymosis. What has occurred, and what is the most appropriate immediate management?","o":["Maxillary sinus barotrauma; seal the access cavity permanently with amalgam immediately","Benign air emphysema; discharge without analgesia or follow-up","Acute anaphylaxis to gutta-percha; immediately force-flush the canal with 5.25% NaOCl again to clear blood clots","Periapical extrusion of NaOCl (hypochlorite accident); immediately stop irrigation, reassure patient, provide local/systemic analgesia, apply cold compresses for the first 24 hours (switching to warm compresses thereafter), prescribe prophylactic antibiotics and short-course corticosteroids if severe oedema/airway risk exists"],"a":3,"e":"Wedging an irrigation needle under positive pressure forces alkaline cytotoxic NaOCl into periradicular tissues, causing immediate excruciating pain, chemical haemolysis/vascular necrosis (profuse canal bleeding, rapid oedema, ecchymosis), and potential neurosensory deficit. Management comprises stopping NaOCl immediately (gentle saline rinse if tolerated), pain control (long-acting nerve block + analgesics), cold packs for 24 h to limit swelling followed by warm compresses to promote resolution, antibiotics to prevent secondary infection, and close monitoring (hospitalise if submandibular/airway involvement).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03166","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Prosthodontics and Crown \u0026 Bridge","topic":"Osseointegration \u0026 Implant Spatial Guidelines","q":"When placing two adjacent endosseous titanium dental implants in the anterior/premolar maxilla next to a natural tooth, what minimum horizontal spacing MUST be maintained between two adjacent implants and between an implant and an adjacent natural tooth root to preserve interproximal crestal bone and gingival papilla?","o":["At least 3.0 mm between two adjacent implants and at least 1.5 mm between an implant and an adjacent natural tooth","At least 0.5 mm between two adjacent implants and 0.2 mm from a natural tooth","Zero spacing required as implants fuse directly with natural cementum","At least 8.0 mm between two adjacent implants and 5.0 mm from a natural tooth"],"a":0,"e":"Following platform establishment, saucer-shaped lateral crestal bone remodelling extends ~1.3-1.5 mm horizontally around each implant collar. If two implants are placed closer than 3.0 mm apart, their lateral bone-loss zones overlap, causing vertical inter-implant bone loss and loss of the interdental papilla (\u0027black triangle\u0027). Maintaining \u003e=1.5 mm from an adjacent natural tooth preserves the tooth\u0027s periodontal blood supply and interproximal bone peak.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03167","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopaedics","topic":"Cervical Vertebral Maturation (CVM) Method","q":"An 11.5-year-old boy with a skeletal Class II mandibular deficiency (ANB +6 degrees, normal maxilla) is evaluated via Baccetti\u0027s Cervical Vertebral Maturation (CVM) method on his lateral cephalogram. Concavities are visible at the lower borders of C2, C3, and C4, and C3/C4 vertebral bodies appear rectangular-horizontal rather than trapezoidal. Which CVM stage does this represent, and why is it critical for Twin Block myofunctional therapy?","o":["Stage CS6 (post-pubertal completion) — peak mandibular velocity will occur in 3 years","Stage CS3-CS4 (circumpubertal peak mandibular growth spurt) — ideal window to initiate functional mandibular advancement before growth completion","Stage CS1 (prepubertal) — too late for any skeletal growth modification, requiring orthognathic surgery only","Stage CS1 — requires immediate extraction of all four first permanent molars"],"a":1,"e":"In Baccetti, Franchi, and McNamara\u0027s 6-stage CVM method: CS1-CS2 show flat lower borders of C3/C4 (pre-peak); CS3 shows concavities at lower borders of C2 and C3 with trapezoidal/horizontal C3-C4 (onset of peak mandibular growth); CS4 shows concavities at C2, C3, and C4 with horizontal-rectangular C3 and C4 (peak/just post-peak growth spurt within 1-2 years). Functional appliances (Twin Block, Herbst, Activator) achieve maximal skeletal mandibular length gains when timed during CS3-CS4.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03168","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Ludwig\u0027s Angina","q":"A 38-year-old poorly controlled diabetic man presents 4 days after pain in a carious mandibular second molar with fever, dysphagia, drooling, muffled \u0027hot potato\u0027 voice, bilateral board-like brawny submandibular and submental induration, and superior-posterior elevation of the floor of the mouth and tongue. What is the diagnosis, anatomical pathway, and absolute FIRST priority in management?","o":["Ranula of the sublingual gland; perform elective marsupialisation next week","Unilateral buccal space abscess; administer oral ibuprofen and discharge","Ludwig\u0027s angina (bilateral submandibular, sublingual, and submental space cellulitis) spreading from mandibular molar apices below the mylohyoid ridge; secure the airway immediately (awake fibreoptic intubation or surgical tracheostomy) before I\u0026D and IV antibiotics","Cavernous sinus thrombosis; immediate craniotomy"],"a":2,"e":"Ludwig\u0027s angina is a rapidly spreading, non-fluctuant, woody cellulitis involving bilateral submandibular, sublingual, and submental spaces simultaneously, most commonly originating from mandibular second/third molar roots whose apices lie inferior to the mylohyoid muscle attachment. Tongue elevation and supraglottic oedema threaten sudden asphyxiation—making immediate airway control (awake nasal fibreoptic intubation or awake tracheostomy; avoiding supine positioning/blind muscle relaxants) the number-one emergency priority ahead of decompression/drainage and broad-spectrum IV antibiotics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03169","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Paediatric and Preventive Dentistry","topic":"Vital Pulpotomy in Primary Molars","q":"A 6-year-old cooperative child has a deep carious lesion causing a pin-point mechanical/carious pulp exposure in a vital mandibular primary second molar (tooth 75) with no spontaneous pain, no mobility, physiological bleeding controlled within 3 minutes with moist cotton, and intact furcation bone on radiograph. Which medicament is preferred over traditional Buckley\u0027s formocresol for vital pulpotomy, and why?","o":["Zinc oxide-eugenol alone as a pulpotomy medicament — because eugenol stimulates tertiary odontoblast differentiation without inflammation","37% phosphoric acid gel — to sterilise and fix the entire radicular pulp permanently","Amalgam alloy packed directly over bleeding pulp horns","Mineral Trioxide Aggregate (MTA) or Biodentine — achieves superior dentine-bridge healing and high clinical/radiographic success without formocresol\u0027s formaldehyde-related cytotoxicity, mutagenicity, and internal root resorption risk"],"a":3,"e":"Vital pulpotomy removes inflamed coronal pulp while preserving healthy radicular pulp in symptom-free primary teeth. Although Buckley\u0027s formocresol (19% formaldehyde, 35% cresol) was historically standard, formaldehyde carries systemic distribution, mutagenicity/carcinogenicity concerns (IARC), and higher rates of internal root resorption. Bioceramic calcium-silicate cements (MTA, Biodentine) provide excellent sealing, alkaline antibacterial pH, biocompatibility, and predictable dentine-bridge formation per AAPD guidelines.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03170","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Periodontology","topic":"Peri-Implant Mucositis vs Peri-Implantitis","q":"Two years after prosthetic loading of a mandibular molar implant, a patient presents on recall with erythematous peri-implant mucosa and bleeding on gentle probing (BOP) with a 4 mm probing depth, but comparison with the 1-year baseline radiograph shows ZERO progressive marginal crestal bone loss beyond initial physiological remodelling. According to the 2017 AAP/EFP World Workshop classification, what is the diagnosis and expected reversibility?","o":["Peri-implant mucositis — reversible plaque-induced inflammation confined to peri-implant soft tissues without supporting bone loss, managed by mechanical debridement and oral hygiene reinforcement","Peri-implantitis — irreversible progressive bone destruction requiring immediate implant explantation","Normal healthy peri-implant mucosa requiring no intervention","Retrograde apical peri-implantitis caused solely by occlusal bruxism"],"a":0,"e":"Per the 2017 AAP/EFP Classification of Periodontal and Peri-Implant Diseases: Peri-implant mucositis is diagnosed by presence of bleeding on gentle probing (with or without erythema/swelling/suppuration) WITHOUT radiographic bone loss beyond initial crestal remodelling; it is reversible upon biofilm removal. Peri-implantitis requires BOTH soft-tissue inflammation (BOP/suppuration) AND progressive radiographic bone loss compared with baseline (or probing depth \u003e=6 mm and \u003e=3 mm bone loss apical to the most coronal intraosseous part if baseline radiographs are absent).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03171","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Prosthodontics and Crown \u0026 Bridge","topic":"Ferrule Effect \u0026 Post-Core Biomechanics","q":"When restoring an endodontically treated maxillary central incisor with a post, core, and full-coverage crown, which structural tooth-preparation feature contributes MOST decisively to resisting vertical root fracture and crown dislodgement under functional oblique occlusal loads?","o":["Eliminating all coronal dentine flush with the gingiva so the crown sits purely on composite core","Preserving a circumferential \u0027ferrule\u0027 of at least 1.5-2.0 mm of sound coronal dentine wall height (and \u003e=1 mm thickness) embraced by the crown margin apical to the core","Placing the widest possible threaded metal screw post to maximise dentine removal","Leaving only 1 mm of apical gutta-percha seal to allow a longer post"],"a":1,"e":"The ferrule effect—a continuous 360-degree band of crown margin encircling 1.5-2.0 mm of sound supragingival tooth structure (coronal to the finish line and apical to the core-tooth junction)—dramatically reduces wedging stress transmission from the post to the root canal walls, cutting vertical root fracture rates compared with teeth lacking a ferrule. Meanwhile, 4-5 mm of intact apical gutta-percha is mandatory to preserve the apical seal, and wide threaded posts dangerously weaken roots.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03172","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopaedics","topic":"Rapid Maxillary Expansion (RME / Hyrax)","q":"A 10-year-old boy with a narrow maxillary arch and bilateral posterior crossbite undergoes Rapid Maxillary Expansion (RME) with a banded Hyrax jackscrew activated 0.5 mm/day (two quarter-turns daily). Ten days into activation, a 3-mm midline diastema appears between the maxillary central incisors alongside occlusal crossbite correction. How should this midline diastema be interpreted and managed?","o":["Apply heavy elastic chains immediately between 11 and 21 while still turning the Hyrax screw to prevent root separation","It indicates the midpalatal suture failed to open and only buccal dental tipping occurred","It is a normal, expected clinical sign confirming successful orthopaedic opening of the midpalatal suture; reassure parents, complete active expansion + 3-6 month retention, and allow transseptal gingival fibres to spontaneously close the diastema before finishing orthodontics","It indicates catastrophic periodontal ligament necrosis of teeth 11/21; remove the Hyrax immediately and extract incisors"],"a":2,"e":"In pre-adolescent patients before complete midpalatal suture interdigitation, heavy RME forces (10-20 lb) split the midpalatal suture in a triangular V-shaped opening wider anteriorly than posteriorly, producing a temporary midline diastema (~half the screw expansion) that clinically confirms true skeletal separation. Over the 3-6 month retention/ossification phase, stretched transseptal periodontal fibres pull the central incisor crowns back together spontaneously.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03173","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Zygomaticomaxillary Complex (ZMC / Tripod) Fracture","q":"A 27-year-old motorist sustains blunt trauma to the left cheek and presents with periorbital ecchymosis, subconjunctival haemorrhage with NO posterior limit, flattening of the left malar prominence, palpable step deformity at the infraorbital rim, numbness of the left upper lip and nasal ala, and restricted mouth opening (trismus). Which fracture and underlying mechanical causes explain the numbness and trismus?","o":["Le Fort I horizontal fracture; lingual nerve injury causes trismus","Isolated nasal bone fracture; optic nerve transection causes subconjunctival haemorrhage","Isolated mandibular condylar neck fracture; facial nerve damage causes upper lip numbness","Left Zygomaticomaxillary Complex (ZMC) fracture; infraorbital nerve (V2) entrapment/injury at the infraorbital foramen/floor causes lip/ala numbness, while depressed zygomatic arch impinges on the mandibular coronoid process/temporalis causing trismus"],"a":3,"e":"ZMC fractures disrupt the zygoma\u0027s four classic articulations (zygomaticofrontal, infraorbital rim/zygomaticomaxillary, zygomaticotemporal arch, and zygomaticosphenoid in the lateral orbital wall). Because the infraorbital rim/floor fracture line traverses the infraorbital canal/foramen, V2 sensory deficit over the cheek, upper lip, nasal ala, and anterior teeth is hallmark, while medial depression of the zygomatic arch/body mechanically blocks anterior-superior excursion of the mandibular coronoid process causing trismus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03174","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Paediatric and Preventive Dentistry","topic":"38% Silver Diamine Fluoride (SDF) in ECC","q":"A 3-year-old pre-cooperative child presents with asymptomatic cavitated dentine caries on maxillary primary incisors without pulpal involvement or abscess. Non-invasive caries arrest using 38% Silver Diamine Fluoride (SDF, 44,800 ppm F-) is chosen. What dual mechanism arrests caries progression, and what mandatory cosmetic disclosure must be obtained in informed consent?","o":["Silver ions exert potent bactericidal and matrix metalloproteinase (MMP)-inhibiting action while fluoride promotes fluorapatite/calcium fluoride remineralisation; arrested carious dentine turns permanently dark black","SDF acts only as a temporary 24-hour mouthwash requiring weekly general anaesthesia","Silver ions bleach carious dentine bright white while fluoride dissolves remaining enamel","SDF stimulates immediate secondary pulpitis to calcify the entire pulp chamber"],"a":0,"e":"38% SDF (Ag(NH3)2F) delivers ~44,800 ppm fluoride and ~253,870 ppm silver. Silver ions disrupt bacterial cell walls/enzymes (\u0027zombie effect\u0027) and inhibit collagen-degrading dentine MMPs/cathepsins, while high-concentration fluoride forms insoluble CaF2 reservoirs and fluorapatite to harden demineralised dentine. Silver precipitation turns ONLY the porous carious lesion permanently jet black (healthy enamel does not stain), requiring explicit caregiver informed consent beforehand.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03175","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"Vertical Root Fracture (VRF)","q":"Four years after root canal treatment and cast post-core crown placement on a mandibular second premolar, a 45-year-old patient reports mild pain on biting. Examination reveals a sinus tract located coronally near the attached gingiva (rather than near the apex), a narrow, deep, isolated 9-mm periodontal pocket exclusively on the buccal aspect (all other sites \u003c=2 mm), and a J-shaped / \u0027halo\u0027 periradicular radiolucency on radiograph/CBCT. What is the most likely diagnosis and prognosis?","o":["Isolated gingival abscess from toothbrush trauma — prescribe chlorhexidine rinse only","Vertical root fracture (VRF) — hopeless prognosis for a single-rooted tooth, typically requiring extraction","Simple reversible occlusal high spot — adjust cusp height and observe","Primary acute apical periodontitis from a missed lingual canal easily cured by routine non-surgical retreatment without fracture evaluation"],"a":1,"e":"Vertical root fracture (often associated with excessive dentine removal, wedging posts, or heavy compaction forces) classically presents with a triad of pathognomonic signs: (1) a narrow, steep, isolated deep periodontal pocket along the fracture line, (2) a sinus tract positioned coronally closer to the gingival margin than the root apex, and (3) a J-shaped/halo lateral-to-apical radiolucency (confirmed on high-resolution CBCT or direct surgical visualisation). In single-rooted teeth, VRF carries a hopeless prognosis requiring extraction (or root resection in multi-rooted molars).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03176","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Prosthodontics and Crown \u0026 Bridge","topic":"Lithium Disilicate vs Monolithic Zirconia Bonding Protocols","q":"When adhesively cementing an all-ceramic veneer/onlay made of lithium disilicate (glass-ceramic) versus a minimally retentive resin-bonded crown/bridge made of monolithic yttria-stabilised tetragonal zirconia (Y-TZP), why do their internal intaglio surface conditioning protocols differ fundamentally?","o":["Lithium disilicate has a silica glass phase etched by 5-9.5% hydrofluoric (HF) acid + silane coupling agent; Y-TZP zirconia is silica-free (inert to HF acid) and requires 50-micron alumina air-particle abrasion followed by a phosphate monomer primer containing 10-MDP","Both ceramics are etched identically with 37% phosphoric acid for 60 seconds without primers","Zirconia requires 20-minute immersion in concentrated HF acid to dissolve glassy quartz","Lithium disilicate cannot bond with resin cements and must be cemented with zinc phosphate only"],"a":0,"e":"Etching lithium disilicate with 5% HF acid (20 seconds) selectively dissolves the glassy silica matrix around lithium disilicate crystals for micromechanical interlocking, while silane creates covalent siloxane (-Si-O-Si-) bonds to resin cement. Conversely, polycrystalline Y-TZP zirconia contains NO glass/silica phase, so HF acid neither etches nor roughens it (and silane fails to bond). Zirconia bonding relies on low-pressure (1-2 bar) 50-micron alumina sandblasting + a 10-MDP (10-methacryloyloxydecyl dihydrogen phosphate) primer forming stable Zr-O-P chemical bonds (\u0027APC\u0027 protocol).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03177","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopaedics","topic":"Optimal Force Magnitude, Frontal vs Undermining Resorption \u0026 Root Resorption","q":"Why do light continuous orthodontic forces (~35-60 g for tipping; ~70-120 g for bodily movement) produce smoother, faster, and biologically safer tooth movement than heavy forces (\u003e250-300 g)?","o":["Heavy forces bypass osteoclasts completely and dissolve bone via acidic saliva","Heavy forces double PDL blood oxygen tension accelerating frontal resorption tenfold","Light forces stay near periodontal capillary blood pressure (~20-26 g/cm2), preserving blood flow for direct osteoclastic \u0027frontal resorption\u0027 along the lamina dura; heavy forces strangulate PDL capillaries causing sterile hyalinisation necrosis, delayed \u0027undermining resorption\u0027, and higher external apical root resorption risk","Light forces induce immediate ankylosis of the cementum to alveolar bone"],"a":2,"e":"Optimal orthodontic force compresses the PDL just enough to stimulate prostaglandin/RANKL-mediated osteoclastic differentiation directly at the lamina dura (frontal resorption) without occluding capillary perfusion (~20-26 mmHg / g/cm2). Excessive heavy force crushes PDL vessels, producing an avascular cell-free \u0027hyalinised\u0027 necrotic zone; tooth movement stalls for 1-2 weeks until marrow-derived osteoclasts tunnel up from behind (undermining resorption), increasing patient pain and external apical root resorption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03178","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Medication-Related Osteonecrosis of the Jaw (MRONJ)","q":"A 67-year-old woman receiving intravenous zoledronate every 4 weeks for metastatic breast cancer presents 10 weeks after a lower molar extraction with persistent exposed necrotic alveolar bone in the extraction site, localised dull pain, mucosal erythema, and purulent exudate. She has NEVER received head-and-neck radiotherapy. Per AAOMS guidelines, what is her MRONJ diagnosis/stage and appropriate initial therapy?","o":["MRONJ Stage 0 (no clinical exposed bone); reassure with observation only","Osteoradionecrosis (ORN) Stage III requiring immediate total hemimandibulectomy","Simple dry socket; place ZOE dressing and ignore antiresorptive history","MRONJ Stage 2 (exposed necrotic bone \u003e8 weeks with antiresorptive exposure, no prior radiation, plus infection/pain); manage with 0.12% chlorhexidine mouthrinse, targeted systemic antibiotics, analgesics, and superficial/conservative debridement of sharp sequestra"],"a":3,"e":"AAOMS defines MRONJ by three criteria: (1) current/previous antiresorptive (bisphosphonate/denosumab) or antiangiogenic therapy, (2) exposed bone or bone probed through an intraoral/extraoral fistula persisting \u003e8 weeks, and (3) NO history of jaw radiation therapy or obvious metastatic disease to the jaw. Stage 1 = exposed bone asymptomatic without infection; Stage 2 = exposed bone WITH pain and clinical infection/erythema/purulence (treated conservatively with antibacterial rinses, systemic antibiotics, pain control, and superficial smoothing); Stage 3 = Stage 2 plus pathologic fracture, extraoral fistula, or osteolysis extending to inferior border/sinus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03179","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Periodontology","topic":"Socransky\u0027s Microbial Complexes","q":"In Socransky\u0027s colour-coded hierarchical clustering of subgingival plaque biofilms, which three anaerobic species constitute the late-colonising \u0027Red Complex\u0027 most strongly correlated with deep periodontal pocket depth, bleeding on probing, and alveolar bone destruction?","o":["Porphyromonas gingivalis, Tannerella forsythia, and Treponema denticola","Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans, Candida albicans, and Lactobacillus acidophilus","Streptococcus sanguinis, Streptococcus mitis, and Actinomyces naeslundii","Fusobacterium nucleatum, Prevotella intermedia, and Campylobacter rectus"],"a":0,"e":"Socransky et al. mapped subgingival biofilm succession: early colonisers (Yellow/Green/Purple complexes such as Streptococcus sanguinis and Actinomyces) attach to the pellicle, followed by bridging \u0027Orange Complex\u0027 species (Fusobacterium nucleatum, Prevotella intermedia, Campylobacter rectus) that facilitate adhesion of the virulent \u0027Red Complex\u0027 triad—Porphyromonas gingivalis, Tannerella forsythia, and Treponema denticola—tightly linked to severe chronic periodontitis, pocket bleeding, and bone loss.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03180","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"Separated Rotary NiTi Instrument Management","q":"During shaping of a severely curved mesiobuccal canal of a vital mandibular first molar, a 3-mm tip of a #25/.04 rotary NiTi file separates strictly apical to the sharp mid-root curvature. Under a dental operating microscope, the fragment is tightly wedged beyond the curve without periapical radiolucency. What is the safest clinical decision-making hierarchy?","o":["Extract the vital mandibular first molar immediately as a mandatory rule","Attempt conservative bypass with fine pre-curved hand K-files and copious EDTA/NaOCl irrigation; if bypass fails without excessive dentine removal (avoiding perforation/root weakening from aggressive ultrasonic staging past the curve), clean/obturate up to the fragment and follow radiographically (retaining surgical micro-endodontics only if symptoms/lesion develop)","Immediately drill straight through the outer root curvature with large Gates-Glidden burs until perforation occurs","Leave the entire root canal system un-irrigated and un-obturated for 12 months"],"a":1,"e":"Management of a separated instrument depends on canal disinfection stage, periapical status, and fragment location relative to the curvature: fragments coronal to or at the curve are often retrieved ultrasonically under magnification; fragments wedged purely apical to a sharp curve carry high risk of strip perforation or vertical root fracture if aggressive ultrasonic dentine removal is attempted. Attempting a gentle bypass (and if unsuccessful in a clean vital tooth, thorough chemomechanical disinfection + obturation to the fragment with periodic follow-up) preserves root integrity and achieves high success.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03181","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Prosthodontics and Crown \u0026 Bridge","topic":"Aramany Classification of Maxillectomy Defects","q":"In maxillofacial prosthodontic rehabilitation using palatal obturators, match each Aramany Classification of partially edentulous maxillectomy defects to its anatomical configuration: 1) Class I, 2) Class II, 3) Class IV, 4) Class VI.","o":["1-Resection along the palatal midline retaining teeth on one side of the arch; 2-Unilateral posterior defect retaining anterior teeth across the midline; 3-Defect crossing the midline involving both anterior and posterior segments leaving a few posterior teeth on one side; 4-Anterior midline defect situated bilaterally anterior to remaining posterior teeth","1-Posterior soft palate only; 2-Bilateral complete edentulism; 3-Mandibular hemisection; 4-Midline hard palate pinhole","1-Anterior defect bilateral to posterior teeth; 2-Midline hemimaxillectomy; 3-Unilateral posterior defect; 4-Subcondylar defect","1-Class I Kennedy mandibular; 2-Class II Kennedy; 3-Class III Kennedy; 4-Class IV Kennedy"],"a":0,"e":"Mohamed Aramany (1978) classified postsurgical maxillectomy defects into 6 classes for RPD/obturator framework design: Class I = classic hemimaxillectomy along midline (linear/tripodal abutment design); Class II = unilateral posterior defect with intact anterior segment across midline (similar to Kennedy Class II); Class III = central hard-palate defect not involving teeth; Class IV = defect crossing midline involving anterior + one posterior side (linear remaining molars); Class V = bilateral posterior defect behind intact anterior teeth; Class VI = rare anterior trauma/surgical defect anterior to remaining bilateral posterior teeth.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03182","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopaedics","topic":"Bolton Anterior Tooth-Size Ratio Calculation","q":"On study model analysis of a permanent dentition, the sum of the mesiodistal widths of the six mandibular anterior teeth (canine to canine) is 39.0 mm, and the sum of the six maxillary anterior teeth is 45.0 mm. Calculate the Bolton Anterior Ratio (norm: 77.2% +/- 1.65%) and identify which arch exhibits relative tooth-size excess.","o":["115.4% — indicates an ideal balanced Bolton ratio","68.5% — indicates a maxillary anterior tooth-size excess","86.7% — indicates a mandibular anterior tooth-size excess (~4.26 mm relative excess)","86.7% — indicates a maxillary anterior tooth-size excess"],"a":2,"e":"Bolton Anterior Ratio = (Sum of Mandibular 6 anterior widths / Sum of Maxillary 6 anterior widths) x 100 = (39.0 / 45.0) x 100 = 86.67% (~86.7%). Because 86.7% exceeds the normal range (77.2% +/- 1.65%), the numerator (mandibular anterior segment) is disproportionately large: ideal mandibular 6 sum for a 45.0 mm maxillary arch = 45.0 x 0.772 = 34.74 mm, revealing 39.0 - 34.74 = 4.26 mm of mandibular anterior excess (often requiring lower incisor interproximal reduction or single lower incisor extraction).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03183","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Frey\u0027s Syndrome (Auriculotemporal / Gustatory Sweating)","q":"Nine months after superficial parotidectomy for a pleomorphic adenoma, a 45-year-old man complains of profuse sweating and cutaneous flushing over the left preauricular cheek whenever he chews sour or spicy foods. Which neurological mechanism causes Frey\u0027s syndrome, and which diagnostic clinical test turns the affected skin dark blue-black?","o":["Viral reactivation of herpes zoster in the geniculate ganglion; confirmed by Tzanck smear","Trigeminal motor branch entrapment inside masseter fascia; confirmed by Schirmer tear test","Direct salivary fistula from Stensen\u0027s duct; confirmed by sialogram alone","Aberrant cross-regeneration of severed postganglionic parasympathetic secretomotor fibres from the auriculotemporal nerve (CN IX -\u003e otic ganglion) onto denervated facial sweat glands/vessels; confirmed by Minor\u0027s starch-iodine test"],"a":3,"e":"During parotidectomy skin-flap elevation, both parasympathetic secretomotor fibres (from CN IX via otic ganglion and auriculotemporal nerve, cholinergic) and sympathetic fibres supplying sweat glands (also cholinergic!) are divided. Aberrant healing connects parasympathetic salivary fibres to sweat glands and cutaneous vessels, causing gustatory sweating and flushing (Frey\u0027s syndrome). Painting the cheek with iodine, dusting with starch powder, and giving a sialogogue turns sweat-exposed areas dark blue-black (Minor\u0027s starch-iodine test); botulinum toxin A intradermally is effective treatment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03184","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Periodontology","topic":"PASS Principles of Guided Bone Regeneration (Wang \u0026 Boyapati)","q":"In periodontal and pre-implant Guided Bone Regeneration (GBR), Wang and Boyapati defined four essential biological surgical prerequisites known as the \u0027PASS\u0027 principles. What does the acronym PASS stand for?","o":["Primary wound closure (tension-free), Angiogenesis (cortical decortication/blood supply), Space creation/maintenance (tenting/rigid barrier), and Stability of the wound and initial blood clot","Plaque control, Antibiotics, Scaling, and Splinting","Probing depth, Attachment level, Sulcular bleeding, and Suppuration","Porcelain crown, Abutment torque, Screw retention, and Seal"],"a":0,"e":"Predictable horizontal/vertical GBR bone regeneration relies on Wang and Boyapati\u0027s PASS principles: (1) Primary closure — tension-free soft-tissue flap advancement preventing membrane exposure and bacterial contamination; (2) Angiogenesis — intramarrow cortical penetration supplying angiogenic and osteoprogenitor cells; (3) Space maintenance — non-resorbable titanium-reinforced PTFE or tenting screws preventing soft-tissue collapse; (4) Stability — immobilising the graft, membrane, and fibrin clot via fixation tacks/sutures.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03185","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"Internal vs External Cervical Root Resorption","q":"A maxillary central incisor presents with a subtle pinkish hue in the cervical crown (\u0027pink tooth of Mummery\u0027). Radiographs show a well-defined, symmetrical, oval/ballooning radiolucent enlargement directly continuous with the mid-coronal pulp canal outline (the original canal wall silhouette disappears into the lesion), and the defect stays centred on mesial/distal SLOB tube-shift views. What is the diagnosis and immediate treatment?","o":["Normal pulp stone calcification — no treatment needed","Internal inflammatory root resorption — perform immediate root canal therapy (pulpectomy with NaOCl/ultrasonic disinfection, Ca(OH)2 dressing, and warm thermoplasticised gutta-percha/MTA obturation) to arrest resorptive odontoclasts","External invasive cervical resorption — leave vital pulp untouched and place amalgam externally only","Horizontal cervical root fracture — extract tooth immediately"],"a":1,"e":"Internal inflammatory resorption arises from chronically inflamed vital coronal pulp recruiting odontoclasts/macrophages beneath damaged predentine. Radiographically it appears as a smooth, symmetrical balloon-like widening continuous with the root canal (canal outline obliterated within the defect) that remains centred on SLOB shift films; in contrast, external resorption shows an intact root canal wall superimposed right through an irregular moth-eaten lesion that shifts on angled films. Prompt pulpectomy removes the vital blood supply driving odontoclasts and halts internal resorption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03186","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Prosthodontics and Crown \u0026 Bridge","topic":"Pankey-Mann-Schuyler (PMS) \u0026 Broadrick Occlusal Plane Analyser","q":"In full-mouth rehabilitation following the Pankey-Mann-Schuyler (PMS) philosophy for severely worn dentitions, why MUST anterior guidance (anterior teeth aesthetics, phonetics, and protrusive/canine disclusion) be established BEFORE restoring the posterior occlusal plane with the Broadrick occlusal plane analyser?","o":["Posterior molars must be restored blindly before anterior teeth so canines remain in heavy group contact during protrusion","PMS philosophy advocates simultaneous bilateral balancing contacts in natural full-mouth dentitions","Anterior guidance dictates the vertical and horizontal disclusion path separating posterior teeth during eccentric excursions; establishing anterior guidance first permits the Broadrick flag (~4-inch radius Curve of Spee centred from canine cusp tip and condyle/distobuccal reference) to generate posterior occlusal curves free of non-working and protrusive interferences","Broadrick analyser establishes a flat reverse Wilson curve eliminating all canine guidance"],"a":2,"e":"The Pankey-Mann-Schuyler (PMS) sequential philosophy proceeds systematically: (1) Examination/diagnosis, (2) Harmonise anterior guidance in harmony with the envelope of function (governing immediate posterior disclusion during protrusion and laterotrusion), (3) Restore lower posterior occlusal plane using the Broadrick flag (4-inch/10-cm compass arc swept from anterior canine tip and posterior condylar/distomolar survey point to establish an ideal Curve of Spee), and (4) Restore upper posterior teeth against the functionally generated/reconstructed mandibular plane to achieve mutually protected occlusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03187","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopaedics","topic":"Buccal Interradicular Miniscrew Safe Zones \u0026 Primary Stability","q":"A 19-year-old patient with bimaxillary dentoalveolar protrusion requires maximum anchorage en-masse anterior retraction following four first-premolar extractions using buccal Temporary Anchorage Devices (miniscrews) in the posterior maxilla. Which interradicular anatomical site provides the widest safe bone corridor, and what mechanism provides immediate miniscrew stability?","o":["Directly through the palatal root of the maxillary first molar at 90 degrees","Inside the maxillary sinus floor solely using soft palatal mucosa","Between the maxillary lateral incisor and central incisor at the gingival margin; relies on full 6-month osseointegration","Between the maxillary second premolar and maxillary first permanent molar (5-6 mm apical to the alveolar crest in attached/mucogingival boundary, angled 30-45 degrees apically); stability comes from mechanical cortical bone interlocking (NOT osseointegration) allowing immediate light loading"],"a":3,"e":"CBCT anatomical mapping shows the widest buccal maxillary interradicular space sits between the second premolar and first molar (especially 5-8 mm apical to the CEJ/alveolar crest; angling 30-45 degrees apically increases cortical bone contact length while steering clear of tapering roots). Unlike prosthetic implants that require osseointegration, orthodontic miniscrews (Ti-6Al-4V) rely on mechanical primary stability in cortical bone—permitting immediate light orthodontic loading (~150-200 g) and straightforward chairside unscrewing after treatment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03188","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Internal Derangement — Disc Displacement With vs Without Reduction","q":"A 26-year-old woman with a 3-year history of painless reciprocal clicking in her right TMJ wakes up unable to open her mouth wider than 24 mm, noting that the clicking sound has suddenly STOPPED completely and her mandible deflects sharply to the RIGHT on attempted wide opening. What intra-articular Wilkes Stage change has occurred?","o":["Progression from anterior disc displacement WITH reduction to acute anterior disc displacement WITHOUT reduction (\u0027closed lock\u0027) — the displaced disc now remains jammed anterior to the condyle blocking translation","Spontaneous full healing of the TMJ bilaminar zone returning the disc to normal superior position","Bilateral TMJ ankylosis with bony fusion developed overnight","Acute subcondylar dislocation with mouth locked wide open (open lock)"],"a":0,"e":"In TMJ internal derangement, anterior disc displacement WITH reduction produces an opening click as the condyle snaps over the posterior band onto the disc, followed by a closing click upon disc re-slipping. When retrodiscal tissues stretch further, the condyle fails to recapture the disc during translation—causing sudden cessation of clicking, acute painful restriction of mouth opening (\u003c25-30 mm limited solely to hinge rotation), and persistent deflection toward the affected right side (\u0027closed lock\u0027, Wilkes Stage III/IV, confirmed by MRI).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03189","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Periodontology","topic":"Cairo\u0027s / Miller\u0027s Gingival Recession Classification","q":"A 33-year-old patient presents for root-coverage mucogingival surgery on a maxillary canine with 4 mm buccal gingival recession extending beyond the mucogingival junction, but interproximal probing shows ZERO interproximal clinical attachment loss and intact interdental papillae/crestal bone on both mesial and distal aspects. According to Cairo\u0027s (2011 / 2018 World Workshop) Recession Classification (and Miller\u0027s Class II), what is the classification and root-coverage prognosis using a Coronally Advanced Flap + Subepithelial Connective Tissue Graft (CAF + SCTG)?","o":["Miller Class IV — requires immediate extraction due to hopeless bone loss","Cairo Recession Type 1 (RT1; corresponding to Miller Class II) — 100% complete root coverage is predictable because interproximal attachment/bone support remains completely intact","Cairo RT3 — root coverage is completely impossible","Cairo RT2 — interproximal attachment loss exceeds buccal recession"],"a":1,"e":"Cairo\u0027s 2011 system (adopted in the 2017/2018 AAP/EFP classification) stratifies gingival recession by interproximal attachment level (the primary biologic determinant of blood supply/flap stability): RT1 (Miller Classes I and II) = facial/lingual recession with NO loss of interproximal attachment (CEJ not detectable mesially or distally) -\u003e 100% root coverage achievable with CAF + SCTG; RT2 (Miller Class III) = interproximal CAL \u003c= buccal CAL -\u003e partial root coverage; RT3 (Miller Class IV) = interproximal CAL \u003e buccal CAL -\u003e root coverage unpredictable/not feasible.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03190","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Paediatric and Preventive Dentistry","topic":"Molar-Incisor Hypomineralisation (MIH)","q":"An 8-year-old boy presents with severe hypersensitivity to cold air/brushing and rapid occlusal enamel crumbling (\u0027post-eruptive breakdown\u0027) on three permanent first molars (16, 36, 46) displaying well-demarcated creamy-yellow-to-brown opacities, plus a demarcated white opacity on tooth 11, while his premolars and primary molars are unaffected. What is the diagnosis, and why do conventional composite restorations frequently fail prematurely unless special surface protocols or preformed Stainless Steel Crowns (SSCs) are used?","o":["Amelogenesis imperfecta affecting all primary and permanent teeth uniformly","Endemic dental fluorosis characterised by symmetrical diffuse horizontal white striations across all teeth","Molar-Incisor Hypomineralisation (MIH) — porous hypomineralised enamel retains high organic protein content hindering phosphoric acid-etch prism tags (improved by 5% NaOCl deproteinisation or GIC interim / Hall-technique SSCs for severe breakdown)","Syphilitic Mulberry molars exclusively from congenital Treponema pallidum"],"a":2,"e":"MIH is a systemic developmental hypomineralisation affecting 1 to 4 permanent first molars (and frequently permanent incisors), caused by disturbed ameloblast maturation during the first year of life. Unlike fluorosis (diffuse symmetrical lines), MIH exhibits sharply demarcated white/yellow/brown porous enamel with markedly reduced mineral density and excess residual amelogenin/albumin proteins—causing intense dentine-like sensitivity, poor local anaesthesia, post-eruptive breakdown, and weak composite micromechanical etching (improved by 5% NaOCl deproteinisation, GIC sealing, or full-coverage SSCs).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03191","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Prosthodontics and Crown \u0026 Bridge","topic":"Screw-Retained vs Cement-Retained Implant Crowns","q":"A mandibular first molar single-tooth implant is placed with ideal prosthetically driven axial angulation, and the peri-implant soft-tissue collar depth is 4 mm subgingivally. Why is a Screw-Retained (or Angled Screw-Channel) zirconia/PFM crown generally favoured over a conventional Cement-Retained crown in deep (\u003e3 mm) subgingival implant margins?","o":["Subgingival resin cement naturally osseointegrates into titanium threads beneficially","Cement-retained crowns are impossible to fracture porcelain around occlusal screws and always retrieve effortlessly","Screw-retained crowns require 10 mm of zinc phosphate cement extrusion into the bone","Deep (\u003e3 mm) subgingival crown margins make complete removal of excess luting cement clinically unpredictable—extruded residual cement acts as a foreign-body biofilm nidus precipitating iatrogenic peri-implant mucositis and peri-implantitis, whereas screw retention eliminates cement entirely and ensures effortless retrievability"],"a":3,"e":"Wilson (2009) demonstrated that excess subgingival dental cement was associated with \u003e80% of cement-retained peri-implant disease cases, and Linkevicius proved residual cement remains undetected on radiographs/probing whenever margin depth exceeds 2-3 mm subgingivally. Screw retention (or custom-milled abutments keeping margins \u003c=1 mm subgingival/equigingival if cemented) completely avoids subgingival cement extrusion and allows non-destructive screw retrieval for screw-loosening checks or hygiene.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03192","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopaedics","topic":"Björk\u0027s Structural Signs of Mandibular Growth Rotation","q":"Using Arne Björk\u0027s implant-derived structural method on a lateral cephalogram to predict whether a growing patient exhibits unfavourable backward (clockwise) versus forward (counterclockwise) internal mandibular rotation, which combination of radiographic features indicates BACKWARD (clockwise) mandibular rotation and vertical open-bite tendency?","o":["Posteriorly inclined condylar head, straight/flat mandibular canal curvature, pronounced deep antegonial notch beneath the mandibular angle, backward-inclined symphysis, and steep mandibular plane angle","Complete fusion of all cranial sutures at age 7","Purely forward projection of nasal spine with zero condylar inclination change","Anteriorly/upwardly curved condylar head, strongly curved mandibular canal, absent/minimal antegonial notch, and deep skeletal overbite"],"a":0,"e":"Björk\u0027s 7 structural signs assess true internal mandibular matrix rotation (which surface remodelling often masks): Backward (clockwise, hyperdivergent) rotators exhibit backward/posterior condylar growth inclination, a flat/straight inferior alveolar canal, heavy apposition under the mandibular angle creating a prominent deep antegonial notch, thin elongated backward-tilted symphysis, increased lower anterior facial height, and open-bite tendency. Forward (counterclockwise, hypodivergent) rotators display forward condylar inclination, curved canal, absent antegonial notch, and deep-bite tendency.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03193","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery","topic":"Conventional \u0026 Mural Unicystic Ameloblastoma Resection Margins","q":"A 38-year-old man presents with a large multilocular follicular conventional ameloblastoma of the right posterior mandible involving the inferior alveolar canal and causing focal lingual cortical perforation. Why is simple enucleation and curettage associated with a \u003e60-80% recurrence rate here, and what surgical protocol achieves \u003c10% recurrence?","o":["Perform simple needle aspiration and 10-day oral amoxicillin only","Microscopic tumour islands infiltrate 2-8 mm into surrounding cancellous medullary bone spaces beyond radiographic borders; perform segmental/marginal resection with 1.0-1.5 cm clear radiographic/anatomical bone margins (and excise overlying perforated periosteum/mucosa) followed by vascularised free-fibula or reconstruction-plate reconstruction","Perform total bilateral maxillectomy with zero mandibular resection","Perform simple marsupialisation with saline irrigation alone as definitive lifelong cure"],"a":1,"e":"Unlike purely intraluminal/plexiform luminal unicystic ameloblastoma (Type 1/2), conventional solid/multicystic ameloblastoma (and Type 3 mural-invasive unicystic ameloblastoma) infiltrates unresorbable cancellous bone trabeculae well beyond the visible radiographic margin. Simple enucleation/curettage leaves microscopic tumour nests behind (~60-90% recurrence), so radical resection with 1.0-1.5 cm linear bone margins (plus supraperiosteal soft-tissue excision where cortex is perforated) and reconstruction (e.g., vascularised fibula free flap) is standard.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03194","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Periodontology","topic":"Root Resection (Amputation) vs Hemisection","q":"A mandibular first molar presents with severe Grade III furcation bone loss and an isolated deep vertical defect destroying bone completely around the distal root, while the mesial root retains \u003e75% healthy alveolar bone support and adequate root-canal anatomy. After completing endodontic therapy on the mesial root, which resective surgical procedure removes BOTH the compromised distal root AND its corresponding coronal crown half (converting the retained mesial half into a premolar-sized abutment)?","o":["Subgingival root planing alone without surgical sectioning","Root amputation (resecting a single maxillary root apical to the CEJ while keeping the entire coronal crown intact)","Hemisection (surgical vertical sectioning of the mandibular molar through the furcation with extraction of the diseased distal root + coronal half)","Gingivectomy of keratinised gingiva only"],"a":2,"e":"Periodontal root-resective therapy distinguishes two techniques: (1) Hemisection splits a two-rooted mandibular molar buccolingually right through the furcation, extracting the hopeless half (root + overlying crown portion) and restoring the remaining healthy root half as a premolarised crown/FPD abutment (bicuspidisation separates both halves without extraction); (2) Root Amputation (Root Resection) removes only the diseased root (most commonly the distobuccal or mesiobuccal root of a three-rooted maxillary molar) at the furcation while preserving the full coronal crown intact.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03195","course":"MDS - Dental Specialisation","subject":"Conservative Dentistry and Endodontics","topic":"Simon, Glick \u0026 Frank Classification of Endo-Perio Lesions","q":"A 34-year-old patient presents with a solitary, narrow, deep 10-mm probing defect on the mid-buccal aspect of a non-vital mandibular first molar (negative to cold and EPT), accompanied by a furcation radiolucency. Full-mouth periodontal charting reveals healthy 1-2 mm sulci elsewhere with minimal plaque/calculus and intact interproximal bone on adjacent teeth. Per Simon, Glick, and Frank\u0027s classification, what is the diagnosis and correct treatment sequence?","o":["Aggressive localised periodontitis — prescribe systemic doxycycline alone without RCT","True combined endo-perio lesion requiring immediate tooth extraction","Primary periodontal lesion with intact vital pulp — perform immediate open-flap periodontal bone grafting without RCT","Primary endodontic lesion (draining coronally through the periodontal ligament space mimicking a furcation/periodontal pocket) — perform non-surgical Root Canal Therapy ALONE first and re-evaluate healing after 2-3 months before any periodontal surgery"],"a":3,"e":"In a Primary Endodontic Lesion (Simon, Glick \u0026 Frank Type I), pulpal necrosis discharges exudate via the apical foramen or furcation accessory canals along the PDL space to exit at the gingival sulcus—creating a narrow, deep, isolated probing defect without generalised periodontitis or calculus. Because the pocket is purely an endodontic sinus tract rather than true plaque-induced attachment loss, thorough non-surgical Root Canal Therapy alone promotes rapid reattachment and complete bone healing within weeks to months without periodontal surgery (and avoids damaging healthy cementum via premature root planing).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03196","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine \u0026 Allied PG","topic":"Acute Coronary Syndromes \u0026 Cardiogenic Shock","q":"A 62-year-old diabetic man presents with inferior ST-elevation myocardial infarction (leads II, III, aVF), hypotension (BP 82/54 mmHg), elevated jugular venous pressure with Kussmaul sign, and clear lung fields. Lead V4R shows 1.5 mm ST elevation. Which intervention is CONTRAINDICATED and what is the preferred immediate haemodynamic support?","o":["Avoid nitrates and loop diuretics; administer cautious isotonic crystalloid bolus to maintain RV preload alongside urgent primary PCI","Initiate high-dose beta-blocker IV bolus before assessing volume status","Perform immediate thrombolysis with streptokinase only if V4R ST elevation resolves after sublingual nitroglycerin","Administer IV nitroglycerin infusion and IV furosemide 40 mg immediately to reduce venous congestion"],"a":0,"e":"Inferior STEMI with hypotension, elevated JVP (Kussmaul sign), clear lung fields, and \u003e=1 mm ST elevation in right-sided lead V4R diagnoses right ventricular myocardial infarction (RVMI). RVMI is exquisitely preload-dependent; venodilators (nitrates, morphine) and diuretics drop preload precipitously and cause profound refractory shock. Treatment requires gentle IV fluid loading to preserve RV filling and prompt reperfusion (primary PCI).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03197","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine \u0026 Allied PG","topic":"Acid-Base \u0026 Electrolyte Disorders","q":"A 54-year-old woman on thiazide diuretics presents with chronic symptomatic hyponatraemia (serum Na+ 112 mEq/L, weight 60 kg, total body water ~30 L). To minimise the risk of osmotic demyelination syndrome (ODS), what is the recommended MAXIMUM correction limit of serum sodium in the first 24 hours?","o":["Do not exceed 8-10 mEq/L in the first 24 hours (typically targeting 4-6 mEq/L initial rise for severe symptoms, \u003c=8 mEq/L/24h)","Correct rapidly by 18-20 mEq/L in the first 24 hours to normalise Na+ above 130 mEq/L","Limit correction strictly to 0.5 mEq/L over 72 hours using oral water loading alone","Infuse 3% NaCl at 500 mL/hr until serum Na+ reaches 135 mEq/L"],"a":0,"e":"In chronic hyponatraemia (\u003e48 hours), brain cells adapt by losing organic osmolytes. Overly rapid correction (\u003e10-12 mEq/L in 24 h or \u003e18 mEq/L in 48 h) precipitates osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis). Current PG medicine guidelines recommend an initial 4-6 mEq/L rise for severe neurological symptoms and capping total 24-hour correction at \u003c=8-10 mEq/L (with \u003c=8 mEq/L preferred in high-risk malnourished/hypokalaemic/alcoholic patients).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03198","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery, OBG \u0026 Surgical PG","topic":"Damage Control Surgery \u0026 Lethal Triad","q":"During emergency laparotomy for massive hepatic and mesenteric trauma, which physiological triad defines the \"lethal triad\" mandating immediate transition to Damage Control Surgery (temporary packing and abbreviated closure)?","o":["Hyperthermia, respiratory alkalosis, and thrombocytosis","Hypothermia, metabolic acidosis, and coagulopathy","Hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respiration","Hypocalcaemia, hyperkalaemia, and metabolic alkalosis"],"a":1,"e":"Prolonged operative exposure and massive resuscitation trigger the self-perpetuating lethal triad: hypothermia (\u003c35 C), acidosis (pH \u003c7.2, lactate elevation), and coagulopathy. Damage control surgery arrests haemorrhage and contamination rapidly via packing and temporary abdominal closure, followed by ICU physiological resuscitation before definitive repair.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03199","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery, OBG \u0026 Surgical PG","topic":"Placenta Accreta Spectrum (PAS)","q":"A 33-year-old G3P2 with two prior lower-segment caesarean deliveries has an anterior placenta praevia on 32-week ultrasound showing loss of the retroplacental hypoechoic clear zone, multiple intraplacental lacunae, and uterovesical hypervascularity on colour Doppler. What is the safest planned delivery strategy?","o":["Attempt vigorous manual removal of placenta after delivery at 39 weeks to preserve fertility at all costs","Perform classical fundal incision with routine placental curettage prior to uterine closure","Scheduled caesarean hysterectomy at 34+0 to 35+6 weeks in a tertiary multidisciplinary centre, leaving the placenta undisturbed in situ without attempting manual separation","Wait for spontaneous onset of labour at 40 weeks and proceed with assisted vaginal delivery"],"a":2,"e":"Prior caesarean sections + anterior placenta praevia with lacunae, loss of clear zone, and uterovesical Doppler bridging strongly indicate Placenta Accreta Spectrum (PAS). Attempting forcible manual removal triggers catastrophic obstetric haemorrhage. Standard management (Williams Obstetrics / FOGSI-ICMR) is planned late-preterm (34+0 to 35+6 weeks) delivery at a tertiary centre with blood bank, interventional/urology support, making a uterine incision avoiding the placenta, and proceeding directly to caesarean hysterectomy with the placenta left in situ.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03200","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Diagnostic \u0026 Paraclinical PG (Path/Micro/Radio/Anaesth)","topic":"Local Anaesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST)","q":"Minutes after receiving an ultrasound-guided brachial plexus block with 0.5% bupivacaine, a 70-kg patient develops perioral numbness, tinnitus, generalised seizures, and rapidly progresses to ventricular tachycardia. Alongside ACLS airway/ventilation management (avoiding vasopressin/CCBs/lidocaine), what specific antidote and initial bolus dose should be administered?","o":["Dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg IV rapid infusion","Protamine sulphate 1 mg per 100 units IV","Flumazenil 0.5 mg IV bolus followed by atropine 3 mg","20% intravenous lipid emulsion (Intralipid) 1.5 mL/kg bolus (~100 mL) over 2-3 minutes followed by 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion"],"a":3,"e":"Bupivacaine carries the highest cardiotoxicity among commonly used local anaesthetics via potent Na+ channel blockade. Local Anaesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST) is treated immediately with 20% lipid emulsion (1.5 mL/kg lean body mass IV bolus, ~100 mL for \u003e=70 kg, followed by 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion), reduced epinephrine doses (\u003c1 mcg/kg), and avoidance of lidocaine, beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, and vasopressin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03201","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics, Community Medicine \u0026 Research Methodology","topic":"Neonatal Resuscitation \u0026 Hypoxic-Ischaemic Encephalopathy","q":"For an infant born at \u003e=36 weeks gestation with moderate-to-severe Hypoxic-Ischaemic Encephalopathy (HIE), within what time window after birth must therapeutic hypothermia (target core temperature 33.5 C for 72 hours) be initiated to achieve neuroprotection?","o":["Within 6 hours of birth","Between 24 and 48 hours of birth","Within 7 days of birth","Only after 72 hours of normothermic observation"],"a":0,"e":"Therapeutic hypothermia (33.5 C +/- 0.5 C for 72 hours followed by slow rewarming over \u003e=6 hours) must be started within 6 hours of birth (before secondary energy failure cascades) in eligible infants \u003e=36 weeks (or \u003e=35 weeks in select protocols) with perinatal asphyxia and moderate-to-severe encephalopathy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03202","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Severe Chronic Hyponatraemia","q":"A 52-year-old man with chronic alcohol use disorder and malnutrition presents with confusion and a witnessed generalised seizure. Serum Na+ is 107 mEq/L, K+ 2.8 mEq/L, and serum osmolality 222 mOsm/kg. After an initial 100 mL IV bolus of 3% NaCl stops the seizure and raises Na+ to 111 mEq/L, he develops brisk water diuresis (urine output 350 mL/h, urine osmolality 90 mOsm/kg) and repeat Na+ at 10 hours is 116 mEq/L. What is the safest immediate management?","o":["Continue 3% NaCl infusion until serum Na+ reaches 125 mEq/L within 24 hours","Stop 3% NaCl, administer IV desmopressin (DDAVP) and 5% dextrose (D5W) to re-lower Na+ within \u003c= 8 mEq/L/24 h, while repleting K+","Restrict all fluids without desmopressin and monitor Na+ every 24 hours","Administer IV mannitol to accelerate free-water diuresis"],"a":1,"e":"Chronic alcohol use, malnutrition, hypokalaemia, and baseline Na+ \u003c= 105-110 mEq/L place this patient at extreme risk for Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS). Although an initial 4-6 mEq/L rise via 3% NaCl bolus treats seizures, total correction MUST NOT exceed 8 mEq/L in 24 hours in high-risk patients (here already +9 mEq/L at 10 hours due to aquaresis plus K+ repletion). Stopping hypertonic saline and giving DDAVP + D5W arrests water diuresis and re-lowers Na+ safely back below the 8 mEq/L 24-hour ceiling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03203","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"ANCA-Associated Vasculitis","q":"A 46-year-old man presents with haemoptysis, epistaxis, bilateral nodular pulmonary infiltrates with diffuse alveolar haemorrhage, serum creatinine 4.1 mg/dL, dysmorphic RBCs with RBC casts, and strongly positive c-ANCA (anti-PR3). Renal biopsy shows pauci-immune necrotising crescentic glomerulonephritis. Which induction regimen is standard of care for organ- and life-threatening disease?","o":["Oral prednisolone 10 mg/day with hydroxychloroquine monotherapy","IV Ceftriaxone plus diuretics while awaiting repeat ANCA titres at 6 weeks","High-dose IV pulse methylprednisolone plus Rituximab (or Cyclophosphamide), with consideration of plasma exchange for severe alveolar haemorrhage","Mycophenolate mofetil monotherapy without corticosteroids"],"a":2,"e":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA, c-ANCA/PR3 positive) presenting with rapidly progressive pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis and diffuse alveolar haemorrhage is organ- and life-threatening. Immediate induction requires high-dose pulse IV glucocorticoids (methylprednisolone) combined with either Rituximab (non-inferior/preferred in relapsing PR3-ANCA) or Cyclophosphamide, with PJP prophylaxis and consideration of plasmapheresis in refractory severe pulmonary haemorrhage or dialysis-requiring renal failure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03204","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Sepsis and Septic Shock","q":"A 65-year-old woman with urosepsis has MAP 54 mmHg and arterial lactate 4.8 mmol/L despite receiving 30 mL/kg of balanced crystalloid over 3 hours. Broad-spectrum IV meropenem has been started after cultures. Which vasoactive sequence is recommended to achieve and maintain a target MAP \u003e= 65 mmHg?","o":["Start Dopamine as first-line vasopressor; add Phenylephrine if tachycardic","Start Dobutamine monotherapy to raise MAP via beta-2 vasodilation","Administer another 60 mL/kg crystalloid bolus before starting any vasopressor","Start Norepinephrine as first-line vasopressor; add fixed-dose Vasopressin (up to 0.03 U/min) if MAP remains \u003c 65 mmHg despite escalating norepinephrine"],"a":3,"e":"Per Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines, Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor for septic shock targeting MAP \u003e= 65 mmHg. When norepinephrine requirements escalate (e.g., 0.25-0.5 mcg/kg/min), adding fixed-dose Vasopressin (up to 0.03 units/min) rather than further escalating norepinephrine alone reduces catecholamine burden and arrhythmia risk; IV hydrocortisone 200 mg/day is added for ongoing refractory shock.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03205","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Damage Control Surgery (DCS)","q":"During emergency laparotomy for a high-velocity road traffic accident, a 32-year-old man has a Grade IV right hepatic laceration with active venous bleeding and a mid-ileal perforation. After 45 minutes in the operating theatre, core temperature is 34.1 degrees C, arterial pH is 7.13, base deficit is -13 mEq/L, and diffuse oozing is noted from all raw surfaces (INR 2.2). What is the most appropriate intraoperative decision?","o":["Perihepatic packing, rapid stapled control of the ileal perforation without anastomosis, and temporary abdominal closure followed by ICU resuscitation","Proceed with formal right hepatic lobectomy and hand-sewn primary ileo-ileal anastomosis","Administer protamine sulphate and perform Pringle manoeuvre continuously for 2 hours","Close the abdomen definitively after placing two corrugated drains without packing"],"a":0,"e":"This patient is in the trauma \u0027lethal triad\u0027 (hypothermia, metabolic acidosis, and coagulopathy). Prolonged definitive anatomic resection or bowel anastomosis carries prohibitive mortality. Damage Control Surgery (DCS) mandates immediate haemorrhage control (perihepatic packing), contamination control (rapid stapled resection/ligation leaving bowel in discontinuity), temporary abdominal closure (e.g., Bogota bag/VAC), ICU physiological restoration (balanced 1:1:1 transfusion, warming), and planned re-exploration at 24-48 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03206","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Acute Mesenteric Ischaemia","q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic atrial fibrillation (non-adherent to anticoagulation) develops sudden-onset severe periumbilical abdominal pain, two episodes of vomiting, and bloody diarrhoea. His abdomen is soft with minimal tenderness despite agonising pain, serum lactate is 5.4 mmol/L, and arterial pH is 7.26. Triphasic CT angiography shows an abrupt filling defect 6 cm distal to the origin of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA). After IV heparin and resuscitation, what is the surgical principle?","o":["Colonoscopy to rule out haemorrhoids","Urgent SMA embolectomy (or endovascular thrombolysis/aspiration) first, followed by assessment of bowel viability and resection of only frankly necrotic segments","Conservative bowel rest and oral warfarin for 7 days","Immediate total enterectomy without attempting SMA revascularisation"],"a":1,"e":"Acute SMA embolism classically presents in patients with atrial fibrillation as severe abdominal pain out of proportion to physical signs, metabolic acidosis, and elevated lactate. The embolus typically lodges distal to the middle colic artery origin (sparing proximal jejunum). Prompt anticoagulation and urgent revascularisation (open Fogarty embolectomy or hybrid/endovascular approach) BEFORE judging final bowel viability salvages borderline ischaemic bowel, often with a planned second-look laparotomy at 24-48 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03207","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Acute Severe Pancreatitis","q":"A 44-year-old man with severe alcoholic necrotising pancreatitis develops persistent high fever and worsening leucocytosis during week 4 of hospitalisation. Contrast-enhanced CT abdomen demonstrates a well-demarcated 11 cm retroperitoneal walled-off necrosis (WON) containing intralesional gas bubbles abutting the posterior gastric wall. Which management strategy yields the lowest mortality and complication rate?","o":["Immediate total pancreatectomy with splenectomy","Immediate open transperitoneal laparotomy and total pancreatic necrosectomy on day 1","IV carbapenem therapy plus image-guided/endoscopic transluminal drainage first (step-up approach), reserving minimally invasive necrosectomy for non-responders","Fine-needle aspiration followed by withholding antibiotics and surgery indefinitely"],"a":2,"e":"Intralesional gas in walled-off necrosis at week 4 confirms infected pancreatic necrosis. Based on the PANTER and TENSION trials, the \u0027step-up\u0027 approach—broad-spectrum antibiotics (e.g., meropenem) combined with primary percutaneous retroperitoneal or endoscopic ultrasound-guided lumen-apposing metal stent drainage—resolves infection in ~35-50% of patients without surgery and restricts necrosectomy (VARD or endoscopic) to non-responders, significantly lowering new-onset organ failure and mortality compared with immediate open necrosectomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03208","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Placenta Accreta Spectrum (PAS)","q":"A 33-year-old gravida 4 para 3 woman with two prior lower-segment Caesarean deliveries undergoes antenatal ultrasound at 31 weeks showing an anterior placenta praevia with loss of the retroplacental hypoechoic clear zone, thinning of the myometrium (\u003c1 mm), and large turbulent placental lacunae with bridging vessels into the bladder wall on colour Doppler. She has had no bleeding episodes. What is the optimal planned delivery strategy?","o":["Routine vaginal induction of labour with misoprostol at 37 weeks","Await spontaneous onset of labour at 40 weeks followed by manual removal of the placenta","Perform classical Caesarean at 38 weeks and forcibly peel the placenta from the uterine wall","Scheduled multidisciplinary Caesarean hysterectomy at 34+0 to 35+6 weeks leaving the placenta undisturbed in situ"],"a":3,"e":"Ultrasound features in a patient with prior Caesarean sections and anterior placenta praevia strongly indicate Placenta Accreta Spectrum (PAS/percreta). Planned delivery by a multidisciplinary team (senior obstetrician, urologist/gynaecologic oncologist, anaesthesiologist, massive transfusion protocol) at 34+0 to 35+6 weeks—via classical/fundal hysterotomy avoiding the placenta, clamping the cord, and performing Caesarean hysterectomy WITHOUT attempting placental removal—minimises catastrophic haemorrhage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03209","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Shoulder Dystocia","q":"Immediately after delivery of the fetal head in a woman with gestational diabetes, the fetal head retracts tightly against the perineum (\u0027turtle sign\u0027) and gentle downward traction fails to deliver the anterior shoulder. Which sequence of initial manoeuvres should be performed FIRST, and which action is strictly contraindicated?","o":["McRoberts manoeuvre (hyperflexion of maternal hips) plus suprapubic pressure; avoid fundal pressure","Administer IV oxytocin bolus to augment expulsive contractions","Apply vigorous fundal pressure plus strong downward traction on the fetal head","Perform immediate symphysiotomy as the first manoeuvre"],"a":0,"e":"Shoulder dystocia is an obstetric emergency caused by impaction of the anterior shoulder behind the maternal symphysis pubis. First-line manoeuvres (releasing \u003e40-50% of cases) are McRoberts manoeuvre (sharp flexion and abduction of maternal thighs onto the abdomen to flatten sacral promontory and rotate symphysis) combined with continuous/rocking suprapubic pressure (Rubin I) to adduct the anterior shoulder. Fundal pressure is strictly contraindicated because it impacts the shoulder further and risks uterine rupture and fetal brachial plexus injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03210","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)","q":"A 38-year-old man with severe viral pneumonia is intubated in the ICU. Height is 175 cm (Predicted Body Weight 70 kg). Chest X-ray shows bilateral diffuse alveolar infiltrates, echocardiography shows normal LV ejection fraction, and on FiO2 0.80 with PEEP 14 cmH2O, PaO2 is 76 mmHg (PaO2/FiO2 = 95 mmHg). Which ventilator strategy and adjunct are evidence-based to reduce mortality?","o":["Tidal volume 10-12 mL/kg actual body weight to normalise PaCO2 to 35 mmHg","Tidal volume 4-6 mL/kg PBW (280-420 mL), plateau pressure \u003c= 30 cmH2O, and early prone positioning for 12-16 hours/day","Zero PEEP (ZEEP) with high-frequency percussive ventilation as primary mode","Routine prolonged neuromuscular paralysis with FiO2 1.0 in supine position indefinitely"],"a":1,"e":"This patient has severe ARDS (PaO2/FiO2 \u003c= 100 mmHg, non-cardiogenic bilateral opacities). The ARDSNet lung-protective strategy—low tidal volume 4-6 mL/kg Predicted Body Weight (PBW), plateau pressure \u003c= 30 cmH2O (keeping driving pressure \u003c 15 cmH2O), adequate PEEP, and permissive hypercapnia (pH \u003e= 7.20)—combined with early prone positioning for \u003e= 12-16 hours/day (PROSEVA trial for P/F \u003c 150) significantly reduces ventilator-induced lung injury and mortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03211","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Malignant Hyperthermia","q":"Fifteen minutes after induction of general anaesthesia with succinylcholine and maintenance with sevoflurane in a 22-year-old man, end-tidal CO2 rises precipitously from 36 to 74 mmHg despite increased minute ventilation, accompanied by masseter muscle rigidity, sinus tachycardia (144/min), and rapidly rising core temperature (39.6 degrees C). Beyond stopping sevoflurane and hyperventilating with 100% O2, what is the specific antidote and its mechanism?","o":["IV Sugammadex (16 mg/kg); encapsulates sevoflurane molecules in plasma","IV Neostigmine with glycopyrrolate; restores neuromuscular junction acetylcholine","IV Dantrolene sodium (2.5 mg/kg initial bolus); inhibits ryanodine receptor (RyR1)-mediated Ca2+ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","IV Calcium chloride 10%; stabilises skeletal muscle sarcolemma"],"a":2,"e":"Malignant hyperthermia is a pharmacogenetic hypermetabolic crisis triggered by volatile anaesthetics and succinylcholine in patients with RYR1 (or CACNA1S) mutations. An unexplained steep rise in ETCO2 is the earliest sensitive indicator, followed by rigidity, tachycardia, mixed acidosis, hyperkalaemia, rhabdomyolysis, and hyperthermia. Immediate treatment stops all triggers, delivers 100% O2 via clean circuit/charcoal filters, and gives IV Dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg (repeated up to 10 mg/kg or more until control), which blocks RyR1 calcium release.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03212","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Hepatocellular Carcinoma","q":"A 59-year-old man with hepatitis B-related liver cirrhosis undergoes multiphasic contrast-enhanced MRI showing a 2.8 cm Segment VIII hepatic observation demonstrating non-rim arterial phase hyperenhancement (APHE), non-peripheral washout appearance in the portal venous phase, and an enhancing capsule in the delayed phase. According to ACR LI-RADS v2018 criteria, how is this lesion categorised and managed?","o":["LR-3 (Intermediate probability); repeat ultrasound in 6 months without treatment","LR-1 (Definitely benign haemangioma); discharge from surveillance","LR-M (Probably malignant, not specific for HCC); mandatory core biopsy","LR-5 (Definitely Hepatocellular Carcinoma); biopsy is not required to establish diagnosis before definitive HCC therapy"],"a":3,"e":"In a high-risk cirrhotic/chronic HBV liver, a \u003e= 20 mm observation with non-rim arterial phase hyperenhancement (APHE) plus at least one major feature—non-peripheral washout OR enhancing capsule (here both are present)—is classified as LI-RADS 5 (LR-5: Definitely HCC). LR-5 has near-100% specificity for HCC, allowing staging and curative/locoregional therapy without tissue biopsy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03213","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Intracranial Haemorrhage","q":"A 49-year-old woman presents 3 hours after an abrupt \u0027worst headache of her life\u0027 with photophobia and neck stiffness. Non-contrast CT (NCCT) head reveals hyperdense material filling the suprasellar cistern, bilateral Sylvian fissures, and interhemispheric fissure. What is the immediate vascular imaging test of choice and medical prophylaxis against delayed cerebral ischaemia?","o":["CT angiography (CTA) or Digital Subtraction Angiography (DSA) to locate the ruptured aneurysm, plus oral Nimodipine 60 mg every 4 hours","Carotid Doppler ultrasound, plus high-dose IV heparin","Repeat NCCT after 48 hours without vascular imaging, plus mannitol alone","MRI brain with gadolinium alone after 72 hours, plus IV Alteplase"],"a":0,"e":"Hyperdensity in the basal cisterns and Sylvian fissures on early NCCT confirms acute subarachnoid haemorrhage (SAH), most commonly from a ruptured saccular aneurysm (anterior communicating, PCom, or MCA bifurcation). Prompt CTA (and/or 4-vessel DSA, the gold standard for endovascular coiling/surgical clipping planning) identifies the culprit aneurysm. Oral Nimodipine (60 mg q4h for 21 days) is the only pharmacologic therapy proven to improve neurological outcomes by reducing delayed cerebral ischaemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03214","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Research Methodology and Biostatistics","topic":"ROC Curve and Likelihood Ratios","q":"In a validation study of a serum biomarker for sepsis, a chosen ROC curve threshold yields Sensitivity = 85% (0.85) and Specificity = 80% (0.80). What are Youden\u0027s J statistic (Youden\u0027s index) and the Positive Likelihood Ratio (LR+) at this threshold?","o":["Youden\u0027s J = 0.05, LR+ = 5.67","Youden\u0027s J = 0.65, LR+ = 4.25","Youden\u0027s J = 0.825, LR+ = 1.06","Youden\u0027s J = 0.65, LR+ = 0.19"],"a":1,"e":"Youden\u0027s index (J) = Sensitivity + Specificity - 1 = 0.85 + 0.80 - 1 = 0.65 (maximising J identifies the optimal ROC cutoff giving equal weight to sensitivity and specificity). Positive Likelihood Ratio (LR+) = Sensitivity / (1 - Specificity) = 0.85 / (1 - 0.80) = 0.85 / 0.20 = 4.25, meaning a positive test is 4.25 times more likely in septic than non-septic patients.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03215","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Research Methodology and Biostatistics","topic":"Time-to-Event Outcomes","q":"A multicentre oncology trial follows 420 patients for 5 years to compare overall survival between two chemotherapy regimens, with patients lost to follow-up or alive at study closure handled by right-censoring. Which pair of methods is appropriate for (1) unadjusted comparison of the two Kaplan-Meier survival curves and (2) multivariable adjustment for age, tumour stage, and ECOG performance status?","o":["(1) Mann-Whitney U test; (2) One-way ANOVA","(1) Unpaired Student\u0027s t-test; (2) Linear regression yielding Beta coefficients","(1) Log-rank test; (2) Cox proportional hazards regression yielding Hazard Ratios","(1) Chi-square test of proportions; (2) Poisson regression ignoring follow-up duration"],"a":2,"e":"Time-to-event data with right-censoring cannot be analysed accurately by simple proportions or means because follow-up durations differ. Kaplan-Meier curves estimate survival probabilities over time, the non-parametric Log-rank test compares entire survival distributions between groups, and Cox proportional hazards regression adjusts simultaneously for multiple baseline covariates to estimate adjusted Hazard Ratios (HR) under the proportional hazards assumption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03216","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Cardiomyopathies","q":"A 24-year-old male athlete with asymmetric septal hypertrophy (maximal LV wall thickness 32 mm on cardiac MRI) experiences an unexplained episode of syncope during exertion. Resting LV outflow tract gradient is 65 mmHg, and Holter shows nonsustained ventricular tachycardia. What is the definitive sudden cardiac death (SCD) prevention and appropriate medical therapy for symptoms?","o":["Permanent dual-chamber pacemaker without ICD capability","High-dose oral nifedipine and competitive sprint sports clearance","Oral digoxin plus IV nitroglycerin for LVOT gradient reduction","Implantable Cardioverter-Defibrillator (ICD) placement plus non-vasodilating beta-blocker (e.g., metoprolol) or verapamil; avoid digoxin and nitrates"],"a":3,"e":"This patient has multiple major risk factors for SCD in Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy: extreme LV hypertrophy (\u003e= 30 mm), unexplained exertional syncope, and NSVT—strongly indicating primary prevention ICD implantation. Obstructive HCM is worsened by preload/afterload reduction or positive inotropy; thus, beta-blockers (or disopyramide/mavacamten) are first-line to reduce heart rate and LVOT gradient, while digoxin, ACEi/ARBs, and nitrates are avoided.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03217","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Venous Thromboembolism","q":"A 55-year-old woman 10 days after knee arthroplasty develops sudden severe dyspnoea, BP 76/48 mmHg, HR 132/min, SpO2 84%, and elevated JVP. Bedside echocardiography reveals severe right ventricular (RV) dilatation, McConnell\u0027s sign, and septal flattening; CTPA confirms saddle pulmonary embolism. She has no bleeding contraindications. Beyond oxygen and cautious fluid/norepinephrine support, what is the definitive emergency therapy?","o":["Systemic thrombolysis (e.g., IV Alteplase 100 mg over 2 hours) plus anticoagulation","Oral rivaroxaban 15 mg BID and discharge after 6 hours","Low-molecular-weight heparin (enoxaparin) alone without reperfusion","High-volume crystalloid resuscitation (4 litres over 1 hour) without thrombolysis"],"a":0,"e":"Acute pulmonary embolism with sustained hypotension (SBP \u003c 90 mmHg or vasopressor requirement) defines Massive (High-Risk) PE, carrying \u003e25-50% mortality without rapid clot dissolution. Systemic thrombolysis (IV alteplase 100 mg over 2 h or weight-based tenecteplase) rapidly reduces pulmonary artery pressure and restores RV output; surgical/catheter embolectomy is used if thrombolysis is contraindicated or fails.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03218","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Drug-Induced Thrombocytopenia","q":"Seven days after starting unfractionated heparin (UFH) following vascular surgery, a 63-year-old man\u0027s platelet count drops from 260,000/mm3 to 72,000/mm3, and he develops an acute deep vein thrombosis in his left leg (4Ts score = 7, high probability). Anti-PF4/heparin ELISA is sent. What is the immediate mandatory management?","o":["Continue UFH at half-dose and initiate high-dose warfarin 10 mg today","Stop ALL heparin (including flushes), start a non-heparin parenteral anticoagulant (argatroban, bivalirudin, or fondaparinux), and avoid platelet transfusion and immediate warfarin","Switch UFH to low-molecular-weight heparin (enoxaparin) and transfuse 6 units of random-donor platelets","Stop heparin without starting any alternative anticoagulant"],"a":1,"e":"High-probability HIT (50% platelet drop at days 5-10 with new thrombosis) is a prothrombotic immune disorder mediated by IgG antibodies against PF4-heparin complexes activating platelets. Management requires immediate cessation of ALL heparin sources AND initiation of therapeutic non-heparin anticoagulation (argatroban, bivalirudin, danaparoid, or fondaparinux). Switching to LMWH cross-reacts, platelet transfusions \u0027fuel the fire\u0027 of thrombosis, and early warfarin depletes Protein C causing venous limb gangrene.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03219","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Opportunistic CNS Infections in HIV","q":"A 34-year-old ART-naive man with HIV (CD4 count 28 cells/mm3) presents with subacute headache, diplopia, and papilloedema. Lumbar puncture reveals opening pressure 34 cmH2O, India ink positive encapsulated yeasts, and positive CSF cryptococcal antigen. Induction therapy with liposomal amphotericin B plus flucytosine and daily therapeutic LPs for raised ICP are started. When should antiretroviral therapy (TLD) be initiated?","o":["Start ART only after 12 months of fluconazole maintenance","Withhold ART permanently in patients with cryptococcal meningitis","Defer ART initiation for 4 to 6 weeks after starting antifungal induction therapy to reduce fatal CNS IRIS","Start ART immediately on Day 1 alongside amphotericin B"],"a":2,"e":"Based on the COAT trial and WHO/NACO HIV guidelines, initiating ART very early (\u003c1-2 weeks) during cryptococcal meningitis significantly increases mortality due to severe, life-threatening CNS Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome (IRIS) in the closed cranial vault. ART should be deferred until 4-6 weeks after effective antifungal induction (amphotericin B + flucytosine, followed by fluconazole consolidation) when CSF fungal burden is controlled.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03220","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Rectal Carcinoma","q":"A 56-year-old man presents with bleeding per rectum and tenesmus. Colonoscopy and biopsy confirm adenocarcinoma 7 cm from the anal verge (mid-rectum). Pelvic MRI shows a T3 tumour invading perirectal fat within 1 mm of the mesorectal fascia (threatened circumferential resection margin) with 2 suspicious mesorectal lymph nodes (cT3N1M0); CT chest/abdomen shows no distant metastases. What is the optimal sequence of treatment?","o":["Immediate abdominoperineal resection without preoperative therapy","Local transanal excision alone","Palliative diverting loop colostomy without oncological resection","Neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy (or total neoadjuvant therapy) followed by Total Mesorectal Excision (TME)"],"a":3,"e":"Locally advanced mid/low rectal cancer (cT3/T4 or N+ or threatened mesorectal fascia/CRM) requires preoperative neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy (e.g., long-course 50.4 Gy with capecitabine/5-FU, or total neoadjuvant therapy) to downstage the tumour, sterilise the threatened circumferential resection margin (CRM), and minimise local recurrence, followed by sharp dissection in the \u0027holy plane\u0027—Total Mesorectal Excision (TME).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03221","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Locally Advanced Breast Cancer","q":"A 45-year-old premenopausal woman has a 4.5 cm invasive ductal carcinoma of the right breast with mobile ipsilateral axillary lymphadenopathy (cT2N1M0). Core biopsy shows ER negative, PR negative, and HER2/neu 3+ on immunohistochemistry. Before initiating neoadjuvant chemotherapy combined with trastuzumab and pertuzumab, which baseline organ assessment is mandatory?","o":["Baseline Echocardiography/MUGA scan for Left Ventricular Ejection Fraction (LVEF) monitoring","24-hour urinary 5-HIAA","Serum anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies","Routine bone marrow trephine biopsy"],"a":0,"e":"Node-positive or \u003e= 2 cm HER2-positive breast cancer is treated with neoadjuvant chemotherapy plus dual anti-HER2 blockade (Trastuzumab + Pertuzumab) to maximise pathological complete response (pCR). Because anti-HER2 monoclonal antibodies carry a reversible risk of cardiotoxicity (LV systolic dysfunction, amplified if given concurrently with anthracyclines), baseline and 3-monthly LVEF evaluation via echocardiography or MUGA scan is mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03222","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Oesophageal Perforation","q":"A 51-year-old man presents 4 hours after forceful vomiting following a heavy alcohol binge with severe retrosternal chest pain radiating to the back, tachypnoea, and palpable subcutaneous emphysema in the suprasternal notch (Mackler\u0027s triad). Chest X-ray shows pneumomediastinum and a left-sided pleural effusion (pleural fluid amylase 1,800 U/L). Which contrast agent should be used FIRST if an oesophagogram is performed to confirm and localise the perforation?","o":["No imaging; discharge with proton pump inhibitors","Water-soluble contrast (e.g., Gastrografin / diatrizoate) initially, avoiding barium as first-line due to mediastinitis risk","Oral methylene blue followed by blind nasogastric tube insertion","Barium sulphate suspension immediately as first-line"],"a":1,"e":"Effort-induced full-thickness oesophageal rupture (Boerhaave syndrome, classically left posterolateral lower third) presents with Mackler\u0027s triad (vomiting, lower thoracic pain, subcutaneous emphysema) and high-salivary-amylase left pleural effusion. Contrast oesophagography (or CT with oral water-soluble contrast) uses water-soluble contrast FIRST because extravasated barium triggers severe chemical mediastinitis (thin barium is only used if water-soluble study is negative despite high suspicion, avoiding aspiration of hyperosmolar Gastrografin).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03223","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Aortic Emergencies","q":"A 73-year-old smoker presents with sudden tearing abdominal and left flank pain, diaphoresis, BP 78/50 mmHg, HR 122/min, and a tender pulsatile mid-abdominal mass. He is alert and oriented. Bedside ultrasound confirms a 7.2 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm with retroperitoneal haematoma. While mobilising the vascular theatre immediately for open repair/EVAR, what is the optimal resuscitation target?","o":["Rapidly infuse 4 litres of normal saline to push SBP \u003e= 140 mmHg before transfer","Administer IV heparin 10,000 IU bolus immediately","Permissive hypotension (target SBP 70-90 mmHg with consciousness maintained) and balanced blood-product readiness rather than aggressive crystalloid normalisation of BP","Delay theatre for formal 3-phase outpatient CT angiography over the next 2 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Ruptured AAA is a surgical emergency (classic triad: abdominal/flank pain, hypotension, pulsatile mass). Over-aggressive crystalloid resuscitation to \u0027normalise\u0027 BP \u003e 100-120 mmHg disrupts the fragile retroperitoneal tamponade clot and induces dilutional coagulopathy, causing fatal free intraperitoneal rupture. Permissive hypotension (maintaining mentation and SBP ~70-90 mmHg) with immediate transfer to OT for aortic cross-clamping or EVAR saves lives.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03224","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Maternal Collapse","q":"During an oxytocin-augmented labour with ruptured membranes, a 31-year-old multipara suddenly develops severe dyspnoea, cyanosis, agitation, profound hypotension (60/30 mmHg), cardiac arrest, and—after initial CPR—torrential oozing from venipuncture sites and the uterus with undetectable fibrinogen and prolonged PT/aPTT (fulminant DIC). What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Supine hypotensive syndrome from aortocaval compression","Simple vasovagal syncope","Uterine inversion without coagulopathy","Amniotic fluid embolism (anaphylactoid syndrome of pregnancy)"],"a":3,"e":"Amniotic fluid embolism is a rare, catastrophic clinical diagnosis characterised by the sudden triad of acute hypoxia/respiratory distress, cardiovascular collapse/arrest, and fulminant consumptive coagulopathy (DIC) occurring during labour, Caesarean delivery, or immediately postpartum. Management is immediate supportive critical care (CPR with left uterine displacement, airway/intubation, vasopressors, massive transfusion 1:1:1 + cryoprecipitate/tranexamic acid, immediate delivery if undelivered).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03225","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Endometrial Carcinoma","q":"A 60-year-old obese, hypertensive woman presents with postmenopausal bleeding. Transvaginal ultrasound shows an irregular endometrial thickness of 14 mm, and Pipelle endometrial biopsy confirms Grade 2 endometrioid adenocarcinoma. MRI pelvis suggests \u003e= 50% myometrial invasion without cervical stromal or distant spread. What is the standard primary surgical management?","o":["Extrafascial total hysterectomy, bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy, peritoneal cytology, and pelvic/para-aortic lymph node assessment (sentinel/lymphadenectomy)","Vaginal hysterectomy alone leaving both ovaries in situ","Preoperative pelvic radiotherapy followed by progesterone tablets without surgery","Diagnostic hysteroscopy and ablation of the endometrium"],"a":0,"e":"Endometrial carcinoma is surgically staged (FIGO): standard primary treatment for medically operable disease is extrafascial total hysterectomy + bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy (BSO) + lymph node staging (sentinel lymph node mapping or pelvic/para-aortic lymphadenectomy, especially indicated for Grade 2-3 or \u003e= 50% myometrial invasion).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03226","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Persistent Pulmonary Hypertension of the Newborn (PPHN)","q":"A term neonate (41 weeks, meconium-stained liquor) is ventilated in the NICU for severe hypoxaemic respiratory failure. Pulse oximetry shows right upper limb (pre-ductal) SpO2 of 91% and lower limb (post-ductal) SpO2 of 76% (gradient 15%). Echocardiography shows suprasystemic pulmonary artery pressure with right-to-left shunting across the ductus arteriosus and structurally normal heart chambers. What is the targeted pulmonary vasodilator of choice?","o":["Inhaled Nitric Oxide (iNO, starting at 20 ppm) alongside optimised lung recruitment and systemic pressure support","Hyperventilation to PaCO2 \u003c 20 mmHg and pH \u003e 7.60","Immediate indomethacin to close the patent ductus arteriosus","Intravenous sodium nitroprusside infusion"],"a":0,"e":"A pre-ductal to post-ductal SpO2 difference \u003e= 5-10% with structurally normal cardiac anatomy and right-to-left ductal shunting confirms PPHN (here secondary to meconium aspiration syndrome). Inhaled Nitric Oxide (iNO, 20 ppm) acts selectively on ventilated alveoli via cGMP to dilate pulmonary vasculature without causing systemic hypotension, improving oxygenation and reducing need for ECMO.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03227","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Thrombotic Microangiopathies","q":"A 4-year-old boy presents with oliguria, pallor, and petechiae 6 days after an episode of bloody diarrhoea. Investigations show Hb 6.8 g/dL, reticulocytes 7%, numerous schistocytes on peripheral smear, platelets 28,000/mm3, elevated LDH, and serum creatinine 2.9 mg/dL. Stool PCR is positive for Shiga toxin-producing Escherichia coli (O157:H7). Which management principle is MOST critical?","o":["Start IV ciprofloxacin plus cotrimoxazole immediately to eradicate E. coli","Supportive fluid/electrolyte management, packed RBC transfusion for symptomatic anaemia, and dialysis if indicated; AVOID antibiotics and routine platelet transfusions","Administer high-dose IV furosemide and loperamide","Transfuse platelets immediately to keep count \u003e 100,000/mm3 despite no active bleeding"],"a":1,"e":"Classic diarrhoea-associated STEC-HUS presents with the triad of microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (schistocytes, negative Coombs, high LDH), thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury. Antibiotics (especially fluoroquinolones/TMP-SMX) lyse bacteria and trigger bacteriophage-mediated Shiga toxin release, worsening HUS; platelet transfusions fuel microvascular thrombosis and are avoided unless active life-threatening bleeding or invasive procedures occur.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03228","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Local Anaesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST)","q":"Two minutes after an ultrasound-guided supraclavicular brachial plexus block with 25 mL of 0.5% bupivacaine, a 45-year-old woman develops circumoral numbness, tinnitus, generalised tonic-clonic seizures, and wide-complex ventricular tachycardia progressing to cardiac arrest. Alongside airway management, CPR, and benzodiazepine for seizures, what is the specific antidote and ACLS modification?","o":["Infuse high-dose propofol 200 mg IV as a lipid source and seizure control","Administer IV lidocaine 1.5 mg/kg for ventricular arrhythmia and full-dose 1 mg epinephrine every 3 minutes","Administer 20% Intravenous Lipid Emulsion (1.5 mL/kg bolus followed by infusion), reduce epinephrine boluses (\u003c 1 mcg/kg), and AVOID vasopressin, lidocaine, and propofol","Administer IV calcium channel blockers and beta-blockers"],"a":2,"e":"Bupivacaine is highly lipophilic and cardiotoxic, causing refractory arrhythmias during Local Anaesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST). ASRA LAST guidelines mandate immediate 20% Lipid Emulsion (\u0027lipid sink\u0027 + myocardial metabolic rescue: 1.5 mL/kg bolus over 1 min + 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion), low-dose epinephrine (\u003c1 mcg/kg) because standard 1 mg boluses impair lipid rescue and worsen arrhythmias, and strict avoidance of vasopressin, calcium-channel blockers, beta-blockers, local anaesthetics (lidocaine), and propofol (which causes profound vasodilation/myocardial depression).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03229","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Unanticipated Difficult Airway","q":"After induction of general anaesthesia and rocuronium paralysis in a 42-year-old man, two attempts at videolaryngoscopy and two attempts at second-generation supraglottic airway insertion fail, two-person facemask ventilation fails, and SpO2 falls rapidly to 68% (\u0027Can\u0027t Intubate, Can\u0027t Oxygenate\u0027 — CICO). According to Difficult Airway Society (DAS) guidelines, what is the immediate life-saving step?","o":["Wait for spontaneous ventilation recovery after giving flumazenil","Administer a third dose of rocuronium and attempt blind nasal intubation","Attempt a fourth direct laryngoscopy with a larger blade while waiting for ENT","Declare CICO and perform immediate Emergency Front-of-Neck Access (eFONA: scalpel-bougie-6.0 mm tube cricothyroidotomy)"],"a":3,"e":"When tracheal intubation, supraglottic airway rescue, and facemask oxygenation all fail in an anaesthetised patient (DAS Plans A, B, and C), Plan D mandates immediately declaring \u0027Can\u0027t Intubate, Can\u0027t Oxygenate\u0027 (CICO) and performing Emergency Front-of-Neck Access (eFONA) via a standardised scalpel-bougie-cuffed 6.0 mm endotracheal tube through the cricothyroid membrane (using the laryngeal handshake).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03230","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Acute Ischaemic Stroke Imaging","q":"A 62-year-old woman (baseline modified Rankin Scale 0) wakes up with dense left hemiparesis and neglect (last known well 10 hours ago, NIHSS 17). Non-contrast CT shows ASPECTS 9 with no haemorrhage; CT angiography reveals an acute right M1 middle cerebral artery occlusion, and CT perfusion demonstrates an ischaemic core volume of 18 mL with a hypoperfused penumbra (Tmax \u003e 6s) of 135 mL. What is the evidence-based reperfusion management?","o":["Proceed immediately to endovascular mechanical thrombectomy (stent-retriever/aspiration) based on favourable core-penumbra mismatch","Perform immediate decompressive hemicraniectomy before angiography","Administer IV alteplase alone without thrombectomy","Withhold all reperfusion therapy because onset exceeded 4.5 hours"],"a":0,"e":"Based on the DAWN and DEFUSE-3 trials, patients with proximal anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion (ICA or M1 MCA) presenting 6 to 24 hours after last known well who have a small irreversible ischaemic core (e.g., \u003c70 mL) and a large salvageable penumbra (mismatch ratio \u003e= 1.8, mismatch volume \u003e= 15 mL) achieve marked functional benefit from urgent endovascular mechanical thrombectomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03231","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Acute Aortic Syndromes","q":"A 67-year-old hypertensive man presents with abrupt maximal-at-onset tearing chest pain radiating to the interscapular back and an absent left radial pulse. ECG-gated CT aortography shows an intimal flap separating true and false lumens beginning just above the aortic root, extending through the ascending aorta, arch, and descending thoracic aorta. How is this classified and managed?","o":["Stanford Type B (DeBakey Type III); medical blood pressure control alone without surgery","Stanford Type A (DeBakey Type I); anti-impulse IV beta-blocker therapy and IMMEDIATE emergency open surgical repair","Stanford Type B; elective endovascular stenting after 6 weeks","Stanford Type A; start systemic IV thrombolysis with tenecteplase"],"a":1,"e":"Any aortic dissection involving the ascending aorta—regardless of site of origin or distal extent—is classified as Stanford Type A (DeBakey Type I involves both ascending and descending aorta; Type II is confined to ascending aorta). Type A dissection carries extreme risk of cardiac tamponade, acute aortic regurgitation, coronary ostial occlusion, and rupture, requiring immediate anti-impulse therapy (IV esmolol/labetalol targeting HR \u003c60, SBP 100-120 mmHg) and emergent open surgical repair.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03232","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Open Fractures","q":"A 30-year-old motorcyclist sustains a high-energy open fracture of the right tibial shaft with a 12 cm contaminated wound, extensive periosteal stripping, exposed cortical bone that cannot be closed primarily without a free/rotational muscle flap, and intact posterior tibial and dorsalis pedis pulses (no vascular repair needed). How is this injury classified under Gustilo-Anderson, and what empiric IV antibiotic regimen is indicated immediately?","o":["Gustilo-Anderson Grade I; oral cephalexin alone","Gustilo-Anderson Grade II; primary tight skin closure in the emergency room","Gustilo-Anderson Grade IIIB; IV Cefazolin (or broad-spectrum cephalosporin) PLUS an aminoglycoside (gentamicin) (+ penicillin if soil/farm contamination)","Gustilo-Anderson Grade IIIC; immediate primary below-knee amputation without debridement"],"a":2,"e":"Gustilo-Anderson Grade III open fractures involve high-energy trauma, extensive soft-tissue injury (\u003e10 cm), or contamination. Grade IIIA has adequate soft-tissue coverage; Grade IIIB has periosteal stripping and exposed bone requiring flap coverage; Grade IIIC involves an arterial injury requiring surgical repair. Grade III fractures require immediate IV Gram-positive + Gram-negative coverage (cephalosporin + aminoglycoside, adding penicillin for anaerobic/farm contamination), tetanus prophylaxis, thorough surgical debridement, and external fixation followed by early flap coverage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03233","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Acute Postoperative Endophthalmitis","q":"Three days after uneventful phacoemulsification with IOL implantation, a 68-year-old man presents with acute severe ocular pain, lid oedema, ciliary injection, a 2 mm hypopyon, dense vitritis obscuring fundus view, and visual acuity reduced to Hand Movements (HM). According to the Endophthalmitis Vitrectomy Study (EVS) principles and standard care, what is the immediate treatment?","o":["Topical prednisolone acetate 1% every hour alone","Systemic oral ciprofloxacin monotherapy without intravitreal injections","Wait 10 days for spontaneous resolution of the hypopyon","Diagnostic aqueous/vitreous tap plus intravitreal Vancomycin (1 mg/0.1 mL) and Ceftazidime (2.25 mg/0.1 mL), reserving immediate pars plana vitrectomy primarily for Light Perception (LP) vision or worse"],"a":3,"e":"Acute postoperative bacterial endophthalmitis (commonly Staphylococcus epidermidis/aureus) requires immediate vitreous tap/biopsy for Gram stain and culture followed by empiric broad-spectrum intravitreal antibiotics: Vancomycin (1 mg/0.1 mL for Gram-positives including MRSA) + Ceftazidime (2.25 mg/0.1 mL for Gram-negatives including Pseudomonas). Per the EVS, immediate pars plana vitrectomy (PPV) showed decisive benefit specifically when visual acuity is Light Perception (LP) only, whereas Hand Movements or better did equally well with tap-and-inject (though many modern vitreoretinal surgeons consider early PPV if severe/worsening).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03234","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Research Methodology and Biostatistics","topic":"Screening Biases","q":"A retrospective evaluation claims that a new annual cancer screening programme prolongs 5-year post-diagnosis survival from 40% to 70%. However, age-specific mortality from the cancer in the screened population remains completely unchanged compared with an unscreened population, because screening merely detected tumours 3 years earlier without altering the actual date of death. Which bias explains this artefactual survival gain?","o":["Lead-time bias","Recall bias","Hawthorne effect","Berkson\u0027s bias"],"a":0,"e":"Lead-time bias occurs when a screening test advances the time of diagnosis without delaying the time of death, artificially inflating measured survival duration from diagnosis (e.g., 5-year survival rate) even though true disease-specific mortality is unchanged. Evaluating mortality rates in randomised screening trials eliminates lead-time bias.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03235","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Research Methodology and Biostatistics","topic":"Non-Inferiority Trials","q":"In a randomised non-inferiority trial comparing a 7-day oral antibiotic course against a standard 14-day regimen for bacteraemia, 22% of participants crossed over or were non-adherent. Why is relying solely on an Intention-to-Treat (ITT) analysis dangerous in a non-inferiority trial, and what is required for valid inference?","o":["Crossovers and non-adherence in ITT dilute true treatment differences toward zero, falsely favouring non-inferiority; therefore BOTH ITT and Per-Protocol (PP) analyses must demonstrate non-inferiority","ITT exaggerates treatment differences in non-inferiority trials, so only Per-Protocol should ever be reported","ITT ignores randomisation, whereas Per-Protocol preserves randomisation completely","Non-inferiority trials use p \u003c 0.05 for equality rather than a pre-specified non-inferiority margin (delta)"],"a":0,"e":"In superiority trials, ITT is conservative because protocol violations/crossovers pull the groups closer together toward the null. Conversely, in a non-inferiority trial, pulling two treatments closer together makes an inferior new regimen appear falsely \u0027non-inferior\u0027 to the active control! Consequently, regulatory guidelines (ICH E9 / CONSORT) require demonstrating non-inferiority (upper/lower bound of 95% CI within margin delta) in BOTH Intention-to-Treat and Per-Protocol populations.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03236","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Pericardial Emergencies","q":"A 52-year-old man with metastatic lung adenocarcinoma presents with acute dyspnoea, BP 78/52 mmHg, HR 134/min, markedly elevated JVP with absent \u0027y\u0027 descent, muffled heart sounds, and an inspiratory fall in systolic BP of 22 mmHg (pulsus paradoxus). ECG shows low-voltage QRS complexes with electrical alternans, and bedside echo confirms a large circumferential pericardial effusion with right atrial and right ventricular diastolic collapse. What is the immediate life-saving step, and why should endotracheal intubation with positive-pressure ventilation be avoided beforehand if possible?","o":["Immediate rapid-sequence intubation with PEEP 15 cmH2O and IV furosemide","Urgent echo-guided pericardiocentesis; positive-pressure ventilation reduces venous return and precipitates cardiovascular arrest in tamponade","IV nitroglycerin infusion and high-dose oral beta-blockers","Elective pericardial biopsy after 72 hours of diuretics"],"a":1,"e":"Beck\u0027s triad (hypotension, distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds), pulsus paradoxus (\u003e10 mmHg), electrical alternans, and RA/RV diastolic collapse confirm cardiac tamponade. Because cardiac filling is severely compressed by high intrapericardial pressure, sudden drops in venous return (from positive intrathoracic pressure during intubation/PEEP, diuretics, or venodilators) can cause immediate pulseless electrical activity arrest. Urgent pericardiocentesis (even removing 50-100 mL rapidly restores stroke volume) must precede intubation whenever feasible.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03237","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Pituitary Emergencies","q":"A 47-year-old man with a known non-functioning pituitary macroadenoma develops sudden-onset severe retro-orbital headache, vomiting, bitemporal hemianopia, left CN III palsy, and hypotension (BP 76/48 mmHg, Na+ 126 mEq/L). Urgent MRI brain demonstrates acute haemorrhage and necrosis within the sellar mass compressing the optic chiasm and cavernous sinus. What is the immediate life-saving medical intervention prior to urgent neurosurgical decompression?","o":["Immediate IV alteplase thrombolysis for suspected ischaemic stroke","Oral desmopressin alone without glucocorticoids","Immediate IV Hydrocortisone 100 mg bolus (followed by 50-100 mg q6-8h) plus isotonic fluid resuscitation","Immediate IV bromocriptine monotherapy without steroids"],"a":2,"e":"Pituitary apoplexy (acute haemorrhage/infarction into a pituitary adenoma) causes sudden headache, visual field/acuity loss, cranial neuropathies (CN III/IV/VI in cavernous sinus), and life-threatening acute secondary adrenal insufficiency (ACTH deficiency -\u003e refractory shock and hyponatraemia). Immediate empirical stress-dose IV hydrocortisone (100 mg bolus) plus fluids is mandatory to prevent fatal adrenal crisis, followed by urgent transsphenoidal decompression for visual/neurological deficits.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03238","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Oncologic Emergencies","q":"A 19-year-old man with Burkitt lymphoma (WBC 95,000/mm3, LDH 4,200 U/L) develops oliguria 24 hours after starting induction chemotherapy. Labs show serum uric acid 14.2 mg/dL, K+ 6.1 mEq/L, phosphate 8.4 mg/dL, calcium 6.8 mg/dL, and creatinine 3.1 mg/dL. He has normal G6PD activity. Along with aggressive IV hydration and hyperkalaemia management, which agent rapidly degrades existing uric acid to soluble allantoin?","o":["Oral Allopurinol alone","IV Sodium bicarbonate to alkalinise urine to pH \u003e 8.0","IV Calcium gluconate 3 g/day routinely for asymptomatic hypocalcaemia","IV Rasburicase (recombinant urate oxidase)"],"a":3,"e":"Established Tumour Lysis Syndrome (hyperuricaemia, hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia, secondary hypocalcaemia, AKI) requires aggressive hydration and rapid reduction of preformed uric acid. Rasburicase (recombinant urate oxidase) enzymatically converts poorly soluble uric acid directly into water-soluble allantoin within hours (contrasting with allopurinol, which only blocks new uric acid synthesis and increases xanthine stones). Crucially, G6PD deficiency must be excluded before rasburicase because H2O2 generation causes severe haemolysis/methaemoglobinaemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03239","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease","q":"A 30-year-old man with ulcerative colitis is admitted with 10 bloody stools/day, fever (38.4 degrees C), HR 104/min, Hb 9.2 g/dL, and CRP 68 mg/L (Truelove-Witts severe flare). Stool Clostridioides difficile toxin and flexible sigmoidoscopy CMV biopsies are negative, and abdominal X-ray shows no toxic megacolon. After 72 hours of IV hydrocortisone (100 mg q6h), he still has 9 bloody stools/day and CRP 52 mg/L. What is the next evidence-based step?","o":["Initiate medical rescue therapy with Infliximab (or Cyclosporine) while coordinating early surgical review for potential colectomy if unresponsive","Switch to oral mesalamine 2.4 g/day alone and discharge","Continue IV hydrocortisone unchanged for another 4 weeks before reassessing","Start high-dose oral loperamide and broad-spectrum ciprofloxacin"],"a":0,"e":"Acute severe ulcerative colitis failing to improve by Day 3 of IV corticosteroids (Travis/Oxford criteria: \u003e8 stools/day OR 3-8 stools/day with CRP \u003e45 mg/L predicts \u003e85% failure of steroids) requires prompt second-line rescue therapy with either IV Infliximab or IV Cyclosporine (after excluding C. difficile, CMV, and toxic megacolon/perforation) alongside multidisciplinary colorectal surgery involvement for timely subtotal colectomy if rescue fails within 5-7 days.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03240","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Oesophageal Motility Disorders","q":"A 35-year-old woman presents with 2 years of progressive dysphagia to BOTH solids and liquids, regurgitation of undigested food, and nocturnal cough. Barium swallow shows a dilated oesophagus with smooth distal \u0027bird-beak\u0027 tapering, and upper GI endoscopy excludes pseudoachalasia/tumour. What is the gold-standard confirmatory test and definitive surgical/endoscopic treatment for Type II achalasia?","o":["CT chest alone; oesophagectomy as routine first-line","High-resolution oesophageal manometry (elevated integrated relaxation pressure + aperistalsis); laparoscopic Heller myotomy with partial fundoplication OR Peroral Endoscopic Myotomy (POEM)","Proton pump inhibitor trial for 12 months","24-hour pH-metry alone; Nissen 360-degree total fundoplication"],"a":1,"e":"Achalasia cardia is caused by loss of inhibitory myenteric plexus (nitric oxide/VIP) neurons in the distal oesophagus, causing failure of lower oesophageal sphincter (LES) relaxation and oesophageal aperistalsis. High-resolution manometry (HRM) is the gold-standard diagnostic test (Chicago Classification Types I-III). Definitive therapy disrupts the LES via laparoscopic Heller myotomy (with partial anterior Dor or posterior Toupet fundoplication to prevent reflux) or Peroral Endoscopic Myotomy (POEM).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03241","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Cystic Neoplasms of Pancreas","q":"A 64-year-old man evaluated for mild epigastric discomfort undergoes MRCP and EUS showing diffuse dilatation of the main pancreatic duct to 13 mm in the pancreatic head with an enhancing 8 mm mural nodule. ERCP demonstrates thick mucin extruding from a widely patulous (\u0027fish-mouth\u0027) major duodenal papilla. What is the diagnosis and appropriate surgical management in a fit patient?","o":["Mucinous cystic neoplasm of the tail; distal pancreatectomy without checking head","Pancreatic pseudocyst; endoscopic cystogastrostomy","Main-duct Intraductal Papillary Mucinous Neoplasm (IPMN) with high-risk stigmata; oncological pancreaticoduodenectomy (Whipple procedure)","Serous cystadenoma; annual ultrasound observation only"],"a":2,"e":"A \u0027fish-mouth\u0027 patulous papilla extruding mucin with main pancreatic duct dilatation \u003e= 10 mm and an enhancing mural nodule \u003e= 5 mm are classic high-risk stigmata of main-duct IPMN (which carries ~60-70% risk of high-grade dysplasia or invasive adenocarcinoma). Fit surgical candidates require oncological resection (Whipple pancreaticoduodenectomy for head involvement) with intraoperative frozen section of the pancreatic duct margin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03242","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma \u0026 MEN 2A","q":"A 28-year-old man presents with a firm thyroid nodule; FNAC shows spindle-shaped cells with amyloid stroma (medullary thyroid carcinoma), serum calcitonin is 840 pg/mL, serum calcium is 11.4 mg/dL (elevated PTH), and genetic testing confirms a germline RET proto-oncogene mutation (MEN 2A). Before scheduling total thyroidectomy and central neck dissection, which mandatory biochemical screening must be performed and acted upon FIRST?","o":["Radioactive iodine (I-131) whole-body uptake scan","Serum thyroglobulin and anti-TPO antibodies","Serum CA 19-9 and CEA alone","Plasma free metanephrines / 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines to rule out occult pheochromocytoma (which must be resected BEFORE thyroid surgery)"],"a":3,"e":"Every patient with Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma (MTC)—especially with germline RET mutation / MEN 2A (MTC + pheochromocytoma + primary hyperparathyroidism)—MUST undergo biochemical exclusion of pheochromocytoma via plasma/urinary metanephrines before ANY neck operation. Operating on the thyroid with an undiagnosed, unblocked pheochromocytoma can trigger fatal intraoperative catecholamine crisis; if present, adrenalectomy (after alpha-blockade) always precedes thyroidectomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03243","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Compartment Syndrome (ACS)","q":"Twenty-four hours after massive resuscitation and laparotomy for ruptured AAA, a ventilated ICU patient develops a tense distended abdomen, peak airway pressures rising from 24 to 44 cmH2O, oliguria (urine output 8 mL/h), and worsening hypotension despite norepinephrine. Trans-vesical (bladder) intra-abdominal pressure (IAP) measured at end-expiration is 26 mmHg. What is the diagnosis and definitive treatment?","o":["Abdominal Compartment Syndrome (IAP \u003e 20 mmHg with new organ dysfunction); immediate surgical decompressive laparotomy with temporary abdominal closure","Acute tubular necrosis alone; start high-dose furosemide infusion without decompression","Simple postoperative ileus; oral erythromycin","Grade I intra-abdominal hypertension; infuse another 4 litres of crystalloids"],"a":0,"e":"Per WSACS definitions, Abdominal Compartment Syndrome (ACS) is sustained intra-abdominal pressure (measured via Foley bladder catheter at end-expiration in supine position) \u003e 20 mmHg associated with new-onset organ dysfunction/failure (renal oliguria/anuria, elevated peak airway pressures/hypoxia, reduced cardiac output from IVC compression). Established refractory ACS requires emergency surgical decompressive laparotomy and temporary abdominal closure (open abdomen).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03244","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Acute Fatty Liver of Pregnancy (AFLP)","q":"A 26-year-old primigravida at 35 weeks of gestation presents with nausea, vomiting, polydipsia/polyuria, jaundice, and drowsiness. Labs show blood glucose 38 mg/dL, total bilirubin 7.4 mg/dL, AST 240 U/L, INR 2.4, fibrinogen 95 mg/dL, serum ammonia 145 micromol/L, WBC 24,000/mm3, and creatinine 2.6 mg/dL (platelets 145,000/mm3, BP 124/78 mmHg). What is the most likely diagnosis and definitive obstetric action?","o":["HELLP syndrome with severe hypertension; expectant management for 2 weeks","Acute Fatty Liver of Pregnancy (AFLP); prompt maternal stabilisation (dextrose/coagulopathy correction) and immediate delivery","Uncomplicated intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy; oral ursodeoxycholic acid until 39 weeks","Hyperemesis gravidarum; oral doxylamine-pyridoxine and outpatient follow-up"],"a":1,"e":"Third-trimester vomiting, encephalopathy, profound hypoglycaemia, hyperammonaemia, coagulopathy (low fibrinogen/high INR), leucocytosis, renal impairment, and hyperbilirubinaemia (satisfying \u003e= 6 Swansea criteria) point strongly to Acute Fatty Liver of Pregnancy (AFLP, linked to fetal LCHAD deficiency) rather than HELLP (which features prominent hypertension, haemolysis, and severe thrombocytopenia, usually without severe hypoglycaemia/hyperammonaemia). Immediate resuscitation (IV dextrose + FFP/cryoprecipitate) and expeditious delivery are life-saving.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03245","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia (GTN)","q":"A 32-year-old woman presents 6 months after a term normal vaginal delivery with irregular vaginal bleeding, haemoptysis, and seizures. Serum beta-hCG is 180,000 IU/L, and imaging reveals a vascular uterine mass, multiple bilateral lung metastases, and two brain metastases (FIGO Stage IV, WHO prognostic score = 13). Which chemotherapy regimen is standard first-line treatment?","o":["Repeat suction and evacuation alone without chemotherapy","Single-agent intramuscular Methotrexate alone","Multi-agent EMA-CO chemotherapy (Etoposide, Methotrexate, Actinomycin D, Cyclophosphamide, Vincristine) with CNS-directed modification/radiotherapy","Oral progesterone maintenance"],"a":2,"e":"GTN with a WHO/FIGO prognostic risk score \u003e= 7 (or Stage IV, post-term choriocarcinoma, brain/liver metastases, beta-hCG \u003e 100,000) is classified as High-Risk GTN. Single-agent methotrexate/actinomycin-D has high failure/resistance rates; multi-agent EMA-CO chemotherapy (with escalated high-dose methotrexate for CNS penetration and/or cranial irradiation/intrathecal methotrexate) achieves cure rates exceeding 80-90%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03246","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Necrotising Enterocolitis (NEC)","q":"A 1,050-g preterm infant (28 weeks gestation) on enteral feeds develops abdominal distension, bilious aspirates, abdominal wall erythema, thrombocytopenia (45,000/mm3), metabolic acidosis, and apnoea on day 12 of life. Supine and cross-table lateral abdominal radiographs show pneumatosis intestinalis, portal venous gas, and free intraperitoneal air (pneumoperitoneum). Beyond keeping NPO, nasogastric decompression, and broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, what is indicated?","o":["Rectal saline enema for presumed meconium plug","Continue expressed breast milk feeds slowly via orogastric tube","Discharge once apnoea resolves with caffeine citrate alone","Urgent surgical intervention (exploratory laparotomy with resection/enterostomy or primary peritoneal drainage in unstable ELBW infants)"],"a":3,"e":"Pneumoperitoneum indicates intestinal perforation, classifying the infant as Bell Stage IIIB Necrotising Enterocolitis (advanced NEC). Pneumoperitoneum is an absolute indication for surgical intervention—exploratory laparotomy (resection of necrotic bowel with ostomy) or bedside primary peritoneal drainage in critically unstable extremely-low-birth-weight neonates—alongside NPO, gastric decompression, inotropes, and broad-spectrum antibiotics covering Gram-negatives and anaerobes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03247","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH)","q":"A 12-day-old infant (46,XX karyotype with clitoromegaly and fused labioscrotal folds) presents with poor feeding, projectile vomiting, severe dehydration, BP 54/30 mmHg, blood glucose 42 mg/dL, Na+ 118 mEq/L, K+ 7.3 mEq/L, metabolic acidosis, and markedly elevated serum 17-hydroxyprogesterone. What is the emergency medical management?","o":["IV 0.9% normal saline resuscitation with dextrose, hyperkalaemia stabilisation (calcium gluconate/insulin-dextrose), and immediate IV Hydrocortisone (followed by oral fludrocortisone once stabilised)","Emergency Ramstedt pyloromyotomy for vomiting","Oral levothyroxine monotherapy","IV potassium chloride supplementation and spironolactone"],"a":0,"e":"Classic salt-wasting 21-hydroxylase deficiency CAH (\u003e90% of CAH) presents in weeks 1-3 of life with adrenal crisis: hyponatraemia, severe hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia, metabolic acidosis, hypovolaemic shock, and virilisation in 46,XX infants (elevated 17-OHP). Emergency care requires immediate 0.9% saline + dextrose bolus, ECG-guided hyperkalaemia therapy, and stress-dose IV hydrocortisone (which provides both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid activity acutely), followed by oral hydrocortisone + fludrocortisone + salt supplementation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03248","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Massive Transfusion Protocol (MTP)","q":"A 35-year-old man arrives 90 minutes after severe pelvic trauma in Class IV haemorrhagic shock (BP 70/40 mmHg, HR 140/min, base deficit -14 mEq/L, persistent pelvic bleeding requiring massive transfusion). Which blood-product ratio and antifibrinolytic timing are supported by the PROPPR and CRASH-2 trials to reduce exsanguination mortality?","o":["10 units of Packed RBCs alone before giving any plasma or platelets, plus Tranexamic acid at 12 hours post-injury","1:1:1 ratio of Plasma (FFP) : Platelets : Packed RBCs PLUS IV Tranexamic acid (1 g over 10 min, then 1 g over 8 h) given within 3 hours of injury","4 litres of 0.9% normal saline first and withhold Tranexamic acid","Whole-blood albumin 5% infusion in a 3:1 ratio to RBCs"],"a":1,"e":"In trauma haemorrhagic shock, balanced damage-control resuscitation targets a 1:1:1 ratio of FFP : Platelets : PRBCs (PROPPR trial) while minimising crystalloids to prevent dilutional coagulopathy, acidosis, and hypothermia. Based on CRASH-2, IV Tranexamic acid (1 g loading + 1 g over 8 h) reduces bleeding mortality when given early (\u003c= 3 hours from injury; notably harmful if started \u003e3 hours).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03249","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Perioperative Anaphylaxis","q":"Two minutes after IV administration of rocuronium and cefazolin at induction of anaesthesia, a 37-year-old woman develops severe bronchospasm (peak airway pressure 48 cmH2O), profound hypotension (55/30 mmHg), tachycardia, and generalised urticaria under the surgical drapes. Beyond stopping suspect agents and administering 100% O2 and rapid IV crystalloids, what is the primary vasoactive drug and confirmatory diagnostic test timing?","o":["IV Hydrocortisone 100 mg as sole initial agent; serum IgE after 1 hour","Nebulised ipratropium alone; arterial lactate after 48 hours","IV Epinephrine (titrated 50-100 mcg boluses, or 0.5 mg IM if no IV access); draw serum mast-cell tryptase at 1-2 hours (plus baseline/24 h comparison)","IV Phenylephrine 10 mg bolus; serum C-reactive protein at 1 hour"],"a":2,"e":"Epinephrine (adrenaline) is the ONLY life-saving first-line drug in anaphylaxis—reversing vasodilation, bronchospasm, myocardial depression, and mast-cell mediator release (steroids and antihistamines are strictly second-line adjuncts). In monitored anaesthetised adults, titrated IV epinephrine boluses (e.g., 50-100 mcg depending on severity, escalating to infusion) + aggressive crystalloids are given. Serial serum tryptase peaks at 1-2 hours post-onset (compared with \u003e24 h baseline) to confirm mast-cell degranulation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03250","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Renal Cystic Masses","q":"On multiphasic contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen, an incidental 4.2 cm right renal cystic mass demonstrates multiple smooth thin septa with a distinct 6 mm enhancing nodular soft-tissue component arising from its wall (\u003e20 HU enhancement). How is this lesion categorised under the Bosniak classification, and what is the clinical implication?","o":["Bosniak Class I; simple benign cyst requiring no follow-up","Bosniak Class IIF; benign, repeat imaging only in 5 years","Bosniak Class II; minimally complex cyst requiring no follow-up","Bosniak Class IV; high probability of malignancy (~85-95% renal cell carcinoma), requiring surgical resection/urological intervention"],"a":3,"e":"Under the Bosniak classification (including v2019), a cystic renal lesion with one or more enhancing soft-tissue nodules (\u003e= 4 mm obtuse or any convex protrusion with measurable enhancement) is Bosniak Class IV, carrying an ~85-95% likelihood of renal cell carcinoma and necessitating surgical excision (partial/radical nephrectomy) or ablation in surgical candidates.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03251","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Prostate Multiparametric MRI","q":"During multiparametric MRI (mpMRI) evaluation of suspected prostate carcinoma using PI-RADS v2.1, which MRI sequences serve as the PRIMARY (dominant) determining sequence for scoring lesions in the Peripheral Zone (PZ) versus the Transition Zone (TZ)?","o":["Diffusion-Weighted Imaging (DWI/ADC) is dominant for the Peripheral Zone; T2-weighted imaging (T2WI) is dominant for the Transition Zone","T2-weighted imaging is dominant for the Peripheral Zone; Dynamic Contrast Enhancement (DCE) is dominant for the Transition Zone","Dynamic Contrast Enhancement (DCE) is dominant for both zones","MR Spectroscopy is dominant for the Peripheral Zone"],"a":0,"e":"Under ACR PI-RADS v2.1, ~70-75% of prostate cancers arise in the Peripheral Zone, where DWI/ADC is the primary dominant sequence (and DCE plays a secondary role only to upgrade PZ PI-RADS 3 to 4 if positive). In the Transition Zone (where BPH nodules complicate DWI), T2-weighted imaging (evaluating lenticular/non-circumscribed \u0027erased charcoal\u0027 hypointensity) is the primary dominant sequence, with DWI serving as secondary modifier.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03252","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Acute Compartment Syndrome","q":"Eight hours after closed reduction and casting of a tibial shaft fracture, a 26-year-old man (BP 110/68 mmHg) develops severe escalating leg pain unrelieved by IV morphine and excruciating pain on passive dorsiflexion of the great toe. The plaster cast and underlying padding are completely split, yet pain persists. Stryker catheter measurement of the anterior compartment pressure is 48 mmHg (delta pressure = DBP - compartment pressure = 20 mmHg). What is the immediate management?","o":["Immediate surgical four-compartment fasciotomy of the leg","Re-apply a tight crepe bandage and elevate the leg above heart level","Administer IV mannitol and re-measure pressure after 12 hours","Wait until dorsalis pedis pulse disappears before operating"],"a":0,"e":"Acute compartment syndrome is diagnosed clinically by pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch of compartment muscles, supported by intracompartmental delta pressure (Diastolic BP minus Compartment pressure) \u003c= 30 mmHg (here 68 - 48 = 20 mmHg) or absolute pressure \u003e 30 mmHg. Once cast/dressing splitting fails to relieve pressure, emergency surgical four-compartment fasciotomy (via two-incision or single-incision technique) is mandatory to prevent irreversible myonecrosis and Volkmann contracture.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03253","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Parotid Tumours","q":"A 38-year-old woman presents with a slow-growing, painless, firm, mobile 3 cm mass in the superficial lobe of the right parotid gland below the ear lobule, with intact facial nerve function. FNAC confirms pleomorphic adenoma (benign mixed tumour). Which surgical procedure minimises recurrence while preserving facial nerve function?","o":["Superficial parotidectomy (or extracapsular dissection with a cuff of normal parotid tissue) identifying and preserving the facial nerve","Intracapsular enucleation (shelling out the tumour along its capsule)","Incisional biopsy followed by observation","Routine radical parotidectomy sacrificing the facial nerve trunk"],"a":0,"e":"Pleomorphic adenoma is the most common benign salivary gland tumour (~80% in parotid). Because its pseudocapsule has microscopic finger-like projections (pseudopodia) extending into adjacent parenchyma, simple enucleation ruptures the capsule, seeds multifocal tumour cells, and leads to 20-45% recurrence (with risk of carcinoma ex-pleomorphic adenoma). Superficial parotidectomy with meticulous facial nerve dissection and preservation achieves \u003c2% recurrence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03254","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Psychiatry","topic":"Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome vs Serotonin Syndrome","q":"A 33-year-old man with schizophrenia develops fever (40.1 degrees C), fluctuating delirium, labile blood pressure, diaphoresis, generalised \u0027lead-pipe\u0027 muscular rigidity, hyporeflexia, leucocytosis, and serum creatine kinase (CK) of 18,500 U/L four days after escalating parenteral haloperidol. How does this condition differ clinically from serotonin syndrome, and what specific pharmacotherapies are used after stopping haloperidol?","o":["Malignant hyperthermia; treat with oral cyproheptadine","Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (bradyreflexia + lead-pipe rigidity evolving over days); treat with supportive cooling/hydration plus Dantrolene and/or Bromocriptine","Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome; treat with IV atropine and continue haloperidol at lower dose","Serotonin syndrome (hyperreflexia + inducible clonus); treat with IV chlorpromazine"],"a":1,"e":"Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS), triggered by dopamine D2-receptor blockade (haloperidol, fluphenazine), evolves over 1-3+ days with hyperthermia, autonomic instability, severe \u0027lead-pipe\u0027 rigidity, diminished/absent reflexes, and marked CK elevation. In contrast, Serotonin Syndrome evolves rapidly (\u003c24 h after serotonergic combinations like SSRI + MAOI/linezolid/tramadol) with neuromuscular excitation—hyperreflexia, inducible/spontaneous clonus, myoclonus, and bowel hypermotility (treated with cyproheptadine). NMS requires immediate cessation of antipsychotics, aggressive cooling/IV fluids, benzodiazepines, Dantrolene, and Bromocriptine (dopamine agonist).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03255","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Research Methodology and Biostatistics","topic":"Heterogeneity and Publication Bias","q":"A meta-analysis pooling 14 randomised trials shows a significant overall odds ratio of 0.68, with Cochran\u0027s Q p = 0.002 and Higgins\u0027 I-squared (I2) = 78%. Additionally, the funnel plot shows missing small negative studies in the bottom-right corner (Egger\u0027s test p = 0.01). What do these metrics indicate?","o":["Type II error in the pooled estimate caused by excessive sample size","Complete homogeneity across trials (requiring a fixed-effect model) with zero publication bias","Substantial statistical heterogeneity across trials (requiring a random-effects model and subgroup/sensitivity exploration) AND probable publication bias","Perfect symmetry confirming all unpublished null trials were included"],"a":2,"e":"Higgins\u0027 I2 quantifies the percentage of total variation across studies attributable to true heterogeneity rather than chance (I2 \u003e 50-75% indicates substantial-to-considerable heterogeneity), favouring a random-effects (DerSimonian-Laird) model and investigation of clinical/methodological sources. Funnel plot asymmetry with missing small null/negative studies and significant Egger\u0027s regression (p \u003c 0.10/0.05) suggests small-study effects / publication bias.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03256","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Heart Failure with Reduced Ejection Fraction (HFrEF)","q":"A 58-year-old man with non-ischaemic dilated cardiomyopathy (LVEF 28%, NYHA Class II-III, BP 118/74 mmHg, eGFR 64 mL/min/1.73 m2, serum K+ 4.4 mEq/L, sinus rhythm HR 76/min) is currently taking only furosemide 40 mg/day. Which four-drug disease-modifying combination represents optimal foundational GDMT proven to reduce cardiovascular mortality and heart failure hospitalisations?","o":["Digoxin + Diltiazem + Furosemide + Ivabradine","Amlodipine + Hydralazine alone + Torsemide","Atenolol + Verapamil + High-dose aspirin","ARNI (Sacubitril-Valsartan, or ACEi/ARB) + Evidence-based Beta-blocker (carvedilol/metoprolol succinate/bisoprolol) + MRA (spironolactone/eplerenone) + SGLT2 inhibitor (dapagliflozin/empagliflozin)"],"a":3,"e":"Current ESC/ACC/AHA guidelines establish four foundational pillars for HFrEF (LVEF \u003c= 40%): (1) ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan, preferred over ACEi/ARB, observing a 36-hour washout if switching from ACEi); (2) HF-specific beta-blocker (carvedilol, metoprolol succinate, or bisoprolol); (3) Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist (spironolactone/eplerenone); and (4) SGLT2 inhibitor (dapagliflozin/empagliflozin regardless of diabetes status), each independently reducing all-cause mortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03257","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Anti-GBM (Goodpasture) Disease","q":"A 27-year-old male smoker presents with acute haemoptysis, bilateral alveolar infiltrates, serum creatinine 5.2 mg/dL, and RBC casts on urinalysis. Serum anti-glomerular basement membrane (anti-GBM) antibodies are strongly positive (ANCA negative), and renal biopsy shows crescentic glomerulonephritis with bright, smooth LINEAR IgG deposition along the glomerular capillary loops. Beyond pulse IV methylprednisolone and cyclophosphamide, which urgent intervention is essential to remove pathogenic circulating antibodies?","o":["Daily therapeutic plasma exchange (plasmapheresis) until anti-GBM antibodies are undetectable","Oral hydroxychloroquine monotherapy","Wait 6 weeks for spontaneous antibody clearance without immunosuppression","High-dose IV furosemide alone"],"a":0,"e":"Goodpasture (anti-GBM) disease is caused by autoantibodies directed against the non-collagenous domain (NC1) of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen in alveolar and glomerular basement membranes, producing linear IgG immunofluorescence and fulminant pulmonary-renal syndrome. Immediate triple therapy—daily plasmapheresis (to rapidly clear pathogenic IgG) + pulse corticosteroids + oral/IV cyclophosphamide (to halt new antibody synthesis)—is mandatory to salvage renal function and stop alveolar haemorrhage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03258","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Hepatorenal Syndrome (HRS-AKI)","q":"A 53-year-old man with decompensated cirrhosis and ascites has serum creatinine rising from 1.1 to 2.9 mg/dL over 4 days despite stopping diuretics and lactulose-induced diarrhoea, ruling out nephrotoxins/shock/structural obstruction, and finding normal urinalysis (\u003c500 mg/day protein, no RBCs). After 48 hours of plasma volume expansion with IV 20% albumin (1 g/kg/day), serum creatinine fails to improve (remains 2.8 mg/dL). What is the diagnosis and standard medical therapy?","o":["Prerenal dehydration alone; restart high-dose spironolactone and furosemide immediately","Hepatorenal Syndrome (HRS-AKI); administer IV Terlipressin (splanchnic vasoconstrictor) PLUS continued IV Albumin (20-40 g/day) while evaluating for liver transplantation","Nephrotic syndrome; start high-dose oral prednisolone","Postrenal obstruction; perform immediate bilateral ureteric stenting"],"a":1,"e":"Under International Club of Ascites (ICA) criteria, HRS-AKI is diagnosed in cirrhosis with ascites when AKI persists despite stopping diuretics and completing a 48-hour IV albumin challenge (1 g/kg/day), in the absence of shock, nephrotoxic drugs, or intrinsic parenchymal/obstructive kidney disease. Because intense splanchnic arterial vasodilation triggers extreme renal vasoconstriction, standard pharmacotherapy combines IV Terlipressin (or norepinephrine in ICU) + IV Albumin to restore effective arterial blood volume and renal perfusion as a bridge to liver transplantation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03259","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Acute Myeloid Leukaemia Subtypes","q":"A 31-year-old woman presents with epistaxis, extensive ecchymoses, WBC 3,200/mm3, platelets 16,000/mm3, fibrinogen 70 mg/dL, and markedly elevated D-dimer (fulminant DIC). Peripheral smear shows hypergranular promyelocytes containing multiple bundled Auer rods (\u0027faggot cells\u0027). Cytogenetics/FISH for t(15;17) PML-RARA will take 48 hours. What is the single most urgent disease-specific action TODAY?","o":["Wait 48 hours until PML-RARA PCR confirmation returns before starting any therapy","Perform immediate splenectomy for thrombocytopenia","Start oral All-Trans Retinoic Acid (ATRA) IMMEDIATELY today upon morphological suspicion—without waiting for t(15;17) cytogenetics—alongside aggressive fibrinogen/platelet transfusion","Start oral imatinib monotherapy"],"a":2,"e":"Acute Promyelocytic Leukaemia (APL, AML-M3, t(15;17) PML-RARA) carries a high risk of early fatal intracranial/pulmonary haemorrhage from severe DIC and hyperfibrinolysis. Starting All-Trans Retinoic Acid (ATRA) IMMEDIATELY on the same day of morphological suspicion (followed by Arsenic Trioxide +/- anthracycline upon confirmation) rapidly induces promyelocyte differentiation and halts lethal coagulopathy, while maintaining platelets \u003e 30,000-50,000/mm3 and fibrinogen \u003e 150 mg/dL (monitoring closely for Differentiation Syndrome).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03260","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Large-Vessel Vasculitis","q":"A 71-year-old woman with polymyalgia rheumatica presents with new-onset throbbing right temporal headache, scalp tenderness while combing hair, jaw claudication when chewing, ESR 96 mm/h, and two brief episodes of transient right-eye vision loss (amaurosis fugax) today. Temporal artery biopsy cannot be scheduled for 3 days. What is the immediate priority?","o":["Start oral aspirin 75 mg alone and discharge","Perform immediate carotid endarterectomy without steroids","Withhold all corticosteroids until temporal artery biopsy is completed in 3 days to avoid altering histology","Administer high-dose IV Methylprednisolone (500-1,000 mg/day for 3 days) or high-dose oral prednisolone IMMEDIATELY today, and still perform temporal artery biopsy within 1-2 weeks"],"a":3,"e":"Giant Cell Arteritis (GCA) with transient visual symptoms (amaurosis fugax) is an ophthalmic emergency threatening permanent bilateral blindness from anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy (AION). High-dose systemic glucocorticoids (IV pulse methylprednisolone when visual symptoms exist, plus low-dose aspirin) MUST be started immediately—granulomatous vasculitic changes remain histologically detectable on temporal artery biopsy for at least 1-2 weeks after starting steroids.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03261","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Incidental Gallbladder Carcinoma","q":"A 54-year-old woman undergoes routine laparoscopic cholecystectomy for symptomatic gallstones. Histopathology of the gallbladder reveals an incidental adenocarcinoma invading the perimuscular connective tissue on the hepatic side without serosal perforation (pT2b), with a negative cystic duct margin. Staging CT chest/abdomen/pelvis shows no distant metastases. What is the definitive surgical management?","o":["Radical re-resection: Segment IVb + V hepatic wedge/anatomical resection plus regional hepatoduodenal/portal lymphadenectomy (without routine bile duct resection unless cystic margin positive)","Laparoscopic port-site excision alone without liver resection or lymphadenectomy","Immediate liver transplantation","No further surgery needed; simple cholecystectomy is curative for all pT2 tumours"],"a":0,"e":"While Tis and pT1a (lamina propria only) incidental gallbladder cancers with negative cystic duct margins are cured by simple cholecystectomy alone, pT1b (muscularis) and pT2/pT3 tumours carry high risk of hepatic bed invasion and nodal metastasis. Radical re-resection consisting of resection of gallbladder bed liver segments IVb and V (or extended right hepatectomy if needed) + regional portal/hepatoduodenal lymphadenectomy significantly improves survival. Routine CBD excision is reserved only for positive cystic duct margins.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03262","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Acute Diverticulitis","q":"A 61-year-old man presents with 4 days of left lower quadrant pain and fever (38.7 degrees C). He is haemodynamically stable without generalised peritonitis. Contrast-enhanced CT abdomen shows sigmoid diverticulitis complicated by a well-defined 6.5 cm pelvic abscess (Hinchey Stage II), with no free intraperitoneal air or diffuse fluid. What is the preferred initial management?","o":["Urgent diagnostic colonoscopy today to biopsy the diverticula","Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics plus CT-guided percutaneous drainage of the abscess, followed by elective interval colonoscopy (after 6-8 weeks) and selective surgical assessment","Immediate emergency Hartmann procedure (sigmoid resection and end colostomy)","Oral paracetamol and outpatient discharge without antibiotics"],"a":1,"e":"Per Hinchey classification of complicated diverticulitis: Stage I (pericolic abscess), Stage II (distant pelvic/retroperitoneal abscess), Stage III (generalised purulent peritonitis), and Stage IV (faeculent peritonitis). Stable Stage II patients with abscesses \u003e= 3-4 cm are treated with IV antibiotics + image-guided percutaneous drainage (avoiding an emergency stoma), followed by colonoscopy ~6 weeks post-resolution to exclude underlying colon carcinoma.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03263","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Parathyroidectomy Complications","q":"Forty-eight hours after parathyroidectomy for severe longstanding primary hyperparathyroidism (preoperative alkaline phosphatase 620 U/L and osteitis fibrosa cystica), a 45-year-old woman develops carpopedal spasm, positive Chvostek sign, and prolonged QTc. Labs show serum calcium 6.2 mg/dL, serum phosphate 1.8 mg/dL (LOW), serum magnesium 1.1 mg/dL (LOW), and normal-to-high intact PTH. What is the diagnosis and treatment?","o":["Vitamin D toxicity; IV furosemide and saline","Postoperative thyroid storm; IV propranolol","Hungry Bone Syndrome; aggressive IV calcium gluconate infusion, oral calcium, high-dose calcitriol, and magnesium repletion","Permanent surgical hypoparathyroidism (which causes HIGH phosphate); IV sodium phosphate"],"a":2,"e":"After abrupt withdrawal of longstanding high PTH in patients with high bone turnover (elevated ALP, bone disease), osteoclast resorption halts abruptly while osteoblastic mineralisation surges, avidly sequestering calcium, phosphate, and magnesium into bone (\u0027Hungry Bone Syndrome\u0027). Crucially, serum PHOSPHATE is LOW in hungry bone syndrome (unlike post-surgical hypoparathyroidism, where phosphate is HIGH and PTH is low/undetectable). Treatment requires prolonged aggressive IV/oral calcium, activated vitamin D (calcitriol), and magnesium replacement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03264","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Chest Wall Injury","q":"A 40-year-old man involved in a high-speed collision has fractures of ribs 4, 5, 6, and 7 at two sites each on the right chest wall, demonstrating paradoxical inward movement during inspiration (flail chest) and underlying pulmonary contusion on CT chest. He is alert with SpO2 91% on 4 L/min O2. Which management bundle reduces pneumonia and need for mechanical ventilation?","o":["Immediate obligatory endotracheal intubation in all flail chest patients regardless of oxygenation","Withholding analgesia to preserve respiratory drive","Taping sandbags tightly over the flail segment and infusing 5 litres of normal saline","Multimodal/regional analgesia (thoracic epidural or erector spinae/paravertebral block), humidified O2, chest physiotherapy, and judicious fluid restriction to avoid worsening pulmonary contusion"],"a":3,"e":"The major cause of hypoxaemia in flail chest is underlying pulmonary contusion and pain-induced splinting/atelectasis. Modern management prioritises aggressive regional analgesia (thoracic epidural/paravertebral catheter), pulmonary toilet, supplemental O2/NIV, and careful fluid balance (avoiding crystalloid overload that exacerbates alveolar oedema in contused lung), reserving intubation for respiratory failure/exhaustion and surgical rib fixation for select severe/non-weaning cases.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03265","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Intrahepatic Cholestasis of Pregnancy (ICP)","q":"A 28-year-old primigravida at 33 weeks of gestation presents with severe generalised pruritus worse at night and on the palms and soles, with excoriations but no primary skin rash. Total serum bile acids are 62 micromol/L and ALT is 140 U/L (viral hepatitis serology and liver ultrasound normal). Which pharmacotherapy relieves symptoms/improves bile flow, and what is the timing of delivery given bile acids \u003e= 40-99 micromol/L?","o":["Oral Ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) and planned delivery around 36-37 weeks (or earlier at 35-36 weeks if bile acids \u003e= 100 micromol/L)","Oral rifampicin + high-dose methotrexate","Immediate Caesarean section today at 33 weeks without fetal lung steroids","Topical permethrin lotion and await spontaneous labour at 41 weeks"],"a":0,"e":"Nocturnal palmar-plantar pruritus without rash and elevated serum bile acids (\u003e= 10 micromol/L) diagnose Intrahepatic Cholestasis of Pregnancy (ICP), which carries an increased risk of sudden unexplained intrauterine fetal demise at term—especially when bile acids \u003e= 100 micromol/L. Ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA 10-15 mg/kg/day) reduces maternal pruritus and improves liver tests, and planned delivery around 36+0 to 37+6 weeks (35-36 weeks if \u003e= 100 micromol/L) mitigates stillbirth risk.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03266","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Umbilical Cord Prolapse","q":"Immediately following spontaneous rupture of membranes in a multiparous woman at 38 weeks with transverse-to-oblique lie (cervix 5 cm dilated), a pulsating loop of umbilical cord is palpated in the vagina and CTG drops abruptly to 60 bpm. While preparing for immediate Category 1 Caesarean section, what is the critical bedside manoeuvre to prevent fetal hypoxia?","o":["Keep two gloved fingers in the vagina pushing the fetal presenting part UPWARD off the cord continuously (plus knee-chest or steep Trendelenburg position +/- bladder filling) until delivery; do NOT attempt to push the cord back inside","Attempt manual replacement of the prolapsed cord back above the fetal head and apply fundal pressure","Clamp and cut the pulsating umbilical cord immediately in the labour room","Administer IV oxytocin bolus to expedite vaginal birth"],"a":0,"e":"Overt umbilical cord prolapse causes acute fetal asphyxia via mechanical cord compression and cold/handling-induced vasospasm. The obstetrician/midwife must immediately insert a gloved hand into the vagina to continuously elevate the presenting part off the cord (maintained until the baby is delivered at Caesarean section), place the mother in exaggerated Sims/Trendelenburg or knee-chest position (or rapidly instil 500 mL saline into the bladder to elevate the head if transfer takes time), and avoid handling/trying to push the cord back into the uterus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03267","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Perinatal Asphyxia","q":"A term neonate (39 weeks, birth weight 3.2 kg) born after prolonged obstructed labour has Apgar scores of 2 at 1 min and 4 at 5 min, umbilical cord arterial pH 6.92 (base deficit -18 mEq/L), and at 2 hours of life exhibits lethargy, hypotonia, weak Moro reflex, and subtle focal seizures (Sarnat Stage II moderate HIE). Which neuroprotective therapy initiated within 6 hours of birth reduces death and neurodevelopmental disability?","o":["Routine high-dose IV dexamethasone for 7 days","Whole-body or selective head Therapeutic Hypothermia maintaining core temperature at 33.5 degrees C (33-34 C) for 72 hours followed by slow rewarming","Active radiant-warmer hyperthermia at 38.5 degrees C for 48 hours","Immediate IV mannitol bolus with fluid overload"],"a":1,"e":"In term/near-term infants (\u003e= 35-36 weeks, \u003e= 1800-2000 g) presenting within 6 hours of birth with severe perinatal acidosis (pH \u003c= 7.00 or BD \u003e= 16) and moderate-to-severe encephalopathy (Sarnat Stage II-III), controlled Therapeutic Hypothermia (target rectal/oesophageal temperature 33.5 C for 72 hours, followed by gradual rewarming at \u003c= 0.5 C/hour) attenuates secondary energy failure, apoptosis, and excitotoxicity, significantly improving survival free of cerebral palsy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03268","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Paediatric Diabetic Ketoacidosis","q":"A 7-year-old girl receiving IV fluids and insulin infusion for severe DKA (initial pH 7.05) suddenly develops a severe headache, repeated vomiting, slowing heart rate from 130 to 68/min, rising blood pressure (132/88 mmHg), and a drop in GCS from 15 to 10 at hour 8 of therapy. What is the immediate life-saving diagnosis and treatment?","o":["Acute hypoglycaemic coma; give 50% dextrose bolus and double IV normal saline rate","Normal recovery phase of DKA; reassure and continue rapid IV fluids","DKA-associated cerebral oedema; immediately administer IV Mannitol (0.5-1 g/kg) OR 3% Hypertonic Saline (2.5-5 mL/kg), elevate head of bed 30 degrees, and reduce IV fluid rate","Acute appendicitis; send for abdominal CT before any brain-directed treatment"],"a":2,"e":"Cerebral oedema is the leading cause of mortality in paediatric DKA, typically manifesting 4-12 hours into therapy with headache, recurrent vomiting, Cushing triad (bradycardia, hypertension, irregular respiration), pupillary changes, and declining GCS. Treatment is a clinical emergency that must NEVER wait for CT head: immediately give IV Mannitol (0.5-1 g/kg over 10-15 min) or 3% Hypertonic Saline, elevate head 30 degrees, reduce fluid infusion rate by one-third, and prepare airway protection avoiding hyperventilation unless herniating.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03269","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Postoperative Residual Neuromuscular Blockade","q":"At the end of laparoscopic surgery under rocuronium relaxation, quantitative ulnar-nerve Train-of-Four (TOF) monitoring reveals a TOF count of 1 twitch only (TOF ratio cannot be calculated; deep block). Why must Neostigmine NOT be given at TOF count 1, and what is the appropriate reversal agent for rocuronium to ensure TOF ratio \u003e= 0.90 before extubation?","o":["Administer high-dose Neostigmine 10 mg IV immediately for rapid reversal of deep block","Administer IV Naloxone 0.4 mg to reverse rocuronium","Extubate immediately at TOF ratio 0.65 because clinical head-lift suffices","Neostigmine cannot reliably reverse deep block (TOF \u003c 2-4 twitches) due to ceiling effect; administer IV Sugammadex (4 mg/kg for PTC 1-2 / deep block) OR maintain anaesthesia until \u003e= 2-4 twitches return"],"a":3,"e":"Neostigmine (an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor) has a ceiling effect and fails to reverse deep neuromuscular blockade (TOF count 0-1), risking dangerous postoperative residual curarisation (TOF ratio \u003c 0.90 causes pharyngeal aspiration, airway obstruction, and impaired hypoxic ventilatory drive). Sugammadex (modified gamma-cyclodextrin) selectively encapsulates aminosteroid blockers (rocuronium/vecuronium) at ANY depth (2 mg/kg for \u003e=2 twitches; 4 mg/kg for deep block 1-2 PTC; 16 mg/kg immediate rescue).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03270","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Post-Dural Puncture Headache (PDPH)","q":"Thirty-six hours after spinal anaesthesia for Caesarean delivery, a 27-year-old woman develops a severe throbbing fronto-occipital headache radiating to the neck with mild photophobia that worsens dramatically within minutes of sitting upright and resolves within minutes of lying flat. After 48 hours of hydration, oral analgesics, and caffeine, the severe postural headache remains incapacitating. What is the gold-standard definitive treatment?","o":["Autologous Epidural Blood Patch (15-20 mL sterile autologous blood injected into the epidural space at or one level below the puncture)","High-dose IV mannitol infusion","Oral sumatriptan monotherapy for 1 month","Immediate lumbar puncture to drain 30 mL more CSF"],"a":0,"e":"PDPH results from persistent CSF leakage through a dural tear exceeding CSF production, causing intracranial hypotension and traction on pain-sensitive meningeal structures/bridging veins when upright. Use of small-gauge (25G-27G) pencil-point (Whitacre/Sprotte) needles reduces incidence. When conservative measures fail or cranial nerve (CN VI) palsy/severe disability persists \u003e24-48 h, an autologous Epidural Blood Patch (15-20 mL) achieves immediate relief in \u003e75-90% by sealing the dural defect and raising epidural pressure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03271","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis (CVST)","q":"A 26-year-old woman 10 days postpartum develops worsening headache, papilloedema, focal motor seizures, and left hemiparesis. Contrast-enhanced CT head shows a triangular filling defect surrounded by enhancing dura within the posterior superior sagittal sinus (\u0027empty delta sign\u0027) and a small right parasagittal haemorrhagic venous infarct. MR venography confirms superior sagittal sinus thrombosis. What is the primary medical treatment?","o":["Immediate IV alteplase systemic thrombolysis","Therapeutic anticoagulation (LMWH or unfractionated heparin bridged to warfarin/DOAC) DESPITE the small venous haemorrhagic component","Withhold all anticoagulation permanently because of the haemorrhagic venous infarct","Immediate carotid stenting"],"a":1,"e":"Puerperium is a classic prothrombotic state for Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis (CVST), showing the hyperdense cord sign on NCCT, \u0027empty delta sign\u0027 on CECT, and absent flow on MRV. Crucially, venous haemorrhagic infarction results from venous outflow hypertension rather than arterial rupture; therefore, therapeutic anticoagulation (LMWH/UFH) is STILL the standard first-line treatment to recanalise the sinus and reduce venous pressure—and is NOT contraindicated by CVST-related haemorrhage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03272","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Thyroid Ultrasound","q":"On high-resolution neck ultrasonography, a 1.3 cm right thyroid nodule is solid (2 pts), markedly hypoechoic (3 pts), taller-than-wide in transverse view (3 pts), has irregular/lobulated margins (2 pts), and contains punctate echogenic foci/microcalcifications (3 pts) — total ACR TI-RADS score 13 points. How is this nodule categorised and managed?","o":["Immediate total thyroidectomy without preoperative cytology","TI-RADS 3 (TR3 — Mildly Suspicious); FNA only if \u003e= 2.5 cm","TI-RADS 5 (TR5 — Highly Suspicious); perform ultrasound-guided Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA) because size \u003e= 1.0 cm","TI-RADS 1 (TR1 — Benign); no follow-up or FNA required"],"a":2,"e":"Under ACR TI-RADS, \u003e= 7 points classifies a nodule as TR5 (Highly Suspicious for malignancy, e.g., papillary thyroid carcinoma). For TR5 nodules, the size threshold for ultrasound-guided FNA is \u003e= 1.0 cm (whereas TR4 FNA threshold is \u003e= 1.5 cm, and TR3 FNA threshold is \u003e= 2.5 cm).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03273","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Adolescent Hip Disorders","q":"An obese 13-year-old boy presents with a 3-week history of progressive limp and right medial thigh/knee pain without significant trauma. On examination, passive flexion of the right hip causes obligatory external rotation and abduction (Drehmann sign). Anteroposterior and frog-leg lateral pelvic radiographs show posterior-inferior displacement of the femoral head epiphysis, and Klein\u0027s line drawn along the superior femoral neck fails to intersect the lateral epiphysis (Trethowan sign). What is the definitive surgical treatment?","o":["Oral NSAIDs and return to contact sports","Immediate total hip arthroplasty","Forceful manipulative closed reduction and spica cast for 12 weeks","Non-weight-bearing immediately followed by urgent in-situ single cannulated screw fixation across the physis (without forceful closed reduction)"],"a":3,"e":"SCFE occurs in peripubertal (often obese or endocrine-disordered) adolescents through the hypertrophic zone of the proximal femoral physis, frequently presenting as referred knee pain and Drehmann sign. Frog-leg lateral radiographs are most sensitive, and Klein\u0027s line misses the slipped epiphysis. Urgent in-situ single central cannulated screw pinning prevents slip progression; forceful closed reduction of a chronic/stable slip carries a high risk of avascular necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03274","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Pharmacogenetic Screening","q":"Before initiating oral Abacavir in a patient with HIV, and before initiating Azathioprine / 6-Mercaptopurine in a patient with inflammatory bowel disease or ALL, which respective genetic/enzyme tests prevent fatal hypersensitivity reaction and severe pancytopenia?","o":["HLA-B*57:01 allele testing before Abacavir; TPMT and NUDT15 genotyping/activity before Azathioprine/6-MP","VKORC1 before Abacavir; NAT2 before Azathioprine","HLA-B*15:02 before Azathioprine; G6PD before Abacavir","CYP2C19 before Abacavir; UGT1A1 before Azathioprine"],"a":0,"e":"HLA-B*57:01 screening is mandatory before prescribing Abacavir; carriers have a ~50%+ risk of life-threatening abacavir hypersensitivity syndrome (and must never be rechallenged). Deficiency of Thiopurine S-Methyltransferase (TPMT) or NUDT15 (especially prevalent in Asian/Indian populations) impairs inactivation of azathioprine/6-mercaptopurine, shunting metabolites to toxic 6-thioguanine nucleotides and causing profound myelosuppression.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03275","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Research Methodology and Biostatistics","topic":"Confounding Control","q":"A pharmacoepidemiology researcher uses hospital electronic health records to compare mortality between two antihypertensive drugs. Propensity Score Matching (PSM) balances all recorded baseline variables (age, sex, diabetes, baseline BP), yet unmeasured frailty and socioeconomic status remain unrecorded. Which statement accurately describes Propensity Score Matching versus Instrumental Variable (IV) / Mendelian Randomisation analysis?","o":["Stratification and multivariable regression eliminate unmeasured confounders completely","Propensity Score Matching balances ONLY measured confounders and remains vulnerable to unmeasured confounding, whereas a valid Instrumental Variable can address unmeasured confounding","Propensity Score Matching eliminates all unmeasured confounding identically to an RCT","Randomisation in an RCT removes measured confounders only, not unmeasured confounders"],"a":1,"e":"Propensity score methods (matching, weighting, stratification) and multivariable regression can balance ONLY observed/measured covariates included in the model; residual bias from unmeasured confounders persists. By contrast, a valid Instrumental Variable (e.g., genetic variants in Mendelian Randomisation) predicts exposure independently of both measured and unmeasured confounders and affects the outcome solely through that exposure—mitigating unmeasured confounding. (Only true randomisation in an adequately powered RCT balances both known and unknown confounders by design).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03276","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Pre-Excitation Syndromes","q":"A 23-year-old man with known Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome (short PR interval and delta wave on baseline ECG) presents with palpitations, BP 82/52 mmHg, and an ECG showing an irregularly irregular, very rapid (220/min) wide-complex tachycardia with beat-to-beat variation in QRS morphology (pre-excited atrial fibrillation). Which intervention is indicated, and why are AV-nodal blockers (adenosine, verapamil, diltiazem, digoxin, beta-blockers) strictly contraindicated?","o":["IV Adenosine 12 mg push followed by IV digoxin","IV Amiodarone + oral diltiazem combination as safest routine first-line in unstable WPW-AF","Immediate synchronised DC cardioversion (or IV Procainamide/Ibutilide if stable); AV-nodal blockers force all atrial impulses down the rapid accessory pathway, precipitating ventricular fibrillation","IV Verapamil 5 mg bolus to slow ventricular rate via the AV node"],"a":2,"e":"Pre-excited atrial fibrillation in WPW conducts rapidly antegrade down the Bundle of Kent (bypassing AV nodal delay), producing a fast, irregular, polymorphic-appearing wide-complex tachycardia. Haemodynamically unstable patients require immediate synchronised DC cardioversion (stable patients receive IV procainamide or ibutilide to prolong accessory pathway refractoriness). AV-nodal blockers (adenosine, diltiazem, verapamil, beta-blockers, digoxin) eliminate competing AV-nodal conduction/concealed retrograde penetration, accelerating accessory pathway rate into lethal Ventricular Fibrillation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03277","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Interstitial Lung Disease","q":"A 67-year-old retired male smoker with insidious exertional dyspnoea, clubbing, and bilateral fine bibasilar \u0027Velcro\u0027 crackles undergoes HRCT chest showing subpleural, basal-predominant reticular opacities, traction bronchiectasis, and subpleural honeycombing without ground-glass predominance (definite Usual Interstitial Pneumonia [UIP] pattern). Secondary causes (CTD, drugs, hypersensitivity pneumonitis) are excluded. Which medical therapy slows FVC decline, and which historical regimen increases mortality?","o":["Oral methotrexate + cyclophosphamide cures UIP honeycombing","Long-term oral macrolide monotherapy reverses pulmonary fibrosis","Chronic high-dose prednisolone + azathioprine is first-line; antifibrotics are contraindicated","Antifibrotic therapy (Nintedanib or Pirfenidone) slows lung-function decline; chronic prednisolone + azathioprine + N-acetylcysteine increases hospitalisation and mortality"],"a":3,"e":"Definite UIP pattern on HRCT (basal/subpleural reticulation, traction bronchiectasis, honeycombing) after excluding secondary ILD establishes Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF) without needing surgical lung biopsy. Based on the INPULSIS/CAPACITY/ASCEND trials, antifibrotics (Nintedanib or Pirfenidone) significantly slow FVC decline, whereas the landmark PANTHER-IPF trial proved that triple immunosuppression (prednisone + azathioprine + NAC) significantly increases death and hospitalisation in IPF.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03278","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Nephrotic Glomerulopathies","q":"A 48-year-old man presents with anasarca, 9.5 g/day proteinuria, serum albumin 1.9 g/dL, and sudden left flank pain with macroscopic haematuria due to acute left renal vein thrombosis. Serum anti-phospholipase A2 receptor (anti-PLA2R) antibodies are strongly positive, and renal biopsy shows diffuse glomerular basement membrane thickening (\u0027spikes\u0027 on silver stain) with granular subepithelial IgG/C3 deposits. What is the diagnosis and high-risk indication for Rituximab/immunosuppression?","o":["Primary Membranous Nephropathy (anti-PLA2R positive); anticoagulation for renal vein thrombosis + Rituximab (or calcineurin inhibitor/cyclophosphamide-steroid) given severe persistent nephrotic syndrome/complications","IgA nephropathy; routine lifelong warfarin without immunosuppression","Minimal change disease; 2-week course of oral amoxicillin","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis; immediate bilateral nephrectomy"],"a":0,"e":"Anti-PLA2R autoantibodies are detected in ~70-80% of Primary Membranous Nephropathy (characterised by subepithelial immune complexes, GBM \u0027spike-and-dome\u0027 appearance, and the highest risk of renal vein thrombosis among nephrotic syndromes due to urinary loss of antithrombin III). Persistent heavy proteinuria/severe hypoalbuminaemia or life-threatening complications warrant anticoagulation + immunosuppression (Rituximab per MENTOR trial, or cyclic cyclophosphamide-glucocorticoid / CNI) alongside ACEi/ARB.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03279","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"HIV Opportunistic Pulmonary Infections","q":"A 36-year-old man with untreated HIV (CD4 count 42 cells/mm3) presents with 3 weeks of progressive exertional dyspnoea, dry cough, bilateral symmetric perihilar interstitial infiltrates, elevated LDH (640 U/L), and positive BAL Gomori methenamine silver stain for Pneumocystis jirovecii. Room-air ABG shows PaO2 56 mmHg and A-a oxygen gradient 48 mmHg. Alongside high-dose Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX), what adjunctive therapy reduces respiratory failure and mortality?","o":["Delay all corticosteroids unless mechanical ventilation fails after 14 days","Early adjunctive systemic Corticosteroids (prednisone 40 mg BID x 5 days, then taper over 21 days) started before or with TMP-SMX because PaO2 \u003c 70 mmHg or A-a gradient \u003e= 35 mmHg","Oral fluconazole 400 mg/day for 21 days","Inhaled salmeterol alone without systemic steroids"],"a":1,"e":"In HIV-associated PJP, antimicrobial lysis of organisms triggers an intense alveolar inflammatory surge over days 2-4 that worsens hypoxaemia. When room-air PaO2 \u003c 70 mmHg or alveolar-arterial (A-a) gradient \u003e= 35 mmHg, starting adjunctive Prednisolone (40 mg BID days 1-5, 40 mg OD days 6-10, 20 mg OD days 11-21) within 72 hours (ideally before/with first TMP-SMX dose) significantly reduces intubation and death.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03280","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Status Epilepticus","q":"A 30-year-old man has ongoing generalised tonic-clonic convulsive status epilepticus for 12 minutes. Airway, oxygen, and point-of-care glucose (normal 110 mg/dL) are secured, and two adequate doses of IV Lorazepam (0.1 mg/kg, 4 mg x 2) fail to terminate seizures by minute 18. According to ESETT and standard status epilepticus protocols, which second-line IV antiseizure medication class is appropriate immediately before escalating to anaesthetic coma?","o":["Oral carbamazepine tablet crushed via NG tube","IV pyridoxine monotherapy in all adults","Weight-based IV Levetiracetam (60 mg/kg, max 4,500 mg) OR IV Fosphenytoin (20 mg PE/kg) OR IV Valproate (40 mg/kg)","Repeat 6 additional doses of IV lorazepam over the next 2 hours without adding a non-benzodiazepine AED"],"a":2,"e":"Per the Established Status Epilepticus Treatment Trial (ESETT) and AAN/AES guidelines, benzodiazepine-refractory convulsive status epilepticus (minutes 20-40) requires prompt full weight-based loading with one of three equally efficacious IV non-benzodiazepine agents: Levetiracetam (60 mg/kg, max 4.5 g), Fosphenytoin (20 mg PE/kg), or Sodium Valproate (40 mg/kg, max 3 g). If seizures persist \u003e40 minutes, third-line continuous anaesthetic infusion (midazolam, propofol, or pentobarbital) with intubation and continuous EEG is initiated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03281","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP)","q":"A 35-year-old woman presents with fever, fluctuating confusion, petechiae, Hb 7.1 g/dL, platelets 11,000/mm3, numerous schistocytes on peripheral smear, negative direct Coombs test, LDH 1,650 U/L, normal PT/aPTT/fibrinogen, and creatinine 1.4 mg/dL (PLASMIC score 7, high probability of TTP). ADAMTS13 activity assay will take 48 hours. What is the mandatory immediate management, and which blood product must be avoided?","o":["Transfuse 8 units of platelets immediately before starting plasma exchange in 48 hours","Administer IV heparin bolus for presumed DIC","Wait 48 hours for ADAMTS13 result before starting TPE","Initiate daily Therapeutic Plasma Exchange (TPE) plus corticosteroids IMMEDIATELY (and consider caplacizumab); AVOID prophylactic platelet transfusion"],"a":3,"e":"Acquired immune TTP (autoantibodies against ADAMTS13 cleaving ultra-large vWF multimers) has \u003e90% mortality if untreated, falling to \u003c10-20% with immediate daily Therapeutic Plasma Exchange (which removes inhibitory autoantibodies and replenishes ADAMTS13) plus glucocorticoids (+/- rituximab/caplacizumab). Because TPE must never wait for send-out ADAMTS13 results when PLASMIC score is high, initiate TPE immediately. Platelet transfusions precipitate catastrophic microvascular arterial/cerebral thrombosis (\u0027fuel on the fire\u0027) and are contraindicated unless life-threatening bleeding occurs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03282","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Oesophageal Carcinoma","q":"A 59-year-old man with progressive dysphagia and weight loss is diagnosed with a locally advanced mid-to-distal thoracic oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma staged on EUS and PET-CT as cT3N1M0 (resectable, no distant metastases, good performance status). Based on the CROSS trial and standard oesophageal oncology guidelines, what is the optimal curative strategy?","o":["Neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy (carboplatin + paclitaxel with 41.4 Gy radiotherapy) followed by surgical oesophagectomy with two-field lymphadenectomy","Primary high-dose brachytherapy alone","Surgery alone followed by no adjuvant therapy","Upfront palliative oesophageal metallic stenting alone"],"a":0,"e":"For locally advanced resectable oesophageal carcinoma (cT2-T4a or N+ M0, thoracic/GEJ), the landmark CROSS trial established that preoperative neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy (weekly carboplatin + paclitaxel concurrent with 41.4 Gy external-beam radiation) followed by transthoracic oesophagectomy significantly increases R0 resection rates, pathological complete response (especially ~49% in squamous cell carcinoma), and 5-year overall survival compared with surgery alone.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03283","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Hepatic Hydatid Disease (Echinococcosis)","q":"A 36-year-old shepherd from rural Kashmir presents with dull right hypochondrial fullness. CECT abdomen reveals a well-defined 9 cm unilocular hepatic cyst in Segment VI-VII containing multiple peripheral daughter cysts and detached endocyst membranes (\u0027water-lily sign\u0027). Prior to percutaneous PAIR or surgical pericystectomy, which perioperative medical protocol and intraoperative precaution are mandatory?","o":["Inject 40% formalin directly into a cyst with frank cystobiliary communication","Perioperative oral Albendazole (starting \u003e= 1-4 weeks pre-procedure) to sterilise protoscolices, plus surrounding the cyst with scolicidal-soaked packs (e.g., hypertonic saline/cetrimide) to prevent anaphylaxis and peritoneal seeding—avoiding formaline if biliary communication exists","Immediate unguided blind bedside aspiration without albendazole or anaphylaxis readiness","Treat with oral albendazole for 5 days alone as permanent cure for a 9 cm cyst"],"a":1,"e":"Echinococcus granulosus hepatic hydatid cysts contain antigenic hydatid fluid and viable protoscolices; accidental spillage triggers fatal IgE-mediated anaphylactic shock and disseminated peritoneal hydatidosis. Pre- and post-procedural Albendazole reduces cyst tension and scolex viability, while scolicidal packs/agents (hypertonic 20% saline, 0.5% cetrimide, or povidone-iodine) protect the field. Scolicidal agents must NEVER be injected if cystobiliary communication is present because they cause devastating sclerosing cholangitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03284","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumours","q":"A 37-year-old woman presents with recurrent early-morning confusion and diaphoresis relieved rapidly by sugary drinks (Whipple\u0027s triad). During a supervised 72-hour fast, at hour 28 her venous plasma glucose drops to 39 mg/dL with neuroglycopenic symptoms, simultaneous serum insulin is 18 mcU/mL (inappropriately high), C-peptide is 3.4 ng/mL (elevated), proinsulin is high, beta-hydroxybutyrate is suppressed, and serum sulfonylurea/meglitinide screen is negative. Endoscopic ultrasound localises a 1.4 cm hypervascular lesion in the body of the pancreas away from the main pancreatic duct. What is the diagnosis and treatment?","o":["Factitious exogenous insulin injection; psychiatric counselling alone","Sulfonylurea overdose; oral diazoxide indefinitely","Benign sporadic Insulinoma; surgical enucleation of the pancreatic tumour","Nesidioblastosis requiring total pancreatectomy immediately"],"a":2,"e":"Whipple\u0027s triad with fasting hypoglycaemia (\u003c55 mg/dL), unsuppressed endogenous insulin (\u003e= 3 mcU/mL), elevated C-peptide (\u003e= 0.6 ng/mL), and negative sulfonylurea screen confirms Insulinoma (\u003e90% benign, solitary, intrapancreatic). Because this 1.4 cm tumour is well-circumscribed and separate from the main pancreatic duct, parenchyma-sparing surgical enucleation is curative (distal pancreatectomy is used if abutting the duct).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03285","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Bladder Carcinoma","q":"A 61-year-old male smoker with painless gross haematuria undergoes TURBT showing high-grade urothelial carcinoma invading the detrusor muscle (muscularis propria, cT2N0M0). Staging CT chest/abdomen/pelvis is negative for metastases, and CrCl is 75 mL/min with good hearing and cardiac function. What is the Category 1 gold-standard curative treatment?","o":["Simple partial cystectomy without systemic chemotherapy","Oral tamoxifen plus observation","Repeat TURBT alone and intravesical BCG instillation","Cisplatin-based Neoadjuvant Chemotherapy (dose-dense MVAC or Gemcitabine-Cisplatin) followed by Radical Cystectomy and bilateral pelvic lymphadenectomy with urinary diversion"],"a":3,"e":"Infiltration into the detrusor muscle (cT2-T4a N0 M0) defines Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer (MIBC). Because micrometastases are common, Level 1 evidence demonstrates an absolute ~5-8% overall survival advantage from Cisplatin-based Neoadjuvant Chemotherapy (ddMVAC or GC) BEFORE Radical Cystectomy + extended pelvic lymph node dissection (with ileal conduit or orthotopic neobladder; trimodality bladder preservation is an alternative in select patients).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03286","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Acute Limb Ischaemia","q":"A 68-year-old man presents 4 hours after sudden onset of severe right leg pain. Examination shows a pale, cold right foot, absent popliteal/pedal pulses, inaudible arterial Doppler signals (audible venous Doppler), sensory loss over the toes and dorsum of the foot, and mild-to-moderate toe dorsiflexion weakness (Rutherford Class IIb — Immediately Threatened). After immediate IV unfractionated heparin bolus, what is the required surgical action?","o":["Immediate emergency surgical thromboembolectomy / revascularisation (without delaying for prolonged catheter-directed thrombolysis over 24-48 h)","Primary above-knee amputation immediately (reserved only for irreversible Class III)","Slow outpatient catheter thrombolysis over 48 hours without urgency","Oral aspirin and warfarin discharge"],"a":0,"e":"Rutherford Acute Limb Ischaemia classification dictates urgency: Class I (viable, no sensory/motor loss, audible arterial Doppler) allows CTA/thrombolysis; Class IIa (marginally threatened, minimal sensory loss, no motor deficit) permits urgent endovascular or surgical revascularisation; Class IIb (immediately threatened, rest pain, sensory loss beyond toes, mild-to-moderate motor weakness) demands EMERGENCY surgical revascularisation (Fogarty embolectomy/bypass + fasciotomy if prolonged) within hours to salvage the limb; Class III (irreversible paralysis, rigor, inaudible venous/arterial Doppler) requires primary amputation to avoid fatal reperfusion syndrome.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03287","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Malignant Ovarian Germ Cell Tumours","q":"A 19-year-old nulliparous woman presents with a rapidly enlarging, solid 11 cm right adnexal mass. Serum LDH is markedly elevated (1,450 U/L), while AFP and CA-125 are normal (mildly elevated beta-hCG in 5%). Staging CT shows a unilateral right ovarian mass with enlarged para-aortic nodes and a normal uterus and left ovary. Frozen section confirms ovarian Dysgerminoma. What is the standard surgical management?","o":["Fertility-sparing unilateral right salpingo-oophorectomy with complete surgical staging (preserving uterus and normal contralateral ovary), followed by BEP chemotherapy","Simple ovarian cystectomy leaving tumour capsule behind without staging","Routine total abdominal hysterectomy and bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy sacrificing future fertility","Neoadjuvant external-beam pelvic radiotherapy before biopsy"],"a":0,"e":"Dysgerminoma is the commonest malignant ovarian germ cell tumour (counterpart of testicular seminoma), typically affecting adolescents/young women, presenting with elevated LDH (whereas yolk sac tumour elevates AFP and choriocarcinoma elevates beta-hCG). Because germ cell tumours are exquisitely chemosensitive (Bleomycin, Etoposide, Cisplatin — BEP) and occur in reproductive-age patients, fertility-sparing unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy + staging (preserving uterus and contralateral ovary, even in nodal/advanced stages) achieves \u003e90-95% cure rates.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03288","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Cervical Insufficiency","q":"A 29-year-old G2P1 woman with a history of one spontaneous painless preterm delivery at 26 weeks is currently carrying a singleton pregnancy at 18 weeks. Serial transvaginal cervical length measurement reveals cervical shortening to 19 mm with funnelling, no uterine contractions, intact membranes, and no vaginal bleeding or infection. What is the evidence-based intervention?","o":["Strict bed rest alone without cerclage or progesterone","Ultrasound-indicated cervical cerclage (McDonald or Shirodkar) alongside vaginal progesterone","Emergency Caesarean section at 18 weeks","Oral nifedipine tocolysis from 18 to 37 weeks"],"a":1,"e":"In a singleton gestation with a prior spontaneous preterm birth \u003c 34 weeks AND a shortened transvaginal cervical length \u003c 25 mm prior to 24 weeks, ultrasound-indicated cervical cerclage significantly reduces recurrent preterm birth and perinatal mortality (whereas in low-risk nulliparas without prior preterm birth and cervical length \u003c= 20-25 mm, vaginal progesterone alone is first-line).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03289","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Duct-Dependent Congenital Heart Disease","q":"A 5-day-old term neonate discharged well on day 2 is brought to the emergency room with acute grey pallor, lethargy, severe tachypnoea, BP 48/26 mmHg, weak-to-absent femoral pulses, hepatomegaly, lactate 11 mmol/L, and arterial pH 7.08, unresponsive to a 20 mL/kg saline bolus. What life-saving infusion MUST be started immediately on suspicion of duct-dependent systemic circulation (e.g., critical coarctation of aorta / hypoplastic left heart syndrome)?","o":["High-volume 100 mL/kg crystalloid bolus alone","IV Epinephrine 1 mg bolus without prostaglandin","IV Prostaglandin E1 (Alprostadil) infusion to reopen and maintain patency of the ductus arteriosus (prepared for apnoea monitoring)","IV Indomethacin to accelerate closure of the ductus arteriosus"],"a":2,"e":"Closure of the ductus arteriosus at days 3-10 of life unmasks duct-dependent systemic obstructive lesions (critical coarctation of aorta, interrupted aortic arch, critical aortic stenosis, HLHS), presenting as sudden cardiogenic shock, absent femoral pulses, differential cyanosis, and severe lactic acidosis mimicking neonatal sepsis. Immediate IV Prostaglandin E1 (0.05-0.1 mcg/kg/min) reopens the PDA to restore lower-body/systemic perfusion while monitoring for PGE1-induced apnoea.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03290","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Systemic Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis (sJIA)","q":"A 6-year-old boy being treated for systemic-onset Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis (quotidian spiking fever, evanescent salmon-pink rash, arthritis) suddenly becomes lethargic with persistent unremitting fever, hepatosplenomegaly, mucosal bleeding, pancytopenia, AST 480 U/L, triglycerides 460 mg/dL, fibrinogen dropping to 90 mg/dL, serum ferritin surging to 18,000 ng/mL, and a PARADOXICAL drop in ESR from 95 to 12 mm/h. What is this complication and its emergency treatment?","o":["Simple iron-overload reaction; deferoxamine alone","Acute rheumatic fever relapse; oral penicillin alone","Resolution of sJIA inflammation because ESR dropped; taper all medications","Macrophage Activation Syndrome (secondary HLH); high-dose pulse IV Methylprednisolone plus Cyclosporine A / IL-1 blockade (Anakinra)"],"a":3,"e":"Macrophage Activation Syndrome (MAS, a secondary haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis cytokine storm) is a fatal complication of sJIA. A classic red flag is a sudden PARADOXICAL fall in ESR (caused by consumptive hypofibrinogenaemia!) alongside unremitting fever, cytopenias, skyrocketing ferritin (\u003e684-10,000 ng/mL), hypertriglyceridaemia, transaminitis, and haemophagocytosis on bone marrow. Immediate pulse IV methylprednisolone + cyclosporine A / anakinra (IL-1 receptor antagonist) is life-saving.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03291","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Smoke Inhalation Injury","q":"A 39-year-old man rescued from an enclosed synthetic-plastics warehouse fire arrives unconscious, hypotensive, with soot around the nares, bright-red venous blood, arterial lactate 14.5 mmol/L, high anion gap metabolic acidosis, and carboxyhaemoglobin only 8%. Suspecting concurrent Cyanide toxicity inhibiting mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase, which antidote safely binds cyanide without impairing oxygen-carrying capacity in a fire victim?","o":["IV Hydroxocobalamin (Vitamin B12a, 5 g IV) forming non-toxic renal-excreted cyanocobalamin","IV Methylene blue 2 mg/kg","IV Sodium nitrite alone to deliberately induce 30% methaemoglobinaemia","Hyperbaric oxygen alone without cyanide antidote"],"a":0,"e":"Combustion of polyurethane/plastics releases hydrogen cyanide alongside carbon monoxide. Severe lactic acidosis (\u003e8-10 mmol/L), cardiovascular collapse, and elevated venous oxygen saturation (\u0027arterialisation\u0027 of venous blood due to inability of tissues to extract O2) in a fire victim strongly signal cyanide poisoning. Hydroxocobalamin (5 g IV over 15 min) directly chelates cyanide into urinary cyanocobalamin safely—avoiding sodium nitrite-induced methaemoglobinaemia, which would compound coexisting carboxyhaemoglobinaemia hypoxia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03292","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Venous Air Embolism (VAE)","q":"During a posterior fossa craniotomy in the sitting position under N2O/O2/sevoflurane anaesthesia, precordial Doppler suddenly changes to a harsh \u0027mill-wheel\u0027 roar, end-tidal CO2 drops abruptly from 35 to 14 mmHg, SpO2 falls to 86%, and BP drops to 68/40 mmHg. Which immediate sequence of actions is mandatory?","o":["Perform immediate bilateral needle thoracostomy for presumed tension pneumothorax","Discontinue nitrous oxide (give 100% O2), inform surgeon to flood surgical field with saline and seal open venous sinuses with bone wax, compress jugular veins briefly, aspirate air via right-atrial CVC, and lower head / place left-lateral decubitus (Durant) position","Administer IV mannitol and elevate the head higher by 30 degrees","Increase nitrous oxide to 70% to wash out nitrogen bubbles faster"],"a":1,"e":"Non-collapsible dural venous sinuses above heart level in the sitting position create negative venous pressure entraining air. Sudden drop in ETCO2 (increased alveolar dead space), hypotension, and \u0027mill-wheel\u0027 murmur indicate massive VAE. Nitrous oxide diffuses into air bubbles rapidly expanding their volume and MUST be stopped immediately (100% O2), while the surgeon floods the field/waxes diploic veins, air is aspirated via the multi-orifice RA catheter, and the patient is lowered/placed in Durant (left-lateral Trendelenburg) position to trap air in the RV apex away from the pulmonary outflow tract.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03293","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC)","q":"On multiphasic pancreatic-protocol CT angiography, a 3.8 cm hypodense mass in the pancreatic head shows 270-degree circumferential soft-tissue encasement (\u003e180 degrees) of the Superior Mesenteric Artery (SMA) and occlusion of the coeliac axis without distant liver/lung metastases. How is this tumour staged for surgical resectability?","o":["Benign chronic mass-forming pancreatitis requiring no biopsy","Borderline Resectable simply because \u003e 180 degrees SMA encasement allows routine arterial grafting in all cases","Locally Advanced Unresectable (arterial encasement \u003e 180 degrees of SMA/coeliac axis); treat with systemic chemotherapy (+/- chemoradiation) rather than upfront Whipple","Clearly Resectable; proceed immediately to upfront Whipple pancreaticoduodenectomy tomorrow"],"a":2,"e":"Under NCCN pancreatic-protocol CT criteria: Resectable = no arterial contact and \u003c= 180-degree SMV/PV contact without vein contour irregularity; Borderline Resectable = \u003c= 180-degree abutment of SMA/coeliac axis or short-segment reconstructible SMV/PV involvement; Locally Advanced Unresectable = \u003e180-degree encasement of SMA or coeliac trunk (or unreconstructible SMV/PV occlusion). Biopsy and systemic chemotherapy (e.g., FOLFIRINOX) are indicated first.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03294","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Paediatric Malignant Bone Tumours","q":"An 11-year-old boy presents with 2 months of worsening right thigh pain, low-grade fever, anaemia, and elevated ESR (mimicking osteomyelitis). Radiograph of the femur reveals an ill-defined permeative \u0027moth-eaten\u0027 osteolytic lesion in the mid-DIAPHYSIS accompanied by concentric multilamellated (\u0027onion-skin\u0027) periosteal reaction and a large soft-tissue mass. Biopsy shows sheets of uniform small round blue cells positive for CD99 and t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1 translocation. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Osteoid osteoma","Conventional osteosarcoma","Chondroblastoma","Ewing sarcoma"],"a":3,"e":"Diaphyseal/metadiaphyseal permeative lytic lesion in a child/adolescent with systemic symptoms (fever, elevated ESR mimicking osteomyelitis), \u0027onion-skin\u0027 lamellated periosteal reaction, CD99+ small round blue cells, and t(11;22)(q24;q12) EWSR1-FLI1 fusion are classic for Ewing sarcoma. Treatment combines multi-agent neoadjuvant/adjuvant chemotherapy (VDC/IE) with wide surgical resection and/or radiotherapy (highly radiosensitive).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03295","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Traumatic Hip Dislocation","q":"A 29-year-old front-seat passenger sustains a \u0027dashboard injury\u0027 in a head-on collision and presents with severe right hip pain, inability to bear weight, and a shortened right lower limb locked in FLEXION, ADDUCTION, and INTERNAL ROTATION, with absent ankle dorsiflexion and foot drop (sciatic/common peroneal nerve stretch). Radiograph confirms posterior hip dislocation without femoral neck fracture. What is the mandatory emergency management?","o":["Urgent closed reduction (e.g., Allis or Stimson manoeuvre) under anaesthesia/sedation WITHIN 6 HOURS of injury, followed by post-reduction CT for concentricity and intra-articular fragments","Elective open reduction after 7 days of skin traction","Apply a Thomas splint in external rotation without reducing the hip","Immediate total hip arthroplasty in the emergency room"],"a":0,"e":"Posterior hip dislocation (~90% of hip dislocations, classic dashboard injury) holds the hip in Flexion, Adduction, and Internal Rotation (FADIR) and places the sciatic nerve (especially peroneal division) at risk (~10-20%). Because kinking/disruption of the medial femoral circumflex retinacular vessels causes Avascular Necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head whose incidence rises sharply beyond 6 hours (\u003c5% if reduced \u003c6 h vs \u003e20-50% if \u003e12 h), urgent closed reduction under full relaxation is a true orthopaedic emergency, followed by CT scan to check posterior acetabular wall fracture or entrapped fragments.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03296","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Panuveitis Syndromes","q":"A 32-year-old woman with no history of penetrating eye trauma presents with bilateral blurred vision, severe headache, meningismus, tinnitus, and bilateral granulomatous panuveitis with multifocal exudative (serous) retinal detachments (\u0027starry sky\u0027 pinpoint leaks on FFA) and CSF lymphocytic pleocytosis. Over subsequent months, perilimbal vitiligo (Sugiura\u0027s sign), poliosis, and \u0027sunset-glow\u0027 fundus develop. What is the diagnosis and urgent initial therapy?","o":["Acute central retinal vein occlusion; intravitreal anti-VEGF alone","Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada (VKH) syndrome; immediate high-dose systemic corticosteroids (IV methylprednisolone followed by slow oral taper) PLUS early steroid-sparing immunosuppressive therapy","Sympathetic ophthalmia (which requires prior penetrating trauma in the exciting eye); immediate enucleation","Fungal panophthalmitis; intravitreal amphotericin B without steroids"],"a":1,"e":"VKH disease is a T-cell-mediated autoimmune attack against melanocyte-associated antigens affecting the uvea, meninges, inner ear, and skin—presenting without prior ocular trauma/surgery as bilateral granulomatous panuveitis with exudative retinal detachments, CSF pleocytosis, auditory/meningeal signs, and later vitiligo/poliosis/sunset-glow fundus. Aggressive early high-dose systemic corticosteroids (slowly tapered over \u003e= 6 months) combined with early immunomodulators (azathioprine, mycophenolate, methotrexate, or cyclosporine) prevents chronic recurrence and blindness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03297","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Sudden Sensorineural Hearing Loss (SSNHL)","q":"A 40-year-old man wakes up with sudden unilateral hearing loss and tinnitus in his right ear without earache or discharge. Otoscopy is normal, Weber lateralises to the left (normal) ear, Rinne is positive bilaterally, and pure-tone audiometry shows a sensorineural hearing loss of 45 dB across three contiguous frequencies occurring within 24 hours (idiopathic SSNHL). What is the time-critical medical treatment and mandatory radiological workup?","o":["Oral amoxicillin + nasal decongestant for 10 days with no audiological/imaging follow-up","Reassure and repeat audiogram after 6 months without treatment","Urgent systemic (and/or intratympanic salvage) Corticosteroids within 2 weeks of onset PLUS gadolinium-enhanced MRI internal auditory canal to exclude vestibular schwannoma","Immediate stapedotomy surgery"],"a":2,"e":"Idiopathic Sudden Sensorineural Hearing Loss (SSNHL) is defined as \u003e= 30 dB sensorineural loss over \u003e= 3 contiguous frequencies developing within \u003c= 72 hours—an otological emergency. High-dose oral prednisolone (1 mg/kg/day up to 60 mg, initiated promptly within 2 weeks) and/or intratympanic dexamethasone salvage significantly improve hearing recovery. Up to 1-4% of SSNHL cases harbour a retrocochlear lesion (vestibular schwannoma / acoustic neuroma), mandating gadolinium-enhanced MRI of the brain/IAC.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03298","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Neutrophilic Dermatoses","q":"A 35-year-old woman with active ulcerative colitis develops a painful pustule on her shin after minor bumping that rapidly breaks down into a deep, exquisitely painful necrotic ulcer with a boggy, violaceous, undermined overhanging border and sterile purulent base (cultures negative). Why is aggressive surgical debridement strictly avoided in Pyoderma Gangrenosum, and what is the primary treatment?","o":["Apply topical permethrin cream alone","Treat as necrotising fasciitis with immediate amputation","Surgical wide debridement cures the ulcer immediately; avoid all immunosuppression","Surgical trauma triggers severe \u0027pathergy\u0027 causing catastrophic ulcer expansion; treat with systemic corticosteroids (plus cyclosporine/anti-TNF infliximab) and gentle local wound care"],"a":3,"e":"Pyoderma gangrenosum is a sterile neutrophilic dermatosis strongly associated with inflammatory bowel disease, rheumatoid arthritis, and myeloid malignancies, presenting as rapidly enlarging painful ulcers with violaceous undermined borders. Approximately 25-50% exhibit pathergy (lesions triggered or violently worsened by minor trauma or surgical debridement). Treatment relies on systemic immunosuppression (high-dose prednisolone, cyclosporine, or infliximab/adalimumab) and treating the underlying IBD while strictly avoiding sharp surgical debridement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03299","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Psychiatry","topic":"Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitor (MAOI) Toxicity","q":"A 46-year-old man taking phenelzine (an irreversible MAOI) for atypical depression eats aged cheddar cheese and red wine at a dinner, then presents 1 hour later with excruciating occipital headache, palpitations, diaphoresis, and BP 226/130 mmHg (\u0027cheese reaction\u0027). What is the pathophysiology and preferred acute IV antihypertensive?","o":["Dietary tyramine escapes gut/hepatic MAO-A degradation and displaces massive norepinephrine from sympathetic vesicles; treat with IV Phentolamine (alpha-blocker) or nicardipine/nitroprusside","Excess serotonin alone; treat with IV propranolol monotherapy (unopposed alpha constriction safe)","Inhibition of dopamine D2 receptors; treat with IM haloperidol","Tyramine directly activates central GABA receptors; treat with IV flumazenil"],"a":0,"e":"Irreversible MAO-A/B inhibitors block intestinal and hepatic tyramine breakdown, allowing dietary tyramine (aged cheese, cured meats, fermented soy, tap beer/chianti wine) to enter sympathetic nerve terminals and displace stored norepinephrine—precipitating severe alpha-adrenergic hypertensive crisis. Acute emergency BP control uses a short-acting parenteral alpha-adrenoceptor antagonist (IV Phentolamine) or titratable IV nicardipine/labetalol (avoiding pure non-selective beta-blockers alone which leave alpha vasoconstriction unopposed).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03300","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Research Methodology and Biostatistics","topic":"Non-Parametric Hypothesis Tests","q":"A surgical resident measures postoperative pain VAS scores (ordinal/heavily skewed non-normal data) in the SAME cohort of 40 patients at 4 serial timepoints (6 h, 12 h, 24 h, and 48 h). Which statistical test is most appropriate to compare pain scores across these 4 repeated timepoints, versus comparing 3 independent surgical technique groups at a single timepoint?","o":["Friedman test for the 4 paired/repeated timepoints; Kruskal-Wallis test for the 3 independent groups","Repeated-measures ANOVA for paired skewed data; Chi-square test for 3 continuous groups","Kruskal-Wallis test for the 4 paired timepoints; Wilcoxon signed-rank test for 3 independent groups","Unpaired Student\u0027s t-test for both designs"],"a":0,"e":"When continuous or ordinal data violate normality (skewed/VAS scores): (1) Comparing \u003e= 3 paired/matched or repeated measurements in the same subjects uses the Friedman test (non-parametric equivalent of repeated-measures ANOVA); (2) Comparing \u003e= 3 independent groups uses the Kruskal-Wallis H test (non-parametric equivalent of one-way ANOVA); (3) Comparing 2 paired groups uses Wilcoxon signed-rank; and (4) Comparing 2 independent groups uses Mann-Whitney U test.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03301","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Adult Congenital Heart Disease","q":"A 36-year-old woman with an unrepaired large ostium secundum Atrial Septal Defect (ASD) presents with progressive cyanosis (SpO2 82%), digital clubbing, loud palpable P2, right ventricular heave, and a right-to-left shunt across the ASD on echocardiography. Right-heart catheterisation shows mean pulmonary artery pressure 72 mmHg and fixed pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) \u003e 8 Wood units unresponsive to inhaled nitric oxide. Should surgical or device closure of the ASD be performed?","o":["YES; emergency surgical ASD patch closure cures Eisenmenger pulmonary hypertension","NO; ASD closure is strictly CONTRAINDICATED in established Eisenmenger syndrome because eliminating the right-to-left decompression \u0027pop-off\u0027 causes acute right ventricular failure and death","YES; percutaneous Amplatzer device closure reverses fixed PVR within 1 week","Perform immediate balloon atrial septostomy to further enlarge the shunt without pulmonary vasodilators"],"a":1,"e":"Chronic large left-to-right shunts induce irreversible pulmonary arteriolar medial hypertrophy and plexiform obliteration, eventually raising pulmonary pressures above systemic pressure and reversing the shunt right-to-left (Eisenmenger syndrome). At this stage, the ASD serves as an essential pressure-relief valve maintaining left ventricular preload and systemic cardiac output. Closing the defect precipitates immediate fatal RV failure; management focuses on targeted pulmonary vasodilators (bosentan, sildenafil), avoiding dehydration/pregnancy/high altitude, and heart-lung transplantation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03302","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Massive Haemoptysis","q":"A 45-year-old man with post-tubercular right upper-lobe bronchiectasis/aspergilloma coughs up 450 mL of bright-red blood over 3 hours, becoming dyspnoeic and hypoxaemic (SpO2 84%). Chest radiograph localises the cavity to the RIGHT lung. What is the immediate positioning and airway/interventional strategy to prevent asphyxiation?","o":["Administer systemic IV alteplase","Place patient with the bleeding RIGHT lung UP so gravity drains blood into the healthy left lung","Place patient in RIGHT lateral decubitus position (bleeding side DOWN to protect the healthy left lung), secure airway with a large single-lumen ETT (or selective left mainstem intubation), and proceed to urgent Bronchial Artery Embolisation (BAE)","Perform immediate bedside bronchoscopy using a tiny 5.5 mm ETT and send home on oral tranexamic acid"],"a":2,"e":"Patients with massive haemoptysis die from asphyxiation (alveolar flooding of the contralateral healthy lung), NOT exsanguination. Always position the known bleeding lung DEPENDENT (bleeding side DOWN) so gravity keeps blood out of the healthy lung, intubate with a large (\u003e= 8.0-8.5 mm) single-lumen endotracheal tube (enabling therapeutic rigid/flexible bronchoscopy, suctioning clots, or balloon tamponade/selective mainstem intubation of the healthy lung), and perform urgent Bronchial Artery Embolisation (since \u003e90% of massive haemoptysis originates from high-pressure bronchial arteries).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03303","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Renal Tubular Acidosis (RTA)","q":"A 34-year-old woman with Sjogren syndrome and recurrent bilateral calcium phosphate kidney stones presents with muscle weakness. Labs show serum Na+ 138, K+ 2.6 mEq/L, Cl- 114 mEq/L, HCO3- 14 mEq/L (normal anion gap 10), arterial pH 7.26, positive urine anion gap, nephrocalcinosis on KUB X-ray, and a persistently alkaline urine pH of 6.5 despite systemic acidaemia. What is the diagnosis and treatment?","o":["Type 4 (Hyporeninaemic hypoaldosteronism) RTA (which presents with HYPERkalaemia); fludrocortisone","Diabetic ketoacidosis; IV insulin","Proximal (Type 2) RTA; high-dose acetazolamide alone","Distal (Type 1) Renal Tubular Acidosis; oral Potassium Citrate / Sodium Bicarbonate (alkali therapy lowers calciuria and corrects hypokalaemia)"],"a":3,"e":"Normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic) metabolic acidosis + hypokalaemia + inability to acidify urine (urine pH \u003e 5.5 despite acidaemia) + positive urine anion gap + nephrocalcinosis/calcium phosphate stones (due to alkaline urine and low urinary citrate) is diagnostic of Distal (Type 1) RTA—impaired alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion in the cortical collecting duct (classically associated with Sjogren syndrome, SLE, amphotericin B). Oral potassium citrate corrects acidosis, raises urinary citrate, and prevents stone recurrence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03304","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Graves\u0027 Disease and Orbitopathy","q":"A 38-year-old female smoker with Graves\u0027 hyperthyroidism develops active moderate-to-severe Graves\u0027 orbitopathy (proptosis, painful diplopia, lid swelling, and mild optic disc congestion). Which definitive hyperthyroidism treatment can markedly WORSEN active Graves\u0027 orbitopathy unless avoided or covered with steroids, and what is the primary acute therapy for sight-threatening/active moderate-severe orbitopathy?","o":["Radioactive Iodine (I-131) without steroid prophylaxis worsens orbitopathy (prefer antithyroid drugs or surgery + strict smoking cessation); treat active moderate-severe/sight-threatening orbitopathy with pulse IV Methylprednisolone","Topical antibiotic drops cure extraocular muscle fibrosis","Subtotal thyroidectomy triggers immediate permanent blindness; avoid smoking cessation","Methimazole causes severe proptosis; treat immediately with unopposed high-dose I-131 alone"],"a":0,"e":"Radioactive iodine (I-131) releases thyroid antigens and causes post-radiation TSH-receptor antibody surges that significantly trigger or worsen Graves\u0027 ophthalmopathy (~15-20%, amplified in smokers) and is avoided in moderate-to-severe/sight-threatening active eye disease (preferring methimazole/carbimazole or thyroidectomy, or oral prednisone cover only in mild active disease). Active moderate-to-severe/dysthyroid optic neuropathy requires smoking cessation, euthyroidism, and pulse IV Methylprednisolone (or teprotumumab / orbital decompression if optic neuropathy refractory).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03305","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukaemia (CLL)","q":"A 69-year-old man with early-stage Rai Stage I Chronic Lymphocytic Leukaemia (WBC 48,000/mm3, mature lymphocytes with smudge cells, CD5+/CD19+/CD23+) develops sudden fatigue, jaundice, splenomegaly, Hb dropping from 12.8 to 6.5 g/dL, reticulocytes 12%, spherocytes on smear, indirect hyperbilirubinaemia, elevated LDH, and a strongly positive Direct Antiglobulin Test (DAT) for IgG. What is the complication and initial treatment?","o":["Cold agglutinin haemolysis from IgM; warm blood transfusion without steroids","Warm Autoimmune Haemolytic Anaemia (AIHA) secondary to CLL; treat with Prednisolone (1 mg/kg/day) +/- Rituximab (avoiding single-agent fludarabine which worsens AIHA)","Richter transformation to DLBCL; immediate CHOP without steroids","Pure red cell aplasia; oral iron supplements alone"],"a":1,"e":"Approximately 5-10% of CLL patients develop autoimmune cytopenias—most commonly Warm IgG Autoimmune Haemolytic Anaemia (spherocytes, reticulocytosis, high LDH/indirect bilirubin, positive IgG DAT) or ITP. First-line treatment targets immune destruction with oral/IV Prednisolone (adding Rituximab if refractory). Crucially, purine analogues such as single-agent Fludarabine can trigger or severely exacerbate AIHA and must be avoided during active haemolysis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03306","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Neurocysticercosis (NCC)","q":"A 25-year-old man from Uttar Pradesh presents with new-onset focal motor seizures. Brain MRI shows three active viable cysticercal vesicles in the cerebral cortex with visible eccentric scolices (\u0027hole-with-dot\u0027 appearance) and mild surrounding perilesional oedema (ocular cysticercosis excluded on fundoscopy). Alongside antiepileptic therapy (levetiracetam), what is the critical timing rule for Corticosteroids and Albendazole (+ Praziquantel)?","o":["Administer high-dose Albendazole alone for 14 days without steroids","Treat with 12 months of empirical HRZE antitubercular therapy","Start Corticosteroids (dexamethasone/prednisolone) BEFORE or simultaneously with Albendazole (+ Praziquantel if \u003e2 cysts) to prevent acute cyst-lysis cerebral oedema and seizures","Give Albendazole immediately if cysts are purely calcified dead granulomas or intraocular"],"a":2,"e":"Killing viable Taenia solium cysts with Albendazole (and Praziquantel when \u003e2 viable parenchymal cysts exist) triggers intense host inflammation and perilesional cerebral oedema around days 2-5, precipitating status epilepticus or herniation. Always exclude ocular/intraocular cysticercosis via fundoscopy first (cysticidal lysis in the eye causes blindness!) and start Dexamethasone/Prednisolone BEFORE or together with antiparasitics. Purely calcified dead lesions need AEDs only, NOT antiparasitics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03307","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma)","q":"A 44-year-old woman with rapidly progressive diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis (anti-RNA polymerase III positive) recently started on prednisolone 30 mg/day develops abrupt malignant hypertension (BP 218/128 mmHg), headache, oliguria, creatinine jumping from 0.9 to 3.4 mg/dL, schistocytes, and thrombocytopenia. What is the specific life-saving antihypertensive therapy, and what mistake must be avoided if creatinine initially ticks up slightly?","o":["Administer beta-blocker monotherapy alone","Treat as TTP with plasma exchange and withhold antihypertensives","Stop ACE inhibitors permanently because creatinine is 3.4 mg/dL and start high-dose IV methylprednisolone","Immediate short-acting oral ACE inhibitor (Captopril, titrated aggressively to normalise BP over 48-72 h) and taper/stop steroids; DO NOT stop Captopril even if creatinine rises initially or dialysis is needed"],"a":3,"e":"Scleroderma Renal Crisis (precipitated by diffuse SSc, anti-RNA polymerase III antibodies, and moderate/high-dose corticosteroids \u003e15 mg/day) causes intense renin-angiotensin-driven renal vasospasm, malignant hypertension, MAHA, and oliguric AKI. Short-acting ACE inhibitors (Captopril) dramatically transformed survival from \u003c10% to \u003e70% and MUST be initiated and continued even if serum creatinine initially rises or temporary dialysis is required (recovery can occur over months).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03308","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Neuroendocrine Tumours","q":"A 41-year-old man presents with multiple recurrent duodenal and jejunal ulcers, oesophagitis, and chronic secretory diarrhoea resolving on nasogastric suction. After stopping PPIs for 1 week (under H2-blocker cover), fasting serum gastrin is 1,350 pg/mL (\u003e1,000 pg/mL) with gastric pH 1.4 (\u003c2.0). What is the diagnosis, most sensitive localisation modality, and associated genetic syndrome to screen for?","o":["Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (gastrinoma); Somatostatin-receptor PET/CT (Ga-68 DOTATATE) + EUS; screen for MEN 1 (serum calcium/PTH and prolactin/pituitary MRI)","Crohn\u0027s disease; oral mesalamine","Pernicious anaemia (which has high gastrin with gastric pH \u003e 4.0 achlorhydria); vitamin B12 alone","Helicobacter pylori antral gastritis; triple therapy alone"],"a":0,"e":"Refractory/distal peptic ulcers + secretory diarrhoea + fasting gastrin \u003e 1,000 pg/mL in the presence of gastric acid hypersecretion (pH \u003c 2.0) confirm Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (gastrinoma, commonly in Passaro\u0027s gastrinoma triangle). Roughly 20-25% are associated with Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1 (MEN 1: hyperparathyroidism, pancreatic NETs, pituitary adenoma), requiring serum calcium/PTH screening. High-dose PPI controls acid hypersecretion, and Ga-68 DOTATATE PET/CT + EUS localises primary/metastatic lesions for surgical resection in sporadic disease.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03309","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)","q":"A 55-year-old man with Child-Pugh Class B cirrhosis (ascites, bilirubin 2.4 mg/dL, MELD 16, platelets 62,000/mm3, HVPG 14 mmHg) has two LI-RADS 5 Hepatocellular Carcinoma nodules (2.6 cm and 2.1 cm) in the liver without macrovascular invasion or extrahepatic spread. Why is Liver Transplantation preferred over major hepatic resection here, and does he meet Milan criteria?","o":["No, fails Milan criteria because \u003e 1 nodule is present; treat with systemic sorafenib alone","Yes, meets Milan criteria (\u003c= 3 nodules each \u003c= 3 cm without vascular invasion/metastasis); Liver Transplantation is preferred because Child-Pugh B cirrhosis with clinically significant portal hypertension carries prohibitive post-hepatectomy liver failure risk","Proceed immediately to right trisectionectomy regardless of Child-Pugh B portal hypertension","Treat with gemcitabine-cisplatin chemotherapy alone"],"a":1,"e":"Milan criteria for Liver Transplantation in HCC are: 1 solitary tumour \u003c= 5 cm, OR \u003c= 3 tumours each \u003c= 3 cm, with NO macrovascular invasion and NO extrahepatic spread. While Child-Pugh A patients with solitary HCC and preserved bilirubin/no portal hypertension undergo hepatic resection, patients with Child-Pugh B/C decompensation or clinically significant portal hypertension (platelets \u003c100,000/splenomegaly/varices) face fatal post-hepatectomy liver failure and achieve \u003e70% 5-year survival with Liver Transplantation (bridged with TACE/RFA if needed).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03310","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Hereditary Colorectal Cancer Syndromes","q":"A 19-year-old man whose father died of colon cancer at age 38 undergoes screening colonoscopy revealing \u003e500 tubular adenomatous polyps carpeting the entire colon and rectum (germline APC mutation on chromosome 5q21; congenital hypertrophy of retinal pigment epithelium on fundoscopy). What is the near-100% lifetime colorectal cancer risk if untreated, and what prophylactic surgery and upper GI surveillance are required?","o":["Oral aspirin monotherapy","Annual polypectomy via colonoscopy indefinitely without colectomy","Near-100% cancer risk by age 40; prophylactic Total Proctocolectomy with Ileal Pouch-Anal Anastomosis (IPAA) PLUS lifelong upper GI endoscopy for duodenal/periampullary adenomas","Segmental sigmoid colectomy alone; no upper GI surveillance needed"],"a":2,"e":"Classic FAP (autosomal dominant APC tumour suppressor mutation) causes hundreds-to-thousands of colorectal adenomas in adolescence with ~100% progression to colorectal carcinoma by age 40. Prophylactic restorative Total Proctocolectomy with IPAA (around late teens/early 20s) eliminates colorectal cancer risk while preserving continence. Following colectomy, periampullary/duodenal adenocarcinoma and intra-abdominal desmoid tumours become the leading causes of FAP-related mortality, mandating lifelong upper GI surveillance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03311","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Cerebrovascular Insufficiency","q":"A 65-year-old man experiences two episodes of transient right arm weakness and left-eye amaurosis fugax (resolved completely within 30 minutes; no haemorrhage or large infarct on brain MRI). Carotid duplex and CTA demonstrate an 85% ulcerated stenosis of the proximal LEFT internal carotid artery (NASCET criteria), with an unobstructed right carotid. Along with aspirin, high-intensity statin, and BP control, when should Carotid Endarterectomy (CEA) be performed?","o":["Withhold CEA permanently because the TIA resolved","Perform external carotid ligation","Delay CEA for 6 months to allow plaque stabilisation","Urgently within 2 weeks (ideally within 48 hours to 14 days) of the index TIA/minor stroke"],"a":3,"e":"Symptomatic high-grade (70-99% NASCET) internal carotid artery stenosis carries the highest risk of major disabling ipsilateral stroke during the FIRST 14 DAYS after a TIA or minor stroke. Per NASCET/ECST pooled data and current vascular guidelines, Carotid Endarterectomy (preferred over stenting in older patients) yields maximum absolute stroke-reduction benefit when performed promptly within 2 weeks of the index neurological event.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03312","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Bladder Trauma","q":"A 33-year-old man with a pelvic fracture presents with gross haematuria. Retrograde urethrogram is intact, and CT cystography (with \u003e= 300 mL retrograde contrast distension) demonstrates \u0027flame-shaped\u0027 contrast extravasation confined exclusively to the extraperitoneal perivesical space of Retzius, with NO contrast outlining bowel loops or paracolic gutters. How is uncomplicated extraperitoneal bladder rupture managed compared with intraperitoneal bladder dome rupture?","o":["Uncomplicated extraperitoneal rupture is managed non-operatively with continuous Foley catheter drainage for 10-14 days (followed by cystogram); intraperitoneal rupture requires surgical laparotomy and layered repair","Both extraperitoneal and intraperitoneal ruptures require emergency cystectomy","Extraperitoneal rupture requires emergency laparotomy, whereas intraperitoneal rupture heals with Foley catheter alone","Remove all catheters and discharge with oral ciprofloxacin"],"a":0,"e":"Pelvic fractures typically cause extraperitoneal bladder rupture (flame-shaped perivesical contrast), which heals reliably (\u003e85-90%) with continuous urethral Foley catheter drainage alone for 10-14 days (unless complicated by bladder neck injury, bone spicules inside bladder, or concurrent laparotomy/pelvic ORIF). Conversely, blunt impact on a distended bladder bursts the weakest dome into the peritoneal cavity (intraperitoneal rupture outlining bowel loops), requiring surgical exploration and watertight two-layer repair to prevent urinary peritonitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03313","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Puerperal Sepsis and Endomyometritis","q":"Forty-eight hours after an emergency Caesarean section for prolonged obstructed labour with chorioamnionitis, a 25-year-old woman develops fever (39.1 degrees C), tachycardia, lower abdominal pain, marked uterine fundal tenderness, subinvolution, and foul-smelling purulent lochia. What is the standard gold-standard parenteral antibiotic regimen for post-Caesarean polymicrobial endomyometritis?","o":["IV Clindamycin (900 mg q8h) PLUS Gentamicin (covering Gram-positives, Gram-negatives, and Bacteroides/anaerobes), adding Ampicillin if unresponsive or Enterococcus suspected","IV Vancomycin alone","Oral amoxicillin monotherapy","Oral fluconazole alone"],"a":0,"e":"Post-Caesarean endometritis is a polymicrobial ascending infection involving aerobes (E. coli, GBS, Enterococcus) and pelvic anaerobes (Bacteroides, Prevotella, Peptostreptococcus). The classic gold-standard parenteral regimen is IV Clindamycin + Gentamicin (or extended-spectrum beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor or cephalosporin + metronidazole), continued until afebrile and asymptomatic for 24-48 hours; persistent fever despite triple therapy raises suspicion for pelvic abscess, wound infection, or septic pelvic thrombophlebitis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03314","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Twin Delivery Mode","q":"A 29-year-old G2P1 woman with an uncomplicated dichorionic diamniotic (DCDA) twin pregnancy at 37+3 weeks presents in early active labour (cervix 4 cm). Bedside ultrasound confirms Twin A (presenting twin) is in COMPLETE BREECH presentation (EFW 2,550 g) and Twin B is in cephalic presentation (EFW 2,600 g). What is the safest recommended mode of delivery?","o":["Routine vaginal breech delivery of Twin A followed by cephalic Twin B (risks head-to-head chin interlocking)","Planned Caesarean delivery (standard recommendation whenever the presenting Twin A is non-vertex)","Wait until Twin A delivers breech and perform internal podalic version on Twin B","External cephalic version of Twin A during active labour at 4 cm"],"a":1,"e":"In twin gestations, the presentation of Twin A (the leading twin) dictates delivery mode. When Twin A is non-vertex (breech or transverse lie), Caesarean delivery is recommended to avoid cord prolapse, breech head entrapment, and the catastrophic obstetric complication of \u0027locked twins\u0027 (chin-to-chin interlocking when Twin A is breech and Twin B is vertex). Conversely, when Twin A is cephalic in DCDA/MCDA twins, vaginal trial is reasonable regardless of Twin B\u0027s lie.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03315","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Neonatal Polycythaemia","q":"A term infant of a diabetic mother (birth weight 4.1 kg, gestational age 39 weeks) appears ruddy/plethoric at 12 hours of life and develops jitteriness, lethargy, poor feeding, hypoglycaemia (blood glucose 34 mg/dL), and tachypnoea. Peripheral venous haematocrit is 71% (confirmed on repeat free-flowing venous sample). Beyond correcting hypoglycaemia with IV dextrose, what is the specific procedure to reduce blood hyperviscosity?","o":["Phlebotomy removing 40 mL blood without fluid replacement","Double-volume exchange transfusion using packed red blood cells","Partial Exchange Transfusion (PET) replacing calculated blood volume with isotonic 0.9% normal saline to target venous haematocrit ~55%","High-dose IV furosemide alone without volume replacement"],"a":2,"e":"Neonatal polycythaemia is defined as venous haematocrit \u003e= 65% (common in IDM, SGA/chronic fetal hypoxia, delayed cord clamping, twin-to-twin recipient). Hyperviscosity impairs cerebral, renal, and mesenteric microcirculation, causing jitteriness, seizures, hypoglycaemia, necrotising enterocolitis, and pulmonary hypertension. Symptomatic infants with venous Hct \u003e= 65% (or asymptomatic \u003e= 70%) require Partial Exchange Transfusion (isovolaemic replacement of blood with 0.9% normal saline) to reduce Hct safely to ~50-55%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03316","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Paediatric Brain Tumours","q":"A 6-year-old boy presents with 4 weeks of early-morning vomiting, occipital headache, papilloedema, and truncal gait ataxia. Contrast MRI brain reveals an avidly enhancing midline cerebellar vermis mass compressing the fourth ventricle with obstructive hydrocephalus and restricted diffusion on DWI (medulloblastoma). Which additional staging imaging MUST be performed alongside brain MRI, and why is an upfront lumbar puncture contraindicated?","o":["Abdominal ultrasound only; wait 6 months before neurosurgical referral","Skull X-ray alone; immediate high-volume LP today","Immediate whole-brain radiotherapy without tissue diagnosis","Contrast MRI of the ENTIRE SPINE (to detect CSF \u0027drop metastases\u0027); upfront LP is contraindicated due to acute obstructive hydrocephalus and tonsillar herniation risk"],"a":3,"e":"Medulloblastoma (an embryonal neuroectodermal tumour of the cerebellar vermis, commonest malignant paediatric brain tumour) spreads via CSF pathways causing spinal \u0027drop metastases\u0027 in ~20-35% of cases. Complete staging requires gadolinium-enhanced MRI of the entire neuraxis (brain + cervical/thoracic/lumbosacral spine—ideally preoperatively to avoid postoperative blood artefacts), preoperative dexamethasone for oedema, and maximal safe surgical resection (LP for CSF cytology is performed only \u003e= 14 days postoperatively after relieving hydrocephalus).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03317","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Acquired Methaemoglobinaemia","q":"Ten minutes after topical benzocaine spray for transoesophageal echocardiography (in a patient also taking dapsone), a 44-year-old man develops central cyanosis refractory to 100% oxygen, pulse oximeter SpO2 stuck at 85%, chocolate-brown arterial blood, and ABG showing PaO2 340 mmHg with co-oximetry Methaemoglobin level 34% (normal G6PD). What is the specific antidote and its mechanism?","o":["IV Methylene Blue (1-2 mg/kg over 5 min); acts as an artificial electron acceptor reduced by NADPH-methaemoglobin reductase to leukomethylene blue, which reduces Fe3+ back to Fe2+","High-flow 100% oxygen alone (sufficient for 34% methaemoglobin)","IV Hydroxocobalamin 5 g","IV Sodium nitrite"],"a":0,"e":"Oxidising agents (benzocaine, prilocaine, dapsone, nitrites, aniline dyes) convert ferrous haem iron (Fe2+) to ferric iron (Fe3+), creating methaemoglobin unable to bind O2 and shifting the oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve leftward. Classic clues are cyanosis unresponsive to O2, chocolate-brown blood, SpO2 plateauing near ~85% despite high PaO2 (\u0027saturation gap\u0027), and co-oximetry confirmation. Symptomatic/ \u003e20-30% methaemoglobinaemia is treated with IV Methylene Blue (1-2 mg/kg), which requires NADPH (hence contraindicated in G6PD deficiency, where high-dose ascorbic acid/exchange transfusion is used).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03318","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Acute Transfusion Reactions","q":"Two hours after receiving 2 units of Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP) from a multiparous female donor, a 49-year-old ICU patient develops acute severe hypoxaemia (PaO2/FiO2 110 mmHg), bilateral alveolar infiltrates on CXR, fever (38.5 degrees C), transient leucopenia, and hypotension (BP 82/48 mmHg), with normal BNP, normal JVP, and normal LV filling pressures on echo. What is the diagnosis and correct fluid/diuretic approach?","o":["Simple febrile non-haemolytic reaction; restart the FFP slowly","Transfusion-Related Acute Lung Injury (TRALI — non-cardiogenic permeability oedema); stop transfusion immediately, provide O2/lung-protective ventilation and cautious fluids—AVOID aggressive diuretics","Acute haemolytic ABO mismatch; infuse 3 litres of saline rapidly","Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload (TACO); administer high-dose IV furosemide 80 mg immediately"],"a":1,"e":"TRALI (acute ARDS within 6 hours of transfusion, classically triggered by anti-HLA or anti-HNA antibodies in multiparous female donor plasma activating primed pulmonary neutrophils) presents with hypoxaemia, bilateral infiltrates, fever, hypotension, and normal cardiac filling pressures/BNP (non-cardiogenic). Contrast with TACO (cardiogenic volume overload with hypertension, elevated JVP, S3 gallop, and markedly elevated BNP responding to IV furosemide). Aggressive diuretics worsen hypotension in TRALI; management is stopping the unit, supportive oxygenation/ventilation, and notifying the blood bank to defer the donor.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03319","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Mechanical Small Bowel Obstruction","q":"A frail 78-year-old woman with longstanding gallstones and no prior abdominal surgeries presents with bilious vomiting and colicky abdominal pain. CT abdomen shows air in the intrahepatic biliary tree (pneumobilia), dilated fluid-filled small bowel loops with a transition point, and an impacted 3.5 cm laminated calcified gallstone in the distal ileum (Rigler\u0027s triad). What is the most appropriate primary surgical procedure in a high-risk elderly patient?","o":["Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography to extract the ileal stone","Conservative oral polyethylene glycol laxatives","Simple enterolithotomy (longitudinal enterotomy proximal to the impaction, stone extraction, and transverse closure—examining rest of bowel for a second stone), deferring cholecystectomy/cholecystoduodenal fistula repair","Immediate one-stage radical cholecystectomy, common bile duct exploration, fistula closure, and ileal resection in a frail septic 78-year-old"],"a":2,"e":"Gallstone ileus arises when a large (\u003e2.5 cm) gallstone erodes via a cholecystoduodenal fistula and impacts at the narrowest small-bowel site (distal ileum / ileocaecal valve), producing Rigler\u0027s triad (pneumobilia, mechanical SBO, ectopic gallstone). In elderly/high-risk patients, a simple enterolithotomy alone (milking the stone proximally into healthy bowel and removing it, checking for synchronous stones) resolves obstruction with significantly lower mortality than a prolonged one-stage fistula repair.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03320","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"ACR BI-RADS Categorisation","q":"On screening mammography and targeted breast ultrasound in a 47-year-old woman, a 1.4 cm solid hypoechoic mass has indistinct margins and clustered pleomorphic microcalcifications, and is assigned ACR BI-RADS Category 4B (Moderate suspicion of malignancy, 10-50% likelihood). What is the mandatory next management step?","o":["Immediate bilateral radical mastectomy without histological confirmation","Return to routine annual screening mammography (BI-RADS 1/2 protocol)","Short-interval repeat imaging follow-up in 6 months without biopsy (BI-RADS 3 protocol)","Image-guided tissue biopsy (core needle biopsy / vacuum-assisted biopsy) for histological diagnosis"],"a":3,"e":"ACR BI-RADS categories standardise breast imaging recommendations: BI-RADS 0 (incomplete—needs additional views/USG); BI-RADS 1/2 (negative/benign—routine screening); BI-RADS 3 (probably benign \u003c= 2% malignancy risk—short-interval 6-month follow-up); BI-RADS 4 (4A/4B/4C suspicious, \u003e2% to \u003c95% risk—tissue diagnosis via core biopsy MANDATORY); BI-RADS 5 (\u003e= 95% highly suggestive of malignancy—biopsy + definitive surgical planning).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03321","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Ankle and Fibular Injuries","q":"A 30-year-old footballer twists his ankle in pronation-external rotation and presents with medial ankle swelling, tenderness over the deltoid ligament/medial malleolus, and widened medial clear space and tibiofibular syndesmosis on ankle X-ray, WITHOUT a distal fibular fracture. Palpation reveals acute tenderness just below the right knee over the proximal fibular neck. Full-length tibia-fibula radiographs confirm a spiral fracture of the proximal third of the fibula. What is this injury pattern and surgical requirement?","o":["Maisonneuve fracture; unstable syndesmotic/interosseous membrane disruption requiring anatomical reduction and syndesmotic screw/suture-button fixation (+ medial repair if needed)","Isolated stable Weber A ankle sprain; walking boot immediately without syndesmotic assessment","Isolated Jones fracture of the 5th metatarsal","Pilon fracture; external fixator across the knee"],"a":0,"e":"A Maisonneuve fracture results from pronation-external rotation force tearing the deltoid ligament (or fracturing the medial malleolus), disrupting the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis and interosseous membrane upward, and exiting as a proximal fibular fracture. Because standard ankle radiographs miss the proximal fibula, examining the entire fibula in any isolated medial ankle injury or widened syndesmosis is mandatory; unstable syndesmotic widening requires reduction and syndesmotic stabilisation (the proximal fibula fracture itself is usually left unplated).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03322","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Toxin-Mediated Shock","q":"A 23-year-old woman who underwent septoplasty with retained nasal packing 4 days ago presents with acute high fever (39.8 degrees C), BP 74/44 mmHg, diffuse \u0027sunburn-like\u0027 macular erythroderma, strawberry tongue, conjunctival hyperaemia, vomiting, diarrhoea, transaminitis, thrombocytopenia, and AKI. What is the diagnosis and why is Clindamycin added to Vancomycin/beta-lactam after removing the nasal packing?","o":["Meningococcaemia; clindamycin alone without removing packing","Staphylococcal Toxic Shock Syndrome (TSST-1 superantigen); Clindamycin inhibits 50S ribosomal protein synthesis to rapidly halt further exotoxin production","Kawasaki disease; aspirin alone","Scarlet fever; oral penicillin alone leaving nasal packing in situ"],"a":1,"e":"High fever, refractory hypotension, diffuse sunburn-like erythroderma (desquamating palms/soles 1-2 weeks later), mucosal hyperaemia, and \u003e= 3 organ-system dysfunction following nasal packing, tampon use, or surgical wounds diagnose Staphylococcal Toxic Shock Syndrome (mediated by TSST-1 superantigen activating up to 20% of T-cells). Immediate source control (removing/debriding packing), aggressive resuscitation, and Vancomycin/anti-staphylococcal beta-lactam PLUS Clindamycin (suppresses ribosomal TSST-1 synthesis regardless of bacterial growth phase) are life-saving.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03323","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Ocular Motor Nerve Palsies","q":"A 49-year-old woman presents with sudden severe retro-orbital headache, complete right upper-eyelid ptosis, the right eye deviated \u0027down and out\u0027, and a 7 mm dilated, non-reactive right pupil (pupil-involving oculomotor nerve palsy). Why is emergency CT angiography (CTA) or MR angiography (MRA) mandatory TODAY?","o":["To plan elective prism glasses after 3 months of observation","To diagnose benign myasthenia gravis (which causes fixed mydriasis)","To rule out a compressing Posterior Communicating (PCom) artery aneurysm threatening fatal subarachnoid haemorrhage (parasympathetic pupillomotor fibres run superficially on the outside of CN III)","Because diabetic microvascular CN III ischaemia always dilates the pupil first"],"a":2,"e":"In oculomotor (CN III) palsy, superficial dorsal-medial parasympathetic pupillomotor fibres receive rich pial blood supply and are spared in diabetic/hypertensive microvascular vasa nervorum infarction (\u0027pupil-sparing CN III palsy\u0027). Conversely, external compression—classically a Posterior Communicating (PCom) artery aneurysm at the junction with the ICA—compresses outer pupillary fibres early, producing painful \u0027pupil-involving\u0027 CN III palsy. Because aneurysmal rupture causes lethal SAH, emergency CTA/MRA/DSA is mandatory.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03324","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Sinonasal Tumours","q":"A 54-year-old man presents with progressive unilateral left nasal obstruction and intermittent epistaxis. Nasal endoscopy shows a firm, lobulated greyish-pink polypoidal mass arising from the lateral nasal wall (middle meatus), and CT shows focal hyperostosis at its maxillary-ethmoid attachment site. Biopsy shows endophytic invagination of respiratory epithelium into underlying stroma (Inverted Schneiderian Papilloma). What two clinical characteristics mandate complete subperiosteal resection (e.g., endoscopic medial maxillectomy) with long-term follow-up?","o":["Exclusively bilateral allergic eosinophilic origin responsive to antihistamines alone","Spontaneous regression after puberty and zero malignancy potential","Complete cure via simple office snare polypectomy","High local recurrence rate with bony erosion AND ~5-15% risk of synchronous or metachronous transformation into Squamous Cell Carcinoma"],"a":3,"e":"Inverted (Schneiderian) papilloma arises from the ectodermal respiratory mucosa of the lateral nasal wall (unlike simple inflammatory polyps) in middle-aged males. Although histologically benign, it exhibits locally aggressive bone remodelling, a high recurrence rate if inadequately excised, and a ~5-15% association with squamous cell carcinoma. Complete resection of the tumour and drilling/removing its hyperostotic bony attachment site (commonly via endoscopic medial maxillectomy) with prolonged endoscopic surveillance is required.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03325","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Research Methodology and Biostatistics","topic":"Multiple Comparisons and Inter-Rater Agreement","q":"A clinical investigator evaluates 5 independent secondary endpoints simultaneously while maintaining an overall family-wise Type I error rate of alpha = 0.05 using the Bonferroni correction, and also measures inter-observer agreement between two radiologists classifying chest X-rays into \u0027Normal / Pneumonia / TB\u0027. What is the Bonferroni-adjusted p-value threshold per test, and which statistic measures chance-corrected categorical agreement?","o":["Adjusted alpha = 0.01 (0.05 / 5); Cohen\u0027s kappa statistic","Adjusted alpha = 0.05; Intraclass Correlation Coefficient (ICC) for categorical classes","Adjusted alpha = 0.001; Bland-Altman plot","Adjusted alpha = 0.25 (0.05 x 5); Pearson correlation coefficient"],"a":0,"e":"Performing k simultaneous hypothesis tests inflates the family-wise Type I error rate; the Bonferroni correction divides the overall alpha by the number of comparisons: alpha_adjusted = 0.05 / 5 = 0.01 (requiring p \u003c 0.01 for individual significance). For inter-rater agreement on nominal/categorical classifications beyond chance, Cohen\u0027s kappa (or weighted kappa for ordinal categories) is used (whereas ICC and Bland-Altman apply to continuous measurements).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03326","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Valvular Heart Disease","q":"A 72-year-old man with ischaemic cardiomyopathy (LVEF 28%) presents with exertional dyspnoea. Transthoracic echocardiography shows a calcified aortic valve with calculated aortic valve area (AVA) 0.72 cm2, stroke volume index 28 mL/m2 (\u003c 35 mL/m2), and a mean transvalvular gradient of only 24 mmHg (\u003c 40 mmHg) — classical low-flow, low-gradient aortic stenosis. Which diagnostic test distinguishes TRUE fixed severe AS from pseudo-severe AS (and assesses LV contractile reserve) to decide on TAVR/SAVR?","o":["Exercise treadmill ECG stress testing at maximal Bruce protocol","Low-dose Dobutamine Stress Echocardiography (increasing stroke volume \u003e= 20%: true severe AS shows mean gradient rising \u003e= 40 mmHg while AVA stays \u003c= 1.0 cm2)","Immediate coronary thrombolysis","24-hour Holter monitor alone"],"a":1,"e":"When LV systolic dysfunction reduces stroke volume, even a severely stenotic aortic valve may generate a low mean gradient (\u003c40 mmHg) — or conversely, a moderately stenotic valve may simply fail to push open fully (\u0027pseudo-severe AS\u0027). Low-dose Dobutamine Stress Echocardiography augments contractility: if stroke volume rises \u003e= 20% (contractile reserve present) and AVA remains \u003c= 1.0 cm2 with mean gradient \u003e= 40 mmHg, TRUE severe AS is confirmed and AVR/TAVR improves survival (if AVA opens \u003e1.0 cm2 with low gradient, it is pseudo-severe AS treated medically; CT calcium score helps if no contractile reserve).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03327","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Sarcoidosis","q":"A 30-year-old woman presents with acute fever, painful erythematous nodules over both shins (erythema nodosum), symmetrical bilateral ankle peri-arthritis, and bilateral symmetrical hilar lymphadenopathy on chest radiograph/CT (Lofgren syndrome). EBUS-TBNA demonstrates non-caseating epithelioid cell granulomas, with negative AFB smear, negative CBNAAT/GeneXpert, and negative Mantoux (anergy). What is the prognosis and initial management once tuberculosis is thoroughly excluded?","o":["Immediate bilateral hilar lymphadenectomy","Immediately start 6 months of empirical HRZE antitubercular therapy despite negative CBNAAT and classic bilateral symmetrical hilar Lofgren triad","Acute Lofgren syndrome (good-prognosis sarcoidosis with high spontaneous remission rate); manage symptomatically with NSAIDs (short-course oral prednisolone only if severe symptoms or organ involvement)","Lifelong cyclophosphamide + azathioprine"],"a":2,"e":"Lofgren syndrome—acute presentation of bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, erythema nodosum, and migratory/ankle polyarthralgia with fever—is an acute form of Sarcoidosis carrying \u003e80% rate of spontaneous remission within 1-2 years. In TB-endemic India, excluding tuberculosis (CBNAAT/culture on EBUS-TBNA/BAL) is mandatory because granulomas overlap; once TB is excluded, asymptomatic or mild Lofgren syndrome requires only NSAIDs (reserving corticosteroids for hypercalcaemia, ocular/cardiac/neuro-sarcoidosis, or progressive lung impairment).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03328","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Vascular Renal Syndromes","q":"A 70-year-old man with severe diffuse aortic atherosclerosis undergoes coronary angiography (with uneventful immediate recovery at 48 hours), then returns 3 weeks later with progressive subacute renal failure (creatinine rising from 1.2 to 4.1 mg/dL), livedo reticularis over both thighs, painful violaceous cyanotic toes (\u0027blue toe syndrome\u0027) with palpable pedal pulses, Hollenhorst plaques on fundoscopy, peripheral eosinophilia, and hypocomplementaemia (low C3/C4). What is the diagnosis and key management rule?","o":["Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia; start high-dose warfarin","Contrast-associated acute kidney injury (which peaks at 3-5 days and resolves within 1-2 weeks); repeat angiography","Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis; oral penicillin","Cholesterol Atheroembolic Renal Disease; supportive BP/renal care, high-intensity statin, and AVOID further arterial catheterisation and unnecessary anticoagulation (which worsens plaque embolisation)"],"a":3,"e":"Catheter manipulation of ulcerated atherosclerotic aorta showers cholesterol crystals distally 1-4 weeks later (unlike contrast AKI which peaks at 3-5 days), occluding renal arcuate/interlobular arteries (needle-shaped biconvex clefts on biopsy), skin (livedo reticularis, blue toes despite intact pulses), retina (Hollenhorst plaques), and activating complement (low C3/C4, eosinophilia). Anticoagulation prevents overlying thrombus formation that otherwise stabilises ruptured plaques and can trigger catastrophic recurrent cholesterol showering; management is supportive + statins + avoiding vascular manipulation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03329","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Myeloproliferative Neoplasms","q":"A 64-year-old man presents with plethora, burning pain/redness in his hands and feet relieved by cooling (erythromelalgia), severe itching after warm showers (aquagenic pruritus), splenomegaly, haemoglobin 20.4 g/dL, haematocrit 61%, elevated WBC and platelets, markedly suppressed serum Erythropoietin (EPO), and a positive JAK2 V617F mutation. What haematocrit target via phlebotomy and antithrombotic/cytoreductive regimen reduces major cardiovascular and thrombotic events?","o":["Phlebotomy to maintain Haematocrit \u003c 45%, low-dose Aspirin (81-100 mg/day), PLUS cytoreductive therapy (Hydroxyurea or pegylated interferon) because age \u003e 60 years places him in the high-risk category","High-dose exogenous erythropoietin injections","Phlebotomy only when haematocrit exceeds 65%; avoid aspirin","Urgent splenectomy as primary treatment"],"a":0,"e":"JAK2 V617F (~95% of Polycythaemia Vera) drives EPO-independent trilineage proliferation (suppressed serum EPO distinguishes PV from secondary hypoxic/tumour erythrocytosis). Based on the CYTO-PV trial, maintaining Haematocrit strictly \u003c 45% via phlebotomy + low-dose aspirin (which also cures microvascular erythromelalgia) cuts cardiovascular death and major thrombosis fourfold. High-risk patients (age \u003e= 60 years or history of thrombosis) additionally require cytoreduction with Hydroxyurea (or pegylated interferon / ruxolitinib).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03330","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Post-SAH Hyponatraemia","q":"Five days after clipping of a ruptured anterior communicating artery aneurysm (SAH), a 50-year-old woman develops hyponatraemia (Na+ 121 mEq/L, Uosm 520 mOsm/kg, UNa+ 110 mEq/L) accompanied by polyuria (3.5 L/day), negative fluid balance (-2.2 L over 24 h), falling central venous pressure, rising haematocrit and BUN, and orthostatic hypotension. How does this condition (Cerebral Salt Wasting) differ in management from SIADH?","o":["Give 2 litres of oral plain free water","Cerebral Salt Wasting is HYPOVOLAEMIC natriuresis; treat with Isotonic/Hypertonic Saline volume repletion (+/- fludrocortisone) and STRICTLY AVOID fluid restriction (which precipitates cerebral vasospasm and stroke)","Fluid restriction (\u003c 800 mL/day) is first-line identically to SIADH","Administer IV furosemide 80 mg BID to lower urine sodium"],"a":1,"e":"Both SIADH and Cerebral Salt Wasting (CSW) cause hyponatraemia with elevated urine osmolality and high urine Na+, making clinical volume status the crucial discriminator. CSW (driven by brain natriuretic peptides after SAH/TBI) causes renal salt/water wasting and true hypovolaemia (negative fluid balance, high urine output, orthostasis, rising Hct/BUN). Restricting fluids—as done in euvolaemic SIADH—in a post-SAH CSW patient triggers severe cerebral vasospasm and delayed cerebral ischaemia; CSW requires saline volume expansion (+/- 3% NaCl/fludrocortisone).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03331","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Invasive Fungal Infections","q":"A 60-year-old diabetic ICU patient receiving broad-spectrum antibiotics and TPN via a central venous catheter develops persistent fever and hypotension; two blood cultures grow budding yeasts (Candida spp., species identification pending). What is the recommended empiric antifungal class and two mandatory bedside/specialist actions?","o":["Start oral fluconazole 100 mg daily, keep the same central line in place, and avoid eye examination","Treat as harmless skin contaminant without antifungals","Start an IV Echinocandin (caspofungin, micafungin, or anidulafungin) as first-line, REMOVE/replace the central venous catheter promptly, AND perform dilated fundoscopy to rule out Candida endophthalmitis","Start IV amphotericin B bladder irrigation alone"],"a":2,"e":"Per IDSA/ICMR invasive candidiasis guidelines, an Echinocandin (caspofungin/micafungin/anidulafungin) is recommended FIRST-LINE for candidaemia—especially in critically ill or azole-exposed patients—owing to fungicidal activity against both C. albicans and azole-resistant non-albicans species (C. glabrata, C. auris, C. krusei). Prompt source control (central venous catheter removal) and dilated retinal exam (within the first week/neutrophil recovery to exclude chorioretinitis/endophthalmitis requiring systemic azole/intravitreal therapy) are mandatory, continuing therapy \u003e= 14 days after first negative blood culture.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03332","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"CNS Demyelinating Disorders","q":"A 33-year-old woman presents with severe sequential bilateral optic neuritis (vision 6/60) and acute paraparesis with sensory level. MRI spine reveals a longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis (LETM) lesion spanning 6 contiguous thoracic vertebral segments (T2-T8), brain MRI shows area postrema dorsal brainstem involvement (with intractable hiccups/vomiting) without Dawson\u0027s fingers, and serum anti-Aquaporin-4 (AQP4-IgG) is positive. Beyond acute pulse IV methylprednisolone/PLEX, which MS therapies MUST be avoided because they trigger severe NMOSD relapses?","o":["Rituximab and Azathioprine worsen NMOSD; use Interferon-beta-1a","Treat solely with oral acyclovir","Wait until 5 more relapses before starting any maintenance","Interferon-beta, Fingolimod, and Natalizumab worsen NMOSD; use B-cell/complement/IL-6 targeted maintenance (e.g., Rituximab, Azathioprine, MMF, Eculizumab, or Inebilizumab)"],"a":3,"e":"NMOSD (Devic\u0027s disease, anti-AQP4 astrocytopathy) causes severe optic neuritis, area postrema syndrome (intractable vomiting/hiccups), and longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis (\u003e= 3 vertebral segments—unlike short asymmetric MS plaques). Distinguishing NMOSD from MS is critical because classic MS disease-modifying therapies (interferon-beta, natalizumab, fingolimod, alemtuzumab) provoke devastating NMOSD relapses; relapse prevention uses Rituximab, azathioprine, MMF, or FDA-approved eculizumab/inebilizumab/satralizumab.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03333","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Barrett\u0027s Oesophagus","q":"A 59-year-old man with longstanding GERD undergoes surveillance upper GI endoscopy showing a 4 cm salmon-coloured segment in the distal oesophagus with intestinal metaplasia (goblet cells) and confirmed High-Grade Dysplasia (HGD) on Prague C2M4 biopsies (no visible invasive nodule or submucosa invasion on EUS). What is the preferred organ-sparing definitive management?","o":["Endoscopic eradication therapy: Endoscopic Mucosal Resection (EMR) of any subtle mucosal irregularity followed by Radiofrequency Ablation (RFA) of the remaining Barrett\u0027s segment + lifelong PPI","Immediate open oesophagectomy as routine first-line for flat HGD without attempting endoscopic ablation","Laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication alone leaving HGD untreated","Repeat surveillance endoscopy in 5 years without intervention"],"a":0,"e":"Barrett\u0027s oesophagus (replacement of distal stratified squamous epithelium with columnar intestinal metaplasia containing goblet cells) with confirmed High-Grade Dysplasia carries ~6-10% annual progression to oesophageal adenocarcinoma. Modern guidelines recommend organ-preserving Endoscopic Eradication Therapy—EMR/ESD of any raised lesions (for accurate T-staging) followed by Radiofrequency Ablation (RFA) of all remaining Barrett\u0027s mucosa—achieving \u003e85-90% complete eradication with vastly lower morbidity than oesophagectomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03334","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Blunt Hepatic Trauma","q":"A 28-year-old man involved in a motorcycle crash has BP 116/74 mmHg, HR 90/min, and soft non-peritonitic abdomen after 1 litre of crystalloid. Contrast-enhanced CT abdomen reveals a Grade IV laceration of the right hepatic lobe with moderate haemoperitoneum and a focal arterial-phase \u0027contrast blush\u0027 (active arterial extravasation) within the liver parenchyma, with no hollow viscus injury. What is the preferred management?","o":["Administer IV tranexamic acid and withhold angiography","Non-operative ICU monitoring PLUS urgent selective hepatic artery angiography and transcatheter angioembolisation of the bleeding arterial branch","Immediate emergency laparotomy and right hepatic lobectomy despite haemodynamic stability","Discharge home with oral analgesics"],"a":1,"e":"Non-operative management (NOM) is standard of care for \u003e80-90% of haemodynamically stable blunt hepatic trauma regardless of CT injury grade (I-V), provided no peritonitis/hollow viscus injury exists. However, an active arterial \u0027contrast blush\u0027 on CECT predicts NOM failure and rebleeding; selective hepatic artery angioembolisation controls arterial extravasation with high salvage rates while avoiding major hepatic surgery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03335","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Anal Canal Carcinoma","q":"A 53-year-old woman (HIV-positive) presents with a 3.5 cm ulcerated HPV-16-associated Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the anal canal invading the internal anal sphincter with one enlarged 1.8 cm inguinal lymph node (cT2N1M0, no distant metastases). What is the standard curative primary treatment?","o":["Local haemorrhoidectomy alone","Single-agent oral tamoxifen","Definitive concurrent Chemoradiotherapy (Nigro protocol: 5-FU + Mitomycin C + external-beam radiotherapy to pelvis/anus/inguinal nodes), reserving Abdominoperineal Resection (APR) strictly for persistent or recurrent disease","Upfront Abdominoperineal Resection (APR) with permanent colostomy as primary therapy"],"a":2,"e":"Unlike rectal adenocarcinoma, localised/locally advanced anal canal Squamous Cell Carcinoma is highly radiosensitive and chemosensitive. Definitive concurrent chemoradiation using the Nigro regimen (5-fluorouracil + Mitomycin C + pelvic/inguinal radiation) achieves ~70-85% complete remission and sphincter preservation; radical APR is reserved strictly for biopsy-proven residual/recurrent disease after \u003e= 26 weeks post-CRT assessment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03336","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Thyroid Neoplasms","q":"A 41-year-old woman has a solitary 2.8 cm right thyroid nodule (euthyroid). Ultrasound-guided FNAC is reported as Bethesda Category IV (\u0027Follicular Neoplasm / Suspicious for a Follicular Neoplasm\u0027, ~25-30% malignancy risk). Why can FNAC NOT distinguish a benign follicular adenoma from a follicular carcinoma, and what is the standard surgical diagnostic step?","o":["Repeat FNAC every month until capsular invasion appears on cytology","Because follicular carcinoma always shows psammoma bodies and Orphan Annie nuclei on FNAC; perform immediate radioactive iodine ablation without surgery","Start lifelong levothyroxine suppression without surgery or molecular testing","FNAC evaluates cellular morphology only, whereas Follicular Carcinoma is defined histologically by CAPSULAR and/or VASCULAR INVASION; perform diagnostic Right Thyroid Lobectomy and Isthmusectomy (after discussing molecular testing if available)"],"a":3,"e":"Unlike papillary thyroid carcinoma (diagnosed cytologically on FNAC by nuclear grooves, intranuclear pseudoinclusions, and Orphan Annie-eye nuclei), benign follicular adenoma and malignant follicular carcinoma share identical cytological features. Histological examination of the intact surgical specimen is required to detect full-thickness capsular or vascular invasion; therefore, diagnostic thyroid lobectomy (+ isthmusectomy; or molecular gene-expression classifier first) is standard for Bethesda IV nodules (completing total thyroidectomy only if widely invasive carcinoma is found).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03337","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Supra-aortic Trunk Disease","q":"A 61-year-old male smoker complains of recurrent episodes of vertigo, diplopia, and ataxia precipitated specifically when washing windows or lifting weights with his LEFT arm, accompanied by cramping pain in the left forearm. Brachial blood pressure is 142/84 mmHg on the right vs 96/60 mmHg on the left (differential \u003e 40 mmHg), with a left supraclavicular bruit. Duplex ultrasound shows flow reversal in the left vertebral artery during arm exercise, and CTA confirms severe stenosis of the proximal left subclavian artery proximal to the vertebral origin. What is the diagnosis and definitive treatment for symptomatic patients?","o":["Subclavian Steal Syndrome; percutaneous endovascular angioplasty/stenting of the proximal left subclavian artery (or carotid-subclavian bypass)","Right carotid artery stenosis; right carotid endarterectomy","Thoracic outlet venous thrombosis; oral warfarin alone","Meniere\u0027s disease; low-salt diet and betahistine"],"a":0,"e":"Atherosclerotic stenosis/occlusion of the proximal subclavian artery BEFORE the origin of the vertebral artery lowers distal subclavian pressure; exercising the ipsilateral arm lowers peripheral resistance and \u0027steals\u0027 blood down the ipsilateral vertebral artery from the basilar circulation—causing arm claudication + posterior circulation vertebrobasilar TIA symptoms + \u003e15-20 mmHg inter-arm SBP difference. Symptomatic patients are cured by endovascular subclavian stenting or surgical carotid-subclavian bypass.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03338","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Septic Pelvic Thrombophlebitis (SPT)","q":"Six days after an emergency Caesarean delivery complicated by endomyometritis, a 28-year-old woman continues to have high spiking \u0027picket-fence\u0027 fevers (39.3 degrees C) despite 5 days of appropriate broad-spectrum IV triple antibiotics (ampicillin + gentamicin + clindamycin). She looks relatively well between fever spikes, wound is clean, pelvic exam reveals minimal tenderness without pelvic abscess on CECT, and CECT shows a filling defect in the right ovarian vein. What is the diagnosis and additional therapeutic step?","o":["Retained placenta; immediate hysterectomy","Septic Pelvic Thrombophlebitis (right ovarian vein thrombosis); add therapeutic anticoagulation (LMWH or unfractionated heparin) alongside continuing IV antibiotics until afebrile","Drug fever alone; stop all antibiotics and discharge","Uncomplicated mastitis; oral paracetamol alone"],"a":1,"e":"Septic Pelvic Thrombophlebitis (affecting the right ovarian vein in ~80-90% of cases due to dextrorotation of the gravid uterus compressing the right ovarian vein at the pelvic brim) is a classic cause of persistent spiking puerperal fever unresponsive to 48-72 hours of adequate broad-spectrum antibiotics after ruling out abscess/wound infection. Adding therapeutic anticoagulation (LMWH/UFH) typically produces rapid defervescence within 48-72 hours and prevents septic pulmonary emboli.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03339","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Rh Alloimmunisation Management","q":"A 28-year-old Rh-negative G3P1 woman with a previously hydrops-affected pregnancy has an Indirect Coombs Test (anti-D antibody) titre of 1:128 at 26 weeks of gestation (paternal genotype homozygous RHD+/RHD+). Serial non-invasive fetal Middle Cerebral Artery Peak Systolic Velocity (MCA-PSV) Doppler measures 1.72 Multiples of the Median (MoM) (\u003e 1.50 MoM). What does MCA-PSV \u003e 1.50 MoM indicate, and what is the definitive next step?","o":["Normal fetal haemoglobin; repeat scan at 38 weeks","Give maternal IM Anti-D immunoglobulin 300 mcg now to reverse sensitisation","Moderate-to-severe fetal anaemia (hyperdynamic low-viscosity fetal circulation); proceed to ultrasound-guided cordocentesis and Intrauterine Intravascular Fetal Blood Transfusion (O-negative, irradiated, CMV-negative, leukodepleted PRBC)","Severe fetal polycythaemia; maternal aspirin"],"a":2,"e":"Once maternal anti-D titres reach critical levels (\u003e= 1:16-1:32) or a prior affected pregnancy occurred, serial non-invasive MCA-PSV Doppler replaces hazardous serial amniocentesis (Liley Delta OD450). Anaemic fetuses increase cardiac output and decrease blood viscosity, accelerating MCA systolic velocity: MCA-PSV \u003e 1.50 MoM predicts moderate-to-severe fetal anaemia with ~100% sensitivity, prompting cordocentesis (umbilical vein sampling) + Intrauterine Transfusion (before 34-35 weeks) to prevent hydrops fetalis and IUFD.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03340","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Infant of a Diabetic Mother (IDM)","q":"A newborn delivered to a mother with poorly controlled pregestational type 1 diabetes mellitus (periconceptional HbA1c 10.4%) has congenital absence of the sacrum and lower lumbar spine with hypoplastic lower extremities and neurogenic bladder. Which malformation is MOST specific/pathognomonic for maternal pregestational diabetes, alongside which common transient cardiac abnormality seen in IDMs?","o":["DiGeorge 22q11 deletion; pulmonary lymphangiectasia","Pierre Robin sequence; persistent truncus arteriosus","Turner syndrome; endocardial cushion defect","Caudal Regression Syndrome (sacral agenesis); transient hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (asymmetric septal hypertrophy) that resolves spontaneously over months"],"a":3,"e":"While congenital heart defects (VSD, TGA) and neural tube defects are numerically commoner in pregestational diabetes, Caudal Regression Syndrome (sacral agenesis) carries the highest relative risk (\u003e200-fold increase, pathognomonic for poorly controlled periconceptional hyperglycaemia). Fetal hyperinsulinaemia also induces transient asymmetric septal hypertrophy/hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (managed supportively with beta-blockers if LVOT obstruction exists—avoiding digoxin/inotropes!), alongside macrosomia, hypoglycaemia, hypocalcaemia, polycythaemia, and small left colon syndrome.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03341","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Connection (TAPVC)","q":"A 2-day-old cyanotic term newborn presents with severe tachypnoea, refractory hypoxaemia (SpO2 62% unresponsive to 100% O2), hepatomegaly, normal heart size, and diffuse bilateral \u0027ground-glass\u0027 pulmonary venous congestion (\u0027white-out\u0027 lungs) on chest X-ray. Echocardiography reveals all four pulmonary veins draining via a vertical descending vein below the diaphragm into the portal vein with severe obstruction (obstructed infracardiac TAPVC) and right-to-left ASD shunt. Why is Prostaglandin E1 often harmful/ineffective alone here, and what is the ONLY life-saving intervention?","o":["PGE1 increases pulmonary blood flow into an obstructed pulmonary venous outlet worsening pulmonary oedema; immediate emergency surgical repair (anastomosing pulmonary venous confluence to left atrium) is mandatory","Perform immediate balloon atrial septostomy only without repairing pulmonary veins","PGE1 cures obstructed TAPVC permanently; delay surgery for 1 year","High-dose IV furosemide alone without surgery resolves portal venous obstruction"],"a":0,"e":"Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Connection (TAPVC) has supracardiac (commonest, classic \u0027snowman\u0027 / figure-of-8 cardiomegaly in older infants via left vertical vein-\u003einnominate-\u003eSVC), cardiac (coronary sinus), and infracardiac (below diaphragm into portal vein/IVC, almost always OBSTRUCTED by hepatic sinusoids/ductus venosus) types. Obstructed infracardiac TAPVC is the single true surgical emergency in neonatal cardiology because PGE1/pulmonary vasodilators flood an obstructed venous bed worsening fulminant pulmonary oedema; emergency surgical redirection is life-saving.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03342","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Negative Pressure Pulmonary Oedema (NPPE)","q":"A muscular 24-year-old man undergoing arthroscopy under general anaesthesia bites down hard on the endotracheal tube and develops severe laryngospasm during emergence, generating forceful inspiratory efforts against a completely obstructed glottis. Moments after relieving the spasm, he develops acute dyspnoea, SpO2 82%, bilateral crackles, pink frothy sputum, and bilateral perihilar alveolar infiltrates on CXR (normal echo). What is the mechanism and primary management of Negative Pressure Pulmonary Oedema (NPPE)?","o":["Severe volume overload; immediate 160 mg IV furosemide + phlebotomy","Markedly negative intrathoracic pressure (-50 to -100 cmH2O) increases venous return and transcapillary hydrostatic gradient flooding alveoli; maintain airway patency, apply 100% O2 + CPAP/PEEP (or brief reintubation with PEEP), and avoid aggressive diuresis unless hypervolaemic","Acute left ventricular infarction; immediate streptokinase thrombolysis","Anaphylactic bronchospasm; high-dose IV epinephrine 5 mg bolus"],"a":1,"e":"Type I NPPE occurs classically in healthy young muscular adults making vigorous inspiratory efforts (Muller manoeuvre) against an obstructed airway (laryngospasm, biting ETT), generating extreme negative intrathoracic pressures that pull systemic venous return into the thorax while raising LV afterload and pulmonary microvascular filtration pressure. Treatment is supportive airway relief, supplemental oxygen, and positive airway pressure (CPAP or PEEP via ETT), resolving rapidly within 12-24 hours; routine aggressive diuretics are unnecessary and risk hypovolaemia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03343","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Succinylcholine Hyperkalaemic Cardiac Arrest","q":"A 30-year-old man who sustained a complete thoracic spinal cord injury (paraplegia) 14 days ago and a 10-year-old boy with Duchenne muscular dystrophy both require urgent general anaesthesia with rapid-sequence induction. Why is Succinylcholine strictly CONTRAINDICATED in both patients, and which non-depolarising neuromuscular blocker should replace it for rapid-sequence intubation?","o":["Succinylcholine causes severe hypokalaemia; use pancuronium","Use high-dose IV neostigmine for induction relaxation","Upregulation/proliferation of extrajunctional acetylcholine receptors (or unstable sarcolemma in dystrophies) causes massive catastrophic K+ efflux and hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest with succinylcholine; use high-dose Rocuronium (1.2 mg/kg, reversible with sugammadex)","Succinylcholine is safe 14 days post-spinal injury and only contraindicated in the first 2 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Starting ~24-48 hours after major thermal burns, crush injury, spinal cord transection, stroke hemiplegia, or prolonged ICU immobility/GBS, immature extrajunctional nicotinic AChRs spread across the entire muscle membrane. Depolarisation by Succinylcholine opens millions of channels simultaneously, releasing a massive surge of intracellular K+ causing refractory hyperkalaemic arrest (and acute rhabdomyolysis/hyperkalaemia in Duchenne/Becker muscular dystrophy at ANY time). High-dose Rocuronium (1.0-1.2 mg/kg) provides 60-second intubating conditions safely.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03344","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Benign vs Malignant Solid Renal Masses","q":"A 32-year-old woman with Tuberous Sclerosis Complex (facial angiofibromas, ash-leaf macules, subependymal giant cell astrocytoma) presents with flank pain. Non-contrast CT abdomen reveals bilateral multiple renal masses containing distinct areas of MACROSCOPIC fat measuring -45 Hounsfield Units (HU) alongside tortuous aneurysms. What is the diagnosis, and which life-threatening haemorrhagic complication occurs when lesions exceed 4 cm?","o":["Simple Bosniak I renal cyst; aspiration","Clear cell renal cell carcinoma; immediate bilateral radical nephrectomy","Oncocytoma; lifelong dialysis immediately","Renal Angiomyolipoma (AML); spontaneous massive retroperitoneal haemorrhage (Wunderlich syndrome), prevented by selective transarterial embolisation or mTOR inhibition (everolimus)"],"a":3,"e":"Demonstration of macroscopic fat (\u003c-10 to -20 HU on thin-slice non-contrast CT without calcification) inside a solid renal cortical mass is virtually diagnostic of benign Renal Angiomyolipoma (composed of dysmorphic blood vessels lacking elastic tissue, smooth muscle, and fat—strongly associated with Tuberous Sclerosis). Dysplastic aneurysms \u003e 5 mm or AMLs \u003e 4 cm carry high risk of spontaneous rupture into the retroperitoneum (Wunderlich syndrome), managed via nephron-sparing selective angioembolisation or mTOR inhibitor (everolimus) in TSC.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03345","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Malignant Spinal Cord Compression (MSCC)","q":"A 63-year-old man with metastatic prostate carcinoma develops worsening mid-back pain (aggravated by coughing/lying flat) followed by bilateral leg heaviness, brisk knee/ankle reflexes, extensor plantars, a sensory level at T8, and post-void urinary residual of 350 mL. What is the immediate medical and radiological sequence?","o":["Administer IV Dexamethasone (10-16 mg bolus immediately) AND perform emergency contrast MRI of the ENTIRE SPINE within 24 hours (to guide urgent surgical decompression + radiotherapy)","Withhold steroids until biopsy confirms prostate metastasis","Perform immediate lumbar puncture below L4 before imaging","Perform plain lumbar X-ray alone and schedule MRI in 3 weeks"],"a":0,"e":"Malignant Spinal Cord Compression (MSCC) is an oncologic emergency where ambulatory status at treatment initiation is the strongest predictor of preserving walking ability. Immediate IV Dexamethasone reduces vasogenic cord oedema within hours, and emergency gadolinium MRI of the WHOLE spine (because ~20-30% of patients harbour synchronous multi-level epidural metastases!) must be performed urgently to direct neurosurgical decompression + radiation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03346","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Osteonecrosis (Avascular Necrosis) of Femoral Head","q":"A 33-year-old man who received prolonged systemic corticosteroids for nephrotic syndrome develops deep left groin pain and limited internal rotation of the hip. Plain anteroposterior and frog-leg lateral pelvic radiographs are completely normal (no subchondral \u0027crescent sign\u0027 or femoral head flattening). MRI of both hips shows a serpiginous subchondral band with a characteristic \u0027double-line sign\u0027 on T2-weighted images in the left femoral head (Ficat-Arlet Stage I/pre-collapse). What is the most appropriate joint-preserving surgical option?","o":["High-dose intra-articular steroid injection into the hip","Core decompression (+/- bone grafting/orthobiologics) while the femoral head remains spherical before subchondral collapse","Immediate bilateral total hip arthroplasty at Ficat Stage I","Wait until complete articular collapse before obtaining MRI"],"a":1,"e":"Non-traumatic osteonecrosis (AVN) of the femoral head is strongly linked to corticosteroids, alcohol excess, and sickle-cell disease (frequently bilateral, mandating bilateral MRI). MRI is \u003e95% sensitive in Ficat Stage I (normal X-ray), demonstrating the pathognomonic T2 \u0027double-line sign\u0027 (hypervascular granulation border adjacent to sclerotic bone). Joint-preserving Core Decompression reduces intraosseous venous hypertension and stimulates revascularisation BEFORE subchondral fracture (\u0027crescent sign\u0027, Stage III) or flattening occurs (when total hip arthroplasty becomes necessary).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03347","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC)","q":"A 44-year-old man from Nagaland (Northeast India) presents with a painless firm 4 cm mass in the upper posterior triangle of the neck (Level V), unilateral right-sided conductive hearing loss from persistent serous otitis media, ipsilateral palatal immobility, and trigeminal neuralgia (Trotter\u0027s triad). Nasal endoscopy shows an exophytic mass in the fossa of Rosenmuller, and biopsy confirms EBV-positive non-keratinising undifferentiated Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma. What is the primary curative treatment?","o":["Radical neck dissection followed by total maxillectomy","Observation alone","Definitive Radiotherapy (for Stage I) or Concurrent Chemoradiotherapy with Cisplatin (for locally advanced disease)—NOT upfront neck dissection or primary surgery","Oral acyclovir for EBV eradication"],"a":2,"e":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC, strongly linked to Epstein-Barr Virus, salted fish nitrosamines, and common in Northeast India/Southern China) originates in the fossa of Rosenmuller, obstructing the Eustachian tube (unilateral adult OME = rule out NPC!) and invading the skull base/levator veli palatini/CN V3 (Trotter\u0027s triad) with early Level V/retropharyngeal nodes. Because the skull base limits surgical margins and NPC is exquisitely radiosensitive, primary radiotherapy/concurrent cisplatin chemoradiotherapy is curative (salvage neck dissection/nasopharyngectomy only for residual disease).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03348","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Age-Related Macular Degeneration (AMD)","q":"A 71-year-old female smoker presents with sudden onset of central wavy distortion of straight lines (metamorphopsia on Amsler grid) and a central scotoma in her right eye. Fundoscopy shows soft confluent macular drusen, subretinal haemorrhage, and greyish subretinal fluid at the fovea; macular OCT confirms intraretinal/subretinal fluid and a choroidal neovascular membrane (CNVM). What is the first-line sight-preserving treatment?","o":["Topical nepafenac eye drops","Oral AREDS2 antioxidant vitamins alone (which slow intermediate dry AMD only)","Immediate panretinal laser photocoagulation across the fovea","Intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy (ranibizumab, aflibercept, bevacizumab, or faricimab)"],"a":3,"e":"Metamorphopsia (straight lines appearing wavy on Amsler grid), subretinal haemorrhage, and OCT fluid/CNVM represent Neovascular (\u0027Wet\u0027) Age-Related Macular Degeneration—the leading cause of severe irreversible central vision loss in older adults. Prompt monthly/treat-and-extend intravitreal anti-VEGF injections regress choroidal neovascularisation, resolve macular fluid, and stabilise or improve vision.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03349","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS)","q":"Four weeks after initiating allopurinol (or phenytoin) for gout, a 52-year-old man develops high fever (39.4 degrees C), marked facial oedema, diffuse confluent morbilliform rash without significant epidermal sloughing, cervical lymphadenopathy, WBC 18,000/mm3 with 22% eosinophils and atypical lymphocytes, ALT 480 U/L, and creatinine 2.1 mg/dL (RegiSCAR \u003e= 6). What is the diagnosis and why must systemic corticosteroids be tapered SLOWLY over weeks to months?","o":["DRESS syndrome (Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms); rapid steroid taper precipitates severe rebound flare of hepatitis/myocarditis/rash (often linked to HHV-6 reactivation)","Stevens-Johnson syndrome; stop allopurinol and give 3 days of oral steroids only","Simple benign morbilliform drug eruption; continue allopurinol with cetirizine","Acute bacterial cellulitis; IV flucloxacillin while continuing allopurinol"],"a":0,"e":"DRESS characteristically has a long latency (2-8 weeks after starting aromatic anticonvulsants, allopurinol, dapsone, sulfonamides, or vancomycin) with fever, facial oedema, widespread rash, lymphadenopathy, haematological abnormalities (eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytosis), internal organ injury (hepatitis \u003e nephritis/pneumonitis/myocarditis), and HHV-6/EBV/CMV reactivation. Immediate permanent withdrawal of culprit drug + systemic corticosteroids tapered slowly over 6-12 weeks prevents fatal visceral relapse (also check thyroiditis months later).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03350","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Research Methodology and Biostatistics","topic":"Risk Reduction and Harm Metrics","q":"In a 3-year RCT of an oral anticoagulant vs placebo after venous thromboembolism, recurrent thrombosis occurred in 10.0% of the placebo arm and 6.0% of the anticoagulant arm, whereas major bleeding occurred in 1.5% of the placebo arm and 4.0% of the anticoagulant arm. What are the Relative Risk Reduction (RRR) for thrombosis and the Number Needed to Harm (NNH) for major bleeding?","o":["RRR = 60%; NNH = 40","RRR = 40%; NNH = 40","RRR = 40%; NNH = 25","RRR = 4%; NNH = 25"],"a":1,"e":"Relative Risk Reduction (RRR) = (CER - EER) / CER = (10.0% - 6.0%) / 10.0% = 4.0 / 10.0 = 40% (while ARR = 4.0%, NNT = 25). Absolute Risk Increase (ARI) for major bleeding = 4.0% - 1.5% = 2.5% (0.025). Number Needed to Harm (NNH) = 1 / ARI = 1 / 0.025 = 40 patients treated to cause one additional major bleed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03351","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","q":"A 64-year-old man with hypertension and diabetes (CHA2DS2-VASc = 3) presents with regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 150 bpm. IV Adenosine (6 mg) transiently increases AV block, revealing regular sawtooth \u0027F\u0027 waves at 300 bpm (negative in II, III, aVF; positive in V1) before returning to 2:1 conduction at 150 bpm. Which two statements accurately govern long-term stroke prophylaxis and definitive rhythm cure for typical cavotricuspid isthmus (CTI)-dependent atrial flutter?","o":["Immediate permanent pacemaker without anticoagulation","Atrial flutter carries zero thromboembolic stroke risk so anticoagulation is never needed; give lifelong digoxin alone","Oral anticoagulation rules are IDENTICAL to Atrial Fibrillation (based on CHA2DS2-VASc = 3); radiofrequency catheter ablation of the Cavotricuspid Isthmus cures typical flutter in \u003e 95%","IV adenosine permanently cures atrial flutter by ablating the macroreentrant circuit"],"a":2,"e":"Typical counterclockwise atrial flutter is a macroreentrant right atrial circuit passing through the Cavotricuspid Isthmus (atrial rate ~300 bpm, classically conducting 2:1 -\u003e regular ventricular rate 150 bpm!). Adenosine transiently slows AV nodal conduction to unmask sawtooth flutter waves without terminating the atrial circuit. Crucially, atrial flutter carries substantial stroke risk (often coexisting with AF), so anticoagulation follows identical CHA2DS2-VASc thresholds, and CTI catheter ablation achieves \u003e95% cure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03352","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome (OHS)","q":"A 48-year-old woman (BMI 44 kg/m2, never-smoker, normal spirometry FEV1/FVC 0.82) presents with daytime hypersomnolence, ankle oedema, pulmonary hypertension, and awake daytime arterial blood gas showing pH 7.36, PaCO2 54 mmHg, HCO3- 31 mEq/L, and PaO2 62 mmHg. Polysomnography shows severe nocturnal hypoventilation/OSA. What is the diagnosis and primary therapy?","o":["Primary pulmonary hypertension; oral sildenafil alone without PAP","Central hypothyroidism alone; furosemide monotherapy","COPD with emphysema; high-flow 100% home oxygen without PAP","Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome (OHS); nocturnal Positive Airway Pressure (CPAP initially if severe concomitant OSA, or BiPAP) PLUS structured weight loss—avoiding isolated supplemental oxygen alone"],"a":3,"e":"Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome (Pickwickian syndrome) is defined by BMI \u003e= 30 kg/m2 + awake daytime hypercapnia (PaCO2 \u003e= 45 mmHg) + sleep-disordered breathing after excluding COPD, restrictive chest wall/neuromuscular disease, or hypothyroidism. First-line treatment is nocturnal PAP (CPAP in the ~90% with coexisting severe OSA, or BiPAP if pure hypoventilation/CPAP fails) + substantial weight loss (including bariatric evaluation). Giving unmonitored supplemental O2 alone worsens hypercapnic coma.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03353","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Lupus Nephritis","q":"A 26-year-old woman with Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) presents with hypertension, periorbital oedema, active urinary sediment (RBC casts, dysmorphic RBCs, 4.5 g/day proteinuria), creatinine rising from 0.8 to 2.1 mg/dL, high anti-dsDNA, and very low C3/C4. Renal biopsy demonstrates endocapillary hypercellularity, subendothelial \u0027wire-loop\u0027 immune deposits, cellular crescents involving \u003e50% of glomeruli, and \u0027full-house\u0027 immunofluorescence (IgG, IgA, IgM, C3, C1q) — ISN/RPS Class IV Diffuse Lupus Nephritis. Which induction regimen is standard of care?","o":["High-dose Glucocorticoids (IV methylprednisolone pulses followed by oral taper) PLUS Mycophenolate Mofetil (MMF) OR low-dose Euro-Lupus IV Cyclophosphamide (plus universal Hydroxychloroquine)","Azathioprine monotherapy alone","Hydroxychloroquine monotherapy without glucocorticoids or immunosuppressants","ACE inhibitor alone without immunosuppression"],"a":0,"e":"Class III (focal) and Class IV (diffuse, commonest and most severe, \u0027wire-loop\u0027 subendothelial deposits and full-house IF) proliferative lupus nephritis threaten rapid ESRD if untreated. Induction combines pulse/tapered glucocorticoids with either MMF (2-3 g/day) or low-dose IV Cyclophosphamide (Euro-Lupus 500 mg q2w x 6), adding belimumab or voclosporin where indicated, alongside background hydroxychloroquine and ACEi/ARB (maintenance follows with MMF or azathioprine).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03354","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Cholestatic Liver Diseases","q":"A 48-year-old woman presents with 1 year of severe nocturnal pruritus, fatigue, xanthelasma around the eyelids, and hepatomegaly. Labs show alkaline phosphatase (ALP) 640 U/L (\u003e4x ULN), GGT 480 U/L, elevated IgM, high total cholesterol, minimally elevated transaminases, normal biliary tree without strictures on MRCP, and strongly positive Anti-Mitochondrial Antibodies (AMA-M2, \u003e95% specific). What is the diagnosis and first-line disease-modifying treatment?","o":["Extrahepatic choledocholithiasis; emergency Whipple procedure","Primary Biliary Cholangitis (PBC); lifelong oral Ursodeoxycholic Acid (UDCA 13-15 mg/kg/day) (+ cholestyramine for pruritus and osteoporosis prophylaxis)","Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis (which shows \u0027beads-on-a-string\u0027 MRCP strictures and p-ANCA/IBD association); immediate colectomy","Autoimmune hepatitis; oral methotrexate monotherapy"],"a":1,"e":"Middle-aged woman with chronic cholestasis (pruritus, xanthelasma, high ALP/GGT, high IgM), normal MRCP bile ducts, and positive Anti-Mitochondrial Antibodies (AMA targeting pyruvate dehydrogenase E2) confirms Primary Biliary Cholangitis (chronic nonsuppurative destructive cholangitis of small interlobular bile ducts—biopsy not required if AMA+ and ALP elevated). Lifelong UDCA (13-15 mg/kg/day) normalises liver biochemistry, delays cirrhosis, and prolongs transplant-free survival (adding obeticholic acid/fibrates if incomplete biochemical response).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03355","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Thyroiditis","q":"Two weeks after an upper respiratory viral illness, a 34-year-old woman presents with fever, severe anterior neck pain radiating to her jaw/ears, an exquisitely tender firm thyroid gland, palpitations, and tremor. Labs show TSH \u003c 0.01 mIU/L, elevated free T4 (3.4 ng/dL), ESR 88 mm/h, negative TRAb, and a 24-hour Radioactive Iodine Uptake (RAIU) of 1.2% (markedly suppressed). What is the diagnosis and appropriate therapy?","o":["Hashimoto\u0027s crisis; lifelong high-dose methimazole","Graves\u0027 disease; immediate I-131 radioiodine ablation","Subacute Granulomatous (de Quervain\u0027s) Thyroiditis; symptomatic NSAIDs (or short oral prednisolone taper if severe pain) PLUS propranolol—NO carbimazole/PTU (antithyroid drugs are ineffective)","Anaplastic thyroid cancer; emergency tracheostomy"],"a":2,"e":"Post-viral painful tender thyroid + elevated ESR + suppressed TSH/high T4 + VERY LOW 24-hour RAIU (\u003c2-5%) confirm destructive Subacute (de Quervain\u0027s) Thyroiditis (classically triphasic: transient thyrotoxicosis from follicle rupture releasing preformed T4/T3 -\u003e transient hypothyroidism -\u003e euthyroid recovery). Because excess hormone comes from passive follicular leakage rather than de novo synthesis, thionamides (methimazole/PTU) and I-131 are useless; beta-blockers control adrenergic symptoms while NSAIDs/prednisolone relieve thyroid pain.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03356","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Sickle Cell Disease Emergencies","q":"A 21-year-old man with Sickle Cell Anaemia (HbSS, baseline Hb 8.2 g/dL) admitted for a vaso-occlusive bone pain crisis develops fever (38.9 degrees C), pleuritic chest pain, tachypnoea, SpO2 86%, Hb dropping to 6.1 g/dL, and a new lobar pulmonary infiltrate on chest X-ray (Acute Chest Syndrome). Beyond oxygen, analgesia without over-sedation, incentive spirometry, and empiric IV ceftriaxone + azithromycin, which haematological intervention reverses intrapulmonary sickling and hypoxaemia?","o":["Give immediate high-dose IV iron sucrose","Wait without transfusion until Hb drops below 3.0 g/dL","Administer IV mannitol and aggressive hyperhydration (6 L/day) causing pulmonary oedema","Urgent Packed RBC transfusion (simple transfusion to target Hb ~10 g/dL, or automated/manual Exchange Transfusion targeting HbS \u003c 30% if severe hypoxia/multilobe infiltrates)"],"a":3,"e":"Acute Chest Syndrome (new pulmonary infiltrate + fever/respiratory symptoms/hypoxaemia in sickle cell disease) is the leading cause of death in adult SCD, driven by infection, fat embolism, and vaso-occlusion. Prompt simple transfusion (avoiding raising Hb \u003e 10-11 g/dL to prevent hyperviscosity!) or urgent Red Cell Exchange Transfusion (if hypoxaemic/multilobar/rapidly deteriorating, lowering HbS \u003c 30%) rapidly restores oxygenation alongside atypical + pneumococcal antibiotic coverage, bronchodilators, and incentive spirometry.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03357","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Motor Neuron Diseases","q":"A 56-year-old man presents with 9 months of progressive asymmetric hand weakness (\u0027split-hand\u0027 atrophy), diffuse fasciculations of the limbs and tongue, dysarthria/dysphagia, YET brisk hyperactive deep tendon reflexes, spasticity, and bilateral Babinski responses—with completely intact sensation, normal bowel/bladder sphincters, and normal extraocular eye movements. EMG confirms widespread active denervation/reinnervation. What is the diagnosis and disease-modifying/respiratory care?","o":["Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS); Riluzole (+ edaravone) modestly prolongs survival, alongside early Non-Invasive Ventilation (BiPAP) when FVC \u003c 50% or orthopnoea develops and gastrostomy feeding","Myasthenia gravis (which spares reflexes and has no atrophy/fasciculations); pyridostigmine","Cervical spondylotic myelopathy alone (which spares the tongue and bulbar muscles); cervical laminectomy cures it","Guillain-Barre syndrome; 5 days of IVIG cures it"],"a":0,"e":"ALS (Lou Gehrig disease) is characterised by progressive degeneration of BOTH Upper Motor Neurons (spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski) AND Lower Motor Neurons (atrophy, fasciculations, weakness) across bulbar, cervical, thoracic, and lumbosacral regions while classically sparing sensory pathways, autonomic/bowel-bladder sphincters, and oculomotor nuclei. Riluzole (glutamate antagonist) and early NIV (BiPAP when FVC \u003c 50% predicted or nocturnal hypoventilation) improve quality of life and survival.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03358","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Non-Variceal Upper GI Bleeding","q":"A 58-year-old man with NSAID-induced haematemesis undergoes urgent upper GI endoscopy within 24 hours, revealing a 1.5 cm duodenal bulb ulcer with a protruding, non-bleeding visible arterial vessel (Forrest Class IIa, high rebleeding risk ~40-50%). What is the recommended endoscopic and post-endoscopic acid-suppression protocol?","o":["Epinephrine injection monotherapy without mechanical/thermal therapy and immediate discharge","Dual endoscopic haemostasis (dilute epinephrine injection PLUS thermal coagulation or haemoclip application—never epinephrine alone) FOLLOWED by 72 hours of high-dose IV PPI (e.g., pantoprazole/omeprazole bolus + infusion or BID)","Oral antacid suspension alone without endoscopic treatment","Immediate subtotal gastrectomy despite easy endoscopic access"],"a":1,"e":"Forrest Class Ia (spurting), Ib (oozing), and IIa (non-bleeding visible vessel) ulcers carry high rebleeding risk and require definitive endoscopic haemostasis—combining dilute epinephrine injection with a second modality (hemoclips or bipolar/heater-probe thermal therapy), because epinephrine monotherapy has unacceptable rebleeding rates. Maintaining intragastric pH \u003e 6 for 72 hours via high-dose IV PPI stabilises the clot, followed by H. pylori testing/eradication.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03359","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Complicated Gallstone Disease","q":"A 55-year-old woman with longstanding gallstones presents with painless-to-colicky obstructive jaundice (bilirubin 5.4 mg/dL, ALP 490 U/L). MRCP reveals a large 2.5 cm gallstone impacted in Hartmann\u0027s pouch/cystic duct externally compressing and partially eroding into the adjacent Common Hepatic Duct (Mirizzi Syndrome Type II cholecystobiliary fistula), with normal distal CBD. Why is preoperative recognition critical before laparoscopic exploration, and what is the safe surgical principle?","o":["Proceed aggressively with blind Calot\u0027s triangle diathermy clipping every tubular structure","Administer oral ursodeoxycholic acid for a 2.5 cm impacted fistula stone","Dense Calot\u0027s triangle inflammation obliterates anatomy and risks mistaking the Common Hepatic Duct for the cystic duct; perform open/conversion subtotal cholecystectomy leaving a gallbladder cuff to reconstruct/patch the CHD defect (+/- Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy if large defect)","Perform simple percutaneous cholecystostomy permanently in a fit patient"],"a":2,"e":"Mirizzi syndrome (Csendes Type I = extrinsic CHD compression; Types II-IV = cholecystocholedochal fistula involving \u003c1/3 to \u003e2/3 of CHD circumference; Type V = bilioenteric fistula) obliterates Calot\u0027s triangle, making standard laparoscopic cholecystectomy notorious for causing Strasberg E bile duct transection. Safe management uses fundus-first/subtotal cholecystectomy (avoiding hazardous Calot\u0027s dissection), stone extraction, cuff choledochoplasty (or bilioenteric Roux-en-Y reconstruction for large Type III/IV defects), and checking frozen section to exclude gallbladder carcinoma.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03360","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Hereditary Non-Polyposis Colorectal Cancer (Lynch Syndrome)","q":"A 36-year-old woman presents with a mucinous adenocarcinoma of the caecum (only 2 other colon polyps). Her mother developed endometrial carcinoma at age 44 and her maternal uncle had ascending colon cancer at age 41 (Amsterdam II criteria met). Tumour immunohistochemistry shows loss of MLH1/PMS2 (or MSH2/MSH6) and high microsatellite instability (MSI-H, without BRAF V600E), confirming germline Lynch syndrome (HNPCC). What surgical scope and extracolonic cancer prophylaxis are indicated?","o":["No germline genetic testing or family cascade screening needed","Simple right hemicolectomy with discharge and no further surveillance","Total proctocolectomy with IPAA reserved strictly for FAP (\u003e100 polyps) only; zero endometrial risk","Subtotal colectomy with ileorectal anastomosis (given high metachronous colon cancer risk) PLUS counselling for prophylactic total hysterectomy + BSO once childbearing is complete (plus regular endometrial/upper GI surveillance)"],"a":3,"e":"Lynch syndrome (autosomal dominant germline mutation in DNA mismatch repair genes MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2, EPCAM) is the commonest hereditary colorectal cancer syndrome (~3% of CRCs), characterised by young-onset proximal/right-sided poorly differentiated/mucinous MSI-H cancers (immunotherapy-responsive if metastatic!) and high lifetime risk of endometrial (~40-60%) and ovarian (~10-15%) cancers. Subtotal colectomy + ileorectal anastomosis reduces metachronous CRC, and risk-reducing hysterectomy-BSO after completing family eliminates gynaecological cancer death.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03361","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Penetrating Neck Trauma","q":"A 27-year-old man sustains a stab wound to the left anterolateral neck (Zone II, between cricoid cartilage and angle of mandible) violating the platysma. On arrival, he has BP 78/48 mmHg, a rapidly expanding pulsatile haematoma in the neck, active arterial bleeding, and inspiratory stridor (\u0027hard signs\u0027). What is the immediate management?","o":["Secure the airway immediately and transfer directly to the Operating Theatre for emergency surgical neck exploration (do NOT delay for CT angiography)","Apply a tight circumferential neck tourniquet","Probe the neck wound deeply with a finger in the emergency bay to find the carotid tear","Send the unstable patient to CT angiography first before airway or surgery"],"a":0,"e":"Regardless of anatomical zone (Zone I/II/III), any penetrating neck wound breaching the platysma with \u0027hard signs\u0027 of vascular or aerodigestive injury—active haemorrhage, expanding/pulsatile haematoma, shock, absent radial pulse, bruit/thrill, cerebral ischaemia, airway compromise, or bubbling air/massive subcutaneous emphysema—mandates immediate airway control and direct transfer to the OR for surgical exploration. (Stable patients WITHOUT hard signs undergo multidetector CT angiography under the modern \u0027no-zone\u0027 selective algorithm).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03362","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Venous Thoracic Outlet Syndrome","q":"A 24-year-old competitive swimmer and weightlifter presents 2 days after intense overhead training with acute painful swelling, cyanosis, and prominent dilated superficial collateral veins over his dominant right shoulder and upper arm. Venous duplex and venography confirm acute occlusion of the right subclavian-axillary vein compressed between the clavicle and first rib (Paget-Schroetter syndrome). What is the optimal acute and definitive management in a young active patient?","o":["Immediate anticoagulation PLUS catheter-directed thrombolysis (within 14 days), followed by surgical thoracic outlet decompression (first-rib resection) to prevent recurrent thrombosis","Oral aspirin alone without anticoagulation","Immediate above-elbow amputation","IV furosemide and strict immobilisation in a sling for 6 months"],"a":0,"e":"Paget-Schroetter syndrome (\u0027effort thrombosis\u0027) is primary axillary-subclavian vein DVT triggered by repetitive overhead arm abduction compressing the vein in the costoclavicular space (between clavicle, first rib, subclavius, and anterior scalene). In young active patients presenting early (\u003c2 weeks), immediate anticoagulation + catheter-directed thrombolysis restores vein patency, and prompt first-rib resection (+/- venolysis/angioplasty) removes the bony pinch preventing chronic post-thrombotic syndrome and re-thrombosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03363","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Vasa Praevia","q":"A 28-year-old woman with a bilobed placenta in early labour at 38 weeks undergoes spontaneous rupture of membranes. Immediately as clear fluid gushes out, fresh vaginal bleeding begins (~150 mL) while maternal pulse and BP remain completely normal, and the CTG instantly transitions to a sinusoidal pattern followed by profound fetal bradycardia (50 bpm). What is the diagnosis and immediate action?","o":["Cervical show from rapid dilatation; augment with IV oxytocin","Ruptured Vasa Praevia (fetal exsanguination from velamentous fetal vessels crossing the internal os); IMMEDIATE Category 1 emergency Caesarean section and prepare neonatal O-negative blood resuscitation","Placenta praevia; perform Macafee expectant management","Maternal abruptio placentae; wait 2 hours for Apt test and Kleihauer-Betke before deciding on delivery"],"a":1,"e":"Vasa praevia occurs when unsupported fetal blood vessels (velamentous cord insertion or vessels bridging a succenturiate/bilobed placenta) traverse the membranes over the internal cervical os below the presenting part. Membrane rupture tears these vessels, causing acute FETAL haemorrhage (classic triad: membrane rupture + painless bleeding + acute sinusoidal tracing/fetal bradycardia with normal maternal vitals!). Because a term fetus has only ~250-300 mL total blood volume, waiting for confirmatory Apt/Kleihauer tests is fatal; immediate emergency CS + neonatal volume/RBC transfusion saves the baby (when diagnosed antenatally on colour Doppler TVS, planned CS at 34-37 weeks prevents rupture).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03364","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Vulvar Carcinoma","q":"A 68-year-old woman with longstanding lichen sclerosus presents with a 3.0 cm ulcerated tumour on the lateral aspect of the right labium majus (\u003e2 cm from midline), biopsied as invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma with 4 mm stromal invasion (\u003e1 mm), clinically negative groin nodes (FIGO Stage IB). What is the standard surgical management?","o":["Simple topical imiquimod cream without surgery","Total abdominal hysterectomy without addressing the vulva or groin","Radical wide local excision (1-2 cm macroscopically clear lateral/deep margins) PLUS ipsilateral inguinofemoral lymphadenectomy (or sentinel lymph node biopsy if unifocal \u003c 4 cm and nodes clinically negative)","Wait-and-watch observation because depth is 4 mm"],"a":2,"e":"Invasive vulvar squamous cell carcinoma with \u003e 1 mm depth of stromal invasion (Stage \u003e= IB) carries significant (~15-20%+) risk of occult inguinofemoral lymph node metastasis, which is the single most important prognostic factor. Triple-incision radical wide local excision (achieving \u003e= 8 mm fixed / 1-2 cm surgical margins) combined with ipsilateral inguinofemoral nodal staging (SLNB via Tc-99m + blue dye for unifocal \u003c4 cm cN0 lesions, or formal inguinofemoral lymphadenectomy; bilateral if \u003c2 cm from midline) replaces mutilating en-bloc butterfly vulvectomy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03365","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Meconium Ileus","q":"A full-term neonate fails to pass meconium in the first 48 hours and develops progressive abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. Abdominal radiograph shows dilated small-bowel loops without air-fluid levels and a characteristic \u0027soap-bubble\u0027 (ground-glass Neuhauser sign) appearance in the right lower quadrant. Contrast enema shows a narrow unused \u0027microcolon\u0027 with inspissated pellets impacted in the terminal ileum (uncomplicated Meconium Ileus, no perforation or volvulus). What is the preferred non-operative treatment and mandatory genetic/metabolic follow-up?","o":["Barium enema alone without hydration","Oral lactulose and outpatient discharge","Immediate total colectomy on Day 2","Therapeutic hyperosmolar water-soluble contrast (Gastrografin) enema under fluoroscopy with generous IV hydration, FOLLOWED by sweat chloride / CFTR genetic testing for Cystic Fibrosis"],"a":3,"e":"Meconium ileus is the earliest clinical manifestation of Cystic Fibrosis (~80-90% of infants with meconium ileus have CF; inspissated viscid meconium blocks the terminal ileum, producing Neuhauser\u0027s \u0027soap-bubble\u0027 appearance without air-fluid levels and a disused microcolon). In uncomplicated cases (no volvulus, atresia, or perforation), a fluoroscopic Gastrografin (hyperosmolar water-soluble + Tween-80 wetting agent) enema draws fluid intraluminally (requiring pre-procedure IV hydration to prevent hypovolaemic shock!) and flushes out the plug in ~60-80%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03366","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Paediatrics","topic":"Congenital TORCH Infections","q":"A newborn presents with SGA, microcephaly, \u0027blueberry muffin\u0027 petechiae, hepatosplenomegaly, conjugated jaundice, chorioretinitis, and bilateral sensorineural hearing loss. Non-contrast head CT/cranial ultrasound reveals PERIVENTRICULAR intracranial calcifications. Which diagnostic test within the first 21 days of life confirms Congenital CMV, and what antiviral therapy improves hearing and neurodevelopmental outcomes?","o":["Saliva or urine CMV PCR within the first 21 days of life; 6 months of oral Valganciclovir (or IV ganciclovir initially)","VDRL alone; benzathine penicillin","Serum IgG CMV alone at 6 months of age (cannot distinguish maternal IgG or postnatal acquisition); oral acyclovir","Skull X-ray showing diffuse cortical calcifications (classic for toxoplasmosis); pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine"],"a":0,"e":"Congenital CMV is the leading non-genetic cause of sensorineural hearing loss, classically showing PERIVENTRICULAR calcifications (vs diffuse parenchymal calcifications + hydrocephalus + chorioretinitis in congenital Toxoplasmosis). Diagnosis MUST use urine or saliva CMV PCR/culture BEFORE 21 days of age (positive PCR after 3 weeks cannot differentiate congenital from postnatal breastmilk/perinatal infection!). Symptomatic cCMV with CNS/hearing involvement treated with 6 months of oral Valganciclovir preserves hearing and improves neurodevelopment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03367","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Perioperative Gastric Aspiration (Mendelson Syndrome)","q":"During rapid-sequence induction for emergency bowel obstruction, a 45-year-old man regurgitates bilious gastric fluid into his oropharynx with audible coarse crackles, bronchospasm, and SpO2 dropping to 86% (chemical pneumonitis / Mendelson syndrome). Immediately after placing him head-down/lateral, suctioning the oropharynx/trachea, intubating, and applying oxygen + PEEP, which statement accurately guides early corticosteroids and antibiotics?","o":["Extubate immediately in the operating theatre regardless of oxygenation","Do NOT administer routine systemic corticosteroids (no benefit, increases infection) or immediate prophylactic antibiotics for pure chemical pneumonitis—reserve antibiotics for persistent/new infiltrates, fever, and purulent sputum beyond 48 hours (or feculent/obstructed-bowel aspiration)","Immediately infuse high-dose IV methylprednisolone 1 g and 3-drug broad-spectrum antibiotics routinely in all uncomplicated acid aspirations","Perform immediate large-volume normal saline bronchial lavage down the ETT to neutralise acid"],"a":1,"e":"Gastric acid injury (\u003c pH 2.5) produces an immediate sterile chemical pneumonitis peaking at 24-36 hours; bronchial saline lavage pushes acid deeper into alveoli and is harmful! Standard management includes prompt airway suctioning (bronchoscopy only for particulate solid food obstruction), PEEP/lung-protective oxygenation, and supportive ICU care. Randomised trials show routine systemic corticosteroids increase Gram-negative superinfection without improving survival, and indiscriminate immediate antibiotics select MDR flora unless bacterial infection develops \u003e48 h (or contaminated intestinal content was aspirated).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03368","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Anaesthesiology and Critical Care","topic":"Total Spinal Anaesthesia","q":"Two minutes after administering a 15 mL epidural top-up of 2% lidocaine-epinephrine for emergency Caesarean section, a 28-year-old parturient complains of hand tingling, sudden loss of voice, apnoea, fixed dilated pupils, unmeasurable BP, severe bradycardia (36/min), and loss of consciousness (inadvertent intrathecal Total Spinal Anaesthesia). What is the immediate life-saving resuscitation sequence?","o":["Administer 20% Intralipid bolus alone without intubation or vasopressors","Sit the patient upright 90 degrees immediately to make the block fall","Immediate 100% O2 intubation and controlled mechanical ventilation, left uterine displacement, rapid IV crystalloid bolus, and IV Epinephrine/Atropine + Ephedrine/Phenylephrine vasopressors (plus perimortem CS within 4-5 min if cardiac arrest persists)","Administer IV labetalol and wait for spontaneous breathing"],"a":2,"e":"Unrecognised dural puncture during epidural bolus injection causes rapid cephalad CSF spread blocking T1-T4 cardiac accelerator fibres (profound bradycardia), total sympathectomy (vasoplegic shock), C3-C5 phrenic nerve/medullary respiratory arrest (apnoea), and intracranial hypotension/brainstem block (dilated pupils, unconsciousness). Unlike LAST, total spinal reverses completely over 1-2 hours IF oxygenation and perfusion are maintained immediately via intubation/ventilation, left uterine displacement (relieving aortocaval compression), aggressive IV fluids, and atropine/ephedrine/epinephrine.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03369","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Radiodiagnosis","topic":"Cerebrovascular Malformations","q":"A 28-year-old man presents with new-onset focal seizures. Brain MRI reveals a well-circumscribed 2.2 cm intra-axial supratentorial lesion with a reticulated \u0027popcorn\u0027 core of mixed T1/T2 blood-breakdown products surrounded by a complete, dark hypointense haemosiderin rim demonstrating marked \u0027blooming\u0027 artefact on T2* gradient-echo / Susceptibility-Weighted Imaging (SWI), with NO mass effect or surrounding vasogenic oedema. Digital subtraction angiography (DSA) is completely normal. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Glioblastoma multiforme (which shows ring enhancement and extensive oedema)","High-flow Arteriovenous Malformation (AVM, which shows dilated feeding arteries/draining veins on DSA)","Acute ischaemic MCA infarct","Cerebral Cavernous Malformation (cavernoma / cavernous haemangioma — angiographically occult)"],"a":3,"e":"Cerebral cavernous malformations are low-flow, thin-walled sinusoidal endothelial clusters lacking intervening brain parenchyma (sporadic or familial CCM1/2/3). Repeated microhaemorrhages create the pathognomonic MRI appearance: a central \u0027popcorn/mulberry\u0027 core of mixed-age blood ringed by a complete haemosiderin halo with pronounced SWI/GRE blooming and absent oedema. Because blood flow is sluggish, cavernomas are classically \u0027angiographically occult\u0027 (normal DSA).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03370","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Orthopaedics","topic":"Young-Burgess Pelvic Ring Classification","q":"A 34-year-old construction worker falls 8 metres from scaffolding landing upright on one leg and arrives in haemorrhagic shock. AP pelvis X-ray and CT show fractures of the left superior and inferior pubic rami combined with complete disruption of the left sacroiliac joint and 2.5 cm cranial (superior) migration of the left hemipelvis (and an L5 transverse process avulsion fracture). Under the Young-Burgess classification, what mechanism is this, and why can a simple circumferential pelvic binder alone be insufficient?","o":["Vertical Shear (VS) injury (completely unstable both rotationally AND vertically, tearing sacrospinous/sacrotuberous ligaments); requires longitudinal skeletal traction / pelvic C-clamp or angioembolisation alongside resuscitation","Lateral Compression Type I (LC-I); stable for immediate weight-bearing","Anteroposterior Compression Type I (APC-I); \u003c 2.5 cm pubic diastasis treated conservatively","Isolated avulsion of ASIS"],"a":0,"e":"In the Young-Burgess classification: APC injuries (\u0027open book\u0027) cause rotational instability (APC II/III); LC injuries cause internal rotation; Vertical Shear (VS) injuries result from high-energy axial vertical loading (falls from height), disrupting BOTH anterior ring and ALL posterior pelvic floor ligaments (sacroiliac, sacrospinous, sacrotuberous -\u003e L5 transverse process fracture!), creating complete rotational and vertical instability with severe arterial/venous retroperitoneal haemorrhage. Alongside binder/C-clamp and pelvic angioembolisation/packing, longitudinal distal femoral traction helps restore vertical height.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03371","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)","topic":"Temporal Bone Paragangliomas","q":"A 48-year-old woman presents with progressive unilateral pulse-synchronous tinnitus and conductive hearing loss (plus hoarseness and dysphagia from CN IX-XI involvement). Otoscopy reveals a reddish-blue pulsatile mass behind the inferior quadrant of an intact tympanic membrane (\u0027rising sun\u0027 sign) that blanches on positive pneumatic otoscopy (Brown\u0027s pulsation sign). High-resolution CT temporal bone shows irregular permeative (\u0027moth-eaten\u0027) erosion of the jugular foramen floor, and MRI shows \u0027salt-and-pepper\u0027 signal. What is the diagnosis and critical outpatient warning?","o":["Glomus jugulare tumour (jugulotympanic paraganglioma); NEVER perform office myringotomy or biopsy through the eardrum (risks massive haemorrhage)—screen catecholamines and manage via embolisation + surgery or stereotactic radiosurgery","Cholesterol granuloma; simple mastoidectomy without imaging","Acute otitis media; myringotomy with grommet insertion","Haemotympanum from barotrauma; immediate wide myringotomy and suction biopsy"],"a":0,"e":"Glomus jugulare (arising from the jugular bulb dome, eroding the jugular foramen and causing lower cranial neuropathies IX-XII / Vernet syndrome) and Glomus tympanicum (promontory) are hypervascular paragangliomas fed mainly by the ascending pharyngeal artery. Classic findings include pulsatile tinnitus, \u0027rising sun\u0027 retrotympanic red mass, Aquino sign (blanching on carotid compression), Brown\u0027s sign, and CT \u0027moth-eaten\u0027 jugular erosion. Trans-tympanic biopsy/myringotomy causes torrential bleeding and is strictly contraindicated; treatment uses preoperative embolisation + resection or stereotactic radiosurgery.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03372","course":"MD / MS","subject":"General Medicine","topic":"Autoimmune Subepidermal Bullous Disorders","q":"A 78-year-old man presents with intensely pruritic urticarial plaques followed by large, TENSE, dome-shaped fluid-filled bullae over the flexural thighs, groin, and lower abdomen, sparing the oral mucosa, with a negative Nikolsky sign. Skin biopsy shows a subepidermal blister rich in eosinophils, and direct immunofluorescence reveals a sharp LINEAR band of IgG and C3 along the dermoepidermal basement membrane zone (autoantibodies against hemidesmosomal BP180/BP230). What is the diagnosis and safe initial therapy in frail elderly patients?","o":["Pemphigus vulgaris; immediate high-dose cyclophosphamide alone","Bullous pemphigoid; superpotent topical corticosteroids (clobetasol propionate 0.05% applied extensively) OR moderate-dose oral prednisolone (0.5 mg/kg/day) +/- doxycycline/azathioprine","Dermatitis herpetiformis; lifelong gluten-free diet alone","Bullous impetigo; topical mupirocin alone"],"a":1,"e":"Bullous pemphigoid (commonest autoimmune blistering disorder of the elderly, autoantibodies to hemidesmosomal BP180/BP230, also triggered by DPP-4 inhibitors/gliptins) causes tense subepidermal bullae, urticarial prodrome, minimal/absent mucosal involvement, negative Nikolsky sign, and linear BMZ IgG/C3 on DIF. Extensive high-potency topical clobetasol propionate achieves equivalent disease control with significantly lower mortality than high-dose systemic prednisone in elderly patients (combining doxycycline+nicotinamide or low-dose methotrexate/azathioprine as steroid-sparers).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03373","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Psychiatry","topic":"Extrapyramidal Syndromes (EPS)","q":"A 22-year-old muscular man receives IM haloperidol 10 mg for acute psychosis and 12 hours later develops acute painful neck twisting (torticollis), jaw clenching, protruded tongue, and sustained involuntary upward deviation of both eyes (oculogyric crisis). What is the diagnosis and immediate rapid-acting treatment?","o":["Akathisia; increase haloperidol dose","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome; immediate oral propranolol alone","Acute dystonia (dopamine-acetylcholine imbalance in nigrostriatal pathway); immediate parenteral anticholinergic/antihistamine (IM/IV Benztropine 1-2 mg, Biperiden, or Diphenhydramine/Promethazine)","Tardive dyskinesia; IV haloperidol 10 mg bolus"],"a":2,"e":"Acute dystonia occurs within hours-to-days of initiating high-potency D2 antagonists (haloperidol, fluphenazine) especially in young males, driven by relative striatal cholinergic excess causing sustained painful spasms (torticollis, oculogyric crisis, opisthotonos, or life-threatening laryngeal dystonia). Immediate IM/IV Benztropine (1-2 mg), Biperiden, or Diphenhydramine/Promethazine reverses symptoms within 5-15 minutes, followed by oral anticholinergic prophylaxis and switching to an atypical antipsychotic (olanzapine/quetiapine/aripiprazole).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03374","course":"MD / MS","subject":"Research Methodology and Biostatistics","topic":"Bayesian Probability Updating","q":"In an outpatient clinic, a patient\u0027s pre-test probability of deep vein thrombosis (DVT) based on clinical Wells score is 20%. A rapid diagnostic test with Positive Likelihood Ratio (LR+) = 8.0 returns POSITIVE. Applying Bayes\u0027 theorem (Pre-test Odds -\u003e Post-test Odds -\u003e Post-test Probability), what is the patient\u0027s post-test probability of DVT?","o":["50.0%","80.0%","88.9%","66.7%"],"a":3,"e":"Step 1: Convert pre-test probability (P_pre = 0.20) to pre-test odds: Odds_pre = P_pre / (1 - P_pre) = 0.20 / 0.80 = 0.25. Step 2: Multiply by LR+: Odds_post = Odds_pre x LR+ = 0.25 x 8.0 = 2.0. Step 3: Convert post-test odds back to probability: P_post = Odds_post / (1 + Odds_post) = 2.0 / (1 + 2.0) = 2 / 3 = 66.7%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03375","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Quality \u0026 Accreditation","topic":"Hospital Bed Occupancy Rate (BOR) \u0026 Turnover","q":"A 200-bed tertiary hospital records 5,400 inpatient-days during a 30-day month. What is the Bed Occupancy Rate (BOR) for that month?","o":["90%","95%","80%","75%"],"a":0,"e":"Bed Occupancy Rate (BOR) = [Total Inpatient Days in Period / (Available Beds x Days in Period)] x 100 = [5,400 / (200 x 30)] x 100 = (5,400 / 6,000) x 100 = 90%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03376","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Operations and Utilization Metrics","topic":"Bed Occupancy Rate and ALOS","q":"A 200-bed multi-specialty hospital records 5,400 total inpatient service days and 900 total discharges/deaths during the month of April (30 days). What are the hospital\u0027s Bed Occupancy Rate (BOR) and Average Length of Stay (ALOS)?","o":["BOR = 75%; ALOS = 4.5 days","BOR = 90%; ALOS = 6.0 days","BOR = 95%; ALOS = 7.5 days","BOR = 85%; ALOS = 5.0 days"],"a":1,"e":"Available bed-days in April = 200 beds x 30 days = 6,000 bed-days. Bed Occupancy Rate (BOR) = (Inpatient service days / Available bed-days) x 100 = (5,400 / 6,000) x 100 = 90%. Average Length of Stay (ALOS) = Total inpatient service days / Total discharges and deaths = 5,400 / 900 = 6.0 days.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03377","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Materials and Inventory Management","topic":"ABC-VED Matrix Analysis","q":"In hospital pharmacy inventory management combining ABC (annual consumption value) and VED (Vital, Essential, Desirable clinical criticality) classification, which items constitute Category I requiring the strictest top-management control, zero stock-out tolerance, and frequent review?","o":["Only items purchased once every five years regardless of clinical criticality","Only Class C Desirable (CD) items with lowest cost","All items in AV, AE, AD, BV, and CV sub-categories (i.e., all Class A items and all Vital items)","Only Class B Essential (BE) and Class C Essential (CE) items"],"a":2,"e":"In the 3x3 ABC-VED matrix, Category I consists of AV, AE, AD, BV, and CV—encompassing both high-cost Class A drugs (~70% of annual drug budget) and all clinically Vital (V) drugs whose absence stops critical patient care. Category II comprises BE, CE, and BD; Category III comprises CD.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03378","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Quality and Patient Safety","topic":"Sentinel Event Management and RCA","q":"A patient in a NABH-accredited tertiary hospital undergoes a wrong-site knee arthroscopy despite pre-operative marking policies. Under NABH accreditation standards for sentinel events, what is the immediate mandatory administrative and quality response?","o":["Terminate the junior OT nurse immediately without analyzing system failures","Conceal the incident from the patient and family to protect hospital reputation","Wait until the annual external surveillance audit before documenting the event","Immediate disclosure and clinical stabilization, preserve evidence, notify leadership/NABH per timeline, and conduct a structured non-punitive Root Cause Analysis (RCA) with corrective and preventive actions (CAPA)"],"a":3,"e":"Wrong-site surgery is a classic sentinel event under NABH Patient Safety \u0026 Quality Improvement (PSQ) standards. Accredited hospitals must immediately care for the patient, transparently communicate, document/report within the stipulated timeframe, and perform a multidisciplinary, systems-focused Root Cause Analysis (RCA) to implement verifiable CAPA (such as enforcing surgical time-out and site-marking verification).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03379","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Infection Control and Waste Management","topic":"Color-Coded Segregation","q":"Under India\u0027s Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules, 2016 (and 2018 amendments), how should discarded blood-soaked cotton swabs, expired cytotoxic drugs, intact glass vials, and metallic needles/scalpel blades be segregated?","o":["Blood-soaked swabs \u0026 expired cytotoxic drugs: Yellow; Intact glass vials: Blue puncture-proof box; Needles \u0026 blades: White translucent puncture-proof container","Needles: Yellow bag; Blood-soaked swabs: White container; Glass vials: Red bag","Blood-soaked swabs: Red; Intact glass vials: Yellow; Needles: Blue; Cytotoxic drugs: General black bin","All four items mixed together in a Red chlorinated plastic bag for autoclaving"],"a":0,"e":"Per BMW Rules 2016: Yellow (non-chlorinated bag) receives anatomical waste, soiled blood-soaked items, expired/discarded medicines and cytotoxic waste (incineration/plasma pyrolysis); Red (non-chlorinated bag) receives recyclable contaminated plastics (tubing, catheters, syringes without needles); White (translucent, puncture-proof, leak-proof) receives sharps/metals; Blue (puncture-proof box/cardboard with blue marking) receives broken/discarded glassware and metallic body implants.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03380","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Healthcare Law and Medico-Legal Administration","topic":"PNDT Act, MTP Act, and Informed Consent","q":"An unconscious 32-year-old road-traffic accident victim arrives at a hospital emergency department with a ruptured spleen and hemorrhagic shock, unaccompanied by relatives. The surgical team needs to perform an emergency life-saving splenectomy immediately. From a medico-legal administration standpoint in India, what is the legally valid course of action?","o":["Wait for a magistrate\u0027s court order before wheeling the patient to the OT","Proceed immediately with life-saving surgery under the doctrine of necessity (emergency exception), documenting the urgent clinical indication and medical superintendent/two-doctor endorsement in the case sheet","Ask an unrelated ambulance driver to sign proxy consent on behalf of the patient","Refuse surgery until police locate next-of-kin and obtain a signed paper consent form"],"a":1,"e":"Under Indian medical jurisprudence and Supreme Court rulings (Parmanand Katara v. Union of India), preserving life in an emergency takes absolute precedence. When an incapacitated patient requires immediate life- or limb-saving surgery and no legally authorized surrogate is available, doctors must proceed without delay under the doctrine of necessity, with thorough documentation (ideally countersigned by a second senior doctor/medical administrator).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03381","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Materials and Inventory Management","topic":"Economic Order Quantity (EOQ)","q":"A hospital store consumes 10,000 units of sterile IV sets per year. The administrative cost to place each purchase order is INR 500, and the annual inventory carrying (holding) cost is INR 10 per unit per year. Using the classic Wilson Economic Order Quantity (EOQ) formula, what is the optimal order quantity per purchase?","o":["100 units per order","2,000 units per order","1,000 units per order","500 units per order"],"a":2,"e":"EOQ = sqrt[(2 x Annual Demand D x Ordering Cost S) / Annual Carrying Cost per unit H] = sqrt[(2 x 10,000 x 500) / 10] = sqrt[10,000,000 / 10] = sqrt(1,000,000) = 1,000 units per order (resulting in 10 orders per year, balancing ordering and holding costs).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03382","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Operations Research","topic":"Single-Server Queuing Model (M/M/1)","q":"At a hospital phlebotomy counter operating as a single-server M/M/1 queue, patients arrive randomly (Poisson) at a mean rate (lambda) of 12 patients/hour, and the phlebotomist serves patients exponentially at a mean rate (mu) of 15 patients/hour. What is the server utilization (rho) and the average number of patients in the system (L = lambda / [mu - lambda])?","o":["Utilization rho = 80%; average patients in system L = 12 patients","Utilization rho = 125%; unstable infinite queue","Utilization rho = 60%; average patients in system L = 1.5 patients","Utilization rho = 80%; average patients in system L = 4 patients"],"a":3,"e":"Server utilization rho = lambda / mu = 12 / 15 = 0.80 (80%). For an M/M/1 queue, average number in the system (waiting + being served) L = lambda / (mu - lambda) = 12 / (15 - 12) = 12 / 3 = 4 patients (average time in system W = L / lambda = 4 / 12 = 20 minutes). Notice how queues grow exponentially as utilization approaches 100%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03383","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Healthcare Law and Medico-Legal Administration","topic":"PCPNDT Act 1994 and MTP Amendment Act 2021","q":"Under India\u0027s Medical Termination of Pregnancy (Amendment) Act, 2021, up to what gestational age is the opinion of ONE registered medical practitioner (RMP) sufficient, and up to what gestational age is the opinion of TWO RMPs required for special categories of women?","o":["One RMP up to 20 weeks; Two RMPs between 20 and 24 weeks (with Medical Board review beyond 24 weeks only for substantial fetal abnormalities)","One RMP up to 28 weeks; no second opinion ever required","Two RMPs required even at 6 weeks gestation for all women","One RMP up to 12 weeks; Two RMPs between 12 and 20 weeks, absolute ban beyond 20 weeks"],"a":0,"e":"The MTP (Amendment) Act, 2021 expanded access in India: termination requires the opinion of 1 RMP up to 20 weeks (for all women, including failure of contraception for unmarried women as clarified by Supreme Court); 2 RMPs between 20 and 24 weeks for notified special categories (e.g., survivors of sexual assault, minors, change of marital status, physical/mental disability, fetal malformation); and State Medical Board approval beyond 24 weeks for substantial fetal abnormalities.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03384","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Infection Control Metrics","topic":"Device-Associated Infection Rate Calculation","q":"During a quarter, a hospital\u0027s medical ICU records 6 laboratory-confirmed Catheter-Associated Urinary Tract Infections (CAUTIs), 1,500 total urinary catheter-days, and 3,000 total ICU patient-days. What is the standardized CAUTI rate per 1,000 urinary catheter-days?","o":["2.0 per 1,000 urinary catheter-days","4.0 per 1,000 urinary catheter-days","0.4 per 1,000 urinary catheter-days","20.0 per 1,000 urinary catheter-days"],"a":1,"e":"Under CDC/NHSN and NABH quality indicator methodology, device-associated infection rates use device-days (not total patient-days) as the denominator to adjust for device exposure risk: CAUTI Rate = (Number of CAUTI events / Total urinary catheter-days) x 1,000 = (6 / 1,500) x 1,000 = 4.0 CAUTIs per 1,000 urinary catheter-days.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03385","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Financial Management","topic":"Break-Even Point Analysis","q":"A hospital plans to lease an automated biochemistry analyzer with a fixed monthly lease and maintenance cost of INR 1,50,000. Each diagnostic panel is priced at INR 500 and incurs a variable reagent/consumable cost of INR 200 per test. How many tests must the lab perform per month to reach the financial break-even point?","o":["214 tests per month","750 tests per month","500 tests per month","300 tests per month"],"a":2,"e":"Break-Even Volume = Fixed Costs / Contribution Margin per Unit, where Contribution Margin per test = Selling Price - Variable Cost = INR 500 - INR 200 = INR 300. Break-Even Volume = 1,50,000 / 300 = 500 tests per month. Performing more than 500 tests generates operating surplus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03386","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Architecture and Planning","topic":"ICU Bed Sizing, Space, and Nurse Staffing","q":"When planning a Level-III adult Intensive Care Unit in a 300-bed Indian tertiary hospital per ISCCM / NABH / IPHS standards, what are the recommended clear bed-space area per ICU bed and standard nurse-to-patient staffing ratio for mechanically ventilated patients?","o":["500 sq ft per bed minimum with no central monitoring; 2:1 doctor-to-bed only without nurses","100% of total hospital beds allocated to ICU at 1:10 nursing ratio","40 sq ft per bed arranged touching head-to-head; 1:15 nurse-to-patient ratio","Approximately 150-200 sq ft (minimum ~120-150 sq ft clear floor area) per bed; 1:1 nurse-to-patient ratio for ventilated patients"],"a":3,"e":"Indian Society of Critical Care Medicine (ISCCM) and NABH Level-III ICU guidelines recommend 150-200 sq ft clear space per ICU bed (to accommodate ventilators, dialysis machines, crash carts, and 360-degree bedside access) with single isolation rooms (\u003e=10-15% of ICU beds), and a 1:1 dedicated nurse-to-patient ratio per shift for unstable/ventilated patients (1:2 for step-down/non-ventilated).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03387","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Quality and Patient Safety","topic":"High-Alert and LASA Medications","q":"Under NABH Management of Medication (MOM) and WHO Patient Safety Solutions, which administrative control is mandatory to prevent fatal accidental IV push administration of concentrated Potassium Chloride (KCl) in general inpatient wards?","o":["Remove concentrated KCl vials from general ward floor stock (restricting storage to pharmacy or locked high-alert ICU/OT cabinets with bright warning labels) and require independent double-checking","Label KCl and Calcium Gluconate identically in plain white font to save printing costs","Store concentrated KCl ampoules next to 0.9% normal saline flush vials on every open nursing trolley","Permit verbal-only telephone orders for concentrated KCl boluses without read-back"],"a":0,"e":"Undiluted IV push of concentrated KCl causes instant fatal hyperkalemic cardiac arrest. NABH MOM and ISMP high-alert medication standards mandate eliminating concentrated electrolytes (KCl, \u003e0.9% NaCl) from general ward floor stock, using distinctive red/high-alert warning labels, Tall-Man lettering for LASA pairs, and mandatory independent two-person verification prior to dilution/infusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03388","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Disaster and Fire Safety","topic":"RACE, PASS, and Medical Gas Zone Valves","q":"A fire alarm (\u0027Code Red\u0027) triggers inside a 10-bed surgical ICU where three patients are on mechanical ventilators. Following the RACE fire protocol, what is the critical administrative and clinical safeguard before shutting off the wall-mounted medical oxygen zone valve for that ICU?","o":["Spray pressurized water directly onto energized electrical ICU ventilator panels","Rescue patients from immediate danger, sound alarm, confine smoke/doors, verify all ventilator-dependent patients are switched to portable oxygen cylinders / self-inflating bag-valve-masks (Ambu bags) first, then close the zone valve to prevent feeding the fire","Shut off the main hospital liquid oxygen tank instantly without warning any ICU staff","Leave all ICU fire doors propped open with wedges to clear smoke faster"],"a":1,"e":"Pressurized piped oxygen dramatically accelerates hospital fires, yet blindly closing an ICU/OT zone valve asphyxiates ventilated patients. Under NABH Facility Management \u0026 Safety (FMS) protocols, staff follow RACE (Rescue, Alarm, Confine by closing fire doors, Extinguish/Evacuate), transfer patients onto manual Ambu bags/portable cylinders prior to evacuation, and shut the designated zone valve under authorized incident command.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03389","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Human Resource Planning","topic":"Shift and Leave Relief Factor","q":"A hospital medical ward requires 4 staff nurses physically on duty during each of the three 8-hour shifts every day (365 days/year). If each full-time nurse works an average of 240 effective duty days per year (after accounting for weekly offs, public holidays, and earned/casual/sick leave), how many total Full-Time Equivalent (FTE) nurses must be sanctioned to cover this ward 24/7 without understaffing?","o":["12.0 FTEs (12 nurses sanctioned)","4.0 FTEs (4 nurses sanctioned)","18.25 FTEs (~19 nurses sanctioned)","28.0 FTEs (28 nurses sanctioned)"],"a":2,"e":"Total nurse-shifts required per year = 4 nurses/shift x 3 shifts/day x 365 days = 4,380 nurse-shifts/year. Dividing by 240 working shifts/days per nurse per year = 4,380 / 240 = 18.25 FTEs (rounded up to 19 sanctioned posts; equivalently, base 12 posts multiplied by a relief factor of 365/240 = 1.52).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03390","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Revenue Cycle and Health Insurance","topic":"Upcoding vs Unbundling in Package Billing","q":"During an internal billing compliance audit of PM-JAY and private TPA cashless claims, the hospital administrator discovers that a surgical unit billed a laparoscopic appendectomy package while separately charging additional itemized fees for routine pre-operative CBC, standard surgical gloves, and basic post-op ward bed charges that are already included in the fixed package rate. What billing error/practice is this?","o":["Upcoding to a higher-severity neurological craniotomy package","Capitation risk-sharing adjustment","Legitimate Value-Added DRG outlier reimbursement","Unbundling (fragmentation of an all-inclusive package rate into separate billable charges)"],"a":3,"e":"Under fixed-package schemes like Ayushman Bharat PM-JAY and TPA surgical packages, routine diagnostics, OT consumables, surgeon/anesthesia fees, and standard ward stay are bundled into one composite tariff. Splitting out constituent items to bill separately is \u0027unbundling\u0027, which triggers claim deductions, penalties, and potential de-empanelment (whereas \u0027upcoding\u0027 means miscoding a simple procedure as a more complex, higher-paying code).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03391","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Quality Improvement","topic":"Pareto Chart vs Fishbone Diagram","q":"A hospital quality team investigates prolonged inpatient discharge turnaround times (\u003e4 hours) across 500 discharges and finds that 78% of all delays stem from just two factors: delayed final pharmacy return billing and pending TPA insurance pre-authorization closure. Which quality tool visually ranks these vital few causes versus the trivial many to prioritize action?","o":["Pareto chart (applying the 80/20 rule with bars ordered by frequency and cumulative percentage line)","Run chart of daily room temperatures alone","Scatter plot of patient heights versus weights","Control p-chart of surgical site infections without cause ranking"],"a":0,"e":"A Pareto chart combines descending bar frequencies with a cumulative percentage curve to separate the \u0027vital few\u0027 root causes (~20% of categories driving ~80% of defects/delays) from the \u0027trivial many\u0027. Once prioritized via Pareto analysis, an Ishikawa (Fishbone) cause-and-effect diagram maps underlying 5M/4P root causes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03392","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Operations and Utilization Metrics","topic":"Bed Turnover Rate and Bed Turnover Interval","q":"A 100-bed surgical unit records 2,400 total inpatient service days and 400 discharges/deaths during a 30-day month (total available bed-days = 3,000). What are the unit\u0027s Bed Turnover Rate (BTR) and Bed Turnover Interval (BTI) for that month?","o":["BTR = 24.0 patients/bed/month; BTI = 6.0 days","BTR = 4.0 patients/bed/month; BTI = 1.5 days vacant between patients","BTR = 8.0 patients/bed/month; BTI = 3.0 days","BTR = 0.25 patients/bed/month; BTI = 0.5 days"],"a":1,"e":"Bed Turnover Rate (BTR) = Total discharges (including deaths) / Total available beds = 400 / 100 = 4.0 patients per bed during the 30-day month. Bed Turnover Interval (BTI) = (Total available bed-days - Actual inpatient service days) / Total discharges = (3,000 - 2,400) / 400 = 600 / 400 = 1.5 days (average vacant time per bed between consecutive admissions).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03393","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Supportive Services","topic":"Unidirectional Zoning and HVAC Pressure","q":"In planning a hospital Central Sterile Supply Department (CSSD) per NABH and infection control engineering norms, how should physical workflow and air-pressure gradients be arranged across the three primary functional zones?","o":["Positive pressure in the soiled decontamination wash room blowing air into sterile storage","Store sterile instrument sets directly inside the open receiving dock beside waste bins","Strictly unidirectional flow (Decontamination/Soiled receiving -\u003e Clean Assembly/Packing -\u003e Sterilization -\u003e Sterile Storage/Issue), with NEGATIVE pressure in Decontamination and POSITIVE pressure in Clean/Sterile Storage","Bidirectional shared corridor where soiled OT trolleys pass through the sterile pack issue counter"],"a":2,"e":"CSSD must enforce a strict one-way (\u0027dirty-to-clean\u0027) physical barrier workflow via pass-through washers and double-door autoclaves so soiled contaminated instruments never cross paths with sterilized packs. Air flows from cleanest to dirtiest: Sterile Storage/Packing maintains positive pressure relative to corridors, while the Decontamination washing area maintains negative pressure (~10 ACH) exhausting bioaerosols outdoors.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03394","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Information Systems (HIS)","topic":"ABHA, HFR, HPR, and Consent Architecture","q":"Under India\u0027s Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM) architecture managed by the National Health Authority (NHA), what are the three core foundational registries/identifiers used by an ABDM-compliant hospital HIS?","o":["ICD-9 paper ledgers, fax machines, and floppy disks","Only private hospital proprietary billing codes with zero patient consent","PAN card, GSTIN, and Passport number exclusively","ABHA (14-digit Ayushman Bharat Health Account for patients), HPR (Healthcare Professionals Registry for doctors/nurses), and HFR (Health Facility Registry for hospitals/clinics/labs)"],"a":3,"e":"ABDM establishes federated digital health interoperability across India using: (1) ABHA (14-digit unique patient health ID), (2) HFR (verified registry of public/private hospitals, clinics, pharmacies, and diagnostics), and (3) HPR (verified registry of modern \u0026 AYUSH doctors, nurses, and allied health professionals), exchanging FHIR-structured records only via explicit patient Consent Manager authorization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03395","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Equipment and Asset Management","topic":"AMC vs CMC and Equipment Uptime Calculation","q":"A tertiary hospital\u0027s 1.5T MRI scanner operates under a Comprehensive Maintenance Contract (CMC) specifying a 95% uptime SLA over a 30-day month (720 scheduled hours, 24x7 availability). During the month, the MRI experiences 54 hours of unplanned downtime due to helium cryocooler fault. What is the achieved uptime percentage, and what key financial difference distinguishes a CMC from an AMC?","o":["Uptime = 92.5% (breaches the 95% SLA); CMC covers BOTH preventive/corrective labor AND spare parts/consumables (except excluded items), whereas AMC covers labor/visits only with spares billed extra","Uptime = 97.5% (meets SLA); AMC includes free X-ray tubes and helium refills","Uptime = 75.0%; CMC covers only software antivirus updates","Uptime = 92.5%; CMC and AMC are legally identical with zero cost difference"],"a":0,"e":"Equipment Uptime (%) = [(Total Scheduled Hours - Downtime Hours) / Total Scheduled Hours] x 100 = [(720 - 54) / 720] x 100 = (666 / 720) x 100 = 92.5%, falling short of the 95% SLA (maximum allowed downtime = 5% of 720 = 36 hours), triggering SLA penalty/extension. An Annual Maintenance Contract (AMC) covers routine preventive visits and labor only, whereas a Comprehensive Maintenance Contract (CMC) bundles labor PLUS costly replacement spare parts.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03396","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Quality and Patient Safety","topic":"IPSG 1 \u0026 IPSG 2 Compliance","q":"Under NABH / International Patient Safety Goals (IPSG 1 and IPSG 2), which practices are strictly mandatory before administering blood products/medications and when receiving a critical laboratory serum potassium value of 6.9 mEq/L over the telephone?","o":["Verify the patient using at least TWO unique identifiers (e.g., Full Name + UHID/Date of Birth—NEVER room or bed number); write down and perform verbal \u0027read-back\u0027 verification of the critical lab value","Identify the patient solely by bed number (\u0027Bed 14\u0027) to save nursing time; accept telephone lab numbers from memory without writing down","Use room number + diagnosis as the two patient identifiers","Verify only the patient\u0027s first name (\u0027Mr. Sharma\u0027); rely on SMS text abbreviations"],"a":0,"e":"IPSG 1 mandates at least two person-specific identifiers (patient\u0027s full name plus hospital UHID number or date of birth) prior to any intervention, explicitly prohibiting room/bed numbers because patients frequently swap beds. IPSG 2 mandates \u0027write down and read back\u0027 (not merely \u0027repeat back\u0027 from memory) for verbal orders and critical lab panic values to eliminate transcription/hearing errors.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03397","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Materials and Inventory Management","topic":"FSN Analysis and FEFO Dispensing","q":"To minimize drug expiry write-offs and clear obsolete shelf inventory in a hospital central pharmacy store, which selective inventory technique categorizes stock by consumption velocity (Fast, Slow, Non-moving), and which issuing rule prioritizes batches by nearest expiry date?","o":["EOQ only; random shelf picking","FSN (Fast-moving, Slow-moving, Non-moving) analysis; FEFO (First Expired, First Out) issuing rule","PERT critical path; FIFO ignoring printed expiry dates","ABC-VED exclusively; LIFO (Last In, First Out) issuing rule"],"a":1,"e":"FSN analysis groups hospital items by movement/issue velocity (Fast, Slow, Non-moving) so administrators can identify stagnant N-category \u0027dead stock\u0027 early for supplier return, redistribution, or disposal. In pharmaceuticals and reagents, FEFO (First Expired, First Out) ensures batches with the earliest expiry date are dispensed first—superior to FIFO when a newer delivery happens to carry an older batch expiry.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03398","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Financial Management","topic":"Activity-Based Costing (ABC)","q":"Why does Activity-Based Costing (ABC) provide far more accurate departmental procedure pricing in a multi-specialty hospital than traditional volume-based overhead allocation (e.g., splitting CSSD, nursing, and utility overheads purely by bed count or revenue)?","o":["ABC ignores all indirect costs and prices surgeries strictly on suture cost","ABC eliminates the need for tracking physician or nurse labor time","ABC traces indirect support resources to specific cost-driving activities (such as CSSD instrument-tray sterilization cycles, OT minutes, or nursing acuity hours) actually consumed by each service, preventing cross-subsidization distortion","ABC charges identical overhead to an OPD eye exam and a 6-hour cardiac bypass"],"a":2,"e":"Hospitals carry high indirect overheads (CSSD, housekeeping, IT, biomedical engineering, nursing supervision). Simple bed-count or revenue ratios severely over-cost low-resource OPD clinics while under-costing resource-heavy robotic/cardiac OTs. Activity-Based Costing assigns indirect cost pools via true operational cost drivers (sterilization loads, nursing hours, square footage, equipment hours), yielding precise unit costs for PM-JAY/corporate package negotiation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03399","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Healthcare Law and Medico-Legal Administration","topic":"Brought-Dead Medico-Legal Case (MLC) Handling","q":"An unidentified 40-year-old man is brought dead to a hospital Emergency Department by bystanders following suspected unknown poisoning/assault. The family arrives 1 hour later demanding immediate release of the body without police involvement and requesting a standard death certificate. What is the legally mandatory hospital administrative protocol?","o":["Demand a cash deposit in exchange for skipping MLC registration","Hand over blood-stained clothes to the bystanders for washing before police arrive","Issue a routine Medical Certificate of Cause of Death (MCCD) listing \u0027cardiac arrest\u0027 and hand over the body immediately","Register as a Medico-Legal Case (MLC), issue immediate written Police Intimation to the nearest police station, preserve all clothing/personal items/samples cleanly in the mortuary, DO NOT issue a natural Cause-of-Death certificate, and release the body only after police inquest and post-mortem clearance"],"a":3,"e":"Under Indian criminal procedure (CrPC 174 / BNSS 194) and medico-legal administration rules, any brought-dead case without prior documented terminal medical care, or any suspected poisoning, trauma, suicide, homicide, or accident, is an obligatory MLC. Doctors must never guess/certify natural death in unnatural/unexplained brought-dead cases; the body and trace evidence must be preserved in cold mortuary custody pending police inquest and forensic autopsy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03400","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Quality and Haemovigilance","topic":"Acute Transfusion Reaction Protocol","q":"Ten minutes into a packed red blood cell (PRBC) transfusion on a surgical ward, the patient develops chills, fever, flank pain, hemoglobinuria, and hypotension (suspected acute hemolytic transfusion reaction). Under NABH Blood Bank \u0026 Clinical Safety SOPs, what must happen to the transfusion and the blood bag?","o":["STOP the transfusion immediately, keep IV access patent with 0.9% normal saline via NEW IV tubing (never flush remaining blood in the line!), perform immediate bedside clerical re-check, and send the clamped blood bag + attached tubing + fresh post-reaction EDTA/clot blood \u0026 urine samples to the Blood Bank for root-cause workup and Haemovigilance Programme of India (HvPI) reporting","Slow the blood drip rate by 50% and give IV paracetamol while finishing the bag","Flush the remaining 50 mL of blood in the IV tubing rapidly into the vein with saline","Throw the blood bag and tubing into the yellow biomedical waste bin immediately"],"a":0,"e":"Acute hemolytic transfusion reactions (often due to ABO clerical mismatch) carry high mortality proportional to volume infused. Stopping immediately, disconnecting the blood set (keeping the cannula open with fresh 0.9% NS tubing so incompatible blood inside the old tubing is NOT flushed into the patient), re-checking patient/bag tags, and returning the intact bag/set plus post-reaction blood/urine samples enables urgent DAT/crossmatch re-testing and mandatory HvPI reporting.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03401","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Healthcare Law and Ethics","topic":"THOTA (Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act)","q":"Under India\u0027s Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act (THOTA), 1994 (and 2011/2014 Rules) for deceased-donor organ retrieval in a registered ICU, how is Brainstem Death legally certified?","o":["By the recipient transplant surgeon alone immediately upon ICU admission","By a 4-member statutory Brainstem Death Certification Board (RMP in charge of the hospital, an independent specialist from the approved panel, a neurologist/neurosurgeon or approved intensivist/surgeon/anesthetist, and the treating medical officer—NONE belonging to the organ transplant/retrieval team) performing two apnea/reflex examinations at least 6 hours apart","Without any apnea test or family authorization","By a single surgical resident signing a verbal note once"],"a":1,"e":"THOTA strictly separates patient care/death certification from organ transplantation to eliminate conflict of interest. Form 10 Brainstem Death Certification requires a 4-member board (Hospital Medical Superintendent/RMP in charge, an independent panel specialist, a neurologist/neurosurgeon—or where unavailable, an approved surgeon/physician/intensivist/anesthetist—and the treating doctor, none linked to the transplant team) conducting two complete brainstem reflex + apnea tests separated by \u003e=6 hours (longer in young infants).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03402","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Operations Management","topic":"OT Utilization Rate and Turnover Bottlenecks","q":"An elective surgical suite with 4 operating rooms is scheduled for 8 hours per room per day (32 total staffed OT hours/day). Over a 25-working-day month (800 available staffed OT hours), actual recorded patient wheel-in-to-wheel-out surgical usage totals 600 hours, while 120 hours were lost to prolonged room cleaning/turnover delays and 80 hours to late first-case starts. What is the OT Utilization Rate?","o":["85%","90%","75% (600 / 800 x 100)","60%"],"a":2,"e":"OT Utilization Rate = (Total Actual Operative/Case Hours Utilized / Total Scheduled Staffed OT Hours Available) x 100 = (600 / 800) x 100 = 75%. Tracking First-Case On-Time Starts (FCOTS) and room turnover time (target \u003c=20-30 min via parallel cleaning/pre-op anesthesia holding) lifts utilization toward the optimal 80-85% benchmark without inducing staff burnout.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03403","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Engineering and Utility Safety","topic":"Redundancy and Emergency Gas Reserve","q":"Under NABH Facility Management \u0026 Safety (FMS) and HTM 02-01 / ISO 7396 medical gas standards, what redundancy architecture is mandatory for a tertiary hospital\u0027s central Medical Oxygen supply system to prevent catastrophic ICU/OT oxygen outages?","o":["Connecting compressed industrial welding oxygen directly into ICU wall outlets","A single unmonitored oxygen cylinder manifold with manual wrench changeover only","Turning off all pipeline low-pressure alarms at night to avoid disturbing patients","Three independent sources (Primary supply e.g., Liquid O2 VIE or PSA + Secondary automatic changeover cylinder manifold + Tertiary emergency reserve bank), plus area isolation zone valves and audio-visual low-pressure alarms at staffed nursing stations"],"a":3,"e":"Continuous oxygen delivery is life-critical. Standards require primary, secondary (auto-switching manifold on depletion), and tertiary emergency reserve supplies (sized for \u003e=24-48 hours of critical load), color-coded non-interchangeable NIST/DISS outlets, locked/accessible zone valves per ICU/OT wing, and redundant audio-visual line-pressure alarms monitored 24x7.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03404","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Laundry and Linen Services","topic":"Thermal Disinfection and Sluicing Prohibition","q":"Which hospital laundry management policy complies with MoHFW / NABH Infection Control guidelines for bedsheets visibly soiled with blood or feces from an isolation ward?","o":["Bag gently without shaking directly at bedside in leak-proof color-coded/soluble-alginate bags, do NOT sort or rinse (\u0027sluice\u0027) soiled linen manually in clinical wards, and wash via thermal disinfection (\u003e=71 degrees C for \u003e=25 minutes) or low-temp chemical sodium hypochlorite cycle","Rinse bloody sheets manually in the ward patient handwashing sink first","Send soiled linen in open wicker baskets alongside clean dietary food trolleys","Shake soiled sheets vigorously in the ward corridor to remove dried debris, then wash in cold 20 degrees C tap water without bleach"],"a":0,"e":"Shaking or manually rinsing/sluicing contaminated linen on clinical wards aerosolizes pathogens (C. difficile, MRSA, norovirus, HBV) and risks sharps injury from hidden needles left in sheets. Standard HIC mandates folding inward gently, bagging in leak-proof/water-soluble bags at point of use, dedicated soiled transport, and validated thermal (\u003e=71 C x 25 min, or \u003e=65 C x 10 min) or chemical hypochlorite disinfection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03405","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Quality and Patient Experience","topic":"Net Promoter Score (NPS) Calculation","q":"In a hospital\u0027s post-discharge patient experience survey asking \u0027How likely are you to recommend this hospital (0-10 scale)?\u0027 across 200 respondents: 120 patients score 9 or 10 (Promoters), 40 score 7 or 8 (Passives), and 40 score 0 to 6 (Detractors). What is the hospital\u0027s Net Promoter Score (NPS)?","o":["+80","+40 (60% Promoters minus 20% Detractors)","+60","-20"],"a":1,"e":"Net Promoter Score (NPS) ranges from -100 to +100 and is calculated as: NPS = % Promoters (scores 9-10) - % Detractors (scores 0-6), excluding Passives (scores 7-8) from the subtraction while keeping them in the denominator. Here: Promoters = 120/200 = 60%; Detractors = 40/200 = 20%; NPS = 60 - 20 = +40.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03406","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Healthcare Law and Medico-Legal Administration","topic":"LAMA / DAMA Protocol","q":"A conscious, oriented 52-year-old man admitted with acute unstable angina demands to leave the hospital immediately to attend a family event against the cardiologist\u0027s advice (LAMA / DAMA). What is the legally and ethically sound hospital administration protocol?","o":["Force him to sign a blank paper stating the hospital is not responsible for anything without explaining clinical risks","Refuse to hand over his ECG reports or discharge summary until he withdraws LAMA","Assess and confirm decision-making capacity, have the treating physician clearly explain specific life-threatening risks (e.g., acute MI, fatal arrhythmia), document informed refusal signed by the patient and an independent witness, provide a detailed discharge summary/medications for interim safety, and keep the door open for immediate return","Lock the ward doors physically and sedate the competent adult without consent to prevent departure"],"a":2,"e":"Under patient autonomy and NABH Patient \u0026 Family Rights (PRE) standards, a mentally competent adult has the legal right to refuse treatment (informed refusal / LAMA). Valid medico-legal protection requires documenting intact mental capacity, explicit disclosure of specific foreseeable clinical risks (death/MI), witnessed signed LAMA acknowledgment, and ethical harm-reduction (providing copies of records, stabilizing medications, and welcoming immediate readmission)—not punitive detention or withholding records.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03407","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Financial Management","topic":"Net Present Value (NPV) vs Payback Period","q":"When a hospital board evaluates an INR 8 crore capital investment to install a new Dual-Plane Cardiac Catheterization Lab with a 10-year useful life, why is Net Present Value (NPV) financially superior to the simple Payback Period method?","o":["NPV ignores the initial INR 8 crore capital purchase price completely","Payback Period accounts for inflation and cost of capital better than NPV","A negative NPV (-INR 1 crore) indicates a highly profitable investment","NPV discounts ALL expected future cash inflows over the full 10-year asset life using the hospital\u0027s cost of capital (accounting for the Time Value of Money), whereas Payback Period ignores cash flows occurring after payback and ignores discounting"],"a":3,"e":"Capital budgeting for high-value medical assets (Cath labs, Linear Accelerators, MRI) requires discounted cash flow analysis. NPV sums the present value of all multi-year net cash inflows minus initial outlay: NPV \u003e 0 (and IRR \u003e WACC/hurdle rate) creates financial value. Simple Payback Period measures only how fast nominal cash recovers initial cost—ignoring both the Time Value of Money and all profits/maintenance burdens occurring in later years.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03408","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Quality Assurance","topic":"The Five-Stage Clinical Audit Cycle","q":"A hospital\u0027s surgical quality committee measures baseline compliance with pre-incision antibiotic prophylaxis (\u003c60 min prior to incision) at 62%, conducts staff training and modifies the OT checklist, and improves compliance to 94%. What final critical step completes the classic Clinical Audit Cycle (\u0027closing the audit loop\u0027)?","o":["Re-audit (repeating data collection prospectively after an interval to verify sustained compliance and drive continuous improvement)","Stop all monitoring forever once a single training lecture is delivered","Convert the audit immediately into punitive salary deductions","Permanently delete the baseline 62% dataset so regulators never see it"],"a":0,"e":"A clinical audit is not a one-off survey; the closed-loop clinical audit cycle comprises: (1) Select topic/criteria, (2) Set explicit target standards, (3) Measure baseline performance, (4) Compare against standard and implement targeted system changes, and (5) Re-audit to confirm sustained improvement (\u0027closing the loop\u0027). Without re-auditing, behaviors frequently regress to baseline.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03409","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Operations Management","topic":"Wave vs Stream Appointment Scheduling","q":"An outpatient specialty clinic struggles with long queues when all 20 morning patients are told to arrive at 9:00 AM (\u0027open block\u0027), yet experiences doctor idle time under rigid individual 15-minute slots whenever a patient arrives late or no-shows. Which hybrid OPD scheduling system brings 3-4 patients at the start of each hour and leaves short catch-up buffers in the second half of each hour?","o":["Pure walk-in FIFO queue without any appointments","Wave (or Modified-Wave) appointment scheduling","Double-booking every 5-minute slot continuously for 8 hours","Annual single-day open-block scheduling"],"a":1,"e":"In Wave scheduling, a small group (e.g., 3-4 patients) is scheduled at the top of each hour and seen in arrival order, leaving the remainder of the hour as a natural buffer so late arrivals, complex consults, or occasional no-shows balance out—dramatically cutting waiting room crowding compared with 9:00 AM single-block dumping while protecting physician productivity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03410","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Infection Control Engineering","topic":"Airborne Infection Isolation Room (AIIR)","q":"When commissioning an Airborne Infection Isolation Room (AIIR) for patients with sputum-positive MDR-TB, measles, or varicella, which HVAC engineering parameters are mandatory under CDC / MoHFW / NABH standards?","o":["Positive pressure (+15 Pa) blowing room air outward into the general medical ward corridor","Zero mechanical ventilation with ceiling fans recirculating air through open corridor transoms","Negative pressure differential (\u003e= -2.5 Pa) relative to the adjacent corridor/anteroom (air flows INWARD under closed doors), \u003e= 12 Air Changes per Hour (ACH) for new construction, and 100% direct exhaust outdoors away from air intakes (or HEPA-filtered recirculation)","1 ACH once per day without pressure-differential monitoring"],"a":2,"e":"AIIRs contain infectious droplet nuclei (\u003c5 microns) by maintaining negative pressure (\u003e= -2.5 Pa; verified daily by magnehelic gauge or smoke-tube test when occupied), \u003e=12 ACH (or \u003e=6 ACH in older existing wards) to dilute/clear aerosols rapidly, self-closing doors, and dedicated 100% outdoor exhaust (or terminal HEPA filtration) well away from fresh-air intakes and walkways.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03411","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Financial Management","topic":"Accounts Receivable (AR) Days Calculation","q":"A multi-specialty hospital reports INR 73 crore in annual net credit patient revenue (from TPAs, PM-JAY, and corporate panels) and has an ending Accounts Receivable (AR) balance of INR 18 crore on its balance sheet. What is the hospital\u0027s average Accounts Receivable (AR) Days (Days in AR)?","o":["4.05 days","20 days","180 days","90 days [(18 / 73) x 365]"],"a":3,"e":"Days in Accounts Receivable (AR Days) measures average collection time after discharge: AR Days = (Ending Accounts Receivable / Annual Net Credit Revenue) x 365 = (18 / 73) x 365 = 18 x 5 = 90 days (since 365 / 73 = 5). High AR days (\u003e60-90 days) due to query backlogs or claim denials tie up cash needed for pharmacy vendors and staff salaries.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03412","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Healthcare Law and Medico-Legal Administration","topic":"Consumer Protection Act, 2019 Jurisdictions","q":"Under India\u0027s Consumer Protection Act, 2019 (and 2021 revised pecuniary jurisdiction rules) for paid healthcare medical-negligence complaints, how is pecuniary jurisdiction determined across the District, State, and National (NCDRC) Consumer Disputes Redressal Commissions?","o":["Based on the value of the \u0027consideration paid\u0027 for the service (NOT the inflated compensation claimed): District Commission up to INR 50 lakh; State Commission \u003eINR 50 lakh up to INR 2 crore; National Commission (NCDRC) \u003eINR 2 crore","Healthcare services are completely exempt from civil consumer courts even when fees are paid","Only criminal high courts hear hospital billing complaints","Based purely on arbitrary compensation demanded by the complainant up to INR 1,000 crore at District level"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike the older 1986 Act (which counted compensation claimed, letting litigants bypass District forums via inflated claims), the Consumer Protection Act, 2019 bases pecuniary jurisdiction on actual \u0027value of goods or services paid as consideration\u0027 (revised under December 2021 rules to: District \u003c= INR 50 lakh; State \u003e INR 50 lakh to INR 2 crore; National NCDRC \u003e INR 2 crore), while completely free government/charitable hospital care without any fee remains outside CPA \u0027service\u0027 definition (IMA v. V.P. Shantha).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03413","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Disaster and Mass Casualty Planning","topic":"Hospital Incident Command System (HICS) Surge Protocol","q":"A district highway train derailment sends 60 injured casualties to a 250-bed hospital within 30 minutes (\u0027Code Brown / Mass Casualty Disaster\u0027). As Incident Commander activating the Hospital Incident Command System (HICS), which immediate operational sequence prevents ER gridlock and optimizes survival?","o":["Lock the hospital main gate completely to turn away all disaster ambulances","Establish an external triage/decontamination station OUTSIDE the ER entrance to sort Red/Yellow/Green/Black casualties before entry, halt non-urgent elective OTs/procedures, mobilize reserve nursing/doctor pools, and immediately execute reverse-triage early discharge of stable ward patients to free surge beds","Let all 60 walking and critically injured casualties crowd uncontrolled directly into the 8-bed ER resuscitation bay together","Continue all routine elective cosmetic/cataract surgeries as normal"],"a":1,"e":"Effective hospital mass-casualty surge management under NDMA/NABH FMS relies on HICS clear command roles: stopping uncontrolled entry at an external outdoor triage post (routing Green minor injuries away from critical Red resuscitation bays), cancelling elective OTs to free surgical teams/recovery beds, and discharging clinically stable inpatients (\u0027reverse triage\u0027) to expand bed capacity by 15-30% within the first hour.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03414","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Infection Control Governance","topic":"SSI Care Bundle Compliance","q":"When auditing hospital compliance with the WHO / CDC Surgical Site Infection (SSI) Prevention Care Bundle, which pre-operative hair-removal and prophylactic antibiotic practices are strictly required?","o":["Administer prophylactic IV cefazolin 4 hours AFTER the surgical incision is closed","Use razor shaving in the ward 24 hours prior to maximize microscopic skin nicks","Leave hair intact unless interfering with the incision (if removal is needed, use electric clippers immediately before surgery—NEVER razor blades!); administer IV prophylactic antibiotic within 60 minutes before skin incision (120 min for vancomycin/fluoroquinolones)","Shave the surgical site dry with a twin-blade razor the night before surgery; start oral antibiotics 3 days after skin closure"],"a":2,"e":"Preoperative razor shaving creates microscopic epidermal abrasions that rapidly colonize with skin staphylococci, doubling SSI rates; WHO strongly recommends NO routine hair removal, or electric clipping just prior to surgery. Additionally, therapeutic tissue antibiotic levels at incision require IV dosing within 60 min pre-incision (redosing if surgery \u003e2 half-lives or massive blood loss), alcohol-chlorhexidine skin prep, normothermia, and glycemic control (\u003c180-200 mg/dL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03415","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Supportive \u0026 Diagnostic Operations","topic":"STAT Diagnostic Workflow Optimization","q":"A tertiary hospital\u0027s audit reveals that Emergency Department STAT troponin results take 95 minutes and stroke non-contrast head CT reports take 65 minutes (delaying STEMI/thrombolysis door-to-needle times). Which operational workflow redesign targets the pre-analytical and post-analytical bottlenecks most effectively?","o":["Batch all ER blood tubes into a single once-every-two-hours manual runner basket","Turn off electronic PACS/LIS interfaces and dictate reports on cassette tapes next morning","Require manual paper requisition stamps from three administrative desks before CT scanning","Install pneumatic tube sample transport (or ED point-of-care troponin), barcode-driven LIS auto-validation with automated critical-value SMS/screen alerts, and a dedicated \u0027Code Stroke\u0027 direct-to-CT ambulance bypass protocol"],"a":3,"e":"In diagnostic TAT (order-to-result), ~60-70% of delays occur in pre-analytical transport/registration and post-analytical transcription/reporting rather than analyzer spin time. Point-of-care troponin/pneumatic tube transport, LIS barcode auto-verification, and pre-notified \u0027direct-to-CT\u0027 Code Stroke pathways compress stroke door-to-CT (\u003c20-25 min) and troponin TAT (\u003c30-45 min) reliably.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03416","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Governance and Bioethics","topic":"Hospital Ethics Committee (HEC) vs Institutional Ethics Committee (IEC/IRB)","q":"In hospital governance in India, how does the statutory mandate of a Clinical Hospital Ethics Committee (HEC) differ from that of an Institutional Ethics Committee (IEC / IRB) registered under the New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules, 2019 (CDSCO / DHR)?","o":["The Clinical HEC provides multidisciplinary consultation on real-time bedside ethical dilemmas (e.g., end-of-life care, DNR/withdrawal of futile life support, surrogate disputes), whereas the IEC/IRB reviews, approves, and monitors biomedical/clinical research protocols to protect human research participants","The Clinical HEC exclusively approves pharmaceutical Phase III clinical trials for profit","The IEC/IRB manages hospital nursing shift rosters and canteen pricing","Both committees are legally prohibited from including non-medical lay/legal community members"],"a":0,"e":"Modern accredited hospitals separate clinical bedside ethics from human-subjects research governance: a Clinical HEC (clinicians, intensivists, ethicist, legal/social worker) mediates value conflicts around end-of-life care, advance directives (Common Cause Supreme Court guidelines), and resource allocation; an IEC/IRB (registered with DHR/CDSCO under NDCT Rules 2019, mandatory external lay/legal/philosopher quorum) safeguards research participants.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03417","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Pharmacy Administration","topic":"2-to-8 Degrees C Biologics \u0026 Vaccine Storage","q":"During a monthly NABH pharmacy safety audit of ward and central pharmacy refrigerators storing insulin, vaccines, and monoclonal antibodies (+2 to +8 degrees C), which non-conformities require immediate corrective action?","o":["Storing staff lunchboxes/water bottles alongside vials, placing temperature-sensitive vials in the refrigerator door shelves, lacking a calibrated continuous digital data logger/min-max thermometer, or lacking generator-backed power","Maintaining +2 to +8 degrees C with continuous min-max digital alarm logs and placing water bottles in the bottom crisper for thermal ballast","Connecting the pharmacy cold-chain refrigerator to an uninterrupted UPS/generator circuit","Keeping insulin vials on middle wire shelves away from freezer coils"],"a":0,"e":"Under NABH MOM (Management of Medication) and WHO cold-chain standards, medical refrigerators must store ONLY pharmaceuticals/specimens (never staff food/beverages, which increase door openings and contamination), avoid unstable door shelves (where temperatures swing widely), maintain middle-shelf air circulation away from freezing evaporator plates, log temperatures twice daily + continuous calibrated digital alarms, and connect to backup emergency power.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03418","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Finance and Reimbursement","topic":"Prospective DRG / Case-Rate Payment Incentives","q":"When an insurance payer or national health authority pays hospitals a fixed prospective Diagnosis-Related Group (DRG) or fixed surgical package tariff per admission (rather than retrospective itemized Fee-for-Service), what primary operational incentive and potential unintended clinical risk arise for hospital administrators?","o":["Guarantees unlimited hospital profit regardless of surgical complications or ICU readmissions","Strong financial incentive to improve clinical pathway efficiency, cut duplicate testing, and shorten excess Average Length of Stay (ALOS)—paired with the quality risk of premature (\u0027quicker-and-sicker\u0027) discharge or cherry-picking low-severity cases unless readmission/outcome audits are enforced","Strong incentive to keep every uncomplicated patient admitted for 45 days to maximize itemized bed charges","Eliminates all incentive for clinical coding accuracy or cost control"],"a":1,"e":"Under retrospective Fee-for-Service (FFS), hospitals earn more revenue by ordering extra tests and prolonging stays (supplier-induced demand). Prospective fixed DRG/package payment shifts financial risk onto the hospital: controlling ALOS, standardizing generics/consumables, and eliminating complications increases margin, whereas payers must monitor 30-day readmissions, mortality, and upcoding to prevent premature discharge or patient dumping.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03419","course":"MHA - Master of Hospital Administration","subject":"Hospital Planning and Universal Design","topic":"Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act 2016 \u0026 CPWD Norms","q":"When designing or auditing a hospital building entrance and corridors for universal accessibility under India\u0027s Harmonized Guidelines \u0026 Standards for Universal Accessibility (RPwD Act, 2016 / NABH AAC), what is the maximum gentle slope gradient permitted for standard wheelchair ramps (paired with bilateral handrails and non-slip landings)?","o":["Wheelchair ramps are optional if stairs have 20 steps","1:4 short steep ramp with polished glossy marble flooring","1:12 maximum gradient (1 unit vertical rise for every 12 units horizontal run, ~4.8 degrees; gentler 1:15 or 1:20 preferred where space permits)","1:2 steep concrete incline (26 degrees) without handrails"],"a":2,"e":"India\u0027s Harmonized Guidelines (CPWD/MoHUA) and NABH require barrier-free hospital access: wheelchair ramps must be no steeper than 1:12 gradient (with level rest landings every \u003c=9 m run, \u003e=1,200 mm clear width, raised edge curbs, and dual-height continuous handrails on both sides), alongside tactile guiding blocks, Braille lift panels, and accessible toilets with grab bars.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03420","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Epidemiology \u0026 Biostatistics","topic":"Cohort vs Case-Control Studies","q":"In a hospital-based case-control study of oral carcinoma, 80 of 100 cases had a history of tobacco chewing compared with 20 of 100 age-matched controls. What is the Odds Ratio (OR) for tobacco chewing associated with oral carcinoma?","o":["4.0","0.0625","8.0","16.0"],"a":3,"e":"Construct the 2x2 table: Cases exposed (a) = 80, Cases unexposed (c) = 20; Controls exposed (b) = 20, Controls unexposed (d) = 80. Odds Ratio (OR) = (a x d) / (b x c) = (80 x 80) / (20 x 20) = 6,400 / 400 = 16.0. Case-control studies estimate OR rather than direct incidence/Relative Risk.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03421","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Epidemiology and Study Design","topic":"Number Needed to Treat (NNT)","q":"In a community-based randomized trial of zinc supplementation for childhood diarrhea prevention, the 6-month incidence of severe diarrhea was 12% in the control group and 8% in the zinc intervention group. What are the Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR) and Number Needed to Treat (NNT)?","o":["ARR = 4%; NNT = 25","ARR = 20%; NNT = 5","ARR = 33.3%; NNT = 3","ARR = 4%; NNT = 4"],"a":0,"e":"Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR) = Risk in control group - Risk in intervention group = 12% - 8% = 4% (0.04). Number Needed to Treat (NNT) = 1 / ARR = 1 / 0.04 = 25 children treated over 6 months to prevent one episode of severe diarrhea.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03422","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Biostatistics","topic":"Confounding vs Effect Modification","q":"An occupational cohort study finds an overall crude Relative Risk (RR) of 2.8 between silica dust exposure and lung cancer. When stratified by smoking status, the stratum-specific RR is 1.4 among non-smokers and 5.6 among smokers (Breslow-Day test for homogeneity p = 0.004). What statistical phenomenon is demonstrated?","o":["Berkson\u0027s selection bias arising from hospital-based controls","Effect measure modification (interaction) by smoking status, requiring stratum-specific RRs to be reported separately","Pure confounding by smoking, requiring reporting of a single Mantel-Haenszel pooled RR","Recall bias caused by differential exposure misclassification"],"a":1,"e":"When stratum-specific measures of association differ substantially across strata of a third variable (1.4 in non-smokers vs 5.6 in smokers, confirmed by a significant homogeneity test p = 0.004), effect measure modification (biological interaction) is present. Pooling into a single Mantel-Haenszel summary RR obscures this synergy; stratum-specific estimates must be reported.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03423","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"National Health Programmes of India","topic":"Drug-Resistant TB Diagnostics and Regimens","q":"Under India\u0027s National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP) Universal Drug Susceptibility Testing (UDST) policy, what is the primary upfront molecular diagnostic test offered to presumptive TB patients at peripheral/district levels, and which newer all-oral shorter regimen was introduced in India for MDR/RR-TB?","o":["Sputum AFB culture on Lowenstein-Jensen medium alone; 24-month kanamycin-containing injectable regimen","Mantoux tuberculin skin test; 9-month isoniazid monotherapy","CBNAAT (GeneXpert) or Truenat for rapid M. tuberculosis and rifampicin resistance detection; 6-month BPaLM regimen (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin)","Chest X-ray alone without microbiological testing; streptomycin-ethambutol regimen"],"a":2,"e":"NTEP mandates Universal DST using rapid WHO-approved nucleic acid amplification tests (CBNAAT / Truenat) upfront to simultaneously detect MTB and rifampicin resistance within hours. India has also adopted the shorter, safer 6-month all-oral BPaLM regimen (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, with moxifloxacin for fluoroquinolone-susceptible MDR/RR-TB) replacing toxic injectable regimens.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03424","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Health Policy and Health Economics","topic":"Cost-Effectiveness vs Cost-Utility Analysis","q":"A state health department compares a new HPV vaccination strategy against current cervical cancer screening. Which economic evaluation method measures health outcomes in Quality-Adjusted Life Years (QALYs) or Disability-Adjusted Life Years (DALYs) averted and compares interventions using an Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio (ICER)?","o":["Cost-of-Illness (COI) descriptive burden study","Cost-Minimization Analysis (CMA)","Budget Impact Analysis (BIA) alone","Cost-Utility Analysis (CUA)"],"a":3,"e":"Cost-Utility Analysis (CUA) is a specialized form of cost-effectiveness analysis where outcomes are expressed in generic preference-weighted summary measures combining quantity and quality of life—QALYs gained or DALYs averted—allowing policy comparisons across diverse diseases using ICER (cost per QALY gained or DALY averted).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03425","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Health Systems and Financing","topic":"Ayushman Bharat Architecture","q":"Under India\u0027s Ayushman Bharat programme, what are the two complementary pillars, and what annual inpatient financial protection coverage per eligible family is provided under PM-JAY?","o":["Health and Wellness Centres (now Ayushman Arogya Mandirs) for comprehensive primary healthcare, and PM-JAY providing INR 5 lakh per family per year for secondary and tertiary hospitalization","Only urban tertiary medical colleges, providing INR 50,000 per individual per year","Private insurance vouchers exclusively for outpatient pharmacy medicines at INR 1 lakh per person","Standalone vertical malaria clinics with INR 2 lakh coverage per village"],"a":0,"e":"Ayushman Bharat comprises two synergistic pillars: (1) ~1.5+ lakh Health and Wellness Centres (Ayushman Arogya Mandirs) delivering Comprehensive Primary Health Care (CPHC) including NCDs, MCH, and free diagnostics/drugs; and (2) Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (PM-JAY), offering cashless secondary and tertiary inpatient care up to INR 5 lakh per family per year.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03426","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Biostatistics","topic":"Determinants of Sample Size","q":"When planning a cluster-randomized community nutrition trial, which change in statistical assumptions will DECREASE the required minimum sample size (assuming all other factors stay constant)?","o":["Increasing statistical power from 80% to 95%","Increasing the minimum clinically meaningful difference (effect size) that the study aims to detect","Accounting for a Design Effect (DEFF) of 2.0 due to intra-cluster correlation","Decreasing Type I error (alpha) from 0.05 to 0.01"],"a":1,"e":"Sample size is inversely proportional to the square of the detectable difference (delta^2): larger differences between groups are much easier to detect with fewer participants. Conversely, demanding higher power (lower beta), stricter alpha (0.01 vs 0.05), higher outcome variance, or multiplying by a cluster Design Effect (\u003e1) all INCREASE the required sample size.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03427","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Epidemiology and Screening","topic":"Lead-Time Bias vs Length-Time Bias","q":"A district introduces low-dose CT screening for lung cancer and observes that 5-year survival from diagnosis among screened patients rises from 20% to 45%, even though age-adjusted lung cancer mortality in the total population remains completely unchanged. Which bias best explains this spurious survival gain if screening merely detects tumors 2 years earlier without delaying death?","o":["Berkson\u0027s admission-rate bias","Neyman\u0027s prevalence-incidence bias","Lead-time bias","Hawthorne observer effect"],"a":2,"e":"Lead-time bias occurs when screening advances the time of diagnosis (the \u0027lead time\u0027) without actually altering the biological course or postponing the date of death. Measuring survival time from an earlier diagnosis date artificially inflates 5-year survival rates while true population disease-specific mortality remains unchanged.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03428","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Biostatistics","topic":"Kaplan-Meier and Cox Proportional Hazards","q":"In a 5-year longitudinal cohort analyzing time to diabetic nephropathy using Kaplan-Meier curves and Cox proportional hazards regression, what does the fundamental \u0027non-informative censoring\u0027 assumption mean?","o":["Censored participants are counted as having died on day 1 of the study","Every participant must develop diabetic nephropathy before the study ends","The hazard ratio between exposed and unexposed groups must change linearly every 6 months","Participants lost to follow-up or withdrawn at study end have the same future hazard of developing the outcome as those who remain under observation"],"a":3,"e":"Valid Kaplan-Meier survival estimates and Cox models rely on non-informative (independent) censoring: the reason a subject is censored (e.g., administrative end of study or moving away) must be unrelated to their underlying prognosis/risk of the event. If sick patients preferentially drop out (\u0027informative censoring\u0027), survival is overestimated.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03429","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Maternal and Child Health Indicators","topic":"Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR)","q":"In a state demographic survey over one calendar year, there are 500,000 total deliveries, 480,000 live births, 15,000,000 women of reproductive age (15-49 years), and 432 maternal deaths (deaths during pregnancy or within 42 days of termination of pregnancy from pregnancy-related causes). What is the Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR)?","o":["90 per 100,000 live births","90 per 1,000 live births","86.4 per 100,000 total deliveries","2.88 per 100,000 reproductive-age women"],"a":0,"e":"Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) = (Number of maternal deaths in a given period / Number of live births in the same period) x 100,000 = (432 / 480,000) x 100,000 = 90 maternal deaths per 100,000 live births. (By contrast, Maternal Mortality Rate divides by women of reproductive age 15-49 years).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03430","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Global Health Governance","topic":"PHEIC Declaration Criteria","q":"Under the WHO International Health Regulations (IHR 2005), within what timeframe must a Member State notify WHO via its National IHR Focal Point once an event is assessed as potentially constituting a Public Health Emergency of International Concern (PHEIC)?","o":["Within 7 days of laboratory confirmation","Within 24 hours of assessment","Only after 1,000 fatalities occur across three continents","Within 30 days via quarterly diplomatic bulletin"],"a":1,"e":"Under Article 6 and Annex 2 of the IHR (2005), States Parties must assess public health events within 48 hours and notify WHO within 24 hours of assessment of any event that may constitute a PHEIC (answering \u0027yes\u0027 to at least two of four Annex 2 criteria: serious public health impact, unusual/unexpected event, significant risk of international spread, or significant risk of international travel/trade restrictions; smallpox, wild polio, novel human influenza, and SARS are always notifiable).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03431","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Epidemiology and Study Design","topic":"Nested Case-Control Study","q":"A prospective cohort of 50,000 adults stored baseline frozen serum samples at enrollment. Ten years later, 300 participants have developed hepatocellular carcinoma. To assay expensive viral/metabolomic biomarkers from baseline serum without testing all 50,000 samples, investigators select all 300 incident cases and 1,200 risk-set matched controls from the cohort. What study design is this, and what key advantage does it retain over a traditional hospital case-control study?","o":["Cross-sectional prevalence survey; it directly measures point prevalence without follow-up","Uncontrolled case series; it yields incidence rates directly without controls","Nested case-control study (with incidence density sampling); it preserves temporal sequence using pre-diagnostic stored specimens and eliminates recall and selection biases","Ecological correlation study; it avoids individual-level biomarker measurement"],"a":2,"e":"A nested case-control study samples incident cases and risk-set (incidence-density) controls from within a well-defined prospective cohort. Because exposures/biomarkers were collected prospectively before disease onset, temporality is assured, recall bias is avoided, controls represent the exact source population, and assay costs drop dramatically (1,500 assays vs 50,000).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03432","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Qualitative and Mixed-Methods Research","topic":"Data Saturation and Triangulation","q":"When conducting in-depth interviews with ASHAs and PHC Medical Officers to explore barriers to maternal iron-folic acid adherence, how do qualitative researchers determine final sample size, and what is \u0027methodological triangulation\u0027?","o":["Interviewers stop after exactly 3 interviews regardless of emerging themes; triangulation means deleting discordant participant quotes","Qualitative research uses random digit dialing to achieve statistical p \u003c 0.05 generalizability","Sample size must always be \u003e=1,000 calculated by Cochran\u0027s formula; triangulation means running a three-way ANOVA","Sample size is determined by thematic/data saturation (when additional interviews yield no new codes or themes); triangulation combines multiple data sources or methods (e.g., interviews + focus groups + quantitative records) to enhance credibility"],"a":3,"e":"Purposive qualitative sampling continues iteratively until data/thematic saturation is reached—meaning successive interviews generate redundant information with no new conceptual dimensions. Triangulation strengthens validity/credibility (Lincoln \u0026 Guba trustworthiness criteria) by cross-verifying findings across multiple methods, investigators, or stakeholder perspectives.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03433","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Epidemiology and Environmental Health","topic":"Attributable Fraction Exposed (AFE) and PAR%","q":"In a cohort study examining chronic biomass cookstove smoke exposure among rural women, the incidence of COPD is 24 per 1,000 person-years in exposed biomass users versus 6 per 1,000 person-years in clean LPG users (Relative Risk = 4.0). Assuming causality, what is the Attributable Fraction among the Exposed (AFE)?","o":["75%","18%","300%","25%"],"a":0,"e":"Attributable Fraction among the Exposed (AFE, or Attributable Risk Percent) = [(RR - 1) / RR] x 100 = [(4.0 - 1) / 4.0] x 100 = (3 / 4) x 100 = 75% (equivalently [(24 - 6) / 24] x 100 = 75%). This means 75% of COPD cases occurring among biomass-exposed women could be prevented by transitioning to clean LPG cooking fuel.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03434","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Health Policy and Health Financing","topic":"Catastrophic Health Expenditure (CHE)","q":"Under Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) Indicator 3.8.2 defined by the WHO and World Bank, when is a household classified as experiencing Catastrophic Health Expenditure (CHE)?","o":["When household out-of-pocket (OOP) health spending exceeds 10% or 25% of total household consumption expenditure or income","When a household spends any amount above INR 100 on over-the-counter vitamins","When government tax revenue spent on public hospitals exceeds 5% of national GDP","Only when an individual purchases private voluntary health insurance"],"a":0,"e":"SDG Indicator 3.8.2 tracks the proportion of the population with large household out-of-pocket (OOP) expenditures on health as a share of total household expenditure or income using two standard thresholds: \u003e10% (moderate catastrophic threshold) and \u003e25% (severe catastrophic threshold). (WHO also historically uses \u003e=40% of household capacity-to-pay/non-subsistence spending).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03435","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Biostatistics","topic":"Logistic Regression and ROC AUC","q":"A multivariable logistic regression evaluating predictors of hospital 30-day readmission reports: Age \u003e65 yrs [aOR = 2.15, 95% CI: 1.38-3.35], Rural residence [aOR = 1.24, 95% CI: 0.88-1.75], and Model ROC Area Under the Curve (AUC) = 0.82. Which conclusion is statistically valid?","o":["Age \u003e65 is a statistically significant independent predictor of readmission, Rural residence is NOT statistically significant (95% CI includes 1.0), and the model shows good discrimination (AUC = 0.82)","Rural residence is statistically significant because aOR \u003e 1.0 regardless of its confidence interval","Both variables are non-significant because AUC is below 1.0","An AUC of 0.82 indicates the model performs no better than random coin-flip guessing"],"a":0,"e":"In ratio measures (OR, RR, HR), the null value of no association is 1.0. Because the 95% CI for Rural residence (0.88-1.75) spans 1.0, it is not statistically significant at alpha = 0.05, whereas Age \u003e65 (1.38-3.35) excludes 1.0 and is significant. An ROC AUC of 0.82 (on a 0.50 random-chance to 1.00 perfect scale) indicates good predictive discrimination.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03436","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Epidemiological Theory and Causal Inference","topic":"Bradford Hill Criteria for Causation","q":"Among Sir Austin Bradford Hill\u0027s nine classic viewpoints (criteria) for inferring causality from epidemiological associations, which single criterion is strictly MANDATORY (sine qua non) for a causal relationship?","o":["Biological gradient (strict linear dose-response in every threshold-free scenario)","Temporality (exposure must precede the onset of disease)","Specificity of one exposure to one single disease","Strength of association (RR \u003e 10)"],"a":1,"e":"Hill emphasized that his nine viewpoints (strength, consistency, specificity, temporality, biological gradient, plausibility, coherence, experiment, analogy) are aids to judgment rather than rigid rules—EXCEPT Temporality: an exposure cannot cause an outcome if the outcome occurred before the exposure. Note that weak associations can still be causal, and single exposures (like smoking) cause multiple diseases (violating strict specificity).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03437","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Demography and Population Health","topic":"Demographic Cycle Stages and Replacement Fertility","q":"According to classical demographic transition theory and India\u0027s National Family Health Survey (NFHS-5) metrics, what Total Fertility Rate (TFR) approximately corresponds to a Net Reproduction Rate (NRR) of 1.0 (replacement-level fertility), and which stage of the demographic cycle is characterized by declining birth rates alongside already low death rates?","o":["TFR ~1.0 child per woman; Stage 2 (Early Expanding stage)","TFR ~3.8 children per woman; Stage 5 (Declining stage)","TFR ~2.1 children per woman; Stage 3 (Late Expanding stage)","TFR ~5.0 children per woman; Stage 1 (High Stationary stage)"],"a":2,"e":"Replacement-level fertility (NRR = 1.0, meaning each woman on average replaces herself with one surviving daughter reaching reproductive age) corresponds to a TFR of ~2.1 births per woman (slightly above 2.0 to account for childhood mortality and sex ratio at birth). Stage 3 (Late Expanding) features falling birth rates and low death rates, producing slowing positive population growth (India\u0027s national TFR reached ~2.0 in NFHS-5).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03438","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Health Programme Planning and Evaluation","topic":"Inputs, Outputs, Outcomes, and Impact","q":"In evaluating a state-wide Maternal Anemia Elimination Programme using a Logical Framework (LogFrame) results chain, how are \u0027Number of IFA tablet strips distributed\u0027, \u0027Percentage of pregnant women completing \u003e=180 IFA tablets\u0027, and \u0027Reduction in maternal anemia mortality\u0027 classified respectively?","o":["Input -\u003e Process -\u003e Output","Outcome -\u003e Input -\u003e Process","Impact -\u003e Input -\u003e Output","Output -\u003e Outcome -\u003e Impact"],"a":3,"e":"In public health M\u0026E logic models: Inputs (budget, drugs, staff) -\u003e Activities/Processes (training, distribution) -\u003e Outputs (immediate tangible deliverables, e.g., IFA strips distributed) -\u003e Outcomes (intermediate behavioral/coverage changes in target population, e.g., \u003e=180-day adherence %) -\u003e Impact (long-term population health/mortality change, e.g., reduced anemia-related MMR).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03439","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"National Health Programmes of India","topic":"Lymphatic Filariasis Elimination","q":"Under India\u0027s accelerated Lymphatic Filariasis Elimination campaign (NCVBDC), which triple-drug Mass Drug Administration (IDA) regimen is administered annually in eligible non-co-endemic districts, and which population groups are EXCLUDED from Ivermectin/DEC/Albendazole MDA?","o":["Ivermectin + Diethylcarbamazine (DEC) + Albendazole (IDA); exclude pregnant women, severely ill persons, and children \u003c2 years (for DEC/Albendazole) or \u003c5 years / \u003c15 kg / \u003c90 cm height for Ivermectin","Chloroquine + Primaquine + Artesunate; exclude only adults \u003e60 years","Single-dose Streptomycin IM for neonates only","Praziquantel + Doxycycline monotherapy; administer to all pregnant women in first trimester"],"a":0,"e":"WHO/NCVBDC introduced the triple-drug IDA regimen (Ivermectin + DEC + Albendazole) to clear microfilaremia rapidly and reach Transmission Assessment Survey (TAS) interruption targets in fewer rounds. Standard exclusion criteria prohibit MDA in pregnant women, severely ill individuals, and young children (\u003c2 years for DA; \u003c5 years / \u003c90 cm height / \u003c15 kg for Ivermectin).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03440","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Biostatistics","topic":"Mann-Whitney U vs Independent Samples t-Test","q":"A public health researcher compares out-of-pocket hospital expenditure (INR) between insured (n = 45) and uninsured (n = 45) trauma patients. Histogram inspection reveals extreme right-skewness with several catastrophic ICU outliers (Shapiro-Wilk p \u003c 0.001). Which summary measures and hypothesis test are most appropriate?","o":["Delete all high-cost ICU outliers so the remaining sample looks normal","Report Median and Interquartile Range (IQR), and compare groups using the non-parametric Mann-Whitney U (Wilcoxon rank-sum) test","Report Mean and Standard Deviation only, and run a paired t-test","Convert expenditure to arbitrary pie charts and run Pearson\u0027s correlation"],"a":1,"e":"Healthcare costs and hospital length of stay are classic heavily right-skewed distributions where means and SDs are distorted by extreme outliers. Non-parametric testing via the Mann-Whitney U (Wilcoxon rank-sum) test compares rank distributions across two independent groups without normality assumptions, paired with Median and IQR.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03441","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Health Economics and HTA","topic":"Discounting and Sensitivity Analysis","q":"When conducting a multi-decade Health Technology Assessment (HTA) cost-effectiveness model of childhood Hepatitis B vaccination in India (where vaccine costs occur today while liver cirrhosis/HCC QALYs are averted 30 years later), why are future costs and future health outcomes discounted (commonly at 3% per annum), and how is parameter uncertainty tested?","o":["Sensitivity analysis deletes all model inputs whose p-value exceeds 0.05","Discounting inflates future costs by 50% per year to penalize preventive vaccines; uncertainty is ignored","Discounting reflects positive social time preference (present resources/health are valued more than identical future gains); uncertainty is evaluated via deterministic one-way (Tornado) and Probabilistic Sensitivity Analysis (Monte Carlo simulation / CEAC)","Discounting is applied only to hospital salaries and never to QALYs/DALYs"],"a":2,"e":"Standard HTA guidelines (WHO-CHOICE, HTAIn India, Second Panel on Cost-Effectiveness) apply equal annual discounting (typically 3% per year) to both future costs and future health benefits (QALYs/DALYs) to account for opportunity cost and time preference. Because model inputs (vaccine price, transition probabilities) carry uncertainty, one-way sensitivity analyses (Tornado diagrams) and Probabilistic Sensitivity Analysis (PSA) test ICER robustness.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03442","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Public Health Nutrition","topic":"WHO Child Growth Standards Criteria","q":"Under WHO and MoHFW India guidelines for children aged 6 to 59 months, which independent anthropometric or clinical criteria define Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM)?","o":["MUAC between 135 mm and 150 mm with normal weight","Height-for-Age z-score (HAZ) \u003c -1 SD alone without wasting","Birth weight 2.8 kg at term delivery","Weight-for-Height/Length z-score (WHZ) \u003c -3 SD of WHO median, OR Mid-Upper Arm Circumference (MUAC) \u003c 115 mm (11.5 cm), OR bilateral nutritional pitting edema"],"a":3,"e":"Any ONE of three independent criteria diagnoses SAM in 6-59-month-old children: (1) Weight-for-Height/Length \u003c -3 SD (severe wasting), (2) MUAC \u003c 115 mm (11.5 cm, red zone on Shakir tape), or (3) bilateral pitting pedal edema (kwashiorkor/marasmic-kwashiorkor). Children with SAM plus medical complications or failed appetite test require inpatient Nutrition Rehabilitation Centre (NRC) stabilization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03443","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"National Health Programmes of India","topic":"Population-Based NCD Screening","q":"Under India\u0027s National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD) operationalized through Ayushman Arogya Mandirs (HWCs), at what minimum age does universal population-based screening via the Community Based Assessment Checklist (CBAC) begin, and which primary screening method is used for cervical cancer at HWCs/PHCs?","o":["Age \u003e=30 years for hypertension, diabetes, and oral/breast/cervical cancers; Visual Inspection with Acetic Acid (VIA) every 5 years for women aged 30-65 years","Age \u003e=65 years only; routine annual pelvic MRI for all women","Age \u003e=10 years; routine coronary angiography in all villages","Age \u003e=50 years; PSA blood testing exclusively"],"a":0,"e":"Under NP-NCD / CPHC Population-Based Screening (PBS) guidelines, ASHAs enumerate individuals \u003e=30 years using the CBAC risk score (\u003e4 high risk) and mobilize them to HWCs/Sub-centres for BP, random blood glucose, oral visual examination, clinical breast exam, and Visual Inspection with 3-5% Acetic Acid (VIA) every 5 years for cervical cancer screening in women aged 30-65 years.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03444","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Field Epidemiology","topic":"Point-Source vs Propagated Epi Curves","q":"When plotting onset times of cases during an outbreak investigation, the epidemiologist observes an abrupt, sharp upward spike in cases followed by a rapid skewed-right tail, with ALL cases falling within a single minimum-to-maximum incubation period and no secondary wave of illness. What transmission pattern does this epidemic curve indicate?","o":["Propagated person-to-person epidemic (e.g., measles or influenza)","Common point-source epidemic (e.g., single contaminated meal or water exposure event)","Seasonal cyclic secular trend over 20 years","Continuous endemic steady-state prevalence without an outbreak"],"a":1,"e":"A classic point-source epidemic curve exhibits a steep upslope, a tight unimodal peak, and a slightly slower downslope with all cases clustered inside the range of a single incubation period (allowing back-calculation of the exact exposure time using median incubation subtracted from peak onset). By contrast, propagated outbreaks show progressively taller successive peaks separated by one serial/incubation interval.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03445","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Public Health Ethics and Governance","topic":"Belmont Report and ICMR Ethical Guidelines","q":"A researcher proposes recruiting indigent rural tribal prisoners exclusively for a risky Phase I drug trial whose benefits will only be affordable to urban private-sector patients. Which core ethical principle of the Belmont Report and ICMR National Ethical Guidelines is MOST directly violated by targeting vulnerable populations to bear risks without equitable access to benefits?","o":["Retrospective ecological fallacy","Equivalence of statistical power","Justice (fair distribution of research burdens and benefits)","Non-inferiority margin calibration"],"a":2,"e":"The Belmont Report defines three foundational principles: (1) Respect for Persons (informed consent + protecting diminished autonomy), (2) Beneficence (do no harm; maximize benefits, minimize risks), and (3) Justice (equitable selection of subjects so disadvantaged or incarcerated groups do not disproportionately bear research risks for therapies benefiting only privileged populations).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03446","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Biostatistics","topic":"Bonferroni Correction for Family-Wise Error","q":"After a significant one-way ANOVA comparing systolic blood pressure across 5 dietary intervention arms, a public health biostatistician runs all 10 possible pairwise post-hoc t-tests while wanting to maintain an overall family-wise Type I error rate (alpha) of 0.05. Applying the classic Bonferroni correction (alpha_adjusted = alpha / k), what p-value threshold must each individual comparison meet to be declared statistically significant?","o":["p \u003c 0.50","p \u003c 0.01 (0.05 / 5)","p \u003c 0.05","p \u003c 0.005 (0.05 / 10)"],"a":3,"e":"Running k = 10 independent comparisons at unadjusted alpha = 0.05 inflates the family-wise probability of at least one false positive to 1 - (0.95)^10 = 40.1%! The Bonferroni adjustment divides overall alpha by the exact number of comparisons performed (k = 10 pairwise tests among 5 groups: 5 x 4 / 2 = 10), yielding an adjusted per-test alpha threshold of 0.05 / 10 = 0.005.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03447","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Public Health Surveillance Evaluation","topic":"Positive Predictive Value (PVP) and Prevalence","q":"A state malaria surveillance system uses a rapid diagnostic test (RDT) with fixed Sensitivity = 95% and Specificity = 98%. After successful vector control slashes true malaria prevalence among febrile clinic attendees from 20% down to 0.5%, what happens to the Positive Predictive Value (PVP) of a positive test result?","o":["PVP drops dramatically (from ~92% down to ~19%), meaning most positive RDT results in the low-prevalence elimination phase are false positives requiring confirmatory microscopy/PCR","PVP rises automatically to 99.9% as prevalence falls","PVP remains completely constant regardless of disease prevalence","Sensitivity and Specificity also drop linearly to 0.5%"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike Sensitivity and Specificity (intrinsic test properties), Positive Predictive Value depends heavily on pre-test disease prevalence. At 20% prevalence (1000 tested: 190 TP, 16 FP -\u003e PVP = 190/206 = 92.2%), whereas at 0.5% prevalence (1000 tested: 4.75 TP, 19.9 FP -\u003e PVP = 4.75/24.65 = 19.3%). Thus, during disease elimination, screening programs generate high proportions of false positives and must confirm positives.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03448","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Vaccine-Preventable Disease Surveillance","topic":"Acute Flaccid Paralysis (AFP) Surveillance Standards","q":"Under WHO / India NPSP (National Polio Surveillance Project) guidelines, when any child aged \u003c15 years presents with Acute Flaccid Paralysis (AFP), what stool specimen collection protocol and annual district-level Non-Polio AFP sensitivity benchmark confirm adequate surveillance?","o":["Collect a single blood serum sample 60 days after paralysis onset; target rate 0 per 100,000","Collect TWO adequate stool specimens (8-10 g each) at least 24-48 hours apart within 14 days of paralysis onset, shipped in reverse cold chain (+2 to +8 degrees C); target non-polio AFP rate \u003e= 2 per 100,000 children \u003c15 years (minimum WHO \u003e=1/100,000)","Investigate only children with bilateral lower-limb spasticity","Collect one CSF sample frozen at -80 degrees C; wait 6 months for 60-day follow-up"],"a":1,"e":"Poliovirus shedding in stool is intermittent and peaks in the first 2 weeks after paralysis onset. Therefore, two stool specimens (\u003e=8 g, 24-48 hrs apart, within 14 days of onset) must reach a WHO-accredited lab in reverse cold chain (+2 to +8 C) within 72 hours. Because Guillain-Barre syndrome and other causes naturally occur at ~1-2 cases per 100,000 children \u003c15 years annually, a non-polio AFP rate \u003e=2/100,000 in endemic/high-risk regions (WHO global \u003e=1/100,000) proves the surveillance net isn\u0027t missing cases.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03449","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Health Promotion and Behavioral Theory","topic":"Health Belief Model vs Stages of Change","q":"A tobacco cessation counselor interviews a smoker who states: \u0027I know smoking increases my heart attack risk and I am thinking about quitting sometime in the next 6 months, though I haven\u0027t set a quit date yet.\u0027 According to Prochaska and DiClemente\u0027s Transtheoretical Model (Stages of Change), which stage is this person in, and what counseling strategy fits best?","o":["Precontemplation stage (completely unaware or unwilling to consider quitting)","Action stage (actively smoke-free for the past 3 months)","Contemplation stage (ambivalent, considering change within 6 months); use motivational interviewing exploring pros/cons and building self-efficacy rather than prematurely imposing rigid action checklists","Maintenance stage (smoke-free \u003e6 months)"],"a":2,"e":"The Transtheoretical Model defines five progressive stages: Precontemplation (no intention in next 6 months), Contemplation (intending change within 6 months while weighing pros and cons), Preparation (planning within 30 days, taking small steps), Action (overtly modified behavior \u003c6 months), and Maintenance (\u003e6 months sustained change). Matching counseling to the Contemplation stage via non-judgmental decisional-balance motivational interviewing resolves ambivalence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03450","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Infectious Disease Policy and One Health","topic":"WHO AWaRe Antibiotic Classification","q":"Under the WHO AWaRe (Access, Watch, Reserve) classification adopted in India\u0027s National Action Plan on Antimicrobial Resistance (NAP-AMR), how are antibiotics like Amoxicillin/Cotrimoxazole versus Ceftriaxone/Meropenem versus Colistin/Linezolid categorized, and what is the WHO country-level consumption target for the Access group?","o":["Watch drugs are for unrestricted over-the-counter veterinary growth promotion","AWaRe classifies only antiviral drugs used in HIV programmes","Colistin = Access; Amoxicillin = Reserve; target \u003e= 90% consumption from Reserve group","Amoxicillin/Cotrimoxazole = Access; Ceftriaxone/Meropenem = Watch; Colistin/Linezolid = Reserve; target \u003e= 60% of total national antibiotic consumption from the Access group"],"a":3,"e":"WHO\u0027s AWaRe tool categorizes antibiotics by resistance potential: Access (first/second-line narrow-spectrum agents like amoxicillin, ampicillin, cotrimoxazole, doxycycline—target \u003e=60% of national use), Watch (broader-spectrum higher-resistance-potential agents like 3rd-gen cephalosporins, fluoroquinolones, macrolides, carbapenems requiring strict stewardship), and Reserve (last-resort salvage agents like colistin, polymyxin B, linezolid, ceftazidime-avibactam for MDR/XDR infections).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03451","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Demography and Population Health","topic":"Total Dependency Ratio Calculation","q":"A district census reports 300,000 children aged 0-14 years, 600,000 working-age adults aged 15-64 years, and 100,000 older adults aged \u003e=65 years (total population = 1,000,000). What is the district\u0027s Total Demographic Dependency Ratio?","o":["66.7 per 100 working-age population (400,000 dependents / 600,000 working-age x 100)","16.7 per 100 working-age population","40.0 per 100 total population","150.0 per 100 working-age population"],"a":0,"e":"Total Dependency Ratio = [(Population aged 0-14 + Population aged \u003e=65) / Population aged 15-64] x 100 = [(300,000 + 100,000) / 600,000] x 100 = (400,000 / 600,000) x 100 = 66.7 dependents per 100 working-age persons (Youth dependency = 50.0; Old-age dependency = 16.7).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03452","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Biostatistics","topic":"Proportional Hazards Assumption","q":"When fitting a multivariable Cox proportional hazards regression model comparing tuberculosis mortality between HIV-coinfected and HIV-negative patients over 36 months, what does the \u0027Proportional Hazards\u0027 assumption require, and how is it checked?","o":["Every participant must die at the exact same median survival day","The Hazard Ratio comparing exposed vs unexposed groups must remain constant over follow-up time (checked via parallel log-minus-log survival plots or Schoenfeld residuals test)","The baseline hazard h0(t) must be strictly linear and constant like a flat horizontal line","Exposed and unexposed Kaplan-Meier survival curves must cross each other repeatedly"],"a":1,"e":"Cox regression is semi-parametric—meaning it makes NO parametric shape assumption about the underlying baseline hazard h0(t) over time, EXCEPT that the ratio of hazards between covariate strata (Hazard Ratio = exp(beta)) remains proportional (constant) across time. Crossing log-minus-log curves or significant Schoenfeld residual time-correlation indicates non-proportionality, requiring stratified Cox or time-varying covariates.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03453","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Epidemiology and Study Design","topic":"Ecological Fallacy","q":"A state public health report correlates aggregate district data and finds that districts with higher per-capita fast-food restaurant density also have lower mean infant mortality rates, concluding that eating fast food protects individual infants from dying. What epidemiological error is being committed?","o":["Reverse Hawthorne observer effect","Berkson\u0027s hospital admission bias","Ecological fallacy (falsely inferring individual-level biological causation from group/aggregate-level correlations confounded by district wealth and healthcare access)","Lead-time bias from early screening"],"a":2,"e":"Ecological studies use populations/districts as the unit of observation rather than individuals. Because aggregate correlations ignore within-group distribution and strong group-level confounding (e.g., wealthier urbanized districts have both more fast-food outlets and better NICU/obstetric care lowering infant mortality), extrapolating group averages to individual causal risk commits the Ecological Fallacy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03454","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"National Health Programmes of India","topic":"IMNCI Color-Coded Triage System","q":"Under India\u0027s Integrated Management of Neonatal and Childhood Illness (IMNCI) color-coded case-management charts for children 0-5 years, what clinical action do the PINK, YELLOW, and GREEN classifications signify respectively?","o":["Pink: Home care only; Yellow: Immediate ICU surgery; Green: Urgent hospital referral","Pink: Normal healthy child; Yellow: Dead on arrival; Green: Outbreak quarantine","All three colors mandate identical IV ceftriaxone inpatient admission","Pink: Urgent pre-referral treatment and immediate referral/inpatient admission; Yellow: Specific outpatient medical treatment (oral antibiotic, antimalarial, ORS/zinc) at clinic; Green: Safe home care counseling only without antimicrobials"],"a":3,"e":"IMNCI standardizes peripheral health-worker triage using intuitive color bands: PINK signals severe disease/danger signs needing immediate first-dose pre-referral stabilization and urgent hospital referral; YELLOW denotes non-severe illness requiring targeted outpatient therapy (e.g., oral amoxicillin for pneumonia, Plan B ORS) and follow-up; GREEN indicates mild self-limiting conditions (cough/cold, no dehydration) managed with home fluids, feeding, and danger-sign counseling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03455","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Environmental Epidemiology","topic":"PM2.5 Fine Particulate Matter Pathophysiology","q":"Why does ambient fine particulate matter \u003c= 2.5 micrometers in aerodynamic diameter (PM2.5) drive far greater global cardiopulmonary and stroke mortality than coarse dust particles \u003e 10 micrometers?","o":["PM2.5 bypasses upper nasopharyngeal filtration and mucociliary clearance to deposit deep within alveoli, triggering systemic endothelial inflammation, oxidative stress, prothrombotic cascades, and translocation into systemic circulation","PM2.5 causes only benign seasonal allergic sneezing without cardiovascular effects","Coarse \u003e10 micron dust penetrates the brain directly through intact scalp skin","PM2.5 is filtered 100% by nose hairs and never reaches the trachea"],"a":0,"e":"Particles \u003e10 microns impact in the nose/pharynx and clear rapidly via sneezing/mucociliary escalator. Fine PM2.5 (and ultrafine PM0.1) from vehicular exhaust, coal/crop burning, and biomass smoke reaches terminal respiratory bronchioles and alveoli—inducing IL-6/TNF-alpha systemic inflammation, autonomic imbalance, accelerated atherosclerosis, ischemic heart disease, stroke, COPD, and lung cancer.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03456","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Health Systems and Quality Management","topic":"Donabedian Triad \u0026 WHO Six Building Blocks","q":"When evaluating maternal care quality across District Hospitals using Avedis Donabedian\u0027s classic framework, how are \u002724x7 availability of blood storage unit and magnesium sulfate\u0027, \u0027percentage of preeclamptic mothers whose BP is monitored hourly\u0027, and \u0027case-fatality rate from eclampsia\u0027 categorized respectively?","o":["Process -\u003e Outcome -\u003e Structure","Structure -\u003e Process -\u003e Outcome","Financing -\u003e Governance -\u003e Input","Outcome -\u003e Structure -\u003e Process"],"a":1,"e":"Donabedian\u0027s quality triad evaluates: (1) Structure (physical infrastructure, equipment, drugs, staffing, accreditation), (2) Process (what clinicians actually do in delivering care—adherence to clinical guidelines, hand hygiene, BP monitoring), and (3) Outcome (end impact on patient health status, mortality, morbidity, and satisfaction).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03457","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Public Health Nutrition","topic":"NIDDCP Universal Salt Iodization","q":"Under India\u0027s National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP) and FSSAI mandatory food fortification standards, what minimum iodine concentration is legally required in iodized edible salt at the production/manufacturing level versus the retail/household consumer level?","o":["Iodization is strictly voluntary only in coastal states","At least 1 ppm at production and 0.1 ppm at household level","At least 30 ppm at manufacturing/production level and at least 15 ppm at retail/consumer household level","At least 500 ppm at production and 250 ppm at household level"],"a":2,"e":"Because potassium iodate degrades partially during humid transport, storage, and open-sack retail handling, Indian FSSAI/NIDDCP regulations mandate \u003e=30 parts per million (ppm) iodine at the point of manufacture so that \u003e=15 ppm remains intact at the distribution/household consumer level (tested rapidly in field surveys with MBI starch-iodate spot test kits, alongside urinary iodine monitoring targeting median 100-199 mcg/L).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03458","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Biostatistics","topic":"Pearson Chi-Square vs Fisher\u0027s Exact Test","q":"When analyzing a 2x2 contingency table comparing adverse vaccine reactions between two small cohorts (n = 28 total), two of the four cells have EXPECTED counts of 2.1 and 3.4 (though one observed cell is 5). Which test should the biostatistician use instead of the standard Pearson Chi-Square test?","o":["Bland-Altman limits of agreement plot","Pearson correlation coefficient r","One-way repeated-measures ANOVA","Fisher\u0027s Exact Test (because \u003e20% of cells have expected frequency \u003c 5, violating the asymptotic chi-square approximation)"],"a":3,"e":"Pearson\u0027s Chi-square test relies on a large-sample chi-square approximation that breaks down when expected cell frequencies are too small (Cochran\u0027s rule: no cell \u003c1 expected, and \u003c=20% of cells \u003c5 expected—so in any 2x2 table, ANY single cell with expected count \u003c5 violates the 20% rule!). Fisher\u0027s Exact Test computes exact hypergeometric probabilities valid for small cell counts.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03459","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Epidemiology and Vital Statistics","topic":"Direct vs Indirect Standardization \u0026 SMR","q":"An occupational cohort of 5,000 chemical plant workers experiences 45 observed cancer deaths over 10 years. Applying national age-specific reference cancer mortality rates to the cohort\u0027s age-person-year distribution yields 30.0 expected deaths. What is the Standardized Mortality Ratio (SMR), and why was Indirect Standardization used here?","o":["SMR = 150 (Observed 45 / Expected 30 x 100, indicating 50% excess cancer mortality); Indirect standardization is preferred when age-specific rates in the small study cohort are unstable or unknown","SMR = 66.7 (30 / 45 x 100); Direct standardization was used","SMR = 1.5 per 100,000 live births","SMR = 15.0 (45 - 30); Direct standardization never requires age-specific rates"],"a":0,"e":"Indirect Age Standardization applies stable age-specific rates from a large reference population to the study population\u0027s age structure to compute Expected deaths, yielding the Standardized Mortality Ratio: SMR = (Observed Deaths / Expected Deaths) x 100 = (45 / 30) x 100 = 150 (or ratio 1.50, meaning 50% higher mortality than expected). Indirect standardization avoids unstable small-cell age rates (whereas Direct standardization applies study age-specific rates to a standard reference population).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03460","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"National Health Programmes of India","topic":"Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK)","q":"Under India\u0027s Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK) implemented across public health institutions, what cashless entitlements are guaranteed to all pregnant women AND sick infants?","o":["A one-time INR 500 reimbursement paid only after 5 years","Completely free normal and cesarean deliveries, free drugs/consumables, free diagnostics/blood transfusion, free inpatient diet (up to 3/7 days), and free transport (home-to-facility, inter-facility referral, and drop-back home) for all pregnant women and sick infants up to 1 year of age","Covers only male newborns up to 7 days of life","Only BPL mothers receive free vaginal delivery; C-sections and sick neonates pay full user charges"],"a":1,"e":"Launched in 2011 under NHM (and later expanded from 30 days to 1 year for sick infants plus antenatal/postnatal complications), JSSK eliminates out-of-pocket expenditure (OOPE) barriers to institutional delivery and neonatal/infant care across all government facilities regardless of income/BPL status—complementing JSY conditional cash incentives and LaQshya labor-room quality standards.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03461","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Public Health Programme Evaluation","topic":"RE-AIM Evaluation Framework","q":"When scaling a pilot CHW-led hypertension screening intervention across 200 Primary Health Centres using Glasgow\u0027s RE-AIM evaluation framework, what do \u0027Adoption\u0027 and \u0027Implementation\u0027 specifically measure (distinct from patient-level \u0027Reach\u0027 and \u0027Effectiveness\u0027)?","o":["Adoption measures the proportion and representativeness of settings/PHCs and staff willing to initiate the programme; Implementation measures fidelity to protocol, adaptations, and delivery cost at the organizational/provider level","Adoption measures biological systolic blood pressure drop in mmHg; Implementation measures total district population","Both measure solely patient drug adherence at 5 years","Adoption measures legal child custody; Implementation measures hospital building construction"],"a":0,"e":"RE-AIM balances internal and external validity across five dimensions: Reach (individual patient participation/representativeness), Effectiveness (individual clinical outcomes/QoL), Adoption (organizational/setting \u0026 staff uptake), Implementation (intervention fidelity, consistency, adaptations, and cost during delivery), and Maintenance (long-term sustainability at both individual and institutional levels \u003e=6-12 months).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03462","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Demography and Reproductive Health","topic":"Pearl Index Calculation","q":"In a community family-planning cohort evaluating a new progestin-only contraceptive pill, 200 women are followed continuously for 12 months (totaling 2,400 woman-months of exposure), during which 6 accidental pregnancies occur. Using the Pearl Index [(Accidental Pregnancies x 1,200) / Total Woman-Months of Exposure], what is the failure rate per 100 woman-years (HWY)?","o":["6.0 per 100 woman-years","30.0 per 100 woman-years","3.0 per 100 woman-years","0.3 per 100 woman-years"],"a":2,"e":"The Pearl Index expresses contraceptive failure rate per 100 woman-years (HWY, i.e., 1,200 woman-months): Pearl Index = (Number of accidental pregnancies x 1,200) / Total woman-months of exposure = (6 x 1,200) / 2,400 = 7,200 / 2,400 = 3.0 pregnancies per 100 woman-years (note: life-table analysis avoids duration-dependent Pearl Index bias when follow-up lengths vary).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03463","course":"MPH - Master of Public Health","subject":"Climate Change and Public Health","topic":"Municipal Heat Action Plans (HAPs)","q":"Based on India Meteorological Department (IMD) and NDMA Heatwave Action Plan guidelines (pioneered in Ahmedabad), when is a \u0027Heatwave\u0027 declared in the plains versus coastal stations, and which evidence-based public health interventions cut heat-stroke mortality?","o":["Heatstroke mortality cannot be reduced by early warnings or work-hour shifts","Plains: \u003e= 25 degrees C; interventions include banning drinking water during afternoon labor","Heat Action Plans rely exclusively on distributing heavy wool blankets","Plains: max temperature \u003e= 40 degrees C (or departure \u003e= +4.5 degrees C from normal; severe \u003e= +6.5 degrees C); Coastal: \u003e= 37 degrees C with \u003e= +4.5 degrees C departure; interventions include early color-coded alerts, cool-roof painting, shaded drinking-water kiosks, shifted outdoor labor hours, and hospital heat-stroke ice-immersion readiness"],"a":3,"e":"IMD declares a heatwave when maximum temperature reaches \u003e=40 C in plains (\u003e=37 C coastal, \u003e=30 C hilly regions) AND departs +4.5 to +6.4 C above normal (severe heatwave \u003e=+6.5 C or actual temp \u003e=47 C). Municipal Heat Action Plans (HAPs) save thousands of lives via early interagency weather alerts, halting outdoor construction during peak 12-4 PM sun, cool-roof reflective coatings, ORS/water stations, and cold-immersion ER corners.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03464","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Advanced Biomechanics and Kinesiology","topic":"Patellofemoral Joint Reaction Force","q":"When rehabilitating a patient with patellofemoral pain syndrome (PFPS), how does the patellofemoral joint reaction force (PFJRF) and contact stress behave during a closed-chain squat from 0 to 90 degrees of knee flexion compared with an open-chain seated knee extension from 90 to 0 degrees?","o":["In closed-chain squatting, PFJRF increases progressively toward 90 degrees of flexion; in open-chain extension, PFJRF peaks near terminal extension (30-0 degrees) with higher contact stress due to smaller patellar contact area","In closed-chain squatting, PFJRF is highest at 0 degrees full extension; in open-chain extension, PFJRF is zero between 30 and 0 degrees","Both open- and closed-chain exercises produce identical PFJRF at 45 degrees and 0 degrees of flexion","Open-chain knee extension from 30 to 0 degrees minimizes patellofemoral contact stress because quadriceps moment arm is maximal"],"a":0,"e":"During closed-chain squatting, the external flexion moment increases as knee flexion increases toward 90 degrees, demanding greater quadriceps force and raising PFJRF, though patellar contact area also expands. Conversely, in open-chain resisted knee extension with a distal ankle weight, the external torque peaks near full extension (30-0 degrees) where patellar contact area is minimal, producing dangerously high contact stress in PFPS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03465","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Neurological Physiotherapy","topic":"Proprioceptive Neuromuscular Facilitation (PNF)","q":"A post-stroke hemiparetic patient has painful limitation of right shoulder external rotation due to tightness and hypertonicity of the subscapularis and pectoralis major, with pain on isotonic movement. Which PNF relaxation technique is specifically indicated when active isotonic contraction against resistance is painful?","o":["Hold-relax (using an isometric contraction of the tight antagonist followed by relaxation and passive/active stretch)","Rhythmic initiation progressing to slow reversal","Contract-relax (requiring full isotonic rotation against manual resistance)","Repeated stretch from the lengthened range"],"a":0,"e":"Hold-relax utilizes an isometric contraction of the restricted antagonist (internal rotators) at the end of the pain-free range without joint motion, invoking autogenic inhibition (Golgi tendon organ mediated) and post-isometric relaxation. Because no joint movement occurs during contraction, it is preferred over contract-relax (which uses isotonic rotation) when joint motion causes pain.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03466","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Physiotherapy","topic":"Early Mobilization Safety Criteria","q":"A cardiorespiratory physiotherapist evaluates four mechanically ventilated ICU patients for out-of-bed dangling and ambulation. Which patient is currently UNSAFE for active out-of-bed mobilization and requires postponement?","o":["Patient with stable tracheostomy on T-piece, MAP 78 mmHg off vasopressors, and hemoglobin 9.5 g/dL","Patient on FiO2 0.80, PEEP 14 cm H2O, escalating norepinephrine infusion, and SpO2 86%","Patient post-CABG day 2 with stable sinus rhythm, MAP 82 mmHg, and FiO2 0.40","Patient on pressure support 8 cm H2O, PEEP 5 cm H2O, FiO2 0.35, resting HR 88/min, and RASS 0"],"a":1,"e":"International ICU early mobilization consensus criteria (Hodgson et al.) consider high ventilatory support (FiO2 \u003e0.60, PEEP \u003e10 cm H2O), hypoxemia (SpO2 \u003c88-90%), and escalating vasopressor doses (\u0027red light\u0027 hemodynamic/respiratory instability) contraindications to active out-of-bed mobilization due to risk of severe desaturation, arrhythmias, or cardiac arrest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03467","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Electrodiagnosis and Electrophysiology","topic":"Rheobase and Chronaxie","q":"When plotting a Strength-Duration (SD) curve on the tibialis anterior muscle 4 weeks after a common peroneal nerve injury, the physiotherapist records a rheobase of 8 mA and a chronaxie of 15 milliseconds with a steep curve shifted upward and to the right, lacking any kink. What does this SD curve indicate?","o":["Normal innervation of the tibialis anterior","Purely upper motor neuron lesion (spastic paralysis)","Complete denervation (pure muscle fiber response)","Advanced nerve regeneration with mixed innervated and denervated fibers"],"a":2,"e":"Normal innervated skeletal muscle has a chronaxie \u003c1 ms (typically 0.1-0.5 ms) and a flat SD curve down to short pulse durations. Complete denervation causes the curve to shift upward and right with a markedly prolonged chronaxie (\u003e1-10 ms, here 15 ms) and no kink, reflecting direct stimulation of slower-responding denervated muscle fibers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03468","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Physiotherapy Research Methodology","topic":"Minimally Clinically Important Difference (MCID)","q":"A large RCT (n = 400) comparing dry needling plus exercise versus exercise alone for chronic low back pain reports a statistically significant between-group difference of 4 points on the Oswestry Disability Index (ODI) (95% CI: 1.5 to 6.5, p = 0.002). Given that the established Minimal Clinically Important Difference (MCID) for ODI is 10 points, how should a postgraduate physiotherapist interpret this finding?","o":["The difference is statistically significant due to large sample size, but it is NOT clinically meaningful because both the point estimate and 95% CI fall below the MCID of 10 points","The intervention should be adopted as mandatory first-line care solely because p \u003c 0.01","The study is underpowered and demonstrates a Type II (beta) error","An MCID of 10 points proves that the ODI lacks construct validity"],"a":0,"e":"With large sample sizes, trivial differences can achieve statistical significance (p \u003c 0.05). Clinical decision-making requires comparing the mean effect (4 points) and its 95% confidence interval (1.5-6.5) against the patient-relevant MCID (10 points). Because even the upper bound (6.5) is below 10, the added intervention does not yield a clinically worthwhile improvement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03469","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Manual Therapy and Arthrokinematics","topic":"Glenohumeral Joint Mobilization","q":"A patient with adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder) has severely restricted active and passive shoulder abduction (limited to 65 degrees) and external rotation. Applying Kaltenborn\u0027s convex-concave rule at the glenohumeral joint, which joint mobilization glide direction should the physiotherapist apply to the humeral head to restore abduction?","o":["Superior (cranial) glide of the humeral head toward the acromion","Anterior glide exclusively to restore pure abduction","Medial distraction only without directional translation","Inferior (caudal) glide of the convex humeral head on the concave glenoid fossa"],"a":3,"e":"According to Kaltenborn\u0027s convex-concave rule, when a convex surface (humeral head) moves on a stable concave surface (glenoid fossa), rolling and gliding occur in OPPOSITE directions. During shoulder abduction, the humerus rolls superiorly while the humeral head must glide inferiorly (caudally) in the glenoid to prevent subacromial impingement. Thus, inferior glide mobilization restores abduction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03470","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Clinical Gait Analysis","topic":"Gluteus Medius Weakness (Trendelenburg Gait)","q":"A patient with right superior gluteal nerve palsy (weak right gluteus medius) walks without an assistive device. During right midstance, instead of allowing the left pelvis to drop, the patient leans his trunk laterally toward the right side. What is the biomechanical purpose of this compensated Trendelenburg lean?","o":["It shifts the body\u0027s center of mass laterally over the right hip joint center, reducing the external adduction moment arm and the torque required from the weak right hip abductors","It shifts weight onto the left swinging leg to accelerate swing phase","It increases right knee hyperextension moment during terminal swing","It lengthens the external moment arm of gravity to stimulate right hip adductor spasticity"],"a":0,"e":"During right single-limb stance, gravity acting at the body\u0027s center of mass (medial to the right hip) creates a large external hip adduction torque that normally requires ~2x body weight force from the right gluteus medius (working at a ~2:1 lever disadvantage). Leaning the trunk over the stance (right) hip moves the gravitational line of action closer to the hip axis, shrinking the external moment arm and unloading the paralyzed abductors.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03471","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Electrodiagnosis and Electrophysiology","topic":"H-Reflex vs F-Wave","q":"A 45-year-old patient presents with left sciatica, numbness along the lateral foot, and an absent left Achilles tendon reflex. On tibial nerve electrodiagnostic testing to soleus/gastrocnemius, which late response provides the electrophysiological correlate of the S1 reflex arc and shows prolonged latency or absence on the affected side?","o":["F-wave, which tests purely proximal motor axon antidromic conduction without a spinal synapse","H-reflex (Hoffmann reflex), which traverses Ia sensory afferents, the S1 spinal synapse, and alpha motor efferents","Blink reflex (R1/R2) mediated via trigeminal and facial nerves","A-wave (axon reflex) generated by ephaptic cross-talk at the ankle"],"a":1,"e":"The H-reflex is elicited by submaximal electrical stimulation of large Ia muscle spindle afferent fibers in the tibial nerve at the popliteal fossa, traveling orthodromically to synapse monosynaptically on alpha motor neurons in the S1 spinal segment and returning down motor fibers to the soleus. A \u003e1.5-2.0 ms side-to-side delay or absent H-reflex strongly supports S1 radiculopathy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03472","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Physiotherapy","topic":"Six-Minute Walk Test (6MWT)","q":"When administering a standardized 6-Minute Walk Test (6MWT) per ATS/ERS guidelines to evaluate a COPD patient\u0027s response to an 8-week pulmonary rehabilitation program, what corridor length, coaching rule, and minimal clinically important difference (MCID) apply?","o":["Circular 5-meter track; measure only after 24 hours of bedrest; MCID 1 meter","Treadmill walking at fixed 4 km/hr speed; MCID 200 meters","Flat 30-meter indoor corridor; use standardized neutral encouragement every 1 minute without walking alongside to pace the patient; MCID ~25-30 meters","10-meter sloped ramp; run beside the patient shouting continuously; MCID 5 meters"],"a":2,"e":"ATS/ERS technical standards specify a flat, straight, hard 30-meter indoor corridor marked every 3 meters with turnaround cones, standardized scripted instructions and neutral time-cue encouragement every minute (never walking alongside or physically pacing), and an established MCID of approximately 25-30 meters (historically 30 m in COPD) to signify meaningful functional gain.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03473","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Physiotherapy Research Methodology","topic":"Likelihood Ratios (LR+ and LR-)","q":"A clinical diagnostic study evaluates the Lachman test for anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) tear and finds Sensitivity = 85% (0.85) and Specificity = 95% (0.95). What is the Positive Likelihood Ratio (LR+) of the Lachman test?","o":["LR+ = 0.16","LR+ = 1.70","LR+ = 5.67","LR+ = 17.0"],"a":3,"e":"Positive Likelihood Ratio (LR+) = Sensitivity / (1 - Specificity) = 0.85 / (1 - 0.95) = 0.85 / 0.05 = 17.0. Because LR+ \u003e10 produces a large, conclusive shift from pre-test to post-test probability, a positive Lachman test strongly rules in an ACL tear (whereas LR- = (1 - 0.85) / 0.95 = 0.16 strongly helps rule it out).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03474","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Spinal Biomechanics","topic":"Nachemson Intradiscal Pressure Studies","q":"Based on Nachemson\u0027s classic in-vivo L3 intradiscal pressure studies (and Wilke\u0027s modern telemetry confirmations), which daily posture generates the HIGHEST lumbar intradiscal compressive load (relative to relaxed upright standing = 100%)?","o":["Slouched forward-flexed sitting (or sitting while bending forward to lift a 20 kg load)","Supine lying with knees slightly flexed","Relaxed standing upright","Prone lying on a flat plinth"],"a":0,"e":"In Nachemson\u0027s measurements, compared with upright standing (100%), supine lying drops L3 intradiscal pressure to ~25%, unsupported upright sitting rises to ~140%, slouched forward-flexed sitting rises to ~185%, and flexing forward while seated with weights exceeds ~275%. Loss of lumbar lordosis shifts compressive load anteriorly onto the nucleus pulposus and tensions the posterior annulus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03475","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Tissue Mechanics and Exercise Physiology","topic":"Heavy Eccentric and Heavy Slow Resistance Loading","q":"A 38-year-old marathon runner presents with chronic midportion Achilles tendinopathy (6 months duration, morning stiffness, thickened tender tendon 4 cm proximal to the calcaneus). Why does progressive heavy eccentric calf loading (Alfredson protocol) or heavy slow resistance training promote recovery more effectively than prolonged complete rest?","o":["Ice massage and ultrasound alone restore tendon stiffness without mechanical strain","Controlled high-magnitude tensile loading stimulates tenocyte mechanotransduction, upregulates aligned Type I collagen synthesis, and reduces pathological neovascularization/nociception","Eccentric loading tears all remaining collagen fibers to trigger acute inflammatory necrosis","Complete immobilization doubles tendon cross-sectional collagen cross-linking"],"a":1,"e":"Tendons adapt via mechanotransduction: progressive high-load tensile strain (such as Alfredson heavy eccentric heel drops or heavy slow resistance) signals tenocytes to synthesize organized Type I collagen, improves fibrillar stiffness and load tolerance, and obliterates aberrant neovessels and accompanying ingrown nociceptive nerves.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03476","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Vestibular Rehabilitation","topic":"Posterior Canal Canalithiasis","q":"During a right-sided Dix-Hallpike maneuver on a 56-year-old woman with brief episodes of vertigo when rolling over in bed, the physiotherapist observes a 5-second latency followed by fatigable upbeating torsional nystagmus (top pole beating toward the right downward ear) lasting 20 seconds. Which diagnosis and treatment maneuver are indicated?","o":["Central brainstem cerebellar stroke; perform immediate cervical high-velocity thrust manipulation","Left horizontal canal cupulolithiasis; perform Brandt-Daroff habituation exclusively without repositioning","Right posterior semicircular canal BPPV (canalithiasis); perform the right Epley canalith repositioning maneuver","Bilateral vestibular neuritis; prescribe permanent strict bedrest and soft cervical collar"],"a":2,"e":"A positive Dix-Hallpike test showing brief latency (2-10 s), crescendo-decrescendo vertigo (\u003c60 s), fatigability, and geotropic upbeating torsional nystagmus toward the dependent (right) ear confirms right posterior semicircular canal canalithiasis. The Epley canalith repositioning maneuver sequentially rotates the head to migrate free-floating otoconia out of the posterior canal back into the utricle.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03477","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Physiotherapy","topic":"Active Cycle of Breathing Techniques (ACBT)","q":"When teaching the Active Cycle of Breathing Techniques (ACBT) to a patient with cystic fibrosis or severe bronchiectasis, why is the Forced Expiration Technique (\u0027huffing\u0027 with an open glottis) intercalated with periods of relaxed Breathing Control and Thoracic Expansion Exercises rather than repetitive vigorous coughing?","o":["ACBT eliminates the need for collateral ventilation channels (pores of Kohn)","Huffing requires closing the vocal cords tightly to maximize airway collapse","Thoracic expansion exercises intentionally restrict inspiratory volume below residual volume","Huffing generates lower peak intrathoracic pressures than coughing (preventing premature dynamic airway collapse/bronchospasm) while moving secretions proximally via equal pressure point physiology, and breathing control prevents desaturation"],"a":3,"e":"ACBT cycles through (1) Breathing Control (relaxed diaphragmatic tidal breathing to prevent bronchospasm/fatigue), (2) Thoracic Expansion Exercises (deep inspirations with 3-s hold to recruit collateral ventilation via pores of Kohn and channels of Lambert behind mucus), and (3) Forced Expiration Technique (1-2 open-glottis huffs at low-to-mid then high lung volumes). Open-glottis huffing avoids the extreme compressive pressures of closed-glottis coughing that collapse floppy bronchiectatic airways.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03478","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Physiotherapy Research Methodology","topic":"Intraclass Correlation Coefficient (ICC) vs Pearson r","q":"Two physiotherapists measure knee flexion ROM with a goniometer across 30 patients. Therapist B consistently records exactly 10 degrees higher than Therapist A on every single patient (r = 1.00). Why is Pearson\u0027s correlation coefficient (r) inappropriate as the sole reliability statistic here, and which index must be reported instead?","o":["Pearson\u0027s r measures only linear association and misses systematic bias (r = 1.00 despite a constant 10-degree disagreement); Intraclass Correlation Coefficient (ICC, two-way absolute agreement) and Bland-Altman limits of agreement should be used","Chi-square goodness-of-fit test is the gold standard for inter-rater goniometry","Cohen\u0027s kappa coefficient should be used because knee ROM is a continuous ratio variable","Pearson\u0027s r detects systematic bias perfectly, whereas ICC ignores systematic error"],"a":0,"e":"Pearson\u0027s r is invariant to linear shifts and scaling: if Rater B adds a constant +10 degrees to every reading, r equals +1.00 even though the raters never agree! Reliability of continuous measurements requires Intraclass Correlation Coefficient (specifically ICC(2,1) absolute agreement for inter-rater generalizability) paired with Standard Error of Measurement (SEM) and Bland-Altman plots.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03479","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Muscle Mechanics and Kinesiology","topic":"Active vs Passive Insufficiency","q":"Why is maximal hand-grip strength significantly greater when the wrist is held in ~20-35 degrees of extension than when the wrist is flexed to 60 degrees?","o":["Wrist extension completely paralyzes the extensor digitorum via tenodesis","Wrist extension prevents active insufficiency of the multi-joint extrinsic finger flexors (FDP/FDS) by maintaining optimal sarcomere length-tension overlap while avoiding passive tension of the wrist/finger extensors","Grip force depends exclusively on intrinsic lumbricals, independent of wrist position","Wrist flexion places the finger flexors in maximal passive stretch to increase cross-bridge cycling"],"a":1,"e":"Extrinsic finger flexors (FDP, FDS) cross both the wrist and finger joints. Flexing the wrist simultaneously with flexing the fingers shortens these multi-joint muscles excessively beyond optimal actin-myosin overlap (\u0027active insufficiency\u0027) while stretching the antagonistic extensor digitorum (\u0027passive insufficiency\u0027), slashing grip force. Slight wrist extension (~20-35 deg) restores optimal flexor sarcomere length-tension.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03480","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Neurological Physiotherapy","topic":"Constraint-Induced Movement Therapy (CIMT)","q":"A patient 5 months post-ischemic stroke demonstrates 20 degrees of active wrist extension and 10 degrees of active finger/thumb extension in the paretic hand, yet relies almost exclusively on the unaffected hand for daily tasks (\u0027learned non-use\u0027). Which rehabilitation intervention specifically targets cortical reorganization and learned non-use?","o":["Immobilizing the paretic arm in a tight shoulder sling for 24 hours/day","Passive continuous stretching alone without active task practice","Constraint-Induced Movement Therapy (CIMT): restraining the unaffected upper limb for up to 90% of waking hours paired with intensive, repetitive task-oriented shaping of the paretic limb","Restricting therapy strictly to the unaffected arm to maximize compensation"],"a":2,"e":"Taub\u0027s Constraint-Induced Movement Therapy (CIMT) targets \u0027learned non-use\u0027 in stroke survivors meeting minimum motor criteria (typically \u003e=10-20 deg active wrist extension and \u003e=10 deg MCP/IP finger extension, intact balance/cognition). Restraining the intact hand via a padded mitt while delivering massed, graded functional shaping tasks drives use-dependent contralateral/perilesional motor cortex reorganization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03481","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Physiotherapy","topic":"Karvonen Heart Rate Reserve (HRR) Method","q":"A 54-year-old post-MI patient enrolled in Phase II cardiac rehabilitation has a resting heart rate (HRrest) of 60 beats/min and achieved a symptom-limited peak heart rate (HRpeak) of 140 beats/min on a graded exercise test while taking his prescribed beta-blocker. Using the Karvonen Heart Rate Reserve (HRR) method, what is his target training heart rate at 60% intensity?","o":["84 beats/min","100 beats/min","132 beats/min","108 beats/min"],"a":3,"e":"Karvonen Target Heart Rate (THR) = [(HRmax/peak - HRrest) x %Intensity] + HRrest. Here, HRR = 140 - 60 = 80 beats/min. At 60% intensity: THR = (80 x 0.60) + 60 = 48 + 60 = 108 beats/min. Using actual symptom-limited peak HR from testing (rather than age-predicted 220 - age) is vital when patients take beta-blockers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03482","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Spinal Cord Injury Rehabilitation","topic":"Autonomic Dysreflexia","q":"During mat transfer training, a patient with chronic C6 complete tetraplegia suddenly develops a pounding throbbing headache, facial flushing, profuse sweating above the nipple line, bradycardia (48/min), and BP surging from 100/64 to 204/116 mmHg. What is the FIRST immediate physical action by the physiotherapist?","o":["Sit the patient upright immediately (90 degrees with legs lowered), loosen tight clothing/abdominal binder/leg straps, and inspect/unkink the Foley urinary catheter for bladder distension","Lay the patient flat in Trendelenburg head-down position immediately","Perform vigorous lower-limb passive stretching to reduce spasticity","Continue gait training while waiting 2 hours to see if headache resolves"],"a":0,"e":"Autonomic dysreflexia (in SCI at or above T6) is a hypertensive emergency caused by uninhibited sympathetic reflex vasoconstriction triggered by a noxious stimulus below the lesion (most commonly bladder distension/blocked catheter or fecal impaction), accompanied by compensatory vagal bradycardia and sweating above the lesion. Immediate sitting upright uses orthostasis to pool blood in the lower limbs and lower cerebral BP while removing constrictive garments and relieving the bladder/bowel trigger.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03483","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Physiotherapy Research Methodology","topic":"Forest Plot and Heterogeneity (I-Squared)","q":"In a systematic review and meta-analysis pooling 10 randomized trials of shockwave therapy for plantar fasciitis, the forest plot reports Cochran\u0027s Q p \u003c 0.001 and I-squared (I2) = 84%. What does I2 = 84% signify, and how should the meta-analysis be modeled and interpreted?","o":["84% of participants dropped out of the included RCTs","Substantial/considerable heterogeneity across trials (84% of total variation across studies reflects true between-study differences rather than random chance); use a random-effects model and investigate clinical/methodological sources via subgroup or sensitivity analysis","The pooled effect size is 84% clinically effective","Zero heterogeneity across studies, proving all 10 trials used identical protocols"],"a":1,"e":"Higgins\u0027 I-squared (I2) quantifies the percentage of total variance across studies in a meta-analysis attributable to genuine heterogeneity rather than sampling error (0-40% low, 30-60% moderate, 50-90% substantial, 75-100% considerable). At I2 = 84%, assuming a single common true effect (fixed-effect model) is inappropriate; a random-effects model and exploration of heterogeneity (dosage, follow-up, risk of bias) are required.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03484","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Biomechanics and Kinesiology","topic":"Third-Class Levers in Human Locomotion","q":"Most human limb muscles (such as the biceps brachii flexing the elbow against a dumbbell held in the hand) operate as Class III levers. What is the mechanical advantage (MA) of a Class III lever, and what functional advantage does this anatomical arrangement provide?","o":["Mechanical advantage \u003e 10, allowing the biceps to lift 100 kg using only 5 kg of muscle tension","Fulcrum lies at the fingertips while load sits at the elbow joint (Class II)","Mechanical advantage \u003c 1 (effort lies between fulcrum and load, requiring muscle force greater than load weight), which maximizes angular velocity and distal range of motion of the hand","Effort and load sit on opposite sides of the fulcrum like a seesaw (Class I)"],"a":2,"e":"In a Class III lever (Fulcrum-Effort-Load), the biceps inserts onto the radial tuberosity very close to the elbow fulcrum (short internal moment arm ~3-5 cm), while the external dumbbell acts at the hand (~30-35 cm external moment arm). Because internal moment arm \u003c external moment arm, MA \u003c 1—meaning the muscle must generate much higher force than the weight lifted, in exchange for huge gains in linear velocity and excursion at the distal limb.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03485","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Pain Science and Electrotherapy","topic":"Conventional High-Frequency vs Acupuncture-Like TENS","q":"When applying Conventional (High-Frequency, Low-Intensity) TENS for acute postoperative incisional pain based on Melzack and Wall\u0027s Gate Control Theory, which parameter combination selectively depolarizes large-diameter A-beta mechanoreceptors without triggering painful A-delta/C fibers or muscle twitch?","o":["Frequency 1-4 Hz, long pulse duration 300 microseconds, strong visible rhythmic muscle contractions","Intensity increased to maximum tolerance until tetanic muscle cramping occurs","Frequency 10,000 Hz continuous direct current causing tissue blistering","Frequency 80-120 Hz, short pulse duration 50-100 microseconds, comfortable sensory tingling (\u0027pins and needles\u0027) below motor threshold"],"a":3,"e":"Large myelinated A-beta sensory afferents have lower chronaxie/excitation thresholds than smaller nociceptive A-delta/C fibers. Conventional TENS uses high frequency (80-120 Hz) and brief pulses (50-100 microseconds) at gentle sensory paresthesia intensity to activate A-beta fibers, exciting inhibitory interneurons in the spinal dorsal horn substantia gelatinosa to \u0027close the gate\u0027 rapidly. Conversely, low-frequency (2-4 Hz) motor-level TENS releases descending endogenous opioids/endorphins.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03486","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Pediatric Neurological Physiotherapy","topic":"GMFCS Levels I-V Prognosis","q":"A 5-year-old boy with spastic diplegic cerebral palsy walks independently indoors across smooth floors using a posterior Kaye walker, climbs stairs holding a railing with assistance, and uses a manual wheelchair for long community distances (\u003e500 m). Which Gross Motor Function Classification System (GMFCS) level accurately describes his functional status?","o":["GMFCS Level III (walks with hand-held mobility device; wheeled mobility for long distances)","GMFCS Level II (walks without hand-held mobility device in all indoor settings by age 5)","GMFCS Level V (transported in manual wheelchair in all settings; severe head/trunk antigravity limitation)","GMFCS Level I (walks indoors/outdoors and climbs stairs without limitations or assistive device)"],"a":0,"e":"The 5-level GMFCS classifies self-initiated functional mobility in cerebral palsy: Level I walks freely without devices; Level II walks without devices indoors/short distances (may need railing on stairs/uneven ground); Level III walks using a hand-held mobility device (walker/crutches) indoors and uses wheeled mobility outdoors/long distances; Level IV uses powered/supported wheeled mobility; Level V lacks independent antigravity head/trunk control.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03487","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Obstetric and Pelvic Floor Physiotherapy","topic":"Diastasis Recti Abdominis (DRA)","q":"When assessing a 6-week postpartum woman in hook-lying for Diastasis Recti Abdominis (DRA) by palpating the linea alba 4.5 cm above, at, and 4.5 cm below the umbilicus while she performs a gentle head-and-shoulder lift, what inter-recti distance (IRD) is traditionally considered clinically significant, and which initial exercise approach is safest?","o":["Perform forced manual compression to surgically suture the rectus sheath","IRD \u003e 2 fingerbreadths (approx. \u003e2.0-2.5 cm) with linea alba doming/invagination; initiate gentle transverse abdominis/pelvic floor co-activation while avoiding high-load Valsalva sit-ups that worsen abdominal doming","IRD \u003e 0.2 cm; prescribe heavy weighted Russian twists and maximal sit-ups with breath-holding","Measure IRD while standing upside down; prescribe strict 6-month bedrest"],"a":1,"e":"Clinically significant DRA is palpated at the level of the umbilicus and +/-4.5 cm above/below during a head/scapular curl-up, defined as \u003e=2 fingerbreadths (~\u003e2.0-2.7 cm) or visible midline bulging (\u0027coning/doming\u0027) indicating poor linea alba tension transfer. Early rehab emphasizes gentle deep core (transverse abdominis + pelvic floor) recruitment on exhalation while progressing loads gradually without intra-abdominal doming.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03488","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Geriatric Physiotherapy","topic":"Timed Up and Go (TUG) Test","q":"A physiotherapist administers the standardized Timed Up and Go (TUG) test to a 76-year-old community-dwelling woman using her regular cane. She rises from a standard armchair, walks 3 meters, turns around a cone, walks back 3 meters, and sits down in 16.5 seconds. How is this score interpreted regarding community fall risk?","o":["Invalid because patients are never allowed to use their habitual cane during TUG","Completely normal athletic mobility (\u003c10 seconds)","Abnormal / High fall risk (exceeds the \u003e=13.5-second community-dwelling older adult fall-risk cutoff, warranting multifactorial balance/gait intervention)","Requires no action unless TUG exceeds 60 seconds"],"a":2,"e":"The standardized TUG measures total seconds from the cue \u0027Go\u0027 until the patient\u0027s buttocks touch the chair seat after walking 3 meters (10 feet), turning, and returning while using their customary footwear and assistive device. Healthy independent adults typically complete TUG in \u003c=10-12 seconds; \u003e=13.5 seconds (Shumway-Cook threshold) indicates elevated fall risk in community-dwelling elders.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03489","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Physiotherapy Research Methodology","topic":"Allocation Concealment vs Blinding","q":"In a physiotherapy RCT comparing knee osteoarthritis manual therapy plus exercise versus exercise alone, why must \u0027allocation concealment\u0027 (e.g., central computer randomization or sequentially numbered opaque sealed envelopes) be preserved prior to enrolling each participant, even though therapists and patients cannot easily be blinded to manual therapy afterward?","o":["Allocation concealment prevents selection bias by ensuring recruiting clinicians cannot predict or manipulate which group an eligible patient enters before enrollment, whereas blinding reduces performance/detection bias after assignment","Allocation concealment guarantees both patient and therapist never feel manual joint mobilizations","Allocation concealment substitutes for Intention-to-Treat analysis when 40% drop out","Allocation concealment is identical to outcome assessor blinding after 6 months"],"a":0,"e":"Allocation concealment protects the randomization sequence BEFORE group assignment so investigators cannot selectively steer sicker or healthier patients into a preferred arm (eliminating selection bias—always achievable even in physical therapy trials where therapist/patient blinding is impossible). Afterward, assessor blinding and Intention-to-Treat (ITT) analysis preserve prognostic balance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03490","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Advanced Biomechanics and Kinesiology","topic":"Screw-Home Mechanism","q":"During the terminal 20 degrees of CLOSED-CHAIN knee extension (e.g., rising from a squat), what obligatory axial rotation occurs to lock the knee (\u0027screw-home mechanism\u0027), and which muscle initiates unlocking at the start of knee flexion?","o":["The patella rotates 90 degrees laterally; the gastrocnemius locks the knee","The femur externally rotates on the fixed tibia to lock; the quadriceps unlocks the knee","Zero axial rotation occurs at the tibiofemoral joint","The femur internally rotates on the fixed tibia to lock; the popliteus muscle contracts to laterally rotate the femur on the fixed tibia (or medially rotate tibia in open chain) to unlock"],"a":3,"e":"Because the medial femoral condyle is longer/more curved anteriorly than the lateral condyle, terminal extension produces ~10 degrees of obligatory axial \u0027screw-home\u0027 rotation: in open chain, the tibia externally rotates on the fixed femur; in closed chain (weight-bearing), the femur internally rotates on the fixed tibia. To initiate flexion from full extension, the oblique popliteus contracts (\u0027key to the knee\u0027)—externally rotating the femur in closed chain (or internally rotating the tibia in open chain) while retracting the lateral meniscus.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03491","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Neurological Physiotherapy","topic":"Parkinson Disease Freezing of Gait","q":"A 67-year-old man with Hoehn and Yahr Stage III Parkinson disease experiences severe freezing of gait (FOG) when crossing doorways and turning, with shuffling festinating steps. Why do high-contrast horizontal floor tape stripes (visual cues) and a rhythmic metronome beat (auditory cues) immediately improve his stride length and gait initiation?","o":["External sensory cues bypass the defective basal ganglia-supplementary motor area (SMA) internal cueing circuit, recruiting intact lateral premotor cortex and cerebello-parietal pathways to drive goal-directed movement","Visual cues work solely by forcing the patient to flex his lumbar spine 60 degrees","Auditory metronome beats induce spastic extensor synergy in the gastrocnemius","Visual floor stripes increase nigrostriatal dopamine synthesis within 3 seconds"],"a":0,"e":"In Parkinson disease, nigrostriatal dopamine depletion impairs automatic internal motor sequencing mediated by the basal ganglia and supplementary motor area (SMA). External visual (floor stripes/laser cane) and rhythmic auditory (metronome/counting) cues shift gait control to externally triggered lateral premotor and parietal-cerebellar networks, dramatically increasing step length and breaking freezing episodes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03492","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Sports Physiotherapy","topic":"ACL Reconstruction Return-to-Sport Criteria","q":"A 22-year-old footballer is 10 weeks post-patellar-tendon autograft ACL reconstruction. Which open-kinetic-chain knee angle range generates the HIGHEST anterior tibial translation shear strain on the healing ACL graft (requiring caution early on), and what minimum Limb Symmetry Index (LSI) is generally required before unrestricted return to pivoting sports?","o":["Closed-chain mini-squats from 0 to 45 degrees tear the ACL graft immediately","Terminal extension from 30 to 0 degrees of knee flexion maximizes ACL strain; return to pivoting sports requires \u003e=90% LSI on isokinetic quadriceps/hamstring strength and functional hop battery (plus psychological readiness and \u003e=9 months post-op)","Deep knee flexion from 90 to 120 degrees maximizes ACL strain; 50% LSI at 8 weeks post-op is safe","Isometric quadriceps sets at 0 degrees should be banned for 12 months"],"a":1,"e":"Because the patellar tendon pulls anteriorly on the tibia near full extension (large anterior shear angle), high-load open-chain resisted knee extension between 30 and 0 degrees places maximal tensile strain on an ACL graft (whereas \u003e60-90 degrees pulls posteriorly and protects the ACL). Current Panther/Aspetar consensus requires passing objective strength and hop test batteries (\u003e=90% Limb Symmetry Index) alongside time (\u003e=9 months) for biological graft ligamentization before pivoting sports.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03493","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Physiotherapy","topic":"Incentive Spirometry and Deep Breathing","q":"On postoperative day 1 after an open cholecystectomy (subcostal incision), a patient exhibits shallow rapid breathing, decreased Functional Residual Capacity (FRC), and bibasilar crackles from basal atelectasis. When instructing the patient on volume-oriented Incentive Spirometry (IS), what breathing mechanics are essential for alveolar recruitment?","o":["Rapid, forceful, explosive blowing out into the mouthpiece as fast as possible","Continuous breath-holding after maximal forced expiration down to residual volume","Slow, sustained maximal inspiration (inhaling slowly to raise the piston/ball and holding at peak inspiration for 3-5 seconds) followed by relaxed passive exhalation, performed ~10 times/hour while awake","Shallow 100 mL panting breaths while slouched flat in bed"],"a":2,"e":"Upper abdominal surgery reduces FRC by ~30-50% via diaphragmatic splinting, causing dependent basilar atelectasis. Incentive spirometry provides visual biofeedback for a Sustained Maximal Inspiration (SMI)—slow laminar inhalation minimizes upper-airway turbulence while a 3-to-5-second end-inspiratory hold maximizes transpulmonary pressure and collateral ventilation (pores of Kohn) to reopen collapsed alveoli—ideally paired with upright positioning and early ambulation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03494","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Physical Agents and Electrophysical Modalities","topic":"Shortwave Diathermy (SWD) Contraindications","q":"A physiotherapist considers continuous Shortwave Diathermy (SWD, 27.12 MHz) for a patient with chronic knee osteoarthritis. Which patient condition is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to continuous SWD application?","o":["Healed adhesive capsulitis with normal cutaneous thermal sensation","Mild chronic lumbar muscle spasm in a healthy 35-year-old adult with no implants","Subacute ankle sprain after 14 days with intact sensation and circulation","Presence of a cardiac pacemaker/ICD, metallic internal fixation/joint prosthesis inside or near the field, pregnancy (over pelvis/abdomen), ischemic vascular disease, or open epiphyseal growth plates"],"a":3,"e":"Continuous SWD delivers deep high-frequency electromagnetic energy (27.12 MHz) converting to intense deep heat. Metallic implants concentrate eddy currents causing lethal deep tissue burns; electromagnetic fields interfere with or damage cardiac pacemakers/ICDs (even outside the immediate field); ischemic tissues cannot dissipate heat; and heating active malignancy, pregnancy, or pediatric growth plates risks metastasis, teratogenesis, or premature physeal arrest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03495","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Physiotherapy Research Methodology","topic":"Minimal Detectable Change (MDC95) Calculation","q":"A reliability study of the Berg Balance Scale (BBS) in chronic stroke reports a Standard Error of Measurement (SEM) of 2.0 points. Using the standard 95% confidence formula (MDC95 = 1.96 x sqrt(2) x SEM, where 1.96 x sqrt(2) ~ 2.77), what is the Minimal Detectable Change (MDC95) required to be 95% confident that a patient\u0027s change score reflects true change beyond measurement error?","o":["5.54 points (typically rounded up to 6 points on an integer scale)","0.72 points","11.08 points","2.00 points"],"a":0,"e":"Because two measurements (pre and post) each carry measurement error (variance = SEM^2 + SEM^2 = 2*SEM^2, so standard error of the difference = sqrt(2) x SEM), the 95% Minimal Detectable Change is MDC95 = 1.96 x 1.414 x SEM = 2.77 x 2.0 = 5.54 points (requiring \u003e=6 points improvement on the integer BBS to exceed random error).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03496","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Pelvic and Spinal Biomechanics","topic":"Form Closure vs Force Closure","q":"In Vleeming and Lee\u0027s biomechanical model of lumbopelvic load transfer across the sacroiliac joint (SIJ), what distinguishes \u0027form closure\u0027 from \u0027force closure\u0027 during single-leg weight bearing?","o":["Form closure refers to passive anatomical joint congruency (wedged sacrum, interlocking cartilage ridges, and ligaments), whereas force closure refers to dynamic transverse compression generated by myofascial slings (transversus abdominis, pelvic floor, multifidus, and posterior oblique gluteus maximus-latissimus dorsi sling)","Form closure means tightening external pelvic orthotic belts only; force closure means surgical screw fusion","Form closure occurs solely in the cervical spine; force closure occurs solely in the ankle","Both terms describe passive ligamentous laxity during pregnancy"],"a":0,"e":"Because the SIJ transmits huge vertical trunk-to-limb shear forces across nearly planar vertical articular surfaces, passive \u0027form closure\u0027 (keystone sacral wedging, roughened ridges, sacrotuberous/interosseous ligaments) alone is insufficient under dynamic loads. \u0027Force closure\u0027 adds active perpendicular compressive friction across the joint via deep core co-activation (transversus abdominis, multifidus, pelvic floor) and oblique thoracolumbar slings (gluteus maximus + contralateral latissimus dorsi).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03497","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Neurological Physiotherapy","topic":"Contraversive Pushing (Pusher Syndrome)","q":"Two weeks after a right posterolateral thalamic stroke, a 62-year-old man forcefully uses his intact right arm and leg to push himself laterally toward his paralyzed left side during sitting and transfers, fiercely resisting any therapist attempt to passively correct him back to upright midline. Why does pushing back manually fail, and what treatment approach is effective?","o":["Push forcefully against his paretic shoulder to overpower his intact right limbs","The patient perceives his body as \u0027upright\u0027 when tilted ~18 degrees toward the non-paretic side (distorted subjective postural vertical, though visual vertical remains intact); therapist should provide clear visual vertical references (mirrors, doorframes) and ask him to actively reach/align himself toward the unaffected side rather than pushing against him","The patient is deliberately uncooperative; apply bilateral wrist restraints to the wheelchair","He has cerebellar ataxia requiring spinning vestibular habituation"],"a":1,"e":"Contraversive pushing (Pusher syndrome, linked to posterolateral thalamic/insular lesions) stems from a central mismatch between intact visual vertical perception and a ~18-degree ipsilesionally tilted Subjective Postural Vertical (SPV). Manually pushing the patient upright triggers severe fear of falling toward the intact side and stronger reflexive pushing. Guiding active weight shifts toward the intact side using external vertical visual landmarks (walls, vertical poles, mirrors) recalibrates postural verticality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03498","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Exercise Physiology and Cardiopulmonary PT","topic":"METs and Rate Pressure Product (RPP)","q":"A 58-year-old stable coronary artery disease patient exercises at 5 METs during Phase III cardiac rehabilitation, with HR = 110/min and Systolic BP = 150 mmHg. Given 1 MET = 3.5 mL O2/kg/min, what is his oxygen uptake (VO2) in mL/kg/min and his Rate Pressure Product (RPP)?","o":["VO2 = 10.5 mL/kg/min; RPP = 15,000","VO2 = 35.0 mL/kg/min; RPP = 1,650","VO2 = 17.5 mL/kg/min; RPP = 16,500 (HR x SBP)","VO2 = 5.0 mL/kg/min; RPP = 260"],"a":2,"e":"1 MET represents resting oxygen consumption (~3.5 mL O2/kg/min); therefore 5 METs = 5 x 3.5 = 17.5 mL/kg/min. Rate Pressure Product (Double Product, RPP = Heart Rate x Systolic Blood Pressure = 110 x 150 = 16,500) correlates closely with myocardial oxygen demand (MVO2)—so angina typically recurs reproducibly at a patient\u0027s specific ischemic RPP threshold.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03499","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Neuromuscular Electrical Stimulation","topic":"Russian Current Parameters","q":"Which electrical stimulation waveform parameters define classic \u0027Russian Current\u0027 used to augment quadriceps muscle strength post-knee surgery while minimizing superficial cutaneous pain?","o":["27.12 MHz continuous electromagnetic thermal wave","1 Hz monophasic direct current continuous galvanic flow for 30 minutes","5 Hz microcurrent at 10 microamperes below sensory threshold","2,500 Hz alternating current carrier wave burst-modulated at 50 bursts/second (50 Hz) with a 10 ms ON / 10 ms OFF burst cycle (and macro 10 s ON / 50 s OFF fatigue-preventing duty cycle)"],"a":3,"e":"Developed by Yakov Kots, Russian stimulation delivers a medium-frequency 2,500 Hz sinusoidal AC carrier (which encounters much lower capacitive skin impedance, reducing sensory discomfort) packaged into 50 Hz physiological tetanic bursts (10 ms burst on, 10 ms rest) to recruit deep motor axons—combined with a 1:5 macro on:off cycle (e.g., 10 s contraction / 50 s recovery) to prevent rapid fast-twitch fatigue.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03500","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Sports Physiotherapy","topic":"Berlin/Amsterdam Graded Return-to-Sport (RTS) Protocol","q":"A 19-year-old university rugby player sustains a mild head collision with transient dizziness, photophobia, and difficulty recalling the play, which mostly improve 20 minutes later on the sidelines. Under international Concussion in Sport Consensus guidelines, may he return to the match immediately, and how must subsequent rehabilitation progress?","o":["NO—mandatory immediate removal from play with ZERO same-day return; after 24-48 hours of relative cognitive/physical rest, progress through a 6-step graded Return-to-Sport protocol (\u003e=24 hours per step, stepping back if symptoms recur)","Confine the athlete to complete dark-room bedrest for 30 consecutive days without reading","Yes—allow immediate return to full tackling once 20 minutes have passed","Resume full-contact scrimmage the very next morning regardless of headache"],"a":0,"e":"Suspected concussion mandates immediate removal from play (\u0027if in doubt, sit them out\u0027) and zero same-day return due to Second Impact Syndrome and prolonged recovery risks. Current Amsterdam/Berlin consensus recommends 24-48 hrs relative rest (light daily walking/screen tolerance below symptom exacerbation threshold) followed by a supervised 6-stage aerobic-to-contact progression spending at least 24 hours per stage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03501","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Physiotherapy Research Methodology","topic":"Multiple Imputation vs Last Observation Carried Forward (LOCF)","q":"In a 12-month randomized trial of chronic neck pain physiotherapy with 18% participant dropout at 6 and 12 months, why do modern CONSORT/biostatistical standards recommend Multiple Imputation (or mixed-effects repeated-measures models) rather than simple Last Observation Carried Forward (LOCF) or Complete-Case analysis?","o":["Complete-case analysis guarantees unbiased results even when sicker patients selectively drop out","LOCF unrealistically assumes patients\u0027 pain stays frozen after dropout and artificially shrinks standard errors (inflating false positives), whereas Multiple Imputation accounts for uncertainty across multiple imputed datasets under Missing At Random (MAR) assumptions","LOCF deletes every patient in the control group automatically","Multiple Imputation fabricates data without any observed baseline covariates"],"a":1,"e":"Complete-case analysis drops incomplete participants (losing power and biasing estimates unless Missing Completely At Random), while single-value LOCF assumes zero post-dropout progression/regression and treats imputed values as observed truth—underestimating variance. Multiple Imputation generates m plausible datasets incorporating observed covariates/outcomes and pools standard errors using Rubin\u0027s rules (assuming MAR).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03502","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Core Discipline Principles","topic":"Maitland Orthopaedic Manual Therapy","q":"A 38-year-old man presents with acute right-sided L4-L5 lumbar facet joint pain rated 8/10 after sudden trunk rotation. Just 10 degrees of lumbar extension provokes sharp pain that persists for 45 minutes after he returns to neutral (high severity, high irritability). According to Maitland\u0027s concept, which mobilisation grade and amplitude are indicated during the initial session?","o":["Grade IV+ high-frequency small-amplitude oscillations held firmly at R2 end-range to stretch the facet capsule","Grade V high-velocity low-amplitude thrust manipulation directly into painful right extension","Grade I or Grade II gentle oscillatory mobilisation performed short of initial tissue resistance (before R1) to modulate pain without provoking irritability","Sustained static Grade III++ end-range stretching for 10 minutes continuously"],"a":2,"e":"In Maitland\u0027s SINSS clinical reasoning framework (Severity, Irritability, Nature, Stage, Stability), high severity and high irritability indicate easily provoked, slow-settling nociception where pain is encountered well before resistance (P1 before R1). Grade I (small-amplitude oscillation at the beginning of range) and Grade II (large-amplitude oscillation within the resistance-free midrange, short of R1) stimulate Type I/II articular mechanoreceptors for neurophysiological analgesia without stressing inflamed capsular tissue.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03503","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Core Discipline Principles","topic":"Mulligan Concept Manual Therapy","q":"A physiotherapist treats a tennis player with chronic lateral epicondylalgia using Mulligan\u0027s Mobilisation with Movement (MWM) at the elbow: applying a sustained pain-free lateral glide of the proximal forearm while the patient performs an active pain-free gripping task. Which strict clinical rule governs Mulligan MWM application (PILL principle)?","o":["The glide is applied solely passively at rest while the patient avoids any active muscle contraction","If the first glide direction increases pain, the therapist must persist for 3 sets of 10 repetitions before reassessing","The therapist must provoke moderate familiar tendon pain (4-5/10) during gripping to remodel collagen cross-links","The sustained accessory glide and active movement together must produce immediate pain relief and functional improvement during the technique (Pain-free, Instant, Long-lasting)"],"a":3,"e":"Mulligan\u0027s MWM combines a sustained manual accessory glide (correcting subtle biomechanical positional faults or altering neurophysiological pain processing) with simultaneous active physiological movement. Under the PILL acronym (Pain-free, Instant result, Long-lasting), the technique must be completely pain-free during execution and yield immediate measurable improvement (e.g., pain-free grip force). If any pain occurs, the glide force, angle, or direction is immediately modified or abandoned.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03504","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Core Discipline Principles","topic":"Differential Diagnosis in Orthopaedic Manual Therapy","q":"Using Cyriax\u0027s selective tissue tension testing on a patient with shoulder pain, the physiotherapist records: full, pain-free passive glenohumeral external rotation, abduction, and internal rotation; painful and weak resisted isometric external rotation at neutral; and painful active abduction in a midrange arc. Which tissue category and probable lesion does this pattern identify?","o":["Contractile tissue lesion — specifically a partial tear or tendinopathy of the infraspinatus/teres minor musculotendinous unit","Inert capsular lesion — early adhesive capsulitis in a capsular pattern","Complete brachial plexus pan-lesion with painless total paralysis","Acromioclavicular joint osteoarthrosis without rotator cuff involvement"],"a":0,"e":"In Cyriax\u0027s selective tissue tension paradigm, inert structures (capsule, ligaments, bursa) are stressed by passive stretching and display restricted/painful passive ROM (often in a capsular or non-capsular pattern) with strong, pain-free resisted tests. Conversely, contractile structures (muscle, tendon, tenoperiosteal junction) show preserved full passive ROM (unless stretched forcefully at extremes) paired with pain on resisted isometric contraction. Painful AND weak resisted external rotation indicates a partial tendon tear (or painful inhibition with structural compromise) of the external rotators (infraspinatus/teres minor).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03505","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Clinical / Practical Applications","topic":"Neurodynamic Assessment and Mobilisation","q":"A 29-year-old runner with persistent posterior thigh and calf pain undergoes Butler\u0027s Slump test. With thoracic/lumbar slump, full cervical flexion, knee extension, and ankle dorsiflexion, his exact posterior thigh and calf pain is reproduced. When he extends his neck back to neutral (releasing cervical flexion) while holding the leg position unchanged, the posterior thigh and calf pain immediately disappears. What does this structural differentiation confirm?","o":["Lumbar zygapophyseal joint locking, because knee extension directly glides the L5 facet","Positive neurodynamic mechanosensitivity (neural tissue involvement), because moving a distant spinal segment alters distal symptoms without changing hamstring length","Isolated proximal hamstring muscle strain, because neck extension relaxes the ischial tuberosity origin","Popliteal artery entrapment, because cervical extension increases lower-limb arterial inflow"],"a":1,"e":"Structural differentiation is the cornerstone of neurodynamic testing (Butler/Shacklock). Because the hip, knee, and ankle angles—and thus hamstring muscle and local joint lengths—remain constant while releasing cervical flexion reduces longitudinal tension along the continuous dura mater and sciatic nerve tract, the immediate symptom abolition confirms neural mechanosensitivity rather than local hamstring or musculoskeletal strain.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03506","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Applied Basic Sciences","topic":"Neuroplasticity in Stroke Rehabilitation","q":"A subacute stroke patient is evaluated for modified Constraint-Induced Movement Therapy (mCIMT) to reverse upper-limb learned non-use. Which minimum active motor criterion in the paretic hand/wrist is standard for CIMT eligibility, and what cortical plasticity change does CIMT induce?","o":["Ashworth Grade 4 wrist flexor spasticity with zero voluntary finger opening; induces spinal Renshaw cell degeneration","At least 90 degrees of passive shoulder flexion only; induces cerebellar vermis hypertrophy within 48 hours","At least 20 degrees of active wrist extension and 10 degrees of active thumb/finger extension; induces use-dependent cortical map expansion in the ipsilesional primary motor cortex (M1)","Complete flaccid paralysis (0/5 wrist and finger extension); induces transcallosal inhibition from the ipsilesional to contralesional M1"],"a":2,"e":"Classic and modified CIMT (EXCITE trial criteria) require sufficient distal motor return—typically \u003e=20 degrees active wrist extension and \u003e=10 degrees active abduction/extension of the thumb and at least two other digits (higher-functioning criteria; lower-functioning criteria allow 10/10 degrees)—so the patient can engage in intensive shaping tasks while wearing a mitt on the less-affected hand. Intensive forced task practice overcomes learned non-use and drives use-dependent synaptic reorganization and expansion of hand motor representation in the ipsilesional M1.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03507","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Applied Basic Sciences","topic":"Non-Invasive Brain Stimulation in Neurorehabilitation","q":"In post-stroke motor rehabilitation guided by the interhemispheric competition model, how are low-frequency and high-frequency repetitive Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation (rTMS) protocols applied to prime cortical excitability prior to task-specific physiotherapy?","o":["High-frequency rTMS (\u003e= 20 Hz) over the contralesional M1 to boost its inhibition over the damaged hemisphere","Continuous 50 Hz rTMS over the brainstem medulla to directly depolarize spinal alpha motor neurons","Low-frequency rTMS (1 Hz) over the ipsilesional M1 to silence surviving perilesional pyramidal neurons","Low-frequency inhibitory rTMS (\u003c= 1 Hz) over the contralesional M1 to suppress excessive transcallosal inhibition, OR high-frequency excitatory rTMS (\u003e= 5-10 Hz) over the ipsilesional M1"],"a":3,"e":"After a unilateral stroke, reduced output from the damaged ipsilesional M1 releases the intact contralesional M1, which exerts excessive GABA-mediated transcallosal inhibition back onto the lesioned hemisphere, further impairing recovery. To rebalance interhemispheric excitability, either low-frequency rTMS (\u003c=1 Hz, LTD-like inhibitory) is delivered over contralesional M1, or high-frequency rTMS (\u003e=5-10 Hz, LTP-like excitatory) is delivered over ipsilesional M1 before motor training.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03508","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Clinical / Practical Applications","topic":"Locomotor Training in Incomplete SCI","q":"A 26-year-old man with chronic incomplete thoracic spinal cord injury (T9, AIS C) begins Body-Weight Supported Treadmill Training (BWSTT) with 35% body-weight unloading and manual kinematic assistance at normal walking speeds. Which sensory inputs during BWSTT are most critical to activate spinal Central Pattern Generators (CPGs) and promote stance-to-swing transitions?","o":["Phasic limb loading through plantar/extensor proprioceptors during stance combined with hip extension stretch at terminal stance","Continuous 100% body-weight unloading with the hips held statically in 40 degrees of flexion","Slow treadmill speeds (\u003c 0.1 m/s) with maximal rigid knee-ankle bracing to eliminate ankle proprioception","Complete visual deprivation combined with passive reciprocal upper-limb cycling only"],"a":0,"e":"Lumbar spinal locomotor Central Pattern Generators (CPGs) rely heavily on task-specific afferent feedback to organize alternating flexor-extensor stepping rhythms. Specifically, weight-bearing load receptors (Ib Golgi tendon organs in ankle/knee extensors and cutaneous plantar mechanoreceptors) maintain stance phase, while hip flexor spindle afferents stretched as the hip extends past neutral in late stance trigger the stance-to-swing transition. Providing partial (e.g., 30-40%) unloading rather than 100% unweighting preserves these critical load signals.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03509","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Core Discipline Principles","topic":"Motor Learning Principles in Neurorehabilitation","q":"A physiotherapist compares two practice schedules for teaching community-dwelling stroke survivors to transfer safely onto different chair heights and surfaces: Group 1 performs random/variable practice with faded Knowledge of Results (KR after every 4th trial), whereas Group 2 performs blocked/constant practice with 100% immediate KR after every trial. Which outcome pattern is consistently predicted by motor learning theory?","o":["Blocked practice with constant chair height maximizes contextual interference and cortical motor reprogramming","Group 2 (blocked, 100% KR) performs better during immediate practice acquisition, whereas Group 1 (random/variable, faded KR) achieves superior long-term retention and transfer to novel tasks","Group 1 performs better during immediate acquisition, whereas Group 2 shows superior 1-month transfer to novel chairs","100% immediate KR after every trial prevents error-detection processing and produces the highest long-term retention"],"a":1,"e":"The contextual interference effect (Shea \u0026 Morgan) and guidance hypothesis of feedback (Salmoni/Schmidt) demonstrate a classic dissociation between immediate acquisition performance and true motor learning (retention/transfer). Blocked practice and continuous 100% KR flatter immediate session performance, but make learners dependent on external feedback and repetitive motor plans. Random/variable practice and faded KR force deeper cognitive-motor retrieval, intrinsic error detection, and schema generalization, yielding superior long-term retention and transfer.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03510","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Clinical / Practical Applications","topic":"Vestibular Rehabilitation: BPPV","q":"A 54-year-old woman reports 20-second episodes of intense spinning vertigo triggered by rolling onto her right side in bed and looking upward. On a right-sided Dix-Hallpike test, after a 4-second latency, she develops vertigo and a crescendo-decrescendo upbeating, rightward-torsional (geotropic) nystagmus lasting 18 seconds that fatigues on repeat testing. What is the exact diagnosis and definitive physiotherapy intervention?","o":["Central cerebellar vermis stroke; refer for immediate thrombolysis and avoid head movement","Right horizontal semicircular canal canalithiasis; treat with the 360-degree Lempert barbecue roll toward the right","Right posterior semicircular canal canalithiasis; treat with the right-sided Epley Canalith Repositioning Procedure (CRP)","Left anterior semicircular canal cupulolithiasis; treat with the left Yacovino deep head-hanging manoeuvre"],"a":2,"e":"Brief (\u003c60 s), fatigable, latent-onset vertigo accompanied by upbeating and geotropic torsional nystagmus (top pole of eyes beating toward the dependent right ear) on the right Dix-Hallpike test is diagnostic of right posterior semicircular canal canalithiasis (free-floating otoconia in the long arm). The right-sided Epley Canalith Repositioning Procedure (CRP) sequentially rotates the head in 90-degree increments to guide otoconia back through the common crus into the utricle.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03511","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Clinical / Practical Applications","topic":"Vestibular Rehabilitation: Horizontal Canal BPPV","q":"A patient with positional vertigo undergoes a supine head roll test (Pagnini-McClure test). Turning the head 90 degrees to the left evokes intense horizontal geotropic nystagmus (beating toward the left/ground) lasting 35 seconds; turning the head 90 degrees to the right evokes milder horizontal geotropic nystagmus (beating toward the right/ground). Which canal is involved and which direction should the Lempert 360-degree Barbecue Roll turn?","o":["Right posterior semicircular canal canalithiasis; perform the left Semont liberatory manoeuvre","Right horizontal semicircular canal canalithiasis; roll sequentially toward the left (unaffected) ear","Left horizontal semicircular canal cupulolithiasis; perform Brandt-Daroff exercises only","Left horizontal semicircular canal canalithiasis; roll sequentially in 90-degree steps toward the right (unaffected) ear"],"a":3,"e":"In horizontal canal canalithiasis (geotropic variant), turning the head toward the affected ear drives otoconia ampullopetally (toward the utricle), generating an excitatory response in the horizontal canal (Ewald\u0027s second law: ampullopetal flow is excitatory in the horizontal canal) and therefore STRONGER geotropic horizontal nystagmus than turning to the healthy ear. Because left roll elicited stronger geotropic nystagmus, the left horizontal canal is affected. The Lempert Barbecue Roll starts with the left ear down and rotates in 90-degree increments toward the unaffected right side to empty debris into the utricle.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03512","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Core Discipline Principles","topic":"Vestibular Hypofunction Rehabilitation","q":"A 42-year-old teacher 3 weeks after acute left vestibular neuritis reports oscillopsia (bouncing vision) and dizziness when turning her head while walking. To drive cerebellar-mediated Vestibulo-Ocular Reflex (VOR) gain adaptation using the VOR x1 viewing exercise, what key execution parameters must the physiotherapist prescribe?","o":["Rotate the head actively horizontally (and vertically) while keeping eyes sharply focused on a stationary visual target at arm\u0027s length, moving as fast as possible just before the target blurs (creating mild retinal slip)","Perform saccadic head-and-target thrusts exclusively in the dark for 2 hours daily","Move the visual target slowly across the field while holding the head completely still to train smooth pursuit","Close the eyes completely during rapid head turns so visual retinal slip is eliminated"],"a":0,"e":"The error signal driving plastic recalibration of Vestibulo-Ocular Reflex (VOR) gain in the cerebellar flocculus is retinal slip—slight motion of a visual image across the fovea during head movement. In VOR x1 adaptation exercises, the patient fixes gaze on a stationary target (x1) while rotating the head at the highest velocity that still keeps the target in clear focus (typically 1-2+ Hz across horizontal and vertical planes, 1-2 minutes per set, multiple times daily).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03513","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Clinical / Practical Applications","topic":"Differential Diagnosis of Acute Vestibular Syndrome","q":"A 67-year-old hypertensive man presents with acute continuous vertigo, nausea, and severe truncal ataxia (unable to sit unsupported) lasting 14 hours. On the bedside HINTS examination, his horizontal Head Impulse Test (HIT) is bilaterally normal (no corrective catch-up saccades), nystagmus changes direction from right-beating on right gaze to left-beating on left gaze, and alternating cover test reveals vertical skew deviation. What is the immediate clinical priority?","o":["Perform a right-sided Epley manoeuvre immediately assuming bilateral posterior canal BPPV","Suspect an acute central posterior-circulation (brainstem/cerebellar) stroke and initiate urgent emergency stroke neurology/MRI evaluation before attempting vestibular exercises","Diagnose persistent postural-perceptual dizziness (PPPD) and reassure the patient without imaging","Diagnose benign acute right vestibular neuritis and immediately discharge home with VOR x1 exercises"],"a":1,"e":"In acute vestibular syndrome (continuous vertigo \u003e24 h or ongoing), the three-step HINTS battery (Head Impulse, Nystagmus, Test of Skew) has higher sensitivity than early MRI for posterior fossa stroke. Any single INFARCT red flag—Impulse Normal (intact VOR in a dizzy patient), Fast-phase Alternating (direction-changing gaze-evoked nystagmus), or Refixation on Cover Test (vertical skew deviation)—indicates central brainstem/cerebellar pathology. Here all three central signs plus severe truncal ataxia are present, demanding urgent stroke activation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03514","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Applied Basic Sciences","topic":"Exercise Cardiopulmonary Physiology","q":"During a maximal cardiopulmonary exercise test (CPET), a 66 kg endurance athlete achieves a maximal cardiac output (Qmax) of 22 L/min and a maximal arteriovenous oxygen content difference (a-vO2 diff) of 150 mL O2 per litre of blood (15 mL O2/dL). Using the Fick equation, what are his absolute VO2max and relative VO2max?","o":["Absolute VO2max = 33.0 L/min; Relative VO2max = 500 mL/kg/min","Absolute VO2max = 1.47 L/min; Relative VO2max = 22.3 mL/kg/min","Absolute VO2max = 3.30 L/min; Relative VO2max = 50.0 mL/kg/min","Absolute VO2max = 2.20 L/min; Relative VO2max = 33.3 mL/kg/min"],"a":2,"e":"By the Fick equation, VO2max = Qmax x (a-vO2 diff max). Converting units consistently: 22 L/min x 150 mL O2/L = 3300 mL O2/min = 3.30 L/min absolute VO2max. Dividing by body mass (66 kg): 3300 mL/min / 66 kg = 50.0 mL/kg/min relative VO2max.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03515","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Core Discipline Principles","topic":"Functional Capacity Field Testing","q":"A cardiopulmonary physiotherapist establishes a standardized Six-Minute Walk Test (6MWT) station according to American Thoracic Society (ATS) guidelines to track outcomes in COPD pulmonary rehabilitation. Which testing protocol and Minimal Clinically Important Difference (MCID) benchmark are accurate?","o":["An outdoor 400-meter oval track where patients run as fast as possible while carrying no pulse oximeter, with an MCID of 100 meters","A 10-meter carpeted hallway with continuous enthusiastic coaching and therapist pacing alongside the patient, and an MCID of 5 meters","A motorized treadmill set at 3.5 km/h with 5% grade and a mandatory 10-minute warm-up, with an MCID of 150 meters","A 30-meter flat indoor corridor with cones at turnarounds, standardized non-cheering prompts every 1 minute, no warm-up prior, and an MCID of approximately 30 meters (25-33 m) in COPD"],"a":3,"e":"ATS 6MWT guidelines specify a flat, straight, hard-surfaced 30-meter indoor corridor marked every 3 meters with turnaround cones (shorter corridors reduce distance via frequent turns), no prior warm-up, patient walking unaccompanied (not paced by the therapist), and standardized calm phrases at 1-minute intervals. Pre/post HR, BP, SpO2, and modified Borg scores are recorded. Updated ATS/ERS technical standards establish ~30 m (25-33 m) as the MCID in chronic respiratory disease such as COPD.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03516","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Applied Basic Sciences","topic":"Cardiopulmonary Exercise Testing (CPET)","q":"During an incremental ramp cycle-ergometer Cardiopulmonary Exercise Test (CPET), breath-by-breath gas analysis shows a non-linear acceleration in CO2 output (VCO2) relative to O2 uptake (VO2) (V-slope inflection) accompanied by a rise in the ventilatory equivalent for oxygen (VE/VO2) while the ventilatory equivalent for carbon dioxide (VE/VCO2) remains flat. Which physiological landmark has been reached?","o":["First Ventilatory Threshold (VT1 / Anaerobic Threshold), reflecting bicarbonate buffering of lactic acid generating non-metabolic CO2","Peak VO2max plateau with cardiac chronotropic incompetence","Exercise-induced arterial hypoxaemia with pulmonary venous shunting","Respiratory Compensation Point (VT2), reflecting failure of isocapnic buffering and uncompensated metabolic acidosis"],"a":0,"e":"At the First Ventilatory Threshold (VT1), rising lactate production is buffered by plasma bicarbonate (H+ + HCO3- -\u003e H2CO3 -\u003e H2O + CO2), adding non-metabolic CO2 to aerobic CO2. This causes VCO2 to rise more steeply than VO2 (V-slope inflection) and stimulates minute ventilation (VE) proportionally to CO2 elimination—so VE/VO2 rises while VE/VCO2 stays flat (isocapnic buffering phase) until VT2 (Respiratory Compensation Point), when VE/VCO2 also begins rising.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03517","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Clinical / Practical Applications","topic":"Chronic Heart Failure Rehabilitation","q":"A clinically stable 60-year-old man with chronic HFrEF (NYHA Class II-III, LVEF 32%, on optimal beta-blocker and ACE-inhibitor therapy) exhibits reduced maximal inspiratory pressure (PImax = 52 cmH2O, \u003c70% predicted) and exertional dyspnea. Which evidence-based physiotherapy prescription safely combines Inspiratory Muscle Training (IMT) with aerobic conditioning?","o":["High-intensity sprint interval training at \u003e95% HRmax without rhythm or blood pressure monitoring","Threshold-loaded IMT at 30%-50% of PImax alongside supervised moderate-intensity aerobic training (40%-70% VO2peak or Borg RPE 11-14) with warm-up/cool-down","Threshold-loaded IMT at 95% of PImax for 60 continuous minutes twice daily plus maximal Valsalva heavy lifting","Complete bed rest and avoidance of all aerobic training whenever LVEF is below 40%"],"a":1,"e":"In stable NYHA II-III HFrEF patients—especially those with inspiratory muscle weakness (PImax \u003c70% predicted)—threshold-loaded IMT initiated around 30%-50% of PImax improves diaphragmatic strength, attenuates the respiratory metaboreflex, and increases 6MWD and quality of life. Combined with progressive moderate aerobic exercise (40%-70% VO2peak/HRR or RPE 11-14, accounting for beta-blocker heart rate blunting via RPE), exercise capacity improves safely without adverse ventricular remodelling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03518","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Ethics, Research \u0026 Recent Advances","topic":"Measurement Reliability in Physiotherapy Research","q":"A postgraduate physiotherapy researcher evaluates the inter-rater reliability of digital inclinometer thoracic kyphosis measurements across three physiotherapists measuring 40 patients, intending to generalize the findings to physiotherapists nationwide. Why is a two-way random-effects, absolute-agreement Intraclass Correlation Coefficient — ICC(2,1) — preferred over Pearson\u0027s correlation coefficient (r)?","o":["ICC(2,1) accounts for both systematic bias (mean differences between raters) and random variance across \u003e=2 raters, whereas Pearson\u0027s r only measures linear association and ignores systematic additive/proportional bias","Pearson\u0027s r can only be computed for categorical ordinal scales like the Berg Balance Scale","ICC(2,1) treats the three raters as the only fixed raters in existence and ignores measurement error","Pearson\u0027s r automatically equals 0.00 whenever more than two raters are compared"],"a":0,"e":"Pearson\u0027s r is restricted to two variables and detects only linear covariance: if Rater B consistently measures 10 degrees higher than Rater A on every patient, Pearson\u0027s r is a deceptive 1.00 despite poor clinical agreement. ICC(2,1) (two-way random effects, single rater, absolute agreement) models raters as a random sample from a larger population of clinicians and penalizes both random error and systematic between-rater differences, making it the standard reliability index for continuous clinical tools.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03519","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Ethics, Research \u0026 Recent Advances","topic":"Clinimetrics and Outcome Responsiveness","q":"In a validation study of the Oswestry Disability Index (ODI, 0-100 scale) for chronic low back pain, the Standard Error of Measurement (SEM) is 3.0 points. What is the Minimal Detectable Change at the 95% confidence level (MDC95), and how should a clinician interpret a patient\u0027s score reduction from 44 to 34 points (10-point improvement) if the anchor-based MCID is 10 points?","o":["MDC95 = 8.3 points (1.96 x sqrt(2) x 3.0); the 10-point change exceeds both random measurement error (MDC95) and the clinically important threshold (MCID)","MDC95 = 4.2 points (1.414 x 3.0); the 10-point change is entirely within measurement noise","MDC95 = 5.9 points (1.96 x 3.0); the 10-point change proves statistical significance in an RCT","MDC95 = 16.6 points; the 10-point change cannot be distinguished from random test-retest error"],"a":0,"e":"The Minimal Detectable Change at 95% confidence (MDC95) accounts for measurement error across two testing occasions (baseline and follow-up): MDC95 = 1.96 x sqrt(2) x SEM = 1.96 x 1.4142 x 3.0 = 8.32 points (~8.3 points). Because the patient\u0027s 10-point improvement exceeds MDC95 (8.3 points), we can be 95% confident it represents real change beyond random measurement error, and because it also meets the MCID (10 points), it is clinically meaningful to the patient.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03520","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Ethics, Research \u0026 Recent Advances","topic":"Randomised Controlled Trial (RCT) Methodology","q":"Assertion (A): In a physiotherapy randomised controlled trial comparing dry needling plus exercise against exercise alone, analysing all participants strictly within the groups to which they were originally randomised—even if some dropped out or crossed over—via Intention-to-Treat (ITT) analysis preserves baseline prognostic balance. Reason (R): Excluding non-adherent participants or dropouts in a Per-Protocol analysis often introduces attrition and selection bias because dropouts frequently differ systematically in symptom severity or prognosis from completers. Choose the correct option.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Intention-to-Treat (ITT) analysis is a core methodological criterion (including PEDro Item 9) that preserves the initial randomization balance of both known and unknown confounding factors across trial arms and estimates pragmatic real-world effectiveness. Conversely, Per-Protocol analysis removes dropouts or crossovers who often leave precisely because treatment failed or caused adverse effects, introducing systematic attrition bias and exaggerating apparent efficacy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03521","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy — Ethics, Research \u0026 Recent Advances","topic":"Clinical Trial Effect Size Metrics","q":"In a multicentre physiotherapy RCT for rotator cuff tendinopathy, 65% of patients receiving progressive heavy-slow resistance training achieved clinically successful return to pain-free work at 12 weeks (Experimental Event Rate = 0.65), compared with 45% in the sham-ultrasound control group (Control Event Rate = 0.45). What is the Number Needed to Treat (NNT) for one additional patient to achieve a successful outcome?","o":["2","10","5","20"],"a":2,"e":"First calculate the Absolute Benefit Increase (or Absolute Risk Difference, ARD): |EER - CER| = |0.65 - 0.45| = 0.20 (20 percentage points). The Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is the reciprocal of the absolute difference: NNT = 1 / 0.20 = 5. On average, 5 patients must receive progressive heavy-slow resistance training instead of sham ultrasound for 1 additional patient to achieve success at 12 weeks.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03522","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Shoulder Biomechanics","topic":"Scapulohumeral Rhythm and Force Couples","q":"During full 180-degree overhead shoulder abduction in the scapular plane, what is the classic overall Inman Scapulohumeral Rhythm ratio, and which primary muscular force couple rotates the scapula upward to prevent subacromial impingement?","o":["Purely 180 degrees glenohumeral motion with the scapula completely immobile","Approximately 1:5 ratio (150 degrees scapular rotation + 30 degrees glenohumeral); driven solely by pectoralis minor","Driven by rhomboids and levator scapulae rotating the glenoid downward","Approximately 2:1 ratio (~120 degrees glenohumeral elevation + ~60 degrees scapulothoracic upward rotation); driven by the force couple of Upper Trapezius, Lower Trapezius, and Serratus Anterior"],"a":3,"e":"After an initial ~30-degree setting phase (predominantly glenohumeral), overhead elevation proceeds in an overall ~2:1 ratio (~120 deg glenohumeral + ~60 deg scapulothoracic upward rotation, plus posterior tilt and external rotation). Upper trapezius, lower trapezius, and serratus anterior form the crucial upward-rotation force couple—maintaining subacromial clearance and optimal deltoid length-tension; serratus anterior paralysis (long thoracic nerve) causes medial winging and impingement.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03523","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Vestibular Rehabilitation","topic":"VOR x1 and VOR x2 Gaze Stabilization","q":"A 44-year-old woman 3 weeks after acute left vestibular neuritis complains of oscillopsia (visual blurring/bouncing of street signs when walking or turning her head). How should the physiotherapist prescribe VOR x1 gaze-stabilization adaptation exercises to stimulate central vestibular compensation?","o":["Have the patient focus continuously on a stationary high-contrast letter target held at arm\u0027s length while oscillating her head horizontally and vertically as fast as possible WITHOUT the target blurring (building toward 1-2 minutes, 3-5x/day)","Perform all exercises with eyes tightly closed inside a dark room","Keep her head locked rigidly in a cervical collar while moving only her eyes back and forth","Prescribe long-term daily meclizine vestibular suppressants indefinitely"],"a":0,"e":"Vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) gain adaptation requires \u0027retinal slip\u0027—a mismatch between head motion and eye stabilization while attempting sharp foveal fixation. In VOR x1 viewing, moving the head rapidly side-to-side/up-down while keeping a stationary optotype crisp drives cerebellar error-based synaptic plasticity to recalibrate VOR gain (progressing to VOR x2 where target and head move in opposite directions, and avoiding chronic vestibular suppressant drugs that block neural compensation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03524","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Physical Agents and Modalities","topic":"1 MHz vs 3 MHz \u0026 Continuous vs Pulsed Duty Cycle","q":"When treating a superficial extensor carpi radialis brevis tendon at the lateral epicondyle (~1 cm depth) in the subacute inflammatory/proliferative phase where deep heating is undesired, which therapeutic ultrasound frequency and duty cycle should be selected?","o":["10 MHz diagnostic imaging probe without ultrasound gel","3 MHz frequency (superficial 1-2 cm absorption) with a 20% pulsed duty cycle (non-thermal acoustic streaming and stable cavitation)","3 MHz continuous mode held motionless without coupling gel","1 MHz frequency with 100% continuous duty cycle at 2.5 W/cm2 held stationary over the bone"],"a":1,"e":"Ultrasound attenuation increases with frequency: 3 MHz is absorbed rapidly in superficial tissues (1-2 cm half-value depth), ideal for superficial patellar/Achilles/wrist extensor tendons, whereas 1 MHz penetrates deeper (3-5 cm) for hip/piriformis/deep lumbar musculature. Using a 20% pulsed duty cycle (2 ms ON, 8 ms OFF) allows heat dissipation while delivering non-thermal mechanical acoustic streaming and micro-massage to stimulate fibroblast/collagen healing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03525","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Cardiopulmonary Physiotherapy","topic":"Borg Rating of Perceived Exertion (RPE 6-20)","q":"A 66-year-old post-CABG patient with a fixed-rate VVI pacemaker and high-dose carvedilol shows virtually no heart-rate rise above 72/min during treadmill walking in Phase II cardiac rehabilitation. Why should the physiotherapist guide exercise intensity primarily using the Borg 6-20 Rating of Perceived Exertion (RPE) scale (targeting 11-14) rather than age-predicted Target Heart Rate?","o":["Because age-predicted 220 minus age accurately reflects carvedilol-blunted heart rate","Because Borg RPE 20 means complete resting sleep","Because beta-blockade and fixed-rate pacing blunt physiological chronotropic heart-rate acceleration, whereas Borg RPE (11 \u0027fairly light\u0027 to 14 \u0027somewhat hard\u0027, alongside talk test and BP/symptoms) tracks subjective metabolic effort reliably","Because pacemakers shock the patient whenever RPE exceeds 11"],"a":2,"e":"In patients with chronotropic incompetence, heart transplant (denervated heart), fixed-rate pacemakers, or maximal beta-blocker therapy, pushing treadmill speed/grade to reach an arbitrary % HRmax causes severe overexertion and angina. The original Borg 6-20 scale (designed roughly around HR/10 in healthy adults: 6 = no exertion, 11-14 = moderate aerobic zone ~40-75% VO2R) plus respiratory/BP/symptom monitoring provides safe individualized intensity control.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03526","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Orthopaedic Manual Therapy","topic":"Upper Limb Neurodynamic Test 1 (ULNT1 - Median Nerve)","q":"During an Upper Limb Neurodynamic Test 1 (ULNT1, median nerve bias) in a patient with C6-C7 radicular forearm tingling, the physiotherapist reproduces the patient\u0027s distal forearm paresthesia at 150 degrees of elbow extension. Which remote structural-differentiation maneuver confirms true neural-tissue mechanosensitivity rather than isolated biceps/brachialis muscle tightness?","o":["Rotating the forearm into pronation without neck movement confirms local biceps strain","Asking the patient to hold their breath for 30 seconds","Flexing the patient\u0027s ipsilateral knee increases median nerve symptoms","Asking the patient to side-bend the cervical spine TOWARD the contralateral (opposite) shoulder increases the forearm tingling, while ipsilateral cervical side-bending toward the tested arm decreases it"],"a":3,"e":"In Butler/Shacklock neurodynamic testing (ULNT1 sequence: scapular depression -\u003e 110 deg abduction -\u003e wrist/finger extension -\u003e supination -\u003e shoulder external rotation -\u003e elbow extension), distal forearm symptoms could reflect either median nerve mechanosensitivity or local muscle stretch. Moving a remote spinal joint (contralateral cervical lateral flexion tensions the C5-T1 nerve roots/dura while ipsilateral lateral flexion slackens them) without altering elbow/wrist muscle length differentiates neural involvement cleanly.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03527","course":"MPT - Master of Physiotherapy","subject":"Physiotherapy Research Methodology","topic":"Crossover Randomized Trial Design","q":"In physiotherapy clinical research, why is a 2x2 Crossover RCT design (where each patient receives Intervention A then Intervention B in random order) effective for comparing two transient pain-relief TENS settings in stable chronic knee osteoarthritis, yet UNSUITABLE for comparing ACL surgery versus conservative rehab or acute post-stroke recovery?","o":["Crossover designs require a stable chronic baseline and an adequate washout period to eliminate residual physiological \u0027carryover effects\u0027—conditions like surgery (irreversible/curative) or rapidly recovering acute stroke violate baseline stability and produce permanent carryover","Crossover designs double the required number of patients compared with parallel RCTs","Crossover designs cannot be used with continuous pain scales like VAS","Crossover designs never require randomization or statistical testing"],"a":0,"e":"Because each subject serves as their own control in a crossover trial, within-subject variance drops sharply (requiring smaller sample sizes than parallel-group RCTs). However, crossover designs strictly assume: (1) chronic stable condition across both treatment periods (violated by spontaneous acute stroke recovery or healing), and (2) reversible short-acting interventions separated by a sufficient washout interval so Period 1 produces zero carryover into Period 2 (violated by surgery or long-lasting motor relearning).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03528","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Modern Pharmaceutics \u0026 Quality Assurance","topic":"ICH Q8(R2) Pharmaceutical Development \u0026 ICH Q1A(R2)","q":"Under ICH Q8(R2) Quality by Design (QbD) principles, what is the regulatory significance of establishing an approved Design Space for a tablet manufacturing process, and what long-term stability storage condition applies to India (Climatic Zone IVb) under ICH Q1A(R2) / WHO guidelines?","o":["Design Space applies solely to raw material vendor audits; Zone IVb long-term stability is 40 C +/- 2 C / 75% RH +/- 5% RH","Movement within the approved Design Space is not considered a regulatory post-approval change; Zone IVb long-term stability is 30 C +/- 2 C / 75% RH +/- 5% RH","Design Space replaces analytical method validation; Zone IVb long-term stability is 5 C +/- 3 C","Any movement within the Design Space requires prior regulatory supplement approval; Zone IVb long-term stability is 25 C +/- 2 C / 60% RH +/- 5% RH"],"a":1,"e":"Per ICH Q8(R2), a Design Space is the multidimensional combination and interaction of input variables (material attributes) and process parameters demonstrated to assure quality; operating within the approved Design Space is not considered a change requiring post-approval regulatory filing (only moving outside it initiates a change). For India (WHO Climatic Zone IVb — hot and very humid), long-term stability testing requires 30 C +/- 2 C and 75% RH +/- 5% RH.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03529","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Biopharmaceutics \u0026 Pharmacokinetics","topic":"BCS Class II Drug Dissolution \u0026 IVIVC","q":"A novel immediate-release oral formulation is developed for an anticonvulsant belonging to BCS Class II (low aqueous solubility, high intestinal permeability). Which step governs oral absorption rate, and which In Vitro-In Vivo Correlation (IVIVC) level provides the strongest scientific basis for requesting a biowaiver for minor post-approval manufacturing changes?","o":["Presystemic hepatic CYP3A4 metabolism is the sole rate-limiting step; Level D rank-order correlation justifies biowaivers","Gastric emptying and intestinal permeability are rate-limiting; Level C single-point correlation suffices for all biowaivers","In vivo dissolution is the rate-limiting step; a validated Level A IVIVC (point-to-point correlation between in vitro dissolution and in vivo absorption) supports biowaivers","Paracellular tight-junction diffusion is rate-limiting; multiple-level C without deconvolution replaces Level A"],"a":2,"e":"BCS Class II drugs exhibit high membrane permeability (P_eff) but poor aqueous solubility across physiological pH, making dissolution in GI fluids the rate-limiting step for absorption. Under USFDA/EMA/CDSCO IVIVC guidance, a Level A IVIVC establishes a linear point-to-point relationship between the entire in vitro dissolution curve and the in vivo input/absorption rate profile (via Wagner-Nelson or Loo-Riegelman deconvolution), enabling dissolution testing to serve as a surrogate for bioequivalence.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03530","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis","topic":"Accuracy, Precision \u0026 Matrix Effect","q":"While validating an LC-MS/MS bioanalytical method for quantifying tacrolimus in human whole blood per ICH M10 guidelines, what are the acceptance criteria for accuracy (% nominal) and precision (%CV) at the Lower Limit of Quantification (LLOQ) compared with low, medium, and high Quality Control (QC) samples, and how should matrix effects be evaluated?","o":["LLOQ within +/-20% accuracy and \u003c=20% CV; Low/Med/High QCs within +/-15% accuracy and \u003c=15% CV; evaluate internal-standard normalised matrix factor across multiple individual lots","LLOQ within +/-5% accuracy and \u003c=5% CV; QCs within +/-25% accuracy and \u003c=25% CV; evaluate matrix effect in neat mobile phase only","LLOQ within +/-30% accuracy and \u003c=30% CV; QCs within +/-20% accuracy; ignore ion suppression when using positive ESI","Both LLOQ and QCs require +/-10% accuracy and \u003c=10% CV; matrix effect is assessed using UV photodiode array purity"],"a":0,"e":"Under ICH M10 (and USFDA/EMA) bioanalytical method validation guidelines, within-run and between-run accuracy and precision must be within +/-20% (\u003c=20% CV) at the LLOQ and within +/-15% (\u003c=15% CV) at Low, Medium, and High QCs. In LC-MS/MS, co-eluting endogenous phospholipids/salts cause ion suppression or enhancement (matrix effect), assessed via matrix factor and IS-normalised matrix factor across \u003e=6 independent biological matrix lots (CV \u003c=15%).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03531","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Regulatory Affairs","topic":"New Drugs and Clinical Trials (NDCT) Rules, 2019","q":"Under India\u0027s New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules, 2019 (CDSCO), within what statutory timelines must an investigator report an initial Serious Adverse Event (SAE) occurrence to the Sponsor, Ethics Committee, and Central Licensing Authority (CLA), and subsequently submit the detailed due-analysis report?","o":["Initial SAE report within 72 hours; detailed report only at annual study closeout","Initial SAE report within 7 working days; detailed report within 90 days","Initial SAE report within 14 days; detailed report within 6 months to the State Licensing Authority only","Initial SAE report within 24 hours of occurrence; detailed report after due analysis within 14 calendar days"],"a":3,"e":"Under the Third Schedule / Rule 42 of the New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules, 2019 (MoHFW, India), the investigator must report any SAE (including death) to the Central Licensing Authority (CDSCO), the Sponsor, and the registered Ethics Committee within 24 hours of its occurrence, and forward a detailed report after due analysis within 14 calendar days (for causality and compensation determination).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03532","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Quality Assurance","topic":"Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA)","q":"During an ICH Q9 Quality Risk Management (QRM) exercise for sterile vial lyophilisation, a process engineering team performs Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) on stoppering failure, assigning Severity (S) = 9, Occurrence (O) = 3, and Detection (D) = 4 on 1-to-10 scales. How is the Risk Priority Number (RPN) calculated, and which single risk-reduction action lowers RPN while directly improving patient safety assurance?","o":["RPN = S x O x D = 108; reduce Occurrence via automated stopper-bowl feed control and reduce Detection score via 100% in-line vision/height inspection","RPN = (S x O) / D = 6.75; remove all in-process checks to reduce Detection number","RPN = S + O + D = 16; increase Severity score to 10 without changing process controls","RPN = S x D = 36; ignore Occurrence because aseptic sterility failures are purely statistical"],"a":0,"e":"In FMEA (ICH Q9), Risk Priority Number (RPN) = Severity x Occurrence x Detection = 9 x 3 x 4 = 108. Because Severity of a failure mode (e.g., loss of container-closure integrity/sterility) is intrinsic to patient harm and rarely reducible without redesign, quality risk mitigation targets lowering Occurrence (preventive engineering controls) and lowering Detection score (higher probability of catching defects before batch release via 100% vision/sensor inspection).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03533","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Moore-Flanner Similarity Factor (f2)","q":"When comparing multipoint in vitro dissolution profiles of a reformulated immediate-release test tablet against the reference listed drug under CDSCO/USFDA/EMA BCS-based biowaiver and SUPAC guidelines, what range of the Moore-Flanner similarity factor (f2) confirms dissolution profile similarity (equivalent to \u003c=10% average difference across time points)?","o":["f2 greater than 200","f2 between 50 and 100 (specifically \u003e= 50)","f2 strictly below 25","f2 between 0 and 15"],"a":1,"e":"The Moore-Flanner similarity factor f2 = 50 * log{[1 + (1/n) * sum((R_t - T_t)^2)]^(-0.5) * 100} equals 100 when Test (T_t) and Reference (R_t) profiles are identical. An f2 value between 50 and 100 (f2 \u003e= 50) demonstrates sameness/equivalence of the two dissolution profiles (\u003c=10% mean difference), whereas the difference factor f1 should be 0-15. Note: if \u003e=85% of drug dissolves in \u003c=15 minutes in both batches across pH 1.2, 4.5, and 6.8, profiles are considered similar without calculating f2.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03534","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis","topic":"Forced Degradation (Stress Testing) \u0026 Mass Balance","q":"During development of a stability-indicating RP-HPLC method for a new oral kinase inhibitor, forced degradation is conducted under acid (0.1 N HCl), base (0.1 N NaOH), peroxide (3% H2O2), thermal, and ICH Q1B photolytic stress aiming for 5-20% degradation. Which two analytical verifications confirm that the active pharmaceutical ingredient (API) peak is resolved from all degradants and that no degradants escaped detection?","o":["Melting point capillary comparison and thin-layer chromatography Rf in neat hexane","Measuring only % RSD of six replicate unstressed standard injections","Photodiode Array (PDA) spectral peak purity (purity angle \u003c purity threshold) or LC-MS confirming homogeneity of the API peak, plus mass balance (% assay + % total degradants ~ 100%)","Refractive index calibration at a single time point and Karl Fischer moisture titration alone"],"a":2,"e":"A validated stability-indicating method must separate the intact API from all degradation products and process impurities. Under ICH Q1A(R2) and Q2(R1)/Q2(R2), stress testing (targeting 5-20% API degradation to avoid secondary over-degradation) uses PDA UV spectral homogeneity (or LC-MS) to prove no degradant co-elutes under the API peak, and checks mass balance (sum of remaining % assay and % degradants approaching 100%, accounting for response-factor differences) to verify no volatile/non-UV-absorbing degradants went undetected.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03535","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems (NDDS)","topic":"Elementary Osmotic Pump (OROS)","q":"Why does an Elementary Osmotic Pump (OROS) tablet—comprising a water-soluble drug/osmogen core coated with a rigid semipermeable cellulose acetate membrane containing a laser-drilled delivery orifice—achieve true zero-order drug release largely independent of gastrointestinal pH, food, and peristalsis?","o":["Because drug diffuses passively through a non-porous wax matrix following square-root-of-time Higuchi kinetics","Because the cellulose acetate shell dissolves instantaneously at pH 6.8 in the duodenum releasing the entire dose as a bolus","Because gastric acid hydrolyses the laser-drilled orifice at variable rates proportional to meal fat content","Because water influx across the semipermeable membrane is driven solely by the constant internal osmotic pressure gradient (while excess solid drug maintains a saturated core solution), pumping dissolved drug out at a constant rate dM/dt = (A/h)*k*pi*C_s"],"a":3,"e":"In an osmotic pump tablet, the semipermeable membrane permits water entry but blocks outward diffusion of drug/osmogen; as long as undissolved solid drug/osmogen remains inside the core, internal osmotic pressure (pi) and drug concentration (C_s) stay constant, driving constant-rate (zero-order) expulsion through the micro-orifice until core saturation drops. Because the orifice acts hydrodynamically and cellulose acetate is insoluble across GI pH, release is independent of GI pH, food, and hydrodynamics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03536","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Regulatory Affairs","topic":"CDSCO / USFDA Bioequivalence Acceptance Criteria","q":"In a standard two-period, two-sequence crossover bioequivalence study comparing a generic oral tablet (Test) with the innovator product (Reference) for CDSCO / USFDA regulatory submission, what statistical criterion must be satisfied for the Test/Reference geometric mean ratios of log-transformed C_max and AUC_0-t?","o":["The 90% confidence intervals for both C_max and AUC_0-t geometric mean ratios must lie entirely within 80.00% to 125.00%","The arithmetic mean difference must fall within +/- 50% using a 70% confidence interval","Only T_max needs to match within +/- 20%, while C_max and AUC may vary up to 150%","The 99% confidence interval of urinary pH must fall between 95.00% and 105.00%"],"a":0,"e":"Average bioequivalence (for standard non-narrow-therapeutic-index drugs) requires that the 90% confidence interval (corresponding to two one-sided t-tests at alpha = 0.05) of the geometric mean Test/Reference ratio for log-transformed rate (C_max) and extent (AUC_0-t and AUC_0-inf) of absorption fall completely inside the 80.00%-125.00% regulatory limits (narrower 90.00%-111.11% applies to narrow therapeutic index drugs in several jurisdictions).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03537","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Modern Pharmaceutics","topic":"Crystal Polymorphism \u0026 Dissolution Impact","q":"During preformulation screening of a poorly water-soluble protease inhibitor, Polymorph Form I melts at a higher temperature and shows lower enthalpy of solution than Polymorph Form II. Which statement accurately predicts their relative thermodynamic stability, apparent solubility, and gold-standard analytical confirmation technique?","o":["Form I is the thermodynamically stable polymorph with lower apparent solubility; Form II is the metastable polymorph with higher free energy and faster initial dissolution (risking solution-mediated conversion); confirmed by Powder X-Ray Diffraction (PXRD) and DSC","Polymorphs have identical crystal lattice energies and identical X-ray diffraction patterns, differing only in particle size","Metastable Form II cannot undergo phase transformation during wet granulation or storage","Form I is metastable with higher aqueous solubility; Form II is permanently stable at all temperatures; confirmed solely by Karl Fischer titration"],"a":0,"e":"In monotropic systems, the higher-melting polymorph (Form I) generally has lower Gibbs free energy, stronger crystal lattice packing, greater thermodynamic stability, and lower aqueous solubility/dissolution rate. The metastable polymorph (Form II) exhibits higher free energy and higher initial solubility/bioavailability but can revert to the stable form during wet granulation or shelf storage (as famously occurred with ritonavir). PXRD (unique 2-theta Bragg peaks) and DSC (endothermic/exothermic melting transitions) are definitive solid-state tools.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03538","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Statistical Moment Theory — MRT \u0026 Vss","q":"Following a single 100 mg intravenous bolus dose of a monoclonal antibody fragment, non-compartmental analysis (NCA) yields AUC_0-inf = 50 mg*h/L and AUMC_0-inf = 400 mg*h^2/L. What are the systemic clearance (CL), Mean Residence Time (MRT_IV), and Volume of Distribution at Steady State (V_ss)?","o":["CL = 4.0 L/h; MRT_IV = 8.0 h; V_ss = 32.0 L","CL = 2.0 L/h; MRT_IV = 8.0 h; V_ss = 16.0 L","CL = 2.0 L/h; MRT_IV = 4.0 h; V_ss = 8.0 L","CL = 0.5 L/h; MRT_IV = 0.125 h; V_ss = 0.0625 L"],"a":1,"e":"Using model-independent Non-Compartmental Analysis (statistical moment theory): Clearance CL = Dose / AUC_0-inf = 100 mg / (50 mg*h/L) = 2.0 L/h. Mean Residence Time after IV bolus MRT_IV = AUMC_0-inf / AUC_0-inf = 400 / 50 = 8.0 hours. Volume of Distribution at Steady State V_ss = CL x MRT_IV = 2.0 L/h x 8.0 h = 16.0 L.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03539","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis","topic":"ICH Q3D Elemental Impurities \u0026 ICP-MS","q":"Under the ICH Q3D(R2) Guideline for Elemental Impurities (adopted by CDSCO/IP/USP \u003c232\u003e/\u003c233\u003e), which four highly toxic Class 1 elements MUST be evaluated across all drug products regardless of whether they are intentionally added during synthesis, and which primary instrumental technique quantifies them down to parts-per-billion (ppb) sensitivity?","o":["Platinum (Pt), Palladium (Pd), Rhodium (Rh), and Ruthenium (Ru); quantified by polarimetry","Iron (Fe), Zinc (Zn), Copper (Cu), and Manganese (Mn); quantified by Karl Fischer coulometry","Cadmium (Cd), Lead (Pb), Arsenic (As), and Mercury (Hg); quantified by Inductively Coupled Plasma-Mass Spectrometry (ICP-MS) or ICP-OES","Sodium (Na), Potassium (K), Calcium (Ca), and Magnesium (Mg); quantified by visual thioacetamide precipitation"],"a":2,"e":"ICH Q3D classifies elemental impurities by toxicity and likelihood of occurrence. Class 1 comprises Cd, Pb, As, and Hg—human toxicants with limited or no use in pharmaceutical manufacture that may enter via mined excipients/water—requiring mandatory risk assessment for all oral, parenteral, and inhalation products against Permitted Daily Exposure (PDE) limits. Multi-element trace quantification is performed via ICP-MS (ppb sensitivity) or ICP-OES, replacing non-specific sulphide heavy-metals limit tests.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03540","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems (NDDS)","topic":"Permeation Kinetics Across Stratum Corneum","q":"A transdermal fentanyl matrix patch is formulated to maintain steady-state zero-order delivery across intact skin, where the stratum corneum acts as the principal diffusion barrier. According to Fick\u0027s first law of diffusion [J_ss = (D * K * C_v) / h], how do chemical penetration enhancers such as oleic acid or ethanol primarily elevate transdermal drug flux (J_ss)?","o":["Inhibit dermal capillary blood flow so drug stays trapped inside the epidermis","Ionise the drug completely inside the adhesive to eliminate passive lipophilic diffusion","Thicken the stratum corneum path length (h) tenfold by hyperkeratinisation","Fluidise intercellular stratum corneum lipid bilayers and alter vehicle-skin partitioning to increase drug diffusivity (D) and partition coefficient (K)"],"a":3,"e":"In steady-state transdermal permeation J_ss = (D * K * C_v) / h, the densely packed ceramide-cholesterol-fatty acid lipid lamellae of the stratum corneum limit diffusion. Cis-unsaturated fatty acids like oleic acid insert into and disrupt ordered lipid packing (raising diffusion coefficient D), while cosolvents like ethanol/propylene glycol enhance drug solubility in the stratum corneum (raising partition coefficient K), jointly multiplying steady-state flux without permanently damaging viable epidermis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03541","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Regulatory Affairs","topic":"ICH Q10 Pharmaceutical Quality System","q":"Which four core operational elements constitute the ICH Q10 Pharmaceutical Quality System (PQS) model designed to link regional GMP regulations with continual improvement across pharmaceutical development, technology transfer, commercial manufacturing, and product discontinuation?","o":["Process Performance \u0026 Product Quality Monitoring System; Corrective and Preventive Action (CAPA) System; Change Management System; Management Review of Process Performance \u0026 Product Quality","Drug Pricing Control; Patent Term Extension; Post-Marketing Advertising; Retail Pharmacy Licensing","Raw Material Harvesting; Excise Tax Auditing; Hospital Formulary Bidding; Packaging Artwork Colouring","Phase I Dose Escalation; Animal LD50 Testing; Customs Import Duty Valuation; Pharmacist Registration"],"a":0,"e":"ICH Q10 establishes a harmonised lifecycle Pharmaceutical Quality System augmenting regional GMPs (such as Schedule M, EU GMP, USFDA 21 CFR 211) via four core elements: (1) Process Performance \u0026 Product Quality Monitoring System, (2) Corrective Action \u0026 Preventive Action (CAPA) System, (3) Change Management System, and (4) Management Review, supported by two key enablers—Knowledge Management and Quality Risk Management (ICH Q9).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03542","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Modern Pharmaceutics","topic":"Amorphous Solid Dispersions (ASDs) via HME / Spray Drying","q":"When formulating a poorly water-soluble BCS Class II drug as an Amorphous Solid Dispersion (ASD) using Hot-Melt Extrusion (HME) or spray drying with HPMCAS polymer, what physical role does the polymer play regarding glass transition temperature (T_g) and the \u0027spring-and-parachute\u0027 dissolution profile?","o":["High-T_g polymer immobilises amorphous drug molecules kinetically preventing recrystallisation during storage (Gordon-Taylor T_g elevation above ambient), while maintaining supersaturated luminal drug concentration (\u0027parachute\u0027) after rapid amorphous dissolution (\u0027spring\u0027)","Polymer converts the amorphous drug into its lowest-energy crystalline polymorph inside the extruder","Polymer lowers ASD T_g below 10 C so the tablet melts at room temperature","Polymer forms an insoluble enteric wax shell preventing any drug release in the small intestine"],"a":0,"e":"Amorphous solids lack long-range crystal lattice order, providing a high-energy dissolution \u0027spring\u0027 that boosts apparent solubility—but are thermodynamically prone to crystallisation. Dispersing the API molecularly in a miscible high-T_g hydrophilic polymer (e.g., HPMCAS, PVP-VA) raises overall composite T_g well above storage temperature (typically T_g \u003e= T_storage + 40-50 C) to suppress molecular mobility, while polymer-drug interactions in GI fluid inhibit nucleation/crystal growth (\u0027parachute\u0027 effect) long enough for absorption.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03543","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Flip-Flop Pharmacokinetics","q":"A drug exhibits an apparent elimination half-life of 2 hours after intravenous bolus administration, yet after oral administration of an extended-release matrix tablet its terminal log-linear plasma half-life measures 12 hours. What pharmacokinetic phenomenon explains this discrepancy, and what does the terminal slope after the extended-release tablet actually estimate?","o":["Saturable Michaelis-Menten renal tubular secretion after a single low dose","Flip-flop kinetics (absorption rate-limited elimination, where k_a \u003c\u003c k_e); the terminal slope reflects the slow first-order absorption/release rate constant (k_a) rather than true elimination (k_e)","Enterohepatic recirculation exclusively after IV bolus","Auto-induction of hepatic CYP3A4 doubling clearance on day 1"],"a":1,"e":"In a standard one-compartment oral model, k_a \u003e k_e so the terminal decline slope yields true k_e. However, when sustained-release oral, depot IM, or transdermal formulations intentionally slow drug input so k_a \u003c k_e (\u0027flip-flop kinetics\u0027), drug elimination cannot outpace slow delivery from the dosage form—so the terminal phase slope equals -k_a / 2.303, while the steeper ascending/feathering residual slope reflects k_e. Comparing with IV bolus PK definitively distinguishes flip-flop kinetics from true prolonged elimination.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03544","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis","topic":"Nitrosamine Impurities (NDMA, NDEA)","q":"Following global and CDSCO recalls of sartan, ranitidine, and metformin batches contaminated with N-nitrosodimethylamine (NDMA) and N-nitrosodiethylamine (NDEA), what chemical reaction generates these ICH M7 \u0027Cohort of Concern\u0027 impurities during API/drug-product manufacture, and which analytical platforms achieve sub-ppm/ng-level Acceptable Intake quantification?","o":["Maillard browning between lactose and magnesium stearate at pH 10; quantified by UV-Vis at 254 nm","Hydrolysis of cellulose acetate phthalate; quantified by Karl Fischer titration","Reaction between secondary/tertiary amine precursors (e.g., dimethylamine) and nitrosating agents (sodium nitrite or trace nitrites in excipients/water under acidic conditions); quantified by LC-MS/MS or GC-MS/MS","Oxidation of ascorbic acid by oxygen; quantified by acid-base back-titration"],"a":2,"e":"N-nitrosamines belong to ICH M7(R2) Cohort of Concern potent mutagenic carcinogens requiring nanogram/day Acceptable Intake (AI) limits. They form when vulnerable secondary/tertiary amines (or amide/DMF degradation products like dimethylamine) react with nitrosating species (nitrous acid from trace nitrites in excipients like microcrystalline cellulose/croscarmellose or water under acidic pH). Because limits sit in parts-per-billion (ppb), validated LC-MS/MS (APCI/ESI) or GC-MS/MS (with artefact-free extraction) is required.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03545","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems (NDDS)","topic":"Solid Lipid Nanoparticles (SLNs) vs Nanostructured Lipid Carriers (NLCs)","q":"First-generation Solid Lipid Nanoparticles (SLNs) frequently suffer from low drug loading capacity and spontaneous drug expulsion during shelf storage. How do second-generation Nanostructured Lipid Carriers (NLCs) overcome these two limitations?","o":["By dissolving the drug exclusively in external bulk water outside the lipid core","By increasing lipid crystallinity to 100% pure tripalmitin beta-polymorphs","By replacing all lipids with crystalline silica beads coated in polyvinyl alcohol","By blending solid lipids with spatially incompatible liquid lipids (oils) to create an imperfect, less-ordered crystal matrix with larger molecular voids that accommodate higher drug payload and prevent polymorphic beta-crystal expulsion"],"a":3,"e":"SLNs made purely from solid lipids recrystallise after cooling from high-energy alpha/beta\u0027 forms into a tightly packed, highly ordered beta-polymorph lattice, leaving little space for drug molecules and expelling encapsulated drug on storage. NLCs incorporate a controlled fraction of liquid oil (e.g., Miglyol/oleic acid) alongside the solid lipid (imperfect, amorphous, or multiple O/F/W types), disrupting crystal perfection, boosting drug entrapment efficiency, and preventing storage-induced expulsion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03546","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Regulatory Affairs","topic":"Common Technical Document (CTD) Architecture","q":"Under the ICH M4 Common Technical Document (CTD) format (also adopted by CDSCO SUGAM/eCTD), match each CTD module to its exact contents: 1) Module 1, 2) Module 2, 3) Module 3, 4) Module 4, 5) Module 5.","o":["1-Region-specific administrative/prescribing info (not harmonised CTD core); 2-CTD Summaries \u0026 Quality Overall Summary (QOS); 3-Quality (CMC); 4-Nonclinical Study Reports; 5-Clinical Study Reports","1-Quality (CMC); 2-Administrative Info; 3-Clinical Reports; 4-CTD Summaries; 5-Nonclinical Reports","1-Post-marketing PSURs only; 2-Patent filings; 3-Price ceiling tables; 4-Pharmacopoeia monographs; 5-GMP inspection checklists","1-Clinical Study Reports; 2-Quality (CMC); 3-Nonclinical Reports; 4-Administrative Forms; 5-CTD Summaries"],"a":0,"e":"The ICH M4 CTD pyramid consists of 5 modules: Module 1 contains region-specific administrative forms, cover letters, and labelling (outside the harmonised CTD core); Module 2 contains the CTD Table of Contents, Introduction, Quality Overall Summary (QOS), Nonclinical Overview/Summaries, and Clinical Overview/Summaries; Module 3 houses Quality (Chemistry, Manufacturing \u0026 Controls — drug substance 3.2.S and drug product 3.2.P); Module 4 houses Nonclinical (pharmacology/toxicology) reports; Module 5 houses Clinical study reports and case report forms.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03547","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Modern Pharmaceutics","topic":"Response Surface Methodology — CCD vs Box-Behnken Design","q":"When optimising a sustained-release matrix tablet formulation across three Critical Material Attributes (polymer %, binder %, compression force) using Response Surface Methodology (RSM) in QbD, why might a scientist select a Box-Behnken Design (BBD) instead of a Central Composite Design (CCD)?","o":["Box-Behnken Design estimates only linear main effects with zero quadratic curvature terms","Box-Behnken Design fits quadratic second-order response surfaces using 3 levels (-1, 0, +1) with midpoints of edges and centre points, avoiding extreme simultaneous all-high or all-low corner runs (+1,+1,+1 or -1,-1,-1) that could damage equipment or yield unprocessable formulations","Box-Behnken Design requires 100 times more experimental runs than a full 3^3 factorial","Central Composite Design cannot include axial star points or centre replicates"],"a":1,"e":"Both CCD and Box-Behnken fit second-order polynomial (quadratic) models for Design Space mapping. CCD embeds a 2^k factorial + axial star points (+/- alpha, requiring 5 levels per factor and testing extreme corners). Box-Behnken is a rotatable/nearly rotatable 3-level incomplete factorial placing design points at edge midpoints plus centre replicates—requiring fewer runs for 3 factors (15 vs 20) and crucially avoiding simultaneous extreme corners where all process parameters hit maximum or minimum limits.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03548","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Modern Pharmaceutics","topic":"Heckel Plot Compaction Analysis","q":"During tablet compaction profiling using the Heckel equation ln[1 / (1 - D)] = K*P + A (where D is relative density and P is applied compression pressure), Excipient X exhibits a steep linear slope K yielding a low mean yield pressure (P_y = 1/K), whereas Excipient Y shows a curved initial region with a shallow slope and high P_y. How do Excipient X and Excipient Y consolidate under compression?","o":["Low P_y indicates zero compressibility and severe capping at 5 MPa","Heckel slope K equals tablet disintegration time in minutes","Excipient X (low P_y) consolidates predominantly by soft plastic deformation (e.g., microcrystalline cellulose); Excipient Y (high P_y) consolidates predominantly by brittle fragmentation (e.g., dicalcium phosphate / lactose)","Excipient X undergoes purely brittle shattering; Excipient Y undergoes pure elastic rubbery rebound with zero compaction"],"a":2,"e":"In Heckel compaction analysis, the reciprocal of the linear slope K equals the material\u0027s mean yield pressure (P_y = 1/K). Materials with low P_y (steep slope K, e.g., MCC, starch) deform plastically at low pressures (Type A behaviour, sensitive to dwell time/speed); materials with high P_y and an initial curved particle-rearrangement/fracture region (Type B, e.g., dibasic calcium phosphate, crystalline lactose) consolidate via brittle fragmentation creating fresh clean bonding surfaces relatively insensitive to speed.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03549","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Transporter-Mediated Drug Interactions (OATP1B1 \u0026 P-gp)","q":"In clinical drug-transporter PK studies (ICH M12 / USFDA DDI guidance), co-administering cyclosporine markedly increases systemic plasma AUC of rosuvastatin (~7-fold), whereas rifampin decreases plasma exposure of dabigatran etexilate (~66%). Inhibition/induction of which specific membrane transporters accounts for these two clinically critical interactions?","o":["Cyclosporine inhibits hepatic sinusoidal uptake transporter OATP1B1 (SLCO1B1) and BCRP blocking rosuvastatin hepatic entry; rifampin induces intestinal efflux transporter P-glycoprotein (P-gp / ABCB1) pumping dabigatran etexilate back into the gut lumen","Cyclosporine induces CYP2D6; rifampin inhibits renal OCT2 tubular secretion","Both interactions occur solely via gastric pH elevation","Cyclosporine inhibits intestinal SGLT1; rifampin blocks hepatic albumin synthesis"],"a":0,"e":"Rosuvastatin undergoes minimal CYP metabolism and relies heavily on hepatic sinusoidal OATP1B1/1B3 uptake (plus intestinal/biliary BCRP efflux); cyclosporine potently inhibits OATP1B1 and BCRP, causing massive rosuvastatin plasma accumulation and rhabdomyolysis risk. Dabigatran etexilate is a sensitive substrate of intestinal P-gp (ABCB1) with ~6-7% bioavailability; potent P-gp inducers (rifampin, carbamazepine, St. John\u0027s wort) reduce dabigatran absorption causing thromboembolic failure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03550","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis","topic":"Limit of Quantification (LOQ) Calculation","q":"During HPLC method validation under ICH Q2(R2), linear regression of a low-level calibration curve gives a slope (S) of 0.50 response units per mcg/mL and a standard deviation of the y-intercept/residual response (sigma) of 0.015 response units. Using the ICH calibration-slope formula, what is the calculated Limit of Quantification (LOQ)?","o":["0.10 mcg/mL","3.00 mcg/mL","0.03 mcg/mL","0.30 mcg/mL"],"a":3,"e":"Under ICH Q2(R1)/Q2(R2) validation guidelines based on calibration curve standard deviation and slope: LOD = (3.3 x sigma) / S and LOQ = (10 x sigma) / S. Substituting sigma = 0.015 and S = 0.50: LOQ = (10 x 0.015) / 0.50 = 0.15 / 0.50 = 0.30 mcg/mL. (Meanwhile LOD = (3.3 x 0.015) / 0.50 = 0.099 ~ 0.10 mcg/mL.)","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03551","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis","topic":"Soft Ionisation Techniques — ESI vs MALDI-TOF","q":"When characterising an intact 150-kDa therapeutic monoclonal antibody and its tryptic peptide map by high-resolution mass spectrometry, why are Electrospray Ionisation (ESI) and Matrix-Assisted Laser Desorption/Ionisation (MALDI) classified as \u0027soft ionisation\u0027 techniques, and what distinctive spectral envelope does ESI produce for large proteins?","o":["Both ionise fragile biomolecules without extensive covalent fragmentation; ESI produces a characteristic charge-state envelope of multiply charged ions ([M + nH]^n+) bringing high-mass proteins within standard m/z analyser ranges, whereas MALDI typically yields predominantly singly charged ions ([M + H]+)","Both smash proteins completely into atomic C+ and N+ fragments like Electron Impact (EI) at 70 eV","ESI requires embedding dry crystals in sinapinic acid matrix under high vacuum; MALDI sprays liquid HPLC effluent directly at atmospheric pressure","MALDI measures only refractive index shifts without mass-to-charge separation"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike harsh 70 eV Electron Impact (EI), ESI and MALDI transfer intact thermolabile polar macromolecules into gas-phase ions with minimal fragmentation. ESI sprays liquid analyte (coupling seamlessly to LC) from charged droplets, generating multiply protonated ions ([M + nH]^n+) whose m/z ratios fall neatly inside 500-4,000 m/z even for a 150-kDa antibody (deconvoluted computationally). MALDI co-crystallises sample with a UV-absorbing matrix (e.g., sinapinic acid/CHCA) desorbed by pulsed laser into mainly singly charged [M+H]+ ions suited for Time-of-Flight (TOF) analysers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03552","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems (NDDS)","topic":"Active Ligand Targeting \u0026 pH-Triggered Endosomal Release","q":"A targeted polymeric PLGA-PEG nanoparticle carrying doxorubicin is surface-functionalised with folic acid and uses an acid-labile hydrazone linker for drug conjugation. How do active targeting and intracellular pH-triggered release work sequentially inside folate-receptor-overexpressing solid tumour cells?","o":["Folic acid binds overexpressed tumour folate receptors triggering receptor-mediated endocytosis into acidic endosomes/lysosomes (pH 5.0-5.5), where low pH selectively hydrolyses the hydrazone bond to release free doxorubicin intracellularly while sparing blood (pH 7.4)","Folic acid dissolves red blood cell membranes immediately at pH 7.4 releasing doxorubicin in systemic plasma","Hydrazone bonds require pH 10.5 pancreatic juice for cleavage inside the tumour nucleus","PLGA nanoparticles pass through nuclear pores solely by magnetic resonance guidance without endocytosis"],"a":0,"e":"Active targeting conjugates high-affinity ligands (folic acid, transferrin, RGD peptides, or antibodies) onto stealth nanocarriers to engage tumour-overexpressed surface receptors, driving clathrin/caveolae-mediated endocytosis (bypassing P-gp efflux). Once trafficked into acidic endosomal/lysosomal compartments (pH 4.5-5.5, vs pH 7.4 in blood), acid-labile linkers (hydrazone, acetal, cis-aconityl) rapidly hydrolyse, liberating active doxorubicin locally near its nuclear topoisomerase II target while minimising off-target cardiotoxicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03553","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems (NDDS)","topic":"Aerodynamic Particle Size \u0026 Lung Deposition","q":"When engineering micronised dry powder inhaler (DPI) particles for deep bronchial and alveolar deposition in COPD/asthma or inhaled insulin therapy, what Mass Median Aerodynamic Diameter (MMAD) range optimises deep lung deposition, and what happens to particles \u003e10 micrometres versus \u003c0.5 micrometres?","o":["Aerodynamic particle size has zero impact on lung deposition once lactose carrier is added","MMAD 1-5 micrometres optimises bronchial/alveolar deposition via gravitational sedimentation; \u003e5-10 micrometres impact inertially in the oropharynx/upper airways, whereas \u003c0.5 micrometres remain suspended and are mostly exhaled","MMAD \u003c0.1 micrometres deposits 100% in trachea via inertial impaction","MMAD 50-100 micrometres reaches alveoli deepest; \u003c5 micrometres impact entirely on the tongue"],"a":1,"e":"Pulmonary aerosol deposition depends on aerodynamic diameter (incorporating geometric diameter and particle density: d_a = d_g * sqrt(rho)). Particles \u003e5-10 microns have high momentum and deposit in the oropharynx/larynx via inertial impaction (wasted/swallowed); particles between 1 and 5 microns navigate airway bifurcations and settle in small bronchioles and alveoli via gravitational sedimentation; sub-0.5-micron particles follow air streamlines by Brownian motion and are largely exhaled before settling (hence porous low-density \u0027large\u0027 particles can also achieve 1-5 micron MMAD).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03554","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Regulatory Affairs","topic":"Indian Patents Act, 1970 (2005 Amendment) — Section 3(d)","q":"Under Section 3(d) of the Indian Patents Act, 1970 (as amended in 2005, famously interpreted by the Supreme Court of India in Novartis AG vs Union of India regarding imatinib mesylate beta-crystalline form), when is a patent application for a new salt, ester, polymorph, or metabolite of a known pharmaceutical substance NOT patentable?","o":["Whenever the drug is manufactured in India under Schedule M GMP","Only when the applicant is a domestic Indian generic company","When the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance does NOT result in the enhancement of the known therapeutic efficacy of that substance (preventing patent evergreening)","When the new polymorph demonstrates a statistically significant enhancement in clinical survival and therapeutic efficacy over the parent compound"],"a":2,"e":"Section 3(d) of the Indian Patents Act bars patentability of \u0027the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance which does not result in the enhancement of the known efficacy of that substance\u0027 (explicitly listing salts, esters, ethers, polymorphs, metabolites, pure forms, particle sizes, isomers, and combinations). In the landmark 2013 Novartis Glivec ruling, the Supreme Court clarified that for pharmaceuticals, \u0027efficacy\u0027 means therapeutic efficacy—mere physicochemical improvements (better flow, lower hygroscopicity, or even 30% higher bioavailability without proven superior clinical efficacy) fail Section 3(d), curbing evergreening.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03555","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Regulatory Affairs","topic":"New Drugs and Clinical Trials (NDCT) Rules, 2019","q":"Under India\u0027s New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules, 2019 (G.S.R. 227(E)), what is the statutory timeline for the investigator to report any Serious Adverse Event (SAE) occurring during a clinical trial, and what is the Central Licensing Authority (CDSCO) review timeline for a clinical trial application of a new drug discovered and proposed to be manufactured in India?","o":["SAE initial report within 72 hours; Indian-discovered new drug CT approval timeline is 90 working days","SAE initial report within 14 calendar days only; no deemed-approval timeline exists under NDCT Rules 2019","SAE initial report within 7 calendar days; Indian-discovered new drug CT approval timeline is 180 working days","SAE initial report within 24 hours (detailed analysis within 14 calendar days); Indian-discovered new drug CT approval timeline is 30 working days"],"a":3,"e":"Under the NDCT Rules, 2019 (Third Schedule), the investigator must report any SAE to the Central Licensing Authority (CDSCO/DCGI), the sponsor, and the Ethics Committee within 24 hours of occurrence/awareness, followed by a detailed due analysis report within 14 calendar days. Additionally, to foster indigenous innovation, applications for clinical trials of new drugs discovered, researched, and developed in India (proposed to be manufactured and marketed in India) are processed within 30 working days (with deemed approval if no communication is issued), compared with 90 working days for drugs developed outside India.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03556","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Regulatory Affairs","topic":"Common Technical Document (ICH M4)","q":"In an ICH M4 Common Technical Document (CTD/eCTD) marketing authorization dossier, where are the Quality Overall Summary (QOS) and the detailed Chemistry, Manufacturing, and Controls (CMC) drug product stability and validation reports placed, respectively?","o":["Quality Overall Summary in Module 2 (2.3) and detailed CMC reports in Module 3 (3.2.P)","Quality Overall Summary in Module 3 and detailed CMC reports in Module 5","Quality Overall Summary in Module 5 and detailed CMC reports in Module 2","Quality Overall Summary in Module 1 and detailed CMC reports in Module 4"],"a":0,"e":"The ICH CTD consists of five modules: Module 1 contains region-specific administrative information and prescribing labeling (technically outside the harmonized CTD triangle); Module 2 contains high-level overviews and summaries—specifically Section 2.3 for the Quality Overall Summary (QOS); Module 3 contains full Quality/CMC technical data (3.2.S for Drug Substance and 3.2.P for Drug Product); Module 4 houses Nonclinical Study Reports; and Module 5 houses Clinical Study Reports.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03557","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems","topic":"Stealth (PEGylated) Liposomes","q":"How does surface grafting of DSPE-PEG2000 onto doxorubicin-loaded liposomes (stealth liposomes) prolong systemic circulation half-life and enhance intratumoral accumulation?","o":["The hydrophilic PEG steric barrier reduces plasma opsonin adsorption and mononuclear phagocyte system (MPS/RES) clearance, allowing passive extravasation via leaky tumor vasculature (EPR effect)","PEG covalently binds to red blood cell membranes to prevent renal glomerular filtration of free drug","PEG triggers immediate macrophage phagocytosis in Kupffer cells to release drug directly into the hepatic portal vein","PEG increases zeta potential above +50 mV to promote rapid electrostatic binding to normal vascular endothelium"],"a":0,"e":"Conventional liposomes are rapidly opsonized by plasma proteins and cleared by macrophages of the mononuclear phagocyte system (MPS/RES) in the liver and spleen. Incorporating polyethylene glycol-modified phospholipids (e.g., DSPE-PEG2000) forms a flexible, hydrated hydrophilic corona that sterically hinders opsonin binding, extending plasma half-life dramatically. Long-circulating nanosized liposomes (~80–100 nm) then extravasate selectively through fenestrated, disorganized tumor angiogenic endothelium and accumulate due to impaired lymphatic drainage—the Enhanced Permeability and Retention (EPR) effect.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03558","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems","topic":"Osmotic Pump Drug Delivery (OROS)","q":"An Elementary Osmotic Pump (EOP) tablet delivers a water-soluble drug through a laser-drilled orifice at a constant zero-order rate according to dM/dt = (A/h) × k × (pi_s - pi_e) × C_s. Under physiological GI conditions where internal core osmotic pressure (pi_s) greatly exceeds GI luminal osmotic pressure (pi_e) and solid drug remains in excess inside the core, why is the drug release rate largely independent of gastrointestinal pH and motility?","o":["Drug release depends entirely on enzymatic cleavage by colonic bacterial azoreductases","Water influx is governed by the constant osmotic pressure gradient across a rigid semipermeable cellulose acetate membrane, and drug exits solely as a saturated solution through a fixed orifice","Hydrostatic back-pressure from gastric peristalsis forces drug out faster in the stomach than in the ileum","The semipermeable membrane dissolves rapidly at pH 6.8 to dump the entire core dose at once"],"a":1,"e":"In an Elementary Osmotic Pump (EOP), a rigid semipermeable membrane (e.g., cellulose acetate) permits only water—not ions or hydrogen/hydroxyl gradients at appreciable rates—to permeate at a rate proportional to membrane permeability (k), surface area/thickness (A/h), and osmotic pressure difference (pi_s - pi_e). As long as excess undissolved drug maintains a saturated internal concentration (C_s) and constant osmotic pressure (pi_s \u003e\u003e pi_e), the zero-order delivery rate remains remarkably unaffected by GI pH variations, food, or hydrodynamic agitation/motility.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03559","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Quality by Design \u0026 Process Validation","topic":"Quality by Design (QbD) Elements","q":"Under ICH Q8(R2) Pharmaceutical Development, after defining the Quality Target Product Profile (QTPP) and linking Critical Material Attributes (CMAs) and Critical Process Parameters (CPPs) to Critical Quality Attributes (CQAs) via Design of Experiments (DoE), a multidimensional Design Space is established and approved. What is the regulatory consequence of adjusting a manufacturing parameter within this approved Design Space post-approval?","o":["Only moving outside the Design Space is permitted without regulatory notification","Any adjustment inside the Design Space requires a full prior-approval supplement and Phase III bioequivalence study","Movement within the approved Design Space is not considered a regulatory change requiring post-approval variation filing","Operating within the Design Space exempts the batch from all routine GMP batch-release testing"],"a":2,"e":"ICH Q8(R2) defines Design Space as the multidimensional combination and interaction of input variables (material attributes) and process parameters demonstrated to provide assurance of quality. Working within the approved Design Space is NOT considered a change; thus, operational flexibility inside those validated boundaries does not trigger post-approval regulatory variation filings, whereas movement out of the Design Space is considered a change and initiates a regulatory post-approval change process.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03560","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Quality by Design \u0026 Process Validation","topic":"Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA)","q":"In Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) conducted under ICH Q9 Quality Risk Management for a fluidized-bed granulation process, how is the Risk Priority Number (RPN) calculated, and which Process Analytical Technology (PAT) sensor is commonly used inline for real-time blend/moisture monitoring?","o":["RPN = Severity + Occurrence + Detection; Karl Fischer coulometric off-line titration","RPN = Occurrence × Detection; Dissolution Apparatus II (Paddle)","RPN = (Severity × Occurrence) / Detection; Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC)","RPN = Severity × Occurrence × Detection; Near-Infrared (NIR) spectroscopy"],"a":3,"e":"Under ICH Q9, FMEA quantifies risk for each potential failure mode using the Risk Priority Number: RPN = Severity (S) × Occurrence (O) × Detectability/Detection (D), where higher scores flag high-priority failure modes requiring mitigation. Integrating inline Process Analytical Technology (PAT) tools such as Near-Infrared (NIR) or Raman spectroscopy monitors moisture content and blend uniformity in real time without sampling delays, lowering the Detection score and enabling Real-Time Release Testing (RTRT).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03561","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Bioanalytical Method Validation","topic":"Incurred Sample Reanalysis (ISR)","q":"During an LC-MS/MS bioequivalence study validated under ICH M10 guidelines, 30 incurred subject plasma samples are selected for Incurred Sample Reanalysis (ISR). What is the mandatory acceptance criterion for the ISR pass rate?","o":["At least 67% (two-thirds, i.e., \u003e= 20 of 30) of repeated sample concentrations must be within ±20% of the mean of the original and repeat values","At least 67% of repeated samples must be within ±10% of the original value","100% of repeated sample concentrations must be within ±5% of the nominal QC value","At least 50% of repeated sample concentrations must be within ±30% of the original value"],"a":0,"e":"Under ICH M10 Bioanalytical Method Validation (and USFDA/EMA guidance), Incurred Sample Reanalysis (ISR) verifies assay reproducibility in actual study samples containing metabolites, comedications, or altered matrices not captured by spiked QCs. The percentage difference is calculated as [(Repeat - Original) / Mean] × 100. For chromatographic assays (e.g., LC-MS/MS), at least two-thirds (67%) of the repeated samples must have a percentage difference within ±20% (note: ±30% applies to ligand binding assays).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03562","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Bioanalytical Method Validation","topic":"Matrix Effect Evaluation","q":"While developing an electrospray ionization (ESI) LC-MS/MS method for a lipophilic antihypertensive drug in human plasma, significant ion suppression is observed at the analyte retention time across 6 individual donor lots when using a structural analogue internal standard (IS-normalized Matrix Factor %CV = 24%). Which strategy most effectively resolves this variable matrix effect?","o":["Lower the lower limit of quantitation (LLOQ) acceptance criterion to ±40%","Replace the structural analogue with a Stable Isotope-Labeled Internal Standard (SIL-IS, e.g., 13C or deuterated analogue) and optimize chromatographic separation or sample cleanup (e.g., SPE/LLE)","Use a single pooled plasma lot for matrix effect testing and ignore individual donor variability","Increase the injection volume threefold without changing chromatography or sample preparation"],"a":1,"e":"Under ICH M10, Matrix Effect evaluated across at least 6 independent blank matrix lots requires the %CV of the IS-normalized Matrix Factor (MF) not to exceed 15% (a %CV of 24% fails). A structural analogue often elutes at a slightly different retention time from the analyte and experiences unequal phospholipid-induced ion suppression in ESI. Switching to a co-eluting Stable Isotope-Labeled Internal Standard (SIL-IS, ideally 13C/15N or non-exchanging deuterated analogue) compensates identically for matrix suppression, and improving SPE/LLE cleanup or chromatographic separation from early/late-eluting phospholipids further stabilizes ionization.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03563","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Phytomedicine \u0026 Phytopharmaceuticals","topic":"CDSCO Definition of Phytopharmaceutical Drug","q":"Under India\u0027s Drugs and Cosmetics Rules / NDCT Rules 2019 (CDSCO), how is a \u0027Phytopharmaceutical Drug\u0027 defined with respect to analytical standardization of its botanical extract?","o":["Any classical Ayurvedic churna or asava prepared strictly according to traditional texts without modern marker quantification","A single pure synthetic alkaloid identical to a plant molecule obtained solely by total chemical synthesis","A purified and standardized fraction of an extract of a medicinal plant (or its parts) containing a minimum of four defined bio-active or phytochemical compounds, qualitatively and quantitatively assessed","Any crude dried herb powder encapsulated without standardization or clinical trial data"],"a":2,"e":"CDSCO defines a \u0027Phytopharmaceutical drug\u0027 (inserted via G.S.R. 918(E) and incorporated in the NDCT Rules, 2019) as a purified and standardized fraction with defined minimum four bio-active or phytochemical compounds (qualitatively and quantitatively assessed) of an extract of a medicinal plant or its part, for internal or external use in human beings or animals for diagnosis, treatment, mitigation, or prevention of any disease or disorder, excluding parenteral administration. Unlike classical ASU drugs under Chapter IVA, phytopharmaceuticals follow modern evidence-based NDA/clinical evaluation pathways.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03564","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Phytomedicine \u0026 Phytopharmaceuticals","topic":"Supercritical Fluid Extraction (SFE)","q":"Supercritical fluid extraction (SFE) using carbon dioxide (CO2) is widely employed to isolate thermolabile sesquiterpenes and volatile phytoconstituents without toxic solvent residues. Above what critical temperature (Tc) and critical pressure (Pc) does CO2 reach its supercritical state, and how can its solvating power be tuned to extract moderately polar flavonoids or alkaloids?","o":["Tc = 100.0 °C and Pc = 1.01 bar; add hexane as a non-polar modifier","Tc = -78.5 °C and Pc = 10.0 bar; freeze the extract to sublime CO2","Tc = 212.0 °C and Pc = 221 bar; operate strictly below critical pressure","Tc = 31.1 °C and Pc = 73.8 bar (7.38 MPa); add a polar co-solvent (modifier) such as ethanol and adjust pressure/temperature"],"a":3,"e":"Carbon dioxide has mild critical constants (Tc = 31.1 °C, Pc = 73.8 bar or 7.38 MPa), enabling low-temperature extraction that preserves thermolabile phytoconstituents and leaves zero solvent residue upon depressurization. Because pure supercritical CO2 is relatively non-polar (comparable to hexane), adding 1–10% of a GRAS polar modifier such as ethanol (or methanol/water) while modulating density via pressure and temperature effectively extends extraction selectivity to moderately polar polyphenols, flavonoids, and alkaloids.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03565","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Modern Pharmaceutics","topic":"Dynamic Vapour Sorption (DVS) \u0026 Powder Angle of Repose","q":"During preformulation profiling of a novel hygroscopic API, Dynamic Vapour Sorption (DVS) demonstrates a sharp step-increase in mass at 45% RH with a wide desorption hysteresis loop that retains stoichiometric water down to 15% RH, while its static angle of repose measures 46 degrees. What solid-state and flowability conclusions follow?","o":["The API forms a stoichiometric crystalline hydrate (rather than purely reversible surface moisture adsorption) and exhibits poor cohesive powder flow (angle of repose \u003e 40 degrees) requiring glidants/granulation before tabletting","Desorption hysteresis proves zero water ever entered the crystal lattice","Angle of repose of 46 degrees indicates ideal zero-friction spherical flow","The API is non-hygroscopic and shows excellent free-flowing compressibility (angle of repose \u003c 25 degrees)"],"a":0,"e":"In DVS gravimetric moisture isotherms (0-90% RH), purely non-porous surface adsorption shows smooth reversible uptake/loss without hysteresis, whereas a sharp stoichiometric step uptake followed by retention on the desorption branch (hysteresis) indicates bulk crystal-lattice incorporation (crystalline hydrate formation) or amorphous deliquescence/recrystallisation. On USP \u003c1174\u003e flow scales, angle of repose \u003c25-30 degrees is excellent, 31-35 good, 36-40 fair, and 46-55 degrees denotes poor cohesive flow requiring dry/wet granulation or glidants.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03566","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Modern Pharmaceutics","topic":"Collapse Temperature (Tc) \u0026 Lyoprotectant Selection","q":"When developing a lyophilised cake for a recombinant therapeutic monoclonal antibody, why must primary drying product temperature remain strictly below the amorphous glass collapse temperature (T_c), and why are non-reducing disaccharides (sucrose or trehalose) preferred as lyoprotectants over reducing sugars (lactose or maltose)?","o":["Exceeding T_c causes viscous flow and structural collapse of the porous cake (trapping residual moisture and prolonging reconstitution); sucrose/trehalose form high-T_g amorphous glasses and water-replacement H-bonds without undergoing Maillard browning with protein lysine amino groups","Exceeding T_c turns the amorphous protein into shiny crystalline needles instantly; lactose provides stronger covalent cross-linking","Reducing sugars have infinite T_g above 300 C while trehalose melts at -40 C","Keeping product below T_c prevents sublimation completely during primary drying"],"a":0,"e":"During primary drying sublimation, if the amorphous frozen matrix temperature rises above its collapse temperature (T_c, closely related to T_g\u0027), viscosity drops and the cake structure collapses—reducing surface area for secondary desorption, trapping high residual moisture, and impairing reconstitution. Non-reducing disaccharides (trehalose, sucrose) vitrify into rigid glasses and hydrogen-bond to polar protein surfaces during dehydration (\u0027water replacement hypothesis\u0027) without reactive aldehyde/ketone groups, avoiding Maillard glycation of lysine residues caused by reducing sugars like lactose/glucose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03567","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Allometric Scaling \u0026 PBPK Paediatric Extrapolation","q":"When predicting first-in-human clearance from preclinical animal species or scaling adult doses down to neonates, how does simple empirical allometric scaling (CL = a * BW^0.75) differ from mechanistic Physiologically Based Pharmacokinetic (PBPK) modelling?","o":["Allometric scaling extrapolates clearance empirically across mammalian body weights using a fixed power exponent (~0.75) assuming conserved physiology; PBPK integrates species/age-specific organ blood flows, tissue partition coefficients (K_p), and enzyme/transporter ontogeny (e.g., neonatal CYP3A7-to-CYP3A4 maturation) to predict human and paediatric PK mechanistically","Allometric scaling requires zero animal PK data; PBPK assumes every neonate has identical liver enzyme abundance per kg as a 70-kg adult","Allometric scaling uses exponent 0.00 for all drugs; PBPK uses a single compartment ignoring organs","Both methods forbid incorporation of renal GFR maturation in neonates"],"a":0,"e":"Empirical allometry relates basal metabolic rate and drug clearance across species via power laws (typically exponent ~0.75 for clearance, ~1.0 for volume of distribution), which can fail in neonates/infants where enzyme expression is immature rather than merely body-size-scaled. Bottom-up PBPK models link physiological compartments by actual arterial/venous blood flows and explicitly incorporate developmental ontogeny (e.g., immature UGTs, CYP1A2, CYP3A4, and rising neonatal GFR), making PBPK the regulatory gold standard for paediatric and complex DDI extrapolation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03568","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis","topic":"Chiral HPLC Enantiomeric Excess (% ee)","q":"A chiral normal-phase HPLC method using an amylose tris(3,5-dimethylphenylcarbamate) stationary phase resolves a batch of (S)-amlodipine, giving normalised peak area percentages of 95.0% for the (S)-eutomer and 5.0% for the (R)-distomer. What is the Enantiomeric Excess (% ee) of the batch?","o":["95.0%","90.0%","50.0%","10.0%"],"a":1,"e":"Enantiomeric Excess (% ee) measures chiral purity as the absolute difference between the percentage of major and minor enantiomers: % ee = |% S - % R| / (% S + % R) x 100 = |95.0% - 5.0%| = 90.0%. (Note: chiral purity = 95%, whereas enantiomeric excess = 90% because 5% S pairs with 5% R as 10% racemate leaving 90% excess S.)","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03569","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis","topic":"Inline NIR \u0026 Raman Spectroscopy for Blend Uniformity","q":"Under USFDA/ICH Process Analytical Technology (PAT) frameworks, why are Near-Infrared (NIR) and Raman fibre-optic probes mounted directly onto V-blenders and fluid-bed dryers with Partial Least Squares (PLS) chemometric models rather than relying solely on offline thief-probe UV/HPLC testing?","o":["Chemometric PLS models convert NIR wavelengths directly into mass-spectrometry molecular weights","Offline thief probes never cause powder segregation or sampling errors","NIR/Raman probes provide rapid, non-destructive, real-time inline monitoring of blend uniformity and residual moisture across the moving powder bed—eliminating thief-sampling segregation bias and enabling real-time release testing (RTRT)","NIR/Raman probes destroy 500 g of tablets per second inside the blender"],"a":2,"e":"Traditional manual thief-probe sampling interrupts blending and mechanically disturbs powder beds (causing cohesive/free-flowing segregation artefacts). PAT integrates non-destructive inline/on-line NIR (overtone/combination O-H, N-H, C-H vibrations sensitive to water and API content) or Raman (polarizable C=C/aromatic bands insensitive to water) with multivariate chemometrics (PCA moving-block SD for blend endpoint; PLS for quantitative assay/moisture) to guarantee quality in real time.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03570","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems (NDDS)","topic":"PAMAM Dendrimer Architecture \u0026 Surface Modification","q":"Poly(amidoamine) (PAMAM) dendrimers differ from conventional linear polymers by having a tree-like, monodisperse branched architecture with exponential terminal functional groups at each generation (G3-G5). Why are unmodified amine-terminated cationic PAMAM dendrimers typically PEGylated or acetylated prior to intravenous drug delivery?","o":["Amine-terminated dendrimers have zero water solubility and precipitate as sand","Acetylation permanently seals all internal cavities so zero drug can be loaded","PEGylation converts PAMAM dendrimers into rigid metallic gold nanorods","Dense primary amine (-NH2) cationic surface charges electrostatically disrupt negatively charged erythrocyte and endothelial membranes causing haemolysis and cytotoxicity; PEGylation/acetylation masks cationic charges while preserving multivalent conjugation and internal cavity encapsulation"],"a":3,"e":"Higher-generation amine-terminated PAMAM dendrimers (e.g., G4-NH2 with 64 surface amines) possess high positive charge density at physiological pH 7.4, interacting strongly with anionic phospholipid bilayers/sialic acid residues on RBCs and cells to induce pore formation, haemolysis, and rapid MPS clearance. Surface modification with PEG chains, acetyl groups, or anionic/neutral ligands neutralises excess cationic toxicity, enhances biocompatibility, and extends systemic circulation while retaining internal hydrophobic cavities and multivalent targeting arms.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03571","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems (NDDS)","topic":"In Situ Gelling Ocular Polymer Systems","q":"To prolong precorneal residence time of eye drops beyond rapid nasolacrimal drainage, match each ocular in situ gelling polymer to its physiological sol-to-gel phase-transition trigger upon instillation into the cul-de-sac: 1) Poloxamer 407 (Pluronic F127), 2) Carbopol 940 / Cellulose Acetate Phthalate (CAP) latex, 3) Gellan gum (Gelrite) / Sodium alginate.","o":["1-Thermosensitive gelation triggered by ocular surface temperature (~34-37 C); 2-pH-triggered gelation as acidic drop (pH ~4.5) neutralises to tear fluid pH 7.4; 3-Ion-activated cross-linking triggered by tear Ca2+/Na+/Mg2+ cations","1-UV photopolymerisation; 2-Magnetic field gelation; 3-Hypotonic osmotic lysis","1-Enzymatic lysozyme cleavage; 2-Thermosensitive melting; 3-pH 1.2 gastric acid gelation","1-pH-triggered gelation; 2-Ion-activated gelation; 3-Thermosensitive gelation"],"a":0,"e":"Ocular in situ gelling vehicles are instilled as easy-to-drop low-viscosity liquids that convert rapidly into viscoelastic gels in the conjunctival sac: (1) Poloxamer 407 (PEO-PPO-PEO triblock copolymer) undergoes reverse thermal micellar packing into a gel near 34-37 C; (2) Carbopol (polyacrylic acid) and CAP latex are liquid at ~pH 4.2-5.0 and swell/coagulate into stiff gels upon buffering to physiological tear pH 7.4; (3) Gellan gum (Gelrite) and alginate chains cross-link rapidly upon contacting tear cations (Na+, Ca2+, Mg2+, as in Timoptic-XE).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03572","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Regulatory Affairs","topic":"CDSCO-DBT Guidelines on Similar Biologics (Biosimilars)","q":"Under India\u0027s DBT-CDSCO Guidelines on Similar Biologics (2016) and WHO biosimilar principles, why does regulatory approval of a biosimilar monoclonal antibody follow a \u0027stepwise totality-of-evidence\u0027 comparability pathway—starting with extensive physicochemical and biological characterisation against the Reference Biologic Product—rather than simple generic C_max/AUC bioequivalence alone?","o":["Because CDSCO requires 50-year post-marketing exclusivity before Phase I testing","Because complex recombinant glycoproteins depend on cell-line expression and post-translational modifications (e.g., glycosylation) affecting folding, effector function, and immunogenicity; therefore head-to-head analytical/functional comparability is mandatory first, followed by abbreviated preclinical testing and comparative Phase I PK/PD + Phase III clinical efficacy/safety/immunogenicity trials","Because biosimilars are chemically synthesised identical small molecules requiring zero clinical trials","Because biosimilars only need dissolution testing in pH 6.8 phosphate buffer"],"a":1,"e":"Unlike chemically identical small-molecule generics, biologics (~150 kDa mAbs) are manufactured in living cells whose glycosylation, charge variants, aggregates, and higher-order structure cannot be proved identical by routine bioequivalence alone. DBT-CDSCO Similar Biologic guidelines mandate extensive orthogonal analytical/functional comparability against an approved Reference Biologic first, followed by targeted nonclinical toxicology and comparative clinical PK/PD, efficacy/safety equivalence, and anti-drug antibody (immunogenicity) studies.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03573","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Regulatory Affairs","topic":"Generic Drug Patent Certifications","q":"Under the US Hatch-Waxman Amendments for an Abbreviated New Drug Application (ANDA, 505(j)), which patent certification asserts that the reference product\u0027s Orange Book-listed patent is invalid, unenforceable, or will not be infringed by the generic product, potentially qualifying the first-to-file applicant for 180 days of generic market exclusivity?","o":["Paragraph III certification","Paragraph I certification","Paragraph IV certification","Paragraph II certification"],"a":2,"e":"An ANDA applicant must submit one of four certifications for each Orange Book patent covering the Reference Listed Drug: Paragraph I (no patent information submitted), Paragraph II (patent has expired), Paragraph III (generic approval sought only after patent expiry), or Paragraph IV (patent is invalid, unenforceable, or not infringed). Filing a Paragraph IV certification notifies the NDA/patent holder (triggering a 45-day window to sue for a 30-month FDA approval stay) and awards 180-day generic market exclusivity to the first qualifying applicant.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03574","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Regulatory Affairs","topic":"ICH Q12 Technical and Regulatory Considerations","q":"Under ICH Q12 (Technical and Regulatory Considerations for Pharmaceutical Product Lifecycle Management), which regulatory tool allows a marketing authorization holder to obtain pre-agreement from regulators on the strategy, acceptance criteria, and reporting category for a future anticipated CMC manufacturing change before generating full validation data?","o":["Annual Periodic Safety Update Report (PSUR)","Certificate of Suitability (CEP) for TSE risk only","Investigator\u0027s Brochure (IB)","Post-Approval Change Management Protocol (PACMP)"],"a":3,"e":"ICH Q12 introduces harmonized tools to enhance predictability in post-approval CMC changes: Established Conditions (ECs, legally binding elements requiring regulatory reporting if changed), the Product Lifecycle Management (PLCM) document, and the Post-Approval Change Management Protocol (PACMP). A PACMP provides regulatory predictability by agreeing upfront on the studies, tests, and acceptance criteria needed to implement a future CMC change—allowing the actual change to subsequently be filed under a lower reporting category.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03575","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems","topic":"Solid Lipid Nanoparticles (SLNs) vs NLCs","q":"First-generation Solid Lipid Nanoparticles (SLNs) composed solely of highly purified solid lipids (e.g., tristearin) frequently suffer from low drug payload and drug expulsion during storage. How do second-generation Nanostructured Lipid Carriers (NLCs) solve this physical stability problem?","o":["Blending spatially incompatible solid lipids with liquid lipids (oils) creates an imperfect/disordered crystal lattice with more voids to accommodate drug and inhibit polymorphic transition to the tightly packed beta-form","Replacing all lipids with high-molecular-weight cross-linked gelatin","Increasing lipid crystallinity to 100% pure beta-polymorph prior to homogenization","Removing all surfactants so particles rapidly coalesce into macro-emulsions"],"a":0,"e":"During storage, pure solid lipids in SLNs recrystallize from high-energy alpha/beta\u0027-polymorphs into the thermodynamically stable, highly ordered beta-modification, leaving minimal lattice space and expelling encapsulated drug molecules. NLCs mix solid lipids with liquid oils (e.g., oleic acid, medium-chain triglycerides) in controlled ratios (typically 70:30 to 99.9:0.1), creating an imperfect, amorphous, or multi-compartment lipid matrix that accommodates higher drug loading and prevents beta-crystal drug expulsion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03576","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems","topic":"Permeation Kinetics Across Stratum Corneum","q":"A matrix transdermal patch has an active surface area (A) of 10 cm² and maintains a constant saturated donor concentration (Cv) of 50 mg/mL across the stratum corneum. If the drug\u0027s skin permeability coefficient (Kp) is 0.02 cm/h, what are the steady-state delivery rate (mg/h) and total drug delivered over 24 hours under sink conditions?","o":["Delivery rate = 25 mg/h; total delivered in 24 h = 600 mg","Delivery rate = 10 mg/h; total delivered in 24 h = 240 mg","Delivery rate = 1.0 mg/h; total delivered in 24 h = 24 mg","Delivery rate = 100 mg/h; total delivered in 24 h = 2,400 mg"],"a":1,"e":"Under Fick\u0027s first law at steady state and sink conditions, transdermal flux J = Kp × Cv = 0.02 cm/h × 50 mg/cm³ = 1.0 mg/(cm²·h). Multiplying flux by the active patch surface area (A = 10 cm²) gives the zero-order input rate: dM/dt = J × A = 1.0 mg/(cm²·h) × 10 cm² = 10 mg/h. Over 24 hours, cumulative drug delivered = 10 mg/h × 24 h = 240 mg.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03577","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Quality by Design \u0026 Process Validation","topic":"Screening vs Optimization Experimental Designs","q":"A formulation scientist has 7 candidate excipient and process variables affecting nanoparticle size and entrapment efficiency, and wants to rapidly identify the 3 most critical main-effect factors in minimal experimental runs before building a quadratic contour plot for Design Space optimization. Which two-stage Design of Experiments (DoE) workflow is statistically optimal?","o":["Stage 1: Central Composite Design on all 7 factors first; Stage 2: Plackett-Burman design on 1 factor","Stage 1: Full 3^7 factorial design (2,187 runs); Stage 2: One-Factor-At-a-Time (OFAT) trials","Stage 1: Plackett-Burman (or 2^(7-4) fractional factorial) screening design; Stage 2: Box-Behnken or Central Composite Response Surface Design (RSM) on the 3 critical factors","Stage 1: Uncontrolled trial-and-error batches; Stage 2: Single-point t-test only"],"a":2,"e":"In QbD DoE workflows, screening designs such as Plackett-Burman (resolving main effects of up to k = N - 1 factors in N runs, e.g., 12 runs for 7 factors) or low-resolution fractional factorials efficiently filter out non-significant variables. Once 2–4 critical factors are isolated, Response Surface Methodology (RSM)—using Central Composite Design (CCD) or Box-Behnken Design (BBD, 3 levels per factor without extreme corner combinations)—estimates linear, two-factor interaction, and quadratic curvature terms to map the Design Space.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03578","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Quality by Design \u0026 Process Validation","topic":"Elements of ICH Q10 PQS","q":"Which set comprises the four core elements of the ICH Q10 Pharmaceutical Quality System (PQS) that operate alongside Knowledge Management and Quality Risk Management across the entire product lifecycle?","o":["Only Preclinical Animal Toxicology; Phase I Dose Escalation; Phase III Randomization; Post-Approval Labeling","Raw Material Pricing; Sales Force Training; Advertising Approval; Patent Litigation","Exclusively Sterility Testing; Endotoxin Screening; Visual Inspection; Warehouse Pest Control","Process Performance and Product Quality Monitoring System; Corrective and Preventive Action (CAPA) System; Change Management System; Management Review"],"a":3,"e":"ICH Q10 augments regional GMP regulations by defining a comprehensive Pharmaceutical Quality System spanning pharmaceutical development, technology transfer, commercial manufacturing, and product discontinuation. Built on the two enablers—Knowledge Management and Quality Risk Management—ICH Q10 establishes four core PQS elements: (1) Process Performance and Product Quality Monitoring System, (2) Corrective and Preventive Action (CAPA) System, (3) Change Management System, and (4) Management Review of process performance and product quality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03579","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Bioanalytical Method Validation","topic":"Carryover and Dilution Integrity","q":"During LC-MS/MS bioanalytical method validation under ICH M10, a blank human plasma sample is injected immediately after the Upper Limit of Quantitation (ULOQ) calibration standard to evaluate autosampler carryover. What are the maximum permissible carryover responses for the analyte and the internal standard (IS) in that blank sample?","o":["Analyte response \u003c= 20% of the LLOQ response, and IS response \u003c= 5% of the average IS response","Analyte response \u003c= 5% of the ULOQ response, and IS response \u003c= 20% of the LLOQ response","Analyte response must be zero counts per second (0% of LLOQ) with no quantitative threshold permitted","Analyte response \u003c= 50% of the ULOQ response, and IS response \u003c= 25% of the average IS response"],"a":0,"e":"Under ICH M10 Bioanalytical Method Validation, carryover—residual analyte or IS transferred from a high-concentration sample into subsequent injections—is evaluated by injecting blank matrix samples immediately after ULOQ samples. To ensure accuracy at the low end of the curve, carryover in the blank must not exceed 20% of the analyte response at the Lower Limit of Quantitation (LLOQ) and must not exceed 5% of the response of the internal standard (IS).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03580","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Bioanalytical Method Validation","topic":"Analyte Stability Validation","q":"When validating biological matrix stability of an analyte in human plasma under ICH M10, which QC concentration levels are tested across freeze-thaw (minimum 3 cycles), bench-top, autosampler, and long-term freezer stability experiments, and what is the acceptance criterion against nominal concentration?","o":["Unspiked blank plasma only; acceptance within ±25% of peak area","Low QC (LQC) and High QC (HQC) levels; mean measured concentration at each level must be within ±15% of nominal concentration (and %CV \u003c= 15%)","Mid QC (MQC) only; acceptance within ±5% of nominal concentration","LLOQ and ULOQ levels only; acceptance within ±30% of nominal concentration"],"a":1,"e":"Under ICH M10, stability evaluations in biological matrix (freeze-thaw for at least 3 cycles, short-term bench-top, processed/autosampler, and long-term frozen storage covering the entire clinical sample storage duration) are performed in at least triplicate at two concentration levels: Low QC (LQC, within 3× LLOQ) and High QC (HQC, \u003e= 75% ULOQ), quantified against freshly prepared calibration standards. Mean concentration at each level must be within ±15% of nominal with precision %CV \u003c= 15%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03581","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Phytomedicine \u0026 Phytopharmaceuticals","topic":"HPTLC Fingerprinting and Densitometry","q":"A researcher standardizes a polyherbal extract rich in quercetin and rutin on silica gel 60 F254 HPTLC plates. After chromatographic development, why is HPTLC preferred for routine botanical fingerprint authentication, and which post-chromatographic spray reagent yields bright yellow/orange-red fluorescence under UV 366 nm for flavonoids?","o":["HPTLC destroys the sample before separation; Biuret reagent","HPTLC requires 2 hours per single sample sequentially in a pressurized capillary; Dragendorff\u0027s reagent","HPTLC enables side-by-side simultaneous profiling of multiple samples and reference markers on one plate with automated densitometry; Natural Products–Polyethylene Glycol (NP-PEG) reagent","HPTLC operates solely in gas phase at 300 °C; Ninhydrin reagent"],"a":2,"e":"High-Performance Thin Layer Chromatography (HPTLC) is the premier fingerprint authentication tool in pharmacopoeias (IP, USP, Ph. Eur.) because multiple botanical batches, adulterants, and reference markers can be separated in parallel under identical mobile-phase conditions on a single plate, scanned quantitatively by UV/fluorescence densitometry, and visualized with selective reagents. Natural Products reagent (2-aminoethyl diphenylborinate) followed by PEG-4000 produces intense yellow, orange, or green fluorescence under UV 366 nm specifically for flavonoids and phenolic acids.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03582","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Phytomedicine \u0026 Phytopharmaceuticals","topic":"Pharmacokinetic Herb-Drug Interactions","q":"A 42-year-old renal transplant recipient maintained stably on cyclosporine and oral contraceptives begins self-medicating with over-the-counter St. John\u0027s Wort (Hypericum perforatum) extract for mild depression. Three weeks later, trough cyclosporine levels plunge precipitously, risking acute allograft rejection. Which phytoconstituent and molecular mechanism explain this interaction?","o":["Rutin chelates cyclosporine in the gastric lumen into an insoluble precipitate","Hypericin irreversibly inhibits renal organic cation transporters (OCT2)","Chlorogenic acid inhibits CYP2C9, accelerating renal excretion of ethinylestradiol","Hyperforin activates the pregnane X receptor (PXR), potently inducing hepatic/intestinal CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein (ABCB1) efflux"],"a":3,"e":"Hyperforin, a phloroglucinol constituent of St. John\u0027s Wort (Hypericum perforatum), is a high-affinity ligand for the nuclear pregnane X receptor (PXR). PXR activation upregulates transcription of CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and intestinal/hepatic P-glycoprotein (P-gp), dramatically accelerating first-pass and systemic clearance of cyclosporine, tacrolimus, oral contraceptives (breakthrough bleeding/unintended pregnancy), warfarin, and protease inhibitors—while also posing serotonin syndrome risk if combined with SSRIs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03583","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Modern Pharmaceutics","topic":"Batch Definition \u0026 Residence Time Distribution (RTD)","q":"Under the ICH Q13 Guideline for Continuous Manufacturing of Drug Substances and Drug Products, how is a \u0027Batch\u0027 defined in an integrated continuous direct-compression line, and what engineering parameter enables precise diversion of a transient off-spec powder disturbance without discarding the entire campaign?","o":["A batch may be defined flexibly by quantity of output material, run time/duration, or quantity of input material; Residence Time Distribution (RTD) modelling tracks material propagation through unit operations to isolate and divert only the affected time-window slice","Every transient 5-second feeder disturbance mandates scrapping 100% of the entire campaign","Continuous lines forbid PAT sensors and rely solely on end-of-week composite sampling","A batch must strictly equal 100,000 tablets regardless of continuous operation; RTD measures tablet coating gloss"],"a":0,"e":"ICH Q13 harmonises regulatory expectations for continuous manufacturing: unlike batch processes bounded by fixed vessel capacity, a continuous \u0027batch\u0027 can be defined by target output mass/units, processing time interval, or input lot mass at controlled flow rate. Characterising the system\u0027s Residence Time Distribution (RTD)—combined with inline PAT sensors and automated reject-chutes—allows engineers to predict exactly when a transient upstream feeder/blend excursion reaches the tablet press and divert only that bounded segment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03584","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Modern Pharmaceutics","topic":"Floating, Mucoadhesive \u0026 Swelling Gastroretentive Tablets","q":"Hydrodynamically Balanced Systems (HBS / floating gastroretentive tablets) maintain a bulk density below 1.004 g/cm3 to remain buoyant on gastric contents for prolonged periods. Which category of drugs benefits MOST from Gastroretentive Drug Delivery Systems (GRDDS), and why does a drug absorbed solely in the distal colon make a poor GRDDS candidate?","o":["Drugs absorbed uniformly across the entire GI tract including colon with 100% bioavailability","Drugs with a narrow absorption window in the stomach/proximal small intestine (e.g., riboflavin, levodopa, metformin, furosemide) or local gastric action (anti-H. pylori clarithromycin/amoxicillin); colon-only absorbed drugs suffer delayed delivery and hepatic/acid exposure if retained in the stomach","Drugs having plasma half-lives \u003e 72 hours (e.g., amiodarone)","Drugs rapidly destroyed by gastric pepsin/acid (e.g., insulin, omeprazole) benefit most from prolonged gastric retention"],"a":1,"e":"GRDDS (floating low-density \u003c1.004 g/cm3, effervescent CO2-generating, raft-forming, swelling/unfolding, or mucoadhesive systems) prolong gastric residence past normal ~2-3 h emptying to feed drugs either acting locally in the stomach (antacids, anti-H. pylori antibiotics, misoprostol) or possessing a narrow upper-GI carrier-mediated absorption window (riboflavin, levodopa, baclofen, metformin, ciprofloxacin). Acid-labile drugs (omeprazole, peptides) and colon-targeted drugs (mesalamine) are UNSUITABLE for GRDDS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03585","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Calculating Maintenance Dose Rate from Vmax and Km","q":"A patient receiving oral phenytoin (100% bioavailable free acid equivalent) has an estimated maximum metabolic elimination rate V_max = 600 mg/day and Michaelis constant K_m = 5.0 mg/L. To achieve a desired steady-state plasma concentration (C_ss) of 10.0 mg/L, what daily maintenance dosing rate (mg/day) is required?","o":["300 mg/day","200 mg/day","400 mg/day","600 mg/day"],"a":2,"e":"For drugs obeying capacity-limited Michaelis-Menten elimination, at steady state input rate equals elimination rate: Dose Rate (DR) = (V_max x C_ss) / (K_m + C_ss). Substituting V_max = 600 mg/day, K_m = 5.0 mg/L, and C_ss = 10.0 mg/L: DR = (600 x 10.0) / (5.0 + 10.0) = 6,000 / 15.0 = 400 mg/day.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03586","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis","topic":"Size-Exclusion Chromatography (SEC-HPLC) of Monoclonal Antibodies","q":"When monitoring soluble protein aggregation of a 150-kDa monoclonal antibody (mAb) batch by Size-Exclusion Chromatography (SEC-HPLC), in what exact elution order do High-Molecular-Weight (HMW) dimers/aggregates (~300+ kDa), intact mAb monomer (150 kDa), and Low-Molecular-Weight (LMW) Fab/Fc fragments (~50 kDa) emerge from the column, and why?","o":["LMW fragments elute FIRST and HMW aggregates elute LAST because SEC works purely by hydrophobic reversed-phase partitioning","All three species co-elute identically at the void volume unless gradient acetonitrile is added","Intact monomer elutes strictly after small urea buffer salts","HMW aggregates elute FIRST, followed by intact mAb monomer, and LMW fragments elute LAST — because larger molecules are excluded from porous stationary-phase beads and travel only through the smaller interstitial void volume"],"a":3,"e":"SEC-HPLC separates macromolecules purely by hydrodynamic radius under gentle non-denaturing isocratic aqueous buffers (zero adsorption ideally). Very large HMW aggregates (\u003e300 kDa) exceed the bead pore diameter, stay in the mobile interstitial volume (V_0), and elute earliest; intermediate intact 150-kDa monomer samples partial pores; small LMW fragments (~50 kDa) and buffer salts diffuse deeply into all internal pore volume (V_0 + V_i) and elute last.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03587","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis","topic":"Karl Fischer Titration — Coulometric vs Volumetric","q":"When determining trace residual moisture (target \u003c 0.5-1.0% w/w) inside lyophilised biologic vials versus higher moisture content (e.g., 5-15% in hydrated lactose monohydrate), how does Coulometric Karl Fischer titration differ from Volumetric Karl Fischer titration?","o":["Coulometric KF generates exact stoichiometric iodine (I2) electrochemically in situ at the generator anode (quantifying microgram/ppm moisture down to \u003c 0.1% per Faraday\u0027s law), whereas Volumetric KF adds standardised I2-methanolic burette titrant suited for higher milligram-level water (0.1-100%)","Volumetric KF generates I2 electrically without burette; Coulometric KF measures weight loss in a 105 C oven","Karl Fischer titration cannot detect crystal water of hydration","Both KF methods measure total carbon dioxide released from carbonates"],"a":0,"e":"Karl Fischer chemistry oxidises SO2 by I2 in methanol/imidazole base stoichiometrically with H2O (1:1 mol ratio in modern methanol systems). In Volumetric KF, calibrated burette addition of I2 reagent accurately quantifies larger water amounts (~1-100 mg, 0.1-100%). In Coulometric KF, anodic oxidation of iodide generates exact microlitres of I2 stoichiometrically from electric charge (10.71 C per mg H2O per Faraday\u0027s law), enabling ultra-sensitive ppm/microgram moisture testing in sealed lyophilised vials and anhydrous oils.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03588","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems (NDDS)","topic":"Ionisable Lipid Nanoparticles (LNPs)","q":"Why do clinically approved mRNA and siRNA Lipid Nanoparticles (LNPs, such as patisiran and mRNA vaccines) rely critically on \u0027ionisable cationic lipids\u0027 (apparent pKa ~6.2-6.5) rather than permanently quaternary-charged cationic lipids like DOTAP?","o":["Ionisable lipids are positively charged at acidic formulation pH (~4) to encapsulate polyanionic mRNA, remain nearly neutral at physiological blood pH 7.4 (minimising haemolysis, complement activation, and rapid MPS clearance), and re-protonate inside acidic endosomes (pH ~5.5) to fuse with anionic endosomal membranes and trigger cytosolic mRNA escape","Ionisable lipids prevent endocytosis completely so mRNA acts exclusively extracellularly","Permanently charged quaternary lipids remain completely uncharged at all pH values","Ionisable lipids convert mRNA directly into double-stranded chromosomal DNA inside plasma"],"a":0,"e":"Permanently charged cationic lipids cause severe systemic toxicity, RBC aggregation, and rapid immune clearance in circulation. Ionisable lipids (e.g., DLin-MC3-DMA, ALC-0315, SM-102) with pKa ~6.2-6.5 solve this paradox: protonated during ethanol-dilution microfluidic mixing at pH 4 to complex negatively charged mRNA efficiently; largely neutral at blood pH 7.4 for safe biocompatibility; and protonated once again inside maturing endosomes (pH 5.0-6.0) where they pair with endogenous anionic endosomal lipids (forming non-bilayer hexagonal H_II phases) to rupture the endosome and release intact mRNA to ribosomes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03589","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems (NDDS)","topic":"Niosomes vs Liposomes","q":"Compared with conventional phospholipid liposomes, what are the principal bilayer constituents of Niosomes, and what pharmaceutical manufacturing advantages do niosomes provide?","o":["Polyvinyl chloride and sodium hydroxide; melt permanently at 37 C","Non-ionic surfactants (e.g., alkyl/dialkyl polyglycerol ethers, Spans, Tweens) combined with cholesterol; offer superior chemical resistance against oxidative rancidity/hydrolysis, lower cost, and simpler storage without inert nitrogen blanketing","Pure phospholipids without surfactants; undergo rapid double-bond peroxidation in air","Cationic proteins and DNA plasmids; require -80 C storage universally"],"a":1,"e":"Niosomes are non-ionic surfactant vesicles formed by hydrating mixtures of non-ionic surfactants (such as Span 60, Tween 60, Brij, or polyglycerol alkyl ethers) with cholesterol (which modulates bilayer rigidity/permeability) and optional charge inducers (dicetyl phosphate/stearylamine). Replacing unsaturated ester-linked natural phospholipids with chemically uniform saturated non-ionic surfactants reduces oxidative degradation, variability, and raw-material cost compared with classical liposomes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03590","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Regulatory Affairs","topic":"Medical Devices Rules, 2017 (CDSCO)","q":"Under India\u0027s Medical Devices Rules, 2017 (framed under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act and administered via CDSCO SUGAM), match each risk class to its representative regulated medical device: 1) Class A (Low risk), 2) Class B (Low-moderate risk), 3) Class C (Moderate-high risk), 4) Class D (High risk).","o":["1-Diagnostic MRI scanners; 2-Artificial heart valves; 3-Cotton gauze; 4-Digital thermometers","1-Haemodialysers; 2-Pacemakers; 3-Alcohol swabs; 4-Tongue depressors","1-Alcohol swabs, surgical dressings \u0026 clinical thermometers; 2-Hypodermic needles, suction pumps \u0026 Foley catheters; 3-Orthopaedic bone fixation plates, haemodialysers \u0026 ventilators; 4-Implantable cardiac pacemakers, heart valves \u0026 drug-eluting coronary stents","1-Drug-eluting coronary stents; 2-Thermometers; 3-Bandages; 4-Hypodermic needles"],"a":2,"e":"India\u0027s Medical Devices Rules, 2017 classify devices into four progressive risk tiers: Class A (non-invasive low-risk items like bandages, thermometers, tongue depressors—regulated by SLA), Class B (low-moderate invasive/active devices like needles, catheters, BP monitors—SLA), Class C (moderate-high risk long-term/life-supporting devices like ventilators, bone plates, dialysers, contact lenses—CLA/CDSCO), and Class D (highest-risk life-sustaining/central-circulatory implants like pacemakers, heart valves, drug-eluting stents—CLA/CDSCO).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03591","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Regulatory Affairs","topic":"Similar Biologics (Biosimilars) in India","q":"Under India\u0027s Guidelines on Similar Biologics (CDSCO/DBT, 2016) and global ICH/EMA/FDA biosimilar frameworks, why is a simple 2-way bioequivalence study (AUC/Cmax alone) insufficient to approve a biosimilar monoclonal antibody, unlike a small-molecule generic?","o":["Because biosimilars are regulated exclusively under the Food Safety and Standards Act (FSSAI)","Because CDSCO bans all clinical trials for recombinant proteins manufactured in India","Because monoclonal antibodies have zero therapeutic activity unless chemically identical by NMR melting point","Because complex recombinant biologics exhibit microheterogeneity (glycosylation, higher-order folding, charge variants) sensitive to cell line and bioprocess changes, requiring stepwise analytical, functional, preclinical, and comparative clinical PK/PD/efficacy/immunogenicity comparability (\u0027totality of evidence\u0027)"],"a":3,"e":"Unlike chemically synthesized small molecules where exact structural identity is verified analytically and bioequivalence confirms clinical interchangeability, biologics (e.g., mAbs, insulins, filgrastim) are large folded proteins produced in living cells with inherent post-translational microheterogeneity (glycoforms, deamidation, aggregation). Therefore, CDSCO/DBT Guidelines on Similar Biologics require a rigorous stepwise \u0027totality of evidence\u0027 comparability exercise against an approved Reference Biologic Product spanning orthogonal physicochemical/biological characterization, nonclinical toxicology, and comparative human PK/PD, clinical efficacy/safety, and anti-drug antibody (ADA) immunogenicity studies.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03592","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Regulatory Affairs","topic":"ICH Q3D Elemental Impurities \u0026 ICH M7","q":"Under ICH Q3D(R2) Elemental Impurities, which four Class 1 human toxicants must always be evaluated across all drug product risk assessments regardless of intentional addition, and what is the lifetime daily Threshold of Toxicological Concern (TTC) for a mutagenic impurity under ICH M7?","o":["Class 1 elements: Cadmium (Cd), Lead (Pb), Arsenic (As), and Mercury (Hg); ICH M7 lifetime TTC = 1.5 µg/day","Class 1 elements: Chromium, Copper, Lithium, and Tin; ICH M7 lifetime TTC = 1.5 mg/day","Class 1 elements: Sodium, Potassium, Calcium, and Magnesium; ICH M7 lifetime TTC = 150 mg/day","Class 1 elements: Platinum, Palladium, Rhodium, and Ruthenium; ICH M7 lifetime TTC = 500 µg/day"],"a":0,"e":"ICH Q3D categorizes elemental impurities by toxicity (Permitted Daily Exposure, PDE) and likelihood of occurrence: Class 1 consists of the \u0027big four\u0027 severe human toxicants—Cd, Pb, As, and Hg—which have limited or no pharmaceutical utility and must ALWAYS be evaluated in the risk assessment across oral, parenteral, and inhalation routes (replacing legacy wet-chemical heavy metals sulfide tests). Separately, ICH M7 controls DNA-reactive (mutagenic) impurities via a conservative lifetime Threshold of Toxicological Concern (TTC) of 1.5 µg/day (corresponding to \u003c1 in 100,000 excess lifetime cancer risk), with higher less-than-lifetime (LTL) limits for shorter treatment durations.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03593","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems","topic":"Floating and Mucoadhesive Systems","q":"Gastroretentive drug delivery systems (GRDDS, such as floating effervescent matrices or high-density/mucoadhesive tablets) prolong gastric residence time above the pyloric sphincter. Which drug property makes an active pharmaceutical ingredient an IDEAL candidate for GRDDS?","o":["Drugs unstable in acidic gastric pH (e.g., erythromycin base, omeprazole)","Drugs absorbed primarily in the stomach or proximal small intestine (narrow absorption window) or acting locally in the stomach (e.g., levodopa, riboflavin, furosemide, or clarithromycin for H. pylori)","Drugs intended solely for systemic delivery across the rectal mucosa","Drugs undergoing extensive first-pass metabolism in the gastric mucosa with primary colonic absorption"],"a":1,"e":"Prolonging gastric residence via buoyant/floating (bulk density \u003c 1.004 g/cm³ of gastric fluid), swelling/unfolding, or mucoadhesive polymers enhances bioavailability specifically for drugs with a narrow absorption window in the upper GI tract (duodenum/jejunum, e.g., levodopa, metformin, riboflavin, furosemide), drugs poorly soluble at alkaline intestinal pH (weak bases like verapamil/diazepam), drugs acting locally in the stomach (anti-H. pylori antibiotics, antacids), and drugs degraded in the colon (e.g., captopril, ranitidine).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03594","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems","topic":"Stimuli-Responsive In Situ Hydrogels","q":"To overcome rapid precorneal drainage of conventional eye drops without blurring vision upon instillation, an ophthalmic formulation uses Poloxamer 407 (Pluronic F127) or Gellan Gum (Gelrite). By what respective physical triggers do Poloxamer 407 and Gellan Gum convert from a drop-ready liquid sol into a mucoadhesive gel on the ocular surface?","o":["Poloxamer 407 gels thermally via hydrophobic micellar packing when warmed from ~25 °C to ocular temperature (~34–37 °C); Gellan Gum cross-links via divalent/monovalent tear cations (Ca2+, Mg2+, Na+)","Poloxamer 407 gels upon UV light exposure; Gellan Gum gels only at pH \u003c 1.0","Poloxamer 407 melts from solid to liquid at 37 °C; Gellan Gum evaporates ethanol instantaneously","Both polymers precipitate irreversibly as opaque crystalline plaques due to tear lysozyme"],"a":0,"e":"In situ gelling ophthalmic systems are instilled as low-viscosity aqueous solutions for accurate drop dosing and undergo rapid sol-to-gel transition in the cul-de-sac to prolong precorneal residence time. Poloxamer 407 (PEO-PPO-PEO triblock copolymer) exhibits reverse thermal gelation: as temperature rises toward ~34–37 °C, dehydration of the central hydrophobic PPO block drives micelle formation and tight cubic packing into a clear gel. Gellan gum (anionic polysaccharide) undergoes ion-activated gelation triggered by tear-fluid Ca2+/Mg2+/Na+ cations (while Carbopol gels via pH rise from ~4.0 to tear pH 7.4).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03595","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Quality by Design \u0026 Process Validation","topic":"USFDA 2011 / EU Annex 15 Three-Stage Validation","q":"Under the modern lifecycle Process Validation framework (USFDA 2011 Guidance / EU GMP Annex 15 / WHO TRS), what are the exact three sequential stages of Process Validation?","o":["Stage 1: Raw Material Purchase; Stage 2: Packaging Only; Stage 3: Stability Testing Only","Stage 1: Phase I Clinical Trial; Stage 2: Phase III Trial; Stage 3: Patent Expiry","Stage 1: Process Design; Stage 2: Process Qualification (Facility/Equipment Qualification + PPQ); Stage 3: Continued Process Verification (CPV)","Stage 1: Retrospective 3-Batch Approval Only; Stage 2: Marketing Launch; Stage 3: Product Recall"],"a":2,"e":"Modern regulatory process validation aligns with ICH Q8/Q9/Q10 as a continuous lifecycle rather than a one-time three-batch check: Stage 1 (Process Design) defines the commercial manufacturing process and control strategy from R\u0026D/QbD knowledge; Stage 2 (Process Qualification) verifies utilities/equipment (IQ/OQ/PQ) and executes Process Performance Qualification (PPQ) batches demonstrating reproducible commercial capability before distribution; Stage 3 (Continued Process Verification, CPV) provides ongoing statistical assurance via trend monitoring, SPC control charts, and CAPA that the process remains in a state of control throughout commercial life.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03596","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Quality by Design \u0026 Process Validation","topic":"Process Capability Indices (Cp and Cpk)","q":"During Continued Process Verification (Stage 3 CPV) of tablet assay (specification limits: LSL = 95.0%, USL = 105.0%), historical batch data yield a process mean of 102.0% and standard deviation (sigma) of 1.0%. What are the Process Capability Index (Cp) and Centered Process Capability Index (Cpk), respectively?","o":["Cp = 2.33 and Cpk = 2.33","Cp = 0.50 and Cpk = 0.33","Cp = 1.00 and Cpk = 1.67","Cp = 1.67 and Cpk = 1.00 (indicating adequate spread potential, but off-center drift toward the upper limit)"],"a":3,"e":"Potential capability Cp = (USL - LSL) / (6 · sigma) = (105.0 - 95.0) / (6 × 1.0) = 10.0 / 6.0 = 1.67. Actual centered capability Cpk = min[(USL - mean) / (3 · sigma), (mean - LSL) / (3 · sigma)] = min[(105.0 - 102.0) / 3.0, (102.0 - 95.0) / 3.0] = min[1.00, 2.33] = 1.00. Because Cpk (1.00) \u003c Cp (1.67) and falls below the industry target (\u003e= 1.33), the process is shifted off-center toward USL and needs centering toward 100.0%.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03597","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Bioanalytical Method Validation","topic":"Calibration Curve \u0026 Study Run QC Criteria","q":"For an LC-MS/MS analytical run analyzing clinical bioequivalence samples under ICH M10 guidelines, what are the mandatory acceptance criteria for the non-zero calibration standards and Quality Control (QC) samples?","o":["At least 75% of non-zero calibrators (minimum 6 levels) within ±15% of nominal (±20% at LLOQ); AND at least 67% of all QCs and \u003e= 50% of QCs at each level (LQC, MQC, HQC) within ±15% of nominal","100% of calibrators within ±5%; AND 100% of QCs within ±5%","At least 50% of calibrators within ±25%; AND at least 50% of total QCs within ±30%","Only a correlation coefficient R² \u003e= 0.90 is checked without examining back-calculated calibrators or QCs"],"a":0,"e":"Under ICH M10, a chromatographic calibration curve requires a blank, a zero sample (blank + IS), and at least 6 non-zero calibrators covering LLOQ to ULOQ. At least 75% of non-zero standards (minimum 6) must back-calculate within ±15% of nominal (±20% at LLOQ), while analytical run QCs (minimum 3 levels: LQC, MQC, HQC, \u003e= 5% of unknown samples or \u003e= 2 per level) must achieve \u003e= 67% passing overall AND \u003e= 50% passing at each QC level within ±15% of nominal.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03598","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Bioanalytical Method Validation","topic":"LBA Bioanalysis of Biologics","q":"While validating a sandwich ELISA Ligand Binding Assay (LBA) for a therapeutic monoclonal antibody in serum, a high-concentration study sample above the ULOQ produces an unexpectedly low optical signal within the linear portion of the 4-parameter logistic (4PL) calibration curve. What phenomenon causes this artifact and how does ICH M10 address it?","o":["The high-dose Hook (prozone) effect, where excess free analyte saturates both capture and detection antibodies independently, preventing sandwich complex formation; detected and resolved by evaluating dilution linearity / parallelism and re-assaying after sample dilution","Detector photobleaching of chromophores at low concentration; resolved by lowering the LLOQ","Ion suppression in ESI mass spectrometry; resolved by adding a deuterated internal standard","Matrix hemolysis degrading the 96-well polystyrene plate completely"],"a":0,"e":"In homogeneous or simultaneous sandwich Ligand Binding Assays (ELISA/ECL), very high analyte concentrations exceeding ULOQ can saturate capture antibodies on the plate and labeled detection antibodies separately in solution, inhibiting ternary \u0027sandwich\u0027 bridge formation and causing a paradoxical drop in signal (\u0027Hook effect\u0027 or prozone phenomenon) that mimics an in-range value. ICH M10 requires validating dilution linearity (spanning concentrations above ULOQ, demonstrating accuracy/precision within ±20% upon dilution into range) and checking parallelism in incurred samples.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03599","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Phytomedicine \u0026 Phytopharmaceuticals","topic":"Bioassay-Guided Fractionation Pitfalls","q":"During bioassay-guided fractionation of a crude aqueous-methanolic bark extract, an active fraction shows potent in vitro inhibition across multiple unrelated purified enzymes (proteases, kinases, and reverse transcriptase), raising suspicion of a non-specific assay artifact. What chemical class is most likely responsible and what clean-up/dereplication step resolves it?","o":["Volatile monoterpene hydrocarbons; remove by adding concentrated sulfuric acid","High-molecular-weight polyphenolic tannins (classic PAINS/protein precipitants); remove tannins via polyamide/PVPP column filtration and perform LC-HRMS/MS dereplication before further isolation","Water-insoluble waxes; remove by direct steam injection into the enzyme assay","Pure inorganic sodium chloride; remove by adding lead acetate"],"a":1,"e":"Polyphenolic tannins bind non-specifically to proteins via multiple hydrogen bonds and hydrophobic interactions, precipitating or denaturing unrelated assay enzymes in vitro and producing false-positive broad-spectrum \u0027hits\u0027 (Pan-Assay Interference Compounds, PAINS). Passing crude extracts through polyamide, polyvinylpolypyrrolidone (PVPP), or gelatin-salt columns selectively strips interfering tannins, while early LC-HRMS/MS molecular networking (e.g., GNPS) dereplicates ubiquitous known compounds to focus isolation strictly on genuine selective bioactives.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03600","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Phytomedicine \u0026 Phytopharmaceuticals","topic":"Phytosome (Herbosome) Technology","q":"Water-soluble polyphenolic extracts such as silybin (milk thistle), curcumin, and green tea catechins suffer poor oral absorption because of large molecular size and poor lipid solubility. How does formulating them as \u0027Phytosomes\u0027 (herbosomes) structurally differ from conventional liposomes to boost oral bioavailability?","o":["Liposomes require zero phospholipids, whereas phytosomes consist purely of synthetic polyacrylamide","In a phytosome, the phytoconstituent is covalently cross-linked to polyethylene glycol via disulfide bonds exclusively","In a phytosome, the phytoconstituent forms a stoichiometric (1:1 or 1:2) hydrogen-bonded molecular complex directly with the polar choline headgroup of phosphatidylcholine (confirmed by 1H/31P-NMR \u0026 FTIR), whereas in a liposome the drug is merely physically entrapped inside an aqueous core without chemical bonding","Phytosomes completely block intestinal lymphatic uptake and convert polyphenols into insoluble calcium salts"],"a":2,"e":"Phytosome (herbosome) technology reacts standardized polyphenolic or flavonoid phytoconstituents with soy phosphatidylcholine in a 1:1 or 1:2 molar ratio in an aprotic solvent (e.g., dioxane/acetone/ethyl acetate). The active hydroxyls form hydrogen bonds with the polar phosphate/choline headgroup (verified by FTIR peak shifts and 1H/31P-NMR broadening), wrapping the hydrophilic polyphenol in a lipophilic phospholipid envelope that dramatically enhances enterocyte membrane permeation and stability compared with free extract or unbonded aqueous-core liposomes.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03601","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Modern Pharmaceutics","topic":"Hansen Solubility Parameters (delta_t) for ASD Miscibility","q":"Before running Hot-Melt Extrusion (HME) to prepare a single-phase amorphous solid dispersion of a poorly soluble drug in Soluplus or PVP-VA polymer, formulators calculate total Hansen Solubility Parameters (delta_t combining dispersion, polar, and H-bonding partial parameters). According to Greenhalgh\u0027s empirical rule, what difference |delta_API - delta_polymer| predicts thermodynamic miscibility versus immiscible phase separation?","o":["Zero difference in Hansen parameters causes explosive melt decomposition","|delta_API - delta_polymer| \u003e 50 MPa^0.5 guarantees spontaneous miscibility","Hansen solubility parameters apply exclusively to gas-phase helium chromatography","|delta_API - delta_polymer| \u003c 7.0 MPa^0.5 predicts high likelihood of molecular miscibility (single-phase glass solution), whereas \u003e 10.0 MPa^0.5 predicts phase separation and drug recrystallisation"],"a":3,"e":"Hansen Solubility Parameters partition cohesive energy density into dispersive (delta_d), polar (delta_p), and hydrogen-bonding (delta_h) contributions (delta_t = sqrt(delta_d^2 + delta_p^2 + delta_h^2)). Per Greenhalgh et al., \u0027like dissolves like\u0027 in solid dispersions when |delta_t(drug) - delta_t(polymer)| \u003c 7.0 MPa^0.5 (miscible glassy solution confirmed by a single T_g on modulated DSC), whereas differences \u003e 10.0 MPa^0.5 lead to amorphous-amorphous phase separation and crystallisation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03602","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Modern Pharmaceutics","topic":"Pharmaceutical Co-Crystals vs Salts","q":"When enhancing the aqueous dissolution rate of a non-ionisable or weakly acidic/basic drug where conventional salt formation fails, how does USFDA/EMA define a \u0027Pharmaceutical Co-Crystal\u0027 in contrast to a crystalline Pharmaceutical Salt?","o":["A crystalline single-phase solid composed of two or more neutral molecular/ionic components (API + benign coformer) in a stoichiometric ratio bound by non-ionic interactions (hydrogen bonds, pi-stacking) WITHOUT complete proton transfer (typically delta pKa \u003c 0-1), whereas salts involve complete proton transfer (delta pKa \u003e 3)","A physical mixture of two separate powders blended in a V-cone mixer with zero lattice integration","A covalent prodrug ester requiring enzymatic cleavage in the liver","An amorphous solid dispersion lacking long-range X-ray diffraction peaks"],"a":0,"e":"Crystal engineering designs pharmaceutical co-crystals (e.g., sacubitril-valsartan, ertugliflozin-L-pyroglutamic acid) by assembling an API and a GRAS coformer (nicotinamide, saccharin, carboxylic acids) into a shared periodic crystal lattice via supramolecular heterosynthons (neutral H-bonds, halogen/pi interactions) without proton transfer (delta pKa = pKa(base) - pKa(acid) \u003c 0, whereas \u003e 3 yields ionic salts). Unlike amorphous dispersions, co-crystals remain thermodynamically crystalline with sharp PXRD peaks while improving solubility and stability.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03603","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Biopharmaceutics and Pharmacokinetics","topic":"Wagner-Nelson vs Loo-Riegelman Deconvolution Methods","q":"When establishing a Level A In Vitro-In Vivo Correlation (IVIVC) by deconvolving oral plasma concentration-time profiles into Fraction of Drug Absorbed [F_a(t)], why can the Wagner-Nelson method be calculated directly from oral data alone (requiring only k_e), whereas the Loo-Riegelman method strictly requires prior intravenous bolus two-compartment parameters (k_12, k_21, k_10)?","o":["Wagner-Nelson requires three-compartment IV PET imaging; Loo-Riegelman works without plasma concentrations","Wagner-Nelson derives exact mass balance for a one-compartment open model [F_a(t) = (C_t + k_e*AUC_0-t) / (k_e*AUC_0-inf)] needing no IV reference curve, whereas Loo-Riegelman accounts for reversible peripheral-compartment distribution (X_p) in two-compartment drugs requiring IV micro-constants","Loo-Riegelman applies only to zero-order IV infusions with zero oral absorption","Wagner-Nelson measures solely renal tubular secretion in urine"],"a":1,"e":"In Level A IVIVC development, Wagner-Nelson and Loo-Riegelman perform model-dependent mass-balance deconvolution to extract the cumulative in vivo absorption profile F_a(t). Wagner-Nelson applies to one-compartment drugs where absorbed amount equals body drug (V_d*C_t) plus eliminated drug (k_e*V_d*AUC_0-t), cancelling V_d cleanly without an IV study. For multi-exponential two-compartment drugs, tissue distribution X_p(t) cannot be solved without IV disposition constants (k_12, k_21, k_10)—requiring the Loo-Riegelman equation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03604","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis","topic":"Capillary Zone Electrophoresis (CZE) Migration Order","q":"In Capillary Zone Electrophoresis (CZE) using an uncoated fused-silica capillary filled with pH 9.0 borate buffer (normal polarity: positive anode at injection inlet, negative cathode at UV detector outlet), in what order do small cations, neutral molecules, and small anions reach the detector, and why?","o":["Neutral species never move from the injection tip under alkaline pH 9.0","All cations, neutrals, and anions elute simultaneously with identical migration times","Small high-charge CATIONS elute first (electrophoretic attraction + cathodic Electroosmotic Flow), NEUTRAL species co-migrate together second at the bulk EOF velocity, and ANIONS elute last (pulled backward toward anode yet swept forward by stronger bulk EOF)","Anions elute first, followed by neutral species, and cations elute last"],"a":2,"e":"Above pH ~3, silanol (Si-OH) groups on uncoated fused silica ionise to negative Si-O-, attracting hydrated buffer cations into the diffuse double layer; under high voltage, this cationic sheath drags bulk solution rapidly toward the negative cathode—generating flat-profile cathodic Electroosmotic Flow (EOF) faster than most electrophoretic velocities. Thus small cations migrate fastest (v_ep + v_EOF), all neutral analytes ride together unseparated at v_EOF, and anions swim backward against the current yet still arrive at the cathode last (v_EOF - v_ep).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03605","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Analysis","topic":"Extractables vs Leachables Profiling","q":"For pre-filled syringes, IV infusion bags, and elastomeric vial stoppers under USP \u003c1663\u003e/\u003c1664\u003e and ICH Q3E principles, how does an \u0027Extractables\u0027 study differ from a \u0027Leachables\u0027 study?","o":["Extractables are chemical species pulled out of packaging materials using aggressive solvents and elevated temperatures to establish a worst-case risk profile, whereas Leachables are the subset of migrants that actually enter the finished drug product under real manufacturing/shelf-life conditions (evaluated above the Analytical Evaluation Threshold, AET)","Extractables are active API impurities from synthesis; Leachables are exclusively microbial endotoxins","Leachables are generated by boiling rubber stoppers in pure hexane at 150 C","Extractables and Leachables apply solely to dry cardboard outer shipping cartons"],"a":0,"e":"USP \u003c1663\u003e defines Extractables as organic/inorganic compounds forced out of primary/secondary packaging or single-use manufacturing components under exaggerated laboratory conditions (e.g., IPA/hexane/water reflux, extreme pH) to map potential migrants. USP \u003c1664\u003e defines Leachables as compounds actually migrating into the formulated drug product during normal processing and ICH stability storage; any leachable exceeding the toxicology-derived Analytical Evaluation Threshold (AET) must be identified (GC-MS/LC-MS/ICP-MS) and qualified.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03606","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems (NDDS)","topic":"Microbiota-Triggered vs pH-Dependent Colon Targeting","q":"Single-unit tablets coated solely with pH-responsive Eudragit S100 (dissolving threshold pH \u003e= 7.0) can unpredictably fail to release 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) in patients with active ulcerative colitis—passing intact into faeces. Why does this failure occur, and which biological colonic trigger offers greater site-specificity?","o":["Active colonic inflammation drops luminal colonic pH down to ~5.5-6.5 (preventing Eudragit S100 dissolution), whereas microbiota-triggered systems (azo-prodrugs like sulfasalazine/balsalazide or pectin/guar gum/chitosan polysaccharide matrices) exploit abundant anaerobic colonic azoreductases and polysaccharidases consistently present in the colon","Ulcerative colitis raises colonic pH above 11.0 melting Eudragit S100 inside the oesophagus","Colonic bacteria lack all glycosidase and azoreductase enzymes","Gastric pepsin digests Eudragit S100 completely within 5 minutes of swallowing"],"a":0,"e":"Healthy ileocaecal/ascending colon pH rises briefly around 7.0-7.4, allowing Eudragit S100 coatings to dissolve; however, in active ulcerative colitis/Crohn\u0027s colitis, mucosal inflammation and bacterial lactate production lower colonic pH to 5.5-6.5—preventing pH-7.0 coatings from dissolving (or conversely, high inter-individual ileal pH causes premature dumping). Exploiting the massive ~10^11-10^12 CFU/g colonic anaerobic microbiome via azo-reductase cleavage or non-starch polysaccharide matrices (guar gum, pectin, inulin, amylose) achieves superior colon specificity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03607","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems (NDDS)","topic":"Dissolving Polymeric Microneedle Arrays for Vaccines","q":"Why do dissolving polymeric microneedle patches (needle height ~300-700 micrometres made from PVP, hyaluronic acid, or trehalose/PVA) enable painless, cold-chain-sparing intradermal vaccine delivery with marked antigen dose-sparing compared with standard 25-mm intramuscular needle syringes?","o":["Microneedles remain permanently embedded as sharp metallic silicon shards in muscle","Microneedles destroy all epidermal dendritic cells via thermal ablation","Microneedles penetrate 3 cm deep into periosteum to stimulate bone marrow B-cells directly","Microneedles painlessly pierce the 15-micron stratum corneum barrier while remaining shallow enough to avoid deep dermal pain nerves and blood vessels, dissolving in interstitial fluid to target antigen directly to dense networks of epidermal Langerhans cells and dermal dendritic cells"],"a":3,"e":"Dissolving polymeric microneedles create transient micron-scale conduits across the impermeable stratum corneum without reaching deeper nociceptors or capillaries, dissolving completely (eliminating sharps waste) in viable epidermis and upper dermis rich in Langerhans cells and dermal dendritic antigen-presenting cells, eliciting equivalent or stronger immune responses at 1/5th to 1/10th the intramuscular antigen dose.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03608","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Regulatory Affairs","topic":"ALCOA+ Data Integrity Principles","q":"During a CDSCO/WHO/USFDA GMP inspection of a QC analytical laboratory, an inspector discovers that three analysts share a single administrator login on the HPLC workstation, turn off the electronic audit trail during trial injections, and transcribe chromatographic peak areas onto loose sticky notes before entering them into the logbook the next day. Which regulatory data-integrity framework is violated across Attributable, Contemporaneous, Original, and Complete criteria?","o":["ALCOA+ Data Integrity principles (Attributable, Legible, Contemporaneous, Original, Accurate + Complete, Consistent, Enduring, Available)","Beer-Lambert spectrophotometric linearity law","Calvert oncology dosing equation","Stokes\u0027 sedimentation law"],"a":0,"e":"WHO/PIC/S/MHRA/USFDA GMP Data Integrity guidelines require all paper and electronic GxP records to satisfy ALCOA+: Attributable (unique individual user IDs—never shared logins), Legible/Enduring (no scrap/sticky notes), Contemporaneous (recorded at the exact time of activity—not next day), Original (primary electronic raw data preserved), Accurate, Complete (all trial injections retained with audit trails enabled—never hidden), Consistent, and Available throughout retention.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03609","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Regulatory Affairs","topic":"USFDA Drug Master Files (DMF)","q":"When an Indian Active Pharmaceutical Ingredient (API) manufacturer supplies a proprietary drug substance to a US generic company filing an ANDA without disclosing confidential synthesis routes to the generic customer, which USFDA Drug Master File (DMF) type is submitted to FDA alongside a Letter of Authorization (LoA)?","o":["Type III DMF (Packaging Material)","Type II DMF (Drug Substance, Drug Substance Intermediate, and Material Used in Their Preparation)","Type V DMF (FDA-Accepted Reference Information)","Type IV DMF (Excipient, Colorant, Flavor, Essence, or Material Used in Their Preparation)"],"a":1,"e":"A USFDA Drug Master File (21 CFR 314.420) permits a third party to submit confidential CMC information directly to the FDA, allowing an NDA/ANDA/IND applicant to cross-reference it via a written Letter of Authorization (LoA) without seeing proprietary manufacturing secrets. Active Pharmaceutical Ingredients (drug substances) and their intermediates fall under Type II DMFs (note: Type I for manufacturing site/facilities was discontinued in 2000).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03610","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmaceutical Regulatory Affairs","topic":"ICH GCP E6(R2) and ALCOA+","q":"In regulatory GMP and ICH GCP E6(R2) data integrity audits, original clinical trial source documents and chromatographic records must comply with \u0027ALCOA+\u0027 principles. What do the core five letters of \u0027ALCOA\u0027 stand for?","o":["Audited, Legalized, Categorized, Ordered, and Approved","Automated, Local, Certified, Outsourced, and Archived","Attributable, Legible, Contemporaneous, Original, and Accurate","Anonymous, Linear, Compressed, Optional, and Approximate"],"a":2,"e":"ALCOA (extended to ALCOA+ by adding Complete, Consistent, Enduring, and Available) is the universal regulatory data integrity framework adopted by ICH GCP, WHO, CDSCO, MHRA, and USFDA: Attributable (who performed/recorded an action and when), Legible (readable throughout retention), Contemporaneous (recorded in real time as work occurs, never backdated), Original (first capture or verified true copy), and Accurate (error-free, with audit trails for any justified corrections).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03611","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems","topic":"Dendrimer Drug Delivery","q":"Polyamidoamine (PAMAM) dendrimers are uniquely distinguished from conventional linear polymers as nanoscale drug carriers by which structural feature, and why are full-generation amine-terminated (-NH2) PAMAM dendrimers routinely PEGylated or acetylated prior to systemic IV administration?","o":["Cross-linked inorganic silica pores; PEGylation turns dendrimers into solid millimeter tablets","Unbranched protein helices; PEGylation increases cationic hemolytic toxicity tenfold","Random polydisperse linear chains lacking internal pockets; PEGylation dissolves the polymer completely in hexane","Monodisperse, hyperbranched 3D radially symmetric tree-like architecture with internal hydrophobic cavities and multivalent surface groups; PEGylation/acetylation masks dense cationic surface charge to prevent hemolysis and macrophage clearance"],"a":3,"e":"Dendrimers (e.g., Tomalia\u0027s PAMAM) are synthesized step-wise by divergent or convergent routes, producing near-monodisperse (PDI ~1.0) globular nanostructures comprising an initiator core, branching generation layers, internal voids capable of host-guest hydrophobic drug encapsulation, and exponentially increasing peripheral reactive groups for covalent prodrug/targeting ligand conjugation. Because high-generation amine-terminated (-NH2) PAMAM dendrimers bear dense positive charges that disrupt anionic erythrocyte membranes (hemolysis) and trigger rapid opsonization/cytotoxicity, surface PEGylation, acetylation, or anionic carboxylation dramatically improves biocompatibility.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03612","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Novel Drug Delivery Systems","topic":"SMEDDS and SNEDDS","q":"A poorly water-soluble lipophilic immunosuppressant (BCS Class II/IV, extensive hepatic CYP3A4 first-pass metabolism) is formulated as an anhydrous isotropic mixture of medium/long-chain triglycerides, Cremophor EL/Tween 80, and Transcutol P (SNEDDS) inside soft gelatin capsules. Upon entering aqueous GI fluid under mild peristalsis, how does this SNEDDS enhance oral bioavailability?","o":["It spontaneously self-emulsifies into \u003c100 nm oil-in-water nanodroplets keeping drug molecularly solubilized without rate-limiting crystal dissolution, while long-chain lipids stimulate chylomicron assembly and intestinal lymphatic transport bypassing hepatic first-pass metabolism","It cross-links intestinal tight junctions irreversibly with covalent bonds","It precipitates the drug immediately into large 500 µm crystals to delay gastric emptying","It raises gastric pH to 9.0 so the neutral lipophilic drug ionizes completely"],"a":0,"e":"Self-Nanoemulsifying/Self-Microemulsifying Drug Delivery Systems (SNEDDS/SMEDDS) are isotropic mixtures of oil, surfactant, and co-surfactant/co-solvent mapped via pseudoternary phase diagrams. On contact with GI fluids, minimal gentle agitation yields thermodynamically/kinetically stable nano-sized O/W droplets (\u003c50–100 nm) presenting huge interfacial area and maintaining the drug in solution across GI transit (avoiding dissolution bottlenecks). Additionally, digestion of long-chain triglycerides promotes mixed-micelle uptake and enterocyte chylomicron incorporation, routing highly lipophilic drugs (log P \u003e 5) via mesenteric lymphatics directly into the thoracic duct—bypassing portal hepatic first-pass extraction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03613","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Quality by Design \u0026 Process Validation","topic":"Levels of Control Strategy \u0026 Real-Time Release Testing","q":"Under the ICH Q8/Q10/Q11 Quality by Design paradigm, how does a \u0027Level 1 Control Strategy\u0027 incorporating Real-Time Release Testing (RTRT) differ from traditional \u0027Level 3\u0027 quality control?","o":["Level 1 prohibits testing raw material attributes before manufacturing","Level 1 uses automatic inline/on-line PAT sensors with feedforward/feedback controls to adjust CPPs dynamically and assure CQAs in real time (potentially replacing end-product batch testing via RTRT), whereas Level 3 relies solely on tight fixed process parameters and destructive end-product QC testing","Level 3 uses AI-driven real-time NIR probes to adjust press force on every tablet","Level 1 eliminates all process monitoring and relies purely on visual inspection after shipping"],"a":1,"e":"ICH Q8(R2)/Q11 describes a spectrum of control strategies: Level 3 (traditional quality-by-testing) freezes narrow fixed operating ranges and relies heavily on post-manufacturing off-line end-product testing; Level 2 introduces wider Design Space flexibility with reduced end-product testing; and Level 1 (highest QbD maturity) deploys engineering controls—such as inline NIR spectroscopy or automatic tablet press weight/force feedback loops—to monitor material/process variability and adjust CPPs in real time, supporting Real-Time Release Testing (RTRT) in lieu of destructive lab testing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03614","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Quality by Design \u0026 Process Validation","topic":"ANOVA and Regression Model Diagnostics","q":"A formulation scientist fits a quadratic Box-Behnken DoE model for sustained-release matrix drug release (t50%) and obtains Model p = 0.0012, Lack-of-Fit p = 0.42, R² = 0.96, Adjusted R² = 0.92, and Predicted R² = 0.25. Which statistical flaw does the large gap between Adjusted R² (0.92) and Predicted R² (0.25) reveal?","o":["Zero statistical significance of the overall model","Significant Lack-of-Fit error because Lack-of-Fit p = 0.42 is \u003e 0.05","Severe model overfitting or an influential outlier/block effect (Adjusted R² - Predicted R² = 0.67 \u003e\u003e 0.20 threshold), indicating poor predictive capability on new batches unless model terms are pruned or outliers examined","Perfect predictive validity because only R² \u003e 0.90 matters"],"a":2,"e":"In DoE ANOVA diagnostics, a healthy predictive polynomial requires: (1) significant Model F-value (p \u003c 0.05), (2) non-significant Lack-of-Fit (p \u003e 0.05, meaning residual error matches pure replicate noise—here p = 0.42 is good), and (3) reasonable agreement between Adjusted R² and Predicted R² (difference \u003c 0.20). A huge discrepancy (0.92 vs 0.25, difference = 0.67) indicates the model fits existing noise via superfluous insignificant quadratic/interaction terms (overfitting) or harbors an outlier, requiring backward/stepwise model reduction or Box-Cox transformation before establishing a Design Space.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03615","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Bioanalytical Method Validation","topic":"Dried Blood Spot (DBS) Bioanalysis","q":"During pediatric pharmacokinetic validation using fixed-diameter sub-punches from Dried Blood Spot (DBS) filter paper cards, samples from neonates with high hematocrit (65%) yield systematically +25% higher analyte concentrations than calibration standards prepared at normal adult hematocrit (42%). What physical mechanism causes this DBS bias, and how does Volumetric Absorptive Microsampling (VAMS) eliminate it?","o":["Fixed-diameter punches measure plasma rather than whole blood; VAMS removes erythrocytes chemically","Higher hematocrit destroys LC-MS/MS electrospray needles completely; VAMS adds heparin directly into the mass spectrometer","Lower hematocrit evaporates analyte faster during drying; VAMS freezes samples at -80 °C prior to spotting","Higher hematocrit increases blood viscosity, reducing radial spreading on cellulose cards so a fixed-diameter sub-punch captures a larger blood volume; VAMS wicks an exact fixed micro-volume (e.g., 10 or 20 µL) analyzed in full regardless of hematocrit"],"a":3,"e":"Conventional DBS sub-punching takes a fixed-area disc (e.g., 3 mm) from a blood spot dried on cellulose paper. High-hematocrit blood is more viscous and spreads less laterally, concentrating more blood volume per mm² inside the fixed punch (positive assay bias, alongside variable extraction recovery/matrix effect). Volumetric Absorptive Microsampling (VAMS) uses a porous hydrophilic polymeric tip engineered to absorb an exact, fixed volumetric blood sample (e.g., 10 µL or 20 µL) analyzed in its entirety—eliminating spot-area spreading hematocrit bias.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03616","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Bioanalytical Method Validation","topic":"Selectivity and Metabolite Back-Conversion","q":"During LC-MS/MS bioanalysis of a phenolic drug alongside its abundant circulating acyl-glucuronide metabolite, the analyst sets up rapid 1.5-minute ballistic chromatography where the glucuronide co-elutes with the parent drug and observes falsely elevated parent concentrations in clinical samples (though spiked neat parent QCs pass). What bioanalytical interference occurred, and how is it resolved under ICH M10 specificity testing?","o":["In-source collision/thermal cleavage of the co-eluting glucuronide metabolite in the heated ESI source converts it back into the parent drug ion (same MRM transition); resolved by chromatographically separating the metabolite from the parent peak and lowering source temperature","Ion enhancement from the deuterated internal standard solely at ULOQ","Complete loss of Q1 quadrupole mass calibration exclusively in clinical samples","Carryover from the blank mobile phase injection"],"a":0,"e":"Labile phase II conjugates (acyl glucuronides, N-oxides, sulfates) frequently undergo thermal or collision-induced dissociation inside the heated electrospray (ESI) or APCI interface, shedding the glucuronide moiety (176 Da) to produce the exact protonated parent ion [M+H]+ that enters Q1 and shares the parent\u0027s MRM transition. Because spiked neat QCs lack metabolites, this error goes undetected until incurred samples are tested (or ISR/metabolite-spiked specificity fails). Resolving it requires chromatographic baseline separation of the metabolite from the parent retention window (plus gentler ESI source temperature and pH-stabilized sample collection).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03617","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Phytomedicine \u0026 Phytopharmaceuticals","topic":"Classification of Botanical Markers","q":"In EMA/WHO/IPC botanical extract standardization, how are \u0027Negative Markers\u0027 (e.g., aristolochic acids in Aristolochia-contaminated herbs or pyrrolizidine alkaloids) distinct from \u0027Active Markers\u0027 and \u0027Analytical Markers\u0027?","o":["Active markers are toxic heavy metals whereas negative markers are therapeutic alkaloids","Negative markers are toxic or allergenic phytoconstituents/contaminants whose maximum permissible limit (or complete absence) must be verified for safety, whereas active markers drive known therapeutic efficacy and analytical markers serve purely for batch-to-batch chromatographic QC","Analytical markers are banned pesticides tested only by organoleptic taste","Negative markers are inert excipients added deliberately to dilute extracts"],"a":1,"e":"Regulatory herbal monographs classify markers by function: (1) Active Markers (constituents with established therapeutic activity directly contributing to efficacy, e.g., sennosides A/B, hypericin/hyperforin); (2) Analytical Markers (characteristic phytochemicals chosen for quantitative batch consistency and identity when clinical efficacy relies on complex multi-component synergy, e.g., valerenic acid, parthenolide); and (3) Negative Markers (toxic, carcinogenic, or allergenic compounds such as nephrotoxic/carcinogenic aristolochic acids, hepatotoxic pyrrolizidine alkaloids, or estragole controlled below strict upper safety thresholds).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03618","course":"M.Pharm - Master of Pharmacy","subject":"Phytomedicine \u0026 Phytopharmaceuticals","topic":"Toxic Heavy Metals in Ayurvedic/Herbal Products","q":"According to the Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India (API) and WHO herbal quality guidelines for finished herbal drug preparations intended for oral consumption, what are the standard maximum permissible limits (in ppm) for Lead (Pb), Cadmium (Cd), Arsenic (As), and Mercury (Hg), respectively?","o":["No limits apply to natural plant extracts because heavy metals are excluded from pharmacopoeias","Lead \u003c= 0.001 ppm, Cadmium \u003c= 10 ppm, Arsenic \u003c= 50 ppm, Mercury \u003c= 20 ppm","Lead \u003c= 10 ppm, Cadmium \u003c= 0.3 ppm, Arsenic \u003c= 3 ppm, Mercury \u003c= 1 ppm","Lead \u003c= 100 ppm, Cadmium \u003c= 50 ppm, Arsenic \u003c= 30 ppm, Mercury \u003c= 25 ppm"],"a":2,"e":"Soil uptake, industrial pollution, and processing equipment can contaminate botanical medicines with toxic heavy metals. Both the Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India (API / Ministry of AYUSH) and WHO Guidelines for Assessing Quality of Herbal Medicines specify strict maximum permissible limits for oral herbal products: Lead (Pb) \u003c= 10 ppm, Cadmium (Cd) \u003c= 0.3 ppm, Arsenic (As) \u003c= 3 ppm, and Mercury (Hg) \u003c= 1 ppm—quantified accurately after closed-vessel microwave acid digestion by Inductively Coupled Plasma–Mass Spectrometry (ICP-MS) or AAS.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03619","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advance Nursing Practice","topic":"Orem\u0027s Self-Care Deficit Nursing Theory","q":"A nurse practitioner designs care for three patients using Dorothea Orem\u0027s Self-Care Deficit Nursing Theory: (1) an intubated, sedated ICU trauma patient; (2) a stable post-appendectomy patient who washes his face but needs assistance ambulating and with wound care; and (3) a newly diagnosed Type 2 diabetes patient learning carbohydrate counting and foot care. Which sequence correctly matches Orem\u0027s three nursing systems?","o":["(1) Partly compensatory, (2) Supportive-educative, (3) Wholly compensatory","(1) Supportive-educative, (2) Wholly compensatory, (3) Partly compensatory","(1) Adaptation mode, (2) Conservation mode, (3) Cultural care mode","(1) Wholly compensatory, (2) Partly compensatory, (3) Supportive-educative"],"a":3,"e":"Orem\u0027s Self-Care Deficit Nursing Theory classifies nursing agency into three systems based on the patient\u0027s self-care agency: (1) Wholly compensatory—nurse performs all self-care because the patient cannot engage in purposeful action (e.g., comatose/sedated ICU patient); (2) Partly compensatory—both nurse and patient perform care measures collaboratively; (3) Supportive-educative—patient can perform self-care but needs guidance, teaching, and decision-making support.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03620","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advanced Clinical Pathophysiology (Medical-Surgical Speciality)","topic":"Septic Shock Pathophysiology and Sepsis-3","q":"A 68-year-old patient with urosepsis remains hypotensive (MAP 54 mmHg) with serum lactate 4.6 mmol/L despite receiving 30 mL/kg IV balanced crystalloids within 3 hours. Which pathophysiological mechanism primarily drives this refractory hypotension, and which first-line vasopressor should be initiated?","o":["Excessive inducible nitric oxide synthase (iNOS)-mediated systemic vasodilation, endothelial glycocalyx injury, and capillary leak; norepinephrine infusion targeting MAP \u003e= 65 mmHg","Mechanical pericardial tamponade; dopamine monotherapy","Isolated vagal bradycardia; oral atenolol","Pure hypovolaemia from polyuria; furosemide infusion"],"a":0,"e":"Under Sepsis-3 criteria, septic shock is defined by sepsis with persistent hypotension requiring vasopressors to maintain MAP \u003e= 65 mmHg and serum lactate \u003e 2 mmol/L despite adequate volume resuscitation. Pro-inflammatory cytokines trigger iNOS overexpression causing profound nitric oxide-mediated vasoplegia, endothelial glycocalyx shedding, microvascular shunting, and third-spacing. Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines recommend norepinephrine as the first-choice vasopressor to restore systemic vascular resistance and organ perfusion.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03621","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Parametric Hypothesis Testing","q":"A postgraduate nursing researcher evaluates post-caesarean pain scores (measured on a 0–100 mm Visual Analogue Scale, normally distributed with equal variances) across three independent randomized groups: Group A (guided imagery), Group B (warm sacral compress), and Group C (routine care). Which primary inferential test is most appropriate to compare the mean pain scores across all three groups simultaneously?","o":["Pearson Chi-square test","One-way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) followed by a post-hoc test (e.g., Tukey HSD) if F is significant","Paired Student\u0027s t-test","McNemar\u0027s test"],"a":1,"e":"When comparing means of a continuous, normally distributed outcome with homogeneity of variance across three or more independent groups, One-way ANOVA (F-test) controls the family-wise Type I error rate that would inflate if multiple pairwise t-tests were run. If the omnibus F-ratio is statistically significant, post-hoc comparisons (e.g., Tukey HSD or Bonferroni) identify which specific pairs of groups differ.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03622","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Chi-Square Test of Independence","q":"In a hospital quality-improvement study, a nurse administrator uses a Pearson Chi-square test of independence to examine whether three nursing shift schedules (Morning, Evening, Night) are associated with four categories of medication incident severity (Near Miss, No Harm, Temporary Harm, Permanent Harm). What are the degrees of freedom (df) for this contingency table?","o":["df = 5","df = 7","df = 6","df = 12"],"a":2,"e":"For an r × c contingency table in a Pearson Chi-square test of independence, degrees of freedom are calculated as df = (r - 1) × (c - 1), where r is the number of rows and c is the number of columns. Here, r = 3 shifts and c = 4 severity categories, so df = (3 - 1) × (4 - 1) = 2 × 3 = 6.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03623","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Education and Management","topic":"Miller\u0027s Pyramid of Clinical Competence","q":"When designing an assessment blueprint for postgraduate critical care nursing students using Miller\u0027s Pyramid of Clinical Competence, which assessment method directly evaluates the \u0027Shows How\u0027 level in a standardized, simulated clinical environment?","o":["Multiple-choice questions (MCQs) testing factual recall","Unsupervised self-reported logbook entries without direct observation","Structured essay examination on ventilator physiology","Objective Structured Clinical Examination (OSCE) using standardized patients or manikins with checklists"],"a":3,"e":"Miller\u0027s Pyramid progresses from cognition (\u0027Knows\u0027—MCQs/short answers; \u0027Knows How\u0027—clinical reasoning/case-based problems) to behaviour (\u0027Shows How\u0027—demonstrating integrated clinical skills under observation in simulation via OSCE/OSPE; and \u0027Does\u0027—real workplace performance assessed via DOPS/mini-CEX/360-degree feedback). OSCEs directly measure the \u0027Shows How\u0027 tier using structured stations and objective rubrics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03624","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advance Nursing Practice","topic":"Roy\u0027s Adaptation Model (RAM)","q":"A postgraduate nurse applies Sister Callista Roy\u0027s Adaptation Model while caring for a newly amputated young adult who withdraws from family visitors, refuses to look at his residual limb, and refuses rehabilitation exercises. According to Roy\u0027s four adaptive modes, which modes are primarily demonstrating ineffective responses here?","o":["Self-concept/group identity mode, role function mode, and interdependence mode","Conservation of energy and structural integrity principles","Wholly compensatory and supportive-educative modes","Physiological-physical mode only"],"a":0,"e":"Roy\u0027s Adaptation Model views the person as a biopsychosocial adaptive system responding to focal, contextual, and residual stimuli through regulator and cognator coping subsystems across four adaptive modes: (1) Physiological-physical, (2) Self-concept/group identity (body image and personal self, impaired by refusing to look at the residual limb), (3) Role function (occupational/rehabilitation role disruption), and (4) Interdependence (giving and receiving love/support, impaired by withdrawing from family).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03625","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advanced Clinical Pathophysiology (Medical-Surgical Speciality)","topic":"Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)","q":"A 45-year-old patient with severe acute pancreatitis develops acute dyspnoea 48 hours after admission, bilateral diffuse alveolar infiltrates on chest radiograph, normal cardiac echocardiogram, and PaO2/FiO2 ratio of 115 mmHg on PEEP 8 cmH2O (severe-to-moderate ARDS). Which pathophysiological hallmark explains refractory hypoxaemia, and which mechanical ventilation strategy reduces ventilator-induced lung injury?","o":["Pleural effusion solely; hypercapnic permissive hypoventilation without PEEP","Diffuse alveolar-capillary membrane injury, surfactant inactivation, alveolar flooding/collapse causing intrapulmonary right-to-left shunting; low tidal volume (4–6 mL/kg predicted body weight), plateau pressure \u003c= 30 cmH2O, adequate PEEP, and prone positioning","Isolated pulmonary embolism; zero PEEP and high peak pressures \u003e 45 cmH2O","Pure bronchospasm; high tidal volume 12 mL/kg to hyperventilate alveoli"],"a":1,"e":"ARDS is characterized by non-cardiogenic protein-rich pulmonary oedema, Type II pneumocyte damage with surfactant loss, and dependent alveolar collapse creating severe intrapulmonary shunting (refractory hypoxaemia poorly responsive to FiO2 alone). The ARDSNet lung-protective strategy—low tidal volume 4–6 mL/kg predicted body weight, limiting plateau pressure \u003c= 30 cmH2O, titrated PEEP, and early prone positioning (for PaO2/FiO2 \u003c 150 mmHg)—prevents barotrauma, volutrauma, and biotrauma while improving survival.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03626","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Statistical Power and Type II Error","q":"A postgraduate nursing thesis protocol sets alpha = 0.05 and power = 0.80 to detect a moderate effect size between two educational interventions, but due to slow recruitment the researcher stops early with half the calculated sample size and obtains p = 0.14. Which methodological vulnerability best explains this non-significant finding?","o":["Inflated Type I (alpha) error proving the intervention caused harm","Elevated Type II (beta) error from an underpowered sample failing to detect a genuine clinical difference","Excessive statistical power above 99%","Violation of double-blinding guaranteeing a false-positive result"],"a":1,"e":"Statistical power (1 - beta) is the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis when a true effect exists (conventional target \u003e= 0.80, corresponding to beta = 0.20). Halving the required sample size sharply lowers statistical power and raises Type II (beta) error—the risk of falsely concluding no difference exists (false negative) simply because the study lacked adequate sample size.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03627","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Content Validity Index (I-CVI)","q":"During psychometric validation of a newly developed Nursing Stress Assessment Scale, 6 clinical nursing experts independently rate Item #4 on a 4-point relevance scale (1 = not relevant, 2 = somewhat relevant, 3 = quite relevant, 4 = highly relevant). Five experts rate the item as 3 or 4, and one expert rates it as 2. What is the Item-level Content Validity Index (I-CVI) for Item #4?","o":["0.90","0.75","0.83","0.67"],"a":2,"e":"The Item-level Content Validity Index (I-CVI) is computed as the number of experts giving a rating of either 3 or 4 (dichotomized as relevant) divided by the total number of experts. Here: I-CVI = 5 ÷ 6 = 0.833, which rounds to 0.83 (meeting Polit \u0026 Beck\u0027s standard threshold of \u003e= 0.83 for a panel of 6 experts).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03628","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research \u0026 Bioethics","topic":"Belmont Report and ICMR Ethical Guidelines","q":"A nurse researcher proposes testing a painful experimental dressing exclusively on indigent, uninsured general-ward patients while intending to market the final protocol to private luxury clinics. Which core bioethical principle of the Belmont Report and ICMR National Ethical Guidelines does this recruitment plan directly violate?","o":["Veracity in laboratory calibration","Respect for statistical normality","Fidelity to hospital marketing goals","Justice (fair distribution of research burdens and benefits without exploiting vulnerable populations)"],"a":3,"e":"The Belmont Report and ICMR National Ethical Guidelines for Biomedical and Health Research Involving Human Participants define three foundational principles: Respect for Persons (autonomy/informed consent and special protections for diminished autonomy), Beneficence (do not harm, maximize benefits/minimize risks), and Justice (equitable selection of subjects so disadvantaged or hospitalized poor populations do not disproportionately bear research risks while affluent groups reap the benefits).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03629","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advance Nursing Practice","topic":"Jean Watson\u0027s Theory of Human Caring","q":"An oncology nurse specialist practices authentic presence, active listening, cultivating hope, and creating a peaceful healing environment for a terminally ill patient rather than treating procedures purely mechanically. Which nursing theorist\u0027s framework centres on \u0027transpersonal caring\u0027 and the 10 Caritas Processes?","o":["Jean Watson\u0027s Theory of Human Caring","Betty Neuman\u0027s Systems Model of Stressors","Hildegard Peplau\u0027s Interpersonal Relations Theory solely","Imogene King\u0027s Theory of Goal Attainment"],"a":0,"e":"Jean Watson\u0027s Philosophy and Theory of Transpersonal Caring distinguishes medical \u0027curing\u0027 from holistic nursing \u0027caring\u0027. Its core structures are the transpersonal caring relationship, the caring moment/occasion, and 10 Caritas Processes (evolving from carative factors)—emphasizing loving-kindness, authentic presence, spiritual/emotional support, and healing environments.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03630","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advanced Clinical Pathophysiology (Neurological Critical Care)","topic":"Cerebral Salt Wasting (CSW) vs SIADH","q":"Five days after clipping a ruptured cerebral aneurysm (subarachnoid haemorrhage), an ICU patient develops serum Na+ 122 mEq/L, urine Na+ 85 mmol/L, and urine osmolality 520 mOsm/kg. Crucially, the patient has negative fluid balance (-1,400 mL/24 h), dry mucous membranes, tachycardia, falling CVP (1 mmHg), and orthostatic hypotension. Which diagnosis explains this, and why would fluid restriction be harmful?","o":["SIADH; fluid restriction cures hypotension immediately","Cerebral Salt Wasting (CSW) causing hypovolaemic natriuresis; fluid restriction would worsen hypovolaemia and cerebral vasospasm/ischaemia—treat instead with isotonic/hypertonic saline volume and sodium replacement","Central Diabetes Insipidus; give desmopressin and water restriction","Hyperaldosteronism; infuse 5% Dextrose without sodium"],"a":1,"e":"Both SIADH and CSW present with hyponatraemia, elevated urine sodium, and concentrated urine after brain injury/SAH. The decisive clinical differentiator is extracellular fluid (ECF) volume status: SIADH is euvolaemic/mildly hypervolaemic (managed with fluid restriction), whereas CSW (mediated by natriuretic peptides/sympathetic disruption) causes renal sodium and water wasting resulting in true hypovolaemia (negative balance, low CVP, hypotension). Fluid-restricting a CSW patient precipitates cerebral hypoperfusion and vasospasm stroke.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03631","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Mann-Whitney U and Kruskal-Wallis Tests","q":"A nurse researcher compares hospital length of stay (strongly right-skewed continuous data failing normality tests) between two independent groups: patients receiving an enhanced recovery (ERAS) pathway versus standard postoperative care. Which non-parametric statistical test is the exact counterpart to the independent-samples t-test?","o":["Kruskal-Wallis H test","Pearson product-moment correlation (r)","Mann-Whitney U test","Wilcoxon signed-rank test"],"a":2,"e":"When comparing two independent groups on an ordinal variable or a severely skewed continuous outcome that violates normality, the Mann-Whitney U test (Wilcoxon rank-sum test) is the appropriate non-parametric equivalent of the independent-samples t-test. By contrast, Wilcoxon signed-rank compares two paired/related measurements, and Kruskal-Wallis H compares three or more independent groups.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03632","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Standard Error of the Mean (SEM)","q":"In a nursing epidemiological study of 100 adult patients with hypertension (n = 100), the sample mean systolic blood pressure is 148 mmHg with a sample standard deviation (SD) of 15 mmHg. What is the Standard Error of the Mean (SEM)?","o":["0.15 mmHg","15.0 mmHg","3.0 mmHg","1.5 mmHg"],"a":3,"e":"The Standard Error of the Mean (SEM) estimates the precision of the sample mean as an estimator of the population mean and is calculated as SEM = SD ÷ sqrt(n). Substituting SD = 15 mmHg and n = 100: SEM = 15 ÷ sqrt(100) = 15 ÷ 10 = 1.5 mmHg. Unlike SD (which describes dispersion of individual observations), SEM decreases as sample size increases.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03633","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Education","topic":"Revised Bloom\u0027s Taxonomy","q":"A nursing educator writes a clinical learning objective for postgraduate critical care students: \u0027Design an original, individualized ventilator-weaning and delirium-prevention protocol integrating patient ABG trends and sedation scores.\u0027 Which highest cognitive level of the Revised Bloom\u0027s Taxonomy does the action verb \u0027Design/Formulate\u0027 represent?","o":["Creating","Remembering","Understanding","Applying"],"a":0,"e":"In Anderson and Krathwohl\u0027s Revised Bloom\u0027s Taxonomy, the cognitive hierarchy progresses from Remembering -\u003e Understanding -\u003e Applying -\u003e Analyzing -\u003e Evaluating -\u003e Creating. Designing, formulating, or constructing a novel, coherent clinical protocol from diverse clinical elements exemplifies \u0027Creating\u0027 (the apex higher-order thinking skill, replacing \u0027Synthesis\u0027 in the 1956 taxonomy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03634","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advance Nursing Practice","topic":"Hildegard Peplau\u0027s Interpersonal Relations Theory","q":"A psychiatric clinical nurse specialist structures care for a patient with severe anxiety using Hildegard Peplau\u0027s Theory of Interpersonal Relations. Which exact chronological sequence represents Peplau\u0027s four phases of the nurse-patient relationship?","o":["Assessment -\u003e Nursing Diagnosis -\u003e Implementation -\u003e Evaluation","Orientation -\u003e Identification -\u003e Exploitation -\u003e Resolution","Exploitation -\u003e Orientation -\u003e Resolution -\u003e Identification","Trust -\u003e Autonomy -\u003e Initiative -\u003e Integrity"],"a":1,"e":"Peplau\u0027s middle-range Psychodynamic Nursing Theory defines four overlapping chronological phases: (1) Orientation (defining the problem, establishing trust and boundaries), (2) Identification (patient identifies with the nurse and begins expressing feelings of helplessness/belonging), (3) Exploitation (patient actively makes full use of all nursing and healthcare services/problem-solving), and (4) Resolution (mutual termination as original needs are met and independence emerges).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03635","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advanced Clinical Pathophysiology (Medical-Surgical Speciality)","topic":"Hepatic Encephalopathy","q":"A patient with decompensated cirrhosis develops asterixis (flapping hand tremor), fetor hepaticus, and confusion (Grade II hepatic encephalopathy) 24 hours after an upper gastrointestinal bleed. Why does oral/rectal lactulose improve his sensorium, and what stool frequency target guides nursing titration?","o":["Lactulose directly binds haemoglobin in blood; target 0 stools/day","Lactulose neutralizes gastric acid purely via calcium carbonate","Colonic bacteria ferment lactulose into organic acids, lowering stool pH so absorbable ammonia (NH3) converts to non-absorbable ammonium (NH4+) and is expelled; titrate to 2–3 soft stools per day","Lactulose causes severe constipation to retain gut flora; target 1 stool per week"],"a":2,"e":"Digestion of intraluminal blood after an upper GI bleed increases colonic nitrogen and ammonia (NH3) production, which bypasses the failing liver via portosystemic shunts and causes astrocyte swelling/neurotoxicity. Lactulose (a non-absorbable disaccharide) is fermented by colonic bacteria into lactic/acetic acids, acidifying the lumen to \u0027trap\u0027 lipid-soluble NH3 as charged, non-absorbable NH4+ ions while acting as an osmotic laxative—titrated to 2–3 soft stools daily (paired with rifaximin to reduce urease-producing gut bacteria).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03636","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Adjusted Odds Ratio (AOR) and Confidence Interval","q":"In a hospital infection-control logistic regression study predicting Catheter-Associated Urinary Tract Infection (CAUTI), catheter duration \u003e 7 days yields an Adjusted Odds Ratio (AOR) = 3.40 with a 95% Confidence Interval (CI) of 1.85 to 6.25. How should the nurse researcher interpret this finding?","o":["Impossible result because odds ratios can never exceed 1.0","Protective effect reducing CAUTI by 340%","Not statistically significant because the 95% CI exceeds 1.0","Statistically significant positive association (95% CI excludes the null value of 1.0): catheterization \u003e 7 days is associated with 3.40 times higher odds of CAUTI after controlling for confounders"],"a":3,"e":"For ratio measures of association (Odds Ratio, Relative Risk, Hazard Ratio), the null value representing \u0027no association\u0027 is 1.0. Because the entire 95% Confidence Interval (1.85–6.25) lies strictly above 1.0 and does not cross 1.0, the association is statistically significant at p \u003c 0.05, indicating 3.40-fold higher odds of CAUTI independently of other covariates in the multivariable model.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03637","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Relative Risk (Risk Ratio) Calculation","q":"In a prospective ICU nursing cohort study on pressure injuries, 30 out of 150 patients with missed turning schedules developed a pressure injury (20% incidence), compared with 10 out of 200 patients receiving strict 2-hourly offloading (5% incidence). What is the Relative Risk (RR) of developing a pressure injury in the missed-turning group compared with the strict-offloading group?","o":["4.0","15.0","0.25","2.0"],"a":0,"e":"Relative Risk (RR) = Incidence in Exposed Group ÷ Incidence in Unexposed Group. Here: Incidence (missed turning) = 30 ÷ 150 = 0.20 (20%); Incidence (strict offloading) = 10 ÷ 200 = 0.05 (5%). Thus, RR = 0.20 ÷ 0.05 = 4.0, meaning patients with missed turning had four times the risk of pressure injury.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03638","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Education","topic":"Kirkpatrick\u0027s Four-Level Evaluation Model","q":"A nursing educator evaluates an ICU central-line bundle training workshop by auditing actual bedside catheter-insertion compliance in the wards 3 months later AND tracking the hospital\u0027s CLABSI rate per 1,000 catheter-days over the following year. Which two levels of Kirkpatrick\u0027s Evaluation Model do these metrics respectively assess?","o":["Input and Process only","Level 3 (Behaviour/transfer to clinical practice) and Level 4 (Results/patient organizational outcomes)","Level 2 (Learning) and Level 1 (Reaction)","Level 1 (Reaction) and Level 2 (Learning)"],"a":1,"e":"Kirkpatrick\u0027s Four-Level Model evaluates training sequentially: Level 1 Reaction (participant satisfaction survey), Level 2 Learning (knowledge/OSCE skill acquisition immediately post-training), Level 3 Behaviour (sustained transfer of learned behaviours into real clinical workplace practice, such as ward bundle compliance audits), and Level 4 Results (ultimate organizational/patient impact, such as reduced CLABSI rates or mortality).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03639","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advance Nursing Practice","topic":"Betty Neuman\u0027s Systems Model","q":"In Betty Neuman\u0027s Systems Model, when a patient\u0027s protective \u0027flexible line of defense\u0027 is penetrated by an acute hospital-acquired stressor causing early symptom instability (penetrating the \u0027normal line of defense\u0027), which nursing intervention category aims to treat symptoms, strengthen internal \u0027lines of resistance\u0027, and restore system stability?","o":["Primary prevention as intervention","Tertiary prevention as intervention","Secondary prevention as intervention","Wholly compensatory nursing agency"],"a":2,"e":"Neuman\u0027s Systems Model depicts the client as an open system surrounded by concentric protective rings: the flexible line of defense (outer dynamic buffer), normal line of defense (usual wellness baseline), and internal lines of resistance. Primary prevention strengthens the flexible line BEFORE a stressor penetrates; Secondary prevention acts AFTER stressor penetration/symptom onset to treat reactions and mobilize internal lines of resistance; Tertiary prevention supports reconstitution and prevents relapse.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03640","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advanced Clinical Pathophysiology (Medical-Surgical Speciality)","topic":"Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC)","q":"A postpartum woman with severe abruptio placentae and septic shock develops oozing from every venipuncture site, gum bleeding, and petechiae. Coagulation studies show platelets 38,000/mm3, prolonged PT and aPTT, plasma fibrinogen 75 mg/dL (critically low), and markedly elevated D-dimer. Which pathophysiological mechanism explains simultaneous thrombosis and bleeding, and what is the cornerstone of management?","o":["Pure idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura; perform immediate splenectomy","Isolated Factor VIII deficiency; give intramuscular vitamin K alone","Warfarin overdose; infuse heparin bolus at 50,000 units","Widespread tissue-factor-mediated microvascular fibrin clotting consuming platelets and coagulation factors alongside secondary fibrinolysis (DIC); cornerstone is treating the underlying trigger urgently plus targeted component support (FFP, cryoprecipitate, platelets)"],"a":3,"e":"In acute DIC, massive endothelial/tissue-factor exposure (sepsis, placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism, trauma) activates systemic thrombin generation, forming widespread microvascular thrombi that consume platelets, fibrinogen, and clotting factors faster than the liver/marrow can replace them while plasmin activation generates high fibrin degradation products/D-dimers that further impair haemostasis. Eliminating the underlying cause (delivering placenta/controlling sepsis) paired with FFP, cryoprecipitate (for fibrinogen \u003c 100 mg/dL), and platelets is decisive.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03641","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Forest Plot and Heterogeneity (I-Squared)","q":"A nurse researcher reviews a systematic review and meta-analysis of 12 nursing trials on music therapy for ICU anxiety. The forest plot reports a pooled standardized mean difference of -0.62 (95% CI: -0.98 to -0.26) with Higgins\u0027 heterogeneity statistic I2 = 78% (p \u003c 0.001). How should the researcher interpret I2 = 78% and model choice?","o":["Substantial/high statistical heterogeneity (78% of total variation across studies reflects true between-study differences rather than chance); use a random-effects model and explore sources via subgroup/sensitivity analysis","The intervention worsened anxiety because the effect size is negative","The meta-analysis is invalid solely because 12 trials were included","Zero heterogeneity across trials; use a fixed-effects model exclusively"],"a":0,"e":"Higgins\u0027 I2 quantifies the percentage of variability in effect estimates across studies attributable to true heterogeneity rather than sampling error (0%–25% low, ~50% moderate, \u003e= 75% substantial/high). With I2 = 78%, assuming a single identical true effect (fixed-effects model) is inappropriate; a random-effects model (accounting for between-study variance) plus subgroup/meta-regression analysis is required. The negative SMD (-0.62, 95% CI excluding 0) indicates significant anxiety reduction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03642","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Number Needed to Treat (NNT)","q":"In an ICU nursing randomized controlled trial testing chlorhexidine-impregnated transparent dressings versus standard dressings for preventing central-line-associated bloodstream infection (CLABSI), CLABSI incidence was 12% (0.12) in the standard control group and 2% (0.02) in the chlorhexidine-dressing group. What is the Number Needed to Treat (NNT) to prevent one CLABSI event?","o":["50 patients","10 patients","5 patients","20 patients"],"a":1,"e":"Step 1: Calculate Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR) = Control Event Rate (CER) - Experimental Event Rate (EER) = 0.12 - 0.02 = 0.10 (10%). Step 2: Calculate Number Needed to Treat (NNT) = 1 ÷ ARR = 1 ÷ 0.10 = 10 patients. Thus, 10 ICU patients need chlorhexidine-impregnated dressings instead of standard dressings to prevent one additional CLABSI.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03643","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Education","topic":"Problem-Based Learning (PBL)","q":"During a postgraduate Problem-Based Learning (PBL) clinical tutorial built around an authentic unedited ICU patient trigger, what is the primary educational role of the M.Sc Nursing faculty tutor?","o":["Read textbook paragraphs verbatim to the group","Deliver a 60-minute uninterrupted PowerPoint lecture providing all diagnoses and answers immediately","Act as a metacognitive facilitator—encouraging students to generate hypotheses, identify learning gaps, conduct self-directed literature search, and synthesize clinical reasoning collaboratively","Sit silently outside the room without any feedback"],"a":2,"e":"Grounded in constructivist adult-learning theory, Problem-Based Learning uses an ill-structured clinical scenario first (before lectures) so learners activate prior knowledge, identify knowledge deficits, research evidence independently, and apply integrated solutions. The tutor guides group dynamics and prompts deep Socratic clinical reasoning rather than spoon-feeding answers.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03644","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advance Nursing Practice","topic":"Madeleine Leininger\u0027s Culture Care Theory","q":"A nurse specialist applies Madeleine Leininger\u0027s Culture Care Diversity and Universality Theory while caring for a rural postpartum family who wishes to: (A) keep the mother warm with traditional nutritious ajwain/dry-fruit porridge; (B) adjust visiting hours for elders\u0027 blessings; and (C) apply cow dung onto the newborn\u0027s umbilical cord. Which matching of Leininger\u0027s three care modes is clinically safe and culturally congruent?","o":["Preserve (C), Repattern (A), Ignore (B)","Repattern all three (A, B, and C) aggressively on Day 1","Preserve all three (A, B, and C) without questioning cow dung application","Cultural care preservation/maintenance for (A), Cultural care accommodation/negotiation for (B), and Cultural care repatterning/restructuring for (C) to safely eliminate tetanus risk"],"a":3,"e":"Leininger\u0027s Sunrise Enabler guides three culturally congruent nursing decisions: (1) Preservation/Maintenance—supporting beneficial or neutral cultural practices (nutritious postpartum porridge); (2) Accommodation/Negotiation—adapting institutional routines respectfully (flexible elders\u0027 blessing times); and (3) Repatterning/Restructuring—collaboratively modifying harmful health practices (replacing unsafe cow-dung cord application with clean dry cord care to prevent neonatal tetanus).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03645","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advanced Clinical Pathophysiology (Cardiovascular Critical Care)","topic":"Cardiac Biomarker Kinetics in Reinfarction","q":"Five days after an acute anterior STEMI treated with fibrinolysis, a hospitalized patient develops recurrent crushing chest pain and new ST elevation. Why is serum CK-MB (or serial high-sensitivity troponin delta tracking rather than a single troponin elevation) particularly helpful at Day 5, given normal cardiac Troponin I kinetics?","o":["Cardiac Troponin I remains elevated for 7–14 days after the initial infarction, masking a single static reading on Day 5, whereas CK-MB rises in 3–6 hours and normalizes within 48–72 hours so a fresh Day 5 spike clearly signals reinfarction","Troponin I clears completely within 6 hours of an MI","CK-MB never rises in myocardial necrosis","Troponin I is exclusively skeletal muscle specific"],"a":0,"e":"Following Type 1 MI from atherosclerotic plaque rupture and occlusive thrombosis, cardiac Troponin I/T rises at 2–4 hours, peaks at 12–24 hours, and stays elevated for 7–14 days due to slow structural myofibrillar release. Because baseline troponin is still elevated on Day 5, detecting early reinfarction relies either on demonstrating a \u003e 20% serial troponin re-elevation or measuring CK-MB, which returns to baseline within 48–72 hours and re-spikes sharply with new necrosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03646","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Quasi-Experimental Designs","q":"A nurse researcher evaluates a structured preoperative anxiety-reduction video in Ward A (intervention) while Ward B receives routine care (comparison), collecting pre-test and post-test anxiety scores from both wards without randomly assigning individual patients. Which research design is used, and which essential property of a true experiment is absent?","o":["Single-case qualitative biography; lacks sample size","Quasi-experimental non-equivalent control group pre-test post-test design; lacks randomization (random assignment), creating potential selection bias","True randomized controlled trial; lacks dependent variable","Descriptive cross-sectional survey; lacks intervention manipulation"],"a":1,"e":"A true experiment requires three pillars: manipulation (intervention), control/comparison group, and randomization (random assignment). Because the researcher manipulates the video intervention across two wards with pre/post measurement but cannot randomize patients between wards, this is a quasi-experimental non-equivalent control group pre-test post-test design—vulnerable to selection-maturation differences unless baselines are verified and adjusted (e.g., ANCOVA).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03647","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Positive Predictive Value (PPV) Calculation","q":"During validation of a bedside nursing delirium screening checklist among 200 elderly ICU patients compared against psychiatric gold-standard diagnosis, results yield: True Positives (TP) = 45, False Positives (FP) = 15, False Negatives (FN) = 5, and True Negatives (TN) = 135. What is the Positive Predictive Value (PPV) of the checklist?","o":["96%","67%","75%","90%"],"a":2,"e":"Positive Predictive Value (PPV) is the probability that a patient who screens positive truly has the condition: PPV = [TP ÷ (TP + FP)] × 100. Substituting TP = 45 and FP = 15: PPV = [45 ÷ (45 + 15)] × 100 = (45 ÷ 60) × 100 = 75%. (By comparison, Sensitivity = 45/50 = 90%, Specificity = 135/150 = 90%, and NPV = 135/140 = 96.4%).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03648","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Education","topic":"Post-Exam Item Analysis","q":"After scoring an undergraduate nursing OSCE/MCQ exam, faculty perform item analysis and find Item #14 has a Difficulty Index (p-value) of 0.52 and a Discrimination Index (D) of -0.18 (negative). What does the negative Discrimination Index indicate, and what action should faculty take?","o":["Negative D means the test reliability is 1.0","Item #14 was too easy because p = 0.52 means 100% answered correctly","Item #14 is an exemplary gold-standard item separating top students cleanly","More low-scoring students answered Item #14 correctly than high-scoring students—indicating a flawed answer key, ambiguous wording, or misleading distractor requiring faculty review/revision"],"a":3,"e":"In educational item analysis, Difficulty Index (p = proportion answering correctly) ideally sits between 0.30 and 0.70 (0.52 is moderately difficult). Discrimination Index (D = P_upper - P_lower) measures how well an item separates high achievers from low achievers (acceptable \u003e= +0.20; strong \u003e= +0.30). A negative D (-0.18) means weaker students outperformed top students—almost always pointing to a miskeyed answer, double-correct option, or confusing stem requiring correction before finalizing grades.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03649","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advance Nursing Practice","topic":"Imogene King\u0027s Theory of Goal Attainment","q":"A nurse specialist partners with an adult newly diagnosed with hypertension to explore mutual perceptions of lifestyle barriers, jointly negotiate an achievable daily walking and salt-reduction plan, and evaluate progress together at follow-up. Which nursing theorist\u0027s framework specifically defines \u0027transaction\u0027 and \u0027mutual goal attainment\u0027 across personal, interpersonal, and social systems?","o":["Imogene King\u0027s Theory of Goal Attainment (Interacting Systems Framework)","Florence Nightingale\u0027s Environmental Theory","Virginia Henderson\u0027s 14 Basic Needs solely","Rosemarie Parse\u0027s Humanbecoming Theory"],"a":0,"e":"Imogene King\u0027s Conceptual System organizes nursing around dynamic open interacting systems (Personal, Interpersonal, and Social). Within the interpersonal system, nurse and client bring separate perceptions and judgements into Action -\u003e Reaction -\u003e Interaction, culminating in \u0027Transaction\u0027—a purposeful collaborative process of mutual goal setting that directly increases goal attainment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03650","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Advanced Clinical Pathophysiology (Cardiology)","topic":"Neurohormonal Remodelling in HFrEF","q":"A 61-year-old patient with ischaemic Heart Failure with Reduced Ejection Fraction (HFrEF, LVEF 30%) is initiated on guideline-directed quadruple therapy (sacubitril/valsartan, carvedilol, spironolactone, and dapagliflozin). Why do beta-blockers and RAAS/MRA antagonists markedly lower long-term mortality in HFrEF despite acute negative inotropic/blood-pressure effects?","o":["They convert systolic HFrEF into acute tamponade","Chronic sympathetic (norepinephrine) and RAAS (angiotensin II/aldosterone) hyperactivation drives maladaptive ventricular remodelling, myocyte apoptosis, and interstitial fibrosis; neurohormonal blockade reverses adverse remodelling and reduces sudden arrhythmic death","They stimulate permanent myocardial hypertrophy and high heart rate","They act exclusively as short-term loop diuretics"],"a":1,"e":"In HFrEF, falling cardiac output initially recruits compensatory sympathetic nervous system (SNS) and RAAS activation, which chronically becomes toxic—promoting sodium retention, vasoconstriction (high afterload), beta-1 receptor desensitization, ventricular dilation, myocardial fibrosis, and malignant arrhythmias. Evidence-based neurohormonal blockade (ARNI/ACEi, beta-blockers, MRAs) plus SGLT2 inhibitors arrests and reverses adverse structural remodelling and cuts mortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03651","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Intention-to-Treat (ITT) vs Per-Protocol Analysis","q":"In a nursing RCT evaluating a 12-week cardiac telerehabilitation programme (n = 100 intervention vs n = 100 usual care), 18 intervention patients drop out due to illness or fatigue before completing the sessions. Why do CONSORT guidelines recommend Intention-to-Treat (ITT) as the primary statistical analysis rather than analysing only completers (Per-Protocol)?","o":["ITT switches non-adherent intervention patients into the control arm","ITT deletes all drop-outs completely to make p-values smaller","ITT analyzes every participant in their originally randomized group regardless of adherence or drop-out—preserving baseline randomization balance, preventing attrition/selection bias, and reflecting real-world clinical effectiveness","Per-protocol analysis always guarantees zero confounding"],"a":2,"e":"Intention-to-Treat (\u0027once randomized, always analyzed\u0027) retains all randomized subjects in their assigned arms, preserving the prognostic comparability created by randomization. Excluding sick or non-adherent drop-outs (Per-Protocol analysis) artificially leaves a healthier, more motivated subgroup in the intervention arm—introducing severe attrition bias and exaggerating treatment efficacy.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03652","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Research and Statistics","topic":"Cohen\u0027s d Effect Size Calculation","q":"In a randomized nursing study comparing preoperative guided-imagery relaxation (n1 = 40, post-test anxiety mean = 42) against routine care (n2 = 40, post-test anxiety mean = 50) with a pooled standard deviation (SD_pooled) of 10, what is Cohen\u0027s d effect size (|Mean1 - Mean2| ÷ SD_pooled) and its standard interpretation?","o":["0.20 (small effect)","0.40 (small-to-medium effect)","1.60 (extreme effect)","0.80 (large effect)"],"a":3,"e":"Cohen\u0027s d expresses the standardized mean difference in standard deviation units: d = |Mean1 - Mean2| ÷ SD_pooled = |42 - 50| ÷ 10 = 8 ÷ 10 = 0.80. Per Cohen\u0027s conventional benchmarks (d = 0.20 small, 0.50 medium, \u003e= 0.80 large), 0.80 represents a large clinical effect size independent of sample size.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03653","course":"M.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Education","topic":"Structured Post-Simulation Debriefing","q":"In high-fidelity clinical nursing simulation (e.g., managing simulated postpartum haemorrhage), which phase is recognized in educational literature as the primary driver of deep clinical reasoning, cognitive reframing, and durable skill retention?","o":["Facilitated, psychologically safe structured debriefing (e.g., GAS or PEARLS framework) immediately following the scenario","Unstructured equipment setup prior to scenario","Grading students punitively in silence without feedback","Skipping reflection to run back-to-back scenarios all day"],"a":0,"e":"Experiential learning theory (Kolb) and INACSL simulation standards emphasize that structured post-simulation debriefing (such as Gather-Analyze-Summarize [GAS] or PEARLS) transforms raw clinical action into reflective insight—uncovering mental models behind decisions, validating strengths, and closing performance gaps in a psychologically safe environment.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03654","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Cockcroft-Gault Creatinine Clearance Calculation","q":"A 70-year-old female inpatient weighing 60 kg (ideal body weight 55 kg, actual weight used per local protocol) has a stable serum creatinine of 1.5 mg/dL. Using the standard Cockcroft-Gault equation with her weight of 60 kg, what is her estimated creatinine clearance (CrCl)?","o":["38.9 mL/min","33.1 mL/min","26.5 mL/min","45.2 mL/min"],"a":1,"e":"Cockcroft-Gault formula: CrCl = [(140 - Age) x Weight (kg) / (72 x SCr)] x 0.85 (for females). Substituting values: [(140 - 70) x 60 / (72 x 1.5)] x 0.85 = (4200 / 108) x 0.85 = 38.89 x 0.85 = 33.06 mL/min (~33.1 mL/min). This indicates moderate-to-severe renal impairment requiring dose adjustment for renally cleared drugs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03655","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacovigilance \u0026 Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Naranjo ADR Probability Scale","q":"A 28-year-old patient develops a generalised maculopapular rash 10 days after starting carbamazepine. A clinical pharmacist scores the Naranjo ADR Probability Scale as follows: previous conclusive reports (+1), reaction appeared after drug administration (+2), improved upon drug discontinuation (+1), no alternative causes identified (+2), and confirmed by objective dermatological examination (+1); rechallenge was not performed (0) and other items scored 0. What is the Naranjo score and causality category?","o":["Score +9 — Definite ADR","Score +4 — Possible ADR","Score +7 — Probable ADR","Score +7 — Definite ADR"],"a":2,"e":"Summing the individual Naranjo items gives 1 + 2 + 1 + 2 + 1 = +7. On the Naranjo ADR Probability Scale: \u003e= 9 is Definite, 5 to 8 is Probable, 1 to 4 is Possible, and \u003c= 0 is Doubtful. Because rechallenge and placebo testing are ethically avoided in hypersensitivity reactions, most well-documented clinical ADRs score in the Probable (5-8) range.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03656","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Phenytoin TDM in Hypoalbuminaemia","q":"A 58-year-old seizure-free inpatient with normal renal function (eGFR \u003e 90 mL/min/1.73 m2) and severe hypoalbuminaemia (serum albumin 2.0 g/dL) has a measured total serum phenytoin concentration of 8.0 mg/L (reference total range 10-20 mg/L). Using the Winter-Tozer equation for normal renal function, what is the corrected total phenytoin concentration and appropriate clinical recommendation?","o":["26.7 mg/L (toxic) — hold phenytoin immediately due to cerebellar toxicity","4.0 mg/L (critically low) — administer an intravenous phenytoin loading dose","8.0 mg/L (subtherapeutic) — double the maintenance dose immediately to achieve 16 mg/L","16.0 mg/L (therapeutic) — maintain the current phenytoin dose and avoid unnecessary escalation"],"a":3,"e":"Phenytoin is ~90% bound to albumin, and only the unbound fraction is pharmacologically active. In hypoalbuminaemia with normal renal function, the Winter-Tozer equation is: C_corrected = C_measured / [(0.2 x Albumin g/dL) + 0.1] = 8.0 / [(0.2 x 2.0) + 0.1] = 8.0 / 0.5 = 16.0 mg/L. Because the corrected concentration lies well within 10-20 mg/L and the patient is seizure-free, escalating the dose would risk free-phenytoin toxicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03657","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Warfarin-Azole Interaction","q":"A 61-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve stabilised on warfarin 5 mg/day (baseline INR 2.8) is prescribed oral fluconazole 200 mg/day for oesophageal candidiasis. Five days later she develops minor epistaxis and an INR of 6.4. What is the mechanistic basis of this drug-drug interaction and the most appropriate immediate intervention?","o":["Fluconazole potently inhibits CYP2C9-mediated metabolism of the active S-warfarin enantiomer; hold warfarin, give low-dose oral vitamin K1 (1-2.5 mg) if bleeding/risk warrants, and monitor INR closely","Fluconazole induces CYP3A4 accelerating R-warfarin clearance; increase warfarin dose to 7.5 mg/day","Fluconazole blocks renal tubular secretion of unchanged warfarin; initiate forced alkaline diuresis","Fluconazole competes with warfarin for vitamin K epoxide reductase directly; administer protamine sulfate"],"a":0,"e":"S-warfarin (3-5 times more potent than R-warfarin) is metabolised primarily by CYP2C9. Fluconazole is a potent CYP2C9 (and moderate CYP3A4) inhibitor, markedly decreasing S-warfarin clearance and elevating INR within 3-5 days. Management of elevated INR with minor bleeding includes holding warfarin, administering low-dose oral phytonadione (vitamin K1) as clinically indicated, and addressing the antifungal regimen.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03658","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Hospital \u0026 Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"SOAP Note Documentation","q":"While documenting a ward round SOAP note for a patient hospitalised with community-acquired pneumonia, a Pharm.D intern records: (1) patient reports worsening productive cough and pleuritic chest pain; (2) temperature 38.9 C, WBC 16,400/mm3, right lower lobe consolidation on chest X-ray; (3) acute bacterial pneumonia likely Streptococcus pneumoniae, suboptimal oral azithromycin due to macrolide resistance; (4) switch to IV ceftriaxone 1 g q24h plus azithromycin and monitor defervescence at 48 h. How do items (1) to (4) map to the SOAP format?","o":["1-Assessment, 2-Objective, 3-Subjective, 4-Plan","1-Subjective, 2-Objective, 3-Assessment, 4-Plan","1-Subjective, 2-Assessment, 3-Objective, 4-Plan","1-Objective, 2-Subjective, 3-Plan, 4-Assessment"],"a":1,"e":"In a standard clinical SOAP note: Subjective (S) captures patient-reported symptoms and history; Objective (O) records measurable vitals, physical exam, lab values, imaging, and current medications; Assessment (A) synthesises the diagnosis, aetiology, and drug-therapy problems; Plan (P) specifies pharmacotherapy changes, monitoring parameters, and patient counselling.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03659","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Vancomycin TDM \u0026 Infusion Reaction","q":"A 52-year-old man with MRSA bacteraemia develops pruritic erythematous flushing over his face, neck, and upper torso 20 minutes into his first intravenous vancomycin 1 g dose infused rapidly over 30 minutes; blood pressure remains 118/74 mmHg without wheezing or angioedema. What is the mechanism of this reaction, immediate clinical management, and the current ASHP/IDSA therapeutic drug monitoring target for serious MRSA infections?","o":["Type I IgE-mediated anaphylaxis requiring permanent avoidance of all glycopeptides; target peak concentration \u003e80 mg/L","Direct nephrotoxic tubular necrosis; target trough concentration \u003c2 mg/L","Non-IgE-mediated direct mast cell degranulation releasing histamine; pause infusion, give diphenhydramine, and resume slowly over 90-120+ minutes; target AUC24/MIC of 400-600 mg*h/L","Immune-complex Type III vasculitis; switch immediately to oral vancomycin 125 mg q6h for bacteraemia"],"a":2,"e":"Rapid vancomycin infusion (\u003c60 min) directly triggers non-IgE-mediated mast cell and basophil degranulation (Vancomycin Infusion Reaction / \u0027Red Man Syndrome\u0027). Unlike true IgE anaphylaxis, pausing the infusion, administering an H1 antihistamine (diphenhydramine), and restarting at a slower rate (\u003c=10-15 mg/min or \u003e=90-120 min) resolves it safely. Per 2020 ASHP/IDSA/PIDS/SIDP consensus guidelines, Bayesian/two-level AUC24/MIC targeting 400-600 mg*h/L (assuming MIC = 1 mg/L) is preferred over trough-only monitoring (15-20 mg/L) to maximise efficacy while reducing nephrotoxicity.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03660","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Intravenous Loading Dose Calculation","q":"An 80-kg patient with acute-on-chronic kidney injury (CrCl 18 mL/min) requires an intravenous loading dose of an antibiotic whose volume of distribution (Vd) is 0.6 L/kg and IV bioavailability (F) is 1.0, to achieve an immediate target plasma concentration (C_target) of 15 mg/L. What is the appropriate loading dose?","o":["1,200 mg","360 mg","180 mg","720 mg"],"a":3,"e":"Loading dose depends solely on volume of distribution (Vd) and target concentration—NOT on renal clearance (clearance governs the maintenance dose): Total Vd = 0.6 L/kg x 80 kg = 48 L. Using LD = (C_target x Vd) / F = (15 mg/L x 48 L) / 1.0 = 720 mg. Even in renal impairment (CrCl 18 mL/min), a full loading dose is required to rapidly achieve therapeutic plasma concentrations unless Vd itself is altered.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03661","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Clopidogrel-PPI Interaction (CYP2C19)","q":"A 63-year-old man receives dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin 75 mg + clopidogrel 75 mg daily) following drug-eluting coronary stent placement and requires gastroprotection with a proton pump inhibitor (PPI) due to prior peptic ulcer bleeding. Which PPI adjustment should the clinical pharmacist recommend, and why?","o":["Switch omeprazole/esomeprazole to pantoprazole (or rabeprazole), because omeprazole potently inhibits hepatic CYP2C19 needed to bioactivate the clopidogrel prodrug into its active thiol metabolite","Replace clopidogrel with oral vitamin K to potentiate aspirin antiplatelet action","Discontinue all gastroprotection immediately because PPIs bind P2Y12 receptors directly","Initiate high-dose omeprazole 40 mg twice daily because it induces CYP2C19 to double clopidogrel platelet inhibition"],"a":0,"e":"Clopidogrel is an inactive thienopyridine prodrug requiring two-step hepatic CYP-mediated oxidation (heavily dependent on CYP2C19) to generate its active metabolite that irreversibly blocks platelet P2Y12 receptors. Omeprazole and esomeprazole strongly inhibit CYP2C19, reducing active metabolite exposure and attenuating antiplatelet efficacy. Pantoprazole and rabeprazole exhibit minimal CYP2C19 inhibition and are preferred when PPI gastroprotection is indicated with clopidogrel.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03662","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Drug-Induced QT Prolongation \u0026 Torsades de Pointes","q":"A 68-year-old hospitalised woman with community-acquired pneumonia receiving IV levofloxacin and ondansetron (with serum K+ 3.0 mEq/L and Mg2+ 1.4 mg/dL) develops a QTc of 560 ms followed by recurrent runs of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia twisting around the isoelectric baseline (Torsades de Pointes). Alongside discontinuing QT-prolonging drugs and correcting potassium, what is the FIRST-LINE intravenous pharmacotherapy to stabilise the myocardium and terminate Torsades de Pointes?","o":["Intravenous amiodarone 300 mg bolus","Intravenous magnesium sulfate 2 g over 10-15 minutes (even if serum magnesium is normal)","Intravenous furosemide 80 mg bolus","Intravenous procainamide loading infusion"],"a":1,"e":"Co-administration of QT-prolonging agents (fluoroquinolones, macrolides, ondansetron, haloperidol) in the presence of hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, or bradycardia inhibits cardiac IKr (hERG) potassium channels, triggering early afterdepolarisations and Torsades de Pointes (TdP). Intravenous magnesium sulfate (2 g IV) is the immediate drug of choice to suppress early afterdepolarisations and prevent recurrent TdP—even when serum Mg2+ is normal—followed by K+ repletion (\u003e4.0-4.5 mEq/L) and withdrawing offending drugs.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03663","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Biostatistics and Research Methodology","topic":"Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR) \u0026 Number Needed to Treat (NNT)","q":"In a 5-year randomised controlled trial of a statin for primary prevention of major cardiovascular events, the event rate was 12% in the placebo (control) arm and 7% in the statin (experimental) arm. What is the Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR) and Number Needed to Treat (NNT) to prevent one additional cardiovascular event?","o":["ARR = 41.7%; NNT = 2.4 patients","ARR = 5% (0.05); NNT = 5 patients","ARR = 5% (0.05); NNT = 20 patients","ARR = 19%; NNT = 53 patients"],"a":2,"e":"Control Event Rate (CER) = 12% = 0.12; Experimental Event Rate (EER) = 7% = 0.07. Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR) = CER - EER = 0.12 - 0.07 = 0.05 (5 percentage points). Number Needed to Treat (NNT) = 1 / ARR = 1 / 0.05 = 20 patients treated over 5 years to prevent 1 cardiovascular event.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03664","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Extended-Interval Aminoglycoside Dosing","q":"A 48-year-old inpatient with Pseudomonas aeruginosa pyelonephritis and normal renal function is started on extended-interval (once-daily) gentamicin 5 mg/kg IV q24h rather than traditional 1.7 mg/kg q8h dosing. Which pharmacodynamic-pharmacokinetic rationale supports high-dose once-daily aminoglycoside administration?","o":["Maximises concentration-dependent bactericidal killing (target C_max/MIC \u003e= 8-10) and prolonged post-antibiotic effect while allowing plasma levels to fall below 1 mg/L (drug-free interval) to limit saturable renal proximal tubular accumulation","Maintains constant trough concentrations above 6 mg/L to prevent resistance via time-dependent killing","Prevents ototoxicity completely regardless of cumulative therapy duration","Eliminates need for serum creatinine monitoring because once-daily gentamicin is cleared purely hepatically"],"a":0,"e":"Aminoglycosides exhibit concentration-dependent killing (optimal efficacy at Peak/MIC = 8-10) and a prolonged post-antibiotic effect (PAE) suppressing bacterial regrowth even after levels drop below MIC. Uptake into renal proximal tubular cells (via megalin) and cochlear hair cells is saturable; therefore, once-daily high dosing achieves high peaks while providing a low-trough (\u003c1 mg/L) washout period that lowers nephrotoxicity compared with sustained moderate troughs from q8h regimens.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03665","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Toxicology","topic":"Paracetamol (Acetaminophen) Overdose","q":"A 21-year-old woman presents to the emergency department 5 hours after an acute intentional ingestion of 18 g of paracetamol (acetaminophen). A 4-hour post-ingestion serum paracetamol level plots above the treatment line on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram. Which toxic metabolite causes centrilobular hepatic necrosis, and how does intravenous N-acetylcysteine (NAC) protect the liver?","o":["COX-3 inhibition triggers hepatic arterial vasospasm; NAC acts as an endothelial nitric oxide antagonist","Paracetamol directly inhibits mitochondrial complex IV; NAC chelates free hepatic copper","UGT1A1 generates glucuronide crystals obstructing bile canaliculi; NAC alkalinises bile","CYP2E1 generates N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI), which depletes glutathione and binds hepatocyte proteins; NAC restores hepatic glutathione stores and supplies cysteine/sulfate precursors to detoxify NAPQI"],"a":3,"e":"At therapeutic doses, paracetamol is safely glucuronidated and sulfated (~90%), with \u003c10% oxidised by CYP2E1 to reactive NAPQI and rapidly conjugated by glutathione (GSH) to non-toxic mercapturic acid. In overdose, conjugation pathways saturate and excess NAPQI depletes GSH (\u003e70%), covalently adducting hepatocyte macromolecules to cause centrilobular (zone 3) necrosis. N-acetylcysteine (most effective within 8 hours) supplies cysteine to replenish GSH, directly conjugates NAPQI, and enhances sulfation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03666","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Lithium Toxicity Precipitated by Diuretics/NSAIDs","q":"A 44-year-old man with bipolar I disorder stably maintained on lithium carbonate 900 mg/day (baseline serum Li+ 0.8 mEq/L) is started on hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg/day for newly diagnosed hypertension. Ten days later he presents with coarse hand tremor, ataxia, dysarthria, polyuria, and a serum lithium level of 2.1 mEq/L. What renal mechanism explains this interaction?","o":["Thiazide-induced distal Na+ loss and mild volume contraction trigger compensatory upregulation of proximal tubular Na+ reabsorption, dragging Li+ (which mimics Na+) back into circulation and reducing lithium clearance","Hydrochlorothiazide directly stimulates pancreatic insulin-mediated intracellular Li+ shift","Hydrochlorothiazide inhibits hepatic CYP2D6 metabolism of lithium carbonate","Hydrochlorothiazide displaces lithium from 99% plasma albumin binding sites"],"a":0,"e":"Lithium is not protein-bound and is not metabolised; it is excreted almost entirely unchanged by the kidneys, where ~80% of filtered Li+ is reabsorbed alongside Na+ in the proximal convoluted tubule. Thiazide diuretics (and volume depletion, NSAIDs, or ACE inhibitors) trigger compensatory proximal tubular Na+ and Li+ reabsorption, decreasing renal lithium clearance by 25-40% and precipitating narrow-therapeutic-index toxicity (\u003e1.5 mEq/L).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03667","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Antibiotic De-escalation Based on Culture \u0026 Susceptibility","q":"A 55-year-old hospitalised patient with catheter-related bloodstream infection was empirically initiated on intravenous vancomycin plus meropenem. At 48 hours, blood cultures isolate methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA, oxacillin/cefoxitin-susceptible), and the patient is haemodynamically stable. Which antimicrobial stewardship intervention should the clinical pharmacist recommend?","o":["De-escalate both empiric drugs and switch to targeted intravenous cefazolin (or cloxacillin/nafcillin), which has superior bactericidal outcomes against MSSA compared with vancomycin and spares carbapenems","Continue vancomycin plus meropenem for 14 days to prevent polymicrobial relapse","Switch to oral azithromycin monotherapy immediately","Escalate meropenem to colistin plus tigecycline"],"a":0,"e":"Under ICMR Antimicrobial Stewardship Programme (AMSP) and IDSA guidelines, once blood cultures confirm MSSA bacteraemia, anti-staphylococcal beta-lactams (IV cefazolin or antistaphylococcal penicillins like cloxacillin) demonstrate significantly faster bacterial clearance and lower mortality than vancomycin—while discontinuing unnecessary meropenem reduces collateral Clostridioides difficile infection and carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales selection.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03668","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacoeconomics","topic":"Cost-Utility Analysis (CUA) \u0026 ICER","q":"A hospital P\u0026T committee evaluates a new oral anticoagulant (Drug B) costing Rs. 55,000/year against standard therapy (Drug A) costing Rs. 15,000/year. Over the modelled horizon, Drug B yields 3.5 Quality-Adjusted Life Years (QALYs) compared with 3.0 QALYs for Drug A. Which pharmacoeconomic method is this, and what is the Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio (ICER)?","o":["Cost-Effectiveness Analysis (CEA); ICER = Rs. 40,000 per QALY gained","Cost-Utility Analysis (CUA); ICER = Rs. 80,000 per QALY gained","Cost-Benefit Analysis (CBA); ICER = Rs. 20,000 per QALY gained","Cost-Minimisation Analysis (CMA); ICER = Rs. 15,000 per life saved"],"a":1,"e":"When health outcomes are measured in Quality-Adjusted Life Years (QALYs, combining survival and health-related quality-of-life utility weights), the evaluation is a Cost-Utility Analysis (CUA). Incremental Cost = Rs. 55,000 - Rs. 15,000 = Rs. 40,000. Incremental QALYs = 3.5 - 3.0 = 0.5 QALYs. Therefore, ICER = Incremental Cost / Incremental QALY = Rs. 40,000 / 0.5 = Rs. 80,000 per QALY gained.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03669","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Digoxin Toxicity Precipitated by Hypokalaemia","q":"A 74-year-old woman with systolic heart failure and atrial fibrillation taking digoxin 0.25 mg/day and furosemide 40 mg/day develops nausea, anorexia, yellow-green visual halos (xanthopsia), and bidirectional ventricular tachycardia. Serum K+ is 2.7 mEq/L, SCr is 1.9 mg/dL, and serum digoxin is 2.9 ng/mL. What mechanism links furosemide-induced hypokalaemia to digoxin toxicity, and what is the definitive antidote for life-threatening arrhythmia?","o":["Furosemide induces hepatic CYP3A4 doubling digoxin conversion into an active metabolite; administer intravenous flumazenil","Hypermagnesaemia from furosemide blocks renal digoxin secretion; administer intravenous atropine only","K+ and digoxin compete for the same extracellular binding site on myocardial Na+/K+-ATPase—hypokalaemia enhances digoxin binding and toxicity; administer Digoxin Immune Fab intravenously alongside potassium repletion","Hypokalaemia displaces digoxin from 98% plasma albumin binding; administer protamine sulfate"],"a":2,"e":"Digoxin inhibits the alpha-subunit of myocardial Na+/K+-ATPase at the extracellular potassium-binding pocket. Loop-diuretic-induced hypokalaemia removes physiological K+ competition, markedly increasing digoxin binding and triggering automaticity/AV block even at borderline serum concentrations (while CKD further impairs digoxin clearance). Life-threatening digoxin toxicity (ventricular arrhythmias, severe hyperkalaemia \u003e5.0 mEq/L from systemic Na+/K+-ATPase blockade, haemodynamic instability) is definitively reversed by Digoxin Immune Fab fragments.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03670","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Serotonin Syndrome vs Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome","q":"A 49-year-old man maintained on oral escitalopram 20 mg/day for depression is started on intravenous linezolid 600 mg q12h for VRE urinary tract infection. Within 24 hours he develops agitation, diaphoresis, fever 39.2 C, tachycardia, dilated pupils, lower-extremity hyperreflexia, and inducible ankle clonus. What syndrome has occurred, and what targeted pharmacotherapy is indicated after stopping offending drugs?","o":["Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) with lead-pipe rigidity; administer IV haloperidol","Malignant hyperthermia from linezolid; administer IV succinylcholine","Anticholinergic toxidrome; administer IV physostigmine","Serotonin syndrome (triggered by linezolid\u0027s reversible MAO-A/B inhibition adding to SSRI serotonin reuptake blockade); supportive cooling, IV benzodiazepines, and oral cyproheptadine (5-HT2A antagonist)"],"a":3,"e":"Linezolid is a weak, non-selective reversible monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI); combining it with SSRIs, SNRIs, or tramadol precipitates acute Serotonin Syndrome (\u003c24 h onset). Neuromuscular hyperexcitability—hyperreflexia, inducible/spontaneous ocular/ankle clonus, and myoclonus—plus diaphoresis distinguishes it from NMS (slow onset over days, \u0027lead-pipe\u0027 rigidity, bradyreflexia) and anticholinergic toxicity (dry flushed skin, absent bowel sounds, normal reflexes). Management stops serotonergic drugs, gives benzodiazepines for agitation/hyperthermia, and adds cyproheptadine (5-HT2A antagonist) in moderate-to-severe cases.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03671","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT Type II)","q":"Seven days after starting subcutaneous unfractionated heparin (UFH) prophylaxis following hip surgery, a 66-year-old woman\u0027s platelet count drops from 260,000/mm3 to 82,000/mm3 (a \u003e68% fall) and she develops an acute left popliteal deep vein thrombosis (4Ts score = 7). What is the diagnosis and MANDATORY immediate anticoagulation switch?","o":["Immune-mediated Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT Type II) driven by anti-PF4/heparin IgG antibodies; immediately stop ALL heparins (including LMWH and catheter flushes) and start a non-heparin parenteral anticoagulant (argatroban, bivalirudin, or fondaparinux)","Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura; transfuse 6 units of platelets and continue UFH","Non-immune benign HIT Type I; switch UFH to enoxaparin (LMWH) and start high-dose warfarin bolus immediately","Vitamin K deficiency; administer IV phytonadione and stop all anticoagulation"],"a":0,"e":"HIT Type II is a prothrombotic immune reaction (typically 5-10 days after heparin exposure, \u003e50% platelet drop) mediated by IgG antibodies against platelet factor 4 (PF4)-heparin complexes that cross-link platelet Fc-gamma-RIIa receptors, triggering massive thrombin generation and paradoxical arterial/venous thrombosis. All heparin sources (UFH, LMWH, heparin flushes) must be stopped immediately and replaced by a non-heparin anticoagulant (argatroban, bivalirudin, danaparoid, or fondaparinux). Avoid platelet transfusions unless bleeding, and delay warfarin until platelets recover \u003e150,000/mm3 to prevent venous limb gangrene.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03672","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Toxicology","topic":"Methanol Poisoning (Formic Acid Toxicity)","q":"A 36-year-old man presents 14 hours after consuming adulterated country liquor with severe epigastric pain, tachypnoea, blurred \u0027snowstorm\u0027 vision, optic disc hyperaemia, arterial pH 7.12, bicarbonate 9 mEq/L, and an elevated anion gap (28 mEq/L) plus osmolar gap. Which toxic metabolite causes the metabolic acidosis and optic neuropathy, and what specific enzyme-blocking antidote is indicated?","o":["Acetaldehyde exclusively; administer disulfiram","Formic acid (generated sequentially via alcohol dehydrogenase and aldehyde dehydrogenase); administer fomepizole (or ethanol) to inhibit alcohol dehydrogenase, plus IV folinic acid (leucovorin) and haemodialysis","Oxalic acid causing calcium oxalate renal crystals exclusively; administer IV calcium gluconate only","N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine; administer naloxone"],"a":1,"e":"Methanol itself causes mild inebriation and an early osmolar gap, but hepatic alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) oxidises it to formaldehyde and aldehyde dehydrogenase converts formaldehyde to formic acid (formate). Formate inhibits retinal mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase causing optic nerve demyelination/blindness (\u0027snowstorm vision\u0027) and high-anion-gap lactic/formic acidosis. Fomepizole (4-methylpyrazole, preferred) or ethanol competitively inhibits ADH, stopping further formate formation, while folinic acid enhances folate-dependent formate oxidation to CO2 and haemodialysis clears methanol and formate.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03673","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Biostatistics and Research Methodology","topic":"Sensitivity, Specificity \u0026 Predictive Values","q":"A rapid biomarker test is evaluated in 200 hospitalised patients (100 with confirmed disease and 100 without disease): True Positives (TP) = 90, False Negatives (FN) = 10, False Positives (FP) = 20, and True Negatives (TN) = 80. Calculate the Sensitivity, Specificity, and Positive Predictive Value (PPV) of the test in this cohort.","o":["Sensitivity = 80%; Specificity = 90%; PPV = 88.9%","Sensitivity = 81.8%; Specificity = 88.9%; PPV = 90%","Sensitivity = 90%; Specificity = 80%; PPV = 81.8%","Sensitivity = 90%; Specificity = 80%; PPV = 45.0%"],"a":2,"e":"From the 2x2 table: Sensitivity = TP / (TP + FN) = 90 / (90 + 10) = 90%. Specificity = TN / (TN + FP) = 80 / (80 + 20) = 80%. Positive Predictive Value (PPV) = TP / (TP + FP) = 90 / (90 + 20) = 90 / 110 = 81.82% (~81.8%). (For completeness, NPV = TN / (TN + FN) = 80 / 90 = 88.9%.)","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03674","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Calculating Elimination Rate Constant (kel) \u0026 Half-Life (t1/2)","q":"A patient receiving intravenous vancomycin has two steady-state serum concentrations drawn within the same dosing interval: C_1 = 32 mg/L at 1 hour post-infusion and C_2 = 8 mg/L at 9 hours post-infusion. Using one-compartment first-order kinetics, calculate the patient-specific elimination rate constant (k_e) and half-life (t_1/2).","o":["k_e = 0.087 h^-1; t_1/2 = 8.0 hours","k_e = 0.693 h^-1; t_1/2 = 1.0 hour","k_e = 0.347 h^-1; t_1/2 = 2.0 hours","k_e = 0.173 h^-1; t_1/2 = 4.0 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Elapsed time between C_1 and C_2 is delta-t = 9 - 1 = 8 hours. Because concentration drops 4-fold from 32 mg/L -\u003e 16 mg/L -\u003e 8 mg/L (exactly 2 half-lives) over 8 hours, t_1/2 = 8 / 2 = 4.0 hours. Formally: k_e = ln(C_1 / C_2) / delta-t = ln(32 / 8) / 8 = 1.386 / 8 = 0.173 h^-1, and t_1/2 = 0.693 / 0.173 = 4.0 hours.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03675","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Valproate-Carbapenem (Meropenem) Interaction","q":"A 42-year-old neurosurgical ICU patient with generalised epilepsy well controlled on sodium valproate (baseline serum valproate 82 mg/L) is started on intravenous meropenem for hospital-acquired pneumonia. Within 48 hours, serum valproate plunges to 14 mg/L and the patient suffers breakthrough generalised tonic-clonic seizures. What clinical management and mechanistic explanation apply?","o":["Carbapenems (meropenem, ertapenem, imipenem) rapidly cut serum valproate by 60-90% via inhibition of acylpeptide hydrolase (blocking enterohepatic deconjugation of valproate glucuronide) and enhanced glucuronidation; switch away from carbapenem or add a non-interacting anti-seizure drug (e.g., levetiracetam)","Meropenem induces CYP2C9 slowly over 4 weeks; double the oral valproate dose alone as reliable long-term fix","Meropenem displaces valproate from albumin raising free toxic levels; hold all anti-seizure medications","Valproate inactivates meropenem beta-lactam ring chemically inside the syringe; infuse both faster"],"a":0,"e":"Carbapenems interact severely and rapidly (within 24-48 h) with valproic acid, plunging serum valproate concentrations by 60-90% mainly by irreversibly inhibiting hepatic acylpeptide hydrolase (which normally hydrolyses valproate-glucuronide back to parent valproate) and increasing valproate glucuronidation/erythrocyte uptake. Simply raising valproate dose rarely overcomes the drop reliably; clinicians must either replace the carbapenem with an alternative antibiotic or add/bridge with a non-interacting anti-seizure drug such as levetiracetam.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03676","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Heart Failure with Reduced Ejection Fraction (HFrEF) GDMT","q":"A 60-year-old man with chronic symptomatic HFrEF (LVEF 28%, NYHA Class II) is currently taking enalapril 10 mg BID, carvedilol 12.5 mg BID, spironolactone 25 mg OD, and dapagliflozin 10 mg OD (BP 126/78 mmHg, K+ 4.4 mEq/L, eGFR 68). To further reduce heart failure hospitalisation and cardiovascular mortality, his cardiologist plans to switch enalapril to sacubitril/valsartan (ARNI). What MANDATORY safety washout rule applies during this switch?","o":["Discontinue enalapril and wait at least 36 hours before initiating sacubitril/valsartan to avoid synergistic bradykinin accumulation and life-threatening angioedema","Stop dapagliflozin and carvedilol permanently before starting sacubitril/valsartan","Administer sacubitril/valsartan simultaneously with enalapril for 7 days to overlap RAAS blockade","Switch spironolactone to high-dose ibuprofen to stabilise renal perfusion"],"a":0,"e":"Sacubitril inhibits neprilysin (increasing vasodilatory natriuretic peptides, but also slowing bradykinin breakdown), while ACE inhibitors (enalapril, ramipril) block ACE-mediated bradykinin degradation. Overlapping an ACE inhibitor with sacubitril/valsartan causes dangerous bradykinin accumulation and severe airway angioedema—mandating a strict \u003e=36-hour washout after the last ACE inhibitor dose (note: no washout is needed when switching from an ARB like valsartan/losartan, and the 4 GDMT pillars—ARNI, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2i—should all be maintained).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03677","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) Management","q":"A 23-year-old man with Type 1 Diabetes is admitted with Diabetic Ketoacidosis (blood glucose 480 mg/dL, arterial pH 7.14, HCO3- 10 mEq/L, positive serum ketones) and receives an initial 1 L bolus of 0.9% NaCl. Stat electrolytes return showing serum potassium (K+) = 2.9 mEq/L. What is the CRITICAL immediate pharmacotherapy sequence before starting intravenous regular insulin?","o":["Restrict all IV fluids to prevent hyponatraemia","HOLD intravenous insulin temporarily and infuse intravenous potassium chloride (20-30 mEq/h) until serum K+ rises \u003e= 3.3 mEq/L, because insulin drives K+ intracellularly and would precipitate fatal hypokalaemic arrhythmia/respiratory arrest","Administer oral metformin 1,000 mg with IV sodium bicarbonate 200 mEq bolus","Start high-dose IV insulin bolus (1.0 unit/kg) immediately without checking or replacing potassium"],"a":1,"e":"Despite severe total-body potassium depletion in DKA, extracellular acidosis often masks hypokalaemia initially. When presenting serum K+ is already \u003c3.3 mEq/L, total-body depletion is extreme; administering insulin immediately stimulates Na+/K+-ATPase to shift K+ rapidly into cells, triggering profound hypokalaemia (\u003c2.0 mEq/L), ventricular fibrillation, and respiratory muscle paralysis. Therefore, per ADA/ICMR DKA protocols, insulin MUST be delayed while IV KCl (with cardiac monitoring) raises serum K+ \u003e=3.3 mEq/L.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03678","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacovigilance \u0026 Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Stevens-Johnson Syndrome / Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (SJS/TEN)","q":"Two weeks after initiating carbamazepine for trigeminal neuralgia, a 34-year-old patient develops fever, painful haemorrhagic oral/ocular mucosal erosions, dusky purpuric macules, and epidermal detachment over 35% of body surface area (positive Nikolsky sign — TEN). Which genetic allele predicts carbamazepine-induced SJS/TEN in Asian populations, and what is the primary therapeutic action?","o":["CYP2C19*2 poor metaboliser allele; reduce carbamazepine dose by 50% and continue","SLCO1B1*5 allele; switch to phenytoin immediately because phenytoin never cross-reacts","HLA-B*15:02 allele; immediately and permanently discontinue carbamazepine, provide intensive burn-unit fluid/wound/ocular supportive care, and document lifelong contraindication to aromatic anticonvulsants","G6PD deficiency; apply topical hydrocortisone 1% cream alone while continuing carbamazepine"],"a":2,"e":"SJS (\u003c10% BSA detachment), SJS/TEN overlap (10-30%), and TEN (\u003e30% BSA detachment) are life-threatening T-cell-mediated Type IVc hypersensitivity reactions. Carriage of HLA-B*15:02 strongly predisposes Han Chinese, Southeast Asian, and Indian patients to carbamazepine-induced SJS/TEN (CPIC/CDSCO/FDA recommend screening before initiation). Early permanent withdrawal of the culprit drug (and avoiding cross-reactive aromatic agents like phenytoin/oxcarbazepine/lamotrigine) plus burn-ICU supportive care improves survival.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03679","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"AGS Beers Criteria — Potentially Inappropriate Medications (PIMs)","q":"During medication reconciliation for a 79-year-old inpatient with mild cognitive impairment and recurrent falls, a Pharm.D resident flags oral diphenhydramine (for insomnia), glibenclamide/glyburide (for Type 2 diabetes), and chronic diazepam (for anxiety) under the AGS Beers Criteria. Which clinical harms justify deprescribing these three medications in older adults?","o":["All three medications cause irreversible pulmonary fibrosis exclusively","Diphenhydramine causes hypertension; glibenclamide causes severe hyperglycaemia; diazepam causes insomnia","They are safe in \u003e=75 years provided taken together at noon","Diphenhydramine causes severe central anticholinergic delirium/urinary retention; glibenclamide causes protracted, severe hypoglycaemia via active renally cleared metabolites; long-acting diazepam precipitates falls, hip fractures, and cognitive impairment"],"a":3,"e":"The AGS Beers Criteria identify Potentially Inappropriate Medications (PIMs) in adults \u003e=65 years: first-generation antihistamines (diphenhydramine, chlorpheniramine) carry high anticholinergic burden causing acute confusion, dry mouth, constipation, and urinary retention; long-acting sulfonylureas (glibenclamide/glyburide, chlorpropamide) accumulate in age-related CKD causing prolonged fatal hypoglycaemia (prefer glipizide or non-hypoglycaemic agents); benzodiazepines (diazepam) have prolonged half-life in elderly adipose tissue raising fall, fracture, and delirium risk.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03680","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Biostatistics and Research Methodology","topic":"Selecting Parametric vs Non-Parametric Statistical Tests","q":"Match each clinical research dataset comparison to the most appropriate statistical hypothesis test: 1) Normally distributed systolic BP across 2 independent groups, 2) Normally distributed HbA1c across 3 independent treatment arms, 3) Skewed/ordinal pain scores (VAS 0-10) across 2 independent groups, 4) Proportions of cure vs failure (categorical) across 2 groups.","o":["1-Unpaired (independent) Student\u0027s t-test; 2-One-way ANOVA; 3-Mann-Whitney U test (Wilcoxon rank-sum); 4-Chi-square test (or Fisher\u0027s exact test for small expected counts)","1-Chi-square test; 2-Paired t-test; 3-Pearson r correlation; 4-One-way ANOVA","1-Kaplan-Meier log-rank; 2-McNemar test; 3-Unpaired t-test; 4-Spearman rho","1-Mann-Whitney U test; 2-Chi-square test; 3-Unpaired t-test; 4-Kruskal-Wallis test"],"a":0,"e":"Selecting statistical tests depends on data scale, distribution, independence, and group count: independent 2-group normally distributed continuous outcomes use unpaired Student\u0027s t-test (non-parametric equivalent: Mann-Whitney U for skewed/ordinal data); \u003e=3 independent continuous normal groups use one-way ANOVA (non-parametric: Kruskal-Wallis); categorical frequency/proportion tables use Pearson Chi-square (or Fisher\u0027s exact test when \u003e20% of expected cell counts \u003c5).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03681","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Systematic Approach to Drug Information Queries","q":"A physician on the ICU ward asks a Pharm.D resident: \u0027Can I administer meropenem via continuous infusion to a critically ill patient?\u0027 Following the modified systematic approach to Drug Information (DI), what is the correct initial sequence of actions before searching primary randomized trials?","o":["Provide a verbal \u0027yes\u0027 based on meropenem\u0027s stability at room temperature without checking patient variables","Obtain requestor demographics and patient-specific clinical background (e.g., renal function, pathogen, stability/line access), frame the ultimate question, and consult tertiary sources first before secondary/primary literature","Immediately search PubMed for the most recent single case report and send the PDF directly to the physician","Consult only the product package insert and stop searching if extended infusion is off-label"],"a":1,"e":"The modified systematic approach to responding to DI requests begins with identifying requestor demographics, gathering essential background data (patient age, renal/hepatic function, site of infection, MIC, vascular access, drug compatibility), and formulating the true ultimate question. The literature search then proceeds systematically from tertiary references (textbooks, clinical practice guidelines, Micromedex/Lexicomp) to secondary indexing databases (PubMed/Embase) and primary original research articles for the latest evidence (such as meropenem room-temperature stability limits of ~4–8 hours requiring frequent bag changes).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03682","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Therapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Phenytoin Therapeutic Drug Monitoring","q":"A 54-year-old man with normal renal function (CrCl 85 mL/min) and nephrotic-range hypoalbuminemia (serum albumin 2.0 g/dL) is receiving oral phenytoin 300 mg/day for seizure prophylaxis. His steady-state total serum phenytoin concentration is 8.0 µg/mL, and he is seizure-free without nystagmus or ataxia. Using the Winter-Tozer equation for normal renal function, what is his estimated albumin-corrected phenytoin concentration and the appropriate clinical action?","o":["26.7 µg/mL (supratherapeutic); hold phenytoin immediately for 48 hours","4.0 µg/mL (subtherapeutic); add a loading dose of 1,000 mg IV immediately","16.0 µg/mL (within therapeutic range 10–20 µg/mL); maintain current dose and monitor clinically","8.0 µg/mL (subtherapeutic); double the daily maintenance dose to 600 mg/day"],"a":2,"e":"Phenytoin is ~90% bound to serum albumin; in hypoalbuminemia, the unbound active fraction increases, making total phenytoin levels appear deceptively low. Using the classic Winter-Tozer equation in patients with normal renal function (CrCl \u003e= 10-20 mL/min): C_corrected = C_measured / [(0.2 × Albumin in g/dL) + 0.1] = 8.0 / [(0.2 × 2.0) + 0.1] = 8.0 / 0.5 = 16.0 µg/mL. Because 16.0 µg/mL lies well within the 10–20 µg/mL reference range and the patient is seizure-free without toxicity, the current regimen should be maintained. (Note: if CrCl \u003c 10 mL/min, a coefficient of 0.1 instead of 0.2 is used, and direct free phenytoin measurement is preferred when available.)","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03683","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Therapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Vancomycin TDM Guidelines","q":"A 62-year-old man with MRSA bacteremia (vancomycin broth microdilution MIC = 1 mg/L) is started on intravenous vancomycin. Under current consensus TDM guidelines (ASHP/IDSA/PIDS/SIDP), what is the preferred pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic target to maximize efficacy while minimizing vancomycin-associated acute kidney injury?","o":["An individualized AUC24/MIC ratio of 400 to 600 mg·h/L (assuming MIC = 1 mg/L) using Bayesian or two-sample first-order equations","Maintaining routine trough concentrations strictly between 20 and 25 µg/mL for all infections","Targeting a peak concentration above 60 µg/mL and trough below 5 µg/mL","Relying solely on a fixed 1 g q12h dose without any serum level monitoring regardless of weight or renal function"],"a":0,"e":"Updated ASHP/IDSA/PIDS/SIDP consensus guidelines recommend transitioning from trough-only monitoring (previously 15–20 µg/mL) to AUC-guided dosing targeting an AUC24/MIC of 400–600 mg·h/L (assuming an MIC of 1 mg/L) for serious MRSA infections. Troughs of 15–20 µg/mL frequently correspond to AUC24 \u003e 600 mg·h/L, increasing nephrotoxicity risk without additional clinical benefit; Bayesian software or two post-distribution levels (peak and trough) enable accurate AUC estimation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03684","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacovigilance","topic":"ADR Causality Assessment","q":"A patient develops serum creatinine elevation 5 days after starting gentamicin. There are previous conclusive reports of gentamicin nephrotoxicity (+1), the reaction appeared after drug administration (+2), improved upon drug discontinuation (+1), no alternative causes could explain the acute kidney injury (+2), and serum trough gentamicin was toxic (+1). Rechallenge was not performed (0), no placebo was given (0), and all other questions scored 0. Using the Naranjo ADR Probability Scale, what is the total score and causality category?","o":["Total score = 0; Doubtful ADR","Total score = 3; Possible ADR","Total score = 10; Definite ADR","Total score = 7; Probable ADR"],"a":3,"e":"Summing the Naranjo Algorithm points: previous conclusive reports (+1) + appeared after suspected drug (+2) + improved on dechallenge (+1) + no alternative causes (+2) + toxic drug concentration (+1) = 7. Under the Naranjo causality classification: Definite \u003e= 9, Probable = 5–8, Possible = 1–4, Doubtful \u003c= 0. Thus, a score of 7 corresponds to a \u0027Probable\u0027 adverse drug reaction, which should be reported to the ADR Monitoring Centre under the Pharmacovigilance Programme of India (PvPI).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03685","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacovigilance","topic":"ADR Classification and Pharmacogenomics","q":"A 28-year-old man of Southeast Asian ancestry develops fever, painful mucosal erosions, and blistering skin detachment involving 15% body surface area (Stevens-Johnson syndrome/TEN overlap) 14 days after initiating carbamazepine for trigeminal neuralgia. Under the Rawlins-Thompson classification, how is this ADR categorized, and which HLA allele strongly predisposes to it?","o":["Type B (Bizarre/idiosyncratic, dose-independent); associated with HLA-B*15:02","Type A (Augmented, predictable, dose-dependent); associated with CYP2C9*3","Type C (Chronic cumulative); associated with TPMT deficiency","Type D (Delayed carcinogenic); associated with NAT2 slow acetylator status"],"a":0,"e":"Carbamazepine-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) represent a severe Type B (bizarre, immunologically mediated, non-dose-dependent, unpredictable from primary pharmacology) hypersensitivity reaction. It is strongly linked to the HLA-B*15:02 allele in individuals of Asian ancestry (including Indian and Southeast Asian populations), prompting regulatory recommendations for HLA-B*15:02 screening prior to starting carbamazepine in at-risk groups and immediate permanent drug withdrawal upon reaction onset.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03686","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics \u0026 Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Cockcroft-Gault Creatinine Clearance","q":"A 68-year-old man (weight 72 kg, equal to ideal body weight; serum creatinine 2.5 mg/dL) is diagnosed with acute deep vein thrombosis and prescribed therapeutic enoxaparin. Using the Cockcroft-Gault equation, what is his estimated CrCl and the required therapeutic enoxaparin regimen?","o":["CrCl = 24.5 mL/min; increase enoxaparin to 1.5 mg/kg subcutaneously every 12 hours","CrCl = 28.8 mL/min; reduce therapeutic enoxaparin to 1 mg/kg subcutaneously once every 24 hours","CrCl = 57.6 mL/min; maintain standard enoxaparin 1 mg/kg subcutaneously every 12 hours","CrCl = 42.0 mL/min; double the enoxaparin dose to compensate for uremia"],"a":1,"e":"Using Cockcroft-Gault for a male: CrCl = [(140 - Age) × Weight (kg)] / [72 × SCr (mg/dL)] = [(140 - 68) × 72] / [72 × 2.5] = (72 × 72) / (72 × 2.5) = 72 / 2.5 = 28.8 mL/min. Because enoxaparin is predominantly cleared renally and accumulates in severe renal impairment (CrCl \u003c 30 mL/min), increasing major hemorrhage risk, the therapeutic DVT/PE dose must be reduced from 1 mg/kg every 12 hours to 1 mg/kg subcutaneously once daily (every 24 hours), or unfractionated heparin may be considered.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03687","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics \u0026 Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Child-Pugh Classification","q":"A 56-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis has total bilirubin 3.4 mg/dL (3 points), serum albumin 2.6 g/dL (3 points), INR 2.4 (3 points), moderate ascites (3 points), and mild grade 1–2 hepatic encephalopathy (2 points). He requires voriconazole for invasive aspergillosis. What is his Child-Pugh class and appropriate dosing strategy?","o":["Child-Pugh Class A (6 points); administer full standard maintenance dose without monitoring","Child-Pugh Class B (8 points); double the maintenance dose due to hypoalbuminemia","Child-Pugh Class C (14 points); voriconazole clearance is severely reduced, requiring cautious evaluation/avoidance or marked maintenance dose reduction with close TDM","Child-Pugh Class C (14 points); give full standard dose because voriconazole is 98% renally excreted unchanged"],"a":2,"e":"The five Child-Pugh-Turcotte parameters (bilirubin, albumin, INR, ascites, encephalopathy) sum to 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 2 = 14 points, placing the patient in Child-Pugh Class C (10–15 points; severe hepatic impairment). Voriconazole is extensively metabolized by hepatic CYP2C19, CYP2C9, and CYP3A4; in Child-Pugh A/B cirrhosis, maintenance dosing is halved after a standard loading dose, while in Child-Pugh C cirrhosis, clearance drops profoundly and voriconazole should only be used if benefit outweighs hepatotoxicity risk, with substantial dose reduction and strict therapeutic drug monitoring (trough 1–5.5 mg/L).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03688","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Evidence-Based Pharmacotherapy","topic":"Heart Failure with Reduced Ejection Fraction (HFrEF)","q":"A 64-year-old man with symptomatic HFrEF (LVEF 30%, NYHA Class III, BP 122/76 mmHg, K+ 4.4 mEq/L, eGFR 58 mL/min/1.73 m²) is currently taking enalapril 10 mg twice daily, carvedilol, spironolactone, and dapagliflozin. His cardiologist plans to switch enalapril to sacubitril/valsartan (ARNI). Which transition protocol is mandatory to prevent life-threatening angioedema?","o":["Switch directly from enalapril to sacubitril/valsartan within 2 hours without any washout","Administer sacubitril/valsartan simultaneously with the evening dose of enalapril for 7 days to avoid rebound hypertension","Discontinue dapagliflozin and spironolactone permanently before starting sacubitril/valsartan","Discontinue enalapril and observe a strict minimum 36-hour washout period before administering the first dose of sacubitril/valsartan"],"a":3,"e":"Sacubitril inhibits neprilysin (neutral endopeptidase), which degrades natriuretic peptides as well as bradykinin and substance P. Concurrent ACE inhibition (enalapril) blocks kininase II, resulting in synergistic bradykinin accumulation and a high risk of severe, potentially fatal angioedema. Therefore, a mandatory 36-hour washout period after the last ACE inhibitor dose is required before initiating sacubitril/valsartan (note: no washout is needed when switching from an ARB like valsartan/losartan). All four GDMT pillars—ARNI, beta-blocker, MRA, and SGLT2 inhibitor—should otherwise be maintained for mortality reduction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03689","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Evidence-Based Pharmacotherapy","topic":"Warfarin Management and Reversal","q":"A 69-year-old woman on chronic warfarin (target INR 2.0–3.0) for mechanical mitral valve replacement completes a 7-day course of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection and presents to the ED with melena, hypotension (BP 86/54 mmHg), hemoglobin 7.1 g/dL, and INR 8.4. Alongside holding warfarin and red-cell resuscitation, what is the optimal immediate pharmacologic reversal regimen?","o":["Four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (4F-PCC) plus slow intravenous vitamin K1 (phytonadione 5–10 mg)","Subcutaneous vitamin K1 10 mg with fresh frozen plasma only if INR \u003e 10","Oral vitamin K1 2.5 mg alone and recheck INR in 48 hours","Protamine sulfate 50 mg IV bolus"],"a":0,"e":"Sulfamethoxazole potently inhibits CYP2C9, blocking clearance of the active S-warfarin enantiomer and precipitating severe supratherapeutic INR. For warfarin-associated major/life-threatening bleeding (hemodynamic instability, acute GI hemorrhage), guidelines recommend immediate administration of 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (4F-PCC, containing factors II, VII, IX, X, plus proteins C and S) for rapid factor replacement within minutes, paired with slow IV vitamin K1 (5–10 mg) to sustain endogenous synthesis of clotting factors once PCC wears off.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03690","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Evidence-Based Pharmacotherapy","topic":"Diabetic Kidney Disease (KDIGO)","q":"A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, eGFR 48 mL/min/1.73 m², and UACR 420 mg/g on maximally tolerated telmisartan is initiated on empagliflozin 10 mg daily. Two weeks later, his eGFR dips transiently from 48 to 43 mL/min/1.73 m² without symptoms or hyperkalemia. What physiological mechanism explains this expected early eGFR dip and long-term renoprotection?","o":["Systemic osmotic diuresis triggers irreversible renal medullary ischemia requiring immediate drug discontinuation","Restoring distal sodium-chloride delivery to the macula densa activates tubuloglomerular feedback, constricting the dilated afferent arteriole and reducing intraglomerular hyperfiltration","Activation of juxtaglomerular renin release dilates the afferent arteriole and raises glomerular pressure","Direct blockade of proximal tubular organic anion transporters causes reversible acute tubular necrosis"],"a":1,"e":"In diabetes, upregulated SGLT2-mediated proximal Na+-glucose co-reabsorption reduces NaCl delivery to the macula densa, suppressing tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) and causing pathologic afferent arteriolar vasodilation and glomerular hyperfiltration. SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin, canagliflozin) restore NaCl delivery to the macula densa, reactivating adenosine-mediated TGF to constrict the afferent arteriole and lower intraglomerular pressure—manifesting as a benign, reversible early eGFR dip (\u003c30%) that predicts long-term slowing of CKD progression.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03691","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Aminophylline-to-Theophylline Salt Factor Calculation","q":"A patient with severe COPD exacerbation has an estimated theophylline clearance (CL) of 2.4 L/h and requires a steady-state plasma theophylline concentration (C_ss) of 12 mg/L via continuous intravenous aminophylline infusion (salt factor S = 0.80, i.e., 80% active theophylline). What continuous maintenance infusion rate of aminophylline (mg/h) should be ordered?","o":["28.8 mg/h","23.0 mg/h","36.0 mg/h","45.0 mg/h"],"a":2,"e":"First calculate the required active theophylline infusion rate: R_theophylline = C_ss x CL = 12 mg/L x 2.4 L/h = 28.8 mg/h. Because intravenous aminophylline (theophylline ethylenediamine) contains only 80% active theophylline (salt factor S = 0.80), the required aminophylline salt infusion rate = 28.8 / 0.80 = 36.0 mg/h.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03692","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Tacrolimus CYP3A4/P-gp Interactions","q":"A 41-year-old renal transplant recipient maintained on tacrolimus (stable trough 8.5 ng/mL), mycophenolate mofetil, and prednisolone is started on daily rifampin for disseminated tuberculosis. Ten days later, her tacrolimus trough plunges to 1.4 ng/mL alongside a rising serum creatinine (acute T-cell-mediated rejection suspected). What pharmacokinetic interaction explains this rapid fall in tacrolimus exposure?","o":["Rifampin strongly induces both hepatic/intestinal CYP3A4 and intestinal P-glycoprotein (P-gp) via PXR activation, accelerating tacrolimus first-pass and systemic clearance and risking acute graft rejection","Rifampin potently inhibits CYP3A4 causing tacrolimus accumulation","Rifampin chelates tacrolimus in gastric acid via magnesium ions","Mycophenolate displaces tacrolimus from red blood cell FKBP-12 receptors"],"a":0,"e":"Tacrolimus is a narrow-therapeutic-index substrate of intestinal/hepatic CYP3A4 and P-gp efflux. Rifampin is one of the most potent known PXR transcriptional inducers of both CYP3A4 and P-gp, often slashing tacrolimus exposure by \u003e70-80% within 1-2 weeks and precipitating acute transplant rejection unless tacrolimus doses are aggressively titrated (2- to 5-fold) with intensive trough TDM—or rifampin is substituted with rifabutin (a much weaker inducer) under specialist oversight.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03693","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Acute Ischaemic Stroke Thrombolysis (Alteplase)","q":"A 64-year-old man arrives at the emergency stroke unit 2.5 hours after sudden right hemiparesis and expressive aphasia. Non-contrast CT head excludes intracranial haemorrhage. His blood pressure is 204/116 mmHg and capillary glucose is 138 mg/dL. Prior to administering intravenous alteplase (0.9 mg/kg, max 90 mg) within the 4.5-hour window, what blood pressure management is MANDATORY?","o":["Drop systolic BP precipitously to \u003c 100 mmHg using sublingual nifedipine","Give full-dose IV unfractionated heparin instead of alteplase","Administer alteplase immediately at 204/116 mmHg and push 325 mg oral aspirin concurrently","Lower blood pressure gently with IV labetalol or nicardipine to strictly \u003c 185/110 mmHg BEFORE alteplase bolus and maintain \u003c 180/105 mmHg for at least 24 hours (withholding antiplatelets/anticoagulants for 24 h)"],"a":3,"e":"Per AHA/ASA and Indian Stroke Guidelines, eligible patients presenting within \u003c=4.5 hours of last known well (with non-contrast CT excluding haemorrhage) must have BP reduced cautiously using titratable IV agents (labetalol 10-20 mg IV or nicardipine infusion) to \u003c185/110 mmHg prior to starting IV alteplase/tenecteplase, and kept \u003c180/105 mmHg for the first 24 h to minimise symptomatic intracerebral haemorrhage (sICH). Antiplatelets and anticoagulants are held for 24 h post-thrombolysis until repeat neuroimaging clears bleeding.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03694","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Community-Acquired Acute Bacterial Meningitis","q":"A 62-year-old man presents with fever, neck stiffness, photophobia, and altered mental status (suspected acute community-acquired bacterial meningitis). Blood cultures are drawn and lumbar puncture is queued. What empiric intravenous antimicrobial and adjunctive corticosteroid regimen is indicated, and WHEN must dexamethasone be given?","o":["IV Ceftriaxone 2 g q12h + IV Vancomycin (+ IV Ampicillin 2 g q4h because age \u003e 50 years covers Listeria monocytogenes); administer IV Dexamethasone 10 mg q6h BEFORE or WITH the first antibiotic dose","IV Cefazolin monotherapy + oral prednisolone started 48 hours after antibiotics","IV Vancomycin monotherapy + IV mannitol only","Oral ciprofloxacin + azithromycin without corticosteroids"],"a":0,"e":"Adults \u003e50 years with suspected acute bacterial meningitis require coverage for Streptococcus pneumoniae (including ceftriaxone-resistant strains -\u003e vancomycin), Neisseria meningitidis (ceftriaxone), AND Listeria monocytogenes (ampicillin, because cephalosporins lack Listeria activity). Adjunctive IV dexamethasone (0.15 mg/kg or 10 mg q6h for 4 days) MUST be given 10-20 min before or simultaneously with the first antibiotic dose to blunt subarachnoid inflammatory cytokine release triggered by bacterial lysis (reducing mortality and hearing loss in pneumococcal meningitis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03695","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Chronic Kidney Disease Mineral and Bone Disorder (CKD-MBD)","q":"A 58-year-old man on maintenance haemodialysis (Stage 5 CKD) has hyperphosphataemia (serum phosphate 6.8 mg/dL), elevated intact PTH (420 pg/mL), borderline-high corrected serum calcium (10.1 mg/dL), and coronary artery calcification on chest CT. Which oral phosphate binder and administration timing should the clinical pharmacist recommend?","o":["Oral sodium phosphate enema solution daily","Sevelamer carbonate (non-calcium-based phosphate binder) taken immediately WITH every meal and snack to bind dietary phosphate in the gut lumen without adding exogenous calcium load","Furosemide 80 mg IV twice daily to excrete phosphate in anuric dialysis","High-dose calcium carbonate 3 g at bedtime on an empty stomach"],"a":1,"e":"In Stage 5 CKD-MBD with hyperphosphataemia, borderline-high calcium, and vascular calcification, KDIGO guidelines recommend restricting calcium-based binders (calcium acetate/carbonate) and choosing a non-calcium-based phosphate binder (sevelamer carbonate or lanthanum carbonate). All phosphate binders work locally in the GI lumen by binding dietary phosphorus and MUST be swallowed with meals/snacks (never on an empty stomach at bedtime).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03696","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Toxicology","topic":"Beta-Blocker \u0026 Calcium Channel Blocker Toxicity","q":"A 28-year-old patient presents with refractory bradycardia (34/min), hypotension (66/38 mmHg), and cardiogenic shock 3 hours after an intentional overdose of sustained-release propranolol and verapamil, unresponsive to atropine and isotonic saline boluses. Alongside vasopressors and IV calcium, which two targeted metabolic/inotropic antidotal therapies bypass blocked beta-adrenergic and L-type calcium channels to restore myocardial contractility?","o":["Intravenous flumazenil and oral activated charcoal in an unprotected airway","Intravenous magnesium sulfate 10 g bolus and furosemide","High-dose intravenous Glucagon (activates myocardial Gs-coupled glucagon receptors independent of beta-receptors to raise intracellular cAMP) and High-Dose Insulin Euglycaemic Therapy (HIET, 1 unit/kg bolus + 0.5-1 unit/kg/h infusion with dextrose and K+ monitoring)","Intravenous physostigmine and high-dose oral nifedipine"],"a":2,"e":"In severe beta-blocker and non-dihydropyridine CCB (verapamil/diltiazem) poisoning, myocardial cells lose cAMP/Ca2+-mediated contractility and develop insulin-resistant metabolic shock. High-dose IV glucagon (3-10 mg bolus + infusion) stimulates distinct Gs-coupled glucagon receptors to elevate cAMP bypassing beta-1 blockade (note antiemetic pre-treatment for vomiting), while High-Dose Insulin Euglycaemic Therapy (HIET) shifts stressed myocardium to efficient carbohydrate metabolism and exerts direct positive inotropy alongside IV calcium salts.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03697","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Biostatistics and Research Methodology","topic":"Cox Proportional Hazards Model \u0026 Hazard Ratio (HR)","q":"In a 4-year randomised cardiovascular outcomes trial comparing Drug X against placebo using Kaplan-Meier survival curves and Cox proportional hazards regression, the primary composite endpoint yields a Hazard Ratio (HR) of 0.65 (95% CI: 0.48 to 0.88; p = 0.005). How should this result be interpreted?","o":["Drug X reduced risk by 65% and is non-significant because p \u003c 0.05","Drug X increased event risk by 65% and is non-significant because the CI excludes 0","The null value for a Hazard Ratio is 0.00, so 0.48-0.88 proves harm","Drug X reduced the instantaneous hazard/rate of the primary event by 35% over follow-up compared with placebo, and the difference is statistically significant because the 95% CI (0.48-0.88) lies completely below 1.00"],"a":3,"e":"For ratio measures of effect (Hazard Ratio, Relative Risk, Odds Ratio), the null hypothesis value of \u0027no difference\u0027 is 1.00 (not 0). An HR of 0.65 means the event rate in the Drug X group at any point in time was 0.65 times that of placebo—a 35% relative hazard reduction (1 - 0.65 = 0.35). Because the entire 95% confidence interval (0.48 to 0.88) does not cross 1.00 (consistent with p = 0.005 \u003c 0.05), the reduction is statistically significant.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03698","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"AGS Beers Criteria","q":"A 78-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes, mild cognitive impairment, and insomnia visits the geriatric clinic taking glibenclamide (glyburide) 5 mg twice daily and diphenhydramine 50 mg at bedtime for sleep. According to the AGS Beers Criteria, why should both drugs be deprescribed or replaced?","o":["Glibenclamide carries a high risk of severe prolonged hypoglycemia in older adults, and diphenhydramine has strong anticholinergic activity causing delirium, falls, and cognitive worsening","Glibenclamide causes hyperkalemia while diphenhydramine induces severe bradycardia via beta-1 blockade","Both drugs require hepatic CYP2D6 induction leading to rapid therapeutic failure","Diphenhydramine causes lactic acidosis while glibenclamide triggers peripheral edema"],"a":0,"e":"The AGS Beers Criteria strongly recommend avoiding long-acting sulfonylureas (glibenclamide/glyburide, chlorpropamide) in older adults because active renal metabolites accumulate and precipitate severe, prolonged hypoglycemia (safer shorter-acting options like glipizide or non-hypoglycemic DPP-4/SGLT2 agents are preferred). First-generation antihistamines like diphenhydramine possess potent central antimuscarinic properties that impair memory, precipitate acute delirium, urinary retention, orthostasis, and falls.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03699","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Therapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Extended-Interval Aminoglycoside Dosing","q":"A hospital antimicrobial stewardship protocol recommends extended-interval (once-daily, 7 mg/kg) gentamicin using the Hartford nomogram for a young adult with Gram-negative pyelonephritis and normal renal function, rather than divided q8h dosing. Which pharmacodynamic and toxicological principle supports extended-interval dosing?","o":["Maximizes Cmax/MIC (\u003e= 8–10) for concentration-dependent killing and post-antibiotic effect while allowing a drug-free trough interval (\u003c1 µg/mL) that limits saturable renal cortical uptake","Maintains serum concentrations continuously above 4 × MIC for 100% of the dosing interval (time-dependent killing)","Prevents aminoglycoside binding to bacterial 30S ribosomes during stationary growth","Eliminates the need for any serum level monitoring even in severe acute kidney injury or pregnancy"],"a":0,"e":"Aminoglycosides exhibit concentration-dependent bactericidal activity (optimal Cmax/MIC ratio \u003e= 8–10) and a prolonged post-antibiotic effect (PAE) persisting after serum levels fall below MIC. Meanwhile, proximal tubular uptake of aminoglycosides via megalin is saturable: high single daily peaks cause less total renal cortical accumulation than sustained trough exposure, and the prolonged low-trough period allows cortical washout and prevents adaptive bacterial resistance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03700","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Therapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Digoxin TDM and Drug Interactions","q":"A 71-year-old man maintained on digoxin 0.25 mg/day for atrial fibrillation has oral amiodarone initiated for recurrent ventricular tachycardia without adjusting his digoxin dose. Three weeks later, he presents with anorexia, vomiting, bidirectional ventricular tachycardia, serum digoxin 3.8 ng/mL, and serum potassium 5.9 mEq/L. What explains this interaction and what is the specific life-saving antidote?","o":["Amiodarone inhibits P-glycoprotein efflux, doubling serum digoxin (requiring an empirical 30–50% digoxin dose reduction upfront); treat acute life-threatening toxicity with digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Digoxin Immune Fab)","Amiodarone induces renal CYP3A4 clearance of digoxin; treat immediately with intravenous calcium gluconate and loop diuretics alone","Amiodarone blocks GI absorption of digoxin; treat with atropine and hypertonic saline","Amiodarone displaces digoxin from red blood cells without altering clearance; treat with activated charcoal after 3 weeks of therapy"],"a":0,"e":"Amiodarone (along with verapamil, quinidine, and clarithromycin) potently inhibits P-glycoprotein (MDR1) in the intestine and renal tubules, decreasing renal and non-renal digoxin clearance and increasing steady-state digoxin concentrations by 70–100%. Empirical 30–50% digoxin dose reduction with TDM is mandatory when starting amiodarone. In acute life-threatening digoxin toxicity—evidenced by malignant arrhythmias (bidirectional VT) and hyperkalemia \u003e 5.0–5.5 mEq/L from systemic Na+/K+-ATPase paralysis—Digoxin Immune Fab is the specific definitive antidote.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03701","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacovigilance","topic":"ADR Severity and Preventability Scales","q":"A patient with a clearly documented penicillin anaphylaxis history in the medical chart is inadvertently prescribed amoxicillin-clavulanate, develops generalized urticaria and bronchospasm requiring drug discontinuation, epinephrine/hydrocortisone therapy, and a 2-day extension of inpatient hospitalization. Using the Schumock-Thornton preventability criteria and Hartwig-Siegel severity scale, how is this ADR classified?","o":["Not an ADR because it resulted from a documented allergy","Definitely preventable (Schumock-Thornton) and Severe / Level 5 (Hartwig-Siegel, requiring intensive care or prolonged hospitalization)","Probably preventable and Fatal / Level 7","Unpreventable and Mild / Level 1 (no change in therapy required)"],"a":1,"e":"Under the modified Schumock-Thornton criteria, an ADR is \u0027Definitely preventable\u0027 if any criterion is met—such as administering a drug despite a documented history of allergy or previous reaction to that medication class. Under the Hartwig-Siegel ADR Severity Assessment Scale, Levels 1–2 are Mild, Levels 3–4 are Moderate (drug held/discontinued + antidote/treatment or \u003e=1 day stay—specifically Level 4b/5 when prolonging hospitalization/intensive care: Level 5 explicitly covers Severe ADRs requiring intensive medical care or causing permanent harm/prolonged stay).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03702","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacovigilance","topic":"Spontaneous Reporting Signal Detection","q":"In post-marketing signal detection within spontaneous ADR databases such as WHO VigiBase or PvPI, what is the purpose of calculating the Proportional Reporting Ratio (PRR) or Reporting Odds Ratio (ROR), and what does the \u0027Weber effect\u0027 describe?","o":["PRR/ROR calculate exact population incidence rates per 10,000 exposed patients; the Weber effect is permanent under-reporting in year 1 only","PRR/ROR measure absolute bioequivalence; the Weber effect refers to enzyme auto-induction","PRR/ROR detect disproportional reporting of a specific drug-event pair compared to all other drugs (hypothesis generation, not true incidence); the Weber effect is a peak in ADR reporting around the end of a drug\u0027s second year post-launch followed by a decline","PRR/ROR replace randomized controlled trials for causality proof; the Weber effect denotes increasing reports indefinitely over 20 years"],"a":2,"e":"Spontaneous reporting systems lack true denominator data (total exposed patients) and suffer from under-reporting, so quantitative signal detection uses disproportionality metrics—such as Proportional Reporting Ratio (PRR), Reporting Odds Ratio (ROR), or Bayesian IC—to flag statistical signals requiring formal pharmacoepidemiologic verification. The classic Weber effect describes an epidemiological reporting pattern where spontaneous ADR submissions rise after market launch, peak near the end of the second year, and subsequently decline despite stable prescribing.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03703","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics \u0026 Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Metformin Dosing in Chronic Kidney Disease","q":"A 66-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes taking metformin 1,000 mg twice daily presents for routine follow-up, and her eGFR has declined from 52 to 26 mL/min/1.73 m². According to KDIGO and regulatory guidelines, what medication adjustment is mandatory?","o":["Switch metformin to immediate-release glibenclamide 10 mg twice daily","Continue metformin 1,000 mg twice daily unchanged down to eGFR 10 mL/min/1.73 m²","Double metformin to 2,000 mg twice daily to overcome reduced renal clearance","Discontinue metformin permanently because eGFR is \u003c30 mL/min/1.73 m² (risk of metformin-associated lactic acidosis) and transition to alternative glycemic therapy such as an SGLT2i/GLP-1 RA or insulin"],"a":3,"e":"Metformin is excreted unchanged via renal glomerular filtration and tubular secretion (OCT2/MATE). Per KDIGO Diabetes in CKD and FDA/CDSCO labeling: metformin may be initiated or continued at full dose when eGFR \u003e= 45 mL/min/1.73 m², continued at a reduced maximum dose (\u003c= 1,000 mg/day, do not initiate newly) when eGFR is 30–44 mL/min/1.73 m², and is strictly contraindicated when eGFR falls below 30 mL/min/1.73 m² due to elevated risk of fatal metformin-associated lactic acidosis (MALA).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03704","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics \u0026 Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"First-Pass Effect and Portosystemic Shunting","q":"In healthy adults, oral propranolol has a high hepatic extraction ratio (ER ≈ 0.75) and low oral bioavailability (F ≈ 25%). When administering oral propranolol for variceal bleeding prophylaxis to a patient with Child-Pugh Class C cirrhosis and extensive portosystemic collateral shunting, why must the initial oral dose be dramatically lowered compared with healthy adults?","o":["Bypassing hepatic first-pass extraction via portosystemic shunts and impaired hepatocyte function sharply increases oral bioavailability (F = 1 - ER approaches 70–90%), risking profound bradycardia and hypotension","Portal hypertension doubles renal glomerular filtration of unchanged propranolol","Ascites fluid selectively binds propranolol in the stomach, requiring higher oral doses","Cirrhosis increases hepatic CYP2D6 activity threefold, accelerating oral clearance"],"a":0,"e":"For high-extraction drugs (ER \u003e 0.7, such as propranolol, morphine, verapamil, lidocaine), oral bioavailability is approximately F = 1 - ER (assuming complete gut absorption). In decompensated cirrhosis, intrahepatic and extrahepatic portosystemic shunts allow absorbed portal blood to bypass functional hepatocytes directly into the systemic circulation, simultaneously reducing intrinsic clearance and collapsing first-pass extraction—multiplying oral F several-fold and requiring sharp initial oral dose reductions with careful heart rate/BP titration.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03705","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Evidence-Based Pharmacotherapy","topic":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (GOLD Guidelines)","q":"A 67-year-old ex-smoker with COPD (GOLD Group E: two moderate exacerbations requiring oral prednisone in the past year; mMRC dyspnea grade 3) has a peripheral blood eosinophil count of 360 cells/µL and no history of recurrent pneumonia. Under current GOLD guidelines, which initial or escalated maintenance inhaler regimen is most strongly supported?","o":["Oral roflumilast monotherapy in place of all inhalers","Single-inhaler triple therapy combining LABA + LAMA + Inhaled Corticosteroid (ICS)","Short-acting beta-agonist (SABA) monotherapy as needed only","Inhaled corticosteroid monotherapy without any bronchodilator"],"a":1,"e":"Under the GOLD ABE classification, patients in Group E (\u003e= 2 moderate exacerbations or \u003e= 1 hospitalization in the past year) start with LABA + LAMA dual bronchodilation; however, GOLD specifically recommends considering initial LABA + LAMA + ICS triple therapy in Group E patients whose blood eosinophil count is \u003e= 300 cells/µL, as high eosinophils predict strong ICS exacerbation reduction while avoiding the pneumonia risk seen with inappropriate ICS use in low-eosinophil (\u003c100 cells/µL) COPD.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03706","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Evidence-Based Pharmacotherapy","topic":"Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP)","q":"A 62-year-old man with no history of MRSA or Pseudomonas colonization is admitted to a general medical ward (non-ICU) for community-acquired pneumonia (lobar consolidation, SpO2 91%, CURB-65 score 2). Which empiric antimicrobial regimen aligns with IDSA/ATS and ICMR antimicrobial stewardship guidelines?","o":["Intravenous vancomycin plus meropenem plus colistin upfront for all non-ICU CAP","Oral metronidazole monotherapy","Intravenous beta-lactam (e.g., ceftriaxone) plus a macrolide (e.g., azithromycin), or monotherapy with a respiratory fluoroquinolone (e.g., levofloxacin)","Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate combined with intravenous gentamicin"],"a":2,"e":"For hospitalized non-ICU adults with CAP lacking risk factors for MRSA or Pseudomonas aeruginosa, IDSA/ATS and ICMR stewardship guidelines recommend empiric coverage of both typical pathogens (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae) and atypical pathogens (Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Legionella) using a beta-lactam (ceftriaxone, cefotaxime, or ampicillin-sulbactam) plus a macrolide (azithromycin) or doxycycline, or alternatively monotherapy with a respiratory fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin or moxifloxacin), with de-escalation and IV-to-oral switch once clinically stable.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03707","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Evidence-Based Pharmacotherapy","topic":"Acute Coronary Syndrome (NSTEMI)","q":"A 68-year-old man presents with an acute non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI) undergoing percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). His medical history is notable for a prior ischemic stroke 2 years ago and current weight 64 kg. Alongside aspirin and high-intensity atorvastatin, which oral P2Y12 inhibitor is strictly contraindicated in this patient?","o":["Ticagrelor","Clopidogrel","Aspirin chewable loading dose 300 mg","Prasugrel (due to significantly increased risk of fatal and intracranial hemorrhage in patients with prior stroke or TIA)"],"a":3,"e":"In the TRITON-TIMI 38 trial, patients with a prior history of transient ischemic attack (TIA) or stroke who received prasugrel had net clinical harm driven by markedly higher rates of major and intracranial bleeding; consequently, prasugrel carries a black-box contraindication in any patient with prior stroke or TIA (and caution in weight \u003c60 kg or age \u003e=75 years). Ticagrelor (preferred in ACS without hemorrhagic stroke history) or clopidogrel are appropriate alternatives alongside aspirin.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03708","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Calvert Formula for Carboplatin Dosing","q":"A 54-year-old woman with ovarian carcinoma has a measured glomerular filtration rate (GFR) of 75 mL/min and is scheduled for intravenous carboplatin targeting an area under the curve (AUC) of 5 mg*min/mL. Using the Calvert formula, what total dose of carboplatin (mg) should be prepared?","o":["500 mg","375 mg","625 mg","125 mg"],"a":0,"e":"Because carboplatin is cleared predominantly by glomerular filtration plus a constant non-renal clearance (~25 mL/min), the Calvert equation calculates total dose in mg directly (not mg/m2): Total Dose (mg) = Target AUC (mg*min/mL) x [GFR (mL/min) + 25] = 5 x (75 + 25) = 5 x 100 = 500 mg (note: USFDA advises capping GFR at 125 mL/min when estimated via Cockcroft-Gault).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03709","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Methotrexate + Cotrimoxazole / NSAID Toxicity \u0026 Leucovorin Rescue","q":"A 59-year-old man receiving oral methotrexate 15 mg/week for rheumatoid arthritis is inadvertently prescribed a 10-day course of oral cotrimoxazole (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) plus high-dose diclofenac for painful cellulitis. Two weeks later he develops severe oral mucositis, acute kidney injury, and profound pancytopenia. What combined mechanism triggered this toxicity, and what specific biochemical antidote salvages normal host cells?","o":["Methotrexate chelates trimethoprim in the stomach; administer activated charcoal 2 weeks later","Diclofenac lowers renal perfusion and blocks OAT3 tubular secretion of methotrexate while cotrimoxazole adds synergistic DHFR folate blockade and displaces/blocks renal elimination; administer intravenous Leucovorin (folinic acid, N5-formyl-THF) to bypass DHFR inhibition","Cotrimoxazole induces CYP2C19 activating methotrexate into cisplatin; administer intravenous Mesna","Diclofenac accelerates hepatic glucuronidation of methotrexate; administer oral folic acid 0.4 mg only"],"a":1,"e":"Methotrexate is cleared \u003e80% unchanged via glomerular filtration and active OAT1/OAT3 proximal tubular secretion. NSAIDs reduce GFR (afferent arteriolar constriction) and compete for OAT secretion, while cotrimoxazole compounds antifolate bone-marrow toxicity via trimethoprim\u0027s additive DHFR inhibition and sulfamethoxazole\u0027s tubular competition/protein displacement. Immediate methotrexate withdrawal, hydration/alkalinisation, and high-dose IV Leucovorin (reduced folinic acid that bypasses blocked DHFR to restore thymidylate/purine synthesis; plus glucarpidase if available in massive HD-MTX renal failure) rescue normal marrow and mucosa.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03710","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Sepsis \u0026 Septic Shock Resuscitation (Surviving Sepsis Campaign)","q":"A 65-year-old woman with urosepsis presents with fever 39.4 C, BP 74/42 mmHg, HR 122/min, serum lactate 4.8 mmol/L, and oliguria (Septic Shock). Following blood culture collection, initial 30 mL/kg IV balanced crystalloid resuscitation, and broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within the first hour, her Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) remains 52 mmHg. What is the FIRST-LINE intravenous vasopressor to restore MAP \u003e= 65 mmHg?","o":["Phenylephrine subcutaneous bolus every 4 hours","Intravenous nitroglycerin infusion","Norepinephrine continuous intravenous infusion","Dopamine high-dose monotherapy as first-line across all septic shock"],"a":2,"e":"Per Surviving Sepsis Campaign (SSC) international guidelines, patients with sepsis-induced hypoperfusion/lactate \u003e=4 mmol/L receive prompt IV crystalloids (at least 30 mL/kg within 3 h) and empiric IV antimicrobials within 1 hour. For septic shock refractory to fluid resuscitation, Norepinephrine (alpha-1 vasoconstriction with modest beta-1 inotropy) is strongly recommended as the first-line vasopressor targeting MAP \u003e= 65 mmHg (adding vasopressin or epinephrine second-line if needed; dopamine is avoided due to higher arrhythmia/mortality risk).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03711","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Rheumatoid Arthritis DMARD Safety \u0026 Biologics","q":"A 43-year-old woman with active seropositive rheumatoid arthritis has persistent erosive synovitis despite 6 months of optimal-dose oral methotrexate (15 mg ONCE WEEKLY + folic acid 5 mg/week), and her rheumatologist plans to add adalimumab (a TNF-alpha monoclonal antibody). What two critical safety rules govern methotrexate frequency verification and mandatory pre-adalimumab infectious screening in India?","o":["Stop folic acid permanently because folate inactivates adalimumab","Screen only for typhoid fever via Widal test before adalimumab","Dispense methotrexate 15 mg DAILY for faster joint relief, and start adalimumab without TB testing","Verify methotrexate is dispensed strictly as ONCE-WEEKLY dosing (never daily, which causes fatal myelosuppression), AND screen for latent tuberculosis (chest X-ray + IGRA/Mantoux) plus Hepatitis B/C prior to starting TNF-alpha blockers to prevent fulminant TB reactivation"],"a":3,"e":"Inadvertent daily dispensing/ingestion of low-dose rheumatoid methotrexate is a classic fatal medication error causing severe mucositis, hepatotoxicity, and pancytopenia—requiring explicit \u0027ONCE WEEKLY on [specific day]\u0027 counselling and folic acid supplementation. Before initiating TNF-alpha antagonists (adalimumab, infliximab, etanercept), TNF blockade disrupts granuloma integrity and precipitates severe disseminated/extrapulmonary tuberculosis reactivation (plus HBV reactivation), making pre-biologic latent TB and viral hepatitis screening mandatory—especially in endemic India.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03712","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Decompensated Cirrhosis — Hepatic Encephalopathy \u0026 Ascites","q":"A 55-year-old man with decompensated alcoholic cirrhosis develops asterixis, confusion (Grade 2 hepatic encephalopathy), and tense ascites. Which pharmacotherapy targets gut ammonia trapping for encephalopathy, and what standard diuretic combination ratio balances natriuresis with normokalaemia in cirrhotic ascites?","o":["Oral lactulose (titrated to 2-3 soft stools/day; bacterial fermentation acidifies colonic pH trapping NH3 as non-absorbable NH4+) plus rifaximin add-on; oral spironolactone : furosemide in a 100 mg : 40 mg ratio","Oral neomycin monotherapy lifelong + high-dose hydrochlorothiazide 100 mg/day","High-protein beef diet restriction + IV mannitol bolus","Oral loperamide to stop bowel movements + IV acetazolamide"],"a":0,"e":"In hepatic encephalopathy, colonic flora ferment non-absorbable disaccharide lactulose into lactic/acetic acids, lowering luminal pH to convert absorbable ammonia (NH3) into impermeant ammonium ions (NH4+) expelled in stool (titrated to 2-3 soft bowel movements daily, avoiding dehydration/hypernatraemia), with non-absorbed rifaximin 550 mg BID reducing ammonia-producing gut bacteria. For cirrhotic ascites driven by secondary hyperaldosteronism, starting spironolactone 100 mg + furosemide 40 mg (maintaining the 100:40 ratio up to 400:160 mg) achieves synergy while keeping serum K+ balanced.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03713","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Toxicology","topic":"Tricyclic Antidepressant (TCA) Poisoning","q":"A 26-year-old woman is brought to casualty obtunded with dilated pupils, dry flushed skin, sinus tachycardia (134/min), BP 82/48 mmHg, and a 12-lead ECG showing QRS duration = 136 ms (plus terminal R wave \u003e3 mm in aVR) 2 hours after an intentional overdose of amitriptyline. What is the MOST critical immediate intravenous antidote to reverse cardiac sodium-channel blockade and prevent ventricular arrhythmias?","o":["Intravenous Amiodarone 300 mg bolus","Intravenous Sodium Bicarbonate (1-2 mEq/kg bolus) targeting arterial pH 7.45-7.55","Intravenous Physostigmine 2 mg rapid push","Intravenous Flumazenil 0.5 mg bolus"],"a":1,"e":"Life-threatening TCA (amitriptyline, imipramine) cardiotoxicity stems from quinidine-like blockade of fast cardiac voltage-gated Na+ channels, manifesting on ECG as QRS \u003e 100 ms (seizure/arrhythmia predictor) and a prominent terminal R wave in lead aVR. Immediate IV hypertonic Sodium Bicarbonate (NaHCO3) provides dual protection: systemic alkalinisation (pH 7.45-7.55) increases TCA protein binding and favours neutral drug unbinding from cardiac Na+ channels, while the Na+ load directly overcomes Na+ channel blockade.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03714","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Biostatistics and Research Methodology","topic":"Intention-to-Treat (ITT) vs Per-Protocol (PP) Analysis","q":"In a multicentre superiority randomised controlled trial (RCT) of a new antihypertensive, 12% of participants discontinue study medication due to cough or cross over to open-label therapy. Why MUST the primary efficacy analysis follow the Intention-to-Treat (ITT) principle rather than Per-Protocol (PP) completer-only analysis?","o":["ITT allows investigators to reassign non-responding drug patients into the placebo arm post-hoc","ITT deletes all dropouts and non-adherent patients from the denominator to maximise statistical p-value","ITT analyses every randomised participant strictly within their originally assigned group regardless of adherence/crossover/withdrawal, preserving baseline randomisation balance, avoiding attrition/selection bias, and reflecting real-world effectiveness","Per-Protocol analysis guarantees zero selection bias when sicker patients drop out selectively"],"a":2,"e":"The Intention-to-Treat (ITT) rule (\u0027once randomised, always analysed\u0027) retains all randomised patients in their allocated arms. Excluding non-adherent or drop-out patients (Per-Protocol analysis) breaks randomisation and introduces severe bias if dropouts occur unevenly due to side effects or poor prognosis, inflating apparent treatment benefit. While ITT provides a conservative estimate in superiority trials, both ITT and PP are typically examined in non-inferiority trials because non-adherence can bias groups toward appearing falsely equivalent.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03715","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Preventing Fatal Wrong-Route Administration","q":"A pediatric leukemia ward schedules both intrathecal methotrexate and intravenous vincristine on the same day. Under WHO and ISMP High-Alert Medication safety standards, what mandatory dispensing and packaging safeguard prevents fatal accidental intrathecal injection of vincristine?","o":["Dispense vincristine strictly diluted in a small-volume IV minibag (never in a syringe), with an explicit warning \u0027FOR INTRAVENOUS USE ONLY – FATAL IF GIVEN BY OTHER ROUTES\u0027, and schedule/segregate delivery away from intrathecal syringes","Dispense vincristine in an identical 5 mL Luer-lock syringe alongside intrathecal methotrexate so nurses can inject both rapidly","Administer vincristine first via the lumbar puncture needle before methotrexate","Add blue food dye to intrathecal methotrexate syringes only"],"a":0,"e":"Accidental intrathecal administration of vinca alkaloids (vincristine, vinblastine, vinorelbine) causes progressive ascending myeloencephalopathy and is nearly 100% fatal. Because syringes physically fit spinal needles, WHO/ISMP/NCCN mandate preparing vincristine exclusively in a 25–50 mL IV minibag (making intrathecal connection physically impossible), labeling it clearly as fatal if given by other routes, and separating IV vincristine and intrathecal chemotherapy by time/location.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03716","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Therapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Carbamazepine TDM and Autoinduction","q":"A 28-year-old man newly diagnosed with focal epilepsy is started on carbamazepine 200 mg twice daily. A serum carbamazepine level drawn on Day 4 is 6.8 µg/mL (within 4–12 µg/mL), yet 4 weeks later he experiences breakthrough seizures and his trough concentration has fallen to 2.9 µg/mL despite confirmed 100% adherence. What pharmacokinetic phenomenon explains this drop?","o":["Oral bioavailability increased fourfold over the first month","Carbamazepine saturable Michaelis-Menten elimination accelerated renal filtration","Day 4 levels reflected carbamazepine-10,11-epoxide accumulation exclusively","Carbamazepine autoinduction of hepatic CYP3A4 over 2–4 weeks shortened its elimination half-life from ~35 hours initially to ~12–17 hours, requiring stepwise dose titration and repeat TDM at 3–4 weeks"],"a":3,"e":"Carbamazepine potently induces its own hepatic CYP3A4-mediated metabolism (autoinduction), a transcriptional process requiring 2 to 4 weeks to reach maximal steady state. Initially its half-life is ~30–35 hours, so Day 4 levels are not yet at true induced steady state; as clearance doubles-to-triples (t1/2 falls to ~12–17 hours), plasma concentrations drop substantially—necessitating low starting doses with gradual upward titration and waiting 3–4 weeks before checking definitive steady-state TDM.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03717","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Therapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"High-Dose Methotrexate Toxicity Rescue","q":"A 19-year-old man receiving high-dose methotrexate (12 g/m²) for osteosarcoma develops acute kidney injury (SCr rises from 0.8 to 2.9 mg/dL) with a dangerously elevated 48-hour serum methotrexate concentration of 65 µmol/L despite hydration and urinary alkalinization. Alongside high-dose leucovorin, intravenous glucarpidase (carboxypeptidase G2) is administered. What is glucarpidase\u0027s mechanism, and what critical scheduling precaution applies to leucovorin?","o":["Glucarpidase enzymatically hydrolyzes extracellular methotrexate into inactive DAMPA and glutamate; separate leucovorin dosing by 2 hours before/after glucarpidase because glucarpidase also cleaves leucovorin","Glucarpidase inhibits renal tubular secretion of methotrexate to trap it in blood","Glucarpidase chelates methotrexate for removal only via peritoneal dialysis","Glucarpidase activates DHFR inside tumor cells selectively"],"a":0,"e":"Glucarpidase (recombinant bacterial carboxypeptidase G2) rapidly cleaves the C-terminal glutamate residue of circulating extracellular methotrexate within 15 minutes (\u003e95% reduction), converting it into non-toxic 2,4-diamino-N10-methylpteroic acid (DAMPA) and glutamate. Because glucarpidase also degrades leucovorin (which shares a glutamate tail), leucovorin must NOT be given within 2 hours before or after glucarpidase; furthermore, post-glucarpidase TDM must use chromatographic (HPLC/LC-MS) rather than immunoassays because DAMPA cross-reacts on standard immunoassays.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03718","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacovigilance","topic":"WHO-UMC Causality Assessment System","q":"Under the World Health Organization–Uppsala Monitoring Centre (WHO-UMC) causality assessment system, what essential clinical criterion differentiates the \u0027Certain\u0027 category from the \u0027Probable/Likely\u0027 category?","o":["\u0027Probable/Likely\u0027 requires a negative dechallenge while the patient dies","\u0027Certain\u0027 requires a satisfactory rechallenge (or definitive pharmacological/phenomenological specificity) in addition to temporal plausibility, absence of alternative causes, and positive dechallenge, whereas \u0027Probable/Likely\u0027 does not require rechallenge","\u0027Certain\u0027 applies whenever two drugs are started simultaneously with no dechallenge","\u0027Certain\u0027 requires only a telephone rumor from an unrelated third party"],"a":1,"e":"In the WHO-UMC system, \u0027Certain\u0027 requires: plausible time relationship to drug intake, cannot be explained by disease or other drugs, clinically reasonable response to withdrawal (dechallenge), definitive pharmacological/phenomenological event (e.g., objective localized reaction or drug-specific test), AND satisfactory rechallenge if necessary. \u0027Probable/Likely\u0027 meets reasonable time, unlikely attribution to disease/other drugs, and positive dechallenge, WITHOUT requiring rechallenge information (which is often unethical in serious ADRs).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03719","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacovigilance","topic":"Drug-Induced QT Prolongation and Torsades de Pointes","q":"A 70-year-old hospitalized woman with hypokalemia (K+ 2.9 mEq/L) and hypomagnesemia receiving intravenous haloperidol, ondansetron, and levofloxacin develops a QTc interval of 560 ms followed by polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with twisting QRS complexes (Torsades de Pointes). Alongside stopping all culprit QT-prolonging drugs and correcting potassium, what is the immediate first-line antiarrhythmic drug of choice?","o":["Oral erythromycin 500 mg","Intravenous amiodarone bolus to further prolong ventricular repolarization","Intravenous magnesium sulfate (1–2 g IV over 10–15 minutes) to suppress early afterdepolarizations","Intravenous sotalol infusion"],"a":2,"e":"Haloperidol, ondansetron, and levofloxacin synergistically block myocardial hERG (IKr) delayed-rectifier potassium channels, prolonging Phase 3 repolarization (QTc \u003e 500 ms) and triggering early afterdepolarizations (EADs) via L-type Ca2+ reactivation—especially under hypokalemia and bradycardia. Intravenous magnesium sulfate (1–2 g IV) is the immediate first-line therapy for Torsades de Pointes because it stabilizes cardiac membranes and suppresses Ca2+-mediated EADs without further prolonging QTc.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03720","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics \u0026 Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Pharmacokinetics in Hemodialysis","q":"A patient with end-stage renal disease on thrice-weekly intermittent hemodialysis receives cefazolin and gabapentin versus phenytoin and amiodarone. Which physicochemical/pharmacokinetic profile characterizes drugs like gabapentin and cefazolin that are substantially cleared by hemodialysis and require post-dialysis supplemental dosing?","o":["99% hepatic clearance with extensive tissue sequestration","Very large volume of distribution (\u003e10 L/kg), \u003e95% albumin binding, and high lipophilicity","Molecular weight \u003e 150,000 Da (monoclonal antibody size)","Low molecular weight (\u003c500 Da), small volume of distribution (Vd \u003c 1 L/kg), low plasma protein binding (\u003c80%), and high aqueous solubility"],"a":3,"e":"Conventional hemodialysis removes unbound drug molecules diffusing across a semipermeable membrane into dialysate down a concentration gradient. Drugs are significantly dialyzable—requiring scheduled administration after dialysis or supplemental post-HD doses—when they have small molecular size (\u003c500 Da, or up to ~1,500 Da with high-flux membranes like vancomycin), low protein binding (high unbound fraction), small Vd (\u003c1 L/kg so most drug resides in vascular/extracellular water), and high water solubility (e.g., gabapentin, aminoglycosides, many beta-lactams).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03721","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics \u0026 Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Analgesic Safety in Decompensated Cirrhosis","q":"A 53-year-old man with Child-Pugh Class B cirrhosis, esophageal varices, and refractory ascites complains of moderate osteoarthritis knee pain. Why are systemic NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen, diclofenac) strictly avoided in decompensated cirrhosis, and which cautious oral analgesic alternative is generally preferred?","o":["NSAIDs inhibit renal prostaglandins (precipitating acute kidney injury/hepatorenal syndrome), worsen sodium retention/ascites, and trigger variceal GI bleeding; reduced-dose acetaminophen (\u003c= 2 g/day) is preferred","NSAIDs accelerate hepatic albumin synthesis excessively; high-dose codeine 240 mg/day is preferred","NSAIDs cause hypernatremia; aspirin 1,000 mg q6h is safest","NSAIDs induce hepatic CYP2E1 glutathione production; oral morphine 60 mg q4h is safest"],"a":0,"e":"In decompensated cirrhosis with splanchnic vasodilation and portal hypertension, renal perfusion depends critically on vasodilatory prostaglandins (PGE2/PGI2). Systemic NSAIDs block COX-mediated renal prostaglandin synthesis, causing intense afferent arteriolar constriction, acute kidney injury/hepatorenal syndrome, diuretic-resistant ascites, and antiplatelet-mediated upper GI/variceal bleeding. Despite common misconception, reduced-dose acetaminophen (maximum 2 g/day, avoiding alcohol) is the safest first-line non-opioid analgesic because glucuronidation/sulfation remain adequate at \u003c= 2 g/day without NAPQI accumulation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03722","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Evidence-Based Pharmacotherapy","topic":"Anticoagulation in Valvular Atrial Fibrillation","q":"A 49-year-old woman with rheumatic heart disease, moderate-to-severe mitral stenosis, and atrial fibrillation (CHA2DS2-VASc score 3) presents for oral anticoagulation initiation. Based on ACC/AHA/ESC guidelines and landmark trial evidence (RE-ALIGN, INVICTUS), which oral anticoagulant regimen is strictly required?","o":["Rivaroxaban 20 mg once daily with the evening meal","Adjusted-dose warfarin (Vitamin K antagonist) targeting INR 2.0–3.0 (never a DOAC)","Dabigatran 150 mg twice daily","Apixaban 5 mg twice daily"],"a":1,"e":"While Direct Oral Anticoagulants (DOACs: apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, edoxaban) are first-line for non-valvular atrial fibrillation (including bioprosthetic valves or aortic/mitral regurgitation), Vitamin K antagonists (warfarin) remain mandatory in two specific \u0027valvular AF\u0027 populations: (1) mechanical prosthetic heart valves (RE-ALIGN showed excess thromboembolism and bleeding with dabigatran), and (2) moderate-to-severe rheumatic mitral stenosis (confirmed by the INVICTUS trial showing lower stroke/mortality with warfarin than rivaroxaban).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03723","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Evidence-Based Pharmacotherapy","topic":"Febrile Neutropenia Management","q":"Ten days after cytotoxic chemotherapy for acute myeloid leukemia, a 45-year-old man develops a single oral temperature of 38.6 °C with an absolute neutrophil count (ANC) of 180 cells/µL, stable blood pressure, no central venous catheter tunnel infection, and no severe mucositis. After obtaining blood cultures, what is the optimal immediate empiric antibiotic regimen per IDSA guidelines?","o":["Oral amoxicillin monotherapy at home with follow-up in 5 days","Intravenous vancomycin monotherapy without Gram-negative coverage","Monotherapy with an intravenous antipseudomonal beta-lactam (cefepime, piperacillin-tazobactam, or meropenem) administered within 60 minutes","Oral fluconazole plus acyclovir alone pending culture results"],"a":2,"e":"High-risk febrile neutropenia (ANC \u003c 500 cells/µL expected \u003e7 days, or inpatient/AML) is a medical emergency requiring intravenous antipseudomonal beta-lactam monotherapy (cefepime, piperacillin-tazobactam, meropenem, or imipenem-cilastatin) within 1 hour to rapidly cover virulent Gram-negative bacilli such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Empiric vancomycin is NOT routinely added upfront unless specific criteria exist (hemodynamic instability/sepsis, suspected catheter-related infection, skin/soft tissue infection, severe mucositis, or known MRSA colonization).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03724","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Evidence-Based Pharmacotherapy","topic":"Acute Severe Hyperkalemia Management","q":"A 64-year-old man with Stage 5 CKD taking lisinopril and spironolactone presents with generalized muscle weakness, serum potassium 6.9 mEq/L, and peaked T waves with QRS widening on ECG. Which agent must be administered IMMEDIATELY first to protect the myocardium, and why?","o":["Intravenous spironolactone bolus to antagonize aldosterone","Intravenous regular insulin alone without dextrose","Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate (Kayexalate) suspension to bind intestinal potassium within 30 seconds","Intravenous calcium gluconate (10%, 10 mL over 2–5 min) to rapidly restore the resting-to-threshold cardiac membrane potential gradient (without lowering serum K+)"],"a":3,"e":"Acute hyperkalemia with ECG conduction abnormalities (peaked T waves, PR prolongation, QRS widening, sine-wave) poses imminent risk of ventricular fibrillation/asystole. Step 1 is immediate IV calcium gluconate (onset 1–3 minutes, lasting 30–60 minutes), which raises the cardiac action potential threshold potential to restore normal myocardial excitability—even though it does NOT lower serum K+. Step 2 shifts K+ intracellularly (IV regular insulin 10 units + 25–50 g dextrose to prevent hypoglycemia, plus nebulized salbutamol). Step 3 removes total body K+ (loop diuretics if urine output, K+ binders like SZC/patiromer, or urgent hemodialysis).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03725","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Winter-Tozer Phenytoin Correction in End-Stage Renal Disease (ESRD)","q":"A 64-year-old seizure-free patient with End-Stage Renal Disease on maintenance haemodialysis (CrCl \u003c 10 mL/min) and hypoalbuminaemia (serum albumin = 2.5 g/dL) has a measured total serum phenytoin level of 5.0 mg/L. Using the renal-failure Winter-Tozer equation (incorporating uraemic toxin displacement of albumin binding), what is the corrected total phenytoin concentration?","o":["14.3 mg/L (therapeutic within 10-20 mg/L — maintain current dose)","8.3 mg/L (subtherapeutic — increase dose)","25.0 mg/L (toxic — hold phenytoin)","5.0 mg/L (critically low — give loading dose)"],"a":0,"e":"In severe renal failure/ESRD (CrCl \u003c 10-20 mL/min), accumulated uraemic organic acids further displace phenytoin from albumin binding sites, dropping the Winter-Tozer albumin multiplier from 0.2 (normal renal function) to 0.1: C_corrected = C_measured / [(0.1 x Albumin g/dL) + 0.1] = 5.0 / [(0.1 x 2.5) + 0.1] = 5.0 / 0.35 = 14.29 mg/L (~14.3 mg/L), well within the 10-20 mg/L therapeutic range (ideally verified with free phenytoin levels 1-2 mg/L when available).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03726","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Amiodarone Interactions With Warfarin \u0026 Digoxin","q":"A 69-year-old man with atrial fibrillation and HFrEF stably maintained on warfarin 5 mg/day (INR 2.4) and digoxin 0.25 mg/day (serum trough 0.9 ng/mL) is initiated on oral amiodarone loading for recurrent ventricular arrhythmias. What pre-emptive adjustments to warfarin and digoxin MUST the clinical pharmacist recommend upon starting amiodarone?","o":["Reduce warfarin dose by ~30-50% with frequent INR monitoring (amiodarone/desethylamiodarone inhibits CYP2C9/3A4) AND reduce digoxin dose by ~50% with trough/ECG monitoring (amiodarone inhibits renal/intestinal P-glycoprotein)","Double both warfarin and digoxin doses immediately because amiodarone induces CYP2C9 and P-gp","Stop warfarin completely and switch digoxin to verapamil","Make zero dose adjustments for 6 months because amiodarone has a 30-minute half-life"],"a":0,"e":"Amiodarone and its major active metabolite desethylamiodarone inhibit CYP2C9 (raising S-warfarin levels and INR substantially over weeks due to amiodarone\u0027s ~40-60 day half-life) AND inhibit P-glycoprotein (P-gp) and renal tubular secretion of digoxin (roughly doubling serum digoxin concentrations). Pre-emptive dose reductions of 30-50% for warfarin (with close serial INRs) and ~50% for digoxin (with serum trough/toxicity checks) prevent severe bleeding and digoxin arrhythmia.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03727","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Direct Oral Anticoagulants (DOACs) Lead-In Dosing Rules","q":"A 50-year-old woman with normal renal function (CrCl 92 mL/min) is diagnosed with an acute unprovoked proximal deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and prefers an all-oral outpatient anticoagulation regimen without initial injectable enoxaparin/heparin. Which oral anticoagulant regimen can be started IMMEDIATELY as single-drug oral monotherapy without a 5-day parenteral heparin lead-in?","o":["Dabigatran etexilate 150 mg BID started on Day 1 without parenteral anticoagulation","Apixaban (10 mg BID for 7 days, then 5 mg BID) OR Rivaroxaban (15 mg BID for 21 days, then 20 mg once daily with food)","Warfarin 5 mg daily started alone without heparin","Edoxaban 60 mg OD started on Day 1 without prior parenteral LMWH/UFH"],"a":1,"e":"In acute VTE (DVT/PE), only the oral Factor Xa inhibitors apixaban (10 mg BID x 7 days -\u003e 5 mg BID) and rivaroxaban (15 mg BID x 21 days -\u003e 20 mg OD with evening meal) are approved as immediate all-oral monotherapy using intensified initial loading doses. By contrast, dabigatran and edoxaban (as well as warfarin, which causes transient Protein C depletion!) strictly require 5-10 days of initial parenteral LMWH/UFH/fondaparinux anticoagulation before starting.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03728","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Acute Severe Asthma (Status Asthmaticus) Rescue","q":"A 24-year-old woman with acute severe asthma exacerbation remains tachypnoeic (RR 32/min), unable to speak in full sentences, with PEF \u003c 35% predicted and silent chest after 1 hour of continuous nebulised salbutamol, nebulised ipratropium bromide, supplemental O2, and IV hydrocortisone. Which intravenous bronchodilator adjunct is recommended by GINA/BTS guidelines for life-threatening asthma refractory to initial therapy?","o":["High-dose oral montelukast 100 mg once","Intravenous morphine sulfate 10 mg bolus for anxiety","Single-dose intravenous Magnesium Sulfate 2 g infused over 20 minutes (relaxes bronchial smooth muscle via Ca2+ channel antagonism)","Intravenous propranolol 5 mg bolus"],"a":2,"e":"In severe/life-threatening acute asthma refractory to first-line inhaled SABA + ipratropium + early systemic corticosteroids, GINA and BTS/ICMR guidelines recommend a single intravenous infusion of Magnesium Sulfate (1.2-2 g over 20 min in adults; 40-50 mg/kg max 2 g in children). Magnesium blocks smooth-muscle Ca2+ influx, inhibits mast-cell degranulation, and reduces hospital admissions in severe airway obstruction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03729","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics","topic":"Preeclampsia With Severe Features \u0026 Eclampsia","q":"A 27-year-old primigravida at 34 weeks\u0027 gestation is admitted with BP 174/114 mmHg, severe frontal headache, hyperreflexia, and +3 proteinuria (preeclampsia with severe features). She is started on IV labetalol for BP control and a Magnesium Sulfate (MgSO4) infusion for seizure prophylaxis. Twenty hours later, her deep tendon patellar reflexes disappear, respiratory rate falls to 10 breaths/min, and urine output drops to 15 mL/h. What immediate action and antidote are indicated?","o":["Switch IV labetalol immediately to intravenous enalaprilat","Double the MgSO4 infusion rate assuming impending eclamptic seizure","Administer intravenous potassium chloride 40 mEq push","Stop the MgSO4 infusion immediately and administer intravenous Calcium Gluconate 1 g (10 mL of 10% solution) slowly over 3-5 minutes to reverse hypermagnesaemic neuromuscular/respiratory paralysis"],"a":3,"e":"Magnesium sulfate is the gold-standard anticonvulsant for eclampsia prophylaxis/treatment (superior to phenytoin/diazepam), cleared solely by the kidneys. Oliguria (\u003c30 mL/h) causes Mg2+ accumulation: loss of patellar deep-tendon reflexes (~7-10 mEq/L) precedes respiratory depression (~10-13 mEq/L) and cardiac arrest (\u003e15 mEq/L). Immediately halt MgSO4 and inject 10% Calcium Gluconate (1 g = 10 mL IV slowly) as the direct physiological Ca2+ antagonist at the neuromuscular junction (note ACEi/ARBs are teratogenic/contraindicated in pregnancy).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03730","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Toxicology","topic":"Acute Cyanide Poisoning Antidotes","q":"A firefighter rescued unconscious from an enclosed plastic warehouse fire presents with hypotension, severe lactic acidosis (lactate 14 mmol/L), bright-red venous blood, and suspected dual carbon monoxide (CO) + hydrogen cyanide poisoning. Why is intravenous Hydroxocobalamin (Vitamin B12a, 5 g IV) strongly preferred over the older amyl/sodium nitrite + sodium thiosulfate kit in smoke inhalation?","o":["Hydroxocobalamin directly chelates cyanide into non-toxic cyanocobalamin (excreted in urine) WITHOUT inducing methaemoglobinaemia—whereas nitrites convert haemoglobin to methaemoglobin, dangerously compounding oxygen-carrying loss caused by concurrent carboxyhaemoglobin","Hydroxocobalamin acts purely by alkalinising urine to pH 8.5","Hydroxocobalamin generates 40% methaemoglobin to displace CO from haemoglobin","Sodium nitrite selectively inhibits cytochrome c oxidase inside mitochondria"],"a":0,"e":"Cyanide inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV), halting aerobic ATP production and producing severe lactic acidosis and \u0027arterialised\u0027 bright-red venous blood. While sodium nitrite intentionally induces ~20-30% methaemoglobin to scavenge cyanide off cytochrome oxidase, inducing methaemoglobinaemia in a smoke-inhalation victim who already carries 20-30% carboxyhaemoglobin can be fatal. Intravenous hydroxocobalamin (5 g over 15 min) exchanges its hydroxyl ligand for cyanide to form benign cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) safely without impairing oxygen transport (harmlessly turning skin/urine transient red).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03731","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Biostatistics and Research Methodology","topic":"Forest Plot Interpretation \u0026 I-Squared (I^2) Heterogeneity","q":"In a systematic review and meta-analysis pooling 10 RCTs of a probiotic for antibiotic-associated diarrhoea, the Forest plot reports Cochran\u0027s Q p = 0.001, Higgins I^2 = 78%, and a pooled Risk Ratio (RR) of 0.58 (95% CI: 0.41 to 0.82). What does I^2 = 78% indicate regarding inter-study heterogeneity and model selection?","o":["Statistical non-significance because the pooled 95% CI excludes 1.00","Substantial-to-considerable heterogeneity (78% of observed variation across studies reflects true between-study differences rather than random chance), favouring a Random-Effects model and subgroup/sensitivity exploration while showing a statistically significant 42% RR reduction","Publication bias proven solely because I^2 \u003e 50%","Zero heterogeneity across studies, requiring a strict Fixed-Effect model only"],"a":1,"e":"Higgins\u0027 I^2 quantifies the percentage of total variance across pooled studies attributable to real heterogeneity rather than sampling error: 0-25% low, 25-50% moderate, \u003e50-75% substantial/high heterogeneity. At I^2 = 78%, clinical/methodological diversity among trials is high—requiring Random-Effects weighting (DerSimonian-Laird/REML) and subgroup/meta-regression investigation rather than Fixed-Effect pooling. Since pooled RR = 0.58 with 95% CI 0.41-0.82 lies completely below 1.00, the overall effect is statistically significant (while Funnel plot/Egger\u0027s test assesses publication bias).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03732","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Transitions of Care Reconciliation","q":"A 72-year-old man admitted for elective hip arthroplasty brings a crumpled outpatient pill strip, and the admitting resident writes \u0027metoprolol succinate 50 mg OD\u0027 without verifying dose frequency from his home pharmacy or family. During ward medication reconciliation, the Pharm.D resident discovers he actually takes metoprolol tartrate 25 mg twice daily, plus apixaban 5 mg BID (omitted from admission orders) and OTC ibuprofen 400 mg TID. Which reconciliation step constitutes creating the Best Possible Medication History (BPMH)?","o":["Copying the incomplete emergency admission medication list verbatim onto the discharge summary","Ignoring OTC NSAIDs because they do not affect surgical bleeding risk","Using at least two independent sources (structured patient/caregiver interview plus outpatient pharmacy dispensing records/prescription bottles) to verify every prescription, OTC, and herbal product—then reconciling unintentional omissions (apixaban) and formulation/dose discrepancies with the surgical team","Discontinuing apixaban permanently without notifying the surgeon or orthopedist"],"a":2,"e":"The WHO High 5s Medication Reconciliation standard defines BPMH as a systematic medication history obtained by interviewing the patient/caregiver AND corroborating with at least one additional reliable source (pharmacy records, previous discharge summaries, pill bottles), capturing prescription, OTC, and herbal agents. Here, identifying omitted apixaban (requiring structured preoperative hold/bridging assessment), formulation mismatch (tartrate IR vs succinate ER), and hidden OTC ibuprofen (perioperative bleeding/AKI risk) and communicating them to the care team prevents serious transition-of-care harm.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03733","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Therapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Lithium Therapeutic Drug Monitoring","q":"A 45-year-old woman on chronic lithium carbonate develops vomiting, severe coarse tremor, confusion, and acute kidney injury (eGFR 18 mL/min/1.73 m²) following NSAID use, with a 12-hour post-dose serum lithium level of 3.2 mEq/L. What is the standardized sampling timing for routine lithium TDM, and what intervention is indicated in this patient with severe neurotoxicity and renal failure?","o":["Routine TDM is drawn randomly; treat solely with activated charcoal","Routine TDM is drawn 1 hour post-dose (peak); treat this patient solely with oral hydrochlorothiazide","Routine TDM is drawn at 48 hours post-dose; continue lithium at half dose","Routine lithium TDM is drawn as a 12-hour post-evening-dose trough at steady state (5–7 days); here, stop lithium/NSAID, administer isotonic saline, and initiate urgent intermittent hemodialysis due to severe neurotoxicity + AKI + Li+ \u003e 2.5 mEq/L"],"a":3,"e":"Because lithium undergoes slow two-compartment tissue distribution, routine steady-state TDM levels (target 0.6–1.2 mEq/L) are strictly standardized at 12 hours after the last evening dose (~5–7 days after initiation/change). In acute-on-chronic toxicity complicated by impaired renal clearance, confusion/encephalopathy, and Li+ \u003e 2.5 mEq/L (EXTRIP criteria: Li+ \u003e 4.0 mEq/L unconditionally in renal impairment, or \u003e 2.5 mEq/L with severe neurologic symptoms/AKI), lithium is ideal for urgent hemodialysis (tiny MW 7 Da, zero protein binding, low Vd ~0.7 L/kg)—remembering to recheck levels post-HD for intracellular redistribution rebound.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03734","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacokinetics \u0026 Therapeutic Drug Monitoring","topic":"Tacrolimus TDM \u0026 CYP3A4/P-gp Interactions","q":"A 50-year-old kidney transplant recipient maintained on tacrolimus 3 mg twice daily (baseline whole-blood trough C0 = 7.5 ng/mL) is started on oral voriconazole for invasive pulmonary aspergillosis. Why must tacrolimus TDM be assayed in EDTA whole blood rather than plasma/serum, and what empirical tacrolimus adjustment is required upon starting voriconazole?","o":["Tacrolimus partitions extensively (\u003e85%) into erythrocytes via temperature-dependent binding to FKBP, requiring EDTA whole-blood trough analysis; empirically reduce tacrolimus daily dose by ~60–67% (to one-third) upfront with frequent trough monitoring because voriconazole potently inhibits CYP3A4/P-gp","Tacrolimus is assayed in acidified urine; continue unchanged without monitoring","Tacrolimus binds 100% to serum albumin; double the tacrolimus dose because voriconazole induces CYP3A4","Tacrolimus does not cross erythrocyte membranes; co-administer rifampicin to counteract voriconazole"],"a":0,"e":"Tacrolimus (and cyclosporine/sirolimus) binds extensively to red blood cell immunophilins in a temperature-sensitive equilibrium; separating plasma at inconsistent room temperatures causes erratic readings, so EDTA whole-blood trough (C0) LC-MS/MS or validated immunoassay is the universal standard matrix. Because voriconazole strongly inhibits CYP3A4 and P-gp (increasing tacrolimus AUC 3-fold or more), failing to preemptively cut the tacrolimus dose by ~67% (e.g., to 1/3) causes rapid acute nephrotoxicity, tremor, hyperkalemia, and posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03735","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacovigilance","topic":"Drug-Induced Liver Injury (DILI)","q":"A patient enrolled in a Phase III trial of a novel oral antifungal develops fatigue and scleral icterus at Week 4. Laboratory tests reveal ALT 420 U/L (10.5× ULN), AST 380 U/L (9.5× ULN), Total Bilirubin 4.6 mg/dL (3.8× ULN), Alkaline Phosphatase 135 U/L (1.1× ULN), and negative viral/autoimmune hepatitis serologies. How does this presentation fulfill Hy\u0027s Law, and what is its clinical significance?","o":["Pure cholestatic DILI (R-value \u003c 2) that resolves spontaneously while continuing the drug","Hepatocellular injury (ALT/AST \u003e= 3× ULN) combined with Total Bilirubin \u003e 2× ULN in the absence of significant cholestasis (ALP \u003c 2× ULN) or alternative cause predicts \u003e= 10% risk of fatal acute liver failure/transplant, mandating immediate permanent discontinuation","Isolated benign Gilbert-like bilirubin elevation with zero hepatocellular necrosis","Normal physiologic enzyme induction requiring a 50% dose increase"],"a":1,"e":"Hy\u0027s Law (formulated by Hyman Zimmerman and adopted by USFDA/CDSCO/EMA DILI guidance) identifies severe drug-induced hepatocellular injury severe enough to impair global hepatic bilirubin clearance: (1) ALT or AST \u003e= 3× ULN (typically hepatocellular R-ratio \u003e= 5), (2) Total Bilirubin \u003e 2× ULN without biliary obstruction/significant cholestasis (ALP \u003c 2× ULN), and (3) no other explanatory cause (viral hepatitis, ischemia, gallstones, acetaminophen). Meeting Hy\u0027s Law carries a 10–50% risk of death or liver transplantation and mandates immediate permanent cessation of the suspect drug.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03736","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Clinical Pharmacy \u0026 Pharmacovigilance","topic":"Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE)","q":"A 63-year-old man treated for 9 months with oral hydralazine (plus isosorbide dinitrate) for resistant hypertension/heart failure develops symmetric arthralgias, low-grade fever, pleuritic chest pain, positive ANA, and strongly positive anti-histone antibodies, with negative anti-dsDNA and normal renal function/complement levels. Which ADR syndrome is most likely, and how is it managed?","o":["Acute serum sickness from hydralazine requiring lifelong steroids while continuing hydralazine","Gouty arthritis caused by hyperuricemia","Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE) due to hydralazine (also classically caused by procainamide and isoniazid); typically resolves within weeks to months upon discontinuing hydralazine","Idiopathic lupus nephritis requiring lifelong cyclophosphamide"],"a":2,"e":"Classic Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE)—most strongly linked to procainamide, hydralazine, isoniazid, minocycline, quinidine, and anti-TNF biologics—typically arises months after chronic exposure (higher risk in NAT2 slow acetylators for hydralazine/procainamide/isoniazid). Unlike idiopathic SLE, DILE predominantly causes arthralgias, myalgias, fever, and serositis while sparing the kidneys and CNS; serology shows positive ANA and anti-histone antibodies (\u003e90–95%) with negative anti-dsDNA and normal C3/C4, and symptoms resolve after stopping the offending drug.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03737","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics \u0026 Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"DOAC Dosing in Chronic Kidney Disease","q":"An 82-year-old woman weighing 54 kg with non-valvular atrial fibrillation has a stable serum creatinine of 1.6 mg/dL (CrCl ≈ 26 mL/min). Comparing DOAC options, why is dabigatran avoided below CrCl 30 mL/min, and what is the correct dose of apixaban for stroke prevention in this patient?","o":["Dabigatran is ~80% renally cleared unchanged and accumulates dangerously below CrCl 30 mL/min; apixaban (~27% renal clearance) should be reduced to 2.5 mg twice daily because she meets \u003e= 2 dose-reduction criteria (Age \u003e= 80, Weight \u003c= 60 kg, SCr \u003e= 1.5 mg/dL)","Dabigatran is 100% hepatically cleared and preferred at CrCl 26 mL/min; apixaban requires 10 mg twice daily","Apixaban requires 5 mg twice daily unless all four criteria including male sex are met","Both dabigatran and apixaban are strictly contraindicated below CrCl 60 mL/min"],"a":0,"e":"Among DOACs, dabigatran relies ~80% on renal excretion (avoided at CrCl \u003c 30 mL/min per ESC/FDA/CDSCO guidelines), rivaroxaban ~35% unchanged renal excretion (15 mg OD with food for CrCl 15–49 mL/min in AF), and apixaban only ~27% renal excretion. For non-valvular AF, standard apixaban 5 mg BID is reduced to 2.5 mg BID ONLY when \u003e= 2 of the \u0027ABC\u0027 criteria are present: Age \u003e= 80 years, Body weight \u003c= 60 kg, or Serum Creatinine \u003e= 1.5 mg/dL (this patient meets all three: 82 y, 54 kg, SCr 1.6 mg/dL).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03738","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Pharmacotherapeutics \u0026 Clinical Pharmacy","topic":"Antitubercular Drug-Induced Liver Injury (ATLI)","q":"Three weeks after starting daily HRZE (isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol) for cavitary pulmonary TB, a 42-year-old man develops nausea, jaundice, total bilirubin 3.1 mg/dL, and ALT 290 U/L (7.2× ULN). According to NTEP / ATS guidelines, what is the immediate management of his TB regimen?","o":["Continue full HRZE unchanged while adding silymarin syrup","Double pyrazinamide because rifampicin induced its metabolism","Stop ethambutol only and continue high-dose isoniazid plus pyrazinamide","Stop all hepatotoxic anti-TB drugs (isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide) immediately; bridge with non-hepatotoxic agents (e.g., ethambutol + streptomycin/levofloxacin) if TB is severe, and sequentially reintroduce rifampicin then isoniazid once ALT \u003c 2× ULN and bilirubin normalizes"],"a":3,"e":"Antitubercular drug-induced liver injury (ATLI) mandates immediate withdrawal of all three hepatotoxic drugs (H, R, Z) when ALT/AST \u003e= 3× ULN with hepatitis symptoms/jaundice OR \u003e= 5× ULN even if asymptomatic. In severe/disseminated/cavitary TB where interrupting therapy is unsafe, non-hepatotoxic \u0027liver-friendly\u0027 bridging (ethambutol + injectable aminoglycoside like streptomycin/amikacin + fluoroquinolone like levofloxacin) maintains antimycobacterial suppression. Once LFTs normalize (\u003c2× ULN), rifampicin and isoniazid are carefully reintroduced sequentially with LFT monitoring (often omitting pyrazinamide and extending duration to 9 months).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03739","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Evidence-Based Pharmacotherapy","topic":"Acute Ischemic Stroke Thrombolysis","q":"A 60-year-old man arrives within 2.5 hours of acute left hemiplegia onset; NCCT head shows no hemorrhage, platelets are 210,000/µL, INR is 1.0, glucose is 130 mg/dL, and BP is 198/114 mmHg. Before initiating IV alteplase (0.9 mg/kg, max 90 mg), what BP target must be achieved with cautious IV labetalol/nicardipine, and how long must aspirin/anticoagulants be withheld afterward?","o":["Lower BP carefully to \u003c 185/110 mmHg before alteplase infusion (maintaining \u003c 180/105 mmHg afterward); withhold aspirin and all anticoagulants for 24 hours until follow-up CT excludes hemorrhagic transformation","Lower BP aggressively to 100/60 mmHg immediately; give aspirin 325 mg concurrently with alteplase","No BP lowering is required below 220/120 mmHg before alteplase; start IV heparin immediately after bolus","Administer tenecteplase 2.5 mg/kg plus dual antiplatelets simultaneously"],"a":0,"e":"Per AHA/ASA and Indian Stroke Association acute ischemic stroke guidelines, IV fibrinolysis (alteplase 0.9 mg/kg, max 90 mg: 10% bolus + 90% over 60 min; or tenecteplase 0.25 mg/kg, max 25 mg) within 4.5 hours requires gentle BP lowering to \u003c 185/110 mmHg prior to bolus using titratable IV labetalol or nicardipine (avoiding steep drops \u003e15% that worsen penumbral ischemia) and maintaining \u003c 180/105 mmHg for 24 h. Aspirin, clopidogrel, and anticoagulants are strictly held for 24 hours post-thrombolysis until repeat neuroimaging rules out symptomatic intracranial hemorrhage.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03740","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Evidence-Based Pharmacotherapy","topic":"Life-Threatening Acute Severe Asthma","q":"A 29-year-old woman with life-threatening status asthmaticus (PEF \u003c 33% predicted, silent chest, hypercapnia) shows poor initial bronchodilation after 1 hour of continuous nebulized salbutamol/ipratropium and IV hydrocortisone. Under GINA and BTS/SIGN guidelines, which single-dose intravenous adjunct provides rapid additional bronchodilation by inhibiting calcium-mediated bronchial smooth muscle contraction?","o":["Intravenous furosemide 40 mg","Intravenous magnesium sulfate (2 g infused over 20 minutes)","Intravenous propranolol 5 mg","Intravenous adenosine 12 mg"],"a":1,"e":"In patients with severe acute asthma (FEV1 \u003c 25–30% predicted or life-threatening features) failing initial inhaled SABA + ipratropium + systemic corticosteroids, GINA and BTS guidelines recommend a single IV infusion of magnesium sulfate (2 g over 20 min). Mg2+ competes with Ca2+ entry into bronchial smooth muscle cells, inhibits acetylcholine release at motor nerve terminals, and stabilizes mast cells, reducing hospital admissions and intubation risk.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03741","course":"Pharm.D - Doctor of Pharmacy","subject":"Evidence-Based Pharmacotherapy","topic":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) Management","q":"A 22-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes is admitted to the ICU in diabetic ketoacidosis (blood glucose 520 mg/dL, arterial pH 7.12, HCO3- 9 mEq/L, anion gap 26 mEq/L) with an initial serum potassium of 2.9 mEq/L. After starting 0.9% NaCl fluid resuscitation, what is the critical mandatory step BEFORE starting the continuous IV regular insulin infusion?","o":["Administer IV sodium bicarbonate 200 mEq bolus first regardless of K+","Start high-dose IV regular insulin 0.5 units/kg/h immediately without checking or replacing potassium","Hold IV insulin temporarily and administer intravenous potassium chloride (KCl) repletion until serum K+ rises to \u003e= 3.3 mEq/L to prevent fatal hypokalemic cardiac arrhythmias","Switch immediately to subcutaneous glargine once daily only"],"a":2,"e":"Despite frequent normal/high initial K+ in acidosis, DKA patients always have massive total-body potassium depletion; when initial serum K+ is already \u003c 3.3 mEq/L, starting insulin immediately drives extracellular K+ rapidly intracellularly via Na+/K+-ATPase stimulation—precipitating lethal hypokalemic ventricular fibrillation and respiratory muscle paralysis. Per ADA DKA consensus guidelines, IV regular insulin (0.1 units/kg/h) MUST be delayed until KCl infusion raises serum K+ to \u003e= 3.3 mEq/L (and KCl is added to IV fluids whenever K+ is 3.3–5.2 mEq/L).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03742","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundation","topic":"NANDA-I Nursing Diagnosis Formulation","q":"A nurse transitioning into Post Basic B.Sc Nursing practice writes two NANDA-I nursing diagnoses for an immobile stroke patient. Which statement correctly follows the PES (Problem, Etiology, Signs/Symptoms) structure for a problem-focused diagnosis versus a risk diagnosis?","o":["\u0027Risk for impaired skin integrity related to prolonged immobility as evidenced by a 3 cm sacral Stage II ulcer\u0027","\u0027Needs physiotherapy daily related to doctor\u0027s order\u0027","\u0027Cerebrovascular accident related to hypertension as evidenced by CT scan\u0027","\u0027Impaired physical mobility related to left-sided hemiparesis as evidenced by inability to turn independently in bed\u0027 (problem-focused); \u0027Risk for falls as evidenced by history of dizziness and unsteady gait\u0027 (risk diagnosis without defining signs of an existing fall)"],"a":3,"e":"A problem-focused NANDA-I nursing diagnosis uses the three-part PES format: Problem (diagnostic label) + Etiology (\u0027related to\u0027 modifiable/nursing-relevant factor) + Signs/Symptoms (\u0027as evidenced by\u0027 objective/subjective defining characteristics). A risk nursing diagnosis uses a two-part format (Problem + Risk factors) because the actual problem has not yet occurred; if a sacral ulcer already exists, it is an actual \u0027Impaired skin integrity\u0027 diagnosis, not a \u0027Risk for\u0027 diagnosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03743","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Maternal Nursing","topic":"Postpartum Haemorrhage (PPH)","q":"Thirty minutes after a spontaneous vaginal birth, a multiparous mother develops brisk vaginal bleeding exceeding 600 mL, pulse 118 bpm, BP 92/58 mmHg, and on abdominal palpation the uterine fundus feels soft, boggy, and displaced above the umbilicus. What is the immediate priority nursing intervention while calling for obstetric assistance?","o":["Perform vigorous fundal uterine massage while calling for help, administer prescribed IV/IM oxytocin, empty the urinary bladder, and establish two large-bore IV lines with crystalloids","Pack the vagina tightly with dry gauze and withhold IV fluids","Administer oral antihypertensives immediately","Place the patient in high Fowler\u0027s position and wait 30 minutes to see if bleeding slows"],"a":0,"e":"A soft, boggy, high uterine fundus indicates uterine atony—the leading cause of primary postpartum haemorrhage (\u0027Tone\u0027 among the 4 Ts: Tone, Trauma, Tissue, Thrombin). Immediate fundal massage stimulates uterine smooth muscle contraction to compress bleeding spiral arteries, while simultaneous call for help, uterotonic therapy (oxytocin/ergometrine/carboprost/misoprostol + tranexamic acid per protocol), bladder emptying (a full bladder displaces the uterus and prevents contraction), and rapid crystalloid resuscitation restore haemodynamics.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03744","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing (Critical Care Monitoring)","topic":"Central Venous Pressure (CVP) Measurement","q":"At an ICU OSPE station, the nurse must level and zero a central venous pressure (CVP) transducer for a supine postoperative patient whose CVP has dropped from 7 mmHg to 1 mmHg alongside tachycardia (HR 124 bpm) and falling urine output. Where must the transducer stopcock be levelled, and what does this trend indicate?","o":["Level at the umbilicus; indicates acute fluid overload","Level at the phlebostatic axis (fourth intercostal space at the mid-axillary line); trend indicates hypovolaemia/intravascular volume depletion","Level at the sternal notch; indicates tension pneumothorax only","Level 15 cm above the bed regardless of chest anatomy; indicates left ventricular failure"],"a":1,"e":"To obtain accurate CVP readings (normal ~2–8 mmHg), the transducer zero-reference port must be levelled precisely to the phlebostatic axis—approximating the right atrium at the 4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line (with the patient supine, head of bed 0–45°). A falling CVP (\u003c 2 mmHg) accompanied by tachycardia and oliguria reflects reduced right ventricular preload due to hypovolaemia or active haemorrhage, guiding fluid challenge evaluation.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03745","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Measures of Disease Frequency","q":"During a hostel food-poisoning outbreak investigation, a community health nurse interviews 200 resident students. Out of 120 students who ate milk kheer, 48 developed acute gastroenteritis within 6 hours. Out of 80 students who did not eat kheer, 4 developed gastroenteritis. What is the Attack Rate of gastroenteritis among students who ate the kheer?","o":["30%","40%","20%","60%"],"a":1,"e":"Attack rate is an incidence proportion used in acute outbreak investigations: Attack Rate (%) = (Number of exposed persons developing the illness ÷ Total number of exposed persons at risk) × 100. For students who ate kheer: (48 ÷ 120) × 100 = 40%. Comparing this with the unexposed attack rate (4 ÷ 80 = 5%; relative risk = 8.0) strongly implicates the kheer as the outbreak vehicle.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03746","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing (Critical Care Monitoring)","topic":"Tension Pneumothorax","q":"A mechanically ventilated chest-trauma patient suddenly develops severe hypotension (BP 72/44 mmHg), HR 138 bpm, SpO2 79%, distended neck veins, absent breath sounds and hyperresonance over the left hemithorax, and tracheal deviation toward the right. What is the immediate priority clinical action?","o":["Administer IV furosemide for distended neck veins","Delay intervention for 20 minutes to obtain a portable chest radiograph first","Recognize tension pneumothorax as a clinical diagnosis requiring immediate emergency needle decompression (followed by tube thoracostomy) without waiting for an X-ray","Increase positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP) to 20 cmH2O"],"a":2,"e":"Unilateral absent breath sounds, hyperresonance, obstructive shock (hypotension, tachycardia, JVD), and contralateral tracheal shift on positive-pressure ventilation signify a tension pneumothorax—a rapidly fatal one-way valve air trap compressing the mediastinum and vena cava. Tension pneumothorax is a clinical emergency treated immediately via needle thoracostomy decompression followed by intercostal chest tube insertion; waiting for imaging causes cardiac arrest.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03747","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Maternal \u0026 Child Health Nursing","topic":"Apgar Scoring","q":"At 1 minute after birth, a newborn has a heart rate of 118 beats/min, a slow irregular cry/respiration, some flexion of the extremities, a grimace on suctioning, and a pink trunk with bluish hands and feet (acrocyanosis). What is the newborn\u0027s 1-minute Apgar score?","o":["8","4","9","6"],"a":3,"e":"Scoring each of the five Apgar criteria (0–2 each): Appearance (pink body, blue extremities/acrocyanosis) = 1; Pulse (HR \u003e= 100 bpm) = 2; Grimace/reflex irritability (grimace only, no vigorous cry/cough) = 1; Activity/muscle tone (some flexion, not active motion) = 1; Respiration (slow, irregular) = 1. Total = 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 6 (indicating moderate depression requiring tactile stimulation, airway clearance, and close observation).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03748","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Diagnostic Screening Evaluation","q":"During a community diabetes screening drive of 500 adults validated against the oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT), 100 adults truly have diabetes and 400 do not. The rapid glucometer correctly identifies 85 of the 100 true diabetics (15 false negatives) and correctly classifies 360 of the 400 non-diabetics (40 false positives). What is the Sensitivity of the screening test?","o":["85%","96%","68%","90%"],"a":0,"e":"Sensitivity is the proportion of true disease cases correctly detected positive by the test: Sensitivity = [True Positives ÷ (True Positives + False Negatives)] × 100. Substituting values: [85 ÷ (85 + 15)] × 100 = (85 ÷ 100) × 100 = 85%. (By contrast, Specificity here is 360 ÷ 400 = 90%).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03749","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundation","topic":"Structured Clinical Handover","q":"A ward nurse needs to urgently escalate a postoperative patient\u0027s sudden drop in blood pressure and urine output to the on-call resident by telephone. Which sequence follows the standardized SBAR handover framework?","o":["Social history, Bed number, Age, Room temperature","Situation (current problem/patient identity), Background (relevant diagnosis/surgery/meds), Assessment (current vitals and clinical impression), Recommendation (specific action/order requested)","Subjective opinion, Blame, Assumption, Refusal","Start with a 10-minute review of childhood illnesses before stating current blood pressure"],"a":1,"e":"SBAR (Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation) is a globally endorsed structured clinical communication tool that prevents critical omissions during shift handovers and urgent physician escalation. Leading concisely with Situation (who the patient is and why calling now), followed by pertinent Background, objective Assessment data, and an explicit Recommendation/request (e.g., immediate bedside evaluation and fluid order) improves response speed and patient safety.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03750","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Administration (Management)","topic":"ABC-VED Matrix Analysis","q":"In hospital nursing and pharmacy materials management using the ABC-VED matrix (combining Annual consumption value A/B/C with Criticality V/E/D), which group of items constitutes Category I requiring strictest top-level supervision and zero tolerance for stock-out?","o":["Only expired items awaiting condemnation","Only low-cost desirable items (CD)","High annual expenditure items and/or Vital life-saving items (AV, AE, AD, BV, CV)—such as emergency vasopressors, oxytocin, and defibrillator consumables","Non-essential stationery and decorative supplies"],"a":2,"e":"ABC analysis classifies inventory by annual usage cost (A = ~70% budget, B = ~20%, C = ~10%), whereas VED classifies by clinical criticality (V = Vital, E = Essential, D = Desirable). Coupling them into a 3×3 ABC-VED matrix places all Category \u0027A\u0027 items plus all \u0027Vital\u0027 items (AV, AE, AD, BV, CV) in Category I—requiring strict buffer stock, frequent audits, and top-level supervision because stock-outs threaten lives while high-cost items dominate budgets.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03751","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundation","topic":"ABC Prioritization Across Patients","q":"At the start of an evening shift, a charge nurse receives handover on four ward patients simultaneously. Using ABC (Airway, Breathing, Circulation) prioritization, which patient must the nurse assess first?","o":["A stable diabetic patient waiting for routine bedtime blood glucose check","A postoperative orthopaedic patient requesting oral analgesic for 4/10 incisional pain","A recovering pneumonia patient ready for discharge medication teaching","A patient 3 hours post-total thyroidectomy reporting neck tightness and exhibiting new inspiratory stridor"],"a":3,"e":"Inspiratory stridor and neck tightness 3 hours post-thyroidectomy warn of acute upper airway compression from an expanding neck haematoma or bilateral laryngeal oedema/nerve injury—an immediate Airway emergency under ABC triage. Immediate bedside assessment, calling the surgeon, loosening dressings if constricting, and preparing emergency intubation/tracheostomy take absolute priority over pain, routine glucose checks, or discharge teaching.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03752","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Maternal \u0026 Child Health Nursing","topic":"Infant Feeding in Maternal HIV","q":"An HIV-positive mother receiving lifelong combination antiretroviral therapy (ART) delivers a term newborn (on infant nevirapine prophylaxis) in a government hospital. Under NACO / WHO PPTCT guidelines in India, what infant feeding practice should the nurse strongly advise and why must mixed feeding be strictly avoided?","o":["Advise mixed feeding (breastmilk plus cow\u0027s milk and water) from Day 1","Advise exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months alongside maternal ART and infant ARV prophylaxis, strictly avoiding mixed feeding (which irritates infant gut mucosa and increases HIV transmission risk)","Advise giving only honey and plain water for the first month","Withhold both maternal ART and infant nevirapine while breastfeeding"],"a":1,"e":"Under NACO/WHO PPTCT guidelines, when replacement feeding does not meet AFASS criteria (or nationally as the standard public-health recommendation), HIV-positive mothers on lifelong ART should exclusively breastfeed for 6 months (continuing up to 12–24 months with complementary foods after 6 months) while the infant receives ARV prophylaxis. Mixed feeding (adding animal milk, formula, or water/solids \u003c 6 months) causes micro-inflammation of infant intestinal mucosa, markedly increasing HIV transmission and diarrhoeal mortality.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03753","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing (Critical Care Monitoring)","topic":"Rapid Shallow Breathing Index (RSBI)","q":"At a critical care OSPE station, an intubated patient recovering from COPD pneumonia undergoes a Spontaneous Breathing Trial (SBT) on CPAP 5 cmH2O with FiO2 0.35. Measured spontaneous respiratory rate (f) is 22 breaths/min and average tidal volume (VT) is 0.44 L (440 mL). What is the Rapid Shallow Breathing Index (RSBI = f / VT), and what does it indicate?","o":["RSBI = 50 breaths/min/L (\u003c 105 threshold); predicts favourable readiness for weaning/extubation alongside haemodynamic stability and intact airway reflexes","RSBI = 200 breaths/min/L; indicates immediate weaning failure","RSBI = 500 breaths/min/L; requires increasing FiO2 to 1.0","RSBI = 10 breaths/min/L; indicates severe apnoea"],"a":0,"e":"The Rapid Shallow Breathing Index (Yang-Tobin index) is calculated as spontaneous respiratory frequency divided by tidal volume in litres: RSBI = f ÷ VT (L) = 22 ÷ 0.44 = 50 breaths/min/L. An RSBI \u003c 105 breaths/min/L predicts higher likelihood of successful weaning and extubation when combined with adequate oxygenation, stable haemodynamics, and strong cough/gag reflexes, whereas RSBI \u003e 105 predicts weaning failure.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03754","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Infant Mortality Rate (IMR)","q":"In a rural Community Health Centre block during 1 calendar year, there were 2,000 live births, 40 deaths among infants aged under 1 year (of which 26 occurred during the first 28 days of life), and 10 stillbirths. What is the Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) for this block per 1,000 live births?","o":["13 per 1,000 live births","20 per 1,000 live births","25 per 1,000 live births","33 per 1,000 live births"],"a":1,"e":"Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) = (Total deaths of children \u003c 1 year of age in a year ÷ Total live births in the same year) × 1,000. Neonatal deaths (26) are already included within total infant deaths (40), and stillbirths (10) are excluded from both numerator and denominator of IMR. Thus: IMR = (40 ÷ 2,000) × 1,000 = 20 per 1,000 live births (while Neonatal Mortality Rate = 26 ÷ 2,000 × 1,000 = 13 per 1,000 live births).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03755","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Administration (Management)","topic":"INC Hospital Nurse Staffing Norms","q":"When planning shift staffing rosters for a multi-specialty hospital Intensive Care Unit (ICU) according to Indian Nursing Council (INC) hospital staffing norms, what is the recommended nurse-to-patient ratio per shift (for each 24-hour critical bed)?","o":["1:15 per shift","1:6 per shift","1:1 per shift (one nurse per critical/ICU patient on each shift, plus 30% leave reserve)","1:25 per shift during night duty only"],"a":2,"e":"Under Indian Nursing Council (INC) staffing standards, high-acuity Intensive Care Units require a 1:1 nurse-to-patient ratio per shift (meaning 3 nurses over three 8-hour shifts per occupied ICU bed, plus a 30% leave/off-duty reserve factor when calculating total sanctioned posts), compared to 1:3 in general medical-surgical wards in teaching hospitals. Maintaining safe ratios directly lowers ICU mortality and medication errors.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03756","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundation","topic":"PICO Clinical Question Formulation","q":"A ward nurse wants to search medical literature on whether 0.12% chlorhexidine oral rinsing reduces ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) compared with normal saline swabbing in mechanically ventilated adult ICU patients. Which breakdown correctly maps the PICO components?","o":["P = Chlorhexidine; I = ICU adults; C = VAP rate; O = Normal saline","P = Normal saline; I = VAP; C = Nurses; O = Mechanical ventilation","P = Cost of mouthwash; I = Retrospective chart; C = None; O = Hospital length","P = Mechanically ventilated adult ICU patients; I = 0.12% chlorhexidine oral rinsing; C = Normal saline oral swabbing; O = Incidence of ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP)"],"a":3,"e":"In Evidence-Based Practice (EBP), the PICO framework structures searchable clinical questions: P (Patient/Population or Problem = mechanically ventilated ICU adults), I (Intervention = 0.12% chlorhexidine oral care), C (Comparison = normal saline swabbing), and O (Outcome = VAP incidence). Optional \u0027T\u0027 (Timeframe) forms PICOT.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03757","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Maternal \u0026 Child Health Nursing","topic":"Infant of Diabetic Mother (IDM)","q":"A macrosomic 4.2 kg newborn delivered to a mother with poorly controlled gestational diabetes develops jitteriness, weak cry, and capillary blood glucose of 32 mg/dL at 1 hour of age. Which mechanism explains this early hypoglycaemia, and what immediate management is indicated for symptomatic hypoglycaemia (\u003c 40 mg/dL)?","o":["Maternal hyperglycaemia stimulated fetal pancreatic beta-cell hyperplasia and hyperinsulinaemia, which persists after umbilical cord clamping cuts off maternal glucose; give immediate IV 10% Dextrose bolus (2 mL/kg) followed by continuous glucose infusion while monitoring blood glucose","Physiological thermoregulation; place naked in a cold room","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia; give IV furosemide","Maternal hypoglycaemia caused fetal glucagon excess; withhold all glucose for 6 hours"],"a":0,"e":"Maternal glucose crosses the placenta freely (Pedersen hypothesis), whereas maternal insulin does not. Chronic fetal hyperglycaemia induces fetal pancreatic islet hypertrophy and hyperinsulinaemia. At birth, severing the umbilical cord abruptly stops maternal glucose supply while high neonatal insulin persists, causing rapid severe hypoglycaemia within 1–3 hours. Symptomatic hypoglycaemia (jitteriness, lethargy, apnoea, seizures) requires immediate IV 10% Dextrose bolus (2 mL/kg) followed by maintenance glucose infusion rate (GIR 6–8 mg/kg/min) and early feeding once stable.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03758","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing (Critical Care Monitoring)","topic":"12-Lead ECG Electrode Placement","q":"At a cardiac monitoring OSPE station, a nurse places the six precordial chest leads (V1–V6) for a 12-lead ECG in a patient with suspected anterior STEMI. Which anatomical landmark placement is exact?","o":["V1 and V2 in the 2nd intercostal space; V4 in the 6th intercostal space right midclavicular line","V1 at 4th ICS right sternal border, V2 at 4th ICS left sternal border, V4 at 5th ICS left midclavicular line, V3 midway between V2 and V4, V5 at 5th ICS left anterior axillary line, and V6 at 5th ICS left mid-axillary line","All six precordial leads placed vertically along the sternum","V1–V6 placed on the right hemithorax routinely"],"a":1,"e":"Standard 12-lead ECG accuracy depends critically on anatomical landmarking using the sternal angle of Louis (2nd costal cartilage) to count down to the 4th and 5th intercostal spaces (ICS): V1 = 4th ICS right sternal border; V2 = 4th ICS left sternal border; V4 = 5th ICS left midclavicular line; V3 = midway between V2 and V4; V5 = 5th ICS left anterior axillary line (horizontal to V4); V6 = 5th ICS left mid-axillary line (horizontal to V4/V5). Misplaced leads can mimic or miss acute myocardial infarction.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03759","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR)","q":"In a district health review over 1 calendar year, there were 50,000 live births and 45 maternal deaths (deaths of women while pregnant or within 42 days of termination of pregnancy from pregnancy-related causes). What is the Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) per 1,00,000 live births?","o":["45 per 1,00,000 live births","110 per 1,00,000 live births","90 per 1,00,000 live births","180 per 1,00,000 live births"],"a":2,"e":"Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) measures obstetric risk per live birth and is expressed per 1,00,000 live births in India (SRS/WHO): MMR = (Number of maternal deaths in a given period ÷ Total number of live births in the same period) × 1,00,000. Substituting values: (45 ÷ 50,000) × 1,00,000 = 45 × 2 = 90 per 1,00,000 live births.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03760","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Administration (Management)","topic":"Zero-Based Budgeting (ZBB)","q":"A nursing superintendent prepares the annual hospital nursing department budget by starting from a base of zero and requiring every ward programme, equipment request, and staffing line item to be freshly justified on cost-effectiveness rather than adding a 10% inflation increment to last year\u0027s spending. Which budgeting approach is used?","o":["Incremental (line-item) budgeting","Capital depreciation only","Petty-cash imprest budgeting","Zero-Based Budgeting (ZBB)"],"a":3,"e":"Zero-Based Budgeting (ZBB) requires managers to build each fiscal year\u0027s budget from a \u0027zero base\u0027, evaluating and prioritizing every programme\u0027s ongoing clinical necessity and cost-benefit ratio rather than automatically rolling over or inflating prior-year allocations. While time-intensive, ZBB eliminates obsolete spending and aligns nursing resources with current patient acuity priorities.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03761","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundation","topic":"Five Rights of Nursing Delegation","q":"On a busy medical ward, a registered nurse works alongside an unlicensed nursing aide (orderly/nursing assistant). Applying the \u0027Five Rights of Delegation\u0027 (Right Task, Right Circumstance, Right Person, Right Direction, Right Supervision), which activity can be safely delegated versus which MUST be retained by the registered nurse?","o":["Delegate routine oral temperature/bed-bath assistance for a stable patient to the aide with clear parameters; RN retains initial/unstable patient assessment, nursing diagnosis, patient education, and IV medication administration","Delegate initial admission nursing assessment and IV potassium push to the aide; RN folds linens","Delegate all clinical accountability to the aide once verbal instructions are given","Delegate blood transfusion verification completely to the aide without RN check"],"a":0,"e":"Effective delegation frees RN time for complex clinical reasoning without compromising patient safety. Routine, standardized, low-risk tasks on stable patients (ADLs, stable vital signs) can be delegated when the delegatee is competent and given clear reporting thresholds. However, the RN retains legal accountability and cannot delegate nursing process judgements (initial/change-of-status assessments, care planning, teaching, blood products, or IV meds).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03762","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Maternal \u0026 Child Health Nursing","topic":"Retinopathy of Prematurity (ROP)","q":"A 28-week preterm neonate (birth weight 1,100 g) is receiving blended oxygen via nasal CPAP in the NICU/SNCU. Why must the nurse titrate FiO2 carefully using an air-oxygen blender to maintain SpO2 strictly within 90%–95% rather than keeping SpO2 at 99%–100%?","o":["To deliberately induce severe hypoxia for lung growth","Sustained hyperoxia (SpO2 \u003e 95%–100%) suppresses retinal VEGF and causes abnormal retinal vasoconstriction followed by neovascular proliferation—leading to Retinopathy of Prematurity (ROP) and potential blindness","To save oxygen cylinder gas costs solely","Preterm neonates cannot tolerate SpO2 above 75%"],"a":1,"e":"In preterm infants (\u003c 32 weeks / \u003c 1,500–2,000 g) whose retinal vasculature is incompletely developed, prolonged high arterial PaO2/hyperoxia damages developing retinal capillaries and triggers pathological neovascularization, scarring, and retinal detachment (Retinopathy of Prematurity, ROP) alongside bronchopulmonary dysplasia. Blended oxygen titrated to target SpO2 90%–95% balances survival against ROP blindness, paired with timely ophthalmologist ROP screening.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03763","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing (Critical Care Monitoring)","topic":"Endotracheal Tube (ETT) Cuff Pressure","q":"At an ICU OSPE station, the nurse connects a calibrated cuff manometer to check the high-volume, low-pressure cuff of an adult patient\u0027s endotracheal tube. Which target cuff pressure range prevents both microaspiration (VAP) and tracheal mucosal ischaemic necrosis?","o":["80–100 cmH2O","20–30 cmH2O","50–65 cmH2O","5–10 cmH2O"],"a":1,"e":"Maintaining endotracheal (or tracheostomy) cuff pressure between 20 and 30 cmH2O (approx. 15–22 mmHg) seals the lower airway effectively against subglottic secretion microaspiration—a major driver of Ventilator-Associated Pneumonia (VAP)—while staying safely below tracheal mucosal capillary perfusion pressure (~30–35 cmH2O) to prevent mucosal ischaemia, ulceration, and post-extubation tracheal stenosis.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03764","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Point Prevalence Calculation","q":"During a cross-sectional hypertension screening survey of 4,000 adults aged \u003e= 30 years in a Community Health Centre area, 600 adults are found to have hypertension (450 previously known cases plus 150 newly diagnosed cases). What is the Point Prevalence of hypertension in this surveyed adult population?","o":["11.25%","3.75%","15%","25%"],"a":2,"e":"Point Prevalence measures all existing cases (both old and newly detected cases present at that specific point/survey) divided by the total population examined at that time: Point Prevalence = (600 ÷ 4,000) × 100 = 0.15 × 100 = 15%. (By contrast, Incidence counts ONLY new cases arising in a disease-free cohort over a defined follow-up period).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03765","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Administration (Management)","topic":"Donabedian Quality Framework","q":"A nursing quality committee audits three ward indicators: (1) availability of functional crash carts and 1:1 ICU nurse staffing; (2) percentage of nurses completing all 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene and medication double-checks; and (3) annual hospital CLABSI rate and patient satisfaction scores. According to Donabedian\u0027s Quality Assurance framework, how are these three indicators classified?","o":["(1) Outcome, (2) Structure, (3) Process","(1) Process, (2) Outcome, (3) Structure","All three are exclusively financial budgets","(1) Structure, (2) Process, (3) Outcome"],"a":3,"e":"Avedis Donabedian\u0027s classic healthcare quality triad evaluates care across: Structure (physical facilities, equipment like crash carts, staffing ratios, and organizational policies), Process (how care is actually delivered—hand-hygiene compliance, bundle checklists, documentation), and Outcome (the resulting impact on patient health status—infection rates, mortality, readmissions, and satisfaction).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03766","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundation","topic":"Kübler-Ross Stages of Grief","q":"A patient newly diagnosed with terminal metastatic cancer throws his water cup onto the floor and shouts at the nurse, \u0027Why did God ruin my life? None of you nurses even care!\u0027 Recognizing the \u0027Anger\u0027 stage of Kübler-Ross\u0027s grief model, what is the nurse\u0027s most therapeutic response?","o":["Stay calm, avoid taking the outburst personally or reacting defensively, ensure physical safety, acknowledge his profound distress (\u0027I can see how angry and overwhelmed you feel right now\u0027), and allow safe expression","Promise him cheerfully that a miracle cure will arrive tomorrow","Tell him he has no right to be angry because other patients are sicker","Shout back firmly that throwing cups is disrespectful and refuse to enter the room for 24 hours"],"a":0,"e":"In Kübler-Ross\u0027s model (Denial, Anger, Bargaining, Depression, Acceptance), anger is often displaced onto healthcare staff, family, or spiritual beliefs as a natural coping response to loss of control. Responding defensively, scolding, or withdrawing reinforces isolation; calm non-judgemental presence and empathetic validation help the patient process grief safely without escalating conflict.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03767","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Maternal \u0026 Child Health Nursing","topic":"Hydatidiform Mole (Gestational Trophoblastic Disease)","q":"A 25-year-old woman undergoes suction evacuation for a complete hydatidiform mole (markedly elevated beta-hCG, \u0027snowstorm\u0027 ultrasound, grape-like vesicles). Upon discharge, she asks why she must undergo regular serial serum beta-hCG blood tests and strictly avoid pregnancy for 6–12 months. What is the exact clinical rationale?","o":["Contraception shrinks ovarian cysts only","Serial quantitative beta-hCG monitoring is essential to detect persistent gestational trophoblastic neoplasia / choriocarcinoma early, and reliable contraception prevents a new pregnancy\u0027s normal hCG surge from masking malignant relapse","Beta-hCG tests measure maternal iron stores solely","Serial beta-hCG checks ensure twins do not form in the fallopian tubes"],"a":1,"e":"Following evacuation of a hydatidiform mole, 15%–20% of complete moles risk progressing to persistent Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia (GTN) or invasive choriocarcinoma, which secretes beta-hCG. Serial quantitative serum beta-hCG measurements track regression down to undetectable levels; avoiding conception for 6–12 months using reliable contraception (e.g., combined OCPs once uterine involution occurs; avoid IUCD before normalization) prevents physiological pregnancy hCG from obscuring rising malignant hCG.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03768","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing (Critical Care Monitoring)","topic":"Arterial Line Square-Wave (Dynamic Response) Test","q":"At an ICU OSPE station, a nurse performs a fast-flush \u0027square-wave\u0027 dynamic response test on a radial arterial line after leveling and zeroing at the phlebostatic axis. Instead of 1–2 crisp oscillations returning to baseline, the monitor shows a rounded, sluggish sloping return with ZERO oscillations and an artificially narrowed pulse pressure (falsely low SBP). What does this indicate?","o":["Optimally damped accurate arterial line","Normal central venous pressure tracing","Overdamped arterial line system (commonly caused by air bubbles in tubing, loose connections, kinked catheter, or blood clots at tip) requiring flushing/air removal","Underdamped resonant system giving falsely high systolic pressure"],"a":2,"e":"A fast-flush square-wave test verifies dynamic fidelity of an invasive arterial pressure line. An optimally damped tracing snaps down sharply and settles after 1–2 oscillations. An overdamped tracing shows zero oscillations and a slurred return—usually caused by air bubbles, compliant soft tubing, loose stopcocks, kinks, or clot/catheter against the vessel wall—artificially blunting systolic peaks and raising diastolic troughs (giving falsely low SBP). Underdamped systems show \u003e 2–3 excessive ringing oscillations (falsely high SBP).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03769","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Neonatal Mortality Rate (NMR)","q":"In a Primary Health Centre area during 1 calendar year, there were 1,500 live births, 18 neonatal deaths (deaths within the first 28 completed days of life), and 12 post-neonatal deaths (deaths from Day 29 to \u003c 1 year). What is the Neonatal Mortality Rate (NMR) per 1,000 live births?","o":["20 per 1,000 live births","30 per 1,000 live births","8 per 1,000 live births","12 per 1,000 live births"],"a":3,"e":"Neonatal Mortality Rate (NMR) = (Number of deaths of infants aged 0–28 days in a year ÷ Total live births in the same year) × 1,000. Substituting values: (18 ÷ 1,500) × 1,000 = 12 per 1,000 live births. (By contrast, Post-Neonatal Mortality Rate = 12 ÷ 1,500 × 1,000 = 8 per 1,000, and total IMR = [18 + 12] ÷ 1,500 × 1,000 = 20 per 1,000).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03770","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Administration (Management)","topic":"Performance Appraisal Biases","q":"A nursing supervisor evaluates a staff nurse\u0027s annual performance and assigns the highest score (\u00275/5 Outstanding\u0027) across all unrelated domains—including punctuality, documentation, and inventory management—solely because the supervisor was impressed by the nurse\u0027s calm handling of a single cardiac arrest code. Which appraisal bias occurred?","o":["Halo effect (allowing one standout positive trait or event to skew ratings favourably across all other competencies)","Central tendency error","Zero-based budgeting","Strictness/severity bias"],"a":0,"e":"The Halo effect occurs when an evaluator generalizes a single impressive characteristic or memorable incident to rate an employee unrealistically high across every separate performance criterion (the reverse negative distortion is the \u0027Horns effect\u0027). Maintaining year-round anecdotal notes and using behaviourally anchored rating scales (BARS) counters Halo, Recency, and Central Tendency biases.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03771","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Foundation","topic":"Maslow\u0027s Hierarchy of Needs","q":"A patient admitted with acute COPD exacerbation has four active nursing diagnoses: (1) Anxiety related to hospitalization; (2) Impaired gas exchange (SpO2 85%); (3) Deficient knowledge regarding inhaler technique; and (4) Social isolation. Applying Maslow\u0027s Hierarchy of Human Needs, which diagnosis MUST be addressed first?","o":["Anxiety related to hospitalization exclusively before giving oxygen","Impaired gas exchange (Physiological survival need — oxygenation)","Deficient knowledge (Cognitive/Self-actualization)","Social isolation (Love and Belonging)"],"a":1,"e":"Maslow\u0027s Hierarchy orders human needs from foundational physiological survival (oxygenation, fluid/electrolyte balance, nutrition, elimination, thermoregulation) upward through Safety/Security, Love/Belonging, Self-Esteem, and Self-Actualization. Acute hypoxaemia (Impaired gas exchange, SpO2 85%) threatens immediate survival—and also fuels anxiety—so restoring oxygenation takes top nursing priority before education or social support.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03772","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Maternal \u0026 Child Health Nursing","topic":"Chronic vs Gestational Hypertension vs Superimposed Preeclampsia","q":"A woman whose BP was 146/94 mmHg at her first antenatal visit at 11 weeks gestation (with zero proteinuria and normal renal/liver labs) returns at 30 weeks gestation with BP 162/106 mmHg, new 2+ proteinuria, and platelets falling to 92,000/mm3. How are her initial 11-week diagnosis and current 30-week progression classified?","o":["White-coat normotension","Eclampsia at 11 weeks solely","Chronic hypertension initially (hypertension present before 20 weeks gestation) progressing to Chronic Hypertension with Superimposed Preeclampsia","Gestational hypertension initially -\u003e Benign normal term pregnancy now"],"a":2,"e":"Hypertension (\u003e= 140/90 mmHg) documented prior to pregnancy or before 20 weeks gestation is classified as Chronic Hypertension (whereas Gestational Hypertension arises newly \u003e= 20 weeks without proteinuria or severe end-organ features). When a woman with chronic hypertension later develops new-onset/worsening proteinuria, thrombocytopenia (\u003c 1,00,000/mm3), elevated liver enzymes, or severe refractory BP surges after 20 weeks, it is diagnosed as Superimposed Preeclampsia requiring close maternal-foetal surveillance.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03773","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Medical-Surgical Nursing (Critical Care Monitoring)","topic":"Waveform Capnography (ETCO2)","q":"At an ICU monitoring OSPE station, a nurse observes continuous waveform capnography (ETCO2) on a mechanically ventilated patient. Suddenly, the normal rectangular capnogram drops abruptly to a flat zero baseline (ETCO2 = 0 mmHg) right after patient repositioning. What critical events must the nurse check immediately?","o":["Mild bronchospasm only; continue current ventilator settings","Hyperventilation alkalosis with ETCO2 38 mmHg","Normal physiological sleep pattern","Acute ventilator circuit disconnection, accidental endotracheal extubation/oesophageal displacement, complete ETT obstruction, or sudden cardiac arrest—check patient chest rise, circuit connections, and pulse immediately"],"a":3,"e":"Continuous waveform capnography measures exhaled end-tidal CO2 breath-by-breath (normal 35–45 mmHg). An abrupt drop of the capnogram to zero flatline signifies immediate loss of alveolar ventilation or pulmonary blood flow—most commonly ventilator tubing disconnection, accidental extubation/oesophageal tube placement, total mucus plug occlusion, or cardiac arrest. (By contrast, a sloping \u0027shark-fin\u0027 upstroke indicates bronchospasm/COPD obstruction).","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03774","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Community Health Nursing","topic":"Crude Birth Rate (CBR)","q":"In a district with an estimated mid-year total population of 2,00,000, health facilities and civil registries recorded 3,600 live births during the calendar year. What is the Crude Birth Rate (CBR) per 1,000 mid-year population?","o":["18 per 1,000 population","36 per 1,000 population","24 per 1,000 population","9 per 1,000 population"],"a":0,"e":"Crude Birth Rate (CBR) = (Total number of live births during a given year ÷ Estimated mid-year total population) × 1,000. Substituting values: (3,600 ÷ 2,00,000) × 1,000 = 3,600 ÷ 200 = 18 per 1,000 mid-year population.","reviewed":false},{"id":"Q03775","course":"Post Basic B.Sc Nursing","subject":"Nursing Administration (Management)","topic":"Conflict Resolution Strategies","q":"Two senior ICU charge nurses strongly disagree over medication double-check workflows. The nursing superintendent convenes both nurses to examine incident data openly, respects both perspectives, and facilitates co-creating a new evidence-based protocol that fully satisfies patient safety and staff workflow concerns. Which conflict-management strategy is used?","o":["Avoiding (ignoring the conflict completely)","Collaborating (high assertiveness and high cooperativeness achieving a mutually beneficial win-win solution)","Competing/Forcing (unilateral win-lose authority)","Accommodating (one side surrendering completely without problem-solving)"],"a":1,"e":"Under the Thomas-Kilmann Conflict Mode model taught in nursing management, Collaborating combines high assertiveness with high cooperativeness—treating conflict as a shared problem to solve openly through creative integration so all parties\u0027 core safety and operational goals are met (\u0027win-win\u0027), unlike Competing (win-lose), Avoiding (lose-lose), or Compromising (both sides give up something partially).","reviewed":false}];
